const PRELOADED_QUESTIONS=[{"id":"mbe-civpro-001","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":1,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A national cosmetics retailer incorporated and headquartered in State A bought makeup manufactured by a company incorporated and headquartered in State B. The contract contained a forum-selection clause providing that any dispute between the parties would be resolved in a state or federal court located in State B. After delivery, the retailer discovered that the makeup was contaminated and could not be sold. The retailer filed a complaint against the company for breach of contract in a state court in State B for $1 million. The company filed a notice of removal with the federal court in State B 14 days after being served with process. The retailer immediately filed a motion to remand. Is the federal court likely to grant the retailer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the forum-selection clause allows the parties to file suit in state or federal court.","B":"No, because the parties are diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.","C":"Yes, because the company is a citizen of State B.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer's notice of removal was untimely."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant can remove a suit from state court to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nA party (usually the plaintiff) can seek to return the case to state court by filing a motion to remand.* One basis for remand is the home-court-advantage rule (ie, forum-defendant rule). This rule prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists since the amount in controversy is $1 million and the retailer and company are citizens of different states (States A and B, respectively). But since the company is a citizen of State B—where the case was filed—the home-court-advantage rule prohibits removal to federal court (Choice B). Therefore, the federal court will likely grant the retailer's motion to remand the case to state court. *A motion for remand places the burden on the defendant to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that removal was proper. (Choice A) A forum-selection clause is a contractual provision that designates the location and/or court where any dispute between the parties must be adjudicated. Although such clauses are generally enforceable, federal courts strictly follow the rules regarding removal—including the home-court-advantage rule—over these clauses.** (Choice D) The defendant must file a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days after receiving the initial complaint or service of the summons—whichever period is shorter. Therefore, the company timely filed the notice of removal 14 days after being served with process. **To enforce a forum-selection clause requiring that a dispute be resolved in federal court, a defendant would need to move to dismiss the state court action. If the state court dismisses the action, the plaintiff would then need to refile the case in federal court. Educational objective: The home-court-advantage rule prohibits removal from state to federal court when (1) the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed. References\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (forum-defendant rule for removal).","rule":"The home-court-advantage rule prohibits removal from state to federal court when (1) the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed.","irac":{"issue":"A national cosmetics retailer incorporated and headquartered in State A bought makeup manufactured by a company incorporated and headquartered in State B. The contract contained a forum-selection clause providing that any dispute between the parties would be resolved in a state or federal court located in State B. After delivery, the retailer discovered that the makeup was contaminated and could not be sold. The retailer filed a complaint against the company for breach of contract in a state court in State B for $1 million. The company filed a notice of removal with the federal court in State B 14 days after being served with process. The retailer immediately filed a motion to remand. Is the federal court likely to grant the retailer's motion?","rule":"The home-court-advantage rule prohibits removal from state to federal court when (1) the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed.","analysis":"A defendant can remove a suit from state court to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nA party (usually the plaintiff) can seek to return the case to state court by filing a motion to remand.* One basis for remand is the home-court-advantage rule (ie, forum-defendant rule). This rule prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists since the amount in controversy is $1 million and the retailer and company are citizens of different states (States A and B, respectively). But since the company is a citizen of State B—where the case was filed—the home-court-advantage rule prohibits removal to federal court (Choice B). Therefore, the federal court will likely grant the retailer's motion to remand the case to state court. *A motion for remand places the burden on the defendant to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that removal was proper. (Choice A) A forum-selection clause is a contractual provision that designates the location and/or court where any dispute between the parties must be adjudicated. Although such clauses are generally enforceable, federal courts strictly follow the rules regarding removal—including the home-court-advantage rule—over these clauses.** (Choice D) The defendant must file a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days after receiving the initial complaint or service of the summons—whichever period is shorter. Therefore, the company timely filed the notice of removal 14 days after being served with process. **To enforce a forum-selection clause requiring that a dispute be resolved in federal court, a defendant would need to move to dismiss the state court action. If the state court dismisses the action, the plaintiff would then need to refile the case in federal court. Educational objective: The home-court-advantage rule prohibits removal from state to federal court when (1) the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed. References\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (forum-defendant rule for removal).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (forum-defendant rule for removal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[1],"sourceAnswerPages":[2,3],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且总部设于 A 州的全国性化妆品零售商，购买了一家在 B 州注册成立且总部设于 B 州的公司所生产的化妆品。合同包含法院选择条款，约定双方之间的任何争议均应由位于 B 州的州法院或联邦法院解决。交货后，零售商发现化妆品受到污染，无法销售。零售商遂在 B 州州法院以违约为由起诉该公司，索赔 100 万美元。该公司在收到诉讼文书送达 14 天后，向 B 州联邦法院提交移送通知。零售商立即提出发回州法院的动议。联邦法院是否可能批准零售商的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法院选择条款允许双方在州法院或联邦法院提起诉讼。","B":"不会，因为双方具有不同州籍，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。","C":"会，因为该公司是 B 州公民。","D":"会，因为制造商的移送通知逾期提交。"},"explanationZh":"如果案件属于联邦法院的原始事项管辖权，被告可以将诉讼从州法院移送至联邦法院。原始管辖权可基于以下两种情况之一产生：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——诉请依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题并非如此）；或者\n\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方当事人具有不同州籍。\n\n当事人（通常为原告）可以提出发回州法院的动议，请求将案件退回州法院。* 发回的理由之一是本州法院优势规则（即法院州被告规则，forum-defendant rule）。在以下情况下，该规则禁止移送：(1) 事项管辖权以多元州籍管辖权为基础；并且 (2) 一名被告是提起诉讼所在州的公民。\n\n本题中，争议金额为 100 万美元，且零售商与公司分别为 A 州和 B 州公民，因此存在多元州籍管辖权。但是，该公司是提起诉讼所在的 B 州公民，所以本州法院优势规则禁止将案件移送至联邦法院（选项 B）。因此，联邦法院很可能批准零售商的动议，将案件发回州法院。\n\n*提出发回动议后，被告负有依优势证据标准证明移送适当的责任。\n\n（选项 A）法院选择条款是指定双方争议必须在哪一地点和／或哪一法院裁判的合同条款。虽然此类条款通常可以执行，但联邦法院会严格适用移送规则，包括本州法院优势规则；这些规则优先于该类条款。**\n\n（选项 D）被告必须在收到最初起诉状或传票送达后 30 天内向联邦法院提交移送通知，以两个期间中较短者为准。因此，该公司在收到诉讼文书送达 14 天后提交移送通知，属于及时提交。\n\n**若要执行要求争议必须在联邦法院解决的法院选择条款，被告需要申请驳回州法院诉讼。若州法院驳回诉讼，原告随后需要在联邦法院重新起诉。\n\n学习目标：在以下情况下，本州法院优势规则禁止将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院：(1) 联邦法院的事项管辖权以多元州籍管辖权为基础；并且 (2) 一名被告是提起诉讼所在州的公民。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2)（移送中的法院州被告规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-001"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-002","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":2,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A bank from State A filed a debt-collection action against a man from State B in a state court located in State A. The bank alleged that the man was liable for credit card charges totaling $80,000. The man filed an answer in which he asserted a counterclaim against the bank. He also joined a credit card company from State A to his counterclaim. The credit card company filed a notice of removal with the federal court in State A. The man moved to remand the action. How will the court likely rule on the man's motion to remand?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the federal court has exclusive jurisdiction over the action.","B":"Deny the motion, because the federal court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the action.","C":"Grant the motion, because the credit card company is not qualified to remove the action.","D":"Grant the motion, because the man did not consent to the action's removal."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant has the power to remove a case from state court to federal court when the case falls within the federal court's original (ie, subject-matter) jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction—which must be established on the face of the plaintiff's complaint—can arise from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.*\n\nHowever, the federal court should remand the case back to state court if there was a procedural error in the removal. Since only defendants listed in the complaint may remove a case, a procedural error occurs when a case is removed by a party other than a defendant. This includes a party joined to the suit by a defendant's counterclaim. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists because (1) the amount in controversy is $80,000 and (2) the man is from State B while the bank and credit card company are from State A (Choice B). But since the credit card company was joined by the man's counterclaim, it is not qualified to remove the action to federal court. Therefore, the court will likely grant the man's motion to remand the case. *After removal, if a plaintiff seeks to join additional defendants who would destroy diversity jurisdiction, the federal court may deny joinder and proceed with the case OR permit joinder and remand the case to state court. (Choice A) Federal and state courts have concurrent jurisdiction over state and federal law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here). (Choice D) When a case has more than one defendant, the consent of all defendants must be obtained before the case can be removed from state to federal court. But here, the man is the only defendant, so the fact that he did not consent to the action's removal is irrelevant. Educational objective: Only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint have the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A party joined to the suit through a counterclaim is not considered a defendant for removal purposes. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• Home Depot U.S.A., Inc. v. Jackson, 139 S. Ct. 1743, 1748–50 (2019) (holding that only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint may remove a case from state to federal court).","rule":"Only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint have the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A party joined to the suit through a counterclaim is not considered a defendant for removal purposes.","irac":{"issue":"A bank from State A filed a debt-collection action against a man from State B in a state court located in State A. The bank alleged that the man was liable for credit card charges totaling $80,000. The man filed an answer in which he asserted a counterclaim against the bank. He also joined a credit card company from State A to his counterclaim. The credit card company filed a notice of removal with the federal court in State A. The man moved to remand the action. How will the court likely rule on the man's motion to remand?","rule":"Only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint have the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A party joined to the suit through a counterclaim is not considered a defendant for removal purposes.","analysis":"A defendant has the power to remove a case from state court to federal court when the case falls within the federal court's original (ie, subject-matter) jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction—which must be established on the face of the plaintiff's complaint—can arise from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.*\n\nHowever, the federal court should remand the case back to state court if there was a procedural error in the removal. Since only defendants listed in the complaint may remove a case, a procedural error occurs when a case is removed by a party other than a defendant. This includes a party joined to the suit by a defendant's counterclaim. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists because (1) the amount in controversy is $80,000 and (2) the man is from State B while the bank and credit card company are from State A (Choice B). But since the credit card company was joined by the man's counterclaim, it is not qualified to remove the action to federal court. Therefore, the court will likely grant the man's motion to remand the case. *After removal, if a plaintiff seeks to join additional defendants who would destroy diversity jurisdiction, the federal court may deny joinder and proceed with the case OR permit joinder and remand the case to state court. (Choice A) Federal and state courts have concurrent jurisdiction over state and federal law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here). (Choice D) When a case has more than one defendant, the consent of all defendants must be obtained before the case can be removed from state to federal court. But here, the man is the only defendant, so the fact that he did not consent to the action's removal is irrelevant. Educational objective: Only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint have the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A party joined to the suit through a counterclaim is not considered a defendant for removal purposes. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• Home Depot U.S.A., Inc. v. Jackson, 139 S. Ct. 1743, 1748–50 (2019) (holding that only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint may remove a case from state to federal court).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• Home Depot U.S.A., Inc. v. Jackson, 139 S. Ct. 1743, 1748–50 (2019) (holding that only defendants listed in the plaintiff's complaint may remove a case from state to federal court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[4],"sourceAnswerPages":[5,6],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一家银行在 A 州州法院对 B 州的一名男子提起债务追收诉讼。银行主张该男子应对总额 80,000 美元的信用卡消费负责。该男子提交答辩状，并在其中对银行提出反诉。他还将 A 州的一家信用卡公司追加至该反诉。信用卡公司向 A 州联邦法院提交移送通知。该男子提出发回州法院的动议。法院可能如何裁定该男子的发回动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回该动议，因为联邦法院对该诉讼享有专属管辖权。","B":"驳回该动议，因为联邦法院对该诉讼享有事项管辖权。","C":"批准该动议，因为信用卡公司不具备移送该诉讼的资格。","D":"批准该动议，因为该男子未同意移送该诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"当案件属于联邦法院的原始管辖权（即事项管辖权）时，被告有权将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。原始管辖权必须从原告起诉状的表面即可确定，其可基于以下两种情况之一产生：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——诉请依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题并非如此）；或者\n\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方当事人具有不同州籍。*\n\n但是，如果移送程序存在错误，联邦法院应当把案件发回州法院。只有起诉状中列明的被告才可以移送案件，因此，由被告以外的当事人移送案件即构成程序错误。这也包括因被告提出反诉而被追加进诉讼的当事人。\n\n本题中，存在多元州籍管辖权，因为：(1) 争议金额为 80,000 美元；且 (2) 该男子来自 B 州，而银行和信用卡公司来自 A 州（选项 B）。但是，信用卡公司是通过该男子的反诉被追加的，因而不具备将诉讼移送至联邦法院的资格。因此，法院很可能批准该男子的动议，将案件发回州法院。\n\n*案件移送后，如果原告试图追加会破坏多元州籍管辖权的新被告，联邦法院可以拒绝追加并继续审理案件，或者允许追加并把案件发回州法院。\n\n（选项 A）州法院和联邦法院对州法及联邦法诉请通常享有并行管辖权，除非宪法或国会明确规定联邦法院享有专属管辖权（本题不存在此情形）。\n\n（选项 D）案件有多名被告时，必须取得所有被告的同意，才能将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。但本题中，该男子是唯一的被告，所以他未同意移送这一事实并不相关。\n\n学习目标：只有原告起诉状中列明的被告才有权将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。通过反诉被追加进诉讼的当事人，不被视为移送规则意义上的被告。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441（移送要求）。\n\n• Home Depot U.S.A., Inc. v. Jackson, 139 S. Ct. 1743, 1748–50 (2019)（认定只有原告起诉状中列明的被告可以将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-001"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-003","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":3,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A woman sued her former employer in state court, asserting age and sex discrimination claims under both state and federal law. The woman's attorney had recently been embarrassed in court by the judge to whom the case was assigned. Wishing to avoid difficulties with the judge, the woman's attorney promptly removed the case to federal court on the basis of federal-question jurisdiction. The employer's attorney has timely moved to remand. How is the federal court likely to proceed?","choices":{"A":"Remand the entire case.","B":"Remand the state claims but keep the federal claims.","C":"Retain the case, because it was timely removed and the woman alleges federal claims.","D":"Retain the case to avoid the risk of bias and impropriety in having it proceed before a judge who has shown clear hostility toward the woman's attorney."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Remand based on improper removal\n\nBasis Example Result\n\nNo subject- • No subject-matter jurisdiction • Remand entire case matter over any claim jurisdiction • No subject-matter jurisdiction • Retain removable over some claims claims\n• Sever & remand nonremovable claims Procedural error • Defendant filed notice of removal • Remand entire case more than 30 days after receiving complaint\n• Not all defendants consented to removal\n• Case removed by plaintiff (not defendant)\n• Notice not delivered to parties or state court\n• Removal violates forum-defendant rule A defendant can remove a case originally filed in state court to federal court if:\n• the federal court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the plaintiff's claims—eg, when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law\n\n• all defendants consent to removal\n\n• the defendant files a notice of removal within 30 days of receiving the plaintiff's initial complaint or service of the summons (whichever period is shorter) and\n\n• a copy of the notice of removal is delivered to the state court and the other parties.\n\nA party can challenge removal by filing a motion to remand (ie, return) the case to state court. This motion must generally be filed in federal court within 30 days after the notice of removal is filed, but a motion based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction can be filed at any time. The burden is then on the party seeking removal to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that removal is proper. If there was a procedural error in the removal, the federal court should remand the entire case to state court.*\n\nHere, the woman sued her former employer in state court, and her attorney promptly removed the case to federal court. However, there was a procedural error in that removal since the woman is the plaintiff—not the defendant. Therefore, the federal court will likely remand the entire case. *The federal court clerk must mail a certified copy of the remand order to the state court clerk. The order may require payment of costs, including attorney's fees. But if a remand is based on procedural error (as in this case), attorney's fees can only be awarded if there was no objectively reasonable basis for removal. (Choice B) Had the federal court lacked subject-matter jurisdiction over some of the woman's claims (eg, her state claims), it would have remanded those claims but kept the removable claims (eg, her federal claims). (Choice C) Although the case may have been timely removed and the woman alleges federal claims, removal is improper because she is the plaintiff. (Choice D) A plaintiff cannot remove a case, even if there is a risk of bias and impropriety in having the case proceed before a hostile state-court judge. Educational objective: If a procedural error occurs in the removal of a case from state to federal court (eg, removal by the plaintiff), the federal court should remand the entire case. References\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1441, 1446 (removal of civil actions).","rule":"If a procedural error occurs in the removal of a case from state to federal court (eg, removal by the plaintiff), the federal court should remand the entire case.","irac":{"issue":"A woman sued her former employer in state court, asserting age and sex discrimination claims under both state and federal law. The woman's attorney had recently been embarrassed in court by the judge to whom the case was assigned. Wishing to avoid difficulties with the judge, the woman's attorney promptly removed the case to federal court on the basis of federal-question jurisdiction. The employer's attorney has timely moved to remand. How is the federal court likely to proceed?","rule":"If a procedural error occurs in the removal of a case from state to federal court (eg, removal by the plaintiff), the federal court should remand the entire case.","analysis":"Remand based on improper removal\n\nBasis Example Result\n\nNo subject- • No subject-matter jurisdiction • Remand entire case matter over any claim jurisdiction • No subject-matter jurisdiction • Retain removable over some claims claims\n• Sever & remand nonremovable claims Procedural error • Defendant filed notice of removal • Remand entire case more than 30 days after receiving complaint\n• Not all defendants consented to removal\n• Case removed by plaintiff (not defendant)\n• Notice not delivered to parties or state court\n• Removal violates forum-defendant rule A defendant can remove a case originally filed in state court to federal court if:\n• the federal court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the plaintiff's claims—eg, when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law\n\n• all defendants consent to removal\n\n• the defendant files a notice of removal within 30 days of receiving the plaintiff's initial complaint or service of the summons (whichever period is shorter) and\n\n• a copy of the notice of removal is delivered to the state court and the other parties.\n\nA party can challenge removal by filing a motion to remand (ie, return) the case to state court. This motion must generally be filed in federal court within 30 days after the notice of removal is filed, but a motion based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction can be filed at any time. The burden is then on the party seeking removal to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that removal is proper. If there was a procedural error in the removal, the federal court should remand the entire case to state court.*\n\nHere, the woman sued her former employer in state court, and her attorney promptly removed the case to federal court. However, there was a procedural error in that removal since the woman is the plaintiff—not the defendant. Therefore, the federal court will likely remand the entire case. *The federal court clerk must mail a certified copy of the remand order to the state court clerk. The order may require payment of costs, including attorney's fees. But if a remand is based on procedural error (as in this case), attorney's fees can only be awarded if there was no objectively reasonable basis for removal. (Choice B) Had the federal court lacked subject-matter jurisdiction over some of the woman's claims (eg, her state claims), it would have remanded those claims but kept the removable claims (eg, her federal claims). (Choice C) Although the case may have been timely removed and the woman alleges federal claims, removal is improper because she is the plaintiff. (Choice D) A plaintiff cannot remove a case, even if there is a risk of bias and impropriety in having the case proceed before a hostile state-court judge. Educational objective: If a procedural error occurs in the removal of a case from state to federal court (eg, removal by the plaintiff), the federal court should remand the entire case. References\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1441, 1446 (removal of civil actions).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1441, 1446 (removal of civil actions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[8],"sourceAnswerPages":[9,10],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在州法院起诉其前雇主，同时依据州法和联邦法主张年龄歧视及性别歧视。该女子的律师不久前曾被承办本案的法官在法庭上羞辱。为避免与该法官发生困难，女子的律师立即以联邦问题管辖权为依据，将案件移送至联邦法院。雇主的律师及时提出发回州法院的动议。联邦法院可能如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"将整个案件发回州法院。","B":"将州法诉请发回州法院，但保留联邦法诉请。","C":"保留该案，因为移送及时，且女子主张了联邦法诉请。","D":"保留该案，以避免案件由一名已对女子的律师表现出明显敌意的法官审理所产生的偏见及不当风险。"},"explanationZh":"因移送不当而发回：\n\n依据／示例／结果\n\n缺乏事项管辖权：\n• 对任何诉请均无事项管辖权——发回整个案件。\n• 仅对部分诉请无事项管辖权——保留可移送的诉请，并分离及发回不可移送的诉请。\n\n程序错误：\n• 被告在收到起诉状 30 天后才提交移送通知；\n• 并非所有被告均同意移送；\n• 案件由原告而非被告移送；\n• 移送通知未送交其他当事人或州法院；\n• 移送违反法院州被告规则。\n发生上述程序错误时，应发回整个案件。\n\n在以下条件下，被告可以把最初在州法院提起的案件移送至联邦法院：\n• 联邦法院对原告的诉请享有事项管辖权，例如诉请依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生；\n• 所有被告均同意移送；\n• 被告在收到原告最初起诉状或传票送达后 30 天内（以较短期间为准）提交移送通知；并且\n• 移送通知副本已送交州法院及其他当事人。\n\n当事人可以提出发回动议，质疑移送并请求案件退回州法院。该动议通常必须在提交移送通知后 30 天内向联邦法院提出，但以缺乏事项管辖权为由的动议可在任何时候提出。随后，寻求移送的一方负有依优势证据标准证明移送适当的责任。移送存在程序错误时，联邦法院应将整个案件发回州法院。*\n\n本题中，女子在州法院起诉其前雇主，随后由她的律师立即将案件移送至联邦法院。但是，女子是原告而非被告，故该移送存在程序错误。因此，联邦法院很可能把整个案件发回州法院。\n\n*联邦法院书记员必须将发回命令的核证副本邮寄给州法院书记员。命令可以要求支付费用，包括律师费；但如果发回是基于程序错误（如本题），只有在移送不存在客观合理依据时才可判给律师费。\n\n（选项 B）如果联邦法院仅对女子的部分诉请（例如州法诉请）缺乏事项管辖权，则应发回这些诉请，同时保留可移送的诉请（例如联邦法诉请）。\n\n（选项 C）即使案件移送及时且女子主张了联邦法诉请，移送仍不适当，因为她是原告。\n\n（选项 D）原告不能移送案件，即使由对其律师抱有敌意的州法院法官审理可能产生偏见和不当风险，也不例外。\n\n学习目标：案件从州法院移送至联邦法院时若发生程序错误（例如由原告移送），联邦法院应将整个案件发回州法院。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1441, 1446（民事诉讼的移送）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-004","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":4,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"An artist living in State A entered into a contract with a gallery from State A that allowed the gallery to display and sell the artist's paintings, with the artist to receive a commission for any paintings sold. Shortly thereafter, the artist moved to State B, where he joined a local artists' collective. The gallery sold several of the artist's paintings but refused to provide the commission the artist thought he was due under the contract. The artist sued the gallery in a federal court in State A for breach of contract and requested $100,000 in damages. The day after filing his complaint, the artist moved back to State A, claiming artistic differences with the artists' collective. The gallery filed an answer that did not include a defense of a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. The gallery then moved to dismiss the artist's complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the federal court most likely rule on the gallery's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the gallery waived its subject-matter jurisdiction defense by failing to include it in its answer.","B":"Deny the motion, because there is diversity of citizenship and the amount in controversy is $100,000.","C":"Grant the motion, because both parties are citizens of State A.","D":"Grant the motion, because state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over state-law claims."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A federal court can only hear the merits of a lawsuit if the court has subject-matter jurisdiction, which can arise under:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse). Citizenship is determined on the date that the plaintiff's complaint is filed with the court. Here, the artist's breach-of-contract suit against the gallery for $100,000 in damages satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement. And though both parties were State A citizens when they entered the contract and are currently State A citizens, the artist was a State B citizen when he filed the complaint. As a result, the parties were diverse when the filing occurred—satisfying the requirements for diversity jurisdiction (Choice C). The federal court therefore has subject-matter jurisdiction to hear this suit and will likely deny the gallery's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) Most defenses are waived if they are not asserted in a defendant's answer. But a defense based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and can be asserted by a party or raised by the court at any time—including after an answer has been filed. (Choice D) State and federal courts have concurrent (ie, shared) jurisdiction over most state-law claims. The only substantive areas over which state courts have exclusive jurisdiction are probate matters (eg, authenticating a will) and domestic relations (eg, issuing a divorce). Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction when (1) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and (2) the opposing parties are citizens of different states on the date the complaint is filed. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (requirements for diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group, Ltd. P'ship, 541 U.S. 567, 570–71 (2004) (holding that the citizenship of the parties in a lawsuit is determined at the time the suit is filed).","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction when (1) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and (2) the opposing parties are citizens of different states on the date the complaint is filed.","irac":{"issue":"An artist living in State A entered into a contract with a gallery from State A that allowed the gallery to display and sell the artist's paintings, with the artist to receive a commission for any paintings sold. Shortly thereafter, the artist moved to State B, where he joined a local artists' collective. The gallery sold several of the artist's paintings but refused to provide the commission the artist thought he was due under the contract. The artist sued the gallery in a federal court in State A for breach of contract and requested $100,000 in damages. The day after filing his complaint, the artist moved back to State A, claiming artistic differences with the artists' collective. The gallery filed an answer that did not include a defense of a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. The gallery then moved to dismiss the artist's complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the federal court most likely rule on the gallery's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction when (1) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and (2) the opposing parties are citizens of different states on the date the complaint is filed.","analysis":"A federal court can only hear the merits of a lawsuit if the court has subject-matter jurisdiction, which can arise under:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse). Citizenship is determined on the date that the plaintiff's complaint is filed with the court. Here, the artist's breach-of-contract suit against the gallery for $100,000 in damages satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement. And though both parties were State A citizens when they entered the contract and are currently State A citizens, the artist was a State B citizen when he filed the complaint. As a result, the parties were diverse when the filing occurred—satisfying the requirements for diversity jurisdiction (Choice C). The federal court therefore has subject-matter jurisdiction to hear this suit and will likely deny the gallery's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) Most defenses are waived if they are not asserted in a defendant's answer. But a defense based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and can be asserted by a party or raised by the court at any time—including after an answer has been filed. (Choice D) State and federal courts have concurrent (ie, shared) jurisdiction over most state-law claims. The only substantive areas over which state courts have exclusive jurisdiction are probate matters (eg, authenticating a will) and domestic relations (eg, issuing a divorce). Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction when (1) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and (2) the opposing parties are citizens of different states on the date the complaint is filed. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (requirements for diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group, Ltd. P'ship, 541 U.S. 567, 570–71 (2004) (holding that the citizenship of the parties in a lawsuit is determined at the time the suit is filed).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (requirements for diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group, Ltd. P'ship, 541 U.S. 567, 570–71 (2004) (holding that the citizenship of the parties in a lawsuit is determined at the time the suit is filed).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[11],"sourceAnswerPages":[12],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"居住在 A 州的一名艺术家与 A 州的一家画廊订立合同，允许画廊展出并销售其画作，艺术家可就售出的每幅画取得佣金。不久后，艺术家迁往 B 州，并加入当地的艺术家团体。画廊售出了数幅画作，却拒绝支付艺术家认为其依合同应得的佣金。艺术家在 A 州联邦法院以违约为由起诉画廊，索赔 100,000 美元。提交起诉状次日，艺术家以与该艺术家团体存在艺术理念分歧为由迁回 A 州。画廊提交答辩状，但未提出缺乏事项管辖权的抗辩，随后又以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回艺术家的起诉。联邦法院最可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为画廊未在答辩状中提出事项管辖权抗辩，已放弃该抗辩。","B":"驳回动议，因为双方具有不同州籍，且争议金额为 100,000 美元。","C":"批准动议，因为双方均为 A 州公民。","D":"批准动议，因为州法院对州法诉请享有专属管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院只有在享有事项管辖权时，才能审理诉讼实体。事项管辖权可以基于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——诉请依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题并非如此）；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方当事人具有不同州籍。\n\n当事人的州籍以原告向法院提交起诉状之日为准。本题中，艺术家就违约向画廊索赔 100,000 美元，满足争议金额要求。虽然双方在订立合同时均为 A 州公民，目前也均为 A 州公民，但艺术家提交起诉状时是 B 州公民。因此，提交起诉状时双方州籍不同，满足多元州籍管辖权的要求（选项 C）。联邦法院对该案享有事项管辖权，故很可能驳回画廊的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）多数抗辩若未在被告答辩状中提出即被放弃。但是，缺乏事项管辖权的抗辩永不因未提出而放弃，当事人或法院可在任何时候提出，包括答辩状提交之后。\n\n（选项 D）州法院和联邦法院对多数州法诉请享有并行管辖权。州法院享有专属管辖权的实体领域仅包括遗嘱认证事项（例如确认遗嘱真伪）和家庭关系事项（例如准予离婚）。\n\n学习目标：在以下情况下，多元州籍管辖权产生事项管辖权：(1) 争议金额超过 75,000 美元；且 (2) 在提交起诉状之日，对立双方当事人具有不同州籍。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权的要求）。\n• Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group, Ltd. P'ship, 541 U.S. 567, 570–71 (2004)（认定诉讼当事人的州籍以提起诉讼时为准）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-002"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-005","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":5,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A university student, a citizen of State A, believes that her university violated federal law when it eliminated funding for the women's varsity volleyball team. She has sued the university, a nonprofit corporation located and incorporated in State B, and the university's athletic director, a citizen of State B, in federal court in State B. What is the best method of serving the defendants?","choices":{"A":"Service as required by State B's rules of civil procedure.","B":"Service by a process server's leaving copies of the summons and complaint with secretaries in the respective offices of the athletic director and the university president.","C":"Service by emailing copies of the summons and complaint to the athletic director and the university president.","D":"Service by the student herself on the athletic director and the university president."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Methods of serving process in U.S.\n\nIndividual • Following state law where district court is located or where service is made\n• Personally serving defendant with summons & complaint\n• Leaving summons & complaint at defendant's dwelling with someone of suitable age & discretion who resides there\n• Delivering summons & complaint to defendant's authorized agent Organization • Following state law where district court is located or where service is made\n• Delivering summons & complaint to officer, managing or general agent, or authorized agent & mailing those documents to defendant if required by statute Due process prohibits a court from adjudicating a defendant's rights before the defendant has received proper notice of the plaintiff's complaint. A defendant receives proper notice when someone who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit serves the defendant with a summons and complaint. And under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4, any defendant (an individual or organization) can be served for a federal suit by following the state law for serving process in the state where the court is located or where service is made. Here, the student sued the university (organization) and its athletic director (individual) in federal court in State B. Since the defendants can be served under the civil procedure rules of State B (where the court is located), this is the best method of serving both defendants. (Choice B) Individuals and organizations can be served by delivering a copy of the summons and complaint to an agent authorized to receive service on the defendant's behalf. But since there is no indication that the secretaries were authorized agents of either defendant, this is not the best method for serving the defendants. (Choice C) Service of process by email is not expressly authorized under FRCP 4. Therefore, absent some indication that State B's rules allow this method of service, email is not the best method of serving the defendants. (Choice D) The student herself cannot serve process because she is a party to the lawsuit. However, any nonparty who is at least 18 years old can serve process. If service was not waived and occurred within the U.S., the process server must typically file a proof of service affidavit.* *Service in a foreign country can be proved (1) pursuant to an applicable treaty or convention, (2) by a receipt signed by the addressee, or (3) by other evidence that satisfies\n\nthe court. However, failure to prove service in the U.S. or abroad does not affect the validity of the service. Educational objective: Any defendant (individual or organization) can be served by (1) following the service-of-process laws of the state where the court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering a copy of the summons and complaint to an agent authorized to receive service on the defendant's behalf. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(c), (e), (h) (service of process upon an individual and an organization).","rule":"Any defendant (individual or organization) can be served by (1) following the service-of-process laws of the state where the court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering a copy of the summons and complaint to an agent authorized to receive service on the defendant's behalf.","irac":{"issue":"A university student, a citizen of State A, believes that her university violated federal law when it eliminated funding for the women's varsity volleyball team. She has sued the university, a nonprofit corporation located and incorporated in State B, and the university's athletic director, a citizen of State B, in federal court in State B. What is the best method of serving the defendants?","rule":"Any defendant (individual or organization) can be served by (1) following the service-of-process laws of the state where the court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering a copy of the summons and complaint to an agent authorized to receive service on the defendant's behalf.","analysis":"Methods of serving process in U.S.\n\nIndividual • Following state law where district court is located or where service is made\n• Personally serving defendant with summons & complaint\n• Leaving summons & complaint at defendant's dwelling with someone of suitable age & discretion who resides there\n• Delivering summons & complaint to defendant's authorized agent Organization • Following state law where district court is located or where service is made\n• Delivering summons & complaint to officer, managing or general agent, or authorized agent & mailing those documents to defendant if required by statute Due process prohibits a court from adjudicating a defendant's rights before the defendant has received proper notice of the plaintiff's complaint. A defendant receives proper notice when someone who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit serves the defendant with a summons and complaint. And under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4, any defendant (an individual or organization) can be served for a federal suit by following the state law for serving process in the state where the court is located or where service is made. Here, the student sued the university (organization) and its athletic director (individual) in federal court in State B. Since the defendants can be served under the civil procedure rules of State B (where the court is located), this is the best method of serving both defendants. (Choice B) Individuals and organizations can be served by delivering a copy of the summons and complaint to an agent authorized to receive service on the defendant's behalf. But since there is no indication that the secretaries were authorized agents of either defendant, this is not the best method for serving the defendants. (Choice C) Service of process by email is not expressly authorized under FRCP 4. Therefore, absent some indication that State B's rules allow this method of service, email is not the best method of serving the defendants. (Choice D) The student herself cannot serve process because she is a party to the lawsuit. However, any nonparty who is at least 18 years old can serve process. If service was not waived and occurred within the U.S., the process server must typically file a proof of service affidavit.* *Service in a foreign country can be proved (1) pursuant to an applicable treaty or convention, (2) by a receipt signed by the addressee, or (3) by other evidence that satisfies\n\nthe court. However, failure to prove service in the U.S. or abroad does not affect the validity of the service. Educational objective: Any defendant (individual or organization) can be served by (1) following the service-of-process laws of the state where the court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering a copy of the summons and complaint to an agent authorized to receive service on the defendant's behalf. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(c), (e), (h) (service of process upon an individual and an organization).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(c), (e), (h) (service of process upon an individual and an organization).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[14],"sourceAnswerPages":[15,16],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 A 州公民的大学生认为，其大学取消女子校队排球队经费的行为违反联邦法律。她在 B 州联邦法院起诉了该大学和大学体育主任。大学是一家位于 B 州并在 B 州注册成立的非营利公司，体育主任是 B 州公民。向两名被告送达诉讼文书的最佳方法是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"依照 B 州民事诉讼规则要求的方式送达。","B":"由送达人将传票和起诉状副本留给体育主任办公室和大学校长办公室各自的秘书。","C":"通过电子邮件将传票和起诉状副本发送给体育主任和大学校长。","D":"由该学生本人向体育主任和大学校长送达。"},"explanationZh":"美国境内诉讼文书的送达方式：\n\n个人：\n• 遵循联邦地区法院所在地州或实际送达地州的法律；\n• 亲自向被告送达传票和起诉状；\n• 将传票和起诉状留在被告住所，交给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人；\n• 将传票和起诉状交付给被告授权的代理人。\n\n组织：\n• 遵循联邦地区法院所在地州或实际送达地州的法律；\n• 将传票和起诉状交付给该组织的高级职员、管理或一般代理人、或获授权代理人；若法律要求，还应将文件邮寄给被告。\n\n正当程序禁止法院在被告获得原告起诉的适当通知之前裁判其权利。由年满 18 岁且不是诉讼当事人的人向被告送达传票和起诉状，即构成适当通知。根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 4 条，联邦诉讼中的任何被告（个人或组织）均可依照法院所在地州或实际送达地州的送达法律接受送达。\n\n本题中，学生在 B 州联邦法院起诉大学（组织）及体育主任（个人）。两名被告均可依法院所在地 B 州的民事诉讼规则接受送达，因此，这是向二者送达的最佳方法。\n\n（选项 B）可以向获授权代表被告接受送达的代理人交付传票和起诉状副本，从而向个人或组织送达。但题目没有表明这些秘书是任一被告的获授权代理人，所以这不是最佳方法。\n\n（选项 C）《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 4 条未明确允许电子邮件送达。因此，在没有信息表明 B 州规则允许该方式时，电子邮件不是最佳送达方法。\n\n（选项 D）该学生是诉讼当事人，不能亲自送达。但是，任何年满 18 岁的非当事人均可送达。若送达未获放弃且在美国境内完成，送达人通常必须提交送达证明宣誓书。*\n\n*在外国完成的送达，可通过下列方式证明：(1) 适用的条约或公约所规定的方式；(2) 收件人签署的收据；或 (3) 令法院满意的其他证据。不过，无论在美国境内还是境外，未能证明送达均不影响送达本身的效力。\n\n学习目标：任何被告（个人或组织）均可通过以下方式接受送达：(1) 遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州的诉讼文书送达法律；或 (2) 将传票和起诉状副本交付给获授权代表被告接受送达的代理人。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(c), (e), (h)（对个人及组织送达诉讼文书）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-006","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":6,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A consumer domiciled in State A sued a manufacturer and a retailer in a federal district court in State B for strict products liability. The complaint alleged that the manufacturer and the retailer are jointly and severally liable for $70,000 and $30,000 in damages, respectively, for harm caused by a defective kitchen appliance manufactured and sold by the defendants. The manufacturer and the retailer are both incorporated and headquartered in State B. The retailer has asserted a $50,000 crossclaim against the manufacturer for breach of contract. The retailer claims that the manufacturer sent the retailer nonconforming power tools in its last shipment. The manufacturer has moved to dismiss the crossclaim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the manufacturer's motion be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because there is diversity of citizenship between the consumer and the manufacturer.","B":"No, because there is supplemental jurisdiction over the crossclaim.","C":"Yes, because the compulsory crossclaim failed to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement.","D":"Yes, because the crossclaim is unrelated to the consumer's claims."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal district courts have original subject-matter jurisdiction to hear disputes that arise from:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nTo meet the amount-in-controversy requirement, a single plaintiff may only aggregate (ie, combine) claims against multiple defendants if the plaintiff alleges that they are jointly liable. Here, the consumer (State A citizen) is diverse from the retailer and the manufacturer (State B citizens). And since the consumer seeks a total of $100,000 from the defendants ($70,000 + $30,000) under joint and several liability, diversity jurisdiction exists over the consumer's strict products liability claims.* However, no diversity jurisdiction exists over the retailer's crossclaim against the manufacturer since they are both State B citizens and only $50,000 is in controversy. When a dispute involves multiple claims and one falls outside of the court's original jurisdiction, the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over that claim. However, the court can only do so if the claims are so related that they share a common nucleus of operative facts. Here, the consumer's claim concerning a kitchen appliance is unrelated to the retailer's crossclaim concerning power tools, so the court lacks supplemental jurisdiction over the crossclaim (Choice B). Therefore, the court should grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss it. *The consumer's strict products liability claim constitutes two claims because it is asserted against two separate entities. The defendants can only be held jointly liable for the total damages if the elements of strict products liability are established against both defendants. (Choice A) Diverse citizenship between the consumer and the manufacturer does not change the lack of diversity between the retailer and the manufacturer or create supplemental jurisdiction over the retailer's crossclaim against the manufacturer. (Choice C) Crossclaims are permissive and can be asserted only if they arise from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim (not seen here). Had the retailer's crossclaim done so, its amount in controversy would be irrelevant since supplemental jurisdiction would exist. Educational objective:\n\nA federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim outside of the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with an original-jurisdiction claim. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• United Mine Workers v. Gibbs, 383 U.S. 715, 725 (1966) (establishing that a supplemental claim must derive from a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction).","rule":"A federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim outside of the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with an original-jurisdiction claim.","irac":{"issue":"A consumer domiciled in State A sued a manufacturer and a retailer in a federal district court in State B for strict products liability. The complaint alleged that the manufacturer and the retailer are jointly and severally liable for $70,000 and $30,000 in damages, respectively, for harm caused by a defective kitchen appliance manufactured and sold by the defendants. The manufacturer and the retailer are both incorporated and headquartered in State B. The retailer has asserted a $50,000 crossclaim against the manufacturer for breach of contract. The retailer claims that the manufacturer sent the retailer nonconforming power tools in its last shipment. The manufacturer has moved to dismiss the crossclaim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the manufacturer's motion be granted?","rule":"A federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim outside of the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with an original-jurisdiction claim.","analysis":"Federal district courts have original subject-matter jurisdiction to hear disputes that arise from:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nTo meet the amount-in-controversy requirement, a single plaintiff may only aggregate (ie, combine) claims against multiple defendants if the plaintiff alleges that they are jointly liable. Here, the consumer (State A citizen) is diverse from the retailer and the manufacturer (State B citizens). And since the consumer seeks a total of $100,000 from the defendants ($70,000 + $30,000) under joint and several liability, diversity jurisdiction exists over the consumer's strict products liability claims.* However, no diversity jurisdiction exists over the retailer's crossclaim against the manufacturer since they are both State B citizens and only $50,000 is in controversy. When a dispute involves multiple claims and one falls outside of the court's original jurisdiction, the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over that claim. However, the court can only do so if the claims are so related that they share a common nucleus of operative facts. Here, the consumer's claim concerning a kitchen appliance is unrelated to the retailer's crossclaim concerning power tools, so the court lacks supplemental jurisdiction over the crossclaim (Choice B). Therefore, the court should grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss it. *The consumer's strict products liability claim constitutes two claims because it is asserted against two separate entities. The defendants can only be held jointly liable for the total damages if the elements of strict products liability are established against both defendants. (Choice A) Diverse citizenship between the consumer and the manufacturer does not change the lack of diversity between the retailer and the manufacturer or create supplemental jurisdiction over the retailer's crossclaim against the manufacturer. (Choice C) Crossclaims are permissive and can be asserted only if they arise from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim (not seen here). Had the retailer's crossclaim done so, its amount in controversy would be irrelevant since supplemental jurisdiction would exist. Educational objective:\n\nA federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim outside of the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with an original-jurisdiction claim. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• United Mine Workers v. Gibbs, 383 U.S. 715, 725 (1966) (establishing that a supplemental claim must derive from a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• United Mine Workers v. Gibbs, 383 U.S. 715, 725 (1966) (establishing that a supplemental claim must derive from a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[17],"sourceAnswerPages":[18,19],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"居住于 A 州的一名消费者，在 B 州联邦地区法院以严格产品责任为由起诉一家制造商和一家零售商。起诉状主张，制造商和零售商应分别就其制造及销售的有缺陷厨房电器造成的损害承担 70,000 美元和 30,000 美元的连带责任。制造商和零售商均在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州。零售商又以违约为由，对制造商提出 50,000 美元的交叉请求，主张制造商在最近一次发货中向其交付了不符合约定的电动工具。制造商以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回该交叉请求。是否应批准制造商的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为消费者与制造商之间具有不同州籍。","B":"不应，因为法院对该交叉请求享有补充管辖权。","C":"应当，因为该强制性交叉请求未满足争议金额要求。","D":"应当，因为该交叉请求与消费者的诉请无关。"},"explanationZh":"联邦地区法院对基于以下情形产生的争议享有原始事项管辖权：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——争议依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题并非如此）；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方当事人具有不同州籍，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n为满足争议金额要求，单一原告只有在主张多个被告承担共同责任时，才可以合并其针对这些被告的请求。本题中，消费者（A 州公民）与零售商及制造商（均为 B 州公民）州籍不同。消费者以连带责任为依据向两被告共索赔 100,000 美元（70,000 美元加 30,000 美元），因此，法院对消费者的严格产品责任请求享有多元州籍管辖权。*\n\n但是，法院对零售商针对制造商的交叉请求不享有多元州籍管辖权，因为二者均为 B 州公民，且争议金额仅为 50,000 美元。争议包含多个请求且其中一个不属于法院原始管辖权时，法院可以对该请求行使补充管辖权；但只有在各请求相互关联、共享同一核心争议事实时，才可如此。\n\n本题中，消费者关于厨房电器的诉请与零售商关于电动工具的交叉请求无关，故法院对该交叉请求不享有补充管辖权（选项 B）。因此，法院应批准制造商的动议，驳回该交叉请求。\n\n*消费者的严格产品责任主张构成两个请求，因为它分别针对两个不同实体提出。只有在对两个被告均能证明严格产品责任的全部要件时，二者才可能对全部损害承担共同责任。\n\n（选项 A）消费者与制造商州籍不同，并不能改变零售商与制造商州籍相同的事实，也不会使法院对零售商针对制造商的交叉请求产生补充管辖权。\n\n（选项 C）交叉请求属于任意性请求，且只有在它与诉讼中的另一请求源于同一交易或事件时才可提出（本题并非如此）。如果该交叉请求确实源于同一交易或事件，法院将享有补充管辖权，其争议金额便无关紧要。\n\n学习目标：对于不属于联邦法院原始事项管辖权的请求，如果该请求与原始管辖权范围内的请求共享同一核心争议事实，联邦法院可以对其行使补充管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367（补充管辖权）。\n• United Mine Workers v. Gibbs, 383 U.S. 715, 725 (1966)（确立补充请求必须与原始事项管辖权内的请求源于同一核心争议事实）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-003"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-007","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":7,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"With the advice and consent of the Senate, the President entered into a self-executing treaty with a foreign country. The treaty provided that citizens of both nations were required to pay whatever tort damages were awarded against them by a court of either nation. A man and a woman who were U.S. citizens and residents of the same state were traveling separately in the foreign country when their cars collided. The foreign court awarded the woman a judgment for $500,000 in damages for her injuries from the accident. The woman filed suit against the man in federal district court in their home state to enforce the judgment. The man filed a motion to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction. Should the court grant the motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the case falls within the federal question jurisdiction of the court.","B":"No, because the treaty power is plenary and not subject to judicial review.","C":"Yes, because the citizenship of the parties is not diverse.","D":"Yes, because the traffic accident was a noncommercial transaction outside interstate commerce."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction\n\n(a court's authority to hear a particular type of case)\n\nFederal-question • Case arises under the Constitution, federal law, or jurisdiction federal treaty\n\nDiversity jurisdiction • Amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 (exclusive of interest & costs) and\n• Parties are citizens of different states A federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction (ie, authority to hear a case) arises under either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states.\n\nHere, the woman filed suit in federal district court to enforce the foreign court's judgment against the man, which he was required to pay pursuant to a valid treaty. Since the case falls within the court's federal-question jurisdiction, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction to hear it. Therefore, the court should deny the man's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) The President has plenary (ie, exclusive) authority to enter into treaties with the Senate's advice and consent. But federal courts have the power to decide cases that arise under such treaties (as seen here). (Choice C) The district court lacks diversity jurisdiction because the parties are citizens of the same state. But the court nevertheless has federal-question jurisdiction because the case arises under a treaty. (Choice D) The accident's relationship to interstate commerce is relevant to Congress's legislative power under the Article I commerce clause—but not to the jurisdiction of federal courts pursuant to Article III. Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction can stem from federal-question jurisdiction (ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty) OR diversity jurisdiction (ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction can stem from federal-question jurisdiction (ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty) OR diversity jurisdiction (ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states).","irac":{"issue":"With the advice and consent of the Senate, the President entered into a self-executing treaty with a foreign country. The treaty provided that citizens of both nations were required to pay whatever tort damages were awarded against them by a court of either nation. A man and a woman who were U.S. citizens and residents of the same state were traveling separately in the foreign country when their cars collided. The foreign court awarded the woman a judgment for $500,000 in damages for her injuries from the accident. The woman filed suit against the man in federal district court in their home state to enforce the judgment. The man filed a motion to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction. Should the court grant the motion to dismiss?","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction can stem from federal-question jurisdiction (ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty) OR diversity jurisdiction (ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states).","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction\n\n(a court's authority to hear a particular type of case)\n\nFederal-question • Case arises under the Constitution, federal law, or jurisdiction federal treaty\n\nDiversity jurisdiction • Amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 (exclusive of interest & costs) and\n• Parties are citizens of different states A federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction (ie, authority to hear a case) arises under either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states.\n\nHere, the woman filed suit in federal district court to enforce the foreign court's judgment against the man, which he was required to pay pursuant to a valid treaty. Since the case falls within the court's federal-question jurisdiction, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction to hear it. Therefore, the court should deny the man's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) The President has plenary (ie, exclusive) authority to enter into treaties with the Senate's advice and consent. But federal courts have the power to decide cases that arise under such treaties (as seen here). (Choice C) The district court lacks diversity jurisdiction because the parties are citizens of the same state. But the court nevertheless has federal-question jurisdiction because the case arises under a treaty. (Choice D) The accident's relationship to interstate commerce is relevant to Congress's legislative power under the Article I commerce clause—but not to the jurisdiction of federal courts pursuant to Article III. Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction can stem from federal-question jurisdiction (ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty) OR diversity jurisdiction (ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[20],"sourceAnswerPages":[21,22],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"总统在参议院建议并同意后，与某外国缔结了一项可自行执行的条约。条约规定，两国公民必须支付由任一国法院判令其承担的任何侵权损害赔偿。一名男子与一名女子均为美国公民，并居住在同一州；二人在该外国分别驾车旅行时发生碰撞。外国法院就女子在事故中所受伤害判给其 500,000 美元损害赔偿。女子在双方本州的联邦地区法院起诉男子，请求执行该判决。男子以缺乏管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准该驳回动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为该案属于法院的联邦问题管辖权。","B":"不应，因为缔约权是完整且排他的，不受司法审查。","C":"应当，因为双方当事人不具有不同州籍。","D":"应当，因为该交通事故属于州际商业以外的非商业交易。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权（法院审理某一类型案件的权限）：\n\n联邦问题管辖权：案件依据宪法、联邦法律或联邦条约产生。\n\n多元州籍管辖权：争议金额超过 75,000 美元（不含利息及费用），且双方当事人是不同州的公民。\n\n联邦法院的事项管辖权可以基于以下任一情形产生：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——案件依据宪法、联邦法律或条约产生；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且双方当事人是不同州的公民。\n\n本题中，女子向联邦地区法院起诉，请求执行外国法院针对男子作出的判决；依有效条约，男子必须支付该判决。该案属于法院的联邦问题管辖权，因此法院享有审理该案的事项管辖权。故法院应驳回男子的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 B）总统在参议院建议并同意后，享有完整且排他的缔约权。但联邦法院有权裁判依据此类条约产生的案件，本题即属此类。\n\n（选项 C）由于双方当事人均为同一州公民，地区法院不享有多元州籍管辖权。但是，该案依据条约产生，法院仍享有联邦问题管辖权。\n\n（选项 D）该事故与州际商业的关系涉及国会依据宪法第一条商业条款所享有的立法权，而不涉及联邦法院依据宪法第三条所享有的管辖权。\n\n学习目标：事项管辖权可以源于联邦问题管辖权（即案件依据宪法、联邦法律或条约产生），也可以源于多元州籍管辖权（即争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且双方当事人是不同州的公民）。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331（联邦问题管辖权）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-008","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":8,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A man domiciled in State A died from complications during surgery at a hospital incorporated and with its principal place of business in State A. The medical license of the surgeon performing the operation was suspended at the time the surgery was performed. The man's legal representative, who was domiciled in State B, sued the hospital on behalf of the man's estate in a state court in State B. The complaint asserted a negligence claim to recover $60,000 and a breach-of-contract claim to recover $40,000. The hospital removed the case to a federal court in State B. The representative filed a motion to remand. Under State B law, claims for negligence and breach of contract survive the death of a person. How is the federal court likely to proceed?","choices":{"A":"Remand the case, because the amount-in-controversy requirement is not satisfied.","B":"Remand the case, because the parties are not diverse.","C":"Retain the case, because the case was properly removed to federal court.","D":"Retain the case, because the federal court has supplemental jurisdiction over the claims."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A defendant has the power to remove a case from state court to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. If such jurisdiction does not exist, the federal court must remand the case back to state court. Original jurisdiction can be established through federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction, which requires:\n• an amount in controversy exceeding $75,000 and\n• complete diversity of citizenship between the opposing parties. Citizenship is usually determined by the domiciles of the named parties to the suit. However, the citizenship of a legal representative is determined by the domicile of the minor, legally incompetent person, or decedent on whose behalf the legal representative acts. Here, the deceased man's legal representative sued the hospital on behalf of the man's estate in state court. The hospital removed the case to federal court. The representative is a citizen of State A because that is where the man was domiciled. The hospital is also a citizen of State A because it is incorporated and has its principal place of business there. Since the opposing parties are both State A citizens, the court lacks diversity (and subject-matter) jurisdiction. Removal was therefore improper, and the federal court will likely remand the case (Choice C). (Choice A) To satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement, a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) claims asserted against a single defendant. Here, the legal representative's complaint satisfies this requirement because the aggregated claims amount to $100,000 ($60,000 for negligence + $40,000 for breach of contract). (Choice D) When a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist over the remaining claims. Here, no claim satisfies original subject-matter jurisdiction, so supplemental jurisdiction is irrelevant. Educational objective: For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, the citizenship of a legal representative is determined by the domicile of the minor, legally incompetent person, or decedent on whose behalf the legal representative acts. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(2) (citizenship of legal representative).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(c) (remand requirements).","rule":"For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, the citizenship of a legal representative is determined by the domicile of the minor, legally incompetent person, or decedent on whose behalf the legal representative acts.","irac":{"issue":"A man domiciled in State A died from complications during surgery at a hospital incorporated and with its principal place of business in State A. The medical license of the surgeon performing the operation was suspended at the time the surgery was performed. The man's legal representative, who was domiciled in State B, sued the hospital on behalf of the man's estate in a state court in State B. The complaint asserted a negligence claim to recover $60,000 and a breach-of-contract claim to recover $40,000. The hospital removed the case to a federal court in State B. The representative filed a motion to remand. Under State B law, claims for negligence and breach of contract survive the death of a person. How is the federal court likely to proceed?","rule":"For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, the citizenship of a legal representative is determined by the domicile of the minor, legally incompetent person, or decedent on whose behalf the legal representative acts.","analysis":"A defendant has the power to remove a case from state court to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. If such jurisdiction does not exist, the federal court must remand the case back to state court. Original jurisdiction can be established through federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction, which requires:\n• an amount in controversy exceeding $75,000 and\n• complete diversity of citizenship between the opposing parties. Citizenship is usually determined by the domiciles of the named parties to the suit. However, the citizenship of a legal representative is determined by the domicile of the minor, legally incompetent person, or decedent on whose behalf the legal representative acts. Here, the deceased man's legal representative sued the hospital on behalf of the man's estate in state court. The hospital removed the case to federal court. The representative is a citizen of State A because that is where the man was domiciled. The hospital is also a citizen of State A because it is incorporated and has its principal place of business there. Since the opposing parties are both State A citizens, the court lacks diversity (and subject-matter) jurisdiction. Removal was therefore improper, and the federal court will likely remand the case (Choice C). (Choice A) To satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement, a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) claims asserted against a single defendant. Here, the legal representative's complaint satisfies this requirement because the aggregated claims amount to $100,000 ($60,000 for negligence + $40,000 for breach of contract). (Choice D) When a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist over the remaining claims. Here, no claim satisfies original subject-matter jurisdiction, so supplemental jurisdiction is irrelevant. Educational objective: For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, the citizenship of a legal representative is determined by the domicile of the minor, legally incompetent person, or decedent on whose behalf the legal representative acts. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(2) (citizenship of legal representative).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(c) (remand requirements).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(2) (citizenship of legal representative).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(c) (remand requirements).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[23],"sourceAnswerPages":[24,25],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名居住于 A 州的男子在一家医院接受手术时因并发症死亡。该医院在 A 州注册成立，主要营业地亦在 A 州。手术进行时，主刀医生的行医执照处于暂停状态。该男子的遗产法律代表居住于 B 州，其代表男子的遗产在 B 州州法院起诉医院。起诉状提出过失请求，索赔 60,000 美元，并提出违约请求，索赔 40,000 美元。医院将案件移送至 B 州联邦法院。法律代表提出发回州法院的动议。根据 B 州法律，过失请求和违约请求在个人死亡后仍继续存在。联邦法院可能如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"发回案件，因为未满足争议金额要求。","B":"发回案件，因为双方当事人不具有不同州籍。","C":"保留案件，因为案件已被适当地移送至联邦法院。","D":"保留案件，因为联邦法院对这些请求享有补充管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"如果案件属于联邦法院的原始事项管辖权，被告有权将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。若不存在此种管辖权，联邦法院必须将案件发回州法院。原始管辖权可以通过联邦问题管辖权（本题不存在）或多元州籍管辖权建立。后者要求：\n• 争议金额超过 75,000 美元；并且\n• 对立双方当事人具有完全不同的州籍。\n\n州籍通常依诉讼中列名当事人的住所确定。但是，法律代表的州籍依其所代表的未成年人、无民事行为能力人或死者的住所确定。\n\n本题中，死者的法律代表代表男子的遗产在州法院起诉医院，医院将案件移送至联邦法院。该法律代表在多元州籍管辖权意义上是 A 州公民，因为死者居住于 A 州。医院也属于 A 州公民，因为其在 A 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 A 州。由于对立双方均为 A 州公民，法院不享有多元州籍管辖权，因而也不享有事项管辖权。移送不当，联邦法院很可能发回案件（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）为满足争议金额要求，单一原告可以合并其针对同一被告提出的多个请求。本题中，法律代表可将 60,000 美元的过失请求与 40,000 美元的违约请求合并计算，共计 100,000 美元，满足该要求。\n\n（选项 D）争议包含多个请求而仅部分请求属于法院原始事项管辖权时，法院可能对其余请求享有补充管辖权。本题没有任何请求满足原始事项管辖权要求，因此补充管辖权并不相关。\n\n学习目标：就多元州籍管辖权而言，法律代表的州籍依其所代表的未成年人、无民事行为能力人或死者的住所确定。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(c)(2)（法律代表的州籍）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441（移送要求）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(c)（发回要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-009","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":9,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A shoe company was incorporated in State A, maintains its headquarters in State B, and stores most of its inventory in State C. At a sales convention in a foreign country, one of the shoe company's corporate officers allegedly assaulted an employee of a shoe distributor. This employee is domiciled in State C. The employee sued the shoe company in a federal court in State C for the actions of its corporate officer. The shoe company filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Will the court likely grant the shoe company's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the shoe company has minimum contacts with State C.","B":"No, because the shoe company is domiciled in State C.","C":"Yes, because no state has personal jurisdiction over this claim since it is based on events that occurred in a foreign country.","D":"Yes, because the employee's claim does not arise out of the shoe company's contacts with State C and the shoe company is not domiciled in State C."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A court must have personal jurisdiction—ie, authority over the parties (or property) before it—to hear a dispute. Personal jurisdiction can be established through specific jurisdiction, which exists when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. These requirements are met if the defendant's contacts with the forum state were so purposeful and substantial that the defendant could have reasonably anticipated being sued in that state (ie, purposeful availment). Here, the State C federal court lacks specific jurisdiction because the employee's claim arises from the corporate officer's actions in the foreign country—not the company's minimum contacts (storing inventory) with State C (Choice A). However, personal jurisdiction can also be established through general jurisdiction. This exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" in that state. Home is where the defendant is domiciled. For a corporation, this is (1) the state where it was incorporated and (2) the state where it maintains its principal place of business. Here, the shoe company was incorporated in State A and has its principal place of business in State B. And though the company conducts business and stores inventory in State C, this is not where the company is domiciled (Choice B). Therefore, the State C federal court lacks general jurisdiction over the company and will likely grant its motion to dismiss. (Choice C) No state has specific jurisdiction over this claim because it is based on events that occurred in the foreign country. But States A and B would have general jurisdiction over this claim since the shoe company is domiciled there. Educational objective: Specific jurisdiction exists when the claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's contacts with the forum state such that the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice. General jurisdiction exists when the defendant's contacts with the forum state make the defendant \"at home\" there. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic contacts required for general jurisdiction).","rule":"Specific jurisdiction exists when the claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's contacts with the forum state such that the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice. General jurisdiction exists when the defendant's contacts with the forum state make the defendant \"at home\" there.","irac":{"issue":"A shoe company was incorporated in State A, maintains its headquarters in State B, and stores most of its inventory in State C. At a sales convention in a foreign country, one of the shoe company's corporate officers allegedly assaulted an employee of a shoe distributor. This employee is domiciled in State C. The employee sued the shoe company in a federal court in State C for the actions of its corporate officer. The shoe company filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Will the court likely grant the shoe company's motion?","rule":"Specific jurisdiction exists when the claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's contacts with the forum state such that the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice. General jurisdiction exists when the defendant's contacts with the forum state make the defendant \"at home\" there.","analysis":"A court must have personal jurisdiction—ie, authority over the parties (or property) before it—to hear a dispute. Personal jurisdiction can be established through specific jurisdiction, which exists when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. These requirements are met if the defendant's contacts with the forum state were so purposeful and substantial that the defendant could have reasonably anticipated being sued in that state (ie, purposeful availment). Here, the State C federal court lacks specific jurisdiction because the employee's claim arises from the corporate officer's actions in the foreign country—not the company's minimum contacts (storing inventory) with State C (Choice A). However, personal jurisdiction can also be established through general jurisdiction. This exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" in that state. Home is where the defendant is domiciled. For a corporation, this is (1) the state where it was incorporated and (2) the state where it maintains its principal place of business. Here, the shoe company was incorporated in State A and has its principal place of business in State B. And though the company conducts business and stores inventory in State C, this is not where the company is domiciled (Choice B). Therefore, the State C federal court lacks general jurisdiction over the company and will likely grant its motion to dismiss. (Choice C) No state has specific jurisdiction over this claim because it is based on events that occurred in the foreign country. But States A and B would have general jurisdiction over this claim since the shoe company is domiciled there. Educational objective: Specific jurisdiction exists when the claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's contacts with the forum state such that the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice. General jurisdiction exists when the defendant's contacts with the forum state make the defendant \"at home\" there. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic contacts required for general jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic contacts required for general jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[26],"sourceAnswerPages":[27,28],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家鞋业公司在 A 州注册成立，总部设于 B 州，并将大部分库存存放在 C 州。在某外国举行的一次销售大会上，该鞋业公司的一名高级职员据称殴打了一家鞋类经销商的雇员。该雇员居住于 C 州。雇员就该高级职员的行为，在 C 州联邦法院起诉鞋业公司。鞋业公司以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否可能批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为鞋业公司与 C 州存在最低限度联系。","B":"不会，因为鞋业公司的住所位于 C 州。","C":"会，因为该请求依据发生在外国的事件提出，所以没有任何州对其享有对人管辖权。","D":"会，因为雇员的请求并非源于鞋业公司与 C 州的联系，且鞋业公司的住所不在 C 州。"},"explanationZh":"法院在审理争议前必须对诉讼当事人（或财产）享有对人管辖权。对人管辖权可以通过特定管辖权建立。特定管辖权存在于以下情形：\n• 原告的请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系；并且\n• 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念。\n\n如果被告与法院州的联系具有充分的目的性和实质性，使其能够合理预见自己会在该州被起诉，即构成有目的地利用该州利益，上述要求即得到满足。\n\n本题中，雇员的请求源于公司高级职员在外国的行为，而不是公司与 C 州之间存放库存这一最低限度联系（选项 A），因此 C 州联邦法院不享有特定管辖权。\n\n不过，对人管辖权还可以通过一般管辖权建立。被告与法院州存在持续、系统且极为实质的联系，使被告实质上“以该州为家”时，一般管辖权即存在。“家”是指被告的住所。对公司而言，住所包括：(1) 公司注册成立州；以及 (2) 公司主要营业地所在州。\n\n本题中，鞋业公司在 A 州注册成立，主要营业地位于 B 州。虽然公司在 C 州经营业务并存放库存，C 州并非公司的住所（选项 B）。因此，C 州联邦法院对公司不享有一般管辖权，很可能批准其驳回动议。\n\n（选项 C）由于该请求基于发生在外国的事件，没有任何州对此请求享有特定管辖权。但是，鞋业公司的住所在 A 州及 B 州，因此两州对该请求享有一般管辖权。\n\n学习目标：当请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的联系，并且行使对人管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义时，特定管辖权存在。当被告与法院州的联系使其“以该州为家”时，一般管辖权存在。\n\n参考资料：\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017)（说明特定管辖权的要求）。\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011)（讨论一般管辖权所要求的持续且系统的联系）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-004"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-010","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":10,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A singer, a citizen of State A, believes that her former record label violated federal law when it sold the rights to her songs to another company. The singer has brought a suit in federal court in State A against the record label, which is headquartered and incorporated in a foreign country, and the record label's president, who resides in the foreign country. What is the best method of serving the defendants?","choices":{"A":"Service as required by State A's rules of civil procedure.","B":"Service by having a process server personally deliver copies of the summons and complaint to the record label's headquarters and its president.","C":"Service by mailing copies of the summons and complaint to the record label and its president.","D":"Service pursuant to an international agreement that is reasonably calculated to give notice."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Methods of serving process in foreign country\n\nIndividual or • Following international agreement reasonably calculated to organization give notice\n• If no international agreement & not prohibited by federal law: – following foreign country's rules – as foreign country directs in response to letter rogatory or – unless prohibited by foreign country, by personal delivery* or mail with request for signed return receipt\n• Following court order, unless prohibited by international agreement Foreign • Following special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign government state\n• If no special arrangement, by international convention\n• If no convention, by mail to head of foreign affairs ministry with request for signed return receipt\n• If service by mail cannot be made within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt *Organizations cannot be served by personal delivery in foreign countries.\n\nDue process requires that a plaintiff notify the defendant of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of the summons and complaint. Individuals and organizations located in a foreign country can be served with process by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice under the circumstances. Therefore, the singer's best method of serving the foreign record label and its president is service pursuant to an applicable international agreement that meets that standard. (Choice A) An individual or organization can be served with process in the U.S. as required by the rules of civil procedure of the forum state—ie, the state in which the court is located. However, this is not a proper method to serve an individual or organization in a foreign country (as seen here). (Choice B) An individual in a foreign country can generally be served with process by having a process server personally deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the individual. However, organizations located abroad cannot be served in this manner.\n\n(Choice C) A plaintiff can generally serve process on individuals and organizations in foreign countries through mail requiring a signed receipt (eg, certified mail), but only when there is no internationally agreed means of service. Educational objective: Individuals and organizations in foreign countries can be served with process by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) & (h)(2) (service of process on individuals and organizations in foreign countries).\n\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1134 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining how process can be served on individuals and organizations located abroad).","rule":"Individuals and organizations in foreign countries can be served with process by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice.","irac":{"issue":"A singer, a citizen of State A, believes that her former record label violated federal law when it sold the rights to her songs to another company. The singer has brought a suit in federal court in State A against the record label, which is headquartered and incorporated in a foreign country, and the record label's president, who resides in the foreign country. What is the best method of serving the defendants?","rule":"Individuals and organizations in foreign countries can be served with process by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice.","analysis":"Methods of serving process in foreign country\n\nIndividual or • Following international agreement reasonably calculated to organization give notice\n• If no international agreement & not prohibited by federal law: – following foreign country's rules – as foreign country directs in response to letter rogatory or – unless prohibited by foreign country, by personal delivery* or mail with request for signed return receipt\n• Following court order, unless prohibited by international agreement Foreign • Following special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign government state\n• If no special arrangement, by international convention\n• If no convention, by mail to head of foreign affairs ministry with request for signed return receipt\n• If service by mail cannot be made within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt *Organizations cannot be served by personal delivery in foreign countries.\n\nDue process requires that a plaintiff notify the defendant of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of the summons and complaint. Individuals and organizations located in a foreign country can be served with process by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice under the circumstances. Therefore, the singer's best method of serving the foreign record label and its president is service pursuant to an applicable international agreement that meets that standard. (Choice A) An individual or organization can be served with process in the U.S. as required by the rules of civil procedure of the forum state—ie, the state in which the court is located. However, this is not a proper method to serve an individual or organization in a foreign country (as seen here). (Choice B) An individual in a foreign country can generally be served with process by having a process server personally deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the individual. However, organizations located abroad cannot be served in this manner.\n\n(Choice C) A plaintiff can generally serve process on individuals and organizations in foreign countries through mail requiring a signed receipt (eg, certified mail), but only when there is no internationally agreed means of service. Educational objective: Individuals and organizations in foreign countries can be served with process by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) & (h)(2) (service of process on individuals and organizations in foreign countries).\n\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1134 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining how process can be served on individuals and organizations located abroad).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) & (h)(2) (service of process on individuals and organizations in foreign countries).\n\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1134 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining how process can be served on individuals and organizations located abroad).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[29],"sourceAnswerPages":[30,31],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 A 州公民的歌手认为，其前唱片公司将她的歌曲权利出售给另一家公司，违反了联邦法律。歌手在 A 州联邦法院起诉该唱片公司及其总裁。唱片公司的总部及注册成立地均在某外国，其总裁也居住在该外国。向两名被告送达诉讼文书的最佳方法是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"按照 A 州民事诉讼规则要求的方式送达。","B":"由送达人亲自将传票和起诉状副本送交唱片公司总部及其总裁。","C":"将传票和起诉状副本邮寄给唱片公司及其总裁。","D":"依照在具体情形下足以合理实现通知的国际协议送达。"},"explanationZh":"在外国送达诉讼文书的方法：\n\n个人或组织：\n• 遵循在具体情形下足以合理实现通知的国际协议；\n• 若无国际协议且联邦法律不禁止，可依外国规则、依外国对司法协助请求书的答复所指定的方式，或在外国不禁止时通过亲自交付*或要求签收回执的邮件送达；\n• 遵循法院命令，除非国际协议禁止。\n\n外国国家：\n• 遵循原告与外国政府之间的特别安排；\n• 无特别安排时，遵循国际公约；\n• 无公约时，向该国外交部门负责人邮寄文件并要求签收回执；\n• 若邮寄送达无法在 30 天内完成，以要求签收的邮件将两份副本寄送给美国国务卿。\n\n*组织在外国不能通过亲自交付方式接受送达。\n\n正当程序要求原告通过送达诉讼文书，即交付传票和起诉状，通知被告存在诉讼。位于外国的个人和组织，可通过任何国际上约定且在具体情形下足以合理实现通知的方式接受送达。因此，向外国唱片公司及其总裁送达的最佳方法，是依符合该标准的适用国际协议送达。\n\n（选项 A）在美国境内，可以依法院州的民事诉讼规则要求向个人或组织送达。但该方式不适用于向位于外国的个人或组织送达。\n\n（选项 B）通常可以由送达人亲自向外国个人交付传票和起诉状副本。但是，位于外国的组织不能以这种方式接受送达。\n\n（选项 C）在外国不存在国际上约定的送达方式时，原告通常可以通过要求签收回执的邮件（例如挂号邮件）向个人或组织送达。\n\n学习目标：位于外国的个人和组织，可以通过任何国际上约定且在具体情形下足以合理实现通知的方式接受诉讼文书送达。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) & (h)(2)（向位于外国的个人及组织送达）。\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1134 (4th ed. 2020)（说明如何向位于外国的个人及组织送达诉讼文书）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-011","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":11,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A corporation has been the subject of several news reports charging that the pollution emitted by its factory endangers the health of hundreds of residents in a subdivision. The corporation filed an action in federal court against the residents to obtain a declaratory judgment that it complied with environmental regulations and is not responsible for any subsequent harm. A federal statute authorizes this suit. The state in which the federal court is located permits service of process by mail or by publishing notice of the suit in a newspaper. The corporation obtained the name and address of each resident in the subdivision from the county tax assessor. To avoid the expense of mailing service to each resident individually, the corporation published notice of the suit in the subdivision's community newspaper pursuant to the state's procedural rules. Did the corporation properly serve the residents with process?","choices":{"A":"No, because service by publication was unreasonable since the corporation knew the name and address of each resident.","B":"No, because the residents were not personally served with process.","C":"Yes, because service by publication was reasonable due to the expense associated with mailing service to each resident.","D":"Yes, because the newspaper publication provided the residents with reasonable notice of the action."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit through service of process, which must comply with both procedural rules and due process. In a federal court action, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4 permits service of process on a defendant-individual located in the U.S. by any of the following methods:\n• Personal service – when process is delivered in person to the defendant\n\n• Substituted service – when process is delivered to the defendant's agent or left at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion\n\n• State procedural rules – when process is served pursuant to the rules of the state where the court sits or service is attempted (as seen with the newspaper publication here) (Choice B)\n\nHowever, attempted service must also satisfy due process. Under this constitutional requirement, service must reasonably apprise the defendant of the pending suit so that the defendant may appear and present objections. This means that the plaintiff must notify the defendant of the lawsuit by the most reasonable means under the circumstances. Service by publication is the most reasonable means in the rare instance when the defendant's identity or address is unknown and unobtainable. But if the plaintiff knows or can reasonably obtain this information, a more reasonable means of service must be used (eg, service by mail). Here, the corporation had obtained the residents' names and addresses from the county tax assessor, so service by publication was unreasonable. Therefore, the residents were not properly served with process (Choices C & D). Educational objective: A plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit that reasonably apprises the defendant of the suit so that the defendant may appear and present objections. Service by publication is permitted only when the defendant's identity or address is unknown and unobtainable. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on individual in the United States).\n\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 319 (1950) (holding that a plaintiff cannot provide notice by publication when the identities and addresses of the defendants are known or easily ascertainable).\n\n• 62B Am. Jur. 2d Process § 206 (2020) (explaining service by publication).","rule":"A plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit that reasonably apprises the defendant of the suit so that the defendant may appear and present objections. Service by publication is permitted only when the defendant's identity or address is unknown and unobtainable.","irac":{"issue":"A corporation has been the subject of several news reports charging that the pollution emitted by its factory endangers the health of hundreds of residents in a subdivision. The corporation filed an action in federal court against the residents to obtain a declaratory judgment that it complied with environmental regulations and is not responsible for any subsequent harm. A federal statute authorizes this suit. The state in which the federal court is located permits service of process by mail or by publishing notice of the suit in a newspaper. The corporation obtained the name and address of each resident in the subdivision from the county tax assessor. To avoid the expense of mailing service to each resident individually, the corporation published notice of the suit in the subdivision's community newspaper pursuant to the state's procedural rules. Did the corporation properly serve the residents with process?","rule":"A plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit that reasonably apprises the defendant of the suit so that the defendant may appear and present objections. Service by publication is permitted only when the defendant's identity or address is unknown and unobtainable.","analysis":"A plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit through service of process, which must comply with both procedural rules and due process. In a federal court action, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4 permits service of process on a defendant-individual located in the U.S. by any of the following methods:\n• Personal service – when process is delivered in person to the defendant\n\n• Substituted service – when process is delivered to the defendant's agent or left at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion\n\n• State procedural rules – when process is served pursuant to the rules of the state where the court sits or service is attempted (as seen with the newspaper publication here) (Choice B)\n\nHowever, attempted service must also satisfy due process. Under this constitutional requirement, service must reasonably apprise the defendant of the pending suit so that the defendant may appear and present objections. This means that the plaintiff must notify the defendant of the lawsuit by the most reasonable means under the circumstances. Service by publication is the most reasonable means in the rare instance when the defendant's identity or address is unknown and unobtainable. But if the plaintiff knows or can reasonably obtain this information, a more reasonable means of service must be used (eg, service by mail). Here, the corporation had obtained the residents' names and addresses from the county tax assessor, so service by publication was unreasonable. Therefore, the residents were not properly served with process (Choices C & D). Educational objective: A plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit that reasonably apprises the defendant of the suit so that the defendant may appear and present objections. Service by publication is permitted only when the defendant's identity or address is unknown and unobtainable. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on individual in the United States).\n\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 319 (1950) (holding that a plaintiff cannot provide notice by publication when the identities and addresses of the defendants are known or easily ascertainable).\n\n• 62B Am. Jur. 2d Process § 206 (2020) (explaining service by publication).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on individual in the United States).\n\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 319 (1950) (holding that a plaintiff cannot provide notice by publication when the identities and addresses of the defendants are known or easily ascertainable).\n\n• 62B Am. Jur. 2d Process § 206 (2020) (explaining service by publication).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[32],"sourceAnswerPages":[33,34],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"多篇新闻报道指控某公司工厂排放的污染物危害一个住宅区内数百名居民的健康。该公司在联邦法院起诉这些居民，请求宣告其遵守环境法规，且不对以后发生的任何损害负责。一项联邦法律授权提起此类诉讼。联邦法院所在州允许以邮寄方式送达，或在报纸上刊登诉讼通知。公司从县税务评估员处取得住宅区每名居民的姓名和地址。为避免分别向每名居民邮寄送达的费用，公司依该州程序规则在住宅区社区报纸上刊登了诉讼通知。公司是否已适当地向居民送达诉讼文书？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为公司知道每名居民的姓名和地址，以公告方式送达并不合理。","B":"没有，因为诉讼文书并未亲自送达居民。","C":"有，因为逐一邮寄送达费用高昂，以公告方式送达是合理的。","D":"有，因为报纸公告向居民提供了对诉讼的合理通知。"},"explanationZh":"原告必须通过诉讼文书送达向被告提供充分的诉讼通知，且送达必须同时符合程序规则和正当程序。联邦诉讼中，《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 4 条允许以以下任何方式向位于美国境内的个人被告送达：\n• 亲自送达——将文书亲自交给被告；\n• 替代送达——将文书交给被告的代理人，或留在被告住所，交给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人；\n• 州程序规则——依照法院所在地州或尝试送达地州的规则送达，本题的报纸公告即属于该类方式（选项 B）。\n\n但是，尝试送达还必须满足正当程序。依该宪法要求，送达必须能够合理告知被告尚待审理的诉讼，使其可以出庭并提出异议。这意味着原告必须采用具体情形下最合理的方式通知被告。仅在被告身份或地址不明且无法取得的少数情况下，公告送达才是最合理的方式。若原告知道或能够合理取得这些信息，则必须采用更合理的方式，例如邮寄送达。\n\n本题中，公司已从县税务评估员处取得居民的姓名及地址，所以公告送达不合理。因此，居民未被适当送达（选项 C、D）。\n\n学习目标：原告必须向被告提供能够合理告知其诉讼存在的充分通知，使被告可以出庭并提出异议。只有在被告身份或地址不明且无法取得时，才允许公告送达。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e)（向美国境内个人送达）。\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 319 (1950)（被告身份和地址已知或容易查明时，不得仅以公告方式通知）。\n• 62B Am. Jur. 2d Process § 206 (2020)（说明公告送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-005"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-012","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":12,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A shop owner domiciled in State A sued a distributor in a federal district court in State A for breach of a contract. The shop owner sought $100,000 in damages for allegedly defective goods that the distributor had provided under the contract. The distributor is incorporated in State B, with its principal place of business in State C. The distributor brought in as a third-party defendant the wholesaler that had provided the goods to the distributor, alleging that the wholesaler had a duty to indemnify the distributor for any damages recovered by the shop owner. The wholesaler is incorporated in State B, with its principal place of business in State A. The wholesaler has asserted a $60,000 counterclaim against the distributor for payment for the goods at issue, and the distributor has moved to dismiss the counterclaim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the motion to dismiss be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because the wholesaler's and the distributor's principal places of business are diverse.","B":"No, because there is supplemental jurisdiction over the wholesaler's counterclaim.","C":"Yes, because there is no diversity of citizenship between the distributor and the wholesaler.","D":"Yes, because there is no diversity of citizenship between the shop owner and the wholesaler."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction generally arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, the court has diversity jurisdiction over the shop owner's (citizen of State A) $100,000 claim against the distributor (citizen of States B and C). But the court lacks diversity jurisdiction over the distributor's indemnity claim against the wholesaler because they are both incorporated in State B (Choice A). And it lacks diversity jurisdiction over the wholesaler's counterclaim since it seeks only $60,000. When a claim does not satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist if the claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim originally within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction. Here, the distributor's indemnity claim and the wholesaler's counterclaim arise from the same defective goods as the shop owner's original diversity claim. But when the original claim is based solely on diversity jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted if the supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship between the opposing plaintiffs and defendants or\n\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention.\n\nHere, the distributor's and wholesaler's lack of diversity would not contaminate the diversity between the shop owner and the distributor (Choice C). Although the shop owner and the wholesaler are both citizens of State A, they are not opposing parties because they have asserted no claims against each other (Choice D). And since the wholesaler's $60,000 counterclaim was asserted by a third-party defendant, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over it and should deny the distributor's motion to dismiss. Educational objective: A court is generally permitted to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that does not satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction when that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim originally within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).","rule":"A court is generally permitted to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that does not satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction when that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim originally within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"A shop owner domiciled in State A sued a distributor in a federal district court in State A for breach of a contract. The shop owner sought $100,000 in damages for allegedly defective goods that the distributor had provided under the contract. The distributor is incorporated in State B, with its principal place of business in State C. The distributor brought in as a third-party defendant the wholesaler that had provided the goods to the distributor, alleging that the wholesaler had a duty to indemnify the distributor for any damages recovered by the shop owner. The wholesaler is incorporated in State B, with its principal place of business in State A. The wholesaler has asserted a $60,000 counterclaim against the distributor for payment for the goods at issue, and the distributor has moved to dismiss the counterclaim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the motion to dismiss be granted?","rule":"A court is generally permitted to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that does not satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction when that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim originally within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction generally arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, the court has diversity jurisdiction over the shop owner's (citizen of State A) $100,000 claim against the distributor (citizen of States B and C). But the court lacks diversity jurisdiction over the distributor's indemnity claim against the wholesaler because they are both incorporated in State B (Choice A). And it lacks diversity jurisdiction over the wholesaler's counterclaim since it seeks only $60,000. When a claim does not satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist if the claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim originally within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction. Here, the distributor's indemnity claim and the wholesaler's counterclaim arise from the same defective goods as the shop owner's original diversity claim. But when the original claim is based solely on diversity jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted if the supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship between the opposing plaintiffs and defendants or\n\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention.\n\nHere, the distributor's and wholesaler's lack of diversity would not contaminate the diversity between the shop owner and the distributor (Choice C). Although the shop owner and the wholesaler are both citizens of State A, they are not opposing parties because they have asserted no claims against each other (Choice D). And since the wholesaler's $60,000 counterclaim was asserted by a third-party defendant, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over it and should deny the distributor's motion to dismiss. Educational objective: A court is generally permitted to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that does not satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction when that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts as a claim originally within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[35],"sourceAnswerPages":[36,37],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"居住在 A 州的一名商店业主，在 A 州联邦地区法院以违约为由起诉一家经销商，就其依合同提供的据称有缺陷的商品索赔 100,000 美元。经销商在 B 州注册成立，主要营业地位于 C 州。经销商将向其提供商品的批发商作为第三方被告加入诉讼，主张若商店业主获得任何损害赔偿，批发商负有补偿经销商的义务。批发商在 B 州注册成立，主要营业地位于 A 州。批发商对经销商提出 60,000 美元的反诉，请求支付涉案商品价款。经销商以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回反诉。是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为批发商与经销商的主要营业地位于不同州。","B":"不应，因为法院对批发商的反诉享有补充管辖权。","C":"应当，因为经销商与批发商不具有不同州籍。","D":"应当，因为商店业主与批发商不具有不同州籍。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权通常基于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——诉请依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方当事人具有不同州籍，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n本题中，法院对商店业主（A 州公民）针对经销商（B 州及 C 州公民）提出的 100,000 美元请求享有多元州籍管辖权。但法院对经销商针对批发商提出的补偿请求不享有多元州籍管辖权，因为二者均在 B 州注册成立（选项 A）。法院对批发商的反诉也不享有多元州籍管辖权，因为其仅请求 60,000 美元。\n\n请求本身不满足事项管辖权要求时，如果它与原本属于法院事项管辖权的请求共享同一核心争议事实，法院可能享有补充管辖权。本题中，经销商的补偿请求和批发商的反诉，均与商店业主原始多元州籍请求所涉的同一批缺陷商品有关。\n\n但是，原始请求仅以多元州籍管辖权为基础时，若补充请求：(1) 会破坏对立原告与被告之间的州籍完全多元性；或 (2) 请求金额不超过 75,000 美元，且由原告针对通过合并、介入或第三方诉讼加入的当事人提出，或由寻求通过强制合并或介入加入诉讼的原告提出，则不得行使补充管辖权。\n\n经销商与批发商州籍相同，并不会破坏商店业主与经销商之间的州籍多元性（选项 C）。虽然商店业主与批发商均为 A 州公民，但双方未相互提出请求，因而不是对立当事人（选项 D）。由于 60,000 美元反诉由第三方被告提出，法院对其享有补充管辖权，应驳回经销商的驳回动议。\n\n学习目标：不满足事项管辖权要求的请求若与原本属于法院事项管辖权的请求共享同一核心争议事实，法院通常可以对其行使补充管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367（补充管辖权）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331（联邦问题管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-006"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-013","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":13,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A salesman met two investors in a large city located in State A, where both investors work. One of the investors lives there as well. The other investor lives in State B, which shares a border with State A. The salesman is a resident of State C and never does business in State B. At the meeting, the salesman pitched the investors on an opportunity to invest in his native country's oil wells. The investors thought about the salesman's proposal overnight, and the following morning each investor called the salesman to confirm the investment. The investors then went to their respective banks located near their homes, and each wired $500,000 to an offshore account. Six months later, the investors learned that the oil wells did not exist. As a result, the investors seek to file a joint complaint against the salesman in federal district court to recover their $1,000,000 investment. Each state is composed of only one federal judicial district. Which of the following federal district courts have both personal jurisdiction over the salesman and proper venue over the investors' suit?","choices":{"A":"The district court of State A only.","B":"The district court of State C only.","C":"The district courts of State A and State C.","D":"The district courts of State A, State B, and State C."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A federal court must have personal jurisdiction over a defendant and be a proper venue to hear a dispute. Personal jurisdiction can be established through:\n• general jurisdiction – when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state and is essentially \"at home\" there or\n\n• specific jurisdiction – when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's contacts with the forum state.\n\nHere, State C has general jurisdiction over the salesman because he resides there. And State A has specific jurisdiction over the salesman because the investors' claim arises from their meeting with the salesman there. However, State B cannot exercise personal jurisdiction over the salesman because he has no contacts there. Therefore, only the district courts of States A and C have personal jurisdiction over the salesman (Choice D). The next consideration is which of these courts is also a proper venue where the case can be heard. Venue is proper in any district where:\n• any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property is located (ie, property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies (ie, fallback provision).\n\nHere, venue is proper in the district court of State C, where the salesman (the sole defendant) resides. A substantial part of the events giving rise to the investors' claim occurred in the districts of State A (where the meeting occurred) and State B (where the investor wired money), so venue is also proper there. But since State B lacks personal jurisdiction over the salesman, only the district courts of States A and C have both personal jurisdiction over the salesman and venue over the investors' suit (Choices A & B). Educational objective: To hear a dispute, a federal court must have personal jurisdiction over the defendant and be a proper venue in which the case can be heard. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (venue).\n\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780-82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for general and specific jurisdiction).","rule":"To hear a dispute, a federal court must have personal jurisdiction over the defendant and be a proper venue in which the case can be heard.","irac":{"issue":"A salesman met two investors in a large city located in State A, where both investors work. One of the investors lives there as well. The other investor lives in State B, which shares a border with State A. The salesman is a resident of State C and never does business in State B. At the meeting, the salesman pitched the investors on an opportunity to invest in his native country's oil wells. The investors thought about the salesman's proposal overnight, and the following morning each investor called the salesman to confirm the investment. The investors then went to their respective banks located near their homes, and each wired $500,000 to an offshore account. Six months later, the investors learned that the oil wells did not exist. As a result, the investors seek to file a joint complaint against the salesman in federal district court to recover their $1,000,000 investment. Each state is composed of only one federal judicial district. Which of the following federal district courts have both personal jurisdiction over the salesman and proper venue over the investors' suit?","rule":"To hear a dispute, a federal court must have personal jurisdiction over the defendant and be a proper venue in which the case can be heard.","analysis":"A federal court must have personal jurisdiction over a defendant and be a proper venue to hear a dispute. Personal jurisdiction can be established through:\n• general jurisdiction – when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state and is essentially \"at home\" there or\n\n• specific jurisdiction – when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's contacts with the forum state.\n\nHere, State C has general jurisdiction over the salesman because he resides there. And State A has specific jurisdiction over the salesman because the investors' claim arises from their meeting with the salesman there. However, State B cannot exercise personal jurisdiction over the salesman because he has no contacts there. Therefore, only the district courts of States A and C have personal jurisdiction over the salesman (Choice D). The next consideration is which of these courts is also a proper venue where the case can be heard. Venue is proper in any district where:\n• any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property is located (ie, property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies (ie, fallback provision).\n\nHere, venue is proper in the district court of State C, where the salesman (the sole defendant) resides. A substantial part of the events giving rise to the investors' claim occurred in the districts of State A (where the meeting occurred) and State B (where the investor wired money), so venue is also proper there. But since State B lacks personal jurisdiction over the salesman, only the district courts of States A and C have both personal jurisdiction over the salesman and venue over the investors' suit (Choices A & B). Educational objective: To hear a dispute, a federal court must have personal jurisdiction over the defendant and be a proper venue in which the case can be heard. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (venue).\n\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780-82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for general and specific jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (venue).\n\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780-82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for general and specific jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[38],"sourceAnswerPages":[39,40],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名推销员在 A 州一座大城市与两名投资者会面，两名投资者均在该市工作，其中一人也居住于该市；另一人居住于与 A 州接壤的 B 州。推销员是 C 州居民，从未在 B 州经营业务。会面时，推销员向投资者推介投资其祖国油井的机会。投资者考虑一夜后，于次日上午分别致电推销员确认投资，随后前往各自住所附近的银行，各向一个境外账户电汇 500,000 美元。六个月后，投资者得知这些油井并不存在。因此，两人拟在联邦地区法院共同起诉推销员，追回其 1,000,000 美元投资。每州仅有一个联邦司法区。以下哪些联邦地区法院既对推销员享有对人管辖权，又是该投资者诉讼的适当审判地？","choicesZh":{"A":"仅 A 州联邦地区法院。","B":"仅 C 州联邦地区法院。","C":"A 州及 C 州联邦地区法院。","D":"A 州、B 州及 C 州联邦地区法院。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院必须对被告享有对人管辖权，并且是审理争议的适当审判地。对人管辖权可通过以下方式建立：\n• 一般管辖权——被告与法院州存在持续且系统的联系，并实质上“以该州为家”；\n• 特定管辖权——原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的联系。\n\nC 州对推销员享有一般管辖权，因为他居住于该州。A 州对推销员享有特定管辖权，因为投资者的请求源于双方在该州的会面。推销员与 B 州没有任何联系，所以 B 州不能对其行使对人管辖权。因此，只有 A 州及 C 州地区法院对推销员享有对人管辖权（选项 D）。\n\n接下来应判断其中哪些法院还是适当审判地。审判地在以下地区适当：\n• 若所有被告均居住于同一州，则任何被告居住的地区；\n• 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地，或诉讼标的财产的实质部分所在地；\n• 仅在前两项均不适用时，任何被告受该法院对人管辖的地区。\n\n本题中，C 州是唯一被告推销员的居住地，故审判地适当。引起请求的实质部分事件也发生在 A 州（会面地）和 B 州（投资者电汇款项地），故两地的审判地同样适当。但 B 州对推销员不享有对人管辖权，所以只有 A 州及 C 州地区法院同时具备对人管辖权和适当审判地（选项 A、B）。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院要审理争议，必须对被告享有对人管辖权，并且是案件的适当审判地。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)（审判地）。\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017)（说明一般与特定管辖权要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-007"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-014","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":14,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A farmer from State A purchased farmland from a woman who lives in State B. Prior to the purchase, a private land inspector from State B tested the soil and concluded that it was adequate for farming operations. Upon taking possession of the land, however, the farmer realized that the woman had polluted the soil to such an extent that he could not maintain farming operations. The farmer sued the woman in federal court under a federal environmental statute for $300,000. The farmer also joined the inspector to this suit to recover $50,000 for the inspector's negligent inspection of the land. The inspector filed a motion to dismiss the farmer's claim against him for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the inspector's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because although there is no federal-question or diversity jurisdiction over the farmer's claim against the inspector, there is supplemental jurisdiction over this claim.","B":"Deny the motion, because there is federal-question jurisdiction over the farmer's claim against the inspector.","C":"Grant the motion, because a federal court can never hear a claim over which it does not have federal-question or diversity jurisdiction.","D":"Grant the motion, because there is no federal-question, diversity, or supplemental jurisdiction over the farmer's claim against the inspector."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction refers to a federal court's authority to hear a type of dispute. This requirement is met when a dispute falls within either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy for each claim exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, federal-question jurisdiction exists over the farmer's claim against the woman because it arises under the federal statute. But such jurisdiction does not exist over his state law negligence claim against the inspector (Choice B). And though the opposing parties reside in different states (States A and B), the federal court lacks diversity jurisdiction over this claim since the amount in controversy is merely $50,000. When only some claims in a dispute fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction (as seen here), the court can exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear the remaining claims in some instances (Choice C). This is permitted when a supplemental claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. But if the original claim is based on federal-question jurisdiction, the court can decline to exercise supplemental jurisdiction for any of the following discretionary reasons:\n• the supplemental claim raises a novel/complex state law issue\n• the supplemental claim predominates over the original claims\n• the original claims have been dismissed or\n• another compelling reason exists. Here, the farmer's claims share a common nucleus of operative facts because they arise from the same polluted soil. And since the farmer's claim against the woman is based on federal-question jurisdiction—and none of the discretionary reasons apply—the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over his claim against the inspector. As a result, the court will likely deny the inspector's motion to dismiss (Choice D). Educational objective: A federal court will generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that falls outside of its original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that arises under federal-question jurisdiction—unless it declines to do so for a discretionary reason. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","rule":"A federal court will generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that falls outside of its original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that arises under federal-question jurisdiction—unless it declines to do so for a discretionary reason.","irac":{"issue":"A farmer from State A purchased farmland from a woman who lives in State B. Prior to the purchase, a private land inspector from State B tested the soil and concluded that it was adequate for farming operations. Upon taking possession of the land, however, the farmer realized that the woman had polluted the soil to such an extent that he could not maintain farming operations. The farmer sued the woman in federal court under a federal environmental statute for $300,000. The farmer also joined the inspector to this suit to recover $50,000 for the inspector's negligent inspection of the land. The inspector filed a motion to dismiss the farmer's claim against him for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the inspector's motion?","rule":"A federal court will generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that falls outside of its original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that arises under federal-question jurisdiction—unless it declines to do so for a discretionary reason.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction refers to a federal court's authority to hear a type of dispute. This requirement is met when a dispute falls within either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy for each claim exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, federal-question jurisdiction exists over the farmer's claim against the woman because it arises under the federal statute. But such jurisdiction does not exist over his state law negligence claim against the inspector (Choice B). And though the opposing parties reside in different states (States A and B), the federal court lacks diversity jurisdiction over this claim since the amount in controversy is merely $50,000. When only some claims in a dispute fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction (as seen here), the court can exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear the remaining claims in some instances (Choice C). This is permitted when a supplemental claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. But if the original claim is based on federal-question jurisdiction, the court can decline to exercise supplemental jurisdiction for any of the following discretionary reasons:\n• the supplemental claim raises a novel/complex state law issue\n• the supplemental claim predominates over the original claims\n• the original claims have been dismissed or\n• another compelling reason exists. Here, the farmer's claims share a common nucleus of operative facts because they arise from the same polluted soil. And since the farmer's claim against the woman is based on federal-question jurisdiction—and none of the discretionary reasons apply—the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over his claim against the inspector. As a result, the court will likely deny the inspector's motion to dismiss (Choice D). Educational objective: A federal court will generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim that falls outside of its original subject-matter jurisdiction if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that arises under federal-question jurisdiction—unless it declines to do so for a discretionary reason. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[41],"sourceAnswerPages":[42,43],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名农民向居住在 B 州的一名女子购买农地。购买前，B 州的一名私人土地检验员检测土壤并认定其适合农业经营。但农民取得土地占有后发现，女子对土壤的污染程度使其无法进行农业经营。农民依据一项联邦环境法律在联邦法院起诉女子，索赔 300,000 美元；并将检验员加入诉讼，就其过失检验索赔 50,000 美元。检验员以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回农民针对他的请求。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为虽然法院对农民针对检验员的请求不享有联邦问题或多元州籍管辖权，但享有补充管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为法院对农民针对检验员的请求享有联邦问题管辖权。","C":"批准动议，因为联邦法院绝不能审理其不享有联邦问题或多元州籍管辖权的请求。","D":"批准动议，因为法院对农民针对检验员的请求不享有联邦问题、多元州籍或补充管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权是指联邦法院审理某一类型争议的权限。争议属于以下任一范围即可满足该要求：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——争议依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生；\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方当事人具有不同州籍，且每项请求的争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n农民针对女子的请求依据联邦法律产生，故存在联邦问题管辖权；但其针对检验员的州法过失请求不享有该管辖权（选项 B）。虽然双方分别居住于 A 州与 B 州，因争议金额仅为 50,000 美元，法院对该请求也不享有多元州籍管辖权。\n\n仅部分请求属于法院原始事项管辖权时，法院在某些情况下可以行使补充管辖权审理其余请求（选项 C）。补充请求与属于法院原始事项管辖权的请求共享同一核心争议事实时，即可如此。但原始请求基于联邦问题管辖权时，如存在以下酌情理由，法院可以拒绝行使补充管辖权：\n• 补充请求提出新颖或复杂的州法问题；\n• 补充请求在诉讼中占主导地位；\n• 原始请求已被驳回；\n• 存在其他令人信服的理由。\n\n农民的两项请求均源于同一片受污染土壤，故共享同一核心争议事实。其针对女子的请求以联邦问题管辖权为基础，且无任何拒绝行使管辖权的酌情理由，因此法院可以对针对检验员的请求行使补充管辖权。法院很可能驳回检验员的驳回动议（选项 D）。\n\n学习目标：不属于联邦法院原始事项管辖权的请求，若与基于联邦问题管辖权的请求共享同一核心争议事实，联邦法院通常会对其行使补充管辖权，除非法院基于酌情理由拒绝。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367（补充管辖权）。\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005)（说明补充管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-008"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-015","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":15,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A small commercial airplane crashed in State A. The passengers and pilot, all citizens of State B, were killed in the crash. The airline that owned and operated the airplane is incorporated and has its maintenance facilities and principal place of business in State C. One day before the statute of limitations on their claims would have run, the estates of the pilot and each of the passengers filed a wrongful death action against the airline in federal court in State A. The airline was served one week later and wants to prevent the State A federal court from hearing the action. Which of the following motions is most likely to accomplish the airline's goal?","choices":{"A":"A motion to dismiss the action for improper venue.","B":"A motion to dismiss the action for lack of personal jurisdiction.","C":"A motion to dismiss the action under the doctrine of forum non conveniens.","D":"A motion to transfer the action to a federal court in State C."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1404, a district court can transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to any judicial district (1) where the suit could have been originally brought or (2) to which all the parties have consented. Venue is proper in a district where:\n• any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (or a substantial part of the property that is the subject of the suit is located) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies).\n\nHere, the airline was sued in federal court in State A—where the airplane crash giving rise to the suit occurred (proper venue) (Choice A). But the airline can likely prevent the State A federal court from hearing the suit by filing a motion to transfer venue to State C since:\n• the suit could have originally been filed in State C—the airline's state of incorporation and principal place of business (ie, residence)\n\n• none of the parties reside in State A and\n\n• State C is likely more convenient for the witnesses since the airline has its maintenance facilities there.\n\n(Choice B) A federal court has personal jurisdiction over a defendant (eg, the airline) if the plaintiff's claim arises from the defendant's minimum contacts with the state where the court is located (ie, the forum state). Since this suit arises from the airline's minimum contacts with State A—flying and crashing its plane there—it will not be dismissed for lack of personal jurisdiction. (Choice C) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a case—even when venue is proper in that court—if a state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear the dispute (not seen here). Educational objective: A district court can transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to any judicial district (1) where the suit could have been originally brought or (2) to which all of the parties have consented. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404 (change of venue).","rule":"A district court can transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to any judicial district (1) where the suit could have been originally brought or (2) to which all of the parties have consented.","irac":{"issue":"A small commercial airplane crashed in State A. The passengers and pilot, all citizens of State B, were killed in the crash. The airline that owned and operated the airplane is incorporated and has its maintenance facilities and principal place of business in State C. One day before the statute of limitations on their claims would have run, the estates of the pilot and each of the passengers filed a wrongful death action against the airline in federal court in State A. The airline was served one week later and wants to prevent the State A federal court from hearing the action. Which of the following motions is most likely to accomplish the airline's goal?","rule":"A district court can transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to any judicial district (1) where the suit could have been originally brought or (2) to which all of the parties have consented.","analysis":"Under 28 U.S.C. § 1404, a district court can transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to any judicial district (1) where the suit could have been originally brought or (2) to which all the parties have consented. Venue is proper in a district where:\n• any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (or a substantial part of the property that is the subject of the suit is located) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies).\n\nHere, the airline was sued in federal court in State A—where the airplane crash giving rise to the suit occurred (proper venue) (Choice A). But the airline can likely prevent the State A federal court from hearing the suit by filing a motion to transfer venue to State C since:\n• the suit could have originally been filed in State C—the airline's state of incorporation and principal place of business (ie, residence)\n\n• none of the parties reside in State A and\n\n• State C is likely more convenient for the witnesses since the airline has its maintenance facilities there.\n\n(Choice B) A federal court has personal jurisdiction over a defendant (eg, the airline) if the plaintiff's claim arises from the defendant's minimum contacts with the state where the court is located (ie, the forum state). Since this suit arises from the airline's minimum contacts with State A—flying and crashing its plane there—it will not be dismissed for lack of personal jurisdiction. (Choice C) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a case—even when venue is proper in that court—if a state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear the dispute (not seen here). Educational objective: A district court can transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to any judicial district (1) where the suit could have been originally brought or (2) to which all of the parties have consented. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404 (change of venue).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404 (change of venue).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[44],"sourceAnswerPages":[45,46],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一架小型商用飞机在 A 州坠毁。乘客及飞行员均为 B 州公民，全部在事故中死亡。拥有并运营飞机的航空公司在 C 州注册成立，其维修设施及主要营业地也位于 C 州。就在其请求权诉讼时效届满前一天，飞行员及各乘客的遗产在 A 州联邦法院对航空公司提起非正常死亡诉讼。一周后，诉讼文书送达航空公司。航空公司希望阻止 A 州联邦法院审理该案。以下哪项动议最可能实现其目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"以审判地不适当为由申请驳回。","B":"以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。","C":"依据不方便法院原则申请驳回。","D":"申请将案件移送至 C 州联邦法院。"},"explanationZh":"根据 28 U.S.C. § 1404，地区法院为了当事人和证人的便利并为实现司法公正，可以将审判地转移至：(1) 原本可以提起该诉讼的任何司法区；或 (2) 所有当事人均同意的司法区。审判地在以下地区适当：\n• 若所有被告均居住在同一州，则任何被告居住的地区；\n• 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地，或诉讼标的财产的实质部分所在地；\n• 仅在前两项不适用时，任何被告受该法院对人管辖的地区。\n\n航空公司在飞机坠毁、从而引起诉讼的 A 州联邦法院被起诉，故审判地适当（选项 A）。但航空公司很可能可以通过申请将审判地转移至 C 州，阻止 A 州联邦法院继续审理，因为：\n• 诉讼原本可以在 C 州提起，C 州既是航空公司的注册成立州，也是其主要营业地所在州，因而是其住所；\n• 无任何当事人居住于 A 州；\n• 航空公司的维修设施位于 C 州，C 州对证人可能更为便利。\n\n（选项 B）若原告请求源于被告与法院州的最低限度联系，联邦法院即对被告享有对人管辖权。本案源于航空公司在 A 州飞行并使飞机在那里坠毁的最低限度联系，故不会因缺乏对人管辖权而被驳回。\n\n（选项 C）即使现法院审判地适当，如某州或外国司法制度更适合审理争议，联邦法院可依据不方便法院原则驳回或中止案件。本题并不存在该情形。\n\n学习目标：为方便当事人和证人并实现司法公正，地区法院可以将审判地转移至诉讼原本可以提起的任何司法区，或所有当事人均同意的司法区。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391（审判地）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404（变更审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-009"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-016","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":16,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"The police searched a woman's home without probable cause or a warrant. Based on the evidence discovered during the search, the woman was charged with first-degree murder in state court. At trial, the woman objected to the admission of the evidence on the grounds that the police unconstitutionally searched her home and seized the evidence. After a hearing on the issue, the court overruled the woman's objection and admitted the evidence. Immediately after this ruling, the woman filed a complaint in federal court to enjoin the state court from proceeding with her prosecution because the police violated her constitutional rights. Should the federal court hear the woman's claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal court does not have subject-matter jurisdiction over the case.","B":"No, because the federal court must abstain from hearing the case.","C":"Yes, because the woman was prosecuted in bad faith.","D":"Yes, because the woman's claim arises under the U.S. Constitution."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A federal court generally must adjudicate a suit over which it has subject-matter jurisdiction. This jurisdictional requirement can be satisfied through diversity jurisdiction or federal-question jurisdiction—ie, when the plaintiff's claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, federal law, or a treaty. Here, the woman's complaint asserts a constitutional violation, so subject-matter jurisdiction exists (Choice A). However, a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction may or must abstain from hearing a case in limited situations. One situation arises under the Younger abstention doctrine, which applies when declaratory or injunctive relief is sought in federal court. This doctrine requires abstention when such relief would interfere with a pending state proceeding on any criminal matter or a particular civil matter* that:\n• involves an important state interest and\n\n• provides an adequate opportunity to litigate the federal issue(s).\n\nHere, the woman is asking the federal court to enjoin the pending state criminal proceeding against her. However, the state has an important interest in enforcing its own criminal law, and the woman was given an opportunity to litigate her constitutional claim at the hearing. As a result, the federal court must abstain from hearing her claim (Choice D). If the woman is convicted, she can raise her constitutional challenge through the state's appellate process. *Only certain civil proceedings qualify for Younger abstention, including enforcement proceedings akin to a prosecution (eg, termination of parental rights) and proceedings that further a state court's ability to perform judicial functions (eg, contempt). (Choice C) There are several exceptions to the Younger abstention doctrine, including when the state initiates a criminal prosecution in bad faith because there was no reasonable expectation of conviction. This exception does not apply here because, while the police may have acted unconstitutionally, there is no indication that the state prosecuted the woman in bad faith. Educational objective: The Younger abstention doctrine requires a federal court to abstain when a declaratory judgment or injunction by that court would interfere with a pending state criminal, or particular civil, proceeding that (1) involves an important state interest and (2) provides an adequate opportunity to litigate federal issues. References\n• Younger v. Harris, 401 U.S. 37, 40 (1971) (establishing the Younger abstention doctrine).\n\n• Sprint Commc'ns, Inc. v. Jacobs, 571 U.S. 69, 81 (2013) (listing the requirements for Younger abstention).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1035 (2020) (explaining Younger abstention).","rule":"The Younger abstention doctrine requires a federal court to abstain when a declaratory judgment or injunction by that court would interfere with a pending state criminal, or particular civil, proceeding that (1) involves an important state interest and (2) provides an adequate opportunity to litigate federal issues.","irac":{"issue":"The police searched a woman's home without probable cause or a warrant. Based on the evidence discovered during the search, the woman was charged with first-degree murder in state court. At trial, the woman objected to the admission of the evidence on the grounds that the police unconstitutionally searched her home and seized the evidence. After a hearing on the issue, the court overruled the woman's objection and admitted the evidence. Immediately after this ruling, the woman filed a complaint in federal court to enjoin the state court from proceeding with her prosecution because the police violated her constitutional rights. Should the federal court hear the woman's claim?","rule":"The Younger abstention doctrine requires a federal court to abstain when a declaratory judgment or injunction by that court would interfere with a pending state criminal, or particular civil, proceeding that (1) involves an important state interest and (2) provides an adequate opportunity to litigate federal issues.","analysis":"A federal court generally must adjudicate a suit over which it has subject-matter jurisdiction. This jurisdictional requirement can be satisfied through diversity jurisdiction or federal-question jurisdiction—ie, when the plaintiff's claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, federal law, or a treaty. Here, the woman's complaint asserts a constitutional violation, so subject-matter jurisdiction exists (Choice A). However, a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction may or must abstain from hearing a case in limited situations. One situation arises under the Younger abstention doctrine, which applies when declaratory or injunctive relief is sought in federal court. This doctrine requires abstention when such relief would interfere with a pending state proceeding on any criminal matter or a particular civil matter* that:\n• involves an important state interest and\n\n• provides an adequate opportunity to litigate the federal issue(s).\n\nHere, the woman is asking the federal court to enjoin the pending state criminal proceeding against her. However, the state has an important interest in enforcing its own criminal law, and the woman was given an opportunity to litigate her constitutional claim at the hearing. As a result, the federal court must abstain from hearing her claim (Choice D). If the woman is convicted, she can raise her constitutional challenge through the state's appellate process. *Only certain civil proceedings qualify for Younger abstention, including enforcement proceedings akin to a prosecution (eg, termination of parental rights) and proceedings that further a state court's ability to perform judicial functions (eg, contempt). (Choice C) There are several exceptions to the Younger abstention doctrine, including when the state initiates a criminal prosecution in bad faith because there was no reasonable expectation of conviction. This exception does not apply here because, while the police may have acted unconstitutionally, there is no indication that the state prosecuted the woman in bad faith. Educational objective: The Younger abstention doctrine requires a federal court to abstain when a declaratory judgment or injunction by that court would interfere with a pending state criminal, or particular civil, proceeding that (1) involves an important state interest and (2) provides an adequate opportunity to litigate federal issues. References\n• Younger v. Harris, 401 U.S. 37, 40 (1971) (establishing the Younger abstention doctrine).\n\n• Sprint Commc'ns, Inc. v. Jacobs, 571 U.S. 69, 81 (2013) (listing the requirements for Younger abstention).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1035 (2020) (explaining Younger abstention).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Younger v. Harris, 401 U.S. 37, 40 (1971) (establishing the Younger abstention doctrine).\n\n• Sprint Commc'ns, Inc. v. Jacobs, 571 U.S. 69, 81 (2013) (listing the requirements for Younger abstention).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1035 (2020) (explaining Younger abstention).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[47],"sourceAnswerPages":[48,49],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"警方在没有合理根据或搜查令的情况下搜查一名女子的住宅。依据搜查所得证据，女子在州法院被控一级谋杀罪。审判中，女子以警方违宪搜查其住宅并扣押证据为由，反对采纳该证据。法院就该问题举行听证后驳回异议并采纳证据。裁定作出后，女子立即向联邦法院提起诉讼，请求禁止州法院继续刑事追诉，理由是警方侵犯了她的宪法权利。联邦法院是否应审理该请求？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为联邦法院对该案不享有事项管辖权。","B":"不应，因为联邦法院必须 abstain（自行克制）而不审理该案。","C":"应当，因为女子受到恶意追诉。","D":"应当，因为女子的请求依据美国宪法产生。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院通常必须裁判其享有事项管辖权的诉讼。该要求可以通过多元州籍管辖权或联邦问题管辖权满足；后者是指原告请求依据美国宪法、联邦法律或条约产生。本题中，女子主张宪法权利受到侵犯，故存在事项管辖权（选项 A）。\n\n但是，在少数情况下，即使联邦法院享有事项管辖权，也可以或必须自行克制而不审理案件。其中一种情形由 Younger 克制原则规定，适用于当事人向联邦法院请求宣告性或禁令性救济的案件。如果此类救济会干预尚待审理的州刑事程序，或某些特定州民事程序，且该州程序：(1) 涉及重要州利益；并且 (2) 提供充分机会审理联邦问题，则联邦法院必须克制。*\n\n女子请求联邦法院禁止尚待审理的州刑事程序。但是，州政府对执行本州刑法具有重要利益，且女子已在听证中获得审理其宪法主张的机会。因此，联邦法院必须自行克制，不审理她的请求（选项 D）。如果女子被定罪，可以通过州上诉程序提出宪法异议。\n\n*只有某些民事程序适用 Younger 克制原则，包括类似刑事追诉的执法程序（例如终止亲权）以及促进州法院履行司法职能的程序（例如藐视法庭程序）。\n\n（选项 C）Younger 克制原则存在若干例外，包括州在没有合理定罪预期的情况下恶意发起刑事追诉。但本题没有事实表明州政府恶意追诉；警方可能采取违宪行为并不足以证明恶意追诉。\n\n学习目标：如果联邦法院作出的宣告性判决或禁令会干预尚待审理的州刑事程序或特定民事程序，且该程序：(1) 涉及重要州利益；并且 (2) 提供充分机会审理联邦问题，则 Younger 克制原则要求联邦法院自行克制。\n\n参考资料：\n• Younger v. Harris, 401 U.S. 37, 40 (1971)（确立 Younger 克制原则）。\n• Sprint Commc'ns, Inc. v. Jacobs, 571 U.S. 69, 81 (2013)（列明 Younger 克制要求）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1035 (2020)（说明 Younger 克制）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-010"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-017","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":17,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A woman from State A was hit in State B by a driver who is a citizen and resident of a foreign country. The driver frequently visited family in State A. Each state is composed of only one federal judicial district. If the woman files a diversity action against the driver in the federal court in State A, which of the following is the woman's strongest argument that venue is proper?","choices":{"A":"Venue is proper in any judicial district because the driver resides in a foreign country.","B":"Venue is proper in any judicial district because the Federal Tort Claims Act applies.","C":"Venue is proper in State A because that is where the woman resides.","D":"Venue is proper in State A because the driver is subject to personal jurisdiction there."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Special rules for where venue is proper\n\nRemoval • Federal district where state action was pending\n\nForeign resident • Any judicial district\n\nFederal official sued in official • Where defendant resides capacity • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• Where plaintiff resides if no real property is involved in suit Foreign government • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• In federal district court of Washington, D.C. Multiparty, multiforum • Where defendant resides litigation • Where substantial part of accident occurred Federal Tort Claims Act • Where plaintiff resides\n• Where act/omission occurred Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. Under the general venue statute, venue is proper in a judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides—but only if all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial portion of the events occurred or a substantial part of the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if venue cannot be established under the above provisions.\n\nHowever, special venue rules apply in certain situations, including when a defendant is a nonresident of the U.S. In this situation, venue is proper in any judicial district.* And since the driver is a resident of a foreign country, this is the woman's strongest argument that venue is proper in State A. *Additionally, when a non-U.S. resident is joined (ie, added) as a defendant, the non-U.S. resident is disregarded when determining proper venue as to the other defendants in the action. (Choice B) The Federal Tort Claims Act applies to suits brought against the U.S. government for the tortious conduct of a federal employee—not suits against a nonresident for his/her tortious conduct (as seen here). But had the Act applied, venue would be proper\n\nonly in the judicial district where the plaintiff resides (State A) or where the act or omission occurred (State B). (Choice C) Venue is never based on the plaintiff's residence unless the Federal Tort Claims Act applies or a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her official capacity or under color of legal authority—none of which is seen here. Therefore, the woman cannot successfully argue that venue is proper based on where she resides. (Choice D) Since venue is proper in any judicial district in an action against a non-U.S. resident, personal jurisdiction has no bearing on where venue is proper. Educational objective: Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. When a defendant is a nonresident of the U.S., venue is proper in any federal judicial district. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(c)(3) (setting forth the proper venue for non-U.S. residents).","rule":"Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. When a defendant is a nonresident of the U.S., venue is proper in any federal judicial district.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A was hit in State B by a driver who is a citizen and resident of a foreign country. The driver frequently visited family in State A. Each state is composed of only one federal judicial district. If the woman files a diversity action against the driver in the federal court in State A, which of the following is the woman's strongest argument that venue is proper?","rule":"Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. When a defendant is a nonresident of the U.S., venue is proper in any federal judicial district.","analysis":"Special rules for where venue is proper\n\nRemoval • Federal district where state action was pending\n\nForeign resident • Any judicial district\n\nFederal official sued in official • Where defendant resides capacity • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• Where plaintiff resides if no real property is involved in suit Foreign government • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• In federal district court of Washington, D.C. Multiparty, multiforum • Where defendant resides litigation • Where substantial part of accident occurred Federal Tort Claims Act • Where plaintiff resides\n• Where act/omission occurred Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. Under the general venue statute, venue is proper in a judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides—but only if all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial portion of the events occurred or a substantial part of the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if venue cannot be established under the above provisions.\n\nHowever, special venue rules apply in certain situations, including when a defendant is a nonresident of the U.S. In this situation, venue is proper in any judicial district.* And since the driver is a resident of a foreign country, this is the woman's strongest argument that venue is proper in State A. *Additionally, when a non-U.S. resident is joined (ie, added) as a defendant, the non-U.S. resident is disregarded when determining proper venue as to the other defendants in the action. (Choice B) The Federal Tort Claims Act applies to suits brought against the U.S. government for the tortious conduct of a federal employee—not suits against a nonresident for his/her tortious conduct (as seen here). But had the Act applied, venue would be proper\n\nonly in the judicial district where the plaintiff resides (State A) or where the act or omission occurred (State B). (Choice C) Venue is never based on the plaintiff's residence unless the Federal Tort Claims Act applies or a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her official capacity or under color of legal authority—none of which is seen here. Therefore, the woman cannot successfully argue that venue is proper based on where she resides. (Choice D) Since venue is proper in any judicial district in an action against a non-U.S. resident, personal jurisdiction has no bearing on where venue is proper. Educational objective: Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. When a defendant is a nonresident of the U.S., venue is proper in any federal judicial district. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(c)(3) (setting forth the proper venue for non-U.S. residents).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(c)(3) (setting forth the proper venue for non-U.S. residents).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[51],"sourceAnswerPages":[52,53],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名女子在 B 州被一名司机撞伤，该司机是某外国的公民和居民，并经常前往 A 州探亲。每州仅有一个联邦司法区。如果女子在 A 州联邦法院对司机提起多元州籍诉讼，以下哪项是她主张审判地适当的最有力理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"审判地在任何司法区均适当，因为司机居住在外国。","B":"审判地在任何司法区均适当，因为《联邦侵权请求法》适用。","C":"审判地在 A 州适当，因为女子居住于该州。","D":"审判地在 A 州适当，因为司机受该州法院的对人管辖。"},"explanationZh":"审判地适当地点的特别规则：\n\n• 移送案件：原州法院诉讼待决地的联邦地区。\n• 外国居民：任何司法区。\n• 以公务身份被起诉的联邦官员：被告居住地、实质事件发生地或财产所在地；诉讼不涉及不动产时，也可在原告居住地。\n• 外国政府：实质事件发生地或财产所在地；或华盛顿哥伦比亚特区联邦地区法院。\n• 多当事人、多法院诉讼：被告居住地或事故实质部分发生地。\n• 《联邦侵权请求法》：原告居住地或行为／不作为发生地。\n\n审判地是可以审理案件的联邦地区法院所在地。依一般审判地法，以下司法区的审判地适当：\n• 若所有被告均居住在同一州，则任何被告居住的司法区；\n• 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地，或涉案财产的实质部分所在地；或者\n• 仅在无法依前两项确定审判地时，任何被告受该法院对人管辖的司法区。\n\n但在某些情况下适用特别规则，其中包括被告不是美国居民的情况。此时，任何司法区的审判地均适当。司机是某外国居民，因此，这是女子主张 A 州审判地适当的最有力理由。\n\n*此外，非美国居民作为被告被追加时，在确定诉讼中其他被告的适当审判地时，不考虑该非美国居民。\n\n（选项 B）《联邦侵权请求法》适用于因联邦雇员侵权行为而针对美国政府提起的诉讼，不适用于因外国居民自身侵权行为而对其提起的诉讼。即使该法适用，审判地也仅在原告居住地 A 州或行为／不作为发生地 B 州适当，而非任何司法区。\n\n（选项 C）除《联邦侵权请求法》适用，或联邦官员、雇员因公务行为或以法律权力名义行事而被起诉外，审判地不以原告住所为依据；本题均不存在这些情况。\n\n（选项 D）针对非美国居民的诉讼在任何司法区的审判地均适当，因此，对人管辖权不影响审判地的确定。\n\n学习目标：审判地是可以审理案件的联邦地区法院所在地。被告不是美国居民时，任何联邦司法区的审判地均适当。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(c)(3)（非美国居民的适当审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-018","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":18,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman from State A contacted an attorney after she developed a lung disorder, which she claims was caused by e-cigarettes. After researching the woman's claim, the attorney discovered that other individuals across the country developed similar lung disorders from the use of the same brand of e-cigarettes manufactured by a company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B. The attorney filed a class action in federal court in State A on behalf of 70 individuals against the company. The woman was the only named plaintiff. Each class member's claim amounted to $100,000, and the damages on behalf of the entire class amount to $7 million. Ten of the unnamed plaintiffs live in State B. The company filed an answer denying responsibility. Twenty days later, it filed a motion to dismiss the class action for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the company's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because diversity jurisdiction exists.","B":"Deny the motion, because the company waived its claim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction by filing its motion after its answer.","C":"Grant the motion, because members of the class and the company are citizens of State B.","D":"Grant the motion, because the class only contains 70 members."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction in class actions\n\nType Requirements\n\nFederal question • Class action arises under U.S. Constitution, treaty, or federal law Diversity • Amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000* and\n• named opposing parties are citizens of different states Class Action Fairness • Class contains ≥ 100 members Act • at least one class member & one defendant are diverse and\n• amount in controversy for aggregated claims exceeds $5 million *A federal court can acquire subject-matter jurisdiction over class members' claims that do not exceed $75,000 through supplemental jurisdiction.\n\nA class action is a suit filed by one or more named plaintiffs (ie, class representatives) on behalf of similarly harmed unnamed plaintiffs. As in any other lawsuit, a federal court can obtain subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the class action arises from the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the named opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse) and the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000.*\n\nHere, the woman from State A (named plaintiff) and the company incorporated and headquartered in State B (named defendant) are diverse. The fact that 10 unnamed plaintiffs are also citizens of State B does not destroy diversity (Choice C). Additionally, the woman—and every other class member—asserted a claim for $100,000, which satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement. Therefore, the federal court has diversity jurisdiction and will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss. *A federal court can acquire subject-matter jurisdiction over class members' claims that do not exceed $75,000 through supplemental jurisdiction. (Choice B) Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(h), a defendant waives certain defenses if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or an answer—whichever occurs first. But a defense based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and can be asserted at any time.\n\n(Choice D) The Class Action Fairness Act provides an alternate basis for subject-matter jurisdiction when (1) the class contains at least 100 members, (2) any member is diverse from at least one defendant, and (3) the aggregated amount in controversy exceeds $5 million. This Act does not apply here because the class has only 70 members, but the court still has subject-matter jurisdiction based on diversity. Educational objective: A federal court has diversity jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the named opposing parties are citizens of different states and (2) the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (class actions).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Supreme Tribe of Ben-Hur v. Cauble, 255 U.S. 356, 366 (1921) (holding that only the named opposing parties in a class action need to be diverse to satisfy diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"A federal court has diversity jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the named opposing parties are citizens of different states and (2) the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A contacted an attorney after she developed a lung disorder, which she claims was caused by e-cigarettes. After researching the woman's claim, the attorney discovered that other individuals across the country developed similar lung disorders from the use of the same brand of e-cigarettes manufactured by a company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B. The attorney filed a class action in federal court in State A on behalf of 70 individuals against the company. The woman was the only named plaintiff. Each class member's claim amounted to $100,000, and the damages on behalf of the entire class amount to $7 million. Ten of the unnamed plaintiffs live in State B. The company filed an answer denying responsibility. Twenty days later, it filed a motion to dismiss the class action for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the company's motion?","rule":"A federal court has diversity jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the named opposing parties are citizens of different states and (2) the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction in class actions\n\nType Requirements\n\nFederal question • Class action arises under U.S. Constitution, treaty, or federal law Diversity • Amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000* and\n• named opposing parties are citizens of different states Class Action Fairness • Class contains ≥ 100 members Act • at least one class member & one defendant are diverse and\n• amount in controversy for aggregated claims exceeds $5 million *A federal court can acquire subject-matter jurisdiction over class members' claims that do not exceed $75,000 through supplemental jurisdiction.\n\nA class action is a suit filed by one or more named plaintiffs (ie, class representatives) on behalf of similarly harmed unnamed plaintiffs. As in any other lawsuit, a federal court can obtain subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the class action arises from the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the named opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse) and the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000.*\n\nHere, the woman from State A (named plaintiff) and the company incorporated and headquartered in State B (named defendant) are diverse. The fact that 10 unnamed plaintiffs are also citizens of State B does not destroy diversity (Choice C). Additionally, the woman—and every other class member—asserted a claim for $100,000, which satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement. Therefore, the federal court has diversity jurisdiction and will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss. *A federal court can acquire subject-matter jurisdiction over class members' claims that do not exceed $75,000 through supplemental jurisdiction. (Choice B) Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(h), a defendant waives certain defenses if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or an answer—whichever occurs first. But a defense based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and can be asserted at any time.\n\n(Choice D) The Class Action Fairness Act provides an alternate basis for subject-matter jurisdiction when (1) the class contains at least 100 members, (2) any member is diverse from at least one defendant, and (3) the aggregated amount in controversy exceeds $5 million. This Act does not apply here because the class has only 70 members, but the court still has subject-matter jurisdiction based on diversity. Educational objective: A federal court has diversity jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the named opposing parties are citizens of different states and (2) the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (class actions).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Supreme Tribe of Ben-Hur v. Cauble, 255 U.S. 356, 366 (1921) (holding that only the named opposing parties in a class action need to be diverse to satisfy diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (class actions).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Supreme Tribe of Ben-Hur v. Cauble, 255 U.S. 356, 366 (1921) (holding that only the named opposing parties in a class action need to be diverse to satisfy diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[54],"sourceAnswerPages":[55,56],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名女子患上肺部疾病，她认为该病由电子烟造成，遂联系一名律师。律师研究后发现，全国其他人使用同一品牌电子烟后也患上类似疾病。制造该电子烟的公司在 B 州注册成立，主要营业地也位于 B 州。律师代表 70 人在 A 州联邦法院对该公司提起集团诉讼，女子是唯一具名原告。每名集团成员的请求金额均为 100,000 美元，全体集团的损害赔偿总额为 700 万美元。十名未具名原告居住在 B 州。公司提交答辩状否认责任，20 天后又以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回集团诉讼。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为存在多元州籍管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为公司在答辩后才提出事项管辖权异议，已经放弃该主张。","C":"批准动议，因为部分集团成员与公司均为 B 州公民。","D":"批准动议，因为该集团仅有 70 名成员。"},"explanationZh":"集团诉讼的事项管辖权：\n\n• 联邦问题：集团诉讼依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生。\n• 多元州籍：任何具名原告的请求金额超过 75,000 美元*，且具名对立当事人具有不同州籍。\n• 《集团诉讼公平法》：集团至少有 100 名成员；至少一名集团成员与一名被告州籍不同；合并计算的争议金额超过 500 万美元。\n\n*联邦法院可通过补充管辖权审理请求金额不超过 75,000 美元的集团成员请求。\n\n集团诉讼是由一名或多名具名原告（即集团代表）代表受到类似损害的未具名原告提起的诉讼。与其他诉讼相同，联邦法院可通过以下方式取得事项管辖权：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——集团诉讼依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——具名对立当事人具有不同州籍，且任何具名原告的请求金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n本题中，A 州女子（具名原告）与在 B 州注册成立并设有总部的公司（具名被告）州籍不同。十名未具名原告也是 B 州公民，并不会破坏州籍多元性（选项 C）。女子以及其他每名集团成员均请求 100,000 美元，满足争议金额要求。因此，联邦法院享有多元州籍管辖权，很可能驳回公司的动议。\n\n（选项 B）根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 12(h) 条，被告若未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出某些抗辩，即放弃这些抗辩。但缺乏事项管辖权的抗辩永不放弃，可在任何时候提出。\n\n（选项 D）《集团诉讼公平法》提供另一项事项管辖权依据，要求：(1) 集团至少有 100 名成员；(2) 任一成员与至少一名被告州籍不同；(3) 合并争议金额超过 500 万美元。本案集团仅有 70 人，该法不适用；但法院仍可基于普通多元州籍管辖权审理。\n\n学习目标：以下情况下，联邦法院对集团诉讼享有多元州籍管辖权：(1) 具名对立当事人是不同州的公民；且 (2) 任何具名原告的请求金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23（集团诉讼）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a)（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• Supreme Tribe of Ben-Hur v. Cauble, 255 U.S. 356, 366 (1921)（集团诉讼中仅具名对立当事人需要州籍不同）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-019","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":19,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A foreign exchange student from Germany was on vacation in State B with his host family, all citizens of State A, when their car collided with a truck owned by a company headquartered and incorporated in Germany. The truck was driven by a citizen of State B. The student and the host family brought a negligence suit against the company and the truck driver in a federal court in State B. The student seeks to recover $200,000 and the family seeks $500,000 in damages. The company and the truck driver have filed a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because actions involving foreign citizens pose a federal question.","B":"No, because the requirements for diversity jurisdiction are satisfied.","C":"Yes, because the doctrine of forum non conveniens requires that the action be heard in Germany.","D":"Yes, because the foreign exchange student and the company are citizens of the same country."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction refers to a federal court's authority to hear a particular type of case. This jurisdictional requirement can be established through federal-question jurisdiction or diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction is established when (1) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and (2) complete diversity of citizenship exists between the opposing parties. As a result, diverse citizenship exists if the suit is between:\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens\n• U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states or\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties. Here, the host family from State A and the German exchange student brought a negligence suit against the truck driver from State B and the German company. Since the suit is between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties, diverse citizenship exists. Additionally, the amount-in-controversy requirement is met because the student seeks $200,000 and the family seeks $500,000 in damages. Therefore, the requirements for diversity jurisdiction are satisfied. (Choice A) Judicial actions involving foreign citizens do not automatically pose a federal question. The cause of action must arise from the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal statute. Since the negligence action asserted here arises from state law, federal-question jurisdiction does not exist. (Choice C) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows, but does not require, a federal court to dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign judicial system provides a more convenient forum to hear the case. Additionally, this doctrine is unrelated to subject-matter jurisdiction. (Choice D) The fact that the foreign exchange student and the company are both German citizens is irrelevant. Diverse citizenship is established since the host family and the truck driver are U.S. citizens domiciled in different states and the foreign citizens are additional parties. Educational objective: Diverse citizenship exists in a suit between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states, or (3) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 628 (2020) (explaining that diverse citizenship exists in a suit between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties).","rule":"Diverse citizenship exists in a suit between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states, or (3) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties.","irac":{"issue":"A foreign exchange student from Germany was on vacation in State B with his host family, all citizens of State A, when their car collided with a truck owned by a company headquartered and incorporated in Germany. The truck was driven by a citizen of State B. The student and the host family brought a negligence suit against the company and the truck driver in a federal court in State B. The student seeks to recover $200,000 and the family seeks $500,000 in damages. The company and the truck driver have filed a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"Diverse citizenship exists in a suit between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states, or (3) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction refers to a federal court's authority to hear a particular type of case. This jurisdictional requirement can be established through federal-question jurisdiction or diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction is established when (1) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and (2) complete diversity of citizenship exists between the opposing parties. As a result, diverse citizenship exists if the suit is between:\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens\n• U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states or\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties. Here, the host family from State A and the German exchange student brought a negligence suit against the truck driver from State B and the German company. Since the suit is between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties, diverse citizenship exists. Additionally, the amount-in-controversy requirement is met because the student seeks $200,000 and the family seeks $500,000 in damages. Therefore, the requirements for diversity jurisdiction are satisfied. (Choice A) Judicial actions involving foreign citizens do not automatically pose a federal question. The cause of action must arise from the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal statute. Since the negligence action asserted here arises from state law, federal-question jurisdiction does not exist. (Choice C) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows, but does not require, a federal court to dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign judicial system provides a more convenient forum to hear the case. Additionally, this doctrine is unrelated to subject-matter jurisdiction. (Choice D) The fact that the foreign exchange student and the company are both German citizens is irrelevant. Diverse citizenship is established since the host family and the truck driver are U.S. citizens domiciled in different states and the foreign citizens are additional parties. Educational objective: Diverse citizenship exists in a suit between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states, or (3) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 628 (2020) (explaining that diverse citizenship exists in a suit between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a) (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 628 (2020) (explaining that diverse citizenship exists in a suit between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states with foreign citizens as additional parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[57],"sourceAnswerPages":[58,59],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自德国的交换生与寄宿家庭在 B 州度假时，其汽车与一辆卡车相撞。寄宿家庭成员均为 A 州公民。卡车属于一家总部及注册成立地均在德国的公司，司机是 B 州公民。交换生与寄宿家庭在 B 州联邦法院以过失为由起诉公司及司机。交换生请求 200,000 美元，寄宿家庭请求 500,000 美元损害赔偿。公司和司机以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否可能批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为涉及外国公民的诉讼构成联邦问题。","B":"不会，因为多元州籍管辖权的要求均已满足。","C":"会，因为不方便法院原则要求该诉讼必须在德国审理。","D":"会，因为交换生与公司均为德国公民。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权是联邦法院审理特定类型案件的权限，可通过联邦问题管辖权或多元州籍管辖权建立。多元州籍管辖权要求：(1) 争议金额超过 75,000 美元；且 (2) 对立双方当事人具有完全不同的州籍。因此，以下诉讼存在州籍多元性：\n• 居住于美国的美国公民与外国公民之间的诉讼；\n• 居住于不同州的美国公民与永久居民之间的诉讼；\n• 居住于不同州的美国公民之间、并有外国公民作为附加当事人的诉讼。\n\n本题中，A 州寄宿家庭和德国交换生起诉 B 州卡车司机及德国公司。该诉讼是居住于不同州的美国公民之间的诉讼，外国公民只是附加当事人，因此存在州籍多元性。交换生请求 200,000 美元，寄宿家庭请求 500,000 美元，争议金额要求也得到满足。因此，多元州籍管辖权的全部要求均满足。\n\n（选项 A）涉及外国公民的司法诉讼不会自动构成联邦问题。诉因必须依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生。本题过失请求依据州法产生，不存在联邦问题管辖权。\n\n（选项 C）如某州或外国司法制度提供更便利的审理地，不方便法院原则允许但不强制联邦法院驳回或中止诉讼。此外，该原则与事项管辖权无关。\n\n（选项 D）交换生与公司均为德国公民这一事实无关紧要。寄宿家庭与卡车司机是居住于不同州的美国公民，外国公民只是附加当事人，故存在州籍多元性。\n\n学习目标：下列诉讼存在州籍多元性：(1) 居住于美国的美国公民与外国公民之间；(2) 居住于不同州的美国公民与永久居民之间；或 (3) 居住于不同州的美国公民之间，并有外国公民作为附加当事人。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332(a)（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 628 (2020)（说明居住于不同州的美国公民之间、并有外国公民作为附加当事人的诉讼存在州籍多元性）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-011"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-020","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":20,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A senior executive domiciled in State B brought an action in a state court in State A against her former employer, a corporation that is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State A. In her complaint, the executive alleges that she was wrongfully terminated after 17 years of employment and seeks $100,000 in lost wages and benefits. The corporation filed an answer two weeks after it received service of process. The answer claimed that the executive was terminated for attempting to license a patent owned by the corporation without its permission, in violation of patent law. The corporation contemporaneously filed a notice of removal in federal court. Twenty-three days later, the executive filed a motion to remand. Should the federal court grant the motion to remand?","choices":{"A":"No, because federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over patent-law violations.","B":"No, because the motion to remand was due within 21 days after the notice of removal was filed.","C":"Yes, because the corporation, as a citizen of State A, cannot remove the case to federal court.","D":"Yes, because the corporation should have filed its notice of removal in state court."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant may remove a case from state to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a federal treaty, or federal law or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nDiversity jurisdiction seeks to protect out-of-state defendants from potential bias in state courts. However, in-state defendants do not face the same risk of bias. Therefore, the forum-defendant rule (ie, home-court advantage rule) prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) the defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists because the amount in controversy is $100,000 and the executive and the corporation are citizens of different states (States B and A, respectively). But since the corporation is a citizen of State A—where the case was filed— the forum-defendant rule prohibits removal. Therefore, the federal court should grant the executive's motion to remand the case to state court. (Choice A) Federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over patent claims, and any party that asserts a patent claim can remove the case to federal court. But here, the corporation has not asserted a patent claim—it merely raised the alleged patent violation to explain why the executive was terminated. (Choice B) A motion to remand must be filed within 30 days after the notice of removal was filed. Therefore, the executive timely filed her motion 23 days after the corporation filed its notice of removal. (Choice D) A notice of removal must be filed in the federal court located in the same district in which the suit is pending—as the corporation did here. A copy of the notice of removal should then be delivered to the parties and the state court. Educational objective: The forum-defendant rule prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) the defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (forum-defendant rule).","rule":"The forum-defendant rule prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) the defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed.","irac":{"issue":"A senior executive domiciled in State B brought an action in a state court in State A against her former employer, a corporation that is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State A. In her complaint, the executive alleges that she was wrongfully terminated after 17 years of employment and seeks $100,000 in lost wages and benefits. The corporation filed an answer two weeks after it received service of process. The answer claimed that the executive was terminated for attempting to license a patent owned by the corporation without its permission, in violation of patent law. The corporation contemporaneously filed a notice of removal in federal court. Twenty-three days later, the executive filed a motion to remand. Should the federal court grant the motion to remand?","rule":"The forum-defendant rule prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) the defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed.","analysis":"A defendant may remove a case from state to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a federal treaty, or federal law or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nDiversity jurisdiction seeks to protect out-of-state defendants from potential bias in state courts. However, in-state defendants do not face the same risk of bias. Therefore, the forum-defendant rule (ie, home-court advantage rule) prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) the defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists because the amount in controversy is $100,000 and the executive and the corporation are citizens of different states (States B and A, respectively). But since the corporation is a citizen of State A—where the case was filed— the forum-defendant rule prohibits removal. Therefore, the federal court should grant the executive's motion to remand the case to state court. (Choice A) Federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over patent claims, and any party that asserts a patent claim can remove the case to federal court. But here, the corporation has not asserted a patent claim—it merely raised the alleged patent violation to explain why the executive was terminated. (Choice B) A motion to remand must be filed within 30 days after the notice of removal was filed. Therefore, the executive timely filed her motion 23 days after the corporation filed its notice of removal. (Choice D) A notice of removal must be filed in the federal court located in the same district in which the suit is pending—as the corporation did here. A copy of the notice of removal should then be delivered to the parties and the state court. Educational objective: The forum-defendant rule prohibits removal when (1) subject-matter jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and (2) the defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (forum-defendant rule).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2) (forum-defendant rule).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[60],"sourceAnswerPages":[61,62],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名居住于 B 州的高级管理人员在 A 州州法院起诉其前雇主。该雇主公司在 A 州注册成立，主要营业地亦位于 A 州。她在起诉状中主张，自己工作 17 年后被非法解雇，并请求 100,000 美元工资及福利损失。公司收到诉讼文书送达两周后提交答辩状，称该管理人员因未经许可试图对公司拥有的专利进行许可、违反专利法而被解雇。公司同时向联邦法院提交移送通知。23 天后，管理人员提出发回州法院的动议。联邦法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为联邦法院对专利法违法行为享有专属管辖权。","B":"不应，因为发回动议应在提交移送通知后 21 天内提出。","C":"应当，因为公司是 A 州公民，不能将案件移送至联邦法院。","D":"应当，因为公司应在州法院提交移送通知。"},"explanationZh":"案件属于联邦法院原始事项管辖权时，被告可以将其从州法院移送至联邦法院。原始管辖权基于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据美国宪法、联邦条约或联邦法律产生；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方当事人具有不同州籍。\n\n多元州籍管辖权旨在保护外州被告免受州法院潜在偏见。本州被告不存在同样风险。因此，法院州被告规则禁止在以下情况下移送：(1) 事项管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权；且 (2) 被告是提起诉讼所在州的公民。\n\n本题存在多元州籍管辖权，因为争议金额为 100,000 美元，管理人员与公司分别是 B 州和 A 州公民。但是，公司是提起诉讼所在 A 州的公民，所以法院州被告规则禁止移送。联邦法院应批准管理人员的动议，将案件发回州法院。\n\n（选项 A）联邦法院对专利请求享有专属管辖权，任何提出专利请求的当事人都可将案件移送至联邦法院。但公司并未提出专利请求，只是以据称违反专利法来解释解雇原因。\n\n（选项 B）发回动议必须在提交移送通知后 30 天内提出。管理人员在第 23 天提出，属于及时。\n\n（选项 D）移送通知应提交至州法院诉讼待决地同一地区的联邦法院，公司正是如此办理。随后应将通知副本送交各方及州法院。\n\n学习目标：在以下情况下，法院州被告规则禁止移送：(1) 事项管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权；且 (2) 被告是提起诉讼所在州的公民。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(b)(2)（法院州被告规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-001"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-021","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":21,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A beneficiary of a trust, who is a citizen of State A, has sued the trustee in federal court in State A for failing to correctly distribute the income from the trust, seeking an accounting. The trustee was personally served with process and the complaint by the beneficiary's attorney while the trustee was vacationing in State A. The trustee is a citizen of State B, and the accounts that are the subject of the trust are located in State B. What is the trustee's best response to the complaint?","choices":{"A":"Answer the complaint and counterclaim for abuse of process.","B":"File an action in a State B court and move to enjoin the State A action.","C":"Move to dismiss for improper service by the attorney.","D":"Move to dismiss for improper venue."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Venue\n\nProper venue Case may be brought in any federal district where:\n\n(28 U.S.C. § 1391) • any defendant resides, if defendants all reside in same state\n• substantial part of events giving rise to claim occurred or property at issue is located\n• any defendant is subject to court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies) Transfer from proper When venue is proper, district court may transfer case to any venue other proper venue for convenience of parties/witnesses & in interest of justice (28 U.S.C. § 1404)\n\nTransfer or dismissal When venue is improper, district court must either: from improper venue\n• dismiss case (28 U.S.C. § 1406) • transfer case to proper venue (if interest of justice requires) Venue can be established in any federal judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides (only applies when all defendants reside in the same state)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred or a substantial part of the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (only applies if neither of the above provisions applies).\n\nHere, the trustee resides and the trust accounts are located in State B. Additionally, the events giving rise to the suit likely occurred in State B since that is where the trust is administered. This means that venue is proper in State B—not State A, where the suit was filed. Therefore, the trustee's best response is to move to dismiss for improper venue. (Choice A) Abuse of process is a tort claim alleging that a party intentionally misused the legal process for an ulterior, improper purpose. Since there is no indication that the beneficiary sued the trustee for an improper purpose, a counterclaim for abuse of process is not the trustee's best response. (Choice B) If the trustee filed an action in State B, the State B court would only enjoin an action in another court in the extremely rare circumstance in which the party seeking the injunction has no adequate alternative remedy. But here, the trustee does not need\n\ninjunctive relief since the trustee could raise the same issues in the State A action as in a State B action. (Choice C) Service of process is proper if it is personally delivered to the defendant by a person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (eg, the beneficiary's attorney). Educational objective: Venue is proper in any district where (1) any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events giving rise to the suit occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction, if the first two provisions do not apply. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","rule":"Venue is proper in any district where (1) any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events giving rise to the suit occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction, if the first two provisions do not apply.","irac":{"issue":"A beneficiary of a trust, who is a citizen of State A, has sued the trustee in federal court in State A for failing to correctly distribute the income from the trust, seeking an accounting. The trustee was personally served with process and the complaint by the beneficiary's attorney while the trustee was vacationing in State A. The trustee is a citizen of State B, and the accounts that are the subject of the trust are located in State B. What is the trustee's best response to the complaint?","rule":"Venue is proper in any district where (1) any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events giving rise to the suit occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction, if the first two provisions do not apply.","analysis":"Venue\n\nProper venue Case may be brought in any federal district where:\n\n(28 U.S.C. § 1391) • any defendant resides, if defendants all reside in same state\n• substantial part of events giving rise to claim occurred or property at issue is located\n• any defendant is subject to court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies) Transfer from proper When venue is proper, district court may transfer case to any venue other proper venue for convenience of parties/witnesses & in interest of justice (28 U.S.C. § 1404)\n\nTransfer or dismissal When venue is improper, district court must either: from improper venue\n• dismiss case (28 U.S.C. § 1406) • transfer case to proper venue (if interest of justice requires) Venue can be established in any federal judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides (only applies when all defendants reside in the same state)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred or a substantial part of the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (only applies if neither of the above provisions applies).\n\nHere, the trustee resides and the trust accounts are located in State B. Additionally, the events giving rise to the suit likely occurred in State B since that is where the trust is administered. This means that venue is proper in State B—not State A, where the suit was filed. Therefore, the trustee's best response is to move to dismiss for improper venue. (Choice A) Abuse of process is a tort claim alleging that a party intentionally misused the legal process for an ulterior, improper purpose. Since there is no indication that the beneficiary sued the trustee for an improper purpose, a counterclaim for abuse of process is not the trustee's best response. (Choice B) If the trustee filed an action in State B, the State B court would only enjoin an action in another court in the extremely rare circumstance in which the party seeking the injunction has no adequate alternative remedy. But here, the trustee does not need\n\ninjunctive relief since the trustee could raise the same issues in the State A action as in a State B action. (Choice C) Service of process is proper if it is personally delivered to the defendant by a person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (eg, the beneficiary's attorney). Educational objective: Venue is proper in any district where (1) any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events giving rise to the suit occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction, if the first two provisions do not apply. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[63],"sourceAnswerPages":[64,65],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 A 州公民的信托受益人在 A 州联邦法院起诉受托人，主张其未正确分配信托收入，并请求查账。受益人的律师趁受托人在 A 州度假时，向其本人送达诉讼文书和起诉状。受托人是 B 州公民，涉案信托账户也位于 B 州。受托人对起诉状的最佳回应是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"答辩并以滥用诉讼程序为由提出反诉。","B":"在 B 州法院另行起诉，并申请禁止 A 州诉讼。","C":"以律师送达不当为由申请驳回。","D":"以审判地不适当为由申请驳回。"},"explanationZh":"审判地：\n\n适当审判地（28 U.S.C. § 1391）：案件可在以下任何联邦地区提起：\n• 若所有被告均居住在同一州，任何被告居住地；\n• 引起请求的实质部分事件发生地，或涉案财产所在地；\n• 仅在前两项均不适用时，任何被告受法院对人管辖的地区。\n\n从适当审判地转移（28 U.S.C. § 1404）：审判地适当时，为便利当事人和证人并实现司法公正，地区法院可以将案件转移至另一适当审判地。\n\n从不适当审判地转移或驳回（28 U.S.C. § 1406）：审判地不适当时，地区法院必须驳回案件，或在司法公正要求时将其转移至适当审判地。\n\n本题中，受托人居住在 B 州，信托账户也位于 B 州。引起诉讼的事件很可能发生在 B 州，因为信托在那里管理。因此，B 州而非起诉地 A 州是适当审判地。受托人的最佳回应是以审判地不适当为由申请驳回。\n\n（选项 A）滥用诉讼程序是一项侵权请求，指当事人为隐蔽且不当的目的故意滥用法律程序。没有事实表明受益人为不当目的起诉，故这不是最佳回应。\n\n（选项 B）如果受托人在 B 州另行起诉，B 州法院只有在申请禁令者没有充分替代救济的极少情况下，才会禁止另一法院的诉讼。本题中，受托人可以在 A 州诉讼中提出与 B 州诉讼相同的问题，无需禁令救济。\n\n（选项 C）由年满 18 岁且不是诉讼当事人的人（例如受益人的律师）向被告本人交付文书，即构成适当送达。\n\n学习目标：审判地在以下地区适当：(1) 若所有被告均居住于同一州，任何被告居住地；(2) 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地或涉案财产所在地；或 (3) 仅在前两项均不适用时，任何被告受法院对人管辖的地区。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)（适当审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-022","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":22,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"An entrepreneur from State A sued a website owner from State B in a State A federal court. The complaint alleges that the owner breached an advertising contract by failing to prominently display the entrepreneur's latest product on the website's homepage, resulting in lost profits totaling $50,000. The entrepreneur also asserted a defamation claim against the owner for $20,000 in damages. Additionally, the complaint includes a request for the court to award the entrepreneur $7,000 in costs related to the action. The website owner has moved to dismiss the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the court has supplemental jurisdiction over the claims.","B":"Deny the motion, because the parties are diverse and the amount-in-controversy requirement is satisfied.","C":"Grant the motion, because the action does not present a federal question and the amount-in-controversy requirement is not satisfied.","D":"Grant the motion, because the action should have been asserted in a State B federal court."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a federal treaty, or a federal statute (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states (as seen here) and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nThe amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint. To meet the required amount, a single plaintiff may aggregate (ie, combine) joined claims asserted against a single defendant. However, costs and interest are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation. Here, the entrepreneur asserted a $50,000 breach-of-contract claim and a $20,000 defamation claim ($70,000 total) against the website owner. Although the complaint also requested $7,000 in costs related to the action, such costs are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation. Since the $70,000 amount in controversy is inadequate, the federal court lacks diversity jurisdiction (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the owner's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) When a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist over the remaining claims. Here, no claim falls within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, so supplemental jurisdiction does not exist. (Choice D) Whether the action should have been commenced in a State B federal court is irrelevant for determining subject-matter jurisdiction. The location of the federal court is relevant when venue is challenged. Educational objective: Diversity jurisdiction requires that the opposing parties be citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceed $75,000. The amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint (excluding costs and interest). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 959 (2020) (explaining that costs are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation).","rule":"Diversity jurisdiction requires that the opposing parties be citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceed $75,000. The amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint (excluding costs and interest).","irac":{"issue":"An entrepreneur from State A sued a website owner from State B in a State A federal court. The complaint alleges that the owner breached an advertising contract by failing to prominently display the entrepreneur's latest product on the website's homepage, resulting in lost profits totaling $50,000. The entrepreneur also asserted a defamation claim against the owner for $20,000 in damages. Additionally, the complaint includes a request for the court to award the entrepreneur $7,000 in costs related to the action. The website owner has moved to dismiss the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the motion?","rule":"Diversity jurisdiction requires that the opposing parties be citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceed $75,000. The amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint (excluding costs and interest).","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a federal treaty, or a federal statute (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states (as seen here) and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nThe amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint. To meet the required amount, a single plaintiff may aggregate (ie, combine) joined claims asserted against a single defendant. However, costs and interest are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation. Here, the entrepreneur asserted a $50,000 breach-of-contract claim and a $20,000 defamation claim ($70,000 total) against the website owner. Although the complaint also requested $7,000 in costs related to the action, such costs are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation. Since the $70,000 amount in controversy is inadequate, the federal court lacks diversity jurisdiction (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the owner's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) When a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist over the remaining claims. Here, no claim falls within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, so supplemental jurisdiction does not exist. (Choice D) Whether the action should have been commenced in a State B federal court is irrelevant for determining subject-matter jurisdiction. The location of the federal court is relevant when venue is challenged. Educational objective: Diversity jurisdiction requires that the opposing parties be citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceed $75,000. The amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint (excluding costs and interest). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 959 (2020) (explaining that costs are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 959 (2020) (explaining that costs are excluded from the amount-in-controversy calculation).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[66],"sourceAnswerPages":[67],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名企业家在 A 州联邦法院起诉 B 州的一名网站所有者。起诉状主张，所有者未按广告合同约定将企业家的最新产品显著展示在网站首页，造成 50,000 美元利润损失，构成违约；企业家还提出诽谤请求，索赔 20,000 美元。此外，起诉状请求法院判给与诉讼有关的 7,000 美元费用。网站所有者以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为法院对这些请求享有补充管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为双方州籍不同且满足争议金额要求。","C":"批准动议，因为诉讼不构成联邦问题，且不满足争议金额要求。","D":"批准动议，因为诉讼本应在 B 州联邦法院提起。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权可通过以下方式建立：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据美国宪法、联邦条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方是不同州的公民（本题如此），且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n争议金额是原告在起诉状中请求的金额。单一原告可合并计算其针对同一被告提出的多个请求，但诉讼费用和利息不计入争议金额。\n\n企业家针对网站所有者提出 50,000 美元违约请求和 20,000 美元诽谤请求，共计 70,000 美元。起诉状虽另请求 7,000 美元诉讼费用，该费用不得计入争议金额。由于 70,000 美元不足，联邦法院不享有多元州籍管辖权（选项 B），很可能批准所有者的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）争议包含多个请求且仅部分请求属于法院原始事项管辖权时，法院可能对其余请求享有补充管辖权。本题没有任何请求属于原始事项管辖权，故不存在补充管辖权。\n\n（选项 D）诉讼是否应在 B 州联邦法院提起与事项管辖权无关。联邦法院的位置在审判地受到质疑时才相关。\n\n学习目标：多元州籍管辖权要求对立双方为不同州公民，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。争议金额是原告起诉状中请求的金额，不包括费用和利息。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 959 (2020)（费用不计入争议金额）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-012"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-023","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":23,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"Before filing a federal civil action against a seller, a buyer's attorney unsuccessfully tried to settle with the seller's attorney. Three days before the limitations period on the buyer's claim expired, the buyer's attorney told the seller's attorney that she would file a complaint that day and asked the seller's attorney whether he would accept service of the summons and complaint. The seller's attorney agreed to do so. The buyer's attorney promptly filed the complaint but forgot to serve the seller's attorney. Four months later, the buyer's attorney received a voicemail from the seller's attorney asking whether she had ever filed the buyer's complaint. The buyer's attorney immediately mailed a copy of the complaint to the seller's attorney. The following week, the seller's attorney moved to dismiss the complaint for failure to effect timely service of process. Is the court likely to grant the seller's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because under the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, the filing of the complaint commences an action and the buyer's complaint was timely filed.","B":"No, because the seller's attorney had notice of the complaint and agreed to accept service.","C":"Yes, because the buyer's attorney did not show good cause for her failure to effect timely service.","D":"Yes, because the limitations period expired without timely service."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A plaintiff must provide a defendant with notice of a lawsuit through service of process— ie, the delivery of a summons and a complaint. Process must be served on a defendant in the U.S. within 90 days after the complaint is filed.* Here, service of process was untimely because the buyer's attorney failed to effectuate service until four months (120 days) after filing the complaint. When this occurs, the court generally must either:\n• dismiss the action without prejudice or\n• order service of process within a specified time. However, if the plaintiff provides good cause for why service was untimely, the court must extend the time for service for an appropriate period. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control—not the plaintiff's own mistake or ignorance. Since the buyer's attorney simply forgot to serve the seller's attorney, good cause does not exist. Therefore, the court will likely grant the seller's motion to dismiss. *The 90-day deadline for service of process applies to defendants in the U.S. For defendants in a foreign country, most federal courts require that process be served with due diligence. (Choice A) FRCP 3 provides that an action is commenced when the plaintiff files the complaint. However, the plaintiff still must timely serve process or provide good cause for failing to do so (neither seen here). (Choice B) The fact that the seller's attorney had informal notice of the complaint and agreed to accept service of process does not excuse the untimely service of process. This formal notice is required so that a defendant has definitive knowledge that the plaintiff intends to litigate the action in court. (Choice D) A statute of limitations is the statutory deadline for a plaintiff to file that claim in court—not serve process on the defendant. Since the buyer's attorney filed the complaint three days before the limitations period on the claim expired, this is not a reason justifying dismissal. Educational objective: A court must extend the time for a plaintiff to serve the defendant with process when the plaintiff provides good cause for failing to timely effectuate service. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m) (deadline for service of process).\n\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1137 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the general time limit for service of process and the good-cause exception).","rule":"A court must extend the time for a plaintiff to serve the defendant with process when the plaintiff provides good cause for failing to timely effectuate service. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control.","irac":{"issue":"Before filing a federal civil action against a seller, a buyer's attorney unsuccessfully tried to settle with the seller's attorney. Three days before the limitations period on the buyer's claim expired, the buyer's attorney told the seller's attorney that she would file a complaint that day and asked the seller's attorney whether he would accept service of the summons and complaint. The seller's attorney agreed to do so. The buyer's attorney promptly filed the complaint but forgot to serve the seller's attorney. Four months later, the buyer's attorney received a voicemail from the seller's attorney asking whether she had ever filed the buyer's complaint. The buyer's attorney immediately mailed a copy of the complaint to the seller's attorney. The following week, the seller's attorney moved to dismiss the complaint for failure to effect timely service of process. Is the court likely to grant the seller's motion to dismiss?","rule":"A court must extend the time for a plaintiff to serve the defendant with process when the plaintiff provides good cause for failing to timely effectuate service. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control.","analysis":"A plaintiff must provide a defendant with notice of a lawsuit through service of process— ie, the delivery of a summons and a complaint. Process must be served on a defendant in the U.S. within 90 days after the complaint is filed.* Here, service of process was untimely because the buyer's attorney failed to effectuate service until four months (120 days) after filing the complaint. When this occurs, the court generally must either:\n• dismiss the action without prejudice or\n• order service of process within a specified time. However, if the plaintiff provides good cause for why service was untimely, the court must extend the time for service for an appropriate period. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control—not the plaintiff's own mistake or ignorance. Since the buyer's attorney simply forgot to serve the seller's attorney, good cause does not exist. Therefore, the court will likely grant the seller's motion to dismiss. *The 90-day deadline for service of process applies to defendants in the U.S. For defendants in a foreign country, most federal courts require that process be served with due diligence. (Choice A) FRCP 3 provides that an action is commenced when the plaintiff files the complaint. However, the plaintiff still must timely serve process or provide good cause for failing to do so (neither seen here). (Choice B) The fact that the seller's attorney had informal notice of the complaint and agreed to accept service of process does not excuse the untimely service of process. This formal notice is required so that a defendant has definitive knowledge that the plaintiff intends to litigate the action in court. (Choice D) A statute of limitations is the statutory deadline for a plaintiff to file that claim in court—not serve process on the defendant. Since the buyer's attorney filed the complaint three days before the limitations period on the claim expired, this is not a reason justifying dismissal. Educational objective: A court must extend the time for a plaintiff to serve the defendant with process when the plaintiff provides good cause for failing to timely effectuate service. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m) (deadline for service of process).\n\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1137 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the general time limit for service of process and the good-cause exception).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m) (deadline for service of process).\n\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1137 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the general time limit for service of process and the good-cause exception).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[69],"sourceAnswerPages":[70,71],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名买方的律师在对卖方提起联邦民事诉讼前，曾与卖方律师尝试和解但未成功。买方请求权的诉讼时效届满前三天，买方律师告知卖方律师她会在当天提交起诉状，并询问其是否愿意接受传票和起诉状送达。卖方律师同意。买方律师及时提交起诉状，却忘记向卖方律师送达。四个月后，买方律师收到卖方律师的语音留言，询问她是否曾提交起诉状。买方律师立即将起诉状副本邮寄给卖方律师。次周，卖方律师以未及时送达为由申请驳回。法院是否可能批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为依《联邦民事诉讼规则》，提交起诉状即开始诉讼，而买方起诉状已及时提交。","B":"不会，因为卖方律师已知悉起诉状且同意接受送达。","C":"会，因为买方律师未能说明未及时送达的正当理由。","D":"会，因为诉讼时效在未及时送达的情况下届满。"},"explanationZh":"原告必须通过诉讼文书送达，即交付传票和起诉状，向被告提供诉讼通知。对美国境内被告，必须在提交起诉状后 90 天内完成送达。*\n\n本题直至提交起诉状四个月（120 天）后才完成送达，属于逾期。发生逾期时，法院通常必须：\n• 不妨碍再次起诉地驳回诉讼；或者\n• 命令在指定期限内完成送达。\n\n但是，如果原告说明逾期送达具有正当理由，法院必须适当延长送达期限。只有送达因原告无法控制的事项而延误，而非原告自身错误或无知时，才存在正当理由。买方律师只是忘记送达，不构成正当理由。因此，法院很可能批准卖方的驳回动议。\n\n*90 天送达期限适用于美国境内被告。对位于外国的被告，多数联邦法院要求原告尽到适当勤勉。\n\n（选项 A）《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 3 条规定，原告提交起诉状时诉讼开始。但原告仍须及时送达，或对未及时送达说明正当理由，本题两者均不存在。\n\n（选项 B）卖方律师非正式知悉起诉状且同意接受送达，不能免除及时完成正式送达的要求。正式通知使被告明确知道原告确实拟在法院进行诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）诉讼时效是原告向法院提交请求的法定期限，而不是向被告送达文书的期限。买方律师在时效届满前三天提交起诉状，故这不是驳回理由。\n\n学习目标：原告说明未及时送达具有正当理由时，法院必须延长送达期限。送达因原告无法控制的事项延误时，才构成正当理由。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m)（送达期限）。\n• 4B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1137 (4th ed. 2020)（一般送达期限及正当理由例外）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-013"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-024","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":24,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"An investor from State A filed an action against his State B stockbroker in federal court in State A. The summons and complaint were served at the stockbroker's office in State B, where the process server handed the documents to the stockbroker's administrative assistant. The stockbroker has answered the complaint, asserting the defense of improper service of process. Assume that both states' requirements for service of process are identical to the requirements of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. Is the court likely to dismiss the action for improper service of process?","choices":{"A":"No, because service was made on a person of suitable age found at the stockbroker's place of employment.","B":"No, because the stockbroker waived her claim for improper service of process by asserting it in her answer.","C":"Yes, because an individual defendant may not be served by delivering process to a third party found at the defendant's place of employment.","D":"Yes, because the process of State A courts is not effective in State B."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant receives notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of a summons and complaint. Under FRCP 4, a defendant who is an individual in the U.S. can be properly served with process within 90 days after the original complaint* is filed by:\n• following the rules for serving process in the state where the court is located or the state where service is made\n\n• delivering process to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized by appointment or law to receive service of process) or\n\n• leaving process at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion.\n\nHere, the service-of-process requirements for State A (where the court is located) and State B (where service was made) are identical to those of FRCP 4. But the process server delivered the summons and complaint to the stockbroker's administrative assistant—not to the stockbroker personally or to her authorized agent. And though the assistant was likely of suitable age and discretion, the documents were left at the stockbroker's office—not her dwelling (Choice A). Therefore, the court will likely dismiss the action for improper service of process. *Copies of other pleadings (eg, amended complaint, answer), motions, and other papers must also be served (ie, delivered) on the opposing party; however, a summons is not required and the deadline for service is governed by other procedural rules. (Choice B) A claim for improper service of process is waived if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. Therefore, the stockbroker preserved this claim by asserting it in her answer since no pre-answer motion had been filed. (Choice D) Service of process is proper if it complies with the rules of the state where service is made or where the court is located—even when service is made in a different state. Therefore, service of process under the rules of State A courts can be effective in State B. Educational objective: Process can be served by (1) following the rules of the state where the court is located or where service is made, (2) delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant personally (or to his/her authorized agent), or (3) leaving the summons and complaint at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (serving process on an individual in the U.S.).","rule":"Process can be served by (1) following the rules of the state where the court is located or where service is made, (2) delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant personally (or to his/her authorized agent), or (3) leaving the summons and complaint at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","irac":{"issue":"An investor from State A filed an action against his State B stockbroker in federal court in State A. The summons and complaint were served at the stockbroker's office in State B, where the process server handed the documents to the stockbroker's administrative assistant. The stockbroker has answered the complaint, asserting the defense of improper service of process. Assume that both states' requirements for service of process are identical to the requirements of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. Is the court likely to dismiss the action for improper service of process?","rule":"Process can be served by (1) following the rules of the state where the court is located or where service is made, (2) delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant personally (or to his/her authorized agent), or (3) leaving the summons and complaint at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","analysis":"A defendant receives notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of a summons and complaint. Under FRCP 4, a defendant who is an individual in the U.S. can be properly served with process within 90 days after the original complaint* is filed by:\n• following the rules for serving process in the state where the court is located or the state where service is made\n\n• delivering process to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized by appointment or law to receive service of process) or\n\n• leaving process at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion.\n\nHere, the service-of-process requirements for State A (where the court is located) and State B (where service was made) are identical to those of FRCP 4. But the process server delivered the summons and complaint to the stockbroker's administrative assistant—not to the stockbroker personally or to her authorized agent. And though the assistant was likely of suitable age and discretion, the documents were left at the stockbroker's office—not her dwelling (Choice A). Therefore, the court will likely dismiss the action for improper service of process. *Copies of other pleadings (eg, amended complaint, answer), motions, and other papers must also be served (ie, delivered) on the opposing party; however, a summons is not required and the deadline for service is governed by other procedural rules. (Choice B) A claim for improper service of process is waived if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. Therefore, the stockbroker preserved this claim by asserting it in her answer since no pre-answer motion had been filed. (Choice D) Service of process is proper if it complies with the rules of the state where service is made or where the court is located—even when service is made in a different state. Therefore, service of process under the rules of State A courts can be effective in State B. Educational objective: Process can be served by (1) following the rules of the state where the court is located or where service is made, (2) delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant personally (or to his/her authorized agent), or (3) leaving the summons and complaint at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (serving process on an individual in the U.S.).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (serving process on an individual in the U.S.).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[72],"sourceAnswerPages":[73,74],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名投资者在 A 州联邦法院起诉其 B 州股票经纪人。送达人在股票经纪人位于 B 州的办公室，将传票和起诉状交给其行政助理。股票经纪人在答辩状中提出送达不当的抗辩。假定两州的送达要求均与《联邦民事诉讼规则》相同，法院是否可能因送达不当而驳回诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为文书已在股票经纪人的工作地点交给一名年龄适当的人。","B":"不会，因为股票经纪人在答辩状中提出送达不当，已放弃该主张。","C":"会，因为不得通过向个人被告工作地点的第三人交付文书来向该被告送达。","D":"会，因为 A 州法院签发的文书在 B 州无效。"},"explanationZh":"被告通过诉讼文书送达，即交付传票和起诉状，获得诉讼通知。根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 4 条，对于位于美国境内的个人被告，可在提交原始起诉状后 90 天内通过以下方式适当送达：\n• 遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州的送达规则；\n• 向被告本人交付文书，或向依约定或法律获授权接受送达的代理人交付；或者\n• 将文书留在被告住所，交给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人。\n\n本题中，A 州和 B 州的要求均与规则第 4 条相同。但送达人将传票和起诉状交给股票经纪人的行政助理，而非经纪人本人或其获授权代理人。即使助理年龄及判断能力可能适当，文书也是留在办公室，而非经纪人住所（选项 A）。因此，法院很可能因送达不当而驳回诉讼。\n\n*其他诉状（例如修订起诉状、答辩状）、动议及文件的副本同样必须送达对方，但无需传票，送达期限也由其他程序规则规定。\n\n（选项 B）若未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出送达不当，该抗辩即被放弃。股票经纪人在未提出答辩前动议的情况下于答辩状中提出，已经保留该主张。\n\n（选项 D）只要送达符合实际送达地州或法院所在地州的规则，即使跨州完成，仍属适当。因此，依 A 州法院规则签发的文书可以在 B 州有效送达。\n\n学习目标：可以通过以下方式送达：(1) 遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州的规则；(2) 向被告本人或其获授权代理人交付传票和起诉状；或 (3) 将传票和起诉状留在被告住所，交给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e)（向美国境内个人送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-014"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-025","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":25,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A bank sued a foreign investor for fraud in a federal court in State A. The bank was unable to ascertain the investor's primary address for 120 days after filing its complaint, but immediately thereafter mailed the investor a copy of the summons and complaint through certified mail to that address. The investor, upon receipt of the summons and complaint, retained counsel in State A. State A permits service of process on an individual outside its borders by mailing the summons and complaint to the individual's primary residence. The foreign country where the investor resides is not a signatory to any international agreement that provides service-of-process rules, and there is no applicable federal statute. The foreign country does not permit service by mail. The investor has moved to dismiss the suit for improper service of process. What is the investor's best argument in support of the motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"The bank did not request that the federal court send a letter rogatory.","B":"The bank served the summons and complaint more than 45 days after filing its complaint with the court.","C":"The foreign country does not permit service by mail.","D":"The investor can only be served by an internationally agreed method of service."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Methods of serving process in foreign country\n\nIndividual or • Following international agreement reasonably calculated to organization give notice\n• If no international agreement & not prohibited by federal law: – following foreign country's rules – as foreign country directs in response to letter rogatory or – unless prohibited by foreign country, by personal delivery* or mail with request for signed return receipt\n• Following court order, unless prohibited by international agreement Foreign • Following special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign government state\n• If no special arrangement, by international convention\n• If no convention, by mail to head of foreign affairs ministry with request for signed return receipt\n• If service by mail cannot be made within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt *Organizations cannot be served by personal delivery in foreign countries.\n\nA plaintiff must give a defendant notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of the summons and complaint. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4(f), a defendant who is an individual can be served in a foreign country by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice (Choice D). However, if there is no such method and no applicable federal law (as seen here), then the defendant can be served:\n• by following the foreign country's rules for service of process\n\n• as the foreign country directs in response to a letter rogatory—ie, a letter from a domestic court asking a foreign court for assistance or\n\n• by delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant personally or by mail with a request for a signed return receipt (eg, certified mail)—unless prohibited by the foreign country.*\n\nHere, the bank served the investor with a copy of the summons and complaint by certified mail. However, this method of service is prohibited by the foreign country. Therefore, this is the investor's best argument in support of the motion to dismiss. *An individual in a foreign country can also be served by any other court-ordered method that does not violate an international agreement. (Choice A) While asking the foreign country for assistance through a letter rogatory is one method to properly serve a foreign defendant, it is not the only method. (Choice B) Absent good cause, a defendant located in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days (not 45 days) after the complaint is filed. However, this 90-day deadline does not apply to a defendant located in a foreign country. Instead, most federal courts require that the plaintiff exercise due diligence in attempting to serve such a defendant. Educational objective: When no internationally agreed method of service or applicable federal law exists, an individual defendant in a foreign country can be served (1) by following the foreign country's service-of-process rules, (2) as the foreign country directs in response to a letter rogatory, or (3) by delivering process to the defendant personally or by mail—if permitted by the foreign country. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) (service on individual in foreign country).","rule":"When no internationally agreed method of service or applicable federal law exists, an individual defendant in a foreign country can be served (1) by following the foreign country's service-of-process rules, (2) as the foreign country directs in response to a letter rogatory, or (3) by delivering process to the defendant personally or by mail—if permitted by the foreign country.","irac":{"issue":"A bank sued a foreign investor for fraud in a federal court in State A. The bank was unable to ascertain the investor's primary address for 120 days after filing its complaint, but immediately thereafter mailed the investor a copy of the summons and complaint through certified mail to that address. The investor, upon receipt of the summons and complaint, retained counsel in State A. State A permits service of process on an individual outside its borders by mailing the summons and complaint to the individual's primary residence. The foreign country where the investor resides is not a signatory to any international agreement that provides service-of-process rules, and there is no applicable federal statute. The foreign country does not permit service by mail. The investor has moved to dismiss the suit for improper service of process. What is the investor's best argument in support of the motion to dismiss?","rule":"When no internationally agreed method of service or applicable federal law exists, an individual defendant in a foreign country can be served (1) by following the foreign country's service-of-process rules, (2) as the foreign country directs in response to a letter rogatory, or (3) by delivering process to the defendant personally or by mail—if permitted by the foreign country.","analysis":"Methods of serving process in foreign country\n\nIndividual or • Following international agreement reasonably calculated to organization give notice\n• If no international agreement & not prohibited by federal law: – following foreign country's rules – as foreign country directs in response to letter rogatory or – unless prohibited by foreign country, by personal delivery* or mail with request for signed return receipt\n• Following court order, unless prohibited by international agreement Foreign • Following special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign government state\n• If no special arrangement, by international convention\n• If no convention, by mail to head of foreign affairs ministry with request for signed return receipt\n• If service by mail cannot be made within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt *Organizations cannot be served by personal delivery in foreign countries.\n\nA plaintiff must give a defendant notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of the summons and complaint. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4(f), a defendant who is an individual can be served in a foreign country by any internationally agreed method of service that is reasonably calculated to give notice (Choice D). However, if there is no such method and no applicable federal law (as seen here), then the defendant can be served:\n• by following the foreign country's rules for service of process\n\n• as the foreign country directs in response to a letter rogatory—ie, a letter from a domestic court asking a foreign court for assistance or\n\n• by delivering the summons and complaint to the defendant personally or by mail with a request for a signed return receipt (eg, certified mail)—unless prohibited by the foreign country.*\n\nHere, the bank served the investor with a copy of the summons and complaint by certified mail. However, this method of service is prohibited by the foreign country. Therefore, this is the investor's best argument in support of the motion to dismiss. *An individual in a foreign country can also be served by any other court-ordered method that does not violate an international agreement. (Choice A) While asking the foreign country for assistance through a letter rogatory is one method to properly serve a foreign defendant, it is not the only method. (Choice B) Absent good cause, a defendant located in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days (not 45 days) after the complaint is filed. However, this 90-day deadline does not apply to a defendant located in a foreign country. Instead, most federal courts require that the plaintiff exercise due diligence in attempting to serve such a defendant. Educational objective: When no internationally agreed method of service or applicable federal law exists, an individual defendant in a foreign country can be served (1) by following the foreign country's service-of-process rules, (2) as the foreign country directs in response to a letter rogatory, or (3) by delivering process to the defendant personally or by mail—if permitted by the foreign country. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) (service on individual in foreign country).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f) (service on individual in foreign country).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[75],"sourceAnswerPages":[76,77],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家银行在 A 州联邦法院以欺诈为由起诉一名外国投资者。银行在提交起诉状后 120 天内无法查明投资者的主要地址，但查明后立即通过挂号邮件将传票和起诉状副本寄往该地址。投资者收到文书后在 A 州聘请律师。A 州允许通过将传票和起诉状邮寄至个人主要住所的方式，向州外个人送达。投资者居住国不是任何规定送达规则的国际协议缔约国，也无适用的联邦法律。该外国不允许邮寄送达。投资者以送达不当为由申请驳回。其支持该动议的最佳理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行未请求联邦法院发出司法协助请求书。","B":"银行在提交起诉状超过 45 天后才送达传票和起诉状。","C":"该外国不允许邮寄送达。","D":"只能依国际约定的送达方式向投资者送达。"},"explanationZh":"在外国送达个人或组织的方法包括：\n• 遵循足以合理实现通知的国际协议；\n• 如无国际协议且联邦法律不禁止，可遵循外国规则、依外国对司法协助请求书的答复所指定的方式，或在该外国不禁止时亲自交付*或以要求签收回执的邮件送达；\n• 遵循法院命令，但不得违反国际协议。\n\n向外国国家送达时，可依原告与外国政府的特别安排；无特别安排时依国际公约；无公约时向该国外交部门负责人邮寄并要求签收；30 天内无法邮寄送达时，可用要求签收的邮件向美国国务卿寄送两份副本。\n\n*外国组织不能通过亲自交付方式接受送达。\n\n原告必须通过交付传票和起诉状向被告提供诉讼通知。根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 4(f) 条，可以通过任何在具体情形下足以合理实现通知的国际约定方式，向外国的个人被告送达（选项 D）。但若无此种方式且无适用联邦法律，则可以：\n• 遵循该外国的送达规则；\n• 依该外国对司法协助请求书的答复所指定的方式；或\n• 在该外国不禁止时，向被告本人交付，或以要求签收回执的邮件（例如挂号邮件）送达。*\n\n银行以挂号邮件向投资者送达，但该方式为投资者居住国所禁止。因此，这是支持驳回动议的最佳理由。\n\n*还可以使用法院命令且不违反国际协议的其他方式向外国个人送达。\n\n（选项 A）通过司法协助请求书请求外国协助，是适当送达外国被告的方法之一，但不是唯一方法。\n\n（选项 B）在无正当理由时，美国境内被告应在提交起诉状后 90 天内而非 45 天内获得送达。该 90 天期限不适用于外国被告；多数联邦法院转而要求原告尽到适当勤勉。\n\n学习目标：不存在国际约定送达方式或适用联邦法律时，可通过以下方式向外国个人被告送达：(1) 遵循外国送达规则；(2) 依外国对司法协助请求书的答复；或 (3) 在外国允许时亲自交付或邮寄送达。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(f)（向外国个人送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-026","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":26,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A journalist domiciled in State A entered into a contract to write an article about a professor who works in State B and is domiciled in State C. The journalist and the professor negotiated and signed the contract in State A. When the journalist failed to produce the article by the deadline stated in the contract, the professor sued the journalist for breach of contract in a federal district court in State B. The following week, the journalist traveled to State B for a court proceeding unrelated to the professor's suit. The professor had the journalist served with process while the journalist walked into the courthouse. The journalist had never traveled to State B prior to this court appearance and has no other contacts with State B. The journalist has moved to dismiss the professor's suit, asserting that the federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over him. Is the journalist's assertion correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because the journalist established minimum contacts with State B by traveling to the state.","B":"No, because the professor had the journalist served with process in State B.","C":"Yes, because the journalist was in State B to attend an unrelated court proceeding.","D":"Yes, because the professor is domiciled in State C."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction is a court's authority over the parties or property before it. One way a federal court can establish personal jurisdiction over a defendant is through tag jurisdiction (ie, transient jurisdiction). Tag jurisdiction exists when the defendant is served with process while voluntarily present in the forum state. As a result, tag jurisdiction does not exist when the defendant was:\n• fraudulently or forcefully brought into the forum state to be served with process or\n• present in the forum state to attend an unrelated judicial proceeding. Here, the professor had the journalist served with process in State B, the forum state where the federal court is located. But since the journalist was in State B for an unrelated judicial proceeding, tag jurisdiction does not exist (Choice B). Therefore, the journalist is correct that the federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over him. (Choice A) Personal jurisdiction is established through specific jurisdiction when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state. In the unlikely event that the journalist's single trip to State B established minimum contacts, specific jurisdiction does not exist because the claim does not arise from nor is it closely related to that trip. (Choice D) The fact that the professor (plaintiff) is domiciled in State C is irrelevant in determining whether the State B federal court has personal jurisdiction over the journalist (defendant). Because a plaintiff consents to personal jurisdiction by filing the suit, only a defendant's domicile is relevant in determining whether personal jurisdiction can be established through general jurisdiction. Educational objective: A federal court obtains personal jurisdiction over a defendant served with process while voluntarily present in the forum state unless the defendant was (1) fraudulently or forcefully brought into the state to be served with process or (2) present in the state to attend an unrelated judicial proceeding. References\n• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 613 (1990) (explaining that tag jurisdiction exists unless the defendant was fraudulently or forcefully brought into the forum state or is present for another judicial proceeding).","rule":"A federal court obtains personal jurisdiction over a defendant served with process while voluntarily present in the forum state unless the defendant was (1) fraudulently or forcefully brought into the state to be served with process or (2) present in the state to attend an unrelated judicial proceeding.","irac":{"issue":"A journalist domiciled in State A entered into a contract to write an article about a professor who works in State B and is domiciled in State C. The journalist and the professor negotiated and signed the contract in State A. When the journalist failed to produce the article by the deadline stated in the contract, the professor sued the journalist for breach of contract in a federal district court in State B. The following week, the journalist traveled to State B for a court proceeding unrelated to the professor's suit. The professor had the journalist served with process while the journalist walked into the courthouse. The journalist had never traveled to State B prior to this court appearance and has no other contacts with State B. The journalist has moved to dismiss the professor's suit, asserting that the federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over him. Is the journalist's assertion correct?","rule":"A federal court obtains personal jurisdiction over a defendant served with process while voluntarily present in the forum state unless the defendant was (1) fraudulently or forcefully brought into the state to be served with process or (2) present in the state to attend an unrelated judicial proceeding.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction is a court's authority over the parties or property before it. One way a federal court can establish personal jurisdiction over a defendant is through tag jurisdiction (ie, transient jurisdiction). Tag jurisdiction exists when the defendant is served with process while voluntarily present in the forum state. As a result, tag jurisdiction does not exist when the defendant was:\n• fraudulently or forcefully brought into the forum state to be served with process or\n• present in the forum state to attend an unrelated judicial proceeding. Here, the professor had the journalist served with process in State B, the forum state where the federal court is located. But since the journalist was in State B for an unrelated judicial proceeding, tag jurisdiction does not exist (Choice B). Therefore, the journalist is correct that the federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over him. (Choice A) Personal jurisdiction is established through specific jurisdiction when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state. In the unlikely event that the journalist's single trip to State B established minimum contacts, specific jurisdiction does not exist because the claim does not arise from nor is it closely related to that trip. (Choice D) The fact that the professor (plaintiff) is domiciled in State C is irrelevant in determining whether the State B federal court has personal jurisdiction over the journalist (defendant). Because a plaintiff consents to personal jurisdiction by filing the suit, only a defendant's domicile is relevant in determining whether personal jurisdiction can be established through general jurisdiction. Educational objective: A federal court obtains personal jurisdiction over a defendant served with process while voluntarily present in the forum state unless the defendant was (1) fraudulently or forcefully brought into the state to be served with process or (2) present in the state to attend an unrelated judicial proceeding. References\n• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 613 (1990) (explaining that tag jurisdiction exists unless the defendant was fraudulently or forcefully brought into the forum state or is present for another judicial proceeding).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 613 (1990) (explaining that tag jurisdiction exists unless the defendant was fraudulently or forcefully brought into the forum state or is present for another judicial proceeding).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[78],"sourceAnswerPages":[79],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名记者订立合同，为一名在 B 州工作、居住于 C 州的教授撰写文章。双方在 A 州谈判并签署合同。记者未在合同期限内交稿，教授遂在 B 州联邦地区法院以违约为由起诉。次周，记者因与教授诉讼无关的另一法院程序前往 B 州。记者进入法院时，教授安排向其送达诉讼文书。记者此前从未到过 B 州，与 B 州也无其他联系。记者以联邦法院对其缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。记者的主张是否正确？","choicesZh":{"A":"不正确，因为记者前往 B 州即与该州建立了最低限度联系。","B":"不正确，因为教授已在 B 州向记者送达诉讼文书。","C":"正确，因为记者前往 B 州是为了参加另一项无关法院程序。","D":"正确，因为教授居住于 C 州。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权是法院对其面前当事人或财产的权限。联邦法院建立对人管辖权的一种方法是“标签管辖权”（即临时在场管辖权）：被告自愿身处法院州时获得送达，法院即享有管辖权。但下列情况下不存在标签管辖权：\n• 被告因欺诈或强制被带入法院州接受送达；或\n• 被告身处法院州是为了参加另一项无关司法程序。\n\n教授虽在法院所在地 B 州向记者送达，但记者是为另一项无关司法程序到场，所以不存在标签管辖权（选项 B）。因此，记者关于联邦法院缺乏对人管辖权的主张正确。\n\n（选项 A）若原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系，法院可通过特定管辖权建立对人管辖权。即使记者单次前往 B 州足以构成最低限度联系，请求也并非源于或关联于该次行程，故不存在特定管辖权。\n\n（选项 D）原告教授居住于 C 州，与 B 州联邦法院是否对被告记者享有对人管辖权无关。原告通过起诉同意法院的对人管辖；判断一般管辖权时，仅被告住所相关。\n\n学习目标：被告自愿身处法院州时获得送达，联邦法院通常取得对人管辖权；但被告因欺诈或强制被带入该州，或为参加另一无关司法程序而到场时除外。\n\n参考资料：\n• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 613 (1990)（说明标签管辖权的例外）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-004"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-027","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":27,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A tire company is incorporated and has its principal place of business in Asia. The company assembles all its tires in Asia and sells them throughout the world. The company purchases tire valves from a manufacturer incorporated and headquartered in Europe. All of the valves are manufactured in Europe and sold exclusively to tire companies in Asia. The manufacturer was aware that tires with its valves were sold in State A, but it never contemplated that its sales to the company would subject it to suit there. A tire assembled by the company exploded when a motorcyclist from State A was driving on a road in State A. The motorcyclist sued the company in a federal court in State A to recover $225,000 in medical expenses and property damage. The motorcyclist alleged that the valve in the tire was defective and caused the accident. The company impleaded the manufacturer for indemnification. The motorcyclist settled his claim with the company, which left the company's indemnity claim against the manufacturer. In its answer, the manufacturer moved to dismiss the indemnity claim for lack of personal jurisdiction. State A has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction to the full extent allowed under the U.S. Constitution. How will the federal court likely rule on the manufacturer's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the manufacturer has continuous, systemic, and substantial contacts with State A.","B":"Deny the motion, because the manufacturer waived any objection to personal jurisdiction by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion.","C":"Grant the motion, because the manufacturer did not purposefully avail itself of State A and exercising jurisdiction would offend notions of fair play and substantial justice.","D":"Grant the motion, because the manufacturer never contemplated that its sales to the company would subject it to suit in State A."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Requirements for specific jurisdiction\n\nLong-arm Forum state statute must authorize jurisdiction under specific circumstances statute\n\nDue Minimum Purposeful availment of forum state's laws so suit is process contacts foreseeable:\n\n• Stream of commerce test – defendant places goods in stream of commerce & takes action that intentionally targets forum state\n• Effects test – defendant commits intentional tort in one state with intent that effects will be felt in forum state Fair play & Reasonableness factors: substantial justice • Burden on defendant to appear & defend in forum state\n• Plaintiff's interest in obtaining relief in forum state\n• Forum state's interest\n• Interstate judicial system's interest in obtaining most efficient resolution of disputes\n• Interest of several states in furthering fundamental substantive social policies Long-arm statutes specify when a court within the forum state can exercise specific personal jurisdiction over a nonresident defendant (eg, European manufacturer). However, due process only permits application of that statute when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. Minimum contacts exist when the defendant purposefully avails itself of the state's protections and benefits so that it should reasonably foresee being sued there. For a defendant-manufacturer, this occurs when it places goods in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets the forum state (eg, advertises there). Here, the manufacturer's valves happened to be (but were not purposely) sold in State A, so no minimum contacts exist.\n\nEven if minimum contacts existed, the court would still dismiss the company's indemnity claim since exercising jurisdiction would offend notions of fair play and substantial justice. That is because:\n• adjudicating the claim in State A would significantly burden the European manufacturer\n\n• the company has minimal interest in adjudicating its claim there since it can be asserted in a foreign judicial forum\n\n• State A has no interest in this dispute since the motorcyclist (State A citizen) has already been compensated through the settlement and\n\n• the interstate judicial system and other states have little interest in the indemnity dispute since neither party resides in the U.S.\n\n(Choice A) General personal jurisdiction exists when the defendant's continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state are so substantial that the defendant is \"at home\" there. Here, the manufacturer has no such contacts with State A because it is domiciled in Europe. (Choice B) An objection to personal jurisdiction must be asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. Therefore, the manufacturer's objection was not waived. (Choice D) Minimum contacts only require that the defendant reasonably foresee—not actually contemplate—that it could be sued in the forum state. Educational objective: A defendant-manufacturer has minimum contacts with the forum state when it places goods in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets that state. References\n• Asahi Metal Indus. Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 480 U.S. 102, 114–16 (1987) (holding that the exercise of personal jurisdiction over a suit between foreign parties offended notions of fair play and substantial justice).\n\n• J. McIntyre Mach., Ltd. v. Nicastro, 564 U.S. 873, 886–87 (2011) (Kennedy, J., plurality) (reaffirming that purposeful availment requires a defendant-manufacturer to purposefully target the forum state).","rule":"A defendant-manufacturer has minimum contacts with the forum state when it places goods in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets that state.","irac":{"issue":"A tire company is incorporated and has its principal place of business in Asia. The company assembles all its tires in Asia and sells them throughout the world. The company purchases tire valves from a manufacturer incorporated and headquartered in Europe. All of the valves are manufactured in Europe and sold exclusively to tire companies in Asia. The manufacturer was aware that tires with its valves were sold in State A, but it never contemplated that its sales to the company would subject it to suit there. A tire assembled by the company exploded when a motorcyclist from State A was driving on a road in State A. The motorcyclist sued the company in a federal court in State A to recover $225,000 in medical expenses and property damage. The motorcyclist alleged that the valve in the tire was defective and caused the accident. The company impleaded the manufacturer for indemnification. The motorcyclist settled his claim with the company, which left the company's indemnity claim against the manufacturer. In its answer, the manufacturer moved to dismiss the indemnity claim for lack of personal jurisdiction. State A has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction to the full extent allowed under the U.S. Constitution. How will the federal court likely rule on the manufacturer's motion to dismiss?","rule":"A defendant-manufacturer has minimum contacts with the forum state when it places goods in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets that state.","analysis":"Requirements for specific jurisdiction\n\nLong-arm Forum state statute must authorize jurisdiction under specific circumstances statute\n\nDue Minimum Purposeful availment of forum state's laws so suit is process contacts foreseeable:\n\n• Stream of commerce test – defendant places goods in stream of commerce & takes action that intentionally targets forum state\n• Effects test – defendant commits intentional tort in one state with intent that effects will be felt in forum state Fair play & Reasonableness factors: substantial justice • Burden on defendant to appear & defend in forum state\n• Plaintiff's interest in obtaining relief in forum state\n• Forum state's interest\n• Interstate judicial system's interest in obtaining most efficient resolution of disputes\n• Interest of several states in furthering fundamental substantive social policies Long-arm statutes specify when a court within the forum state can exercise specific personal jurisdiction over a nonresident defendant (eg, European manufacturer). However, due process only permits application of that statute when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. Minimum contacts exist when the defendant purposefully avails itself of the state's protections and benefits so that it should reasonably foresee being sued there. For a defendant-manufacturer, this occurs when it places goods in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets the forum state (eg, advertises there). Here, the manufacturer's valves happened to be (but were not purposely) sold in State A, so no minimum contacts exist.\n\nEven if minimum contacts existed, the court would still dismiss the company's indemnity claim since exercising jurisdiction would offend notions of fair play and substantial justice. That is because:\n• adjudicating the claim in State A would significantly burden the European manufacturer\n\n• the company has minimal interest in adjudicating its claim there since it can be asserted in a foreign judicial forum\n\n• State A has no interest in this dispute since the motorcyclist (State A citizen) has already been compensated through the settlement and\n\n• the interstate judicial system and other states have little interest in the indemnity dispute since neither party resides in the U.S.\n\n(Choice A) General personal jurisdiction exists when the defendant's continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state are so substantial that the defendant is \"at home\" there. Here, the manufacturer has no such contacts with State A because it is domiciled in Europe. (Choice B) An objection to personal jurisdiction must be asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. Therefore, the manufacturer's objection was not waived. (Choice D) Minimum contacts only require that the defendant reasonably foresee—not actually contemplate—that it could be sued in the forum state. Educational objective: A defendant-manufacturer has minimum contacts with the forum state when it places goods in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets that state. References\n• Asahi Metal Indus. Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 480 U.S. 102, 114–16 (1987) (holding that the exercise of personal jurisdiction over a suit between foreign parties offended notions of fair play and substantial justice).\n\n• J. McIntyre Mach., Ltd. v. Nicastro, 564 U.S. 873, 886–87 (2011) (Kennedy, J., plurality) (reaffirming that purposeful availment requires a defendant-manufacturer to purposefully target the forum state).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Asahi Metal Indus. Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 480 U.S. 102, 114–16 (1987) (holding that the exercise of personal jurisdiction over a suit between foreign parties offended notions of fair play and substantial justice).\n\n• J. McIntyre Mach., Ltd. v. Nicastro, 564 U.S. 873, 886–87 (2011) (Kennedy, J., plurality) (reaffirming that purposeful availment requires a defendant-manufacturer to purposefully target the forum state).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[81],"sourceAnswerPages":[82,83],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家轮胎公司在亚洲注册成立，主要营业地亦在亚洲，并在亚洲组装全部轮胎后销往全球。公司从一家在欧洲注册成立且总部位于欧洲的制造商购买气门。全部气门均在欧洲制造，并只销售给亚洲轮胎公司。制造商知道装有其气门的轮胎会在 A 州销售，但从未认为向该公司销售会使其在 A 州被诉。一名 A 州摩托车手在 A 州道路行驶时，轮胎爆炸。他在 A 州联邦法院起诉轮胎公司，索赔 225,000 美元医疗费和财产损失，并主张气门存在缺陷导致事故。轮胎公司将制造商列为第三方被告，请求补偿。摩托车手与轮胎公司和解后，仅剩轮胎公司对制造商的补偿请求。制造商在答辩状中以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。A 州长臂法允许其法院在美国宪法允许的全部范围内行使对人管辖权。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为制造商与 A 州存在持续、系统且实质的联系。","B":"驳回动议，因为制造商未在答辩前动议中提出，已放弃对人管辖权异议。","C":"批准动议，因为制造商没有有目的地利用 A 州利益，行使管辖权也违反公平处理和实质正义。","D":"批准动议，因为制造商从未实际想到向轮胎公司销售会使其在 A 州被诉。"},"explanationZh":"特定管辖权要求：\n\n• 长臂法：法院州法律必须授权在具体情形下行使管辖权。\n• 正当程序／最低限度联系：被告须有目的地利用法院州法律，使在该州被诉具有可预见性。\n  – 商业流通测试：被告将商品投入商业流通，并采取有意针对法院州的行动。\n  – 效果测试：被告在一州实施故意侵权，并意图使其效果在法院州发生。\n• 公平处理和实质正义：考量被告赴法院州应诉的负担、原告在当地获得救济的利益、法院州利益、州际司法系统高效解决争议的利益，以及各州促进基本实体社会政策的利益。\n\n长臂法规定法院何时可以对非居民被告行使特定对人管辖权。但正当程序仅在以下情况下允许适用：\n• 原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系；并且\n• 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义。\n\n被告有目的地利用该州的保护和利益，从而应当合理预见会在那里被诉时，存在最低限度联系。对制造商而言，必须将商品投入商业流通，并另外有目的地针对法院州，例如在那里做广告。制造商的气门只是偶然而非有目的地在 A 州出售，所以不存在最低限度联系。\n\n即使存在最低限度联系，法院仍应驳回补偿请求，因为在 A 州行使管辖权违反公平处理和实质正义：\n• 在 A 州裁判会给欧洲制造商造成重大负担；\n• 轮胎公司可以在外国法院提出请求，对在 A 州裁判仅有很小利益；\n• A 州摩托车手已通过和解得到补偿，A 州对剩余争议无利益；\n• 双方均不居住在美国，州际司法系统及其他州对该补偿争议利益很小。\n\n（选项 A）被告与法院州的持续且系统联系达到实质上“以该州为家”的程度时，存在一般管辖权。制造商居住于欧洲，与 A 州无此类联系。\n\n（选项 B）对人管辖权异议必须在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出。制造商在答辩状中提出，未放弃。\n\n（选项 D）最低限度联系只要求被告可以合理预见会在法院州被诉，不要求其实际想到这一结果。\n\n学习目标：制造商将商品投入商业流通并另有目的地针对法院州时，与该州存在最低限度联系。\n\n参考资料：\n• Asahi Metal Indus. Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 480 U.S. 102, 114–16 (1987)（外国当事人之间诉讼行使管辖权违反公平处理和实质正义）。\n• J. McIntyre Mach., Ltd. v. Nicastro, 564 U.S. 873, 886–87 (2011)（有目的利用要求制造商有目的地针对法院州）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-028","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":28,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A woman from State A drove to visit her friend in a foreign country. During the visit, the brakes on the woman's car began to squeal, so she took the car to a nearby auto repair shop. The shop is owned by a company incorporated in State B and headquartered in the Eastern District of State C. The shop replaced her brakes with a set made by a brake manufacturer incorporated in State C and headquartered in State D. In State C, articles of incorporation must be filed with the Secretary of State's office located in the Western District of State C. Two days later, the woman was driving her car in the foreign country when her brakes failed. This caused her to lose control of the car and crash. She suffered over $500,000 in injuries and property damage. The woman seeks to file a joint complaint against the auto repair company and the manufacturer in federal court in State C. In which federal judicial district would venue be proper?","choices":{"A":"No judicial district.","B":"The Eastern District of State C only.","C":"The Western District of State C only.","D":"The Eastern and Western Districts of State C."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Venue is the geographical location of specific federal districts in which a plaintiff's claim may be heard. A state can have multiple federal districts within its borders. And venue may be proper in more than one district because it is proper in any district where:\n• any defendant resides—so long as all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies (ie, fallback provision).\n\nA defendant corporation resides in any district in which it is subject to personal jurisdiction. In a state with multiple judicial districts (as seen here), each district is treated as a separate state. And the defendant is deemed to reside in any district in which its contacts would create personal jurisdiction—eg, where the defendant filed its articles of incorporation or has its principal place of business. If no such district exists, then the defendant resides in the district where it has the most significant contacts. Here, the auto repair company's headquarters (ie, principal place of business) are in the Eastern District of State C, and the manufacturer filed its articles of incorporation in the Western District of State C. As a result, both defendants are subject to personal jurisdiction in, and are therefore residents of, State C. This means that venue would be proper in any district in State C where one of the defendants resides—ie, the Eastern and Western Districts of State C (Choices A, B & C). Educational objective: Residency-based venue is proper in any judicial district where a defendant resides—as long as all defendants reside in the same state. And a defendant corporation only resides in judicial districts where it would be subject to personal jurisdiction if each district were a state. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)–(d) (residency-based venue for corporate defendants).","rule":"Residency-based venue is proper in any judicial district where a defendant resides—as long as all defendants reside in the same state. And a defendant corporation only resides in judicial districts where it would be subject to personal jurisdiction if each district were a state.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A drove to visit her friend in a foreign country. During the visit, the brakes on the woman's car began to squeal, so she took the car to a nearby auto repair shop. The shop is owned by a company incorporated in State B and headquartered in the Eastern District of State C. The shop replaced her brakes with a set made by a brake manufacturer incorporated in State C and headquartered in State D. In State C, articles of incorporation must be filed with the Secretary of State's office located in the Western District of State C. Two days later, the woman was driving her car in the foreign country when her brakes failed. This caused her to lose control of the car and crash. She suffered over $500,000 in injuries and property damage. The woman seeks to file a joint complaint against the auto repair company and the manufacturer in federal court in State C. In which federal judicial district would venue be proper?","rule":"Residency-based venue is proper in any judicial district where a defendant resides—as long as all defendants reside in the same state. And a defendant corporation only resides in judicial districts where it would be subject to personal jurisdiction if each district were a state.","analysis":"Venue is the geographical location of specific federal districts in which a plaintiff's claim may be heard. A state can have multiple federal districts within its borders. And venue may be proper in more than one district because it is proper in any district where:\n• any defendant resides—so long as all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies (ie, fallback provision).\n\nA defendant corporation resides in any district in which it is subject to personal jurisdiction. In a state with multiple judicial districts (as seen here), each district is treated as a separate state. And the defendant is deemed to reside in any district in which its contacts would create personal jurisdiction—eg, where the defendant filed its articles of incorporation or has its principal place of business. If no such district exists, then the defendant resides in the district where it has the most significant contacts. Here, the auto repair company's headquarters (ie, principal place of business) are in the Eastern District of State C, and the manufacturer filed its articles of incorporation in the Western District of State C. As a result, both defendants are subject to personal jurisdiction in, and are therefore residents of, State C. This means that venue would be proper in any district in State C where one of the defendants resides—ie, the Eastern and Western Districts of State C (Choices A, B & C). Educational objective: Residency-based venue is proper in any judicial district where a defendant resides—as long as all defendants reside in the same state. And a defendant corporation only resides in judicial districts where it would be subject to personal jurisdiction if each district were a state. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)–(d) (residency-based venue for corporate defendants).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)–(d) (residency-based venue for corporate defendants).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[84],"sourceAnswerPages":[85],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名女子驾车前往某外国探望朋友。其间汽车刹车发出异响，她将车送往附近维修店。维修店由一家在 B 州注册成立、总部位于 C 州东区的公司所有。维修店更换了刹车，所用刹车由一家在 C 州注册成立、总部位于 D 州的制造商生产。C 州规定，公司章程必须向位于 C 州西区的州务卿办公室提交。两天后，女子仍在该外国驾车时刹车失灵，车辆失控并发生事故，造成超过 500,000 美元人身伤害和财产损失。女子拟在 C 州联邦法院共同起诉汽车维修公司及制造商。哪个联邦司法区的审判地适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有任何司法区。","B":"仅 C 州东区。","C":"仅 C 州西区。","D":"C 州东区和西区。"},"explanationZh":"审判地是可以审理原告请求的特定联邦地区所在地。一州境内可以有多个联邦地区，且多个地区均可能是适当审判地。以下地区的审判地适当：\n• 若所有被告均居住在同一州，任何被告居住的地区；\n• 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地，或涉案财产的实质部分所在地；\n• 仅在前两项均不适用时，任何被告受法院对人管辖的地区。\n\n被告公司居住于其受对人管辖的任何地区。一州有多个司法区时，每个地区均被视为一个独立州；公司被视为居住于其联系足以产生对人管辖权的任何地区，例如提交公司章程地或主要营业地。如果不存在此类地区，公司即居住于联系最显著的地区。\n\n汽车维修公司的总部即主要营业地位于 C 州东区；制造商向 C 州西区提交公司章程。因此，两被告均受 C 州对人管辖，且均为 C 州居民。这意味着，C 州内任何一名被告居住的地区均为适当审判地，即东区和西区（选项 A、B、C）。\n\n学习目标：所有被告均居住于同一州时，任何被告居住的司法区均可依据住所确定审判地。若将一州的每个司法区视为独立州，公司仅居住于其会受对人管辖的司法区。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)–(d)（公司被告的住所型审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-015"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-029","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":29,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A photographer sued a foreign publisher in a federal district court in State A for copyright infringement. The photographer followed the foreign country's service of process rules to serve the publisher with a copy of the summons and complaint. The publisher has never been to State A and conducts no business there. The publisher filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the case for improper service of process. The district court denied this motion. Twelve days after receiving notice of the denial, the publisher filed and served an answer that included a motion to dismiss the case for lack of personal jurisdiction. Is the court likely to grant the publisher's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the publisher failed to file and serve his answer within 10 days after receiving notice of the court's denial of his pre-answer motion.","B":"No, because the publisher waived any objection to personal jurisdiction.","C":"Yes, because the publisher has never physically traveled to State A and conducts no business there.","D":"Yes, because the publisher properly filed and served his answer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Waiver of defenses\n\nDefenses Waiver\n\n• Lack of subject-matter Never waived jurisdiction\n• Lack of personal jurisdiction Waived if not asserted in pre-answer motion or\n• Improper venue answer (whichever occurs first)\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim Waived if not asserted before end of trial\n• Failure to join required party\n• All other legal defenses Waived if not asserted in answer or amended answer\n\nPersonal jurisdiction is a court's authority over the parties or property before it. One way to establish personal jurisdiction is through the parties' consent. A plaintiff consents to the court's jurisdiction by filing the complaint in that court. In contrast, a defendant can consent in any of the following ways:\n• Express consent – by contractually agreeing to the court's jurisdiction (eg, forum-selection clause)\n\n• Implied consent – by engaging in an activity that the forum state has a substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n\n• Waiver – by failing to assert a personal-jurisdiction objection in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first\n\nHere, the publisher filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss for improper* service of process. But he previously waived any objection to personal jurisdiction by failing to assert this objection in his pre-answer motion. As a result, the publisher consented to the court's jurisdiction, and the court will likely deny his motion to dismiss. *Service of process is proper here because a foreign defendant can be served by following the foreign country's rules for service of process. (Choices A & D) If the defendant files a pre-answer motion, the defendant must file and serve an answer within 14 days after receiving notice of the court's denial or postponement of that motion—as the publisher did here. Nevertheless, the publisher waived his personal-jurisdiction defense by failing to assert it in his pre-answer motion.\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the publisher has never physically traveled to State A and conducts no business there shows that the court cannot establish personal jurisdiction over him through specific jurisdiction. However, it can still establish personal jurisdiction through the publisher's consent. Educational objective: A court can acquire personal jurisdiction through the defendant's consent—ie, express consent, implied consent, or waiver. A defendant waives a personal-jurisdiction objection by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(1) (waiver of personal-jurisdiction defense).","rule":"A court can acquire personal jurisdiction through the defendant's consent—ie, express consent, implied consent, or waiver. A defendant waives a personal-jurisdiction objection by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first.","irac":{"issue":"A photographer sued a foreign publisher in a federal district court in State A for copyright infringement. The photographer followed the foreign country's service of process rules to serve the publisher with a copy of the summons and complaint. The publisher has never been to State A and conducts no business there. The publisher filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the case for improper service of process. The district court denied this motion. Twelve days after receiving notice of the denial, the publisher filed and served an answer that included a motion to dismiss the case for lack of personal jurisdiction. Is the court likely to grant the publisher's motion?","rule":"A court can acquire personal jurisdiction through the defendant's consent—ie, express consent, implied consent, or waiver. A defendant waives a personal-jurisdiction objection by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first.","analysis":"Waiver of defenses\n\nDefenses Waiver\n\n• Lack of subject-matter Never waived jurisdiction\n• Lack of personal jurisdiction Waived if not asserted in pre-answer motion or\n• Improper venue answer (whichever occurs first)\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim Waived if not asserted before end of trial\n• Failure to join required party\n• All other legal defenses Waived if not asserted in answer or amended answer\n\nPersonal jurisdiction is a court's authority over the parties or property before it. One way to establish personal jurisdiction is through the parties' consent. A plaintiff consents to the court's jurisdiction by filing the complaint in that court. In contrast, a defendant can consent in any of the following ways:\n• Express consent – by contractually agreeing to the court's jurisdiction (eg, forum-selection clause)\n\n• Implied consent – by engaging in an activity that the forum state has a substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n\n• Waiver – by failing to assert a personal-jurisdiction objection in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first\n\nHere, the publisher filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss for improper* service of process. But he previously waived any objection to personal jurisdiction by failing to assert this objection in his pre-answer motion. As a result, the publisher consented to the court's jurisdiction, and the court will likely deny his motion to dismiss. *Service of process is proper here because a foreign defendant can be served by following the foreign country's rules for service of process. (Choices A & D) If the defendant files a pre-answer motion, the defendant must file and serve an answer within 14 days after receiving notice of the court's denial or postponement of that motion—as the publisher did here. Nevertheless, the publisher waived his personal-jurisdiction defense by failing to assert it in his pre-answer motion.\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the publisher has never physically traveled to State A and conducts no business there shows that the court cannot establish personal jurisdiction over him through specific jurisdiction. However, it can still establish personal jurisdiction through the publisher's consent. Educational objective: A court can acquire personal jurisdiction through the defendant's consent—ie, express consent, implied consent, or waiver. A defendant waives a personal-jurisdiction objection by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(1) (waiver of personal-jurisdiction defense).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(1) (waiver of personal-jurisdiction defense).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[87],"sourceAnswerPages":[88,89],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名摄影师在 A 州联邦地区法院以侵犯版权为由起诉一名外国出版商。摄影师遵循该外国的送达规则，向出版商送达传票和起诉状副本。出版商从未去过 A 州，也未在那里经营业务。出版商在答辩前以送达不当为由申请驳回，地区法院驳回该动议。出版商收到驳回通知 12 天后，提交并送达答辩状，其中又以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否可能批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为出版商未在收到答辩前动议驳回通知后 10 天内提交并送达答辩状。","B":"不会，因为出版商已放弃对人管辖权异议。","C":"会，因为出版商从未亲自前往 A 州，也未在那里经营业务。","D":"会，因为出版商适当地提交并送达了答辩状。"},"explanationZh":"抗辩的放弃：\n\n• 缺乏事项管辖权：永不放弃。\n• 缺乏对人管辖权、审判地不当、诉讼文书或送达不足：若未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出，即被放弃。\n• 未陈述可获救济的请求、未合并必要当事人：若未在审判终结前提出，即被放弃。\n• 其他法律抗辩：若未在答辩状或修订答辩状中提出，即被放弃。\n\n对人管辖权是法院对当事人或财产的权限，可通过当事人同意建立。原告向法院提交起诉状即同意其管辖。被告可以通过以下方式同意：\n• 明示同意——通过合同同意法院管辖，例如法院选择条款；\n• 默示同意——从事法院州具有重大规制利益的活动，例如在公共道路驾驶；\n• 放弃——未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出异议。\n\n出版商在答辩前动议中仅主张送达不当*，没有同时提出对人管辖权异议，因此此前已经放弃该异议。出版商由此同意法院管辖，法院很可能驳回其动议。\n\n*本题送达适当，因为外国被告可依外国送达规则获得送达。\n\n（选项 A、D）被告提出答辩前动议后，应在收到法院驳回或延期裁定通知后 14 天内提交并送达答辩状，出版商已遵守。但他仍因未在答辩前动议中提出而放弃对人管辖权抗辩。\n\n（选项 C）出版商从未前往 A 州且未在那里经营，说明法院无法通过特定管辖权对其取得管辖；但仍可通过出版商的同意建立管辖权。\n\n学习目标：法院可以通过被告的明示同意、默示同意或放弃取得对人管辖权。被告未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出对人管辖权异议，即放弃该异议。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(1)（放弃对人管辖权抗辩）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-030","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":30,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"An accounting firm incorporated and headquartered in State A advertised its estate-planning services. An attorney in State A saw the firm's advertisement and wrote a newspaper editorial claiming that the firm violated State A's statute on the unauthorized practice of law by not employing licensed attorneys. After the editorial was published, demand for the firm's estate-planning services significantly declined. The firm then sued the attorney for defamation in a State A federal court to recover $150,000. In his answer, the attorney asserted that the editorial contained truthful statements protected by the free speech clause of the First Amendment. The attorney also asserted a $100,000 counterclaim to recover damages suffered as a result of the firm's suit. The attorney did not object to venue or jurisdiction. After the pleadings were filed and served, the court dismissed the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. The firm appealed. Is the appellate court likely to reverse the dismissal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the final-judgment rule prevents the appellate court from considering the appeal.","B":"No, because the firm failed to establish subject-matter jurisdiction.","C":"Yes, because the attorney did not object to the court's jurisdiction.","D":"Yes, because the suit involves a federal question."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is a federal court's authority to hear the merits of a particular case or controversy. This is a pure legal issue that an appellate court reviews de novo, giving no deference to the legal conclusions made by the lower court. This jurisdictional requirement can be established through:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy in the plaintiff's complaint exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states (not seen here) or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the plaintiff asserts a claim that arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a federal treaty. Federal-question jurisdiction must be established on the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint—as opposed to the defendant's answer or counterclaim.* This means that the complaint must show that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action. If the asserted federal issue is frivolous or later abandoned, then federal-question jurisdiction ceases to exist. Here, the firm sued the attorney for defamation, a state-law claim. The attorney defended on the ground that his editorial contained truthful statements protected by the First Amendment. Since this federal issue arose in the attorney's answer—not the firm's complaint—the court lacks federal-question jurisdiction (Choice D). Therefore, the appellate court is not likely to reverse the dismissal. *Congress created an exception that authorizes federal-question jurisdiction when a defendant (1) asserts a counterclaim arising from a federal patent or copyright statute and (2) removes the case from state to federal court. (Choice A) The final-judgment rule generally precludes federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the federal district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. A dismissal for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is a final judgment, so the appellate court here can consider the appeal. (Choice C) Subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived or consented to, so it is immaterial that the attorney did not object to the court's jurisdiction. Instead, a court must dismiss a case on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) if, at any time, it determines that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction. Educational objective: Federal-question jurisdiction exists when a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a federal treaty appears on the face of the plaintiff's complaint. A federal issue raised in the defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction.\n\nReferences\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908) (establishing the well-pleaded complaint rule).\n\n• Holmes Group, Inc. v. Vornado Air Circulation Sys., Inc., 535 U.S. 826, 831 (2002) (holding that a defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction).","rule":"Federal-question jurisdiction exists when a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a federal treaty appears on the face of the plaintiff's complaint. A federal issue raised in the defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"An accounting firm incorporated and headquartered in State A advertised its estate-planning services. An attorney in State A saw the firm's advertisement and wrote a newspaper editorial claiming that the firm violated State A's statute on the unauthorized practice of law by not employing licensed attorneys. After the editorial was published, demand for the firm's estate-planning services significantly declined. The firm then sued the attorney for defamation in a State A federal court to recover $150,000. In his answer, the attorney asserted that the editorial contained truthful statements protected by the free speech clause of the First Amendment. The attorney also asserted a $100,000 counterclaim to recover damages suffered as a result of the firm's suit. The attorney did not object to venue or jurisdiction. After the pleadings were filed and served, the court dismissed the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. The firm appealed. Is the appellate court likely to reverse the dismissal?","rule":"Federal-question jurisdiction exists when a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a federal treaty appears on the face of the plaintiff's complaint. A federal issue raised in the defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is a federal court's authority to hear the merits of a particular case or controversy. This is a pure legal issue that an appellate court reviews de novo, giving no deference to the legal conclusions made by the lower court. This jurisdictional requirement can be established through:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy in the plaintiff's complaint exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states (not seen here) or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the plaintiff asserts a claim that arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a federal treaty. Federal-question jurisdiction must be established on the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint—as opposed to the defendant's answer or counterclaim.* This means that the complaint must show that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action. If the asserted federal issue is frivolous or later abandoned, then federal-question jurisdiction ceases to exist. Here, the firm sued the attorney for defamation, a state-law claim. The attorney defended on the ground that his editorial contained truthful statements protected by the First Amendment. Since this federal issue arose in the attorney's answer—not the firm's complaint—the court lacks federal-question jurisdiction (Choice D). Therefore, the appellate court is not likely to reverse the dismissal. *Congress created an exception that authorizes federal-question jurisdiction when a defendant (1) asserts a counterclaim arising from a federal patent or copyright statute and (2) removes the case from state to federal court. (Choice A) The final-judgment rule generally precludes federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the federal district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. A dismissal for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is a final judgment, so the appellate court here can consider the appeal. (Choice C) Subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived or consented to, so it is immaterial that the attorney did not object to the court's jurisdiction. Instead, a court must dismiss a case on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) if, at any time, it determines that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction. Educational objective: Federal-question jurisdiction exists when a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a federal treaty appears on the face of the plaintiff's complaint. A federal issue raised in the defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction.\n\nReferences\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908) (establishing the well-pleaded complaint rule).\n\n• Holmes Group, Inc. v. Vornado Air Circulation Sys., Inc., 535 U.S. 826, 831 (2002) (holding that a defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908) (establishing the well-pleaded complaint rule).\n\n• Holmes Group, Inc. v. Vornado Air Circulation Sys., Inc., 535 U.S. 826, 831 (2002) (holding that a defendant's answer or counterclaim does not establish federal-question jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[90],"sourceAnswerPages":[91,92],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且总部位于 A 州的会计师事务所宣传其遗产规划服务。A 州一名律师看到广告后，在报纸发表社论，主张该事务所没有聘用持证律师，违反 A 州关于未经许可从事法律业务的法律。社论发表后，事务所遗产规划服务需求大幅下降。事务所遂在 A 州联邦法院以诽谤为由起诉律师，索赔 150,000 美元。律师在答辩状中主张社论陈述真实，且受第一修正案言论自由条款保护；还提出 100,000 美元反诉，请求赔偿因事务所诉讼遭受的损失。律师未对审判地或管辖权提出异议。诉状提交并送达后，法院以缺乏事项管辖权为由驳回案件。事务所上诉。上诉法院是否可能撤销驳回？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为终局判决规则禁止上诉法院审理该上诉。","B":"不会，因为事务所未能确立事项管辖权。","C":"会，因为律师未对法院管辖权提出异议。","D":"会，因为诉讼涉及联邦问题。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权是联邦法院审理特定案件或争议实体的权限。这是纯法律问题，上诉法院进行重新审查，不尊重下级法院的法律结论。事项管辖权可以基于：\n• 多元州籍管辖权——原告起诉状中的争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州公民（本题不存在）；或者\n• 联邦问题管辖权——原告提出依据宪法、联邦法律或联邦条约产生的请求。\n\n联邦问题必须出现在原告妥善陈述的起诉状表面，而非被告答辩或反诉中。起诉状必须表明联邦问题是原告诉因的必要要素。若联邦问题属轻率主张或后来被放弃，联邦问题管辖权即不复存在。\n\n事务所以州法诽谤请求起诉律师。律师在答辩中主张其真实陈述受第一修正案保护。该联邦问题出现在律师答辩而非事务所起诉状中，故法院不享有联邦问题管辖权（选项 D）。上诉法院不太可能撤销驳回。\n\n*国会规定了一项例外：被告提出依据联邦专利或版权法律产生的反诉，并将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院时，可以建立联邦问题管辖权。\n\n（选项 A）终局判决规则通常禁止在联邦地区法院作出完全解决争议实体、仅剩执行的终局判决前上诉。因缺乏事项管辖权而驳回属于终局判决，上诉法院可以审理。\n\n（选项 C）事项管辖权不能放弃或通过同意建立，律师未提出异议并不重要。法院一旦发现缺乏事项管辖权，必须依职权驳回。\n\n学习目标：依据宪法、联邦法律或条约产生的请求出现在原告起诉状表面时，存在联邦问题管辖权。被告答辩或反诉中提出的联邦问题不能建立联邦问题管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331（联邦问题管辖权）。\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908)（妥善陈述的起诉状规则）。\n• Holmes Group, Inc. v. Vornado Air Circulation Sys., Inc., 535 U.S. 826, 831 (2002)（答辩或反诉不建立联邦问题管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-016"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-031","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":31,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A bus passenger from State A was injured when a bus owned by a company incorporated and headquartered in State B negligently collided with another vehicle on a road in State A. The passenger agreed to release the company from liability in exchange for a free lifetime bus membership valued at $60,000. Two years later, Congress passed a law that prohibited bus companies from providing free lifetime bus memberships, so the company revoked the passenger's membership. The passenger sued the company in federal court for breach of contract. In his complaint, the passenger stated that the company would likely argue that its breach was necessary to comply with the federal statute. The passenger claimed that this defense is invalid because the statute violates the U.S. Constitution. The company filed a motion to dismiss the passenger's suit for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction before filing an answer. How will the court likely rule on the company's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the company failed to challenge subject-matter jurisdiction in its answer.","B":"Deny the motion, because the passenger's claim presents a federal issue.","C":"Grant the motion, because state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over state-law claims.","D":"Grant the motion, because the court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is a court's authority to hear a case on the merits. The party seeking to invoke a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction (generally the plaintiff) has the burden to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the court has either:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 (not $60,000, as seen here) or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the plaintiff asserts a cause of action that arises under the Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law.\n\nFor federal-question jurisdiction to be invoked, the federal issue must appear on the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint—not the defendant's answer or counterclaim. The jurisdictional statement in the complaint must show that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action.* Merely mentioning a federal issue that may be asserted in the defendant's answer is insufficient. Here, the passenger's complaint asserts a breach of contract claim. It also states that the company would likely argue that it breached the contract to comply with a federal statute, which the passenger alleges is unconstitutional. Since this federal issue arises from an anticipated defense—not the cause of action—the complaint fails to establish federal-question jurisdiction (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the company's motion. *In rare situations, the complete-preemption doctrine allows a state-law claim to be considered a federal-law claim when Congress clearly intended for a federal statute to completely preempt (ie, supersede) state law. (Choice A) A party or the court can challenge subject-matter jurisdiction at any time, including in a pre-answer motion. (Choice C) State and federal courts have concurrent (ie, shared) jurisdiction over most state-law claims. The only substantive areas over which state courts have exclusive jurisdiction are probate matters (eg, authenticating a will) and domestic relations (eg, issuing a divorce). Educational objective: Federal-question jurisdiction exists when the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint asserts a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty. A complaint is well-pleaded when its jurisdictional statement shows that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908) (establishing the well-pleaded complaint rule).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 854 (2020) (explaining that anticipated defenses cannot be the basis for federal-question jurisdiction).","rule":"Federal-question jurisdiction exists when the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint asserts a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty. A complaint is well-pleaded when its jurisdictional statement shows that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action.","irac":{"issue":"A bus passenger from State A was injured when a bus owned by a company incorporated and headquartered in State B negligently collided with another vehicle on a road in State A. The passenger agreed to release the company from liability in exchange for a free lifetime bus membership valued at $60,000. Two years later, Congress passed a law that prohibited bus companies from providing free lifetime bus memberships, so the company revoked the passenger's membership. The passenger sued the company in federal court for breach of contract. In his complaint, the passenger stated that the company would likely argue that its breach was necessary to comply with the federal statute. The passenger claimed that this defense is invalid because the statute violates the U.S. Constitution. The company filed a motion to dismiss the passenger's suit for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction before filing an answer. How will the court likely rule on the company's motion?","rule":"Federal-question jurisdiction exists when the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint asserts a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty. A complaint is well-pleaded when its jurisdictional statement shows that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is a court's authority to hear a case on the merits. The party seeking to invoke a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction (generally the plaintiff) has the burden to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the court has either:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 (not $60,000, as seen here) or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the plaintiff asserts a cause of action that arises under the Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law.\n\nFor federal-question jurisdiction to be invoked, the federal issue must appear on the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint—not the defendant's answer or counterclaim. The jurisdictional statement in the complaint must show that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action.* Merely mentioning a federal issue that may be asserted in the defendant's answer is insufficient. Here, the passenger's complaint asserts a breach of contract claim. It also states that the company would likely argue that it breached the contract to comply with a federal statute, which the passenger alleges is unconstitutional. Since this federal issue arises from an anticipated defense—not the cause of action—the complaint fails to establish federal-question jurisdiction (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the company's motion. *In rare situations, the complete-preemption doctrine allows a state-law claim to be considered a federal-law claim when Congress clearly intended for a federal statute to completely preempt (ie, supersede) state law. (Choice A) A party or the court can challenge subject-matter jurisdiction at any time, including in a pre-answer motion. (Choice C) State and federal courts have concurrent (ie, shared) jurisdiction over most state-law claims. The only substantive areas over which state courts have exclusive jurisdiction are probate matters (eg, authenticating a will) and domestic relations (eg, issuing a divorce). Educational objective: Federal-question jurisdiction exists when the face of the plaintiff's well-pleaded complaint asserts a claim arising under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty. A complaint is well-pleaded when its jurisdictional statement shows that the federal issue is a necessary element of the plaintiff's cause of action. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908) (establishing the well-pleaded complaint rule).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 854 (2020) (explaining that anticipated defenses cannot be the basis for federal-question jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908) (establishing the well-pleaded complaint rule).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 854 (2020) (explaining that anticipated defenses cannot be the basis for federal-question jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[93],"sourceAnswerPages":[94,95],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名公交乘客因一家公交公司所有的车辆在 A 州道路上过失撞击另一车辆而受伤。公司在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州。乘客同意免除公司责任，交换条件是获得价值 60,000 美元的终身免费乘车会员资格。两年后，国会通过法律禁止公交公司提供免费终身会员，公司遂撤销其会员资格。乘客在联邦法院以违约为由起诉。起诉状称，公司很可能主张违约是遵守联邦法律所必需；乘客主张该抗辩无效，因为法律违反美国宪法。公司在提交答辩前以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为公司未在答辩状中质疑事项管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为乘客的请求提出联邦问题。","C":"批准动议，因为州法院对州法请求享有专属管辖权。","D":"批准动议，因为法院缺乏事项管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权是法院审理案件实体的权限。通常由寻求援引联邦法院管辖权的一方（原告）负担优势证据证明责任，证明法院享有：\n• 多元州籍管辖权——双方州籍不同，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元（本题仅 60,000 美元）；或者\n• 联邦问题管辖权——原告提出依据宪法、条约或联邦法律产生的诉因。\n\n联邦问题必须出现在原告妥善陈述的起诉状表面，而非被告答辩或反诉中。起诉状的管辖权陈述必须表明联邦问题是原告诉因的必要要素。仅提及被告可能在答辩中提出的联邦问题不足以建立管辖权。\n\n乘客起诉状提出违约请求，并称公司可能主张为遵守联邦法律而违约，而乘客认为该法律违宪。该联邦问题来自预期抗辩，而非诉因本身，故起诉状未建立联邦问题管辖权（选项 B）。法院很可能批准公司动议。\n\n*在少数情况下，完全先占原则允许州法请求被视为联邦请求，前提是国会明确意图以联邦法律完全取代州法。\n\n（选项 A）当事人或法院可以在任何时候质疑事项管辖权，包括在答辩前动议中。\n\n（选项 C）州法院与联邦法院对多数州法请求享有并行管辖权。仅遗嘱认证和家庭关系等实体领域由州法院专属管辖。\n\n学习目标：原告妥善陈述的起诉状表面提出依据宪法、联邦法律或条约产生的请求时，存在联邦问题管辖权。只有管辖权陈述表明联邦问题是原告诉因必要要素，起诉状才属妥善陈述。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331（联邦问题管辖权）。\n• Louisville & Nashville R.R. Co. v. Mottley, 211 U.S. 149, 152 (1908)（妥善陈述的起诉状规则）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 854 (2020)（预期抗辩不能成为联邦问题管辖权基础）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-017"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-032","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":32,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A business incorporated in State A hired an advertiser living in State B and a lawyer living in State C to help the business's advertising efforts in a foreign country. The contract was signed in the foreign country, and all work to be performed under the contract was to be done in that country. The advertiser and the lawyer failed to arrange for the advertising in the foreign country as required by the contract. The business sued the advertiser and the lawyer in State B federal court for breach of contract, requesting $400,000 in damages. The advertiser was properly served with process at his residence, and the lawyer was properly served with process while conducting business in State B. The two defendants have timely filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the case for improper venue. How should the federal court rule on the defendants' motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the advertiser resides in State B.","B":"Deny the motion, because the defendants are subject to personal jurisdiction in State B.","C":"Grant the motion, because a substantial portion of the events giving rise to the breach of contract claim did not occur in State B.","D":"Grant the motion, because the lawyer does not reside in State B."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under 28 U.S.C § 1391(b), venue is proper in any federal district where:\n• any defendant resides—as long as all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (ie, property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only when venue cannot be established under the first two provisions (ie, fallback provision).\n\nHere, there is no residency-based venue because the defendants reside in different states— States B and C (Choice A). Additionally, there is no events-based venue because the events—where the contract was signed and to be performed—occurred in the foreign country. And since this case does not concern property, there is no property-based venue. However, venue is proper in State B under the fallback provision because the State B federal court has personal jurisdiction over the advertiser who resides (ie, is domiciled) there. The court also has personal jurisdiction over the lawyer because he was served with process while voluntarily in State B on business. Therefore, the defendants' motion to dismiss for improper venue should be denied (Choices C & D). Educational objective: Venue is proper in any federal district where: (1) any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not apply. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","rule":"Venue is proper in any federal district where: (1) any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not apply.","irac":{"issue":"A business incorporated in State A hired an advertiser living in State B and a lawyer living in State C to help the business's advertising efforts in a foreign country. The contract was signed in the foreign country, and all work to be performed under the contract was to be done in that country. The advertiser and the lawyer failed to arrange for the advertising in the foreign country as required by the contract. The business sued the advertiser and the lawyer in State B federal court for breach of contract, requesting $400,000 in damages. The advertiser was properly served with process at his residence, and the lawyer was properly served with process while conducting business in State B. The two defendants have timely filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the case for improper venue. How should the federal court rule on the defendants' motion to dismiss?","rule":"Venue is proper in any federal district where: (1) any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not apply.","analysis":"Under 28 U.S.C § 1391(b), venue is proper in any federal district where:\n• any defendant resides—as long as all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (ie, property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only when venue cannot be established under the first two provisions (ie, fallback provision).\n\nHere, there is no residency-based venue because the defendants reside in different states— States B and C (Choice A). Additionally, there is no events-based venue because the events—where the contract was signed and to be performed—occurred in the foreign country. And since this case does not concern property, there is no property-based venue. However, venue is proper in State B under the fallback provision because the State B federal court has personal jurisdiction over the advertiser who resides (ie, is domiciled) there. The court also has personal jurisdiction over the lawyer because he was served with process while voluntarily in State B on business. Therefore, the defendants' motion to dismiss for improper venue should be denied (Choices C & D). Educational objective: Venue is proper in any federal district where: (1) any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred or the property at issue is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not apply. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[96],"sourceAnswerPages":[97],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立的企业，聘请居住在 B 州的广告商及居住在 C 州的律师，协助其在某外国开展广告业务。合同在该外国签署，全部履行工作也应在那里完成。广告商与律师未按合同要求安排广告。企业在 B 州联邦法院以违约为由起诉二人，索赔 400,000 美元。广告商在其住所获得适当送达；律师在 B 州开展业务时获得适当送达。两被告及时在答辩前以审判地不当为由申请驳回。法院应如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为广告商居住在 B 州。","B":"驳回动议，因为两被告均受 B 州对人管辖。","C":"批准动议，因为引起违约请求的实质部分事件未发生在 B 州。","D":"批准动议，因为律师不居住在 B 州。"},"explanationZh":"根据 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)，以下任何联邦地区的审判地适当：\n• 若所有被告均居住于同一州，任何被告居住的地区；\n• 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地，或涉案财产的实质部分所在地；\n• 仅在前两项均无法确定审判地时，任何被告受法院对人管辖的地区。\n\n本题不存在住所型审判地，因为两被告分别居住于 B 州和 C 州（选项 A）。合同签署及预定履行均在外国，也不存在事件型审判地。案件不涉及财产，所以不存在财产型审判地。\n\n但依兜底条款，B 州审判地适当。B 州联邦法院对居住于该州的广告商享有对人管辖权；律师自愿前往 B 州开展业务并在那里获得送达，法院对其也享有对人管辖权。因此，应驳回两被告的审判地不当动议（选项 C、D）。\n\n学习目标：以下任何联邦地区的审判地适当：(1) 若所有被告均居住于同一州，任何被告居住的地区；(2) 实质部分事件发生地或涉案财产所在地；或 (3) 仅在前两项均不适用时，任何被告受对人管辖的地区。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)（适当审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-009"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-033","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":33,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A bicyclist from State A collided with a hiker from State B on a trail in State A. The hiker sued the bicyclist for negligence in a federal district court to recover $75,000 in medical expenses. The case was tried to the court. After the close of evidence, the judge announced findings of fact and conclusions of law from the bench and entered a judgment in favor of the hiker. The bicyclist appealed. Is the appellate court likely to reverse the judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judge properly issued findings and conclusions on the record.","B":"No, because the judge's findings and conclusions are the law of the case.","C":"Yes, because federal appellate courts do not have jurisdiction to decide issues of state law.","D":"Yes, because the judge had no authority to hear the case."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A federal court has authority to hear a case that falls within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction. This jurisdictional requirement, unlike personal jurisdiction, cannot be waived or established by the parties' consent. As a result, a challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction can be raised by a party or on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte) at any time—including on appeal* (as seen here). Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 (not seen here). Since the hiker's claim does not satisfy federal-question or diversity jurisdiction, the district court judge had no authority to hear the case. Therefore, the appellate court will likely reverse the judgment and remand with instructions to dismiss the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. *If a party had an opportunity to challenge the court's subject-matter jurisdiction at some point during the proceedings, that party cannot collaterally attack the court's jurisdiction in subsequent litigation (eg, in a proceeding to enforce the court's judgment). (Choice A) In a bench trial, the judge acts as the finder of fact and interpreter of the law. This dual role requires the judge to issue (orally or in writing) findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record. Although the judge did so here, she lacked authority to hear the case and so the judgment should be reversed. (Choice B) The law-of-the-case doctrine prohibits the relitigation of legal (not factual) issues decided by a higher court in the same case. This means that a district court must apply an appellate court's legal determinations on remand, but an appellate court is not bound by a district court's conclusions. (Choice C) Federal appellate courts do have jurisdiction to decide issues of state law appealed from a federal district court. However, federal courts (except the U.S. Supreme Court) lack jurisdiction over an appeal from a state's highest court pursuant to the Rooker-Feldman doctrine. Educational objective: A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived. It can be raised by the parties or by the court on its own initiative at any time, including on appeal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(3) (stating that a court must dismiss an action once it determines, at any time, that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction).\n\n• Ins. Corp. of Ir., Ltd., et al. v. Compagnie des Bauxites de Guinee, 456 U.S. 694, 702 (1982) (explaining that an appellate court has the power to raise lack of subject-matter jurisdiction on its own initiative).\n\n诉讼标的管辖权绝不可放弃，可在任何时间提出（包括上诉） 未声明索赔，未加入诉讼的一方，可放弃，需在判决前提出\n\n人身管辖权，不当地点，程序送达不足 ，可放弃 必须在pre-answer motion 或第一份answer中提出，否则视为放弃","rule":"A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived. It can be raised by the parties or by the court on its own initiative at any time, including on appeal.","irac":{"issue":"A bicyclist from State A collided with a hiker from State B on a trail in State A. The hiker sued the bicyclist for negligence in a federal district court to recover $75,000 in medical expenses. The case was tried to the court. After the close of evidence, the judge announced findings of fact and conclusions of law from the bench and entered a judgment in favor of the hiker. The bicyclist appealed. Is the appellate court likely to reverse the judgment?","rule":"A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived. It can be raised by the parties or by the court on its own initiative at any time, including on appeal.","analysis":"A federal court has authority to hear a case that falls within the court's subject-matter jurisdiction. This jurisdictional requirement, unlike personal jurisdiction, cannot be waived or established by the parties' consent. As a result, a challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction can be raised by a party or on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte) at any time—including on appeal* (as seen here). Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 (not seen here). Since the hiker's claim does not satisfy federal-question or diversity jurisdiction, the district court judge had no authority to hear the case. Therefore, the appellate court will likely reverse the judgment and remand with instructions to dismiss the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. *If a party had an opportunity to challenge the court's subject-matter jurisdiction at some point during the proceedings, that party cannot collaterally attack the court's jurisdiction in subsequent litigation (eg, in a proceeding to enforce the court's judgment). (Choice A) In a bench trial, the judge acts as the finder of fact and interpreter of the law. This dual role requires the judge to issue (orally or in writing) findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record. Although the judge did so here, she lacked authority to hear the case and so the judgment should be reversed. (Choice B) The law-of-the-case doctrine prohibits the relitigation of legal (not factual) issues decided by a higher court in the same case. This means that a district court must apply an appellate court's legal determinations on remand, but an appellate court is not bound by a district court's conclusions. (Choice C) Federal appellate courts do have jurisdiction to decide issues of state law appealed from a federal district court. However, federal courts (except the U.S. Supreme Court) lack jurisdiction over an appeal from a state's highest court pursuant to the Rooker-Feldman doctrine. Educational objective: A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction cannot be waived. It can be raised by the parties or by the court on its own initiative at any time, including on appeal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(3) (stating that a court must dismiss an action once it determines, at any time, that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction).\n\n• Ins. Corp. of Ir., Ltd., et al. v. Compagnie des Bauxites de Guinee, 456 U.S. 694, 702 (1982) (explaining that an appellate court has the power to raise lack of subject-matter jurisdiction on its own initiative).\n\n诉讼标的管辖权绝不可放弃，可在任何时间提出（包括上诉） 未声明索赔，未加入诉讼的一方，可放弃，需在判决前提出\n\n人身管辖权，不当地点，程序送达不足 ，可放弃 必须在pre-answer motion 或第一份answer中提出，否则视为放弃","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(3) (stating that a court must dismiss an action once it determines, at any time, that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction).\n\n• Ins. Corp. of Ir., Ltd., et al. v. Compagnie des Bauxites de Guinee, 456 U.S. 694, 702 (1982) (explaining that an appellate court has the power to raise lack of subject-matter jurisdiction on its own initiative).\n\n诉讼标的管辖权绝不可放弃，可在任何时间提出（包括上诉） 未声明索赔，未加入诉讼的一方，可放弃，需在判决前提出\n\n人身管辖权，不当地点，程序送达不足 ，可放弃 必须在pre-answer motion 或第一份answer中提出，否则视为放弃","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[99],"sourceAnswerPages":[100,101],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名骑自行车者在 A 州一条小径上与 B 州的一名徒步者相撞。徒步者在联邦地区法院以过失为由起诉骑车者，请求 75,000 美元医疗费。案件由法官审理。证据提出完毕后，法官当庭宣布事实认定与法律结论，并判徒步者胜诉。骑车者上诉。上诉法院是否可能撤销判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法官已适当地将事实认定和法律结论记入记录。","B":"不会，因为法官的认定和结论构成本案既定法律。","C":"会，因为联邦上诉法院无权裁判州法问题。","D":"会，因为法官无权审理该案。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院只有在案件属于其事项管辖权时才有权审理。事项管辖权与对人管辖权不同，不能被放弃，也不能通过当事人同意建立。因此，当事人或法院可以在任何时候提出事项管辖权问题，包括在上诉阶段由法院依职权提出。\n\n事项管辖权可以通过以下方式建立：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方是不同州公民，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元（本题请求恰为 75,000 美元，并未“超过”）。\n\n徒步者的请求既不满足联邦问题管辖权，也不满足多元州籍管辖权，地区法院法官无权审理。因此，上诉法院很可能撤销判决，并发回且指示以缺乏事项管辖权为由驳回。\n\n*若当事人在原程序中曾有机会质疑事项管辖权，其不能在后续诉讼中对该管辖权进行附带攻击，例如在执行判决程序中。\n\n（选项 A）法官审判中，法官兼为事实认定者和法律解释者，必须以口头或书面方式将事实认定及法律结论记入记录。法官虽已如此办理，但其无权审理，判决仍应撤销。\n\n（选项 B）本案既定法律原则禁止在同一案件中重新争议由上级法院已经决定的法律问题，而非事实问题。地区法院发回后必须遵循上诉法院的法律裁定，但上诉法院不受地区法院结论约束。\n\n（选项 C）联邦上诉法院可以审理从联邦地区法院上诉的州法问题。但根据 Rooker–Feldman 原则，除美国最高法院外，联邦法院无权审理从一州最高法院提出的上诉。\n\n学习目标：事项管辖权异议不能放弃。当事人或法院可以在任何时候提出，包括上诉阶段。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(3)（法院一旦认定缺乏事项管辖权，必须驳回）。\n• Ins. Corp. of Ir., Ltd. v. Compagnie des Bauxites de Guinee, 456 U.S. 694, 702 (1982)（上诉法院可依职权提出事项管辖权问题）。\n\n复习提示：事项管辖权永不放弃，可在包括上诉在内的任何时候提出；未陈述可获救济请求及未加入必要当事人的抗辩应在审判终结前提出；对人管辖权、审判地不当、诉讼文书或送达不足必须在答辩前动议或第一份答辩中提出，否则放弃。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-018"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-034","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":34,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A bank located in State A sued a homeowner who resides in State B in a federal court in State A to recover $200,000. The bank's complaint alleges that the homeowner failed to pay his mortgage on a vacation home located in State A. The homeowner's answer admits that he completed and signed an online preapproval application for the mortgage but never finished the mortgage-application process because he decided to rent a time-share in State A instead. The homeowner claims that his signature on the mortgage was forged. The homeowner also included in his answer a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. The preapproval application contained a clause that states: \"All disputes between the parties involved in this mortgage-application process are to be resolved in a state or federal court in State A.\" How should the federal court rule on the homeowner's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the forum-selection clause gives the court personal jurisdiction over the homeowner.","B":"Deny the motion, because the homeowner's time-share is in State A.","C":"Grant the motion, because forum-selection clauses are unenforceable in federal court.","D":"Grant the motion, because the homeowner resides in State B."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) Personal jurisdiction refers to a court's authority over the parties (or property) in a lawsuit. Since a plaintiff consents to the court's jurisdiction by filing the lawsuit, the exercise of personal jurisdiction focuses on the defendant. A defendant can consent to the court's jurisdiction in any of the following ways:\n• Waiver – by failing to assert an objection to personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion or answer (whichever occurs first)\n\n• Implied consent – by engaging in an activity that the forum state has a substantial interest in regulating\n\n• Express consent – by contractually agreeing to the court's jurisdiction (eg, forum-selection clause)\n\nA forum-selection clause is a provision in a contract that designates the location and/or court where any dispute between the parties must be adjudicated. Such clauses are generally enforceable in state and federal courts so long as a party's agreement was not induced by fraud (Choice C).\n\nHere, the mortgage-preapproval application has a forum-selection clause stating that \"[a]ll disputes between the parties involved in this mortgage-application process are to be resolved in a state or federal court in State A.\" Since there is no indication that the homeowner was fraudulently induced to complete the preapproval application, he expressly consented to personal jurisdiction in State A. Therefore, the court should deny his motion to dismiss. (Choice B) The fact that the homeowner's time-share is in State A does not establish personal jurisdiction. This is because the claim arises from the vacation home—not the time-share (no specific jurisdiction)—and the homeowner's domicile is State B (no general jurisdiction). (Choice D) Although the homeowner resides in State B, he expressly consented to the State A court's personal jurisdiction by signing the mortgage-preapproval application with a forum-selection clause. Educational objective: Personal jurisdiction can be established through the defendant's waiver, implied consent, or express consent—eg, a forum-selection clause. References\n• Nat'l Equip. Rental, Ltd. v. Szukhent, 375 U.S. 311, 316 (1964) (stating that \"parties to a contract may agree in advance to submit to the jurisdiction of a given court\").","rule":"Personal jurisdiction can be established through the defendant's waiver, implied consent, or express consent—eg, a forum-selection clause.","irac":{"issue":"A bank located in State A sued a homeowner who resides in State B in a federal court in State A to recover $200,000. The bank's complaint alleges that the homeowner failed to pay his mortgage on a vacation home located in State A. The homeowner's answer admits that he completed and signed an online preapproval application for the mortgage but never finished the mortgage-application process because he decided to rent a time-share in State A instead. The homeowner claims that his signature on the mortgage was forged. The homeowner also included in his answer a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. The preapproval application contained a clause that states: \"All disputes between the parties involved in this mortgage-application process are to be resolved in a state or federal court in State A.\" How should the federal court rule on the homeowner's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Personal jurisdiction can be established through the defendant's waiver, implied consent, or express consent—eg, a forum-selection clause.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) Personal jurisdiction refers to a court's authority over the parties (or property) in a lawsuit. Since a plaintiff consents to the court's jurisdiction by filing the lawsuit, the exercise of personal jurisdiction focuses on the defendant. A defendant can consent to the court's jurisdiction in any of the following ways:\n• Waiver – by failing to assert an objection to personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion or answer (whichever occurs first)\n\n• Implied consent – by engaging in an activity that the forum state has a substantial interest in regulating\n\n• Express consent – by contractually agreeing to the court's jurisdiction (eg, forum-selection clause)\n\nA forum-selection clause is a provision in a contract that designates the location and/or court where any dispute between the parties must be adjudicated. Such clauses are generally enforceable in state and federal courts so long as a party's agreement was not induced by fraud (Choice C).\n\nHere, the mortgage-preapproval application has a forum-selection clause stating that \"[a]ll disputes between the parties involved in this mortgage-application process are to be resolved in a state or federal court in State A.\" Since there is no indication that the homeowner was fraudulently induced to complete the preapproval application, he expressly consented to personal jurisdiction in State A. Therefore, the court should deny his motion to dismiss. (Choice B) The fact that the homeowner's time-share is in State A does not establish personal jurisdiction. This is because the claim arises from the vacation home—not the time-share (no specific jurisdiction)—and the homeowner's domicile is State B (no general jurisdiction). (Choice D) Although the homeowner resides in State B, he expressly consented to the State A court's personal jurisdiction by signing the mortgage-preapproval application with a forum-selection clause. Educational objective: Personal jurisdiction can be established through the defendant's waiver, implied consent, or express consent—eg, a forum-selection clause. References\n• Nat'l Equip. Rental, Ltd. v. Szukhent, 375 U.S. 311, 316 (1964) (stating that \"parties to a contract may agree in advance to submit to the jurisdiction of a given court\").","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Nat'l Equip. Rental, Ltd. v. Szukhent, 375 U.S. 311, 316 (1964) (stating that \"parties to a contract may agree in advance to submit to the jurisdiction of a given court\").","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[102],"sourceAnswerPages":[103,104],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"位于 A 州的一家银行在 A 州联邦法院起诉居住于 B 州的一名房主，追偿 200,000 美元。银行主张房主未支付位于 A 州的一套度假住宅的抵押贷款。房主在答辩中承认曾在线填写并签署抵押贷款预批准申请，但因决定在 A 州租用分时度假物业，未完成正式申请；其主张抵押贷款上的签名系伪造。房主还在答辩中以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。预批准申请载有条款：“参与本抵押贷款申请流程各方之间的一切争议，均由 A 州的州法院或联邦法院解决。”法院应如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为法院选择条款使法院对房主享有对人管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为房主的分时度假物业位于 A 州。","C":"批准动议，因为法院选择条款在联邦法院不可执行。","D":"批准动议，因为房主居住于 B 州。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权是法院对被告的权限，可以通过以下方式建立：\n• 送达：被告自愿身处法院州时向其送达；\n• 同意：明示同意（例如法院选择条款）、默示同意（从事州具有重大规制利益的活动）、未及时异议而放弃，或自愿出庭争议案件实体；\n• 特定管辖权：请求源于或密切关联于被告有目的利用法院州利益所形成的最低限度联系，且行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义；\n• 一般管辖权：被告与法院州持续、系统的联系达到实质上“以该州为家”，即被告住所。\n\n原告通过提起诉讼同意法院管辖，因此重点在被告。被告可通过以下方式同意：\n• 未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出而放弃；\n• 从事法院州有重大规制利益的活动而默示同意；\n• 以合同约定法院管辖而明示同意。\n\n法院选择条款是指定当事人争议必须在哪一地点和／或法院裁判的合同条款。只要同意并非因欺诈诱导，此类条款通常可在州法院和联邦法院执行（选项 C）。\n\n预批准申请明确约定所有相关争议在 A 州州法院或联邦法院解决。没有事实表明房主因欺诈诱导而填写预批准申请，所以他明示同意 A 州法院对其行使对人管辖权。法院应驳回其动议。\n\n（选项 B）分时度假物业位于 A 州不能建立管辖权，因为请求源于度假住宅而非分时度假物业，不存在特定管辖权；房主住所为 B 州，也不存在一般管辖权。\n\n（选项 D）房主虽居住于 B 州，但通过签署含法院选择条款的预批准申请，已明示同意 A 州法院管辖。\n\n学习目标：对人管辖权可以通过被告放弃、默示同意或明示同意（例如法院选择条款）建立。\n\n参考资料：\n• Nat'l Equip. Rental, Ltd. v. Szukhent, 375 U.S. 311, 316 (1964)（合同当事人可以预先同意服从特定法院管辖）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-035","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":35,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A federal government employee, a citizen of State A, was driving home after his shift in State B when he lost control of his automobile and hit a pedestrian, a citizen of State C. The employee has never been to State C. The pedestrian filed a negligence suit against the employee in federal court in State C to recover $80,000 in damages. The employee filed a motion to dismiss the action based on improper venue. The pedestrian responded that venue was proper in State C, where she resides, because the employee works for the federal government. If the court grants the employee's motion, what is the likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"A federal government employee cannot be sued where the plaintiff resides.","B":"The employee must be sued in State A, where he resides.","C":"The pedestrian sued the employee in his individual capacity.","D":"The suit does not arise under a federal statute."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Proper venue for suit against federal officer or employee\n\nOfficial capacity Special venue rules permit suit in district where:\n\n(recovery from government) • any Δ resides\n• substantial portion of events occurred or property located\n• π resides if no real property involved Individual capacity General venue statute permits suit in district where:\n\n(recovery from • any Δ resides, if all Δs reside in same state officer/employee) • substantial portion of events occurred or property located\n• any Δ subject to personal jurisdiction if neither of above applies π = plaintiff; Δ = defendant.\n\nVenue refers to the federal judicial district where a case may be heard. When a plaintiff sues a federal officer or employee, proper venue depends on whether the defendant is sued in his/her official or individual capacity. When an officer or employee is sued in an official capacity, recovery is sought from the federal government and special venue rules apply. Under one such rule, venue is proper where the plaintiff resides. In contrast, when an officer or employee is sued in an individual capacity, recovery is sought from the officer or employee. In these suits, the general venue statute applies, and venue is proper only where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state (residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial portion of the events giving rise to the suit occurred (events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction, but only if neither of the above provisions applies (fallback provision).\n\nHere, the pedestrian (plaintiff) sued the federal employee (defendant) in State C, where the pedestrian resides. Therefore, venue would only be proper in State C if the employee was sued in his official capacity (Choice A). So, if the court dismisses the action for improper venue, it is likely because the pedestrian sued the employee in his individual capacity.\n\n(Choice B) The employee could have been sued in State A, where he resides. However, he also could have been sued in State B, where the accident giving rise to the suit occurred. (Choice D) The fact that the suit does not arise under a federal statute relates to subject-matter jurisdiction, not venue. Additionally, subject-matter jurisdiction exists through diversity jurisdiction since the employee and pedestrian are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Educational objective: When a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her official capacity, special venue rules apply—eg, venue is proper where the plaintiff resides. However, the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(e)(1) (proper venue for federal officer or employee sued in official capacity).\n\n• Stafford v. Briggs, 444 U.S. 527, 542 (1980) (holding that the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity).","rule":"When a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her official capacity, special venue rules apply—eg, venue is proper where the plaintiff resides. However, the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity.","irac":{"issue":"A federal government employee, a citizen of State A, was driving home after his shift in State B when he lost control of his automobile and hit a pedestrian, a citizen of State C. The employee has never been to State C. The pedestrian filed a negligence suit against the employee in federal court in State C to recover $80,000 in damages. The employee filed a motion to dismiss the action based on improper venue. The pedestrian responded that venue was proper in State C, where she resides, because the employee works for the federal government. If the court grants the employee's motion, what is the likely explanation?","rule":"When a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her official capacity, special venue rules apply—eg, venue is proper where the plaintiff resides. However, the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity.","analysis":"Proper venue for suit against federal officer or employee\n\nOfficial capacity Special venue rules permit suit in district where:\n\n(recovery from government) • any Δ resides\n• substantial portion of events occurred or property located\n• π resides if no real property involved Individual capacity General venue statute permits suit in district where:\n\n(recovery from • any Δ resides, if all Δs reside in same state officer/employee) • substantial portion of events occurred or property located\n• any Δ subject to personal jurisdiction if neither of above applies π = plaintiff; Δ = defendant.\n\nVenue refers to the federal judicial district where a case may be heard. When a plaintiff sues a federal officer or employee, proper venue depends on whether the defendant is sued in his/her official or individual capacity. When an officer or employee is sued in an official capacity, recovery is sought from the federal government and special venue rules apply. Under one such rule, venue is proper where the plaintiff resides. In contrast, when an officer or employee is sued in an individual capacity, recovery is sought from the officer or employee. In these suits, the general venue statute applies, and venue is proper only where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state (residency-based venue)\n\n• a substantial portion of the events giving rise to the suit occurred (events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (property-based venue) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction, but only if neither of the above provisions applies (fallback provision).\n\nHere, the pedestrian (plaintiff) sued the federal employee (defendant) in State C, where the pedestrian resides. Therefore, venue would only be proper in State C if the employee was sued in his official capacity (Choice A). So, if the court dismisses the action for improper venue, it is likely because the pedestrian sued the employee in his individual capacity.\n\n(Choice B) The employee could have been sued in State A, where he resides. However, he also could have been sued in State B, where the accident giving rise to the suit occurred. (Choice D) The fact that the suit does not arise under a federal statute relates to subject-matter jurisdiction, not venue. Additionally, subject-matter jurisdiction exists through diversity jurisdiction since the employee and pedestrian are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Educational objective: When a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her official capacity, special venue rules apply—eg, venue is proper where the plaintiff resides. However, the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(e)(1) (proper venue for federal officer or employee sued in official capacity).\n\n• Stafford v. Briggs, 444 U.S. 527, 542 (1980) (holding that the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(e)(1) (proper venue for federal officer or employee sued in official capacity).\n\n• Stafford v. Briggs, 444 U.S. 527, 542 (1980) (holding that the general venue statute applies when a federal officer or employee is sued in his/her individual capacity).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[105],"sourceAnswerPages":[106,107],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 A 州公民的联邦政府雇员，在 B 州下班驾车回家途中失控撞伤 C 州的一名行人。该雇员从未去过 C 州。行人在 C 州联邦法院以过失为由起诉雇员，索赔 80,000 美元。雇员以审判地不当为由申请驳回。行人答称，雇员为联邦政府工作，故其本人居住的 C 州审判地适当。如果法院批准雇员的动议，最可能的理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能在原告居住地起诉联邦政府雇员。","B":"必须在雇员居住的 A 州起诉。","C":"行人以雇员个人身份起诉他。","D":"该诉讼并非依据联邦法律产生。"},"explanationZh":"针对联邦官员或雇员的适当审判地：\n\n以公务身份被诉（赔偿由政府承担）时，特别规则允许在以下地区起诉：\n• 任何被告居住地；\n• 实质部分事件发生地或财产所在地；\n• 不涉及不动产时，原告居住地。\n\n以个人身份被诉（赔偿由官员／雇员承担）时，适用一般规则：\n• 若所有被告均居住于同一州，任何被告居住地；\n• 实质部分事件发生地或财产所在地；\n• 仅在前两项不适用时，任何被告受对人管辖的地区。\n\n原告起诉联邦官员或雇员时，适当审判地取决于被告是以公务身份还是个人身份被诉。公务身份诉讼从联邦政府取得救济，适用特别规则，其中允许在原告住所起诉。个人身份诉讼从官员或雇员本人取得救济，适用一般审判地法。\n\n行人在其居住的 C 州起诉联邦雇员。因此，只有在雇员以公务身份被诉时，C 州才可因原告住所而成为适当审判地（选项 A）。若法院以审判地不当驳回，最可能是因为行人以雇员个人身份起诉。\n\n（选项 B）可在雇员居住的 A 州起诉，也可在事故发生的 B 州起诉。\n\n（选项 D）诉讼是否依据联邦法律产生关系到事项管辖权，而非审判地。本题还可基于多元州籍管辖权建立事项管辖权，因为双方州籍不同且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n学习目标：联邦官员或雇员以公务身份被诉时适用特别审判地规则，例如可在原告住所起诉；以个人身份被诉时适用一般审判地法。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(e)(1)（联邦官员或雇员以公务身份被诉时的审判地）。\n• Stafford v. Briggs, 444 U.S. 527, 542 (1980)（以个人身份被诉时适用一般审判地法）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-036","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":36,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"An entrepreneur from State A decided to sell hot sauce to the public, labeling it \"Best Hot Sauce.\" A company incorporated in State B and headquartered in State C sued the entrepreneur in federal court in State C. The complaint sought $50,000 in damages and alleged that the entrepreneur's use of the name \"Best Hot Sauce\" infringed the company's federal trademark. The entrepreneur filed an answer denying the allegations, and the parties began discovery. Six months later, the entrepreneur moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the entrepreneur's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company's claim arises under federal law.","B":"No, because the entrepreneur waived the right to challenge subject-matter jurisdiction by not raising the issue initially by motion or in the answer.","C":"Yes, because although the claim arises under federal law, the amount in controversy is not satisfied.","D":"Yes, because although there is diversity, the amount in controversy is not satisfied."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction refers to a court's authority to hear a particular type of case or controversy. A federal court* should presume that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction until the party seeking to invoke the court's jurisdiction (generally the plaintiff) proves that the court has either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states.\n\nHere, the company alleges that the entrepreneur infringed upon the company's federal trademark, which involves federal law. As a result, the court has federal-question jurisdiction (and therefore subject-matter jurisdiction) to hear the case. Accordingly, the court should deny the entrepreneur's motion to dismiss. *Most civil actions invoking federal subject-matter jurisdiction are brought before a federal district court and reviewed by the federal appellate court encompassing that district. However, Congress also created special federal courts with jurisdiction over specific types of cases. (Choice B) A lack of personal jurisdiction (ie, a court's authority over a defendant) must be raised at the start of a case or it is waived. In contrast, the right to challenge subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived. Instead, this issue can be raised at any time by the parties or on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte). As a result, the timing of the entrepreneur's motion challenging subject-matter jurisdiction is immaterial. (Choice C) Federal-question jurisdiction does not have an amount-in-controversy requirement. (Choice D) Although the company and the entrepreneur are citizens of different states, the court lacks diversity jurisdiction because the amount in controversy is only $50,000 (less than $75,000). However, the court still has subject-matter jurisdiction because the case involves a federal question. Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction exists if there is federal-question jurisdiction—ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty—or diversity jurisdiction—ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction exists if there is federal-question jurisdiction—ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty—or diversity jurisdiction—ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states.","irac":{"issue":"An entrepreneur from State A decided to sell hot sauce to the public, labeling it \"Best Hot Sauce.\" A company incorporated in State B and headquartered in State C sued the entrepreneur in federal court in State C. The complaint sought $50,000 in damages and alleged that the entrepreneur's use of the name \"Best Hot Sauce\" infringed the company's federal trademark. The entrepreneur filed an answer denying the allegations, and the parties began discovery. Six months later, the entrepreneur moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the entrepreneur's motion?","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction exists if there is federal-question jurisdiction—ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty—or diversity jurisdiction—ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction refers to a court's authority to hear a particular type of case or controversy. A federal court* should presume that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction until the party seeking to invoke the court's jurisdiction (generally the plaintiff) proves that the court has either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states.\n\nHere, the company alleges that the entrepreneur infringed upon the company's federal trademark, which involves federal law. As a result, the court has federal-question jurisdiction (and therefore subject-matter jurisdiction) to hear the case. Accordingly, the court should deny the entrepreneur's motion to dismiss. *Most civil actions invoking federal subject-matter jurisdiction are brought before a federal district court and reviewed by the federal appellate court encompassing that district. However, Congress also created special federal courts with jurisdiction over specific types of cases. (Choice B) A lack of personal jurisdiction (ie, a court's authority over a defendant) must be raised at the start of a case or it is waived. In contrast, the right to challenge subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived. Instead, this issue can be raised at any time by the parties or on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte). As a result, the timing of the entrepreneur's motion challenging subject-matter jurisdiction is immaterial. (Choice C) Federal-question jurisdiction does not have an amount-in-controversy requirement. (Choice D) Although the company and the entrepreneur are citizens of different states, the court lacks diversity jurisdiction because the amount in controversy is only $50,000 (less than $75,000). However, the court still has subject-matter jurisdiction because the case involves a federal question. Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction exists if there is federal-question jurisdiction—ie, the case arises under the Constitution, a federal law, or a treaty—or diversity jurisdiction—ie, the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[108],"sourceAnswerPages":[109,110],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名企业家决定向公众销售辣酱，并将产品标为“Best Hot Sauce”。一家在 B 州注册成立、总部位于 C 州的公司在 C 州联邦法院起诉企业家。起诉状索赔 50,000 美元，并主张企业家使用“Best Hot Sauce”侵犯公司的联邦商标。企业家答辩否认，双方开始证据开示。六个月后，企业家以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为公司的请求依据联邦法律产生。","B":"不应，因为企业家未在最初动议或答辩中提出，已放弃质疑事项管辖权。","C":"应当，因为请求虽依据联邦法律产生，但不满足争议金额要求。","D":"应当，因为双方虽州籍不同，但不满足争议金额要求。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权是法院审理特定类型案件或争议的权限。联邦法院应推定自己缺乏事项管辖权，直至援引管辖权的一方（通常为原告）证明存在：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——案件依据宪法、联邦法律或条约产生；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且双方是不同州公民。\n\n公司主张企业家侵犯其联邦商标，该请求涉及联邦法律。因此，法院享有联邦问题管辖权，进而享有事项管辖权，应驳回企业家的驳回动议。\n\n*多数援引联邦事项管辖权的民事诉讼在联邦地区法院提起，并由涵盖该地区的联邦上诉法院审查。国会也设立了对特定案件享有管辖权的专门联邦法院。\n\n（选项 B）缺乏对人管辖权必须在案件开始时提出，否则放弃；但质疑事项管辖权的权利永不放弃，当事人或法院依职权均可随时提出，所以动议时间并不重要。\n\n（选项 C）联邦问题管辖权没有争议金额要求。\n\n（选项 D）公司与企业家虽州籍不同，但争议金额仅 50,000 美元，确实不满足多元州籍管辖权；法院仍因联邦问题享有事项管辖权。\n\n学习目标：存在联邦问题管辖权，即案件依据宪法、联邦法律或条约产生，或存在多元州籍管辖权，即争议金额超过 75,000 美元且双方州籍不同时，法院享有事项管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331（联邦问题管辖权）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-019"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-037","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":37,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"Plaintiff, a new homeowner, entered into a contract with the defendants, a designer and an architect. The contract required the defendants to remodel the plaintiff's home in State A. The designer resides in the Eastern District of State B, and the architect resides in the Western District of State B. The defendants failed to do any of the work, so the plaintiff sued them together in a federal court in the Eastern District of State B. The defendants filed a motion to dismiss for improper venue. Will the federal court likely grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because both defendants are subject to personal jurisdiction in State B.","B":"No, because the designer resides in the Eastern District of State B and the architect resides in the Western District of State B.","C":"Yes, because the contract was to be performed at the plaintiff's home in State A.","D":"Yes, because the architect resides in the Western District of State B."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Venue—ie, the geographical location of the federal court that can hear a lawsuit—is proper in any judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides—so long as all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n• a substantial portion of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (ie, property-based venue) or\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies (ie, fallback provision). Here, both defendants reside in State B. And since the designer resides in the Eastern District of State B, residency-based venue exists there. The fact that venue would also be proper in the Western District of State B (since the architect resides there) does not negate the proper venue in the Eastern District of State B (Choice D). Therefore, the court will likely deny the defendants' motion to dismiss. (Choice A) Whether a defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction will only be considered for venue purposes if (1) the defendant is a corporation (since it resides where it is subject to personal jurisdiction) or (2) the fallback provision applies because residency, events, and property-based venue cannot be shown. Neither instance is seen here. (Choice C) State A is a proper, events-based venue because the contract giving rise to the suit was to be performed there. But venue can be proper in multiple judicial districts— including the Eastern District of State B. Educational objective: Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial portion of the events occurred or a substantial part of the property is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not exist. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","rule":"Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial portion of the events occurred or a substantial part of the property is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not exist.","irac":{"issue":"Plaintiff, a new homeowner, entered into a contract with the defendants, a designer and an architect. The contract required the defendants to remodel the plaintiff's home in State A. The designer resides in the Eastern District of State B, and the architect resides in the Western District of State B. The defendants failed to do any of the work, so the plaintiff sued them together in a federal court in the Eastern District of State B. The defendants filed a motion to dismiss for improper venue. Will the federal court likely grant the motion?","rule":"Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial portion of the events occurred or a substantial part of the property is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not exist.","analysis":"Venue—ie, the geographical location of the federal court that can hear a lawsuit—is proper in any judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides—so long as all defendants reside in the same state (ie, residency-based venue)\n• a substantial portion of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred (ie, events-based venue) or a substantial part of the property at issue is located (ie, property-based venue) or\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies (ie, fallback provision). Here, both defendants reside in State B. And since the designer resides in the Eastern District of State B, residency-based venue exists there. The fact that venue would also be proper in the Western District of State B (since the architect resides there) does not negate the proper venue in the Eastern District of State B (Choice D). Therefore, the court will likely deny the defendants' motion to dismiss. (Choice A) Whether a defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction will only be considered for venue purposes if (1) the defendant is a corporation (since it resides where it is subject to personal jurisdiction) or (2) the fallback provision applies because residency, events, and property-based venue cannot be shown. Neither instance is seen here. (Choice C) State A is a proper, events-based venue because the contract giving rise to the suit was to be performed there. But venue can be proper in multiple judicial districts— including the Eastern District of State B. Educational objective: Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides so long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial portion of the events occurred or a substantial part of the property is located, or (3) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if the first two provisions do not exist. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[111],"sourceAnswerPages":[112],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名新房主与设计师及建筑师订立合同，要求二人改造原告位于 A 州的住宅。设计师居住在 B 州东区，建筑师居住在 B 州西区。二人未完成任何工作，原告遂在 B 州东区联邦法院共同起诉。两被告以审判地不当为由申请驳回。法院是否可能批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为两被告均受 B 州对人管辖。","B":"不会，因为设计师居住在 B 州东区，建筑师居住在 B 州西区。","C":"会，因为合同预定在原告 A 州住宅履行。","D":"会，因为建筑师居住在 B 州西区。"},"explanationZh":"审判地即可以审理诉讼的联邦法院地理位置。以下司法区的审判地适当：\n• 若所有被告均居住在同一州，任何被告居住的司法区；\n• 引起诉讼的实质部分事件发生地，或涉案财产的实质部分所在地；\n• 仅在前两项均不适用时，任何被告受法院对人管辖的司法区。\n\n两被告均居住于 B 州。设计师居住于 B 州东区，因此东区存在住所型审判地。建筑师居住于西区、使西区也成为适当审判地，并不会否定东区的适当性（选项 D）。法院很可能驳回两被告的动议。\n\n（选项 A）只有被告为公司（公司居住于其受对人管辖的地方），或住所、事件及财产型审判地均不能建立而适用兜底条款时，才以对人管辖权确定审判地。本题均非如此。\n\n（选项 C）合同拟在 A 州履行，A 州属于适当的事件型审判地。但多个司法区可以同时适当，其中包括 B 州东区。\n\n学习目标：以下联邦地区的审判地适当：(1) 若所有被告均居住于同一州，任何被告居住地；(2) 实质部分事件发生地或实质部分财产所在地；或 (3) 仅在前两项不适用时，任何被告受对人管辖的地区。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)（适当审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-007"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-038","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":38,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"After being fired, an executive from State A sued her former employer in state court in State B for $100,000 in damages. The executive's complaint alleged that her former employer, a citizen of State B, violated a federal statute that prohibits gender discrimination in the workplace. The employer timely filed a notice of removal in the federal court in State B and delivered copies to the state court in State B and the executive. Twenty-two days later, the executive filed a motion to remand. How will the federal court likely rule on the executive's motion to remand?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the federal court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the case.","B":"Deny the motion, because the plaintiff did not consent to the removal.","C":"Grant the motion, because it was timely filed.","D":"Grant the motion, because the employer is a citizen of State B."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A plaintiff can choose to file certain lawsuits in state or federal court. When a suit is filed in state court, the defendant can remove the suit to the federal court located in the same district as the state court if the suit falls within the federal court's original (ie, subject-matter) jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction can arise from either:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (as seen here).\n\nRemoval requires the consent of all other defendants (if any) but not the plaintiff (Choice B). The defendant must then file a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days of receipt of the initial complaint or service of the summons—whichever period is shorter. A copy of the notice must also be delivered to the state court and the other parties. The plaintiff can then seek to return the case to state court by filing a motion to remand in federal court within 30 days* after the notice of removal is filed. But the plaintiff's motion will be denied when, as here, subject-matter jurisdiction exists and the requirements for removal are met (Choice C). *A motion to remand for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction does not need to meet this 30-day deadline and can be filed at any time. (Choice D) The home-court advantage (ie, forum-defendant) rule prohibits removal when the federal court's original jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and a defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. Here, the State B federal court's original jurisdiction arises from federal-question jurisdiction, so the fact that the employer is a citizen of State B is irrelevant. Educational objective: A defendant can remove a case to federal court if (1) the case falls within that court's original jurisdiction, (2) all other defendants consent to removal, (3) the defendant files a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days of receiving the summons or complaint, and (4) a copy of the notice is provided to the state court and other parties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1446–1447 (removal procedures).","rule":"A defendant can remove a case to federal court if (1) the case falls within that court's original jurisdiction, (2) all other defendants consent to removal, (3) the defendant files a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days of receiving the summons or complaint, and (4) a copy of the notice is provided to the state court and other parties.","irac":{"issue":"After being fired, an executive from State A sued her former employer in state court in State B for $100,000 in damages. The executive's complaint alleged that her former employer, a citizen of State B, violated a federal statute that prohibits gender discrimination in the workplace. The employer timely filed a notice of removal in the federal court in State B and delivered copies to the state court in State B and the executive. Twenty-two days later, the executive filed a motion to remand. How will the federal court likely rule on the executive's motion to remand?","rule":"A defendant can remove a case to federal court if (1) the case falls within that court's original jurisdiction, (2) all other defendants consent to removal, (3) the defendant files a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days of receiving the summons or complaint, and (4) a copy of the notice is provided to the state court and other parties.","analysis":"A plaintiff can choose to file certain lawsuits in state or federal court. When a suit is filed in state court, the defendant can remove the suit to the federal court located in the same district as the state court if the suit falls within the federal court's original (ie, subject-matter) jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction can arise from either:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (as seen here).\n\nRemoval requires the consent of all other defendants (if any) but not the plaintiff (Choice B). The defendant must then file a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days of receipt of the initial complaint or service of the summons—whichever period is shorter. A copy of the notice must also be delivered to the state court and the other parties. The plaintiff can then seek to return the case to state court by filing a motion to remand in federal court within 30 days* after the notice of removal is filed. But the plaintiff's motion will be denied when, as here, subject-matter jurisdiction exists and the requirements for removal are met (Choice C). *A motion to remand for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction does not need to meet this 30-day deadline and can be filed at any time. (Choice D) The home-court advantage (ie, forum-defendant) rule prohibits removal when the federal court's original jurisdiction arises from diversity jurisdiction and a defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. Here, the State B federal court's original jurisdiction arises from federal-question jurisdiction, so the fact that the employer is a citizen of State B is irrelevant. Educational objective: A defendant can remove a case to federal court if (1) the case falls within that court's original jurisdiction, (2) all other defendants consent to removal, (3) the defendant files a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days of receiving the summons or complaint, and (4) a copy of the notice is provided to the state court and other parties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1446–1447 (removal procedures).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1446–1447 (removal procedures).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[114],"sourceAnswerPages":[115],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 A 州的高级管理人员被解雇后，在 B 州州法院起诉其前雇主，索赔 100,000 美元。起诉状主张，该 B 州公民雇主违反禁止工作场所性别歧视的联邦法律。雇主及时向 B 州联邦法院提交移送通知，并向 B 州州法院及管理人员送交副本。22 天后，管理人员提出发回州法院的动议。联邦法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为联邦法院对该案享有事项管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为原告未同意移送。","C":"批准动议，因为该动议及时提出。","D":"批准动议，因为雇主是 B 州公民。"},"explanationZh":"原告可以选择将某些诉讼提交州法院或联邦法院。在州法院起诉后，若案件属于联邦法院原始事项管辖权，被告可将其移送至州法院所在同一地区的联邦法院。原始管辖权可基于：\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方州籍不同；或\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生，本题即属此类。\n\n移送需要其他全部被告（如有）同意，但不需要原告同意（选项 B）。被告必须在收到最初起诉状或传票送达后 30 天内（以较短期间为准）向联邦法院提交移送通知，并将副本送交州法院及其他当事人。\n\n原告随后可在提交移送通知后 30 天内向联邦法院提出发回动议。*但如本题一样，联邦法院享有事项管辖权且移送要求均满足，原告动议应被驳回（选项 C）。\n\n*以缺乏事项管辖权为由的发回动议不受 30 天期限约束，可随时提出。\n\n（选项 D）法院州被告规则在联邦法院原始管辖权基于多元州籍，且一名被告是起诉所在州公民时禁止移送。本题管辖权基于联邦问题，所以雇主为 B 州公民无关。\n\n学习目标：被告可在以下条件下移送：(1) 案件属于联邦法院原始管辖权；(2) 其他全部被告同意；(3) 在收到传票或起诉状后 30 天内提交通知；(4) 向州法院及其他当事人提供副本。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441（移送要求）。\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1446–1447（移送程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-020"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-039","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":39,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"An investor from State A purchased a house in State B for $300,000 and immediately rented it out. Shortly thereafter, a company incorporated and headquartered in State B sued the investor in a federal court in State B for breaching a shareholder's agreement. The investor signed and allegedly breached the agreement in State C and was served with process at his home in State A. The company sought to attach the investor's house in State B to satisfy any judgment in case the investor could not pay. The investor moved to dismiss the suit for lack of personal jurisdiction. State B has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction over property located within the state. Is the court likely to grant the investor's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because State B's long-arm statute establishes personal jurisdiction.","B":"No, because the presence of the house in State B confers personal jurisdiction over the investor.","C":"Yes, because ownership of property cannot establish minimum contacts.","D":"Yes, because the company's claim is unrelated to the investor's ownership of the house."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction refers to a court's authority over the parties or property before it. A federal district court has the same personal jurisdiction as the courts of the state in which it is located. Therefore, a federal district court can use the forum state's long-arm statute to acquire personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant—so long as the exercise of personal jurisdiction comports with due process. This constitutional requirement is met when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from the defendant's minimum contacts with the state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice.*\n\nA defendant's ownership of property in the forum state establishes minimum contacts in an action related to that property—ie, in rem and quasi in rem type 1 actions (Choice C). However, this does not establish minimum contacts in an action unrelated to that property—ie, a quasi in rem type 2 action in which the property is attached to satisfy a judgment against the defendant. Here, the company sued the investor and attached his house in State B to satisfy any judgment. The house cannot serve as the basis for minimum contacts since this suit for breach of a shareholder's agreement is unrelated to the house. And there is no indication that the investor has any other contacts with State B since he resides in State A and the agreement was signed and allegedly breached in State C (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the investor's motion. *Due process is also satisfied if the defendant consented to jurisdiction or was served with process within the forum state. (Choice A) State B's long-arm statute gives state and federal courts in State B personal jurisdiction over property in the state. But since the investor lacks minimum contacts with State B, using the long-arm statute would violate due process. Educational objective: Property located within the state can establish the minimum contacts necessary for personal jurisdiction if the property is related to the dispute—ie, in rem or quasi in rem type 1 action. However, minimum contacts are not established if the property is unrelated to the plaintiff's claim—ie, quasi in rem type 2 action. References\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 212 (1977) (holding that personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant must always satisfy the minimum contacts requirement).","rule":"Property located within the state can establish the minimum contacts necessary for personal jurisdiction if the property is related to the dispute—ie, in rem or quasi in rem type 1 action. However, minimum contacts are not established if the property is unrelated to the plaintiff's claim—ie, quasi in rem type 2 action.","irac":{"issue":"An investor from State A purchased a house in State B for $300,000 and immediately rented it out. Shortly thereafter, a company incorporated and headquartered in State B sued the investor in a federal court in State B for breaching a shareholder's agreement. The investor signed and allegedly breached the agreement in State C and was served with process at his home in State A. The company sought to attach the investor's house in State B to satisfy any judgment in case the investor could not pay. The investor moved to dismiss the suit for lack of personal jurisdiction. State B has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction over property located within the state. Is the court likely to grant the investor's motion?","rule":"Property located within the state can establish the minimum contacts necessary for personal jurisdiction if the property is related to the dispute—ie, in rem or quasi in rem type 1 action. However, minimum contacts are not established if the property is unrelated to the plaintiff's claim—ie, quasi in rem type 2 action.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction refers to a court's authority over the parties or property before it. A federal district court has the same personal jurisdiction as the courts of the state in which it is located. Therefore, a federal district court can use the forum state's long-arm statute to acquire personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant—so long as the exercise of personal jurisdiction comports with due process. This constitutional requirement is met when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from the defendant's minimum contacts with the state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice.*\n\nA defendant's ownership of property in the forum state establishes minimum contacts in an action related to that property—ie, in rem and quasi in rem type 1 actions (Choice C). However, this does not establish minimum contacts in an action unrelated to that property—ie, a quasi in rem type 2 action in which the property is attached to satisfy a judgment against the defendant. Here, the company sued the investor and attached his house in State B to satisfy any judgment. The house cannot serve as the basis for minimum contacts since this suit for breach of a shareholder's agreement is unrelated to the house. And there is no indication that the investor has any other contacts with State B since he resides in State A and the agreement was signed and allegedly breached in State C (Choice B). Therefore, the court will likely grant the investor's motion. *Due process is also satisfied if the defendant consented to jurisdiction or was served with process within the forum state. (Choice A) State B's long-arm statute gives state and federal courts in State B personal jurisdiction over property in the state. But since the investor lacks minimum contacts with State B, using the long-arm statute would violate due process. Educational objective: Property located within the state can establish the minimum contacts necessary for personal jurisdiction if the property is related to the dispute—ie, in rem or quasi in rem type 1 action. However, minimum contacts are not established if the property is unrelated to the plaintiff's claim—ie, quasi in rem type 2 action. References\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 212 (1977) (holding that personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant must always satisfy the minimum contacts requirement).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 212 (1977) (holding that personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant must always satisfy the minimum contacts requirement).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[117],"sourceAnswerPages":[118,119],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的一名投资者以 300,000 美元在 B 州购买住宅并立即出租。不久，一家在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州的公司在 B 州联邦法院起诉投资者违反股东协议。投资者在 C 州签署并据称违反该协议，且在其 A 州住所获得送达。公司申请扣押投资者位于 B 州的住宅，以便投资者无力支付时用于满足判决。投资者以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。B 州长臂法授权其法院对州内财产行使对人管辖权。法院是否可能批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为 B 州长臂法建立了对人管辖权。","B":"不会，因为住宅位于 B 州使法院对投资者享有对人管辖权。","C":"会，因为财产所有权不能建立最低限度联系。","D":"会，因为公司的请求与投资者拥有该住宅无关。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权是法院对当事人或其面前财产的权限。联邦地区法院享有与其所在州法院相同的对人管辖权，因此可以利用法院州长臂法对外州被告取得管辖，但行使必须符合正当程序。满足正当程序要求需：\n• 原告请求源于被告与该州的最低限度联系；并且\n• 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义。*\n\n被告在法院州拥有财产，在与该财产有关的诉讼，即对物诉讼及第一类准对物诉讼中，可以建立最低限度联系（选项 C）。但在与该财产无关的诉讼中，即扣押财产仅为满足针对被告判决的第二类准对物诉讼，不能建立最低限度联系。\n\n公司起诉投资者并扣押其 B 州住宅以满足可能判决。但违反股东协议的诉讼与住宅无关，住宅不能成为最低限度联系基础。投资者居住于 A 州，协议在 C 州签署并据称在那里违反，也无其他 B 州联系（选项 B）。法院很可能批准动议。\n\n*被告同意管辖或在法院州境内获得送达时，也满足正当程序。\n\n（选项 A）B 州长臂法虽授权州及联邦法院对州内财产行使管辖，但投资者缺乏最低限度联系，适用长臂法会违反正当程序。\n\n学习目标：州内财产与争议有关时，可建立对人管辖权所需最低限度联系，即对物或第一类准对物诉讼；财产与原告请求无关、仅用于满足判决时，即第二类准对物诉讼，不能建立。\n\n参考资料：\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 212 (1977)（对外州被告行使管辖始终必须满足最低限度联系要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-021"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-040","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":40,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in a federal court in State A to quiet title to a farm. The plaintiff attempted to serve the defendant with process through certified mail, but the letter was returned to the plaintiff marked \"unclaimed.\" The plaintiff attempted service through certified mail a second time, but the letter was returned to the plaintiff in the same fashion. The plaintiff did not attempt to contact the defendant again. At trial, the defendant did not appear and defend. As a result, the court entered a default judgment in the plaintiff's favor. Two months later, the defendant discovered the plaintiff on the farm and learned about the default judgment. The defendant immediately moved for relief from the default judgment in the federal court in State A on the ground that the plaintiff failed to provide him with adequate notice of the suit. State A's rules of procedure permit service of process by certified or ordinary mail. Will the federal court likely grant the defendant's motion for relief?","choices":{"A":"No, because the plaintiff attempted to serve the defendant with process twice.","B":"No, because the plaintiff complied with State A's service-of-process rules.","C":"Yes, because the defendant did not receive actual notice of the lawsuit.","D":"Yes, because the plaintiff failed to take additional reasonable steps to provide notice."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Service-of-process requirements\n\nProcedural Process may be served on defendant-individual located in U.S. by: rules\n• delivering process to defendant personally or authorized agent (FRCP 4(e)) • leaving process at defendant's dwelling with resident of suitable age & discretion or\n• following rules of state where court sits or service is made Due process • Notice must reasonably apprise defendant of pending suit & afford opportunity to present objections\n• If plaintiff knows defendant did not receive notice, plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA defendant can obtain relief from a default judgment when the defendant's failure to timely serve an answer was due to excusable neglect. Excusable neglect can arise when the defendant failed to receive adequate notice of the suit through service of process as required by procedural rules and due process. FRCP 4 provides several procedures for serving process on a defendant-individual in the U.S., including:\n• delivering process to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized to receive process)\n• leaving process at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion or\n• following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made. Here, the plaintiff complied with the service-of-process rules for State A (where the federal court sits) by attempting to serve the defendant with process twice through certified mail. However, her attempt to provide adequate notice must also comply with due process. Under this constitutional requirement, actual notice is not required (Choice C). Instead, notice must reasonably apprise the defendant of the pending suit and afford the defendant an opportunity to present objections. So if the plaintiff knows that the defendant did not receive notice (eg, because the letter was returned \"unclaimed\"), then the plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice. The plaintiff here failed to take such steps, so the court will likely grant the defendant's motion for relief (Choices A & B). Educational objective:\n\nNotice, as required by due process, means that a defendant must be reasonably apprised of the pending suit and afforded an opportunity to present objections. So if the plaintiff knows the defendant did not receive notice through service of process, then the plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Jones v. Flowers, 547 U.S. 220, 234 (2006) (explaining that when the plaintiff knows that the defendant has not received notice, additional reasonable steps need to be taken to provide such notice).\n\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 314 (1950) (stating that due process requires notice apprising the defendant of the action and affording an opportunity to be heard).","rule":"Notice, as required by due process, means that a defendant must be reasonably apprised of the pending suit and afforded an opportunity to present objections. So if the plaintiff knows the defendant did not receive notice through service of process, then the plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in a federal court in State A to quiet title to a farm. The plaintiff attempted to serve the defendant with process through certified mail, but the letter was returned to the plaintiff marked \"unclaimed.\" The plaintiff attempted service through certified mail a second time, but the letter was returned to the plaintiff in the same fashion. The plaintiff did not attempt to contact the defendant again. At trial, the defendant did not appear and defend. As a result, the court entered a default judgment in the plaintiff's favor. Two months later, the defendant discovered the plaintiff on the farm and learned about the default judgment. The defendant immediately moved for relief from the default judgment in the federal court in State A on the ground that the plaintiff failed to provide him with adequate notice of the suit. State A's rules of procedure permit service of process by certified or ordinary mail. Will the federal court likely grant the defendant's motion for relief?","rule":"Notice, as required by due process, means that a defendant must be reasonably apprised of the pending suit and afforded an opportunity to present objections. So if the plaintiff knows the defendant did not receive notice through service of process, then the plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"Service-of-process requirements\n\nProcedural Process may be served on defendant-individual located in U.S. by: rules\n• delivering process to defendant personally or authorized agent (FRCP 4(e)) • leaving process at defendant's dwelling with resident of suitable age & discretion or\n• following rules of state where court sits or service is made Due process • Notice must reasonably apprise defendant of pending suit & afford opportunity to present objections\n• If plaintiff knows defendant did not receive notice, plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA defendant can obtain relief from a default judgment when the defendant's failure to timely serve an answer was due to excusable neglect. Excusable neglect can arise when the defendant failed to receive adequate notice of the suit through service of process as required by procedural rules and due process. FRCP 4 provides several procedures for serving process on a defendant-individual in the U.S., including:\n• delivering process to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized to receive process)\n• leaving process at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion or\n• following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made. Here, the plaintiff complied with the service-of-process rules for State A (where the federal court sits) by attempting to serve the defendant with process twice through certified mail. However, her attempt to provide adequate notice must also comply with due process. Under this constitutional requirement, actual notice is not required (Choice C). Instead, notice must reasonably apprise the defendant of the pending suit and afford the defendant an opportunity to present objections. So if the plaintiff knows that the defendant did not receive notice (eg, because the letter was returned \"unclaimed\"), then the plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice. The plaintiff here failed to take such steps, so the court will likely grant the defendant's motion for relief (Choices A & B). Educational objective:\n\nNotice, as required by due process, means that a defendant must be reasonably apprised of the pending suit and afforded an opportunity to present objections. So if the plaintiff knows the defendant did not receive notice through service of process, then the plaintiff must take additional reasonable steps to provide notice. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Jones v. Flowers, 547 U.S. 220, 234 (2006) (explaining that when the plaintiff knows that the defendant has not received notice, additional reasonable steps need to be taken to provide such notice).\n\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 314 (1950) (stating that due process requires notice apprising the defendant of the action and affording an opportunity to be heard).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Jones v. Flowers, 547 U.S. 220, 234 (2006) (explaining that when the plaintiff knows that the defendant has not received notice, additional reasonable steps need to be taken to provide such notice).\n\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 314 (1950) (stating that due process requires notice apprising the defendant of the action and affording an opportunity to be heard).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[120],"sourceAnswerPages":[121,122],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"原告在 A 州联邦法院对被告提起确认农场产权的多元州籍诉讼。原告两次尝试以挂号邮件送达，但信件均以“无人领取”退回，此后未再联系被告。审判时被告未出庭答辩，法院遂作出原告胜诉的缺席判决。两个月后，被告在农场发现原告，得知缺席判决，立即以原告未提供充分诉讼通知为由申请救济。A 州程序规则允许挂号或普通邮件送达。联邦法院是否可能批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为原告已两次尝试送达。","B":"不会，因为原告遵守 A 州送达规则。","C":"会，因为被告没有实际收到诉讼通知。","D":"会，因为原告未采取额外合理步骤提供通知。"},"explanationZh":"送达要求：\n\n程序规则：对美国境内个人被告，可向本人或获授权代理人交付，留在被告住所交给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人，或遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州规则。\n\n正当程序：通知必须能够合理告知被告尚待审理的诉讼，并给予提出异议的机会；若原告知道被告未收到通知，必须采取额外合理步骤。\n\n被告未及时答辩是因可原谅的疏忽时，可以获得缺席判决救济。被告未按照程序规则和正当程序要求通过送达获得充分通知，可构成可原谅疏忽。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》第 4 条允许向本人或获授权代理人交付、留在住所交给年龄及判断能力适当的居民，或遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州规则。原告两次以挂号邮件尝试送达，符合 A 州规则。\n\n但通知还必须符合正当程序。正当程序不要求实际通知（选项 C），而要求以能够合理告知被告诉讼并给予提出异议机会的方式通知。如果原告知道被告没有收到，例如邮件以“无人领取”退回，就必须采取额外合理步骤。原告未采取此类步骤，法院很可能批准救济（选项 A、B）。\n\n学习目标：正当程序要求被告获得能够合理告知尚待诉讼并给予提出异议机会的通知。原告知道送达未使被告收到通知时，必须采取额外合理步骤。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Jones v. Flowers, 547 U.S. 220, 234 (2006)（已知被告未收到通知时必须采取额外合理步骤）。\n• Mullane v. Cent. Hanover Bank & Trust Co., 339 U.S. 306, 314 (1950)（正当程序要求足以告知诉讼并给予陈述机会的通知）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-041","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":41,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"Two tourists from State A planned a trip to a foreign country. Before leaving, one of the tourists saw a television advertisement for a guided hike in the foreign country. The tourist called the company responsible for the advertisement, which is incorporated and has its principal place of business in the foreign country. During the phone call, the tourist told the company's representative that neither of the tourists had ever been on a professional hike. The representative ensured the tourist that all of the company's guides were properly trained. The tourist then hired a guide from that company. In the foreign country, the guide led the tourists on a steep and dangerous mountain trail meant for expert hikers. During the hike, the tourists fell into a deep ravine and died. Another group of hikers, all of whom reside in the foreign country, witnessed the event. The deceased tourists' personal representatives joined together to sue the company in federal court in State A for false advertisement and wrongful death. The company wants the suit to be heard in its own country instead of State A. Which of the following motions gives the company its best chance to achieve its goal?","choices":{"A":"A motion to dismiss the lawsuit for improper venue.","B":"A motion to dismiss the lawsuit for lack of personal jurisdiction.","C":"A motion to dismiss the lawsuit under the doctrine of forum non conveniens.","D":"A motion to transfer the lawsuit to a court located in the foreign country."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Doctrine of forum non conveniens\n\nDefinition • Power of federal court to dismiss or stay lawsuit when state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear case Private interest • Accessibility of evidence factors • Availability of witnesses\n• Burden on defendant\n• Enforceability of judgment Public interest • Ability to comprehend substantive law factors • Choice of law\n• Interest of jury/community\n• Functionality of foreign judicial system The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows federal courts to dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear the case. This is permitted even when subject-matter jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction, and venue are proper—but should rarely be applied. As a result, the court must consider several private and public interest factors, including the accessibility of the evidence and the witnesses. Here, the foreign company wants the lawsuit to be heard in its own country instead of in State A. Several factors weigh in the company's favor since much of the evidence and all the witnesses are in the foreign country. Therefore, the company's best chance to have the suit heard in its own country is to file a motion to dismiss under the doctrine of forum non conveniens. (Choices A & B) Venue is proper in any federal district where a defendant resides as long as all the defendants reside in the same state. A defendant business resides in every district where it is subject to personal jurisdiction. Here, the company is subject to personal jurisdiction in any State A district since its minimum contacts with State A—the advertisement, business phone call, and agreement—gave rise to the tourists' claims. Therefore, motions to dismiss for improper venue or lack of personal jurisdiction would fail. (Choice D) A federal court can only transfer a suit to another federal court. Therefore, a suit can only be moved to a state or foreign judicial system if the federal court dismisses or stays the suit under the doctrine of forum non conveniens so that the plaintiff can refile the suit in an alternative judicial system. Educational objective:\n\nUnder the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is better suited to hear it. When making this decision, the court will examine several factors, including accessibility of evidence and witnesses. References\n• Am. Dredging Co. v. Miller, 510 U.S. 443, 447–48 (1994) (explaining the doctrine of forum non conveniens).\n\n• Gulf Oil Corp. v. Gilbert, 330 U.S. 501, 508–09 (1947) (listing private and public interest factors to consider in a forum non conveniens analysis).","rule":"Under the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is better suited to hear it. When making this decision, the court will examine several factors, including accessibility of evidence and witnesses.","irac":{"issue":"Two tourists from State A planned a trip to a foreign country. Before leaving, one of the tourists saw a television advertisement for a guided hike in the foreign country. The tourist called the company responsible for the advertisement, which is incorporated and has its principal place of business in the foreign country. During the phone call, the tourist told the company's representative that neither of the tourists had ever been on a professional hike. The representative ensured the tourist that all of the company's guides were properly trained. The tourist then hired a guide from that company. In the foreign country, the guide led the tourists on a steep and dangerous mountain trail meant for expert hikers. During the hike, the tourists fell into a deep ravine and died. Another group of hikers, all of whom reside in the foreign country, witnessed the event. The deceased tourists' personal representatives joined together to sue the company in federal court in State A for false advertisement and wrongful death. The company wants the suit to be heard in its own country instead of State A. Which of the following motions gives the company its best chance to achieve its goal?","rule":"Under the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is better suited to hear it. When making this decision, the court will examine several factors, including accessibility of evidence and witnesses.","analysis":"Doctrine of forum non conveniens\n\nDefinition • Power of federal court to dismiss or stay lawsuit when state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear case Private interest • Accessibility of evidence factors • Availability of witnesses\n• Burden on defendant\n• Enforceability of judgment Public interest • Ability to comprehend substantive law factors • Choice of law\n• Interest of jury/community\n• Functionality of foreign judicial system The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows federal courts to dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear the case. This is permitted even when subject-matter jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction, and venue are proper—but should rarely be applied. As a result, the court must consider several private and public interest factors, including the accessibility of the evidence and the witnesses. Here, the foreign company wants the lawsuit to be heard in its own country instead of in State A. Several factors weigh in the company's favor since much of the evidence and all the witnesses are in the foreign country. Therefore, the company's best chance to have the suit heard in its own country is to file a motion to dismiss under the doctrine of forum non conveniens. (Choices A & B) Venue is proper in any federal district where a defendant resides as long as all the defendants reside in the same state. A defendant business resides in every district where it is subject to personal jurisdiction. Here, the company is subject to personal jurisdiction in any State A district since its minimum contacts with State A—the advertisement, business phone call, and agreement—gave rise to the tourists' claims. Therefore, motions to dismiss for improper venue or lack of personal jurisdiction would fail. (Choice D) A federal court can only transfer a suit to another federal court. Therefore, a suit can only be moved to a state or foreign judicial system if the federal court dismisses or stays the suit under the doctrine of forum non conveniens so that the plaintiff can refile the suit in an alternative judicial system. Educational objective:\n\nUnder the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is better suited to hear it. When making this decision, the court will examine several factors, including accessibility of evidence and witnesses. References\n• Am. Dredging Co. v. Miller, 510 U.S. 443, 447–48 (1994) (explaining the doctrine of forum non conveniens).\n\n• Gulf Oil Corp. v. Gilbert, 330 U.S. 501, 508–09 (1947) (listing private and public interest factors to consider in a forum non conveniens analysis).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Am. Dredging Co. v. Miller, 510 U.S. 443, 447–48 (1994) (explaining the doctrine of forum non conveniens).\n\n• Gulf Oil Corp. v. Gilbert, 330 U.S. 501, 508–09 (1947) (listing private and public interest factors to consider in a forum non conveniens analysis).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[123],"sourceAnswerPages":[124,125],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州的两名游客计划前往某外国旅行。出发前，其中一人看到该外国徒步导览的电视广告，遂致电负责广告的公司。公司在该外国注册成立，主要营业地也在那里。游客告诉公司代表，两人从未参加专业徒步活动；代表保证公司的所有向导均受过适当训练。游客于是雇用该公司的一名向导。在外国，向导带二人进入一条仅适合专业徒步者的陡峭危险山路，二人跌入深谷死亡。另一组全部居住于该外国的徒步者目睹事故。死者的遗产代表共同在 A 州联邦法院以虚假广告和非正常死亡为由起诉公司。公司希望案件在本国而非 A 州审理。以下哪项动议最可能实现其目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"以审判地不当为由申请驳回。","B":"以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。","C":"依据不方便法院原则申请驳回。","D":"申请将诉讼转移至该外国法院。"},"explanationZh":"不方便法院原则：\n\n定义：当州或外国司法制度更适合审理案件时，联邦法院有权驳回或中止诉讼。\n\n私人利益因素：\n• 证据可取得性；\n• 证人可到庭性；\n• 被告负担；\n• 判决可执行性。\n\n公共利益因素：\n• 理解实体法的能力；\n• 法律选择；\n• 陪审团及社区利益；\n• 外国司法制度的运作能力。\n\n即使事项管辖权、对人管辖权和审判地均适当，如果州或外国司法制度更适合审理，不方便法院原则仍允许联邦法院驳回或中止，但应极少适用。法院必须考量多项私人和公共利益因素，包括证据和证人的可取得性。\n\n本题中，公司希望案件在本国而非 A 州审理。大量证据以及全部目击证人均在外国，多项因素有利于公司。因此，公司最有机会通过依据不方便法院原则申请驳回，使案件在本国审理。\n\n（选项 A、B）若所有被告均居住于同一州，任何被告居住地均为适当审判地。公司在其受对人管辖的每个地区均视为居民。公司针对 A 州投放广告、进行业务电话并达成协议，这些最低限度联系产生了游客请求，所以公司在 A 州各地区均受对人管辖。因此，以审判地不当或缺乏对人管辖权申请驳回均会失败。\n\n（选项 D）联邦法院只能将案件转移至另一联邦法院。若要让州或外国司法制度审理，只能依不方便法院原则驳回或中止，之后由原告在替代司法制度重新起诉。\n\n学习目标：州或外国司法辖区更适合审理时，联邦法院可依不方便法院原则驳回或中止。法院会考察包括证据和证人可取得性在内的多项因素。\n\n参考资料：\n• Am. Dredging Co. v. Miller, 510 U.S. 443, 447–48 (1994)（说明不方便法院原则）。\n• Gulf Oil Corp. v. Gilbert, 330 U.S. 501, 508–09 (1947)（列明私人和公共利益因素）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-042","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":42,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"After 10 years of marriage, a husband and wife decided to separate. The husband remained in the family home in State A, while the wife moved into an apartment in State B. Seven months later, the wife filed for divorce in a state court in State B. She knew that her husband would be attending a professional conference in State B the following week, so she hired a process server to personally hand-deliver a copy of the divorce petition and the State B summons to him at the conference. The husband moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the husband's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the husband was personally served with process in State B.","B":"No, because there is no other state that is more convenient for both of the parties than State B.","C":"Yes, because State A was the couple's home state for 10 years.","D":"Yes, because the husband was in State B for a matter unrelated to the marriage."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) Due process requires that a court have personal jurisdiction over all of the parties in a dispute before it. One way to establish personal jurisdiction is through tag jurisdiction (ie, transient jurisdiction). Under this method, personal jurisdiction is acquired by serving the defendant* with formal process while the defendant is voluntarily in the forum state— even if briefly and for a purpose unrelated to the plaintiff's claim. Here, the husband was served with the divorce petition and State B summons while he was in State B for a conference—a matter unrelated to the marriage (Choice D). Therefore, the court has personal jurisdiction over the husband and should deny his motion to dismiss. *Personal jurisdiction is always focused on the defendant because the plaintiff consents to personal jurisdiction by filing the suit in that court. (Choice B) Whether another state or court is a more convenient forum for the parties is only considered when a change of venue is requested. (Choice C) Although State A was the couple's home state for 10 years, State B has personal jurisdiction since the wife initiated the suit there and the husband was served with process there.\n\nEducational objective: Personal jurisdiction can be established by serving the defendant with formal process while the defendant is voluntarily in the forum state even if briefly and for a purpose unrelated to the plaintiff's claim. References\n• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 619 (1990) (holding that personal jurisdiction is established when the defendant is served with process in the forum state).","rule":"Personal jurisdiction can be established by serving the defendant with formal process while the defendant is voluntarily in the forum state even if briefly and for a purpose unrelated to the plaintiff's claim.","irac":{"issue":"After 10 years of marriage, a husband and wife decided to separate. The husband remained in the family home in State A, while the wife moved into an apartment in State B. Seven months later, the wife filed for divorce in a state court in State B. She knew that her husband would be attending a professional conference in State B the following week, so she hired a process server to personally hand-deliver a copy of the divorce petition and the State B summons to him at the conference. The husband moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the husband's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Personal jurisdiction can be established by serving the defendant with formal process while the defendant is voluntarily in the forum state even if briefly and for a purpose unrelated to the plaintiff's claim.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) Due process requires that a court have personal jurisdiction over all of the parties in a dispute before it. One way to establish personal jurisdiction is through tag jurisdiction (ie, transient jurisdiction). Under this method, personal jurisdiction is acquired by serving the defendant* with formal process while the defendant is voluntarily in the forum state— even if briefly and for a purpose unrelated to the plaintiff's claim. Here, the husband was served with the divorce petition and State B summons while he was in State B for a conference—a matter unrelated to the marriage (Choice D). Therefore, the court has personal jurisdiction over the husband and should deny his motion to dismiss. *Personal jurisdiction is always focused on the defendant because the plaintiff consents to personal jurisdiction by filing the suit in that court. (Choice B) Whether another state or court is a more convenient forum for the parties is only considered when a change of venue is requested. (Choice C) Although State A was the couple's home state for 10 years, State B has personal jurisdiction since the wife initiated the suit there and the husband was served with process there.\n\nEducational objective: Personal jurisdiction can be established by serving the defendant with formal process while the defendant is voluntarily in the forum state even if briefly and for a purpose unrelated to the plaintiff's claim. References\n• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 619 (1990) (holding that personal jurisdiction is established when the defendant is served with process in the forum state).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 619 (1990) (holding that personal jurisdiction is established when the defendant is served with process in the forum state).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[126],"sourceAnswerPages":[127,128],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"结婚十年后，一对夫妻决定分居。丈夫留在 A 州家庭住宅，妻子搬入 B 州一套公寓。七个月后，妻子在 B 州州法院申请离婚。她知道丈夫次周会到 B 州参加职业会议，遂聘请送达人在会议现场亲自向丈夫交付离婚申请书及 B 州传票。丈夫以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为丈夫已在 B 州境内获得亲自送达。","B":"不应，因为没有其他州比 B 州对双方更为便利。","C":"应当，因为 A 州是夫妻十年的家庭所在州。","D":"应当，因为丈夫到 B 州是为与婚姻无关的事项。"},"explanationZh":"正当程序要求法院对争议中的所有当事人享有对人管辖权。一种建立方式是标签管辖权（临时在场管辖权）：被告自愿身处法院州时向其正式送达，即使停留短暂且目的与原告请求无关，也可取得管辖权。\n\n丈夫因参加与婚姻无关的会议身处 B 州时，收到离婚申请及 B 州传票送达（选项 D）。因此，法院对其享有对人管辖权，应驳回其动议。\n\n*对人管辖权分析始终聚焦被告，因为原告向该法院起诉即同意其管辖。\n\n（选项 B）只有在请求变更审判地时，才考量另一州或法院是否对双方更便利。\n\n（选项 C）A 州虽是夫妻十年的家庭所在州，妻子在 B 州起诉且丈夫在该州获得送达，B 州仍享有对人管辖权。\n\n学习目标：被告自愿身处法院州时获得正式送达，即使停留短暂且目的与原告请求无关，也可建立对人管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 495 U.S. 604, 619 (1990)（被告在法院州获得送达即建立对人管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-043","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":43,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A businesswoman sued a website owner in federal court for violating federal copyright law. The businesswoman sent a copy of the complaint and a form requesting waiver of service of process to the website owner's residence via first-class mail. The request named the court where the complaint was filed and the date when the request was sent. The businesswoman did not receive anything back from the website owner, so 31 days after mailing the request, she had a process server hand-deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the website owner. Is the website owner liable for the expenses associated with the process server?","choices":{"A":"No, because the businesswoman did not include all that was necessary for a waiver request.","B":"No, because the businesswoman did not wait 60 days before hiring the process server.","C":"Yes, because a court must impose the cost of service upon a defendant who fails to waive service.","D":"Yes, because a defendant has a duty to avoid unnecessary service-of-process expenses."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Waiving service of process\n\n(FRCP 4(d))\n\nRequest for waiver • In writing\n• Addressed to defendant (or authorized agent)\n• Names court where complaint filed\n• Provides copy of complaint, two waiver forms & prepaid means to return form\n• Informs defendant about consequences of waiving & not waiving\n• Lists date request is sent\n• Sent via first-class mail or other reliable means\n• Allows 30 days (or 60 days for defendant abroad) from date sent for defendant to return waiver Benefits of waiver • Defendant's time to file answer extended to 60 days (or 90 days if abroad) from date sent Consequences of • Defendant pays expenses of future service of process failing to waive • Defendant pays reasonable expenses of any motion required to collect service expenses (eg, attorney's fees) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, by delivering a copy of the summons and complaint. Since this can be expensive and burdensome, defendants are encouraged to waive service of process upon the plaintiff's written request. That request must:\n• be sent to the defendant via first-class mail or other reliable means\n• name the court where the complaint was filed and the date the request was sent and\n• contain one copy of the complaint, two copies of the waiver form, and a prepaid means for returning the signed waiver form (eg, a self-addressed and stamped envelope). If the defendant waives service of process, the defendant receives extra time to respond to the complaint—60 days from the date the request was sent instead of 21 days from the date of service (the carrot). But if the defendant (1) fails to waive service and (2) lacks good cause for doing so, the defendant must pay the expenses of future service and of any motion required to collect those expenses (the stick).\n\nHere, the businesswoman's waiver request only contained one copy of the waiver form and did not include a prepaid means to return the signed form. Therefore, the website owner is not liable for the process-server expenses (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A plaintiff must give a defendant reasonable time to return the waiver—ie, at least 30 days when the defendant is in the U.S. (as seen here) or at least 60 days if the defendant is abroad. Educational objective: A plaintiff must send a written waiver-of-service request to the defendant via first-class mail or other reliable means. The waiver must state the name of the court and the date the request was sent. It also must contain a copy of the complaint, two copies of the waiver form, and a prepaid means to return the signed form. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(d) (waiving service of process).","rule":"A plaintiff must send a written waiver-of-service request to the defendant via first-class mail or other reliable means. The waiver must state the name of the court and the date the request was sent. It also must contain a copy of the complaint, two copies of the waiver form, and a prepaid means to return the signed form.","irac":{"issue":"A businesswoman sued a website owner in federal court for violating federal copyright law. The businesswoman sent a copy of the complaint and a form requesting waiver of service of process to the website owner's residence via first-class mail. The request named the court where the complaint was filed and the date when the request was sent. The businesswoman did not receive anything back from the website owner, so 31 days after mailing the request, she had a process server hand-deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the website owner. Is the website owner liable for the expenses associated with the process server?","rule":"A plaintiff must send a written waiver-of-service request to the defendant via first-class mail or other reliable means. The waiver must state the name of the court and the date the request was sent. It also must contain a copy of the complaint, two copies of the waiver form, and a prepaid means to return the signed form.","analysis":"Waiving service of process\n\n(FRCP 4(d))\n\nRequest for waiver • In writing\n• Addressed to defendant (or authorized agent)\n• Names court where complaint filed\n• Provides copy of complaint, two waiver forms & prepaid means to return form\n• Informs defendant about consequences of waiving & not waiving\n• Lists date request is sent\n• Sent via first-class mail or other reliable means\n• Allows 30 days (or 60 days for defendant abroad) from date sent for defendant to return waiver Benefits of waiver • Defendant's time to file answer extended to 60 days (or 90 days if abroad) from date sent Consequences of • Defendant pays expenses of future service of process failing to waive • Defendant pays reasonable expenses of any motion required to collect service expenses (eg, attorney's fees) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA plaintiff must provide the defendant with adequate notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, by delivering a copy of the summons and complaint. Since this can be expensive and burdensome, defendants are encouraged to waive service of process upon the plaintiff's written request. That request must:\n• be sent to the defendant via first-class mail or other reliable means\n• name the court where the complaint was filed and the date the request was sent and\n• contain one copy of the complaint, two copies of the waiver form, and a prepaid means for returning the signed waiver form (eg, a self-addressed and stamped envelope). If the defendant waives service of process, the defendant receives extra time to respond to the complaint—60 days from the date the request was sent instead of 21 days from the date of service (the carrot). But if the defendant (1) fails to waive service and (2) lacks good cause for doing so, the defendant must pay the expenses of future service and of any motion required to collect those expenses (the stick).\n\nHere, the businesswoman's waiver request only contained one copy of the waiver form and did not include a prepaid means to return the signed form. Therefore, the website owner is not liable for the process-server expenses (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A plaintiff must give a defendant reasonable time to return the waiver—ie, at least 30 days when the defendant is in the U.S. (as seen here) or at least 60 days if the defendant is abroad. Educational objective: A plaintiff must send a written waiver-of-service request to the defendant via first-class mail or other reliable means. The waiver must state the name of the court and the date the request was sent. It also must contain a copy of the complaint, two copies of the waiver form, and a prepaid means to return the signed form. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(d) (waiving service of process).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(d) (waiving service of process).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[129],"sourceAnswerPages":[130,131],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女商人在联邦法院起诉一名网站所有者违反联邦版权法。她通过普通一类邮件向所有者住所寄送一份起诉状和一份请求放弃送达的表格。请求中列明起诉法院及寄出日期。网站所有者没有回寄任何材料，所以女商人在寄出请求 31 天后，安排送达人亲自向其交付传票和起诉状。网站所有者是否应承担送达人费用？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为女商人未在放弃请求中附上全部必要材料。","B":"不应，因为女商人在雇用送达人前没有等待 60 天。","C":"应当，因为法院必须向未放弃送达的被告征收送达费用。","D":"应当，因为被告有义务避免不必要的送达费用。"},"explanationZh":"放弃送达（FRCP 4(d)）：\n\n放弃请求必须：\n• 采用书面形式；\n• 致被告或获授权代理人；\n• 列明提起诉讼的法院；\n• 附起诉状一份、放弃表格两份及预付回寄方式；\n• 告知放弃及不放弃的后果；\n• 列明寄出日期；\n• 通过一类邮件或其他可靠方式寄送；\n• 给予境内被告 30 天、境外被告 60 天回寄。\n\n放弃的利益：被告答辩期限延长至寄出日起 60 天；境外被告延长至 90 天。\n\n无正当理由不放弃的后果：被告承担之后正式送达费用，以及为追偿该费用所需动议的合理费用，例如律师费。\n\n诉讼文书送达可能昂贵繁琐，因此鼓励被告在原告书面请求后放弃送达。请求应以一类邮件或其他可靠方式寄送，列明法院和寄出日期，并附一份起诉状、两份放弃表格和预付的签署表格回寄方式，例如写好地址并贴邮票的信封。\n\n若被告放弃，答辩期限从通常的送达后 21 天延长为请求寄出后 60 天，这是鼓励；若被告无正当理由不放弃，则须承担之后送达及追偿费用，这是约束。\n\n女商人的请求仅含一份放弃表格，也未附预付回寄方式，所以网站所有者不承担送达人费用（选项 C、D）。\n\n（选项 B）原告应给予被告合理回寄期限：美国境内至少 30 天，本题即如此；境外至少 60 天。\n\n学习目标：书面放弃请求须通过一类邮件或其他可靠方式寄送，列明法院及寄出日期，并附起诉状一份、放弃表格两份及预付回寄方式。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(d)（放弃送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-044","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":44,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A technology company incorporated and headquartered in State A hired an engineer domiciled in State B. The employment contract between the company and the engineer stated that all work product produced by the engineer was owned by the company. While employed by the company, the engineer developed a software program that was unrelated to the company's business. When the engineer left the company, she attempted to sell her program for $50,000. The technology company sued the engineer in a federal court in State A to enjoin the sale and for a declaratory judgment that the company owned the rights to the software program. The company claimed that it could incorporate the program into its business and increase profits by $100,000. The engineer defended on the ground that the contract does not apply to work that is unrelated to the company's business. The case proceeded to trial. After the close of evidence, the engineer moved to dismiss the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the engineer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the engineer waived this defense by failing to assert it before the close of evidence.","B":"No, because the parties are diverse and the value of the requested relief exceeds $75,000.","C":"Yes, because the company failed to demand damages in addition to the requested relief.","D":"Yes, because the cost to the engineer to comply with the requested relief is only $50,000."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is established when a federal court has federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction over a particular case. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy is generally determined by the monetary damages requested in the plaintiff's complaint. However, it can also be determined when a plaintiff requests equitable relief—eg, declaratory judgment, injunction (both requested here). Since equitable relief has no explicit monetary value, its implicit monetary value is calculated by the larger of the following:\n• the cost to the defendant if forced to comply with the requested equitable relief (eg, lost sales) or\n• the worth to the plaintiff if the requested equitable relief is granted (eg, expected profits). Here, the parties are diverse because the company is incorporated and headquartered in State A and the engineer is domiciled in State B. The cost to the engineer if forced to comply with the requested equitable relief is only the $50,000 she would lose if her sale is barred. However, the requested equitable relief is worth $100,000 in expected profits to the company, so the amount-in-controversy requirement is satisfied (Choice D). Therefore, diversity jurisdiction exists, and the court should deny the engineer's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) The right to challenge subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived. Instead, this issue can be raised at any time by the parties or on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte). (Choice C) A plaintiff need not demand damages to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement. The reason is that a request for equitable relief can satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement on its own if the value of the relief—based on its cost to the defendant or worth to the plaintiff—exceeds $75,000. Educational objective: In a diversity action, a request for equitable relief satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement if the value of the relief exceeds $75,000 based on its (1) cost to the defendant or (2) worth to the plaintiff—whichever is larger. References\n• Hunt v. Wash. State Apple Advert. Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 347–48 (1977) (holding that the amount in controversy in an action requesting equitable relief is measured by the value of the object of the litigation).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 962 (2020) (explaining how the amount in controversy is determined in actions that request equitable relief).","rule":"In a diversity action, a request for equitable relief satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement if the value of the relief exceeds $75,000 based on its (1) cost to the defendant or (2) worth to the plaintiff—whichever is larger.","irac":{"issue":"A technology company incorporated and headquartered in State A hired an engineer domiciled in State B. The employment contract between the company and the engineer stated that all work product produced by the engineer was owned by the company. While employed by the company, the engineer developed a software program that was unrelated to the company's business. When the engineer left the company, she attempted to sell her program for $50,000. The technology company sued the engineer in a federal court in State A to enjoin the sale and for a declaratory judgment that the company owned the rights to the software program. The company claimed that it could incorporate the program into its business and increase profits by $100,000. The engineer defended on the ground that the contract does not apply to work that is unrelated to the company's business. The case proceeded to trial. After the close of evidence, the engineer moved to dismiss the case for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the engineer's motion?","rule":"In a diversity action, a request for equitable relief satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement if the value of the relief exceeds $75,000 based on its (1) cost to the defendant or (2) worth to the plaintiff—whichever is larger.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is established when a federal court has federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction over a particular case. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy is generally determined by the monetary damages requested in the plaintiff's complaint. However, it can also be determined when a plaintiff requests equitable relief—eg, declaratory judgment, injunction (both requested here). Since equitable relief has no explicit monetary value, its implicit monetary value is calculated by the larger of the following:\n• the cost to the defendant if forced to comply with the requested equitable relief (eg, lost sales) or\n• the worth to the plaintiff if the requested equitable relief is granted (eg, expected profits). Here, the parties are diverse because the company is incorporated and headquartered in State A and the engineer is domiciled in State B. The cost to the engineer if forced to comply with the requested equitable relief is only the $50,000 she would lose if her sale is barred. However, the requested equitable relief is worth $100,000 in expected profits to the company, so the amount-in-controversy requirement is satisfied (Choice D). Therefore, diversity jurisdiction exists, and the court should deny the engineer's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) The right to challenge subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived. Instead, this issue can be raised at any time by the parties or on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte). (Choice C) A plaintiff need not demand damages to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement. The reason is that a request for equitable relief can satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement on its own if the value of the relief—based on its cost to the defendant or worth to the plaintiff—exceeds $75,000. Educational objective: In a diversity action, a request for equitable relief satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement if the value of the relief exceeds $75,000 based on its (1) cost to the defendant or (2) worth to the plaintiff—whichever is larger. References\n• Hunt v. Wash. State Apple Advert. Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 347–48 (1977) (holding that the amount in controversy in an action requesting equitable relief is measured by the value of the object of the litigation).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 962 (2020) (explaining how the amount in controversy is determined in actions that request equitable relief).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Hunt v. Wash. State Apple Advert. Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 347–48 (1977) (holding that the amount in controversy in an action requesting equitable relief is measured by the value of the object of the litigation).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 962 (2020) (explaining how the amount in controversy is determined in actions that request equitable relief).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[132],"sourceAnswerPages":[133,134],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且总部位于 A 州的科技公司雇用居住于 B 州的一名工程师。劳动合同规定，工程师产生的全部工作成果归公司所有。任职期间，工程师开发了一款与公司业务无关的软件。离职时，她试图以 50,000 美元出售该软件。公司在 A 州联邦法院起诉，请求禁止出售，并宣告公司拥有软件权利。公司称可将软件纳入业务并增加 100,000 美元利润。工程师抗辩称合同不适用于与公司业务无关的工作。案件审理至举证结束后，工程师以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为工程师未在举证结束前提出，已放弃该抗辩。","B":"不应，因为双方州籍不同且所请求救济价值超过 75,000 美元。","C":"应当，因为公司除请求该救济外未另行请求损害赔偿。","D":"应当，因为工程师遵守所请求救济的成本仅为 50,000 美元。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院对案件享有联邦问题管辖权（本题不存在）或多元州籍管辖权时，事项管辖权成立。多元州籍管辖权要求对立双方州籍不同，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n争议金额通常依原告起诉状所请求的金钱赔偿确定，也可由原告请求的衡平救济确定，例如本题的宣告判决及禁令。衡平救济无明确金额，隐含价值取以下两者中较大者：\n• 被告被迫遵守救济的成本，例如损失的销售款；或\n• 原告获得救济的价值，例如预期利润。\n\n公司在 A 州注册并设总部，工程师居住于 B 州，双方州籍不同。工程师因禁售损失的成本仅为 50,000 美元，但救济对公司的价值为 100,000 美元预期利润，所以满足争议金额要求（选项 D）。存在多元州籍管辖权，法院应驳回工程师的动议。\n\n（选项 A）质疑事项管辖权的权利永不放弃，当事人或法院依职权均可随时提出。\n\n（选项 C）原告无需请求损害赔偿。衡平救济按被告成本或原告价值中较大者计算，超过 75,000 美元即可单独满足争议金额。\n\n学习目标：多元州籍诉讼中，衡平救济按其对被告的成本或对原告的价值中较大者计算，超过 75,000 美元即满足争议金额。\n\n参考资料：\n• Hunt v. Wash. State Apple Advert. Comm'n, 432 U.S. 333, 347–48 (1977)（衡平救济的争议金额按诉讼标的价值衡量）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 962 (2020)（说明衡平救济案件的争议金额）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-022"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-045","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":45,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"Two ranchers, both citizens of State A, brought an action in a state court in State A against a developer, a citizen of State B. The ranchers alleged a state-law tort claim for water runoff damage to their properties caused by construction on the developer's neighboring property. The first rancher claimed $250,000 in damages and the second rancher claimed $50,000. In their complaint, the ranchers cited federal law regarding the calculation of damages due to water runoff. The developer timely removed the action to federal court. Is removal proper?","choices":{"A":"No, because the ranchers are not diverse from each other.","B":"No, because the second rancher's claim does not meet the amount-in-controversy requirement.","C":"Yes, because the complaint includes a federal question.","D":"Yes, because the ranchers are diverse from the developer and both ranchers' claims arise from the same facts."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A defendant can remove a case from state to federal court if the claims fall within the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states. Here, the ranchers' complaint cited federal law regarding the calculation of damages. But since their claim is based on a state-law tort, there is no federal-question jurisdiction (Choice C). Instead, diversity jurisdiction exists over the first rancher's claim because it seeks $250,000 and the ranchers (State A citizens) are diverse from the developer (State B citizen). However, there is no diversity jurisdiction over the second rancher's claim since it seeks only $50,000. When a claim fails to satisfy original jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction generally exists over that claim if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original jurisdiction. But when the original claim is based on diversity jurisdiction (as seen here), supplemental jurisdiction is barred if the supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship between the opposing parties or\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention. Here, the ranchers' claims arise from the same construction on the developer's property. Although the ranchers are not diverse from each other, this does not contaminate diversity between the ranchers and their opponent—the developer (Choice A). Additionally, the second rancher's $50,000 claim is asserted against the original defendant and the second rancher permissibly joined the suit. Therefore, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over this claim and removal is proper (Choice B). Educational objective: A case can be removed to federal court if a claim falls within that court's original jurisdiction. A claim that does not satisfy original jurisdiction generally can also be removed through supplemental jurisdiction if that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original jurisdiction. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1331–32 (federal-question & diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Services, 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005) (explaining when the exercise of supplemental jurisdiction is barred).","rule":"A case can be removed to federal court if a claim falls within that court's original jurisdiction. A claim that does not satisfy original jurisdiction generally can also be removed through supplemental jurisdiction if that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"Two ranchers, both citizens of State A, brought an action in a state court in State A against a developer, a citizen of State B. The ranchers alleged a state-law tort claim for water runoff damage to their properties caused by construction on the developer's neighboring property. The first rancher claimed $250,000 in damages and the second rancher claimed $50,000. In their complaint, the ranchers cited federal law regarding the calculation of damages due to water runoff. The developer timely removed the action to federal court. Is removal proper?","rule":"A case can be removed to federal court if a claim falls within that court's original jurisdiction. A claim that does not satisfy original jurisdiction generally can also be removed through supplemental jurisdiction if that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original jurisdiction.","analysis":"A defendant can remove a case from state to federal court if the claims fall within the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states. Here, the ranchers' complaint cited federal law regarding the calculation of damages. But since their claim is based on a state-law tort, there is no federal-question jurisdiction (Choice C). Instead, diversity jurisdiction exists over the first rancher's claim because it seeks $250,000 and the ranchers (State A citizens) are diverse from the developer (State B citizen). However, there is no diversity jurisdiction over the second rancher's claim since it seeks only $50,000. When a claim fails to satisfy original jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction generally exists over that claim if it shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original jurisdiction. But when the original claim is based on diversity jurisdiction (as seen here), supplemental jurisdiction is barred if the supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship between the opposing parties or\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention. Here, the ranchers' claims arise from the same construction on the developer's property. Although the ranchers are not diverse from each other, this does not contaminate diversity between the ranchers and their opponent—the developer (Choice A). Additionally, the second rancher's $50,000 claim is asserted against the original defendant and the second rancher permissibly joined the suit. Therefore, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over this claim and removal is proper (Choice B). Educational objective: A case can be removed to federal court if a claim falls within that court's original jurisdiction. A claim that does not satisfy original jurisdiction generally can also be removed through supplemental jurisdiction if that claim shares a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original jurisdiction. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1331–32 (federal-question & diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Services, 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005) (explaining when the exercise of supplemental jurisdiction is barred).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1331–32 (federal-question & diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Services, 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005) (explaining when the exercise of supplemental jurisdiction is barred).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[135],"sourceAnswerPages":[136,137],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"两名均为 A 州公民的牧场主，在 A 州州法院起诉 B 州的一名开发商。他们依州法主张，开发商在相邻土地施工造成地表水径流，损害其财产。第一名牧场主请求 250,000 美元，第二名请求 50,000 美元。起诉状引用联邦法律中关于径流损害金额计算的规定。开发商及时将案件移送至联邦法院。移送是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为两名牧场主彼此州籍相同。","B":"不适当，因为第二名牧场主的请求不满足争议金额要求。","C":"适当，因为起诉状包含联邦问题。","D":"适当，因为牧场主与开发商州籍不同，且两名牧场主的请求源于相同事实。"},"explanationZh":"请求属于联邦法院事项管辖权时，被告可以将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。原始管辖权可基于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州的公民。\n\n牧场主虽在起诉状中引用关于损害计算的联邦法律，但其请求本身是州法侵权请求，不存在联邦问题管辖权（选项 C）。\n\n第一名牧场主请求 250,000 美元，且 A 州牧场主与 B 州开发商州籍不同，法院对其请求享有多元州籍管辖权；第二名牧场主仅请求 50,000 美元，其请求不单独满足多元州籍管辖权。\n\n不满足原始管辖权的请求若与原始管辖权范围内请求共享同一核心争议事实，通常存在补充管辖权。但原始请求基于多元州籍时，若补充请求会破坏对立双方州籍多元性，或金额不超过 75,000 美元且由原告针对通过合并、介入或第三方诉讼加入的当事人提出，或由通过强制合并或介入加入的原告提出，则不得行使。\n\n两名牧场主的请求均源于开发商同一施工。二人彼此州籍相同并不破坏他们与对立方开发商之间的州籍多元性（选项 A）。第二名牧场主的 50,000 美元请求针对原始被告，且其通过任意合并适当加入诉讼。因此，法院对该请求享有补充管辖权，移送适当（选项 B）。\n\n学习目标：一项请求属于联邦法院原始管辖权时，案件可以移送；不满足原始管辖权的另一请求若共享同一核心争议事实，通常也可通过补充管辖权一并移送。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441（移送要求）。\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1331–1332（联邦问题及多元州籍管辖权）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367（补充管辖权）。\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Services, 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005)（补充管辖权的限制）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-023"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-046","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":46,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A bicyclist who was a citizen of a foreign country and a permanent resident of State A was riding on a road in State B when she was hit by a car driven by a man domiciled in State A. The bicyclist sued the man for negligence in a federal court in State B to recover $80,000 in damages. The man moved to dismiss the bicyclist's complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the federal court likely rule on the man's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the bicyclist and the man are citizens of different countries and the amount in controversy is $80,000.","B":"Deny the motion, because the collision occurred in State B.","C":"Grant the motion, because a foreign citizen cannot sue a U.S. citizen in federal court.","D":"Grant the motion, because the parties are domiciled in the same state."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction can be based on federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction is established when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and there is diverse citizenship between the opposing parties. Diverse citizenship exists in a suit between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states. It also exists when the suit is between:\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in different states, in which foreign citizens are additional parties or\n• U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states.* Here, the bicyclist (permanent resident domiciled in State A) sued the man (U.S. citizen domiciled in State A) in federal court for $80,000. Since the bicyclist and the man are domiciled in the same state, there is no diversity of citizenship. As a result, the court lacks diversity (and subject-matter) jurisdiction and will likely grant the man's motion to dismiss (Choice A). *Diverse citizenship also exists in the rare instance when a foreign government sues a U.S. citizen. (Choice B) The location of the collision is irrelevant in analyzing subject-matter jurisdiction. Instead, this fact is relevant in determining whether other federal court requirements—ie, personal jurisdiction and venue—are satisfied. (Choice C) A foreign citizen can sue a U.S. citizen in federal court if subject-matter jurisdiction is established through diversity or federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here). Educational objective: Diverse citizenship exists when a suit is between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states, in which foreign citizens are additional parties, or (3) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 616 (2020) (explaining when diverse citizenship exists between U.S. citizens and foreign citizens).","rule":"Diverse citizenship exists when a suit is between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states, in which foreign citizens are additional parties, or (3) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states.","irac":{"issue":"A bicyclist who was a citizen of a foreign country and a permanent resident of State A was riding on a road in State B when she was hit by a car driven by a man domiciled in State A. The bicyclist sued the man for negligence in a federal court in State B to recover $80,000 in damages. The man moved to dismiss the bicyclist's complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How will the federal court likely rule on the man's motion?","rule":"Diverse citizenship exists when a suit is between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states, in which foreign citizens are additional parties, or (3) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction can be based on federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction is established when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and there is diverse citizenship between the opposing parties. Diverse citizenship exists in a suit between U.S. citizens domiciled in different states. It also exists when the suit is between:\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens\n• U.S. citizens domiciled in different states, in which foreign citizens are additional parties or\n• U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states.* Here, the bicyclist (permanent resident domiciled in State A) sued the man (U.S. citizen domiciled in State A) in federal court for $80,000. Since the bicyclist and the man are domiciled in the same state, there is no diversity of citizenship. As a result, the court lacks diversity (and subject-matter) jurisdiction and will likely grant the man's motion to dismiss (Choice A). *Diverse citizenship also exists in the rare instance when a foreign government sues a U.S. citizen. (Choice B) The location of the collision is irrelevant in analyzing subject-matter jurisdiction. Instead, this fact is relevant in determining whether other federal court requirements—ie, personal jurisdiction and venue—are satisfied. (Choice C) A foreign citizen can sue a U.S. citizen in federal court if subject-matter jurisdiction is established through diversity or federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here). Educational objective: Diverse citizenship exists when a suit is between (1) U.S. citizens domiciled in the U.S. and foreign citizens, (2) U.S. citizens domiciled in different states, in which foreign citizens are additional parties, or (3) U.S. citizens and permanent residents domiciled in different states. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 616 (2020) (explaining when diverse citizenship exists between U.S. citizens and foreign citizens).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 616 (2020) (explaining when diverse citizenship exists between U.S. citizens and foreign citizens).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[138],"sourceAnswerPages":[139],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名外国公民、同时是 A 州永久居民的骑自行车者，在 B 州道路上被一名居住于 A 州的男子驾车撞伤。骑车者在 B 州联邦法院以过失为由起诉男子，索赔 80,000 美元。男子以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。联邦法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为骑车者与男子属于不同国家公民，且争议金额为 80,000 美元。","B":"驳回动议，因为碰撞发生在 B 州。","C":"批准动议，因为外国公民不能在联邦法院起诉美国公民。","D":"批准动议，因为双方均居住于同一州。"},"explanationZh":"事项管辖权可以基于联邦问题管辖权（本题不存在）或多元州籍管辖权。后者要求争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方具有不同州籍。美国公民居住于不同州时存在州籍多元性；以下诉讼也存在：\n• 居住于美国的美国公民与外国公民之间；\n• 居住于不同州的美国公民之间，外国公民作为附加当事人；或\n• 美国公民与居住于不同州的永久居民之间。*\n\n骑车者是居住于 A 州的永久居民，男子是居住于 A 州的美国公民。二人均居住于同一州，不存在州籍多元性。因此，法院缺乏多元州籍及事项管辖权，很可能批准男子动议（选项 A）。\n\n*外国政府起诉美国公民的少数情形也存在州籍多元性。\n\n（选项 B）碰撞地点与事项管辖权分析无关，但可能影响对人管辖权和审判地。\n\n（选项 C）若通过多元州籍或联邦问题建立事项管辖权，外国公民可以在联邦法院起诉美国公民；本题未满足。\n\n学习目标：以下情况下存在州籍多元性：(1) 居住于美国的美国公民与外国公民之间；(2) 居住于不同州的美国公民之间，外国公民作为附加当事人；或 (3) 美国公民与居住于不同州的永久居民之间。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 616 (2020)（美国公民与外国公民之间的州籍多元性）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-011"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-047","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":47,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A popular food blogger from State A got into a heated online argument with the owner of a successful vegan restaurant located in State B about the merits of veganism. To get back at the owner, the blogger wrote a review of the owner's vegan restaurant in which she falsely claimed that the restaurant was infested with vermin and used animal products in its cuisine. The blogger had never eaten at the owner's restaurant or traveled to State B. The blog post had over 500,000 views from people around the United States. Within one month of the post, the restaurant shut down. The owner sued the blogger in a federal court in State B for defamation. Based on the allegations in the owner's complaint, the blogger filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. State B has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction over out-of-state defendants that intentionally harm State B residents or businesses. How will the federal court likely rule on the blogger's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the blogger knew that her review would negatively impact the restaurant in State B.","B":"Deny the motion, unless the blogger failed to attach an affidavit to the motion demonstrating why personal jurisdiction does not exist.","C":"Grant the motion, because the blogger has never traveled to State B.","D":"Grant the motion, because the blogger wrote the review in State A."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Requirements for specific jurisdiction\n\nLong-arm Forum state statute must authorize jurisdiction under specific circumstances statute\n\nDue Minimum Purposeful availment of forum state's laws so suit is process contacts foreseeable:\n\n• Stream of commerce test – defendant places goods in stream of commerce & takes action that intentionally targets forum state\n• Effects test – defendant commits intentional tort in one state with intent that effects will be felt in forum state Fair play & Reasonableness factors: substantial justice • Burden on defendant to appear & defend in forum state\n• Plaintiff's interest in obtaining relief in forum state\n• Forum state's interest\n• Interstate judicial system's interest in obtaining most efficient resolution of disputes\n• Interest of several states in furthering fundamental substantive social policies A federal court* has specific personal jurisdiction over a nonresident defendant if (1) the forum state has a long-arm statute that authorizes jurisdiction under the specific circumstances and (2) applying that statute complies with due process. This due process requirement is met when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice.\n\nMinimum contacts exist when the defendant purposefully avails him/herself of the state's protections and benefits so that the defendant should reasonably foresee being sued there. Purposeful availment can occur even if the defendant never physically enters the forum state—eg, committing an intentional tort in one state with the intent that the effects will be felt in the forum state (Choices C & D).\n\nHere, State B's long-arm statute authorizes jurisdiction over out-of-state defendants (the blogger) who intentionally harm a State B business or resident (the owner). Applying the statute complies with due process because the blogger purposefully availed herself of State B's benefits when she wrote a review to harm the owner in State B—making this defamation suit foreseeable there. And since there is no indication that exercising jurisdiction would offend notions of fair play and substantial justice, the State B federal court has specific jurisdiction over the blogger. Therefore, her motion to dismiss will likely be denied. *Federal courts have the same jurisdictional reach as courts of the state in which they sit. (Choice B) When a defendant asserts lack of personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion to dismiss (as seen here), the defendant can use the allegations in the pleadings or an attached affidavit to show why personal jurisdiction does not exist. Educational objective: A defendant has minimum contacts with a forum state when he/she purposefully avails him/herself of the state's protections and benefits, so that a lawsuit is reasonably foreseeable there. This can occur when the defendant commits an intentional tort in one state with the purpose or knowledge that the effects will be felt in the forum state. References\n• Calder v. Jones, 465 U.S. 783, 789–90 (1984) (holding that out-of-state defendants were subject to specific jurisdiction because their libelous article intentionally targeted the forum state's resident).\n\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).","rule":"A defendant has minimum contacts with a forum state when he/she purposefully avails him/herself of the state's protections and benefits, so that a lawsuit is reasonably foreseeable there. This can occur when the defendant commits an intentional tort in one state with the purpose or knowledge that the effects will be felt in the forum state.","irac":{"issue":"A popular food blogger from State A got into a heated online argument with the owner of a successful vegan restaurant located in State B about the merits of veganism. To get back at the owner, the blogger wrote a review of the owner's vegan restaurant in which she falsely claimed that the restaurant was infested with vermin and used animal products in its cuisine. The blogger had never eaten at the owner's restaurant or traveled to State B. The blog post had over 500,000 views from people around the United States. Within one month of the post, the restaurant shut down. The owner sued the blogger in a federal court in State B for defamation. Based on the allegations in the owner's complaint, the blogger filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. State B has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction over out-of-state defendants that intentionally harm State B residents or businesses. How will the federal court likely rule on the blogger's motion to dismiss?","rule":"A defendant has minimum contacts with a forum state when he/she purposefully avails him/herself of the state's protections and benefits, so that a lawsuit is reasonably foreseeable there. This can occur when the defendant commits an intentional tort in one state with the purpose or knowledge that the effects will be felt in the forum state.","analysis":"Requirements for specific jurisdiction\n\nLong-arm Forum state statute must authorize jurisdiction under specific circumstances statute\n\nDue Minimum Purposeful availment of forum state's laws so suit is process contacts foreseeable:\n\n• Stream of commerce test – defendant places goods in stream of commerce & takes action that intentionally targets forum state\n• Effects test – defendant commits intentional tort in one state with intent that effects will be felt in forum state Fair play & Reasonableness factors: substantial justice • Burden on defendant to appear & defend in forum state\n• Plaintiff's interest in obtaining relief in forum state\n• Forum state's interest\n• Interstate judicial system's interest in obtaining most efficient resolution of disputes\n• Interest of several states in furthering fundamental substantive social policies A federal court* has specific personal jurisdiction over a nonresident defendant if (1) the forum state has a long-arm statute that authorizes jurisdiction under the specific circumstances and (2) applying that statute complies with due process. This due process requirement is met when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice.\n\nMinimum contacts exist when the defendant purposefully avails him/herself of the state's protections and benefits so that the defendant should reasonably foresee being sued there. Purposeful availment can occur even if the defendant never physically enters the forum state—eg, committing an intentional tort in one state with the intent that the effects will be felt in the forum state (Choices C & D).\n\nHere, State B's long-arm statute authorizes jurisdiction over out-of-state defendants (the blogger) who intentionally harm a State B business or resident (the owner). Applying the statute complies with due process because the blogger purposefully availed herself of State B's benefits when she wrote a review to harm the owner in State B—making this defamation suit foreseeable there. And since there is no indication that exercising jurisdiction would offend notions of fair play and substantial justice, the State B federal court has specific jurisdiction over the blogger. Therefore, her motion to dismiss will likely be denied. *Federal courts have the same jurisdictional reach as courts of the state in which they sit. (Choice B) When a defendant asserts lack of personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion to dismiss (as seen here), the defendant can use the allegations in the pleadings or an attached affidavit to show why personal jurisdiction does not exist. Educational objective: A defendant has minimum contacts with a forum state when he/she purposefully avails him/herself of the state's protections and benefits, so that a lawsuit is reasonably foreseeable there. This can occur when the defendant commits an intentional tort in one state with the purpose or knowledge that the effects will be felt in the forum state. References\n• Calder v. Jones, 465 U.S. 783, 789–90 (1984) (holding that out-of-state defendants were subject to specific jurisdiction because their libelous article intentionally targeted the forum state's resident).\n\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Calder v. Jones, 465 U.S. 783, 789–90 (1984) (holding that out-of-state defendants were subject to specific jurisdiction because their libelous article intentionally targeted the forum state's resident).\n\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[141],"sourceAnswerPages":[142,143],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名知名美食博主，就纯素主义的优点与 B 州一家成功纯素餐厅的所有者发生激烈网络争论。为报复所有者，博主发表餐厅评论，虚假声称餐厅有害虫泛滥，并在菜品中使用动物制品。博主从未在餐厅用餐，也从未前往 B 州。帖子在全美获得超过 500,000 次浏览，餐厅在一个月内停业。所有者在 B 州联邦法院以诽谤为由起诉博主。博主依据起诉状陈述，以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。B 州长臂法授权其法院对故意损害 B 州居民或企业的外州被告行使管辖权。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为博主知道其评论会对 B 州餐厅造成负面影响。","B":"驳回动议，除非博主未在动议中附宣誓书说明为何不存在对人管辖权。","C":"批准动议，因为博主从未前往 B 州。","D":"批准动议，因为博主在 A 州撰写评论。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院对非居民被告享有特定对人管辖权，需要：(1) 法院州长臂法授权具体情形下的管辖；且 (2) 适用该法符合正当程序。正当程序要求：\n• 原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系；并且\n• 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义。\n\n被告有目的地利用该州的保护和利益，使其应合理预见在该州被诉时，存在最低限度联系。即使被告从未亲自进入法院州，也可能构成有目的利用，例如在一州实施故意侵权，并意图使效果在法院州发生（选项 C、D）。\n\nB 州长臂法授权对故意损害 B 州企业或居民的外州被告行使管辖。博主为损害 B 州所有者而发表评论，有目的地利用 B 州利益，使在那里发生诽谤诉讼可合理预见；适用该法符合正当程序。无事实表明行使管辖权违反公平处理和实质正义，因此 B 州联邦法院对博主享有特定管辖权，应驳回动议。\n\n*联邦法院的管辖范围与其所在州法院相同。\n\n（选项 B）被告在答辩前动议中提出缺乏对人管辖权时，可以依诉状陈述或附具宣誓书说明不存在管辖权；宣誓书并非必需。\n\n学习目标：被告有目的地利用法院州的保护和利益，使在该州被诉可以合理预见时，即存在最低限度联系。在一州实施故意侵权并意图或明知效果会在法院州发生，也可构成这种联系。\n\n参考资料：\n• Calder v. Jones, 465 U.S. 783, 789–90 (1984)（诽谤文章有意针对法院州居民，外州被告受特定管辖）。\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017)（说明特定管辖所需最低限度联系）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-048","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":48,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State A sued a partnership located in State B in a State A federal court to recover $30,000 for a breach of contract claim and $60,000 for a misrepresentation claim. After the partnership filed an answer denying liability, the partnership's attorney learned that one of the partners had permanently moved to State A before the company filed its complaint. The attorney moved to dismiss the suit for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How should the court rule on the motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the company and the partnership are diverse and the amount in controversy is satisfied.","B":"Deny the motion, because the partnership waived its subject-matter jurisdiction defense by failing to include it in its answer.","C":"Grant the motion, because neither of the company's claims independently exceeds $75,000.","D":"Grant the motion, because the company and the partnership are not diverse."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Citizenship for diversity jurisdiction\n\nPerson Citizen of state where domiciled—ie, physically present with intent to remain indefinitely\n\nCorporation Citizen of every state where it (1) has been incorporated and/or (2) has its principal place of business\n\nUnincorporated Citizen of every state where its members are domiciled association\n\nA federal court can only hear a suit over which it has subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, authority to hear a particular type of case or controversy. The burden is on the party seeking to invoke the court's subject-matter jurisdiction (ie, the claimant) to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the court has either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse) at the time the complaint is filed.\n\nA corporation (eg, the company) is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business (here, State A). In contrast, an unincorporated association (eg, the partnership) is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled—ie, where they reside and intend to remain indefinitely. Since a partner moved to State A before the complaint was filed, the partnership is a State A citizen*—just like the corporation. Therefore, the parties are not diverse and the partnership's motion to dismiss should be granted (Choice A). *The partnership is also a citizen of any other state where other partners are domiciled. (Choice B) Most defenses are waived if they are not asserted in a defendant's answer. But a defense based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and can be asserted by a party or raised by the court at any time. (Choice C) Although neither of the company's claims independently exceeds $75,000, the company can aggregate (ie, combine) them to $90,000 to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement for diversity jurisdiction. Educational objective:\n\nFor purposes of diversity jurisdiction, a corporation is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business. An unincorporated association is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled—ie, where the members reside and intend to remain indefinitely. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Carden v. Arkoma Assocs., 494 U.S. 185, 195–96 (1990) (holding that an unincorporated association, such as a partnership, is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled).","rule":"For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, a corporation is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business. An unincorporated association is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled—ie, where the members reside and intend to remain indefinitely.","irac":{"issue":"A company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State A sued a partnership located in State B in a State A federal court to recover $30,000 for a breach of contract claim and $60,000 for a misrepresentation claim. After the partnership filed an answer denying liability, the partnership's attorney learned that one of the partners had permanently moved to State A before the company filed its complaint. The attorney moved to dismiss the suit for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. How should the court rule on the motion?","rule":"For purposes of diversity jurisdiction, a corporation is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business. An unincorporated association is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled—ie, where the members reside and intend to remain indefinitely.","analysis":"Citizenship for diversity jurisdiction\n\nPerson Citizen of state where domiciled—ie, physically present with intent to remain indefinitely\n\nCorporation Citizen of every state where it (1) has been incorporated and/or (2) has its principal place of business\n\nUnincorporated Citizen of every state where its members are domiciled association\n\nA federal court can only hear a suit over which it has subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, authority to hear a particular type of case or controversy. The burden is on the party seeking to invoke the court's subject-matter jurisdiction (ie, the claimant) to prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the court has either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse) at the time the complaint is filed.\n\nA corporation (eg, the company) is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business (here, State A). In contrast, an unincorporated association (eg, the partnership) is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled—ie, where they reside and intend to remain indefinitely. Since a partner moved to State A before the complaint was filed, the partnership is a State A citizen*—just like the corporation. Therefore, the parties are not diverse and the partnership's motion to dismiss should be granted (Choice A). *The partnership is also a citizen of any other state where other partners are domiciled. (Choice B) Most defenses are waived if they are not asserted in a defendant's answer. But a defense based on lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and can be asserted by a party or raised by the court at any time. (Choice C) Although neither of the company's claims independently exceeds $75,000, the company can aggregate (ie, combine) them to $90,000 to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement for diversity jurisdiction. Educational objective:\n\nFor purposes of diversity jurisdiction, a corporation is a citizen of every state where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business. An unincorporated association is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled—ie, where the members reside and intend to remain indefinitely. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Carden v. Arkoma Assocs., 494 U.S. 185, 195–96 (1990) (holding that an unincorporated association, such as a partnership, is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• Carden v. Arkoma Assocs., 494 U.S. 185, 195–96 (1990) (holding that an unincorporated association, such as a partnership, is a citizen of every state where its members are domiciled).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[144],"sourceAnswerPages":[145,146],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 A 州的公司，在 A 州联邦法院起诉位于 B 州的一家合伙企业，就违约请求 30,000 美元，就虚假陈述请求 60,000 美元。合伙企业答辩否认责任后，其律师得知，一名合伙人在公司提交起诉状前已永久迁往 A 州。律师以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院应如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为公司与合伙企业州籍不同，且满足争议金额。","B":"驳回动议，因为合伙企业未在答辩状中提出，已放弃事项管辖权抗辩。","C":"批准动议，因为公司的两项请求均未单独超过 75,000 美元。","D":"批准动议，因为公司与合伙企业不具有不同州籍。"},"explanationZh":"多元州籍管辖权中的州籍：\n\n• 个人：实际身处某州并有无限期居留意图，即住所所在州的公民。\n• 公司：其注册成立州和／或主要营业地所在州的公民。\n• 非公司组织：每名成员住所所在州的公民。\n\n联邦法院只能审理其享有事项管辖权的诉讼。援引管辖权的一方负有优势证据证明责任，证明存在：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据美国宪法、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——提交起诉状时争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州公民。\n\n公司是其注册成立州及主要营业地所在州的公民，本题为 A 州。非公司组织（如合伙企业）则是每名成员住所所在州的公民。一名合伙人在提交起诉状前迁往 A 州并有永久居留意图，故合伙企业也是 A 州公民*，与公司相同。双方不具有不同州籍，应批准合伙企业动议（选项 A）。\n\n*合伙企业同时也是其他合伙人住所所在的任何州的公民。\n\n（选项 B）多数抗辩未在答辩中提出会被放弃；缺乏事项管辖权永不放弃，当事人或法院可随时提出。\n\n（选项 C）两项请求虽均未单独超过 75,000 美元，公司可以合并计算为 90,000 美元以满足争议金额。\n\n学习目标：公司是其注册成立州和主要营业地所在州的公民；非公司组织是每名成员住所所在州的公民。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• Carden v. Arkoma Assocs., 494 U.S. 185, 195–96 (1990)（合伙企业等非公司组织是其每名成员住所所在州的公民）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-049","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":49,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"The legislature of an oil-rich state enacted a statute that created a complex regulatory scheme to manage drilling in the state's numerous oil fields. The statute created a commission that was authorized to approve oil-drilling permits after an extensive review process. The statute also established a system of judicial review of the commission's decisions by the state's courts. The commission approved an in-state firm's application for a permit to drill in the eastern part of the state but denied an out-of-state company's application for a permit to drill in the same area. The company then filed two suits against the commission. One suit was filed in a federal court; the other was filed in a state court. In both suits, the company sought to enjoin enforcement of the commission's decision on the ground that the commission violated the company's federal constitutional rights by misapplying the state's regulations. How is the federal court likely to proceed?","choices":{"A":"Abstain from hearing the case, because the federal court should defer to the state's regulatory scheme.","B":"Dismiss the case, because the federal court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction.","C":"Hear the case on the merits, because it is unlikely that the federal litigation will interfere with the state's regulatory scheme.","D":"Hear the case on the merits, because the state court lacks jurisdiction over the company's First Amendment claim."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A federal court must generally hear the merits of a case over which it has subject-matter jurisdiction. Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through diversity or federal-question jurisdiction. Federal-question jurisdiction exists when the plaintiff's claim arises under a federal law, a treaty, or the U.S. Constitution (as seen here). Therefore, the federal court has subject-matter jurisdiction over this case (Choice B). However, a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction may or must abstain from hearing a case in limited circumstances. One such circumstance arises under the Burford abstention doctrine, which applies when declaratory or injunctive relief is sought in federal court. In this situation, the federal court has the discretion to abstain when such relief would interfere with a complex state regulatory scheme that:\n• serves an important state policy (eg, regulation of natural resources) and\n• provides for timely and adequate judicial review by the state's courts. Here, the company asks the federal court to enjoin enforcement of the state commission's denial of the permit. The commission furthers the state's important interest in oil drilling by extensively reviewing permit applications, and its decisions are subject to judicial review by state courts. An injunction would likely interfere with this complex regulatory scheme since the federal court would have to determine whether the regulations were properly applied (Choice C). Therefore, the federal court should defer to this scheme and abstain from hearing the case. (Choice D) State courts are courts of \"general jurisdiction,\" so they have the power to hear any type of case that is not under the exclusive jurisdiction of federal courts (including the First Amendment claim here). Educational objective: The Burford abstention doctrine allows a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction to abstain when a declaratory judgment or an injunction by that court would interfere with a complex state regulatory scheme that (1) serves an important state policy and (2) provides timely and adequate judicial review by the state's courts. References\n• Burford v. Sun Oil Co., 319 U.S. 315, 317–18 (1943) (establishing the Burford abstention doctrine).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1024 (2020) (explaining Burford abstention).","rule":"The Burford abstention doctrine allows a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction to abstain when a declaratory judgment or an injunction by that court would interfere with a complex state regulatory scheme that (1) serves an important state policy and (2) provides timely and adequate judicial review by the state's courts.","irac":{"issue":"The legislature of an oil-rich state enacted a statute that created a complex regulatory scheme to manage drilling in the state's numerous oil fields. The statute created a commission that was authorized to approve oil-drilling permits after an extensive review process. The statute also established a system of judicial review of the commission's decisions by the state's courts. The commission approved an in-state firm's application for a permit to drill in the eastern part of the state but denied an out-of-state company's application for a permit to drill in the same area. The company then filed two suits against the commission. One suit was filed in a federal court; the other was filed in a state court. In both suits, the company sought to enjoin enforcement of the commission's decision on the ground that the commission violated the company's federal constitutional rights by misapplying the state's regulations. How is the federal court likely to proceed?","rule":"The Burford abstention doctrine allows a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction to abstain when a declaratory judgment or an injunction by that court would interfere with a complex state regulatory scheme that (1) serves an important state policy and (2) provides timely and adequate judicial review by the state's courts.","analysis":"A federal court must generally hear the merits of a case over which it has subject-matter jurisdiction. Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through diversity or federal-question jurisdiction. Federal-question jurisdiction exists when the plaintiff's claim arises under a federal law, a treaty, or the U.S. Constitution (as seen here). Therefore, the federal court has subject-matter jurisdiction over this case (Choice B). However, a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction may or must abstain from hearing a case in limited circumstances. One such circumstance arises under the Burford abstention doctrine, which applies when declaratory or injunctive relief is sought in federal court. In this situation, the federal court has the discretion to abstain when such relief would interfere with a complex state regulatory scheme that:\n• serves an important state policy (eg, regulation of natural resources) and\n• provides for timely and adequate judicial review by the state's courts. Here, the company asks the federal court to enjoin enforcement of the state commission's denial of the permit. The commission furthers the state's important interest in oil drilling by extensively reviewing permit applications, and its decisions are subject to judicial review by state courts. An injunction would likely interfere with this complex regulatory scheme since the federal court would have to determine whether the regulations were properly applied (Choice C). Therefore, the federal court should defer to this scheme and abstain from hearing the case. (Choice D) State courts are courts of \"general jurisdiction,\" so they have the power to hear any type of case that is not under the exclusive jurisdiction of federal courts (including the First Amendment claim here). Educational objective: The Burford abstention doctrine allows a federal court with subject-matter jurisdiction to abstain when a declaratory judgment or an injunction by that court would interfere with a complex state regulatory scheme that (1) serves an important state policy and (2) provides timely and adequate judicial review by the state's courts. References\n• Burford v. Sun Oil Co., 319 U.S. 315, 317–18 (1943) (establishing the Burford abstention doctrine).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1024 (2020) (explaining Burford abstention).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Burford v. Sun Oil Co., 319 U.S. 315, 317–18 (1943) (establishing the Burford abstention doctrine).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1024 (2020) (explaining Burford abstention).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[147],"sourceAnswerPages":[148],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某石油资源丰富州的立法机关制定一项法律，建立复杂监管体系，以管理州内众多油田的钻探。该法设立一委员会，授权其经过全面审查程序批准石油钻探许可证。该法还建立由本州法院对委员会决定进行司法审查的制度。委员会批准一家州内公司在该州东部钻探的许可申请，却拒绝一家州外公司在同一地区钻探的许可申请。该公司随后针对委员会提起两起诉讼：一起在联邦法院，另一起在州法院。两案中，公司均请求禁止执行委员会的决定，理由是委员会错误适用州法规，侵犯其联邦宪法权利。联邦法院可能如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"停止审理此案，因为联邦法院应尊重该州的监管体系。","B":"驳回此案，因为联邦法院缺乏事项管辖权。","C":"就案件实体进行审理，因为联邦诉讼不太可能干扰该州的监管体系。","D":"就案件实体进行审理，因为州法院对公司的第一修正案请求缺乏管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院通常必须就其享有事项管辖权的案件进行实体审理。事项管辖权可通过多元州籍管辖权或联邦问题管辖权建立。原告请求依据联邦法律、条约或《美国宪法》产生时，存在联邦问题管辖权（本题即如此）。因此，联邦法院对本案享有事项管辖权（选项 B）。\n\n但是，在有限情形下，享有事项管辖权的联邦法院可以或必须停止审理案件。其中一种情形源自 Burford 回避原则，适用于当事人在联邦法院请求宣告性救济或禁令救济的案件。在这种情况下，如果该项救济会干扰一套符合以下条件的复杂州监管体系，联邦法院可酌情停止审理：\n• 该体系服务于重要州政策（例如自然资源监管）；并且\n• 该州法院能够对其提供及时且充分的司法审查。\n\n本题中，公司请求联邦法院禁止执行州委员会拒发许可证的决定。委员会通过全面审查许可申请，促进该州对石油钻探的重要利益；其决定亦受州法院司法审查。禁令很可能干扰这一复杂监管体系，因为联邦法院必须判断相关法规是否得到正确适用（选项 C）。因此，联邦法院应尊重该体系，并停止审理本案。\n\n（选项 D）州法院属于“普通管辖权”法院，因此有权审理不属于联邦法院专属管辖的任何类型案件，包括本题中的第一修正案请求。\n\n学习目标：依 Burford 回避原则，享有事项管辖权的联邦法院可以停止审理案件，条件是该法院作出的宣告判决或禁令会干扰一套复杂州监管体系，而该体系：(1) 服务于重要州政策；且 (2) 由该州法院提供及时、充分的司法审查。\n\n参考资料：\n• Burford v. Sun Oil Co., 319 U.S. 315, 317–18 (1943)（确立 Burford 回避原则）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1024 (2020)（解释 Burford 回避）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-024"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-050","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":50,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A driver from State A was texting and driving when she hit a pedestrian from State B in a crosswalk in State B. The pedestrian suffered multiple injuries and sued the driver for gross negligence in a federal district court in State B. In his complaint, the pedestrian sought compensatory damages of $75,000 for lost wages and medical expenses, $10,000 for attorney's fees, and $100,000 for punitive damages. At the conclusion of trial, the jury awarded the pedestrian $35,000 for lost wages and medical expenses and $30,000 for punitive damages. The driver immediately moved to dismiss the case, arguing that the amount in controversy was insufficient to establish subject-matter jurisdiction. State B permits the recovery of punitive damages but not attorney's fees in a gross negligence action. Should the court grant the driver's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the total amount in controversy was $185,000.","B":"No, because the total amount in controversy was $175,000.","C":"Yes, because the total amount in controversy was $75,000.","D":"Yes, because the total amount in controversy was $65,000."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is established when a case satisfies either federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are completely diverse—eg, the plaintiff and defendant are citizens of different states—and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy is determined at the time the plaintiff files the complaint and includes all amounts asserted in good faith except interest, costs, and damages that are not legally recoverable. As a result, the following sums may be included in the amount in controversy:\n• Compensatory damages – money sought to compensate the plaintiff for his/her actual loss or harm (eg, pain and suffering, lost wages) that is always included in the amount in controversy\n\n• Punitive damages – money sought to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, and evil conduct that is included if permitted by state law\n\n• Attorney's fees – amount billed to a client for legal services provided that is included if permitted by state law or contract\n\nHere, the complaint requested $75,000 in compensatory damages, $100,000 in punitive damages, and $10,000 in attorney's fees. State B permits the recovery of punitive damages but not attorney's fees in this gross negligence action, so the amount in controversy was $175,000 ($75,000 + $100,000). Therefore, the amount-in-controversy requirement was met and the driver's motion should be denied (Choices A & C). (Choice D) The fact that the jury awarded the pedestrian only $65,000 is irrelevant in calculating the amount in controversy. The reason is that this amount is determined at the time the complaint is filed—not by later events. Educational objective: Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are completely diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy always includes compensatory damages and includes punitive damages (if permitted by state law) and attorney's fees (if permitted by state law or contract). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 952 (2020) (explaining that punitive damages are included in the amount in controversy if permitted by law).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 960 (2020) (describing that attorney's fees are included in the amount in controversy if permitted by law or contract).","rule":"Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are completely diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy always includes compensatory damages and includes punitive damages (if permitted by state law) and attorney's fees (if permitted by state law or contract).","irac":{"issue":"A driver from State A was texting and driving when she hit a pedestrian from State B in a crosswalk in State B. The pedestrian suffered multiple injuries and sued the driver for gross negligence in a federal district court in State B. In his complaint, the pedestrian sought compensatory damages of $75,000 for lost wages and medical expenses, $10,000 for attorney's fees, and $100,000 for punitive damages. At the conclusion of trial, the jury awarded the pedestrian $35,000 for lost wages and medical expenses and $30,000 for punitive damages. The driver immediately moved to dismiss the case, arguing that the amount in controversy was insufficient to establish subject-matter jurisdiction. State B permits the recovery of punitive damages but not attorney's fees in a gross negligence action. Should the court grant the driver's motion?","rule":"Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are completely diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy always includes compensatory damages and includes punitive damages (if permitted by state law) and attorney's fees (if permitted by state law or contract).","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction is established when a case satisfies either federal-question jurisdiction (not seen here) or diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are completely diverse—eg, the plaintiff and defendant are citizens of different states—and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy is determined at the time the plaintiff files the complaint and includes all amounts asserted in good faith except interest, costs, and damages that are not legally recoverable. As a result, the following sums may be included in the amount in controversy:\n• Compensatory damages – money sought to compensate the plaintiff for his/her actual loss or harm (eg, pain and suffering, lost wages) that is always included in the amount in controversy\n\n• Punitive damages – money sought to punish and deter outrageous, malicious, and evil conduct that is included if permitted by state law\n\n• Attorney's fees – amount billed to a client for legal services provided that is included if permitted by state law or contract\n\nHere, the complaint requested $75,000 in compensatory damages, $100,000 in punitive damages, and $10,000 in attorney's fees. State B permits the recovery of punitive damages but not attorney's fees in this gross negligence action, so the amount in controversy was $175,000 ($75,000 + $100,000). Therefore, the amount-in-controversy requirement was met and the driver's motion should be denied (Choices A & C). (Choice D) The fact that the jury awarded the pedestrian only $65,000 is irrelevant in calculating the amount in controversy. The reason is that this amount is determined at the time the complaint is filed—not by later events. Educational objective: Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are completely diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. The amount in controversy always includes compensatory damages and includes punitive damages (if permitted by state law) and attorney's fees (if permitted by state law or contract). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 952 (2020) (explaining that punitive damages are included in the amount in controversy if permitted by law).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 960 (2020) (describing that attorney's fees are included in the amount in controversy if permitted by law or contract).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 952 (2020) (explaining that punitive damages are included in the amount in controversy if permitted by law).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 960 (2020) (describing that attorney's fees are included in the amount in controversy if permitted by law or contract).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[150],"sourceAnswerPages":[151,152],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名驾驶员一边发短信一边开车，在 B 州人行横道撞到一名来自 B 州的行人。行人多处受伤，遂在 B 州联邦地区法院以重大过失为由起诉驾驶员。行人在起诉状中请求：误工损失及医疗费用的补偿性赔偿 75,000 美元、律师费 10,000 美元以及惩罚性赔偿 100,000 美元。审判结束时，陪审团判给行人误工损失及医疗费用 35,000 美元，并判给惩罚性赔偿 30,000 美元。驾驶员随即申请驳回，主张争议金额不足以建立事项管辖权。B 州允许在重大过失诉讼中获得惩罚性赔偿，但不允许获得律师费。法院是否应批准驾驶员的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为争议总金额为 185,000 美元。","B":"不应，因为争议总金额为 175,000 美元。","C":"应当，因为争议总金额为 75,000 美元。","D":"应当，因为争议总金额为 65,000 美元。"},"explanationZh":"案件满足联邦问题管辖权（本题不存在）或多元州籍管辖权时，即可建立事项管辖权。对立双方具有完全不同州籍——例如原告与被告是不同州的公民——且争议金额超过 75,000 美元时，存在多元州籍管辖权。\n\n争议金额在原告提交起诉状时确定，包括原告善意主张的全部金额，但利息、诉讼费用以及依法无法获得的损害赔偿除外。因此，下列款项可以计入争议金额：\n• 补偿性赔偿——为补偿原告实际损失或伤害（例如痛苦与折磨、误工损失）而请求的金钱；始终计入争议金额。\n• 惩罚性赔偿——为惩罚和遏制骇人、恶意及邪恶行为而请求的金钱；州法允许时计入。\n• 律师费——就所提供法律服务向客户收取的费用；州法或合同允许时计入。\n\n本题中，起诉状请求补偿性赔偿 75,000 美元、惩罚性赔偿 100,000 美元及律师费 10,000 美元。B 州允许在本重大过失诉讼中获得惩罚性赔偿，但不允许获得律师费，因此争议金额为 175,000 美元（75,000 美元 + 100,000 美元）。故争议金额要求得到满足，法院应驳回驾驶员的动议（选项 A、C）。\n\n（选项 D）陪审团最终仅判给行人 65,000 美元，与争议金额的计算无关，因为争议金额在提交起诉状时确定，而不是由之后发生的事件决定。\n\n学习目标：对立双方具有完全不同州籍且争议金额超过 75,000 美元时，存在多元州籍管辖权。争议金额始终包括补偿性赔偿，也包括州法允许的惩罚性赔偿以及州法或合同允许的律师费。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 952 (2020)（说明法律允许的惩罚性赔偿计入争议金额）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 960 (2020)（说明法律或合同允许的律师费计入争议金额）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-012"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-051","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":51,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"An artist from State A sold his paintings over the internet to an art collector from State B. The total purchase price amounted to $80,000. The collector sent $20,000 to the artist as a down payment but claims that she never received any of the paintings. As a result, the collector sued the artist in a state court in State B for breach of contract to recover her down payment. The artist filed an answer in which he alleged that all of his paintings were properly delivered to the collector and that the collector is attempting to defraud him. He also filed a counterclaim to recover $60,000 based on a federal statute that prohibits fraud in sales across state lines. If the collector seeks to remove the case to a federal court in State B, is she likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the collector is attempting to remove an action that has been filed in her home state.","B":"No, because the collector is not qualified to remove the case to federal court.","C":"Yes, because the artist's counterclaim is based on an alleged violation of a federal statute.","D":"Yes, because the total amount in controversy is $80,000 and the parties are citizens of different states."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Only a defendant has the power to remove a case from state to federal court when the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction, which must be established on the face of a plaintiff's complaint, can arise from either:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the plaintiff asserts a claim that arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law.\n\nHere, the collector (plaintiff) sued the artist (defendant) in state court. The artist then filed a counterclaim that alleged a violation of a federal statute prohibiting fraud in sales across state lines. But as the Supreme Court held in Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets, a defendant's counterclaim does not transform a plaintiff into a defendant for removal purposes.* Since the collector remains the plaintiff for removal purposes, she is not qualified to remove the case to federal court (Choice C). Even the artist, as the defendant, could not remove this case since the collector's complaint did not establish original jurisdiction. Her breach-of-contract claim arises under state (not federal) law, so the federal court lacks federal-question jurisdiction. And though the artist and collector are citizens of different states, the federal court lacks diversity jurisdiction since the collector demanded only $20,000 in damages. The $60,000 demanded in the artist's counterclaim is not counted in determining whether the amount in controversy is satisfied (Choice D). *Congress created a narrow exception to this rule when it enacted 28 U.S.C. § 1454, a special removal rule that permits removal by any party (including a plaintiff) when a federal patent or copyright claim is asserted. (Choice A) The home-court advantage (ie, forum-defendant) rule prohibits removal when the federal court's original jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction and a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed. But since the collector is the plaintiff, her residence in State B has no bearing on whether removal is proper. Educational objective: Only a defendant has the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A defendant's counterclaim does not transform a plaintiff into a defendant for removal purposes. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446 (removal procedure).\n\n• Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets, 313 U.S. 100, 107–08 (1941) (holding that a counterclaim does not change a plaintiff to a defendant for removal purposes).","rule":"Only a defendant has the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A defendant's counterclaim does not transform a plaintiff into a defendant for removal purposes.","irac":{"issue":"An artist from State A sold his paintings over the internet to an art collector from State B. The total purchase price amounted to $80,000. The collector sent $20,000 to the artist as a down payment but claims that she never received any of the paintings. As a result, the collector sued the artist in a state court in State B for breach of contract to recover her down payment. The artist filed an answer in which he alleged that all of his paintings were properly delivered to the collector and that the collector is attempting to defraud him. He also filed a counterclaim to recover $60,000 based on a federal statute that prohibits fraud in sales across state lines. If the collector seeks to remove the case to a federal court in State B, is she likely to succeed?","rule":"Only a defendant has the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A defendant's counterclaim does not transform a plaintiff into a defendant for removal purposes.","analysis":"Only a defendant has the power to remove a case from state to federal court when the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction, which must be established on the face of a plaintiff's complaint, can arise from either:\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states or\n\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the plaintiff asserts a claim that arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law.\n\nHere, the collector (plaintiff) sued the artist (defendant) in state court. The artist then filed a counterclaim that alleged a violation of a federal statute prohibiting fraud in sales across state lines. But as the Supreme Court held in Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets, a defendant's counterclaim does not transform a plaintiff into a defendant for removal purposes.* Since the collector remains the plaintiff for removal purposes, she is not qualified to remove the case to federal court (Choice C). Even the artist, as the defendant, could not remove this case since the collector's complaint did not establish original jurisdiction. Her breach-of-contract claim arises under state (not federal) law, so the federal court lacks federal-question jurisdiction. And though the artist and collector are citizens of different states, the federal court lacks diversity jurisdiction since the collector demanded only $20,000 in damages. The $60,000 demanded in the artist's counterclaim is not counted in determining whether the amount in controversy is satisfied (Choice D). *Congress created a narrow exception to this rule when it enacted 28 U.S.C. § 1454, a special removal rule that permits removal by any party (including a plaintiff) when a federal patent or copyright claim is asserted. (Choice A) The home-court advantage (ie, forum-defendant) rule prohibits removal when the federal court's original jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction and a defendant is a citizen of the state where the case was filed. But since the collector is the plaintiff, her residence in State B has no bearing on whether removal is proper. Educational objective: Only a defendant has the power to remove a case from state to federal court. A defendant's counterclaim does not transform a plaintiff into a defendant for removal purposes. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446 (removal procedure).\n\n• Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets, 313 U.S. 100, 107–08 (1941) (holding that a counterclaim does not change a plaintiff to a defendant for removal purposes).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446 (removal procedure).\n\n• Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets, 313 U.S. 100, 107–08 (1941) (holding that a counterclaim does not change a plaintiff to a defendant for removal purposes).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[153],"sourceAnswerPages":[154,155],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名艺术家通过互联网向 B 州一名艺术品收藏家出售其画作，总购买价为 80,000 美元。收藏家向艺术家支付 20,000 美元首付款，但称自己从未收到任何画作。因此，收藏家在 B 州州法院以违约为由起诉艺术家，请求返还首付款。艺术家提交答辩状，称其全部画作均已妥善交付收藏家，而收藏家正企图欺诈他。他还依据一项禁止跨州销售欺诈的联邦法律提起反诉，请求获得 60,000 美元。若收藏家试图将案件移送至 B 州联邦法院，她可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为收藏家试图移送的是在其本州提起的诉讼。","B":"不能，因为收藏家不具备将案件移送至联邦法院的资格。","C":"能，因为艺术家的反诉基于对联邦法律的涉嫌违反。","D":"能，因为争议总金额为 80,000 美元，且双方是不同州的公民。"},"explanationZh":"只有被告有权将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院，且案件必须属于联邦法院的原始事项管辖权。原始管辖权必须体现于原告起诉状表面，可源于：\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州的公民；或\n• 联邦问题管辖权——原告提出的请求依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生。\n\n本题中，收藏家（原告）在州法院起诉艺术家（被告）。艺术家随后提起反诉，主张对禁止跨州销售欺诈的联邦法律的违反。但是，正如最高法院在 Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets 案中所裁定，被告的反诉不会为了移送目的而把原告转变为被告。* 由于收藏家在移送问题上仍是原告，她不具备将案件移送至联邦法院的资格（选项 C）。\n\n即使是作为被告的艺术家，也不能移送本案，因为收藏家的起诉状没有建立原始管辖权。她的违约请求依据州法而非联邦法产生，故联邦法院不享有联邦问题管辖权。尽管艺术家与收藏家是不同州的公民，联邦法院仍缺乏多元州籍管辖权，因为收藏家仅请求 20,000 美元损害赔偿。确定是否满足争议金额时，不计入艺术家反诉所请求的 60,000 美元（选项 D）。\n\n*国会制定 28 U.S.C. § 1454 时创设了这一规则的狭窄例外：当事人主张联邦专利或版权请求时，这项特别移送规则允许任何一方（包括原告）移送案件。\n\n（选项 A）本州法院优势规则（即法院州被告规则）禁止在以下情形移送：联邦法院原始管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权，且一名被告是提起案件所在州的公民。但收藏家是原告，故其居住于 B 州并不影响移送是否适当。\n\n学习目标：只有被告有权将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。被告的反诉不会为了移送目的而把原告转变为被告。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441（移送要求）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446（移送程序）。\n• Shamrock Oil & Gas Corp. v. Sheets, 313 U.S. 100, 107–08 (1941)（认定反诉不会为了移送目的而将原告变为被告）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-001"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-052","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":52,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A limited liability company and a corporation entered into a contract in which the corporation agreed to deliver 100 tons of refined ore to the limited liability company in State A. The company maintains its principal place of business in State A. It has two members: one lives in State A and the other lives in State B. The corporation is both incorporated and keeps its principal place of business in State B. The corporation frequently conducts business in State A and keeps a refinery there to service its State A customers. After the corporation failed to deliver 100 tons of refined ore, the company sued the corporation for breach of contract in a federal court in State A, seeking $1 million in damages. Sixty-one days after the company filed suit, a professional process server personally served the summons and complaint on the corporation's chief operating officer while she was touring the refinery, and copies of the summons and complaint were mailed to the corporation. Upon receiving service of process, the corporation moved to dismiss the lawsuit. If the court grants the motion, which is the most likely reason?","choices":{"A":"Improper venue, because the corporation's principal place of business is in State B.","B":"Lack of personal jurisdiction, because the corporation is domiciled in State B.","C":"Lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, because the opposing parties are not diverse.","D":"Untimely service of process, because the summons and complaint were served more than 60 days after the complaint was filed."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A lawsuit should be dismissed if the court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, the authority to hear a particular type of case. This breach-of-contract case does not present a federal question, so subject-matter jurisdiction must be established through diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states. A party's citizenship is determined as follows:\n• Individual – the state where the individual resides and intends to remain indefinitely (ie, state of domicile)\n\n• Corporation – the state(s) where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business\n\n• Unincorporated association (eg, partnership, limited liability company) – every state where its partners or members are domiciled\n\nHere, the corporation is a citizen of State B, where it is incorporated and has its principal place of business. The limited liability company is a citizen of State A and State B, where its two members are domiciled. Since both parties are citizens of State B, there is no diversity of citizenship. Therefore, the motion to dismiss was likely granted on this basis. (Choice A) Venue is proper in the federal district where a substantial part of the events giving rise to the claim occurred—eg, where a contract was to be performed. Therefore, venue is proper in State A, where the ore was to be delivered. (Choice B) A federal court acquires personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state corporation when a corporate officer (here, the chief operating officer) is served with process while voluntarily in the state where the court sits. Therefore, the State A court has personal jurisdiction over the State B corporation. (Choice D) Service of process is timely if the summons and complaint are served within 90 days after the complaint is filed. Therefore, process was timely served 61 days after the complaint was filed. Educational objective: Individuals are only citizens of their state of domicile. In contrast, corporations are citizens of the state(s) where they are incorporated and/or have their principal place of business, and unincorporated associations are citizens of every state where their members are domiciled. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"Individuals are only citizens of their state of domicile. In contrast, corporations are citizens of the state(s) where they are incorporated and/or have their principal place of business, and unincorporated associations are citizens of every state where their members are domiciled.","irac":{"issue":"A limited liability company and a corporation entered into a contract in which the corporation agreed to deliver 100 tons of refined ore to the limited liability company in State A. The company maintains its principal place of business in State A. It has two members: one lives in State A and the other lives in State B. The corporation is both incorporated and keeps its principal place of business in State B. The corporation frequently conducts business in State A and keeps a refinery there to service its State A customers. After the corporation failed to deliver 100 tons of refined ore, the company sued the corporation for breach of contract in a federal court in State A, seeking $1 million in damages. Sixty-one days after the company filed suit, a professional process server personally served the summons and complaint on the corporation's chief operating officer while she was touring the refinery, and copies of the summons and complaint were mailed to the corporation. Upon receiving service of process, the corporation moved to dismiss the lawsuit. If the court grants the motion, which is the most likely reason?","rule":"Individuals are only citizens of their state of domicile. In contrast, corporations are citizens of the state(s) where they are incorporated and/or have their principal place of business, and unincorporated associations are citizens of every state where their members are domiciled.","analysis":"A lawsuit should be dismissed if the court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, the authority to hear a particular type of case. This breach-of-contract case does not present a federal question, so subject-matter jurisdiction must be established through diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states. A party's citizenship is determined as follows:\n• Individual – the state where the individual resides and intends to remain indefinitely (ie, state of domicile)\n\n• Corporation – the state(s) where it is incorporated and/or has its principal place of business\n\n• Unincorporated association (eg, partnership, limited liability company) – every state where its partners or members are domiciled\n\nHere, the corporation is a citizen of State B, where it is incorporated and has its principal place of business. The limited liability company is a citizen of State A and State B, where its two members are domiciled. Since both parties are citizens of State B, there is no diversity of citizenship. Therefore, the motion to dismiss was likely granted on this basis. (Choice A) Venue is proper in the federal district where a substantial part of the events giving rise to the claim occurred—eg, where a contract was to be performed. Therefore, venue is proper in State A, where the ore was to be delivered. (Choice B) A federal court acquires personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state corporation when a corporate officer (here, the chief operating officer) is served with process while voluntarily in the state where the court sits. Therefore, the State A court has personal jurisdiction over the State B corporation. (Choice D) Service of process is timely if the summons and complaint are served within 90 days after the complaint is filed. Therefore, process was timely served 61 days after the complaint was filed. Educational objective: Individuals are only citizens of their state of domicile. In contrast, corporations are citizens of the state(s) where they are incorporated and/or have their principal place of business, and unincorporated associations are citizens of every state where their members are domiciled. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[157],"sourceAnswerPages":[158,159],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家有限责任公司与一家股份公司签订合同，股份公司同意向有限责任公司交付 100 吨精炼矿石至 A 州。有限责任公司的主要营业地位于 A 州，并有两名成员：一人居住于 A 州，另一人居住于 B 州。股份公司在 B 州注册成立，主要营业地也位于 B 州。股份公司经常在 A 州开展业务，并在那里设有一家炼厂，为其 A 州客户提供服务。股份公司未能交付 100 吨精炼矿石后，有限责任公司在 A 州联邦法院以违约为由起诉股份公司，请求 100 万美元损害赔偿。有限责任公司提起诉讼 61 天后，一名专业送达人在股份公司首席运营官巡视炼厂时向其亲自送达传票和起诉状，并将传票和起诉状副本邮寄给股份公司。股份公司收到诉讼文书送达后，申请驳回诉讼。若法院批准该动议，最可能基于以下哪项理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"审判地不当，因为股份公司的主要营业地位于 B 州。","B":"缺乏对人管辖权，因为股份公司的住所位于 B 州。","C":"缺乏事项管辖权，因为对立双方州籍并不不同。","D":"诉讼文书送达不及时，因为传票和起诉状在提交起诉状超过 60 天后才送达。"},"explanationZh":"法院缺乏事项管辖权——即审理特定类型案件的权力——时，应驳回诉讼。本违约案件未提出联邦问题，所以必须通过多元州籍管辖权建立事项管辖权。争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州公民时，存在多元州籍管辖权。当事人的州籍按以下方式确定：\n• 个人——其居住并有无限期居留意图的州（即住所州）。\n• 股份公司——其注册成立州和／或主要营业地所在州。\n• 非公司组织（例如合伙企业、有限责任公司）——其每名合伙人或成员的住所所在州。\n\n本题中，股份公司是 B 州公民，因为其在 B 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 B 州。有限责任公司则是 A 州和 B 州公民，因为其两名成员分别以两州为住所。由于双方都是 B 州公民，不存在州籍多元性。因此，驳回动议很可能以此为由获得批准。\n\n（选项 A）引发请求的大部分事件发生地——例如合同履行地——所在联邦地区是适当审判地。因此，矿石预定交付地 A 州是适当审判地。\n\n（选项 B）州外股份公司的高级管理人员（本题为首席运营官）自愿身处联邦法院所在州并获诉讼文书送达时，联邦法院即取得对该公司的对人管辖权。因此，A 州法院对 B 州股份公司享有对人管辖权。\n\n（选项 D）传票和起诉状在提交起诉状后 90 天内送达，即属及时。因此，在提交起诉状 61 天后送达诉讼文书是及时的。\n\n学习目标：个人只具有其住所州州籍。相比之下，股份公司具有其注册成立州和／或主要营业地所在州州籍；非公司组织则具有其每名成员住所所在的每一州州籍。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-025"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-053","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":53,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"An airline incorporated and with its principal place of business in State A contracted with a manufacturer incorporated and headquartered in State B to buy a new series of planes. The manufacturer bought the planes' engines from a company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State C. The manufacturer delivered the planes to the airline. After six months, all of the planes' engines failed. The airline filed a complaint against the manufacturer and the company in a state court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $500 million in damages. The manufacturer and the company were served with process, and the company immediately filed a notice of removal. The manufacturer filed a motion to remand. Will the federal court likely grant the manufacturer's motion to remand?","choices":{"A":"No, because due process requires that a federal court hear cases involving diverse parties.","B":"No, because the parties are diverse and the amount in controversy is $500 million.","C":"Yes, because the airline is based in State A.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer did not consent to removal."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A plaintiff can choose to file suit in state or federal court. However, a defendant may remove a suit filed in state court to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction. This can arise from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nA defendant must file a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days after receipt of the initial complaint or service of the summons—whichever period is shorter.* The defendant must also obtain the consent of all other defendants to the removal. If these requirements are not met or some other procedural error occurs, the federal court should grant a motion to remand (ie, return) the entire case to state court. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists because the airline from State A sued the manufacturer from State B and the company from State C for $500 million in damages. The company timely filed a notice of removal after being served with process but failed to obtain the manufacturer's consent to the removal. As a result, removal is improper, and the federal court will likely grant the manufacturer's motion to remand (Choice B). *If a suit later becomes removable based on an amended pleading or motion, notice must be filed within 30 days after receipt of the amended pleading or motion. But if more than one year has passed since the suit was commenced and removal is based on diversity jurisdiction, then the suit can only be removed if the plaintiff acted in bad faith. (Choice A) Due process does not require that federal courts hear cases involving diverse parties. State courts may also hear such cases unless Congress grants federal courts exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here). (Choice C) The fact that the airline (plaintiff) is from State A does not provide a basis to remand the case. The home-court advantage (ie, forum-defendant) rule prohibits removal in a diversity case when a defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. Educational objective: A defendant must obtain the consent of all the other defendants to remove the suit from state to federal court. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(2)(A) (consent requirement for removal).","rule":"A defendant must obtain the consent of all the other defendants to remove the suit from state to federal court.","irac":{"issue":"An airline incorporated and with its principal place of business in State A contracted with a manufacturer incorporated and headquartered in State B to buy a new series of planes. The manufacturer bought the planes' engines from a company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State C. The manufacturer delivered the planes to the airline. After six months, all of the planes' engines failed. The airline filed a complaint against the manufacturer and the company in a state court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $500 million in damages. The manufacturer and the company were served with process, and the company immediately filed a notice of removal. The manufacturer filed a motion to remand. Will the federal court likely grant the manufacturer's motion to remand?","rule":"A defendant must obtain the consent of all the other defendants to remove the suit from state to federal court.","analysis":"A plaintiff can choose to file suit in state or federal court. However, a defendant may remove a suit filed in state court to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction. This can arise from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nA defendant must file a notice of removal in federal court within 30 days after receipt of the initial complaint or service of the summons—whichever period is shorter.* The defendant must also obtain the consent of all other defendants to the removal. If these requirements are not met or some other procedural error occurs, the federal court should grant a motion to remand (ie, return) the entire case to state court. Here, diversity jurisdiction exists because the airline from State A sued the manufacturer from State B and the company from State C for $500 million in damages. The company timely filed a notice of removal after being served with process but failed to obtain the manufacturer's consent to the removal. As a result, removal is improper, and the federal court will likely grant the manufacturer's motion to remand (Choice B). *If a suit later becomes removable based on an amended pleading or motion, notice must be filed within 30 days after receipt of the amended pleading or motion. But if more than one year has passed since the suit was commenced and removal is based on diversity jurisdiction, then the suit can only be removed if the plaintiff acted in bad faith. (Choice A) Due process does not require that federal courts hear cases involving diverse parties. State courts may also hear such cases unless Congress grants federal courts exclusive jurisdiction (not seen here). (Choice C) The fact that the airline (plaintiff) is from State A does not provide a basis to remand the case. The home-court advantage (ie, forum-defendant) rule prohibits removal in a diversity case when a defendant is a citizen of the state in which the case was filed. Educational objective: A defendant must obtain the consent of all the other defendants to remove the suit from state to federal court. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(2)(A) (consent requirement for removal).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1441 (removal requirements).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(2)(A) (consent requirement for removal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[160],"sourceAnswerPages":[161,162],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 A 州的航空公司，与一家在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州的制造商签订合同，购买一个新系列的飞机。制造商从一家在 C 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 C 州的公司购买飞机发动机。制造商将飞机交付航空公司。六个月后，所有飞机的发动机均发生故障。航空公司在 A 州州法院以违约为由起诉制造商和发动机公司，请求 5 亿美元损害赔偿。制造商和发动机公司均获诉讼文书送达，发动机公司随即提交移送通知。制造商申请将案件发回州法院。联邦法院可能批准制造商的发回动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为正当程序要求联邦法院审理当事人州籍不同的案件。","B":"不会，因为当事人州籍不同，且争议金额为 5 亿美元。","C":"会，因为航空公司的所在地是 A 州。","D":"会，因为制造商未同意移送。"},"explanationZh":"原告可以选择在州法院或联邦法院起诉。但是，如果案件属于联邦法院的事项管辖权，被告可以将州法院中的诉讼移送至联邦法院。这种管辖权可源于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州的公民。\n\n被告必须在收到最初起诉状或传票送达后 30 天内——以较短期间为准——向联邦法院提交移送通知。* 被告还必须取得其他所有被告对移送的同意。如果未满足这些要求，或发生其他程序错误，联邦法院应批准将整个案件发回州法院的动议。\n\n本题存在多元州籍管辖权，因为 A 州航空公司起诉 B 州制造商和 C 州发动机公司，请求 5 亿美元损害赔偿。发动机公司获诉讼文书送达后及时提交移送通知，却未取得制造商对移送的同意。因此，移送不适当，联邦法院很可能批准制造商的发回动议（选项 B）。\n\n*若诉讼之后因修改后的诉状或动议而变得可以移送，则必须在收到该修改后诉状或动议 30 天内提交通知。但是，如果自诉讼开始已超过一年，且移送基于多元州籍管辖权，则只有原告存在恶意行为时才可移送。\n\n（选项 A）正当程序并不要求联邦法院审理当事人州籍不同的案件。除非国会将某类案件划归联邦法院专属管辖（本题并非如此），州法院也可以审理此类案件。\n\n（选项 C）航空公司（原告）来自 A 州这一事实并非将案件发回州法院的依据。本州法院优势规则（即法院州被告规则）禁止在多元州籍案件中移送，条件是有一名被告为案件提起州的公民。\n\n学习目标：被告若要将诉讼从州法院移送至联邦法院，必须取得其他所有被告的同意。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441（移送要求）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1446(b)(2)(A)（移送的同意要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-020"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-054","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":54,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A bakery from State A sued a company from State B for breach of contract in a state court located in State A for $1 million. The bakery alleged that the company violated the contract by selling a rival bakery's products in State C. The company does no business in State A, and the contract between the two parties was signed in State B. Each state is composed of one federal district. After being served with process, the company removed the suit to the federal district court in State A. The company then moved to dismiss the suit for improper venue. How will the federal court likely rule on the company's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the bakery is from State A.","B":"Deny the motion, because the company removed the suit.","C":"Grant the motion, because the alleged breach occurred in State C.","D":"Grant the motion, because the company is from State B and the contract was signed there."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Special rules for where venue is proper\n\nRemoval • Federal district where state action was pending\n\nForeign resident • Any judicial district\n\nFederal official sued in official • Where defendant resides capacity • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• Where plaintiff resides if no real property is involved in suit Foreign government • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• In federal district court of Washington, D.C. Multiparty, multiforum • Where defendant resides litigation • Where substantial part of accident occurred Federal Tort Claims Act • Where plaintiff resides\n• Where act/omission occurred Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. Under the general venue statute, venue can be established in any of the following ways:\n• Residency-based venue – a district where any defendant resides (but only if all defendants reside in the same state)\n\n• Events-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred\n\n• Property-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the property at issue is located\n\n• Fallback provision – a district where any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (only applies if none of the above provisions can be established)\n\nVenue can also be established through special venue rules in certain situations, including when a suit is removed from state court to federal court. In that situation, venue is proper in the federal district encompassing the state court from which the suit was removed—even if venue cannot be established under the general venue statute. This is because the defendant has consented to venue by removing the suit to that federal district court.\n\nHere, the company (defendant) removed the suit from state to federal court in State A, so venue is proper there. Therefore, the court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss for improper venue. (Choice A) The fact that the bakery (plaintiff) is from State A does not establish proper venue since residency-based venue is predicated on the defendant's residence. (Choices C & D) Venue could have been established in State C under events-based venue since the alleged breach of contract occurred there. It also could have been established in State B since that is where the company resides and the contract was signed. However, venue is also proper in the federal district court in State A because the company removed the suit there. Educational objective: When a suit is removed from state to federal court, venue is proper in the federal district encompassing the state court from which the suit was removed. This is true even if venue cannot be established under the general venue statute. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(a) (venue in removal actions).\n\n• Polizzi v. Cowles Magazines, Inc., 345 U.S. 663, 665–66 (1953) (explaining that venue is proper in the federal district court to which a suit was removed from state court).","rule":"When a suit is removed from state to federal court, venue is proper in the federal district encompassing the state court from which the suit was removed. This is true even if venue cannot be established under the general venue statute.","irac":{"issue":"A bakery from State A sued a company from State B for breach of contract in a state court located in State A for $1 million. The bakery alleged that the company violated the contract by selling a rival bakery's products in State C. The company does no business in State A, and the contract between the two parties was signed in State B. Each state is composed of one federal district. After being served with process, the company removed the suit to the federal district court in State A. The company then moved to dismiss the suit for improper venue. How will the federal court likely rule on the company's motion to dismiss?","rule":"When a suit is removed from state to federal court, venue is proper in the federal district encompassing the state court from which the suit was removed. This is true even if venue cannot be established under the general venue statute.","analysis":"Special rules for where venue is proper\n\nRemoval • Federal district where state action was pending\n\nForeign resident • Any judicial district\n\nFederal official sued in official • Where defendant resides capacity • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• Where plaintiff resides if no real property is involved in suit Foreign government • Where substantial events occurred or property is located\n• In federal district court of Washington, D.C. Multiparty, multiforum • Where defendant resides litigation • Where substantial part of accident occurred Federal Tort Claims Act • Where plaintiff resides\n• Where act/omission occurred Venue is the geographic location of a federal district court where a case may be heard. Under the general venue statute, venue can be established in any of the following ways:\n• Residency-based venue – a district where any defendant resides (but only if all defendants reside in the same state)\n\n• Events-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred\n\n• Property-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the property at issue is located\n\n• Fallback provision – a district where any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (only applies if none of the above provisions can be established)\n\nVenue can also be established through special venue rules in certain situations, including when a suit is removed from state court to federal court. In that situation, venue is proper in the federal district encompassing the state court from which the suit was removed—even if venue cannot be established under the general venue statute. This is because the defendant has consented to venue by removing the suit to that federal district court.\n\nHere, the company (defendant) removed the suit from state to federal court in State A, so venue is proper there. Therefore, the court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss for improper venue. (Choice A) The fact that the bakery (plaintiff) is from State A does not establish proper venue since residency-based venue is predicated on the defendant's residence. (Choices C & D) Venue could have been established in State C under events-based venue since the alleged breach of contract occurred there. It also could have been established in State B since that is where the company resides and the contract was signed. However, venue is also proper in the federal district court in State A because the company removed the suit there. Educational objective: When a suit is removed from state to federal court, venue is proper in the federal district encompassing the state court from which the suit was removed. This is true even if venue cannot be established under the general venue statute. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(a) (venue in removal actions).\n\n• Polizzi v. Cowles Magazines, Inc., 345 U.S. 663, 665–66 (1953) (explaining that venue is proper in the federal district court to which a suit was removed from state court).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(a) (venue in removal actions).\n\n• Polizzi v. Cowles Magazines, Inc., 345 U.S. 663, 665–66 (1953) (explaining that venue is proper in the federal district court to which a suit was removed from state court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[163],"sourceAnswerPages":[164,165],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一家面包店在 A 州州法院以违约为由起诉 B 州一家公司，请求 100 万美元。面包店主张，该公司在 C 州销售一家竞争面包店的产品，违反双方合同。该公司不在 A 州开展业务，双方合同在 B 州签订。每州均只设一个联邦地区。获诉讼文书送达后，该公司将诉讼移送至 A 州联邦地区法院，随后以审判地不当为由申请驳回。联邦法院可能如何裁定公司的驳回动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为面包店来自 A 州。","B":"驳回动议，因为公司将诉讼移送至该法院。","C":"批准动议，因为所称违约发生于 C 州。","D":"批准动议，因为公司来自 B 州且合同在那里签订。"},"explanationZh":"审判地适当的特别规则：\n\n情形｜适当审判地\n移送至联邦法院｜州法院诉讼原先待决地所在的联邦地区\n外国居民｜任何司法地区\n以公务身份被诉的联邦官员｜被告居住地；重大事件发生地或财产所在地；若诉讼不涉及不动产，亦可在原告居住地\n外国政府｜重大事件发生地或财产所在地；或哥伦比亚特区联邦地区法院\n多方、多法院诉讼｜被告居住地；事故重大部分发生地\n《联邦侵权请求法》｜原告居住地；作为或不作为发生地\n\n审判地是可以审理案件的某一联邦地区法院的地理位置。根据一般审判地法律，可通过下列任一方式建立适当审判地：\n• 基于居住地的审判地——任何被告居住的地区，但仅限全部被告均居住于同一州时。\n• 基于事件的审判地——引发诉讼的大部分事件发生地所在地区。\n• 基于财产的审判地——争议财产的大部分所在地所在地区。\n• 兜底规定——任何被告受该法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在上述规定均无法建立审判地时适用。\n\n在特定情形下，还可依据特别审判地规则建立审判地，包括将诉讼从州法院移送至联邦法院时。此时，审判地在涵盖原州法院的联邦地区内适当，即使依据一般审判地法律无法建立审判地，也是如此。这是因为被告将诉讼移送至该联邦地区法院，即已同意该审判地。\n\n本题中，公司（被告）将诉讼从 A 州州法院移送至 A 州联邦法院，所以该地审判地适当。因此，法院很可能驳回公司以审判地不当提出的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）面包店（原告）来自 A 州这一事实不能建立适当审判地，因为基于居住地的审判地取决于被告的居住地。\n\n（选项 C、D）所称违约发生于 C 州，所以可以依据事件发生地在 C 州建立审判地。B 州是公司居住地和合同签订地，也可在那里建立审判地。但是，公司已将诉讼移送至 A 州联邦地区法院，所以该法院同样是适当审判地。\n\n学习目标：诉讼从州法院移送至联邦法院时，涵盖原州法院的联邦地区是适当审判地，即使依据一般审判地法律无法在那里建立审判地，也是如此。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)（适当审判地）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1441(a)（移送诉讼的审判地）。\n• Polizzi v. Cowles Magazines, Inc., 345 U.S. 663, 665–66 (1953)（说明从州法院移送的诉讼在接收该案的联邦地区法院审判地适当）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-055","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":55,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A man was fired from a company for refusing to work on Saturday mornings because his religious beliefs require him to attend worship services during that time. The man sued the company in federal court in State A for violating a federal law that prohibits religious discrimination in the workplace. State A's rules of procedure permit service of process on an organization by leaving a copy of the summons and complaint at the organization's headquarters with an employee of suitable age and discretion. The company has designated its receptionist as an agent authorized to receive service of process on its behalf. The man hired an 18-year-old process server to deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the company's headquarters. The receptionist was not in the office on the day the process server arrived, so the process server left the summons and complaint with a 16-year-old employee who was filling in for the receptionist. The company filed an answer, which included a motion to dismiss for improper service of process. Will the court likely grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because process was served in conformance with State A's rules of service.","B":"No, because the company waived its claim for improper service of process.","C":"Yes, because process was not delivered to the company's authorized agent.","D":"Yes, because the process server was not of suitable age to serve process."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Methods of serving process on defendant-organization\n\nIn United • Follow state law where district court is located or where service is States made\n• Deliver summons & complaint to officer or managing / general / authorized agent & mail to defendant if required by statute In foreign • Follow international agreement reasonably calculated to give notice country • If no international agreement & not prohibited by federal law: – follow foreign country's rules – serve as foreign country directs in response to letter rogatory or – unless prohibited by foreign country, serve by mail with request for signed return receipt\n• Follow court order, unless prohibited by international agreement Before a federal district court can adjudicate a suit, the plaintiff must properly serve the defendant with process—ie, deliver a copy of the summons and complaint. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4(h), a defendant-organization can be properly served in the United States by either:\n• following the service-of-process rules of the state where the court is located (ie, the forum state) or where service is made or\n\n• delivering process to an officer, managing or general agent, or agent authorized by appointment or law to receive service AND, if required by statute, mailing process to the defendant.\n\nHere, State A (the forum state) permits service of process on an organization by leaving a copy of the summons and complaint at the organization's headquarters with an employee of suitable age and discretion. Such an employee is one who is old enough to possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation (eg, a 16-year-old employee). Therefore, the court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) Under FRCP 12(h), a defendant waives the defense that process was improperly served if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or the defendant's answer—whichever occurs first. Here, the company did not waive this defense because it asserted the defense in its answer and no pre-answer motion had been filed. (Choice C) Although process was not delivered to the receptionist, the company's authorized agent for service of process, service was still valid since it complied with the forum state's law.\n\n(Choice D) Under FRCP 4(c), process can be delivered by any person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (eg, the 18-year-old process server). Educational objective: An organization can be served by (1) following the service-of-process rules of the state where the district court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering process to an officer or agent authorized to receive it and, if required by statute, mailing process to the organization. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(h) (service on organizations).","rule":"An organization can be served by (1) following the service-of-process rules of the state where the district court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering process to an officer or agent authorized to receive it and, if required by statute, mailing process to the organization.","irac":{"issue":"A man was fired from a company for refusing to work on Saturday mornings because his religious beliefs require him to attend worship services during that time. The man sued the company in federal court in State A for violating a federal law that prohibits religious discrimination in the workplace. State A's rules of procedure permit service of process on an organization by leaving a copy of the summons and complaint at the organization's headquarters with an employee of suitable age and discretion. The company has designated its receptionist as an agent authorized to receive service of process on its behalf. The man hired an 18-year-old process server to deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the company's headquarters. The receptionist was not in the office on the day the process server arrived, so the process server left the summons and complaint with a 16-year-old employee who was filling in for the receptionist. The company filed an answer, which included a motion to dismiss for improper service of process. Will the court likely grant the motion?","rule":"An organization can be served by (1) following the service-of-process rules of the state where the district court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering process to an officer or agent authorized to receive it and, if required by statute, mailing process to the organization.","analysis":"Methods of serving process on defendant-organization\n\nIn United • Follow state law where district court is located or where service is States made\n• Deliver summons & complaint to officer or managing / general / authorized agent & mail to defendant if required by statute In foreign • Follow international agreement reasonably calculated to give notice country • If no international agreement & not prohibited by federal law: – follow foreign country's rules – serve as foreign country directs in response to letter rogatory or – unless prohibited by foreign country, serve by mail with request for signed return receipt\n• Follow court order, unless prohibited by international agreement Before a federal district court can adjudicate a suit, the plaintiff must properly serve the defendant with process—ie, deliver a copy of the summons and complaint. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4(h), a defendant-organization can be properly served in the United States by either:\n• following the service-of-process rules of the state where the court is located (ie, the forum state) or where service is made or\n\n• delivering process to an officer, managing or general agent, or agent authorized by appointment or law to receive service AND, if required by statute, mailing process to the defendant.\n\nHere, State A (the forum state) permits service of process on an organization by leaving a copy of the summons and complaint at the organization's headquarters with an employee of suitable age and discretion. Such an employee is one who is old enough to possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation (eg, a 16-year-old employee). Therefore, the court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) Under FRCP 12(h), a defendant waives the defense that process was improperly served if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or the defendant's answer—whichever occurs first. Here, the company did not waive this defense because it asserted the defense in its answer and no pre-answer motion had been filed. (Choice C) Although process was not delivered to the receptionist, the company's authorized agent for service of process, service was still valid since it complied with the forum state's law.\n\n(Choice D) Under FRCP 4(c), process can be delivered by any person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (eg, the 18-year-old process server). Educational objective: An organization can be served by (1) following the service-of-process rules of the state where the district court is located or where service is made or (2) delivering process to an officer or agent authorized to receive it and, if required by statute, mailing process to the organization. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(h) (service on organizations).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(h) (service on organizations).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[166],"sourceAnswerPages":[167,168],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子因拒绝在周六上午工作而被公司解雇，因为其宗教信仰要求他在该时段参加礼拜。男子在 A 州联邦法院起诉公司违反禁止工作场所宗教歧视的联邦法律。A 州程序规则允许向组织送达诉讼文书，方式是将传票和起诉状副本留置于组织总部，交给年龄和判断能力适当的雇员。公司指定其前台接待员为获授权代其接收诉讼文书的代理人。男子聘请一名 18 岁的送达人，将传票和起诉状副本送至公司总部。送达人抵达当日，前台接待员不在办公室，送达人遂将传票和起诉状交给一名代班的 16 岁雇员。公司提交答辩状，其中包含一项以诉讼文书送达不当为由提出的驳回动议。法院可能批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为诉讼文书依照 A 州送达规则完成送达。","B":"不会，因为公司已放弃诉讼文书送达不当的抗辩。","C":"会，因为诉讼文书未交付公司的授权代理人。","D":"会，因为送达人的年龄不足以送达诉讼文书。"},"explanationZh":"向作为被告的组织送达诉讼文书的方法：\n\n在美国境内：\n• 遵循地区法院所在地州或实际送达地州的法律；\n• 将传票和起诉状交付给高级管理人员、管理代理人、一般代理人或授权代理人；法律要求时，还须邮寄给被告。\n\n在外国：\n• 遵循合理设计为给予通知的国际协议；\n• 若无国际协议且联邦法律不禁止：遵循该外国规则；按照该外国对司法协助请求书的答复所指示方式送达；或在该外国不禁止时，以要求签收回执的邮件送达；\n• 遵循法院命令，但国际协议禁止者除外。\n\n联邦地区法院能够审理诉讼前，原告必须向被告适当送达诉讼文书，即交付传票和起诉状副本。依《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）4(h)，在美国境内可以通过以下任一方式向作为被告的组织适当送达：\n• 遵循法院所在地州（即法院州）或实际送达地州的诉讼文书送达规则；或\n• 将诉讼文书交付给高级管理人员、管理代理人、一般代理人或依指定或法律获授权接收送达的代理人；并且在法律要求时，将诉讼文书邮寄给被告。\n\n本题中，A 州（法院州）允许把传票和起诉状副本留置于组织总部，交给年龄和判断能力适当的雇员。此类雇员是年龄足以具有理解该情形所需有限能力的人，例如 16 岁雇员。因此，法院很可能驳回公司的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 B）依 FRCP 12(h)，如果被告未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出诉讼文书送达不当抗辩，即放弃该抗辩。本题中，公司在答辩状内提出该抗辩，且此前未提出答辩前动议，故并未放弃。\n\n（选项 C）诉讼文书虽未交给作为公司授权送达代理人的前台接待员，但因送达符合法院州法律，仍然有效。\n\n（选项 D）依 FRCP 4(c)，任何年满 18 岁且不是诉讼当事人的人均可送达诉讼文书，例如本题 18 岁的送达人。\n\n学习目标：可通过以下方式向组织送达：(1) 遵循地区法院所在地州或实际送达地州的诉讼文书送达规则；或 (2) 将诉讼文书交付给高级管理人员或获授权接收送达的代理人，并在法律要求时邮寄给该组织。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(h)（向组织送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-056","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":56,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"Members of a nonprofit organization sued the Department of Homeland Security in federal court for posting a statement that \"faith in God is essential to the security of the United States of America\" on a wall in one of its offices. The nonprofit members claim that the posted statement violates the First Amendment establishment clause. The complaint does not mention the Secretary of Homeland Security. The nonprofit members sent a copy of the complaint and summons by certified U.S. mail to the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office and the Attorney General of the United States. The Department of Homeland Security maintains that service was inadequate. Must the nonprofit members do anything else to perfect service of process in this case?","choices":{"A":"No, because all of the necessary entities were properly served.","B":"No, because the federal rules allow the complaint and summons to be sent by certified mail.","C":"Yes, they must serve the Department of Homeland Security.","D":"Yes, they must serve the Secretary of Homeland Security."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Special service-of-process rules\n\nIncompetent or minor Plaintiff must follow rules of state where service is made\n\nFederal Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint to all of the government/agency following:\n\n• (1) U.S. attorney for district where action is brought or designated employee or (2) civil-process clerk at U.S. attorney's office via registered or certified mail\n• U.S. Attorney General via registered or certified mail AND\n• agency or officer whose conduct is challenged via registered or certified mail Foreign government Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint:\n\n• by special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign state\n• if no special arrangement, by international convention\n• if no convention, by sending to head of ministry of foreign affairs via mail requiring signed receipt OR\n• if no notice by mail within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt State or local Plaintiff must either: government\n• deliver notice to chief executive officer OR\n• follow rules of state where government is located A defendant must receive notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, proper delivery of the summons and complaint. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4(i) has special service-of-process requirements when the defendant is the United States or a federal agency or employee acting within an official capacity. This rule requires that the plaintiff deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to all of the following:*\n• the U.S. attorney (or designated agent) for the district where the suit is filed OR the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office by registered or certified mail\n\n• the U.S. Attorney General by registered or certified mail and\n\n• any agency or officer whose official conduct is being challenged by registered or certified mail.\n\nHere, the nonprofit members sued the Department of Homeland Security in federal court. They properly sent a copy of the complaint and summons by certified mail to the\n\ncivil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office and the U.S. Attorney General (Choice A). But to perfect service of process, they must also serve the Department of Homeland Security— the agency whose official conduct is being challenged. *Process must be served on the appropriate U.S. attorney and the U.S. Attorney General because these individuals are responsible for representing the United States, federal agencies, and federal employees acting in their official capacity. (Choice B) Although FRCP 4(i) permits the plaintiff to serve the summons and complaint by certified mail, service of process is not perfected unless all of the entities requiring service receive it (not seen here). (Choice D) A nonparty agency or employee (eg, the Secretary of Homeland Security) need only be served if the suit challenges the nonparty's order (not seen here). Educational objective: When suing the United States, its agencies, or its employees, the plaintiff must serve process on (1) the U.S. attorney (or designated agent) for the district where the suit is filed or the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office, (2) the U.S. Attorney General, and (3) any agency or officer whose official conduct is being challenged. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i) (serving the United States and its agencies or employees).","rule":"When suing the United States, its agencies, or its employees, the plaintiff must serve process on (1) the U.S. attorney (or designated agent) for the district where the suit is filed or the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office, (2) the U.S. Attorney General, and (3) any agency or officer whose official conduct is being challenged.","irac":{"issue":"Members of a nonprofit organization sued the Department of Homeland Security in federal court for posting a statement that \"faith in God is essential to the security of the United States of America\" on a wall in one of its offices. The nonprofit members claim that the posted statement violates the First Amendment establishment clause. The complaint does not mention the Secretary of Homeland Security. The nonprofit members sent a copy of the complaint and summons by certified U.S. mail to the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office and the Attorney General of the United States. The Department of Homeland Security maintains that service was inadequate. Must the nonprofit members do anything else to perfect service of process in this case?","rule":"When suing the United States, its agencies, or its employees, the plaintiff must serve process on (1) the U.S. attorney (or designated agent) for the district where the suit is filed or the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office, (2) the U.S. Attorney General, and (3) any agency or officer whose official conduct is being challenged.","analysis":"Special service-of-process rules\n\nIncompetent or minor Plaintiff must follow rules of state where service is made\n\nFederal Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint to all of the government/agency following:\n\n• (1) U.S. attorney for district where action is brought or designated employee or (2) civil-process clerk at U.S. attorney's office via registered or certified mail\n• U.S. Attorney General via registered or certified mail AND\n• agency or officer whose conduct is challenged via registered or certified mail Foreign government Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint:\n\n• by special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign state\n• if no special arrangement, by international convention\n• if no convention, by sending to head of ministry of foreign affairs via mail requiring signed receipt OR\n• if no notice by mail within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt State or local Plaintiff must either: government\n• deliver notice to chief executive officer OR\n• follow rules of state where government is located A defendant must receive notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, proper delivery of the summons and complaint. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4(i) has special service-of-process requirements when the defendant is the United States or a federal agency or employee acting within an official capacity. This rule requires that the plaintiff deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to all of the following:*\n• the U.S. attorney (or designated agent) for the district where the suit is filed OR the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office by registered or certified mail\n\n• the U.S. Attorney General by registered or certified mail and\n\n• any agency or officer whose official conduct is being challenged by registered or certified mail.\n\nHere, the nonprofit members sued the Department of Homeland Security in federal court. They properly sent a copy of the complaint and summons by certified mail to the\n\ncivil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office and the U.S. Attorney General (Choice A). But to perfect service of process, they must also serve the Department of Homeland Security— the agency whose official conduct is being challenged. *Process must be served on the appropriate U.S. attorney and the U.S. Attorney General because these individuals are responsible for representing the United States, federal agencies, and federal employees acting in their official capacity. (Choice B) Although FRCP 4(i) permits the plaintiff to serve the summons and complaint by certified mail, service of process is not perfected unless all of the entities requiring service receive it (not seen here). (Choice D) A nonparty agency or employee (eg, the Secretary of Homeland Security) need only be served if the suit challenges the nonparty's order (not seen here). Educational objective: When suing the United States, its agencies, or its employees, the plaintiff must serve process on (1) the U.S. attorney (or designated agent) for the district where the suit is filed or the civil-process clerk at the U.S. attorney's office, (2) the U.S. Attorney General, and (3) any agency or officer whose official conduct is being challenged. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i) (serving the United States and its agencies or employees).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i) (serving the United States and its agencies or employees).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[169],"sourceAnswerPages":[170,171],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某非营利组织的成员在联邦法院起诉国土安全部，原因是该部门在其一处办公室的墙上张贴声明：“对上帝的信仰对美利坚合众国的安全至关重要。”非营利组织成员主张，该声明违反第一修正案的政教分离条款。起诉状未提及国土安全部长。非营利组织成员通过美国挂号邮件，将起诉状和传票副本寄送给联邦检察官办公室的民事诉讼文书书记员以及美国司法部长。国土安全部主张送达不充分。为在本案中完成诉讼文书送达，非营利组织成员还必须采取其他行动吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不必，因为所有必须送达的实体均已获得适当送达。","B":"不必，因为联邦规则允许通过挂号邮件寄送起诉状和传票。","C":"必须，他们还须向国土安全部送达。","D":"必须，他们还须向国土安全部长送达。"},"explanationZh":"诉讼文书送达的特别规则：\n\n被告类型｜原告必须采取的送达方式\n无行为能力人或未成年人｜遵循实际送达地州的规则\n联邦政府／机构｜原告必须向以下各方交付传票和起诉状：\n• 诉讼提起地区的联邦检察官或其指定雇员；或者通过挂号或认证邮件寄给联邦检察官办公室的民事诉讼文书书记员；\n• 通过挂号或认证邮件寄给美国司法部长；并且\n• 通过挂号或认证邮件寄给其行为受质疑的机构或官员\n外国政府｜原告必须交付传票和起诉状：\n• 按原告与外国之间的特别安排；\n• 若无特别安排，按国际公约；\n• 若无公约，以要求签收回执的邮件寄给外交部长；或者\n• 若邮寄后 30 天内未收到通知，以要求签收回执的邮件向美国国务卿寄送两份\n州或地方政府｜原告必须选择：\n• 向行政首长交付通知；或\n• 遵循该政府所在地州的规则\n\n被告必须通过诉讼文书送达——即适当交付传票和起诉状——获得诉讼通知。《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）4(i) 对被告为美国、联邦机构或以公务身份行事的联邦雇员时规定了特别送达要求。该规则要求原告向以下所有对象交付传票和起诉状副本：*\n• 诉讼提起地区的联邦检察官（或指定代理人），或者通过挂号或认证邮件寄给联邦检察官办公室的民事诉讼文书书记员；\n• 通过挂号或认证邮件寄给美国司法部长；并且\n• 通过挂号或认证邮件寄给其公务行为受质疑的任何机构或官员。\n\n本题中，非营利组织成员在联邦法院起诉国土安全部。他们以认证邮件将起诉状及传票副本寄送给联邦检察官办公室的民事诉讼文书书记员和美国司法部长，这些送达均属适当（选项 A）。但是，为完成诉讼文书送达，他们还必须向公务行为受到质疑的机构——国土安全部——送达。\n\n*之所以必须向相应联邦检察官和美国司法部长送达，是因为这些人员负责代表美国、联邦机构及以公务身份行事的联邦雇员。\n\n（选项 B）FRCP 4(i) 虽允许原告通过认证邮件送达传票和起诉状，但只有全部必须送达的实体均收到文书，诉讼文书送达才告完成；本题尚未做到。\n\n（选项 D）非当事人机构或雇员（例如国土安全部长）仅在诉讼质疑该非当事人的命令时才必须获得送达，本题并非如此。\n\n学习目标：起诉美国、其机构或其雇员时，原告必须向以下各方送达诉讼文书：(1) 诉讼提起地区的联邦检察官（或指定代理人），或者联邦检察官办公室的民事诉讼文书书记员；(2) 美国司法部长；以及 (3) 其公务行为受到质疑的任何机构或官员。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i)（向美国及其机构或雇员送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-057","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":57,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff from State A sued his State B business accountant in a state court in State A for breach of contract. The plaintiff sought $100,000 in damages. The accountant promptly removed the case to the federal district court in State A. Immediately after the case was removed, the district court entered an order remanding the case back to state court on the ground that the district court lacked subject-matter jurisdiction. One day later, the accountant appealed the remand order. Should the federal appellate court hear the merits of the accountant's appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the appellate court lacks jurisdiction over the appeal of the remand order.","B":"No, because the plaintiff did not raise subject-matter jurisdiction in a motion to remand.","C":"Yes, because the accountant timely filed the appeal.","D":"Yes, because the district court committed clear error when it remanded the case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A defendant can remove a case from state to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Such jurisdiction arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nA party, usually the plaintiff, can request that the federal district court return the case to state court by filing a motion to remand based on a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or a procedural error in the removal. The federal district court may also remand a case sua sponte—but only if it concludes that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction (as the court did here) (Choice B). A district court's order to remand the case is typically not appealable because Congress has explicitly forbidden these appeals to expedite litigation and avoid drawn-out removal disputes. As a result, federal appellate courts lack jurisdiction over such appeals absent limited exceptions (see image above). None of these exceptions apply here, so the appellate court should not hear the merits of the accountant's appeal. (Choice C) The fact that the accountant timely filed the appeal within 30 days after the remand order was entered does not give the appellate court jurisdiction to review that order. (Choice D) The clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review factual findings in a bench trial—not a district court's order remanding a case back to state court (as seen here). Regardless, the appellate court lacks jurisdiction to review the district court's decision to order a remand since Congress explicitly forbade such review. Educational objective: A federal district court's order to remand a case to state court—whether for a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or procedural error in the removal—cannot be appealed absent limited exceptions. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(d) (prohibition against appeal of remand order).\n\n• 14C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3740 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the appealability of order remanding case to state court).","rule":"A federal district court's order to remand a case to state court—whether for a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or procedural error in the removal—cannot be appealed absent limited exceptions.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff from State A sued his State B business accountant in a state court in State A for breach of contract. The plaintiff sought $100,000 in damages. The accountant promptly removed the case to the federal district court in State A. Immediately after the case was removed, the district court entered an order remanding the case back to state court on the ground that the district court lacked subject-matter jurisdiction. One day later, the accountant appealed the remand order. Should the federal appellate court hear the merits of the accountant's appeal?","rule":"A federal district court's order to remand a case to state court—whether for a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or procedural error in the removal—cannot be appealed absent limited exceptions.","analysis":"A defendant can remove a case from state to federal court if the case falls within the federal court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Such jurisdiction arises from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states.\n\nA party, usually the plaintiff, can request that the federal district court return the case to state court by filing a motion to remand based on a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or a procedural error in the removal. The federal district court may also remand a case sua sponte—but only if it concludes that it lacks subject-matter jurisdiction (as the court did here) (Choice B). A district court's order to remand the case is typically not appealable because Congress has explicitly forbidden these appeals to expedite litigation and avoid drawn-out removal disputes. As a result, federal appellate courts lack jurisdiction over such appeals absent limited exceptions (see image above). None of these exceptions apply here, so the appellate court should not hear the merits of the accountant's appeal. (Choice C) The fact that the accountant timely filed the appeal within 30 days after the remand order was entered does not give the appellate court jurisdiction to review that order. (Choice D) The clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review factual findings in a bench trial—not a district court's order remanding a case back to state court (as seen here). Regardless, the appellate court lacks jurisdiction to review the district court's decision to order a remand since Congress explicitly forbade such review. Educational objective: A federal district court's order to remand a case to state court—whether for a lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or procedural error in the removal—cannot be appealed absent limited exceptions. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(d) (prohibition against appeal of remand order).\n\n• 14C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3740 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the appealability of order remanding case to state court).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(d) (prohibition against appeal of remand order).\n\n• 14C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3740 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the appealability of order remanding case to state court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[172],"sourceAnswerPages":[173],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名原告在 A 州州法院起诉其来自 B 州的企业会计师违约，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。会计师随即将案件移送至 A 州联邦地区法院。案件刚被移送后，地区法院便以其缺乏事项管辖权为由，下令将案件发回州法院。一天后，会计师就发回命令提起上诉。联邦上诉法院是否应审理会计师上诉的实体问题？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为上诉法院对发回命令的上诉缺乏管辖权。","B":"不应，因为原告没有在发回动议中提出事项管辖权问题。","C":"应当，因为会计师及时提起上诉。","D":"应当，因为地区法院将案件发回时犯有明显错误。"},"explanationZh":"若案件属于联邦法院的原始事项管辖权，被告可将案件从州法院移送至联邦法院。这种管辖权源于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州的公民。\n\n当事人（通常是原告）可以事项管辖权缺失或移送程序错误为由提出发回动议，请求联邦地区法院将案件退回州法院。联邦地区法院也可以依职权将案件发回，但仅限其认定自己缺乏事项管辖权时，本题法院正是如此（选项 B）。\n\n地区法院的发回命令通常不得上诉，因为国会为加快诉讼并避免移送争议久拖不决，已明确禁止此类上诉。因此，除有限例外外，联邦上诉法院对此类上诉缺乏管辖权（见上图）。本题不适用任何例外，所以上诉法院不应审理会计师上诉的实体问题。\n\n（选项 C）会计师在发回命令作出后 30 天内及时提起上诉，并不能使上诉法院取得审查该命令的管辖权。\n\n（选项 D）明显错误审查标准用于审查法官审判中的事实认定，而非地区法院将案件发回州法院的命令。无论如何，由于国会已明确禁止审查此类发回决定，上诉法院没有管辖权进行审查。\n\n学习目标：联邦地区法院因事项管辖权缺失或移送程序错误而将案件发回州法院的命令，除有限例外外不得上诉。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1447(d)（禁止就发回命令上诉）。\n• 14C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3740 (4th ed. 2020)（说明将案件发回州法院之命令的可上诉性）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-026"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-058","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":58,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"Two siblings inherited their $250,000 family home located in State A after their parents died in a car accident. The brother is 20 years old and lives in State A. The sister is 13 years old and now lives with her uncle, who has not yet been appointed her legal guardian. The brother wants to sell the home, while the uncle wants to use the home as a rental property. The brother filed a lawsuit against his sister in a federal court in State A, requesting that the court order the sale of the home and split the proceeds between the children. The brother hired a process server, who hand-delivered a copy of the summons and complaint to the uncle at his apartment in State B. A State A statute provides that process may be served on a minor by \"leaving a copy of the summons and complaint with the minor's parent or legally appointed guardian.\" A State B statute allows service of process on a minor by \"leaving a copy of the summons and complaint with the minor's parent or legally appointed guardian, an adult family member with whom the minor resides, or a married-minor's adult spouse.\" The sister's lawyer filed a motion to dismiss the suit for insufficient service of process. Will the court likely grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the brother complied with the State B service-of-process statute.","B":"No, because the federal rules allow a family member to receive process on a minor's behalf.","C":"Yes, because minors cannot be sued in federal court.","D":"Yes, because the brother did not comply with the State A service-of-process statute."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Special service-of-process rules\n\nIncompetent or minor Plaintiff must follow rules of state where service is made\n\nFederal Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint to all of the government/agency following:\n\n• (1) U.S. attorney for district where action is brought or designated employee or (2) civil-process clerk at U.S. attorney's office via registered or certified mail\n• U.S. Attorney General via registered or certified mail AND\n• agency or officer whose conduct is challenged via registered or certified mail Foreign government Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint:\n\n• by special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign state\n• if no special arrangement, by international convention\n• if no convention, by sending to head of ministry of foreign affairs via mail requiring signed receipt OR\n• if no notice by mail within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt State or local Plaintiff must either: government\n• deliver notice to chief executive officer OR\n• follow rules of state where government is located A plaintiff must give a defendant notice of a lawsuit by properly serving the defendant with a summons and complaint (ie, service of process). Like all defendants located in the U.S. who are sued in federal court, a minor or legally incompetent defendant must be served within 90 days after the complaint is filed. But under FRCP 4(g), a minor or legally incompetent defendant must be served in accordance with the service-of-process rules of the state where service is made. Here, those are the rules of State B. State B allows a minor to be served by leaving process with \"an adult family member with whom the minor resides.\" Here, the brother's process server hand-delivered the summons and complaint to the uncle at his apartment in State B. And since the 13-year-old sister resides with that uncle, service of process was sufficient. Therefore, the court will likely deny the sister's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) A family member can receive process on a minor's behalf only if the state where service is made allows such service of process—eg, here, allowed in State B but not State A.\n\n(Choice C) Minors can be sued in federal court—as anticipated by FRCP 4's provision for serving a minor. (Choice D) A minor must be served with process pursuant to the service-of-process rules of the state where service is made (State B)—not the state where the suit is filed (State A). Educational objective: Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4(g), a minor or legally incompetent defendant can only be served with process by following the service rules of the state where service is made. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(g) (service on minor or incompetent person).","rule":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4(g), a minor or legally incompetent defendant can only be served with process by following the service rules of the state where service is made. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"Two siblings inherited their $250,000 family home located in State A after their parents died in a car accident. The brother is 20 years old and lives in State A. The sister is 13 years old and now lives with her uncle, who has not yet been appointed her legal guardian. The brother wants to sell the home, while the uncle wants to use the home as a rental property. The brother filed a lawsuit against his sister in a federal court in State A, requesting that the court order the sale of the home and split the proceeds between the children. The brother hired a process server, who hand-delivered a copy of the summons and complaint to the uncle at his apartment in State B. A State A statute provides that process may be served on a minor by \"leaving a copy of the summons and complaint with the minor's parent or legally appointed guardian.\" A State B statute allows service of process on a minor by \"leaving a copy of the summons and complaint with the minor's parent or legally appointed guardian, an adult family member with whom the minor resides, or a married-minor's adult spouse.\" The sister's lawyer filed a motion to dismiss the suit for insufficient service of process. Will the court likely grant the motion?","rule":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4(g), a minor or legally incompetent defendant can only be served with process by following the service rules of the state where service is made. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"Special service-of-process rules\n\nIncompetent or minor Plaintiff must follow rules of state where service is made\n\nFederal Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint to all of the government/agency following:\n\n• (1) U.S. attorney for district where action is brought or designated employee or (2) civil-process clerk at U.S. attorney's office via registered or certified mail\n• U.S. Attorney General via registered or certified mail AND\n• agency or officer whose conduct is challenged via registered or certified mail Foreign government Plaintiff must deliver summons & complaint:\n\n• by special arrangement between plaintiff & foreign state\n• if no special arrangement, by international convention\n• if no convention, by sending to head of ministry of foreign affairs via mail requiring signed receipt OR\n• if no notice by mail within 30 days, by sending two copies to U.S. Secretary of State via mail requiring signed receipt State or local Plaintiff must either: government\n• deliver notice to chief executive officer OR\n• follow rules of state where government is located A plaintiff must give a defendant notice of a lawsuit by properly serving the defendant with a summons and complaint (ie, service of process). Like all defendants located in the U.S. who are sued in federal court, a minor or legally incompetent defendant must be served within 90 days after the complaint is filed. But under FRCP 4(g), a minor or legally incompetent defendant must be served in accordance with the service-of-process rules of the state where service is made. Here, those are the rules of State B. State B allows a minor to be served by leaving process with \"an adult family member with whom the minor resides.\" Here, the brother's process server hand-delivered the summons and complaint to the uncle at his apartment in State B. And since the 13-year-old sister resides with that uncle, service of process was sufficient. Therefore, the court will likely deny the sister's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) A family member can receive process on a minor's behalf only if the state where service is made allows such service of process—eg, here, allowed in State B but not State A.\n\n(Choice C) Minors can be sued in federal court—as anticipated by FRCP 4's provision for serving a minor. (Choice D) A minor must be served with process pursuant to the service-of-process rules of the state where service is made (State B)—not the state where the suit is filed (State A). Educational objective: Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 4(g), a minor or legally incompetent defendant can only be served with process by following the service rules of the state where service is made. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(g) (service on minor or incompetent person).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(g) (service on minor or incompetent person).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[175],"sourceAnswerPages":[176,177],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一对兄妹的父母在车祸中去世后，二人继承了位于 A 州、价值 250,000 美元的家庭住宅。哥哥 20 岁，居住于 A 州。妹妹 13 岁，目前与叔叔同住，而叔叔尚未获任命为她的法定监护人。哥哥想出售住宅，叔叔则想将其用作出租物业。哥哥在 A 州联邦法院起诉妹妹，请求法院下令出售住宅，并由两名子女平分所得款项。哥哥聘请送达人，在叔叔位于 B 州的公寓向其亲手交付传票和起诉状副本。A 州法律规定，可通过“将传票和起诉状副本留给未成年人的父母或依法任命的监护人”向未成年人送达诉讼文书。B 州法律允许通过“将传票和起诉状副本留给未成年人的父母或依法任命的监护人、与未成年人同住的成年家庭成员，或已婚未成年人的成年配偶”向未成年人送达。妹妹的律师以诉讼文书送达不充分为由申请驳回诉讼。法院可能批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为哥哥遵守了 B 州诉讼文书送达法律。","B":"不会，因为联邦规则允许家庭成员代未成年人接收诉讼文书。","C":"会，因为不得在联邦法院起诉未成年人。","D":"会，因为哥哥未遵守 A 州诉讼文书送达法律。"},"explanationZh":"诉讼文书送达的特别规则：\n\n被告类型｜原告必须采取的送达方式\n无行为能力人或未成年人｜遵循实际送达地州的规则\n联邦政府／机构｜原告必须向以下各方交付传票和起诉状：\n• 诉讼提起地区的联邦检察官或其指定雇员；或者通过挂号或认证邮件寄给联邦检察官办公室的民事诉讼文书书记员；\n• 通过挂号或认证邮件寄给美国司法部长；并且\n• 通过挂号或认证邮件寄给其行为受质疑的机构或官员\n外国政府｜原告必须交付传票和起诉状：\n• 按原告与外国之间的特别安排；\n• 若无特别安排，按国际公约；\n• 若无公约，以要求签收回执的邮件寄给外交部长；或者\n• 若邮寄后 30 天内未收到通知，以要求签收回执的邮件向美国国务卿寄送两份\n州或地方政府｜原告必须选择：\n• 向行政首长交付通知；或\n• 遵循该政府所在地州的规则\n\n原告必须通过向被告适当送达传票和起诉状（即诉讼文书送达），使被告获得诉讼通知。与在美国境内、被诉至联邦法院的其他被告一样，未成年人或无行为能力被告也必须在提交起诉状后 90 天内获得送达。但是，根据 FRCP 4(g)，向未成年人或无行为能力被告送达时，必须遵循实际送达地州的诉讼文书送达规则。本题适用 B 州规则。\n\nB 州允许将诉讼文书留给“与未成年人同住的成年家庭成员”来向未成年人送达。本题中，哥哥的送达人在叔叔位于 B 州的公寓亲手向其交付传票和起诉状。由于 13 岁妹妹与该叔叔同住，诉讼文书送达充分。因此，法院很可能驳回妹妹的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 B）只有实际送达地州允许这种送达方式时，家庭成员才可代未成年人接收诉讼文书。例如本题 B 州允许，但 A 州不允许。\n\n（选项 C）可以在联邦法院起诉未成年人，FRCP 4 关于向未成年人送达的规定本身即预见了这种情形。\n\n（选项 D）必须依实际送达地州（B 州）的诉讼文书送达规则向未成年人送达，而非依提起诉讼州（A 州）的规则。\n\n学习目标：根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》4(g)，只能依实际送达地州的送达规则向未成年人或无行为能力被告送达诉讼文书。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(g)（向未成年人或无行为能力人送达）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-059","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":59,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A celebrity from State A sued a reporter from State B in a State A federal court for defamation, invoking diversity jurisdiction. The celebrity hired a 20-year-old process server to deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the reporter's home in State B. The process server went to the reporter's home and left the summons and complaint with the reporter's nephew, who had answered the door while staying at the reporter's home for a family reunion. The nephew is a 14-year-old honors student. He placed the documents on the reporter's kitchen table, but the reporter did not discover the documents until two weeks later. The reporter immediately filed a motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process. Assume that both states' requirements for service of process are identical to the requirements of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the nephew was of suitable age and discretion.","B":"No, because the reporter did not comply with the service-waiver provisions.","C":"Yes, because the nephew did not reside in the reporter's home.","D":"Yes, because the process server was not of suitable age to deliver process."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Serving process on defendant-individual\n\n(FRCP 4(e))\n\nType of service Requirements\n\nState rules Follow rules of state where court sits or service is made\n\nActual delivery Deliver process to defendant personally\n\nSubstituted Deliver process to: delivery\n• defendant's agent authorized by appointment or law to receive process or\n• resident of defendant's dwelling who is of suitable age & discretion FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA defendant must receive notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of the summons and complaint. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4, a plaintiff can properly serve a defendant who is an individual located in the United States by:\n• following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized to receive process) or\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion—ie, old enough to possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation.\n\nHere, the service rules for State A (where the court sits) and State B (where service was attempted) are identical to FRCP 4. The summons and complaint were left with the reporter's nephew, but there is no indication that he was the reporter's authorized agent. And though the nephew was likely of suitable age (14 years old) and discretion (honors student), he was merely a guest (not a resident) at the reporter's home (Choice A). Therefore, the motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process should be granted. (Choice B) A plaintiff can request that the defendant waive service in exchange for more time (60 days) to respond to the complaint. But since the celebrity did not request a waiver, the reporter did not need to comply with the waiver requirements. (Choice D) FRCP 4 allows process to be served by any person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (as seen here).*\n\n*The process server (other than a U.S. or deputy marshal) must then file a proof-of-service affidavit if service was made in the U.S. and not waived. In contrast, proof of service in a foreign country can be made pursuant to an applicable treaty or convention, by a receipt signed by the addressee, or by other evidence that satisfies the court. However, providing proof of service does not affect the validity of service. Educational objective: A plaintiff can serve an individual defendant located in the U.S. by (1) following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made, (2) having process delivered to the defendant personally (or his/her authorized agent), or (3) having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on an individual in the United States).","rule":"A plaintiff can serve an individual defendant located in the U.S. by (1) following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made, (2) having process delivered to the defendant personally (or his/her authorized agent), or (3) having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion.","irac":{"issue":"A celebrity from State A sued a reporter from State B in a State A federal court for defamation, invoking diversity jurisdiction. The celebrity hired a 20-year-old process server to deliver a copy of the summons and complaint to the reporter's home in State B. The process server went to the reporter's home and left the summons and complaint with the reporter's nephew, who had answered the door while staying at the reporter's home for a family reunion. The nephew is a 14-year-old honors student. He placed the documents on the reporter's kitchen table, but the reporter did not discover the documents until two weeks later. The reporter immediately filed a motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process. Assume that both states' requirements for service of process are identical to the requirements of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"A plaintiff can serve an individual defendant located in the U.S. by (1) following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made, (2) having process delivered to the defendant personally (or his/her authorized agent), or (3) having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion.","analysis":"Serving process on defendant-individual\n\n(FRCP 4(e))\n\nType of service Requirements\n\nState rules Follow rules of state where court sits or service is made\n\nActual delivery Deliver process to defendant personally\n\nSubstituted Deliver process to: delivery\n• defendant's agent authorized by appointment or law to receive process or\n• resident of defendant's dwelling who is of suitable age & discretion FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA defendant must receive notice of a lawsuit through service of process—ie, delivery of the summons and complaint. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4, a plaintiff can properly serve a defendant who is an individual located in the United States by:\n• following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized to receive process) or\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion—ie, old enough to possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation.\n\nHere, the service rules for State A (where the court sits) and State B (where service was attempted) are identical to FRCP 4. The summons and complaint were left with the reporter's nephew, but there is no indication that he was the reporter's authorized agent. And though the nephew was likely of suitable age (14 years old) and discretion (honors student), he was merely a guest (not a resident) at the reporter's home (Choice A). Therefore, the motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process should be granted. (Choice B) A plaintiff can request that the defendant waive service in exchange for more time (60 days) to respond to the complaint. But since the celebrity did not request a waiver, the reporter did not need to comply with the waiver requirements. (Choice D) FRCP 4 allows process to be served by any person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (as seen here).*\n\n*The process server (other than a U.S. or deputy marshal) must then file a proof-of-service affidavit if service was made in the U.S. and not waived. In contrast, proof of service in a foreign country can be made pursuant to an applicable treaty or convention, by a receipt signed by the addressee, or by other evidence that satisfies the court. However, providing proof of service does not affect the validity of service. Educational objective: A plaintiff can serve an individual defendant located in the U.S. by (1) following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made, (2) having process delivered to the defendant personally (or his/her authorized agent), or (3) having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on an individual in the United States).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on an individual in the United States).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[178],"sourceAnswerPages":[179,180],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名名人在 A 州联邦法院依据多元州籍管辖权，以诽谤为由起诉 B 州一名记者。名人聘请一名 20 岁的送达人，将传票和起诉状副本送至记者位于 B 州的住宅。送达人前往记者住宅，把传票和起诉状留给前来开门的记者外甥；外甥当时因家庭聚会暂住在记者家中。外甥是一名 14 岁的优等生。他把文件放在记者厨房桌上，但记者两周后才发现。记者随即以诉讼文书送达不充分为由申请驳回。假设两州的诉讼文书送达要求均与《联邦民事诉讼规则》相同。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为外甥的年龄和判断能力适当。","B":"不应，因为记者未遵守放弃送达的规定。","C":"应当，因为外甥并不居住在记者家中。","D":"应当，因为送达人的年龄不足以交付诉讼文书。"},"explanationZh":"向作为被告的个人送达诉讼文书（FRCP 4(e)）：\n\n送达类型｜要求\n州规则｜遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州的规则\n亲自交付｜将诉讼文书亲自交付被告\n替代交付｜将诉讼文书交付给：依指定或法律获授权接收诉讼文书的被告代理人；或居住于被告住所、年龄和判断能力适当的人\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n被告必须通过诉讼文书送达——即交付传票和起诉状——获得诉讼通知。根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）4，可通过以下任一方式向位于美国境内的个人被告适当送达：\n• 遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州的规则；\n• 将诉讼文书亲自交给被告（或获授权接收诉讼文书的代理人）；或\n• 将诉讼文书送至被告住所，留给居住于该住所且年龄和判断能力适当的人——即年龄足以具备理解该情形所需有限能力的人。\n\n本题中，法院所在的 A 州和尝试送达的 B 州之送达规则均与 FRCP 4 相同。传票和起诉状虽留给记者外甥，但没有迹象表明外甥是记者的授权代理人。外甥很可能年龄适当（14 岁）且具有适当判断能力（优等生），但他只是记者住宅的访客，并非居民（选项 A）。因此，应批准以诉讼文书送达不充分为由提出的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 B）原告可以请求被告放弃送达，作为交换，被告获得更长的答辩时间（60 天）。但名人没有提出放弃请求，所以记者无需遵守放弃送达要求。\n\n（选项 D）FRCP 4 允许任何年满 18 岁且不是诉讼当事人的人送达诉讼文书，本题即如此。*\n\n*若在美国境内送达且未放弃送达，送达人（美国法警或副法警除外）之后必须提交送达证明宣誓书。相比之下，在外国的送达证明可以依据适用条约或公约、收件人签署的收据或法院认可的其他证据作出。但是，是否提供送达证明不影响送达本身的效力。\n\n学习目标：原告可以通过以下方式向位于美国境内的个人被告送达：(1) 遵循法院所在地州或实际送达地州的规则；(2) 将诉讼文书亲自交付被告或其授权代理人；或 (3) 将诉讼文书送至被告住所，留给居住于该住所、年龄和判断能力适当的人。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e)（向美国境内个人送达诉讼文书）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-060","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":60,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A computer company, incorporated in State A and with its principal place of business in State B, manufactured a laptop that would overheat if left on for over 24 hours. A college student purchased this laptop in State B, where she resided. While at her apartment, the student left the laptop on for over 24 hours. The laptop exploded, causing her to sustain severe injuries. After recuperating from her injuries, the student moved to State C and sued the computer company in State C federal court to recover $200,000 for her injuries. The computer company filed a motion to transfer the lawsuit to the State B federal court on the ground that State C is an improper venue. How will the State C federal court likely rule on the computer company's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the court only has the power to dismiss the student's lawsuit.","B":"Deny the motion, because venue is proper in State C.","C":"Grant the motion, because the doctrine of forum non conveniens allows for such a transfer.","D":"Grant the motion, because venue is improper in State C."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Venue\n\nProper venue Case may be brought in any federal district where:\n\n(28 U.S.C. § 1391) • any defendant resides, if defendants all reside in same state\n• substantial part of events giving rise to claim occurred or property at issue is located\n• any defendant is subject to court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies) Transfer from proper When venue is proper, district court may transfer case to any venue other proper venue for convenience of parties/witnesses & in interest of justice (28 U.S.C. § 1404)\n\nTransfer or dismissal When venue is improper, district court must either: from improper venue\n• dismiss case (28 U.S.C. § 1406) • transfer case to proper venue (if interest of justice requires) Venue—ie, the geographic location of the specific federal district in which suit may be brought—is proper in any federal district where:\n• any defendant resides—eg, where a company is incorporated or maintains its principal place of business—so long as all the defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred or the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies).\n\nHere, the computer company resides in State A (state of incorporation) and State B (principal place of business)—not State C. And the events giving rise to the suit—the computer purchase and the explosion—occurred only in State B. Therefore, venue is improper in State C (Choice B). When venue is improper, the federal court must either (1) dismiss the lawsuit or (2) transfer it to a federal court with proper venue (Choice A). Federal courts prefer transfer over dismissal for judicial efficiency. Therefore, the State C federal court will likely grant the computer company's motion to transfer the student's lawsuit to the State B federal court—where venue is proper.\n\n(Choice C) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a lawsuit—even when venue is proper in that court—if a state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear the dispute (not seen here). Educational objective: A federal court with improper venue must either transfer the suit to a federal court with proper venue (preferred) or dismiss the case. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406 (transfer when venue is improper).","rule":"A federal court with improper venue must either transfer the suit to a federal court with proper venue (preferred) or dismiss the case.","irac":{"issue":"A computer company, incorporated in State A and with its principal place of business in State B, manufactured a laptop that would overheat if left on for over 24 hours. A college student purchased this laptop in State B, where she resided. While at her apartment, the student left the laptop on for over 24 hours. The laptop exploded, causing her to sustain severe injuries. After recuperating from her injuries, the student moved to State C and sued the computer company in State C federal court to recover $200,000 for her injuries. The computer company filed a motion to transfer the lawsuit to the State B federal court on the ground that State C is an improper venue. How will the State C federal court likely rule on the computer company's motion?","rule":"A federal court with improper venue must either transfer the suit to a federal court with proper venue (preferred) or dismiss the case.","analysis":"Venue\n\nProper venue Case may be brought in any federal district where:\n\n(28 U.S.C. § 1391) • any defendant resides, if defendants all reside in same state\n• substantial part of events giving rise to claim occurred or property at issue is located\n• any defendant is subject to court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies) Transfer from proper When venue is proper, district court may transfer case to any venue other proper venue for convenience of parties/witnesses & in interest of justice (28 U.S.C. § 1404)\n\nTransfer or dismissal When venue is improper, district court must either: from improper venue\n• dismiss case (28 U.S.C. § 1406) • transfer case to proper venue (if interest of justice requires) Venue—ie, the geographic location of the specific federal district in which suit may be brought—is proper in any federal district where:\n• any defendant resides—eg, where a company is incorporated or maintains its principal place of business—so long as all the defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred or the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies).\n\nHere, the computer company resides in State A (state of incorporation) and State B (principal place of business)—not State C. And the events giving rise to the suit—the computer purchase and the explosion—occurred only in State B. Therefore, venue is improper in State C (Choice B). When venue is improper, the federal court must either (1) dismiss the lawsuit or (2) transfer it to a federal court with proper venue (Choice A). Federal courts prefer transfer over dismissal for judicial efficiency. Therefore, the State C federal court will likely grant the computer company's motion to transfer the student's lawsuit to the State B federal court—where venue is proper.\n\n(Choice C) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a lawsuit—even when venue is proper in that court—if a state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear the dispute (not seen here). Educational objective: A federal court with improper venue must either transfer the suit to a federal court with proper venue (preferred) or dismiss the case. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406 (transfer when venue is improper).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406 (transfer when venue is improper).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[181],"sourceAnswerPages":[182,183],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立、主要营业地位于 B 州的计算机公司，制造了一款在连续开机超过 24 小时后会过热的笔记本电脑。一名大学生在其居住的 B 州购买该电脑。学生在公寓里让电脑连续开机超过 24 小时，电脑发生爆炸，致其严重受伤。伤愈后，学生迁往 C 州，并在 C 州联邦法院起诉计算机公司，请求就伤害获得 200,000 美元赔偿。计算机公司以 C 州并非适当审判地为由，申请将诉讼转移至 B 州联邦法院。C 州联邦法院可能如何裁定公司的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为法院只有权驳回学生的诉讼。","B":"驳回动议，因为 C 州审判地适当。","C":"批准动议，因为不方便法院原则允许此种转移。","D":"批准动议，因为 C 州审判地不当。"},"explanationZh":"审判地：\n\n适当审判地（28 U.S.C. § 1391）｜案件可在下列任何联邦地区提起：\n• 任何被告居住的地区，但全部被告必须居住于同一州；\n• 引发请求的大部分事件发生地或争议财产所在地；\n• 任何被告受该法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在前述规定均不适用时。\n\n从适当审判地转移（28 U.S.C. § 1404）｜审判地适当时，为便利当事人／证人及司法公正，地区法院可以将案件转移至其他适当审判地。\n\n从不当审判地转移或驳回（28 U.S.C. § 1406）｜审判地不当时，地区法院必须：\n• 驳回案件；或\n• 若司法公正需要，将案件转移至适当审判地。\n\n审判地是可以提起诉讼的具体联邦地区之地理位置。下列任何联邦地区均为适当审判地：\n• 任何被告居住的地区——例如公司注册成立地或主要营业地——但全部被告必须居住于同一州；\n• 引发诉讼的大部分事件发生地或争议财产所在地；或\n• 任何被告受该法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在上述规定均不适用时。\n\n本题中，计算机公司居住于 A 州（注册成立州）和 B 州（主要营业地），而非 C 州。且引发诉讼的事件——购买电脑及电脑爆炸——仅发生于 B 州。因此，C 州审判地不当（选项 B）。审判地不当时，联邦法院必须：(1) 驳回诉讼；或 (2) 将其转移至审判地适当的联邦法院（选项 A）。为提高司法效率，联邦法院通常更倾向于转移而非驳回。因此，C 州联邦法院很可能批准计算机公司的动议，将学生的诉讼转移至审判地适当的 B 州联邦法院。\n\n（选项 C）不方便法院原则允许联邦法院在州或外国司法制度更适合审理争议时，即使本法院审判地适当，也驳回或中止诉讼。本题不属于这种情形。\n\n学习目标：审判地不当的联邦法院必须把诉讼转移至审判地适当的联邦法院（优先做法），或者驳回案件。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391（审判地）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406（审判地不当时的转移）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-061","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":61,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A driver from the Western District of State A was involved in a traffic collision in the Northern District of State B with a truck owned and operated by a shipping company incorporated in the Eastern District of State B. The shipping company does the bulk of its business in the Southern District of State B, where its headquarters are located. The employee who drove the truck was terminated by the shipping company. The employee is a resident of State C, which has one federal judicial district. If the driver files a joint complaint against the shipping company and its former employee, in which federal judicial district(s) would venue be proper?","choices":{"A":"The Western District of State A.","B":"The Northern District of State B.","C":"The Western District of State A and the Northern District of State B.","D":"The Eastern District of State B, the Southern District of State B, and the District of State C."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Venue refers to the federal judicial districts where a case may be heard and can be established in the following ways:\n• Residency-based venue – a district where any defendant resides, if all of the defendants reside in the same state\n\n• Events-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred\n\n• Property-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the property at issue is located (not seen here)\n\n• Fallback provision – a district where any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (only applies if none of the above provisions can be established)\n\nFor venue purposes, a corporate defendant (eg, the shipping company) is a resident of any judicial district where it is subject to personal jurisdiction—eg, where it is incorporated or maintains its headquarters. Here, venue is improper in the Western District of State A (where the driver resides) because venue is never based on the plaintiff's residence (Choices A & C). And residency-based venue does not apply here because the defendants are residents of different states— the company is incorporated and headquartered in State B, while the former employee is a resident of State C (Choice D). However, events-based venue is always available, so venue is proper in the Northern District of State B—where the accident occurred. Educational objective: Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides, as long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred, (3) the property at issue is located, or (4) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if venue does not arise under the preceding provisions. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","rule":"Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides, as long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred, (3) the property at issue is located, or (4) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if venue does not arise under the preceding provisions.","irac":{"issue":"A driver from the Western District of State A was involved in a traffic collision in the Northern District of State B with a truck owned and operated by a shipping company incorporated in the Eastern District of State B. The shipping company does the bulk of its business in the Southern District of State B, where its headquarters are located. The employee who drove the truck was terminated by the shipping company. The employee is a resident of State C, which has one federal judicial district. If the driver files a joint complaint against the shipping company and its former employee, in which federal judicial district(s) would venue be proper?","rule":"Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides, as long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred, (3) the property at issue is located, or (4) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if venue does not arise under the preceding provisions.","analysis":"Venue refers to the federal judicial districts where a case may be heard and can be established in the following ways:\n• Residency-based venue – a district where any defendant resides, if all of the defendants reside in the same state\n\n• Events-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred\n\n• Property-based venue – a district where a substantial part of the property at issue is located (not seen here)\n\n• Fallback provision – a district where any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (only applies if none of the above provisions can be established)\n\nFor venue purposes, a corporate defendant (eg, the shipping company) is a resident of any judicial district where it is subject to personal jurisdiction—eg, where it is incorporated or maintains its headquarters. Here, venue is improper in the Western District of State A (where the driver resides) because venue is never based on the plaintiff's residence (Choices A & C). And residency-based venue does not apply here because the defendants are residents of different states— the company is incorporated and headquartered in State B, while the former employee is a resident of State C (Choice D). However, events-based venue is always available, so venue is proper in the Northern District of State B—where the accident occurred. Educational objective: Venue is proper in any federal district where (1) any defendant resides, as long as all defendants reside in the same state, (2) a substantial part of the events occurred, (3) the property at issue is located, or (4) any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction—but only if venue does not arise under the preceding provisions. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b) (proper venue).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[184],"sourceAnswerPages":[185],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州西区一名驾驶员，在 B 州北区与一辆卡车发生交通碰撞。该卡车由一家在 B 州东区注册成立的运输公司所有并运营。运输公司的大部分业务在 B 州南区开展，其总部也位于该区。驾驶卡车的雇员已被运输公司解雇。该雇员现居住于仅设一个联邦司法区的 C 州。若驾驶员共同起诉运输公司及其前雇员，哪个或哪些联邦司法区的审判地适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"A 州西区。","B":"B 州北区。","C":"A 州西区和 B 州北区。","D":"B 州东区、B 州南区及 C 州区。"},"explanationZh":"审判地是可以审理案件的联邦司法区，可通过以下方式建立：\n• 基于居住地的审判地——任何被告居住的地区，但全部被告必须居住于同一州；\n• 基于事件的审判地——引发诉讼的大部分事件发生地所在地区；\n• 基于财产的审判地——争议财产的大部分所在地所在地区（本题不存在）；\n• 兜底规定——任何被告受该法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在上述规定均无法建立审判地时适用。\n\n就审判地而言，作为被告的公司（例如运输公司）在其受对人管辖的每一司法区均视为居民，例如其注册成立地或总部所在地。A 州西区是驾驶员居住地，但审判地绝不以原告居住地为基础，故该区审判地不当（选项 A、C）。\n\n本题也不适用基于居住地的审判地，因为两名被告居住于不同州：运输公司在 B 州注册并设总部，前雇员则居住于 C 州（选项 D）。但是，基于事件的审判地始终可以适用，因此事故发生地 B 州北区是适当审判地。\n\n学习目标：下列任何联邦地区均为适当审判地：(1) 任何被告居住的地区，但全部被告必须居住于同一州；(2) 大部分事件发生地；(3) 争议财产所在地；或 (4) 任何被告受对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在前述规定均不适用时。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391(b)（适当审判地）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-015"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-062","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":62,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"An attorney filed a class action in federal court on behalf of thousands of individuals against a cell phone company that is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State C. The two named plaintiffs are citizens of State A and State B, respectively, and the unnamed plaintiffs reside in every other state. The complaint alleges that the company violated a law enacted by states nationwide by overcharging its customers $3.50 per month for the past 20 months. The damages on behalf of the entire class are alleged to be $10 million. The cell phone company moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Will the federal court likely grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over class actions.","B":"No, because the amount in controversy is sufficient in the aggregate.","C":"Yes, because at least one of the unnamed plaintiffs is not diverse from the company.","D":"Yes, because the amount in controversy is insufficient individually."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction in class actions\n\nType Requirements\n\nFederal question • Class action arises under U.S. Constitution, treaty, or federal law Diversity • Amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000* and\n• named opposing parties are citizens of different states Class Action Fairness • Class contains ≥ 100 members Act • at least one class member & one defendant are diverse and\n• amount in controversy for aggregated claims exceeds $5 million *A federal court can acquire subject-matter jurisdiction over class members' claims that do not exceed $75,000 through supplemental jurisdiction.\n\nA federal district court's subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action, like any other lawsuit, can arise from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the class action arises from the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not state law, as seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the named opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, no single plaintiff can individually satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement because the complaint alleges that the cell phone company overcharged each plaintiff only $70 ($3.50 × 20 months). And individual claims generally cannot be aggregated (ie, added together) unless an alternate basis of subject-matter jurisdiction over the class action exists. The Class Action Fairness Act (CAFA) provides such a basis when:\n• the class contains at least 100 members\n• any class member is diverse from at least one defendant (ie, minimal diversity) and\n• the amount in controversy exceeds $5 million when the members' claims are aggregated. Here, the class contains over 100 members. And since unnamed plaintiffs live in each state, at least one member of that class is diverse from the defendant cell phone company (Choice C). Furthermore, the amount in controversy—based on the class members' aggregated\n\ndamages—is $10 million. Therefore, the CAFA does apply, and the court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction (Choice D). (Choice A) Federal courts do not have exclusive jurisdiction over class actions. The reason is that state and federal courts have concurrent jurisdiction over claims arising from state or federal law unless the Constitution or Congress expressly states otherwise (not seen here). Educational objective: The Class Action Fairness Act gives a federal court an alternative basis for subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the class contains 100 members, (2) at least one class member is diverse from at least one defendant, and (3) the amount in controversy of the aggregated claims exceeds $5 million. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction and the Class Action Fairness Act).","rule":"The Class Action Fairness Act gives a federal court an alternative basis for subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the class contains 100 members, (2) at least one class member is diverse from at least one defendant, and (3) the amount in controversy of the aggregated claims exceeds $5 million.","irac":{"issue":"An attorney filed a class action in federal court on behalf of thousands of individuals against a cell phone company that is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State C. The two named plaintiffs are citizens of State A and State B, respectively, and the unnamed plaintiffs reside in every other state. The complaint alleges that the company violated a law enacted by states nationwide by overcharging its customers $3.50 per month for the past 20 months. The damages on behalf of the entire class are alleged to be $10 million. The cell phone company moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Will the federal court likely grant the motion?","rule":"The Class Action Fairness Act gives a federal court an alternative basis for subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the class contains 100 members, (2) at least one class member is diverse from at least one defendant, and (3) the amount in controversy of the aggregated claims exceeds $5 million.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction in class actions\n\nType Requirements\n\nFederal question • Class action arises under U.S. Constitution, treaty, or federal law Diversity • Amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000* and\n• named opposing parties are citizens of different states Class Action Fairness • Class contains ≥ 100 members Act • at least one class member & one defendant are diverse and\n• amount in controversy for aggregated claims exceeds $5 million *A federal court can acquire subject-matter jurisdiction over class members' claims that do not exceed $75,000 through supplemental jurisdiction.\n\nA federal district court's subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action, like any other lawsuit, can arise from either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the class action arises from the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not state law, as seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the named opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy for any named plaintiff's claim exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, no single plaintiff can individually satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement because the complaint alleges that the cell phone company overcharged each plaintiff only $70 ($3.50 × 20 months). And individual claims generally cannot be aggregated (ie, added together) unless an alternate basis of subject-matter jurisdiction over the class action exists. The Class Action Fairness Act (CAFA) provides such a basis when:\n• the class contains at least 100 members\n• any class member is diverse from at least one defendant (ie, minimal diversity) and\n• the amount in controversy exceeds $5 million when the members' claims are aggregated. Here, the class contains over 100 members. And since unnamed plaintiffs live in each state, at least one member of that class is diverse from the defendant cell phone company (Choice C). Furthermore, the amount in controversy—based on the class members' aggregated\n\ndamages—is $10 million. Therefore, the CAFA does apply, and the court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction (Choice D). (Choice A) Federal courts do not have exclusive jurisdiction over class actions. The reason is that state and federal courts have concurrent jurisdiction over claims arising from state or federal law unless the Constitution or Congress expressly states otherwise (not seen here). Educational objective: The Class Action Fairness Act gives a federal court an alternative basis for subject-matter jurisdiction over a class action when (1) the class contains 100 members, (2) at least one class member is diverse from at least one defendant, and (3) the amount in controversy of the aggregated claims exceeds $5 million. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction and the Class Action Fairness Act).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1331 (federal-question jurisdiction).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction and the Class Action Fairness Act).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[187],"sourceAnswerPages":[188,189],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名律师代表数千名个人，在联邦法院对一家在 C 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 C 州的手机公司提起集体诉讼。两名具名原告分别为 A 州和 B 州公民，未具名原告则居住于其他每个州。起诉状主张，公司违反由全国各州制定的一项法律，在过去 20 个月中每月向客户多收 3.50 美元。起诉状代表整个集体主张 1,000 万美元损害赔偿。手机公司以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。联邦法院可能批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦法院对集体诉讼享有专属管辖权。","B":"不会，因为合并计算后的争议金额足够。","C":"会，因为至少一名未具名原告与公司州籍相同。","D":"会，因为各人单独计算的争议金额不足。"},"explanationZh":"集体诉讼中的事项管辖权：\n\n类型｜要求\n联邦问题｜集体诉讼依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生\n多元州籍｜任一具名原告请求的争议金额超过 75,000 美元*；且具名对立双方是不同州的公民\n《集体诉讼公平法》（CAFA）｜集体至少包含 100 名成员；至少一名集体成员与一名被告州籍不同；且合并计算后的争议金额超过 500 万美元\n\n*联邦法院可以通过补充管辖权，对请求金额不超过 75,000 美元的集体成员请求取得事项管辖权。\n\n与其他诉讼一样，联邦地区法院对集体诉讼的事项管辖权可源于：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——集体诉讼依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生，而非本题中的州法；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——具名对立双方是不同州的公民，且任一具名原告请求的争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n本题没有任何一名原告能单独满足争议金额要求，因为起诉状主张手机公司仅向每名原告多收 70 美元（3.50 美元 × 20 个月）。除非存在对集体诉讼行使事项管辖权的其他依据，否则通常不能合并计算各项个人请求。《集体诉讼公平法》（CAFA）在符合以下条件时提供这种依据：\n• 集体至少包含 100 名成员；\n• 任一集体成员与至少一名被告州籍不同，即最低限度州籍多元性；并且\n• 合并计算集体成员的请求后，争议金额超过 500 万美元。\n\n本题集体成员超过 100 人。由于未具名原告居住于每个州，集体中至少一名成员与作为被告的手机公司州籍不同（选项 C）。此外，以集体成员的损害赔偿合并计算，争议金额为 1,000 万美元。因此，CAFA 适用，法院很可能驳回公司以缺乏事项管辖权提出的驳回动议（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 A）联邦法院对集体诉讼并不享有专属管辖权。除非《宪法》或国会另有明文规定（本题没有），州法院和联邦法院对依据州法或联邦法产生的请求享有并行管辖权。\n\n学习目标：《集体诉讼公平法》在以下条件下为联邦法院提供对集体诉讼行使事项管辖权的替代依据：(1) 集体包含 100 名成员；(2) 至少一名集体成员与至少一名被告州籍不同；且 (3) 合并计算后的请求争议金额超过 500 万美元。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1331（联邦问题管辖权）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权及《集体诉讼公平法》）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-063","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":63,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A woman, domiciled in State A, entered into a contract with an art dealer, domiciled in State B, to purchase several pieces of artwork for the woman's office in State C. The parties negotiated and signed the contract in State C. The contract required the dealer to deliver the artwork to the woman's office on a specified date. The woman paid the dealer the contract price. When the specified date arrived, the dealer refused to deliver the artwork because he wanted to keep the pieces for himself. He also refused to return the woman's money. The dealer stored the artwork in a warehouse he owned in State A and returned home. The woman sued the dealer in a federal district court in State A, seeking a declaratory judgment that she lawfully owns the artwork. The dealer filed an answer and asserted a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the dealer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the artwork is located in State A.","B":"No, because the dealer waived this defense by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion.","C":"Yes, because the contract was negotiated, signed, and allegedly breached in State C.","D":"Yes, because the dealer is domiciled in State B."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction is a court's authority over the parties or property before it. The exercise of personal jurisdiction over all actions—in rem, quasi in rem, and in personam— must comport with due process. This occurs when the plaintiff's claim arises from the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. A quasi in rem type 1 action is brought directly against an individual to determine the named parties' interests in property located in the forum state. This includes a request for a declaratory judgment that the plaintiff, not the defendant, owns personal property in the forum state. These actions typically comport with due process because:\n• minimum contacts almost always exist since the action relates to the defendant's interest in property in the forum state* (eg, the artwork in State A) and\n• exercising jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state. As a result, due process is satisfied in this case and the court has jurisdiction over the dealer. The dealer's motion to dismiss should therefore be denied. *Minimum contacts almost always exist for in rem actions as well. These actions are brought directly against property to determine all persons' interests in that property (eg, condemnation). (Choice B) A challenge to personal jurisdiction is waived if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. Here, the dealer preserved this defense by asserting it in his answer since no pre-answer motion had been filed. (Choices C & D) Personal jurisdiction over a defendant can exist in multiple jurisdictions. Although personal jurisdiction over the dealer likely exists in State C (where the contract was negotiated, signed, and breached) and State B (where the dealer is domiciled), personal jurisdiction in State A is still valid. Educational objective: In a quasi in rem type 1 action, minimum contacts almost always exist because the action relates to the defendant's interest in property in the forum state. And the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state. References\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977) (stating that a court generally has personal jurisdiction when property in the forum state is the source of the underlying controversy between the plaintiff and the defendant).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining in personam, in rem, and quasi in rem actions).","rule":"In a quasi in rem type 1 action, minimum contacts almost always exist because the action relates to the defendant's interest in property in the forum state. And the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state.","irac":{"issue":"A woman, domiciled in State A, entered into a contract with an art dealer, domiciled in State B, to purchase several pieces of artwork for the woman's office in State C. The parties negotiated and signed the contract in State C. The contract required the dealer to deliver the artwork to the woman's office on a specified date. The woman paid the dealer the contract price. When the specified date arrived, the dealer refused to deliver the artwork because he wanted to keep the pieces for himself. He also refused to return the woman's money. The dealer stored the artwork in a warehouse he owned in State A and returned home. The woman sued the dealer in a federal district court in State A, seeking a declaratory judgment that she lawfully owns the artwork. The dealer filed an answer and asserted a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the dealer's motion?","rule":"In a quasi in rem type 1 action, minimum contacts almost always exist because the action relates to the defendant's interest in property in the forum state. And the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction is a court's authority over the parties or property before it. The exercise of personal jurisdiction over all actions—in rem, quasi in rem, and in personam— must comport with due process. This occurs when the plaintiff's claim arises from the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. A quasi in rem type 1 action is brought directly against an individual to determine the named parties' interests in property located in the forum state. This includes a request for a declaratory judgment that the plaintiff, not the defendant, owns personal property in the forum state. These actions typically comport with due process because:\n• minimum contacts almost always exist since the action relates to the defendant's interest in property in the forum state* (eg, the artwork in State A) and\n• exercising jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state. As a result, due process is satisfied in this case and the court has jurisdiction over the dealer. The dealer's motion to dismiss should therefore be denied. *Minimum contacts almost always exist for in rem actions as well. These actions are brought directly against property to determine all persons' interests in that property (eg, condemnation). (Choice B) A challenge to personal jurisdiction is waived if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. Here, the dealer preserved this defense by asserting it in his answer since no pre-answer motion had been filed. (Choices C & D) Personal jurisdiction over a defendant can exist in multiple jurisdictions. Although personal jurisdiction over the dealer likely exists in State C (where the contract was negotiated, signed, and breached) and State B (where the dealer is domiciled), personal jurisdiction in State A is still valid. Educational objective: In a quasi in rem type 1 action, minimum contacts almost always exist because the action relates to the defendant's interest in property in the forum state. And the exercise of personal jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state. References\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977) (stating that a court generally has personal jurisdiction when property in the forum state is the source of the underlying controversy between the plaintiff and the defendant).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining in personam, in rem, and quasi in rem actions).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977) (stating that a court generally has personal jurisdiction when property in the forum state is the source of the underlying controversy between the plaintiff and the defendant).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining in personam, in rem, and quasi in rem actions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[190],"sourceAnswerPages":[191,192],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名以 A 州为住所的女子，与一名以 B 州为住所的艺术品经销商订立合同，购买数件艺术品用于女子位于 C 州的办公室。双方在 C 州协商并签署合同。合同要求经销商在指定日期将艺术品交付女子办公室。女子已向经销商支付合同价款。指定日期到来时，经销商因想自己保留艺术品而拒绝交付，也拒绝返还女子的款项。经销商将艺术品存放在其位于 A 州的一间仓库后返回家中。女子在 A 州联邦地区法院起诉经销商，请求作出宣告判决，确认她依法拥有艺术品。经销商提交答辩状，并以缺乏对人管辖权为由提出驳回动议。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为艺术品位于 A 州。","B":"不应，因为经销商未在答辩前动议中提出该抗辩，已予放弃。","C":"应当，因为合同在 C 州协商、签署并据称被违反。","D":"应当，因为经销商的住所位于 B 州。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权是法院对其面前当事人或财产行使权力的权限。无论是对物诉讼、准对物诉讼还是对人诉讼，法院行使对人管辖权都必须符合正当程序。当原告请求源于被告与法院州的最低限度联系，且行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念时，即符合正当程序。\n\n第一类准对物诉讼直接针对个人提起，旨在确定具名当事人对位于法院州财产的权益。其中包括请求作出宣告判决，确认原告而非被告拥有法院州内的动产。此类诉讼通常符合正当程序，因为：\n• 诉讼涉及被告对法院州内财产的权益，所以几乎总是存在最低限度联系*，例如本题位于 A 州的艺术品；并且\n• 只要原告不是以欺诈方式将财产带入法院州，行使管辖权即符合公平处理和实质正义。\n\n因此，本案满足正当程序，法院对经销商享有管辖权。故应驳回经销商的驳回动议。\n\n*对物诉讼也几乎总存在最低限度联系。此类诉讼直接针对财产提起，以确定所有人对该财产的权益，例如征收。\n\n（选项 B）若未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出对人管辖权异议，该抗辩即被放弃。本题中，此前并未提出答辩前动议，经销商在答辩状中提出该抗辩，故已妥善保留。\n\n（选项 C、D）多个司法辖区均可能对同一被告享有对人管辖权。尽管 C 州（合同协商、签署及被违反之地）和 B 州（经销商住所地）很可能都对经销商享有对人管辖权，A 州的对人管辖权仍然有效。\n\n学习目标：在第一类准对物诉讼中，诉讼涉及被告对法院州内财产的权益，故几乎总是存在最低限度联系。只要原告不是以欺诈方式将财产带入法院州，行使对人管辖权也符合公平处理和实质正义。\n\n参考资料：\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977)（说明法院州内财产构成原被告之间基础争议来源时，法院通常享有对人管辖权）。\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（解释对人、对物及准对物诉讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-021"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-064","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":64,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"Two individuals, both domiciled in State A, created an unincorporated nonprofit organization and promised investors that their monetary contributions would be donated to charities focused on ending world hunger. The contributions were instead used to purchase several properties in State B. The individuals have never physically traveled to and have no other connections with State B, as the properties are approximately 100 miles away from State A. The federal government filed a civil forfeiture action in a federal district court in State B to seize the properties located there. After being properly served with notice and the complaint, the individuals filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because a court where the property is being seized always has jurisdiction over the civil forfeiture action.","B":"No, because the action is related to the properties located in State B.","C":"Yes, because it would be unfair to hale the individuals to court in State B.","D":"Yes, because the individuals lack minimum contacts with State B."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction refers to a court's authority over the parties or property in the action before it. There are three types of actions that can be asserted: in rem, quasi in rem, and in personam (see above image). Regardless of the type of action, a federal court's exercise of personal jurisdiction must comport with due process. This constitutional requirement is satisfied when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. Civil forfeiture is an in rem action initiated by the government to seize property used in illegal activity. For in rem actions, minimum contacts almost always exist since these actions relate to property located in the forum state (Choice D). Therefore, the federal court has personal jurisdiction over the individuals' properties in State B and should deny their motion to dismiss. (Choice A) A court where the property is being seized may not have jurisdiction over the civil forfeiture action in the rare instance that the exercise of jurisdiction does not comply with fair play and substantial justice. This issue typically arises if the seized property was fraudulently brought to the forum state (not seen here). (Choice C) In civil forfeiture and other in rem actions, the property itself is the defendant. As a result, minimum contacts for personal jurisdiction depend on the location of the property and its relation to the action. Jurisdiction does not depend on the individuals' domicile or their minimum contacts with the forum state (eg, whether it would be unfair to hale them to court in the state). Educational objective: A court almost always has personal jurisdiction in an in rem action (eg, civil forfeiture action). Minimum contacts exist because an in rem action relates to property in the forum state. And exercising jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state. References\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977) (stating that a court generally has personal jurisdiction when property in the forum state is the source of the underlying controversy between the plaintiff and the defendant).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining in personam, in rem, and quasi in rem actions).","rule":"A court almost always has personal jurisdiction in an in rem action (eg, civil forfeiture action). Minimum contacts exist because an in rem action relates to property in the forum state. And exercising jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state.","irac":{"issue":"Two individuals, both domiciled in State A, created an unincorporated nonprofit organization and promised investors that their monetary contributions would be donated to charities focused on ending world hunger. The contributions were instead used to purchase several properties in State B. The individuals have never physically traveled to and have no other connections with State B, as the properties are approximately 100 miles away from State A. The federal government filed a civil forfeiture action in a federal district court in State B to seize the properties located there. After being properly served with notice and the complaint, the individuals filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the motion to dismiss?","rule":"A court almost always has personal jurisdiction in an in rem action (eg, civil forfeiture action). Minimum contacts exist because an in rem action relates to property in the forum state. And exercising jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction refers to a court's authority over the parties or property in the action before it. There are three types of actions that can be asserted: in rem, quasi in rem, and in personam (see above image). Regardless of the type of action, a federal court's exercise of personal jurisdiction must comport with due process. This constitutional requirement is satisfied when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. Civil forfeiture is an in rem action initiated by the government to seize property used in illegal activity. For in rem actions, minimum contacts almost always exist since these actions relate to property located in the forum state (Choice D). Therefore, the federal court has personal jurisdiction over the individuals' properties in State B and should deny their motion to dismiss. (Choice A) A court where the property is being seized may not have jurisdiction over the civil forfeiture action in the rare instance that the exercise of jurisdiction does not comply with fair play and substantial justice. This issue typically arises if the seized property was fraudulently brought to the forum state (not seen here). (Choice C) In civil forfeiture and other in rem actions, the property itself is the defendant. As a result, minimum contacts for personal jurisdiction depend on the location of the property and its relation to the action. Jurisdiction does not depend on the individuals' domicile or their minimum contacts with the forum state (eg, whether it would be unfair to hale them to court in the state). Educational objective: A court almost always has personal jurisdiction in an in rem action (eg, civil forfeiture action). Minimum contacts exist because an in rem action relates to property in the forum state. And exercising jurisdiction complies with fair play and substantial justice so long as the plaintiff did not fraudulently bring the property into the forum state. References\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977) (stating that a court generally has personal jurisdiction when property in the forum state is the source of the underlying controversy between the plaintiff and the defendant).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining in personam, in rem, and quasi in rem actions).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977) (stating that a court generally has personal jurisdiction when property in the forum state is the source of the underlying controversy between the plaintiff and the defendant).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining in personam, in rem, and quasi in rem actions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[193],"sourceAnswerPages":[194],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"两名个人均以 A 州为住所，他们创设一家非公司制非营利组织，并向投资者承诺，其金钱出资将捐给致力于消除世界饥饿的慈善机构。但这些出资却被用于购买 B 州的数处不动产。两人从未亲自前往 B 州，与 B 州也没有其他联系；这些不动产距 A 州约 100 英里。联邦政府在 B 州联邦地区法院提起民事没收诉讼，请求扣押位于该州的不动产。两人适当收到通知和起诉状送达后，以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为扣押财产所在地法院始终对民事没收诉讼享有管辖权。","B":"不应，因为诉讼与位于 B 州的不动产有关。","C":"应当，因为要求两人在 B 州出庭并不公平。","D":"应当，因为两人与 B 州缺乏最低限度联系。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权是法院对其面前诉讼中的当事人或财产行使权力的权限。可以提起三类诉讼：对物诉讼、准对物诉讼和对人诉讼（见上图）。无论属于哪类，联邦法院行使对人管辖权都必须符合正当程序。满足以下条件时，即符合该宪法要求：\n• 原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系；并且\n• 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念。\n\n民事没收是政府为扣押用于违法活动的财产而提起的对物诉讼。在对物诉讼中，因诉讼与位于法院州的财产有关，几乎总是存在最低限度联系（选项 D）。因此，联邦法院对两人在 B 州的不动产享有对人管辖权，应驳回其驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）在极少数情况下，扣押财产所在地法院可能不对民事没收诉讼享有管辖权，即行使管辖权不符合公平处理和实质正义时。该问题通常发生在被扣押财产被以欺诈方式带入法院州时，本题并非如此。\n\n（选项 C）在民事没收及其他对物诉讼中，财产本身即为被告。因此，对人管辖权所要求的最低限度联系取决于财产所在地及财产与诉讼的关系，而不取决于个人住所或其与法院州的最低限度联系，例如要求他们到该州出庭是否不公平。\n\n学习目标：法院在对物诉讼（例如民事没收诉讼）中几乎总享有对人管辖权。由于对物诉讼涉及法院州内财产，因此存在最低限度联系；只要原告不是以欺诈方式将财产带入法院州，行使管辖权也符合公平处理和实质正义。\n\n参考资料：\n• Shaffer v. Heitner, 433 U.S. 186, 207–08 (1977)（说明法院州内财产构成原被告之间基础争议来源时，法院通常享有对人管辖权）。\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 5–6 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（解释对人、对物及准对物诉讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-021"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-065","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":65,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A husband and wife were walking in their neighborhood when an out-of-state driver failed to stop at a crosswalk and struck the wife. The husband, who had jumped out of the way of the vehicle, and the driver got into an argument, which resulted in the driver punching the husband in the face. The wife brought a negligence action against the driver in federal court to recover $185,000 for her injuries. The husband joined his wife's suit and asserted a battery claim against the driver to recover $5,000 in damages he suffered from being punched. The driver files a pre-answer motion to dismiss the husband's claim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the driver's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court has absolute discretion to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the husband's claim.","B":"No, because the husband's claim arises out of the same common nucleus of operative facts as his wife's claim.","C":"Yes, because the husband's claim does not meet the amount-in-controversy requirement.","D":"Yes, because the husband's claim would contaminate diversity of citizenship."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, a federal court's authority to hear a type of dispute— exists when a dispute satisfies either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, the couple and the driver live in different states, but only the wife's $185,000 claim meets the amount-in-controversy requirement. Therefore, the federal court has diversity jurisdiction over the wife's claim—but not the husband's claim. However, when a dispute involves multiple claims—and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction (as seen here)—the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear the remaining claims. This is generally permitted when these supplemental claims share a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. But when the original claim is based solely on diversity jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted if a supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship or\n\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention (Choice A).\n\nHere, the couple's claims arise from the same failure to stop. The wife's claim is based on diversity jurisdiction, but the husband's claim would not contaminate diversity of citizenship since he and the driver are from different states (Choice D). And though the husband's claim seeks only $5,000, it is asserted against the original defendant and the husband permissibly joined the suit. Therefore, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over his claim, and the driver's motion to dismiss should be denied (Choice C). Educational objective: Supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted when an original claim satisfies diversity jurisdiction and another claim (1) would contaminate diversity of citizenship or (2) seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff against a joining, intervening, or impleaded party OR a plaintiff seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","rule":"Supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted when an original claim satisfies diversity jurisdiction and another claim (1) would contaminate diversity of citizenship or (2) seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff against a joining, intervening, or impleaded party OR a plaintiff seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention.","irac":{"issue":"A husband and wife were walking in their neighborhood when an out-of-state driver failed to stop at a crosswalk and struck the wife. The husband, who had jumped out of the way of the vehicle, and the driver got into an argument, which resulted in the driver punching the husband in the face. The wife brought a negligence action against the driver in federal court to recover $185,000 for her injuries. The husband joined his wife's suit and asserted a battery claim against the driver to recover $5,000 in damages he suffered from being punched. The driver files a pre-answer motion to dismiss the husband's claim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the driver's motion?","rule":"Supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted when an original claim satisfies diversity jurisdiction and another claim (1) would contaminate diversity of citizenship or (2) seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff against a joining, intervening, or impleaded party OR a plaintiff seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention.","analysis":"Subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, a federal court's authority to hear a type of dispute— exists when a dispute satisfies either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, the couple and the driver live in different states, but only the wife's $185,000 claim meets the amount-in-controversy requirement. Therefore, the federal court has diversity jurisdiction over the wife's claim—but not the husband's claim. However, when a dispute involves multiple claims—and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction (as seen here)—the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear the remaining claims. This is generally permitted when these supplemental claims share a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. But when the original claim is based solely on diversity jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted if a supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship or\n\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention (Choice A).\n\nHere, the couple's claims arise from the same failure to stop. The wife's claim is based on diversity jurisdiction, but the husband's claim would not contaminate diversity of citizenship since he and the driver are from different states (Choice D). And though the husband's claim seeks only $5,000, it is asserted against the original defendant and the husband permissibly joined the suit. Therefore, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over his claim, and the driver's motion to dismiss should be denied (Choice C). Educational objective: Supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted when an original claim satisfies diversity jurisdiction and another claim (1) would contaminate diversity of citizenship or (2) seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff against a joining, intervening, or impleaded party OR a plaintiff seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[196],"sourceAnswerPages":[197,198],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一对夫妻在住所附近步行时，一名外州驾驶员未在人行横道停车，撞到妻子。丈夫及时跳开躲过车辆，随后与驾驶员发生争执，驾驶员一拳打在丈夫脸上。妻子在联邦法院对驾驶员提起过失诉讼，请求就其伤害获得 185,000 美元赔偿。丈夫加入妻子的诉讼，并对驾驶员提出殴打请求，就挨打造成的损害请求 5,000 美元赔偿。驾驶员在答辩前以缺乏事项管辖权为由，申请驳回丈夫的请求。法院是否应批准驾驶员的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为法院对是否就丈夫的请求行使补充管辖权享有绝对酌情权。","B":"不应，因为丈夫的请求与妻子的请求源于同一共同核心争议事实。","C":"应当，因为丈夫的请求不满足争议金额要求。","D":"应当，因为丈夫的请求会破坏州籍多元性。"},"explanationZh":"争议符合以下任一条件时，存在事项管辖权——即联邦法院审理某类争议的权限：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——争议依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方是不同州的公民，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n本题中，夫妻与驾驶员居住于不同州，但只有妻子的 185,000 美元请求满足争议金额要求。因此，联邦法院对妻子的请求享有多元州籍管辖权，但对丈夫的请求不享有原始管辖权。\n\n但是，当争议涉及多项请求，而只有部分请求属于法院的原始事项管辖权时（本题即如此），法院可以行使补充管辖权，审理其余请求。通常，只要这些补充请求与原始事项管辖权范围内的请求共享同一核心争议事实，即可行使补充管辖权。但原始请求仅基于多元州籍管辖权时，若补充请求符合以下任一情形，则不得行使补充管辖权：\n• 会破坏州籍多元性；或\n• 请求金额为 75,000 美元或以下，且由一名原告：(1) 针对通过当事人合并、介入或第三方诉讼加入的当事人提出；或者 (2) 该原告寻求通过强制合并或介入加入诉讼（选项 A）。\n\n本题中，夫妻二人的请求均源于驾驶员同一次未停车行为。妻子的请求基于多元州籍管辖权，而丈夫与驾驶员来自不同州，所以丈夫的请求不会破坏州籍多元性（选项 D）。虽然丈夫仅请求 5,000 美元，但请求针对原始被告，且丈夫是通过任意合并适当加入诉讼。因此，法院对其请求享有补充管辖权，应驳回驾驶员的驳回动议（选项 C）。\n\n学习目标：当原始请求满足多元州籍管辖权时，若另一请求：(1) 会破坏州籍多元性；或 (2) 金额为 75,000 美元或以下，且由原告针对通过合并、介入或第三方诉讼加入的当事人提出，或者由寻求通过强制合并或介入加入诉讼的原告提出，则不得行使补充管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367（补充管辖权）。\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 557–60 (2005)（解释补充管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-008"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-066","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":66,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A poet living in State A sued a publisher from State B in a federal court in State B for failing to pay the poet $150,000 in royalties. The publisher joined an agent to the suit through third-party practice, claiming that the agent sold the poems to the publisher without disclosing that the poet was the true author. The federal court in State B is located within 50 miles of the State C border. The agent was served with process at her home in State C, which is located 75 miles from the federal court. The agent filed a motion to dismiss the publisher's third-party complaint for lack of personal jurisdiction. State B has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction over out-of-state defendants that are served with process within its borders. Is the court likely to grant the agent's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because a court does not need personal jurisdiction over an impleaded party.","B":"No, because the court has personal jurisdiction over the agent.","C":"Yes, because personal jurisdiction cannot be established through the State B long-arm statute.","D":"Yes, because the publisher must file a separate lawsuit against the agent in State C."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Due process requires that a court have personal jurisdiction over all the parties in a dispute (Choice A). Federal courts have the same jurisdictional reach as the courts of the state in which they sit. Additionally, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4(k) provides a special rule—the \"100-mile bulge rule\"—for federal courts to acquire personal jurisdiction over a party:\n• added to the suit through impleader (ie, third-party practice) or required joinder and\n\n• served with process within 100 miles of the federal court where the suit is pending—even if the party is served in another state.*\n\nHere, the publisher joined the agent to the suit through third-party practice. The agent was served with process at her home in State C, which is 75 miles from the federal court in State B. As a result, the \"100-mile bulge rule\" establishes personal jurisdiction over the agent, and the court will likely deny her motion to dismiss. *FRCP 4(k) contains another special rule granting a federal court personal jurisdiction over a defendant served with process outside the forum state when (1) the plaintiff's claim arises from federal law, (2) the defendant is not subject to personal jurisdiction in any state, and (3) the exercise of jurisdiction complies with the U.S. Constitution and federal laws. (Choice C) A long-arm statute is a state statute that gives courts within the state (including federal courts) personal jurisdiction over nonresident defendants. But federal courts can also establish personal jurisdiction in other ways—eg, through the \"100-mile bulge rule\" (as seen here). (Choice D) Since the federal court in State B has personal jurisdiction over the agent through the 100-mile bulge rule, the publisher need not file a separate suit in State C. Educational objective: The \"100-mile bulge rule\" establishes personal jurisdiction over a party (1) added to the suit through impleader or required joinder and (2) served with process within 100 miles of the federal court where the suit is pending. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(B) (100-mile bulge rule).","rule":"The \"100-mile bulge rule\" establishes personal jurisdiction over a party (1) added to the suit through impleader or required joinder and (2) served with process within 100 miles of the federal court where the suit is pending.","irac":{"issue":"A poet living in State A sued a publisher from State B in a federal court in State B for failing to pay the poet $150,000 in royalties. The publisher joined an agent to the suit through third-party practice, claiming that the agent sold the poems to the publisher without disclosing that the poet was the true author. The federal court in State B is located within 50 miles of the State C border. The agent was served with process at her home in State C, which is located 75 miles from the federal court. The agent filed a motion to dismiss the publisher's third-party complaint for lack of personal jurisdiction. State B has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction over out-of-state defendants that are served with process within its borders. Is the court likely to grant the agent's motion to dismiss?","rule":"The \"100-mile bulge rule\" establishes personal jurisdiction over a party (1) added to the suit through impleader or required joinder and (2) served with process within 100 miles of the federal court where the suit is pending.","analysis":"Due process requires that a court have personal jurisdiction over all the parties in a dispute (Choice A). Federal courts have the same jurisdictional reach as the courts of the state in which they sit. Additionally, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 4(k) provides a special rule—the \"100-mile bulge rule\"—for federal courts to acquire personal jurisdiction over a party:\n• added to the suit through impleader (ie, third-party practice) or required joinder and\n\n• served with process within 100 miles of the federal court where the suit is pending—even if the party is served in another state.*\n\nHere, the publisher joined the agent to the suit through third-party practice. The agent was served with process at her home in State C, which is 75 miles from the federal court in State B. As a result, the \"100-mile bulge rule\" establishes personal jurisdiction over the agent, and the court will likely deny her motion to dismiss. *FRCP 4(k) contains another special rule granting a federal court personal jurisdiction over a defendant served with process outside the forum state when (1) the plaintiff's claim arises from federal law, (2) the defendant is not subject to personal jurisdiction in any state, and (3) the exercise of jurisdiction complies with the U.S. Constitution and federal laws. (Choice C) A long-arm statute is a state statute that gives courts within the state (including federal courts) personal jurisdiction over nonresident defendants. But federal courts can also establish personal jurisdiction in other ways—eg, through the \"100-mile bulge rule\" (as seen here). (Choice D) Since the federal court in State B has personal jurisdiction over the agent through the 100-mile bulge rule, the publisher need not file a separate suit in State C. Educational objective: The \"100-mile bulge rule\" establishes personal jurisdiction over a party (1) added to the suit through impleader or required joinder and (2) served with process within 100 miles of the federal court where the suit is pending. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(B) (100-mile bulge rule).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(B) (100-mile bulge rule).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[199],"sourceAnswerPages":[200],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"居住于 A 州的一名诗人，在 B 州联邦法院起诉来自 B 州的一家出版社，主张出版社未向其支付 150,000 美元版税。出版社通过第三方诉讼将一名代理人加入案件，主张代理人将诗歌出售给出版社时，没有披露诗人才是真正作者。B 州联邦法院距 C 州边界不到 50 英里。代理人在其位于 C 州的住宅获得诉讼文书送达，该住宅距联邦法院 75 英里。代理人以缺乏对人管辖权为由，申请驳回出版社的第三方起诉状。B 州长臂法规定，其法院对在 B 州境内获得诉讼文书送达的州外被告享有对人管辖权。法院可能批准代理人的驳回动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法院无需对通过第三方诉讼加入的当事人享有对人管辖权。","B":"不会，因为法院对代理人享有对人管辖权。","C":"会，因为无法通过 B 州长臂法建立对人管辖权。","D":"会，因为出版社必须在 C 州对代理人另行提起诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"正当程序要求法院对争议中的所有当事人享有对人管辖权（选项 A）。联邦法院的管辖范围与其所在州法院相同。此外，《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）4(k) 规定一项特别规则——“百英里扩张规则”，联邦法院可据此对符合以下条件的当事人取得对人管辖权：\n• 通过第三方诉讼或必要当事人合并加入诉讼；并且\n• 在诉讼待决的联邦法院 100 英里范围内获得诉讼文书送达，即使送达发生于另一州。*\n\n本题中，出版社通过第三方诉讼将代理人加入案件。代理人在 C 州住宅获得诉讼文书送达，该住宅距 B 州联邦法院 75 英里。因此，“百英里扩张规则”建立了法院对代理人的对人管辖权，法院很可能驳回其驳回动议。\n\n*FRCP 4(k) 还包含另一项特别规则：当 (1) 原告请求依据联邦法律产生；(2) 被告不受任何州法院对人管辖权约束；且 (3) 行使管辖权符合《美国宪法》和联邦法律时，联邦法院对在法院州外获得诉讼文书送达的被告享有对人管辖权。\n\n（选项 C）长臂法是赋予州内法院（包括联邦法院）对非居民被告享有对人管辖权的州法律。但是，联邦法院也可以通过其他方式建立对人管辖权，例如本题的“百英里扩张规则”。\n\n（选项 D）B 州联邦法院依据百英里扩张规则对代理人享有对人管辖权，所以出版社无需在 C 州另行起诉。\n\n学习目标：“百英里扩张规则”使联邦法院对符合以下条件的当事人享有对人管辖权：(1) 该当事人通过第三方诉讼或必要当事人合并加入案件；且 (2) 在诉讼待决联邦法院 100 英里范围内获得诉讼文书送达。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(k)(1)(B)（百英里扩张规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-027"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-067","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":67,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A taxpayer alleged that she overpaid her federal taxes and attempted on numerous occasions to get a refund from the Internal Revenue Service (IRS). An IRS agent refused to grant the taxpayer a refund without auditing her tax returns. The taxpayer, after exhausting all administrative remedies, sued the agent in his individual capacity in federal court to recover damages pursuant to a federal statute permitting this type of action. The agent was personally served with process at his residence 30 days after the complaint was filed. The United States government also received process. Can the agent successfully argue that service of process was improper?","choices":{"A":"No, because the agent was personally served at his residence.","B":"No, because the taxpayer still had time to cure any defect with service of process.","C":"Yes, because the agent, as a government employee, cannot be served with process at his residence.","D":"Yes, because the taxpayer should have served the IRS instead of the agent."},"answer":"A","explanation":"In most lawsuits, only the defendant(s) named in the plaintiff's complaint need to be served with process. However, FRCP 4(i) provides special service rules when the plaintiff sues a federal government officer or employee for actions related to his/her government duties. The requisite service of process depends on whether the officer or employee is sued in an:\n• official capacity – when recovery is sought from the U.S. government's treasury or\n• individual capacity – when recovery is sought from the officer's or employee's own pocket. When the officer or employee is sued in an individual capacity, the officer or employee must be served with process pursuant to the rules for serving an individual—eg, personal delivery of process. If these rules are not followed, then the action should be dismissed without prejudice. Process must also be served on the U.S. government. If it is not, then the court must allow the plaintiff a reasonable time to cure that failure. Here, the taxpayer sued the IRS agent in his individual capacity for actions related to his government employment. The agent was properly served with process since it was personally delivered to him at his residence (Choice C). And since the taxpayer also served the U.S. government, the agent cannot successfully argue that service of process was improper. (Choice B) Process was properly served here. But had the taxpayer failed to serve the U.S. government, she would have had a reasonable amount of time to cure that failure—not any defect with service of process. (Choice D) A federal agency must be served with process when the agency is sued or a lawsuit challenges the agency's orders—not when, as here, the agency's officer or employee is sued in an individual capacity. Educational objective: A federal officer or employee sued in an individual capacity for actions related to his/her government duties must be served with process pursuant to the rules for serving an individual. Process must also be served on the U.S. government, but if the plaintiff fails to do so, the court must allow a reasonable time to cure that failure. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i)(2)–(3) (service of process on federal officer or employee).\n\n• Kentucky v. Graham, 473 U.S. 159, 165–66 (1985) (explaining the difference between suing a federal employee in his/her individual and official capacities).","rule":"A federal officer or employee sued in an individual capacity for actions related to his/her government duties must be served with process pursuant to the rules for serving an individual. Process must also be served on the U.S. government, but if the plaintiff fails to do so, the court must allow a reasonable time to cure that failure. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A taxpayer alleged that she overpaid her federal taxes and attempted on numerous occasions to get a refund from the Internal Revenue Service (IRS). An IRS agent refused to grant the taxpayer a refund without auditing her tax returns. The taxpayer, after exhausting all administrative remedies, sued the agent in his individual capacity in federal court to recover damages pursuant to a federal statute permitting this type of action. The agent was personally served with process at his residence 30 days after the complaint was filed. The United States government also received process. Can the agent successfully argue that service of process was improper?","rule":"A federal officer or employee sued in an individual capacity for actions related to his/her government duties must be served with process pursuant to the rules for serving an individual. Process must also be served on the U.S. government, but if the plaintiff fails to do so, the court must allow a reasonable time to cure that failure. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"In most lawsuits, only the defendant(s) named in the plaintiff's complaint need to be served with process. However, FRCP 4(i) provides special service rules when the plaintiff sues a federal government officer or employee for actions related to his/her government duties. The requisite service of process depends on whether the officer or employee is sued in an:\n• official capacity – when recovery is sought from the U.S. government's treasury or\n• individual capacity – when recovery is sought from the officer's or employee's own pocket. When the officer or employee is sued in an individual capacity, the officer or employee must be served with process pursuant to the rules for serving an individual—eg, personal delivery of process. If these rules are not followed, then the action should be dismissed without prejudice. Process must also be served on the U.S. government. If it is not, then the court must allow the plaintiff a reasonable time to cure that failure. Here, the taxpayer sued the IRS agent in his individual capacity for actions related to his government employment. The agent was properly served with process since it was personally delivered to him at his residence (Choice C). And since the taxpayer also served the U.S. government, the agent cannot successfully argue that service of process was improper. (Choice B) Process was properly served here. But had the taxpayer failed to serve the U.S. government, she would have had a reasonable amount of time to cure that failure—not any defect with service of process. (Choice D) A federal agency must be served with process when the agency is sued or a lawsuit challenges the agency's orders—not when, as here, the agency's officer or employee is sued in an individual capacity. Educational objective: A federal officer or employee sued in an individual capacity for actions related to his/her government duties must be served with process pursuant to the rules for serving an individual. Process must also be served on the U.S. government, but if the plaintiff fails to do so, the court must allow a reasonable time to cure that failure. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i)(2)–(3) (service of process on federal officer or employee).\n\n• Kentucky v. Graham, 473 U.S. 159, 165–66 (1985) (explaining the difference between suing a federal employee in his/her individual and official capacities).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i)(2)–(3) (service of process on federal officer or employee).\n\n• Kentucky v. Graham, 473 U.S. 159, 165–66 (1985) (explaining the difference between suing a federal employee in his/her individual and official capacities).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[202],"sourceAnswerPages":[203,204],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名纳税人主张自己多缴了联邦税款，并多次试图从美国国税局（IRS）取得退税。一名 IRS 工作人员拒绝在不审计其纳税申报表的情况下给予退税。穷尽所有行政救济后，纳税人依据允许此类诉讼的一项联邦法律，在联邦法院以该工作人员个人身份为被告提起诉讼，请求获得损害赔偿。起诉状提交 30 天后，工作人员在其住所亲自获得诉讼文书送达。美国政府也收到诉讼文书送达。该工作人员能否成功主张诉讼文书送达不当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为工作人员已在其住所获得亲自送达。","B":"不能，因为纳税人仍有时间补正任何送达瑕疵。","C":"能，因为工作人员作为政府雇员，不能在其住所获得诉讼文书送达。","D":"能，因为纳税人应向 IRS 而非工作人员送达。"},"explanationZh":"在大多数诉讼中，只有原告起诉状所列的被告必须获得诉讼文书送达。但是，当原告就联邦政府官员或雇员与其政府职务有关的行为起诉时，FRCP 4(i) 规定了特别送达规则。所需送达取决于该官员或雇员是以何种身份被诉：\n• 公务身份——请求从美国政府国库获得赔偿；或\n• 个人身份——请求从该官员或雇员个人财产获得赔偿。\n\n官员或雇员以个人身份被诉时，必须依向个人送达的规则向其送达，例如亲自交付诉讼文书。若未遵循这些规则，诉讼应无损害驳回。还必须向美国政府送达诉讼文书；若未送达，法院必须给予原告合理时间补正。\n\n本题中，纳税人以 IRS 工作人员个人身份，就其与政府职务相关的行为起诉。诉讼文书已在工作人员住所亲自交付给本人，故送达适当（选项 C）。纳税人也已向美国政府送达，所以工作人员不能成功主张诉讼文书送达不当。\n\n（选项 B）本题送达本就适当。但如果纳税人未向美国政府送达，她会获得合理时间补正该项遗漏，而不是任何一种诉讼文书送达瑕疵。\n\n（选项 D）联邦机构本身被诉，或诉讼质疑该机构命令时，必须向该联邦机构送达；本题则是以该机构官员或雇员个人身份为被告，并非这种情形。\n\n学习目标：就与政府职务有关的行为以个人身份起诉联邦官员或雇员时，必须依据向个人送达的规则向其送达诉讼文书。还必须向美国政府送达；若原告未做到，法院必须给予合理时间补正。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(i)(2)–(3)（向联邦官员或雇员送达诉讼文书）。\n• Kentucky v. Graham, 473 U.S. 159, 165–66 (1985)（解释以个人身份与公务身份起诉联邦雇员的区别）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-028"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-068","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":68,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A man domiciled in State A sued a corporation in a federal court in State A for violating a federal law that prohibits collaboration with terror organizations. The man alleged that the corporation's South American subsidiary provided funds to a notorious terror group that carried out an attack in South America, resulting in the deaths of the man's parents. The man claims that the corporation is vicariously liable for the subsidiary's actions. The corporation is incorporated and has its headquarters in Europe. The corporation also has a United States subsidiary that maintains an office in State A. The corporation has moved to dismiss the lawsuit for lack of personal jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the corporation's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because general jurisdiction exists in State A.","B":"Deny the motion, because specific jurisdiction exists in State A.","C":"Grant the motion, because foreign corporations are never subject to personal jurisdiction in the United States.","D":"Grant the motion, because neither specific nor general jurisdiction exists in State A."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Courts must have personal jurisdiction over the parties to satisfy the constitutional guarantee of due process. Personal jurisdiction can be established through a defendant's consent or by serving process while the defendant is physically present in the state where the court is located (ie, the forum state)—not seen here. Personal jurisdiction can also be established through:\n• specific jurisdiction – when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state such that the exercise of jurisdiction would not offend notions of fair play and substantial justice or\n\n• general jurisdiction – when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" there (ie, the defendant's domicile).\n\nA corporation is domiciled in the state where it is incorporated and where it maintains its principal place of business (eg, its headquarters). However, the location and activities of a corporation's subsidiary have no effect on its domicile. Here, the State A federal court lacks specific jurisdiction since the claim arises from and is closely related to the corporation's contacts with South America—where its subsidiary allegedly funded the terror group and the attack occurred (Choice B). The State A federal court also lacks general jurisdiction because the corporation is domiciled in Europe—where it is incorporated and maintains its headquarters (Choice A). Therefore, the court will likely grant the corporation's motion to dismiss. (Choice C) Foreign corporations can be subject to personal jurisdiction in the United States—eg, through consent or minimum contacts. Educational objective: General jurisdiction exists when a defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" there. A corporation is at home (ie, domiciled) in the state where it is incorporated and maintains its principal place of business—not where its subsidiary has an office. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Daimler AG v. Bauman, 571 U.S. 117, 138–39 (2014) (holding that a subsidiary's activities do not subject a parent corporation to general jurisdiction).","rule":"General jurisdiction exists when a defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" there. A corporation is at home (ie, domiciled) in the state where it is incorporated and maintains its principal place of business—not where its subsidiary has an office.","irac":{"issue":"A man domiciled in State A sued a corporation in a federal court in State A for violating a federal law that prohibits collaboration with terror organizations. The man alleged that the corporation's South American subsidiary provided funds to a notorious terror group that carried out an attack in South America, resulting in the deaths of the man's parents. The man claims that the corporation is vicariously liable for the subsidiary's actions. The corporation is incorporated and has its headquarters in Europe. The corporation also has a United States subsidiary that maintains an office in State A. The corporation has moved to dismiss the lawsuit for lack of personal jurisdiction. How will the court likely rule on the corporation's motion to dismiss?","rule":"General jurisdiction exists when a defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" there. A corporation is at home (ie, domiciled) in the state where it is incorporated and maintains its principal place of business—not where its subsidiary has an office.","analysis":"Courts must have personal jurisdiction over the parties to satisfy the constitutional guarantee of due process. Personal jurisdiction can be established through a defendant's consent or by serving process while the defendant is physically present in the state where the court is located (ie, the forum state)—not seen here. Personal jurisdiction can also be established through:\n• specific jurisdiction – when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state such that the exercise of jurisdiction would not offend notions of fair play and substantial justice or\n\n• general jurisdiction – when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" there (ie, the defendant's domicile).\n\nA corporation is domiciled in the state where it is incorporated and where it maintains its principal place of business (eg, its headquarters). However, the location and activities of a corporation's subsidiary have no effect on its domicile. Here, the State A federal court lacks specific jurisdiction since the claim arises from and is closely related to the corporation's contacts with South America—where its subsidiary allegedly funded the terror group and the attack occurred (Choice B). The State A federal court also lacks general jurisdiction because the corporation is domiciled in Europe—where it is incorporated and maintains its headquarters (Choice A). Therefore, the court will likely grant the corporation's motion to dismiss. (Choice C) Foreign corporations can be subject to personal jurisdiction in the United States—eg, through consent or minimum contacts. Educational objective: General jurisdiction exists when a defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" there. A corporation is at home (ie, domiciled) in the state where it is incorporated and maintains its principal place of business—not where its subsidiary has an office. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Daimler AG v. Bauman, 571 U.S. 117, 138–39 (2014) (holding that a subsidiary's activities do not subject a parent corporation to general jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the requirements for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Daimler AG v. Bauman, 571 U.S. 117, 138–39 (2014) (holding that a subsidiary's activities do not subject a parent corporation to general jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[205],"sourceAnswerPages":[206],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名以 A 州为住所的男子在 A 州联邦法院起诉一家公司，主张该公司违反禁止与恐怖组织合作的联邦法律。男子称，该公司的南美洲子公司向一个臭名昭著的恐怖组织提供资金，该组织随后在南美洲发动袭击，致其父母死亡。男子主张母公司应对子公司的行为承担替代责任。该公司在欧洲注册成立，总部也位于欧洲。该公司另有一家美国子公司，在 A 州设有办公室。公司以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回诉讼。法院可能如何裁定公司的驳回动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为 A 州存在一般管辖权。","B":"驳回动议，因为 A 州存在特定管辖权。","C":"批准动议，因为外国公司绝不会在美国受对人管辖权约束。","D":"批准动议，因为 A 州既不存在特定管辖权，也不存在一般管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"为满足宪法的正当程序保障，法院必须对当事人享有对人管辖权。对人管辖权可以通过被告同意，或被告身处法院所在州（即法院州）时向其送达诉讼文书而建立，本题均不存在。还可通过以下方式建立：\n• 特定管辖权——原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系，且行使管辖权不违反公平处理和实质正义观念；或\n• 一般管辖权——被告与法院州持续且系统的联系如此重大，以致被告实质上“安居于此”，即该州为被告住所。\n\n公司的住所是其注册成立州及主要营业地（例如总部）所在州。但是，子公司的所在地和活动不影响母公司的住所。\n\n本题中，A 州联邦法院缺乏特定管辖权，因为请求源于并密切关联于公司与南美洲的联系——其子公司据称在那里资助恐怖组织，袭击也发生在那里——而非与 A 州的联系（选项 B）。A 州联邦法院同样缺乏一般管辖权，因为公司住所位于其注册成立和设总部的欧洲（选项 A）。因此，法院很可能批准公司的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 C）外国公司可能受美国法院对人管辖权约束，例如通过同意或最低限度联系。\n\n学习目标：被告与法院州持续且系统的联系如此重大，以致实质上“安居于此”时，存在一般管辖权。公司的住所（即安居地）为其注册成立州及主要营业地所在州，而不是其子公司设有办公室的州。\n\n参考资料：\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017)（解释特定管辖权的要求）。\n• Daimler AG v. Bauman, 571 U.S. 117, 138–39 (2014)（认定子公司的活动不会使母公司受一般管辖权约束）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-004"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-069","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":69,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A custom car owner domiciled in State A agreed to drive across the country to State B to deliver a car to a buyer pursuant to the contract between the two parties. The buyer had agreed to purchase the car after seeing the owner's advertisement for buying and selling used, custom cars on a website that was accessible across the nation. As the owner drove toward State B, he got into a car accident in State C. The driver of the other car was domiciled in State D and was vacationing in State C. The owner did not suffer any physical harm, but the other driver was taken to the nearest hospital to be treated for her injuries. After being released from the hospital, the driver returned home to State D, where she continues to receive treatment for her injuries. Three months after the accident, the driver sued the owner in a federal district court in State D to recover $1 million for the personal injuries she suffered. After the owner was properly served with process in State A, he immediately moved to dismiss the suit for lack of personal jurisdiction. State D has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction to the full extent allowed under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. How should the federal court rule on the owner's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because it is foreseeable that vacationers from State D will drive in State C.","B":"Deny the motion, because the owner's website was accessible in State D.","C":"Grant the motion, because the claim does not arise from and is not closely related to the owner's minimum contacts with State D.","D":"Grant the motion, because the claim may only be brought in a court located in State C."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) A court must have personal jurisdiction—ie, authority over the parties (or property) before the court—to hear a case. A federal court has the same personal jurisdiction as the courts of the state in which it is located (ie, the forum state). If a defendant has not consented to a court's personal jurisdiction or been served with process within the forum state, personal jurisdiction must be established through:\n• general jurisdiction – when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, where a defendant is domiciled) or\n\n• specific jurisdiction – when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and the exercise of jurisdiction would comply with notions of fair play and substantial justice.\n\nHere, the federal court in State D lacks general jurisdiction over the owner since he is domiciled in State A. The court also lacks specific jurisdiction because the driver's claim arises from and is closely related to the owner's minimum contacts with State C (where the\n\naccident occurred)—not State D (the forum state). Therefore, the court should grant the owner's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) Whether it is foreseeable that drivers from other states (including State D) will drive in State C is irrelevant since personal jurisdiction turns on the defendant's contacts with the forum state—State D. (Choice B) The driver's claim arises out of the car accident in State C—not the owner's nationwide, online advertisement that was accessible in State D. (Choice D) A court in State C could have exercised specific jurisdiction over the claim since the accident occurred there. But a court in State A could also have exercised general jurisdiction since the owner is domiciled there. Educational objective: Specific personal jurisdiction exists when (1) the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and (2) the exercise of jurisdiction would comply with notions of fair play and substantial justice. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic contacts required for general jurisdiction).","rule":"Specific personal jurisdiction exists when (1) the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and (2) the exercise of jurisdiction would comply with notions of fair play and substantial justice.","irac":{"issue":"A custom car owner domiciled in State A agreed to drive across the country to State B to deliver a car to a buyer pursuant to the contract between the two parties. The buyer had agreed to purchase the car after seeing the owner's advertisement for buying and selling used, custom cars on a website that was accessible across the nation. As the owner drove toward State B, he got into a car accident in State C. The driver of the other car was domiciled in State D and was vacationing in State C. The owner did not suffer any physical harm, but the other driver was taken to the nearest hospital to be treated for her injuries. After being released from the hospital, the driver returned home to State D, where she continues to receive treatment for her injuries. Three months after the accident, the driver sued the owner in a federal district court in State D to recover $1 million for the personal injuries she suffered. After the owner was properly served with process in State A, he immediately moved to dismiss the suit for lack of personal jurisdiction. State D has a long-arm statute that gives its courts personal jurisdiction to the full extent allowed under the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. How should the federal court rule on the owner's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Specific personal jurisdiction exists when (1) the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and (2) the exercise of jurisdiction would comply with notions of fair play and substantial justice.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) A court must have personal jurisdiction—ie, authority over the parties (or property) before the court—to hear a case. A federal court has the same personal jurisdiction as the courts of the state in which it is located (ie, the forum state). If a defendant has not consented to a court's personal jurisdiction or been served with process within the forum state, personal jurisdiction must be established through:\n• general jurisdiction – when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, where a defendant is domiciled) or\n\n• specific jurisdiction – when the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and the exercise of jurisdiction would comply with notions of fair play and substantial justice.\n\nHere, the federal court in State D lacks general jurisdiction over the owner since he is domiciled in State A. The court also lacks specific jurisdiction because the driver's claim arises from and is closely related to the owner's minimum contacts with State C (where the\n\naccident occurred)—not State D (the forum state). Therefore, the court should grant the owner's motion to dismiss. (Choice A) Whether it is foreseeable that drivers from other states (including State D) will drive in State C is irrelevant since personal jurisdiction turns on the defendant's contacts with the forum state—State D. (Choice B) The driver's claim arises out of the car accident in State C—not the owner's nationwide, online advertisement that was accessible in State D. (Choice D) A court in State C could have exercised specific jurisdiction over the claim since the accident occurred there. But a court in State A could also have exercised general jurisdiction since the owner is domiciled there. Educational objective: Specific personal jurisdiction exists when (1) the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and (2) the exercise of jurisdiction would comply with notions of fair play and substantial justice. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic contacts required for general jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic contacts required for general jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[208],"sourceAnswerPages":[209,210],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名定制汽车车主以 A 州为住所。根据其与买方订立的合同，他同意驾车横跨全国前往 B 州，把汽车交付买方。买方是在一个全国均可访问的二手定制汽车买卖网站上看到车主广告后同意购买。车主驾车驶往 B 州途中，在 C 州发生交通事故。另一辆车的驾驶员以 D 州为住所，当时正在 C 州度假。车主未受身体伤害，另一名驾驶员则被送往最近的医院接受治疗。出院后，驾驶员返回 D 州家中，并继续在那里接受伤情治疗。事故发生三个月后，该驾驶员在 D 州联邦地区法院起诉车主，请求就其人身伤害获得 100 万美元赔偿。车主在 A 州适当获得诉讼文书送达后，立即以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。D 州长臂法赋予其法院在第十四修正案正当程序条款允许的全部范围内行使对人管辖权。联邦法院应如何裁定车主的驳回动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为可以预见 D 州度假者会在 C 州驾车。","B":"驳回动议，因为车主的网站在 D 州可以访问。","C":"批准动议，因为请求并非源于且不密切关联于车主与 D 州的最低限度联系。","D":"批准动议，因为该请求只能在 C 州法院提起。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权（法院对被告行使权力的权限）：\n\n类型｜要求\n诉讼文书送达｜被告自愿身处法院所在州（即法院州）时向其送达\n同意｜明示：合同约定，例如法院选择条款；默示：从事州具有重大监管利益的特定活动，例如在公共道路驾车；放弃：未及时对对人管辖权提出异议；出庭：自愿到庭就案件实体进行诉讼\n特定管辖权（与案件相关）｜原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系（即有目的利用）；且行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念\n一般管辖权（适用于所有请求）｜被告与法院州的联系持续且系统，并重大到使被告实质上“安居于此”，即该州为被告住所\n\n法院必须享有对人管辖权——即对其面前当事人或财产行使权力的权限——方可审理案件。联邦法院的对人管辖权范围与其所在州（法院州）法院相同。若被告没有同意法院管辖，也未在法院州境内获得诉讼文书送达，则必须通过以下方式建立对人管辖权：\n• 一般管辖权——被告与法院州存在持续、系统且如此重大的联系，以致其实质上“安居于此”，即被告住所所在地；或\n• 特定管辖权——原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系，且行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念。\n\n本题中，D 州联邦法院对车主缺乏一般管辖权，因为其住所位于 A 州。法院也缺乏特定管辖权，因为驾驶员的请求源于并密切关联于车主与 C 州的最低限度联系——事故发生地——而非与法院州 D 州的联系。因此，法院应批准车主的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）其他州（包括 D 州）的驾驶员可能在 C 州驾车是否可预见并不相关，因为对人管辖权取决于被告与法院州 D 州的联系。\n\n（选项 B）驾驶员的请求源于 C 州交通事故，而不是车主在 D 州也可访问的全国性网络广告。\n\n（选项 D）C 州法院因事故发生于该州，可以对请求行使特定管辖权；但 A 州法院也可因车主以 A 州为住所而行使一般管辖权。\n\n学习目标：符合以下条件时存在特定对人管辖权：(1) 原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系；且 (2) 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念。\n\n参考资料：\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017)（解释特定管辖权所需的最低限度联系）。\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011)（讨论一般管辖权所需的持续、系统联系）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-070","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":70,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A woman domiciled in a foreign country brought a wrongful discharge action in federal court in State A against her former employer. The employer is incorporated in State A and headquartered in State B. The woman had worked at the employer's office in the foreign country. Her on-site manager, who had sole discretion over personnel matters, had made the decision to discharge her. The manager has now retired and continues to live in the foreign country. Under the employment laws of the foreign country, if the woman succeeds in her action, her damages would be limited to two years' salary. If the woman succeeds in her action under State A employment law, her damages could be much greater and could include emotional distress and punitive damages. The employer filed a motion to dismiss the action for insufficient service of process, which the court denied. The employer then moved to dismiss the action for forum non conveniens. Is the court likely to dismiss the action for forum non conveniens?","choices":{"A":"No, because the employer waived its right to challenge the forum by failing to include that challenge in its first motion to dismiss.","B":"No, because the remedies available to the woman under the foreign country's laws are less favorable than those available under State A law.","C":"Yes, because the foreign country is the more appropriate forum given that the discharge occurred there and the evidence is located there.","D":"Yes, because the woman is a foreign citizen and therefore her choice of forum is not entitled to deference."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Doctrine of forum non conveniens\n\nDefinition • Power of federal court to dismiss or stay lawsuit when state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear case Private interest • Accessibility of evidence factors • Availability of witnesses\n• Burden on defendant\n• Enforceability of judgment Public interest • Ability to comprehend substantive law factors • Choice of law\n• Interest of jury/community\n• Functionality of foreign judicial system The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign judicial system provides a more convenient forum to hear the case. In making this determination, the federal court must weigh several private and public interest factors (see table above)—none of which are determinative on their own. Here, one factor that weighs against dismissing the woman's action is that the remedies available under the foreign country's laws are less favorable than those available under State A law. However, the following factors weigh in favor of dismissal:\n• The foreign country has a heightened interest in having its courts adjudicate the action since the events occurred and the parties are located there.\n\n• There is only a slight burden on the employer to litigate in the foreign country because it has offices there.\n\n• The witnesses are readily available in the foreign country since the woman is domiciled there and the manager who discharged her lives there.\n\n• The evidence is more easily accessible in the foreign country since the events leading to the discharge occurred there.\n\nTherefore, the federal court is likely to dismiss the action for forum non conveniens (Choice B). (Choice A) A defendant waives certain defenses that are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or an answer, whichever occurs first. Unlike challenging the forum based on improper venue, forum non conveniens is not one of these waivable defenses.\n\n(Choice D) A federal court generally gives less deference to a foreign plaintiff's choice of forum. However, the fact that the woman is a foreign citizen, by itself, does not determine whether the court should dismiss for forum non conveniens. Instead, the court must weigh private and public interest factors to determine the most convenient forum for the parties. Educational objective: Under the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is a more convenient forum. To make this decision, the court will examine several factors, including the accessibility of evidence and witnesses. References\n• Piper Aircraft Co. v. Reyno, 454 U.S. 235, 257–60 (1981) (explaining how a court weighs private and public factors to make a forum non conveniens determination).","rule":"Under the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is a more convenient forum. To make this decision, the court will examine several factors, including the accessibility of evidence and witnesses.","irac":{"issue":"A woman domiciled in a foreign country brought a wrongful discharge action in federal court in State A against her former employer. The employer is incorporated in State A and headquartered in State B. The woman had worked at the employer's office in the foreign country. Her on-site manager, who had sole discretion over personnel matters, had made the decision to discharge her. The manager has now retired and continues to live in the foreign country. Under the employment laws of the foreign country, if the woman succeeds in her action, her damages would be limited to two years' salary. If the woman succeeds in her action under State A employment law, her damages could be much greater and could include emotional distress and punitive damages. The employer filed a motion to dismiss the action for insufficient service of process, which the court denied. The employer then moved to dismiss the action for forum non conveniens. Is the court likely to dismiss the action for forum non conveniens?","rule":"Under the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is a more convenient forum. To make this decision, the court will examine several factors, including the accessibility of evidence and witnesses.","analysis":"Doctrine of forum non conveniens\n\nDefinition • Power of federal court to dismiss or stay lawsuit when state or foreign judicial system is better suited to hear case Private interest • Accessibility of evidence factors • Availability of witnesses\n• Burden on defendant\n• Enforceability of judgment Public interest • Ability to comprehend substantive law factors • Choice of law\n• Interest of jury/community\n• Functionality of foreign judicial system The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign judicial system provides a more convenient forum to hear the case. In making this determination, the federal court must weigh several private and public interest factors (see table above)—none of which are determinative on their own. Here, one factor that weighs against dismissing the woman's action is that the remedies available under the foreign country's laws are less favorable than those available under State A law. However, the following factors weigh in favor of dismissal:\n• The foreign country has a heightened interest in having its courts adjudicate the action since the events occurred and the parties are located there.\n\n• There is only a slight burden on the employer to litigate in the foreign country because it has offices there.\n\n• The witnesses are readily available in the foreign country since the woman is domiciled there and the manager who discharged her lives there.\n\n• The evidence is more easily accessible in the foreign country since the events leading to the discharge occurred there.\n\nTherefore, the federal court is likely to dismiss the action for forum non conveniens (Choice B). (Choice A) A defendant waives certain defenses that are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or an answer, whichever occurs first. Unlike challenging the forum based on improper venue, forum non conveniens is not one of these waivable defenses.\n\n(Choice D) A federal court generally gives less deference to a foreign plaintiff's choice of forum. However, the fact that the woman is a foreign citizen, by itself, does not determine whether the court should dismiss for forum non conveniens. Instead, the court must weigh private and public interest factors to determine the most convenient forum for the parties. Educational objective: Under the doctrine of forum non conveniens, a federal court can dismiss or stay a lawsuit if a state or foreign jurisdiction is a more convenient forum. To make this decision, the court will examine several factors, including the accessibility of evidence and witnesses. References\n• Piper Aircraft Co. v. Reyno, 454 U.S. 235, 257–60 (1981) (explaining how a court weighs private and public factors to make a forum non conveniens determination).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Piper Aircraft Co. v. Reyno, 454 U.S. 235, 257–60 (1981) (explaining how a court weighs private and public factors to make a forum non conveniens determination).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[211],"sourceAnswerPages":[212,213],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名以外国为住所的女子，在 A 州联邦法院对前雇主提起非法解雇诉讼。雇主在 A 州注册成立，总部位于 B 州。女子曾在雇主位于该外国的办公室工作。对人事事项享有唯一决定权的现场经理决定解雇她。经理现已退休，仍居住于该外国。根据该外国劳动法，若女子胜诉，其损害赔偿以两年工资为限；若依据 A 州劳动法胜诉，其赔偿可能高得多，并可包括精神痛苦和惩罚性赔偿。雇主曾以诉讼文书送达不充分为由申请驳回，但法院予以驳回。雇主随后依据不方便法院原则申请驳回。法院可能依据不方便法院原则驳回诉讼吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为雇主未在第一次驳回动议中提出法院问题，已放弃质疑法院的权利。","B":"不会，因为该外国法律向女子提供的救济不如 A 州法律提供的救济有利。","C":"会，因为解雇发生于该外国且证据位于那里，该外国是更适当的法院。","D":"会，因为女子是外国公民，所以其法院选择不应获得尊重。"},"explanationZh":"不方便法院原则：\n\n定义｜当州或外国司法制度更适合审理案件时，联邦法院有权驳回或中止诉讼\n私人利益因素｜证据可取得性；证人可到庭性；被告负担；判决可执行性\n公共利益因素｜理解实体法的能力；法律选择；陪审团／社区利益；外国司法制度的运作能力\n\n如果州或外国司法制度提供了更便利的审理法院，不方便法院原则允许联邦法院驳回或中止诉讼。作出判断时，联邦法院必须权衡多项私人及公共利益因素（见上表），任何单一因素均非决定性。\n\n本题中，该外国法律提供的救济不如 A 州法律有利，是不利于驳回女子诉讼的一项因素。但是，以下因素支持驳回：\n• 事件在该外国发生且有关人员位于那里，所以该外国对由其法院审理诉讼具有更强利益；\n• 雇主在该外国设有办公室，在那里进行诉讼对其仅造成轻微负担；\n• 女子的住所在该外国，解雇她的经理也居住在那里，故证人很容易在该外国出庭；\n• 导致解雇的事件发生在那里，故证据在该外国更易取得。\n\n因此，联邦法院很可能依据不方便法院原则驳回诉讼（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）若未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出某些抗辩，被告会放弃这些抗辩。与以审判地不当为由质疑法院不同，不方便法院原则不属于此类可放弃的抗辩。\n\n（选项 D）联邦法院通常对外国原告的法院选择给予较少尊重。但是，女子是外国公民这一事实本身并不能决定法院是否应依据不方便法院原则驳回。法院必须权衡私人及公共利益因素，确定对当事人而言最便利的法院。\n\n学习目标：依据不方便法院原则，如果州或外国司法辖区是更便利的法院，联邦法院可以驳回或中止诉讼。作出判断时，法院会考察多项因素，包括证据和证人的可取得性。\n\n参考资料：\n• Piper Aircraft Co. v. Reyno, 454 U.S. 235, 257–60 (1981)（解释法院如何权衡私人及公共因素来作出不方便法院判断）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-071","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":71,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A plaintiff domiciled in State A brought a wrongful death action in a federal court in State A against a State B parent corporation and one of its foreign subsidiaries. The plaintiff alleged that a tire manufactured by the subsidiary in Europe had caused his wife's death in an automobile accident in Europe. The parent corporation does significant business throughout the United States, including in State A. The subsidiary conducts no business and has no employees or bank accounts in State A. The subsidiary manufactures its tires for the European market, but 2% of its tires are distributed in State A by the parent corporation. The subsidiary has moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the subsidiary's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because 2% of the subsidiary's tires entered State A through the stream of commerce.","B":"No, because of the general personal jurisdiction established over the parent corporation.","C":"Yes, because the accident did not occur in the United States.","D":"Yes, because the subsidiary lacks continuous, systematic, and substantial contacts with State A."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) Personal jurisdiction is established when the defendant consents or is served with process in the forum state (not seen here). It can also be established through specific or general jurisdiction. Specific jurisdiction exists when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. A defendant manufacturer (eg, the subsidiary) has minimum contacts with the forum state if it places a product in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets that state. Here, 2% of the subsidiary's tires entered State A through the stream of commerce. But the subsidiary did not purposefully target State A because it did not conduct business or have employees or bank accounts there. Therefore, the court lacks specific jurisdiction (Choice A). In contrast, general jurisdiction exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, domiciled) there. Here, the subsidiary has no such contacts with\n\nState A because it is domiciled in Europe. Therefore, the court also lacks general jurisdiction over the subsidiary, and it should grant the motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. (Choice B) The court's general jurisdiction over the parent corporation would not give it jurisdiction over the subsidiary unless the subsidiary was acting as the parent corporation's agent or alter ego. Here, the subsidiary did not act as an agent or alter ego because its business (manufacturing tires) is separate from the parent corporation's (distributing tires). (Choice C) Although the accident occurred outside the United States, the court could have had general jurisdiction over the subsidiary had it had continuous and systematic contacts with State A. Educational objective: General jurisdiction over a defendant exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, domiciled) there. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic business contacts required for general jurisdiction).","rule":"General jurisdiction over a defendant exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, domiciled) there.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff domiciled in State A brought a wrongful death action in a federal court in State A against a State B parent corporation and one of its foreign subsidiaries. The plaintiff alleged that a tire manufactured by the subsidiary in Europe had caused his wife's death in an automobile accident in Europe. The parent corporation does significant business throughout the United States, including in State A. The subsidiary conducts no business and has no employees or bank accounts in State A. The subsidiary manufactures its tires for the European market, but 2% of its tires are distributed in State A by the parent corporation. The subsidiary has moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. Should the court grant the subsidiary's motion?","rule":"General jurisdiction over a defendant exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, domiciled) there.","analysis":"Personal jurisdiction\n\n(court's authority over defendant)\n\nType Requirements\n\nService of • Serving process on defendant voluntarily in state where court is process located (ie, forum state)\n\nConsent • Express – contractual agreement (eg, forum-selection clause)\n• Implied – engaging in specific activity that state has substantial interest in regulating (eg, driving on public roads)\n• Waiver – untimely objection to personal jurisdiction\n• Appearance – voluntarily appearing in court to litigate merits of case Specific • Plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to defendant's minimum contacts (ie, purposeful availment) with forum state AND (case-linked)\n• Exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play & substantial justice General • Defendant has continuous & systematic contacts with forum state so substantial that defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, defendant's (all-purpose) domicile) Personal jurisdiction is established when the defendant consents or is served with process in the forum state (not seen here). It can also be established through specific or general jurisdiction. Specific jurisdiction exists when:\n• the plaintiff's claim arises from or is closely related to the defendant's minimum contacts with the forum state and\n\n• the exercise of jurisdiction complies with notions of fair play and substantial justice. A defendant manufacturer (eg, the subsidiary) has minimum contacts with the forum state if it places a product in the stream of commerce plus purposefully targets that state. Here, 2% of the subsidiary's tires entered State A through the stream of commerce. But the subsidiary did not purposefully target State A because it did not conduct business or have employees or bank accounts there. Therefore, the court lacks specific jurisdiction (Choice A). In contrast, general jurisdiction exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, domiciled) there. Here, the subsidiary has no such contacts with\n\nState A because it is domiciled in Europe. Therefore, the court also lacks general jurisdiction over the subsidiary, and it should grant the motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. (Choice B) The court's general jurisdiction over the parent corporation would not give it jurisdiction over the subsidiary unless the subsidiary was acting as the parent corporation's agent or alter ego. Here, the subsidiary did not act as an agent or alter ego because its business (manufacturing tires) is separate from the parent corporation's (distributing tires). (Choice C) Although the accident occurred outside the United States, the court could have had general jurisdiction over the subsidiary had it had continuous and systematic contacts with State A. Educational objective: General jurisdiction over a defendant exists when the defendant has continuous and systematic contacts with the forum state that are so substantial that the defendant is essentially \"at home\" (ie, domiciled) there. References\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic business contacts required for general jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017) (explaining the minimum contacts required for specific jurisdiction).\n\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011) (discussing the continuous and systematic business contacts required for general jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[214],"sourceAnswerPages":[215,216],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名以 A 州为住所的原告，在 A 州联邦法院对 B 州一家母公司及其一家外国子公司提起非正常死亡诉讼。原告主张，该子公司在欧洲制造的一条轮胎导致其妻子在欧洲的一场汽车事故中死亡。母公司在全美（包括 A 州）开展大量业务。子公司不在 A 州开展任何业务，在该州也无雇员或银行账户。子公司为欧洲市场制造轮胎，但其轮胎中有 2% 由母公司在 A 州销售。子公司以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准子公司的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为子公司 2% 的轮胎通过商业流通进入 A 州。","B":"不应，因为法院已对母公司建立一般对人管辖权。","C":"应当，因为事故并非发生于美国。","D":"应当，因为子公司与 A 州缺乏持续、系统且重大的联系。"},"explanationZh":"对人管辖权（法院对被告行使权力的权限）：\n\n类型｜要求\n诉讼文书送达｜被告自愿身处法院所在州（即法院州）时向其送达\n同意｜明示：合同约定，例如法院选择条款；默示：从事州具有重大监管利益的特定活动，例如在公共道路驾车；放弃：未及时对对人管辖权提出异议；出庭：自愿到庭就案件实体进行诉讼\n特定管辖权（与案件相关）｜原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系（即有目的利用）；且行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念\n一般管辖权（适用于所有请求）｜被告与法院州的联系持续且系统，并重大到使被告实质上“安居于此”，即该州为被告住所\n\n被告同意或在法院州获得诉讼文书送达时，可以建立对人管辖权，本题均不存在。也可通过特定或一般管辖权建立。\n\n符合以下条件时存在特定管辖权：\n• 原告请求源于或密切关联于被告与法院州的最低限度联系；并且\n• 行使管辖权符合公平处理和实质正义观念。\n\n制造商被告（例如子公司）将产品置入商业流通，并且有目的地针对法院州时，即与该州存在最低限度联系。本题中，子公司有 2% 的轮胎通过商业流通进入 A 州。但是，子公司不在 A 州开展业务，也无雇员或银行账户，故没有有目的地针对 A 州。因此，法院缺乏特定管辖权（选项 A）。\n\n相比之下，被告与法院州具有持续、系统且如此重大的联系，以致其实质上“安居于此”（即以该州为住所）时，存在一般管辖权。本题中，子公司以欧洲为住所，与 A 州没有此类联系。因此，法院对其也缺乏一般管辖权，应批准其以缺乏对人管辖权提出的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 B）除非子公司作为母公司代理人或另一个自我而行事，法院对母公司享有一般管辖权并不会使其取得对子公司的管辖权。本题中，子公司的业务是制造轮胎，母公司的业务则是销售轮胎，彼此分离，因此子公司并非代理人或另一个自我。\n\n（选项 C）事故虽发生于美国境外，但若子公司与 A 州存在持续、系统的联系，法院仍可能对其享有一般管辖权。\n\n学习目标：被告与法院州存在持续、系统且如此重大的联系，以致其实际“安居于此”（即以该州为住所）时，存在一般管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• Bristol-Myers Squibb Co. v. Super. Ct. of Cal., 137 S. Ct. 1773, 1780–82 (2017)（解释特定管辖权所需的最低限度联系）。\n• Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, 564 U.S. 915, 926–29 (2011)（讨论一般管辖权所需的持续、系统业务联系）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-072","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":72,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"An investor from State A sued a company from State B in a federal court. The investor's complaint asserted a misrepresentation claim for $40,000 and a breach-of-contract claim for $40,000. The claims arose from unrelated investments that the investor made in the company. The company moved to dismiss the complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because original jurisdiction exists over the claims.","B":"No, because supplemental jurisdiction exists over the claims.","C":"Yes, because neither claim independently satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement.","D":"Yes, because the claims arose from unrelated investments."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Amount in controversy\n\n(aggregation of claims)\n\nProcedural Claims asserted Outcome posture\n\nOne π v. one Δ • Negligence – $40K • Aggregation allowed\n• Battery – $60K • Amount in controversy = $100K One π v. • Negligence against • Aggregation not allowed unless Δs multiple Δs Δ1 – $40K jointly liable\n• Battery against Δ2 – • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K $60K Multiple πs v. • π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation not allowed unless πs one Δ – $40K enforcing common or undivided\n• π2 asserts battery – interest $60K • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K\n• π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation allowed for π1's – $40K claims\n• π1 asserts breach of • Aggregation not allowed for π2's contract – $40K claim\n• π2 asserts battery – • Amounts in controversy = $80K & $60K $60K π = plaintiff; Δ = defendant.\n\nA federal court has the authority to hear a case or controversy if it falls within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction is established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse) and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nThe amount in controversy is the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint, excluding interest and costs. To meet the required amount, a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) any claims asserted against a single defendant. This is true even if those claims are legally and/or factually unrelated—eg, the investor's claims that arose from unrelated investments (Choice D).*\n\nHere, the investor and the company are diverse since they are from States A and B, respectively. Neither of the investor's claims independently exceeds $75,000. But since the claims are asserted against a single defendant, they can be aggregated to $80,000 to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement (Choice C). As a result, original jurisdiction exists over the claims, and the federal court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss. *FRCP 42 allows a federal court to order separate trials of related and/or unrelated claims for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or to expedite and economize. (Choice B) A federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear certain claims that do not arise under the court's original jurisdiction—ie, federal-question or diversity jurisdiction. But here, supplemental jurisdiction need not be analyzed since the court has diversity jurisdiction over the aggregated claims. Educational objective: To meet the amount-in-controversy requirement for diversity jurisdiction (excess of $75,000), a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) his/her claims against a single defendant—even if those claims are unrelated. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"To meet the amount-in-controversy requirement for diversity jurisdiction (excess of $75,000), a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) his/her claims against a single defendant—even if those claims are unrelated.","irac":{"issue":"An investor from State A sued a company from State B in a federal court. The investor's complaint asserted a misrepresentation claim for $40,000 and a breach-of-contract claim for $40,000. The claims arose from unrelated investments that the investor made in the company. The company moved to dismiss the complaint for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"To meet the amount-in-controversy requirement for diversity jurisdiction (excess of $75,000), a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) his/her claims against a single defendant—even if those claims are unrelated.","analysis":"Amount in controversy\n\n(aggregation of claims)\n\nProcedural Claims asserted Outcome posture\n\nOne π v. one Δ • Negligence – $40K • Aggregation allowed\n• Battery – $60K • Amount in controversy = $100K One π v. • Negligence against • Aggregation not allowed unless Δs multiple Δs Δ1 – $40K jointly liable\n• Battery against Δ2 – • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K $60K Multiple πs v. • π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation not allowed unless πs one Δ – $40K enforcing common or undivided\n• π2 asserts battery – interest $60K • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K\n• π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation allowed for π1's – $40K claims\n• π1 asserts breach of • Aggregation not allowed for π2's contract – $40K claim\n• π2 asserts battery – • Amounts in controversy = $80K & $60K $60K π = plaintiff; Δ = defendant.\n\nA federal court has the authority to hear a case or controversy if it falls within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. Original jurisdiction is established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states (ie, are diverse) and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nThe amount in controversy is the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint, excluding interest and costs. To meet the required amount, a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) any claims asserted against a single defendant. This is true even if those claims are legally and/or factually unrelated—eg, the investor's claims that arose from unrelated investments (Choice D).*\n\nHere, the investor and the company are diverse since they are from States A and B, respectively. Neither of the investor's claims independently exceeds $75,000. But since the claims are asserted against a single defendant, they can be aggregated to $80,000 to satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement (Choice C). As a result, original jurisdiction exists over the claims, and the federal court will likely deny the company's motion to dismiss. *FRCP 42 allows a federal court to order separate trials of related and/or unrelated claims for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or to expedite and economize. (Choice B) A federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear certain claims that do not arise under the court's original jurisdiction—ie, federal-question or diversity jurisdiction. But here, supplemental jurisdiction need not be analyzed since the court has diversity jurisdiction over the aggregated claims. Educational objective: To meet the amount-in-controversy requirement for diversity jurisdiction (excess of $75,000), a single plaintiff can aggregate (ie, combine) his/her claims against a single defendant—even if those claims are unrelated. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[217],"sourceAnswerPages":[218,219],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名投资者在联邦法院起诉 B 州一家公司。投资者的起诉状提出一项 40,000 美元的虚假陈述请求和一项 40,000 美元的违约请求。两项请求源于投资者对该公司作出的互不相关投资。公司以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回起诉状。法院可能批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法院对这些请求享有原始管辖权。","B":"不会，因为法院对这些请求享有补充管辖权。","C":"会，因为两项请求均未单独满足争议金额要求。","D":"会，因为这些请求源于互不相关的投资。"},"explanationZh":"争议金额（请求的合并计算）：\n\n程序形态｜提出的请求｜结果\n一名原告诉一名被告｜过失 40,000 美元；殴打 60,000 美元｜允许合并；争议金额为 100,000 美元\n一名原告诉多名被告｜对被告一提出过失请求 40,000 美元；对被告二提出殴打请求 60,000 美元｜除非被告承担连带责任，否则不得合并；争议金额分别为 40,000 美元和 60,000 美元\n多名原告诉一名被告｜原告一提出过失请求 40,000 美元；原告二提出殴打请求 60,000 美元｜除非原告主张共同或不可分利益，否则不得合并；争议金额分别为 40,000 美元和 60,000 美元\n多名原告诉一名被告｜原告一提出过失请求 40,000 美元；原告一提出违约请求 40,000 美元；原告二提出殴打请求 60,000 美元｜允许合并原告一的请求；不得合并原告二的请求；争议金额分别为 80,000 美元和 60,000 美元\n\n联邦法院有权审理属于其原始事项管辖权的案件或争议。原始管辖权通过以下任一方式建立：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生（本题不存在）；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方是不同州的公民，即州籍不同，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n争议金额是原告起诉状所请求的金额，不包括利息和诉讼费用。为满足金额要求，一名原告可以合并其针对同一被告提出的任何请求。即使这些请求在法律上和／或事实上互不相关——例如本题源于互不相关投资的两项请求——也是如此（选项 D）。*\n\n本题中，投资者与公司分别来自 A 州和 B 州，因此州籍不同。投资者的两项请求均未单独超过 75,000 美元，但因两项请求针对同一被告，可以合并为 80,000 美元，满足争议金额要求（选项 C）。因此，法院对这些请求享有原始管辖权，很可能驳回公司的驳回动议。\n\n*FRCP 42 允许联邦法院为了便利、避免不利影响或加快诉讼并节省资源，对相关和／或无关请求分别审判。\n\n（选项 B）联邦法院可以行使补充管辖权，审理某些不属于其原始管辖权——即联邦问题或多元州籍管辖权——的请求。但本题合并计算后的请求属于法院多元州籍管辖权，无需分析补充管辖权。\n\n学习目标：为满足多元州籍管辖权的争议金额要求（超过 75,000 美元），一名原告可以合并其针对同一被告提出的各项请求，即使请求之间互不相关。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-073","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":73,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A plane crashed in State C, killing all 120 passengers on board. The deceased passengers were domiciled in States A, B, and C. The plane was owned by an airline incorporated in State A, headquartered in State B, and licensed to do business in State C. The plane's manufacturer is incorporated, headquartered, and licensed to do business in State C. The estates of the deceased passengers brought an action against the airline and the manufacturer in a State B federal court. The estates assert wrongful death claims on behalf of the deceased passengers to recover $120 million in damages. The airline and the manufacturer have moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the defendants' motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court has original jurisdiction under the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act.","B":"No, because the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the claims.","C":"Yes, because the crash giving rise to the dispute occurred in State C.","D":"Yes, because the parties lack complete diversity of citizenship."},"answer":"A","explanation":"This case does not present a federal question, so original subject-matter jurisdiction must be established through diversity jurisdiction. This requires an amount in controversy exceeding $75,000 and complete diversity of citizenship. The diversity requirement is typically met if the opposing parties are citizens of different states (not seen here). However, the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act (MMTJA) relaxes these requirements for cases arising from an accident in which at least 75 people died at a discrete location. Under this act, original jurisdiction exists when there is minimal diversity—ie, at least one plaintiff and one defendant are citizens of different states—and one of the following is established:\n• A defendant resides* in a different state from where a substantial part of the accident occurred, even if that defendant also resides in the same state as the accident (here, the airline resides in States A, B, and C, while the crash occurred in State C)\n\n• Any two defendants reside in different states, even if the defendants also reside in the same state (here, the airline resides in States A, B, and C, while the manufacturer resides in State C)\n\n• Substantial parts of the accident occurred in different states (not seen here)\n\nSince the MMTJA requirements are satisfied and there is no basis for abstention (see image above), the court has original subject-matter jurisdiction over this minimally diverse action (Choice D). Therefore, the court should deny the defendants' motion to dismiss. *For purposes of the MMTJA, a corporation is deemed to reside in any state(s) in which it is incorporated, licensed to do business, or doing business. (Choice B) When a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist over the remaining claims. Here, all the claims fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, so supplemental jurisdiction is irrelevant. (Choice C) The fact that the crash occurred in State C may provide a basis to dismiss for improper venue but not for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Educational objective: A federal court has original subject-matter jurisdiction over an action in which minimal diversity of citizenship exists when the requirements of the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act (MMTJA) are satisfied. References\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §1369 (Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act).","rule":"A federal court has original subject-matter jurisdiction over an action in which minimal diversity of citizenship exists when the requirements of the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act (MMTJA) are satisfied.","irac":{"issue":"A plane crashed in State C, killing all 120 passengers on board. The deceased passengers were domiciled in States A, B, and C. The plane was owned by an airline incorporated in State A, headquartered in State B, and licensed to do business in State C. The plane's manufacturer is incorporated, headquartered, and licensed to do business in State C. The estates of the deceased passengers brought an action against the airline and the manufacturer in a State B federal court. The estates assert wrongful death claims on behalf of the deceased passengers to recover $120 million in damages. The airline and the manufacturer have moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the defendants' motion?","rule":"A federal court has original subject-matter jurisdiction over an action in which minimal diversity of citizenship exists when the requirements of the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act (MMTJA) are satisfied.","analysis":"This case does not present a federal question, so original subject-matter jurisdiction must be established through diversity jurisdiction. This requires an amount in controversy exceeding $75,000 and complete diversity of citizenship. The diversity requirement is typically met if the opposing parties are citizens of different states (not seen here). However, the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act (MMTJA) relaxes these requirements for cases arising from an accident in which at least 75 people died at a discrete location. Under this act, original jurisdiction exists when there is minimal diversity—ie, at least one plaintiff and one defendant are citizens of different states—and one of the following is established:\n• A defendant resides* in a different state from where a substantial part of the accident occurred, even if that defendant also resides in the same state as the accident (here, the airline resides in States A, B, and C, while the crash occurred in State C)\n\n• Any two defendants reside in different states, even if the defendants also reside in the same state (here, the airline resides in States A, B, and C, while the manufacturer resides in State C)\n\n• Substantial parts of the accident occurred in different states (not seen here)\n\nSince the MMTJA requirements are satisfied and there is no basis for abstention (see image above), the court has original subject-matter jurisdiction over this minimally diverse action (Choice D). Therefore, the court should deny the defendants' motion to dismiss. *For purposes of the MMTJA, a corporation is deemed to reside in any state(s) in which it is incorporated, licensed to do business, or doing business. (Choice B) When a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction may exist over the remaining claims. Here, all the claims fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction, so supplemental jurisdiction is irrelevant. (Choice C) The fact that the crash occurred in State C may provide a basis to dismiss for improper venue but not for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Educational objective: A federal court has original subject-matter jurisdiction over an action in which minimal diversity of citizenship exists when the requirements of the Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act (MMTJA) are satisfied. References\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §1369 (Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. §1369 (Multiparty, Multiforum Trial Jurisdiction Act).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[220],"sourceAnswerPages":[221,222],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一架飞机在 C 州坠毁，机上 120 名乘客全部死亡。死者的住所分别位于 A、B、C 三州。飞机由一家航空公司所有；该公司在 A 州注册成立，总部位于 B 州，并获准在 C 州开展业务。飞机制造商在 C 州注册成立、设总部，并获准在该州开展业务。死者遗产在 B 州联邦法院起诉航空公司与制造商，代表死者提出非正常死亡请求，请求 1.2 亿美元损害赔偿。航空公司与制造商以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准被告的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为法院依据《多方、多法院审判管辖权法》享有原始管辖权。","B":"不应，因为法院可以对这些请求行使补充管辖权。","C":"应当，因为引发争议的坠机事故发生于 C 州。","D":"应当，因为当事人之间不具有完全州籍多元性。"},"explanationZh":"本案未提出联邦问题，因此必须通过多元州籍管辖权建立原始事项管辖权。通常要求争议金额超过 75,000 美元且当事人具有完全州籍多元性；对立双方为不同州公民时，通常满足州籍多元性要求，但本题并非如此。\n\n但是，《多方、多法院审判管辖权法》（MMTJA）放宽了因某一特定地点发生、至少造成 75 人死亡的事故而起案件的要求。依据该法，只要存在最低限度州籍多元性——即至少一名原告与一名被告是不同州的公民——并且符合下列任一条件，即存在原始管辖权：\n• 一名被告居住*于与事故大部分发生地不同的州，即使该被告也居住于事故发生州。本题中，航空公司居住于 A、B、C 三州，而坠机发生于 C 州；\n• 任意两名被告居住于不同州，即使二者也居住于同一州。本题中，航空公司居住于 A、B、C 三州，制造商居住于 C 州；\n• 事故的重大部分发生于不同州，本题不存在。\n\n由于满足 MMTJA 的要求，且没有停止审理的依据（见上图），法院对本最低限度州籍多元性诉讼享有原始事项管辖权（选项 D）。因此，法院应驳回被告的驳回动议。\n\n*就 MMTJA 而言，公司在其注册成立、获准开展业务或实际开展业务的任何州均视为居民。\n\n（选项 B）争议涉及多项请求，且只有部分请求属于法院原始事项管辖权时，法院可能对其余请求享有补充管辖权。本题所有请求均属于法院原始事项管辖权，所以补充管辖权无关。\n\n（选项 C）坠机发生于 C 州这一事实可能构成以审判地不当为由驳回案件的依据，但并不构成以缺乏事项管辖权为由驳回的依据。\n\n学习目标：存在最低限度州籍多元性且满足《多方、多法院审判管辖权法》（MMTJA）要求时，联邦法院对该诉讼享有原始事项管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1369（《多方、多法院审判管辖权法》）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-029"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-074","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":74,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A husband and wife were married in State A and lived there for 10 years before separating. One month later, the wife permanently moved to State B and immediately filed for divorce in a federal court in State B. The wife claims that she is entitled to $300,000 in alimony. The husband appeared in the action and has filed a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court has diversity jurisdiction over the case.","B":"No, because the husband waived a subject-matter jurisdiction challenge by appearing in the case.","C":"Yes, because state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over this type of action.","D":"Yes, because the wife did not establish a domicile in State B."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Federal diversity jurisdiction exceptions\n\nFederal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving:\n\n• probate matters (eg, authenticating wills, administering estates) or\n• domestic relations (eg, issuing divorce, alimony, or child-custody decrees) A federal court must possess subject-matter jurisdiction to hear the merits of a case before it. Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states. Here, diversity jurisdiction is established since the wife claims that she is entitled to $300,000 and the parties are citizens of different states (States A and B). However, federal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving probate matters or domestic relations. Instead, state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over these types of actions (Choice A).* Therefore, the husband's motion to dismiss should be granted. *The probate and domestic-relations exceptions exist because states have a strong interest in these substantive areas and are more qualified to deal with the constant judicial involvement these types of cases require. (Choice B) A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived. However, a challenge to personal jurisdiction is waived if the defendant has voluntarily appeared in the case, unless it was a special appearance for the express purpose of objecting to personal jurisdiction. (Choice D) An individual is a citizen of the state where he/she is domiciled—ie, physically present with the intent to remain indefinitely. Since the wife permanently moved to State B, she has established her domicile there. Educational objective: Federal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving probate matters or domestic relations. Instead, state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over these types of cases. References\n• Ankenbrandt v. Richards, 504 U.S. 689, 703–04 (1992) (explaining the domestic-relations exception to diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"Federal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving probate matters or domestic relations. Instead, state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over these types of cases.","irac":{"issue":"A husband and wife were married in State A and lived there for 10 years before separating. One month later, the wife permanently moved to State B and immediately filed for divorce in a federal court in State B. The wife claims that she is entitled to $300,000 in alimony. The husband appeared in the action and has filed a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"Federal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving probate matters or domestic relations. Instead, state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over these types of cases.","analysis":"Federal diversity jurisdiction exceptions\n\nFederal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving:\n\n• probate matters (eg, authenticating wills, administering estates) or\n• domestic relations (eg, issuing divorce, alimony, or child-custody decrees) A federal court must possess subject-matter jurisdiction to hear the merits of a case before it. Subject-matter jurisdiction can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or federal law (not seen here) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the opposing parties are citizens of different states. Here, diversity jurisdiction is established since the wife claims that she is entitled to $300,000 and the parties are citizens of different states (States A and B). However, federal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving probate matters or domestic relations. Instead, state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over these types of actions (Choice A).* Therefore, the husband's motion to dismiss should be granted. *The probate and domestic-relations exceptions exist because states have a strong interest in these substantive areas and are more qualified to deal with the constant judicial involvement these types of cases require. (Choice B) A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived. However, a challenge to personal jurisdiction is waived if the defendant has voluntarily appeared in the case, unless it was a special appearance for the express purpose of objecting to personal jurisdiction. (Choice D) An individual is a citizen of the state where he/she is domiciled—ie, physically present with the intent to remain indefinitely. Since the wife permanently moved to State B, she has established her domicile there. Educational objective: Federal courts cannot exercise diversity jurisdiction over cases involving probate matters or domestic relations. Instead, state courts have exclusive jurisdiction over these types of cases. References\n• Ankenbrandt v. Richards, 504 U.S. 689, 703–04 (1992) (explaining the domestic-relations exception to diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Ankenbrandt v. Richards, 504 U.S. 689, 703–04 (1992) (explaining the domestic-relations exception to diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[223],"sourceAnswerPages":[224,225],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一对夫妻在 A 州结婚，并在该州共同生活十年后分居。一个月后，妻子永久迁往 B 州，并立即在 B 州联邦法院申请离婚。妻子主张自己有权获得 300,000 美元赡养费。丈夫已在诉讼中出庭，并以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为法院对本案享有多元州籍管辖权。","B":"不应，因为丈夫在案件中出庭，已放弃事项管辖权异议。","C":"应当，因为州法院对这类诉讼享有专属管辖权。","D":"应当，因为妻子尚未在 B 州建立住所。"},"explanationZh":"联邦多元州籍管辖权的例外：\n\n联邦法院不得对涉及以下事项的案件行使多元州籍管辖权：\n• 遗嘱认证事项，例如验证遗嘱、管理遗产；或\n• 家庭关系事项，例如签发离婚、赡养费或子女监护判令。\n\n联邦法院必须享有事项管辖权，方可审理其面前案件的实体问题。事项管辖权可以通过以下方式建立：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求依据《美国宪法》、条约或联邦法律产生，本题不存在；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且对立双方是不同州的公民。\n\n本题中，妻子主张有权获得 300,000 美元，且双方分别为 A 州和 B 州公民，通常已满足多元州籍管辖权。但是，联邦法院不得对涉及遗嘱认证或家庭关系的案件行使多元州籍管辖权。相反，州法院对这类诉讼享有专属管辖权（选项 A）。* 因此，应批准丈夫的驳回动议。\n\n*遗嘱认证和家庭关系例外之所以存在，是因为各州对这些实体领域具有重大利益，也更有能力处理这类案件所需的持续司法介入。\n\n（选项 B）事项管辖权异议永不放弃。但是，被告自愿出庭时，对人管辖权异议会被放弃；专为反对对人管辖权而特别出庭的除外。\n\n（选项 D）个人是其住所所在州的公民；住所要求实际身处该州并有无限期居留意图。妻子永久迁往 B 州，故已在那里建立住所。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院不得对涉及遗嘱认证事项或家庭关系的案件行使多元州籍管辖权；州法院对这类案件享有专属管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• Ankenbrandt v. Richards, 504 U.S. 689, 703–04 (1992)（解释多元州籍管辖权的家庭关系例外）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-075","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":75,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A man and a woman bicycling on a road were both struck by a driver who was texting on his phone. The man and the woman filed a negligence action in federal district court. The man, who suffered severe injuries, claimed $80,000 in medical damages. The woman suffered minor injuries and requested $2,000 in medical damages. The man and the woman are citizens of State A, and the driver is a citizen of State B. The driver moved to dismiss the woman's claim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the driver's motion be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court has absolute discretion to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the woman's claim.","B":"No, because the woman's claim arises out of a common nucleus of operative facts with the man's claim.","C":"Yes, because the woman's claim does not meet the amount-in-controversy requirement.","D":"Yes, because the woman's claim would contaminate diversity of citizenship."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Federal district courts have original subject-matter jurisdiction over disputes that fall within either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a federal treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, the man and the woman (State A citizens) are diverse from the driver (State B citizen), but only the man's $80,000 claim meets the amount-in-controversy requirement (Choice C).* Therefore, the federal court has diversity jurisdiction over the man's claim—but not the woman's claim. However, when a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction (as seen here), the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the other claims (Choice A). This is generally permitted when these supplemental claims share a common nucleus of operative facts (here, the automobile accident) with a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. But when that claim is based solely on diversity jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted if a supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship—not seen here because the woman and the driver are citizens of different states (Choice D)—or\n\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention—neither of which is seen here.\n\nTherefore, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over the woman's claim, and the driver's motion to dismiss that claim should be denied. *The woman cannot satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement by aggregating (ie, combining) her claim with the man's claim. That is because multiple plaintiffs cannot aggregate their claims to meet this requirement unless they seek to enforce a single title or right to a common or undivided interest (eg, wrongful death action by surviving beneficiaries). Educational objective: A federal court may generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over claims that fall outside its original subject-matter jurisdiction when those claims share a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that falls within its original subject-matter jurisdiction. References\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","rule":"A federal court may generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over claims that fall outside its original subject-matter jurisdiction when those claims share a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that falls within its original subject-matter jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"A man and a woman bicycling on a road were both struck by a driver who was texting on his phone. The man and the woman filed a negligence action in federal district court. The man, who suffered severe injuries, claimed $80,000 in medical damages. The woman suffered minor injuries and requested $2,000 in medical damages. The man and the woman are citizens of State A, and the driver is a citizen of State B. The driver moved to dismiss the woman's claim for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Should the driver's motion be granted?","rule":"A federal court may generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over claims that fall outside its original subject-matter jurisdiction when those claims share a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that falls within its original subject-matter jurisdiction.","analysis":"Federal district courts have original subject-matter jurisdiction over disputes that fall within either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – the dispute arises under the U.S. Constitution, a federal treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nHere, the man and the woman (State A citizens) are diverse from the driver (State B citizen), but only the man's $80,000 claim meets the amount-in-controversy requirement (Choice C).* Therefore, the federal court has diversity jurisdiction over the man's claim—but not the woman's claim. However, when a dispute involves multiple claims and only some fall within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction (as seen here), the court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the other claims (Choice A). This is generally permitted when these supplemental claims share a common nucleus of operative facts (here, the automobile accident) with a claim within the court's original subject-matter jurisdiction. But when that claim is based solely on diversity jurisdiction, supplemental jurisdiction is not permitted if a supplemental claim:\n• would contaminate diversity of citizenship—not seen here because the woman and the driver are citizens of different states (Choice D)—or\n\n• seeks $75,000 or less and is made by a plaintiff (1) against parties added through joinder, intervention, or impleader or (2) seeking to join through compulsory joinder or intervention—neither of which is seen here.\n\nTherefore, the court has supplemental jurisdiction over the woman's claim, and the driver's motion to dismiss that claim should be denied. *The woman cannot satisfy the amount-in-controversy requirement by aggregating (ie, combining) her claim with the man's claim. That is because multiple plaintiffs cannot aggregate their claims to meet this requirement unless they seek to enforce a single title or right to a common or undivided interest (eg, wrongful death action by surviving beneficiaries). Educational objective: A federal court may generally exercise supplemental jurisdiction over claims that fall outside its original subject-matter jurisdiction when those claims share a common nucleus of operative facts with a claim that falls within its original subject-matter jurisdiction. References\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. §1367 (supplemental jurisdiction).\n\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005) (explaining supplemental jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[226],"sourceAnswerPages":[227,228],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一男一女在道路上骑自行车时，均被一名正在用手机发短信的驾驶员撞伤。二人在联邦地区法院提起过失诉讼。男子伤势严重，请求 80,000 美元医疗损害赔偿；女子伤势轻微，请求 2,000 美元医疗损害赔偿。男子和女子均为 A 州公民，驾驶员为 B 州公民。驾驶员以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回女子的请求。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为法院对是否就女子的请求行使补充管辖权享有绝对酌情权。","B":"不应，因为女子的请求与男子的请求共享同一核心争议事实。","C":"应当，因为女子的请求不满足争议金额要求。","D":"应当，因为女子的请求会破坏州籍多元性。"},"explanationZh":"联邦地区法院对属于以下任一范围的争议享有原始事项管辖权：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——争议依据《美国宪法》、联邦条约或联邦法律产生，本题不存在；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对立双方是不同州的公民，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。\n\n本题中，男子与女子（A 州公民）和驾驶员（B 州公民）州籍不同，但只有男子的 80,000 美元请求满足争议金额要求（选项 C）。* 因此，联邦法院对男子的请求享有多元州籍管辖权，但对女子的请求不享有原始管辖权。\n\n但是，争议涉及多项请求，而仅有部分请求属于法院原始事项管辖权时（本题即如此），法院可以对其他请求行使补充管辖权（选项 A）。只要这些补充请求与原始事项管辖权范围内的请求共享同一核心争议事实——本题为同一汽车事故——通常即可行使。\n\n不过，原始请求仅基于多元州籍管辖权时，若补充请求符合下列任一情形，则不得行使补充管辖权：\n• 会破坏州籍多元性——本题并非如此，因为女子与驾驶员是不同州公民（选项 D）；或\n• 请求金额为 75,000 美元或以下，且由一名原告：(1) 针对通过当事人合并、介入或第三方诉讼加入的当事人提出；或 (2) 该原告寻求通过强制合并或介入加入诉讼——本题均不存在。\n\n因此，法院对女子的请求享有补充管辖权，应驳回驾驶员针对该请求提出的驳回动议。\n\n*女子不能将自己的请求与男子的请求合并计算以满足争议金额要求，因为多名原告不得合并各自请求来满足该要求，除非他们寻求执行单一权利或对共同且不可分利益的权利，例如生存受益人共同提起非正常死亡诉讼。\n\n学习目标：不属于联邦法院原始事项管辖权的请求，若与属于原始事项管辖权的请求共享同一核心争议事实，联邦法院通常可以对其行使补充管辖权。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1367（补充管辖权）。\n• Exxon Mobil Corp. v. Allapattah Servs., 545 U.S. 546, 560 (2005)（解释补充管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-023"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-076","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":76,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"An employee filed an employment discrimination suit in federal court against a corporation without the assistance of an attorney. After filing suit, the employee was severely injured in a car accident and hospitalized for three months. Immediately upon being released from the hospital, the employee hired an attorney to represent her in the discrimination suit. The attorney then had the corporation served with process 100 days after the employee's complaint had been filed. The corporation moved to dismiss for improper service of process. Is the court likely to grant the corporation's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the corporation was served with process within 90 days after the employee hired an attorney.","B":"No, because the employee can show good cause for the untimely service of process.","C":"Yes, because the corporation was not served with process within 90 days after the complaint was filed.","D":"Yes, because the delay in service of process was not caused by the corporation's misconduct."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A plaintiff must provide a defendant with notice of a lawsuit through service of process. A defendant in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days after the plaintiff's complaint is filed.* Here, service of process was untimely because the corporation was served 100 days after the employee filed her complaint. This would generally require the court—by motion or on its own initiative after notifying the plaintiff—to dismiss the action without prejudice OR order service of process within a specified time. However, if the plaintiff provides good cause as to why service was untimely, the court must extend the time for service for an appropriate period. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control (eg, sudden illness or injury)— not the plaintiff's own mistake or ignorance. Since the employee here was severely injured and hospitalized for three months after filing the complaint, good cause exists. Therefore, the court will likely deny the corporation's motion to dismiss (Choice C). *The 90-day deadline for service of process applies to defendants in the U.S. For defendants in a foreign country, most federal courts require that process be served with due diligence. (Choice A) The deadline for service of process is measured from the date the complaint is filed—not the date the plaintiff hires an attorney. That is because parties are not required to hire an attorney and can proceed pro se (ie, without legal counsel). (Choice D) A defendant's misconduct provides good cause for untimely service of process. Although there is no indication that the corporation engaged in any misconduct, good cause exists since the employee's serious injury and hospitalization prevented her from serving the corporation within 90 days. Educational objective: A defendant in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days after the plaintiff files the complaint unless good cause exists for missing this deadline. If good cause is shown, the court must extend the time for service of process for an appropriate period. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m) (deadline for service of process).\n\n• Floyd v. United States, 900 F.2d 1045, 1047 (7th Cir. 1990) (explaining that good cause exists for untimely service of process when extenuating factors such as a sudden illness occur).","rule":"A defendant in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days after the plaintiff files the complaint unless good cause exists for missing this deadline. If good cause is shown, the court must extend the time for service of process for an appropriate period.","irac":{"issue":"An employee filed an employment discrimination suit in federal court against a corporation without the assistance of an attorney. After filing suit, the employee was severely injured in a car accident and hospitalized for three months. Immediately upon being released from the hospital, the employee hired an attorney to represent her in the discrimination suit. The attorney then had the corporation served with process 100 days after the employee's complaint had been filed. The corporation moved to dismiss for improper service of process. Is the court likely to grant the corporation's motion?","rule":"A defendant in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days after the plaintiff files the complaint unless good cause exists for missing this deadline. If good cause is shown, the court must extend the time for service of process for an appropriate period.","analysis":"A plaintiff must provide a defendant with notice of a lawsuit through service of process. A defendant in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days after the plaintiff's complaint is filed.* Here, service of process was untimely because the corporation was served 100 days after the employee filed her complaint. This would generally require the court—by motion or on its own initiative after notifying the plaintiff—to dismiss the action without prejudice OR order service of process within a specified time. However, if the plaintiff provides good cause as to why service was untimely, the court must extend the time for service for an appropriate period. Good cause exists if service was delayed due to matters outside of the plaintiff's control (eg, sudden illness or injury)— not the plaintiff's own mistake or ignorance. Since the employee here was severely injured and hospitalized for three months after filing the complaint, good cause exists. Therefore, the court will likely deny the corporation's motion to dismiss (Choice C). *The 90-day deadline for service of process applies to defendants in the U.S. For defendants in a foreign country, most federal courts require that process be served with due diligence. (Choice A) The deadline for service of process is measured from the date the complaint is filed—not the date the plaintiff hires an attorney. That is because parties are not required to hire an attorney and can proceed pro se (ie, without legal counsel). (Choice D) A defendant's misconduct provides good cause for untimely service of process. Although there is no indication that the corporation engaged in any misconduct, good cause exists since the employee's serious injury and hospitalization prevented her from serving the corporation within 90 days. Educational objective: A defendant in the U.S. must be served with process within 90 days after the plaintiff files the complaint unless good cause exists for missing this deadline. If good cause is shown, the court must extend the time for service of process for an appropriate period. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m) (deadline for service of process).\n\n• Floyd v. United States, 900 F.2d 1045, 1047 (7th Cir. 1990) (explaining that good cause exists for untimely service of process when extenuating factors such as a sudden illness occur).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m) (deadline for service of process).\n\n• Floyd v. United States, 900 F.2d 1045, 1047 (7th Cir. 1990) (explaining that good cause exists for untimely service of process when extenuating factors such as a sudden illness occur).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[229],"sourceAnswerPages":[230],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名雇员没有律师协助，自行在联邦法院对一家股份公司提起就业歧视诉讼。提起诉讼后，雇员在车祸中严重受伤并住院三个月。出院后，她立即聘请一名律师代理该歧视诉讼。律师随后在雇员提交起诉状 100 天后向公司送达诉讼文书。公司以诉讼文书送达不当为由申请驳回。法院可能批准公司的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为公司在雇员聘请律师后 90 天内获得诉讼文书送达。","B":"不会，因为雇员能够证明逾期送达存在正当理由。","C":"会，因为公司未在提交起诉状后 90 天内获得诉讼文书送达。","D":"会，因为送达延误并非由公司的不当行为造成。"},"explanationZh":"原告必须通过诉讼文书送达使被告获得诉讼通知。位于美国境内的被告必须在原告提交起诉状后 90 天内获得诉讼文书送达。* 本题在提交起诉状后第 100 天才向公司送达，因此送达逾期。\n\n通常，这会要求法院——根据动议，或由法院依职权在通知原告后——选择无损害驳回诉讼，或者命令在指定时间内完成送达。但是，如果原告能够说明送达逾期的正当理由，法院必须适当延长送达期限。因原告无法控制的事项（例如突发疾病或受伤）导致送达延误时，存在正当理由；原告自身的错误或无知则不构成正当理由。\n\n本题雇员提交起诉状后严重受伤并住院三个月，故存在正当理由。因此，法院很可能驳回公司的驳回动议（选项 C）。\n\n*90 天送达期限适用于位于美国境内的被告。对于外国被告，多数联邦法院要求以应有勤勉完成送达。\n\n（选项 A）诉讼文书送达期限自提交起诉状之日计算，而非自原告聘请律师之日计算，因为当事人无需聘请律师，可以自行诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）被告不当行为可以构成逾期送达的正当理由。虽然没有迹象表明公司存在不当行为，但雇员的严重受伤及住院使其无法在 90 天内向公司送达，同样构成正当理由。\n\n学习目标：除非错过期限存在正当理由，美国境内的被告必须在原告提交起诉状后 90 天内获得诉讼文书送达。原告证明存在正当理由时，法院必须适当延长送达期限。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m)（诉讼文书送达期限）。\n• Floyd v. United States, 900 F.2d 1045, 1047 (7th Cir. 1990)（说明突发疾病等情有可原的因素导致逾期送达时存在正当理由）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-013"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-077","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":77,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"After being forcibly ejected from a casino, a tourist brought a federal diversity action against the casino, seeking $105,000 in damages. The tourist claimed that the casino's security guard had used excessive force when ejecting him from the casino, causing severe personal injuries. At trial, the tourist's attorney attempted to introduce testimony of the security guard's ex-wife, who had filed several complaints of spousal abuse against the security guard. The casino's attorney objected that the evidence was irrelevant and unfairly prejudicial. The court overruled the objection, and the ex-wife testified. The jury returned a verdict for the tourist, awarding him $82,000 in damages. The casino's attorney believes that the ex-wife's testimony was unfairly prejudicial and should not have been admitted. What is the best way for the casino's attorney to attack the verdict?","choices":{"A":"Appeal, arguing that the trial court erred in admitting the testimony, which was so prejudicial that the judgment should be reversed.","B":"Move for a new trial, arguing that the court erred in admitting the testimony and that a new trial without the testimony is necessary.","C":"Move for judgment as a matter of law, arguing that the court erred in admitting the testimony and that without the testimony, the evidence was legally insufficient to find for the tourist.","D":"Move for relief from judgment, arguing that the court's admission of the testimony was a mistake."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Within 28 days after entry of a final judgment, a party can move for a new trial on several grounds (see above image)—including that a prejudicial trial error occurred because the court erroneously admitted evidence. Here, the casino's attorney believes that the testimony by the security guard's ex-wife was unfairly prejudicial and should have been excluded. Therefore, the best way to attack the jury's verdict is to move for a new trial, arguing that the court erred in admitting the testimony and that a new trial without the testimony is necessary. (Choice A) A party may appeal to the federal appellate court encompassing the federal district court that issued an adverse finding, conclusion, or ruling (eg, erroneously admitted evidence). But since the appellate process is time-consuming and expensive, the most immediate way to obtain the desired relief is to move for a new trial. (Choice C) A judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. However, once the jury has returned its verdict, a party can only move for a renewed JMOL if that party had moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (not seen here). (Choice D) A party may move for relief from a final judgment in limited circumstances, including when the judgment was due to a mistake by the parties or the court. Judicial mistakes that qualify for such relief include mistakes in the entry of judgment, but not in admitting evidence. Therefore, this is not the best way to attack the verdict. Educational objective: A motion for a new trial can be made on several grounds, including that a prejudicial trial error occurred because the trial court erroneously admitted evidence that should have been excluded. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2805 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the grounds to move for a new trial).","rule":"A motion for a new trial can be made on several grounds, including that a prejudicial trial error occurred because the trial court erroneously admitted evidence that should have been excluded.","irac":{"issue":"After being forcibly ejected from a casino, a tourist brought a federal diversity action against the casino, seeking $105,000 in damages. The tourist claimed that the casino's security guard had used excessive force when ejecting him from the casino, causing severe personal injuries. At trial, the tourist's attorney attempted to introduce testimony of the security guard's ex-wife, who had filed several complaints of spousal abuse against the security guard. The casino's attorney objected that the evidence was irrelevant and unfairly prejudicial. The court overruled the objection, and the ex-wife testified. The jury returned a verdict for the tourist, awarding him $82,000 in damages. The casino's attorney believes that the ex-wife's testimony was unfairly prejudicial and should not have been admitted. What is the best way for the casino's attorney to attack the verdict?","rule":"A motion for a new trial can be made on several grounds, including that a prejudicial trial error occurred because the trial court erroneously admitted evidence that should have been excluded.","analysis":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Within 28 days after entry of a final judgment, a party can move for a new trial on several grounds (see above image)—including that a prejudicial trial error occurred because the court erroneously admitted evidence. Here, the casino's attorney believes that the testimony by the security guard's ex-wife was unfairly prejudicial and should have been excluded. Therefore, the best way to attack the jury's verdict is to move for a new trial, arguing that the court erred in admitting the testimony and that a new trial without the testimony is necessary. (Choice A) A party may appeal to the federal appellate court encompassing the federal district court that issued an adverse finding, conclusion, or ruling (eg, erroneously admitted evidence). But since the appellate process is time-consuming and expensive, the most immediate way to obtain the desired relief is to move for a new trial. (Choice C) A judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. However, once the jury has returned its verdict, a party can only move for a renewed JMOL if that party had moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (not seen here). (Choice D) A party may move for relief from a final judgment in limited circumstances, including when the judgment was due to a mistake by the parties or the court. Judicial mistakes that qualify for such relief include mistakes in the entry of judgment, but not in admitting evidence. Therefore, this is not the best way to attack the verdict. Educational objective: A motion for a new trial can be made on several grounds, including that a prejudicial trial error occurred because the trial court erroneously admitted evidence that should have been excluded. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2805 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the grounds to move for a new trial).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2805 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the grounds to move for a new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[232],"sourceAnswerPages":[233,234],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名游客被强行逐出赌场后，对赌场提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，请求 105,000 美元损害赔偿。游客主张，赌场保安将其逐出时使用过度武力，造成严重人身伤害。审判中，游客的律师试图提出保安前妻的证言；前妻曾多次投诉保安实施配偶虐待。赌场律师以证据不相关且具有不公平偏见为由提出异议。法院驳回异议，前妻出庭作证。陪审团作出有利于游客的裁决，判给 82,000 美元损害赔偿。赌场律师认为前妻证言具有不公平偏见，不应获准采纳。赌场律师攻击该裁决的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提起上诉，主张审判法院采纳证言属于错误，且偏见严重到应撤销判决。","B":"申请重新审判，主张法院采纳证言有误，必须在排除该证言后重新审判。","C":"申请依法判决，主张法院采纳证言有误，且若无该证言，证据在法律上不足以支持游客胜诉。","D":"申请判决救济，主张法院采纳该证言属于错误。"},"explanationZh":"重新审判的理由：\n• 造成不利影响的审判错误，例如错误的证据裁定；\n• 法官、律师、当事人或陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为；\n• 裁决不受证据明显权重支持；\n• 裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；\n• 损害赔偿过高或不足；\n• 新发现证据。\n\n在终局判决登记后 28 天内，当事人可以基于若干理由申请重新审判（见上图），包括审判法院错误采纳证据，导致造成不利影响的审判错误。\n\n本题中，赌场律师认为保安前妻的证言具有不公平偏见，本应排除。因此，攻击陪审团裁决的最佳方式是申请重新审判，主张法院采纳该证言有误，必须在不采纳该证言的情况下重新审判。\n\n（选项 A）当事人可以就联邦地区法院作出的不利认定、结论或裁定——例如错误采纳证据——向管辖该地区法院的联邦上诉法院上诉。但是，上诉程序耗时且费用高昂，因此，获得所需救济最直接的方式是申请重新审判。\n\n（选项 C）依法判决（JMOL）是因证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决，而为动议方作出的判决。但是，陪审团作出裁决后，当事人只有在案件交付陪审团前曾提出 JMOL 动议，才能提出续行 JMOL 动议；本题未见此情形。\n\n（选项 D）当事人可在有限情形下申请从终局判决获得救济，包括判决源于当事人或法院错误时。符合该救济条件的司法错误包括登记判决时发生的错误，但不包括证据采纳错误。因此，这不是攻击裁决的最佳方式。\n\n学习目标：审判法院错误采纳本应排除的证据，导致造成不利影响的审判错误时，当事人可以此为由申请重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（重新审判动议）。\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2805 (4th ed. 2020)（解释申请重新审判的理由）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-078","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":78,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"An investor purchased stock through a corporation's initial stock offering. When the price of the stock plummeted soon after the purchase, the investor sued the company in a federal district court, claiming that the company's offering materials violated federal securities laws and seeking $1 million in damages. The investor's suit proceeded to a bench trial. At the end of trial, the judge announced his findings of fact and conclusions of law and entered final judgment in favor of the corporation. Four years later, the U.S. government brought charges against current and former members of the corporation's board for bribing judges and jurors in suits involving the corporation. The charges described how the corporation had bribed the judge in the investor's case to find in its favor. The investor now seeks relief from the judgment. What is the best way for the investor to seek relief from the judgment?","choices":{"A":"Move for a new trial.","B":"Move for judgment as a matter of law.","C":"Move for relief from a void judgment.","D":"Move to set aside the judgment for fraud on the court."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A party may use a variety of posttrial motions to challenge a final judgment entered by a federal court. Posttrial motions generally have fixed deadlines (see image above). However, a federal court may at any time:\n• entertain an independent action to relieve a party from a judgment, order, or proceeding\n• grant relief as Congress directs in a federal statute or\n• set aside a judgment due to fraud on the court. Fraud on the court occurs when the integrity of the court is corrupted due to egregious conduct by judicial officers—eg, fabrication of evidence by an attorney, bribery of a judge or juror (as alleged here). Therefore, the best way for the investor to seek relief from the judgment four years after it was entered is to move to set it aside for fraud on the court. (Choice A) A motion for new trial must be made within 28 days after entry of the judgment—not four years later (as seen here). (Choice B) A motion for judgment as a matter of law must be made before a case is submitted to a jury—not four years after a bench trial (as seen here). In a bench trial, a party should instead move for a judgment on partial findings if the plaintiff has failed to prove his/her case. (Choice C) A party can seek relief from judgment on the ground that the judgment is void. This occurs when the court lacked subject-matter jurisdiction or violated due process by failing to give notice or an opportunity to be heard. Here, subject-matter jurisdiction existed because the claim arose under federal securities laws, and due process was satisfied since the investor could participate. Therefore, the judgment is not void. Educational objective: At any time, a federal court may (1) entertain an independent action to relieve a party from a judgment, order, or proceeding, (2) grant relief pursuant to a federal statute, or (3) set aside a judgment for fraud on the court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(d) (other powers to grant relief).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2870 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining fraud on the court).\n\n• Kupferman v. Consol. Research & Mfg. Corp., 459 F.2d 1072, 1078 (2d Cir. 1972) (defining the scope of fraud on the court).\n\nA car manufacturer sued one of its competitors for infringing the manufacturer's patented technology for its self-driving cars. During discovery, the manufacturer requested that the competitor produce all documents related to the competitor's research into self-driving technology. The competitor turned over millions of documents, none of which contained any information related to the manufacturer's patented technology. The case proceeded to trial, and the jury returned a verdict in favor of the competitor. Six months after the court entered a judgment consistent with this verdict, the competitor's CEO was charged with criminal negligence after several of the competitor's self-driving cars caused accidents. During a search of the competitor's research labs, the police discovered several documents containing information about the manufacturer's patented self-driving technology. The documents had been saved to the competitor's computers three years earlier. What action may the manufacturer take upon discovering this new evidence?\n\nA. Move for a new trial. B. Move for judgment as a matter of law. C. Move for relief from the prior judgment. D. Move for summary judgment.\n\nJudgment on the Pleadings 只看诉状直接裁判 双方已提交完complaint 和answer (rule 12c) Summary Judgment no genuine dispute of material fact and, 任何时间提出，但不迟于发现程序结束后30天内 (rule 56) entitled to judgment as a matter of law 非动议方提供具体证据，或请求延期 事实无争议，仅法律判断\n\nDefault Judgment 双方未答辩/未出庭 被告缺席，请求直接判胜 (rule 55) Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of 法律规定索赔律师费 final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment (rule 50) 对方证据完全不够支持其胜诉 New trial • Prejudicial trial error 偏见审判（程序或证据上错误） (rule 59a)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, 司法人员,陪审员,当事人不当行为 rule 50(a) 审判中判决 attorney, party, juror （陪审员私下调查）→疏忽 对方举证后\n\nrule 50(b)陪审团裁决后 • Verdict not supported by clear 判决与证据严重不符 28天内推翻裁定 renewed 有a才能有b, 无a不能提b weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on 基于虚假，不存在的证据 false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate 赔偿金额不合理 damages 审理已完成，新审重来 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据(重开庭审程序） Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment 明显的法律或事实错误 basis for judgment (rule 59e) • Intervening change in 适用法律发生重大变更 controlling law 已作出判决，可补入判决 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据（修改已有判决内容） Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake (rule 60a) • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission 文字错误；疏忽或遗漏错误 Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief (rule 60b) excusable neglect 错误，意外疏忽 final judgment 判决已生效，撤销重新来 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据(推翻判决,寻求实质性撤销)\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, 对方欺诈，误导或不正当行为 misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment 无效判决 Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, 判决已履行，解除或废止 discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief 其他足以构成救济的原因\n\nRelief (rule 60d) • Independent action to relief 独立诉讼撤销原判决\n• defendant not notified 被告未收到案件通知\n• set aside a judgment for fraud 法官受贿（法院系统本身欺诈）→故意\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to move for extraordinary relief from a final judgment or court order on limited grounds. This motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of a final judgment or order when based on any of the following grounds:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court.\n\n• The moving party discovered new evidence that (1) existed at the time of the trial and (2) could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial (ie, within 28 days of the entry of final judgment).\n\n• The opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.\n\nHere, the competitor engaged in misconduct during discovery when it failed to disclose the documents containing information about the manufacturer's patented technology. This newly discovered evidence existed at the time of trial but could not have reasonably been discovered until now. And since only six months have passed since the court entered a final judgment, the manufacturer can move for relief from this prior judgment. (Choice A) A motion for a new trial must be filed within 28 days after the final judgment is entered. Therefore, this motion would be untimely and likely denied. (Choice B) A party can renew a motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) within 28 days after the court enters final judgment as long as that party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury. (Choice D) A motion for summary judgment must be filed within 30 days after the end of discovery (ie, before trial). Educational objective: A party can move for extraordinary relief within one year from the entry of a final judgment or court order that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","rule":"At any time, a federal court may (1) entertain an independent action to relieve a party from a judgment, order, or proceeding, (2) grant relief pursuant to a federal statute, or (3) set aside a judgment for fraud on the court.","irac":{"issue":"An investor purchased stock through a corporation's initial stock offering. When the price of the stock plummeted soon after the purchase, the investor sued the company in a federal district court, claiming that the company's offering materials violated federal securities laws and seeking $1 million in damages. The investor's suit proceeded to a bench trial. At the end of trial, the judge announced his findings of fact and conclusions of law and entered final judgment in favor of the corporation. Four years later, the U.S. government brought charges against current and former members of the corporation's board for bribing judges and jurors in suits involving the corporation. The charges described how the corporation had bribed the judge in the investor's case to find in its favor. The investor now seeks relief from the judgment. What is the best way for the investor to seek relief from the judgment?","rule":"At any time, a federal court may (1) entertain an independent action to relieve a party from a judgment, order, or proceeding, (2) grant relief pursuant to a federal statute, or (3) set aside a judgment for fraud on the court.","analysis":"A party may use a variety of posttrial motions to challenge a final judgment entered by a federal court. Posttrial motions generally have fixed deadlines (see image above). However, a federal court may at any time:\n• entertain an independent action to relieve a party from a judgment, order, or proceeding\n• grant relief as Congress directs in a federal statute or\n• set aside a judgment due to fraud on the court. Fraud on the court occurs when the integrity of the court is corrupted due to egregious conduct by judicial officers—eg, fabrication of evidence by an attorney, bribery of a judge or juror (as alleged here). Therefore, the best way for the investor to seek relief from the judgment four years after it was entered is to move to set it aside for fraud on the court. (Choice A) A motion for new trial must be made within 28 days after entry of the judgment—not four years later (as seen here). (Choice B) A motion for judgment as a matter of law must be made before a case is submitted to a jury—not four years after a bench trial (as seen here). In a bench trial, a party should instead move for a judgment on partial findings if the plaintiff has failed to prove his/her case. (Choice C) A party can seek relief from judgment on the ground that the judgment is void. This occurs when the court lacked subject-matter jurisdiction or violated due process by failing to give notice or an opportunity to be heard. Here, subject-matter jurisdiction existed because the claim arose under federal securities laws, and due process was satisfied since the investor could participate. Therefore, the judgment is not void. Educational objective: At any time, a federal court may (1) entertain an independent action to relieve a party from a judgment, order, or proceeding, (2) grant relief pursuant to a federal statute, or (3) set aside a judgment for fraud on the court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(d) (other powers to grant relief).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2870 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining fraud on the court).\n\n• Kupferman v. Consol. Research & Mfg. Corp., 459 F.2d 1072, 1078 (2d Cir. 1972) (defining the scope of fraud on the court).\n\nA car manufacturer sued one of its competitors for infringing the manufacturer's patented technology for its self-driving cars. During discovery, the manufacturer requested that the competitor produce all documents related to the competitor's research into self-driving technology. The competitor turned over millions of documents, none of which contained any information related to the manufacturer's patented technology. The case proceeded to trial, and the jury returned a verdict in favor of the competitor. Six months after the court entered a judgment consistent with this verdict, the competitor's CEO was charged with criminal negligence after several of the competitor's self-driving cars caused accidents. During a search of the competitor's research labs, the police discovered several documents containing information about the manufacturer's patented self-driving technology. The documents had been saved to the competitor's computers three years earlier. What action may the manufacturer take upon discovering this new evidence?\n\nA. Move for a new trial. B. Move for judgment as a matter of law. C. Move for relief from the prior judgment. D. Move for summary judgment.\n\nJudgment on the Pleadings 只看诉状直接裁判 双方已提交完complaint 和answer (rule 12c) Summary Judgment no genuine dispute of material fact and, 任何时间提出，但不迟于发现程序结束后30天内 (rule 56) entitled to judgment as a matter of law 非动议方提供具体证据，或请求延期 事实无争议，仅法律判断\n\nDefault Judgment 双方未答辩/未出庭 被告缺席，请求直接判胜 (rule 55) Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of 法律规定索赔律师费 final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment (rule 50) 对方证据完全不够支持其胜诉 New trial • Prejudicial trial error 偏见审判（程序或证据上错误） (rule 59a)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, 司法人员,陪审员,当事人不当行为 rule 50(a) 审判中判决 attorney, party, juror （陪审员私下调查）→疏忽 对方举证后\n\nrule 50(b)陪审团裁决后 • Verdict not supported by clear 判决与证据严重不符 28天内推翻裁定 renewed 有a才能有b, 无a不能提b weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on 基于虚假，不存在的证据 false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate 赔偿金额不合理 damages 审理已完成，新审重来 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据(重开庭审程序） Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment 明显的法律或事实错误 basis for judgment (rule 59e) • Intervening change in 适用法律发生重大变更 controlling law 已作出判决，可补入判决 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据（修改已有判决内容） Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake (rule 60a) • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission 文字错误；疏忽或遗漏错误 Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief (rule 60b) excusable neglect 错误，意外疏忽 final judgment 判决已生效，撤销重新来 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据(推翻判决,寻求实质性撤销)\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, 对方欺诈，误导或不正当行为 misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment 无效判决 Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, 判决已履行，解除或废止 discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief 其他足以构成救济的原因\n\nRelief (rule 60d) • Independent action to relief 独立诉讼撤销原判决\n• defendant not notified 被告未收到案件通知\n• set aside a judgment for fraud 法官受贿（法院系统本身欺诈）→故意\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to move for extraordinary relief from a final judgment or court order on limited grounds. This motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of a final judgment or order when based on any of the following grounds:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court.\n\n• The moving party discovered new evidence that (1) existed at the time of the trial and (2) could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial (ie, within 28 days of the entry of final judgment).\n\n• The opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.\n\nHere, the competitor engaged in misconduct during discovery when it failed to disclose the documents containing information about the manufacturer's patented technology. This newly discovered evidence existed at the time of trial but could not have reasonably been discovered until now. And since only six months have passed since the court entered a final judgment, the manufacturer can move for relief from this prior judgment. (Choice A) A motion for a new trial must be filed within 28 days after the final judgment is entered. Therefore, this motion would be untimely and likely denied. (Choice B) A party can renew a motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) within 28 days after the court enters final judgment as long as that party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury. (Choice D) A motion for summary judgment must be filed within 30 days after the end of discovery (ie, before trial). Educational objective: A party can move for extraordinary relief within one year from the entry of a final judgment or court order that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(d) (other powers to grant relief).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2870 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining fraud on the court).\n\n• Kupferman v. Consol. Research & Mfg. Corp., 459 F.2d 1072, 1078 (2d Cir. 1972) (defining the scope of fraud on the court).\n\nA car manufacturer sued one of its competitors for infringing the manufacturer's patented technology for its self-driving cars. During discovery, the manufacturer requested that the competitor produce all documents related to the competitor's research into self-driving technology. The competitor turned over millions of documents, none of which contained any information related to the manufacturer's patented technology. The case proceeded to trial, and the jury returned a verdict in favor of the competitor. Six months after the court entered a judgment consistent with this verdict, the competitor's CEO was charged with criminal negligence after several of the competitor's self-driving cars caused accidents. During a search of the competitor's research labs, the police discovered several documents containing information about the manufacturer's patented self-driving technology. The documents had been saved to the competitor's computers three years earlier. What action may the manufacturer take upon discovering this new evidence?\n\nA. Move for a new trial. B. Move for judgment as a matter of law. C. Move for relief from the prior judgment. D. Move for summary judgment.\n\nJudgment on the Pleadings 只看诉状直接裁判 双方已提交完complaint 和answer (rule 12c) Summary Judgment no genuine dispute of material fact and, 任何时间提出，但不迟于发现程序结束后30天内 (rule 56) entitled to judgment as a matter of law 非动议方提供具体证据，或请求延期 事实无争议，仅法律判断\n\nDefault Judgment 双方未答辩/未出庭 被告缺席，请求直接判胜 (rule 55) Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of 法律规定索赔律师费 final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment (rule 50) 对方证据完全不够支持其胜诉 New trial • Prejudicial trial error 偏见审判（程序或证据上错误） (rule 59a)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, 司法人员,陪审员,当事人不当行为 rule 50(a) 审判中判决 attorney, party, juror （陪审员私下调查）→疏忽 对方举证后\n\nrule 50(b)陪审团裁决后 • Verdict not supported by clear 判决与证据严重不符 28天内推翻裁定 renewed 有a才能有b, 无a不能提b weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on 基于虚假，不存在的证据 false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate 赔偿金额不合理 damages 审理已完成，新审重来 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据(重开庭审程序） Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment 明显的法律或事实错误 basis for judgment (rule 59e) • Intervening change in 适用法律发生重大变更 controlling law 已作出判决，可补入判决 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据（修改已有判决内容） Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake (rule 60a) • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission 文字错误；疏忽或遗漏错误 Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief (rule 60b) excusable neglect 错误，意外疏忽 final judgment 判决已生效，撤销重新来 • Newly discovered evidence 新发现证据(推翻判决,寻求实质性撤销)\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, 对方欺诈，误导或不正当行为 misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment 无效判决 Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, 判决已履行，解除或废止 discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief 其他足以构成救济的原因\n\nRelief (rule 60d) • Independent action to relief 独立诉讼撤销原判决\n• defendant not notified 被告未收到案件通知\n• set aside a judgment for fraud 法官受贿（法院系统本身欺诈）→故意\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to move for extraordinary relief from a final judgment or court order on limited grounds. This motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of a final judgment or order when based on any of the following grounds:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court.\n\n• The moving party discovered new evidence that (1) existed at the time of the trial and (2) could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial (ie, within 28 days of the entry of final judgment).\n\n• The opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.\n\nHere, the competitor engaged in misconduct during discovery when it failed to disclose the documents containing information about the manufacturer's patented technology. This newly discovered evidence existed at the time of trial but could not have reasonably been discovered until now. And since only six months have passed since the court entered a final judgment, the manufacturer can move for relief from this prior judgment. (Choice A) A motion for a new trial must be filed within 28 days after the final judgment is entered. Therefore, this motion would be untimely and likely denied. (Choice B) A party can renew a motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) within 28 days after the court enters final judgment as long as that party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury. (Choice D) A motion for summary judgment must be filed within 30 days after the end of discovery (ie, before trial). Educational objective: A party can move for extraordinary relief within one year from the entry of a final judgment or court order that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[235],"sourceAnswerPages":[236,237,238,239,240],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名投资者通过一家公司的首次公开募股购买股票。股票价格在买入后不久暴跌，投资者遂在联邦地区法院起诉公司，主张公司的发行材料违反联邦证券法，请求 100 万美元损害赔偿。案件经法官审判。审判结束时，法官宣布事实认定和法律结论，并作出有利于公司的终局判决。四年后，美国政府起诉公司现任及前任董事会成员，指控他们在涉及公司的诉讼中贿赂法官和陪审员。指控说明，公司曾贿赂投资者案件的法官，使其作出有利于公司的裁判。投资者现请求从该判决获得救济。投资者寻求判决救济的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请重新审判。","B":"申请依法判决。","C":"申请从无效判决获得救济。","D":"以欺诈法院为由申请撤销判决。"},"explanationZh":"当事人可以使用多种审判后动议，挑战联邦法院作出的终局判决。审判后动议通常有固定期限（见上图）。但是，联邦法院可以随时：\n• 受理请求解除当事人所受判决、命令或程序约束的独立诉讼；\n• 按国会在联邦法律中的规定给予救济；或\n• 因欺诈法院而撤销判决。\n\n司法人员的恶劣行为腐蚀法院廉正性时，构成欺诈法院，例如律师伪造证据、贿赂法官或陪审员，本题所称行为即属此类。因此，判决作出四年后，投资者寻求救济的最佳方式，是以欺诈法院为由申请撤销判决。\n\n（选项 A）重新审判动议必须在登记判决后 28 天内提出，而不能在四年后提出。\n\n（选项 B）依法判决动议必须在案件交付陪审团前提出，而不能在法官审判四年后提出。在法官审判中，原告未能证明其案件时，当事人应申请就部分认定作出判决。\n\n（选项 C）当事人可以判决无效为由请求判决救济。法院缺乏事项管辖权，或未给予通知或陈述意见机会而违反正当程序时，判决无效。本题请求依据联邦证券法产生，故法院享有事项管辖权；投资者能够参与诉讼，正当程序也已满足。因此，判决并非无效。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院可以随时：(1) 受理请求解除当事人所受判决、命令或程序约束的独立诉讼；(2) 根据联邦法律给予救济；或 (3) 因欺诈法院而撤销判决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(d)（给予救济的其他权力）。\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2870 (3d ed. 2020)（解释欺诈法院）。\n• Kupferman v. Consol. Research & Mfg. Corp., 459 F.2d 1072, 1078 (2d Cir. 1972)（界定欺诈法院的范围）。\n\n补充例题：\n\n一家汽车制造商起诉其竞争对手侵犯该制造商自动驾驶汽车的专利技术。在证据开示期间，制造商请求竞争对手提交与其自动驾驶技术研究相关的全部文件。竞争对手移交数百万份文件，但没有一份包含与制造商专利技术相关的信息。案件进入审判，陪审团作出有利于竞争对手的裁决。法院依该裁决登记判决六个月后，因竞争对手的多辆自动驾驶汽车造成事故，其首席执行官被控刑事过失。警方搜查竞争对手的研究实验室时，发现多份包含制造商自动驾驶专利技术信息的文件。这些文件三年前已保存于竞争对手的计算机。制造商发现这项新证据后，可以采取什么行动？\n\nA. 申请重新审判。\nB. 申请依法判决。\nC. 申请从先前判决获得救济。\nD. 申请简易判决。\n\n诉状判决｜仅依据诉状直接裁判；起诉状和答辩状均已提交完毕（Rule 12(c)）。\n\n简易判决｜不存在真实的重大事实争议，且动议方依法有权获得判决；可在任何时间提出，但不得迟于证据开示结束后 30 天（Rule 56）。非动议方须提出具体证据，或请求延期。事实无争议，仅需作法律判断。\n\n缺席判决｜一方未答辩或未出庭时，另一方请求直接判胜（Rule 55）。\n\n审判后救济：\n\n救济｜理由｜时限\n律师费｜法律或规则允许获得律师费｜终局判决登记后 14 天内\n依法判决（Rule 50）｜证据在法律上不足以支持非动议方胜诉｜终局判决登记后 28 天内；Rule 50(a) 为审判中、对方举证后提出；Rule 50(b) 为陪审团裁决后续行提出，必须先有 50(a)，否则不能提出 50(b)\n重新审判（Rule 59(a)）｜造成不利影响的审判错误；法官、律师、当事人或陪审员造成不利影响的不当行为，例如陪审员私下调查；裁决不受证据明显权重支持；裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；损害赔偿过高或不足；新发现证据｜审判已完成，重新开始审理\n修改判决（Rule 59(e)）｜构成判决基础的明显法律或事实错误；适用控制性法律发生重大变化；新发现证据｜在已有判决中作出修改\n更正错误（Rule 60(a)）｜书记性错误；因疏忽或遗漏产生的错误｜可以自由更正；已进入上诉法院审理日程时须经上诉法院许可\n特别救济（Rule 60(b)）｜错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；新发现证据；对方欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为｜终局判决登记后一年内\n特别救济（Rule 60(b)）｜无效判决；判决已履行、解除或撤销；其他足以给予救济的理由｜合理时间内\n其他救济（Rule 60(d)）｜提起独立诉讼撤销原判决；被告未收到案件通知；以欺诈法院为由撤销判决，例如法官受贿｜依其性质处理\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》60(b) 允许当事人在有限理由下，申请从终局判决或法院命令获得特别救济。此动议通常须在合理时间内提出；若基于下列任一理由，则必须在终局判决或命令登记后一年内提出：\n• 判决源于当事人或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；\n• 动议方发现新证据，且该证据：(1) 在审判时已经存在；并且 (2) 无法合理地在提出重新审判动议的期限内——即终局判决登记后 28 天内——发现；\n• 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n本题中，竞争对手在证据开示期间没有披露包含制造商专利技术信息的文件，构成不当行为。该新发现证据在审判时已经存在，却无法在此前合理发现。法院登记终局判决后仅经过六个月，因此，制造商可以申请从先前判决获得救济。\n\n（选项 A）重新审判动议必须在终局判决登记后 28 天内提交，所以该动议已经逾期，很可能被驳回。\n\n（选项 B）只要当事人在案件交付陪审团前已提出依法判决（JMOL）动议，即可在法院登记终局判决后 28 天内续行提出 JMOL 动议。\n\n（选项 D）简易判决动议必须在证据开示结束后 30 天内，即审判前提交。\n\n学习目标：若终局判决或法院命令基于以下任一原因，当事人可在登记后一年内申请特别救济：(1) 错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；(2) 新发现证据；或 (3) 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（从判决获得特别救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-030"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-079","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":79,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A consumer from State A sued a fireworks manufacturer from State B in federal court. The consumer claimed that she suffered severe burns when the fuses on the manufacturer's fireworks malfunctioned. At the conclusion of trial, the jury awarded the consumer $200,000 for her medical bills, $50,000 for her lost wages, and $5 million for her pain and suffering. The court entered judgment consistent with this verdict. The consumer then filed a motion claiming that the amount of damages awarded for her medical bills and lost wages are inadequate and requesting that the court increase these awards. The manufacturer filed a motion claiming that the amount of damages awarded for pain and suffering is excessive and requesting that the court decrease that amount or order a new trial on damages. The court agrees with both parties' contentions. How will the court likely rule on the parties' motions?","choices":{"A":"Deny both motions.","B":"Deny the consumer's motion and grant the manufacturer's motion.","C":"Grant both motions.","D":"Grant the consumer's motion and deny the manufacturer's motion."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Altering jury's award of damages\n\n(FRCP 59(e))\n\nType of Definition Applicability alteration\n\nRemittitur Defendant's request to reduce jury Plaintiff must be offered choice award of excessive damages between:\n\n• reduced damages or\n• new trial on damages Additur Plaintiff's request to increase jury award Never allowed in federal court of inadequate damages\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nAdditur is a request that a court increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages. Additur is permitted in many state courts, but it is not allowed in federal court because it violates the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial.* That is because additur allows the court to grant an award of damages that was not found by the jury—the ultimate finder of fact. Therefore, the court will deny the consumer's request to increase the jury award of damages for medical bills and lost wages (Choices C & D). Remittitur, in contrast, is a request that the court reduce an allegedly excessive jury award of damages. If the request is granted, the opposing party must choose between (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages. Remittitur does not violate the Seventh Amendment because the jury has already determined the damages and the court is simply trimming the excess. Since the court agrees that the damages for pain and suffering are excessive, it will likely grant the manufacturer's motion regarding those damages (Choices A & D). *The Seventh Amendment has never been applied to the states, so states need not provide a jury trial to civil litigants. Educational objective: A federal court cannot grant a request to increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages (ie, additur), but it can reduce an allegedly excessive jury award or order a new trial on damages (ie, remittitur). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motions for additur and remittitur).\n\n• Dimick v. Schiedt, 293 U.S. 474, 486–87 (1935) (holding that additur violates the Seventh Amendment).\n\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998) (explaining the requirements for remittitur).","rule":"A federal court cannot grant a request to increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages (ie, additur), but it can reduce an allegedly excessive jury award or order a new trial on damages (ie, remittitur).","irac":{"issue":"A consumer from State A sued a fireworks manufacturer from State B in federal court. The consumer claimed that she suffered severe burns when the fuses on the manufacturer's fireworks malfunctioned. At the conclusion of trial, the jury awarded the consumer $200,000 for her medical bills, $50,000 for her lost wages, and $5 million for her pain and suffering. The court entered judgment consistent with this verdict. The consumer then filed a motion claiming that the amount of damages awarded for her medical bills and lost wages are inadequate and requesting that the court increase these awards. The manufacturer filed a motion claiming that the amount of damages awarded for pain and suffering is excessive and requesting that the court decrease that amount or order a new trial on damages. The court agrees with both parties' contentions. How will the court likely rule on the parties' motions?","rule":"A federal court cannot grant a request to increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages (ie, additur), but it can reduce an allegedly excessive jury award or order a new trial on damages (ie, remittitur).","analysis":"Altering jury's award of damages\n\n(FRCP 59(e))\n\nType of Definition Applicability alteration\n\nRemittitur Defendant's request to reduce jury Plaintiff must be offered choice award of excessive damages between:\n\n• reduced damages or\n• new trial on damages Additur Plaintiff's request to increase jury award Never allowed in federal court of inadequate damages\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nAdditur is a request that a court increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages. Additur is permitted in many state courts, but it is not allowed in federal court because it violates the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial.* That is because additur allows the court to grant an award of damages that was not found by the jury—the ultimate finder of fact. Therefore, the court will deny the consumer's request to increase the jury award of damages for medical bills and lost wages (Choices C & D). Remittitur, in contrast, is a request that the court reduce an allegedly excessive jury award of damages. If the request is granted, the opposing party must choose between (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages. Remittitur does not violate the Seventh Amendment because the jury has already determined the damages and the court is simply trimming the excess. Since the court agrees that the damages for pain and suffering are excessive, it will likely grant the manufacturer's motion regarding those damages (Choices A & D). *The Seventh Amendment has never been applied to the states, so states need not provide a jury trial to civil litigants. Educational objective: A federal court cannot grant a request to increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages (ie, additur), but it can reduce an allegedly excessive jury award or order a new trial on damages (ie, remittitur). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motions for additur and remittitur).\n\n• Dimick v. Schiedt, 293 U.S. 474, 486–87 (1935) (holding that additur violates the Seventh Amendment).\n\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998) (explaining the requirements for remittitur).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motions for additur and remittitur).\n\n• Dimick v. Schiedt, 293 U.S. 474, 486–87 (1935) (holding that additur violates the Seventh Amendment).\n\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998) (explaining the requirements for remittitur).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[241],"sourceAnswerPages":[242,243],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名消费者在联邦法院起诉 B 州一家烟花制造商。消费者主张，该制造商的烟花引信发生故障，造成其严重烧伤。审判结束时，陪审团判给消费者 200,000 美元医疗费用、50,000 美元误工损失以及 500 万美元痛苦与折磨赔偿。法院依该裁决登记判决。消费者随后提出动议，主张医疗费用和误工损失赔偿不足，请求法院增加这两项金额。制造商也提出动议，主张痛苦与折磨赔偿过高，请求法院减少金额或就损害赔偿重新审判。法院同意双方的主张。法院可能如何裁定双方动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回双方动议。","B":"驳回消费者的动议，批准制造商的动议。","C":"批准双方动议。","D":"批准消费者的动议，驳回制造商的动议。"},"explanationZh":"改变陪审团判给的损害赔偿（FRCP 59(e)）：\n\n改变类型｜定义｜适用性\n减额裁决（remittitur）｜被告请求减少陪审团判给的过高损害赔偿｜必须让原告选择：接受减少后的赔偿，或就损害赔偿重新审判\n增额裁决（additur）｜原告请求增加陪审团判给的不足损害赔偿｜联邦法院绝不允许\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n增额裁决是请求法院增加被指过低的陪审团损害赔偿。许多州法院允许增额裁决，但联邦法院不允许，因为这违反第七修正案所保障的陪审团审判权。* 原因是，增额裁决允许法院判给陪审团——最终事实认定者——并未认定的赔偿。因此，法院会驳回消费者增加医疗费用和误工损失赔偿的请求（选项 C、D）。\n\n相比之下，减额裁决是请求法院减少被指过高的陪审团损害赔偿。若批准该请求，必须让对方在以下两项中选择：(1) 接受减少后的赔偿；或 (2) 就损害赔偿重新审判。减额裁决不违反第七修正案，因为陪审团已经确定损害赔偿，法院只是削减超出部分。法院同意痛苦与折磨赔偿过高，所以很可能批准制造商针对该项赔偿提出的动议（选项 A、D）。\n\n*第七修正案从未适用于各州，所以各州无需向民事诉讼当事人提供陪审团审判。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院不得批准增加被指过低的陪审团损害赔偿的请求，即增额裁决；但可以减少被指过高的陪审团赔偿或命令就损害赔偿重新审判，即减额裁决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（增额裁决和减额裁决动议）。\n• Dimick v. Schiedt, 293 U.S. 474, 486–87 (1935)（认定增额裁决违反第七修正案）。\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998)（解释减额裁决的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-080","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":80,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A group of parents sued a public school district in a federal district court to enjoin the school district from using public funds and resources for educational programs at religious schools. The judge presiding over the case concluded that the programs violated the establishment clause and issued a permanent injunction against the school to prevent it from continuing with these programs. Twenty years later, the school district filed a motion in the federal district court to obtain relief from the injunction. The school district argued that establishment clause law had changed so significantly that the programs are now constitutional. May the court consider this motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because only an appellate court may provide relief from the judgment.","B":"No, because the motion was not filed within one year from the entry of final judgment.","C":"Yes, because the motion concerns a constitutional issue.","D":"Yes, because the motion concerns law that may have significantly changed."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b), a party may move for extraordinary relief from a court's final judgment or order on limited grounds. The motion must be made within one year from the court's judgment for any of the following reasons: 1. The judgment was based on the parties or the court's mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect. 2. The moving party discovered new evidence. 3. An opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. All other FRCP 60(b) motions can be made within a reasonable time under the circumstances. This includes FRCP 60(b)(5), which authorizes relief from a judgment that:\n• has been satisfied, released, or discharged\n• is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated or\n• will violate equity if applied prospectively. In Agostini v. Felton, the Supreme Court held that it violates equity to prospectively apply a judgment that was based on facts or law that have significantly changed since the court issued it. The school district's motion for relief challenges the permanent injunction on this ground, so the one-year deadline does not apply. And the motion was made within a reasonable time because the school district needed to wait until the law had significantly changed before requesting relief. As a result, the court may consider the school district's motion (Choice B).\n\n(Choice A) An appellate court may only provide relief from a judgment under FRCP 60(b) after a district court has entered a final order pertaining to such relief, which has yet to occur here. (Choice C) The mere fact that the motion concerns a constitutional issue (ie, the establishment clause) does not provide a basis to consider this motion for relief. Educational objective: Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (relief from judgment).\n\n• Agostini v. Felton, 521 U.S. 203, 237 (1997) (holding that it is inequitable to prospectively apply an injunction that is based on law that has significantly changed since the injunction was issued).","rule":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively.","irac":{"issue":"A group of parents sued a public school district in a federal district court to enjoin the school district from using public funds and resources for educational programs at religious schools. The judge presiding over the case concluded that the programs violated the establishment clause and issued a permanent injunction against the school to prevent it from continuing with these programs. Twenty years later, the school district filed a motion in the federal district court to obtain relief from the injunction. The school district argued that establishment clause law had changed so significantly that the programs are now constitutional. May the court consider this motion?","rule":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively.","analysis":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b), a party may move for extraordinary relief from a court's final judgment or order on limited grounds. The motion must be made within one year from the court's judgment for any of the following reasons: 1. The judgment was based on the parties or the court's mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect. 2. The moving party discovered new evidence. 3. An opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. All other FRCP 60(b) motions can be made within a reasonable time under the circumstances. This includes FRCP 60(b)(5), which authorizes relief from a judgment that:\n• has been satisfied, released, or discharged\n• is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated or\n• will violate equity if applied prospectively. In Agostini v. Felton, the Supreme Court held that it violates equity to prospectively apply a judgment that was based on facts or law that have significantly changed since the court issued it. The school district's motion for relief challenges the permanent injunction on this ground, so the one-year deadline does not apply. And the motion was made within a reasonable time because the school district needed to wait until the law had significantly changed before requesting relief. As a result, the court may consider the school district's motion (Choice B).\n\n(Choice A) An appellate court may only provide relief from a judgment under FRCP 60(b) after a district court has entered a final order pertaining to such relief, which has yet to occur here. (Choice C) The mere fact that the motion concerns a constitutional issue (ie, the establishment clause) does not provide a basis to consider this motion for relief. Educational objective: Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (relief from judgment).\n\n• Agostini v. Felton, 521 U.S. 203, 237 (1997) (holding that it is inequitable to prospectively apply an injunction that is based on law that has significantly changed since the injunction was issued).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (relief from judgment).\n\n• Agostini v. Felton, 521 U.S. 203, 237 (1997) (holding that it is inequitable to prospectively apply an injunction that is based on law that has significantly changed since the injunction was issued).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[244],"sourceAnswerPages":[245,246],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一群家长在联邦地区法院起诉某公立学区，请求禁止该学区使用公共资金和资源，为宗教学校的教育项目提供支持。主审法官认定这些项目违反政教分离条款，并对学区发布永久禁令，禁止其继续实施项目。二十年后，学区向该联邦地区法院提出动议，请求从禁令获得救济。学区主张，政教分离条款法律已发生如此重大的变化，以致这些项目现已符合宪法。法院可以审理该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为只有上诉法院可以给予判决救济。","B":"不可以，因为该动议未在终局判决登记后一年内提出。","C":"可以，因为该动议涉及宪法问题。","D":"可以，因为该动议涉及可能已经发生重大变化的法律。"},"explanationZh":"从终局判决获得救济（FRCP 60(b)）：\n\n救济理由｜提出时限\n错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽｜一年内\n新发现证据｜一年内\n欺诈、虚假陈述、不当行为｜一年内\n无效判决，例如缺乏管辖权｜合理时间内\n判决已履行、解除或撤销；判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或者继续适用将违反衡平原则｜合理时间内\n其他足以给予救济的理由（极少适用）｜合理时间内\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）60(b)，当事人可以基于有限理由申请从法院终局判决或命令获得特别救济。基于以下任一理由提出时，动议必须在法院判决后一年内提出：\n1. 判决基于当事人或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；\n2. 动议方发现新证据；\n3. 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n其他 FRCP 60(b) 动议均可在具体情形下的合理时间内提出。其中包括 FRCP 60(b)(5)，该条授权从符合下列任一情形的判决获得救济：\n• 判决已经履行、解除或撤销；\n• 判决基于先前已被撤销或废止的判决；或\n• 继续适用判决将违反衡平原则。\n\n在 Agostini v. Felton 案中，最高法院认定，判决作出后，如果其依据的事实或法律发生重大变化，继续适用该判决将违反衡平原则。学区的救济动议正是以此为由挑战永久禁令，故不适用一年期限。学区必须等待法律发生重大变化后方可请求救济，所以动议也在合理时间内提出。因此，法院可以审理学区的动议（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）只有在地区法院已针对该项救济作出终局命令后，上诉法院才能依据 FRCP 60(b) 提供判决救济；本题尚未发生。\n\n（选项 C）动议涉及宪法问题——即政教分离条款——这一事实本身，并不构成法院审理该救济动议的依据。\n\n学习目标：依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》60(b)(5)，当事人可以在合理时间内申请从符合以下任一情形的判决获得救济：(1) 判决已履行、解除或撤销；(2) 判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或 (3) 继续适用判决将违反衡平原则。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（判决救济）。\n• Agostini v. Felton, 521 U.S. 203, 237 (1997)（认定基于禁令作出后已发生重大变化之法律而继续适用禁令，违反衡平原则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-081","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":81,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action in a federal district court and served process on the defendant. After thoroughly reviewing the plaintiff's complaint, the defendant's attorney could not reasonably identify the grounds upon which the plaintiff's claims for relief rest. The defendant's attorney moved for a more definite statement, and the court ordered the plaintiff's attorney to file a more definite statement. What should the plaintiff's attorney do?","choices":{"A":"File a reply within 21 days.","B":"File a supplemental complaint within 14 days.","C":"File an amended complaint within 14 days.","D":"File an answer within 21 days."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Motion for more definite statement\n\n(FRCP 12(e))\n\nRequest Motion must:\n\n• specify pleading's defects & details needed to cure defects and\n• be made before filing responsive pleading (eg, answer) Response Defect must be cured:\n\n• by filing amended pleading within 14 days after court order granting motion FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA party should file a motion for a more definite statement of a pleading (eg, complaint) if the pleading is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably prepare a responsive pleading (eg, answer). Here, the defendant's attorney could not reasonably identify the grounds upon which the plaintiff's claims for relief rest after thoroughly reviewing the complaint. Therefore, a motion for a more definite statement was appropriate. When the court grants such a motion, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement in an amended pleading within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court. Failure to do so allows the court to strike the pleading or issue another appropriate order. Therefore, the plaintiff's attorney should file an amended complaint within 14 days of the court order. (Choice A) A reply is a response to an answer that is made only when the court orders it. If the court orders a reply, the party must serve it within 21 days after being served with the court order (unless the order states otherwise). Here, the plaintiff cannot file a reply since the defendant has not yet filed an answer and no court order authorizes a reply. (Choice B) A supplemental pleading (eg, supplemental complaint) sets forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed. Conversely, an amended pleading sets forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed (as seen here). (Choice D) An answer is a pleading that addresses the merits of the dispute and presents defenses and counterclaims. This pleading is appropriate only in response to a plaintiff's complaint or a defendant's counterclaim. Since neither is being responded to here, the plaintiff cannot file an answer. Educational objective:\n\nWhen a court grants a motion for a more definite statement, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement in an amended pleading within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1379 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining that the nonmovant must file an amended pleading within 14 days after a court orders a more definite statement).","rule":"When a court grants a motion for a more definite statement, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement in an amended pleading within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action in a federal district court and served process on the defendant. After thoroughly reviewing the plaintiff's complaint, the defendant's attorney could not reasonably identify the grounds upon which the plaintiff's claims for relief rest. The defendant's attorney moved for a more definite statement, and the court ordered the plaintiff's attorney to file a more definite statement. What should the plaintiff's attorney do?","rule":"When a court grants a motion for a more definite statement, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement in an amended pleading within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court.","analysis":"Motion for more definite statement\n\n(FRCP 12(e))\n\nRequest Motion must:\n\n• specify pleading's defects & details needed to cure defects and\n• be made before filing responsive pleading (eg, answer) Response Defect must be cured:\n\n• by filing amended pleading within 14 days after court order granting motion FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA party should file a motion for a more definite statement of a pleading (eg, complaint) if the pleading is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably prepare a responsive pleading (eg, answer). Here, the defendant's attorney could not reasonably identify the grounds upon which the plaintiff's claims for relief rest after thoroughly reviewing the complaint. Therefore, a motion for a more definite statement was appropriate. When the court grants such a motion, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement in an amended pleading within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court. Failure to do so allows the court to strike the pleading or issue another appropriate order. Therefore, the plaintiff's attorney should file an amended complaint within 14 days of the court order. (Choice A) A reply is a response to an answer that is made only when the court orders it. If the court orders a reply, the party must serve it within 21 days after being served with the court order (unless the order states otherwise). Here, the plaintiff cannot file a reply since the defendant has not yet filed an answer and no court order authorizes a reply. (Choice B) A supplemental pleading (eg, supplemental complaint) sets forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed. Conversely, an amended pleading sets forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed (as seen here). (Choice D) An answer is a pleading that addresses the merits of the dispute and presents defenses and counterclaims. This pleading is appropriate only in response to a plaintiff's complaint or a defendant's counterclaim. Since neither is being responded to here, the plaintiff cannot file an answer. Educational objective:\n\nWhen a court grants a motion for a more definite statement, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement in an amended pleading within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1379 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining that the nonmovant must file an amended pleading within 14 days after a court orders a more definite statement).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1379 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining that the nonmovant must file an amended pleading within 14 days after a court orders a more definite statement).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[247],"sourceAnswerPages":[248,249],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院提起多元州籍诉讼，并向被告送达诉讼文书。被告律师彻底审阅原告的起诉状后，仍无法合理识别原告救济请求所依据的理由。被告律师申请更明确陈述，法院命令原告律师提交更明确陈述。原告律师应当怎么做？","choicesZh":{"A":"在 21 天内提交答复。","B":"在 14 天内提交补充起诉状。","C":"在 14 天内提交修改后的起诉状。","D":"在 21 天内提交答辩状。"},"explanationZh":"申请更明确陈述（FRCP 12(e)）：\n\n请求｜动议必须：\n• 指明诉状缺陷及补正缺陷所需的细节；并且\n• 在提交回应性诉状（例如答辩状）前提出。\n\n回应｜必须通过以下方式补正缺陷：\n• 在法院批准动议的命令作出后 14 天内，提交修改后的诉状。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n一份诉状（例如起诉状）含糊或模棱两可到使当事人无法合理准备回应性诉状（例如答辩状）时，该当事人应申请更明确陈述。本题中，被告律师彻底审阅起诉状后，仍无法合理识别原告救济请求所依据的理由。因此，申请更明确陈述是适当的。\n\n法院批准该动议时，非动议方必须在收到命令通知后 14 天内，或法院另定期限内，通过修改后的诉状提供更明确陈述。未这样做时，法院可以删除该诉状或作出其他适当命令。因此，原告律师应在法院命令后 14 天内提交修改后的起诉状。\n\n（选项 A）答复是对答辩状的回应，只有法院命令时方可提出。法院命令答复时，除命令另有规定外，当事人必须在收到法院命令送达后 21 天内送达答复。本题被告尚未提交答辩状，也没有法院命令授权答复，所以原告不能提交答复。\n\n（选项 B）补充诉状（例如补充起诉状）陈述被补充诉状提交后才发生的交易、事件或事实。相反，修改后的诉状陈述被修改诉状提交前已经发生的交易、事件或事实，本题属于后者。\n\n（选项 D）答辩状是回应争议实体并提出抗辩和反诉的诉状，只有在回应原告起诉状或被告反诉时才适当。本题原告并非回应这两者，故不能提交答辩状。\n\n学习目标：法院批准更明确陈述动议时，非动议方必须在收到命令通知后 14 天内，或法院另定期限内，通过修改后的诉状提供更明确陈述。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e)（更明确陈述动议）。\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1379 (3d ed. 2020)（说明法院命令作出更明确陈述后，非动议方必须在 14 天内提交修改后的诉状）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-082","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":82,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A construction worker sued an insulation manufacturer in federal court, claiming that he had developed a chronic health condition as a result of 20 years of exposure to the manufacturer's insulation at his work sites. The manufacturer answered, denying all liability and stating that it had never supplied its insulation to the worker's employer. The worker's attorney deposed the manufacturer's president, and the manufacturer's attorney deposed the worker. Immediately thereafter, the manufacturer moved for summary judgment on the ground that the worker had no evidence showing that the insulation had ever been used by the worker's employer. What would be the worker's best response to the motion for summary judgment?","choices":{"A":"Argue that more time is needed for additional discovery to show the manufacturer's liability, and attach a declaration describing the desired discovery.","B":"Argue that the motion should be denied, because a central issue in the case will be the manufacturer's credibility on the question of its distribution of the insulation, and only a jury can decide questions of credibility.","C":"Argue that the motion should be denied, because the manufacturer failed to attach any evidence to its motion to show that the insulation was not used by the worker's employer.","D":"Make a cross-motion for summary judgment arguing that the manufacturer has introduced no evidence to show that its insulation did not harm the worker."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for summary judgment requests that the court enter judgment without a full trial because (1) there is no genuine dispute of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The movant can meet this burden by asserting that the nonmovant lacks evidentiary support for any issue that the nonmovant has the burden to prove—eg, no evidence that the manufacturer's insulation had been used by the worker's employer. If this occurs, the nonmovant can avoid summary judgment by either:\n• presenting evidence to support that issue through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence or\n\n• requesting that the court postpone consideration of the motion until additional discovery can take place and including an affidavit or declaration that describes the desired discovery and why it is needed.\n\nSince the manufacturer moved for summary judgment after only two depositions, the worker likely needs additional discovery to obtain evidence showing that its employer had used the manufacturer's insulation. Therefore, the worker should argue that more time is\n\nneeded for additional discovery and attach a declaration or affidavit describing the desired discovery. (Choice B) Although the manufacturer's credibility will be an important issue at trial, a court never considers the credibility of evidence when making a summary judgment determination. Instead, it will assume that the manufacturer's no-evidence assertion is true unless the worker (nonmovant) presents evidence creating a genuine dispute of material fact. That evidence will then be viewed in the light most favorable to the worker. (Choices C & D) Since the worker has the burden to prove that the insulation was used by his employer and harmed him, the manufacturer is not required to provide evidence negating these assertions. It need only claim that the worker lacks evidence to support those elements of his claim. Educational objective: After receiving a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering it until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration specifying why the evidence has not been obtained and describing the desired discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986) (explaining when summary judgment is proper).","rule":"After receiving a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering it until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration specifying why the evidence has not been obtained and describing the desired discovery.","irac":{"issue":"A construction worker sued an insulation manufacturer in federal court, claiming that he had developed a chronic health condition as a result of 20 years of exposure to the manufacturer's insulation at his work sites. The manufacturer answered, denying all liability and stating that it had never supplied its insulation to the worker's employer. The worker's attorney deposed the manufacturer's president, and the manufacturer's attorney deposed the worker. Immediately thereafter, the manufacturer moved for summary judgment on the ground that the worker had no evidence showing that the insulation had ever been used by the worker's employer. What would be the worker's best response to the motion for summary judgment?","rule":"After receiving a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering it until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration specifying why the evidence has not been obtained and describing the desired discovery.","analysis":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for summary judgment requests that the court enter judgment without a full trial because (1) there is no genuine dispute of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The movant can meet this burden by asserting that the nonmovant lacks evidentiary support for any issue that the nonmovant has the burden to prove—eg, no evidence that the manufacturer's insulation had been used by the worker's employer. If this occurs, the nonmovant can avoid summary judgment by either:\n• presenting evidence to support that issue through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence or\n\n• requesting that the court postpone consideration of the motion until additional discovery can take place and including an affidavit or declaration that describes the desired discovery and why it is needed.\n\nSince the manufacturer moved for summary judgment after only two depositions, the worker likely needs additional discovery to obtain evidence showing that its employer had used the manufacturer's insulation. Therefore, the worker should argue that more time is\n\nneeded for additional discovery and attach a declaration or affidavit describing the desired discovery. (Choice B) Although the manufacturer's credibility will be an important issue at trial, a court never considers the credibility of evidence when making a summary judgment determination. Instead, it will assume that the manufacturer's no-evidence assertion is true unless the worker (nonmovant) presents evidence creating a genuine dispute of material fact. That evidence will then be viewed in the light most favorable to the worker. (Choices C & D) Since the worker has the burden to prove that the insulation was used by his employer and harmed him, the manufacturer is not required to provide evidence negating these assertions. It need only claim that the worker lacks evidence to support those elements of his claim. Educational objective: After receiving a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering it until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration specifying why the evidence has not been obtained and describing the desired discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986) (explaining when summary judgment is proper).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986) (explaining when summary judgment is proper).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[250],"sourceAnswerPages":[251,252],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名建筑工人在联邦法院起诉一家保温材料制造商，主张其在工作场所接触该制造商的保温材料达 20 年，因而患上慢性疾病。制造商提交答辩状，否认全部责任，并称从未向工人的雇主供应其保温材料。工人的律师对制造商总裁进行了证言录取，制造商律师也对工人进行了证言录取。紧接着，制造商以工人没有证据证明其雇主曾使用该保温材料为由申请简易判决。工人对简易判决动议的最佳回应是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"主张需要更多时间进行额外证据开示以证明制造商的责任，并附具声明说明所需的证据开示。","B":"主张应驳回动议，因为制造商关于其保温材料销售情况的可信度将是案件核心问题，而可信度只能由陪审团判断。","C":"主张应驳回动议，因为制造商没有在动议中附具证据证明工人的雇主未使用该保温材料。","D":"提出交叉简易判决动议，主张制造商没有提出证据证明其保温材料未伤害工人。"},"explanationZh":"简易判决动议（FRCP 56）：\n\n提出时间｜可在证据开示结束后 30 天前提出，或者按当地规则或法院规定的时间提出。\n\n证明责任标准｜动议方负有初步证明责任：\n• 可依靠诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料支持；\n• 初步责任满足后，责任转移至非动议方，后者必须选择：\n  – 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料，证明具体争议事实，不能仅依赖诉状；或\n  – 请求延期以进行额外证据开示，并以宣誓书或声明支持请求。\n\n批准标准｜不存在任何重大事实的真实争议，且动议方依法有权获得判决：\n• 必须以最有利于非动议方的方式看待证据；\n• 所有疑问均应作有利于非动议方的解决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n简易判决动议请求法院不经完整审判直接判决，因为：(1) 不存在重大事实的真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决。动议方可以主张非动议方对其负有证明责任的某一问题缺乏证据支持，从而满足该责任，例如主张没有证据表明工人雇主曾使用制造商的保温材料。\n\n发生这种情况时，非动议方可以通过以下任一方式避免简易判决：\n• 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采证据的材料，提出支持该问题的证据；或\n• 请求法院在进行额外证据开示前暂缓审理动议，并附具宣誓书或声明，说明所需的证据开示及其必要性。\n\n制造商仅在完成两次证言录取后便申请简易判决，工人很可能需要额外证据开示，以取得证明其雇主曾使用制造商保温材料的证据。因此，工人应主张需要更多时间进行额外证据开示，并附具声明或宣誓书说明所需证据开示。\n\n（选项 B）虽然制造商的可信度会是审判中的重要问题，但法院在判断简易判决时绝不衡量证据可信度。相反，除非工人（非动议方）提出证据形成重大事实的真实争议，否则法院会接受制造商关于不存在证据的主张。若工人提出证据，法院会以最有利于工人的方式看待。\n\n（选项 C、D）工人负有证明其雇主使用该保温材料并因此受害的责任，所以制造商无需提出证据否定这些主张，只须指出工人缺乏支持其请求要件的证据。\n\n学习目标：收到简易判决动议后，非动议方可以请求法院暂缓审理，直至完成额外证据开示。请求必须附具宣誓书或声明，说明尚未取得证据的原因及所需证据开示。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56（简易判决）。\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986)（解释何时适合简易判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-083","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":83,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A patient filed a medical malpractice action against a hospital in federal court, alleging that hospital staff had failed to diagnose the patient's cancer based on an X-ray that had been taken at the hospital. The patient's cancer was diagnosed six months later, based on the same X-ray, when he sought a second opinion. In the interim, the cancer had spread. Fact and expert discovery have been completed in the action. The hospital has moved for summary judgment. In support of its motion, the hospital has submitted a memorandum identifying facts that it claims are not in dispute. It has also cited and attached supporting exhibits, including a declaration signed under penalty of perjury from the hospital's radiologist, who found no signs of cancer on the X-ray. What is the best way for the patient to raise a genuine dispute of material fact?","choices":{"A":"Submit a report from the patient's expert radiologist contradicting the findings in the declaration of the hospital's radiologist.","B":"Submit an affidavit from the patient's attorney detailing his conversations with the patient's expert radiologist.","C":"Submit an affidavit from the patient's expert radiologist with findings that contradict the declaration of the hospital's radiologist.","D":"Submit the patient's medical records showing his current cancer diagnosis."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court should grant summary judgment in lieu of a full trial when (1) there is no genuine dispute of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The nonmovant can raise a genuine dispute of material fact through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts. Affidavits are written statements sworn in the presence of a notary public, and declarations are written statements signed under penalty of perjury. They can be used in determining a summary judgment if they:\n• are based on the affiant's or declarant's personal knowledge\n• contain admissible facts and\n• show that the affiant or declarant is competent to testify to the matters stated therein. Here, the hospital's radiologist stated in his declaration that he found no signs of cancer on the patient's X-ray. The patient can genuinely dispute this material fact by submitting an affidavit from his expert radiologist with findings that contradict the declaration. This affidavit can be used since the patient's radiologist has personal knowledge of the patient's X-ray, the findings are likely admissible, and the radiologist is competent to testify about this subject.\n\n(Choice A) Most courts will not consider expert reports in deciding summary judgment motions because they are not sworn or signed under penalty of perjury and therefore contain inadmissible facts. (Choice B) An affidavit from the patient's attorney detailing his conversations with the patient's radiologist is not valid since it (1) is not based on the attorney's personal knowledge of the patient's X-ray, (2) contains inadmissible hearsay, and (3) does not show that the attorney is competent to testify on medical issues. (Choice D) The patient's medical records showing his current cancer diagnosis do not dispute whether there were signs of cancer on the X-ray taken before his diagnosis. Educational objective: In a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can raise a genuine issue of material fact through affidavits and declarations that (1) are based on the affiant's or declarant's personal knowledge, (2) set out admissible facts, and (3) show the affiant's or declarant's competency to testify to the matters stated therein. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(c) (support for factual positions on summary judgment).","rule":"In a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can raise a genuine issue of material fact through affidavits and declarations that (1) are based on the affiant's or declarant's personal knowledge, (2) set out admissible facts, and (3) show the affiant's or declarant's competency to testify to the matters stated therein.","irac":{"issue":"A patient filed a medical malpractice action against a hospital in federal court, alleging that hospital staff had failed to diagnose the patient's cancer based on an X-ray that had been taken at the hospital. The patient's cancer was diagnosed six months later, based on the same X-ray, when he sought a second opinion. In the interim, the cancer had spread. Fact and expert discovery have been completed in the action. The hospital has moved for summary judgment. In support of its motion, the hospital has submitted a memorandum identifying facts that it claims are not in dispute. It has also cited and attached supporting exhibits, including a declaration signed under penalty of perjury from the hospital's radiologist, who found no signs of cancer on the X-ray. What is the best way for the patient to raise a genuine dispute of material fact?","rule":"In a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can raise a genuine issue of material fact through affidavits and declarations that (1) are based on the affiant's or declarant's personal knowledge, (2) set out admissible facts, and (3) show the affiant's or declarant's competency to testify to the matters stated therein.","analysis":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court should grant summary judgment in lieu of a full trial when (1) there is no genuine dispute of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The nonmovant can raise a genuine dispute of material fact through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts. Affidavits are written statements sworn in the presence of a notary public, and declarations are written statements signed under penalty of perjury. They can be used in determining a summary judgment if they:\n• are based on the affiant's or declarant's personal knowledge\n• contain admissible facts and\n• show that the affiant or declarant is competent to testify to the matters stated therein. Here, the hospital's radiologist stated in his declaration that he found no signs of cancer on the patient's X-ray. The patient can genuinely dispute this material fact by submitting an affidavit from his expert radiologist with findings that contradict the declaration. This affidavit can be used since the patient's radiologist has personal knowledge of the patient's X-ray, the findings are likely admissible, and the radiologist is competent to testify about this subject.\n\n(Choice A) Most courts will not consider expert reports in deciding summary judgment motions because they are not sworn or signed under penalty of perjury and therefore contain inadmissible facts. (Choice B) An affidavit from the patient's attorney detailing his conversations with the patient's radiologist is not valid since it (1) is not based on the attorney's personal knowledge of the patient's X-ray, (2) contains inadmissible hearsay, and (3) does not show that the attorney is competent to testify on medical issues. (Choice D) The patient's medical records showing his current cancer diagnosis do not dispute whether there were signs of cancer on the X-ray taken before his diagnosis. Educational objective: In a motion for summary judgment, the nonmovant can raise a genuine issue of material fact through affidavits and declarations that (1) are based on the affiant's or declarant's personal knowledge, (2) set out admissible facts, and (3) show the affiant's or declarant's competency to testify to the matters stated therein. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(c) (support for factual positions on summary judgment).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(c) (support for factual positions on summary judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[253],"sourceAnswerPages":[254,255],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名患者在联邦法院对一家医院提起医疗事故诉讼，主张医院工作人员未依据在医院拍摄的一张 X 光片诊断出患者癌症。六个月后，患者寻求第二意见，医生依据同一张 X 光片诊断出癌症；期间癌症已经扩散。本案事实及专家证据开示均已完成。医院申请简易判决。为支持动议，医院提交一份备忘录，列明其主张不存在争议的事实，并引用及附具支持性材料，其中包括医院放射科医生在伪证罪处罚下签署的声明，称其在 X 光片上未发现癌症迹象。患者提出重大事实真实争议的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提交患者专家放射科医生的报告，反驳医院放射科医生声明中的认定。","B":"提交患者律师的宣誓书，详述其与患者专家放射科医生的谈话。","C":"提交患者专家放射科医生的宣誓书，载明与医院放射科医生声明相矛盾的认定。","D":"提交患者显示其当前癌症诊断的医疗记录。"},"explanationZh":"简易判决动议（FRCP 56）：\n\n提出时间｜可在证据开示结束后 30 天前提出，或者按当地规则或法院规定的时间提出。\n\n证明责任标准｜动议方负有初步证明责任：\n• 可依靠诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料支持；\n• 初步责任满足后，责任转移至非动议方，后者必须选择：\n  – 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料，证明具体争议事实，不能仅依赖诉状；或\n  – 请求延期以进行额外证据开示，并以宣誓书或声明支持请求。\n\n批准标准｜不存在任何重大事实的真实争议，且动议方依法有权获得判决：\n• 必须以最有利于非动议方的方式看待证据；\n• 所有疑问均应作有利于非动议方的解决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n不存在重大事实的真实争议，且动议方依法有权获得判决时，法院应以简易判决代替完整审判。非动议方可以通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料，提出重大事实真实争议。\n\n宣誓书是在公证员面前宣誓作出的书面陈述；声明则是在伪证罪处罚下签署的书面陈述。它们在符合以下条件时可用于判断简易判决：\n• 基于宣誓人或声明人的亲身知识；\n• 包含可采事实；并且\n• 表明宣誓人或声明人有能力就其中所述事项作证。\n\n本题中，医院放射科医生在声明中称，其未在患者 X 光片上发现癌症迹象。患者可以提交其专家放射科医生的宣誓书，载明与该声明相矛盾的认定，从而对此重大事实形成真实争议。患者的放射科医生对患者 X 光片具有亲身知识，其认定很可能可以采纳，且放射科医生有能力就该事项作证，因此该宣誓书可供法院考虑。\n\n（选项 A）多数法院在决定简易判决动议时不会考虑专家报告，因为报告没有宣誓，也没有在伪证罪处罚下签署，因而包含不可采事实。\n\n（选项 B）患者律师详述其与放射科医生谈话的宣誓书无效，因为：(1) 律师对患者 X 光片没有亲身知识；(2) 内容包含不可采传闻；且 (3) 未显示律师有能力就医疗问题作证。\n\n（选项 D）患者显示当前癌症诊断的医疗记录，并不能争议在患者获诊断前拍摄的那张 X 光片上是否已有癌症迹象。\n\n学习目标：在简易判决动议中，非动议方可通过符合以下条件的宣誓书或声明提出重大事实真实争议：(1) 基于宣誓人或声明人的亲身知识；(2) 陈述可采事实；且 (3) 表明宣誓人或声明人有能力就其中事项作证。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(c)（简易判决中事实立场的支持材料）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-084","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":84,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A consumer brought a federal diversity action against a manufacturer, alleging strict products liability. After the parties presented their evidence at trial, the judge instructed the jury to return a special verdict providing written answers to specific questions about each factual issue but refraining from finding in favor of a party. The jury returned its special verdict, specifically finding that the manufacturer's product was defectively designed and caused the consumer foreseeable harm. Before the judge entered a judgment consistent with the jury's findings, the manufacturer moved for judgment as a matter of law, arguing that the evidence was insufficient to support the jury's determinations. Should the judge consider the manufacturer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the case has already been submitted to the jury.","B":"No, because the jury is the conclusive determiner of the sufficiency of the evidence.","C":"Yes, because the consumer has had the opportunity to present evidence to the jury.","D":"Yes, because the court has not yet entered a final judgment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court issue a judgment in the movant's favor because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in favor of the nonmovant. A party may move for JMOL after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present evidence to establish a claim or defense. However, this motion must be made before the case is submitted to the jury or the motion is waived. Here, the manufacturer moved for JMOL after the jury returned its special verdict. Although the consumer has had the opportunity to present evidence to the jury, the manufacturer failed to timely move for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (Choice C). Therefore, the judge should not consider the manufacturer's motion for JMOL. (Choice B) The jury is not the conclusive determiner of the sufficiency of the evidence because a judge may assess whether the evidence was legally sufficient to support the verdict in ruling on a properly filed motion for JMOL. (Choice D) A party must move for JMOL before the case is submitted to the jury—not before the court enters a final judgment. However, a party may file a renewed motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment so long as the party properly moved for JMOL (not seen here). Educational objective: A party may move for judgment as a matter of law after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present its case to the jury. However, this motion must be asserted before the case is submitted to the jury or the motion is waived. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"A party may move for judgment as a matter of law after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present its case to the jury. However, this motion must be asserted before the case is submitted to the jury or the motion is waived.","irac":{"issue":"A consumer brought a federal diversity action against a manufacturer, alleging strict products liability. After the parties presented their evidence at trial, the judge instructed the jury to return a special verdict providing written answers to specific questions about each factual issue but refraining from finding in favor of a party. The jury returned its special verdict, specifically finding that the manufacturer's product was defectively designed and caused the consumer foreseeable harm. Before the judge entered a judgment consistent with the jury's findings, the manufacturer moved for judgment as a matter of law, arguing that the evidence was insufficient to support the jury's determinations. Should the judge consider the manufacturer's motion?","rule":"A party may move for judgment as a matter of law after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present its case to the jury. However, this motion must be asserted before the case is submitted to the jury or the motion is waived.","analysis":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court issue a judgment in the movant's favor because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in favor of the nonmovant. A party may move for JMOL after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present evidence to establish a claim or defense. However, this motion must be made before the case is submitted to the jury or the motion is waived. Here, the manufacturer moved for JMOL after the jury returned its special verdict. Although the consumer has had the opportunity to present evidence to the jury, the manufacturer failed to timely move for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (Choice C). Therefore, the judge should not consider the manufacturer's motion for JMOL. (Choice B) The jury is not the conclusive determiner of the sufficiency of the evidence because a judge may assess whether the evidence was legally sufficient to support the verdict in ruling on a properly filed motion for JMOL. (Choice D) A party must move for JMOL before the case is submitted to the jury—not before the court enters a final judgment. However, a party may file a renewed motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment so long as the party properly moved for JMOL (not seen here). Educational objective: A party may move for judgment as a matter of law after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present its case to the jury. However, this motion must be asserted before the case is submitted to the jury or the motion is waived. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[256],"sourceAnswerPages":[257],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名消费者在联邦法院对一家制造商提起多元州籍诉讼，主张严格产品责任。双方在审判中提出证据后，法官指示陪审团作出特别裁决：就各项事实问题以书面形式回答具体问题，但不得直接判决任何一方胜诉。陪审团作出特别裁决，明确认定制造商产品设计有缺陷，并对消费者造成可预见伤害。在法官依据陪审团认定登记判决前，制造商申请依法判决，主张证据不足以支持陪审团认定。法官是否应审理制造商的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为案件已经交付陪审团。","B":"不应，因为陪审团是证据充分性的最终决定者。","C":"应当，因为消费者已经有机会向陪审团提出证据。","D":"应当，因为法院尚未登记终局判决。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决（JMOL）动议请求法院为动议方作出判决，理由是证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。非动议方已有机会提出证据证明请求或抗辩后，当事人可以申请 JMOL。但是，该动议必须在案件交付陪审团前提出，否则即被放弃。\n\n本题中，制造商在陪审团作出特别裁决后才申请 JMOL。消费者虽已有机会向陪审团提出证据，但制造商未在案件交付陪审团前及时申请 JMOL（选项 C）。因此，法官不应审理制造商的 JMOL 动议。\n\n（选项 B）陪审团并非证据充分性的最终决定者，因为在裁定适当提出的 JMOL 动议时，法官可以判断证据在法律上是否足以支持裁决。\n\n（选项 D）当事人必须在案件交付陪审团前，而不是在法院登记终局判决前，申请 JMOL。但是，只要当事人此前适当申请过 JMOL（本题没有），即可在登记终局判决后 28 天内提出续行 JMOL 动议。\n\n学习目标：非动议方已有机会向陪审团陈述案件后，当事人可以申请依法判决；但必须在案件交付陪审团前提出，否则即被放弃。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50（依法判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-031"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-085","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":85,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant for fraud in federal court. The complaint contained a short and plain statement of facts giving rise to the claim. One week after being served with process, the defendant filed and served an answer that denied the plaintiff's allegations. Five days later, the defendant filed and served a motion for a more definite statement. The motion alleged that the complaint failed to state the circumstances giving rise to the claim with particularity and specified the details that needed to be included. Should the court grant the defendant's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the complaint contained a short and plain statement of the facts giving rise to the claim.","B":"No, because the motion was filed after the defendant's responsive pleading was filed.","C":"Yes, because the circumstances giving rise to the claim must be stated with particularity.","D":"Yes, because the motion was filed within 21 days after the defendant was served with process."},"answer":"B","explanation":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(e) allows a party to move for a more definite statement of a pleading that is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. This motion is appropriate when a complaint does not contain the requisite specificity—eg, fails to state the circumstances giving rise to a fraud claim with particularity. A motion for a more definite statement must:\n• specify the pleading's defects and the details needed to cure these defects and\n\n• be made before filing a responsive pleading (eg, answer).*\n\nIf the court grants the motion, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court. Failure to do so allows the court to strike the pleading or issue another appropriate order. Here, the defendant's motion for a more definite statement alleged that the plaintiff's complaint failed to state the circumstances giving rise to the fraud claim with particularity and specified the details sought to cure this defect. However, the motion was not timely since it was filed after the answer. Therefore, the court should deny the defendant's motion. *Since the purpose of a motion for a more definite statement is to help a party adequately answer a vague pleading, the motion must be made before the answer is filed. (Choices A & C) A complaint generally need only contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. However, the circumstances giving rise to a fraud claim must be stated with particularity.\n\n(Choice D) A defendant generally must file an answer within 21 days after being served with process. Here, the defendant timely filed and served an answer one week after being served with process. But the motion for a more definite statement was untimely since it was made after the defendant filed an answer. Educational objective: A party may move for a more definite statement of a pleading that is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. This motion must (1) specify the pleading's defects and the details sought that will cure these defects and (2) be made before filing a responsive pleading. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 416 (2019) (explaining that a motion for a more definite statement must be made before an answer is filed).","rule":"A party may move for a more definite statement of a pleading that is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. This motion must (1) specify the pleading's defects and the details sought that will cure these defects and (2) be made before filing a responsive pleading.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant for fraud in federal court. The complaint contained a short and plain statement of facts giving rise to the claim. One week after being served with process, the defendant filed and served an answer that denied the plaintiff's allegations. Five days later, the defendant filed and served a motion for a more definite statement. The motion alleged that the complaint failed to state the circumstances giving rise to the claim with particularity and specified the details that needed to be included. Should the court grant the defendant's motion?","rule":"A party may move for a more definite statement of a pleading that is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. This motion must (1) specify the pleading's defects and the details sought that will cure these defects and (2) be made before filing a responsive pleading.","analysis":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(e) allows a party to move for a more definite statement of a pleading that is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. This motion is appropriate when a complaint does not contain the requisite specificity—eg, fails to state the circumstances giving rise to a fraud claim with particularity. A motion for a more definite statement must:\n• specify the pleading's defects and the details needed to cure these defects and\n\n• be made before filing a responsive pleading (eg, answer).*\n\nIf the court grants the motion, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement within 14 days after receiving notice of the order or a time otherwise set by the court. Failure to do so allows the court to strike the pleading or issue another appropriate order. Here, the defendant's motion for a more definite statement alleged that the plaintiff's complaint failed to state the circumstances giving rise to the fraud claim with particularity and specified the details sought to cure this defect. However, the motion was not timely since it was filed after the answer. Therefore, the court should deny the defendant's motion. *Since the purpose of a motion for a more definite statement is to help a party adequately answer a vague pleading, the motion must be made before the answer is filed. (Choices A & C) A complaint generally need only contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. However, the circumstances giving rise to a fraud claim must be stated with particularity.\n\n(Choice D) A defendant generally must file an answer within 21 days after being served with process. Here, the defendant timely filed and served an answer one week after being served with process. But the motion for a more definite statement was untimely since it was made after the defendant filed an answer. Educational objective: A party may move for a more definite statement of a pleading that is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. This motion must (1) specify the pleading's defects and the details sought that will cure these defects and (2) be made before filing a responsive pleading. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 416 (2019) (explaining that a motion for a more definite statement must be made before an answer is filed).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 416 (2019) (explaining that a motion for a more definite statement must be made before an answer is filed).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[259],"sourceAnswerPages":[260,261],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦法院起诉被告欺诈。起诉状包含一份简短、明确的事实陈述，说明引发请求的事实。被告获诉讼文书送达一周后，提交并送达答辩状，否认原告指控。五天后，被告提交并送达更明确陈述动议。动议主张，起诉状未具体说明引发请求的情形，并指明必须补充的细节。法院是否应批准被告的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为起诉状包含了引发请求之事实的简短、明确陈述。","B":"不应，因为动议在被告提交回应性诉状后才提出。","C":"应当，因为引发该请求的情形必须具体陈述。","D":"应当，因为动议在被告获诉讼文书送达后 21 天内提出。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 12 动议：\n\n12(b)｜主张以下任一抗辩的动议：\n• 缺乏事项管辖权或对人管辖权；\n• 审判地不当；\n• 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分；\n• 未陈述可给予救济的请求；\n• 未合并必要当事人。\n\n12(c)｜诉状判决动议。\n12(e)｜诉状更明确陈述动议。\n12(f)｜删除诉状中不充分抗辩或不相关事项的动议。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》12(e) 允许当事人针对含糊或模棱两可到无法合理起草回应性诉状的诉状，申请更明确陈述。一份起诉状不具有所需具体性时，该动议适当，例如未具体说明引发欺诈请求的情形。更明确陈述动议必须：\n• 指明诉状缺陷及补正缺陷所需的细节；并且\n• 在提交回应性诉状（例如答辩状）前提出。*\n\n法院批准动议时，非动议方必须在收到命令通知后 14 天内，或法院另定期限内，提供更明确陈述。未这样做时，法院可以删除该诉状或作出其他适当命令。\n\n本题中，被告的更明确陈述动议主张，原告起诉状未具体说明引发欺诈请求的情形，并指明补正该缺陷所需的细节。但是，该动议在答辩状之后才提出，因而逾期。因此，法院应驳回被告的动议。\n\n*更明确陈述动议旨在帮助当事人充分回应含糊诉状，所以必须在提交答辩状前提出。\n\n（选项 A、C）起诉状通常只须包含一份简短、明确的请求陈述，表明原告有权获得救济。但是，引发欺诈请求的情形必须具体陈述。\n\n（选项 D）被告通常必须在获诉讼文书送达后 21 天内提交答辩状。本题被告在送达一周后及时提交并送达答辩状，但更明确陈述动议因在答辩状后提出而逾期。\n\n学习目标：当事人可针对含糊或模棱两可到无法合理起草回应性诉状的诉状申请更明确陈述。该动议必须：(1) 指明诉状缺陷及可补正缺陷的所需细节；并且 (2) 在提交回应性诉状前提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e)（更明确陈述动议）。\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 416 (2019)（说明更明确陈述动议必须在提交答辩状前提出）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-086","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":86,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A jury sitting in a federal court case returned a verdict that found in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her $100,000. The federal district court entered a final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict. The defendant immediately filed a notice of appeal, alleging that the district court erred by failing to exclude several pieces of evidence from the jury's consideration. The appellate court docketed the case. The day after the appeal was docketed, the district court discovered that the final judgment listed a $1,000,000 award for the plaintiff. May the district court correct the mistake?","choices":{"A":"No, because both parties must receive notice and an opportunity to be heard on this action.","B":"No, because leave of the appellate court is required once an appeal is docketed.","C":"Yes, because a district court may correct clerical mistakes on its own initiative.","D":"Yes, because a district court may freely alter and amend final judgments in the interest of justice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Posttrial correction of mistakes\n\n(FRCP 60(a))\n\nType of mistake Definition Time of correction\n\nClerical Misstatement of court's intent • Before appeal docketed: on court's own initiative\n• Eg, court entered or by motion judgment for \"$10,000\"\n• After appeal docketed: instead of \"$100,000\" with appellate court's Oversight/omission Exclusion misrepresents court's leave intent\n\n• Eg, judgment failed to include required interest in award FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA district court may correct a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission in a judgment, order, or other part of the record. The court may do so on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed. But after an appeal is docketed, the district court can correct the mistake only with the appellate court's leave (ie, permission). Here, a clerical mistake occurred since the jury's verdict awarded the plaintiff $100,000 but the final judgment awarded her $1,000,000. Since the appeal has been docketed, the district court cannot correct the mistake without leave of the appellate court. (Choice A) A district court need not provide the parties with notice or an opportunity to be heard before it corrects a mistake. (Choice C) The district court could have corrected the mistake on its own initiative had the appeal not been docketed. (Choice D) A district court cannot freely alter or amend final judgments. It may do so only when (1) the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact, (2) the controlling law has changed, or (3) new evidence is discovered. Educational objective: Before an appeal is docketed in the appellate court, a district court can correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's\n\nmotion. But after an appeal has been docketed, the district court can correct the mistake only with the appellate court's leave. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a) (correction of mistake in judgment or order).","rule":"Before an appeal is docketed in the appellate court, a district court can correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's\n\nmotion. But after an appeal has been docketed, the district court can correct the mistake only with the appellate court's leave.","irac":{"issue":"A jury sitting in a federal court case returned a verdict that found in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her $100,000. The federal district court entered a final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict. The defendant immediately filed a notice of appeal, alleging that the district court erred by failing to exclude several pieces of evidence from the jury's consideration. The appellate court docketed the case. The day after the appeal was docketed, the district court discovered that the final judgment listed a $1,000,000 award for the plaintiff. May the district court correct the mistake?","rule":"Before an appeal is docketed in the appellate court, a district court can correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's\n\nmotion. But after an appeal has been docketed, the district court can correct the mistake only with the appellate court's leave.","analysis":"Posttrial correction of mistakes\n\n(FRCP 60(a))\n\nType of mistake Definition Time of correction\n\nClerical Misstatement of court's intent • Before appeal docketed: on court's own initiative\n• Eg, court entered or by motion judgment for \"$10,000\"\n• After appeal docketed: instead of \"$100,000\" with appellate court's Oversight/omission Exclusion misrepresents court's leave intent\n\n• Eg, judgment failed to include required interest in award FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA district court may correct a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission in a judgment, order, or other part of the record. The court may do so on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed. But after an appeal is docketed, the district court can correct the mistake only with the appellate court's leave (ie, permission). Here, a clerical mistake occurred since the jury's verdict awarded the plaintiff $100,000 but the final judgment awarded her $1,000,000. Since the appeal has been docketed, the district court cannot correct the mistake without leave of the appellate court. (Choice A) A district court need not provide the parties with notice or an opportunity to be heard before it corrects a mistake. (Choice C) The district court could have corrected the mistake on its own initiative had the appeal not been docketed. (Choice D) A district court cannot freely alter or amend final judgments. It may do so only when (1) the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact, (2) the controlling law has changed, or (3) new evidence is discovered. Educational objective: Before an appeal is docketed in the appellate court, a district court can correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's\n\nmotion. But after an appeal has been docketed, the district court can correct the mistake only with the appellate court's leave. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a) (correction of mistake in judgment or order).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a) (correction of mistake in judgment or order).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[262],"sourceAnswerPages":[263,264],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"联邦法院案件中的陪审团作出有利于原告的裁决，判给其 100,000 美元。联邦地区法院依陪审团裁决登记终局判决。被告立即提交上诉通知，主张地区法院未将数项证据排除在陪审团考虑范围之外，构成错误。上诉法院将案件列入审理日程。上诉列入日程次日，地区法院发现终局判决列明判给原告 1,000,000 美元。地区法院可以更正该错误吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为必须给予双方通知及就该行动陈述意见的机会。","B":"不可以，因为上诉列入审理日程后必须取得上诉法院许可。","C":"可以，因为地区法院可以依职权更正书记性错误。","D":"可以，因为为实现司法公正，地区法院可以自由修改终局判决。"},"explanationZh":"审判后更正错误（FRCP 60(a)）：\n\n错误类型｜定义｜更正时间\n书记性错误｜错误陈述法院意图，例如法院本欲登记“100,000 美元”判决，却登记为“10,000 美元”｜上诉列入日程前：法院可依职权或根据动议更正；上诉列入日程后：须经上诉法院许可\n疏忽／遗漏｜遗漏导致法院意图被错误表达，例如判决未将依法必须判给的利息计入金额｜上诉列入日程前：法院可依职权或根据动议更正；上诉列入日程后：须经上诉法院许可\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n地区法院可以更正判决、命令或案卷其他部分中的书记性错误，或因疏忽、遗漏产生的错误。在就判决或命令提起的上诉列入审理日程前，法院可依职权或根据当事人动议更正。但是，上诉列入审理日程后，地区法院只能经上诉法院许可方可更正。\n\n本题发生书记性错误，因为陪审团裁决判给原告 100,000 美元，终局判决却判给 1,000,000 美元。由于上诉已经列入审理日程，地区法院未经上诉法院许可不能更正。\n\n（选项 A）地区法院更正错误前，无需向当事人发出通知或给予陈述意见机会。\n\n（选项 C）如果上诉尚未列入审理日程，地区法院本可依职权更正错误。\n\n（选项 D）地区法院不能自由修改终局判决。只有在以下情况下方可修改：(1) 判决基于明显法律或事实错误；(2) 控制性法律发生变化；或 (3) 发现新证据。\n\n学习目标：上诉在上诉法院列入审理日程前，地区法院可以依职权或根据当事人动议，更正判决、命令或案卷其他部分中的错误；上诉列入日程后，地区法院只能经上诉法院许可更正。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a)（更正判决或命令中的错误）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-087","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":87,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A stockbroker was convicted in federal court of criminal fraud in the sale of certain securities. A former client then sued the stockbroker in a federal civil action, alleging fraud in the sale of the same securities for which the stockbroker had been criminally convicted. The client's attorney moved to preclude the stockbroker from relitigating the liability issues that were common to the criminal and civil actions. The court granted the motion and, after a bench trial on damages, entered judgment against the stockbroker for $100,000. On appeal from the stockbroker's criminal conviction, the appellate court reversed and vacated the conviction. The stockbroker promptly moved for relief from the final judgment in the civil action. The motion was filed 15 months after entry of the final judgment in the civil action. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because it is untimely.","B":"No, because the stockbroker did not appeal the civil judgment.","C":"Yes, because the court in the civil action should not have based the civil judgment on the criminal judgment before it had been reviewed on appeal.","D":"Yes, because the criminal judgment on which the civil judgment was based has been reversed and vacated."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b) allows a party to move for extraordinary relief from a court's final judgment on limited grounds. The motion must be made within one year from the court's judgment if the motion is based on any of the grounds listed in FRCP 60(b)(1)–(3) (see image above). All other FRCP 60(b) motions can be made within a reasonable time under the circumstances. This includes a motion under FRCP 60(b)(5), which authorizes relief from a judgment that:\n• has been satisfied, released, or discharged\n• is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated or\n• will violate equity if applied prospectively. Here, the federal court granted the client's motion in the civil fraud action to prevent the stockbroker from relitigating the liability issues that had been decided in the stockbroker's criminal fraud action. An appellate court then reversed and vacated the criminal fraud conviction, and the stockbroker promptly moved for relief in the federal civil action pursuant to FRCP 60(b)(5). As a result, the one-year deadline does not apply, and the court should grant the stockbroker's timely motion for relief (Choice A). (Choice B) A party need not appeal before moving to set aside a judgment under FRCP 60(b). Had the stockbroker appealed the civil judgment, the appeal likely would have been unsuccessful since the court properly applied issue preclusion to prevent the relitigation of identical issues decided in the criminal action.\n\n(Choice C) A final judgment entered in a civil or criminal action is entitled to immediate preclusive effect. This means that the court in the civil action validly based the civil judgment on the criminal judgment before it had been reviewed on appeal. Educational objective: Under FRCP 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (relief from judgment).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2863 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining that a party may move for relief from a judgment that is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated).","rule":"Under FRCP 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively.","irac":{"issue":"A stockbroker was convicted in federal court of criminal fraud in the sale of certain securities. A former client then sued the stockbroker in a federal civil action, alleging fraud in the sale of the same securities for which the stockbroker had been criminally convicted. The client's attorney moved to preclude the stockbroker from relitigating the liability issues that were common to the criminal and civil actions. The court granted the motion and, after a bench trial on damages, entered judgment against the stockbroker for $100,000. On appeal from the stockbroker's criminal conviction, the appellate court reversed and vacated the conviction. The stockbroker promptly moved for relief from the final judgment in the civil action. The motion was filed 15 months after entry of the final judgment in the civil action. Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"Under FRCP 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively.","analysis":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b) allows a party to move for extraordinary relief from a court's final judgment on limited grounds. The motion must be made within one year from the court's judgment if the motion is based on any of the grounds listed in FRCP 60(b)(1)–(3) (see image above). All other FRCP 60(b) motions can be made within a reasonable time under the circumstances. This includes a motion under FRCP 60(b)(5), which authorizes relief from a judgment that:\n• has been satisfied, released, or discharged\n• is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated or\n• will violate equity if applied prospectively. Here, the federal court granted the client's motion in the civil fraud action to prevent the stockbroker from relitigating the liability issues that had been decided in the stockbroker's criminal fraud action. An appellate court then reversed and vacated the criminal fraud conviction, and the stockbroker promptly moved for relief in the federal civil action pursuant to FRCP 60(b)(5). As a result, the one-year deadline does not apply, and the court should grant the stockbroker's timely motion for relief (Choice A). (Choice B) A party need not appeal before moving to set aside a judgment under FRCP 60(b). Had the stockbroker appealed the civil judgment, the appeal likely would have been unsuccessful since the court properly applied issue preclusion to prevent the relitigation of identical issues decided in the criminal action.\n\n(Choice C) A final judgment entered in a civil or criminal action is entitled to immediate preclusive effect. This means that the court in the civil action validly based the civil judgment on the criminal judgment before it had been reviewed on appeal. Educational objective: Under FRCP 60(b)(5), a party may file a motion within a reasonable time to obtain relief from a judgment that (1) has been satisfied, released, or discharged, (2) is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated, or (3) will violate equity if applied prospectively. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (relief from judgment).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2863 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining that a party may move for relief from a judgment that is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (relief from judgment).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2863 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining that a party may move for relief from a judgment that is based on an earlier judgment that has been reversed or vacated).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[265],"sourceAnswerPages":[266,267],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名股票经纪人在联邦法院因销售某些证券构成刑事欺诈而被定罪。一名前客户随后在联邦法院对股票经纪人提起民事诉讼，主张经纪人在销售其因而获刑事定罪的同一证券时构成欺诈。客户律师提出动议，请求禁止股票经纪人重新争讼刑事与民事诉讼共同的责任问题。法院批准该动议，并在法官就损害赔偿审判后，判决股票经纪人承担 100,000 美元。股票经纪人对刑事定罪提起上诉后，上诉法院撤销并废止该定罪。股票经纪人立即申请从民事诉讼的终局判决获得救济。该动议在民事终局判决登记 15 个月后提出。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为动议逾期。","B":"不应，因为股票经纪人没有对民事判决提起上诉。","C":"应当，因为民事诉讼法院不应在刑事判决经上诉审查前以其作为民事判决基础。","D":"应当，因为民事判决所依据的刑事判决已被撤销并废止。"},"explanationZh":"从终局判决获得救济（FRCP 60(b)）：\n\n救济理由｜提出时限\n错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽｜一年内\n新发现证据｜一年内\n欺诈、虚假陈述、不当行为｜一年内\n无效判决，例如缺乏管辖权｜合理时间内\n判决已履行、解除或撤销；判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或者继续适用将违反衡平原则｜合理时间内\n其他足以给予救济的理由（极少适用）｜合理时间内\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）60(b) 允许当事人在有限理由下申请从法院终局判决获得特别救济。若动议基于 FRCP 60(b)(1)–(3) 所列理由（见上图），必须在法院判决后一年内提出。其他所有 FRCP 60(b) 动议均可在具体情形下的合理时间内提出。\n\n其中包括 FRCP 60(b)(5) 动议；该条授权从符合下列任一情形的判决获得救济：\n• 判决已履行、解除或撤销；\n• 判决基于先前已被撤销或废止的判决；或\n• 继续适用判决将违反衡平原则。\n\n本题中，联邦法院在民事欺诈诉讼中批准客户动议，禁止股票经纪人重新争讼刑事欺诈诉讼已经裁定的责任问题。上诉法院随后撤销并废止刑事欺诈定罪，股票经纪人立即依据 FRCP 60(b)(5) 申请从联邦民事判决获得救济。因此，一年期限不适用，法院应批准股票经纪人及时提出的救济动议（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）当事人在依据 FRCP 60(b) 申请撤销判决前无需先提起上诉。即使股票经纪人曾对民事判决上诉，上诉也很可能失败，因为法院此前适用争点排除，禁止重新争讼刑事诉讼中已经裁定的相同问题，是适当的。\n\n（选项 C）民事或刑事诉讼中登记的终局判决立即产生排除效力。这意味着，即使刑事判决当时尚未经过上诉审查，民事诉讼法院以刑事判决作为民事判决基础仍然有效。\n\n学习目标：依据 FRCP 60(b)(5)，当事人可以在合理时间内申请从符合以下任一情形的判决获得救济：(1) 判决已履行、解除或撤销；(2) 判决基于后来被撤销或废止的先前判决；或 (3) 继续适用判决将违反衡平原则。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（判决救济）。\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2863 (3d ed. 2020)（说明当事人可从基于后来被撤销或废止之先前判决的判决获得救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-088","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":88,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A boutique sued a dressmaker in federal court for breach of contract. The boutique's complaint alleged that the dressmaker violated the contract between the parties by delivering the wrong dresses. The dressmaker immediately filed an answer, in which he admitted to delivering the wrong dresses. The answer also contained an affirmative defense that the boutique failed to file its complaint within the applicable statute of limitations. The dressmaker wants the court to decide the case based solely on the information contained in the complaint and answer. Which motion will best accomplish the dressmaker's goal?","choices":{"A":"A motion for default judgment.","B":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law.","C":"A motion for judgment on the pleadings.","D":"A motion for summary judgment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Pretrial motions\n\nMotion Grounds\n\nDefault • Defendant failed to timely serve answer judgment • Any party failed to comply with court order Pre-answer • No subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service or process\n• Failure to join required party\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted Judgment on the • Pleadings, attached exhibits & matters of public record show pleadings (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law Voluntary • Plaintiff requests dismissal dismissal\n\nInvoluntary • Defendant requests dismissal because plaintiff failed to (1) dismissal prosecute action or (2) comply with rule/court order\n\nSummary • Pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery & other materials judgment show (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law A motion for judgment on the pleadings can be used to dismiss a suit once the pleadings have closed—ie, when all required pleadings have been served. Since the court's consideration of this motion is limited to the contents of the pleadings (including attached exhibits) and matters of public record, this motion will best accomplish the dressmaker's goal. The motion will likely be granted if the pleadings show that the statute of limitations has expired because there would be no issues of material fact and the dressmaker would be entitled to judgment as a matter of law. (Choice A) A default judgment is appropriate (1) when a defendant fails to timely serve an answer or (2) as a sanction when a party fails to comply with a court order—neither of which applies here. (Choice B) A party can move for judgment as a matter of law during trial once the nonmovant has presented its case.\n\n(Choice D) The standards to grant a motion for summary judgment are identical to those required for a judgment on the pleadings, but a court can consider materials outside the pleadings (eg, discovery materials). Since the dressmaker seeks a resolution based solely on the pleadings, a judgment on the pleadings is more appropriate. Educational objective: A party may move for judgment on the pleadings once the pleadings are closed. In considering such a motion, the court is limited to the contents of the pleadings (including exhibits) and matters of public record. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c) (motion for judgment on the pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2019) (explaining the requirements for judgment on the pleadings).","rule":"A party may move for judgment on the pleadings once the pleadings are closed. In considering such a motion, the court is limited to the contents of the pleadings (including exhibits) and matters of public record.","irac":{"issue":"A boutique sued a dressmaker in federal court for breach of contract. The boutique's complaint alleged that the dressmaker violated the contract between the parties by delivering the wrong dresses. The dressmaker immediately filed an answer, in which he admitted to delivering the wrong dresses. The answer also contained an affirmative defense that the boutique failed to file its complaint within the applicable statute of limitations. The dressmaker wants the court to decide the case based solely on the information contained in the complaint and answer. Which motion will best accomplish the dressmaker's goal?","rule":"A party may move for judgment on the pleadings once the pleadings are closed. In considering such a motion, the court is limited to the contents of the pleadings (including exhibits) and matters of public record.","analysis":"Pretrial motions\n\nMotion Grounds\n\nDefault • Defendant failed to timely serve answer judgment • Any party failed to comply with court order Pre-answer • No subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service or process\n• Failure to join required party\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted Judgment on the • Pleadings, attached exhibits & matters of public record show pleadings (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law Voluntary • Plaintiff requests dismissal dismissal\n\nInvoluntary • Defendant requests dismissal because plaintiff failed to (1) dismissal prosecute action or (2) comply with rule/court order\n\nSummary • Pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery & other materials judgment show (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law A motion for judgment on the pleadings can be used to dismiss a suit once the pleadings have closed—ie, when all required pleadings have been served. Since the court's consideration of this motion is limited to the contents of the pleadings (including attached exhibits) and matters of public record, this motion will best accomplish the dressmaker's goal. The motion will likely be granted if the pleadings show that the statute of limitations has expired because there would be no issues of material fact and the dressmaker would be entitled to judgment as a matter of law. (Choice A) A default judgment is appropriate (1) when a defendant fails to timely serve an answer or (2) as a sanction when a party fails to comply with a court order—neither of which applies here. (Choice B) A party can move for judgment as a matter of law during trial once the nonmovant has presented its case.\n\n(Choice D) The standards to grant a motion for summary judgment are identical to those required for a judgment on the pleadings, but a court can consider materials outside the pleadings (eg, discovery materials). Since the dressmaker seeks a resolution based solely on the pleadings, a judgment on the pleadings is more appropriate. Educational objective: A party may move for judgment on the pleadings once the pleadings are closed. In considering such a motion, the court is limited to the contents of the pleadings (including exhibits) and matters of public record. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c) (motion for judgment on the pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2019) (explaining the requirements for judgment on the pleadings).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c) (motion for judgment on the pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2019) (explaining the requirements for judgment on the pleadings).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[268],"sourceAnswerPages":[269,270],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家精品店在联邦法院起诉一名服装制作者违约。精品店起诉状主张，服装制作者交付错误的连衣裙，违反双方合同。服装制作者立即提交答辩状，承认交付了错误连衣裙。答辩状还提出诉讼时效抗辩，称精品店未在适用时效期间内提交起诉状。服装制作者希望法院仅依据起诉状和答辩状所载信息裁决案件。哪项动议最能实现其目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"缺席判决动议。","B":"依法判决动议。","C":"诉状判决动议。","D":"简易判决动议。"},"explanationZh":"审前动议：\n\n动议｜理由\n缺席判决｜被告未及时送达答辩状；任何当事人未遵守法院命令\n答辩前动议｜缺乏事项管辖权或对人管辖权；审判地不当；诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分；未合并必要当事人；未陈述可给予救济的请求\n诉状判决｜诉状、所附材料及公共记录事项表明：(1) 不存在重大事实的真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决\n自愿驳回｜原告请求驳回\n非自愿驳回｜因原告未推进诉讼或未遵守规则／法院命令，被告请求驳回\n简易判决｜诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料及其他材料表明：(1) 不存在重大事实的真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决\n\n诉状提交完毕——即所有必要诉状均已送达——后，可使用诉状判决动议请求驳回诉讼。法院审理该动议时仅限考虑诉状内容（包括所附材料）及公共记录事项，所以该动议最能实现服装制作者的目标。\n\n如果诉状显示诉讼时效已届满，动议很可能获得批准，因为此时不存在重大事实问题，服装制作者依法有权获得判决。\n\n（选项 A）被告未及时送达答辩状，或者当事人未遵守法院命令而将缺席判决作为制裁时，适合缺席判决。本题均不适用。\n\n（选项 B）非动议方在审判中陈述案件后，当事人可以申请依法判决。\n\n（选项 D）批准简易判决动议的标准与诉状判决相同，但法院可以考虑诉状以外的材料，例如证据开示资料。服装制作者仅寻求依诉状解决案件，所以诉状判决更适当。\n\n学习目标：诉状提交完毕后，当事人可以申请诉状判决。法院审理该动议时，仅限考虑诉状内容（包括所附材料）及公共记录事项。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c)（诉状判决动议）。\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2019)（解释诉状判决的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-089","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":89,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A customer sued a retailer in federal court, claiming that the retailer had intentionally engaged in trade practices that violated federal law. At the close of evidence, the retailer filed a motion for judgment as a matter of law, which was denied. The jury returned a verdict for the customer. Twenty-five days after the court entered judgment on the verdict, the retailer filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law. The trial court determined that the evidence was legally insufficient to find in the customer's favor because the customer's main witness was not credible. The trial court then issued an order granting the retailer's motion. Twenty-eight days later, the customer appealed. Is the appellate court likely to uphold the trial court's ruling?","choices":{"A":"No, because the retailer renewed its motion for judgment as a matter of law more than 21 days after the trial court entered judgment on the verdict.","B":"No, because the trial court considered the credibility of the customer's main witness when granting the retailer's renewed motion.","C":"Yes, because the appellate court must give great deference to the trial court's ruling.","D":"Yes, because the customer appealed more than 30 days after the trial court entered judgment on the verdict."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court enter a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. An initial motion for JMOL must be filed before the case is submitted to the jury. If that motion is denied, the movant can file a renewed motion within 28 days after the entry of final judgment on the jury's verdict.* When considering an initial or renewed motion for JMOL, the trial court must:\n• view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant\n\n• disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe and\n\n• not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.\n\nHere, the retailer timely renewed its motion for JMOL 25 days after the trial court entered judgment on the jury's verdict (Choice A). The trial court granted this motion after determining that the evidence was legally insufficient to find in the customer's\n\nfavor. However, the appellate court will likely overturn that ruling because the trial court improperly considered the credibility of the customer's main witness. *A trial court can grant the renewed motion even if the initial motion was denied. This is often done so that the jury's verdict can be reinstated if the trial court's decision is overturned on appeal. (Choice C) A trial court's discretionary rulings (eg, admissibility of evidence) are reviewed for abuse of discretion and are given great deference. However, a trial court's legal rulings (eg, granting or denying JMOL) are reviewed de novo and are given no deference. (Choice D) A notice of appeal must be filed within 30 days after entry of a final judgment OR an order disposing of a posttrial motion. Therefore, the customer timely appealed 28 days after the trial court entered a posttrial order granting the renewed motion for JMOL. Educational objective: When considering a motion for JMOL, a court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in favor of the nonmovant, (2) disregard evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000) (explaining the procedural requirements for considering a JMOL motion).","rule":"When considering a motion for JMOL, a court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in favor of the nonmovant, (2) disregard evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.","irac":{"issue":"A customer sued a retailer in federal court, claiming that the retailer had intentionally engaged in trade practices that violated federal law. At the close of evidence, the retailer filed a motion for judgment as a matter of law, which was denied. The jury returned a verdict for the customer. Twenty-five days after the court entered judgment on the verdict, the retailer filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law. The trial court determined that the evidence was legally insufficient to find in the customer's favor because the customer's main witness was not credible. The trial court then issued an order granting the retailer's motion. Twenty-eight days later, the customer appealed. Is the appellate court likely to uphold the trial court's ruling?","rule":"When considering a motion for JMOL, a court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in favor of the nonmovant, (2) disregard evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.","analysis":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court enter a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. An initial motion for JMOL must be filed before the case is submitted to the jury. If that motion is denied, the movant can file a renewed motion within 28 days after the entry of final judgment on the jury's verdict.* When considering an initial or renewed motion for JMOL, the trial court must:\n• view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant\n\n• disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe and\n\n• not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.\n\nHere, the retailer timely renewed its motion for JMOL 25 days after the trial court entered judgment on the jury's verdict (Choice A). The trial court granted this motion after determining that the evidence was legally insufficient to find in the customer's\n\nfavor. However, the appellate court will likely overturn that ruling because the trial court improperly considered the credibility of the customer's main witness. *A trial court can grant the renewed motion even if the initial motion was denied. This is often done so that the jury's verdict can be reinstated if the trial court's decision is overturned on appeal. (Choice C) A trial court's discretionary rulings (eg, admissibility of evidence) are reviewed for abuse of discretion and are given great deference. However, a trial court's legal rulings (eg, granting or denying JMOL) are reviewed de novo and are given no deference. (Choice D) A notice of appeal must be filed within 30 days after entry of a final judgment OR an order disposing of a posttrial motion. Therefore, the customer timely appealed 28 days after the trial court entered a posttrial order granting the renewed motion for JMOL. Educational objective: When considering a motion for JMOL, a court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in favor of the nonmovant, (2) disregard evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000) (explaining the procedural requirements for considering a JMOL motion).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000) (explaining the procedural requirements for considering a JMOL motion).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[271],"sourceAnswerPages":[272,273],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名顾客在联邦法院起诉一家零售商，主张零售商故意从事违反联邦法律的商业行为。举证结束时，零售商申请依法判决，法院予以驳回。陪审团作出有利于顾客的裁决。法院依据裁决登记判决 25 天后，零售商提出续行依法判决动议。审判法院认定，顾客的主要证人不可信，因此证据在法律上不足以支持顾客胜诉。审判法院遂发布命令批准零售商的动议。28 天后，顾客提起上诉。上诉法院可能维持审判法院的裁定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为零售商在审判法院依据裁决登记判决超过 21 天后才续行申请依法判决。","B":"不会，因为审判法院在批准零售商的续行动议时衡量了顾客主要证人的可信度。","C":"会，因为上诉法院必须高度尊重审判法院的裁定。","D":"会，因为顾客在审判法院依据裁决登记判决超过 30 天后才上诉。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决动议（FRCP 50）：\n\n提出时间：\n• 可在非动议方陈述案件后、案件交付陪审团前提出；\n• 可在登记终局判决后 28 天内续行提出。\n\n程序要求：\n• 动议方必须指明所请求的判决，以及使其有权获得判决的法律／事实；\n• 法院必须：\n  – 审视证据，并作出一切有利于非动议方的合理推论；\n  – 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；\n  – 不得衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n批准标准：\n• 证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n依法判决（JMOL）动议请求法院为动议方登记判决，因为证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。最初的 JMOL 动议必须在案件交付陪审团前提出。若该动议被驳回，动议方可以在根据陪审团裁决登记终局判决后 28 天内续行提出。*\n\n审理最初或续行 JMOL 动议时，审判法院必须：\n• 以最有利于非动议方的方式审视证据，并作出所有合理推论；\n• 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；并且\n• 不得衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n本题中，零售商在审判法院依据陪审团裁决登记判决 25 天后及时续行申请 JMOL（选项 A）。审判法院认定证据在法律上不足以支持顾客胜诉，因而批准动议。但是，上诉法院很可能撤销该裁定，因为审判法院不当衡量了顾客主要证人的可信度。\n\n*即使最初的动议被驳回，审判法院仍可批准续行动议。这样做通常是为了在审判法院决定被上诉撤销时，可直接恢复陪审团裁决，而无需重新审判。\n\n（选项 C）审判法院的酌情裁定，例如证据可采性，适用滥用酌情权标准审查并获得高度尊重。但审判法院的法律裁定，例如批准或驳回 JMOL，适用重新审查标准，不获得尊重。\n\n（选项 D）上诉通知必须在登记终局判决后 30 天内，或在处理审判后动议的命令作出后 30 天内提交。因此，顾客在审判法院发布批准续行 JMOL 动议的审判后命令 28 天后上诉，是及时的。\n\n学习目标：审理 JMOL 动议时，法院必须：(1) 以有利于非动议方的方式审视证据并作出所有合理推论；(2) 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；且 (3) 不衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a)（依法判决动议）。\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000)（解释审理 JMOL 动议的程序要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-090","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":90,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man from State A sued a woman from State B in a proper federal district court to recover $250,000 for the damage to his car and personal injuries he suffered when the two were involved in a car accident at a four-way intersection. The man requested a jury trial. The only issue in dispute is who had the green light as the cars approached the intersection. At trial, the man called his 18-year-old son to the stand. The son testified that he was sitting in the passenger seat of the man's car and that his father was driving reasonably. On cross-examination, the son admitted that he did not see the light as the man's car approached the intersection. The man called no other witnesses and presented no other evidence. In her defense, the woman called five witnesses to the accident. All five testified that the woman had the green light as she approached the intersection. At the close of evidence, the woman's attorney moved for judgment as a matter of law. The court denied this motion and allowed the case to go to the jury. After deliberating for twenty minutes, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the man. If the woman seeks to overturn the jury's verdict and obtain a judgment in her favor, which of the following actions would most effectively and efficiently accomplish her goal?","choices":{"A":"Move for a judgment on partial findings.","B":"Move for a new trial.","C":"Petition for writ of mandamus.","D":"Renew her motion for judgment as a matter of law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL or directed verdict) is a request that the court issue a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This motion can only be filed after the nonmovant has presented its case but before the case is submitted to the jury. That is because the motion, if granted, removes the case from the jury's consideration. If a motion for JMOL is denied during trial (as seen here), the movant can file a renewed motion for JMOL no later than 28 days after the entry of judgment. A judge can grant the renewed motion even if the original motion was denied. This is often done so that, if the trial court's decision is overturned on appeal, the jury's verdict can simply be reinstated instead of a new trial being required. The motion also provides an effective and efficient means to overturn a jury verdict—especially here, since the evidence on the green light overwhelmingly favors the woman. (Choice A) A judgment on partial findings is analogous to a JMOL but is used when a case is tried before a judge—not a jury (as seen here). (Choice B) The losing party can file a motion for new trial within 28 days after the court enters a final judgment. But a second trial would be expensive and time-consuming, so this motion would not provide the most efficient means to obtain a favorable judgment. (Choice C) A petition for writ of mandamus is a rare procedure that allows a party to seek appellate review before a final judgment is issued—but only when no other remedy (eg, JMOL) is available. Educational objective: A motion for judgment as a matter of law is used to remove a case from the jury's consideration when there is legally insufficient evidence for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. The motion can be renewed within 28 days after the entry of judgment to seek to overturn an adverse verdict. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law is used to remove a case from the jury's consideration when there is legally insufficient evidence for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. The motion can be renewed within 28 days after the entry of judgment to seek to overturn an adverse verdict.","irac":{"issue":"A man from State A sued a woman from State B in a proper federal district court to recover $250,000 for the damage to his car and personal injuries he suffered when the two were involved in a car accident at a four-way intersection. The man requested a jury trial. The only issue in dispute is who had the green light as the cars approached the intersection. At trial, the man called his 18-year-old son to the stand. The son testified that he was sitting in the passenger seat of the man's car and that his father was driving reasonably. On cross-examination, the son admitted that he did not see the light as the man's car approached the intersection. The man called no other witnesses and presented no other evidence. In her defense, the woman called five witnesses to the accident. All five testified that the woman had the green light as she approached the intersection. At the close of evidence, the woman's attorney moved for judgment as a matter of law. The court denied this motion and allowed the case to go to the jury. After deliberating for twenty minutes, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the man. If the woman seeks to overturn the jury's verdict and obtain a judgment in her favor, which of the following actions would most effectively and efficiently accomplish her goal?","rule":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law is used to remove a case from the jury's consideration when there is legally insufficient evidence for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. The motion can be renewed within 28 days after the entry of judgment to seek to overturn an adverse verdict.","analysis":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL or directed verdict) is a request that the court issue a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This motion can only be filed after the nonmovant has presented its case but before the case is submitted to the jury. That is because the motion, if granted, removes the case from the jury's consideration. If a motion for JMOL is denied during trial (as seen here), the movant can file a renewed motion for JMOL no later than 28 days after the entry of judgment. A judge can grant the renewed motion even if the original motion was denied. This is often done so that, if the trial court's decision is overturned on appeal, the jury's verdict can simply be reinstated instead of a new trial being required. The motion also provides an effective and efficient means to overturn a jury verdict—especially here, since the evidence on the green light overwhelmingly favors the woman. (Choice A) A judgment on partial findings is analogous to a JMOL but is used when a case is tried before a judge—not a jury (as seen here). (Choice B) The losing party can file a motion for new trial within 28 days after the court enters a final judgment. But a second trial would be expensive and time-consuming, so this motion would not provide the most efficient means to obtain a favorable judgment. (Choice C) A petition for writ of mandamus is a rare procedure that allows a party to seek appellate review before a final judgment is issued—but only when no other remedy (eg, JMOL) is available. Educational objective: A motion for judgment as a matter of law is used to remove a case from the jury's consideration when there is legally insufficient evidence for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. The motion can be renewed within 28 days after the entry of judgment to seek to overturn an adverse verdict. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[274],"sourceAnswerPages":[275],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名男子在适当的联邦地区法院起诉 B 州一名女子，请求就二人在十字路口发生汽车事故造成的车辆损坏和人身伤害获得 250,000 美元赔偿。男子请求陪审团审判。唯一争议是两车驶近路口时哪一方为绿灯。审判中，男子传唤其 18 岁儿子作证。儿子称自己坐在男子车辆的副驾驶座，父亲驾驶方式合理。交叉询问时，儿子承认车辆驶近路口时自己没有看到信号灯。男子未传唤其他证人，也未提出其他证据。女子传唤五名事故目击证人为自己辩护，五人均称女子驶近路口时是绿灯。举证结束时，女子律师申请依法判决。法院驳回动议，将案件交由陪审团。陪审团评议 20 分钟后，作出有利于男子的裁决。若女子希望推翻陪审团裁决并取得有利于自己的判决，以下哪项行动最有效且最高效？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请就部分认定作出判决。","B":"申请重新审判。","C":"申请强制令。","D":"续行申请依法判决。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决（JMOL，又称指示裁决）动议请求法院为动议方作出判决，因为证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。该动议只能在非动议方陈述案件后、案件交付陪审团前提出，因为批准动议即把案件排除在陪审团考虑之外。\n\n如果审判中的 JMOL 动议被驳回（本题即如此），动议方可以在登记判决后不超过 28 天续行申请 JMOL。即使最初动议被驳回，法官仍可批准续行动议。这样做通常是为了在审判法院决定被上诉撤销时，可以直接恢复陪审团裁决，而不必重新审判。\n\n该动议也是推翻陪审团裁决的有效、高效方式；本题尤其如此，因为关于绿灯的证据压倒性地有利于女子。\n\n（选项 A）就部分认定作出判决类似于 JMOL，但适用于由法官而非陪审团审理的案件；本题为陪审团审判。\n\n（选项 B）败诉方可以在法院登记终局判决后 28 天内申请重新审判。但第二次审判成本高、耗时长，所以这不是取得有利判决的最高效方式。\n\n（选项 C）强制令申请是一种罕见程序，允许当事人在终局判决作出前寻求上诉审查，但只有不存在其他救济（例如 JMOL）时方可使用。\n\n学习目标：当证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决时，可使用依法判决动议将案件排除在陪审团考虑之外。该动议可在登记判决后 28 天内续行提出，以寻求推翻不利裁决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50（依法判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-091","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":91,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A firm sued a corporation in a federal district court for allegedly engaging in unfair trade practices in violation of a federal statute. The firm sought $100,000 and an injunction. At the conclusion of a bench trial, the judge announced her findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record, and judgment was entered in favor of the corporation. Twenty-eight days later, the firm discovered a legal theory it initially overlooked that entitles it to relief. That same day, the firm filed a motion for a new trial based on this newly discovered legal theory. Is the court likely to grant the firm's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the legal theory could have been asserted at trial.","B":"No, because the motion was not timely made.","C":"Yes, because the judge's factual findings and legal conclusions were not in writing.","D":"Yes, because the legal theory shows that the firm is entitled to relief."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence A party can move for a new trial within 28 days after the entry of final judgment, as the firm did here (Choice B). A court may grant a new trial on several grounds. But it will not grant a new trial based on a newly discovered legal theory that the party could have raised at trial—even if the theory entitles the party to relief (Choice D). This encourages attorneys to fully investigate their case before trial is over so that the case can be resolved without perpetual reexamination. (Choice C) After the close of evidence in a bench trial, the judge must provide—orally or in writing—findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record. Educational objective: A new trial cannot be granted based on a newly discovered legal theory that could have been raised at trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 320 (2019) (explaining that a new legal theory or defense is not a basis for a new trial).","rule":"A new trial cannot be granted based on a newly discovered legal theory that could have been raised at trial.","irac":{"issue":"A firm sued a corporation in a federal district court for allegedly engaging in unfair trade practices in violation of a federal statute. The firm sought $100,000 and an injunction. At the conclusion of a bench trial, the judge announced her findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record, and judgment was entered in favor of the corporation. Twenty-eight days later, the firm discovered a legal theory it initially overlooked that entitles it to relief. That same day, the firm filed a motion for a new trial based on this newly discovered legal theory. Is the court likely to grant the firm's motion?","rule":"A new trial cannot be granted based on a newly discovered legal theory that could have been raised at trial.","analysis":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence A party can move for a new trial within 28 days after the entry of final judgment, as the firm did here (Choice B). A court may grant a new trial on several grounds. But it will not grant a new trial based on a newly discovered legal theory that the party could have raised at trial—even if the theory entitles the party to relief (Choice D). This encourages attorneys to fully investigate their case before trial is over so that the case can be resolved without perpetual reexamination. (Choice C) After the close of evidence in a bench trial, the judge must provide—orally or in writing—findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record. Educational objective: A new trial cannot be granted based on a newly discovered legal theory that could have been raised at trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 320 (2019) (explaining that a new legal theory or defense is not a basis for a new trial).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 320 (2019) (explaining that a new legal theory or defense is not a basis for a new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[277],"sourceAnswerPages":[278],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家事务所在联邦地区法院起诉一家股份公司，主张该公司实施违反联邦法律的不公平商业行为。事务所请求 100,000 美元及禁令救济。法官审判结束时，法官在案卷中宣布事实认定与法律结论，并登记有利于公司的判决。28 天后，事务所发现一项最初遗漏、但使其有权获得救济的法律理论，并于同日以该新发现法律理论为由申请重新审判。法院可能批准事务所的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该法律理论本可在审判时提出。","B":"不会，因为动议未及时提出。","C":"会，因为法官的事实认定和法律结论没有采用书面形式。","D":"会，因为该法律理论表明事务所有权获得救济。"},"explanationZh":"重新审判的理由：\n• 造成不利影响的审判错误，例如错误的证据裁定；\n• 法官、律师、当事人或陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为；\n• 裁决不受证据明显权重支持；\n• 裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；\n• 损害赔偿过高或不足；\n• 新发现证据。\n\n当事人可以在登记终局判决后 28 天内申请重新审判，事务所本题正是在该期限内提出（选项 B）。法院可基于多种理由批准重新审判。但是，对于当事人本可在审判时提出的新发现法律理论，即使该理论使其有权获得救济，法院也不会据此批准重新审判（选项 D）。这项规则鼓励律师在审判结束前充分调查案件，使案件得以解决而无需不断重新审查。\n\n（选项 C）法官审判举证结束后，法官必须在案卷中以口头或书面方式提供事实认定及法律结论。\n\n学习目标：不得以本可在审判时提出的新发现法律理论为由批准重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（重新审判动议）。\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 320 (2019)（说明新的法律理论或抗辩不是重新审判的依据）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-092","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":92,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A man sued his employer in federal court for sexual harassment in violation of a federal statute. The man planned to have several of his coworkers testify that they witnessed the employer make sexual advances toward the man on several occasions. However, at their depositions, the coworkers testified that they never witnessed the employer engage in inappropriate behavior. The man had no other evidence to support his claim, so the court entered summary judgment in favor of the employer. Two years later, one of the coworkers who was supposed to testify on the man's behalf told the man that the employer had paid the coworkers a sum of money to lie at their depositions. The man immediately filed a motion in the same federal court to obtain relief from the entry of summary judgment. How is the court likely to rule on his motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because it was not made within one year of the entry of summary judgment.","B":"Deny the motion, because the man failed to appeal.","C":"Grant the motion, because it was made within a reasonable time.","D":"Grant the motion, because the employer engaged in misconduct."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court's final judgment and orders will typically not be disturbed. This promotes confidence in their finality so they can be enforced without perpetual reexamination of the case. However, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b) allows a party to obtain extraordinary relief from a judgment or order in limited circumstances. An FRCP 60(b) motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but three of the grounds for relief must be raised within one year of the court's judgment:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court.\n\n• The moving party discovered new evidence that (1) existed at the time of the trial and (2) could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial (ie, within 28 days of the entry of final judgment).\n\n• An opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.\n\nHere, the man's motion seeks relief from the judgment due to the employer's misconduct in paying the coworkers to lie at their depositions. As a result, this motion was due within one year after the court entered summary judgment in the employer's favor. Since the motion was filed two years later, it was untimely and will likely be denied (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A party need not file an appeal to request relief under FRCP 60(b). Educational objective:\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to obtain extraordinary relief within one year of the entry of a final judgment or order based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) an opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","rule":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to obtain extraordinary relief within one year of the entry of a final judgment or order based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) an opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.","irac":{"issue":"A man sued his employer in federal court for sexual harassment in violation of a federal statute. The man planned to have several of his coworkers testify that they witnessed the employer make sexual advances toward the man on several occasions. However, at their depositions, the coworkers testified that they never witnessed the employer engage in inappropriate behavior. The man had no other evidence to support his claim, so the court entered summary judgment in favor of the employer. Two years later, one of the coworkers who was supposed to testify on the man's behalf told the man that the employer had paid the coworkers a sum of money to lie at their depositions. The man immediately filed a motion in the same federal court to obtain relief from the entry of summary judgment. How is the court likely to rule on his motion?","rule":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to obtain extraordinary relief within one year of the entry of a final judgment or order based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) an opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.","analysis":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court's final judgment and orders will typically not be disturbed. This promotes confidence in their finality so they can be enforced without perpetual reexamination of the case. However, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b) allows a party to obtain extraordinary relief from a judgment or order in limited circumstances. An FRCP 60(b) motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but three of the grounds for relief must be raised within one year of the court's judgment:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court.\n\n• The moving party discovered new evidence that (1) existed at the time of the trial and (2) could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial (ie, within 28 days of the entry of final judgment).\n\n• An opposing party engaged in fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.\n\nHere, the man's motion seeks relief from the judgment due to the employer's misconduct in paying the coworkers to lie at their depositions. As a result, this motion was due within one year after the court entered summary judgment in the employer's favor. Since the motion was filed two years later, it was untimely and will likely be denied (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A party need not file an appeal to request relief under FRCP 60(b). Educational objective:\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 60(b) allows a party to obtain extraordinary relief within one year of the entry of a final judgment or order based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) an opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[279],"sourceAnswerPages":[280,281],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在联邦法院起诉其雇主，主张雇主实施违反联邦法律的性骚扰。男子计划让数名同事作证，称他们曾多次目睹雇主对男子作出性挑逗。但在证言录取中，同事们均称从未目睹雇主实施不当行为。男子没有其他证据支持其请求，因此法院为雇主作出简易判决。两年后，一名原本应为男子作证的同事告诉男子，雇主曾向同事们支付一笔钱，让他们在证言录取中说谎。男子立即向同一联邦法院提出动议，请求从简易判决获得救济。法院可能如何裁定其动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为该动议未在登记简易判决后一年内提出。","B":"驳回动议，因为男子没有提起上诉。","C":"批准动议，因为动议在合理时间内提出。","D":"批准动议，因为雇主实施了不当行为。"},"explanationZh":"从终局判决获得救济（FRCP 60(b)）：\n\n救济理由｜提出时限\n错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽｜一年内\n新发现证据｜一年内\n欺诈、虚假陈述、不当行为｜一年内\n无效判决，例如缺乏管辖权｜合理时间内\n判决已履行、解除或撤销；判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或者继续适用将违反衡平原则｜合理时间内\n其他足以给予救济的理由（极少适用）｜合理时间内\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n法院的终局判决和命令通常不会受到干扰。这样可以增强对其终局性的信赖，使其得以执行，而无需对案件无休止地重新审查。但是，《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）60(b) 允许当事人在有限情形下从判决或命令获得特别救济。\n\nFRCP 60(b) 动议通常可以在合理时间内提出，但下列三项救济理由必须在法院判决后一年内提出：\n• 判决源于当事人或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；\n• 动议方发现新证据，且该证据：(1) 在审判时已经存在；并且 (2) 无法合理地在提出重新审判动议的期限内——即登记终局判决后 28 天内——发现；\n• 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n本题中，男子因雇主付钱让同事在证言录取中说谎这一不当行为，请求从判决获得救济。因此，该动议应在法院为雇主作出简易判决后一年内提出。男子在两年后才提出，已经逾期，很可能被驳回（选项 C、D）。\n\n（选项 B）当事人无需先提起上诉，方可依据 FRCP 60(b) 请求救济。\n\n学习目标：《联邦民事诉讼规则》60(b) 允许当事人在登记终局判决或命令后一年内，基于以下原因获得特别救济：(1) 错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；(2) 新发现证据；或 (3) 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（从判决获得特别救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-093","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":93,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A mechanic sued his former employer in federal court, claiming that the employer had discharged him because of his age in violation of federal law. The employer answered, denying the claims and promptly moving for summary judgment. In support of the motion, the employer attached the mechanic's employment evaluations for the past three years, which rated his skills and performance as poor and culminated in a recommendation for his discharge. What is the mechanic's best argument to defeat the summary judgment motion?","choices":{"A":"The allegations in the complaint conflict with the mechanic's employment evaluations, raising a genuine dispute as to material facts.","B":"The employer cannot rely in his motion on matters outside the pleadings.","C":"The essential facts are unavailable to the mechanic and therefore discovery is required.","D":"The motion was filed before the close of discovery."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A motion for summary judgment requests that the court enter judgment without a full trial because (1) there is no genuine dispute of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. This motion may be filed at any time until 30 days after the close of discovery (Choice D).* The movant must support this motion with admissible facts contained in any of the following: pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence (eg, employment evaluations) (Choice B). Once the movant satisfies this initial burden, the burden shifts to the nonmovant to avoid summary judgment by either:\n• showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence or\n\n• requesting that the court postpone consideration of the motion until additional discovery can take place, and including an affidavit or declaration that describes the desired discovery and why it is needed.\n\nSince the employer promptly moved for summary judgment after submitting an answer, no discovery has taken place and the mechanic has not had time to acquire the essential facts needed to avoid summary judgment. Therefore, the mechanic's best argument to defeat this motion is that the essential facts are unavailable and discovery is required. The mechanic must also attach an affidavit or declaration describing the discovery needed to obtain these facts. *The court or a local rule may set a different deadline for filing a motion for summary judgment. (Choice A) The nonmovant cannot rely on the pleadings in response to a motion for summary judgment because pleadings contain general allegations. Instead, the nonmovant must show specific disputed facts or request that the court postpone consideration. Educational objective: The nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering a motion for summary judgment until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration describing the desired discovery and why it is needed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• 73 Am. Jur. 2d Summary Judgment § 27 (2020) (explaining summary judgment procedure when facts are unavailable to the nonmovant).","rule":"The nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering a motion for summary judgment until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration describing the desired discovery and why it is needed.","irac":{"issue":"A mechanic sued his former employer in federal court, claiming that the employer had discharged him because of his age in violation of federal law. The employer answered, denying the claims and promptly moving for summary judgment. In support of the motion, the employer attached the mechanic's employment evaluations for the past three years, which rated his skills and performance as poor and culminated in a recommendation for his discharge. What is the mechanic's best argument to defeat the summary judgment motion?","rule":"The nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering a motion for summary judgment until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration describing the desired discovery and why it is needed.","analysis":"A motion for summary judgment requests that the court enter judgment without a full trial because (1) there is no genuine dispute of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. This motion may be filed at any time until 30 days after the close of discovery (Choice D).* The movant must support this motion with admissible facts contained in any of the following: pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence (eg, employment evaluations) (Choice B). Once the movant satisfies this initial burden, the burden shifts to the nonmovant to avoid summary judgment by either:\n• showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence or\n\n• requesting that the court postpone consideration of the motion until additional discovery can take place, and including an affidavit or declaration that describes the desired discovery and why it is needed.\n\nSince the employer promptly moved for summary judgment after submitting an answer, no discovery has taken place and the mechanic has not had time to acquire the essential facts needed to avoid summary judgment. Therefore, the mechanic's best argument to defeat this motion is that the essential facts are unavailable and discovery is required. The mechanic must also attach an affidavit or declaration describing the discovery needed to obtain these facts. *The court or a local rule may set a different deadline for filing a motion for summary judgment. (Choice A) The nonmovant cannot rely on the pleadings in response to a motion for summary judgment because pleadings contain general allegations. Instead, the nonmovant must show specific disputed facts or request that the court postpone consideration. Educational objective: The nonmovant can request that the court postpone considering a motion for summary judgment until additional discovery can take place. This request must include an affidavit or declaration describing the desired discovery and why it is needed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• 73 Am. Jur. 2d Summary Judgment § 27 (2020) (explaining summary judgment procedure when facts are unavailable to the nonmovant).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• 73 Am. Jur. 2d Summary Judgment § 27 (2020) (explaining summary judgment procedure when facts are unavailable to the nonmovant).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[282],"sourceAnswerPages":[283],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名机械师在联邦法院起诉前雇主，主张雇主因其年龄而解雇他，违反联邦法律。雇主提交答辩状，否认请求，并立即申请简易判决。为支持动议，雇主附具机械师过去三年的工作评价；评价认为其技能和表现不佳，最终建议解雇。机械师为击败简易判决动议提出的最佳论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"起诉状指控与机械师的工作评价相冲突，形成重大事实真实争议。","B":"雇主不得在动议中依赖诉状以外的事项。","C":"机械师无法取得关键事实，因此需要进行证据开示。","D":"动议在证据开示结束前提出。"},"explanationZh":"简易判决动议请求法院不经完整审判直接登记判决，因为：(1) 不存在重大事实的真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决。该动议可在任何时间提出，直至证据开示结束后 30 天（选项 D）。*\n\n动议方必须用以下任一材料所包含的可采事实支持动议：诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采证据的材料，例如工作评价（选项 B）。\n\n动议方满足初步责任后，责任转移至非动议方。为避免简易判决，非动议方必须选择：\n• 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采证据的材料，证明具体争议事实；或\n• 请求法院在完成额外证据开示前暂缓审理动议，并附具宣誓书或声明，说明所需证据开示及其必要性。\n\n雇主提交答辩状后立即申请简易判决，证据开示尚未进行，机械师没有时间取得避免简易判决所需的关键事实。因此，机械师击败动议的最佳论点是关键事实尚不可取得，需要进行证据开示。机械师还须附具宣誓书或声明，说明取得这些事实所需的证据开示。\n\n*法院或当地规则可以另定简易判决动议的提交期限。\n\n（选项 A）非动议方回应简易判决动议时不能依赖诉状，因为诉状只包含一般性指控。相反，非动议方必须证明具体争议事实，或请求法院暂缓审理。\n\n学习目标：非动议方可以请求法院在完成额外证据开示前暂缓审理简易判决动议。请求必须附具宣誓书或声明，说明所需证据开示及其必要性。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56（简易判决）。\n• 73 Am. Jur. 2d Summary Judgment § 27 (2020)（解释非动议方无法取得事实时的简易判决程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-033"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-094","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":94,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in federal court for trademark infringement. At the conclusion of trial, the judge instructed the 10-member jury. In the jury deliberation room, one of the jurors pulled out his cell phone and searched for \"trademark infringement.\" He read aloud from an article that said, \"trademark infringement hurts the U.S. economy.\" The jury foreman directed the juror to stop reading and sent a note to the judge describing the incident. The judge brought the jury back to the courtroom and reminded the members of their obligation not to consult outside sources during deliberations. She confiscated their phones, instructed them to ignore the juror's misconduct, and directed them to continue deliberating. The jury returned to the deliberation room. After three hours, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The plaintiff moved for a new trial based on the juror's conduct 21 days after the entry of the final judgment. How will the judge likely rule on the plaintiff's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the juror's misconduct was harmless.","B":"Deny the motion, because the plaintiff waived its right to challenge the juror's conduct by filing an untimely motion for new trial.","C":"Grant the motion, because the judge should have removed the juror from the jury.","D":"Grant the motion, because the juror engaged in misconduct by consulting outside information."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence A court can grant a new trial if a juror (or judge, party, or attorney) engaged in prejudicial misconduct during the trial. Misconduct is prejudicial when it affects a party's substantial rights. This requires the court to determine with fair assurance whether the jury's verdict was influenced by the misconduct. If there was no improper influence, then the misconduct was harmless and does not provide a basis for a new trial. For example, misconduct can be made harmless (ie, \"cured\") if the judge admonishes the jury to disregard that conduct. Here, a juror read aloud from an article that \"trademark infringement hurts the U.S. economy.\" This misconduct may have initially prejudiced the defendant who had allegedly engaged in trademark infringement. But it did not prejudice the plaintiff because the statement supported the plaintiff as the holder of the trademark.* Therefore, the court will likely find that the misconduct was harmless and deny the plaintiff's motion for a new trial (Choice D). *Even if this misconduct had prejudiced the plaintiff, the prejudice was likely cured when the judge confiscated the jurors' phones, instructed them to ignore the juror's misconduct, and directed them to continue deliberating. (Choice B) A party must file a motion for a new trial within 28 days after the entry of a final judgment. Therefore, the plaintiff's motion for new trial—filed 21 days after the court entered the final judgment—was timely. (Choice C) A juror may be removed during trial if the juror can no longer capably serve on the jury (eg, due to medical issues) or if the juror engaged in misconduct (as seen here). However, the judge has considerable discretion to decide whether removal is warranted. Educational objective: A new trial is warranted when a juror engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial.\n\nReferences\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61 (harmless error).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 143 (2019) (explaining that a juror's misconduct must be prejudicial to warrant a new trial).","rule":"A new trial is warranted when a juror engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in federal court for trademark infringement. At the conclusion of trial, the judge instructed the 10-member jury. In the jury deliberation room, one of the jurors pulled out his cell phone and searched for \"trademark infringement.\" He read aloud from an article that said, \"trademark infringement hurts the U.S. economy.\" The jury foreman directed the juror to stop reading and sent a note to the judge describing the incident. The judge brought the jury back to the courtroom and reminded the members of their obligation not to consult outside sources during deliberations. She confiscated their phones, instructed them to ignore the juror's misconduct, and directed them to continue deliberating. The jury returned to the deliberation room. After three hours, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The plaintiff moved for a new trial based on the juror's conduct 21 days after the entry of the final judgment. How will the judge likely rule on the plaintiff's motion?","rule":"A new trial is warranted when a juror engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial.","analysis":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence A court can grant a new trial if a juror (or judge, party, or attorney) engaged in prejudicial misconduct during the trial. Misconduct is prejudicial when it affects a party's substantial rights. This requires the court to determine with fair assurance whether the jury's verdict was influenced by the misconduct. If there was no improper influence, then the misconduct was harmless and does not provide a basis for a new trial. For example, misconduct can be made harmless (ie, \"cured\") if the judge admonishes the jury to disregard that conduct. Here, a juror read aloud from an article that \"trademark infringement hurts the U.S. economy.\" This misconduct may have initially prejudiced the defendant who had allegedly engaged in trademark infringement. But it did not prejudice the plaintiff because the statement supported the plaintiff as the holder of the trademark.* Therefore, the court will likely find that the misconduct was harmless and deny the plaintiff's motion for a new trial (Choice D). *Even if this misconduct had prejudiced the plaintiff, the prejudice was likely cured when the judge confiscated the jurors' phones, instructed them to ignore the juror's misconduct, and directed them to continue deliberating. (Choice B) A party must file a motion for a new trial within 28 days after the entry of a final judgment. Therefore, the plaintiff's motion for new trial—filed 21 days after the court entered the final judgment—was timely. (Choice C) A juror may be removed during trial if the juror can no longer capably serve on the jury (eg, due to medical issues) or if the juror engaged in misconduct (as seen here). However, the judge has considerable discretion to decide whether removal is warranted. Educational objective: A new trial is warranted when a juror engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial.\n\nReferences\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61 (harmless error).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 143 (2019) (explaining that a juror's misconduct must be prejudicial to warrant a new trial).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61 (harmless error).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 143 (2019) (explaining that a juror's misconduct must be prejudicial to warrant a new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[285],"sourceAnswerPages":[286,287],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦法院起诉被告商标侵权。审判结束时，法官向由 10 人组成的陪审团作出指示。在陪审团评议室，一名陪审员拿出手机搜索“商标侵权”。他朗读一篇文章，其中称：“商标侵权损害美国经济。”陪审团主席让他停止朗读，并向法官递交纸条说明此事。法官将陪审团召回法庭，提醒成员在评议期间不得查询外部资料。法官没收其手机，指示陪审团忽略该陪审员的不当行为并继续评议。陪审团返回评议室，三小时后作出有利于被告的裁决。法院依裁决登记终局判决。终局判决登记 21 天后，原告以陪审员行为为由申请重新审判。法官可能如何裁定原告的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为陪审员的不当行为属于无害错误。","B":"驳回动议，因为原告未及时申请重新审判，已放弃质疑陪审员行为的权利。","C":"批准动议，因为法官本应将该陪审员从陪审团中除名。","D":"批准动议，因为陪审员查询外部信息，实施了不当行为。"},"explanationZh":"重新审判的理由：\n• 造成不利影响的审判错误，例如错误的证据裁定；\n• 法官、律师、当事人或陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为；\n• 裁决不受证据明显权重支持；\n• 裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；\n• 损害赔偿过高或不足；\n• 新发现证据。\n\n陪审员（或法官、当事人、律师）在审判中实施造成不利影响的不当行为时，法院可以批准重新审判。不当行为影响一方的重大权利时，才属于造成不利影响；法院必须以相当把握判断陪审团裁决是否受该行为影响。若不存在不当影响，该行为属于无害错误，不构成重新审判依据。例如，法官告诫陪审团忽略该行为，可以消除其影响，即予以“补救”。\n\n本题中，陪审员朗读的文章称“商标侵权损害美国经济”。该行为最初可能对被指控实施商标侵权的被告不利，但不会对作为商标权人的原告造成不利影响，因为该陈述支持原告。* 因此，法院很可能认定该不当行为无害，驳回原告的重新审判动议（选项 D）。\n\n*即使该行为对原告不利，法官没收陪审员手机、指示陪审团忽略不当行为并继续评议，也很可能已经消除不利影响。\n\n（选项 B）当事人必须在登记终局判决后 28 天内申请重新审判。原告在法院登记终局判决 21 天后提出，属于及时。\n\n（选项 C）陪审员因医疗问题等原因无法继续胜任，或实施不当行为（本题即如此）时，可以在审判中被除名。但法官对是否应除名享有相当大的酌情权。\n\n学习目标：陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为，即通过影响陪审团裁决而侵害当事人重大权利时，应当重新审判。若该不当行为未造成不利影响，则只是无害错误，不足以支持重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61（无害错误）。\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 143 (2019)（说明陪审员不当行为必须造成不利影响，方可重新审判）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-095","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":95,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A woman brought a diversity action against a pharmaceutical company in a federal court in State A for strict products liability arising under State A law. The woman claims that the company defectively manufactured her medication, which caused her to suffer a stroke that led to complete paralysis of the left side of her body. The company moved for summary judgment, arguing that a State A statute forbids strict products liability lawsuits against pharmaceutical companies. The federal court granted the motion and entered summary judgment dismissing the woman's action with prejudice. Fourteen days after the federal court entered summary judgment, the highest court in State A held that the State A statute granting immunity to pharmaceutical companies only applies to strict products liability actions alleging a design defect. What is the best course of action the woman's lawyer could take?","choices":{"A":"File a motion for judgment as a matter of law.","B":"File a motion to amend the summary judgment.","C":"File a motion to correct the mistake in the summary judgment.","D":"Refile the strict products liability action against the pharmaceutical company."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Motion to alter or amend final judgment\n\nMotion must be made within 28 days on any of following grounds:\n\n• Judgment based on manifest error of law or fact (eg, court applied wrong law)\n• Necessary to prevent manifest injustice (eg, attorney or juror misconduct)\n• Discovery of new evidence that was unavailable during trial\n• Intervening change in controlling law A party can file a motion to alter or amend a final judgment (eg, a summary judgment) within 28 days after entry of that judgment. This motion can be based on several grounds (see image above), including an intervening change in the controlling law. Here, a State A federal district court entered summary judgment in favor of the pharmaceutical company because a State A statute forbade strict products liability lawsuits against pharmaceutical companies. Fourteen days later, the highest court in State A held that the statute only grants such immunity in strict products liability actions alleging a design defect. Since the woman alleges a manufacturing defect, the best course her lawyer could take is to quickly file a motion to amend the summary judgment. This will allow her case to proceed in federal court. (Choice A) A party can move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) only after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present evidence at trial (not seen here). Additionally, there is no indication that the woman has offered proof that the company defectively manufactured the medication at issue that would entitle her to JMOL. (Choice C) A motion to correct a mistake is appropriate when a judgment contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission. However, no such mistakes exist in the summary judgment at issue here. Instead, the summary judgment was based on substantive controlling law that has since changed. (Choice D) The woman could refile her strict products liability action against the pharmaceutical company. However, this would not be the best way to challenge the summary judgment since this would likely be more expensive and time-consuming than moving to amend the judgment. Educational objective: A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment, including a summary judgment, within 28 days after entry of the judgment. One ground for such a motion is that there has been an intervening change in the controlling law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to alter or amend a judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2020) (explaining the grounds for a motion to alter or amend a final judgment).","rule":"A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment, including a summary judgment, within 28 days after entry of the judgment. One ground for such a motion is that there has been an intervening change in the controlling law.","irac":{"issue":"A woman brought a diversity action against a pharmaceutical company in a federal court in State A for strict products liability arising under State A law. The woman claims that the company defectively manufactured her medication, which caused her to suffer a stroke that led to complete paralysis of the left side of her body. The company moved for summary judgment, arguing that a State A statute forbids strict products liability lawsuits against pharmaceutical companies. The federal court granted the motion and entered summary judgment dismissing the woman's action with prejudice. Fourteen days after the federal court entered summary judgment, the highest court in State A held that the State A statute granting immunity to pharmaceutical companies only applies to strict products liability actions alleging a design defect. What is the best course of action the woman's lawyer could take?","rule":"A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment, including a summary judgment, within 28 days after entry of the judgment. One ground for such a motion is that there has been an intervening change in the controlling law.","analysis":"Motion to alter or amend final judgment\n\nMotion must be made within 28 days on any of following grounds:\n\n• Judgment based on manifest error of law or fact (eg, court applied wrong law)\n• Necessary to prevent manifest injustice (eg, attorney or juror misconduct)\n• Discovery of new evidence that was unavailable during trial\n• Intervening change in controlling law A party can file a motion to alter or amend a final judgment (eg, a summary judgment) within 28 days after entry of that judgment. This motion can be based on several grounds (see image above), including an intervening change in the controlling law. Here, a State A federal district court entered summary judgment in favor of the pharmaceutical company because a State A statute forbade strict products liability lawsuits against pharmaceutical companies. Fourteen days later, the highest court in State A held that the statute only grants such immunity in strict products liability actions alleging a design defect. Since the woman alleges a manufacturing defect, the best course her lawyer could take is to quickly file a motion to amend the summary judgment. This will allow her case to proceed in federal court. (Choice A) A party can move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) only after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present evidence at trial (not seen here). Additionally, there is no indication that the woman has offered proof that the company defectively manufactured the medication at issue that would entitle her to JMOL. (Choice C) A motion to correct a mistake is appropriate when a judgment contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission. However, no such mistakes exist in the summary judgment at issue here. Instead, the summary judgment was based on substantive controlling law that has since changed. (Choice D) The woman could refile her strict products liability action against the pharmaceutical company. However, this would not be the best way to challenge the summary judgment since this would likely be more expensive and time-consuming than moving to amend the judgment. Educational objective: A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment, including a summary judgment, within 28 days after entry of the judgment. One ground for such a motion is that there has been an intervening change in the controlling law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to alter or amend a judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2020) (explaining the grounds for a motion to alter or amend a final judgment).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to alter or amend a judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2020) (explaining the grounds for a motion to alter or amend a final judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[288],"sourceAnswerPages":[289,290],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子依据 A 州法律，在 A 州联邦法院对一家制药公司提起多元州籍严格产品责任诉讼。女子主张，公司在制造其药物时存在缺陷，导致她中风，并造成左半身完全瘫痪。公司申请简易判决，主张 A 州法律禁止针对制药公司的严格产品责任诉讼。联邦法院批准动议，并登记简易判决，有损害驳回女子的诉讼。联邦法院登记简易判决 14 天后，A 州最高法院裁定，A 州赋予制药公司豁免权的法律仅适用于主张设计缺陷的严格产品责任诉讼。女子律师可采取的最佳行动是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提出依法判决动议。","B":"提出修改简易判决的动议。","C":"提出更正简易判决错误的动议。","D":"重新对制药公司提起严格产品责任诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"修改终局判决动议：\n\n动议必须在 28 天内提出，并基于以下任一理由：\n• 判决基于明显法律或事实错误，例如法院适用错误法律；\n• 为防止明显不公所必需，例如律师或陪审员不当行为；\n• 发现审判期间无法取得的新证据；\n• 控制性法律在此期间发生变化。\n\n当事人可以在登记终局判决（例如简易判决）后 28 天内提出修改动议。该动议可以基于多项理由（见上图），包括控制性法律在此期间发生变化。\n\n本题中，A 州联邦地区法院以 A 州法律禁止针对制药公司的严格产品责任诉讼为由，为制药公司登记简易判决。14 天后，A 州最高法院裁定，该法律仅在主张设计缺陷的严格产品责任诉讼中给予此种豁免。女子主张的是制造缺陷，所以其律师的最佳做法是立即提出修改简易判决的动议。这会使案件能够在联邦法院继续进行。\n\n（选项 A）只有非动议方在审判中已有机会提出证据后，当事人才能申请依法判决（JMOL），本题尚未进入审判。此外，没有迹象表明女子已经提出足以证明公司制造药物存在缺陷、使她有权取得 JMOL 的证据。\n\n（选项 C）判决包含书记性错误或因疏忽、遗漏产生的错误时，适合提出更正错误动议。本题简易判决不存在此类错误，而是其所依据的实体控制性法律后来发生变化。\n\n（选项 D）女子可以重新对制药公司提起严格产品责任诉讼。但这不是挑战简易判决的最佳方式，因为相比申请修改判决，重新起诉很可能成本更高、耗时更长。\n\n学习目标：当事人可以在登记包括简易判决在内的终局判决后 28 天内申请修改。其中一项理由是控制性法律在此期间发生变化。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e)（修改判决动议）。\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2020)（解释修改终局判决动议的理由）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-096","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":96,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff has brought a federal diversity action against a defendant, alleging that the defendant defamed and inflicted emotional distress on the plaintiff. The plaintiff had the summons and complaint delivered to the defendant's home by the plaintiff's 18-year-old nephew. The nephew handed the documents to the defendant's 16-year-old daughter who resides there. She placed the documents on the kitchen table, and the defendant reviewed those documents later that week. After reading the complaint, the defendant could not determine what conduct the plaintiff was claiming had caused the emotional distress. What is the defendant's best response to the plaintiff's complaint?","choices":{"A":"File a motion for a more definite statement.","B":"File a motion for judgment on the pleadings.","C":"File a motion to dismiss for improper service of process.","D":"File a motion to strike the emotional distress claim."},"answer":"A","explanation":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nWhen a party believes that a pleading (eg, complaint) is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a response, the party should file an FRCP 12(e) motion for a more definite statement. If the court grants this motion because the pleading lacks the requisite specificity, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement (eg, amended complaint) that will allow the movant to file a responsive pleading (eg, answer). Here, the defendant could not determine what conduct the plaintiff was claiming had caused the emotional distress. Therefore, a motion for a more definite statement is the defendant's best response to the plaintiff's unclear complaint. (Choice B) Under FRCP 12(c), a party may move for a judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings have closed—ie, once the plaintiff's complaint and the defendant's answer have been served. Since the defendant has not yet served an answer, a motion for judgment on the pleadings would be improper. (Choice C) An individual can be served with process if the summons and complaint are delivered to the individual's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion (16-year-old daughter). Process can be served by any person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (18-year-old nephew). As a result, a motion to dismiss for improper service of process would be unsuccessful. (Choice D) A party may move to strike an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter from an opposing party's pleading under FRCP 12(f). Since the plaintiff's complaint contains none of these matters, a motion to strike the emotional distress claim would not be the defendant's best response.\n\nEducational objective: A party should move for a more definite statement when a pleading is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).","rule":"A party should move for a more definite statement when a pleading is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff has brought a federal diversity action against a defendant, alleging that the defendant defamed and inflicted emotional distress on the plaintiff. The plaintiff had the summons and complaint delivered to the defendant's home by the plaintiff's 18-year-old nephew. The nephew handed the documents to the defendant's 16-year-old daughter who resides there. She placed the documents on the kitchen table, and the defendant reviewed those documents later that week. After reading the complaint, the defendant could not determine what conduct the plaintiff was claiming had caused the emotional distress. What is the defendant's best response to the plaintiff's complaint?","rule":"A party should move for a more definite statement when a pleading is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading.","analysis":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nWhen a party believes that a pleading (eg, complaint) is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a response, the party should file an FRCP 12(e) motion for a more definite statement. If the court grants this motion because the pleading lacks the requisite specificity, the nonmovant must provide a more definite statement (eg, amended complaint) that will allow the movant to file a responsive pleading (eg, answer). Here, the defendant could not determine what conduct the plaintiff was claiming had caused the emotional distress. Therefore, a motion for a more definite statement is the defendant's best response to the plaintiff's unclear complaint. (Choice B) Under FRCP 12(c), a party may move for a judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings have closed—ie, once the plaintiff's complaint and the defendant's answer have been served. Since the defendant has not yet served an answer, a motion for judgment on the pleadings would be improper. (Choice C) An individual can be served with process if the summons and complaint are delivered to the individual's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion (16-year-old daughter). Process can be served by any person who is at least 18 years old and not a party to the suit (18-year-old nephew). As a result, a motion to dismiss for improper service of process would be unsuccessful. (Choice D) A party may move to strike an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter from an opposing party's pleading under FRCP 12(f). Since the plaintiff's complaint contains none of these matters, a motion to strike the emotional distress claim would not be the defendant's best response.\n\nEducational objective: A party should move for a more definite statement when a pleading is so vague or ambiguous that the party cannot reasonably draft a responsive pleading. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (motion for a more definite statement).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[291],"sourceAnswerPages":[292,293],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告对被告提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，主张被告诽谤原告并使其遭受精神痛苦。原告让自己 18 岁的外甥将传票和起诉状送至被告住宅。外甥把文件交给住在该处的被告 16 岁女儿。女儿将文件放在厨房桌上，被告在该周稍后查看。阅读起诉状后，被告无法确定原告所称造成精神痛苦的是何种行为。被告对原告起诉状的最佳回应是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提出更明确陈述动议。","B":"提出诉状判决动议。","C":"以诉讼文书送达不当为由提出驳回动议。","D":"提出删除精神痛苦请求的动议。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 12 动议：\n\n12(b)｜主张以下任一抗辩的动议：\n• 缺乏事项管辖权或对人管辖权；\n• 审判地不当；\n• 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分；\n• 未陈述可给予救济的请求；\n• 未合并必要当事人。\n\n12(c)｜诉状判决动议。\n12(e)｜诉状更明确陈述动议。\n12(f)｜删除诉状中不充分抗辩或不相关事项的动议。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n当事人认为诉状（例如起诉状）含糊或模棱两可到无法合理起草回应时，应依据 FRCP 12(e) 提出更明确陈述动议。若法院因诉状缺乏所需具体性而批准动议，非动议方必须提供更明确陈述，例如修改后的起诉状，使动议方能够提交回应性诉状，例如答辩状。\n\n本题中，被告无法确定原告所称造成精神痛苦的是何种行为。因此，更明确陈述动议是被告回应这份不明确起诉状的最佳方式。\n\n（选项 B）依据 FRCP 12(c)，诉状提交完毕——即原告起诉状和被告答辩状均已送达——后，当事人可以申请诉状判决。被告尚未送达答辩状，所以提出诉状判决动议并不适当。\n\n（选项 C）传票和起诉状送至个人住所，并留给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人（16 岁女儿）时，即可向个人完成送达。任何年满 18 岁且不是诉讼当事人的人（18 岁外甥）均可送达诉讼文书。因此，以诉讼文书送达不当为由提出的驳回动议不会成功。\n\n（选项 D）依据 FRCP 12(f)，当事人可以申请从对方诉状中删除不充分抗辩或任何重复、诽谤性、不适当或不相关事项。原告起诉状不包含此类事项，所以删除精神痛苦请求的动议并非最佳回应。\n\n学习目标：诉状含糊或模棱两可到使当事人无法合理起草回应性诉状时，应申请更明确陈述。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e)（更明确陈述动议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-097","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":97,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in federal court for injuries arising out of an accident involving the parties. The plaintiff alleged and presented evidence at trial demonstrating that her injuries had left her legs permanently paralyzed. The jury found in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her $5 million in damages. Two months after the court entered judgment, the defendant was given a video made that day showing the plaintiff jogging with her doctor. What is the best way for the defendant to seek relief from the judgment?","choices":{"A":"Move for a new trial or in the alternative for remittitur to reduce the award in light of the shortened duration of the plaintiff's injuries.","B":"Move for relief from the judgment on the ground that the judgment was based on the jury's mistaken belief that the plaintiff's injuries would be permanent.","C":"Move for relief from the judgment on the ground that the plaintiff committed a fraud in obtaining damages for permanent injuries.","D":"Move for relief from the judgment on the ground that there is newly discovered evidence that the plaintiff's injuries were not permanent."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b), a party can seek extraordinary relief from a district court's final judgment in limited circumstances. This motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of the final judgment when the motion asserts any of the following grounds for relief:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court (Choice B).\n\n• The party has discovered new evidence that existed at the time of the trial and could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial—ie, within 28 days of the final judgment.\n\n• The opposing party engaged in misrepresentation, misconduct, or fraud.\n\nHere, the court entered a final judgment awarding the plaintiff $5 million for injuries that allegedly left her legs permanently paralyzed. Two months later, the defendant received a video showing the plaintiff jogging. Since this video shows that the plaintiff committed a fraud in obtaining damages, the defendant should move for relief from the judgment on this ground. (Choice A) Motions for new trial and motions for remittitur must be filed within 28 days after a final judgment is entered. Since this deadline has passed, the defendant cannot obtain relief through these motions.\n\n(Choice D) Evidence is \"newly discovered\" if it existed at the time of the trial and could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial. Therefore, the video created two months after trial is not newly discovered evidence within the meaning of FRCP 60(b). Educational objective: A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","rule":"A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in federal court for injuries arising out of an accident involving the parties. The plaintiff alleged and presented evidence at trial demonstrating that her injuries had left her legs permanently paralyzed. The jury found in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her $5 million in damages. Two months after the court entered judgment, the defendant was given a video made that day showing the plaintiff jogging with her doctor. What is the best way for the defendant to seek relief from the judgment?","rule":"A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.","analysis":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 60(b), a party can seek extraordinary relief from a district court's final judgment in limited circumstances. This motion may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of the final judgment when the motion asserts any of the following grounds for relief:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or the court (Choice B).\n\n• The party has discovered new evidence that existed at the time of the trial and could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial—ie, within 28 days of the final judgment.\n\n• The opposing party engaged in misrepresentation, misconduct, or fraud.\n\nHere, the court entered a final judgment awarding the plaintiff $5 million for injuries that allegedly left her legs permanently paralyzed. Two months later, the defendant received a video showing the plaintiff jogging. Since this video shows that the plaintiff committed a fraud in obtaining damages, the defendant should move for relief from the judgment on this ground. (Choice A) Motions for new trial and motions for remittitur must be filed within 28 days after a final judgment is entered. Since this deadline has passed, the defendant cannot obtain relief through these motions.\n\n(Choice D) Evidence is \"newly discovered\" if it existed at the time of the trial and could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial. Therefore, the video created two months after trial is not newly discovered evidence within the meaning of FRCP 60(b). Educational objective: A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment that is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[294],"sourceAnswerPages":[295,296],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦法院起诉被告，请求赔偿双方事故造成的伤害。原告主张并在审判中提出证据证明，其伤害导致双腿永久瘫痪。陪审团判原告胜诉，并判给 500 万美元损害赔偿。法院登记判决两个月后，被告收到一段当日拍摄的视频，显示原告与其医生一起慢跑。被告寻求判决救济的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请重新审判，或者备选申请减额裁决，鉴于原告伤势持续时间缩短而降低赔偿额。","B":"以判决基于陪审团误信原告伤势永久存在为由，申请从判决获得救济。","C":"以原告为取得永久伤害赔偿而实施欺诈为由，申请从判决获得救济。","D":"以存在证明原告伤势并非永久的新发现证据为由，申请从判决获得救济。"},"explanationZh":"从终局判决获得救济（FRCP 60(b)）：\n\n救济理由｜提出时限\n错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽｜一年内\n新发现证据｜一年内\n欺诈、虚假陈述、不当行为｜一年内\n无效判决，例如缺乏管辖权｜合理时间内\n判决已履行、解除或撤销；判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或者继续适用将违反衡平原则｜合理时间内\n其他足以给予救济的理由（极少适用）｜合理时间内\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）60(b)，当事人可在有限情形下从地区法院终局判决获得特别救济。该动议通常可以在合理时间内提出；但若主张以下任一救济理由，则必须在登记终局判决后一年内提出：\n• 判决源于当事人或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽（选项 B）；\n• 当事人发现了审判时已存在、且无法合理地在重新审判动议期限——终局判决后 28 天内——发现的新证据；\n• 对方实施虚假陈述、不当行为或欺诈。\n\n本题中，法院登记终局判决，判给原告 500 万美元，以赔偿据称导致其双腿永久瘫痪的伤害。两个月后，被告收到显示原告正在慢跑的视频。该视频表明原告为取得损害赔偿实施欺诈，所以被告应以此为由申请从判决获得救济。\n\n（选项 A）重新审判动议和减额裁决动议必须在登记终局判决后 28 天内提出。该期限已经届满，被告不能通过这些动议取得救济。\n\n（选项 D）若证据在审判时已经存在，且无法合理地在重新审判动议期限内发现，才属于“新发现”证据。因此，审判两个月后才制作的视频不属于 FRCP 60(b) 意义上的新发现证据。\n\n学习目标：判决基于以下任一原因时，当事人可以在登记终局判决后一年内获得特别救济：(1) 当事人或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；(2) 新发现证据；或 (3) 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（从判决获得特别救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-098","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":98,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man brought a federal diversity action against his insurance company, alleging that the company had breached its duty under his insurance policy by refusing to pay for his medical expenses resulting from a mountain-biking accident. At the jury trial, the man presented evidence that he had paid all premiums on the insurance policy and that the policy covered personal-injury-related medical expenses arising from accidents. After he rested his case, the company presented evidence that a provision of the policy excluded payment for injury-related expenses resulting from an insured's \"unduly risky\" behavior. The company also presented a witness who testified that the accident had occurred in an area where posted signs warned bikers not to enter. The man did not cross-examine the witness. After resting its case, the company moved for judgment as a matter of law. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because a motion for judgment as a matter of law must first be made at the close of the plaintiff's case-in-chief.","B":"No, because whether the man's behavior was unduly risky is a question of fact for the jury to resolve.","C":"Yes, because the company's uncontradicted evidence of the man's unduly risky behavior means that no reasonable jury could find that the policy covers his injuries.","D":"Yes, because the man waived his right to rebut the company's evidence by not addressing the \"unduly risky\" policy provision in his case-in-chief."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court enter a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This means that the court should deny the motion for JMOL and allow the jury to decide the case if reasonable persons could reach different conclusions based on the evidence. Here, the issue is whether the man's actions constituted \"unduly risky\" behavior. The company presented evidence that the man went mountain biking in an area where posted signs warned bikers not to enter. But since it did not present evidence showing why these signs were posted, a reasonable jury could conclude that they were meant to keep bikers out for multiple reasons (eg, to protect wildlife). As a result, whether the man's behavior was unduly risky is a question of fact for the jury to resolve, and the company's motion for JMOL should be denied. (Choice A) A motion for JMOL may be filed any time after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present evidence supporting a claim or defense and before the case is submitted to the jury. Therefore, the company did not need to move for JMOL at the close of the man's case-in-chief.\n\n(Choice C) Although the man submitted no evidence to contradict the company's evidence, a reasonable jury could still conclude that his actions were not \"unduly risky.\" (Choice D) The man had no duty to address the \"unduly risky\" policy provision in his case-in-chief because the company raised it as a defense. The man could have rebutted the company's evidence through cross-examination but was not required to do so. Educational Objective: A court may grant a motion for judgment as a matter of law when the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. As a result, the court should deny the motion and allow a jury to decide the case if reasonable persons could reach different conclusions based on the evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"A court may grant a motion for judgment as a matter of law when the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. As a result, the court should deny the motion and allow a jury to decide the case if reasonable persons could reach different conclusions based on the evidence.","irac":{"issue":"A man brought a federal diversity action against his insurance company, alleging that the company had breached its duty under his insurance policy by refusing to pay for his medical expenses resulting from a mountain-biking accident. At the jury trial, the man presented evidence that he had paid all premiums on the insurance policy and that the policy covered personal-injury-related medical expenses arising from accidents. After he rested his case, the company presented evidence that a provision of the policy excluded payment for injury-related expenses resulting from an insured's \"unduly risky\" behavior. The company also presented a witness who testified that the accident had occurred in an area where posted signs warned bikers not to enter. The man did not cross-examine the witness. After resting its case, the company moved for judgment as a matter of law. Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"A court may grant a motion for judgment as a matter of law when the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. As a result, the court should deny the motion and allow a jury to decide the case if reasonable persons could reach different conclusions based on the evidence.","analysis":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court enter a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This means that the court should deny the motion for JMOL and allow the jury to decide the case if reasonable persons could reach different conclusions based on the evidence. Here, the issue is whether the man's actions constituted \"unduly risky\" behavior. The company presented evidence that the man went mountain biking in an area where posted signs warned bikers not to enter. But since it did not present evidence showing why these signs were posted, a reasonable jury could conclude that they were meant to keep bikers out for multiple reasons (eg, to protect wildlife). As a result, whether the man's behavior was unduly risky is a question of fact for the jury to resolve, and the company's motion for JMOL should be denied. (Choice A) A motion for JMOL may be filed any time after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present evidence supporting a claim or defense and before the case is submitted to the jury. Therefore, the company did not need to move for JMOL at the close of the man's case-in-chief.\n\n(Choice C) Although the man submitted no evidence to contradict the company's evidence, a reasonable jury could still conclude that his actions were not \"unduly risky.\" (Choice D) The man had no duty to address the \"unduly risky\" policy provision in his case-in-chief because the company raised it as a defense. The man could have rebutted the company's evidence through cross-examination but was not required to do so. Educational Objective: A court may grant a motion for judgment as a matter of law when the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. As a result, the court should deny the motion and allow a jury to decide the case if reasonable persons could reach different conclusions based on the evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[297],"sourceAnswerPages":[298,299],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子对其保险公司提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，主张公司拒绝支付其山地自行车事故所致医疗费用，违反保险单义务。在陪审团审判中，男子提出证据证明自己已支付全部保险费，且保险单承保事故所致人身伤害相关医疗费用。男子陈述案件完毕后，公司提出证据，证明保险单有一条款排除支付被保险人“过度冒险”行为所致的伤害费用。公司还传唤一名证人，证人称事故发生地区设有警示骑车者不得进入的标志。男子未交叉询问该证人。公司陈述案件完毕后，申请依法判决。法院是否应批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为依法判决动议必须首先在原告本案举证结束时提出。","B":"不应，因为男子的行为是否过度冒险是应由陪审团解决的事实问题。","C":"应当，因为公司关于男子过度冒险行为的未受反驳证据表明，任何合理陪审团均不能认定保险单承保其伤害。","D":"应当，因为男子未在本案举证中处理“过度冒险”保险条款，已放弃反驳公司证据的权利。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决动议（FRCP 50）：\n\n提出时间：\n• 可在非动议方陈述案件后、案件交付陪审团前提出；\n• 可在登记终局判决后 28 天内续行提出。\n\n程序要求：\n• 动议方必须指明所请求判决，以及使其有权获得判决的法律／事实；\n• 法院必须：\n  – 审视证据，并作出一切有利于非动议方的合理推论；\n  – 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；\n  – 不衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n批准标准：\n• 证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n依法判决（JMOL）动议请求法院为动议方登记判决，因为证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。这意味着，只要合理的人可能依据证据得出不同结论，法院就应驳回 JMOL 动议，让陪审团裁决案件。\n\n本题争点是男子的行为是否构成“过度冒险”。公司提出证据表明，男子在设有警示骑车者不得进入标志的地区骑山地自行车。但公司没有提出证据说明为何设置这些标志，合理陪审团可能认定标志旨在基于多种其他理由阻止骑车者进入，例如保护野生动物。因此，男子行为是否过度冒险是应由陪审团解决的事实问题，公司 JMOL 动议应被驳回。\n\n（选项 A）非动议方已有机会提出支持请求或抗辩的证据后、案件交付陪审团前，可以随时申请 JMOL。因此，公司无需在男子本案举证结束时提出 JMOL。\n\n（选项 C）男子虽未提出证据反驳公司证据，但合理陪审团仍可能认定其行为并非“过度冒险”。\n\n（选项 D）“过度冒险”保险条款由公司作为抗辩提出，男子在本案举证中没有义务处理。他可以通过交叉询问反驳公司证据，但并非必须这样做。\n\n学习目标：证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决时，法院可以批准依法判决动议。因此，只要合理的人可能依据证据得出不同结论，法院就应驳回动议，让陪审团裁决案件。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50（依法判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-099","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":99,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"The parents of a teenager from State A sued a construction company from State B for negligence on behalf of the teenager in a federal district court. The parents requested $100,000 in damages and demanded a jury trial. The construction company denied liability. At trial, the parents presented evidence that the construction company failed to sufficiently warn about the excavation pit on its worksite, these insufficient warnings caused the teenager to fall into the pit, and the teenager suffered severe injuries as a result. Immediately after the parents presented their case and before offering any evidence in its defense, the construction company moved for judgment as a matter of law. May the court consider the construction company's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the construction company has not presented any evidence in its defense.","B":"No, because the construction company may only move for judgment as a matter of law after the court enters a final judgment.","C":"Yes, because the construction company may move for judgment as a matter of law at any time before the case is submitted to the jury.","D":"Yes, because the parents had an opportunity to present their case."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court issue a judgment in the movant's favor because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in favor of the nonmovant. This motion may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury but only after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present evidence to establish a claim or defense (Choice C). That means that a defendant may move for JMOL after the plaintiff presents its case. Here, the parents (plaintiffs) had an opportunity to present evidence that the construction company (defendant) was negligent. Since the construction company moved for JMOL after the parents had that opportunity, the court may consider the motion. The court will then grant the motion if the parents failed to present legally sufficient evidence for the jury to reasonably conclude that the construction company is liable for negligence—eg, if the parents failed to present evidence establishing the construction company's duty of care. (Choice A) The construction company may move for JMOL even though it has presented no evidence in its defense. That is because the parents, as the plaintiffs, have the burden to prove their claim. (Choice B) A party may renew its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the court enters a final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict if that party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury and the court denied it. Educational objective: A motion for judgment as a matter of law requests that the court issue a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This motion may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury but only after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present its case. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law requests that the court issue a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This motion may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury but only after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present its case.","irac":{"issue":"The parents of a teenager from State A sued a construction company from State B for negligence on behalf of the teenager in a federal district court. The parents requested $100,000 in damages and demanded a jury trial. The construction company denied liability. At trial, the parents presented evidence that the construction company failed to sufficiently warn about the excavation pit on its worksite, these insufficient warnings caused the teenager to fall into the pit, and the teenager suffered severe injuries as a result. Immediately after the parents presented their case and before offering any evidence in its defense, the construction company moved for judgment as a matter of law. May the court consider the construction company's motion?","rule":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law requests that the court issue a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This motion may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury but only after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present its case.","analysis":"A motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a request that the court issue a judgment in the movant's favor because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in favor of the nonmovant. This motion may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury but only after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present evidence to establish a claim or defense (Choice C). That means that a defendant may move for JMOL after the plaintiff presents its case. Here, the parents (plaintiffs) had an opportunity to present evidence that the construction company (defendant) was negligent. Since the construction company moved for JMOL after the parents had that opportunity, the court may consider the motion. The court will then grant the motion if the parents failed to present legally sufficient evidence for the jury to reasonably conclude that the construction company is liable for negligence—eg, if the parents failed to present evidence establishing the construction company's duty of care. (Choice A) The construction company may move for JMOL even though it has presented no evidence in its defense. That is because the parents, as the plaintiffs, have the burden to prove their claim. (Choice B) A party may renew its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the court enters a final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict if that party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury and the court denied it. Educational objective: A motion for judgment as a matter of law requests that the court issue a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This motion may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury but only after the nonmovant has had the opportunity to present its case. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[300],"sourceAnswerPages":[301],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名少年的父母代表少年，在联邦地区法院以过失为由起诉 B 州一家建筑公司。父母请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿并要求陪审团审判。建筑公司否认责任。审判中，父母提出证据证明：建筑公司未对工地挖掘坑作出充分警示；不充分警示导致少年跌入坑内；少年因此受重伤。父母陈述案件后，建筑公司在提出任何辩护证据前立即申请依法判决。法院可以审理建筑公司的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为建筑公司尚未提出任何辩护证据。","B":"不可以，因为建筑公司只能在法院登记终局判决后申请依法判决。","C":"可以，因为建筑公司可在案件交付陪审团前随时申请依法判决。","D":"可以，因为父母已有机会陈述其案件。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决（JMOL）动议请求法院为动议方作出判决，因为证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。该动议可以在案件交付陪审团前提出，但只能在非动议方已有机会提出证据证明请求或抗辩后提出（选项 C）。这意味着，被告可以在原告陈述案件后申请 JMOL。\n\n本题中，父母（原告）已有机会提出证据证明建筑公司（被告）存在过失。建筑公司在父母获得该机会后申请 JMOL，所以法院可以审理该动议。之后，若父母未提出在法律上足以使陪审团合理认定建筑公司承担过失责任的证据，例如未提出证明建筑公司注意义务的证据，法院将批准动议。\n\n（选项 A）即使建筑公司尚未提出任何辩护证据，也可以申请 JMOL，因为父母作为原告负有证明其请求的责任。\n\n（选项 B）若当事人在案件交付陪审团前曾申请 JMOL，而法院予以驳回，则该当事人可在法院依陪审团裁决登记终局判决后 28 天内续行动议。\n\n学习目标：依法判决动议请求法院为动议方作出判决，因为证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。该动议可在案件交付陪审团前提出，但只能在非动议方已有机会陈述案件后提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50（依法判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-031"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-100","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":100,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A retailer brought a federal diversity action against a wholesaler, alleging breach of contract and fraudulent misrepresentation. After the parties presented their evidence at trial, the court instructed the jury on the law. Neither party filed a motion for judgment as a matter of law before the case went to the jury. The jury found for the retailer on both claims. After the court entered judgment on the verdict, the wholesaler moved for a new trial and for judgment as a matter of law, arguing that the evidence was insufficient to support the jury verdict on either claim. The court acknowledged that there had been problems with some of the evidence, but it denied the motions. The wholesaler appealed, challenging the sufficiency of the evidence. Should the appellate court consider the wholesaler's challenge?","choices":{"A":"No, because a determination of the sufficiency of the evidence is solely within the jury's province.","B":"No, because the wholesaler did not raise the sufficiency-of-the-evidence issue in a motion for judgment as a matter of law before the case went to the jury.","C":"Yes, because, as the trial court acknowledged, the wholesaler has strong arguments on the challenge.","D":"Yes, because the challenge was raised and ruled on by the trial court before the wholesaler filed the appeal."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A party may challenge on appeal the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict so long as that party made the appropriate motions during and after trial. To preserve a sufficiency-of-the-evidence issue for appeal, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 50 requires that a party do the following:\n• Before the case is submitted to the jury, move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL)—ie, a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor\n\n• Within 28 days after the entry of final judgment, file a renewed motion for JMOL— the party may also include an alternative or joint request for a new trial with this motion\n\nHere, the wholesaler moved for JMOL and a new trial after the court entered judgment on the jury's verdict. However, the wholesaler did not raise the sufficiency-of-the-evidence issue in a motion for JMOL before the case went to the jury as required by FRCP 50. As a result, the wholesaler did not preserve this issue for appeal, and the appellate court should not consider the wholesaler's challenge. (Choice A) A determination of the sufficiency of the evidence is not solely within the jury's province because the trial and appellate court may assess whether the evidence was legally sufficient to support the verdict. (Choices C & D) The trial court acknowledged that the wholesaler has strong arguments for challenging the sufficiency of the evidence and ruled on the wholesaler's posttrial motions.* However, the appellate court cannot consider that challenge because the wholesaler failed to move for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury. *Technically, the trial court erred in considering the merits of the wholesaler's motion for JMOL since the motion was not made before the case was submitted to the jury. Educational objective: A party may challenge on appeal the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006) (explaining the procedural requirements to challenge the sufficiency of evidence supporting a jury verdict).","rule":"A party may challenge on appeal the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of judgment.","irac":{"issue":"A retailer brought a federal diversity action against a wholesaler, alleging breach of contract and fraudulent misrepresentation. After the parties presented their evidence at trial, the court instructed the jury on the law. Neither party filed a motion for judgment as a matter of law before the case went to the jury. The jury found for the retailer on both claims. After the court entered judgment on the verdict, the wholesaler moved for a new trial and for judgment as a matter of law, arguing that the evidence was insufficient to support the jury verdict on either claim. The court acknowledged that there had been problems with some of the evidence, but it denied the motions. The wholesaler appealed, challenging the sufficiency of the evidence. Should the appellate court consider the wholesaler's challenge?","rule":"A party may challenge on appeal the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of judgment.","analysis":"A party may challenge on appeal the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict so long as that party made the appropriate motions during and after trial. To preserve a sufficiency-of-the-evidence issue for appeal, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 50 requires that a party do the following:\n• Before the case is submitted to the jury, move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL)—ie, a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor\n\n• Within 28 days after the entry of final judgment, file a renewed motion for JMOL— the party may also include an alternative or joint request for a new trial with this motion\n\nHere, the wholesaler moved for JMOL and a new trial after the court entered judgment on the jury's verdict. However, the wholesaler did not raise the sufficiency-of-the-evidence issue in a motion for JMOL before the case went to the jury as required by FRCP 50. As a result, the wholesaler did not preserve this issue for appeal, and the appellate court should not consider the wholesaler's challenge. (Choice A) A determination of the sufficiency of the evidence is not solely within the jury's province because the trial and appellate court may assess whether the evidence was legally sufficient to support the verdict. (Choices C & D) The trial court acknowledged that the wholesaler has strong arguments for challenging the sufficiency of the evidence and ruled on the wholesaler's posttrial motions.* However, the appellate court cannot consider that challenge because the wholesaler failed to move for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury. *Technically, the trial court erred in considering the merits of the wholesaler's motion for JMOL since the motion was not made before the case was submitted to the jury. Educational objective: A party may challenge on appeal the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006) (explaining the procedural requirements to challenge the sufficiency of evidence supporting a jury verdict).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006) (explaining the procedural requirements to challenge the sufficiency of evidence supporting a jury verdict).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[303],"sourceAnswerPages":[304,305],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家零售商对一家批发商提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，主张违约及欺诈性虚假陈述。双方在审判中提出证据后，法院向陪审团说明法律。案件交付陪审团前，双方均未申请依法判决。陪审团判零售商在两项请求上均胜诉。法院依据裁决登记判决后，批发商申请重新审判及依法判决，主张证据不足以支持陪审团对任何请求作出的裁决。法院承认部分证据存在问题，但驳回两项动议。批发商提起上诉，质疑证据充分性。上诉法院是否应审理批发商的质疑？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为确定证据充分性完全属于陪审团职权。","B":"不应，因为批发商未在案件交付陪审团前通过依法判决动议提出证据充分性问题。","C":"应当，因为审判法院已经承认批发商的质疑理由有力。","D":"应当，因为批发商上诉前，该质疑已在审判法院提出并获裁定。"},"explanationZh":"当事人在审判中和审判后提出适当动议，即可在上诉中质疑支持陪审团裁决的证据充分性。为保留证据充分性问题以供上诉，《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）50 要求当事人：\n• 在案件交付陪审团前申请依法判决（JMOL），即因证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决，而请求为动议方登记判决；\n• 在登记终局判决后 28 天内提出续行 JMOL 动议；当事人也可以在该动议中备选或一并请求重新审判。\n\n本题中，法院依据陪审团裁决登记判决后，批发商才申请 JMOL 和重新审判。但是，批发商没有依 FRCP 50 要求，在案件交付陪审团前通过 JMOL 动议提出证据充分性问题。因此，批发商未为上诉保留该问题，上诉法院不应审理其质疑。\n\n（选项 A）证据充分性的确定并非完全属于陪审团职权，因为审判法院和上诉法院均可判断证据在法律上是否足以支持裁决。\n\n（选项 C、D）审判法院承认批发商对证据充分性的质疑理由有力，也已裁定其审判后动议。* 但是，批发商未在案件交付陪审团前申请 JMOL，上诉法院不能审理该质疑。\n\n*严格而言，批发商未在案件交付陪审团前申请 JMOL，审判法院审理其 JMOL 动议实体属于错误。\n\n学习目标：当事人只有在以下情况下，方可在上诉中质疑支持陪审团裁决的证据充分性：(1) 在案件交付陪审团前申请依法判决（JMOL）；且 (2) 在登记判决后 28 天内续行申请 JMOL。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a)（依法判决动议）。\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006)（解释质疑支持陪审团裁决之证据充分性的程序要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-031"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-101","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":101,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"After being fired, a woman sued her former employer in federal court, alleging that her supervisor had discriminated against her on the basis of her sex. The woman's complaint included a lengthy description of what the supervisor had said and done over the years, quoting his telephone calls and emails to her and her own emails to the supervisor's manager asking for help. The employer moved for summary judgment, alleging that the woman was a pathological liar who had filed the action and included fictitious documents in revenge for having been fired. Because the woman's attorney was at a lengthy out-of-state trial when the summary judgment motion was filed, he failed to respond to it. The court therefore granted the motion in a one-line order and entered final judgment. The woman has appealed. Is the appellate court likely to uphold the trial court's ruling?","choices":{"A":"No, because the complaint's allegations were detailed and specific.","B":"No, because the employer moved for summary judgment on the basis that the woman was not credible, creating a factual dispute.","C":"Yes, because the woman's failure to respond to the summary judgment motion means that there was no sworn affidavit to support her allegations and supporting documents.","D":"Yes, because the woman's failure to respond to the summary judgment motion was a default giving sufficient basis to grant the motion."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for summary judgment is a request that the court enter judgment without a full trial. A trial court can only grant summary judgment if the movant shows that:\n• there is no genuine dispute of material fact (ie, a fact of consequence to the outcome of the case) and\n\n• the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor, so the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.\n\nTherefore, an appellate court will uphold a trial court's grant of summary judgment only if these elements are met. Here, the employer's motion for summary judgment alleged that the woman was a pathological liar who used fictitious documents to support her sex discrimination claim. By challenging the woman's credibility in this manner, the employer disputed all the factual allegations set forth in her complaint. Since this created a genuine dispute of material fact, the appellate court will likely reverse (ie, not uphold) the trial court's grant of summary judgment.\n\n(Choice A) The detail and specificity of the woman's allegations do not automatically prevent the court from entering summary judgment. Instead, the woman's allegations and the allegations raised in the employer's motion must show that there is a genuine dispute of material fact that bars summary judgment (as seen here). (Choices C & D) The woman was not required to respond to the summary judgment motion until the man showed that he was entitled to such relief. Had this initial burden been met, the burden would have shifted to the woman to show the existence of a genuine dispute of material fact through affidavits, declarations, or other materials containing admissible facts. Only then would her failure to respond constitute a default. Educational objective: Summary judgment should be granted if the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. A factual dispute exists if the movant challenges the credibility of the nonmovant's allegations. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986) (explaining the summary judgment process and the support required from the moving and nonmoving parties).","rule":"Summary judgment should be granted if the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. A factual dispute exists if the movant challenges the credibility of the nonmovant's allegations.","irac":{"issue":"After being fired, a woman sued her former employer in federal court, alleging that her supervisor had discriminated against her on the basis of her sex. The woman's complaint included a lengthy description of what the supervisor had said and done over the years, quoting his telephone calls and emails to her and her own emails to the supervisor's manager asking for help. The employer moved for summary judgment, alleging that the woman was a pathological liar who had filed the action and included fictitious documents in revenge for having been fired. Because the woman's attorney was at a lengthy out-of-state trial when the summary judgment motion was filed, he failed to respond to it. The court therefore granted the motion in a one-line order and entered final judgment. The woman has appealed. Is the appellate court likely to uphold the trial court's ruling?","rule":"Summary judgment should be granted if the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. A factual dispute exists if the movant challenges the credibility of the nonmovant's allegations.","analysis":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for summary judgment is a request that the court enter judgment without a full trial. A trial court can only grant summary judgment if the movant shows that:\n• there is no genuine dispute of material fact (ie, a fact of consequence to the outcome of the case) and\n\n• the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor, so the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.\n\nTherefore, an appellate court will uphold a trial court's grant of summary judgment only if these elements are met. Here, the employer's motion for summary judgment alleged that the woman was a pathological liar who used fictitious documents to support her sex discrimination claim. By challenging the woman's credibility in this manner, the employer disputed all the factual allegations set forth in her complaint. Since this created a genuine dispute of material fact, the appellate court will likely reverse (ie, not uphold) the trial court's grant of summary judgment.\n\n(Choice A) The detail and specificity of the woman's allegations do not automatically prevent the court from entering summary judgment. Instead, the woman's allegations and the allegations raised in the employer's motion must show that there is a genuine dispute of material fact that bars summary judgment (as seen here). (Choices C & D) The woman was not required to respond to the summary judgment motion until the man showed that he was entitled to such relief. Had this initial burden been met, the burden would have shifted to the woman to show the existence of a genuine dispute of material fact through affidavits, declarations, or other materials containing admissible facts. Only then would her failure to respond constitute a default. Educational objective: Summary judgment should be granted if the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. A factual dispute exists if the movant challenges the credibility of the nonmovant's allegations. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986) (explaining the summary judgment process and the support required from the moving and nonmoving parties).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986) (explaining the summary judgment process and the support required from the moving and nonmoving parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[306],"sourceAnswerPages":[307,308],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子被解雇后，在联邦法院起诉前雇主，主张主管基于性别歧视她。女子起诉状详细说明主管多年来的言行，引用主管与她的电话和电子邮件，以及她向主管的经理求助时发送的电子邮件。雇主申请简易判决，主张女子是病态说谎者，为报复被解雇而提起诉讼并加入虚假文件。简易判决动议提出时，女子律师正在外州进行一场长期审判，因而未作回应。法院遂以一行文字的命令批准动议并登记终局判决。女子已提起上诉。上诉法院可能维持审判法院的裁定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为起诉状指控详细且具体。","B":"不会，因为雇主以女子不可信为依据申请简易判决，形成事实争议。","C":"会，因为女子未回应简易判决动议，意味着没有宣誓书支持其指控和证明材料。","D":"会，因为女子未回应简易判决动议构成缺席，足以批准动议。"},"explanationZh":"简易判决动议（FRCP 56）：\n\n提出时间｜可在证据开示结束后 30 天前提出，或者按当地规则或法院规定的时间提出。\n\n证明责任标准｜动议方负有初步证明责任：\n• 可依靠诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料支持；\n• 初步责任满足后，责任转移至非动议方，后者必须选择：\n  – 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料，证明具体争议事实，不能仅依赖诉状；或\n  – 请求延期以进行额外证据开示，并以宣誓书或声明支持请求。\n\n批准标准｜不存在任何重大事实的真实争议，且动议方依法有权获得判决：\n• 必须以最有利于非动议方的方式审视证据；\n• 所有疑问均应作有利于非动议方的解决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n简易判决动议请求法院不经完整审判直接登记判决。只有动议方证明以下两点，审判法院才可批准简易判决：\n• 不存在重大事实——即对案件结果有影响的事实——的真实争议；并且\n• 证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决，所以动议方依法有权获得判决。\n\n因此，只有满足这些要件时，上诉法院才会维持审判法院批准简易判决的裁定。\n\n本题中，雇主的简易判决动议主张女子是病态说谎者，并用虚假文件支持其性别歧视请求。雇主以这种方式质疑女子的可信度，也就争议了她起诉状所列全部事实指控。这形成了重大事实真实争议，所以，上诉法院很可能撤销而非维持审判法院批准简易判决的裁定。\n\n（选项 A）女子指控的详细、具体本身并不会自动阻止法院登记简易判决。关键是女子指控与雇主动议中的主张是否表明存在阻止简易判决的重大事实真实争议，本题确实存在。\n\n（选项 C、D）在雇主证明自己有权获得简易判决前，女子没有义务回应动议。若雇主满足初步责任，责任才转移至女子，由她通过宣誓书、声明或其他包含可采事实的材料证明重大事实真实争议。只有到那时，她未回应才构成缺席。\n\n学习目标：动议方证明以下两点时，应批准简易判决：(1) 不存在任何重大事实的真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决。动议方质疑非动议方指控的可信度时，即存在事实争议。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56（简易判决）。\n• Celotex Corp. v. Catrett, 477 U.S. 317, 323–24 (1986)（解释简易判决程序以及动议方和非动议方所需支持材料）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-102","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":102,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court for breach of contract. After the trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her $1,000,000. The district court entered a final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict. The defendant then filed a notice of appeal, alleging that the district court abused its discretion by failing to exclude several pieces of evidence during trial. The day after the notice of appeal was filed, the district court discovered that the final judgment listed only a $1,000 award for the plaintiff. The appellate court has not docketed the appeal of the final judgment. How is the district court likely to proceed?","choices":{"A":"Correct the mistake in the judgment on its own initiative.","B":"Instruct the appellate court to correct the mistake in the judgment on appeal.","C":"Provide the parties with notice and an opportunity to be heard before correcting the mistake in the judgment.","D":"Request permission from the appellate court to correct the mistake in the judgment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Posttrial correction of mistakes\n\n(FRCP 60(a))\n\nType of mistake Definition Time of correction\n\nClerical Misstatement of court's intent • Before appeal docketed: on court's own initiative\n• Eg, court entered or by motion judgment for \"$10,000\"\n• After appeal docketed: instead of \"$100,000\" with appellate court's Oversight/omission Exclusion misrepresents court's leave intent\n\n• Eg, judgment failed to include required interest in award FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFRCP 60 allows a district court to correct a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission in a judgment, order, or other part of the record. The court may do so on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed—ie, added to the appellate court's schedule. The appellate court's leave (ie, permission) is only required after the appeal has been docketed. Here, a clerical mistake occurred since the jury's verdict awarded the plaintiff $1,000,000 but the final judgment awarded her only $1,000. Although the defendant had filed a notice of appeal from the judgment, the appeal has not yet been docketed. Therefore, the district court does not need to obtain the appellate court's permission and likely will correct the mistake on its own initiative (Choice D). (Choice B) A district court does not have the power to instruct an appellate court how to act. Instead, appellate courts have the power to review the decisions of district courts within their boundaries and issue instructions that the district court must follow when a case is remanded. (Choice C) A district court need not provide the parties with notice or an opportunity to be heard before it corrects a mistake in a judgment before an appeal is docketed. Instead, the court may do so on its own initiative. Educational objective:\n\nA district court may correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed. The district court need only obtain the appellate court's leave to correct the mistake after the appeal has been docketed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a) (correction of mistake in judgment or order).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2856 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a court may correct a mistake after an appeal has been taken).","rule":"A district court may correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed. The district court need only obtain the appellate court's leave to correct the mistake after the appeal has been docketed.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court for breach of contract. After the trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her $1,000,000. The district court entered a final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict. The defendant then filed a notice of appeal, alleging that the district court abused its discretion by failing to exclude several pieces of evidence during trial. The day after the notice of appeal was filed, the district court discovered that the final judgment listed only a $1,000 award for the plaintiff. The appellate court has not docketed the appeal of the final judgment. How is the district court likely to proceed?","rule":"A district court may correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed. The district court need only obtain the appellate court's leave to correct the mistake after the appeal has been docketed.","analysis":"Posttrial correction of mistakes\n\n(FRCP 60(a))\n\nType of mistake Definition Time of correction\n\nClerical Misstatement of court's intent • Before appeal docketed: on court's own initiative\n• Eg, court entered or by motion judgment for \"$10,000\"\n• After appeal docketed: instead of \"$100,000\" with appellate court's Oversight/omission Exclusion misrepresents court's leave intent\n\n• Eg, judgment failed to include required interest in award FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFRCP 60 allows a district court to correct a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission in a judgment, order, or other part of the record. The court may do so on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed—ie, added to the appellate court's schedule. The appellate court's leave (ie, permission) is only required after the appeal has been docketed. Here, a clerical mistake occurred since the jury's verdict awarded the plaintiff $1,000,000 but the final judgment awarded her only $1,000. Although the defendant had filed a notice of appeal from the judgment, the appeal has not yet been docketed. Therefore, the district court does not need to obtain the appellate court's permission and likely will correct the mistake on its own initiative (Choice D). (Choice B) A district court does not have the power to instruct an appellate court how to act. Instead, appellate courts have the power to review the decisions of district courts within their boundaries and issue instructions that the district court must follow when a case is remanded. (Choice C) A district court need not provide the parties with notice or an opportunity to be heard before it corrects a mistake in a judgment before an appeal is docketed. Instead, the court may do so on its own initiative. Educational objective:\n\nA district court may correct a mistake in a judgment, order, or other part of the record on its own initiative or pursuant to a party's motion before an appeal from the judgment or order is docketed. The district court need only obtain the appellate court's leave to correct the mistake after the appeal has been docketed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a) (correction of mistake in judgment or order).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2856 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a court may correct a mistake after an appeal has been taken).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a) (correction of mistake in judgment or order).\n\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2856 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a court may correct a mistake after an appeal has been taken).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[309],"sourceAnswerPages":[310,311],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院起诉被告违约。审判后，陪审团作出有利于原告的裁决，判给 1,000,000 美元。地区法院依陪审团裁决登记终局判决。被告随后提交上诉通知，主张地区法院在审判中未排除数项证据，构成滥用酌情权。提交上诉通知次日，地区法院发现终局判决只列明判给原告 1,000 美元。上诉法院尚未将终局判决的上诉列入审理日程。地区法院可能如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"依职权更正判决中的错误。","B":"指示上诉法院在上诉中更正判决错误。","C":"更正判决错误前，向当事人发出通知并给予陈述意见机会。","D":"请求上诉法院许可更正判决错误。"},"explanationZh":"审判后更正错误（FRCP 60(a)）：\n\n错误类型｜定义｜更正时间\n书记性错误｜错误陈述法院意图，例如法院本欲登记“100,000 美元”判决，却登记为“10,000 美元”｜上诉列入日程前：法院可依职权或根据动议更正；上诉列入日程后：须经上诉法院许可\n疏忽／遗漏｜遗漏导致法院意图被错误表达，例如判决未将依法必须判给的利息计入金额｜上诉列入日程前：法院可依职权或根据动议更正；上诉列入日程后：须经上诉法院许可\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\nFRCP 60 允许地区法院更正判决、命令或案卷其他部分中的书记性错误，或因疏忽、遗漏产生的错误。在对判决或命令的上诉列入上诉法院审理日程前，法院可以依职权或根据当事人动议更正。只有在上诉已经列入日程后，才需要上诉法院许可。\n\n本题发生书记性错误，因为陪审团裁决判给原告 1,000,000 美元，终局判决却只判给 1,000 美元。被告虽已提交判决上诉通知，但上诉尚未列入日程。因此，地区法院无需取得上诉法院许可，很可能依职权更正错误（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 B）地区法院无权指示上诉法院如何行动。相反，上诉法院有权审查其辖区内地区法院的决定，并在案件发回后发布地区法院必须遵循的指示。\n\n（选项 C）上诉列入日程前，地区法院更正判决错误时，无需向当事人发出通知或给予陈述意见机会，而可以依职权更正。\n\n学习目标：对判决、命令或案卷其他部分的上诉列入日程前，地区法院可以依职权或根据当事人动议更正其中错误。只有在上诉列入日程后，地区法院才须取得上诉法院许可。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(a)（更正判决或命令中的错误）。\n• 11 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2856 (3d ed. 2020)（解释提起上诉后法院如何更正错误）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-103","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":103,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A group of consumers sued a company in federal court for violating federal antitrust laws. Immediately after presenting evidence in its defense, the company moved for judgment as a matter of law. The judge denied the motion. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the consumers. The court expressed surprise at the verdict, noting the scarcity of evidence in favor of the consumers, but nevertheless entered a judgment consistent with the verdict. Twenty days later, the company renewed its motion for judgment as a matter of law, challenging the sufficiency of the evidence. The motion was denied. The company then filed a notice of appeal, challenging the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict. Is the appellate court likely to review the company's challenge?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company's motion for judgment as a matter of law at the close of its defense was untimely.","B":"No, because the jury has complete discretion to determine the sufficiency of the evidence presented by the consumers.","C":"Yes, because the company timely renewed its motion for judgment as a matter of law.","D":"Yes, because the jury verdict based on scarce evidence was clearly erroneous."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 50, a party can challenge the sufficiency of the evidence supporting a jury verdict on appeal if it:\n• moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and\n• filed a renewed motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment that could have included an alternative or joint request for a new trial.* Here, the company moved for JMOL after presenting its evidence and before the case was submitted to the jury, which the judge denied. The company then timely renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after final judgment was entered. This means that the company preserved its sufficiency-of-the-evidence challenge for appeal, and the appellate court is likely to review this challenge. *If the court grants a renewed motion for JMOL, it also must conditionally rule on any motion for a new trial by determining whether a new trial would be warranted if the court's judgment is later reversed. (Choice A) A defendant can move for JMOL any time after the plaintiff has presented its case but before the case is submitted to the jury. And since the defendant here moved for JMOL at the close of its defense and before the case was submitted to the jury, the motion was timely. (Choice B) The jury, as the trier of fact, has complete discretion to weigh the evidence presented at trial to determine if a party has met its burden of proof. However, the appellate court may still review the legal sufficiency of a jury's determinations. When doing so, the appellate court must give great deference to and uphold the jury's determinations if they are supported by substantial evidence (ie, more than a scintilla). (Choice D) Although the jury's verdict was based on scarce evidence, the clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review factual findings in a bench (not jury) trial. Educational objective: A party may challenge the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict on appeal if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006) (explaining the procedural requirements to challenge the sufficiency of evidence supporting a jury verdict).","rule":"A party may challenge the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict on appeal if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment.","irac":{"issue":"A group of consumers sued a company in federal court for violating federal antitrust laws. Immediately after presenting evidence in its defense, the company moved for judgment as a matter of law. The judge denied the motion. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the consumers. The court expressed surprise at the verdict, noting the scarcity of evidence in favor of the consumers, but nevertheless entered a judgment consistent with the verdict. Twenty days later, the company renewed its motion for judgment as a matter of law, challenging the sufficiency of the evidence. The motion was denied. The company then filed a notice of appeal, challenging the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict. Is the appellate court likely to review the company's challenge?","rule":"A party may challenge the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict on appeal if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 50, a party can challenge the sufficiency of the evidence supporting a jury verdict on appeal if it:\n• moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and\n• filed a renewed motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment that could have included an alternative or joint request for a new trial.* Here, the company moved for JMOL after presenting its evidence and before the case was submitted to the jury, which the judge denied. The company then timely renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after final judgment was entered. This means that the company preserved its sufficiency-of-the-evidence challenge for appeal, and the appellate court is likely to review this challenge. *If the court grants a renewed motion for JMOL, it also must conditionally rule on any motion for a new trial by determining whether a new trial would be warranted if the court's judgment is later reversed. (Choice A) A defendant can move for JMOL any time after the plaintiff has presented its case but before the case is submitted to the jury. And since the defendant here moved for JMOL at the close of its defense and before the case was submitted to the jury, the motion was timely. (Choice B) The jury, as the trier of fact, has complete discretion to weigh the evidence presented at trial to determine if a party has met its burden of proof. However, the appellate court may still review the legal sufficiency of a jury's determinations. When doing so, the appellate court must give great deference to and uphold the jury's determinations if they are supported by substantial evidence (ie, more than a scintilla). (Choice D) Although the jury's verdict was based on scarce evidence, the clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review factual findings in a bench (not jury) trial. Educational objective: A party may challenge the sufficiency of the evidence supporting the jury's verdict on appeal if that party (1) moved for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) before the case was submitted to the jury and (2) renewed its motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006) (explaining the procedural requirements to challenge the sufficiency of evidence supporting a jury verdict).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006) (explaining the procedural requirements to challenge the sufficiency of evidence supporting a jury verdict).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[312],"sourceAnswerPages":[313,314],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一群消费者在联邦法院起诉一家公司违反联邦反垄断法。公司陈述辩护证据完毕后立即申请依法判决，法官予以驳回。陪审团作出有利于消费者的裁决。法院表示对裁决感到意外，并指出有利于消费者的证据非常稀少，但仍依裁决登记判决。20 天后，公司续行申请依法判决，质疑证据充分性。该动议被驳回。公司随后提交上诉通知，质疑支持陪审团裁决的证据充分性。上诉法院可能审理公司的质疑吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为公司在辩护举证结束时申请依法判决已经逾期。","B":"不会，因为陪审团对判断消费者所提证据的充分性享有完全酌情权。","C":"会，因为公司及时续行申请依法判决。","D":"会，因为陪审团基于稀少证据作出的裁决属于明显错误。"},"explanationZh":"依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》50，当事人符合以下条件时，可以在上诉中质疑支持陪审团裁决的证据充分性：\n• 在案件交付陪审团前申请依法判决（JMOL）；并且\n• 在登记终局判决后 28 天内续行申请 JMOL；该动议可备选或一并请求重新审判。*\n\n本题中，公司在提出辩护证据后、案件交付陪审团前申请 JMOL，法官予以驳回。之后，公司在登记终局判决后 28 天内及时续行申请 JMOL。这意味着公司已为上诉保留其证据充分性质疑，上诉法院很可能进行审理。\n\n*法院批准续行 JMOL 动议时，还必须对任何重新审判动议作出附条件裁定，判断若法院判决后来被撤销，是否应当重新审判。\n\n（选项 A）原告陈述案件后、案件交付陪审团前，被告可随时申请 JMOL。本题被告在辩护举证结束时、案件交付陪审团前提出，属于及时。\n\n（选项 B）陪审团作为事实认定者，对权衡审判证据、判断一方是否满足证明责任享有完全酌情权。但是，上诉法院仍可审查陪审团认定在法律上的证据充分性。审查时，上诉法院必须高度尊重陪审团认定；只要有实质证据——即不止一丝证据——支持，就应维持。\n\n（选项 D）陪审团裁决虽仅由稀少证据支持，但明显错误审查标准适用于法官审判而非陪审团审判中的事实认定。\n\n学习目标：当事人只有在以下情况下，方可在上诉中质疑支持陪审团裁决的证据充分性：(1) 在案件交付陪审团前申请依法判决（JMOL）；且 (2) 在登记终局判决后 28 天内续行申请 JMOL。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50（依法判决）。\n• Unitherm Food Sys., Inc. v. Swift-Eckrich, Inc., 546 U.S. 394, 399–402 (2006)（解释质疑支持陪审团裁决之证据充分性的程序要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-031"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-104","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":104,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"An artist from State A filed a federal diversity suit in State B against an art collector from State B for conversion. The complaint alleges that the collector stole a painting from the artist 50 years ago and requests a jury trial on this issue. The statute of limitations for conversion, measured from the time the plaintiff learns that the defendant possesses the property at issue, is 25 years in State A and 10 years in State B. During discovery, the collector deposed the artist. At her deposition, the artist stated that she knew that the collector had purchased the painting 20 years ago at an auction, but she did not try to recover the painting at that time because she did not want the hassle of a court case to distract her from creating art. However, due to her advancing age, she now seeks to reclaim her painting. The collector immediately moved for summary judgment. The collector stipulated to all of the facts alleged by the artist at her deposition and attached a transcript of her deposition testimony to the motion. The artist did not respond. Is the federal court likely to grant the collector's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the collector failed to attach an affidavit to the motion.","B":"No, because the statute of limitations has not expired.","C":"Yes, because the artist did not respond to the motion.","D":"Yes, because the collector is entitled to judgment as a matter of law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for summary judgment is a request that the court enter judgment without a full trial. A court may grant summary judgment if the movant shows that:\n• there is no genuine dispute of material fact and\n\n• the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor, so the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.\n\nHere, there is no genuine issue of material fact because the collector stipulated to all of the facts alleged by the artist at her deposition. The artist testified that she learned that the collector purchased the painting 20 years ago. Since the federal court must apply the substantive law of the state in which it is located, her claim was filed after State B's 10-year statute of limitations had expired (Choice B). As a result, the collector is entitled to judgment as a matter of law, and the court should grant his motion for summary judgment. (Choice A) The collector did not need to attach an affidavit to his motion. That is because he can rely on pleadings, discovery materials, and other materials containing admissible facts to support his request for summary judgment.\n\n(Choice C) The artist is not required to respond to the motion since the collector has the burden to prove that he is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Educational objective: A court should grant summary judgment if the movant shows that there is no genuine issue of material fact and he/she is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (eg, because the statute of limitations has expired). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).","rule":"A court should grant summary judgment if the movant shows that there is no genuine issue of material fact and he/she is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (eg, because the statute of limitations has expired).","irac":{"issue":"An artist from State A filed a federal diversity suit in State B against an art collector from State B for conversion. The complaint alleges that the collector stole a painting from the artist 50 years ago and requests a jury trial on this issue. The statute of limitations for conversion, measured from the time the plaintiff learns that the defendant possesses the property at issue, is 25 years in State A and 10 years in State B. During discovery, the collector deposed the artist. At her deposition, the artist stated that she knew that the collector had purchased the painting 20 years ago at an auction, but she did not try to recover the painting at that time because she did not want the hassle of a court case to distract her from creating art. However, due to her advancing age, she now seeks to reclaim her painting. The collector immediately moved for summary judgment. The collector stipulated to all of the facts alleged by the artist at her deposition and attached a transcript of her deposition testimony to the motion. The artist did not respond. Is the federal court likely to grant the collector's motion?","rule":"A court should grant summary judgment if the movant shows that there is no genuine issue of material fact and he/she is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (eg, because the statute of limitations has expired).","analysis":"Motion for summary judgment\n\n(FRCP 56)\n\nTime to file • Motion may be filed until 30 days after close of discovery OR time set by local rule or court Burden of • Movant has initial burden to establish standard proof – may support by relying on pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts\n• If initial burden is met, burden shifts to nonmovant to either: – show specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible facts (cannot rely on pleadings) or – request postponement for additional discovery & support with affidavit or declaration Standard • No genuine dispute as to any material fact AND movant entitled to for grant judgment as a matter of law – evidence must be viewed in light most favorable to nonmovant – all doubts must be resolved in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for summary judgment is a request that the court enter judgment without a full trial. A court may grant summary judgment if the movant shows that:\n• there is no genuine dispute of material fact and\n\n• the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor, so the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.\n\nHere, there is no genuine issue of material fact because the collector stipulated to all of the facts alleged by the artist at her deposition. The artist testified that she learned that the collector purchased the painting 20 years ago. Since the federal court must apply the substantive law of the state in which it is located, her claim was filed after State B's 10-year statute of limitations had expired (Choice B). As a result, the collector is entitled to judgment as a matter of law, and the court should grant his motion for summary judgment. (Choice A) The collector did not need to attach an affidavit to his motion. That is because he can rely on pleadings, discovery materials, and other materials containing admissible facts to support his request for summary judgment.\n\n(Choice C) The artist is not required to respond to the motion since the collector has the burden to prove that he is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Educational objective: A court should grant summary judgment if the movant shows that there is no genuine issue of material fact and he/she is entitled to judgment as a matter of law (eg, because the statute of limitations has expired). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[315],"sourceAnswerPages":[316,317],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名艺术家在 B 州对 B 州一名艺术品收藏家提起联邦多元州籍侵占诉讼。起诉状主张收藏家 50 年前从艺术家处偷走一幅画，并请求就该问题进行陪审团审判。侵占诉讼时效自原告得知被告占有争议财产时起算；A 州为 25 年，B 州为 10 年。证据开示期间，收藏家对艺术家进行证言录取。艺术家在证言录取中称，她 20 年前已知道收藏家在一次拍卖中购得该画，但当时不想让法院案件的麻烦分散自己创作艺术的注意力，所以未尝试追回。如今因年事已高，她希望取回画作。收藏家立即申请简易判决，对艺术家在证言录取中主张的全部事实均予认可，并在动议中附具证言录取记录。艺术家未回应。联邦法院可能批准收藏家的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为收藏家未在动议中附具宣誓书。","B":"不会，因为诉讼时效尚未届满。","C":"会，因为艺术家没有回应动议。","D":"会，因为收藏家依法有权获得判决。"},"explanationZh":"简易判决动议（FRCP 56）：\n\n提出时间｜可在证据开示结束后 30 天前提出，或者按当地规则或法院规定的时间提出。\n\n证明责任标准｜动议方负有初步证明责任：\n• 可依靠诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料支持；\n• 初步责任满足后，责任转移至非动议方，后者必须选择：\n  – 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采事实的材料，证明具体争议事实，不能仅依赖诉状；或\n  – 请求延期以进行额外证据开示，并以宣誓书或声明支持请求。\n\n批准标准｜不存在任何重大事实的真实争议，且动议方依法有权获得判决：\n• 必须以最有利于非动议方的方式审视证据；\n• 所有疑问均应作有利于非动议方的解决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n简易判决动议请求法院不经完整审判直接登记判决。动议方证明以下两点时，法院可以批准简易判决：\n• 不存在重大事实的真实争议；并且\n• 证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决，所以动议方依法有权获得判决。\n\n本题不存在重大事实真实争议，因为收藏家认可艺术家在证言录取中主张的全部事实。艺术家作证称，自己 20 年前已经得知收藏家购买该画。联邦法院必须适用其所在地州的实体法，所以她在 B 州 10 年诉讼时效届满后才提出请求（选项 B）。因此，收藏家依法有权获得判决，法院应批准其简易判决动议。\n\n（选项 A）收藏家无需在动议中附具宣誓书，因为他可以依靠诉状、证据开示资料及其他包含可采事实的材料支持简易判决请求。\n\n（选项 C）收藏家负有证明自己依法有权获得判决的责任，所以艺术家并非必须回应动议。\n\n学习目标：动议方证明不存在重大事实真实争议且依法有权获得判决时，法院应批准简易判决，例如诉讼时效已经届满。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56（简易判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-105","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":105,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A basketball coach sued the university that employed her for violating a federal law that prohibits religious discrimination. The coach claimed that the university dismissed her due to her religious beliefs. The university denied this allegation and asserted that the coach was dismissed because she had a subpar coaching record. At trial, the coach testified that the university's athletic director frequently disparaged the coach's religious beliefs when the two met to discuss the coach's employment status. The athletic director had died before the trial commenced. Based on this evidence, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the coach and awarded her $3 million in damages. Six months after the district court entered judgment consistent with the verdict, the university discovered that the coach had secretly recorded her meetings with the athletic director but never disclosed the existence of the recordings when the university requested that she turn over all relevant evidence before trial. In these recordings, the athletic director never mentioned the coach's religious beliefs and focused entirely on her subpar coaching record. What is the best way for the university to seek relief from the judgment?","choices":{"A":"Move for a new trial based on the newly discovered evidence.","B":"Move for relief from the judgment based on the newly discovered evidence.","C":"Move to alter or amend the judgment based on the newly discovered evidence.","D":"Move to correct the mistaken judgment based on the newly discovered evidence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA party can seek extraordinary relief from a district court's final judgment in limited circumstances (see table above). This motion for relief may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of the final judgment when the motion asserts any of the following grounds for relief:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the nonmovant or the court.\n\n• The movant has discovered new evidence that existed at the time of the trial and could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial—ie, within 28 days of the final judgment.\n\n• The nonmovant engaged in misrepresentation, misconduct, or fraud.\n\nHere, the university requested that the coach turn over all relevant evidence before trial. The coach's recordings of her conversations with the deceased athletic director existed at that time, but she did not disclose them. As a result, the university did not learn of the recordings until six months after entry of the final judgment. The university should therefore move for relief from the judgment based on this newly discovered evidence before the one-year deadline expires.* *The university could also move to obtain relief on the ground that the coach engaged in misconduct by failing to disclose the existence of the recordings when all relevant evidence was requested before trial.\n\n(Choices A & C) A motion for a new trial and a motion to alter or amend a judgment must be filed within 28 days after a final judgment is entered. Since this deadline has passed, the university cannot obtain relief through these motions. (Choice D) A motion to correct a mistake is appropriate when a judgment contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission. However, the district court made no such mistakes when entering the final judgment. Educational objective: A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment when the motion is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","rule":"A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment when the motion is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.","irac":{"issue":"A basketball coach sued the university that employed her for violating a federal law that prohibits religious discrimination. The coach claimed that the university dismissed her due to her religious beliefs. The university denied this allegation and asserted that the coach was dismissed because she had a subpar coaching record. At trial, the coach testified that the university's athletic director frequently disparaged the coach's religious beliefs when the two met to discuss the coach's employment status. The athletic director had died before the trial commenced. Based on this evidence, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the coach and awarded her $3 million in damages. Six months after the district court entered judgment consistent with the verdict, the university discovered that the coach had secretly recorded her meetings with the athletic director but never disclosed the existence of the recordings when the university requested that she turn over all relevant evidence before trial. In these recordings, the athletic director never mentioned the coach's religious beliefs and focused entirely on her subpar coaching record. What is the best way for the university to seek relief from the judgment?","rule":"A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment when the motion is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct.","analysis":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA party can seek extraordinary relief from a district court's final judgment in limited circumstances (see table above). This motion for relief may generally be made within a reasonable time, but it must be made within one year from the entry of the final judgment when the motion asserts any of the following grounds for relief:\n• The judgment was due to mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the nonmovant or the court.\n\n• The movant has discovered new evidence that existed at the time of the trial and could not have reasonably been discovered in time to move for a new trial—ie, within 28 days of the final judgment.\n\n• The nonmovant engaged in misrepresentation, misconduct, or fraud.\n\nHere, the university requested that the coach turn over all relevant evidence before trial. The coach's recordings of her conversations with the deceased athletic director existed at that time, but she did not disclose them. As a result, the university did not learn of the recordings until six months after entry of the final judgment. The university should therefore move for relief from the judgment based on this newly discovered evidence before the one-year deadline expires.* *The university could also move to obtain relief on the ground that the coach engaged in misconduct by failing to disclose the existence of the recordings when all relevant evidence was requested before trial.\n\n(Choices A & C) A motion for a new trial and a motion to alter or amend a judgment must be filed within 28 days after a final judgment is entered. Since this deadline has passed, the university cannot obtain relief through these motions. (Choice D) A motion to correct a mistake is appropriate when a judgment contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission. However, the district court made no such mistakes when entering the final judgment. Educational objective: A party may obtain extraordinary relief from a final judgment within one year from the entry of a judgment when the motion is based on (1) mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect by the parties or court, (2) newly discovered evidence, or (3) the opposing party's fraud, misrepresentation, or misconduct. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[318],"sourceAnswerPages":[319,320],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名篮球教练起诉雇用她的大学违反禁止宗教歧视的联邦法律。教练主张大学因其宗教信仰而将她解雇。大学否认该指控，并主张教练因执教成绩不佳而被解雇。审判中，教练作证称，二人会面讨论其任职状况时，大学体育主管经常贬低她的宗教信仰。体育主管在审判开始前已经去世。陪审团依据该证据作出有利于教练的裁决，判给 300 万美元损害赔偿。地区法院依据裁决登记判决六个月后，大学发现教练曾秘密录下她与体育主管的会面，但在大学审判前请求其移交全部相关证据时，从未披露录音存在。录音中，体育主管从未提及教练的宗教信仰，而始终只讨论其不佳的执教成绩。大学寻求判决救济的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"以新发现证据为由申请重新审判。","B":"以新发现证据为由申请从判决获得救济。","C":"以新发现证据为由申请修改判决。","D":"以新发现证据为由申请更正错误判决。"},"explanationZh":"从终局判决获得救济（FRCP 60(b)）：\n\n救济理由｜提出时限\n错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽｜一年内\n新发现证据｜一年内\n欺诈、虚假陈述、不当行为｜一年内\n无效判决，例如缺乏管辖权｜合理时间内\n判决已履行、解除或撤销；判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或者继续适用将违反衡平原则｜合理时间内\n其他足以给予救济的理由（极少适用）｜合理时间内\n\n当事人可在有限情形下从地区法院终局判决获得特别救济（见上表）。该救济动议通常可以在合理时间内提出；但若主张以下任一理由，必须在登记终局判决后一年内提出：\n• 判决源于非动议方或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；\n• 动议方发现了审判时已存在，且无法合理地在申请重新审判的期限——终局判决后 28 天内——发现的新证据；\n• 非动议方实施虚假陈述、不当行为或欺诈。\n\n本题中，大学在审判前请求教练移交全部相关证据。教练与已故体育主管的谈话录音当时已经存在，但她没有披露。因此，大学直到登记终局判决六个月后才得知录音。大学应在一年期限届满前，以该新发现证据为由申请从判决获得救济。*\n\n*大学也可基于教练的不当行为请求救济，因为在审判前有人请求全部相关证据时，她未披露录音存在。\n\n（选项 A、C）重新审判动议和修改判决动议必须在登记终局判决后 28 天内提出。该期限已经届满，大学不能通过这些动议取得救济。\n\n（选项 D）判决包含书记性错误，或因疏忽、遗漏产生的错误时，适合更正错误动议。但地区法院登记终局判决时没有发生此类错误。\n\n学习目标：判决基于以下任一原因时，当事人可以在登记终局判决后一年内获得特别救济：(1) 当事人或法院的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；(2) 新发现证据；或 (3) 对方实施欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（从判决获得特别救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-106","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":106,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A purchaser filed a federal diversity action against a seller, alleging breach of contract. The seller answered the complaint and included as a separate defense an allegation that the purchaser had brought and lost a similar contract claim against a different seller three years earlier, and that this history represented a pattern of filing frivolous lawsuits. The purchaser believes that the earlier lawsuit was factually completely different from the current one and is therefore irrelevant. What is the purchaser's best response to the seller's answer?","choices":{"A":"File a reply that includes a denial of the separate defense.","B":"Move for sanctions against the seller for asserting a frivolous defense.","C":"Move to amend the complaint to add allegations about the differences between the lawsuits.","D":"Move to strike the separate defense as irrelevant."},"answer":"D","explanation":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(f), a party may move to strike an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter from an opposing party's pleading. A party must make this motion:\n• before responding to the pleading if a response is allowed (eg, when the defendant's answer contains a counterclaim or the court orders a reply) or\n\n• within 21 days after being served with the pleading if a response is not allowed.*\n\nHere, the purchaser believes that the seller's separate defense is irrelevant (ie, immaterial). Therefore, the purchaser's best response is to move to strike this defense from the answer. The motion must be filed within 21 days after the seller's answer was served because the answer does not contain a counterclaim and the court has not ordered a reply. *The court may also strike defenses and matters in pleadings at any time on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte). (Choice A) A reply is a response to an answer that can only be filed pursuant to a court order (not seen here). (Choice B) A court may impose sanctions on a party that asserts a frivolous claim or defense—ie, one that has no factual or legal basis and was asserted to harass, delay, or embarrass. Since there is no indication that the seller asserted the defense for any of these illegitimate purposes, the court is unlikely to grant a motion for sanctions.\n\n(Choice C) A plaintiff may move to amend the complaint to correct errors and omissions, allege new claims, or change the defendant. However, an amendment to respond to allegations in the defendant's answer is not allowed. Educational objective: A party can move to strike from a pleading an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter. A party must make this motion (1) before responding to the pleading or (2) within 21 days after being served with the pleading if a response is not allowed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f) (motion to strike).","rule":"A party can move to strike from a pleading an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter. A party must make this motion (1) before responding to the pleading or (2) within 21 days after being served with the pleading if a response is not allowed.","irac":{"issue":"A purchaser filed a federal diversity action against a seller, alleging breach of contract. The seller answered the complaint and included as a separate defense an allegation that the purchaser had brought and lost a similar contract claim against a different seller three years earlier, and that this history represented a pattern of filing frivolous lawsuits. The purchaser believes that the earlier lawsuit was factually completely different from the current one and is therefore irrelevant. What is the purchaser's best response to the seller's answer?","rule":"A party can move to strike from a pleading an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter. A party must make this motion (1) before responding to the pleading or (2) within 21 days after being served with the pleading if a response is not allowed.","analysis":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(f), a party may move to strike an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter from an opposing party's pleading. A party must make this motion:\n• before responding to the pleading if a response is allowed (eg, when the defendant's answer contains a counterclaim or the court orders a reply) or\n\n• within 21 days after being served with the pleading if a response is not allowed.*\n\nHere, the purchaser believes that the seller's separate defense is irrelevant (ie, immaterial). Therefore, the purchaser's best response is to move to strike this defense from the answer. The motion must be filed within 21 days after the seller's answer was served because the answer does not contain a counterclaim and the court has not ordered a reply. *The court may also strike defenses and matters in pleadings at any time on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte). (Choice A) A reply is a response to an answer that can only be filed pursuant to a court order (not seen here). (Choice B) A court may impose sanctions on a party that asserts a frivolous claim or defense—ie, one that has no factual or legal basis and was asserted to harass, delay, or embarrass. Since there is no indication that the seller asserted the defense for any of these illegitimate purposes, the court is unlikely to grant a motion for sanctions.\n\n(Choice C) A plaintiff may move to amend the complaint to correct errors and omissions, allege new claims, or change the defendant. However, an amendment to respond to allegations in the defendant's answer is not allowed. Educational objective: A party can move to strike from a pleading an insufficient defense or any redundant, scandalous, impertinent, or immaterial matter. A party must make this motion (1) before responding to the pleading or (2) within 21 days after being served with the pleading if a response is not allowed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f) (motion to strike).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f) (motion to strike).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[321],"sourceAnswerPages":[322,323],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名买方对一名卖方提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，主张违约。卖方在答辩状中另列一项抗辩，主张买方三年前曾对另一卖方提出类似合同请求并败诉，而该历史表明买方一贯提起无理诉讼。买方认为，先前诉讼在事实上与当前诉讼完全不同，因而无关。买方对卖方答辩状的最佳回应是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提交答复，否认该项单独抗辩。","B":"以卖方提出无理抗辩为由申请制裁。","C":"申请修改起诉状，增加关于两起诉讼之间差异的指控。","D":"申请删除无关的单独抗辩。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 12 动议：\n\n12(b)｜主张以下任一抗辩的动议：\n• 缺乏事项管辖权或对人管辖权；\n• 审判地不当；\n• 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分；\n• 未陈述可给予救济的请求；\n• 未合并必要当事人。\n\n12(c)｜诉状判决动议。\n12(e)｜诉状更明确陈述动议。\n12(f)｜删除诉状中不充分抗辩或不相关事项的动议。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》12(f)，当事人可以申请从对方诉状中删除不充分抗辩，或任何重复、诽谤性、不适当或不相关事项。当事人必须在以下时间提出：\n• 若允许回应诉状，例如被告答辩状包含反诉或法院命令提交答复，则应在回应前提出；或\n• 若不允许回应，则应在收到诉状送达后 21 天内提出。*\n\n本题中，买方认为卖方的单独抗辩无关，即不相关。因此，最佳回应是申请从答辩状中删除该抗辩。答辩状不包含反诉，法院也未命令提交答复，所以该动议必须在收到卖方答辩状送达后 21 天内提出。\n\n*法院也可以随时依职权删除诉状中的抗辩和事项。\n\n（选项 A）答复是对答辩状的回应，只有依据法院命令方可提交；本题没有这种命令。\n\n（选项 B）当事人提出没有事实或法律依据，且为骚扰、拖延或使人难堪而提出的无理请求或抗辩时，法院可以实施制裁。没有迹象表明卖方出于这些不正当目的提出抗辩，所以法院不太可能批准制裁动议。\n\n（选项 C）原告可以申请修改起诉状，以更正错误和遗漏、提出新请求或变更被告。但不得通过修改起诉状来回应被告答辩状中的指控。\n\n学习目标：当事人可以申请从诉状中删除不充分抗辩或任何重复、诽谤性、不适当或不相关事项。该动议必须：(1) 在回应诉状前提出；或 (2) 若不允许回应，在收到诉状送达后 21 天内提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f)（删除动议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-107","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":107,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A driver from State A and a bicyclist from State B were involved in a collision that caused the driver to incur $150,000 in medical expenses. The driver filed a federal diversity action in State B based on negligence. The summons and complaint were served at the bicyclist's home, where the process server handed the documents to the bicyclist's 16-year-old daughter. The daughter failed to share those documents with the bicyclist who, unbeknownst to the driver, was in a coma. No one else was informed about the lawsuit. After three months, no response had been filed so the clerk entered the bicyclist's default into the record. The court then entered a default judgment in favor of the driver. Ten months after the entry of default judgment, the bicyclist awoke from the coma and learned about the default judgment. The bicyclist immediately hired a lawyer who filed a motion to set aside the default judgment based on excusable neglect. An affidavit from an eyewitness to the accident, who stated that the bicyclist did not swerve into the driver's lane as the driver claimed, was attached to the motion. Is the court likely to grant the bicyclist's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because more than one year has passed since the driver filed his complaint.","B":"No, because the driver did not engage in misconduct.","C":"Yes, because service of process was improper.","D":"Yes, because the attached affidavit provides a meritorious defense."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA default judgment is a final judgment that is entered when the defendant has failed to timely serve an answer. A party can move for extraordinary relief from a default judgment (or any other final judgment) in limited circumstances. The motion must typically be filed within a reasonable time, but it must be filed within one year from the entry of judgment when asserting any of the following grounds for relief: 1. The judgment was due to the court's or party's mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect. 2. The movant discovered new evidence. 3. The nonmovant engaged in misrepresentation, misconduct, or fraud. A motion based on mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect will be granted if the movant also demonstrates a meritorious defense. Here, the bicyclist's failure to timely serve an answer was excusable because he was in a coma. And the affidavit attached to the bicyclist's motion provides a meritorious defense. That is because the eyewitness statement challenges the substance of the driver's claim (rather than merely raising a technical or procedural issue). Therefore, the court will likely grant the bicyclist's motion to set aside the default judgment. (Choice A) A motion for extraordinary relief based on excusable neglect must be made within one year after the judgment was entered—not after the complaint was filed.\n\n(Choice B) The driver did not engage in misconduct since he was unaware that the bicyclist was in a coma. However, the bicyclist may still obtain relief since his failure to file a timely answer was excusable. (Choice C) Service of process is proper if it is delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion. Most teenagers are of suitable age and discretion since they possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation. Therefore, service of process on the bicyclist's 16-year-old daughter was proper. Educational objective: A motion for extraordinary relief from a final judgment (eg, default judgment) based on mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect will be granted if the movant demonstrates a meritorious defense. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 663 (2019) (meritorious-defense requirement).","rule":"A motion for extraordinary relief from a final judgment (eg, default judgment) based on mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect will be granted if the movant demonstrates a meritorious defense.","irac":{"issue":"A driver from State A and a bicyclist from State B were involved in a collision that caused the driver to incur $150,000 in medical expenses. The driver filed a federal diversity action in State B based on negligence. The summons and complaint were served at the bicyclist's home, where the process server handed the documents to the bicyclist's 16-year-old daughter. The daughter failed to share those documents with the bicyclist who, unbeknownst to the driver, was in a coma. No one else was informed about the lawsuit. After three months, no response had been filed so the clerk entered the bicyclist's default into the record. The court then entered a default judgment in favor of the driver. Ten months after the entry of default judgment, the bicyclist awoke from the coma and learned about the default judgment. The bicyclist immediately hired a lawyer who filed a motion to set aside the default judgment based on excusable neglect. An affidavit from an eyewitness to the accident, who stated that the bicyclist did not swerve into the driver's lane as the driver claimed, was attached to the motion. Is the court likely to grant the bicyclist's motion?","rule":"A motion for extraordinary relief from a final judgment (eg, default judgment) based on mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect will be granted if the movant demonstrates a meritorious defense.","analysis":"Relief from final judgment\n\n(FRCP 60(b))\n\nGrounds for relief Time to file\n\n• Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect Within one year\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct\n• Void judgment (eg, lack of jurisdiction) Within reasonable\n• Judgment: time – satisfied, released, discharged – based on reversed or vacated judgment or – will violate equity if applied prospectively\n• Other reason justifying relief (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA default judgment is a final judgment that is entered when the defendant has failed to timely serve an answer. A party can move for extraordinary relief from a default judgment (or any other final judgment) in limited circumstances. The motion must typically be filed within a reasonable time, but it must be filed within one year from the entry of judgment when asserting any of the following grounds for relief: 1. The judgment was due to the court's or party's mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect. 2. The movant discovered new evidence. 3. The nonmovant engaged in misrepresentation, misconduct, or fraud. A motion based on mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect will be granted if the movant also demonstrates a meritorious defense. Here, the bicyclist's failure to timely serve an answer was excusable because he was in a coma. And the affidavit attached to the bicyclist's motion provides a meritorious defense. That is because the eyewitness statement challenges the substance of the driver's claim (rather than merely raising a technical or procedural issue). Therefore, the court will likely grant the bicyclist's motion to set aside the default judgment. (Choice A) A motion for extraordinary relief based on excusable neglect must be made within one year after the judgment was entered—not after the complaint was filed.\n\n(Choice B) The driver did not engage in misconduct since he was unaware that the bicyclist was in a coma. However, the bicyclist may still obtain relief since his failure to file a timely answer was excusable. (Choice C) Service of process is proper if it is delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion. Most teenagers are of suitable age and discretion since they possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation. Therefore, service of process on the bicyclist's 16-year-old daughter was proper. Educational objective: A motion for extraordinary relief from a final judgment (eg, default judgment) based on mistake, inadvertence, surprise, or excusable neglect will be granted if the movant demonstrates a meritorious defense. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 663 (2019) (meritorious-defense requirement).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c) (extraordinary relief from judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 663 (2019) (meritorious-defense requirement).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[324],"sourceAnswerPages":[325,326],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名驾驶员与 B 州一名骑自行车者发生碰撞，导致驾驶员产生 150,000 美元医疗费用。驾驶员在 B 州提起联邦多元州籍过失诉讼。传票和起诉状送至骑车者住宅，送达人把文件交给骑车者 16 岁的女儿。女儿没有把文件交给骑车者；驾驶员不知道骑车者当时处于昏迷状态。也无人向其他人告知诉讼。三个月后，仍无人提交回应，书记员遂在案卷中登记骑车者缺席。法院之后登记有利于驾驶员的缺席判决。缺席判决登记十个月后，骑车者从昏迷中醒来并得知判决。他立即聘请律师，以可原谅的疏忽为由申请撤销缺席判决。动议附具一名事故目击者的宣誓书，目击者称骑车者没有像驾驶员所说那样驶入驾驶员车道。法院可能批准骑车者的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为自驾驶员提交起诉状以来已经超过一年。","B":"不会，因为驾驶员没有实施不当行为。","C":"会，因为诉讼文书送达不当。","D":"会，因为所附宣誓书提出了有实质理由的抗辩。"},"explanationZh":"从终局判决获得救济（FRCP 60(b)）：\n\n救济理由｜提出时限\n错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽｜一年内\n新发现证据｜一年内\n欺诈、虚假陈述、不当行为｜一年内\n无效判决，例如缺乏管辖权｜合理时间内\n判决已履行、解除或撤销；判决基于后来被撤销或废止的判决；或者继续适用将违反衡平原则｜合理时间内\n其他足以给予救济的理由（极少适用）｜合理时间内\n\n缺席判决是被告未及时送达答辩状时登记的终局判决。当事人可在有限情形下申请从缺席判决或其他终局判决获得特别救济。动议通常必须在合理时间内提出；但若主张以下任一理由，必须在登记判决后一年内提出：\n1. 判决源于法院或当事人的错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽；\n2. 动议方发现新证据；\n3. 非动议方实施虚假陈述、不当行为或欺诈。\n\n基于错误、无意、意外或可原谅疏忽的动议，在动议方同时证明有实质理由的抗辩时，应获批准。\n\n本题中，骑车者处于昏迷状态，未及时送达答辩状可以原谅。骑车者动议所附宣誓书还提出有实质理由的抗辩，因为目击者陈述质疑驾驶员请求的实体内容，而非仅提出技术或程序问题。因此，法院很可能批准骑车者撤销缺席判决的动议。\n\n（选项 A）基于可原谅疏忽的特别救济动议必须在登记判决后一年内，而不是提交起诉状后一年内提出。\n\n（选项 B）驾驶员不知道骑车者昏迷，故没有实施不当行为。但骑车者未及时提交答辩状可以原谅，仍可获得救济。\n\n（选项 C）诉讼文书送至被告住所，并留给居住于该处、年龄和判断能力适当的人时，送达即属适当。多数青少年具有理解该情形所需的有限能力，故年龄和判断能力适当。因此，向骑车者 16 岁女儿送达适当。\n\n学习目标：基于错误、无意、意外或可原谅的疏忽，申请从终局判决（例如缺席判决）获得特别救济时，动议方证明有实质理由的抗辩，动议应获批准。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b)–(c)（从判决获得特别救济）。\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 663 (2019)（有实质理由抗辩要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-108","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":108,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A state opened a new park near a residential neighborhood. One of the residents sued the state in federal district court, claiming that the state took some of his property for the new park without providing him just compensation. At trial, the resident entered an old, unrecorded document into evidence that supported his claim that he owned the disputed property. In its defense, the state entered hundreds of documents into evidence that showed that the disputed property was state-owned land. At the end of trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the state, and the court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The next day, the resident hired a professional researcher to meticulously search the state and county records relating to the property. The researcher discovered a misfiled document in the county records that conclusively showed that the resident, not the state, owned the disputed property. This document was not entered into evidence at trial. The resident immediately moved for a new trial based on this newly discovered evidence. Is the court likely to grant the resident's motion for a new trial?","choices":{"A":"No, because the resident could have discovered the document before the end of trial had he exercised due diligence.","B":"No, because the resident must present the document on appeal.","C":"Yes, because the document is material to the resident's case.","D":"Yes, because the document will likely produce a different result at a new trial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 59 allows a party to move for a new trial after the entry of a final judgment on certain grounds, including when new evidence is discovered after trial. A court will grant a new trial based on newly discovered evidence if the movant shows that:\n• the evidence was unknown to the movant at the time of trial\n• the movant used due diligence to discover the evidence before the end of trial\n• the evidence is material—not cumulative or impeaching—and\n• a new trial considering the evidence will likely result in a different outcome. Here, the researcher discovered a document after trial that had not been entered into evidence. This document conclusively shows that the resident owns the disputed property, so it is material to his case and would likely lead to a different result at a new trial. However, the resident did not use due diligence to discover the document because he could have hired the researcher or conducted his own search before the end of trial. As a result, the court will likely deny the resident's motion for a new trial on this ground (Choices C & D). (Choice B) When an appellate court reviews a decision by a lower court, it is limited to the facts and evidence contained in the record. Therefore, the resident cannot present the document for the first time on appeal. Educational objective: A court will grant a motion for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence if the movant shows that (1) the evidence was unknown to the movant at trial, (2) due diligence was used to discover the evidence before trial ended, (3) the evidence is material, and (4) a new trial with the evidence will likely have a different outcome. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(a) (new trial).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 291 (2019) (explaining the requirements for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence).","rule":"A court will grant a motion for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence if the movant shows that (1) the evidence was unknown to the movant at trial, (2) due diligence was used to discover the evidence before trial ended, (3) the evidence is material, and (4) a new trial with the evidence will likely have a different outcome.","irac":{"issue":"A state opened a new park near a residential neighborhood. One of the residents sued the state in federal district court, claiming that the state took some of his property for the new park without providing him just compensation. At trial, the resident entered an old, unrecorded document into evidence that supported his claim that he owned the disputed property. In its defense, the state entered hundreds of documents into evidence that showed that the disputed property was state-owned land. At the end of trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the state, and the court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The next day, the resident hired a professional researcher to meticulously search the state and county records relating to the property. The researcher discovered a misfiled document in the county records that conclusively showed that the resident, not the state, owned the disputed property. This document was not entered into evidence at trial. The resident immediately moved for a new trial based on this newly discovered evidence. Is the court likely to grant the resident's motion for a new trial?","rule":"A court will grant a motion for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence if the movant shows that (1) the evidence was unknown to the movant at trial, (2) due diligence was used to discover the evidence before trial ended, (3) the evidence is material, and (4) a new trial with the evidence will likely have a different outcome.","analysis":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 59 allows a party to move for a new trial after the entry of a final judgment on certain grounds, including when new evidence is discovered after trial. A court will grant a new trial based on newly discovered evidence if the movant shows that:\n• the evidence was unknown to the movant at the time of trial\n• the movant used due diligence to discover the evidence before the end of trial\n• the evidence is material—not cumulative or impeaching—and\n• a new trial considering the evidence will likely result in a different outcome. Here, the researcher discovered a document after trial that had not been entered into evidence. This document conclusively shows that the resident owns the disputed property, so it is material to his case and would likely lead to a different result at a new trial. However, the resident did not use due diligence to discover the document because he could have hired the researcher or conducted his own search before the end of trial. As a result, the court will likely deny the resident's motion for a new trial on this ground (Choices C & D). (Choice B) When an appellate court reviews a decision by a lower court, it is limited to the facts and evidence contained in the record. Therefore, the resident cannot present the document for the first time on appeal. Educational objective: A court will grant a motion for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence if the movant shows that (1) the evidence was unknown to the movant at trial, (2) due diligence was used to discover the evidence before trial ended, (3) the evidence is material, and (4) a new trial with the evidence will likely have a different outcome. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(a) (new trial).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 291 (2019) (explaining the requirements for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(a) (new trial).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 291 (2019) (explaining the requirements for a new trial based on newly discovered evidence).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[327],"sourceAnswerPages":[328,329],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某州在住宅区附近开放一座新公园。一名居民在联邦地区法院起诉该州，主张州政府未给予公正补偿即为新公园征用其部分财产。审判中，居民提出一份支持其拥有争议财产的未登记旧文件作为证据。州政府则提出数百份文件作为证据，证明争议财产是州有土地。审判结束时，陪审团作出有利于州政府的裁决，法院依裁决登记终局判决。次日，居民聘请一名专业研究员，细致搜查与该财产有关的州和县记录。研究员在县记录中发现一份归档错误的文件，确凿证明争议财产属于居民而非州政府。该文件未在审判中作为证据提出。居民立即以该新发现证据为由申请重新审判。法院可能批准居民的重新审判动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为居民若尽到应有勤勉，本可在审判结束前发现该文件。","B":"不会，因为居民必须在上诉中提出该文件。","C":"会，因为该文件对居民案件具有实质重要性。","D":"会，因为该文件很可能在重新审判中产生不同结果。"},"explanationZh":"重新审判的理由：\n• 造成不利影响的审判错误，例如错误的证据裁定；\n• 法官、律师、当事人或陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为；\n• 裁决不受证据明显权重支持；\n• 裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；\n• 损害赔偿过高或不足；\n• 新发现证据。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》59 允许当事人在登记终局判决后基于特定理由申请重新审判，包括审判后发现新证据。若动议方证明以下各点，法院将以新发现证据为由批准重新审判：\n• 审判时动议方不知道该证据；\n• 动议方在审判结束前曾尽到应有勤勉寻找证据；\n• 证据具有实质重要性，而不只是重复或用于弹劾；并且\n• 在重新审判中考虑该证据很可能产生不同结果。\n\n本题中，研究员在审判后发现一份未曾作为证据提出的文件。该文件确凿证明居民拥有争议财产，所以对其案件具有实质重要性，并很可能在重新审判中产生不同结果。但是，居民没有尽到应有勤勉寻找该文件，因为他本可在审判结束前聘请研究员或自行搜索。因此，法院很可能以此为由驳回居民的重新审判动议（选项 C、D）。\n\n（选项 B）上诉法院审查下级法院决定时，只能考虑案卷中已有的事实和证据。因此，居民不能在上诉中首次提出该文件。\n\n学习目标：若动议方证明以下各点，法院会基于新发现证据批准重新审判：(1) 审判时动议方不知道证据；(2) 审判结束前曾尽到应有勤勉寻找证据；(3) 证据具有实质重要性；且 (4) 考虑该证据后重新审判很可能产生不同结果。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(a)（重新审判）。\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 291 (2019)（解释基于新发现证据重新审判的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-109","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":109,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff domiciled in State A has brought a federal diversity action in State A against a defendant domiciled in State B, seeking damages for injuries the defendant allegedly caused the plaintiff in State B. The defendant has never been to State A and has no connections there. The defendant's attorney knows that the defendant was properly served but does not believe that the State A court has personal jurisdiction over her. What is the best way for the attorney to raise the argument that the court lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant?","choices":{"A":"Move for discovery on the issue of personal jurisdiction.","B":"Move for judgment on the pleadings, seeking dismissal of the action for lack of personal jurisdiction.","C":"Move for sanctions against the plaintiff and his attorney for filing the action in a court that has no personal jurisdiction over the defendant.","D":"Move to dismiss the action for lack of personal jurisdiction based on the complaint allegations and an affidavit from the defendant about her lack of connection to State A."},"answer":"D","explanation":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe defendant's attorney believes that the State A federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant. A defendant* can assert a lack of personal jurisdiction in an FRCP 12(b) pre-answer motion to dismiss or an answer (whichever occurs first). A pre-answer motion can use the allegations in the pleadings and/or an attached affidavit that shows why personal jurisdiction does not exist—eg, that the defendant has no connection to the forum state. Therefore, this motion is the best way for the attorney to challenge the court's personal jurisdiction. *Personal jurisdiction is always focused on the defendant because the plaintiff consents to personal jurisdiction by filing the suit in that court. (Choice A) Lack of personal jurisdiction must be raised in a pre-answer motion or answer (whichever occurs first); otherwise this challenge is waived. Since a motion for discovery is not a pre-answer motion, it is not an effective means to challenge personal jurisdiction. (Choice B) A party may move for judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings have closed—ie, once the plaintiff's complaint and the defendant's answer have been served. Since a personal-jurisdiction challenge must be raised before the pleadings have closed, this motion is improper. (Choice C) Sanctions may be imposed against a party, attorney, or law firm for filing the action in a court with no personal jurisdiction over the defendant. However, the defendant must first move to dismiss the action and show that the court lacks personal jurisdiction. Educational objective:\n\nA defendant can raise a lack of personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion to dismiss or an answer (whichever occurs first). The motion can be supported by the allegations in the pleadings or an attached affidavit. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b) (pre-answer motions).","rule":"A defendant can raise a lack of personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion to dismiss or an answer (whichever occurs first). The motion can be supported by the allegations in the pleadings or an attached affidavit. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff domiciled in State A has brought a federal diversity action in State A against a defendant domiciled in State B, seeking damages for injuries the defendant allegedly caused the plaintiff in State B. The defendant has never been to State A and has no connections there. The defendant's attorney knows that the defendant was properly served but does not believe that the State A court has personal jurisdiction over her. What is the best way for the attorney to raise the argument that the court lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant?","rule":"A defendant can raise a lack of personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion to dismiss or an answer (whichever occurs first). The motion can be supported by the allegations in the pleadings or an attached affidavit. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"FRCP 12 motions\n\n12(b) Motion asserting any of the following defenses:\n\n• Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted\n• Failure to join required party 12(c) Motion for judgment on the pleadings\n\n12(e) Motion for more definite statement in pleading\n\n12(f) Motion to strike insufficient defense or immaterial matter from pleading\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe defendant's attorney believes that the State A federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant. A defendant* can assert a lack of personal jurisdiction in an FRCP 12(b) pre-answer motion to dismiss or an answer (whichever occurs first). A pre-answer motion can use the allegations in the pleadings and/or an attached affidavit that shows why personal jurisdiction does not exist—eg, that the defendant has no connection to the forum state. Therefore, this motion is the best way for the attorney to challenge the court's personal jurisdiction. *Personal jurisdiction is always focused on the defendant because the plaintiff consents to personal jurisdiction by filing the suit in that court. (Choice A) Lack of personal jurisdiction must be raised in a pre-answer motion or answer (whichever occurs first); otherwise this challenge is waived. Since a motion for discovery is not a pre-answer motion, it is not an effective means to challenge personal jurisdiction. (Choice B) A party may move for judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings have closed—ie, once the plaintiff's complaint and the defendant's answer have been served. Since a personal-jurisdiction challenge must be raised before the pleadings have closed, this motion is improper. (Choice C) Sanctions may be imposed against a party, attorney, or law firm for filing the action in a court with no personal jurisdiction over the defendant. However, the defendant must first move to dismiss the action and show that the court lacks personal jurisdiction. Educational objective:\n\nA defendant can raise a lack of personal jurisdiction in a pre-answer motion to dismiss or an answer (whichever occurs first). The motion can be supported by the allegations in the pleadings or an attached affidavit. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b) (pre-answer motions).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b) (pre-answer motions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[330],"sourceAnswerPages":[331,332],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名以 A 州为住所的原告在 A 州对一名以 B 州为住所的被告提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，请求赔偿被告据称在 B 州给原告造成的伤害。被告从未去过 A 州，与该州也没有联系。被告律师知道被告已获适当送达，但认为 A 州法院对她缺乏对人管辖权。律师提出法院对被告缺乏对人管辖权这一主张的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请就对人管辖权问题进行证据开示。","B":"申请诉状判决，请求以缺乏对人管辖权为由驳回诉讼。","C":"以原告及其律师在对被告没有对人管辖权的法院提起诉讼为由，申请对其制裁。","D":"根据起诉状指控及被告关于其与 A 州缺乏联系的宣誓书，以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 12 动议：\n\n12(b)｜主张以下任一抗辩的动议：\n• 缺乏事项管辖权或对人管辖权；\n• 审判地不当；\n• 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分；\n• 未陈述可给予救济的请求；\n• 未合并必要当事人。\n\n12(c)｜诉状判决动议。\n12(e)｜诉状更明确陈述动议。\n12(f)｜删除诉状中不充分抗辩或不相关事项的动议。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n被告律师认为 A 州联邦法院对被告缺乏对人管辖权。被告*可以在 FRCP 12(b) 答辩前驳回动议或答辩状中——以先发生者为准——主张缺乏对人管辖权。答辩前动议可以使用诉状中的指控和／或所附宣誓书，说明为何不存在对人管辖权，例如被告与法院州没有联系。因此，该动议是律师质疑法院对人管辖权的最佳方式。\n\n*对人管辖权分析始终聚焦被告，因为原告在该法院提起诉讼即同意其对人管辖权。\n\n（选项 A）缺乏对人管辖权必须在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出，否则即被放弃。证据开示动议不属于答辩前动议，不能有效质疑对人管辖权。\n\n（选项 B）诉状提交完毕——即原告起诉状和被告答辩状均已送达——后，当事人可以申请诉状判决。对人管辖权异议必须在诉状提交完毕前提出，所以该动议不适当。\n\n（选项 C）当事人、律师或律师事务所在对被告没有对人管辖权的法院提起诉讼时，可能受到制裁。但是，被告必须先申请驳回并证明法院缺乏对人管辖权。\n\n学习目标：被告可以在答辩前驳回动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出缺乏对人管辖权。该动议可由诉状指控或所附宣誓书支持。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(b)（答辩前动议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-110","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":110,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A railroad worker's widow brought a wrongful death action in federal court against the railroad, claiming that its negligence had caused her husband's death. At trial, the widow offered the testimony of a coworker of the husband. The coworker testified that he had seen the rail car on which the husband was riding slow down and the cars behind it gain speed. The coworker also stated that he later heard a loud crash, but did not turn around to look because loud noises were common in the yard. Three other railroad employees testified that no collision had occurred. At the close of the evidence, the railroad moved for judgment as a matter of law, which was denied, and the case was submitted to the jury. The jury returned a verdict for the widow. The railroad has made a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law. What standard should the court apply to determine how to rule on the motion?","choices":{"A":"Whether the evidence revealed a genuine dispute of material fact supporting the widow's claim.","B":"Whether the verdict is against the weight of the evidence.","C":"Whether the widow presented a scintilla of evidence to support the verdict.","D":"Whether there is substantial evidence in the record to support the verdict, resolving all disputed issues in the widow's favor."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury. If denied, a renewed motion for JMOL can be filed after trial—as the railroad did here. The standard the court will apply is whether the evidence is legally sufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This means that the motion will be denied if there is substantial evidence in the record to support a verdict for the nonmovant. When making this determination, the court must view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant. Therefore, all disputed issues must be resolved in the widow's favor. (Choice A) Whether the evidence revealed a genuine issue of material fact would be pertinent to a motion for summary judgment, which must be made before trial. (Choice B) A party may move for a new trial on several grounds, one of which is that the verdict is against the clear weight of the evidence. (Choice C) Historically, a court would deny a motion for JMOL if a scintilla (ie, very small amount) of evidence supported a verdict for the nonmovant. Today, all federal courts and most state courts require that the verdict be supported by substantial evidence.\n\nEducational objective: The standard applied by a court in deciding a motion or renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law is whether there is legally sufficient (ie, substantial) evidence in the record to support a verdict for the nonmovant. The court must view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the nonmovant's favor. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 319 (explaining the standard for judgment as a matter of law).","rule":"The standard applied by a court in deciding a motion or renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law is whether there is legally sufficient (ie, substantial) evidence in the record to support a verdict for the nonmovant. The court must view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the nonmovant's favor.","irac":{"issue":"A railroad worker's widow brought a wrongful death action in federal court against the railroad, claiming that its negligence had caused her husband's death. At trial, the widow offered the testimony of a coworker of the husband. The coworker testified that he had seen the rail car on which the husband was riding slow down and the cars behind it gain speed. The coworker also stated that he later heard a loud crash, but did not turn around to look because loud noises were common in the yard. Three other railroad employees testified that no collision had occurred. At the close of the evidence, the railroad moved for judgment as a matter of law, which was denied, and the case was submitted to the jury. The jury returned a verdict for the widow. The railroad has made a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law. What standard should the court apply to determine how to rule on the motion?","rule":"The standard applied by a court in deciding a motion or renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law is whether there is legally sufficient (ie, substantial) evidence in the record to support a verdict for the nonmovant. The court must view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the nonmovant's favor.","analysis":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) may be filed before the case is submitted to the jury. If denied, a renewed motion for JMOL can be filed after trial—as the railroad did here. The standard the court will apply is whether the evidence is legally sufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. This means that the motion will be denied if there is substantial evidence in the record to support a verdict for the nonmovant. When making this determination, the court must view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant. Therefore, all disputed issues must be resolved in the widow's favor. (Choice A) Whether the evidence revealed a genuine issue of material fact would be pertinent to a motion for summary judgment, which must be made before trial. (Choice B) A party may move for a new trial on several grounds, one of which is that the verdict is against the clear weight of the evidence. (Choice C) Historically, a court would deny a motion for JMOL if a scintilla (ie, very small amount) of evidence supported a verdict for the nonmovant. Today, all federal courts and most state courts require that the verdict be supported by substantial evidence.\n\nEducational objective: The standard applied by a court in deciding a motion or renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law is whether there is legally sufficient (ie, substantial) evidence in the record to support a verdict for the nonmovant. The court must view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the nonmovant's favor. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 319 (explaining the standard for judgment as a matter of law).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50 (judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 319 (explaining the standard for judgment as a matter of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[333],"sourceAnswerPages":[334,335],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名铁路工人的遗孀在联邦法院对铁路公司提起非正常死亡诉讼，主张铁路公司的过失导致丈夫死亡。审判中，遗孀提出丈夫一名同事的证言。同事称，他看见丈夫所乘轨道车减速，而后方车辆加速；之后听到一声巨响，但因站场内巨响常见而没有回头查看。另有三名铁路雇员作证称没有发生碰撞。举证结束时，铁路公司申请依法判决，法院予以驳回并将案件交付陪审团。陪审团作出有利于遗孀的裁决。铁路公司现已续行申请依法判决。法院应采用什么标准裁定该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"证据是否揭示支持遗孀请求的重大事实真实争议。","B":"裁决是否违背证据权重。","C":"遗孀是否提出一丝支持裁决的证据。","D":"案卷中是否有实质证据支持裁决，并将所有争议问题作有利于遗孀的解决。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决动议（FRCP 50）：\n\n提出时间：\n• 可在非动议方陈述案件后、案件交付陪审团前提出；\n• 可在登记终局判决后 28 天内续行提出。\n\n程序要求：\n• 动议方必须指明所请求判决，以及使其有权获得判决的法律／事实；\n• 法院必须：\n  – 审视证据，并作出一切有利于非动议方的合理推论；\n  – 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；\n  – 不衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n批准标准：\n• 证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n依法判决（JMOL）动议可以在案件交付陪审团前提出。若被驳回，可以在审判后续行申请，本题铁路公司正是如此。法院适用的标准是：证据在法律上是否足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。这意味着，只要案卷中有实质证据支持非动议方胜诉，动议即应被驳回。\n\n作出该判断时，法院必须以最有利于非动议方的方式审视证据，并作出所有合理推论。因此，所有争议问题均须作有利于遗孀的解决。\n\n（选项 A）证据是否揭示重大事实真实争议，是审判前必须提出的简易判决动议所适用的问题。\n\n（选项 B）当事人可以基于多种理由申请重新审判，其中一项是裁决违背证据的明显权重。\n\n（选项 C）历史上，只要一丝——即极少量——证据支持非动议方胜诉，法院即驳回 JMOL 动议。如今，所有联邦法院和多数州法院都要求裁决获得实质证据支持。\n\n学习目标：法院裁定最初或续行依法判决动议时，标准是案卷中是否有法律上充分的实质证据支持非动议方胜诉。法院必须以有利于非动议方的方式审视证据，并作出所有合理推论。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50（依法判决）。\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 319（解释依法判决标准）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-111","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":111,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An actor brought a federal diversity action against a theater company for breach of contract. Twenty days after discovery closed, the company moved for summary judgment. The company attached to the motion affidavits of its managing director and artistic director and a verified copy of its contract with the actor. In their affidavits, the directors stated facts establishing that the company had fully performed the contract. The actor's brief opposing the motion relied solely on the allegations of the complaint. Should the court grant the summary-judgment motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because in deciding the motion, the court must take the allegations and inferences of the complaint as true.","B":"No, because the company did not timely file the motion.","C":"Yes, because the actor filed no evidence in opposition to the motion.","D":"Yes, because there is no genuine dispute as to any material fact and the company is entitled to judgment as a matter of law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"In a summary-judgment motion, the movant has the initial burden to show that there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The movant must support this motion with admissible facts contained in pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials. Once the movant satisfies its initial burden, the burden shifts to the nonmovant to avoid summary judgment by either:\n• requesting that the court postpone consideration of the motion until additional discovery can take place (not seen here) or\n• showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence, but not through pleadings. Here, the company supported its summary-judgment motion through its directors' affidavits, which stated facts establishing that the company had fully performed its contract with the actor. The actor then failed to show specific disputed facts because his brief opposing the motion relied solely on allegations in the complaint. This means that there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the company is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Therefore, the court should grant the company's motion. (Choice A) In deciding a summary-judgment motion, a court must view the evidence in the light most favorable to the nonmovant. However, a court is not required to take the complaint's allegations and inferences as true. (Choice B) A summary-judgment motion may be filed at any time until 30 days after the close of discovery. Therefore, the company timely moved for summary judgment 20 days after discovery closed. (Choice C) The fact that the actor filed no evidence in opposition to the motion is not, by itself, sufficient for the court to grant the motion. Instead, the motion should be granted because the company satisfied its burden. Educational objective: The nonmovant can defeat a summary-judgment motion by showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence. However, the nonmovant cannot defeat the motion by relying on allegations in the pleadings. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2727 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the burden-shifting process of a motion for summary judgment).","rule":"The nonmovant can defeat a summary-judgment motion by showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence. However, the nonmovant cannot defeat the motion by relying on allegations in the pleadings.","irac":{"issue":"An actor brought a federal diversity action against a theater company for breach of contract. Twenty days after discovery closed, the company moved for summary judgment. The company attached to the motion affidavits of its managing director and artistic director and a verified copy of its contract with the actor. In their affidavits, the directors stated facts establishing that the company had fully performed the contract. The actor's brief opposing the motion relied solely on the allegations of the complaint. Should the court grant the summary-judgment motion?","rule":"The nonmovant can defeat a summary-judgment motion by showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence. However, the nonmovant cannot defeat the motion by relying on allegations in the pleadings.","analysis":"In a summary-judgment motion, the movant has the initial burden to show that there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The movant must support this motion with admissible facts contained in pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials. Once the movant satisfies its initial burden, the burden shifts to the nonmovant to avoid summary judgment by either:\n• requesting that the court postpone consideration of the motion until additional discovery can take place (not seen here) or\n• showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence, but not through pleadings. Here, the company supported its summary-judgment motion through its directors' affidavits, which stated facts establishing that the company had fully performed its contract with the actor. The actor then failed to show specific disputed facts because his brief opposing the motion relied solely on allegations in the complaint. This means that there is no genuine dispute of material fact and the company is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Therefore, the court should grant the company's motion. (Choice A) In deciding a summary-judgment motion, a court must view the evidence in the light most favorable to the nonmovant. However, a court is not required to take the complaint's allegations and inferences as true. (Choice B) A summary-judgment motion may be filed at any time until 30 days after the close of discovery. Therefore, the company timely moved for summary judgment 20 days after discovery closed. (Choice C) The fact that the actor filed no evidence in opposition to the motion is not, by itself, sufficient for the court to grant the motion. Instead, the motion should be granted because the company satisfied its burden. Educational objective: The nonmovant can defeat a summary-judgment motion by showing specific disputed facts through affidavits, declarations, discovery, or other materials containing admissible evidence. However, the nonmovant cannot defeat the motion by relying on allegations in the pleadings. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2727 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the burden-shifting process of a motion for summary judgment).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2727 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the burden-shifting process of a motion for summary judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[336],"sourceAnswerPages":[337],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名演员对一家剧团提起联邦多元州籍违约诉讼。证据开示结束 20 天后，剧团申请简易判决。剧团在动议中附具其常务总监和艺术总监的宣誓书，以及与演员所订合同的核证副本。两名总监在宣誓书中陈述了证明剧团已完全履行合同的事实。演员反对动议的书面意见只依赖起诉状中的指控。法院是否应批准简易判决动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为法院裁定动议时必须把起诉状指控及其推论视为真实。","B":"不应，因为剧团未及时提出动议。","C":"应当，因为演员未提交反对动议的证据。","D":"应当，因为不存在任何重大事实真实争议，且剧团依法有权获得判决。"},"explanationZh":"在简易判决动议中，动议方负有初步责任，证明不存在重大事实真实争议且其依法有权获得判决。动议方必须使用诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他材料所包含的可采事实支持动议。\n\n动议方满足初步责任后，责任转移至非动议方。为避免简易判决，非动议方必须选择：\n• 请求法院暂缓审理动议，直至完成额外证据开示，本题没有；或\n• 通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采证据的材料，证明具体争议事实，而不能仅依赖诉状。\n\n本题中，剧团用两名总监的宣誓书支持简易判决动议，宣誓书陈述了证明剧团已完全履行与演员合同的事实。演员反对动议的书面意见只依赖起诉状指控，因而未证明具体争议事实。这意味着不存在重大事实真实争议，剧团依法有权获得判决。因此，法院应批准剧团动议。\n\n（选项 A）裁定简易判决动议时，法院必须以最有利于非动议方的方式看待证据。但是，法院无需把起诉状中的指控及推论视为真实。\n\n（选项 B）简易判决动议可以在证据开示结束后 30 天前提出。剧团在证据开示结束 20 天后提出，属于及时。\n\n（选项 C）演员没有提交反对动议的证据这一事实，本身不足以使法院批准动议。法院之所以应批准，是因为剧团已满足其证明责任。\n\n学习目标：非动议方可以通过宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料或其他包含可采证据的材料，证明具体争议事实，从而击败简易判决动议；但不能仅依赖诉状中的指控。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56（简易判决）。\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2727 (4th ed. 2020)（解释简易判决动议的责任转移程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-033"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-112","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":112,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A patient from State A sued a doctor from State B in federal court for medical malpractice, seeking $100,000 in damages. During trial, the doctor's attorney presented a substantial amount of evidence that clearly demonstrated the doctor was not negligent, while the patient presented little evidence that showed the doctor was at fault. Neither party filed any motions before the case was submitted to the jury. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the patient and awarded her $70,000 after deliberating for an hour, and the judge entered final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict that same day. The doctor's attorney believes that the verdict was not supported by the clear weight of evidence and seeks to challenge the verdict before filing a notice of appeal. What action should the doctor's attorney take?","choices":{"A":"Move for a new trial.","B":"Move for a renewed judgment as a matter of law.","C":"Move for summary judgment.","D":"Move to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment\n\nNew trial • Prejudicial trial error\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment basis for judgment\n• Intervening change in controlling law\n• Newly discovered evidence Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief excusable neglect final judgment\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief\n\nWithin 28 days after entry of a final judgment, a party can move for a new trial on several grounds, including when the jury's verdict is not supported by the clear weight of evidence. Here, the doctor's attorney presented substantial evidence that showed the doctor did not commit malpractice, while the patient presented little evidence that the doctor was at fault. Since the jury's verdict in favor of the patient went against the clear weight of this evidence, the doctor's attorney should move for a new trial. (Choice B) A judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. But once the jury has returned its verdict, a party can only move for a renewed JMOL if that party had moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (not seen here). (Choice C) A motion for summary judgment must be filed no later than 30 days after the close of discovery—not after the trial has concluded. (Choice D) The court has subject-matter jurisdiction through diversity jurisdiction because (1) the patient and doctor are citizens of different states and (2) the amount in controversy—ie, the amount demanded in the patient's complaint—exceeds $75,000. Educational objective: A party can move for a new trial within 28 days after entry of a final judgment on several grounds, such as when the jury's verdict is not supported by the clear weight of evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).","rule":"A party can move for a new trial within 28 days after entry of a final judgment on several grounds, such as when the jury's verdict is not supported by the clear weight of evidence.","irac":{"issue":"A patient from State A sued a doctor from State B in federal court for medical malpractice, seeking $100,000 in damages. During trial, the doctor's attorney presented a substantial amount of evidence that clearly demonstrated the doctor was not negligent, while the patient presented little evidence that showed the doctor was at fault. Neither party filed any motions before the case was submitted to the jury. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the patient and awarded her $70,000 after deliberating for an hour, and the judge entered final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict that same day. The doctor's attorney believes that the verdict was not supported by the clear weight of evidence and seeks to challenge the verdict before filing a notice of appeal. What action should the doctor's attorney take?","rule":"A party can move for a new trial within 28 days after entry of a final judgment on several grounds, such as when the jury's verdict is not supported by the clear weight of evidence.","analysis":"Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment\n\nNew trial • Prejudicial trial error\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment basis for judgment\n• Intervening change in controlling law\n• Newly discovered evidence Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief excusable neglect final judgment\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief\n\nWithin 28 days after entry of a final judgment, a party can move for a new trial on several grounds, including when the jury's verdict is not supported by the clear weight of evidence. Here, the doctor's attorney presented substantial evidence that showed the doctor did not commit malpractice, while the patient presented little evidence that the doctor was at fault. Since the jury's verdict in favor of the patient went against the clear weight of this evidence, the doctor's attorney should move for a new trial. (Choice B) A judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. But once the jury has returned its verdict, a party can only move for a renewed JMOL if that party had moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (not seen here). (Choice C) A motion for summary judgment must be filed no later than 30 days after the close of discovery—not after the trial has concluded. (Choice D) The court has subject-matter jurisdiction through diversity jurisdiction because (1) the patient and doctor are citizens of different states and (2) the amount in controversy—ie, the amount demanded in the patient's complaint—exceeds $75,000. Educational objective: A party can move for a new trial within 28 days after entry of a final judgment on several grounds, such as when the jury's verdict is not supported by the clear weight of evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[339],"sourceAnswerPages":[340,341],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名患者在联邦法院起诉 B 州一名医生医疗事故，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。审判中，医生律师提出大量证据，清楚证明医生没有过失；患者则仅提出少量表明医生有过错的证据。案件交付陪审团前，双方均未提出任何动议。陪审团评议一小时后作出有利于患者的裁决，判给 70,000 美元；法官同日依陪审团裁决登记终局判决。医生律师认为裁决不受证据明显权重支持，希望在提交上诉通知前质疑裁决。医生律师应采取什么行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请重新审判。","B":"续行申请依法判决。","C":"申请简易判决。","D":"以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。"},"explanationZh":"审判后救济：\n\n救济｜理由｜时限\n律师费｜法律或规则允许获得律师费｜登记终局判决后 14 天内\n依法判决｜证据在法律上不足以支持非动议方胜诉｜登记终局判决后 28 天内\n重新审判｜造成不利影响的审判错误；法官、律师、当事人或陪审员造成不利影响的不当行为；裁决不受证据明显权重支持；裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；损害赔偿过高或不足；新发现证据｜登记终局判决后 28 天内\n修改判决｜构成判决基础的明显法律或事实错误；控制性法律在此期间发生变化；新发现证据｜登记终局判决后 28 天内\n更正错误｜书记性错误；因疏忽或遗漏产生的错误｜可自由更正；上诉列入日程后须经上诉法院许可\n特别救济｜错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽；新发现证据；对方欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为｜登记终局判决后一年内\n特别救济｜无效判决；判决已履行、解除或撤销；其他足以给予救济的理由｜合理时间内\n\n当事人可以在登记终局判决后 28 天内基于多种理由申请重新审判，包括陪审团裁决不受证据明显权重支持。\n\n本题中，医生律师提出大量证据表明医生没有实施医疗事故，而患者只提出少量表明医生有过错的证据。陪审团作出有利于患者的裁决，违背这些证据的明显权重，所以医生律师应申请重新审判。\n\n（选项 B）依法判决（JMOL）是因证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决，而为动议方登记的判决。但是，陪审团已经作出裁决后，当事人只有在案件交付陪审团前曾申请 JMOL，才能续行申请；本题没有。\n\n（选项 C）简易判决动议必须在证据开示结束后不超过 30 天提出，而不能在审判结束后提出。\n\n（选项 D）法院通过多元州籍管辖权享有事项管辖权，因为：(1) 患者和医生是不同州的公民；且 (2) 争议金额——患者起诉状所请求金额——超过 75,000 美元。\n\n学习目标：当事人可以在登记终局判决后 28 天内基于多种理由申请重新审判，例如陪审团裁决不受证据明显权重支持。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（重新审判动议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-113","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":113,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff filed a diversity action against a defendant in a federal court in State A. The plaintiff claims that the defendant caused extensive damage to the plaintiff's summer cottage in State B. The complaint also alleges that the defendant has a nefarious reputation in the community. The defendant resides in State B and has no contacts with State A. Before filing an answer, the defendant moved to strike from the complaint the assertion that she had a nefarious reputation in the community. The court granted the motion, finding that the assertion was scandalous and immaterial to the case. The defendant now moves to dismiss the complaint for lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue. How will the court likely rule on the motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the defendant failed to include these defenses in her motion to strike.","B":"Deny the motion, because the defendant must assert each defense in a separate motion.","C":"Grant the motion for improper venue, because the defendant resides and the cottage is located in State B.","D":"Grant the motion for lack of personal jurisdiction, because the defendant has no contacts with State A."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A defendant must respond to a plaintiff's complaint by filing an answer or a pre-answer motion. For example, under FRCP 12 a party may file a pre-answer motion to strike from an opposing party's pleading any insufficient defense or any redundant, impertinent, scandalous, or immaterial matter—as the defendant did here. When the defendant files a pre-answer motion, the omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single motion for efficiency purposes. This means that the defendant cannot file a subsequent pre-answer motion raising an omitted FRCP 12 defense, except for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, that was available when the initial motion was filed. Because of the omnibus motion rule, the following FRCP 12 defenses are waived and cannot be asserted later if omitted from a pre-answer motion:\n• Lack of personal jurisdiction, which generally requires the defendant to have minimum contacts with the forum state\n\n• Improper venue, which is proper in any judicial district where the defendant resides or a substantial portion of the property at issue is located\n\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n\nHere, the State A federal court likely lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant because she has no contacts with State A (Choice D). Additionally, the State A federal court is likely an improper venue since the defendant resides and the cottage is located in State B (Choice C). However, since the defendant's initial pre-answer motion to strike failed to include these defenses, they are waived. As a result, the court will likely deny the defendant's motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue. (Choice B) FRCP 12 defenses that are raised after a pre-answer motion has been filed are generally waived under the omnibus motion rule. Therefore, the defendant cannot assert her lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue defenses in separate motions. Educational objective: The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a single pre-answer motion or in an answer, whichever occurs first. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f)–(h) (motion to strike; consolidating and waiving pre-answer defenses).","rule":"The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a single pre-answer motion or in an answer, whichever occurs first.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff filed a diversity action against a defendant in a federal court in State A. The plaintiff claims that the defendant caused extensive damage to the plaintiff's summer cottage in State B. The complaint also alleges that the defendant has a nefarious reputation in the community. The defendant resides in State B and has no contacts with State A. Before filing an answer, the defendant moved to strike from the complaint the assertion that she had a nefarious reputation in the community. The court granted the motion, finding that the assertion was scandalous and immaterial to the case. The defendant now moves to dismiss the complaint for lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue. How will the court likely rule on the motion?","rule":"The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a single pre-answer motion or in an answer, whichever occurs first.","analysis":"A defendant must respond to a plaintiff's complaint by filing an answer or a pre-answer motion. For example, under FRCP 12 a party may file a pre-answer motion to strike from an opposing party's pleading any insufficient defense or any redundant, impertinent, scandalous, or immaterial matter—as the defendant did here. When the defendant files a pre-answer motion, the omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single motion for efficiency purposes. This means that the defendant cannot file a subsequent pre-answer motion raising an omitted FRCP 12 defense, except for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, that was available when the initial motion was filed. Because of the omnibus motion rule, the following FRCP 12 defenses are waived and cannot be asserted later if omitted from a pre-answer motion:\n• Lack of personal jurisdiction, which generally requires the defendant to have minimum contacts with the forum state\n\n• Improper venue, which is proper in any judicial district where the defendant resides or a substantial portion of the property at issue is located\n\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n\nHere, the State A federal court likely lacks personal jurisdiction over the defendant because she has no contacts with State A (Choice D). Additionally, the State A federal court is likely an improper venue since the defendant resides and the cottage is located in State B (Choice C). However, since the defendant's initial pre-answer motion to strike failed to include these defenses, they are waived. As a result, the court will likely deny the defendant's motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue. (Choice B) FRCP 12 defenses that are raised after a pre-answer motion has been filed are generally waived under the omnibus motion rule. Therefore, the defendant cannot assert her lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue defenses in separate motions. Educational objective: The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a single pre-answer motion or in an answer, whichever occurs first. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f)–(h) (motion to strike; consolidating and waiving pre-answer defenses).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f)–(h) (motion to strike; consolidating and waiving pre-answer defenses).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[342],"sourceAnswerPages":[343],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在 A 州联邦法院对被告提起多元州籍诉讼，主张被告严重损坏原告位于 B 州的避暑别墅。起诉状还称，被告在社区中声名恶劣。被告居住于 B 州，与 A 州没有任何联系。提交答辩状前，被告申请从起诉状中删除关于其在社区声名恶劣的陈述。法院认定该陈述具有诽谤性且与案件无关，遂批准动议。被告现以缺乏对人管辖权及审判地不当为由申请驳回起诉状。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为被告未在删除动议中一并提出这些抗辩。","B":"驳回动议，因为被告必须通过分别提出的动议主张每项抗辩。","C":"以审判地不当为由批准动议，因为被告居住于 B 州，别墅也位于 B 州。","D":"以缺乏对人管辖权为由批准动议，因为被告与 A 州没有联系。"},"explanationZh":"被告必须通过提交答辩状或答辩前动议回应原告起诉状。例如，依据 FRCP 12，当事人可以在答辩前申请从对方诉状中删除任何不充分抗辩，或任何重复、不适当、诽谤性或不相关事项，本题被告正是如此。\n\n被告提交答辩前动议时，综合动议规则为提高效率，要求把全部 FRCP 12 抗辩合并在同一动议中。这意味着，被告不得通过后续答辩前动议提出初次动议时已经可以提出却遗漏的 FRCP 12 抗辩；缺乏事项管辖权除外。\n\n依据综合动议规则，若在答辩前动议中遗漏，下列 FRCP 12 抗辩即被放弃，之后不得再提出：\n• 缺乏对人管辖权；一般要求被告与法院州存在最低限度联系；\n• 审判地不当；被告居住的任何司法地区，或争议财产重大部分所在地区，均为适当审判地；\n• 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分。\n\n本题中，被告与 A 州没有联系，所以 A 州联邦法院很可能对其缺乏对人管辖权（选项 D）。此外，被告居住于 B 州，别墅也位于 B 州，所以 A 州联邦法院很可能是不得当的审判地（选项 C）。但是，被告最初提出的答辩前删除动议未包含这些抗辩，所以它们已经被放弃。因此，法院很可能驳回被告以缺乏对人管辖权及审判地不当提出的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 B）答辩前动议提交后才提出的 FRCP 12 抗辩，依据综合动议规则通常被视为放弃。因此，被告不能通过分别提出的动议主张缺乏对人管辖权和审判地不当。\n\n学习目标：缺乏对人管辖权、审判地不当，以及诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分，若未在单一答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出，即被放弃。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(f)–(h)（删除动议；答辩前抗辩的合并与放弃）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-034"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-114","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":114,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"An inmate sued a private prison-management company in federal court for allegedly mistreating her in violation of federal law. During the jury trial, the inmate's attorney questioned one of the company's prison guards. When the guard repeatedly gave nonresponsive answers, the inmate's attorney became frustrated and accidentally complained out loud in the presence of the jury, \"The guard obviously has something to hide.\" The company's counsel objected to the comment. The inmate's attorney immediately apologized and withdrew the comment from the record, and the judge admonished the jury to disregard the comment. The inmate's attorney did not make any other improper comments throughout the rest of the trial. At the end of the trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the inmate, finding that the overwhelming amount of evidence supported her claim. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The company immediately moved for a new trial on the ground that the inmate's attorney made the improper comment. Will the court likely grant the company's motion for a new trial?","choices":{"A":"No, because the comment was harmless.","B":"No, because the comment was unintentional.","C":"Yes, because the comment was made in the presence of the jury.","D":"Yes, because the comment was prejudicial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence A court can grant a new trial if an attorney (or judge, party, or juror) engaged in prejudicial misconduct during the trial. Misconduct is prejudicial when it affects a party's substantial rights. This requires the court to determine with fair assurance whether the jury's verdict was influenced by the error. If there was no improper influence, then the error was harmless and does not provide a basis for a new trial. Here, the jury found that the overwhelming amount of evidence supported the inmate's claim. This shows that the attorney's improper comment likely did not influence the jury's verdict. Even if the comment initially affected the jury, any improper influence was cured when the attorney apologized and withdrew the comment from the record, and the judge admonished the jury to disregard it. As a result, the court will likely find the error was harmless and deny the company's motion for a new trial (Choices C & D). (Choice B) The fact that the inmate's attorney unintentionally made the comment has no bearing on the company's motion for a new trial. Had the comment influenced the jury's verdict, the motion likely would have been granted. Educational objective: A new trial is warranted when an attorney engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61 (harmless error).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 101 (2019) (explaining that an attorney's misconduct must be prejudicial to warrant new trial).","rule":"A new trial is warranted when an attorney engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial.","irac":{"issue":"An inmate sued a private prison-management company in federal court for allegedly mistreating her in violation of federal law. During the jury trial, the inmate's attorney questioned one of the company's prison guards. When the guard repeatedly gave nonresponsive answers, the inmate's attorney became frustrated and accidentally complained out loud in the presence of the jury, \"The guard obviously has something to hide.\" The company's counsel objected to the comment. The inmate's attorney immediately apologized and withdrew the comment from the record, and the judge admonished the jury to disregard the comment. The inmate's attorney did not make any other improper comments throughout the rest of the trial. At the end of the trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the inmate, finding that the overwhelming amount of evidence supported her claim. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The company immediately moved for a new trial on the ground that the inmate's attorney made the improper comment. Will the court likely grant the company's motion for a new trial?","rule":"A new trial is warranted when an attorney engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial.","analysis":"Grounds for new trial\n\n• Prejudicial trial error (eg, erroneous evidentiary ruling)\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, or juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence A court can grant a new trial if an attorney (or judge, party, or juror) engaged in prejudicial misconduct during the trial. Misconduct is prejudicial when it affects a party's substantial rights. This requires the court to determine with fair assurance whether the jury's verdict was influenced by the error. If there was no improper influence, then the error was harmless and does not provide a basis for a new trial. Here, the jury found that the overwhelming amount of evidence supported the inmate's claim. This shows that the attorney's improper comment likely did not influence the jury's verdict. Even if the comment initially affected the jury, any improper influence was cured when the attorney apologized and withdrew the comment from the record, and the judge admonished the jury to disregard it. As a result, the court will likely find the error was harmless and deny the company's motion for a new trial (Choices C & D). (Choice B) The fact that the inmate's attorney unintentionally made the comment has no bearing on the company's motion for a new trial. Had the comment influenced the jury's verdict, the motion likely would have been granted. Educational objective: A new trial is warranted when an attorney engaged in prejudicial misconduct—ie, misconduct that affects a party's substantial rights by influencing the jury's verdict. If misconduct is not prejudicial, then it is merely harmless error that does not warrant a new trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61 (harmless error).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 101 (2019) (explaining that an attorney's misconduct must be prejudicial to warrant new trial).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61 (harmless error).\n\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 101 (2019) (explaining that an attorney's misconduct must be prejudicial to warrant new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[345],"sourceAnswerPages":[346],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名在押人员在联邦法院起诉一家私人监狱管理公司，主张公司以违反联邦法律的方式虐待她。在陪审团审判中，在押人员的律师询问公司的一名狱警。狱警一再答非所问，律师感到沮丧，在陪审团面前不慎脱口抱怨：“狱警显然有所隐瞒。”公司律师对该评论提出异议。在押人员律师立即道歉，并从案卷中撤回评论；法官也告诫陪审团忽略该评论。此后整个审判中，律师未再发表任何不当评论。审判结束时，陪审团认定压倒性证据支持在押人员的请求，遂作出有利于她的裁决。法院依裁决登记终局判决。公司立即以该不当评论为由申请重新审判。法院可能批准公司的重新审判动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为该评论属于无害错误。","B":"不会，因为该评论并非故意作出。","C":"会，因为该评论在陪审团面前作出。","D":"会，因为该评论造成不利影响。"},"explanationZh":"重新审判的理由：\n• 造成不利影响的审判错误，例如错误的证据裁定；\n• 法官、律师、当事人或陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为；\n• 裁决不受证据明显权重支持；\n• 裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；\n• 损害赔偿过高或不足；\n• 新发现证据。\n\n律师（或法官、当事人、陪审员）在审判中实施造成不利影响的不当行为时，法院可以批准重新审判。不当行为影响一方重大权利时，才属于造成不利影响；法院必须以相当把握判断陪审团裁决是否受该错误影响。若不存在不当影响，该错误属于无害错误，不构成重新审判依据。\n\n本题中，陪审团认定压倒性证据支持在押人员的请求。这表明律师的不当评论很可能没有影响陪审团裁决。即使评论最初影响了陪审团，律师道歉并从案卷中撤回评论，且法官告诫陪审团忽略，也已消除任何不当影响。因此，法院很可能认定该错误无害，驳回公司的重新审判动议（选项 C、D）。\n\n（选项 B）在押人员律师并非故意作出评论，与公司的重新审判动议无关。若评论实际影响陪审团裁决，动议很可能获准。\n\n学习目标：律师实施造成不利影响的不当行为——即通过影响陪审团裁决而侵害一方重大权利——时，应当重新审判。若不当行为未造成不利影响，则只是无害错误，不足以支持重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 61（无害错误）。\n• 58 Am. Jur. 2d New Trial § 101 (2019)（说明律师不当行为必须造成不利影响，方可重新审判）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-115","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":115,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A woman sued her former employer in federal court for sexual discrimination in violation of federal law. At the jury trial, the woman presented evidence that she had been repeatedly passed over for promotions that went to her less-qualified male colleagues. After the woman rested her case, the employer presented evidence that the promotions were made pursuant to a merit-based system that did not take sex into account. To rebut the employer's evidence, the woman provided witness testimony that the system was just a pretext to discriminate against female employees. At the conclusion of evidence, the employer moved for judgment as a matter of law. Viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to the employer, the court granted the employer's motion. Twenty-two days later, the woman appealed the district court's entry of judgment as a matter of law. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the judgment as a matter of law?","choices":{"A":"No, because the entry of judgment as a matter of law was appropriate under the circumstances.","B":"No, because the woman failed to timely appeal the district court's entry of judgment as a matter of law.","C":"Yes, because the district court failed to follow the proper procedural requirements in assessing the motion for judgment as a matter of law.","D":"Yes, because the employer failed to move for judgment as a matter of law at the close of the woman's case-in-chief."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA party may move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) at any time after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present its case-in-chief but before the case is submitted to the jury. A motion for JMOL is a request that the court enter a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. When considering such a motion, the district court must:\n• view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant\n\n• disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe and\n\n• not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.\n\nHere, the employer timely moved for JMOL after the woman presented her case-in-chief and before the case was submitted to the jury (Choice D). However, the district court improperly viewed the evidence in the light most favorable to the employer (movant). As a result, the entry of JMOL was not appropriate under the circumstances, and the appellate court will likely overturn it (Choice A).\n\n(Choice B) A party generally must appeal an adverse ruling, judgment, or order within 30 days after the entry of final judgment.* Therefore, the woman timely appealed the JMOL 22 days after it was entered. *The time to appeal is extended to 60 days if one of the parties is (1) the United States, (2) a federal agency, or (3) a federal officer or employee sued for conduct that relates to government duties. Educational objective: When considering a motion for JMOL, the district court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant, (2) disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000) (explaining the procedural requirements for considering a JMOL motion).","rule":"When considering a motion for JMOL, the district court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant, (2) disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.","irac":{"issue":"A woman sued her former employer in federal court for sexual discrimination in violation of federal law. At the jury trial, the woman presented evidence that she had been repeatedly passed over for promotions that went to her less-qualified male colleagues. After the woman rested her case, the employer presented evidence that the promotions were made pursuant to a merit-based system that did not take sex into account. To rebut the employer's evidence, the woman provided witness testimony that the system was just a pretext to discriminate against female employees. At the conclusion of evidence, the employer moved for judgment as a matter of law. Viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to the employer, the court granted the employer's motion. Twenty-two days later, the woman appealed the district court's entry of judgment as a matter of law. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the judgment as a matter of law?","rule":"When considering a motion for JMOL, the district court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant, (2) disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.","analysis":"Motion for judgment as a matter of law\n\n(FRCP 50)\n\nTime to file • May be filed after nonmovant presents its case but before case is submitted to jury\n• May be renewed within 28 days after entry of final judgment Procedural • Movant must: requirements – specify judgment sought & law/facts entitling movant to judgment\n• Court must: – view evidence & draw all reasonable inferences in nonmovant's favor – disregard evidence favorable to movant that jury is not required to believe – not consider credibility of witnesses or evaluate weight of evidence Standard for grant • Evidence is legally insufficient for reasonable jury to find in nonmovant's favor FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA party may move for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) at any time after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to present its case-in-chief but before the case is submitted to the jury. A motion for JMOL is a request that the court enter a judgment in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. When considering such a motion, the district court must:\n• view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant\n\n• disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe and\n\n• not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence.\n\nHere, the employer timely moved for JMOL after the woman presented her case-in-chief and before the case was submitted to the jury (Choice D). However, the district court improperly viewed the evidence in the light most favorable to the employer (movant). As a result, the entry of JMOL was not appropriate under the circumstances, and the appellate court will likely overturn it (Choice A).\n\n(Choice B) A party generally must appeal an adverse ruling, judgment, or order within 30 days after the entry of final judgment.* Therefore, the woman timely appealed the JMOL 22 days after it was entered. *The time to appeal is extended to 60 days if one of the parties is (1) the United States, (2) a federal agency, or (3) a federal officer or employee sued for conduct that relates to government duties. Educational objective: When considering a motion for JMOL, the district court must (1) view the evidence and draw all reasonable inferences in the light most favorable to the nonmovant, (2) disregard any evidence favorable to the movant that the jury is not required to believe, and (3) not consider the credibility of witnesses or evaluate the weight of evidence. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000) (explaining the procedural requirements for considering a JMOL motion).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a) (motion for judgment as a matter of law).\n\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000) (explaining the procedural requirements for considering a JMOL motion).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[348],"sourceAnswerPages":[349,350],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在联邦法院起诉前雇主实施违反联邦法律的性别歧视。在陪审团审判中，女子提出证据证明，她一再未获晋升，而职位均给了资历不如她的男性同事。女子本案举证结束后，雇主提出证据证明晋升依据不考虑性别的绩效制度作出。为反驳雇主证据，女子提出证人证言，证明该制度只是歧视女性雇员的借口。举证结束时，雇主申请依法判决。法院以最有利于雇主的方式看待证据，批准其动议。22 天后，女子对地区法院登记依法判决提起上诉。上诉法院可能撤销该依法判决吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为在该情形下登记依法判决适当。","B":"不会，因为女子未及时对地区法院登记依法判决提起上诉。","C":"会，因为地区法院审理依法判决动议时未遵循适当程序要求。","D":"会，因为雇主未在女子本案举证结束时申请依法判决。"},"explanationZh":"依法判决动议（FRCP 50）：\n\n提出时间：\n• 可在非动议方陈述案件后、案件交付陪审团前提出；\n• 可在登记终局判决后 28 天内续行提出。\n\n程序要求：\n• 动议方必须指明所请求判决，以及使其有权获得判决的法律／事实；\n• 法院必须：\n  – 以最有利于非动议方的方式审视证据，并作出所有合理推论；\n  – 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；\n  – 不衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n批准标准：\n• 证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。\n\n非动议方已有机会陈述其本案后、案件交付陪审团前，当事人可以随时申请依法判决（JMOL）。JMOL 请求法院为动议方登记判决，因为证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决。\n\n审理该动议时，地区法院必须：\n• 以最有利于非动议方的方式审视证据，并作出所有合理推论；\n• 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；并且\n• 不衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n本题中，雇主在女子陈述本案后、案件交付陪审团前及时申请 JMOL（选项 D）。但是，地区法院错误地以最有利于雇主（动议方）的方式看待证据。因此，在该情形下登记 JMOL 不适当，上诉法院很可能予以撤销（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）当事人通常必须在登记终局判决后 30 天内，对不利裁定、判决或命令提起上诉。* 女子在登记 JMOL 后 22 天上诉，属于及时。\n\n*若一方是美国、联邦机构，或就与政府职务有关行为被诉的联邦官员或雇员，上诉期限延长为 60 天。\n\n学习目标：审理 JMOL 动议时，地区法院必须：(1) 以有利于非动议方的方式审视证据并作出所有合理推论；(2) 忽略有利于动议方但陪审团并非必须相信的证据；且 (3) 不衡量证人可信度或评价证据权重。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(a)（依法判决动议）。\n• Reeves v. Sanderson Plumbing Prods., Inc., 530 U.S. 133, 150–51 (2000)（解释审理 JMOL 动议的程序要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-116","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":116,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man in State A posted his exotic car for sale on a website. He received an email with an offer to buy the car for $150,000 from a 16-year-old girl from State B. The man accepted the girl's offer but received a phone call from the girl's father rescinding the offer the following day. The man filed a complaint in a federal court in State A against the girl for breach of contract. The girl timely filed an answer in which she admitted to the facts provided in the man's complaint but asserted the affirmative defense of accord and satisfaction. The man moved for judgment on the pleadings. Statutes in both State A and State B treat contracts with persons below the age of 18 for items other than necessities as void. Necessities are defined as food, shelter, and clothing. Is the court likely to grant the man's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because plaintiffs cannot move for judgment on the pleadings.","B":"No, because the man failed to state a claim upon which relief may be granted.","C":"Yes, because accord and satisfaction is not a valid defense in this case.","D":"Yes, because the girl admitted to the facts alleged in the man's complaint."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Pretrial motions\n\nMotion Grounds\n\nDefault • Defendant failed to timely serve answer judgment • Any party failed to comply with court order Pre-answer • No subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service or process\n• Failure to join required party\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted Judgment on the • Pleadings, attached exhibits & matters of public record show pleadings (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law Voluntary • Plaintiff requests dismissal dismissal\n\nInvoluntary • Defendant requests dismissal because plaintiff failed to (1) dismissal prosecute action or (2) comply with rule/court order\n\nSummary • Pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery & other materials judgment show (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(c), any party may move for judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings have closed—ie, once the plaintiff's complaint and the defendant's answer have been served (Choice A). To succeed on this motion, the movant must show that (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Here, there are no genuine issues of material fact since the girl admitted to the facts alleged in the man's complaint for breach of contract (Choice D). But since the girl is under the age of majority and a car is not a necessity under either state's statute, the contract was void. This means that the man failed to state a claim upon which relief may be granted and is not entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Therefore, the court will likely deny the man's motion for judgment on the pleadings. (Choice C) An accord is a party's promise to relieve another person of contractual obligations in return for a specific act. Upon satisfaction of that act, that person is excused from further performance under the contract. Accord and satisfaction is not a valid\n\naffirmative defense here since the contract was void. However, the man cannot succeed on his motion since he failed to state a valid claim. Educational objective: A court should enter judgment on the pleadings when the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c) (judgment on the pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2020) (explaining that movant must allege some viable legal theory to succeed on motion for judgment on the pleadings).","rule":"A court should enter judgment on the pleadings when the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.","irac":{"issue":"A man in State A posted his exotic car for sale on a website. He received an email with an offer to buy the car for $150,000 from a 16-year-old girl from State B. The man accepted the girl's offer but received a phone call from the girl's father rescinding the offer the following day. The man filed a complaint in a federal court in State A against the girl for breach of contract. The girl timely filed an answer in which she admitted to the facts provided in the man's complaint but asserted the affirmative defense of accord and satisfaction. The man moved for judgment on the pleadings. Statutes in both State A and State B treat contracts with persons below the age of 18 for items other than necessities as void. Necessities are defined as food, shelter, and clothing. Is the court likely to grant the man's motion?","rule":"A court should enter judgment on the pleadings when the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law.","analysis":"Pretrial motions\n\nMotion Grounds\n\nDefault • Defendant failed to timely serve answer judgment • Any party failed to comply with court order Pre-answer • No subject-matter or personal jurisdiction\n• Improper venue\n• Insufficient process or service or process\n• Failure to join required party\n• Failure to state claim upon which relief can be granted Judgment on the • Pleadings, attached exhibits & matters of public record show pleadings (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law Voluntary • Plaintiff requests dismissal dismissal\n\nInvoluntary • Defendant requests dismissal because plaintiff failed to (1) dismissal prosecute action or (2) comply with rule/court order\n\nSummary • Pleadings, affidavits, declarations, discovery & other materials judgment show (1) no genuine dispute of material fact & (2) movant entitled to judgment as a matter of law Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(c), any party may move for judgment on the pleadings after the pleadings have closed—ie, once the plaintiff's complaint and the defendant's answer have been served (Choice A). To succeed on this motion, the movant must show that (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Here, there are no genuine issues of material fact since the girl admitted to the facts alleged in the man's complaint for breach of contract (Choice D). But since the girl is under the age of majority and a car is not a necessity under either state's statute, the contract was void. This means that the man failed to state a claim upon which relief may be granted and is not entitled to judgment as a matter of law. Therefore, the court will likely deny the man's motion for judgment on the pleadings. (Choice C) An accord is a party's promise to relieve another person of contractual obligations in return for a specific act. Upon satisfaction of that act, that person is excused from further performance under the contract. Accord and satisfaction is not a valid\n\naffirmative defense here since the contract was void. However, the man cannot succeed on his motion since he failed to state a valid claim. Educational objective: A court should enter judgment on the pleadings when the movant shows that (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c) (judgment on the pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2020) (explaining that movant must allege some viable legal theory to succeed on motion for judgment on the pleadings).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c) (judgment on the pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2020) (explaining that movant must allege some viable legal theory to succeed on motion for judgment on the pleadings).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[351],"sourceAnswerPages":[352,353],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名男子在网站上发布其异国跑车出售广告。他收到 B 州一名 16 岁女孩的电子邮件，女孩提出以 150,000 美元购买汽车。男子接受要约，但次日接到女孩父亲电话，撤销该要约。男子在 A 州联邦法院对女孩提起违约诉讼。女孩及时提交答辩状，承认男子起诉状所述事实，但提出和解与清偿的积极抗辩。男子申请诉状判决。A、B 两州法律均规定，未满 18 岁者就生活必需品以外物品订立的合同无效；生活必需品定义为食物、住所和衣物。法院可能批准男子的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为原告不能申请诉状判决。","B":"不会，因为男子未陈述可给予救济的请求。","C":"会，因为和解与清偿在本案中不是有效抗辩。","D":"会，因为女孩承认男子起诉状所述事实。"},"explanationZh":"审前动议：\n\n动议｜理由\n缺席判决｜被告未及时送达答辩状；任何当事人未遵守法院命令\n答辩前动议｜缺乏事项管辖权或对人管辖权；审判地不当；诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分；未合并必要当事人；未陈述可给予救济的请求\n诉状判决｜诉状、所附材料及公共记录事项表明：(1) 不存在重大事实真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决\n自愿驳回｜原告请求驳回\n非自愿驳回｜因原告未推进诉讼或未遵守规则／法院命令，被告请求驳回\n简易判决｜诉状、宣誓书、声明、证据开示资料及其他材料表明：(1) 不存在重大事实真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决\n\n依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》12(c)，诉状提交完毕——即原告起诉状和被告答辩状均已送达——后，任何一方均可申请诉状判决（选项 A）。要成功，动议方必须证明：(1) 不存在重大事实真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决。\n\n本题中，女孩承认男子违约起诉状所述事实，故不存在重大事实真实争议（选项 D）。但是，女孩未达到法定成年年龄，且汽车不属于任何一州法律规定的生活必需品，所以合同无效。这意味着男子未陈述可给予救济的请求，依法无权获得判决。因此，法院很可能驳回男子的诉状判决动议。\n\n（选项 C）和解是当事人承诺以某项特定行为为交换，解除另一人的合同义务；该行为完成即为清偿，该人无需再履行合同。因本案合同无效，和解与清偿不是有效积极抗辩。但是，男子仍不能赢得动议，因为其未陈述有效请求。\n\n学习目标：动议方证明以下两点时，法院应登记诉状判决：(1) 不存在重大事实真实争议；且 (2) 动议方依法有权获得判决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(c)（诉状判决）。\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 560 (2020)（说明动议方必须提出可行法律理论，方可赢得诉状判决动议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-117","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":117,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A public high school student was accused by a classmate of smoking marijuana on school grounds. The school resource officer detected no smoke aroma on the student and found no marijuana contraband in the student's pockets or bag. The officer then placed the student in a locked classroom and went to deal with another incident. The officer forgot about the student, who remained in the room for two hours doing homework until a teacher let him out. The student sued the officer in federal court for violating a federal statute that allows recovery for false imprisonment by an officer of the law, which includes school resource officers. At trial, the student presented evidence that the experience required him to see a counselor twice a week and has caused him emotional harm. After the close of evidence, the judge properly instructed the jury that the statute allows an individual to recover compensatory damages for loss of liberty and physical or emotional injury. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the student and awarded him $5 million in compensatory damages, which is 100 times more than any other plaintiff has received under this statute. The officer's attorney believes that this award of damages is not supported by the evidence. What should the officer's attorney do to most efficiently challenge the damages award?","choices":{"A":"Move for a new trial of the entire case.","B":"Move for additur.","C":"Move for judgment as a matter of law.","D":"Move for remittitur."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Altering jury's award of damages\n\n(FRCP 59(e))\n\nType of Definition Applicability alteration\n\nRemittitur Defendant's request to reduce jury Plaintiff must be offered choice award of excessive damages between:\n\n• reduced damages or\n• new trial on damages Additur Plaintiff's request to increase jury award Never allowed in federal court of inadequate damages\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA losing party can challenge an allegedly excessive jury award of damages through a motion for remittitur. A court will grant the motion when the evidence presented at trial is insufficient to support the amount awarded by the jury. The plaintiff will then be given a choice between either (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages. Therefore, the officer's attorney should move for remittitur to efficiently challenge the damages award. (Choice A) A motion for a new trial of the entire case would be inefficient since the officer's attorney only wants to challenge the award of damages, which can be done through a motion for remittitur. (Choice B) Additur is a party's request that a trial judge increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages. Although additur is permitted in many state courts, it is not allowed in federal court because it violates the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial. (Choice C) A judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. To make a motion for JMOL after a jury verdict, a party must have moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (not seen here). Educational objective: A motion for remittitur is used to challenge an allegedly excessive jury award of damages. If granted, the plaintiff must then choose between (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages. References\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for remittitur).\n\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998) (explaining the requirements for remittitur).","rule":"A motion for remittitur is used to challenge an allegedly excessive jury award of damages. If granted, the plaintiff must then choose between (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages.","irac":{"issue":"A public high school student was accused by a classmate of smoking marijuana on school grounds. The school resource officer detected no smoke aroma on the student and found no marijuana contraband in the student's pockets or bag. The officer then placed the student in a locked classroom and went to deal with another incident. The officer forgot about the student, who remained in the room for two hours doing homework until a teacher let him out. The student sued the officer in federal court for violating a federal statute that allows recovery for false imprisonment by an officer of the law, which includes school resource officers. At trial, the student presented evidence that the experience required him to see a counselor twice a week and has caused him emotional harm. After the close of evidence, the judge properly instructed the jury that the statute allows an individual to recover compensatory damages for loss of liberty and physical or emotional injury. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the student and awarded him $5 million in compensatory damages, which is 100 times more than any other plaintiff has received under this statute. The officer's attorney believes that this award of damages is not supported by the evidence. What should the officer's attorney do to most efficiently challenge the damages award?","rule":"A motion for remittitur is used to challenge an allegedly excessive jury award of damages. If granted, the plaintiff must then choose between (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages.","analysis":"Altering jury's award of damages\n\n(FRCP 59(e))\n\nType of Definition Applicability alteration\n\nRemittitur Defendant's request to reduce jury Plaintiff must be offered choice award of excessive damages between:\n\n• reduced damages or\n• new trial on damages Additur Plaintiff's request to increase jury award Never allowed in federal court of inadequate damages\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA losing party can challenge an allegedly excessive jury award of damages through a motion for remittitur. A court will grant the motion when the evidence presented at trial is insufficient to support the amount awarded by the jury. The plaintiff will then be given a choice between either (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages. Therefore, the officer's attorney should move for remittitur to efficiently challenge the damages award. (Choice A) A motion for a new trial of the entire case would be inefficient since the officer's attorney only wants to challenge the award of damages, which can be done through a motion for remittitur. (Choice B) Additur is a party's request that a trial judge increase an allegedly inadequate jury award of damages. Although additur is permitted in many state courts, it is not allowed in federal court because it violates the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial. (Choice C) A judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) is a judgment entered in favor of the movant because the evidence is legally insufficient for a reasonable jury to find in the nonmovant's favor. To make a motion for JMOL after a jury verdict, a party must have moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (not seen here). Educational objective: A motion for remittitur is used to challenge an allegedly excessive jury award of damages. If granted, the plaintiff must then choose between (1) a reduction in damages or (2) a new trial on damages. References\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for remittitur).\n\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998) (explaining the requirements for remittitur).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for remittitur).\n\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998) (explaining the requirements for remittitur).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[354],"sourceAnswerPages":[355,356],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名公立高中学生被同学指控在校园内吸食大麻。学校资源警察没有在学生身上闻到烟味，也未在其口袋或书包中发现大麻违禁品。警察随后将学生留在一间上锁的教室内，前去处理另一事件。警察忘记了学生，学生在教室内做了两个小时作业，直到一名教师将其放出。学生在联邦法院起诉警察违反一项联邦法律，该法允许就执法人员（包括学校资源警察）实施的非法拘禁获得赔偿。审判中，学生提出证据证明，该经历使其必须每周看两次心理咨询师，并对其造成情绪伤害。举证结束后，法官正确指示陪审团，该法律允许个人就自由丧失及身体或情绪伤害获得补偿性赔偿。陪审团判学生胜诉，判给 500 万美元补偿性赔偿，该金额是该法下其他任何原告所得最高赔偿的 100 倍。警察律师认为该赔偿不受证据支持。警察律师应如何最高效地质疑赔偿额？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请对整个案件重新审判。","B":"申请增额裁决。","C":"申请依法判决。","D":"申请减额裁决。"},"explanationZh":"改变陪审团判给的损害赔偿（FRCP 59(e)）：\n\n改变类型｜定义｜适用性\n减额裁决（remittitur）｜被告请求减少陪审团判给的过高损害赔偿｜必须让原告选择：接受减少后的赔偿，或就损害赔偿重新审判\n增额裁决（additur）｜原告请求增加陪审团判给的不足损害赔偿｜联邦法院绝不允许\n\n败诉方可以通过减额裁决动议，质疑被指过高的陪审团损害赔偿。审判中所提证据不足以支持陪审团判给金额时，法院会批准动议。之后，原告可选择：(1) 接受减少后的赔偿；或 (2) 就损害赔偿重新审判。因此，警察律师应申请减额裁决，以高效质疑赔偿额。\n\n（选项 A）警察律师只想质疑损害赔偿额，而这可以通过减额裁决动议解决。申请对整个案件重新审判效率较低。\n\n（选项 B）增额裁决是当事人请求审判法官增加被指过低的陪审团损害赔偿。许多州法院虽允许增额裁决，但联邦法院不允许，因为它违反第七修正案陪审团审判权。\n\n（选项 C）依法判决（JMOL）是因证据在法律上不足以使合理陪审团作出有利于非动议方的裁决，而为动议方登记的判决。陪审团作出裁决后提出 JMOL 动议，当事人必须曾在案件交付陪审团前申请 JMOL；本题没有。\n\n学习目标：减额裁决动议用于质疑被指过高的陪审团损害赔偿。若动议获准，原告必须选择：(1) 接受减少后的赔偿；或 (2) 就损害赔偿重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（减额裁决动议）。\n• Hetzel v. Prince William Cty., 523 U.S. 208, 211–12 (1998)（解释减额裁决要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-118","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":118,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"An investor from State A sued a man from State B in a federal court in State A for defrauding the investor of $100,000. The man filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the investor's complaint for improper venue and lack of personal jurisdiction. The court denied this motion. The man then filed an answer denying liability. One month later, the man moved for judgment on the pleadings. The motion claims that the investor failed to state a claim on which relief can be granted. Should the court consider the man's motion for judgment on the pleadings?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man waived the right to challenge the pleading by not raising the issue in his answer.","B":"No, because the man waived the right to challenge the pleading by not raising the issue in his pre-answer motion.","C":"Yes, because the man's right to challenge the pleading was not waived.","D":"Yes, because the right to challenge a pleading can never be waived."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Most defenses are waived if they are not asserted in the defendant's answer.* However, a defendant has until the end of trial to assert the defenses of failure to join a required party OR failure to state a claim on which relief can be granted—as asserted in the man's motion for judgment on the pleadings. Since this motion was filed before the end of trial, the man has not waived his right to raise this challenge (Choice A). Therefore, the court should consider his motion. *Federal courts generally allow defendants to amend their original answer during and after trial to add defenses not previously asserted. (Choice B) Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and insufficient process or service of process are the only challenges that are waived if not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. (Choice D) Lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is the only defense that is never waived. All other defenses are subject to waiver. Educational objective: A defense is generally waived if it is not asserted in the defendant's answer. But failure to join a required party and failure to state a claim on which relief can be granted are defenses that can be asserted at any time before the end of trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(2) (waiver of failure to state a claim defense).","rule":"A defense is generally waived if it is not asserted in the defendant's answer. But failure to join a required party and failure to state a claim on which relief can be granted are defenses that can be asserted at any time before the end of trial.","irac":{"issue":"An investor from State A sued a man from State B in a federal court in State A for defrauding the investor of $100,000. The man filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the investor's complaint for improper venue and lack of personal jurisdiction. The court denied this motion. The man then filed an answer denying liability. One month later, the man moved for judgment on the pleadings. The motion claims that the investor failed to state a claim on which relief can be granted. Should the court consider the man's motion for judgment on the pleadings?","rule":"A defense is generally waived if it is not asserted in the defendant's answer. But failure to join a required party and failure to state a claim on which relief can be granted are defenses that can be asserted at any time before the end of trial.","analysis":"Most defenses are waived if they are not asserted in the defendant's answer.* However, a defendant has until the end of trial to assert the defenses of failure to join a required party OR failure to state a claim on which relief can be granted—as asserted in the man's motion for judgment on the pleadings. Since this motion was filed before the end of trial, the man has not waived his right to raise this challenge (Choice A). Therefore, the court should consider his motion. *Federal courts generally allow defendants to amend their original answer during and after trial to add defenses not previously asserted. (Choice B) Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and insufficient process or service of process are the only challenges that are waived if not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. (Choice D) Lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is the only defense that is never waived. All other defenses are subject to waiver. Educational objective: A defense is generally waived if it is not asserted in the defendant's answer. But failure to join a required party and failure to state a claim on which relief can be granted are defenses that can be asserted at any time before the end of trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(2) (waiver of failure to state a claim defense).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(2) (waiver of failure to state a claim defense).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[357],"sourceAnswerPages":[358],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名投资者在 A 州联邦法院起诉 B 州一名男子，主张男子欺诈投资者 100,000 美元。男子提交答辩前动议，以审判地不当及缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回投资者起诉状。法院驳回该动议。男子随后提交答辩状，否认责任。一个月后，男子申请诉状判决，主张投资者未陈述可给予救济的请求。法院是否应审理男子的诉状判决动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为男子未在答辩状中提出该问题，已放弃质疑诉状的权利。","B":"不应，因为男子未在答辩前动议中提出该问题，已放弃质疑诉状的权利。","C":"应当，因为男子质疑诉状的权利尚未被放弃。","D":"应当，因为质疑诉状的权利永远不会被放弃。"},"explanationZh":"大多数抗辩若未在被告答辩状中提出，即被放弃。* 但是，被告可在审判结束前提出未合并必要当事人或未陈述可给予救济的请求抗辩，本题男子在诉状判决动议中提出的正是后者。由于动议在审判结束前提交，男子尚未放弃提出该质疑的权利（选项 A）。因此，法院应审理其动议。\n\n*联邦法院通常允许被告在审判中和审判后修改原答辩状，加入此前未提出的抗辩。\n\n（选项 B）缺乏对人管辖权、审判地不当，以及诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分，是仅有的若未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出即被放弃的质疑。\n\n（选项 D）缺乏事项管辖权是唯一永不放弃的抗辩，其他所有抗辩均可能被放弃。\n\n学习目标：抗辩若未在被告答辩状中提出，通常被放弃。但未合并必要当事人和未陈述可给予救济的请求，可以在审判结束前任何时间提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)(2)（未陈述请求抗辩的放弃）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-018"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-119","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":119,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A woman domiciled in State A was driving in State B when the brakes on her car malfunctioned. As a result, she lost control of her car and hit a concrete traffic barrier. The woman later brought a strict products liability action against the brake manufacturer in a federal court in State A to recover $100,000 in damages. The manufacturer is incorporated and headquartered in State B. The manufacturer conducts no business and has no employees in State A. The manufacturer filed a motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process, which was denied. The manufacturer has now filed an answer to the woman's complaint and asserted therein a motion to dismiss for improper venue. The woman failed to respond to the motion. Is the court likely to dismiss the action for improper venue?","choices":{"A":"No, because the manufacturer waived its right to challenge venue.","B":"No, unless the court determines that a federal court in State B is a more convenient forum under the doctrine of forum non conveniens.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer resides in State B and the accident occurred there.","D":"Yes, because the woman failed to respond to the manufacturer's motion to dismiss."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Waiver of defenses\n\nDefenses Waiver\n\n• Lack of subject-matter Never waived jurisdiction\n• Lack of personal jurisdiction Waived if not asserted in pre-answer motion or\n• Improper venue answer (whichever occurs first)\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim Waived if not asserted before end of trial\n• Failure to join required party\n• All other legal defenses Waived if not asserted in answer or amended answer\n\nThe following defenses are waived if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first: (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process. Here, the manufacturer filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process, but that motion failed to assert the defense of improper venue. The manufacturer therefore waived its right to challenge this issue, and the court is unlikely to dismiss the action for improper venue. Had the manufacturer preserved its right to challenge venue, the court would have applied the general venue statute to determine if venue is proper in State A. Under this statute, venue is proper in any judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state (here, the manufacturer resides in State B)*\n\n• a substantial part of the events giving rise to the claim occurred or a substantial portion of the property at issue is located (here, the accident occurred in State B) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction, but only if venue cannot be established under either of the above provisions (not applicable here).\n\nTherefore, venue is proper in State B but improper in State A. However, the manufacturer waived its right to challenge venue by failing to do so in its pre-answer motion (Choice C). *An entity resides in any judicial district where it is subject to personal jurisdiction. As a result, the manufacturer resides in State B because it is incorporated and headquartered\n\nthere. But it does not reside in State A because it conducts no business and has no employees there. (Choice B) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a case if a state or foreign judicial system, not another federal court, is better suited to hear the dispute. (Choice D) The woman had no duty to respond to the manufacturer's answer containing the motion to dismiss for improper venue. That is because a plaintiff need only respond to a defendant's answer if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or the court orders a reply (neither seen here). Educational objective: The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h) (waiver of defenses).","rule":"The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first.","irac":{"issue":"A woman domiciled in State A was driving in State B when the brakes on her car malfunctioned. As a result, she lost control of her car and hit a concrete traffic barrier. The woman later brought a strict products liability action against the brake manufacturer in a federal court in State A to recover $100,000 in damages. The manufacturer is incorporated and headquartered in State B. The manufacturer conducts no business and has no employees in State A. The manufacturer filed a motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process, which was denied. The manufacturer has now filed an answer to the woman's complaint and asserted therein a motion to dismiss for improper venue. The woman failed to respond to the motion. Is the court likely to dismiss the action for improper venue?","rule":"The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first.","analysis":"Waiver of defenses\n\nDefenses Waiver\n\n• Lack of subject-matter Never waived jurisdiction\n• Lack of personal jurisdiction Waived if not asserted in pre-answer motion or\n• Improper venue answer (whichever occurs first)\n• Insufficient process or service of process\n• Failure to state claim Waived if not asserted before end of trial\n• Failure to join required party\n• All other legal defenses Waived if not asserted in answer or amended answer\n\nThe following defenses are waived if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first: (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process. Here, the manufacturer filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss for insufficient service of process, but that motion failed to assert the defense of improper venue. The manufacturer therefore waived its right to challenge this issue, and the court is unlikely to dismiss the action for improper venue. Had the manufacturer preserved its right to challenge venue, the court would have applied the general venue statute to determine if venue is proper in State A. Under this statute, venue is proper in any judicial district where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state (here, the manufacturer resides in State B)*\n\n• a substantial part of the events giving rise to the claim occurred or a substantial portion of the property at issue is located (here, the accident occurred in State B) or\n\n• any defendant is subject to personal jurisdiction, but only if venue cannot be established under either of the above provisions (not applicable here).\n\nTherefore, venue is proper in State B but improper in State A. However, the manufacturer waived its right to challenge venue by failing to do so in its pre-answer motion (Choice C). *An entity resides in any judicial district where it is subject to personal jurisdiction. As a result, the manufacturer resides in State B because it is incorporated and headquartered\n\nthere. But it does not reside in State A because it conducts no business and has no employees there. (Choice B) The doctrine of forum non conveniens allows a federal court to dismiss or stay a case if a state or foreign judicial system, not another federal court, is better suited to hear the dispute. (Choice D) The woman had no duty to respond to the manufacturer's answer containing the motion to dismiss for improper venue. That is because a plaintiff need only respond to a defendant's answer if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or the court orders a reply (neither seen here). Educational objective: The defenses of (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process are waived if they are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or answer, whichever occurs first. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h) (waiver of defenses).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h) (waiver of defenses).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[360],"sourceAnswerPages":[361,362],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名以 A 州为住所的女子在 B 州驾车时，汽车制动器发生故障，导致她失去控制并撞上混凝土交通护栏。女子后来在 A 州联邦法院对制动器制造商提起严格产品责任诉讼，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。制造商在 B 州注册成立，总部也位于 B 州；制造商不在 A 州开展业务，也无雇员。制造商以诉讼文书送达不充分为由申请驳回，法院予以驳回。制造商现已提交答辩状，并在其中以审判地不当为由提出驳回动议。女子未回应动议。法院可能以审判地不当为由驳回诉讼吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为制造商已放弃质疑审判地的权利。","B":"不会，除非法院依据不方便法院原则认定 B 州联邦法院是更便利的法院。","C":"会，因为制造商居住于 B 州，事故也发生在那里。","D":"会，因为女子未回应制造商的驳回动议。"},"explanationZh":"抗辩的放弃：\n\n抗辩｜放弃规则\n缺乏事项管辖权｜永不放弃\n缺乏对人管辖权；审判地不当；诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分｜若未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出，即被放弃\n未陈述请求；未合并必要当事人｜若未在审判结束前提出，即被放弃\n其他所有法律抗辩｜若未在答辩状或修改后的答辩状中提出，即被放弃\n\n以下抗辩若未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出，即被放弃：(1) 缺乏对人管辖权；(2) 审判地不当；(3) 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分。\n\n本题中，制造商提交答辩前动议，以诉讼文书送达不充分为由申请驳回，但该动议没有提出审判地不当抗辩。因此，制造商已放弃质疑审判地的权利，法院不太可能以审判地不当为由驳回诉讼。\n\n如果制造商保留了质疑审判地的权利，法院会适用一般审判地法律判断 A 州审判地是否适当。依据该法，下列任何司法地区均为适当审判地：\n• 任何被告居住的地区，但全部被告须居住于同一州；本题制造商居住于 B 州*；\n• 引发请求的大部分事件发生地，或争议财产重大部分所在地；本题事故发生于 B 州；或\n• 任何被告受该法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在前述规定均不能建立审判地时适用，本题不适用。\n\n因此，B 州审判地适当，而 A 州不当。但是，制造商未在答辩前动议中提出，已经放弃质疑审判地（选项 C）。\n\n*实体在其受对人管辖权约束的任何司法地区均视为居民。因此，制造商在 B 州注册成立并设总部，居住于 B 州；但其不在 A 州开展业务且没有雇员，所以不居住于 A 州。\n\n（选项 B）不方便法院原则允许联邦法院在州或外国司法制度——而非另一联邦法院——更适合审理争议时，驳回或中止案件。\n\n（选项 D）制造商答辩状含审判地不当驳回动议，女子没有义务回应。原告只有在被告提出反诉或法院命令答复时才须回应答辩状，本题均没有。\n\n学习目标：缺乏对人管辖权、审判地不当，以及诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分，若未在答辩前动议或答辩状——以先发生者为准——中提出，即被放弃。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(h)（抗辩的放弃）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-120","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":120,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in federal district court to determine liability in a fatal auto accident. During jury deliberations, a juror brought a highway manual that he had found at the local library into the jury room. The manual listed the skid mark distances created when a vehicle brakes at various speeds. The jurors used the manual's data to analyze the admitted photographic evidence of the skid marks from the accident scene. They determined that the plaintiff must have been traveling at a recklessly high speed, which caused the accident. Based on the data from the highway manual, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant. The judge presiding over the case entered a final judgment consistent with that verdict on the same day. The plaintiff made no posttrial motions and did not file a notice of appeal. Sixty days after the court entered final judgment, one of the jurors went to the presiding judge's chambers and told her about the highway manual. The juror then told the judge that the data from the highway manual was the primary reason why the jury found in favor of the defendant. The judge was concerned about this revelation and asked her clerk to research whether the court could order a new trial. After extensive research, the clerk determined that the judge may not order a new trial sua sponte in the action. What is the most likely reason the clerk determined that the judge may not order a new trial sua sponte?","choices":{"A":"A court may not order a new trial sua sponte.","B":"A court may not order a new trial sua sponte unless a party engaged in misconduct.","C":"A court may not order a new trial sua sponte until an appeal from the judgment is filed.","D":"A court may not order a new trial sua sponte when more than 28 days have passed since the entry of final judgment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment\n\nNew trial • Prejudicial trial error\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment basis for judgment\n• Intervening change in controlling law\n• Newly discovered evidence Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief excusable neglect final judgment\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief\n\nA court may order a new trial on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) only under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 59 (Choice A). A court may do so on any ground that has previously provided a basis for a new trial in federal court. One such ground is when a juror (or a judge, attorney, or party) engaged in prejudicial misconduct. Here, prejudicial misconduct occurred when a juror brought in, and the jury based its verdict on, evidence outside the record—the highway manual. However, FRCP 59 requires that a court's sua sponte order (or a party's motion) for a new trial be filed within 28 days after entry of the final judgment. Since 60 days have passed since final judgment was entered, this is the most likely reason the judge may not order a new trial sua sponte. However, a party may still seek relief from final judgment under FRCP 60 if extraordinary circumstances exist and a motion for such relief is made within a reasonable time after the entry of final judgment. Here, the discovery of prejudicial juror misconduct after the deadline for the court to order a new trial had passed would warrant such relief, and the plaintiff may still timely file this motion. Therefore, the plaintiff is not without a remedy. (Choice B) A court may order a new trial on several grounds—including, but not limited to, when a judge, attorney, party, or juror engaged in prejudicial misconduct. (Choice C) A court may order a new trial sua sponte before a notice of appeal is filed so long as the order was filed within 28 days after the entry of final judgment. Educational objective: A party may move for a new trial—or a court may order a new trial on its own initiative— within 28 days after the entry of final judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (explaining that a court may order a new trial sua sponte within 28 days after a final judgment is entered).\n\n• Montgomery Ward & Co. v. Duncan, 311 U.S. 243, 251 (1940) (articulating grounds for a new trial).","rule":"A party may move for a new trial—or a court may order a new trial on its own initiative— within 28 days after the entry of final judgment.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in federal district court to determine liability in a fatal auto accident. During jury deliberations, a juror brought a highway manual that he had found at the local library into the jury room. The manual listed the skid mark distances created when a vehicle brakes at various speeds. The jurors used the manual's data to analyze the admitted photographic evidence of the skid marks from the accident scene. They determined that the plaintiff must have been traveling at a recklessly high speed, which caused the accident. Based on the data from the highway manual, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant. The judge presiding over the case entered a final judgment consistent with that verdict on the same day. The plaintiff made no posttrial motions and did not file a notice of appeal. Sixty days after the court entered final judgment, one of the jurors went to the presiding judge's chambers and told her about the highway manual. The juror then told the judge that the data from the highway manual was the primary reason why the jury found in favor of the defendant. The judge was concerned about this revelation and asked her clerk to research whether the court could order a new trial. After extensive research, the clerk determined that the judge may not order a new trial sua sponte in the action. What is the most likely reason the clerk determined that the judge may not order a new trial sua sponte?","rule":"A party may move for a new trial—or a court may order a new trial on its own initiative— within 28 days after the entry of final judgment.","analysis":"Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment\n\nNew trial • Prejudicial trial error\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment basis for judgment\n• Intervening change in controlling law\n• Newly discovered evidence Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief excusable neglect final judgment\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief\n\nA court may order a new trial on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) only under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 59 (Choice A). A court may do so on any ground that has previously provided a basis for a new trial in federal court. One such ground is when a juror (or a judge, attorney, or party) engaged in prejudicial misconduct. Here, prejudicial misconduct occurred when a juror brought in, and the jury based its verdict on, evidence outside the record—the highway manual. However, FRCP 59 requires that a court's sua sponte order (or a party's motion) for a new trial be filed within 28 days after entry of the final judgment. Since 60 days have passed since final judgment was entered, this is the most likely reason the judge may not order a new trial sua sponte. However, a party may still seek relief from final judgment under FRCP 60 if extraordinary circumstances exist and a motion for such relief is made within a reasonable time after the entry of final judgment. Here, the discovery of prejudicial juror misconduct after the deadline for the court to order a new trial had passed would warrant such relief, and the plaintiff may still timely file this motion. Therefore, the plaintiff is not without a remedy. (Choice B) A court may order a new trial on several grounds—including, but not limited to, when a judge, attorney, party, or juror engaged in prejudicial misconduct. (Choice C) A court may order a new trial sua sponte before a notice of appeal is filed so long as the order was filed within 28 days after the entry of final judgment. Educational objective: A party may move for a new trial—or a court may order a new trial on its own initiative— within 28 days after the entry of final judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (explaining that a court may order a new trial sua sponte within 28 days after a final judgment is entered).\n\n• Montgomery Ward & Co. v. Duncan, 311 U.S. 243, 251 (1940) (articulating grounds for a new trial).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (explaining that a court may order a new trial sua sponte within 28 days after a final judgment is entered).\n\n• Montgomery Ward & Co. v. Duncan, 311 U.S. 243, 251 (1940) (articulating grounds for a new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[363],"sourceAnswerPages":[364,365],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院对被告提起多元州籍诉讼，请求确定一起致命汽车事故的责任。陪审团评议期间，一名陪审员把从当地图书馆找到的公路手册带入陪审团室。手册列出车辆以不同速度制动时形成的刹车痕距离。陪审员利用手册数据分析已采纳的事故现场刹车痕照片证据，并认定原告一定以鲁莽的高速行驶，从而造成事故。陪审团依据公路手册数据作出有利于被告的裁决。主审法官同日依该裁决登记终局判决。原告未提出审判后动议，也未提交上诉通知。法院登记终局判决 60 天后，一名陪审员前往主审法官办公室，向其说明公路手册一事，并称手册数据是陪审团判被告胜诉的首要原因。法官对此感到担忧，让书记员研究法院能否命令重新审判。经广泛研究，书记员认定法官不得依职权命令重新审判。书记员得出这一结论的最可能理由是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院不得依职权命令重新审判。","B":"除非一方实施不当行为，否则法院不得依职权命令重新审判。","C":"在对判决提起上诉前，法院不得依职权命令重新审判。","D":"登记终局判决超过 28 天后，法院不得依职权命令重新审判。"},"explanationZh":"审判后救济：\n\n救济｜理由｜时限\n律师费｜法律或规则允许获得律师费｜登记终局判决后 14 天内\n依法判决｜证据在法律上不足以支持非动议方胜诉｜登记终局判决后 28 天内\n重新审判｜造成不利影响的审判错误；法官、律师、当事人或陪审员造成不利影响的不当行为；裁决不受证据明显权重支持；裁决基于虚假或不存在的证据；损害赔偿过高或不足；新发现证据｜登记终局判决后 28 天内\n修改判决｜构成判决基础的明显法律或事实错误；控制性法律在此期间发生变化；新发现证据｜登记终局判决后 28 天内\n更正错误｜书记性错误；因疏忽或遗漏产生的错误｜可自由更正；上诉列入日程后须经上诉法院许可\n特别救济｜错误、无意、意外、可原谅的疏忽；新发现证据；对方欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为｜登记终局判决后一年内\n特别救济｜无效判决；判决已履行、解除或撤销；其他足以给予救济的理由｜合理时间内\n\n法院只有依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）59，才可以依职权命令重新审判（选项 A）。此前曾在联邦法院构成重新审判依据的任何理由，法院均可据此命令。其中一种理由是陪审员（或法官、律师、当事人）实施造成不利影响的不当行为。\n\n本题中，一名陪审员将案卷以外的证据——公路手册——带入，陪审团还以其作为裁决依据，构成造成不利影响的不当行为。但是，FRCP 59 要求法院依职权作出的重新审判命令（或当事人的重新审判动议）必须在登记终局判决后 28 天内提出。终局判决登记后已经过 60 天，所以这最可能是法官不得依职权命令重新审判的理由。\n\n不过，若存在特别情形，且在登记终局判决后的合理时间内提出救济动议，当事人仍可依据 FRCP 60 请求从终局判决获得救济。本题在法院命令重新审判期限届满后才发现造成不利影响的陪审员不当行为，足以支持此类救济；原告仍可及时提出动议。因此，原告并非没有救济。\n\n（选项 B）法院可基于多种理由命令重新审判，包括但不限于法官、律师、当事人或陪审员实施造成不利影响的不当行为。\n\n（选项 C）只要命令在登记终局判决后 28 天内提出，法院可以在提交上诉通知前依职权命令重新审判。\n\n学习目标：当事人可以申请重新审判，法院也可以依职权命令重新审判，但必须在登记终局判决后 28 天内。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（说明法院可在登记终局判决后 28 天内依职权命令重新审判）。\n• Montgomery Ward & Co. v. Duncan, 311 U.S. 243, 251 (1940)（阐述重新审判理由）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-121","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":121,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A recently enacted federal statute allows individuals to sue medical providers for fraudulent billing practices. A woman sued a hospital in a federal district court for allegedly violating this statute. The hospital did not challenge the woman's factual assertions but argued that its conduct was not \"fraudulent\" as defined by the statute. After a bench trial, the district court issued its findings of fact and conclusions of law. The findings explained what the court believed to be the correct definition of \"fraudulent\" under the statute and concluded that the hospital's billing practices did not meet this definition. A final judgment in favor of the hospital was entered the same day. Twenty-one days later, a federal appellate court, whose boundaries include the district court, issued an opinion in a different case that involved the same federal statute. In its opinion, the appellate court defined the term \"fraudulent\" differently than the district court. The appellate court's definition would have allowed the woman to prevail in her suit. What is the most efficient action the woman can take to challenge the final judgment issued by the district court?","choices":{"A":"File a motion for a new trial.","B":"File a motion to alter or amend the judgment.","C":"File a motion to correct the mistaken judgment.","D":"File a petition for writ of mandamus."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment\n\nNew trial • Prejudicial trial error\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment basis for judgment\n• Intervening change in controlling law\n• Newly discovered evidence Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief excusable neglect final judgment\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief\n\nUnder FRCP 59(e), a party can file a motion to alter or amend a final judgment within 28 days after entry of the judgment when:\n• the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact\n• the alteration or amendment is necessary to prevent manifest injustice\n• new evidence that was unavailable during trial has been discovered or\n• there has been an intervening change in the controlling law. Appellate court decisions are binding on all district courts within the appellate court's boundaries. As a result, an intervening change in the controlling law occurs when an appellate court issues a decision that contradicts its district court's interpretation of the law. Here, an intervening change in the controlling law occurred when the appellate court issued an opinion that defined \"fraudulent\" differently than the district court. Under the appellate court's definition, the woman would have prevailed in her suit. Therefore, the most efficient way to challenge the final judgment is to file a motion with the district court to alter or amend its judgment in accordance with the appellate court's definition. (Choice A) Challenging the district court's definition of \"fraudulent\" by filing a motion for a new trial would not be the most efficient way to challenge the final judgment since a new trial would likely be expensive and time-consuming. (Choice C) A motion to correct a mistaken judgment is appropriate when that judgment contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission (not seen here). (Choice D) A petition for writ of mandamus is a rare procedure that allows a party to seek appellate review before a final judgment is issued and only when no other remedy (eg, alter or amend judgment) is available. Educational objective: A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment within 28 days after the judgment is entered when (1) the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact, (2) it is necessary to prevent manifest injustice, (3) new evidence has been discovered, or (4) an intervening change in the controlling law has occurred. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to alter or amend a judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2019) (explaining the grounds for a motion to alter or amend a final judgment).","rule":"A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment within 28 days after the judgment is entered when (1) the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact, (2) it is necessary to prevent manifest injustice, (3) new evidence has been discovered, or (4) an intervening change in the controlling law has occurred. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A recently enacted federal statute allows individuals to sue medical providers for fraudulent billing practices. A woman sued a hospital in a federal district court for allegedly violating this statute. The hospital did not challenge the woman's factual assertions but argued that its conduct was not \"fraudulent\" as defined by the statute. After a bench trial, the district court issued its findings of fact and conclusions of law. The findings explained what the court believed to be the correct definition of \"fraudulent\" under the statute and concluded that the hospital's billing practices did not meet this definition. A final judgment in favor of the hospital was entered the same day. Twenty-one days later, a federal appellate court, whose boundaries include the district court, issued an opinion in a different case that involved the same federal statute. In its opinion, the appellate court defined the term \"fraudulent\" differently than the district court. The appellate court's definition would have allowed the woman to prevail in her suit. What is the most efficient action the woman can take to challenge the final judgment issued by the district court?","rule":"A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment within 28 days after the judgment is entered when (1) the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact, (2) it is necessary to prevent manifest injustice, (3) new evidence has been discovered, or (4) an intervening change in the controlling law has occurred. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"Posttrial relief\n\nRelief Grounds Timing\n\nAttorney's fees • Statute or rule allows recovery ≤ 14 days after entry of final judgment\n\nJudgment as • Evidence legally insufficient to ≤ 28 days after entry of matter of law find for nonmovant final judgment\n\nNew trial • Prejudicial trial error\n• Prejudicial misconduct by judge, attorney, party, juror\n• Verdict not supported by clear weight of evidence\n• Verdict based on false/nonexistent evidence\n• Excessive or inadequate damages\n• Newly discovered evidence Alter/amend • Manifest error of law or fact is judgment basis for judgment\n• Intervening change in controlling law\n• Newly discovered evidence Correction of • Clerical mistake Freely OR with appellate mistake • Mistake arising from oversight court's leave if docketed or omission Extraordinary • Mistake, inadvertence, surprise, ≤ 1 year after entry of relief excusable neglect final judgment\n• Newly discovered evidence\n• Fraud, misrepresentation, misconduct by opposing party\n• Void judgment Within reasonable time\n• Judgment satisfied, released, discharged\n• Other reason justifying relief\n\nUnder FRCP 59(e), a party can file a motion to alter or amend a final judgment within 28 days after entry of the judgment when:\n• the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact\n• the alteration or amendment is necessary to prevent manifest injustice\n• new evidence that was unavailable during trial has been discovered or\n• there has been an intervening change in the controlling law. Appellate court decisions are binding on all district courts within the appellate court's boundaries. As a result, an intervening change in the controlling law occurs when an appellate court issues a decision that contradicts its district court's interpretation of the law. Here, an intervening change in the controlling law occurred when the appellate court issued an opinion that defined \"fraudulent\" differently than the district court. Under the appellate court's definition, the woman would have prevailed in her suit. Therefore, the most efficient way to challenge the final judgment is to file a motion with the district court to alter or amend its judgment in accordance with the appellate court's definition. (Choice A) Challenging the district court's definition of \"fraudulent\" by filing a motion for a new trial would not be the most efficient way to challenge the final judgment since a new trial would likely be expensive and time-consuming. (Choice C) A motion to correct a mistaken judgment is appropriate when that judgment contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission (not seen here). (Choice D) A petition for writ of mandamus is a rare procedure that allows a party to seek appellate review before a final judgment is issued and only when no other remedy (eg, alter or amend judgment) is available. Educational objective: A party can move to alter or amend a final judgment within 28 days after the judgment is entered when (1) the judgment is based on a manifest error of law or fact, (2) it is necessary to prevent manifest injustice, (3) new evidence has been discovered, or (4) an intervening change in the controlling law has occurred. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to alter or amend a judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2019) (explaining the grounds for a motion to alter or amend a final judgment).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to alter or amend a judgment).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2019) (explaining the grounds for a motion to alter or amend a final judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[366],"sourceAnswerPages":[367,368],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一项新近制定的联邦法律允许个人就欺诈性收费行为起诉医疗服务提供者。一名女子在联邦地区法院起诉一家医院涉嫌违反该法。医院不争议女子的事实主张，但认为其行为不属于该法所定义的“欺诈”。法官审判后，地区法院作出事实认定与法律结论，说明其认为该法中“欺诈”的正确定义，并认定医院收费行为不符合该定义。同日登记有利于医院的终局判决。21 天后，管辖该地区法院的联邦上诉法院在涉及同一联邦法律的另一案件中发表判决，以不同方式定义“欺诈”；按照上诉法院定义，女子本可胜诉。女子质疑地区法院终局判决的最高效行动是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请重新审判。","B":"申请修改判决。","C":"申请更正错误判决。","D":"申请强制令。"},"explanationZh":"审判后救济：\n\n救济｜理由｜时限\n律师费｜法律或规则允许获得律师费｜终局判决后 14 天内\n依法判决｜证据在法律上不足以支持非动议方胜诉｜终局判决后 28 天内\n重新审判｜造成不利影响的审判错误或不当行为；裁决不受证据明显权重支持；裁决基于虚假／不存在的证据；赔偿过高或不足；新发现证据｜终局判决后 28 天内\n修改判决｜明显法律或事实错误；控制性法律在此期间发生变化；新发现证据｜终局判决后 28 天内\n更正错误｜书记性错误；疏忽或遗漏｜可自由更正；上诉列入日程后须经上诉法院许可\n特别救济｜错误、无意、意外、可原谅疏忽；新发现证据；对方欺诈、虚假陈述或不当行为｜终局判决后一年内\n特别救济｜无效判决；判决已履行、解除或撤销；其他足以给予救济的理由｜合理时间内\n\n依据 FRCP 59(e)，当事人可在登记终局判决后 28 天内，基于以下理由申请修改判决：\n• 判决基于明显法律或事实错误；\n• 为防止明显不公必须修改；\n• 发现审判期间无法取得的新证据；或\n• 控制性法律在此期间发生变化。\n\n上诉法院决定对其辖区内所有地区法院具有约束力。因此，上诉法院作出与地区法院法律解释相冲突的决定，即构成控制性法律在此期间发生变化。\n\n本题中，上诉法院对“欺诈”的定义不同于地区法院，且按该定义女子本可胜诉。因此，最高效方式是请求地区法院依上诉法院定义修改判决。\n\n（选项 A）通过重新审判质疑地区法院对“欺诈”的定义效率较低，因为重新审判成本高且耗时。\n\n（选项 C）判决含书记性错误或因疏忽、遗漏产生错误时，才适合更正错误动议；本题没有。\n\n（选项 D）强制令是一种罕见程序，仅在终局判决作出前且没有其他救济时允许寻求上诉审查。本题可以申请修改判决。\n\n学习目标：当事人可在登记终局判决后 28 天内申请修改判决，理由包括：(1) 明显法律或事实错误；(2) 防止明显不公；(3) 新发现证据；或 (4) 控制性法律在此期间发生变化。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e)（修改判决动议）。\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 636 (2019)（解释修改终局判决的理由）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-122","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":122,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A store owner from State A had a dispute with its supplier incorporated and headquartered in State B over the quality of the produce the supplier had delivered to the store. The store owner sued the supplier in a federal court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $200,000 in damages. The supplier filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the case based solely on lack of personal jurisdiction, which the court denied. The supplier now seeks to assert that the federal court is an improper venue and that the store owner failed to state a claim on which relief can be granted. Which of the following statements best describes the effect of the supplier's pre-answer motion on these two defenses?","choices":{"A":"It has no effect on the supplier's \"improper venue\" or \"failure to state a claim\" defense.","B":"It resulted in a waiver of the supplier's \"failure to state a claim\" defense.","C":"It resulted in a waiver of the supplier's \"improper venue\" defense.","D":"It resulted in a waiver of the supplier's \"improper venue\" and \"failure to state a claim\" defenses."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A defendant must respond to a plaintiff's complaint by filing an answer or a pre-answer motion. When the defendant files a pre-answer motion, the omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single motion for efficiency purposes. This means that the defendant cannot file a subsequent pre-answer motion raising an omitted FRCP 12 defense—except for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction*—that was available when the initial motion was filed. Because of the omnibus motion rule, the following FRCP 12 defenses are waived and cannot be asserted later if omitted from a pre-answer motion: (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process. Here, the supplier's pre-answer motion failed to include the \"improper venue\" defense, so this defense was waived (Choice A). However, the following FRCP 12 defenses may still be later asserted in an answer or post-answer motion if omitted from a pre-answer motion: (1) failure to state a claim, (2) failure to state a legal defense, and (3) failure to join a required party. As a result, the supplier's omission of its \"failure to state a claim\" defense from the pre-answer motion did not waive this defense. It may still be asserted in the supplier's answer or in a post-answer motion (Choices B & D). *Lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and may be asserted at any time, including in a subsequent pre-answer motion. Educational objective: The omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single pre-answer motion. Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and insufficient process or service of process are waived if omitted. But failure to state a claim, state a legal defense, or join a required party may still be asserted in an answer or post-answer motion. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(g)–(h) (consolidating and waiving pre-answer defenses).\n\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1385 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the omnibus motion rule).","rule":"The omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single pre-answer motion. Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and insufficient process or service of process are waived if omitted. But failure to state a claim, state a legal defense, or join a required party may still be asserted in an answer or post-answer motion.","irac":{"issue":"A store owner from State A had a dispute with its supplier incorporated and headquartered in State B over the quality of the produce the supplier had delivered to the store. The store owner sued the supplier in a federal court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $200,000 in damages. The supplier filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the case based solely on lack of personal jurisdiction, which the court denied. The supplier now seeks to assert that the federal court is an improper venue and that the store owner failed to state a claim on which relief can be granted. Which of the following statements best describes the effect of the supplier's pre-answer motion on these two defenses?","rule":"The omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single pre-answer motion. Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and insufficient process or service of process are waived if omitted. But failure to state a claim, state a legal defense, or join a required party may still be asserted in an answer or post-answer motion.","analysis":"A defendant must respond to a plaintiff's complaint by filing an answer or a pre-answer motion. When the defendant files a pre-answer motion, the omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single motion for efficiency purposes. This means that the defendant cannot file a subsequent pre-answer motion raising an omitted FRCP 12 defense—except for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction*—that was available when the initial motion was filed. Because of the omnibus motion rule, the following FRCP 12 defenses are waived and cannot be asserted later if omitted from a pre-answer motion: (1) lack of personal jurisdiction, (2) improper venue, and (3) insufficient process or service of process. Here, the supplier's pre-answer motion failed to include the \"improper venue\" defense, so this defense was waived (Choice A). However, the following FRCP 12 defenses may still be later asserted in an answer or post-answer motion if omitted from a pre-answer motion: (1) failure to state a claim, (2) failure to state a legal defense, and (3) failure to join a required party. As a result, the supplier's omission of its \"failure to state a claim\" defense from the pre-answer motion did not waive this defense. It may still be asserted in the supplier's answer or in a post-answer motion (Choices B & D). *Lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived and may be asserted at any time, including in a subsequent pre-answer motion. Educational objective: The omnibus motion rule requires that all FRCP 12 defenses be consolidated in a single pre-answer motion. Lack of personal jurisdiction, improper venue, and insufficient process or service of process are waived if omitted. But failure to state a claim, state a legal defense, or join a required party may still be asserted in an answer or post-answer motion. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(g)–(h) (consolidating and waiving pre-answer defenses).\n\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1385 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the omnibus motion rule).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(g)–(h) (consolidating and waiving pre-answer defenses).\n\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1385 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the omnibus motion rule).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[370],"sourceAnswerPages":[371],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一名商店店主与一家在 B 州注册成立并设总部的供应商，就供应商交付产品的质量发生争议。店主在 A 州联邦法院以违约为由起诉供应商，请求 200,000 美元损害赔偿。供应商仅以缺乏对人管辖权为由提交答辩前驳回动议，法院予以驳回。供应商现欲主张联邦法院审判地不当，且店主未陈述可给予救济的请求。以下哪项最准确说明供应商答辩前动议对这两项抗辩的影响？","choicesZh":{"A":"对“审判地不当”和“未陈述请求”抗辩均无影响。","B":"导致供应商放弃“未陈述请求”抗辩。","C":"导致供应商放弃“审判地不当”抗辩。","D":"导致供应商同时放弃“审判地不当”和“未陈述请求”抗辩。"},"explanationZh":"被告必须通过答辩状或答辩前动议回应原告起诉状。被告提出答辩前动议时，综合动议规则要求为提高效率，将所有 FRCP 12 抗辩合并在一个动议中。这意味着，被告不能在之后的答辩前动议中提出初次动议时已可提出却遗漏的 FRCP 12 抗辩；缺乏事项管辖权除外。*\n\n依据综合动议规则，以下抗辩若从答辩前动议中遗漏，即被放弃且之后不得提出：(1) 缺乏对人管辖权；(2) 审判地不当；(3) 诉讼文书或诉讼文书送达不充分。\n\n本题供应商的答辩前动议未包含“审判地不当”抗辩，故该抗辩被放弃（选项 A）。\n\n但是，以下 FRCP 12 抗辩即使从答辩前动议中遗漏，仍可之后在答辩状或答辩后动议中提出：(1) 未陈述请求；(2) 未陈述法律抗辩；(3) 未合并必要当事人。因此，供应商没有因遗漏而放弃“未陈述请求”抗辩，仍可在答辩状或答辩后动议中提出（选项 B、D）。\n\n*缺乏事项管辖权永不放弃，可随时提出，包括在后续答辩前动议中。\n\n学习目标：综合动议规则要求将所有 FRCP 12 抗辩合并在单一答辩前动议中。遗漏缺乏对人管辖权、审判地不当，以及诉讼文书或送达不充分，即属放弃；但未陈述请求、未陈述法律抗辩或未合并必要当事人，仍可在答辩状或答辩后动议中提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(g)–(h)（答辩前抗辩的合并与放弃）。\n• 5C Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1385 (3d ed. 2020)（解释综合动议规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-034"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-123","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":123,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A gas station sued a supplier for breach of contract in an appropriate federal district court located in State A. The gas station alleged that it entered into a contract with the supplier for emergency gasoline delivery as a hurricane approached, but the supplier failed to deliver the gas. The gas station claimed that it lost $100,000 in profits as a result of the supplier's failure to deliver. In its defense, the supplier asserted that the time frame specified in the contract was ambiguous, argued that the state of emergency excused its nondelivery, and disputed the amount of damages. The gas station maintains that the state of emergency is no defense because the contract was for an emergency delivery. The parties submitted cross-motions for partial summary judgment on the issue of whether the state of emergency excused the supplier's failure to meet the delivery deadline. This is an issue of first impression under the law of State A, which controls this contract. The judge ruled that the state of emergency did not excuse the supplier's failure to meet the delivery deadline. As a result, the judge granted the gas station's motion for partial summary judgment, denied the supplier's motion, and set discovery and trial dates. The supplier immediately appealed the decision to the appropriate federal appellate court. What is the proper response by the federal appellate court?","choices":{"A":"Certify the novel legal issue to the highest court of State A.","B":"Dismiss the appeal for lack of jurisdiction.","C":"Mandate that the district court judge certify the issue for appeal.","D":"Reverse the district court judge's order."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment The final-judgment rule is a jurisdictional requirement that bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment. A final judgment fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. Therefore, absent limited exceptions, an appellate court must dismiss an appeal for lack of jurisdiction if no final judgment has been entered. This promotes judicial economy and avoids undue delay caused by multiple appeals. Here, the federal district court's ruling on the motions for partial summary judgment addressed whether the state of emergency excused the supplier's failure to meet the delivery deadline. But it did not fully resolve the dispute since the ambiguity of the contract terms and the amount of damages were not decided. As a result, the final-judgment rule requires that the appellate court dismiss the appeal for lack of jurisdiction. (Choice A) Most states allow federal courts to certify (ie, request to assign) an unsettled issue of state law to the state's highest court, even if the decision would be an advisory opinion. But this cannot be done when, as here, the federal court lacks jurisdiction over the dispute.\n\n(Choice C) A district court can certify (ie, approve) an issue for appeal before a final judgment when (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal will materially advance the ultimate termination of litigation. But an appellate court has no power to mandate that a district court do so. (Choice D) The appellate court cannot reverse the district judge's order because the appellate court lacks jurisdiction. Educational objective: Under the final-judgment rule, a federal appellate court generally has no jurisdiction to hear an appeal until the district court has issued a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (exceptions to final judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).","rule":"Under the final-judgment rule, a federal appellate court generally has no jurisdiction to hear an appeal until the district court has issued a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits.","irac":{"issue":"A gas station sued a supplier for breach of contract in an appropriate federal district court located in State A. The gas station alleged that it entered into a contract with the supplier for emergency gasoline delivery as a hurricane approached, but the supplier failed to deliver the gas. The gas station claimed that it lost $100,000 in profits as a result of the supplier's failure to deliver. In its defense, the supplier asserted that the time frame specified in the contract was ambiguous, argued that the state of emergency excused its nondelivery, and disputed the amount of damages. The gas station maintains that the state of emergency is no defense because the contract was for an emergency delivery. The parties submitted cross-motions for partial summary judgment on the issue of whether the state of emergency excused the supplier's failure to meet the delivery deadline. This is an issue of first impression under the law of State A, which controls this contract. The judge ruled that the state of emergency did not excuse the supplier's failure to meet the delivery deadline. As a result, the judge granted the gas station's motion for partial summary judgment, denied the supplier's motion, and set discovery and trial dates. The supplier immediately appealed the decision to the appropriate federal appellate court. What is the proper response by the federal appellate court?","rule":"Under the final-judgment rule, a federal appellate court generally has no jurisdiction to hear an appeal until the district court has issued a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits.","analysis":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment The final-judgment rule is a jurisdictional requirement that bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment. A final judgment fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. Therefore, absent limited exceptions, an appellate court must dismiss an appeal for lack of jurisdiction if no final judgment has been entered. This promotes judicial economy and avoids undue delay caused by multiple appeals. Here, the federal district court's ruling on the motions for partial summary judgment addressed whether the state of emergency excused the supplier's failure to meet the delivery deadline. But it did not fully resolve the dispute since the ambiguity of the contract terms and the amount of damages were not decided. As a result, the final-judgment rule requires that the appellate court dismiss the appeal for lack of jurisdiction. (Choice A) Most states allow federal courts to certify (ie, request to assign) an unsettled issue of state law to the state's highest court, even if the decision would be an advisory opinion. But this cannot be done when, as here, the federal court lacks jurisdiction over the dispute.\n\n(Choice C) A district court can certify (ie, approve) an issue for appeal before a final judgment when (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal will materially advance the ultimate termination of litigation. But an appellate court has no power to mandate that a district court do so. (Choice D) The appellate court cannot reverse the district judge's order because the appellate court lacks jurisdiction. Educational objective: Under the final-judgment rule, a federal appellate court generally has no jurisdiction to hear an appeal until the district court has issued a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (exceptions to final judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (exceptions to final judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[373],"sourceAnswerPages":[374,375],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家加油站在 A 州适当的联邦地区法院起诉供应商违约。加油站主张，飓风临近时，其与供应商订立紧急汽油交付合同，但供应商未交付，导致其损失 100,000 美元利润。供应商抗辩称合同规定的期限含糊，紧急状态免除其不交付责任，并争议损害金额。加油站认为合同本就用于紧急交付，紧急状态不构成抗辩。双方就紧急状态是否免除供应商未遵守交付期限的问题，交叉申请部分简易判决。该问题是本合同所适用的 A 州法律中的首次裁判问题。法官认定紧急状态不免除供应商未遵守期限，遂批准加油站的部分简易判决动议，驳回供应商动议，并确定证据开示及审判日期。供应商立即向适当的联邦上诉法院上诉。上诉法院应如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"将新颖法律问题提请 A 州最高法院裁定。","B":"以缺乏管辖权为由驳回上诉。","C":"命令地区法院法官批准该问题上诉。","D":"撤销地区法院法官的命令。"},"explanationZh":"终局判决规则：\n\n规则｜只有终局判决后方可上诉。涉及多项请求／当事人时，若法院明示认定没有正当理由拖延，可就少于全部请求／当事人作出可上诉终局判决。\n\n例外｜涉及以下事项的中间上诉*：批准／拒绝禁令；地区法院批准上诉；集体诉讼认证；指定接管人；海事案件；附带命令原则；特定破产命令；强制令；只剩核算的专利侵权命令。\n\n记忆法：In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely。\n\n*中间上诉是在登记终局判决前，对审判法院裁定提起的上诉。\n\n终局判决规则是一项管辖权要求，禁止联邦上诉法院在地区法院登记终局判决前审理上诉。终局判决完全解决争议实体，除执行判决外不留其他事项。因此，除有限例外外，没有终局判决时，上诉法院必须以缺乏管辖权为由驳回上诉。该规则促进司法经济，避免多次上诉造成不当拖延。\n\n本题地区法院对部分简易判决动议的裁定，仅解决紧急状态是否免除供应商未遵守交付期限；合同条款是否含糊及损害金额均未裁定，所以争议尚未完全解决。终局判决规则要求上诉法院以缺乏管辖权为由驳回上诉。\n\n（选项 A）多数州允许联邦法院把未解决的州法问题提请州最高法院裁定，即使决定会构成咨询意见。但本题联邦法院对上诉缺乏管辖权，不能这样做。\n\n（选项 C）若控制性法律问题存在重大意见分歧，且上诉将实质推进诉讼最终终结，地区法院可以批准在终局判决前上诉。但上诉法院无权命令地区法院这样做。\n\n（选项 D）上诉法院缺乏管辖权，不能撤销地区法院命令。\n\n学习目标：依据终局判决规则，在地区法院作出完全解决争议实体的终局判决前，联邦上诉法院通常没有管辖权审理上诉。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291（终局判决规则）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292（终局判决规则例外）。\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989)（定义终局判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-124","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":124,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A worker was injured when a machine he was using on the job malfunctioned. The worker brought a federal diversity action against both the machine's manufacturer and the company responsible for the machine's maintenance. At trial, the worker submitted a proposed jury instruction on negligence. The court did not accept the proposed instruction and instead gave a negligence instruction that the worker's attorney believed was less favorable and legally incorrect. The attorney did not object to the negligence instruction before it was given. The jury returned a verdict for the defendants. The worker has moved for a new trial on the ground that the court's negligence instruction was improper. What argument has the best chance of persuading the court to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"Issues of law can be raised at any time.","B":"The court's negligence instruction was incorrect and the worker's objection to it was preserved when he submitted his proposed negligence instruction.","C":"The court's negligence instruction was plain error that affected the worker's substantial rights.","D":"The need for a formal objection to a judicial ruling in order to preserve an argument has been eliminated in the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A party must generally raise a formal objection at trial to preserve and challenge an error in a post-trial motion (eg, motion for new trial) or on appeal. An error in the jury instructions is preserved if the objection was raised either:\n• at the close of evidence – by filing a written request for a proposed instruction and obtaining a definitive ruling from the court on the record or\n• before the court instructed the jury and before closing arguments – by clearly identifying, and stating the grounds for, the objection on the record. If a party failed to do so, the challenged error can only be reviewed for plain error (Choice D). Under a plain error review, a motion for new trial will be granted if the movant shows that an obvious error affected a substantial right and the fairness of judicial proceedings—eg, a jury instruction that misstated the law. Here, the worker submitted a proposed jury instruction on negligence. That proposal was not accepted by the court, but it was not definitively denied on the record (no preserved error) (Choice B). And though the worker's attorney disagreed with the court's instruction, the attorney did not object to it on the record (no preserved error). But since the attorney believed that the court's instruction was legally incorrect, the best argument to support the motion for new trial is that the instruction was plain error that affected the worker's substantial rights. (Choice A) Most issues—including issues of law—must be raised at trial before they can be raised post-trial (eg, improper jury instruction). Only certain issues can be raised at any time (eg, lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or standing). Educational objective: A party must generally raise a formal objection at trial to preserve his/her right to challenge an error in a post-trial motion or on appeal. But if a party fails to do so, the challenged error can still be reviewed for plain error—ie, an obvious error that affected a substantial right and the fairness of judicial proceedings. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51 (preserving error regarding requests for, and objections to, jury instructions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59, 61 (motion and standard for new trial).","rule":"A party must generally raise a formal objection at trial to preserve his/her right to challenge an error in a post-trial motion or on appeal. But if a party fails to do so, the challenged error can still be reviewed for plain error—ie, an obvious error that affected a substantial right and the fairness of judicial proceedings.","irac":{"issue":"A worker was injured when a machine he was using on the job malfunctioned. The worker brought a federal diversity action against both the machine's manufacturer and the company responsible for the machine's maintenance. At trial, the worker submitted a proposed jury instruction on negligence. The court did not accept the proposed instruction and instead gave a negligence instruction that the worker's attorney believed was less favorable and legally incorrect. The attorney did not object to the negligence instruction before it was given. The jury returned a verdict for the defendants. The worker has moved for a new trial on the ground that the court's negligence instruction was improper. What argument has the best chance of persuading the court to grant the motion?","rule":"A party must generally raise a formal objection at trial to preserve his/her right to challenge an error in a post-trial motion or on appeal. But if a party fails to do so, the challenged error can still be reviewed for plain error—ie, an obvious error that affected a substantial right and the fairness of judicial proceedings.","analysis":"A party must generally raise a formal objection at trial to preserve and challenge an error in a post-trial motion (eg, motion for new trial) or on appeal. An error in the jury instructions is preserved if the objection was raised either:\n• at the close of evidence – by filing a written request for a proposed instruction and obtaining a definitive ruling from the court on the record or\n• before the court instructed the jury and before closing arguments – by clearly identifying, and stating the grounds for, the objection on the record. If a party failed to do so, the challenged error can only be reviewed for plain error (Choice D). Under a plain error review, a motion for new trial will be granted if the movant shows that an obvious error affected a substantial right and the fairness of judicial proceedings—eg, a jury instruction that misstated the law. Here, the worker submitted a proposed jury instruction on negligence. That proposal was not accepted by the court, but it was not definitively denied on the record (no preserved error) (Choice B). And though the worker's attorney disagreed with the court's instruction, the attorney did not object to it on the record (no preserved error). But since the attorney believed that the court's instruction was legally incorrect, the best argument to support the motion for new trial is that the instruction was plain error that affected the worker's substantial rights. (Choice A) Most issues—including issues of law—must be raised at trial before they can be raised post-trial (eg, improper jury instruction). Only certain issues can be raised at any time (eg, lack of subject-matter jurisdiction or standing). Educational objective: A party must generally raise a formal objection at trial to preserve his/her right to challenge an error in a post-trial motion or on appeal. But if a party fails to do so, the challenged error can still be reviewed for plain error—ie, an obvious error that affected a substantial right and the fairness of judicial proceedings. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51 (preserving error regarding requests for, and objections to, jury instructions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59, 61 (motion and standard for new trial).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51 (preserving error regarding requests for, and objections to, jury instructions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59, 61 (motion and standard for new trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[376],"sourceAnswerPages":[377],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名工人因工作中使用的机器发生故障而受伤。工人在联邦法院对机器制造商和负责维修机器的公司提起多元州籍诉讼。审判中，工人提交一份拟议过失陪审团指示。法院未采纳，改用工人律师认为较为不利且法律上错误的过失指示。律师在指示作出前没有提出异议。陪审团判被告胜诉。工人以法院过失指示不当为由申请重新审判。哪项论点最有可能说服法院批准动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"法律问题可以随时提出。","B":"法院的过失指示错误，且工人提交拟议过失指示时已保留异议。","C":"法院的过失指示属于影响工人重大权利的明显错误。","D":"《联邦民事诉讼规则》已取消必须对司法裁定提出正式异议才能保留主张的要求。"},"explanationZh":"当事人通常必须在审判中提出正式异议，方可保留在审判后动议（例如重新审判）或上诉中质疑错误的权利。陪审团指示错误在下列情形下获得保留：\n• 举证结束时——提交拟议指示书面请求，并取得法院在案卷中的明确裁定；或\n• 法院指示陪审团前及结案陈词前——在案卷中清楚指明异议及理由。\n\n当事人未这样做时，受质疑错误只能按明显错误标准审查（选项 D）。依据该标准，若动议方证明明显错误影响重大权利及司法程序公平，例如陪审团指示错误陈述法律，法院会批准重新审判。\n\n本题工人提交了拟议过失指示，但法院未采纳，并未在案卷中明确拒绝，故没有保留错误（选项 B）。律师虽不同意法院指示，却未在案卷中提出异议，同样没有保留错误。但律师认为指示在法律上错误，所以支持重新审判动议的最佳论点，是该指示属于影响工人重大权利的明显错误。\n\n（选项 A）包括法律问题在内的大多数问题，必须在审判中提出，方可在审判后提出，例如不当陪审团指示。只有部分问题可随时提出，例如缺乏事项管辖权或诉讼资格。\n\n学习目标：当事人通常必须在审判中提出正式异议，方可保留在审判后动议或上诉中质疑错误的权利。但未这样做时，仍可按明显错误审查，即影响重大权利及司法程序公平的明显错误。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51（保留陪审团指示请求及异议错误）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59, 61（重新审判动议及标准）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-035"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-125","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":125,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in federal court in State A. At a pretrial conference, the attorneys stipulated to a jury of 14 jurors and 4 alternates and submitted this request to the judge. State courts in State A allow 14-person juries. Is the judge likely to comply with the parties' request?","choices":{"A":"No, because a 14-person jury is not allowed in federal court.","B":"No, because there must be half as many alternate jurors as selected jurors.","C":"Yes, because state courts in State A allow 14-person juries.","D":"Yes, because the parties stipulated to a 14-person jury."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 48(a), a jury in federal civil cases must have at least 6 but no more than 12 members. The parties may stipulate to the size of the jury so long as the stipulation stays within the requisite range. Here, the parties asked the judge to seat 14 jurors, which exceeds the maximum amount of 12 possible jurors (Choice D). Therefore, the judge is unlikely to comply with the parties' request. (Choice B) An alternate juror is a person who steps in when a member of the jury is excused for good cause (eg, illness, family emergency). There is no requirement for the number of alternate jurors that must be selected, but federal courts generally select two or three. (Choice C) Although state courts in State A allow 14-person juries, federal law controls procedural issues (eg, jury size) in diversity actions. Therefore, juries in federal civil cases must have 6 to 12 members. Educational objective: A federal civil jury trial must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The parties may stipulate to the size of the jury so long as the stipulation stays within the requisite range. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(a) (number of jurors).","rule":"A federal civil jury trial must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The parties may stipulate to the size of the jury so long as the stipulation stays within the requisite range.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in federal court in State A. At a pretrial conference, the attorneys stipulated to a jury of 14 jurors and 4 alternates and submitted this request to the judge. State courts in State A allow 14-person juries. Is the judge likely to comply with the parties' request?","rule":"A federal civil jury trial must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The parties may stipulate to the size of the jury so long as the stipulation stays within the requisite range.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 48(a), a jury in federal civil cases must have at least 6 but no more than 12 members. The parties may stipulate to the size of the jury so long as the stipulation stays within the requisite range. Here, the parties asked the judge to seat 14 jurors, which exceeds the maximum amount of 12 possible jurors (Choice D). Therefore, the judge is unlikely to comply with the parties' request. (Choice B) An alternate juror is a person who steps in when a member of the jury is excused for good cause (eg, illness, family emergency). There is no requirement for the number of alternate jurors that must be selected, but federal courts generally select two or three. (Choice C) Although state courts in State A allow 14-person juries, federal law controls procedural issues (eg, jury size) in diversity actions. Therefore, juries in federal civil cases must have 6 to 12 members. Educational objective: A federal civil jury trial must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The parties may stipulate to the size of the jury so long as the stipulation stays within the requisite range. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(a) (number of jurors).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(a) (number of jurors).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[379],"sourceAnswerPages":[380],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在 A 州联邦法院对被告提起多元州籍诉讼。在审前会议中，双方律师约定由 14 名正式陪审员和 4 名候补陪审员组成陪审团，并向法官提出请求。A 州州法院允许 14 人陪审团。法官可能同意双方请求吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦法院不允许 14 人陪审团。","B":"不会，因为候补陪审员人数必须为正式陪审员的一半。","C":"会，因为 A 州州法院允许 14 人陪审团。","D":"会，因为双方约定使用 14 人陪审团。"},"explanationZh":"依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》48(a)，联邦民事案件陪审团必须至少 6 人、至多 12 人。双方可以约定陪审团人数，但必须在该范围内。\n\n本题双方请求法官安排 14 名正式陪审员，超过最多 12 人的限制（选项 D）。因此，法官不太可能同意。\n\n（选项 B）候补陪审员是在正式陪审员因正当理由——例如疾病或家庭紧急情况——被免除时替补的人。候补人数没有强制要求，但联邦法院通常选择两至三人。\n\n（选项 C）A 州州法院虽允许 14 人陪审团，但在多元州籍诉讼中，程序问题（例如陪审团人数）由联邦法控制。因此，联邦民事陪审团必须为 6 至 12 人。\n\n学习目标：联邦民事陪审团审判开始时必须至少 6 人、至多 12 人。双方可以约定人数，但须在该范围内。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(a)（陪审员人数）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-036"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-126","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":126,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A buyer brought a diversity suit in State A federal court against a seller for breach of contract. During the case, the seller engaged in fraudulent conduct, attempted to delay the proceedings, ignored several court orders, and unduly oppressed and harassed the buyer. The judge issued an order to show cause as to why the seller should not be held in contempt. After a hearing, the judge held the seller in contempt and ordered him to pay a fine to the court and the buyer. At the conclusion of trial, the seller objected to this fine. The seller argued that State A law does not authorize a fine under these circumstances and that the federal court was bound to follow State A law. The seller also argued that no federal statute or rule authorizes this fine for contempt. If the seller is correct in his arguments, will he likely succeed in his objection?","choices":{"A":"No, because federal common law allows the court to impose the fine for contempt.","B":"No, because the case was brought in federal court.","C":"Yes, because a federal court sitting in diversity must follow state law.","D":"Yes, because no federal statute or rule authorized the imposition of this fine."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues (Choice B). However, there are instances when it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural (eg, a fine for contempt) and there is no federal law (ie, constitutional provision, statute, or rule) that directly addresses the issue. When this occurs, the Erie analysis* is used to determine if a federal court can supplant state law with federal common law (ie, judge-made rules). Under the Erie analysis, state law applies if it is outcome determinative AND there is no federal countervailing interest. State law is outcome determinative if the failure to apply state law would result in:\n• forum shopping – litigants will be encouraged to sue in federal court to take advantage of benefits not afforded in state court or\n• inequitable administration of the laws – the application of substantially different rules in federal and state court would cause unfair outcomes. Here, the contempt fine will not cause forum shopping or the inequitable administration of the laws. That is because contempt fines are ordered only if a party fails to abide by court rules and are not tied to substantive issues underlying the case. As a result, state law is not outcome determinative, and the court can apply federal common law to impose the contempt fine over the seller's objection (Choice C). *The Erie and federal-rule analyses seek to ensure that federal courts sitting in diversity apply federal law to procedural issues only. Therefore, the more substantive an issue appears to be, the more likely state law must be applied. (Choice D) Although no federal statute or rule authorizes the fine, federal courts have inherent authority to hold a party in contempt of court. Educational objective: Under the Erie analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law if (1) it would be outcome determinative—ie, failing to apply it would result in forum shopping or inequitable administration of the laws—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest. References\n• Chambers v. NASCO, Inc., 501 U.S. 32, 51–53 (1991) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity has the inherent authority to impose a fine for contempt even if state law would not permit it).","rule":"Under the Erie analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law if (1) it would be outcome determinative—ie, failing to apply it would result in forum shopping or inequitable administration of the laws—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest.","irac":{"issue":"A buyer brought a diversity suit in State A federal court against a seller for breach of contract. During the case, the seller engaged in fraudulent conduct, attempted to delay the proceedings, ignored several court orders, and unduly oppressed and harassed the buyer. The judge issued an order to show cause as to why the seller should not be held in contempt. After a hearing, the judge held the seller in contempt and ordered him to pay a fine to the court and the buyer. At the conclusion of trial, the seller objected to this fine. The seller argued that State A law does not authorize a fine under these circumstances and that the federal court was bound to follow State A law. The seller also argued that no federal statute or rule authorizes this fine for contempt. If the seller is correct in his arguments, will he likely succeed in his objection?","rule":"Under the Erie analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law if (1) it would be outcome determinative—ie, failing to apply it would result in forum shopping or inequitable administration of the laws—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest.","analysis":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues (Choice B). However, there are instances when it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural (eg, a fine for contempt) and there is no federal law (ie, constitutional provision, statute, or rule) that directly addresses the issue. When this occurs, the Erie analysis* is used to determine if a federal court can supplant state law with federal common law (ie, judge-made rules). Under the Erie analysis, state law applies if it is outcome determinative AND there is no federal countervailing interest. State law is outcome determinative if the failure to apply state law would result in:\n• forum shopping – litigants will be encouraged to sue in federal court to take advantage of benefits not afforded in state court or\n• inequitable administration of the laws – the application of substantially different rules in federal and state court would cause unfair outcomes. Here, the contempt fine will not cause forum shopping or the inequitable administration of the laws. That is because contempt fines are ordered only if a party fails to abide by court rules and are not tied to substantive issues underlying the case. As a result, state law is not outcome determinative, and the court can apply federal common law to impose the contempt fine over the seller's objection (Choice C). *The Erie and federal-rule analyses seek to ensure that federal courts sitting in diversity apply federal law to procedural issues only. Therefore, the more substantive an issue appears to be, the more likely state law must be applied. (Choice D) Although no federal statute or rule authorizes the fine, federal courts have inherent authority to hold a party in contempt of court. Educational objective: Under the Erie analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law if (1) it would be outcome determinative—ie, failing to apply it would result in forum shopping or inequitable administration of the laws—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest. References\n• Chambers v. NASCO, Inc., 501 U.S. 32, 51–53 (1991) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity has the inherent authority to impose a fine for contempt even if state law would not permit it).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Chambers v. NASCO, Inc., 501 U.S. 32, 51–53 (1991) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity has the inherent authority to impose a fine for contempt even if state law would not permit it).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[382],"sourceAnswerPages":[383],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名买方在 A 州联邦法院对卖方提起多元州籍违约诉讼。案件期间，卖方实施欺诈行为、试图拖延程序、无视多项法院命令，并过度压迫、骚扰买方。法官发布说明理由令，要求卖方说明为何不应被认定藐视法庭。听证后，法官认定卖方藐视法庭，命令其向法院和买方支付罚款。审判结束时，卖方对罚款提出异议，主张 A 州法律不授权在该情形下罚款，联邦法院必须遵循 A 州法律；且没有联邦制定法或规则授权该藐视罚款。若卖方主张均正确，其异议可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦普通法允许法院就藐视法庭处以罚款。","B":"不会，因为案件在联邦法院提起。","C":"会，因为行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须遵循州法。","D":"会，因为没有联邦制定法或规则授权处以该罚款。"},"explanationZh":"行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对实体问题适用州法，对程序问题适用联邦法（选项 B）。但有时问题属于实体还是程序并不明确，例如藐视罚款，且没有联邦法律——宪法条款、制定法或规则——直接处理。这时使用 Erie 分析*，判断联邦法院能否以联邦普通法（法官制定规则）取代州法。\n\n依据 Erie 分析，若州法会决定结果，且不存在相反的联邦利益，则适用州法。若不适用州法会造成以下情形，州法即会决定结果：\n• 挑选法院——诉讼人会为利用州法院没有的利益而选择联邦法院；或\n• 法律适用不平等——州、联邦法院适用实质不同规则，造成不公平结果。\n\n本题藐视罚款不会造成挑选法院或法律适用不平等，因为只有当事人不遵守法院规则时才会处以藐视罚款，与案件基础实体问题无关。因此，州法不会决定结果，法院可以不顾卖方异议，适用联邦普通法处以藐视罚款（选项 C）。\n\n*Erie 分析和联邦规则分析旨在确保行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院只对程序问题适用联邦法。因此，一个问题越具有实体性，就越可能必须适用州法。\n\n（选项 D）虽然没有联邦制定法或规则授权该罚款，但联邦法院具有认定当事人藐视法庭的固有权力。\n\n学习目标：依据 Erie 分析，行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须在以下情况下适用州法：(1) 州法会决定结果，即不适用会造成挑选法院或法律适用不平等；且 (2) 不存在相反的联邦政策利益。\n\n参考资料：\n• Chambers v. NASCO, Inc., 501 U.S. 32, 51–53 (1991)（说明行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院即使在州法不允许时，仍有固有权力处以藐视罚款）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-037"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-127","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":127,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A retailer brought a federal diversity action against an architect, alleging fraudulent misrepresentations in the architect's design of the retailer's store. The complaint did not include a jury demand. One week later, the architect timely moved to dismiss the action for failure to state a claim; he did not file an answer. Twenty days after being served with the motion, the retailer filed and served an amended complaint that added a defamation claim based on the architect's recent statements about the retailer in a local newspaper. In the amended complaint, the retailer demanded a jury on both claims. Has the retailer properly demanded a jury trial?","choices":{"A":"No, because the retailer filed the demand more than 14 days after service of the motion to dismiss.","B":"No, because the retailer filed the demand more than 14 days after service of the original complaint.","C":"Yes, but on the defamation claim only, because the original complaint did not contain a jury demand.","D":"Yes, on both claims, because the architect had not answered the original complaint when the retailer filed the amended complaint with the jury demand."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The retailer's original complaint did not demand a jury trial. But under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 15, a party may amend its original pleading once as a matter of course (ie, without the court's permission) within 21 days after:\n• serving the original pleading (eg, complaint) or\n• being served with a responsive pleading (eg, answer) or motion under FRCP 12(b), (e), or (f) (eg, motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim)—whichever occurs first. The retailer amended its complaint more than 21 days after serving the original complaint. However, the amendment occurred within 21 days of the retailer being served with the architect's motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. Therefore, the amendment—which added a defamation claim and jury trial demand—was timely and permissible. And under FRCP 38, a written jury trial demand on any triable jury issue is timely when the demand is:\n• served on the other parties no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served (Choices A & B) and\n• filed with the court within a reasonable time after service of the jury trial demand. An answer is typically the last pleading directed at the triable jury issue. Since the architect had not filed an answer when the retailer filed and served the amended complaint that demanded a jury trial, the demand was proper. Therefore, the retailer is entitled to a jury on both claims (Choice C). Educational objective: A party may demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service. An answer is typically the last pleading directed at the triable jury issue. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a) (amended pleadings).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38 (demand for a jury trial).","rule":"A party may demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service. An answer is typically the last pleading directed at the triable jury issue.","irac":{"issue":"A retailer brought a federal diversity action against an architect, alleging fraudulent misrepresentations in the architect's design of the retailer's store. The complaint did not include a jury demand. One week later, the architect timely moved to dismiss the action for failure to state a claim; he did not file an answer. Twenty days after being served with the motion, the retailer filed and served an amended complaint that added a defamation claim based on the architect's recent statements about the retailer in a local newspaper. In the amended complaint, the retailer demanded a jury on both claims. Has the retailer properly demanded a jury trial?","rule":"A party may demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service. An answer is typically the last pleading directed at the triable jury issue.","analysis":"The retailer's original complaint did not demand a jury trial. But under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 15, a party may amend its original pleading once as a matter of course (ie, without the court's permission) within 21 days after:\n• serving the original pleading (eg, complaint) or\n• being served with a responsive pleading (eg, answer) or motion under FRCP 12(b), (e), or (f) (eg, motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim)—whichever occurs first. The retailer amended its complaint more than 21 days after serving the original complaint. However, the amendment occurred within 21 days of the retailer being served with the architect's motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. Therefore, the amendment—which added a defamation claim and jury trial demand—was timely and permissible. And under FRCP 38, a written jury trial demand on any triable jury issue is timely when the demand is:\n• served on the other parties no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served (Choices A & B) and\n• filed with the court within a reasonable time after service of the jury trial demand. An answer is typically the last pleading directed at the triable jury issue. Since the architect had not filed an answer when the retailer filed and served the amended complaint that demanded a jury trial, the demand was proper. Therefore, the retailer is entitled to a jury on both claims (Choice C). Educational objective: A party may demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service. An answer is typically the last pleading directed at the triable jury issue. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a) (amended pleadings).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38 (demand for a jury trial).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a) (amended pleadings).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38 (demand for a jury trial).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[385],"sourceAnswerPages":[386],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家零售商对一名建筑师提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，主张建筑师在设计零售商店铺时作出欺诈性虚假陈述。起诉状未包含陪审团请求。一周后，建筑师及时以未陈述请求为由申请驳回，且未提交答辩状。收到动议送达 20 天后，零售商提交并送达修改后的起诉状，增加一项基于建筑师最近在当地报纸上发表言论的诽谤请求，并就两项请求均要求陪审团审判。零售商是否适当请求陪审团审判？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为零售商在收到驳回动议送达超过 14 天后才提出请求。","B":"没有，因为零售商在收到原起诉状送达超过 14 天后才提出请求。","C":"有，但仅限诽谤请求，因为原起诉状未提出陪审团请求。","D":"有，两项请求均适用，因为零售商提交含陪审团请求的修改后起诉状时，建筑师尚未答辩。"},"explanationZh":"零售商原起诉状没有请求陪审团审判。但是，依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）15，当事人可以在以下时间后 21 天内，将原诉状修改一次而无需法院许可：\n• 送达原诉状，例如起诉状；或\n• 收到回应性诉状（例如答辩状）或 FRCP 12(b)、(e)、(f) 动议（例如未陈述请求驳回动议）的送达；以先发生者为准。\n\n零售商在送达原起诉状超过 21 天后修改，但在收到建筑师未陈述请求驳回动议送达后 21 天内修改。因此，增加诽谤请求及陪审团审判请求的修改及时且适当。\n\n依据 FRCP 38，就任何可由陪审团审理的问题提出书面请求，在满足以下条件时及时：\n• 不迟于针对该问题的最后一份诉状送达后 14 天，向其他当事人送达（选项 A、B）；并且\n• 陪审团请求送达后，在合理时间内向法院提交。\n\n答辩状通常是针对可由陪审团审理问题的最后一份诉状。零售商提交并送达请求陪审团审判的修改后起诉状时，建筑师尚未提交答辩状，所以请求适当。因此，两项请求均有权由陪审团审理（选项 C）。\n\n学习目标：当事人可以通过以下方式请求陪审团审判：(1) 不迟于针对该问题的最后一份诉状送达后 14 天，向其他当事人送达书面陪审团请求；并且 (2) 在送达请求后的合理时间内向法院提交。答辩状通常是针对可由陪审团审理问题的最后一份诉状。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a)（修改诉状）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38（陪审团审判请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-038"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-128","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":128,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A federal agency sued a corporation in a federal court in State A. The agency claimed that the corporation intentionally issued false and misleading advertisements in violation of federal law. At the conclusion of trial, the court found that the advertisements were false and misleading and entered a declaratory judgment in favor of the agency. A group of consumers then filed a class action in a federal court in State B against the same corporation to recover damages suffered as a result of the false and misleading advertisements. The corporation demanded a jury trial. The group moved for partial summary judgment, asserting that collateral estoppel (issue preclusion) prevents the corporation from relitigating whether the advertisements were false and misleading. How will the federal court likely rule on the group's motion for partial summary judgment?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the corporation is entitled to a jury trial on this factual issue.","B":"Deny the motion, because the group was not a party to the first action.","C":"Grant the motion, because offensive nonmutual collateral estoppel is permitted.","D":"Grant the motion, because the law-of-the-case doctrine applies."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Collateral estoppel\n\n(ie, issue preclusion)\n\nMutual v. Mutual Parties from first action assert collateral estoppel in Nonmutual subsequent action against other parties from first action\n\nNonmutual Nonparties from first action assert collateral estoppel in subsequent action against parties from first action\n\nDefensive v. Defensive Defendant in second action asserts to avoid relitigating Offensive issue from first action\n\nOffensive Plaintiff in second action asserts to establish issue from first action. Not permitted if:\n\n• plaintiff could have easily joined first action\n• defendant had little incentive to vigorously defend in first action\n• second action affords procedural opportunities unavailable in first action or\n• inconsistent findings on issue exist Collateral estoppel (ie, issue preclusion) precludes the relitigation of an issue that was actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. There are two types of collateral estoppel:\n• Mutual – where parties from the first action assert collateral estoppel in a subsequent action against other parties from the first action\n\n• Nonmutual – where nonparties from the first action assert collateral estoppel in a subsequent action against parties from the first action (Choice B)\n\nBoth types are permitted in federal court, and both can be used in a defensive or offensive manner. Defensive estoppel is used by a defendant in the second action to avoid relitigating an issue from the first action. Offensive estoppel is used by a plaintiff in the second action to establish an issue from the first action—with limited exceptions (see table above). Here, a federal court entered a declaratory judgment in favor of the federal agency after finding that the corporation's advertisements were false and misleading. A group of consumers then filed a class action against that corporation. The group (nonparty in first action) moved for partial summary judgment on the ground that the prior judgment\n\nestablished that the advertisements were false and misleading. Since offensive nonmutual collateral estoppel is permitted in federal court, the motion will likely be granted.* *None of the exceptions apply here since (1) it is difficult to join a federal agency's suit, (2) there is a great incentive to defend against allegations by the federal government, (3) both actions were brought in federal court, and (4) no inconsistent findings exist. (Choice A) In Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, the Supreme Court held that preventing the relitigation of factual issues through collateral estoppel does not violate the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial. (Choice D) The law-of-the-case doctrine prevents relitigating legal issues that have been decided at successive stages of the same case—eg, trial court must apply appellate court's legal determinations on remand—not different cases (as seen here). Educational objective: Collateral estoppel can be asserted by parties (mutual) or nonparties (nonmutual) to the first action against parties to the first action either (1) defensively, when used by a defendant in the second action to avoid an issue or (2) offensively, when used by a plaintiff in the second action to establish an issue. References\n• Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 329–31 (1979) (explaining how nonmutual collateral estoppel applies in federal courts).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).","rule":"Collateral estoppel can be asserted by parties (mutual) or nonparties (nonmutual) to the first action against parties to the first action either (1) defensively, when used by a defendant in the second action to avoid an issue or (2) offensively, when used by a plaintiff in the second action to establish an issue.","irac":{"issue":"A federal agency sued a corporation in a federal court in State A. The agency claimed that the corporation intentionally issued false and misleading advertisements in violation of federal law. At the conclusion of trial, the court found that the advertisements were false and misleading and entered a declaratory judgment in favor of the agency. A group of consumers then filed a class action in a federal court in State B against the same corporation to recover damages suffered as a result of the false and misleading advertisements. The corporation demanded a jury trial. The group moved for partial summary judgment, asserting that collateral estoppel (issue preclusion) prevents the corporation from relitigating whether the advertisements were false and misleading. How will the federal court likely rule on the group's motion for partial summary judgment?","rule":"Collateral estoppel can be asserted by parties (mutual) or nonparties (nonmutual) to the first action against parties to the first action either (1) defensively, when used by a defendant in the second action to avoid an issue or (2) offensively, when used by a plaintiff in the second action to establish an issue.","analysis":"Collateral estoppel\n\n(ie, issue preclusion)\n\nMutual v. Mutual Parties from first action assert collateral estoppel in Nonmutual subsequent action against other parties from first action\n\nNonmutual Nonparties from first action assert collateral estoppel in subsequent action against parties from first action\n\nDefensive v. Defensive Defendant in second action asserts to avoid relitigating Offensive issue from first action\n\nOffensive Plaintiff in second action asserts to establish issue from first action. Not permitted if:\n\n• plaintiff could have easily joined first action\n• defendant had little incentive to vigorously defend in first action\n• second action affords procedural opportunities unavailable in first action or\n• inconsistent findings on issue exist Collateral estoppel (ie, issue preclusion) precludes the relitigation of an issue that was actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. There are two types of collateral estoppel:\n• Mutual – where parties from the first action assert collateral estoppel in a subsequent action against other parties from the first action\n\n• Nonmutual – where nonparties from the first action assert collateral estoppel in a subsequent action against parties from the first action (Choice B)\n\nBoth types are permitted in federal court, and both can be used in a defensive or offensive manner. Defensive estoppel is used by a defendant in the second action to avoid relitigating an issue from the first action. Offensive estoppel is used by a plaintiff in the second action to establish an issue from the first action—with limited exceptions (see table above). Here, a federal court entered a declaratory judgment in favor of the federal agency after finding that the corporation's advertisements were false and misleading. A group of consumers then filed a class action against that corporation. The group (nonparty in first action) moved for partial summary judgment on the ground that the prior judgment\n\nestablished that the advertisements were false and misleading. Since offensive nonmutual collateral estoppel is permitted in federal court, the motion will likely be granted.* *None of the exceptions apply here since (1) it is difficult to join a federal agency's suit, (2) there is a great incentive to defend against allegations by the federal government, (3) both actions were brought in federal court, and (4) no inconsistent findings exist. (Choice A) In Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, the Supreme Court held that preventing the relitigation of factual issues through collateral estoppel does not violate the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial. (Choice D) The law-of-the-case doctrine prevents relitigating legal issues that have been decided at successive stages of the same case—eg, trial court must apply appellate court's legal determinations on remand—not different cases (as seen here). Educational objective: Collateral estoppel can be asserted by parties (mutual) or nonparties (nonmutual) to the first action against parties to the first action either (1) defensively, when used by a defendant in the second action to avoid an issue or (2) offensively, when used by a plaintiff in the second action to establish an issue. References\n• Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 329–31 (1979) (explaining how nonmutual collateral estoppel applies in federal courts).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 329–31 (1979) (explaining how nonmutual collateral estoppel applies in federal courts).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[388],"sourceAnswerPages":[389,390],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家联邦机构在 A 州联邦法院起诉一家股份公司。机构主张，公司故意发布虚假、误导性广告，违反联邦法律。审判结束时，法院认定广告虚假且具误导性，并登记有利于机构的宣告判决。一群消费者随后在 B 州联邦法院对同一公司提起集体诉讼，请求赔偿虚假、误导性广告造成的损害。公司请求陪审团审判。消费者群体申请部分简易判决，主张争点排除禁止公司重新争讼广告是否虚假、误导。联邦法院可能如何裁定消费者群体的部分简易判决动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为公司有权就该事实问题接受陪审团审判。","B":"驳回动议，因为消费者群体不是第一起诉讼的当事人。","C":"批准动议，因为进攻性非相互争点排除可以适用。","D":"批准动议，因为本案法律原则适用。"},"explanationZh":"争点排除：\n\n相互与非相互：\n• 相互——第一案当事人在后续诉讼中，对第一案其他当事人主张争点排除。\n• 非相互——第一案非当事人在后续诉讼中，对第一案当事人主张争点排除。\n\n防御性与进攻性：\n• 防御性——第二案被告主张，以避免重新争讼第一案问题。\n• 进攻性——第二案原告主张，以确立第一案问题。但在以下情形不允许：\n  – 原告本可轻易加入第一案；\n  – 被告在第一案中几乎没有积极抗辩的动力；\n  – 第二案提供第一案没有的程序机会；或\n  – 关于该问题存在相互矛盾的认定。\n\n争点排除禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。争点排除分为：\n• 相互——第一案当事人在后续诉讼中，对第一案其他当事人主张；\n• 非相互——第一案非当事人在后续诉讼中，对第一案当事人主张（选项 B）。\n\n联邦法院允许两类争点排除，且均可防御性或进攻性使用。防御性排除由第二案被告使用，以避免重新争讼第一案问题；进攻性排除由第二案原告使用，以确立第一案问题，但有有限例外（见上表）。\n\n本题联邦法院在认定公司广告虚假、误导后，登记有利于联邦机构的宣告判决。一群消费者随后对该公司提起集体诉讼。消费者群体作为第一案非当事人，申请部分简易判决，主张先前判决已确立广告虚假、误导。联邦法院允许进攻性非相互争点排除，所以动议很可能获准。*\n\n*本题没有例外适用，因为：(1) 难以加入联邦机构提起的诉讼；(2) 公司有充分动力抗辩联邦政府指控；(3) 两案均在联邦法院；且 (4) 不存在相互矛盾的认定。\n\n（选项 A）最高法院在 Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore 案认定，通过争点排除禁止重新争讼事实问题，不违反第七修正案陪审团审判权。\n\n（选项 D）本案法律原则禁止在同一案件的连续阶段重新争讼已裁定法律问题，例如案件发回时审判法院必须适用上诉法院法律认定；它不适用于不同案件。\n\n学习目标：第一案当事人（相互）或非当事人（非相互）均可针对第一案当事人主张争点排除；既可防御性使用，由第二案被告避免某一问题，也可进攻性使用，由第二案原告确立某一问题。\n\n参考资料：\n• Parklane Hosiery Co. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 329–31 (1979)（解释非相互争点排除在联邦法院中的适用）。\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义争点排除）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-129","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":129,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"An accounting firm brought a federal diversity action against a former client for failing to pay for the firm's audit of the client's financial statements. After the client answered, the parties settled, and the court dismissed the action with prejudice. The client subsequently sued the firm for negligently performing the audit. The firm moved to dismiss the negligence action on the basis of res judicata (claim preclusion). Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the firm's negligence was never raised or decided in the first action.","B":"No, because the first action was resolved by settlement.","C":"Yes, because the court dismissed the first action with prejudice.","D":"Yes, because the negligence claim was transactionally related to the claim in the first action and should have been asserted as a counterclaim."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Res judicata (claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes the relitigation of identical claims between identical parties. Parties are identical if they occupy the same roles in the first and subsequent actions—eg, the same party must be the plaintiff in both actions. But when the same parties occupy different roles (as seen here), claim preclusion will still apply if a party violated the first court's compulsory-counterclaim rule. Here, the accounting firm brought the first action in federal court. Under FRCP 13, a counterclaim is compulsory (ie, must be asserted) if it:\n• arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and\n\n• does not require adding a party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction.\n\nThe client's negligence claim against the accounting firm was transactionally related to the firm's claim in the first action since both concern the same audit. Since the client's claim does not require adding any parties, it should have been asserted as a counterclaim in the first action. By violating the compulsory-counterclaim rule, the client is precluded from asserting that claim in the second action. Therefore, the court in the second action will likely grant the firm's motion to dismiss based on res judicata. (Choice A) Although the firm's negligence was never raised or decided in the first action, claim preclusion bars a claim that should have been asserted in a prior action (as seen here). (Choice B) A judgment entered due to a settlement is entitled to claim-preclusive effect. However, such a judgment does not trigger issue preclusion, which requires actual litigation. (Choice C) The fact that the first court dismissed the action with prejudice is insufficient by itself to trigger claim preclusion. That is because the claims in the first and second actions must also be transactionally related to trigger the compulsory-counterclaim rule. Educational objective: Claim preclusion applies if the parties occupy the same roles in the first and subsequent actions—ie, the parties are identical. If the parties are not identical, claim preclusion will still apply if a party violated the first court's compulsory-counterclaim rule. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining compulsory-counterclaim exception to identical-parties requirement).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) (compulsory counterclaim).","rule":"Claim preclusion applies if the parties occupy the same roles in the first and subsequent actions—ie, the parties are identical. If the parties are not identical, claim preclusion will still apply if a party violated the first court's compulsory-counterclaim rule. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"An accounting firm brought a federal diversity action against a former client for failing to pay for the firm's audit of the client's financial statements. After the client answered, the parties settled, and the court dismissed the action with prejudice. The client subsequently sued the firm for negligently performing the audit. The firm moved to dismiss the negligence action on the basis of res judicata (claim preclusion). Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"Claim preclusion applies if the parties occupy the same roles in the first and subsequent actions—ie, the parties are identical. If the parties are not identical, claim preclusion will still apply if a party violated the first court's compulsory-counterclaim rule. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"Res judicata (claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes the relitigation of identical claims between identical parties. Parties are identical if they occupy the same roles in the first and subsequent actions—eg, the same party must be the plaintiff in both actions. But when the same parties occupy different roles (as seen here), claim preclusion will still apply if a party violated the first court's compulsory-counterclaim rule. Here, the accounting firm brought the first action in federal court. Under FRCP 13, a counterclaim is compulsory (ie, must be asserted) if it:\n• arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and\n\n• does not require adding a party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction.\n\nThe client's negligence claim against the accounting firm was transactionally related to the firm's claim in the first action since both concern the same audit. Since the client's claim does not require adding any parties, it should have been asserted as a counterclaim in the first action. By violating the compulsory-counterclaim rule, the client is precluded from asserting that claim in the second action. Therefore, the court in the second action will likely grant the firm's motion to dismiss based on res judicata. (Choice A) Although the firm's negligence was never raised or decided in the first action, claim preclusion bars a claim that should have been asserted in a prior action (as seen here). (Choice B) A judgment entered due to a settlement is entitled to claim-preclusive effect. However, such a judgment does not trigger issue preclusion, which requires actual litigation. (Choice C) The fact that the first court dismissed the action with prejudice is insufficient by itself to trigger claim preclusion. That is because the claims in the first and second actions must also be transactionally related to trigger the compulsory-counterclaim rule. Educational objective: Claim preclusion applies if the parties occupy the same roles in the first and subsequent actions—ie, the parties are identical. If the parties are not identical, claim preclusion will still apply if a party violated the first court's compulsory-counterclaim rule. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining compulsory-counterclaim exception to identical-parties requirement).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) (compulsory counterclaim).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining compulsory-counterclaim exception to identical-parties requirement).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) (compulsory counterclaim).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[391],"sourceAnswerPages":[392,393],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家会计师事务所对前客户提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，主张客户未支付事务所审计其财务报表的费用。客户答辩后，双方达成和解，法院有损害驳回诉讼。客户随后起诉事务所审计时存在过失。事务所依据请求权排除（既判力）申请驳回过失诉讼。法院可能批准动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为事务所的过失从未在第一案中提出或裁定。","B":"不会，因为第一案通过和解解决。","C":"会，因为法院有损害驳回第一案。","D":"会，因为过失请求与第一案请求在交易上相关，本应作为反诉提出。"},"explanationZh":"请求权排除规定，一项有效的实体终局判决，禁止相同当事人之间重新争讼相同请求。当事人在第一案与后案中占据相同诉讼地位时，即为相同当事人，例如同一方在两案中均为原告。\n\n但相同当事人在两案中占据不同地位时（本题即如此），若一方违反第一法院的强制反诉规则，请求权排除仍然适用。依据 FRCP 13，反诉符合以下条件时属于强制反诉，即必须提出：\n• 与对方请求源于同一交易或事件；且\n• 无需加入法院无法取得管辖权的当事人。\n\n客户针对会计师事务所的过失请求与第一案中事务所的请求在交易上相关，因为二者均涉及同一次审计。该请求也无需增加任何当事人，所以本应在第一案中作为反诉提出。客户违反强制反诉规则，故不得在第二案中提出该请求。因此，第二案法院很可能依据请求权排除批准事务所的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）事务所过失虽未在第一案中提出或裁定，请求权排除仍会阻止本应在先前诉讼中提出的请求。\n\n（选项 B）因和解登记的判决具有请求权排除效力。但它不触发要求实际争讼的争点排除。\n\n（选项 C）第一法院有损害驳回诉讼，本身不足以触发请求权排除。第一案与第二案请求还必须在交易上相关，方可触发强制反诉规则。\n\n学习目标：第一案与后案中当事人占据相同地位时，请求权排除适用。即使当事人地位不同，若一方违反第一法院的强制反诉规则，请求权排除仍然适用。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 22 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（解释相同当事人要求的强制反诉例外）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a)（强制反诉）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-039"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-130","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":130,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A wholesaler brought a federal diversity action against a large pharmaceutical company for breach of contract. During jury selection, one potential juror stated that five years earlier he had been an employee of the company and still owned several hundred shares of its stock. In response to questioning from the judge, the potential juror stated that he could fairly consider the evidence in the case. The wholesaler's attorney has asked the judge to strike the potential juror for cause. Should the judge strike the potential juror for cause?","choices":{"A":"No, because the potential juror said that he could fairly consider the evidence in the case.","B":"No, because the wholesaler's attorney could use a peremptory challenge to strike the potential juror.","C":"Yes, because other potential jurors still remain available for the jury panel.","D":"Yes, because the potential juror is presumed to be biased because of his relationship to the company."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Challenges to potential jurors\n\n(FRCP 47)\n\nNumber Basis for disqualification allowed\n\nPeremptory 3 per party* • Any basis other than race, ethnicity, or gender\n• No explanation needed For cause Unlimited • For bias or lack of impartiality—eg: – relationship with party – financial stake in party FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*When a civil action involves multiple plaintiffs or defendants, the court may grant each side (1) three peremptory challenges or (2) more than three peremptory challenges that each side's parties exercise separately or jointly. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 47 permits the court, the parties, or the parties' attorneys to examine potential jurors. During this process, referred to as voir dire, the prospective jurors are questioned on their backgrounds and potential biases before being chosen to sit on the jury.* Each party is entitled to:\n• three peremptory challenges – to disqualify a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, or gender), without the need for an explanation and\n• an unlimited number of challenges for cause – to disqualify a potential juror on the grounds that he/she is biased or otherwise cannot be impartial. A peremptory challenge is granted automatically. But a challenge for cause will only be granted if the court finds evidence of bias (eg, employment relationship with a party, financial stake in a party). And here, there is sufficient evidence of bias since the potential juror is a former employee of the pharmaceutical company and still owns several hundred shares of company stock. Therefore, the judge should strike the potential juror for cause. *The court may examine the jurors or allow the attorneys to do so. If the court examines the jurors, it must either (1) permit the parties or their attorneys to make any further inquiry the court deems proper or (2) ask any of their additional questions the court considers proper. (Choice A) In deciding how to rule, the judge may consider the potential juror's statement that he could fairly consider the evidence in the case. But this statement alone is not sufficient for the judge to refuse to disqualify the potential juror for cause.\n\n(Choice B) The judge cannot refuse to strike the potential juror for cause simply because the wholesaler's attorney could use a peremptory challenge to strike him. And since there is sufficient evidence that the potential juror cannot be impartial because of his relationship with the company, the judge must exclude him. (Choice C) The fact that other prospective jurors still remain available for the jury panel is irrelevant in determining whether the judge should strike the potential juror for cause. Educational objective: During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges—to disqualify a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) and without explanation—and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause—to disqualify a potential juror on the grounds that he/she is biased or otherwise cannot be impartial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47 (jury selection process).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870 (challenging prospective jurors).\n\n• Darbin v. Nourse, 664 F.2d 1109, 1113 (9th Cir. 1981) (explaining that a challenge for cause is warranted when a prospective juror's relationships, pecuniary interests, or clear biases threaten impartiality).","rule":"During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges—to disqualify a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) and without explanation—and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause—to disqualify a potential juror on the grounds that he/she is biased or otherwise cannot be impartial.","irac":{"issue":"A wholesaler brought a federal diversity action against a large pharmaceutical company for breach of contract. During jury selection, one potential juror stated that five years earlier he had been an employee of the company and still owned several hundred shares of its stock. In response to questioning from the judge, the potential juror stated that he could fairly consider the evidence in the case. The wholesaler's attorney has asked the judge to strike the potential juror for cause. Should the judge strike the potential juror for cause?","rule":"During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges—to disqualify a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) and without explanation—and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause—to disqualify a potential juror on the grounds that he/she is biased or otherwise cannot be impartial.","analysis":"Challenges to potential jurors\n\n(FRCP 47)\n\nNumber Basis for disqualification allowed\n\nPeremptory 3 per party* • Any basis other than race, ethnicity, or gender\n• No explanation needed For cause Unlimited • For bias or lack of impartiality—eg: – relationship with party – financial stake in party FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*When a civil action involves multiple plaintiffs or defendants, the court may grant each side (1) three peremptory challenges or (2) more than three peremptory challenges that each side's parties exercise separately or jointly. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 47 permits the court, the parties, or the parties' attorneys to examine potential jurors. During this process, referred to as voir dire, the prospective jurors are questioned on their backgrounds and potential biases before being chosen to sit on the jury.* Each party is entitled to:\n• three peremptory challenges – to disqualify a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, or gender), without the need for an explanation and\n• an unlimited number of challenges for cause – to disqualify a potential juror on the grounds that he/she is biased or otherwise cannot be impartial. A peremptory challenge is granted automatically. But a challenge for cause will only be granted if the court finds evidence of bias (eg, employment relationship with a party, financial stake in a party). And here, there is sufficient evidence of bias since the potential juror is a former employee of the pharmaceutical company and still owns several hundred shares of company stock. Therefore, the judge should strike the potential juror for cause. *The court may examine the jurors or allow the attorneys to do so. If the court examines the jurors, it must either (1) permit the parties or their attorneys to make any further inquiry the court deems proper or (2) ask any of their additional questions the court considers proper. (Choice A) In deciding how to rule, the judge may consider the potential juror's statement that he could fairly consider the evidence in the case. But this statement alone is not sufficient for the judge to refuse to disqualify the potential juror for cause.\n\n(Choice B) The judge cannot refuse to strike the potential juror for cause simply because the wholesaler's attorney could use a peremptory challenge to strike him. And since there is sufficient evidence that the potential juror cannot be impartial because of his relationship with the company, the judge must exclude him. (Choice C) The fact that other prospective jurors still remain available for the jury panel is irrelevant in determining whether the judge should strike the potential juror for cause. Educational objective: During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges—to disqualify a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) and without explanation—and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause—to disqualify a potential juror on the grounds that he/she is biased or otherwise cannot be impartial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47 (jury selection process).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870 (challenging prospective jurors).\n\n• Darbin v. Nourse, 664 F.2d 1109, 1113 (9th Cir. 1981) (explaining that a challenge for cause is warranted when a prospective juror's relationships, pecuniary interests, or clear biases threaten impartiality).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47 (jury selection process).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870 (challenging prospective jurors).\n\n• Darbin v. Nourse, 664 F.2d 1109, 1113 (9th Cir. 1981) (explaining that a challenge for cause is warranted when a prospective juror's relationships, pecuniary interests, or clear biases threaten impartiality).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[394],"sourceAnswerPages":[395,396],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家批发商对一家大型制药公司提起联邦多元州籍违约诉讼。遴选陪审员时，一名候选陪审员称自己五年前曾是公司雇员，且至今仍持有公司数百股股票。法官询问时，该候选人称自己可以公平考虑案件证据。批发商律师请求法官有因排除该候选陪审员。法官是否应当有因排除？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为候选陪审员称自己可以公平考虑案件证据。","B":"不应，因为批发商律师可以使用无因回避排除候选陪审员。","C":"应当，因为仍有其他候选陪审员可供组成陪审团。","D":"应当，因为候选陪审员因与公司的关系而推定存在偏见。"},"explanationZh":"对候选陪审员的回避（FRCP 47）：\n\n类型｜允许数量｜取消资格的依据\n无因回避｜每方 3 次*｜除种族、族裔或性别以外的任何理由；无需说明\n有因回避｜不限次数｜存在偏见或不能保持公正，例如与一方有关系、在一方具有经济利益\n\n*民事诉讼有多名原告或被告时，法院可给予每一方：(1) 三次无因回避；或 (2) 三次以上由同一方各当事人分别或共同使用的无因回避。\n\nFRCP 47 允许法院、当事人或其律师审查候选陪审员。在该称为陪审员资格询问的过程中，正式选任前会询问候选人的背景与潜在偏见。* 每一方有权：\n• 行使三次无因回避——基于除种族、族裔或性别外的任何理由取消候选资格，无需说明；及\n• 行使不限次数的有因回避——以候选人有偏见或不能公正为由取消资格。\n\n无因回避自动获准；有因回避只有法院发现偏见证据时才会获准，例如与一方存在雇佣关系或在该方具有经济利益。本题候选陪审员是制药公司前雇员，仍持有数百股公司股票，已有充分偏见证据。因此，法官应有因排除。\n\n*法院可以自行询问陪审员，也可允许律师询问。若由法院询问，必须：(1) 允许当事人或律师作法院认为适当的进一步询问；或 (2) 代为提出其认为适当的附加问题。\n\n（选项 A）法官可以考虑候选陪审员关于能够公平考虑证据的陈述，但该陈述本身不足以拒绝有因取消其资格。\n\n（选项 B）法官不能仅因批发商律师可使用无因回避，就拒绝有因排除。候选人因与公司的关系已有充分证据表明其不能公正，法官必须排除。\n\n（选项 C）仍有其他候选陪审员，与是否应有因排除无关。\n\n学习目标：遴选陪审员时，每方有权：(1) 行使三次无因回避，基于除种族、族裔或性别外的任何理由且无需说明；及 (2) 行使不限次数的有因回避，以候选人有偏见或不能公正为由取消资格。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47（陪审员遴选程序）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870（质疑候选陪审员）。\n• Darbin v. Nourse, 664 F.2d 1109, 1113 (9th Cir. 1981)（候选人的关系、经济利益或明显偏见威胁公正时，应有因回避）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-131","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":131,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A manufacturer brought a patent infringement action in federal court against a company to recover $50,000. The complaint did not include a jury demand. The company timely filed and served its answer, which included a jury trial demand on the issue of damages. Ten days after being served with the answer, the manufacturer filed and served a written jury trial demand for all triable jury issues. The company immediately moved to strike the manufacturer's jury trial demand. Is the court likely to grant the motion to strike?","choices":{"A":"No, because the manufacturer properly demanded a jury trial.","B":"No, because the manufacturer's jury demand was served within 30 days after the company's jury demand.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer failed to include a jury demand in its complaint.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer's jury demand was untimely filed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 38, a civil litigant (plaintiff or defendant) may properly demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by:\n• serving the other parties with a written jury demand within 14 days after the last pleading (usually the defendant's answer) is served and\n\n• filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.\n\nWhen a party demands a jury trial on specific issues, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other triable jury issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court (Choice B).* Here, the company timely filed and served its answer, which included a jury trial demand on the specific issue of damages. The manufacturer then served a written jury trial demand for all other triable jury issues 10 days after the company served its original demand, which was within the 14-day deadline. Therefore, the court likely will deny the company's motion to strike the manufacturer's jury trial demand (Choice D). *If a party does not specify the issues for which it is demanding a jury trial, the demand is treated as a request for a jury trial on all triable jury issues. (Choice C) A jury trial demand may, but need not, be included in a pleading (eg, a complaint). The demand can also be made in a separate, stand-alone document (as seen here). Educational objective: When a party demands a jury trial on specific issues, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other triable jury issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(c) (jury trial demand on specific issues).","rule":"When a party demands a jury trial on specific issues, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other triable jury issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court.","irac":{"issue":"A manufacturer brought a patent infringement action in federal court against a company to recover $50,000. The complaint did not include a jury demand. The company timely filed and served its answer, which included a jury trial demand on the issue of damages. Ten days after being served with the answer, the manufacturer filed and served a written jury trial demand for all triable jury issues. The company immediately moved to strike the manufacturer's jury trial demand. Is the court likely to grant the motion to strike?","rule":"When a party demands a jury trial on specific issues, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other triable jury issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 38, a civil litigant (plaintiff or defendant) may properly demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by:\n• serving the other parties with a written jury demand within 14 days after the last pleading (usually the defendant's answer) is served and\n\n• filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.\n\nWhen a party demands a jury trial on specific issues, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other triable jury issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court (Choice B).* Here, the company timely filed and served its answer, which included a jury trial demand on the specific issue of damages. The manufacturer then served a written jury trial demand for all other triable jury issues 10 days after the company served its original demand, which was within the 14-day deadline. Therefore, the court likely will deny the company's motion to strike the manufacturer's jury trial demand (Choice D). *If a party does not specify the issues for which it is demanding a jury trial, the demand is treated as a request for a jury trial on all triable jury issues. (Choice C) A jury trial demand may, but need not, be included in a pleading (eg, a complaint). The demand can also be made in a separate, stand-alone document (as seen here). Educational objective: When a party demands a jury trial on specific issues, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other triable jury issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(c) (jury trial demand on specific issues).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(c) (jury trial demand on specific issues).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[397],"sourceAnswerPages":[398],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家制造商在联邦法院对一家公司提起专利侵权诉讼，请求 50,000 美元。起诉状未包含陪审团请求。公司及时提交并送达答辩状，其中就损害赔偿问题要求陪审团审判。收到答辩状送达十天后，制造商提交并送达书面请求，要求就全部可由陪审团审理的问题进行陪审团审判。公司立即申请删除制造商的陪审团请求。法院可能批准删除动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为制造商适当请求了陪审团审判。","B":"不会，因为制造商在公司提出陪审团请求后 30 天内送达其请求。","C":"会，因为制造商未在起诉状中包含陪审团请求。","D":"会，因为制造商的陪审团请求逾期提交。"},"explanationZh":"依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》38，民事诉讼当事人（原告或被告）可以通过以下方式，就任何可由陪审团审理的问题适当请求陪审团审判：\n• 在最后一份诉状（通常为被告答辩状）送达后 14 天内，向其他当事人送达书面陪审团请求；并且\n• 在请求送达后的合理时间内向法院提交。\n\n一方只针对特定问题请求陪审团审判时，另一方可以在原请求送达后 14 天内，或法院规定的更短期限内，就其他任何可由陪审团审理的问题送达请求（选项 B）。*\n\n本题公司及时提交、送达答辩状，其中仅就损害赔偿问题要求陪审团审判。制造商在公司送达原请求十天后，就其他全部可由陪审团审理的问题送达书面请求，符合 14 天期限。因此，法院很可能驳回公司删除制造商陪审团请求的动议（选项 D）。\n\n*一方未具体说明请求陪审团审理的问题时，该请求视为针对全部可由陪审团审理的问题。\n\n（选项 C）陪审团请求可以但不必包含于诉状（例如起诉状），也可通过单独文件提出，本题即如此。\n\n学习目标：一方针对特定问题请求陪审团审判时，另一方可在原请求送达后 14 天内，或法院规定的更短期限内，就其他任何可由陪审团审理的问题提出请求。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(c)（针对特定问题的陪审团请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-040"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-132","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":132,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A distributor brought a diversity action against its competitor in a federal district court in State A. During discovery, the competitor moved for summary judgment. The court entered summary judgment in favor of the competitor after finding that there was no genuine dispute of material fact and that the competitor was entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The distributor then filed the same claim against the competitor in a federal district court in State B. The competitor filed an answer in which it moved to dismiss the suit based on claim preclusion (res judicata). Should the court grant the competitor's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the first action did not result in a final judgment on the merits.","B":"No, because the second action was brought in a different federal court.","C":"Yes, because the distributor's claim is barred by the entry of summary judgment.","D":"Yes, because the distributor's claim merged with the entry of summary judgment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The doctrine of claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) prohibits identical parties from relitigating a claim after any court enters a valid final judgment on the merits on an identical claim (Choice B). A summary judgment is final because it fully resolves a dispute and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. It is also issued on the merits because it is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense. Entry of final judgment has two effects, both of which extinguish the plaintiff's ability to raise the claim in a later case:\n• When final judgment is entered in favor of the plaintiff, the plaintiff's claim merges with the judgment and is extinguished.\n\n• When final judgment is entered in favor of the defendant, the plaintiff's claim is barred by the judgment and is extinguished.\n\nHere, the first action resulted in a valid final judgment on the merits when the federal court in State A entered summary judgment in favor of the competitor (Choice A). The distributor then filed the same claim against the same competitor in a federal court in State B. Since summary judgment was entered in favor of the competitor (defendant) in the first action, the distributor's claim in the second suit is barred by the judgment (Choice D). As a result, the court should grant the competitor's motion to dismiss the suit based on claim preclusion.* *Instead of filing the same claim in a different court, the distributor should have appealed the grant of summary judgment. An appellate court has the power to review final judgments entered by district courts within its boundaries. Educational objective: Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Summary judgment is a final judgment on the merits because it fully resolves a dispute (leaving only enforcement) and is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).","rule":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Summary judgment is a final judgment on the merits because it fully resolves a dispute (leaving only enforcement) and is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense.","irac":{"issue":"A distributor brought a diversity action against its competitor in a federal district court in State A. During discovery, the competitor moved for summary judgment. The court entered summary judgment in favor of the competitor after finding that there was no genuine dispute of material fact and that the competitor was entitled to judgment as a matter of law. The distributor then filed the same claim against the competitor in a federal district court in State B. The competitor filed an answer in which it moved to dismiss the suit based on claim preclusion (res judicata). Should the court grant the competitor's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Summary judgment is a final judgment on the merits because it fully resolves a dispute (leaving only enforcement) and is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense.","analysis":"The doctrine of claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) prohibits identical parties from relitigating a claim after any court enters a valid final judgment on the merits on an identical claim (Choice B). A summary judgment is final because it fully resolves a dispute and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. It is also issued on the merits because it is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense. Entry of final judgment has two effects, both of which extinguish the plaintiff's ability to raise the claim in a later case:\n• When final judgment is entered in favor of the plaintiff, the plaintiff's claim merges with the judgment and is extinguished.\n\n• When final judgment is entered in favor of the defendant, the plaintiff's claim is barred by the judgment and is extinguished.\n\nHere, the first action resulted in a valid final judgment on the merits when the federal court in State A entered summary judgment in favor of the competitor (Choice A). The distributor then filed the same claim against the same competitor in a federal court in State B. Since summary judgment was entered in favor of the competitor (defendant) in the first action, the distributor's claim in the second suit is barred by the judgment (Choice D). As a result, the court should grant the competitor's motion to dismiss the suit based on claim preclusion.* *Instead of filing the same claim in a different court, the distributor should have appealed the grant of summary judgment. An appellate court has the power to review final judgments entered by district courts within its boundaries. Educational objective: Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Summary judgment is a final judgment on the merits because it fully resolves a dispute (leaving only enforcement) and is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[400],"sourceAnswerPages":[401],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家经销商在 A 州联邦地区法院对竞争对手提起多元州籍诉讼。证据开示期间，竞争对手申请简易判决。法院认定不存在重大事实真实争议，且竞争对手依法有权获得判决，遂为其登记简易判决。经销商随后在 B 州联邦地区法院对竞争对手提出同一请求。竞争对手在答辩状中依据请求权排除（既判力）申请驳回诉讼。法院是否应批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为第一案未产生实体终局判决。","B":"不应，因为第二案在不同联邦法院提起。","C":"应当，因为登记简易判决使经销商的请求被禁止。","D":"应当，因为经销商的请求与简易判决合并。"},"explanationZh":"请求权排除禁止相同当事人在任何法院对相同请求重新争讼，前提是已经有任何法院作出有效的实体终局判决（选项 B）。\n\n简易判决具有终局性，因为它完全解决争议，法院除执行判决外别无事项；它也是实体判决，因为以请求或抗辩的实体要件为基础。\n\n登记终局判决有两项效果，均消灭原告以后提出请求的能力：\n• 为原告登记终局判决时，原告请求与判决合并并被消灭；\n• 为被告登记终局判决时，原告请求被判决禁止并被消灭。\n\n本题第一案中，A 州联邦法院为竞争对手登记简易判决，由此产生有效的实体终局判决（选项 A）。经销商随后在 B 州联邦法院对同一竞争对手提出同一请求。第一案简易判决为竞争对手（被告）登记，所以第二案中经销商请求被该判决禁止（选项 D）。因此，法院应依据请求权排除批准竞争对手的驳回动议。*\n\n*经销商不应在另一法院提出同一请求，而应对简易判决提起上诉。上诉法院有权审查其辖区内地区法院登记的终局判决。\n\n学习目标：请求权排除规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。简易判决属于实体终局判决，因为它完全解决争议（只剩执行），并以请求或抗辩的实体要件为基础。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义请求权排除）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-039"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-133","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":133,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A company from State A filed a federal diversity action against its distributor, seeking $750,000 in damages. The company demanded a jury trial, and 10 jurors were selected to hear the case. After closing arguments, the judge instructed the jury. The jury deliberated for three days until the foreman sent a note to the judge informing her that the jury could not reach a decision. The judge brought the jury back into the courtroom and instructed the jurors on their duty to carefully and deliberately consider the evidence to reach a decision. The jury returned a verdict the next day in which nine jurors found in favor of the company for the full amount sought. The judge agreed with the jury and entered a final judgment in accordance with the verdict. The distributor has timely filed a motion for a new trial. Should the court grant the distributor's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because a super-majority of jurors agreed on the verdict.","B":"No, because the judge agrees with the jury's verdict.","C":"Yes, because the verdict was not unanimous.","D":"Yes, because there were fewer than 12 jurors."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 48, a jury must consist of at least 6 and no more than 12 members (Choice D). Unless otherwise stipulated, the jury must reach a unanimous verdict—ie, a formal decision on factual issues that is rendered at the conclusion of trial.* Here, only 9 out of the 10 jurors returned a verdict in favor of the company. Since there is no indication that the parties stipulated to a non-unanimous verdict, the court should grant the distributor's motion for new trial (Choice A). *In reaching a verdict, jurors must listen to the evidence presented in court and weigh it fairly. They should not consider matters not formally admitted into evidence or discuss the facts of the case with nonjurors. (Choice B) Whether the judge agrees with the jury's verdict is irrelevant since it was not unanimous. Educational objective: A jury verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 (but no more than 12) members unless the parties stipulate otherwise. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48 (number of jurors and unanimous-verdict requirements).","rule":"A jury verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 (but no more than 12) members unless the parties stipulate otherwise.","irac":{"issue":"A company from State A filed a federal diversity action against its distributor, seeking $750,000 in damages. The company demanded a jury trial, and 10 jurors were selected to hear the case. After closing arguments, the judge instructed the jury. The jury deliberated for three days until the foreman sent a note to the judge informing her that the jury could not reach a decision. The judge brought the jury back into the courtroom and instructed the jurors on their duty to carefully and deliberately consider the evidence to reach a decision. The jury returned a verdict the next day in which nine jurors found in favor of the company for the full amount sought. The judge agreed with the jury and entered a final judgment in accordance with the verdict. The distributor has timely filed a motion for a new trial. Should the court grant the distributor's motion?","rule":"A jury verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 (but no more than 12) members unless the parties stipulate otherwise.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 48, a jury must consist of at least 6 and no more than 12 members (Choice D). Unless otherwise stipulated, the jury must reach a unanimous verdict—ie, a formal decision on factual issues that is rendered at the conclusion of trial.* Here, only 9 out of the 10 jurors returned a verdict in favor of the company. Since there is no indication that the parties stipulated to a non-unanimous verdict, the court should grant the distributor's motion for new trial (Choice A). *In reaching a verdict, jurors must listen to the evidence presented in court and weigh it fairly. They should not consider matters not formally admitted into evidence or discuss the facts of the case with nonjurors. (Choice B) Whether the judge agrees with the jury's verdict is irrelevant since it was not unanimous. Educational objective: A jury verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 (but no more than 12) members unless the parties stipulate otherwise. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48 (number of jurors and unanimous-verdict requirements).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48 (number of jurors and unanimous-verdict requirements).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[403],"sourceAnswerPages":[404],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"A 州一家公司对其经销商提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，请求 750,000 美元损害赔偿。公司要求陪审团审判，并选出十名陪审员。结案陈词后，法官向陪审团作出指示。陪审团评议三天后，主席向法官递交纸条，称无法达成决定。法官将陪审团召回法庭，指示陪审员有义务认真、审慎考虑证据以达成决定。次日，陪审团作出裁决，其中九名陪审员判公司获得所请求全部金额。法官同意陪审团裁决并依其登记终局判决。经销商及时申请重新审判。法院是否应批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为绝大多数陪审员同意该裁决。","B":"不应，因为法官同意陪审团裁决。","C":"应当，因为裁决并非全体一致。","D":"应当，因为陪审员少于十二人。"},"explanationZh":"依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》48，陪审团必须至少 6 人、至多 12 人（选项 D）。除非双方另有约定，陪审团必须作出全体一致裁决，即审判结束时就事实问题作出的正式决定。*\n\n本题十名陪审员中只有九名作出有利于公司的裁决。没有迹象表明双方约定非全体一致裁决，所以法院应批准经销商的重新审判动议（选项 A）。\n\n*作出裁决时，陪审员必须听取法庭提出的证据并公平权衡。不得考虑未正式采纳为证据的事项，也不得与非陪审员讨论案件事实。\n\n（选项 B）法官是否同意陪审团裁决无关，因为裁决并非全体一致。\n\n学习目标：除非双方另有约定，陪审团裁决必须由至少 6 名、至多 12 名陪审员全体一致作出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48（陪审员人数及全体一致裁决要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-036"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-134","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":134,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A plaintiff filed a tort action in state court but then failed to prosecute the action. The defendant moved to dismiss the action, and the court granted the motion in an order that stated: \"The defendant's motion to dismiss is granted, and this action is dismissed with prejudice.\" The court accordingly entered judgment for the defendant. The plaintiff then filed the same claim against the defendant in federal court, invoking diversity jurisdiction. The defendant has asserted the defense of res judicata (claim preclusion) in its answer. Should the federal court give preclusive effect to the state court judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judgment was entered by a state court, not a federal court.","B":"No, because the state court did not rule on the merits in its dismissal.","C":"Yes, because a dismissal with prejudice operates as a judgment on the merits.","D":"Yes, because a judgment for failure to prosecute operates as a judgment on the merits under the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from relitigating an identical claim against the same party—regardless of whether the actions were filed in state or federal court (Choice A). However, the court in which the judgment was entered determines which claim-preclusion rule applies:\n• A judgment entered in state court means that state's law applies to the second action.\n• A judgment entered in federal court means federal law applies to the second action. Here, the plaintiff's first tort action was dismissed with prejudice by the state court. A dismissal with prejudice constitutes a final judgment on the merits, so the federal court must give preclusive effect to the state court judgment (Choice B). And since the judgment was entered by a state court, that state's rule on claim preclusion should apply. (Choice D) Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 41(b), a dismissal for failure to prosecute a case in federal court operates as a judgment on the merits in that federal court. But since the state court rendered the judgment dismissing the plaintiff's claim, state claim-preclusion law (not the FRCP) applies. Educational objective: Under the doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from relitigating an identical claim against the same party—regardless of whether the actions were filed in state or federal court. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738 (full faith and credit statute).\n\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980) (stating that federal courts must give the same preclusive effect to state court judgments that courts in the judgment-rendering state would give).","rule":"Under the doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from relitigating an identical claim against the same party—regardless of whether the actions were filed in state or federal court.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff filed a tort action in state court but then failed to prosecute the action. The defendant moved to dismiss the action, and the court granted the motion in an order that stated: \"The defendant's motion to dismiss is granted, and this action is dismissed with prejudice.\" The court accordingly entered judgment for the defendant. The plaintiff then filed the same claim against the defendant in federal court, invoking diversity jurisdiction. The defendant has asserted the defense of res judicata (claim preclusion) in its answer. Should the federal court give preclusive effect to the state court judgment?","rule":"Under the doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from relitigating an identical claim against the same party—regardless of whether the actions were filed in state or federal court.","analysis":"The doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from relitigating an identical claim against the same party—regardless of whether the actions were filed in state or federal court (Choice A). However, the court in which the judgment was entered determines which claim-preclusion rule applies:\n• A judgment entered in state court means that state's law applies to the second action.\n• A judgment entered in federal court means federal law applies to the second action. Here, the plaintiff's first tort action was dismissed with prejudice by the state court. A dismissal with prejudice constitutes a final judgment on the merits, so the federal court must give preclusive effect to the state court judgment (Choice B). And since the judgment was entered by a state court, that state's rule on claim preclusion should apply. (Choice D) Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 41(b), a dismissal for failure to prosecute a case in federal court operates as a judgment on the merits in that federal court. But since the state court rendered the judgment dismissing the plaintiff's claim, state claim-preclusion law (not the FRCP) applies. Educational objective: Under the doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from relitigating an identical claim against the same party—regardless of whether the actions were filed in state or federal court. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738 (full faith and credit statute).\n\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980) (stating that federal courts must give the same preclusive effect to state court judgments that courts in the judgment-rendering state would give).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1738 (full faith and credit statute).\n\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980) (stating that federal courts must give the same preclusive effect to state court judgments that courts in the judgment-rendering state would give).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[405],"sourceAnswerPages":[406],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在州法院提起侵权诉讼，之后未推进诉讼。被告申请驳回，法院发布命令称：“批准被告的驳回动议，本诉讼有损害驳回。”法院据此为被告登记判决。原告随后依据多元州籍管辖权，在联邦法院对同一被告提出同一请求。被告在答辩状中主张请求权排除（既判力）。联邦法院是否应给予州法院判决排除效力？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为判决由州法院而非联邦法院登记。","B":"不应，因为州法院在驳回时没有就实体作出裁判。","C":"应当，因为有损害驳回视为实体判决。","D":"应当，因为依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》，因未推进诉讼而作出的判决视为实体判决。"},"explanationZh":"请求权排除规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止一方对同一当事人重新争讼相同请求，无论案件在州法院还是联邦法院提起（选项 A）。但是，登记判决的法院决定适用哪套请求权排除规则：\n• 判决在州法院登记，第二案适用该州法律；\n• 判决在联邦法院登记，第二案适用联邦法律。\n\n本题第一起侵权诉讼被州法院有损害驳回。有损害驳回构成实体终局判决，所以联邦法院必须给予州法院判决排除效力（选项 B）。由于判决由州法院登记，应适用该州请求权排除规则。\n\n（选项 D）依据 FRCP 41(b)，联邦法院因未推进诉讼而驳回案件，在该联邦法院中视为实体判决。但本题是州法院作出驳回原告请求的判决，所以应适用州请求权排除法，而不是 FRCP。\n\n学习目标：请求权排除规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止一方对同一当事人重新争讼相同请求，无论诉讼在州法院还是联邦法院提起。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738（完全诚信与信用法）。\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980)（联邦法院必须给予州法院判决与判决州法院所给予的相同排除效力）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-041"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-135","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":135,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"An employee complained to his company's human resources department that the company employed undocumented immigrants. Unbeknownst to the employee, the company was currently engaged in a class action lawsuit in which it stood accused of conspiring to lower its employees' wages by hiring undocumented workers. The company's attorney met with the employee and pressured him to recant his complaint, so it would not be used against the company in the class action lawsuit. The employee refused, and he was subsequently fired. The employee sued the company in federal court, alleging that his termination violated federal law. During discovery, the employee requested that the company turn over all documents and information concerning the company's decision to fire him. The company refused on the ground that the information was protected by the attorney-client privilege. The employee then filed a motion to compel production. The district court entered an order granting the employee's motion on the ground that the company waived this privilege. The company immediately filed a notice of appeal on the ground that the order constituted a collateral order. Will the appellate court likely hear the appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the collateral-order doctrine is not expressly authorized by a federal statute.","B":"No, because this issue can be effectively reviewed on appeal from the final judgment.","C":"Yes, because an appellate court decision on this issue will likely resolve the dispute.","D":"Yes, because due process requires that the appellate court immediately hear the appeal."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Absent narrow exceptions, 28 U.S.C. § 1291 limits appeals to final judgments—ie, decisions that fully resolve a dispute on the merits and leave nothing for the district court to do but enforce the judgment. One such exception is the collateral-order doctrine, which allows an interlocutory appeal when:\n• a district court order conclusively resolves an important issue\n• that issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim and\n• that order is effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. In Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter, the Supreme Court held that a district court's order rejecting a party's attorney-client privilege claim (as seen here) is not a collateral order. That is because this important issue, though separate from the merits, can be effectively reviewed on appeal from the final judgment. For example, if the appellate court finds the privilege applicable, it can remand the case for a new trial without the privileged evidence. Therefore, the appellate court likely will not hear the company's appeal under the collateral-order doctrine. (Choice A) Most exceptions to the final-judgment rule are expressly authorized in 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (eg, district court certification) or other federal statutes (eg, certain orders in bankruptcy cases). Although the collateral-order doctrine is not expressly authorized by a federal statute, it is still a valid exception to the final-judgment rule because it was established by the Supreme Court. (Choice C) Even if an appellate court decision on the privilege issue would resolve the parties' dispute, it would not provide a basis for an immediate appeal under the collateral-order doctrine. (Choice D) Due process does not require the appellate court to immediately hear the appeal. Instead, appellate review will be available once the district court enters a final judgment. Educational objective: The collateral-order doctrine is an exception to the final-judgment rule. It allows an appeal before final judgment when (1) a district court order conclusively resolves an important issue, (2) that issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim, and (3) that order is effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949) (establishing the collateral-order doctrine).\n\n• Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter, 558 U.S. 100, 109 (2009) (holding that a district court order denying a party's attorney-client privilege claim is not a collateral order subject to immediate appeal).","rule":"The collateral-order doctrine is an exception to the final-judgment rule. It allows an appeal before final judgment when (1) a district court order conclusively resolves an important issue, (2) that issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim, and (3) that order is effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment.","irac":{"issue":"An employee complained to his company's human resources department that the company employed undocumented immigrants. Unbeknownst to the employee, the company was currently engaged in a class action lawsuit in which it stood accused of conspiring to lower its employees' wages by hiring undocumented workers. The company's attorney met with the employee and pressured him to recant his complaint, so it would not be used against the company in the class action lawsuit. The employee refused, and he was subsequently fired. The employee sued the company in federal court, alleging that his termination violated federal law. During discovery, the employee requested that the company turn over all documents and information concerning the company's decision to fire him. The company refused on the ground that the information was protected by the attorney-client privilege. The employee then filed a motion to compel production. The district court entered an order granting the employee's motion on the ground that the company waived this privilege. The company immediately filed a notice of appeal on the ground that the order constituted a collateral order. Will the appellate court likely hear the appeal?","rule":"The collateral-order doctrine is an exception to the final-judgment rule. It allows an appeal before final judgment when (1) a district court order conclusively resolves an important issue, (2) that issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim, and (3) that order is effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment.","analysis":"Absent narrow exceptions, 28 U.S.C. § 1291 limits appeals to final judgments—ie, decisions that fully resolve a dispute on the merits and leave nothing for the district court to do but enforce the judgment. One such exception is the collateral-order doctrine, which allows an interlocutory appeal when:\n• a district court order conclusively resolves an important issue\n• that issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim and\n• that order is effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. In Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter, the Supreme Court held that a district court's order rejecting a party's attorney-client privilege claim (as seen here) is not a collateral order. That is because this important issue, though separate from the merits, can be effectively reviewed on appeal from the final judgment. For example, if the appellate court finds the privilege applicable, it can remand the case for a new trial without the privileged evidence. Therefore, the appellate court likely will not hear the company's appeal under the collateral-order doctrine. (Choice A) Most exceptions to the final-judgment rule are expressly authorized in 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (eg, district court certification) or other federal statutes (eg, certain orders in bankruptcy cases). Although the collateral-order doctrine is not expressly authorized by a federal statute, it is still a valid exception to the final-judgment rule because it was established by the Supreme Court. (Choice C) Even if an appellate court decision on the privilege issue would resolve the parties' dispute, it would not provide a basis for an immediate appeal under the collateral-order doctrine. (Choice D) Due process does not require the appellate court to immediately hear the appeal. Instead, appellate review will be available once the district court enters a final judgment. Educational objective: The collateral-order doctrine is an exception to the final-judgment rule. It allows an appeal before final judgment when (1) a district court order conclusively resolves an important issue, (2) that issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim, and (3) that order is effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949) (establishing the collateral-order doctrine).\n\n• Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter, 558 U.S. 100, 109 (2009) (holding that a district court order denying a party's attorney-client privilege claim is not a collateral order subject to immediate appeal).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949) (establishing the collateral-order doctrine).\n\n• Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter, 558 U.S. 100, 109 (2009) (holding that a district court order denying a party's attorney-client privilege claim is not a collateral order subject to immediate appeal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[408],"sourceAnswerPages":[409,410],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名雇员向公司人力资源部门投诉，公司雇用无证移民。雇员不知道，公司当时正面临一宗集体诉讼，被指控通过雇用无证工人合谋压低雇员工资。公司律师与雇员会面，施压要求他撤回投诉，以免在集体诉讼中被用来对付公司。雇员拒绝，之后被解雇。雇员在联邦法院起诉公司，主张解雇违反联邦法律。证据开示期间，雇员请求公司移交关于解雇决定的全部文件和信息。公司以信息受律师—客户特权保护为由拒绝。雇员申请强制提交，地区法院认定公司已放弃特权，发布命令批准动议。公司立即提交上诉通知，主张该命令属于附带命令。上诉法院可能审理该上诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为附带命令原则没有得到联邦制定法明确授权。","B":"不会，因为该问题可在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查。","C":"会，因为上诉法院对该问题的决定很可能解决争议。","D":"会，因为正当程序要求上诉法院立即审理。"},"explanationZh":"除狭窄例外外，28 U.S.C. § 1291 将上诉限制于终局判决，即完全解决争议实体、地区法院除执行判决外无其他事项的决定。\n\n附带命令原则是一项例外，在符合以下条件时允许中间上诉：\n• 地区法院命令终局性地解决重要问题；\n• 该问题与基础请求实体相分离；并且\n• 该命令无法在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查。\n\n最高法院在 Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter 案认定，地区法院驳回当事人律师—客户特权主张的命令（本题即如此）不属于附带命令。原因是，该重要问题虽与实体相分离，却可在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查。例如，上诉法院若认定特权适用，可以发回案件，在不采用特权证据的情况下重新审判。因此，上诉法院很可能不会依据附带命令原则立即审理公司的上诉。\n\n（选项 A）终局判决规则的大多数例外由 28 U.S.C. § 1292 或其他联邦法律明确授权。附带命令原则虽未由联邦制定法明文授权，仍是有效例外，因为由最高法院确立。\n\n（选项 C）即使上诉法院关于特权问题的决定会解决双方争议，也不构成依附带命令原则立即上诉的依据。\n\n（选项 D）正当程序不要求上诉法院立即审理；地区法院登记终局判决后仍可上诉审查。\n\n学习目标：附带命令原则是终局判决规则的例外，允许在终局判决前就符合以下条件的命令上诉：(1) 终局性地解决重要问题；(2) 问题与基础请求实体相分离；且 (3) 无法在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291（终局判决规则）。\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949)（确立附带命令原则）。\n• Mohawk Indus., Inc. v. Carpenter, 558 U.S. 100, 109 (2009)（认定驳回律师—客户特权主张的地区法院命令不是可立即上诉的附带命令）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-042"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-136","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":136,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A motorcyclist was involved in a collision with a truck. The motorcyclist sued the truck driver in state court for damage to the motorcycle. The jury returned a verdict for the truck driver, and the court entered judgment. The motorcyclist then sued the company that employed the driver and owned the truck in federal court for personal-injury damages, and the company moved to dismiss based on the state court judgment. If the court grants the company's motion, what is the likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"Claim preclusion (res judicata) bars the motorcyclist's action against the company.","B":"Issue preclusion (collateral estoppel) establishes the company's lack of negligence.","C":"The motorcyclist violated the doctrine of election of remedies.","D":"The state court judgment is the law of the case."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes relitigation of an identical claim between identical parties in a subsequent action.* Claims are identical when they arise from the same transaction or occurrence. Parties are identical if they (or those in privity with them) occupy the same roles in both actions. Employers are in privity with their employees since they are vicariously liable for their employees' actions. Here, the state court entered a valid final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict (ie, on the merits) in the motorcyclist's suit against the truck driver. The motorcyclist then sued the truck driver's employer in federal court. The claims in both suits are identical because they arose from the same collision. The parties are also identical since the defendants in both actions—company (employer) and driver (employee), respectively—are in privity. Therefore, the company's motion would likely be granted because claim preclusion bars the action. *If the plaintiff wins in the first action, the claim merges with the judgment. If the plaintiff loses in the first action, the claim is barred by the judgment. Both preclude the plaintiff from asserting the same claim against the same party in future litigation. (Choice B) Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) prohibits relitigating issues of law or fact that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. Here, the truck driver's negligence is the issue in both actions. But the jury may not have determined this issue in the first action since it could have found in the driver's favor based on the motorcyclist's contributory negligence. (Choice C) The election-of-remedies doctrine was a common law rule that forbade plaintiffs from asserting alternative or inconsistent remedies. However, this doctrine does not apply in federal court. (Choice D) The law-of-the-case doctrine prohibits the relitigation of legal issues decided at successive stages of the same case—eg, a trial court must apply an appellate court's legal determinations when a case is remanded. This doctrine does not apply here since there are two different cases. Educational objective: Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits prohibits relitigating identical claims between identical parties. Claims are identical when they arise from the same transaction or occurrence. Parties are identical if they (or their privies) occupy the same roles in both actions. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining identical claims).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 51 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining that employers are in privity with their employees and are identical parties for preclusion purposes).","rule":"Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits prohibits relitigating identical claims between identical parties. Claims are identical when they arise from the same transaction or occurrence. Parties are identical if they (or their privies) occupy the same roles in both actions.","irac":{"issue":"A motorcyclist was involved in a collision with a truck. The motorcyclist sued the truck driver in state court for damage to the motorcycle. The jury returned a verdict for the truck driver, and the court entered judgment. The motorcyclist then sued the company that employed the driver and owned the truck in federal court for personal-injury damages, and the company moved to dismiss based on the state court judgment. If the court grants the company's motion, what is the likely explanation?","rule":"Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits prohibits relitigating identical claims between identical parties. Claims are identical when they arise from the same transaction or occurrence. Parties are identical if they (or their privies) occupy the same roles in both actions.","analysis":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes relitigation of an identical claim between identical parties in a subsequent action.* Claims are identical when they arise from the same transaction or occurrence. Parties are identical if they (or those in privity with them) occupy the same roles in both actions. Employers are in privity with their employees since they are vicariously liable for their employees' actions. Here, the state court entered a valid final judgment consistent with the jury's verdict (ie, on the merits) in the motorcyclist's suit against the truck driver. The motorcyclist then sued the truck driver's employer in federal court. The claims in both suits are identical because they arose from the same collision. The parties are also identical since the defendants in both actions—company (employer) and driver (employee), respectively—are in privity. Therefore, the company's motion would likely be granted because claim preclusion bars the action. *If the plaintiff wins in the first action, the claim merges with the judgment. If the plaintiff loses in the first action, the claim is barred by the judgment. Both preclude the plaintiff from asserting the same claim against the same party in future litigation. (Choice B) Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) prohibits relitigating issues of law or fact that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. Here, the truck driver's negligence is the issue in both actions. But the jury may not have determined this issue in the first action since it could have found in the driver's favor based on the motorcyclist's contributory negligence. (Choice C) The election-of-remedies doctrine was a common law rule that forbade plaintiffs from asserting alternative or inconsistent remedies. However, this doctrine does not apply in federal court. (Choice D) The law-of-the-case doctrine prohibits the relitigation of legal issues decided at successive stages of the same case—eg, a trial court must apply an appellate court's legal determinations when a case is remanded. This doctrine does not apply here since there are two different cases. Educational objective: Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits prohibits relitigating identical claims between identical parties. Claims are identical when they arise from the same transaction or occurrence. Parties are identical if they (or their privies) occupy the same roles in both actions. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining identical claims).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 51 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining that employers are in privity with their employees and are identical parties for preclusion purposes).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining identical claims).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 51 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining that employers are in privity with their employees and are identical parties for preclusion purposes).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[411],"sourceAnswerPages":[412,413],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名摩托车手与一辆卡车发生碰撞。摩托车手在州法院起诉卡车驾驶员，请求赔偿摩托车损失。陪审团判卡车驾驶员胜诉，法院登记判决。摩托车手随后在联邦法院起诉雇用驾驶员并拥有卡车的公司，请求人身伤害赔偿。公司依据州法院判决申请驳回。若法院批准，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"请求权排除（既判力）禁止摩托车手对公司提起诉讼。","B":"争点排除确立公司不存在过失。","C":"摩托车手违反救济选择原则。","D":"州法院判决构成本案法律原则。"},"explanationZh":"请求权排除规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止在后续诉讼中，相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。* 请求源于同一交易或事件时即为相同；当事人或其具有法律利益关系者在两案中占据相同地位时，即为相同当事人。雇主须为雇员行为承担替代责任，所以二者具有法律利益关系。\n\n本题州法院在摩托车手对卡车驾驶员的诉讼中，依据陪审团裁决登记有效的实体终局判决。摩托车手随后在联邦法院起诉驾驶员的雇主。两案请求均源于同一次碰撞，所以相同；两案被告分别为公司（雇主）和驾驶员（雇员），二者具有法律利益关系，故当事人也相同。因此，公司动议很可能获准，因为请求权排除禁止该诉讼。\n\n*原告在第一案胜诉时，请求与判决合并；原告在第一案败诉时，请求被判决禁止。两者均阻止原告在后续诉讼中对相同当事人提出相同请求。\n\n（选项 B）争点排除禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的法律或事实问题。卡车驾驶员过失是两案问题，但陪审团在第一案中可能未裁定该问题，因为也可能基于摩托车手的共同过失判驾驶员胜诉。\n\n（选项 C）救济选择原则是禁止原告提出备选或不一致救济的普通法规则，但不适用于联邦法院。\n\n（选项 D）本案法律原则禁止在同一案件连续阶段重新争讼已经裁定的法律问题，例如案件发回时审判法院必须适用上诉法院法律认定。本题是两个不同案件，不适用。\n\n学习目标：依据请求权排除，有效的实体终局判决禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。请求源于同一交易或事件时相同；当事人或其具有法律利益关系者在两案中占据相同地位时相同。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（解释相同请求）。\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 51 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（说明雇主与雇员具有法律利益关系，在排除问题上属于相同当事人）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-043"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-137","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":137,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A restaurant owner sued its supplier in a federal district court for breach of contract and trademark infringement. The owner sought $80,000 in damages and a permanent injunction. The supplier filed an answer in which he denied the allegations set forth in the complaint and demanded a jury trial. The district court denied the supplier's jury-trial demand on the ground that the equitable claim predominated the case. The supplier filed a motion to reconsider, laying out all the precedents clearly requiring a jury in the case. The court entered an order denying the supplier's motion. What should the supplier do to best protect his right to a jury trial?","choices":{"A":"File a motion for a protective order.","B":"File a motion for recusal.","C":"File a motion to correct the mistake.","D":"File a petition for a writ of mandamus."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Absent limited exceptions, a party cannot seek appellate review of a district court's order until a final judgment has been entered. A petition for a writ of mandamus is one such exception. It requests that an appellate court direct a district court to do or refrain from doing something when exceptional circumstances clearly exist and no other adequate remedy is available. As a result, this writ is appropriate only in extremely limited situations—eg:\n• when a district court clearly exercises unlawful jurisdiction\n• when a district court clearly usurps another branch's power and threatens separation of powers\n• to protect a party's Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial The supplier should therefore file a petition for a writ of mandamus to protect his jury-trial right. (Choice A) Protective orders are primarily issued to protect a party from annoyance, embarrassment, or undue expense during discovery. A protective order would not protect the supplier's right to a jury trial. (Choice B) A motion for recusal requests that the judge presiding over the case excuse him/herself from the case due to bias or prejudice against a party—neither of which is seen here. (Choice C) A motion to correct a mistake is appropriate when a judgment or order contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission—not an erroneous interpretation of law. Therefore, this is not a basis for the supplier to protect his right to a jury trial. Educational objective: An appellate court may grant a petition for a writ of mandamus prior to entry of a final judgment when exceptional circumstances clearly exist—eg, denial of the right to a jury trial—and there is no other adequate remedy. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1651 (writ of mandamus).\n\n• Dairy Queen, Inc. v. Wood, 369 U.S. 469, 472 (1962) (stating that appellate courts have an obligation to \"grant mandamus where necessary to protect the constitutional right to trial by jury\").","rule":"An appellate court may grant a petition for a writ of mandamus prior to entry of a final judgment when exceptional circumstances clearly exist—eg, denial of the right to a jury trial—and there is no other adequate remedy.","irac":{"issue":"A restaurant owner sued its supplier in a federal district court for breach of contract and trademark infringement. The owner sought $80,000 in damages and a permanent injunction. The supplier filed an answer in which he denied the allegations set forth in the complaint and demanded a jury trial. The district court denied the supplier's jury-trial demand on the ground that the equitable claim predominated the case. The supplier filed a motion to reconsider, laying out all the precedents clearly requiring a jury in the case. The court entered an order denying the supplier's motion. What should the supplier do to best protect his right to a jury trial?","rule":"An appellate court may grant a petition for a writ of mandamus prior to entry of a final judgment when exceptional circumstances clearly exist—eg, denial of the right to a jury trial—and there is no other adequate remedy.","analysis":"Absent limited exceptions, a party cannot seek appellate review of a district court's order until a final judgment has been entered. A petition for a writ of mandamus is one such exception. It requests that an appellate court direct a district court to do or refrain from doing something when exceptional circumstances clearly exist and no other adequate remedy is available. As a result, this writ is appropriate only in extremely limited situations—eg:\n• when a district court clearly exercises unlawful jurisdiction\n• when a district court clearly usurps another branch's power and threatens separation of powers\n• to protect a party's Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial The supplier should therefore file a petition for a writ of mandamus to protect his jury-trial right. (Choice A) Protective orders are primarily issued to protect a party from annoyance, embarrassment, or undue expense during discovery. A protective order would not protect the supplier's right to a jury trial. (Choice B) A motion for recusal requests that the judge presiding over the case excuse him/herself from the case due to bias or prejudice against a party—neither of which is seen here. (Choice C) A motion to correct a mistake is appropriate when a judgment or order contains a clerical mistake or a mistake arising from an oversight or omission—not an erroneous interpretation of law. Therefore, this is not a basis for the supplier to protect his right to a jury trial. Educational objective: An appellate court may grant a petition for a writ of mandamus prior to entry of a final judgment when exceptional circumstances clearly exist—eg, denial of the right to a jury trial—and there is no other adequate remedy. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1651 (writ of mandamus).\n\n• Dairy Queen, Inc. v. Wood, 369 U.S. 469, 472 (1962) (stating that appellate courts have an obligation to \"grant mandamus where necessary to protect the constitutional right to trial by jury\").","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1651 (writ of mandamus).\n\n• Dairy Queen, Inc. v. Wood, 369 U.S. 469, 472 (1962) (stating that appellate courts have an obligation to \"grant mandamus where necessary to protect the constitutional right to trial by jury\").","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[414],"sourceAnswerPages":[415],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家餐馆老板在联邦地区法院起诉其供应商违约及商标侵权。老板请求 80,000 美元损害赔偿和永久禁令。供应商提交答辩状，否认起诉状中的指控并要求陪审团审判。地区法院以衡平法请求在案件中占主导地位为由，驳回供应商的陪审团审判请求。供应商提出复议动议，清楚列明所有明确要求本案由陪审团审理的先例。法院发布命令驳回复议动议。供应商应采取什么措施，才能最大程度保护其陪审团审判权？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请保护令。","B":"申请法官回避。","C":"申请纠正错误。","D":"申请签发强制令。"},"explanationZh":"除有限例外外，当事人必须等到终局判决登记后，才能请求上诉审查地区法院的命令。申请签发强制令就是其中一项例外。在明显存在特殊情形且没有其他充分救济时，该申请请求上诉法院命令地区法院实施或停止实施某项行为。因此，强制令只适用于极其有限的情形，例如：\n• 地区法院明显违法行使管辖权；\n• 地区法院明显侵夺另一政府部门的权力并威胁权力分立；或\n• 保护当事人依据第七修正案享有的陪审团审判权。\n\n因此，供应商应申请签发强制令，以保护其陪审团审判权。\n\n（选项 A）保护令主要用于保护当事人在证据开示期间免受烦扰、难堪或不当费用。保护令不能保护供应商的陪审团审判权。\n\n（选项 B）法官回避动议请求主持案件的法官因对一方存在偏见或成见而退出案件；本题均未出现这两种情况。\n\n（选项 C）判决或命令含有笔误，或因疏忽、遗漏产生错误时，可以申请纠正错误；但对法律作出错误解释不属于这种情形。因此，这不能成为供应商保护其陪审团审判权的依据。\n\n学习目标：在明显存在特殊情形（例如陪审团审判权被剥夺）且没有其他充分救济时，上诉法院可以在终局判决登记前批准强制令申请。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1651（强制令）。\n• Dairy Queen, Inc. v. Wood, 369 U.S. 469, 472 (1962)（指出上诉法院有义务“在保护宪法规定的陪审团审判权所必需时签发强制令”）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-042"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-138","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":138,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A distributor brought a diversity action against a manufacturer in a federal court in State A for breach of contract. The contract was signed in State B and allegedly breached in State C. The distributor filed this action two years after the alleged breach occurred and nine months after discovering the alleged breach. The manufacturer immediately moved to dismiss the action on the ground that the statute of limitations has expired. The statute of limitations in State A is one year from the date on which the alleged breach occurred, and State A applies the law of the state where the alleged breach occurred. The statute of limitations in State B is six months from the date on which the alleged breach was discovered, and State B applies the law of the state where the alleged breach occurred. The statute of limitations in State C is one year from the date on which the alleged breach was discovered, and State C applies the law of the state where the contract was signed. Is the court likely to grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss the action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the manufacturer failed to file an answer.","B":"No, because the statute of limitations under State C law has not expired.","C":"Yes, because the statute of limitations under State A law has expired.","D":"Yes, because the statute of limitations under State B law has expired."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Examples of procedural & substantive issues\n\nProcedural • Filing deadlines\n• Court rules & procedures (processes & procedures)\n• Discovery practices\n• Rules of evidence Substantive • Elements of claim or defense\n• Burdens of proof (legal rights & duties)\n• Statutes of limitations Federal courts sitting in diversity (as seen here) must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. Substantive issues include the elements of a claim or defense, the applicable burden of proof, and the statute of limitations. Therefore, state law governs the statute of limitations for the distributor's breach-of-contract action against the manufacturer. The federal court must then determine which state's law governs the issue by applying the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits. Here, the federal court sits in State A, so it must apply State A's choice-of-law rules. These rules require a court to apply the law of the state where the alleged breach occurred. Since the alleged breach occurred in State C, State C's law governs the statute of limitations in this action (Choices C & D). State C's statute of limitations for breach-of-contract claims is one year from the date that the alleged breach was discovered. Since the distributor filed this action nine months after discovering the manufacturer's alleged breach, the statute of limitations has not expired. Therefore, the federal court will likely deny the manufacturer's motion to dismiss the action. (Choice A) FRCP 12(b) allows a defendant to file a motion to dismiss before filing an answer. One basis for such a motion is that the plaintiff failed to state a claim on which relief can be granted—eg, that the statute of limitations on the claim expired (as seen here). Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, statute of limitations). To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Guaranty Trust Co. v. York, 326 U.S. 99, 110 (1945) (holding that state statute of limitations applies in a federal diversity action).\n\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits).","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, statute of limitations). To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","irac":{"issue":"A distributor brought a diversity action against a manufacturer in a federal court in State A for breach of contract. The contract was signed in State B and allegedly breached in State C. The distributor filed this action two years after the alleged breach occurred and nine months after discovering the alleged breach. The manufacturer immediately moved to dismiss the action on the ground that the statute of limitations has expired. The statute of limitations in State A is one year from the date on which the alleged breach occurred, and State A applies the law of the state where the alleged breach occurred. The statute of limitations in State B is six months from the date on which the alleged breach was discovered, and State B applies the law of the state where the alleged breach occurred. The statute of limitations in State C is one year from the date on which the alleged breach was discovered, and State C applies the law of the state where the contract was signed. Is the court likely to grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss the action?","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, statute of limitations). To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","analysis":"Examples of procedural & substantive issues\n\nProcedural • Filing deadlines\n• Court rules & procedures (processes & procedures)\n• Discovery practices\n• Rules of evidence Substantive • Elements of claim or defense\n• Burdens of proof (legal rights & duties)\n• Statutes of limitations Federal courts sitting in diversity (as seen here) must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. Substantive issues include the elements of a claim or defense, the applicable burden of proof, and the statute of limitations. Therefore, state law governs the statute of limitations for the distributor's breach-of-contract action against the manufacturer. The federal court must then determine which state's law governs the issue by applying the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits. Here, the federal court sits in State A, so it must apply State A's choice-of-law rules. These rules require a court to apply the law of the state where the alleged breach occurred. Since the alleged breach occurred in State C, State C's law governs the statute of limitations in this action (Choices C & D). State C's statute of limitations for breach-of-contract claims is one year from the date that the alleged breach was discovered. Since the distributor filed this action nine months after discovering the manufacturer's alleged breach, the statute of limitations has not expired. Therefore, the federal court will likely deny the manufacturer's motion to dismiss the action. (Choice A) FRCP 12(b) allows a defendant to file a motion to dismiss before filing an answer. One basis for such a motion is that the plaintiff failed to state a claim on which relief can be granted—eg, that the statute of limitations on the claim expired (as seen here). Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, statute of limitations). To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Guaranty Trust Co. v. York, 326 U.S. 99, 110 (1945) (holding that state statute of limitations applies in a federal diversity action).\n\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Guaranty Trust Co. v. York, 326 U.S. 99, 110 (1945) (holding that state statute of limitations applies in a federal diversity action).\n\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[417],"sourceAnswerPages":[418,419],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家经销商在 A 州联邦法院对一家制造商提起多元州籍违约诉讼。合同在 B 州签订，据称在 C 州遭到违反。经销商在据称违约发生两年后、发现该违约九个月后提起本诉。制造商立即以诉讼时效已经届满为由申请驳回。A 州的诉讼时效为据称违约发生之日起一年，并适用据称违约发生州的法律。B 州的诉讼时效为发现据称违约之日起六个月，并适用据称违约发生州的法律。C 州的诉讼时效为发现据称违约之日起一年，并适用合同签订州的法律。法院可能批准制造商的驳回动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为制造商未提交答辩状。","B":"不会，因为依据 C 州法律，诉讼时效尚未届满。","C":"会，因为依据 A 州法律，诉讼时效已经届满。","D":"会，因为依据 B 州法律，诉讼时效已经届满。"},"explanationZh":"程序性问题与实体性问题示例：\n\n程序性问题：\n• 提交期限\n• 法院规则与程序（流程与手续）\n• 证据开示惯例\n• 证据规则\n\n实体性问题：\n• 请求或抗辩的构成要件\n• 证明责任（法律权利与义务）\n• 诉讼时效\n\n行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院（本题即如此）必须对程序性问题适用联邦法，对实体性问题适用州法。实体性问题包括请求或抗辩的构成要件、适用的证明责任和诉讼时效。因此，经销商对制造商所提违约诉讼的诉讼时效由州法支配。\n\n随后，联邦法院必须适用法院所在地州的法律选择规则，确定由哪个州的法律支配该问题。本题联邦法院位于 A 州，所以必须适用 A 州的法律选择规则。这些规则要求法院适用据称违约发生州的法律。由于据称违约发生在 C 州，本诉的诉讼时效由 C 州法律支配（选项 C、D）。\n\nC 州对违约请求规定的诉讼时效为发现据称违约之日起一年。经销商在发现制造商据称违约九个月后提起本诉，所以诉讼时效尚未届满。因此，联邦法院很可能驳回制造商的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）FRCP 12(b) 允许被告在提交答辩状前提出驳回动议。该动议的一项依据是原告未提出可获救济的请求，例如请求的诉讼时效已经届满（本题即如此）。\n\n学习目标：在联邦多元州籍诉讼中，法院必须对程序性问题适用联邦法，对实体性问题（例如诉讼时效）适用州法。为确定适用哪个州的实体法，联邦法院必须适用其所在地州的法律选择规则。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Guaranty Trust Co. v. York, 326 U.S. 99, 110 (1945)（判定在联邦多元州籍诉讼中适用州诉讼时效）。\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941)（说明行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须适用其所在地州的法律选择规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-139","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":139,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A woman domiciled in State A was using a rideshare program while traveling in State B when her driver from State B lost control of his vehicle and collided with a building. The woman suffered serious injuries. A police report determined that the driver was heavily intoxicated at the time of the crash. The woman brought a diversity action against the driver in a federal district court in State A, alleging that the driver's negligence caused her $100,000 in damages. The driver filed a motion to transfer the case to a federal court in State B, which was granted. What law of negligence should the federal court in State B apply in this action?","choices":{"A":"The court should apply the federal common law of negligence.","B":"The court should consider the negligence law of both State A and State B and apply the law that the court finds most appropriate.","C":"The court should determine which state's negligence law a state court in State A would apply and apply that law.","D":"The court should determine which state's negligence law a state court in State B would apply and apply that law."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When a case is transferred from one federal court to another (as seen here), the receiving court must apply the appropriate choice-of-law rules to determine which substantive laws govern the action. The appropriate choice-of-law rules depend on whether venue was proper or improper in the original court. Venue is proper in any federal district where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial portion of the events occurred or the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies.\n\nHere, venue was improper in State A (original court) because the driver does not reside there and no events occurred there. Venue is proper in State B because the driver resides and the events occurred there. Next, the receiving court must determine whether the case arises under federal-question or diversity jurisdiction. This case arises under diversity jurisdiction because the negligence claim arises under state law, the parties are citizens of different states (States A and B), and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. As a result, the receiving court (State B federal court) should apply the choice-of-law rules of the forum state (State B), determine which state's negligence law a state court in State B would apply, and apply that law in this action.* *Had this case arisen under federal-question jurisdiction, federal choice-of-law rules would have applied. (Choice A) There is no federal common law of negligence, so federal courts must adhere to state law on this substantive matter. (Choice B) The court cannot review the negligence law of both states and select the law that the court finds most appropriate because the court would be improperly developing its own federal choice-of-law rules. (Choice C) The choice-of-law rules of the original court would have applied upon transfer if venue had been proper in that court. Educational objective: Upon transfer, the receiving court applies the forum state's choice-of-law rules when (1) venue was improper in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406 (transfer when venue is improper).\n\n• 14D Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3827 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining that the choice-of-law rules of the receiving court apply upon transfer when venue was improper in the original court).","rule":"Upon transfer, the receiving court applies the forum state's choice-of-law rules when (1) venue was improper in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"A woman domiciled in State A was using a rideshare program while traveling in State B when her driver from State B lost control of his vehicle and collided with a building. The woman suffered serious injuries. A police report determined that the driver was heavily intoxicated at the time of the crash. The woman brought a diversity action against the driver in a federal district court in State A, alleging that the driver's negligence caused her $100,000 in damages. The driver filed a motion to transfer the case to a federal court in State B, which was granted. What law of negligence should the federal court in State B apply in this action?","rule":"Upon transfer, the receiving court applies the forum state's choice-of-law rules when (1) venue was improper in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction.","analysis":"When a case is transferred from one federal court to another (as seen here), the receiving court must apply the appropriate choice-of-law rules to determine which substantive laws govern the action. The appropriate choice-of-law rules depend on whether venue was proper or improper in the original court. Venue is proper in any federal district where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial portion of the events occurred or the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies.\n\nHere, venue was improper in State A (original court) because the driver does not reside there and no events occurred there. Venue is proper in State B because the driver resides and the events occurred there. Next, the receiving court must determine whether the case arises under federal-question or diversity jurisdiction. This case arises under diversity jurisdiction because the negligence claim arises under state law, the parties are citizens of different states (States A and B), and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. As a result, the receiving court (State B federal court) should apply the choice-of-law rules of the forum state (State B), determine which state's negligence law a state court in State B would apply, and apply that law in this action.* *Had this case arisen under federal-question jurisdiction, federal choice-of-law rules would have applied. (Choice A) There is no federal common law of negligence, so federal courts must adhere to state law on this substantive matter. (Choice B) The court cannot review the negligence law of both states and select the law that the court finds most appropriate because the court would be improperly developing its own federal choice-of-law rules. (Choice C) The choice-of-law rules of the original court would have applied upon transfer if venue had been proper in that court. Educational objective: Upon transfer, the receiving court applies the forum state's choice-of-law rules when (1) venue was improper in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406 (transfer when venue is improper).\n\n• 14D Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3827 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining that the choice-of-law rules of the receiving court apply upon transfer when venue was improper in the original court).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (venue).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406 (transfer when venue is improper).\n\n• 14D Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3827 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining that the choice-of-law rules of the receiving court apply upon transfer when venue was improper in the original court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[420],"sourceAnswerPages":[421,422],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名住所位于 A 州的女子在 B 州旅行时使用网约车服务，她的 B 州司机失去车辆控制并撞上一栋建筑。女子受重伤。警方报告认定，司机在撞车时严重醉酒。女子在 A 州联邦地区法院对司机提起多元州籍诉讼，主张司机的过失给她造成了 100,000 美元损害。司机申请将案件移送至 B 州联邦法院，法院批准了该动议。B 州联邦法院在本诉中应适用哪一州的过失法？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院应适用联邦过失普通法。","B":"法院应同时考虑 A 州和 B 州的过失法，并适用法院认为最适当的法律。","C":"法院应确定 A 州州法院会适用哪一州的过失法，并适用该法律。","D":"法院应确定 B 州州法院会适用哪一州的过失法，并适用该法律。"},"explanationZh":"案件从一个联邦法院移送至另一个联邦法院时（本题即如此），受移送法院必须适用适当的法律选择规则，确定由哪些实体法支配该诉讼。适当的法律选择规则取决于原法院的审判地是否适当。以下任何联邦司法地区均为适当审判地：\n• 任何被告居住的地区，但前提是所有被告均居住在同一州；\n• 构成诉讼基础的重大部分事件发生地，或争议财产所在地；或\n• 对任何被告拥有对人管辖权的地区——但仅在上述两项规定均不适用时。\n\n本题 A 州（原法院）的审判地不适当，因为司机不居住于该州，且没有任何相关事件发生于该州。B 州审判地适当，因为司机居住于该州，相关事件也发生于该州。\n\n接下来，受移送法院必须确定案件的事项管辖权源于联邦问题还是多元州籍管辖权。本案源于多元州籍管辖权，因为过失请求基于州法，双方是不同州（A 州和 B 州）的公民，且争议金额超过 75,000 美元。因此，受移送法院（B 州联邦法院）应适用法院州（B 州）的法律选择规则，确定 B 州州法院会适用哪一州的过失法，并在本诉中适用该法律。*\n\n*若本案源于联邦问题管辖权，则适用联邦法律选择规则。\n\n（选项 A）不存在联邦过失普通法，所以联邦法院必须就这一实体问题遵循州法。\n\n（选项 B）法院不能审查两州的过失法，再选用其认为最适当的法律；否则，法院就会不当地创设自己的联邦法律选择规则。\n\n（选项 C）如果原法院的审判地适当，移送后应适用原法院的法律选择规则。\n\n学习目标：案件移送后，若 (1) 原法院的审判地不适当，且 (2) 事项管辖权源于多元州籍管辖权，受移送法院适用法院州的法律选择规则。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391（审判地）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1406（审判地不适当时的移送）。\n• 14D Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 3827 (4th ed. 2020)（说明原法院审判地不适当时，移送后适用受移送法院的法律选择规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-044"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-140","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":140,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man filed a federal diversity action against a bus company, seeking damages for injuries he had sustained in an accident while riding a bus owned by the company. The man demanded a jury trial. After the parties' attorneys examined the prospective jurors and exercised their challenges, six jurors and two alternate jurors were chosen. During the trial, two jurors became ill and were replaced by the alternate jurors. At the conclusion of the trial, a third juror also became ill, and the court excused that juror. The parties' attorneys stipulated to the return of a verdict from a five-person jury. The jury then deliberated and returned a verdict for the company. The man timely filed a motion for a new trial, arguing that the five-person jury was not large enough to return a verdict. Should the court grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court properly excused the three jurors due to illness.","B":"No, because the parties stipulated to a verdict from a jury of fewer than six jurors.","C":"Yes, because there must be at least six jurors on a federal civil jury.","D":"Yes, because there must be at least 12 jurors on a federal civil jury."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 48, a jury must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. Each juror must participate in the verdict unless excused for good cause (eg, illness, family emergency). And unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and returned by at least 6 jurors. A court should grant a party's motion for a new trial if any of these requirements is not met. Here, the jury began with 6 members and 2 alternates. During the trial, 2 jurors were excused for illness (good cause) and replaced by alternates, and a third juror was also excused for illness at the conclusion of the trial. This left 5 jurors to return a verdict. And though a verdict normally requires at least 6 jurors, the parties stipulated to a 5-person jury (Choice C). Therefore, the court should deny the man's motion for a new trial. (Choice A) Even if jurors are properly excused for good cause, at least 6 jurors must return a verdict unless the parties stipulate otherwise. (Choice D) FRCP 48 requires that a jury begin with a minimum of 6 jurors—not 12 (which is the maximum). Educational objective: FRCP 48 requires that a jury begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 members—unless the parties stipulate otherwise. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48 (number of jurors and verdict).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(c) (excusing a juror for good cause).","rule":"FRCP 48 requires that a jury begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 members—unless the parties stipulate otherwise.","irac":{"issue":"A man filed a federal diversity action against a bus company, seeking damages for injuries he had sustained in an accident while riding a bus owned by the company. The man demanded a jury trial. After the parties' attorneys examined the prospective jurors and exercised their challenges, six jurors and two alternate jurors were chosen. During the trial, two jurors became ill and were replaced by the alternate jurors. At the conclusion of the trial, a third juror also became ill, and the court excused that juror. The parties' attorneys stipulated to the return of a verdict from a five-person jury. The jury then deliberated and returned a verdict for the company. The man timely filed a motion for a new trial, arguing that the five-person jury was not large enough to return a verdict. Should the court grant the motion?","rule":"FRCP 48 requires that a jury begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 members—unless the parties stipulate otherwise.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 48, a jury must begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. Each juror must participate in the verdict unless excused for good cause (eg, illness, family emergency). And unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and returned by at least 6 jurors. A court should grant a party's motion for a new trial if any of these requirements is not met. Here, the jury began with 6 members and 2 alternates. During the trial, 2 jurors were excused for illness (good cause) and replaced by alternates, and a third juror was also excused for illness at the conclusion of the trial. This left 5 jurors to return a verdict. And though a verdict normally requires at least 6 jurors, the parties stipulated to a 5-person jury (Choice C). Therefore, the court should deny the man's motion for a new trial. (Choice A) Even if jurors are properly excused for good cause, at least 6 jurors must return a verdict unless the parties stipulate otherwise. (Choice D) FRCP 48 requires that a jury begin with a minimum of 6 jurors—not 12 (which is the maximum). Educational objective: FRCP 48 requires that a jury begin with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. The verdict must be unanimous and returned by a jury of at least 6 members—unless the parties stipulate otherwise. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48 (number of jurors and verdict).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(c) (excusing a juror for good cause).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48 (number of jurors and verdict).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(c) (excusing a juror for good cause).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[423],"sourceAnswerPages":[424],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子对一家公交公司提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，请求赔偿其乘坐该公司所有的公交车发生事故时所受伤害。男子要求陪审团审判。双方律师审查候选陪审员并行使回避权后，选出了六名陪审员和两名候补陪审员。审判期间，两名陪审员生病，由候补陪审员替换。审判结束时，第三名陪审员也生病，法院准许该陪审员退出。双方律师约定，由五人陪审团作出裁决。陪审团随后进行评议，并作出有利于公司的裁决。男子及时申请重新审判，主张五人陪审团人数不足，不能作出裁决。法院应批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为法院基于疾病适当准许三名陪审员退出。","B":"不应，因为双方约定由少于六人的陪审团作出裁决。","C":"应当，因为联邦民事陪审团必须至少有六名陪审员。","D":"应当，因为联邦民事陪审团必须至少有十二名陪审员。"},"explanationZh":"依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）48，陪审团开始审判时必须至少有 6 名、至多有 12 名成员。除非基于正当理由（例如疾病、家庭紧急情况）获准退出，每名陪审员都必须参与裁决。除非双方另有约定，裁决必须全体一致，并由至少 6 名陪审员作出。若其中任何一项要求未得到满足，法院应批准当事人的重新审判动议。\n\n本题陪审团开始时有 6 名成员和 2 名候补陪审员。审判期间，2 名陪审员因疾病（正当理由）获准退出并由候补陪审员替换；审判结束时，第三名陪审员也因疾病获准退出。于是只剩 5 名陪审员作出裁决。尽管裁决通常必须由至少 6 名陪审员作出，但双方已约定采用 5 人陪审团（选项 C）。因此，法院应驳回男子的重新审判动议。\n\n（选项 A）即使陪审员因正当理由适当获准退出，仍须由至少 6 名陪审员作出裁决，除非双方另有约定。\n\n（选项 D）FRCP 48 要求陪审团开始时至少有 6 名陪审员，而不是 12 名；12 名是人数上限。\n\n学习目标：FRCP 48 要求陪审团开始时至少有 6 名、至多有 12 名成员。除非双方另有约定，裁决必须全体一致，并由至少 6 名成员组成的陪审团作出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48（陪审员人数与裁决）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(c)（基于正当理由准许陪审员退出）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-036"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-141","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":141,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action in State A federal court for breach of contract and negligence. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her damages. The court entered judgment accordingly. The defendant moved for a new trial on the ground that the jury awarded excessive damages. Federal common law applies a \"shock the conscience\" test in a review of an allegedly excessive jury verdict. However, State A law applies a much more rigorous \"materially deviates\" test to prevent excessive awards. What body of law should the federal court apply to rule on the defendant's motion for a new trial?","choices":{"A":"Federal law, because the case was brought in federal court.","B":"Federal law, because the issue is procedural.","C":"State law, because a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law.","D":"State law, because the issue is substantive."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues (Choices A & C). However, there are instances when it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural—as seen here with review of a jury verdict. A court must then determine if a federal law directly addresses the issue. Federal law encompasses federal constitutional provisions, statutes, and rules—but not federal common law. As a result, no direct federal law applies here. In such a case, the Erieanalysis must be used to decide if a federal court should supplant state law with federal common law. Under this analysis, the issue will be considered substantive and state law will therefore apply if it is outcome determinative AND there is no countervailing federal policy interest. State law is outcome determinative if the failure to apply state law would result in:\n• forum-shopping – litigants will be encouraged to sue in federal court to take advantage of benefits not afforded in state court or\n\n• inequitable administration of the laws – the application of substantially different rules in federal and state court would cause unfair outcomes.\n\nHere, failure to apply State A's test will result in forum-shopping because plaintiffs will want to take advantage of the federal courts' less rigorous test. It will also cause inequity because higher awards will be allowed to stand in federal court for the same harm. As a result, the state law is outcome determinative. And since there is no federal policy that justifies not applying the State A test, whether a jury verdict is excessive is a substantive issue. Accordingly, State A's test should be used to determine this issue (Choice B). Educational objective: The Erie analysis is used when it is unclear if an issue is procedural or substantive and no direct federal law applies. Under Erie, state law applies if (1) it is outcome determinative— ie, forum-shopping or inequitable administration of the laws would result if it is not applied—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest. References\n• Gasperini v. Ctr. for Humanities, Inc., 518 U.S. 427–30 (1996) (explaining that a state law test to examine the excessiveness of jury verdicts is outcome determinative).","rule":"The Erie analysis is used when it is unclear if an issue is procedural or substantive and no direct federal law applies. Under Erie, state law applies if (1) it is outcome determinative— ie, forum-shopping or inequitable administration of the laws would result if it is not applied—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action in State A federal court for breach of contract and negligence. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff and awarded her damages. The court entered judgment accordingly. The defendant moved for a new trial on the ground that the jury awarded excessive damages. Federal common law applies a \"shock the conscience\" test in a review of an allegedly excessive jury verdict. However, State A law applies a much more rigorous \"materially deviates\" test to prevent excessive awards. What body of law should the federal court apply to rule on the defendant's motion for a new trial?","rule":"The Erie analysis is used when it is unclear if an issue is procedural or substantive and no direct federal law applies. Under Erie, state law applies if (1) it is outcome determinative— ie, forum-shopping or inequitable administration of the laws would result if it is not applied—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest.","analysis":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues (Choices A & C). However, there are instances when it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural—as seen here with review of a jury verdict. A court must then determine if a federal law directly addresses the issue. Federal law encompasses federal constitutional provisions, statutes, and rules—but not federal common law. As a result, no direct federal law applies here. In such a case, the Erieanalysis must be used to decide if a federal court should supplant state law with federal common law. Under this analysis, the issue will be considered substantive and state law will therefore apply if it is outcome determinative AND there is no countervailing federal policy interest. State law is outcome determinative if the failure to apply state law would result in:\n• forum-shopping – litigants will be encouraged to sue in federal court to take advantage of benefits not afforded in state court or\n\n• inequitable administration of the laws – the application of substantially different rules in federal and state court would cause unfair outcomes.\n\nHere, failure to apply State A's test will result in forum-shopping because plaintiffs will want to take advantage of the federal courts' less rigorous test. It will also cause inequity because higher awards will be allowed to stand in federal court for the same harm. As a result, the state law is outcome determinative. And since there is no federal policy that justifies not applying the State A test, whether a jury verdict is excessive is a substantive issue. Accordingly, State A's test should be used to determine this issue (Choice B). Educational objective: The Erie analysis is used when it is unclear if an issue is procedural or substantive and no direct federal law applies. Under Erie, state law applies if (1) it is outcome determinative— ie, forum-shopping or inequitable administration of the laws would result if it is not applied—and (2) there is no countervailing federal policy interest. References\n• Gasperini v. Ctr. for Humanities, Inc., 518 U.S. 427–30 (1996) (explaining that a state law test to examine the excessiveness of jury verdicts is outcome determinative).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Gasperini v. Ctr. for Humanities, Inc., 518 U.S. 427–30 (1996) (explaining that a state law test to examine the excessiveness of jury verdicts is outcome determinative).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[426],"sourceAnswerPages":[427],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在 A 州联邦法院提起多元州籍违约及过失诉讼。陪审团作出有利于原告的裁决，并判给她损害赔偿。法院据此登记判决。被告以陪审团判给的损害赔偿过高为由申请重新审判。联邦普通法在审查被指过高的陪审团裁决时适用“震撼良知”标准；但 A 州法律为防止裁赔过高，适用严格得多的“实质性偏离”标准。联邦法院裁定被告的重新审判动议时应适用哪一法律体系？","choicesZh":{"A":"联邦法，因为案件在联邦法院提起。","B":"联邦法，因为该问题属于程序性问题。","C":"州法，因为行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须适用州法。","D":"州法，因为该问题属于实体性问题。"},"explanationZh":"行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，对程序性问题适用联邦法（选项 A、C）。但是，有些情况下难以确定问题属于实体性还是程序性，本题对陪审团裁决的审查即属于这种情况。\n\n法院随后必须确定是否有联邦法直接处理该问题。联邦法包括联邦宪法条款、制定法和规则，但不包括联邦普通法。因此，本题没有直接适用的联邦法。在这种情况下，必须运用 Erie 分析，决定联邦法院是否应以联邦普通法取代州法。\n\n根据该分析，若该问题具有结果决定性，且不存在与之相抵的联邦政策利益，该问题就会被视为实体性问题，因而适用州法。不适用州法会产生下列后果之一时，州法具有结果决定性：\n• 挑选法院——鼓励诉讼当事人在联邦法院起诉，以利用州法院无法提供的利益；或\n• 法律适用不公平——联邦法院与州法院适用显著不同的规则，会造成不公平结果。\n\n本题若不适用 A 州的标准，就会导致挑选法院，因为原告会希望利用联邦法院较宽松的标准。这也会造成不公平，因为对于同一损害，较高的裁赔金额在联邦法院能够维持。因此，该州法具有结果决定性。又因为不存在足以支持不适用 A 州标准的联邦政策，所以陪审团裁决是否过高属于实体性问题。据此，应使用 A 州的标准裁定该问题（选项 B）。\n\n学习目标：当问题究竟属于程序性还是实体性并不明确，且没有直接适用的联邦法时，采用 Erie 分析。根据 Erie，若 (1) 州法具有结果决定性——即不适用将导致挑选法院或法律适用不公平；且 (2) 不存在与之相抵的联邦政策利益，则适用州法。\n\n参考资料：\n• Gasperini v. Ctr. for Humanities, Inc., 518 U.S. 427–30 (1996)（说明审查陪审团裁决是否过高的州法标准具有结果决定性）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-037"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-142","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":142,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A motorist sued a police officer in a federal district court for using excessive force during an arrest in violation of a federal statute. The officer moved for summary judgment on the ground that he was immune from suit. The district court entered an order denying the motion and ruling that the officer was not immune from suit as a matter of law. The officer immediately filed a notice of appeal. Does the appellate court have jurisdiction to hear the appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the appeal was not certified by the district court.","B":"No, because the district court's decision on immunity is final.","C":"Yes, because a contrary decision on appeal could terminate the action.","D":"Yes, because an order denying immunity to a government official is an appealable collateral order."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Examples of collateral orders\n\nDistrict court orders:\n\n• denying state 11th Amendment immunity\n• denying government official/employee immunity\n• vacating attachment of vessel\n• refusing to require security bond pursuant to state law\n• remanding action to state court based on abstention A final judgment generally must be entered before an appellate court can acquire jurisdiction over a case. A final judgment is a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. An order denying summary judgment (as seen here) is not final because it merely determines that there is some dispute of material fact—it does not fully resolve the underlying claim. As a result, these orders are typically not appealable. However, the collateral-order doctrine provides a narrow exception to the final-judgment rule. Under this doctrine, an interlocutory order will be characterized as final and immediately appealable if three elements are met:\n• The order conclusively resolves an important issue (the officer's immunity).\n\n• That issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim (whether the officer used excessive force).\n\n• The order cannot be effectively reviewed on appeal from a final judgment (the officer's claim of immunity will be ineffective once the case has gone to trial).\n\nTherefore, the court's order denying the police officer immunity constitutes a collateral order over which the appellate court has jurisdiction to hear an immediate appeal. (Choice A) An interlocutory order can be immediately appealed in limited circumstances— including when there is a statutory right to appeal (eg, order granting, modifying, or denying a preliminary injunction), the district court certifies the appeal, or the collateral-order doctrine applies (as seen here). (Choice B) The district court's decision on immunity is not final because it can be immediately challenged on appeal under the collateral-order doctrine. (Choice C) The fact that a contrary appellate decision could terminate the action does not provide a basis for an immediate appeal. Instead, the collateral-order doctrine authorizes the appeal at this stage.\n\nEducational objective: The collateral-order doctrine allows an immediate appeal from an interlocutory order that (1) conclusively resolves an important issue that (2) is separate from the merits of the claim and (3) cannot be effectively reviewed on appeal from a final judgment—eg, an order denying a claim of governmental immunity. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949) (establishing the collateral-order doctrine).\n\n• Plumhoff v. Rickard, 572 U.S. 765, 771–72 (2014) (explaining that an order denying government immunity is immediately appealable under the collateral-order doctrine).","rule":"The collateral-order doctrine allows an immediate appeal from an interlocutory order that (1) conclusively resolves an important issue that (2) is separate from the merits of the claim and (3) cannot be effectively reviewed on appeal from a final judgment—eg, an order denying a claim of governmental immunity.","irac":{"issue":"A motorist sued a police officer in a federal district court for using excessive force during an arrest in violation of a federal statute. The officer moved for summary judgment on the ground that he was immune from suit. The district court entered an order denying the motion and ruling that the officer was not immune from suit as a matter of law. The officer immediately filed a notice of appeal. Does the appellate court have jurisdiction to hear the appeal?","rule":"The collateral-order doctrine allows an immediate appeal from an interlocutory order that (1) conclusively resolves an important issue that (2) is separate from the merits of the claim and (3) cannot be effectively reviewed on appeal from a final judgment—eg, an order denying a claim of governmental immunity.","analysis":"Examples of collateral orders\n\nDistrict court orders:\n\n• denying state 11th Amendment immunity\n• denying government official/employee immunity\n• vacating attachment of vessel\n• refusing to require security bond pursuant to state law\n• remanding action to state court based on abstention A final judgment generally must be entered before an appellate court can acquire jurisdiction over a case. A final judgment is a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. An order denying summary judgment (as seen here) is not final because it merely determines that there is some dispute of material fact—it does not fully resolve the underlying claim. As a result, these orders are typically not appealable. However, the collateral-order doctrine provides a narrow exception to the final-judgment rule. Under this doctrine, an interlocutory order will be characterized as final and immediately appealable if three elements are met:\n• The order conclusively resolves an important issue (the officer's immunity).\n\n• That issue is separate from the merits of the underlying claim (whether the officer used excessive force).\n\n• The order cannot be effectively reviewed on appeal from a final judgment (the officer's claim of immunity will be ineffective once the case has gone to trial).\n\nTherefore, the court's order denying the police officer immunity constitutes a collateral order over which the appellate court has jurisdiction to hear an immediate appeal. (Choice A) An interlocutory order can be immediately appealed in limited circumstances— including when there is a statutory right to appeal (eg, order granting, modifying, or denying a preliminary injunction), the district court certifies the appeal, or the collateral-order doctrine applies (as seen here). (Choice B) The district court's decision on immunity is not final because it can be immediately challenged on appeal under the collateral-order doctrine. (Choice C) The fact that a contrary appellate decision could terminate the action does not provide a basis for an immediate appeal. Instead, the collateral-order doctrine authorizes the appeal at this stage.\n\nEducational objective: The collateral-order doctrine allows an immediate appeal from an interlocutory order that (1) conclusively resolves an important issue that (2) is separate from the merits of the claim and (3) cannot be effectively reviewed on appeal from a final judgment—eg, an order denying a claim of governmental immunity. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949) (establishing the collateral-order doctrine).\n\n• Plumhoff v. Rickard, 572 U.S. 765, 771–72 (2014) (explaining that an order denying government immunity is immediately appealable under the collateral-order doctrine).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949) (establishing the collateral-order doctrine).\n\n• Plumhoff v. Rickard, 572 U.S. 765, 771–72 (2014) (explaining that an order denying government immunity is immediately appealable under the collateral-order doctrine).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[429],"sourceAnswerPages":[430,431],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名驾车者在联邦地区法院起诉一名警察，主张该警察在逮捕过程中违反联邦制定法，使用了过度武力。警察以自己享有诉讼豁免为由申请简易判决。地区法院发布命令驳回该动议，并裁定从法律上看，警察不享有诉讼豁免。警察立即提交上诉通知。上诉法院是否有管辖权审理该上诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为该上诉未经地区法院核准。","B":"没有，因为地区法院关于豁免的决定是终局决定。","C":"有，因为上诉中作出相反决定可能终结本诉。","D":"有，因为驳回政府官员豁免的命令属于可上诉的附带命令。"},"explanationZh":"附带命令示例：\n\n地区法院作出的下列命令：\n• 驳回州依据第十一修正案主张的豁免；\n• 驳回政府官员或雇员主张的豁免；\n• 解除对船舶的扣押；\n• 拒绝要求依州法提供担保保证金；\n• 基于回避管辖原则将诉讼发回州法院。\n\n通常只有登记终局判决后，上诉法院才能取得案件管辖权。终局判决是完全解决争议实体，使法院除执行判决外别无事项的决定。驳回简易判决动议的命令（本题即如此）并非终局，因为它只确定存在某项重大事实争议，并未完全解决基础请求。因此，这类命令通常不能上诉。\n\n但是，附带命令原则对终局判决规则规定了一项狭窄例外。根据该原则，一项中间命令满足以下三个要件时，会被视为终局且可以立即上诉：\n• 该命令终局性地解决一项重要问题（警察的豁免）；\n• 该问题与基础请求的实体相分离（警察是否使用过度武力）；并且\n• 该命令无法在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查（案件一旦进入审判，警察的豁免主张就失去实效）。\n\n因此，法院驳回警察豁免的命令构成附带命令，上诉法院有管辖权审理即时上诉。\n\n（选项 A）中间命令在有限情形下可以立即上诉，包括法律规定上诉权（例如批准、修改或驳回初步禁令的命令）、地区法院核准上诉，或附带命令原则适用（本题即如此）。\n\n（选项 B）地区法院关于豁免的决定并非终局判决，因为可以依据附带命令原则立即提出上诉质疑。\n\n（选项 C）上诉法院作出相反决定可能终结本诉这一事实，并不构成立即上诉的依据；允许现阶段上诉的是附带命令原则。\n\n学习目标：附带命令原则允许对符合下列条件的中间命令立即上诉：(1) 终局性地解决一项重要问题；(2) 该问题与请求实体相分离；且 (3) 该问题无法在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查，例如驳回政府豁免主张的命令。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291（终局判决规则）。\n• Cohen v. Beneficial Indus. Loan Corp., 337 U.S. 541, 545–46 (1949)（确立附带命令原则）。\n• Plumhoff v. Rickard, 572 U.S. 765, 771–72 (2014)（说明驳回政府豁免的命令可以依据附带命令原则立即上诉）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-143","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":143,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A patient from State A sued a doctor from State B in a State A federal court for medical malpractice that allegedly occurred in State B. At the conclusion of trial, the jury found in favor of the patient and awarded him $3,000,000 in damages. The court entered a judgment consistent with the verdict. Fifteen days later, the patient brought an action to enforce the judgment in the same federal court. The Federal Rules of Civil Procedure impose an automatic 30-day stay on all final judgments awarding damages. State A law, in contrast, imposes a 14-day stay on all final judgments awarding damages and provides that the law of the state where the injury occurred must be applied to all malpractice claims. However, State B law imposes a 21-day stay on all final judgments awarding damages and provides that State B law must be applied to all tort claims. Should the federal court enter an order to enforce the judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure impose a 30-day stay.","B":"No, because the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure modify a substantive right.","C":"Yes, because State A substantive law imposes a 14-day stay.","D":"Yes, because State B substantive law imposes a 21-day stay."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. When it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural, the court must determine if a valid federal law (ie, federal rule, statute, or constitutional provision) directly addresses the issue. If so, federal-rule analysis provides that federal law applies if it is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 62 directly imposes an automatic 30-day stay on a final judgment entered in federal court that awards damages. This valid rule* is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right because the rule regulates the process of enforcing a judgment—not a party's right to a judgment (Choice B). As a result, the federal court should apply this federal rule. And since only 15 days have passed since the judgment was entered, the court should not enforce the judgment. *No FRCP has ever been found unconstitutional or invalid. This is likely because the Supreme Court reviews and sends proposed rules to Congress before they are enacted. (Choices C & D) Neither State A nor State B law applies to the length of the stay since this issue is procedural in nature. Additionally, there is no need to apply State A's choice-of-law rules because they are only necessary to determine which state's substantive law applies. Educational objective: To determine whether an issue is substantive or procedural, federal-rule analysis applies if a valid federal law directly addresses the issue. Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply that federal law so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987) (explaining federal-rule analysis).","rule":"To determine whether an issue is substantive or procedural, federal-rule analysis applies if a valid federal law directly addresses the issue. Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply that federal law so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A patient from State A sued a doctor from State B in a State A federal court for medical malpractice that allegedly occurred in State B. At the conclusion of trial, the jury found in favor of the patient and awarded him $3,000,000 in damages. The court entered a judgment consistent with the verdict. Fifteen days later, the patient brought an action to enforce the judgment in the same federal court. The Federal Rules of Civil Procedure impose an automatic 30-day stay on all final judgments awarding damages. State A law, in contrast, imposes a 14-day stay on all final judgments awarding damages and provides that the law of the state where the injury occurred must be applied to all malpractice claims. However, State B law imposes a 21-day stay on all final judgments awarding damages and provides that State B law must be applied to all tort claims. Should the federal court enter an order to enforce the judgment?","rule":"To determine whether an issue is substantive or procedural, federal-rule analysis applies if a valid federal law directly addresses the issue. Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply that federal law so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. When it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural, the court must determine if a valid federal law (ie, federal rule, statute, or constitutional provision) directly addresses the issue. If so, federal-rule analysis provides that federal law applies if it is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 62 directly imposes an automatic 30-day stay on a final judgment entered in federal court that awards damages. This valid rule* is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right because the rule regulates the process of enforcing a judgment—not a party's right to a judgment (Choice B). As a result, the federal court should apply this federal rule. And since only 15 days have passed since the judgment was entered, the court should not enforce the judgment. *No FRCP has ever been found unconstitutional or invalid. This is likely because the Supreme Court reviews and sends proposed rules to Congress before they are enacted. (Choices C & D) Neither State A nor State B law applies to the length of the stay since this issue is procedural in nature. Additionally, there is no need to apply State A's choice-of-law rules because they are only necessary to determine which state's substantive law applies. Educational objective: To determine whether an issue is substantive or procedural, federal-rule analysis applies if a valid federal law directly addresses the issue. Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply that federal law so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987) (explaining federal-rule analysis).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987) (explaining federal-rule analysis).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[432],"sourceAnswerPages":[433],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的患者在 A 州联邦法院起诉一名来自 B 州的医生，主张医生在 B 州实施医疗过失。审判结束时，陪审团判患者胜诉，并判给他 3,000,000 美元损害赔偿。法院依照裁决登记判决。十五天后，患者在同一联邦法院提起执行判决的诉讼。《联邦民事诉讼规则》对所有判给损害赔偿的终局判决规定自动中止执行 30 天。相比之下，A 州法律对所有判给损害赔偿的终局判决规定中止执行 14 天，并规定所有医疗过失请求必须适用伤害发生州的法律。但 B 州法律对所有判给损害赔偿的终局判决规定中止执行 21 天，并规定所有侵权请求必须适用 B 州法律。联邦法院是否应发布执行判决的命令？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为《联邦民事诉讼规则》规定中止执行 30 天。","B":"不应，因为《联邦民事诉讼规则》改变了一项实体权利。","C":"应当，因为 A 州实体法规定中止执行 14 天。","D":"应当，因为 B 州实体法规定中止执行 21 天。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院的事项管辖权基于多元州籍时，法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，对程序性问题适用联邦法。问题属于实体性还是程序性并不明确时，法院必须确定是否有有效的联邦法（即联邦规则、制定法或宪法条款）直接处理该问题。若有，联邦规则分析规定：只要联邦法可以合理视为程序性，且不改变实体权利，就适用联邦法。\n\nFRCP 62 直接规定，对联邦法院登记的判给损害赔偿的终局判决自动中止执行 30 天。这项有效规则*可以合理视为程序性，也没有改变实体权利，因为该规则调整的是执行判决的程序，而不是当事人取得判决的权利（选项 B）。因此，联邦法院应适用该联邦规则。判决登记后仅过去 15 天，所以法院不应执行判决。\n\n*从未有任何一条《联邦民事诉讼规则》被认定违宪或无效。这很可能是因为拟议规则生效前，最高法院会先审查并将其送交国会。\n\n（选项 C、D）中止期间的长度具有程序性质，因此 A 州和 B 州法律均不适用。此外，无需适用 A 州的法律选择规则，因为这类规则只在确定适用哪一州的实体法时才有必要。\n\n学习目标：为确定问题属于实体性还是程序性，如果有效的联邦法直接处理该问题，就适用联邦规则分析。根据该分析，行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须适用该联邦法，只要该法律可以合理视为程序性，且不改变实体权利。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987)（说明联邦规则分析）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-045"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-144","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":144,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A man sued a company in a state court in State A. The man claimed that the company negligently manufactured the man's lawn mower and that this defect caused the man serious injuries. The company admitted that it negligently manufactured the lawn mower but alleged that the man was contributorily negligent, which serves as a complete bar to recovery in State A. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the man, and the court entered judgment. Two months later, a woman brought a federal diversity action in State B asserting the same claim against the same company. The woman moved for summary judgment, arguing that the prior state-court judgment established the company's negligence under the rules of preclusion. Is the federal court likely to grant the woman's motion for summary judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parties are different and the company's negligence was not actually litigated.","B":"No, because the prior judgment was entered by a state court.","C":"Yes, because claim preclusion (res judicata) establishes the company's negligence.","D":"Yes, because the jury determined that the company negligently manufactured the lawn mower."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The rules of preclusion mitigate the expense of multiple lawsuits, conserve judicial resources, and minimize the possibility of inconsistent judgments. Claim preclusion achieves these goals by barring identical parties from relitigating identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. Here, the parties in each action are different because the man sued the company in the first action and the woman has sued the company in the second action. As a result, claim preclusion does not apply (Choice C). In contrast, issue preclusion bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is actually litigated when it is submitted to the trier of fact for determination—not merely stipulated, conceded, or admitted. Here, the company admitted that it negligently manufactured the lawn mower in the first action, so issue preclusion cannot be asserted in the second action. Therefore, the woman's motion for summary judgment will likely be denied. (Choice B) The fact that the prior judgment was entered by a state court is irrelevant. That is because all courts must give full faith and credit to state court judgments. (Choice D) Because the company admitted to its negligence, that issue was not submitted to the jury for determination. Instead, the court would have instructed the jury that the only issue for it to decide was whether the man was contributorily negligent. Educational objective: Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).","rule":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment.","irac":{"issue":"A man sued a company in a state court in State A. The man claimed that the company negligently manufactured the man's lawn mower and that this defect caused the man serious injuries. The company admitted that it negligently manufactured the lawn mower but alleged that the man was contributorily negligent, which serves as a complete bar to recovery in State A. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the man, and the court entered judgment. Two months later, a woman brought a federal diversity action in State B asserting the same claim against the same company. The woman moved for summary judgment, arguing that the prior state-court judgment established the company's negligence under the rules of preclusion. Is the federal court likely to grant the woman's motion for summary judgment?","rule":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment.","analysis":"The rules of preclusion mitigate the expense of multiple lawsuits, conserve judicial resources, and minimize the possibility of inconsistent judgments. Claim preclusion achieves these goals by barring identical parties from relitigating identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. Here, the parties in each action are different because the man sued the company in the first action and the woman has sued the company in the second action. As a result, claim preclusion does not apply (Choice C). In contrast, issue preclusion bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is actually litigated when it is submitted to the trier of fact for determination—not merely stipulated, conceded, or admitted. Here, the company admitted that it negligently manufactured the lawn mower in the first action, so issue preclusion cannot be asserted in the second action. Therefore, the woman's motion for summary judgment will likely be denied. (Choice B) The fact that the prior judgment was entered by a state court is irrelevant. That is because all courts must give full faith and credit to state court judgments. (Choice D) Because the company admitted to its negligence, that issue was not submitted to the jury for determination. Instead, the court would have instructed the jury that the only issue for it to decide was whether the man was contributorily negligent. Educational objective: Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[435],"sourceAnswerPages":[436],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在 A 州州法院起诉一家公司。男子主张，该公司在制造他的割草机时存在过失，而且该缺陷使男子遭受严重伤害。公司承认其过失制造割草机，但主张男子存在共同过失；在 A 州，共同过失完全阻止原告获得赔偿。陪审团作出有利于男子的裁决，法院登记判决。两个月后，一名女子在 B 州提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，对同一公司提出相同请求。女子申请简易判决，主张根据排除规则，先前的州法院判决已经确立公司的过失。联邦法院可能批准女子的简易判决动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为当事人不同，而且公司的过失没有经过实际争讼。","B":"不会，因为先前判决由州法院登记。","C":"会，因为请求权排除（既判力）确立公司的过失。","D":"会，因为陪审团认定公司过失制造割草机。"},"explanationZh":"排除规则能够减轻多起诉讼的费用、节约司法资源，并最大程度降低判决不一致的可能性。请求权排除通过以下方式实现这些目标：有效的实体终局判决登记后，禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。本题每起诉讼的当事人不同，因为第一案由男子起诉公司，第二案由女子起诉公司。因此，请求权排除不适用（选项 C）。\n\n相比之下，争点排除禁止重新争讼先前诉讼中已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。一个问题提交事实裁判者裁定时，才属于经过实际争讼；仅仅约定、让步或承认的问题并未实际争讼。本题公司在第一案中承认其过失制造割草机，所以第二案不能主张争点排除。因此，女子的简易判决动议很可能被驳回。\n\n（选项 B）先前判决由州法院登记这一事实无关。所有法院都必须对州法院判决给予完全诚信与信用。\n\n（选项 D）因为公司承认其过失，该问题没有提交陪审团裁定。相反，法院会指示陪审团，其唯一需要裁定的问题是男子是否存在共同过失。\n\n学习目标：请求权排除（即既判力）在有效的实体终局判决登记后，禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。争点排除（即附带禁反言）禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义请求权排除）。\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义争点排除）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-046"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-145","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":145,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"Before the close of evidence in a federal negligence trial, the defendant submitted a proposed jury instruction on contributory negligence. Before instructing the jury, the judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give, which did not include the defendant's proposed contributory-negligence instruction but did include the court's own instruction on contributory negligence. Neither party objected, either then or after the judge had given the instructions. The jury returned a verdict for the plaintiff, and the judge entered judgment on the verdict. The defendant would like to appeal the verdict on the ground that the judge should have instructed the jury using the defendant's proposed instruction on contributory negligence. Has the defendant preserved the issue for appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the defendant failed to object after the judge gave the instructions to the jury.","B":"No, because the defendant failed to object after the judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give.","C":"Yes, because the defendant submitted a proposed instruction on contributory negligence.","D":"Yes, because the judge's failure to give the defendant's contributory-negligence instruction amounted to a ruling on the instruction."},"answer":"B","explanation":"To challenge a jury instruction on appeal (ie, to preserve error), a party must raise the issue at trial. This can be done at the close of evidence (or otherwise at the court's direction) by:\n• filing a written request for the jury instruction that the party would like the court to give and\n\n• obtaining a definitive ruling on that request from the court on the record.\n\nThis can also be done after the court has informed the parties of its proposed instructions (but before it has instructed the jury and before final arguments) by clearly identifying the matter objected to, and the grounds for the objection, on the record. Here, the defendant proposed a jury instruction on contributory negligence before the close of evidence. But the defendant never obtained a definitive ruling on that proposal from the court on the record (eg, \"Your request is denied\"). Instead, the judge merely informed the parties of the instructions she would give, which did not include the defendant's proposed instruction (Choice D). And since the defendant failed to object before the judge instructed the jury, this issue was not preserved for appeal (Choice A). (Choice C) Submitting a proposed jury instruction only preserves the issue for appeal when the court definitively rules on that request on the record—which the court failed to do here. Educational objective: A jury-instruction error is preserved for appeal when a party raises the issue (1) at the close of evidence by filing a written request for the proposed instruction and obtaining a definitive court ruling on the record OR (2) before the court instructs the jury by clearly identifying, and stating the grounds for, the objection on the record. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51 (preserving error regarding requests for, and objections to, jury instructions).","rule":"A jury-instruction error is preserved for appeal when a party raises the issue (1) at the close of evidence by filing a written request for the proposed instruction and obtaining a definitive court ruling on the record OR (2) before the court instructs the jury by clearly identifying, and stating the grounds for, the objection on the record.","irac":{"issue":"Before the close of evidence in a federal negligence trial, the defendant submitted a proposed jury instruction on contributory negligence. Before instructing the jury, the judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give, which did not include the defendant's proposed contributory-negligence instruction but did include the court's own instruction on contributory negligence. Neither party objected, either then or after the judge had given the instructions. The jury returned a verdict for the plaintiff, and the judge entered judgment on the verdict. The defendant would like to appeal the verdict on the ground that the judge should have instructed the jury using the defendant's proposed instruction on contributory negligence. Has the defendant preserved the issue for appeal?","rule":"A jury-instruction error is preserved for appeal when a party raises the issue (1) at the close of evidence by filing a written request for the proposed instruction and obtaining a definitive court ruling on the record OR (2) before the court instructs the jury by clearly identifying, and stating the grounds for, the objection on the record.","analysis":"To challenge a jury instruction on appeal (ie, to preserve error), a party must raise the issue at trial. This can be done at the close of evidence (or otherwise at the court's direction) by:\n• filing a written request for the jury instruction that the party would like the court to give and\n\n• obtaining a definitive ruling on that request from the court on the record.\n\nThis can also be done after the court has informed the parties of its proposed instructions (but before it has instructed the jury and before final arguments) by clearly identifying the matter objected to, and the grounds for the objection, on the record. Here, the defendant proposed a jury instruction on contributory negligence before the close of evidence. But the defendant never obtained a definitive ruling on that proposal from the court on the record (eg, \"Your request is denied\"). Instead, the judge merely informed the parties of the instructions she would give, which did not include the defendant's proposed instruction (Choice D). And since the defendant failed to object before the judge instructed the jury, this issue was not preserved for appeal (Choice A). (Choice C) Submitting a proposed jury instruction only preserves the issue for appeal when the court definitively rules on that request on the record—which the court failed to do here. Educational objective: A jury-instruction error is preserved for appeal when a party raises the issue (1) at the close of evidence by filing a written request for the proposed instruction and obtaining a definitive court ruling on the record OR (2) before the court instructs the jury by clearly identifying, and stating the grounds for, the objection on the record. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51 (preserving error regarding requests for, and objections to, jury instructions).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51 (preserving error regarding requests for, and objections to, jury instructions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[438],"sourceAnswerPages":[439],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在联邦过失审判的举证结束前，被告提交了一份关于共同过失的拟议陪审团指示。法官向陪审团作出指示前，将其准备采用的指示告知双方；其中没有被告拟议的共同过失指示，但包含法院自己拟定的共同过失指示。当时以及法官作出指示后，双方均未提出异议。陪审团作出有利于原告的裁决，法官依裁决登记判决。被告希望以法官本应采用被告拟议的共同过失指示向陪审团作出指示为由，对裁决提起上诉。被告是否保留了该问题供上诉审查？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为被告在法官向陪审团作出指示后未提出异议。","B":"没有，因为被告在法官告知双方其准备采用的指示后未提出异议。","C":"有，因为被告提交了关于共同过失的拟议指示。","D":"有，因为法官未采用被告的共同过失指示，等同于对该指示作出裁定。"},"explanationZh":"当事人若要在上诉中质疑陪审团指示（即保留错误），必须在审判时提出该问题。当事人可在举证结束时（或依照法院的其他指示），通过以下方式提出：\n• 书面请求法院采用该方希望法院作出的陪审团指示；并且\n• 取得法院在案卷记录中对该请求作出的明确终局裁定。\n\n法院将其拟议指示告知双方后（但在法院向陪审团作出指示及双方作结案陈词前），当事人也可以在案卷记录中明确指出异议事项及其异议理由，从而保留该问题。\n\n本题被告在举证结束前提出了一份关于共同过失的陪审团指示。但被告从未取得法院在案卷记录中对该提议作出的明确终局裁定（例如“驳回你的请求”）。相反，法官只是将其准备采用的指示告知双方，而其中没有被告拟议的指示（选项 D）。被告没有在法官向陪审团作出指示前提出异议，所以没有保留该问题供上诉审查（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 C）仅提交拟议陪审团指示，只有在法院于案卷记录中对该请求作出明确终局裁定时，才能保留问题供上诉审查；本题法院并未这样做。\n\n学习目标：当事人可通过以下任一方式保留陪审团指示错误供上诉审查：(1) 在举证结束时提交拟议指示的书面请求，并取得法院在案卷记录中作出的明确终局裁定；或 (2) 在法院向陪审团作出指示前，于案卷记录中明确指出异议并说明理由。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51（保留与陪审团指示请求及异议有关的错误）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-047"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-146","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":146,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"Before the close of evidence in a federal defamation trial, the plaintiff and the defendant each submitted proposed jury instructions on the elements of defamation. Before the trial judge instructed the jury and the parties gave their closing arguments, the trial judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give, which did not include the plaintiff's proposed instructions but did include the defendant's instructions. The plaintiff objected, but the judge overruled the objection. The judge then instructed the jury before the parties gave their closing arguments. The plaintiff objected again, which the judge overruled. The jury returned a verdict for the defendant, and the judge entered judgment on the verdict. The plaintiff has appealed the verdict on the ground that the trial judge erred when she instructed the jury before closing arguments. Is the appellate court likely to find that the trial judge erred?","choices":{"A":"No, because the trial judge may instruct the jury before closing arguments.","B":"No, because the trial judge must instruct the jury before closing arguments.","C":"Yes, because the trial judge must instruct the jury after closing arguments.","D":"Yes, because the trial judge must instruct the jury before the close of evidence."},"answer":"A","explanation":"In a jury trial, the court must provide jury instructions that advise jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 51 sets forth the following requirements for instructing the jury:\n• The trial court must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before it instructs the jury and the parties give their closing arguments.\n• The trial court must give the parties an opportunity to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered to the jury.\n• The trial court may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged—ie, released from its duties in the case. Here, the trial court informed the parties of its proposed instructions and gave them an opportunity to object before it instructed the jury and before closing arguments. And since the trial court has discretion to instruct the jury at any time prior to discharge, it properly instructed the jury before closing arguments (Choice C). Therefore, the trial court did not abuse its discretion and the appellate court is unlikely to find that the trial court erred. (Choice B) A trial court may, but is not required to, instruct the jury prior to the parties' closing arguments. (Choice D) The parties generally must submit their proposed jury instructions before the close of evidence. However, the trial court need not instruct the jury before this time. Educational objective: In a jury trial, a court (1) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, (2) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the jury's presence before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered, and (3) may instruct the jury at any time before it is discharged. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b) (court requirements for providing jury instructions).","rule":"In a jury trial, a court (1) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, (2) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the jury's presence before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered, and (3) may instruct the jury at any time before it is discharged.","irac":{"issue":"Before the close of evidence in a federal defamation trial, the plaintiff and the defendant each submitted proposed jury instructions on the elements of defamation. Before the trial judge instructed the jury and the parties gave their closing arguments, the trial judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give, which did not include the plaintiff's proposed instructions but did include the defendant's instructions. The plaintiff objected, but the judge overruled the objection. The judge then instructed the jury before the parties gave their closing arguments. The plaintiff objected again, which the judge overruled. The jury returned a verdict for the defendant, and the judge entered judgment on the verdict. The plaintiff has appealed the verdict on the ground that the trial judge erred when she instructed the jury before closing arguments. Is the appellate court likely to find that the trial judge erred?","rule":"In a jury trial, a court (1) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, (2) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the jury's presence before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered, and (3) may instruct the jury at any time before it is discharged.","analysis":"In a jury trial, the court must provide jury instructions that advise jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 51 sets forth the following requirements for instructing the jury:\n• The trial court must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before it instructs the jury and the parties give their closing arguments.\n• The trial court must give the parties an opportunity to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered to the jury.\n• The trial court may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged—ie, released from its duties in the case. Here, the trial court informed the parties of its proposed instructions and gave them an opportunity to object before it instructed the jury and before closing arguments. And since the trial court has discretion to instruct the jury at any time prior to discharge, it properly instructed the jury before closing arguments (Choice C). Therefore, the trial court did not abuse its discretion and the appellate court is unlikely to find that the trial court erred. (Choice B) A trial court may, but is not required to, instruct the jury prior to the parties' closing arguments. (Choice D) The parties generally must submit their proposed jury instructions before the close of evidence. However, the trial court need not instruct the jury before this time. Educational objective: In a jury trial, a court (1) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, (2) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the jury's presence before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered, and (3) may instruct the jury at any time before it is discharged. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b) (court requirements for providing jury instructions).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b) (court requirements for providing jury instructions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[441],"sourceAnswerPages":[442],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在联邦诽谤审判的举证结束前，原告和被告各自提交了关于诽谤构成要件的拟议陪审团指示。审判法官向陪审团作出指示以及双方作结案陈词前，将其准备采用的指示告知双方；其中没有原告的拟议指示，但包含被告的指示。原告提出异议，但法官驳回异议。随后，法官在双方作结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示。原告再次提出异议，法官再次驳回。陪审团作出有利于被告的裁决，法官依裁决登记判决。原告以审判法官在结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示属于错误为由，对裁决提起上诉。上诉法院可能认定审判法官犯有错误吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为审判法官可以在结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示。","B":"不会，因为审判法官必须在结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示。","C":"会，因为审判法官必须在结案陈词后向陪审团作出指示。","D":"会，因为审判法官必须在举证结束前向陪审团作出指示。"},"explanationZh":"在陪审团审判中，法院必须提供陪审团指示，告知陪审员评议程序和适用法律。《联邦民事诉讼规则》51 对向陪审团作出指示规定了以下要求：\n• 审判法院必须在向陪审团作出指示以及双方作结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方。\n• 审判法院必须在向陪审团作出指示以及双方作结案陈词前，给予双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议的机会。\n• 审判法院可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候向其作出指示；解散是指解除陪审团在案件中的职责。\n\n本题审判法院在向陪审团作出指示和结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方并给予他们提出异议的机会。由于审判法院有权酌情在陪审团解散前的任何时候作出指示，所以其在结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示是适当的（选项 C）。因此，审判法院没有滥用裁量权，上诉法院不太可能认定其犯有错误。\n\n（选项 B）审判法院可以在双方作结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示，但没有义务必须这样做。\n\n（选项 D）双方通常必须在举证结束前提交拟议陪审团指示；但审判法院无需在此时间前向陪审团作出指示。\n\n学习目标：在陪审团审判中，法院 (1) 必须在向陪审团作出指示和结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方；(2) 必须在向陪审团作出指示和结案陈词前，允许双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议；并且 (3) 可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候向其作出指示。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b)（法院提供陪审团指示的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-048"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-147","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":147,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"An 18-year-old sued the manufacturer of the e-cigarettes he had vaped for the previous two years in an appropriate federal district court for $1 million. The teenager claimed that the e-cigarettes caused him to develop a serious lung disease. The teenager also alleged that the manufacturer knew the risks posed by e-cigarettes but failed to disclose these risks to its users. After a trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the teenager for $1 million in compensatory damages and $20 million in punitive damages. The judge entered a final judgment in accordance with the jury's verdict the same day. Three weeks later, the manufacturer filed a motion for remittitur to reduce the amount of punitive damages, arguing that the damages award was excessive and violated the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. The district court denied the motion, and the manufacturer appealed that ruling on due process grounds. Is the federal appellate court likely to affirm the district court's denial of the motion for remittitur?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judge abused his discretion.","B":"No, because the punitive damages awarded were excessive.","C":"Yes, because the judge had no authority to alter the jury's award of damages.","D":"Yes, because the motion for remittitur was untimely filed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Altering jury's award of damages\n\n(FRCP 59(e))\n\nType of Definition Applicability alteration\n\nRemittitur Defendant's request to reduce jury Plaintiff must be offered choice award of excessive damages between:\n\n• reduced damages or\n• new trial on damages Additur Plaintiff's request to increase jury award Never allowed in federal court of inadequate damages\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA losing party may file a motion for remittitur within 28 days of the entry of a final judgment to request that the trial judge reduce an allegedly excessive jury award of damages (Choice C). Here, the manufacturer timely filed its motion for remittitur of the jury's punitive damages award three weeks (21 days) after the trial judge entered a final judgment (Choice D). Although this motion was denied, the manufacturer can appeal that ruling. A ruling on a motion for remittitur is typically discretionary and reviewed by an appellate court for abuse of discretion. But when that ruling is challenged on constitutional grounds—eg, due process—it presents an issue of law that is reviewed de novo (Choice A). A review of the constitutionality of a punitive damages award requires an examination of three factors:\n• The degree of reprehensibility of the defendant's misconduct\n• The size of the award in comparison to the statutory penalties for comparable misconduct\n• The ratio of punitive damages to compensatory damages (this factor receives great weight) Although the Supreme Court has never identified a precise unconstitutional ratio, it stated in State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell that the ratio generally cannot exceed 9 to 1. Here, the jury awarded the teenager $20 million in punitive damages compared to $1 million in compensatory damages—a 20-to-1 ratio. Therefore, the appellate court will likely conclude that the punitive damages were unconstitutionally excessive and reverse the district court's denial of the motion for remittitur.\n\nEducational objective: Appellate courts apply the de novo standard of review to conclusions of law—eg, that punitive damages were not unconstitutionally excessive. Punitive damages are almost always unconstitutional when the ratio of punitive to compensatory damages is greater than 9 to 1. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to amend judgment).\n\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001) (requiring de novo review of punitive damages awards).\n\n• State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell, 538 U.S. 408, 425 (2003) (holding that punitive damages are likely unconstitutional if they exceed a 9-to-1 ratio).","rule":"Appellate courts apply the de novo standard of review to conclusions of law—eg, that punitive damages were not unconstitutionally excessive. Punitive damages are almost always unconstitutional when the ratio of punitive to compensatory damages is greater than 9 to 1.","irac":{"issue":"An 18-year-old sued the manufacturer of the e-cigarettes he had vaped for the previous two years in an appropriate federal district court for $1 million. The teenager claimed that the e-cigarettes caused him to develop a serious lung disease. The teenager also alleged that the manufacturer knew the risks posed by e-cigarettes but failed to disclose these risks to its users. After a trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the teenager for $1 million in compensatory damages and $20 million in punitive damages. The judge entered a final judgment in accordance with the jury's verdict the same day. Three weeks later, the manufacturer filed a motion for remittitur to reduce the amount of punitive damages, arguing that the damages award was excessive and violated the due process clause of the Fourteenth Amendment. The district court denied the motion, and the manufacturer appealed that ruling on due process grounds. Is the federal appellate court likely to affirm the district court's denial of the motion for remittitur?","rule":"Appellate courts apply the de novo standard of review to conclusions of law—eg, that punitive damages were not unconstitutionally excessive. Punitive damages are almost always unconstitutional when the ratio of punitive to compensatory damages is greater than 9 to 1.","analysis":"Altering jury's award of damages\n\n(FRCP 59(e))\n\nType of Definition Applicability alteration\n\nRemittitur Defendant's request to reduce jury Plaintiff must be offered choice award of excessive damages between:\n\n• reduced damages or\n• new trial on damages Additur Plaintiff's request to increase jury award Never allowed in federal court of inadequate damages\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA losing party may file a motion for remittitur within 28 days of the entry of a final judgment to request that the trial judge reduce an allegedly excessive jury award of damages (Choice C). Here, the manufacturer timely filed its motion for remittitur of the jury's punitive damages award three weeks (21 days) after the trial judge entered a final judgment (Choice D). Although this motion was denied, the manufacturer can appeal that ruling. A ruling on a motion for remittitur is typically discretionary and reviewed by an appellate court for abuse of discretion. But when that ruling is challenged on constitutional grounds—eg, due process—it presents an issue of law that is reviewed de novo (Choice A). A review of the constitutionality of a punitive damages award requires an examination of three factors:\n• The degree of reprehensibility of the defendant's misconduct\n• The size of the award in comparison to the statutory penalties for comparable misconduct\n• The ratio of punitive damages to compensatory damages (this factor receives great weight) Although the Supreme Court has never identified a precise unconstitutional ratio, it stated in State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell that the ratio generally cannot exceed 9 to 1. Here, the jury awarded the teenager $20 million in punitive damages compared to $1 million in compensatory damages—a 20-to-1 ratio. Therefore, the appellate court will likely conclude that the punitive damages were unconstitutionally excessive and reverse the district court's denial of the motion for remittitur.\n\nEducational objective: Appellate courts apply the de novo standard of review to conclusions of law—eg, that punitive damages were not unconstitutionally excessive. Punitive damages are almost always unconstitutional when the ratio of punitive to compensatory damages is greater than 9 to 1. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to amend judgment).\n\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001) (requiring de novo review of punitive damages awards).\n\n• State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell, 538 U.S. 408, 425 (2003) (holding that punitive damages are likely unconstitutional if they exceed a 9-to-1 ratio).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e) (motion to amend judgment).\n\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001) (requiring de novo review of punitive damages awards).\n\n• State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell, 538 U.S. 408, 425 (2003) (holding that punitive damages are likely unconstitutional if they exceed a 9-to-1 ratio).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[444],"sourceAnswerPages":[445,446],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 18 岁青年在有适当管辖权的联邦地区法院起诉他过去两年吸食的电子烟制造商，请求 100 万美元。青年主张，电子烟使他患上严重肺病。他还指控制造商知道电子烟构成的风险，却未向使用者披露。审判后，陪审团作出有利于青年的裁决，判给 100 万美元补偿性损害赔偿和 2,000 万美元惩罚性损害赔偿。法官当天依陪审团裁决登记终局判决。三周后，制造商提出减额裁决动议，请求减少惩罚性损害赔偿金额，理由是赔偿额过高并违反第十四修正案的正当程序条款。地区法院驳回动议，制造商以正当程序为由对该裁定提起上诉。联邦上诉法院可能维持地区法院对减额裁决动议的驳回吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法官滥用了裁量权。","B":"不会，因为判给的惩罚性损害赔偿过高。","C":"会，因为法官无权改变陪审团判给的损害赔偿。","D":"会，因为减额裁决动议提交逾期。"},"explanationZh":"改变陪审团判给的损害赔偿（FRCP 59(e)）：\n\n改变类型｜定义｜适用方式\n减额裁决｜被告请求减少陪审团判给的过高损害赔偿｜必须让原告在以下两者中选择：\n• 接受减少后的损害赔偿；或\n• 就损害赔偿重新审判\n增额裁决｜原告请求增加陪审团判给的不足损害赔偿｜联邦法院绝不允许\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n败诉方可在终局判决登记后 28 天内提出减额裁决动议，请求审判法官减少被指过高的陪审团损害赔偿裁赔（选项 C）。本题制造商在审判法官登记终局判决三周（21 天）后，及时就陪审团判给的惩罚性损害赔偿提出减额裁决动议（选项 D）。\n\n尽管该动议被驳回，制造商仍可对该裁定提起上诉。减额裁决动议的裁定通常属于裁量性裁定，上诉法院以滥用裁量权标准审查。但是，如果基于宪法理由（例如正当程序）质疑该裁定，该裁定提出的是法律问题，应重新审查（选项 A）。\n\n审查惩罚性损害赔偿裁赔是否符合宪法，需要考察三个因素：\n• 被告不当行为的应受谴责程度；\n• 裁赔金额与类似不当行为的法定处罚相比的大小；以及\n• 惩罚性损害赔偿与补偿性损害赔偿之比（该因素权重很大）。\n\n最高法院虽然从未明确一个精确的违宪比例，但在 State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell 案中指出，该比例通常不能超过 9:1。本题陪审团判给青年 2,000 万美元惩罚性损害赔偿，而补偿性损害赔偿为 100 万美元，比例为 20:1。因此，上诉法院很可能认定惩罚性损害赔偿高到违宪，并推翻地区法院对减额裁决动议的驳回。\n\n学习目标：上诉法院对法律结论适用重新审查标准，例如惩罚性损害赔偿并非高到违宪的结论。当惩罚性损害赔偿与补偿性损害赔偿之比超过 9:1 时，惩罚性损害赔偿几乎总是违宪。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59(e)（修改判决动议）。\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001)（要求重新审查惩罚性损害赔偿裁赔）。\n• State Farm Mut. Ins. Co. v. Campbell, 538 U.S. 408, 425 (2003)（判定惩罚性损害赔偿超过 9:1 比例时可能违宪）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-148","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":148,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A car manufacturer produced a car that was sold nationwide. Problems with the car's brakes allegedly caused several accidents and injuries. Two individual buyers of the car each filed a class action, in different states, against the manufacturer, asserting the same products liability claims on behalf of all buyers nationwide. One class action was filed in federal court and the other was filed in state court. The parties in the federal action reached a court-approved settlement, and the court entered judgment dismissing the action with prejudice. The manufacturer's attorney has moved to dismiss the state court action on the basis of res judicata (claim preclusion). Should the state court look to federal or state law to decide the effect of the judgment?","choices":{"A":"Federal law, because the judgment was entered in federal court.","B":"Federal law, because the judgment was the result of a nationwide action governed by the federal class action rule.","C":"State law, because the judgment is being asserted in a state court.","D":"State law, because there is no federal common law and preclusion is a common law doctrine."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from litigating an identical claim against the same party in a second action. The court presiding over the second action must decide the judgment's preclusive effect by applying the correct law:\n• If the judgment was entered in state court, then that state's law applies to the second action.\n• If the judgment was entered in federal court, then federal law applies to the second action. Here, a judgment dismissing the class action with prejudice (ie, on the merits) was entered in federal court. As a result, the state court presiding over the pending action should look to federal law to determine the preclusive effect of the federal court's final judgment.* *Pursuant to federal common law, a federal court sitting in diversity will apply the law of the state in which the court sits. (Choice B) The applicable claim-preclusion law depends on which court entered the judgment—not the law the judgment was based on. Therefore, the fact that the judgment was the result of a nationwide action governed by the federal class action rule (ie, FRCP 23) is irrelevant. (Choice C) The court in which the judgment was entered (not asserted) determines the applicable law for evaluating the judgment's preclusive effect. Here, judgment was entered in federal court so federal law applies. (Choice D) Federal common law does exist and includes the doctrine of claim preclusion. Educational objective: If a valid final judgment on the merits was entered in state court, then the court presiding over a subsequent action involving the same claim and parties must apply that state's claim-preclusion law to the second action. But if judgment was entered by a federal court, then federal law applies. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Semtek Int'l Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497, 508 (2001) (holding that federal common law governs the preclusive effect of a judgment entered by a federal court).","rule":"If a valid final judgment on the merits was entered in state court, then the court presiding over a subsequent action involving the same claim and parties must apply that state's claim-preclusion law to the second action. But if judgment was entered by a federal court, then federal law applies. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","irac":{"issue":"A car manufacturer produced a car that was sold nationwide. Problems with the car's brakes allegedly caused several accidents and injuries. Two individual buyers of the car each filed a class action, in different states, against the manufacturer, asserting the same products liability claims on behalf of all buyers nationwide. One class action was filed in federal court and the other was filed in state court. The parties in the federal action reached a court-approved settlement, and the court entered judgment dismissing the action with prejudice. The manufacturer's attorney has moved to dismiss the state court action on the basis of res judicata (claim preclusion). Should the state court look to federal or state law to decide the effect of the judgment?","rule":"If a valid final judgment on the merits was entered in state court, then the court presiding over a subsequent action involving the same claim and parties must apply that state's claim-preclusion law to the second action. But if judgment was entered by a federal court, then federal law applies. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","analysis":"The doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes a party from litigating an identical claim against the same party in a second action. The court presiding over the second action must decide the judgment's preclusive effect by applying the correct law:\n• If the judgment was entered in state court, then that state's law applies to the second action.\n• If the judgment was entered in federal court, then federal law applies to the second action. Here, a judgment dismissing the class action with prejudice (ie, on the merits) was entered in federal court. As a result, the state court presiding over the pending action should look to federal law to determine the preclusive effect of the federal court's final judgment.* *Pursuant to federal common law, a federal court sitting in diversity will apply the law of the state in which the court sits. (Choice B) The applicable claim-preclusion law depends on which court entered the judgment—not the law the judgment was based on. Therefore, the fact that the judgment was the result of a nationwide action governed by the federal class action rule (ie, FRCP 23) is irrelevant. (Choice C) The court in which the judgment was entered (not asserted) determines the applicable law for evaluating the judgment's preclusive effect. Here, judgment was entered in federal court so federal law applies. (Choice D) Federal common law does exist and includes the doctrine of claim preclusion. Educational objective: If a valid final judgment on the merits was entered in state court, then the court presiding over a subsequent action involving the same claim and parties must apply that state's claim-preclusion law to the second action. But if judgment was entered by a federal court, then federal law applies. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Semtek Int'l Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497, 508 (2001) (holding that federal common law governs the preclusive effect of a judgment entered by a federal court).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Semtek Int'l Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497, 508 (2001) (holding that federal common law governs the preclusive effect of a judgment entered by a federal court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[447],"sourceAnswerPages":[448],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家汽车制造商生产了一款在全国销售的汽车。据称，该车的制动系统问题导致多起事故和人员受伤。两名汽车买家分别在不同州对制造商提起集体诉讼，各自代表全国所有买家提出相同的产品责任请求。其中一起集体诉讼在联邦法院提起，另一起在州法院提起。联邦诉讼的双方达成经法院批准的和解，法院登记判决，有损害驳回该诉讼。制造商律师以既判力（请求权排除）为由，申请驳回州法院诉讼。州法院应依据联邦法还是州法，决定该判决的效力？","choicesZh":{"A":"联邦法，因为判决在联邦法院登记。","B":"联邦法，因为该判决源于一项受联邦集体诉讼规则支配的全国性诉讼。","C":"州法，因为该判决是在州法院中被主张。","D":"州法，因为不存在联邦普通法，而排除是一项普通法原则。"},"explanationZh":"既判力（即请求权排除）原则规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止一方在第二起诉讼中对同一方争讼相同请求。主持第二起诉讼的法院必须适用正确法律，决定判决的排除效力：\n• 判决在州法院登记时，第二起诉讼适用该州法律。\n• 判决在联邦法院登记时，第二起诉讼适用联邦法。\n\n本题有损害驳回集体诉讼（即实体驳回）的判决在联邦法院登记。因此，主持待决诉讼的州法院应依据联邦法，确定联邦法院终局判决的排除效力。*\n\n*根据联邦普通法，行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院将适用其所在地州的法律。\n\n（选项 B）适用哪套请求权排除法取决于哪个法院登记判决，而不取决于判决所依据的法律。因此，该判决源于受联邦集体诉讼规则（即 FRCP 23）支配的全国性诉讼这一事实无关。\n\n（选项 C）决定用哪套法律评估判决排除效力的是登记判决的法院，而不是主张该判决的法院。本题判决在联邦法院登记，所以适用联邦法。\n\n（选项 D）联邦普通法确实存在，其中包括请求权排除原则。\n\n学习目标：有效的实体终局判决若在州法院登记，主持涉及相同请求和当事人的后续诉讼的法院，必须对第二起诉讼适用该州的请求权排除法。但判决若由联邦法院登记，则适用联邦法。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Semtek Int'l Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp., 531 U.S. 497, 508 (2001)（判定联邦普通法支配联邦法院登记判决的排除效力）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-041"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-149","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":149,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A plaintiff from State A sued a defendant from State B for allegedly breaching a contract formed and executed in State B. Suit was filed in a federal district court in State A for $250,000 in damages. The defendant moved to dismiss the action for improper venue. A hearing was held so the parties could make arguments and present evidence of proper venue. During the hearing, the parties' arguments drifted beyond venue and into the more substantive aspects of their action, including the existence of a valid contract. After the hearing, the federal court granted the defendant's motion and entered a judgment dismissing the action based on improper venue. When announcing its judgment, the court mentioned that based on the arguments it heard at the hearing, it had doubts as to whether the parties formed a valid contract. The plaintiff subsequently sued the defendant in a state court in State B on the same breach-of-contract claim. The defendant filed an answer in which it asserted the defense of res judicata (claim preclusion). Is the defendant likely to succeed on this defense?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judgment was entered by a federal court and the second lawsuit was filed in a state court.","B":"No, because the judgment was not on the merits.","C":"Yes, because the actions involve the same parties and the same claim.","D":"Yes, because the judgment was entered after a hearing in which both parties presented evidence."},"answer":"B","explanation":"After a valid final judgment on the merits of a claim has been entered, the defense of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) can be raised if the same parties raise an identical claim in a subsequent action. A final judgment is on the merits when it is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense, rather than technical or procedural grounds. Therefore, judgments not on the merits include dismissals based on:\n• lack of subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, a court's authority to hear a particular type of case\n• lack of personal jurisdiction—ie, a court's authority over the parties before it\n• invalid party joinder—ie, adding parties to a suit\n• improper venue—ie, the geographical location of the court that will hear a suit Here, the federal court in State A made an oral comment regarding the merits of the plaintiff's action, but it entered a final judgment dismissing the action for improper venue. Therefore, the final judgment was not on the merits and the defense of res judicata does not apply in the second action—even though it involves the same parties and the same claim (Choice C). (Choice A) A state court must give full faith and credit to a prior judgment entered by a competent federal court. So had the federal judgment been a final judgment on the merits, the State B court would have been required to give the judgment preclusive effect. (Choice D) The fact that the judgment was entered after a hearing in which both parties presented evidence is irrelevant. The determining factor is whether the hearing resolved the parties' dispute on a procedural or technical ground (not on the merits) OR on the substantive elements of a claim or defense (on the merits). Educational objective: Under res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), identical parties cannot relitigate identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. A judgment on the merits is based on the substance of a claim or defense—not a technical or procedural ground (eg, lack of jurisdiction, invalid party joinder, improper venue). References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining that claim preclusion does not apply to a dismissal based on a procedural ground).","rule":"Under res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), identical parties cannot relitigate identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. A judgment on the merits is based on the substance of a claim or defense—not a technical or procedural ground (eg, lack of jurisdiction, invalid party joinder, improper venue).","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff from State A sued a defendant from State B for allegedly breaching a contract formed and executed in State B. Suit was filed in a federal district court in State A for $250,000 in damages. The defendant moved to dismiss the action for improper venue. A hearing was held so the parties could make arguments and present evidence of proper venue. During the hearing, the parties' arguments drifted beyond venue and into the more substantive aspects of their action, including the existence of a valid contract. After the hearing, the federal court granted the defendant's motion and entered a judgment dismissing the action based on improper venue. When announcing its judgment, the court mentioned that based on the arguments it heard at the hearing, it had doubts as to whether the parties formed a valid contract. The plaintiff subsequently sued the defendant in a state court in State B on the same breach-of-contract claim. The defendant filed an answer in which it asserted the defense of res judicata (claim preclusion). Is the defendant likely to succeed on this defense?","rule":"Under res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), identical parties cannot relitigate identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. A judgment on the merits is based on the substance of a claim or defense—not a technical or procedural ground (eg, lack of jurisdiction, invalid party joinder, improper venue).","analysis":"After a valid final judgment on the merits of a claim has been entered, the defense of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) can be raised if the same parties raise an identical claim in a subsequent action. A final judgment is on the merits when it is based on the substantive elements of a claim or defense, rather than technical or procedural grounds. Therefore, judgments not on the merits include dismissals based on:\n• lack of subject-matter jurisdiction—ie, a court's authority to hear a particular type of case\n• lack of personal jurisdiction—ie, a court's authority over the parties before it\n• invalid party joinder—ie, adding parties to a suit\n• improper venue—ie, the geographical location of the court that will hear a suit Here, the federal court in State A made an oral comment regarding the merits of the plaintiff's action, but it entered a final judgment dismissing the action for improper venue. Therefore, the final judgment was not on the merits and the defense of res judicata does not apply in the second action—even though it involves the same parties and the same claim (Choice C). (Choice A) A state court must give full faith and credit to a prior judgment entered by a competent federal court. So had the federal judgment been a final judgment on the merits, the State B court would have been required to give the judgment preclusive effect. (Choice D) The fact that the judgment was entered after a hearing in which both parties presented evidence is irrelevant. The determining factor is whether the hearing resolved the parties' dispute on a procedural or technical ground (not on the merits) OR on the substantive elements of a claim or defense (on the merits). Educational objective: Under res judicata (ie, claim preclusion), identical parties cannot relitigate identical claims after the entry of a valid final judgment on the merits. A judgment on the merits is based on the substance of a claim or defense—not a technical or procedural ground (eg, lack of jurisdiction, invalid party joinder, improper venue). References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining that claim preclusion does not apply to a dismissal based on a procedural ground).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining claim preclusion).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining that claim preclusion does not apply to a dismissal based on a procedural ground).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[450],"sourceAnswerPages":[451],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的原告起诉一名来自 B 州的被告，主张被告违反了一份在 B 州订立并履行的合同。原告在 A 州联邦地区法院提起诉讼，请求 250,000 美元损害赔偿。被告以审判地不适当为由申请驳回诉讼。法院举行听证，让双方就审判地是否适当进行辩论和出示证据。听证期间，双方的辩论超出审判地问题，涉及诉讼中更具实体性的方面，包括有效合同是否存在。听证后，联邦法院批准被告动议，并以审判地不适当为由登记驳回诉讼的判决。法院宣布判决时提到，根据其在听证中听到的辩论，法院怀疑双方是否订立了有效合同。原告随后在 B 州州法院就同一违约请求起诉被告。被告提交答辩状，主张既判力（请求权排除）抗辩。该抗辩可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为判决由联邦法院登记，而第二起诉讼在州法院提起。","B":"不会，因为该判决并非实体判决。","C":"会，因为两起诉讼涉及相同当事人和相同请求。","D":"会，因为判决是在双方均出示证据的听证后登记。"},"explanationZh":"一项请求已有有效的实体终局判决登记后，若相同当事人在后续诉讼中提出相同请求，就可以主张既判力（即请求权排除）抗辩。终局判决如果基于请求或抗辩的实体要件，而不是技术性或程序性理由，即为实体判决。因此，下列驳回不属于实体判决：\n• 缺乏事项管辖权——即法院无权审理特定类型案件；\n• 缺乏对人管辖权——即法院对到庭当事人没有权力；\n• 当事人合并无效——即在诉讼中增加当事人无效；\n• 审判地不适当——即审理诉讼的法院地理位置不适当。\n\n本题 A 州联邦法院虽对原告诉讼的实体作出口头评论，但其以审判地不适当为由登记了驳回诉讼的终局判决。因此，该终局判决并非实体判决，既判力抗辩不适用于第二起诉讼，即使第二案涉及相同当事人和相同请求也是如此（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）州法院必须对有管辖权的联邦法院先前登记的判决给予完全诚信与信用。因此，如果联邦判决属于实体终局判决，B 州法院就必须给予该判决排除效力。\n\n（选项 D）判决在双方均出示证据的听证后登记这一事实无关。决定因素是听证以程序性或技术性理由（非实体）解决双方争议，还是依据请求或抗辩的实体要件（实体）解决双方争议。\n\n学习目标：依据既判力（即请求权排除），有效的实体终局判决登记后，相同当事人不能重新争讼相同请求。实体判决以请求或抗辩的实体内容为基础，而不是技术性或程序性理由（例如缺乏管辖权、当事人合并无效、审判地不适当）。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 17 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义请求权排除）。\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（说明基于程序性理由作出的驳回不适用请求权排除）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-039"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-150","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":150,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"In a federal court diversity action by a beneficiary against an insurance company on an insurance claim, a question arose regarding whether the court should apply a presumption that, where both husband and wife were killed in a common accident, the husband died last. What body of law determines whether this presumption should be applied?","choices":{"A":"Federal statutory law.","B":"The federal common law.","C":"The law of the state whose substantive law is applied.","D":"Traditional common law."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction (as seen here), the court must apply:\n• federal procedural law – for issues regarding processes and procedures (eg, to determine the deadline for filing an answer) and\n\n• state substantive law – for issues regarding legal rights and duties (eg, to determine the elements of a claim or affirmative defense).\n\nThe presumption at issue here states that, where both husband and wife were killed in a common accident, the husband died last. Since this suit concerns the beneficiary of an insurance claim, the presumption affects a legal right (ie, whether the plaintiff is a rightful beneficiary). Therefore, applicability of the presumption is a substantive issue that must be determined by state law—not federal law (Choices A & B). The federal court will then look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which state's substantive law applies. (Choice D) A federal court hearing a diversity case must apply the current substantive law of the appropriate state—not traditional common law. Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the court must apply (1) federal procedural law for issues regarding remedies and procedures and (2) state substantive law for issues regarding legal rights and duties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1652 (federal court must apply state substantive law in diversity cases).","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply (1) federal procedural law for issues regarding remedies and procedures and (2) state substantive law for issues regarding legal rights and duties.","irac":{"issue":"In a federal court diversity action by a beneficiary against an insurance company on an insurance claim, a question arose regarding whether the court should apply a presumption that, where both husband and wife were killed in a common accident, the husband died last. What body of law determines whether this presumption should be applied?","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply (1) federal procedural law for issues regarding remedies and procedures and (2) state substantive law for issues regarding legal rights and duties.","analysis":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction (as seen here), the court must apply:\n• federal procedural law – for issues regarding processes and procedures (eg, to determine the deadline for filing an answer) and\n\n• state substantive law – for issues regarding legal rights and duties (eg, to determine the elements of a claim or affirmative defense).\n\nThe presumption at issue here states that, where both husband and wife were killed in a common accident, the husband died last. Since this suit concerns the beneficiary of an insurance claim, the presumption affects a legal right (ie, whether the plaintiff is a rightful beneficiary). Therefore, applicability of the presumption is a substantive issue that must be determined by state law—not federal law (Choices A & B). The federal court will then look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which state's substantive law applies. (Choice D) A federal court hearing a diversity case must apply the current substantive law of the appropriate state—not traditional common law. Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the court must apply (1) federal procedural law for issues regarding remedies and procedures and (2) state substantive law for issues regarding legal rights and duties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1652 (federal court must apply state substantive law in diversity cases).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1652 (federal court must apply state substantive law in diversity cases).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[453],"sourceAnswerPages":[454],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (1997); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在一宗受益人就保险请求对保险公司提起的联邦多元州籍诉讼中，法院需要决定是否适用一项推定：夫妻二人在同一事故中死亡时，推定丈夫后死亡。是否适用该推定，应由哪一法律体系决定？","choicesZh":{"A":"联邦制定法。","B":"联邦普通法。","C":"其实体法适用州的法律。","D":"传统普通法。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院的事项管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权（本题即如此）时，法院必须适用：\n• 联邦程序法——处理流程和程序问题，例如确定提交答辩状的期限；以及\n• 州实体法——处理法律权利和义务问题，例如确定请求或积极抗辩的构成要件。\n\n本题争议推定规定，夫妻二人在同一事故中死亡时，推定丈夫后死亡。由于本诉涉及保险请求的受益人，该推定会影响一项法律权利，即原告是否为合法受益人。因此，该推定是否适用属于必须由州法而非联邦法决定的实体性问题（选项 A、B）。随后，联邦法院将依据法院所在地州的法律选择规则，确定适用哪个州的实体法。\n\n（选项 D）联邦法院审理多元州籍案件时，必须适用适当州的现行实体法，而不是传统普通法。\n\n学习目标：在联邦多元州籍诉讼中，法院必须适用：(1) 联邦程序法处理救济与程序问题；(2) 州实体法处理法律权利与义务问题。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1652（联邦法院审理多元州籍案件时必须适用州实体法）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-037"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-151","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":151,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A football team entered into a 10-year lease with a city for use of the city's athletic stadium. Five years into the lease, the team threatened to leave the stadium and move to another city. The city sued the team in federal court, seeking a permanent injunction to prevent the team from breaching its lease and leaving. In its answer, the team included a counterclaim seeking $10 million in damages for losses caused by the city's alleged failure to properly maintain the stadium, as the lease required. The team demanded a jury trial on the counterclaim. The city moved to try its claim for a permanent injunction before the trial on the team's counterclaim. The team objected and moved that the jury trial of its counterclaim be held before the trial of the city's injunction claim. How should the court rule on the parties' motions?","choices":{"A":"The court should first hold a jury trial of the team's counterclaim, and then a nonjury trial of the issues remaining in the city's claim.","B":"The court should first hold a nonjury trial of the city's claim, and then a jury trial of the issues remaining in the team's counterclaim.","C":"The court should first hold a nonjury trial of the city's claim without giving binding effect to its findings or conclusions in the later jury trial of the team's counterclaim.","D":"The court should schedule a jury trial of both the city's claim and the team's counterclaim."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Right to jury trial in civil case\n\nRight • Legal claim – seeks monetary remedy to compensate for loss (eg, tort/contract damages) AND\n\n• > $20 in controversy No • Equitable claim – seeks nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction, specific right performance) since legal remedy is inadequate OR\n\n• ≤ $20 in controversy The Seventh Amendment guarantees the right to a jury trial in civil cases where the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars. But this right only applies to legal claims (eg, the team's $10 million counterclaim)—not equitable claims (eg, the city's permanent injunction claim). And when, as here, a case involves both legal and equitable claims that share common issues of fact (eg, obligations under the stadium lease), the court must first hold a jury trial on the legal claim (Choice B). The judge must then hold a nonjury trial on the equitable claim, relying on the jury's findings of fact on the legal claim to do so (Choices C & D). Educational objective: When a case involves legal and equitable claims that share common fact issues, the court should hold a jury trial on the legal claim before holding a nonjury trial on the equitable claim. References\n• U.S. Const. amend. VII (right to a jury trial).\n\n• Beacon Theatres v. Westover, 359 U.S. 500, 510–11 (1959) (stating that legal claims must be decided by a jury before the court determines related equitable claims).","rule":"When a case involves legal and equitable claims that share common fact issues, the court should hold a jury trial on the legal claim before holding a nonjury trial on the equitable claim.","irac":{"issue":"A football team entered into a 10-year lease with a city for use of the city's athletic stadium. Five years into the lease, the team threatened to leave the stadium and move to another city. The city sued the team in federal court, seeking a permanent injunction to prevent the team from breaching its lease and leaving. In its answer, the team included a counterclaim seeking $10 million in damages for losses caused by the city's alleged failure to properly maintain the stadium, as the lease required. The team demanded a jury trial on the counterclaim. The city moved to try its claim for a permanent injunction before the trial on the team's counterclaim. The team objected and moved that the jury trial of its counterclaim be held before the trial of the city's injunction claim. How should the court rule on the parties' motions?","rule":"When a case involves legal and equitable claims that share common fact issues, the court should hold a jury trial on the legal claim before holding a nonjury trial on the equitable claim.","analysis":"Right to jury trial in civil case\n\nRight • Legal claim – seeks monetary remedy to compensate for loss (eg, tort/contract damages) AND\n\n• > $20 in controversy No • Equitable claim – seeks nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction, specific right performance) since legal remedy is inadequate OR\n\n• ≤ $20 in controversy The Seventh Amendment guarantees the right to a jury trial in civil cases where the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars. But this right only applies to legal claims (eg, the team's $10 million counterclaim)—not equitable claims (eg, the city's permanent injunction claim). And when, as here, a case involves both legal and equitable claims that share common issues of fact (eg, obligations under the stadium lease), the court must first hold a jury trial on the legal claim (Choice B). The judge must then hold a nonjury trial on the equitable claim, relying on the jury's findings of fact on the legal claim to do so (Choices C & D). Educational objective: When a case involves legal and equitable claims that share common fact issues, the court should hold a jury trial on the legal claim before holding a nonjury trial on the equitable claim. References\n• U.S. Const. amend. VII (right to a jury trial).\n\n• Beacon Theatres v. Westover, 359 U.S. 500, 510–11 (1959) (stating that legal claims must be decided by a jury before the court determines related equitable claims).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• U.S. Const. amend. VII (right to a jury trial).\n\n• Beacon Theatres v. Westover, 359 U.S. 500, 510–11 (1959) (stating that legal claims must be decided by a jury before the court determines related equitable claims).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[456],"sourceAnswerPages":[457],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一支橄榄球队与一座城市签订十年租约，使用该市的体育场。租约履行五年后，球队威胁离开该体育场并迁往另一座城市。市政府在联邦法院起诉球队，请求永久禁令，阻止球队违反租约并离开。球队在答辩状中提出反诉，请求赔偿因市政府据称未按照租约要求适当维护体育场而造成的 1,000 万美元损失。球队要求就反诉进行陪审团审判。市政府申请先审理其永久禁令请求，再审理球队的反诉。球队提出异议，并申请先由陪审团审理其反诉，再审理市政府的禁令请求。法院应如何裁定双方动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院应先由陪审团审理球队的反诉，再对市政府请求中的剩余问题进行无陪审团审判。","B":"法院应先对市政府请求进行无陪审团审判，再由陪审团审理球队反诉中的剩余问题。","C":"法院应先对市政府请求进行无陪审团审判，但其认定或结论在后来由陪审团审理球队反诉时不具有约束力。","D":"法院应安排由陪审团同时审理市政府请求和球队反诉。"},"explanationZh":"民事案件中的陪审团审判权：\n\n有陪审团审判权：\n• 法律请求——请求以金钱救济补偿损失，例如侵权或违约损害赔偿；并且\n• 争议金额超过 20 美元。\n\n无陪审团审判权：\n• 衡平法请求——因法律救济不足而请求非金钱救济，例如禁令、实际履行；或\n• 争议金额不超过 20 美元。\n\n第七修正案保障争议金额超过 20 美元的民事案件由陪审团审判的权利。但是，该权利只适用于法律请求（例如球队请求 1,000 万美元的反诉），不适用于衡平法请求（例如市政府的永久禁令请求）。而且，本题案件同时涉及共享共同事实问题（例如体育场租约项下义务）的法律请求和衡平法请求时，法院必须先由陪审团审理法律请求（选项 B）。随后，法官必须对衡平法请求进行无陪审团审判，并依据陪审团对法律请求所作的事实认定作出裁判（选项 C、D）。\n\n学习目标：案件中的法律请求与衡平法请求共享共同事实问题时，法院应先由陪审团审理法律请求，再对衡平法请求进行无陪审团审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• U.S. Const. amend. VII（陪审团审判权）。\n• Beacon Theatres v. Westover, 359 U.S. 500, 510–11 (1959)（指出法院裁定相关衡平法请求前，必须先由陪审团裁定法律请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-152","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":152,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"An employee brought a federal diversity action against her former employer for negligence. Before the close of evidence, the parties submitted proposed jury instructions. Prior to the parties' closing arguments, the judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give, provided them an opportunity to object, and instructed the jury. During the employer's closing argument, the employee repeatedly yelled out, \"That's a lie!\" After several warnings, the judge ordered the bailiff to remove the employee from the courtroom. At the conclusion of the employer's closing argument, the employer has requested that the judge instruct the jury to disregard the employee's outbursts. What argument has the best chance of persuading the court to give this jury instruction?","choices":{"A":"The employee's conduct was plain error that affected the employer's substantial rights.","B":"The employer could not have reasonably anticipated this issue by the close of evidence.","C":"The judge did not set a deadline for jury-instruction requests.","D":"The parties may request jury instructions at any time before the jury is discharged."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Jury instructions\n\n(FRCP 51)\n\nRequests Party:\n\n• must file request by close of evidence unless earlier, reasonable deadline set by court\n• may file request after close of evidence when: – request relates to issue that party could not have reasonably anticipated by deadline or – court permits late submission related to any issue Instructions Court:\n\n• must inform parties of proposed instructions before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• must allow parties to object on record & outside jury's presence before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• may consider objection made after court instructs jury if instruction constituted plain error\n• may instruct jury at any time before discharge Objections Party:\n\n• must distinctly state objection & its grounds on record\n• must object at court-appointed time or promptly after learning of proposed instruction FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nJury instructions advise jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. These instructions can be formulated by the court or the parties. FRCP 51 allows each party to request specific jury instructions before or at the close of evidence unless the court sets an earlier, reasonable deadline.* Additionally, a request may be filed after the close of evidence if:\n• the request relates to an issue that the party could not have reasonably anticipated by the deadline or\n\n• the court permits a late submission that relates to any issue.\n\nHere, the employer requested after the close of evidence that the judge instruct the jury to disregard the employee's outbursts during the employer's closing argument. As a result,\n\nthe employer's best argument to persuade the court to give this instruction is that the employer could not have reasonably anticipated this issue by the close of evidence. *To request a jury instruction, a party generally must file a written request with the court and furnish it to every other party. Some courts allow verbal requests, but they are highly disfavored. (Choice A) When a party fails to preserve an error by objecting at trial, the error can only be reviewed via posttrial relief or appeal if it is a plain error. However, the employer is not seeking posttrial relief or an appeal, so this is not the best argument. (Choice C) When a trial judge does not set a deadline for jury-instruction requests, the default deadline is the close of evidence. Since that deadline has already passed, this argument is unlikely to persuade the court. (Choice D) The court may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged—ie, released from its duties in the case. However, the parties must file their requests for jury instructions before or at the close of evidence unless an exception applies (as seen here). Educational objective: A party may request specific jury instructions after the close of evidence when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a) (jury-instruction requests).","rule":"A party may request specific jury instructions after the close of evidence when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission.","irac":{"issue":"An employee brought a federal diversity action against her former employer for negligence. Before the close of evidence, the parties submitted proposed jury instructions. Prior to the parties' closing arguments, the judge informed the parties of the instructions she would give, provided them an opportunity to object, and instructed the jury. During the employer's closing argument, the employee repeatedly yelled out, \"That's a lie!\" After several warnings, the judge ordered the bailiff to remove the employee from the courtroom. At the conclusion of the employer's closing argument, the employer has requested that the judge instruct the jury to disregard the employee's outbursts. What argument has the best chance of persuading the court to give this jury instruction?","rule":"A party may request specific jury instructions after the close of evidence when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission.","analysis":"Jury instructions\n\n(FRCP 51)\n\nRequests Party:\n\n• must file request by close of evidence unless earlier, reasonable deadline set by court\n• may file request after close of evidence when: – request relates to issue that party could not have reasonably anticipated by deadline or – court permits late submission related to any issue Instructions Court:\n\n• must inform parties of proposed instructions before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• must allow parties to object on record & outside jury's presence before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• may consider objection made after court instructs jury if instruction constituted plain error\n• may instruct jury at any time before discharge Objections Party:\n\n• must distinctly state objection & its grounds on record\n• must object at court-appointed time or promptly after learning of proposed instruction FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nJury instructions advise jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. These instructions can be formulated by the court or the parties. FRCP 51 allows each party to request specific jury instructions before or at the close of evidence unless the court sets an earlier, reasonable deadline.* Additionally, a request may be filed after the close of evidence if:\n• the request relates to an issue that the party could not have reasonably anticipated by the deadline or\n\n• the court permits a late submission that relates to any issue.\n\nHere, the employer requested after the close of evidence that the judge instruct the jury to disregard the employee's outbursts during the employer's closing argument. As a result,\n\nthe employer's best argument to persuade the court to give this instruction is that the employer could not have reasonably anticipated this issue by the close of evidence. *To request a jury instruction, a party generally must file a written request with the court and furnish it to every other party. Some courts allow verbal requests, but they are highly disfavored. (Choice A) When a party fails to preserve an error by objecting at trial, the error can only be reviewed via posttrial relief or appeal if it is a plain error. However, the employer is not seeking posttrial relief or an appeal, so this is not the best argument. (Choice C) When a trial judge does not set a deadline for jury-instruction requests, the default deadline is the close of evidence. Since that deadline has already passed, this argument is unlikely to persuade the court. (Choice D) The court may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged—ie, released from its duties in the case. However, the parties must file their requests for jury instructions before or at the close of evidence unless an exception applies (as seen here). Educational objective: A party may request specific jury instructions after the close of evidence when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a) (jury-instruction requests).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a) (jury-instruction requests).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[458],"sourceAnswerPages":[459,460],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名雇员对前雇主提起联邦多元州籍过失诉讼。举证结束前，双方提交了拟议陪审团指示。在双方作结案陈词前，法官将其准备采用的指示告知双方，给予双方提出异议的机会，并向陪审团作出指示。雇主作结案陈词期间，雇员反复大喊：“那是谎话！”法官警告数次后，命令法警将雇员带离法庭。雇主结案陈词结束时，请求法官指示陪审团不考虑雇员的喊话。下列哪一项论点最有可能说服法院作出该陪审团指示？","choicesZh":{"A":"雇员的行为构成影响雇主重大权利的明显错误。","B":"在举证结束时，雇主不可能合理预见这一问题。","C":"法官没有规定陪审团指示请求的期限。","D":"双方可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候请求陪审团指示。"},"explanationZh":"陪审团指示（FRCP 51）：\n\n请求——当事人：\n• 必须在举证结束时前提交请求，除非法院规定了更早且合理的期限；\n• 在下列情况下，可于举证结束后提交请求：\n  – 请求涉及该方在期限届满前不可能合理预见的问题；或\n  – 法院允许就任何问题逾期提交。\n\n指示——法院：\n• 必须在向陪审团作出指示及结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方；\n• 必须在向陪审团作出指示及结案陈词前，允许双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议；\n• 如果指示构成明显错误，可以考虑法院作出指示后才提出的异议；\n• 可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候向其作出指示。\n\n异议——当事人：\n• 必须在案卷记录中明确陈述异议及其理由；\n• 必须在法院指定的时间提出异议，或得知拟议指示后立即提出。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n陪审团指示告知陪审员评议程序和适用法律。这些指示可以由法院或双方拟定。FRCP 51 允许各方在举证结束前或结束时请求特定陪审团指示，除非法院规定了更早且合理的期限。* 此外，在下列情况下，可以在举证结束后提交请求：\n• 请求涉及该方在期限届满前不可能合理预见的问题；或\n• 法院允许就任何问题逾期提交。\n\n本题雇主在举证结束后，请求法官指示陪审团不考虑雇员在雇主结案陈词期间的喊话。因此，雇主说服法院作出该指示的最佳论点是：在举证结束时，雇主不可能合理预见这一问题。\n\n*请求陪审团指示通常必须向法院提交书面请求，并将其提供给其他各方。有些法院允许口头请求，但非常不鼓励采用这种方式。\n\n（选项 A）当事人未在审判时提出异议而保留错误时，只有该错误属于明显错误，才可通过审判后救济或上诉获得审查。但是，雇主并非请求审判后救济或提起上诉，所以这不是最佳论点。\n\n（选项 C）审判法官未规定陪审团指示请求期限时，默认期限是举证结束时。该期限已经届满，所以这一论点不太可能说服法院。\n\n（选项 D）法院可以在陪审团解散，即解除其案件职责前的任何时候向其作出指示。但是，除非存在例外（本题即如此），双方必须在举证结束前或结束时提交陪审团指示请求。\n\n学习目标：当事人可在下列情况下于举证结束后请求特定陪审团指示：(1) 该指示涉及期限届满前不可能合理预见的问题；或 (2) 法院准许。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a)（陪审团指示请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-153","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":153,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A woman approached a stop sign at a four-way intersection and applied her brakes, which failed. The woman drove into the intersection and was hit by a truck, causing her to sustain severe injuries. The woman then brought a diversity action against the brake manufacturer in a federal court in State A for negligence. The manufacturer filed an answer to the woman's complaint, asserting the affirmative defense of contributory negligence. The jury returned a verdict for the woman, specifically determining that the manufacturer had negligently designed the brakes and that the woman was not negligent. The court then entered a final judgment pursuant to the verdict. Five months later, a man who purchased the same type of brakes was involved in an accident caused by brake failure. He filed a negligence action against the manufacturer in the State A federal court. The man argued that the judgment in the first action bound the court on the issue of whether the manufacturer negligently designed the brakes. State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion. Should the court give preclusive effect to the judgment entered in the first action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the determination that the manufacturer negligently designed the brakes was not essential to the judgment.","B":"No, because the man was not a party to the first action.","C":"Yes, because the man could not have joined the first action.","D":"Yes, because the jury determined that the manufacturer negligently designed the brakes."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) bars the relitigation of issues of fact or law that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is essential if it provided a clear and necessary component of the ultimate decision or, stated differently, if the final judgment depended on the determination of that issue. Conversely, when multiple issues were determined in a prior action, issues that were contrary to the final judgment are not essential.* Here, the parties in the first action actually litigated and the jury determined that the manufacturer negligently designed the brakes and that the woman was not negligent. Based on these determinations, the court entered a valid final judgment against the manufacturer. And since the final judgment depended on a determination of the manufacturer's negligence, the court in the second action should give preclusive effect to the judgment entered in the first action (Choice A). *The requirement that the issue be essential to the judgment stems from the concern that the fact finder will not use sufficient care in deciding nonessential issues. (Choice B) The man may assert issue preclusion even though he was not a party to the first action. That is because State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion, which allows nonparties from the first action to assert issue preclusion against parties from the first action. (Choice C) The man could not have joined the first action because his accident had not yet occurred. If he could have, the court may have barred his offensive use of collateral estoppel—ie, when a plaintiff asserts collateral estoppel against a defendant based on an issue decided in a previous case with a different plaintiff. However, courts have broad discretion to determine whether offensive collateral estoppel should be allowed. Educational objective: Issue preclusion prevents the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is essential when it provided a clear and necessary component of the ultimate decision, and an issue that was contrary to the final judgment is not essential. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 cmt. h (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining when the determination of an issue is essential to a judgment).\n\n• 18 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4421 (3d ed. 2020) (describing when an issue is and is not essential to a final judgment for purposes of issue preclusion).\n\n• Parkland Hosiery Co., Inc. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 326–31 (1979) (discussing the development of nonmutual issue preclusion and the offensive use of collateral estoppel).","rule":"Issue preclusion prevents the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is essential when it provided a clear and necessary component of the ultimate decision, and an issue that was contrary to the final judgment is not essential.","irac":{"issue":"A woman approached a stop sign at a four-way intersection and applied her brakes, which failed. The woman drove into the intersection and was hit by a truck, causing her to sustain severe injuries. The woman then brought a diversity action against the brake manufacturer in a federal court in State A for negligence. The manufacturer filed an answer to the woman's complaint, asserting the affirmative defense of contributory negligence. The jury returned a verdict for the woman, specifically determining that the manufacturer had negligently designed the brakes and that the woman was not negligent. The court then entered a final judgment pursuant to the verdict. Five months later, a man who purchased the same type of brakes was involved in an accident caused by brake failure. He filed a negligence action against the manufacturer in the State A federal court. The man argued that the judgment in the first action bound the court on the issue of whether the manufacturer negligently designed the brakes. State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion. Should the court give preclusive effect to the judgment entered in the first action?","rule":"Issue preclusion prevents the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is essential when it provided a clear and necessary component of the ultimate decision, and an issue that was contrary to the final judgment is not essential.","analysis":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) bars the relitigation of issues of fact or law that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is essential if it provided a clear and necessary component of the ultimate decision or, stated differently, if the final judgment depended on the determination of that issue. Conversely, when multiple issues were determined in a prior action, issues that were contrary to the final judgment are not essential.* Here, the parties in the first action actually litigated and the jury determined that the manufacturer negligently designed the brakes and that the woman was not negligent. Based on these determinations, the court entered a valid final judgment against the manufacturer. And since the final judgment depended on a determination of the manufacturer's negligence, the court in the second action should give preclusive effect to the judgment entered in the first action (Choice A). *The requirement that the issue be essential to the judgment stems from the concern that the fact finder will not use sufficient care in deciding nonessential issues. (Choice B) The man may assert issue preclusion even though he was not a party to the first action. That is because State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion, which allows nonparties from the first action to assert issue preclusion against parties from the first action. (Choice C) The man could not have joined the first action because his accident had not yet occurred. If he could have, the court may have barred his offensive use of collateral estoppel—ie, when a plaintiff asserts collateral estoppel against a defendant based on an issue decided in a previous case with a different plaintiff. However, courts have broad discretion to determine whether offensive collateral estoppel should be allowed. Educational objective: Issue preclusion prevents the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment in a prior lawsuit. An issue is essential when it provided a clear and necessary component of the ultimate decision, and an issue that was contrary to the final judgment is not essential. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 cmt. h (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining when the determination of an issue is essential to a judgment).\n\n• 18 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4421 (3d ed. 2020) (describing when an issue is and is not essential to a final judgment for purposes of issue preclusion).\n\n• Parkland Hosiery Co., Inc. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 326–31 (1979) (discussing the development of nonmutual issue preclusion and the offensive use of collateral estoppel).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 cmt. h (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining when the determination of an issue is essential to a judgment).\n\n• 18 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4421 (3d ed. 2020) (describing when an issue is and is not essential to a final judgment for purposes of issue preclusion).\n\n• Parkland Hosiery Co., Inc. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 326–31 (1979) (discussing the development of nonmutual issue preclusion and the offensive use of collateral estoppel).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[461],"sourceAnswerPages":[462,463],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子驾车接近四向路口的停车标志时踩下制动踏板，但制动器失灵。女子驶入路口后被一辆卡车撞上，身受重伤。随后，她在 A 州联邦法院对制动器制造商提起多元州籍过失诉讼。制造商对女子的起诉状提交答辩状，主张共同过失这一积极抗辩。陪审团作出有利于女子的裁决，并明确认定制造商在设计制动器时存在过失，女子不存在过失。法院随后依裁决登记终局判决。五个月后，一名购买同款制动器的男子因制动器失灵发生事故。他在 A 州联邦法院对制造商提起过失诉讼。男子主张，第一案判决在制造商设计制动器是否存在过失这一问题上对法院具有约束力。A 州允许非相互性争点排除。法院是否应给予第一案登记的判决排除效力？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为制造商过失设计制动器的认定并非判决所必要。","B":"不应，因为男子并非第一案当事人。","C":"应当，因为男子无法加入第一案。","D":"应当，因为陪审团认定制造商过失设计制动器。"},"explanationZh":"争点排除（即附带禁反言）禁止重新争讼先前诉讼中已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的事实或法律问题。如果一项问题构成最终决定清楚且必要的组成部分，或者换言之，终局判决以该问题的裁定为依据，该问题就是判决所必要的。相反，如果先前诉讼裁定了多个问题，与终局判决相抵触的问题并非判决所必要。*\n\n本题第一案双方对制造商是否过失设计制动器以及女子是否存在过失进行了实际争讼，陪审团也对这两个问题作出裁定。法院依据这些裁定登记了对制造商不利的有效终局判决。由于终局判决以制造商过失的认定为依据，第二案法院应给予第一案判决排除效力（选项 A）。\n\n*要求该问题为判决所必要，是因为担心事实裁判者在裁定非必要问题时不会给予充分审慎。\n\n（选项 B）男子即使不是第一案当事人，仍可主张争点排除。A 州允许非相互性争点排除，第一案的非当事人可以对第一案当事人主张争点排除。\n\n（选项 C）男子无法加入第一案，因为当时他的事故尚未发生。如果他本可加入，法院可能禁止他进攻性使用附带禁反言，即原告依据先前由另一原告提起的案件中裁定的问题，对被告主张附带禁反言。但是，是否允许进攻性附带禁反言，法院享有广泛裁量权。\n\n学习目标：争点排除禁止重新争讼先前诉讼中已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。一项问题构成最终决定清楚且必要的组成部分时，即为判决所必要；与终局判决相抵触的问题并非判决所必要。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 cmt. h (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（说明问题的裁定何时为判决所必要）。\n• 18 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4421 (3d ed. 2020)（说明为适用争点排除，一项问题何时属于或不属于终局判决所必要）。\n• Parkland Hosiery Co., Inc. v. Shore, 439 U.S. 322, 326–31 (1979)（讨论非相互性争点排除的发展及进攻性使用附带禁反言）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-049"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-154","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":154,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"In the first two weeks of a four-week bench trial in federal court, the plaintiff had called six witnesses to testify. Before the defense had an opportunity to call its own witnesses, the judge presiding over the case suddenly died from a heart attack. How is the case likely to proceed?","choices":{"A":"A new judge will take over, and the plaintiff's witnesses must testify again in the new judge's presence.","B":"A new judge will take over upon certifying familiarity with the record and determining that the case may be completed without prejudice.","C":"A new trial will be ordered.","D":"The case will be dismissed without prejudice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When a judge presiding over a hearing or trial is unable to proceed (as seen here), another judge may take over so long as this successor judge:\n• certifies that he/she is familiar with the record and\n\n• determines that the case may be completed without prejudice to the parties.\n\nWhen the successor judge takes over a hearing or bench trial, witnesses who previously testified need only testify again in the successor judge's presence when (1) their testimony is material and disputed, (2) they can do so without undue burden, and (3) a party so requested. Therefore, the plaintiff's witnesses will only need to testify again if these requirements are satisfied—which cannot be determined under these facts (Choice A). (Choice C) In the event a judge is incapacitated and cannot proceed, a new trial will be ordered only if the successor judge cannot certify familiarity with the record OR proceeding with the case will prejudice the parties (both of which are extremely rare). (Choice D) When a case is dismissed without prejudice, the plaintiff may sue the defendant on the same claim in the future—ie, the dismissal is not an adjudication on the merits. However, a case will not be dismissed due to the incapacitation of the judge. Educational objective: A successor judge may take over upon certifying familiarity with the record and determining that the case may be completed without prejudice to the parties. Otherwise, a new trial will be ordered. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 63 (judge's inability to proceed).","rule":"A successor judge may take over upon certifying familiarity with the record and determining that the case may be completed without prejudice to the parties. Otherwise, a new trial will be ordered.","irac":{"issue":"In the first two weeks of a four-week bench trial in federal court, the plaintiff had called six witnesses to testify. Before the defense had an opportunity to call its own witnesses, the judge presiding over the case suddenly died from a heart attack. How is the case likely to proceed?","rule":"A successor judge may take over upon certifying familiarity with the record and determining that the case may be completed without prejudice to the parties. Otherwise, a new trial will be ordered.","analysis":"When a judge presiding over a hearing or trial is unable to proceed (as seen here), another judge may take over so long as this successor judge:\n• certifies that he/she is familiar with the record and\n\n• determines that the case may be completed without prejudice to the parties.\n\nWhen the successor judge takes over a hearing or bench trial, witnesses who previously testified need only testify again in the successor judge's presence when (1) their testimony is material and disputed, (2) they can do so without undue burden, and (3) a party so requested. Therefore, the plaintiff's witnesses will only need to testify again if these requirements are satisfied—which cannot be determined under these facts (Choice A). (Choice C) In the event a judge is incapacitated and cannot proceed, a new trial will be ordered only if the successor judge cannot certify familiarity with the record OR proceeding with the case will prejudice the parties (both of which are extremely rare). (Choice D) When a case is dismissed without prejudice, the plaintiff may sue the defendant on the same claim in the future—ie, the dismissal is not an adjudication on the merits. However, a case will not be dismissed due to the incapacitation of the judge. Educational objective: A successor judge may take over upon certifying familiarity with the record and determining that the case may be completed without prejudice to the parties. Otherwise, a new trial will be ordered. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 63 (judge's inability to proceed).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 63 (judge's inability to proceed).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[466],"sourceAnswerPages":[467],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一宗为期四周的联邦法院法官审判进行到前两周时，原告已经传唤六名证人作证。被告方尚未有机会传唤自己的证人，主持案件的法官突然因心脏病发作去世。案件可能如何继续？","choicesZh":{"A":"由新法官接手，原告证人必须在新法官面前重新作证。","B":"新法官确认熟悉案卷记录并认定案件可以在不损害双方利益的情况下完成后，接手案件。","C":"法院将命令重新审判。","D":"案件将无损害驳回。"},"explanationZh":"主持听证或审判的法官无法继续履行职责时（本题即如此），另一名法官可以接手，但继任法官必须：\n• 确认自己熟悉案卷记录；并且\n• 认定案件可以在不损害双方利益的情况下完成。\n\n继任法官接手听证或法官审判时，先前作证的证人只有在以下条件全部满足时，才需要在继任法官面前重新作证：(1) 其证言重大且有争议；(2) 证人重新作证不会承受不当负担；且 (3) 一方提出请求。因此，原告证人只有在满足这些要求时才需要重新作证；根据题干事实无法确定这些要求是否满足（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 C）法官丧失履职能力而无法继续时，只有继任法官无法确认熟悉案卷记录，或继续审理案件将损害双方利益，才会命令重新审判；这两种情况都极为少见。\n\n（选项 D）案件无损害驳回后，原告将来可以就同一请求再次起诉被告，即该驳回不属于实体裁判。但是，案件不会因为法官丧失履职能力而被驳回。\n\n学习目标：继任法官确认熟悉案卷记录并认定案件可以在不损害双方利益的情况下完成后，可以接手案件。否则，法院将命令重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 63（法官无法继续履职）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-050"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-155","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":155,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A partnership registered in State A sued a former employee domiciled in State A in a federal court in State A. The partnership claimed that the employee breached the noncompete provision of her contract, which resulted in a $100,000 loss in revenue for the partnership. The partnership's members are all domiciled in State B. The summons and complaint were served at the employee's apartment, where the process server handed the documents to the employee's 17-year-old roommate. The roommate neglected to give the documents to the employee, so the employee did not file an answer or any other response. Thirty days later, the partnership asked the court clerk to enter the employee's default, and the clerk did so. The partnership then applied to the court for the entry of a default judgment. Upon receiving notice of the default-judgment hearing, the employee filed a motion to set aside the entry of default. In the motion, the employee argued that the noncompete provision was invalid. If the court grants the employee's motion, what is the most likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"The clerk erred by entering the employee's default.","B":"The court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction over the case because the parties are not diverse.","C":"The employee provided good cause for failing to timely respond.","D":"The employee was improperly served with process because the process server delivered process to the employee's roommate."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A default occurs when a defendant fails to timely serve an answer, which is generally due within 21 days after the defendant is served with process.* When the plaintiff shows this failure to the court clerk, the clerk must enter the defendant's default into the record of the case (as the clerk did here) (Choice A). However, a federal court can set aside an entry of default for good cause, which exists when:\n• the defendant's failure to timely respond was the result of excusable neglect\n• the defendant presented a meritorious defense and\n• the plaintiff would not be substantially prejudiced if the case is reopened. Here, the employee's failure to timely respond was the result of excusable neglect because the roommate did not give the employee the summons and complaint. The employee then presented a meritorious defense by arguing that the noncompete provision was invalid. Reopening the case would not substantially prejudice the partnership since it can still pursue its claim. Therefore, if the court grants the employee's motion to set aside the entry of default, the most likely explanation is that the employee provided good cause for failing to timely respond. *If the defendant files a pre-answer FRCP 12 motion, the defendant must serve an answer within 14 days after notice of the court's denial of the motion. A default occurs if the defendant fails to serve an answer by this deadline. (Choice B) The court does have subject-matter jurisdiction based in diversity. The amount in controversy is $100,000 (exceeds $75,000), and the parties are citizens of different states (ie, diverse) because the employee is domiciled in State A and the partnership's members are domiciled in State B. (Choice D) A defendant-individual in the U.S. can be properly served with process by leaving the summons and complaint at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion—eg, the employee's 17-year-old roommate. Educational objective: A federal court may set aside an entry of default for good cause, which exists when (1) the failure to timely respond was the result of the defendant's excusable neglect, (2) the defendant presented a meritorious defense, and (3) the plaintiff would not be substantially prejudiced if the case is reopened. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c) (setting aside a default or a default judgment).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2696 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining when a federal court will set aside an entry of default).","rule":"A federal court may set aside an entry of default for good cause, which exists when (1) the failure to timely respond was the result of the defendant's excusable neglect, (2) the defendant presented a meritorious defense, and (3) the plaintiff would not be substantially prejudiced if the case is reopened.","irac":{"issue":"A partnership registered in State A sued a former employee domiciled in State A in a federal court in State A. The partnership claimed that the employee breached the noncompete provision of her contract, which resulted in a $100,000 loss in revenue for the partnership. The partnership's members are all domiciled in State B. The summons and complaint were served at the employee's apartment, where the process server handed the documents to the employee's 17-year-old roommate. The roommate neglected to give the documents to the employee, so the employee did not file an answer or any other response. Thirty days later, the partnership asked the court clerk to enter the employee's default, and the clerk did so. The partnership then applied to the court for the entry of a default judgment. Upon receiving notice of the default-judgment hearing, the employee filed a motion to set aside the entry of default. In the motion, the employee argued that the noncompete provision was invalid. If the court grants the employee's motion, what is the most likely explanation?","rule":"A federal court may set aside an entry of default for good cause, which exists when (1) the failure to timely respond was the result of the defendant's excusable neglect, (2) the defendant presented a meritorious defense, and (3) the plaintiff would not be substantially prejudiced if the case is reopened.","analysis":"A default occurs when a defendant fails to timely serve an answer, which is generally due within 21 days after the defendant is served with process.* When the plaintiff shows this failure to the court clerk, the clerk must enter the defendant's default into the record of the case (as the clerk did here) (Choice A). However, a federal court can set aside an entry of default for good cause, which exists when:\n• the defendant's failure to timely respond was the result of excusable neglect\n• the defendant presented a meritorious defense and\n• the plaintiff would not be substantially prejudiced if the case is reopened. Here, the employee's failure to timely respond was the result of excusable neglect because the roommate did not give the employee the summons and complaint. The employee then presented a meritorious defense by arguing that the noncompete provision was invalid. Reopening the case would not substantially prejudice the partnership since it can still pursue its claim. Therefore, if the court grants the employee's motion to set aside the entry of default, the most likely explanation is that the employee provided good cause for failing to timely respond. *If the defendant files a pre-answer FRCP 12 motion, the defendant must serve an answer within 14 days after notice of the court's denial of the motion. A default occurs if the defendant fails to serve an answer by this deadline. (Choice B) The court does have subject-matter jurisdiction based in diversity. The amount in controversy is $100,000 (exceeds $75,000), and the parties are citizens of different states (ie, diverse) because the employee is domiciled in State A and the partnership's members are domiciled in State B. (Choice D) A defendant-individual in the U.S. can be properly served with process by leaving the summons and complaint at the defendant's dwelling with a resident of suitable age and discretion—eg, the employee's 17-year-old roommate. Educational objective: A federal court may set aside an entry of default for good cause, which exists when (1) the failure to timely respond was the result of the defendant's excusable neglect, (2) the defendant presented a meritorious defense, and (3) the plaintiff would not be substantially prejudiced if the case is reopened. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c) (setting aside a default or a default judgment).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2696 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining when a federal court will set aside an entry of default).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c) (setting aside a default or a default judgment).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2696 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining when a federal court will set aside an entry of default).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[469],"sourceAnswerPages":[470,471],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册的合伙企业，在 A 州联邦法院起诉一名住所位于 A 州的前雇员。合伙企业主张，该雇员违反其合同中的竞业禁止条款，导致合伙企业损失 100,000 美元收入。合伙企业的所有合伙人住所均位于 B 州。传票和起诉状在雇员的公寓送达，送达员将文件交给雇员 17 岁的室友。室友忘记将文件交给雇员，所以雇员没有提交答辩状或任何其他回应。三十天后，合伙企业请求法院书记员登记雇员缺席，书记员予以登记。随后，合伙企业向法院申请登记缺席判决。雇员收到缺席判决听证通知后，提出撤销缺席登记动议。雇员在动议中主张竞业禁止条款无效。如果法院批准雇员动议，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"书记员登记雇员缺席属于错误。","B":"法院对案件缺乏事项管辖权，因为双方不具有多元州籍。","C":"雇员对未及时回应提出了正当理由。","D":"诉讼文书送达不适当，因为送达员将文书交给了雇员的室友。"},"explanationZh":"被告未及时送达答辩状时即构成缺席；通常须在被告诉讼文书送达后 21 天内提交答辩状。* 原告向法院书记员证明被告未及时回应后，书记员必须将被告缺席登记于案件记录中，本题书记员正是如此处理（选项 A）。\n\n但是，联邦法院可以基于正当理由撤销缺席登记；在下列条件满足时，存在正当理由：\n• 被告未及时回应是可原谅的疏忽所致；\n• 被告提出了有实体依据的抗辩；并且\n• 重新开启案件不会使原告遭受重大不利。\n\n本题雇员未及时回应是可原谅的疏忽所致，因为室友没有将传票和起诉状交给她。雇员随后主张竞业禁止条款无效，由此提出有实体依据的抗辩。重新开启案件不会使合伙企业遭受重大不利，因为企业仍可继续主张其请求。因此，如果法院批准雇员撤销缺席登记的动议，最可能的解释是雇员对未及时回应提出了正当理由。\n\n*如果被告在答辩前提出 FRCP 12 动议，被告必须在收到法院驳回动议的通知后 14 天内送达答辩状。被告未在该期限内送达答辩状，即构成缺席。\n\n（选项 B）法院基于多元州籍管辖权拥有事项管辖权。争议金额为 100,000 美元，超过 75,000 美元；双方是不同州的公民，因而具有多元州籍，因为雇员住所位于 A 州，而合伙企业的所有合伙人住所均位于 B 州。\n\n（选项 D）可以将传票和起诉状留在美国境内被告个人的住所，交给居住在该处且年龄与判断能力适当的人，例如雇员 17 岁的室友，从而适当完成诉讼文书送达。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院可基于正当理由撤销缺席登记；在以下情况下存在正当理由：(1) 未及时回应是被告可原谅的疏忽所致；(2) 被告提出有实体依据的抗辩；且 (3) 重新开启案件不会使原告遭受重大不利。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c)（撤销缺席或缺席判决）。\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2696 (4th ed. 2020)（说明联邦法院何时会撤销缺席登记）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-156","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":156,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A gallery contracted with an owner of a painting to display the painting for five years, at which time the gallery agreed to return the painting to the owner. During the fifth year of the contract, the gallery agreed to loan the painting to a foreign businesswoman without obtaining the owner's permission. The businesswoman took the painting and has not been heard from since. When the owner learned that the gallery no longer possessed the painting, the owner sued the gallery in federal court for breach of contract. At the conclusion of trial, the court entered judgment in favor of the owner for $100,000, the market value of the painting when the parties entered the contract. The owner believed that the court should have awarded him $350,000, the market value of the painting when the contract ended. To recover this amount, the owner sued the gallery in federal court for conversion. The gallery has moved to dismiss this action on the basis of res judicata (claim preclusion). Is the court likely to grant the gallery's motion?","choices":{"A":"No.","B":"Yes, because the issue of whether the gallery failed to return the painting to the owner was actually litigated and necessarily decided.","C":"Yes, because the owner violated the election-of-remedies doctrine.","D":"Yes, because the owner's actions involve identical claims between the same parties."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action because the claim existed and could have been joined. This is true even if the claims are labeled differently. Here, the federal court in the first action entered a final judgment in the owner's favor for the gallery's breach of contract. The owner has now sued the gallery for conversion (identical parties). The breach of contract and conversion claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence because:\n• the facts are related in time, space, origin, and motivation (the gallery loaning the owner's painting to the businesswoman without the owner's permission)\n\n• the facts form a convenient trial unit (the same evidence applies to both claims) and\n\n• the treatment of the facts as a unit conforms to the parties' expectations (the parties were aware of these facts at the time of the first action).\n\nThe conversion claim existed at the time of the first action and could have been joined in that action, so the claims are identical. Therefore, the federal court in the second action is likely to grant the gallery's motion to dismiss based on res judicata (Choice A).* *The first judgment was in favor of the owner (plaintiff), so the owner's conversion claim merges with the judgment and is extinguished. Had the judgment been in favor of the gallery (defendant), the owner's claim would be barred by the judgment and extinguished. (Choice B) Claim preclusion applies even if the first action was not actually litigated (eg, entry of a default judgment). In contrast, the related doctrine of issue preclusion prohibits the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. (Choice C) The election-of-remedies doctrine was a common law rule that forbade plaintiffs from asserting alternative or inconsistent remedies. However, this doctrine does not apply in federal court. Educational objective: Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 24–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining identical claims for claim preclusion).","rule":"Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action.","irac":{"issue":"A gallery contracted with an owner of a painting to display the painting for five years, at which time the gallery agreed to return the painting to the owner. During the fifth year of the contract, the gallery agreed to loan the painting to a foreign businesswoman without obtaining the owner's permission. The businesswoman took the painting and has not been heard from since. When the owner learned that the gallery no longer possessed the painting, the owner sued the gallery in federal court for breach of contract. At the conclusion of trial, the court entered judgment in favor of the owner for $100,000, the market value of the painting when the parties entered the contract. The owner believed that the court should have awarded him $350,000, the market value of the painting when the contract ended. To recover this amount, the owner sued the gallery in federal court for conversion. The gallery has moved to dismiss this action on the basis of res judicata (claim preclusion). Is the court likely to grant the gallery's motion?","rule":"Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action.","analysis":"The doctrine of res judicata (ie, claim preclusion) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action because the claim existed and could have been joined. This is true even if the claims are labeled differently. Here, the federal court in the first action entered a final judgment in the owner's favor for the gallery's breach of contract. The owner has now sued the gallery for conversion (identical parties). The breach of contract and conversion claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence because:\n• the facts are related in time, space, origin, and motivation (the gallery loaning the owner's painting to the businesswoman without the owner's permission)\n\n• the facts form a convenient trial unit (the same evidence applies to both claims) and\n\n• the treatment of the facts as a unit conforms to the parties' expectations (the parties were aware of these facts at the time of the first action).\n\nThe conversion claim existed at the time of the first action and could have been joined in that action, so the claims are identical. Therefore, the federal court in the second action is likely to grant the gallery's motion to dismiss based on res judicata (Choice A).* *The first judgment was in favor of the owner (plaintiff), so the owner's conversion claim merges with the judgment and is extinguished. Had the judgment been in favor of the gallery (defendant), the owner's claim would be barred by the judgment and extinguished. (Choice B) Claim preclusion applies even if the first action was not actually litigated (eg, entry of a default judgment). In contrast, the related doctrine of issue preclusion prohibits the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. (Choice C) The election-of-remedies doctrine was a common law rule that forbade plaintiffs from asserting alternative or inconsistent remedies. However, this doctrine does not apply in federal court. Educational objective: Under claim preclusion, a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 24–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining identical claims for claim preclusion).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 24–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining identical claims for claim preclusion).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[472],"sourceAnswerPages":[473,474],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家画廊与一幅画的所有人订立合同，约定展出该画五年，期满时画廊将画返还所有人。合同第五年期间，画廊未经所有人许可，同意将画借给一名外国女商人。女商人取走画后便杳无音讯。所有人得知画廊已不再占有该画后，在联邦法院起诉画廊违约。审判结束时，法院登记有利于所有人的判决，判给 100,000 美元，即双方订立合同时该画的市场价值。所有人认为法院本应判给他 350,000 美元，即合同终止时该画的市场价值。为获得该金额，所有人在联邦法院以侵占为由起诉画廊。画廊以既判力（请求权排除）为由申请驳回本诉。法院可能批准画廊动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会。","B":"会，因为画廊是否未将画返还给所有人这一问题已经实际争讼并得到必要裁定。","C":"会，因为所有人违反了救济选择原则。","D":"会，因为所有人的两起诉讼涉及相同当事人之间的相同请求。"},"explanationZh":"既判力（即请求权排除）原则规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。请求符合以下条件时即为相同：(1) 源于同一交易、事件或一系列交易或事件；并且 (2) 第一案提起时请求已经存在且能够合并，所以本可在第一案中提出。即使请求使用不同名称，这一规则仍然适用。\n\n本题第一案中，联邦法院就画廊违约登记了有利于所有人的终局判决。现在所有人以侵占为由起诉画廊，当事人相同。违约请求与侵占请求源于同一交易或事件，因为：\n• 相关事实在时间、地点、起因和动机上有关联，即画廊未经所有人许可将其画借给女商人；\n• 相关事实构成便于一并审理的单元，即相同证据适用于两项请求；并且\n• 将相关事实作为一个单元处理符合双方预期，即第一案提起时双方均已知道这些事实。\n\n第一案提起时侵占请求已经存在，且本可合并于该案，所以两项请求相同。因此，第二案的联邦法院很可能依据既判力批准画廊的驳回动议（选项 A）。*\n\n*第一项判决有利于所有人（原告），所以所有人的侵占请求与判决合并并被消灭。如果判决有利于画廊（被告），所有人的请求将被该判决禁止并消灭。\n\n（选项 B）即使第一案没有经过实际争讼，例如登记缺席判决，请求权排除仍然适用。相比之下，相关的争点排除原则禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。\n\n（选项 C）救济选择原则是一项普通法规则，禁止原告主张备选或不一致的救济。但是，该原则不适用于联邦法院。\n\n学习目标：依据请求权排除，有效的实体终局判决禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。请求符合以下条件时即为相同：(1) 源于同一交易、事件或一系列交易或事件；且 (2) 本可在第一案中提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments §§ 24–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义请求权排除中的相同请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-051"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-157","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":157,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"After a federal trial in which the jury awarded the plaintiff $100,000 in compensatory damages and $7 million in punitive damages, the defendant moved for a new trial on the ground that the verdict was not supported by the evidence and also that the punitive damages award was unconstitutionally excessive. The trial court, after reviewing the evidence, denied the motion on the condition that the plaintiff accept a reduced punitive damages award of $1 million, which the plaintiff did. The defendant now wants to appeal. Which of the following principles governs the appellate court's review?","choices":{"A":"The trial court cannot weigh the evidence in ruling on a motion for a new trial.","B":"The trial court must give the plaintiff the minimum amount that the jury could have awarded under the evidence.","C":"The trial court's decision to reduce the punitive damages award is reviewed de novo.","D":"The trial court's reduction of the punitive damages award improperly invaded the province of the jury."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference Pure legal issues\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial judge misinterpreted law Clear error • High deference Factual issues in\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge would bench trials have made finding Substantial • High deference Factual issues in jury evidence* • Reverse if no reasonable jury would trials have made finding Abuse of • High deference Discretionary rulings discretion • Reverse if decision was by judge unreasonable/arbitrary *Courts may also reverse if there is insufficient/no evidence.\n\nA party may move for a new trial on the ground that the finder of fact (typically the jury) awarded an excessive amount of damages. If the trial court rules that the amount was excessive, it must give the opposing party the option to choose between a new trial on damages or a reduced amount of damages set by the court (ie, remittitur). Here, the plaintiff accepted a reduced punitive damages award, which led the court to properly deny the defendant's motion for a new trial. If an appeal is filed, the appellate court will review the trial court's ruling under the appropriate standard of review. A ruling on a motion for a new trial is typically discretionary and reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this standard or review, the trial court's discretionary rulings are given high deference and will be reversed only if unreasonable or arbitrary. However, a ruling challenged on constitutional grounds presents a pure legal issue that is reviewed de novo. Under this standard of review, a trial court's conclusions of law are given no deference and will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the trial court misinterpreted the law. Since the defendant challenged the punitive damages award as unconstitutionally excessive, the trial court's decision to reduce that award is reviewed de novo. (Choice A) A trial court may weigh the evidence in ruling on a motion for a new trial.\n\n(Choice B) When reducing the amount of damages that the jury awarded, a trial court has discretion to award any (not just the minimum) amount the jury could have awarded under the evidence. (Choice D) A federal trial court has the power to reduce a jury's excessive damages award because the jury has already determined the damages and the court is simply trimming the excess.* *In contrast, a trial court does not have the power to increase a jury's inadequate damages award (ie, additur) because it would allow the court to award damages that were not found by the jury. Educational objective: A ruling on a motion for a new trial is generally discretionary and reviewed on appeal for abuse of discretion. However, when such a ruling is challenged on constitutional grounds, the ruling presents a pure legal issue that is reviewed de novo. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001) (requiring de novo review of punitive damages awards).","rule":"A ruling on a motion for a new trial is generally discretionary and reviewed on appeal for abuse of discretion. However, when such a ruling is challenged on constitutional grounds, the ruling presents a pure legal issue that is reviewed de novo.","irac":{"issue":"After a federal trial in which the jury awarded the plaintiff $100,000 in compensatory damages and $7 million in punitive damages, the defendant moved for a new trial on the ground that the verdict was not supported by the evidence and also that the punitive damages award was unconstitutionally excessive. The trial court, after reviewing the evidence, denied the motion on the condition that the plaintiff accept a reduced punitive damages award of $1 million, which the plaintiff did. The defendant now wants to appeal. Which of the following principles governs the appellate court's review?","rule":"A ruling on a motion for a new trial is generally discretionary and reviewed on appeal for abuse of discretion. However, when such a ruling is challenged on constitutional grounds, the ruling presents a pure legal issue that is reviewed de novo.","analysis":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference Pure legal issues\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial judge misinterpreted law Clear error • High deference Factual issues in\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge would bench trials have made finding Substantial • High deference Factual issues in jury evidence* • Reverse if no reasonable jury would trials have made finding Abuse of • High deference Discretionary rulings discretion • Reverse if decision was by judge unreasonable/arbitrary *Courts may also reverse if there is insufficient/no evidence.\n\nA party may move for a new trial on the ground that the finder of fact (typically the jury) awarded an excessive amount of damages. If the trial court rules that the amount was excessive, it must give the opposing party the option to choose between a new trial on damages or a reduced amount of damages set by the court (ie, remittitur). Here, the plaintiff accepted a reduced punitive damages award, which led the court to properly deny the defendant's motion for a new trial. If an appeal is filed, the appellate court will review the trial court's ruling under the appropriate standard of review. A ruling on a motion for a new trial is typically discretionary and reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this standard or review, the trial court's discretionary rulings are given high deference and will be reversed only if unreasonable or arbitrary. However, a ruling challenged on constitutional grounds presents a pure legal issue that is reviewed de novo. Under this standard of review, a trial court's conclusions of law are given no deference and will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the trial court misinterpreted the law. Since the defendant challenged the punitive damages award as unconstitutionally excessive, the trial court's decision to reduce that award is reviewed de novo. (Choice A) A trial court may weigh the evidence in ruling on a motion for a new trial.\n\n(Choice B) When reducing the amount of damages that the jury awarded, a trial court has discretion to award any (not just the minimum) amount the jury could have awarded under the evidence. (Choice D) A federal trial court has the power to reduce a jury's excessive damages award because the jury has already determined the damages and the court is simply trimming the excess.* *In contrast, a trial court does not have the power to increase a jury's inadequate damages award (ie, additur) because it would allow the court to award damages that were not found by the jury. Educational objective: A ruling on a motion for a new trial is generally discretionary and reviewed on appeal for abuse of discretion. However, when such a ruling is challenged on constitutional grounds, the ruling presents a pure legal issue that is reviewed de novo. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001) (requiring de novo review of punitive damages awards).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59 (motion for a new trial).\n\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001) (requiring de novo review of punitive damages awards).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[475],"sourceAnswerPages":[476,477],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一宗联邦审判结束后，陪审团判给原告 100,000 美元补偿性损害赔偿和 700 万美元惩罚性损害赔偿。被告以裁决没有证据支持，且惩罚性损害赔偿高到违宪为由，申请重新审判。审判法院审查证据后，驳回该动议，但条件是原告接受将惩罚性损害赔偿减至 100 万美元；原告予以接受。被告现在希望提起上诉。下列哪项原则支配上诉法院的审查？","choicesZh":{"A":"审判法院裁定重新审判动议时不能衡量证据。","B":"审判法院必须给予原告证据所能支持的陪审团最低裁赔金额。","C":"审判法院减少惩罚性损害赔偿的决定应受重新审查。","D":"审判法院减少惩罚性损害赔偿，不当地侵犯了陪审团的职权范围。"},"explanationZh":"上诉审查标准：\n\n标准｜审查程度｜适用范围\n重新审查｜不予尊重；若上诉法院有合理理由认为审判法官错误解释法律，则推翻｜纯粹法律问题\n明显错误｜高度尊重；若任何合理法官都不会作出该认定，则推翻｜法官审判中的事实问题\n实质证据*｜高度尊重；若任何合理陪审团都不会作出该认定，则推翻｜陪审团审判中的事实问题\n滥用裁量权｜高度尊重；若法官决定不合理或武断，则推翻｜法官的裁量性裁定\n\n*证据不足或没有证据时，法院也可以推翻。\n\n一方可以事实裁判者（通常为陪审团）判给的损害赔偿金额过高为由，申请重新审判。如果审判法院裁定金额过高，必须让对方在以下两项中选择：就损害赔偿重新审判，或接受法院确定的较低损害赔偿金额，即减额裁决。本题原告接受减少后的惩罚性损害赔偿，使法院得以适当驳回被告的重新审判动议。\n\n如果提起上诉，上诉法院将按适当的审查标准审查审判法院的裁定。重新审判动议的裁定通常属于裁量性裁定，适用滥用裁量权标准审查。根据该审查标准，审判法院的裁量性裁定受到高度尊重，只有决定不合理或武断时才会被推翻。\n\n但是，基于宪法理由质疑的裁定提出纯粹法律问题，应受重新审查。根据这一标准，审判法院的法律结论不受任何尊重；如果上诉法院有合理理由认为审判法院错误解释法律，就会推翻。由于被告主张惩罚性损害赔偿高到违宪，审判法院减少该裁赔金额的决定应受重新审查。\n\n（选项 A）审判法院裁定重新审判动议时可以衡量证据。\n\n（选项 B）审判法院减少陪审团判给的损害赔偿时，有裁量权判给证据所能支持的任何金额，而不只限于最低金额。\n\n（选项 D）联邦审判法院有权减少陪审团判给的过高损害赔偿，因为陪审团已经确定损害赔偿，法院只是削减超出部分。*\n\n*相比之下，审判法院无权增加陪审团判给的不足损害赔偿，即作出增额裁决，因为这将允许法院判给未经陪审团认定的损害赔偿。\n\n学习目标：重新审判动议的裁定通常属于裁量性裁定，上诉时适用滥用裁量权标准审查。但是，如果基于宪法理由质疑该裁定，裁定就提出纯粹法律问题，应受重新审查。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 59（重新审判动议）。\n• Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Leatherman Tool Grp., Inc., 532 U.S. 424, 435 (2001)（要求重新审查惩罚性损害赔偿裁赔）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-158","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":158,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A customer brought a diversity action against a company in federal court. The customer's complaint demanded an injunction and monetary damages. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the customer for $100,000 and the judge awarded the customer an injunction. That same day, the district court determined that the verdict was reasonable and entered a final judgment consistent with these awards. The judgment did not include language about when the judgment could be enforced. The company believed that the verdict was excessive and that the injunction was burdensome. Ten days after entry of the judgment, the company filed a motion for remittitur and, in the alternative, a motion for new trial. The company also directed its employees to continue engaging in the enjoined activity until further notice. Fifteen days after entry of the judgment, the customer sought to enforce the injunction and execute the damages portion of the judgment. Which of the following actions is the court most likely to take?","choices":{"A":"Enforce the customer's injunction.","B":"Execute the customer's damages award.","C":"Grant the company's motion for new trial.","D":"Grant the company's motion for remittitur."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Stay of enforcement/execution\n\n(FRCP 62)\n\nMost final judgments Automatic 30-day stay unless court orders otherwise\n\nCertain orders No stay, unless court orders otherwise, for order that:\n\n• grants injunction or receivership or\n• directs accounting in patent-infringement action FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 62, most final judgments are automatically stayed for 30 days. This means that the judgment cannot be enforced or executed during the time in which posttrial motions or a notice of appeal can be filed. However, an automatic stay does not apply to orders (1) granting an injunction or receivership or (2) directing an accounting in a patent-infringement action. These orders can therefore be enforced and executed during this time period unless otherwise ordered by the court. Here, the final judgment entered by the district court awarded the customer damages and an injunction. The damages award is subject to the automatic 30-day stay, so the court is not likely to execute the damages award 15 days after entry of the judgment (Choice B). However, the injunction is not subject to the automatic 30-day stay, so the court is likely to enforce the injunction. (Choices C & D) A party may move for a new trial within 28 days after the entry of a final judgment, as the company did here. A new trial can be granted on several grounds, including excessive or inadequate damages. If a verdict is excessive, then the court may offer a reduction of the verdict (ie, remittitur). However, the court here already determined that the verdict of $100,000 was reasonable—not excessive. Educational objective: Most final judgments are automatically stayed for 30 days unless the court orders otherwise. However, this rule does not apply to orders (1) granting an injunction or receivership or (2) directing an accounting in a patent-infringement action. These orders can be enforced and executed immediately. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 62 (stays).","rule":"Most final judgments are automatically stayed for 30 days unless the court orders otherwise. However, this rule does not apply to orders (1) granting an injunction or receivership or (2) directing an accounting in a patent-infringement action. These orders can be enforced and executed immediately.","irac":{"issue":"A customer brought a diversity action against a company in federal court. The customer's complaint demanded an injunction and monetary damages. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the customer for $100,000 and the judge awarded the customer an injunction. That same day, the district court determined that the verdict was reasonable and entered a final judgment consistent with these awards. The judgment did not include language about when the judgment could be enforced. The company believed that the verdict was excessive and that the injunction was burdensome. Ten days after entry of the judgment, the company filed a motion for remittitur and, in the alternative, a motion for new trial. The company also directed its employees to continue engaging in the enjoined activity until further notice. Fifteen days after entry of the judgment, the customer sought to enforce the injunction and execute the damages portion of the judgment. Which of the following actions is the court most likely to take?","rule":"Most final judgments are automatically stayed for 30 days unless the court orders otherwise. However, this rule does not apply to orders (1) granting an injunction or receivership or (2) directing an accounting in a patent-infringement action. These orders can be enforced and executed immediately.","analysis":"Stay of enforcement/execution\n\n(FRCP 62)\n\nMost final judgments Automatic 30-day stay unless court orders otherwise\n\nCertain orders No stay, unless court orders otherwise, for order that:\n\n• grants injunction or receivership or\n• directs accounting in patent-infringement action FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 62, most final judgments are automatically stayed for 30 days. This means that the judgment cannot be enforced or executed during the time in which posttrial motions or a notice of appeal can be filed. However, an automatic stay does not apply to orders (1) granting an injunction or receivership or (2) directing an accounting in a patent-infringement action. These orders can therefore be enforced and executed during this time period unless otherwise ordered by the court. Here, the final judgment entered by the district court awarded the customer damages and an injunction. The damages award is subject to the automatic 30-day stay, so the court is not likely to execute the damages award 15 days after entry of the judgment (Choice B). However, the injunction is not subject to the automatic 30-day stay, so the court is likely to enforce the injunction. (Choices C & D) A party may move for a new trial within 28 days after the entry of a final judgment, as the company did here. A new trial can be granted on several grounds, including excessive or inadequate damages. If a verdict is excessive, then the court may offer a reduction of the verdict (ie, remittitur). However, the court here already determined that the verdict of $100,000 was reasonable—not excessive. Educational objective: Most final judgments are automatically stayed for 30 days unless the court orders otherwise. However, this rule does not apply to orders (1) granting an injunction or receivership or (2) directing an accounting in a patent-infringement action. These orders can be enforced and executed immediately. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 62 (stays).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 62 (stays).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[478],"sourceAnswerPages":[479],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名顾客在联邦法院对一家公司提起多元州籍诉讼。顾客的起诉状请求禁令和金钱损害赔偿。审判结束时，陪审团作出有利于顾客的裁决，判给 100,000 美元；法官也向顾客颁发禁令。当天，地区法院认定裁决合理，并依这些救济登记终局判决。判决没有说明何时可以执行。公司认为裁赔金额过高，禁令负担过重。判决登记十天后，公司提出减额裁决动议，并备选提出重新审判动议。公司还指示雇员在收到进一步通知前继续实施禁令所禁止的活动。判决登记十五天后，顾客请求执行禁令并执行判决中的损害赔偿部分。法院最可能采取下列哪项措施？","choicesZh":{"A":"执行顾客取得的禁令。","B":"执行顾客取得的损害赔偿裁赔。","C":"批准公司的重新审判动议。","D":"批准公司的减额裁决动议。"},"explanationZh":"中止判决的执行（FRCP 62）：\n\n大多数终局判决｜自动中止执行 30 天，除非法院另有命令\n\n某些命令｜除非法院另有命令，下列命令不中止执行：\n• 颁发禁令或设立接管；或\n• 指示在专利侵权诉讼中进行账目核算\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》62，大多数终局判决自动中止执行 30 天。这意味着在可提交审判后动议或上诉通知的期间内，判决不得执行。但是，以下命令不适用自动中止：(1) 颁发禁令或设立接管的命令；或 (2) 指示在专利侵权诉讼中进行账目核算的命令。因此，在此期间可以执行这些命令，除非法院另有命令。\n\n本题地区法院登记的终局判决向顾客判给损害赔偿并颁发禁令。损害赔偿裁赔适用自动中止执行 30 天，所以法院不太可能在判决登记 15 天后执行损害赔偿裁赔（选项 B）。但是，禁令不适用自动中止执行 30 天，所以法院很可能执行禁令。\n\n（选项 C、D）一方可以在终局判决登记后 28 天内申请重新审判，本题公司即如此。重新审判可以基于多项理由获准，包括损害赔偿过高或不足。如果裁赔过高，法院可以提出减少裁赔，即减额裁决。但是，本题法院已经认定 100,000 美元裁赔合理，并非过高。\n\n学习目标：除非法院另有命令，大多数终局判决自动中止执行 30 天。但是，该规则不适用于以下命令：(1) 颁发禁令或设立接管；或 (2) 指示在专利侵权诉讼中进行账目核算。这些命令可以立即执行。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 62（中止执行）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-159","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":159,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"At the conclusion of a federal civil trial, a 10-person jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff for $22 million. Immediately after reading the verdict, the court polled the jurors individually to see if the verdict was unanimous. Every juror except for two stated that he or she agreed with the verdict. After the poll was completed, the defendant moved for a new trial. The court denied the motion and directed the jurors to deliberate further. The plaintiff objected on the record. After two hours, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant. The court polled each juror again, and every juror stated that he or she concurred in the verdict. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The plaintiff appealed, arguing that the trial court abused its discretion by polling the jurors and directing them to deliberate further after returning the first verdict. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because a trial court's discretionary decisions on issues pertaining to the jury are unreviewable.","B":"No, because the trial court had the power to poll the jury members and direct them to deliberate further.","C":"Yes, because the first verdict was overwhelmingly in favor of the plaintiff.","D":"Yes, because the trial court should have ordered a new trial when the jury failed to return a unanimous verdict."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Unless otherwise stipulated, a jury must reach a unanimous verdict. To determine whether a verdict is unanimous, the court may poll the jurors individually after a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged. This must be done upon a party's request, or the court may do so on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte). If the poll reveals that the verdict is not unanimous, the court can either order a new trial OR direct the jury to deliberate further—as the court did here (Choice D). Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm this judgment. (Choice A) An appellate court may review a trial court's discretionary decisions on all issues (including issues pertaining to the jury) for abuse of discretion. Under this highly deferential standard of review, the trial court's ruling will only be reversed if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. Since the trial court reasonably directed the jury to deliberate further, it did not abuse its discretion. (Choice C) The fact that the first verdict was overwhelmingly in favor of the plaintiff is irrelevant because the verdict must be unanimous in the absence of a stipulation. Since there is no indication that the parties stipulated to a non-unanimous verdict, the trial court had the power to direct the jury to deliberate further. Educational objective: A court must on a party's request, or may on its own initiative, poll the jurors individually after a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged. If the poll reveals that the verdict is not unanimous, the court can (1) direct the jury to deliberate further or (2) order a new trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(c) (polling jurors).","rule":"A court must on a party's request, or may on its own initiative, poll the jurors individually after a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged. If the poll reveals that the verdict is not unanimous, the court can (1) direct the jury to deliberate further or (2) order a new trial.","irac":{"issue":"At the conclusion of a federal civil trial, a 10-person jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff for $22 million. Immediately after reading the verdict, the court polled the jurors individually to see if the verdict was unanimous. Every juror except for two stated that he or she agreed with the verdict. After the poll was completed, the defendant moved for a new trial. The court denied the motion and directed the jurors to deliberate further. The plaintiff objected on the record. After two hours, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant. The court polled each juror again, and every juror stated that he or she concurred in the verdict. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The plaintiff appealed, arguing that the trial court abused its discretion by polling the jurors and directing them to deliberate further after returning the first verdict. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the judgment?","rule":"A court must on a party's request, or may on its own initiative, poll the jurors individually after a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged. If the poll reveals that the verdict is not unanimous, the court can (1) direct the jury to deliberate further or (2) order a new trial.","analysis":"Unless otherwise stipulated, a jury must reach a unanimous verdict. To determine whether a verdict is unanimous, the court may poll the jurors individually after a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged. This must be done upon a party's request, or the court may do so on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte). If the poll reveals that the verdict is not unanimous, the court can either order a new trial OR direct the jury to deliberate further—as the court did here (Choice D). Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm this judgment. (Choice A) An appellate court may review a trial court's discretionary decisions on all issues (including issues pertaining to the jury) for abuse of discretion. Under this highly deferential standard of review, the trial court's ruling will only be reversed if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. Since the trial court reasonably directed the jury to deliberate further, it did not abuse its discretion. (Choice C) The fact that the first verdict was overwhelmingly in favor of the plaintiff is irrelevant because the verdict must be unanimous in the absence of a stipulation. Since there is no indication that the parties stipulated to a non-unanimous verdict, the trial court had the power to direct the jury to deliberate further. Educational objective: A court must on a party's request, or may on its own initiative, poll the jurors individually after a verdict is returned but before the jury is discharged. If the poll reveals that the verdict is not unanimous, the court can (1) direct the jury to deliberate further or (2) order a new trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(c) (polling jurors).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(c) (polling jurors).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[481],"sourceAnswerPages":[482],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一宗联邦民事审判结束时，十人陪审团作出有利于原告的裁决，判给 2,200 万美元。宣读裁决后，法院立即逐一询问陪审员，以确定裁决是否全体一致。除两人外，其他陪审员均称自己同意该裁决。询问结束后，被告申请重新审判。法院驳回动议，并指示陪审团继续评议。原告在案卷记录中提出异议。两小时后，陪审团作出有利于被告的裁决。法院再次逐一询问陪审员，每位陪审员均称同意该裁决。法院依裁决登记终局判决。原告提起上诉，主张审判法院在陪审团作出第一次裁决后逐一询问陪审员并指示他们继续评议，构成滥用裁量权。上诉法院可能推翻判决吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为审判法院对涉及陪审团问题作出的裁量性决定不受审查。","B":"不会，因为审判法院有权逐一询问陪审员并指示他们继续评议。","C":"会，因为第一次裁决以压倒性多数支持原告。","D":"会，因为陪审团未能作出全体一致裁决时，审判法院本应命令重新审判。"},"explanationZh":"除非双方另有约定，陪审团必须作出全体一致裁决。为确定裁决是否全体一致，法院可以在裁决作出后、陪审团解散前逐一询问陪审员。一方提出请求时，法院必须进行询问；法院也可以自行进行询问。如果询问显示裁决并非全体一致，法院可以命令重新审判，或者指示陪审团继续评议，本题法院即采取后一种方式（选项 D）。因此，上诉法院很可能维持该判决。\n\n（选项 A）上诉法院可以对审判法院就所有问题（包括涉及陪审团的问题）作出的裁量性决定，适用滥用裁量权标准审查。根据这一高度尊重的审查标准，只有审判法院裁定明显武断或不合理时才会被推翻。审判法院合理指示陪审团继续评议，所以没有滥用裁量权。\n\n（选项 C）第一次裁决以压倒性多数支持原告这一事实无关，因为双方没有约定时，裁决必须全体一致。没有迹象表明双方约定采用非全体一致裁决，所以审判法院有权指示陪审团继续评议。\n\n学习目标：一方提出请求时，法院必须在裁决作出后、陪审团解散前逐一询问陪审员；法院也可以自行询问。如果询问显示裁决并非全体一致，法院可以：(1) 指示陪审团继续评议；或 (2) 命令重新审判。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(c)（逐一询问陪审员）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-052"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-160","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":160,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man from State A sued a woman from State B in a federal district court for negligence to recover $100,000. In her answer denying liability, the woman requested a jury trial. The case proceeded to trial. Before the judge instructed the jury, the man moved for judgment as a matter of law. The judge denied this motion. On March 1, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the woman, and the judge entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict that same day. On March 21, the man filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law. On April 15, the judge entered an order denying that motion. On May 1, the man filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court. Does the appellate court have jurisdiction to hear the appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man did not file the notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate court.","B":"No, because the man failed to file the notice of appeal within 30 days of the entry of final judgment.","C":"Yes, because the man preserved the renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law.","D":"Yes, because the man timely filed the notice of appeal with the proper clerk."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 30 days after the district court enters final judgment to allow an appellate court to acquire jurisdiction over the case.* But if a party files a posttrial motion within 28 days after the entry of final judgment, then the notice of appeal need not be filed until 30 days after the court enters an order disposing of that motion. Here, the district court entered a final judgment against the man on March 1. The man timely filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) within 28 days after entry of final judgment—on March 21. The judge denied this motion on April 15. The man then timely filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court within 30 days after the denial—on May 1. Therefore, the appellate court has jurisdiction to hear this appeal (Choice B). *The time to file the notice of appeal is extended to 60 days if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued in an official capacity. (Choice A) A notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the district court, not the appellate court. (Choice C) A party can file a renewed motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment only if the party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (as seen here). However, the fact that the man preserved the renewed motion for JMOL does not grant the appellate court jurisdiction. Educational objective: A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 30 days after the entry of final judgment unless a posttrial motion is filed within 28 days after final judgment is entered. In that case, the notice of appeal must be filed within 30 days after the court enters an order disposing of the motion. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 2107 (timing of appeal).\n\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4 (notice of appeal).","rule":"A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 30 days after the entry of final judgment unless a posttrial motion is filed within 28 days after final judgment is entered. In that case, the notice of appeal must be filed within 30 days after the court enters an order disposing of the motion.","irac":{"issue":"A man from State A sued a woman from State B in a federal district court for negligence to recover $100,000. In her answer denying liability, the woman requested a jury trial. The case proceeded to trial. Before the judge instructed the jury, the man moved for judgment as a matter of law. The judge denied this motion. On March 1, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the woman, and the judge entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict that same day. On March 21, the man filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law. On April 15, the judge entered an order denying that motion. On May 1, the man filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court. Does the appellate court have jurisdiction to hear the appeal?","rule":"A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 30 days after the entry of final judgment unless a posttrial motion is filed within 28 days after final judgment is entered. In that case, the notice of appeal must be filed within 30 days after the court enters an order disposing of the motion.","analysis":"A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 30 days after the district court enters final judgment to allow an appellate court to acquire jurisdiction over the case.* But if a party files a posttrial motion within 28 days after the entry of final judgment, then the notice of appeal need not be filed until 30 days after the court enters an order disposing of that motion. Here, the district court entered a final judgment against the man on March 1. The man timely filed a renewed motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL) within 28 days after entry of final judgment—on March 21. The judge denied this motion on April 15. The man then timely filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court within 30 days after the denial—on May 1. Therefore, the appellate court has jurisdiction to hear this appeal (Choice B). *The time to file the notice of appeal is extended to 60 days if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued in an official capacity. (Choice A) A notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the district court, not the appellate court. (Choice C) A party can file a renewed motion for JMOL within 28 days after the entry of final judgment only if the party moved for JMOL before the case was submitted to the jury (as seen here). However, the fact that the man preserved the renewed motion for JMOL does not grant the appellate court jurisdiction. Educational objective: A notice of appeal must be filed with the district court clerk within 30 days after the entry of final judgment unless a posttrial motion is filed within 28 days after final judgment is entered. In that case, the notice of appeal must be filed within 30 days after the court enters an order disposing of the motion. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 2107 (timing of appeal).\n\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4 (notice of appeal).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 2107 (timing of appeal).\n\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4 (notice of appeal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[484],"sourceAnswerPages":[485],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的男子在联邦地区法院以过失为由起诉一名来自 B 州的女子，请求赔偿 100,000 美元。女子在否认责任的答辩状中要求陪审团审判。案件进入审判。法官向陪审团作出指示前，男子申请依法判决。法官驳回动议。3 月 1 日，陪审团作出有利于女子的裁决，法官当天依裁决登记终局判决。3 月 21 日，男子提出续行依法判决动议。4 月 15 日，法官发布命令驳回该动议。5 月 1 日，男子向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知。上诉法院是否有管辖权审理该上诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为男子没有向上诉法院书记员提交上诉通知。","B":"没有，因为男子未在终局判决登记后 30 天内提交上诉通知。","C":"有，因为男子保留了续行依法判决动议。","D":"有，因为男子及时向适当的书记员提交上诉通知。"},"explanationZh":"上诉通知必须在地区法院登记终局判决后 30 天内向地区法院书记员提交，上诉法院才能取得案件管辖权。* 但是，如果一方在终局判决登记后 28 天内提出审判后动议，则可以等到法院登记处理该动议的命令后 30 天内，再提交上诉通知。\n\n本题地区法院于 3 月 1 日登记对男子不利的终局判决。男子在终局判决登记后 28 天内，即 3 月 21 日，及时提出续行依法判决（JMOL）动议。法官于 4 月 15 日驳回该动议。随后，男子于 5 月 1 日，即驳回后 30 天内，及时向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知。因此，上诉法院有管辖权审理该上诉（选项 B）。\n\n*如果一方是美国政府、联邦机构，或以职务身份被诉的联邦官员或雇员，上诉通知提交期限延长至 60 天。\n\n（选项 A）上诉通知必须向地区法院书记员而不是上诉法院书记员提交。\n\n（选项 C）只有在案件提交陪审团前申请依法判决（本题即如此），一方才能在终局判决登记后 28 天内提出续行依法判决动议。但是，男子保留续行依法判决动议这一事实本身，并不会赋予上诉法院管辖权。\n\n学习目标：上诉通知必须在终局判决登记后 30 天内向地区法院书记员提交，但终局判决登记后 28 天内提出审判后动议时除外。在这种情况下，上诉通知必须在法院登记处理该动议的命令后 30 天内提交。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 2107（上诉期限）。\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4（上诉通知）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-053"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-161","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":161,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A tire manufacturer sued its competitor in federal district court for infringing the patent on the manufacturer's best-selling tire design. The manufacturer sought compensatory damages as well as preliminary and permanent injunctions. After a pretrial hearing on the preliminary injunction, the court concluded that the manufacturer presented considerable evidence that it would suffer irreparable harm without the injunction and had a reasonable chance to succeed on the merits of its case. The court then issued the preliminary injunction. The competitor immediately filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court, arguing that the court improperly granted the preliminary injunction. What action will the appellate court likely take with respect to the competitor's appeal?","choices":{"A":"Affirm the issuance of the preliminary injunction because the district court did not abuse its discretion.","B":"Dismiss the appeal because the competitor did not file the notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate court.","C":"Dismiss the appeal because the district court has not yet entered a final judgment.","D":"Overturn the issuance of the preliminary injunction because the district court's decision was clearly erroneous."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the final-judgment rule, an appeal cannot be taken before a final judgment has been entered except in limited circumstances. One circumstance arises when a district court rules on a request for a preliminary injunction—as seen here (Choice C). Since rulings on preliminary injunctions are discretionary, they are reviewed under the highly deferential abuse-of-discretion standard. This means the appellate court will only reverse the district court's ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. A preliminary injunction may be granted if it is established that:\n• the movant is likely to succeed on the merits\n• the movant is likely to suffer irreparable harm in the absence of relief\n• the balance of equities is in the movant's favor and\n• the injunction is in the best interests of the public. Since the district court granted the preliminary injunction after the manufacturer presented considerable evidence supporting the above-listed elements, the court did not abuse its discretion in granting the preliminary injunction. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm the district court's decision. (Choice B) Most appeals are initiated by filing a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court (as seen here). Only certain interlocutory appeals (eg, appeals certified by a district court) require that a notice of appeal be filed with the clerk of the appellate court. (Choice D) The clearly-erroneous standard is used to review factual findings in a bench trial—not a district court's discretionary rulings in a pretrial hearing (as seen here). Under that standard, the district court's factual findings will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. Educational objective: An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. This is a highly deferential standard under which the appellate court will only reverse the district court's ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. References\n• Ashcroft v. Am. Civil Liberties Union, 542 U.S. 656, 664–66 (2004) (explaining that abuse-of-discretion review applies to preliminary injunctions and setting forth the traditional requirements to obtain a preliminary injunction).","rule":"An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. This is a highly deferential standard under which the appellate court will only reverse the district court's ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable.","irac":{"issue":"A tire manufacturer sued its competitor in federal district court for infringing the patent on the manufacturer's best-selling tire design. The manufacturer sought compensatory damages as well as preliminary and permanent injunctions. After a pretrial hearing on the preliminary injunction, the court concluded that the manufacturer presented considerable evidence that it would suffer irreparable harm without the injunction and had a reasonable chance to succeed on the merits of its case. The court then issued the preliminary injunction. The competitor immediately filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court, arguing that the court improperly granted the preliminary injunction. What action will the appellate court likely take with respect to the competitor's appeal?","rule":"An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. This is a highly deferential standard under which the appellate court will only reverse the district court's ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable.","analysis":"Under the final-judgment rule, an appeal cannot be taken before a final judgment has been entered except in limited circumstances. One circumstance arises when a district court rules on a request for a preliminary injunction—as seen here (Choice C). Since rulings on preliminary injunctions are discretionary, they are reviewed under the highly deferential abuse-of-discretion standard. This means the appellate court will only reverse the district court's ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. A preliminary injunction may be granted if it is established that:\n• the movant is likely to succeed on the merits\n• the movant is likely to suffer irreparable harm in the absence of relief\n• the balance of equities is in the movant's favor and\n• the injunction is in the best interests of the public. Since the district court granted the preliminary injunction after the manufacturer presented considerable evidence supporting the above-listed elements, the court did not abuse its discretion in granting the preliminary injunction. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm the district court's decision. (Choice B) Most appeals are initiated by filing a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court (as seen here). Only certain interlocutory appeals (eg, appeals certified by a district court) require that a notice of appeal be filed with the clerk of the appellate court. (Choice D) The clearly-erroneous standard is used to review factual findings in a bench trial—not a district court's discretionary rulings in a pretrial hearing (as seen here). Under that standard, the district court's factual findings will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. Educational objective: An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. This is a highly deferential standard under which the appellate court will only reverse the district court's ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. References\n• Ashcroft v. Am. Civil Liberties Union, 542 U.S. 656, 664–66 (2004) (explaining that abuse-of-discretion review applies to preliminary injunctions and setting forth the traditional requirements to obtain a preliminary injunction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Ashcroft v. Am. Civil Liberties Union, 542 U.S. 656, 664–66 (2004) (explaining that abuse-of-discretion review applies to preliminary injunctions and setting forth the traditional requirements to obtain a preliminary injunction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[487],"sourceAnswerPages":[488],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家轮胎制造商在联邦地区法院起诉其竞争对手，主张对方侵犯制造商最畅销轮胎设计的专利。制造商请求补偿性损害赔偿、初步禁令和永久禁令。法院就初步禁令举行审前听证后，认定制造商提出了大量证据，表明若无禁令将遭受无法弥补的损害，而且其案件在实体上有合理的胜诉可能。法院随后颁发初步禁令。竞争对手立即向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知，主张法院错误批准初步禁令。对于竞争对手的上诉，上诉法院可能采取什么措施？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持初步禁令，因为地区法院没有滥用裁量权。","B":"驳回上诉，因为竞争对手没有向上诉法院书记员提交上诉通知。","C":"驳回上诉，因为地区法院尚未登记终局判决。","D":"推翻初步禁令，因为地区法院的决定明显错误。"},"explanationZh":"根据终局判决规则，除有限情形外，终局判决登记前不得提起上诉。地区法院对初步禁令请求作出裁定时，就是其中一种例外情形，本题即如此（选项 C）。\n\n由于初步禁令裁定属于裁量性裁定，应依据高度尊重的滥用裁量权标准审查。这意味着，只有地区法院的裁定明显武断或不合理时，上诉法院才会推翻。符合以下条件时，可以颁发初步禁令：\n• 动议方很可能在实体上胜诉；\n• 若无救济，动议方很可能遭受无法弥补的损害；\n• 衡平利益权衡有利于动议方；并且\n• 禁令符合公共最佳利益。\n\n地区法院是在制造商提出大量证据支持上述要件后颁发初步禁令的，所以没有滥用裁量权。因此，上诉法院很可能维持地区法院的决定。\n\n（选项 B）大多数上诉通过向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知启动，本题即如此。只有某些中间上诉，例如经地区法院核准的上诉，才要求向上诉法院书记员提交上诉申请。\n\n（选项 D）明显错误标准用于审查法官审判中的事实认定，而不用于审查地区法院在审前听证中作出的裁量性裁定，本题即属于后者。根据明显错误标准，只有任何合理法官都不会作出该事实认定时，才会推翻地区法院的认定。\n\n学习目标：上诉法院审查地区法院的裁量性裁定，例如批准或驳回初步禁令时，适用滥用裁量权标准。这是一项高度尊重的标准，只有地区法院的裁定明显武断或不合理时，上诉法院才会推翻。\n\n参考资料：\n• Ashcroft v. Am. Civil Liberties Union, 542 U.S. 656, 664–66 (2004)（说明初步禁令适用滥用裁量权审查，并列明取得初步禁令的传统要件）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-054"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-162","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":162,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"Two days before his home was to be sold at a foreclosure sale, a homeowner obtained a temporary restraining order (TRO) in federal court that prevented his lender from proceeding with the sale for 14 days or until a preliminary injunction hearing could take place, whichever was sooner. When a preliminary injunction hearing could not be scheduled within the original 14-day period, the court extended the TRO for another 30 days. The lender appealed the court's order extending the TRO. The homeowner has moved to dismiss the appeal. Is the appellate court likely to dismiss the appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because a TRO is immediately appealable.","B":"No, because the 30-day extension makes the TRO equivalent to a preliminary injunction and therefore appealable.","C":"Yes, because a TRO is not appealable under the interlocutory appeals statute.","D":"Yes, because there is no final judgment from which an appeal may be taken."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment The final-judgment rule bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the federal district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a judicial decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. But the interlocutory appeals statute (28 U.S.C. § 1292) provides exceptions to this rule that allow an immediate appeal in limited circumstances. One of those circumstances arises when a district court grants or denies a preliminary injunction—ie, a court order entered before or during trial that commands or prohibits a specified action while the case is before the court. In contrast, a temporary restraining order (TRO)—ie, a court order that commands or prohibits specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary injunction hearing can take place (whichever occurs first)—is not immediately appealable under this statute (Choice A). But when a TRO is extended beyond 14 days (as seen here), it becomes equivalent to a preliminary injunction. As a result, the extended TRO is immediately appealable even though there is no final judgment (Choices C & D). Therefore, the appellate court is unlikely to dismiss the lender's appeal of the order extending the TRO.\n\nEducational objective: The interlocutory appeals statute allows certain orders to be appealed before the entry of a final judgment—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. A temporary restraining order extended beyond 14 days is equivalent to a preliminary injunction and is immediately appealable under this statute. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (exceptions to final judgment rule).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65 (preliminary injunctions and temporary restraining orders).\n\n• Sampson v. Murray, 415 U.S. 61, 86–88 (1974) (holding that a temporary restraining order extended beyond its time limit is the equivalent of a preliminary injunction).","rule":"The interlocutory appeals statute allows certain orders to be appealed before the entry of a final judgment—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. A temporary restraining order extended beyond 14 days is equivalent to a preliminary injunction and is immediately appealable under this statute.","irac":{"issue":"Two days before his home was to be sold at a foreclosure sale, a homeowner obtained a temporary restraining order (TRO) in federal court that prevented his lender from proceeding with the sale for 14 days or until a preliminary injunction hearing could take place, whichever was sooner. When a preliminary injunction hearing could not be scheduled within the original 14-day period, the court extended the TRO for another 30 days. The lender appealed the court's order extending the TRO. The homeowner has moved to dismiss the appeal. Is the appellate court likely to dismiss the appeal?","rule":"The interlocutory appeals statute allows certain orders to be appealed before the entry of a final judgment—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. A temporary restraining order extended beyond 14 days is equivalent to a preliminary injunction and is immediately appealable under this statute.","analysis":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment The final-judgment rule bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the federal district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a judicial decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. But the interlocutory appeals statute (28 U.S.C. § 1292) provides exceptions to this rule that allow an immediate appeal in limited circumstances. One of those circumstances arises when a district court grants or denies a preliminary injunction—ie, a court order entered before or during trial that commands or prohibits a specified action while the case is before the court. In contrast, a temporary restraining order (TRO)—ie, a court order that commands or prohibits specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary injunction hearing can take place (whichever occurs first)—is not immediately appealable under this statute (Choice A). But when a TRO is extended beyond 14 days (as seen here), it becomes equivalent to a preliminary injunction. As a result, the extended TRO is immediately appealable even though there is no final judgment (Choices C & D). Therefore, the appellate court is unlikely to dismiss the lender's appeal of the order extending the TRO.\n\nEducational objective: The interlocutory appeals statute allows certain orders to be appealed before the entry of a final judgment—eg, the grant or denial of a preliminary injunction. A temporary restraining order extended beyond 14 days is equivalent to a preliminary injunction and is immediately appealable under this statute. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (exceptions to final judgment rule).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65 (preliminary injunctions and temporary restraining orders).\n\n• Sampson v. Murray, 415 U.S. 61, 86–88 (1974) (holding that a temporary restraining order extended beyond its time limit is the equivalent of a preliminary injunction).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1292 (exceptions to final judgment rule).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65 (preliminary injunctions and temporary restraining orders).\n\n• Sampson v. Murray, 415 U.S. 61, 86–88 (1974) (holding that a temporary restraining order extended beyond its time limit is the equivalent of a preliminary injunction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[490],"sourceAnswerPages":[491,492],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在其住宅预定被止赎拍卖的两天前，一名房主从联邦法院取得临时限制令（TRO），禁止贷款人继续拍卖，期限为 14 天，或持续至初步禁令听证能够举行时，以较早者为准。初步禁令听证无法在原 14 天期限内安排，法院遂将 TRO 再延长 30 天。贷款人对法院延长 TRO 的命令提起上诉。房主申请驳回上诉。上诉法院可能驳回该上诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为 TRO 可以立即上诉。","B":"不会，因为延长 30 天使 TRO 等同于初步禁令，因而可以上诉。","C":"会，因为根据中间上诉制定法，TRO 不得上诉。","D":"会，因为不存在可供上诉的终局判决。"},"explanationZh":"终局判决规则：\n\n规则｜只能在终局判决后上诉\n• 涉及多项请求或多名当事人时，如果法院明确认定没有正当理由拖延，可以就少于全部请求或当事人登记终局判决\n\n例外｜涉及下列事项的中间上诉*：\n• 禁令（批准或驳回）\n• 地区法院核准\n• 集体诉讼核准\n• 指定接管人\n• 海事案件\n• 附带命令原则\n• 破产案件（某些命令）\n• 强制令（申请签发）\n• 专利侵权命令（只剩账目核算）\n\n记忆口诀：在特定情形下，上诉可以提前提出。\n\n*中间上诉是对终局判决登记前作出的审判法院裁定提起上诉。\n\n终局判决规则禁止联邦上诉法院在联邦地区法院登记终局判决前审理上诉。终局判决是完全解决争议实体，使法院除执行判决外别无事项的司法决定。但是，中间上诉制定法（28 U.S.C. § 1292）对该规则规定了一些例外，允许在有限情形下立即上诉。\n\n其中一种情形是地区法院批准或驳回初步禁令。初步禁令是在审判前或审判期间登记的法院命令，在案件审理期间命令实施或禁止某项特定行为。相比之下，临时限制令（TRO）是命令实施或禁止特定行为 14 天，或持续至初步禁令听证可以举行时（以较早者为准）的法院命令；依据该制定法，TRO 不能立即上诉（选项 A）。\n\n但是，TRO 延长超过 14 天时（本题即如此），就等同于初步禁令。因此，即使没有终局判决，延长后的 TRO 也可以立即上诉（选项 C、D）。所以，上诉法院不太可能驳回贷款人对延长 TRO 命令所提的上诉。\n\n学习目标：中间上诉制定法允许某些命令在终局判决登记前上诉，例如批准或驳回初步禁令。临时限制令延长超过 14 天时等同于初步禁令，可以根据该制定法立即上诉。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292（终局判决规则的例外）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65（初步禁令和临时限制令）。\n• Sampson v. Murray, 415 U.S. 61, 86–88 (1974)（判定临时限制令延长超过其期限时等同于初步禁令）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-163","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":163,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A woman from State A sued a pharmaceutical company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B in federal district court for $1 million in damages. The woman alleged that the company engaged in deceptive marketing by failing to disclose all the health risks associated with one of its drugs. She claimed that this contributed to her becoming addicted to the drug, which led to severe medical complications. The case was tried to the court. The woman presented evidence to support her claim and immediately after, the company moved for a judgment on partial findings. The judge announced her findings of fact and conclusions of law from the bench and explained that the woman had failed to present legally sufficient evidence to establish the causation element of her claim. Specifically, the judge did not believe that the woman's witnesses concerning that element were credible and discounted their testimony. The judge then granted the company's motion and entered a judgment in favor of the company. Were the judge's actions proper?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judge acted improperly when she discounted the testimony of the witnesses.","B":"No, because the judge failed to state her findings and conclusions in writing.","C":"Yes, because a judgment on partial findings may be entered at any time during trial.","D":"Yes, because the woman had an opportunity to be fully heard on her claim."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When a case is tried to the court (ie, a bench trial), a party can file a motion for judgment on partial findings in lieu of a motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL). This motion requests that the court enter a partial finding against the nonmovant after the nonmovant has been fully heard on the issue. If that finding resolves a claim or defense, the court may enter a judgment on partial findings. The court must provide factual findings and legal conclusions (orally or in writing) on the record in support of that judgment (Choice B). Here, the judge announced her findings and conclusions on the company's motion for judgment on partial findings from the bench (ie, on the record). The judge found that the woman's witnesses were not credible, so the woman did not have legally sufficient evidence to establish the causation element of her claim. And since the woman had been fully heard on her claim, the court acted properly in entering the judgment on partial findings.* *The woman can still challenge the judge's decision on appeal. The appellate court will review the judge's factual findings for clear error (high deference) and the judge's conclusions of law de novo (no deference). (Choice A) As the fact finder in a bench trial, a judge may consider the credibility of witnesses and the weight of the evidence to determine if they are sufficient to render a decision for the movant before entering a judgment on partial findings.** Therefore, the judge acted properly when she discounted the testimony of the woman's witnesses after assessing their credibility. **In contrast, the court may not evaluate the credibility of witnesses or the weight of the evidence when considering an initial or renewed motion for JMOL. (Choice C) A judgment on partial findings can be entered only after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to be fully heard. So had the company moved for a judgment on partial findings before the woman presented her case, the court could not have considered the motion. Educational objective: In a bench trial, a judge may enter a partial finding against the nonmovant after the nonmovant has been fully heard on the issue. If that finding resolves a party's claim or defense, the court may then enter a judgment on partial findings. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(c) (judgment on partial findings).","rule":"In a bench trial, a judge may enter a partial finding against the nonmovant after the nonmovant has been fully heard on the issue. If that finding resolves a party's claim or defense, the court may then enter a judgment on partial findings.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A sued a pharmaceutical company incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B in federal district court for $1 million in damages. The woman alleged that the company engaged in deceptive marketing by failing to disclose all the health risks associated with one of its drugs. She claimed that this contributed to her becoming addicted to the drug, which led to severe medical complications. The case was tried to the court. The woman presented evidence to support her claim and immediately after, the company moved for a judgment on partial findings. The judge announced her findings of fact and conclusions of law from the bench and explained that the woman had failed to present legally sufficient evidence to establish the causation element of her claim. Specifically, the judge did not believe that the woman's witnesses concerning that element were credible and discounted their testimony. The judge then granted the company's motion and entered a judgment in favor of the company. Were the judge's actions proper?","rule":"In a bench trial, a judge may enter a partial finding against the nonmovant after the nonmovant has been fully heard on the issue. If that finding resolves a party's claim or defense, the court may then enter a judgment on partial findings.","analysis":"When a case is tried to the court (ie, a bench trial), a party can file a motion for judgment on partial findings in lieu of a motion for judgment as a matter of law (JMOL). This motion requests that the court enter a partial finding against the nonmovant after the nonmovant has been fully heard on the issue. If that finding resolves a claim or defense, the court may enter a judgment on partial findings. The court must provide factual findings and legal conclusions (orally or in writing) on the record in support of that judgment (Choice B). Here, the judge announced her findings and conclusions on the company's motion for judgment on partial findings from the bench (ie, on the record). The judge found that the woman's witnesses were not credible, so the woman did not have legally sufficient evidence to establish the causation element of her claim. And since the woman had been fully heard on her claim, the court acted properly in entering the judgment on partial findings.* *The woman can still challenge the judge's decision on appeal. The appellate court will review the judge's factual findings for clear error (high deference) and the judge's conclusions of law de novo (no deference). (Choice A) As the fact finder in a bench trial, a judge may consider the credibility of witnesses and the weight of the evidence to determine if they are sufficient to render a decision for the movant before entering a judgment on partial findings.** Therefore, the judge acted properly when she discounted the testimony of the woman's witnesses after assessing their credibility. **In contrast, the court may not evaluate the credibility of witnesses or the weight of the evidence when considering an initial or renewed motion for JMOL. (Choice C) A judgment on partial findings can be entered only after the nonmovant has had an opportunity to be fully heard. So had the company moved for a judgment on partial findings before the woman presented her case, the court could not have considered the motion. Educational objective: In a bench trial, a judge may enter a partial finding against the nonmovant after the nonmovant has been fully heard on the issue. If that finding resolves a party's claim or defense, the court may then enter a judgment on partial findings. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(c) (judgment on partial findings).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(c) (judgment on partial findings).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[493],"sourceAnswerPages":[494],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的女子在联邦地区法院起诉一家在 B 州注册成立、主要营业地也位于 B 州的制药公司，请求 100 万美元损害赔偿。女子主张，该公司未披露其一种药物的全部健康风险，构成欺骗性营销。她称，这促使她对该药物成瘾，并导致严重医疗并发症。案件由法官审判。女子提出支持其请求的证据后，公司立即申请根据部分事实认定作出判决。法官当庭宣布事实认定和法律结论，说明女子未能提出法律上足以证明其请求中因果关系要件的证据。具体而言，法官认为女子就该要件传唤的证人不可信，因而降低其证言权重。随后，法官批准公司动议并登记有利于公司的判决。法官的做法是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为法官降低证人证言权重的做法不当。","B":"不适当，因为法官没有以书面形式陈述事实认定和法律结论。","C":"适当，因为可以在审判期间的任何时候根据部分事实认定作出判决。","D":"适当，因为女子已有机会就其请求得到充分听取。"},"explanationZh":"案件由法院审判，即法官审判时，一方可以提出根据部分事实认定作出判决的动议，以代替依法判决（JMOL）动议。该动议请求法院在非动议方已就相关问题得到充分听取后，作出对其不利的部分事实认定。如果该认定解决一项请求或抗辩，法院可以根据部分事实认定登记判决。法院必须在案卷记录中以口头或书面形式提供支持该判决的事实认定和法律结论（选项 B）。\n\n本题法官就公司根据部分事实认定作出判决的动议，当庭即在案卷记录中宣布事实认定和法律结论。法官认定女子的证人不可信，所以女子没有法律上足以证明其请求中因果关系要件的证据。女子已经就其请求得到充分听取，因此，法院根据部分事实认定登记判决的做法适当。*\n\n*女子仍可在上诉中质疑法官的决定。上诉法院将以明显错误标准审查法官的事实认定（高度尊重），并重新审查法官的法律结论（不予尊重）。\n\n（选项 A）在法官审判中，法官作为事实裁判者，可以考虑证人的可信度和证据权重，以确定其是否足以在根据部分事实认定登记判决前为动议方作出裁判。因此，法官评估女子证人的可信度后降低其证言权重，做法适当。**\n\n**相比之下，法院审理初次或续行依法判决动议时，不得评估证人的可信度或证据权重。\n\n（选项 C）只有在非动议方已经有机会得到充分听取后，才能根据部分事实认定作出判决。因此，如果公司在女子陈述其案件前提出该动议，法院就不能审理。\n\n学习目标：在法官审判中，非动议方就相关问题得到充分听取后，法官可以作出对其不利的部分事实认定。如果该认定解决一方的请求或抗辩，法院随后可以根据部分事实认定登记判决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(c)（根据部分事实认定作出判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-055"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-164","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":164,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A man brought a federal diversity action against his former business partner for breach of contract seeking $100,000 in damages. The business partner moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. After the court denied the motion, the business partner filed and served an answer in which she denied the man's claim. Ten days later, the man filed and served a jury demand, which the business partner moved to strike. Is the court likely to grant the motion to strike?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man properly demanded a jury trial.","B":"No, because the right to a jury trial extends to civil claims.","C":"Yes, because the jury demand was untimely filed.","D":"Yes, because the man failed to assert the jury demand in a pleading."},"answer":"A","explanation":"All civil litigants (plaintiffs and defendants) have the constitutional right to a jury trial for any legal claim where the amount in controversy exceeds $20 (as seen here). However, a party waives this right if a proper demand is not made. Under FRCP 38, a party may properly demand a jury trial by:\n• serving the other parties with a written jury demand within 14 days after the last pleading (usually the defendant's answer) is served and\n\n• filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.*\n\nHere, the man filed and served his written jury demand 10 days after being served with the business partner's answer. Since the demand was timely and properly made, the court is unlikely to strike it (Choice C). *A jury trial demand is treated as a request that a jury decide all issues unless the party specifies otherwise. If so specified, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court. (Choice B) Parties have a jury trial right for legal claims—ie, claims seeking a monetary remedy to compensate for a loss (eg, damages). But this right does not extend to equitable claims—ie, claims seeking a nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction). (Choice D) A jury trial demand may, but need not, be included in a pleading. The demand can also be made in a separate, stand-alone document (as seen here). Educational objective: Any party may secure the right to a jury trial on any legal claim by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after serving it. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b) (jury trial demand).","rule":"Any party may secure the right to a jury trial on any legal claim by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after serving it. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A man brought a federal diversity action against his former business partner for breach of contract seeking $100,000 in damages. The business partner moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. After the court denied the motion, the business partner filed and served an answer in which she denied the man's claim. Ten days later, the man filed and served a jury demand, which the business partner moved to strike. Is the court likely to grant the motion to strike?","rule":"Any party may secure the right to a jury trial on any legal claim by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after serving it. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"All civil litigants (plaintiffs and defendants) have the constitutional right to a jury trial for any legal claim where the amount in controversy exceeds $20 (as seen here). However, a party waives this right if a proper demand is not made. Under FRCP 38, a party may properly demand a jury trial by:\n• serving the other parties with a written jury demand within 14 days after the last pleading (usually the defendant's answer) is served and\n\n• filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.*\n\nHere, the man filed and served his written jury demand 10 days after being served with the business partner's answer. Since the demand was timely and properly made, the court is unlikely to strike it (Choice C). *A jury trial demand is treated as a request that a jury decide all issues unless the party specifies otherwise. If so specified, another party may serve a jury trial demand for any other issue within 14 days after service of the original demand or a shorter time ordered by the court. (Choice B) Parties have a jury trial right for legal claims—ie, claims seeking a monetary remedy to compensate for a loss (eg, damages). But this right does not extend to equitable claims—ie, claims seeking a nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction). (Choice D) A jury trial demand may, but need not, be included in a pleading. The demand can also be made in a separate, stand-alone document (as seen here). Educational objective: Any party may secure the right to a jury trial on any legal claim by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading is served and (2) filing the demand with the court within a reasonable time after serving it. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b) (jury trial demand).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b) (jury trial demand).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[496],"sourceAnswerPages":[497],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子对其前商业合伙人提起联邦多元州籍违约诉讼，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。商业合伙人以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。法院驳回动议后，商业合伙人提交并送达答辩状，否认男子的请求。十天后，男子提交并送达陪审团请求，商业合伙人申请删除该请求。法院可能批准删除动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为男子适当要求了陪审团审判。","B":"不会，因为陪审团审判权适用于民事请求。","C":"会，因为陪审团请求提交逾期。","D":"会，因为男子没有在诉状中提出陪审团请求。"},"explanationZh":"所有民事诉讼当事人（原告和被告）对于争议金额超过 20 美元的任何法律请求，均享有宪法规定的陪审团审判权，本题即如此。但是，一方没有适当提出请求，就会放弃该权利。根据 FRCP 38，一方可以通过以下方式适当要求陪审团审判：\n• 在最后一份诉状（通常为被告答辩状）送达后 14 天内，向其他各方送达书面陪审团请求；并且\n• 在请求送达后的合理时间内向法院提交。*\n\n本题男子在商业合伙人的答辩状送达后 10 天，提交并送达书面陪审团请求。该请求及时且适当，法院不太可能将其删除（选项 C）。\n\n*陪审团审判请求视为请求陪审团裁定全部问题，除非该方另有明确说明。若明确限于特定问题，另一方可以在原请求送达后 14 天内，或法院规定的更短期限内，就其他任何问题送达陪审团审判请求。\n\n（选项 B）双方对法律请求享有陪审团审判权，即请求以金钱救济补偿损失的请求，例如损害赔偿。但该权利不适用于衡平法请求，即请求非金钱救济的请求，例如禁令。\n\n（选项 D）陪审团审判请求可以包含于诉状中，但并非必须如此。请求也可以通过单独文件提出，本题即如此。\n\n学习目标：任何一方均可通过以下方式保障其对任何法律请求的陪审团审判权：(1) 最迟在最后一份诉状送达后 14 天内，向其他各方送达书面陪审团请求；并且 (2) 在送达后合理时间内向法院提交该请求。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b)（陪审团审判请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-056"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-165","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":165,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A man was apprehended for illegally crossing the U.S. border. The man was held at a detention facility for over a month before the government realized that he was a U.S. citizen. After he was released, the man filed a complaint in federal court against the government under a federal statute that waives the federal government's immunity in these types of suits. The man sought $100,000 in damages and demanded a jury trial. At the close of evidence at trial, the government and the man both submitted proposed jury instructions. The court decided to hear the parties' closing arguments before ruling on which instructions to use. Both parties made their closing arguments. The court then informed the parties outside the jury's presence of its proposed instructions, which differed significantly from the instructions proposed by the parties. Both parties objected on the record. Did the court err in providing its proposed jury instructions in this manner?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court has complete authority to decide when and how to provide jury instructions.","B":"No, because the court need not to inform the parties of its proposed instructions until after closing arguments.","C":"Yes, because the court failed to inform the parties of its proposed instructions and give them an opportunity to object before closing arguments.","D":"Yes, because the court failed to instruct the jury before closing arguments."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Jury instructions\n\n(FRCP 51)\n\nRequests Party:\n\n• must file request by close of evidence unless earlier, reasonable deadline set by court\n• may file request after close of evidence when: – request relates to issue that party could not have reasonably anticipated by deadline or – court permits late submission related to any issue Instructions Court:\n\n• must inform parties of proposed instructions before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• must allow parties to object on record & outside jury's presence before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• may consider objection made after court instructs jury if instruction constituted plain error\n• may instruct jury at any time before discharge Objections Party:\n\n• must distinctly state objection & its grounds on record\n• must object at court-appointed time or promptly after learning of proposed instruction FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nIn a jury trial, the court must provide jury instructions that advise jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. The court has the ultimate authority to decide when and how to provide jury instructions so long as it complies with the requirements of Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 51 (Choice A). Under FRCP 51, the court:\n• may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged—ie, released from its duties in the case\n\n• must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before it instructs the jury and the parties give their closing arguments and\n\n• must give the parties an opportunity to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered to the jury.\n\nHere, the court properly informed the parties of its proposed instructions and gave them an opportunity to object before it instructed the jury. But the court erred in failing to do so before closing arguments (Choice B). As a result, the parties could not tailor their closing arguments to the applicable law provided in the court's jury instructions. (Choice D) The court did not err in failing to instruct the jury before the parties gave their closing arguments because a court may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged. Instead, the court erred in failing to inform the parties of its proposed instructions and give them an opportunity to object before closing arguments. Educational objective: In a jury trial, a court (1) may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged, (2) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, and (3) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b) (court requirements for providing jury instructions).","rule":"In a jury trial, a court (1) may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged, (2) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, and (3) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered.","irac":{"issue":"A man was apprehended for illegally crossing the U.S. border. The man was held at a detention facility for over a month before the government realized that he was a U.S. citizen. After he was released, the man filed a complaint in federal court against the government under a federal statute that waives the federal government's immunity in these types of suits. The man sought $100,000 in damages and demanded a jury trial. At the close of evidence at trial, the government and the man both submitted proposed jury instructions. The court decided to hear the parties' closing arguments before ruling on which instructions to use. Both parties made their closing arguments. The court then informed the parties outside the jury's presence of its proposed instructions, which differed significantly from the instructions proposed by the parties. Both parties objected on the record. Did the court err in providing its proposed jury instructions in this manner?","rule":"In a jury trial, a court (1) may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged, (2) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, and (3) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered.","analysis":"Jury instructions\n\n(FRCP 51)\n\nRequests Party:\n\n• must file request by close of evidence unless earlier, reasonable deadline set by court\n• may file request after close of evidence when: – request relates to issue that party could not have reasonably anticipated by deadline or – court permits late submission related to any issue Instructions Court:\n\n• must inform parties of proposed instructions before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• must allow parties to object on record & outside jury's presence before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• may consider objection made after court instructs jury if instruction constituted plain error\n• may instruct jury at any time before discharge Objections Party:\n\n• must distinctly state objection & its grounds on record\n• must object at court-appointed time or promptly after learning of proposed instruction FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nIn a jury trial, the court must provide jury instructions that advise jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. The court has the ultimate authority to decide when and how to provide jury instructions so long as it complies with the requirements of Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 51 (Choice A). Under FRCP 51, the court:\n• may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged—ie, released from its duties in the case\n\n• must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before it instructs the jury and the parties give their closing arguments and\n\n• must give the parties an opportunity to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered to the jury.\n\nHere, the court properly informed the parties of its proposed instructions and gave them an opportunity to object before it instructed the jury. But the court erred in failing to do so before closing arguments (Choice B). As a result, the parties could not tailor their closing arguments to the applicable law provided in the court's jury instructions. (Choice D) The court did not err in failing to instruct the jury before the parties gave their closing arguments because a court may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged. Instead, the court erred in failing to inform the parties of its proposed instructions and give them an opportunity to object before closing arguments. Educational objective: In a jury trial, a court (1) may instruct the jury at any time before the jury is discharged, (2) must inform the parties of its proposed instructions before instructing the jury and closing arguments, and (3) must allow the parties to object on the record and outside the presence of the jury before the instructions and closing arguments are delivered. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b) (court requirements for providing jury instructions).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b) (court requirements for providing jury instructions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[499],"sourceAnswerPages":[500,501],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子因非法越过美国边境被拘捕。政府将男子关押在拘留设施一个多月后，才发现他是美国公民。获释后，男子依据一项放弃联邦政府对此类诉讼豁免的联邦制定法，在联邦法院对政府提起诉讼。男子请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿，并要求陪审团审判。审判举证结束时，政府和男子均提交了拟议陪审团指示。法院决定先听取双方结案陈词，再裁定采用哪些指示。双方均作了结案陈词。随后，法院在陪审团不在场时将拟议指示告知双方；这些指示与双方所提指示存在重大差异。双方均在案卷记录中提出异议。法院以这种方式提供拟议陪审团指示是否有误？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为法院对于何时及如何提供陪审团指示拥有完全权力。","B":"没有，因为法院在结案陈词后才将拟议指示告知双方也无妨。","C":"有，因为法院未在结案陈词前将拟议指示告知双方并给予他们提出异议的机会。","D":"有，因为法院未在结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示。"},"explanationZh":"陪审团指示（FRCP 51）：\n\n请求——当事人：\n• 必须在举证结束时前提交请求，除非法院规定了更早且合理的期限；\n• 在下列情况下，可于举证结束后提交请求：\n  – 请求涉及该方在期限届满前不可能合理预见的问题；或\n  – 法院允许就任何问题逾期提交。\n\n指示——法院：\n• 必须在向陪审团作出指示及结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方；\n• 必须在向陪审团作出指示及结案陈词前，允许双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议；\n• 如果指示构成明显错误，可以考虑法院作出指示后才提出的异议；\n• 可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候向其作出指示。\n\n异议——当事人：\n• 必须在案卷记录中明确陈述异议及其理由；\n• 必须在法院指定的时间提出异议，或得知拟议指示后立即提出。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n在陪审团审判中，法院必须提供陪审团指示，告知陪审员评议程序和适用法律。只要遵守《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）51 的要求，法院对何时及如何提供陪审团指示拥有最终决定权（选项 A）。根据 FRCP 51，法院：\n• 可以在陪审团解散，即解除其案件职责前的任何时候向其作出指示；\n• 必须在向陪审团作出指示以及双方作结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方；并且\n• 必须在向陪审团作出指示以及双方作结案陈词前，给予双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议的机会。\n\n本题法院在向陪审团作出指示前，将拟议指示告知双方并给予他们提出异议的机会，这部分做法适当。但是，法院没有在结案陈词前这样做，因而犯有错误（选项 B）。结果是，双方无法依照法院陪审团指示中提供的适用法律调整其结案陈词。\n\n（选项 D）法院未在双方作结案陈词前向陪审团作出指示，并不构成错误，因为法院可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候向其作出指示。真正的错误在于，法院没有在结案陈词前将拟议指示告知双方并给予他们提出异议的机会。\n\n学习目标：在陪审团审判中，法院：(1) 可以在陪审团解散前的任何时候向其作出指示；(2) 必须在向陪审团作出指示和结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方；并且 (3) 必须在向陪审团作出指示和结案陈词前，允许双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(b)（法院提供陪审团指示的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-166","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":166,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A woman brought a diversity action against her former employer in a federal district court in State A. In her complaint, the woman alleged that she contracted a lung disease at work due to her employer's negligence. The employer moved to dismiss the woman's action for failure to state a claim. The court granted the motion because State A does not recognize claims for occupational diseases. The woman appealed. While the woman's appeal was pending, the highest state court in State A issued a ruling that, for the first time, recognized claims for occupational diseases. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the dismissal?","choices":{"A":"No, because federal law permits a dismissal for failure to state a claim.","B":"No, because State A did not recognize claims for occupational diseases at the time the action was dismissed.","C":"Yes, because the district court committed clear error when it dismissed the action.","D":"Yes, because the recent ruling by State A's highest court controls."},"answer":"D","explanation":"All federal courts sitting in diversity must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. Issues concerning the elements of a claim or defense are clearly substantive and therefore determined by state law. Since the elements of a claim or defense are a pure legal issue, the appellate court will review the district court's interpretation of state law de novo. Under this standard of review, the appellate court gives no deference to the district court's interpretation and decides the issue anew. However, the appellate court must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending. Here, the district court properly applied State A law when it dismissed the woman's action for failure to state a claim because State A did not recognize her claim at that time. However, while the appeal was pending, the highest state court in State A issued a ruling that recognized the woman's claim. Since the appellate court must follow that ruling, it will likely overturn the dismissal of the woman's action (Choice B). (Choice A) Federal law provides procedural devices to dismiss an action for failure to state a claim. However, a federal court must apply state substantive law to determine if a claim was adequately stated. As a result, State A law governs whether the woman adequately stated a claim for relief. (Choice C) A district court's factual findings in a bench trial are reviewed for clear error. Under this highly deferential standard of review, a district court's findings of facts will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. But here, this pure legal issue will be reviewed de novo. Educational objective: Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, the elements of a claim or defense). An appellate court reviewing a district court's interpretation of state law must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending. References\n• Vandenbark v. Owens-Ill. Glass Co., 311 U.S. 538, 543 (1941) (holding that federal appellate courts must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending).","rule":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, the elements of a claim or defense). An appellate court reviewing a district court's interpretation of state law must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending.","irac":{"issue":"A woman brought a diversity action against her former employer in a federal district court in State A. In her complaint, the woman alleged that she contracted a lung disease at work due to her employer's negligence. The employer moved to dismiss the woman's action for failure to state a claim. The court granted the motion because State A does not recognize claims for occupational diseases. The woman appealed. While the woman's appeal was pending, the highest state court in State A issued a ruling that, for the first time, recognized claims for occupational diseases. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the dismissal?","rule":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, the elements of a claim or defense). An appellate court reviewing a district court's interpretation of state law must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending.","analysis":"All federal courts sitting in diversity must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. Issues concerning the elements of a claim or defense are clearly substantive and therefore determined by state law. Since the elements of a claim or defense are a pure legal issue, the appellate court will review the district court's interpretation of state law de novo. Under this standard of review, the appellate court gives no deference to the district court's interpretation and decides the issue anew. However, the appellate court must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending. Here, the district court properly applied State A law when it dismissed the woman's action for failure to state a claim because State A did not recognize her claim at that time. However, while the appeal was pending, the highest state court in State A issued a ruling that recognized the woman's claim. Since the appellate court must follow that ruling, it will likely overturn the dismissal of the woman's action (Choice B). (Choice A) Federal law provides procedural devices to dismiss an action for failure to state a claim. However, a federal court must apply state substantive law to determine if a claim was adequately stated. As a result, State A law governs whether the woman adequately stated a claim for relief. (Choice C) A district court's factual findings in a bench trial are reviewed for clear error. Under this highly deferential standard of review, a district court's findings of facts will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. But here, this pure legal issue will be reviewed de novo. Educational objective: Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues (eg, the elements of a claim or defense). An appellate court reviewing a district court's interpretation of state law must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending. References\n• Vandenbark v. Owens-Ill. Glass Co., 311 U.S. 538, 543 (1941) (holding that federal appellate courts must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Vandenbark v. Owens-Ill. Glass Co., 311 U.S. 538, 543 (1941) (holding that federal appellate courts must follow any rulings issued by the state's highest court while the appeal was pending).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[502],"sourceAnswerPages":[503],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在 A 州联邦地区法院对前雇主提起多元州籍诉讼。女子在起诉状中主张，由于雇主的过失，她在工作中患上肺病。雇主以未提出可获救济的请求为由，申请驳回女子诉讼。法院批准动议，因为 A 州不承认职业病请求。女子提起上诉。上诉审理期间，A 州最高法院作出一项裁决，首次承认职业病请求。上诉法院可能推翻驳回裁定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦法允许以未提出可获救济的请求为由驳回诉讼。","B":"不会，因为诉讼被驳回时，A 州不承认职业病请求。","C":"会，因为地区法院驳回诉讼时犯有明显错误。","D":"会，因为 A 州最高法院最近作出的裁决具有控制力。"},"explanationZh":"所有行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院都必须对程序性问题适用联邦法，对实体性问题适用州法。请求或抗辩构成要件方面的问题显然属于实体性问题，因此由州法决定。\n\n由于请求或抗辩的构成要件属于纯粹法律问题，上诉法院将重新审查地区法院对州法的解释。根据该审查标准，上诉法院不尊重地区法院的解释，而是重新决定该问题。但是，上诉法院必须遵循上诉审理期间该州最高法院作出的任何裁决。\n\n本题地区法院驳回女子诉讼时适当适用了 A 州法律，因为当时 A 州不承认她的请求。然而，上诉审理期间，A 州最高法院作出裁决，承认女子的请求。由于上诉法院必须遵循该裁决，所以很可能推翻对女子诉讼的驳回（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A）联邦法提供了以未提出可获救济的请求为由驳回诉讼的程序性机制。但是，联邦法院必须适用州实体法，确定请求是否充分提出。因此，女子是否充分提出可获救济的请求，由 A 州法律支配。\n\n（选项 C）法官审判中地区法院的事实认定适用明显错误标准审查。根据这项高度尊重的审查标准，只有任何合理法官都不会作出该事实认定时，才会推翻。但是，本题这一纯粹法律问题将受重新审查。\n\n学习目标：行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对程序性问题适用联邦法，对实体性问题（例如请求或抗辩的构成要件）适用州法。上诉法院审查地区法院对州法的解释时，必须遵循上诉审理期间该州最高法院作出的任何裁决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Vandenbark v. Owens-Ill. Glass Co., 311 U.S. 538, 543 (1941)（判定联邦上诉法院必须遵循上诉审理期间该州最高法院作出的任何裁决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-057"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-167","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":167,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A truck driver from State A and a bus driver from State B were involved in a collision in State B that injured the truck driver. The truck driver filed a federal diversity action in State B based on negligence, seeking $100,000 in damages from the bus driver. What law of negligence should the court apply?","choices":{"A":"The court should apply the federal common law of negligence.","B":"The court should apply the negligence law of State A, the truck driver's state of citizenship.","C":"The court should consider the negligence law of both State A and State B and apply the law that the court believes most appropriately governs negligence in this action.","D":"The court should determine which state's negligence law a state court in State B would apply and apply that law in this action."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction, the dispute is between citizens of different states. As a result, the court must determine which state's substantive law applies to the case. If no federal rule, federal statute, or constitutional provision controls the substantive issue, then the court must follow the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits. Here, the federal court in State B has diversity jurisdiction over this negligence case. There is no federal law on negligence. Therefore, the court must use the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits (State B) to determine which state's (State A's or State B's) negligence law a court in State B should apply. (Choice A) There is no federal common law of negligence, so federal courts must adhere to state law on this substantive matter. (Choice B) The court cannot simply select the law of the truck driver's state of citizenship. Instead, the court must look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which competing state's substantive law should apply to this case. (Choice C) If the court were to review the negligence law of both states and select the law the court found most appropriate, then the court would be erroneously developing its own federal choice-of-law rules. Educational objective: In a federal diversity action where no federal substantive law applies, a federal court must look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which state's substantive law to follow. References\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court hearing a diversity case must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which the court sits).","rule":"In a federal diversity action where no federal substantive law applies, a federal court must look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which state's substantive law to follow.","irac":{"issue":"A truck driver from State A and a bus driver from State B were involved in a collision in State B that injured the truck driver. The truck driver filed a federal diversity action in State B based on negligence, seeking $100,000 in damages from the bus driver. What law of negligence should the court apply?","rule":"In a federal diversity action where no federal substantive law applies, a federal court must look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which state's substantive law to follow.","analysis":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction, the dispute is between citizens of different states. As a result, the court must determine which state's substantive law applies to the case. If no federal rule, federal statute, or constitutional provision controls the substantive issue, then the court must follow the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits. Here, the federal court in State B has diversity jurisdiction over this negligence case. There is no federal law on negligence. Therefore, the court must use the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits (State B) to determine which state's (State A's or State B's) negligence law a court in State B should apply. (Choice A) There is no federal common law of negligence, so federal courts must adhere to state law on this substantive matter. (Choice B) The court cannot simply select the law of the truck driver's state of citizenship. Instead, the court must look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which competing state's substantive law should apply to this case. (Choice C) If the court were to review the negligence law of both states and select the law the court found most appropriate, then the court would be erroneously developing its own federal choice-of-law rules. Educational objective: In a federal diversity action where no federal substantive law applies, a federal court must look to the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits to determine which state's substantive law to follow. References\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court hearing a diversity case must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which the court sits).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court hearing a diversity case must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which the court sits).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[505],"sourceAnswerPages":[506],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的卡车司机与一名来自 B 州的公交车司机在 B 州发生碰撞，卡车司机受伤。卡车司机在 B 州提起联邦多元州籍过失诉讼，向公交车司机请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。法院应适用哪一州的过失法？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院应适用联邦过失普通法。","B":"法院应适用卡车司机公民州 A 州的过失法。","C":"法院应同时考虑 A 州与 B 州的过失法，并适用法院认为最适当支配本诉过失问题的法律。","D":"法院应确定 B 州州法院会适用哪一州的过失法，并在本诉中适用该法律。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院的事项管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权时，争议发生在不同州的公民之间。因此，法院必须确定适用哪个州的实体法。如果没有联邦规则、联邦制定法或宪法条款控制该实体问题，法院就必须遵循其所在地州的法律选择规则。\n\n本题 B 州联邦法院对这起过失案件拥有多元州籍管辖权。不存在有关过失的联邦法。因此，法院必须适用其所在地州（B 州）的法律选择规则，确定 B 州法院应适用哪一州（A 州或 B 州）的过失法。\n\n（选项 A）不存在联邦过失普通法，所以联邦法院必须就这一实体问题遵循州法。\n\n（选项 B）法院不能直接选择卡车司机公民州的法律。相反，法院必须依据其所在地州的法律选择规则，确定应对本案适用哪个存在竞争的州的实体法。\n\n（选项 C）如果法院审查两州的过失法，再选择其认为最适当的法律，就会错误地创设自己的联邦法律选择规则。\n\n学习目标：在没有联邦实体法适用的联邦多元州籍诉讼中，联邦法院必须依据其所在地州的法律选择规则，确定应遵循哪一州的实体法。\n\n参考资料：\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941)（说明联邦法院审理多元州籍案件时，必须适用其所在地州的法律选择规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-057"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-168","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":168,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A former secretary sued a large corporation in an appropriate federal district court for violating the federal sex discrimination statute. The secretary seeks to recover $1 million and properly requested a jury trial. During voir dire to seat a six-person jury, potential jurors revealed several facts about themselves. An elderly woman stated that she did not believe that female employees fired for discriminatory reasons deserve compensation because \"people blame everything on sex.\" A contractor explained that he did not want to serve on the jury because he really wanted to get back to work. A man revealed that he is the brother of the defendant's lawyer. A businesswoman said that she owned stock in a retirement fund that she did not think contained any of the corporation's stock, but she could not be 100% certain. If the secretary's counsel seeks to strike each of these potential jurors from serving on the jury, which potential juror can only be stricken with a peremptory challenge?","choices":{"A":"The businesswoman.","B":"The contractor.","C":"The elderly woman.","D":"The man."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Challenges to potential jurors\n\n(FRCP 47)\n\nNumber Basis for disqualification allowed\n\nPeremptory 3 per party* • Any basis other than race, ethnicity, or gender\n• No explanation needed For cause Unlimited • For bias or lack of impartiality—eg: – relationship with party – financial stake in party FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*When a civil action involves multiple plaintiffs or defendants, the court may grant each side (1) three peremptory challenges or (2) more than three peremptory challenges that each side's parties exercise separately or jointly. In all federal civil cases where the amount in controversy exceeds $20, the Seventh Amendment guarantees the right to a jury trial. To select the jury, the court, the parties, or the parties' attorneys may question the potential jurors about their backgrounds and potential biases. During this voir dire process, each party can strike potential jurors from service through:\n• challenges for cause – to strike a potential juror based on his/her inability to serve as a fair and impartial finder of fact (eg, financial stake in a party, bias, relation to party or party's counsel) and\n\n• peremptory challenges – to strike a potential juror for any reason—except for race, ethnicity, or gender—without an explanation.\n\nHere, the secretary's attorney can successfully challenge three of the four potential jurors for cause. The businesswoman may have a financial stake in the corporation through her retirement fund stock (Choice A). The elderly woman displayed a clear bias by stating that she did not believe that female employees fired for discriminatory reasons deserve compensation because \"people blame everything on sex\" (Choice C). And the man is the brother of the defendant's attorney (Choice D). However, the contractor's statement that he did not want to serve on the jury because he wanted to get back to work reveals no basis for a challenge for cause. This response may cause the secretary's counsel concern as to whether the contractor will take jury service seriously but does not show that he will be unable to serve fairly and impartially. Therefore,\n\nthe secretary's counsel must use a peremptory challenge to strike the contractor. And since there is no indication of a racial, ethnic, or gender-based motive for the challenge, it should succeed. Educational objective: During jury selection in a federal civil action, each party may strike potential jurors from service through (1) challenges for cause—to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality—and (2) peremptory challenges—to strike a potential juror for any reason (except race, ethnicity, or gender) without explanation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47 (jury-selection process).","rule":"During jury selection in a federal civil action, each party may strike potential jurors from service through (1) challenges for cause—to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality—and (2) peremptory challenges—to strike a potential juror for any reason (except race, ethnicity, or gender) without explanation.","irac":{"issue":"A former secretary sued a large corporation in an appropriate federal district court for violating the federal sex discrimination statute. The secretary seeks to recover $1 million and properly requested a jury trial. During voir dire to seat a six-person jury, potential jurors revealed several facts about themselves. An elderly woman stated that she did not believe that female employees fired for discriminatory reasons deserve compensation because \"people blame everything on sex.\" A contractor explained that he did not want to serve on the jury because he really wanted to get back to work. A man revealed that he is the brother of the defendant's lawyer. A businesswoman said that she owned stock in a retirement fund that she did not think contained any of the corporation's stock, but she could not be 100% certain. If the secretary's counsel seeks to strike each of these potential jurors from serving on the jury, which potential juror can only be stricken with a peremptory challenge?","rule":"During jury selection in a federal civil action, each party may strike potential jurors from service through (1) challenges for cause—to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality—and (2) peremptory challenges—to strike a potential juror for any reason (except race, ethnicity, or gender) without explanation.","analysis":"Challenges to potential jurors\n\n(FRCP 47)\n\nNumber Basis for disqualification allowed\n\nPeremptory 3 per party* • Any basis other than race, ethnicity, or gender\n• No explanation needed For cause Unlimited • For bias or lack of impartiality—eg: – relationship with party – financial stake in party FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*When a civil action involves multiple plaintiffs or defendants, the court may grant each side (1) three peremptory challenges or (2) more than three peremptory challenges that each side's parties exercise separately or jointly. In all federal civil cases where the amount in controversy exceeds $20, the Seventh Amendment guarantees the right to a jury trial. To select the jury, the court, the parties, or the parties' attorneys may question the potential jurors about their backgrounds and potential biases. During this voir dire process, each party can strike potential jurors from service through:\n• challenges for cause – to strike a potential juror based on his/her inability to serve as a fair and impartial finder of fact (eg, financial stake in a party, bias, relation to party or party's counsel) and\n\n• peremptory challenges – to strike a potential juror for any reason—except for race, ethnicity, or gender—without an explanation.\n\nHere, the secretary's attorney can successfully challenge three of the four potential jurors for cause. The businesswoman may have a financial stake in the corporation through her retirement fund stock (Choice A). The elderly woman displayed a clear bias by stating that she did not believe that female employees fired for discriminatory reasons deserve compensation because \"people blame everything on sex\" (Choice C). And the man is the brother of the defendant's attorney (Choice D). However, the contractor's statement that he did not want to serve on the jury because he wanted to get back to work reveals no basis for a challenge for cause. This response may cause the secretary's counsel concern as to whether the contractor will take jury service seriously but does not show that he will be unable to serve fairly and impartially. Therefore,\n\nthe secretary's counsel must use a peremptory challenge to strike the contractor. And since there is no indication of a racial, ethnic, or gender-based motive for the challenge, it should succeed. Educational objective: During jury selection in a federal civil action, each party may strike potential jurors from service through (1) challenges for cause—to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality—and (2) peremptory challenges—to strike a potential juror for any reason (except race, ethnicity, or gender) without explanation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47 (jury-selection process).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47 (jury-selection process).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[508],"sourceAnswerPages":[509,510],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名前秘书在有适当管辖权的联邦地区法院起诉一家大型公司违反联邦性别歧视制定法。秘书请求获得 100 万美元赔偿，并适当要求陪审团审判。在为组成六人陪审团进行陪审员资格询问时，候选陪审员披露了若干自身情况。一名老妇称，她不认为因歧视性理由被解雇的女性雇员应获得赔偿，因为“人们什么事都怪到性别上”。一名承包商解释，他不想担任陪审员，因为非常想回去工作。一名男子透露，他是被告律师的兄弟。一名女商人称，她持有一只退休基金的股份，认为该基金并未持有涉案公司的股票，但无法百分之百确定。如果秘书的律师希望排除上述每名候选陪审员，哪一名候选人只能通过无因回避排除？","choicesZh":{"A":"女商人。","B":"承包商。","C":"老妇。","D":"该男子。"},"explanationZh":"对候选陪审员的回避（FRCP 47）：\n\n类型｜允许数量｜取消资格的依据\n无因回避｜每方 3 次*｜除种族、族裔或性别外的任何理由；无需说明\n有因回避｜不限次数｜存在偏见或不能保持公正，例如与一方有关系、在一方具有经济利益\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*民事诉讼有多名原告或被告时，法院可给予每一方：(1) 三次无因回避；或 (2) 三次以上由同一方各当事人分别或共同使用的无因回避。\n\n在所有争议金额超过 20 美元的联邦民事案件中，第七修正案保障陪审团审判权。为选出陪审团，法院、双方或双方律师可以询问候选陪审员的背景和潜在偏见。在陪审员资格询问过程中，每一方可通过以下方式排除候选陪审员：\n• 有因回避——基于候选陪审员无法作为公平公正的事实裁判者履行职责而将其排除，例如在一方具有经济利益、存在偏见、与一方或一方律师存在亲属关系；以及\n• 无因回避——基于任何理由将候选陪审员排除，无需说明，但不得基于种族、族裔或性别。\n\n本题秘书的律师可以成功有因回避四名候选陪审员中的三名。女商人可能通过退休基金股票在涉案公司具有经济利益（选项 A）。老妇明确表示，她不认为因歧视性理由被解雇的女性雇员应获得赔偿，因为“人们什么事都怪到性别上”，显示出明显偏见（选项 C）。该男子则是被告律师的兄弟（选项 D）。\n\n但是，承包商称自己因为想回去工作而不愿担任陪审员，并未表明任何有因回避依据。这一回答可能使秘书的律师担心承包商是否会认真履行陪审员职责，但没有表明他无法公平公正地履职。因此，秘书的律师必须使用无因回避排除承包商。由于没有迹象表明回避动机基于种族、族裔或性别，该回避应会成功。\n\n学习目标：联邦民事诉讼遴选陪审员时，每一方均可通过以下方式排除候选陪审员：(1) 有因回避——因候选人存在偏见或不能公正履职而排除；(2) 无因回避——基于任何理由，无需说明，但不得基于种族、族裔或性别。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47（陪审员遴选程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-169","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":169,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"An administrator brought a federal diversity action on behalf of a deceased woman's estate against a farmer to quiet title to 200 acres of land. The jury returned a verdict for the farmer, and the court entered judgment. The beneficiary of the woman's estate then sued the farmer in federal court contesting ownership of the land. The farmer has moved to dismiss the beneficiary's action on the basis of collateral estoppel (issue preclusion). Should the court grant the farmer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the beneficiary was not a party to the action between the administrator and the farmer.","B":"No, because the claims are not sufficiently identical.","C":"Yes, because the beneficiary's interests were adequately represented in the administrator's action.","D":"Yes, because the farmer was a party to the first action."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Exceptions to rule against nonparty preclusion\n\nIssue preclusion may be asserted against nonparty who:\n\n• agreed to be bound by judgment\n• had substantial legal relationship with party (eg, bailor–bailee)\n• was adequately represented by party with same interests (eg, class action)\n• assumed control over case (eg, insurer–insured)\n• attempts to relitigate through proxy or\n• was sued under special statutes that bar relitigation & satisfy due process Collateral estoppel (ie, issue preclusion) is a defense that bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. This defense can be asserted against parties to the first action or their privies. But it typically cannot be asserted against a nonparty to the first action because the nonparty would be deprived of the opportunity to fully and fairly litigate his/her claims and defenses. However, one exception arises when the nonparty's interests were adequately represented in the first action by a party with the same interests. For example, a beneficiary is adequately represented in a suit involving an administrator of an estate since the administrator represents the interests of the estate and the beneficiaries in that suit. Here, the administrator of the woman's estate brought the first quiet title action against the farmer, and a final judgment was entered in the farmer's favor. The beneficiary of the woman's estate then filed a second quiet title action against the farmer contesting ownership of the same land. Although the beneficiary was not a party to the first action, the administrator adequately represented the beneficiary's interest in the land (Choice A). Therefore, the court should grant the farmer's motion to dismiss the beneficiary's action. (Choice B) Claim preclusion bars identical claims, while issue preclusion (asserted here) bars subsequent cases that involve the same issues of fact or law as a prior action. (Choice D) For collateral estoppel to apply, the party against whom it is asserted (here, the beneficiary) must have been a party to the first action—absent an exception. But whether the party asserting collateral estoppel (here, the farmer) was a party to the first action is irrelevant. Educational objective: Collateral estoppel bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment and can be asserted against a nonparty to the first\n\naction in limited situations—eg, when the nonparty's interests were adequately represented in the first action by a party with the same interests. References\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893–95 (2008) (listing the exceptions to the rule against nonparty preclusion).\n\n• 18A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4454 (3rd ed. 2020) (explaining that issue preclusion can be asserted against a nonparty when he/she was adequately represented in the first action).","rule":"Collateral estoppel bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment and can be asserted against a nonparty to the first\n\naction in limited situations—eg, when the nonparty's interests were adequately represented in the first action by a party with the same interests.","irac":{"issue":"An administrator brought a federal diversity action on behalf of a deceased woman's estate against a farmer to quiet title to 200 acres of land. The jury returned a verdict for the farmer, and the court entered judgment. The beneficiary of the woman's estate then sued the farmer in federal court contesting ownership of the land. The farmer has moved to dismiss the beneficiary's action on the basis of collateral estoppel (issue preclusion). Should the court grant the farmer's motion?","rule":"Collateral estoppel bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment and can be asserted against a nonparty to the first\n\naction in limited situations—eg, when the nonparty's interests were adequately represented in the first action by a party with the same interests.","analysis":"Exceptions to rule against nonparty preclusion\n\nIssue preclusion may be asserted against nonparty who:\n\n• agreed to be bound by judgment\n• had substantial legal relationship with party (eg, bailor–bailee)\n• was adequately represented by party with same interests (eg, class action)\n• assumed control over case (eg, insurer–insured)\n• attempts to relitigate through proxy or\n• was sued under special statutes that bar relitigation & satisfy due process Collateral estoppel (ie, issue preclusion) is a defense that bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. This defense can be asserted against parties to the first action or their privies. But it typically cannot be asserted against a nonparty to the first action because the nonparty would be deprived of the opportunity to fully and fairly litigate his/her claims and defenses. However, one exception arises when the nonparty's interests were adequately represented in the first action by a party with the same interests. For example, a beneficiary is adequately represented in a suit involving an administrator of an estate since the administrator represents the interests of the estate and the beneficiaries in that suit. Here, the administrator of the woman's estate brought the first quiet title action against the farmer, and a final judgment was entered in the farmer's favor. The beneficiary of the woman's estate then filed a second quiet title action against the farmer contesting ownership of the same land. Although the beneficiary was not a party to the first action, the administrator adequately represented the beneficiary's interest in the land (Choice A). Therefore, the court should grant the farmer's motion to dismiss the beneficiary's action. (Choice B) Claim preclusion bars identical claims, while issue preclusion (asserted here) bars subsequent cases that involve the same issues of fact or law as a prior action. (Choice D) For collateral estoppel to apply, the party against whom it is asserted (here, the beneficiary) must have been a party to the first action—absent an exception. But whether the party asserting collateral estoppel (here, the farmer) was a party to the first action is irrelevant. Educational objective: Collateral estoppel bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment and can be asserted against a nonparty to the first\n\naction in limited situations—eg, when the nonparty's interests were adequately represented in the first action by a party with the same interests. References\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893–95 (2008) (listing the exceptions to the rule against nonparty preclusion).\n\n• 18A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4454 (3rd ed. 2020) (explaining that issue preclusion can be asserted against a nonparty when he/she was adequately represented in the first action).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893–95 (2008) (listing the exceptions to the rule against nonparty preclusion).\n\n• 18A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4454 (3rd ed. 2020) (explaining that issue preclusion can be asserted against a nonparty when he/she was adequately represented in the first action).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[511],"sourceAnswerPages":[512,513],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名遗产管理人代表一名已故女子的遗产，对一名农场主提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，请求确认 200 英亩土地的所有权。陪审团作出有利于农场主的裁决，法院登记判决。随后，女子遗产的受益人在联邦法院起诉农场主，争议该土地的所有权。农场主依据附带禁反言（争点排除）申请驳回受益人的诉讼。法院应批准农场主的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为受益人不是遗产管理人与农场主之间诉讼的当事人。","B":"不应，因为两项请求不够相同。","C":"应当，因为受益人的利益在遗产管理人提起的诉讼中得到了充分代表。","D":"应当，因为农场主是第一案当事人。"},"explanationZh":"禁止对非当事人适用排除规则的例外：\n\n可以对符合下列情形的非当事人主张争点排除：\n• 同意受判决约束；\n• 与一方存在实质法律关系，例如寄托人与受托人；\n• 由具有相同利益的一方充分代表，例如集体诉讼；\n• 控制了案件，例如保险人与被保险人；\n• 试图通过代理人重新争讼；或\n• 依据禁止重新争讼且符合正当程序的特别制定法被起诉。\n\n附带禁反言（即争点排除）是一项抗辩，禁止重新争讼先前已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。该抗辩可以对第一案当事人或与其具有法律利益关系者主张。但是，通常不能对第一案的非当事人主张，因为这会剥夺非当事人充分、公平争讼其请求和抗辩的机会。\n\n不过，如果非当事人的利益在第一案中由利益相同的一方充分代表，则构成例外。例如，在涉及遗产管理人的诉讼中，受益人的利益得到充分代表，因为遗产管理人在诉讼中代表遗产及受益人的利益。\n\n本题女子遗产的管理人对农场主提起第一起土地所有权确认诉讼，法院登记了有利于农场主的终局判决。随后，女子遗产的受益人对农场主提起第二起土地所有权确认诉讼，争议同一土地的所有权。受益人虽非第一案当事人，但遗产管理人充分代表了受益人对土地的利益（选项 A）。因此，法院应批准农场主驳回受益人诉讼的动议。\n\n（选项 B）请求权排除禁止相同请求，而本题主张的争点排除禁止后案审理与先前诉讼相同的事实或法律问题。\n\n（选项 D）要适用附带禁反言，被主张该规则的一方（本题为受益人）必须是第一案当事人，除非存在例外。但主张附带禁反言的一方（本题为农场主）是否为第一案当事人无关。\n\n学习目标：附带禁反言禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。在有限情形下，可以对第一案非当事人主张，例如该非当事人的利益在第一案中由具有相同利益的一方充分代表。\n\n参考资料：\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893–95 (2008)（列举禁止对非当事人适用排除规则的例外）。\n• 18A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4454 (3rd ed. 2020)（说明非当事人在第一案中得到充分代表时，可以对其主张争点排除）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-170","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":170,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A builder brought a federal diversity action against a homeowner for breach of contract, seeking $115,000 in damages. The homeowner answered and counterclaimed for $93,000 in damages for breach of warranty. Four weeks later, when the homeowner had not received any responsive pleading from the builder, the homeowner moved for entry of default and a default judgment on the counterclaim. After the clerk entered default, the homeowner asked the clerk to enter a default judgment. May the clerk enter a default judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because only the court can enter a default judgment under these circumstances.","B":"No, because the builder's time to respond has not expired.","C":"Yes, because the builder has failed to defend himself against the counterclaim.","D":"Yes, because the damages the homeowner seeks are a sum certain."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A default generally occurs when a defendant fails to timely serve an answer to a plaintiff's complaint. However, it can also occur when a plaintiff fails to timely answer a defendant's counterclaim within 21 days after it was served. When this failure is shown to the court clerk, the clerk must enter the default into the record. Since the builder has failed to respond to the homeowner's counterclaim four weeks after it was asserted, the clerk properly entered the builder's default (Choice B). A default judgment can then be sought from the clerk or the court. The clerk may enter a default judgment only when:\n• the claim is for a sum certain or a sum that can be made certain by calculation\n• the request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due\n• the defaulting party failed to appear—ie, did not file a motion or otherwise act before the court—and\n• the defaulting party is not legally incompetent or a minor. Here, the homeowner's $93,000 counterclaim is for a sum certain and there is no indication that the builder is legally incompetent or a minor. However, there is no indication that the homeowner included an affidavit in the request, and the builder appeared before the court by filing his complaint. This means that only the court, not the clerk, can enter a default judgment under these circumstances (Choice D).* *Since the builder has appeared in the action, the court may enter a default judgment if the builder is served with written notice of the homeowner's application for default judgment at least seven days before a hearing on the matter. (Choice C) The fact that the builder failed to timely defend himself against the homeowner's counterclaim allows the clerk to enter the builder's default into the record. However, the clerk may not enter a default judgment since the homeowner failed to include an affidavit in the request and the builder has appeared in the action. Educational objective: A clerk may enter a default judgment when (1) the claim is for a sum certain, (2) the request includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defaulting party failed to appear, and (4) the defaulting party is not legally incompetent or a minor. Otherwise, only the court may enter a default judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2683 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining when a court clerk may enter a default judgment).","rule":"A clerk may enter a default judgment when (1) the claim is for a sum certain, (2) the request includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defaulting party failed to appear, and (4) the defaulting party is not legally incompetent or a minor. Otherwise, only the court may enter a default judgment.","irac":{"issue":"A builder brought a federal diversity action against a homeowner for breach of contract, seeking $115,000 in damages. The homeowner answered and counterclaimed for $93,000 in damages for breach of warranty. Four weeks later, when the homeowner had not received any responsive pleading from the builder, the homeowner moved for entry of default and a default judgment on the counterclaim. After the clerk entered default, the homeowner asked the clerk to enter a default judgment. May the clerk enter a default judgment?","rule":"A clerk may enter a default judgment when (1) the claim is for a sum certain, (2) the request includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defaulting party failed to appear, and (4) the defaulting party is not legally incompetent or a minor. Otherwise, only the court may enter a default judgment.","analysis":"A default generally occurs when a defendant fails to timely serve an answer to a plaintiff's complaint. However, it can also occur when a plaintiff fails to timely answer a defendant's counterclaim within 21 days after it was served. When this failure is shown to the court clerk, the clerk must enter the default into the record. Since the builder has failed to respond to the homeowner's counterclaim four weeks after it was asserted, the clerk properly entered the builder's default (Choice B). A default judgment can then be sought from the clerk or the court. The clerk may enter a default judgment only when:\n• the claim is for a sum certain or a sum that can be made certain by calculation\n• the request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due\n• the defaulting party failed to appear—ie, did not file a motion or otherwise act before the court—and\n• the defaulting party is not legally incompetent or a minor. Here, the homeowner's $93,000 counterclaim is for a sum certain and there is no indication that the builder is legally incompetent or a minor. However, there is no indication that the homeowner included an affidavit in the request, and the builder appeared before the court by filing his complaint. This means that only the court, not the clerk, can enter a default judgment under these circumstances (Choice D).* *Since the builder has appeared in the action, the court may enter a default judgment if the builder is served with written notice of the homeowner's application for default judgment at least seven days before a hearing on the matter. (Choice C) The fact that the builder failed to timely defend himself against the homeowner's counterclaim allows the clerk to enter the builder's default into the record. However, the clerk may not enter a default judgment since the homeowner failed to include an affidavit in the request and the builder has appeared in the action. Educational objective: A clerk may enter a default judgment when (1) the claim is for a sum certain, (2) the request includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defaulting party failed to appear, and (4) the defaulting party is not legally incompetent or a minor. Otherwise, only the court may enter a default judgment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2683 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining when a court clerk may enter a default judgment).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).\n\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2683 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining when a court clerk may enter a default judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[514],"sourceAnswerPages":[515,516],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名建筑商对一名房主提起联邦多元州籍违约诉讼，请求 115,000 美元损害赔偿。房主提交答辩状，并以违反保证为由提出反诉，请求 93,000 美元损害赔偿。四周后，房主仍未收到建筑商的任何回应性诉状，于是申请就反诉登记缺席及缺席判决。书记员登记缺席后，房主请求书记员登记缺席判决。书记员可以登记缺席判决吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在这种情况下只有法院可以登记缺席判决。","B":"不可以，因为建筑商的回应期限尚未届满。","C":"可以，因为建筑商未就反诉为自己进行抗辩。","D":"可以，因为房主请求的损害赔偿是确定金额。"},"explanationZh":"被告未及时就原告起诉状送达答辩状时，通常构成缺席。但是，原告在被告反诉送达后 21 天内未及时答辩，也可构成缺席。将该未回应情况向法院书记员证明后，书记员必须将缺席登记于案卷记录。\n\n房主提出反诉四周后，建筑商仍未回应，所以书记员适当登记了建筑商缺席（选项 B）。随后，可以请求书记员或法院登记缺席判决。只有符合以下条件时，书记员才可登记缺席判决：\n• 请求的金额确定，或可以通过计算确定；\n• 缺席判决请求附有证明到期金额的宣誓书；\n• 缺席方未出庭，即未提交动议或以其他方式在法院采取行动；并且\n• 缺席方并非法律上无行为能力者或未成年人。\n\n本题房主 93,000 美元反诉属于确定金额，且没有迹象表明建筑商在法律上无行为能力或是未成年人。但是，没有迹象表明房主在请求中附有宣誓书，而且建筑商通过提交起诉状已经出庭。这意味着在这种情况下只有法院而非书记员可以登记缺席判决（选项 D）。*\n\n*建筑商已经在诉讼中出庭，所以如果在缺席判决申请听证前至少七天，向建筑商送达房主申请缺席判决的书面通知，法院可以登记缺席判决。\n\n（选项 C）建筑商未及时就房主反诉进行抗辩，允许书记员将其缺席登记于案卷记录。但是，由于房主没有在请求中附上宣誓书，而且建筑商已经出庭，书记员不得登记缺席判决。\n\n学习目标：符合以下条件时，书记员可以登记缺席判决：(1) 请求金额确定；(2) 请求附有证明到期金额的宣誓书；(3) 缺席方未出庭；且 (4) 缺席方并非法律上无行为能力者或未成年人。否则，只有法院可以登记缺席判决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55（缺席判决）。\n• 10A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2683 (4th ed. 2020)（说明法院书记员何时可以登记缺席判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-058"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-171","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":171,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A firm incorporated and headquartered in State A sued its competitor from State B in a State A state court. The firm sought $100,000 for the competitor's defamatory statements about the firm's business practices. The complaint did not include a jury demand. Under State A law, a party must request a jury trial in its initial pleading or the right to a jury is waived. Fifteen days after the competitor served its answer, the competitor removed the case to federal court on the basis of diversity jurisdiction. Ten days after being served with the notice of removal, the firm served a written demand for a jury trial. Which of the following findings will the court likely make regarding the firm's demand?","choices":{"A":"The firm preserved its right to a jury trial by asserting a legal claim in the complaint.","B":"The firm preserved its right to a jury trial by timely serving its demand for a jury.","C":"The firm waived its right to a jury trial by failing to assert the demand in state court.","D":"The firm waived its right to a jury trial by serving the demand more than 14 days after service of the competitor's answer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"All civil litigants in federal court have the constitutional right to a jury trial for any legal claim in which the amount in controversy exceeds $20. A party generally preserves this right by demanding a jury trial within 14 days after the last pleading (usually the defendant's answer) is served. However, in an action removed from state to federal court, a party preserves this right by either:\n• demanding a jury trial in state court before removal or\n\n• serving a jury trial demand within 14 days after filing or being served with the notice of removal—so long as all necessary pleadings (ie, the complaint and answer) had been served before removal.*\n\nHere, the firm did not demand a jury trial in state court before the competitor removed the case to federal court. However, the complaint and answer were served before removal and the firm timely served its demand for a jury trial 10 days after being served with the competitor's notice of removal. As a result, the federal court will likely find that the firm preserved its jury trial right (Choices C & D). *If the necessary pleadings were not served before removal, then the general deadline for demanding a jury—ie, 14 days after the last pleading is served—applies. (Choice A) The firm has the right to a jury trial because its complaint asserted a legal claim—ie, a claim that seeks a monetary remedy—in which the $100,000 amount in controversy far exceeds the $20 threshold. However, the firm may only exercise this right if it made a timely demand. Educational objective: In a removal action, a party preserves its right to a jury trial by (1) demanding a jury in state court before removal or (2) serving a jury trial demand within 14 days after filing or being served with the notice of removal, so long as all necessary pleadings had been served before removal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 81(c)(3) (jury trial demand in removal action).\n\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2319 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how to properly demand a jury trial in an action removed from state to federal court).","rule":"In a removal action, a party preserves its right to a jury trial by (1) demanding a jury in state court before removal or (2) serving a jury trial demand within 14 days after filing or being served with the notice of removal, so long as all necessary pleadings had been served before removal.","irac":{"issue":"A firm incorporated and headquartered in State A sued its competitor from State B in a State A state court. The firm sought $100,000 for the competitor's defamatory statements about the firm's business practices. The complaint did not include a jury demand. Under State A law, a party must request a jury trial in its initial pleading or the right to a jury is waived. Fifteen days after the competitor served its answer, the competitor removed the case to federal court on the basis of diversity jurisdiction. Ten days after being served with the notice of removal, the firm served a written demand for a jury trial. Which of the following findings will the court likely make regarding the firm's demand?","rule":"In a removal action, a party preserves its right to a jury trial by (1) demanding a jury in state court before removal or (2) serving a jury trial demand within 14 days after filing or being served with the notice of removal, so long as all necessary pleadings had been served before removal.","analysis":"All civil litigants in federal court have the constitutional right to a jury trial for any legal claim in which the amount in controversy exceeds $20. A party generally preserves this right by demanding a jury trial within 14 days after the last pleading (usually the defendant's answer) is served. However, in an action removed from state to federal court, a party preserves this right by either:\n• demanding a jury trial in state court before removal or\n\n• serving a jury trial demand within 14 days after filing or being served with the notice of removal—so long as all necessary pleadings (ie, the complaint and answer) had been served before removal.*\n\nHere, the firm did not demand a jury trial in state court before the competitor removed the case to federal court. However, the complaint and answer were served before removal and the firm timely served its demand for a jury trial 10 days after being served with the competitor's notice of removal. As a result, the federal court will likely find that the firm preserved its jury trial right (Choices C & D). *If the necessary pleadings were not served before removal, then the general deadline for demanding a jury—ie, 14 days after the last pleading is served—applies. (Choice A) The firm has the right to a jury trial because its complaint asserted a legal claim—ie, a claim that seeks a monetary remedy—in which the $100,000 amount in controversy far exceeds the $20 threshold. However, the firm may only exercise this right if it made a timely demand. Educational objective: In a removal action, a party preserves its right to a jury trial by (1) demanding a jury in state court before removal or (2) serving a jury trial demand within 14 days after filing or being served with the notice of removal, so long as all necessary pleadings had been served before removal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 81(c)(3) (jury trial demand in removal action).\n\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2319 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how to properly demand a jury trial in an action removed from state to federal court).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 81(c)(3) (jury trial demand in removal action).\n\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2319 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how to properly demand a jury trial in an action removed from state to federal court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[517],"sourceAnswerPages":[518],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且总部位于 A 州的公司，在 A 州州法院起诉一家来自 B 州的竞争对手。公司就竞争对手针对其商业惯例发表的诽谤性陈述，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。起诉状未包含陪审团请求。根据 A 州法律，一方必须在最初诉状中要求陪审团审判，否则视为放弃该权利。竞争对手送达答辩状十五天后，依据多元州籍管辖权将案件移送至联邦法院。公司收到移送通知送达十天后，送达了书面陪审团审判请求。对于公司的请求，法院可能作出下列哪项认定？","choicesZh":{"A":"公司在起诉状中提出法律请求，因此保留了陪审团审判权。","B":"公司及时送达陪审团请求，因此保留了陪审团审判权。","C":"公司未在州法院提出请求，因此放弃了陪审团审判权。","D":"公司在竞争对手答辩状送达超过 14 天后才送达请求，因此放弃了陪审团审判权。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院中的所有民事诉讼当事人，对于争议金额超过 20 美元的任何法律请求，均享有宪法规定的陪审团审判权。一方通常须在最后一份诉状（一般为被告答辩状）送达后 14 天内要求陪审团审判，才能保留该权利。\n\n但是，案件从州法院移送至联邦法院时，一方可以通过以下任一方式保留该权利：\n• 在移送前于州法院要求陪审团审判；或\n• 在提交移送通知或收到该通知送达后 14 天内送达陪审团审判请求，但移送前所有必要诉状（即起诉状和答辩状）必须均已送达。*\n\n本题公司在竞争对手将案件移送至联邦法院前，没有在州法院要求陪审团审判。但是，移送前起诉状和答辩状均已送达，而且公司在竞争对手的移送通知送达后 10 天及时送达陪审团审判请求。因此，联邦法院很可能认定公司保留了陪审团审判权（选项 C、D）。\n\n*如果移送前必要诉状尚未送达，则适用要求陪审团审判的一般期限，即最后一份诉状送达后 14 天。\n\n（选项 A）公司享有陪审团审判权，因为起诉状提出一项法律请求，即请求金钱救济，而且 100,000 美元争议金额远超 20 美元门槛。但是，公司只有及时提出请求，才能行使该权利。\n\n学习目标：在移送案件中，一方可通过以下方式保留陪审团审判权：(1) 移送前在州法院要求陪审团审判；或 (2) 在提交移送通知或收到该通知送达后 14 天内送达陪审团审判请求，但移送前所有必要诉状必须均已送达。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 81(c)(3)（移送案件中的陪审团审判请求）。\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2319 (3d ed. 2020)（说明如何在从州法院移送至联邦法院的案件中适当要求陪审团审判）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-059"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-172","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":172,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"An individual investor purchased stock through a company's stock offering. When the price of the stock plummeted, the investor sued the company in a state court in State A, claiming that the company's offering materials had fraudulently induced him to purchase the stock and seeking $25,000 in damages. A university that had purchased the company's stock through the same offering sued the company in federal court in State B, claiming that the offering materials violated federal securities laws and seeking $1 million in damages. The individual investor's suit proceeded to trial. The state court ruled that the company's offering materials contained false information and awarded the investor a $25,000 judgment. The university immediately moved for partial summary judgment in its federal action against the company, arguing that the state court judgment bound the federal court on the issue of whether the company's offering materials contained false information. Neither State A nor State B permits nonmutual issue preclusion. Should the court grant the university's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because State A does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion.","B":"No, because the federal court sits in a state that does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion.","C":"Yes, because federal law permits nonmutual issue preclusion.","D":"Yes, because the issue of whether the materials contained false information was actually litigated and necessarily decided."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes subsequent litigation of the same issue actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment issued on an earlier claim. There are two types of issue preclusion:\n• Mutual – issue preclusion in second action asserted by parties to the first action against other parties to the first action\n\n• Nonmutual – issue preclusion in second action asserted by nonparties to the first action against parties to the first action\n\nThe court will look to the court that entered the first judgment to determine which issue-preclusion rule applies to the second action. A judgment entered by a federal court means federal preclusion law applies,* while a judgment entered by a state court means that state's preclusion law applies. Here, the state court in State A entered the first judgment, so State A's preclusion law applies to the second action in federal court. State A does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion. As a result, the university (a nonparty to the first action) cannot argue that the state court judgment bound the federal court on the same issue. The federal court should therefore deny the university's motion for partial summary judgment. *A federal court sitting in diversity will generally apply the preclusion law of the state in which it sits unless the state law is incompatible with federal interests. (Choice B) A judgment's preclusive effect is determined by the law of the forum that entered the judgment (here, State A)—not the forum where the second court sits (here, State B). (Choice C) Although federal law permits nonmutual issue preclusion, the State A law prohibiting nonmutual issue preclusion applies. (Choice D) The issue of whether the company's materials contained false information was actually litigated and necessarily decided in the first action. But since State A does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion, the university cannot assert it in the second action. Educational objective: Issue preclusion can be asserted by parties to the first action (mutual) OR by nonparties to the first action (nonmutual) against parties to the first action. If the first action was decided by a state court, then that state's issue-preclusion rule applies. References\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980) (stating that federal courts must give the same preclusive effect to state court judgments that courts in the judgment-rendering state would apply).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 549 (2020) (explaining mutual and nonmutual estoppel).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738 (full faith and credit statute).","rule":"Issue preclusion can be asserted by parties to the first action (mutual) OR by nonparties to the first action (nonmutual) against parties to the first action. If the first action was decided by a state court, then that state's issue-preclusion rule applies.","irac":{"issue":"An individual investor purchased stock through a company's stock offering. When the price of the stock plummeted, the investor sued the company in a state court in State A, claiming that the company's offering materials had fraudulently induced him to purchase the stock and seeking $25,000 in damages. A university that had purchased the company's stock through the same offering sued the company in federal court in State B, claiming that the offering materials violated federal securities laws and seeking $1 million in damages. The individual investor's suit proceeded to trial. The state court ruled that the company's offering materials contained false information and awarded the investor a $25,000 judgment. The university immediately moved for partial summary judgment in its federal action against the company, arguing that the state court judgment bound the federal court on the issue of whether the company's offering materials contained false information. Neither State A nor State B permits nonmutual issue preclusion. Should the court grant the university's motion?","rule":"Issue preclusion can be asserted by parties to the first action (mutual) OR by nonparties to the first action (nonmutual) against parties to the first action. If the first action was decided by a state court, then that state's issue-preclusion rule applies.","analysis":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes subsequent litigation of the same issue actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment issued on an earlier claim. There are two types of issue preclusion:\n• Mutual – issue preclusion in second action asserted by parties to the first action against other parties to the first action\n\n• Nonmutual – issue preclusion in second action asserted by nonparties to the first action against parties to the first action\n\nThe court will look to the court that entered the first judgment to determine which issue-preclusion rule applies to the second action. A judgment entered by a federal court means federal preclusion law applies,* while a judgment entered by a state court means that state's preclusion law applies. Here, the state court in State A entered the first judgment, so State A's preclusion law applies to the second action in federal court. State A does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion. As a result, the university (a nonparty to the first action) cannot argue that the state court judgment bound the federal court on the same issue. The federal court should therefore deny the university's motion for partial summary judgment. *A federal court sitting in diversity will generally apply the preclusion law of the state in which it sits unless the state law is incompatible with federal interests. (Choice B) A judgment's preclusive effect is determined by the law of the forum that entered the judgment (here, State A)—not the forum where the second court sits (here, State B). (Choice C) Although federal law permits nonmutual issue preclusion, the State A law prohibiting nonmutual issue preclusion applies. (Choice D) The issue of whether the company's materials contained false information was actually litigated and necessarily decided in the first action. But since State A does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion, the university cannot assert it in the second action. Educational objective: Issue preclusion can be asserted by parties to the first action (mutual) OR by nonparties to the first action (nonmutual) against parties to the first action. If the first action was decided by a state court, then that state's issue-preclusion rule applies. References\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980) (stating that federal courts must give the same preclusive effect to state court judgments that courts in the judgment-rendering state would apply).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 549 (2020) (explaining mutual and nonmutual estoppel).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738 (full faith and credit statute).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980) (stating that federal courts must give the same preclusive effect to state court judgments that courts in the judgment-rendering state would apply).\n\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 549 (2020) (explaining mutual and nonmutual estoppel).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738 (full faith and credit statute).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[520],"sourceAnswerPages":[521,522],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名个人投资者通过一家公司的股票发行购买了股票。股价暴跌后，投资者在 A 州州法院起诉该公司，主张公司的发行材料以欺诈方式诱使他购买股票，并请求 25,000 美元损害赔偿。一所通过同次发行购买该公司股票的大学，在 B 州联邦法院起诉该公司，主张发行材料违反联邦证券法，并请求 100 万美元损害赔偿。个人投资者的诉讼进入审判。州法院裁定公司发行材料包含虚假信息，并判给投资者 25,000 美元。大学随即在其对公司的联邦诉讼中申请部分简易判决，主张州法院判决在公司发行材料是否包含虚假信息这一问题上对联邦法院具有约束力。A 州与 B 州均不允许非相互性争点排除。法院应批准大学的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为 A 州不允许非相互性争点排除。","B":"不应，因为联邦法院所在地州不允许非相互性争点排除。","C":"应当，因为联邦法允许非相互性争点排除。","D":"应当，因为材料是否包含虚假信息这一问题已经实际争讼并得到必要裁定。"},"explanationZh":"争点排除（即附带禁反言）禁止在后续诉讼中争讼先前请求中已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的相同问题。争点排除有两种：\n• 相互性争点排除——在第二案中，由第一案当事人对第一案其他当事人主张；\n• 非相互性争点排除——在第二案中，由第一案非当事人对第一案当事人主张。\n\n法院将考察登记第一项判决的法院，以确定第二案适用哪套争点排除规则。判决由联邦法院登记时，适用联邦排除法；* 判决由州法院登记时，适用该州的排除法。\n\n本题 A 州州法院登记了第一项判决，所以联邦法院中的第二案适用 A 州排除法。A 州不允许非相互性争点排除。因此，大学作为第一案非当事人，不能主张州法院判决在相同问题上对联邦法院具有约束力。所以，联邦法院应驳回大学的部分简易判决动议。\n\n*行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院通常适用其所在地州的排除法，除非该州法律与联邦利益不相容。\n\n（选项 B）判决的排除效力由登记该判决的法院地法律决定，本题为 A 州，而不是第二法院所在地法律决定，本题第二法院位于 B 州。\n\n（选项 C）联邦法虽然允许非相互性争点排除，但本题适用 A 州禁止该做法的法律。\n\n（选项 D）公司材料是否包含虚假信息这一问题，在第一案中已经实际争讼并得到必要裁定。但是，由于 A 州不允许非相互性争点排除，大学不能在第二案中主张该规则。\n\n学习目标：第一案当事人可以在后案中对第一案其他当事人主张争点排除，即相互性争点排除；第一案非当事人也可能在后案中对第一案当事人主张，即非相互性争点排除。如果第一案由州法院裁判，适用该州的争点排除规则。\n\n参考资料：\n• Allen v. McCurry, 449 U.S. 90, 96 (1980)（指出联邦法院必须给予州法院判决与判决登记州法院所给予的相同排除效力）。\n• 47 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 549 (2020)（说明相互性和非相互性禁反言）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1738（完全诚信与信用制定法）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-060"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-173","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":173,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A man was charged in a state court in State A with involuntary manslaughter for causing the death of a pedestrian while driving under the influence of alcohol. The jury found the man guilty, and the court entered a judgment of conviction. The pedestrian's family has now filed a wrongful death action against the man in a federal district court in State B. The family has moved for partial summary judgment, arguing that the state court judgment binds the federal court on the issues of whether the man breached his duty and caused the pedestrian's death. State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion. However, State B prohibits nonmutual issue preclusion. Should the federal court grant the family's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the prior judgment was entered by a state court.","B":"No, because the state court action involved a criminal prosecution while the federal court action involves a civil claim.","C":"Yes, because the issues of whether the man breached his duty and caused the pedestrian's death were actually litigated and necessarily decided in the state court action.","D":"Yes, because the state court judgment is the law of the case."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment in a prior civil action OR criminal prosecution (Choice B). There are two types of issue preclusion:\n• Mutual – when issue preclusion is asserted by parties to the first action against other parties to the first action\n\n• Nonmutual – when issue preclusion is asserted by nonparties to the first action against parties to the first action\n\nTo determine which preclusion rule applies, the second court will look to the law of the forum that entered the first judgment. Here, the State A state court entered judgment in the prior criminal prosecution, so the State B federal court should now apply State A's preclusion law in this wrongful death action. Since State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion, the family (nonparty to the first action) may assert issue preclusion against the man (party to the first action). In the criminal prosecution, the jury found the man guilty of involuntary manslaughter—ie, an unintentional killing with criminal negligence. This means that the issues of whether the man breached a duty and caused the pedestrian's death were actually litigated and necessarily decided in the first action. Therefore, the federal court should grant the family's motion for partial summary judgment on these issues.* *The family cannot obtain full summary judgment because it still must prove damages, which were not actually litigated or necessarily determined in the criminal prosecution. (Choice A) A court must give full faith and credit to federal and state court judgments. Therefore, the fact that the prior judgment was entered by a state court is irrelevant. (Choice D) The law-of-the-case doctrine prohibits the relitigation of legal issues decided at a previous stage of the same case. This doctrine does not apply here since the criminal prosecution and the wrongful death action are two different cases. Educational objective: Issue preclusion bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated and necessarily determined by a valid final judgment in a prior civil action or criminal prosecution. The second court will look to the law of the forum that entered the first judgment to determine if mutual or nonmutual issue preclusion applies in the subsequent action. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 85 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining the effect of a criminal judgment in a subsequent civil action).\n\n• 18B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4474 (2d ed. 2020) (describing how nonparties to a criminal prosecution may assert issue preclusion against a party to the prosecution in a later civil action).","rule":"Issue preclusion bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated and necessarily determined by a valid final judgment in a prior civil action or criminal prosecution. The second court will look to the law of the forum that entered the first judgment to determine if mutual or nonmutual issue preclusion applies in the subsequent action.","irac":{"issue":"A man was charged in a state court in State A with involuntary manslaughter for causing the death of a pedestrian while driving under the influence of alcohol. The jury found the man guilty, and the court entered a judgment of conviction. The pedestrian's family has now filed a wrongful death action against the man in a federal district court in State B. The family has moved for partial summary judgment, arguing that the state court judgment binds the federal court on the issues of whether the man breached his duty and caused the pedestrian's death. State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion. However, State B prohibits nonmutual issue preclusion. Should the federal court grant the family's motion?","rule":"Issue preclusion bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated and necessarily determined by a valid final judgment in a prior civil action or criminal prosecution. The second court will look to the law of the forum that entered the first judgment to determine if mutual or nonmutual issue preclusion applies in the subsequent action.","analysis":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) precludes the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment in a prior civil action OR criminal prosecution (Choice B). There are two types of issue preclusion:\n• Mutual – when issue preclusion is asserted by parties to the first action against other parties to the first action\n\n• Nonmutual – when issue preclusion is asserted by nonparties to the first action against parties to the first action\n\nTo determine which preclusion rule applies, the second court will look to the law of the forum that entered the first judgment. Here, the State A state court entered judgment in the prior criminal prosecution, so the State B federal court should now apply State A's preclusion law in this wrongful death action. Since State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion, the family (nonparty to the first action) may assert issue preclusion against the man (party to the first action). In the criminal prosecution, the jury found the man guilty of involuntary manslaughter—ie, an unintentional killing with criminal negligence. This means that the issues of whether the man breached a duty and caused the pedestrian's death were actually litigated and necessarily decided in the first action. Therefore, the federal court should grant the family's motion for partial summary judgment on these issues.* *The family cannot obtain full summary judgment because it still must prove damages, which were not actually litigated or necessarily determined in the criminal prosecution. (Choice A) A court must give full faith and credit to federal and state court judgments. Therefore, the fact that the prior judgment was entered by a state court is irrelevant. (Choice D) The law-of-the-case doctrine prohibits the relitigation of legal issues decided at a previous stage of the same case. This doctrine does not apply here since the criminal prosecution and the wrongful death action are two different cases. Educational objective: Issue preclusion bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated and necessarily determined by a valid final judgment in a prior civil action or criminal prosecution. The second court will look to the law of the forum that entered the first judgment to determine if mutual or nonmutual issue preclusion applies in the subsequent action. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 85 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining the effect of a criminal judgment in a subsequent civil action).\n\n• 18B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4474 (2d ed. 2020) (describing how nonparties to a criminal prosecution may assert issue preclusion against a party to the prosecution in a later civil action).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 85 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (explaining the effect of a criminal judgment in a subsequent civil action).\n\n• 18B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4474 (2d ed. 2020) (describing how nonparties to a criminal prosecution may assert issue preclusion against a party to the prosecution in a later civil action).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[523],"sourceAnswerPages":[524,525],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在 A 州州法院被控非故意杀人罪，原因是他酒后驾车导致一名行人死亡。陪审团认定男子有罪，法院登记有罪判决。现行人家属在 B 州联邦地区法院对男子提起非正常死亡诉讼。家属申请部分简易判决，主张州法院判决在男子是否违反义务并导致行人死亡这两个问题上，对联邦法院具有约束力。A 州允许非相互性争点排除，但 B 州禁止。联邦法院应批准家属动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为先前判决由州法院登记。","B":"不应，因为州法院诉讼属于刑事追诉，而联邦法院诉讼涉及民事请求。","C":"应当，因为男子是否违反义务并导致行人死亡这两个问题，在州法院诉讼中已经实际争讼并得到必要裁定。","D":"应当，因为州法院判决构成本案法律。"},"explanationZh":"争点排除（即附带禁反言）禁止重新争讼先前民事诉讼或刑事追诉中已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题（选项 B）。争点排除有两种：\n• 相互性——由第一案当事人在第二案中对第一案其他当事人主张；\n• 非相互性——由第一案非当事人在第二案中对第一案当事人主张。\n\n为确定适用哪套排除规则，第二法院将考察登记第一项判决的法院地法律。本题 A 州州法院在先前刑事追诉中登记判决，所以 B 州联邦法院现在应对非正常死亡诉讼适用 A 州排除法。\n\nA 州允许非相互性争点排除，所以家属作为第一案非当事人，可以对男子这一第一案当事人主张争点排除。在刑事追诉中，陪审团认定男子犯非故意杀人罪，即以刑事过失造成非故意死亡。这意味着，男子是否违反义务并导致行人死亡这两个问题，在第一案中已经实际争讼并得到必要裁定。因此，联邦法院应就这些问题批准家属的部分简易判决动议。*\n\n*家属不能取得全部简易判决，因为仍须证明损害，而损害问题并未在刑事追诉中实际争讼或得到必要裁定。\n\n（选项 A）法院必须对联邦法院和州法院判决给予完全诚信与信用。因此，先前判决由州法院登记这一事实无关。\n\n（选项 D）本案法律原则禁止重新争讼同一案件先前阶段已经裁定的法律问题。本题刑事追诉和非正常死亡诉讼是两个不同案件，所以该原则不适用。\n\n学习目标：争点排除禁止重新争讼先前民事诉讼或刑事追诉中，已经实际争讼并由有效终局判决作出必要裁定的问题。第二法院将考察登记第一项判决的法院地法律，确定后案适用相互性还是非相互性争点排除。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 85 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（说明刑事判决在后续民事诉讼中的效力）。\n• 18B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 4474 (2d ed. 2020)（说明刑事追诉的非当事人如何在后续民事诉讼中，对刑事追诉当事人主张争点排除）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-061"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-174","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":174,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A writer and a publisher entered into a contract in which the publisher agreed to pay the writer royalties from the sale of her book at a set percentage. At the end of the first year of the contract, the writer received a royalty check for an amount that she believed was lower than agreed. The writer sued the publisher in state court for breach of contract. The writer claimed that the contract set the royalty percentage at 7%. However, the publisher claimed that the contract only required 4% royalties due to a condition precedent. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the writer, and the court entered a judgment consistent with the verdict. At the end of the second year of the contract, the writer received another royalty check for an amount she again believed was low. She sued the publisher in a federal court for breaching the same contractual provision. The publisher has asserted the defense of claim preclusion (res judicata) in its answer. Is the federal court likely to give preclusive effect to the state court judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judgment was entered by a state court.","B":"No, because the second action involves a different claim.","C":"Yes, because both actions involve identical claims between the same parties.","D":"Yes, because the first action was actually litigated."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under the doctrine of claim preclusion (ie, res judicata), a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action. To determine whether claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence, courts consider the following factors:\n• whether the facts are related in time, space, origin, or motivation\n• whether the facts form a convenient trial unit and\n• whether treatment of the facts as a unit conforms to the parties' expectations or business usage. Here, the state court entered a final judgment in favor of the writer for the publisher's year-one breach of the contract. The writer then sued the publisher in federal court for the publisher's year-two breach. Although both claims arise from the same contractual provision, the writer's claim for her year-two royalties did not exist at the time of the first action. Therefore, the claims are not identical, and the federal court is unlikely to give preclusive effect to the state court judgment (Choice C). (Choice A) The fact that a state court entered the judgment in the first action is irrelevant because all courts must give full faith and credit to state court judgments. (Choice D) Claim preclusion applies even if the first action was not actually litigated (eg, when a default judgment was entered). In contrast, the related doctrine of issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) applies only when issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment are raised in a subsequent action. Educational objective: Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining identical claims).\n\n• Lawlor v. Nat'l Screen Serv. Corp., 349 U.S. 322, 327–28 (1955) (holding that claim preclusion only applies to claims that existed at the time of the first action).","rule":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action.","irac":{"issue":"A writer and a publisher entered into a contract in which the publisher agreed to pay the writer royalties from the sale of her book at a set percentage. At the end of the first year of the contract, the writer received a royalty check for an amount that she believed was lower than agreed. The writer sued the publisher in state court for breach of contract. The writer claimed that the contract set the royalty percentage at 7%. However, the publisher claimed that the contract only required 4% royalties due to a condition precedent. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the writer, and the court entered a judgment consistent with the verdict. At the end of the second year of the contract, the writer received another royalty check for an amount she again believed was low. She sued the publisher in a federal court for breaching the same contractual provision. The publisher has asserted the defense of claim preclusion (res judicata) in its answer. Is the federal court likely to give preclusive effect to the state court judgment?","rule":"Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action.","analysis":"Under the doctrine of claim preclusion (ie, res judicata), a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action. To determine whether claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence, courts consider the following factors:\n• whether the facts are related in time, space, origin, or motivation\n• whether the facts form a convenient trial unit and\n• whether treatment of the facts as a unit conforms to the parties' expectations or business usage. Here, the state court entered a final judgment in favor of the writer for the publisher's year-one breach of the contract. The writer then sued the publisher in federal court for the publisher's year-two breach. Although both claims arise from the same contractual provision, the writer's claim for her year-two royalties did not exist at the time of the first action. Therefore, the claims are not identical, and the federal court is unlikely to give preclusive effect to the state court judgment (Choice C). (Choice A) The fact that a state court entered the judgment in the first action is irrelevant because all courts must give full faith and credit to state court judgments. (Choice D) Claim preclusion applies even if the first action was not actually litigated (eg, when a default judgment was entered). In contrast, the related doctrine of issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) applies only when issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment are raised in a subsequent action. Educational objective: Claim preclusion (ie, res judicata) provides that a valid final judgment on the merits precludes identical parties from relitigating identical claims. Claims are identical if they (1) arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) could have been raised in the first action. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining identical claims).\n\n• Lawlor v. Nat'l Screen Serv. Corp., 349 U.S. 322, 327–28 (1955) (holding that claim preclusion only applies to claims that existed at the time of the first action).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining identical claims).\n\n• Lawlor v. Nat'l Screen Serv. Corp., 349 U.S. 322, 327–28 (1955) (holding that claim preclusion only applies to claims that existed at the time of the first action).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[526],"sourceAnswerPages":[527],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名作家与一家出版社订立合同，出版社同意按固定百分比向作家支付图书销售版税。合同第一年结束时，作家收到一张版税支票，金额低于她认为双方约定的数额。作家在州法院起诉出版社违约。作家主张合同规定的版税比例为 7%，但出版社主张，由于一项先决条件，合同只要求支付 4% 的版税。陪审团作出有利于作家的裁决，法院依裁决登记判决。合同第二年结束时，作家又收到一张她认为金额过低的版税支票。她在联邦法院起诉出版社违反同一合同条款。出版社在答辩状中主张请求权排除（既判力）抗辩。联邦法院可能给予州法院判决排除效力吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为判决由州法院登记。","B":"不会，因为第二案涉及不同请求。","C":"会，因为两案涉及相同当事人之间的相同请求。","D":"会，因为第一案经过实际争讼。"},"explanationZh":"根据请求权排除（即既判力）原则，有效的实体终局判决禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。请求符合以下条件时即为相同：(1) 源于同一交易、事件或一系列交易或事件；并且 (2) 本可在第一案中提出。法院在确定请求是否源于同一交易或事件时，会考虑以下因素：\n• 相关事实在时间、地点、起因或动机上是否有关联；\n• 相关事实是否构成便于一并审理的单元；以及\n• 将相关事实作为一个单元处理是否符合双方预期或商业惯例。\n\n本题州法院就出版社第一年违反合同，登记了有利于作家的终局判决。随后，作家在联邦法院起诉出版社第二年违约。虽然两项请求源于同一合同条款，但作家的第二年版税请求在第一案提起时尚不存在。因此，两项请求并不相同，联邦法院不太可能给予州法院判决请求权排除效力（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）第一案判决由州法院登记这一事实无关，因为所有法院都必须对州法院判决给予完全诚信与信用。\n\n（选项 D）即使第一案没有经过实际争讼，例如登记缺席判决，请求权排除仍然适用。相比之下，相关的争点排除（即附带禁反言）只有在后案提出先前已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题时才适用。\n\n学习目标：请求权排除（即既判力）规定，有效的实体终局判决禁止相同当事人重新争讼相同请求。请求符合以下条件时即为相同：(1) 源于同一交易、事件或一系列交易或事件；且 (2) 本可在第一案中提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义相同请求）。\n• Lawlor v. Nat'l Screen Serv. Corp., 349 U.S. 322, 327–28 (1955)（判定请求权排除只适用于第一案提起时已经存在的请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-039"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-175","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":175,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A patient domiciled in State A sued a surgeon domiciled in State B in a federal court in State A, alleging claims for malpractice. The surgeon moved to dismiss the action for lack of personal jurisdiction. The court denied the motion and set discovery cutoff and trial dates. The surgeon has appealed the denial of the motion. Should the appellate court hear the merits of the surgeon's appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the appellate court lacks jurisdiction over the appeal.","B":"No, because the district court's decision on jurisdiction is final.","C":"Yes, because a contrary appellate decision could terminate the action.","D":"Yes, because the surgeon's personal-jurisdiction challenge raises a constitutional question."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment 28 U.S.C. § 1291 limits appellate jurisdiction to appeals from a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the district court to do but enforce the judgment. This rule promotes judicial efficiency and avoids undue delay caused by multiple appeals. Therefore, absent specific exceptions, an appeal must be dismissed for lack of jurisdiction if no final judgment has been entered. Here, the district court's denial of the surgeon's motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction is not a final judgment. That is because the denial did not fully resolve the dispute by deciding whether the surgeon is liable for malpractice. And since a denial of this motion is not excepted from the final-judgment rule, the appellate court lacks jurisdiction to hear the merits of the surgeon's appeal. (Choice B) The district court's decision on jurisdiction is not final and can be challenged in an appeal from a final judgment. (Choice C) Although a contrary appellate decision—ie, a decision that personal jurisdiction is lacking—could terminate the action, an appeal at this stage is premature because the district court has not issued a final judgment.\n\n(Choice D) The fact that the surgeon's personal-jurisdiction challenge raises a constitutional question does not give the appellate court jurisdiction to hear the appeal at this time. Instead, the appellate court will decide all contested issues—constitutional and otherwise—in one appeal after a final judgment is issued. Educational objective: The final-judgment rule bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits and simply requires the district court to enforce the judgment. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).","rule":"The final-judgment rule bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits and simply requires the district court to enforce the judgment.","irac":{"issue":"A patient domiciled in State A sued a surgeon domiciled in State B in a federal court in State A, alleging claims for malpractice. The surgeon moved to dismiss the action for lack of personal jurisdiction. The court denied the motion and set discovery cutoff and trial dates. The surgeon has appealed the denial of the motion. Should the appellate court hear the merits of the surgeon's appeal?","rule":"The final-judgment rule bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits and simply requires the district court to enforce the judgment.","analysis":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment 28 U.S.C. § 1291 limits appellate jurisdiction to appeals from a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the district court to do but enforce the judgment. This rule promotes judicial efficiency and avoids undue delay caused by multiple appeals. Therefore, absent specific exceptions, an appeal must be dismissed for lack of jurisdiction if no final judgment has been entered. Here, the district court's denial of the surgeon's motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction is not a final judgment. That is because the denial did not fully resolve the dispute by deciding whether the surgeon is liable for malpractice. And since a denial of this motion is not excepted from the final-judgment rule, the appellate court lacks jurisdiction to hear the merits of the surgeon's appeal. (Choice B) The district court's decision on jurisdiction is not final and can be challenged in an appeal from a final judgment. (Choice C) Although a contrary appellate decision—ie, a decision that personal jurisdiction is lacking—could terminate the action, an appeal at this stage is premature because the district court has not issued a final judgment.\n\n(Choice D) The fact that the surgeon's personal-jurisdiction challenge raises a constitutional question does not give the appellate court jurisdiction to hear the appeal at this time. Instead, the appellate court will decide all contested issues—constitutional and otherwise—in one appeal after a final judgment is issued. Educational objective: The final-judgment rule bars federal appellate courts from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves the dispute on the merits and simply requires the district court to enforce the judgment. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[529],"sourceAnswerPages":[530,531],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名住所位于 A 州的患者在 A 州联邦法院起诉一名住所位于 B 州的外科医生，提出医疗过失请求。外科医生以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回诉讼。法院驳回动议，并规定证据开示截止日和审判日期。外科医生已对驳回动议的裁定提起上诉。上诉法院是否应审理外科医生上诉的实体？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为上诉法院对该上诉缺乏管辖权。","B":"不应，因为地区法院关于管辖权的决定是终局决定。","C":"应当，因为上诉法院作出相反决定可能终结诉讼。","D":"应当，因为外科医生对对人管辖权的质疑提出了宪法问题。"},"explanationZh":"终局判决规则：\n\n规则｜只能在终局判决后上诉\n• 涉及多项请求或多名当事人时，如果法院明确认定没有正当理由拖延，可以就少于全部请求或当事人登记终局判决\n\n例外｜涉及下列事项的中间上诉*：\n• 禁令（批准或驳回）\n• 地区法院核准\n• 集体诉讼核准\n• 指定接管人\n• 海事案件\n• 附带命令原则\n• 破产案件（某些命令）\n• 强制令（申请签发）\n• 专利侵权命令（只剩账目核算）\n\n记忆口诀：在特定情形下，上诉可以提前提出。\n\n*中间上诉是对终局判决登记前作出的审判法院裁定提起上诉。\n\n28 U.S.C. § 1291 将上诉管辖权限于对终局判决提起的上诉。终局判决是完全解决争议实体，使地区法院除执行判决外别无事项的决定。该规则促进司法效率，并避免多次上诉造成不当拖延。因此，除非适用特定例外，未登记终局判决时，上诉必须因缺乏管辖权而被驳回。\n\n本题地区法院驳回外科医生以缺乏对人管辖权为由提出的驳回动议，并非终局判决，因为该裁定没有通过决定外科医生是否应对医疗过失负责而完全解决争议。而且，驳回这种动议不属于终局判决规则的例外。因此，上诉法院缺乏管辖权审理外科医生上诉的实体。\n\n（选项 B）地区法院关于管辖权的决定并非终局决定，可以在对终局判决提起的上诉中予以质疑。\n\n（选项 C）上诉法院作出相反决定，即认定缺乏对人管辖权，虽然可能终结诉讼，但现阶段上诉为时过早，因为地区法院尚未登记终局判决。\n\n（选项 D）外科医生对对人管辖权的质疑提出宪法问题这一事实，不会赋予上诉法院现阶段审理上诉的管辖权。相反，上诉法院将在终局判决登记后的一次上诉中，裁定所有争议问题，包括宪法问题及其他问题。\n\n学习目标：终局判决规则禁止联邦上诉法院在地区法院登记终局判决前审理上诉。终局判决是完全解决争议实体，只需地区法院执行判决的决定。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291（终局判决规则）。\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989)（定义终局判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-176","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":176,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A 50-year-old man was set to marry a 25-year-old woman when the woman called off the wedding two days before it was set to take place. The man had spent over $200,000 on the lavish ceremony, reception, and honeymoon. He sued the woman in federal court to recover half of this amount. During voir dire, the man's attorney attempted to use all three of his peremptory challenges to exclude three potential jurors who were 25 years old. The woman's attorney objected. The man's attorney explained that he was striking the three potential jurors based on their age. If the court believes the attorney's justification for the peremptory challenges, will it strike those potential jurors?","choices":{"A":"No, because civil litigants are entitled to only one peremptory challenge.","B":"No, because the age of the potential jurors does not reveal any bias or lack of impartiality.","C":"Yes, because peremptory challenges can be used for any reason.","D":"Yes, because peremptory challenges can be used to strike potential jurors based on their age."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Challenges to potential jurors\n\n(FRCP 47)\n\nNumber Basis for disqualification allowed\n\nPeremptory 3 per party* • Any basis other than race, ethnicity, or gender\n• No explanation needed For cause Unlimited • For bias or lack of impartiality—eg: – relationship with party – financial stake in party FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*When a civil action involves multiple plaintiffs or defendants, the court may grant each side (1) three peremptory challenges or (2) more than three peremptory challenges that each side's parties exercise separately or jointly. Voir dire is the stage in a jury trial when the court, parties, or attorneys question potential jurors about their backgrounds and potential biases. During this process, each party may strike potential jurors from serving on the jury through:\n• three peremptory challenges, which can be used to strike a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, or gender) without explanation (Choices A & C) and\n\n• unlimited challenges for cause, which can be used to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality (Choice B).\n\nHere, the man's attorney attempted to use all of his peremptory challenges to exclude three potential jurors. He explained that he was striking the jurors based on their age. If the court believes this justification—ie, there is no indication that the challenges are based on race, ethnicity, or gender—then the court will strike the three potential jurors. Educational objective: During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges to strike a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) without an explanation and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(b) (peremptory challenges).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870 (jury challenges).\n\n• J.E.B. v. Alabama, 511 U.S. 127, 143 (1994) (explaining that peremptory challenges may be used to remove potential jurors based on their age).","rule":"During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges to strike a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) without an explanation and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality.","irac":{"issue":"A 50-year-old man was set to marry a 25-year-old woman when the woman called off the wedding two days before it was set to take place. The man had spent over $200,000 on the lavish ceremony, reception, and honeymoon. He sued the woman in federal court to recover half of this amount. During voir dire, the man's attorney attempted to use all three of his peremptory challenges to exclude three potential jurors who were 25 years old. The woman's attorney objected. The man's attorney explained that he was striking the three potential jurors based on their age. If the court believes the attorney's justification for the peremptory challenges, will it strike those potential jurors?","rule":"During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges to strike a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) without an explanation and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality.","analysis":"Challenges to potential jurors\n\n(FRCP 47)\n\nNumber Basis for disqualification allowed\n\nPeremptory 3 per party* • Any basis other than race, ethnicity, or gender\n• No explanation needed For cause Unlimited • For bias or lack of impartiality—eg: – relationship with party – financial stake in party FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*When a civil action involves multiple plaintiffs or defendants, the court may grant each side (1) three peremptory challenges or (2) more than three peremptory challenges that each side's parties exercise separately or jointly. Voir dire is the stage in a jury trial when the court, parties, or attorneys question potential jurors about their backgrounds and potential biases. During this process, each party may strike potential jurors from serving on the jury through:\n• three peremptory challenges, which can be used to strike a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, or gender) without explanation (Choices A & C) and\n\n• unlimited challenges for cause, which can be used to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality (Choice B).\n\nHere, the man's attorney attempted to use all of his peremptory challenges to exclude three potential jurors. He explained that he was striking the jurors based on their age. If the court believes this justification—ie, there is no indication that the challenges are based on race, ethnicity, or gender—then the court will strike the three potential jurors. Educational objective: During jury selection, each party is entitled to (1) three peremptory challenges to strike a potential juror for any reason (other than race, ethnicity, gender) without an explanation and (2) an unlimited number of challenges for cause to strike a potential juror for bias or lack of impartiality. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(b) (peremptory challenges).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870 (jury challenges).\n\n• J.E.B. v. Alabama, 511 U.S. 127, 143 (1994) (explaining that peremptory challenges may be used to remove potential jurors based on their age).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(b) (peremptory challenges).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870 (jury challenges).\n\n• J.E.B. v. Alabama, 511 U.S. 127, 143 (1994) (explaining that peremptory challenges may be used to remove potential jurors based on their age).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[532],"sourceAnswerPages":[533,534],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名 50 岁男子原定与一名 25 岁女子结婚，但女子在婚礼预定举行前两天取消婚礼。男子已经为豪华婚礼仪式、招待宴会和蜜月花费超过 200,000 美元。他在联邦法院起诉女子，请求返还该金额的一半。陪审员资格询问期间，男子的律师试图用完三次无因回避，排除三名年龄均为 25 岁的候选陪审员。女子的律师提出异议。男子的律师解释，他是基于三名候选陪审员的年龄将其排除。如果法院相信律师对无因回避理由的说明，法院会排除这些候选陪审员吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为民事诉讼当事人只能行使一次无因回避。","B":"不会，因为候选陪审员的年龄没有表明任何偏见或不能保持公正。","C":"会，因为无因回避可以基于任何理由使用。","D":"会，因为无因回避可以基于候选陪审员的年龄将其排除。"},"explanationZh":"对候选陪审员的回避（FRCP 47）：\n\n类型｜允许数量｜取消资格的依据\n无因回避｜每方 3 次*｜除种族、族裔或性别外的任何理由；无需说明\n有因回避｜不限次数｜存在偏见或不能保持公正，例如与一方有关系、在一方具有经济利益\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*民事诉讼有多名原告或被告时，法院可给予每一方：(1) 三次无因回避；或 (2) 三次以上由同一方各当事人分别或共同使用的无因回避。\n\n陪审员资格询问是陪审团审判中的一个阶段，法院、双方或律师在此阶段询问候选陪审员的背景和潜在偏见。在此过程中，每一方可通过下列方式排除候选陪审员：\n• 三次无因回避——可基于任何理由排除候选陪审员，无需说明，但不得基于种族、族裔或性别（选项 A、C）；以及\n• 不限次数的有因回避——可因候选陪审员存在偏见或不能保持公正而将其排除（选项 B）。\n\n本题男子的律师试图用完三次无因回避，排除三名候选陪审员。他解释说，回避基于陪审员的年龄。如果法院相信该理由，即没有迹象表明回避基于种族、族裔或性别，法院就会排除三名候选陪审员。\n\n学习目标：遴选陪审员时，每一方有权：(1) 行使三次无因回避，基于任何理由且无需说明，但不得基于种族、族裔或性别；以及 (2) 行使不限次数的有因回避，排除存在偏见或不能保持公正的候选陪审员。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 47(b)（无因回避）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1870（陪审员回避）。\n• J.E.B. v. Alabama, 511 U.S. 127, 143 (1994)（说明可以基于候选陪审员的年龄行使无因回避）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-177","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":177,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man who owned riverfront property sued an upstream factory in federal court for polluting the river, seeking injunctive relief and $250,000 in damages. The factory moved for summary judgment on the ground of res judicata (claim preclusion), arguing that the man had sued on and lost an identical claim one year before. The court denied the motion. The factory has asked its attorney's advice as to whether it may appeal the court's denial of summary judgment in order to avoid an expensive trial. What advice should the attorney give?","choices":{"A":"The factory may appeal if the appellate court finds that the case involves a controlling question of law upon which the courts are divided.","B":"The factory may appeal if the trial court certifies that there is no just reason for delay.","C":"The factory may not appeal because the denial of summary judgment is a collateral order.","D":"The factory may not appeal until after a trial on the merits or other disposition resulting in a final judgment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment A party may only appeal, absent limited exceptions, after the district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. This rule is designed to promote judicial efficiency and avoid unnecessary delays in the litigation process. Here, the court's denial of the factory's motion for summary judgment based on claim preclusion is not a final judgment. That is because the denial did not fully resolve the dispute on its merits by deciding whether the factory polluted the river and what relief (if any) to award the man. Therefore, the attorney should advise the factory that it may not appeal until after a trial on the merits or other disposition resulting in a final judgment. (Choice A) A district court can certify (ie, approve) an issue for appeal before a final judgment when (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal will materially advance the ultimate termination of litigation. Only then will an appellate court decide whether to hear the appeal. (Choice B) When an action involves multiple claims or parties (not seen here), a district court may enter final judgment as to fewer than all claims or parties if it expressly\n\ndetermines that there is no just reason for delay. A party may then appeal that judgment without waiting for a final judgment to be entered on all claims in the action. (Choice C) The denial of summary judgment is not a collateral order—ie, a district court order that conclusively resolves an important issue that is (1) separate from the merits of the claim and (2) effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. That is because an appellate court can easily (and routinely does) review the denial of summary judgment on appeal. Educational objective: Absent limited exceptions, an appellate court can only hear appeals from a final judgment— ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).\n\n• Ortiz v. Jordan, 562 U.S. 180, 184 (2011) (explaining that almost all orders denying summary judgment are interlocutory orders that do not qualify as final judgments subject to immediate appeal).","rule":"Absent limited exceptions, an appellate court can only hear appeals from a final judgment— ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits.","irac":{"issue":"A man who owned riverfront property sued an upstream factory in federal court for polluting the river, seeking injunctive relief and $250,000 in damages. The factory moved for summary judgment on the ground of res judicata (claim preclusion), arguing that the man had sued on and lost an identical claim one year before. The court denied the motion. The factory has asked its attorney's advice as to whether it may appeal the court's denial of summary judgment in order to avoid an expensive trial. What advice should the attorney give?","rule":"Absent limited exceptions, an appellate court can only hear appeals from a final judgment— ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits.","analysis":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment A party may only appeal, absent limited exceptions, after the district court has entered a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment. This rule is designed to promote judicial efficiency and avoid unnecessary delays in the litigation process. Here, the court's denial of the factory's motion for summary judgment based on claim preclusion is not a final judgment. That is because the denial did not fully resolve the dispute on its merits by deciding whether the factory polluted the river and what relief (if any) to award the man. Therefore, the attorney should advise the factory that it may not appeal until after a trial on the merits or other disposition resulting in a final judgment. (Choice A) A district court can certify (ie, approve) an issue for appeal before a final judgment when (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal will materially advance the ultimate termination of litigation. Only then will an appellate court decide whether to hear the appeal. (Choice B) When an action involves multiple claims or parties (not seen here), a district court may enter final judgment as to fewer than all claims or parties if it expressly\n\ndetermines that there is no just reason for delay. A party may then appeal that judgment without waiting for a final judgment to be entered on all claims in the action. (Choice C) The denial of summary judgment is not a collateral order—ie, a district court order that conclusively resolves an important issue that is (1) separate from the merits of the claim and (2) effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. That is because an appellate court can easily (and routinely does) review the denial of summary judgment on appeal. Educational objective: Absent limited exceptions, an appellate court can only hear appeals from a final judgment— ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).\n\n• Ortiz v. Jordan, 562 U.S. 180, 184 (2011) (explaining that almost all orders denying summary judgment are interlocutory orders that do not qualify as final judgments subject to immediate appeal).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989) (defining final judgment).\n\n• Ortiz v. Jordan, 562 U.S. 180, 184 (2011) (explaining that almost all orders denying summary judgment are interlocutory orders that do not qualify as final judgments subject to immediate appeal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[535],"sourceAnswerPages":[536,537],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名拥有河滨土地的男子在联邦法院起诉一家上游工厂污染河流，请求禁令救济和 250,000 美元损害赔偿。工厂依据既判力（请求权排除）申请简易判决，主张男子一年前曾就相同请求提起诉讼并败诉。法院驳回动议。为避免成本高昂的审判，工厂询问律师能否对驳回简易判决的裁定提起上诉。律师应提供什么建议？","choicesZh":{"A":"如果上诉法院认定案件涉及各法院意见分歧的控制性法律问题，工厂可以上诉。","B":"如果审判法院核准不存在拖延的正当理由，工厂可以上诉。","C":"工厂不能上诉，因为驳回简易判决属于附带命令。","D":"在实体审判或其他产生终局判决的处理完成前，工厂不能上诉。"},"explanationZh":"终局判决规则：\n\n规则｜只能在终局判决后上诉\n• 涉及多项请求或多名当事人时，如果法院明确认定没有正当理由拖延，可以就少于全部请求或当事人登记终局判决\n\n例外｜涉及下列事项的中间上诉*：\n• 禁令（批准或驳回）\n• 地区法院核准\n• 集体诉讼核准\n• 指定接管人\n• 海事案件\n• 附带命令原则\n• 破产案件（某些命令）\n• 强制令（申请签发）\n• 专利侵权命令（只剩账目核算）\n\n记忆口诀：在特定情形下，上诉可以提前提出。\n\n*中间上诉是对终局判决登记前作出的审判法院裁定提起上诉。\n\n除有限例外外，一方只有在地区法院登记终局判决后才能上诉。终局判决是完全解决争议实体，使法院除执行判决外别无事项的决定。该规则旨在促进司法效率，避免诉讼程序发生不必要的拖延。\n\n本题法院驳回工厂依据请求权排除提出的简易判决动议，并非终局判决，因为该裁定没有通过决定工厂是否污染河流以及应向男子判给何种救济（如有），来完全解决争议实体。因此，律师应告知工厂，在实体审判或其他产生终局判决的处理完成前，不能提起上诉。\n\n（选项 A）在以下情况下，地区法院可以于终局判决前核准一个问题上诉：(1) 对该控制性法律问题存在重大意见分歧；且 (2) 上诉将实质推进诉讼的最终结束。只有此后，上诉法院才会决定是否审理上诉。\n\n（选项 B）诉讼涉及多项请求或多名当事人时（本题并非如此），如果地区法院明确认定没有拖延的正当理由，可以就少于全部请求或当事人登记终局判决。随后，一方无需等待诉讼中所有请求均登记终局判决，即可对该判决上诉。\n\n（选项 C）驳回简易判决不属于附带命令。附带命令是地区法院终局性地解决一项重要问题的命令，而且该问题：(1) 与请求实体相分离；并且 (2) 无法在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查。驳回简易判决不符合该定义，因为上诉法院可以在上诉中轻易审查该裁定，实践中也经常如此。\n\n学习目标：除有限例外外，上诉法院只能审理对终局判决的上诉。终局判决是完全解决争议实体的决定。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291（终局判决规则）。\n• Lauro Lines s.r.l. v. Chasser, 490 U.S. 495, 497 (1989)（定义终局判决）。\n• Ortiz v. Jordan, 562 U.S. 180, 184 (2011)（说明几乎所有驳回简易判决的命令都是中间命令，不构成可立即上诉的终局判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-178","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":178,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"The parents of a deceased teenager sued the manufacturer of an energy drink in federal court, alleging that their teenager had a heart attack and died after ingesting the energy drink. The parents further alleged that the manufacturer knew the drink could result in a heart attack but failed to disclose this risk in violation of federal law. The manufacturer denied the parents' allegations. Both parties demanded a jury trial. At the conclusion of the trial, the judge instructed the jury to provide written answers to specific questions about each factual issue but to refrain from finding in favor of a party. The manufacturer objected to this procedure. Is this procedure permissible?","choices":{"A":"No, because the jury must apply the law to its findings of fact and return a verdict in favor of a party.","B":"No, because this procedure violates the manufacturer's constitutional right to a jury trial.","C":"Yes, because the judge may apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with the jury's answers.","D":"Yes, because the judge may ask for the jury's advice on factual issues before deciding the case herself."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Types of jury verdicts\n\n(FRCP 49)\n\nGeneral verdict • Jury finds in favor of, and awards any damages to, particular party\n• No statement on jury's findings of fact General verdict with • Jury finds in favor of, and awards any damages to, answers particular party\n• Jury answers questions on specified factual issues Special verdict • Jury answers questions on specified factual issues\n• Judge applies law to jury's answers & enters appropriate judgment FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\nAt the conclusion of a jury trial, the jury will issue a verdict that determines the factual issues in the case. There are three types of verdicts—general verdicts (most common), general verdicts with answers, and special verdicts—and the judge has complete discretion to choose which verdict to use. A special verdict requires that:\n• the jury answer questions in writing on specified factual issues and\n• the judge apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with those answers. The purposes of a special verdict are to prevent the jury's view of the correct result from interfering with its factual findings and to identify the bases for the jury's decision. Therefore, the judge permissibly instructed the jury to provide written answers to specific questions about each factual issue but to refrain from finding in favor of a party (Choice A). (Choice B) The Seventh Amendment guarantees civil litigants the right to a jury trial when the amount in controversy exceeds $20 and a party makes a timely jury trial demand. A special verdict does not violate this constitutional right because the jury still determines the factual issues in the case. (Choice D) The judge cannot ask for the jury's advice on factual issues before deciding the case because the jury is the decider of fact. Once the jury has decided the issues of fact, the judge may apply the law and enter an appropriate judgment. Educational objective: In a jury trial, the judge has complete discretion to decide which verdict to use—general verdict, general verdict with answers, or special verdict. A special verdict requires that (1)\n\nthe jury answer questions in writing on specified factual issues and (2) the judge apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with those answers. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(a) (special verdicts).","rule":"In a jury trial, the judge has complete discretion to decide which verdict to use—general verdict, general verdict with answers, or special verdict. A special verdict requires that (1)\n\nthe jury answer questions in writing on specified factual issues and (2) the judge apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with those answers.","irac":{"issue":"The parents of a deceased teenager sued the manufacturer of an energy drink in federal court, alleging that their teenager had a heart attack and died after ingesting the energy drink. The parents further alleged that the manufacturer knew the drink could result in a heart attack but failed to disclose this risk in violation of federal law. The manufacturer denied the parents' allegations. Both parties demanded a jury trial. At the conclusion of the trial, the judge instructed the jury to provide written answers to specific questions about each factual issue but to refrain from finding in favor of a party. The manufacturer objected to this procedure. Is this procedure permissible?","rule":"In a jury trial, the judge has complete discretion to decide which verdict to use—general verdict, general verdict with answers, or special verdict. A special verdict requires that (1)\n\nthe jury answer questions in writing on specified factual issues and (2) the judge apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with those answers.","analysis":"Types of jury verdicts\n\n(FRCP 49)\n\nGeneral verdict • Jury finds in favor of, and awards any damages to, particular party\n• No statement on jury's findings of fact General verdict with • Jury finds in favor of, and awards any damages to, answers particular party\n• Jury answers questions on specified factual issues Special verdict • Jury answers questions on specified factual issues\n• Judge applies law to jury's answers & enters appropriate judgment FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\nAt the conclusion of a jury trial, the jury will issue a verdict that determines the factual issues in the case. There are three types of verdicts—general verdicts (most common), general verdicts with answers, and special verdicts—and the judge has complete discretion to choose which verdict to use. A special verdict requires that:\n• the jury answer questions in writing on specified factual issues and\n• the judge apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with those answers. The purposes of a special verdict are to prevent the jury's view of the correct result from interfering with its factual findings and to identify the bases for the jury's decision. Therefore, the judge permissibly instructed the jury to provide written answers to specific questions about each factual issue but to refrain from finding in favor of a party (Choice A). (Choice B) The Seventh Amendment guarantees civil litigants the right to a jury trial when the amount in controversy exceeds $20 and a party makes a timely jury trial demand. A special verdict does not violate this constitutional right because the jury still determines the factual issues in the case. (Choice D) The judge cannot ask for the jury's advice on factual issues before deciding the case because the jury is the decider of fact. Once the jury has decided the issues of fact, the judge may apply the law and enter an appropriate judgment. Educational objective: In a jury trial, the judge has complete discretion to decide which verdict to use—general verdict, general verdict with answers, or special verdict. A special verdict requires that (1)\n\nthe jury answer questions in writing on specified factual issues and (2) the judge apply the law and enter a judgment consistent with those answers. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(a) (special verdicts).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(a) (special verdicts).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[538],"sourceAnswerPages":[539,540],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名已故青少年的父母在联邦法院起诉一家能量饮料制造商，主张孩子饮用该能量饮料后心脏病发作并死亡。父母还主张，制造商知道该饮料可能导致心脏病发作，却违反联邦法未披露该风险。制造商否认父母的指控。双方均要求陪审团审判。审判结束时，法官指示陪审团就每个事实问题回答特定书面问题，但不要作出有利于任何一方的裁决。制造商反对这一程序。该程序是否允许？","choicesZh":{"A":"不允许，因为陪审团必须将法律适用于其事实认定，并作出有利于一方的裁决。","B":"不允许，因为该程序侵犯制造商的宪法陪审团审判权。","C":"允许，因为法官可以适用法律，并依照陪审团答案登记判决。","D":"允许，因为法官可以先就事实问题征求陪审团意见，再自行裁判案件。"},"explanationZh":"陪审团裁决类型（FRCP 49）：\n\n一般裁决：\n• 陪审团判特定一方胜诉，并向其判给任何损害赔偿；\n• 不说明陪审团的事实认定。\n\n附带问题答案的一般裁决：\n• 陪审团判特定一方胜诉，并向其判给任何损害赔偿；\n• 陪审团回答关于特定事实问题的问题。\n\n特别裁决：\n• 陪审团回答关于特定事实问题的问题；\n• 法官将法律适用于陪审团答案，并登记适当判决。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n陪审团审判结束时，陪审团将作出裁决，决定案件的事实问题。裁决有三种：一般裁决（最常见）、附带问题答案的一般裁决和特别裁决；法官有完全裁量权决定采用哪一种。\n\n特别裁决要求：\n• 陪审团就特定事实问题作出书面回答；并且\n• 法官将法律适用于这些答案，并依照答案登记判决。\n\n特别裁决的目的，是防止陪审团对正确结果的看法干扰其事实认定，并明确陪审团决定的依据。因此，法官可以指示陪审团就每个事实问题回答特定书面问题，但不要作出有利于任何一方的裁决（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）第七修正案保障民事诉讼当事人在争议金额超过 20 美元且一方及时要求陪审团审判时，享有陪审团审判权。特别裁决不侵犯该宪法权利，因为案件的事实问题仍由陪审团裁定。\n\n（选项 D）法官不能先就事实问题征求陪审团意见再自行裁判，因为陪审团才是事实裁判者。陪审团裁定事实问题后，法官可以适用法律并登记适当判决。\n\n学习目标：在陪审团审判中，法官有完全裁量权决定采用一般裁决、附带问题答案的一般裁决或特别裁决。特别裁决要求：(1) 陪审团就特定事实问题作出书面回答；并且 (2) 法官将法律适用于这些答案并依其登记判决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(a)（特别裁决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-179","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":179,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A patent holder brought a patent infringement action, which involves triable jury issues, in federal court against a licensee of the patent. The patent holder believed that a jury would be more sympathetic to his claims than a judge and asked his lawyer to obtain a jury trial. What should the lawyer do to secure the patent holder's right to a jury trial?","choices":{"A":"File and serve a complaint that includes a jury trial demand.","B":"File and serve a jury trial demand at the close of discovery.","C":"File and serve a jury trial demand within 30 days after the close of the pleadings.","D":"Make a jury trial demand at the initial pretrial conference."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 38, a party may demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by:\n• serving the other parties with a written jury trial demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to that issue is served and\n\n• filing the jury trial demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.*\n\nThe demand can be included in a pleading or set forth in a separate document. And the right to a jury trial is waived if the above requirements are not met. The lawyer here can therefore secure a jury trial by including the jury trial demand in a filed and served complaint—ie, the pleading that starts a case. *A proper jury trial demand may be withdrawn later, but only with the other parties' consent. (Choice B) Discovery is a pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties are compelled to disclose information related to the case. Since this is a lengthy process that often continues more than 14 days after the last pleading is served, a jury trial demand filed and served at the close of discovery would likely be untimely. (Choice C) A jury trial demand must be served within 14 (not 30) days after the last pleading directed to the triable issue is served. And the demand would be even more untimely if the last pleading concerning the triable issue was served before pleadings closed. (Choice D) An initial pretrial conference is typically held several weeks after the pleadings have closed, so a jury trial demand made at that conference would likely be untimely. Educational objective: A party may secure the right to a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury trial demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the jury trial demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38 (jury trial demand).","rule":"A party may secure the right to a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury trial demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the jury trial demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.","irac":{"issue":"A patent holder brought a patent infringement action, which involves triable jury issues, in federal court against a licensee of the patent. The patent holder believed that a jury would be more sympathetic to his claims than a judge and asked his lawyer to obtain a jury trial. What should the lawyer do to secure the patent holder's right to a jury trial?","rule":"A party may secure the right to a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury trial demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the jury trial demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 38, a party may demand a jury trial on any triable jury issue by:\n• serving the other parties with a written jury trial demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to that issue is served and\n\n• filing the jury trial demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand.*\n\nThe demand can be included in a pleading or set forth in a separate document. And the right to a jury trial is waived if the above requirements are not met. The lawyer here can therefore secure a jury trial by including the jury trial demand in a filed and served complaint—ie, the pleading that starts a case. *A proper jury trial demand may be withdrawn later, but only with the other parties' consent. (Choice B) Discovery is a pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties are compelled to disclose information related to the case. Since this is a lengthy process that often continues more than 14 days after the last pleading is served, a jury trial demand filed and served at the close of discovery would likely be untimely. (Choice C) A jury trial demand must be served within 14 (not 30) days after the last pleading directed to the triable issue is served. And the demand would be even more untimely if the last pleading concerning the triable issue was served before pleadings closed. (Choice D) An initial pretrial conference is typically held several weeks after the pleadings have closed, so a jury trial demand made at that conference would likely be untimely. Educational objective: A party may secure the right to a jury trial on any triable jury issue by (1) serving the other parties with a written jury trial demand no later than 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue is served and (2) filing the jury trial demand with the court within a reasonable time after service of the demand. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38 (jury trial demand).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38 (jury trial demand).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[541],"sourceAnswerPages":[542],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名专利权人在联邦法院对专利被许可人提起专利侵权诉讼，其中涉及可以由陪审团审理的问题。专利权人认为陪审团会比法官更同情其请求，因而要求律师取得陪审团审判。律师应如何保障专利权人的陪审团审判权？","choicesZh":{"A":"提交并送达一份包含陪审团审判请求的起诉状。","B":"在证据开示结束时提交并送达陪审团审判请求。","C":"在诉状交换结束后 30 天内提交并送达陪审团审判请求。","D":"在首次审前会议上提出陪审团审判请求。"},"explanationZh":"根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》38，一方可以通过以下方式，就任何可以由陪审团审理的问题要求陪审团审判：\n• 最迟在针对该问题的最后一份诉状送达后 14 天内，向其他各方送达书面陪审团审判请求；并且\n• 在请求送达后的合理时间内向法院提交陪审团审判请求。*\n\n该请求可以包含于诉状中，也可以通过单独文件提出。如果未满足上述要求，陪审团审判权即被放弃。因此，本题律师可以在提交并送达的起诉状，即启动案件的诉状中加入陪审团审判请求，从而保障陪审团审判。\n\n*适当提出的陪审团审判请求以后可以撤回，但必须取得其他各方同意。\n\n（选项 B）证据开示是诉讼的审前阶段，双方被要求披露与案件有关的信息。该过程耗时较长，常常持续到最后一份诉状送达超过 14 天以后，所以在证据开示结束时提交并送达陪审团审判请求很可能逾期。\n\n（选项 C）陪审团审判请求必须在针对可审问题的最后一份诉状送达后 14 天而非 30 天内送达。如果涉及可审问题的最后一份诉状在诉状交换结束前就已送达，到诉状交换结束后才提出请求则逾期更久。\n\n（选项 D）首次审前会议通常在诉状交换结束数周后举行，所以到该会议上才提出陪审团审判请求很可能逾期。\n\n学习目标：一方可以通过以下方式保障其对任何可由陪审团审理问题的陪审团审判权：(1) 最迟在针对该问题的最后一份诉状送达后 14 天内，向其他各方送达书面陪审团审判请求；并且 (2) 在请求送达后的合理时间内向法院提交。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 38（陪审团审判请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-056"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-180","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":180,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A company incorporated and headquartered in State A sued a plumber domiciled in State B in a federal court in State A, alleging that the plumber had negligently installed pipes in a manner that resulted in $250,000 in damage to the company's headquarters building. In response to the complaint, the plumber filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. The court denied the motion. Thereafter, the plumber did not file an answer or any other response to the company's action. Sixty days after the court's order denying the motion to dismiss, the company asked the clerk to enter the plumber's default, and the clerk did so. The company applied to the court for the entry of a default judgment and notified the plumber three days before the default-judgment hearing. After an ex parte hearing in which the court received evidence on the damages amount, the court entered a default judgment for the full amount sought. Ten days later, the plumber filed a motion to set aside the default judgment. Is the court likely to grant the plumber's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court could fix the amount of damages without hearing the plumber's evidence.","B":"No, because the plumber failed to defend against the company's action for default judgment.","C":"Yes, because the plumber was not given adequate notice of the hearing on the company's application for the entry of a default judgment.","D":"Yes, because the State A federal court lacked personal jurisdiction over the plumber as a State B citizen."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 55, there are two steps for securing a default judgment—ie, a judgment entered when the defendant has failed to timely serve an answer. The first step requires that the court clerk enter a default on the record once it is shown that the defendant failed to timely serve an answer—due within 14 days after an FRCP 12 motion (eg, challenging personal jurisdiction) is denied. The second step requires that a default judgment be entered by the clerk or the court. The court may enter a default judgment even if:\n• the plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain\n• the defendant is a minor or legally incompetent or\n• the defendant has appeared by filing a motion (as seen here) or otherwise acting before the court. But if the defendant has appeared, the defendant must be served with written notice of the plaintiff's application for default judgment at least seven days before a hearing on the matter. Here, the company notified the plumber of its application only three days before the hearing. Therefore, the entry of default judgment was improper, and the plumber's motion to set aside that judgment will likely be granted. (Choice A) The court could have fixed the amount of damages—which could be made certain by calculation—without hearing the plumber's evidence had he received proper notice. (Choice B) Although the plumber failed to defend against the company's action for default judgment, he had made an appearance by filing a motion to dismiss. As a result, he was entitled to seven days' notice. (Choice D) Although the plumber is a State B citizen, the State A federal court had personal jurisdiction because the plumber's minimum contacts with State A—working on the company's building—gave rise to the claim. Educational objective: To obtain a default judgment against a defendant who has appeared before the court, the plaintiff must serve the defendant with written notice of the default-judgment application at least seven days before a hearing on the matter. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).","rule":"To obtain a default judgment against a defendant who has appeared before the court, the plaintiff must serve the defendant with written notice of the default-judgment application at least seven days before a hearing on the matter.","irac":{"issue":"A company incorporated and headquartered in State A sued a plumber domiciled in State B in a federal court in State A, alleging that the plumber had negligently installed pipes in a manner that resulted in $250,000 in damage to the company's headquarters building. In response to the complaint, the plumber filed a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction. The court denied the motion. Thereafter, the plumber did not file an answer or any other response to the company's action. Sixty days after the court's order denying the motion to dismiss, the company asked the clerk to enter the plumber's default, and the clerk did so. The company applied to the court for the entry of a default judgment and notified the plumber three days before the default-judgment hearing. After an ex parte hearing in which the court received evidence on the damages amount, the court entered a default judgment for the full amount sought. Ten days later, the plumber filed a motion to set aside the default judgment. Is the court likely to grant the plumber's motion?","rule":"To obtain a default judgment against a defendant who has appeared before the court, the plaintiff must serve the defendant with written notice of the default-judgment application at least seven days before a hearing on the matter.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 55, there are two steps for securing a default judgment—ie, a judgment entered when the defendant has failed to timely serve an answer. The first step requires that the court clerk enter a default on the record once it is shown that the defendant failed to timely serve an answer—due within 14 days after an FRCP 12 motion (eg, challenging personal jurisdiction) is denied. The second step requires that a default judgment be entered by the clerk or the court. The court may enter a default judgment even if:\n• the plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain\n• the defendant is a minor or legally incompetent or\n• the defendant has appeared by filing a motion (as seen here) or otherwise acting before the court. But if the defendant has appeared, the defendant must be served with written notice of the plaintiff's application for default judgment at least seven days before a hearing on the matter. Here, the company notified the plumber of its application only three days before the hearing. Therefore, the entry of default judgment was improper, and the plumber's motion to set aside that judgment will likely be granted. (Choice A) The court could have fixed the amount of damages—which could be made certain by calculation—without hearing the plumber's evidence had he received proper notice. (Choice B) Although the plumber failed to defend against the company's action for default judgment, he had made an appearance by filing a motion to dismiss. As a result, he was entitled to seven days' notice. (Choice D) Although the plumber is a State B citizen, the State A federal court had personal jurisdiction because the plumber's minimum contacts with State A—working on the company's building—gave rise to the claim. Educational objective: To obtain a default judgment against a defendant who has appeared before the court, the plaintiff must serve the defendant with written notice of the default-judgment application at least seven days before a hearing on the matter. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[544],"sourceAnswerPages":[545],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且总部位于 A 州的公司，在 A 州联邦法院起诉一名住所位于 B 州的水管工，主张水管工安装管道时存在过失，给公司总部大楼造成 250,000 美元损失。水管工针对起诉状，以缺乏对人管辖权为由提出驳回动议。法院驳回动议。此后，水管工没有提交答辩状，也没有对公司诉讼作出任何其他回应。法院发布驳回动议命令六十天后，公司请求书记员登记水管工缺席，书记员予以登记。公司向法院申请登记缺席判决，并在缺席判决听证前三天通知水管工。法院举行单方听证，接收有关损害金额的证据后，按公司请求的全部金额登记缺席判决。十天后，水管工申请撤销缺席判决。法院可能批准水管工的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法院无需听取水管工的证据，即可确定损害金额。","B":"不会，因为水管工未对公司的缺席判决诉讼进行抗辩。","C":"会，因为水管工没有得到公司申请登记缺席判决听证的充分通知。","D":"会，因为水管工是 B 州公民，所以 A 州联邦法院对其缺乏对人管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）55，取得缺席判决，即被告未及时送达答辩状时登记的判决，需要两个步骤。\n\n第一步，被告未及时送达答辩状得到证明后，法院书记员必须将缺席登记于案卷记录。FRCP 12 动议（例如质疑对人管辖权的动议）被驳回后，答辩状须在 14 天内提交。\n\n第二步，由书记员或法院登记缺席判决。即使存在下列情形，法院仍可登记缺席判决：\n• 原告请求的金额并非确定金额；\n• 被告是未成年人或法律上无行为能力者；或\n• 被告通过提交动议（本题即如此）或以其他方式在法院采取行动而出庭。\n\n但是，被告已经出庭时，必须在听证前至少七天，向被告送达原告缺席判决申请的书面通知。\n\n本题公司只在听证前三天将其申请通知水管工。因此，缺席判决登记不当，水管工撤销该判决的动议很可能获准。\n\n（选项 A）如果水管工获得适当通知，法院无需听取其证据，也可以确定可通过计算确定的损害金额。\n\n（选项 B）水管工虽未对公司的缺席判决诉讼进行抗辩，但他已通过提出驳回动议而出庭。因此，他有权获得七天通知。\n\n（选项 D）水管工虽然是 B 州公民，但 A 州联邦法院拥有对人管辖权，因为水管工与 A 州的最低限度联系，即在公司大楼施工，产生了本案请求。\n\n学习目标：要对已经在法院出庭的被告取得缺席判决，原告必须在听证前至少七天，向被告送达缺席判决申请的书面通知。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55（缺席判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-058"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-181","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":181,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A man brought a diversity action against a woman in a federal court in State B for conversion. The man claimed that the woman stole several historic artifacts from his home in State A and sold them to a pawn shop in State B. State A law requires that conversion be proven by a preponderance of the evidence, and its choice-of-law rules for all tort claims provide that a court must apply the law of the state where the injury occurred. State B law requires that conversion be proven by clear and convincing evidence, and its choice-of-law rules provide that, in conversion actions, a court must apply the law of the state where the property was taken. What body of law governs the burden of proof in this action?","choices":{"A":"Federal common law.","B":"Federal statutory law.","C":"State A law.","D":"State B law."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Examples of procedural & substantive issues\n\nProcedural • Filing deadlines\n• Court rules & procedures (processes & procedures)\n• Discovery practices\n• Rules of evidence Substantive • Elements of claim or defense\n• Burdens of proof (legal rights & duties)\n• Statutes of limitations When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction (as seen here), the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. Certain issues are clearly substantive, including the elements of a claim or defense, the statute of limitations, and the applicable burden of proof. As a result, state law—not federal statutory or common law—governs the burden of proof in the man's conversion action against the woman (Choices A & B). However, the federal court must still determine which state's law governs by applying the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits. Here, the federal court sits in State B, so it must apply State B's choice-of-law rules. Those rules provide that a court must apply the law of the state where the property was taken. Since the historic artifacts were allegedly stolen in State A, State A's law governs the burden of proof in this action (Choice D). Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. References\n• Bank of Am. Nat'l Tr. & Sav. Ass'n v. Parnell, 352 U.S. 29, 34 (1956) (holding that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to the substantive issue of burden of proof).\n\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits).","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits.","irac":{"issue":"A man brought a diversity action against a woman in a federal court in State B for conversion. The man claimed that the woman stole several historic artifacts from his home in State A and sold them to a pawn shop in State B. State A law requires that conversion be proven by a preponderance of the evidence, and its choice-of-law rules for all tort claims provide that a court must apply the law of the state where the injury occurred. State B law requires that conversion be proven by clear and convincing evidence, and its choice-of-law rules provide that, in conversion actions, a court must apply the law of the state where the property was taken. What body of law governs the burden of proof in this action?","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits.","analysis":"Examples of procedural & substantive issues\n\nProcedural • Filing deadlines\n• Court rules & procedures (processes & procedures)\n• Discovery practices\n• Rules of evidence Substantive • Elements of claim or defense\n• Burdens of proof (legal rights & duties)\n• Statutes of limitations When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction (as seen here), the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. Certain issues are clearly substantive, including the elements of a claim or defense, the statute of limitations, and the applicable burden of proof. As a result, state law—not federal statutory or common law—governs the burden of proof in the man's conversion action against the woman (Choices A & B). However, the federal court must still determine which state's law governs by applying the choice-of-law rules of the state where the court sits. Here, the federal court sits in State B, so it must apply State B's choice-of-law rules. Those rules provide that a court must apply the law of the state where the property was taken. Since the historic artifacts were allegedly stolen in State A, State A's law governs the burden of proof in this action (Choice D). Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the court must apply federal law to procedural issues and state law to substantive issues. To determine which state's substantive law applies, a federal court must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. References\n• Bank of Am. Nat'l Tr. & Sav. Ass'n v. Parnell, 352 U.S. 29, 34 (1956) (holding that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to the substantive issue of burden of proof).\n\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Bank of Am. Nat'l Tr. & Sav. Ass'n v. Parnell, 352 U.S. 29, 34 (1956) (holding that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to the substantive issue of burden of proof).\n\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941) (explaining that a federal court sitting in diversity must apply the choice-of-law rules of the state in which it sits).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[547],"sourceAnswerPages":[548],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在 B 州联邦法院对一名女子提起多元州籍侵占诉讼。男子主张，女子从他位于 A 州的住宅偷走数件历史文物，并将其卖给 B 州的一家当铺。A 州法律要求以优势证据证明侵占，其对所有侵权请求适用的法律选择规则规定，法院必须适用伤害发生州的法律。B 州法律要求以清楚且令人信服的证据证明侵占，其法律选择规则规定，在侵占诉讼中，法院必须适用财产被取走州的法律。本诉的证明责任由哪一法律体系支配？","choicesZh":{"A":"联邦普通法。","B":"联邦制定法。","C":"A 州法律。","D":"B 州法律。"},"explanationZh":"程序性问题与实体性问题示例：\n\n程序性问题：\n• 提交期限\n• 法院规则与程序（流程与手续）\n• 证据开示惯例\n• 证据规则\n\n实体性问题：\n• 请求或抗辩的构成要件\n• 证明责任（法律权利与义务）\n• 诉讼时效\n\n联邦法院的事项管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权时（本题即如此），法院必须对程序性问题适用联邦法，对实体性问题适用州法。某些问题显然属于实体性问题，包括请求或抗辩的构成要件、诉讼时效以及适用的证明责任。因此，男子对女子所提侵占诉讼的证明责任由州法而不是联邦制定法或普通法支配（选项 A、B）。\n\n但是，联邦法院仍须适用其所在地州的法律选择规则，确定由哪个州的法律支配。本题联邦法院位于 B 州，所以必须适用 B 州的法律选择规则。该规则规定，法院必须适用财产被取走州的法律。据称历史文物在 A 州被盗，所以本诉证明责任由 A 州法律支配（选项 D）。\n\n学习目标：在联邦多元州籍诉讼中，法院必须对程序性问题适用联邦法，对实体性问题适用州法。为确定适用哪一州的实体法，联邦法院必须适用其所在地州的法律选择规则。\n\n参考资料：\n• Bank of Am. Nat'l Tr. & Sav. Ass'n v. Parnell, 352 U.S. 29, 34 (1956)（判定行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对证明责任这一实体问题适用州法）。\n• Klaxon Co. v. Stentor Elec. Mfg. Co., 313 U.S. 487, 496–97 (1941)（说明行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须适用其所在地州的法律选择规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-182","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":182,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"While a consumer was using a product at home in State A, the product malfunctioned. The consumer sued the manufacturer of the product for $100,000 in damages in a federal district court in State A on a products liability claim. The manufacturer wants to transfer venue to a federal district court in State B, the state in which it is incorporated and has its principal place of business. The manufacturer believes that State B's products liability laws afford greater protections to defendants. State A's choice-of-law rules require the application of State A's products liability laws. State B's choice-of-law rules require the application of State B's products liability laws. Is the manufacturer likely to succeed in having State B's products liability laws apply upon transfer?","choices":{"A":"No, because State A's products liability laws will apply upon transfer.","B":"No, because State B is an improper venue.","C":"Yes, because State A is an improper venue.","D":"Yes, because State B's choice-of-law rules require the application of State B's products liability laws."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A federal court with proper venue can transfer a case to another federal judicial district where venue is proper for the convenience of the parties and witnesses and in the interest of justice. Proper venue exists where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial portion of the events occurred or the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies.\n\nHere, venue is proper in State A because the product malfunction and injury (substantial events) occurred there (Choice C). Venue is also proper in State B since the manufacturer is incorporated and has its principal place of business (residence) there (Choice B). As a result, the case may be transferred from the federal district court in State A to the federal district court in State B—so long as it is convenient for the parties and witnesses and in the interest of justice. Upon transfer, the receiving court must apply the appropriate choice-of-law rules to determine which state's substantive laws govern the action. The original court's choice-of-law rules apply when (1) proper venue existed in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Here, the consumer's products liability action arises under diversity jurisdiction because she is a citizen of State A while the manufacturer is a citizen of State B, and the amount in controversy is $100,000. And since venue was proper in the State A federal district court, State A's choice-of-law rules apply. Under these rules, State A's products liability laws govern the action, so the manufacturer is unlikely to succeed in having State B's products liability laws apply upon transfer (Choice D). Educational objective: When venue is transferred between federal districts, the original court's choice-of-law rules apply when (1) proper venue existed in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction. References\n• Ferens v. John Deere Co., 494 U.S. 516, 531 (1990) (holding that the original court's choice-of-law rules apply upon transfer when venue was proper in the original court and the action arises under diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"When venue is transferred between federal districts, the original court's choice-of-law rules apply when (1) proper venue existed in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"While a consumer was using a product at home in State A, the product malfunctioned. The consumer sued the manufacturer of the product for $100,000 in damages in a federal district court in State A on a products liability claim. The manufacturer wants to transfer venue to a federal district court in State B, the state in which it is incorporated and has its principal place of business. The manufacturer believes that State B's products liability laws afford greater protections to defendants. State A's choice-of-law rules require the application of State A's products liability laws. State B's choice-of-law rules require the application of State B's products liability laws. Is the manufacturer likely to succeed in having State B's products liability laws apply upon transfer?","rule":"When venue is transferred between federal districts, the original court's choice-of-law rules apply when (1) proper venue existed in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction.","analysis":"A federal court with proper venue can transfer a case to another federal judicial district where venue is proper for the convenience of the parties and witnesses and in the interest of justice. Proper venue exists where:\n• any defendant resides, so long as all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial portion of the events occurred or the property at issue is located or\n\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction—but only if neither of the above provisions applies.\n\nHere, venue is proper in State A because the product malfunction and injury (substantial events) occurred there (Choice C). Venue is also proper in State B since the manufacturer is incorporated and has its principal place of business (residence) there (Choice B). As a result, the case may be transferred from the federal district court in State A to the federal district court in State B—so long as it is convenient for the parties and witnesses and in the interest of justice. Upon transfer, the receiving court must apply the appropriate choice-of-law rules to determine which state's substantive laws govern the action. The original court's choice-of-law rules apply when (1) proper venue existed in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction. Diversity jurisdiction exists when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Here, the consumer's products liability action arises under diversity jurisdiction because she is a citizen of State A while the manufacturer is a citizen of State B, and the amount in controversy is $100,000. And since venue was proper in the State A federal district court, State A's choice-of-law rules apply. Under these rules, State A's products liability laws govern the action, so the manufacturer is unlikely to succeed in having State B's products liability laws apply upon transfer (Choice D). Educational objective: When venue is transferred between federal districts, the original court's choice-of-law rules apply when (1) proper venue existed in the original court and (2) subject-matter jurisdiction arises under diversity jurisdiction. References\n• Ferens v. John Deere Co., 494 U.S. 516, 531 (1990) (holding that the original court's choice-of-law rules apply upon transfer when venue was proper in the original court and the action arises under diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Ferens v. John Deere Co., 494 U.S. 516, 531 (1990) (holding that the original court's choice-of-law rules apply upon transfer when venue was proper in the original court and the action arises under diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[550],"sourceAnswerPages":[551,552],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名消费者在 A 州家中使用一件产品时，产品发生故障。消费者在 A 州联邦地区法院以产品责任为由起诉产品制造商，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。制造商希望将审判地移送至 B 州联邦地区法院；制造商在 B 州注册成立，主要营业地也位于该州。制造商认为 B 州产品责任法给予被告更大保护。A 州的法律选择规则要求适用 A 州产品责任法，B 州的法律选择规则要求适用 B 州产品责任法。案件移送后，制造商可能成功使 B 州产品责任法得到适用吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为移送后将适用 A 州产品责任法。","B":"不会，因为 B 州是审判地不适当的地点。","C":"会，因为 A 州是审判地不适当的地点。","D":"会，因为 B 州法律选择规则要求适用 B 州产品责任法。"},"explanationZh":"审判地适当的联邦法院可以为方便双方和证人并符合司法利益，将案件移送至另一个审判地适当的联邦司法地区。以下地点的审判地适当：\n• 任何被告居住的地区，但所有被告必须均居住于同一州；\n• 构成诉讼基础的重大部分事件发生地，或争议财产所在地；或\n• 任何被告受法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在上述两项规定均不适用时。\n\n本题产品故障和伤害这些重大事件发生在 A 州，所以 A 州审判地适当（选项 C）。B 州审判地也适当，因为制造商在该州注册成立且主要营业地位于该州，即居住于该州（选项 B）。因此，只要方便双方和证人并符合司法利益，案件可以从 A 州联邦地区法院移送至 B 州联邦地区法院。\n\n移送后，受移送法院必须适用适当的法律选择规则，确定由哪一州的实体法支配诉讼。在以下情况下，适用原法院的法律选择规则：(1) 原法院的审判地适当；并且 (2) 事项管辖权源于多元州籍管辖权。\n\n相对双方是不同州的公民且争议金额超过 75,000 美元时，存在多元州籍管辖权。本题消费者是 A 州公民，制造商是 B 州公民，争议金额为 100,000 美元，所以消费者的产品责任诉讼源于多元州籍管辖权。又因为 A 州联邦地区法院的审判地适当，所以适用 A 州的法律选择规则。根据该规则，本诉由 A 州产品责任法支配，所以制造商不太可能成功使 B 州产品责任法在移送后得到适用（选项 D）。\n\n学习目标：案件在联邦司法地区之间移送时，如果 (1) 原法院的审判地适当，且 (2) 事项管辖权源于多元州籍管辖权，则适用原法院的法律选择规则。\n\n参考资料：\n• Ferens v. John Deere Co., 494 U.S. 516, 531 (1990)（判定原法院审判地适当且诉讼源于多元州籍管辖权时，移送后适用原法院的法律选择规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-044"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-183","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":183,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"Ten months after surgery in a hospital, a patient who had suffered complications from the surgery sued the surgeon and the hospital in federal court for medical malpractice, seeking $750,000 in damages. Timely personal service was made on the surgeon and the hospital. Three months later, during discovery, the patient learned that the hospital was owned by a national health-care company and moved to amend the complaint to substitute the company for the hospital. The company moved to dismiss, arguing that the forum state had enacted a one-year statute of limitations for medical malpractice actions and that the company had been served after the limitations period had expired. The company also noted that the state's highest court has interpreted the limitations statute as forbidding any relation back of amendments adding parties in medical malpractice actions. The patient argued that the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure control, and that they allow relation back under the circumstances of this case. Which law governs whether relation back will be permitted under these circumstances?","choices":{"A":"Federal law, because the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure govern over conflicting state rules that deny relation back.","B":"Federal law, because the state law on relation back is common law and federal courts are bound only by state statutory law.","C":"State law, because statutes of limitation are substantive and state law controls substantive matters.","D":"State law, because the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure authorize the use of state law for relation back."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. When it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural, the court must determine if a valid federal law (ie, federal rule, statute, or constitutional provision) directly addresses the issue. If so, federal-rule analysis provides that the federal law governs over a conflicting state law if the federal law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 15 directly addresses whether an amended pleading relates back to the date on which the original pleading was filed. This valid rule* is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right because it regulates the manner and means of a lawsuit (ie, the filing of a pleading)—not the parties' legal rights (ie, what they may sue for). As a result, this federal rule governs whether the patient's amended complaint substituting the company as the defendant relates back to the date that the original complaint was filed. *No FRCP has ever been found unconstitutional or invalid. This is likely because the Supreme Court reviews and sends proposed rules to Congress before they are enacted. (Choice B) Federal courts sitting in diversity are bound by both state statutory law and common law. (Choice C) Statutes of limitation are substantive issues governed by state law. However, whether an amended pleading relates back to avoid the expiration of a statute of limitations is a procedural issue governed by FRCP 15. (Choice D) FRCP 15 provides that an amended pleading relates back if the state statute-of-limitations law allows relation back. However, FRCP 15 provides other avenues for relation back that govern if the state statute of limitations is silent about or prohibits relation back. Educational objective: Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 15 governs the procedural issue of whether an amended pleading relates back to the date that the original pleading was filed. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460, 471 (1965) (establishing the federal-rule analysis).\n\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987) (discussing the federal-rule analysis).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (providing the \"relation back\" rule for federal court).","rule":"Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 15 governs the procedural issue of whether an amended pleading relates back to the date that the original pleading was filed. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"Ten months after surgery in a hospital, a patient who had suffered complications from the surgery sued the surgeon and the hospital in federal court for medical malpractice, seeking $750,000 in damages. Timely personal service was made on the surgeon and the hospital. Three months later, during discovery, the patient learned that the hospital was owned by a national health-care company and moved to amend the complaint to substitute the company for the hospital. The company moved to dismiss, arguing that the forum state had enacted a one-year statute of limitations for medical malpractice actions and that the company had been served after the limitations period had expired. The company also noted that the state's highest court has interpreted the limitations statute as forbidding any relation back of amendments adding parties in medical malpractice actions. The patient argued that the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure control, and that they allow relation back under the circumstances of this case. Which law governs whether relation back will be permitted under these circumstances?","rule":"Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 15 governs the procedural issue of whether an amended pleading relates back to the date that the original pleading was filed. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"When a federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. When it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural, the court must determine if a valid federal law (ie, federal rule, statute, or constitutional provision) directly addresses the issue. If so, federal-rule analysis provides that the federal law governs over a conflicting state law if the federal law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 15 directly addresses whether an amended pleading relates back to the date on which the original pleading was filed. This valid rule* is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right because it regulates the manner and means of a lawsuit (ie, the filing of a pleading)—not the parties' legal rights (ie, what they may sue for). As a result, this federal rule governs whether the patient's amended complaint substituting the company as the defendant relates back to the date that the original complaint was filed. *No FRCP has ever been found unconstitutional or invalid. This is likely because the Supreme Court reviews and sends proposed rules to Congress before they are enacted. (Choice B) Federal courts sitting in diversity are bound by both state statutory law and common law. (Choice C) Statutes of limitation are substantive issues governed by state law. However, whether an amended pleading relates back to avoid the expiration of a statute of limitations is a procedural issue governed by FRCP 15. (Choice D) FRCP 15 provides that an amended pleading relates back if the state statute-of-limitations law allows relation back. However, FRCP 15 provides other avenues for relation back that govern if the state statute of limitations is silent about or prohibits relation back. Educational objective: Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 15 governs the procedural issue of whether an amended pleading relates back to the date that the original pleading was filed. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460, 471 (1965) (establishing the federal-rule analysis).\n\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987) (discussing the federal-rule analysis).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (providing the \"relation back\" rule for federal court).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460, 471 (1965) (establishing the federal-rule analysis).\n\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987) (discussing the federal-rule analysis).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (providing the \"relation back\" rule for federal court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[553],"sourceAnswerPages":[554,555],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名患者在医院接受手术十个月后，因手术出现并发症，在联邦法院起诉外科医生和医院医疗过失，请求 750,000 美元损害赔偿。外科医生和医院均被及时亲自送达诉讼文书。三个月后，患者在证据开示期间得知，医院由一家全国性医疗保健公司所有，遂申请修改起诉状，以该公司替换医院。公司申请驳回，主张法院州已对医疗过失诉讼制定一年诉讼时效，而公司是在时效期满后才被送达。公司还指出，该州最高法院已将诉讼时效制定法解释为：医疗过失诉讼中，增加当事人的修正不得溯及既往。患者主张《联邦民事诉讼规则》具有控制力，而且在本案情形下允许溯及既往。在这些情形下，是否允许溯及既往由哪一法律支配？","choicesZh":{"A":"联邦法，因为《联邦民事诉讼规则》优先于拒绝溯及既往的冲突州规则。","B":"联邦法，因为关于溯及既往的州法是普通法，而联邦法院只受州制定法约束。","C":"州法，因为诉讼时效属于实体性问题，实体事项由州法控制。","D":"州法，因为《联邦民事诉讼规则》授权对溯及既往适用州法。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院的事项管辖权基于多元州籍时，法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，对程序性问题适用联邦法。一个问题究竟属于实体性还是程序性并不明确时，法院必须确定是否有有效的联邦法，即联邦规则、制定法或宪法条款，直接处理该问题。若有，根据联邦规则分析，只要联邦法可以合理视为程序性且不改变实体权利，联邦法即优先于相冲突的州法。\n\nFRCP 15 直接处理修改后的诉状是否溯及原诉状提交之日的问题。这项有效规则*可以合理视为程序性，且不改变实体权利，因为它调整诉讼的方式和手段，即诉状提交，而非双方的法律权利，即可以为何事提起诉讼。因此，由该联邦规则决定患者以公司替换被告的修正起诉状是否溯及原起诉状提交之日。\n\n*从未有任何一条《联邦民事诉讼规则》被认定违宪或无效。这很可能是因为拟议规则生效前，最高法院会先审查并将其送交国会。\n\n（选项 B）行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院同时受州制定法和州普通法约束。\n\n（选项 C）诉讼时效属于由州法支配的实体性问题。但是，修改后的诉状是否可以溯及既往以避免诉讼时效届满，属于由 FRCP 15 支配的程序性问题。\n\n（选项 D）FRCP 15 规定，如果州诉讼时效法允许溯及既往，修正诉状即可溯及既往。但是，如果州诉讼时效法对溯及既往没有规定或予以禁止，FRCP 15 还提供其他具有控制力的溯及途径。\n\n学习目标：根据联邦规则分析，行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须适用一项有效的联邦法，只要该法律可以合理视为程序性且不改变实体权利。FRCP 15 支配修改后的诉状是否溯及原诉状提交之日这一程序性问题。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Hanna v. Plumer, 380 U.S. 460, 471 (1965)（确立联邦规则分析）。\n• Burlington N. R.R. Co. v. Woods, 480 U.S. 1, 4–5 (1987)（讨论联邦规则分析）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15（规定联邦法院的“溯及既往”规则）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-037"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-184","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":184,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A man brought a diversity action against a pharmaceutical company in a federal district court in State A. In his complaint, the man alleged that the company violated State A's false advertising law. The company moved to dismiss the man's action for failure to state a claim on the ground that the false advertising law does not apply to advertising by pharmaceutical companies. Several lower state courts in State A have recognized the company's defense. The highest state court in State A has never ruled or noted in dicta whether this defense is valid. Based on the lower state courts' decisions, the federal district court ruled that the company's defense applies and granted its motion to dismiss. The man appealed. State A law permits a federal district court to certify an unsettled question of state substantive law to the state's highest court for clarification. Is the federal appellate court likely to overturn the dismissal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal district court properly relied on the rulings issued by the state's lower courts.","B":"No, because the federal district court's decision was not clearly erroneous.","C":"Yes, because the federal district court abused its discretion when it dismissed the action.","D":"Yes, because the federal district court failed to certify the issue to the highest state court in State A."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues, including the validity of a defense (as seen here). A federal court interpreting state substantive law must follow the rulings issued by the state's highest court. If the state's highest court has never definitively ruled on the issue, the federal court must predict how the state's highest court would rule by looking to other sources of law. Priority is given in the following order: 1. dicta in related decisions by the state's highest court 2. rulings issued by the state's lower courts (ie, intermediate appellate courts and trial courts) 3. other persuasive authority (eg, rulings issued in other jurisdictions, restatements, public policy) Here, State A's highest court has never ruled or noted in dicta whether the company's defense is valid, so the federal district court may look to rulings issued by the state's lower courts. Since several lower state courts in State A have recognized the company's defense, the federal district court properly relied on these rulings in dismissing the case. Therefore, the federal appellate court is unlikely to overturn the dismissal. (Choices B & C) A federal district court's interpretation of a state's substantive law involves a pure legal issue that is subject to the de novo standard of review. A district court's findings of fact in a bench trial are subject to clearly erroneous review, and its discretionary rulings (eg, admissibility of evidence) are subject to abuse of discretion review. (Choice D) A federal district court may, but is not required to, certify a question of unsettled state law to the state's highest court for clarification if certification is permitted by the state. Educational objective: A federal court sitting in diversity must interpret state substantive law pursuant to decisions issued by the state's highest court. When no such decisions exist, the federal court should prioritize other sources of law in this order: (1) dicta by the state's highest court, (2) rulings by the state's lower courts, (3) other persuasive authority. References\n• Stoner v. N.Y. Life Ins. Co., 311 U.S. 464, 467 (1940) (holding that lower state court decisions control in a federal diversity action if the state's highest court has not ruled and no evidence exists that it would rule differently).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts §§ 351–52 (2020) (explaining that a federal court must determine how a state's highest court would rule on a substantive issue when the issue has not been ruled on by that court).","rule":"A federal court sitting in diversity must interpret state substantive law pursuant to decisions issued by the state's highest court. When no such decisions exist, the federal court should prioritize other sources of law in this order: (1) dicta by the state's highest court, (2) rulings by the state's lower courts, (3) other persuasive authority.","irac":{"issue":"A man brought a diversity action against a pharmaceutical company in a federal district court in State A. In his complaint, the man alleged that the company violated State A's false advertising law. The company moved to dismiss the man's action for failure to state a claim on the ground that the false advertising law does not apply to advertising by pharmaceutical companies. Several lower state courts in State A have recognized the company's defense. The highest state court in State A has never ruled or noted in dicta whether this defense is valid. Based on the lower state courts' decisions, the federal district court ruled that the company's defense applies and granted its motion to dismiss. The man appealed. State A law permits a federal district court to certify an unsettled question of state substantive law to the state's highest court for clarification. Is the federal appellate court likely to overturn the dismissal?","rule":"A federal court sitting in diversity must interpret state substantive law pursuant to decisions issued by the state's highest court. When no such decisions exist, the federal court should prioritize other sources of law in this order: (1) dicta by the state's highest court, (2) rulings by the state's lower courts, (3) other persuasive authority.","analysis":"Federal courts sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues, including the validity of a defense (as seen here). A federal court interpreting state substantive law must follow the rulings issued by the state's highest court. If the state's highest court has never definitively ruled on the issue, the federal court must predict how the state's highest court would rule by looking to other sources of law. Priority is given in the following order: 1. dicta in related decisions by the state's highest court 2. rulings issued by the state's lower courts (ie, intermediate appellate courts and trial courts) 3. other persuasive authority (eg, rulings issued in other jurisdictions, restatements, public policy) Here, State A's highest court has never ruled or noted in dicta whether the company's defense is valid, so the federal district court may look to rulings issued by the state's lower courts. Since several lower state courts in State A have recognized the company's defense, the federal district court properly relied on these rulings in dismissing the case. Therefore, the federal appellate court is unlikely to overturn the dismissal. (Choices B & C) A federal district court's interpretation of a state's substantive law involves a pure legal issue that is subject to the de novo standard of review. A district court's findings of fact in a bench trial are subject to clearly erroneous review, and its discretionary rulings (eg, admissibility of evidence) are subject to abuse of discretion review. (Choice D) A federal district court may, but is not required to, certify a question of unsettled state law to the state's highest court for clarification if certification is permitted by the state. Educational objective: A federal court sitting in diversity must interpret state substantive law pursuant to decisions issued by the state's highest court. When no such decisions exist, the federal court should prioritize other sources of law in this order: (1) dicta by the state's highest court, (2) rulings by the state's lower courts, (3) other persuasive authority. References\n• Stoner v. N.Y. Life Ins. Co., 311 U.S. 464, 467 (1940) (holding that lower state court decisions control in a federal diversity action if the state's highest court has not ruled and no evidence exists that it would rule differently).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts §§ 351–52 (2020) (explaining that a federal court must determine how a state's highest court would rule on a substantive issue when the issue has not been ruled on by that court).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Stoner v. N.Y. Life Ins. Co., 311 U.S. 464, 467 (1940) (holding that lower state court decisions control in a federal diversity action if the state's highest court has not ruled and no evidence exists that it would rule differently).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts §§ 351–52 (2020) (explaining that a federal court must determine how a state's highest court would rule on a substantive issue when the issue has not been ruled on by that court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[556],"sourceAnswerPages":[557,558],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在 A 州联邦地区法院对一家制药公司提起多元州籍诉讼。男子在起诉状中主张，公司违反 A 州虚假广告法。公司以未提出可获救济的请求为由申请驳回男子诉讼，理由是虚假广告法不适用于制药公司的广告。A 州数个下级法院已经承认公司的该项抗辩。A 州最高法院从未就该抗辩是否有效作出裁判，也未在附带意见中提及。联邦地区法院依据下级州法院的判决，裁定公司的抗辩适用，并批准驳回动议。男子提起上诉。A 州法律允许联邦地区法院将尚未确定的州实体法问题提交该州最高法院，请其澄清。联邦上诉法院可能推翻驳回裁定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦地区法院适当依据了该州下级法院作出的裁判。","B":"不会，因为联邦地区法院的决定并非明显错误。","C":"会，因为联邦地区法院驳回诉讼时滥用了裁量权。","D":"会，因为联邦地区法院没有将该问题提交 A 州最高法院。"},"explanationZh":"行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，包括抗辩是否有效，本题即如此。联邦法院解释州实体法时，必须遵循该州最高法院作出的裁判。如果该州最高法院从未就问题作出确定性裁判，联邦法院必须参考其他法律来源，预测该州最高法院会如何裁判。应按照以下优先顺序：\n1. 该州最高法院在相关裁判中的附带意见；\n2. 该州下级法院，即中级上诉法院和初审法院作出的裁判；\n3. 其他具有说服力的权威，例如其他法域的裁判、法律重述、公共政策。\n\n本题 A 州最高法院从未就公司的抗辩是否有效作出裁判，也未在附带意见中提及，所以联邦地区法院可以参考该州下级法院作出的裁判。A 州数个下级法院已经承认公司的该项抗辩，所以联邦地区法院在驳回案件时适当依据了这些裁判。因此，联邦上诉法院不太可能推翻驳回裁定。\n\n（选项 B、C）联邦地区法院对州实体法的解释涉及纯粹法律问题，适用重新审查标准。法官审判中地区法院的事实认定适用明显错误标准，其裁量性裁定，例如证据可采性，则适用滥用裁量权标准。\n\n（选项 D）如果州法律允许提交，联邦地区法院可以将尚未确定的州法问题提交该州最高法院澄清，但没有义务必须这样做。\n\n学习目标：行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须依照州最高法院的裁判解释州实体法。没有此类裁判时，联邦法院应按以下顺序优先参考其他法律来源：(1) 州最高法院的附带意见；(2) 州下级法院的裁判；(3) 其他具有说服力的权威。\n\n参考资料：\n• Stoner v. N.Y. Life Ins. Co., 311 U.S. 464, 467 (1940)（判定州最高法院未作裁判且没有证据表明其会作出不同裁判时，州下级法院的裁判在联邦多元州籍诉讼中具有控制力）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts §§ 351–52 (2020)（说明州最高法院未就实体问题作出裁判时，联邦法院必须确定该州最高法院会如何裁判）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-062"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-185","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":185,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A student at a private university sued the university in federal court for negligence after he fell from scaffolding in a university-owned theater building. At trial, after briefing from both parties, the court permitted the jury to hear testimony that there had been several previous accidents in the same building. The jury found for the student, and the university appealed. One of the university's arguments on appeal is that the testimony about the previous accidents should have been excluded as irrelevant and highly prejudicial. Which standard of review applies to this argument?","choices":{"A":"Abuse of discretion.","B":"Clearly erroneous.","C":"De novo.","D":"Harmless error."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference to trial judge's legal Pure legal issues, eg: determination\n• conclusions of law\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial\n• content of jury judge misinterpreted law instructions Clear error • Highly deferential to trial judge's Factual issues in bench factual findings trials, eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• factual determinations Substantial • Highly deferential to jury's factual Factual issues in jury trials, evidence findings eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable jury\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• jury's verdict Abuse of • Highly deferential to trial judge's Discretionary rulings by discretion discretionary decisions judge, eg:\n• Reverse only if decision was\n• grant/denial of new unreasonable/arbitrary trial\n• admissibility of evidence A party can challenge a final judgment on appeal by alleging that the trial court erred. The standard of review used by the appellate court depends on the error alleged. A trial court's discretionary rulings are reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this highly deferential standard, the trial court's ruling will only be reversed if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. That is because the trial court is best suited to understand the circumstances surrounding the discretionary ruling. Trial courts regularly make discretionary rulings on the admissibility of evidence—as the trial court did here by allowing the jury to hear testimony regarding the previous accidents. One of the university's arguments is that this testimony should have been\n\nexcluded as irrelevant and highly prejudicial. Since this argument challenges the trial court's discretionary ruling, abuse-of-discretion review applies. (Choice B) The clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review a trial court's factual findings in a bench trial—not discretionary rulings in a jury trial (as seen here). This standard gives great deference to the trial court, so a factual finding will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. (Choice C) An appellate court reviews pure legal issues under a de novo standard of review, which allows the appellate court to decide the issue anew without deferring to the lower court's legal interpretation. Evidentiary rulings are not pure issues of law because the trial court must apply the applicable legal rule to the facts. (Choice D) Harmless error is a conclusion reached by an appellate court when it determines that an erroneous evidentiary ruling did not affect a party's substantial rights. It is not the standard of review that the appellate court applies to determine whether the evidence was erroneously admitted in the first instance. Educational objective: The abuse-of-discretion standard of appellate review applies to a trial court's discretionary rulings—eg, whether to admit evidence. Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling. References\n• Gen. Elec. Co. v. Joiner, 522 U.S. 136, 141 (1997) (stating that \"abuse of discretion is the proper standard of review of a district court's evidentiary rulings\").","rule":"The abuse-of-discretion standard of appellate review applies to a trial court's discretionary rulings—eg, whether to admit evidence. Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling.","irac":{"issue":"A student at a private university sued the university in federal court for negligence after he fell from scaffolding in a university-owned theater building. At trial, after briefing from both parties, the court permitted the jury to hear testimony that there had been several previous accidents in the same building. The jury found for the student, and the university appealed. One of the university's arguments on appeal is that the testimony about the previous accidents should have been excluded as irrelevant and highly prejudicial. Which standard of review applies to this argument?","rule":"The abuse-of-discretion standard of appellate review applies to a trial court's discretionary rulings—eg, whether to admit evidence. Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling.","analysis":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference to trial judge's legal Pure legal issues, eg: determination\n• conclusions of law\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial\n• content of jury judge misinterpreted law instructions Clear error • Highly deferential to trial judge's Factual issues in bench factual findings trials, eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• factual determinations Substantial • Highly deferential to jury's factual Factual issues in jury trials, evidence findings eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable jury\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• jury's verdict Abuse of • Highly deferential to trial judge's Discretionary rulings by discretion discretionary decisions judge, eg:\n• Reverse only if decision was\n• grant/denial of new unreasonable/arbitrary trial\n• admissibility of evidence A party can challenge a final judgment on appeal by alleging that the trial court erred. The standard of review used by the appellate court depends on the error alleged. A trial court's discretionary rulings are reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this highly deferential standard, the trial court's ruling will only be reversed if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. That is because the trial court is best suited to understand the circumstances surrounding the discretionary ruling. Trial courts regularly make discretionary rulings on the admissibility of evidence—as the trial court did here by allowing the jury to hear testimony regarding the previous accidents. One of the university's arguments is that this testimony should have been\n\nexcluded as irrelevant and highly prejudicial. Since this argument challenges the trial court's discretionary ruling, abuse-of-discretion review applies. (Choice B) The clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review a trial court's factual findings in a bench trial—not discretionary rulings in a jury trial (as seen here). This standard gives great deference to the trial court, so a factual finding will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. (Choice C) An appellate court reviews pure legal issues under a de novo standard of review, which allows the appellate court to decide the issue anew without deferring to the lower court's legal interpretation. Evidentiary rulings are not pure issues of law because the trial court must apply the applicable legal rule to the facts. (Choice D) Harmless error is a conclusion reached by an appellate court when it determines that an erroneous evidentiary ruling did not affect a party's substantial rights. It is not the standard of review that the appellate court applies to determine whether the evidence was erroneously admitted in the first instance. Educational objective: The abuse-of-discretion standard of appellate review applies to a trial court's discretionary rulings—eg, whether to admit evidence. Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling. References\n• Gen. Elec. Co. v. Joiner, 522 U.S. 136, 141 (1997) (stating that \"abuse of discretion is the proper standard of review of a district court's evidentiary rulings\").","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Gen. Elec. Co. v. Joiner, 522 U.S. 136, 141 (1997) (stating that \"abuse of discretion is the proper standard of review of a district court's evidentiary rulings\").","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[559],"sourceAnswerPages":[560,561],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一所私立大学的学生从大学所有的剧院大楼脚手架上跌落后，在联邦法院起诉大学过失。审判时，在双方提交书面论证后，法院允许陪审团听取该楼曾发生数起事故的证言。陪审团判学生胜诉，大学提起上诉。大学的一项上诉理由是，关于先前事故的证言与案件无关且极具不当偏害，应予排除。该论点适用哪一审查标准？","choicesZh":{"A":"滥用裁量权。","B":"明显错误。","C":"重新审查。","D":"无害错误。"},"explanationZh":"上诉审查标准：\n\n标准｜审查程度｜适用范围\n重新审查｜不尊重审判法官的法律判断；若有合理理由认为审判法官错误解释法律则推翻｜纯粹法律问题，例如法律结论、陪审团指示内容\n明显错误｜高度尊重审判法官的事实认定；只有任何合理法官都不会作出该认定时才推翻｜法官审判中的事实问题，例如证人可信度、事实认定\n实质证据｜高度尊重陪审团的事实认定；只有任何合理陪审团都不会作出该认定时才推翻｜陪审团审判中的事实问题，例如证人可信度、陪审团裁决\n滥用裁量权｜高度尊重审判法官的裁量性决定；只有决定不合理或武断时才推翻｜法官的裁量性裁定，例如批准或驳回重新审判、证据可采性\n\n一方可以主张审判法院犯有错误，从而在上诉中质疑终局判决。上诉法院采用的审查标准取决于所主张错误的类型。审判法院的裁量性裁定适用滥用裁量权标准审查。根据这一高度尊重的标准，只有审判法院的裁定明显武断或不合理时才会被推翻，因为审判法院最适合了解裁量性裁定周围的具体情形。\n\n审判法院经常就证据可采性作出裁量性裁定，本题法院允许陪审团听取先前事故的证言即属如此。大学主张该证言与案件无关且极具不当偏害，本应排除。由于该论点质疑审判法院的裁量性裁定，应适用滥用裁量权审查。\n\n（选项 B）明显错误标准用于审查法官审判中审判法院的事实认定，而不是陪审团审判中的裁量性裁定（本题即如此）。该标准高度尊重审判法院，只有任何合理法官都不会作出该事实认定时才会推翻。\n\n（选项 C）上诉法院以重新审查标准审查纯粹法律问题，可以不尊重下级法院的法律解释而重新决定问题。证据裁定并非纯粹法律问题，因为审判法院必须将适用法律规则运用于事实。\n\n（选项 D）无害错误是上诉法院认定错误的证据裁定没有影响一方重大权利时得出的结论。它不是上诉法院最初判断证据是否被错误采纳时采用的审查标准。\n\n学习目标：滥用裁量权这一上诉审查标准适用于审判法院的裁量性裁定，例如是否采纳证据。根据这一高度尊重的标准，上诉法院只会推翻明显武断或不合理的裁定。\n\n参考资料：\n• Gen. Elec. Co. v. Joiner, 522 U.S. 136, 141 (1997)（指出“滥用裁量权是审查地区法院证据裁定的适当标准”）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-186","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":186,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A business owner sued a federal agency in federal district court, claiming that the agency's recent regulation requiring businesses to report the race and gender of all employees was unconstitutional. The business owner sought a permanent injunction to prevent the agency from enforcing the regulation. At the end of a bench trial, the district court entered a final judgment in favor of the business owner, granting a permanent injunction. Forty-five days later, the federal agency filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court. How is the federal appellate court likely to proceed?","choices":{"A":"The appellate court will hear the merits of the appeal, because a notice of appeal may be filed within 60 days of the entry of final judgment when a federal agency is a party to the suit.","B":"The appellate court will hear the merits of the appeal, because the district court's grant of an injunction is appealable under the interlocutory-appeals statute.","C":"The appellate court will refuse to hear the merits of the appeal and will dismiss it, because a notice of appeal of an injunction must be filed within 10 days of the entry of final judgment.","D":"The appellate court will refuse to hear the merits of the appeal and will dismiss it, because the federal agency did not file the notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate court."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A party may challenge an adverse federal district court ruling, finding, or conclusion by appealing to the federal appellate court encompassing that district. The party must generally file a notice of appeal with the district court clerk within 30 days after the entry of final judgment. However, the time to file a notice of appeal is extended to 60 days if one of the parties is (1) the United States, (2) a federal agency, or (3) a federal officer or employee sued for conduct that relates to government duties. Here, a federal agency is a party to the suit, so the time to file a notice of appeal is extended to 60 days. This means that the federal agency timely filed its notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court 45 days after final judgment was entered. Therefore, the appellate court will hear the merits of the appeal. (Choices B & C) An appeal is typically allowed only after a final judgment. However, the interlocutory-appeals statute provides certain exceptions to this final-judgment rule—one of which permits the immediate appeal of a preliminary injunction if the application is made within 10 days of the trial court's ruling. But this statute is inapplicable here because this permanent injunction constitutes a final judgment—not a temporary ruling. (Choice D) A notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the district court—not the appellate court. Educational objective: The time to file a notice of appeal with the district court clerk is extended to 60 days after the entry of final judgment if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued for conduct related to government duties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 2107 (timing of appeal).\n\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4 (notice of appeal).","rule":"The time to file a notice of appeal with the district court clerk is extended to 60 days after the entry of final judgment if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued for conduct related to government duties.","irac":{"issue":"A business owner sued a federal agency in federal district court, claiming that the agency's recent regulation requiring businesses to report the race and gender of all employees was unconstitutional. The business owner sought a permanent injunction to prevent the agency from enforcing the regulation. At the end of a bench trial, the district court entered a final judgment in favor of the business owner, granting a permanent injunction. Forty-five days later, the federal agency filed a notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court. How is the federal appellate court likely to proceed?","rule":"The time to file a notice of appeal with the district court clerk is extended to 60 days after the entry of final judgment if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued for conduct related to government duties.","analysis":"A party may challenge an adverse federal district court ruling, finding, or conclusion by appealing to the federal appellate court encompassing that district. The party must generally file a notice of appeal with the district court clerk within 30 days after the entry of final judgment. However, the time to file a notice of appeal is extended to 60 days if one of the parties is (1) the United States, (2) a federal agency, or (3) a federal officer or employee sued for conduct that relates to government duties. Here, a federal agency is a party to the suit, so the time to file a notice of appeal is extended to 60 days. This means that the federal agency timely filed its notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court 45 days after final judgment was entered. Therefore, the appellate court will hear the merits of the appeal. (Choices B & C) An appeal is typically allowed only after a final judgment. However, the interlocutory-appeals statute provides certain exceptions to this final-judgment rule—one of which permits the immediate appeal of a preliminary injunction if the application is made within 10 days of the trial court's ruling. But this statute is inapplicable here because this permanent injunction constitutes a final judgment—not a temporary ruling. (Choice D) A notice of appeal must be filed with the clerk of the district court—not the appellate court. Educational objective: The time to file a notice of appeal with the district court clerk is extended to 60 days after the entry of final judgment if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued for conduct related to government duties. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 2107 (timing of appeal).\n\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4 (notice of appeal).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 2107 (timing of appeal).\n\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4 (notice of appeal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[562],"sourceAnswerPages":[563],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名企业主在联邦地区法院起诉一家联邦机构，主张该机构最近制定的、要求企业报告所有雇员种族和性别的规章违宪。企业主请求永久禁令，禁止该机构执行规章。法官审判结束时，地区法院登记有利于企业主的终局判决并颁发永久禁令。四十五天后，联邦机构向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知。联邦上诉法院可能如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"上诉法院将审理上诉实体，因为联邦机构为诉讼一方时，可以在终局判决登记后 60 天内提交上诉通知。","B":"上诉法院将审理上诉实体，因为地区法院颁发禁令可依据中间上诉制定法上诉。","C":"上诉法院将拒绝审理上诉实体并驳回上诉，因为禁令上诉通知必须在终局判决登记后 10 天内提交。","D":"上诉法院将拒绝审理上诉实体并驳回上诉，因为联邦机构没有向上诉法院书记员提交上诉通知。"},"explanationZh":"一方可以向管辖该地区的联邦上诉法院提起上诉，质疑联邦地区法院对其不利的裁定、认定或结论。通常必须在终局判决登记后 30 天内向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知。但是，如果一方是：(1) 美国政府；(2) 联邦机构；或 (3) 因与政府职务有关的行为被诉的联邦官员或雇员，上诉通知提交期限延长至 60 天。\n\n本题联邦机构是诉讼一方，所以提交上诉通知的期限延长至 60 天。联邦机构在终局判决登记后 45 天向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知，属于及时提交。因此，上诉法院将审理上诉实体。\n\n（选项 B、C）通常只有终局判决才能上诉。不过，中间上诉制定法对终局判决规则规定某些例外，其中包括：如果在审判法院作出裁定后 10 天内申请，可以立即对初步禁令提起上诉。但该制定法不适用于本题，因为永久禁令构成终局判决，而非临时裁定。\n\n（选项 D）上诉通知必须向地区法院书记员而不是上诉法院书记员提交。\n\n学习目标：如果一方是美国政府、联邦机构，或因与政府职务有关的行为被诉的联邦官员或雇员，向地区法院书记员提交上诉通知的期限延长至终局判决登记后 60 天。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 2107（上诉期限）。\n• Fed. R. App. P. 4（上诉通知）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-063"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-187","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":187,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A construction contractor brought a breach-of-contract claim in federal court against a homeowner who had hired the contractor to build an apartment over an existing garage. The action turned on the scope of the work covered by the contract. The contractor and the homeowner were the only witnesses at the bench trial, and they strongly disagreed about the scope of the work. At the end of the trial, the judge stated findings of fact on the record but never issued a written opinion. Neither party objected to the findings. The judge found in favor of the homeowner, and the contractor appealed. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the findings?","choices":{"A":"No, because the appellate court must give due regard to the trial judge's opportunity to determine witness credibility.","B":"No, because the contractor failed to object to the findings when the judge stated them in open court.","C":"Yes, because a judge must set forth findings of fact in a written opinion or memorandum of decision.","D":"Yes, because there were disputed issues of fact at trial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"In a bench trial, the judge acts as both the finder of fact and interpreter of the law. As a result, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 52 requires that the judge provide those factual findings and legal conclusions on the record (orally or in writing) after the close of evidence. The appellate court can then review the trial judge's decision under the following standards:\n• de novo (no deference) – where a judge's conclusions of law (eg, elements of a claim) will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the judge misinterpreted the applicable law\n\n• clear error (high deference) – where a judge's findings of fact (eg, witness credibility) will be reversed only if they were clearly erroneous such that no reasonable judge would have made them\n\nUnder clear-error review, the appellate court must give due regard to the trial judge's opportunity to determine witness credibility because the judge is the one who heard the witnesses' testimony. As a result, the appellate court will likely affirm the trial judge's findings of fact in this suit. (Choice B) FRCP 52 allows a party to challenge a trial judge's factual findings on appeal even if no objection was made on the record at the bench trial (as seen here). This is an exception to the general rule requiring a party to formally object at trial to preserve an issue for appeal. (Choice C) In a bench trial, a judge may set forth findings of fact in a written opinion or memorandum of decision but is not required to do so. The findings need only be stated on the record—orally or in writing. (Choice D) A judge is responsible for resolving disputed issues of fact in a bench trial. Therefore, the presence of such disputes is not a basis to overturn the judge's findings. Educational objective: In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and the interpreter of law. An appellate court reviews the judge's findings of fact for clear error (high deference) and conclusions of law de novo (no deference). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).\n\n• United States v. U.S. Gypsum Co., 333 U.S. 364, 394–95 (1948) (explaining clear-error appellate review).","rule":"In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and the interpreter of law. An appellate court reviews the judge's findings of fact for clear error (high deference) and conclusions of law de novo (no deference).","irac":{"issue":"A construction contractor brought a breach-of-contract claim in federal court against a homeowner who had hired the contractor to build an apartment over an existing garage. The action turned on the scope of the work covered by the contract. The contractor and the homeowner were the only witnesses at the bench trial, and they strongly disagreed about the scope of the work. At the end of the trial, the judge stated findings of fact on the record but never issued a written opinion. Neither party objected to the findings. The judge found in favor of the homeowner, and the contractor appealed. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the findings?","rule":"In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and the interpreter of law. An appellate court reviews the judge's findings of fact for clear error (high deference) and conclusions of law de novo (no deference).","analysis":"In a bench trial, the judge acts as both the finder of fact and interpreter of the law. As a result, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 52 requires that the judge provide those factual findings and legal conclusions on the record (orally or in writing) after the close of evidence. The appellate court can then review the trial judge's decision under the following standards:\n• de novo (no deference) – where a judge's conclusions of law (eg, elements of a claim) will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the judge misinterpreted the applicable law\n\n• clear error (high deference) – where a judge's findings of fact (eg, witness credibility) will be reversed only if they were clearly erroneous such that no reasonable judge would have made them\n\nUnder clear-error review, the appellate court must give due regard to the trial judge's opportunity to determine witness credibility because the judge is the one who heard the witnesses' testimony. As a result, the appellate court will likely affirm the trial judge's findings of fact in this suit. (Choice B) FRCP 52 allows a party to challenge a trial judge's factual findings on appeal even if no objection was made on the record at the bench trial (as seen here). This is an exception to the general rule requiring a party to formally object at trial to preserve an issue for appeal. (Choice C) In a bench trial, a judge may set forth findings of fact in a written opinion or memorandum of decision but is not required to do so. The findings need only be stated on the record—orally or in writing. (Choice D) A judge is responsible for resolving disputed issues of fact in a bench trial. Therefore, the presence of such disputes is not a basis to overturn the judge's findings. Educational objective: In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and the interpreter of law. An appellate court reviews the judge's findings of fact for clear error (high deference) and conclusions of law de novo (no deference). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).\n\n• United States v. U.S. Gypsum Co., 333 U.S. 364, 394–95 (1948) (explaining clear-error appellate review).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).\n\n• United States v. U.S. Gypsum Co., 333 U.S. 364, 394–95 (1948) (explaining clear-error appellate review).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[565],"sourceAnswerPages":[566],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名建筑承包商在联邦法院对一名房主提出违约请求；房主曾雇用承包商在现有车库上方建造一套公寓。诉讼取决于合同涵盖的工作范围。法官审判中，承包商和房主是仅有的两名证人，双方对工作范围存在严重分歧。审判结束时，法官在案卷记录中陈述事实认定，但从未发布书面意见。双方均未对认定提出异议。法官判房主胜诉，承包商提起上诉。上诉法院可能推翻这些事实认定吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为上诉法院必须适当顾及审判法官判断证人可信度的机会。","B":"不会，因为法官当庭陈述事实认定时，承包商未提出异议。","C":"会，因为法官必须在书面意见或裁判备忘录中列明事实认定。","D":"会，因为审判时存在有争议的事实问题。"},"explanationZh":"法官审判中，法官同时担任事实裁判者和法律解释者。因此，《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）52 要求法官在举证结束后，以口头或书面形式在案卷记录中提供事实认定和法律结论。随后，上诉法院可以依据以下标准审查审判法官的决定：\n• 重新审查（不予尊重）——审查法官的法律结论，例如请求的构成要件；如果上诉法院有合理理由认为法官错误解释适用法律，就会推翻；\n• 明显错误（高度尊重）——审查法官的事实认定，例如证人可信度；只有认定明显错误到任何合理法官都不会作出时才会推翻。\n\n根据明显错误审查，上诉法院必须适当顾及审判法官判断证人可信度的机会，因为是该法官亲自听取证人证言。因此，上诉法院很可能维持本诉审判法官的事实认定。\n\n（选项 B）FRCP 52 允许一方在上诉中质疑审判法官的事实认定，即使法官审判时没有在案卷记录中提出异议（本题即如此）。这是必须在审判时正式提出异议才能保留问题供上诉审查这一一般规则的例外。\n\n（选项 C）法官审判中，法官可以在书面意见或裁判备忘录中列明事实认定，但并非必须如此。只需以口头或书面形式在案卷记录中陈述。\n\n（选项 D）法官负责解决法官审判中的争议事实问题。因此，存在这类争议并不是推翻法官事实认定的依据。\n\n学习目标：法官审判中，法官担任事实裁判者和法律解释者。上诉法院对法官事实认定适用明显错误标准（高度尊重），对法律结论适用重新审查标准（不予尊重）。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a)（事实认定和法律结论）。\n• United States v. U.S. Gypsum Co., 333 U.S. 364, 394–95 (1948)（说明明显错误上诉审查）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-064"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-188","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":188,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A drunken man confronted a heavyweight boxer in a bar, and a fight ensued. During the melee, the boxer struck the man's head and the man instantly died. The boxer was convicted of manslaughter and sentenced to 15 years in prison. While the boxer was serving his sentence, the man's spouse brought a wrongful death suit against the boxer in federal court. The spouse sought $10 million in damages for loss of future income and loss of companionship. The boxer was served with process while in prison, but he did not respond. Thirty days later, the spouse asked the clerk to enter the boxer's default, and the clerk did so. The spouse then applied to the clerk to enter a default judgment. May the clerk enter a default judgment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the boxer was not given notice of the spouse's application for the entry of default judgment.","B":"No, because the spouse did not seek a sum certain or attach an affidavit showing the amount due.","C":"Yes, because the boxer failed to appear.","D":"Yes, because the clerk may set damages at the amount demanded in the complaint."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 55 provides a two-step process for a plaintiff to secure a default judgment. First, the clerk must enter the defendant's default into the record once the plaintiff shows that the defendant failed to timely serve an answer—as the boxer failed to do within 21 days after being served with process. Second, the court or the clerk may enter a default judgment. However, the clerk is prohibited from doing so if any of the following apply:\n• The defendant has appeared—ie, filed a motion or otherwise acted before the court.\n\n• The defendant is legally incompetent or a minor.\n\n• The plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain (ie, a specified or set amount) or sum that can be made certain by calculation.\n\n• The plaintiff failed to include an affidavit establishing the amount due.\n\nHere, the boxer did not appear, and there is no indication that he is legally incompetent or a minor. However, the spouse's request for $10 million is not a sum certain and cannot be made certain by calculation. That is because the man's future earning potential and the value of his companionship are not specified or set amounts. Additionally, the spouse failed to attach an affidavit showing the amount due. Therefore, the clerk may not enter a default judgment (Choice C).* *The court may still enter a default judgment after the judge conducts a hearing to determine the proper amount of damages to award. (Choice A) Had the boxer appeared, he would have been entitled to written notice at least seven days before the court conducted a hearing on the entry of default judgment. (Choice D) The clerk may set damages at the amount demanded in the complaint when the claim is for a sum certain and an attached affidavit establishes the amount due—neither of which is seen here. Educational objective: A default judgment may be entered by the clerk of the court unless: (1) the defendant has appeared, (2) the defendant is legally incompetent or a minor, (3) the plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain or a sum that can be made certain by calculation, or (4) the plaintiff failed to include an affidavit establishing the amount due. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).\n\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 260 (2019) (explaining sum certain).","rule":"A default judgment may be entered by the clerk of the court unless: (1) the defendant has appeared, (2) the defendant is legally incompetent or a minor, (3) the plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain or a sum that can be made certain by calculation, or (4) the plaintiff failed to include an affidavit establishing the amount due.","irac":{"issue":"A drunken man confronted a heavyweight boxer in a bar, and a fight ensued. During the melee, the boxer struck the man's head and the man instantly died. The boxer was convicted of manslaughter and sentenced to 15 years in prison. While the boxer was serving his sentence, the man's spouse brought a wrongful death suit against the boxer in federal court. The spouse sought $10 million in damages for loss of future income and loss of companionship. The boxer was served with process while in prison, but he did not respond. Thirty days later, the spouse asked the clerk to enter the boxer's default, and the clerk did so. The spouse then applied to the clerk to enter a default judgment. May the clerk enter a default judgment?","rule":"A default judgment may be entered by the clerk of the court unless: (1) the defendant has appeared, (2) the defendant is legally incompetent or a minor, (3) the plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain or a sum that can be made certain by calculation, or (4) the plaintiff failed to include an affidavit establishing the amount due.","analysis":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 55 provides a two-step process for a plaintiff to secure a default judgment. First, the clerk must enter the defendant's default into the record once the plaintiff shows that the defendant failed to timely serve an answer—as the boxer failed to do within 21 days after being served with process. Second, the court or the clerk may enter a default judgment. However, the clerk is prohibited from doing so if any of the following apply:\n• The defendant has appeared—ie, filed a motion or otherwise acted before the court.\n\n• The defendant is legally incompetent or a minor.\n\n• The plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain (ie, a specified or set amount) or sum that can be made certain by calculation.\n\n• The plaintiff failed to include an affidavit establishing the amount due.\n\nHere, the boxer did not appear, and there is no indication that he is legally incompetent or a minor. However, the spouse's request for $10 million is not a sum certain and cannot be made certain by calculation. That is because the man's future earning potential and the value of his companionship are not specified or set amounts. Additionally, the spouse failed to attach an affidavit showing the amount due. Therefore, the clerk may not enter a default judgment (Choice C).* *The court may still enter a default judgment after the judge conducts a hearing to determine the proper amount of damages to award. (Choice A) Had the boxer appeared, he would have been entitled to written notice at least seven days before the court conducted a hearing on the entry of default judgment. (Choice D) The clerk may set damages at the amount demanded in the complaint when the claim is for a sum certain and an attached affidavit establishes the amount due—neither of which is seen here. Educational objective: A default judgment may be entered by the clerk of the court unless: (1) the defendant has appeared, (2) the defendant is legally incompetent or a minor, (3) the plaintiff's claim is not for a sum certain or a sum that can be made certain by calculation, or (4) the plaintiff failed to include an affidavit establishing the amount due. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).\n\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 260 (2019) (explaining sum certain).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).\n\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 260 (2019) (explaining sum certain).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[568],"sourceAnswerPages":[569,570],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名醉酒男子在酒吧挑衅一名重量级拳击手，双方发生打斗。混战中，拳击手击中男子头部，男子当场死亡。拳击手被判非故意杀人罪，获刑 15 年。拳击手服刑期间，男子的配偶在联邦法院对其提起非正常死亡诉讼。配偶就未来收入损失及配偶关系利益损失请求 1,000 万美元损害赔偿。拳击手在监狱服刑时被送达诉讼文书，但没有回应。三十天后，配偶请求书记员登记拳击手缺席，书记员予以登记。随后，配偶向书记员申请登记缺席判决。书记员可以登记缺席判决吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为拳击手没有收到配偶申请登记缺席判决的通知。","B":"不可以，因为配偶请求的并非确定金额，也没有附上证明到期金额的宣誓书。","C":"可以，因为拳击手未出庭。","D":"可以，因为书记员可以按起诉状请求的金额确定损害赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"《联邦民事诉讼规则》55 规定原告取得缺席判决需要两个步骤。第一步，原告证明被告未及时送达答辩状后，书记员必须将被告缺席登记于案卷记录；拳击手被送达诉讼文书后 21 天内没有答辩，即属如此。第二步，由法院或书记员登记缺席判决。但是，存在下列任何情形时，书记员不得登记：\n• 被告已经出庭，即提交动议或以其他方式在法院采取行动；\n• 被告在法律上无行为能力或为未成年人；\n• 原告请求的并非确定金额，即明确固定的数额，或无法通过计算确定；或\n• 原告没有附上证明到期金额的宣誓书。\n\n本题拳击手没有出庭，也没有迹象表明他在法律上无行为能力或为未成年人。但是，配偶请求的 1,000 万美元并非确定金额，且无法通过计算确定，因为男子未来收入潜力和配偶关系利益的价值都不是明确固定的数额。此外，配偶没有附上证明到期金额的宣誓书。因此，书记员不得登记缺席判决（选项 C）。*\n\n*法官举行听证确定适当损害赔偿金额后，法院仍可登记缺席判决。\n\n（选项 A）如果拳击手已经出庭，他有权在法院就登记缺席判决举行听证前至少七天收到书面通知。\n\n（选项 D）请求属于确定金额且所附宣誓书证明到期金额时，书记员可以按照起诉状请求的金额确定损害赔偿；本题两个条件均不具备。\n\n学习目标：除非存在下列情形，法院书记员可以登记缺席判决：(1) 被告已经出庭；(2) 被告在法律上无行为能力或为未成年人；(3) 原告请求并非确定金额或无法通过计算确定；或 (4) 原告没有附上证明到期金额的宣誓书。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55（缺席判决）。\n• 46 Am. Jur. 2d Judgments § 260 (2019)（说明确定金额）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-058"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-189","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":189,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court for allegedly violating a recently enacted federal statute. The defendant moved to dismiss the plaintiff's complaint for failure to state a claim. The district court denied the defendant's motion, basing the ruling on a provision of the statute that has been interpreted differently by several district courts. In the written order denying the motion, the district court stated that \"there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and an immediate appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of this litigation.\" Forty days after the district court entered this order, the defendant applied to the federal appellate court to hear the appeal of the order. May the appellate court consider this appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because only a final judgment can be appealed.","B":"No, because the application for appeal was untimely.","C":"Yes, because an appellate court must consider an appeal if it is certified by the lower court.","D":"Yes, because an immediate appeal is required to avoid protracted and expensive litigation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Appellate courts may only hear appeals from a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment—absent limited exceptions. One exception to this final-judgment rule arises when the district court certifies an interlocutory appeal by stating in a written order that:\n• there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and\n• an appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation. The party seeking an appeal must then apply to the appellate court within 10 days after the district court enters the order, and the appellate court will decide whether to hear the appeal (Choices A & C).* Since the defendant here did not file an application for appeal until 40 days after the district court entered its order certifying the interlocutory appeal, the application was untimely. Therefore, the appellate court cannot hear this appeal. *An application for such an appeal will stay (ie, halt) the proceedings in the district court if the district or appellate court so orders. (Choice D) The fact that an immediate appeal may avoid protracted and expensive litigation is not a basis for an interlocutory appeal. In fact, allowing interlocutory appeals may increase the cost of litigation if the litigants pursue repeated appeals instead of filing one appeal after final judgment is entered. Educational objective: A district court may certify an order for appeal if it states in writing that (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation. The application to the appellate court must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b) (district court certification).\n\n• Liberty Mut. Ins. Co. v. Wetzel, 424 U.S. 737, 745 (1976) (explaining that an application for appeal based on a district court's certification must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order).","rule":"A district court may certify an order for appeal if it states in writing that (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation. The application to the appellate court must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court for allegedly violating a recently enacted federal statute. The defendant moved to dismiss the plaintiff's complaint for failure to state a claim. The district court denied the defendant's motion, basing the ruling on a provision of the statute that has been interpreted differently by several district courts. In the written order denying the motion, the district court stated that \"there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and an immediate appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of this litigation.\" Forty days after the district court entered this order, the defendant applied to the federal appellate court to hear the appeal of the order. May the appellate court consider this appeal?","rule":"A district court may certify an order for appeal if it states in writing that (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation. The application to the appellate court must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order.","analysis":"Appellate courts may only hear appeals from a final judgment—ie, a decision that fully resolves a dispute on the merits and leaves nothing for the court to do but enforce the judgment—absent limited exceptions. One exception to this final-judgment rule arises when the district court certifies an interlocutory appeal by stating in a written order that:\n• there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and\n• an appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation. The party seeking an appeal must then apply to the appellate court within 10 days after the district court enters the order, and the appellate court will decide whether to hear the appeal (Choices A & C).* Since the defendant here did not file an application for appeal until 40 days after the district court entered its order certifying the interlocutory appeal, the application was untimely. Therefore, the appellate court cannot hear this appeal. *An application for such an appeal will stay (ie, halt) the proceedings in the district court if the district or appellate court so orders. (Choice D) The fact that an immediate appeal may avoid protracted and expensive litigation is not a basis for an interlocutory appeal. In fact, allowing interlocutory appeals may increase the cost of litigation if the litigants pursue repeated appeals instead of filing one appeal after final judgment is entered. Educational objective: A district court may certify an order for appeal if it states in writing that (1) there is a substantial difference of opinion on the controlling question of law and (2) an appeal may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation. The application to the appellate court must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b) (district court certification).\n\n• Liberty Mut. Ins. Co. v. Wetzel, 424 U.S. 737, 745 (1976) (explaining that an application for appeal based on a district court's certification must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1291 (final-judgment rule).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b) (district court certification).\n\n• Liberty Mut. Ins. Co. v. Wetzel, 424 U.S. 737, 745 (1976) (explaining that an application for appeal based on a district court's certification must be made within 10 days after the entry of the order).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[571],"sourceAnswerPages":[572],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院起诉一名被告，主张被告违反一项新近制定的联邦制定法。被告以未提出可获救济的请求为由，申请驳回原告起诉状。地区法院驳回被告动议，其裁定依据是制定法中的一项条款；多个地区法院对该条款作出了不同解释。地区法院在驳回动议的书面命令中指出：“对该控制性法律问题存在重大意见分歧，而且立即上诉可能实质推进本诉的最终结束。”地区法院登记该命令四十天后，被告向联邦上诉法院申请审理对该命令的上诉。上诉法院可以审理该上诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为只有终局判决可以上诉。","B":"不可以，因为上诉申请逾期。","C":"可以，因为下级法院核准上诉后，上诉法院必须审理。","D":"可以，因为为避免诉讼旷日持久且费用高昂，必须立即上诉。"},"explanationZh":"除有限例外外，上诉法院只能审理对终局判决的上诉。终局判决是完全解决争议实体，使法院除执行判决外别无事项的决定。终局判决规则的一项例外是，地区法院在书面命令中作出以下说明，从而核准中间上诉：\n• 对该控制性法律问题存在重大意见分歧；并且\n• 上诉可能实质推进诉讼的最终结束。\n\n随后，寻求上诉的一方必须在地区法院登记命令后 10 天内向上诉法院提出申请，再由上诉法院决定是否审理上诉（选项 A、C）。* 本题被告直到地区法院登记核准中间上诉的命令 40 天后才提交上诉申请，所以申请逾期。因此，上诉法院不能审理该上诉。\n\n*如果地区法院或上诉法院作出命令，该上诉申请将中止地区法院的诉讼程序。\n\n（选项 D）立即上诉可能避免诉讼旷日持久且费用高昂，这一事实本身不是中间上诉的依据。事实上，如果诉讼当事人反复上诉而不是在终局判决后一次上诉，允许中间上诉可能增加诉讼成本。\n\n学习目标：地区法院可以在书面命令中说明以下事项，从而核准命令上诉：(1) 对控制性法律问题存在重大意见分歧；且 (2) 上诉可能实质推进诉讼的最终结束。必须在命令登记后 10 天内向上诉法院提出申请。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1291（终局判决规则）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1292(b)（地区法院核准）。\n• Liberty Mut. Ins. Co. v. Wetzel, 424 U.S. 737, 745 (1976)（说明依据地区法院核准提出的上诉申请，必须在命令登记后 10 天内提出）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-042"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-190","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":190,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"An investor sued a company's chief executive officer (CEO) for federal securities fraud in a federal court. The investor properly served process on the CEO, but the CEO did not file an answer or any other response. Thirty days after service of process, the investor asked the clerk of the court to enter the CEO's default, which the clerk promptly did. The investor then submitted to the clerk a request for default judgment that included an affidavit stating that the CEO defrauded her of $100,000. The clerk entered a default judgment for this amount. Twenty days later, the CEO learned of the default judgment and immediately filed a motion to set it aside because he was abroad when the default judgment was entered. How will the court likely rule on the CEO's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because a court may not set aside an entry of a default judgment.","B":"Deny the motion, because the clerk properly entered the default judgment.","C":"Grant the motion, because only a judge may enter a default judgment.","D":"Grant the motion, because the CEO was not notified of the request for default judgment."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A default refers to a defendant's failure to timely serve an answer to a lawsuit, which is generally due within 21 days after the defendant is served with process. When the plaintiff shows this failure to the court clerk, the clerk must enter the defendant's default into the record of the case (as seen here). The default can then be entered as a default judgment by the clerk or the court (Choice C). The clerk must enter a default judgment when:\n• the plaintiff's claim is for a sum certain—ie, a specified or set amount—or a sum that can be made certain by calculation\n\n• the plaintiff's request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due\n\n• the defendant failed to appear—ie, did not file a motion or otherwise act before the court—and\n\n• the defendant is not legally incompetent or a minor.\n\nHere, the investor filed an affidavit that claimed the CEO defrauded her of $100,000—a sum certain. And since the CEO failed to appear and is not legally incompetent or a minor, the clerk properly entered a default judgment. Therefore, the court will likely deny the CEO's motion to set aside the default judgment. (Choice A) A court may set aside the clerk's entry of default for good cause. And a court may set aside a default judgment for any reason justifying relief from a final judgment— none of which apply here. (Choice D) If the defendant previously appeared before the court, the plaintiff must serve the defendant with written notice of the application for default judgment seven days before a hearing on the matter. But here, the CEO failed to appear, so no such notice was required. Educational objective: A court clerk must enter a default judgment when (1) the plaintiff's claim is for a sum certain, (2) the plaintiff's request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defendant failed to appear, and (4) the defendant is not legally incompetent or a minor. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).","rule":"A court clerk must enter a default judgment when (1) the plaintiff's claim is for a sum certain, (2) the plaintiff's request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defendant failed to appear, and (4) the defendant is not legally incompetent or a minor.","irac":{"issue":"An investor sued a company's chief executive officer (CEO) for federal securities fraud in a federal court. The investor properly served process on the CEO, but the CEO did not file an answer or any other response. Thirty days after service of process, the investor asked the clerk of the court to enter the CEO's default, which the clerk promptly did. The investor then submitted to the clerk a request for default judgment that included an affidavit stating that the CEO defrauded her of $100,000. The clerk entered a default judgment for this amount. Twenty days later, the CEO learned of the default judgment and immediately filed a motion to set it aside because he was abroad when the default judgment was entered. How will the court likely rule on the CEO's motion?","rule":"A court clerk must enter a default judgment when (1) the plaintiff's claim is for a sum certain, (2) the plaintiff's request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defendant failed to appear, and (4) the defendant is not legally incompetent or a minor.","analysis":"A default refers to a defendant's failure to timely serve an answer to a lawsuit, which is generally due within 21 days after the defendant is served with process. When the plaintiff shows this failure to the court clerk, the clerk must enter the defendant's default into the record of the case (as seen here). The default can then be entered as a default judgment by the clerk or the court (Choice C). The clerk must enter a default judgment when:\n• the plaintiff's claim is for a sum certain—ie, a specified or set amount—or a sum that can be made certain by calculation\n\n• the plaintiff's request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due\n\n• the defendant failed to appear—ie, did not file a motion or otherwise act before the court—and\n\n• the defendant is not legally incompetent or a minor.\n\nHere, the investor filed an affidavit that claimed the CEO defrauded her of $100,000—a sum certain. And since the CEO failed to appear and is not legally incompetent or a minor, the clerk properly entered a default judgment. Therefore, the court will likely deny the CEO's motion to set aside the default judgment. (Choice A) A court may set aside the clerk's entry of default for good cause. And a court may set aside a default judgment for any reason justifying relief from a final judgment— none of which apply here. (Choice D) If the defendant previously appeared before the court, the plaintiff must serve the defendant with written notice of the application for default judgment seven days before a hearing on the matter. But here, the CEO failed to appear, so no such notice was required. Educational objective: A court clerk must enter a default judgment when (1) the plaintiff's claim is for a sum certain, (2) the plaintiff's request for default judgment includes an affidavit establishing the amount due, (3) the defendant failed to appear, and (4) the defendant is not legally incompetent or a minor. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55 (default judgments).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[574],"sourceAnswerPages":[575],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名投资者在联邦法院起诉一家公司首席执行官（CEO）实施联邦证券欺诈。投资者向 CEO 适当送达诉讼文书，但 CEO 没有提交答辩状或作出任何其他回应。诉讼文书送达三十天后，投资者请求法院书记员登记 CEO 缺席，书记员立即予以登记。随后，投资者向书记员提交缺席判决请求，并附上一份宣誓书，称 CEO 骗取了她 100,000 美元。书记员按该金额登记缺席判决。二十天后，CEO 得知缺席判决，并立即以缺席判决登记时自己身在国外为由，申请撤销。法院可能如何裁定 CEO 的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回，因为法院不能撤销已经登记的缺席判决。","B":"驳回，因为书记员适当登记了缺席判决。","C":"批准，因为只有法官可以登记缺席判决。","D":"批准，因为 CEO 没有收到缺席判决请求的通知。"},"explanationZh":"缺席是指被告未及时送达诉讼答辩状；通常须在被告诉讼文书送达后 21 天内提交。原告向法院书记员证明被告未及时答辩后，书记员必须将被告缺席登记于案卷记录，本题即如此。随后，书记员或法院可以将缺席登记为缺席判决（选项 C）。\n\n符合以下条件时，书记员必须登记缺席判决：\n• 原告请求属于确定金额，即明确固定的数额，或可以通过计算确定；\n• 原告的缺席判决请求附有证明到期金额的宣誓书；\n• 被告未出庭，即未提交动议或以其他方式在法院采取行动；并且\n• 被告并非法律上无行为能力者或未成年人。\n\n本题投资者提交宣誓书，主张 CEO 骗取她 100,000 美元，属于确定金额。CEO 未出庭，也并非法律上无行为能力者或未成年人，所以书记员适当登记了缺席判决。因此，法院很可能驳回 CEO 撤销缺席判决的动议。\n\n（选项 A）法院可以基于正当理由撤销书记员的缺席登记。法院也可以基于任何足以解除终局判决的理由撤销缺席判决，但本题不存在此类理由。\n\n（选项 D）被告先前已经在法院出庭时，原告必须在缺席判决申请听证前七天，向被告送达书面通知。但本题 CEO 未出庭，所以不要求通知。\n\n学习目标：符合以下条件时，法院书记员必须登记缺席判决：(1) 原告请求属于确定金额；(2) 缺席判决请求附有证明到期金额的宣誓书；(3) 被告未出庭；且 (4) 被告并非法律上无行为能力者或未成年人。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 55（缺席判决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-058"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-191","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":191,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A member of Congress fired a female employee because he believed that male employees are better workers. The female employee sued the member of Congress in federal court to recover monetary damages. She claimed that the termination of her employment constituted sex-based discrimination in violation of the Fifth Amendment. No federal statute authorizes an employment discrimination claim for damages against a member of Congress. The member of Congress has moved to dismiss the female employee's suit for failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the federal court may fill a gap in a federal statutory scheme.","B":"No, because the female employee's claim presents an implied cause of action.","C":"Yes, because the female employee's claim is not explicitly authorized by a federal statute.","D":"Yes, because the member of Congress filed the motion before filing his answer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Implied causes of action for damages\n\nSCOTUS precedent Implied cause of action recognized*\n\nBivens v. Six Unknown Fed. Fourth Amendment claim for unlawful search & seizure Narcotics Agents\n\nDavis v. Passman Fifth Amendment due process claim for sex-based employment discrimination\n\nCarlson v. Green Eighth Amendment cruel & unusual punishment claim for failure to provide medical treatment\n\nSCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\n*SCOTUS has not recognized implied cause of action for damages in the last 40 years.\n\nA federal court generally may award damages against a federal official only if expressly authorized by statute. But in the absence of authorization, federal common law may imply that damages are recoverable in rare instances involving unconstitutional conduct. These implied causes of action (ie, Bivens actions) are disfavored because Congress can best determine the liability of federal officials. As a result, SCOTUS has recognized only three Bivens actions:\n• A Fourth Amendment claim for an unlawful search and seizure\n• A Fifth Amendment due process claim for sex-based employment discrimination\n• An Eighth Amendment cruel and unusual punishment claim for failure to provide medical treatment Here, no federal statute explicitly authorized the female employee's claim for damages against the member of Congress, who is a federal official. But since a Fifth Amendment sex-based employment discrimination claim is a recognized Bivens action, the female employee presented a claim upon which damages can be granted. Therefore, the federal court will likely deny the member's motion (Choice C). (Choice A) A federal court may use federal common law to fill a gap in a federal statutory scheme (eg, by interpreting statutory terms). But since no federal statute is on point here, there is no gap to fill. (Choice D) The defense of failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted may be raised at any time before the end of trial without being waived (as seen here). However, the\n\nmember's motion should be denied since the female employee asserted a Bivens action upon which damages can be granted. Educational objective: The following implied causes of action permit the recovery of damages against a federal official: (1) a Fourth Amendment claim for unlawful search and seizure, (2) a Fifth Amendment claim for sex-based employment discrimination, and (3) an Eighth Amendment claim for failure to provide medical treatment. References\n• Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents of the Bureau of Fed. Narcotics, 403 U.S. 388, 389 (1971) (establishing implied causes of action for certain constitutional violations by federal officials).\n\n• Davis v. Passman, 442 U.S. 228, 248–49 (1979) (holding that an implied cause of action exists to recover damages against a member of Congress who violated the Fifth Amendment).","rule":"The following implied causes of action permit the recovery of damages against a federal official: (1) a Fourth Amendment claim for unlawful search and seizure, (2) a Fifth Amendment claim for sex-based employment discrimination, and (3) an Eighth Amendment claim for failure to provide medical treatment.","irac":{"issue":"A member of Congress fired a female employee because he believed that male employees are better workers. The female employee sued the member of Congress in federal court to recover monetary damages. She claimed that the termination of her employment constituted sex-based discrimination in violation of the Fifth Amendment. No federal statute authorizes an employment discrimination claim for damages against a member of Congress. The member of Congress has moved to dismiss the female employee's suit for failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"The following implied causes of action permit the recovery of damages against a federal official: (1) a Fourth Amendment claim for unlawful search and seizure, (2) a Fifth Amendment claim for sex-based employment discrimination, and (3) an Eighth Amendment claim for failure to provide medical treatment.","analysis":"Implied causes of action for damages\n\nSCOTUS precedent Implied cause of action recognized*\n\nBivens v. Six Unknown Fed. Fourth Amendment claim for unlawful search & seizure Narcotics Agents\n\nDavis v. Passman Fifth Amendment due process claim for sex-based employment discrimination\n\nCarlson v. Green Eighth Amendment cruel & unusual punishment claim for failure to provide medical treatment\n\nSCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.\n\n*SCOTUS has not recognized implied cause of action for damages in the last 40 years.\n\nA federal court generally may award damages against a federal official only if expressly authorized by statute. But in the absence of authorization, federal common law may imply that damages are recoverable in rare instances involving unconstitutional conduct. These implied causes of action (ie, Bivens actions) are disfavored because Congress can best determine the liability of federal officials. As a result, SCOTUS has recognized only three Bivens actions:\n• A Fourth Amendment claim for an unlawful search and seizure\n• A Fifth Amendment due process claim for sex-based employment discrimination\n• An Eighth Amendment cruel and unusual punishment claim for failure to provide medical treatment Here, no federal statute explicitly authorized the female employee's claim for damages against the member of Congress, who is a federal official. But since a Fifth Amendment sex-based employment discrimination claim is a recognized Bivens action, the female employee presented a claim upon which damages can be granted. Therefore, the federal court will likely deny the member's motion (Choice C). (Choice A) A federal court may use federal common law to fill a gap in a federal statutory scheme (eg, by interpreting statutory terms). But since no federal statute is on point here, there is no gap to fill. (Choice D) The defense of failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted may be raised at any time before the end of trial without being waived (as seen here). However, the\n\nmember's motion should be denied since the female employee asserted a Bivens action upon which damages can be granted. Educational objective: The following implied causes of action permit the recovery of damages against a federal official: (1) a Fourth Amendment claim for unlawful search and seizure, (2) a Fifth Amendment claim for sex-based employment discrimination, and (3) an Eighth Amendment claim for failure to provide medical treatment. References\n• Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents of the Bureau of Fed. Narcotics, 403 U.S. 388, 389 (1971) (establishing implied causes of action for certain constitutional violations by federal officials).\n\n• Davis v. Passman, 442 U.S. 228, 248–49 (1979) (holding that an implied cause of action exists to recover damages against a member of Congress who violated the Fifth Amendment).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents of the Bureau of Fed. Narcotics, 403 U.S. 388, 389 (1971) (establishing implied causes of action for certain constitutional violations by federal officials).\n\n• Davis v. Passman, 442 U.S. 228, 248–49 (1979) (holding that an implied cause of action exists to recover damages against a member of Congress who violated the Fifth Amendment).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[577],"sourceAnswerPages":[578,579],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名国会议员因认为男性雇员工作能力更强而解雇一名女性雇员。女性雇员在联邦法院起诉该国会议员，请求金钱损害赔偿。她主张，解雇构成违反第五修正案的性别歧视。没有任何联邦制定法授权针对国会议员提出就业歧视损害赔偿请求。国会议员以未提出可获救济的请求为由，申请驳回女性雇员诉讼。法院可能批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦法院可以填补联邦制定法体系中的空白。","B":"不会，因为女性雇员的请求构成默示诉因。","C":"会，因为女性雇员的请求没有得到联邦制定法明确授权。","D":"会，因为国会议员在提交答辩状前提出该动议。"},"explanationZh":"默示损害赔偿诉因：\n\n最高法院先例｜得到承认的默示诉因*\nBivens v. Six Unknown Fed. Narcotics Agents｜因非法搜查与扣押提出的第四修正案请求\nDavis v. Passman｜因性别就业歧视提出的第五修正案正当程序请求\nCarlson v. Green｜因未提供医疗救治提出的第八修正案残酷与非常惩罚请求\n\nSCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n*最高法院过去 40 年没有承认新的默示损害赔偿诉因。\n\n通常只有制定法明确授权时，联邦法院才能判令联邦官员支付损害赔偿。但在没有授权时，联邦普通法可能在少数涉及违宪行为的情形中默示可以获得损害赔偿。这些默示诉因，即 Bivens 诉讼，不受法院青睐，因为国会最适合决定联邦官员的责任。因此，最高法院只承认了三种 Bivens 诉讼：\n• 因非法搜查与扣押提出的第四修正案请求；\n• 因性别就业歧视提出的第五修正案正当程序请求；\n• 因未提供医疗救治提出的第八修正案残酷与非常惩罚请求。\n\n本题没有联邦制定法明确授权女性雇员对国会议员这一联邦官员提出损害赔偿请求。但是，第五修正案性别就业歧视请求属于已经承认的 Bivens 诉讼，所以女性雇员提出了可获损害赔偿救济的请求。因此，联邦法院很可能驳回国会议员的动议（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 A）联邦法院可以运用联邦普通法填补联邦制定法体系中的空白，例如解释制定法术语。但是，本题没有相关联邦制定法，所以不存在需要填补的空白。\n\n（选项 D）未提出可获救济请求的抗辩，可以在审判结束前的任何时候提出而不被放弃，本题即如此。但是，国会议员的动议应被驳回，因为女性雇员提出了可获损害赔偿救济的 Bivens 诉讼。\n\n学习目标：下列默示诉因允许对联邦官员请求损害赔偿：(1) 因非法搜查与扣押提出的第四修正案请求；(2) 因性别就业歧视提出的第五修正案请求；以及 (3) 因未提供医疗救治提出的第八修正案请求。\n\n参考资料：\n• Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents of the Bureau of Fed. Narcotics, 403 U.S. 388, 389 (1971)（为联邦官员实施的某些宪法侵权确立默示诉因）。\n• Davis v. Passman, 442 U.S. 228, 248–49 (1979)（判定可以对违反第五修正案的国会议员提出默示损害赔偿诉因）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-192","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":192,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A plaintiff residing in State A sues her former employer for $3 million, alleging employment discrimination in violation of federal law, breach of her employment contract, and infringement of her patent. The suit is filed in a federal court located in State B, which is the employer's state of incorporation and principal place of business. Under State B's choice-of-law rules, State B law applies to all state substantive law claims. What substantive law should the federal court apply to the plaintiff's various claims?","choices":{"A":"The court should apply federal law to all three claims.","B":"The court should apply State B law to all three claims.","C":"The court should apply State B law to the breach of contract claim and federal law to the employment discrimination and patent claims.","D":"The court should apply State B law to the employment discrimination and patent claims and federal law to the breach of contract claim."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Choice of law in federal court\n\nType of Applicability Substantive Procedural jurisdiction issues issues\n\nFederal • Claim arises under U.S. Apply federal law question Constitution, federal law, or treaty Diversity • Amount in controversy > Apply state Apply federal $75,000 law* law AND\n\n• Parties are citizens of different states *Federal court must follow choice-of-law rules of state where it sits.\n\nA party may join multiple claims against an opposing party in federal court (as seen here). This ensures judicial efficiency since a party does not need to file separate lawsuits for each claim. But the court must have subject-matter jurisdiction over each claim, which generally arises under either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (eg, federal statute, patent law) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the claim arises under state law (eg, breach of contract), the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000, and the parties are citizens of different states. When a claim arises under federal question jurisdiction, the court must apply federal law to procedural and substantive issues. Here, the court has federal question jurisdiction over the plaintiff's employment discrimination and patent infringement claims since they arise under federal law. Therefore, the State B federal court should apply federal substantive law to these claims (Choices B & D). But when a claim arises under diversity jurisdiction, the court must apply federal procedural law and state substantive law. To determine which state's substantive law applies, the court must follow the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. Here, the court has diversity jurisdiction over the breach of contract claim since it arises under state law, the amount in controversy is $3 million, and the parties are citizens of different states. Therefore, the court must follow State B's choice-of-law rules and apply State B substantive law to this claim (Choices A & D). Educational objective:\n\nFederal courts must apply federal procedural and substantive law to claims arising under federal question jurisdiction. And they must apply federal procedural law—but state substantive law based on the choice-of-law rules of the state where they sit—to claims arising under diversity jurisdiction. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18 (joinder of claims).\n\n• 28 U.S.C § 1652 (federal court must apply federal substantive law to claims arising under federal law but state substantive law to claims arising under state law).","rule":"Federal courts must apply federal procedural and substantive law to claims arising under federal question jurisdiction. And they must apply federal procedural law—but state substantive law based on the choice-of-law rules of the state where they sit—to claims arising under diversity jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff residing in State A sues her former employer for $3 million, alleging employment discrimination in violation of federal law, breach of her employment contract, and infringement of her patent. The suit is filed in a federal court located in State B, which is the employer's state of incorporation and principal place of business. Under State B's choice-of-law rules, State B law applies to all state substantive law claims. What substantive law should the federal court apply to the plaintiff's various claims?","rule":"Federal courts must apply federal procedural and substantive law to claims arising under federal question jurisdiction. And they must apply federal procedural law—but state substantive law based on the choice-of-law rules of the state where they sit—to claims arising under diversity jurisdiction.","analysis":"Choice of law in federal court\n\nType of Applicability Substantive Procedural jurisdiction issues issues\n\nFederal • Claim arises under U.S. Apply federal law question Constitution, federal law, or treaty Diversity • Amount in controversy > Apply state Apply federal $75,000 law* law AND\n\n• Parties are citizens of different states *Federal court must follow choice-of-law rules of state where it sits.\n\nA party may join multiple claims against an opposing party in federal court (as seen here). This ensures judicial efficiency since a party does not need to file separate lawsuits for each claim. But the court must have subject-matter jurisdiction over each claim, which generally arises under either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when the claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (eg, federal statute, patent law) or\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the claim arises under state law (eg, breach of contract), the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000, and the parties are citizens of different states. When a claim arises under federal question jurisdiction, the court must apply federal law to procedural and substantive issues. Here, the court has federal question jurisdiction over the plaintiff's employment discrimination and patent infringement claims since they arise under federal law. Therefore, the State B federal court should apply federal substantive law to these claims (Choices B & D). But when a claim arises under diversity jurisdiction, the court must apply federal procedural law and state substantive law. To determine which state's substantive law applies, the court must follow the choice-of-law rules of the state where it sits. Here, the court has diversity jurisdiction over the breach of contract claim since it arises under state law, the amount in controversy is $3 million, and the parties are citizens of different states. Therefore, the court must follow State B's choice-of-law rules and apply State B substantive law to this claim (Choices A & D). Educational objective:\n\nFederal courts must apply federal procedural and substantive law to claims arising under federal question jurisdiction. And they must apply federal procedural law—but state substantive law based on the choice-of-law rules of the state where they sit—to claims arising under diversity jurisdiction. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18 (joinder of claims).\n\n• 28 U.S.C § 1652 (federal court must apply federal substantive law to claims arising under federal law but state substantive law to claims arising under state law).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18 (joinder of claims).\n\n• 28 U.S.C § 1652 (federal court must apply federal substantive law to claims arising under federal law but state substantive law to claims arising under state law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[580],"sourceAnswerPages":[581,582],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名居住在 A 州的原告起诉前雇主，请求 300 万美元，主张雇主违反联邦法实施就业歧视、违反其雇佣合同并侵犯其专利。诉讼在 B 州联邦法院提起；B 州是雇主的注册成立州和主要营业地。根据 B 州法律选择规则，所有州实体法请求均适用 B 州法律。联邦法院应对原告的各项请求适用何种实体法？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院应对三项请求全部适用联邦法。","B":"法院应对三项请求全部适用 B 州法律。","C":"法院应对违约请求适用 B 州法律，对就业歧视和专利请求适用联邦法。","D":"法院应对就业歧视和专利请求适用 B 州法律，对违约请求适用联邦法。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院的法律选择：\n\n管辖权类型｜适用条件｜实体性问题｜程序性问题\n联邦问题｜请求源于美国宪法、联邦法律或条约｜适用联邦法｜适用联邦法\n多元州籍｜争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且双方是不同州公民｜适用州法*｜适用联邦法\n\n*联邦法院必须遵循其所在地州的法律选择规则。\n\n一方可以在联邦法院将针对相对方的多项请求合并，本题即如此。这能提高司法效率，因为一方无需就每项请求分别提起诉讼。但是，法院必须对每项请求拥有事项管辖权，通常源于以下之一：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求源于美国宪法、条约或联邦法律，例如联邦制定法、专利法；或\n• 多元州籍管辖权——请求源于州法，例如违约；争议金额超过 75,000 美元；且双方是不同州公民。\n\n请求源于联邦问题管辖权时，法院必须对程序性和实体性问题适用联邦法。本题原告的就业歧视和专利侵权请求源于联邦法，所以法院对其拥有联邦问题管辖权。因此，B 州联邦法院应对这些请求适用联邦实体法（选项 B、D）。\n\n请求源于多元州籍管辖权时，法院必须适用联邦程序法和州实体法。为确定适用哪一州的实体法，法院必须遵循其所在地州的法律选择规则。本题违约请求源于州法，争议金额为 300 万美元，双方是不同州公民，所以法院对其拥有多元州籍管辖权。因此，法院必须遵循 B 州法律选择规则，并对该请求适用 B 州实体法（选项 A、D）。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院必须对源于联邦问题管辖权的请求适用联邦程序法和实体法。对于源于多元州籍管辖权的请求，必须适用联邦程序法，并依据法院所在地州的法律选择规则适用州实体法。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18（请求合并）。\n• 28 U.S.C § 1652（联邦法院必须对源于联邦法的请求适用联邦实体法，对源于州法的请求适用州实体法）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-193","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":193,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A bank sued a borrower in federal district court for violating the terms of a mortgage agreement between the two parties. The borrower filed an answer that denied the bank's claim and contained a counterclaim alleging that the bank had engaged in predatory lending in violation of a federal statute. Neither party demanded a jury trial. At the close of evidence, the judge orally stated his findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record. He found that the overwhelming evidence established that the borrower failed to make her monthly mortgage payments on several occasions during the past year. Based on this fact, the judge concluded that the borrower violated the terms of the mortgage agreement. The judge also concluded that the evidence was insufficient for him to find that the bank engaged in predatory lending. The judge then entered a written final judgment in favor of the bank. The borrower properly filed an appeal challenging the judge's finding that she had failed to make the mortgage payments. How will the appellate court likely rule?","choices":{"A":"Affirm the judgment, because the judge is the final arbiter of facts in the absence of a jury.","B":"Affirm the judgment, because there was no clearly erroneous finding of fact.","C":"Reverse the judgment, because a jury must determine issues of fact.","D":"Reverse the judgment, because the findings of fact and conclusions of law were not in writing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference to trial judge's legal Pure legal issues, eg: determination\n• conclusions of law\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial\n• content of jury judge misinterpreted law instructions Clear error • Highly deferential to trial judge's Factual issues in bench factual findings trials, eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• factual determinations Substantial • Highly deferential to jury's factual Factual issues in jury trials, evidence findings eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable jury\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• jury's verdict Abuse of • Highly deferential to trial judge's Discretionary rulings by discretion discretionary decisions judge, eg:\n• Reverse only if decision was\n• grant/denial of new unreasonable/arbitrary trial\n• admissibility of evidence The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial is waived when, as here, no written jury demand is served on the other parties within 14 days after service of the last pleading related to the jury issue. The parties must then proceed with a bench trial, during which the judge serves as the finder of fact and interpreter of the law (Choice C). And after the close of evidence, the judge must provide—orally or in writing—findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record (Choice D). However, the trial judge is not the final arbiter of the law or facts (Choice A). That is because an appellate court can review the trial judge's decision:\n\n• for clear error (high deference) – where a judge's findings of fact will be reversed only if they were clearly erroneous such that no reasonable judge would have made them or\n\n• de novo (no deference) – where a judge's conclusions of law will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the judge misinterpreted the applicable law.\n\nAnd since there was overwhelming evidence that the borrower failed to make her monthly mortgage payments on several occasions, this finding of fact by the district court judge was not clearly erroneous. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm the district court's judgment. Educational objective: In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and interpreter of law. On appeal, the judge's findings of fact are reviewed for clear error (high deference), while its conclusions of law are reviewed de novo (no deference). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).","rule":"In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and interpreter of law. On appeal, the judge's findings of fact are reviewed for clear error (high deference), while its conclusions of law are reviewed de novo (no deference).","irac":{"issue":"A bank sued a borrower in federal district court for violating the terms of a mortgage agreement between the two parties. The borrower filed an answer that denied the bank's claim and contained a counterclaim alleging that the bank had engaged in predatory lending in violation of a federal statute. Neither party demanded a jury trial. At the close of evidence, the judge orally stated his findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record. He found that the overwhelming evidence established that the borrower failed to make her monthly mortgage payments on several occasions during the past year. Based on this fact, the judge concluded that the borrower violated the terms of the mortgage agreement. The judge also concluded that the evidence was insufficient for him to find that the bank engaged in predatory lending. The judge then entered a written final judgment in favor of the bank. The borrower properly filed an appeal challenging the judge's finding that she had failed to make the mortgage payments. How will the appellate court likely rule?","rule":"In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and interpreter of law. On appeal, the judge's findings of fact are reviewed for clear error (high deference), while its conclusions of law are reviewed de novo (no deference).","analysis":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference to trial judge's legal Pure legal issues, eg: determination\n• conclusions of law\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial\n• content of jury judge misinterpreted law instructions Clear error • Highly deferential to trial judge's Factual issues in bench factual findings trials, eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• factual determinations Substantial • Highly deferential to jury's factual Factual issues in jury trials, evidence findings eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable jury\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• jury's verdict Abuse of • Highly deferential to trial judge's Discretionary rulings by discretion discretionary decisions judge, eg:\n• Reverse only if decision was\n• grant/denial of new unreasonable/arbitrary trial\n• admissibility of evidence The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial is waived when, as here, no written jury demand is served on the other parties within 14 days after service of the last pleading related to the jury issue. The parties must then proceed with a bench trial, during which the judge serves as the finder of fact and interpreter of the law (Choice C). And after the close of evidence, the judge must provide—orally or in writing—findings of fact and conclusions of law on the record (Choice D). However, the trial judge is not the final arbiter of the law or facts (Choice A). That is because an appellate court can review the trial judge's decision:\n\n• for clear error (high deference) – where a judge's findings of fact will be reversed only if they were clearly erroneous such that no reasonable judge would have made them or\n\n• de novo (no deference) – where a judge's conclusions of law will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the judge misinterpreted the applicable law.\n\nAnd since there was overwhelming evidence that the borrower failed to make her monthly mortgage payments on several occasions, this finding of fact by the district court judge was not clearly erroneous. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm the district court's judgment. Educational objective: In a bench trial, the judge serves as the finder of fact and interpreter of law. On appeal, the judge's findings of fact are reviewed for clear error (high deference), while its conclusions of law are reviewed de novo (no deference). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[583],"sourceAnswerPages":[584,585],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家银行在联邦地区法院起诉一名借款人违反双方抵押贷款协议条款。借款人答辩否认银行请求，并提出反诉，主张银行违反联邦制定法实施掠夺性放贷。双方均未要求陪审团审判。举证结束时，法官在案卷记录中口头陈述事实认定和法律结论。法官认定，大量压倒性证据证明借款人在过去一年多次未按月偿还抵押贷款，并据此认定她违反抵押贷款协议条款。法官还认定，证据不足以证明银行实施掠夺性放贷，随后登记有利于银行的书面终局判决。借款人适当提起上诉，质疑法官关于她未偿还抵押贷款的事实认定。上诉法院可能如何裁判？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持判决，因为没有陪审团时，法官是事实的终局裁判者。","B":"维持判决，因为不存在明显错误的事实认定。","C":"推翻判决，因为事实问题必须由陪审团裁定。","D":"推翻判决，因为事实认定和法律结论没有采用书面形式。"},"explanationZh":"上诉审查标准：\n\n标准｜审查程度｜适用范围\n重新审查｜不尊重审判法官的法律判断；若有合理理由认为其错误解释法律则推翻｜纯粹法律问题，例如法律结论、陪审团指示内容\n明显错误｜高度尊重审判法官的事实认定；只有任何合理法官都不会作出该认定时才推翻｜法官审判中的事实问题，例如证人可信度、事实认定\n实质证据｜高度尊重陪审团事实认定；只有任何合理陪审团都不会作出该认定时才推翻｜陪审团审判中的事实问题，例如证人可信度、陪审团裁决\n滥用裁量权｜高度尊重法官的裁量性决定；只有决定不合理或武断时才推翻｜裁量性裁定，例如批准或驳回重新审判、证据可采性\n\n如果在与陪审团问题有关的最后一份诉状送达后 14 天内，没有向其他各方送达书面陪审团请求，第七修正案陪审团审判权即被放弃，本题即如此。随后双方必须进行法官审判，由法官担任事实裁判者和法律解释者（选项 C）。举证结束后，法官必须以口头或书面形式在案卷记录中提供事实认定和法律结论（选项 D）。\n\n但审判法官并非法律或事实的最终裁判者（选项 A），因为上诉法院可以审查其决定：\n• 明显错误（高度尊重）——只有法官的事实认定明显错误到任何合理法官都不会作出时才推翻；或\n• 重新审查（不予尊重）——如果上诉法院有合理理由认为法官错误解释适用法律，则推翻法律结论。\n\n大量压倒性证据表明借款人曾多次未按月偿还抵押贷款，所以地区法院法官的该事实认定并非明显错误。因此，上诉法院很可能维持地区法院判决。\n\n学习目标：法官审判中，法官担任事实裁判者和法律解释者。上诉时，法官事实认定适用明显错误标准（高度尊重），法律结论适用重新审查标准（不予尊重）。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a)（事实认定和法律结论）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-194","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":194,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman filed a class action on behalf of thousands of individuals against an insurance company in a federal court in State A. The individuals sought to recover $20 million in statutory penalties for the company's failure to pay claims within the time specified by State A law. The company moved to dismiss the case based on a State A statute that prohibits class actions seeking statutory penalties against insurance companies. Will the federal court likely grant the company's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because a state cannot prohibit class actions.","B":"No, because maintenance of a class action in federal court is a procedural issue governed by federal law.","C":"Yes, because the federal court must apply State A substantive law, and State A prohibits this type of class action.","D":"Yes, because there is no federal common law pertaining to class action requirements."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. When it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural, the court must determine if a valid federal law (ie, federal statute, rule, or constitutional provision) directly addresses the issue. If so, then pursuant to the federal-rule analysis, federal law applies if it is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 23 directly addresses whether a class action can be maintained in federal court. This valid rule* is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right (Choice C). That is because it regulates the manner and means of a lawsuit (ie, who may join the suit)—not the parties' legal rights (ie, what they may sue for). As a result, the court will apply this federal rule and likely deny the company's motion to dismiss based on the State A statute prohibiting this type of class action. *No FRCP has ever been found unconstitutional or invalid. This is likely because the Supreme Court reviews and sends proposed rules to Congress before they are enacted. (Choice A) States can prohibit class actions in state courts. But federal courts must follow FRCP 23 to determine if a class action can be maintained in federal court. (Choice D) Although there is no federal common law pertaining to class action requirements, FRCP 23 provides such requirements. Educational objective: Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that directly addresses an issue so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Shady Grove Orthopedics Assocs., P.A. v. Allstate Ins. Co., 559 U.S. 393, 407–08 (2010) (explaining that class actions in federal court are governed by FRCP 23 and not state law).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (class actions).","rule":"Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that directly addresses an issue so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A woman filed a class action on behalf of thousands of individuals against an insurance company in a federal court in State A. The individuals sought to recover $20 million in statutory penalties for the company's failure to pay claims within the time specified by State A law. The company moved to dismiss the case based on a State A statute that prohibits class actions seeking statutory penalties against insurance companies. Will the federal court likely grant the company's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that directly addresses an issue so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"A federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. When it is unclear whether an issue is substantive or procedural, the court must determine if a valid federal law (ie, federal statute, rule, or constitutional provision) directly addresses the issue. If so, then pursuant to the federal-rule analysis, federal law applies if it is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP 23 directly addresses whether a class action can be maintained in federal court. This valid rule* is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right (Choice C). That is because it regulates the manner and means of a lawsuit (ie, who may join the suit)—not the parties' legal rights (ie, what they may sue for). As a result, the court will apply this federal rule and likely deny the company's motion to dismiss based on the State A statute prohibiting this type of class action. *No FRCP has ever been found unconstitutional or invalid. This is likely because the Supreme Court reviews and sends proposed rules to Congress before they are enacted. (Choice A) States can prohibit class actions in state courts. But federal courts must follow FRCP 23 to determine if a class action can be maintained in federal court. (Choice D) Although there is no federal common law pertaining to class action requirements, FRCP 23 provides such requirements. Educational objective: Under federal-rule analysis, a federal court sitting in diversity must apply a valid federal law that directly addresses an issue so long as the law is arguably procedural and does not modify a substantive right. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Shady Grove Orthopedics Assocs., P.A. v. Allstate Ins. Co., 559 U.S. 393, 407–08 (2010) (explaining that class actions in federal court are governed by FRCP 23 and not state law).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (class actions).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Shady Grove Orthopedics Assocs., P.A. v. Allstate Ins. Co., 559 U.S. 393, 407–08 (2010) (explaining that class actions in federal court are governed by FRCP 23 and not state law).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23 (class actions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[586],"sourceAnswerPages":[587],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子代表数千人在 A 州联邦法院对一家保险公司提起集体诉讼。这些人请求公司支付 2,000 万美元法定罚款，理由是公司未在 A 州法律规定的期限内支付保险请求。公司依据 A 州一项禁止以保险公司为被告、请求法定罚款的集体诉讼制定法，申请驳回案件。联邦法院可能批准公司的驳回动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为州不得禁止集体诉讼。","B":"不会，因为能否在联邦法院维持集体诉讼属于由联邦法支配的程序性问题。","C":"会，因为联邦法院必须适用 A 州实体法，而 A 州禁止这类集体诉讼。","D":"会，因为不存在有关集体诉讼要求的联邦普通法。"},"explanationZh":"行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，对程序性问题适用联邦法。问题属于实体性还是程序性并不明确时，法院必须确定是否有有效的联邦法，即联邦制定法、规则或宪法条款，直接处理该问题。若有，根据联邦规则分析，只要联邦法可以合理视为程序性且不改变实体权利，就适用联邦法。\n\nFRCP 23 直接处理能否在联邦法院维持集体诉讼。这项有效规则*可以合理视为程序性，且不改变实体权利（选项 C），因为它调整的是诉讼方式和手段，即谁可以加入诉讼，而不是双方的法律权利，即可以为何事提起诉讼。因此，法院将适用该联邦规则，并很可能驳回公司依据禁止这类集体诉讼的 A 州制定法提出的驳回动议。\n\n*从未有任何一条《联邦民事诉讼规则》被认定违宪或无效。这很可能是因为拟议规则生效前，最高法院会先审查并将其送交国会。\n\n（选项 A）州可以禁止在州法院提起集体诉讼。但联邦法院必须遵循 FRCP 23，确定能否在联邦法院维持集体诉讼。\n\n（选项 D）虽然不存在有关集体诉讼要求的联邦普通法，但 FRCP 23 规定了这些要求。\n\n学习目标：根据联邦规则分析，行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须适用直接处理问题的有效联邦法，只要该法律可以合理视为程序性且不改变实体权利。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Shady Grove Orthopedics Assocs., P.A. v. Allstate Ins. Co., 559 U.S. 393, 407–08 (2010)（说明联邦法院集体诉讼由 FRCP 23 而非州法支配）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23（集体诉讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-037"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-195","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":195,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"Hundreds of consumers filed a class action against an automotive company in a federal district court for allegedly violating a federal statute prohibiting price-fixing. Over the company's objection, the district court entered an order certifying the consumers as a class. Twelve days later, the company filed with the appellate court a petition for permission to appeal the district court's certification order. Does the appellate court have jurisdiction to hear the appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the appeal was not timely filed.","B":"No, because the order is not a final judgment.","C":"Yes, because certifying a class is a collateral order.","D":"Yes, because the order certified the consumers as a class."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment Appellate courts only have jurisdiction to hear appeals from a final judgment unless an exception applies. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 23(f) provides such an exception for orders granting or denying class action certification. A petition to appeal this order must be filed with the appellate court clerk within 14 days after the order is entered.* Since the company filed its petition 12 days after the district court entered its order certifying the consumers as a class, the appellate court has jurisdiction to hear the appeal (Choices A & B). *The time to file a petition for appeal of the order granting or denying certification is extended to 45 days if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued in an official capacity. (Choice C) A collateral order conclusively resolves an important issue that is (1) separate from the merits of the claim and (2) effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. Certifying a class is not a collateral order because an appellate court can review and remedy an improper certification after a final judgment has been entered. Instead, FRCP 23(f) provides the basis for the exception.\n\nEducational objective: An appellate court has jurisdiction to hear an appeal from an order granting or denying class action certification if the petition for such an appeal is filed with the clerk of the appellate court within 14 days after the order is entered. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(f) (appealing class action certification).","rule":"An appellate court has jurisdiction to hear an appeal from an order granting or denying class action certification if the petition for such an appeal is filed with the clerk of the appellate court within 14 days after the order is entered.","irac":{"issue":"Hundreds of consumers filed a class action against an automotive company in a federal district court for allegedly violating a federal statute prohibiting price-fixing. Over the company's objection, the district court entered an order certifying the consumers as a class. Twelve days later, the company filed with the appellate court a petition for permission to appeal the district court's certification order. Does the appellate court have jurisdiction to hear the appeal?","rule":"An appellate court has jurisdiction to hear an appeal from an order granting or denying class action certification if the petition for such an appeal is filed with the clerk of the appellate court within 14 days after the order is entered.","analysis":"Final-judgment rule\n\nRule Appeal only allowed after final judgment\n\n• If multiple claims/parties, final judgment as to fewer than all claims/parties appropriate if court expressly determines no justifiable reason for delay Exceptions Interlocutory appeals* concerning:\n\n• Injunction (grant/denial)\n• Certification by district court\n• Class action certification\n• Appointment of receiver\n• Admiralty case\n• Collateral-order doctrine\n• Bankruptcy cases (certain orders)\n• Mandamus (petition for writ)\n• Patent infringement order (only accounting left) Mnemonic: In Certain Circumstances, An Appeal Can Be Made Prematurely\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment.\n\n*Appeal from trial court ruling that occurs before entry of final judgment Appellate courts only have jurisdiction to hear appeals from a final judgment unless an exception applies. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 23(f) provides such an exception for orders granting or denying class action certification. A petition to appeal this order must be filed with the appellate court clerk within 14 days after the order is entered.* Since the company filed its petition 12 days after the district court entered its order certifying the consumers as a class, the appellate court has jurisdiction to hear the appeal (Choices A & B). *The time to file a petition for appeal of the order granting or denying certification is extended to 45 days if one of the parties is the United States, a federal agency, or a federal officer or employee sued in an official capacity. (Choice C) A collateral order conclusively resolves an important issue that is (1) separate from the merits of the claim and (2) effectively unreviewable on appeal from a final judgment. Certifying a class is not a collateral order because an appellate court can review and remedy an improper certification after a final judgment has been entered. Instead, FRCP 23(f) provides the basis for the exception.\n\nEducational objective: An appellate court has jurisdiction to hear an appeal from an order granting or denying class action certification if the petition for such an appeal is filed with the clerk of the appellate court within 14 days after the order is entered. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(f) (appealing class action certification).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(f) (appealing class action certification).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[589],"sourceAnswerPages":[590,591],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"数百名消费者在联邦地区法院对一家汽车公司提起集体诉讼，主张公司违反禁止固定价格的联邦制定法。尽管公司反对，地区法院仍发布命令，核准消费者组成集体。十二天后，公司向上诉法院提交请求，申请对地区法院的集体诉讼核准命令提起上诉。上诉法院是否有管辖权审理该上诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为上诉未及时提交。","B":"没有，因为该命令不是终局判决。","C":"有，因为核准集体诉讼属于附带命令。","D":"有，因为该命令核准消费者组成集体。"},"explanationZh":"终局判决规则通常只允许上诉法院审理终局判决，但也存在例外。FRCP 23(f) 对批准或驳回集体诉讼核准的命令规定了例外。请求对该命令上诉，必须在命令登记后 14 天内向上诉法院书记员提交。* 公司在地区法院登记核准消费者组成集体的命令后 12 天提交请求，所以及时，上诉法院有管辖权审理该上诉（选项 A、B）。\n\n*如果一方是美国政府、联邦机构，或以职务身份被诉的联邦官员或雇员，针对批准或驳回核准命令提交上诉请求的期限延长至 45 天。\n\n（选项 C）附带命令终局性地解决一项重要问题，而且该问题：(1) 与请求实体相分离；并且 (2) 无法在终局判决上诉中得到有效审查。核准集体诉讼不属于附带命令，因为终局判决登记后，上诉法院仍可审查并补救不当核准。例外的依据是 FRCP 23(f)，而不是附带命令原则。\n\n学习目标：批准或驳回集体诉讼核准的命令登记后 14 天内，向上诉法院书记员提交上诉请求，上诉法院即有管辖权审理该上诉。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(f)（对集体诉讼核准提起上诉）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-196","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":196,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A bakery incorporated and headquartered in State A had a dispute with a mill incorporated and headquartered in State B over the quality of the flour the mill had delivered to the bakery. The bakery sued the mill in a federal court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $100,000 in damages. The contract between the bakery and the mill contained a clause designating State B courts as the sole venue for litigating disputes arising under the contract. Under precedent of the highest court in State A, forum-selection clauses are unenforceable as against public policy; under U.S. Supreme Court precedent, such clauses are enforceable. The mill has moved to transfer the case to a federal court in State B, citing the forum-selection clause in the parties' contract and asserting the facts that the flour was produced in State B and that the majority of likely witnesses are in State B. Is the court likely to grant the mill's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because State A law treats forum-selection clauses as unenforceable.","B":"No, because the mill should have instead filed a motion to dismiss for improper venue.","C":"Yes, because federal common law makes the forum-selection clause controlling.","D":"Yes, because federal law governs transfers of venue, and it would be more convenient for the witnesses and parties to litigate the claim in State B."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Venue\n\nProper venue Case may be brought in any federal district where:\n\n(28 U.S.C. § 1391) • any defendant resides, if defendants all reside in same state\n• substantial part of events giving rise to claim occurred or property at issue is located\n• any defendant is subject to court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies) Transfer from proper When venue is proper, district court may transfer case to any venue other proper venue for convenience of parties/witnesses & in interest of justice (28 U.S.C. § 1404)\n\nTransfer or dismissal When venue is improper, district court must either: from improper venue\n• dismiss case (28 U.S.C. § 1406) • transfer case to proper venue (if interest of justice requires) A federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states. In a diversity action, the court must apply:\n• state substantive law – for issues regarding legal rights and duties (eg, to determine the elements of a claim or affirmative defense) and\n• federal procedural law – for issues regarding remedies and procedures (eg, to determine venue, sufficiency of pleadings, or rules of discovery). Here, the State A federal court has diversity jurisdiction since the amount in controversy ($100,000) exceeds $75,000 and the bakery and mill are citizens of different states (States A and B, respectively). Therefore, the court must determine what law to apply. Since State A law treats forum-selection clauses as unenforceable, the court could not transfer the case if state law applied. However, transfer of venue is a procedural issue that is governed by federal law (Choice A). Federal law allows a district court to transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to (1) any federal district to which all the parties have consented or (2) another proper venue. Venue is proper in any district where:\n• any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred or property at issue is located or\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies). Here, venue in State A was proper since the flour that gave rise to this suit was delivered to the bakery there (Choice B). But it would be more convenient to litigate the claim in State B, where the flour was produced and most witnesses are located. And since venue would also be proper in State B (where the only defendant resides), the court is likely to grant the mill's motion to transfer. (Choice C) Although forum-selection clauses are enforceable under Supreme Court precedent (ie, federal common law), federal statutory law supersedes federal common law. Therefore, the federal statute governing transfer of venue (28 U.S.C. § 1404) controls. Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the district court must apply state substantive law (eg, elements of a claim or defense) and federal procedural law (eg, venue—the district where a federal case can be heard). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1652 (state law determines substantive issues in federal diversity cases).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (proper venue).\n\n• Stewart Org., Inc. v. Ricoh Corp., 487 U.S. 22, 32 (1988) (holding that 28 U.S.C. § 1404 governs whether a district court should give effect to a forum-selection clause and transfer a case).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404(a) (transfer of venue).","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the district court must apply state substantive law (eg, elements of a claim or defense) and federal procedural law (eg, venue—the district where a federal case can be heard).","irac":{"issue":"A bakery incorporated and headquartered in State A had a dispute with a mill incorporated and headquartered in State B over the quality of the flour the mill had delivered to the bakery. The bakery sued the mill in a federal court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $100,000 in damages. The contract between the bakery and the mill contained a clause designating State B courts as the sole venue for litigating disputes arising under the contract. Under precedent of the highest court in State A, forum-selection clauses are unenforceable as against public policy; under U.S. Supreme Court precedent, such clauses are enforceable. The mill has moved to transfer the case to a federal court in State B, citing the forum-selection clause in the parties' contract and asserting the facts that the flour was produced in State B and that the majority of likely witnesses are in State B. Is the court likely to grant the mill's motion?","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the district court must apply state substantive law (eg, elements of a claim or defense) and federal procedural law (eg, venue—the district where a federal case can be heard).","analysis":"Venue\n\nProper venue Case may be brought in any federal district where:\n\n(28 U.S.C. § 1391) • any defendant resides, if defendants all reside in same state\n• substantial part of events giving rise to claim occurred or property at issue is located\n• any defendant is subject to court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies) Transfer from proper When venue is proper, district court may transfer case to any venue other proper venue for convenience of parties/witnesses & in interest of justice (28 U.S.C. § 1404)\n\nTransfer or dismissal When venue is improper, district court must either: from improper venue\n• dismiss case (28 U.S.C. § 1406) • transfer case to proper venue (if interest of justice requires) A federal court's subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity when the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000 and the parties are citizens of different states. In a diversity action, the court must apply:\n• state substantive law – for issues regarding legal rights and duties (eg, to determine the elements of a claim or affirmative defense) and\n• federal procedural law – for issues regarding remedies and procedures (eg, to determine venue, sufficiency of pleadings, or rules of discovery). Here, the State A federal court has diversity jurisdiction since the amount in controversy ($100,000) exceeds $75,000 and the bakery and mill are citizens of different states (States A and B, respectively). Therefore, the court must determine what law to apply. Since State A law treats forum-selection clauses as unenforceable, the court could not transfer the case if state law applied. However, transfer of venue is a procedural issue that is governed by federal law (Choice A). Federal law allows a district court to transfer venue for the convenience of parties and witnesses, and in the interest of justice, to (1) any federal district to which all the parties have consented or (2) another proper venue. Venue is proper in any district where:\n• any defendant resides, if all defendants reside in the same state\n\n• a substantial part of the events that gave rise to the suit occurred or property at issue is located or\n• any defendant is subject to the court's personal jurisdiction (if neither of the above provisions applies). Here, venue in State A was proper since the flour that gave rise to this suit was delivered to the bakery there (Choice B). But it would be more convenient to litigate the claim in State B, where the flour was produced and most witnesses are located. And since venue would also be proper in State B (where the only defendant resides), the court is likely to grant the mill's motion to transfer. (Choice C) Although forum-selection clauses are enforceable under Supreme Court precedent (ie, federal common law), federal statutory law supersedes federal common law. Therefore, the federal statute governing transfer of venue (28 U.S.C. § 1404) controls. Educational objective: In a federal diversity action, the district court must apply state substantive law (eg, elements of a claim or defense) and federal procedural law (eg, venue—the district where a federal case can be heard). References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1652 (state law determines substantive issues in federal diversity cases).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (proper venue).\n\n• Stewart Org., Inc. v. Ricoh Corp., 487 U.S. 22, 32 (1988) (holding that 28 U.S.C. § 1404 governs whether a district court should give effect to a forum-selection clause and transfer a case).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404(a) (transfer of venue).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1652 (state law determines substantive issues in federal diversity cases).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391 (proper venue).\n\n• Stewart Org., Inc. v. Ricoh Corp., 487 U.S. 22, 32 (1988) (holding that 28 U.S.C. § 1404 governs whether a district court should give effect to a forum-selection clause and transfer a case).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404(a) (transfer of venue).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[592],"sourceAnswerPages":[593,594],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立且总部位于 A 州的面包店，与一家在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州的磨坊，就磨坊交付面粉的质量发生争议。面包店在 A 州联邦法院起诉磨坊违约，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。双方合同包含一项条款，指定 B 州法院为审理合同争议的唯一审判地。依据 A 州最高法院先例，法院选择条款因违反公共政策而不可执行；依据美国最高法院先例，该类条款可以执行。磨坊依据合同中的法院选择条款，申请将案件移送至 B 州联邦法院，并指出面粉在 B 州生产，大多数可能出庭的证人也位于 B 州。法院可能批准磨坊动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为 A 州法律认定法院选择条款不可执行。","B":"不会，因为磨坊本应申请以审判地不适当为由驳回。","C":"会，因为联邦普通法使法院选择条款具有控制力。","D":"会，因为审判地移送由联邦法支配，而且在 B 州审理对证人和双方更为便利。"},"explanationZh":"审判地：\n\n适当审判地（28 U.S.C. § 1391）｜案件可在下列任何联邦地区提起：\n• 任何被告居住地，但所有被告必须均居住于同一州；\n• 构成请求基础的重大部分事件发生地或争议财产所在地；\n• 任何被告受法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在上述规定均不适用时。\n\n从适当审判地移送（28 U.S.C. § 1404）｜审判地适当时，地区法院可为方便双方和证人并符合司法利益，将案件移送至其他任何适当审判地。\n\n从不适当审判地移送或驳回（28 U.S.C. § 1406）｜审判地不适当时，地区法院必须：\n• 驳回案件；或\n• 在司法利益需要时，将案件移送至适当审判地。\n\n争议金额超过 75,000 美元且双方是不同州公民时，联邦法院依据多元州籍拥有事项管辖权。在多元州籍诉讼中，法院必须适用：\n• 州实体法——处理法律权利和义务，例如确定请求或积极抗辩的构成要件；以及\n• 联邦程序法——处理救济和程序，例如确定审判地、诉状是否充分或证据开示规则。\n\n本题争议金额 100,000 美元，超过 75,000 美元，面包店与磨坊分别为 A 州和 B 州公民，所以 A 州联邦法院拥有多元州籍管辖权。A 州法律认定法院选择条款不可执行，因此若适用州法，法院不能移送案件。但审判地移送属于由联邦法支配的程序性问题（选项 A）。\n\n联邦法允许地区法院为方便双方和证人并符合司法利益，将审判地移送至：(1) 所有当事人同意的任何联邦地区；或 (2) 另一适当审判地。下列地区的审判地适当：\n• 任何被告居住地，但所有被告必须均居住于同一州；\n• 构成诉讼基础的重大部分事件发生地或争议财产所在地；或\n• 任何被告受法院对人管辖权约束的地区，但仅在上述规定均不适用时。\n\n本题引起诉讼的面粉交付给位于 A 州的面包店，所以 A 州审判地适当（选项 B）。但在面粉生产地且多数证人所在地 B 州审理更为便利。B 州也是适当审判地，因为唯一被告居住于该州。因此，法院很可能批准磨坊的移送动议。\n\n（选项 C）尽管依据最高法院先例，即联邦普通法，法院选择条款可以执行，但联邦制定法优先于联邦普通法。因此，由规范审判地移送的联邦制定法 28 U.S.C. § 1404 控制。\n\n学习目标：在联邦多元州籍诉讼中，地区法院必须适用州实体法（例如请求或抗辩构成要件）和联邦程序法（例如审判地，即联邦案件可审理的地区）。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1652（州法决定联邦多元州籍案件的实体问题）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1391（适当审判地）。\n• Stewart Org., Inc. v. Ricoh Corp., 487 U.S. 22, 32 (1988)（判定地区法院是否应执行法院选择条款并移送案件，由 28 U.S.C. § 1404 支配）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1404(a)（审判地移送）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-197","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":197,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A woman brought a diversity action against a man in a federal district court in State A to recover damages for an accident that occurred in State B. The woman hired a process server, who delivered a copy of the summons and complaint to the man's wife at their home. The man filed an answer in which he moved to dismiss the woman's action for improper service of process. Laws in State A and State B require that service of process be personally delivered to the defendant, and their choice-of-law rules provide that a court must apply the law of the state where the injury occurred. Should the court grant the man's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man waived this defense by failing to assert it in a pre-answer motion.","B":"No, because the man was served in accordance with federal law.","C":"Yes, because the man was not served in accordance with State A law.","D":"Yes, because the man was not served in accordance with State B law."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Choice of law in federal court\n\nType of Applicability Substantive Procedural jurisdiction issues issues\n\nFederal • Claim arises under U.S. Apply federal law question Constitution, federal law, or treaty Diversity • Amount in controversy > Apply state Apply federal $75,000 law* law AND\n\n• Parties are citizens of different states *Federal court must follow choice-of-law rules of state where it sits.\n\nA federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. Procedural issues include filing deadlines, discovery rules, and service-of-process requirements. Therefore, federal law (not state law) governs service of process (Choices C & D). FRCP 4(e) provides that a plaintiff can properly serve process on a defendant-individual in the United States by:\n• following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized to receive process) or\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion.\n\nHere, the process server delivered a copy of the summons and complaint to the man's wife at their home. The wife is of suitable age and discretion since she is old enough to possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation. Therefore, service of process was proper under federal law, and the court will likely deny the man's motion to dismiss the woman's action. (Choice A) Under FRCP 12(h), a defendant waives an improper-service-of-process defense if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or the defendant's answer—whichever occurs first. Here, the man did not waive this defense because he asserted it in his answer and no pre-answer motion had been filed. Educational objective:\n\nIn a federal diversity action, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. Therefore, federal service-of-process rules always apply in federal court. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Miss. Publ'g Corp. v. Murphree, 326 U.S. 438, 444–45 (1946) (explaining that serving process is a procedural issue that is governed by federal law).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on an individual located in the United States).","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. Therefore, federal service-of-process rules always apply in federal court. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","irac":{"issue":"A woman brought a diversity action against a man in a federal district court in State A to recover damages for an accident that occurred in State B. The woman hired a process server, who delivered a copy of the summons and complaint to the man's wife at their home. The man filed an answer in which he moved to dismiss the woman's action for improper service of process. Laws in State A and State B require that service of process be personally delivered to the defendant, and their choice-of-law rules provide that a court must apply the law of the state where the injury occurred. Should the court grant the man's motion to dismiss?","rule":"In a federal diversity action, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. Therefore, federal service-of-process rules always apply in federal court. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","analysis":"Choice of law in federal court\n\nType of Applicability Substantive Procedural jurisdiction issues issues\n\nFederal • Claim arises under U.S. Apply federal law question Constitution, federal law, or treaty Diversity • Amount in controversy > Apply state Apply federal $75,000 law* law AND\n\n• Parties are citizens of different states *Federal court must follow choice-of-law rules of state where it sits.\n\nA federal court sitting in diversity must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. Procedural issues include filing deadlines, discovery rules, and service-of-process requirements. Therefore, federal law (not state law) governs service of process (Choices C & D). FRCP 4(e) provides that a plaintiff can properly serve process on a defendant-individual in the United States by:\n• following the rules of the state where the court sits or service is made\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant personally (or to an agent authorized to receive process) or\n\n• having process delivered to the defendant's dwelling and left with a resident of suitable age and discretion.\n\nHere, the process server delivered a copy of the summons and complaint to the man's wife at their home. The wife is of suitable age and discretion since she is old enough to possess the limited capacity necessary to comprehend the situation. Therefore, service of process was proper under federal law, and the court will likely deny the man's motion to dismiss the woman's action. (Choice A) Under FRCP 12(h), a defendant waives an improper-service-of-process defense if it is not asserted in a pre-answer motion or the defendant's answer—whichever occurs first. Here, the man did not waive this defense because he asserted it in his answer and no pre-answer motion had been filed. Educational objective:\n\nIn a federal diversity action, the court must apply state law to substantive issues and federal law to procedural issues. Therefore, federal service-of-process rules always apply in federal court. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Miss. Publ'g Corp. v. Murphree, 326 U.S. 438, 444–45 (1946) (explaining that serving process is a procedural issue that is governed by federal law).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on an individual located in the United States).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Miss. Publ'g Corp. v. Murphree, 326 U.S. 438, 444–45 (1946) (explaining that serving process is a procedural issue that is governed by federal law).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e) (service of process on an individual located in the United States).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[595],"sourceAnswerPages":[596,597],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在 A 州联邦地区法院对一名男子提起多元州籍诉讼，请求赔偿发生于 B 州的一起事故造成的损害。女子雇用送达员，后者在男子家中将传票和起诉状副本交给男子的妻子。男子提交答辩状，并在其中以诉讼文书送达不当为由申请驳回女子诉讼。A 州和 B 州法律均要求诉讼文书亲自交给被告；两州的法律选择规则均规定，法院必须适用伤害发生州的法律。法院应批准男子的驳回动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为男子没有在答辩前动议中主张该抗辩，因而已经放弃。","B":"不应，因为男子的诉讼文书送达符合联邦法。","C":"应当，因为男子未依 A 州法律得到送达。","D":"应当，因为男子未依 B 州法律得到送达。"},"explanationZh":"联邦法院的法律选择：\n\n管辖权类型｜适用条件｜实体性问题｜程序性问题\n联邦问题｜请求源于美国宪法、联邦法律或条约｜适用联邦法｜适用联邦法\n多元州籍｜争议金额超过 75,000 美元，且双方是不同州公民｜适用州法*｜适用联邦法\n\n*联邦法院必须遵循其所在地州的法律选择规则。\n\n行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，对程序性问题适用联邦法。程序性问题包括提交期限、证据开示规则和诉讼文书送达要求。因此，诉讼文书送达由联邦法而非州法支配（选项 C、D）。\n\nFRCP 4(e) 规定，在美国境内可以通过以下方式向被告个人适当送达诉讼文书：\n• 遵循法院所在地州或送达地州的规则；\n• 将文书亲自交给被告或有权接收文书的代理人；或\n• 将文书交至被告住所，留给居住于该处且年龄与判断能力适当的人。\n\n本题送达员在男子家中将传票和起诉状副本交给他的妻子。妻子年龄与判断能力适当，因为她已具备理解该情形所需的有限能力。因此，诉讼文书送达符合联邦法，法院很可能驳回男子的驳回动议。\n\n（选项 A）根据 FRCP 12(h)，被告如果没有在答辩前动议或答辩状中（以较早者为准）主张诉讼文书送达不当抗辩，即视为放弃。本题男子没有提交答辩前动议，并在答辩状中主张了该抗辩，所以没有放弃。\n\n学习目标：在联邦多元州籍诉讼中，法院必须对实体性问题适用州法，对程序性问题适用联邦法。因此，联邦法院始终适用联邦诉讼文书送达规则。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Miss. Publ'g Corp. v. Murphree, 326 U.S. 438, 444–45 (1946)（说明诉讼文书送达属于由联邦法支配的程序性问题）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(e)（向位于美国境内的个人送达诉讼文书）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-198","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":198,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A farm employee sued four pesticide companies in a federal district court. The employee claimed that his exposure to the pesticide manufactured by one of these companies caused him to suffer a terminal illness. The companies filed a joint motion to dismiss the claim on the ground that the employee did not allege which company manufactured the pesticide to which he was exposed. The court denied the companies' motion, concluding that the employee did not need to specifically allege which company manufactured the pesticide because market-share liability applied. The court also informed the parties that all requests for jury instructions must be filed by the start of trial. Neither side filed a request by the deadline. At the close of evidence, the companies filed a request that the court use the companies' jury instructions on market-share liability. The court denied the companies' request on the ground that the companies filed their request too late. Did the court properly deny the companies' request?","choices":{"A":"No, because although the companies did not submit their request by the deadline, they could not have reasonably anticipated that market-share liability would be an issue.","B":"No, because the companies submitted their request at the close of evidence.","C":"Yes, because only the court may formulate jury instructions.","D":"Yes, because the companies did not submit their request by the deadline and should have reasonably anticipated that market-share liability would be an issue."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Jury instructions\n\n(FRCP 51)\n\nRequests Party:\n\n• must file request by close of evidence unless earlier, reasonable deadline set by court\n• may file request after close of evidence when: – request relates to issue that party could not have reasonably anticipated by deadline or – court permits late submission related to any issue Instructions Court:\n\n• must inform parties of proposed instructions before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• must allow parties to object on record & outside jury's presence before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• may consider objection made after court instructs jury if instruction constituted plain error\n• may instruct jury at any time before discharge Objections Party:\n\n• must distinctly state objection & its grounds on record\n• must object at court-appointed time or promptly after learning of proposed instruction FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court must provide jury instructions that advise the jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. These instructions can be formulated by the court or the parties (Choice C). Each party can request specific jury instructions before or at the close of evidence unless the court sets an earlier, reasonable deadline—eg, by the start of trial (as seen here) (Choice B).* Additionally, a request may be filed after the close of evidence if:\n• the request relates to an issue that the party could not have reasonably anticipated by the deadline or\n• the court permits a late submission that relates to any issue. Here, the companies filed no jury instructions by the deadline set by the court. And the companies should have reasonably anticipated that market-share liability would be an\n\nissue. That is because the court concluded that market-share liability applied to this case when it denied the companies' motion to dismiss (Choice A). Therefore, the court properly denied the companies' jury-instruction request on market-share liability. *To request a jury instruction, a party generally must file a written request with the court and furnish it to every other party. Some courts allow verbal requests, but they are highly disfavored. Educational objective: A party must request specific jury instructions at the close of evidence unless an earlier, reasonable deadline is set by the court. A request can be filed after the close of evidence only when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a) (jury-instruction requests).","rule":"A party must request specific jury instructions at the close of evidence unless an earlier, reasonable deadline is set by the court. A request can be filed after the close of evidence only when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission.","irac":{"issue":"A farm employee sued four pesticide companies in a federal district court. The employee claimed that his exposure to the pesticide manufactured by one of these companies caused him to suffer a terminal illness. The companies filed a joint motion to dismiss the claim on the ground that the employee did not allege which company manufactured the pesticide to which he was exposed. The court denied the companies' motion, concluding that the employee did not need to specifically allege which company manufactured the pesticide because market-share liability applied. The court also informed the parties that all requests for jury instructions must be filed by the start of trial. Neither side filed a request by the deadline. At the close of evidence, the companies filed a request that the court use the companies' jury instructions on market-share liability. The court denied the companies' request on the ground that the companies filed their request too late. Did the court properly deny the companies' request?","rule":"A party must request specific jury instructions at the close of evidence unless an earlier, reasonable deadline is set by the court. A request can be filed after the close of evidence only when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission.","analysis":"Jury instructions\n\n(FRCP 51)\n\nRequests Party:\n\n• must file request by close of evidence unless earlier, reasonable deadline set by court\n• may file request after close of evidence when: – request relates to issue that party could not have reasonably anticipated by deadline or – court permits late submission related to any issue Instructions Court:\n\n• must inform parties of proposed instructions before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• must allow parties to object on record & outside jury's presence before instructing jury & closing arguments\n• may consider objection made after court instructs jury if instruction constituted plain error\n• may instruct jury at any time before discharge Objections Party:\n\n• must distinctly state objection & its grounds on record\n• must object at court-appointed time or promptly after learning of proposed instruction FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court must provide jury instructions that advise the jurors on their deliberation procedures and the applicable law. These instructions can be formulated by the court or the parties (Choice C). Each party can request specific jury instructions before or at the close of evidence unless the court sets an earlier, reasonable deadline—eg, by the start of trial (as seen here) (Choice B).* Additionally, a request may be filed after the close of evidence if:\n• the request relates to an issue that the party could not have reasonably anticipated by the deadline or\n• the court permits a late submission that relates to any issue. Here, the companies filed no jury instructions by the deadline set by the court. And the companies should have reasonably anticipated that market-share liability would be an\n\nissue. That is because the court concluded that market-share liability applied to this case when it denied the companies' motion to dismiss (Choice A). Therefore, the court properly denied the companies' jury-instruction request on market-share liability. *To request a jury instruction, a party generally must file a written request with the court and furnish it to every other party. Some courts allow verbal requests, but they are highly disfavored. Educational objective: A party must request specific jury instructions at the close of evidence unless an earlier, reasonable deadline is set by the court. A request can be filed after the close of evidence only when (1) the instruction relates to an issue that could not have been reasonably anticipated by the deadline or (2) the court grants permission. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a) (jury-instruction requests).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a) (jury-instruction requests).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[598],"sourceAnswerPages":[599,600],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名农场雇员在联邦地区法院起诉四家农药公司。雇员主张，接触其中一家公司制造的农药使其患上绝症。各公司共同申请驳回，理由是雇员没有指明其接触的农药由哪家公司制造。法院驳回动议，认定市场份额责任适用，所以雇员无需具体指明制造商。法院还通知双方，所有陪审团指示请求必须在审判开始前提交。双方均未在期限内提交。举证结束时，各公司请求法院采用其拟定的市场份额责任陪审团指示。法院以请求提交过迟为由予以驳回。法院是否适当驳回各公司的请求？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为各公司虽未在期限内提交，但不可能合理预见市场份额责任会成为问题。","B":"不适当，因为各公司在举证结束时提交了请求。","C":"适当，因为只有法院可以拟定陪审团指示。","D":"适当，因为各公司没有在期限内提交请求，而且本应合理预见市场份额责任会成为问题。"},"explanationZh":"陪审团指示（FRCP 51）：\n\n请求——当事人必须在举证结束时前提交，除非法院规定了更早且合理的期限；在请求涉及期限前不可能合理预见的问题，或法院准许就任何问题逾期提交时，可以在举证结束后提交。\n\n指示——法院必须在向陪审团作出指示及结案陈词前，将拟议指示告知双方，并允许双方在案卷记录中、陪审团不在场时提出异议；指示构成明显错误时，可以考虑作出指示后才提出的异议；法院可在陪审团解散前的任何时候作出指示。\n\n异议——当事人必须在案卷记录中明确陈述异议及理由，并在法院指定时间或得知拟议指示后立即提出。\n\n法院必须提供陪审团指示，告知陪审员评议程序和适用法律。这些指示可以由法院或双方拟定（选项 C）。除非法院规定更早且合理的期限，例如审判开始时（本题即如此），各方可在举证结束前或结束时请求特定指示（选项 B）。* 此外，请求涉及期限前不可能合理预见的问题，或法院准许就任何问题逾期提交时，可以在举证结束后提交。\n\n本题各公司未在法院规定期限内提交指示，而且本应合理预见市场份额责任会成为问题，因为法院驳回其驳回动议时已认定该责任适用（选项 A）。因此，法院适当驳回各公司的市场份额责任指示请求。\n\n*请求陪审团指示通常必须向法院提交书面请求，并提供给其他各方。有些法院允许口头请求，但非常不鼓励。\n\n学习目标：一方必须在举证结束时请求特定陪审团指示，除非法院规定更早且合理的期限。只有指示涉及期限前不可能合理预见的问题，或法院准许时，才能在举证结束后提交。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 51(a)（陪审团指示请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-199","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":199,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A computer company fired one of its female employees from her sales position for failing to meet her sales quotas. The female employee sued the company in federal court, alleging that the company violated a federal law that prohibits sex discrimination in the workplace. Specifically, the female employee claimed that she failed to meet her quotas because the company did not introduce her to potential sales leads or provide her training opportunities in the same way that it did for its male employees. The company conceded that it did not provide the female employee these opportunities but argued that the federal law did not apply to these actions. After discovery but before trial, the female employee filed a motion for summary judgment. The trial court granted the motion based on its conclusion that the federal law did apply to the company's actions. The company appealed the trial court's grant of summary judgment. On appeal, what standard of review will the appellate court apply?","choices":{"A":"Abuse of discretion.","B":"Clear and convincing.","C":"Clearly erroneous.","D":"De novo."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference to trial judge's legal Pure legal issues, eg: determination\n• conclusions of law\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial\n• content of jury judge misinterpreted law instructions Clear error • Highly deferential to trial judge's Factual issues in bench factual findings trials, eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• factual determinations Substantial • Highly deferential to jury's factual Factual issues in jury trials, evidence findings eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable jury\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• jury's verdict Abuse of • Highly deferential to trial judge's Discretionary rulings by discretion discretionary decisions judge, eg:\n• Reverse only if decision was\n• grant/denial of new unreasonable/arbitrary trial\n• admissibility of evidence A party can appeal an adverse final judgment issued by the trial court. The standard of review used to review that judgment depends on the issues in dispute. Pure legal issues are reviewed de novo, which allows the appellate court to decide the issue anew without deferring to the lower court's legal interpretation. And the judgment will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the lower court misinterpreted the law. A final judgment is the last decision issued by a court that settles the parties' rights and resolves all issues in dispute. This includes summary judgments, where a judgment is entered without a full trial because (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the moving party is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. As a result, a grant of summary judgment is a pure legal issue that the appellate court will review de novo.\n\n(Choice A) A trial court's discretionary (not purely legal) rulings are reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this highly deferential standard, the trial court's decision will only be reversed if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. (Choice B) \"Clear and convincing\" is an evidentiary burden of proof—not an appellate standard of review—that is often used in civil suits involving fraud, child custody, and restraining orders. It requires the party with this burden to show that its contention is highly or substantially likely to be true. (Choice C) The clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review the trial court's factual (not legal) findings in a bench trial. This standard gives great deference to the trial court, so the court's factual finding will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. Educational objective: The de novo standard of review applies to pure legal issues—eg, grant of summary judgment. Under this standard, no deference is given to the trial court's legal interpretations, and its ruling will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the trial court misinterpreted the law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Borchardt Rifle Corp. v. Cook, 684 F.3d 1037, 1041–42 (10th Cir. 2012) (holding that de novo review applies to appellate review of trial court's summary judgment decision).","rule":"The de novo standard of review applies to pure legal issues—eg, grant of summary judgment. Under this standard, no deference is given to the trial court's legal interpretations, and its ruling will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the trial court misinterpreted the law.","irac":{"issue":"A computer company fired one of its female employees from her sales position for failing to meet her sales quotas. The female employee sued the company in federal court, alleging that the company violated a federal law that prohibits sex discrimination in the workplace. Specifically, the female employee claimed that she failed to meet her quotas because the company did not introduce her to potential sales leads or provide her training opportunities in the same way that it did for its male employees. The company conceded that it did not provide the female employee these opportunities but argued that the federal law did not apply to these actions. After discovery but before trial, the female employee filed a motion for summary judgment. The trial court granted the motion based on its conclusion that the federal law did apply to the company's actions. The company appealed the trial court's grant of summary judgment. On appeal, what standard of review will the appellate court apply?","rule":"The de novo standard of review applies to pure legal issues—eg, grant of summary judgment. Under this standard, no deference is given to the trial court's legal interpretations, and its ruling will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the trial court misinterpreted the law.","analysis":"Appellate standards of review\n\nStandard Level of review Applicability\n\nDe novo • No deference to trial judge's legal Pure legal issues, eg: determination\n• conclusions of law\n• Reverse if reasonably believe trial\n• content of jury judge misinterpreted law instructions Clear error • Highly deferential to trial judge's Factual issues in bench factual findings trials, eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable judge\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• factual determinations Substantial • Highly deferential to jury's factual Factual issues in jury trials, evidence findings eg:\n• Reverse if no reasonable jury\n• credibility of would have made this finding witnesses\n• jury's verdict Abuse of • Highly deferential to trial judge's Discretionary rulings by discretion discretionary decisions judge, eg:\n• Reverse only if decision was\n• grant/denial of new unreasonable/arbitrary trial\n• admissibility of evidence A party can appeal an adverse final judgment issued by the trial court. The standard of review used to review that judgment depends on the issues in dispute. Pure legal issues are reviewed de novo, which allows the appellate court to decide the issue anew without deferring to the lower court's legal interpretation. And the judgment will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the lower court misinterpreted the law. A final judgment is the last decision issued by a court that settles the parties' rights and resolves all issues in dispute. This includes summary judgments, where a judgment is entered without a full trial because (1) there is no genuine issue of material fact and (2) the moving party is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. As a result, a grant of summary judgment is a pure legal issue that the appellate court will review de novo.\n\n(Choice A) A trial court's discretionary (not purely legal) rulings are reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this highly deferential standard, the trial court's decision will only be reversed if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. (Choice B) \"Clear and convincing\" is an evidentiary burden of proof—not an appellate standard of review—that is often used in civil suits involving fraud, child custody, and restraining orders. It requires the party with this burden to show that its contention is highly or substantially likely to be true. (Choice C) The clearly-erroneous standard of review is used to review the trial court's factual (not legal) findings in a bench trial. This standard gives great deference to the trial court, so the court's factual finding will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. Educational objective: The de novo standard of review applies to pure legal issues—eg, grant of summary judgment. Under this standard, no deference is given to the trial court's legal interpretations, and its ruling will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the trial court misinterpreted the law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Borchardt Rifle Corp. v. Cook, 684 F.3d 1037, 1041–42 (10th Cir. 2012) (holding that de novo review applies to appellate review of trial court's summary judgment decision).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).\n\n• Borchardt Rifle Corp. v. Cook, 684 F.3d 1037, 1041–42 (10th Cir. 2012) (holding that de novo review applies to appellate review of trial court's summary judgment decision).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[601],"sourceAnswerPages":[602,603],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家电脑公司因一名女性销售员未达到销售配额将其解雇。女性雇员在联邦法院起诉公司，主张公司违反禁止职场性别歧视的联邦法。具体而言，她称自己未达到配额，是因为公司不像对待男性雇员那样向她介绍潜在销售线索或提供培训机会。公司承认没有向她提供这些机会，但主张该联邦法不适用于这些行为。证据开示后、审判前，女性雇员申请简易判决。审判法院认定联邦法适用于公司的行为，因而批准动议。公司对该简易判决提起上诉。上诉法院将适用哪一审查标准？","choicesZh":{"A":"滥用裁量权。","B":"清楚且令人信服。","C":"明显错误。","D":"重新审查。"},"explanationZh":"纯粹法律问题适用重新审查标准，上诉法院可以不尊重下级法院的法律解释而重新决定；如有合理理由认为下级法院错误解释法律，则推翻。终局判决是确定双方权利并解决全部争议问题的最后决定，其中包括简易判决：因不存在重大事实真实争议且动议方依法有权获得判决，法院无需完整审判即登记判决。因此，批准简易判决属于纯粹法律问题，上诉法院将重新审查。\n\n（选项 A）审判法院的裁量性而非纯粹法律裁定适用滥用裁量权标准。该标准高度尊重，只有决定明显武断或不合理时才推翻。\n\n（选项 B）“清楚且令人信服”是证据证明责任而非上诉审查标准，常用于欺诈、子女监护和限制令等民事诉讼，要求承担责任的一方证明其主张极有可能或实质上可能为真。\n\n（选项 C）明显错误标准用于审查法官审判中审判法院的事实而非法律认定。该标准高度尊重，只有任何合理法官都不会作出该事实认定时才推翻。\n\n学习目标：重新审查标准适用于纯粹法律问题，例如批准简易判决。该标准不尊重审判法院的法律解释；上诉法院有合理理由认为其错误解释法律时，将推翻裁定。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 56（简易判决）。\n• Borchardt Rifle Corp. v. Cook, 684 F.3d 1037, 1041–42 (10th Cir. 2012)（判定上诉审查审判法院简易判决决定时适用重新审查）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-200","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":200,"topic":"Claim Preclusion & Issue Preclusion","question":"A motorcyclist and her friend collided with an automobile at an intersection. The motorcyclist sued the automobile driver in a state court in State A to recover for damages to her motorcycle and personal injuries. The friend did not join this suit. The jury returned a verdict for the automobile driver, specifically finding that she was not negligent. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. Three months later, the friend brought a diversity action in a federal court in State B against the automobile driver to recover damages for the personal injuries he sustained in the collision. The automobile driver defended on the ground that the prior judgment established that she was not negligent. State A permits mutual and nonmutual issue preclusion. State B permits only mutual issue preclusion. Is the automobile driver likely to succeed in her defense?","choices":{"A":"No, because State B does not permit nonmutual issue preclusion.","B":"No, because the friend was not a party to the first action.","C":"Yes, because State A permits nonmutual issue preclusion.","D":"Yes, because the jury specifically found that the automobile driver was not negligent."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) is a defense that bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. There are two types of issue preclusion:\n• Mutual – which allows parties from the first action to assert issue preclusion in the second action\n• Nonmutual – which allows nonparties from the first action to assert issue preclusion in the second action Both types of issue preclusion can be asserted against parties to the first action or their privies, absent limited exceptions. However, they cannot be asserted against a nonparty to the first action because it would deprive that party of the opportunity to fully and fairly litigate his/her claims and defenses. Here, the motorcyclist sued the automobile driver in the first action. The jury specifically found that the automobile driver was not negligent, and the court entered a judgment in her favor. The friend then sued the automobile driver in the second action. Since the friend was not a party to the first action, the automobile driver cannot assert issue preclusion against him (Choice D). Therefore, this defense is unlikely to succeed. (Choices A & C) Since the judgment was entered by a state court in State A, the federal court in State B must apply State A's preclusion law. Although State A allows nonmutual issue preclusion, it cannot be asserted against the friend since he was not a party to the first action. Educational objective: Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment. This doctrine can only be asserted against parties to the first action because they have had an opportunity to fully and fairly litigate their claims/defenses. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).\n\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893 (2008) (stating that a nonparty is not bound by a judgment because each person has the due process right to have his/her day in court).","rule":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment. This doctrine can only be asserted against parties to the first action because they have had an opportunity to fully and fairly litigate their claims/defenses.","irac":{"issue":"A motorcyclist and her friend collided with an automobile at an intersection. The motorcyclist sued the automobile driver in a state court in State A to recover for damages to her motorcycle and personal injuries. The friend did not join this suit. The jury returned a verdict for the automobile driver, specifically finding that she was not negligent. The court entered a final judgment consistent with the verdict. Three months later, the friend brought a diversity action in a federal court in State B against the automobile driver to recover damages for the personal injuries he sustained in the collision. The automobile driver defended on the ground that the prior judgment established that she was not negligent. State A permits mutual and nonmutual issue preclusion. State B permits only mutual issue preclusion. Is the automobile driver likely to succeed in her defense?","rule":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment. This doctrine can only be asserted against parties to the first action because they have had an opportunity to fully and fairly litigate their claims/defenses.","analysis":"Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) is a defense that bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and essential to a valid final judgment. There are two types of issue preclusion:\n• Mutual – which allows parties from the first action to assert issue preclusion in the second action\n• Nonmutual – which allows nonparties from the first action to assert issue preclusion in the second action Both types of issue preclusion can be asserted against parties to the first action or their privies, absent limited exceptions. However, they cannot be asserted against a nonparty to the first action because it would deprive that party of the opportunity to fully and fairly litigate his/her claims and defenses. Here, the motorcyclist sued the automobile driver in the first action. The jury specifically found that the automobile driver was not negligent, and the court entered a judgment in her favor. The friend then sued the automobile driver in the second action. Since the friend was not a party to the first action, the automobile driver cannot assert issue preclusion against him (Choice D). Therefore, this defense is unlikely to succeed. (Choices A & C) Since the judgment was entered by a state court in State A, the federal court in State B must apply State A's preclusion law. Although State A allows nonmutual issue preclusion, it cannot be asserted against the friend since he was not a party to the first action. Educational objective: Issue preclusion (ie, collateral estoppel) bars the relitigation of issues that were actually litigated, determined, and necessary to a valid final judgment. This doctrine can only be asserted against parties to the first action because they have had an opportunity to fully and fairly litigate their claims/defenses. References\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).\n\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893 (2008) (stating that a nonparty is not bound by a judgment because each person has the due process right to have his/her day in court).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982) (defining issue preclusion).\n\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893 (2008) (stating that a nonparty is not bound by a judgment because each person has the due process right to have his/her day in court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[604],"sourceAnswerPages":[605],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名摩托车手与朋友在路口同一辆汽车相撞。摩托车手在 A 州州法院起诉汽车驾驶员，请求赔偿摩托车损失和人身伤害。朋友没有加入该诉讼。陪审团作出有利于汽车驾驶员的裁决，并明确认定她没有过失，法院依裁决登记终局判决。三个月后，朋友在 B 州联邦法院对汽车驾驶员提起多元州籍诉讼，请求赔偿碰撞所致人身伤害。汽车驾驶员以先前判决已经确立其无过失为由进行抗辩。A 州允许相互性和非相互性争点排除；B 州只允许相互性争点排除。汽车驾驶员的抗辩可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为 B 州不允许非相互性争点排除。","B":"不会，因为朋友不是第一案当事人。","C":"会，因为 A 州允许非相互性争点排除。","D":"会，因为陪审团明确认定汽车驾驶员没有过失。"},"explanationZh":"争点排除（即附带禁反言）是一项抗辩，禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。争点排除有两种：\n• 相互性——允许第一案当事人在第二案中主张争点排除；\n• 非相互性——允许第一案非当事人在第二案中主张争点排除。\n\n除有限例外外，两种争点排除都只能对第一案当事人或与其具有法律利益关系者主张。不得对第一案非当事人主张，否则会剥夺该方充分、公平争讼其请求和抗辩的机会。\n\n本题第一案由摩托车手起诉汽车驾驶员。陪审团明确认定汽车驾驶员无过失，法院登记有利于她的判决。随后，朋友在第二案起诉汽车驾驶员。朋友并非第一案当事人，所以汽车驾驶员不能对其主张争点排除（选项 D）。因此，该抗辩不太可能成功。\n\n（选项 A、C）第一项判决由 A 州州法院登记，所以 B 州联邦法院必须适用 A 州排除法。A 州虽允许非相互性争点排除，但不能对朋友主张，因为他不是第一案当事人。\n\n学习目标：争点排除（即附带禁反言）禁止重新争讼已经实际争讼、裁定且为有效终局判决所必要的问题。该原则只能对第一案当事人主张，因为他们已有机会充分、公平争讼其请求或抗辩。\n\n参考资料：\n• Restatement (Second) of Judgments § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1982)（定义争点排除）。\n• Taylor v. Sturgell, 553 U.S. 880, 893 (2008)（指出非当事人不受判决约束，因为每个人都有依正当程序获得亲自出庭机会的权利）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-061"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-201","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":201,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A man took skydiving lessons over the course of several days. During the man's first solo jump out of an airplane without being attached to an instructor, the man panicked and ripped his backpack containing the parachute. The man was not able to release his parachute and died upon impact. The man's estate brought a diversity action in federal court against the skydiving company requesting $3 million in damages. The estate demanded a jury trial. At the conclusion of trial, the judge gave the jury a verdict form that instructed the jury to determine which party should prevail and to answer specific questions related to each factual issue. When asked whether the man was contributorily negligent, the jury answered \"Yes.\" When asked to give the percentage of the man's negligence, the jury answered \"0%.\" The jury awarded the man's estate $3 million. The applicable law requires damages to be reduced by the percentage of the plaintiff's contributory negligence. What action should the court take upon receiving the jury's verdict and answers?","choices":{"A":"Direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict.","B":"Disregard the jury's verdict and enter a judgment that is equitable.","C":"Enter a judgment in favor of the man but reduce the amount awarded based on the evidence.","D":"Enter a judgment in favor of the man for $3 million."},"answer":"A","explanation":"General verdict with answers\n\n(FRCP 49(b))\n\nAnswers consistent with verdict Judge must enter judgment consistent with verdict & answers\n\nAnswers inconsistent with verdict Judge must:\n\n• order new trial\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict or\n• disregard verdict & enter judgment consistent with answers Answers inconsistent with each Judge must: other & verdict\n• order new trial or\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe presiding judge in a jury trial has the discretion to choose which type of verdict to use—a general verdict, special verdict, or general verdict with answers. If the judge selects a general verdict with answers and the jury's answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, the judge must order a new trial or direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict. Here, the judge selected a general verdict with answers by instructing the jury to determine which party should prevail and answer specific questions related to the factual issues in the case. The jury found that the man was contributorily negligent and 0% negligent (inconsistent answers). The jury also awarded the man the full amount demanded with no reduction for his negligence, despite finding him contributorily negligent (answer inconsistent with verdict). Therefore, the court should direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict (Choice D). (Choice B) When the jury's answers are inconsistent with the verdict, the judge may disregard the verdict and enter a judgment consistent with the answers. But a judge cannot enter a judgment that he/she thinks is equitable (ie, fair) because the jury is responsible for determining the factual issues.\n\n(Choice C) A court may alter an allegedly excessive award of damages through remittitur, which gives the winning party a choice between a reduction in damages or a new trial on damages. However, the jury must first return a consistent verdict. Educational objective: A general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and answer specific questions on each factual issue. If the answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, the judge must either order a new trial or direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b) (general verdict with answers).","rule":"A general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and answer specific questions on each factual issue. If the answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, the judge must either order a new trial or direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict.","irac":{"issue":"A man took skydiving lessons over the course of several days. During the man's first solo jump out of an airplane without being attached to an instructor, the man panicked and ripped his backpack containing the parachute. The man was not able to release his parachute and died upon impact. The man's estate brought a diversity action in federal court against the skydiving company requesting $3 million in damages. The estate demanded a jury trial. At the conclusion of trial, the judge gave the jury a verdict form that instructed the jury to determine which party should prevail and to answer specific questions related to each factual issue. When asked whether the man was contributorily negligent, the jury answered \"Yes.\" When asked to give the percentage of the man's negligence, the jury answered \"0%.\" The jury awarded the man's estate $3 million. The applicable law requires damages to be reduced by the percentage of the plaintiff's contributory negligence. What action should the court take upon receiving the jury's verdict and answers?","rule":"A general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and answer specific questions on each factual issue. If the answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, the judge must either order a new trial or direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict.","analysis":"General verdict with answers\n\n(FRCP 49(b))\n\nAnswers consistent with verdict Judge must enter judgment consistent with verdict & answers\n\nAnswers inconsistent with verdict Judge must:\n\n• order new trial\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict or\n• disregard verdict & enter judgment consistent with answers Answers inconsistent with each Judge must: other & verdict\n• order new trial or\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe presiding judge in a jury trial has the discretion to choose which type of verdict to use—a general verdict, special verdict, or general verdict with answers. If the judge selects a general verdict with answers and the jury's answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, the judge must order a new trial or direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict. Here, the judge selected a general verdict with answers by instructing the jury to determine which party should prevail and answer specific questions related to the factual issues in the case. The jury found that the man was contributorily negligent and 0% negligent (inconsistent answers). The jury also awarded the man the full amount demanded with no reduction for his negligence, despite finding him contributorily negligent (answer inconsistent with verdict). Therefore, the court should direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict (Choice D). (Choice B) When the jury's answers are inconsistent with the verdict, the judge may disregard the verdict and enter a judgment consistent with the answers. But a judge cannot enter a judgment that he/she thinks is equitable (ie, fair) because the jury is responsible for determining the factual issues.\n\n(Choice C) A court may alter an allegedly excessive award of damages through remittitur, which gives the winning party a choice between a reduction in damages or a new trial on damages. However, the jury must first return a consistent verdict. Educational objective: A general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and answer specific questions on each factual issue. If the answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, the judge must either order a new trial or direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b) (general verdict with answers).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b) (general verdict with answers).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[607],"sourceAnswerPages":[608,609],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子连续数日参加跳伞课程。男子第一次在没有与教练连接的情况下独自跳出飞机时惊慌失措，撕破了装有降落伞的背包。他无法释放降落伞，落地时死亡。男子遗产在联邦法院对跳伞公司提起多元州籍诉讼，请求 300 万美元损害赔偿，并要求陪审团审判。审判结束时，法官向陪审团提供一份裁决表，指示陪审团决定哪一方应胜诉，并回答与每项事实问题有关的具体问题。当被问及男子是否存在共同过失时，陪审团回答“是”；被问及男子的过失比例时，回答“0%”。陪审团判给男子遗产 300 万美元。适用法律要求按照原告共同过失的比例减少损害赔偿。法院收到陪审团裁决和答案后应采取什么措施？","choicesZh":{"A":"指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决。","B":"不理会陪审团裁决，登记法院认为公平的判决。","C":"登记有利于男子的判决，但依据证据减少裁赔金额。","D":"登记有利于男子、金额为 300 万美元的判决。"},"explanationZh":"附带问题答案的一般裁决（FRCP 49(b)）：\n\n答案与裁决一致｜法官必须依照裁决和答案登记判决\n答案与裁决不一致｜法官必须：\n• 命令重新审判；\n• 指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决；或\n• 不理会裁决，并依照答案登记判决\n各答案相互不一致且与裁决不一致｜法官必须：\n• 命令重新审判；或\n• 指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n主持陪审团审判的法官有裁量权选择一般裁决、特别裁决或附带问题答案的一般裁决。如果选择附带问题答案的一般裁决，而陪审团答案相互不一致且与裁决不一致，法官必须命令重新审判，或指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决。\n\n本题法官要求陪审团决定哪一方胜诉，并回答案件事实问题的具体问题，因而选择了附带问题答案的一般裁决。陪审团既认定男子存在共同过失，又认定其过失比例为 0%，答案相互矛盾。陪审团还判给男子请求的全部金额，完全没有因其过失而减少，故答案也与裁决不一致。因此，法院应指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决（选项 D）。\n\n（选项 B）陪审团答案与裁决不一致时，法官可以不理会裁决，并依照答案登记判决。但法官不能登记自己认为公平的判决，因为事实问题由陪审团裁定。\n\n（选项 C）法院可以通过减额裁决改变被指过高的损害赔偿，让胜诉方在减少损害赔偿与就损害赔偿重新审判之间选择。但陪审团必须先作出一致的裁决。\n\n学习目标：附带问题答案的一般裁决要求陪审团决定哪一方胜诉，并回答每项事实问题的具体问题。如果答案相互不一致且与裁决不一致，法官必须命令重新审判，或指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b)（附带问题答案的一般裁决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-202","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":202,"topic":"Jury Trial & Trial Motions","question":"A plaintiff brought a class action in federal court against a nationwide grocery-store chain for violating a federal disability-rights statute that requires public buildings to be wheelchair-accessible. The plaintiff sought an injunction requiring the grocery chain to modify the entrances to its stores to comply with the statute. The plaintiff demanded a jury trial. The statute is silent on the right to a jury trial. The grocery chain does not want a jury trial. What is the grocery chain's strongest argument that the plaintiff is not entitled to a jury trial?","choices":{"A":"Class actions are so complex as to be outside the jury's competence to decide.","B":"Disability-rights claims did not exist at common law when the Seventh Amendment was adopted.","C":"The remedy the plaintiff seeks is primarily equitable in nature.","D":"The statute is silent on the right to a jury trial."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Right to jury trial in civil case\n\nRight • Legal claim – seeks monetary remedy to compensate for loss (eg, tort/contract damages) AND\n\n• > $20 in controversy No • Equitable claim – seeks nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction, specific right performance) since legal remedy is inadequate OR\n\n• ≤ $20 in controversy The Seventh Amendment guarantees the right to a jury trial in federal civil cases where the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars.* This right applies only to legal claims—ie, claims that seek a monetary remedy to compensate for loss (eg, damages). It does not apply to equitable claims—ie, claims that seek a nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction). Therefore, the grocery-store chain's strongest argument that the plaintiff is not entitled to a jury trial is that the injunction the plaintiff seeks is primarily equitable in nature. *The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial does not apply to state civil cases because this right has never been incorporated against the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. (Choice A) A jury is considered competent to decide class actions and other complex cases. As a result, the right to a jury trial cannot be defeated simply because the case is complex. (Choice B) The right to a jury trial depends on the type of relief sought, not whether the asserted claim existed at common law when the Seventh Amendment was adopted. (Choice D) The Seventh Amendment preserves the right to a jury trial in statutory actions that provide for claims analogous to claims tried at common law in which legal/monetary relief is sought. This is true even if the statute is silent on the right to a jury trial. Educational objective: The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial applies to federal civil cases when the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars and a party asserts a legal claim (ie, a claim seeking a monetary remedy). However, this right does not apply when a party asserts an equitable claim (ie, a claim seeking a nonmonetary remedy). References\n\n• U.S. Const. amend. VII (right to a jury trial).\n\n• Feltner v. Columbia Pictures Television, Inc., 523 U.S. 340, 347–48 (1998) (explaining that the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial only applies in legal, not equitable, actions).","rule":"The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial applies to federal civil cases when the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars and a party asserts a legal claim (ie, a claim seeking a monetary remedy). However, this right does not apply when a party asserts an equitable claim (ie, a claim seeking a nonmonetary remedy).","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a class action in federal court against a nationwide grocery-store chain for violating a federal disability-rights statute that requires public buildings to be wheelchair-accessible. The plaintiff sought an injunction requiring the grocery chain to modify the entrances to its stores to comply with the statute. The plaintiff demanded a jury trial. The statute is silent on the right to a jury trial. The grocery chain does not want a jury trial. What is the grocery chain's strongest argument that the plaintiff is not entitled to a jury trial?","rule":"The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial applies to federal civil cases when the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars and a party asserts a legal claim (ie, a claim seeking a monetary remedy). However, this right does not apply when a party asserts an equitable claim (ie, a claim seeking a nonmonetary remedy).","analysis":"Right to jury trial in civil case\n\nRight • Legal claim – seeks monetary remedy to compensate for loss (eg, tort/contract damages) AND\n\n• > $20 in controversy No • Equitable claim – seeks nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction, specific right performance) since legal remedy is inadequate OR\n\n• ≤ $20 in controversy The Seventh Amendment guarantees the right to a jury trial in federal civil cases where the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars.* This right applies only to legal claims—ie, claims that seek a monetary remedy to compensate for loss (eg, damages). It does not apply to equitable claims—ie, claims that seek a nonmonetary remedy (eg, injunction). Therefore, the grocery-store chain's strongest argument that the plaintiff is not entitled to a jury trial is that the injunction the plaintiff seeks is primarily equitable in nature. *The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial does not apply to state civil cases because this right has never been incorporated against the states through the Fourteenth Amendment due process clause. (Choice A) A jury is considered competent to decide class actions and other complex cases. As a result, the right to a jury trial cannot be defeated simply because the case is complex. (Choice B) The right to a jury trial depends on the type of relief sought, not whether the asserted claim existed at common law when the Seventh Amendment was adopted. (Choice D) The Seventh Amendment preserves the right to a jury trial in statutory actions that provide for claims analogous to claims tried at common law in which legal/monetary relief is sought. This is true even if the statute is silent on the right to a jury trial. Educational objective: The Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial applies to federal civil cases when the amount in controversy exceeds 20 dollars and a party asserts a legal claim (ie, a claim seeking a monetary remedy). However, this right does not apply when a party asserts an equitable claim (ie, a claim seeking a nonmonetary remedy). References\n\n• U.S. Const. amend. VII (right to a jury trial).\n\n• Feltner v. Columbia Pictures Television, Inc., 523 U.S. 340, 347–48 (1998) (explaining that the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial only applies in legal, not equitable, actions).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• U.S. Const. amend. VII (right to a jury trial).\n\n• Feltner v. Columbia Pictures Television, Inc., 523 U.S. 340, 347–48 (1998) (explaining that the Seventh Amendment right to a jury trial only applies in legal, not equitable, actions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[610],"sourceAnswerPages":[611,612],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦法院对一家全国性连锁杂货店提起集体诉讼，主张其违反一项要求公共建筑可供轮椅通行的联邦残疾人权利制定法。原告请求禁令，要求连锁店改造各门店入口以符合制定法。原告要求陪审团审判。制定法没有规定陪审团审判权。连锁店不希望进行陪审团审判。连锁店关于原告无权要求陪审团审判的最有力论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"集体诉讼过于复杂，超出陪审团的裁判能力。","B":"第七修正案通过时，普通法中不存在残疾人权利请求。","C":"原告请求的救济主要具有衡平法性质。","D":"制定法没有规定陪审团审判权。"},"explanationZh":"民事案件中的陪审团审判权：\n\n有权：\n• 法律请求——请求以金钱救济补偿损失，例如侵权或违约损害赔偿；并且\n• 争议金额超过 20 美元。\n\n无权：\n• 衡平法请求——因法律救济不足而请求非金钱救济，例如禁令、实际履行；或\n• 争议金额不超过 20 美元。\n\n第七修正案保障争议金额超过 20 美元的联邦民事案件中的陪审团审判权。* 该权利只适用于法律请求，即请求以金钱救济补偿损失的请求，例如损害赔偿；不适用于衡平法请求，即请求非金钱救济的请求，例如禁令。因此，连锁杂货店最有力的论点是，原告请求的禁令主要具有衡平法性质。\n\n*第七修正案陪审团审判权不适用于州民事案件，因为该权利从未通过第十四修正案正当程序条款并入而适用于各州。\n\n（选项 A）陪审团被认为有能力裁判集体诉讼及其他复杂案件。因此，不能仅因案件复杂就剥夺陪审团审判权。\n\n（选项 B）陪审团审判权取决于请求救济的类型，而不是第七修正案通过时普通法中是否存在该请求。\n\n（选项 D）制定法设立与普通法审判请求类似且请求法律或金钱救济的诉因时，即使制定法没有规定陪审团审判权，第七修正案仍保留该权利。\n\n学习目标：第七修正案陪审团审判权适用于争议金额超过 20 美元且一方提出法律请求，即请求金钱救济的联邦民事案件。但该权利不适用于衡平法请求，即请求非金钱救济的请求。\n\n参考资料：\n• U.S. Const. amend. VII（陪审团审判权）。\n• Feltner v. Columbia Pictures Television, Inc., 523 U.S. 340, 347–48 (1998)（说明第七修正案陪审团审判权只适用于法律诉讼，不适用于衡平法诉讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-203","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":203,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in a federal district court for negligence. The defendant demanded a jury trial. At the close of evidence, both parties submitted proposed jury instructions to the court. The court denied these submissions and informed the parties of its proposed instructions. The plaintiff objected, stating that the court's proposed instructions materially misstated the law. The court overruled this objection and delivered its proposed instructions to the jury. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant, and the plaintiff appealed. On appeal, the plaintiff has argued that the court improperly denied her proposed jury instructions and that the contents of the court's jury instructions materially misstated the law. What standard(s) of review should the appellate court apply to these arguments?","choices":{"A":"Abuse-of-discretion review as to both arguments.","B":"Abuse-of-discretion review as to the denial of the woman's proposed instructions and de novo review as to the contents of the court's jury instructions.","C":"De novo review as to both arguments.","D":"De novo review as to the denial of the woman's proposed instructions and abuse-of-discretion review as to the contents of the court's jury instructions."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An appellate court will review a district court's jury instructions under the following standards:\n• Abuse of discretion (high deference) – where the district court's discretionary decisions (eg, the creation of jury instructions) will be reversed if they were unreasonable or arbitrary\n\n• De novo (no deference) – where a district court's conclusions of law (eg, the contents of jury instructions) will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the court misinterpreted or misstated the law\n\nThe woman's first argument is that the district court improperly denied her proposed jury instructions. Since a district court has broad discretion to decide what language to use in its instructions to the jury, this discretionary ruling should be reviewed for abuse of discretion (Choices C & D). However, the instructions given to the jury must correctly state the governing law. And since the woman's second argument is that the contents of the court's jury instructions materially misstated the law, this legal issue should be reviewed de novo (Choices A & D) Educational objective: On appeal, a district court's discretionary decisions (eg, creation of jury instructions) are reviewed for abuse of discretion while its conclusions of law (eg, contents of jury instructions) are reviewed de novo. References\n• Crowley v. EpiCept Corp., 883 F.3d 739, 747 (9th Cir. 2018) (explaining that abuse-of-discretion review applies to the creation of jury instructions and de novo review applies to whether the jury instructions correctly state the law).","rule":"On appeal, a district court's discretionary decisions (eg, creation of jury instructions) are reviewed for abuse of discretion while its conclusions of law (eg, contents of jury instructions) are reviewed de novo.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in a federal district court for negligence. The defendant demanded a jury trial. At the close of evidence, both parties submitted proposed jury instructions to the court. The court denied these submissions and informed the parties of its proposed instructions. The plaintiff objected, stating that the court's proposed instructions materially misstated the law. The court overruled this objection and delivered its proposed instructions to the jury. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the defendant, and the plaintiff appealed. On appeal, the plaintiff has argued that the court improperly denied her proposed jury instructions and that the contents of the court's jury instructions materially misstated the law. What standard(s) of review should the appellate court apply to these arguments?","rule":"On appeal, a district court's discretionary decisions (eg, creation of jury instructions) are reviewed for abuse of discretion while its conclusions of law (eg, contents of jury instructions) are reviewed de novo.","analysis":"An appellate court will review a district court's jury instructions under the following standards:\n• Abuse of discretion (high deference) – where the district court's discretionary decisions (eg, the creation of jury instructions) will be reversed if they were unreasonable or arbitrary\n\n• De novo (no deference) – where a district court's conclusions of law (eg, the contents of jury instructions) will be reversed if the appellate court reasonably believes that the court misinterpreted or misstated the law\n\nThe woman's first argument is that the district court improperly denied her proposed jury instructions. Since a district court has broad discretion to decide what language to use in its instructions to the jury, this discretionary ruling should be reviewed for abuse of discretion (Choices C & D). However, the instructions given to the jury must correctly state the governing law. And since the woman's second argument is that the contents of the court's jury instructions materially misstated the law, this legal issue should be reviewed de novo (Choices A & D) Educational objective: On appeal, a district court's discretionary decisions (eg, creation of jury instructions) are reviewed for abuse of discretion while its conclusions of law (eg, contents of jury instructions) are reviewed de novo. References\n• Crowley v. EpiCept Corp., 883 F.3d 739, 747 (9th Cir. 2018) (explaining that abuse-of-discretion review applies to the creation of jury instructions and de novo review applies to whether the jury instructions correctly state the law).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Crowley v. EpiCept Corp., 883 F.3d 739, 747 (9th Cir. 2018) (explaining that abuse-of-discretion review applies to the creation of jury instructions and de novo review applies to whether the jury instructions correctly state the law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[613],"sourceAnswerPages":[614],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院对一名被告提起多元州籍过失诉讼。被告要求陪审团审判。举证结束时，双方均向法院提交拟议陪审团指示。法院驳回这些指示，并将自己的拟议指示告知双方。原告提出异议，称法院拟议指示严重错误陈述法律。法院驳回异议，并向陪审团作出其拟议指示。陪审团判被告胜诉，原告提起上诉。原告在上诉中主张，法院错误驳回她的拟议陪审团指示，而且法院陪审团指示的内容严重错误陈述法律。上诉法院应分别对这些论点适用什么审查标准？","choicesZh":{"A":"两个论点均适用滥用裁量权审查。","B":"驳回原告拟议指示适用滥用裁量权审查；法院指示内容适用重新审查。","C":"两个论点均适用重新审查。","D":"驳回原告拟议指示适用重新审查；法院指示内容适用滥用裁量权审查。"},"explanationZh":"上诉法院依据以下标准审查地区法院的陪审团指示：\n• 滥用裁量权（高度尊重）——审查地区法院的裁量性决定，例如拟定陪审团指示；决定不合理或武断时予以推翻。\n• 重新审查（不予尊重）——审查地区法院的法律结论，例如陪审团指示内容；上诉法院有合理理由认为法院错误解释或陈述法律时予以推翻。\n\n原告的第一个论点是，地区法院错误驳回她拟议的陪审团指示。地区法院对于在陪审团指示中采用何种措辞享有广泛裁量权，所以该裁量性裁定应适用滥用裁量权审查（选项 C、D）。\n\n但是，向陪审团作出的指示必须正确陈述支配性法律。原告的第二个论点是法院陪审团指示内容严重错误陈述法律，所以该法律问题应受重新审查（选项 A、D）。\n\n学习目标：上诉时，地区法院的裁量性决定，例如拟定陪审团指示，适用滥用裁量权审查；其法律结论，例如陪审团指示内容，适用重新审查。\n\n参考资料：\n• Crowley v. EpiCept Corp., 883 F.3d 739, 747 (9th Cir. 2018)（说明拟定陪审团指示适用滥用裁量权审查，而陪审团指示是否正确陈述法律适用重新审查）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-054"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-204","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":204,"topic":"Erie / Choice of Law","question":"A man was injured when a military drone crashed into his house in State A. The drone was manufactured for the U.S. government by a company incorporated and headquartered in State B pursuant to the government's design specifications. The man filed a diversity action against the company in federal court in State A to recover $100,000 in damages. The man claimed that the drone was defectively designed. In its answer, the company asserted a defense that a manufacturer of military equipment for the U.S. government cannot be held liable for injuries caused by defectively designed equipment. The company's defense is not explicitly authorized by a federal statute, but many federal courts in other jurisdictions have recognized this defense as federal common law. State A law allows all manufacturers to be held liable for their defectively designed products. Should the federal court look to federal law or state law to decide if the company can validly assert this defense?","choices":{"A":"Federal law, because the man filed the suit in federal court.","B":"Federal law, because the uniquely federal interest at stake significantly conflicts with state law.","C":"State law, because the company's defense is not explicitly authorized by a federal statute.","D":"State law, because there is no federal common law on this issue."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Applicability of substantive federal common law\n\nImplied cause of Allows recovery of damages against federal official for violation of action Fourth, Fifth, or Eighth Amendment\n\nDiversity actions Governs substantive issues when uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with operation of state law\n\nGap-filling Fills gaps in federal statutory / regulatory scheme\n\nStatutory Interprets federal statutes, federal regulations, or U.S. Constitution interpretation\n\nA federal court sitting in diversity (as seen here) almost always applies state law to substantive issues (eg, elements of a defense). However, in extremely limited circumstances, federal common law governs substantive issues. Federal common law consists of rules of law created by federal courts in judicial decisions instead of by Congress in statutes. In a diversity action, substantive federal common law applies when a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with the operation of state law. In Boyle v. United Technologies Corp., SCOTUS held that protecting military-equipment manufacturers from liability is a uniquely federal interest. That is because imposing liability on such manufacturers would adversely affect their contracts with the federal government (eg, by increasing costs). This uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with State A's law that allows all manufacturers to be held liable for defectively designed products. As a result, the court should look to federal common law to decide if the company's defense is valid. (Choice A) The fact that the man filed suit in federal court does not automatically mean that federal law applies. In the vast majority of diversity actions filed in federal court, state law governs substantive issues. (Choice C) Although the company's defense is not explicitly authorized by a federal statute, federal common law recognizes this defense. And since State A's law significantly conflicts with the uniquely federal interest posed by this defense, federal common law governs the defense's applicability. (Choice D) There is federal common law on this issue because many federal courts have recognized the company's defense. Educational objective:\n\nA federal court sitting in diversity must generally apply state law to substantive issues. However, federal common law governs substantive issues in the rare instance when a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with the operation of state law. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States. References\n• Boyle v. United Technologies Corp., 487 U.S. 500, 506–07 (1988) (holding that federal common law governs a defense in a diversity case because a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with state law).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 364 (explaining that federal courts may create federal common law when a significant conflict exists between a unique federal interest and state law).","rule":"A federal court sitting in diversity must generally apply state law to substantive issues. However, federal common law governs substantive issues in the rare instance when a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with the operation of state law. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","irac":{"issue":"A man was injured when a military drone crashed into his house in State A. The drone was manufactured for the U.S. government by a company incorporated and headquartered in State B pursuant to the government's design specifications. The man filed a diversity action against the company in federal court in State A to recover $100,000 in damages. The man claimed that the drone was defectively designed. In its answer, the company asserted a defense that a manufacturer of military equipment for the U.S. government cannot be held liable for injuries caused by defectively designed equipment. The company's defense is not explicitly authorized by a federal statute, but many federal courts in other jurisdictions have recognized this defense as federal common law. State A law allows all manufacturers to be held liable for their defectively designed products. Should the federal court look to federal law or state law to decide if the company can validly assert this defense?","rule":"A federal court sitting in diversity must generally apply state law to substantive issues. However, federal common law governs substantive issues in the rare instance when a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with the operation of state law. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States.","analysis":"Applicability of substantive federal common law\n\nImplied cause of Allows recovery of damages against federal official for violation of action Fourth, Fifth, or Eighth Amendment\n\nDiversity actions Governs substantive issues when uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with operation of state law\n\nGap-filling Fills gaps in federal statutory / regulatory scheme\n\nStatutory Interprets federal statutes, federal regulations, or U.S. Constitution interpretation\n\nA federal court sitting in diversity (as seen here) almost always applies state law to substantive issues (eg, elements of a defense). However, in extremely limited circumstances, federal common law governs substantive issues. Federal common law consists of rules of law created by federal courts in judicial decisions instead of by Congress in statutes. In a diversity action, substantive federal common law applies when a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with the operation of state law. In Boyle v. United Technologies Corp., SCOTUS held that protecting military-equipment manufacturers from liability is a uniquely federal interest. That is because imposing liability on such manufacturers would adversely affect their contracts with the federal government (eg, by increasing costs). This uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with State A's law that allows all manufacturers to be held liable for defectively designed products. As a result, the court should look to federal common law to decide if the company's defense is valid. (Choice A) The fact that the man filed suit in federal court does not automatically mean that federal law applies. In the vast majority of diversity actions filed in federal court, state law governs substantive issues. (Choice C) Although the company's defense is not explicitly authorized by a federal statute, federal common law recognizes this defense. And since State A's law significantly conflicts with the uniquely federal interest posed by this defense, federal common law governs the defense's applicability. (Choice D) There is federal common law on this issue because many federal courts have recognized the company's defense. Educational objective:\n\nA federal court sitting in diversity must generally apply state law to substantive issues. However, federal common law governs substantive issues in the rare instance when a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with the operation of state law. SCOTUS = Supreme Court of the United States. References\n• Boyle v. United Technologies Corp., 487 U.S. 500, 506–07 (1988) (holding that federal common law governs a defense in a diversity case because a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with state law).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 364 (explaining that federal courts may create federal common law when a significant conflict exists between a unique federal interest and state law).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Boyle v. United Technologies Corp., 487 U.S. 500, 506–07 (1988) (holding that federal common law governs a defense in a diversity case because a uniquely federal interest significantly conflicts with state law).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 364 (explaining that federal courts may create federal common law when a significant conflict exists between a unique federal interest and state law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[616],"sourceAnswerPages":[617,618],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一架军用无人机坠毁在 A 州一名男子的住宅，导致男子受伤。该无人机由一家在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州的公司，依照美国政府的设计规格为政府制造。男子在 A 州联邦法院对公司提起多元州籍诉讼，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿，主张无人机设计存在缺陷。公司在答辩状中主张，为美国政府制造军事装备的制造商，不应对设计缺陷装备造成的伤害承担责任。该抗辩没有得到联邦制定法明确授权，但其他法域的许多联邦法院已将其承认为联邦普通法。A 州法律允许追究所有制造商对其设计缺陷产品的责任。联邦法院应依据联邦法还是州法，决定公司能否有效主张该抗辩？","choicesZh":{"A":"联邦法，因为男子在联邦法院提起诉讼。","B":"联邦法，因为所涉独特联邦利益与州法存在重大冲突。","C":"州法，因为公司抗辩没有得到联邦制定法明确授权。","D":"州法，因为该问题不存在联邦普通法。"},"explanationZh":"实体联邦普通法的适用：\n\n默示诉因｜允许因联邦官员违反第四、第五或第八修正案而向其请求损害赔偿\n多元州籍诉讼｜独特联邦利益与州法适用存在重大冲突时，支配实体性问题\n填补空白｜填补联邦制定法或规章体系中的空白\n制定法解释｜解释联邦制定法、联邦规章或美国宪法\n\n行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院（本题即如此）几乎总是对实体性问题，例如抗辩构成要件，适用州法。但是，在极为有限的情形下，实体性问题由联邦普通法支配。联邦普通法是联邦法院通过司法判决而非国会通过制定法创设的法律规则。\n\n在多元州籍诉讼中，如果独特联邦利益与州法适用存在重大冲突，就适用实体联邦普通法。在 Boyle v. United Technologies Corp. 案中，美国最高法院认定，保护军事装备制造商免于承担责任是一项独特联邦利益，因为追究这类制造商的责任会对其与联邦政府的合同产生不利影响，例如增加成本。\n\n该独特联邦利益与 A 州允许追究所有制造商设计缺陷产品责任的法律存在重大冲突。因此，法院应依据联邦普通法决定公司抗辩是否有效。\n\n（选项 A）男子在联邦法院提起诉讼这一事实，并不自动意味着适用联邦法。在绝大多数联邦多元州籍诉讼中，实体性问题由州法支配。\n\n（选项 C）公司抗辩虽未得到联邦制定法明确授权，但联邦普通法承认该抗辩。由于 A 州法律与该抗辩涉及的独特联邦利益存在重大冲突，抗辩是否适用由联邦普通法支配。\n\n（选项 D）该问题存在联邦普通法，因为许多联邦法院已经承认公司的抗辩。\n\n学习目标：行使多元州籍管辖权的联邦法院通常必须对实体性问题适用州法。但是，在独特联邦利益与州法适用存在重大冲突的少数情形中，实体性问题由联邦普通法支配。SCOTUS = 美国最高法院。\n\n参考资料：\n• Boyle v. United Technologies Corp., 487 U.S. 500, 506–07 (1988)（判定因独特联邦利益与州法存在重大冲突，多元州籍案件中的抗辩由联邦普通法支配）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 364（说明独特联邦利益与州法存在重大冲突时，联邦法院可以创设联邦普通法）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-205","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":205,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A woman sued a landlord in an appropriate federal district court under a federal statute that prohibits landlords from discriminating against a potential tenant based on familial status. Her complaint included a request for $10,000. The woman claimed that the landlord denied her rental application because she had two young children. The woman also included a demand for a jury trial in her complaint. No law would have prohibited the woman from occupying the apartment. Under the federal statute, the plaintiff has the burden to establish a prima facie case that the defendant unlawfully discriminated against the plaintiff. If the plaintiff meets this burden, then the defendant must provide a nondiscriminatory reason why the plaintiff was not selected as a tenant. If the defendant does so, the burden shifts back to the plaintiff to show that the defendant's purported reason was a mere pretext for discrimination. At the end of the trial, the woman demanded that all factual issues be submitted to the jury. She also requested that the jury return its verdict with answers to specific questions relating to each of those issues. The landlord consented to this type of verdict. The judge gave the jury a proper verdict form that asked the jury to answer the specific questions that the woman had requested and to determine which party should prevail. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the woman and awarded her $10,000 in damages. In its answers to the specific questions, the jury indicated that the woman had presented sufficient evidence that the landlord discriminated against her. It also found that the landlord had a nondiscriminatory reason for denying the woman's application and that this reason was not a pretext for discrimination. Which action would be IMPROPER for the judge to take?","choices":{"A":"Direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict.","B":"Enter a judgment in favor of the landlord.","C":"Enter a judgment in favor of the woman.","D":"Order a new trial."},"answer":"C","explanation":"General verdict with answers\n\n(FRCP 49(b))\n\nAnswers consistent with verdict Judge must enter judgment consistent with verdict & answers\n\nAnswers inconsistent with verdict Judge must:\n\n• order new trial\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict or\n• disregard verdict & enter judgment consistent with answers Answers inconsistent with each Judge must: other & verdict\n• order new trial or\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA verdict is the jury's determination of factual issues in a case. There are three types of verdicts—general verdicts, special verdicts, and general verdicts with answers—and the judge has the discretion to choose which verdict to use. Here, the judge asked the jury to return a general verdict with answers when he instructed them to (1) find in favor of, and award damages to, a particular party and (2) answer specific questions on each factual issue. If the jury's answers and verdict are consistent, the judge must enter a judgment consistent with those determinations. But when, as here, the jury's answers (no pretext for discrimination) and verdict (in favor of the woman) are inconsistent, the judge must:\n• order a new trial\n• direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict or\n• disregard the jury's verdict and enter a judgment consistent with the answers provided. This means that the judge cannot enter a judgment consistent with the jury's verdict and find in favor of the woman (Choices A, B & D). Educational objective:\n\nA general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and provide answers to questions on each factual issue. If the verdict and answers are inconsistent, the judge must (1) order a new trial, (2) direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict, or (3) enter a judgment consistent with the answers. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b) (general verdict with answers).","rule":"A general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and provide answers to questions on each factual issue. If the verdict and answers are inconsistent, the judge must (1) order a new trial, (2) direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict, or (3) enter a judgment consistent with the answers.","irac":{"issue":"A woman sued a landlord in an appropriate federal district court under a federal statute that prohibits landlords from discriminating against a potential tenant based on familial status. Her complaint included a request for $10,000. The woman claimed that the landlord denied her rental application because she had two young children. The woman also included a demand for a jury trial in her complaint. No law would have prohibited the woman from occupying the apartment. Under the federal statute, the plaintiff has the burden to establish a prima facie case that the defendant unlawfully discriminated against the plaintiff. If the plaintiff meets this burden, then the defendant must provide a nondiscriminatory reason why the plaintiff was not selected as a tenant. If the defendant does so, the burden shifts back to the plaintiff to show that the defendant's purported reason was a mere pretext for discrimination. At the end of the trial, the woman demanded that all factual issues be submitted to the jury. She also requested that the jury return its verdict with answers to specific questions relating to each of those issues. The landlord consented to this type of verdict. The judge gave the jury a proper verdict form that asked the jury to answer the specific questions that the woman had requested and to determine which party should prevail. The jury returned a verdict in favor of the woman and awarded her $10,000 in damages. In its answers to the specific questions, the jury indicated that the woman had presented sufficient evidence that the landlord discriminated against her. It also found that the landlord had a nondiscriminatory reason for denying the woman's application and that this reason was not a pretext for discrimination. Which action would be IMPROPER for the judge to take?","rule":"A general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and provide answers to questions on each factual issue. If the verdict and answers are inconsistent, the judge must (1) order a new trial, (2) direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict, or (3) enter a judgment consistent with the answers.","analysis":"General verdict with answers\n\n(FRCP 49(b))\n\nAnswers consistent with verdict Judge must enter judgment consistent with verdict & answers\n\nAnswers inconsistent with verdict Judge must:\n\n• order new trial\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict or\n• disregard verdict & enter judgment consistent with answers Answers inconsistent with each Judge must: other & verdict\n• order new trial or\n• direct jury to further consider its answers & verdict FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA verdict is the jury's determination of factual issues in a case. There are three types of verdicts—general verdicts, special verdicts, and general verdicts with answers—and the judge has the discretion to choose which verdict to use. Here, the judge asked the jury to return a general verdict with answers when he instructed them to (1) find in favor of, and award damages to, a particular party and (2) answer specific questions on each factual issue. If the jury's answers and verdict are consistent, the judge must enter a judgment consistent with those determinations. But when, as here, the jury's answers (no pretext for discrimination) and verdict (in favor of the woman) are inconsistent, the judge must:\n• order a new trial\n• direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict or\n• disregard the jury's verdict and enter a judgment consistent with the answers provided. This means that the judge cannot enter a judgment consistent with the jury's verdict and find in favor of the woman (Choices A, B & D). Educational objective:\n\nA general verdict with answers requires that the jury decide which party should prevail and provide answers to questions on each factual issue. If the verdict and answers are inconsistent, the judge must (1) order a new trial, (2) direct the jury to further consider its answers and verdict, or (3) enter a judgment consistent with the answers. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b) (general verdict with answers).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b) (general verdict with answers).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[619],"sourceAnswerPages":[620,621],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子依据禁止房东基于家庭状况歧视潜在租户的联邦制定法，在有适当管辖权的联邦地区法院起诉一名房东。起诉状请求 10,000 美元。女子主张，房东因她有两个年幼子女而拒绝其租房申请，没有任何法律禁止女子居住该公寓。根据联邦制定法，原告首先负有证明被告对其实施非法歧视的表面成立案件之责任。原告满足该责任后，被告必须说明未选原告为租户的非歧视性理由；如被告说明，责任再转回原告，要求证明被告所谓理由只是歧视的借口。审判结束时，女子要求将全部事实问题提交陪审团，并请求陪审团在裁决中回答与每项问题有关的具体问题。房东同意这种裁决。法官向陪审团提供适当裁决表，要求回答女子请求的具体问题，并决定哪一方应胜诉。陪审团作出有利于女子的裁决，判给 10,000 美元损害赔偿。针对具体问题，陪审团认定女子提出了足够证据证明房东歧视她；同时认定房东拒绝其申请有非歧视性理由，且该理由并非歧视借口。法官采取下列哪项措施属于不适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决。","B":"登记有利于房东的判决。","C":"登记有利于女子的判决。","D":"命令重新审判。"},"explanationZh":"附带问题答案的一般裁决（FRCP 49(b)）：\n\n答案与裁决一致｜法官必须依照裁决和答案登记判决\n答案与裁决不一致｜法官必须：\n• 命令重新审判；\n• 指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决；或\n• 不理会裁决，并依照答案登记判决\n各答案相互不一致且与裁决不一致｜法官必须：\n• 命令重新审判；或\n• 指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n裁决是陪审团对案件事实问题的决定。裁决有一般裁决、特别裁决和附带问题答案的一般裁决三种，法官有裁量权选择采用哪一种。本题法官要求陪审团：(1) 判特定一方胜诉并向其判给损害赔偿；且 (2) 回答每项事实问题的具体问题，因此要求作出附带问题答案的一般裁决。\n\n如果陪审团答案与裁决一致，法官必须依照二者登记判决。但是，本题陪审团的答案（不存在歧视借口）与裁决（女子胜诉）不一致，法官必须：\n• 命令重新审判；\n• 指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决；或\n• 不理会陪审团裁决，并依照答案登记判决。\n\n因此，法官不能依照陪审团裁决登记有利于女子的判决（选项 A、B、D）。\n\n学习目标：附带问题答案的一般裁决要求陪审团决定哪一方胜诉，并回答每项事实问题。如果裁决与答案不一致，法官必须：(1) 命令重新审判；(2) 指示陪审团进一步考虑其答案和裁决；或 (3) 依照答案登记判决。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b)（附带问题答案的一般裁决）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-206","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":206,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court. The defendant filed an answer denying the plaintiff's allegations and asserting several affirmative defenses. After reviewing the defendant's answer, the court concluded that the affirmative defenses were not warranted by existing law. The court issued an order to show cause on the defendant's attorney to explain why sanctions should not be imposed. At the show-cause hearing, the defendant's attorney failed to provide a nonfrivolous argument for modifying or reversing the existing law. The court ordered the defendant's attorney to pay a $3,000 fine to the court and struck all affirmative defenses from the defendant's answer. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff, and the court entered judgment accordingly. The defendant's attorney immediately appealed the order of sanctions. Will the appellate court likely overturn the sanctions?","choices":{"A":"No, because the district court did not abuse its discretion.","B":"No, because the district court did not commit clear error.","C":"Yes, because the district court abused its discretion.","D":"Yes, because the district court committed clear error."},"answer":"A","explanation":"FRCP 11(b) requires that all claims, defenses, and legal contentions be warranted by existing law or supported by a nonfrivolous argument for modifying or reversing that law. A court has the discretion to impose sanctions on a law firm, attorney, or party if they violate this rule. Since the imposition of sanctions is a discretionary ruling, it is reviewed on appeal under the highly deferential abuse-of-discretion standard. This means that the appellate court will only reverse the ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. Here, the district court believed that none of the affirmative defenses in the defendant's answer were warranted by existing law. At the show-cause hearing, the defendant's attorney failed to provide a nonfrivolous argument for modifying or reversing that law. As a result, the district court did not abuse its discretion by fining the attorney $3,000 and striking the affirmative defenses from the defendant's answer (Choice C). (Choices B & D) The clearly-erroneous standard is used to review factual findings in a bench trial—not a district court's discretionary imposition of sanctions (as seen here). Under that standard, the district court's factual findings will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. Educational objective: An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings (eg, the imposition of sanctions). Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (representations to the court & sanctions for violating this rule).\n\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmax Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990) (holding that the abuse-of-discretion standard applies to an appellate review of a district court's imposition of sanctions).","rule":"An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings (eg, the imposition of sanctions). Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court. The defendant filed an answer denying the plaintiff's allegations and asserting several affirmative defenses. After reviewing the defendant's answer, the court concluded that the affirmative defenses were not warranted by existing law. The court issued an order to show cause on the defendant's attorney to explain why sanctions should not be imposed. At the show-cause hearing, the defendant's attorney failed to provide a nonfrivolous argument for modifying or reversing the existing law. The court ordered the defendant's attorney to pay a $3,000 fine to the court and struck all affirmative defenses from the defendant's answer. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the plaintiff, and the court entered judgment accordingly. The defendant's attorney immediately appealed the order of sanctions. Will the appellate court likely overturn the sanctions?","rule":"An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings (eg, the imposition of sanctions). Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure","analysis":"FRCP 11(b) requires that all claims, defenses, and legal contentions be warranted by existing law or supported by a nonfrivolous argument for modifying or reversing that law. A court has the discretion to impose sanctions on a law firm, attorney, or party if they violate this rule. Since the imposition of sanctions is a discretionary ruling, it is reviewed on appeal under the highly deferential abuse-of-discretion standard. This means that the appellate court will only reverse the ruling if it was clearly arbitrary or unreasonable. Here, the district court believed that none of the affirmative defenses in the defendant's answer were warranted by existing law. At the show-cause hearing, the defendant's attorney failed to provide a nonfrivolous argument for modifying or reversing that law. As a result, the district court did not abuse its discretion by fining the attorney $3,000 and striking the affirmative defenses from the defendant's answer (Choice C). (Choices B & D) The clearly-erroneous standard is used to review factual findings in a bench trial—not a district court's discretionary imposition of sanctions (as seen here). Under that standard, the district court's factual findings will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. Educational objective: An appellate court uses the abuse-of-discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary rulings (eg, the imposition of sanctions). Under this highly deferential standard, the appellate court will only reverse a clearly arbitrary or unreasonable ruling. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (representations to the court & sanctions for violating this rule).\n\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmax Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990) (holding that the abuse-of-discretion standard applies to an appellate review of a district court's imposition of sanctions).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (representations to the court & sanctions for violating this rule).\n\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmax Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990) (holding that the abuse-of-discretion standard applies to an appellate review of a district court's imposition of sanctions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[622],"sourceAnswerPages":[623],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院起诉一名被告。被告提交答辩状，否认原告指控并提出数项积极抗辩。法院审查答辩状后，认定这些积极抗辩没有现行法律依据。法院向被告律师发布说明理由令，要求解释为何不应给予制裁。说明理由听证中，被告律师未能提出修改或推翻现行法律的非轻率论证。法院命令被告律师向法院缴纳 3,000 美元罚款，并从答辩状中删除全部积极抗辩。审判结束时，陪审团判原告胜诉，法院据此登记判决。被告律师立即对制裁命令提起上诉。上诉法院可能推翻制裁吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为地区法院没有滥用裁量权。","B":"不会，因为地区法院没有犯明显错误。","C":"会，因为地区法院滥用了裁量权。","D":"会，因为地区法院犯有明显错误。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 11(b) 要求所有请求、抗辩和法律主张均有现行法律依据，或得到修改、推翻现行法律的非轻率论证支持。律师事务所、律师或当事人违反该规则时，法院有裁量权给予制裁。给予制裁属于裁量性裁定，因此在上诉中适用高度尊重的滥用裁量权标准审查。这意味着，只有裁定明显武断或不合理时，上诉法院才会推翻。\n\n本题地区法院认为，被告答辩状中的积极抗辩均无现行法律依据。在说明理由听证中，被告律师未能提出修改或推翻现行法律的非轻率论证。因此，地区法院对律师罚款 3,000 美元并从答辩状中删除积极抗辩，没有滥用裁量权（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 B、D）明显错误标准用于审查法官审判中的事实认定，而不是地区法院裁量性给予制裁（本题即如此）。根据该标准，只有任何合理法官都不会作出该事实认定时，才会推翻。\n\n学习目标：上诉法院审查地区法院的裁量性裁定，例如给予制裁时，适用滥用裁量权标准。根据这一高度尊重的标准，上诉法院只会推翻明显武断或不合理的裁定。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11（向法院作出的陈述及违反该规则的制裁）。\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmax Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990)（判定上诉审查地区法院给予制裁时适用滥用裁量权标准）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-054"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-207","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":207,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A manufacturer sued a buyer in federal court for failing to make timely payments under the parties' sales contract. The case was tried to the court solely on documentary evidence. Immediately after the close of the evidence, the judge announced from the bench, \"Judgment shall be entered for the manufacturer,\" and judgment was so entered. The buyer has appealed the judgment. What is the buyer's best argument for persuading the appellate court to reverse the judgment?","choices":{"A":"The judgment is clearly erroneous because it was based solely on documentary evidence.","B":"The manufacturer was required to file proposed findings and conclusions before the trial court ruled.","C":"The trial court erred because it announced the judgment without giving the parties an opportunity to submit proposed findings and conclusions.","D":"The trial court erred by not providing findings and conclusions."},"answer":"D","explanation":"In a case tried to the court (ie, a bench trial), the judge serves as both the finder of fact and interpreter of the law. As a result, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 52(a) requires that the judge provide factual findings and legal conclusions (orally or in writing) on the record after the close of evidence. This allows the parties and the appellate court to have a clear understanding of the judge's reasoning. If a judge fails to provide these required findings and conclusions, the appellate court should reverse the trial court's judgment. In this bench trial, the judge announced, \"Judgment shall be entered for the manufacturer,\" immediately after the close of evidence. However, the judge failed to provide the required factual findings and legal conclusions. As a result, that is the buyer's best argument to persuade the appellate court to reverse the judgment. (Choice A) Factual findings in a bench trial will only be reversed on appeal if they were clearly erroneous because no reasonable judge would have made them. However, a judgment based solely on documentary evidence is not clearly erroneous. That is because such evidence can provide strong support for the judge's findings—especially in a breach-of-contract case (as seen here). (Choices B & C) The parties are not required to file proposed findings and conclusions but may do so if the trial court allows. Additionally, a trial court does not need to give the parties an opportunity to submit proposed findings and conclusions. Educational objective: In a bench trial, the judge must provide factual findings and legal conclusions on the record (orally or in writing) after the close of evidence. Failure to do so will result in a reversal of the judgment on appeal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).","rule":"In a bench trial, the judge must provide factual findings and legal conclusions on the record (orally or in writing) after the close of evidence. Failure to do so will result in a reversal of the judgment on appeal.","irac":{"issue":"A manufacturer sued a buyer in federal court for failing to make timely payments under the parties' sales contract. The case was tried to the court solely on documentary evidence. Immediately after the close of the evidence, the judge announced from the bench, \"Judgment shall be entered for the manufacturer,\" and judgment was so entered. The buyer has appealed the judgment. What is the buyer's best argument for persuading the appellate court to reverse the judgment?","rule":"In a bench trial, the judge must provide factual findings and legal conclusions on the record (orally or in writing) after the close of evidence. Failure to do so will result in a reversal of the judgment on appeal.","analysis":"In a case tried to the court (ie, a bench trial), the judge serves as both the finder of fact and interpreter of the law. As a result, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 52(a) requires that the judge provide factual findings and legal conclusions (orally or in writing) on the record after the close of evidence. This allows the parties and the appellate court to have a clear understanding of the judge's reasoning. If a judge fails to provide these required findings and conclusions, the appellate court should reverse the trial court's judgment. In this bench trial, the judge announced, \"Judgment shall be entered for the manufacturer,\" immediately after the close of evidence. However, the judge failed to provide the required factual findings and legal conclusions. As a result, that is the buyer's best argument to persuade the appellate court to reverse the judgment. (Choice A) Factual findings in a bench trial will only be reversed on appeal if they were clearly erroneous because no reasonable judge would have made them. However, a judgment based solely on documentary evidence is not clearly erroneous. That is because such evidence can provide strong support for the judge's findings—especially in a breach-of-contract case (as seen here). (Choices B & C) The parties are not required to file proposed findings and conclusions but may do so if the trial court allows. Additionally, a trial court does not need to give the parties an opportunity to submit proposed findings and conclusions. Educational objective: In a bench trial, the judge must provide factual findings and legal conclusions on the record (orally or in writing) after the close of evidence. Failure to do so will result in a reversal of the judgment on appeal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a) (findings of fact and conclusions of law).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[625],"sourceAnswerPages":[626],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家制造商在联邦法院起诉一名买方未按照双方销售合同及时付款。案件完全依据书面证据由法官审判。举证结束后，法官立即当庭宣布：“应为制造商登记判决”，法院据此登记判决。买方对判决提起上诉。买方说服上诉法院推翻判决的最佳论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"该判决明显错误，因为完全以书面证据为依据。","B":"审判法院裁判前，制造商必须提交拟议事实认定和法律结论。","C":"审判法院未给予双方提交拟议事实认定和法律结论的机会就宣布判决，构成错误。","D":"审判法院没有提供事实认定和法律结论，构成错误。"},"explanationZh":"案件由法院审判，即法官审判时，法官同时担任事实裁判者和法律解释者。因此，《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）52(a) 要求法官在举证结束后，以口头或书面形式在案卷记录中提供事实认定和法律结论，使双方和上诉法院能够清楚了解法官的理由。如果法官没有提供这些必要认定和结论，上诉法院应推翻审判法院判决。\n\n本题法官在举证结束后立即宣布“应为制造商登记判决”，但没有提供必要的事实认定和法律结论。因此，这是买方说服上诉法院推翻判决的最佳论点。\n\n（选项 A）法官审判中的事实认定，只有明显错误到任何合理法官都不会作出时，才会在上诉中被推翻。但是，完全以书面证据为依据的判决并非因此就明显错误，因为这类证据可以有力支持法官认定，尤其是在违约案件中（本题即如此）。\n\n（选项 B、C）双方没有义务提交拟议事实认定和法律结论，但经审判法院允许时可以提交。此外，审判法院无需给予双方提交拟议认定和结论的机会。\n\n学习目标：法官审判中，法官必须在举证结束后，以口头或书面形式在案卷记录中提供事实认定和法律结论。未提供将导致判决在上诉中被推翻。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 52(a)（事实认定和法律结论）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-064"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-208","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":208,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"An executive signed an employment contract with a steel plant. The contract contained a noncompete clause that prohibited the executive from working for any of the steel plant's competitors for one year after leaving the plant. The contract also contained a nondisclosure clause that prohibited the executive from disclosing the steel plant's trade secrets and other confidential information. On September 1, 2015, the executive left the steel plant. On August 1, 2016, she began working for one of the steel plant's competitors. On April 1, 2018, the steel plant filed a complaint against the executive in a federal court for breaching the noncompete clause. On November 1, 2018, the steel plant discovered that the executive divulged its trade secrets to its competitor when she began working there. The steel plant immediately filed a motion with the court to amend its complaint to add a claim for infringement of trade secrets against the executive. The applicable statute of limitations for each claim is two years from the date the violation allegedly occurred. The laws that establish these statutes of limitations do not mention whether relation back is allowed for these claims. Should the federal court allow the steel plant to amend its complaint to include the trade secrets claim?","choices":{"A":"No, because the executive did not consent to the amendment.","B":"No, because the statute of limitations for the claim has expired.","C":"Yes, because a federal court should freely allow amendments to pleadings when justice so requires.","D":"Yes, because the amended claim arises from the transaction and occurrence set out in the original pleading."},"answer":"D","explanation":"An amended pleading filed after the statute of limitations has expired is permitted only when the relation-back doctrine applies. This doctrine treats the amendment as if it were filed on the same date as the original pleading—ie, the amendment \"relates back\" to the date of the original pleading. An amendment that asserts a new claim relates back when:\n• the statute of limitations for the new claim allows relation back (not seen here) or\n• the claim arises out of the conduct or occurrence set forth in the original pleading. Here, the steel plant filed a motion to amend its complaint to add a trade secrets claim after the statute of limitations had expired on August 1, 2018. But this claim relates back to the date the steel plant filed its claim for breach of the noncompete clause—before the statute of limitations expired. That is because both claims arise from the executive's contract with the steel plant and her employment with the competitor. Therefore, the federal court should allow the steel plant to amend its complaint to include the trade secrets claim (Choice B). (Choices A & C) The executive's (ie, opposing party's) consent is unnecessary because a federal court can permit amendments to pleadings without the defendant's consent and should do so when justice requires. But if consent is not given, any amendment must be filed before the statute of limitations expires or relate back to the date of the original pleading (as seen here). Educational objective: The relation-back doctrine allows an amended pleading to assert a new claim after the statute of limitations has expired when (1) the applicable statute of limitations allows relation back or (2) the claim arises out of the same conduct or occurrence as the original pleading. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).","rule":"The relation-back doctrine allows an amended pleading to assert a new claim after the statute of limitations has expired when (1) the applicable statute of limitations allows relation back or (2) the claim arises out of the same conduct or occurrence as the original pleading.","irac":{"issue":"An executive signed an employment contract with a steel plant. The contract contained a noncompete clause that prohibited the executive from working for any of the steel plant's competitors for one year after leaving the plant. The contract also contained a nondisclosure clause that prohibited the executive from disclosing the steel plant's trade secrets and other confidential information. On September 1, 2015, the executive left the steel plant. On August 1, 2016, she began working for one of the steel plant's competitors. On April 1, 2018, the steel plant filed a complaint against the executive in a federal court for breaching the noncompete clause. On November 1, 2018, the steel plant discovered that the executive divulged its trade secrets to its competitor when she began working there. The steel plant immediately filed a motion with the court to amend its complaint to add a claim for infringement of trade secrets against the executive. The applicable statute of limitations for each claim is two years from the date the violation allegedly occurred. The laws that establish these statutes of limitations do not mention whether relation back is allowed for these claims. Should the federal court allow the steel plant to amend its complaint to include the trade secrets claim?","rule":"The relation-back doctrine allows an amended pleading to assert a new claim after the statute of limitations has expired when (1) the applicable statute of limitations allows relation back or (2) the claim arises out of the same conduct or occurrence as the original pleading.","analysis":"An amended pleading filed after the statute of limitations has expired is permitted only when the relation-back doctrine applies. This doctrine treats the amendment as if it were filed on the same date as the original pleading—ie, the amendment \"relates back\" to the date of the original pleading. An amendment that asserts a new claim relates back when:\n• the statute of limitations for the new claim allows relation back (not seen here) or\n• the claim arises out of the conduct or occurrence set forth in the original pleading. Here, the steel plant filed a motion to amend its complaint to add a trade secrets claim after the statute of limitations had expired on August 1, 2018. But this claim relates back to the date the steel plant filed its claim for breach of the noncompete clause—before the statute of limitations expired. That is because both claims arise from the executive's contract with the steel plant and her employment with the competitor. Therefore, the federal court should allow the steel plant to amend its complaint to include the trade secrets claim (Choice B). (Choices A & C) The executive's (ie, opposing party's) consent is unnecessary because a federal court can permit amendments to pleadings without the defendant's consent and should do so when justice requires. But if consent is not given, any amendment must be filed before the statute of limitations expires or relate back to the date of the original pleading (as seen here). Educational objective: The relation-back doctrine allows an amended pleading to assert a new claim after the statute of limitations has expired when (1) the applicable statute of limitations allows relation back or (2) the claim arises out of the same conduct or occurrence as the original pleading. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[628],"sourceAnswerPages":[629],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名高管与一家钢铁厂签订雇佣合同。合同包含竞业禁止条款，禁止高管离职后一年内为钢铁厂任何竞争对手工作；还包含保密条款，禁止她披露钢铁厂的商业秘密及其他机密信息。2015 年 9 月 1 日，高管离开钢铁厂。2016 年 8 月 1 日，她开始为钢铁厂的一家竞争对手工作。2018 年 4 月 1 日，钢铁厂在联邦法院起诉高管违反竞业禁止条款。2018 年 11 月 1 日，钢铁厂发现，高管开始为竞争对手工作时曾向其泄露钢铁厂的商业秘密。钢铁厂立即申请修改起诉状，增加针对高管的侵犯商业秘密请求。每项请求适用的诉讼时效，均为据称违法行为发生之日起两年。规定这些诉讼时效的法律没有说明是否允许溯及既往。联邦法院是否应允许钢铁厂修改起诉状，加入商业秘密请求？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为高管没有同意修正。","B":"不应，因为该请求的诉讼时效已经届满。","C":"应当，因为正义需要时，联邦法院应自由准许修改诉状。","D":"应当，因为修正请求源于原诉状所述交易和事件。"},"explanationZh":"诉讼时效届满后提交的修正诉状，只有溯及既往原则适用时才允许。该原则将修正视为与原诉状在同一天提交，即修正“溯及”原诉状提交之日。提出新请求的修正在下列情形下溯及既往：\n• 新请求适用的诉讼时效法允许溯及既往（本题并非如此）；或\n• 请求源于原诉状所述行为或事件。\n\n本题钢铁厂在商业秘密请求诉讼时效于 2018 年 8 月 1 日届满后，申请修改起诉状增加该请求。但是，该请求溯及钢铁厂提出违反竞业禁止条款请求的日期，而该日期在诉讼时效届满前。原因是，两项请求均源于高管与钢铁厂的合同以及她受雇于竞争对手。因此，联邦法院应允许钢铁厂修改起诉状，加入商业秘密请求（选项 B）。\n\n（选项 A、C）无需高管即相对方同意，因为联邦法院可以在被告不同意时准许修改诉状，并应在正义需要时准许。但是，如果未取得同意，任何修正必须在诉讼时效届满前提交，或溯及原诉状提交之日（本题即如此）。\n\n学习目标：诉讼时效届满后，如果 (1) 适用诉讼时效法允许溯及既往；或 (2) 请求源于原诉状所述相同行为或事件，溯及既往原则允许修正诉状提出新请求。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c)（修正诉状的溯及既往）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-065"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-209","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":209,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An employee brought a federal diversity action against his former employer, a chemical manufacturing company, seeking damages for injuries sustained on the job. During discovery, the employee's attorney served the company with notice of a written deposition she intended to conduct of one of the company's former managers. The employee's attorney attached the questions she intended to have the court officer ask the manager at the deposition. Eight days later, the company sent the manager and the employee the questions it wanted the officer to ask the manager on cross-examination. The employee's attorney served written objections to the form of the cross-examination questions four days after they were served. Twelve days later, the employee's attorney served the questions she wanted the officer to ask on redirect. Six days after service of the redirect questions, the company served its objections to the redirect questions and the questions it wanted the officer to ask on recross-examination. Which of the following events was untimely?","choices":{"A":"Service of the cross-examination questions.","B":"Service of the objections to the form of the cross-examination questions.","C":"Service of the redirect-examination questions.","D":"Service of the recross-examination questions."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A written deposition is a method of discovery in which an officer asks a deponent questions and records the responses under oath and outside of court. The party requesting the deposition must serve the deponent and all other parties with written notice and a copy of the questions to be asked on direct examination (direct questions). The other parties may then serve the deponent and all other parties with responsive questions as follows:\n• Cross-questions – questions to be asked during cross-examination must be served within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions (here, they were timely served 8 days later) (Choice A).\n\n• Redirect questions – questions to be asked during redirect must be served within 7 days after service of the cross-questions (here, they were untimely served 12 days later).\n\n• Recross-questions – questions to be asked during recross must be served within 7 days after service of the redirect questions (here, they were timely served 6 days later) (Choice D).\n\nTherefore, service of the redirect-examination questions in this case was untimely. (Choice B) A party must serve any written objections to the form of a question within the time required for serving a responsive question. This means that objections to the company's cross-questions were due at the same time as the redirect questions—7 days after service of the cross-questions. Therefore, the employee's attorney timely served written objections to the form of the cross-questions 4 days after they were served. Educational objective: For a written deposition, (1) cross-questions must be served within 14 days after service of direct questions, (2) redirect questions must be served within 7 days after service of cross-questions, and (3) recross-questions must be served within 7 days after service of redirect questions. Objections are due at the same times. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31 (depositions by written questions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 32 (objections to form of questions in written depositions).","rule":"For a written deposition, (1) cross-questions must be served within 14 days after service of direct questions, (2) redirect questions must be served within 7 days after service of cross-questions, and (3) recross-questions must be served within 7 days after service of redirect questions. Objections are due at the same times.","irac":{"issue":"An employee brought a federal diversity action against his former employer, a chemical manufacturing company, seeking damages for injuries sustained on the job. During discovery, the employee's attorney served the company with notice of a written deposition she intended to conduct of one of the company's former managers. The employee's attorney attached the questions she intended to have the court officer ask the manager at the deposition. Eight days later, the company sent the manager and the employee the questions it wanted the officer to ask the manager on cross-examination. The employee's attorney served written objections to the form of the cross-examination questions four days after they were served. Twelve days later, the employee's attorney served the questions she wanted the officer to ask on redirect. Six days after service of the redirect questions, the company served its objections to the redirect questions and the questions it wanted the officer to ask on recross-examination. Which of the following events was untimely?","rule":"For a written deposition, (1) cross-questions must be served within 14 days after service of direct questions, (2) redirect questions must be served within 7 days after service of cross-questions, and (3) recross-questions must be served within 7 days after service of redirect questions. Objections are due at the same times.","analysis":"A written deposition is a method of discovery in which an officer asks a deponent questions and records the responses under oath and outside of court. The party requesting the deposition must serve the deponent and all other parties with written notice and a copy of the questions to be asked on direct examination (direct questions). The other parties may then serve the deponent and all other parties with responsive questions as follows:\n• Cross-questions – questions to be asked during cross-examination must be served within 14 days after service of the notice and direct questions (here, they were timely served 8 days later) (Choice A).\n\n• Redirect questions – questions to be asked during redirect must be served within 7 days after service of the cross-questions (here, they were untimely served 12 days later).\n\n• Recross-questions – questions to be asked during recross must be served within 7 days after service of the redirect questions (here, they were timely served 6 days later) (Choice D).\n\nTherefore, service of the redirect-examination questions in this case was untimely. (Choice B) A party must serve any written objections to the form of a question within the time required for serving a responsive question. This means that objections to the company's cross-questions were due at the same time as the redirect questions—7 days after service of the cross-questions. Therefore, the employee's attorney timely served written objections to the form of the cross-questions 4 days after they were served. Educational objective: For a written deposition, (1) cross-questions must be served within 14 days after service of direct questions, (2) redirect questions must be served within 7 days after service of cross-questions, and (3) recross-questions must be served within 7 days after service of redirect questions. Objections are due at the same times. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31 (depositions by written questions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 32 (objections to form of questions in written depositions).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31 (depositions by written questions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 32 (objections to form of questions in written depositions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[631],"sourceAnswerPages":[632],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名雇员对前雇主——一家化学品制造公司——提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，请求赔偿工作中所受伤害。证据开示期间，雇员律师向公司送达通知，称她拟对公司一名前经理进行书面问题证言录取，并附上拟由法院工作人员在证言录取中向经理提出的问题。八天后，公司向经理和雇员送达其希望工作人员在交叉询问中提出的问题。交叉询问问题送达四天后，雇员律师送达了针对问题形式的书面异议。十二天后，雇员律师送达其希望工作人员在再次直接询问中提出的问题。再次直接询问问题送达六天后，公司送达对这些问题的异议，以及希望工作人员在再次交叉询问中提出的问题。下列哪一事项逾期？","choicesZh":{"A":"送达交叉询问问题。","B":"送达针对交叉询问问题形式的异议。","C":"送达再次直接询问问题。","D":"送达再次交叉询问问题。"},"explanationZh":"书面问题证言录取是一种证据开示方法，由工作人员在法庭外向受询问人提问，并以宣誓形式记录回答。请求证言录取的一方必须向受询问人及所有其他各方送达书面通知，以及直接询问时拟提出的问题副本。随后，其他各方可向受询问人及所有其他各方送达回应问题：\n• 交叉询问问题——须在通知和直接询问问题送达后 14 天内送达；本题在 8 天后送达，及时（选项 A）。\n• 再次直接询问问题——须在交叉询问问题送达后 7 天内送达；本题在 12 天后送达，逾期。\n• 再次交叉询问问题——须在再次直接询问问题送达后 7 天内送达；本题在 6 天后送达，及时（选项 D）。\n\n因此，本题再次直接询问问题的送达逾期。\n\n（选项 B）一方必须在回应问题的送达期限内，对问题形式提出书面异议。这意味着，对公司交叉询问问题的异议与再次直接询问问题同样应在交叉询问问题送达后 7 天内提出。因此，雇员律师在问题送达 4 天后提出书面形式异议，属于及时。\n\n学习目标：书面问题证言录取中：(1) 交叉询问问题须在直接询问问题送达后 14 天内送达；(2) 再次直接询问问题须在交叉询问问题送达后 7 天内送达；(3) 再次交叉询问问题须在再次直接询问问题送达后 7 天内送达。异议期限相同。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31（通过书面问题录取证言）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 32（对书面问题证言录取中问题形式的异议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-066"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-210","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":210,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A pharmaceutical retailer sued a drug manufacturer in federal court for antitrust and unfair-competition violations under federal and state law. After the parties completed discovery, the retailer submitted a pretrial narrative statement designating a broad set of facts and issues to be tried. The manufacturer disputed the statement and submitted a much narrower one. At the final pretrial conference, the court entered its final order, ruling in favor of the retailer's broader statement as the one the court would read to the jury during voir dire and would use to define the facts and issues to be tried. The manufacturer's attorney is concerned that trying many of the facts and issues listed in the pretrial order would reveal litigation strategies important in other actions pending against the manufacturer. What is the best way for the manufacturer's attorney to seek relief from the court's ruling on the pretrial statement?","choices":{"A":"Appeal from the final pretrial order, arguing that it is overbroad on its face.","B":"Object in the trial court and appeal any adverse ruling on the objection.","C":"Object in the trial court and file a motion to delay the trial.","D":"Object in the trial court and move to modify the order to prevent manifest injustice."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A federal district court judge may hold one or more pretrial conferences with the parties' attorneys or unrepresented parties. At these conferences, the judge has broad discretion to address and act on a wide range of issues to help expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. At the final pretrial conference, the judge will enter a final pretrial order that formulates a plan for trial.* The court may modify this order only to prevent manifest injustice. Here, the federal district court entered its final order at the final pretrial conference. In that order, the court adopted the retailer's broad narrative statement to use during voir dire and to define the facts and issues to be tried. Since the manufacturer's attorney wants to narrow this narrative statement, the best way to seek relief is to object in the trial court and move to modify the order to prevent manifest injustice. *A final pretrial conference must be held as close to the start of trial as is reasonable and must be attended by at least one attorney who will conduct the trial for each party or by any unrepresented party. (Choice A) Under the final-judgment rule, an appeal cannot be taken before a final judgment has been entered unless limited exceptions apply. A final pretrial order is not a final judgment and none of the exceptions apply, so an immediate appeal is unjustified. (Choice B) The manufacturer's attorney must move to modify the order at the trial court level to preserve the issue for appeal, where the order will be reviewed for abuse of discretion. A mere objection will not suffice. (Choice C) A motion to delay the trial will not help the manufacturer's attorney seek relief from the final pretrial order. Instead, the attorney should move to modify the order to prevent manifest injustice. Educational objective: At the final pretrial conference, a federal district court judge will issue a final pretrial order that formulates a plan for trial. The court may modify this order only to prevent manifest injustice. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(e) (final pretrial conference and orders).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1527.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a party can modify or seek relief from a pretrial order).","rule":"At the final pretrial conference, a federal district court judge will issue a final pretrial order that formulates a plan for trial. The court may modify this order only to prevent manifest injustice.","irac":{"issue":"A pharmaceutical retailer sued a drug manufacturer in federal court for antitrust and unfair-competition violations under federal and state law. After the parties completed discovery, the retailer submitted a pretrial narrative statement designating a broad set of facts and issues to be tried. The manufacturer disputed the statement and submitted a much narrower one. At the final pretrial conference, the court entered its final order, ruling in favor of the retailer's broader statement as the one the court would read to the jury during voir dire and would use to define the facts and issues to be tried. The manufacturer's attorney is concerned that trying many of the facts and issues listed in the pretrial order would reveal litigation strategies important in other actions pending against the manufacturer. What is the best way for the manufacturer's attorney to seek relief from the court's ruling on the pretrial statement?","rule":"At the final pretrial conference, a federal district court judge will issue a final pretrial order that formulates a plan for trial. The court may modify this order only to prevent manifest injustice.","analysis":"A federal district court judge may hold one or more pretrial conferences with the parties' attorneys or unrepresented parties. At these conferences, the judge has broad discretion to address and act on a wide range of issues to help expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. At the final pretrial conference, the judge will enter a final pretrial order that formulates a plan for trial.* The court may modify this order only to prevent manifest injustice. Here, the federal district court entered its final order at the final pretrial conference. In that order, the court adopted the retailer's broad narrative statement to use during voir dire and to define the facts and issues to be tried. Since the manufacturer's attorney wants to narrow this narrative statement, the best way to seek relief is to object in the trial court and move to modify the order to prevent manifest injustice. *A final pretrial conference must be held as close to the start of trial as is reasonable and must be attended by at least one attorney who will conduct the trial for each party or by any unrepresented party. (Choice A) Under the final-judgment rule, an appeal cannot be taken before a final judgment has been entered unless limited exceptions apply. A final pretrial order is not a final judgment and none of the exceptions apply, so an immediate appeal is unjustified. (Choice B) The manufacturer's attorney must move to modify the order at the trial court level to preserve the issue for appeal, where the order will be reviewed for abuse of discretion. A mere objection will not suffice. (Choice C) A motion to delay the trial will not help the manufacturer's attorney seek relief from the final pretrial order. Instead, the attorney should move to modify the order to prevent manifest injustice. Educational objective: At the final pretrial conference, a federal district court judge will issue a final pretrial order that formulates a plan for trial. The court may modify this order only to prevent manifest injustice. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(e) (final pretrial conference and orders).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1527.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a party can modify or seek relief from a pretrial order).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(e) (final pretrial conference and orders).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1527.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a party can modify or seek relief from a pretrial order).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[634],"sourceAnswerPages":[635],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家药品零售商在联邦法院起诉一家药品制造商违反联邦法和州法中的反垄断及不正当竞争规定。双方完成证据开示后，零售商提交审前叙述性陈述，指定了广泛的待审事实和问题。制造商对此提出争议，并提交一份范围窄得多的陈述。最终审前会议上，法院发布最终命令，采纳零售商较宽泛的陈述，决定在陪审员资格询问时向陪审团宣读，并以其界定待审事实和问题。制造商律师担心，审理审前命令中列出的许多事实和问题，会暴露在其他针对制造商的待决诉讼中很重要的诉讼策略。制造商律师请求法院就审前陈述裁定给予救济的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"对最终审前命令提起上诉，主张其表面上范围过宽。","B":"在审判法院提出异议，并对任何不利裁定提起上诉。","C":"在审判法院提出异议，并申请推迟审判。","D":"在审判法院提出异议，并申请修改命令以防止明显不公。"},"explanationZh":"联邦地区法院法官可以与双方律师或无律师代理的当事人举行一次或多次审前会议。法官在会议中享有广泛裁量权，可以处理各类问题，以加快诉讼、提高审判质量并促进和解。最终审前会议上，法官将发布制定审判计划的最终审前命令。* 法院只能为防止明显不公而修改该命令。\n\n本题联邦地区法院在最终审前会议上发布最终命令，采纳零售商较宽泛的叙述性陈述，用于陪审员资格询问并界定待审事实和问题。制造商律师希望缩小该陈述范围，所以最佳救济方式是在审判法院提出异议，并申请修改命令以防止明显不公。\n\n*最终审前会议必须在合理情况下尽量临近审判开始时举行；每一方至少须有一名将主持审判的律师出席，无律师代理的一方本人须出席。\n\n（选项 A）根据终局判决规则，除非适用有限例外，终局判决登记前不得上诉。最终审前命令并非终局判决，且不适用任何例外，所以不能立即上诉。\n\n（选项 B）制造商律师必须在审判法院申请修改命令，才能保留该问题供上诉审查；上诉时适用滥用裁量权标准。仅提出异议不够。\n\n（选项 C）申请推迟审判无助于制造商律师取得针对最终审前命令的救济。律师应申请修改命令，以防止明显不公。\n\n学习目标：最终审前会议上，联邦地区法院法官将发布制定审判计划的最终审前命令。法院只能为防止明显不公而修改该命令。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(e)（最终审前会议和命令）。\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1527.1 (3d ed. 2020)（说明一方如何修改审前命令或取得相关救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-211","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":211,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A patient suffered severe complications from a surgery on his leg. The patient hired an attorney to file a negligence lawsuit in federal court against the surgeon who performed the operation. Which of the following does the patient's attorney NOT need to include in the complaint?","choices":{"A":"A demand for the relief the patient seeks.","B":"A detailed statement of the claim showing that the patient is entitled to relief.","C":"A short and plain statement showing the grounds for the court's subject-matter jurisdiction.","D":"The attorney's address, phone number, and email address."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A complaint is the initial pleading that starts a civil action and states the basis for the plaintiff's claim. A complaint filed in federal court must contain all of the following:\n• Caption with the court's name, a title, the parties, a file number, and a designation as a \"complaint\"\n\n• Short and plain statement showing the grounds of the court's subject-matter jurisdiction (Choice C)\n\n• Short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief\n\n• Demand for the relief (ie, remedy) the plaintiff seeks (Choice A)\n\n• Signature, address, phone number, and email address of the plaintiff's attorney (or unrepresented plaintiff) (Choice D)\n\nThe statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists. Detailed, specific facts are unnecessary unless the claim alleges fraud or mistake (neither of which applies here). Therefore, the patient's attorney need not include in the complaint a detailed statement of the claim showing that the patient is entitled to relief. Educational objective: A complaint filed in federal court must contain (1) a caption with the court, title, and parties, (2) a short and plain statement of the court's jurisdiction, (3) a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief, (4) a demand for relief, and (5) the attorney's or unrepresented plaintiff's signature and contact information. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8–11 (pleading requirements).","rule":"A complaint filed in federal court must contain (1) a caption with the court, title, and parties, (2) a short and plain statement of the court's jurisdiction, (3) a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief, (4) a demand for relief, and (5) the attorney's or unrepresented plaintiff's signature and contact information.","irac":{"issue":"A patient suffered severe complications from a surgery on his leg. The patient hired an attorney to file a negligence lawsuit in federal court against the surgeon who performed the operation. Which of the following does the patient's attorney NOT need to include in the complaint?","rule":"A complaint filed in federal court must contain (1) a caption with the court, title, and parties, (2) a short and plain statement of the court's jurisdiction, (3) a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief, (4) a demand for relief, and (5) the attorney's or unrepresented plaintiff's signature and contact information.","analysis":"A complaint is the initial pleading that starts a civil action and states the basis for the plaintiff's claim. A complaint filed in federal court must contain all of the following:\n• Caption with the court's name, a title, the parties, a file number, and a designation as a \"complaint\"\n\n• Short and plain statement showing the grounds of the court's subject-matter jurisdiction (Choice C)\n\n• Short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief\n\n• Demand for the relief (ie, remedy) the plaintiff seeks (Choice A)\n\n• Signature, address, phone number, and email address of the plaintiff's attorney (or unrepresented plaintiff) (Choice D)\n\nThe statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists. Detailed, specific facts are unnecessary unless the claim alleges fraud or mistake (neither of which applies here). Therefore, the patient's attorney need not include in the complaint a detailed statement of the claim showing that the patient is entitled to relief. Educational objective: A complaint filed in federal court must contain (1) a caption with the court, title, and parties, (2) a short and plain statement of the court's jurisdiction, (3) a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief, (4) a demand for relief, and (5) the attorney's or unrepresented plaintiff's signature and contact information. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8–11 (pleading requirements).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8–11 (pleading requirements).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[637],"sourceAnswerPages":[638],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名患者因腿部手术发生严重并发症。患者聘请律师，在联邦法院对实施手术的外科医生提起过失诉讼。患者律师无需在起诉状中包含下列哪一项？","choicesZh":{"A":"患者请求救济的要求。","B":"表明患者有权获得救济的详细请求陈述。","C":"简短、明确说明法院事项管辖权依据的陈述。","D":"律师的地址、电话号码和电子邮件地址。"},"explanationZh":"起诉状是启动民事诉讼并陈述原告请求依据的最初诉状。向联邦法院提交的起诉状必须包含：\n• 标题栏，包括法院名称、文书标题、各方、案号以及“起诉状”标识；\n• 简短、明确说明法院事项管辖权依据的陈述（选项 C）；\n• 简短、明确陈述请求，表明原告有权获得救济；\n• 原告请求救济，即补救措施的要求（选项 A）；\n• 原告律师（或无律师代理的原告）的签名、地址、电话号码及电子邮件地址（选项 D）。\n\n请求陈述只需包含足够事实，使法院可以合理推断存在可获救济的请求。除非请求主张欺诈或错误（本题均不涉及），无需详细、具体的事实。因此，患者律师无需在起诉状中包含表明患者有权获得救济的详细请求陈述。\n\n学习目标：向联邦法院提交的起诉状必须包含：(1) 载有法院、标题及各方的标题栏；(2) 简短、明确说明法院管辖权的陈述；(3) 简短、明确说明原告有权获得救济的请求陈述；(4) 救济请求；以及 (5) 律师或无律师代理原告的签名和联系信息。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8–11（诉状要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-067"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-212","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":212,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A pedestrian was struck in a crosswalk by a truck and severely injured. The pedestrian brought a federal diversity action against the driver of the truck, alleging the following in the complaint: \"On January 15, 2019, on Broad Street in City A, located in State B, the defendant negligently drove a motor vehicle, striking the plaintiff. As a result, the plaintiff was physically injured, lost wages or income, suffered physical and mental pain, and incurred medical expenses of $100,000.\" The truck driver has moved to dismiss the action for failure to state a claim, arguing that the complaint lacks sufficient detail. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the complaint alleges facts showing plausible entitlement to relief.","B":"No, because the pedestrian has not had an opportunity to conduct discovery.","C":"Yes, because the complaint fails to allege facts showing probable entitlement to relief.","D":"Yes, because the complaint uses the term \"negligently,\" which is a legal conclusion."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Specificity of pleadings\n\nClaim Pleading requirements\n\nGeneral rule • Complaint – short & plain statement of claim showing plaintiff is entitled to relief\n• Answer – short & plain statement of admissions, denials, defenses & claims against other parties Fraud/mistake/mental • Malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition condition may be alleged generally\n• Circumstances giving rise to fraud/mistake must be stated with particularity Conditions precedent • Assertion that conditions precedent occurred may be alleged generally\n• Denial that conditions precedent occurred must be stated with particularity Damages • General damages may be alleged generally\n• Special damages must be specifically stated A complaint is the pleading that starts a civil lawsuit and states the basis for the plaintiff's claim. It generally must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief.* The statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists. A court may dismiss a complaint for failure to state a claim if the complaint does not satisfy this low standard. Here, the pedestrian's complaint sets forth that the truck driver negligently struck the pedestrian on a particular date (January 15, 2019) and at a specific location (Broad Street in City A, located in State B). It then lists the damages that the pedestrian seeks to recover. Therefore, the complaint alleges facts showing plausible entitlement to relief, and the court will likely deny the truck driver's motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. *Detailed, specific facts are unnecessary unless the claim alleges fraud or mistake (neither of which applies here). (Choice B) Discovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit that occurs after the pleading phase in which the parties are compelled to disclose information related to the case. Although the pedestrian has had no opportunity to conduct discovery, the court will deny the truck driver's motion because the complaint sets forth a plausible claim for relief.\n\n(Choice C) Plausible entitlement to relief is a lower standard than probable entitlement to relief. The higher standard of probable entitlement is only used in situations where a preliminary injunction is sought (not seen here). (Choice D) A plaintiff can state legal conclusions in a complaint. Therefore, the fact that the pedestrian's complaint uses the legal term \"negligently\" does not render the complaint defective. Educational objective: A complaint must generally contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. The statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8 (complaint requirements).\n\n• Bell Atl. Corp. v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544, 555–56 (2007) (holding that a complaint must contain a short and plain statement containing enough facts for the court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists).","rule":"A complaint must generally contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. The statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists.","irac":{"issue":"A pedestrian was struck in a crosswalk by a truck and severely injured. The pedestrian brought a federal diversity action against the driver of the truck, alleging the following in the complaint: \"On January 15, 2019, on Broad Street in City A, located in State B, the defendant negligently drove a motor vehicle, striking the plaintiff. As a result, the plaintiff was physically injured, lost wages or income, suffered physical and mental pain, and incurred medical expenses of $100,000.\" The truck driver has moved to dismiss the action for failure to state a claim, arguing that the complaint lacks sufficient detail. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"A complaint must generally contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. The statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists.","analysis":"Specificity of pleadings\n\nClaim Pleading requirements\n\nGeneral rule • Complaint – short & plain statement of claim showing plaintiff is entitled to relief\n• Answer – short & plain statement of admissions, denials, defenses & claims against other parties Fraud/mistake/mental • Malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition condition may be alleged generally\n• Circumstances giving rise to fraud/mistake must be stated with particularity Conditions precedent • Assertion that conditions precedent occurred may be alleged generally\n• Denial that conditions precedent occurred must be stated with particularity Damages • General damages may be alleged generally\n• Special damages must be specifically stated A complaint is the pleading that starts a civil lawsuit and states the basis for the plaintiff's claim. It generally must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief.* The statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists. A court may dismiss a complaint for failure to state a claim if the complaint does not satisfy this low standard. Here, the pedestrian's complaint sets forth that the truck driver negligently struck the pedestrian on a particular date (January 15, 2019) and at a specific location (Broad Street in City A, located in State B). It then lists the damages that the pedestrian seeks to recover. Therefore, the complaint alleges facts showing plausible entitlement to relief, and the court will likely deny the truck driver's motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim. *Detailed, specific facts are unnecessary unless the claim alleges fraud or mistake (neither of which applies here). (Choice B) Discovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit that occurs after the pleading phase in which the parties are compelled to disclose information related to the case. Although the pedestrian has had no opportunity to conduct discovery, the court will deny the truck driver's motion because the complaint sets forth a plausible claim for relief.\n\n(Choice C) Plausible entitlement to relief is a lower standard than probable entitlement to relief. The higher standard of probable entitlement is only used in situations where a preliminary injunction is sought (not seen here). (Choice D) A plaintiff can state legal conclusions in a complaint. Therefore, the fact that the pedestrian's complaint uses the legal term \"negligently\" does not render the complaint defective. Educational objective: A complaint must generally contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. The statement of the claim need only contain enough facts for a court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8 (complaint requirements).\n\n• Bell Atl. Corp. v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544, 555–56 (2007) (holding that a complaint must contain a short and plain statement containing enough facts for the court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8 (complaint requirements).\n\n• Bell Atl. Corp. v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544, 555–56 (2007) (holding that a complaint must contain a short and plain statement containing enough facts for the court to plausibly infer that a claim for relief exists).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[640],"sourceAnswerPages":[641,642],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名行人在斑马线上被卡车撞伤，伤势严重。行人对卡车司机提起联邦多元州籍诉讼，起诉状中主张：“2019 年 1 月 15 日，在 B 州 A 市 Broad Street，被告过失驾驶机动车撞击原告。结果，原告身体受伤、损失工资或收入、遭受身体和精神痛苦，并产生 100,000 美元医疗费用。”卡车司机以未提出可获救济的请求为由申请驳回，主张起诉状细节不足。法院可能批准动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为起诉状主张的事实表明原告合理可能有权获得救济。","B":"不会，因为行人尚无机会进行证据开示。","C":"会，因为起诉状未主张表明原告很可能有权获得救济的事实。","D":"会，因为起诉状使用“过失”这一法律结论。"},"explanationZh":"诉状的具体程度：\n\n请求｜诉状要求\n一般规则｜起诉状——简短、明确陈述请求，表明原告有权获得救济；答辩状——简短、明确陈述承认、否认、抗辩及针对其他各方的请求\n欺诈、错误及精神状态｜恶意、意图、知情或其他精神状态可作一般性主张；引起欺诈或错误的情形必须具体陈述\n先决条件｜可一般性主张先决条件已经发生；否认先决条件发生必须具体陈述\n损害｜一般损害可以一般性主张；特别损害必须具体陈述\n\n起诉状是启动民事诉讼并陈述原告请求依据的诉状。通常必须包含简短、明确的请求陈述，表明原告有权获得救济。* 请求陈述只需包含足够事实，使法院可以合理推断存在可获救济的请求。如果起诉状不符合这一较低标准，法院可以未提出可获救济请求为由驳回。\n\n本题起诉状陈述，卡车司机在特定日期（2019 年 1 月 15 日）和具体地点（B 州 A 市 Broad Street）过失撞击行人，并列出行人请求赔偿的损害。因此，起诉状主张的事实表明原告合理可能有权获得救济，法院很可能驳回卡车司机的驳回动议。\n\n*除非请求主张欺诈或错误（本题均不涉及），无需详细、具体的事实。\n\n（选项 B）证据开示是诉状阶段后的审前阶段，双方在该阶段被要求披露案件相关信息。行人虽尚无机会进行证据开示，法院驳回司机动议的原因是起诉状提出了合理可能获得救济的请求。\n\n（选项 C）合理可能有权获得救济的标准低于很可能有权获得救济的标准。较高的“很可能”标准仅用于请求初步禁令的情形，本题并非如此。\n\n（选项 D）原告可以在起诉状中陈述法律结论。因此，起诉状使用法律术语“过失”，不会使其存在缺陷。\n\n学习目标：起诉状通常必须包含简短、明确的请求陈述，表明原告有权获得救济。只需包含足够事实，使法院可以合理推断存在可获救济的请求。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8（起诉状要求）。\n• Bell Atl. Corp. v. Twombly, 550 U.S. 544, 555–56 (2007)（判定起诉状必须包含简短、明确的陈述及足够事实，使法院可合理推断存在可获救济的请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-213","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":213,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman from State A purchased a luxury motorhome online and wired the dealership a deposit for $80,000. The dealership is incorporated and headquartered in State B. When the dealership shipped the motorhome to the woman, she discovered that it failed to meet the contract specifications and refused to accept delivery. The woman then permanently moved to State C. The woman sued the dealership in a federal court in State C for breach of contract to recover her deposit. The dealership moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue. The court granted the motion on both grounds and dismissed the action. The woman immediately filed the same breach-of-contract claim in a federal court in State B. The dealership filed a motion to dismiss, arguing that the dismissal of the first action precludes the second action. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because a dismissal does not operate as an adjudication on the merits.","B":"No, because the first action was dismissed without prejudice.","C":"Yes, because the dealership obtained a court order to dismiss the first action.","D":"Yes, because the first action was involuntarily dismissed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Voluntary v. Involuntary dismissal\n\n(FRCP 41)\n\nProcess Effect\n\nVoluntary π may dismiss suit without court Dismissal without prejudice dismissal order when: unless:\n\n• notice of dismissal filed • π previously dismissed before ∆ serves answer or federal or state action summary judgment motion or based on same claim or\n• all parties sign stipulation of • notice or stipulation dismissal states otherwise π may request court order to dismiss suit unless:\n\n• ∆'s counterclaim cannot be independently adjudicated Involuntary ∆ may move to dismiss suit if π failed Dismissal with prejudice unless: dismissal to:\n• court order states\n• prosecute own action or otherwise or\n• comply with rules or court • based on lack of order jurisdiction, improper ∆ may move to dismiss suit via: venue, or failure to join required party\n• pre-answer motion\n• judgment on the pleadings or\n• summary judgment FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; π = plaintiff; ∆ = defendant.\n\nA suit can be dismissed in two ways:\n• Voluntary dismissal – when the plaintiff moves to dismiss the suit\n• Involuntary dismissal – when the defendant moves to dismiss the suit (as seen here) An involuntary dismissal generally results in an adjudication on the merits (Choice A). This means that the plaintiff's action is dismissed with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the same defendant on the same claim in the future (ie, claim preclusion).\n\nHowever, an involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join an indispensable party is without prejudice (as seen here). This means that the dismissal is not an adjudication on the merits and does not trigger claim preclusion, so the woman can assert the same claim against the dealership in the State B federal court (Choice D). Therefore, the court will likely deny the dealership's motion to dismiss the second action. (Choice C) Whether the court entered an order dismissing the action would be relevant in a voluntary dismissal analysis to determine whether the dismissal was with or without prejudice. However, it is irrelevant in an involuntary dismissal analysis because such dismissals always require a court order. Educational objective: An involuntary dismissal is generally with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the same defendant on the same claim in the future. However, an involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party is without prejudice. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b) (involuntary dismissal).\n\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2373 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the effect of an involuntary dismissal for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party).","rule":"An involuntary dismissal is generally with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the same defendant on the same claim in the future. However, an involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party is without prejudice.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A purchased a luxury motorhome online and wired the dealership a deposit for $80,000. The dealership is incorporated and headquartered in State B. When the dealership shipped the motorhome to the woman, she discovered that it failed to meet the contract specifications and refused to accept delivery. The woman then permanently moved to State C. The woman sued the dealership in a federal court in State C for breach of contract to recover her deposit. The dealership moved to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction and improper venue. The court granted the motion on both grounds and dismissed the action. The woman immediately filed the same breach-of-contract claim in a federal court in State B. The dealership filed a motion to dismiss, arguing that the dismissal of the first action precludes the second action. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"An involuntary dismissal is generally with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the same defendant on the same claim in the future. However, an involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party is without prejudice.","analysis":"Voluntary v. Involuntary dismissal\n\n(FRCP 41)\n\nProcess Effect\n\nVoluntary π may dismiss suit without court Dismissal without prejudice dismissal order when: unless:\n\n• notice of dismissal filed • π previously dismissed before ∆ serves answer or federal or state action summary judgment motion or based on same claim or\n• all parties sign stipulation of • notice or stipulation dismissal states otherwise π may request court order to dismiss suit unless:\n\n• ∆'s counterclaim cannot be independently adjudicated Involuntary ∆ may move to dismiss suit if π failed Dismissal with prejudice unless: dismissal to:\n• court order states\n• prosecute own action or otherwise or\n• comply with rules or court • based on lack of order jurisdiction, improper ∆ may move to dismiss suit via: venue, or failure to join required party\n• pre-answer motion\n• judgment on the pleadings or\n• summary judgment FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; π = plaintiff; ∆ = defendant.\n\nA suit can be dismissed in two ways:\n• Voluntary dismissal – when the plaintiff moves to dismiss the suit\n• Involuntary dismissal – when the defendant moves to dismiss the suit (as seen here) An involuntary dismissal generally results in an adjudication on the merits (Choice A). This means that the plaintiff's action is dismissed with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the same defendant on the same claim in the future (ie, claim preclusion).\n\nHowever, an involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join an indispensable party is without prejudice (as seen here). This means that the dismissal is not an adjudication on the merits and does not trigger claim preclusion, so the woman can assert the same claim against the dealership in the State B federal court (Choice D). Therefore, the court will likely deny the dealership's motion to dismiss the second action. (Choice C) Whether the court entered an order dismissing the action would be relevant in a voluntary dismissal analysis to determine whether the dismissal was with or without prejudice. However, it is irrelevant in an involuntary dismissal analysis because such dismissals always require a court order. Educational objective: An involuntary dismissal is generally with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the same defendant on the same claim in the future. However, an involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party is without prejudice. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b) (involuntary dismissal).\n\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2373 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the effect of an involuntary dismissal for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b) (involuntary dismissal).\n\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2373 (4th ed. 2020) (explaining the effect of an involuntary dismissal for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[643],"sourceAnswerPages":[644,645],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的女子在网上购买一辆豪华房车，并向经销商电汇 80,000 美元定金。经销商在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州。经销商将房车运给女子时，她发现车辆不符合合同规格，拒绝收货。随后，女子永久迁往 C 州。女子在 C 州联邦法院起诉经销商违约，请求返还定金。经销商以缺乏对人管辖权及审判地不适当为由申请驳回。法院依据两项理由批准动议并驳回诉讼。女子立即在 B 州联邦法院提出相同违约请求。经销商申请驳回，主张第一案的驳回排除第二案。法院可能批准该动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为驳回不构成实体裁判。","B":"不会，因为第一案属于无损害驳回。","C":"会，因为经销商取得了驳回第一案的法院命令。","D":"会，因为第一案是非自愿驳回。"},"explanationZh":"自愿驳回与非自愿驳回（FRCP 41）：\n\n类型｜程序｜效力\n自愿驳回｜原告在以下情形下可以不经法院命令驳回诉讼：(1) 被告送达答辩状或简易判决动议前提交驳回通知；或 (2) 所有各方签署驳回约定。除非原告此前曾基于相同请求驳回联邦或州诉讼，或通知或约定另有说明，否则为无损害驳回。原告也可申请法院命令驳回，但被告反诉无法独立裁判时除外。\n非自愿驳回｜原告未推进自己的诉讼、未遵守规则或法院命令时，被告可以申请驳回；也可通过答辩前动议、诉状判决或简易判决申请驳回。除非法院命令另有说明，或基于缺乏管辖权、审判地不适当或未合并必要当事人，否则为有损害驳回。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》；π = 原告；∆ = 被告。\n\n诉讼可以通过两种方式驳回：\n• 自愿驳回——原告申请驳回诉讼；\n• 非自愿驳回——被告申请驳回诉讼（本题即如此）。\n\n非自愿驳回通常构成实体裁判（选项 A），即有损害驳回，禁止原告以后对同一被告提出相同请求，也就是触发请求权排除。\n\n但是，基于缺乏管辖权、审判地不适当或未合并不可或缺当事人的非自愿驳回属于无损害驳回（本题即如此）。这意味着驳回并非实体裁判，不触发请求权排除，所以女子可以在 B 州联邦法院对经销商提出相同请求（选项 D）。因此，法院很可能驳回经销商要求驳回第二案的动议。\n\n（选项 C）法院是否登记驳回命令，在自愿驳回分析中可能与判断有损害或无损害有关。但在非自愿驳回分析中无关，因为非自愿驳回总是需要法院命令。\n\n学习目标：非自愿驳回通常为有损害驳回，禁止原告以后对同一被告提出相同请求。但是，基于缺乏管辖权、审判地不适当或未合并必要当事人的非自愿驳回为无损害驳回。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b)（非自愿驳回）。\n• 9 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2373 (4th ed. 2020)（说明因缺乏管辖权、审判地不适当或未合并必要当事人而非自愿驳回的效力）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-214","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":214,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A manufacturer sued its competitor for patent infringement in a federal district court. On February 1, the manufacturer served the competitor with a summons and complaint. The day after the competitor was served with process, the competitor filed and served an answer. On February 28, the manufacturer delivered to the competitor a request to produce certain documents within the next 15 days. The competitor did not respond to the request for production or produce the requested documents by March 15. As a result, the manufacturer filed a motion to compel a written response to the request for production on April 1, the day after the parties' initial planning conference. Will the federal court likely grant the manufacturer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because a request for production is not permitted less than 30 days after service of process.","B":"No, because the competitor's response is not due until 30 days after the initial planning conference.","C":"Yes, because the competitor failed to produce the requested documents by the 15-day deadline.","D":"Yes, because the competitor's response was due 30 days after the request for production was delivered."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A request for production is a method of discovery used to compel an opposing party to produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information, tangible things, and land. Such a request is permitted 21 days after service of process but is typically not made until the parties have held an initial planning conference (Choice A). When the request is served after the initial planning conference, the party receiving the request must serve a response in writing within 30 days of service. However, FRCP 26(d) allows a party to deliver an \"early\" request for production before the initial planning conference. When the request is delivered before the initial planning conference, a response is not due until 30 days after the conference.* That is because the early request is considered served at the initial planning conference. Here, the competitor was served with process on February 1, which is when the manufacturer delivered the summons and complaint. On February 28, the manufacturer delivered to the competitor a request to produce certain documents within 15 days. However, this request was delivered before the initial planning conference on March 31, so the competitor has until April 30 to respond to the manufacturer's request. Therefore, the court will likely deny the manufacturer's motion to compel. *The parties may stipulate to, or the court may order, a different deadline to serve a response to a request for production—regardless of whether the request for production is delivered before or served after the initial planning conference. (Choice C) The parties may stipulate to discovery deadlines that are shorter or longer than those set by the federal rules. However, one party cannot unilaterally amend a discovery deadline—as the manufacturer attempted to do here. (Choice D) The competitor's response was due 30 days after the initial planning conference—not 30 days from when the request for production was delivered. Educational objective: A request for production can be served before the parties hold an initial planning conference, and the other party must respond to that request in writing within 30 days after that conference. If the request for production is served after the conference, then the response is due within 30 days after the request is served. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d)(2) (timing of request for production or inspection).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34 (request for production or inspection).","rule":"A request for production can be served before the parties hold an initial planning conference, and the other party must respond to that request in writing within 30 days after that conference. If the request for production is served after the conference, then the response is due within 30 days after the request is served.","irac":{"issue":"A manufacturer sued its competitor for patent infringement in a federal district court. On February 1, the manufacturer served the competitor with a summons and complaint. The day after the competitor was served with process, the competitor filed and served an answer. On February 28, the manufacturer delivered to the competitor a request to produce certain documents within the next 15 days. The competitor did not respond to the request for production or produce the requested documents by March 15. As a result, the manufacturer filed a motion to compel a written response to the request for production on April 1, the day after the parties' initial planning conference. Will the federal court likely grant the manufacturer's motion?","rule":"A request for production can be served before the parties hold an initial planning conference, and the other party must respond to that request in writing within 30 days after that conference. If the request for production is served after the conference, then the response is due within 30 days after the request is served.","analysis":"A request for production is a method of discovery used to compel an opposing party to produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information, tangible things, and land. Such a request is permitted 21 days after service of process but is typically not made until the parties have held an initial planning conference (Choice A). When the request is served after the initial planning conference, the party receiving the request must serve a response in writing within 30 days of service. However, FRCP 26(d) allows a party to deliver an \"early\" request for production before the initial planning conference. When the request is delivered before the initial planning conference, a response is not due until 30 days after the conference.* That is because the early request is considered served at the initial planning conference. Here, the competitor was served with process on February 1, which is when the manufacturer delivered the summons and complaint. On February 28, the manufacturer delivered to the competitor a request to produce certain documents within 15 days. However, this request was delivered before the initial planning conference on March 31, so the competitor has until April 30 to respond to the manufacturer's request. Therefore, the court will likely deny the manufacturer's motion to compel. *The parties may stipulate to, or the court may order, a different deadline to serve a response to a request for production—regardless of whether the request for production is delivered before or served after the initial planning conference. (Choice C) The parties may stipulate to discovery deadlines that are shorter or longer than those set by the federal rules. However, one party cannot unilaterally amend a discovery deadline—as the manufacturer attempted to do here. (Choice D) The competitor's response was due 30 days after the initial planning conference—not 30 days from when the request for production was delivered. Educational objective: A request for production can be served before the parties hold an initial planning conference, and the other party must respond to that request in writing within 30 days after that conference. If the request for production is served after the conference, then the response is due within 30 days after the request is served. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d)(2) (timing of request for production or inspection).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34 (request for production or inspection).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d)(2) (timing of request for production or inspection).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34 (request for production or inspection).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[646],"sourceAnswerPages":[647],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家制造商在联邦地区法院起诉竞争对手侵犯其专利。2 月 1 日，制造商向竞争对手送达传票和起诉状。诉讼文书送达次日，竞争对手提交并送达答辩状。2 月 28 日，制造商向竞争对手交付请求，要求其在随后 15 天内提交特定文件。竞争对手没有在 3 月 15 日前回应提交请求或提供所请求文件。因此，制造商于 4 月 1 日，即双方首次规划会议次日，申请强制书面回应提交请求。联邦法院可能批准制造商的动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为诉讼文书送达后不足 30 天不得提出提交请求。","B":"不会，因为竞争对手的回应直到首次规划会议后 30 天才到期。","C":"会，因为竞争对手未在 15 天期限内提交所请求文件。","D":"会，因为竞争对手的回应在提交请求交付后 30 天到期。"},"explanationZh":"提交请求是一种证据开示方法，用于迫使相对方提交并允许查阅文件、电子存储信息、有形物及土地。诉讼文书送达 21 天后即可提出这种请求，但通常要到双方举行首次规划会议后才提出（选项 A）。\n\n请求在首次规划会议后送达时，收到请求的一方必须在送达后 30 天内送达书面回应。但是，FRCP 26(d) 允许一方在首次规划会议前交付“提前”提交请求。请求在会议前交付时，回应直到会议后 30 天才到期，* 因为提前请求视为在首次规划会议时送达。\n\n本题竞争对手于 2 月 1 日收到传票和起诉状，即诉讼文书送达。2 月 28 日，制造商交付要求其在 15 天内提交特定文件的请求。但该请求是在 3 月 31 日首次规划会议前交付，所以竞争对手可在 4 月 30 日前回应。因此，法院很可能驳回制造商的强制动议。\n\n*无论提交请求在首次规划会议前交付还是会议后送达，双方均可约定或法院可命令采用不同回应期限。\n\n（选项 C）双方可约定比联邦规则规定更短或更长的证据开示期限，但一方不能单方面修改期限，本题制造商即试图如此。\n\n（选项 D）竞争对手的回应在首次规划会议后 30 天到期，而不是请求交付后 30 天到期。\n\n学习目标：提交请求可以在双方首次规划会议前交付；相对方必须在该会议后 30 天内书面回应。如果请求在会议后送达，则回应须在请求送达后 30 天内作出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d)(2)（提交或查验请求的时间）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34（提交或查验请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-068"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-215","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":215,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A distributor brought a diversity action against a manufacturer in a federal court for breach of contract. During discovery, the distributor's attorney signed and served a request that the manufacturer produce every contract that the manufacturer entered during the past 50 years. The manufacturer's attorney objected in writing to the request, arguing that the request was unduly burdensome and inconsistent with the relevant case law. The distributor's attorney filed a motion to compel the manufacturer to produce the requested documents. After a hearing on the motion, the trial judge concluded on the record that she agreed with the manufacturer's objections. The judge then ordered the distributor to pay the reasonable expenses incurred by the manufacturer related to the request and hearing. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict for the distributor, and the judge entered judgment on the verdict. The distributor has appealed the award of sanctions. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the sanctions?","choices":{"A":"No.","B":"Yes, because the jury entered a verdict in favor of the distributor.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer did not request the sanctions.","D":"Yes, because the trial judge should have issued the sanctions against the distributor's attorney, not against the distributor directly."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Discovery certifications\n\n(FRCP 26(g))\n\nEvery discovery disclosure, request, response & objection must be signed by attorney (or unrepresented party), thereby certifying that:\n\n• disclosure is complete & correct or\n• request, response, or objection is: – consistent with existing law or supported by nonfrivolous argument to extend, modify, or reverse existing law or to establish new law – not made for improper purpose (eg, harass, cause unnecessary delay, needlessly increase cost of litigation) and – reasonable & not unduly burdensome or expensive FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties make required disclosures and seek information through various discovery requests, including requests for production. Every disclosure, request, response, or objection must be signed by an attorney or unrepresented party. By signing, the attorney or party certifies to the best of the person's knowledge that (1) the disclosure is complete and correct or (2) the request, response, or objection is:\n• consistent with existing law or supported by a nonfrivolous argument to extend, modify, or reverse existing law or to establish new law\n• not made for any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation and\n• reasonable and not unduly burdensome or expensive. A violation of these rules, without substantial justification, requires the court to impose appropriate sanctions on the signer, the party on whose behalf the signer acted, or both. Sanctions may include an order to pay reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the violation. Here, the trial judge determined that the request for production signed by the distributor's attorney was unduly burdensome and inconsistent with the relevant case law. As a result, the judge had the authority to order the distributor (party on whose behalf the attorney acted) to pay the manufacturer's reasonable expenses (Choice D). Therefore, the appellate court will likely uphold, not overturn, the sanctions. (Choice B) Sanctions may be imposed on a party or its attorney for improper behavior even if that party ultimately prevails on the merits of the case (as seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the manufacturer did not request sanctions is irrelevant because a court may impose sanctions on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte). Educational objective: A court must impose appropriate sanctions on an attorney, party, or both when a discovery disclosure, request, response, or objection is improperly certified. Sanctions may include an order to pay reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the violation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(g) (certification of discovery requirements).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2052 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining what sanctions may be issued for improper discovery certification).","rule":"A court must impose appropriate sanctions on an attorney, party, or both when a discovery disclosure, request, response, or objection is improperly certified. Sanctions may include an order to pay reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the violation.","irac":{"issue":"A distributor brought a diversity action against a manufacturer in a federal court for breach of contract. During discovery, the distributor's attorney signed and served a request that the manufacturer produce every contract that the manufacturer entered during the past 50 years. The manufacturer's attorney objected in writing to the request, arguing that the request was unduly burdensome and inconsistent with the relevant case law. The distributor's attorney filed a motion to compel the manufacturer to produce the requested documents. After a hearing on the motion, the trial judge concluded on the record that she agreed with the manufacturer's objections. The judge then ordered the distributor to pay the reasonable expenses incurred by the manufacturer related to the request and hearing. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict for the distributor, and the judge entered judgment on the verdict. The distributor has appealed the award of sanctions. Is the appellate court likely to overturn the sanctions?","rule":"A court must impose appropriate sanctions on an attorney, party, or both when a discovery disclosure, request, response, or objection is improperly certified. Sanctions may include an order to pay reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the violation.","analysis":"Discovery certifications\n\n(FRCP 26(g))\n\nEvery discovery disclosure, request, response & objection must be signed by attorney (or unrepresented party), thereby certifying that:\n\n• disclosure is complete & correct or\n• request, response, or objection is: – consistent with existing law or supported by nonfrivolous argument to extend, modify, or reverse existing law or to establish new law – not made for improper purpose (eg, harass, cause unnecessary delay, needlessly increase cost of litigation) and – reasonable & not unduly burdensome or expensive FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties make required disclosures and seek information through various discovery requests, including requests for production. Every disclosure, request, response, or objection must be signed by an attorney or unrepresented party. By signing, the attorney or party certifies to the best of the person's knowledge that (1) the disclosure is complete and correct or (2) the request, response, or objection is:\n• consistent with existing law or supported by a nonfrivolous argument to extend, modify, or reverse existing law or to establish new law\n• not made for any improper purpose, such as to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation and\n• reasonable and not unduly burdensome or expensive. A violation of these rules, without substantial justification, requires the court to impose appropriate sanctions on the signer, the party on whose behalf the signer acted, or both. Sanctions may include an order to pay reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the violation. Here, the trial judge determined that the request for production signed by the distributor's attorney was unduly burdensome and inconsistent with the relevant case law. As a result, the judge had the authority to order the distributor (party on whose behalf the attorney acted) to pay the manufacturer's reasonable expenses (Choice D). Therefore, the appellate court will likely uphold, not overturn, the sanctions. (Choice B) Sanctions may be imposed on a party or its attorney for improper behavior even if that party ultimately prevails on the merits of the case (as seen here).\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the manufacturer did not request sanctions is irrelevant because a court may impose sanctions on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte). Educational objective: A court must impose appropriate sanctions on an attorney, party, or both when a discovery disclosure, request, response, or objection is improperly certified. Sanctions may include an order to pay reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the violation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(g) (certification of discovery requirements).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2052 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining what sanctions may be issued for improper discovery certification).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(g) (certification of discovery requirements).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2052 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining what sanctions may be issued for improper discovery certification).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[649],"sourceAnswerPages":[650,651],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家经销商在联邦法院对一家制造商提起多元州籍违约诉讼。证据开示期间，经销商律师签署并送达一项请求，要求制造商提交过去 50 年订立的每份合同。制造商律师以该请求负担过重且与相关判例法不符为由，提出书面异议。经销商律师申请强制制造商提交所请求文件。动议听证后，审判法官在案卷记录中认定其同意制造商的异议，随后命令经销商支付制造商因该请求和听证而产生的合理费用。审判结束时，陪审团判经销商胜诉，法官依裁决登记判决。经销商对制裁提起上诉。上诉法院可能推翻制裁吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会。","B":"会，因为陪审团判经销商胜诉。","C":"会，因为制造商没有请求制裁。","D":"会，因为审判法官本应制裁经销商律师，而非直接制裁经销商。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示证明（FRCP 26(g)）：\n\n每项证据开示披露、请求、回应和异议，均须由律师或无律师代理的一方签署，由此证明：\n• 披露完整且正确；或\n• 请求、回应或异议：\n  – 符合现行法律，或得到延伸、修改、推翻现行法律或确立新法律的非轻率论证支持；\n  – 并非出于不当目的，例如骚扰、造成不必要拖延或无谓增加诉讼成本；并且\n  – 合理，且负担或费用不过重。\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n证据开示是诉讼的审前阶段，双方作出必要披露，并通过包括提交请求在内的各种证据开示请求寻求信息。每项披露、请求、回应或异议都必须由律师或无律师代理的一方签署。签署即证明，据其所知：(1) 披露完整且正确；或 (2) 请求、回应或异议符合上述三项要求。\n\n在没有实质正当理由的情况下违反这些规则，法院必须对签署人、签署人代表的一方或二者给予适当制裁。制裁可以包括命令支付因违规产生的合理费用，包括律师费。\n\n本题审判法官认定，经销商律师签署的提交请求负担过重且与相关判例法不符。因此，法官有权命令经销商，即律师代表的一方，支付制造商的合理费用（选项 D）。所以上诉法院很可能维持而非推翻制裁。\n\n（选项 B）即使一方最终在案件实体上胜诉（本题即如此），仍可因该方或其律师的不当行为受到制裁。\n\n（选项 C）制造商是否请求制裁无关，因为法院可以自行给予制裁。\n\n学习目标：证据开示披露、请求、回应或异议被不当证明时，法院必须对律师、当事人或二者给予适当制裁。制裁可包括命令支付违规造成的合理费用，包括律师费。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(g)（证据开示要求的证明）。\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2052 (3d ed. 2020)（说明不当证据开示证明可能受到的制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-216","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":216,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A manufacturer sued its competitor in federal district court for allegedly violating the manufacturer's patent. The competitor filed an answer that claimed the patent was invalid and asserted a counterclaim for damages. During a bench trial, the competitor presented overwhelming evidence showing that the manufacturer's chief executive officer (CEO) had used deceptive methods to obtain the patent. At the close of evidence, the judge issued findings of fact and conclusions of law, and the court entered judgment in favor of the competitor. The competitor was worried that the manufacturer would declare bankruptcy and be unable to pay the judgment, so the competitor moved to amend its counterclaim to add the manufacturer's CEO as a party. It also requested that the court alter the judgment to require the CEO to pay the judgment in the event the manufacturer files for bankruptcy. The district court immediately granted the competitor's motion, and the CEO appealed. The applicable statute of limitations governing the competitor's counterclaim has yet to expire. Is the appellate court likely to affirm the district court's action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the district court violated the CEO's due process rights by not allowing him to respond to the counterclaim.","B":"No, because the manufacturer did not consent in writing to the competitor's amendment.","C":"Yes, because the applicable statute of limitations for the competitor's counterclaim against the CEO has not expired.","D":"Yes, because the CEO should have been aware of the pending lawsuit."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under FRCP 15, a party may amend his/her pleading (eg, counterclaim) with the opposing party's written consent or the court's permission before, during, or after trial. The court's permission should be freely granted when justice so requires and the amendment will not violate due process. In Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc., the Supreme Court held that a court violates due process when it allows a new party to be added in an amended pleading but does not permit the new party to respond.* Here, the district court permitted the competitor to amend its counterclaim to add the manufacturer's CEO as a party. The court then violated the CEO's due process rights by not giving him an opportunity to respond before altering the judgment to hold him personally liable in the event the manufacturer declares bankruptcy. Therefore, the appellate court will likely reverse and remand to give the CEO an opportunity to respond. *Under FRCP 15(a), a new party may respond within the time remaining to respond to the original pleading or within 14 days after being served with the amended pleading— whichever is later. (Choice B) The manufacturer did not need to consent in writing to the competitor's amendment. That is because a federal court can permit amendments to pleadings without the opposing party's consent and should do so when justice requires—so long as the amendment complies with due process (not seen here). (Choice C) Although the applicable statute of limitations has not expired, the court violated the CEO's due process rights by amending the counterclaim and altering the judgment without allowing him a chance to respond. (Choice D) The fact that the CEO should have been aware of the pending lawsuit does not change the due process requirement that he be given an opportunity to respond to the competitor's amended counterclaim. Educational objective: A court should freely allow parties to amend their pleadings when justice so requires and due process will not be violated. When a court grants a party's motion to amend a pleading to add a new party, due process requires that the new party be given an opportunity to respond. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(b) (amending pleadings during and after trial).\n\n• Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc., 529 U.S. 460, 465–67 (2000) (explaining that due process requires that a new party be given an opportunity to respond to an amended pleading).","rule":"A court should freely allow parties to amend their pleadings when justice so requires and due process will not be violated. When a court grants a party's motion to amend a pleading to add a new party, due process requires that the new party be given an opportunity to respond.","irac":{"issue":"A manufacturer sued its competitor in federal district court for allegedly violating the manufacturer's patent. The competitor filed an answer that claimed the patent was invalid and asserted a counterclaim for damages. During a bench trial, the competitor presented overwhelming evidence showing that the manufacturer's chief executive officer (CEO) had used deceptive methods to obtain the patent. At the close of evidence, the judge issued findings of fact and conclusions of law, and the court entered judgment in favor of the competitor. The competitor was worried that the manufacturer would declare bankruptcy and be unable to pay the judgment, so the competitor moved to amend its counterclaim to add the manufacturer's CEO as a party. It also requested that the court alter the judgment to require the CEO to pay the judgment in the event the manufacturer files for bankruptcy. The district court immediately granted the competitor's motion, and the CEO appealed. The applicable statute of limitations governing the competitor's counterclaim has yet to expire. Is the appellate court likely to affirm the district court's action?","rule":"A court should freely allow parties to amend their pleadings when justice so requires and due process will not be violated. When a court grants a party's motion to amend a pleading to add a new party, due process requires that the new party be given an opportunity to respond.","analysis":"Under FRCP 15, a party may amend his/her pleading (eg, counterclaim) with the opposing party's written consent or the court's permission before, during, or after trial. The court's permission should be freely granted when justice so requires and the amendment will not violate due process. In Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc., the Supreme Court held that a court violates due process when it allows a new party to be added in an amended pleading but does not permit the new party to respond.* Here, the district court permitted the competitor to amend its counterclaim to add the manufacturer's CEO as a party. The court then violated the CEO's due process rights by not giving him an opportunity to respond before altering the judgment to hold him personally liable in the event the manufacturer declares bankruptcy. Therefore, the appellate court will likely reverse and remand to give the CEO an opportunity to respond. *Under FRCP 15(a), a new party may respond within the time remaining to respond to the original pleading or within 14 days after being served with the amended pleading— whichever is later. (Choice B) The manufacturer did not need to consent in writing to the competitor's amendment. That is because a federal court can permit amendments to pleadings without the opposing party's consent and should do so when justice requires—so long as the amendment complies with due process (not seen here). (Choice C) Although the applicable statute of limitations has not expired, the court violated the CEO's due process rights by amending the counterclaim and altering the judgment without allowing him a chance to respond. (Choice D) The fact that the CEO should have been aware of the pending lawsuit does not change the due process requirement that he be given an opportunity to respond to the competitor's amended counterclaim. Educational objective: A court should freely allow parties to amend their pleadings when justice so requires and due process will not be violated. When a court grants a party's motion to amend a pleading to add a new party, due process requires that the new party be given an opportunity to respond. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(b) (amending pleadings during and after trial).\n\n• Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc., 529 U.S. 460, 465–67 (2000) (explaining that due process requires that a new party be given an opportunity to respond to an amended pleading).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(b) (amending pleadings during and after trial).\n\n• Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc., 529 U.S. 460, 465–67 (2000) (explaining that due process requires that a new party be given an opportunity to respond to an amended pleading).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[652],"sourceAnswerPages":[653,654],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家制造商在联邦地区法院起诉竞争对手侵犯其专利。竞争对手提交答辩状，主张专利无效，并提出损害赔偿反诉。法官审判期间，竞争对手提出大量压倒性证据，表明制造商的首席执行官（CEO）采用欺骗手段取得专利。举证结束时，法官发布事实认定和法律结论，法院登记有利于竞争对手的判决。竞争对手担心制造商会宣布破产而无力支付判决，遂申请修改反诉，将制造商 CEO 增加为当事人；同时请求法院修改判决，要求制造商申请破产时由 CEO 支付判决。地区法院立即批准竞争对手动议，CEO 提起上诉。竞争对手对 CEO 的反诉所适用的诉讼时效尚未届满。上诉法院可能维持地区法院的做法吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为地区法院没有允许 CEO 回应反诉，侵犯其正当程序权利。","B":"不会，因为制造商没有书面同意竞争对手的修正。","C":"会，因为竞争对手对 CEO 的反诉所适用诉讼时效尚未届满。","D":"会，因为 CEO 本应知道待决诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"根据 FRCP 15，一方可以在审判前、审判期间或审判后，经相对方书面同意或法院许可，修改诉状（例如反诉）。正义需要且修正不违反正当程序时，法院应自由准许。\n\n在 Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc. 案中，最高法院认定，法院允许通过修正诉状增加新当事人，却不允许新当事人回应时，侵犯正当程序。* 本题地区法院允许竞争对手修改反诉，将制造商 CEO 增加为当事人。法院在修改判决、规定制造商破产时由 CEO 承担个人责任前，没有给予 CEO 回应机会，因而侵犯其正当程序权利。因此，上诉法院很可能推翻并发回案件，给予 CEO 回应机会。\n\n*根据 FRCP 15(a)，新当事人可在回应原诉状的剩余期限内，或修正诉状送达后 14 天内回应，以较晚者为准。\n\n（选项 B）制造商无需书面同意竞争对手的修正，因为联邦法院可以在相对方不同意时准许修改诉状，并应在正义需要时准许，但修正必须符合正当程序，本题并不符合。\n\n（选项 C）适用诉讼时效虽未届满，但法院在未给予 CEO 回应机会的情况下修改反诉并变更判决，侵犯了 CEO 的正当程序权利。\n\n（选项 D）CEO 本应知道待决诉讼这一事实，不改变必须给予其回应竞争对手修正反诉机会的正当程序要求。\n\n学习目标：正义需要且不违反正当程序时，法院应自由允许双方修改诉状。法院批准增加新当事人的修正动议时，正当程序要求给予新当事人回应机会。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(b)（审判期间和审判后的诉状修改）。\n• Nelson v. Adams USA, Inc., 529 U.S. 460, 465–67 (2000)（说明正当程序要求给予新当事人回应修正诉状的机会）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-069"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-217","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":217,"topic":"Discovery","question":"The owner of a food processing plant sued a food preservative company in federal court for federal antitrust violations. During discovery, the owner's lawyer served the company with notice of a written deposition of the company. The notice stated the company's name and address and provided the name and address of the officer before whom the deposition would be taken. The company refused to attend the written deposition. The owner moved for sanctions. The company is likely to succeed in defeating the owner's motion on which of the following grounds?","choices":{"A":"The owner failed to designate an individual from the company to answer the questions.","B":"The owner failed to include a subpoena with the notice.","C":"The owner failed to obtain the court's leave before serving the company with the notice.","D":"The owner failed to reasonably describe the matters to be examined."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contents for written notice of deposition*\n\nType Similar contents Unique contents\n\nOral • Deponent's name & address (if known) OR • Time & place of general description to identify deponent or deposition group to which deponent belongs (if name • Method of unknown) recording Written • If deponent is organization, matters for • Officer's name / examination described with reasonable descriptive title & particularity address\n• Copy of deposition questions *Must be served on party deponent to compel his/her attendance.\n\nA deposition is a method of discovery in which a deponent (party or nonparty) is examined under oath and outside court. There are two types of depositions:\n• Oral depositions (most common) – in which the parties' attorneys question and cross-examine the deponent as they would at trial and an officer records that testimony\n\n• Written depositions – in which an officer asks the deponent questions submitted by the parties and then records the deponent's responses\n\nTo compel a party deponent's attendance at either deposition, the deposing party must serve the party deponent with written notice.* If the party deponent is an organization, the notice must describe the matters for examination with reasonable particularity and satisfy all other notice requirements. Based on the matters identified for examination, the organization must then designate one or more individuals to testify on its behalf. If the deponent fails to attend the deposition, the court may impose sanctions. Here, the owner served the company with notice of a written deposition, but the company did not attend. However, the notice was deficient because it failed to reasonably describe the matters for examination. As a result, the company is likely to defeat the owner's motion for sanctions on this ground. *The deposing party must also serve a copy of the written notice on the other parties and, for written depositions, on the officer before whom the deposition will be taken. (Choice A) The company, not the owner, must designate an individual(s) to answer the deposition questions.\n\n(Choice B) A subpoena is required to compel a nonparty deponent's attendance, but a party deponent (eg, the company) can be compelled to attend through written notice. (Choice C) A party may conduct a deposition without the court's leave or the parties' stipulation unless (1) the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition maximum, (2) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, or (3) the deponent was already deposed in the case. None of these exceptions apply here. Educational objective: To compel a party deponent's attendance at a deposition, the deposing party must serve the deponent with written notice. When the deponent is an organization, the notice must describe the matters for examination with reasonable particularity. The organization must then designate one or more individuals to testify on its behalf. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31 (depositions by written questions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6) (notice requirements for deposing an organization).","rule":"To compel a party deponent's attendance at a deposition, the deposing party must serve the deponent with written notice. When the deponent is an organization, the notice must describe the matters for examination with reasonable particularity. The organization must then designate one or more individuals to testify on its behalf.","irac":{"issue":"The owner of a food processing plant sued a food preservative company in federal court for federal antitrust violations. During discovery, the owner's lawyer served the company with notice of a written deposition of the company. The notice stated the company's name and address and provided the name and address of the officer before whom the deposition would be taken. The company refused to attend the written deposition. The owner moved for sanctions. The company is likely to succeed in defeating the owner's motion on which of the following grounds?","rule":"To compel a party deponent's attendance at a deposition, the deposing party must serve the deponent with written notice. When the deponent is an organization, the notice must describe the matters for examination with reasonable particularity. The organization must then designate one or more individuals to testify on its behalf.","analysis":"Contents for written notice of deposition*\n\nType Similar contents Unique contents\n\nOral • Deponent's name & address (if known) OR • Time & place of general description to identify deponent or deposition group to which deponent belongs (if name • Method of unknown) recording Written • If deponent is organization, matters for • Officer's name / examination described with reasonable descriptive title & particularity address\n• Copy of deposition questions *Must be served on party deponent to compel his/her attendance.\n\nA deposition is a method of discovery in which a deponent (party or nonparty) is examined under oath and outside court. There are two types of depositions:\n• Oral depositions (most common) – in which the parties' attorneys question and cross-examine the deponent as they would at trial and an officer records that testimony\n\n• Written depositions – in which an officer asks the deponent questions submitted by the parties and then records the deponent's responses\n\nTo compel a party deponent's attendance at either deposition, the deposing party must serve the party deponent with written notice.* If the party deponent is an organization, the notice must describe the matters for examination with reasonable particularity and satisfy all other notice requirements. Based on the matters identified for examination, the organization must then designate one or more individuals to testify on its behalf. If the deponent fails to attend the deposition, the court may impose sanctions. Here, the owner served the company with notice of a written deposition, but the company did not attend. However, the notice was deficient because it failed to reasonably describe the matters for examination. As a result, the company is likely to defeat the owner's motion for sanctions on this ground. *The deposing party must also serve a copy of the written notice on the other parties and, for written depositions, on the officer before whom the deposition will be taken. (Choice A) The company, not the owner, must designate an individual(s) to answer the deposition questions.\n\n(Choice B) A subpoena is required to compel a nonparty deponent's attendance, but a party deponent (eg, the company) can be compelled to attend through written notice. (Choice C) A party may conduct a deposition without the court's leave or the parties' stipulation unless (1) the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition maximum, (2) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, or (3) the deponent was already deposed in the case. None of these exceptions apply here. Educational objective: To compel a party deponent's attendance at a deposition, the deposing party must serve the deponent with written notice. When the deponent is an organization, the notice must describe the matters for examination with reasonable particularity. The organization must then designate one or more individuals to testify on its behalf. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31 (depositions by written questions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6) (notice requirements for deposing an organization).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31 (depositions by written questions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6) (notice requirements for deposing an organization).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[655],"sourceAnswerPages":[656,657],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家食品加工厂的所有人在联邦法院起诉一家食品防腐剂公司违反联邦反垄断法。证据开示期间，所有人律师向公司送达对公司进行书面问题证言录取的通知。通知写明公司名称和地址，并提供主持证言录取的工作人员姓名和地址。公司拒绝参加书面问题证言录取。所有人申请制裁。公司最可能依据下列哪项理由成功反对该制裁动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"所有人没有指定公司中回答问题的个人。","B":"所有人没有在通知中附上传票。","C":"所有人向公司送达通知前没有取得法院许可。","D":"所有人没有以合理具体程度说明待询问事项。"},"explanationZh":"证言录取书面通知的内容*：\n\n类型｜共同内容｜独有内容\n口头证言录取｜受询问人姓名和地址（如已知），或足以识别受询问人或其所属群体的一般说明（姓名未知时）｜证言录取时间和地点；记录方式\n书面问题证言录取｜组织为受询问人时，以合理具体程度说明待询问事项｜工作人员姓名或描述性职称及地址；证言录取问题副本\n\n*必须向作为当事人的受询问人送达，才能强制其出席。\n\n证言录取是一种证据开示方法，受询问人（当事人或非当事人）在法庭外接受宣誓询问。证言录取有两种：\n• 口头证言录取（最常见）——双方律师像审判时一样询问、交叉询问受询问人，由工作人员记录证言；\n• 书面问题证言录取——工作人员向受询问人提出双方提交的问题，再记录回答。\n\n要强制作为当事人的受询问人参加任一种证言录取，进行证言录取的一方必须向其送达书面通知。* 如果受询问人是组织，通知必须以合理具体程度说明待询问事项，并满足所有其他通知要求。组织随后依据所列事项指定一人或多人代表其作证。受询问人不出席时，法院可以制裁。\n\n本题所有人向公司送达书面问题证言录取通知，但公司没有出席。然而，通知没有以合理具体程度说明待询问事项，存在缺陷。因此，公司很可能据此成功反对所有人的制裁动议。\n\n*进行证言录取的一方还必须向其他各方送达书面通知副本；书面问题证言录取中，还须向主持证言录取的工作人员送达。\n\n（选项 A）应由公司而不是所有人指定回答证言录取问题的一人或多人。\n\n（选项 B）强制非当事人受询问人出席需要传票，但作为当事人的受询问人（例如该公司）可通过书面通知被强制出席。\n\n（选项 C）除非：(1) 证言录取超过每方十次的上限；(2) 在首次规划会议前寻求证言录取；或 (3) 受询问人在案件中已经接受过证言录取，一方无需法院许可或双方约定即可进行证言录取。本题没有任何例外适用。\n\n学习目标：要强制作为当事人的受询问人参加证言录取，必须向其送达书面通知。受询问人是组织时，通知必须以合理具体程度说明待询问事项，随后由组织指定一人或多人代表其作证。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 31（通过书面问题录取证言）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6)（对组织录取证言的通知要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-218","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":218,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A construction company bought lumber from an out-of-state supplier. The lumber was supposed to be pressure-treated, which renders the lumber resistant to rot and termites. The construction company used the lumber to build its headquarters, and after one year, the lumber began to show signs of rot and termites. The construction company sued the supplier for breach of contract in a federal district court. Which method of discovery should the construction company use to most efficiently identify the supplier's employees who might have information about how the lumber was treated?","choices":{"A":"Interrogatories.","B":"Oral deposition.","C":"Request for production of documents.","D":"Subpoena."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Methods of discovery\n\nDepositions • Written or oral examination of party or witness under oath\n• 10 per party Interrogatories • Written questions served on party\n• Serve up to 25\n• Written responses due within 30 days of service Request for • Request served on party (or subpoena served on nonparty) production to produce & allow inspection of documents, electronic information, tangible items, or land\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Requests for • Requests served on other party to admit truth of facts within admission scope of discovery\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Physical/mental • Order by court or parties' agreement for physical or mental exams examination of party if those conditions are in controversy\n\nInterrogatories are written questions served by one party on another party that may inquire about any facts, opinions, and contentions within the scope of discovery. One of the primary purposes of interrogatories is to ascertain facts and information that might lead to other evidence, and the main benefit of interrogatories is that they are simple and inexpensive. Therefore, interrogatories are the most efficient way for the construction company to identify the supplier's employees who might have information about the lumber's treatment. (Choice B) A deposition is a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court. Oral depositions are useful to question potential witnesses because their answers can be used at trial if they are inconsistent with the witnesses' trial testimony or the witnesses are unavailable to testify. But an oral deposition is time-consuming and expensive, so it is not the most efficient method to identify potential witnesses. (Choice C) A request for production is used to make another party produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information, tangible things, or land in their control. Here, the supplier may have documents that show which employees treated the lumber. But the more efficient method to obtain this information is through interrogatories since they place the burden on the responding party (eg, the supplier) to find the answer.\n\n(Choice D) A subpoena is a written court order that can be used in conjunction with other discovery methods to compel a person to appear in court, take part in a deposition, or produce documents in the person's possession. But it would not be useful here since the company is trying to identify potential witnesses. Educational objective: Interrogatories are written questions served by one party on another party that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. This simple and inexpensive method of discovery is primarily used to identify facts and information that might lead to other evidence—eg, the identity of potential witnesses. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","rule":"Interrogatories are written questions served by one party on another party that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. This simple and inexpensive method of discovery is primarily used to identify facts and information that might lead to other evidence—eg, the identity of potential witnesses.","irac":{"issue":"A construction company bought lumber from an out-of-state supplier. The lumber was supposed to be pressure-treated, which renders the lumber resistant to rot and termites. The construction company used the lumber to build its headquarters, and after one year, the lumber began to show signs of rot and termites. The construction company sued the supplier for breach of contract in a federal district court. Which method of discovery should the construction company use to most efficiently identify the supplier's employees who might have information about how the lumber was treated?","rule":"Interrogatories are written questions served by one party on another party that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. This simple and inexpensive method of discovery is primarily used to identify facts and information that might lead to other evidence—eg, the identity of potential witnesses.","analysis":"Methods of discovery\n\nDepositions • Written or oral examination of party or witness under oath\n• 10 per party Interrogatories • Written questions served on party\n• Serve up to 25\n• Written responses due within 30 days of service Request for • Request served on party (or subpoena served on nonparty) production to produce & allow inspection of documents, electronic information, tangible items, or land\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Requests for • Requests served on other party to admit truth of facts within admission scope of discovery\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Physical/mental • Order by court or parties' agreement for physical or mental exams examination of party if those conditions are in controversy\n\nInterrogatories are written questions served by one party on another party that may inquire about any facts, opinions, and contentions within the scope of discovery. One of the primary purposes of interrogatories is to ascertain facts and information that might lead to other evidence, and the main benefit of interrogatories is that they are simple and inexpensive. Therefore, interrogatories are the most efficient way for the construction company to identify the supplier's employees who might have information about the lumber's treatment. (Choice B) A deposition is a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court. Oral depositions are useful to question potential witnesses because their answers can be used at trial if they are inconsistent with the witnesses' trial testimony or the witnesses are unavailable to testify. But an oral deposition is time-consuming and expensive, so it is not the most efficient method to identify potential witnesses. (Choice C) A request for production is used to make another party produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information, tangible things, or land in their control. Here, the supplier may have documents that show which employees treated the lumber. But the more efficient method to obtain this information is through interrogatories since they place the burden on the responding party (eg, the supplier) to find the answer.\n\n(Choice D) A subpoena is a written court order that can be used in conjunction with other discovery methods to compel a person to appear in court, take part in a deposition, or produce documents in the person's possession. But it would not be useful here since the company is trying to identify potential witnesses. Educational objective: Interrogatories are written questions served by one party on another party that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. This simple and inexpensive method of discovery is primarily used to identify facts and information that might lead to other evidence—eg, the identity of potential witnesses. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[658],"sourceAnswerPages":[659,660],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家建筑公司从外州供应商购买木材。该木材本应经过加压防腐处理，使其能够抵抗腐烂和白蚁。建筑公司用木材建造总部，一年后木材开始出现腐烂和白蚁迹象。建筑公司在联邦地区法院起诉供应商违约。建筑公司应采用哪种证据开示方法，最高效地查明供应商中可能了解木材如何处理的雇员？","choicesZh":{"A":"书面询问。","B":"口头证言录取。","C":"文件提交请求。","D":"传票。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示方法：\n\n证言录取｜在宣誓下对当事人或证人进行书面或口头询问；每方 10 次\n书面询问｜向当事人送达书面问题；最多 25 个；书面回应在送达后 30 天内到期\n提交请求｜向当事人送达请求（或向非当事人送达传票），要求提交并允许查阅文件、电子信息、有形物或土地；数量不限；书面回应在送达后 30 天内到期\n承认请求｜向其他当事人送达请求，要求承认证据开示范围内事实的真实性；数量不限；书面回应在送达后 30 天内到期\n身体或精神检查｜争议涉及一方的身体或精神状况时，由法院命令或双方约定进行检查\n\n书面询问是一方向另一方送达的书面问题，可以询问证据开示范围内的任何事实、意见和主张。其主要目的之一是查明可能导向其他证据的事实和信息，主要优点是简单、费用低。因此，书面询问是建筑公司查明供应商中可能了解木材处理情况的雇员的最高效方式。\n\n（选项 B）证言录取是在宣誓下于法庭外对当事人或非当事人进行书面或口头询问。口头证言录取适合询问潜在证人，因为证人在审判中的证言不一致或无法出庭时，其回答可用于审判。但口头证言录取耗时、昂贵，所以不是查明潜在证人的最高效方法。\n\n（选项 C）提交请求用于要求另一方提交并允许查阅其控制的文件、电子存储信息、有形物或土地。供应商可能有文件显示哪些雇员处理了木材，但通过书面询问获取信息更高效，因为寻找答案的负担由回应方（供应商）承担。\n\n（选项 D）传票是书面法院命令，可配合其他证据开示方法，强制一人出庭、参加证言录取或提交其占有的文件。但公司目前是在查明潜在证人，所以传票无用。\n\n学习目标：书面询问是一方向另一方送达的书面问题，可以询问证据开示范围内的任何事项。这种简单、费用低的证据开示方法主要用于查明可能导向其他证据的事实和信息，例如潜在证人的身份。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33（书面询问）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-219","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":219,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"An engaged couple broke up one month before their planned wedding. Upon the breakup, the man asked the woman to reimburse him for the costs associated with the wedding and to return the engagement ring that had belonged to his great-grandmother. The woman, who had moved to another state after the breakup, refused to return the ring. The man sued the woman in a federal district court to recover the expenses and the engagement ring, which totaled over $100,000. He seeks to possess the ring pending the outcome of the litigation on the merits. Which of the following provisional remedies would most likely accomplish the man's goal?","choices":{"A":"Garnishment.","B":"Receivership.","C":"Replevin.","D":"Temporary restraining order."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Provisional remedies*\n\nPreliminary Court order commanding or prohibiting specified action while the injunction case is pending\n\nTemporary Court order commanding or prohibiting specified action for 14 days restraining order or until preliminary-injunction hearing can take place (whichever occurs first)\n\nAttachment Seizure of person's property to secure or satisfy future judgment\n\nReplevin Seizure of personal property in defendant's possession that is transferred to plaintiff's possession pending outcome of suit\n\nGarnishment Court order directing third party to hold indebted party's assets (eg, wages) until further judicial notice\n\nReceivership Appointment of neutral third party to manage & administer disputed assets/property\n\n*A federal court can issue any provisional remedy provided under the law of the state where the court is located.\n\nProvisional remedies are temporary remedies issued before trial that are designed to maintain the status quo until a final judgment is rendered. Such remedies include, but are not limited to, the following:\n• Garnishment – a court order directing a third party (eg, bank or employer) to hold onto an indebted party's assets (eg, employee's wages) until the court provides further notice (Choice A)\n\n• Receivership – a court order appointing a neutral third party (ie, receiver) to manage and administer disputed assets and/or property (often used against companies in bankruptcy proceedings) (Choice B)\n\n• Temporary restraining order – a court order commanding or prohibiting specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place— whichever occurs first\n\n• Replevin – a court order seizing property possessed by the defendant and transferring it to the plaintiff pending the outcome of the lawsuit\n\nHere, the man (plaintiff) seeks to possess his great-grandmother's ring for the duration of his suit against the woman (defendant). Since a court order of replevin would force the woman to physically turn over the ring to the man pending the outcome of the litigation— not just for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing occurs—replevin would best accomplish the man's goal (Choice D). Educational objective: Provisional remedies are temporary remedies that maintain the status quo until a final judgment is issued. Replevin is a provisional remedy that allows the court to seize property possessed by the defendant and transfer it to the plaintiff pending the outcome of the lawsuit. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 64 (provisional remedies).","rule":"Provisional remedies are temporary remedies that maintain the status quo until a final judgment is issued. Replevin is a provisional remedy that allows the court to seize property possessed by the defendant and transfer it to the plaintiff pending the outcome of the lawsuit.","irac":{"issue":"An engaged couple broke up one month before their planned wedding. Upon the breakup, the man asked the woman to reimburse him for the costs associated with the wedding and to return the engagement ring that had belonged to his great-grandmother. The woman, who had moved to another state after the breakup, refused to return the ring. The man sued the woman in a federal district court to recover the expenses and the engagement ring, which totaled over $100,000. He seeks to possess the ring pending the outcome of the litigation on the merits. Which of the following provisional remedies would most likely accomplish the man's goal?","rule":"Provisional remedies are temporary remedies that maintain the status quo until a final judgment is issued. Replevin is a provisional remedy that allows the court to seize property possessed by the defendant and transfer it to the plaintiff pending the outcome of the lawsuit.","analysis":"Provisional remedies*\n\nPreliminary Court order commanding or prohibiting specified action while the injunction case is pending\n\nTemporary Court order commanding or prohibiting specified action for 14 days restraining order or until preliminary-injunction hearing can take place (whichever occurs first)\n\nAttachment Seizure of person's property to secure or satisfy future judgment\n\nReplevin Seizure of personal property in defendant's possession that is transferred to plaintiff's possession pending outcome of suit\n\nGarnishment Court order directing third party to hold indebted party's assets (eg, wages) until further judicial notice\n\nReceivership Appointment of neutral third party to manage & administer disputed assets/property\n\n*A federal court can issue any provisional remedy provided under the law of the state where the court is located.\n\nProvisional remedies are temporary remedies issued before trial that are designed to maintain the status quo until a final judgment is rendered. Such remedies include, but are not limited to, the following:\n• Garnishment – a court order directing a third party (eg, bank or employer) to hold onto an indebted party's assets (eg, employee's wages) until the court provides further notice (Choice A)\n\n• Receivership – a court order appointing a neutral third party (ie, receiver) to manage and administer disputed assets and/or property (often used against companies in bankruptcy proceedings) (Choice B)\n\n• Temporary restraining order – a court order commanding or prohibiting specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place— whichever occurs first\n\n• Replevin – a court order seizing property possessed by the defendant and transferring it to the plaintiff pending the outcome of the lawsuit\n\nHere, the man (plaintiff) seeks to possess his great-grandmother's ring for the duration of his suit against the woman (defendant). Since a court order of replevin would force the woman to physically turn over the ring to the man pending the outcome of the litigation— not just for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing occurs—replevin would best accomplish the man's goal (Choice D). Educational objective: Provisional remedies are temporary remedies that maintain the status quo until a final judgment is issued. Replevin is a provisional remedy that allows the court to seize property possessed by the defendant and transfer it to the plaintiff pending the outcome of the lawsuit. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 64 (provisional remedies).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 64 (provisional remedies).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[661],"sourceAnswerPages":[662,663],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一对订婚男女在预定婚礼前一个月分手。分手时，男子要求女子偿还婚礼相关费用，并返还曾属于男子曾祖母的订婚戒指。女子在分手后迁往另一州，拒绝返还戒指。男子在联邦地区法院起诉女子，请求追回费用和订婚戒指，二者总价值超过 100,000 美元。他希望在案件实体结果作出前占有戒指。下列哪一临时救济最可能实现男子的目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"扣押第三人持有财产。","B":"指定接管人。","C":"返还原物。","D":"临时限制令。"},"explanationZh":"临时救济*：\n\n初步禁令｜案件待决期间命令实施或禁止特定行为的法院命令\n临时限制令｜命令实施或禁止特定行为 14 天，或持续至初步禁令听证可以举行时（以较早者为准）的法院命令\n财产扣押｜扣押一人财产，以保障或满足未来判决\n返还原物｜扣押被告占有的动产，并在诉讼结果作出前移交原告占有\n扣押第三人持有财产｜命令第三人持有债务方资产，例如工资，直至法院进一步通知\n指定接管人｜指定中立第三人管理争议资产或财产\n\n*联邦法院可以签发其所在地州法律规定的任何临时救济。\n\n临时救济是在审判前签发的临时性补救措施，旨在终局判决作出前维持现状，包括但不限于：\n• 扣押第三人持有财产——命令第三人，例如银行或雇主，持有债务方资产，例如雇员工资，直至法院进一步通知（选项 A）；\n• 指定接管人——指定中立第三人管理争议资产或财产，常用于针对破产企业的程序（选项 B）；\n• 临时限制令——命令实施或禁止特定行为 14 天，或持续至初步禁令听证可以举行时，以较早者为准；\n• 返还原物——扣押被告占有的财产，并在诉讼结果作出前移交原告。\n\n本题男子（原告）希望在针对女子（被告）的诉讼期间占有曾祖母的戒指。返还原物命令会迫使女子在诉讼结果作出前将戒指实际交给男子，而不是仅持续 14 天或至初步禁令听证，因此最能实现男子目标（选项 D）。\n\n学习目标：临时救济是在终局判决作出前维持现状的临时性救济。返还原物允许法院扣押被告占有的财产，并在诉讼结果作出前移交原告。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 64（临时救济）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-220","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":220,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A man filed a class action in federal court against an insurance company on behalf of thousands of individuals nationwide. The complaint asserted that the insurance company denied these individuals' legitimate claims pursuant to a company policy that violates federal law. The insurance company denied the allegations. After the parties held an initial planning conference, the man served the insurance company with 25 interrogatories. One of the interrogatories asked the company to list its net profits over the past five years. The interrogatories stated that they were to be answered within 30 days after service. What is the insurance company's best response to the interrogatory about its net profits?","choices":{"A":"Serve an objection on the ground that interrogatories may only be served on individuals, not companies.","B":"Serve an objection on the ground that the interrogatories exceed the number allowed without permission from the court or an agreement between the parties.","C":"Serve an answer identifying the specific documents that contain information about the company's net profits and provide the man's attorney an opportunity to inspect and copy those documents.","D":"Serve an answer identifying the specific documents that contain information about the company's net profits and provide the man's attorney an opportunity to inspect, but not copy, those documents."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Interrogatories\n\n(FRCP 33)\n\nQuestions • Written questions that inquire about facts, opinions & contentions within scope of discovery\n• Limit of 25 interrogatories unless stipulated by parties or ordered by court Responses • Must answer each interrogatory fully & separately in writing, under oath & signed by responding party\n• Objections must be specific & signed by responding party's attorney\n• Due within 30 days of service Production of • May answer by specifying business records from which answer records may be derived if: – burden of deriving answers is substantially same for interrogating & responding party and – responding party allows interrogating party to examine & copy records FRCP = Federal Rule of Procedure.\n\nInterrogatories are a method of discovery in which one party serves another party with written questions that inquire about facts, opinions, and contentions within the scope of discovery. The responding party must serve answers within 30 days after being served with the interrogatories.* The responding party may also answer an interrogatory by specifying business records from which the answer may be derived if:\n• the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for the interrogating or responding party and\n• the responding party provides the interrogating party with an opportunity to examine and copy such records. Here, one of the man's interrogatories asked the insurance company to list its net profits over the past five years, which can easily be derived from the company's business records. As a result, the company can respond by (1) serving an answer identifying the specific documents that contain this information and (2) providing the man's attorney an opportunity to inspect and copy those documents. *The parties may stipulate, or the court may order, a shorter or longer time to respond.\n\n(Choice A) Interrogatories may be served on companies that are parties to the suit. Interrogatories served on a corporation, partnership, association, or government entity must be answered by an officer or agent. (Choice B) A party may serve up to 25 interrogatories on any other party without the court's permission or an agreement between the parties (as seen here). (Choice D) The company must provide the man's attorney an opportunity to inspect and copy the documents that contain the information about the company's net profits. Educational objective: A party may respond to an interrogatory by specifying business records from which the answer may be derived if (1) the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for the interrogating or responding party and (2) the responding party provides the interrogating party with an opportunity to examine and copy such records. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","rule":"A party may respond to an interrogatory by specifying business records from which the answer may be derived if (1) the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for the interrogating or responding party and (2) the responding party provides the interrogating party with an opportunity to examine and copy such records.","irac":{"issue":"A man filed a class action in federal court against an insurance company on behalf of thousands of individuals nationwide. The complaint asserted that the insurance company denied these individuals' legitimate claims pursuant to a company policy that violates federal law. The insurance company denied the allegations. After the parties held an initial planning conference, the man served the insurance company with 25 interrogatories. One of the interrogatories asked the company to list its net profits over the past five years. The interrogatories stated that they were to be answered within 30 days after service. What is the insurance company's best response to the interrogatory about its net profits?","rule":"A party may respond to an interrogatory by specifying business records from which the answer may be derived if (1) the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for the interrogating or responding party and (2) the responding party provides the interrogating party with an opportunity to examine and copy such records.","analysis":"Interrogatories\n\n(FRCP 33)\n\nQuestions • Written questions that inquire about facts, opinions & contentions within scope of discovery\n• Limit of 25 interrogatories unless stipulated by parties or ordered by court Responses • Must answer each interrogatory fully & separately in writing, under oath & signed by responding party\n• Objections must be specific & signed by responding party's attorney\n• Due within 30 days of service Production of • May answer by specifying business records from which answer records may be derived if: – burden of deriving answers is substantially same for interrogating & responding party and – responding party allows interrogating party to examine & copy records FRCP = Federal Rule of Procedure.\n\nInterrogatories are a method of discovery in which one party serves another party with written questions that inquire about facts, opinions, and contentions within the scope of discovery. The responding party must serve answers within 30 days after being served with the interrogatories.* The responding party may also answer an interrogatory by specifying business records from which the answer may be derived if:\n• the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for the interrogating or responding party and\n• the responding party provides the interrogating party with an opportunity to examine and copy such records. Here, one of the man's interrogatories asked the insurance company to list its net profits over the past five years, which can easily be derived from the company's business records. As a result, the company can respond by (1) serving an answer identifying the specific documents that contain this information and (2) providing the man's attorney an opportunity to inspect and copy those documents. *The parties may stipulate, or the court may order, a shorter or longer time to respond.\n\n(Choice A) Interrogatories may be served on companies that are parties to the suit. Interrogatories served on a corporation, partnership, association, or government entity must be answered by an officer or agent. (Choice B) A party may serve up to 25 interrogatories on any other party without the court's permission or an agreement between the parties (as seen here). (Choice D) The company must provide the man's attorney an opportunity to inspect and copy the documents that contain the information about the company's net profits. Educational objective: A party may respond to an interrogatory by specifying business records from which the answer may be derived if (1) the burden of deriving the answer is substantially the same for the interrogating or responding party and (2) the responding party provides the interrogating party with an opportunity to examine and copy such records. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[664],"sourceAnswerPages":[665,666],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子代表全国数千人在联邦法院对一家保险公司提起集体诉讼。起诉状主张，该保险公司依据一项违反联邦法的公司政策，拒绝这些人的合法保险请求。保险公司否认指控。双方举行首次规划会议后，男子向保险公司送达 25 个书面询问。其中一个要求公司列出过去五年的净利润。书面询问说明，应在送达后 30 天内回答。保险公司对有关净利润问题的最佳回应是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"以书面询问只能向个人而不能向公司送达为由提出异议。","B":"以未经法院许可或双方约定，书面询问数量超过允许上限为由提出异议。","C":"送达一份答复，指明载有公司净利润信息的具体文件，并给予男子律师查阅和复制这些文件的机会。","D":"送达一份答复，指明载有公司净利润信息的具体文件，并给予男子律师查阅但不复制这些文件的机会。"},"explanationZh":"书面询问（FRCP 33）：\n\n问题｜询问证据开示范围内事实、意见和主张的书面问题；除非双方约定或法院命令，否则上限为 25 个\n回应｜必须以书面形式逐项完整回答并宣誓，由回应方签署；异议必须具体，并由回应方律师签署；在送达后 30 天内到期\n通过提交记录回应｜如果从商业记录中推导答案的负担对询问方与回应方大致相同，可指明可以推导答案的商业记录，但回应方必须允许询问方查阅和复制记录\n\nFRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n书面询问是一方向另一方送达的书面问题，询问证据开示范围内的事实、意见和主张。回应方必须在书面询问送达后 30 天内送达答案。* 如果符合以下条件，回应方也可以通过指明可从中推导答案的商业记录来回答：\n• 推导答案的负担对询问方与回应方大致相同；并且\n• 回应方给予询问方查阅和复制记录的机会。\n\n本题一项书面询问要求保险公司列出过去五年的净利润，该信息可以很容易地从公司商业记录中推导。因此，公司可以：(1) 送达答复，指明载有该信息的具体文件；并且 (2) 给予男子律师查阅和复制文件的机会。\n\n*双方可以约定或法院可以命令采用更短或更长的回应期限。\n\n（选项 A）可以向作为诉讼当事人的公司送达书面询问。向公司、合伙、协会或政府实体送达的书面询问，必须由其官员或代理人回答。\n\n（选项 B）一方无需法院许可或双方约定，即可向任何其他一方送达最多 25 个书面询问，本题即如此。\n\n（选项 D）公司必须给予男子律师查阅并复制载有公司净利润信息的文件的机会。\n\n学习目标：如果 (1) 推导答案的负担对询问方与回应方大致相同；且 (2) 回应方给予询问方查阅和复制记录的机会，一方可以通过指明可从中推导答案的商业记录来回应书面询问。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33（书面询问）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-221","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":221,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A politician from State A sued a newspaper located in State B in a federal court in State A for $200,000. The politician claimed that the newspaper invaded his privacy by publishing his federal income tax records. During a deposition in State B, the politician's attorney asked the reporter who wrote the article to reveal the name of the source who supplied the records. The reporter, who resides in State A, refused to answer the question to preserve an alleged privilege. At the conclusion of the deposition, the politician's attorney filed a motion in the federal court in State B to compel the reporter to answer the question. After a hearing, the court held that no privilege existed and ordered the reporter to reveal the source. The reporter refused to comply with the court order. The court held the reporter in contempt and sentenced him to jail until he reveals the source. Was the federal court's action proper?","choices":{"A":"No, because a federal court cannot sentence a person to jail for violating a court order in a civil case.","B":"No, because only a federal court in State A may order the reporter to reveal his source and impose sanctions.","C":"Yes, because a deponent must answer every question at a deposition.","D":"Yes, because the reporter failed to comply with the court's order."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Sanctions for violating discovery order\n\n(FRCP 37(b))\n\nCourt location Sanctions allowed\n\nWhere deposition • Hold in contempt of court occurred\n\nWhere suit is pending • Direct that certain facts be construed against violator\n• Prohibit violator from offering certain evidence\n• Strike pleadings in whole or in part\n• Stay further proceedings until order is obeyed\n• Hold in contempt of court*\n• Dismiss claims in whole or in part\n• Issue default judgment against violator\n• Order payment of reasonable expenses (eg, attorney's fees) caused by violation FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed for failing to comply with order for physical or mental examination.\n\nWhen a deponent fails to answer a question at a deposition, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 37 allows the party conducting the deposition to file a motion to compel a response.* When the deponent is a party, the motion must be filed in the court where the lawsuit is pending. But when the deponent is a nonparty (as seen here), the motion must be filed in the federal district where the deposition occurred. The court may then grant the motion and order the deponent to respond. If the deponent refuses to comply with the court's order, the court may impose sanctions—eg, hold the deponent in criminal or civil contempt of court. The purpose of criminal contempt is to punish the violator by imposing a fixed monetary fine or jail sentence. The purpose of civil contempt is to coerce compliance with the court order by imposing a fine or jail sentence that lasts until the violator complies (eg, reveals a source) (Choice A). And since the reporter here refused to comply with the court order, the court properly held him in civil contempt. *A motion to compel can also be filed when an opposing party fails to make a required disclosure, answer an interrogatory, or produce a document.\n\n(Choice B) Since the reporter is not a party in this suit, the federal court in State B (where the deposition occurred) may order him to reveal his source and impose sanctions. (Choice C) A deponent must answer every question at a deposition unless refusing to answer is necessary to (1) enforce a court-ordered limitation, (2) present a motion to terminate or limit the deposition, or (3) preserve a privilege. Educational objective: When a nonparty fails to answer a deposition question, the court where the deposition occurred can order the nonparty to answer the question. If the nonparty fails to obey this order, the court can sanction the nonparty by holding him/her in criminal or civil contempt of court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)–(b) (motion to compel & sanctions).","rule":"When a nonparty fails to answer a deposition question, the court where the deposition occurred can order the nonparty to answer the question. If the nonparty fails to obey this order, the court can sanction the nonparty by holding him/her in criminal or civil contempt of court.","irac":{"issue":"A politician from State A sued a newspaper located in State B in a federal court in State A for $200,000. The politician claimed that the newspaper invaded his privacy by publishing his federal income tax records. During a deposition in State B, the politician's attorney asked the reporter who wrote the article to reveal the name of the source who supplied the records. The reporter, who resides in State A, refused to answer the question to preserve an alleged privilege. At the conclusion of the deposition, the politician's attorney filed a motion in the federal court in State B to compel the reporter to answer the question. After a hearing, the court held that no privilege existed and ordered the reporter to reveal the source. The reporter refused to comply with the court order. The court held the reporter in contempt and sentenced him to jail until he reveals the source. Was the federal court's action proper?","rule":"When a nonparty fails to answer a deposition question, the court where the deposition occurred can order the nonparty to answer the question. If the nonparty fails to obey this order, the court can sanction the nonparty by holding him/her in criminal or civil contempt of court.","analysis":"Sanctions for violating discovery order\n\n(FRCP 37(b))\n\nCourt location Sanctions allowed\n\nWhere deposition • Hold in contempt of court occurred\n\nWhere suit is pending • Direct that certain facts be construed against violator\n• Prohibit violator from offering certain evidence\n• Strike pleadings in whole or in part\n• Stay further proceedings until order is obeyed\n• Hold in contempt of court*\n• Dismiss claims in whole or in part\n• Issue default judgment against violator\n• Order payment of reasonable expenses (eg, attorney's fees) caused by violation FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed for failing to comply with order for physical or mental examination.\n\nWhen a deponent fails to answer a question at a deposition, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 37 allows the party conducting the deposition to file a motion to compel a response.* When the deponent is a party, the motion must be filed in the court where the lawsuit is pending. But when the deponent is a nonparty (as seen here), the motion must be filed in the federal district where the deposition occurred. The court may then grant the motion and order the deponent to respond. If the deponent refuses to comply with the court's order, the court may impose sanctions—eg, hold the deponent in criminal or civil contempt of court. The purpose of criminal contempt is to punish the violator by imposing a fixed monetary fine or jail sentence. The purpose of civil contempt is to coerce compliance with the court order by imposing a fine or jail sentence that lasts until the violator complies (eg, reveals a source) (Choice A). And since the reporter here refused to comply with the court order, the court properly held him in civil contempt. *A motion to compel can also be filed when an opposing party fails to make a required disclosure, answer an interrogatory, or produce a document.\n\n(Choice B) Since the reporter is not a party in this suit, the federal court in State B (where the deposition occurred) may order him to reveal his source and impose sanctions. (Choice C) A deponent must answer every question at a deposition unless refusing to answer is necessary to (1) enforce a court-ordered limitation, (2) present a motion to terminate or limit the deposition, or (3) preserve a privilege. Educational objective: When a nonparty fails to answer a deposition question, the court where the deposition occurred can order the nonparty to answer the question. If the nonparty fails to obey this order, the court can sanction the nonparty by holding him/her in criminal or civil contempt of court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)–(b) (motion to compel & sanctions).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)–(b) (motion to compel & sanctions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[667],"sourceAnswerPages":[668,669],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的政治人物在 A 州联邦法院起诉一家位于 B 州的报社，请求 200,000 美元，主张报社公布其联邦所得税记录侵犯隐私。在 B 州进行证言录取期间，政治人物律师要求撰写文章的记者披露提供记录的消息来源姓名。居住于 A 州的记者以保留一项声称存在的特权为由拒绝回答。证言录取结束时，政治人物律师向 B 州联邦法院申请强制记者回答。听证后，法院认定不存在特权，命令记者披露消息来源。记者拒绝遵守法院命令。法院认定记者藐视法庭，判其入狱直至披露消息来源。联邦法院的做法是否适当？","choicesZh":{"A":"不适当，因为联邦法院不能因一人在民事案件中违反法院命令而判其入狱。","B":"不适当，因为只有 A 州联邦法院可以命令记者披露消息来源并给予制裁。","C":"适当，因为受询问人在证言录取中必须回答每个问题。","D":"适当，因为记者未遵守法院命令。"},"explanationZh":"违反证据开示命令的制裁（FRCP 37(b)）：\n\n法院所在地｜允许的制裁\n证言录取发生地｜认定藐视法庭\n诉讼待决地｜指示将某些事实作不利于违规者的解释；禁止违规者提出特定证据；全部或部分删除诉状；中止后续程序直至遵守命令；认定藐视法庭*；全部或部分驳回请求；对违规者登记缺席判决；命令支付违规造成的合理费用，例如律师费\n\n*不遵守身体或精神检查命令时，不得采用藐视法庭制裁。\n\n受询问人在证言录取中拒绝回答问题时，《联邦民事诉讼规则》37 允许进行证言录取的一方申请强制回应。* 受询问人是当事人时，动议必须向诉讼待决法院提交；受询问人是非当事人时（本题即如此），必须向证言录取发生地联邦地区法院提交。法院可以批准动议并命令回应。受询问人拒绝遵守时，法院可以给予制裁，例如认定刑事或民事藐视法庭。\n\n刑事藐视法庭旨在通过固定罚款或监禁刑罚惩罚违规者；民事藐视法庭旨在通过持续至违规者遵守命令为止的罚款或监禁，强迫其遵守，例如披露消息来源（选项 A）。本题记者拒绝遵守法院命令，所以法院适当认定其构成民事藐视法庭。\n\n*相对方未作必要披露、未回答书面询问或未提交文件时，也可以申请强制。\n\n（选项 B）记者不是诉讼当事人，所以证言录取发生地 B 州联邦法院可以命令其披露消息来源并给予制裁。\n\n（选项 C）除非拒绝回答是为：(1) 执行法院命令的限制；(2) 提出终止或限制证言录取的动议；或 (3) 保留特权，否则受询问人必须回答证言录取中的每个问题。\n\n学习目标：非当事人拒绝回答证言录取问题时，证言录取发生地法院可以命令其回答。非当事人不遵守命令时，法院可以通过认定刑事或民事藐视法庭给予制裁。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)–(b)（强制动议及制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-222","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":222,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A doctor from State B performed surgery on a man from State A and implanted a device in the man's lower spine. The device was manufactured by a corporation incorporated and with its principal place of business in State C. Following the surgery, the device fell apart inside the man's back and caused him to suffer severe injuries. The man sued the corporation in a federal district court in State B for $250,000. The corporation filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the man's suit for failing to join the doctor as a required party. The corporation claimed that the doctor improperly installed the device and, therefore, is jointly and severally liable for the man's injuries. Will the court likely grant the corporation's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the corporation must file an answer before it can make this motion.","B":"No, because the doctor is not a required party to the suit.","C":"Yes, because it is inequitable to proceed without the doctor.","D":"Yes, because joining the doctor to the suit is not feasible."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 19, an absent party must be joined (ie, added) to a suit in certain circumstances. This required-joinder rule provides a three-step process for determining whether joinder is necessary. The first step requires the court to determine whether the absent party is a required (ie, indispensable) party because:\n• complete relief cannot be granted to the existing parties without that party's presence\n\n• the party's absence will subject existing parties to a substantial risk of multiple or inconsistent obligations or\n\n• the party's absence will prejudice his/her ability to protect an interest related to the suit.\n\nPotential tortfeasors facing joint and several liability are not required parties. Joint and several liability allows a plaintiff to obtain complete relief from any one of the tortfeasors. That tortfeasor is not subject to multiple or inconsistent obligations because the tortfeasor may sue any absent tortfeasor for contribution. The absent tortfeasor can then protect its interests in the contribution suit. As a result, the doctor is not a required party in the man's suit, and the court will likely deny the corporation's motion to dismiss.* *If the corporation seeks to add the doctor because he is allegedly liable to the corporation for all or part of the man's claim, it should add the doctor to the suit through third-party practice (ie, impleader). (Choice A) FRCP 12 allows a defendant to make several motions before filing his/her answer—including a motion to dismiss for failing to join a required party (as seen here). (Choices C & D) The second step in the required-joinder rule considers whether it is feasible to join the required party, and the third step considers whether it is equitable to proceed without the required party. However, these steps need only be considered if the absent party is a required party (not seen here). Educational objective: Alleged tortfeasors facing joint and several liability are not required parties because (1) complete relief can be granted in their absence, (2) the tortfeasors in the suit will not be subjected to multiple or inconsistent obligations, and (3) the absent tortfeasors can adequately protect their interests in a future action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).\n\n• Temple v. Synthes Corp., Ltd., 498 U.S. 5, 7–8 (1990) (explaining that joint tortfeasors are not required parties).","rule":"Alleged tortfeasors facing joint and several liability are not required parties because (1) complete relief can be granted in their absence, (2) the tortfeasors in the suit will not be subjected to multiple or inconsistent obligations, and (3) the absent tortfeasors can adequately protect their interests in a future action.","irac":{"issue":"A doctor from State B performed surgery on a man from State A and implanted a device in the man's lower spine. The device was manufactured by a corporation incorporated and with its principal place of business in State C. Following the surgery, the device fell apart inside the man's back and caused him to suffer severe injuries. The man sued the corporation in a federal district court in State B for $250,000. The corporation filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the man's suit for failing to join the doctor as a required party. The corporation claimed that the doctor improperly installed the device and, therefore, is jointly and severally liable for the man's injuries. Will the court likely grant the corporation's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Alleged tortfeasors facing joint and several liability are not required parties because (1) complete relief can be granted in their absence, (2) the tortfeasors in the suit will not be subjected to multiple or inconsistent obligations, and (3) the absent tortfeasors can adequately protect their interests in a future action.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 19, an absent party must be joined (ie, added) to a suit in certain circumstances. This required-joinder rule provides a three-step process for determining whether joinder is necessary. The first step requires the court to determine whether the absent party is a required (ie, indispensable) party because:\n• complete relief cannot be granted to the existing parties without that party's presence\n\n• the party's absence will subject existing parties to a substantial risk of multiple or inconsistent obligations or\n\n• the party's absence will prejudice his/her ability to protect an interest related to the suit.\n\nPotential tortfeasors facing joint and several liability are not required parties. Joint and several liability allows a plaintiff to obtain complete relief from any one of the tortfeasors. That tortfeasor is not subject to multiple or inconsistent obligations because the tortfeasor may sue any absent tortfeasor for contribution. The absent tortfeasor can then protect its interests in the contribution suit. As a result, the doctor is not a required party in the man's suit, and the court will likely deny the corporation's motion to dismiss.* *If the corporation seeks to add the doctor because he is allegedly liable to the corporation for all or part of the man's claim, it should add the doctor to the suit through third-party practice (ie, impleader). (Choice A) FRCP 12 allows a defendant to make several motions before filing his/her answer—including a motion to dismiss for failing to join a required party (as seen here). (Choices C & D) The second step in the required-joinder rule considers whether it is feasible to join the required party, and the third step considers whether it is equitable to proceed without the required party. However, these steps need only be considered if the absent party is a required party (not seen here). Educational objective: Alleged tortfeasors facing joint and several liability are not required parties because (1) complete relief can be granted in their absence, (2) the tortfeasors in the suit will not be subjected to multiple or inconsistent obligations, and (3) the absent tortfeasors can adequately protect their interests in a future action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).\n\n• Temple v. Synthes Corp., Ltd., 498 U.S. 5, 7–8 (1990) (explaining that joint tortfeasors are not required parties).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).\n\n• Temple v. Synthes Corp., Ltd., 498 U.S. 5, 7–8 (1990) (explaining that joint tortfeasors are not required parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[670],"sourceAnswerPages":[671,672],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 B 州的医生为一名来自 A 州的男子实施手术，并在男子下段脊柱植入一个装置。该装置由一家在 C 州注册成立且主要营业地位于 C 州的公司制造。手术后，装置在男子背部体内解体，造成严重伤害。男子在 B 州联邦地区法院起诉公司，请求 250,000 美元。公司在答辩前申请以未合并医生这一必要当事人为由驳回诉讼。公司主张医生不当安装装置，所以对男子伤害承担连带责任。法院可能批准公司的驳回动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为公司提出该动议前必须先提交答辩状。","B":"不会，因为医生不是诉讼的必要当事人。","C":"会，因为没有医生参与而继续诉讼不公平。","D":"会，因为将医生合并于诉讼并不可行。"},"explanationZh":"根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）19，在某些情况下，必须将缺席当事人合并，即加入诉讼。必要合并规则通过三个步骤确定是否必须合并。第一步要求法院判断缺席方是否为必要（即不可或缺）当事人，理由包括：\n• 没有该方参与，就无法向现有各方给予完整救济；\n• 该方缺席会使现有各方面临承担多重或不一致义务的重大风险；或\n• 该方缺席会损害其保护与诉讼有关利益的能力。\n\n面临连带责任的潜在侵权人不是必要当事人。连带责任允许原告从任何一名侵权人处取得完整救济。诉讼中的侵权人不会承担多重或不一致义务，因为其可以起诉缺席侵权人要求分担；缺席侵权人可以在分担诉讼中保护自身利益。因此，医生不是男子诉讼的必要当事人，法院很可能驳回公司的驳回动议。*\n\n*如果公司希望加入医生，理由是医生可能对男子请求的全部或部分向公司承担责任，应通过第三方诉讼，即第三方引入，将医生加入。\n\n（选项 A）FRCP 12 允许被告在提交答辩状前提出多种动议，包括以未合并必要当事人为由申请驳回（本题即如此）。\n\n（选项 C、D）必要合并规则第二步考察加入必要当事人是否可行，第三步考察缺少必要当事人时继续诉讼是否公平。但只有缺席方属于必要当事人时才需考察这些步骤，本题并非如此。\n\n学习目标：面临连带责任的被指侵权人不是必要当事人，因为：(1) 没有其参与也能给予完整救济；(2) 诉讼中的侵权人不会承担多重或不一致义务；且 (3) 缺席侵权人可在以后诉讼中充分保护其利益。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19（必要当事人的合并）。\n• Temple v. Synthes Corp., Ltd., 498 U.S. 5, 7–8 (1990)（说明共同侵权人不是必要当事人）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-070"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-223","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":223,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A motorcyclist from State A, who suffered severe injuries in an automobile collision with a driver from State B, filed a complaint against the driver in federal court. In her complaint, the motorcyclist sought to recover special damages for her medical expenses by requesting that the driver pay \"my medical expenses.\" Fifteen days after being served with process, the driver filed a motion for a more definite statement. The motion alleged that the complaint failed to state with specificity the medical expenses incurred by the motorcyclist and set forth the details that needed to be included. Will the driver likely succeed on his motion for a more definite statement?","choices":{"A":"No, because claims for damages may be alleged generally.","B":"No, because the driver has yet to conduct discovery.","C":"No, because the driver must file an answer before he can make this motion.","D":"Yes, because the motorcyclist failed to specifically state her claim for special damages."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Specificity of pleadings\n\nClaim Pleading requirements\n\nGeneral rule • Complaint – short & plain statement of claim showing plaintiff is entitled to relief\n• Answer – short & plain statement of admissions, denials, defenses & claims against other parties Fraud/mistake/mental • Malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition condition may be alleged generally\n• Circumstances giving rise to fraud/mistake must be stated with particularity Conditions precedent • Assertion that conditions precedent occurred may be alleged generally\n• Denial that conditions precedent occurred must be stated with particularity Damages • General damages may be alleged generally\n• Special damages must be specifically stated A complaint is a pleading that initiates the plaintiff's lawsuit. It generally must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. However, there are special pleading requirements for certain claims, including compensatory damages. There are two main types of compensatory damages:\n• General damages – the natural and necessary results of a wrongful act that do not have a fixed value and may be alleged generally (eg, pain and suffering)\n• Special damages – the natural, but not necessary, results of a wrongful act that have a fixed value and must be specifically stated (eg, medical expenses) When a complaint includes claims for special damages that do not contain the requisite specificity, the defendant may file a motion for a more definite statement that identifies the pleading's defects and the details needed to cure them. Here, the motorcyclist's complaint sought to recover special damages for her medical expenses by requesting that the driver pay \"my medical expenses.\" But the motorcyclist failed to specifically state her medical expenses—eg, \"the accident caused me to suffer medical expenses totaling $100,000.\" Therefore, the driver will likely succeed on his motion for a more definite statement. (Choice A) Claims for general damages may be alleged generally. But the motorcyclist's claim is for special damages, which must be specifically stated (not seen here).\n\n(Choice B) Discovery is the pretrial phase of the lawsuit during which the parties are compelled to disclose information related to the case. Although the driver will likely learn more about the motorcyclist's medical expenses when discovery later occurs, this does not excuse the motorcyclist's failure to state her claim for medical expenses with the requisite specificity. (Choice C) FRCP 12 allows a defendant to make several motions before filing an answer— including a motion for a more definite statement (as seen here). Educational objective: General damages (eg, pain and suffering) do not have a fixed value and may be alleged generally in a complaint. Special damages (eg, medical expenses) do have a fixed value and must be specifically stated in the complaint. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(g) (pleading special damages).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (filing a motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 22 Am. Jur. 2d Damages §§ 42–43 (2022) (defining general and special damages).","rule":"General damages (eg, pain and suffering) do not have a fixed value and may be alleged generally in a complaint. Special damages (eg, medical expenses) do have a fixed value and must be specifically stated in the complaint. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A motorcyclist from State A, who suffered severe injuries in an automobile collision with a driver from State B, filed a complaint against the driver in federal court. In her complaint, the motorcyclist sought to recover special damages for her medical expenses by requesting that the driver pay \"my medical expenses.\" Fifteen days after being served with process, the driver filed a motion for a more definite statement. The motion alleged that the complaint failed to state with specificity the medical expenses incurred by the motorcyclist and set forth the details that needed to be included. Will the driver likely succeed on his motion for a more definite statement?","rule":"General damages (eg, pain and suffering) do not have a fixed value and may be alleged generally in a complaint. Special damages (eg, medical expenses) do have a fixed value and must be specifically stated in the complaint. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"Specificity of pleadings\n\nClaim Pleading requirements\n\nGeneral rule • Complaint – short & plain statement of claim showing plaintiff is entitled to relief\n• Answer – short & plain statement of admissions, denials, defenses & claims against other parties Fraud/mistake/mental • Malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition condition may be alleged generally\n• Circumstances giving rise to fraud/mistake must be stated with particularity Conditions precedent • Assertion that conditions precedent occurred may be alleged generally\n• Denial that conditions precedent occurred must be stated with particularity Damages • General damages may be alleged generally\n• Special damages must be specifically stated A complaint is a pleading that initiates the plaintiff's lawsuit. It generally must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. However, there are special pleading requirements for certain claims, including compensatory damages. There are two main types of compensatory damages:\n• General damages – the natural and necessary results of a wrongful act that do not have a fixed value and may be alleged generally (eg, pain and suffering)\n• Special damages – the natural, but not necessary, results of a wrongful act that have a fixed value and must be specifically stated (eg, medical expenses) When a complaint includes claims for special damages that do not contain the requisite specificity, the defendant may file a motion for a more definite statement that identifies the pleading's defects and the details needed to cure them. Here, the motorcyclist's complaint sought to recover special damages for her medical expenses by requesting that the driver pay \"my medical expenses.\" But the motorcyclist failed to specifically state her medical expenses—eg, \"the accident caused me to suffer medical expenses totaling $100,000.\" Therefore, the driver will likely succeed on his motion for a more definite statement. (Choice A) Claims for general damages may be alleged generally. But the motorcyclist's claim is for special damages, which must be specifically stated (not seen here).\n\n(Choice B) Discovery is the pretrial phase of the lawsuit during which the parties are compelled to disclose information related to the case. Although the driver will likely learn more about the motorcyclist's medical expenses when discovery later occurs, this does not excuse the motorcyclist's failure to state her claim for medical expenses with the requisite specificity. (Choice C) FRCP 12 allows a defendant to make several motions before filing an answer— including a motion for a more definite statement (as seen here). Educational objective: General damages (eg, pain and suffering) do not have a fixed value and may be alleged generally in a complaint. Special damages (eg, medical expenses) do have a fixed value and must be specifically stated in the complaint. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(g) (pleading special damages).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (filing a motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 22 Am. Jur. 2d Damages §§ 42–43 (2022) (defining general and special damages).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(g) (pleading special damages).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e) (filing a motion for a more definite statement).\n\n• 22 Am. Jur. 2d Damages §§ 42–43 (2022) (defining general and special damages).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[673],"sourceAnswerPages":[674,675],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的摩托车手与一名来自 B 州的司机发生汽车碰撞并受重伤，随后在联邦法院对司机提交起诉状。摩托车手请求司机支付“我的医疗费用”，以获得医疗费用这一特别损害赔偿。诉讼文书送达十五天后，司机申请更明确陈述，主张起诉状没有具体说明摩托车手产生的医疗费用，并列明需要加入的细节。司机的更明确陈述动议可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为损害赔偿请求可以一般性主张。","B":"不会，因为司机尚未进行证据开示。","C":"不会，因为司机提出该动议前必须先提交答辩状。","D":"会，因为摩托车手没有具体陈述其特别损害赔偿请求。"},"explanationZh":"诉状的具体程度：\n\n请求｜诉状要求\n一般规则｜起诉状——简短、明确陈述请求，表明原告有权获得救济；答辩状——简短、明确陈述承认、否认、抗辩及针对其他各方的请求\n欺诈、错误及精神状态｜恶意、意图、知情或其他精神状态可作一般性主张；引起欺诈或错误的情形必须具体陈述\n先决条件｜可一般性主张先决条件已经发生；否认先决条件发生必须具体陈述\n损害｜一般损害可以一般性主张；特别损害必须具体陈述\n\n起诉状是启动原告诉讼的诉状。通常必须包含简短、明确的请求陈述，表明原告有权获得救济。不过，某些请求适用特别诉状要求，包括补偿性损害赔偿。补偿性损害赔偿主要分为两种：\n• 一般损害——不法行为的自然且必然结果，没有固定价值，可以一般性主张，例如疼痛与痛苦；\n• 特别损害——不法行为的自然但非必然结果，具有固定价值，必须具体陈述，例如医疗费用。\n\n起诉状包含特别损害请求却没有达到必要具体程度时，被告可以提出更明确陈述动议，指出诉状缺陷及纠正所需细节。\n\n本题摩托车手请求司机支付“我的医疗费用”，以获得医疗费用这一特别损害赔偿，但没有具体陈述金额，例如“事故使我产生共计 100,000 美元医疗费用”。因此，司机的更明确陈述动议很可能成功。\n\n（选项 A）一般损害请求可以一般性主张，但摩托车手请求的是必须具体陈述的特别损害，本题并未具体陈述。\n\n（选项 B）证据开示是诉讼的审前阶段，双方在该阶段被要求披露案件相关信息。司机虽然以后会在证据开示中了解更多医疗费用信息，但这不能免除摩托车手按必要具体程度陈述医疗费用请求的义务。\n\n（选项 C）FRCP 12 允许被告在提交答辩状前提出多种动议，包括更明确陈述动议（本题即如此）。\n\n学习目标：一般损害，例如疼痛与痛苦，没有固定价值，可以在起诉状中一般性主张。特别损害，例如医疗费用，具有固定价值，必须在起诉状中具体陈述。FRCP =《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(g)（特别损害的陈述）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 12(e)（提交更明确陈述动议）。\n• 22 Am. Jur. 2d Damages §§ 42–43 (2022)（定义一般损害和特别损害）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-224","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":224,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A customer brought a federal diversity action against a corporation. During discovery, the customer requested information that was stored on hard drives at the corporation's headquarters. The corporation informed the customer that the hard drives had been wiped clean when the corporation switched over to a new information technology firm a few months before the customer filed suit. The customer moved for sanctions. After a hearing, the court found that the corporation negligently failed to preserve the information contained in the hard drives, but that the corporation did not act intentionally to prevent the customer from accessing the information. The court also found that the information cannot be obtained by any other means and that the loss of this information prejudices the customer's claim. Based on those findings, what action should the court take?","choices":{"A":"Enter a default judgment in favor of the customer.","B":"Instruct the jury at trial that it must presume that the lost information was unfavorable to the corporation.","C":"Permit the parties to present evidence at trial regarding the loss of information.","D":"Prohibit the corporation from seeking discovery in the lawsuit to put the parties at a similar disadvantage."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Sanctions may be appropriate if a party failed to preserve electronically stored information (ESI) that:\n• should have been preserved in the anticipation or conduct of litigation\n• is lost because the party failed to take reasonable steps to preserve it and\n• cannot be replaced or restored through additional discovery. If the court finds that the party intentionally deprived the requesting party of the ESI, the court may:\n• presume that the lost ESI was unfavorable to the party that failed to preserve it\n• instruct the jury that it may or must presume the lost ESI was unfavorable to that party (Choice B) or\n• dismiss the action or enter a default judgment against that party (Choice A). These sanctions are not available here because the corporation acted negligently—not intentionally. As a result, the court should only order measures that are no greater than necessary to cure the prejudice caused to the customer—eg, allow the parties to present evidence at trial regarding the loss of the ESI. (Choice D) It is unlikely that a court would prohibit a party from seeking discovery as a form of sanction as that would frustrate the purpose of discovery—ie, to uncover facts, narrow the issues in dispute, and otherwise prepare both parties for trial. Educational objective: A court may order measures no greater than necessary to cure prejudice caused by a party's failure to preserve electronically stored information that (1) should have been preserved in the anticipation or conduct of litigation, (2) is lost because the party failed to take reasonable steps to preserve it, and (3) cannot be replaced or restored. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e) (failure to preserve electronically stored information).","rule":"A court may order measures no greater than necessary to cure prejudice caused by a party's failure to preserve electronically stored information that (1) should have been preserved in the anticipation or conduct of litigation, (2) is lost because the party failed to take reasonable steps to preserve it, and (3) cannot be replaced or restored.","irac":{"issue":"A customer brought a federal diversity action against a corporation. During discovery, the customer requested information that was stored on hard drives at the corporation's headquarters. The corporation informed the customer that the hard drives had been wiped clean when the corporation switched over to a new information technology firm a few months before the customer filed suit. The customer moved for sanctions. After a hearing, the court found that the corporation negligently failed to preserve the information contained in the hard drives, but that the corporation did not act intentionally to prevent the customer from accessing the information. The court also found that the information cannot be obtained by any other means and that the loss of this information prejudices the customer's claim. Based on those findings, what action should the court take?","rule":"A court may order measures no greater than necessary to cure prejudice caused by a party's failure to preserve electronically stored information that (1) should have been preserved in the anticipation or conduct of litigation, (2) is lost because the party failed to take reasonable steps to preserve it, and (3) cannot be replaced or restored.","analysis":"Sanctions may be appropriate if a party failed to preserve electronically stored information (ESI) that:\n• should have been preserved in the anticipation or conduct of litigation\n• is lost because the party failed to take reasonable steps to preserve it and\n• cannot be replaced or restored through additional discovery. If the court finds that the party intentionally deprived the requesting party of the ESI, the court may:\n• presume that the lost ESI was unfavorable to the party that failed to preserve it\n• instruct the jury that it may or must presume the lost ESI was unfavorable to that party (Choice B) or\n• dismiss the action or enter a default judgment against that party (Choice A). These sanctions are not available here because the corporation acted negligently—not intentionally. As a result, the court should only order measures that are no greater than necessary to cure the prejudice caused to the customer—eg, allow the parties to present evidence at trial regarding the loss of the ESI. (Choice D) It is unlikely that a court would prohibit a party from seeking discovery as a form of sanction as that would frustrate the purpose of discovery—ie, to uncover facts, narrow the issues in dispute, and otherwise prepare both parties for trial. Educational objective: A court may order measures no greater than necessary to cure prejudice caused by a party's failure to preserve electronically stored information that (1) should have been preserved in the anticipation or conduct of litigation, (2) is lost because the party failed to take reasonable steps to preserve it, and (3) cannot be replaced or restored. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e) (failure to preserve electronically stored information).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e) (failure to preserve electronically stored information).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[676],"sourceAnswerPages":[677],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名顾客对一家公司提起联邦多元州籍诉讼。证据开示期间，顾客请求存储在公司总部硬盘上的信息。公司告知顾客，公司在顾客提起诉讼数月前更换新信息技术服务商时，已将硬盘全部擦除。顾客申请制裁。听证后，法院认定公司因过失未保存硬盘中的信息，但并非故意阻止顾客获取信息。法院还认定，该信息无法通过其他方式取得，信息丢失对顾客请求造成不利。根据这些认定，法院应采取什么措施？","choicesZh":{"A":"登记有利于顾客的缺席判决。","B":"在审判时指示陪审团必须推定丢失信息对公司不利。","C":"允许双方在审判时提出有关信息丢失的证据。","D":"禁止公司在诉讼中寻求证据开示，使双方处于类似不利地位。"},"explanationZh":"一方未保存符合下列条件的电子存储信息（ESI）时，可能适宜给予制裁：\n• 在预期诉讼或进行诉讼时本应保存；\n• 因该方未采取合理保存措施而丢失；并且\n• 无法通过进一步证据开示替换或恢复。\n\n如果法院认定该方故意剥夺请求方取得 ESI 的机会，可以：\n• 推定丢失 ESI 对未保存方不利；\n• 指示陪审团可以或必须推定丢失 ESI 对该方不利（选项 B）；或\n• 驳回诉讼，或对该方登记缺席判决（选项 A）。\n\n本题公司只是过失而非故意，所以不能采用这些制裁。因此，法院只能命令不超过纠正顾客所受不利之必要程度的措施，例如允许双方在审判时提出有关 ESI 丢失的证据。\n\n（选项 D）法院不太可能以禁止一方寻求证据开示作为制裁，因为这会妨碍证据开示的目的，即查明事实、缩小争议问题并帮助双方准备审判。\n\n学习目标：一方未保存符合以下条件的电子存储信息时，法院可以命令不超过纠正所致不利之必要程度的措施：(1) 在预期诉讼或进行诉讼时本应保存；(2) 因该方未采取合理保存措施而丢失；且 (3) 无法替换或恢复。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e)（未保存电子存储信息）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-071"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-225","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":225,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A consumer filed a class action in federal court on behalf of millions of users of a social media site for $100 million, claiming that the corporation that owns the site shared the users' data without their consent. The court certified the class action after determining that common questions of law and fact predominated and that the class action was the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute. Notice was sent by email to users of the social media site, informing them of their right to opt out of the class action. However, due to a technical error, a certain group of users was never sent the notice. The class eventually reached a settlement with the corporation, and the court entered an order dismissing the class action with prejudice. Two months later, the group of users who never received notice of the first class action filed an identical class action against the same corporation in federal court. The corporation moved to dismiss. How will the court likely rule on the corporation's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because email is not an appropriate means to send notice of a class action.","B":"Deny the motion, because the group had no opportunity to opt out of the first class action.","C":"Grant the motion, because notice of the first class action was appropriately provided under the circumstances.","D":"Grant the motion, because the first class action was dismissed with prejudice."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Adequate notice for class action\n\nContents Notice must clearly & concisely state in plain language:\n\n• nature of action\n• definition of class\n• claims, issues, or defenses\n• class member may appear through attorney\n• court will exclude member who opts out of class\n• time & manner for opting out and\n• binding effect of class judgment Delivery Court must provide notice by most practical means—including individual notice to reasonably identifiable members\n\nCosts Plaintiff bears cost of notifying members of class action\n\nA federal court has the power to certify (ie, authorize) the following types of class actions:\n• Prejudicial risk – separate actions would create a risk of (1) inconsistent decisions regarding the parties or (2) impairing absent class members' interests\n\n• Common question – common questions of law or fact predominate over individual questions, and a class action is the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute (as seen here)\n\n• Final equitable relief – injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class\n\nIn a \"common question\" class action, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action and sue on their own behalf.* Notice must be provided by the most practical means under the circumstances, including U.S. mail, email, or other reasonable means. A class member who receives notice but fails to opt out cannot pursue a subsequent suit involving the same claim. Here, notice of the first class action against the corporation was appropriately provided by email to users of the social media site (Choice A). However, a group of users never received this notice due to a technical error and had no opportunity to opt out of the first class action. As a result, the group may pursue a subsequent class action against the corporation involving the same claim, and the court will likely deny the corporation's motion to dismiss (Choice C).\n\n*In \"prejudicial risk\" and \"final equitable relief\" class actions, notice is not required because class members do not have a right to opt out. Instead, a court has the discretion to provide notice and an opportunity to opt out of such class actions. (Choice D) The court order dismissing the first class action with prejudice means that the members of that class action cannot sue on the same claim in the future. However, it does not prevent the group from asserting the same claim since the group was not provided with notice and had no opportunity to opt out. Educational objective: In a \"common question\" class action, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action. A class member who receives such notice but fails to opt out cannot pursue a subsequent suit involving the same claim. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2) (notice requirements for \"common question\" class action).\n\n• 7AA Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 167; 1786 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the notice requirements for a \"common question\" class action).","rule":"In a \"common question\" class action, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action. A class member who receives such notice but fails to opt out cannot pursue a subsequent suit involving the same claim.","irac":{"issue":"A consumer filed a class action in federal court on behalf of millions of users of a social media site for $100 million, claiming that the corporation that owns the site shared the users' data without their consent. The court certified the class action after determining that common questions of law and fact predominated and that the class action was the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute. Notice was sent by email to users of the social media site, informing them of their right to opt out of the class action. However, due to a technical error, a certain group of users was never sent the notice. The class eventually reached a settlement with the corporation, and the court entered an order dismissing the class action with prejudice. Two months later, the group of users who never received notice of the first class action filed an identical class action against the same corporation in federal court. The corporation moved to dismiss. How will the court likely rule on the corporation's motion?","rule":"In a \"common question\" class action, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action. A class member who receives such notice but fails to opt out cannot pursue a subsequent suit involving the same claim.","analysis":"Adequate notice for class action\n\nContents Notice must clearly & concisely state in plain language:\n\n• nature of action\n• definition of class\n• claims, issues, or defenses\n• class member may appear through attorney\n• court will exclude member who opts out of class\n• time & manner for opting out and\n• binding effect of class judgment Delivery Court must provide notice by most practical means—including individual notice to reasonably identifiable members\n\nCosts Plaintiff bears cost of notifying members of class action\n\nA federal court has the power to certify (ie, authorize) the following types of class actions:\n• Prejudicial risk – separate actions would create a risk of (1) inconsistent decisions regarding the parties or (2) impairing absent class members' interests\n\n• Common question – common questions of law or fact predominate over individual questions, and a class action is the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute (as seen here)\n\n• Final equitable relief – injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class\n\nIn a \"common question\" class action, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action and sue on their own behalf.* Notice must be provided by the most practical means under the circumstances, including U.S. mail, email, or other reasonable means. A class member who receives notice but fails to opt out cannot pursue a subsequent suit involving the same claim. Here, notice of the first class action against the corporation was appropriately provided by email to users of the social media site (Choice A). However, a group of users never received this notice due to a technical error and had no opportunity to opt out of the first class action. As a result, the group may pursue a subsequent class action against the corporation involving the same claim, and the court will likely deny the corporation's motion to dismiss (Choice C).\n\n*In \"prejudicial risk\" and \"final equitable relief\" class actions, notice is not required because class members do not have a right to opt out. Instead, a court has the discretion to provide notice and an opportunity to opt out of such class actions. (Choice D) The court order dismissing the first class action with prejudice means that the members of that class action cannot sue on the same claim in the future. However, it does not prevent the group from asserting the same claim since the group was not provided with notice and had no opportunity to opt out. Educational objective: In a \"common question\" class action, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action. A class member who receives such notice but fails to opt out cannot pursue a subsequent suit involving the same claim. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2) (notice requirements for \"common question\" class action).\n\n• 7AA Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 167; 1786 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the notice requirements for a \"common question\" class action).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2) (notice requirements for \"common question\" class action).\n\n• 7AA Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 167; 1786 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the notice requirements for a \"common question\" class action).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[679],"sourceAnswerPages":[680,681],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名消费者代表某社交媒体网站的数百万用户，在联邦法院提起请求 1 亿美元的集体诉讼，主张网站所属公司未经用户同意分享其数据。法院认定共同法律和事实问题占主导地位，且集体诉讼是公平、高效裁判争议的最佳方法，遂核准集体诉讼。法院通过电子邮件向网站用户发送通知，告知其选择退出集体诉讼的权利。但由于技术错误，某一组用户从未收到通知。集体最终与公司达成和解，法院发布有损害驳回集体诉讼的命令。两个月后，未收到第一起集体诉讼通知的该组用户，在联邦法院对同一公司提起相同集体诉讼。公司申请驳回。法院可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为电子邮件不是发送集体诉讼通知的适当方式。","B":"驳回动议，因为该组用户没有机会选择退出第一起集体诉讼。","C":"批准动议，因为根据具体情形，第一起集体诉讼的通知方式适当。","D":"批准动议，因为第一起集体诉讼被有损害驳回。"},"explanationZh":"集体诉讼的充分通知：\n\n内容｜通知必须以通俗语言清楚、简洁说明：诉讼性质；集体定义；请求、问题或抗辩；集体成员可以通过律师出庭；法院将排除选择退出的成员；退出时间和方式；集体判决的约束效力\n送达｜法院必须采用最可行方式通知，包括对能够合理识别的成员进行个别通知\n费用｜原告承担通知集体成员的费用\n\n联邦法院有权核准下列集体诉讼：\n• 不利风险型——分别诉讼会导致：(1) 针对双方作出不一致决定；或 (2) 损害缺席集体成员利益的风险；\n• 共同问题型——共同法律或事实问题比个别问题占主导地位，且集体诉讼是公平、高效裁判争议的最佳方法（本题即如此）；\n• 终局衡平救济型——相对方行为普遍适用于整个集体，适宜给予禁令或确认性救济。\n\n“共同问题型”集体诉讼必须向所有集体成员提供充分通知，因为他们有权退出并以自己名义起诉。* 通知必须采用具体情形下最可行的方式，包括美国邮件、电子邮件或其他合理方式。收到通知但没有退出的成员，不得以后就相同请求提起诉讼。\n\n本题通过电子邮件通知社交媒体网站用户，方式适当（选项 A）。但是，一组用户因技术错误从未收到通知，也没有机会退出第一起集体诉讼。因此，该组可以就相同请求对公司提起后续集体诉讼，法院很可能驳回公司的驳回动议（选项 C）。\n\n*“不利风险型”和“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼无需通知，因为成员无权退出。但法院可以酌情提供通知及退出机会。\n\n（选项 D）有损害驳回第一起集体诉讼的命令意味着该集体成员以后不能就相同请求起诉。但是，该组用户未得到通知、没有机会退出，所以该命令不妨碍其提出相同请求。\n\n学习目标：“共同问题型”集体诉讼必须向所有成员提供充分通知，因为他们有权退出。收到通知但未退出的成员不能以后就相同请求提起诉讼。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2)（“共同问题型”集体诉讼的通知要求）。\n• 7AA Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 167; 1786 (3d ed. 2020)（说明“共同问题型”集体诉讼的通知要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-226","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":226,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A software developer left her company to open her own software firm. The developer and her new employees immediately began contacting the company's clients in violation of her noncompete agreement with the company. The company filed a diversity action in federal court against the developer. The complaint requested damages and preliminary and permanent injunctions. After a hearing at which both parties were present, the court granted a preliminary injunction that prohibited \"[the developer] and her employees from contacting any of [the company's] clients pending the outcome of the case.\" The court also explained why the injunction was issued. The developer and her employees received copies of the preliminary injunction the following day. Are the employees bound by the preliminary injunction?","choices":{"A":"No, because the employees are not parties to the lawsuit.","B":"No, because the preliminary injunction violates due process.","C":"Yes, because the employees received actual notice of the preliminary injunction.","D":"Yes, because the preliminary injunction specifically mentioned the employees."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Preliminary injunction\n\nGrounds* Movant establishes that:\n\n• movant is likely to succeed on merits\n• movant is likely to suffer irreparable harm in absence of relief\n• balance of equities favors movant and\n• injunction is in public's best interests Contents Order granting injunction must:\n\n• state reasons for issuance\n• reasonably describe prohibited or commanded acts and\n• state terms specifically Effect Binds the following persons who receive actual notice of order:\n\n• parties\n• parties' officers, agents, employees & attorneys and\n• anyone in active concert or participation with above-listed persons *Movant also must provide bond to pay costs & damages if nonmovant was wrongfully enjoined.\n\nA preliminary injunction is a temporary court order that commands or prohibits certain actions while the case is pending before the court. It binds any of the following persons who receive actual notice of it:\n• The parties to the case\n• Those parties' officers, agents, employees, and attorneys\n• Any person who is in active concert or participation with the aforementioned persons Since the developer's employees received copies of the preliminary injunction, they are bound by it (Choice A). (Choice B) Due process protects persons against unfair deprivation of life, liberty, or property. Only public employees who are terminable for cause (as opposed to at-will employees) have a property interest in their jobs. And even if the employees here had such a property interest, the injunction's prohibition against contacting the company's clients would not deprive them of that interest. (Choice D) The employees are not bound merely because they are mentioned in the preliminary injunction. Instead, they are bound because they received actual notice of it.\n\nEducational objective: A preliminary injunction binds the following persons who receive actual notice of it: (1) the parties, (2) their officers, agents, employees, and attorneys, and (3) any person in active concert or participation with the aforementioned persons. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d) (persons bound by preliminary injunction).","rule":"A preliminary injunction binds the following persons who receive actual notice of it: (1) the parties, (2) their officers, agents, employees, and attorneys, and (3) any person in active concert or participation with the aforementioned persons.","irac":{"issue":"A software developer left her company to open her own software firm. The developer and her new employees immediately began contacting the company's clients in violation of her noncompete agreement with the company. The company filed a diversity action in federal court against the developer. The complaint requested damages and preliminary and permanent injunctions. After a hearing at which both parties were present, the court granted a preliminary injunction that prohibited \"[the developer] and her employees from contacting any of [the company's] clients pending the outcome of the case.\" The court also explained why the injunction was issued. The developer and her employees received copies of the preliminary injunction the following day. Are the employees bound by the preliminary injunction?","rule":"A preliminary injunction binds the following persons who receive actual notice of it: (1) the parties, (2) their officers, agents, employees, and attorneys, and (3) any person in active concert or participation with the aforementioned persons.","analysis":"Preliminary injunction\n\nGrounds* Movant establishes that:\n\n• movant is likely to succeed on merits\n• movant is likely to suffer irreparable harm in absence of relief\n• balance of equities favors movant and\n• injunction is in public's best interests Contents Order granting injunction must:\n\n• state reasons for issuance\n• reasonably describe prohibited or commanded acts and\n• state terms specifically Effect Binds the following persons who receive actual notice of order:\n\n• parties\n• parties' officers, agents, employees & attorneys and\n• anyone in active concert or participation with above-listed persons *Movant also must provide bond to pay costs & damages if nonmovant was wrongfully enjoined.\n\nA preliminary injunction is a temporary court order that commands or prohibits certain actions while the case is pending before the court. It binds any of the following persons who receive actual notice of it:\n• The parties to the case\n• Those parties' officers, agents, employees, and attorneys\n• Any person who is in active concert or participation with the aforementioned persons Since the developer's employees received copies of the preliminary injunction, they are bound by it (Choice A). (Choice B) Due process protects persons against unfair deprivation of life, liberty, or property. Only public employees who are terminable for cause (as opposed to at-will employees) have a property interest in their jobs. And even if the employees here had such a property interest, the injunction's prohibition against contacting the company's clients would not deprive them of that interest. (Choice D) The employees are not bound merely because they are mentioned in the preliminary injunction. Instead, they are bound because they received actual notice of it.\n\nEducational objective: A preliminary injunction binds the following persons who receive actual notice of it: (1) the parties, (2) their officers, agents, employees, and attorneys, and (3) any person in active concert or participation with the aforementioned persons. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d) (persons bound by preliminary injunction).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d) (persons bound by preliminary injunction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[682],"sourceAnswerPages":[683,684],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名软件开发人员离开原公司，开办自己的软件企业。开发人员及其新雇员立即开始联系原公司客户，违反了她与原公司的竞业禁止协议。原公司在联邦法院对开发人员提起多元州籍诉讼，起诉状请求损害赔偿、初步禁令和永久禁令。在双方均出席的听证后，法院颁发初步禁令，禁止“[开发人员]及其雇员在案件结果作出前联系[原公司]任何客户”。法院还说明颁发禁令的理由。次日，开发人员及其雇员收到初步禁令副本。雇员是否受初步禁令约束？","choicesZh":{"A":"不受约束，因为雇员不是诉讼当事人。","B":"不受约束，因为初步禁令违反正当程序。","C":"受约束，因为雇员实际收到了初步禁令通知。","D":"受约束，因为初步禁令特别提到了雇员。"},"explanationZh":"初步禁令：\n\n依据*｜动议方证明：很可能在实体上胜诉；若无救济很可能遭受无法弥补的损害；衡平利益权衡有利于动议方；禁令符合公共最佳利益\n内容｜颁发禁令的命令必须：说明颁发理由；合理说明禁止或命令的行为；具体陈述条款\n效力｜约束实际收到命令通知的下列人员：双方；双方官员、代理人、雇员及律师；与上述人员积极协同行动的任何人\n\n*动议方还必须提供保证金，以在非动议方被错误禁制时支付费用和损害赔偿。\n\n初步禁令是在案件待决期间命令实施或禁止特定行为的临时法院命令。它约束实际收到通知的下列人员：\n• 案件双方；\n• 双方的官员、代理人、雇员及律师；\n• 与上述人员积极协同或共同参与的任何人。\n\n开发人员的雇员收到了初步禁令副本，所以受其约束（选项 A）。\n\n（选项 B）正当程序保护个人免遭对生命、自由或财产的不公平剥夺。只有仅可基于正当理由解雇的公职雇员，而非任意雇佣雇员，对其职位拥有财产利益。即使本题雇员拥有这种财产利益，禁令禁止联系原公司客户也不会剥夺该利益。\n\n（选项 D）雇员并非仅因初步禁令提及他们而受约束，而是因为实际收到了禁令通知。\n\n学习目标：初步禁令约束实际收到通知的下列人员：(1) 双方；(2) 双方官员、代理人、雇员及律师；以及 (3) 与上述人员积极协同或共同参与的任何人。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d)（受初步禁令约束的人员）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-227","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":227,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"Hundreds of same-sex couples domiciled across the country initiated a class action in a federal district court against a health insurance company. The suit alleged that the insurance company violated a federal statute by charging the couples higher insurance premiums solely because they were in a same-sex relationship. The suit sought an injunction to stop the insurance company's discriminatory premiums as well as individualized monetary damages of $5,000 to $20,000 for each couple. The district court entered an order certifying the suit as a \"final equitable relief\" class action after it determined that injunctive relief was appropriate because the insurance company's actions applied generally to the whole class. The insurance company immediately appealed the certification order. Is the federal appellate court likely to overturn the certification order?","choices":{"A":"No, because subject-matter jurisdiction exists over the class action.","B":"No, because the district court has not entered a final judgment.","C":"Yes, because the amount-in-controversy requirement was not satisfied.","D":"Yes, because the class action seeks individualized monetary damages."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A federal appellate court applies the abuse of discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary certification order authorizing a class action to proceed. Class action certification is proper if the case satisfies four prerequisites (shown above) and is one of the following types of class actions:\n• Prejudicial risk – when separate actions would create a risk of (1) inconsistent decisions regarding the parties or (2) impairing absent class members' interests\n\n• Common question – when common questions of law or fact predominate over individual questions and a class action is the best method to resolve the dispute\n\n• Final equitable relief – when injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class\n\nA certified \"final equitable relief\" class action is valid if the suit also seeks incidental monetary damages. Such damages flow from liability to the class and do not require individualized determinations (eg, statutorily mandated damages). In contrast, a certified \"final equitable relief\" class action is invalid if the suit also seeks individualized monetary relief (as seen here).* Therefore, the district court abused its discretion in certifying the couples' suit as a \"final equitable relief\" class action, and the certification order will likely be overturned. *A certified \"final equitable relief\" class action that seeks individualized monetary relief may violate due process because, unlike in \"common question\" class actions, class members are not entitled to notice or an opportunity to opt out and pursue individual suits. (Choices A & C) Subject-matter jurisdiction exists through federal-question jurisdiction, which has no amount-in-controversy requirement, because this class action arises under a federal statute. However, this action was improperly certified since it contains individualized monetary claims. (Choice B) The final-judgment rule bars a federal appellate court from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment—absent limited exceptions. One such exception is an appeal of a district court's order granting or denying class action certification (as seen here). Educational objective: A suit may be certified as a \"final equitable relief\" class action when injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class. Certification of such a class action is appropriate even if it also seeks incidental monetary damages—but not individualized monetary relief. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a)–(b) (class action certification requirements).\n\n• Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes, 564 U.S. 338, 360–62 (2011) (explaining that certification of a \"final equitable relief\" class action is invalid when the class members seek individualized monetary relief).","rule":"A suit may be certified as a \"final equitable relief\" class action when injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class. Certification of such a class action is appropriate even if it also seeks incidental monetary damages—but not individualized monetary relief.","irac":{"issue":"Hundreds of same-sex couples domiciled across the country initiated a class action in a federal district court against a health insurance company. The suit alleged that the insurance company violated a federal statute by charging the couples higher insurance premiums solely because they were in a same-sex relationship. The suit sought an injunction to stop the insurance company's discriminatory premiums as well as individualized monetary damages of $5,000 to $20,000 for each couple. The district court entered an order certifying the suit as a \"final equitable relief\" class action after it determined that injunctive relief was appropriate because the insurance company's actions applied generally to the whole class. The insurance company immediately appealed the certification order. Is the federal appellate court likely to overturn the certification order?","rule":"A suit may be certified as a \"final equitable relief\" class action when injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class. Certification of such a class action is appropriate even if it also seeks incidental monetary damages—but not individualized monetary relief.","analysis":"A federal appellate court applies the abuse of discretion standard when reviewing a district court's discretionary certification order authorizing a class action to proceed. Class action certification is proper if the case satisfies four prerequisites (shown above) and is one of the following types of class actions:\n• Prejudicial risk – when separate actions would create a risk of (1) inconsistent decisions regarding the parties or (2) impairing absent class members' interests\n\n• Common question – when common questions of law or fact predominate over individual questions and a class action is the best method to resolve the dispute\n\n• Final equitable relief – when injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class\n\nA certified \"final equitable relief\" class action is valid if the suit also seeks incidental monetary damages. Such damages flow from liability to the class and do not require individualized determinations (eg, statutorily mandated damages). In contrast, a certified \"final equitable relief\" class action is invalid if the suit also seeks individualized monetary relief (as seen here).* Therefore, the district court abused its discretion in certifying the couples' suit as a \"final equitable relief\" class action, and the certification order will likely be overturned. *A certified \"final equitable relief\" class action that seeks individualized monetary relief may violate due process because, unlike in \"common question\" class actions, class members are not entitled to notice or an opportunity to opt out and pursue individual suits. (Choices A & C) Subject-matter jurisdiction exists through federal-question jurisdiction, which has no amount-in-controversy requirement, because this class action arises under a federal statute. However, this action was improperly certified since it contains individualized monetary claims. (Choice B) The final-judgment rule bars a federal appellate court from hearing an appeal until the district court has entered a final judgment—absent limited exceptions. One such exception is an appeal of a district court's order granting or denying class action certification (as seen here). Educational objective: A suit may be certified as a \"final equitable relief\" class action when injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because the opposing party's actions generally apply to the whole class. Certification of such a class action is appropriate even if it also seeks incidental monetary damages—but not individualized monetary relief. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a)–(b) (class action certification requirements).\n\n• Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes, 564 U.S. 338, 360–62 (2011) (explaining that certification of a \"final equitable relief\" class action is invalid when the class members seek individualized monetary relief).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a)–(b) (class action certification requirements).\n\n• Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes, 564 U.S. 338, 360–62 (2011) (explaining that certification of a \"final equitable relief\" class action is invalid when the class members seek individualized monetary relief).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[685],"sourceAnswerPages":[686,687],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"居住于全国各地的数百对同性伴侣，在联邦地区法院对一家健康保险公司提起集体诉讼。诉讼主张，该保险公司仅因他们处于同性关系，就收取更高保险费，违反联邦制定法。诉讼请求禁令，制止保险公司的歧视性保费，并为每对伴侣请求 5,000 至 20,000 美元的个别金钱损害赔偿。地区法院认定保险公司行为普遍适用于整个集体、适宜给予禁令救济，遂发布命令，将诉讼核准为“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼。保险公司立即对核准命令提起上诉。联邦上诉法院可能推翻该命令吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法院对集体诉讼拥有事项管辖权。","B":"不会，因为地区法院尚未登记终局判决。","C":"会，因为未满足争议金额要求。","D":"会，因为集体诉讼请求个别化金钱损害赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"联邦上诉法院审查地区法院准许集体诉讼进行的裁量性核准命令时，适用滥用裁量权标准。案件符合四项先决条件并属于下列类型之一时，集体诉讼核准适当：\n• 不利风险型——分别诉讼会导致：(1) 针对双方作出不一致决定；或 (2) 损害缺席集体成员利益的风险；\n• 共同问题型——共同法律或事实问题比个别问题占主导地位，且集体诉讼是解决争议的最佳方法；\n• 终局衡平救济型——相对方行为普遍适用于整个集体，适宜给予禁令或确认性救济。\n\n已经核准的“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼也请求附带金钱损害赔偿时仍然有效。这类损害赔偿由对集体的责任自然产生，无需个别裁定，例如制定法规定的损害赔偿。相反，如果诉讼还请求个别化金钱救济（本题即如此），核准为“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼无效。* 因此，地区法院核准伴侣诉讼时滥用了裁量权，核准命令很可能被推翻。\n\n*请求个别化金钱救济的“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼可能违反正当程序，因为与“共同问题型”不同，该类型成员无权获得通知或退出后自行起诉。\n\n（选项 A、C）该集体诉讼源于联邦制定法，所以联邦问题管辖权存在，且没有争议金额要求。但是，该诉讼包含个别化金钱请求，因而核准不当。\n\n（选项 B）终局判决规则禁止联邦上诉法院在地区法院登记终局判决前审理上诉，但有限例外除外。其中一项例外就是对地区法院批准或驳回集体诉讼核准的命令提起上诉，本题即如此。\n\n学习目标：相对方行为普遍适用于整个集体、适宜给予禁令或确认性救济时，可以核准“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼。即使同时请求附带金钱损害赔偿，核准仍适当；但请求个别化金钱救济时不适当。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a)–(b)（集体诉讼核准要求）。\n• Wal-Mart Stores, Inc. v. Dukes, 564 U.S. 338, 360–62 (2011)（说明集体成员请求个别化金钱救济时，“终局衡平救济型”集体诉讼核准无效）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-072"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-228","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":228,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An entrepreneur from State A sued a company incorporated and headquartered in State B in federal court in State B to recover $100,000 in damages. The entrepreneur alleged that the company breached their contract by disclosing confidential information supplied by the entrepreneur's consultant, a U.S. citizen who resides in a foreign country. The company denies receiving this information from the consultant. The entrepreneur asked the consultant to voluntarily attend a deposition or submit an affidavit, but he refused. The entrepreneur then moved for, and the court issued a subpoena requiring, the consultant's appearance at a deposition in State B. The consultant was served with the subpoena by mail in accordance with an international agreement signed by the foreign country. The subpoena contained the contents required by federal rules and stated that the consultant must pay his own expenses to attend the deposition. What is the consultant's best argument that he does not need to comply with the subpoena?","choices":{"A":"The consultant's testimony is unnecessary in the interest of justice.","B":"The court lacks authority to issue a subpoena for a person in a foreign country.","C":"The entrepreneur failed to pay the consultant's travel and attendance expenses.","D":"The entrepreneur used an improper method to serve the subpoena."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A subpoena is a written court order that generally commands a person to testify at a trial, hearing, or deposition. Federal courts have the authority to subpoena U.S. citizens in a foreign country* (eg, the consultant) pursuant to special rules governing the issuance and service of such subpoenas (Choice B). Such a subpoena is validly issued when it:\n• contains the required contents (as seen here)\n• is issued from the court where the action is pending (as seen here) and\n• seeks testimony that is (1) necessary in the interest of justice and (2) impractical to obtain in an admissible form without the witness's personal appearance. Here, the consultant's testimony about the disclosure of confidential information is necessary in the interest of justice because it is the basis for the entrepreneur's breach-of-contract claim (Choice A). And it is impractical to obtain this testimony without the consultant's appearance because he refused to voluntarily attend a deposition or submit an affidavit. As a result, the subpoena was validly issued. The entrepreneur then had to validly serve the subpoena on the consultant by:\n• using a method of service that the federal rules allow for service of process in a foreign country (eg, pursuant to an international agreement) and\n• paying the consultant's travel and attendance expenses. Here, the consultant was served with the subpoena by mail. This is a proper method of service since an international agreement signed by the foreign country permits such service (Choice D). However, the subpoena was invalidly served because the entrepreneur failed to pay the consultant's travel and attendance expenses. As a result, this is the consultant's best argument that he does not need to comply with the subpoena. *A federal court may not issue subpoenas to foreign citizens in foreign countries since they owe no allegiance to the U.S. Educational objective: Federal courts can subpoena U.S. citizens in a foreign country when their testimony is necessary in the interest of justice and impractical to obtain by other means. Such subpoenas are validly served when the serving party (1) uses a method of service allowed by the federal rules and (2) pays the citizen's attendance and travel expenses. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1783 (subpoena of person in foreign country).\n\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2462 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements to issue and serve subpoenas to U.S. citizens in foreign countries).","rule":"Federal courts can subpoena U.S. citizens in a foreign country when their testimony is necessary in the interest of justice and impractical to obtain by other means. Such subpoenas are validly served when the serving party (1) uses a method of service allowed by the federal rules and (2) pays the citizen's attendance and travel expenses.","irac":{"issue":"An entrepreneur from State A sued a company incorporated and headquartered in State B in federal court in State B to recover $100,000 in damages. The entrepreneur alleged that the company breached their contract by disclosing confidential information supplied by the entrepreneur's consultant, a U.S. citizen who resides in a foreign country. The company denies receiving this information from the consultant. The entrepreneur asked the consultant to voluntarily attend a deposition or submit an affidavit, but he refused. The entrepreneur then moved for, and the court issued a subpoena requiring, the consultant's appearance at a deposition in State B. The consultant was served with the subpoena by mail in accordance with an international agreement signed by the foreign country. The subpoena contained the contents required by federal rules and stated that the consultant must pay his own expenses to attend the deposition. What is the consultant's best argument that he does not need to comply with the subpoena?","rule":"Federal courts can subpoena U.S. citizens in a foreign country when their testimony is necessary in the interest of justice and impractical to obtain by other means. Such subpoenas are validly served when the serving party (1) uses a method of service allowed by the federal rules and (2) pays the citizen's attendance and travel expenses.","analysis":"A subpoena is a written court order that generally commands a person to testify at a trial, hearing, or deposition. Federal courts have the authority to subpoena U.S. citizens in a foreign country* (eg, the consultant) pursuant to special rules governing the issuance and service of such subpoenas (Choice B). Such a subpoena is validly issued when it:\n• contains the required contents (as seen here)\n• is issued from the court where the action is pending (as seen here) and\n• seeks testimony that is (1) necessary in the interest of justice and (2) impractical to obtain in an admissible form without the witness's personal appearance. Here, the consultant's testimony about the disclosure of confidential information is necessary in the interest of justice because it is the basis for the entrepreneur's breach-of-contract claim (Choice A). And it is impractical to obtain this testimony without the consultant's appearance because he refused to voluntarily attend a deposition or submit an affidavit. As a result, the subpoena was validly issued. The entrepreneur then had to validly serve the subpoena on the consultant by:\n• using a method of service that the federal rules allow for service of process in a foreign country (eg, pursuant to an international agreement) and\n• paying the consultant's travel and attendance expenses. Here, the consultant was served with the subpoena by mail. This is a proper method of service since an international agreement signed by the foreign country permits such service (Choice D). However, the subpoena was invalidly served because the entrepreneur failed to pay the consultant's travel and attendance expenses. As a result, this is the consultant's best argument that he does not need to comply with the subpoena. *A federal court may not issue subpoenas to foreign citizens in foreign countries since they owe no allegiance to the U.S. Educational objective: Federal courts can subpoena U.S. citizens in a foreign country when their testimony is necessary in the interest of justice and impractical to obtain by other means. Such subpoenas are validly served when the serving party (1) uses a method of service allowed by the federal rules and (2) pays the citizen's attendance and travel expenses. References\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1783 (subpoena of person in foreign country).\n\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2462 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements to issue and serve subpoenas to U.S. citizens in foreign countries).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. § 1783 (subpoena of person in foreign country).\n\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2462 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements to issue and serve subpoenas to U.S. citizens in foreign countries).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[688],"sourceAnswerPages":[689,690],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自 A 州的企业家在 B 州联邦法院起诉一家在 B 州注册成立且总部位于 B 州的公司，请求 100,000 美元损害赔偿。企业家主张，公司披露了其顾问提供的机密信息，构成违约；该顾问是居住在外国的美国公民。公司否认从顾问处收到该信息。企业家请求顾问自愿参加证言录取或提交宣誓书，但遭到拒绝。企业家随后提出申请，法院签发传票，要求顾问在 B 州参加证言录取。依据该外国签署的国际协议，传票通过邮件向顾问送达。传票包含联邦规则要求的内容，并说明顾问必须自行支付参加证言录取的费用。顾问关于无需遵守传票的最佳论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"为实现司法利益，无需该顾问的证言。","B":"法院无权向身处外国的人签发传票。","C":"企业家没有支付顾问的旅行和出席费用。","D":"企业家采用了不当的传票送达方式。"},"explanationZh":"传票是通常命令一人在审判、听证或证言录取中作证的书面法院命令。根据规范这类传票签发和送达的特别规则，联邦法院有权传唤身处外国的美国公民*，例如该顾问（选项 B）。符合以下条件时，传票有效签发：\n• 包含必要内容（本题即如此）；\n• 由诉讼待决法院签发（本题即如此）；并且\n• 所寻求证言：(1) 为实现司法利益所必要；且 (2) 如果证人不亲自出席，就无法切实以可采形式取得。\n\n本题顾问关于机密信息披露的证言是企业家违约请求的依据，为实现司法利益所必要（选项 A）。顾问拒绝自愿参加证言录取或提交宣誓书，所以在其不出席时无法切实取得该证言。因此，传票有效签发。\n\n随后，企业家必须通过以下方式向顾问有效送达：\n• 使用联邦规则允许在外国送达诉讼文书的方法，例如依据国际协议；并且\n• 支付顾问的旅行和出席费用。\n\n本题传票通过邮件送达。该外国签署的国际协议允许这种方式，所以送达方法适当（选项 D）。但是，企业家没有支付顾问的旅行和出席费用，所以传票送达无效。这是顾问关于无需遵守传票的最佳论点。\n\n*联邦法院不能向身处外国的外国公民签发传票，因为其不对美国负有效忠义务。\n\n学习目标：证言为实现司法利益所必要且无法通过其他方式切实取得时，联邦法院可以传唤身处外国的美国公民。这类传票的有效送达要求：(1) 采用联邦规则允许的送达方法；并且 (2) 支付该公民的出席和旅行费用。\n\n参考资料：\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1783（传唤身处外国的人）。\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2462 (3d ed. 2020)（说明向身处外国的美国公民签发和送达传票的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-073"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-229","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":229,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in federal court. During discovery, the plaintiff orally deposed the defendant. Upon completion of that deposition, the plaintiff sought to depose the defendant again, which would be the tenth deposition taken by the plaintiff in the case. The plaintiff served the defendant with written notice stating the time and place of the deposition. Upon receipt of the notice, the defendant's attorney called the plaintiff's attorney to tell him that the defendant would not appear at the deposition. The plaintiff's attorney demanded that the defendant appear as noticed. Neither the defendant nor the defendant's attorney appeared at the deposition. The plaintiff's attorney has moved for sanctions. Is the court likely to impose sanctions?","choices":{"A":"No, because the defendant had already been deposed in the action.","B":"No, because the plaintiff failed to serve a subpoena to compel the defendant's attendance.","C":"Yes, because the defendant received written notice of the deposition.","D":"Yes, because the plaintiff is entitled to take up to 10 depositions."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Leave of court for oral or written deposition\n\nCourt's leave* or parties' stipulation required to conduct deposition when:\n\n• deposition exceeds 10-deposition limit\n• deposition sought before parties' initial planning conference or\n• deponent already deposed in action *Leave of court is always required when deponent is in prison.\n\nAn oral deposition is a method of discovery in which the deposing party conducts an oral examination of another party or nonparty (deponent) under oath and outside of court. To compel the attendance and testimony of a party deponent, the deposing party must provide the party deponent with written notice. If the party deponent fails to attend, the federal court may impose sanctions. Notice may be provided and the deposition conducted without the court's leave (ie, permission) or the parties' stipulation unless:\n• the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition limit (which includes both oral and written depositions)\n• the deposition is sought before the parties' initial planning conference* or\n• the deponent was already deposed in the action. Here, the plaintiff sought to conduct an oral deposition of the defendant after the defendant had already been deposed in the action. Since the plaintiff failed to obtain the court's leave and the parties did not stipulate to the second deposition, the plaintiff's written notice to the defendant was improper (Choice C). As a result, the defendant was not required to attend the deposition, and the court is unlikely to impose sanctions. *Leave of court or the parties' stipulation is not required for an oral deposition conducted before the initial planning conference if the deposing party certifies (and provides supporting facts) in the notice that the deponent is expected to leave the U.S and be unavailable for examination. (Choice B) A subpoena is required to compel a nonparty deponent's attendance at an oral deposition. But a party deponent can be compelled to attend through written notice. (Choice D) Although the plaintiff was entitled to take up to 10 depositions, leave of court or a stipulation between the parties was required before the plaintiff could depose the defendant a second time. Educational objective:\n\nA party may provide notice of and conduct an oral deposition without the court's leave or the parties' stipulation unless (1) the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition limit, (2) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, or (3) the deponent was already deposed in the case. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(a) (when court's leave or parties' stipulation required for oral deposition).","rule":"A party may provide notice of and conduct an oral deposition without the court's leave or the parties' stipulation unless (1) the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition limit, (2) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, or (3) the deponent was already deposed in the case.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff brought a diversity action against a defendant in federal court. During discovery, the plaintiff orally deposed the defendant. Upon completion of that deposition, the plaintiff sought to depose the defendant again, which would be the tenth deposition taken by the plaintiff in the case. The plaintiff served the defendant with written notice stating the time and place of the deposition. Upon receipt of the notice, the defendant's attorney called the plaintiff's attorney to tell him that the defendant would not appear at the deposition. The plaintiff's attorney demanded that the defendant appear as noticed. Neither the defendant nor the defendant's attorney appeared at the deposition. The plaintiff's attorney has moved for sanctions. Is the court likely to impose sanctions?","rule":"A party may provide notice of and conduct an oral deposition without the court's leave or the parties' stipulation unless (1) the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition limit, (2) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, or (3) the deponent was already deposed in the case.","analysis":"Leave of court for oral or written deposition\n\nCourt's leave* or parties' stipulation required to conduct deposition when:\n\n• deposition exceeds 10-deposition limit\n• deposition sought before parties' initial planning conference or\n• deponent already deposed in action *Leave of court is always required when deponent is in prison.\n\nAn oral deposition is a method of discovery in which the deposing party conducts an oral examination of another party or nonparty (deponent) under oath and outside of court. To compel the attendance and testimony of a party deponent, the deposing party must provide the party deponent with written notice. If the party deponent fails to attend, the federal court may impose sanctions. Notice may be provided and the deposition conducted without the court's leave (ie, permission) or the parties' stipulation unless:\n• the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition limit (which includes both oral and written depositions)\n• the deposition is sought before the parties' initial planning conference* or\n• the deponent was already deposed in the action. Here, the plaintiff sought to conduct an oral deposition of the defendant after the defendant had already been deposed in the action. Since the plaintiff failed to obtain the court's leave and the parties did not stipulate to the second deposition, the plaintiff's written notice to the defendant was improper (Choice C). As a result, the defendant was not required to attend the deposition, and the court is unlikely to impose sanctions. *Leave of court or the parties' stipulation is not required for an oral deposition conducted before the initial planning conference if the deposing party certifies (and provides supporting facts) in the notice that the deponent is expected to leave the U.S and be unavailable for examination. (Choice B) A subpoena is required to compel a nonparty deponent's attendance at an oral deposition. But a party deponent can be compelled to attend through written notice. (Choice D) Although the plaintiff was entitled to take up to 10 depositions, leave of court or a stipulation between the parties was required before the plaintiff could depose the defendant a second time. Educational objective:\n\nA party may provide notice of and conduct an oral deposition without the court's leave or the parties' stipulation unless (1) the deposition exceeds the 10-deposition limit, (2) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, or (3) the deponent was already deposed in the case. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(a) (when court's leave or parties' stipulation required for oral deposition).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(a) (when court's leave or parties' stipulation required for oral deposition).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[691],"sourceAnswerPages":[692,693],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦法院对一名被告提起多元州籍诉讼。证据开示期间，原告对被告进行口头证言录取。完成后，原告希望再次对被告录取证言；这将是原告在案件中进行的第十次证言录取。原告向被告送达书面通知，说明证言录取时间和地点。被告律师收到通知后致电原告律师，告知被告不会出席。原告律师要求被告按通知出席，但被告及其律师均未出席。原告律师申请制裁。法院可能给予制裁吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为被告在本诉中已经接受过证言录取。","B":"不会，因为原告没有送达传票强制被告出席。","C":"会，因为被告收到了证言录取书面通知。","D":"会，因为原告有权进行至多十次证言录取。"},"explanationZh":"进行口头或书面证言录取所需法院许可：\n\n在下列情况下，必须取得法院许可*或双方约定：\n• 证言录取超过十次上限；\n• 在双方首次规划会议前寻求证言录取；或\n• 受询问人在案件中已经接受过证言录取。\n\n*受询问人在监狱服刑时，始终必须取得法院许可。\n\n口头证言录取是一种证据开示方法，进行方在法庭外宣誓询问另一方或非当事人。为强制作为当事人的受询问人出席、作证，必须向其提供书面通知；其不出席时，联邦法院可以制裁。\n\n除非存在以下情形，可以不经法院许可或双方约定提供通知并进行证言录取：\n• 超过十次证言录取上限（包括口头和书面）；\n• 在双方首次规划会议前寻求证言录取*；或\n• 受询问人在本诉中已经接受过证言录取。\n\n本题原告希望在被告已经接受过一次证言录取后，再次对其进行口头证言录取。原告没有取得法院许可，双方也没有约定第二次证言录取，所以向被告送达的书面通知不适当（选项 C）。被告无需出席，法院不太可能给予制裁。\n\n*在首次规划会议前进行口头证言录取，如果进行方在通知中证明并提供支持事实，说明受询问人预计将离开美国且之后无法接受询问，则无需法院许可或双方约定。\n\n（选项 B）强制非当事人受询问人参加口头证言录取需要传票，但作为当事人的受询问人可以通过书面通知被强制出席。\n\n（选项 D）原告虽有权进行至多十次证言录取，但再次对同一被告录取证言前，仍须取得法院许可或双方约定。\n\n学习目标：除非：(1) 证言录取超过十次上限；(2) 在首次规划会议前寻求证言录取；或 (3) 受询问人在案件中已经接受过证言录取，一方可以不经法院许可或双方约定，通知并进行口头证言录取。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(a)（口头证言录取何时需要法院许可或双方约定）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-230","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":230,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A company incorporated in State A and with its principal place of business in State B sued its former employee from State C in a federal district court in State A for allegedly embezzling $80,000. During a deposition of the employee in State B, the company's attorney asked the employee whether he stole the money. The employee's attorney objected and directed the employee not to answer to preserve his Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination. The company's attorney thinks this privilege does not apply because she believes that the statute of limitations has run on any criminal proceedings that could be brought against the employee for his conduct. How should the company's attorney proceed?","choices":{"A":"Adjourn the deposition and file a motion with the federal court in State B to compel the employee to answer.","B":"Adjourn the deposition and file a motion with the federal court in State C to compel the employee to answer.","C":"Proceed with the deposition and then file a motion with the federal court in State A to compel the employee to answer.","D":"Proceed with the deposition and then file a motion with the federal court in State B to compel the employee to answer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Depositions\n\nType Procedure Objections Answers\n\nOral • Questions asked Concisely Answer required unless: by attorneys stated on (most record • enforcing court-• No notice of common) ordered limitation questions\n• presenting motion to\n• Responses terminate or limit or recorded by officer\n• preserving privilege Written • Questions asked Served in by officer writing\n• Notice of questions required\n• Responses recorded by officer A deposition is a method of discovery in which a party conducts a written or oral examination of another (ie, the deponent) under oath and outside of court. In an oral deposition, the parties' attorneys question the deponent. Like at a trial, any objections to these questions must be stated on the record or they may be waived. But unlike a trial, a judge is not present to rule on any objections. As a result, the objection is noted, the examination proceeds, and the deponent must answer the questions unless refusing to answer is necessary to:\n• enforce a court-ordered limitation (eg, an order limiting the deposition's scope)\n\n• present a motion to terminate or limit the deposition (eg, to prevent unreasonable annoyance or embarrassment of the deponent) or\n\n• preserve a privilege (eg, privilege against self-incrimination)—as seen here.\n\nThe questioning party may adjourn or complete the deposition before filing a motion to compel a response. If the deponent is a nonparty, the motion must be filed where the deposition occurred. But if the deponent is a party (as seen here), it must be filed where the lawsuit is pending. Therefore, the company's attorney should proceed with the deposition and file a motion in State A federal court to compel the employee's answer (Choices A, B & D). The judge will then assess the merits of the employee's privilege claim and decide if a response is required. Educational objective:\n\nWhen a deponent refuses to answer a question at a deposition, the questioning party may file a motion to compel a response. When the deponent is a nonparty, that motion must be filed in the district where the deposition occurred. But when the deponent is a party, the motion must be filed where the lawsuit is pending. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(c)(2) (objections at depositions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(2)–(3) (motion to compel deposition answer).","rule":"When a deponent refuses to answer a question at a deposition, the questioning party may file a motion to compel a response. When the deponent is a nonparty, that motion must be filed in the district where the deposition occurred. But when the deponent is a party, the motion must be filed where the lawsuit is pending.","irac":{"issue":"A company incorporated in State A and with its principal place of business in State B sued its former employee from State C in a federal district court in State A for allegedly embezzling $80,000. During a deposition of the employee in State B, the company's attorney asked the employee whether he stole the money. The employee's attorney objected and directed the employee not to answer to preserve his Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination. The company's attorney thinks this privilege does not apply because she believes that the statute of limitations has run on any criminal proceedings that could be brought against the employee for his conduct. How should the company's attorney proceed?","rule":"When a deponent refuses to answer a question at a deposition, the questioning party may file a motion to compel a response. When the deponent is a nonparty, that motion must be filed in the district where the deposition occurred. But when the deponent is a party, the motion must be filed where the lawsuit is pending.","analysis":"Depositions\n\nType Procedure Objections Answers\n\nOral • Questions asked Concisely Answer required unless: by attorneys stated on (most record • enforcing court-• No notice of common) ordered limitation questions\n• presenting motion to\n• Responses terminate or limit or recorded by officer\n• preserving privilege Written • Questions asked Served in by officer writing\n• Notice of questions required\n• Responses recorded by officer A deposition is a method of discovery in which a party conducts a written or oral examination of another (ie, the deponent) under oath and outside of court. In an oral deposition, the parties' attorneys question the deponent. Like at a trial, any objections to these questions must be stated on the record or they may be waived. But unlike a trial, a judge is not present to rule on any objections. As a result, the objection is noted, the examination proceeds, and the deponent must answer the questions unless refusing to answer is necessary to:\n• enforce a court-ordered limitation (eg, an order limiting the deposition's scope)\n\n• present a motion to terminate or limit the deposition (eg, to prevent unreasonable annoyance or embarrassment of the deponent) or\n\n• preserve a privilege (eg, privilege against self-incrimination)—as seen here.\n\nThe questioning party may adjourn or complete the deposition before filing a motion to compel a response. If the deponent is a nonparty, the motion must be filed where the deposition occurred. But if the deponent is a party (as seen here), it must be filed where the lawsuit is pending. Therefore, the company's attorney should proceed with the deposition and file a motion in State A federal court to compel the employee's answer (Choices A, B & D). The judge will then assess the merits of the employee's privilege claim and decide if a response is required. Educational objective:\n\nWhen a deponent refuses to answer a question at a deposition, the questioning party may file a motion to compel a response. When the deponent is a nonparty, that motion must be filed in the district where the deposition occurred. But when the deponent is a party, the motion must be filed where the lawsuit is pending. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(c)(2) (objections at depositions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(2)–(3) (motion to compel deposition answer).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(c)(2) (objections at depositions).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(2)–(3) (motion to compel deposition answer).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[694],"sourceAnswerPages":[695,696],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家在 A 州注册成立、主要营业地位于 B 州的公司，在 A 州联邦地区法院起诉一名来自 C 州的前雇员，据称其侵吞 80,000 美元。在 B 州对雇员进行证言录取时，公司律师询问雇员是否偷了这笔钱。雇员律师提出异议，并指示雇员不要回答，以保留其第五修正案不得自证其罪特权。公司律师认为该特权不适用，因为她认为针对雇员行为提起任何刑事程序的诉讼时效已经届满。公司律师应如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"暂停证言录取，并向 B 州联邦法院申请强制雇员回答。","B":"暂停证言录取，并向 C 州联邦法院申请强制雇员回答。","C":"继续进行证言录取，之后向 A 州联邦法院申请强制雇员回答。","D":"继续进行证言录取，之后向 B 州联邦法院申请强制雇员回答。"},"explanationZh":"证言录取：\n\n类型｜程序｜异议｜回答\n口头（最常见）｜律师提问；无需事先通知问题；工作人员记录回答｜在案卷记录中简洁提出｜必须回答，除非为：执行法院命令的限制；提出终止或限制动议；保留特权\n书面｜工作人员提问；必须事先通知问题；工作人员记录回答｜书面送达\n\n证言录取是一方在法庭外宣誓书面或口头询问另一人（受询问人）的证据开示方法。口头证言录取中，由双方律师询问。与审判相同，对问题的异议必须在案卷记录中提出，否则可能放弃。但与审判不同，没有法官在场裁定异议。因此，异议被记录后，询问继续，受询问人必须回答，除非拒绝回答是为：\n• 执行法院命令的限制，例如限制证言录取范围的命令；\n• 提出终止或限制证言录取动议，例如防止对受询问人造成不合理烦扰或难堪；或\n• 保留特权，例如不得自证其罪特权，本题即如此。\n\n询问方可以暂停或完成证言录取后，再申请强制回答。受询问人是非当事人时，动议必须向证言录取发生地法院提出；受询问人是当事人时（本题即如此），必须向诉讼待决法院提出。因此，公司律师应继续进行证言录取，再向 A 州联邦法院申请强制雇员回答（选项 A、B、D）。法官随后将评估雇员特权主张的实体，决定是否必须回答。\n\n学习目标：受询问人在证言录取中拒绝回答问题时，询问方可以申请强制回应。受询问人是非当事人时，必须向证言录取发生地区法院提出；受询问人是当事人时，必须向诉讼待决法院提出。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(c)(2)（证言录取中的异议）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(2)–(3)（强制回答证言录取问题的动议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-231","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":231,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff filed an action in federal district court and served the defendant with the summons and complaint. The defendant moved to dismiss the complaint for failure to state a claim. Instead of opposing the motion to dismiss, the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed the action and filed a new action alleging the same claims and addressing the pleading defects outlined in the defendant's motion to dismiss. The defendant moved to dismiss the second action, and the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed the second action instead of filing opposition papers. The plaintiff has filed a third action asserting the same claims and new allegations that are responsive to the defendant's second motion. The defendant has moved to dismiss the third action; the plaintiff opposes the motion. Is the court likely to grant the defendant's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the plaintiff has promptly and diligently attempted to address the pleading defects.","B":"No, because the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed each previous action before the defendant filed an answer or moved for summary judgment.","C":"Yes, because the plaintiff failed to seek a court order dismissing the second action.","D":"Yes, because the plaintiff's previously dismissed actions asserting the same claims operate as an adjudication on the merits."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 41(a), a plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss a suit without a court order by filing (1) a stipulation of dismissal signed by all the parties or (2) a notice of dismissal before the defendant serves an answer or a summary judgment motion. Here, no court order was required since the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed the first and second actions before an answer or summary judgment motion was filed (Choice C). A voluntary dismissal is generally entered without prejudice. This means that the plaintiff can sue the defendant on the same claim in the future. However, a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice and operates as an adjudication on the merits when stated in an order, notice, or stipulation of dismissal OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff:\n• voluntarily dismissed the first action in federal or state court without a court order and\n\n• voluntarily dismissed the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal.\n\nHere, the plaintiff likely dismissed both actions by a notice of dismissal since there is no indication that the court issued an order or the parties signed a stipulation. Since these actions asserted the same claims, the second dismissal operated as an adjudication on the merits. Therefore, the court will likely grant the defendant's motion to dismiss the third action. (Choice A) The fact that the plaintiff has promptly and diligently attempted to address the pleading defects raised by the defendant does not affect the application of the two-dismissal rule. (Choice B) Although the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed each previous action before the defendant filed an answer or moved for summary judgment, the two-dismissal rule still bars the third action. Educational objective: Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal of a second action operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).","rule":"Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal of a second action operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff filed an action in federal district court and served the defendant with the summons and complaint. The defendant moved to dismiss the complaint for failure to state a claim. Instead of opposing the motion to dismiss, the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed the action and filed a new action alleging the same claims and addressing the pleading defects outlined in the defendant's motion to dismiss. The defendant moved to dismiss the second action, and the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed the second action instead of filing opposition papers. The plaintiff has filed a third action asserting the same claims and new allegations that are responsive to the defendant's second motion. The defendant has moved to dismiss the third action; the plaintiff opposes the motion. Is the court likely to grant the defendant's motion?","rule":"Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal of a second action operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 41(a), a plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss a suit without a court order by filing (1) a stipulation of dismissal signed by all the parties or (2) a notice of dismissal before the defendant serves an answer or a summary judgment motion. Here, no court order was required since the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed the first and second actions before an answer or summary judgment motion was filed (Choice C). A voluntary dismissal is generally entered without prejudice. This means that the plaintiff can sue the defendant on the same claim in the future. However, a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice and operates as an adjudication on the merits when stated in an order, notice, or stipulation of dismissal OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff:\n• voluntarily dismissed the first action in federal or state court without a court order and\n\n• voluntarily dismissed the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal.\n\nHere, the plaintiff likely dismissed both actions by a notice of dismissal since there is no indication that the court issued an order or the parties signed a stipulation. Since these actions asserted the same claims, the second dismissal operated as an adjudication on the merits. Therefore, the court will likely grant the defendant's motion to dismiss the third action. (Choice A) The fact that the plaintiff has promptly and diligently attempted to address the pleading defects raised by the defendant does not affect the application of the two-dismissal rule. (Choice B) Although the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed each previous action before the defendant filed an answer or moved for summary judgment, the two-dismissal rule still bars the third action. Educational objective: Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal of a second action operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[697],"sourceAnswerPages":[698],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院提起诉讼，并向被告送达传票和起诉状。被告以未提出可获救济的请求为由申请驳回起诉状。原告没有反对该动议，而是自愿驳回诉讼，另行提起新诉，提出相同请求并纠正被告驳回动议指出的诉状缺陷。被告申请驳回第二案，原告仍未提交反对文件，而是自愿驳回第二案。原告现已提起第三案，提出相同请求，并增加回应被告第二次动议的新指控。被告申请驳回第三案，原告提出反对。法院可能批准被告动议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为原告一直及时、勤勉地尝试纠正诉状缺陷。","B":"不会，因为原告每次都在被告提交答辩状或简易判决动议前自愿驳回先前诉讼。","C":"会，因为原告未申请法院命令驳回第二案。","D":"会，因为原告先前提出相同请求后驳回的诉讼构成实体裁判。"},"explanationZh":"根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》41(a)，原告可以通过以下方式，不经法院命令自愿驳回诉讼：(1) 提交所有各方签署的驳回约定；或 (2) 在被告送达答辩状或简易判决动议前提交驳回通知。本题第一案和第二案均在提交答辩状或简易判决动议前自愿驳回，所以不需要法院命令（选项 C）。\n\n自愿驳回通常为无损害驳回，即原告以后可就相同请求再次起诉被告。但是，在下列情况下，自愿驳回为有损害驳回并构成实体裁判：驳回命令、通知或约定作出如此说明，或“两次驳回规则”适用。两次驳回规则适用于原告：\n• 不经法院命令，在联邦或州法院自愿驳回第一案；并且\n• 通过提交驳回通知，在联邦法院自愿驳回基于相同请求的第二案。\n\n本题没有迹象表明法院发布命令或双方签署约定，所以原告很可能通过驳回通知驳回两案。两案提出相同请求，所以第二次驳回构成实体裁判。因此，法院很可能批准被告驳回第三案的动议。\n\n（选项 A）原告一直及时、勤勉尝试纠正被告指出的诉状缺陷，不影响两次驳回规则的适用。\n\n（选项 B）原告虽然每次都在被告提交答辩状或简易判决动议前自愿驳回，但两次驳回规则仍禁止第三案。\n\n学习目标：根据两次驳回规则，原告在下列情况下自愿驳回第二案即构成实体裁判：(1) 不经法院命令，在联邦或州法院自愿驳回第一案；且 (2) 通过提交驳回通知，在联邦法院自愿驳回基于相同请求的第二案。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a)（自愿驳回）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-074"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-232","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":232,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An electrician and a plumber filed separate diversity actions against a construction company in which they both claim that the company failed to pay them the amount specified in their contracts. The company defended on the ground that the electrician and plumber failed to complete their work on time. The electrician served a request for admission on the company, requesting that the company admit that it repeatedly denied contractors such as the electrician access to the construction site. The company did not respond to the request. Forty-five days after the electrician's request was served, the plumber asked the court during a pretrial conference to enter an order conclusively establishing the fact that the company repeatedly denied contractors access to the construction site. The plumber relied on the company's failure to timely respond to the electrician's request for admission. Is the court likely to enter the order?","choices":{"A":"No, because the construction company has sixty days to respond to the electrician's request for admission.","B":"No, because the plumber cannot rely on the construction company's failure to respond to the request for admission in the electrician's action.","C":"Yes, because the construction company's failure to respond to the electrician's request for admission within 30 days after service is treated as an admission.","D":"Yes, because the court has the authority to enter orders at pretrial conferences that will expedite litigation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"In a request for admission, a party requests that another party admit the truth about matters within the scope of discovery that relate to facts, the application of law to facts, opinions about either, or the genuineness of documents. The party served with such a request must serve a written response within 30 days that:\n• admits the matter\n• specifically denies the matter in whole or in part\n• explains why the matter cannot be admitted or denied or\n• objects that the matter falls outside the scope of discovery. A party's failure to adequately respond within 30 days results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the pending action. However, it does not serve as an admission for any other purpose and cannot be used against the party in another proceeding. Here, the electrician and the plumber filed separate actions against the construction company. The electrician then served the company with a request to admit that it repeatedly denied contractors access to the construction site. The company's failure to respond within 30 days after the request was served results in the matter being established in the electrician's action (Choice A). However, the plumber cannot rely on the company's failure to respond in the electrician's action, so the court is unlikely to enter the plumber's requested order (Choice C). (Choice D) At a pretrial conference, a federal court has broad authority to consider matters and enter appropriate orders that will help expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. Here, an order establishing that the company repeatedly denied contractors access to the construction site would be improper since the plumber cannot rely on the company's failure to respond in the electrician's action. Educational objective: A party's failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after the request is served results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the pending action. However, it does not serve as an admission for any other purpose and cannot be used against the party in another proceeding. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36 (requests for admission).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2264 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the effect of failing to respond to a request for admission).","rule":"A party's failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after the request is served results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the pending action. However, it does not serve as an admission for any other purpose and cannot be used against the party in another proceeding.","irac":{"issue":"An electrician and a plumber filed separate diversity actions against a construction company in which they both claim that the company failed to pay them the amount specified in their contracts. The company defended on the ground that the electrician and plumber failed to complete their work on time. The electrician served a request for admission on the company, requesting that the company admit that it repeatedly denied contractors such as the electrician access to the construction site. The company did not respond to the request. Forty-five days after the electrician's request was served, the plumber asked the court during a pretrial conference to enter an order conclusively establishing the fact that the company repeatedly denied contractors access to the construction site. The plumber relied on the company's failure to timely respond to the electrician's request for admission. Is the court likely to enter the order?","rule":"A party's failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after the request is served results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the pending action. However, it does not serve as an admission for any other purpose and cannot be used against the party in another proceeding.","analysis":"In a request for admission, a party requests that another party admit the truth about matters within the scope of discovery that relate to facts, the application of law to facts, opinions about either, or the genuineness of documents. The party served with such a request must serve a written response within 30 days that:\n• admits the matter\n• specifically denies the matter in whole or in part\n• explains why the matter cannot be admitted or denied or\n• objects that the matter falls outside the scope of discovery. A party's failure to adequately respond within 30 days results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the pending action. However, it does not serve as an admission for any other purpose and cannot be used against the party in another proceeding. Here, the electrician and the plumber filed separate actions against the construction company. The electrician then served the company with a request to admit that it repeatedly denied contractors access to the construction site. The company's failure to respond within 30 days after the request was served results in the matter being established in the electrician's action (Choice A). However, the plumber cannot rely on the company's failure to respond in the electrician's action, so the court is unlikely to enter the plumber's requested order (Choice C). (Choice D) At a pretrial conference, a federal court has broad authority to consider matters and enter appropriate orders that will help expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. Here, an order establishing that the company repeatedly denied contractors access to the construction site would be improper since the plumber cannot rely on the company's failure to respond in the electrician's action. Educational objective: A party's failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after the request is served results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the pending action. However, it does not serve as an admission for any other purpose and cannot be used against the party in another proceeding. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36 (requests for admission).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2264 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the effect of failing to respond to a request for admission).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36 (requests for admission).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2264 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the effect of failing to respond to a request for admission).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[700],"sourceAnswerPages":[701],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名电工和一名水管工分别对一家建筑公司提起多元州籍诉讼，均主张公司未按合同约定金额付款。公司抗辩称，电工和水管工未按时完成工作。电工向公司送达承认请求，要求公司承认曾反复拒绝包括电工在内的承包商进入施工现场。公司没有回应。电工请求送达四十五天后，水管工在审前会议上请求法院发布命令，终局性确立公司曾反复拒绝承包商进入施工现场这一事实。水管工依据的是公司未及时回应电工的承认请求。法院可能发布该命令吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为建筑公司有六十天回应电工的承认请求。","B":"不会，因为水管工不能依赖建筑公司在电工诉讼中未回应承认请求这一事实。","C":"会，因为建筑公司未在电工承认请求送达后 30 天内回应，视为承认。","D":"会，因为法院有权在审前会议上发布加快诉讼的命令。"},"explanationZh":"承认请求中，一方要求另一方承认证据开示范围内的事项，包括事实、法律对事实的适用、对二者的意见或文件真实性。收到请求的一方必须在 30 天内送达书面回应，以：\n• 承认事项；\n• 明确全部或部分否认；\n• 说明为何无法承认或否认；或\n• 以事项超出证据开示范围为由提出异议。\n\n一方未在 30 天内充分回应，导致该事项在待决诉讼中被承认并得到终局性确立。但是，该承认不用于任何其他目的，也不能在另一程序中对该方使用。\n\n本题电工和水管工分别对建筑公司提起诉讼。电工向公司送达承认请求，要求承认曾反复拒绝承包商进入施工现场。公司未在请求送达后 30 天内回应，使该事项在电工诉讼中得到确立（选项 A）。但是，水管工不能依赖公司在电工诉讼中未回应，所以法院不太可能发布水管工请求的命令（选项 C）。\n\n（选项 D）审前会议上，联邦法院有广泛权力考虑事项并发布适当命令，以加快诉讼、提高审判质量并促进和解。但本题水管工不能依赖公司在电工诉讼中未回应，因此确立该事实的命令不适当。\n\n学习目标：一方未在承认请求送达后 30 天内充分回应，导致该事项在待决诉讼中被承认并得到终局性确立。但该承认不用于其他目的，也不能在另一程序中对该方使用。\n\n参考资料：\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36（承认请求）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2264 (3d ed. 2020)（说明未回应承认请求的效力）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-075"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-233","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":233,"topic":"Discovery","question":"During an interview, a company's chief executive officer (CEO) made a prepared statement denying that the company was in merger talks with one of its competitors. After the interview aired, the company's stock price dropped, and an investor sold his stock in the company at a great loss. Two weeks later, the company announced that it was merging with its competitor. The investor filed a lawsuit against the company in an appropriate federal court based on misrepresentation. After the parties held their initial planning conference, the investor requested that the company produce all documents and data related to the CEO's prepared statement and the subsequent announcement of the merger. In response, the company carefully screened its records and produced approximately three million documents, including electronically stored information. It also produced a privilege log of documents withheld. After production, the company realized that it had inadvertently produced 102 potentially privileged documents. The company immediately notified the investor of the inadvertent disclosure, provided a basis for its privilege claim, and asked that the documents be immediately returned. The investor, who disputed the company's privilege claim, immediately isolated the documents from the rest of the production but refused to return them. The investor has presented the documents and the parties' privilege arguments to the court for a determination of the privilege claim. A hearing was scheduled to be held in 15 days. In response, the company promptly moved for an injunction that would require the investor to immediately return the documents to the company and destroy any copies in his possession before the hearing. How should the court rule on the company's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the company has waived attorney-client privilege by disclosing the documents to a third party.","B":"Deny the motion, because the investor promptly sequestered the documents and presented the information to the court.","C":"Grant the motion, because the investor has failed to return the documents.","D":"Grant the motion, because the party that produced the privileged information determines whether it is returned, sequestered, or destroyed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When privileged information is inadvertently produced, FRCP 26(b)(5)(B) permits the producing party to promptly notify the receiving party of the disclosure and provide the basis for the privilege claim. After being notified, the receiving party must:\n• promptly return, sequester, or destroy the information and any copies\n• not use or disclose the information until the privilege claim is resolved and\n• take reasonable steps to retrieve information already disclosed to others. This rule gives the receiving party the option to return, sequester, or destroy the documents at issue because the rule contemplates that the receiving party may dispute the privilege claim. In this situation, sequestering the documents achieves the purpose of the rule— protecting the potentially privileged information—while enabling the receiving party to present the documents and the disputed privilege claim to the court for resolution. Here, the investor properly sequestered the documents and presented the parties' privilege dispute to the court for resolution. Therefore, the court will likely deny the company's motion for an injunction requiring the investor to immediately return and destroy the purportedly privileged documents before a hearing on the privilege claim.* *However, the investor may still have to return or destroy the documents if the court later determines that they are in fact privileged. (Choice A) An inadvertent disclosure does not waive the privilege so long as the disclosing party took reasonable steps to prevent disclosure—eg, carefully screened its records and produced a privilege log—and promptly rectified the disclosure (as seen here). (Choice C) Since the investor sequestered the privileged documents, he is not required to immediately return them to the company. (Choice D) The party that received (as opposed to produced) the privileged information determines whether it is returned, sequestered, or destroyed when disputing the producing party's privilege claim. Educational objective: After being notified that privileged information was produced, the receiving party must (1) promptly return, sequester, or destroy the information and any copies, (2) not use or disclose the information until the claim of privilege is resolved, and (3) take reasonable steps to retrieve information already disclosed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(5) (production of privileged materials).\n\n• Fed. R. Evid. 502(b) (describing inadvertent disclosure).","rule":"After being notified that privileged information was produced, the receiving party must (1) promptly return, sequester, or destroy the information and any copies, (2) not use or disclose the information until the claim of privilege is resolved, and (3) take reasonable steps to retrieve information already disclosed.","irac":{"issue":"During an interview, a company's chief executive officer (CEO) made a prepared statement denying that the company was in merger talks with one of its competitors. After the interview aired, the company's stock price dropped, and an investor sold his stock in the company at a great loss. Two weeks later, the company announced that it was merging with its competitor. The investor filed a lawsuit against the company in an appropriate federal court based on misrepresentation. After the parties held their initial planning conference, the investor requested that the company produce all documents and data related to the CEO's prepared statement and the subsequent announcement of the merger. In response, the company carefully screened its records and produced approximately three million documents, including electronically stored information. It also produced a privilege log of documents withheld. After production, the company realized that it had inadvertently produced 102 potentially privileged documents. The company immediately notified the investor of the inadvertent disclosure, provided a basis for its privilege claim, and asked that the documents be immediately returned. The investor, who disputed the company's privilege claim, immediately isolated the documents from the rest of the production but refused to return them. The investor has presented the documents and the parties' privilege arguments to the court for a determination of the privilege claim. A hearing was scheduled to be held in 15 days. In response, the company promptly moved for an injunction that would require the investor to immediately return the documents to the company and destroy any copies in his possession before the hearing. How should the court rule on the company's motion?","rule":"After being notified that privileged information was produced, the receiving party must (1) promptly return, sequester, or destroy the information and any copies, (2) not use or disclose the information until the claim of privilege is resolved, and (3) take reasonable steps to retrieve information already disclosed.","analysis":"When privileged information is inadvertently produced, FRCP 26(b)(5)(B) permits the producing party to promptly notify the receiving party of the disclosure and provide the basis for the privilege claim. After being notified, the receiving party must:\n• promptly return, sequester, or destroy the information and any copies\n• not use or disclose the information until the privilege claim is resolved and\n• take reasonable steps to retrieve information already disclosed to others. This rule gives the receiving party the option to return, sequester, or destroy the documents at issue because the rule contemplates that the receiving party may dispute the privilege claim. In this situation, sequestering the documents achieves the purpose of the rule— protecting the potentially privileged information—while enabling the receiving party to present the documents and the disputed privilege claim to the court for resolution. Here, the investor properly sequestered the documents and presented the parties' privilege dispute to the court for resolution. Therefore, the court will likely deny the company's motion for an injunction requiring the investor to immediately return and destroy the purportedly privileged documents before a hearing on the privilege claim.* *However, the investor may still have to return or destroy the documents if the court later determines that they are in fact privileged. (Choice A) An inadvertent disclosure does not waive the privilege so long as the disclosing party took reasonable steps to prevent disclosure—eg, carefully screened its records and produced a privilege log—and promptly rectified the disclosure (as seen here). (Choice C) Since the investor sequestered the privileged documents, he is not required to immediately return them to the company. (Choice D) The party that received (as opposed to produced) the privileged information determines whether it is returned, sequestered, or destroyed when disputing the producing party's privilege claim. Educational objective: After being notified that privileged information was produced, the receiving party must (1) promptly return, sequester, or destroy the information and any copies, (2) not use or disclose the information until the claim of privilege is resolved, and (3) take reasonable steps to retrieve information already disclosed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(5) (production of privileged materials).\n\n• Fed. R. Evid. 502(b) (describing inadvertent disclosure).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(5) (production of privileged materials).\n\n• Fed. R. Evid. 502(b) (describing inadvertent disclosure).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[703],"sourceAnswerPages":[704,705],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在一次采访中，某公司的首席执行官（CEO）宣读了一份事先准备好的声明，否认公司正在与一家竞争对手进行合并谈判。采访播出后，公司股价下跌，一名投资者以巨额亏损卖出了其持有的公司股票。两周后，公司宣布将与该竞争对手合并。该投资者以虚假陈述为由，在具有适当管辖权的联邦法院对公司提起诉讼。双方举行初始规划会议后，投资者要求公司提供与CEO事先准备的声明及随后宣布合并有关的所有文件和数据。作为回应，公司仔细筛查了其记录，并提供了约300万份文件，其中包括电子存储信息。公司还提供了一份关于所扣留文件的特权文件清单。完成提供后，公司发现其无意中提供了102份可能受特权保护的文件。公司立即将该无意披露通知投资者，说明其主张特权的依据，并要求立即返还这些文件。投资者对公司的特权主张有异议；他立即将这些文件与其余已提供材料隔离，但拒绝返还。投资者已将这些文件以及双方关于特权的论点提交法院，请求法院裁定特权主张。听证会定于15天后举行。对此，公司迅速申请禁令，要求投资者在听证会前立即把文件返还公司，并销毁其持有的所有副本。法院应如何裁定公司的申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回申请，因为公司向第三方披露这些文件，已经放弃律师—客户保密特权。","B":"驳回申请，因为投资者已及时隔离这些文件，并将相关信息提交法院。","C":"批准申请，因为投资者没有返还这些文件。","D":"批准申请，因为应由提供特权信息的一方决定该信息应被返还、隔离还是销毁。"},"explanationZh":"特权信息被无意提供时，《联邦民事诉讼规则》26(b)(5)(B)允许提供方及时将该披露通知接收方，并说明特权主张的依据。收到通知后，接收方必须：\n• 及时返还、隔离或销毁该信息及其所有副本；\n• 在特权主张得到解决前，不得使用或披露该信息；并且\n• 采取合理措施取回已经向他人披露的信息。\n\n该规则赋予接收方返还、隔离或销毁争议文件的选择权，因为规则预见到接收方可能会对特权主张提出异议。在这种情况下，隔离文件既实现了规则保护可能受特权保护信息的目的，也使接收方能够把文件和有争议的特权主张提交法院解决。\n\n本题中，投资者已适当地隔离这些文件，并把双方的特权争议提交法院裁决。因此，法院很可能会驳回公司的禁令申请，即不会要求投资者在特权听证会前立即返还并销毁据称受特权保护的文件。*\n\n*不过，如果法院随后认定这些文件确实受特权保护，投资者仍可能必须返还或销毁这些文件。\n\n（选项A）只要披露方已采取合理措施防止披露——例如仔细筛查其记录并制作特权文件清单——且及时纠正披露（本题正是如此），无意披露就不会导致特权放弃。\n\n（选项C）投资者已经隔离受特权保护的文件，因此无须立即将其返还公司。\n\n（选项D）接收（而非提供）特权信息的一方在对提供方的特权主张提出异议时，有权决定返还、隔离还是销毁该信息。\n\n学习目标：收到特权信息被提供的通知后，接收方必须：(1)及时返还、隔离或销毁该信息及其所有副本；(2)在特权主张得到解决前，不得使用或披露该信息；以及(3)采取合理措施取回已经披露的信息。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(5)（特权材料的提供）。\n• Fed. R. Evid. 502(b)（说明无意披露）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-076"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-234","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":234,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A pedestrian sued an out-of-state driver in a federal district court for allegedly hitting the pedestrian in a crosswalk, which the pedestrian claims resulted in $1 million in medical expenses, lost wages, and pain and suffering. The driver filed an answer denying liability. Which of the following must the pedestrian automatically provide the driver without waiting for a discovery request?","choices":{"A":"A copy of the pedestrian's medical bills for her alleged injuries.","B":"A copy of the pedestrian's medical insurance agreement.","C":"A list of the cases in which the pedestrian has been a witness over the previous four years.","D":"The name of a witness who knows about the driver's past car accidents."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nType Required disclosures Time to disclose\n\nInitial • Persons with relevant information • ≤ 14 days after initial disclosures* • Items in party's possession that planning conference support claim/defense • ≤ 30 days after post-• Computation of damages & conference service supporting material or joinder\n• Relevant insurance policies • As court orders or parties stipulate Expert • Identity of expert witnesses • ≥ 90 days before trial testimony • Expert's written report • ≤ 30 days after\n• If no report, subject matter & disclosure to rebut summary of testimony evidence\n• As court orders or parties stipulate Pretrial • Testifying witnesses • ≥ 30 days before trial disclosures* • Deposition testimony to be used • As court orders at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(a), parties are required to make initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and pretrial disclosures even without a discovery request. A party must initially disclose:\n• the name, address, and phone number of persons with relevant information that the party may use to support a claim or defense\n\n• all documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things in the party's possession that the party may use to support a claim or defense\n\n• any insurance agreement with a company that may be liable for all or part of any judgment and\n\n• a computation of damages and the materials on which it was based.\n\nAll of this information must be disclosed within 14 days after the initial planning conference or by a date set by the court or parties.* Here, since part of the pedestrian's damages are based on her medical expenses, she must provide the driver with a copy of her medical bills without waiting for a discovery request. *If a party is added after the initial planning conference, initial disclosures must be made within 30 days after service or joinder of that party. (Choice B) The pedestrian does not need to provide a copy of her health insurance agreement because her insurance company will not be liable. Instead, the driver must provide his automobile insurance agreement because his insurance company may be liable for the pedestrian's damages. (Choice C) A testifying expert witness (not seen here) must disclose a written report that lists the cases in which the witness has testified over the past four years. But this is only required if the witness (1) is a specially hired expert or (2) regularly gives expert testimony as part of his/her employment duties with the party. (Choice D) Testimony from a witness who knows about the driver's past driving habits may be used to impeach the driver's trial testimony. But initial disclosures do not apply to witnesses or evidence used solely for impeachment. Educational objective: A party's initial disclosures must include (1) persons and items that the party may use to support a claim or defense, (2) any insurance agreement with a company that may be liable, and (3) a computation of damages and the materials on which it was based. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) (required initial disclosures).","rule":"A party's initial disclosures must include (1) persons and items that the party may use to support a claim or defense, (2) any insurance agreement with a company that may be liable, and (3) a computation of damages and the materials on which it was based.","irac":{"issue":"A pedestrian sued an out-of-state driver in a federal district court for allegedly hitting the pedestrian in a crosswalk, which the pedestrian claims resulted in $1 million in medical expenses, lost wages, and pain and suffering. The driver filed an answer denying liability. Which of the following must the pedestrian automatically provide the driver without waiting for a discovery request?","rule":"A party's initial disclosures must include (1) persons and items that the party may use to support a claim or defense, (2) any insurance agreement with a company that may be liable, and (3) a computation of damages and the materials on which it was based.","analysis":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nType Required disclosures Time to disclose\n\nInitial • Persons with relevant information • ≤ 14 days after initial disclosures* • Items in party's possession that planning conference support claim/defense • ≤ 30 days after post-• Computation of damages & conference service supporting material or joinder\n• Relevant insurance policies • As court orders or parties stipulate Expert • Identity of expert witnesses • ≥ 90 days before trial testimony • Expert's written report • ≤ 30 days after\n• If no report, subject matter & disclosure to rebut summary of testimony evidence\n• As court orders or parties stipulate Pretrial • Testifying witnesses • ≥ 30 days before trial disclosures* • Deposition testimony to be used • As court orders at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(a), parties are required to make initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and pretrial disclosures even without a discovery request. A party must initially disclose:\n• the name, address, and phone number of persons with relevant information that the party may use to support a claim or defense\n\n• all documents, electronically stored information, and tangible things in the party's possession that the party may use to support a claim or defense\n\n• any insurance agreement with a company that may be liable for all or part of any judgment and\n\n• a computation of damages and the materials on which it was based.\n\nAll of this information must be disclosed within 14 days after the initial planning conference or by a date set by the court or parties.* Here, since part of the pedestrian's damages are based on her medical expenses, she must provide the driver with a copy of her medical bills without waiting for a discovery request. *If a party is added after the initial planning conference, initial disclosures must be made within 30 days after service or joinder of that party. (Choice B) The pedestrian does not need to provide a copy of her health insurance agreement because her insurance company will not be liable. Instead, the driver must provide his automobile insurance agreement because his insurance company may be liable for the pedestrian's damages. (Choice C) A testifying expert witness (not seen here) must disclose a written report that lists the cases in which the witness has testified over the past four years. But this is only required if the witness (1) is a specially hired expert or (2) regularly gives expert testimony as part of his/her employment duties with the party. (Choice D) Testimony from a witness who knows about the driver's past driving habits may be used to impeach the driver's trial testimony. But initial disclosures do not apply to witnesses or evidence used solely for impeachment. Educational objective: A party's initial disclosures must include (1) persons and items that the party may use to support a claim or defense, (2) any insurance agreement with a company that may be liable, and (3) a computation of damages and the materials on which it was based. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) (required initial disclosures).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1) (required initial disclosures).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[706],"sourceAnswerPages":[707,708],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名行人在联邦地区法院起诉一名外州司机，指称司机在行人通过人行横道时撞到行人，造成100万美元的医疗费用、误工损失以及疼痛与痛苦。司机提交答辩状否认责任。下列哪一项是行人无须等待证据开示请求就必须自动向司机提供的？","choicesZh":{"A":"行人因其所称伤害产生的医疗账单副本。","B":"行人的医疗保险协议副本。","C":"过去四年中行人曾作为证人参与的案件清单。","D":"一名了解司机以往汽车事故的证人的姓名。"},"explanationZh":"强制性证据开示披露\n\n（FRCP 26(a)）\n\n类型｜必须披露的内容｜披露时间\n\n初始披露\n• 掌握相关信息的人员\n• 当事人持有且可用来支持请求或抗辩的物品\n• 损害赔偿计算及支持材料\n• 相关保险单\n时间：\n• 初始规划会议后不超过14天*\n• 在规划会议后才送达诉状或加入诉讼的当事人，应在送达或加入后不超过30天\n• 或依照法院命令或当事人约定\n\n专家证言披露\n• 专家证人的身份\n• 专家的书面报告\n• 如无须提交报告，则披露证言主题及证言概要\n时间：\n• 审判前至少90天\n• 反驳证据应在相对方披露后不超过30天\n• 或依照法院命令或当事人约定\n\n审前披露*\n• 将出庭作证的证人\n• 审判时将使用的证言笔录\n• 审判时将使用的文件或证物\n时间：\n• 审判前至少30天\n• 或依照法院命令\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不适用于仅为弹劾证人可信度而使用的证据。\n\n根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》26(a)，即使没有收到证据开示请求，当事人也必须进行初始披露、专家证言披露和审前披露。当事人必须在初始披露中提供：\n• 其可能用来支持请求或抗辩、并掌握相关信息之人员的姓名、地址和电话号码；\n• 当事人持有且可能用来支持请求或抗辩的所有文件、电子存储信息和有形物；\n• 与可能承担全部或部分判决责任的公司订立的任何保险协议；以及\n• 损害赔偿的计算及该计算所依据的材料。\n\n所有这些信息必须在初始规划会议后14天内，或者在法院或当事人确定的日期披露。* 本题中，由于行人的部分损害赔偿以其医疗费用为依据，她必须无须等待证据开示请求就向司机提供医疗账单副本。\n\n*如果某一方在初始规划会议后才加入诉讼，则必须在该方收到送达或加入诉讼后30天内进行初始披露。\n\n（选项B）行人无须提供其医疗保险协议副本，因为其保险公司不会对本案判决承担责任。相反，司机必须提供其汽车保险协议，因为司机的保险公司可能对行人的损害承担责任。\n\n（选项C）出庭作证的专家证人（本题并无此情形）必须提交一份书面报告，列明该证人在过去四年中曾作证的案件。但只有在该证人是：(1)专门聘请的专家，或(2)其在本方任职期间的常规职责包括提供专家证言时，才有此要求。\n\n（选项D）了解司机以往驾驶习惯的证人所作证言，可能被用于弹劾司机在审判中的证言。然而，初始披露不适用于仅用于弹劾证人可信度的证人或证据。\n\n学习目标：一方的初始披露必须包括：(1)该方可能用来支持请求或抗辩的人员和物品；(2)与可能承担责任的公司订立的任何保险协议；以及(3)损害赔偿计算及其所依据的材料。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(1)（强制性初始披露）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-235","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":235,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman's house burned down after the wiring in her refrigerator sparked and started a fire. The following week, the woman received adequate notice that a class action had been filed in federal court against the manufacturer of the refrigerator for damages caused by the refrigerator's faulty wiring system. The woman did nothing when she received the notice and was not named as a class representative. The court certified the class action after determining that common questions of law and fact predominated and that the class action was the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute. During discovery, the named class representatives reached a proposed settlement with the manufacturer. At a hearing, the woman objected to the proposed settlement on the ground that it was inadequate to cover the damage to her house. Despite her objection, the court approved the settlement after concluding that it was fair, reasonable, and adequate. The woman then sued the manufacturer in another federal court to recover damages for the remaining damage to her house. The manufacturer moved to dismiss this suit. Is the court likely to grant the manufacturer's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman objected to the proposed class action settlement.","B":"No, because the woman was not a named representative in the class action.","C":"Yes, because the court certified the class action.","D":"Yes, because the woman failed to opt out of the class action."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 23 recognizes three types of class actions: prejudicial risk, final equitable relief, and common question. Here, the court certified (ie, authorized) a \"common question\" class action against the manufacturer because it determined that (1) common questions of law and fact predominated and (2) the class action was the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute. In \"common question\" class actions, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action and sue on their own behalf.* If a class member fails to opt out, he/she cannot pursue an individual suit involving the same claim. Instead, that class member is bound by any resulting final judgment or court-approved settlement, compromise, or voluntary dismissal. Here, the woman failed to opt out of the class action because she did nothing after receiving adequate notice of the class action. This means that she is bound by the court-approved settlement and barred from asserting an individual suit against the manufacturer. Therefore, the court will likely grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss her suit. *In \"prejudicial risk\" and \"final equitable relief\" class actions, notice is not required because class members do not have a right to opt out. Instead, a court has the discretion to provide notice and an opportunity to opt out of such class actions. (Choice A) A class member who objects to a proposed settlement shows that his/her interests diverge from those of the class. As a result, an objection allows the class member to appeal if the court approves the proposal—but not to file an individual suit. (Choice B) Named class representatives and unnamed class members alike are bound by a court-approved settlement except for those members who opted out of the class action (not seen here). (Choice C) The fact that the court certified the class action allowed the class action to proceed in federal court. However, it does not provide a basis for dismissing the woman's suit. Educational objective: A class member must opt out of a \"common question\" class action to pursue an individual suit involving the same claim. Otherwise, the class member is bound by any final judgment or court-approved settlement, compromise, or voluntary dismissal of the class action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2) (notice requirements for \"common question\" class action).\n\n• Matsushita Elec. Indus. Co., Ltd. v. Epstein, 516 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1996) (explaining that class members who fail to opt out of a class action are bound by a resulting court-approved settlement agreement).","rule":"A class member must opt out of a \"common question\" class action to pursue an individual suit involving the same claim. Otherwise, the class member is bound by any final judgment or court-approved settlement, compromise, or voluntary dismissal of the class action.","irac":{"issue":"A woman's house burned down after the wiring in her refrigerator sparked and started a fire. The following week, the woman received adequate notice that a class action had been filed in federal court against the manufacturer of the refrigerator for damages caused by the refrigerator's faulty wiring system. The woman did nothing when she received the notice and was not named as a class representative. The court certified the class action after determining that common questions of law and fact predominated and that the class action was the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute. During discovery, the named class representatives reached a proposed settlement with the manufacturer. At a hearing, the woman objected to the proposed settlement on the ground that it was inadequate to cover the damage to her house. Despite her objection, the court approved the settlement after concluding that it was fair, reasonable, and adequate. The woman then sued the manufacturer in another federal court to recover damages for the remaining damage to her house. The manufacturer moved to dismiss this suit. Is the court likely to grant the manufacturer's motion?","rule":"A class member must opt out of a \"common question\" class action to pursue an individual suit involving the same claim. Otherwise, the class member is bound by any final judgment or court-approved settlement, compromise, or voluntary dismissal of the class action.","analysis":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 23 recognizes three types of class actions: prejudicial risk, final equitable relief, and common question. Here, the court certified (ie, authorized) a \"common question\" class action against the manufacturer because it determined that (1) common questions of law and fact predominated and (2) the class action was the best method to fairly and efficiently adjudicate the dispute. In \"common question\" class actions, adequate notice must be provided to all class members because they have the right to opt out of the class action and sue on their own behalf.* If a class member fails to opt out, he/she cannot pursue an individual suit involving the same claim. Instead, that class member is bound by any resulting final judgment or court-approved settlement, compromise, or voluntary dismissal. Here, the woman failed to opt out of the class action because she did nothing after receiving adequate notice of the class action. This means that she is bound by the court-approved settlement and barred from asserting an individual suit against the manufacturer. Therefore, the court will likely grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss her suit. *In \"prejudicial risk\" and \"final equitable relief\" class actions, notice is not required because class members do not have a right to opt out. Instead, a court has the discretion to provide notice and an opportunity to opt out of such class actions. (Choice A) A class member who objects to a proposed settlement shows that his/her interests diverge from those of the class. As a result, an objection allows the class member to appeal if the court approves the proposal—but not to file an individual suit. (Choice B) Named class representatives and unnamed class members alike are bound by a court-approved settlement except for those members who opted out of the class action (not seen here). (Choice C) The fact that the court certified the class action allowed the class action to proceed in federal court. However, it does not provide a basis for dismissing the woman's suit. Educational objective: A class member must opt out of a \"common question\" class action to pursue an individual suit involving the same claim. Otherwise, the class member is bound by any final judgment or court-approved settlement, compromise, or voluntary dismissal of the class action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2) (notice requirements for \"common question\" class action).\n\n• Matsushita Elec. Indus. Co., Ltd. v. Epstein, 516 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1996) (explaining that class members who fail to opt out of a class action are bound by a resulting court-approved settlement agreement).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2) (notice requirements for \"common question\" class action).\n\n• Matsushita Elec. Indus. Co., Ltd. v. Epstein, 516 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1996) (explaining that class members who fail to opt out of a class action are bound by a resulting court-approved settlement agreement).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[709],"sourceAnswerPages":[710,711],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子家中的冰箱线路打出火花并引发火灾，致使房屋烧毁。一周后，该女子收到充分通知，得知有人已在联邦法院针对冰箱制造商提起集团诉讼，就冰箱线路系统缺陷造成的损害请求赔偿。女子收到通知后未采取任何行动，也未被列为集团代表。法院认定共同的法律和事实问题占主导地位，而且集团诉讼是公平、高效裁判该争议的最佳方法，因而批准了该集团诉讼。在证据开示期间，具名集团代表与制造商达成一项拟议和解。在听证会上，女子以和解金额不足以弥补其房屋损失为由反对该拟议和解。尽管她提出反对，法院仍在认定该和解公平、合理且充分后批准了和解。随后，女子在另一家联邦法院起诉制造商，要求赔偿其房屋尚未获赔的损失。制造商申请驳回该诉讼。法院是否可能批准制造商的申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为女子反对了拟议的集团诉讼和解。","B":"不会，因为女子不是该集团诉讼中的具名代表。","C":"会，因为法院已经批准该集团诉讼。","D":"会，因为女子未选择退出该集团诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"《联邦民事诉讼规则》23承认三类集团诉讼：损害风险型、最终衡平救济型以及共同问题型。本题中，法院批准（即准许）针对制造商的“共同问题型”集团诉讼，因为法院认定：(1)共同的法律和事实问题占主导地位；且(2)集团诉讼是公平、高效裁判该争议的最佳方法。\n\n在“共同问题型”集团诉讼中，必须向所有集团成员提供充分通知，因为这些成员有权选择退出集团诉讼并以自己的名义起诉。* 集团成员如果未选择退出，就不能就同一请求另行提起个人诉讼。相反，该成员会受由此产生的任何终局判决，或者经法院批准的和解、妥协或自愿撤诉所约束。\n\n本题中，女子在收到集团诉讼的充分通知后未采取任何行动，因此没有选择退出。这意味着她受法院批准的和解约束，并被禁止对制造商另行提起个人诉讼。因此，法院很可能会批准制造商提出的驳回申请。\n\n*在“损害风险型”和“最终衡平救济型”集团诉讼中，因为集团成员无权退出，所以不要求通知。但法院有权酌情向其提供通知以及退出该类集团诉讼的机会。\n\n（选项A）集团成员反对拟议和解，表明其利益与集团利益存在分歧。因此，若法院批准该方案，提出反对可使该集团成员有权上诉，但不使其有权另行提起个人诉讼。\n\n（选项B）除选择退出集团诉讼的成员外（本题并非如此），具名集团代表与未具名集团成员均受法院批准的和解约束。\n\n（选项C）法院批准集团诉讼这一事实使该集团诉讼得以在联邦法院继续进行，但它本身并不构成驳回该女子诉讼的依据。\n\n学习目标：集团成员若要就同一请求另行提起个人诉讼，必须选择退出“共同问题型”集团诉讼。否则，该成员将受集团诉讼的任何终局判决或者法院批准的和解、妥协或自愿撤诉所约束。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(c)(2)（“共同问题型”集团诉讼的通知要求）。\n• Matsushita Elec. Indus. Co., Ltd. v. Epstein, 516 U.S. 367, 378–79 (1996)（说明未选择退出集团诉讼的集团成员受由此产生的法院批准的和解协议约束）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-077"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-236","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":236,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A company sued a competitor in federal court, alleging that the competitor stole the company's trade secrets regarding its 3D printing technology. The company is concerned that discovery will lead to disclosure of its other trade secrets. What is the most efficient strategy for the company to protect these trade secrets from disclosure?","choices":{"A":"Assert that its trade secrets are privileged.","B":"File a motion for an ex parte temporary restraining order.","C":"Obtain a stipulation that limits the scope of discovery.","D":"Withhold information relating to its trade secrets."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Discovery stipulations\n\n(FRCP 29)\n\nDepositions Parties may stipulate that depositions be taken:\n\n• before any person\n• at any time or place\n• on any notice\n• in any manner Other discovery procedures Parties may stipulate to modifications:\n\n• limiting scope of discovery\n• expanding limits set by other rules\n• extending time to complete particular requests* FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Court approval required if extension would interfere with deadlines for completing all discovery, hearing a motion, or trial.\n\nParties can use various methods of discovery to obtain nonprivileged information that is relevant and proportional to the needs of the case. The broad scope of discovery requires disclosure of evidence that could be used at trial as well as information that may lead to the discovery of such evidence. As a result, it is possible that discovery could lead to the disclosure of the company's other trade secrets. To protect that information from disclosure, the company could confer with the competitor to reach an agreement that modifies discovery rules and procedures. FRCP 29 allows the parties to stipulate to modifications that:\n• expand the limits imposed by rules governing discovery (eg, increase the number of interrogatories above 25)\n\n• extend the time to complete particular discovery requests (eg, allow more than 30 days to respond to a request for production) or\n\n• limit the scope of discovery (eg, bar discovery of certain materials—as the company seeks to do here).\n\nA stipulation would be more efficient and less expensive than pursuing a motion to obtain a court order to modify the scope of discovery. Therefore, a stipulation is the most efficient strategy to prevent disclosure of the company's other trade secrets.\n\n(Choice A) Trade secrets are not privileged, so this assertion could not be used to protect that information. (Choice B) The company could file a motion for an ex parte temporary restraining order (TRO) to protect its other trade secrets from disclosure. But that would not be the most efficient strategy since an ex parte TRO generally remains in effect for only 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first.* *An ex parte TRO automatically remains in effect for 14 days unless that time period is (1) shortened by the court or (2) extended by the court for good cause or based on the adverse party's consent. (Choice D) Since the company's other trade secrets are likely discoverable, they cannot be withheld without a stipulation or court order that limits the scope of discovery. Educational objective: Discovery stipulations between the parties are an efficient way to modify discovery rules and procedures. They can be used to (1) expand the limits imposed by discovery rules, (2) extend the time to complete particular discovery requests, or (3) limit the scope of discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 29 (discovery stipulations).\n\n• 23 Am. Jur. 2d Depositions and Discovery § 10 (2019) (explaining stipulations regarding discovery).","rule":"Discovery stipulations between the parties are an efficient way to modify discovery rules and procedures. They can be used to (1) expand the limits imposed by discovery rules, (2) extend the time to complete particular discovery requests, or (3) limit the scope of discovery.","irac":{"issue":"A company sued a competitor in federal court, alleging that the competitor stole the company's trade secrets regarding its 3D printing technology. The company is concerned that discovery will lead to disclosure of its other trade secrets. What is the most efficient strategy for the company to protect these trade secrets from disclosure?","rule":"Discovery stipulations between the parties are an efficient way to modify discovery rules and procedures. They can be used to (1) expand the limits imposed by discovery rules, (2) extend the time to complete particular discovery requests, or (3) limit the scope of discovery.","analysis":"Discovery stipulations\n\n(FRCP 29)\n\nDepositions Parties may stipulate that depositions be taken:\n\n• before any person\n• at any time or place\n• on any notice\n• in any manner Other discovery procedures Parties may stipulate to modifications:\n\n• limiting scope of discovery\n• expanding limits set by other rules\n• extending time to complete particular requests* FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Court approval required if extension would interfere with deadlines for completing all discovery, hearing a motion, or trial.\n\nParties can use various methods of discovery to obtain nonprivileged information that is relevant and proportional to the needs of the case. The broad scope of discovery requires disclosure of evidence that could be used at trial as well as information that may lead to the discovery of such evidence. As a result, it is possible that discovery could lead to the disclosure of the company's other trade secrets. To protect that information from disclosure, the company could confer with the competitor to reach an agreement that modifies discovery rules and procedures. FRCP 29 allows the parties to stipulate to modifications that:\n• expand the limits imposed by rules governing discovery (eg, increase the number of interrogatories above 25)\n\n• extend the time to complete particular discovery requests (eg, allow more than 30 days to respond to a request for production) or\n\n• limit the scope of discovery (eg, bar discovery of certain materials—as the company seeks to do here).\n\nA stipulation would be more efficient and less expensive than pursuing a motion to obtain a court order to modify the scope of discovery. Therefore, a stipulation is the most efficient strategy to prevent disclosure of the company's other trade secrets.\n\n(Choice A) Trade secrets are not privileged, so this assertion could not be used to protect that information. (Choice B) The company could file a motion for an ex parte temporary restraining order (TRO) to protect its other trade secrets from disclosure. But that would not be the most efficient strategy since an ex parte TRO generally remains in effect for only 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first.* *An ex parte TRO automatically remains in effect for 14 days unless that time period is (1) shortened by the court or (2) extended by the court for good cause or based on the adverse party's consent. (Choice D) Since the company's other trade secrets are likely discoverable, they cannot be withheld without a stipulation or court order that limits the scope of discovery. Educational objective: Discovery stipulations between the parties are an efficient way to modify discovery rules and procedures. They can be used to (1) expand the limits imposed by discovery rules, (2) extend the time to complete particular discovery requests, or (3) limit the scope of discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 29 (discovery stipulations).\n\n• 23 Am. Jur. 2d Depositions and Discovery § 10 (2019) (explaining stipulations regarding discovery).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 29 (discovery stipulations).\n\n• 23 Am. Jur. 2d Depositions and Discovery § 10 (2019) (explaining stipulations regarding discovery).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[712],"sourceAnswerPages":[713,714],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家公司在联邦法院起诉一家竞争对手，指称该竞争对手窃取了公司有关3D打印技术的商业秘密。公司担心证据开示会导致其其他商业秘密被披露。公司为防止这些商业秘密被披露，最高效的策略是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"主张其商业秘密受特权保护。","B":"申请单方临时限制令。","C":"取得一项限制证据开示范围的约定。","D":"扣留与其商业秘密有关的信息。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示约定\n\n（FRCP 29）\n\n证言录取\n当事人可以约定，证言录取可以：\n• 在任何人面前进行；\n• 在任何时间或地点进行；\n• 依任何通知进行；\n• 以任何方式进行。\n\n其他证据开示程序\n当事人可以约定作出以下变更：\n• 限制证据开示范围；\n• 扩大其他规则规定的限额；\n• 延长完成特定请求的时间。*\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*如果延长期限会影响完成全部证据开示、审理动议或进行审判的期限，则须经法院批准。\n\n当事人可运用各种证据开示方法，取得与案件需要相关且相称的非特权信息。证据开示范围广泛，既要求披露可能在审判中使用的证据，也要求披露可能引出该等证据的信息。因此，证据开示确有可能导致公司的其他商业秘密被披露。\n\n为保护该信息不被披露，公司可以与竞争对手协商，达成一项修改证据开示规则和程序的协议。《联邦民事诉讼规则》29允许当事人约定作出下列变更：\n• 扩大证据开示规则设定的限额（例如把书面询问的数量提高到25项以上）；\n• 延长完成特定证据开示请求的时间（例如允许用30天以上答复文件提供请求）；或者\n• 限制证据开示范围（例如禁止对特定材料进行证据开示——这正是本公司所寻求的）。\n\n与通过动议取得法院命令来修改证据开示范围相比，约定的效率更高且费用更低。因此，约定是防止公司其他商业秘密被披露的最高效策略。\n\n（选项A）商业秘密不受特权保护，因此不能通过该主张保护相关信息。\n\n（选项B）公司可以申请单方临时限制令（TRO），以防止其其他商业秘密被披露。但这并非最高效的策略，因为单方TRO通常只维持14天，或者维持到举行初步禁令听证会为止，以先发生者为准。*\n\n*单方TRO自动维持14天，除非该期限：(1)由法院缩短；或(2)由法院基于正当理由延长，或经对方同意而延长。\n\n（选项D）公司的其他商业秘密很可能属于可开示范围，因此若无一项限制证据开示范围的约定或法院命令，公司不能扣留这些信息。\n\n学习目标：当事人之间的证据开示约定是修改证据开示规则和程序的一种高效方法。约定可用于：(1)扩大证据开示规则设定的限额；(2)延长完成特定证据开示请求的时间；或(3)限制证据开示范围。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 29（证据开示约定）。\n• 23 Am. Jur. 2d Depositions and Discovery § 10 (2019)（说明有关证据开示的约定）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-237","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":237,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An employee from State A sued his former company in a federal court in State A for discrimination in violation of federal employment law. The employee served an officer of the company with a subpoena to attend a deposition in the city where the employee now resides. The city is 175 miles away from the company's headquarters in State B and 200 miles away from where the officer resides in State A. The officer performs all her employment duties at the company's headquarters. Must the officer comply with the subpoena?","choices":{"A":"No, because the city is located 200 miles away from the officer's residence.","B":"No, because the city is located in a different state than the company's headquarters.","C":"Yes, because the city is located 175 miles away from the company's headquarters.","D":"Yes, because the city is located in the state where the officer resides."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Place of compliance for subpoenas\n\n(FRCP 45(c))\n\nTrial, hearing, Subpoena may command person's attendance: or deposition\n• within 100 miles of where person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business or\n• within state where person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business when person is: – party or party's officer or – commanded to attend trial & would not incur substantial expense Other discovery Subpoena may command:\n\n• production of documents, tangible things, or ESI within 100 miles of where person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business and\n• inspection of premises at place to be inspected FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; ESI = electronically stored information\n\nA subpoena is a written court order that commands a person to attend a trial, hearing, or deposition OR to produce documents or things in his/her possession. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 45(c) limits a subpoena's reach, so it may command attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition:\n• within 100 miles of where the subpoenaed person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business or\n• within the state where the subpoenaed person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business if he/she is a party or a party's officer (or a person commanded to attend trial who would not incur substantial expense). Here, the subpoena commanded an officer of the company (defendant) to attend a deposition in the State A city where the employee resides. The city is 175 miles away from where the officer regularly transacts business (the company's headquarters) and 200 miles away from the officer's State A residence. But since the city where the deposition will occur is in the state where the officer resides, the subpoena may command her attendance at the deposition (Choices A & C). Therefore, the officer must comply with the subpoena. (Choice B) The city where the deposition will occur is located in a different state (State A) than the company's headquarters (State B)—where the officer performs all her\n\nemployment duties. However, the subpoena can still command the officer's attendance since she resides in State A. Educational objective: A subpoena can compel attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition within 100 miles of where the subpoenaed person resides, works, or transacts business. It can also compel attendance of the parties and their officers—and persons commanded to attend trial who would not incur substantial expense—within the state where the person resides, works, or transacts business. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(c) (place of compliance for subpoena).","rule":"A subpoena can compel attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition within 100 miles of where the subpoenaed person resides, works, or transacts business. It can also compel attendance of the parties and their officers—and persons commanded to attend trial who would not incur substantial expense—within the state where the person resides, works, or transacts business.","irac":{"issue":"An employee from State A sued his former company in a federal court in State A for discrimination in violation of federal employment law. The employee served an officer of the company with a subpoena to attend a deposition in the city where the employee now resides. The city is 175 miles away from the company's headquarters in State B and 200 miles away from where the officer resides in State A. The officer performs all her employment duties at the company's headquarters. Must the officer comply with the subpoena?","rule":"A subpoena can compel attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition within 100 miles of where the subpoenaed person resides, works, or transacts business. It can also compel attendance of the parties and their officers—and persons commanded to attend trial who would not incur substantial expense—within the state where the person resides, works, or transacts business.","analysis":"Place of compliance for subpoenas\n\n(FRCP 45(c))\n\nTrial, hearing, Subpoena may command person's attendance: or deposition\n• within 100 miles of where person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business or\n• within state where person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business when person is: – party or party's officer or – commanded to attend trial & would not incur substantial expense Other discovery Subpoena may command:\n\n• production of documents, tangible things, or ESI within 100 miles of where person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business and\n• inspection of premises at place to be inspected FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; ESI = electronically stored information\n\nA subpoena is a written court order that commands a person to attend a trial, hearing, or deposition OR to produce documents or things in his/her possession. Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 45(c) limits a subpoena's reach, so it may command attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition:\n• within 100 miles of where the subpoenaed person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business or\n• within the state where the subpoenaed person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business if he/she is a party or a party's officer (or a person commanded to attend trial who would not incur substantial expense). Here, the subpoena commanded an officer of the company (defendant) to attend a deposition in the State A city where the employee resides. The city is 175 miles away from where the officer regularly transacts business (the company's headquarters) and 200 miles away from the officer's State A residence. But since the city where the deposition will occur is in the state where the officer resides, the subpoena may command her attendance at the deposition (Choices A & C). Therefore, the officer must comply with the subpoena. (Choice B) The city where the deposition will occur is located in a different state (State A) than the company's headquarters (State B)—where the officer performs all her\n\nemployment duties. However, the subpoena can still command the officer's attendance since she resides in State A. Educational objective: A subpoena can compel attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition within 100 miles of where the subpoenaed person resides, works, or transacts business. It can also compel attendance of the parties and their officers—and persons commanded to attend trial who would not incur substantial expense—within the state where the person resides, works, or transacts business. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(c) (place of compliance for subpoena).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(c) (place of compliance for subpoena).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[715],"sourceAnswerPages":[716,717],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的雇员以违反联邦就业法的歧视为由，在A州联邦法院起诉其前公司。雇员向该公司的一名高级职员送达传票，命令她到雇员现居住的城市参加证言录取。该城市距公司位于B州的总部175英里，距该高级职员在A州的住所200英里。该高级职员的全部工作职责均在公司总部履行。该高级职员是否必须遵守传票？","choicesZh":{"A":"不必，因为该城市距该高级职员住所200英里。","B":"不必，因为该城市与公司总部位于不同州。","C":"必须，因为该城市距公司总部175英里。","D":"必须，因为该城市位于该高级职员居住的州。"},"explanationZh":"传票的履行地点\n\n（FRCP 45(c)）\n\n审判、听证会或证言录取\n传票可以命令某人在下列地点出席：\n• 距该人居住地、工作地或通常开展业务地100英里以内；或者\n• 位于该人居住、工作或通常开展业务之州内，但须该人是：\n  – 当事人或当事人的高级职员；或者\n  – 被命令出席审判且不会因此承担巨额费用的人。\n\n其他证据开示\n传票可以命令：\n• 在距该人居住地、工作地或通常开展业务地100英里以内提供文件、有形物或电子存储信息；以及\n• 在待检查场所进行现场检查。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》；ESI＝电子存储信息。\n\n传票是一项书面法院命令，要求某人出席审判、听证会或证言录取，或者提供其持有的文件或物品。《联邦民事诉讼规则》45(c)限制传票的效力范围。因此，传票可以命令某人在以下地点出席审判、听证会或证言录取：\n• 距被传唤人居住地、工作地或通常开展业务地100英里以内；或者\n• 位于被传唤人居住、工作或通常开展业务之州内，但该人须为当事人或当事人的高级职员（或者是被命令出席审判且不会因此承担巨额费用的人）。\n\n本题中，传票命令被告公司的高级职员到雇员居住的A州城市参加证言录取。该城市距高级职员通常开展业务的地点（公司总部）175英里，距其位于A州的住所200英里。但是，因为进行证言录取的城市位于该高级职员居住的州，所以传票可以命令她参加该证言录取（选项A和C）。因此，该高级职员必须遵守传票。\n\n（选项B）进行证言录取的城市位于A州，与公司总部所在的B州不同；该高级职员的全部工作职责均在公司总部履行。不过，由于她居住在A州，传票仍可以命令她出席。\n\n学习目标：传票可以强制某人在距其居住地、工作地或开展业务地100英里以内出席审判、听证会或证言录取。传票也可以在该人居住、工作或开展业务所在州内，强制当事人及其高级职员出席；对于被命令出席审判且不会因此承担巨额费用的人，也同样如此。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(c)（传票的履行地点）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-238","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":238,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A symphony was scheduled to perform a famous songwriter's music at a concert in 10 days. After a disagreement with the symphony's conductor, the songwriter told the conductor that he could no longer use her songs. The conductor stated that he was going to proceed with the concert as planned. The songwriter filed a verified complaint against the conductor and the symphony in a federal district court for copyright infringement. The songwriter also moved for a temporary restraining order (TRO) to stop the concert from proceeding. The songwriter did not provide notice to the defendants but argued that the court should immediately issue an ex parte TRO. How will the federal district court likely rule on the songwriter's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because notice must be given to an opposing party before a court may issue a TRO.","B":"Deny the motion, because the songwriter's attorney failed to certify in writing why the defendants need not be notified.","C":"Grant the motion, because an ex parte TRO is justified under the circumstances.","D":"Grant the motion, because federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over claims based on copyright infringement."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Preliminary relief\n\n(FRCP 65)\n\nType Required contents Effect\n\nPreliminary Notice of • State reasons Persons bound upon receiving injunction hearing issued actual notice of order:\n• Reasonably Temporary • parties to the case describe acts restraining • parties' officers, agents, restrained or order employees & attorneys required\n• anyone in active\n• Specify terms concert/participation Ex parte Above requirements plus: with above persons (no • date & hour issued notice) • why injury is irreparable\n• why issued without notice\n• promptly filed with clerk & entered into record FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that commands a party to do or stop doing a specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. This preserves the parties' status quo until the court can fully consider the issue at a hearing. Unlike a preliminary injunction, a TRO may be issued without notice to the nonmovant (ie, ex parte). But to prevent abuse and protect the rights of all involved, a federal court can issue an ex parte TRO only if:\n• the movant files an affidavit or verified complaint containing specific facts showing that he/she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm before the nonmovant can be heard and\n\n• the movant's attorney certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and the reasons why notice should not be required (Choice A).*\n\nHere, even if the songwriter's verified complaint specifically shows that she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm, the songwriter's attorney failed to certify in writing why\n\nthe defendants need not be notified. As a result, an ex parte TRO is not justified under the circumstances, and the court will likely deny the songwriter's motion (Choice C). *Additionally, a federal court will issue a preliminary injunction or TRO only if the movant provides security (eg, a bond) sufficient to pay the costs and damages if the nonmovant is improperly restrained. (Choice D) Although federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over copyright infringement claims, this does not provide a basis to grant a TRO. Educational objective: A temporary restraining order can be issued without notice only if (1) the movant files an affidavit or verified complaint that specifically shows he/she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm and (2) the movant's attorney certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and why notice is not required. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b) (requirements for an ex parte TRO).\n\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2952 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the process for issuing an ex parte TRO).","rule":"A temporary restraining order can be issued without notice only if (1) the movant files an affidavit or verified complaint that specifically shows he/she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm and (2) the movant's attorney certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and why notice is not required.","irac":{"issue":"A symphony was scheduled to perform a famous songwriter's music at a concert in 10 days. After a disagreement with the symphony's conductor, the songwriter told the conductor that he could no longer use her songs. The conductor stated that he was going to proceed with the concert as planned. The songwriter filed a verified complaint against the conductor and the symphony in a federal district court for copyright infringement. The songwriter also moved for a temporary restraining order (TRO) to stop the concert from proceeding. The songwriter did not provide notice to the defendants but argued that the court should immediately issue an ex parte TRO. How will the federal district court likely rule on the songwriter's motion?","rule":"A temporary restraining order can be issued without notice only if (1) the movant files an affidavit or verified complaint that specifically shows he/she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm and (2) the movant's attorney certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and why notice is not required.","analysis":"Preliminary relief\n\n(FRCP 65)\n\nType Required contents Effect\n\nPreliminary Notice of • State reasons Persons bound upon receiving injunction hearing issued actual notice of order:\n• Reasonably Temporary • parties to the case describe acts restraining • parties' officers, agents, restrained or order employees & attorneys required\n• anyone in active\n• Specify terms concert/participation Ex parte Above requirements plus: with above persons (no • date & hour issued notice) • why injury is irreparable\n• why issued without notice\n• promptly filed with clerk & entered into record FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that commands a party to do or stop doing a specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. This preserves the parties' status quo until the court can fully consider the issue at a hearing. Unlike a preliminary injunction, a TRO may be issued without notice to the nonmovant (ie, ex parte). But to prevent abuse and protect the rights of all involved, a federal court can issue an ex parte TRO only if:\n• the movant files an affidavit or verified complaint containing specific facts showing that he/she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm before the nonmovant can be heard and\n\n• the movant's attorney certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and the reasons why notice should not be required (Choice A).*\n\nHere, even if the songwriter's verified complaint specifically shows that she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm, the songwriter's attorney failed to certify in writing why\n\nthe defendants need not be notified. As a result, an ex parte TRO is not justified under the circumstances, and the court will likely deny the songwriter's motion (Choice C). *Additionally, a federal court will issue a preliminary injunction or TRO only if the movant provides security (eg, a bond) sufficient to pay the costs and damages if the nonmovant is improperly restrained. (Choice D) Although federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over copyright infringement claims, this does not provide a basis to grant a TRO. Educational objective: A temporary restraining order can be issued without notice only if (1) the movant files an affidavit or verified complaint that specifically shows he/she will suffer immediate and irreparable harm and (2) the movant's attorney certifies in writing any efforts made to give notice and why notice is not required. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b) (requirements for an ex parte TRO).\n\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2952 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the process for issuing an ex parte TRO).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b) (requirements for an ex parte TRO).\n\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2952 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the process for issuing an ex parte TRO).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[718],"sourceAnswerPages":[719,720],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"某交响乐团原定于10天后在音乐会上演奏一位著名词曲作者的音乐。该词曲作者与乐团指挥发生争执后，告诉指挥不得再使用她的歌曲。指挥表示仍将按计划举行音乐会。词曲作者在联邦地区法院对指挥和交响乐团提起版权侵权诉讼，并提交经核实的起诉状。她还申请临时限制令（TRO），以阻止音乐会举行。词曲作者没有向被告提供通知，而是主张法院应立即发布单方TRO。联邦地区法院很可能如何裁定该申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回申请，因为法院发布TRO前必须通知对方当事人。","B":"驳回申请，因为词曲作者的律师没有以书面形式证明为何无须通知被告。","C":"批准申请，因为在本案情形下发布单方TRO具有正当理由。","D":"批准申请，因为联邦法院对基于版权侵权的请求享有专属管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"初步救济\n\n（FRCP 65）\n\n类型｜必备内容｜效力\n\n初步禁令\n• 发出听证会通知。\n\n临时限制令\n• 说明发布命令的理由；\n• 合理具体地说明被限制或被要求实施的行为；\n• 明确命令条款。\n\n收到命令的实际通知后，下列人员受其约束：\n• 案件当事人；\n• 当事人的高级职员、代理人、雇员和律师；\n• 与上述人员积极协同行动或参与的任何人。\n\n单方命令（无通知）\n除上述要求外，还须载明：\n• 发布日期和时间；\n• 为何损害不可弥补；\n• 为何在无通知情况下发布；\n• 并须及时提交书记员并记入案卷。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n临时限制令（TRO）是法院命令，要求一方在14天内，或在能够举行初步禁令听证会之前（以先发生者为准），实施或停止实施特定行为。这可以维持双方现状，直到法院能在听证会上充分审议该问题。与初步禁令不同，TRO可以在不通知非申请方的情况下发布（即单方发布）。但是，为防止滥用并保护所有相关人员的权利，联邦法院只有在符合下列条件时才能发布单方TRO：\n• 申请人提交宣誓书或经核实的起诉状，其中载有具体事实，表明在非申请方有机会陈述意见前，申请人将遭受即时且不可弥补的损害；并且\n• 申请人的律师以书面形式证明已经作出的通知努力，以及为何不应要求通知（选项A）。*\n\n本题中，即使词曲作者经核实的起诉状具体表明她将遭受即时且不可弥补的损害，其律师也没有以书面形式证明为何无须通知被告。因此，本案情形不足以支持单方TRO，法院很可能驳回词曲作者的申请（选项C）。\n\n*此外，联邦法院只有在申请人提供足以支付非申请方被不当限制时所产生费用和损害的担保（例如保证金）后，才会发布初步禁令或TRO。\n\n（选项D）虽然联邦法院对版权侵权请求享有专属管辖权，但这并不构成批准TRO的依据。\n\n学习目标：只有在下列情况下，法院才能在无通知时发布临时限制令：(1)申请人提交宣誓书或经核实的起诉状，具体表明其将遭受即时且不可弥补的损害；并且(2)申请人的律师以书面形式证明已经作出的通知努力以及为何无须通知。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b)（单方TRO的要求）。\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2952 (3d ed. 2020)（说明单方TRO的发布程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-239","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":239,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A plaintiff from State A sued a defendant from State B in a federal court in State B. The plaintiff claims that the defendant breached a provision of their contract, which resulted in $100,000 in damages. The defendant filed and served an answer denying liability. Three months later, the plaintiff stopped performing his duties under the contract. The defendant would now like to assert a claim against the plaintiff for breach of contract for $80,000. What is the best way for the defendant to pursue her claim?","choices":{"A":"File a new suit in the same court.","B":"File a reply.","C":"File an amended answer as a matter of course.","D":"Move for leave to file a supplemental answer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Amended v. Supplemental pleading\n\n(FRCP 15)\n\nAmended pleading • Asserts transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before pleading to be amended was filed\n• Amend by right, with opposing party's written consent, or by leave of court\n• Supersedes pleading to be amended Supplemental • Asserts transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after pleading pleading to be supplemented was filed\n• Supplement by leave of court\n• Does not supersede pleading to be supplemented FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\nParties can modify their pleadings in two ways:\n• Amended pleadings – to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed\n\n• Supplemental pleadings – to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed\n\nHere, the plaintiff allegedly breached his contractual duties three months after the defendant filed and served her answer. Therefore, the best way for the defendant to pursue a counterclaim for breach of contract is to move for leave (ie, permission) to file a supplemental answer that sets forth that claim. The court will then determine whether to allow the supplemental pleading. (Choice A) The defendant could file a new suit against the plaintiff in the same court, but this would likely be more time-consuming and expensive than filing a supplemental answer in the pending action. Therefore, this is not the best way for the defendant to pursue her breach-of-contract claim. (Choice B) Filing a reply is not the best way for the defendant to assert her counterclaim against the plaintiff. That is because a reply is typically a response made by the plaintiff to the defendant's answer and is permitted only when the court orders one (not seen here).* *A reply can also be a response by the defendant to a plaintiff's counterclaim answer, a third-party answer, or a crossclaim answer—none of which is seen here.\n\n(Choice C) An amended answer would have been proper had the defendant's breach-of-contract claim arisen before she filed her original answer. And even if it had, she could not have amended her answer as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the plaintiff's written consent. That is because more than 21 days have elapsed since her answer was served. Educational objective: An amended pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed. In contrast, a supplemental pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (amended and supplemental pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 669 (2019) (explaining the difference between an amended and supplemental pleading).","rule":"An amended pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed. In contrast, a supplemental pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff from State A sued a defendant from State B in a federal court in State B. The plaintiff claims that the defendant breached a provision of their contract, which resulted in $100,000 in damages. The defendant filed and served an answer denying liability. Three months later, the plaintiff stopped performing his duties under the contract. The defendant would now like to assert a claim against the plaintiff for breach of contract for $80,000. What is the best way for the defendant to pursue her claim?","rule":"An amended pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed. In contrast, a supplemental pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed.","analysis":"Amended v. Supplemental pleading\n\n(FRCP 15)\n\nAmended pleading • Asserts transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before pleading to be amended was filed\n• Amend by right, with opposing party's written consent, or by leave of court\n• Supersedes pleading to be amended Supplemental • Asserts transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after pleading pleading to be supplemented was filed\n• Supplement by leave of court\n• Does not supersede pleading to be supplemented FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\nParties can modify their pleadings in two ways:\n• Amended pleadings – to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed\n\n• Supplemental pleadings – to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed\n\nHere, the plaintiff allegedly breached his contractual duties three months after the defendant filed and served her answer. Therefore, the best way for the defendant to pursue a counterclaim for breach of contract is to move for leave (ie, permission) to file a supplemental answer that sets forth that claim. The court will then determine whether to allow the supplemental pleading. (Choice A) The defendant could file a new suit against the plaintiff in the same court, but this would likely be more time-consuming and expensive than filing a supplemental answer in the pending action. Therefore, this is not the best way for the defendant to pursue her breach-of-contract claim. (Choice B) Filing a reply is not the best way for the defendant to assert her counterclaim against the plaintiff. That is because a reply is typically a response made by the plaintiff to the defendant's answer and is permitted only when the court orders one (not seen here).* *A reply can also be a response by the defendant to a plaintiff's counterclaim answer, a third-party answer, or a crossclaim answer—none of which is seen here.\n\n(Choice C) An amended answer would have been proper had the defendant's breach-of-contract claim arisen before she filed her original answer. And even if it had, she could not have amended her answer as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the plaintiff's written consent. That is because more than 21 days have elapsed since her answer was served. Educational objective: An amended pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose before the pleading to be amended was filed. In contrast, a supplemental pleading is used to set forth a transaction, occurrence, or event that arose after the pleading to be supplemented was filed. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (amended and supplemental pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 669 (2019) (explaining the difference between an amended and supplemental pleading).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15 (amended and supplemental pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 669 (2019) (explaining the difference between an amended and supplemental pleading).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[721],"sourceAnswerPages":[722,723],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的原告在B州联邦法院起诉一名来自B州的被告。原告主张被告违反了双方合同中的一项条款，造成10万美元损害。被告提交并送达答辩状，否认责任。三个月后，原告停止履行其合同义务。被告现希望以违约为由向原告提出8万美元的请求。被告寻求该请求的最佳方式是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"在同一法院另行起诉。","B":"提交答复状。","C":"依当然权利提交修正答辩状。","D":"申请许可提交补充答辩状。"},"explanationZh":"修正诉状与补充诉状\n\n（FRCP 15）\n\n修正诉状\n• 主张在待修正诉状提交之前发生的交易、事件或事项；\n• 可依当然权利修正、经对方书面同意修正，或经法院许可修正；\n• 取代待修正的诉状。\n\n补充诉状\n• 主张在待补充诉状提交之后发生的交易、事件或事项；\n• 须经法院许可补充；\n• 不取代待补充的诉状。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n当事人可通过两种方式修改其诉状：\n• 修正诉状——用于陈述在待修正诉状提交之前发生的交易、事件或事项；\n• 补充诉状——用于陈述在待补充诉状提交之后发生的交易、事件或事项。\n\n本题中，原告被指在被告提交并送达答辩状三个月后才违反其合同义务。因此，被告寻求违约反请求的最佳方式，是申请许可（即请求法院准许）提交补充答辩状，在其中陈述该请求。随后，法院将决定是否准许该补充诉状。\n\n（选项A）被告可以在同一法院另行起诉原告，但与在待决诉讼中提交补充答辩状相比，这很可能更加耗时且费用更高。因此，这不是被告寻求其违约请求的最佳方式。\n\n（选项B）提交答复状不是被告对原告主张反请求的最佳方式。这是因为答复状通常是原告对被告答辩状所作的回应，而且只有法院下令时才允许提交（本题没有这种情况）。*\n\n*答复状也可以是被告对原告反请求答辩状、第三方答辩状或交叉请求答辩状所作的回应；本题均无这些情形。\n\n（选项C）如果被告的违约请求是在其提交原答辩状之前发生，提交修正答辩状才是适当的。即使如此，被告也不能依当然权利修正答辩状——即不能在未经法院许可或原告书面同意的情况下修正——因为其答辩状送达后已经超过21天。\n\n学习目标：修正诉状用于陈述在待修正诉状提交之前发生的交易、事件或事项。相反，补充诉状用于陈述在待补充诉状提交之后发生的交易、事件或事项。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15（修正与补充诉状）。\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 669 (2019)（说明修正诉状与补充诉状的区别）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-240","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":240,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A mail clerk domiciled in State A slipped and fell on ice that had formed near the loading dock of the building in State B where the clerk's State B employer leased space for its headquarters. The building was owned and operated by a State C corporation. As a result of the fall, the clerk was injured and the employer's expensive computer he was carrying was badly damaged. The clerk sued the building owner for negligence in a federal district court in State B, seeking $100,000 in personal-injury damages. The employer has filed a timely motion to intervene, asserting an $80,000 negligence claim against the building owner for the damage to its computer. Is the court likely to grant the employer's motion to intervene?","choices":{"A":"No, because although the employer has an interest in the clerk's action, that interest is not likely to be impaired in the employer's absence.","B":"No, because the clerk chose not to join the employer as a co-plaintiff in his action.","C":"Yes, because the employer is an indispensable party.","D":"Yes, because the employer's claim shares common questions of law and fact with the clerk's action."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Intervention\n\n(FRCP 24)\n\nIntervention of • On timely motion, court must permit intervention when either: right – nonparty's interest in case not adequately represented by existing parties & deciding case may impair nonparty's ability to protect that interest – federal statute gives unconditional right to intervene Permissive • On timely motion, court may permit intervention when either: intervention – nonparty's claim or defense shares common question of law or fact with main action – federal statute gives conditional right to intervene\n• Court must consider whether intervention will unduly delay or prejudice adjudication of original parties' rights FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA nonparty can join a case to protect his/her interest by timely filing a motion to intervene under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 24.* There are two types of intervention:\n• Intervention of right – the court must permit an intervention when (1) the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented by the existing parties and (2) deciding the case may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest.\n• Permissive intervention – the court may permit an intervention when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action. But a court may deny permissive intervention if it would unduly delay or prejudice the adjudication of the original parties' rights. Here, the employer timely moved to intervene in the clerk's action against the building owner. Although the employer's interest (property-damage claim) is inadequately represented by the existing parties, deciding the clerk's personal-injury action is not likely to impair the employer's ability to protect its interest (no intervention of right). But since both actions are based on the owner's alleged negligence (common questions of law and fact), the court will likely allow the employer to permissively intervene (Choice A). *To determine if a motion to intervene is timely filed, the court considers (1) the length of time the movant knew or should have known about its threatened interest, (2) the prejudice to the parties if intervention is permitted, and (3) the prejudice to the movant if intervention is denied.\n\n(Choice B) The clerk's decision to exclude the employer as a co-plaintiff does not affect the employer's ability to intervene. (Choice C) Under FRCP 19, an indispensable party must be joined in a lawsuit. But the employer is not an indispensable party because the employer's absence would not affect the court's ability to provide relief to the clerk or the employer's ability to protect its related property interest. Educational objective: Intervention must be permitted when the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and deciding the case may impair his/her ability to protect that interest (ie, intervention of right). And intervention may be permitted when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question with the main action (ie, permissive intervention). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 24 (intervention).","rule":"Intervention must be permitted when the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and deciding the case may impair his/her ability to protect that interest (ie, intervention of right). And intervention may be permitted when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question with the main action (ie, permissive intervention).","irac":{"issue":"A mail clerk domiciled in State A slipped and fell on ice that had formed near the loading dock of the building in State B where the clerk's State B employer leased space for its headquarters. The building was owned and operated by a State C corporation. As a result of the fall, the clerk was injured and the employer's expensive computer he was carrying was badly damaged. The clerk sued the building owner for negligence in a federal district court in State B, seeking $100,000 in personal-injury damages. The employer has filed a timely motion to intervene, asserting an $80,000 negligence claim against the building owner for the damage to its computer. Is the court likely to grant the employer's motion to intervene?","rule":"Intervention must be permitted when the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and deciding the case may impair his/her ability to protect that interest (ie, intervention of right). And intervention may be permitted when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question with the main action (ie, permissive intervention).","analysis":"Intervention\n\n(FRCP 24)\n\nIntervention of • On timely motion, court must permit intervention when either: right – nonparty's interest in case not adequately represented by existing parties & deciding case may impair nonparty's ability to protect that interest – federal statute gives unconditional right to intervene Permissive • On timely motion, court may permit intervention when either: intervention – nonparty's claim or defense shares common question of law or fact with main action – federal statute gives conditional right to intervene\n• Court must consider whether intervention will unduly delay or prejudice adjudication of original parties' rights FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA nonparty can join a case to protect his/her interest by timely filing a motion to intervene under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 24.* There are two types of intervention:\n• Intervention of right – the court must permit an intervention when (1) the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented by the existing parties and (2) deciding the case may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest.\n• Permissive intervention – the court may permit an intervention when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action. But a court may deny permissive intervention if it would unduly delay or prejudice the adjudication of the original parties' rights. Here, the employer timely moved to intervene in the clerk's action against the building owner. Although the employer's interest (property-damage claim) is inadequately represented by the existing parties, deciding the clerk's personal-injury action is not likely to impair the employer's ability to protect its interest (no intervention of right). But since both actions are based on the owner's alleged negligence (common questions of law and fact), the court will likely allow the employer to permissively intervene (Choice A). *To determine if a motion to intervene is timely filed, the court considers (1) the length of time the movant knew or should have known about its threatened interest, (2) the prejudice to the parties if intervention is permitted, and (3) the prejudice to the movant if intervention is denied.\n\n(Choice B) The clerk's decision to exclude the employer as a co-plaintiff does not affect the employer's ability to intervene. (Choice C) Under FRCP 19, an indispensable party must be joined in a lawsuit. But the employer is not an indispensable party because the employer's absence would not affect the court's ability to provide relief to the clerk or the employer's ability to protect its related property interest. Educational objective: Intervention must be permitted when the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and deciding the case may impair his/her ability to protect that interest (ie, intervention of right). And intervention may be permitted when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question with the main action (ie, permissive intervention). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 24 (intervention).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 24 (intervention).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[724],"sourceAnswerPages":[725,726],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名住所地在A州的邮件收发员在B州某建筑物的装卸区附近因冰面滑倒。其B州雇主在该建筑物内租赁场地作为总部。该建筑物由一家C州公司所有和运营。收发员因摔倒受伤，其当时携带的雇主昂贵电脑也严重损坏。收发员在B州联邦地区法院以过失为由起诉建筑物所有人，请求10万美元人身伤害赔偿。雇主及时申请参加诉讼，并就电脑损坏向建筑物所有人提出8万美元的过失请求。法院是否可能批准雇主的参加诉讼申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为尽管雇主在收发员的诉讼中具有利益，但在雇主缺席时，该利益不太可能受到损害。","B":"不会，因为收发员选择不把雇主作为共同原告加入其诉讼。","C":"会，因为雇主是不可或缺当事人。","D":"会，因为雇主的请求与收发员的诉讼具有共同的法律和事实问题。"},"explanationZh":"参加诉讼\n\n（FRCP 24）\n\n权利参加\n当及时提出申请时，法院在下列任一情形下必须准许参加：\n• 非当事人在案件中的利益未获现有当事人充分代表，而且案件裁判可能损害该非当事人保护其利益的能力；\n• 联邦制定法赋予无条件参加权。\n\n许可参加\n当及时提出申请时，法院在下列任一情形下可以准许参加：\n• 非当事人的请求或抗辩与本诉具有共同的法律或事实问题；\n• 联邦制定法赋予附条件参加权。\n• 法院必须考虑参加诉讼是否会对原当事人权利的裁判造成不当延误或损害。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n非当事人可以根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）24及时提交参加诉讼申请，以加入案件并保护其利益。* 参加诉讼分为两类：\n• 权利参加——如果(1)非当事人的利益未获现有当事人充分代表，且(2)案件裁判可能损害该非当事人保护其利益的能力，法院必须准许其参加。\n• 许可参加——如果非当事人的请求或抗辩与本诉具有共同的法律或事实问题，法院可以准许其参加。但是，如果参加诉讼会不当延误或损害对原当事人权利的裁判，法院可以拒绝许可参加。\n\n本题中，雇主及时申请参加收发员针对建筑物所有人提起的诉讼。尽管雇主的利益（财产损害请求）未获现有当事人充分代表，但裁判收发员的人身伤害诉讼不太可能损害雇主保护其利益的能力（因而不存在权利参加）。不过，由于两个诉讼均以所有人所称过失为依据，具有共同的法律和事实问题，法院很可能会允许雇主许可参加（选项A）。\n\n*判断参加诉讼申请是否及时提出时，法院会考虑：(1)申请人知道或应当知道其利益受到威胁已有多长时间；(2)准许参加会给当事人造成何种损害；以及(3)拒绝参加会给申请人造成何种损害。\n\n（选项B）收发员决定不把雇主列为共同原告，并不影响雇主参加诉讼的能力。\n\n（选项C）根据FRCP 19，不可或缺当事人必须加入诉讼。但雇主并非不可或缺当事人，因为雇主缺席既不会影响法院向收发员提供救济的能力，也不会影响雇主保护其相关财产利益的能力。\n\n学习目标：如果非当事人的利益未获充分代表，而且案件裁判可能损害其保护该利益的能力，则必须准许参加诉讼（即权利参加）。如果非当事人的请求或抗辩与本诉具有共同问题，则可以准许其参加诉讼（即许可参加）。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 24（参加诉讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-241","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":241,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"On March 1, 2017, a woman tripped and fell on a cruise ship, sustaining severe injuries. On February 28, 2018, she brought a diversity action in federal court against the entity she believed owned the cruise ship. The entity was served with process the same day. The entity immediately forwarded the complaint to the office of legal counsel for the corporation that owned the cruise ship. Two weeks later, the entity filed and served its answer in which it denied ownership of the cruise ship. The entity explained that it was merely a ticketing agency with a name similar to the corporation that owned the ship. The next day, the woman amended her complaint to substitute the corporation that owned the cruise ship as the defendant and had process served on the corporation. The corporation immediately moved to dismiss the suit, arguing that the statute of limitations had expired. The applicable statute of limitations for the claim is one year from the date the accident allegedly occurred. The law establishing this statute of limitations does not mention relation back. Should the court grant the corporation's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the amended complaint relates back to the date the original complaint was filed.","B":"No, because the woman may freely amend her complaint once as a matter of course.","C":"Yes, because the amended complaint was filed after the statute of limitations expired.","D":"Yes, because the applicable law does not mention relation back."},"answer":"A","explanation":"An amended complaint filed after the statute of limitations has expired is prohibited unless the relation-back doctrine applies. This doctrine treats the amended complaint as if it were filed on the same date as the original complaint. An amendment that changes a defendant \"relates back\" only if the statute of limitations allows (not seen here) OR when:\n• the amendment concerns the same transaction or occurrence as the original complaint\n\n• the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original complaint was filed and\n\n• the new party knew or should have known that the suit would have been brought against it but for a mistake concerning the proper party's identity.\n\nHere, the woman's amended complaint concerns the same cruise ship accident. The corporation that owns the cruise ship received notice when the ticketing agency forwarded the complaint. And the corporation should have known that the suit would have been brought against it but for the woman's mistake about its identity. As a result, the amendment relates back to the date the original complaint was filed on February 28, 2018—before the statute of limitations expired. Therefore, the corporation's motion to dismiss should be denied (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A party may amend its complaint once as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the opposing party's written consent—within 21 days after being served with an answer. But the amendment must be filed (or relate back to an original pleading filed) before the statute of limitations expires. Educational objective: An amended complaint will \"relate back\" to the date of the original complaint if (1) the same occurrence is at issue, (2) the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original complaint was filed, and (3) the new party knew or should have known the suit would have been brought but for a mistake about its identity. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).\n\n• Krupski v. Costa Crociere S.p.A, 560 U.S. 538, 557 (2010) (holding that the relation-back doctrine applies when a company should have known that it would have been sued had the plaintiff not made a mistake).","rule":"An amended complaint will \"relate back\" to the date of the original complaint if (1) the same occurrence is at issue, (2) the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original complaint was filed, and (3) the new party knew or should have known the suit would have been brought but for a mistake about its identity.","irac":{"issue":"On March 1, 2017, a woman tripped and fell on a cruise ship, sustaining severe injuries. On February 28, 2018, she brought a diversity action in federal court against the entity she believed owned the cruise ship. The entity was served with process the same day. The entity immediately forwarded the complaint to the office of legal counsel for the corporation that owned the cruise ship. Two weeks later, the entity filed and served its answer in which it denied ownership of the cruise ship. The entity explained that it was merely a ticketing agency with a name similar to the corporation that owned the ship. The next day, the woman amended her complaint to substitute the corporation that owned the cruise ship as the defendant and had process served on the corporation. The corporation immediately moved to dismiss the suit, arguing that the statute of limitations had expired. The applicable statute of limitations for the claim is one year from the date the accident allegedly occurred. The law establishing this statute of limitations does not mention relation back. Should the court grant the corporation's motion to dismiss?","rule":"An amended complaint will \"relate back\" to the date of the original complaint if (1) the same occurrence is at issue, (2) the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original complaint was filed, and (3) the new party knew or should have known the suit would have been brought but for a mistake about its identity.","analysis":"An amended complaint filed after the statute of limitations has expired is prohibited unless the relation-back doctrine applies. This doctrine treats the amended complaint as if it were filed on the same date as the original complaint. An amendment that changes a defendant \"relates back\" only if the statute of limitations allows (not seen here) OR when:\n• the amendment concerns the same transaction or occurrence as the original complaint\n\n• the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original complaint was filed and\n\n• the new party knew or should have known that the suit would have been brought against it but for a mistake concerning the proper party's identity.\n\nHere, the woman's amended complaint concerns the same cruise ship accident. The corporation that owns the cruise ship received notice when the ticketing agency forwarded the complaint. And the corporation should have known that the suit would have been brought against it but for the woman's mistake about its identity. As a result, the amendment relates back to the date the original complaint was filed on February 28, 2018—before the statute of limitations expired. Therefore, the corporation's motion to dismiss should be denied (Choices C & D). (Choice B) A party may amend its complaint once as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the opposing party's written consent—within 21 days after being served with an answer. But the amendment must be filed (or relate back to an original pleading filed) before the statute of limitations expires. Educational objective: An amended complaint will \"relate back\" to the date of the original complaint if (1) the same occurrence is at issue, (2) the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original complaint was filed, and (3) the new party knew or should have known the suit would have been brought but for a mistake about its identity. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).\n\n• Krupski v. Costa Crociere S.p.A, 560 U.S. 538, 557 (2010) (holding that the relation-back doctrine applies when a company should have known that it would have been sued had the plaintiff not made a mistake).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).\n\n• Krupski v. Costa Crociere S.p.A, 560 U.S. 538, 557 (2010) (holding that the relation-back doctrine applies when a company should have known that it would have been sued had the plaintiff not made a mistake).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[727],"sourceAnswerPages":[728],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"2017年3月1日，一名女子在一艘邮轮上绊倒并严重受伤。2018年2月28日，她在联邦法院对其认为是邮轮所有人的实体提起多元州籍诉讼。该实体于同日收到诉讼文书送达，并立即将起诉状转交给实际拥有邮轮之公司的法律顾问办公室。两周后，该实体提交并送达答辩状，否认其拥有该邮轮。该实体解释说，它只是一家票务代理机构，其名称与拥有该船的公司相似。次日，女子修正起诉状，把被告替换为实际拥有邮轮的公司，并向该公司送达诉讼文书。公司立即申请驳回诉讼，主张诉讼时效已经届满。该请求适用的诉讼时效为自所称事故发生之日起一年。设立该诉讼时效的法律没有提及追溯适用。法院是否应批准公司的驳回申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为修正起诉状追溯至原起诉状提交之日。","B":"不应，因为女子可以依当然权利自由修正一次起诉状。","C":"应当，因为修正起诉状是在诉讼时效届满后提交的。","D":"应当，因为适用法律没有提及追溯适用。"},"explanationZh":"诉讼时效届满后提交修正起诉状原则上受到禁止，除非追溯适用原则成立。根据该原则，修正起诉状视为在原起诉状提交之日提交。变更被告的修正只有在诉讼时效法允许时（本题没有），或者符合下列条件时，才“追溯适用”：\n• 修正涉及与原起诉状相同的交易或事件；\n• 新当事人在原起诉状提交后90天内收到诉讼通知；并且\n• 新当事人知道或应当知道，若非对正确当事人身份的误认，诉讼本应对其提起。\n\n本题中，女子的修正起诉状涉及同一起邮轮事故。拥有邮轮的公司在票务代理机构转交起诉状时收到了诉讼通知；公司也应当知道，若非女子误认其身份，诉讼本应对公司提起。因此，该修正追溯至原起诉状提交日，即2018年2月28日——当时诉讼时效尚未届满。因此，应驳回公司的驳回申请（选项C和D）。\n\n（选项B）一方可在答辩状送达后21天内，依当然权利修正一次起诉状——即无须法院许可或对方书面同意。但该修正必须在诉讼时效届满前提交，或追溯至在诉讼时效届满前提交的原诉状。\n\n学习目标：如果(1)争议涉及同一事件；(2)新当事人在原起诉状提交后90天内收到诉讼通知；且(3)新当事人知道或应当知道，若非对其身份的误认，诉讼本应对其提起，则修正起诉状将“追溯适用”至原起诉状提交之日。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c)（修正诉状的追溯适用）。\n• Krupski v. Costa Crociere S.p.A, 560 U.S. 538, 557 (2010)（认定若非原告发生误认，公司本应知道自己会被起诉时，追溯适用原则成立）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-065"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-242","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":242,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A franchisee sued a franchisor in a federal court in State A for breach of contract. The franchisee is domiciled in State B, and the franchisor's headquarters and principal place of business are in State A. The franchisee's complaint included one line about subject-matter jurisdiction that stated: \"The court has subject-matter jurisdiction over this suit.\" In response, the franchisor filed a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Upon reviewing the motion and the complaint, the judge issued an order to show cause to the franchisee's attorney to explain why the attorney should not be sanctioned for failing to adequately plead subject-matter jurisdiction. At the show-cause hearing, the franchisee's attorney arrived with an amended complaint to substitute for the original complaint. The judge accepted the amended complaint and denied the franchisor's motion to dismiss. The judge also imposed sanctions against the franchisee's attorney in the amount of $2,000, payable to the court. The case proceeded, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the franchisee, and the judge issued a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The franchisee's attorney then filed an appeal related to the court's imposition of sanctions. What is the appellate court's likely response?","choices":{"A":"Affirm the order imposing sanctions.","B":"Vacate the order imposing sanctions, because the judge accepted the amended complaint.","C":"Vacate the order imposing sanctions, because the judge acted sua sponte.","D":"Vacate the order imposing sanctions, because the judge failed to allow 21 days to pass before imposing sanctions."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Representations to court\n\n(FRCP 11(b))\n\nEvery pleading, motion, or other document filed must be signed by attorney (or unrepresented party), thereby certifying that:\n\n1. document is presented for proper purpose—not to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase cost of litigation 1. claims, defenses & legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law 2. factual assertions have or will have evidentiary support and 3. factual denials are warranted by evidence or reasonably based on belief/lack of information FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court can impose sanctions on a law firm (default), attorney, or party if the court finds that they violated one of the provisions of Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 11(b)— shown above. Sanction proceedings can be initiated by:\n• an opposing party's motion – which requires that party to serve the motion on the violator but refrain from filing it with the court for 21 days to allow the violation to be corrected (ie, safe-harbor rule) or\n• on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte) – which requires the judge to issue an order to show cause on the violator but does not require the judge to apply the safe-harbor rule. Here, the franchisee's complaint simply alleged that the federal court \"has subject-matter jurisdiction over this suit.\" The complaint did not include the grounds supporting this assertion as required by FRCP 8—eg, by providing the facts that established subject-matter jurisdiction. This means that the franchisee's jurisdictional claim was not warranted by law and violated FRCP 11(b)(2). As a result, the judge had the authority to initiate sanction proceedings sua sponte and impose sanctions after the show-cause hearing (Choices C & D). Such sanctions should be sufficient to deter the violator's conduct in the future—eg, a $2,000 fine payable to the court (as seen here). However, the sanctions order can be appealed. The appellate court will then review the sanctions order for abuse of discretion, thereby giving great deference to the trial court's ruling and only reversing that ruling if it was unreasonable or arbitrary. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm the order imposing sanctions.\n\n(Choice B) The judge's acceptance of the amended complaint does not negate the judge's power to sanction the attorney for his deficient original complaint. Educational objective: A court can impose sanctions for violating Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 11(b). Sanction proceedings can be initiated by (1) an opposing party, who must give the violator 21 days to correct the violation, or (2) the court, which need not give the violator time to do so. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c) (sanctions for violating this rule).\n\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmarx Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990) (establishing that abuse of discretion is appropriate appellate standard of review for Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 sanctions).","rule":"A court can impose sanctions for violating Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 11(b). Sanction proceedings can be initiated by (1) an opposing party, who must give the violator 21 days to correct the violation, or (2) the court, which need not give the violator time to do so.","irac":{"issue":"A franchisee sued a franchisor in a federal court in State A for breach of contract. The franchisee is domiciled in State B, and the franchisor's headquarters and principal place of business are in State A. The franchisee's complaint included one line about subject-matter jurisdiction that stated: \"The court has subject-matter jurisdiction over this suit.\" In response, the franchisor filed a motion to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Upon reviewing the motion and the complaint, the judge issued an order to show cause to the franchisee's attorney to explain why the attorney should not be sanctioned for failing to adequately plead subject-matter jurisdiction. At the show-cause hearing, the franchisee's attorney arrived with an amended complaint to substitute for the original complaint. The judge accepted the amended complaint and denied the franchisor's motion to dismiss. The judge also imposed sanctions against the franchisee's attorney in the amount of $2,000, payable to the court. The case proceeded, the jury returned a verdict in favor of the franchisee, and the judge issued a final judgment consistent with the verdict. The franchisee's attorney then filed an appeal related to the court's imposition of sanctions. What is the appellate court's likely response?","rule":"A court can impose sanctions for violating Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 11(b). Sanction proceedings can be initiated by (1) an opposing party, who must give the violator 21 days to correct the violation, or (2) the court, which need not give the violator time to do so.","analysis":"Representations to court\n\n(FRCP 11(b))\n\nEvery pleading, motion, or other document filed must be signed by attorney (or unrepresented party), thereby certifying that:\n\n1. document is presented for proper purpose—not to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase cost of litigation 1. claims, defenses & legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law 2. factual assertions have or will have evidentiary support and 3. factual denials are warranted by evidence or reasonably based on belief/lack of information FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA court can impose sanctions on a law firm (default), attorney, or party if the court finds that they violated one of the provisions of Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 11(b)— shown above. Sanction proceedings can be initiated by:\n• an opposing party's motion – which requires that party to serve the motion on the violator but refrain from filing it with the court for 21 days to allow the violation to be corrected (ie, safe-harbor rule) or\n• on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte) – which requires the judge to issue an order to show cause on the violator but does not require the judge to apply the safe-harbor rule. Here, the franchisee's complaint simply alleged that the federal court \"has subject-matter jurisdiction over this suit.\" The complaint did not include the grounds supporting this assertion as required by FRCP 8—eg, by providing the facts that established subject-matter jurisdiction. This means that the franchisee's jurisdictional claim was not warranted by law and violated FRCP 11(b)(2). As a result, the judge had the authority to initiate sanction proceedings sua sponte and impose sanctions after the show-cause hearing (Choices C & D). Such sanctions should be sufficient to deter the violator's conduct in the future—eg, a $2,000 fine payable to the court (as seen here). However, the sanctions order can be appealed. The appellate court will then review the sanctions order for abuse of discretion, thereby giving great deference to the trial court's ruling and only reversing that ruling if it was unreasonable or arbitrary. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm the order imposing sanctions.\n\n(Choice B) The judge's acceptance of the amended complaint does not negate the judge's power to sanction the attorney for his deficient original complaint. Educational objective: A court can impose sanctions for violating Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 11(b). Sanction proceedings can be initiated by (1) an opposing party, who must give the violator 21 days to correct the violation, or (2) the court, which need not give the violator time to do so. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c) (sanctions for violating this rule).\n\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmarx Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990) (establishing that abuse of discretion is appropriate appellate standard of review for Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 sanctions).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c) (sanctions for violating this rule).\n\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmarx Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990) (establishing that abuse of discretion is appropriate appellate standard of review for Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 sanctions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[730],"sourceAnswerPages":[731,732],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名特许经营商在A州联邦法院以违约为由起诉特许授权商。特许经营商的住所地在B州，特许授权商的总部和主要营业地均在A州。特许经营商的起诉状只用一行陈述事项管辖权：“法院对本诉讼具有事项管辖权。”作为回应，特许授权商以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法官审查该申请和起诉状后，向特许经营商的律师发出说明理由令，要求其解释为何不应因未充分陈述事项管辖权而受到制裁。在说明理由听证会上，特许经营商的律师带来一份修正起诉状，用于替代原起诉状。法官接受修正起诉状并驳回特许授权商的驳回申请。法官还对特许经营商的律师处以2,000美元制裁金，向法院缴纳。案件继续审理，陪审团作出有利于特许经营商的裁决，法官据此作出终局判决。随后，特许经营商的律师就法院施加制裁提出上诉。上诉法院最可能如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持施加制裁的命令。","B":"撤销施加制裁的命令，因为法官接受了修正起诉状。","C":"撤销施加制裁的命令，因为法官依职权采取了行动。","D":"撤销施加制裁的命令，因为法官在施加制裁前没有给予21天期限。"},"explanationZh":"向法院作出的保证\n\n（FRCP 11(b)）\n\n提交的每份诉状、动议或其他文件均须由律师（或无律师代理的当事人）签署，从而证明：\n1. 文件是为正当目的提出，而非为了骚扰、造成不必要延误或无谓增加诉讼费用；\n2. 请求、抗辩和法律主张有现行法律依据，或有不属无理取闹、旨在修改现行法律或创设新法的论证依据；\n3. 事实陈述已有或将会有证据支持；并且\n4. 对事实的否认有证据依据，或根据相信或缺乏信息而合理作出。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n如果法院认定律师事务所（原则上的受制裁对象）、律师或当事人违反《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）11(b)的任何规定——如上所示——法院可以施加制裁。制裁程序可以通过以下方式启动：\n• 对方当事人提出动议——该方必须向违规者送达动议，但在21天内不得向法院提交，以便违规者有机会纠正违规行为（即安全港规则）；或者\n• 法院依职权启动——法官须向违规者发出说明理由令，但无须适用安全港规则。\n\n本题中，特许经营商的起诉状只是声称联邦法院“对本诉讼具有事项管辖权”。起诉状没有按照FRCP 8的要求写明支持该主张的依据，例如说明构成事项管辖权的事实。这意味着该管辖权主张没有法律依据，违反FRCP 11(b)(2)。因此，法官有权依职权启动制裁程序，并在说明理由听证后施加制裁（选项C和D）。此类制裁应足以遏制违规者今后再为同类行为，例如向法院缴纳2,000美元罚款（本题即如此）。\n\n不过，制裁令可以上诉。上诉法院将依滥用裁量权标准审查制裁令，对初审法院的裁定给予高度尊重，只有在裁定不合理或恣意时才予以推翻。因此，上诉法院很可能维持施加制裁的命令。\n\n（选项B）法官接受修正起诉状，并不消除法官就律师原起诉状的缺陷对其施加制裁的权力。\n\n学习目标：法院可对违反《联邦民事诉讼规则》11(b)的行为施加制裁。制裁程序可由：(1)对方当事人启动，但其必须给予违规者21天纠正期限；或(2)法院启动，而法院无须给予违规者该纠正期限。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c)（违反本规则的制裁）。\n• Cooter & Gell v. Hartmarx Corp., 496 U.S. 384, 405 (1990)（确立滥用裁量权是审查Fed. R. Civ. P. 11制裁的适当上诉审查标准）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-243","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":243,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A tech company sued a software firm in a federal district court for allegedly violating federal antitrust laws. The tech company employs a developer who regularly provides expert testimony for the company in litigation. The developer is advising the company in its suit against the firm, but the company is not going to call the developer as an expert witness in this case. Instead, the company hired an economist to testify as an expert witness about the economic impact caused by the software firm's anticompetitive behavior. Whom must the tech company disclose to the software firm at least 90 days before trial without waiting for a discovery request?","choices":{"A":"Neither the economist nor the developer.","B":"Only the economist.","C":"Only the developer.","D":"Both the economist and the developer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nType Required disclosures Time to disclose\n\nInitial • Persons with relevant information • ≤ 14 days after initial disclosures* • Items in party's possession that planning conference support claim/defense • ≤ 30 days after post-• Computation of damages & conference service supporting material or joinder\n• Relevant insurance policies • As court orders or parties stipulate Expert • Identity of expert witnesses • ≥ 90 days before trial testimony • Expert's written report • ≤ 30 days after\n• If no report, subject matter & disclosure to rebut summary of testimony evidence\n• As court orders or parties stipulate Pretrial • Testifying witnesses • ≥ 30 days before trial disclosures* • Deposition testimony to be used • As court orders at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nParties must disclose certain information during discovery to facilitate litigation. Most of this information is disclosed in response to an opposing party's discovery requests. But three types of disclosures are required without such requests: initial disclosures, pretrial disclosures, and disclosure of expert witness testimony. This requires that the parties disclose the identity of all expert witnesses expected to testify at trial AND provide either:\n• a written report for any witness who was specially hired to provide expert testimony or who regularly gives expert testimony as part of his/her employment duties with the party or\n\n• a summary of the subject matter, facts, and opinions of the testimony for all other expert witnesses (eg, treating physicians).\n\nA party must disclose this information at least 90 days before trial unless otherwise ordered by the court or stipulated by the parties.* Here, the tech company hired the economist to testify at trial as an expert witness about the economic impact of the software firm's anticompetitive behavior. As a result, the company must disclose the economist's identity at least 90 days before trial without waiting for a discovery request (Choices A & C). A written report must also be included since the economist was specially hired to provide this expert testimony. Since the developer regularly gives expert testimony as part of her employment with the tech company, she also would have needed to provide a written report if she was going to testify in this case. However, the developer is only advising the company and is not going to testify. Therefore, the company does not need to disclose the developer's identity (Choices C & D). *If a party intends to rebut an opposing party's expert witness, expert witness disclosure is required within 30 days after the opposing party discloses its expert witness information. Educational objective: A party must disclose the identity of an expert witness expected to testify at trial and provide a written report or summary to the opposing party at least 90 days before trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(2) (required disclosure of expert witnesses).","rule":"A party must disclose the identity of an expert witness expected to testify at trial and provide a written report or summary to the opposing party at least 90 days before trial.","irac":{"issue":"A tech company sued a software firm in a federal district court for allegedly violating federal antitrust laws. The tech company employs a developer who regularly provides expert testimony for the company in litigation. The developer is advising the company in its suit against the firm, but the company is not going to call the developer as an expert witness in this case. Instead, the company hired an economist to testify as an expert witness about the economic impact caused by the software firm's anticompetitive behavior. Whom must the tech company disclose to the software firm at least 90 days before trial without waiting for a discovery request?","rule":"A party must disclose the identity of an expert witness expected to testify at trial and provide a written report or summary to the opposing party at least 90 days before trial.","analysis":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nType Required disclosures Time to disclose\n\nInitial • Persons with relevant information • ≤ 14 days after initial disclosures* • Items in party's possession that planning conference support claim/defense • ≤ 30 days after post-• Computation of damages & conference service supporting material or joinder\n• Relevant insurance policies • As court orders or parties stipulate Expert • Identity of expert witnesses • ≥ 90 days before trial testimony • Expert's written report • ≤ 30 days after\n• If no report, subject matter & disclosure to rebut summary of testimony evidence\n• As court orders or parties stipulate Pretrial • Testifying witnesses • ≥ 30 days before trial disclosures* • Deposition testimony to be used • As court orders at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nParties must disclose certain information during discovery to facilitate litigation. Most of this information is disclosed in response to an opposing party's discovery requests. But three types of disclosures are required without such requests: initial disclosures, pretrial disclosures, and disclosure of expert witness testimony. This requires that the parties disclose the identity of all expert witnesses expected to testify at trial AND provide either:\n• a written report for any witness who was specially hired to provide expert testimony or who regularly gives expert testimony as part of his/her employment duties with the party or\n\n• a summary of the subject matter, facts, and opinions of the testimony for all other expert witnesses (eg, treating physicians).\n\nA party must disclose this information at least 90 days before trial unless otherwise ordered by the court or stipulated by the parties.* Here, the tech company hired the economist to testify at trial as an expert witness about the economic impact of the software firm's anticompetitive behavior. As a result, the company must disclose the economist's identity at least 90 days before trial without waiting for a discovery request (Choices A & C). A written report must also be included since the economist was specially hired to provide this expert testimony. Since the developer regularly gives expert testimony as part of her employment with the tech company, she also would have needed to provide a written report if she was going to testify in this case. However, the developer is only advising the company and is not going to testify. Therefore, the company does not need to disclose the developer's identity (Choices C & D). *If a party intends to rebut an opposing party's expert witness, expert witness disclosure is required within 30 days after the opposing party discloses its expert witness information. Educational objective: A party must disclose the identity of an expert witness expected to testify at trial and provide a written report or summary to the opposing party at least 90 days before trial. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(2) (required disclosure of expert witnesses).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(2) (required disclosure of expert witnesses).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[733],"sourceAnswerPages":[734,735],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家科技公司在联邦地区法院起诉一家软件公司，指称其违反联邦反托拉斯法。科技公司雇用了一名开发人员，该开发人员经常在诉讼中为公司提供专家证言。开发人员正在为公司针对软件公司的诉讼提供咨询，但公司不会在本案中传唤她作为专家证人。公司另行聘请了一名经济学家，就软件公司的反竞争行为造成的经济影响提供专家证言。科技公司必须在审判前至少90天、无须等待证据开示请求就向软件公司披露谁？","choicesZh":{"A":"经济学家和开发人员均无须披露。","B":"仅披露经济学家。","C":"仅披露开发人员。","D":"同时披露经济学家和开发人员。"},"explanationZh":"强制性证据开示披露\n\n（FRCP 26(a)）\n\n类型｜必须披露的内容｜披露时间\n\n初始披露\n• 掌握相关信息的人员\n• 当事人持有且可用来支持请求或抗辩的物品\n• 损害赔偿计算及支持材料\n• 相关保险单\n时间：\n• 初始规划会议后不超过14天*\n• 在规划会议后才送达诉状或加入诉讼的当事人，应在送达或加入后不超过30天\n• 或依照法院命令或当事人约定\n\n专家证言披露\n• 专家证人的身份\n• 专家书面报告\n• 如无报告，则披露证言主题及概要\n时间：\n• 审判前至少90天\n• 反驳证据应在相对方披露后不超过30天\n• 或依照法院命令或当事人约定\n\n审前披露*\n• 将出庭作证的证人\n• 审判时将使用的证言笔录\n• 审判时将使用的文件或证物\n时间：\n• 审判前至少30天\n• 或依照法院命令\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不适用于仅为弹劾证人可信度而使用的证据。\n\n当事人必须在证据开示期间披露某些信息，以促进诉讼进行。这些信息大多根据对方的证据开示请求予以披露。但有三类信息无须请求即必须披露：初始披露、审前披露以及专家证人证言披露。为此，当事人必须披露所有预期在审判中作证之专家证人的身份，并提供下列材料之一：\n• 对于为提供专家证言而专门聘请的证人，或者在为该方工作的职责中经常提供专家证言的证人，提供书面报告；或者\n• 对于其他所有专家证人（例如治疗医生），提供证言主题、事实及意见的概要。\n\n除非法院另有命令或当事人另有约定，当事人必须在审判前至少90天披露这些信息。* 本题中，科技公司聘请经济学家在审判中就软件公司的反竞争行为造成的经济影响提供专家证言。因此，公司必须在审判前至少90天，无须等待证据开示请求即披露经济学家的身份（选项A和C）。由于经济学家是专门为提供该专家证言而聘请的，披露还必须包括书面报告。\n\n开发人员在科技公司的工作职责中经常提供专家证言，因此如果她将在本案中作证，也必须提交书面报告。但她只为公司提供咨询而不会作证，所以公司无须披露开发人员的身份（选项C和D）。\n\n*如果一方拟反驳对方的专家证人，则必须在对方披露其专家证人信息后30天内进行专家证人披露。\n\n学习目标：一方必须在审判前至少90天，向对方披露预期在审判中作证的专家证人身份，并提供书面报告或概要。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(2)（专家证人的强制披露）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-244","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":244,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A consumer from State A filed a $100,000 products liability action in federal court against a manufacturer incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B. The consumer claimed that a flaw in the manufacturer's product had resulted in severe injuries to the consumer. In its answer, the manufacturer asserted a third-party complaint against the product designer, also incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B. Believing that the consumer had sued the wrong defendant, the manufacturer claimed both that the designer was solely responsible for the flaw that had led to the consumer's injuries and that the manufacturer was not at fault. The designer is aware that the manufacturer did not follow all of the designer's specifications when making the product. Which of the following arguments is most likely to achieve the designer's goal of dismissal of the third-party complaint?","choices":{"A":"The court does not have subject-matter jurisdiction over the third-party complaint, because both the manufacturer and the designer are citizens of State B.","B":"The manufacturer failed to obtain the court's leave to file the third-party complaint.","C":"The manufacturer's failure to follow the designer's specifications caused the flaw that resulted in the consumer's injuries.","D":"The manufacturer's third-party complaint failed to state a proper third-party claim."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A defendant may add a nonparty to a suit through third-party practice (ie, impleader). However, the third-party complaint must be based on derivative liability— ie, it must assert that the nonparty is liable to the defendant for all (ie, indemnity) or part (ie, contribution) of the plaintiff's claim. A third-party complaint should therefore be dismissed if it asserts that the nonparty is directly liable to the plaintiff or liable to the defendant for another claim. Here, the defendant-manufacturer's third-party complaint against the nonparty-designer asserts that the plaintiff-consumer sued the wrong party. Since the manufacturer is claiming that the designer is solely responsible for the consumer's injuries—ie, directly liable to the consumer—the best argument to dismiss the third-party complaint is that it fails to state a proper third-party claim. (Choice A) The court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the third-party complaint through supplemental jurisdiction because it (1) shares the same facts as the consumer's claim, (2) does not destroy diversity between the consumer and manufacturer, and (3) is made by a defendant. (Choice B) A defendant must obtain the court's leave (ie, permission) to file a third-party complaint if more than 14 days have passed since the defendant served his/her original answer. But since this third-party complaint was asserted within the manufacturer's answer, leave of court was not required. (Choice C) An argument that the manufacturer failed to follow the designer's specifications is a factual allegation. But the truth of this allegation is irrelevant because the court will accept a complaint's factual allegations as true and ignore the opposing party's (designer's) allegations when assessing if the complaint states a proper claim. Educational objective: Third-party practice (ie, impleader) allows a defendant to add a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim (ie, derivative liability). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (third-party practice).","rule":"Third-party practice (ie, impleader) allows a defendant to add a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim (ie, derivative liability).","irac":{"issue":"A consumer from State A filed a $100,000 products liability action in federal court against a manufacturer incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B. The consumer claimed that a flaw in the manufacturer's product had resulted in severe injuries to the consumer. In its answer, the manufacturer asserted a third-party complaint against the product designer, also incorporated and with its principal place of business in State B. Believing that the consumer had sued the wrong defendant, the manufacturer claimed both that the designer was solely responsible for the flaw that had led to the consumer's injuries and that the manufacturer was not at fault. The designer is aware that the manufacturer did not follow all of the designer's specifications when making the product. Which of the following arguments is most likely to achieve the designer's goal of dismissal of the third-party complaint?","rule":"Third-party practice (ie, impleader) allows a defendant to add a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim (ie, derivative liability).","analysis":"A defendant may add a nonparty to a suit through third-party practice (ie, impleader). However, the third-party complaint must be based on derivative liability— ie, it must assert that the nonparty is liable to the defendant for all (ie, indemnity) or part (ie, contribution) of the plaintiff's claim. A third-party complaint should therefore be dismissed if it asserts that the nonparty is directly liable to the plaintiff or liable to the defendant for another claim. Here, the defendant-manufacturer's third-party complaint against the nonparty-designer asserts that the plaintiff-consumer sued the wrong party. Since the manufacturer is claiming that the designer is solely responsible for the consumer's injuries—ie, directly liable to the consumer—the best argument to dismiss the third-party complaint is that it fails to state a proper third-party claim. (Choice A) The court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the third-party complaint through supplemental jurisdiction because it (1) shares the same facts as the consumer's claim, (2) does not destroy diversity between the consumer and manufacturer, and (3) is made by a defendant. (Choice B) A defendant must obtain the court's leave (ie, permission) to file a third-party complaint if more than 14 days have passed since the defendant served his/her original answer. But since this third-party complaint was asserted within the manufacturer's answer, leave of court was not required. (Choice C) An argument that the manufacturer failed to follow the designer's specifications is a factual allegation. But the truth of this allegation is irrelevant because the court will accept a complaint's factual allegations as true and ignore the opposing party's (designer's) allegations when assessing if the complaint states a proper claim. Educational objective: Third-party practice (ie, impleader) allows a defendant to add a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim (ie, derivative liability). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (third-party practice).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (third-party practice).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[736],"sourceAnswerPages":[737],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的消费者在联邦法院对一家在B州注册成立且主要营业地也在B州的制造商提起10万美元的产品责任诉讼。消费者主张制造商产品中的缺陷使其遭受严重伤害。制造商在答辩状中对产品设计商提出第三方起诉；该设计商同样在B州注册成立，主要营业地也在B州。制造商认为消费者起诉了错误的被告，因此既主张该设计商对造成消费者伤害的缺陷负全部责任，也主张制造商本身没有过错。设计商知道制造商生产产品时没有遵循设计商的全部规格。下列哪项论点最有可能实现设计商驳回第三方起诉的目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院对第三方起诉不具有事项管辖权，因为制造商和设计商均为B州公民。","B":"制造商未取得法院许可即提交第三方起诉。","C":"制造商未遵循设计商规格的行为造成了导致消费者受伤的缺陷。","D":"制造商的第三方起诉未能陈述适当的第三方请求。"},"explanationZh":"被告可以通过第三方程序（即引入第三方被告）把非当事人加入诉讼。不过，第三方起诉必须以派生责任为依据，即必须主张非当事人应就原告请求的全部（即补偿）或一部分（即分担）向被告承担责任。因此，如果第三方起诉主张非当事人直接对原告负责，或就另一项请求对被告负责，则应予驳回。\n\n本题中，被告制造商对非当事人设计商提出的第三方起诉主张，原告消费者起诉了错误的一方。制造商声称设计商对消费者的伤害负全部责任——即直接对消费者承担责任——因此，驳回第三方起诉的最佳论点是该起诉未能陈述适当的第三方请求。\n\n（选项A）法院通过补充管辖权对第三方起诉具有事项管辖权，因为该起诉：(1)与消费者的请求具有相同事实；(2)不破坏消费者与制造商之间的多元州籍；且(3)由被告提出。\n\n（选项B）如果被告送达原答辩状后已超过14天，提交第三方起诉须取得法院许可（即准许）。但本题的第三方起诉是在制造商答辩状中提出，因此无须法院许可。\n\n（选项C）关于制造商未遵循设计商规格的论点属于事实陈述。但该陈述是否真实并不相关，因为法院判断起诉状是否陈述适当请求时，会把起诉状中的事实陈述视为真实，并忽略对方（设计商）的事实陈述。\n\n学习目标：第三方程序（即引入第三方被告）允许被告加入可能就原告请求的全部或部分向被告承担责任的非当事人（即派生责任）。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14（第三方程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-078"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-245","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":245,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A man from State A and a woman from State B started a fashion business together in State A. After a year of working together, the man and the woman began to have different opinions on how the business should operate. The woman was also concerned that the man used dress designs from his previous employer, thereby risking a lawsuit from the former employer. They tried to figure out a solution to wind down the business without involving lawyers but failed. The woman sued the man in federal district court, seeking $2 million for breach of contract. During discovery, the woman served a subpoena on the man's former employer seeking copies of the employer's dress designs. The employer is worried that disclosing these designs will reveal his trade secrets. What is the most efficient action the employer can take to prevent disclosure of his trade secrets?","choices":{"A":"Claim privilege over the dress designs.","B":"File a motion to quash the subpoena.","C":"Intervene in the case.","D":"Request a temporary restraining order."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Quashing or modifying subpoenas\n\n(FRCP 45(d)(3))\n\nRequired Court must quash or modify subpoena that:\n\n• fails to allow reasonable time to comply\n• requires person to comply beyond geographical limits\n• requires disclosure of privileged or protected matter or\n• subjects person to undue burden Permitted Court may quash or modify subpoena that requires disclosing:\n\n• unretained expert's opinion or information that (1) does not describe occurrences in dispute & (2) results from expert's study not requested by party\n• trade secret or confidential research, development, or commercial information FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA subpoena is a written court order that compels a person (party or nonparty) to appear in court or at a deposition OR to produce documents or items in his/her possession.* Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 45(d), a person subjected to a subpoena may file a motion to modify or quash that subpoena. A court may grant the motion if the subpoena requires disclosure of:\n• an unretained expert witness's opinion or information that (1) does not describe specific occurrences in dispute and (2) results from the expert's independent study instead of a party's request or\n\n• trade secrets or other confidential research, development, or commercial information.\n\nHere, the woman served a subpoena on the man's former employer (nonparty) to produce copies of his dress designs. Since the employer is concerned that disclosure will reveal his trade secrets and a motion is relatively cost-effective, the most efficient action to prevent disclosure is to file a motion to quash the subpoena. *A person commanded to produce documents or items need not appear in person at the place of production or inspection unless also commanded to appear for a deposition, hearing, or trial.\n\n(Choice A) In general, trade secrets are not privileged. If they were, the court would be required to modify or quash a subpoena compelling the disclosure of that privileged information. (Choice C) A nonparty may intervene (ie, join) and fully participate in a pending suit to protect his/her interests. But here, the employer does not have an interest in the pending suit other than the subpoena, and he can efficiently protect this interest by filing a motion to quash. (Choice D) A temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that preserves the status quo of the parties until there is an opportunity to hold a full hearing on an application for a preliminary injunction. But since the employer is not a party to the suit and a TRO provides only a temporary solution, it is not the most efficient action. Educational objective: A court may modify or quash a subpoena that requires disclosing (1) an unretained expert's opinion or information that does not describe specific occurrences in dispute and results from the expert's independent study instead of a party's request or (2) trade secrets or other confidential research, development, or commercial information. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(d)(3) (modifying or quashing a subpoena).","rule":"A court may modify or quash a subpoena that requires disclosing (1) an unretained expert's opinion or information that does not describe specific occurrences in dispute and results from the expert's independent study instead of a party's request or (2) trade secrets or other confidential research, development, or commercial information.","irac":{"issue":"A man from State A and a woman from State B started a fashion business together in State A. After a year of working together, the man and the woman began to have different opinions on how the business should operate. The woman was also concerned that the man used dress designs from his previous employer, thereby risking a lawsuit from the former employer. They tried to figure out a solution to wind down the business without involving lawyers but failed. The woman sued the man in federal district court, seeking $2 million for breach of contract. During discovery, the woman served a subpoena on the man's former employer seeking copies of the employer's dress designs. The employer is worried that disclosing these designs will reveal his trade secrets. What is the most efficient action the employer can take to prevent disclosure of his trade secrets?","rule":"A court may modify or quash a subpoena that requires disclosing (1) an unretained expert's opinion or information that does not describe specific occurrences in dispute and results from the expert's independent study instead of a party's request or (2) trade secrets or other confidential research, development, or commercial information.","analysis":"Quashing or modifying subpoenas\n\n(FRCP 45(d)(3))\n\nRequired Court must quash or modify subpoena that:\n\n• fails to allow reasonable time to comply\n• requires person to comply beyond geographical limits\n• requires disclosure of privileged or protected matter or\n• subjects person to undue burden Permitted Court may quash or modify subpoena that requires disclosing:\n\n• unretained expert's opinion or information that (1) does not describe occurrences in dispute & (2) results from expert's study not requested by party\n• trade secret or confidential research, development, or commercial information FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA subpoena is a written court order that compels a person (party or nonparty) to appear in court or at a deposition OR to produce documents or items in his/her possession.* Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 45(d), a person subjected to a subpoena may file a motion to modify or quash that subpoena. A court may grant the motion if the subpoena requires disclosure of:\n• an unretained expert witness's opinion or information that (1) does not describe specific occurrences in dispute and (2) results from the expert's independent study instead of a party's request or\n\n• trade secrets or other confidential research, development, or commercial information.\n\nHere, the woman served a subpoena on the man's former employer (nonparty) to produce copies of his dress designs. Since the employer is concerned that disclosure will reveal his trade secrets and a motion is relatively cost-effective, the most efficient action to prevent disclosure is to file a motion to quash the subpoena. *A person commanded to produce documents or items need not appear in person at the place of production or inspection unless also commanded to appear for a deposition, hearing, or trial.\n\n(Choice A) In general, trade secrets are not privileged. If they were, the court would be required to modify or quash a subpoena compelling the disclosure of that privileged information. (Choice C) A nonparty may intervene (ie, join) and fully participate in a pending suit to protect his/her interests. But here, the employer does not have an interest in the pending suit other than the subpoena, and he can efficiently protect this interest by filing a motion to quash. (Choice D) A temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that preserves the status quo of the parties until there is an opportunity to hold a full hearing on an application for a preliminary injunction. But since the employer is not a party to the suit and a TRO provides only a temporary solution, it is not the most efficient action. Educational objective: A court may modify or quash a subpoena that requires disclosing (1) an unretained expert's opinion or information that does not describe specific occurrences in dispute and results from the expert's independent study instead of a party's request or (2) trade secrets or other confidential research, development, or commercial information. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(d)(3) (modifying or quashing a subpoena).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(d)(3) (modifying or quashing a subpoena).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[739],"sourceAnswerPages":[740,741],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的男子与一名来自B州的女子在A州共同创办了一家时装企业。合作一年后，两人对企业应如何运营产生分歧。女子还担心男子使用了其前雇主的服装设计，从而使企业面临前雇主起诉的风险。他们试图在不聘请律师的情况下找到结束企业经营的方案，但没有成功。女子在联邦地区法院起诉男子，以违约为由请求200万美元。证据开示期间，女子向男子的前雇主送达传票，要求其提供雇主服装设计的副本。前雇主担心披露这些设计会泄露其商业秘密。前雇主为防止商业秘密被披露，可以采取的最高效措施是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"主张服装设计受特权保护。","B":"申请撤销传票。","C":"参加该案件。","D":"申请临时限制令。"},"explanationZh":"撤销或修改传票\n\n（FRCP 45(d)(3)）\n\n必须撤销或修改\n法院必须撤销或修改具有下列情形的传票：\n• 未给予合理的履行时间；\n• 要求某人在地域限制之外履行；\n• 要求披露受特权保护或受保护的事项；或者\n• 使某人承受不当负担。\n\n可以撤销或修改\n法院可以撤销或修改要求披露下列内容的传票：\n• 未受聘专家的意见或信息，且该内容：(1)并非描述争议中的具体事件；且(2)源于专家并非应当事人请求而进行的研究；\n• 商业秘密，或其他保密的研究、开发或商业信息。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n传票是一项书面法院命令，强制某人（当事人或非当事人）出庭或参加证言录取，或者提供其持有的文件或物品。* 根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》45(d)，受传票约束的人可申请修改或撤销该传票。如果传票要求披露下列内容，法院可以批准申请：\n• 未受聘专家证人的意见或信息，且该内容：(1)并非描述争议中的具体事件；且(2)源于专家的独立研究，而非应一方请求进行的研究；或者\n• 商业秘密，或其他保密的研究、开发或商业信息。\n\n本题中，女子向男子的前雇主（非当事人）送达传票，要求其提供服装设计副本。由于前雇主担心披露会泄露其商业秘密，而且提出动议相对具有成本效益，防止披露的最高效措施是申请撤销传票。\n\n*被命令提供文件或物品的人，无须亲自到提供或检查地点，除非传票同时命令其参加证言录取、听证会或审判。\n\n（选项A）一般而言，商业秘密不受特权保护。如果其受特权保护，法院将必须修改或撤销强制披露该特权信息的传票。\n\n（选项C）非当事人可参加（即加入）待决诉讼并全面参与，以保护其利益。但本题中，除传票外，前雇主在待决诉讼中没有其他利益；他可以通过申请撤销传票高效保护该利益。\n\n（选项D）临时限制令（TRO）是一项维持当事人现状的法院命令，持续至有机会对初步禁令申请举行完整听证会为止。但前雇主不是本案当事人，而且TRO只提供临时解决方案，因此并非最高效措施。\n\n学习目标：如果传票要求披露：(1)未受聘专家的意见或信息，且该内容不描述争议中的具体事件，并源于专家的独立研究而非一方请求；或(2)商业秘密或其他保密的研究、开发或商业信息，法院可以修改或撤销传票。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(d)(3)（修改或撤销传票）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-246","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":246,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A husband and wife from State A divorced. As part of their divorce settlement, the husband agreed to keep the wife as the sole beneficiary of his employer-sponsored life insurance policy worth $100,000. A court entered this settlement agreement as part of its divorce decree. The husband remarried and moved with his second wife to State B. The husband then revised his life insurance policy to name the second wife as the sole beneficiary. When the husband later died, the first and second wife each filed claims with the life insurance company to recover the proceeds from his policy. The insurance company, which is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State B, wants to avoid paying the proceeds twice. If the insurance company files suit in federal district court, what procedure should the insurance company use to avoid double liability?","choices":{"A":"Class action.","B":"Intervention.","C":"Rule interpleader.","D":"Statutory interpleader."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Interpleader is a procedure used when multiple persons claim an interest in the same property (ie, the stake)—like the wives claiming an interest in the same life insurance proceeds here. It allows the possessor of the stake (ie, stakeholder) to avoid multiple liability by joining the claimants to litigate the ownership of the stake among themselves. There are two types of interpleader with distinct subject-matter jurisdiction requirements that must be met before a federal court can hear the dispute:\n• Rule interpleader – requires that (1) the action involve a federal question or (2) the amount in controversy exceed $75,000 and complete diversity exist between the stakeholder and the claimants—ie, they must be citizens of different states\n\n• Statutory interpleader – requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants are citizens of different states\n\nHere, rule interpleader is unavailable because the insurance company (stakeholder) from State B is not completely diverse from the wives (claimants) from States A and B (Choice C). But subject-matter jurisdiction exists under statutory interpleader because the wives are diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $500. Therefore, the insurance company should use statutory interpleader to avoid paying the proceeds twice. (Choice A) A certified class action allows named parties to sue or be sued on behalf of similarly situated unnamed parties. One requirement for certification is that the class be so numerous that joinder of all members is impracticable, which is not met by the two wives here. (Choice B) A nonparty may intervene (ie, join) and participate in a pending suit to protect his/her interests. But here, there would be no need for the insurance company to intervene since it would protect its interests as a party to the suit if one is filed. Educational objective: Diversity jurisdiction for rule interpleader requires complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants and an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000. But statutory interpleader only requires minimal diversity between the claimants and an amount in controversy of at least $500. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22 (rule interpleader).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory interpleader).\n\n• State Farm Fire & Cas. Co. v. Tashire, 386 U.S. 523, 530–31 (1967) (explaining the minimal diversity requirements for statutory interpleader).","rule":"Diversity jurisdiction for rule interpleader requires complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants and an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000. But statutory interpleader only requires minimal diversity between the claimants and an amount in controversy of at least $500.","irac":{"issue":"A husband and wife from State A divorced. As part of their divorce settlement, the husband agreed to keep the wife as the sole beneficiary of his employer-sponsored life insurance policy worth $100,000. A court entered this settlement agreement as part of its divorce decree. The husband remarried and moved with his second wife to State B. The husband then revised his life insurance policy to name the second wife as the sole beneficiary. When the husband later died, the first and second wife each filed claims with the life insurance company to recover the proceeds from his policy. The insurance company, which is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State B, wants to avoid paying the proceeds twice. If the insurance company files suit in federal district court, what procedure should the insurance company use to avoid double liability?","rule":"Diversity jurisdiction for rule interpleader requires complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants and an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000. But statutory interpleader only requires minimal diversity between the claimants and an amount in controversy of at least $500.","analysis":"Interpleader is a procedure used when multiple persons claim an interest in the same property (ie, the stake)—like the wives claiming an interest in the same life insurance proceeds here. It allows the possessor of the stake (ie, stakeholder) to avoid multiple liability by joining the claimants to litigate the ownership of the stake among themselves. There are two types of interpleader with distinct subject-matter jurisdiction requirements that must be met before a federal court can hear the dispute:\n• Rule interpleader – requires that (1) the action involve a federal question or (2) the amount in controversy exceed $75,000 and complete diversity exist between the stakeholder and the claimants—ie, they must be citizens of different states\n\n• Statutory interpleader – requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants are citizens of different states\n\nHere, rule interpleader is unavailable because the insurance company (stakeholder) from State B is not completely diverse from the wives (claimants) from States A and B (Choice C). But subject-matter jurisdiction exists under statutory interpleader because the wives are diverse and the amount in controversy exceeds $500. Therefore, the insurance company should use statutory interpleader to avoid paying the proceeds twice. (Choice A) A certified class action allows named parties to sue or be sued on behalf of similarly situated unnamed parties. One requirement for certification is that the class be so numerous that joinder of all members is impracticable, which is not met by the two wives here. (Choice B) A nonparty may intervene (ie, join) and participate in a pending suit to protect his/her interests. But here, there would be no need for the insurance company to intervene since it would protect its interests as a party to the suit if one is filed. Educational objective: Diversity jurisdiction for rule interpleader requires complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants and an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000. But statutory interpleader only requires minimal diversity between the claimants and an amount in controversy of at least $500. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22 (rule interpleader).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory interpleader).\n\n• State Farm Fire & Cas. Co. v. Tashire, 386 U.S. 523, 530–31 (1967) (explaining the minimal diversity requirements for statutory interpleader).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22 (rule interpleader).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory interpleader).\n\n• State Farm Fire & Cas. Co. v. Tashire, 386 U.S. 523, 530–31 (1967) (explaining the minimal diversity requirements for statutory interpleader).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[742],"sourceAnswerPages":[743,744],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"来自A州的一对夫妻离婚。作为离婚协议的一部分，丈夫同意继续把妻子列为其价值10万美元的雇主提供人寿保险单的唯一受益人。法院把该和解协议纳入离婚判令。丈夫后来再婚，并与第二任妻子迁往B州。随后，丈夫修改人寿保险单，把第二任妻子列为唯一受益人。丈夫后来去世时，第一任妻子和第二任妻子分别向人寿保险公司提出请求，要求领取保险金。该保险公司在B州注册成立且主要营业地也在B州，希望避免重复支付保险金。如果保险公司在联邦地区法院提起诉讼，应使用何种程序避免双重责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"集团诉讼。","B":"参加诉讼。","C":"规则上的权益争讼。","D":"制定法上的权益争讼。"},"explanationZh":"权益争讼是一种在多人对同一财产（即争议标的）主张利益时使用的程序——本题中两位妻子都对同一笔人寿保险金主张利益。该程序允许争议标的的持有人（即标的持有人）把各请求人加入诉讼，由其相互诉讼确定标的所有权，从而避免承担多重责任。\n\n权益争讼分为两类；联邦法院只有在各自不同的事项管辖权要求得到满足后，才能审理争议：\n• 规则上的权益争讼——要求：(1)诉讼涉及联邦问题；或者(2)争议金额超过75,000美元，且标的持有人与各请求人之间存在完全多元州籍，即他们必须是不同州的公民；\n• 制定法上的权益争讼——要求争议金额至少为500美元，并且各请求人之间存在最低限度的多元州籍，即至少两名请求人是不同州的公民。\n\n本题中，规则上的权益争讼不可用，因为来自B州的保险公司（标的持有人）与来自A州和B州的两位妻子（请求人）之间不存在完全多元州籍（选项C）。但是，制定法上的权益争讼具有事项管辖权，因为两位妻子具有多元州籍，且争议金额超过500美元。因此，保险公司应使用制定法上的权益争讼，避免重复支付保险金。\n\n（选项A）经批准的集团诉讼允许具名当事人代表处境相似的未具名当事人起诉或应诉。批准条件之一是集团人数众多，以至于将所有成员合并诉讼并不切实可行；本题只有两位妻子，不符合该条件。\n\n（选项B）非当事人可以参加（即加入）并参与待决诉讼，以保护其利益。但本题中，如果保险公司提起诉讼，它本身就是案件当事人并可保护自身利益，所以无须参加诉讼。\n\n学习目标：规则上的权益争讼的多元州籍管辖权要求标的持有人与请求人之间存在完全多元州籍，且争议金额超过75,000美元；制定法上的权益争讼只要求请求人之间存在最低限度多元州籍，且争议金额至少为500美元。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22（规则上的权益争讼）。\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361（制定法上的权益争讼）。\n• State Farm Fire & Cas. Co. v. Tashire, 386 U.S. 523, 530–31 (1967)（说明制定法上的权益争讼的最低限度多元州籍要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-079"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-247","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":247,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A defendant sued in federal court seeks information from a nonparty witness. Which of the following discovery devices can the defendant use to obtain this information?","choices":{"A":"Interrogatory.","B":"Mental exam.","C":"Oral deposition.","D":"Request for admissions."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Methods of discovery\n\nDepositions • Written or oral examination of party or witness under oath\n• 10 per party Interrogatories • Written questions served on party\n• Serve up to 25\n• Written responses due within 30 days of service Request for • Request served on party (or subpoena served on nonparty) production to produce & allow inspection of documents, electronic information, tangible items, or land\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Requests for • Requests served on other party to admit truth of facts within admission scope of discovery\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Physical/mental • Order by court or parties' agreement for physical or mental exams examination of party if those conditions are in controversy\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties can obtain information related to the case from other parties and nonparties through various methods. A deposition is a discovery method in which a party conducts a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court. Therefore, the defendant can use an oral deposition to get information from the nonparty witness—and, if necessary, can obtain a subpoena to compel the nonparty's attendance. (Choice A) Interrogatories are written questions served on another party that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery—but they cannot be served on a nonparty. (Choice B) A court can only order a physical or mental exam of a party by a suitably licensed or certified examiner if that party's physical or mental condition is in controversy. (Choice D) A request for admissions is a discovery method in which a party requests that another party admit the truth of specified facts within the scope of discovery. But this method cannot be used against a nonparty. Educational objective: During discovery, the parties can use various methods to obtain information and evidence concerning the case from other parties and nonparties. A deposition is a discovery method\n\nin which a party conducts a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30–31 (depositions).","rule":"During discovery, the parties can use various methods to obtain information and evidence concerning the case from other parties and nonparties. A deposition is a discovery method\n\nin which a party conducts a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court.","irac":{"issue":"A defendant sued in federal court seeks information from a nonparty witness. Which of the following discovery devices can the defendant use to obtain this information?","rule":"During discovery, the parties can use various methods to obtain information and evidence concerning the case from other parties and nonparties. A deposition is a discovery method\n\nin which a party conducts a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court.","analysis":"Methods of discovery\n\nDepositions • Written or oral examination of party or witness under oath\n• 10 per party Interrogatories • Written questions served on party\n• Serve up to 25\n• Written responses due within 30 days of service Request for • Request served on party (or subpoena served on nonparty) production to produce & allow inspection of documents, electronic information, tangible items, or land\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Requests for • Requests served on other party to admit truth of facts within admission scope of discovery\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Physical/mental • Order by court or parties' agreement for physical or mental exams examination of party if those conditions are in controversy\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties can obtain information related to the case from other parties and nonparties through various methods. A deposition is a discovery method in which a party conducts a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court. Therefore, the defendant can use an oral deposition to get information from the nonparty witness—and, if necessary, can obtain a subpoena to compel the nonparty's attendance. (Choice A) Interrogatories are written questions served on another party that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery—but they cannot be served on a nonparty. (Choice B) A court can only order a physical or mental exam of a party by a suitably licensed or certified examiner if that party's physical or mental condition is in controversy. (Choice D) A request for admissions is a discovery method in which a party requests that another party admit the truth of specified facts within the scope of discovery. But this method cannot be used against a nonparty. Educational objective: During discovery, the parties can use various methods to obtain information and evidence concerning the case from other parties and nonparties. A deposition is a discovery method\n\nin which a party conducts a written or oral examination of a party or nonparty under oath and outside of court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30–31 (depositions).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30–31 (depositions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[745],"sourceAnswerPages":[746,747],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名在联邦法院被起诉的被告希望从一名非当事人证人处取得信息。被告可以使用下列哪种证据开示方式取得该信息？","choicesZh":{"A":"书面询问。","B":"精神检查。","C":"口头证言录取。","D":"承认请求。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示方式\n\n证言录取\n• 对当事人或证人在宣誓后进行书面或口头询问；\n• 每一方10次。\n\n书面询问\n• 向当事人送达书面问题；\n• 最多可送达25项；\n• 书面答复应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n提供请求\n• 向当事人送达请求（或向非当事人送达传票），要求其提供并允许检查文件、电子信息、有形物或土地；\n• 数量不限；\n• 书面答复应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n承认请求\n• 向另一方送达请求，要求其承认证据开示范围内特定事实的真实性；\n• 数量不限；\n• 书面答复应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n身体／精神检查\n• 当一方的身体或精神状况属于争议事项时，依法院命令或当事人协议对其进行身体或精神检查。\n\n证据开示是诉讼的审前阶段，在此期间，当事人可通过各种方式从其他当事人和非当事人处取得与案件有关的信息。证言录取是一种证据开示方式，由一方在庭外对当事人或非当事人进行经宣誓的书面或口头询问。因此，被告可以通过口头证言录取从非当事人证人处取得信息；如有必要，还可取得传票强制该非当事人出席。\n\n（选项A）书面询问是向另一方送达的书面问题，可以询问证据开示范围内的任何事项，但不能向非当事人送达。\n\n（选项B）只有当某一方的身体或精神状况属于争议事项时，法院才能命令由具有适当执照或认证的检查人员对该方进行身体或精神检查。\n\n（选项D）承认请求是一种证据开示方式，由一方请求另一方承认证据开示范围内特定事实的真实性。但这种方式不能对非当事人使用。\n\n学习目标：证据开示期间，当事人可运用多种方式，从其他当事人和非当事人处取得与案件有关的信息和证据。证言录取是一种证据开示方式，由一方在庭外对当事人或非当事人进行经宣誓的书面或口头询问。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30–31（证言录取）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-248","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":248,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A homeowners' association sued a federal agency in a federal district court to enjoin the agency from issuing a license to a fracking company to drill near the homeowners' association's neighborhood. An environmental group that owns a park in the neighborhood seeks to join and fully participate in that case to obtain the same remedy as the homeowners' association. What action should the environmental group take to achieve its goal?","choices":{"A":"File a motion to intervene as a matter of right.","B":"File a motion to join as an indispensable party.","C":"File a motion to permissibly intervene.","D":"File an amicus brief."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Intervention\n\n(FRCP 24)\n\nIntervention of • On timely motion, court must permit intervention when either: right – nonparty's interest in case not adequately represented by existing parties & deciding case may impair nonparty's ability to protect that interest – federal statute gives unconditional right to intervene Permissive • On timely motion, court may permit intervention when either: intervention – nonparty's claim or defense shares common question of law or fact with main action – federal statute gives conditional right to intervene\n• Court must consider whether intervention will unduly delay or prejudice adjudication of original parties' rights FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe environmental group (nonparty) seeks to fully participate in the suit between the homeowners' association and the federal agency. A nonparty can join and fully participate in a pending suit to protect his/her interest by filing a motion to intervene under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 24. There are two types of intervention:\n• Intervention of right – the court must permit intervention when (1) the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented by the existing parties and (2) deciding the case may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest\n\n• Permissive intervention – the court may permit intervention when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action*\n\nThe environmental group likely cannot intervene as a matter of right since the homeowners' association will adequately represent the group's interest in its park (Choice A). This is because both entities seek to enjoin the federal agency from issuing a fracking license near the neighborhood. But since both claims share common questions of law and fact, the group should file a motion to permissibly intervene in the case. *Intervention is also allowed when a federal statute gives a person an unconditional or conditional right to intervene (not seen here). (Choice B) Only a party may file an FRCP 19 motion to join an indispensable party. But the group is not indispensable because (1) complete relief can be provided to the existing\n\nparties, (2) the group can protect its park through future litigation, and (3) no threat of inconsistent obligations exists since the entities seek the same remedy. (Choice D) An amicus (ie, friend-of-the-court) brief is a legal document filed by a nonparty that provides the court with relevant information to help it decide the disputed issues. But amicus briefs do not allow the nonparty to fully participate in the case and are most commonly filed in appeals. Educational objective: A nonparty may intervene (1) as a matter of right, if the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and a decision may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest or (2) permissively, if the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (intervention).","rule":"A nonparty may intervene (1) as a matter of right, if the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and a decision may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest or (2) permissively, if the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action.","irac":{"issue":"A homeowners' association sued a federal agency in a federal district court to enjoin the agency from issuing a license to a fracking company to drill near the homeowners' association's neighborhood. An environmental group that owns a park in the neighborhood seeks to join and fully participate in that case to obtain the same remedy as the homeowners' association. What action should the environmental group take to achieve its goal?","rule":"A nonparty may intervene (1) as a matter of right, if the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and a decision may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest or (2) permissively, if the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action.","analysis":"Intervention\n\n(FRCP 24)\n\nIntervention of • On timely motion, court must permit intervention when either: right – nonparty's interest in case not adequately represented by existing parties & deciding case may impair nonparty's ability to protect that interest – federal statute gives unconditional right to intervene Permissive • On timely motion, court may permit intervention when either: intervention – nonparty's claim or defense shares common question of law or fact with main action – federal statute gives conditional right to intervene\n• Court must consider whether intervention will unduly delay or prejudice adjudication of original parties' rights FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe environmental group (nonparty) seeks to fully participate in the suit between the homeowners' association and the federal agency. A nonparty can join and fully participate in a pending suit to protect his/her interest by filing a motion to intervene under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 24. There are two types of intervention:\n• Intervention of right – the court must permit intervention when (1) the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented by the existing parties and (2) deciding the case may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest\n\n• Permissive intervention – the court may permit intervention when the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action*\n\nThe environmental group likely cannot intervene as a matter of right since the homeowners' association will adequately represent the group's interest in its park (Choice A). This is because both entities seek to enjoin the federal agency from issuing a fracking license near the neighborhood. But since both claims share common questions of law and fact, the group should file a motion to permissibly intervene in the case. *Intervention is also allowed when a federal statute gives a person an unconditional or conditional right to intervene (not seen here). (Choice B) Only a party may file an FRCP 19 motion to join an indispensable party. But the group is not indispensable because (1) complete relief can be provided to the existing\n\nparties, (2) the group can protect its park through future litigation, and (3) no threat of inconsistent obligations exists since the entities seek the same remedy. (Choice D) An amicus (ie, friend-of-the-court) brief is a legal document filed by a nonparty that provides the court with relevant information to help it decide the disputed issues. But amicus briefs do not allow the nonparty to fully participate in the case and are most commonly filed in appeals. Educational objective: A nonparty may intervene (1) as a matter of right, if the nonparty's interest is inadequately represented and a decision may impair the nonparty's ability to protect that interest or (2) permissively, if the nonparty's claim or defense shares a common question of law or fact with the main action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (intervention).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (intervention).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[748],"sourceAnswerPages":[749,750],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一个业主协会在联邦地区法院起诉一家联邦机构，请求禁止该机构向一家水力压裂公司发放在该协会社区附近钻探的许可证。一个在该社区拥有公园的环保组织，希望加入并全面参与该案件，以取得与业主协会相同的救济。环保组织应采取何种行动实现其目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请依权利参加诉讼。","B":"申请作为不可或缺当事人加入诉讼。","C":"申请许可参加诉讼。","D":"提交法庭之友意见书。"},"explanationZh":"参加诉讼\n\n（FRCP 24）\n\n权利参加\n当及时提出申请时，法院在下列任一情形下必须准许参加：\n• 非当事人在案件中的利益未获现有当事人充分代表，而且案件裁判可能损害该非当事人保护其利益的能力；\n• 联邦制定法赋予无条件参加权。\n\n许可参加\n当及时提出申请时，法院在下列任一情形下可以准许参加：\n• 非当事人的请求或抗辩与本诉具有共同的法律或事实问题；\n• 联邦制定法赋予附条件参加权。\n• 法院必须考虑参加诉讼是否会对原当事人权利的裁判造成不当延误或损害。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n环保组织（非当事人）希望全面参与业主协会与联邦机构之间的诉讼。根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）24，非当事人可以通过申请参加诉讼，加入并全面参与待决诉讼，以保护其利益。参加诉讼分为两类：\n• 权利参加——如果(1)非当事人的利益未获现有当事人充分代表，且(2)案件裁判可能损害该非当事人保护其利益的能力，法院必须准许其参加；\n• 许可参加——如果非当事人的请求或抗辩与本诉具有共同的法律或事实问题，法院可以准许其参加。*\n\n环保组织很可能不能依权利参加，因为业主协会将充分代表该组织在其公园中的利益（选项A）。这是因为两个实体都请求禁止联邦机构在社区附近发放水力压裂许可证。但由于两项请求具有共同的法律和事实问题，该组织应申请许可参加本案。\n\n*联邦制定法赋予某人无条件或附条件参加权时，也允许参加诉讼（本题没有此情形）。\n\n（选项B）只有当事人才可以根据FRCP 19申请加入不可或缺当事人。但该组织并非不可或缺，因为：(1)可以向现有当事人提供完全救济；(2)该组织可通过将来的诉讼保护其公园；且(3)两个实体寻求相同救济，不存在义务相互矛盾的风险。\n\n（选项D）法庭之友（即法院之友）意见书是非当事人提交的法律文件，用于向法院提供相关信息，帮助法院裁判争议问题。但法庭之友意见书不会使非当事人得以全面参与案件，而且最常见于上诉程序。\n\n学习目标：非当事人可以：(1)依权利参加诉讼，条件是其利益未获充分代表且案件裁判可能损害其保护该利益的能力；或(2)许可参加诉讼，条件是其请求或抗辩与本诉具有共同的法律或事实问题。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14（参加诉讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-249","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":249,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman died with a life insurance policy worth $50,000. The woman had named \"my son\" as the sole beneficiary of the policy proceeds. The woman's three sons, two of whom are domiciled in State A and one of whom is domiciled in State B, individually filed claims with the insurance company to recover the proceeds of the woman's policy. The insurance company, which is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State B, brought a statutory-interpleader action in federal court against all three sons. The sons have moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Will the court likely grant the sons' motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction over actions involving life insurance policies.","B":"No, because there is minimal diversity between the sons and the amount-in-controversy requirement is satisfied.","C":"Yes, because the amount in controversy does not exceed $75,000.","D":"Yes, because there is not complete diversity between the insurance company and the sons."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An interpleader action is available in federal court when multiple persons claim an interest in the same property (known as the \"stake\"). This action allows the possessor of the stake (the \"stakeholder\") to avoid multiple liability by joining the claimants to litigate the stake's ownership among themselves. There are two types of interpleader, each with distinct subject-matter jurisdiction requirements that must be satisfied before a court can hear the dispute:\n• Rule interpleader requires an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000 and complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants—ie, they must be citizens of different states.*\n\n• Statutory interpleader requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants must be citizens of different states.\n\nHere, the insurance company filed a statutory-interpleader action in federal court against the three sons. The amount-in-controversy requirement is satisfied because the policy is worth $50,000. Additionally, minimal diversity exists between the claimants since two sons are domiciled in State A while one son is domiciled in State B. Therefore, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the action and will deny the sons' motion to dismiss. *Subject-matter jurisdiction can also be established in a rule-interpleader action if the claim presents a federal question (not seen here). (Choice A) Federal and state courts share jurisdiction over both state and federal law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction. Actions involving life insurance policies are not actions over which federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction. (Choice C) For most federal civil actions arising from state law, including rule-interpleader actions, subject-matter jurisdiction requires an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000. However, statutory-interpleader actions only require an amount in controversy of at least $500. (Choice D) Rule interpleader requires complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants, but statutory interpleader only requires minimal diversity between the claimants. Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction for statutory interpleader requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants must be citizens of different states. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22 (rule interpleader).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1335 (statutory interpleader).\n\n• 7 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1710 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the subject-matter jurisdiction requirements for rule and statutory interpleader).","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction for statutory interpleader requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants must be citizens of different states.","irac":{"issue":"A woman died with a life insurance policy worth $50,000. The woman had named \"my son\" as the sole beneficiary of the policy proceeds. The woman's three sons, two of whom are domiciled in State A and one of whom is domiciled in State B, individually filed claims with the insurance company to recover the proceeds of the woman's policy. The insurance company, which is incorporated and has its principal place of business in State B, brought a statutory-interpleader action in federal court against all three sons. The sons have moved to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction. Will the court likely grant the sons' motion to dismiss?","rule":"Subject-matter jurisdiction for statutory interpleader requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants must be citizens of different states.","analysis":"An interpleader action is available in federal court when multiple persons claim an interest in the same property (known as the \"stake\"). This action allows the possessor of the stake (the \"stakeholder\") to avoid multiple liability by joining the claimants to litigate the stake's ownership among themselves. There are two types of interpleader, each with distinct subject-matter jurisdiction requirements that must be satisfied before a court can hear the dispute:\n• Rule interpleader requires an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000 and complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants—ie, they must be citizens of different states.*\n\n• Statutory interpleader requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants must be citizens of different states.\n\nHere, the insurance company filed a statutory-interpleader action in federal court against the three sons. The amount-in-controversy requirement is satisfied because the policy is worth $50,000. Additionally, minimal diversity exists between the claimants since two sons are domiciled in State A while one son is domiciled in State B. Therefore, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction over the action and will deny the sons' motion to dismiss. *Subject-matter jurisdiction can also be established in a rule-interpleader action if the claim presents a federal question (not seen here). (Choice A) Federal and state courts share jurisdiction over both state and federal law claims unless the Constitution or Congress expressly provides federal courts with exclusive jurisdiction. Actions involving life insurance policies are not actions over which federal courts have exclusive jurisdiction. (Choice C) For most federal civil actions arising from state law, including rule-interpleader actions, subject-matter jurisdiction requires an amount in controversy that exceeds $75,000. However, statutory-interpleader actions only require an amount in controversy of at least $500. (Choice D) Rule interpleader requires complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants, but statutory interpleader only requires minimal diversity between the claimants. Educational objective: Subject-matter jurisdiction for statutory interpleader requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between the claimants—ie, at least two claimants must be citizens of different states. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22 (rule interpleader).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1335 (statutory interpleader).\n\n• 7 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1710 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the subject-matter jurisdiction requirements for rule and statutory interpleader).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22 (rule interpleader).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1335 (statutory interpleader).\n\n• 7 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1710 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the subject-matter jurisdiction requirements for rule and statutory interpleader).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[751],"sourceAnswerPages":[752,753],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子去世时留有一份价值5万美元的人寿保险单。女子把“我的儿子”指定为保险金的唯一受益人。女子的三个儿子分别向保险公司提出请求，要求领取保险金；其中两人的住所地在A州，一人的住所地在B州。该保险公司在B州注册成立且主要营业地也在B州，并在联邦法院对三个儿子提起制定法上的权益争讼。三个儿子以缺乏事项管辖权为由申请驳回。法院是否可能批准该申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为联邦法院对涉及人寿保险单的诉讼享有专属管辖权。","B":"不会，因为三个儿子之间存在最低限度的多元州籍，且争议金额要求得到满足。","C":"会，因为争议金额未超过75,000美元。","D":"会，因为保险公司与三个儿子之间不存在完全多元州籍。"},"explanationZh":"多人对同一财产（称为“争议标的”）主张利益时，可以在联邦法院提起权益争讼。该诉讼允许标的持有人把各请求人加入诉讼，由其相互诉讼确定标的所有权，从而避免承担多重责任。权益争讼分为两类；法院只有在各自不同的事项管辖权要求得到满足后，才能审理争议：\n• 规则上的权益争讼要求争议金额超过75,000美元，且标的持有人与请求人之间存在完全多元州籍，即他们必须是不同州的公民。*\n• 制定法上的权益争讼要求争议金额至少为500美元，且请求人之间存在最低限度的多元州籍，即至少两名请求人必须是不同州的公民。\n\n本题中，保险公司在联邦法院对三个儿子提起制定法上的权益争讼。保险单价值5万美元，因而符合争议金额要求。此外，请求人之间存在最低限度的多元州籍，因为两个儿子的住所地在A州，另一个儿子的住所地在B州。因此，法院对该诉讼具有事项管辖权，将驳回三个儿子的驳回申请。\n\n*如果规则上的权益争讼请求提出联邦问题，也可确立事项管辖权（本题没有）。\n\n（选项A）除非宪法或国会明确赋予联邦法院专属管辖权，联邦法院与州法院对州法和联邦法请求均共享管辖权。涉及人寿保险单的诉讼不属于联邦法院享有专属管辖权的诉讼。\n\n（选项C）对于大多数基于州法的联邦民事诉讼，包括规则上的权益争讼，事项管辖权要求争议金额超过75,000美元。但是，制定法上的权益争讼只要求争议金额至少为500美元。\n\n（选项D）规则上的权益争讼要求标的持有人与请求人之间存在完全多元州籍，但制定法上的权益争讼只要求请求人之间存在最低限度的多元州籍。\n\n学习目标：制定法上的权益争讼的事项管辖权要求争议金额至少为500美元，且请求人之间存在最低限度的多元州籍，即至少两名请求人必须是不同州的公民。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 22（规则上的权益争讼）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1335（制定法上的权益争讼）。\n• 7 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1710 (3d ed. 2020)（说明规则上的和制定法上的权益争讼的事项管辖权要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-079"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-250","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":250,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A woman sued a large company in federal court for violating federal environmental laws. During discovery, the woman's attorney requested that the company produce all electronically stored information (ESI) pertaining to the company's carbon dioxide production but did not specify the form in which this ESI was to be produced. The company provided a written response confirming that it would produce the requested documents. One month later, the company sent the woman's attorney thousands of documents, emails, spreadsheets, and other files in their ordinarily maintained electronic forms. The attorney did not have the software necessary to access all of the produced ESI, so he sent the company a request for the production of the same ESI that specified the form in which it should be produced. The company refused. The attorney filed a motion with the federal court to compel the company to produce the ESI again in the specified form. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company may choose to produce the ESI in any form.","B":"No, because the company produced the ESI in forms in which they are ordinarily maintained.","C":"Yes, because the attorney is unable to access the ESI through his software.","D":"Yes, because the attorney may specify the form in which the ESI is to be produced."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Request for production\n\n(FRCP 34)\n\nRequest • Must describe each item with reasonable particularity\n• Must specify reasonable time, place & manner of production\n• May specify form in which ESI is to be produced Response • Must state that production or inspection will be permitted as requested OR specify grounds & reasons for objections\n• Due within 30 days of service Production • Must produce documents as kept in usual course of business OR organize & label to correspond to categories in request\n• Must produce ESI in ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form (if form not specified in request)\n• Need not produce same ESI in more than one form FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; ESI = electronically stored information\n\nA request for production is a method of discovery used to compel an opposing party to produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information (ESI), tangible things, and real property. Such a request:\n• must describe with reasonable particularity each item to be produced or inspected\n• must specify a reasonable time, place, and manner for the production or inspection and\n• may specify the form in which ESI is to be produced. If a request for ESI does not specify a form for production, the producing party must produce ESI in its ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form (Choice A). However, the producing party need not produce the same ESI in more than one form. Here, the attorney's first request for production did not specify the form in which the ESI was to be produced. As a result, the company properly produced its various ESI in their ordinarily maintained forms. And since the company need not produce the ESI in multiple forms, it does not need to comply with the attorney's second request. Therefore, the court is unlikely to compel production. (Choice C) The company is not required to reproduce the ESI merely because the attorney cannot access it through his software. That is because the attorney could have specified the form in his original request for production.\n\n(Choice D) The attorney could have specified the form in which the ESI was to be produced in his original request for production—but not after the company produced the ESI in its ordinarily maintained form. Educational objective: A party requesting production of electronically stored information (ESI) may specify the form in which it is to be produced. If no form is specified, the producing party must produce ESI in its ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form but need not produce the same ESI in more than one form. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34(b) (requesting & producing electronically stored information).","rule":"A party requesting production of electronically stored information (ESI) may specify the form in which it is to be produced. If no form is specified, the producing party must produce ESI in its ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form but need not produce the same ESI in more than one form.","irac":{"issue":"A woman sued a large company in federal court for violating federal environmental laws. During discovery, the woman's attorney requested that the company produce all electronically stored information (ESI) pertaining to the company's carbon dioxide production but did not specify the form in which this ESI was to be produced. The company provided a written response confirming that it would produce the requested documents. One month later, the company sent the woman's attorney thousands of documents, emails, spreadsheets, and other files in their ordinarily maintained electronic forms. The attorney did not have the software necessary to access all of the produced ESI, so he sent the company a request for the production of the same ESI that specified the form in which it should be produced. The company refused. The attorney filed a motion with the federal court to compel the company to produce the ESI again in the specified form. Is the court likely to grant the motion?","rule":"A party requesting production of electronically stored information (ESI) may specify the form in which it is to be produced. If no form is specified, the producing party must produce ESI in its ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form but need not produce the same ESI in more than one form.","analysis":"Request for production\n\n(FRCP 34)\n\nRequest • Must describe each item with reasonable particularity\n• Must specify reasonable time, place & manner of production\n• May specify form in which ESI is to be produced Response • Must state that production or inspection will be permitted as requested OR specify grounds & reasons for objections\n• Due within 30 days of service Production • Must produce documents as kept in usual course of business OR organize & label to correspond to categories in request\n• Must produce ESI in ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form (if form not specified in request)\n• Need not produce same ESI in more than one form FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; ESI = electronically stored information\n\nA request for production is a method of discovery used to compel an opposing party to produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information (ESI), tangible things, and real property. Such a request:\n• must describe with reasonable particularity each item to be produced or inspected\n• must specify a reasonable time, place, and manner for the production or inspection and\n• may specify the form in which ESI is to be produced. If a request for ESI does not specify a form for production, the producing party must produce ESI in its ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form (Choice A). However, the producing party need not produce the same ESI in more than one form. Here, the attorney's first request for production did not specify the form in which the ESI was to be produced. As a result, the company properly produced its various ESI in their ordinarily maintained forms. And since the company need not produce the ESI in multiple forms, it does not need to comply with the attorney's second request. Therefore, the court is unlikely to compel production. (Choice C) The company is not required to reproduce the ESI merely because the attorney cannot access it through his software. That is because the attorney could have specified the form in his original request for production.\n\n(Choice D) The attorney could have specified the form in which the ESI was to be produced in his original request for production—but not after the company produced the ESI in its ordinarily maintained form. Educational objective: A party requesting production of electronically stored information (ESI) may specify the form in which it is to be produced. If no form is specified, the producing party must produce ESI in its ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable form but need not produce the same ESI in more than one form. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34(b) (requesting & producing electronically stored information).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34(b) (requesting & producing electronically stored information).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[754],"sourceAnswerPages":[755,756],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在联邦法院起诉一家大型公司，指称其违反联邦环境法。证据开示期间，女子的律师要求公司提供与公司二氧化碳排放有关的所有电子存储信息（ESI），但没有指定应以何种形式提供。公司书面答复确认将提供所请求的文件。一个月后，公司把数千份文件、电子邮件、电子表格和其他档案，以其日常保存的电子形式发送给女子的律师。该律师没有访问全部已提供ESI所需的软件，因此又向公司提出请求，要求以其指定的形式再次提供同一ESI。公司拒绝。律师向联邦法院申请强制公司按指定形式再次提供ESI。法院是否可能批准该申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为公司可以选择以任何形式提供ESI。","B":"不会，因为公司以日常保存的形式提供了ESI。","C":"会，因为律师无法通过其软件访问ESI。","D":"会，因为律师可以指定ESI的提供形式。"},"explanationZh":"提供请求\n\n（FRCP 34）\n\n请求\n• 必须以合理的具体程度描述每一项目；\n• 必须指定合理的提供时间、地点和方式；\n• 可以指定ESI的提供形式。\n\n答复\n• 必须说明将按请求允许提供或检查，或者具体说明异议的依据与理由；\n• 应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n提供\n• 必须按照文件在正常业务过程中保存的方式提供，或者按照请求中的类别组织并标注；\n• 如果请求未指定形式，必须以ESI日常保存的形式或合理可用的形式提供；\n• 无须以一种以上形式提供同一ESI。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》；ESI＝电子存储信息。\n\n提供请求是一种证据开示方式，用于强制对方提供并允许检查文件、电子存储信息（ESI）、有形物和不动产。该请求：\n• 必须以合理的具体程度描述每一项待提供或检查的项目；\n• 必须指定合理的提供或检查时间、地点和方式；并且\n• 可以指定ESI的提供形式。\n\n如果ESI请求没有指定提供形式，提供方必须以其日常保存的形式或合理可用的形式提供ESI（选项A）。但是，提供方无须以一种以上形式提供同一ESI。\n\n本题中，律师的第一次提供请求没有指定ESI的提供形式。因此，公司以各种ESI日常保存的形式提供，做法适当。公司无须以多种形式提供ESI，所以也无须遵守律师的第二次请求。因此，法院不太可能命令再次提供。\n\n（选项C）仅仅因为律师无法通过其软件访问ESI，公司并无义务重新提供。这是因为律师本可在最初的提供请求中指定形式。\n\n（选项D）律师本可在最初的提供请求中指定ESI的提供形式，但不能等到公司已按日常保存形式提供后才这样做。\n\n学习目标：请求提供电子存储信息（ESI）的一方可以指定提供形式。未指定形式时，提供方必须以日常保存形式或合理可用形式提供ESI，但无须以一种以上形式提供同一ESI。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 34(b)（请求和提供电子存储信息）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-251","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":251,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A homeowner brought a diversity action against a mining company in federal court for negligence, claiming that the company's negligent mining practices caused extensive damage to her property. Forty-five days before trial, the homeowner sent the company the evidence that she plans to use at trial, including a witness whose testimony she intends to present by deposition. The homeowner also attached a transcript of the pertinent depositions. Twenty-one days later, the company objected to the use of the deposition testimony on the ground that it is inadmissible hearsay. Should the court hear the merits of the company's objection?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company failed to object at least 30 days before trial.","B":"No, because the company failed to object within 14 days after the homeowner disclosed the deposition testimony she intended to use at trial.","C":"Yes, because the company objected within 30 days after the homeowner disclosed the deposition testimony she intended to use at trial.","D":"Yes, because the homeowner failed to disclose the deposition testimony at least 90 days before trial."},"answer":"B","explanation":"During discovery, parties must automatically disclose certain information through required disclosures. This includes pretrial disclosures, which must contain the following:\n• The name, address, and phone number of each witness, separately identifying witnesses expected to testify at trial and those who may be called if the need arises\n• The designation of witnesses whose testimony will be presented by deposition, including a transcript of the pertinent parts of a deposition that was not taken stenographically (ie, by shorthand)\n• All documents and exhibits, separately identifying items expected to be offered as evidence and those that may be offered if the need arises These must be disclosed at least 30 days before trial. Any objections to the use at trial of deposition testimony, documents, or exhibits must be made within 14 days after the disclosures.* An objection not made within this time is waived unless (1) excused by the court for good cause or (2) the objection is based on relevance. Here, the homeowner timely disclosed 45 days before trial the witness whose deposition testimony she intends to present at trial and attached the deposition transcript. The company untimely objected 21 days later to the use of the deposition testimony at trial. There is no indication that the company's untimely objection is excused by good cause, and the objection is based on hearsay—not relevance. Therefore, the company waived its objection, and the court should not hear the merits of the objection (Choice C). *A court may also set different deadlines for the parties to make pretrial disclosures and object to such disclosures. (Choice A) Pretrial disclosures, not objections to such disclosures, must be made at least 30 days before trial. Objections to pretrial disclosures must instead be made within 14 days after the disclosures. (Choice D) Expert witness testimony, not pretrial disclosures, must be disclosed at least 90 days before trial. Educational objective: Pretrial disclosures must be made at least 30 days before trial. Objections to the use at trial of disclosed deposition testimony, documents, or exhibits must be made within 14 days after the disclosures or such objections are generally waived. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3) (pretrial disclosures).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2054 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when pretrial disclosures and objections to such disclosures must be made).","rule":"Pretrial disclosures must be made at least 30 days before trial. Objections to the use at trial of disclosed deposition testimony, documents, or exhibits must be made within 14 days after the disclosures or such objections are generally waived.","irac":{"issue":"A homeowner brought a diversity action against a mining company in federal court for negligence, claiming that the company's negligent mining practices caused extensive damage to her property. Forty-five days before trial, the homeowner sent the company the evidence that she plans to use at trial, including a witness whose testimony she intends to present by deposition. The homeowner also attached a transcript of the pertinent depositions. Twenty-one days later, the company objected to the use of the deposition testimony on the ground that it is inadmissible hearsay. Should the court hear the merits of the company's objection?","rule":"Pretrial disclosures must be made at least 30 days before trial. Objections to the use at trial of disclosed deposition testimony, documents, or exhibits must be made within 14 days after the disclosures or such objections are generally waived.","analysis":"During discovery, parties must automatically disclose certain information through required disclosures. This includes pretrial disclosures, which must contain the following:\n• The name, address, and phone number of each witness, separately identifying witnesses expected to testify at trial and those who may be called if the need arises\n• The designation of witnesses whose testimony will be presented by deposition, including a transcript of the pertinent parts of a deposition that was not taken stenographically (ie, by shorthand)\n• All documents and exhibits, separately identifying items expected to be offered as evidence and those that may be offered if the need arises These must be disclosed at least 30 days before trial. Any objections to the use at trial of deposition testimony, documents, or exhibits must be made within 14 days after the disclosures.* An objection not made within this time is waived unless (1) excused by the court for good cause or (2) the objection is based on relevance. Here, the homeowner timely disclosed 45 days before trial the witness whose deposition testimony she intends to present at trial and attached the deposition transcript. The company untimely objected 21 days later to the use of the deposition testimony at trial. There is no indication that the company's untimely objection is excused by good cause, and the objection is based on hearsay—not relevance. Therefore, the company waived its objection, and the court should not hear the merits of the objection (Choice C). *A court may also set different deadlines for the parties to make pretrial disclosures and object to such disclosures. (Choice A) Pretrial disclosures, not objections to such disclosures, must be made at least 30 days before trial. Objections to pretrial disclosures must instead be made within 14 days after the disclosures. (Choice D) Expert witness testimony, not pretrial disclosures, must be disclosed at least 90 days before trial. Educational objective: Pretrial disclosures must be made at least 30 days before trial. Objections to the use at trial of disclosed deposition testimony, documents, or exhibits must be made within 14 days after the disclosures or such objections are generally waived. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3) (pretrial disclosures).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2054 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when pretrial disclosures and objections to such disclosures must be made).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3) (pretrial disclosures).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2054 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when pretrial disclosures and objections to such disclosures must be made).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[757],"sourceAnswerPages":[758,759],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名房主在联邦法院对一家采矿公司提起多元州籍过失诉讼，主张公司的过失采矿作业对其财产造成严重损害。审判前45天，房主向公司发送其计划在审判中使用的证据，其中包括一名她打算通过证言录取方式提交证言的证人。房主还附上相关证言录取的笔录。21天后，公司以该证言属于不可采的传闻证据为由，反对使用证言录取证言。法院是否应审查公司异议的实体理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为公司没有在审判前至少30天提出异议。","B":"不应，因为公司没有在房主披露拟在审判中使用的证言录取证言后14天内提出异议。","C":"应当，因为公司在房主披露拟在审判中使用的证言录取证言后30天内提出了异议。","D":"应当，因为房主没有在审判前至少90天披露证言录取证言。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示期间，当事人必须通过强制披露自动披露某些信息，其中包括审前披露。审前披露必须包含：\n• 每名证人的姓名、地址和电话号码，并分别列明预期在审判中作证的证人，以及在需要时可能被传唤的证人；\n• 将通过证言录取提交证言的证人，并包括非速记方式录取之证言的相关部分笔录；\n• 所有文件和证物，并分别列明预期将作为证据提交的项目，以及在需要时可能提交的项目。\n\n这些内容必须在审判前至少30天披露。对审判中使用已披露的证言录取证言、文件或证物提出的任何异议，必须在披露后14天内作出。* 未在该期限内提出的异议即被放弃，除非：(1)法院基于正当理由予以宽免；或(2)该异议以相关性为依据。\n\n本题中，房主在审判前45天及时披露了其拟通过证言录取证言在审判中作证的证人，并附上证言录取笔录。公司在21天后才反对在审判中使用该证言，异议不及时。没有迹象表明该迟延具有正当理由，而且异议基于传闻，而非相关性。因此，公司已放弃异议，法院不应审查异议的实体理由（选项C）。\n\n*法院也可以另行确定当事人进行审前披露以及反对该披露的期限。\n\n（选项A）须在审判前至少30天作出的是审前披露，而不是对审前披露的异议。对审前披露的异议应在披露后14天内提出。\n\n（选项D）须在审判前至少90天披露的是专家证人证言，而非审前披露。\n\n学习目标：审前披露必须在审判前至少30天作出。对审判中使用已披露的证言录取证言、文件或证物的异议，必须在披露后14天内提出，否则一般视为放弃。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3)（审前披露）。\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2054 (3d ed. 2020)（说明审前披露及对其提出异议的期限）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-080"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-252","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":252,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A sailor filed a diversity action against a defendant in a federal district court to recover damages for injuries sustained in a boating accident. The district court ordered the attorneys for both parties to attend the first pretrial conference, but the sailor's attorney failed to appear. This was the first and only hearing that the attorney failed to attend. As a result, the district court entered an order dismissing the sailor's action with prejudice. The court also ordered the sailor to pay the expenses incurred by the defendant for attending the conference. The sailor immediately appealed. What is the sailor's best argument for persuading the appellate court to reverse the dismissal?","choices":{"A":"The dismissal was unreasonable because the attorney had not shown a pattern of noncompliance or disobedience.","B":"The dismissal was unwarranted because the sailor was also ordered to pay the defendant's expenses.","C":"The district court committed clear error when it dismissed the case before trial.","D":"The district court may sanction a party only when it fails to obey a pretrial order."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Pretrial conferences are designed to improve the quality of trial, facilitate settlement, and expedite litigation. To underscore the importance of these conferences, a federal court typically must order* a party and/or attorney to pay the other party's reasonable expenses when the party or attorney:\n• failed to attend a pretrial conference\n• was substantially unprepared for the conference\n• did not participate in good faith in the conference or\n• failed to obey a pretrial order. In addition to ordering the payment of expenses, the court may impose sanctions on the noncompliant party or attorney (Choice B). The court has discretion to decide which sanction to impose, but the sanction must be reasonable under the circumstances—ie, the punishment must fit the violation. Otherwise, an arbitrary or unreasonable sanction will be reversed on appeal for abuse of discretion. Here, the district court dismissed the sailor's action with prejudice because the sailor's attorney failed to attend the initial pretrial conference. Dismissal of an action is one of the harshest sanctions and is appropriate to punish a pattern of disobedience or noncompliance (not seen here). Therefore, the sailor's best argument to reverse the dismissal is that this sanction was unreasonable under the circumstances. *A court should not order the payment of the other party's reasonable expenses when (1) the noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust. (Choice C) A district court's factual findings in a bench trial are reviewed for clear error and will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. But here, the district court's discretionary decision to sanction the sailor's attorney and dismiss the sailor's action with prejudice before trial will be reviewed for abuse of discretion. (Choice D) A district court may sanction a party if it failed to attend, did not participate in good faith in, or was substantially unprepared for a pretrial conference—not just when it failed to obey a pretrial order. Educational objective: A federal court must order the payment of the other party's reasonable expenses and may impose reasonable sanctions on any attorney or party who (1) failed to attend a pretrial conference, (2) was substantially unprepared for the conference, (3) did not participate in good faith in the conference, or (4) failed to obey a pretrial order. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f) (pretrial conference sanctions).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to comply with pretrial conference or order).","rule":"A federal court must order the payment of the other party's reasonable expenses and may impose reasonable sanctions on any attorney or party who (1) failed to attend a pretrial conference, (2) was substantially unprepared for the conference, (3) did not participate in good faith in the conference, or (4) failed to obey a pretrial order.","irac":{"issue":"A sailor filed a diversity action against a defendant in a federal district court to recover damages for injuries sustained in a boating accident. The district court ordered the attorneys for both parties to attend the first pretrial conference, but the sailor's attorney failed to appear. This was the first and only hearing that the attorney failed to attend. As a result, the district court entered an order dismissing the sailor's action with prejudice. The court also ordered the sailor to pay the expenses incurred by the defendant for attending the conference. The sailor immediately appealed. What is the sailor's best argument for persuading the appellate court to reverse the dismissal?","rule":"A federal court must order the payment of the other party's reasonable expenses and may impose reasonable sanctions on any attorney or party who (1) failed to attend a pretrial conference, (2) was substantially unprepared for the conference, (3) did not participate in good faith in the conference, or (4) failed to obey a pretrial order.","analysis":"Pretrial conferences are designed to improve the quality of trial, facilitate settlement, and expedite litigation. To underscore the importance of these conferences, a federal court typically must order* a party and/or attorney to pay the other party's reasonable expenses when the party or attorney:\n• failed to attend a pretrial conference\n• was substantially unprepared for the conference\n• did not participate in good faith in the conference or\n• failed to obey a pretrial order. In addition to ordering the payment of expenses, the court may impose sanctions on the noncompliant party or attorney (Choice B). The court has discretion to decide which sanction to impose, but the sanction must be reasonable under the circumstances—ie, the punishment must fit the violation. Otherwise, an arbitrary or unreasonable sanction will be reversed on appeal for abuse of discretion. Here, the district court dismissed the sailor's action with prejudice because the sailor's attorney failed to attend the initial pretrial conference. Dismissal of an action is one of the harshest sanctions and is appropriate to punish a pattern of disobedience or noncompliance (not seen here). Therefore, the sailor's best argument to reverse the dismissal is that this sanction was unreasonable under the circumstances. *A court should not order the payment of the other party's reasonable expenses when (1) the noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust. (Choice C) A district court's factual findings in a bench trial are reviewed for clear error and will only be reversed if no reasonable judge would have made such a finding. But here, the district court's discretionary decision to sanction the sailor's attorney and dismiss the sailor's action with prejudice before trial will be reviewed for abuse of discretion. (Choice D) A district court may sanction a party if it failed to attend, did not participate in good faith in, or was substantially unprepared for a pretrial conference—not just when it failed to obey a pretrial order. Educational objective: A federal court must order the payment of the other party's reasonable expenses and may impose reasonable sanctions on any attorney or party who (1) failed to attend a pretrial conference, (2) was substantially unprepared for the conference, (3) did not participate in good faith in the conference, or (4) failed to obey a pretrial order. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f) (pretrial conference sanctions).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to comply with pretrial conference or order).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f) (pretrial conference sanctions).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to comply with pretrial conference or order).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[760],"sourceAnswerPages":[761,762],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名水手在联邦地区法院对一名被告提起多元州籍诉讼，要求赔偿其在船舶事故中所受伤害。地区法院命令双方律师参加第一次审前会议，但水手的律师没有出席。这是该律师第一次也是唯一一次未参加听证会。因此，地区法院作出命令，以有偏见方式驳回水手的诉讼。法院还命令水手支付被告为参加会议而支出的费用。水手立即上诉。水手说服上诉法院推翻驳回裁定的最佳论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回不合理，因为该律师并未表现出持续不遵守或不服从的行为模式。","B":"驳回缺乏正当依据，因为法院还命令水手支付被告的费用。","C":"地区法院在审判前驳回案件，构成明显错误。","D":"地区法院只有在一方不遵守审前命令时才能对其制裁。"},"explanationZh":"审前会议旨在提高审判质量、促进和解并加快诉讼。为强调此类会议的重要性，联邦法院通常必须命令*存在下列行为的一方或律师支付对方的合理费用：\n• 未参加审前会议；\n• 对会议准备严重不足；\n• 未善意参与会议；或者\n• 不遵守审前命令。\n\n除命令支付费用外，法院还可对不遵守要求的一方或律师施加制裁（选项B）。法院有权酌情决定采用何种制裁，但制裁在具体情形下必须合理，即处罚必须与违规相称。否则，恣意或不合理的制裁将在上诉中以滥用裁量权为由被推翻。\n\n本题中，地区法院因水手的律师未参加第一次审前会议，而以有偏见方式驳回水手的诉讼。驳回诉讼是最严厉的制裁之一，适合用于惩罚持续不服从或不遵守要求的行为模式（本题并无这种情况）。因此，水手推翻驳回裁定的最佳论点是，该制裁在具体情形下不合理。\n\n*如果：(1)不遵守要求具有充分正当理由；或(2)命令支付费用会造成不公，法院不应命令支付对方的合理费用。\n\n（选项C）地区法院在法官审判中作出的事实认定，按明显错误标准审查；只有不存在任何合理法官会作出该认定时才会被推翻。但本题中，地区法院在审判前酌情决定制裁水手的律师并以有偏见方式驳回诉讼，应按滥用裁量权标准审查。\n\n（选项D）地区法院并非只能在一方不遵守审前命令时施加制裁；一方未参加审前会议、未善意参与会议或准备严重不足时，法院也可以制裁。\n\n学习目标：联邦法院必须命令存在下列行为的律师或当事人支付对方的合理费用，并可以施加合理制裁：(1)未参加审前会议；(2)对会议准备严重不足；(3)未善意参与会议；或(4)不遵守审前命令。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f)（审前会议制裁）。\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020)（说明何时适合因不遵守审前会议或命令而施加制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-081"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-253","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":253,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A bicyclist sued the drivers of two cars in a federal district court after all three were involved in an accident. In her answer, the first driver denied liability and alleged that the second driver caused the accident by running a red light. The first driver also seeks to assert a claim against the second driver to recover damages for the injuries she suffered in the accident. May the first driver include this claim in her answer?","choices":{"A":"No, because the first driver must first implead the second driver.","B":"No, because the first driver must obtain the bicyclist's permission before asserting the claim.","C":"Yes, because the claim arises from the accident that gave rise to the bicyclist's claim.","D":"Yes, because the first driver may assert as many claims as she has against the second driver."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A crossclaim is a claim for relief asserted against a coparty—eg, a defendant against a codefendant (as seen here). Crossclaims are permissive (not compulsory) and can only be asserted if they arise from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the lawsuit (Choice D). Here, the first driver's claim against the second driver arises from the same accident as the bicyclist's claims against both drivers. Therefore, the first driver may assert that crossclaim in her answer. (Choice A) Impleader (ie, third-party practice) allows—but does not require—a defendant to add a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all (ie, indemnity) or part (ie, contribution) of the plaintiff's claim. But since the bicyclist included the second driver in the suit, impleader is unnecessary. (Choice B) A party asserting a crossclaim (first driver) against a coparty (second driver) does not need permission from the opposing party (bicyclist). Educational objective: A crossclaim is a permissive claim for relief against a coparty that can only be asserted if it arises from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the suit. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(g) (crossclaim).","rule":"A crossclaim is a permissive claim for relief against a coparty that can only be asserted if it arises from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the suit.","irac":{"issue":"A bicyclist sued the drivers of two cars in a federal district court after all three were involved in an accident. In her answer, the first driver denied liability and alleged that the second driver caused the accident by running a red light. The first driver also seeks to assert a claim against the second driver to recover damages for the injuries she suffered in the accident. May the first driver include this claim in her answer?","rule":"A crossclaim is a permissive claim for relief against a coparty that can only be asserted if it arises from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the suit.","analysis":"A crossclaim is a claim for relief asserted against a coparty—eg, a defendant against a codefendant (as seen here). Crossclaims are permissive (not compulsory) and can only be asserted if they arise from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the lawsuit (Choice D). Here, the first driver's claim against the second driver arises from the same accident as the bicyclist's claims against both drivers. Therefore, the first driver may assert that crossclaim in her answer. (Choice A) Impleader (ie, third-party practice) allows—but does not require—a defendant to add a nonparty who may be liable to the defendant for all (ie, indemnity) or part (ie, contribution) of the plaintiff's claim. But since the bicyclist included the second driver in the suit, impleader is unnecessary. (Choice B) A party asserting a crossclaim (first driver) against a coparty (second driver) does not need permission from the opposing party (bicyclist). Educational objective: A crossclaim is a permissive claim for relief against a coparty that can only be asserted if it arises from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the suit. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(g) (crossclaim).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(g) (crossclaim).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[765],"sourceAnswerPages":[766],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名骑自行车者在与两辆汽车的驾驶员共同卷入事故后，在联邦地区法院起诉两名驾驶员。第一名驾驶员在答辩状中否认责任，并主张第二名驾驶员闯红灯造成事故。第一名驾驶员还希望向第二名驾驶员提出请求，以赔偿其在事故中所受伤害。第一名驾驶员可以在答辩状中加入该请求吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为第一名驾驶员必须先引入第二名驾驶员作为第三方被告。","B":"不可以，因为第一名驾驶员提出该请求前必须取得骑自行车者的许可。","C":"可以，因为该请求源于引起骑自行车者请求的同一起事故。","D":"可以，因为第一名驾驶员可以向第二名驾驶员提出其拥有的任意数量请求。"},"explanationZh":"交叉请求是针对共同当事人提出的救济请求，例如一名被告对共同被告提出请求（本题即如此）。交叉请求属于许可性请求而非强制性请求，而且只有在其源于诉讼中另一项请求所涉及的同一交易或事件时才能提出（选项D）。\n\n本题中，第一名驾驶员针对第二名驾驶员的请求，源于骑自行车者针对两名驾驶员所提请求涉及的同一起事故。因此，第一名驾驶员可以在答辩状中提出该交叉请求。\n\n（选项A）引入第三方被告（即第三方程序）允许——但不要求——被告加入可能就原告请求的全部（即补偿）或一部分（即分担）向被告承担责任的非当事人。但骑自行车者已经把第二名驾驶员列入诉讼，所以无须引入第三方被告。\n\n（选项B）一方（第一名驾驶员）向共同当事人（第二名驾驶员）提出交叉请求，无须取得对方当事人（骑自行车者）的许可。\n\n学习目标：交叉请求是针对共同当事人的许可性救济请求，只有在其源于诉讼中另一项请求所涉及的同一交易或事件时才能提出。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(g)（交叉请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-082"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-254","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":254,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A group of neighbors sued a power plant in a federal court for allegedly contaminating the river that flows through their properties. In its answer, the power plant included a counterclaim for defamation. The power plant claimed that the neighbors maliciously spread false information that it had contaminated the river. One week before the start of trial, the judge and the parties had a final pretrial conference. At the conference, the judge ordered a separate trial for the power plant's defamation claim, decided that the river contamination trial would proceed first, and established that each party would get five days to present evidence in each trial as this was the customary time allotted in the jurisdiction. The power plant objected to each of these orders. At the river contamination trial, the jury found in favor of the neighbors and awarded them $3 million in damages. As a result of this trial, the judge dismissed the power plant's defamation counterclaim. The power plant appealed, arguing that the trial judge's orders during the pretrial conference were invalid. How is the federal appellate court likely to rule?","choices":{"A":"Affirm all three orders.","B":"Reverse the trial judge's order requiring a separate trial on the counterclaim.","C":"Reverse the trial judge's order allowing each side just five days to present evidence.","D":"Reverse the trial judge's order to hear the river contamination trial before the defamation trial."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Pretrial conferences\n\n(FRCP 16(c))\n\nPurpose Matters for consideration\n\nExpedite litigation • Eliminate frivolous claims & defenses\n• Obtain admissions & stipulations\n• Set timeline for summary judgment & dispose of pending motions\n• Schedule discovery, pretrial briefs & future conferences Improve quality of • Amend pleadings trial • Refer matters to magistrate or master\n• Require separate trial for different claims\n• Adopt special procedures for complex/unusual legal issues\n• Rule on admissibility of evidence\n• Avoid cumulative evidence & limit expert testimony\n• Establish order & time limit for presentation of evidence Facilitate settlement • Facilitate just, speedy & inexpensive resolution\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 16, a federal district court judge may hold one or more pretrial conferences to help expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. At these conferences, the judge has broad discretion to address and act on a wide range of issues, including:\n• requiring separate trials of a claim, counterclaim, crossclaim, third-party claim, or other issue (Choice B)\n• determining the order in which the separate trials will be heard (Choice D) and\n• establishing a reasonable time limit to present evidence at trial (Choice C). On appeal, these discretionary orders are reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this standard of review, the appellate court gives great deference to the district court judge and will only reverse an unreasonable or arbitrary order. Here, the district court judge had authority to issue the pretrial conference orders, and there is no indication that those orders were unreasonable or arbitrary. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm all three orders. Educational objective:\n\nDuring pretrial conferences, a trial judge has wide discretion to issue orders—eg, (1) requiring separate trials for different claims, (2) deciding the order of trials, and (3) establishing a reasonable time limit to present evidence. On appeal, such orders are reviewed for abuse of discretion and will only be reversed if unreasonable or arbitrary. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16 (pretrial conferences).","rule":"During pretrial conferences, a trial judge has wide discretion to issue orders—eg, (1) requiring separate trials for different claims, (2) deciding the order of trials, and (3) establishing a reasonable time limit to present evidence. On appeal, such orders are reviewed for abuse of discretion and will only be reversed if unreasonable or arbitrary.","irac":{"issue":"A group of neighbors sued a power plant in a federal court for allegedly contaminating the river that flows through their properties. In its answer, the power plant included a counterclaim for defamation. The power plant claimed that the neighbors maliciously spread false information that it had contaminated the river. One week before the start of trial, the judge and the parties had a final pretrial conference. At the conference, the judge ordered a separate trial for the power plant's defamation claim, decided that the river contamination trial would proceed first, and established that each party would get five days to present evidence in each trial as this was the customary time allotted in the jurisdiction. The power plant objected to each of these orders. At the river contamination trial, the jury found in favor of the neighbors and awarded them $3 million in damages. As a result of this trial, the judge dismissed the power plant's defamation counterclaim. The power plant appealed, arguing that the trial judge's orders during the pretrial conference were invalid. How is the federal appellate court likely to rule?","rule":"During pretrial conferences, a trial judge has wide discretion to issue orders—eg, (1) requiring separate trials for different claims, (2) deciding the order of trials, and (3) establishing a reasonable time limit to present evidence. On appeal, such orders are reviewed for abuse of discretion and will only be reversed if unreasonable or arbitrary.","analysis":"Pretrial conferences\n\n(FRCP 16(c))\n\nPurpose Matters for consideration\n\nExpedite litigation • Eliminate frivolous claims & defenses\n• Obtain admissions & stipulations\n• Set timeline for summary judgment & dispose of pending motions\n• Schedule discovery, pretrial briefs & future conferences Improve quality of • Amend pleadings trial • Refer matters to magistrate or master\n• Require separate trial for different claims\n• Adopt special procedures for complex/unusual legal issues\n• Rule on admissibility of evidence\n• Avoid cumulative evidence & limit expert testimony\n• Establish order & time limit for presentation of evidence Facilitate settlement • Facilitate just, speedy & inexpensive resolution\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 16, a federal district court judge may hold one or more pretrial conferences to help expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. At these conferences, the judge has broad discretion to address and act on a wide range of issues, including:\n• requiring separate trials of a claim, counterclaim, crossclaim, third-party claim, or other issue (Choice B)\n• determining the order in which the separate trials will be heard (Choice D) and\n• establishing a reasonable time limit to present evidence at trial (Choice C). On appeal, these discretionary orders are reviewed for abuse of discretion. Under this standard of review, the appellate court gives great deference to the district court judge and will only reverse an unreasonable or arbitrary order. Here, the district court judge had authority to issue the pretrial conference orders, and there is no indication that those orders were unreasonable or arbitrary. Therefore, the appellate court will likely affirm all three orders. Educational objective:\n\nDuring pretrial conferences, a trial judge has wide discretion to issue orders—eg, (1) requiring separate trials for different claims, (2) deciding the order of trials, and (3) establishing a reasonable time limit to present evidence. On appeal, such orders are reviewed for abuse of discretion and will only be reversed if unreasonable or arbitrary. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16 (pretrial conferences).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16 (pretrial conferences).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[768],"sourceAnswerPages":[769,770],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一群邻居在联邦法院起诉一家发电厂，指称其污染流经邻居各自房产的河流。发电厂在答辩状中提出诽谤反请求，主张邻居恶意散布其污染河流的虚假信息。审判开始前一周，法官和双方举行最终审前会议。会上，法官命令对发电厂的诽谤请求另行审判，决定先审理河流污染案件，并规定在每一场审判中每方有五天时间提交证据，因为这是该司法辖区的惯常时限。发电厂反对这三项命令。河流污染案审判中，陪审团裁决邻居胜诉并判给300万美元损害赔偿。基于该审判结果，法官驳回发电厂的诽谤反请求。发电厂提出上诉，主张初审法官在审前会议上作出的命令无效。联邦上诉法院很可能如何裁判？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持全部三项命令。","B":"推翻初审法官要求对反请求另行审判的命令。","C":"推翻初审法官只允许每方用五天提交证据的命令。","D":"推翻初审法官先审理河流污染案、后审理诽谤案的命令。"},"explanationZh":"审前会议\n\n（FRCP 16(c)）\n\n目的｜可审议事项\n\n加快诉讼\n• 排除无理取闹的请求和抗辩；\n• 取得承认和约定；\n• 设定简易判决时间表并处理待决动议；\n• 安排证据开示、审前书状和未来会议。\n\n提高审判质量\n• 修正诉状；\n• 将事项交由司法官或专门审理人；\n• 要求对不同请求分别审判；\n• 针对复杂或特殊法律问题采用专门程序；\n• 裁定证据可采性；\n• 避免重复证据并限制专家证言；\n• 确定提交证据的顺序和时限。\n\n促进和解\n• 促进公正、迅速且低成本地解决案件。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》16，联邦地区法院法官可以举行一次或多次审前会议，以帮助加快诉讼、提高审判质量和促进和解。在此类会议中，法官对于处理和决定广泛事项拥有宽泛裁量权，包括：\n• 命令对请求、反请求、交叉请求、第三方请求或其他事项分别审判（选项B）；\n• 决定各场独立审判的先后顺序（选项D）；以及\n• 对审判中提交证据设定合理时限（选项C）。\n\n上诉时，这些裁量性命令按滥用裁量权标准审查。依该标准，上诉法院会高度尊重地区法院法官，只有在命令不合理或恣意时才会推翻。本题中，地区法院法官有权发布这些审前会议命令，也没有迹象表明命令不合理或恣意。因此，上诉法院很可能维持全部三项命令。\n\n学习目标：在审前会议中，初审法官拥有宽泛裁量权发布命令，例如：(1)要求不同请求分别审判；(2)决定审判顺序；以及(3)规定提交证据的合理时限。上诉时，此类命令按滥用裁量权标准审查，只有在不合理或恣意时才会被推翻。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16（审前会议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-255","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":255,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A pedestrian filed a federal diversity action against a motorcyclist, alleging that the motorcyclist ran a stop sign and hit the pedestrian in a crosswalk outside of a bar. During discovery, the pedestrian's attorney interviewed a bartender who was working at the bar on the night the accident occurred. The bartender told the attorney that the motorcyclist drank four bottles of beer and two shots of tequila in the hour immediately preceding the accident. The attorney wrote down the bartender's statements in a document. The motorcyclist requested that the pedestrian produce all documents related to the case, including statements by potential witnesses. In response, the pedestrian's attorney produced several documents and a privilege log of the documents withheld. The privilege log included a description of the document containing the bartender's statements. The motorcyclist filed a motion to compel production of this document. Should the court grant the motorcyclist's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the document is inadmissible at trial.","B":"No, because the motorcyclist can obtain the information in the document by other means.","C":"Yes, because the document does not contain the attorney's opinions or legal theories.","D":"Yes, because the motorcyclist has a right to receive all materials containing information relevant to the case."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Work product\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(3))\n\nType Definition Discoverability\n\nOrdinary Materials created in anticipation of Not discoverable unless requesting litigation that contain factual information party: related to case\n• has substantial need for materials and\n• cannot obtain information without undue hardship Opinion Materials created in anticipation of Not discoverable unless: litigation that contain attorney's mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, or • material concerns legal theories attorney's alleged fraudulent/illegal conduct (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDuring discovery, a party can use various methods (eg, request for production) to obtain nonprivileged information that is (1) relevant to any party's claim or defense and (2) proportional to the needs of the case. As a result, a party generally cannot obtain privileged information—including materials prepared by or for an attorney in anticipation of litigation (ie, work product) (Choice D). Work product falls into one of two categories:\n• Ordinary – materials containing factual information related to the case (eg, a document containing a witness's statements—as seen here)\n• Opinion – materials containing the attorney's mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, or legal theories (eg, a document containing the attorney's trial strategy) Opinion work product is almost never discoverable.* And ordinary work product is only discoverable if the requesting party shows that it (1) has a substantial need for the materials and (2) cannot obtain the information contained therein without undue hardship—eg, the witness dies before the requesting party can interview him/her. Here, the motorcyclist cannot demonstrate a substantial need for the document or undue hardship in obtaining the information contained therein. That is because the motorcyclist can easily obtain this information by other means—eg, by having his attorney interview or\n\ndepose the bartender. Therefore, the court should deny the motorcyclist's motion to compel production of the document. *Most courts have held that opinion work product is only discoverable in the rare instance when the materials show that the attorney committed fraud or another crime. (Choice A) The document's admissibility at trial has no effect on whether it is discoverable because the scope of discovery includes inadmissible information and materials. (Choice C) The document is not opinion work product because it does not contain the attorney's impressions, conclusions, opinions, or legal theories. However, it is ordinary work product that is not subject to discovery. Educational objective: Ordinary work product is any material prepared by or for an attorney in anticipation of litigation that contains factual information related to the case. These materials are protected from discovery unless the requesting party shows that it (1) has a substantial need for the materials and (2) cannot obtain the information without undue hardship. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3) (work product and trial-preparation materials).\n\n• Hickman v. Taylor, 329 U.S. 495, 511–12 (1947) (explaining the importance of protecting work-product materials from disclosure and when such materials may need to be disclosed).","rule":"Ordinary work product is any material prepared by or for an attorney in anticipation of litigation that contains factual information related to the case. These materials are protected from discovery unless the requesting party shows that it (1) has a substantial need for the materials and (2) cannot obtain the information without undue hardship.","irac":{"issue":"A pedestrian filed a federal diversity action against a motorcyclist, alleging that the motorcyclist ran a stop sign and hit the pedestrian in a crosswalk outside of a bar. During discovery, the pedestrian's attorney interviewed a bartender who was working at the bar on the night the accident occurred. The bartender told the attorney that the motorcyclist drank four bottles of beer and two shots of tequila in the hour immediately preceding the accident. The attorney wrote down the bartender's statements in a document. The motorcyclist requested that the pedestrian produce all documents related to the case, including statements by potential witnesses. In response, the pedestrian's attorney produced several documents and a privilege log of the documents withheld. The privilege log included a description of the document containing the bartender's statements. The motorcyclist filed a motion to compel production of this document. Should the court grant the motorcyclist's motion?","rule":"Ordinary work product is any material prepared by or for an attorney in anticipation of litigation that contains factual information related to the case. These materials are protected from discovery unless the requesting party shows that it (1) has a substantial need for the materials and (2) cannot obtain the information without undue hardship.","analysis":"Work product\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(3))\n\nType Definition Discoverability\n\nOrdinary Materials created in anticipation of Not discoverable unless requesting litigation that contain factual information party: related to case\n• has substantial need for materials and\n• cannot obtain information without undue hardship Opinion Materials created in anticipation of Not discoverable unless: litigation that contain attorney's mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, or • material concerns legal theories attorney's alleged fraudulent/illegal conduct (rare) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDuring discovery, a party can use various methods (eg, request for production) to obtain nonprivileged information that is (1) relevant to any party's claim or defense and (2) proportional to the needs of the case. As a result, a party generally cannot obtain privileged information—including materials prepared by or for an attorney in anticipation of litigation (ie, work product) (Choice D). Work product falls into one of two categories:\n• Ordinary – materials containing factual information related to the case (eg, a document containing a witness's statements—as seen here)\n• Opinion – materials containing the attorney's mental impressions, conclusions, opinions, or legal theories (eg, a document containing the attorney's trial strategy) Opinion work product is almost never discoverable.* And ordinary work product is only discoverable if the requesting party shows that it (1) has a substantial need for the materials and (2) cannot obtain the information contained therein without undue hardship—eg, the witness dies before the requesting party can interview him/her. Here, the motorcyclist cannot demonstrate a substantial need for the document or undue hardship in obtaining the information contained therein. That is because the motorcyclist can easily obtain this information by other means—eg, by having his attorney interview or\n\ndepose the bartender. Therefore, the court should deny the motorcyclist's motion to compel production of the document. *Most courts have held that opinion work product is only discoverable in the rare instance when the materials show that the attorney committed fraud or another crime. (Choice A) The document's admissibility at trial has no effect on whether it is discoverable because the scope of discovery includes inadmissible information and materials. (Choice C) The document is not opinion work product because it does not contain the attorney's impressions, conclusions, opinions, or legal theories. However, it is ordinary work product that is not subject to discovery. Educational objective: Ordinary work product is any material prepared by or for an attorney in anticipation of litigation that contains factual information related to the case. These materials are protected from discovery unless the requesting party shows that it (1) has a substantial need for the materials and (2) cannot obtain the information without undue hardship. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3) (work product and trial-preparation materials).\n\n• Hickman v. Taylor, 329 U.S. 495, 511–12 (1947) (explaining the importance of protecting work-product materials from disclosure and when such materials may need to be disclosed).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3) (work product and trial-preparation materials).\n\n• Hickman v. Taylor, 329 U.S. 495, 511–12 (1947) (explaining the importance of protecting work-product materials from disclosure and when such materials may need to be disclosed).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[771],"sourceAnswerPages":[772,773],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名行人在联邦法院对一名摩托车手提起多元州籍诉讼，指称摩托车手闯停车标志，并在一家酒吧外的人行横道撞到行人。证据开示期间，行人的律师询问了事故当晚在酒吧工作的酒保。酒保告诉律师，在事故发生前紧接的一小时内，摩托车手喝了四瓶啤酒和两杯龙舌兰烈酒。律师把酒保的陈述写入一份文件。摩托车手要求行人提供与案件有关的所有文件，包括潜在证人的陈述。作为回应，行人的律师提供了若干文件，并提交所扣留文件的特权文件清单，其中包含对载有酒保陈述之文件的描述。摩托车手申请强制提供该文件。法院是否应批准该申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为该文件在审判中不可采。","B":"不应，因为摩托车手可以通过其他方式取得文件中的信息。","C":"应当，因为该文件不包含律师的意见或法律理论。","D":"应当，因为摩托车手有权取得包含案件相关信息的所有材料。"},"explanationZh":"诉讼准备材料\n\n（FRCP 26(b)(3)）\n\n类型｜定义｜可开示性\n\n普通诉讼准备材料\n为预期诉讼而制作，包含与案件有关事实信息的材料。\n除非请求方：\n• 对材料有重大需要；且\n• 不承受不当困难就无法取得信息，\n否则不可开示。\n\n意见诉讼准备材料\n为预期诉讼而制作，包含律师的思维印象、结论、意见或法律理论的材料。\n除非：\n• 材料涉及律师被指实施的欺诈或违法行为（罕见），\n否则不可开示。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n证据开示期间，一方可以运用多种方式（例如提供请求），取得：(1)与任何一方请求或抗辩相关，且(2)与案件需要相称的非特权信息。因此，一方通常不能取得特权信息，包括由律师或为律师预期诉讼而准备的材料（即诉讼准备材料）（选项D）。诉讼准备材料分为两类：\n• 普通材料——包含与案件有关的事实信息，例如载有证人陈述的文件（本题即如此）；\n• 意见材料——包含律师的思维印象、结论、意见或法律理论，例如载有律师审判策略的文件。\n\n意见诉讼准备材料几乎从不可开示。* 普通诉讼准备材料只有在请求方证明：(1)其对该材料有重大需要；且(2)不承受不当困难就无法取得其中信息时，才可开示；例如证人在请求方能够询问前去世。\n\n本题中，摩托车手无法证明对该文件有重大需要，也无法证明取得其中信息存在不当困难。这是因为摩托车手可以轻易通过其他方式取得该信息，例如让其律师询问酒保或对酒保进行证言录取。因此，法院应驳回摩托车手要求强制提供文件的申请。\n\n*多数法院认为，只有在材料表明律师实施了欺诈或其他犯罪这一罕见情形下，意见诉讼准备材料才可开示。\n\n（选项A）该文件在审判中是否可采，不影响其是否可开示，因为证据开示范围包括不可采的信息和材料。\n\n（选项C）该文件不包含律师的思维印象、结论、意见或法律理论，所以不是意见诉讼准备材料。但它是不可开示的普通诉讼准备材料。\n\n学习目标：普通诉讼准备材料是由律师或为律师预期诉讼而准备、包含与案件有关事实信息的任何材料。除非请求方证明：(1)对材料有重大需要；且(2)不承受不当困难就无法取得信息，否则该材料受保护而不得开示。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3)（诉讼准备材料与审判准备材料）。\n• Hickman v. Taylor, 329 U.S. 495, 511–12 (1947)（说明保护诉讼准备材料不被披露的重要性，以及何时可能需要披露）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-256","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":256,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A man asked his attorney whether he had a legal basis to challenge a rate increase proposed by his electric utility company. The attorney said that he did and filed a federal action seeking a declaratory judgment that the proposed increase would violate federal law. Soon thereafter, the court issued an order requiring the man and his attorney to show cause why they should not be sanctioned under Rule 11 and required to pay a monetary penalty to the court for filing a frivolous complaint. The order cited several recent judicial decisions holding that rate increases such as the one proposed do not violate federal law. May the court impose this monetary sanction on the man?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court cannot impose a monetary sanction under Rule 11 without a motion seeking such a sanction.","B":"No, because the man is represented by an attorney.","C":"Yes, because the action is not warranted by existing law.","D":"Yes, because the court issued a show-cause order before imposing any sanction."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Common FRCP 11 sanctions\n\nMonetary* • Order fine payable to court\n• Order payment of opposing party's attorney's fees & other expenses Nonmonetary • Strike pleading, motion, or other document\n• Admonish, reprimand, or censure offending party, attorney, or law firm\n• Require participation in training/seminar\n• Refer issue to disciplinary authorities (eg, state legal ethics committee) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed on represented party for violation of FRCP 11(b)(2).\n\nEvery pleading, motion, or other document filed with the court must be signed by an attorney or an unrepresented party. This signature certifies that the document complies with the provisions of FRCP 11(b): 1. The document is presented for a proper purpose—not to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.\n\n2. All claims, defenses, and legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law.\n\n3. All factual assertions have or will have evidentiary support.\n\n4. All factual denials are warranted by evidence or are reasonably based on a belief or a lack of information.\n\nA court can impose sanctions for violations of these provisions but cannot impose a monetary sanction on a party represented by an attorney for a violation of FRCP 11(b)(2). Here, the man sought a declaratory judgment that the electric utility company's proposed rate increase would violate federal law even though several recent judicial decisions have held that such increases do not do so. The court issued an order requiring the man and his attorney to show cause why it should not impose monetary sanctions on them for filing a frivolous complaint in violation of FRCP 11(b)(2). But since the man is represented by an attorney, the court may not impose the monetary sanction on the man (Choice C).*\n\n*The court may still impose a monetary sanction on the man's attorney or the attorney's law firm. (Choices A & D) A court may impose sanctions on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) if it previously issued an order to show cause on the alleged violator. Although the court properly issued a show-cause order before imposing a sanction, a monetary sanction cannot be imposed against the man for an alleged violation of FRCP 11(b)(2) since he is represented by an attorney. Educational objective: A court cannot impose a monetary sanction on a party represented by an attorney for violating FRCP 11(b)(2). This provision requires that all claims, defenses, and legal contentions be warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c) (sanctions for violating this rule).","rule":"A court cannot impose a monetary sanction on a party represented by an attorney for violating FRCP 11(b)(2). This provision requires that all claims, defenses, and legal contentions be warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law.","irac":{"issue":"A man asked his attorney whether he had a legal basis to challenge a rate increase proposed by his electric utility company. The attorney said that he did and filed a federal action seeking a declaratory judgment that the proposed increase would violate federal law. Soon thereafter, the court issued an order requiring the man and his attorney to show cause why they should not be sanctioned under Rule 11 and required to pay a monetary penalty to the court for filing a frivolous complaint. The order cited several recent judicial decisions holding that rate increases such as the one proposed do not violate federal law. May the court impose this monetary sanction on the man?","rule":"A court cannot impose a monetary sanction on a party represented by an attorney for violating FRCP 11(b)(2). This provision requires that all claims, defenses, and legal contentions be warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law.","analysis":"Common FRCP 11 sanctions\n\nMonetary* • Order fine payable to court\n• Order payment of opposing party's attorney's fees & other expenses Nonmonetary • Strike pleading, motion, or other document\n• Admonish, reprimand, or censure offending party, attorney, or law firm\n• Require participation in training/seminar\n• Refer issue to disciplinary authorities (eg, state legal ethics committee) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed on represented party for violation of FRCP 11(b)(2).\n\nEvery pleading, motion, or other document filed with the court must be signed by an attorney or an unrepresented party. This signature certifies that the document complies with the provisions of FRCP 11(b): 1. The document is presented for a proper purpose—not to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.\n\n2. All claims, defenses, and legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law.\n\n3. All factual assertions have or will have evidentiary support.\n\n4. All factual denials are warranted by evidence or are reasonably based on a belief or a lack of information.\n\nA court can impose sanctions for violations of these provisions but cannot impose a monetary sanction on a party represented by an attorney for a violation of FRCP 11(b)(2). Here, the man sought a declaratory judgment that the electric utility company's proposed rate increase would violate federal law even though several recent judicial decisions have held that such increases do not do so. The court issued an order requiring the man and his attorney to show cause why it should not impose monetary sanctions on them for filing a frivolous complaint in violation of FRCP 11(b)(2). But since the man is represented by an attorney, the court may not impose the monetary sanction on the man (Choice C).*\n\n*The court may still impose a monetary sanction on the man's attorney or the attorney's law firm. (Choices A & D) A court may impose sanctions on its own initiative (ie, sua sponte) if it previously issued an order to show cause on the alleged violator. Although the court properly issued a show-cause order before imposing a sanction, a monetary sanction cannot be imposed against the man for an alleged violation of FRCP 11(b)(2) since he is represented by an attorney. Educational objective: A court cannot impose a monetary sanction on a party represented by an attorney for violating FRCP 11(b)(2). This provision requires that all claims, defenses, and legal contentions be warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c) (sanctions for violating this rule).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c) (sanctions for violating this rule).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[774],"sourceAnswerPages":[775,776],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子询问其律师，他是否有法律依据挑战电力公用事业公司拟议的费率上调。律师称有依据，并提起联邦诉讼，请求作出宣告判决，确认拟议上调将违反联邦法。此后不久，法院发布命令，要求男子及其律师说明理由，解释为何不应因提出无理取闹的起诉状而依据规则11受到制裁，并被要求向法院支付罚款。该命令引用了若干近期司法判决，这些判决认定此类拟议费率上调不违反联邦法。法院可以对该男子施加这项金钱制裁吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为没有寻求该制裁的动议，法院不能依据规则11施加金钱制裁。","B":"不可以，因为该男子由律师代理。","C":"可以，因为该诉讼没有现行法律依据。","D":"可以，因为法院在施加任何制裁前发布了说明理由令。"},"explanationZh":"常见FRCP 11制裁\n\n金钱制裁*\n• 命令向法院缴纳罚款；\n• 命令支付对方的律师费及其他费用。\n\n非金钱制裁\n• 删除诉状、动议或其他文件；\n• 告诫、申斥或谴责违规当事人、律师或律师事务所；\n• 要求参加培训或研讨会；\n• 将问题移送纪律机构（例如州法律伦理委员会）。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不能因违反FRCP 11(b)(2)而对由律师代理的当事人施加金钱制裁。\n\n向法院提交的每份诉状、动议或其他文件均必须由律师或无律师代理的当事人签署。该签名证明文件符合FRCP 11(b)的规定：\n1. 文件是为正当目的提出，而非为了骚扰、造成不必要延误或无谓增加诉讼费用。\n2. 所有请求、抗辩和法律主张有现行法律依据，或有不属无理取闹、旨在修改现行法律或创设新法的论证依据。\n3. 所有事实陈述已有或将会有证据支持。\n4. 所有事实否认均有证据依据，或根据相信或缺乏信息而合理作出。\n\n法院可以对违反这些规定的行为施加制裁，但不能因违反FRCP 11(b)(2)而对由律师代理的当事人施加金钱制裁。本题中，男子请求作出宣告判决，确认电力公用事业公司拟议上调费率将违反联邦法，尽管若干近期司法判决已经认定此类上调并不违反联邦法。法院发布命令，要求男子及其律师说明理由，解释为何不应因提出无理取闹的起诉状、违反FRCP 11(b)(2)而对其施加金钱制裁。但是，由于男子由律师代理，法院不得对男子本人施加该金钱制裁（选项C）。*\n\n*法院仍可对男子的律师或律师事务所施加金钱制裁。\n\n（选项A和D）法院可以依职权施加制裁，但须事先向被指违规者发布说明理由令。虽然法院在施加制裁前适当地发布了说明理由令，但由于男子由律师代理，不能因其被指违反FRCP 11(b)(2)而对他施加金钱制裁。\n\n学习目标：法院不能因违反FRCP 11(b)(2)而对由律师代理的当事人施加金钱制裁。该条要求所有请求、抗辩和法律主张有现行法律依据，或有不属无理取闹、旨在修改现行法律或创设新法的论证依据。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c)（违反本规则的制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-257","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":257,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman from State A contracted with a builder from State B to construct her new home in State A for $500,000. The builder ordered all the necessary materials, including drywall, and built the home to the woman's specifications. After one year, the drywall throughout the home began to crumble and give off an awful odor. The home became uninhabitable. The woman sued the builder for breach of contract in a federal district court in State A. The builder believes that the drywall supplier from State B should reimburse him for any damages he may owe the woman. The builder has yet to file his answer. What is the most efficient action the builder can take to seek reimbursement from the supplier?","choices":{"A":"File a crossclaim against the supplier.","B":"File a motion to join the supplier as an indispensable party.","C":"File a separate lawsuit against the supplier.","D":"File a third-party complaint against the supplier."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Ways to add parties\n\nRequired Requires addition of necessary party to suit joinder\n\nPermissive Allows addition of nonessential party to suit joinder\n\nIntervention Allows nonparty whose interests may be affected to join suit\n\nImpleader Allows defendant to add nonparty who may be liable to defendant for all or part of asserted claim to suit\n\nInterpleader Allows possessor of property to force persons who claim ownership of property to resolve dispute in single suit\n\nClass action Allows party to represent interests of entire class of similarly situated individuals\n\nImpleader (ie, third-party practice) allows a defendant to add a nonparty to a suit who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim. This means that the nonparty would reimburse the defendant for any damages paid to the plaintiff. To initiate impleader, the defendant can file a third-party complaint (1) within 14 days of serving his/her original answer or (2) after this deadline with the court's permission. Here, impleader is appropriate since the builder (defendant) believes that the supplier (nonparty) should reimburse the builder for any damages he may owe the woman (plaintiff) in the pending suit. Since the builder has not yet filed an answer, the court's permission is not required. Therefore, the most efficient way for the builder to seek reimbursement is to file a third-party complaint against the supplier. (Choice A) A crossclaim is a claim for relief asserted against a coparty—eg, a defendant against a codefendant. But here, the builder seeks relief from the supplier—a nonparty. As a result, the builder cannot file a crossclaim to obtain relief from the supplier. (Choice B) The supplier is not an indispensable party and should not be joined as such. That is because the supplier's absence will not (1) prevent the existing parties from obtaining complete relief, (2) prejudice the supplier's interest, or (3) subject an existing party to multiple or inconsistent obligations. (Choice C) The builder could file a separate lawsuit against the supplier. But since this would be more costly and time-consuming than filing a third-party complaint in the pending action, it is not the most efficient way for the builder to seek reimbursement.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant may add a nonparty to a suit by filing a third-party complaint alleging that the nonparty is liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim. The defendant need only obtain the court's leave if more than 14 days have passed since the defendant served his/her original answer. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (third-party practice).","rule":"A defendant may add a nonparty to a suit by filing a third-party complaint alleging that the nonparty is liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim. The defendant need only obtain the court's leave if more than 14 days have passed since the defendant served his/her original answer.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A contracted with a builder from State B to construct her new home in State A for $500,000. The builder ordered all the necessary materials, including drywall, and built the home to the woman's specifications. After one year, the drywall throughout the home began to crumble and give off an awful odor. The home became uninhabitable. The woman sued the builder for breach of contract in a federal district court in State A. The builder believes that the drywall supplier from State B should reimburse him for any damages he may owe the woman. The builder has yet to file his answer. What is the most efficient action the builder can take to seek reimbursement from the supplier?","rule":"A defendant may add a nonparty to a suit by filing a third-party complaint alleging that the nonparty is liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim. The defendant need only obtain the court's leave if more than 14 days have passed since the defendant served his/her original answer.","analysis":"Ways to add parties\n\nRequired Requires addition of necessary party to suit joinder\n\nPermissive Allows addition of nonessential party to suit joinder\n\nIntervention Allows nonparty whose interests may be affected to join suit\n\nImpleader Allows defendant to add nonparty who may be liable to defendant for all or part of asserted claim to suit\n\nInterpleader Allows possessor of property to force persons who claim ownership of property to resolve dispute in single suit\n\nClass action Allows party to represent interests of entire class of similarly situated individuals\n\nImpleader (ie, third-party practice) allows a defendant to add a nonparty to a suit who may be liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim. This means that the nonparty would reimburse the defendant for any damages paid to the plaintiff. To initiate impleader, the defendant can file a third-party complaint (1) within 14 days of serving his/her original answer or (2) after this deadline with the court's permission. Here, impleader is appropriate since the builder (defendant) believes that the supplier (nonparty) should reimburse the builder for any damages he may owe the woman (plaintiff) in the pending suit. Since the builder has not yet filed an answer, the court's permission is not required. Therefore, the most efficient way for the builder to seek reimbursement is to file a third-party complaint against the supplier. (Choice A) A crossclaim is a claim for relief asserted against a coparty—eg, a defendant against a codefendant. But here, the builder seeks relief from the supplier—a nonparty. As a result, the builder cannot file a crossclaim to obtain relief from the supplier. (Choice B) The supplier is not an indispensable party and should not be joined as such. That is because the supplier's absence will not (1) prevent the existing parties from obtaining complete relief, (2) prejudice the supplier's interest, or (3) subject an existing party to multiple or inconsistent obligations. (Choice C) The builder could file a separate lawsuit against the supplier. But since this would be more costly and time-consuming than filing a third-party complaint in the pending action, it is not the most efficient way for the builder to seek reimbursement.\n\nEducational objective: A defendant may add a nonparty to a suit by filing a third-party complaint alleging that the nonparty is liable to the defendant for all or part of the plaintiff's claim. The defendant need only obtain the court's leave if more than 14 days have passed since the defendant served his/her original answer. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (third-party practice).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 (third-party practice).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[777],"sourceAnswerPages":[778,779],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的女子与一名来自B州的建筑商订立合同，由建筑商以50万美元在A州为她建造新住宅。建筑商订购了包括石膏板在内的全部必要材料，并按女子的要求建成住宅。一年后，住宅各处的石膏板开始碎裂并散发恶臭，住宅变得无法居住。女子在A州联邦地区法院以违约为由起诉建筑商。建筑商认为，来自B州的石膏板供应商应偿还其可能须向女子支付的任何损害赔偿。建筑商尚未提交答辩状。建筑商为向供应商寻求偿还，可以采取的最高效措施是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"对供应商提出交叉请求。","B":"申请将供应商作为不可或缺当事人加入诉讼。","C":"另行起诉供应商。","D":"对供应商提出第三方起诉。"},"explanationZh":"加入当事人的方式\n\n强制合并\n要求把必要当事人加入诉讼。\n\n许可合并\n允许把非必要当事人加入诉讼。\n\n参加诉讼\n允许利益可能受影响的非当事人加入诉讼。\n\n引入第三方被告\n允许被告把可能就已提出请求的全部或部分向被告承担责任的非当事人加入诉讼。\n\n权益争讼\n允许财产持有人强制主张该财产所有权的人在同一诉讼中解决争议。\n\n集团诉讼\n允许一方代表整个处境相似群体的利益。\n\n引入第三方被告（即第三方程序）允许被告把可能就原告请求的全部或部分向被告承担责任的非当事人加入诉讼。这意味着，该非当事人将偿还被告向原告支付的任何损害赔偿。被告可通过以下方式启动第三方程序：(1)在送达其原答辩状后14天内提交第三方起诉；或(2)超过该期限后，经法院许可提交。\n\n本题中，建筑商（被告）认为供应商（非当事人）应偿还其在待决诉讼中可能须向女子（原告）支付的任何损害赔偿，因此适合引入第三方被告。建筑商尚未提交答辩状，所以无须法院许可。因此，建筑商寻求偿还的最高效方式，是对供应商提出第三方起诉。\n\n（选项A）交叉请求是针对共同当事人提出的救济请求，例如一名被告针对共同被告提出请求。但本题中，建筑商向供应商——一名非当事人——寻求救济。因此，建筑商不能通过交叉请求向供应商寻求救济。\n\n（选项B）供应商不是不可或缺当事人，不应以此身份加入。这是因为供应商缺席不会：(1)阻止现有当事人获得完全救济；(2)损害供应商的利益；或(3)使现有当事人承担多重或相互矛盾的义务。\n\n（选项C）建筑商可以另行起诉供应商。但与在待决诉讼中提出第三方起诉相比，另行诉讼费用更高且更耗时，所以并非寻求偿还的最高效方式。\n\n学习目标：被告可以提交第三方起诉，把非当事人加入诉讼，并主张该非当事人就原告请求的全部或部分向被告承担责任。只有在被告送达原答辩状后超过14天时，才须取得法院许可。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 14（第三方程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-258","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":258,"topic":"Discovery","question":"The attorney for a plaintiff in an action filed in federal district court served the defendant with the summons, the complaint, and 25 interrogatories asking questions about the defendant's contentions in the case. The interrogatories stated that they were to be answered within 30 days after service. The defendant is likely to succeed in obtaining a protective order on which of the following grounds?","choices":{"A":"Interrogatories are only proper to discover facts, not contentions.","B":"Interrogatories may not be served until an answer to the complaint is filed.","C":"Interrogatories may not be served until the parties have conferred to arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a discovery plan.","D":"The interrogatories exceed the number permitted without permission from the court or an agreement between the parties."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Methods of discovery\n\nDepositions • Written or oral examination of party or witness under oath\n• 10 per party Interrogatories • Written questions served on party\n• Serve up to 25\n• Written responses due within 30 days of service Request for • Request served on party (or subpoena served on nonparty) production to produce & allow inspection of documents, electronic information, tangible items, or land\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Requests for • Requests served on other party to admit truth of facts within admission scope of discovery\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Physical/mental • Order by court or parties' agreement for physical or mental exams examination of party if those conditions are in controversy\n\nExcept in limited circumstances,* a party may not seek discovery (including interrogatories) until the parties have held an initial planning conference—ie, a meeting where the parties arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a plan for discovery. If a discovery request is submitted before this conference is held (as seen here), the court would likely grant a protective order. This shields the served party from the undue burden of responding to that request, especially since the disclosure of the requested information will likely be arranged at the initial planning conference. *Discovery may be sought before an initial planning conference (1) in a proceeding exempt from initial disclosures or (2) if permitted by a court order, the parties' stipulation, or a procedural rule. (Choice A) Interrogatories are written questions (served by one party on another party) that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery—including facts, opinions, and contentions that pertain to the case. (Choice B) Interrogatories may be served after an initial planning conference has been held—not when an answer to the complaint has been filed. (Choice D) A party may serve up to 25 interrogatories on any other party without the court's permission or an agreement between the parties (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Discovery requests generally cannot be served until the parties have held an initial planning conference to arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a discovery plan. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d) (timing of discovery).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","rule":"Discovery requests generally cannot be served until the parties have held an initial planning conference to arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a discovery plan.","irac":{"issue":"The attorney for a plaintiff in an action filed in federal district court served the defendant with the summons, the complaint, and 25 interrogatories asking questions about the defendant's contentions in the case. The interrogatories stated that they were to be answered within 30 days after service. The defendant is likely to succeed in obtaining a protective order on which of the following grounds?","rule":"Discovery requests generally cannot be served until the parties have held an initial planning conference to arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a discovery plan.","analysis":"Methods of discovery\n\nDepositions • Written or oral examination of party or witness under oath\n• 10 per party Interrogatories • Written questions served on party\n• Serve up to 25\n• Written responses due within 30 days of service Request for • Request served on party (or subpoena served on nonparty) production to produce & allow inspection of documents, electronic information, tangible items, or land\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Requests for • Requests served on other party to admit truth of facts within admission scope of discovery\n• No limit\n• Written response due within 30 days of service Physical/mental • Order by court or parties' agreement for physical or mental exams examination of party if those conditions are in controversy\n\nExcept in limited circumstances,* a party may not seek discovery (including interrogatories) until the parties have held an initial planning conference—ie, a meeting where the parties arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a plan for discovery. If a discovery request is submitted before this conference is held (as seen here), the court would likely grant a protective order. This shields the served party from the undue burden of responding to that request, especially since the disclosure of the requested information will likely be arranged at the initial planning conference. *Discovery may be sought before an initial planning conference (1) in a proceeding exempt from initial disclosures or (2) if permitted by a court order, the parties' stipulation, or a procedural rule. (Choice A) Interrogatories are written questions (served by one party on another party) that may inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery—including facts, opinions, and contentions that pertain to the case. (Choice B) Interrogatories may be served after an initial planning conference has been held—not when an answer to the complaint has been filed. (Choice D) A party may serve up to 25 interrogatories on any other party without the court's permission or an agreement between the parties (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Discovery requests generally cannot be served until the parties have held an initial planning conference to arrange for initial disclosures and prepare a discovery plan. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d) (timing of discovery).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d) (timing of discovery).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[780],"sourceAnswerPages":[781,782],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"在联邦地区法院提起的一项诉讼中，原告律师向被告送达传票、起诉状以及25项书面询问；这些书面询问涉及被告在案件中的主张，并注明应在送达后30天内答复。被告最可能基于下列哪一理由成功取得保护令？","choicesZh":{"A":"书面询问只能用于查明事实，不能用于查明主张。","B":"在提交起诉状答辩前，不得送达书面询问。","C":"在双方会商安排初始披露并拟订证据开示计划前，不得送达书面询问。","D":"未经法院许可或双方协议，这些书面询问超过允许的数量。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示方式\n\n证言录取\n• 对当事人或证人在宣誓后进行书面或口头询问；\n• 每一方10次。\n\n书面询问\n• 向当事人送达书面问题；\n• 最多可送达25项；\n• 书面答复应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n提供请求\n• 向当事人送达请求（或向非当事人送达传票），要求其提供并允许检查文件、电子信息、有形物或土地；\n• 数量不限；\n• 书面答复应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n承认请求\n• 向另一方送达请求，要求其承认证据开示范围内事实的真实性；\n• 数量不限；\n• 书面答复应在送达后30天内作出。\n\n身体／精神检查\n• 当一方的身体或精神状况属于争议事项时，依法院命令或双方协议对该方进行身体或精神检查。\n\n除少数例外情形外，* 在双方举行初始规划会议之前，一方不得寻求证据开示（包括书面询问）。初始规划会议是双方安排初始披露并制定证据开示计划的会议。如果在会议举行前提出证据开示请求（本题即如此），法院很可能发布保护令。这可使收到请求的一方免受答复所带来的不当负担，尤其是因为所请求信息的披露很可能将在初始规划会议上安排。\n\n*在以下情形下，可在初始规划会议前寻求证据开示：(1)该程序豁免初始披露；或(2)法院命令、双方约定或程序规则允许。\n\n（选项A）书面询问是一方向另一方送达的书面问题，可以询问证据开示范围内的任何事项，包括与案件有关的事实、意见和主张。\n\n（选项B）书面询问可在初始规划会议举行后送达，并非必须等到起诉状答辩提交后。\n\n（选项D）未经法院许可或双方协议，一方可以向任何另一方送达最多25项书面询问（本题正好25项）。\n\n学习目标：通常只有在双方举行初始规划会议、安排初始披露并拟订证据开示计划后，才能送达证据开示请求。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(d)（证据开示的时间）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33（书面询问）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-259","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":259,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A plastic surgeon left her business partner to open her own plastic surgery clinic in a neighboring state. The business partner brought a diversity action against the plastic surgeon in federal court for violating a noncompete clause in the partnership agreement. The business partner properly filed an ex parte application for a temporary restraining order (TRO) that would prohibit the plastic surgeon from operating her new clinic. After a hearing at which only the business partner was present, the court properly issued the ex parte TRO, finding that the partner would be immediately and irreparably harmed by the loss of goodwill caused by the plastic surgeon's violation of the noncompete clause. The TRO was served on the plastic surgeon the next day. One week later, the business partner moved to convert the TRO into a preliminary injunction. He requested that the court grant the motion immediately without providing notice to the plastic surgeon. Will the court grant the business partner's request?","choices":{"A":"No, because notice must be given to an opposing party before a court may issue a preliminary injunction.","B":"No, because the partner's attorney failed to certify in writing why the plastic surgeon need not be notified.","C":"Yes, because the ex parte TRO had expired and was no longer in effect.","D":"Yes, because the partner will suffer immediate and irreparable harm if the request is not granted."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Preliminary relief\n\n(FRCP 65)\n\nType Required contents Effect\n\nPreliminary Notice of • State reasons Persons bound upon receiving injunction hearing issued actual notice of order:\n• Reasonably Temporary • parties to the case describe acts restraining • parties' officers, agents, restrained or order employees & attorneys required\n• anyone in active\n• Specify terms concert/participation Ex parte Above requirements plus: with above persons (no • date & hour issued notice) • why injury is irreparable\n• why issued without notice\n• promptly filed with clerk & entered into record FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that commands a party to do or stop doing a specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. A court may issue a TRO without notice to the adverse party (ie, ex parte). But when a party attempts to convert a TRO into a preliminary injunction, the adverse party must receive notice. Therefore, the court will deny the business partner's request because the plastic surgeon did not receive notice of the preliminary-injunction hearing. (Choice B) The business partner's attorney must certify in writing why the plastic surgeon need not be notified when applying for an ex parte TRO—not a preliminary injunction. That is because, unlike an ex parte TRO, a preliminary injunction may be issued only after the adverse party has received notice of the preliminary-injunction hearing. (Choice C) An ex parte TRO automatically remains in effect for 14 days unless that time period is (1) shortened by the court or (2) extended by the court for good cause or the adverse party's consent. Here, the TRO was seven days old when the business partner\n\nmoved to convert it into a preliminary injunction. Since there is no indication that the TRO was to last fewer than 14 days, it had not expired and was still in effect. (Choice D) Although the business partner will suffer immediate and irreparable harm if his motion to convert the TRO into a preliminary injunction is denied, the court cannot grant a preliminary injunction on that basis alone. Even if it could, the motion cannot be granted because the plastic surgeon did not receive notice of the hearing. Educational objective: A court may issue a temporary restraining order without providing notice to the adverse party (ie, ex parte). However, the adverse party must receive notice before the court can convert a TRO into a preliminary injunction. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(a) (preliminary injunction).","rule":"A court may issue a temporary restraining order without providing notice to the adverse party (ie, ex parte). However, the adverse party must receive notice before the court can convert a TRO into a preliminary injunction.","irac":{"issue":"A plastic surgeon left her business partner to open her own plastic surgery clinic in a neighboring state. The business partner brought a diversity action against the plastic surgeon in federal court for violating a noncompete clause in the partnership agreement. The business partner properly filed an ex parte application for a temporary restraining order (TRO) that would prohibit the plastic surgeon from operating her new clinic. After a hearing at which only the business partner was present, the court properly issued the ex parte TRO, finding that the partner would be immediately and irreparably harmed by the loss of goodwill caused by the plastic surgeon's violation of the noncompete clause. The TRO was served on the plastic surgeon the next day. One week later, the business partner moved to convert the TRO into a preliminary injunction. He requested that the court grant the motion immediately without providing notice to the plastic surgeon. Will the court grant the business partner's request?","rule":"A court may issue a temporary restraining order without providing notice to the adverse party (ie, ex parte). However, the adverse party must receive notice before the court can convert a TRO into a preliminary injunction.","analysis":"Preliminary relief\n\n(FRCP 65)\n\nType Required contents Effect\n\nPreliminary Notice of • State reasons Persons bound upon receiving injunction hearing issued actual notice of order:\n• Reasonably Temporary • parties to the case describe acts restraining • parties' officers, agents, restrained or order employees & attorneys required\n• anyone in active\n• Specify terms concert/participation Ex parte Above requirements plus: with above persons (no • date & hour issued notice) • why injury is irreparable\n• why issued without notice\n• promptly filed with clerk & entered into record FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that commands a party to do or stop doing a specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. A court may issue a TRO without notice to the adverse party (ie, ex parte). But when a party attempts to convert a TRO into a preliminary injunction, the adverse party must receive notice. Therefore, the court will deny the business partner's request because the plastic surgeon did not receive notice of the preliminary-injunction hearing. (Choice B) The business partner's attorney must certify in writing why the plastic surgeon need not be notified when applying for an ex parte TRO—not a preliminary injunction. That is because, unlike an ex parte TRO, a preliminary injunction may be issued only after the adverse party has received notice of the preliminary-injunction hearing. (Choice C) An ex parte TRO automatically remains in effect for 14 days unless that time period is (1) shortened by the court or (2) extended by the court for good cause or the adverse party's consent. Here, the TRO was seven days old when the business partner\n\nmoved to convert it into a preliminary injunction. Since there is no indication that the TRO was to last fewer than 14 days, it had not expired and was still in effect. (Choice D) Although the business partner will suffer immediate and irreparable harm if his motion to convert the TRO into a preliminary injunction is denied, the court cannot grant a preliminary injunction on that basis alone. Even if it could, the motion cannot be granted because the plastic surgeon did not receive notice of the hearing. Educational objective: A court may issue a temporary restraining order without providing notice to the adverse party (ie, ex parte). However, the adverse party must receive notice before the court can convert a TRO into a preliminary injunction. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(a) (preliminary injunction).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(a) (preliminary injunction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[783],"sourceAnswerPages":[784,785],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名整形外科医生离开其商业伙伴，到邻州开设自己的整形外科诊所。商业伙伴在联邦法院以违反合伙协议中的竞业禁止条款为由，对该医生提起多元州籍诉讼。商业伙伴适当地提出单方临时限制令（TRO）申请，请求禁止医生经营新诊所。在只有商业伙伴出席的听证会后，法院适当地发布单方TRO，并认定医生违反竞业禁止条款所造成的商誉损失，会给商业伙伴带来即时且不可弥补的损害。TRO于次日送达医生。一周后，商业伙伴申请把TRO转为初步禁令，并请求法院在不通知医生的情况下立即批准。法院是否会批准商业伙伴的请求？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法院发布初步禁令前必须通知对方当事人。","B":"不会，因为商业伙伴的律师没有以书面形式证明为何无须通知医生。","C":"会，因为单方TRO已经到期，不再有效。","D":"会，因为不批准请求会使商业伙伴遭受即时且不可弥补的损害。"},"explanationZh":"初步救济\n\n（FRCP 65）\n\n类型｜必备内容｜效力\n\n初步禁令\n• 发出听证会通知。\n\n临时限制令\n• 说明发布命令的理由；\n• 合理具体地说明被限制或被要求实施的行为；\n• 明确命令条款。\n\n收到命令实际通知后，下列人员受其约束：\n• 案件当事人；\n• 当事人的高级职员、代理人、雇员和律师；\n• 与上述人员积极协同行动或参与的任何人。\n\n单方命令（无通知）\n除上述要求外，还须载明：\n• 发布日期和时间；\n• 为何损害不可弥补；\n• 为何在无通知情况下发布；\n• 并须及时提交书记员并记入案卷。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n临时限制令（TRO）是一项法院命令，要求一方在14天内，或在能够举行初步禁令听证会之前（以先发生者为准），实施或停止实施特定行为。法院可以在不通知对方的情况下发布TRO（即单方发布）。但是，一方试图把TRO转为初步禁令时，必须通知对方。因此，法院将驳回商业伙伴的请求，因为整形外科医生没有收到初步禁令听证会通知。\n\n（选项B）申请单方TRO时，商业伙伴的律师必须以书面形式证明为何无须通知医生；申请初步禁令则不适用这一要求。这是因为，与单方TRO不同，初步禁令只能在对方收到初步禁令听证会通知后发布。\n\n（选项C）单方TRO自动维持14天，除非该期限：(1)由法院缩短；或(2)由法院基于正当理由，或经对方同意而延长。本题中，商业伙伴申请把TRO转为初步禁令时，该TRO才发布七天。没有迹象表明其期限少于14天，所以TRO尚未到期且仍然有效。\n\n（选项D）尽管驳回把TRO转为初步禁令的申请会使商业伙伴遭受即时且不可弥补的损害，法院不能仅凭该理由发布初步禁令。即使可以，也不能批准该动议，因为医生没有收到听证会通知。\n\n学习目标：法院可以不通知对方而发布临时限制令（即单方发布）。但是，法院把TRO转为初步禁令之前，必须通知对方。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(a)（初步禁令）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-260","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":260,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"An injured pedestrian filed a diversity action in federal court against a truck driver. The truck driver filed an answer in which he denied liability and asserted an affirmative defense that the pedestrian was contributorily negligent. The truck driver did not assert any counterclaims against the pedestrian in his answer. Thirty days later, the pedestrian seeks to file a pleading to respond to the allegations in the truck driver's answer. The court has not yet issued any orders in the case. May the pedestrian file a response?","choices":{"A":"No, because the pedestrian did not file a reply within 14 days after the answer was filed.","B":"No, because the truck driver did not assert a counterclaim and the court did not order a reply.","C":"Yes, because due process allows the pedestrian to file a reply.","D":"Yes, because the truck driver asserted an affirmative defense."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Types of pleadings\n\n(FRCP 7)\n\nComplaint Plaintiff's assertion of cause of action, court's jurisdiction & demand for relief\n\nAnswer Response to complaint, counterclaim, crossclaim, or third-party complaint that asserts admissions, denials, affirmative defenses & other claims\n\nThird-party Party's assertion that nonparty is liable for damages party may owe in complaint original suit based on legal theory (eg, indemnity, contribution)\n\nReply Response to answer (only required if court ordered)\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 7(a) lists the pleadings allowed in federal court. The most common pleadings are the plaintiff's complaint against the defendant and the defendant's answer in response to that complaint. A plaintiff may also respond to the defendant's answer by filing:\n• an answer, but only if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or\n• a reply, but only in the rare instance that the court orders it. Here, the truck driver did not assert a counterclaim in his answer, and the court did not order a reply. Therefore, the pedestrian may not respond to the allegations in the truck driver's answer. Instead, the pedestrian's nonresponse is automatically treated as a denial of the affirmative defense alleged in the truck driver's answer. (Choice A) Had the court ordered a reply, the pedestrian would have needed to serve it within 21 days after being served with the court order—not within 14 days after the answer was filed. (Choice C) Due process does not allow the pedestrian to file a reply. The pedestrian may only do so pursuant to a court order (not seen here). (Choice D) The truck driver asserted an affirmative defense (not a counterclaim) alleging that the pedestrian was contributorily negligent. As a result, the pedestrian may not file an answer to respond to the affirmative defense. Educational objective:\n\nA plaintiff may respond to a defendant's answer by filing (1) an answer if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or (2) a reply if the court orders it. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 7(a) (pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 365 (2019) (explaining the permissibility of a reply in federal court).","rule":"A plaintiff may respond to a defendant's answer by filing (1) an answer if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or (2) a reply if the court orders it.","irac":{"issue":"An injured pedestrian filed a diversity action in federal court against a truck driver. The truck driver filed an answer in which he denied liability and asserted an affirmative defense that the pedestrian was contributorily negligent. The truck driver did not assert any counterclaims against the pedestrian in his answer. Thirty days later, the pedestrian seeks to file a pleading to respond to the allegations in the truck driver's answer. The court has not yet issued any orders in the case. May the pedestrian file a response?","rule":"A plaintiff may respond to a defendant's answer by filing (1) an answer if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or (2) a reply if the court orders it.","analysis":"Types of pleadings\n\n(FRCP 7)\n\nComplaint Plaintiff's assertion of cause of action, court's jurisdiction & demand for relief\n\nAnswer Response to complaint, counterclaim, crossclaim, or third-party complaint that asserts admissions, denials, affirmative defenses & other claims\n\nThird-party Party's assertion that nonparty is liable for damages party may owe in complaint original suit based on legal theory (eg, indemnity, contribution)\n\nReply Response to answer (only required if court ordered)\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 7(a) lists the pleadings allowed in federal court. The most common pleadings are the plaintiff's complaint against the defendant and the defendant's answer in response to that complaint. A plaintiff may also respond to the defendant's answer by filing:\n• an answer, but only if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or\n• a reply, but only in the rare instance that the court orders it. Here, the truck driver did not assert a counterclaim in his answer, and the court did not order a reply. Therefore, the pedestrian may not respond to the allegations in the truck driver's answer. Instead, the pedestrian's nonresponse is automatically treated as a denial of the affirmative defense alleged in the truck driver's answer. (Choice A) Had the court ordered a reply, the pedestrian would have needed to serve it within 21 days after being served with the court order—not within 14 days after the answer was filed. (Choice C) Due process does not allow the pedestrian to file a reply. The pedestrian may only do so pursuant to a court order (not seen here). (Choice D) The truck driver asserted an affirmative defense (not a counterclaim) alleging that the pedestrian was contributorily negligent. As a result, the pedestrian may not file an answer to respond to the affirmative defense. Educational objective:\n\nA plaintiff may respond to a defendant's answer by filing (1) an answer if the defendant asserted a counterclaim or (2) a reply if the court orders it. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 7(a) (pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 365 (2019) (explaining the permissibility of a reply in federal court).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 7(a) (pleadings).\n\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 365 (2019) (explaining the permissibility of a reply in federal court).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[786],"sourceAnswerPages":[787,788],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名受伤的行人在联邦法院对卡车司机提起多元州籍诉讼。卡车司机提交答辩状，否认责任，并提出行人存在共同过失这一积极抗辩。卡车司机在答辩状中没有对行人提出任何反请求。30天后，行人希望提交一份诉状，对卡车司机答辩状中的陈述作出回应。法院尚未在本案中发布任何命令。行人可以提交回应吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为行人没有在答辩状提交后14天内提交答复状。","B":"不可以，因为卡车司机没有提出反请求，而且法院没有命令提交答复状。","C":"可以，因为正当程序允许行人提交答复状。","D":"可以，因为卡车司机提出了积极抗辩。"},"explanationZh":"诉状的种类\n\n（FRCP 7）\n\n起诉状\n原告陈述诉因、法院管辖权和救济请求。\n\n答辩状\n对起诉状、反请求、交叉请求或第三方起诉作出回应，提出承认、否认、积极抗辩及其他请求。\n\n第三方起诉\n一方主张非当事人基于某项法律理论（例如补偿或分担），对该方在原诉讼中可能承担的损害赔偿负责。\n\n答复状\n对答辩状作出回应（只有法院命令时才要求）。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》7(a)列出了联邦法院允许的诉状。最常见的是原告针对被告的起诉状，以及被告回应起诉状的答辩状。原告还可以通过以下方式回应被告的答辩状：\n• 提交答辩状，但仅限于被告提出反请求时；或\n• 提交答复状，但仅限于法院在罕见情况下命令提交时。\n\n本题中，卡车司机没有在答辩状中提出反请求，法院也没有命令提交答复状。因此，行人不得回应卡车司机答辩状中的陈述。相反，行人未作回应将自动视为否认卡车司机答辩状中的积极抗辩。\n\n（选项A）如果法院命令提交答复状，行人必须在法院命令送达后21天内送达答复状，而不是在答辩状提交后14天内。\n\n（选项C）正当程序并不使行人有权提交答复状。只有依照法院命令（本题没有）才可以提交。\n\n（选项D）卡车司机提出的是积极抗辩（而非反请求），主张行人存在共同过失。因此，行人不得提交答辩状回应该积极抗辩。\n\n学习目标：原告可以通过以下方式回应被告的答辩状：(1)被告提出反请求时，提交答辩状；或(2)法院命令时，提交答复状。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 7(a)（诉状）。\n• 61A Am. Jur. 2d Pleading § 365 (2019)（说明在联邦法院提交答复状的许可性）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-261","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":261,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A company located in State A filed a lawsuit against its distributor located in State B in a federal district court. The company's complaint asserted a breach of contract claim for $60,000 and a negligence claim for $30,000. These claims arose from different facts. May the federal court hear these claims in a single suit?","choices":{"A":"No, because neither claim satisfies subject-matter jurisdiction on its own.","B":"No, because the claims arose out of different facts.","C":"Yes, because the claims were properly joined, and the federal court has diversity jurisdiction.","D":"Yes, because the claims were properly joined, and the federal court has supplemental jurisdiction."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Amount in controversy\n\n(aggregation of claims)\n\nProcedural Claims asserted Outcome posture\n\nOne π v. one Δ • Negligence – $40K • Aggregation allowed\n• Battery – $60K • Amount in controversy = $100K One π v. • Negligence against • Aggregation not allowed unless Δs multiple Δs Δ1 – $40K jointly liable\n• Battery against Δ2 – • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K $60K Multiple πs v. • π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation not allowed unless πs one Δ – $40K enforcing common or undivided\n• π2 asserts battery – interest $60K • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K\n• π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation allowed for π1's – $40K claims\n• π1 asserts breach of • Aggregation not allowed for π2's contract – $40K claim\n• π2 asserts battery – • Amounts in controversy = $80K & $60K $60K π = plaintiff; Δ = defendant.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 18 allows a party to join (ie, add) as many claims as it has against an opposing party in a single lawsuit—even if the claims arise from different facts or occurrences* (as seen here) (Choice B). But like all disputes in federal court, a suit involving multiple claims must satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction. This can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nThe amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint (excluding costs and interest). To meet the required amount, a single plaintiff may aggregate (ie, combine) joined claims asserted against a single defendant. Here, the company and the distributor are citizens of different states (States A and B, respectively). The company properly joined a breach of contract claim for $60,000 and a negligence claim for $30,000, even though they arise from different facts. And though neither claim individually satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement, they can be aggregated to $90,000 to meet that requirement since both claims are asserted against the distributor. Therefore, the federal district court has diversity jurisdiction and can hear both claims in a single suit (Choice A). *FRCP 42 allows a federal court to order separate trials of claims for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or to expedite and economize. (Choice D) A federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear certain claims that do not independently satisfy federal-question or diversity jurisdiction. But there is no need for supplemental jurisdiction here since the court has diversity jurisdiction over the aggregated claims. Educational objective: A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party in federal court—even if the claims arise out of different facts or occurrences—provided subject-matter jurisdiction is satisfied. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18 (joinder of claims).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","rule":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party in federal court—even if the claims arise out of different facts or occurrences—provided subject-matter jurisdiction is satisfied.","irac":{"issue":"A company located in State A filed a lawsuit against its distributor located in State B in a federal district court. The company's complaint asserted a breach of contract claim for $60,000 and a negligence claim for $30,000. These claims arose from different facts. May the federal court hear these claims in a single suit?","rule":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party in federal court—even if the claims arise out of different facts or occurrences—provided subject-matter jurisdiction is satisfied.","analysis":"Amount in controversy\n\n(aggregation of claims)\n\nProcedural Claims asserted Outcome posture\n\nOne π v. one Δ • Negligence – $40K • Aggregation allowed\n• Battery – $60K • Amount in controversy = $100K One π v. • Negligence against • Aggregation not allowed unless Δs multiple Δs Δ1 – $40K jointly liable\n• Battery against Δ2 – • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K $60K Multiple πs v. • π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation not allowed unless πs one Δ – $40K enforcing common or undivided\n• π2 asserts battery – interest $60K • Amounts in controversy = $40K & $60K\n• π1 asserts negligence • Aggregation allowed for π1's – $40K claims\n• π1 asserts breach of • Aggregation not allowed for π2's contract – $40K claim\n• π2 asserts battery – • Amounts in controversy = $80K & $60K $60K π = plaintiff; Δ = defendant.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 18 allows a party to join (ie, add) as many claims as it has against an opposing party in a single lawsuit—even if the claims arise from different facts or occurrences* (as seen here) (Choice B). But like all disputes in federal court, a suit involving multiple claims must satisfy subject-matter jurisdiction. This can be established through either:\n• federal-question jurisdiction – when a claim arises under the U.S. Constitution, a treaty, or a federal law (not seen here) or\n\n• diversity jurisdiction – when the opposing parties are citizens of different states and the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000.\n\nThe amount in controversy refers to the amount demanded in the plaintiff's complaint (excluding costs and interest). To meet the required amount, a single plaintiff may aggregate (ie, combine) joined claims asserted against a single defendant. Here, the company and the distributor are citizens of different states (States A and B, respectively). The company properly joined a breach of contract claim for $60,000 and a negligence claim for $30,000, even though they arise from different facts. And though neither claim individually satisfies the amount-in-controversy requirement, they can be aggregated to $90,000 to meet that requirement since both claims are asserted against the distributor. Therefore, the federal district court has diversity jurisdiction and can hear both claims in a single suit (Choice A). *FRCP 42 allows a federal court to order separate trials of claims for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or to expedite and economize. (Choice D) A federal court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction to hear certain claims that do not independently satisfy federal-question or diversity jurisdiction. But there is no need for supplemental jurisdiction here since the court has diversity jurisdiction over the aggregated claims. Educational objective: A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party in federal court—even if the claims arise out of different facts or occurrences—provided subject-matter jurisdiction is satisfied. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18 (joinder of claims).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18 (joinder of claims).\n\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332 (diversity jurisdiction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[789],"sourceAnswerPages":[790,791],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家位于A州的公司在联邦地区法院起诉一家位于B州的经销商。公司的起诉状提出一项6万美元的违约请求和一项3万美元的过失请求。两项请求源于不同事实。联邦法院可以在同一诉讼中审理这两项请求吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为两项请求均无法单独满足事项管辖权。","B":"不可以，因为两项请求源于不同事实。","C":"可以，因为两项请求已适当合并，且联邦法院具有多元州籍管辖权。","D":"可以，因为两项请求已适当合并，且联邦法院具有补充管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"争议金额\n\n（请求的合并计算）\n\n程序格局｜提出的请求｜结果\n\n一名原告诉一名被告\n• 过失——4万美元\n• 殴打——6万美元\n结果：\n• 允许合并计算\n• 争议金额＝10万美元\n\n一名原告诉多名被告\n• 针对被告1的过失——4万美元\n• 针对被告2的殴打——6万美元\n结果：\n• 除非各被告承担共同责任，否则不允许合并计算\n• 争议金额分别为4万美元和6万美元\n\n多名原告诉一名被告\n• 原告1主张过失——4万美元\n• 原告2主张殴打——6万美元\n结果：\n• 除非各原告主张共同或不可分割的利益，否则不允许合并计算\n• 争议金额分别为4万美元和6万美元\n\n多名原告诉一名被告\n• 原告1主张过失——4万美元\n• 原告1主张违约——4万美元\n• 原告2主张殴打——6万美元\n结果：\n• 原告1的请求允许合并计算\n• 原告2的请求不允许与原告1合并计算\n• 争议金额分别为8万美元和6万美元\n\n原告＝π；被告＝Δ。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）18允许一方在同一诉讼中，把其针对对方拥有的任意数量请求合并（即加入），即使这些请求源于不同事实或事件*（本题即如此）（选项B）。但是，与联邦法院的所有争议一样，涉及多项请求的诉讼必须满足事项管辖权。事项管辖权可通过以下任一种方式确立：\n• 联邦问题管辖权——请求基于美国宪法、条约或联邦法律（本题没有）；或者\n• 多元州籍管辖权——对方当事人是不同州公民，且争议金额超过75,000美元。\n\n争议金额是指原告起诉状中要求的金额（不包括诉讼费用和利息）。为达到规定金额，一名原告可以把针对一名被告提出且已合并的请求合并计算。\n\n本题中，公司和经销商分别为A州和B州公民。公司适当地合并了6万美元违约请求和3万美元过失请求，尽管二者源于不同事实。虽然任何单项请求均未独立达到争议金额要求，但两项请求均针对同一经销商，所以可合并计算为9万美元，从而满足要求。因此，联邦地区法院具有多元州籍管辖权，可以在同一诉讼中审理两项请求（选项A）。\n\n*FRCP 42允许联邦法院为便利、避免损害或加快诉讼并节省成本，命令对各项请求分别审判。\n\n（选项D）联邦法院可以通过补充管辖权审理某些本身不独立满足联邦问题或多元州籍管辖权的请求。但本题无需补充管辖权，因为法院对合并计算后的请求具有多元州籍管辖权。\n\n学习目标：在满足事项管辖权的前提下，一方可在联邦法院把其针对对方拥有的任意数量请求合并，即使这些请求源于不同事实或事件。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 18（请求合并）。\n• 28 U.S.C. § 1332（多元州籍管辖权）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-262","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":262,"topic":"Judgments, Post-Trial Motions & Appeals","question":"A miner sued a coal company in a federal district court to recover damages for severe injuries he sustained during a mining explosion. Sixty days prior to trial, the company's attorney interviewed a forklift operator who witnessed the incident. The operator stated that he saw the miner violate several safety protocols leading up to the explosion. At trial, the company sought to have the forklift operator testify to support its contributory negligence defense. The miner's attorney objected and moved for sanctions on the ground that the company had failed to disclose the operator's identity prior to trial. The company's attorney stated that she did not disclose the operator's identity because the miner's attorney never made a formal request. The district court then sanctioned the company by (1) prohibiting the operator from testifying, (2) informing the jury of the company's nondisclosure, and (3) ordering the company to pay the miner's reasonable expenses. After the jury returned a verdict for the miner, the company appealed the sanctions. How should the appellate court rule?","choices":{"A":"Affirm all the sanctions.","B":"Affirm the sanction ordering the company to pay the miner's reasonable expenses but reverse the other sanctions.","C":"Affirm the sanction prohibiting the operator from testifying at trial but reverse the other sanctions.","D":"Reverse all the sanctions."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Parties are required to make initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and pretrial disclosures without waiting for a discovery request. Such required disclosures are designed to prevent surprises at trial. As part of their pretrial disclosures, parties must disclose the identity of witnesses who may testify at trial. Since the company intended to call the forklift operator as a witness, it was required to disclose his identity as part of its pretrial disclosures. However, the company failed to do so. To underscore the importance of such disclosures, a court has wide discretion to impose sanctions on a party who fails to make required disclosures. The most common sanction is to prohibit the use of the undisclosed information unless the nondisclosure was substantially justified or harmless. In addition to or in lieu of this sanction, the court may:\n• order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the nondisclosure\n• inform the jury of the party's failure to disclose and\n• impose any other appropriate sanction (except for contempt of court). Here, the company provided no justification for its failure to disclose the forklift operator's identity, and this nondisclosure was harmful to the miner since he had no time to prepare for the operator's testimony. As a result, the court did not abuse its discretion in prohibiting the operator from testifying, informing the jury of the nondisclosure, and ordering the company to pay the miner's reasonable expenses. The appellate court should therefore affirm all the sanctions (Choices B, C & D). Educational objective: A court may impose the following sanctions on a party who fails to make required disclosures: (1) prohibit the use of the undisclosed evidence, (2) order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses, (3) inform the jury of the nondisclosure, and (4) impose any other appropriate sanction (except for contempt of court). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a) (mandatory-disclosure requirements).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(1) (sanctions for failure to make mandatory disclosures).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2289.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to make required disclosures).","rule":"A court may impose the following sanctions on a party who fails to make required disclosures: (1) prohibit the use of the undisclosed evidence, (2) order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses, (3) inform the jury of the nondisclosure, and (4) impose any other appropriate sanction (except for contempt of court).","irac":{"issue":"A miner sued a coal company in a federal district court to recover damages for severe injuries he sustained during a mining explosion. Sixty days prior to trial, the company's attorney interviewed a forklift operator who witnessed the incident. The operator stated that he saw the miner violate several safety protocols leading up to the explosion. At trial, the company sought to have the forklift operator testify to support its contributory negligence defense. The miner's attorney objected and moved for sanctions on the ground that the company had failed to disclose the operator's identity prior to trial. The company's attorney stated that she did not disclose the operator's identity because the miner's attorney never made a formal request. The district court then sanctioned the company by (1) prohibiting the operator from testifying, (2) informing the jury of the company's nondisclosure, and (3) ordering the company to pay the miner's reasonable expenses. After the jury returned a verdict for the miner, the company appealed the sanctions. How should the appellate court rule?","rule":"A court may impose the following sanctions on a party who fails to make required disclosures: (1) prohibit the use of the undisclosed evidence, (2) order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses, (3) inform the jury of the nondisclosure, and (4) impose any other appropriate sanction (except for contempt of court).","analysis":"Parties are required to make initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and pretrial disclosures without waiting for a discovery request. Such required disclosures are designed to prevent surprises at trial. As part of their pretrial disclosures, parties must disclose the identity of witnesses who may testify at trial. Since the company intended to call the forklift operator as a witness, it was required to disclose his identity as part of its pretrial disclosures. However, the company failed to do so. To underscore the importance of such disclosures, a court has wide discretion to impose sanctions on a party who fails to make required disclosures. The most common sanction is to prohibit the use of the undisclosed information unless the nondisclosure was substantially justified or harmless. In addition to or in lieu of this sanction, the court may:\n• order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, caused by the nondisclosure\n• inform the jury of the party's failure to disclose and\n• impose any other appropriate sanction (except for contempt of court). Here, the company provided no justification for its failure to disclose the forklift operator's identity, and this nondisclosure was harmful to the miner since he had no time to prepare for the operator's testimony. As a result, the court did not abuse its discretion in prohibiting the operator from testifying, informing the jury of the nondisclosure, and ordering the company to pay the miner's reasonable expenses. The appellate court should therefore affirm all the sanctions (Choices B, C & D). Educational objective: A court may impose the following sanctions on a party who fails to make required disclosures: (1) prohibit the use of the undisclosed evidence, (2) order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses, (3) inform the jury of the nondisclosure, and (4) impose any other appropriate sanction (except for contempt of court). References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a) (mandatory-disclosure requirements).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(1) (sanctions for failure to make mandatory disclosures).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2289.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to make required disclosures).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a) (mandatory-disclosure requirements).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(1) (sanctions for failure to make mandatory disclosures).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2289.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to make required disclosures).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[792],"sourceAnswerPages":[793],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名矿工在联邦地区法院起诉一家煤炭公司，要求赔偿其在矿井爆炸中遭受的严重伤害。审判前60天，公司律师询问了一名目睹事故的叉车操作员。操作员称，他看到矿工在爆炸发生前违反多项安全规程。审判中，公司试图传唤该操作员作证，以支持其共同过失抗辩。矿工律师提出异议并申请制裁，理由是公司未在审判前披露操作员的身份。公司律师称她没有披露，是因为矿工律师从未提出正式请求。地区法院随后对公司作出以下制裁：(1)禁止操作员作证；(2)告知陪审团公司未予披露；以及(3)命令公司支付矿工的合理费用。陪审团裁决矿工胜诉后，公司就这些制裁提出上诉。上诉法院应如何裁判？","choicesZh":{"A":"维持全部制裁。","B":"维持命令公司支付矿工合理费用的制裁，但推翻其他制裁。","C":"维持禁止操作员在审判中作证的制裁，但推翻其他制裁。","D":"推翻全部制裁。"},"explanationZh":"当事人无须等待证据开示请求，就必须进行初始披露、专家证言披露和审前披露。此类强制披露旨在防止审判中的意外。作为审前披露的一部分，当事人必须披露可能在审判中作证的证人身份。由于公司打算传唤叉车操作员作证，公司必须在审前披露中披露其身份，但公司没有这样做。\n\n为强调此类披露的重要性，法院对于未进行强制披露的一方拥有宽泛裁量权施加制裁。最常见的制裁是禁止使用未披露的信息，除非未披露具有充分正当理由或不造成损害。除该制裁以外或替代该制裁，法院还可以：\n• 命令支付因未披露给对方造成的合理费用，包括律师费；\n• 告知陪审团该方未予披露；以及\n• 施加任何其他适当制裁（藐视法庭除外）。\n\n本题中，公司没有为未披露叉车操作员身份提供任何正当理由；该未披露也损害了矿工，因为他没有时间为操作员的证言作准备。因此，法院禁止操作员作证、告知陪审团未披露并命令公司支付矿工的合理费用，并未滥用裁量权。所以上诉法院应维持全部制裁（选项B、C和D）。\n\n学习目标：法院可对未进行强制披露的一方施加以下制裁：(1)禁止使用未披露证据；(2)命令支付对方的合理费用；(3)告知陪审团未披露；以及(4)施加任何其他适当制裁（藐视法庭除外）。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)（强制披露要求）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(1)（未进行强制披露的制裁）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2289.1 (3d ed. 2020)（说明何时适合对未进行强制披露施加制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-083"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-263","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":263,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A pharmaceutical company sued its competitor for patent infringement in federal court. The competitor's attorney hired a pharmacist to examine the case and testify as an expert at trial. The pharmacist studied the case and submitted an initial draft of her report to the attorney. After reading the draft report, the attorney noticed that certain facts and data were missing. To correct this issue, the attorney emailed the pharmacist and provided additional facts and data to include in the report. The pharmacist then revised her report based on this additional information. After receiving this revised report from the competitor's attorney, the pharmaceutical company served a request for production on the attorney to produce the email exchanged with the pharmacist. Must the attorney produce the email?","choices":{"A":"No, because attorney communications with expert witnesses are privileged.","B":"No, because the contents of the email are discoverable through interrogatories served on the pharmacist.","C":"Yes, because a draft of an expert report is discoverable.","D":"Yes, because the email contained facts and data that the pharmacist relied on to revise her report."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Discovery of expert materials\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(4))\n\nNontestifying • Facts known or opinions held by expert not discoverable unless: expert – relate to court-ordered physical/mental examination or – exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain information by other means Testifying • Facts known & opinions held by expert discoverable through expert deposition\n• Drafts of expert reports & disclosures not discoverable\n• Communications between attorney & expert not discoverable unless: – relate to expert's compensation or – identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by attorney that expert considered in forming opinions FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nParties can use various discovery methods, including requests for production, to obtain nonprivileged information that is relevant to any claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case. Materials held by an expert witness expected to testify at trial are discoverable with two exceptions:\n• Drafts of expert reports or disclosures\n• Privileged attorney-expert communications All attorney-expert communications are privileged unless they (1) relate to the expert's compensation or (2) identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by the attorney that the expert considered or relied on in forming his/her opinions. Here, the competitor's attorney hired the pharmacist to examine the case and testify as an expert at trial. After reading the pharmacist's draft report, the attorney emailed the pharmacist and provided her with additional facts and data that were missing in the draft. The pharmacist then relied on those facts and data to revise her report, so the email is not a privileged communication (Choice A). Therefore, the attorney must produce the email. (Choice B) Interrogatories are a discovery request in which a party serves written questions on another party that inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. However, interrogatories may not be served on a nonparty such as an expert\n\nexpected to testify at trial. Therefore, the contents of the email are not discoverable through interrogatories served on the nonparty pharmacist. (Choice C) Drafts of expert reports are not discoverable. Therefore, the attorney would not be required to produce the email on this basis. Educational objective: All attorney-expert communications are privileged unless they (1) relate to the expert's compensation or (2) identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by the attorney that the expert considered or relied on in forming his/her opinions. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4) (discovery of expert materials).","rule":"All attorney-expert communications are privileged unless they (1) relate to the expert's compensation or (2) identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by the attorney that the expert considered or relied on in forming his/her opinions.","irac":{"issue":"A pharmaceutical company sued its competitor for patent infringement in federal court. The competitor's attorney hired a pharmacist to examine the case and testify as an expert at trial. The pharmacist studied the case and submitted an initial draft of her report to the attorney. After reading the draft report, the attorney noticed that certain facts and data were missing. To correct this issue, the attorney emailed the pharmacist and provided additional facts and data to include in the report. The pharmacist then revised her report based on this additional information. After receiving this revised report from the competitor's attorney, the pharmaceutical company served a request for production on the attorney to produce the email exchanged with the pharmacist. Must the attorney produce the email?","rule":"All attorney-expert communications are privileged unless they (1) relate to the expert's compensation or (2) identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by the attorney that the expert considered or relied on in forming his/her opinions.","analysis":"Discovery of expert materials\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(4))\n\nNontestifying • Facts known or opinions held by expert not discoverable unless: expert – relate to court-ordered physical/mental examination or – exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain information by other means Testifying • Facts known & opinions held by expert discoverable through expert deposition\n• Drafts of expert reports & disclosures not discoverable\n• Communications between attorney & expert not discoverable unless: – relate to expert's compensation or – identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by attorney that expert considered in forming opinions FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nParties can use various discovery methods, including requests for production, to obtain nonprivileged information that is relevant to any claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case. Materials held by an expert witness expected to testify at trial are discoverable with two exceptions:\n• Drafts of expert reports or disclosures\n• Privileged attorney-expert communications All attorney-expert communications are privileged unless they (1) relate to the expert's compensation or (2) identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by the attorney that the expert considered or relied on in forming his/her opinions. Here, the competitor's attorney hired the pharmacist to examine the case and testify as an expert at trial. After reading the pharmacist's draft report, the attorney emailed the pharmacist and provided her with additional facts and data that were missing in the draft. The pharmacist then relied on those facts and data to revise her report, so the email is not a privileged communication (Choice A). Therefore, the attorney must produce the email. (Choice B) Interrogatories are a discovery request in which a party serves written questions on another party that inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. However, interrogatories may not be served on a nonparty such as an expert\n\nexpected to testify at trial. Therefore, the contents of the email are not discoverable through interrogatories served on the nonparty pharmacist. (Choice C) Drafts of expert reports are not discoverable. Therefore, the attorney would not be required to produce the email on this basis. Educational objective: All attorney-expert communications are privileged unless they (1) relate to the expert's compensation or (2) identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by the attorney that the expert considered or relied on in forming his/her opinions. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4) (discovery of expert materials).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4) (discovery of expert materials).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[795],"sourceAnswerPages":[796,797],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家制药公司在联邦法院起诉竞争对手侵犯专利。竞争对手的律师聘请一名药剂师审查案件并在审判中提供专家证言。药剂师研究案件后，向律师提交其报告初稿。律师阅读后发现缺少某些事实和数据。为纠正这一问题，律师向药剂师发送电子邮件，提供应纳入报告的补充事实和数据。药剂师随后根据这些补充信息修订报告。制药公司从竞争对手律师处收到修订后的报告后，向该律师送达提供请求，要求其提供与药剂师往来的电子邮件。律师是否必须提供该电子邮件？","choicesZh":{"A":"不必，因为律师与专家证人之间的通信受特权保护。","B":"不必，因为可以通过向药剂师送达书面询问取得电子邮件内容。","C":"必须，因为专家报告草稿可予开示。","D":"必须，因为电子邮件包含药剂师修订报告时所依赖的事实和数据。"},"explanationZh":"专家材料的证据开示\n\n（FRCP 26(b)(4)）\n\n不作证专家\n专家知悉的事实或持有的意见不得开示，除非：\n• 与法院命令的身体／精神检查有关；或\n• 存在特殊情形，使通过其他方式取得信息并不切实可行。\n\n作证专家\n• 专家知悉的事实和持有的意见，可通过专家证言录取予以开示；\n• 专家报告和披露的草稿不得开示；\n• 律师与专家之间的通信不得开示，除非：\n  – 与专家报酬有关；或\n  – 识别律师提供且专家在形成意见时考虑的事实、数据或假设。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n当事人可以运用包括提供请求在内的各种证据开示方式，取得与任何请求或抗辩相关、与案件需要相称的非特权信息。预期在审判中作证的专家证人持有的材料可以开示，但有两项例外：\n• 专家报告或披露的草稿；\n• 受特权保护的律师—专家通信。\n\n所有律师—专家通信均受特权保护，除非其：(1)与专家报酬有关；或(2)识别律师提供且专家在形成其意见时考虑或依赖的事实、数据或假设。\n\n本题中，竞争对手的律师聘请药剂师审查案件并在审判中提供专家证言。阅读药剂师的报告草稿后，律师向其发送电子邮件，提供草稿中缺少的补充事实和数据。药剂师随后依赖这些事实和数据修订报告，所以该电子邮件不属于受特权保护的通信（选项A）。因此，律师必须提供该电子邮件。\n\n（选项B）书面询问是一种证据开示请求，由一方向另一方送达书面问题，询问证据开示范围内的任何事项。但是，书面询问不得送达给预期在审判中作证的专家等非当事人。因此，不能通过向非当事人药剂师送达书面询问取得电子邮件内容。\n\n（选项C）专家报告草稿不得开示。因此，律师不会基于该理由被要求提供电子邮件。\n\n学习目标：所有律师—专家通信均受特权保护，除非其：(1)与专家报酬有关；或(2)识别律师提供且专家在形成意见时考虑或依赖的事实、数据或假设。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4)（专家材料的证据开示）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-264","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":264,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A musician sued a political candidate in a federal court for federal copyright infringement. The complaint requested damages and a preliminary injunction to stop the candidate from using the musician's songs at his political rallies. Notice of a hearing on the preliminary injunction was provided to both parties. After the hearing was conducted, the judge granted the musician's request for a preliminary injunction and stated the reasons for doing so. In addition, the injunction reads as follows: \"The political candidate and any person working or volunteering to put on a rally for the candidate may not play any of the musician's songs. The political campaign is directed to distribute this injunction to all persons, including workers, independent contractors, and volunteers, who are involved in the candidate's rallies. A fine of $1,000 per violation will be assessed against anyone who fails to adhere to this injunction.\" At the candidate's next rally, a volunteer who had received a copy of the preliminary injunction nonetheless played the musician's song. Is the volunteer subject to the monetary fine for violating the preliminary injunction?","choices":{"A":"No, because the volunteer did not receive notice of the hearing.","B":"No, because the volunteer is not a party in the musician's suit against the candidate.","C":"Yes, because the injunction is in the best interests of the public.","D":"Yes, because the injunction reasonably described the prohibited acts."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Preliminary injunction\n\nGrounds* Movant establishes that:\n\n• movant is likely to succeed on merits\n• movant is likely to suffer irreparable harm in absence of relief\n• balance of equities favors movant and\n• injunction is in public's best interests Contents Order granting injunction must:\n\n• state reasons for issuance\n• reasonably describe prohibited or commanded acts and\n• state terms specifically Effect Binds the following persons who receive actual notice of order:\n\n• parties\n• parties' officers, agents, employees & attorneys and\n• anyone in active concert or participation with above-listed persons *Movant also must provide bond to pay costs & damages if nonmovant was wrongfully enjoined.\n\nA preliminary injunction is a temporary court order that is issued before or during trial to prohibit or command a specified action while a case is pending before the court. A preliminary injunction can only be granted after notice of the hearing to the adverse party, and it must contain all of the following:\n• The reasons why it was granted\n• A reasonable description of the prohibited or commanded acts\n• The specific terms (eg, persons bound, penalties for noncompliance) Additionally, actual notice of the preliminary injunction must be provided to any person bound by it, including (1) the parties, (2) their officers, agents, employees, and attorneys, and (3) persons in active concert or participation with the parties or their subordinates. Only persons who receive this notice can be held in contempt of court (eg, charged a monetary fine) for violating the injunction. Here, the judge issued a preliminary injunction and stated the reasons why it was granted. The injunction bound the candidate and all persons working or volunteering on his behalf (specific terms) to stop playing the musician's songs (prohibited acts). And since the volunteer received a copy of the injunction (actual notice), the volunteer is subject to the monetary fine for violating the preliminary injunction.\n\n(Choice A) The volunteer had to receive actual notice of the injunction to be subject to it, but the volunteer did not need to receive notice of the hearing since the candidate was the adverse party in this suit. (Choice B) Although the volunteer is not a party in the musician's suit against the candidate, the volunteer worked on the candidate's behalf and received actual notice of the injunction. As a result, the volunteer is bound by the injunction. (Choice C) The injunction may be in the public's best interests because the enforcement of copyright laws protects intellectual property rights. But that alone is not a basis to enforce the injunction against the volunteer. Educational objective: A preliminary injunction must contain (1) the reasons why it was issued, (2) a reasonable description of the prohibited or commanded acts, and (3) its specific terms. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d) (required contents and scope of preliminary injunction).","rule":"A preliminary injunction must contain (1) the reasons why it was issued, (2) a reasonable description of the prohibited or commanded acts, and (3) its specific terms.","irac":{"issue":"A musician sued a political candidate in a federal court for federal copyright infringement. The complaint requested damages and a preliminary injunction to stop the candidate from using the musician's songs at his political rallies. Notice of a hearing on the preliminary injunction was provided to both parties. After the hearing was conducted, the judge granted the musician's request for a preliminary injunction and stated the reasons for doing so. In addition, the injunction reads as follows: \"The political candidate and any person working or volunteering to put on a rally for the candidate may not play any of the musician's songs. The political campaign is directed to distribute this injunction to all persons, including workers, independent contractors, and volunteers, who are involved in the candidate's rallies. A fine of $1,000 per violation will be assessed against anyone who fails to adhere to this injunction.\" At the candidate's next rally, a volunteer who had received a copy of the preliminary injunction nonetheless played the musician's song. Is the volunteer subject to the monetary fine for violating the preliminary injunction?","rule":"A preliminary injunction must contain (1) the reasons why it was issued, (2) a reasonable description of the prohibited or commanded acts, and (3) its specific terms.","analysis":"Preliminary injunction\n\nGrounds* Movant establishes that:\n\n• movant is likely to succeed on merits\n• movant is likely to suffer irreparable harm in absence of relief\n• balance of equities favors movant and\n• injunction is in public's best interests Contents Order granting injunction must:\n\n• state reasons for issuance\n• reasonably describe prohibited or commanded acts and\n• state terms specifically Effect Binds the following persons who receive actual notice of order:\n\n• parties\n• parties' officers, agents, employees & attorneys and\n• anyone in active concert or participation with above-listed persons *Movant also must provide bond to pay costs & damages if nonmovant was wrongfully enjoined.\n\nA preliminary injunction is a temporary court order that is issued before or during trial to prohibit or command a specified action while a case is pending before the court. A preliminary injunction can only be granted after notice of the hearing to the adverse party, and it must contain all of the following:\n• The reasons why it was granted\n• A reasonable description of the prohibited or commanded acts\n• The specific terms (eg, persons bound, penalties for noncompliance) Additionally, actual notice of the preliminary injunction must be provided to any person bound by it, including (1) the parties, (2) their officers, agents, employees, and attorneys, and (3) persons in active concert or participation with the parties or their subordinates. Only persons who receive this notice can be held in contempt of court (eg, charged a monetary fine) for violating the injunction. Here, the judge issued a preliminary injunction and stated the reasons why it was granted. The injunction bound the candidate and all persons working or volunteering on his behalf (specific terms) to stop playing the musician's songs (prohibited acts). And since the volunteer received a copy of the injunction (actual notice), the volunteer is subject to the monetary fine for violating the preliminary injunction.\n\n(Choice A) The volunteer had to receive actual notice of the injunction to be subject to it, but the volunteer did not need to receive notice of the hearing since the candidate was the adverse party in this suit. (Choice B) Although the volunteer is not a party in the musician's suit against the candidate, the volunteer worked on the candidate's behalf and received actual notice of the injunction. As a result, the volunteer is bound by the injunction. (Choice C) The injunction may be in the public's best interests because the enforcement of copyright laws protects intellectual property rights. But that alone is not a basis to enforce the injunction against the volunteer. Educational objective: A preliminary injunction must contain (1) the reasons why it was issued, (2) a reasonable description of the prohibited or commanded acts, and (3) its specific terms. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d) (required contents and scope of preliminary injunction).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d) (required contents and scope of preliminary injunction).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[798],"sourceAnswerPages":[799,800],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名音乐家在联邦法院起诉一名政治候选人侵犯联邦版权。起诉状请求损害赔偿和初步禁令，以阻止候选人在政治集会上使用音乐家的歌曲。双方均收到初步禁令听证会通知。听证会举行后，法官批准音乐家的初步禁令请求，并说明了理由。此外，禁令内容如下：“政治候选人以及任何为该候选人的集会工作或担任志愿者的人，均不得播放音乐家的任何歌曲。现命令竞选团队把本禁令分发给所有参与候选人集会的人，包括工作人员、独立承包商和志愿者。任何不遵守本禁令的人，每次违规将被处以1,000美元罚款。”在候选人下一次集会上，一名已收到初步禁令副本的志愿者仍播放了音乐家的歌曲。该志愿者是否因违反初步禁令而须受金钱罚款？","choicesZh":{"A":"不须，因为志愿者没有收到听证会通知。","B":"不须，因为志愿者不是音乐家针对候选人所提诉讼的当事人。","C":"须受罚，因为该禁令符合公众最佳利益。","D":"须受罚，因为该禁令合理说明了被禁止的行为。"},"explanationZh":"初步禁令\n\n理由*\n申请人须证明：\n• 其很可能在实体问题上胜诉；\n• 如无救济，其很可能遭受不可弥补的损害；\n• 衡平利益权衡有利于申请人；且\n• 禁令符合公众最佳利益。\n\n内容\n发布禁令的命令必须：\n• 说明发布理由；\n• 合理说明被禁止或被命令实施的行为；并且\n• 具体说明条款。\n\n效力\n下列收到命令实际通知的人受其约束：\n• 当事人；\n• 当事人的高级职员、代理人、雇员和律师；以及\n• 与上述人员积极协同行动或参与的任何人。\n\n*申请人还必须提供保证金，以支付非申请方被错误禁止时产生的费用和损害。\n\n初步禁令是一项临时法院命令，在审判前或审判期间、案件尚在法院待决时发布，用于禁止或命令实施特定行为。只有在对方收到听证会通知后，法院才能发布初步禁令；禁令必须包含以下全部内容：\n• 发布理由；\n• 对被禁止或被命令实施之行为的合理说明；\n• 具体条款（例如受约束人员以及不遵守的处罚）。\n\n此外，必须向受初步禁令约束的任何人提供禁令的实际通知，包括：(1)当事人；(2)其高级职员、代理人、雇员和律师；以及(3)与当事人或其下属积极协同行动或参与的人。只有收到该通知的人，才可因违反禁令而按藐视法庭处理（例如被处以罚款）。\n\n本题中，法官发布初步禁令并说明发布理由。禁令要求候选人以及所有代表其工作或担任志愿者的人（具体条款）停止播放音乐家的歌曲（被禁止的行为）。由于志愿者收到禁令副本（实际通知），其违反初步禁令应受到金钱罚款。\n\n（选项A）志愿者要受禁令约束，必须收到禁令的实际通知；但其无须收到听证会通知，因为本诉讼中的对方当事人是候选人。\n\n（选项B）尽管志愿者不是音乐家针对候选人所提诉讼的当事人，但其代表候选人工作并收到禁令的实际通知。因此，志愿者受禁令约束。\n\n（选项C）由于执行版权法可以保护知识产权，该禁令可能符合公众最佳利益。但仅凭这一点，不足以作为对志愿者执行禁令的依据。\n\n学习目标：初步禁令必须包含：(1)发布理由；(2)对被禁止或被命令实施行为的合理说明；以及(3)具体条款。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(d)（初步禁令的必备内容和范围）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-265","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":265,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A fisherman from State A repeatedly trespassed across a homeowner's property in State B to fish in a publicly owned lake. After one of the fisherman's trips across the homeowner's property, the homeowner discovered that her $80,000 marble statue had been destroyed. The homeowner filed a lawsuit in a federal court in State B to enjoin the fisherman from trespassing and to recover for the damage to her statue. During discovery, the homeowner interviewed a former friend of the fisherman. The friend said that the fisherman had bragged about trespassing across the homeowner's property and accidently knocking over a marble statue on one occasion. The homeowner expects the fisherman to testify at trial that he did not know he was trespassing and never saw the statue. To impeach the fisherman's anticipated testimony, the homeowner intends to call the friend to testify. The homeowner does not want to disclose the friend's identity to the fisherman. What is the homeowner's strongest argument to accomplish her goal?","choices":{"A":"The fisherman could have discovered this evidence on his own.","B":"The fisherman will have a full and fair opportunity to cross-examine the friend.","C":"The friend's testimony will be used solely to impeach the fisherman's testimony.","D":"The homeowner's failure to disclose the friend as a witness is harmless."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nType Required disclosures Time to disclose\n\nInitial • Persons with relevant information • ≤ 14 days after initial disclosures* • Items in party's possession that planning conference support claim/defense • ≤ 30 days after post-• Computation of damages & conference service supporting material or joinder\n• Relevant insurance policies • As court orders or parties stipulate Expert • Identity of expert witnesses • ≥ 90 days before trial testimony • Expert's written report • ≤ 30 days after\n• If no report, subject matter & disclosure to rebut summary of testimony evidence\n• As court orders or parties stipulate Pretrial • Testifying witnesses • ≥ 30 days before trial disclosures* • Deposition testimony to be used • As court orders at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(a) requires parties to automatically make initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and pretrial disclosures. Pretrial disclosures must include:\n• the name, address, and phone number of each witness—separately identifying witnesses expected to testify and those who may be called if the need arises\n\n• the deposition testimony that will be used at trial and\n\n• all documents and exhibits—separately identifying items expected to be offered as evidence and those that may be offered if the need arises.\n\nThe purpose of pretrial disclosures is to avoid surprises at trial regarding substantive evidence—ie, evidence offered to help prove a material fact or issue. As a result, these requirements do not apply to impeachment evidence—ie, evidence used solely to discredit a witness and not to prove a material fact or issue. Therefore, the homeowner's strongest argument to avoid disclosing the friend's identity is that his testimony will be used solely to impeach the fisherman's testimony. (Choices A & B) The fact that the fisherman could have discovered this evidence on his own and will have a full and fair opportunity to cross-examine the friend does not provide grounds for nondisclosure. Instead, the homeowner can withhold the friend's identity only if his testimony will be used solely for impeachment. (Choice D) Failure to identify a witness in pretrial disclosures bars the nondisclosing party from using that witness's testimony in a motion, hearing, or trial—unless the failure to disclose was substantially justified or is harmless. But if the witness's testimony will be used only for impeachment purposes, the rules for pretrial disclosures do not apply and there is no need to show that the nondisclosure is harmless. Educational objective: A party's pretrial disclosures must include (1) the name, address, and phone number of each witness, (2) deposition testimony that will be used at trial, and (3) all documents and exhibits. But these requirements do not apply to witnesses and evidence that will be used solely for impeachment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3) (required pretrial disclosures).","rule":"A party's pretrial disclosures must include (1) the name, address, and phone number of each witness, (2) deposition testimony that will be used at trial, and (3) all documents and exhibits. But these requirements do not apply to witnesses and evidence that will be used solely for impeachment.","irac":{"issue":"A fisherman from State A repeatedly trespassed across a homeowner's property in State B to fish in a publicly owned lake. After one of the fisherman's trips across the homeowner's property, the homeowner discovered that her $80,000 marble statue had been destroyed. The homeowner filed a lawsuit in a federal court in State B to enjoin the fisherman from trespassing and to recover for the damage to her statue. During discovery, the homeowner interviewed a former friend of the fisherman. The friend said that the fisherman had bragged about trespassing across the homeowner's property and accidently knocking over a marble statue on one occasion. The homeowner expects the fisherman to testify at trial that he did not know he was trespassing and never saw the statue. To impeach the fisherman's anticipated testimony, the homeowner intends to call the friend to testify. The homeowner does not want to disclose the friend's identity to the fisherman. What is the homeowner's strongest argument to accomplish her goal?","rule":"A party's pretrial disclosures must include (1) the name, address, and phone number of each witness, (2) deposition testimony that will be used at trial, and (3) all documents and exhibits. But these requirements do not apply to witnesses and evidence that will be used solely for impeachment.","analysis":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nType Required disclosures Time to disclose\n\nInitial • Persons with relevant information • ≤ 14 days after initial disclosures* • Items in party's possession that planning conference support claim/defense • ≤ 30 days after post-• Computation of damages & conference service supporting material or joinder\n• Relevant insurance policies • As court orders or parties stipulate Expert • Identity of expert witnesses • ≥ 90 days before trial testimony • Expert's written report • ≤ 30 days after\n• If no report, subject matter & disclosure to rebut summary of testimony evidence\n• As court orders or parties stipulate Pretrial • Testifying witnesses • ≥ 30 days before trial disclosures* • Deposition testimony to be used • As court orders at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(a) requires parties to automatically make initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and pretrial disclosures. Pretrial disclosures must include:\n• the name, address, and phone number of each witness—separately identifying witnesses expected to testify and those who may be called if the need arises\n\n• the deposition testimony that will be used at trial and\n\n• all documents and exhibits—separately identifying items expected to be offered as evidence and those that may be offered if the need arises.\n\nThe purpose of pretrial disclosures is to avoid surprises at trial regarding substantive evidence—ie, evidence offered to help prove a material fact or issue. As a result, these requirements do not apply to impeachment evidence—ie, evidence used solely to discredit a witness and not to prove a material fact or issue. Therefore, the homeowner's strongest argument to avoid disclosing the friend's identity is that his testimony will be used solely to impeach the fisherman's testimony. (Choices A & B) The fact that the fisherman could have discovered this evidence on his own and will have a full and fair opportunity to cross-examine the friend does not provide grounds for nondisclosure. Instead, the homeowner can withhold the friend's identity only if his testimony will be used solely for impeachment. (Choice D) Failure to identify a witness in pretrial disclosures bars the nondisclosing party from using that witness's testimony in a motion, hearing, or trial—unless the failure to disclose was substantially justified or is harmless. But if the witness's testimony will be used only for impeachment purposes, the rules for pretrial disclosures do not apply and there is no need to show that the nondisclosure is harmless. Educational objective: A party's pretrial disclosures must include (1) the name, address, and phone number of each witness, (2) deposition testimony that will be used at trial, and (3) all documents and exhibits. But these requirements do not apply to witnesses and evidence that will be used solely for impeachment. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3) (required pretrial disclosures).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3) (required pretrial disclosures).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[801],"sourceAnswerPages":[802,803],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的渔民为到公共所有的湖中钓鱼，反复擅自穿过一名房主位于B州的房产。渔民某次穿过该房产后，房主发现其价值8万美元的大理石雕像被毁。房主在B州联邦法院起诉，请求禁止渔民擅自进入并赔偿雕像损失。证据开示期间，房主询问了渔民的一名前朋友。该朋友称，渔民曾吹嘘自己擅自穿过房主的房产，而且有一次不慎撞倒一座大理石雕像。房主预计渔民将在审判中作证称其不知道自己是在擅自进入，而且从未见过雕像。为弹劾渔民预期作出的证言，房主拟传唤该朋友作证。房主不希望向渔民披露朋友的身份。为实现该目标，房主最有力的论点是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"渔民本可自行发现这项证据。","B":"渔民将有充分且公平的机会交叉询问该朋友。","C":"该朋友的证言将仅用于弹劾渔民的证言。","D":"房主未披露该朋友为证人不会造成损害。"},"explanationZh":"强制性证据开示披露\n\n（FRCP 26(a)）\n\n初始披露\n• 掌握相关信息的人员\n• 当事人持有且支持请求或抗辩的物品\n• 损害赔偿计算及支持材料\n• 相关保险单\n时间：\n• 初始规划会议后不超过14天*\n• 在会议后才送达诉状或加入诉讼的当事人，应在送达或加入后不超过30天\n• 或依照法院命令或当事人约定\n\n专家证言披露\n• 专家证人的身份\n• 专家书面报告\n• 如无报告，则披露证言主题及概要\n时间：\n• 审判前至少90天\n• 反驳证据应在相对方披露后不超过30天\n• 或依照法院命令或当事人约定\n\n审前披露*\n• 出庭作证的证人\n• 审判时将使用的证言录取证言\n• 审判时将使用的文件或证物\n时间：\n• 审判前至少30天\n• 或依照法院命令\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不适用于仅为弹劾证人可信度而使用的证据。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》26(a)要求当事人自动进行初始披露、专家证言披露和审前披露。审前披露必须包括：\n• 每名证人的姓名、地址和电话号码，并分别列明预期作证的证人，以及在需要时可能被传唤的证人；\n• 审判中将使用的证言录取证言；以及\n• 所有文件和证物，并分别列明预期将作为证据提交的项目，以及在需要时可能提交的项目。\n\n审前披露旨在避免审判中对实体证据产生意外；实体证据是为帮助证明重要事实或问题而提出的证据。因此，这些要求不适用于弹劾证据，即仅用于削弱证人可信度、而非证明重要事实或问题的证据。因此，房主避免披露朋友身份的最有力论点，是其证言将仅用于弹劾渔民的证言。\n\n（选项A和B）渔民本可自行发现证据，以及将有充分且公平的机会交叉询问该朋友，均不构成不披露的理由。只有在该朋友的证言仅用于弹劾时，房主才能扣留其身份。\n\n（选项D）未在审前披露中识别证人，会使未披露方不得在动议、听证会或审判中使用该证人的证言，除非不披露具有充分正当理由或不造成损害。但若证言仅用于弹劾，审前披露规则本就不适用，无须证明不披露不造成损害。\n\n学习目标：一方的审前披露必须包括：(1)每名证人的姓名、地址和电话号码；(2)审判中将使用的证言录取证言；以及(3)所有文件和证物。但这些要求不适用于仅用于弹劾的证人和证据。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(a)(3)（强制性审前披露）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-266","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":266,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An employee brought a diversity suit against his employer in federal district court for intentional infliction of emotional distress. The employee claimed that the employer repeatedly engaged in conduct that caused the employee to suffer severe emotional distress that has resulted in physical manifestations. During discovery, the court issued a valid order compelling the employee to submit to a mental and physical examination by a licensed medical professional chosen by the employer. The employee refused to submit to the examination. The employer's attorney has moved for sanctions against the employee. Which of the following sanctions, if any, may the court order?","choices":{"A":"None, because the employee has the right to choose the person who conducts the examination.","B":"None, because a court cannot compel a person to submit to a mental examination.","C":"Hold the employee in contempt of court.","D":"Stay the proceeding until the order for examination is obeyed."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Sanctions for violating discovery order\n\n(FRCP 37(b))\n\nCourt location Sanctions allowed\n\nWhere deposition • Hold in contempt of court occurred\n\nWhere suit is pending • Direct that certain facts be construed against violator\n• Prohibit violator from offering certain evidence\n• Strike pleadings in whole or in part\n• Stay further proceedings until order is obeyed\n• Hold in contempt of court*\n• Dismiss claims in whole or in part\n• Issue default judgment against violator\n• Order payment of reasonable expenses (eg, attorney's fees) caused by violation FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed for failing to comply with order for physical or mental examination.\n\nDuring discovery, the court where the lawsuit is pending may order a party to undergo a mental or physical examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner (Choice B). This court order is valid when:\n• that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy\n\n• the motion for the order is based on good cause and\n\n• the order provides notice by specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, and scope of the exam, as well as the person(s) who will perform it.\n\nIf the party fails to comply with such a court order, as the employee did here, FRCP 37 allows the court to impose certain sanctions (see table above). Such sanctions include staying the proceeding until the order is obeyed.* *Instead of or in addition to such sanctions, the court must order the noncompliant party, his/her attorney, or both to pay the other party's reasonable expenses (including attorney's fees) unless the noncompliance was substantially justified or the fees would be unjust.\n\n(Choice A) The party to be examined does not have the right to choose who will perform the mental or physical examination. Instead, the party requesting the examination may choose the examiner unless the examined party has a valid objection. In that case, the court will appoint the examiner. (Choice C) The court where the lawsuit is pending may generally hold a party in contempt of court for failing to comply with a court order that compels discovery. However, a party cannot be held in contempt for failing to comply with a discovery order that requires the party to undergo a mental or physical examination (as seen here). Educational objective: A federal court may impose an FRCP 37 sanction on a party who fails to comply with a court order requiring a mental or physical examination, including staying the proceeding until the order is obeyed. But the court cannot hold the noncompliant party in contempt of court for such a violation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35 (physical and mental exams).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(b) (sanctions for noncompliance with court's discovery order).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2238 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining what sanctions may be issued against a party who fails to undergo a court-ordered mental or physical examination).","rule":"A federal court may impose an FRCP 37 sanction on a party who fails to comply with a court order requiring a mental or physical examination, including staying the proceeding until the order is obeyed. But the court cannot hold the noncompliant party in contempt of court for such a violation.","irac":{"issue":"An employee brought a diversity suit against his employer in federal district court for intentional infliction of emotional distress. The employee claimed that the employer repeatedly engaged in conduct that caused the employee to suffer severe emotional distress that has resulted in physical manifestations. During discovery, the court issued a valid order compelling the employee to submit to a mental and physical examination by a licensed medical professional chosen by the employer. The employee refused to submit to the examination. The employer's attorney has moved for sanctions against the employee. Which of the following sanctions, if any, may the court order?","rule":"A federal court may impose an FRCP 37 sanction on a party who fails to comply with a court order requiring a mental or physical examination, including staying the proceeding until the order is obeyed. But the court cannot hold the noncompliant party in contempt of court for such a violation.","analysis":"Sanctions for violating discovery order\n\n(FRCP 37(b))\n\nCourt location Sanctions allowed\n\nWhere deposition • Hold in contempt of court occurred\n\nWhere suit is pending • Direct that certain facts be construed against violator\n• Prohibit violator from offering certain evidence\n• Strike pleadings in whole or in part\n• Stay further proceedings until order is obeyed\n• Hold in contempt of court*\n• Dismiss claims in whole or in part\n• Issue default judgment against violator\n• Order payment of reasonable expenses (eg, attorney's fees) caused by violation FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed for failing to comply with order for physical or mental examination.\n\nDuring discovery, the court where the lawsuit is pending may order a party to undergo a mental or physical examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner (Choice B). This court order is valid when:\n• that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy\n\n• the motion for the order is based on good cause and\n\n• the order provides notice by specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, and scope of the exam, as well as the person(s) who will perform it.\n\nIf the party fails to comply with such a court order, as the employee did here, FRCP 37 allows the court to impose certain sanctions (see table above). Such sanctions include staying the proceeding until the order is obeyed.* *Instead of or in addition to such sanctions, the court must order the noncompliant party, his/her attorney, or both to pay the other party's reasonable expenses (including attorney's fees) unless the noncompliance was substantially justified or the fees would be unjust.\n\n(Choice A) The party to be examined does not have the right to choose who will perform the mental or physical examination. Instead, the party requesting the examination may choose the examiner unless the examined party has a valid objection. In that case, the court will appoint the examiner. (Choice C) The court where the lawsuit is pending may generally hold a party in contempt of court for failing to comply with a court order that compels discovery. However, a party cannot be held in contempt for failing to comply with a discovery order that requires the party to undergo a mental or physical examination (as seen here). Educational objective: A federal court may impose an FRCP 37 sanction on a party who fails to comply with a court order requiring a mental or physical examination, including staying the proceeding until the order is obeyed. But the court cannot hold the noncompliant party in contempt of court for such a violation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35 (physical and mental exams).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(b) (sanctions for noncompliance with court's discovery order).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2238 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining what sanctions may be issued against a party who fails to undergo a court-ordered mental or physical examination).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35 (physical and mental exams).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(b) (sanctions for noncompliance with court's discovery order).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2238 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining what sanctions may be issued against a party who fails to undergo a court-ordered mental or physical examination).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[804],"sourceAnswerPages":[805,806],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名雇员在联邦地区法院对其雇主提起多元州籍诉讼，主张故意造成精神痛苦。雇员称，雇主反复实施使其遭受严重精神痛苦的行为，并已产生身体症状。证据开示期间，法院发布有效命令，强制雇员接受由雇主选定的持证医疗专业人员进行精神和身体检查。雇员拒绝接受检查。雇主律师申请对雇员施加制裁。法院可以命令下列哪项制裁（如有）？","choicesZh":{"A":"不得施加任何制裁，因为雇员有权选择进行检查的人。","B":"不得施加任何制裁，因为法院不能强制某人接受精神检查。","C":"以藐视法庭处理雇员。","D":"中止诉讼程序，直至雇员遵守检查命令。"},"explanationZh":"违反证据开示命令的制裁\n\n（FRCP 37(b)）\n\n法院所在地｜允许的制裁\n\n证言录取进行地法院\n• 以藐视法庭处理。\n\n诉讼待决法院\n• 命令把某些事实作不利于违规者的认定；\n• 禁止违规者提出某些证据；\n• 全部或部分删除诉状；\n• 中止后续程序，直至命令得到遵守；\n• 以藐视法庭处理；*\n• 全部或部分驳回请求；\n• 对违规者作出缺席判决；\n• 命令支付违规所造成的合理费用（例如律师费）。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不遵守身体或精神检查命令时，不得采用藐视法庭制裁。\n\n证据开示期间，诉讼待决法院可以命令一方接受由具有适当执照或认证的检查人员进行的精神或身体检查（选项B）。该法院命令在下列条件下有效：\n• 该方的精神或身体状况属于争议事项；\n• 请求命令的动议有正当理由；且\n• 命令通过指定检查的时间、地点、方式、条件、范围及执行人员而提供通知。\n\n如果一方像本题雇员一样不遵守该法院命令，FRCP 37允许法院施加某些制裁（见上表），包括中止诉讼程序直至命令得到遵守。*\n\n*除该类制裁外或在其基础上，法院必须命令不遵守的一方、其律师或二者支付对方的合理费用（包括律师费），除非不遵守具有充分正当理由或费用裁定会造成不公。\n\n（选项A）被检查方无权选择进行精神或身体检查的人。相反，请求检查的一方可以选择检查人员，除非被检查方提出有效异议；在该情况下，由法院指定检查人员。\n\n（选项C）诉讼待决法院通常可以因一方不遵守强制证据开示的法院命令而以藐视法庭处理。但是，不得因一方不遵守要求其接受精神或身体检查的证据开示命令而以藐视法庭处理（本题正是如此）。\n\n学习目标：一方不遵守要求精神或身体检查的法院命令时，联邦法院可以依据FRCP 37施加制裁，包括中止程序直至其遵守命令；但法院不能因该违规而以藐视法庭处理该方。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35（身体和精神检查）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(b)（不遵守法院证据开示命令的制裁）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2238 (3d ed. 2020)（说明可以对未接受法院命令之精神或身体检查的一方施加何种制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-267","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":267,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A dispute arose between a woman and a man over who was entitled to insurance policy proceeds held by a company incorporated and headquartered in the northern district of State A. The woman resides in the southern district of State A, and the man resides in the eastern district of State B. The company filed a statutory interpleader action against the woman and the man in a federal court in the eastern district of State B. The company posted a bond of $50,000, which represents the amount of the disputed insurance policy proceeds. The woman and the man were served with process at their respective homes. The woman has moved to dismiss the action, claiming that the federal court has no subject-matter jurisdiction over the claim, lacks personal jurisdiction over her, and is not a proper venue. Is the court likely to grant the woman's motion?","choices":{"A":"No.","B":"Yes, because the court lacks personal jurisdiction over her.","C":"Yes, because the parties lack complete diversity of citizenship.","D":"Yes, because venue is improper."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Rule interpleader v. Statutory interpleader\n\nRequirements Rule interpleader Statutory interpleader\n\nSubject-matter • Complete diversity between • Minimal diversity jurisdiction stakeholder & claimants + between claimants + amount in amount in controversy controversy >$75,000 or ≥$500\n• Claim arises under U.S. Constitution, treaty, or federal law Personal • Consent • Nationwide service of jurisdiction • Service of process process\n• Specific jurisdiction\n• General jurisdiction Venue Any district where: Any district where\n\n• any claimant resides, if all • any claimant resides, reside in same state even if claimants reside\n• property located/events in different states occurred or\n• any claimant is subject to personal jurisdiction (if neither of above applies) Deposit Not required Deposit property with court or post bond\n\nThe company has filed a statutory interpleader action in federal court. This action, like rule interpleader, is available when multiple persons claim an interest in the same property (ie, the stake). It allows the possessor of the stake (ie, the stakeholder) to avoid multiple liability by joining the claimants to litigate the ownership of the stake among themselves. However, a statutory interpleader action must satisfy special requirements for:\n• subject-matter jurisdiction – requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity of citizenship between at least two claimants\n\n• personal jurisdiction – exists over any claimant who is served with process anywhere within the U.S. and\n\n• venue – proper in any judicial district where any claimant resides, even if the claimants reside in different states.\n\nHere, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction since the disputed proceeds are $50,000 and the woman and the man are citizens of States A and B, respectively. Personal jurisdiction exists over the woman because she was served with process in State A (Choice B). And venue is proper since the man resides in the eastern district of State B—where the court is located (Choice D). Therefore, the court will likely deny the woman's motion. (Choice C) Rule interpleader actions based on diversity jurisdiction require complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants. However, statutory interpleader actions (as seen here) only require minimal diversity between the claimants.* *Subject-matter jurisdiction can also be established in a rule interpleader action if the claim presents a federal question. Educational objective: For statutory interpleader actions, subject-matter jurisdiction requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between two or more claimants. Personal jurisdiction exists over any claimant who was served with process anywhere in the U.S. Venue is proper in any judicial district where any claimant resides. References\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory interpleader).","rule":"For statutory interpleader actions, subject-matter jurisdiction requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between two or more claimants. Personal jurisdiction exists over any claimant who was served with process anywhere in the U.S. Venue is proper in any judicial district where any claimant resides.","irac":{"issue":"A dispute arose between a woman and a man over who was entitled to insurance policy proceeds held by a company incorporated and headquartered in the northern district of State A. The woman resides in the southern district of State A, and the man resides in the eastern district of State B. The company filed a statutory interpleader action against the woman and the man in a federal court in the eastern district of State B. The company posted a bond of $50,000, which represents the amount of the disputed insurance policy proceeds. The woman and the man were served with process at their respective homes. The woman has moved to dismiss the action, claiming that the federal court has no subject-matter jurisdiction over the claim, lacks personal jurisdiction over her, and is not a proper venue. Is the court likely to grant the woman's motion?","rule":"For statutory interpleader actions, subject-matter jurisdiction requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between two or more claimants. Personal jurisdiction exists over any claimant who was served with process anywhere in the U.S. Venue is proper in any judicial district where any claimant resides.","analysis":"Rule interpleader v. Statutory interpleader\n\nRequirements Rule interpleader Statutory interpleader\n\nSubject-matter • Complete diversity between • Minimal diversity jurisdiction stakeholder & claimants + between claimants + amount in amount in controversy controversy >$75,000 or ≥$500\n• Claim arises under U.S. Constitution, treaty, or federal law Personal • Consent • Nationwide service of jurisdiction • Service of process process\n• Specific jurisdiction\n• General jurisdiction Venue Any district where: Any district where\n\n• any claimant resides, if all • any claimant resides, reside in same state even if claimants reside\n• property located/events in different states occurred or\n• any claimant is subject to personal jurisdiction (if neither of above applies) Deposit Not required Deposit property with court or post bond\n\nThe company has filed a statutory interpleader action in federal court. This action, like rule interpleader, is available when multiple persons claim an interest in the same property (ie, the stake). It allows the possessor of the stake (ie, the stakeholder) to avoid multiple liability by joining the claimants to litigate the ownership of the stake among themselves. However, a statutory interpleader action must satisfy special requirements for:\n• subject-matter jurisdiction – requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity of citizenship between at least two claimants\n\n• personal jurisdiction – exists over any claimant who is served with process anywhere within the U.S. and\n\n• venue – proper in any judicial district where any claimant resides, even if the claimants reside in different states.\n\nHere, the court has subject-matter jurisdiction since the disputed proceeds are $50,000 and the woman and the man are citizens of States A and B, respectively. Personal jurisdiction exists over the woman because she was served with process in State A (Choice B). And venue is proper since the man resides in the eastern district of State B—where the court is located (Choice D). Therefore, the court will likely deny the woman's motion. (Choice C) Rule interpleader actions based on diversity jurisdiction require complete diversity between the stakeholder and the claimants. However, statutory interpleader actions (as seen here) only require minimal diversity between the claimants.* *Subject-matter jurisdiction can also be established in a rule interpleader action if the claim presents a federal question. Educational objective: For statutory interpleader actions, subject-matter jurisdiction requires an amount in controversy of at least $500 and minimal diversity between two or more claimants. Personal jurisdiction exists over any claimant who was served with process anywhere in the U.S. Venue is proper in any judicial district where any claimant resides. References\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory interpleader).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361 (statutory interpleader).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[807],"sourceAnswerPages":[808,809],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子与一名男子就谁有权取得一家公司持有的保险金发生争议。该公司在A州北区注册成立且总部位于该区。女子居住在A州南区，男子居住在B州东区。公司在B州东区联邦法院对女子和男子提起制定法上的权益争讼。公司提供5万美元保证金，该金额等于争议保险金。女子和男子分别在各自住所收到诉讼文书送达。女子申请驳回诉讼，主张联邦法院对请求不具有事项管辖权、对她不具有对人管辖权，而且审判地不适当。法院是否可能批准该申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会。","B":"会，因为法院对她缺乏对人管辖权。","C":"会，因为各方缺乏完全多元州籍。","D":"会，因为审判地不适当。"},"explanationZh":"规则上的权益争讼与制定法上的权益争讼\n\n要求｜规则上的权益争讼｜制定法上的权益争讼\n\n事项管辖权\n规则上的：\n• 标的持有人与请求人之间完全多元州籍，且争议金额超过75,000美元；或\n• 请求基于美国宪法、条约或联邦法律。\n制定法上的：\n• 请求人之间最低限度多元州籍，且争议金额至少为500美元。\n\n对人管辖权\n规则上的：\n• 同意；\n• 诉讼文书送达；\n• 特定管辖权；\n• 一般管辖权。\n制定法上的：\n• 可在全国范围送达诉讼文书。\n\n审判地\n规则上的，以下任何地区：\n• 若所有请求人居住在同一州，则任何请求人居住地；\n• 财产所在地或事件发生地；或者\n• 如前两者均不适用，任何请求人受对人管辖权约束的地区。\n制定法上的：\n• 任何请求人居住的任何地区，即使请求人居住在不同州。\n\n交存\n规则上的：\n• 不要求。\n制定法上的：\n• 将财产交存法院或提供保证金。\n\n公司已在联邦法院提起制定法上的权益争讼。与规则上的权益争讼一样，该诉讼适用于多人对同一财产（即争议标的）主张利益的情形。它允许标的持有人把请求人加入诉讼，由其相互诉讼确定标的所有权，从而避免承担多重责任。不过，制定法上的权益争讼必须满足以下特别要求：\n• 事项管辖权——争议金额至少为500美元，且至少两名请求人之间存在最低限度的多元州籍；\n• 对人管辖权——任何请求人在美国境内任何地点收到诉讼文书送达，法院即对其具有对人管辖权；以及\n• 审判地——任何请求人居住的任何司法区均属适当，即使请求人居住在不同州。\n\n本题中，争议保险金为5万美元，女子和男子分别为A州和B州公民，所以法院具有事项管辖权。女子在A州收到诉讼文书送达，因此法院对她具有对人管辖权（选项B）。男子居住在法院所在的B州东区，所以审判地适当（选项D）。因此，法院很可能驳回女子的驳回申请。\n\n（选项C）基于多元州籍管辖权的规则上权益争讼要求标的持有人与请求人之间完全多元州籍；但制定法上的权益争讼（本题所涉）只要求请求人之间存在最低限度多元州籍。*\n\n*如果规则上的权益争讼提出联邦问题，也可以确立事项管辖权。\n\n学习目标：制定法上的权益争讼要求：事项管辖权方面，争议金额至少为500美元且两名或以上请求人之间存在最低限度多元州籍；对人管辖权方面，任何请求人在美国境内任何地点收到诉讼文书送达即可；审判地方面，任何请求人居住的司法区均适当。\n\n参考资料\n• 28 U.S.C. §§ 1335, 1397, 2361（制定法上的权益争讼）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-268","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":268,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A distributor brought a diversity action against a manufacturer in federal district court for breach of contract. The district court ordered the parties' attorneys to attend a pretrial conference to obtain admissions and stipulations about the facts in the case. When the conference started, the manufacturer's attorney announced that his client had not authorized him to make admissions or stipulations. The district court canceled the conference as a result. This was the third time the court had to cancel a pretrial conference due to the attorney's failure to obtain authorization to make admissions or stipulations on behalf of his client. After the conference, the district court issued an order that struck one of the manufacturer's affirmative defenses and required the manufacturer to pay the attorney's fees incurred by the distributor for attending the pretrial conference. At the conclusion of trial, the manufacturer appealed the order. Is the appellate court likely to reverse the district court's order?","choices":{"A":"No, because the district court has absolute discretion to issue sanctions.","B":"No, because the order was reasonable under the circumstances.","C":"Yes, because the district court is limited to requiring the manufacturer to pay the distributor's attorney's fees.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer did not authorize the attorney to make admissions or stipulations."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A federal district court may order the attorneys and any unrepresented parties to attend one or more pretrial conferences. These conferences are designed to improve the trial's quality, facilitate settlement, and expedite litigation (eg, by obtaining admissions and stipulations). To promote those goals, a district court may impose reasonable sanctions on any party or attorney who:\n• failed to attend a pretrial conference\n• was substantially unprepared to participate in the conference\n• did not participate in good faith in the conference or\n• failed to obey a pretrial order. Here, the manufacturer's attorney was substantially unprepared to participate in the pretrial conference because he failed to obtain the manufacturer's authorization to make admissions and stipulations. This led the district court to strike one of the manufacturer's affirmative defenses. This sanction was reasonable since this was the third time the court had to cancel a pretrial conference due to the attorney's failure to obtain such authorization (Choice C). Additionally, a court must order a noncompliant party and/or attorney to pay the other party's reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, unless (1) the noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust. The attorney's unpreparedness was not substantially justified because he should have obtained the manufacturer's authorization before the conference (Choice D). And since there is no indication that awarding attorney's fees would be unjust, the appellate court will likely affirm the order. (Choice A) Although the district court has discretion to decide which sanctions to impose, this discretion is not absolute. The reason is that an appellate court has the power to reverse a district court's arbitrary or unreasonable sanction as an abuse of discretion (not seen here). Educational objective: A federal court may issue reasonable sanctions against any party or attorney who is substantially unprepared to participate in a pretrial conference. Additionally, a court must order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses unless (1) noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f) (pretrial conference sanctions).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to comply with pretrial conference and/or order).","rule":"A federal court may issue reasonable sanctions against any party or attorney who is substantially unprepared to participate in a pretrial conference. Additionally, a court must order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses unless (1) noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust.","irac":{"issue":"A distributor brought a diversity action against a manufacturer in federal district court for breach of contract. The district court ordered the parties' attorneys to attend a pretrial conference to obtain admissions and stipulations about the facts in the case. When the conference started, the manufacturer's attorney announced that his client had not authorized him to make admissions or stipulations. The district court canceled the conference as a result. This was the third time the court had to cancel a pretrial conference due to the attorney's failure to obtain authorization to make admissions or stipulations on behalf of his client. After the conference, the district court issued an order that struck one of the manufacturer's affirmative defenses and required the manufacturer to pay the attorney's fees incurred by the distributor for attending the pretrial conference. At the conclusion of trial, the manufacturer appealed the order. Is the appellate court likely to reverse the district court's order?","rule":"A federal court may issue reasonable sanctions against any party or attorney who is substantially unprepared to participate in a pretrial conference. Additionally, a court must order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses unless (1) noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust.","analysis":"A federal district court may order the attorneys and any unrepresented parties to attend one or more pretrial conferences. These conferences are designed to improve the trial's quality, facilitate settlement, and expedite litigation (eg, by obtaining admissions and stipulations). To promote those goals, a district court may impose reasonable sanctions on any party or attorney who:\n• failed to attend a pretrial conference\n• was substantially unprepared to participate in the conference\n• did not participate in good faith in the conference or\n• failed to obey a pretrial order. Here, the manufacturer's attorney was substantially unprepared to participate in the pretrial conference because he failed to obtain the manufacturer's authorization to make admissions and stipulations. This led the district court to strike one of the manufacturer's affirmative defenses. This sanction was reasonable since this was the third time the court had to cancel a pretrial conference due to the attorney's failure to obtain such authorization (Choice C). Additionally, a court must order a noncompliant party and/or attorney to pay the other party's reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees, unless (1) the noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust. The attorney's unpreparedness was not substantially justified because he should have obtained the manufacturer's authorization before the conference (Choice D). And since there is no indication that awarding attorney's fees would be unjust, the appellate court will likely affirm the order. (Choice A) Although the district court has discretion to decide which sanctions to impose, this discretion is not absolute. The reason is that an appellate court has the power to reverse a district court's arbitrary or unreasonable sanction as an abuse of discretion (not seen here). Educational objective: A federal court may issue reasonable sanctions against any party or attorney who is substantially unprepared to participate in a pretrial conference. Additionally, a court must order the payment of the opposing party's reasonable expenses unless (1) noncompliance was substantially justified or (2) the payment would be unjust. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f) (pretrial conference sanctions).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to comply with pretrial conference and/or order).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f) (pretrial conference sanctions).\n\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining when sanctions are appropriate for failure to comply with pretrial conference and/or order).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[810],"sourceAnswerPages":[811,812],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名经销商在联邦地区法院对一家制造商提起多元州籍违约诉讼。地区法院命令双方律师参加审前会议，以取得对案件事实的承认和约定。会议开始时，制造商律师宣布其客户没有授权他作出承认或约定，地区法院因此取消会议。这已是法院第三次因该律师未取得代表客户作出承认或约定的授权而取消审前会议。会议后，地区法院发布命令，删除制造商的一项积极抗辩，并要求制造商支付经销商因参加审前会议产生的律师费。审判结束后，制造商就该命令提出上诉。上诉法院是否可能推翻地区法院的命令？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为地区法院对施加制裁拥有绝对裁量权。","B":"不会，因为该命令在具体情形下合理。","C":"会，因为地区法院只能命令制造商支付经销商的律师费。","D":"会，因为制造商没有授权律师作出承认或约定。"},"explanationZh":"联邦地区法院可以命令律师和任何无律师代理的当事人参加一次或多次审前会议。这些会议旨在提高审判质量、促进和解并加快诉讼，例如通过取得承认和约定。为促进这些目标，地区法院可以对存在下列行为的任何当事人或律师施加合理制裁：\n• 未参加审前会议；\n• 对参加会议准备严重不足；\n• 未善意参与会议；或者\n• 不遵守审前命令。\n\n本题中，制造商律师因未取得制造商授权其作出承认和约定，而对参加审前会议准备严重不足。这导致地区法院删除制造商的一项积极抗辩。由于这已经是法院第三次因律师未取得该授权而不得不取消审前会议，该制裁合理（选项C）。\n\n此外，法院必须命令不遵守要求的当事人和／或律师支付对方的合理费用，包括律师费，除非：(1)不遵守具有充分正当理由；或(2)支付费用会造成不公。律师准备不足不具有充分正当理由，因为其本应在会议前取得制造商的授权（选项D）。没有迹象表明裁定律师费会造成不公，所以上诉法院很可能维持该命令。\n\n（选项A）尽管地区法院有权酌情决定采用何种制裁，但该裁量权不是绝对的。上诉法院有权把地区法院恣意或不合理的制裁作为滥用裁量权予以推翻（本题不存在这种情况）。\n\n学习目标：对于对参加审前会议准备严重不足的任何当事人或律师，联邦法院可以施加合理制裁。此外，法院必须命令其支付对方的合理费用，除非：(1)不遵守具有充分正当理由；或(2)支付会造成不公。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16(f)（审前会议制裁）。\n• 6A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 1531 (3d ed. 2020)（说明何时适合因不遵守审前会议和／或命令而施加制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-081"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-269","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":269,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A corporation brought a patent infringement action in federal court against a manufacturer. The corporation alleged that the manufacturer infringed on the corporation's patent for a shoe design. During discovery, the corporation sent the manufacturer a request to \"produce all electronic documents relating to the production of the shoe.\" The corporation did not specify the form in which the documents were to be produced. The manufacturer produced one year's worth of electronic documents relating to the production of the shoe, but it argued that it could not produce any documents beyond this time because they had been deleted from its server. The corporation filed a motion to compel the production of the documents. In response to the motion, the manufacturer established that recovering the electronic documents would be unduly burdensome and costly because they could be recovered only if the manufacturer hired an expensive data-recovery firm. How should the court rule on the corporation's motion to compel?","choices":{"A":"It should deny the motion, because the corporation failed to specify the form in which the electronic documents should be produced.","B":"It should deny the motion, if the manufacturer provides a meritorious defense to the claim.","C":"It should grant the motion, if the corporation demonstrates that it will prevail on the merits of the case.","D":"It should grant the motion, if the corporation shows good cause for the production."},"answer":"D","explanation":"During discovery, parties can use various methods to compel disclosure of information related to the case. A request for production is a discovery method used to compel an opposing party to produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information (ESI), tangible things, and real property. A party generally must produce ESI in the form specified in the request or, if no form is specified, then in the ESI's ordinarily maintained or usable form. If ESI is not produced, the requesting party may file a motion to compel production. The producing party must then show that the ESI is not reasonably accessible due to undue burden or cost. If this is established, the court will order the production of ESI only if the requesting party shows good cause for the production. Here, the corporation filed a motion to compel the production of the manufacturer's electronic documents related to the shoe design at issue. The manufacturer established that retrieving the documents that had been deleted from its server would be unduly burdensome and costly because it would have to hire an expensive data-recovery firm. However, if the corporation shows good cause for the production, the court should grant the corporation's motion to compel. (Choice A) A party requesting the production of ESI may, but is not required to, specify the form in which the ESI is to be produced. (Choices B & C) In opposing a motion to compel discovery, the producing party (the manufacturer) need not provide a meritorious defense. Additionally, the requesting party (the corporation) does not need to show that it will prevail on the merits. Instead, the focus is on whether the discovery at issue should be produced. Educational objective: In response to a motion to compel the production of electronically stored information (ESI), the producing party must show that the ESI is not reasonably accessible due to undue burden or cost. If shown, the court will order the production of the ESI only if the requesting party demonstrates good cause for the production. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(B) (limitations on electronic discovery).\n\n• 8 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2008.2 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the procedure for inaccessible ESI).","rule":"In response to a motion to compel the production of electronically stored information (ESI), the producing party must show that the ESI is not reasonably accessible due to undue burden or cost. If shown, the court will order the production of the ESI only if the requesting party demonstrates good cause for the production.","irac":{"issue":"A corporation brought a patent infringement action in federal court against a manufacturer. The corporation alleged that the manufacturer infringed on the corporation's patent for a shoe design. During discovery, the corporation sent the manufacturer a request to \"produce all electronic documents relating to the production of the shoe.\" The corporation did not specify the form in which the documents were to be produced. The manufacturer produced one year's worth of electronic documents relating to the production of the shoe, but it argued that it could not produce any documents beyond this time because they had been deleted from its server. The corporation filed a motion to compel the production of the documents. In response to the motion, the manufacturer established that recovering the electronic documents would be unduly burdensome and costly because they could be recovered only if the manufacturer hired an expensive data-recovery firm. How should the court rule on the corporation's motion to compel?","rule":"In response to a motion to compel the production of electronically stored information (ESI), the producing party must show that the ESI is not reasonably accessible due to undue burden or cost. If shown, the court will order the production of the ESI only if the requesting party demonstrates good cause for the production.","analysis":"During discovery, parties can use various methods to compel disclosure of information related to the case. A request for production is a discovery method used to compel an opposing party to produce and allow inspection of documents, electronically stored information (ESI), tangible things, and real property. A party generally must produce ESI in the form specified in the request or, if no form is specified, then in the ESI's ordinarily maintained or usable form. If ESI is not produced, the requesting party may file a motion to compel production. The producing party must then show that the ESI is not reasonably accessible due to undue burden or cost. If this is established, the court will order the production of ESI only if the requesting party shows good cause for the production. Here, the corporation filed a motion to compel the production of the manufacturer's electronic documents related to the shoe design at issue. The manufacturer established that retrieving the documents that had been deleted from its server would be unduly burdensome and costly because it would have to hire an expensive data-recovery firm. However, if the corporation shows good cause for the production, the court should grant the corporation's motion to compel. (Choice A) A party requesting the production of ESI may, but is not required to, specify the form in which the ESI is to be produced. (Choices B & C) In opposing a motion to compel discovery, the producing party (the manufacturer) need not provide a meritorious defense. Additionally, the requesting party (the corporation) does not need to show that it will prevail on the merits. Instead, the focus is on whether the discovery at issue should be produced. Educational objective: In response to a motion to compel the production of electronically stored information (ESI), the producing party must show that the ESI is not reasonably accessible due to undue burden or cost. If shown, the court will order the production of the ESI only if the requesting party demonstrates good cause for the production. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(B) (limitations on electronic discovery).\n\n• 8 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2008.2 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the procedure for inaccessible ESI).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(B) (limitations on electronic discovery).\n\n• 8 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2008.2 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the procedure for inaccessible ESI).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[815],"sourceAnswerPages":[816],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家公司在联邦法院对一家制造商提起专利侵权诉讼，主张制造商侵犯其鞋款设计专利。证据开示期间，公司向制造商发出请求，要求“提供与该鞋生产有关的所有电子文件”。公司没有指定文件的提供形式。制造商提供了与鞋生产有关的一年期电子文件，但主张无法提供更早的文件，因为这些文件已从服务器删除。公司申请强制提供这些文件。制造商在答复动议时证明，只有聘请费用高昂的数据恢复公司才能恢复电子文件，因此恢复工作会造成不当负担且费用高昂。法院应如何裁定公司的强制提供申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"应驳回申请，因为公司没有指定电子文件的提供形式。","B":"如果制造商对请求提出有法律依据的抗辩，则应驳回申请。","C":"如果公司证明其将在案件实体问题上胜诉，则应批准申请。","D":"如果公司证明提供文件具有正当理由，则应批准申请。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示期间，当事人可以运用多种方式强制披露与案件有关的信息。提供请求是一种证据开示方式，用于强制对方提供并允许检查文件、电子存储信息（ESI）、有形物和不动产。一方通常必须按请求指定的形式提供ESI；未指定形式时，则按ESI日常保存的形式或可用形式提供。\n\n如果ESI未被提供，请求方可以申请强制提供。提供方随后必须证明，由于不当负担或费用，该ESI并非合理可访问。如果这一点得到证明，只有在请求方证明提供具有正当理由时，法院才会命令提供ESI。\n\n本题中，公司申请强制提供制造商与争议鞋款设计有关的电子文件。制造商证明，从服务器恢复已删除文件会造成不当负担且费用高昂，因为必须聘请昂贵的数据恢复公司。但是，如果公司证明提供具有正当理由，法院就应批准公司的强制提供申请。\n\n（选项A）请求提供ESI的一方可以指定ESI的提供形式，但并非必须指定。\n\n（选项B和C）反对强制证据开示申请时，提供方（制造商）无须提出有法律依据的实体抗辩。请求方（公司）也无须证明其将在实体问题上胜诉。重点在于争议证据是否应被提供。\n\n学习目标：面对强制提供电子存储信息（ESI）的申请，提供方必须证明由于不当负担或费用，ESI并非合理可访问。证明后，只有在请求方证明提供具有正当理由时，法院才会命令提供ESI。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(B)（电子证据开示的限制）。\n• 8 Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2008.2 (3d ed. 2020)（说明无法访问ESI的处理程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-084"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-270","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":270,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A woman bought a piece of farmland in the state where she resides. Three years after this purchase, a man informed her that he held a mortgage against the farmland and that she must pay him. The woman then filed a quiet title action in an appropriate federal court in the state where the farmland is located. The man was properly served with the complaint and summons. The day after receiving the complaint and summons, the man called the woman and informed her that he co-owns the mortgage on the farmland with his brother. Both the man and his brother live in another state. What is the easiest action the woman can take to join the man's brother in the suit?","choices":{"A":"File a motion for compulsory joinder.","B":"Immediately file and serve an amended complaint listing both siblings as defendants.","C":"Move the court for permission to join the other sibling.","D":"Voluntarily dismiss the case and refile a complaint naming both siblings as defendants."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 19, a person must be joined as a party to a lawsuit in certain situations. Here, for example, the brother is likely a required party in the woman's quiet title action since his absence could prejudice his mortgage interest. And though the woman failed to name him in her complaint, FRCP 15 allows a party to amend a pleading once as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the opposing party's written consent. But this must be done within 21 days after:\n• serving that pleading if a responsive pleading is not required or\n• being served with a responsive pleading or FRCP 12 motion (whichever occurs first) if a responsive pleading is required. A defendant is required to file and serve an answer in response to the plaintiff's complaint. And since the man has not yet filed an answer (or FRCP 12 motion), the woman can amend her complaint without the court's permission or the man's written consent (Choice C). Therefore, the easiest way the woman can join the brother in the suit is to immediately file and serve an amended complaint listing both siblings as defendants. (Choice A) Compulsory (ie, required) joinder makes it mandatory to include all necessary parties in a lawsuit—eg, co-owners of property in a quiet title action. Therefore, a defendant would file a motion for compulsory joinder to add any necessary parties, while a plaintiff would simply amend the complaint (as seen here). (Choice D) A plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss a suit if the plaintiff wants to file it in a different court or has reached a settlement with the defendant. But the plaintiff need not dismiss a complaint to add another party. Educational objective: A party can amend a pleading once as a matter of course within 21 days (1) after serving that pleading or (2) if a responsive pleading is required, after being served with a responsive pleading or motion under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a) (amending complaint before trial).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","rule":"A party can amend a pleading once as a matter of course within 21 days (1) after serving that pleading or (2) if a responsive pleading is required, after being served with a responsive pleading or motion under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12.","irac":{"issue":"A woman bought a piece of farmland in the state where she resides. Three years after this purchase, a man informed her that he held a mortgage against the farmland and that she must pay him. The woman then filed a quiet title action in an appropriate federal court in the state where the farmland is located. The man was properly served with the complaint and summons. The day after receiving the complaint and summons, the man called the woman and informed her that he co-owns the mortgage on the farmland with his brother. Both the man and his brother live in another state. What is the easiest action the woman can take to join the man's brother in the suit?","rule":"A party can amend a pleading once as a matter of course within 21 days (1) after serving that pleading or (2) if a responsive pleading is required, after being served with a responsive pleading or motion under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 19, a person must be joined as a party to a lawsuit in certain situations. Here, for example, the brother is likely a required party in the woman's quiet title action since his absence could prejudice his mortgage interest. And though the woman failed to name him in her complaint, FRCP 15 allows a party to amend a pleading once as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the opposing party's written consent. But this must be done within 21 days after:\n• serving that pleading if a responsive pleading is not required or\n• being served with a responsive pleading or FRCP 12 motion (whichever occurs first) if a responsive pleading is required. A defendant is required to file and serve an answer in response to the plaintiff's complaint. And since the man has not yet filed an answer (or FRCP 12 motion), the woman can amend her complaint without the court's permission or the man's written consent (Choice C). Therefore, the easiest way the woman can join the brother in the suit is to immediately file and serve an amended complaint listing both siblings as defendants. (Choice A) Compulsory (ie, required) joinder makes it mandatory to include all necessary parties in a lawsuit—eg, co-owners of property in a quiet title action. Therefore, a defendant would file a motion for compulsory joinder to add any necessary parties, while a plaintiff would simply amend the complaint (as seen here). (Choice D) A plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss a suit if the plaintiff wants to file it in a different court or has reached a settlement with the defendant. But the plaintiff need not dismiss a complaint to add another party. Educational objective: A party can amend a pleading once as a matter of course within 21 days (1) after serving that pleading or (2) if a responsive pleading is required, after being served with a responsive pleading or motion under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a) (amending complaint before trial).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a) (amending complaint before trial).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[818],"sourceAnswerPages":[819],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在其居住州购买一块农地。购买三年后，一名男子告知她，自己对该农地享有抵押权，女子必须向其付款。女子随后在农地所在州具有适当管辖权的联邦法院提起确认产权诉讼。起诉状和传票适当地送达男子。收到起诉状和传票的次日，男子致电女子，告知他与其兄弟共同拥有该农地上的抵押权。男子及其兄弟均居住在另一州。女子把男子的兄弟加入诉讼，最简便的措施是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提交强制合并申请。","B":"立即提交并送达把兄弟二人均列为被告的修正起诉状。","C":"请求法院准许加入另一名兄弟。","D":"自愿撤诉，并重新提交把兄弟二人均列为被告的起诉状。"},"explanationZh":"根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）19，在某些情况下，某人必须作为当事人加入诉讼。例如，本题中的兄弟很可能是女子确认产权诉讼中的必要当事人，因为其缺席可能损害其抵押权利益。\n\n尽管女子未在起诉状中把他列为被告，FRCP 15允许一方依当然权利修正一次诉状，即无须法院许可或对方书面同意，但必须在下列时间内进行：\n• 如果无须回应诉状，则在送达该诉状后21天内；或者\n• 如果须回应诉状，则在收到回应诉状或FRCP 12动议送达后21天内，以先发生者为准。\n\n被告必须提交并送达答辩状以回应原告的起诉状。由于男子尚未提交答辩状（或FRCP 12动议），女子可以无须法院许可或男子书面同意而修正起诉状（选项C）。因此，把兄弟加入诉讼最简便的方式，是立即提交并送达把兄弟二人均列为被告的修正起诉状。\n\n（选项A）强制（即必要）合并要求把所有必要当事人列入诉讼，例如确认产权诉讼中的财产共同所有人。因此，被告会通过强制合并申请加入任何必要当事人，而原告只需修正起诉状（本题即如此）。\n\n（选项D）原告希望改在另一法院提起诉讼或已与被告达成和解时，可以自愿撤诉。但为加入另一当事人，原告无须撤回起诉状。\n\n学习目标：一方可依当然权利修正一次诉状，期限为：(1)送达该诉状后21天内；或(2)如须回应诉状，则在收到回应诉状或《联邦民事诉讼规则》12动议送达后21天内。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(a)（审判前修正起诉状）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19（当事人的强制合并）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-069"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-271","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":271,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"On July 1, 2015, a man was seriously injured when the helicopter he was piloting crashed. The man's attorney diligently investigated and determined that a valve malfunction caused the crash. The attorney contacted the helicopter distributor and requested the name of the company that manufactured the valve in the man's helicopter. The helicopter distributor mistakenly gave the attorney the name of the wrong valve manufacturer. On June 1, 2017, the man sued the helicopter distributor and the valve manufacturer in federal court to recover $5 million in damages. On January 3, 2018, the name of the valve manufacturer that actually manufactured the valve in the man's helicopter was revealed during discovery. The same day, the man moved to amend his complaint to substitute the correct valve manufacturer as the defendant. The correct valve company did not receive notice of the man's suit until this time. The applicable statute of limitations provides: \"Any personal injury action shall be brought within two years from the date the alleged injuries occurred. A complaint may be amended to properly name a defendant not identified until after the complaint has been filed if the complaint was filed within the designated limitations period.\" May the man amend his complaint?","choices":{"A":"No, because the correct valve manufacturer did not receive timely notice of the suit.","B":"No, because the statute of limitations has expired.","C":"Yes, because the amendment concerns the same transaction asserted in the original pleading.","D":"Yes, because the amendment is permitted under the relation-back doctrine."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A pleading (eg, complaint) cannot be amended after the statute of limitations has expired unless the relation-back doctrine applies. Under this doctrine, an amendment is treated as if it were filed on the same date as the original pleading so that the amendment \"relates back\" to the date of the original pleading. An amendment that changes a party relates back if the applicable statute of limitations allows. Here, the statute of limitations on the man's personal-injury claim expired on July 1, 2017— two years after his alleged injury. He moved to amend his complaint on January 1, 2018— six months after the statute of limitations had expired. But the applicable law allows relation back when the plaintiff made a mistake as to the proper defendant's identity. Therefore, the amendment \"relates back\" to the date the man's original complaint was filed on June 1, 2017—before the statute of limitations expired—and the man may amend his complaint (Choice B). Had the applicable statute of limitations been silent about relation back, the amendment would still relate back if:\n• the amendment concerned the same transaction or occurrence as the original pleading (Choice C)\n\n• the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original pleading was filed (Choice A) and\n\n• the new party knew or should have known that the suit would have been brought against it but for a mistake concerning the proper party's identity (as seen here).\n\nBut this analysis is unnecessary since the applicable law does allow relation back under these circumstances. Educational objective: An amended pleading that changes a party to a lawsuit after the statute of limitations has expired will \"relate back\" to the date of the original pleading if the statute of limitations allows. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).","rule":"An amended pleading that changes a party to a lawsuit after the statute of limitations has expired will \"relate back\" to the date of the original pleading if the statute of limitations allows.","irac":{"issue":"On July 1, 2015, a man was seriously injured when the helicopter he was piloting crashed. The man's attorney diligently investigated and determined that a valve malfunction caused the crash. The attorney contacted the helicopter distributor and requested the name of the company that manufactured the valve in the man's helicopter. The helicopter distributor mistakenly gave the attorney the name of the wrong valve manufacturer. On June 1, 2017, the man sued the helicopter distributor and the valve manufacturer in federal court to recover $5 million in damages. On January 3, 2018, the name of the valve manufacturer that actually manufactured the valve in the man's helicopter was revealed during discovery. The same day, the man moved to amend his complaint to substitute the correct valve manufacturer as the defendant. The correct valve company did not receive notice of the man's suit until this time. The applicable statute of limitations provides: \"Any personal injury action shall be brought within two years from the date the alleged injuries occurred. A complaint may be amended to properly name a defendant not identified until after the complaint has been filed if the complaint was filed within the designated limitations period.\" May the man amend his complaint?","rule":"An amended pleading that changes a party to a lawsuit after the statute of limitations has expired will \"relate back\" to the date of the original pleading if the statute of limitations allows.","analysis":"A pleading (eg, complaint) cannot be amended after the statute of limitations has expired unless the relation-back doctrine applies. Under this doctrine, an amendment is treated as if it were filed on the same date as the original pleading so that the amendment \"relates back\" to the date of the original pleading. An amendment that changes a party relates back if the applicable statute of limitations allows. Here, the statute of limitations on the man's personal-injury claim expired on July 1, 2017— two years after his alleged injury. He moved to amend his complaint on January 1, 2018— six months after the statute of limitations had expired. But the applicable law allows relation back when the plaintiff made a mistake as to the proper defendant's identity. Therefore, the amendment \"relates back\" to the date the man's original complaint was filed on June 1, 2017—before the statute of limitations expired—and the man may amend his complaint (Choice B). Had the applicable statute of limitations been silent about relation back, the amendment would still relate back if:\n• the amendment concerned the same transaction or occurrence as the original pleading (Choice C)\n\n• the new party received notice of the suit within 90 days after the original pleading was filed (Choice A) and\n\n• the new party knew or should have known that the suit would have been brought against it but for a mistake concerning the proper party's identity (as seen here).\n\nBut this analysis is unnecessary since the applicable law does allow relation back under these circumstances. Educational objective: An amended pleading that changes a party to a lawsuit after the statute of limitations has expired will \"relate back\" to the date of the original pleading if the statute of limitations allows. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (relation back of amended pleadings).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[821],"sourceAnswerPages":[822],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"2015年7月1日，一名男子驾驶的直升机坠毁，男子严重受伤。其律师勤勉调查后认定，阀门故障导致坠毁。律师联系直升机经销商，要求提供制造该直升机阀门的公司名称。经销商误把另一家阀门制造商的名称提供给律师。2017年6月1日，男子在联邦法院起诉直升机经销商和该错误的阀门制造商，请求500万美元损害赔偿。2018年1月3日，在证据开示过程中，实际制造男子直升机所用阀门的制造商名称被揭示。同日，男子申请修正起诉状，把被告替换为正确的阀门制造商。正确的阀门公司此前一直未收到男子诉讼的通知。适用的诉讼时效规定：“任何人身伤害诉讼均应在所称伤害发生之日起两年内提起。如果起诉状在规定时效期间内提交，而被告直到提交起诉状后才得到正确识别，可以修正起诉状以正确列明该被告。”男子可以修正起诉状吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为正确的阀门制造商没有及时收到诉讼通知。","B":"不可以，因为诉讼时效已经届满。","C":"可以，因为修正涉及原诉状中主张的同一交易。","D":"可以，因为追溯适用原则允许该修正。"},"explanationZh":"诉讼时效届满后，除非追溯适用原则成立，否则诉状（例如起诉状）不得修正。根据该原则，修正视为在原诉状提交之日提交，因此修正“追溯适用”至原诉状提交之日。如果适用的诉讼时效法允许，变更当事人的修正可以追溯适用。\n\n本题中，男子人身伤害请求的诉讼时效于2017年7月1日届满，即所称伤害发生两年后。他于2018年1月3日申请修正起诉状，已在诉讼时效届满约六个月后。但是，适用法律允许在原告误认正确被告身份时追溯适用。因此，该修正“追溯适用”至男子原起诉状提交日，即2017年6月1日——当时诉讼时效尚未届满——男子可以修正起诉状（选项B）。\n\n如果适用的诉讼时效法未提及追溯适用，在下列条件下修正仍会追溯适用：\n• 修正涉及与原诉状相同的交易或事件（选项C）；\n• 新当事人在原诉状提交后90天内收到诉讼通知（选项A）；并且\n• 新当事人知道或应当知道，若非对正确当事人身份的误认，诉讼本应对其提起（本题即如此）。\n\n但本题无需进行上述分析，因为适用法律已经明确允许在本案情形下追溯适用。\n\n学习目标：诉讼时效届满后，变更诉讼当事人的修正诉状，如果诉讼时效法允许，将“追溯适用”至原诉状提交之日。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c)（修正诉状的追溯适用）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-085"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-272","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":272,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A man brought a diversity action against a retail store in federal district court for injuries he sustained from a fall in the store. The man alleged that the store allowed its entryway to become slippery from a pipe that leaked water. Sixty days before trial, the store sent the man its pretrial disclosures, which included a spreadsheet containing the names, addresses, and telephone numbers of the witnesses whom the store expected to testify at trial. Due to a formatting problem, one of the testifying witnesses was omitted from the spreadsheet. The store discovered the omission the following day. Twenty-one days before trial, the store sent the man's attorney an amended spreadsheet containing the testifying witness's information. This was the first time the man's attorney had heard of the testifying witness. The man's attorney argues that the witness should be excluded from testifying at trial. Which of the following arguments is most likely to achieve the man's goal of preventing the witness from testifying at trial?","choices":{"A":"The parties did not stipulate that pretrial disclosures could be amended before trial.","B":"The store failed to timely correct its pretrial disclosures.","C":"The store never obtained the court's leave before correcting its pretrial disclosures.","D":"The store's initial pretrial disclosures were untimely filed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nInitial disclosures* • Persons with relevant information\n• Items in party's possession that support claim/defense\n• Computation of damages & supporting material\n• Relevant insurance policies Expert testimony • Identity of expert witnesses\n• Expert's written report\n• If no report, subject matter & summary of testimony Pretrial disclosures* • Testifying witnesses\n• Deposition testimony to be used at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nDuring discovery, certain information must be automatically disclosed through required disclosures. This includes pretrial disclosures, the purpose of which is to avoid surprise at trial regarding substantive evidence. Therefore, pretrial disclosures must, at a minimum, identify the witnesses expected to testify and the evidence expected to be offered at trial. If a party learns that its disclosures were materially incomplete or incorrect, it must supplement or correct them in a timely manner unless the other parties were made aware of that information during discovery or in writing.* Timeliness is generally evaluated based on how soon the supplemental/corrected information was disclosed after it became available to the disclosing party. Failure to timely supplement generally results in exclusion of the omitted witness or evidence at trial. Here, the store learned that its pretrial disclosures were materially incomplete because they omitted a testifying witness. The store was required to and did correct those disclosures because the man's attorney had no knowledge of the omission. But waiting nearly 40 days after discovering the mistake before doing so was likely untimely. Therefore, arguing that the correction was untimely will most likely prevent the witness from testifying. *A party also must supplement or correct any incomplete or incorrect initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and responses to interrogatories, requests for production, and requests for admission.\n\n(Choices A & C) Neither the court's leave (ie, permission) nor the parties' stipulation is required for a party to supplement or correct its pretrial disclosures. (Choice D) Pretrial disclosures must be made at least 30 days before trial. Therefore, the store's disclosures were timely submitted 60 days before trial. Educational objective: A party must timely supplement or correct pretrial disclosures when (1) the party learns that its disclosures were materially incomplete or incorrect and (2) the other parties have not been made aware of the supplemental or corrected information during discovery or in writing. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(e) (supplementing disclosures and responses).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2049.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining a party's duty to supplement or correct initial disclosures, expert testimony, pretrial disclosures, and certain discovery responses).","rule":"A party must timely supplement or correct pretrial disclosures when (1) the party learns that its disclosures were materially incomplete or incorrect and (2) the other parties have not been made aware of the supplemental or corrected information during discovery or in writing.","irac":{"issue":"A man brought a diversity action against a retail store in federal district court for injuries he sustained from a fall in the store. The man alleged that the store allowed its entryway to become slippery from a pipe that leaked water. Sixty days before trial, the store sent the man its pretrial disclosures, which included a spreadsheet containing the names, addresses, and telephone numbers of the witnesses whom the store expected to testify at trial. Due to a formatting problem, one of the testifying witnesses was omitted from the spreadsheet. The store discovered the omission the following day. Twenty-one days before trial, the store sent the man's attorney an amended spreadsheet containing the testifying witness's information. This was the first time the man's attorney had heard of the testifying witness. The man's attorney argues that the witness should be excluded from testifying at trial. Which of the following arguments is most likely to achieve the man's goal of preventing the witness from testifying at trial?","rule":"A party must timely supplement or correct pretrial disclosures when (1) the party learns that its disclosures were materially incomplete or incorrect and (2) the other parties have not been made aware of the supplemental or corrected information during discovery or in writing.","analysis":"Required discovery disclosures\n\n(FRCP 26(a))\n\nInitial disclosures* • Persons with relevant information\n• Items in party's possession that support claim/defense\n• Computation of damages & supporting material\n• Relevant insurance policies Expert testimony • Identity of expert witnesses\n• Expert's written report\n• If no report, subject matter & summary of testimony Pretrial disclosures* • Testifying witnesses\n• Deposition testimony to be used at trial\n• Documents/exhibits to be used at trial FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Does not apply to evidence used solely for impeachment.\n\nDuring discovery, certain information must be automatically disclosed through required disclosures. This includes pretrial disclosures, the purpose of which is to avoid surprise at trial regarding substantive evidence. Therefore, pretrial disclosures must, at a minimum, identify the witnesses expected to testify and the evidence expected to be offered at trial. If a party learns that its disclosures were materially incomplete or incorrect, it must supplement or correct them in a timely manner unless the other parties were made aware of that information during discovery or in writing.* Timeliness is generally evaluated based on how soon the supplemental/corrected information was disclosed after it became available to the disclosing party. Failure to timely supplement generally results in exclusion of the omitted witness or evidence at trial. Here, the store learned that its pretrial disclosures were materially incomplete because they omitted a testifying witness. The store was required to and did correct those disclosures because the man's attorney had no knowledge of the omission. But waiting nearly 40 days after discovering the mistake before doing so was likely untimely. Therefore, arguing that the correction was untimely will most likely prevent the witness from testifying. *A party also must supplement or correct any incomplete or incorrect initial disclosures, disclosures of expert testimony, and responses to interrogatories, requests for production, and requests for admission.\n\n(Choices A & C) Neither the court's leave (ie, permission) nor the parties' stipulation is required for a party to supplement or correct its pretrial disclosures. (Choice D) Pretrial disclosures must be made at least 30 days before trial. Therefore, the store's disclosures were timely submitted 60 days before trial. Educational objective: A party must timely supplement or correct pretrial disclosures when (1) the party learns that its disclosures were materially incomplete or incorrect and (2) the other parties have not been made aware of the supplemental or corrected information during discovery or in writing. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(e) (supplementing disclosures and responses).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2049.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining a party's duty to supplement or correct initial disclosures, expert testimony, pretrial disclosures, and certain discovery responses).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(e) (supplementing disclosures and responses).\n\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2049.1 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining a party's duty to supplement or correct initial disclosures, expert testimony, pretrial disclosures, and certain discovery responses).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[824],"sourceAnswerPages":[825,826],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在联邦地区法院对一家零售商店提起多元州籍诉讼，要求赔偿其在商店内跌倒所受伤害。男子主张，一根管道漏水使商店入口变得湿滑。审判前60天，商店向男子发送审前披露，其中包括一份电子表格，列有商店预期在审判中作证之证人的姓名、地址和电话号码。由于格式问题，一名出庭证人被遗漏出表格。商店次日发现该遗漏。审判前21天，商店向男子律师发送修正后的电子表格，包含该出庭证人的信息。男子律师此前从未听说该证人，并主张应禁止其在审判中作证。下列哪项论点最可能实现男子阻止该证人作证的目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"双方没有约定可在审判前修正审前披露。","B":"商店未及时更正其审前披露。","C":"商店更正审前披露前从未取得法院许可。","D":"商店最初的审前披露提交不及时。"},"explanationZh":"强制性证据开示披露\n\n（FRCP 26(a)）\n\n初始披露*\n• 掌握相关信息的人员；\n• 当事人持有且支持请求或抗辩的物品；\n• 损害赔偿计算及支持材料；\n• 相关保险单。\n\n专家证言\n• 专家证人的身份；\n• 专家书面报告；\n• 如无报告，则披露证言主题及概要。\n\n审前披露*\n• 出庭作证的证人；\n• 审判时将使用的证言录取证言；\n• 审判时将使用的文件或证物。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不适用于仅为弹劾证人可信度而使用的证据。\n\n证据开示期间，某些信息必须通过强制披露自动提供。其中包括审前披露，其目的在于避免审判中对实体证据产生意外。因此，审前披露至少必须识别预期在审判中作证的证人，以及预期在审判中提出的证据。\n\n如果一方得知其披露存在重大遗漏或错误，必须及时补充或更正，除非其他当事人已经在证据开示期间或通过书面方式获知该信息。* 是否及时，通常根据披露方取得补充或更正信息后多快作出披露来判断。未及时补充通常会导致被遗漏的证人或证据在审判中被排除。\n\n本题中，商店得知审前披露存在重大遗漏，因为其中漏列一名出庭证人。男子律师不知道该遗漏，所以商店必须更正，而且确实作出了更正。但是，商店发现错误后等待近40天才更正，很可能不及时。因此，主张更正不及时最可能阻止该证人作证。\n\n*一方还必须补充或更正任何不完整或错误的初始披露、专家证言披露，以及对书面询问、提供请求和承认请求的答复。\n\n（选项A和C）一方补充或更正审前披露，既无须法院许可，也无须双方约定。\n\n（选项D）审前披露必须在审判前至少30天作出。因此，商店在审判前60天提交披露是及时的。\n\n学习目标：在以下条件下，一方必须及时补充或更正审前披露：(1)该方得知披露存在重大遗漏或错误；且(2)其他当事人尚未在证据开示期间或通过书面方式获知补充或更正的信息。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(e)（补充披露和答复）。\n• 8A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2049.1 (3d ed. 2020)（说明补充或更正初始披露、专家证言、审前披露及某些证据开示答复的义务）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-273","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":273,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A landowner sued a company in a federal district court in State A for violating a federal environmental statute. The landowner alleged that the company dumped chemicals into a stream, which polluted the groundwater near his home. The company denied the landowner's allegation and sought to prove that its waste system prevents chemicals from being dumped into the stream. The company's attorney signed and issued a subpoena that required the engineer who designed the waste system to testify at trial. The engineer was personally served with the subpoena, which contained the contents required by the federal rules. The courthouse where the engineer will testify is 81 miles away from where she resides. The engineer has asked her attorney's advice as to whether she must comply with the subpoena and testify at trial. What advice should the engineer's attorney give?","choices":{"A":"The engineer must comply with the subpoena if the company's attorney is authorized to practice in State A's federal court.","B":"The engineer must comply with the subpoena if the landowner signed it.","C":"The engineer need not comply with the subpoena because she is not a party to the suit.","D":"The engineer need not comply with the subpoena because the courthouse is located more than 75 miles from where she resides."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Subpoena requirements\n\n(FRCP 45(a))\n\nContents • Must state (1) court from which subpoena issued, (2) title of action & civil-action number, (3) time & place for performance & (4) person's rights & duties and\n• Must command person to: – attend & testify at hearing, trial, deposition* – produce documents, electronically stored information, things and/or – permit inspection of premises Issuance Subpoena must issue from court where action is pending by court clerk or attorney authorized to practice law in issuing court\n\nService Subpoena served in U.S. must:\n\n• be personally delivered by nonparty who is at least 18 years old and\n• if attendance is required, include fees for one day's attendance & mileage unless issued on behalf of federal government/agency/officer *Subpoena commanding attendance at deposition must also state method for recording testimony.\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA subpoena is a written court order that generally commands a person (party or nonparty) to attend and testify at a trial, hearing, or deposition. A subpoena is valid and enforceable when it:\n• contains the contents required by the federal rules (see image above)\n• is signed and issued by the court clerk or an attorney who is authorized to practice law in the court where the action is pending* and\n• is properly served in the U.S. through personal delivery by a nonparty who is at least 18 years old. Practicing attorneys may issue subpoenas in their capacity as officers of the court because this eases the court's administrative burdens. Here, the engineer was properly served with a subpoena containing the contents required by the federal rules. The company's attorney had the power to sign and issue that subpoena without the court clerk's participation if the attorney was authorized to practice law in State A's federal court. The attorney was presumably authorized to do so since the attorney is\n\nrepresenting the company in the action pending before that court. Therefore, the engineer's attorney should advise her to comply with the subpoena and testify at trial. *This means that parties representing themselves without the assistance of an attorney (ie, pro se litigants) must always request that the court clerk issue their subpoenas. (Choice B) The opposing party (landowner) need not sign a subpoena for it to be effectively issued. (Choice C) Subpoenas can be served on parties and nonparties, so the engineer must comply if the subpoena was validly issued and served. (Choice D) A subpoena may command a person's attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition within 100 miles (not 75 miles) of where that person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business. Since the courthouse where the trial will occur is within 100 miles of where the engineer resides, the subpoena may command her attendance. Educational objective: A subpoena is valid and enforceable when it (1) contains the contents required by the federal rules, (2) is signed and issued by the court clerk or an attorney authorized to practice law in the court where the action is pending, and (3) is properly served. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(a) (subpoena form and contents requirements).\n\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2453 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements to issue a subpoena).","rule":"A subpoena is valid and enforceable when it (1) contains the contents required by the federal rules, (2) is signed and issued by the court clerk or an attorney authorized to practice law in the court where the action is pending, and (3) is properly served.","irac":{"issue":"A landowner sued a company in a federal district court in State A for violating a federal environmental statute. The landowner alleged that the company dumped chemicals into a stream, which polluted the groundwater near his home. The company denied the landowner's allegation and sought to prove that its waste system prevents chemicals from being dumped into the stream. The company's attorney signed and issued a subpoena that required the engineer who designed the waste system to testify at trial. The engineer was personally served with the subpoena, which contained the contents required by the federal rules. The courthouse where the engineer will testify is 81 miles away from where she resides. The engineer has asked her attorney's advice as to whether she must comply with the subpoena and testify at trial. What advice should the engineer's attorney give?","rule":"A subpoena is valid and enforceable when it (1) contains the contents required by the federal rules, (2) is signed and issued by the court clerk or an attorney authorized to practice law in the court where the action is pending, and (3) is properly served.","analysis":"Subpoena requirements\n\n(FRCP 45(a))\n\nContents • Must state (1) court from which subpoena issued, (2) title of action & civil-action number, (3) time & place for performance & (4) person's rights & duties and\n• Must command person to: – attend & testify at hearing, trial, deposition* – produce documents, electronically stored information, things and/or – permit inspection of premises Issuance Subpoena must issue from court where action is pending by court clerk or attorney authorized to practice law in issuing court\n\nService Subpoena served in U.S. must:\n\n• be personally delivered by nonparty who is at least 18 years old and\n• if attendance is required, include fees for one day's attendance & mileage unless issued on behalf of federal government/agency/officer *Subpoena commanding attendance at deposition must also state method for recording testimony.\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA subpoena is a written court order that generally commands a person (party or nonparty) to attend and testify at a trial, hearing, or deposition. A subpoena is valid and enforceable when it:\n• contains the contents required by the federal rules (see image above)\n• is signed and issued by the court clerk or an attorney who is authorized to practice law in the court where the action is pending* and\n• is properly served in the U.S. through personal delivery by a nonparty who is at least 18 years old. Practicing attorneys may issue subpoenas in their capacity as officers of the court because this eases the court's administrative burdens. Here, the engineer was properly served with a subpoena containing the contents required by the federal rules. The company's attorney had the power to sign and issue that subpoena without the court clerk's participation if the attorney was authorized to practice law in State A's federal court. The attorney was presumably authorized to do so since the attorney is\n\nrepresenting the company in the action pending before that court. Therefore, the engineer's attorney should advise her to comply with the subpoena and testify at trial. *This means that parties representing themselves without the assistance of an attorney (ie, pro se litigants) must always request that the court clerk issue their subpoenas. (Choice B) The opposing party (landowner) need not sign a subpoena for it to be effectively issued. (Choice C) Subpoenas can be served on parties and nonparties, so the engineer must comply if the subpoena was validly issued and served. (Choice D) A subpoena may command a person's attendance at a trial, hearing, or deposition within 100 miles (not 75 miles) of where that person resides, is employed, or regularly transacts business. Since the courthouse where the trial will occur is within 100 miles of where the engineer resides, the subpoena may command her attendance. Educational objective: A subpoena is valid and enforceable when it (1) contains the contents required by the federal rules, (2) is signed and issued by the court clerk or an attorney authorized to practice law in the court where the action is pending, and (3) is properly served. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(a) (subpoena form and contents requirements).\n\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2453 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements to issue a subpoena).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(a) (subpoena form and contents requirements).\n\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2453 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements to issue a subpoena).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[827],"sourceAnswerPages":[828,829],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名土地所有人在A州联邦地区法院起诉一家公司违反联邦环境制定法。土地所有人主张公司把化学品倾倒进溪流，污染了其住宅附近的地下水。公司否认该指控，并试图证明其废物处理系统能够防止化学品被倾倒入溪流。公司律师签发一张传票，要求设计该废物处理系统的工程师在审判中作证。传票由专人送达工程师，并包含联邦规则要求的内容。工程师作证的法院距其住所81英里。工程师询问其律师，她是否必须遵守传票并在审判中作证。律师应如何建议？","choicesZh":{"A":"如果公司律师获准在A州该联邦法院执业，工程师就必须遵守传票。","B":"如果土地所有人签署传票，工程师就必须遵守。","C":"工程师无须遵守传票，因为她不是诉讼当事人。","D":"工程师无须遵守传票，因为法院距其住所超过75英里。"},"explanationZh":"传票的要求\n\n（FRCP 45(a)）\n\n内容\n• 必须载明：(1)签发传票的法院；(2)诉讼名称和民事案号；(3)履行时间与地点；以及(4)被传唤人的权利和义务；\n• 必须命令该人：\n  – 出席听证会、审判或证言录取并作证；*\n  – 提供文件、电子存储信息或物品；和／或\n  – 允许检查场所。\n\n签发\n传票必须由诉讼待决法院签发，并由法院书记员或获准在该法院执业的律师签发。\n\n送达\n在美国境内送达的传票必须：\n• 由年满18周岁的非当事人亲自交付；并且\n• 如要求出席，应随附一天的出席费和里程费；但代表联邦政府、机构或官员签发的除外。\n\n*要求参加证言录取的传票还必须载明记录证言的方式。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n传票是一项书面法院命令，通常命令某人（当事人或非当事人）出席审判、听证会或证言录取并作证。传票在符合下列条件时有效且可执行：\n• 包含联邦规则要求的内容（见上表）；\n• 由法院书记员，或获准在诉讼待决法院执业的律师签署并签发；* 并且\n• 在美国境内由年满18周岁的非当事人亲自交付，完成适当送达。\n\n执业律师作为法院官员可以签发传票，因为这能减轻法院的行政负担。本题中，工程师收到的传票已经适当送达，并包含联邦规则要求的内容。如果公司律师获准在A州该联邦法院执业，就有权在没有法院书记员参与的情况下签署并签发传票。该律师代表公司处理在该法院待决的诉讼，推定其已获准执业。因此，工程师的律师应建议她遵守传票并在审判中作证。\n\n*这意味着，没有律师协助而自行代理的当事人必须始终请求法院书记员签发传票。\n\n（选项B）传票无须由对方当事人（土地所有人）签署即可有效签发。\n\n（选项C）传票可以向当事人和非当事人送达，因此，如果传票有效签发并送达，工程师必须遵守。\n\n（选项D）传票可以命令某人在距其居住地、工作地或通常开展业务地100英里以内（而非75英里以内）出席审判、听证会或证言录取。审判法院距工程师住所不足100英里，因此传票可以命令她出席。\n\n学习目标：传票在以下情况下有效且可执行：(1)包含联邦规则要求的内容；(2)由法院书记员或获准在诉讼待决法院执业的律师签署并签发；且(3)得到适当送达。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 45(a)（传票形式和内容要求）。\n• 9A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2453 (3d ed. 2020)（说明签发传票的要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-274","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":274,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A dancer at an adult entertainment establishment sued her employer in a federal district court for allegedly creating a hostile work environment and engaging in sexual harassment in violation of federal law. The dancer alleged that the employer constantly pressured her to engage in sexual intercourse, but she repeatedly refused his advances. At the dancer's deposition, the employer's attorney asked the dancer about her sexual history with the employer. The dancer denied having any sexual relations with the employer. In an effort to embarrass the dancer, the employer's attorney asked about her general sexual history. The dancer's attorney objected, directed the dancer not to answer the question, and immediately suspended the deposition. The next day, the dancer's attorney filed a motion in the court where the dancer's suit was brought for a protective order to prevent the employer from asking the dancer about her general sexual history. The dancer's attorney did not confer or attempt to confer with the employer's attorney before filing the motion. Is the court likely to grant the protective order?","choices":{"A":"No, because the dancer did not answer the deposition question.","B":"No, because the dancer's attorney made no attempt to confer with the employer's attorney.","C":"Yes, because the dancer will suffer irreparable harm if the questioning is allowed to continue.","D":"Yes, because the motion was properly filed in the court where the action is pending."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Protective orders\n\n(FRCP 26(c))\n\nRequirements • Good cause – to protect parties & persons from annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, undue burden, or undue expense\n• Certification – movant conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with party seeking discovery to resolve dispute without court action Filing • In court where action is pending or where deposition will occur\n\nPossible • Forbid, limit, or specify terms for discovery contents • Prescribe alternative discovery method\n• Designate persons who may be present during discovery\n• Require that discovery be sealed & opened only on court order FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA protective order is a court order issued to protect a party or other person from whom discovery is sought from annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, undue burden, or undue expense. A court will grant a protective order if the movant (1) shows good cause for the order and (2) certifies that he/she conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with the party seeking discovery to resolve the matter without court action. The purpose of the conference requirement is to save judicial time and resources by limiting the court's involvement in discovery. Here, there is good cause to issue a protective order because the employer's attorney asked about the dancer's general sexual history to embarrass her. However, the dancer's attorney made no attempt to confer with the employer's attorney before filing the motion. As a result, the dancer failed to satisfy her duty to confer, and the court will likely deny her motion for a protective order. (Choice A) A protective order can be granted regardless of whether the movant answered the deposition question. But the motion will likely be denied here since the dancer did not make a good-faith attempt to resolve the matter without court action. (Choice C) Irreparable harm must be shown to obtain a temporary restraining order—not a protective order. A temporary restraining order is sought to prevent immediate and irreparable harm to the movant by keeping the status quo until the court can hold a hearing on the issue. In contrast, a discovery protective order is sought to limit the scope of discovery and prevent abuse.\n\n(Choice D) A motion for a protective order may be filed in the court where the deposition will be taken or where the action is pending (as seen here). But since the dancer failed to confer with the employer before filing such a motion, the court cannot grant it. Educational objective: A court will grant a protective order if the movant (1) shows good cause for the order and (2) certifies that he/she conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with the party seeking discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(c) (protective orders).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(d)(3) (moving to terminate or limit deposition).","rule":"A court will grant a protective order if the movant (1) shows good cause for the order and (2) certifies that he/she conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with the party seeking discovery.","irac":{"issue":"A dancer at an adult entertainment establishment sued her employer in a federal district court for allegedly creating a hostile work environment and engaging in sexual harassment in violation of federal law. The dancer alleged that the employer constantly pressured her to engage in sexual intercourse, but she repeatedly refused his advances. At the dancer's deposition, the employer's attorney asked the dancer about her sexual history with the employer. The dancer denied having any sexual relations with the employer. In an effort to embarrass the dancer, the employer's attorney asked about her general sexual history. The dancer's attorney objected, directed the dancer not to answer the question, and immediately suspended the deposition. The next day, the dancer's attorney filed a motion in the court where the dancer's suit was brought for a protective order to prevent the employer from asking the dancer about her general sexual history. The dancer's attorney did not confer or attempt to confer with the employer's attorney before filing the motion. Is the court likely to grant the protective order?","rule":"A court will grant a protective order if the movant (1) shows good cause for the order and (2) certifies that he/she conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with the party seeking discovery.","analysis":"Protective orders\n\n(FRCP 26(c))\n\nRequirements • Good cause – to protect parties & persons from annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, undue burden, or undue expense\n• Certification – movant conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with party seeking discovery to resolve dispute without court action Filing • In court where action is pending or where deposition will occur\n\nPossible • Forbid, limit, or specify terms for discovery contents • Prescribe alternative discovery method\n• Designate persons who may be present during discovery\n• Require that discovery be sealed & opened only on court order FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA protective order is a court order issued to protect a party or other person from whom discovery is sought from annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, undue burden, or undue expense. A court will grant a protective order if the movant (1) shows good cause for the order and (2) certifies that he/she conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with the party seeking discovery to resolve the matter without court action. The purpose of the conference requirement is to save judicial time and resources by limiting the court's involvement in discovery. Here, there is good cause to issue a protective order because the employer's attorney asked about the dancer's general sexual history to embarrass her. However, the dancer's attorney made no attempt to confer with the employer's attorney before filing the motion. As a result, the dancer failed to satisfy her duty to confer, and the court will likely deny her motion for a protective order. (Choice A) A protective order can be granted regardless of whether the movant answered the deposition question. But the motion will likely be denied here since the dancer did not make a good-faith attempt to resolve the matter without court action. (Choice C) Irreparable harm must be shown to obtain a temporary restraining order—not a protective order. A temporary restraining order is sought to prevent immediate and irreparable harm to the movant by keeping the status quo until the court can hold a hearing on the issue. In contrast, a discovery protective order is sought to limit the scope of discovery and prevent abuse.\n\n(Choice D) A motion for a protective order may be filed in the court where the deposition will be taken or where the action is pending (as seen here). But since the dancer failed to confer with the employer before filing such a motion, the court cannot grant it. Educational objective: A court will grant a protective order if the movant (1) shows good cause for the order and (2) certifies that he/she conferred or attempted to confer in good faith with the party seeking discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(c) (protective orders).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(d)(3) (moving to terminate or limit deposition).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(c) (protective orders).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(d)(3) (moving to terminate or limit deposition).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[830],"sourceAnswerPages":[831,832],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名成人娱乐场所的舞者在联邦地区法院起诉其雇主，指称其营造敌意工作环境并实施违反联邦法的性骚扰。舞者称雇主不断施压要求与她发生性关系，但她一再拒绝。在舞者的证言录取中，雇主律师询问她与雇主之间的性关系史。舞者否认与雇主发生过任何性关系。为使舞者难堪，雇主律师继续询问她一般性的性经历。舞者律师提出异议，指示舞者不要回答，并立即中止证言录取。次日，舞者律师向提起本诉讼的法院申请保护令，阻止雇主询问舞者的一般性性经历。提交申请前，舞者律师没有与雇主律师会商，也没有尝试会商。法院是否可能发布保护令？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为舞者没有回答证言录取中的问题。","B":"不会，因为舞者律师没有尝试与雇主律师会商。","C":"会，因为如果允许继续询问，舞者将遭受不可弥补的损害。","D":"会，因为申请已适当地向诉讼待决法院提交。"},"explanationZh":"保护令\n\n（FRCP 26(c)）\n\n要求\n• 正当理由——保护当事人和其他人免受烦扰、难堪、压迫、不当负担或不当费用；\n• 证明——申请人已与寻求证据开示的一方善意会商或尝试会商，以求在无须法院介入的情况下解决争议。\n\n提交法院\n• 诉讼待决法院，或将进行证言录取的法院。\n\n可能内容\n• 禁止、限制证据开示或为其规定条件；\n• 规定替代性的证据开示方法；\n• 指定可在证据开示期间在场的人员；\n• 要求证据开示材料密封，只有依照法院命令才能开启。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n保护令是法院为保护被寻求证据开示的当事人或其他人免受烦扰、难堪、压迫、不当负担或不当费用而发布的命令。如果申请人：(1)证明发布命令具有正当理由；且(2)证明其已与寻求证据开示的一方善意会商或尝试会商，以便在无须法院介入的情况下解决事项，法院将发布保护令。要求会商的目的，是通过限制法院对证据开示的介入来节省司法时间和资源。\n\n本题中，雇主律师为使舞者难堪而询问其一般性性经历，发布保护令具有正当理由。但是，舞者律师在提出申请前没有尝试与雇主律师会商。因此，舞者未履行会商义务，法院很可能驳回其保护令申请。\n\n（选项A）无论申请人是否回答了证言录取问题，法院均可发布保护令。但本题中，舞者没有善意尝试在无须法院介入的情况下解决事项，所以申请很可能被驳回。\n\n（选项C）取得临时限制令须证明不可弥补的损害，而非取得保护令。临时限制令旨在维持现状，防止申请人遭受即时且不可弥补的损害，直至法院能就问题举行听证会。相反，证据开示保护令旨在限制证据开示范围并防止滥用。\n\n（选项D）保护令申请可以向进行证言录取的法院或诉讼待决法院（本题即如此）提交。但舞者提交该申请前未与雇主会商，因此法院不能批准。\n\n学习目标：如果申请人：(1)证明发布保护令具有正当理由；且(2)证明其已与寻求证据开示的一方善意会商或尝试会商，法院将发布保护令。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(c)（保护令）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(d)(3)（申请终止或限制证言录取）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-275","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":275,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court for negligently causing an accident in which the plaintiff suffered severe injuries. The defendant answered the complaint and denied liability. The plaintiff served a request for admission on the defendant. The plaintiff requested that the defendant admit that the defendant failed to stop at the intersection where the collision occurred. The defendant did not respond to the request. At the final pretrial conference two months later, the plaintiff claims that the defendant's failure to stop at the intersection should be deemed conclusively established at trial. Is the plaintiff correct?","choices":{"A":"No, because the defendant denied liability in his answer to the complaint.","B":"No, because the defendant did not admit that he failed to stop at the intersection.","C":"Yes, because the defendant failed to respond to the request for admission.","D":"Yes, but only if the plaintiff demonstrates by a preponderance of the evidence that the defendant failed to stop at the intersection."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A request for admission seeks to expedite litigation and decrease costs by alleviating the need to prove undisputed matters at trial. In this method of discovery, a party requests that another party admit the truth about matters within the scope of discovery that relate to facts, the application of law to facts, opinions about either, or the genuineness of documents. The party served with the request must then serve a written response* within 30 days that:\n• admits the matter\n• specifically denies the matter in whole or in part\n• explains why the matter cannot be admitted or denied or\n• objects that the matter falls outside the scope of discovery. An admission or a failure to adequately respond within 30 days results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the action unless the admission is later withdrawn or amended. Here, the plaintiff requested that the defendant admit that he failed to stop at the intersection where the collision occurred. The defendant did not admit this fact, but he failed to respond before the final pretrial conference that took place two months after the request was served. As a result, the plaintiff is correct that the defendant's failure to stop at the intersection should be deemed conclusively established at trial (Choice B). *Alternatively, a party served with a request for admission may move for a protective order or ask for an extension of time to respond to the request. (Choice A) The defendant's denial of liability in the answer to the complaint does not serve as a denial to the plaintiff's request for admission. (Choice D) To prevail at trial, the plaintiff must prove each element of negligence by a preponderance of the evidence. But the plaintiff need not satisfy this burden to conclusively establish a fact in a request for admission. The plaintiff simply needs to show that the defendant failed to respond to the request (as seen here). Educational objective: A party's admission or failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after being served with the request results in the matter being conclusively established in the action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36 (requests for admission).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright, Arthur R. Miller & Richard L. Marcus, Federal Practice and Procedure § 2259 (3d ed. 2020) (responses to requests for admissions).","rule":"A party's admission or failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after being served with the request results in the matter being conclusively established in the action.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff sued a defendant in a federal district court for negligently causing an accident in which the plaintiff suffered severe injuries. The defendant answered the complaint and denied liability. The plaintiff served a request for admission on the defendant. The plaintiff requested that the defendant admit that the defendant failed to stop at the intersection where the collision occurred. The defendant did not respond to the request. At the final pretrial conference two months later, the plaintiff claims that the defendant's failure to stop at the intersection should be deemed conclusively established at trial. Is the plaintiff correct?","rule":"A party's admission or failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after being served with the request results in the matter being conclusively established in the action.","analysis":"A request for admission seeks to expedite litigation and decrease costs by alleviating the need to prove undisputed matters at trial. In this method of discovery, a party requests that another party admit the truth about matters within the scope of discovery that relate to facts, the application of law to facts, opinions about either, or the genuineness of documents. The party served with the request must then serve a written response* within 30 days that:\n• admits the matter\n• specifically denies the matter in whole or in part\n• explains why the matter cannot be admitted or denied or\n• objects that the matter falls outside the scope of discovery. An admission or a failure to adequately respond within 30 days results in the matter being admitted and conclusively established in the action unless the admission is later withdrawn or amended. Here, the plaintiff requested that the defendant admit that he failed to stop at the intersection where the collision occurred. The defendant did not admit this fact, but he failed to respond before the final pretrial conference that took place two months after the request was served. As a result, the plaintiff is correct that the defendant's failure to stop at the intersection should be deemed conclusively established at trial (Choice B). *Alternatively, a party served with a request for admission may move for a protective order or ask for an extension of time to respond to the request. (Choice A) The defendant's denial of liability in the answer to the complaint does not serve as a denial to the plaintiff's request for admission. (Choice D) To prevail at trial, the plaintiff must prove each element of negligence by a preponderance of the evidence. But the plaintiff need not satisfy this burden to conclusively establish a fact in a request for admission. The plaintiff simply needs to show that the defendant failed to respond to the request (as seen here). Educational objective: A party's admission or failure to adequately respond to a request for admission within 30 days after being served with the request results in the matter being conclusively established in the action. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36 (requests for admission).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright, Arthur R. Miller & Richard L. Marcus, Federal Practice and Procedure § 2259 (3d ed. 2020) (responses to requests for admissions).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36 (requests for admission).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright, Arthur R. Miller & Richard L. Marcus, Federal Practice and Procedure § 2259 (3d ed. 2020) (responses to requests for admissions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[833],"sourceAnswerPages":[834,835],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院起诉一名被告，主张被告因过失造成事故，使原告遭受严重伤害。被告对起诉状作出答辩并否认责任。原告向被告送达承认请求，要求被告承认自己没有在碰撞发生的交叉路口停车。被告没有回应请求。两个月后的最终审前会议上，原告主张被告未在该路口停车这一事实应在审判中视为确定无疑。原告的主张是否正确？","choicesZh":{"A":"不正确，因为被告已在起诉状答辩中否认责任。","B":"不正确，因为被告没有承认其未在交叉路口停车。","C":"正确，因为被告没有回应承认请求。","D":"正确，但仅限于原告以优势证据证明被告未在交叉路口停车。"},"explanationZh":"承认请求旨在免除审判中证明无争议事项的必要，从而加快诉讼并降低费用。在这种证据开示方式中，一方请求另一方承认证据开示范围内事项的真实性；这些事项可涉及事实、法律对事实的适用、对二者的意见，或文件的真实性。收到请求的一方必须在30天内送达书面答复，*并：\n• 承认该事项；\n• 具体地全部或部分否认该事项；\n• 解释为何无法承认或否认该事项；或者\n• 以该事项超出证据开示范围为由提出异议。\n\n作出承认，或未在30天内充分回应，会导致该事项在诉讼中被承认并确定无疑，除非该承认后来被撤回或修正。\n\n本题中，原告要求被告承认其未在碰撞发生的交叉路口停车。被告虽未承认该事实，但直到请求送达两个月后的最终审前会议仍未回应。因此，原告正确地认为，被告未在交叉路口停车这一事实应在审判中视为确定无疑（选项B）。\n\n*收到承认请求的一方也可以申请保护令，或请求延长答复期限。\n\n（选项A）被告在起诉状答辩中否认责任，并不构成对原告承认请求的否认。\n\n（选项D）要在审判中胜诉，原告须以优势证据证明过失的每个要件。但原告无须达到该证明标准，即可通过承认请求确定某项事实。原告只须证明被告未回应请求（本题即如此）。\n\n学习目标：一方作出承认，或在承认请求送达后30天内未充分回应，会使相关事项在诉讼中被确定无疑。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 36（承认请求）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright, Arthur R. Miller & Richard L. Marcus, Federal Practice and Procedure § 2259 (3d ed. 2020)（承认请求的答复）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-075"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-276","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":276,"topic":"Venue, Removal & Transfer","question":"A purchaser of a luxury car sued the car's manufacturer in a state court in State A. The purchaser claimed that the steering wheel was defective and caused a car accident resulting in over $300,000 in damages. Before the manufacturer responded, the purchaser filed a notice of dismissal. Two weeks later, the purchaser filed a complaint in a federal court in State B against the manufacturer for the same claim. The manufacturer filed an answer that included a motion to dismiss for lack of venue. The purchaser did not oppose this motion, but she did file a stipulation of dismissal signed by both parties. One month later, the purchaser filed a complaint in a federal court in State C alleging the same claim asserted in the prior actions. The manufacturer moved to dismiss the suit, arguing that the purchaser was precluded from bringing this suit a third time. Will the court likely grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice only when specified in a court order.","B":"No, because the second action was dismissed by a stipulation signed by both parties.","C":"Yes, because the purchaser failed to obtain a court order to dismiss the second action.","D":"Yes, because the second dismissal occurred in federal court."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss a suit for any reason. A voluntary dismissal is generally without prejudice, which allows the plaintiff to sue the defendant again on the same claim. But a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice and operates as an adjudication on the merits when specified in a court order, notice, or stipulation OR the two-dismissal rule applies (Choice A). This rule applies when:\n• the plaintiff's first action was voluntarily dismissed without a court order in state or federal court and\n\n• the plaintiff brought a second action on the same claim in federal court and filed a notice of voluntary dismissal.* Here, the purchaser dismissed the first action by a notice of dismissal and then dismissed the second action asserting the same claim by a stipulation in federal court. However, the second dismissal did not operate as an adjudication on the merits because it was dismissed by the parties' signed stipulation—not a notice of voluntary dismissal. Therefore, the two-dismissal rule does not apply, and the court will likely deny the manufacturer's motion to dismiss the purchaser's third action. *The two-dismissal rule's purpose is to prevent the plaintiff from harassing the defendant by perpetually filing and dismissing the same claim. Since a court order or stipulation does not pose this risk, the rule does not apply when the second action is dismissed by these methods. (Choice C) A voluntary dismissal without a court order is permitted when the plaintiff files (1) a notice of dismissal before the defendant serves an answer or a summary judgment motion or (2) a stipulation of dismissal that has been signed by all parties (as seen here). (Choice D) The two-dismissal rule requires that the second dismissal occur in federal court and be pursuant to a notice of voluntary dismissal. But here, the second dismissal was pursuant to the parties' signed stipulation, so this rule does not apply. Educational objective: Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff (1) voluntarily dismissed an action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) filed a notice of voluntary dismissal in a second action on the same claim in federal court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).\n\n• 24 Am. Jur. 2d Dismissal, Discontinuance, and Nonsuit § 95 (2019) (explaining that the two-dismissal rule does not apply when the second action was dismissed by the parties' stipulation).","rule":"Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff (1) voluntarily dismissed an action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) filed a notice of voluntary dismissal in a second action on the same claim in federal court.","irac":{"issue":"A purchaser of a luxury car sued the car's manufacturer in a state court in State A. The purchaser claimed that the steering wheel was defective and caused a car accident resulting in over $300,000 in damages. Before the manufacturer responded, the purchaser filed a notice of dismissal. Two weeks later, the purchaser filed a complaint in a federal court in State B against the manufacturer for the same claim. The manufacturer filed an answer that included a motion to dismiss for lack of venue. The purchaser did not oppose this motion, but she did file a stipulation of dismissal signed by both parties. One month later, the purchaser filed a complaint in a federal court in State C alleging the same claim asserted in the prior actions. The manufacturer moved to dismiss the suit, arguing that the purchaser was precluded from bringing this suit a third time. Will the court likely grant the manufacturer's motion to dismiss?","rule":"Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff (1) voluntarily dismissed an action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) filed a notice of voluntary dismissal in a second action on the same claim in federal court.","analysis":"A plaintiff may voluntarily dismiss a suit for any reason. A voluntary dismissal is generally without prejudice, which allows the plaintiff to sue the defendant again on the same claim. But a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice and operates as an adjudication on the merits when specified in a court order, notice, or stipulation OR the two-dismissal rule applies (Choice A). This rule applies when:\n• the plaintiff's first action was voluntarily dismissed without a court order in state or federal court and\n\n• the plaintiff brought a second action on the same claim in federal court and filed a notice of voluntary dismissal.* Here, the purchaser dismissed the first action by a notice of dismissal and then dismissed the second action asserting the same claim by a stipulation in federal court. However, the second dismissal did not operate as an adjudication on the merits because it was dismissed by the parties' signed stipulation—not a notice of voluntary dismissal. Therefore, the two-dismissal rule does not apply, and the court will likely deny the manufacturer's motion to dismiss the purchaser's third action. *The two-dismissal rule's purpose is to prevent the plaintiff from harassing the defendant by perpetually filing and dismissing the same claim. Since a court order or stipulation does not pose this risk, the rule does not apply when the second action is dismissed by these methods. (Choice C) A voluntary dismissal without a court order is permitted when the plaintiff files (1) a notice of dismissal before the defendant serves an answer or a summary judgment motion or (2) a stipulation of dismissal that has been signed by all parties (as seen here). (Choice D) The two-dismissal rule requires that the second dismissal occur in federal court and be pursuant to a notice of voluntary dismissal. But here, the second dismissal was pursuant to the parties' signed stipulation, so this rule does not apply. Educational objective: Under the two-dismissal rule, a voluntary dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits when the plaintiff (1) voluntarily dismissed an action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) filed a notice of voluntary dismissal in a second action on the same claim in federal court. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).\n\n• 24 Am. Jur. 2d Dismissal, Discontinuance, and Nonsuit § 95 (2019) (explaining that the two-dismissal rule does not apply when the second action was dismissed by the parties' stipulation).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).\n\n• 24 Am. Jur. 2d Dismissal, Discontinuance, and Nonsuit § 95 (2019) (explaining that the two-dismissal rule does not apply when the second action was dismissed by the parties' stipulation).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[836],"sourceAnswerPages":[837,838],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名豪华汽车购买者在A州州法院起诉汽车制造商。购买者主张方向盘存在缺陷并引发车祸，造成超过30万美元损害。制造商作出回应前，购买者提交撤诉通知。两周后，购买者在B州联邦法院就同一请求起诉制造商。制造商提交答辩状，其中包含一项以审判地不适当为由的驳回申请。购买者没有反对该申请，但提交了由双方签署的撤诉约定。一个月后，购买者在C州联邦法院提交起诉状，提出与先前诉讼相同的请求。制造商申请驳回，主张购买者因第三次提起本诉讼而受到排除。法院是否可能批准制造商的驳回申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为只有法院命令明确规定时，自愿撤诉才是有偏见撤诉。","B":"不会，因为第二项诉讼是依双方签署的约定撤诉。","C":"会，因为购买者未取得法院命令即撤回第二项诉讼。","D":"会，因为第二次撤诉发生在联邦法院。"},"explanationZh":"原告可以基于任何理由自愿撤诉。自愿撤诉通常是无偏见撤诉，因此原告可以就同一请求再次起诉被告。但是，如果法院命令、撤诉通知或撤诉约定明确规定，或者两次撤诉规则适用，自愿撤诉就属于有偏见撤诉，并具有实体裁判的效力（选项A）。两次撤诉规则在以下情形适用：\n• 原告在州法院或联邦法院无须法院命令而自愿撤回第一项诉讼；并且\n• 原告在联邦法院基于同一请求提起第二项诉讼，并提交自愿撤诉通知。*\n\n本题中，购买者通过撤诉通知撤回第一项诉讼，之后在联邦法院依双方签署的约定撤回提出同一请求的第二项诉讼。但是，第二次撤诉不具有实体裁判效力，因为它是依双方签署的撤诉约定，而不是依自愿撤诉通知作出。因此，两次撤诉规则不适用，法院很可能驳回制造商针对购买者第三项诉讼提出的驳回申请。\n\n*两次撤诉规则旨在防止原告不断提出并撤回同一请求来骚扰被告。法院命令或约定不存在这种风险，所以第二项诉讼通过这些方式撤回时，该规则不适用。\n\n（选项C）在下列情况下，可以无须法院命令自愿撤诉：(1)原告在被告送达答辩状或简易判决动议前提交撤诉通知；或(2)原告提交由所有当事人签署的撤诉约定（本题即如此）。\n\n（选项D）两次撤诉规则要求第二次撤诉发生在联邦法院，并且依据自愿撤诉通知。本题第二次撤诉依据双方签署的约定，所以规则不适用。\n\n学习目标：根据两次撤诉规则，在以下情况下，自愿撤诉具有实体裁判效力：(1)原告在州法院或联邦法院无须法院命令自愿撤回一项诉讼；且(2)原告在联邦法院就同一请求提起第二项诉讼后，提交自愿撤诉通知。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a)（自愿撤诉）。\n• 24 Am. Jur. 2d Dismissal, Discontinuance, and Nonsuit § 95 (2019)（说明第二项诉讼依双方约定撤回时，两次撤诉规则不适用）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-074"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-277","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":277,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A farmer sued a seed supplier in federal district court for allegedly selling the farmer's patented seeds without the farmer's permission. The supplier filed and served an answer denying liability, and the court entered a scheduling order with discovery deadlines. Over the next year, the supplier repeatedly tried to contact the farmer through phone calls, regular mail, and email. The supplier also sent several discovery requests to the farmer. The farmer never responded to the supplier, made no discovery requests, and failed to adhere to the discovery deadlines. The supplier wants to end this litigation and prevent the farmer from suing her on the same claim in the future. Which action will best achieve the supplier's goals?","choices":{"A":"Move for a voluntary dismissal with prejudice.","B":"Move for a voluntary dismissal without prejudice.","C":"Move for an involuntary dismissal with prejudice.","D":"Move for an involuntary dismissal without prejudice."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Voluntary v. Involuntary dismissal\n\n(FRCP 41)\n\nProcess Effect\n\nVoluntary π may dismiss suit without court Dismissal without prejudice dismissal order when: unless:\n\n• notice of dismissal filed • π previously dismissed before ∆ serves answer or federal or state action summary judgment motion or based on same claim or\n• all parties sign stipulation of • notice or stipulation dismissal states otherwise π may request court order to dismiss suit unless:\n\n• ∆'s counterclaim cannot be independently adjudicated Involuntary ∆ may move to dismiss suit if π failed Dismissal with prejudice unless: dismissal to:\n• court order states\n• prosecute own action or otherwise or\n• comply with rules or court • based on lack of order jurisdiction, improper ∆ may move to dismiss suit via: venue, or failure to join required party\n• pre-answer motion\n• judgment on the pleadings or\n• summary judgment FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; π = plaintiff; ∆ = defendant.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 41, a suit can be dismissed in two ways:\n• Voluntary dismissal – when the plaintiff moves to dismiss the suit\n• Involuntary dismissal – when the defendant moves to dismiss the suit A defendant can move for an involuntary dismissal under FRCP 41* if the plaintiff failed to prosecute the action or comply with a rule or court order—just as the farmer (plaintiff) did here. Therefore, the supplier (defendant) can move for an involuntary dismissal (Choices A & B).\n\nAn involuntary dismissal under FRCP 41 operates as an adjudication on the merits unless the court orders otherwise. This means that the plaintiff's action is dismissed with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the defendant on the same claim in the future. The supplier should therefore move for an involuntary dismissal with prejudice to end the suit and prevent the farmer from suing her again on the same claim (Choice D). *A defendant may also move for an involuntary dismissal through other rules (eg, pre-answer motion to dismiss under FRCP 12). An involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party is without prejudice. Educational objective: A defendant can move for an involuntary dismissal if the plaintiff failed to prosecute the case or comply with a rule or court order. If the motion is granted, the case will be dismissed with prejudice (unless the court states otherwise) to prevent the plaintiff from suing the defendant on the same claim in the future. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b) (involuntary dismissal).","rule":"A defendant can move for an involuntary dismissal if the plaintiff failed to prosecute the case or comply with a rule or court order. If the motion is granted, the case will be dismissed with prejudice (unless the court states otherwise) to prevent the plaintiff from suing the defendant on the same claim in the future.","irac":{"issue":"A farmer sued a seed supplier in federal district court for allegedly selling the farmer's patented seeds without the farmer's permission. The supplier filed and served an answer denying liability, and the court entered a scheduling order with discovery deadlines. Over the next year, the supplier repeatedly tried to contact the farmer through phone calls, regular mail, and email. The supplier also sent several discovery requests to the farmer. The farmer never responded to the supplier, made no discovery requests, and failed to adhere to the discovery deadlines. The supplier wants to end this litigation and prevent the farmer from suing her on the same claim in the future. Which action will best achieve the supplier's goals?","rule":"A defendant can move for an involuntary dismissal if the plaintiff failed to prosecute the case or comply with a rule or court order. If the motion is granted, the case will be dismissed with prejudice (unless the court states otherwise) to prevent the plaintiff from suing the defendant on the same claim in the future.","analysis":"Voluntary v. Involuntary dismissal\n\n(FRCP 41)\n\nProcess Effect\n\nVoluntary π may dismiss suit without court Dismissal without prejudice dismissal order when: unless:\n\n• notice of dismissal filed • π previously dismissed before ∆ serves answer or federal or state action summary judgment motion or based on same claim or\n• all parties sign stipulation of • notice or stipulation dismissal states otherwise π may request court order to dismiss suit unless:\n\n• ∆'s counterclaim cannot be independently adjudicated Involuntary ∆ may move to dismiss suit if π failed Dismissal with prejudice unless: dismissal to:\n• court order states\n• prosecute own action or otherwise or\n• comply with rules or court • based on lack of order jurisdiction, improper ∆ may move to dismiss suit via: venue, or failure to join required party\n• pre-answer motion\n• judgment on the pleadings or\n• summary judgment FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure; π = plaintiff; ∆ = defendant.\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 41, a suit can be dismissed in two ways:\n• Voluntary dismissal – when the plaintiff moves to dismiss the suit\n• Involuntary dismissal – when the defendant moves to dismiss the suit A defendant can move for an involuntary dismissal under FRCP 41* if the plaintiff failed to prosecute the action or comply with a rule or court order—just as the farmer (plaintiff) did here. Therefore, the supplier (defendant) can move for an involuntary dismissal (Choices A & B).\n\nAn involuntary dismissal under FRCP 41 operates as an adjudication on the merits unless the court orders otherwise. This means that the plaintiff's action is dismissed with prejudice, which precludes the plaintiff from suing the defendant on the same claim in the future. The supplier should therefore move for an involuntary dismissal with prejudice to end the suit and prevent the farmer from suing her again on the same claim (Choice D). *A defendant may also move for an involuntary dismissal through other rules (eg, pre-answer motion to dismiss under FRCP 12). An involuntary dismissal based on lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join a required party is without prejudice. Educational objective: A defendant can move for an involuntary dismissal if the plaintiff failed to prosecute the case or comply with a rule or court order. If the motion is granted, the case will be dismissed with prejudice (unless the court states otherwise) to prevent the plaintiff from suing the defendant on the same claim in the future. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b) (involuntary dismissal).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b) (involuntary dismissal).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[839],"sourceAnswerPages":[840,841],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名农民在联邦地区法院起诉种子供应商，指称其未经许可出售农民享有专利的种子。供应商提交并送达答辩状，否认责任；法院发布了包含证据开示期限的日程命令。此后一年中，供应商多次试图通过电话、普通邮件和电子邮件联系农民，也向其发送了若干证据开示请求。农民从未回应供应商，没有提出任何证据开示请求，也没有遵守证据开示期限。供应商希望终结诉讼，并防止农民将来就同一请求再次起诉她。采取哪项行动最能实现供应商的目标？","choicesZh":{"A":"申请有偏见的自愿撤诉。","B":"申请无偏见的自愿撤诉。","C":"申请有偏见的非自愿驳回。","D":"申请无偏见的非自愿驳回。"},"explanationZh":"自愿撤诉与非自愿驳回\n\n（FRCP 41）\n\n程序｜效力\n\n自愿撤诉\n在下列情形下，原告可以无须法院命令撤诉：\n• 在被告送达答辩状或简易判决动议前提交撤诉通知；或\n• 所有当事人签署撤诉约定。\n除以下情形外，撤诉无偏见：\n• 原告此前已撤回基于同一请求的联邦或州法院诉讼；或\n• 撤诉通知或约定另有说明。\n原告可以请求法院命令撤诉，但以下情形除外：\n• 被告反请求无法独立裁判。\n\n非自愿驳回\n原告存在下列行为时，被告可以申请驳回诉讼：\n• 未推进其诉讼；或\n• 不遵守规则或法院命令。\n被告也可通过以下方式申请驳回：\n• 答辩前动议；\n• 依诉状判决；或\n• 简易判决。\n除以下情形外，驳回有偏见：\n• 法院命令另有说明；或\n• 基于缺乏管辖权、审判地不当或未加入必要当事人。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》；π＝原告；Δ＝被告。\n\n根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）41，诉讼可通过两种方式结束：\n• 自愿撤诉——由原告申请撤诉；\n• 非自愿驳回——由被告申请驳回。\n\n如果原告未推进诉讼，或不遵守规则或法院命令——如本题农民（原告）所为——被告可以依据FRCP 41申请非自愿驳回。因而，供应商（被告）可以申请非自愿驳回（选项A和B）。\n\n除非法院另有命令，FRCP 41下的非自愿驳回具有实体裁判效力。这意味着原告的诉讼以有偏见方式驳回，禁止原告将来就同一请求再次起诉被告。因此，为终结诉讼并防止农民再次就同一请求起诉，供应商应申请有偏见的非自愿驳回（选项D）。\n\n*被告也可依其他规则申请非自愿驳回，例如依据FRCP 12提出答辩前驳回动议。基于缺乏管辖权、审判地不当或未加入必要当事人的非自愿驳回是无偏见的。\n\n学习目标：如果原告未推进案件，或不遵守规则或法院命令，被告可以申请非自愿驳回。申请获准时，除非法院另有说明，案件将以有偏见方式驳回，从而阻止原告将来就同一请求再次起诉被告。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(b)（非自愿驳回）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-278","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":278,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A woman and her wife believed that a company providing credit scores intentionally lowered their scores because they are a same-sex couple. The couple's lawyer discovered 10 other individuals who had their credit scores lowered by the same company. These individuals claim that their scores were lowered based on their race. The lawyer filed suit in federal court on behalf of the couple and the 10 other individuals against the company for violating a federal statute. One provision of the statute forbids racial discrimination and another provision prohibits sexual-orientation discrimination. The couple are the only plaintiffs named in the complaint. The lawyer moved to certify the case as a class action. Which of the following would be an invalid argument for the company to challenge class action certification?","choices":{"A":"The class does not share common questions of law or fact.","B":"The class is not numerous.","C":"The company does not discriminate against any class when calculating credit scores.","D":"The named couple's claim is not typical of the class."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Certification requirements for class action\n\n(FRCP 23)\n\nPrerequisites • Numerosity: class is so numerous that joinder of all members is impracticable\n• Commonality: class shares common questions of law or fact\n• Typicality: claims or defenses of named parties are typical of class AND\n• Adequacy: named parties will fairly & adequately protect class's interests Case suitable for • Separate actions would create risk of (1) inconsistent class action decisions regarding parties or (2) impairment of absent class members' interests\n• Injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because opposing party's actions generally applied to whole class OR\n• Common questions of law or fact predominate over individual questions & class action is superior method to resolve dispute FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\nA class action is a suit filed by one or more named plaintiffs as class representatives on behalf of similarly harmed unnamed plaintiffs.* All actions filed by a purported class representative require certification by the court at the earliest practical time. Plaintiffs seeking class action certification must establish—and the defendant can challenge certification on—all of the following prerequisites:\n• Numerosity – the class is so numerous that joining all the members as named plaintiffs is impracticable (usually met when there are over 40 members) (Choice B)\n\n• Commonality – the class shares common questions of law or fact (Choice A)\n\n• Typicality – the named plaintiffs' claims are typical of the claims of the class (Choice D)\n\n• Adequacy – the named plaintiffs will fairly and adequately protect the interests of the class\n\nHowever, the argument that the company does not discriminate against any class when calculating credit scores goes toward the merits of the case—not class action\n\ncertification. Since the court will not consider the merits of the underlying action when determining whether to certify a class, this is an invalid argument to challenge certification. *A class action may also be asserted against named defendants on behalf of similarly situated unnamed defendants, but this procedure is extremely rare. Educational objective: A party seeking class action certification must establish four prerequisites: numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy. A court deciding whether to certify a class action will not consider the merits of the underlying case. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a) (prerequisites for class action certification).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1476 (2019) (explaining that a court will not examine a case's merits when deciding class action certification).","rule":"A party seeking class action certification must establish four prerequisites: numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy. A court deciding whether to certify a class action will not consider the merits of the underlying case.","irac":{"issue":"A woman and her wife believed that a company providing credit scores intentionally lowered their scores because they are a same-sex couple. The couple's lawyer discovered 10 other individuals who had their credit scores lowered by the same company. These individuals claim that their scores were lowered based on their race. The lawyer filed suit in federal court on behalf of the couple and the 10 other individuals against the company for violating a federal statute. One provision of the statute forbids racial discrimination and another provision prohibits sexual-orientation discrimination. The couple are the only plaintiffs named in the complaint. The lawyer moved to certify the case as a class action. Which of the following would be an invalid argument for the company to challenge class action certification?","rule":"A party seeking class action certification must establish four prerequisites: numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy. A court deciding whether to certify a class action will not consider the merits of the underlying case.","analysis":"Certification requirements for class action\n\n(FRCP 23)\n\nPrerequisites • Numerosity: class is so numerous that joinder of all members is impracticable\n• Commonality: class shares common questions of law or fact\n• Typicality: claims or defenses of named parties are typical of class AND\n• Adequacy: named parties will fairly & adequately protect class's interests Case suitable for • Separate actions would create risk of (1) inconsistent class action decisions regarding parties or (2) impairment of absent class members' interests\n• Injunctive or declaratory relief is appropriate because opposing party's actions generally applied to whole class OR\n• Common questions of law or fact predominate over individual questions & class action is superior method to resolve dispute FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\nA class action is a suit filed by one or more named plaintiffs as class representatives on behalf of similarly harmed unnamed plaintiffs.* All actions filed by a purported class representative require certification by the court at the earliest practical time. Plaintiffs seeking class action certification must establish—and the defendant can challenge certification on—all of the following prerequisites:\n• Numerosity – the class is so numerous that joining all the members as named plaintiffs is impracticable (usually met when there are over 40 members) (Choice B)\n\n• Commonality – the class shares common questions of law or fact (Choice A)\n\n• Typicality – the named plaintiffs' claims are typical of the claims of the class (Choice D)\n\n• Adequacy – the named plaintiffs will fairly and adequately protect the interests of the class\n\nHowever, the argument that the company does not discriminate against any class when calculating credit scores goes toward the merits of the case—not class action\n\ncertification. Since the court will not consider the merits of the underlying action when determining whether to certify a class, this is an invalid argument to challenge certification. *A class action may also be asserted against named defendants on behalf of similarly situated unnamed defendants, but this procedure is extremely rare. Educational objective: A party seeking class action certification must establish four prerequisites: numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy. A court deciding whether to certify a class action will not consider the merits of the underlying case. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a) (prerequisites for class action certification).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1476 (2019) (explaining that a court will not examine a case's merits when deciding class action certification).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a) (prerequisites for class action certification).\n\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1476 (2019) (explaining that a court will not examine a case's merits when deciding class action certification).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[842],"sourceAnswerPages":[843,844],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子与其妻子认为，一家提供信用评分的公司因二人是同性伴侣而故意降低其评分。二人的律师发现另有10人也被同一家公司降低信用评分，这些人主张评分因其种族而被降低。律师代表这对伴侣和另10人，在联邦法院起诉该公司违反某项联邦制定法。该法一项条款禁止种族歧视，另一项条款禁止性取向歧视。起诉状中仅将这对伴侣列为具名原告。律师申请批准案件为集团诉讼。公司以下列哪项理由质疑集团诉讼批准将属无效？","choicesZh":{"A":"集团不具有共同的法律或事实问题。","B":"集团人数不够众多。","C":"公司计算信用评分时不歧视任何群体。","D":"具名伴侣的请求不是集团请求的典型。"},"explanationZh":"集团诉讼的批准要求\n\n（FRCP 23）\n\n先决条件\n• 人数众多性：集团人数众多，以至于把所有成员合并诉讼并不切实可行；\n• 共同性：集团具有共同的法律或事实问题；\n• 典型性：具名当事人的请求或抗辩具有集团典型性；并且\n• 充分代表性：具名当事人将公平且充分地保护集团利益。\n\n案件适合集团诉讼\n• 分别诉讼会产生以下风险：(1)对各方作出不一致裁判，或(2)损害缺席集团成员利益；\n• 由于对方行为普遍适用于整个集团，禁令或宣告救济适当；或者\n• 共同法律或事实问题优于个别问题，而且集团诉讼是解决争议的更优方法。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n集团诉讼是由一名或多名具名原告作为集团代表，代表受到相似损害的未具名原告提起的诉讼。* 所有由拟议集团代表提起的诉讼，都必须由法院在最早可行时间予以批准。寻求集团诉讼批准的原告必须证明下列全部先决条件；被告也可针对这些条件提出质疑：\n• 人数众多性——集团人数众多，以至于把所有成员列为具名原告并不切实可行（通常在超过40名成员时得到满足）（选项B）；\n• 共同性——集团具有共同的法律或事实问题（选项A）；\n• 典型性——具名原告的请求具有集团请求的典型性（选项D）；\n• 充分代表性——具名原告将公平且充分地保护集团利益。\n\n然而，公司在计算信用评分时不歧视任何群体这一论点，涉及案件实体问题，而非集团诉讼批准。法院决定是否批准集团诉讼时，不会审查基础诉讼的实体问题，因此该论点不能有效质疑批准。\n\n*集团诉讼也可以针对具名被告提出，由其代表处境相似的未具名被告，但这种程序极为少见。\n\n学习目标：寻求集团诉讼批准的一方必须证明四项先决条件：人数众多性、共同性、典型性和充分代表性。法院决定是否批准集团诉讼时，不会审查基础案件的实体问题。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(a)（集团诉讼批准的先决条件）。\n• 32A Am. Jur. 2d Federal Courts § 1476 (2019)（说明法院决定集团诉讼批准时不会审查案件实体问题）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-279","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":279,"topic":"Jurisdiction","question":"A consumer filed an action against a bank in state court alleging improper deduction of fees from a checking account. Before the bank filed its answer, the consumer voluntarily dismissed the action without prejudice. One month later, the consumer filed a suit alleging the same claims against the bank in federal court. The bank moved to dismiss the action for lack of personal jurisdiction. Instead of challenging the bank's assertion, the consumer dismissed the action by filing a notice of dismissal. Two weeks later, the consumer sued the bank in a federal court located in another state on the same claims asserted in the previous two actions. The bank moved to dismiss this suit. All the actions were filed within the applicable statute of limitations. Should the court grant the bank's motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the consumer filed all the actions within the applicable statute of limitations.","B":"No, because the first dismissal occurred in state court.","C":"Yes, because the consumer failed to obtain a court order before dismissing the second suit.","D":"Yes, because the second dismissal operated as an adjudication on the merits."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 41 allows a plaintiff to voluntarily dismiss a suit for any reason. A voluntary dismissal without a court order is permitted when (1) the parties signed a stipulation of dismissal or (2) the plaintiff unilaterally filed a notice of dismissal before the defendant served an answer or a summary judgment motion. Therefore, the consumer did not need a court order to dismiss the first or second action since he filed a notice of dismissal before the bank filed an answer or moved for summary judgment (Choice C). Most voluntarily dismissals are without prejudice, thereby allowing the plaintiff to sue the defendant on the same claim in the future. However, a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice and operates as an adjudication on the merits when specified in a court order, notice, or stipulation of dismissal OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff:\n• voluntarily dismissed the first action in federal or state court without a court order and\n• voluntarily dismissed the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. Here, the consumer voluntarily dismissed the first action in state court. He then dismissed the second action in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. Since these actions asserted the same claims, the second dismissal operated as an adjudication on the merits. Therefore, the court will likely grant the bank's motion to dismiss the third action (Choice B). (Choice A) Although the consumer filed all the actions within the statute of limitations, the two-dismissal rule precludes the third action. Educational objective: A voluntary dismissal is with prejudice when stated in a court order, notice, or stipulation OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).","rule":"A voluntary dismissal is with prejudice when stated in a court order, notice, or stipulation OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal.","irac":{"issue":"A consumer filed an action against a bank in state court alleging improper deduction of fees from a checking account. Before the bank filed its answer, the consumer voluntarily dismissed the action without prejudice. One month later, the consumer filed a suit alleging the same claims against the bank in federal court. The bank moved to dismiss the action for lack of personal jurisdiction. Instead of challenging the bank's assertion, the consumer dismissed the action by filing a notice of dismissal. Two weeks later, the consumer sued the bank in a federal court located in another state on the same claims asserted in the previous two actions. The bank moved to dismiss this suit. All the actions were filed within the applicable statute of limitations. Should the court grant the bank's motion to dismiss?","rule":"A voluntary dismissal is with prejudice when stated in a court order, notice, or stipulation OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal.","analysis":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 41 allows a plaintiff to voluntarily dismiss a suit for any reason. A voluntary dismissal without a court order is permitted when (1) the parties signed a stipulation of dismissal or (2) the plaintiff unilaterally filed a notice of dismissal before the defendant served an answer or a summary judgment motion. Therefore, the consumer did not need a court order to dismiss the first or second action since he filed a notice of dismissal before the bank filed an answer or moved for summary judgment (Choice C). Most voluntarily dismissals are without prejudice, thereby allowing the plaintiff to sue the defendant on the same claim in the future. However, a voluntary dismissal is with prejudice and operates as an adjudication on the merits when specified in a court order, notice, or stipulation of dismissal OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff:\n• voluntarily dismissed the first action in federal or state court without a court order and\n• voluntarily dismissed the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. Here, the consumer voluntarily dismissed the first action in state court. He then dismissed the second action in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. Since these actions asserted the same claims, the second dismissal operated as an adjudication on the merits. Therefore, the court will likely grant the bank's motion to dismiss the third action (Choice B). (Choice A) Although the consumer filed all the actions within the statute of limitations, the two-dismissal rule precludes the third action. Educational objective: A voluntary dismissal is with prejudice when stated in a court order, notice, or stipulation OR the two-dismissal rule applies. This rule applies when the plaintiff voluntarily dismissed (1) the first action in federal or state court without a court order and (2) the second action based on the same claim in federal court by filing a notice of dismissal. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a) (voluntary dismissals).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[845],"sourceAnswerPages":[846],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名消费者在州法院起诉一家银行，主张银行不当从其支票账户扣除费用。银行提交答辩状前，消费者自愿无偏见撤回诉讼。一个月后，消费者在联邦法院就相同请求起诉银行。银行以缺乏对人管辖权为由申请驳回。消费者没有反驳银行的主张，而是提交撤诉通知撤回诉讼。两周后，消费者在另一州的一家联邦法院就前两项诉讼中的相同请求再次起诉银行。银行申请驳回本诉讼。所有诉讼均在适用诉讼时效内提起。法院是否应批准银行的驳回申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为消费者在适用诉讼时效内提起了所有诉讼。","B":"不应，因为第一次撤诉发生在州法院。","C":"应当，因为消费者在撤回第二项诉讼前未取得法院命令。","D":"应当，因为第二次撤诉具有实体裁判效力。"},"explanationZh":"《联邦民事诉讼规则》41允许原告基于任何理由自愿撤诉。在下列情况下，可以无须法院命令自愿撤诉：(1)各方签署撤诉约定；或(2)原告在被告送达答辩状或简易判决动议前，单方提交撤诉通知。因此，消费者在银行提交答辩状或申请简易判决前提交撤诉通知，无须法院命令即可撤回第一项或第二项诉讼（选项C）。\n\n大多数自愿撤诉是无偏见的，从而允许原告将来就同一请求再次起诉被告。但是，如果法院命令、撤诉通知或撤诉约定明确规定，或者两次撤诉规则适用，自愿撤诉将属于有偏见撤诉并具有实体裁判效力。两次撤诉规则在原告存在以下行为时适用：\n• 无须法院命令，在联邦或州法院自愿撤回第一项诉讼；并且\n• 在联邦法院通过提交撤诉通知，自愿撤回基于同一请求的第二项诉讼。\n\n本题中，消费者在州法院自愿撤回第一项诉讼，之后在联邦法院提交撤诉通知撤回第二项诉讼。两项诉讼提出相同请求，因此第二次撤诉具有实体裁判效力。所以，法院很可能批准银行针对第三项诉讼提出的驳回申请（选项B）。\n\n（选项A）尽管消费者在诉讼时效内提起所有诉讼，两次撤诉规则仍排除第三项诉讼。\n\n学习目标：如果法院命令、撤诉通知或撤诉约定如此规定，或者两次撤诉规则适用，自愿撤诉就是有偏见撤诉。两次撤诉规则在以下情形适用：原告无须法院命令自愿撤回(1)联邦或州法院中的第一项诉讼，以及(2)在联邦法院通过提交撤诉通知，撤回基于同一请求的第二项诉讼。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a)（自愿撤诉）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-074"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-280","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":280,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A police officer pulled over a speeding driver and administered a breathalyzer test. The driver failed this test, and the officer peacefully took him into custody. These events were recorded on the officer's dashboard and body cameras. Six months later, the driver sued the officer in a federal court, claiming that she violated a federal civil rights statute that prohibits police officers from using excessive force. The driver claimed that the officer slammed him against the car and repeatedly punched him in the face while making the arrest. The officer vehemently believes that the driver has no evidence to support his contention and is only pursuing the suit to harass her. The officer filed an answer denying the driver's allegations and served the driver with a motion for sanctions to recover attorney's fees incurred in filing the answer. Immediately thereafter, the officer filed the motion for sanctions with the court. Is the court likely to grant the officer's motion for sanctions?","choices":{"A":"No, because the judge did not issue an order to show cause.","B":"No, because the officer did not wait 21 days before filing the motion with the court.","C":"Yes, because the driver has no evidence to support the facts asserted in his complaint.","D":"Yes, because the officer believes that the driver filed the suit to harass her."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Common FRCP 11 sanctions\n\nMonetary* • Order fine payable to court\n• Order payment of opposing party's attorney's fees & other expenses Nonmonetary • Strike pleading, motion, or other document\n• Admonish, reprimand, or censure offending party, attorney, or law firm\n• Require participation in training/seminar\n• Refer issue to disciplinary authorities (eg, state legal ethics committee) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed on represented party for violation of FRCP 11(b)(2).\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 11 requires that every pleading, motion, or other document filed with the court be signed by an attorney or an unrepresented party. This signature certifies the following to the court: 1. The document is presented for a proper purpose—not to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.\n\n2. All claims, defenses, and legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law.\n\n3. All factual assertions have or will have evidentiary support.\n\n4. All factual denials are warranted by evidence or are reasonably based on a belief or a lack of information.\n\nHere, the officer believes that the driver filed the complaint to harass her and has no evidence to support the facts asserted in his complaint—both violations of FRCP 11 (Choices C & D). These violations can subject a law firm (default), attorney, or party to sanctions, including a court order to pay the opposing party's attorney's fees. Sanction proceedings can be initiated:\n• on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte) – which requires the judge to issue an order to show cause to the alleged violator or\n\n• by a party's motion – which requires the party to serve the motion on the alleged violator but refrain from filing it for 21 days after serving it to allow any violation to be corrected (ie, safe-harbor rule).\n\nHere, the officer served the driver with a motion for sanctions to recover attorney's fees incurred in filing the answer. However, the officer filed the motion with the court immediately thereafter instead of waiting 21 days before filing it. Therefore, the court will likely deny the officer's motion for sanctions. (Choice A) The judge need not issue an order to show cause when sanction proceedings are initiated by a party's motion (as seen here). Educational objective: A party cannot file a motion for sanctions until 21 days after serving that motion on the alleged violator. This safe-harbor rule gives a violator time to correct the violation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (sanctions).","rule":"A party cannot file a motion for sanctions until 21 days after serving that motion on the alleged violator. This safe-harbor rule gives a violator time to correct the violation.","irac":{"issue":"A police officer pulled over a speeding driver and administered a breathalyzer test. The driver failed this test, and the officer peacefully took him into custody. These events were recorded on the officer's dashboard and body cameras. Six months later, the driver sued the officer in a federal court, claiming that she violated a federal civil rights statute that prohibits police officers from using excessive force. The driver claimed that the officer slammed him against the car and repeatedly punched him in the face while making the arrest. The officer vehemently believes that the driver has no evidence to support his contention and is only pursuing the suit to harass her. The officer filed an answer denying the driver's allegations and served the driver with a motion for sanctions to recover attorney's fees incurred in filing the answer. Immediately thereafter, the officer filed the motion for sanctions with the court. Is the court likely to grant the officer's motion for sanctions?","rule":"A party cannot file a motion for sanctions until 21 days after serving that motion on the alleged violator. This safe-harbor rule gives a violator time to correct the violation.","analysis":"Common FRCP 11 sanctions\n\nMonetary* • Order fine payable to court\n• Order payment of opposing party's attorney's fees & other expenses Nonmonetary • Strike pleading, motion, or other document\n• Admonish, reprimand, or censure offending party, attorney, or law firm\n• Require participation in training/seminar\n• Refer issue to disciplinary authorities (eg, state legal ethics committee) FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Cannot be imposed on represented party for violation of FRCP 11(b)(2).\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 11 requires that every pleading, motion, or other document filed with the court be signed by an attorney or an unrepresented party. This signature certifies the following to the court: 1. The document is presented for a proper purpose—not to harass, cause unnecessary delay, or needlessly increase the cost of litigation.\n\n2. All claims, defenses, and legal contentions are warranted by existing law or by a nonfrivolous argument for revising or establishing law.\n\n3. All factual assertions have or will have evidentiary support.\n\n4. All factual denials are warranted by evidence or are reasonably based on a belief or a lack of information.\n\nHere, the officer believes that the driver filed the complaint to harass her and has no evidence to support the facts asserted in his complaint—both violations of FRCP 11 (Choices C & D). These violations can subject a law firm (default), attorney, or party to sanctions, including a court order to pay the opposing party's attorney's fees. Sanction proceedings can be initiated:\n• on the court's own initiative (ie, sua sponte) – which requires the judge to issue an order to show cause to the alleged violator or\n\n• by a party's motion – which requires the party to serve the motion on the alleged violator but refrain from filing it for 21 days after serving it to allow any violation to be corrected (ie, safe-harbor rule).\n\nHere, the officer served the driver with a motion for sanctions to recover attorney's fees incurred in filing the answer. However, the officer filed the motion with the court immediately thereafter instead of waiting 21 days before filing it. Therefore, the court will likely deny the officer's motion for sanctions. (Choice A) The judge need not issue an order to show cause when sanction proceedings are initiated by a party's motion (as seen here). Educational objective: A party cannot file a motion for sanctions until 21 days after serving that motion on the alleged violator. This safe-harbor rule gives a violator time to correct the violation. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (sanctions).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11 (sanctions).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[848],"sourceAnswerPages":[849,850],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名警察拦下一名超速驾驶人并进行呼气式酒精测试。驾驶人未通过测试，警察平静地将其拘留。这些事件由警察的行车记录仪和随身摄像机拍摄。六个月后，驾驶人在联邦法院起诉警察，主张她违反禁止警察使用过度武力的联邦民权制定法。驾驶人声称，警察在逮捕时把他猛推到汽车上，并反复击打其面部。警察坚信驾驶人没有任何证据支持其主张，提起诉讼只是为了骚扰她。警察提交答辩状否认驾驶人的指控，并向驾驶人送达制裁动议，请求赔偿其提交答辩状产生的律师费。紧接着，警察立即向法院提交制裁动议。法院是否可能批准该动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为法官没有发布说明理由令。","B":"不会，因为警察没有等待21天便向法院提交动议。","C":"会，因为驾驶人没有证据支持起诉状中的事实陈述。","D":"会，因为警察认为驾驶人提起诉讼是为骚扰她。"},"explanationZh":"常见FRCP 11制裁\n\n金钱制裁*\n• 命令向法院缴纳罚款；\n• 命令支付对方律师费及其他费用。\n\n非金钱制裁\n• 删除诉状、动议或其他文件；\n• 告诫、申斥或谴责违规当事人、律师或律师事务所；\n• 要求参加培训或研讨会；\n• 将问题移送纪律机构（例如州法律伦理委员会）。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*不能因违反FRCP 11(b)(2)而对由律师代理的当事人施加金钱制裁。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）11要求，向法院提交的每份诉状、动议或其他文件必须由律师或无律师代理的当事人签署。该签名向法院证明：\n1. 文件是为正当目的提出，而非为了骚扰、造成不必要延误或无谓增加诉讼费用。\n2. 所有请求、抗辩和法律主张有现行法律依据，或有不属无理取闹、旨在修改现行法律或创设新法的论证依据。\n3. 所有事实陈述已有或将会有证据支持。\n4. 所有事实否认均有证据依据，或根据相信或缺乏信息而合理作出。\n\n本题中，警察认为驾驶人提起诉讼是为了骚扰她，而且没有证据支持其起诉状中的事实陈述；二者均属违反FRCP 11的情形（选项C和D）。这些违规可导致律师事务所（原则上的受制裁对象）、律师或当事人受到制裁，包括法院命令支付对方律师费。制裁程序可以通过以下方式启动：\n• 法院依职权启动——法官须向被指违规者发布说明理由令；或\n• 一方提出动议——该方须向被指违规者送达动议，但在送达后21天内不得向法院提交，以便违规者有机会纠正违规（即安全港规则）。\n\n本题中，警察向驾驶人送达制裁动议，请求赔偿其提交答辩状所产生的律师费。但是，她紧接着立即向法院提交动议，没有等待21天。因此，法院很可能驳回该制裁动议。\n\n（选项A）制裁程序由一方动议启动时（本题即如此），法官无须发布说明理由令。\n\n学习目标：一方只有在向被指违规者送达制裁动议21天后，才能向法院提交。该安全港规则给予违规者纠正违规的时间。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 11（制裁）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-281","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":281,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An employer believed that a former employee had disclosed the employer's trade secrets. As a result, the employer sued the employee in federal court for infringement of trade secrets. The employer sought to conduct an oral deposition of the employee. The employer's attorney sent the employee's attorney written notice of the deposition. On the day of the deposition, the employee and the employee's attorney arrived at the designated location, but the employer and the employer's attorney failed to attend. What should the federal court do upon request?","choices":{"A":"Award the employee the reasonable expenses of attending the deposition.","B":"Dismiss the employer's claim against the employee.","C":"Enter a protective order that prohibits a future deposition of the employee.","D":"Hold the employer in contempt of court."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A deposition is a discovery method in which a party conducts a written or oral examination* of someone (deponent) under oath and outside of court. The party seeking to conduct an oral deposition (deposing party) must provide notice to all other parties. When the deponent is a party, such notice compels the attendance of the party. If parties that were given notice attend a deposition and the deposing party does not attend, then the deposing party is liable for the reasonable expenses of attendance (including attorney's fees) incurred by the parties that attended. The federal court should therefore, upon request, award the employee the reasonable expenses of attending the deposition. *An oral deposition is preferred and more common because an attorney can question the deponent in real time and adjust his/her questions based on the deponent's answers. (Choice B) A court may impose sanctions (eg, dismissing a claim) on a party who fails to attend his/her own deposition. But here, sanctions are unwarranted because the employer failed to attend the employee's deposition—not its own. (Choice C) A protective order is a court order issued to protect a person from whom discovery is sought from annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, undue burden, or undue expense. Here, there is no indication that any such harm will result from a future deposition, so the court is unlikely to issue a protective order. (Choice D) A court may hold a nonparty deponent in contempt of court for failing to comply with a subpoena that compels his/her attendance at a deposition (not seen here). Educational objective: A deposing party must provide notice of an oral deposition to all other parties. If parties that were given notice attend a deposition and the deposing party does not attend, then the deposing party is liable for the reasonable expenses of attendance, including attorney's fees, incurred by the parties that attended. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(g) (failure to attend deposition or serve subpoena).","rule":"A deposing party must provide notice of an oral deposition to all other parties. If parties that were given notice attend a deposition and the deposing party does not attend, then the deposing party is liable for the reasonable expenses of attendance, including attorney's fees, incurred by the parties that attended.","irac":{"issue":"An employer believed that a former employee had disclosed the employer's trade secrets. As a result, the employer sued the employee in federal court for infringement of trade secrets. The employer sought to conduct an oral deposition of the employee. The employer's attorney sent the employee's attorney written notice of the deposition. On the day of the deposition, the employee and the employee's attorney arrived at the designated location, but the employer and the employer's attorney failed to attend. What should the federal court do upon request?","rule":"A deposing party must provide notice of an oral deposition to all other parties. If parties that were given notice attend a deposition and the deposing party does not attend, then the deposing party is liable for the reasonable expenses of attendance, including attorney's fees, incurred by the parties that attended.","analysis":"A deposition is a discovery method in which a party conducts a written or oral examination* of someone (deponent) under oath and outside of court. The party seeking to conduct an oral deposition (deposing party) must provide notice to all other parties. When the deponent is a party, such notice compels the attendance of the party. If parties that were given notice attend a deposition and the deposing party does not attend, then the deposing party is liable for the reasonable expenses of attendance (including attorney's fees) incurred by the parties that attended. The federal court should therefore, upon request, award the employee the reasonable expenses of attending the deposition. *An oral deposition is preferred and more common because an attorney can question the deponent in real time and adjust his/her questions based on the deponent's answers. (Choice B) A court may impose sanctions (eg, dismissing a claim) on a party who fails to attend his/her own deposition. But here, sanctions are unwarranted because the employer failed to attend the employee's deposition—not its own. (Choice C) A protective order is a court order issued to protect a person from whom discovery is sought from annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, undue burden, or undue expense. Here, there is no indication that any such harm will result from a future deposition, so the court is unlikely to issue a protective order. (Choice D) A court may hold a nonparty deponent in contempt of court for failing to comply with a subpoena that compels his/her attendance at a deposition (not seen here). Educational objective: A deposing party must provide notice of an oral deposition to all other parties. If parties that were given notice attend a deposition and the deposing party does not attend, then the deposing party is liable for the reasonable expenses of attendance, including attorney's fees, incurred by the parties that attended. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(g) (failure to attend deposition or serve subpoena).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(g) (failure to attend deposition or serve subpoena).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[851],"sourceAnswerPages":[852],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名雇主认为前雇员披露了其商业秘密，因此在联邦法院起诉该雇员侵犯商业秘密。雇主拟对雇员进行口头证言录取，雇主律师向雇员律师发送了证言录取书面通知。证言录取当日，雇员及其律师到达指定地点，但雇主及其律师没有出席。经请求，联邦法院应如何处理？","choicesZh":{"A":"判给雇员参加证言录取的合理费用。","B":"驳回雇主针对雇员的请求。","C":"发布保护令，禁止以后对雇员进行证言录取。","D":"以藐视法庭处理雇主。"},"explanationZh":"证言录取是一种证据开示方式，由一方在庭外对某人（被询问人）进行经宣誓的书面或口头询问。* 寻求进行口头证言录取的一方（询问方）必须通知所有其他当事人。被询问人是当事人时，该通知即强制该方出席。\n\n收到通知的当事人出席证言录取，而询问方没有出席时，询问方应承担已出席当事人产生的合理出席费用，包括律师费。因此，经请求，联邦法院应判给雇员参加证言录取的合理费用。\n\n*口头证言录取更受偏好且更常见，因为律师可以实时询问被询问人，并根据其回答调整问题。\n\n（选项B）一方未参加对其本人的证言录取时，法院可以施加制裁，例如驳回其请求。但本题中，雇主未参加的是雇员的证言录取，而不是对雇主本人的证言录取，所以不宜施加此类制裁。\n\n（选项C）保护令是为保护被寻求证据开示的人免受烦扰、难堪、压迫、不当负担或不当费用而发布的法院命令。本题没有迹象表明将来的证言录取会造成此类损害，因此法院不太可能发布保护令。\n\n（选项D）非当事人被询问人不遵守强制其参加证言录取的传票时，法院可以以藐视法庭处理（本题没有这种情况）。\n\n学习目标：询问方必须向所有其他当事人提供口头证言录取通知。收到通知的当事人出席，而询问方未出席时，询问方应承担出席当事人产生的合理费用，包括律师费。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(g)（未参加证言录取或未送达传票）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-086"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-282","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":282,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A woman sued the manufacturer of her hip implant in federal court, claiming that she developed neurological and other health symptoms as a result of her exposure to the implant's material. During discovery, the woman's attorney hired a doctor as an expert to assess the woman's case. The doctor evaluated the woman, reviewed her medical records, and concluded that the hip implant was not the cause of the woman's symptoms. After sharing this conclusion with the woman's attorney, the doctor was paid for the time spent assessing the woman's case. The woman's attorney did not retain the doctor's services for trial. The manufacturer's attorney then served a subpoena on the doctor to take her deposition. The woman's attorney moved to quash the subpoena. How will the court likely rule on the motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the doctor has evidence relevant to the case.","B":"Deny the motion, because the woman's attorney hired the doctor as an expert.","C":"Grant the motion, because the doctor is not a party in the case.","D":"Grant the motion, because the facts known and opinions held by the doctor are privileged."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Discovery of expert materials\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(4))\n\nNontestifying • Facts known or opinions held by expert not discoverable unless: expert – relate to court-ordered physical/mental examination or – exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain information by other means Testifying • Facts known & opinions held by expert discoverable through expert deposition\n• Drafts of expert reports & disclosures not discoverable\n• Communications between attorney & expert not discoverable unless: – relate to expert's compensation or – identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by attorney that expert considered in forming opinions FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties can use various methods (eg, depositions) to obtain nonprivileged information that is (1) relevant to any party's claim or defense and (2) proportional to the needs of the case. Information held by an expert witness expected to testify at trial is generally nonprivileged and discoverable. But facts known and opinions held by an expert not expected to testify are privileged and not discoverable unless:\n• that information relates to a court-ordered physical or mental examination or\n\n• exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain that information by other means—eg, a party dies and cannot be evaluated.\n\nHere, the woman's attorney hired the doctor as an expert to assess the woman's case but not to testify at trial. Since no court-ordered examination took place and no exceptional circumstances exist, the facts known and opinions held by the doctor are privileged and not discoverable. Therefore, the court will likely grant the motion to quash the subpoena (Choice B). (Choice A) Although the doctor has evidence relevant to the case and favorable to the manufacturer, this evidence is privileged.\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the doctor is not a party does not preclude her from being deposed. That is because a party can depose any party or nonparty to obtain nonprivileged information relevant and proportional to the needs of the case. Educational objective: The facts known and opinions held by an expert not expected to testify at trial are privileged and not discoverable unless (1) the information relates to a court-ordered physical or mental examination or (2) exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain that information by other means. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4) (discovery of expert witnesses).","rule":"The facts known and opinions held by an expert not expected to testify at trial are privileged and not discoverable unless (1) the information relates to a court-ordered physical or mental examination or (2) exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain that information by other means.","irac":{"issue":"A woman sued the manufacturer of her hip implant in federal court, claiming that she developed neurological and other health symptoms as a result of her exposure to the implant's material. During discovery, the woman's attorney hired a doctor as an expert to assess the woman's case. The doctor evaluated the woman, reviewed her medical records, and concluded that the hip implant was not the cause of the woman's symptoms. After sharing this conclusion with the woman's attorney, the doctor was paid for the time spent assessing the woman's case. The woman's attorney did not retain the doctor's services for trial. The manufacturer's attorney then served a subpoena on the doctor to take her deposition. The woman's attorney moved to quash the subpoena. How will the court likely rule on the motion?","rule":"The facts known and opinions held by an expert not expected to testify at trial are privileged and not discoverable unless (1) the information relates to a court-ordered physical or mental examination or (2) exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain that information by other means.","analysis":"Discovery of expert materials\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(4))\n\nNontestifying • Facts known or opinions held by expert not discoverable unless: expert – relate to court-ordered physical/mental examination or – exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain information by other means Testifying • Facts known & opinions held by expert discoverable through expert deposition\n• Drafts of expert reports & disclosures not discoverable\n• Communications between attorney & expert not discoverable unless: – relate to expert's compensation or – identify facts, data, or assumptions provided by attorney that expert considered in forming opinions FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties can use various methods (eg, depositions) to obtain nonprivileged information that is (1) relevant to any party's claim or defense and (2) proportional to the needs of the case. Information held by an expert witness expected to testify at trial is generally nonprivileged and discoverable. But facts known and opinions held by an expert not expected to testify are privileged and not discoverable unless:\n• that information relates to a court-ordered physical or mental examination or\n\n• exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain that information by other means—eg, a party dies and cannot be evaluated.\n\nHere, the woman's attorney hired the doctor as an expert to assess the woman's case but not to testify at trial. Since no court-ordered examination took place and no exceptional circumstances exist, the facts known and opinions held by the doctor are privileged and not discoverable. Therefore, the court will likely grant the motion to quash the subpoena (Choice B). (Choice A) Although the doctor has evidence relevant to the case and favorable to the manufacturer, this evidence is privileged.\n\n(Choice C) The fact that the doctor is not a party does not preclude her from being deposed. That is because a party can depose any party or nonparty to obtain nonprivileged information relevant and proportional to the needs of the case. Educational objective: The facts known and opinions held by an expert not expected to testify at trial are privileged and not discoverable unless (1) the information relates to a court-ordered physical or mental examination or (2) exceptional circumstances make it impracticable to obtain that information by other means. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4) (discovery of expert witnesses).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4) (discovery of expert witnesses).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[854],"sourceAnswerPages":[855,856],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名女子在联邦法院起诉其髋关节植入物的制造商，主张接触植入物材料使其出现神经系统症状及其他健康问题。证据开示期间，女子律师聘请一名医生作为专家评估女子的案件。医生评估女子、审查其医疗记录后，认定髋关节植入物并非女子症状的原因。医生把结论告知女子律师后，获得了评估案件所花时间的报酬。女子律师没有聘请医生为审判提供服务。随后，制造商律师向医生送达传票，要求对其进行证言录取。女子律师申请撤销传票。法院很可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回申请，因为医生掌握与案件有关的证据。","B":"驳回申请，因为女子律师聘请医生作为专家。","C":"批准申请，因为医生不是案件当事人。","D":"批准申请，因为医生知悉的事实和持有的意见受特权保护。"},"explanationZh":"专家材料的证据开示\n\n（FRCP 26(b)(4)）\n\n不作证专家\n专家知悉的事实或持有的意见不得开示，除非：\n• 与法院命令的身体／精神检查有关；或\n• 存在特殊情形，使通过其他方式取得信息并不切实可行。\n\n作证专家\n• 专家知悉的事实和持有的意见，可通过专家证言录取予以开示；\n• 专家报告和披露的草稿不得开示；\n• 律师与专家之间的通信不得开示，除非：\n  – 与专家报酬有关；或\n  – 识别律师提供且专家在形成意见时考虑的事实、数据或假设。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n证据开示是诉讼的审前阶段，当事人可运用多种方式（例如证言录取），取得与任何一方请求或抗辩相关、与案件需要相称的非特权信息。预期在审判中作证的专家证人所掌握的信息通常不受特权保护且可以开示。但是，不预期作证的专家知悉的事实和持有的意见受特权保护，不得开示，除非：\n• 该信息与法院命令的身体或精神检查有关；或\n• 存在特殊情形，使通过其他方式取得该信息并不切实可行，例如一方去世而无法接受评估。\n\n本题中，女子律师聘请医生作为专家评估案件，而不是在审判中作证。由于没有法院命令的检查，也不存在特殊情形，医生知悉的事实和持有的意见受特权保护，不得开示。因此，法院很可能批准撤销传票的申请（选项B）。\n\n（选项A）尽管医生掌握与案件有关且有利于制造商的证据，该证据仍受特权保护。\n\n（选项C）医生不是当事人这一事实并不阻止对其进行证言录取，因为一方可以对任何当事人或非当事人进行证言录取，以取得与案件有关、与案件需要相称的非特权信息。\n\n学习目标：不预期在审判中作证的专家知悉的事实和持有的意见受特权保护，不得开示，除非：(1)信息与法院命令的身体或精神检查有关；或(2)存在特殊情形，使通过其他方式取得该信息并不切实可行。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4)（专家证人的证据开示）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-283","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":283,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A homeowner from State A sued a mortgage lender in a federal court located in State A. The mortgage lender is incorporated and maintains its principal place of business in State B. The homeowner's complaint alleged that the lender charged her an interest rate on her home loan that exceeded the maximum rate allowed under a federal statute. The homeowner requested $250,000 in damages and a release from the mortgage. The mortgage lender filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the homeowner's suit for failure to state a claim, which the court denied. The lender then filed an answer and moved to dismiss the suit for failure to join a required party. The lender claims that the homeowner's husband must be joined in the suit because he cosigned the mortgage and his name is on the deed to the home. The homeowner and her husband have been separated for five years, and he permanently resides in State B. Should the court grant the mortgage lender's motion to dismiss for failure to join a required party?","choices":{"A":"No, because although the husband is a required party, joining him to the suit is feasible.","B":"No, because the lender waived this defense by failing to assert it in the pre-answer motion.","C":"Yes, because joining the husband to the suit would destroy proper venue.","D":"Yes, because joining the husband to the suit would destroy subject-matter jurisdiction."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 19, an absent party must be joined (ie, added) to a suit in certain circumstances. This required (ie, compulsory) joinder rule provides a process for determining whether joinder is necessary. The first step in this process considers whether the absent party is a required party because:\n• complete relief cannot be granted without that party's presence\n\n• the party's absence will subject the other parties to a substantial risk of multiple or inconsistent obligations or\n\n• the party's absence will prejudice his/her ability to protect an interest related to the lawsuit—as seen with the husband here because he cosigned the mortgage and his name is on the deed.\n\nThe second step considers whether it is feasible to join the required party. When it is feasible to join the required party, the court will deny the motion to dismiss for failure to join the party and instead order that the party be joined to the suit. Here, it is feasible to join the husband because:\n• the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over him because the suit arises from his ownership of the home in State A (ie, minimum contacts with State A)\n\n• subject-matter jurisdiction will be preserved after joinder because the homeowner's suit is based on a federal statute (ie, arises from federal-question jurisdiction) (Choice D) and\n\n• proper venue will remain because the home is located in State A (Choice C).\n\nTherefore, the court should deny the lender's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) Under FRCP 12(h), a defendant waives certain defenses that are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or an answer, whichever occurs first. But a defense based on failure to join a required party can be asserted at any time before the end of trial, so it was not waived by failing to assert it in the pre-answer motion. Educational objective: A required party must be joined in a suit when feasible—ie, when (1) the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the required party, (2) subject-matter jurisdiction will be preserved after joinder, and (3) proper venue will remain. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","rule":"A required party must be joined in a suit when feasible—ie, when (1) the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the required party, (2) subject-matter jurisdiction will be preserved after joinder, and (3) proper venue will remain.","irac":{"issue":"A homeowner from State A sued a mortgage lender in a federal court located in State A. The mortgage lender is incorporated and maintains its principal place of business in State B. The homeowner's complaint alleged that the lender charged her an interest rate on her home loan that exceeded the maximum rate allowed under a federal statute. The homeowner requested $250,000 in damages and a release from the mortgage. The mortgage lender filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the homeowner's suit for failure to state a claim, which the court denied. The lender then filed an answer and moved to dismiss the suit for failure to join a required party. The lender claims that the homeowner's husband must be joined in the suit because he cosigned the mortgage and his name is on the deed to the home. The homeowner and her husband have been separated for five years, and he permanently resides in State B. Should the court grant the mortgage lender's motion to dismiss for failure to join a required party?","rule":"A required party must be joined in a suit when feasible—ie, when (1) the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the required party, (2) subject-matter jurisdiction will be preserved after joinder, and (3) proper venue will remain.","analysis":"Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 19, an absent party must be joined (ie, added) to a suit in certain circumstances. This required (ie, compulsory) joinder rule provides a process for determining whether joinder is necessary. The first step in this process considers whether the absent party is a required party because:\n• complete relief cannot be granted without that party's presence\n\n• the party's absence will subject the other parties to a substantial risk of multiple or inconsistent obligations or\n\n• the party's absence will prejudice his/her ability to protect an interest related to the lawsuit—as seen with the husband here because he cosigned the mortgage and his name is on the deed.\n\nThe second step considers whether it is feasible to join the required party. When it is feasible to join the required party, the court will deny the motion to dismiss for failure to join the party and instead order that the party be joined to the suit. Here, it is feasible to join the husband because:\n• the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over him because the suit arises from his ownership of the home in State A (ie, minimum contacts with State A)\n\n• subject-matter jurisdiction will be preserved after joinder because the homeowner's suit is based on a federal statute (ie, arises from federal-question jurisdiction) (Choice D) and\n\n• proper venue will remain because the home is located in State A (Choice C).\n\nTherefore, the court should deny the lender's motion to dismiss. (Choice B) Under FRCP 12(h), a defendant waives certain defenses that are not asserted in a pre-answer motion or an answer, whichever occurs first. But a defense based on failure to join a required party can be asserted at any time before the end of trial, so it was not waived by failing to assert it in the pre-answer motion. Educational objective: A required party must be joined in a suit when feasible—ie, when (1) the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the required party, (2) subject-matter jurisdiction will be preserved after joinder, and (3) proper venue will remain. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[857],"sourceAnswerPages":[858,859],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的房主在A州联邦法院起诉一家抵押贷款机构。该机构在B州注册成立且主要营业地也在B州。房主起诉状主张，贷款机构对其住房贷款收取的利率超过某项联邦制定法允许的最高利率。房主请求25万美元损害赔偿并解除抵押。贷款机构先以未能陈述可获救济的请求为由提出答辩前驳回动议，法院予以驳回。随后，贷款机构提交答辩状，并以未加入必要当事人为由申请驳回诉讼。贷款机构称，房主的丈夫必须加入诉讼，因为他共同签署了抵押贷款文件，住宅契据上也有其姓名。房主与丈夫已分居五年，丈夫永久居住在B州。法院是否应以未加入必要当事人为由批准贷款机构的驳回申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为丈夫虽是必要当事人，但将其加入诉讼是可行的。","B":"不应，因为贷款机构未在答辩前动议中提出该抗辩，已将其放弃。","C":"应当，因为加入丈夫会破坏适当审判地。","D":"应当，因为加入丈夫会破坏事项管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）19，缺席当事人在特定情形下必须加入（即被增加至）诉讼。这项强制合并规则规定了判断是否必须合并的程序。\n\n第一步考察缺席者是否属于必要当事人，理由包括：\n• 没有该方在场就无法提供完全救济；\n• 该方缺席会使其他当事人面临承担多重或相互矛盾义务的重大风险；或\n• 该方缺席会损害其保护与诉讼有关利益的能力——本题中的丈夫即如此，因为他共同签署抵押贷款文件且住宅契据上有其姓名。\n\n第二步考察加入必要当事人是否可行。如果可行，法院会驳回以未加入该方为由的驳回申请，并命令将该方加入诉讼。本题中，加入丈夫是可行的，因为：\n• 本诉讼源于其对A州住宅的所有权（即其与A州具有最低限度联系），法院可以对他取得对人管辖权；\n• 加入后仍保有事项管辖权，因为房主的诉讼基于联邦制定法，即源于联邦问题管辖权（选项D）；并且\n• 住宅位于A州，所以适当审判地仍然存在（选项C）。\n\n因此，法院应驳回贷款机构的驳回申请。\n\n（选项B）依据FRCP 12(h)，被告未在答辩前动议或答辩状（以先发生者为准）中提出某些抗辩时，将放弃这些抗辩。但是，未加入必要当事人的抗辩可在审判结束前的任何时候提出，因此未在答辩前动议中提出并不构成放弃。\n\n学习目标：在可行时，必要当事人必须加入诉讼；所谓可行，是指：(1)法院可对必要当事人取得对人管辖权；(2)加入后事项管辖权仍然存在；且(3)适当审判地仍然存在。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19（当事人的强制合并）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-070"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-284","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":284,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A man sued his supervisor in a federal district court for sexual harassment in violation of federal law. During discovery, the man's attorney served the supervisor with a request to admit that the supervisor made derogatory jokes about the employee at a company party. The supervisor admitted to making the jokes. The man's attorney has now filed a motion with the district court for permission to take the depositions of 15 employees who will testify to hearing the supervisor make the derogatory jokes at the company party. If the court denies the man's motion, what is the likely explanation?","choices":{"A":"The company did not designate the employees to testify on its behalf.","B":"The court may not alter the 10-deposition limit.","C":"The depositions would be unreasonably cumulative and duplicative.","D":"The employees cannot be deposed because they are not parties in the suit."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Limitations on discovery\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(2))\n\nPermissible Court may alter limits upon:\n\n• number of depositions, interrogatories, or requests for admission and\n• length of oral depositions Required Court must limit discovery that:\n\n• is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative\n• is obtainable from more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive source\n• contains information that party had ample opportunity to obtain or\n• is outside scope of discovery FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties can use various methods to compel disclosure of information related to the case. Information is within the scope of discovery if it is relevant to any party's claim or defense, proportional to the needs of the case, and not privileged. Although the scope of discovery is broad, a federal court must limit the frequency or extent of discovery pursuant to a party's motion or on its own initiative when the discovery sought:\n• is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative\n• can be obtained from a more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive alternative source\n• contains information that the party has had ample opportunity to obtain by other means or\n• is outside the scope of discovery. Here, the man seeks to take the depositions of employees who will testify that they heard the supervisor make derogatory jokes about the man. This nonprivileged information is within the scope of discovery since it is relevant and proportional to the man's sexual harassment claim. However, the depositions would be unreasonably cumulative and duplicative because the supervisor admitted to making the jokes in response to the man's request for admission. Therefore, this is the likely explanation if the court denies the man's motion.\n\n(Choice A) An organization (eg, the company) that has been served with proper written notice of a deposition must designate individuals to testify on its behalf. However, such a designation is irrelevant here since the company is not a deponent in the case. (Choice B) A federal court may alter the 10-deposition limit if a party requests the court's leave (ie, permission).* *Leave of court is also required when (1) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, (2) the deponent has already been deposed in the case, or (3) the deponent is in prison. (Choice D) A party may depose any other party or nonparty—including the employees. Educational objective: A court must limit the frequency or extent of discovery when the discovery sought (1) is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative, (2) can be obtained from a more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive source, (3) contains information that the party has had time to obtain by other means, or (4) is outside the scope of discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(C) (required limitations on discovery).","rule":"A court must limit the frequency or extent of discovery when the discovery sought (1) is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative, (2) can be obtained from a more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive source, (3) contains information that the party has had time to obtain by other means, or (4) is outside the scope of discovery.","irac":{"issue":"A man sued his supervisor in a federal district court for sexual harassment in violation of federal law. During discovery, the man's attorney served the supervisor with a request to admit that the supervisor made derogatory jokes about the employee at a company party. The supervisor admitted to making the jokes. The man's attorney has now filed a motion with the district court for permission to take the depositions of 15 employees who will testify to hearing the supervisor make the derogatory jokes at the company party. If the court denies the man's motion, what is the likely explanation?","rule":"A court must limit the frequency or extent of discovery when the discovery sought (1) is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative, (2) can be obtained from a more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive source, (3) contains information that the party has had time to obtain by other means, or (4) is outside the scope of discovery.","analysis":"Limitations on discovery\n\n(FRCP 26(b)(2))\n\nPermissible Court may alter limits upon:\n\n• number of depositions, interrogatories, or requests for admission and\n• length of oral depositions Required Court must limit discovery that:\n\n• is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative\n• is obtainable from more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive source\n• contains information that party had ample opportunity to obtain or\n• is outside scope of discovery FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nDiscovery is the pretrial phase of a lawsuit during which the parties can use various methods to compel disclosure of information related to the case. Information is within the scope of discovery if it is relevant to any party's claim or defense, proportional to the needs of the case, and not privileged. Although the scope of discovery is broad, a federal court must limit the frequency or extent of discovery pursuant to a party's motion or on its own initiative when the discovery sought:\n• is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative\n• can be obtained from a more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive alternative source\n• contains information that the party has had ample opportunity to obtain by other means or\n• is outside the scope of discovery. Here, the man seeks to take the depositions of employees who will testify that they heard the supervisor make derogatory jokes about the man. This nonprivileged information is within the scope of discovery since it is relevant and proportional to the man's sexual harassment claim. However, the depositions would be unreasonably cumulative and duplicative because the supervisor admitted to making the jokes in response to the man's request for admission. Therefore, this is the likely explanation if the court denies the man's motion.\n\n(Choice A) An organization (eg, the company) that has been served with proper written notice of a deposition must designate individuals to testify on its behalf. However, such a designation is irrelevant here since the company is not a deponent in the case. (Choice B) A federal court may alter the 10-deposition limit if a party requests the court's leave (ie, permission).* *Leave of court is also required when (1) the deposition is sought before the initial planning conference, (2) the deponent has already been deposed in the case, or (3) the deponent is in prison. (Choice D) A party may depose any other party or nonparty—including the employees. Educational objective: A court must limit the frequency or extent of discovery when the discovery sought (1) is unreasonably cumulative or duplicative, (2) can be obtained from a more convenient, less burdensome, or less expensive source, (3) contains information that the party has had time to obtain by other means, or (4) is outside the scope of discovery. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(C) (required limitations on discovery).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(C) (required limitations on discovery).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[860],"sourceAnswerPages":[861,862],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名男子在联邦地区法院起诉其主管实施违反联邦法的性骚扰。证据开示期间，男子律师向主管送达承认请求，要求其承认在公司聚会上讲了贬损该雇员的笑话。主管承认讲过这些笑话。男子律师现向地区法院申请许可，对15名雇员进行证言录取；这些人将证明自己在公司聚会上听到主管讲贬损笑话。如果法院驳回男子的申请，最可能的解释是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"公司没有指定这些雇员代表公司作证。","B":"法院不能改变10次证言录取的限额。","C":"这些证言录取会造成不合理的累积和重复。","D":"这些雇员不是诉讼当事人，所以不能对其进行证言录取。"},"explanationZh":"证据开示的限制\n\n（FRCP 26(b)(2)）\n\n可以调整\n法院可以调整以下限额：\n• 证言录取、书面询问或承认请求的数量；以及\n• 口头证言录取的时长。\n\n必须限制\n法院必须限制具有下列情形的证据开示：\n• 不合理地累积或重复；\n• 可从更便利、负担更小或费用更低的来源取得；\n• 包含当事人已有充分机会取得的信息；或\n• 超出证据开示范围。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n证据开示是诉讼的审前阶段，当事人可运用多种方式强制披露与案件有关的信息。信息若与任何一方的请求或抗辩相关、与案件需要相称且不受特权保护，即属于证据开示范围。\n\n尽管证据开示范围广泛，依据一方动议或法院依职权，在所请求证据开示具有下列情形时，联邦法院必须限制其频率或范围：\n• 不合理地累积或重复；\n• 可从更便利、负担更小或费用更低的替代来源取得；\n• 包含该方已有充分机会通过其他方式取得的信息；或\n• 超出证据开示范围。\n\n本题中，男子希望对多名雇员进行证言录取，以证明他们听到主管讲贬损该男子的笑话。该非特权信息与男子的性骚扰请求相关且与案件需要相称，属于证据开示范围。但是，这些证言录取会造成不合理的累积和重复，因为主管已在回应男子的承认请求时承认讲过这些笑话。因此，如果法院驳回男子的申请，这就是最可能的解释。\n\n（选项A）组织（例如公司）收到适当的书面证言录取通知后，必须指定个人代表组织作证。但是，本题中的被询问人不是公司，该指定要求与本题无关。\n\n（选项B）一方请求法院许可时，联邦法院可以调整10次证言录取的限额。*\n\n*在下列情形也须取得法院许可：(1)拟在初始规划会议前进行证言录取；(2)被询问人在本案中已经接受过证言录取；或(3)被询问人正在服刑。\n\n（选项D）一方可以对任何其他当事人或非当事人进行证言录取，包括这些雇员。\n\n学习目标：在所请求的证据开示具有下列情形时，法院必须限制其频率或范围：(1)不合理地累积或重复；(2)可从更便利、负担更小或费用更低的来源取得；(3)包含一方已有时间通过其他方式取得的信息；或(4)超出证据开示范围。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(2)(C)（必须限制证据开示的情形）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-285","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":285,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A designer sued a clothing store in a federal district court for selling clothes that featured the designer's trademarked designs without the designer's permission. As the trial approached, the judge ordered the parties' attorneys to attend a final pretrial conference. Which of the following can the judge NOT do at the conference?","choices":{"A":"Decide which party has the burden of proof.","B":"Establish a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence.","C":"Limit each party's use of expert testimony.","D":"Rule on the admissibility of evidence."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Pretrial conferences\n\n(FRCP 16(c))\n\nPurpose Matters for consideration\n\nExpedite litigation • Eliminate frivolous claims & defenses\n• Obtain admissions & stipulations\n• Set timeline for summary judgment & dispose of pending motions\n• Schedule discovery, pretrial briefs & future conferences Improve quality of • Amend pleadings trial • Refer matters to magistrate or master\n• Require separate trial for different claims\n• Adopt special procedures for complex/unusual legal issues\n• Rule on admissibility of evidence\n• Avoid cumulative evidence & limit expert testimony\n• Establish order & time limit for presentation of evidence Facilitate settlement • Facilitate just, speedy & inexpensive resolution\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 16 permits a judge to order the attorneys and any unrepresented parties to attend one or more pretrial conferences. These conferences are designed to expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. During these conferences, the judge has broad discretion to consider and act on a wide range of issues, including:\n• establishing a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence (Choice B)\n• limiting each party's use of expert testimony (Choice C) and\n• ruling on the admissibility of evidence (Choice D). However, the judge may not decide which party has the burden of proof. That is because a plaintiff (here, the designer) always has the burden to prove each element of the claim, often by a preponderance of the evidence. Educational objective: During pretrial conferences, the judge has broad discretion to act on a wide range of issues—eg, (1) establish a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence, (2) limit each\n\nparty's use of expert testimony, and (3) rule on the admissibility of evidence. However, the judge may not decide which party has the burden of proof. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16 (pretrial conferences).","rule":"During pretrial conferences, the judge has broad discretion to act on a wide range of issues—eg, (1) establish a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence, (2) limit each\n\nparty's use of expert testimony, and (3) rule on the admissibility of evidence. However, the judge may not decide which party has the burden of proof.","irac":{"issue":"A designer sued a clothing store in a federal district court for selling clothes that featured the designer's trademarked designs without the designer's permission. As the trial approached, the judge ordered the parties' attorneys to attend a final pretrial conference. Which of the following can the judge NOT do at the conference?","rule":"During pretrial conferences, the judge has broad discretion to act on a wide range of issues—eg, (1) establish a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence, (2) limit each\n\nparty's use of expert testimony, and (3) rule on the admissibility of evidence. However, the judge may not decide which party has the burden of proof.","analysis":"Pretrial conferences\n\n(FRCP 16(c))\n\nPurpose Matters for consideration\n\nExpedite litigation • Eliminate frivolous claims & defenses\n• Obtain admissions & stipulations\n• Set timeline for summary judgment & dispose of pending motions\n• Schedule discovery, pretrial briefs & future conferences Improve quality of • Amend pleadings trial • Refer matters to magistrate or master\n• Require separate trial for different claims\n• Adopt special procedures for complex/unusual legal issues\n• Rule on admissibility of evidence\n• Avoid cumulative evidence & limit expert testimony\n• Establish order & time limit for presentation of evidence Facilitate settlement • Facilitate just, speedy & inexpensive resolution\n\nFRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nFederal Rule of Civil Procedure 16 permits a judge to order the attorneys and any unrepresented parties to attend one or more pretrial conferences. These conferences are designed to expedite litigation, improve the trial's quality, and facilitate settlement. During these conferences, the judge has broad discretion to consider and act on a wide range of issues, including:\n• establishing a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence (Choice B)\n• limiting each party's use of expert testimony (Choice C) and\n• ruling on the admissibility of evidence (Choice D). However, the judge may not decide which party has the burden of proof. That is because a plaintiff (here, the designer) always has the burden to prove each element of the claim, often by a preponderance of the evidence. Educational objective: During pretrial conferences, the judge has broad discretion to act on a wide range of issues—eg, (1) establish a time limit for each party's presentation of evidence, (2) limit each\n\nparty's use of expert testimony, and (3) rule on the admissibility of evidence. However, the judge may not decide which party has the burden of proof. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16 (pretrial conferences).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16 (pretrial conferences).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[863],"sourceAnswerPages":[864,865],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名设计师在联邦地区法院起诉一家服装店未经许可销售带有其注册商标设计的服装。临近审判时，法官命令双方律师参加最终审前会议。下列哪项是法官在会议上不能做的？","choicesZh":{"A":"决定哪一方承担证明责任。","B":"规定每一方提交证据的时间限制。","C":"限制每一方使用专家证言。","D":"裁定证据的可采性。"},"explanationZh":"审前会议\n\n（FRCP 16(c)）\n\n目的｜可审议事项\n\n加快诉讼\n• 排除无理取闹的请求和抗辩；\n• 取得承认和约定；\n• 设定简易判决时间表并处理待决动议；\n• 安排证据开示、审前书状和未来会议。\n\n提高审判质量\n• 修正诉状；\n• 将事项交由司法官或专门审理人；\n• 要求对不同请求分别审判；\n• 针对复杂或特殊法律问题采用专门程序；\n• 裁定证据可采性；\n• 避免重复证据并限制专家证言；\n• 确定提交证据的顺序和时限。\n\n促进和解\n• 促进公正、迅速且低成本地解决案件。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n《联邦民事诉讼规则》16允许法官命令律师和任何无律师代理的当事人参加一次或多次审前会议。这些会议旨在加快诉讼、提高审判质量和促进和解。在会议中，法官对于审议和处理广泛事项拥有宽泛裁量权，包括：\n• 规定每一方提交证据的时限（选项B）；\n• 限制每一方使用专家证言（选项C）；以及\n• 裁定证据的可采性（选项D）。\n\n但是，法官不能决定哪一方承担证明责任。这是因为原告（本题中的设计师）始终负有证明其请求每个要件的责任，通常须达到优势证据标准。\n\n学习目标：在审前会议中，法官对于处理广泛事项拥有宽泛裁量权，例如：(1)规定每一方提交证据的时限；(2)限制每一方使用专家证言；以及(3)裁定证据的可采性。但是，法官不能决定哪一方承担证明责任。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 16（审前会议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-286","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":286,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A pedestrian from State A sued a driver from State B in a federal court in State B to recover $85,000 in damages. The pedestrian alleged that the driver ran a red light and hit the pedestrian in a crosswalk. Prior to trial, the driver's attorney disclosed that he intended to call a witness to testify that the traffic light facing the driver was green when the pedestrian entered the crosswalk. To verify whether the witness is truthful, the pedestrian's attorney sent the witness interrogatories. The witness failed to respond to any of the interrogatories. After attempting to confer with the witness and the driver's attorney, the pedestrian's attorney filed a motion to compel the witness to respond to the interrogatories. The driver's attorney opposed the motion. How should the court rule on the pedestrian's motion to compel?","choices":{"A":"The court should deny the motion and award the driver reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion.","B":"The court should deny the motion but not award the driver reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion.","C":"The court should grant the motion and award the pedestrian reasonable expenses incurred in making the motion.","D":"The court should grant the motion but not award the pedestrian reasonable expenses incurred in making the motion."},"answer":"A","explanation":"FRCP 37 allows a party to file a motion to compel a discovery response or disclosure. Interrogatories are a method of discovery in which a party serves written questions on another party that inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. However, interrogatories may not be served on nonparties. As a result, the nonparty witness need not respond to the pedestrian's interrogatories, and the court should deny the pedestrian's motion to compel those responses (Choices C & D). When ruling on a motion to compel, the court may award reasonable expenses associated with the motion based on the following scenarios:\n• If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant the reasonable expenses incurred in making the motion unless an exception applies.\n• If the motion is granted in part and denied in part, the court may apportion the reasonable expenses as it sees fit.\n• If the motion is denied, the court must award the nonmovant the reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion unless the motion was substantially justified or the award would be unjust.* Here, the pedestrian's motion to compel was not substantially justified since the pedestrian's attorney should have known that interrogatories may not be served on nonparties. And an award of reasonable expenses would be fair because the motion wasted legal and judicial resources. Therefore, the court should deny the pedestrian's motion to compel and award the driver reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion (Choice B). *Before awarding expenses, the court must give the parties an opportunity to be heard. Additionally, the court may issue a protective order when it denies a motion in whole or in part. Educational objective: If a court denies a motion to compel, it must award the nonmovant the reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion unless the motion was substantially justified or the award would be unjust. If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant's expenses. If it is granted in part and denied in part, expenses may be apportioned. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(5) (motion to compel expenses).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2288 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a court should award expenses in ruling on a motion to compel).","rule":"If a court denies a motion to compel, it must award the nonmovant the reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion unless the motion was substantially justified or the award would be unjust. If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant's expenses. If it is granted in part and denied in part, expenses may be apportioned. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"A pedestrian from State A sued a driver from State B in a federal court in State B to recover $85,000 in damages. The pedestrian alleged that the driver ran a red light and hit the pedestrian in a crosswalk. Prior to trial, the driver's attorney disclosed that he intended to call a witness to testify that the traffic light facing the driver was green when the pedestrian entered the crosswalk. To verify whether the witness is truthful, the pedestrian's attorney sent the witness interrogatories. The witness failed to respond to any of the interrogatories. After attempting to confer with the witness and the driver's attorney, the pedestrian's attorney filed a motion to compel the witness to respond to the interrogatories. The driver's attorney opposed the motion. How should the court rule on the pedestrian's motion to compel?","rule":"If a court denies a motion to compel, it must award the nonmovant the reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion unless the motion was substantially justified or the award would be unjust. If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant's expenses. If it is granted in part and denied in part, expenses may be apportioned. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"FRCP 37 allows a party to file a motion to compel a discovery response or disclosure. Interrogatories are a method of discovery in which a party serves written questions on another party that inquire about any matter within the scope of discovery. However, interrogatories may not be served on nonparties. As a result, the nonparty witness need not respond to the pedestrian's interrogatories, and the court should deny the pedestrian's motion to compel those responses (Choices C & D). When ruling on a motion to compel, the court may award reasonable expenses associated with the motion based on the following scenarios:\n• If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant the reasonable expenses incurred in making the motion unless an exception applies.\n• If the motion is granted in part and denied in part, the court may apportion the reasonable expenses as it sees fit.\n• If the motion is denied, the court must award the nonmovant the reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion unless the motion was substantially justified or the award would be unjust.* Here, the pedestrian's motion to compel was not substantially justified since the pedestrian's attorney should have known that interrogatories may not be served on nonparties. And an award of reasonable expenses would be fair because the motion wasted legal and judicial resources. Therefore, the court should deny the pedestrian's motion to compel and award the driver reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion (Choice B). *Before awarding expenses, the court must give the parties an opportunity to be heard. Additionally, the court may issue a protective order when it denies a motion in whole or in part. Educational objective: If a court denies a motion to compel, it must award the nonmovant the reasonable expenses incurred in opposing the motion unless the motion was substantially justified or the award would be unjust. If the motion is granted, the court must award the movant's expenses. If it is granted in part and denied in part, expenses may be apportioned. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(5) (motion to compel expenses).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2288 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a court should award expenses in ruling on a motion to compel).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33 (interrogatories).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(5) (motion to compel expenses).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2288 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a court should award expenses in ruling on a motion to compel).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[866],"sourceAnswerPages":[867,868],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的行人在B州联邦法院起诉一名来自B州的驾驶人，请求85,000美元损害赔偿。行人主张驾驶人闯红灯并在人行横道撞到自己。审判前，驾驶人律师披露其拟传唤一名证人作证，称行人进入人行横道时，驾驶人方向的交通信号灯为绿灯。为核实证人是否如实陈述，行人律师向该证人发送书面询问。证人没有回答任何问题。行人律师尝试与证人及驾驶人律师会商后，申请强制证人答复书面询问。驾驶人律师反对该申请。法院应如何裁定行人的强制申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"法院应驳回申请，并判给驾驶人因反对申请产生的合理费用。","B":"法院应驳回申请，但不判给驾驶人因反对申请产生的合理费用。","C":"法院应批准申请，并判给行人提出申请产生的合理费用。","D":"法院应批准申请，但不判给行人提出申请产生的合理费用。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 37允许一方申请强制作出证据开示答复或披露。书面询问是一种证据开示方式，由一方向另一方送达书面问题，询问证据开示范围内的任何事项。但是，书面询问不得向非当事人送达。因此，非当事人证人无须回应行人的书面询问，法院应驳回强制答复申请（选项C和D）。\n\n法院裁定强制申请时，可依据下列情形判给与申请有关的合理费用：\n• 申请获准时，除非例外情形适用，法院必须判给申请人提出申请产生的合理费用；\n• 申请部分获准、部分驳回时，法院可按其认为适当的方式分摊合理费用；\n• 申请被驳回时，除非申请具有充分正当理由或费用裁定会造成不公，法院必须判给非申请人因反对申请产生的合理费用。*\n\n本题中，行人的强制申请不具有充分正当理由，因为行人律师本应知道书面询问不得向非当事人送达。判给合理费用也是公平的，因为该申请浪费了法律和司法资源。因此，法院应驳回行人的强制申请，并判给驾驶人因反对申请产生的合理费用（选项B）。\n\n*在判给费用前，法院必须给予双方陈述意见的机会。此外，法院全部或部分驳回强制申请时，可以发布保护令。\n\n学习目标：法院驳回强制申请时，除非申请具有充分正当理由或费用裁定会造成不公，必须判给非申请人因反对申请产生的合理费用。申请获准时，必须判给申请人的费用；部分获准、部分驳回时，可以分摊费用。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 33（书面询问）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)(5)（强制申请的费用）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2288 (3d ed. 2020)（说明法院裁定强制申请时应如何判给费用）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-087"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-287","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":287,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"A pedestrian domiciled in State A was crossing a street in State B when he was hit by a car driven by a citizen of a foreign country. Both the pedestrian and the driver suffered injuries. The pedestrian filed a negligence action against the driver in a federal district court in State B, seeking $100,000 in damages. The driver believes that the pedestrian was crossing the street illegally and is therefore responsible for the accident. The driver seeks an attorney's advice on how best to respond to the action. Assume that State B is a contributory negligence state. How should the attorney advise the driver to respond?","choices":{"A":"File an answer raising the affirmative defense of contributory negligence and asserting a counterclaim for negligence, seeking damages for the driver's injuries.","B":"File an answer raising the affirmative defense of contributory negligence and move for judgment on the pleadings.","C":"Move to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction, because the driver is not a citizen of State B.","D":"Move to dismiss for lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, because the driver is not a U.S. citizen."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Contents of answer\n\nRequired • Admissions & denials\n• Motions not waived\n• Affirmative defenses\n• Compulsory counterclaims Permitted • Permissive counterclaims\n• Crossclaims A defendant must file an answer in response to a plaintiff's lawsuit unless the suit is dismissed (eg, when the court grants a Rule 12(b) motion).* An answer must include the following items or else they are waived:\n• Admissions or denials for each allegation\n• Motions that have not already been waived\n• Affirmative defenses—ie, assertions that can negate or reduce liability even if every element of the opposing party's claim is proven (eg, contributory negligence)\n• Compulsory counterclaims—ie, claims asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim that (1) arise from the same transaction or occurrence as the earlier claim and (2) do not require adding another party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction Here, the driver has an affirmative defense based on the pedestrian's contributory negligence in illegally crossing the street. The driver also has a compulsory counterclaim because his injuries arose out of the same accident as the pedestrian's claim and no additional party is required. Therefore, the attorney should advise the driver to file an answer raising the affirmative defense and the compulsory counterclaim seeking damages for his injuries. *The defendant must also serve the answer on the plaintiff. If the plaintiff is represented by an attorney, service must be made on the plaintiff's attorney unless the court orders otherwise. (Choice B) A court can enter judgment on the pleadings when the undisputed material facts in the pleadings establish that the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. But here, the material facts are disputed since each party claims that the other party was responsible. (Choice C) Although the driver is not a State B citizen, the court has personal jurisdiction because the driver's minimum contacts with State B—driving on its roads—gave rise to the pedestrian's claim.\n\n(Choice D) Subject-matter jurisdiction based on diversity exists in a suit involving a foreign citizen if (1) he/she is not a permanent resident living in the same state as the opposing party and (2) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Here, both requirements are satisfied. Educational objective: An answer must include (1) admissions and denials, (2) motions that have not been waived, (3) affirmative defenses, and (4) compulsory counterclaims. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8(c) (affirmative defenses).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) (compulsory counterclaims).","rule":"An answer must include (1) admissions and denials, (2) motions that have not been waived, (3) affirmative defenses, and (4) compulsory counterclaims.","irac":{"issue":"A pedestrian domiciled in State A was crossing a street in State B when he was hit by a car driven by a citizen of a foreign country. Both the pedestrian and the driver suffered injuries. The pedestrian filed a negligence action against the driver in a federal district court in State B, seeking $100,000 in damages. The driver believes that the pedestrian was crossing the street illegally and is therefore responsible for the accident. The driver seeks an attorney's advice on how best to respond to the action. Assume that State B is a contributory negligence state. How should the attorney advise the driver to respond?","rule":"An answer must include (1) admissions and denials, (2) motions that have not been waived, (3) affirmative defenses, and (4) compulsory counterclaims.","analysis":"Contents of answer\n\nRequired • Admissions & denials\n• Motions not waived\n• Affirmative defenses\n• Compulsory counterclaims Permitted • Permissive counterclaims\n• Crossclaims A defendant must file an answer in response to a plaintiff's lawsuit unless the suit is dismissed (eg, when the court grants a Rule 12(b) motion).* An answer must include the following items or else they are waived:\n• Admissions or denials for each allegation\n• Motions that have not already been waived\n• Affirmative defenses—ie, assertions that can negate or reduce liability even if every element of the opposing party's claim is proven (eg, contributory negligence)\n• Compulsory counterclaims—ie, claims asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim that (1) arise from the same transaction or occurrence as the earlier claim and (2) do not require adding another party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction Here, the driver has an affirmative defense based on the pedestrian's contributory negligence in illegally crossing the street. The driver also has a compulsory counterclaim because his injuries arose out of the same accident as the pedestrian's claim and no additional party is required. Therefore, the attorney should advise the driver to file an answer raising the affirmative defense and the compulsory counterclaim seeking damages for his injuries. *The defendant must also serve the answer on the plaintiff. If the plaintiff is represented by an attorney, service must be made on the plaintiff's attorney unless the court orders otherwise. (Choice B) A court can enter judgment on the pleadings when the undisputed material facts in the pleadings establish that the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. But here, the material facts are disputed since each party claims that the other party was responsible. (Choice C) Although the driver is not a State B citizen, the court has personal jurisdiction because the driver's minimum contacts with State B—driving on its roads—gave rise to the pedestrian's claim.\n\n(Choice D) Subject-matter jurisdiction based on diversity exists in a suit involving a foreign citizen if (1) he/she is not a permanent resident living in the same state as the opposing party and (2) the amount in controversy exceeds $75,000. Here, both requirements are satisfied. Educational objective: An answer must include (1) admissions and denials, (2) motions that have not been waived, (3) affirmative defenses, and (4) compulsory counterclaims. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8(c) (affirmative defenses).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) (compulsory counterclaims).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8(c) (affirmative defenses).\n\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a) (compulsory counterclaims).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[869],"sourceAnswerPages":[870,871],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2019); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名住所地在A州的行人在B州横过街道时，被一名外国公民驾驶的汽车撞到。行人和驾驶人均受伤。行人在B州联邦地区法院对驾驶人提起过失诉讼，请求10万美元损害赔偿。驾驶人认为行人当时违法横穿街道，因此应对事故负责。驾驶人就如何最佳回应诉讼向律师寻求建议。假定B州适用共同过失规则，律师应建议驾驶人如何回应？","choicesZh":{"A":"提交答辩状，提出共同过失积极抗辩，并提出过失反请求，就驾驶人的伤害请求损害赔偿。","B":"提交答辩状，提出共同过失积极抗辩，并申请依诉状判决。","C":"以驾驶人不是B州公民为由，申请因缺乏对人管辖权而驳回。","D":"以驾驶人不是美国公民为由，申请因缺乏事项管辖权而驳回。"},"explanationZh":"答辩状的内容\n\n必须包括\n• 承认与否认；\n• 尚未放弃的动议；\n• 积极抗辩；\n• 强制反请求。\n\n可以包括\n• 许可反请求；\n• 交叉请求。\n\n除非诉讼被驳回（例如法院批准规则12(b)动议），被告必须提交答辩状回应原告诉讼。* 答辩状必须包含以下事项，否则即视为放弃：\n• 对每项指控的承认或否认；\n• 尚未放弃的动议；\n• 积极抗辩，即即使对方请求的每个要件均得到证明，也可以消除或减轻责任的主张（例如共同过失）；\n• 强制反请求，即针对对方较早请求提出，并且：(1)源于与较早请求相同的交易或事件；且(2)无须加入法院无法取得管辖权之另一方的请求。\n\n本题中，驾驶人基于行人违法横穿街道的共同过失拥有积极抗辩。驾驶人还拥有强制反请求，因为其伤害与行人的请求源于同一起事故，而且无须加入其他当事人。因此，律师应建议驾驶人提交答辩状，提出积极抗辩和强制反请求，就其伤害请求损害赔偿。\n\n*被告还必须向原告送达答辩状。如果原告由律师代理，除非法院另有命令，须向原告律师送达。\n\n（选项B）如果诉状中无争议的重要事实表明申请人依法有权获得判决，法院可以作出依诉状判决。但本题的重要事实存在争议，因为双方都主张对方应对事故负责。\n\n（选项C）尽管驾驶人不是B州公民，法院仍具有对人管辖权，因为驾驶人与B州的最低限度联系——在该州道路驾驶——引发了行人的请求。\n\n（选项D）涉及外国公民的诉讼如符合以下条件，即存在基于多元州籍的事项管辖权：(1)该外国公民不是与对方居住在同一州的永久居民；且(2)争议金额超过75,000美元。本题两项条件均满足。\n\n学习目标：答辩状必须包含：(1)承认与否认；(2)尚未放弃的动议；(3)积极抗辩；以及(4)强制反请求。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 8(c)（积极抗辩）。\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(a)（强制反请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-288","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":288,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A patient brought a diversity action against a doctor in federal court for medical malpractice. The patient alleges that the doctor negligently administered medication to the patient after surgery, which caused further complications. During discovery, the patient's attorney sent the doctor 30 requests for admission. One request stated, \"Please admit that the medication administered to the patient poses a higher risk to individuals in the patient's age group.\" The doctor served a written response 21 days later, in which she specifically denied that the medication poses a higher risk to such individuals. The case proceeded to trial. The patient offered several of the doctor's records into evidence that demonstrated the doctor knew at the time she prescribed the medication that it was unsafe for individuals in the patient's age group. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict for the patient, specifically finding that the patient proved that the medication is unsafe for individuals in the patient's age group. The patient then moved for sanctions against the doctor to pay the reasonable expenses incurred in proving this matter. Is the court likely to grant the patient's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because the patient's motion for sanctions after the jury returned its verdict was untimely.","B":"No, because the requests for admission exceeded the number allowed.","C":"Yes, because requests for admission are proper to obtain the truth about facts.","D":"Yes, because the patient proved that the matter was true."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Request for admission\n\n• Definition – request served on other party to admit truth of facts, application of law to facts, opinions, or genuineness of documents\n• Limit – none\n• Response – written response due within 30 days of service Requests for admission allow a party to ask another party to admit the truth about matters within the scope of discovery relating to facts, the application of law to facts, opinions about either, or the genuineness of documents. This discovery method seeks to expedite litigation and decrease costs by alleviating the need to prove undisputed matters at trial. If the responding party fails to admit a matter that is later proven true, then the requesting party can move for the responding party to pay reasonable expenses incurred, including attorney's fees, in making that proof. The court must grant this motion unless one of these conditions applies:\n• The court had sustained a prior objection to the request.\n• The admission sought was of no substantial importance.\n• The responding party reasonably believed that it would prevail on the matter.\n• Another good reason existed for the failure to admit.* Here, the doctor responded to the patient's request for admission by denying that the medication poses a higher risk to individuals in the patient's age group. However, the jury found that the patient proved that this matter was true. And since none of the above conditions apply, the court is likely to grant the patient's motion. *Courts have found a good reason when the responding party could not reasonably obtain information that would allow it to admit that the matter was true. (Choice A) The requesting party may move for sanctions after the matter that the responding party failed to admit is proven true. Therefore, the motion here was timely because it was made after the jury's verdict found that the patient's claim that the medication posed a higher risk to individuals in the patient's age group was true. (Choice B) The requests for admission did not exceed the number allowed since a party may serve an unlimited number of requests for admission. (Choice C) Requests for admission are proper to obtain the truth about facts. But the reason that the patient's motion will be granted is that he proved the medication is unsafe for individuals in his age group—a fact the doctor denied. Educational objective:\n\nA party that fails to admit a matter later proven true must generally pay the reasonable expenses incurred in making that proof. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(2) (sanction for failure to admit).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2290 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the process to obtain a sanction for failure to admit).","rule":"A party that fails to admit a matter later proven true must generally pay the reasonable expenses incurred in making that proof.","irac":{"issue":"A patient brought a diversity action against a doctor in federal court for medical malpractice. The patient alleges that the doctor negligently administered medication to the patient after surgery, which caused further complications. During discovery, the patient's attorney sent the doctor 30 requests for admission. One request stated, \"Please admit that the medication administered to the patient poses a higher risk to individuals in the patient's age group.\" The doctor served a written response 21 days later, in which she specifically denied that the medication poses a higher risk to such individuals. The case proceeded to trial. The patient offered several of the doctor's records into evidence that demonstrated the doctor knew at the time she prescribed the medication that it was unsafe for individuals in the patient's age group. At the conclusion of trial, the jury returned a verdict for the patient, specifically finding that the patient proved that the medication is unsafe for individuals in the patient's age group. The patient then moved for sanctions against the doctor to pay the reasonable expenses incurred in proving this matter. Is the court likely to grant the patient's motion?","rule":"A party that fails to admit a matter later proven true must generally pay the reasonable expenses incurred in making that proof.","analysis":"Request for admission\n\n• Definition – request served on other party to admit truth of facts, application of law to facts, opinions, or genuineness of documents\n• Limit – none\n• Response – written response due within 30 days of service Requests for admission allow a party to ask another party to admit the truth about matters within the scope of discovery relating to facts, the application of law to facts, opinions about either, or the genuineness of documents. This discovery method seeks to expedite litigation and decrease costs by alleviating the need to prove undisputed matters at trial. If the responding party fails to admit a matter that is later proven true, then the requesting party can move for the responding party to pay reasonable expenses incurred, including attorney's fees, in making that proof. The court must grant this motion unless one of these conditions applies:\n• The court had sustained a prior objection to the request.\n• The admission sought was of no substantial importance.\n• The responding party reasonably believed that it would prevail on the matter.\n• Another good reason existed for the failure to admit.* Here, the doctor responded to the patient's request for admission by denying that the medication poses a higher risk to individuals in the patient's age group. However, the jury found that the patient proved that this matter was true. And since none of the above conditions apply, the court is likely to grant the patient's motion. *Courts have found a good reason when the responding party could not reasonably obtain information that would allow it to admit that the matter was true. (Choice A) The requesting party may move for sanctions after the matter that the responding party failed to admit is proven true. Therefore, the motion here was timely because it was made after the jury's verdict found that the patient's claim that the medication posed a higher risk to individuals in the patient's age group was true. (Choice B) The requests for admission did not exceed the number allowed since a party may serve an unlimited number of requests for admission. (Choice C) Requests for admission are proper to obtain the truth about facts. But the reason that the patient's motion will be granted is that he proved the medication is unsafe for individuals in his age group—a fact the doctor denied. Educational objective:\n\nA party that fails to admit a matter later proven true must generally pay the reasonable expenses incurred in making that proof. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(2) (sanction for failure to admit).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2290 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the process to obtain a sanction for failure to admit).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(2) (sanction for failure to admit).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2290 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the process to obtain a sanction for failure to admit).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[872],"sourceAnswerPages":[873,874],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名患者在联邦法院对一名医生提起医疗过失多元州籍诉讼。患者主张，医生在手术后过失地给患者使用药物，导致进一步并发症。证据开示期间，患者律师向医生发送30项承认请求。其中一项写道：“请承认，给患者使用的药物对患者所属年龄组的人具有更高风险。”医生在21天后送达书面答复，明确否认该药物对该年龄组的人具有更高风险。案件进入审判。患者提交医生的若干记录作为证据，证明医生开具该药物时知道其对患者年龄组的人不安全。审判结束时，陪审团裁决患者胜诉，并明确认定患者已证明该药物对其年龄组的人不安全。随后，患者申请制裁，要求医生支付为证明该事项产生的合理费用。法院是否可能批准？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为患者在陪审团作出裁决后才申请制裁，已经不及时。","B":"不会，因为承认请求超过允许的数量。","C":"会，因为承认请求适合用于查明事实真相。","D":"会，因为患者证明了该事项为真。"},"explanationZh":"承认请求\n\n• 定义——向另一方送达请求，要求其承认事实、法律对事实的适用、意见或文件真实性；\n• 限额——无；\n• 答复——应在送达后30天内作出书面答复。\n\n承认请求允许一方要求另一方承认证据开示范围内事项的真实性，包括事实、法律对事实的适用、对二者的意见或文件的真实性。这种证据开示方式旨在免除审判中证明无争议事项的必要，从而加快诉讼并降低费用。\n\n如果回应方拒绝承认后来被证明为真的事项，请求方可以申请命令回应方支付为完成该证明产生的合理费用，包括律师费。除非存在以下任一情形，法院必须批准：\n• 法院先前已支持对该请求的异议；\n• 所要求承认的事项并无重大意义；\n• 回应方有合理理由相信自己会在该事项上胜诉；\n• 不予承认存在其他正当理由。*\n\n本题中，医生回应患者的承认请求时，否认该药物对患者年龄组的人具有更高风险。但是，陪审团认定患者证明该事项为真。由于上述例外均不适用，法院很可能批准患者的申请。\n\n*法院曾在回应方无法合理取得足以承认事项为真的信息时，认定存在正当理由。\n\n（选项A）请求方可以在回应方未承认的事项被证明为真后申请制裁。因此，本题申请及时，因为陪审团裁决认定患者关于该药物对其年龄组的人风险更高的主张为真后，患者才提出申请。\n\n（选项B）承认请求没有超过允许数量，因为一方可以送达数量不限的承认请求。\n\n（选项C）承认请求确实适合用于查明事实真相。但患者申请获准的原因，是其证明医生所否认的事实——药物对其年龄组的人不安全——为真。\n\n学习目标：一方未承认后来被证明为真的事项时，通常必须支付为完成该证明产生的合理费用。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(c)(2)（未予承认的制裁）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2290 (3d ed. 2020)（说明就未予承认取得制裁的程序）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-289","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":289,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A woman from State A sued a man from State B in a federal district court in State B for intentional infliction of emotional distress and sought $80,000 in damages. The woman claimed that the man's actions caused her to suffer post-traumatic stress disorder. The man wishes to subject the woman to a mental examination by a licensed psychiatrist of his own choosing before trial. Which is true about the prospect of a mental examination?","choices":{"A":"A federal district court in State A may order the woman to undergo a mental examination because that is where the woman resides.","B":"Only the federal district court in State B may order the woman to undergo a mental examination because that is where the lawsuit is pending.","C":"The examining psychiatrist is entitled to receive the woman's prior psychiatric records because he cannot conduct a complete examination without this information.","D":"The woman is entitled to choose who will perform the examination because the party to be examined always has this right."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Physical & mental examinations\n\n(FRCP 35)\n\nExamination • Party's mental or physical condition is in controversy requirements* • Motion for court order based on good cause\n• Court order issued where suit is pending provides notice specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope & person performing exam Examiner's • In writing & detail all findings (eg, diagnoses, reportrequirements conclusions & test results)\n• Deliver to examined party upon request Effects of examination • By requesting & receiving report, examined party waives any privilege concerning testimony about condition\n• After delivering report, party who sought exam may request & must receive previous reports of examined party's condition FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Requirements unnecessary if parties stipulate to exam.\n\nDuring discovery, the court where the lawsuit is pending may order a party to undergo a mental or physical examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. This court order is valid when:\n• that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy—ie, a physical injury or mental disorder is the subject of a claim or defense in the suit\n\n• the motion for the order is based on good cause—eg, the information sought cannot be easily attained by other means—and\n\n• the order provides notice by specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and person who will perform the exam.\n\nHere, the woman's mental condition is in controversy since she claims that the man caused her to suffer post-traumatic stress disorder. There also is good cause for a mental examination because the man likely cannot obtain information about her true mental condition by other means. But since the suit is pending in the federal district court in State B, only that court may order the woman to undergo a mental examination (Choice A).\n\n(Choice C) The examining psychiatrist is not entitled to receive the woman's prior psychiatric records before conducting the examination. Instead, such records need only be disclosed to the man if he requests them after the woman requests and receives a copy of the psychiatrist's report. (Choice D) The party to be examined does not possess an absolute right to choose who will perform the examination. Instead, the party requesting the examination may choose the examiner unless the examined party has a valid objection. In that case, the court will appoint the examiner. Educational objective: The court where a lawsuit is pending may order a physical or mental examination of a party when (1) that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy, (2) the motion for the order is based on good cause, and (3) the order provides notice specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and person to perform the exam. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35 (physical and mental exams).\n\n• Schlagenhauf v. Holder, 379 U.S. 104, 118–19 (1964) (explaining the \"in controversy\" and \"good cause\" requirements for a mental or physical examination of a party during discovery).","rule":"The court where a lawsuit is pending may order a physical or mental examination of a party when (1) that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy, (2) the motion for the order is based on good cause, and (3) the order provides notice specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and person to perform the exam.","irac":{"issue":"A woman from State A sued a man from State B in a federal district court in State B for intentional infliction of emotional distress and sought $80,000 in damages. The woman claimed that the man's actions caused her to suffer post-traumatic stress disorder. The man wishes to subject the woman to a mental examination by a licensed psychiatrist of his own choosing before trial. Which is true about the prospect of a mental examination?","rule":"The court where a lawsuit is pending may order a physical or mental examination of a party when (1) that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy, (2) the motion for the order is based on good cause, and (3) the order provides notice specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and person to perform the exam.","analysis":"Physical & mental examinations\n\n(FRCP 35)\n\nExamination • Party's mental or physical condition is in controversy requirements* • Motion for court order based on good cause\n• Court order issued where suit is pending provides notice specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope & person performing exam Examiner's • In writing & detail all findings (eg, diagnoses, reportrequirements conclusions & test results)\n• Deliver to examined party upon request Effects of examination • By requesting & receiving report, examined party waives any privilege concerning testimony about condition\n• After delivering report, party who sought exam may request & must receive previous reports of examined party's condition FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\n*Requirements unnecessary if parties stipulate to exam.\n\nDuring discovery, the court where the lawsuit is pending may order a party to undergo a mental or physical examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner. This court order is valid when:\n• that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy—ie, a physical injury or mental disorder is the subject of a claim or defense in the suit\n\n• the motion for the order is based on good cause—eg, the information sought cannot be easily attained by other means—and\n\n• the order provides notice by specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and person who will perform the exam.\n\nHere, the woman's mental condition is in controversy since she claims that the man caused her to suffer post-traumatic stress disorder. There also is good cause for a mental examination because the man likely cannot obtain information about her true mental condition by other means. But since the suit is pending in the federal district court in State B, only that court may order the woman to undergo a mental examination (Choice A).\n\n(Choice C) The examining psychiatrist is not entitled to receive the woman's prior psychiatric records before conducting the examination. Instead, such records need only be disclosed to the man if he requests them after the woman requests and receives a copy of the psychiatrist's report. (Choice D) The party to be examined does not possess an absolute right to choose who will perform the examination. Instead, the party requesting the examination may choose the examiner unless the examined party has a valid objection. In that case, the court will appoint the examiner. Educational objective: The court where a lawsuit is pending may order a physical or mental examination of a party when (1) that party's mental or physical condition is in controversy, (2) the motion for the order is based on good cause, and (3) the order provides notice specifying the time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and person to perform the exam. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35 (physical and mental exams).\n\n• Schlagenhauf v. Holder, 379 U.S. 104, 118–19 (1964) (explaining the \"in controversy\" and \"good cause\" requirements for a mental or physical examination of a party during discovery).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35 (physical and mental exams).\n\n• Schlagenhauf v. Holder, 379 U.S. 104, 118–19 (1964) (explaining the \"in controversy\" and \"good cause\" requirements for a mental or physical examination of a party during discovery).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[875],"sourceAnswerPages":[876,877],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的女子在B州联邦地区法院起诉一名来自B州的男子故意造成精神痛苦，请求8万美元损害赔偿。女子主张男子的行为使其患上创伤后应激障碍。男子希望在审判前让女子接受由其自行选择的持证精神科医生进行的精神检查。关于该精神检查，下列哪项正确？","choicesZh":{"A":"A州联邦地区法院可以命令女子接受精神检查，因为女子居住在A州。","B":"只有B州联邦地区法院可以命令女子接受精神检查，因为诉讼在该法院待决。","C":"进行检查的精神科医生有权取得女子以往的精神科记录，因为没有这些信息就无法完成全面检查。","D":"女子有权选择进行检查的人，因为被检查方始终享有该权利。"},"explanationZh":"身体与精神检查\n\n（FRCP 35）\n\n检查要求*\n• 一方的精神或身体状况属于争议事项；\n• 申请法院命令具有正当理由；\n• 由诉讼待决法院发布的命令通过指定检查的时间、地点、方式、条件、范围及执行人员而提供通知。\n\n检查人员报告要求\n• 以书面形式详细说明所有发现，例如诊断、结论和测试结果；\n• 经请求，向被检查方交付。\n\n检查的效力\n• 被检查方请求并收到报告后，即放弃与该状况有关证言的任何特权；\n• 寻求检查的一方交付报告后，可以请求并必须取得被检查方该状况的以往报告。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*双方约定检查时，无须满足这些要求。\n\n证据开示期间，诉讼待决法院可以命令一方接受由具有适当执照或认证的检查人员进行的精神或身体检查。该法院命令在下列条件下有效：\n• 该方的精神或身体状况属于争议事项，即身体伤害或精神障碍是诉讼请求或抗辩的标的；\n• 申请命令具有正当理由，例如无法轻易通过其他方式取得所寻求的信息；并且\n• 命令通过指定检查的时间、地点、方式、条件、范围及执行人员而提供通知。\n\n本题中，女子主张男子使其患上创伤后应激障碍，因此她的精神状况属于争议事项。精神检查也具有正当理由，因为男子很可能无法通过其他方式取得关于其真实精神状况的信息。但诉讼在B州联邦地区法院待决，所以只有该法院可以命令女子接受精神检查（选项A）。\n\n（选项C）进行检查的精神科医生无权在检查前取得女子以往的精神科记录。只有在女子请求并收到精神科医生报告的副本后，如果男子提出请求，才须向其披露这些记录。\n\n（选项D）被检查方没有选择检查人员的绝对权利。相反，请求检查的一方可以选择检查人员，除非被检查方提出有效异议；在该情况下，由法院指定。\n\n学习目标：诉讼待决法院可以在下列条件下命令对一方进行身体或精神检查：(1)该方的精神或身体状况属于争议事项；(2)申请命令具有正当理由；且(3)命令通过指定检查的时间、地点、方式、条件、范围及执行人员而提供通知。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 35（身体和精神检查）。\n• Schlagenhauf v. Holder, 379 U.S. 104, 118–19 (1964)（说明证据开示期间对一方进行精神或身体检查的“属于争议事项”和“正当理由”要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-290","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":290,"topic":"Pleadings, Service & Rule 11/12","question":"An insurance company from State A paid out $500,000 to a dentist from State B after she filed a claim for property damage allegedly caused by a spontaneous fire. After further investigation, however, the insurance company concluded that the dentist intentionally set the fire to recover the insurance money. The insurance company sued the dentist in a federal court in State B to recover the $500,000 it had paid her. In its complaint, the insurance company claimed that the dentist \"intentionally devised a plan to defraud the insurer and obtain money by false pretenses.\" The dentist filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the complaint for failure to state a claim on which relief may be granted. Will the dentist likely succeed on her motion to dismiss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the complaint contains a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the insurance company is entitled to relief.","B":"No, because the dentist must file an answer before she can make this motion.","C":"Yes, because a federal court cannot hear claims based on fraud.","D":"Yes, because claims based on fraud must be stated with particularity."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Specificity of pleadings\n\nClaim Pleading requirements\n\nGeneral rule • Complaint – short & plain statement of claim showing plaintiff is entitled to relief\n• Answer – short & plain statement of admissions, denials, defenses & claims against other parties Fraud/mistake/mental • Malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition condition may be alleged generally\n• Circumstances giving rise to fraud/mistake must be stated with particularity Conditions precedent • Assertion that conditions precedent occurred may be alleged generally\n• Denial that conditions precedent occurred must be stated with particularity Damages • General damages may be alleged generally\n• Special damages must be specifically stated A complaint is the pleading that starts a civil lawsuit and states the basis for the plaintiff's claim. It generally must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. However, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 9(b) sets forth different pleading requirements for a complaint alleging mistake or fraud (as seen here) or mental conditions. Such complaints:\n• may allege generally the defendant's malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition—eg, stating that the defendant acted intentionally—but\n\n• must state with particularity the circumstances giving rise to the claim—eg, specifying the time, place, and nature of the alleged fraud.\n\nHere, the insurance company's complaint alleged that the dentist \"intentionally devised a plan to defraud the insurer and obtain money by false pretenses.\" But it failed to state the circumstances giving rise to the fraud claim, like when and how the dentist set fire to her office (Choice A). As a result, the complaint failed to state a claim on which relief may be granted, and the dentist will likely succeed on her motion to dismiss.* *However, the court will likely allow the insurance company to amend its complaint to correct the defect since amendments are liberally allowed in federal court.\n\n(Choice B) FRCP 12 allows a defendant to make several motions before filing his/her answer—including a motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim on which relief may be granted (as seen here). (Choice C) Federal courts can hear most state law claims—including fraud claims—when their subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction. The only state law claims that may not be heard in federal court are those involving probate matters (eg, authenticating wills) or domestic relations (eg, issuing divorces). Educational objective: A complaint must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. When a complaint alleges mistake, fraud, or a mental condition, (1) the defendant's intent or other mental condition may be alleged generally but (2) the circumstances giving rise to the claim must be stated with particularity. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(b) (heightened pleading standards).","rule":"A complaint must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. When a complaint alleges mistake, fraud, or a mental condition, (1) the defendant's intent or other mental condition may be alleged generally but (2) the circumstances giving rise to the claim must be stated with particularity.","irac":{"issue":"An insurance company from State A paid out $500,000 to a dentist from State B after she filed a claim for property damage allegedly caused by a spontaneous fire. After further investigation, however, the insurance company concluded that the dentist intentionally set the fire to recover the insurance money. The insurance company sued the dentist in a federal court in State B to recover the $500,000 it had paid her. In its complaint, the insurance company claimed that the dentist \"intentionally devised a plan to defraud the insurer and obtain money by false pretenses.\" The dentist filed a pre-answer motion to dismiss the complaint for failure to state a claim on which relief may be granted. Will the dentist likely succeed on her motion to dismiss?","rule":"A complaint must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. When a complaint alleges mistake, fraud, or a mental condition, (1) the defendant's intent or other mental condition may be alleged generally but (2) the circumstances giving rise to the claim must be stated with particularity.","analysis":"Specificity of pleadings\n\nClaim Pleading requirements\n\nGeneral rule • Complaint – short & plain statement of claim showing plaintiff is entitled to relief\n• Answer – short & plain statement of admissions, denials, defenses & claims against other parties Fraud/mistake/mental • Malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition condition may be alleged generally\n• Circumstances giving rise to fraud/mistake must be stated with particularity Conditions precedent • Assertion that conditions precedent occurred may be alleged generally\n• Denial that conditions precedent occurred must be stated with particularity Damages • General damages may be alleged generally\n• Special damages must be specifically stated A complaint is the pleading that starts a civil lawsuit and states the basis for the plaintiff's claim. It generally must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. However, Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 9(b) sets forth different pleading requirements for a complaint alleging mistake or fraud (as seen here) or mental conditions. Such complaints:\n• may allege generally the defendant's malice, intent, knowledge, or other mental condition—eg, stating that the defendant acted intentionally—but\n\n• must state with particularity the circumstances giving rise to the claim—eg, specifying the time, place, and nature of the alleged fraud.\n\nHere, the insurance company's complaint alleged that the dentist \"intentionally devised a plan to defraud the insurer and obtain money by false pretenses.\" But it failed to state the circumstances giving rise to the fraud claim, like when and how the dentist set fire to her office (Choice A). As a result, the complaint failed to state a claim on which relief may be granted, and the dentist will likely succeed on her motion to dismiss.* *However, the court will likely allow the insurance company to amend its complaint to correct the defect since amendments are liberally allowed in federal court.\n\n(Choice B) FRCP 12 allows a defendant to make several motions before filing his/her answer—including a motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim on which relief may be granted (as seen here). (Choice C) Federal courts can hear most state law claims—including fraud claims—when their subject-matter jurisdiction is based on diversity jurisdiction. The only state law claims that may not be heard in federal court are those involving probate matters (eg, authenticating wills) or domestic relations (eg, issuing divorces). Educational objective: A complaint must contain a short and plain statement of the claim showing that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. When a complaint alleges mistake, fraud, or a mental condition, (1) the defendant's intent or other mental condition may be alleged generally but (2) the circumstances giving rise to the claim must be stated with particularity. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(b) (heightened pleading standards).","conclusion":"Choice D is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(b) (heightened pleading standards).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[878],"sourceAnswerPages":[879,880],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家来自A州的保险公司向来自B州的一名牙医支付了50万美元，牙医提出的理赔称财产损害由自发火灾造成。但进一步调查后，保险公司认定牙医为取得保险金而故意纵火。保险公司在B州联邦法院起诉牙医，要求返还已支付的50万美元。保险公司在起诉状中主张，牙医“故意设计计划欺诈保险公司，并以虚假借口取得金钱”。牙医以未能陈述可获救济的请求为由，提出答辩前驳回动议。牙医的动议是否可能成功？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为起诉状包含简短、明白的请求陈述，表明保险公司有权获得救济。","B":"不会，因为牙医必须先提交答辩状才能提出该动议。","C":"会，因为联邦法院不能审理以欺诈为依据的请求。","D":"会，因为以欺诈为依据的请求必须具体陈述。"},"explanationZh":"诉状的具体程度\n\n请求类型｜诉状要求\n\n一般规则\n• 起诉状——以简短、明白的方式陈述请求，表明原告有权获得救济；\n• 答辩状——以简短、明白的方式陈述承认、否认、抗辩及针对其他当事人的请求。\n\n欺诈／错误／精神状态\n• 恶意、故意、明知或其他精神状态可概括陈述；\n• 引发欺诈或错误的具体情形必须具体陈述。\n\n先决条件\n• 可概括主张先决条件已经发生；\n• 否认先决条件发生时必须具体陈述。\n\n损害\n• 一般损害可概括陈述；\n• 特别损害必须具体陈述。\n\n起诉状是启动民事诉讼并陈述原告请求依据的诉状。通常，它必须以简短、明白的方式陈述请求，表明原告有权获得救济。但是，《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）9(b)对主张错误或欺诈（本题即如此）或精神状态的起诉状规定不同要求。此类起诉状：\n• 可以概括主张被告的恶意、故意、明知或其他精神状态，例如称被告故意行事；但\n• 必须具体陈述引发请求的情形，例如说明所称欺诈的时间、地点和性质。\n\n本题中，保险公司的起诉状主张牙医“故意设计计划欺诈保险公司，并以虚假借口取得金钱”。但是，起诉状没有陈述引发欺诈请求的具体情形，例如牙医何时以及如何纵火烧毁其诊所（选项A）。因此，起诉状未能陈述可获救济的请求，牙医的驳回动议很可能成功。*\n\n*不过，联邦法院宽松允许修正诉状，因此法院很可能允许保险公司修正起诉状以纠正缺陷。\n\n（选项B）FRCP 12允许被告在提交答辩状前提出若干动议，其中包括以未能陈述可获救济的请求为由的驳回动议（本题即如此）。\n\n（选项C）事项管辖权基于多元州籍管辖权时，联邦法院可以审理大多数州法请求，包括欺诈请求。联邦法院不得审理的州法请求只有涉及遗嘱认证事项（例如验证遗嘱）或家庭关系（例如颁发离婚判令）的请求。\n\n学习目标：起诉状必须以简短、明白的方式陈述请求，表明原告有权获得救济。起诉状主张错误、欺诈或精神状态时：(1)被告的故意或其他精神状态可概括陈述；但(2)引发请求的情形必须具体陈述。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 9(b)（提高的诉状陈述标准）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-291","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":291,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"Two women filed a joint complaint in federal court against their employer for allegedly violating a federal law that prohibits workplace discrimination against pregnant women. The complaint alleged that the women applied for promotion to two, newly opened supervisor positions when they were six and seven months pregnant, respectively. The women claimed that their employer told each of them separately that he was denying them the promotion because they would miss work as a result of their pregnancies. The complaint also alleged that the women were fired from their positions shortly after these meetings. One woman seeks to be reinstated to her former position, while the other woman requests $100,000 in damages. The employer denied these allegations and filed a motion asking the court to sever the women's claims. How will the court likely rule on the employer's motion?","choices":{"A":"Deny the motion, because the women are required parties to the lawsuit.","B":"Deny the motion, because the women's claims arise out of the same series of occurrences and share common questions of law and fact.","C":"Grant the motion, because the women requested different relief.","D":"Grant the motion, because trying both women's claims together would prejudice the employer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Joinder of parties\n\nCompulsory Person must be joined in lawsuit—provided subject matter jurisdiction joinder is preserved—if absence will:\n\n(FRCP 19) • prevent court from granting complete relief to existing parties\n• prejudice absent person's interest or\n• subject existing party to multiple or inconsistent obligations Permissive Person may be joined in lawsuit if: joinder\n• claims by or against person arise out of same (FRCP 20) transaction/occurrence and\n• common question of fact or law will arise FRCP = Federal Rules of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe employer's motion to sever the women's complaint should be granted if their claims regarding workplace discrimination were improperly joined. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 20—the permissive joinder rule—multiple persons may be joined as plaintiffs or defendants in the same suit if:\n• the claims asserted by or against the joined parties arise out of the same transaction, occurrence, or series of transactions or occurrences and\n\n• the action will involve a common question of law or fact among all joined parties.\n\nHere, the claims asserted by both women stem from their mutual employer's alleged discrimination (same series of occurrences). Their claims are based on the same federal statute (common question of law) and the same allegation that they were denied a promotion and fired because they were pregnant (common question of fact). Therefore, the women were properly joined as plaintiffs, and the employer's motion to sever will likely be denied. (Choice A) The women are not required parties to the lawsuit under FRCP 19. This is because the women could separately sue the employer without (1) preventing the court from granting them complete relief, (2) prejudicing the absent woman's interest, or (3) subjecting the parties to multiple or inconsistent obligations. (Choice C) Parties need not seek (or defend against) the same relief to be joined. Therefore, the fact that the women requested different relief is not a basis for severing their joint complaint. (Choice D) FRCP 20(b) allows a judge to take protective measures (eg, ordering separate trials) when the joinder of parties would cause an opposing party embarrassment, delay,\n\nexpense, or other prejudice. But this can only be done when the claims by or against the joined parties do not concern the opposing party (not seen here). Educational objective: Permissive joinder of parties is appropriate when (1) the claims asserted by or against the joined parties arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) a common question of law or fact will arise among them. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 20 (permissive joinder of parties).","rule":"Permissive joinder of parties is appropriate when (1) the claims asserted by or against the joined parties arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) a common question of law or fact will arise among them.","irac":{"issue":"Two women filed a joint complaint in federal court against their employer for allegedly violating a federal law that prohibits workplace discrimination against pregnant women. The complaint alleged that the women applied for promotion to two, newly opened supervisor positions when they were six and seven months pregnant, respectively. The women claimed that their employer told each of them separately that he was denying them the promotion because they would miss work as a result of their pregnancies. The complaint also alleged that the women were fired from their positions shortly after these meetings. One woman seeks to be reinstated to her former position, while the other woman requests $100,000 in damages. The employer denied these allegations and filed a motion asking the court to sever the women's claims. How will the court likely rule on the employer's motion?","rule":"Permissive joinder of parties is appropriate when (1) the claims asserted by or against the joined parties arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) a common question of law or fact will arise among them.","analysis":"Joinder of parties\n\nCompulsory Person must be joined in lawsuit—provided subject matter jurisdiction joinder is preserved—if absence will:\n\n(FRCP 19) • prevent court from granting complete relief to existing parties\n• prejudice absent person's interest or\n• subject existing party to multiple or inconsistent obligations Permissive Person may be joined in lawsuit if: joinder\n• claims by or against person arise out of same (FRCP 20) transaction/occurrence and\n• common question of fact or law will arise FRCP = Federal Rules of Civil Procedure.\n\nThe employer's motion to sever the women's complaint should be granted if their claims regarding workplace discrimination were improperly joined. Under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure (FRCP) 20—the permissive joinder rule—multiple persons may be joined as plaintiffs or defendants in the same suit if:\n• the claims asserted by or against the joined parties arise out of the same transaction, occurrence, or series of transactions or occurrences and\n\n• the action will involve a common question of law or fact among all joined parties.\n\nHere, the claims asserted by both women stem from their mutual employer's alleged discrimination (same series of occurrences). Their claims are based on the same federal statute (common question of law) and the same allegation that they were denied a promotion and fired because they were pregnant (common question of fact). Therefore, the women were properly joined as plaintiffs, and the employer's motion to sever will likely be denied. (Choice A) The women are not required parties to the lawsuit under FRCP 19. This is because the women could separately sue the employer without (1) preventing the court from granting them complete relief, (2) prejudicing the absent woman's interest, or (3) subjecting the parties to multiple or inconsistent obligations. (Choice C) Parties need not seek (or defend against) the same relief to be joined. Therefore, the fact that the women requested different relief is not a basis for severing their joint complaint. (Choice D) FRCP 20(b) allows a judge to take protective measures (eg, ordering separate trials) when the joinder of parties would cause an opposing party embarrassment, delay,\n\nexpense, or other prejudice. But this can only be done when the claims by or against the joined parties do not concern the opposing party (not seen here). Educational objective: Permissive joinder of parties is appropriate when (1) the claims asserted by or against the joined parties arise from the same transaction, occurrence, or series thereof and (2) a common question of law or fact will arise among them. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 20 (permissive joinder of parties).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 20 (permissive joinder of parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[881],"sourceAnswerPages":[882,883],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"两名女子在联邦法院共同起诉其雇主，指称其违反禁止工作场所歧视孕妇的联邦法律。起诉状称，两名女子分别在怀孕六个月和七个月时，申请两个新设的主管职位。二人主张，雇主分别告诉她们，由于怀孕会导致她们缺勤，所以拒绝晋升。起诉状还主张，二人在这些会面后不久被解雇。其中一名女子请求恢复原职，另一名请求10万美元损害赔偿。雇主否认这些指控，并申请把两名女子的请求分开。法院很可能如何裁定？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回动议，因为两名女子是诉讼的必要当事人。","B":"驳回动议，因为两名女子的请求源于同一系列事件，并具有共同的法律和事实问题。","C":"批准动议，因为两名女子请求不同的救济。","D":"批准动议，因为共同审理两名女子的请求会损害雇主。"},"explanationZh":"当事人合并\n\n强制合并（FRCP 19）\n在不破坏事项管辖权的前提下，如果某人缺席会：\n• 阻止法院向现有当事人提供完全救济；\n• 损害缺席者的利益；或\n• 使现有当事人承担多重或相互矛盾的义务，\n则必须将其加入诉讼。\n\n许可合并（FRCP 20）\n如果：\n• 由该人提出或针对该人的请求源于同一交易或事件；并且\n• 将出现共同的事实或法律问题，\n则可以将其加入诉讼。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n如果两名女子关于工作场所歧视的请求合并不当，雇主请求拆分其共同起诉状的动议就应获批准。依据《联邦民事诉讼规则》（FRCP）20——许可合并规则——多名人士可以在同一诉讼中合并为原告或被告，条件是：\n• 由合并各方提出或针对各方的请求源于同一交易、事件或同一系列交易或事件；并且\n• 诉讼会涉及合并各方之间共同的法律或事实问题。\n\n本题中，两名女子的请求均源于共同雇主所称的歧视（同一系列事件）。其请求基于同一联邦制定法（共同法律问题），也基于同一指控，即二人因怀孕被拒绝晋升并遭解雇（共同事实问题）。因此，两名女子作为原告得到适当合并，雇主的拆分动议很可能被驳回。\n\n（选项A）两名女子不是FRCP 19下的必要当事人，因为每名女子均可分别起诉雇主，而不会：(1)阻止法院向其提供完全救济；(2)损害缺席女子的利益；或(3)使各方承担多重或相互矛盾的义务。\n\n（选项C）合并的当事人无须寻求（或抗辩）同一救济。因此，两名女子请求不同救济，并不构成拆分其共同起诉状的依据。\n\n（选项D）如果当事人合并会使对方难堪、延误、增加费用或遭受其他损害，FRCP 20(b)允许法官采取保护措施，例如命令分别审判。但只有在合并各方提出或面对的请求与该对方无关时才能这样做（本题并非如此）。\n\n学习目标：如果：(1)由合并各方提出或针对各方的请求源于同一交易、事件或同一系列交易或事件；且(2)各方之间会出现共同的法律或事实问题，则适合许可合并当事人。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 20（当事人的许可合并）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-292","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":292,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A bank loaned a borrower $20,000 to buy a car. The borrower signed a promissory note to pay back the loan over the course of four years. After six months of payments, the borrower stopped paying because he heard a report that the bank was fined by a federal agency for systematically misrepresenting the terms in its loans. The borrower sued the bank in a federal court for misrepresentations in the loan paperwork in violation of federal law. The bank believes that the borrower has breached the terms of the promissory note and seeks to recover the remaining balance due, which amounts to $18,000. In addition to denying the borrower's allegations in its answer, what should the bank do?","choices":{"A":"Assert a compulsory counterclaim for breach of contract.","B":"Assert a compulsory crossclaim for breach of contract.","C":"Assert a permissive counterclaim for breach of contract.","D":"Assert a permissive crossclaim for breach of contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Here, the borrower (plaintiff) sued the bank (defendant) for misrepresentations in the loan paperwork. The bank now seeks to recover $18,000 from the borrower in the same suit. The bank can do so by asserting a counterclaim—ie, a claim for relief asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim. There are two types of counterclaims:\n• Compulsory – one that (1) arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and (2) does not require adding a party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction, which must be asserted in the pending lawsuit or it is waived\n• Permissive – all other counterclaims, which may be asserted in the pending lawsuit or future litigation Since the bank's and borrower's claims arise from the same loan and no other party is required, the bank's counterclaim is compulsory (Choice C). As a result, it must be asserted in the bank's answer. (Choices B & D) A crossclaim is a claim for relief asserted against a coparty (eg, a defendant against a codefendant). This type of claim is always permissive (not compulsory) and can only be asserted if it arises from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the lawsuit. Here, since the bank and borrower are opposing parties, the bank's proper claim for relief is a counterclaim. Educational objective: A counterclaim is a claim for relief asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim. A counterclaim is compulsory when it (1) arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and (2) does not require adding another party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13 (compulsory and permissive counterclaims).","rule":"A counterclaim is a claim for relief asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim. A counterclaim is compulsory when it (1) arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and (2) does not require adding another party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction.","irac":{"issue":"A bank loaned a borrower $20,000 to buy a car. The borrower signed a promissory note to pay back the loan over the course of four years. After six months of payments, the borrower stopped paying because he heard a report that the bank was fined by a federal agency for systematically misrepresenting the terms in its loans. The borrower sued the bank in a federal court for misrepresentations in the loan paperwork in violation of federal law. The bank believes that the borrower has breached the terms of the promissory note and seeks to recover the remaining balance due, which amounts to $18,000. In addition to denying the borrower's allegations in its answer, what should the bank do?","rule":"A counterclaim is a claim for relief asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim. A counterclaim is compulsory when it (1) arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and (2) does not require adding another party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction.","analysis":"Here, the borrower (plaintiff) sued the bank (defendant) for misrepresentations in the loan paperwork. The bank now seeks to recover $18,000 from the borrower in the same suit. The bank can do so by asserting a counterclaim—ie, a claim for relief asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim. There are two types of counterclaims:\n• Compulsory – one that (1) arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and (2) does not require adding a party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction, which must be asserted in the pending lawsuit or it is waived\n• Permissive – all other counterclaims, which may be asserted in the pending lawsuit or future litigation Since the bank's and borrower's claims arise from the same loan and no other party is required, the bank's counterclaim is compulsory (Choice C). As a result, it must be asserted in the bank's answer. (Choices B & D) A crossclaim is a claim for relief asserted against a coparty (eg, a defendant against a codefendant). This type of claim is always permissive (not compulsory) and can only be asserted if it arises from the same transaction or occurrence as another claim in the lawsuit. Here, since the bank and borrower are opposing parties, the bank's proper claim for relief is a counterclaim. Educational objective: A counterclaim is a claim for relief asserted against an opposing party in response to that party's earlier claim. A counterclaim is compulsory when it (1) arises from the same transaction or occurrence as the opposing party's claim and (2) does not require adding another party over whom the court cannot acquire jurisdiction. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13 (compulsory and permissive counterclaims).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13 (compulsory and permissive counterclaims).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[884],"sourceAnswerPages":[885],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一家银行向一名借款人贷款2万美元购买汽车。借款人签署本票，同意在四年内偿还贷款。还款六个月后，借款人停止付款，因为他听到报道说，该银行因系统性地虚假陈述贷款条款而被一家联邦机构罚款。借款人在联邦法院起诉银行，主张贷款文件中的虚假陈述违反联邦法。银行认为借款人违反本票条款，拟追回尚欠余额18,000美元。除在答辩状中否认借款人的指控外，银行还应做什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"提出违约强制反请求。","B":"提出违约强制交叉请求。","C":"提出违约许可反请求。","D":"提出违约许可交叉请求。"},"explanationZh":"本题中，借款人（原告）以贷款文件中的虚假陈述为由起诉银行（被告）。银行现希望在同一诉讼中向借款人追回18,000美元。银行可以通过提出反请求实现该目的；反请求是针对对方当事人提出的救济请求，用来回应该方较早提出的请求。\n\n反请求分为两类：\n• 强制反请求——(1)源于与对方请求相同的交易或事件；且(2)无须加入法院无法取得管辖权之当事人的反请求；必须在待决诉讼中提出，否则即被放弃；\n• 许可反请求——所有其他反请求，可以在待决诉讼或未来诉讼中提出。\n\n银行和借款人的请求均源于同一笔贷款，且无须加入其他当事人，所以银行的反请求属于强制反请求（选项C）。因此，必须在银行答辩状中提出。\n\n（选项B和D）交叉请求是针对共同当事人提出的救济请求，例如一名被告对共同被告提出请求。交叉请求始终属于许可性而非强制性，而且只有在源于诉讼中另一请求所涉及的同一交易或事件时才能提出。本题中，银行与借款人是对方当事人，所以银行适当的救济请求是反请求。\n\n学习目标：反请求是为回应对方当事人较早提出的请求而针对该方提出的救济请求。如果反请求：(1)源于与对方请求相同的交易或事件；且(2)无须加入法院无法取得管辖权的另一方，则属于强制反请求。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 13（强制与许可反请求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-082"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-293","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":293,"topic":"Discovery","question":"A plaintiff filed a diversity action in federal district court against a defendant. The day after the defendant was served with process, the defendant filed and served an answer. When must the parties confer to develop a discovery plan?","choices":{"A":"At least 21 days before a scheduling conference is held or a scheduling order is due.","B":"At least 30 days before a scheduling conference is held or a scheduling order is due.","C":"Within 21 days after the defendant's answer was served.","D":"Within 30 days after the defendant's answer was served."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Initial conference\n\n(FRCP 26(f))\n\nTiming • Confer at least 21 days before scheduling conference is held or scheduling order is due Conference • Consider basis of claims & defenses content • Consider settlement possibilities\n• Make or arrange for required disclosures\n• Discuss preservation of discoverable information\n• Develop proposed discovery plan Discovery plan* • What discovery may be needed content • Schedule for discovery\n• Modifications to scope of discovery\n• Timing of required disclosures\n• Issues about electronically stored information or privilege\n• Orders court should issue FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\n*Due within 14 days after conference\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(f), the parties must hold an initial conference to plan for discovery. During the conference, the parties should:\n• consider the nature and basis of their claims and defenses\n• consider the possibilities for prompt settlement\n• make or arrange for required disclosures\n• discuss any issues about preserving discoverable information and\n• develop a proposed discovery plan. The initial conference must be held as soon as practicable, but at least 21 days before a scheduling conference is held or the judge's scheduling order is due (Choice B). If a party fails to confer in good faith, the court may order the party or his/her attorney to pay the opposing party's reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees. (Choices C & D) A defendant may amend its answer once as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the opposing party's written consent—within 21 days of serving it. Educational objective:\n\nThe parties must hold an initial conference to plan for discovery. The conference must be held as soon as practicable and at least 21 days before a scheduling conference with the court is held or the judge's scheduling order is due. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(f) (initial conference).","rule":"The parties must hold an initial conference to plan for discovery. The conference must be held as soon as practicable and at least 21 days before a scheduling conference with the court is held or the judge's scheduling order is due.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff filed a diversity action in federal district court against a defendant. The day after the defendant was served with process, the defendant filed and served an answer. When must the parties confer to develop a discovery plan?","rule":"The parties must hold an initial conference to plan for discovery. The conference must be held as soon as practicable and at least 21 days before a scheduling conference with the court is held or the judge's scheduling order is due.","analysis":"Initial conference\n\n(FRCP 26(f))\n\nTiming • Confer at least 21 days before scheduling conference is held or scheduling order is due Conference • Consider basis of claims & defenses content • Consider settlement possibilities\n• Make or arrange for required disclosures\n• Discuss preservation of discoverable information\n• Develop proposed discovery plan Discovery plan* • What discovery may be needed content • Schedule for discovery\n• Modifications to scope of discovery\n• Timing of required disclosures\n• Issues about electronically stored information or privilege\n• Orders court should issue FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure\n\n*Due within 14 days after conference\n\nUnder Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 26(f), the parties must hold an initial conference to plan for discovery. During the conference, the parties should:\n• consider the nature and basis of their claims and defenses\n• consider the possibilities for prompt settlement\n• make or arrange for required disclosures\n• discuss any issues about preserving discoverable information and\n• develop a proposed discovery plan. The initial conference must be held as soon as practicable, but at least 21 days before a scheduling conference is held or the judge's scheduling order is due (Choice B). If a party fails to confer in good faith, the court may order the party or his/her attorney to pay the opposing party's reasonable expenses, including attorney's fees. (Choices C & D) A defendant may amend its answer once as a matter of course—ie, without the court's permission or the opposing party's written consent—within 21 days of serving it. Educational objective:\n\nThe parties must hold an initial conference to plan for discovery. The conference must be held as soon as practicable and at least 21 days before a scheduling conference with the court is held or the judge's scheduling order is due. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(f) (initial conference).","conclusion":"Choice A is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(f) (initial conference).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[887],"sourceAnswerPages":[888,889],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦地区法院对一名被告提起多元州籍诉讼。被告收到诉讼文书送达的次日即提交并送达答辩状。双方必须何时会商以制定证据开示计划？","choicesZh":{"A":"至少在日程会议举行或日程命令到期前21天。","B":"至少在日程会议举行或日程命令到期前30天。","C":"被告答辩状送达后21天内。","D":"被告答辩状送达后30天内。"},"explanationZh":"初始会议\n\n（FRCP 26(f)）\n\n时间\n• 至少在日程会议举行或日程命令到期前21天会商。\n\n会议内容\n• 考虑请求与抗辩的依据；\n• 考虑和解可能性；\n• 作出或安排强制披露；\n• 讨论保存可开示信息；\n• 制定拟议证据开示计划。\n\n证据开示计划内容*\n• 可能需要何种证据开示；\n• 证据开示日程；\n• 对证据开示范围的修改；\n• 强制披露的时间；\n• 电子存储信息或特权相关问题；\n• 法院应发布的命令。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n*会议后14天内提交。\n\n根据《联邦民事诉讼规则》26(f)，双方必须举行初始会议，规划证据开示。在会议中，双方应：\n• 考虑其请求与抗辩的性质和依据；\n• 考虑迅速和解的可能性；\n• 作出或安排强制披露；\n• 讨论保存可开示信息的任何问题；以及\n• 制定拟议证据开示计划。\n\n初始会议必须尽快举行，但不得迟于日程会议举行或法官的日程命令到期前21天（选项B）。一方未善意会商时，法院可命令该方或其律师支付对方的合理费用，包括律师费。\n\n（选项C和D）被告可在答辩状送达后21天内依当然权利修正一次答辩状，即无须法院许可或对方书面同意。\n\n学习目标：双方必须举行初始会议，规划证据开示。会议须尽快举行，且至少在与法院举行日程会议或法官的日程命令到期前21天举行。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(f)（初始会议）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-294","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":294,"topic":"Pretrial Adjudication & Case Management","question":"A plaintiff obtained an ex parte temporary restraining order (TRO) in a federal civil action and posted a bond the same day. One day later, the plaintiff served the defendant with copies of the summons and complaint, the TRO, and supporting documents. The court scheduled a hearing on the preliminary injunction to occur 14 days after the TRO had been issued. Two days before the hearing, the plaintiff moved to extend the TRO and postpone the hearing for one week on the ground that its principal witness would be unavailable to testify on the scheduled day due to a planned vacation. The defendant opposed the motion and moved to dissolve the TRO. Is the court likely to grant the defendant's motion to dissolve the TRO?","choices":{"A":"No, because a TRO can be extended once for 14 days.","B":"No, because the plaintiff posted a bond and its principal witness's testimony is critical to the hearing.","C":"Yes, because the plaintiff's principal witness's vacation is not good cause to extend the TRO.","D":"Yes, because the TRO will expire before a rescheduled hearing can be held."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Temporary restraining orders\n\n(FRCP 65(b))\n\nObtaining Movant must provide:\n\n• specific facts in affidavit or verified complaint clearly showing immediate & irreparable injury, loss, or damage\n• written certification by movant's attorney of efforts to give notice & reasons why it should not be required and\n• security to pay costs & damages sustained by wrongfully restrained party Extending Limited to 14 days or until preliminary-injunction hearing can take place unless:\n\n• opposing party consents to extension or\n• good cause exists Dissolving • Party moving to dissolve order must give two days' notice\n• Court must hear & decide motion as promptly as justice requires FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that commands a party to do or stop doing a specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. Once this period expires, the court should dissolve the TRO unless it has already been extended because:\n• the opposing party consented to the extension (not seen here) or\n\n• there was good cause, which generally requires the movant to show that an unforeseeable event prevents the scheduled hearing from taking place.\n\nHere, the witness's vacation was planned, so the witness's unavailability at the preliminary-injunction hearing was not a surprise. The plaintiff should have known the availability of its principal witness before seeking a TRO and planned accordingly. Since the plaintiff cannot provide good cause for extending the TRO and postponing the preliminary-injunction hearing, the court is likely to grant the defendant's motion to dissolve the TRO. (Choice A) A TRO can be extended only if the opposing party consents or good cause exists. Therefore, it cannot be automatically extended once for 14 days. (Choice B) A federal court will issue a TRO only if the movant provides security (eg, a bond) sufficient to pay the costs and damages if the nonmovant is improperly\n\nrestrained. However, the plaintiff's posting of a bond does not affect whether the court should extend the TRO. Additionally, the critical nature of the principal witness's testimony does not alone establish good cause needed to extend a TRO. (Choice D) Had the plaintiff provided good cause, the court could have extended the TRO for a \"like period,\" which cannot exceed the period provided in the initial order. Here, the initial TRO period was 14 days, so extending the TRO and postponing the hearing for a week would have been valid had good cause existed. Educational objective: A TRO generally applies for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. A court can extend a TRO beyond this time if (1) the opposing party consents or (2) the movant provides good cause. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b)(2) (extension of TRO).\n\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2953 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a TRO can be extended beyond 14 days).","rule":"A TRO generally applies for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. A court can extend a TRO beyond this time if (1) the opposing party consents or (2) the movant provides good cause.","irac":{"issue":"A plaintiff obtained an ex parte temporary restraining order (TRO) in a federal civil action and posted a bond the same day. One day later, the plaintiff served the defendant with copies of the summons and complaint, the TRO, and supporting documents. The court scheduled a hearing on the preliminary injunction to occur 14 days after the TRO had been issued. Two days before the hearing, the plaintiff moved to extend the TRO and postpone the hearing for one week on the ground that its principal witness would be unavailable to testify on the scheduled day due to a planned vacation. The defendant opposed the motion and moved to dissolve the TRO. Is the court likely to grant the defendant's motion to dissolve the TRO?","rule":"A TRO generally applies for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. A court can extend a TRO beyond this time if (1) the opposing party consents or (2) the movant provides good cause.","analysis":"Temporary restraining orders\n\n(FRCP 65(b))\n\nObtaining Movant must provide:\n\n• specific facts in affidavit or verified complaint clearly showing immediate & irreparable injury, loss, or damage\n• written certification by movant's attorney of efforts to give notice & reasons why it should not be required and\n• security to pay costs & damages sustained by wrongfully restrained party Extending Limited to 14 days or until preliminary-injunction hearing can take place unless:\n\n• opposing party consents to extension or\n• good cause exists Dissolving • Party moving to dissolve order must give two days' notice\n• Court must hear & decide motion as promptly as justice requires FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.\n\nA temporary restraining order (TRO) is a court order that commands a party to do or stop doing a specified action for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. Once this period expires, the court should dissolve the TRO unless it has already been extended because:\n• the opposing party consented to the extension (not seen here) or\n\n• there was good cause, which generally requires the movant to show that an unforeseeable event prevents the scheduled hearing from taking place.\n\nHere, the witness's vacation was planned, so the witness's unavailability at the preliminary-injunction hearing was not a surprise. The plaintiff should have known the availability of its principal witness before seeking a TRO and planned accordingly. Since the plaintiff cannot provide good cause for extending the TRO and postponing the preliminary-injunction hearing, the court is likely to grant the defendant's motion to dissolve the TRO. (Choice A) A TRO can be extended only if the opposing party consents or good cause exists. Therefore, it cannot be automatically extended once for 14 days. (Choice B) A federal court will issue a TRO only if the movant provides security (eg, a bond) sufficient to pay the costs and damages if the nonmovant is improperly\n\nrestrained. However, the plaintiff's posting of a bond does not affect whether the court should extend the TRO. Additionally, the critical nature of the principal witness's testimony does not alone establish good cause needed to extend a TRO. (Choice D) Had the plaintiff provided good cause, the court could have extended the TRO for a \"like period,\" which cannot exceed the period provided in the initial order. Here, the initial TRO period was 14 days, so extending the TRO and postponing the hearing for a week would have been valid had good cause existed. Educational objective: A TRO generally applies for 14 days or until a preliminary-injunction hearing can take place—whichever occurs first. A court can extend a TRO beyond this time if (1) the opposing party consents or (2) the movant provides good cause. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b)(2) (extension of TRO).\n\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2953 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a TRO can be extended beyond 14 days).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b)(2) (extension of TRO).\n\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2953 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining how a TRO can be extended beyond 14 days).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[890],"sourceAnswerPages":[891,892],"sourceStatus":"NCBE-released question (2014); included in the UWorld Civil Procedure question bank","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名原告在联邦民事诉讼中取得单方临时限制令（TRO），并于同日提供保证金。一天后，原告向被告送达传票和起诉状、TRO及支持文件的副本。法院安排在TRO发布后第14天举行初步禁令听证会。听证会前两天，原告以主要证人因计划中的休假而无法在预定日期作证为由，申请延长TRO并把听证会推迟一周。被告反对该申请，并申请解除TRO。法院是否可能批准被告解除TRO的申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为TRO可以延长一次，为期14天。","B":"不会，因为原告已提供保证金，且其主要证人的证言对听证会至关重要。","C":"会，因为原告主要证人的休假不构成延长TRO的正当理由。","D":"会，因为TRO会在重新安排的听证会举行前到期。"},"explanationZh":"临时限制令\n\n（FRCP 65(b)）\n\n取得\n申请人必须提供：\n• 宣誓书或经核实的起诉状中的具体事实，清楚表明即时且不可弥补的伤害、损失或损害；\n• 申请人律师就通知努力以及为何不应要求通知所作的书面证明；以及\n• 用于支付被错误限制一方所受费用和损害的担保。\n\n延长\n期限为14天，或至能够举行初步禁令听证会时为止，除非：\n• 对方同意延长；或\n• 存在正当理由。\n\n解除\n• 申请解除命令的一方必须提前两天通知；\n• 法院必须在公正所要求的范围内尽快审理并裁定。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n临时限制令（TRO）是一项法院命令，要求一方在14天内，或在能够举行初步禁令听证会之前（以先发生者为准），实施或停止实施特定行为。该期限届满后，法院应解除TRO，除非其已因下列原因得到延长：\n• 对方同意延长（本题没有）；或\n• 存在正当理由，通常要求申请人证明不可预见的事件阻止预定听证会举行。\n\n本题中，证人的休假早有计划，因此证人无法在初步禁令听证会上出席并非意外。原告在申请TRO前本应了解主要证人的时间安排并相应规划。原告无法为延长TRO和推迟听证会提供正当理由，所以法院很可能批准被告解除TRO的申请。\n\n（选项A）只有在对方同意或存在正当理由时才能延长TRO。因此，TRO不能当然延长一次、为期14天。\n\n（选项B）只有在申请人提供足以支付非申请方被不当限制时产生之费用和损害的担保（例如保证金）后，联邦法院才会发布TRO。但是，原告提供保证金不影响法院是否应延长TRO。此外，主要证人的证言具有关键性这一点，本身不能构成延长TRO所需的正当理由。\n\n（选项D）如果原告提供了正当理由，法院可以将TRO延长“相同期间”，不得超过原命令规定的期间。本题原TRO期限为14天，因此若存在正当理由，延长TRO并把听证会推迟一周本可有效。\n\n学习目标：TRO通常适用14天，或至能够举行初步禁令听证会时为止，以先发生者为准。如果：(1)对方同意；或(2)申请人提供正当理由，法院可以在该期限后延长TRO。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(b)(2)（延长TRO）。\n• 11A Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2953 (3d ed. 2020)（说明如何把TRO延长至14天以后）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":[],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-295","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":295,"topic":"Discovery","question":"An employee, a citizen of State A, has sued his former employer, a corporation incorporated and with its headquarters in State B, in a federal district court in State A. The employee claims that the employer fired the employee because of his disability in violation of federal law. During discovery, the employee learned that the employer had previously been investigated for discriminatory practices. After the employer was served with process, the employee served the employer with a request to produce documents related to the discrimination investigation. The employer failed to produce the requested documents at the initial planning conference. Sixty days later, the employer still had not responded or produced the documents. Without attempting to confer with the employer's attorney, the employee filed a motion in the federal court in State A to compel the employer to produce the requested documents. The employer was properly served with the motion. The employer will likely succeed in defending against the motion to compel on which of the following grounds?","choices":{"A":"The employee did not serve the employer with a subpoena when he requested the documents' production.","B":"The employee failed to include a certification of good-faith conferral with the motion.","C":"The employee should have filed the motion in State B, where the employer is incorporated and headquartered.","D":"The employer still has time to respond to the discovery request."},"answer":"B","explanation":"FRCP 37 allows a party to file and serve a motion to compel a required disclosure or a response to a discovery request (eg, request for production). This motion must include a certification that the movant has, in good faith, conferred or attempted to confer with the noncompliant party or nonparty (ie, certification of good-faith conferral).* The motion also must be filed in the proper court:\n• A motion to compel a party must be filed with the court in which the action is pending.\n• A motion to compel a nonparty must be filed in the court where the discovery is or will be taken. Here, the employee properly filed the motion to compel the employer (defendant) to produce the requested documents in the federal court in State A—where the action is pending (Choice C). However, the employee's attorney did not confer or attempt to confer with the employer's attorney before filing the motion to compel. As a result, no certification of good-faith conferral was included with the motion. Therefore, the employer will likely succeed in defending against the employee's motion on this ground. *Good-faith conferral is required because the court wants the parties to resolve a discovery issue on their own before using judicial resources to do so. (Choice A) A subpoena is required to obtain documents or things in a nonparty's possession. But since the employer is a party to the action, the employee was not required to serve the employer with a subpoena when he requested the documents' production. (Choice D) The employer no longer has time to respond to the employee's request for production. That is because a response must be served in writing within 30 days after the parties' initial planning conference if a request for production was served before that conference (as seen here). Educational objective: A motion to compel must include a certification that the movant has, in good faith, conferred or attempted to confer with the noncompliant party or nonparty. A motion to compel a party must be filed with the court in which the action is pending, while a motion to compel a nonparty must be filed in the court where the discovery is or will be taken. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a) (motion-to-compel requirements).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2285 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements for a motion to compel required disclosures or discovery responses).","rule":"A motion to compel must include a certification that the movant has, in good faith, conferred or attempted to confer with the noncompliant party or nonparty. A motion to compel a party must be filed with the court in which the action is pending, while a motion to compel a nonparty must be filed in the court where the discovery is or will be taken. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","irac":{"issue":"An employee, a citizen of State A, has sued his former employer, a corporation incorporated and with its headquarters in State B, in a federal district court in State A. The employee claims that the employer fired the employee because of his disability in violation of federal law. During discovery, the employee learned that the employer had previously been investigated for discriminatory practices. After the employer was served with process, the employee served the employer with a request to produce documents related to the discrimination investigation. The employer failed to produce the requested documents at the initial planning conference. Sixty days later, the employer still had not responded or produced the documents. Without attempting to confer with the employer's attorney, the employee filed a motion in the federal court in State A to compel the employer to produce the requested documents. The employer was properly served with the motion. The employer will likely succeed in defending against the motion to compel on which of the following grounds?","rule":"A motion to compel must include a certification that the movant has, in good faith, conferred or attempted to confer with the noncompliant party or nonparty. A motion to compel a party must be filed with the court in which the action is pending, while a motion to compel a nonparty must be filed in the court where the discovery is or will be taken. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure.","analysis":"FRCP 37 allows a party to file and serve a motion to compel a required disclosure or a response to a discovery request (eg, request for production). This motion must include a certification that the movant has, in good faith, conferred or attempted to confer with the noncompliant party or nonparty (ie, certification of good-faith conferral).* The motion also must be filed in the proper court:\n• A motion to compel a party must be filed with the court in which the action is pending.\n• A motion to compel a nonparty must be filed in the court where the discovery is or will be taken. Here, the employee properly filed the motion to compel the employer (defendant) to produce the requested documents in the federal court in State A—where the action is pending (Choice C). However, the employee's attorney did not confer or attempt to confer with the employer's attorney before filing the motion to compel. As a result, no certification of good-faith conferral was included with the motion. Therefore, the employer will likely succeed in defending against the employee's motion on this ground. *Good-faith conferral is required because the court wants the parties to resolve a discovery issue on their own before using judicial resources to do so. (Choice A) A subpoena is required to obtain documents or things in a nonparty's possession. But since the employer is a party to the action, the employee was not required to serve the employer with a subpoena when he requested the documents' production. (Choice D) The employer no longer has time to respond to the employee's request for production. That is because a response must be served in writing within 30 days after the parties' initial planning conference if a request for production was served before that conference (as seen here). Educational objective: A motion to compel must include a certification that the movant has, in good faith, conferred or attempted to confer with the noncompliant party or nonparty. A motion to compel a party must be filed with the court in which the action is pending, while a motion to compel a nonparty must be filed in the court where the discovery is or will be taken. FRCP = Federal Rule of Civil Procedure. References\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a) (motion-to-compel requirements).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2285 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements for a motion to compel required disclosures or discovery responses).","conclusion":"Choice B is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a) (motion-to-compel requirements).\n\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2285 (3d ed. 2020) (explaining the requirements for a motion to compel required disclosures or discovery responses).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[893],"sourceAnswerPages":[894,895],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名A州公民雇员在A州联邦地区法院起诉其前雇主；该雇主是一家在B州注册成立且总部也在B州的公司。雇员主张雇主因其残疾而将其解雇，违反联邦法。证据开示期间，雇员得知雇主以前曾因歧视做法受到调查。雇主收到诉讼文书送达后，雇员向其送达提供请求，要求提供与歧视调查有关的文件。雇主没有在初始规划会议上提供所请求的文件。60天后，雇主仍未答复或提供文件。雇员没有尝试与雇主律师会商，便在A州联邦法院申请强制雇主提供所请求的文件。该动议已适当地向雇主送达。雇主最可能基于下列哪一理由成功抗辩该强制申请？","choicesZh":{"A":"雇员请求提供文件时，没有向雇主送达传票。","B":"雇员没有在动议中附上善意会商证明。","C":"雇员本应在雇主注册成立且总部所在的B州提交动议。","D":"雇主仍有时间回应证据开示请求。"},"explanationZh":"FRCP 37允许一方提交并送达动议，强制作出强制披露或证据开示请求（例如提供请求）的答复。该动议必须包含证明，表明申请人已与不遵守要求的当事人或非当事人善意会商或尝试会商（即善意会商证明）。* 动议还必须向适当法院提交：\n• 强制当事人的动议，必须向诉讼待决法院提交；\n• 强制非当事人的动议，必须向进行或将进行证据开示的法院提交。\n\n本题中，雇员适当地在A州联邦法院——诉讼待决法院——提交动议，请求强制雇主（被告）提供所请求的文件（选项C）。但是，雇员律师提交强制申请前，没有与雇主律师会商或尝试会商。因此，动议未附善意会商证明。雇主很可能基于该理由成功抗辩雇员的动议。\n\n*要求善意会商，是因为法院希望双方在动用司法资源前自行解决证据开示问题。\n\n（选项A）取得非当事人持有的文件或物品时须使用传票。但雇主是诉讼当事人，所以雇员请求提供文件时无须向其送达传票。\n\n（选项D）雇主已经没有时间回应提供请求。如果提供请求在双方初始规划会议前送达（本题即如此），书面答复必须在初始规划会议后30天内送达。\n\n学习目标：强制申请必须附上证明，表明申请人已与不遵守要求的当事人或非当事人善意会商或尝试会商。强制当事人的动议必须向诉讼待决法院提交；强制非当事人的动议必须向进行或将进行证据开示的法院提交。\n\nFRCP＝《联邦民事诉讼规则》。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(a)（强制申请的要求）。\n• 8B Charles Alan Wright et al., Federal Practice and Procedure § 2285 (3d ed. 2020)（说明强制作出必要披露或证据开示答复的申请要求）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-088"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"mbe-civpro-296","exam":"MBE","subject":"Civil Procedure","examSet":"Civil Procedure Question Bank","questionNumber":296,"topic":"Parties, Joinder & Class Actions","question":"A buyer from State A and a seller from State B entered into a contract in which the buyer agreed to purchase a parcel of State B land from the seller for $200,000. However, the seller did not disclose to the buyer that the seller co-owned the land with his mother, who is from State A. The seller's mother was never mentioned in the contract, and she never authorized the sale of the land. The buyer paid the seller $200,000 pursuant to the contract but was prevented from taking possession of the land by the seller's mother. The buyer sued the seller in federal court in State B to obtain possession of the land. The seller has filed a motion to dismiss the buyer's lawsuit for failing to join the seller's mother in the action. Should the court grant the seller's motion to dismiss the case?","choices":{"A":"No, because although the seller's mother is a required party to the lawsuit, dismissing the case would leave the buyer with no adequate remedy.","B":"No, because the seller's mother is not a required party to the lawsuit.","C":"Yes, because although the seller's mother is a required party to the lawsuit, joining her in the action would destroy subject-matter jurisdiction.","D":"Yes, because although the seller's mother is a required party to the lawsuit, the State B federal court lacks personal jurisdiction over her."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 19—the required (ie, compulsory) joinder rule— provides a three-step process for determining whether an absent party must be joined in a lawsuit. The first step considers whether the absent party is a required party because:\n• complete relief cannot be granted without that party's presence\n\n• the party's absence will subject the other parties to a substantial risk of multiple or inconsistent obligations or\n\n• the party's absence will impair his/her ability to protect an interest related to the lawsuit—as seen here with the seller's mother since she has a co-ownership interest in the land (Choice B).\n\nThe second step considers whether it is feasible to join the required party. Joinder is feasible when:\n• the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the required party\n• subject-matter jurisdiction remains after joinder and\n• proper venue remains. Here, the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the seller's mother because the suit arises from her minimum contacts with State B—her ownership of State B land (Choice D). But joining her as a party would destroy subject-matter jurisdiction because she and the buyer are both citizens of State A (no diversity) and the claim arises under state law (no federal question). Therefore, joinder is not feasible. When joinder is not feasible, the third step considers whether equity allows the action to proceed without the required party or compels a dismissal. Here, dismissal is required— and the seller's motion should be granted—because:\n• a judgment would severely prejudice the mother's interest in the land\n\n• the court cannot reasonably lessen or avoid this prejudice\n\n• in the mother's absence, the judgment would be inadequate since she could later sue to enforce her ownership rights and\n\n• the buyer can pursue an adequate remedy by refiling this action in state court (Choice A).\n\nEducational objective: A required party must be joined in a lawsuit when feasible—ie, when personal jurisdiction, subject-matter jurisdiction, and proper venue exist. If joinder is not feasible, the action must be dismissed if it would be inequitable to proceed without the required party.\n\nReferences\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","rule":"A required party must be joined in a lawsuit when feasible—ie, when personal jurisdiction, subject-matter jurisdiction, and proper venue exist. If joinder is not feasible, the action must be dismissed if it would be inequitable to proceed without the required party.","irac":{"issue":"A buyer from State A and a seller from State B entered into a contract in which the buyer agreed to purchase a parcel of State B land from the seller for $200,000. However, the seller did not disclose to the buyer that the seller co-owned the land with his mother, who is from State A. The seller's mother was never mentioned in the contract, and she never authorized the sale of the land. The buyer paid the seller $200,000 pursuant to the contract but was prevented from taking possession of the land by the seller's mother. The buyer sued the seller in federal court in State B to obtain possession of the land. The seller has filed a motion to dismiss the buyer's lawsuit for failing to join the seller's mother in the action. Should the court grant the seller's motion to dismiss the case?","rule":"A required party must be joined in a lawsuit when feasible—ie, when personal jurisdiction, subject-matter jurisdiction, and proper venue exist. If joinder is not feasible, the action must be dismissed if it would be inequitable to proceed without the required party.","analysis":"Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 19—the required (ie, compulsory) joinder rule— provides a three-step process for determining whether an absent party must be joined in a lawsuit. The first step considers whether the absent party is a required party because:\n• complete relief cannot be granted without that party's presence\n\n• the party's absence will subject the other parties to a substantial risk of multiple or inconsistent obligations or\n\n• the party's absence will impair his/her ability to protect an interest related to the lawsuit—as seen here with the seller's mother since she has a co-ownership interest in the land (Choice B).\n\nThe second step considers whether it is feasible to join the required party. Joinder is feasible when:\n• the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the required party\n• subject-matter jurisdiction remains after joinder and\n• proper venue remains. Here, the court can obtain personal jurisdiction over the seller's mother because the suit arises from her minimum contacts with State B—her ownership of State B land (Choice D). But joining her as a party would destroy subject-matter jurisdiction because she and the buyer are both citizens of State A (no diversity) and the claim arises under state law (no federal question). Therefore, joinder is not feasible. When joinder is not feasible, the third step considers whether equity allows the action to proceed without the required party or compels a dismissal. Here, dismissal is required— and the seller's motion should be granted—because:\n• a judgment would severely prejudice the mother's interest in the land\n\n• the court cannot reasonably lessen or avoid this prejudice\n\n• in the mother's absence, the judgment would be inadequate since she could later sue to enforce her ownership rights and\n\n• the buyer can pursue an adequate remedy by refiling this action in state court (Choice A).\n\nEducational objective: A required party must be joined in a lawsuit when feasible—ie, when personal jurisdiction, subject-matter jurisdiction, and proper venue exist. If joinder is not feasible, the action must be dismissed if it would be inequitable to proceed without the required party.\n\nReferences\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","conclusion":"Choice C is correct."},"references":"• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19 (required joinder of parties).","source":"2025MBE QBank Civil Procedure.pdf","sourceQuestionPages":[896],"sourceAnswerPages":[897,898],"sourceStatus":"UWorld-authored or modified; exact NCBE-original status is not identified","favorite":false,"attempts":[],"notes":"","questionZh":"一名来自A州的买方与一名来自B州的卖方订立合同，买方同意以20万美元从卖方购买一块位于B州的土地。但是，卖方没有向买方披露，其与来自A州的母亲共同拥有该土地。合同从未提及卖方母亲，她也从未授权出售土地。买方按照合同向卖方支付20万美元，但卖方母亲阻止买方占有土地。买方在B州联邦法院起诉卖方，请求取得土地占有。卖方以未将其母亲加入诉讼为由申请驳回。法院是否应批准驳回案件的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不应，因为尽管卖方母亲是诉讼的必要当事人，驳回案件会使买方得不到适当救济。","B":"不应，因为卖方母亲不是诉讼的必要当事人。","C":"应当，因为尽管卖方母亲是必要当事人，将其加入诉讼会破坏事项管辖权。","D":"应当，因为尽管卖方母亲是必要当事人，B州联邦法院对她缺乏对人管辖权。"},"explanationZh":"《联邦民事诉讼规则》19——强制合并规则——规定了判断缺席当事人是否必须加入诉讼的三步程序。\n\n第一步考察缺席者是否属于必要当事人，理由包括：\n• 没有该方在场就无法提供完全救济；\n• 该方缺席会使其他当事人面临承担多重或相互矛盾义务的重大风险；或\n• 该方缺席会损害其保护与诉讼有关利益的能力——本题中卖方母亲对土地具有共同所有权利益，正是如此（选项B）。\n\n第二步考察加入必要当事人是否可行。在下列情形下，加入可行：\n• 法院可以对必要当事人取得对人管辖权；\n• 加入后仍保有事项管辖权；且\n• 适当审判地仍然存在。\n\n本题中，本诉讼源于卖方母亲与B州的最低限度联系——她拥有B州土地——所以法院可以对其取得对人管辖权（选项D）。但将其加入诉讼会破坏事项管辖权，因为她和买方均为A州公民（不具多元州籍），而请求基于州法（不存在联邦问题）。因此，加入不可行。\n\n加入不可行时，第三步考察衡平是否允许诉讼在缺少必要当事人的情况下继续，还是要求驳回。本题必须驳回——卖方动议应获批准——因为：\n• 判决会严重损害母亲在土地中的利益；\n• 法院无法合理减轻或避免该损害；\n• 母亲缺席时，判决并不充分，因为她以后可以起诉以执行其所有权；并且\n• 买方可以在州法院重新提起诉讼，寻求适当救济（选项A）。\n\n学习目标：在可行时，必要当事人必须加入诉讼，即存在对人管辖权、事项管辖权和适当审判地。加入不可行时，如果在缺少必要当事人的情况下继续诉讼有失衡平，必须驳回诉讼。\n\n参考资料\n• Fed. R. Civ. P. 19（当事人的强制合并）。","translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","questionMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）","explanationAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","explanationMethod":"ChatGPT翻译（内置）"},"assetIds":["civpro-graphic-070"],"createdAt":"2026-07-28T00:00:00+08:00","isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[]},{"id":"pdf-contracts-001","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Interpretation","question":"A man and a university donor club entered into a written contract drafted by the university that obligated the man to purchase a $100,000 life insurance policy with the university as the sole, irrevocable beneficiary. In exchange, the man would have preferred membership status and the \"lifelong opportunity to purchase season tickets to university athletic games.\" The man purchased the life insurance policy as agreed. Several years later, the university instituted a program that required all its donor club members, including the man, to pay an annual $325 per-seat license in order to retain the \"opportunity to purchase season tickets.\" The man has filed an action against the university for a declaratory judgment that he is entitled to purchase season tickets without paying the seat license fee. Who should prevail in this action?","choices":{"A":"The man, because the contract is unambiguous and does not contemplate any additional fees.","B":"The man, because the contract will be interpreted against the university as the drafter.","C":"The university, because the contract does not prohibit the university from increasing ticket prices.","D":"The university, because the man was not deprived of the opportunity to purchase tickets."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Rules of contract interpretation\n\nPlain-\n• Court interprets contract terms according to their plain meaning meaning rule (unless parties clearly intended specialized meaning)\n• Goal is to effectuate parties' outward expressions of intent (most courts)\n• Extrinsic evidence may be used to clarify ambiguous or overly vague terms Context rule\n• Court interprets contract terms by considering all facts & circumstances related to transaction (some courts)\n• Goal is to effectuate parties' actual intent This action for declaratory judgment asks the court to define the contracting parties' rights and obligations under their agreement. The court's main concern is to give effect to the parties' outward expressions of intent, so the court will interpret the contract according to its plain (ie, ordinary) meaning whenever possible. Accordingly, if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous, the court will stop its interpretive process with that language.* Here, the contract's language is clear and unambiguous. So long as the man fulfills his contractual obligations (and he has), the university must provide him the \"opportunity to purchase\" season tickets. The contract does not contemplate any additional fees that the man must pay to retain this opportunity. Therefore, the man should prevail in his action for declaratory judgment based on the contract's plain meaning. *The court may go outside the four corners of the document to clarify the meaning of terms that are ambiguous (ie, subject to more than one reasonable interpretation) or overly vague (ie, imprecise). (Choice B) Under the doctrine of contra proferentem, ambiguous contract terms are interpreted against the drafter to protect the rights of the nondrafter. But this doctrine does not apply when, as here, the terms are unambiguous. (Choice C) The contract made no guarantees regarding ticket prices, so the university is free to increase ticket prices. However, the university cannot impose additional fees that the man must pay to retain the opportunity to purchase tickets at the prevailing price, whatever that may be. (Choice D) Although the man has not been deprived of the opportunity to purchase tickets, he would be deprived of the benefit he bargained for if he is required to pay additional fees before being allowed to exercise this opportunity.\n\nEducational objective: The plain-meaning rule of contract interpretation instructs courts to rely on the ordinary meaning of the words if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous.\n\nReferences\n• 17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 325 (2020) (interpreting unambiguous contracts).","educationalObjective":"The plain-meaning rule of contract interpretation instructs courts to rely on the ordinary meaning of the words if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous.","references":"• 17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 325 (2020) (interpreting unambiguous contracts).","rule":"The plain-meaning rule of contract interpretation instructs courts to rely on the ordinary meaning of the words if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous.","irac":{"issue":"Who should prevail in this action?","rule":"The plain-meaning rule of contract interpretation instructs courts to rely on the ordinary meaning of the words if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous.","analysis":"Rules of contract interpretation\n\nPlain-\n• Court interprets contract terms according to their plain meaning meaning rule (unless parties clearly intended specialized meaning)\n• Goal is to effectuate parties' outward expressions of intent (most courts)\n• Extrinsic evidence may be used to clarify ambiguous or overly vague terms Context rule\n• Court interprets contract terms by considering all facts & circumstances related to transaction (some courts)\n• Goal is to effectuate parties' actual intent This action for declaratory judgment asks the court to define the contracting parties' rights and obligations under their agreement. The court's main concern is to give effect to the parties' outward expressions of intent, so the court will interpret the contract according to its plain (ie, ordinary) meaning whenever possible. Accordingly, if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous, the court will stop its interpretive process with that language.* Here, the contract's language is clear and unambiguous. So long as the man fulfills his contractual obligations (and he has), the university must provide him the \"opportunity to purchase\" season tickets. The contract does not contemplate any additional fees that the man must pay to retain this opportunity. Therefore, the man should prevail in his action for declaratory judgment based on the contract's plain meaning. *The court may go outside the four corners of the document to clarify the meaning of terms that are ambiguous (ie, subject to more than one reasonable interpretation) or overly vague (ie, imprecise). (Choice B) Under the doctrine of contra proferentem, ambiguous contract terms are interpreted against the drafter to protect the rights of the nondrafter. But this doctrine does not apply when, as here, the terms are unambiguous. (Choice C) The contract made no guarantees regarding ticket prices, so the university is free to increase ticket prices. However, the university cannot impose additional fees that the man must pay to retain the opportunity to purchase tickets at the prevailing price, whatever that may be. (Choice D) Although the man has not been deprived of the opportunity to purchase tickets, he would be deprived of the benefit he bargained for if he is required to pay additional fees before being allowed to exercise this opportunity.","conclusion":"Answer A: The man, because the contract is unambiguous and does not contemplate any additional fees."},"questionZh":"一名男子和大学捐助俱乐部签订了一份由大学起草的书面合同，要求该男子购买一份价值 10 万美元的人寿保险，大学是唯一的、不可撤销的受益人。作为交换，该男子将拥有优先的会员身份和“购买大学体育比赛季票的终身机会”。该男子按照约定购买了人寿保险。几年后，该大学制定了一项计划，要求包括该男子在内的所有捐赠俱乐部成员每年支付每席位每年 325 美元的席位许可费，以保留“购买季票的机会”。该男子已对大学提起诉讼，要求做出宣告性判决，证明他有权在不支付座位许可费的情况下购买季票。这场行动中谁应该占上风？","choicesZh":{"A":"这名男子，因为合同是明确的，并且未约定任何额外费用。","B":"该男子表示，因为该合同将被解释为不利于起草人的大学。","C":"大学，因为合同并没有禁止大学提高门票价格。","D":"大学里，男子并没有因为被剥夺了购买门票的机会。"},"explanationZh":"合同解释规则\n\n普通-\n• 法院根据合同条款的普通含义规则来解释合同条款（除非双方明确表达了专门含义）\n• 目标是实现当事人对外表达的意图（大多数法院）\n• 外在证据可用于澄清不明确或过于模糊的术语 上下文规则\n• 法院通过考虑与交易相关的所有事实和情况来解释合同条款（某些法院）\n• 目标是实现双方的实际意图 该宣告性判决诉讼要求法院界定缔约方在其协议下的权利和义务。法院的主要关注点是使当事人对外表达的意图生效，因此法院将尽可能根据合同的简单（即普通）含义来解释合同。因此，如果合同的语言清晰无误，法院将停止使用该语言的解释程序。*在这里，合同的语言清晰无误。只要该男子履行了合同义务（他已经履行了），大学就必须为他提供“购买季票的机会”。合同并未考虑该男子为保留这一机会而必须支付的任何额外费用。因此，男方在诉讼中应以合同的明示意思为依据进行宣告性判断。 *法院可能会超出文件的四个角来澄清不明确（即受到不止一种合理解释）或过于模糊（即不精确）的术语的含义。 （选项B）根据反约定原则，对不明确的合同条款进行不利于起草人的解释，以保护非起草人的权利。但当术语明确时（如此处所示），该原则并不适用。 （选项C） 合同中没有对门票价格作出保证，因此学校可以随意提高门票价格。然而，大学不能强制该男子支付额外费用，以保留以现行价格购买门票的机会，无论价格是多少。 （选项D）虽然该男子并没有被剥夺购买门票的机会，但如果他在被允许行使这个机会之前被要求支付额外费用，他就被剥夺了讨价还价的好处。\n\n教育目标：合同解释的简单含义规则指示法院在合同语言清晰无误的情况下依赖词语的普通含义。\n\n参考文献\n• 17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 325 (2020)（解释明确的合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同解释的简单含义规则指示法院在合同语言清晰无误的情况下依赖词语的普通含义。","iracZh":{"issue":"这场行动中谁应该占上风？","rule":"合同解释的简单含义规则指示法院在合同语言清晰无误的情况下依赖词语的普通含义。","analysis":"合同解释规则\n\n普通-\n• 法院根据合同条款的普通含义规则来解释合同条款（除非双方明确表达了专门含义）\n• 目标是实现当事人对外表达的意图（大多数法院）\n• 外在证据可用于澄清不明确或过于模糊的术语 上下文规则\n• 法院通过考虑与交易相关的所有事实和情况来解释合同条款（某些法院）\n• 目标是实现双方的实际意图 该宣告性判决诉讼要求法院界定缔约方在其协议下的权利和义务。法院的主要关注点是使当事人对外表达的意图生效，因此法院将尽可能根据合同的简单（即普通）含义来解释合同。因此，如果合同的语言清晰无误，法院将停止使用该语言的解释程序。*在这里，合同的语言清晰无误。只要该男子履行了合同义务（他已经履行了），大学就必须为他提供“购买季票的机会”。合同并未考虑该男子为保留这一机会而必须支付的任何额外费用。因此，男方在诉讼中应以合同的明示意思为依据进行宣告性判断。 *法院可能会超出文件的四个角来澄清不明确（即受到不止一种合理解释）或过于模糊（即不精确）的术语的含义。 （选项B）根据反约定原则，对不明确的合同条款进行不利于起草人的解释，以保护非起草人的权利。但当术语明确时（如此处所示），该原则并不适用。 （选项C） 合同中没有对门票价格作出保证，因此学校可以随意提高门票价格。然而，大学不能强制该男子支付额外费用，以保留以现行价格购买门票的机会，无论价格是多少。 （选项D）虽然该男子并没有被剥夺购买门票的机会，但如果他在被允许行使这个机会之前被要求支付额外费用，他就被剥夺了讨价还价的好处。\n\n教育目标：合同解释的简单含义规则指示法院在合同语言清晰无误的情况下依赖词语的普通含义。\n\n参考文献\n• 17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 325 (2020)（解释明确的合同）。","conclusion":"答案 A：这名男子，因为合同是明确的，并且未约定任何额外费用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contract interpretation","ipa":"/kˈɑntɹˌækt ˌɪntˌɝpɹɪtˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"con·tract in·ter·pre·ta·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合同解释：确定合同用语及当事人权利义务的过程","contextualMeaning":"合同解释：确定合同用语及当事人权利义务的过程","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The plain-meaning rule of contract interpretation instructs courts to rely on the ordinary meaning of the words if the contract's language is clear and unambiguous.","synonyms":["construction of contract","contract construction"]},{"term":"ambiguous contract term","ipa":"/æmbˈɪɡjuəs kˈɑntɹˌækt tˈɝm/","syllables":"am·bigu·ous con·tract term","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"歧义合同条款：存在两种以上合理解释的用语","contextualMeaning":"歧义合同条款：存在两种以上合理解释的用语","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) Under the doctrine of contra proferentem, ambiguous contract terms are interpreted against the drafter to protect the rights of the nondrafter.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"declaratory judgment","ipa":"/dɪklˈɛɹʌtˌɔɹi dʒˈədʒmənt/","syllables":"de·clara·tory judg·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"确认判决：法院确定当事人法律权利义务的判决","contextualMeaning":"确认判决：法院确定当事人法律权利义务的判决","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"\" The man has filed an action against the university for a declaratory judgment that he is entitled to purchase season tickets without paying the seat license fee.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"plain meaning rule","ipa":"/plˈeɪn mˈinɪŋ ɹˈul/","syllables":"plain mean·ing rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"通常含义规则：无歧义条款按普通含义解释","contextualMeaning":"通常含义规则：无歧义条款按普通含义解释","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A man and a university donor club entered into a written contract drafted by the university that obligated the man to purchase a $100,000 life insurance policy with the university as the sole, irrevocable beneficiary.","synonyms":["ordinary-meaning rule","four-corners approach"]},{"term":"extrinsic evidence","ipa":"/ɛkstɹˈɪnsɪk ˈɛvədəns/","syllables":"ex·trin·sic ev·i·dence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"外部证据：合同文字以外、用于解释或补充的证据","contextualMeaning":"外部证据：合同文字以外、用于解释或补充的证据","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Rules of contract interpretation Plain- • Court interprets contract terms according to their plain meaning meaning rule (unless parties clearly intended specialized meaning) • Goal is to effectuate parties' outward expressions of intent (most courts) • Extrinsic evidence may be used to clarify ambiguous or overly vague terms Context rule • Court interprets contract terms by considering all facts & circumstances relat","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"actual intent","ipa":"/ˈæktʃəwəl ˌɪntˈɛnt/","syllables":"ac·tual in·tent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实际意图：当事人真实希望合同表达的意思","contextualMeaning":"实际意图：当事人真实希望合同表达的意思","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Rules of contract interpretation Plain- • Court interprets contract terms according to their plain meaning meaning rule (unless parties clearly intended specialized meaning) • Goal is to effectuate parties' outward expressions of intent (most courts) • Extrinsic evidence may be used to clarify ambiguous or overly vague terms Context rule • Court interprets contract terms by considering all facts & circumstances relat","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"context rule","ipa":"/kˈɑntɛkst ɹˈul/","syllables":"con·text rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"语境规则：结合交易事实与背景解释合同用语","contextualMeaning":"语境规则：结合交易事实与背景解释合同用语","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Rules of contract interpretation Plain- • Court interprets contract terms according to their plain meaning meaning rule (unless parties clearly intended specialized meaning) • Goal is to effectuate parties' outward expressions of intent (most courts) • Extrinsic evidence may be used to clarify ambiguous or overly vague terms Context rule • Court interprets contract terms by considering all facts & circumstances relat","synonyms":["contextual approach","contextual interpretation"]},{"term":"beneficiary","ipa":"/bˌɛnʌfˈɪʃiˌɛɹi/","syllables":"ben·e·fi·ciary","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 受惠者, 受益人；a. 受封的","contextualMeaning":"受益人：依合同履行取得利益的人","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:beneficiaries","sourceSentence":"A man and a university donor club entered into a written contract drafted by the university that obligated the man to purchase a $100,000 life insurance policy with the university as the sole, irrevocable beneficiary.","synonyms":["recipient of benefit"]},{"term":"unambiguous","ipa":"/ˌʌnæmbˈɪɡjəwəs/","syllables":"un·am·bigu·ous","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 不含糊的, 明确的；[法] 清晰的, 明白的, 不模糊的","contextualMeaning":"无歧义：只有一种合理解释、文字清楚明确","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"The man, because the contract is unambiguous and does not contemplate any additional fees.","synonyms":["clear","unequivocal"]},{"term":"guarantees","ipa":"/ɡˌɛɹʌntˈiz/","syllables":"guar·an·tees","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 担保( guarantee的名词复数 )； 担保品； 保修单","contextualMeaning":"保证：对履行、品质或他人债务承担的合同性承诺","wordForm":"词；原形 guarantee；词形 0:guarantee/1:s3","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The contract made no guarantees regarding ticket prices, so the university is free to increase ticket prices.","synonyms":["warrant","warrantee","warranty","guaranty"]},{"term":"bargained","ipa":"/bˈɑɹɡənd/","syllables":"bar·gained","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 讨价还价, 商谈( bargain的过去式和过去分词 )； 提出条件, 要求得到","contextualMeaning":"协商交换：为取得对方允诺或履行而作出的交易安排","wordForm":"词；原形 bargain；词形 0:bargain/1:dp/d:bargained/p:bargained","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Although the man has not been deprived of the opportunity to purchase tickets, he would be deprived of the benefit he bargained for if he is required to pay additional fees before being allowed to exercise this opportunity.","synonyms":["deal","buy","steal","dicker"]},{"term":"drafter","ipa":"/dɹˈæftɝ/","syllables":"drafter","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 起草者","contextualMeaning":"起草方：拟定合同文字的一方","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:drafters","sourceSentence":"The man, because the contract will be interpreted against the university as the drafter.","synonyms":["contract writer","drafting party"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":1,"sourceQuestionPage":1,"sourceQuestionPages":[1],"sourceExplanationPages":[2,3],"sourceAnswerPages":[2,3],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-002","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"An innkeeper, who had no previous experience in the motel or commercial laundry business and who knew nothing about the trade usages of either business, bought a motel and signed an agreement with a laundry company for the motel's laundry services. The agreement was for a term of one year and provided for \"daily service at $500 a week.\" From their conversations during negotiation, the laundry company owner knew that the innkeeper expected laundry services seven days a week. When the laundry company refused to pick up the motel's laundry on two successive Sundays and indicated that it would never do so, the innkeeper canceled the agreement. The laundry company sued the innkeeper for breach of contract. At trial, clear evidence was introduced to show that in the commercial laundry business \"daily service\" did not include service on Sundays. Is the laundry company likely to succeed in its action?","choices":{"A":"No, because the laundry company knew the meaning the innkeeper attached to \"daily service,\" and therefore the innkeeper's meaning will control.","B":"No, because the parties attached materially different meanings to \"daily service,\" and therefore no contract was formed.","C":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule will not permit the innkeeper to prove the meaning he attached to \"daily service.\"","D":"Yes, because the trade usage will control the interpretation of \"daily service.\""},"answer":"A","explanation":"A misunderstanding occurs when the parties to a contract attach different meanings to the same term. If the misunderstanding involves a material term, most often there is no contract because there is no meeting of the minds. But if one party knows or has reason to know about the misunderstanding, then a valid contract will be formed based on the meaning of the term as understood by the unknowing party. Here, the laundry company agreed to provide \"daily service\" to the innkeeper's motel. The innkeeper included Sundays in \"daily service\" but the laundry company—per trade usage in the commercial laundry business—did not, so the parties attached different meanings to the term. Since the laundry company knew that the innkeeper expected laundry services seven days a week, including Sundays, the innkeeper's understanding of \"daily service\" will control. Therefore, the laundry company is unlikely to succeed (Choice B). (Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. However, parol evidence is admissible to establish an ambiguous term—eg, when, as here, a term is subject to a misunderstanding. (Choice D) Trade usage can be used to show that a word or phrase has a particular meaning—eg, that \"daily service\" does not include Sundays in the commercial laundry business. But trade usage will not control when, as here, a party knows that the other party attaches a different meaning to the term.\n\nEducational objective: When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (whose meaning prevails when a misunderstanding occurs).","educationalObjective":"When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 201 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (whose meaning prevails when a misunderstanding occurs).","rule":"When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.","irac":{"issue":"Is the laundry company likely to succeed in its action?","rule":"When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.","analysis":"A misunderstanding occurs when the parties to a contract attach different meanings to the same term. If the misunderstanding involves a material term, most often there is no contract because there is no meeting of the minds. But if one party knows or has reason to know about the misunderstanding, then a valid contract will be formed based on the meaning of the term as understood by the unknowing party. Here, the laundry company agreed to provide \"daily service\" to the innkeeper's motel. The innkeeper included Sundays in \"daily service\" but the laundry company—per trade usage in the commercial laundry business—did not, so the parties attached different meanings to the term. Since the laundry company knew that the innkeeper expected laundry services seven days a week, including Sundays, the innkeeper's understanding of \"daily service\" will control. Therefore, the laundry company is unlikely to succeed (Choice B). (Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. However, parol evidence is admissible to establish an ambiguous term—eg, when, as here, a term is subject to a misunderstanding. (Choice D) Trade usage can be used to show that a word or phrase has a particular meaning—eg, that \"daily service\" does not include Sundays in the commercial laundry business. But trade usage will not control when, as here, a party knows that the other party attaches a different meaning to the term.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the laundry company knew the meaning the innkeeper attached to \"daily service,\" and therefore the innkeeper's meaning will control."},"questionZh":"一位旅馆老板以前没有汽车旅馆或商业洗衣业务的经验，而且对这两种业务的商业用途一无所知，他购买了一家汽车旅馆，并与一家洗衣公司签署了汽车旅馆洗衣服务的协议。该协议期限为一年，并规定“每周 500 美元的日常服务”。从谈判期间的谈话中，洗衣公司老板知道旅馆老板希望每周 7 天提供洗衣服务。当洗衣公司连续两个周日拒绝收取汽车旅馆的衣物并表示永远不会这样做时，旅馆老板取消了协议。洗衣公司起诉旅馆老板违反合同。在审判中，提出了明确的证据表明，在商业洗衣业务中，“日常服务”不包括周日服务。洗衣公司的行动有可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为洗衣公司知道客栈老板对“日常服务”的含义，因此客栈老板的意思将占主导地位。","B":"不能，因为双方对“日常服务”的理解存在重大差异，因此没有形成合同。","C":"是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不允许旅馆老板证明他所赋予的“日常服务”的含义。","D":"是的，因为贸易用途将控制“日常服务”的解释。"},"explanationZh":"当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。如果误解涉及实质性条款，则通常不会签订合同，因为没有达成共识。但如果一方当事人知道或有理由知道该误解，则根据不知情方所理解的该术语的含义，将形成有效的合同。在这里，洗衣公司同意向旅馆老板的汽车旅馆提供“日常服务”。旅馆老板将星期日纳入“日常服务”，但洗衣公司（商业洗衣业务中的按交易用途）却没有，因此双方对该术语赋予了不同的含义。由于洗衣公司知道旅馆老板希望每周 7 天（包括周日）提供洗衣服务，因此旅馆老板对“日常服务”的理解将起主导作用。因此，洗衣公司成功的可能性不大（选择B）。 （选择 C） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前口头或书面协议的证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款。然而，口头证据可以用来建立一个不明确的术语——例如，当一个术语容易被误解时，就像这里一样。 （选项D） 行业用法可用于表明某个单词或短语具有特定含义，例如，“日常服务”不包括商业洗衣业务中的周日。但当一方知道另一方对该术语赋予不同的含义时，贸易用法将不受控制，就像这里一样。\n\n教育目标：当合同一方知道或有理由知道另一方对某个重要术语赋予不同的含义时，则不知情的一方对该术语的含义将占主导地位。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 201（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（发生误解时以其含义为准）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当合同一方知道或有理由知道另一方对某一重要术语赋予不同的含义时，则以不知情的一方对该术语的含义为准。","iracZh":{"issue":"洗衣公司的行动有可能成功吗？","rule":"当合同一方知道或有理由知道另一方对某一重要术语赋予不同的含义时，则以不知情的一方对该术语的含义为准。","analysis":"当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。如果误解涉及实质性条款，则通常不会签订合同，因为没有达成共识。但如果一方当事人知道或有理由知道该误解，则根据不知情方所理解的该术语的含义，将形成有效的合同。在这里，洗衣公司同意向旅馆老板的汽车旅馆提供“日常服务”。旅馆老板将星期日纳入“日常服务”，但洗衣公司（商业洗衣业务中的按交易用途）却没有，因此双方对该术语赋予了不同的含义。由于洗衣公司知道旅馆老板希望每周 7 天（包括周日）提供洗衣服务，因此旅馆老板对“日常服务”的理解将起主导作用。因此，洗衣公司成功的可能性不大（选择B）。 （选择 C） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前口头或书面协议的证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款。然而，口头证据可以用来建立一个不明确的术语——例如，当一个术语容易被误解时，就像这里一样。 （选项D） 行业用法可用于表明某个单词或短语具有特定含义，例如，“日常服务”不包括商业洗衣业务中的周日。但当一方知道另一方对该术语赋予不同的含义时，贸易用法将不受控制，就像这里一样。\n\n教育目标：当合同一方知道或有理由知道另一方对某个重要术语赋予不同的含义时，则不知情的一方对该术语的含义将占主导地位。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 201（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（发生误解时以其含义为准）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为洗衣公司知道客栈老板对“日常服务”的含义，因此客栈老板的意思将占主导地位。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"parol evidence rule","ipa":"/pə'rəul ˈɛvədəns ɹˈul/","syllables":"parol ev·i·dence rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"口头证据规则：限制用先前或同时陈述改变最终书面合同","contextualMeaning":"口头证据规则：限制用先前或同时陈述改变最终书面合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule will not permit the innkeeper to prove the meaning he attached to \"daily service.","synonyms":["prior-statements rule","extrinsic-evidence limitation"]},{"term":"different meaning","ipa":"/dˈɪfɝənt mˈinɪŋ/","syllables":"dif·fer·ent mean·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不同含义：双方对同一合同用语作出不同合理理解","contextualMeaning":"不同含义：双方对同一合同用语作出不同合理理解","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the parties attached materially different meanings to \"daily service,\" and therefore no contract was formed.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"misunderstanding","ipa":"/mˌɪsʌndɝstˈændɪŋ/","syllables":"mis·un·der·stand·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 误会, 误解；[法] 误解, 误会, 不和","contextualMeaning":"误解：双方对重要合同用语赋予不同含义","wordForm":"词；原形 misunderstand；词形 s:misunderstandings/i:misunderstanding/0:misunderstanding/1:i","sourceSentence":"A misunderstanding occurs when the parties to a contract attach different meanings to the same term.","synonyms":["misconstrue","misinterpret","misconceive","misapprehend"]},{"term":"unknowing party","ipa":"/ʌnnˈoʊɪŋ pˈɑɹti/","syllables":"un·know·ing party","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不知情方：不知道且无理由知道双方理解不同的一方","contextualMeaning":"不知情方：不知道且无理由知道双方理解不同的一方","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"\" When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"material term","ipa":"/mʌtˈɪɹiəl tˈɝm/","syllables":"ma·te·r·ial term","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"重要条款：足以影响合意或履行核心内容的合同用语","contextualMeaning":"重要条款：足以影响合意或履行核心内容的合同用语","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"\" When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"knowing party","ipa":"/nˈoʊɪŋ pˈɑɹti/","syllables":"know·ing party","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"知情方：知道或应知道对方赋予条款不同含义的一方","contextualMeaning":"知情方：知道或应知道对方赋予条款不同含义的一方","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"\" When one party to a contract knows or has reason to know that the other party attaches a different meaning to a material term, then the unknowing party's meaning of the term will control.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"trade usage","ipa":"/tɹˈeɪd jˈusədʒ/","syllables":"trade us·age","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"行业惯例：特定行业经常遵循并可用于解释合同的做法","contextualMeaning":"行业惯例：特定行业经常遵循并可用于解释合同的做法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"An innkeeper, who had no previous experience in the motel or commercial laundry business and who knew nothing about the trade usages of either business, bought a motel and signed an agreement with a laundry company for the motel's laundry services.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"breach","ipa":"/bɹˈitʃ/","syllables":"breach","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 裂口, 违背, 破坏, 违反, 突破, 破裂；vt. 攻破, 突破；vi. 跳出水面","contextualMeaning":"违约：未履行到期合同义务且无合法抗辩","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:breaches/d:breached/i:breaching/p:breached/3:breaches","sourceSentence":"The laundry company sued the innkeeper for breach of contract.","synonyms":["contract violation","nonperformance"]},{"term":"negotiation","ipa":"/nɪɡˌoʊʃiˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ne·go·ti·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 谈判, 磋商, 交涉；[经] 谈判, 协商","contextualMeaning":"合同谈判：形成合同前就条款进行协商","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:negotiations","sourceSentence":"\" From their conversations during negotiation, the laundry company owner knew that the innkeeper expected laundry services seven days a week.","synonyms":["dialogue","talks"]},{"term":"modify","ipa":"/mˈɑdʌfˌaɪ/","syllables":"mod·ify","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 修正, 变更, 修饰, 缓和, 减轻；vi. 被修改；[计] 修改","contextualMeaning":"变更合同：通过合意改变既有合同义务","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:modified/i:modifying/p:modified/3:modifies","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement.","synonyms":["change","alter","qualify"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-322d1f1239d2d4a2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":2,"sourceQuestionPage":4,"sourceQuestionPages":[4],"sourceExplanationPages":[5,6],"sourceAnswerPages":[5,6],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-003","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Capacity & Ratification","question":"A 16-year-old girl received $3,000 on her birthday to buy a car. Later that week, the girl noticed an old car parked in her neighbor's driveway. A sign posted on the car's windshield read: \"For Sale! $5,000 O.B.O.\" The girl contacted the neighbor about purchasing the car for $5,000. He agreed to sell it to her for a $1,000 down payment and monthly payments of $75 until the car was paid off. The girl made regular payments until she turned 18 years old. Soon after turning 18, the girl attempted to pay off the car. The neighbor rejected the girl's lump-sum payment, returned all of her prior payments, and took possession of the car. If the girl sues the neighbor for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the girl was a minor when she agreed to buy the car, so the contract was void.","B":"No, because the neighbor voided the contract by returning the girl's prior payments.","C":"Yes, because the contract ceased to be voidable by either party once the girl turned 18 years old.","D":"Yes, because the girl ratified the contract after she reached the age of majority."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Unmarried, unemancipated minors (ie, persons under the age of 18) lack capacity to contract because of their infancy. As a result, any contract entered by a minor is voidable by the minor.* This means that the minor can either:\n• void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming the contract through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under the contract or\n\n• ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) otherwise agreeing to be bound by it—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.\n\nThe contract becomes enforceable if the minor ratifies it. Here, the girl was a 16-year-old minor when she contracted to buy the neighbor's car for $5,000. The neighbor tried to disaffirm the contract after the girl turned 18 by returning her prior payments and taking possession of the car. However, the contract was voidable only by the girl (Choices B & C). And since she ratified the contract soon after turning 18 by attempting to pay off the car, the contract was enforceable. Therefore, the girl will likely prevail in a suit against the neighbor for breach of contract. *By statute in some jurisdictions, certain contracts entered by a minor (eg, student loan agreements, insurance contracts) are not voidable by the minor but instead are fully enforceable by the other contracting party. (Choice A) A void contract is invalid upon creation. But a contract entered by a minor is merely voidable, meaning that it is valid until the minor takes steps to avoid it.\n\nEducational objective: Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity based on infancy).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 85 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promise to perform voidable duty).","educationalObjective":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity based on infancy).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 85 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promise to perform voidable duty).","rule":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the girl sues the neighbor for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","rule":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","analysis":"Unmarried, unemancipated minors (ie, persons under the age of 18) lack capacity to contract because of their infancy. As a result, any contract entered by a minor is voidable by the minor.* This means that the minor can either:\n• void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming the contract through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under the contract or\n\n• ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) otherwise agreeing to be bound by it—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.\n\nThe contract becomes enforceable if the minor ratifies it. Here, the girl was a 16-year-old minor when she contracted to buy the neighbor's car for $5,000. The neighbor tried to disaffirm the contract after the girl turned 18 by returning her prior payments and taking possession of the car. However, the contract was voidable only by the girl (Choices B & C). And since she ratified the contract soon after turning 18 by attempting to pay off the car, the contract was enforceable. Therefore, the girl will likely prevail in a suit against the neighbor for breach of contract. *By statute in some jurisdictions, certain contracts entered by a minor (eg, student loan agreements, insurance contracts) are not voidable by the minor but instead are fully enforceable by the other contracting party. (Choice A) A void contract is invalid upon creation. But a contract entered by a minor is merely voidable, meaning that it is valid until the minor takes steps to avoid it.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the girl ratified the contract after she reached the age of majority."},"questionZh":"一名 16 岁女孩在生日那天收到了 3000 美元，用于购买一辆车。那周晚些时候，女孩注意到邻居的车道上停着一辆旧车。汽车挡风玻璃上贴着一块牌子，上面写着：“出售！$5,000 O.B.O。”女孩联系了邻居，决定以 5000 美元购买这辆车。他同意以 1,000 美元的首付和每月 75 美元的付款将其卖给她，直到车还清。该女孩定期付款直至年满 18 岁。 18 岁生日后不久，女孩就试图还清车贷。邻居拒绝了女孩的一次性付款，退还了她之前的所有付款，并占有了这辆车。如果女孩起诉邻居违约，她有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为女孩同意买车时还未成年，所以合同无效。","B":"不，因为邻居退还了女孩之前的付款，从而使合同无效。","C":"是的，因为一旦女孩年满 18 岁，合同就不再可以被任何一方撤销。","D":"是的，因为女孩在达到成年年龄后才追认了合同。"},"explanationZh":"未婚、没有独立生活的未成年人（即18岁以下的人）由于其婴儿期而缺乏契约能力。因此，未成年人签订的任何合同均可被未成年人撤销。*这意味着未成年人可以：\n• 在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久，通过以下方式使合同无效：(1) 通过言语或行动否定合同，以及 (2) 退还根据合同收到的任何仍然存在的福利，或\n\n• 年满 18 岁后，通过以下方式追认合同：(1) 未能及时使合同无效，或 (2) 以其他方式同意受合同约束——例如，试图履行合同或根据合同付款。\n\n如果未成年人批准，合同即可强制执行。在这里，女孩在签订合同以 5,000 美元购买邻居的汽车时，还是一名 16 岁的未成年人。女孩年满 18 岁后，邻居试图通过退还她之前的付款并占有汽车来取消合同。然而，只有女孩才能撤销合同（选择 B 和 C）。由于她在年满 18 岁后不久就试图还清汽车贷款，从而批准了该合同，因此该合同具有可执行性。因此，女孩以违约罪起诉邻居，很可能会胜诉。 *根据某些司法管辖区的法规，未成年人签订的某些合同（例如学生贷款协议、保险合同）不可被未成年人撤销，而是可由另一缔约方完全执行。 （选项A）撤销合同一经成立即无效。但未成年人签订的合同只是可撤销的，这意味着在未成年人采取措施避免该合同之前，该合同一直有效。\n\n教育目标：未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此签订合同的未成年人可以 (1) 在 18 岁之前或之后不久撤销合同，或 (2) 在 18 岁之后追认合同，例如，通过尝试履行合同或根据合同付款。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于婴儿期的无行为能力）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 85（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（承诺履行可撤销义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此签订合同的未成年人可以 (1) 在 18 岁之前或之后不久撤销合同，或 (2) 在 18 岁之后追认合同，例如，尝试履行合同或根据合同付款。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果女孩起诉邻居违约，她有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此签订合同的未成年人可以 (1) 在 18 岁之前或之后不久撤销合同，或 (2) 在 18 岁之后追认合同，例如，尝试履行合同或根据合同付款。","analysis":"未婚、没有独立生活的未成年人（即18岁以下的人）由于其婴儿期而缺乏契约能力。因此，未成年人签订的任何合同均可被未成年人撤销。*这意味着未成年人可以：\n• 在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久，通过以下方式使合同无效：(1) 通过言语或行动否定合同，以及 (2) 退还根据合同收到的任何仍然存在的福利，或\n\n• 年满 18 岁后，通过以下方式追认合同：(1) 未能及时使合同无效，或 (2) 以其他方式同意受合同约束——例如，试图履行合同或根据合同付款。\n\n如果未成年人批准，合同即可强制执行。在这里，女孩在签订合同以 5,000 美元购买邻居的汽车时，还是一名 16 岁的未成年人。女孩年满 18 岁后，邻居试图通过退还她之前的付款并占有汽车来取消合同。然而，只有女孩才能撤销合同（选择 B 和 C）。由于她在年满 18 岁后不久就试图还清汽车贷款，从而批准了该合同，因此该合同具有可执行性。因此，女孩以违约罪起诉邻居，很可能会胜诉。 *根据某些司法管辖区的法规，未成年人签订的某些合同（例如学生贷款协议、保险合同）不可被未成年人撤销，而是可由另一缔约方完全执行。 （选项A）撤销合同一经成立即无效。但未成年人签订的合同只是可撤销的，这意味着在未成年人采取措施避免该合同之前，该合同一直有效。\n\n教育目标：未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此签订合同的未成年人可以 (1) 在 18 岁之前或之后不久撤销合同，或 (2) 在 18 岁之后追认合同，例如，通过尝试履行合同或根据合同付款。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于婴儿期的无行为能力）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 85（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（承诺履行可撤销义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为女孩在达到成年年龄后才追认了合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"capacity to contract","ipa":"/kʌpˈæsəti tˈu kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"ca·pac·ity to con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"缔约能力：当事人承担合同义务的法律能力","contextualMeaning":"缔约能力：当事人承担合同义务的法律能力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","synonyms":["contractual capacity"]},{"term":"age of majority","ipa":"/ˈeɪdʒ ˈəv mʌdʒˈɔɹəti/","syllables":"age of ma·jor·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"成年年龄：取得完整一般缔约能力的法定年龄","contextualMeaning":"成年年龄：取得完整一般缔约能力的法定年龄","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the girl ratified the contract after she reached the age of majority.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"performance","ipa":"/pɝfˈɔɹməns/","syllables":"per·for·mance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 施行, 工作情况, 成绩, 行为, 表现, 演出；[电] 绩效, 性能","contextualMeaning":"履行：完成合同要求的允诺或行为","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:performances","sourceSentence":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","synonyms":["execution","carrying out","carrying into action","public presentation"]},{"term":"disaffirm","ipa":"/.disə'fə:m/","syllables":"dis·af·firm","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"vt. 废弃, 否认, 取消；[法] 撤销以往的判决, 否认, 取消","contextualMeaning":"否认合同：依法拒绝受可撤销合同约束","wordForm":"动词","sourceSentence":"* This means that the minor can either: • void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming the contract through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under the contract or • ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) otherwise agreeing to be bound by it—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the cont","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"ratify","ipa":"/ɹˈætʌfˌaɪ/","syllables":"rat·ify","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 批准, 认可；[经] 证实, 肯定, 确认","contextualMeaning":"追认：确认原本可撤销的合同并同意继续受其约束","wordForm":"词；词形 d:ratified/p:ratified/i:ratifying/3:ratifies","sourceSentence":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","synonyms":["affirm","confirm"]},{"term":"statute","ipa":"/stˈætʃut/","syllables":"statute","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 法令, 成文法律, 法规, 章程, 条例；[法] 法令, 法规, 成文法","contextualMeaning":"成文法：由立法机关制定的法律规则","wordForm":"词；词形 s:statutes","sourceSentence":"*By statute in some jurisdictions, certain contracts entered by a minor (eg, student loan agreements, insurance contracts) are not voidable by the minor but instead are fully enforceable by the other contracting party.","synonyms":["legislative act","codified"]},{"term":"minors","ipa":"/mˈaɪnɝz/","syllables":"mi·nors","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 未成年人（minor的复数）","contextualMeaning":"未成年人：尚未达到法定成年年龄的缔约人","wordForm":"词；词形 0:minor/1:s","sourceSentence":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a minor who enters a contract can either (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify the contract after turning 18—eg, by attempting to render performance of or payment under the contract.","synonyms":["minor league","bush league"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-53e7aed7cf41638a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":3,"sourceQuestionPage":7,"sourceQuestionPages":[7],"sourceExplanationPages":[8,9],"sourceAnswerPages":[8,9],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-004","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A computer retail outlet contracted to service a bank's computer equipment for one year at a fixed monthly fee under a contract that was silent as to assignment or delegation by either party. Three months later, the retail outlet sold the service portion of its business to an experienced and well-financed computer service company. The only provision in the agreement between the retail outlet and the computer service company relating to the outlet's contract with the bank stated that the outlet \"hereby assigns all of its computer service contracts to the computer service company.\" The computer service company performed the monthly maintenance required under the service contract. Its performance was defective, however, and caused damage to the bank's operations. Whom can the bank sue for damages arising from the computer service company's defective performance?","choices":{"A":"Either the retail outlet or the computer service company, because since each has the right to enforce the bank's performance of its contract with the retail outlet, mutuality of remedy renders either potentially liable for the defective performance.","B":"Either the retail outlet or the computer service company, because the bank has not released the outlet and the bank is an intended beneficiary of the outlet's agreement with the computer service company.","C":"The computer service company only, because it is a qualified and a financially responsible supplier of computer services.","D":"The retail outlet only, because the computer service company made no promises to the bank."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contractual duties can be delegated by a party (ie, the promisor) to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee). An assignment of the entire contract will be construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties. But even after a delegation of duties, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties. Here, the computer retail outlet and the bank entered a service contract that was silent as to assignment or delegation by either party. The retail outlet assigned the entire contract (ie, rights and duties) to a computer service company, but the bank did not release the retail outlet from its duties. So even though the computer service company was qualified and financially responsible, the bank can sue the retail outlet for damages arising from the computer service company's defective performance (Choice C). When, as here, the promisor delegates his/her duties by forming a separate contract with the delegatee, the promisee of the original contract becomes an intended beneficiary of that separate contract. This allows the promisee to sue the delegatee (in addition to the promisor) in the event of a breach. The bank, as the intended beneficiary of the agreement between the retail outlet and the computer service company, can therefore sue the computer service company for damages—even though it made no promises to the bank (Choice D). (Choice A) Under the mutuality-of-remedy doctrine, neither party to a contract can obtain specific performance unless that remedy is available to both parties. But this doctrine is irrelevant to determining whom the bank can sue for damages.\n\nEducational objective: A party to a contract (ie, the promisor) can delegate his/her duties to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee). But even after this occurs, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that delegation of a contractual duty does not discharge the promisor's liability for breach).","educationalObjective":"A party to a contract (ie, the promisor) can delegate his/her duties to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee). But even after this occurs, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that delegation of a contractual duty does not discharge the promisor's liability for breach).","rule":"A party to a contract (ie, the promisor) can delegate his/her duties to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee). But even after this occurs, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties.","irac":{"issue":"Whom can the bank sue for damages arising from the computer service company's defective performance?","rule":"A party to a contract (ie, the promisor) can delegate his/her duties to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee). But even after this occurs, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties.","analysis":"Contractual duties can be delegated by a party (ie, the promisor) to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee). An assignment of the entire contract will be construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties. But even after a delegation of duties, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties. Here, the computer retail outlet and the bank entered a service contract that was silent as to assignment or delegation by either party. The retail outlet assigned the entire contract (ie, rights and duties) to a computer service company, but the bank did not release the retail outlet from its duties. So even though the computer service company was qualified and financially responsible, the bank can sue the retail outlet for damages arising from the computer service company's defective performance (Choice C). When, as here, the promisor delegates his/her duties by forming a separate contract with the delegatee, the promisee of the original contract becomes an intended beneficiary of that separate contract. This allows the promisee to sue the delegatee (in addition to the promisor) in the event of a breach. The bank, as the intended beneficiary of the agreement between the retail outlet and the computer service company, can therefore sue the computer service company for damages—even though it made no promises to the bank (Choice D). (Choice A) Under the mutuality-of-remedy doctrine, neither party to a contract can obtain specific performance unless that remedy is available to both parties. But this doctrine is irrelevant to determining whom the bank can sue for damages.","conclusion":"Answer B: Either the retail outlet or the computer service company, because the bank has not released the outlet and the bank is an intended beneficiary of the outlet's agreement with the computer service company."},"questionZh":"一家计算机零售店签订了一份合同，以固定的月费为银行的计算机设备提供一年的服务，合同中没有提及任何一方的转让或委托。三个月后，零售店将其业务的服务部分出售给一家经验丰富且资金雄厚的计算机服务公司。零售网点与计算机服务公司之间的协议中有关网点与银行合同的唯一条款规定，网点“特此将其所有计算机服务合同转让给计算机服务公司”。计算机服务公司根据服务合同进行了每月的维护。然而，其表现存在缺陷，并对银行的运营造成了损害。银行可以向谁起诉因计算机服务公司的履行瑕疵而造成的损失？","choicesZh":{"A":"无论是零售店还是计算机服务公司，因为由于各自都有权强制银行履行其与零售店的合同，因此相互救济使得任何一方都可能对有缺陷的履行承担责任。","B":"要么是零售网点，要么是计算机服务公司，因为银行尚未放行网点，而银行是网点与计算机服务公司协议的预期受益人。","C":"仅限计算机服务公司，因为它是合格且对财务负责的计算机服务供应商。","D":"仅限零售店，因为计算机服务公司没有向银行做出任何承诺。"},"explanationZh":"合同义务可以由当事人（即承诺人）委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。整个合同的转让将被解释为权利转让和义务委托。但即使在委托职责之后，如果受托人未能履行义务，承诺人仍然要承担违约责任，除非受诺人同意解除承诺人的职责。在这里，计算机零售店和银行签订了一份服务合同，其中没有提及任何一方的转让或委托。零售店将整个合同（即权利和义务）转让给一家计算机服务公司，但银行并没有免除零售店的职责。因此，即使计算机服务公司有资格并承担财务责任，银行也可以起诉零售店，要求其因计算机服务公司的履行瑕疵而造成损失（选项C）。当承诺人通过与受托人签订单独的合同来委托其职责时，原合同的受诺人就成为该单独合同的预期受益人。这使得受诺人可以在违约时起诉受委托人（除了承诺人之外）。因此，银行作为零售店与计算机服务公司之间协议的预期受益人，可以起诉计算机服务公司，要求损害赔偿——即使该公司没有向银行做出任何承诺（选项 D）。 （选择A）根据补救措施的相互原则，除非双方均可获得该补救措施，否则合同任何一方都无法获得具体履行。但这一原则与确定银行可以向谁提起损害赔偿诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标：合同当事人（即承诺人）可以将其职责委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。但即使在这种情况发生之后，如果受托人未能履行承诺，承诺人仍然要承担违约责任，除非受诺人同意免除承诺人的职责。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释合同义务的委托并不解除承诺人的违约责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同当事人（即承诺人）可以将其职责委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。但即使在这种情况发生之后，如果受托人未能履行承诺，承诺人仍然要承担违约责任，除非受诺人同意免除承诺人的职责。","iracZh":{"issue":"银行可以向谁起诉因计算机服务公司的履行瑕疵而造成的损失？","rule":"合同当事人（即承诺人）可以将其职责委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。但即使在这种情况发生之后，如果受托人未能履行承诺，承诺人仍然要承担违约责任，除非受诺人同意免除承诺人的职责。","analysis":"合同义务可以由当事人（即承诺人）委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。整个合同的转让将被解释为权利转让和义务委托。但即使在委托职责之后，如果受托人未能履行义务，承诺人仍然要承担违约责任，除非受诺人同意解除承诺人的职责。在这里，计算机零售店和银行签订了一份服务合同，其中没有提及任何一方的转让或委托。零售店将整个合同（即权利和义务）转让给一家计算机服务公司，但银行并没有免除零售店的职责。因此，即使计算机服务公司有资格并承担财务责任，银行也可以起诉零售店，要求其因计算机服务公司的履行瑕疵而造成损失（选项C）。当承诺人通过与受托人签订单独的合同来委托其职责时，原合同的受诺人就成为该单独合同的预期受益人。这使得受诺人可以在违约时起诉受委托人（除了承诺人之外）。因此，银行作为零售店与计算机服务公司之间协议的预期受益人，可以起诉计算机服务公司，要求损害赔偿——即使该公司没有向银行做出任何承诺（选项 D）。 （选择A）根据补救措施的相互原则，除非双方均可获得该补救措施，否则合同任何一方都无法获得具体履行。但这一原则与确定银行可以向谁提起损害赔偿诉讼无关。\n\n教育目标：合同当事人（即承诺人）可以将其职责委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。但即使在这种情况发生之后，如果受托人未能履行承诺，承诺人仍然要承担违约责任，除非受诺人同意免除承诺人的职责。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释合同义务的委托并不解除承诺人的违约责任）。","conclusion":"答案 B：要么是零售网点，要么是计算机服务公司，因为银行尚未放行网点，而银行是网点与计算机服务公司协议的预期受益人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"intended beneficiary","ipa":"/ˌɪntˈɛndɪd bˌɛnʌfˈɪʃiˌɛɹi/","syllables":"in·tended ben·e·fi·ciary","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"预期受益人：缔约方有意赋予可执行利益的第三人","contextualMeaning":"预期受益人：缔约方有意赋予可执行利益的第三人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Either the retail outlet or the computer service company, because the bank has not released the outlet and the bank is an intended beneficiary of the outlet's agreement with the computer service company.","synonyms":["intended third-party beneficiary"]},{"term":"assignment of rights","ipa":"/ʌsˈaɪnmənt ˈəv ɹˈaɪts/","syllables":"as·sign·ment of rights","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"权利让与：将合同权利转移给受让人","contextualMeaning":"权利让与：将合同权利转移给受让人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"An assignment of the entire contract will be construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties.","synonyms":["transfer of rights"]},{"term":"delegation of duties","ipa":"/dˌɛlʌɡˈeɪʃən ˈəv dˈutiz/","syllables":"del·e·ga·tion of du·ties","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"义务委托：安排第三人履行合同义务","contextualMeaning":"义务委托：安排第三人履行合同义务","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"An assignment of the entire contract will be construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties.","synonyms":["transfer of performance duties"]},{"term":"specific performance","ipa":"/spʌsˈɪfɪk pɝfˈɔɹməns/","syllables":"spe·cific per·for·mance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"特定履行：命令违约方实际完成独特标的的衡平救济","contextualMeaning":"特定履行：命令违约方实际完成独特标的的衡平救济","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Under the mutuality-of-remedy doctrine, neither party to a contract can obtain specific performance unless that remedy is available to both parties.","synonyms":["equitable performance decree"]},{"term":"promises","ipa":"/pɹˈɑməsəz/","syllables":"promises","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 允诺","contextualMeaning":"允诺：表示将实施或不实施某行为的合同承诺","wordForm":"词；原形 promise；词形 0:promise/1:s3","sourceSentence":"The retail outlet only, because the computer service company made no promises to the bank.","synonyms":["undertaking","commitment"]},{"term":"promisor","ipa":"/.prɒmi'sɒ:/","syllables":"promisor","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 契约者；[经] 立约人(出票人)","contextualMeaning":"允诺人：作出合同允诺的一方","wordForm":"词；词形 s:promisors","sourceSentence":"A party to a contract (ie, the promisor) can delegate his/her duties to a nonparty (ie, the delegatee).","synonyms":["promiser"]},{"term":"promisee","ipa":"/.prɒmi'si:/","syllables":"promisee","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 受约人；[经] 受约人","contextualMeaning":"受诺人：允诺所指向、享有请求权的一方","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:promisees","sourceSentence":"But even after this occurs, the promisor remains liable for breach if the delegatee fails to perform—unless the promisee agrees to release the promisor from his/her duties.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-a4f77e0a8f2e5c53"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":4,"sourceQuestionPage":10,"sourceQuestionPages":[10],"sourceExplanationPages":[11,12],"sourceAnswerPages":[11,12],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-005","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"On January 5, a creditor loaned $1,000 to a debtor under a contract calling for the debtor to repay the loan at the rate of $100 per month payable on the first day of each month. On February 1, at the debtor's request, the creditor agreed to permit payment on February 5. On March 1, the debtor requested a similar time extension and the creditor replied, \"Don't bother me each month. Just change the date of payment to the fifth of the month. But you must now make the payments by cashier's check.\" The debtor said, \"Okay,\" and made payments on March 5 and April 5 by cashier's check. On April 6, the creditor sold the loan contract to a bank but did not tell the bank about the agreement permitting payments on the fifth of the month. On April 6, the bank wrote to the debtor: \"Your debt to [the creditor] has been assigned to us. We hereby inform you that all payments must be made on the first day of the month.\" Can the debtor justifiably insist that the payment date for the rest of the installments is the fifth of each month?","choices":{"A":"No, because a contract modification is not binding on an assignee who had no knowledge of the modification.","B":"No, because although the creditor waived the condition of payment on the first of the month, the bank reinstated it.","C":"Yes, because although the creditor waived the condition of payment on the first of the month, the creditor could not assign to the bank his right to reinstate that condition.","D":"Yes, because the creditor could assign to the bank only those rights the creditor had in the contract at the time of the assignment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract An assignment is the transfer of rights under a contract to another (ie, assignee). The assignee takes all of the rights of the assignor as the contract stands at the time of the assignment. Here, the contract between the debtor and the creditor called for the debtor to make payments on the first day of each month. The parties effectively modified that contract when the creditor promised to accept payments on the fifth day of each month in exchange for the debtor's promise to make the payments by cashier's check.* Since this occurred before the creditor assigned his rights under the loan contract to the bank, the debtor can justifiably insist that the payment date for the rest of the installments is the fifth of each month. *Note that there was consideration for the creditor's promise to accept payment at a later date (and the modification was effective) because the debtor altered his preexisting duty to pay the debt by promising to make future payments by cashier's check. (Choice A) There is no rule that makes a contract modification ineffective as to an assignee simply because the assignee had no notice of it. As such, the assignee (here, the bank) is bound by the modification. (Choices B & C) It is true that if a party waives a condition, that party may reinstate the condition with respect to future acts of performance. However, that rule is not applicable\n\nhere because the parties modified the contract by mutual agreement and the opportunity to reinstate a condition was no longer available.\n\nEducational objective: An assignee (ie, one to whom rights under a contract are transferred) takes all of the assignor's rights as the contract stands at the time of the assignment.","educationalObjective":"An assignee (ie, one to whom rights under a contract are transferred) takes all of the assignor's rights as the contract stands at the time of the assignment.","references":"","rule":"An assignee (ie, one to whom rights under a contract are transferred) takes all of the assignor's rights as the contract stands at the time of the assignment.","irac":{"issue":"On January 5, a creditor loaned $1,000 to a debtor under a contract calling for the debtor to repay the loan at the rate of $100 per month payable on the first day of each month. On February 1, at the debtor's request, the creditor agreed to permit payment on February 5. On March 1, the debtor requested a similar time extension and the creditor replied, \"Don't bother me each month. Just change the date of payment to the fifth of the month. But you must now make the payments by cashier's check.\" The debtor said, \"Okay,\" and made payments on March 5 and April 5 by cashier's check. On April 6, the creditor sold the loan contract to a bank but did not tell the bank about the agreement permitting payments on the fifth of the month. On April 6, the bank wrote to the debtor: \"Your debt to [the creditor] has been assigned to us. We hereby inform you that all payments must be made on the first day of the month.\" Can the debtor justifiably insist that the payment date for the rest of the installments is the fifth of each month?","rule":"An assignee (ie, one to whom rights under a contract are transferred) takes all of the assignor's rights as the contract stands at the time of the assignment.","analysis":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract An assignment is the transfer of rights under a contract to another (ie, assignee). The assignee takes all of the rights of the assignor as the contract stands at the time of the assignment. Here, the contract between the debtor and the creditor called for the debtor to make payments on the first day of each month. The parties effectively modified that contract when the creditor promised to accept payments on the fifth day of each month in exchange for the debtor's promise to make the payments by cashier's check.* Since this occurred before the creditor assigned his rights under the loan contract to the bank, the debtor can justifiably insist that the payment date for the rest of the installments is the fifth of each month. *Note that there was consideration for the creditor's promise to accept payment at a later date (and the modification was effective) because the debtor altered his preexisting duty to pay the debt by promising to make future payments by cashier's check. (Choice A) There is no rule that makes a contract modification ineffective as to an assignee simply because the assignee had no notice of it. As such, the assignee (here, the bank) is bound by the modification. (Choices B & C) It is true that if a party waives a condition, that party may reinstate the condition with respect to future acts of performance. However, that rule is not applicable\n\nhere because the parties modified the contract by mutual agreement and the opportunity to reinstate a condition was no longer available.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the creditor could assign to the bank only those rights the creditor had in the contract at the time of the assignment."},"questionZh":"1 月 5 日，债权人根据合同向债务人借了 1,000 美元，要求债务人按每月 100 美元的利率偿还贷款，并于每月第一天偿还。 2月1日，应债务人的要求，债权人同意允许在2月5日付款。3月1日，债务人要求类似的延期，债权人回复说：“每个月不要打扰我，把付款日期改到当月五号就可以了。但现在必须用银行本票付款。”债务人说：“好的。”并于3月5日和4月5日用银行本票付款。 4月6日，债权人将贷款合同出售给银行，但没有告知银行有关允许5日还款的协议。 4月6日，银行写信给债务人：“您对[债权人]的债务已转让给我们。我们特此通知您，所有付款必须在当月的第一天支付。”债务人是否可以合理地坚持剩余分期付款的付款日期是每月的五号？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合同修改对不知情的受让人不具有约束力。","B":"不，因为虽然债权人在月初放弃了付款条件，但银行恢复了付款条件。","C":"是的，因为虽然债权人在当月的第一天放弃了付款条件，但债权人不能将恢复该条件的权利转让给银行。","D":"是的，因为债权人只能将债权人在转让时在合同中拥有的那些权利转让给银行。"},"explanationZh":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 转让是将合同项下的权利转让给另一人（即受让人）。受让人享有转让时合同规定的转让人的所有权利。在此，债务人和债权人之间的合同要求债务人在每月的第一天付款。当债权人承诺在每月的第五天接受付款，以换取债务人以银行本票付款的承诺时，双方实际上修改了该合同。*由于这发生在债权人将其在贷款合同下的权利转让给银行之前，债务人可以有理由坚持剩余分期付款的付款日期是每月的第五天。 *请注意，债权人承诺稍后接受付款（并且修改生效）是考虑在内的，因为债务人通过承诺通过银行本票支付未来付款来改变其先前存在的偿还债务的义务。 （选项A） 没有规定仅仅因为受让人没有通知就使合同变更对受让人无效。因此，受让人（此处为银行）受到修改的约束。 （选项 B 和 C） 确实，如果一方放弃某个条件，该方可以针对未来的履约行为恢复该条件。但该规则不适用\n\n这是因为双方同意修改了合同，并且不再有恢复条件的机会。\n\n教育目标：受让人（即合同下的权利被转让的人）获得转让时合同规定的转让人的所有权利。","educationalObjectiveZh":"受让人（即合同下的权利被转让的人）取得转让人在转让时的合同规定的所有权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"1 月 5 日，债权人根据合同向债务人借了 1,000 美元，要求债务人按每月 100 美元的利率偿还贷款，并于每月第一天偿还。 2月1日，应债务人的要求，债权人同意允许在2月5日付款。3月1日，债务人要求类似的延期，债权人回复说：“每个月不要打扰我，把付款日期改到当月五号就可以了。但现在必须用银行本票付款。”债务人说：“好的。”并于3月5日和4月5日用银行本票付款。 4月6日，债权人将贷款合同出售给银行，但没有告知银行有关允许5日还款的协议。 4月6日，银行写信给债务人：“您对[债权人]的债务已转让给我们。我们特此通知您，所有付款必须在当月的第一天支付。”债务人是否可以合理地坚持剩余分期付款的付款日期是每月的五号？","rule":"受让人（即合同下的权利被转让的人）取得转让人在转让时的合同规定的所有权利。","analysis":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 转让是将合同项下的权利转让给另一人（即受让人）。受让人享有转让时合同规定的转让人的所有权利。在此，债务人和债权人之间的合同要求债务人在每月的第一天付款。当债权人承诺在每月的第五天接受付款，以换取债务人以银行本票付款的承诺时，双方实际上修改了该合同。*由于这发生在债权人将其在贷款合同下的权利转让给银行之前，债务人可以有理由坚持剩余分期付款的付款日期是每月的第五天。 *请注意，债权人承诺稍后接受付款（并且修改生效）是考虑在内的，因为债务人通过承诺通过银行本票支付未来付款来改变其先前存在的偿还债务的义务。 （选项A） 没有规定仅仅因为受让人没有通知就使合同变更对受让人无效。因此，受让人（此处为银行）受到修改的约束。 （选项 B 和 C） 确实，如果一方放弃某个条件，该方可以针对未来的履约行为恢复该条件。但该规则不适用\n\n这是因为双方同意修改了合同，并且不再有恢复条件的机会。\n\n教育目标：受让人（即合同下的权利被转让的人）获得转让时合同规定的转让人的所有权利。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为债权人只能将债权人在转让时在合同中拥有的那些权利转让给银行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"promissory estoppel","ipa":"/pɹˈɑmʌsˌɔɹi i'stɒpl/","syllables":"promis·sory estop·pel","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"允诺禁反言：对可合理预见且实际发生的信赖提供救济","contextualMeaning":"允诺禁反言：对可合理预见且实际发生的信赖提供救济","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Assignment of contractual rights Limitations • Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements • Present intent to immediately assign right • Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estopp","synonyms":["detrimental-reliance doctrine"]},{"term":"consideration","ipa":"/kʌnsˌɪdɝˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"con·sid·er·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 考虑, 原因；[法] 考虑, 思考, 报酬","contextualMeaning":"约因：经协商交换的法律利益或不利益","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:considerations","sourceSentence":"Assignment of contractual rights Limitations • Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements • Present intent to immediately assign right • Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estopp","synonyms":["bargained-for exchange"]},{"term":"installments","ipa":"/ˌɪnstˈɔlmənts/","syllables":"in·stall·ments","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 部分( installment的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"分期或分批履行：按约定分次付款或交付","wordForm":"名词；原形 installment；词形 0:installment/1:s","sourceSentence":"\" Can the debtor justifiably insist that the payment date for the rest of the installments is the fifth of each month?","synonyms":["scheduled payment","partial payment"]},{"term":"condition","ipa":"/kʌndˈɪʃən/","syllables":"con·di·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 情况, 条件；vt. 使健康, 以...为条件, 决定, 使适应；[计] 条件","contextualMeaning":"条件：影响合同履行义务发生、持续或消灭的事件","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:conditions/d:conditioned/i:conditioning/p:conditioned/3:conditions","sourceSentence":"No, because although the creditor waived the condition of payment on the first of the month, the bank reinstated it.","synonyms":["contingency","prerequisite"]},{"term":"obligor","ipa":"/.ɒbli'gɒ:/","syllables":"obligor","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 义务人, 债务人；[经] 债务人, 义务人, 欠债者","contextualMeaning":"债务人：负有合同履行义务的人","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Assignment of contractual rights Limitations • Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements • Present intent to immediately assign right • Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estopp","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"accept","ipa":"/æksˈɛpt/","syllables":"ac·cept","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 接受, 承认, 同意, 相信, 赞成, 承担, 承兑, 采纳, 接纳, 容忍；vi. 同意","contextualMeaning":"承诺或接受：以言行同意要约，或接收所交货物","wordForm":"动词；词形 d:accepted/p:accepted/i:accepting/3:accepts","sourceSentence":"The parties effectively modified that contract when the creditor promised to accept payments on the fifth day of each month in exchange for the debtor's promise to make the payments by cashier's check.","synonyms":["assent to","agree to"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":5,"sourceQuestionPage":13,"sourceQuestionPages":[13],"sourceExplanationPages":[14,15],"sourceAnswerPages":[14,15],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-006","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"Under the terms of a written contract, a contractor agreed to construct a garage for a homeowner for $10,000. Nothing was stated in the parties' negotiations or in the contract about progress payments during the course of the work. After completing 25% of the garage strictly according to the homeowner's specifications, the contractor assigned his rights under the contract to a bank as security for an $8,000 loan. The bank immediately notified the homeowner of the assignment. The contractor then, without legal excuse, abandoned the job before it was half- complete. The contractor subsequently defaulted on the loan from the bank. The contractor has no assets. It will cost the homeowner at least $8,000 to get the garage finished by another builder. If the bank sues the homeowner for $8,000, which of the following will the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The bank wins, because the bank as a secured creditor over the contractor is entitled to priority over the homeowner's unsecured claim against the contractor.","B":"The bank wins, because the contract was in existence and the contractor was not in breach when the bank gave the homeowner notice of the assignment.","C":"The homeowner wins, because her claim against the contractor arose prior to the contractor's default on his loan from the bank.","D":"The homeowner wins, because her right to recoupment on account of the contractor's breach is available against the bank as the contractor's assignee."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract A party to a contract is generally free to assign (ie, transfer) its contractual rights to another. The recipient of those rights (assignee) then steps into the shoes of the assigning party (assignor) and assumes all of the assignor's contractual rights to the obligor's performance as they stand at the time of the assignment. However, those rights are subject to any claims or defenses that:\n• accrued before the obligor received notice of the assignment or\n• stem from the assignor's present or future failure to perform (Choice B). Here, the contractor assigned his contractual rights to the bank—including the right to sue the homeowner for money owed under the contract up to the amount of the bank loan ($8,000). However, the bank's recovery is subject to the homeowner's right to recoup $8,000 for the contractor's subsequent failure to finish the garage. Since this fully offsets the bank's recovery, the court will decide for the homeowner. (Choice A) The priority of secured and unsecured claims is relevant in bankruptcy proceedings and other situations where claims are paid in order of priority. But here, the bank's secured-creditor status over the contractor does not affect the homeowner's right to offset the bank's recovery. (Choice C) Had the homeowner's claim been unrelated to the contractor's failure to perform, the timing of the breach and when she received notice of the assignment would\n\naffect her ability to prevail. But the time that the contractor defaulted on the bank's loan is irrelevant to her success.\n\nEducational objective: An assignee assumes all of the assignor's contractual rights that exist at the time of the assignment. However, the assignment is subject to any claims or defenses that (1) the obligor could have raised before receiving notice of the assignment or (2) stem from the assignor's failure to perform.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 336 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defenses against assignee).","educationalObjective":"An assignee assumes all of the assignor's contractual rights that exist at the time of the assignment. However, the assignment is subject to any claims or defenses that (1) the obligor could have raised before receiving notice of the assignment or (2) stem from the assignor's failure to perform.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 336 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defenses against assignee).","rule":"An assignee assumes all of the assignor's contractual rights that exist at the time of the assignment. However, the assignment is subject to any claims or defenses that (1) the obligor could have raised before receiving notice of the assignment or (2) stem from the assignor's failure to perform.","irac":{"issue":"If the bank sues the homeowner for $8,000, which of the following will the court decide?","rule":"An assignee assumes all of the assignor's contractual rights that exist at the time of the assignment. However, the assignment is subject to any claims or defenses that (1) the obligor could have raised before receiving notice of the assignment or (2) stem from the assignor's failure to perform.","analysis":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract A party to a contract is generally free to assign (ie, transfer) its contractual rights to another. The recipient of those rights (assignee) then steps into the shoes of the assigning party (assignor) and assumes all of the assignor's contractual rights to the obligor's performance as they stand at the time of the assignment. However, those rights are subject to any claims or defenses that:\n• accrued before the obligor received notice of the assignment or\n• stem from the assignor's present or future failure to perform (Choice B). Here, the contractor assigned his contractual rights to the bank—including the right to sue the homeowner for money owed under the contract up to the amount of the bank loan ($8,000). However, the bank's recovery is subject to the homeowner's right to recoup $8,000 for the contractor's subsequent failure to finish the garage. Since this fully offsets the bank's recovery, the court will decide for the homeowner. (Choice A) The priority of secured and unsecured claims is relevant in bankruptcy proceedings and other situations where claims are paid in order of priority. But here, the bank's secured-creditor status over the contractor does not affect the homeowner's right to offset the bank's recovery. (Choice C) Had the homeowner's claim been unrelated to the contractor's failure to perform, the timing of the breach and when she received notice of the assignment would\n\naffect her ability to prevail. But the time that the contractor defaulted on the bank's loan is irrelevant to her success.","conclusion":"Answer D: The homeowner wins, because her right to recoupment on account of the contractor's breach is available against the bank as the contractor's assignee."},"questionZh":"根据书面合同条款，承包商同意以 10,000 美元的价格为房主建造一个车库。双方的谈判或合同中均未提及工程过程中的进度付款。在严格按照房主的规格完成车库 25% 的建设后，承包商将合同项下的权利转让给银行，作为 8,000 美元贷款的担保。银行立即将转让事宜通知房主。随后，承包商在没有合法理由的情况下，在工程完成一半之前就放弃了工程。承包商随后拖欠银行贷款。承包商没有资产。要让另一位建筑商完成车库，房主至少需要花费 8,000 美元。如果银行起诉房主索赔 8,000 美元，法院将做出以下哪项裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"银行获胜，因为银行作为承包商的有担保债权人，有权优先于房主对承包商的无担保债权。","B":"银行获胜，因为当银行向房主发出转让通知时，合同已经存在，并且承包商没有违约。","C":"房主获胜，因为她对承包商的索赔是在承包商拖欠银行贷款之前发生的。","D":"房主获胜，因为她有权因承包商的违约行为而向作为承包商受让人的银行索取赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 合同一方通常可以自由地将其合同权利转让（即转移）给另一方。然后，这些权利的接收者（受让人）接替转让方（转让人）的立场，并承担转让人在转让时对债务人履约的所有合同权利。然而，这些权利受到以下任何索赔或抗辩的约束：\n• 在债务人收到转让通知之前应计或\n• 源于转让人当前或未来未能履行义务（选择B）。在这里，承包商将其合同权利转让给银行，包括起诉房主的权利，要求其追究合同项下所欠银行贷款金额（8,000 美元）的款项。然而，银行的追偿取决于房主有权因承包商随后未能完成车库而获得 8,000 美元的赔偿。由于这完全抵消了银行的追偿，法院将做出有利于房主的裁决。 （选项 A）有担保和无担保债权的优先权与破产程序和其他按优先顺序支付债权的情况有关。但在这里，银行相对于承包商的有担保债权人地位并不影响房主抵消银行复苏的权利。 （选择 C）如果房主的索赔与承包商未能履约无关，则违约时间以及她收到转让通知的时间将\n\n影响她的胜算。但承包商拖欠银行贷款的时间与她的成功无关。\n\n教育目标：受让人承担转让时存在的所有转让人合同权利。然而，转让须受到以下任何索赔或抗辩的约束：(1) 债务人在收到转让通知之前可能提出的索赔或抗辩，或 (2) 因转让人未能履行而引起的索赔或抗辩。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 336（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受让人的抗辩）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"受让人承担转让时存在的所有转让人合同权利。然而，转让须受到以下任何索赔或抗辩的约束：(1) 债务人在收到转让通知之前可能提出的索赔或抗辩，或 (2) 因转让人未能履行而引起的索赔或抗辩。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果银行起诉房主索赔 8,000 美元，法院将做出以下哪项裁决？","rule":"受让人承担转让时存在的所有转让人合同权利。然而，转让须受到以下任何索赔或抗辩的约束：(1) 债务人在收到转让通知之前可能提出的索赔或抗辩，或 (2) 因转让人未能履行而引起的索赔或抗辩。","analysis":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 合同一方通常可以自由地将其合同权利转让（即转移）给另一方。然后，这些权利的接收者（受让人）接替转让方（转让人）的立场，并承担转让人在转让时对债务人履约的所有合同权利。然而，这些权利受到以下任何索赔或抗辩的约束：\n• 在债务人收到转让通知之前应计或\n• 源于转让人当前或未来未能履行义务（选择B）。在这里，承包商将其合同权利转让给银行，包括起诉房主的权利，要求其追究合同项下所欠银行贷款金额（8,000 美元）的款项。然而，银行的追偿取决于房主有权因承包商随后未能完成车库而获得 8,000 美元的赔偿。由于这完全抵消了银行的追偿，法院将做出有利于房主的裁决。 （选项 A）有担保和无担保债权的优先权与破产程序和其他按优先顺序支付债权的情况有关。但在这里，银行相对于承包商的有担保债权人地位并不影响房主抵消银行复苏的权利。 （选择 C）如果房主的索赔与承包商未能履约无关，则违约时间以及她收到转让通知的时间将\n\n影响她的胜算。但承包商拖欠银行贷款的时间与她的成功无关。\n\n教育目标：受让人承担转让时存在的所有转让人合同权利。然而，转让须受到以下任何索赔或抗辩的约束：(1) 债务人在收到转让通知之前可能提出的索赔或抗辩，或 (2) 因转让人未能履行而引起的索赔或抗辩。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 336（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受让人的抗辩）。","conclusion":"答案 D：房主获胜，因为她有权因承包商的违约行为而向作为承包商受让人的银行索取赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"excuse","ipa":"/ɪkskjˈus/","syllables":"ex·cuse","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 原谅, 申辩, 做为...的托辞；n. 致歉, 理由, 饶恕, 借口","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“excuse”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:excuses/d:excused/p:excused/i:excusing/3:excuses","sourceSentence":"The contractor then, without legal excuse, abandoned the job before it was half- complete.","synonyms":["apology","alibi","exculpation","self-justification"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":6,"sourceQuestionPage":16,"sourceQuestionPages":[16],"sourceExplanationPages":[17,18],"sourceAnswerPages":[17,18],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-007","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Capacity & Ratification","question":"An elderly woman underwent major surgery and spent two weeks in the hospital. The woman continued to take powerful pain medication for several weeks after she returned home. During her recovery, she offered to sell her car for $450 to her neighbor, who owned a house-cleaning service. The neighbor said, \"That's great! I need a car to transport all the people who work for me to their job sites.\" In fact, the woman's car was worth $3,000, and the neighbor knew this. He was also aware that the woman had undergone surgery and noted that she seemed \"out of it\" because of the medication she was taking. Several days later, the woman's son found out about the deal and contacted the neighbor, telling him that the woman would sell him the car, but for $3,450. The next day, when the neighbor tendered $450 and demanded that the woman give him the car, she refused. If the neighbor sues the woman for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contract was voidable due to the woman's apparent incapacity.","B":"No, because the woman put nothing in writing.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor's reliance on the otherwise voidable contract made it enforceable.","D":"Yes, because the woman's offer and the neighbor's acceptance created an enforceable contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Incapacity to form contract\n\nInfancy\n• Person under 18 enters contract\n• Voidable if disaffirmed by minor before or soon after turning 18\n• Minor must return any still-existing benefit received under contract Mental\n• Mental capacity so deficient that party cannot reasonably illness understand (or act in relation to) nature or consequences of contract AND other party had reason to know\n• Voidable if disaffirmed by incapacitated party when lucid or by legal representative Intoxication\n• Party too intoxicated to reasonably understand (or act in relation to) nature or consequences of contract AND other party had reason to know\n• Voidable if promptly disaffirmed by intoxicated party upon becoming sober\n• Intoxicated party must offer to return any consideration received & may be liable for fair value of goods or services provided A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration—eg, when the woman offered to sell her car and the neighbor accepted. That contract is enforceable unless the breaching party establishes a defense (Choice D). Incapacity due to intoxication is a defense when:\n• an intoxicated party could not reasonably understand or act in relation to the nature or consequences of the contract (eg, selling a car worth $3,000 for only $450) and\n• the other party had reason to know of the intoxication (eg, noticing that the intoxicated party was \"out of it\" when the contract was formed). This renders a contract voidable, so it can be set aside if the intoxicated party acts promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) to disaffirm it upon becoming sober. And here, the woman likely was still under medication when she disaffirmed the contract by refusing to give the neighbor the car, so the neighbor is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) A contract need only be in writing and signed by the party to be charged if it falls within the statute of frauds. This rule applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more, but it does not apply to this contract to sell the car for only $450. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it relied when no valid contract was formed. But the doctrine only allows recovery if\n\nthe party's reliance was reasonable and caused substantial detriment—neither of which is seen here.\n\nEducational objective: Incapacity due to intoxication renders a contract voidable if the intoxicated party could not reasonably understand the nature or consequences of the contract AND the other party had reason to know of the intoxication. The contract can then be set aside if the intoxicated party promptly disaffirms it upon becoming sober.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 12–16 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity to contract).","educationalObjective":"Incapacity due to intoxication renders a contract voidable if the intoxicated party could not reasonably understand the nature or consequences of the contract AND the other party had reason to know of the intoxication. The contract can then be set aside if the intoxicated party promptly disaffirms it upon becoming sober.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 12–16 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity to contract).","rule":"Incapacity due to intoxication renders a contract voidable if the intoxicated party could not reasonably understand the nature or consequences of the contract AND the other party had reason to know of the intoxication. The contract can then be set aside if the intoxicated party promptly disaffirms it upon becoming sober.","irac":{"issue":"If the neighbor sues the woman for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","rule":"Incapacity due to intoxication renders a contract voidable if the intoxicated party could not reasonably understand the nature or consequences of the contract AND the other party had reason to know of the intoxication. The contract can then be set aside if the intoxicated party promptly disaffirms it upon becoming sober.","analysis":"Incapacity to form contract\n\nInfancy\n• Person under 18 enters contract\n• Voidable if disaffirmed by minor before or soon after turning 18\n• Minor must return any still-existing benefit received under contract Mental\n• Mental capacity so deficient that party cannot reasonably illness understand (or act in relation to) nature or consequences of contract AND other party had reason to know\n• Voidable if disaffirmed by incapacitated party when lucid or by legal representative Intoxication\n• Party too intoxicated to reasonably understand (or act in relation to) nature or consequences of contract AND other party had reason to know\n• Voidable if promptly disaffirmed by intoxicated party upon becoming sober\n• Intoxicated party must offer to return any consideration received & may be liable for fair value of goods or services provided A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration—eg, when the woman offered to sell her car and the neighbor accepted. That contract is enforceable unless the breaching party establishes a defense (Choice D). Incapacity due to intoxication is a defense when:\n• an intoxicated party could not reasonably understand or act in relation to the nature or consequences of the contract (eg, selling a car worth $3,000 for only $450) and\n• the other party had reason to know of the intoxication (eg, noticing that the intoxicated party was \"out of it\" when the contract was formed). This renders a contract voidable, so it can be set aside if the intoxicated party acts promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) to disaffirm it upon becoming sober. And here, the woman likely was still under medication when she disaffirmed the contract by refusing to give the neighbor the car, so the neighbor is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) A contract need only be in writing and signed by the party to be charged if it falls within the statute of frauds. This rule applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more, but it does not apply to this contract to sell the car for only $450. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it relied when no valid contract was formed. But the doctrine only allows recovery if\n\nthe party's reliance was reasonable and caused substantial detriment—neither of which is seen here.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the contract was voidable due to the woman's apparent incapacity."},"questionZh":"一名老年妇女接受了大手术，并在医院住了两周。该妇女回家后继续服用强效止痛药数周。在康复期间，她提出以 450 美元的价格将自己的汽车卖给拥有房屋清洁服务的邻居。邻居说：“太好了！我需要一辆车来运送所有为我工作的人到他们的工作地点。”事实上，这位女士的车价值3000美元，邻居也知道这一点。他还知道该女子已经接受了手术，并指出由于她正在服用的药物，她似乎“不适应”。几天后，该妇女的儿子发现了这笔交易，并联系了邻居，告诉他该妇女愿意将这辆车卖给他，但价格为 3,450 美元。第二天，当邻居出价 450 美元并要求该女子把车给他时，她拒绝了。如果邻居起诉该女子违约，他有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为由于该女子明显无行为能力，合同可以撤销。","B":"没有，因为那位女士没有任何书面记录。","C":"是的，因为邻居对原本可撤销的合同的依赖使其具有可执行性。","D":"是的，因为女人的提议和邻居的接受形成了一份可执行的合同。"},"explanationZh":"无能力订立合同\n\n婴儿期\n• 18 岁以下人士签订合同\n• 如果在 18 岁之前或之后不久被未成年人否定，则无效\n• 未成年人必须退还根据心理合同获得的任何仍然存在的福利\n• 精神能力严重不足，以致一方无法合理地理解合同的性质或后果（或采取相关行动），而另一方有理由知道\n• 如果清醒时无行为能力一方或法定代表人否认，则无效\n• 一方过于陶醉，无法合理理解合同的性质或后果（或采取相关行动），而另一方有理由知道\n• 如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即否认，则无效\n• 醉酒一方必须主动退还收到的任何对价，并可能对所提供的商品或服务的公允价值承担责任。 当相互协议得到充分对价的支持时，通常会形成有效的合同——例如，当妇女提出出售她的汽车并且邻居接受时。除非违约方提出抗辩，否则该合同是可执行的（选择 D）。在以下情况下，因中毒而丧失行为能力是一种抗辩：\n• 醉酒一方无法合理理解合同的性质或后果，也无法采取行动（例如，仅以 450 美元的价格出售价值 3,000 美元的汽车），并且\n• 另一方有理由知道醉酒情况（例如，在合同成立时注意到醉酒一方已经“清醒”）。这使得合同无效，因此，如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即（即在合理的时间内）采取行动否认合同，则合同可以被撤销。在这里，当这名妇女拒绝将汽车交给邻居而取消合同时，她很可能仍在接受药物治疗，因此邻居不太可能获胜。 （选择B）如果属于欺诈行为，则只需签订书面合同并由被指控方签署即可。该规则适用于 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同，但不适用于仅 450 美元的汽车销售合同。 （选项C） 根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其所依据的承诺。但该学说仅允许恢复，如果\n\n该党的依赖是合理的，并造成了重大损害——这两种情况在这里都没有看到。\n\n教育目标：如果醉酒一方无法合理理解合同的性质或后果并且另一方有理由知道醉酒情况，则因醉酒而丧失行为能力将使合同无效。如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即否认合同，则合同可以撤销。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 12–16（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（无合同能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果醉酒一方无法合理理解合同的性质或后果并且另一方有理由知道醉酒情况，则因醉酒而丧失行为能力会使合同无效。如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即否认合同，则合同可以撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果邻居起诉该女子违约，他有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"如果醉酒一方无法合理理解合同的性质或后果并且另一方有理由知道醉酒情况，则因醉酒而丧失行为能力会使合同无效。如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即否认合同，则合同可以撤销。","analysis":"无能力订立合同\n\n婴儿期\n• 18 岁以下人士签订合同\n• 如果在 18 岁之前或之后不久被未成年人否定，则无效\n• 未成年人必须退还根据心理合同获得的任何仍然存在的福利\n• 精神能力严重不足，以致一方无法合理地理解合同的性质或后果（或采取相关行动），而另一方有理由知道\n• 如果清醒时无行为能力一方或法定代表人否认，则无效\n• 一方过于陶醉，无法合理理解合同的性质或后果（或采取相关行动），而另一方有理由知道\n• 如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即否认，则无效\n• 醉酒一方必须主动退还收到的任何对价，并可能对所提供的商品或服务的公允价值承担责任。 当相互协议得到充分对价的支持时，通常会形成有效的合同——例如，当妇女提出出售她的汽车并且邻居接受时。除非违约方提出抗辩，否则该合同是可执行的（选择 D）。在以下情况下，因中毒而丧失行为能力是一种抗辩：\n• 醉酒一方无法合理理解合同的性质或后果，也无法采取行动（例如，仅以 450 美元的价格出售价值 3,000 美元的汽车），并且\n• 另一方有理由知道醉酒情况（例如，在合同成立时注意到醉酒一方已经“清醒”）。这使得合同无效，因此，如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即（即在合理的时间内）采取行动否认合同，则合同可以被撤销。在这里，当这名妇女拒绝将汽车交给邻居而取消合同时，她很可能仍在接受药物治疗，因此邻居不太可能获胜。 （选择B）如果属于欺诈行为，则只需签订书面合同并由被指控方签署即可。该规则适用于 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同，但不适用于仅 450 美元的汽车销售合同。 （选项C） 根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其所依据的承诺。但该学说仅允许恢复，如果\n\n该党的依赖是合理的，并造成了重大损害——这两种情况在这里都没有看到。\n\n教育目标：如果醉酒一方无法合理理解合同的性质或后果并且另一方有理由知道醉酒情况，则因醉酒而丧失行为能力将使合同无效。如果醉酒一方在清醒后立即否认合同，则合同可以撤销。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 12–16（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（无合同能力）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为由于该女子明显无行为能力，合同可以撤销。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"voidable contract","ipa":"/'vɒidəbl kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"void·able con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可撤销合同：有效但可由受保护方选择撤销的合同","contextualMeaning":"可撤销合同：有效但可由受保护方选择撤销的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the neighbor's reliance on the otherwise voidable contract made it enforceable.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"statute of frauds","ipa":"/stˈætʃut ˈəv fɹˈɔdz/","syllables":"statute of frauds","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"防欺诈法：要求特定种类合同有经签署书面证据","contextualMeaning":"防欺诈法：要求特定种类合同有经签署书面证据","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice B) A contract need only be in writing and signed by the party to be charged if it falls within the statute of frauds.","synonyms":["writing requirement"]},{"term":"reasonable time","ipa":"/ɹˈizənəbəl tˈaɪm/","syllables":"rea·son·able time","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合理时间：结合交易性质、目的与情境判断的适当期限","contextualMeaning":"合理时间：结合交易性质、目的与情境判断的适当期限","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"This renders a contract voidable, so it can be set aside if the intoxicated party acts promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) to disaffirm it upon becoming sober.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"sale of goods","ipa":"/sˈeɪl ˈəv ɡˈʊdz/","syllables":"sale of goods","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"货物买卖：UCC Article 2 所规制的可移动物交易","contextualMeaning":"货物买卖：UCC Article 2 所规制的可移动物交易","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"This rule applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more, but it does not apply to this contract to sell the car for only $450.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"detriment","ipa":"/dˈɛtɹəmənt/","syllables":"detri·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 损害, 损害物；[法] 损害, 伤害, 危害","contextualMeaning":"法律不利益：作出无义务作出的行为或放弃有权实施的行为","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:detriments","sourceSentence":"But the doctrine only allows recovery if the party's reliance was reasonable and caused substantial detriment—neither of which is seen here.","synonyms":["hurt"]},{"term":"tendered","ipa":"/tˈɛndɝd/","syllables":"ten·dered","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 提出( tender的过去式和过去分词 )； 使变温柔； 使变脆弱","contextualMeaning":"履行提出：按合同提供货物、付款或其他履行","wordForm":"词；原形 tender；词形 0:tender/1:dp","sourceSentence":"The next day, when the neighbor tendered $450 and demanded that the woman give him the car, she refused.","synonyms":["offer of performance","delivery"]},{"term":"offer","ipa":"/ˈɔfɝ/","syllables":"of·fer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 给予(物), 出价, 提议, 意图, 报价；vt. 提供, 出价, 奉献, 试图, 使出现, 演出；vi. 出现, 献祭, 提议, 求婚","contextualMeaning":"要约：足够确定并表示一经承诺即愿受约束的提议","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:offered/3:offers/i:offering/p:offered/s:offers","sourceSentence":"During her recovery, she offered to sell her car for $450 to her neighbor, who owned a house-cleaning service.","synonyms":["proposal","tender of terms"]},{"term":"reliance","ipa":"/ɹɪlˈaɪəns/","syllables":"re·liance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 信任, 信赖, 受信赖的人(或事)；[法] 信任, 信赖, 信心","contextualMeaning":"信赖：因相信允诺或表示而改变立场","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the neighbor's reliance on the otherwise voidable contract made it enforceable.","synonyms":["detrimental reliance","dependence"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":7,"sourceQuestionPage":19,"sourceQuestionPages":[19],"sourceExplanationPages":[20,21],"sourceAnswerPages":[20,21],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-008","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"During an ice storm, a man's car slipped down an embankment and became lodged against a large tree. The man called a towing company and told the company's manager that the car was 100 feet down the embankment. \"That's lucky,\" said the manager, \"because our winch only goes 100 feet.\" After the manager and the man agreed on a price, an employee of the company attempted to reach the car but could not because the car turned out to be 120 feet down the embankment. Is the towing company's performance excused on the grounds of mistake?","choices":{"A":"No, because both parties were uncertain about the distance.","B":"No, because the towing company assumed the risk by the manager's failure to examine the distance himself.","C":"Yes, because at the time of contracting, both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption on which the contract was based.","D":"Yes, because the agreement did not allocate the risk of mistake to either party."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When party assumes risk of mistake\n\nExpress Contract allocates risk to party\n\nConscious Party enters contract despite conscious ignorance of facts related to ignorance mistake\n\nAdjudication Court allocates risk to party because reasonable under circumstances\n\nWhen both parties enter a contract based on the same mistake (ie, mutual mistake), the contract is voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n\n• the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nHere, both parties entered the agreement based on the erroneous belief that the man's car was 100 feet down the embankment (Choice A). That mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract since the towing company's winch only goes 100 feet. And it materially affects the agreed-upon exchange because the towing company cannot be expected to tow a car that its winch cannot reach. Therefore, the towing company's performance will be excused because it also did not assume the risk of the mistake—ie:\n• the agreement did not expressly allocate the risk of the mistake to the towing company\n\n• there is no indication that the towing company knew that the man's car was probably more than 100 feet down the embankment and contracted to tow it anyway and\n\n• it would not be reasonable for a court to allocate the risk of the mistake to the towing company when its winch cannot even reach the car (Choice D).\n\n(Choice B) A party's failure to know or discover facts before entering into a contract (eg, the manager's failure to examine the distance himself) does not prevent the party from avoiding the contract.\n\nEducational objective: The adversely affected party can avoid a contract on the grounds of mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when mutual mistake makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a party bears the risk of a mistake).","educationalObjective":"The adversely affected party can avoid a contract on the grounds of mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when mutual mistake makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a party bears the risk of a mistake).","rule":"The adversely affected party can avoid a contract on the grounds of mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"Is the towing company's performance excused on the grounds of mistake?","rule":"The adversely affected party can avoid a contract on the grounds of mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.","analysis":"When party assumes risk of mistake\n\nExpress Contract allocates risk to party\n\nConscious Party enters contract despite conscious ignorance of facts related to ignorance mistake\n\nAdjudication Court allocates risk to party because reasonable under circumstances\n\nWhen both parties enter a contract based on the same mistake (ie, mutual mistake), the contract is voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n\n• the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nHere, both parties entered the agreement based on the erroneous belief that the man's car was 100 feet down the embankment (Choice A). That mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract since the towing company's winch only goes 100 feet. And it materially affects the agreed-upon exchange because the towing company cannot be expected to tow a car that its winch cannot reach. Therefore, the towing company's performance will be excused because it also did not assume the risk of the mistake—ie:\n• the agreement did not expressly allocate the risk of the mistake to the towing company\n\n• there is no indication that the towing company knew that the man's car was probably more than 100 feet down the embankment and contracted to tow it anyway and\n\n• it would not be reasonable for a court to allocate the risk of the mistake to the towing company when its winch cannot even reach the car (Choice D).\n\n(Choice B) A party's failure to know or discover facts before entering into a contract (eg, the manager's failure to examine the distance himself) does not prevent the party from avoiding the contract.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because at the time of contracting, both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption on which the contract was based."},"questionZh":"在一场冰暴期间，一名男子的汽车从路堤上滑落，撞在一棵大树上。该男子打电话给一家拖车公司，并告诉该公司经理，汽车已驶离路堤 100 英尺。 “这很幸运，”经理说，“因为我们的绞盘只能移动 100 英尺。”经理和该男子谈妥价格后，该公司的一名员工试图接近汽车，但未能成功，因为汽车距离路堤有 120 英尺。拖车公司的表现是否会因失误而被原谅？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为双方都不确定距离。","B":"不会，因为拖车公司承担了经理未能亲自检查距离的风险。","C":"是的，因为在签订合同时，双方都错误地理解了合同所依据的基本假设。","D":"是的，因为协议没有将错误的风险分配给任何一方。"},"explanationZh":"当一方承担错误风险时\n\n快速合同将风险分配给当事人\n\n尽管自觉不了解与无知错误相关的事实，但自觉方仍签订合同\n\n裁决法院将风险分配给当事人，因为在情况下是合理的\n\n当双方基于相同错误（即共同错误）签订合同时，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方不承担错误的风险。\n\n在此，双方基于错误地认为该男子的汽车位于路堤下方 100 英尺处（选项 A）而签订了协议。这个错误与合同的基本假设有关，因为拖车公司的绞车只能行驶 100 英尺。这对商定的交换产生了重大影响，因为不能指望拖车公司拖曳绞车无法到达的汽车。因此，拖车公司的表现将被原谅，因为它也没有承担错误的风险，即：\n• 协议没有明确将错误风险分配给拖车公司\n\n• 没有迹象表明拖车公司知道该男子的汽车可能已深入路堤 100 多英尺，并签订了拖车合同，并且\n\n• 当绞盘甚至无法到达汽车时，法院将错误风险分配给拖车公司是不合理的（选择D）。\n\n（选择B） 一方在签订合同之前未能了解或发现事实（例如，经理未能亲自检查距离）并不妨碍该方解除合同。\n\n教育目标：如果（1）该错误与合同的基本假设有关，（2）该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，并且（3）受不利影响的一方不承担该错误的风险，则受不利影响的一方可以以共同错误为由宣告合同无效。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当相互错误导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方承担错误风险时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果满足以下条件，不利影响方可以以共同错误为由宣告合同无效：（1）该错误涉及合同的基本假设；（2）该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响；（3）不利影响方不承担该错误的风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"拖车公司的表现是否会因失误而被原谅？","rule":"如果满足以下条件，不利影响方可以以共同错误为由宣告合同无效：（1）该错误涉及合同的基本假设；（2）该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响；（3）不利影响方不承担该错误的风险。","analysis":"当一方承担错误风险时\n\n快速合同将风险分配给当事人\n\n尽管自觉不了解与无知错误相关的事实，但自觉方仍签订合同\n\n裁决法院将风险分配给当事人，因为在情况下是合理的\n\n当双方基于相同错误（即共同错误）签订合同时，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方不承担错误的风险。\n\n在此，双方基于错误地认为该男子的汽车位于路堤下方 100 英尺处（选项 A）而签订了协议。这个错误与合同的基本假设有关，因为拖车公司的绞车只能行驶 100 英尺。这对商定的交换产生了重大影响，因为不能指望拖车公司拖曳绞车无法到达的汽车。因此，拖车公司的表现将被原谅，因为它也没有承担错误的风险，即：\n• 协议没有明确将错误风险分配给拖车公司\n\n• 没有迹象表明拖车公司知道该男子的汽车可能已深入路堤 100 多英尺，并签订了拖车合同，并且\n\n• 当绞盘甚至无法到达汽车时，法院将错误风险分配给拖车公司是不合理的（选择D）。\n\n（选择B） 一方在签订合同之前未能了解或发现事实（例如，经理未能亲自检查距离）并不妨碍该方解除合同。\n\n教育目标：如果（1）该错误与合同的基本假设有关，（2）该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，并且（3）受不利影响的一方不承担该错误的风险，则受不利影响的一方可以以共同错误为由宣告合同无效。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当相互错误导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方承担错误风险时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为在签订合同时，双方都错误地理解了合同所依据的基本假设。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"mutual mistake","ipa":"/mjˈutʃuəl mɪstˈeɪk/","syllables":"mu·tual mis·take","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"共同错误：双方对基本事实共有重大错误认识","contextualMeaning":"共同错误：双方对基本事实共有重大错误认识","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The adversely affected party can avoid a contract on the grounds of mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party does not assume the risk of the mistake.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":8,"sourceQuestionPage":22,"sourceQuestionPages":[22],"sourceExplanationPages":[23,24],"sourceAnswerPages":[23,24],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-009","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A buyer expressed interest in purchasing an industrial air-conditioning system manufactured by the seller. The parties agreed orally on a price of $100,000 for the system, but continued to negotiate over several points. When all matters regarding the air- conditioning system were finally settled, the parties signed a written agreement. It provided that the price for the system, which would be delivered on June 1, would be $110,000. The written agreement, a lengthy form contract, did not contain a merger clause. The seller delivered the system on June 1, but the buyer refused to pay more than $100,000, citing the earlier oral agreement as to price. The seller sued the buyer for the additional $10,000 under the written agreement. Is the court likely to admit the evidence of the orally agreed price of $100,000?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer assumed the risk of any mistake as to price.","B":"No, because the oral price term would contradict an express term in the written agreement.","C":"Yes, because the oral price term is relevant to whether the writing should be reformed.","D":"Yes, because the written agreement did not contain a merger clause."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Integration\n\nType Definition Effect\n\nComplete Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms\n• Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some terms\n• Parol evidence admissible to supplement (not contradict) contract Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of an integrated writing. A writing is integrated if the parties intended for it to be a final expression of their agreement about some or all of the terms. Here, the parties intended for the written contract to be the final expression of their agreement since they entered into the contract only after they had settled \"all matters\" regarding the purchase of the air-conditioning system. As a result, the court is unlikely to admit evidence of the $100,000 oral price term because it contradicts the $110,000 price term expressed in the written contract. (Choice A) Evidence used to establish a defense to formation or enforceability of a contract (eg, mistake) is excepted from the parol evidence rule. Here, there is no evidence of mistake (eg, that the $110,000 price term in the written contract was due to a clerical error). But if there were, evidence of the mistake would be admissible. (Choice C) Evidence used to establish a ground for granting a remedy (eg, reformation) is also excepted from the parol evidence rule. Mistake and misrepresentation are grounds for granting reformation, but the oral price term is evidence of neither. (Choice D) A merger clause is strong but not definitive evidence that a contract is completely integrated. But here, it does not matter whether the contract was completely or partially integrated; in either case, the parties cannot use parol evidence to contradict the written terms.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC parol evidence rule bars evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) that contradicts the terms of an integrated writing. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"The UCC parol evidence rule bars evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) that contradicts the terms of an integrated writing. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule bars evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) that contradicts the terms of an integrated writing. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to admit the evidence of the orally agreed price of $100,000?","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule bars evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) that contradicts the terms of an integrated writing. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Integration\n\nType Definition Effect\n\nComplete Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms\n• Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some terms\n• Parol evidence admissible to supplement (not contradict) contract Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of an integrated writing. A writing is integrated if the parties intended for it to be a final expression of their agreement about some or all of the terms. Here, the parties intended for the written contract to be the final expression of their agreement since they entered into the contract only after they had settled \"all matters\" regarding the purchase of the air-conditioning system. As a result, the court is unlikely to admit evidence of the $100,000 oral price term because it contradicts the $110,000 price term expressed in the written contract. (Choice A) Evidence used to establish a defense to formation or enforceability of a contract (eg, mistake) is excepted from the parol evidence rule. Here, there is no evidence of mistake (eg, that the $110,000 price term in the written contract was due to a clerical error). But if there were, evidence of the mistake would be admissible. (Choice C) Evidence used to establish a ground for granting a remedy (eg, reformation) is also excepted from the parol evidence rule. Mistake and misrepresentation are grounds for granting reformation, but the oral price term is evidence of neither. (Choice D) A merger clause is strong but not definitive evidence that a contract is completely integrated. But here, it does not matter whether the contract was completely or partially integrated; in either case, the parties cannot use parol evidence to contradict the written terms.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the oral price term would contradict an express term in the written agreement."},"questionZh":"一位买家表示有兴趣购买卖家制造的工业空调系统。双方口头同意系统价格为 10 万美元，但仍就几个问题继续进行谈判。当有关空调系统的所有问题最终解决后，双方签署了书面协议。该公司表示，该系统将于 6 月 1 日交付，价格为 11 万美元。书面协议是一份冗长的合同，不包含合并条款。卖方于 6 月 1 日交付了系统，但买方以之前的口头价格协议为由，拒绝支付超过 10 万美元。卖方根据书面协议起诉买方，要求额外支付 10,000 美元。法院可能会采纳口头约定价格 10 万美元的证据吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为买方承担任何价格错误的风险。","B":"不可以，因为口头价格条款与书面协议中的明确条款相矛盾。","C":"是的，因为口头价格术语与书面是否应该改革有关。","D":"是的，因为书面协议不包含合并条款。"},"explanationZh":"整合\n\n类型 定义 效果\n\n完整的写作阐述\n• 排除整合各方关于所有条款的最终协议范围内的口头证据\n• 口头证据不可用于补充（即添加）或反驳合同中规定的部分书面内容\n• 排除口头证据整合双方的最终协议与某些条款的协议不一致的情况\n• 口头证据可用于补充（而非反驳）合同 根据UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），任何先前协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据均不能用于反驳综合书面条款。如果双方希望书面文件成为其对部分或全部条款的协议的最终表达，则该书面文件被整合。在此，双方希望以书面合同作为其协议的最终表达，因为他们是在解决了有关购买空调系统的“所有事项”后才签订合同的。因此，法院不太可能接受 100,000 美元口头价格条款的证据，因为它与书面合同中表述的 110,000 美元价格条款相矛盾。 （选择 A）用于对合同的成立或可执行性进行抗辩的证据（例如错误）不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。此处，没有任何错误证据（例如，书面合同中 110,000 美元的价格条款是由于文书错误造成的）。但如果有的话，错误的证据就会被接受。 （选项C）用于确立给予补救（例如改造）理由的证据也不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。错误和失实陈述是批准改革的理由，但口头价格条款并不是这两者的证据。 （选项 D） 合并条款是有力的证据，但不是合同完全整合的决定性证据。但在这里，合同是完全集成还是部分集成并不重要。在任何一种情况下，双方都不能使用口头证据来反驳书面条款。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供任何与综合写作条款相矛盾的先前协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供任何与综合书面条款相矛盾的先前协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会采纳口头约定价格 10 万美元的证据吗？","rule":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供任何与综合书面条款相矛盾的先前协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"整合\n\n类型 定义 效果\n\n完整的写作阐述\n• 排除整合各方关于所有条款的最终协议范围内的口头证据\n• 口头证据不可用于补充（即添加）或反驳合同中规定的部分书面内容\n• 排除口头证据整合双方的最终协议与某些条款的协议不一致的情况\n• 口头证据可用于补充（而非反驳）合同 根据UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），任何先前协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据均不能用于反驳综合书面条款。如果双方希望书面文件成为其对部分或全部条款的协议的最终表达，则该书面文件被整合。在此，双方希望以书面合同作为其协议的最终表达，因为他们是在解决了有关购买空调系统的“所有事项”后才签订合同的。因此，法院不太可能接受 100,000 美元口头价格条款的证据，因为它与书面合同中表述的 110,000 美元价格条款相矛盾。 （选择 A）用于对合同的成立或可执行性进行抗辩的证据（例如错误）不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。此处，没有任何错误证据（例如，书面合同中 110,000 美元的价格条款是由于文书错误造成的）。但如果有的话，错误的证据就会被接受。 （选项C）用于确立给予补救（例如改造）理由的证据也不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。错误和失实陈述是批准改革的理由，但口头价格条款并不是这两者的证据。 （选项 D） 合并条款是有力的证据，但不是合同完全整合的决定性证据。但在这里，合同是完全集成还是部分集成并不重要。在任何一种情况下，双方都不能使用口头证据来反驳书面条款。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供任何与综合写作条款相矛盾的先前协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为口头价格条款与书面协议中的明确条款相矛盾。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"Uniform Commercial Code","ipa":"/jˈunʌfˌɔɹm kʌmˈɝʃəl kˈoʊd/","syllables":"Uni·form Com·mer·cial Code","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"《统一商法典》（UCC）：MBE 货物买卖规则的主要成文法来源","contextualMeaning":"《统一商法典》（UCC）：MBE 货物买卖规则的主要成文法来源","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"UCC = Uniform Commercial Code Integration Type Definition Effect Complete Writing that sets forth • Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms • Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth • Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some ter","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"misrepresentation","ipa":"/mɪsɹˌɛpɹɪzɛntˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"mis·rep·re·sen·ta·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[计] 错误表示法；[经] 误列, 错误表示, 告知不实","contextualMeaning":"虚假陈述：足以诱使缔约的不实事实表示","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:misrepresentations","sourceSentence":"Mistake and misrepresentation are grounds for granting reformation, but the oral price term is evidence of neither.","synonyms":["falsification","deceit","deception"]},{"term":"integration","ipa":"/ˌɪntʌɡɹˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"in·te·gra·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 综合, 与环境协调的行为, 集成；[化] 集成； 整合","contextualMeaning":"合同整合：把先前协商纳入最终书面表达","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:integrations","sourceSentence":"UCC = Uniform Commercial Code Integration Type Definition Effect Complete Writing that sets forth • Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms • Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth • Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some ter","synonyms":["integrating","desegregation","consolidation"]},{"term":"seller","ipa":"/sˈɛlɝ/","syllables":"seller","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 销售者；[化] 卖方","contextualMeaning":"卖方：货物买卖中转让货物的一方","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:sellers","sourceSentence":"A buyer expressed interest in purchasing an industrial air-conditioning system manufactured by the seller.","synonyms":["marketer","vender","vendor","trafficker"]},{"term":"buyer","ipa":"/bˈaɪɝ/","syllables":"buyer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 买主, 买方；[经] 买主, 买方, 买手","contextualMeaning":"买方：货物买卖中购买并支付价款的一方","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:buyers","sourceSentence":"A buyer expressed interest in purchasing an industrial air-conditioning system manufactured by the seller.","synonyms":["purchaser","emptor","vendee"]},{"term":"integrated","ipa":"/ˈɪntʌɡɹˌeɪtəd/","syllables":"in·te·grated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 综合的, 完整的, 互相协调的；[法] 成为整体的, 综合的, 统筹的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“integrated”","wordForm":"形容词；原形 integrate；词形 0:integrate/1:dp/d:integrated/p:integrated","sourceSentence":"The UCC parol evidence rule bars evidence of any prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) that contradicts the terms of an integrated writing.","synonyms":["incorporate","desegregate","mix"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":9,"sourceQuestionPage":25,"sourceQuestionPages":[25],"sourceExplanationPages":[26,27],"sourceAnswerPages":[26,27],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-010","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A homeowner went to an appliance store to purchase a new refrigerator. The homeowner's old refrigerator had just been destroyed by an electrical surge, and he needed to purchase a new one immediately to prevent food spoilage. The homeowner did not understand English, so he conversed with the salesperson in Spanish. The homeowner told the salesperson about the electrical surge and explained that he did not have enough cash for a new refrigerator. The salesperson told the homeowner not to worry because the appliance store offered no-interest financing for a \"small finance fee.\" Then the homeowner signed a contract printed in English. The contract provided for a purchase price of $900, payable in three equal monthly installments, plus a finance fee of $500. The homeowner made three installment payments of $300 each but never paid the finance fee. If the appliance store sues the homeowner for breach of contract and seeks to exclude any evidence of the conversation between the homeowner and the salesperson, how will the court likely rule?","choices":{"A":"Admit the evidence as proof of a collateral agreement.","B":"Admit the evidence as proof of unfair surprise in the contract's terms.","C":"Exclude the evidence as contradicting an unambiguous term of the contract.","D":"Exclude the evidence on the basis of a finding that the contract was completely integrated."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties that contradict the unambiguous terms of a written contract that is completely or partially integrated.* But this rule does not apply to evidence that is offered to establish a defense to the enforceability of the contract. One such defense is unconscionability, which is available if the contract involves some element of unfair surprise. Here, the homeowner signed a written contract to purchase the refrigerator. Before doing so, the homeowner had told the salesperson about his financial situation, and the salesperson had told the homeowner that the store offered no-interest financing for a \"small fee.\" Evidence of this conversation shows that the $500 finance fee for a $900 refrigerator was not in line with the homeowner's expectations—particularly since the contract was in English. Therefore, the court should admit the conversation as proof of unconscionability due to unfair surprise. *However, evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement (ie, add to) but do not contradict the writing is only barred by the parol evidence rule if the contract is completely integrated. (Choice A) The parol evidence rule does not apply to evidence of a collateral agreement— ie, a separate deal with its own consideration. But here, the conversation between the salesperson and the homeowner did not concern a separate deal since it directly applied to the sale of the refrigerator. (Choice C) Evidence that the finance fee was characterized as \"small\" would ordinarily be barred by the parol evidence rule because it contradicts the unambiguous, $500 finance fee in the contract's terms. But since the evidence helps show that the fee unfairly surprised the homeowner, it is admissible. (Choice D) Even if a contract is completely integrated, parol evidence may be offered to establish a defense to the enforceability of the contract (eg, unconscionability).\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule does NOT bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties when the evidence is offered to establish a defense to enforceability (eg, unconscionability).\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule does NOT bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties when the evidence is offered to establish a defense to enforceability (eg, unconscionability).","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","rule":"The parol evidence rule does NOT bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties when the evidence is offered to establish a defense to enforceability (eg, unconscionability).","irac":{"issue":"If the appliance store sues the homeowner for breach of contract and seeks to exclude any evidence of the conversation between the homeowner and the salesperson, how will the court likely rule?","rule":"The parol evidence rule does NOT bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties when the evidence is offered to establish a defense to enforceability (eg, unconscionability).","analysis":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties that contradict the unambiguous terms of a written contract that is completely or partially integrated.* But this rule does not apply to evidence that is offered to establish a defense to the enforceability of the contract. One such defense is unconscionability, which is available if the contract involves some element of unfair surprise. Here, the homeowner signed a written contract to purchase the refrigerator. Before doing so, the homeowner had told the salesperson about his financial situation, and the salesperson had told the homeowner that the store offered no-interest financing for a \"small fee.\" Evidence of this conversation shows that the $500 finance fee for a $900 refrigerator was not in line with the homeowner's expectations—particularly since the contract was in English. Therefore, the court should admit the conversation as proof of unconscionability due to unfair surprise. *However, evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement (ie, add to) but do not contradict the writing is only barred by the parol evidence rule if the contract is completely integrated. (Choice A) The parol evidence rule does not apply to evidence of a collateral agreement— ie, a separate deal with its own consideration. But here, the conversation between the salesperson and the homeowner did not concern a separate deal since it directly applied to the sale of the refrigerator. (Choice C) Evidence that the finance fee was characterized as \"small\" would ordinarily be barred by the parol evidence rule because it contradicts the unambiguous, $500 finance fee in the contract's terms. But since the evidence helps show that the fee unfairly surprised the homeowner, it is admissible. (Choice D) Even if a contract is completely integrated, parol evidence may be offered to establish a defense to the enforceability of the contract (eg, unconscionability).","conclusion":"Answer B: Admit the evidence as proof of unfair surprise in the contract's terms."},"questionZh":"一位房主去一家电器商店购买了一台新冰箱。房主的旧冰箱刚刚被电涌损坏，他需要立即购买一台新冰箱，以防止食物变质。房主不懂英语，所以他用西班牙语与销售人员交谈。房主向销售人员讲述了电涌的情况，并解释说他没有足够的现金购买新冰箱。销售人员告诉房主不用担心，因为家电商店提供无息融资，只需支付“少量财务费用”。然后房主签署了一份英文打印的合同。合同规定购买价格为 900 美元，每月分三期等额支付，另加 500 美元的财务费用。房主分三期付款，每次 300 美元，但从未支付财务费用。如果家电卖场起诉房主违约，并试图排除房主与销售人员谈话的任何证据，法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"承认证据作为抵押协议的证据。","B":"承认证据作为合同条款中不公平意外的证据。","C":"排除与合同明确条款相矛盾的证据。","D":"根据合同完全完整的结论排除证据。"},"explanationZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供与完全或部分完整的书面合同的明确条款相抵触的缔约方之间先前或同期通信的证据。*但该规则不适用于为对合同的可执行性进行抗辩而提供的证据。其中一种抗辩理由是不合理性，如果合同涉及某些不公平的意外因素，则可以使用这种抗辩理由。在这里，房主签订了购买冰箱的书面合同。在此之前，房主已向销售人员告知了自己的财务状况，而销售人员则告诉房主，商店提供无息融资，只需“少量费用”。这次对话的证据表明，一台 900 美元的冰箱需要 500 美元的财务费用，这与房主的预期不符，特别是因为合同是英文的。因此，法院应该承认这次谈话是由于不公平的意外而导致不合理的证据。 *但是，只有在合同完全整合的情况下，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）才会禁止补充（即添加）但不与书面内容相矛盾的先前或同时期通信的证据。 （选择A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不适用于附属协议的证据——即具有自身对价的单独交易。但在这里，销售人员和房主之间的对话并不涉及单独的交易，因为它直接适用于冰箱的销售。 （选项 C） 财务费用被定性为“小额”的证据通常会受到口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的禁止，因为它与合同条款中明确的 500 美元财务费用相矛盾。但由于证据有助于表明这笔费用让房主感到不公平，因此这是可以接受的。 （选择D）即使合同完全完整，也可以提供口头证据来对合同的可执行性进行抗辩（例如，不合情理）。\n\n教育目标：当提供证据来证明可执行性（例如，不合情理）时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供缔约方之间先前或同期沟通的证据。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当提供证据以建立对可执行性的抗辩（例如，不合情理）时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供缔约方之间先前或同期沟通的证据。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果家电卖场起诉房主违约，并试图排除房主与销售人员谈话的任何证据，法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"当提供证据以建立对可执行性的抗辩（例如，不合情理）时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供缔约方之间先前或同期沟通的证据。","analysis":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供与完全或部分完整的书面合同的明确条款相抵触的缔约方之间先前或同期通信的证据。*但该规则不适用于为对合同的可执行性进行抗辩而提供的证据。其中一种抗辩理由是不合理性，如果合同涉及某些不公平的意外因素，则可以使用这种抗辩理由。在这里，房主签订了购买冰箱的书面合同。在此之前，房主已向销售人员告知了自己的财务状况，而销售人员则告诉房主，商店提供无息融资，只需“少量费用”。这次对话的证据表明，一台 900 美元的冰箱需要 500 美元的财务费用，这与房主的预期不符，特别是因为合同是英文的。因此，法院应该承认这次谈话是由于不公平的意外而导致不合理的证据。 *但是，只有在合同完全整合的情况下，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）才会禁止补充（即添加）但不与书面内容相矛盾的先前或同时期通信的证据。 （选择A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不适用于附属协议的证据——即具有自身对价的单独交易。但在这里，销售人员和房主之间的对话并不涉及单独的交易，因为它直接适用于冰箱的销售。 （选项 C） 财务费用被定性为“小额”的证据通常会受到口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的禁止，因为它与合同条款中明确的 500 美元财务费用相矛盾。但由于证据有助于表明这笔费用让房主感到不公平，因此这是可以接受的。 （选择D）即使合同完全完整，也可以提供口头证据来对合同的可执行性进行抗辩（例如，不合情理）。\n\n教育目标：当提供证据来证明可执行性（例如，不合情理）时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供缔约方之间先前或同期沟通的证据。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","conclusion":"答案 B：承认证据作为合同条款中不公平意外的证据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"expectations","ipa":"/ˌɛkspɛktˈeɪʃənz/","syllables":"ex·pec·ta·tions","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 希望( expectation的复数形式 )； 预料； （被）预期","contextualMeaning":"期待利益：合同适当履行时一方本应取得的利益","wordForm":"名词；原形 expectation；词形 0:expectation/1:s","sourceSentence":"\" Evidence of this conversation shows that the $500 finance fee for a $900 refrigerator was not in line with the homeowner's expectations—particularly since the contract was in English.","synonyms":["outlook","prospect","arithmetic mean","first moment"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":10,"sourceQuestionPage":28,"sourceQuestionPages":[28],"sourceExplanationPages":[29,30],"sourceAnswerPages":[29,30],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-011","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"An elderly widow had one child, an adult son, who lived out-of-state. The widow was in poor health, so she hired a live-in caregiver to tend to her daily needs. Knowing that the widow owned a successful bakery business, the caregiver befriended the widow in order to convince her to sell her business to the caregiver. When the widow later decided to sell the business to her son, the caregiver became upset. She falsely told the widow that there were no other reliable caregivers available in the widow's community. The caregiver then threatened to quit her job and never speak to the widow again unless the widow agreed to sell the business to the caregiver. Although the widow knew that there were other caregivers available, she depended on the caregiver and was terrified of being without her. The widow therefore executed a contract selling the business to the caregiver for less than half its market value. Shortly thereafter, the widow was placed under the son's guardianship. When the son learned about the contract and the caregiver's threat, he sued to have the contract annulled on the widow's behalf. On what basis is the son most likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"Duress.","B":"Lack of capacity.","C":"Misrepresentation.","D":"Undue influence."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A contract is voidable for undue influence if a party's assent was the product of unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with that party. Persuasion is unfair or excessive if it seriously impairs the assenting party's ability to exercise free and competent judgment. The following factors are relevant to making this determination:\n• Susceptibility of the assenting party (eg, elderly, in poor health)\n• Unfairness of the resulting bargain (eg, sale of business for less than half its market value)\n• Unavailability of independent advice (eg, attorney unavailable to discuss sale) Here, the widow relied on the caregiver to tend to her daily needs (dominant relationship). The caregiver's threat to quit and never speak to the widow again likely impaired the widow's judgment and persuaded her to sell the caregiver her business. This is further evidenced by the below-market sales price (unfair bargain) and the widow's age and poor health (susceptibility). Therefore, the son is most likely to prevail on this basis. (Choice A) Duress renders a contract voidable if a party's assent was induced by an improper threat that left the party with no reasonable alternative but to succumb to that threat. Here, even if the caregiver's threat was improper, the widow had a reasonable alternative (eg, hiring a new caregiver). (Choice B) A person placed under another's guardianship lacks the capacity to contract, so a contract entered by that person is void (not merely voidable). But here, the widow was placed under the son's guardianship after she agreed to sell her business to the caregiver. (Choice C) A contract is voidable for a misrepresentation that (1) was fraudulent or material, (2) induced assent to the contract, and (3) was justifiably relied on by the assenting party. But since the widow knew other caregivers were available, her reliance on the caregiver's misrepresentation was unjustified.\n\nEducational objective: Undue influence renders a contract voidable when assent stemmed from unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 177 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when undue influence makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Undue influence renders a contract voidable when assent stemmed from unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 177 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when undue influence makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Undue influence renders a contract voidable when assent stemmed from unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","irac":{"issue":"On what basis is the son most likely to prevail?","rule":"Undue influence renders a contract voidable when assent stemmed from unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","analysis":"A contract is voidable for undue influence if a party's assent was the product of unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with that party. Persuasion is unfair or excessive if it seriously impairs the assenting party's ability to exercise free and competent judgment. The following factors are relevant to making this determination:\n• Susceptibility of the assenting party (eg, elderly, in poor health)\n• Unfairness of the resulting bargain (eg, sale of business for less than half its market value)\n• Unavailability of independent advice (eg, attorney unavailable to discuss sale) Here, the widow relied on the caregiver to tend to her daily needs (dominant relationship). The caregiver's threat to quit and never speak to the widow again likely impaired the widow's judgment and persuaded her to sell the caregiver her business. This is further evidenced by the below-market sales price (unfair bargain) and the widow's age and poor health (susceptibility). Therefore, the son is most likely to prevail on this basis. (Choice A) Duress renders a contract voidable if a party's assent was induced by an improper threat that left the party with no reasonable alternative but to succumb to that threat. Here, even if the caregiver's threat was improper, the widow had a reasonable alternative (eg, hiring a new caregiver). (Choice B) A person placed under another's guardianship lacks the capacity to contract, so a contract entered by that person is void (not merely voidable). But here, the widow was placed under the son's guardianship after she agreed to sell her business to the caregiver. (Choice C) A contract is voidable for a misrepresentation that (1) was fraudulent or material, (2) induced assent to the contract, and (3) was justifiably relied on by the assenting party. But since the widow knew other caregivers were available, her reliance on the caregiver's misrepresentation was unjustified.","conclusion":"Answer D: Undue influence."},"questionZh":"一位年老的寡妇有一个孩子，一个成年儿子，住在州外。寡妇的健康状况不佳，因此她聘请了一名住家保姆来照顾她的日常需要。知道寡妇拥有一家成功的面包店生意后，看护人与寡妇成为了朋友，以说服她将自己的生意卖给看护人。当寡妇后来决定将生意卖给她的儿子时，看护人感到很沮丧。她错误地告诉寡妇，寡妇所在的社区没有其他可靠的照顾者。然后，该看护人威胁要辞去工作，并且不再与寡妇说话，除非寡妇同意将业务出售给看护人。尽管寡妇知道还有其他照顾者，但她依赖该照顾者，并且害怕没有她。因此，寡妇签订了一份合同，以低于市场价值一半的价格将企业出售给护理人员。此后不久，寡妇就被置于儿子的监护之下。当儿子得知合同和看护人的威胁后，他代表寡妇提起诉讼，要求撤销合同。儿子最有可能获胜的依据是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"胁迫。","B":"缺乏能力。","C":"失实陈述。","D":"不当影响。"},"explanationZh":"如果一方的同意是由主导或与该方共享特殊信任和信心关系的人不公平或过度说服的结果，则合同可因不当影响而无效。如果说服严重损害同意方行使自由和合格判断的能力，则说服是不公平或过度的。以下因素与做出此决定相关：\n• 同意方的易感性（例如，老年人、健康状况不佳）\n• 交易结果不公平（例如，以低于市场价值一半的价格出售业务）\n• 无法获得独立建议（例如，律师无法讨论出售事宜） 在此，寡妇依靠看护者来满足她的日常需求（主导关系）。照顾者威胁要辞职并且不再与寡妇说话，这可能会损害寡妇的判断力，并说服她将自己的生意卖给照顾者。低于市场的销售价格（不公平交易）以及寡妇的年龄和健康状况不佳（易感性）进一步证明了这一点。所以，在此基础上，儿子最有可能获胜。 （选择A）如果一方的同意是由不当威胁引起的，该威胁使该方别无合理选择，只能屈服于该威胁，则胁迫使合同无效。在这里，即使照顾者的威胁不恰当，寡妇也有合理的选择（例如，雇用新的照顾者）。 （选项B） 受他人监护的人没有订立合同的能力，因此该人签订的合同无效（不仅仅是可撤销的）。但在这里，寡妇在同意将自己的生意卖给看护人后，就被置于儿子的监护之下。 （选择 C）合同因以下虚假陈述而无效：(1) 具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 诱导同意合同；(3) 同意方有正当理由依赖。但由于寡妇知道还有其他照顾者，她对照顾者的虚假陈述的依赖是不合理的。\n\n教育目标：当同意是由于与同意方具有支配性或共享特殊信任和信心关系的人不公平或过度说服而获得的同意时，不当影响会使合同无效。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 177 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当不当影响导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果同意是由于与同意方具有支配性或共享特殊信任和信心关系的人不公平或过度说服而产生的，则不当影响会使合同无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"儿子最有可能获胜的依据是什么？","rule":"如果同意是由于与同意方具有支配性或共享特殊信任和信心关系的人不公平或过度说服而产生的，则不当影响会使合同无效。","analysis":"如果一方的同意是由主导或与该方共享特殊信任和信心关系的人不公平或过度说服的结果，则合同可因不当影响而无效。如果说服严重损害同意方行使自由和合格判断的能力，则说服是不公平或过度的。以下因素与做出此决定相关：\n• 同意方的易感性（例如，老年人、健康状况不佳）\n• 交易结果不公平（例如，以低于市场价值一半的价格出售业务）\n• 无法获得独立建议（例如，律师无法讨论出售事宜） 在此，寡妇依靠看护者来满足她的日常需求（主导关系）。照顾者威胁要辞职并且不再与寡妇说话，这可能会损害寡妇的判断力，并说服她将自己的生意卖给照顾者。低于市场的销售价格（不公平交易）以及寡妇的年龄和健康状况不佳（易感性）进一步证明了这一点。所以，在此基础上，儿子最有可能获胜。 （选择A）如果一方的同意是由不当威胁引起的，该威胁使该方别无合理选择，只能屈服于该威胁，则胁迫使合同无效。在这里，即使照顾者的威胁不恰当，寡妇也有合理的选择（例如，雇用新的照顾者）。 （选项B） 受他人监护的人没有订立合同的能力，因此该人签订的合同无效（不仅仅是可撤销的）。但在这里，寡妇在同意将自己的生意卖给看护人后，就被置于儿子的监护之下。 （选择 C）合同因以下虚假陈述而无效：(1) 具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 诱导同意合同；(3) 同意方有正当理由依赖。但由于寡妇知道还有其他照顾者，她对照顾者的虚假陈述的依赖是不合理的。\n\n教育目标：当同意是由于与同意方具有支配性或共享特殊信任和信心关系的人不公平或过度说服而获得的同意时，不当影响会使合同无效。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 177 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当不当影响导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 D：不当影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"undue influence","ipa":"/ʌndˈu ˈɪnfluəns/","syllables":"un·due in·flu·ence","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不当影响：利用信赖或支配关系造成不公平说服","contextualMeaning":"不当影响：利用信赖或支配关系造成不公平说服","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Undue influence.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"duress","ipa":"/dˈʊɹɛs/","syllables":"duress","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 强迫, 监禁；[法] 强迫, 威胁, 监禁","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“duress”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:duresses","sourceSentence":"Duress.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"fraudulent","ipa":"/fɹˈɔdʒələnt/","syllables":"fraud·u·lent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 欺诈的, 不正的, 不诚实的；[法] 欺诈的, 欺骗的, 骗取的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“fraudulent”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) A contract is voidable for a misrepresentation that (1) was fraudulent or material, (2) induced assent to the contract, and (3) was justifiably relied on by the assenting party.","synonyms":["deceitful","fallacious"]},{"term":"assent","ipa":"/ʌsˈɛnt/","syllables":"as·sent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 赞成；vi. 同意","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“assent”","wordForm":"名词；词形 p:assented/d:assented/i:assenting/s:assents/3:assents","sourceSentence":"Undue influence renders a contract voidable when assent stemmed from unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominated or shared a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","synonyms":["acquiescence","accede","acquiesce"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-7c141650886b654a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":11,"sourceQuestionPage":31,"sourceQuestionPages":[31],"sourceExplanationPages":[32,33],"sourceAnswerPages":[32,33],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-012","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"In a written and signed contract, a buyer agreed to purchase Lot 1 in a subdivision from a developer. Unbeknownst to either party, there was an error in the subdivision's recorded plat, and two separate lots had each been designated as \"Lot 1.\" The buyer intended to purchase the Lot 1 located in the southern half of the subdivision, while the developer intended to sell the Lot 1 located in the northern half of the subdivision. On the day of the closing, the developer presented the buyer with a deed for the northern Lot 1. Which of the following statements concerning this exchange is accurate?","choices":{"A":"The buyer can enforce the contract for the sale of the northern Lot 1 if he waives the misunderstanding and adopts the developer's understanding of \"Lot 1.\"","B":"The buyer can enforce the contract for the sale of the southern Lot 1, because the risk of a plat error can be allocated to the developer that created the plat.","C":"The developer can enforce the contract for the sale of the northern Lot 1, because it did not know or have reason to know of the buyer's interpretation of \"Lot 1\" when the contract was made.","D":"The parties must seek reformation of the contract, and the court will decide which of the plats is the true \"Lot 1.\""},"answer":"A","explanation":"Misunderstanding of material term\n\nGeneral rule Waiver\n\nNeither / both parties know No contract formed Either party can enforce of misunderstanding other party's understanding\n\nOne party knows of Contract formed based on Unknowing party can enforce misunderstanding unknowing party's knowing party's understanding understanding\n\nA misunderstanding arises when there is no meeting of the minds because the parties to a contract assign different meanings to the same term. Where the misunderstanding involves a material term and neither party (or both parties) knew or had reason to know of the misunderstanding, ordinarily there is no contract. However, either party can waive the misunderstanding and choose to enforce the contract in accordance with the other party's understanding. Here, the buyer and the developer assigned different meanings to the term \"Lot 1.\" Neither party was aware of the misunderstanding, and there is no indication that either of them had reason to know of the misunderstanding. Although this would ordinarily result in no contract, the buyer can waive his own misunderstanding to enforce the contract for the sale of the northern Lot 1—the developer's understanding of \"Lot 1.\" (Choice B) A court can allocate the risk of a mistake—ie, an erroneous belief of fact held by one or both parties—to a party upon a finding that it is reasonable in the circumstances to do so (eg, based on that party's culpability). But a court cannot allocate the risk of a misunderstanding—ie, the attachment of different meanings to the same term. (Choice C) The developer cannot enforce the sale of the northern Lot 1 simply because he was unaware of the buyer's misinterpretation. And though the developer could waive the misunderstanding, this would allow the developer to enforce the contract based on the buyer's understanding (the southern Lot 1)—not the developer's understanding (the northern Lot 1). (Choice D) When a writing fails to reflect the true meaning of the parties, either party may ask the court to reform the contract to accurately express the parties' agreement. But such reformation is generally a remedy for a mistake, not a misunderstanding.\n\nEducational objective: A misunderstanding involving a material term ordinarily results in no contract if neither (or both) parties knew or had reason to know of the misunderstanding. However, one party\n\ncan waive the misunderstanding and enforce the contract in accordance with the other party's understanding.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","educationalObjective":"A misunderstanding involving a material term ordinarily results in no contract if neither (or both) parties knew or had reason to know of the misunderstanding. However, one party\n\ncan waive the misunderstanding and enforce the contract in accordance with the other party's understanding.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","rule":"A misunderstanding involving a material term ordinarily results in no contract if neither (or both) parties knew or had reason to know of the misunderstanding. However, one party\n\ncan waive the misunderstanding and enforce the contract in accordance with the other party's understanding.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements concerning this exchange is accurate?","rule":"A misunderstanding involving a material term ordinarily results in no contract if neither (or both) parties knew or had reason to know of the misunderstanding. However, one party\n\ncan waive the misunderstanding and enforce the contract in accordance with the other party's understanding.","analysis":"Misunderstanding of material term\n\nGeneral rule Waiver\n\nNeither / both parties know No contract formed Either party can enforce of misunderstanding other party's understanding\n\nOne party knows of Contract formed based on Unknowing party can enforce misunderstanding unknowing party's knowing party's understanding understanding\n\nA misunderstanding arises when there is no meeting of the minds because the parties to a contract assign different meanings to the same term. Where the misunderstanding involves a material term and neither party (or both parties) knew or had reason to know of the misunderstanding, ordinarily there is no contract. However, either party can waive the misunderstanding and choose to enforce the contract in accordance with the other party's understanding. Here, the buyer and the developer assigned different meanings to the term \"Lot 1.\" Neither party was aware of the misunderstanding, and there is no indication that either of them had reason to know of the misunderstanding. Although this would ordinarily result in no contract, the buyer can waive his own misunderstanding to enforce the contract for the sale of the northern Lot 1—the developer's understanding of \"Lot 1.\" (Choice B) A court can allocate the risk of a mistake—ie, an erroneous belief of fact held by one or both parties—to a party upon a finding that it is reasonable in the circumstances to do so (eg, based on that party's culpability). But a court cannot allocate the risk of a misunderstanding—ie, the attachment of different meanings to the same term. (Choice C) The developer cannot enforce the sale of the northern Lot 1 simply because he was unaware of the buyer's misinterpretation. And though the developer could waive the misunderstanding, this would allow the developer to enforce the contract based on the buyer's understanding (the southern Lot 1)—not the developer's understanding (the northern Lot 1). (Choice D) When a writing fails to reflect the true meaning of the parties, either party may ask the court to reform the contract to accurately express the parties' agreement. But such reformation is generally a remedy for a mistake, not a misunderstanding.","conclusion":"Answer A: The buyer can enforce the contract for the sale of the northern Lot 1 if he waives the misunderstanding and adopts the developer's understanding of \"Lot 1.\""},"questionZh":"在书面并签署的合同中，买方同意从开发商处购买细分地段的 1 号地块。双方都不知道的是，该分区的记录地块存在错误，两个独立的地块均被指定为“地块 1”。买方打算购买位于该小区南半部的1号地块，而开发商则打算出售位于该小区北半部的1号地块。交割当天，开发商向买方出示了北部地块 1 的地契。以下关于本次交换的哪些表述是准确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"如果买方放弃误解并采用开发商对“Lot 1”的理解，则可以执行北部Lot 1的销售合同。","B":"买方可以执行南部地块 1 的销售合同，因为平台错误的风险可以分配给创建该平台的开发商。","C":"开发商可以执行北部1号地块的销售合同，因为它在签订合同时不知道或没有理由知道买方对“1号地块”的解释。","D":"双方必须寻求修改合同，法院将决定哪一个地块是真正的“Lot 1”。"},"explanationZh":"对物质术语的误解\n\n一般规则豁免\n\n双方都不知道 没有形成合同 任何一方都可以强制执行误解另一方的理解\n\n一方知情者基于不知情方的理解而形成的合同可以强制不知情方的知情方的理解产生误解\n\n当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同的含义而意见不一致时，就会产生误解。如果误解涉及重大条款，并且双方（或双方）均不知道或没有理由知道该误解，则通常不存在合同。但任何一方都可以放弃误解并选择按照对方的理解执行合同。在这里，买方和开发商对“Lot 1”一词赋予了不同的含义。双方均不知道存在误会，也没有迹象表明双方有理由知道存在误会。虽然这通常会导致不签订合同，但买方可以放弃自己的误解，执行北部 1 号地块的销售合同——开发商对“1 号地块”的理解。 （选择B）法院可以将错误的风险（即一方或双方对事实的错误信念）分配给一方，前提是认为在当时的情况下这样做是合理的（例如，基于该方的罪责）。但法院无法分配误解的风险，即对同一术语赋予不同的含义。 （选项C） 开发商不能仅仅因为不知道买方的误解而强制出售北1号地块。尽管开发商可以放弃误解，但这将允许开发商根据买方的理解（南部1号地块）而不是开发商的理解（北部1号地块）来执行合同。 （选项D）当书面内容未能反映双方当事人的真实意思时，任何一方均可请求法院修改合同以准确表达双方的同意。但这种改革通常是对错误的补救，而不是误解。\n\n教育目标：如果双方（或双方）都不知道或没有理由知道该误解，则涉及实质性条款的误解通常不会导致合同成立。然而，一方\n\n可以免除误会并按照对方的理解执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果双方（或双方）均不知道或没有理由知道该误解，则涉及重大条款的误解通常不会导致合同成立。然而，一方\n\n可以免除误会并按照对方的理解执行合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下关于本次交换的哪些表述是准确的？","rule":"如果双方（或双方）均不知道或没有理由知道该误解，则涉及重大条款的误解通常不会导致合同成立。然而，一方\n\n可以免除误会并按照对方的理解执行合同。","analysis":"对物质术语的误解\n\n一般规则豁免\n\n双方都不知道 没有形成合同 任何一方都可以强制执行误解另一方的理解\n\n一方知情者基于不知情方的理解而形成的合同可以强制不知情方的知情方的理解产生误解\n\n当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同的含义而意见不一致时，就会产生误解。如果误解涉及重大条款，并且双方（或双方）均不知道或没有理由知道该误解，则通常不存在合同。但任何一方都可以放弃误解并选择按照对方的理解执行合同。在这里，买方和开发商对“Lot 1”一词赋予了不同的含义。双方均不知道存在误会，也没有迹象表明双方有理由知道存在误会。虽然这通常会导致不签订合同，但买方可以放弃自己的误解，执行北部 1 号地块的销售合同——开发商对“1 号地块”的理解。 （选择B）法院可以将错误的风险（即一方或双方对事实的错误信念）分配给一方，前提是认为在当时的情况下这样做是合理的（例如，基于该方的罪责）。但法院无法分配误解的风险，即对同一术语赋予不同的含义。 （选项C） 开发商不能仅仅因为不知道买方的误解而强制出售北1号地块。尽管开发商可以放弃误解，但这将允许开发商根据买方的理解（南部1号地块）而不是开发商的理解（北部1号地块）来执行合同。 （选项D）当书面内容未能反映双方当事人的真实意思时，任何一方均可请求法院修改合同以准确表达双方的同意。但这种改革通常是对错误的补救，而不是误解。\n\n教育目标：如果双方（或双方）都不知道或没有理由知道该误解，则涉及实质性条款的误解通常不会导致合同成立。然而，一方\n\n可以免除误会并按照对方的理解执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 A：如果买方放弃误解并采用开发商对“Lot 1”的理解，则可以执行北部Lot 1的销售合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"waiver","ipa":"/wˈeɪvɝ/","syllables":"waiver","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 放弃, 弃权, 弃权声明书；[经] 弃权, 放弃权利","contextualMeaning":"放弃：有权方自愿表示不坚持某项权利或条件","wordForm":"词；词形 s:waivers/p:waivered","sourceSentence":"Misunderstanding of material term General rule Waiver Neither / both parties know No contract formed Either party can enforce of misunderstanding other party's understanding One party knows of Contract formed based on Unknowing party can enforce misunderstanding unknowing party's knowing party's understanding understanding A misunderstanding arises when there is no meeting of the minds because the parties to a contra","synonyms":["relinquishment","abandonment of right"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":12,"sourceQuestionPage":34,"sourceQuestionPages":[34],"sourceExplanationPages":[35,36],"sourceAnswerPages":[35,36],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-013","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A commercial baker and an ingredient supplier orally agreed that the baker would purchase 1,000 pounds of flour from the supplier for a price between 50 and 75 cents per pound. The parties agreed that they would determine the final price upon delivery. The supplier promised to deliver the flour in two weeks. Later that day, the baker sent the supplier a signed note reminding the supplier about the terms of their agreement, including the quantity of flour. The supplier received the note, read it, and took no further action. When the supplier failed to deliver the flour, the baker sued the supplier for breach of contract. Can the baker enforce the contract against the supplier?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parties did not agree to a definitive price term.","B":"No, because the supplier did not sign the note.","C":"Yes, because the baker sent the supplier a signed confirmation.","D":"Yes, because the parties' oral agreement is enforceable."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nThe UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods valued at $500 or more. To be enforceable, these agreements must be evidenced by a writing that:\n• provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed (eg, the baker's note)\n• lists the quantity of goods sold or the means to determine quantity (eg, 1,000 pounds of flour) and\n• is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (here, the supplier) (Choice B). However, the UCC relaxes the signature requirement in agreements between merchants—ie, regular dealers of the goods involved in the transaction. Under the merchant exception, a written confirmation need only be signed by one merchant and sent to the other merchant. If the recipient has reason to know the confirmation's contents and does not object within 10 days, then it is enforceable against both merchants. Here, the baker and the supplier are both merchants—regular dealers in flour. The baker's signed note was a sufficient writing because it confirmed that a contract was formed and listed the quantity of goods sold. Additionally, the supplier had reason to know the contents of the note (received and read it) and did not object within 10 days. Therefore, the contract was enforceable against both parties. (Choice A) Although the parties did not agree to a definitive price term, they did agree to determine the final price at delivery. And even if they are unable to do so, the contract is enforceable since UCC gap-fillers will supply the price term at the time of delivery.\n\n(Choice D) An oral agreement is unenforceable when, as here, it is subject to the statute of frauds. However, the parties' oral agreement was memorialized in a written confirmation that satisfied the merchant exception to this statute and can therefore be enforced.\n\nEducational objective: Under the merchant exception to the UCC statute of frauds, a confirmation signed and sent by one merchant to another binds both parties if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not object within 10 days.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (describing the UCC statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-305(1)(b) (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (describing open price terms).","educationalObjective":"Under the merchant exception to the UCC statute of frauds, a confirmation signed and sent by one merchant to another binds both parties if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not object within 10 days.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (describing the UCC statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-305(1)(b) (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (describing open price terms).","rule":"Under the merchant exception to the UCC statute of frauds, a confirmation signed and sent by one merchant to another binds both parties if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not object within 10 days.","irac":{"issue":"Can the baker enforce the contract against the supplier?","rule":"Under the merchant exception to the UCC statute of frauds, a confirmation signed and sent by one merchant to another binds both parties if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not object within 10 days.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nThe UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods valued at $500 or more. To be enforceable, these agreements must be evidenced by a writing that:\n• provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed (eg, the baker's note)\n• lists the quantity of goods sold or the means to determine quantity (eg, 1,000 pounds of flour) and\n• is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (here, the supplier) (Choice B). However, the UCC relaxes the signature requirement in agreements between merchants—ie, regular dealers of the goods involved in the transaction. Under the merchant exception, a written confirmation need only be signed by one merchant and sent to the other merchant. If the recipient has reason to know the confirmation's contents and does not object within 10 days, then it is enforceable against both merchants. Here, the baker and the supplier are both merchants—regular dealers in flour. The baker's signed note was a sufficient writing because it confirmed that a contract was formed and listed the quantity of goods sold. Additionally, the supplier had reason to know the contents of the note (received and read it) and did not object within 10 days. Therefore, the contract was enforceable against both parties. (Choice A) Although the parties did not agree to a definitive price term, they did agree to determine the final price at delivery. And even if they are unable to do so, the contract is enforceable since UCC gap-fillers will supply the price term at the time of delivery.\n\n(Choice D) An oral agreement is unenforceable when, as here, it is subject to the statute of frauds. However, the parties' oral agreement was memorialized in a written confirmation that satisfied the merchant exception to this statute and can therefore be enforced.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the baker sent the supplier a signed confirmation."},"questionZh":"一位商业面包师和一位原料供应商口头同意，面包师将从供应商处购买 1,000 磅面粉，价格为每磅 50 至 75 美分。双方同意在交货时确定最终价格。供应商承诺两周内交付面粉。当天晚些时候，面包师向供应商发送了一份签名的便条，提醒供应商协议条款，包括面粉的数量。供应商收到该通知并阅读后，没有采取进一步行动。当供应商未能交付面粉时，面包师起诉供应商违约。面包师可以对供应商执行合同吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为双方没有就明确的价格条款达成一致。","B":"不，因为供应商没有签署该单据。","C":"是的，因为面包师向供应商发送了一份签名确认书。","D":"是的，因为双方的口头协议是可执行的。"},"explanationZh":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\nUCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同。为了具有可执行性，这些协议必须以书面形式证明：\n• 提供合理的依据以相信合同已成立（例如面包师的注释）\n• 列出所售商品的数量或确定数量的方法（例如，1,000 磅面粉）以及\n• 由寻求执行的一方（此处为供应商）签署（选择B）。然而，UCC 放宽了商家（即参与交易的商品的常规经销商）之间协议中的签名要求。在商户例外情况下，书面确认仅需由一个商户签署并发送给另一商户。如果收件人有理由知道确认内容并且在 10 天内没有提出异议，则可以对两家商家强制执行。在这里，面包师和供应商都是商人——面粉的常规经销商。面包师签名的便条已经足够了，因为它确认了合同的成立并列出了所售商品的数量。此外，供应商有理由知道该通知的内容（已收到并阅读）并且在 10 天内未提出异议。因此，该合同对双方均具有强制执行力。 （选项A）虽然双方没有就最终价格条款达成一致，但他们同意在交货时确定最终价格。即使他们无法这样做，合同也是可执行的，因为 UCC 缺口填充者将在交货时提供价格条款。\n\n（选项 D）当口头协议受到欺诈法的约束时，如此处所示，它是不可执行的。然而，双方的口头协议已记录在书面确认中，满足了该法规的商家例外情况，因此可以强制执行。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的商户例外规定，如果接收方有理由知道其内容并且在 10 天内没有提出异议，则由一个商户签署并发送给另一商户的确认书对双方均具有约束力。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（描述 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-305(1)(b)（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（描述公开价格条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的商家例外规定，如果接收方有理由知道其内容并且在 10 天内未提出异议，则由一个商家签署并发送给另一商家的确认对双方均具有约束力。","iracZh":{"issue":"面包师可以对供应商执行合同吗？","rule":"根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的商家例外规定，如果接收方有理由知道其内容并且在 10 天内未提出异议，则由一个商家签署并发送给另一商家的确认对双方均具有约束力。","analysis":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\nUCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同。为了具有可执行性，这些协议必须以书面形式证明：\n• 提供合理的依据以相信合同已成立（例如面包师的注释）\n• 列出所售商品的数量或确定数量的方法（例如，1,000 磅面粉）以及\n• 由寻求执行的一方（此处为供应商）签署（选择B）。然而，UCC 放宽了商家（即参与交易的商品的常规经销商）之间协议中的签名要求。在商户例外情况下，书面确认仅需由一个商户签署并发送给另一商户。如果收件人有理由知道确认内容并且在 10 天内没有提出异议，则可以对两家商家强制执行。在这里，面包师和供应商都是商人——面粉的常规经销商。面包师签名的便条已经足够了，因为它确认了合同的成立并列出了所售商品的数量。此外，供应商有理由知道该通知的内容（已收到并阅读）并且在 10 天内未提出异议。因此，该合同对双方均具有强制执行力。 （选项A）虽然双方没有就最终价格条款达成一致，但他们同意在交货时确定最终价格。即使他们无法这样做，合同也是可执行的，因为 UCC 缺口填充者将在交货时提供价格条款。\n\n（选项 D）当口头协议受到欺诈法的约束时，如此处所示，它是不可执行的。然而，双方的口头协议已记录在书面确认中，满足了该法规的商家例外情况，因此可以强制执行。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的商户例外规定，如果接收方有理由知道其内容并且在 10 天内没有提出异议，则由一个商户签署并发送给另一商户的确认书对双方均具有约束力。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（描述 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-305(1)(b)（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（描述公开价格条款）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为面包师向供应商发送了一份签名确认书。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"common law","ipa":"/kˈɑmən lˈɔ/","syllables":"com·mon law","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"普通法：除货物买卖外合同通常适用的判例法规则","contextualMeaning":"普通法：除货物买卖外合同通常适用的判例法规则","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Statute of frauds requirements Writing Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND • at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance • under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark ","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"merchant","ipa":"/mˈɝtʃənt/","syllables":"mer·chant","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 商人, 店主；a. 商业的, 商人的","contextualMeaning":"商人：经常经营相关货物或具有相关专业知识的人","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:merchants/i:merchanting","sourceSentence":"Under the merchant exception to the UCC statute of frauds, a confirmation signed and sent by one merchant to another binds both parties if the recipient has reason to know its contents and does not object within 10 days.","synonyms":["merchandiser"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":13,"sourceQuestionPage":37,"sourceQuestionPages":[37],"sourceExplanationPages":[38,39],"sourceAnswerPages":[38,39],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-014","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A man who wanted to sell his home executed a written listing agreement with a real estate broker. The agreement was signed by both parties and gave the broker the exclusive right to sell the home for 60 days. It also gave her the right to receive a commission for up to 30 days after the exclusivity period had terminated if the home sold to a buyer with whom the broker had negotiated during the exclusivity period. A clause in the agreement provided that any modifications must be in writing. Two weeks later, the parties orally agreed to extend the exclusivity period by 60 days (for a total of 120 days). The 120-day exclusivity period ended without a sale. Twenty days later, a buyer with whom the broker had negotiated while the exclusivity period was still in force offered to purchase the home. The man accepted the offer and concluded the sale but refused to pay the broker a commission. If the broker sues the man for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parties waived the right to modify the written agreement.","B":"No, because the parol evidence rule will prevent proof of the parties' oral agreement.","C":"Yes, because the original agreement is not subject to the statute of frauds.","D":"Yes, because the parties' oral agreement was an effective modification of their written agreement."},"answer":"D","explanation":"\"No oral modification\" (NOM) clause\n\nCommon law\n• NOM clause enforceable only if modification must be in writing under statute of frauds (contracts for services or real estate)\n\nUCC\n• All parties are merchants – NOM clause always enforceable\n• > 1 party is nonmerchant – NOM clause in merchant's form (contracts for sale of must be separately signed by nonmerchant(s) to be goods) enforceable UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, a prior written contract can be modified by a subsequent oral agreement unless the modified contract falls within the statute of frauds. This is true even if the written contract contains a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause purporting to require that any modification be in writing (Choice A). That is because of the common-law principle that the parties to a contract should not be deterred from changing their minds. However, any modification (oral or written) must still be supported by consideration to be enforceable. Here, the parties orally agreed to extend the exclusivity period by 60 days. Although the written agreement contained a NOM clause, the modified exclusivity period did not need to be evidenced by a writing since no statute of frauds provision applies.* And since there was consideration—60 more days of exclusivity in exchange for 60 more days of service—the parties' oral agreement effectively modified their written agreement. The terms of the modified contract are found partly in the original written agreement and partly in the modifying oral agreement. As a result, the broker had the right to receive a commission for up to 30 days after the 120-day exclusivity period had ended if the home sold to a buyer with whom she had negotiated during that period—which it did. Therefore, the broker will likely prevail. *Contracts to pay a real estate broker a commission ordinarily fall outside the land-contract provision of the statute of frauds. Some jurisdictions have added a provision to their statute of frauds requiring such contracts to be in writing, but that is not the case here. (Choice B) The parol evidence rule generally prevents proof of prior or contemporaneous agreements that modify or contradict the terms of a written contract. But this rule does not prevent proof of subsequent agreements between the parties—eg, the oral agreement entered after the written listing agreement was executed.\n\n(Choice C) The agreement to pay the real estate broker a commission is not subject to the statute of frauds. Nevertheless, the broker can only prevail if the oral modification agreement also falls outside of the statute of frauds.\n\nEducational objective: Common law ignores \"no oral modification\" clauses and allows written contracts to be modified by subsequent oral agreements unless the modified contract falls within the statute of frauds.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 149 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (oral modifications).","educationalObjective":"Common law ignores \"no oral modification\" clauses and allows written contracts to be modified by subsequent oral agreements unless the modified contract falls within the statute of frauds.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 149 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (oral modifications).","rule":"Common law ignores \"no oral modification\" clauses and allows written contracts to be modified by subsequent oral agreements unless the modified contract falls within the statute of frauds.","irac":{"issue":"If the broker sues the man for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","rule":"Common law ignores \"no oral modification\" clauses and allows written contracts to be modified by subsequent oral agreements unless the modified contract falls within the statute of frauds.","analysis":"\"No oral modification\" (NOM) clause\n\nCommon law\n• NOM clause enforceable only if modification must be in writing under statute of frauds (contracts for services or real estate)\n\nUCC\n• All parties are merchants – NOM clause always enforceable\n• > 1 party is nonmerchant – NOM clause in merchant's form (contracts for sale of must be separately signed by nonmerchant(s) to be goods) enforceable UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, a prior written contract can be modified by a subsequent oral agreement unless the modified contract falls within the statute of frauds. This is true even if the written contract contains a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause purporting to require that any modification be in writing (Choice A). That is because of the common-law principle that the parties to a contract should not be deterred from changing their minds. However, any modification (oral or written) must still be supported by consideration to be enforceable. Here, the parties orally agreed to extend the exclusivity period by 60 days. Although the written agreement contained a NOM clause, the modified exclusivity period did not need to be evidenced by a writing since no statute of frauds provision applies.* And since there was consideration—60 more days of exclusivity in exchange for 60 more days of service—the parties' oral agreement effectively modified their written agreement. The terms of the modified contract are found partly in the original written agreement and partly in the modifying oral agreement. As a result, the broker had the right to receive a commission for up to 30 days after the 120-day exclusivity period had ended if the home sold to a buyer with whom she had negotiated during that period—which it did. Therefore, the broker will likely prevail. *Contracts to pay a real estate broker a commission ordinarily fall outside the land-contract provision of the statute of frauds. Some jurisdictions have added a provision to their statute of frauds requiring such contracts to be in writing, but that is not the case here. (Choice B) The parol evidence rule generally prevents proof of prior or contemporaneous agreements that modify or contradict the terms of a written contract. But this rule does not prevent proof of subsequent agreements between the parties—eg, the oral agreement entered after the written listing agreement was executed.\n\n(Choice C) The agreement to pay the real estate broker a commission is not subject to the statute of frauds. Nevertheless, the broker can only prevail if the oral modification agreement also falls outside of the statute of frauds.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the parties' oral agreement was an effective modification of their written agreement."},"questionZh":"一名想要出售房屋的男子与房地产经纪人签署了一份书面挂牌协议。双方签署了该协议，并赋予经纪人 60 天的独家出售房屋的权利。如果将房屋出售给经纪人在排他期内与经纪人协商的买家，她还有权在排他期终止后获得最多 30 天的佣金。协议中的一项条款规定，任何修改都必须以书面形式进行。两周后，双方口头同意将独占期延长 60 天（总计 120 天）。 120 天的独占期结束后，没有发生任何销售。二十天后，在排他性期限仍然有效的情况下，经纪人与一位买家进行了谈判，提出购买该房屋。该男子接受了报价并完成了销售，但拒绝向经纪人支付佣金。如果经纪人起诉该男子违约，她有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为双方放弃了修改书面协议的权利。","B":"不可以，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）将阻止证明当事人的口头协议。","C":"是的，因为原始协议不受欺诈法的约束。","D":"是的，因为双方的口头协议是对其书面协议的有效修改。"},"explanationZh":"“禁止口头修改”(NOM) 条款\n\n普通法\n• 仅当修改必须根据防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（服务或房地产合同）以书面形式进行时，NOM 条款才可执行\n\nUCC\n• 各方都是商家 – NOM 条款始终可执行\n• > 1 方是非商户 – 商户形式的 NOM 条款（销售合同必须由非商户单独签署才能成为货物）可执行 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据普通法，先前的书面合同可以通过随后的口头协议进行修改，除非修改后的合同属于欺诈法的范围。即使书面合同包含“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，旨在要求任何修改均以书面形式进行（选择 A），情况也是如此。这是因为普通法原则规定，不应阻止合同当事人改变主意。然而，任何修改（口头或书面）仍必须得到可执行的考虑的支持。在此，双方口头同意将独占期延长 60 天。尽管书面协议包含 NOM 条款，但修改后的排他性期限不需要以书面形式证明，因为不适用任何防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。*并且由于存在考虑因素（多 60 天的排他性以换取多 60 天的服务），双方的口头协议有效地修改了他们的书面协议。修改后的合同条款部分见于原始书面协议，部分见于修改后的口头协议。因此，如果经纪人在 120 天的独家经营期结束后将房屋卖给了她在这段时间内与之谈判过的买家（事实确实如此），那么经纪人有权在 120 天的独家经营期结束后收取最多 30 天的佣金。因此，经纪人很可能会占上风。 *向房地产经纪人支付佣金的合同通常不属于欺诈法的土地合同条款的范围。一些司法管辖区在其反防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）中增加了一项条款，要求此类合同采用书面形式，但这里的情况并非如此。 （选择 B）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常阻止对修改或与书面合同条款相抵触的先前或同期协议进行证明。但该规则并不妨碍证明当事人之间的后续协议，例如书面上市协议签署后签订的口头协议。\n\n（选项C） 向房地产经纪人支付佣金的协议不受欺诈法的约束。然而，只有当口头修改协议也不属于欺诈法的范围时，经纪人才能胜诉。\n\n教育目标：普通法忽略“不得口头修改”条款，并允许通过随后的口头协议修改书面合同，除非修改后的合同属于欺诈法的范围。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 149（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头修改）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"普通法忽略“不得口头修改”条款，并允许通过随后的口头协议修改书面合同，除非修改后的合同属于欺诈法的范围。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果经纪人起诉该男子违约，她有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"普通法忽略“不得口头修改”条款，并允许通过随后的口头协议修改书面合同，除非修改后的合同属于欺诈法的范围。","analysis":"“禁止口头修改”(NOM) 条款\n\n普通法\n• 仅当修改必须根据防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（服务或房地产合同）以书面形式进行时，NOM 条款才可执行\n\nUCC\n• 各方都是商家 – NOM 条款始终可执行\n• > 1 方是非商户 – 商户形式的 NOM 条款（销售合同必须由非商户单独签署才能成为货物）可执行 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据普通法，先前的书面合同可以通过随后的口头协议进行修改，除非修改后的合同属于欺诈法的范围。即使书面合同包含“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，旨在要求任何修改均以书面形式进行（选择 A），情况也是如此。这是因为普通法原则规定，不应阻止合同当事人改变主意。然而，任何修改（口头或书面）仍必须得到可执行的考虑的支持。在此，双方口头同意将独占期延长 60 天。尽管书面协议包含 NOM 条款，但修改后的排他性期限不需要以书面形式证明，因为不适用任何防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。*并且由于存在考虑因素（多 60 天的排他性以换取多 60 天的服务），双方的口头协议有效地修改了他们的书面协议。修改后的合同条款部分见于原始书面协议，部分见于修改后的口头协议。因此，如果经纪人在 120 天的独家经营期结束后将房屋卖给了她在这段时间内与之谈判过的买家（事实确实如此），那么经纪人有权在 120 天的独家经营期结束后收取最多 30 天的佣金。因此，经纪人很可能会占上风。 *向房地产经纪人支付佣金的合同通常不属于欺诈法的土地合同条款的范围。一些司法管辖区在其反防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）中增加了一项条款，要求此类合同采用书面形式，但这里的情况并非如此。 （选择 B）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常阻止对修改或与书面合同条款相抵触的先前或同期协议进行证明。但该规则并不妨碍证明当事人之间的后续协议，例如书面上市协议签署后签订的口头协议。\n\n（选项C） 向房地产经纪人支付佣金的协议不受欺诈法的约束。然而，只有当口头修改协议也不属于欺诈法的范围时，经纪人才能胜诉。\n\n教育目标：普通法忽略“不得口头修改”条款，并允许通过随后的口头协议修改书面合同，除非修改后的合同属于欺诈法的范围。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 149（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头修改）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为双方的口头协议是对其书面协议的有效修改。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":14,"sourceQuestionPage":40,"sourceQuestionPages":[40],"sourceExplanationPages":[41,42],"sourceAnswerPages":[41,42],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-015","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A ski-shop operator, in a telephone conversation with a glove manufacturer, ordered 12 pairs of vortex-lined ski gloves at the list price of $600 per dozen \"for delivery in 30 days.\" The glove manufacturer orally accepted the offer and immediately faxed to the ski- shop operator this signed memo: \"Confirming our agreement today for your purchase of a dozen pairs of vortex-lined ski gloves for $600, the shipment will be delivered in 30 days.\" Although the ski-shop operator received and read the manufacturer's message within minutes after its dispatch, she changed her mind three weeks later about the purchase and rejected the conforming shipment when it timely arrived. On learning of the rejection, does the manufacturer have a cause of action against the ski- shop operator for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the ski-shop operator neither paid for nor accepted any of the goods tendered.","B":"No, because the ski-shop operator never signed a writing evidencing a contract with the manufacturer.","C":"Yes, because the gloves were identified in the contract and tendered to the ski-shop operator.","D":"Yes, because the manufacturer's faxed memo to the ski-shop operator was sufficient to make the agreement enforceable."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nUnder the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of goods of $500 or more is only enforceable if it is in a writing signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. The UCC relaxes these requirements in transactions between merchants so that the statute of frauds is met as to both parties if one party sends a written confirmation and the recipient does not object in writing within 10 days. The written confirmation must:\n• provide a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed (eg, memo confirming the order)\n• contain the quantity of the goods or a means to determine it (eg, 12 pairs of vortex- lined ski gloves) and\n• contain a signature or other identification sufficient to bind the sender (eg, sender's fax number) Here, the manufacturer faxed the ski-shop operator a memo confirming the order for 12 pairs of vortex-lined ski gloves, which the ski-shop operator did not object to until three weeks later (ie, after the 10-day deadline). Since the fax number will identify the manufacturer as the sender, the memo will be sufficient to bind both parties—even without the ski-shop-operator's signature (Choice B). Therefore, the manufacturer has a cause of action for breach of contract. (Choice A) There are three exceptions to the UCC statute of frauds, one of which applies when some amount of payment or goods was received and accepted (not seen here). But since the parties here are merchants, the manufacturer's memo created an enforceable agreement.\n\n(Choice C) The gloves did not need to be tendered to the ski-shop operator to create an enforceable contract. The written confirmation merely needed to evidence the merchants' agreement and identify the quantity of the goods.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a transaction between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if (1) one party sends a written confirmation that satisfies the statute of frauds and (2) the other party does not object in writing within 10 days.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds formal requirements).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a transaction between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if (1) one party sends a written confirmation that satisfies the statute of frauds and (2) the other party does not object in writing within 10 days.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds formal requirements).","rule":"Under the UCC, a transaction between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if (1) one party sends a written confirmation that satisfies the statute of frauds and (2) the other party does not object in writing within 10 days.","irac":{"issue":"On learning of the rejection, does the manufacturer have a cause of action against the ski- shop operator for breach of contract?","rule":"Under the UCC, a transaction between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if (1) one party sends a written confirmation that satisfies the statute of frauds and (2) the other party does not object in writing within 10 days.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nUnder the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of goods of $500 or more is only enforceable if it is in a writing signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. The UCC relaxes these requirements in transactions between merchants so that the statute of frauds is met as to both parties if one party sends a written confirmation and the recipient does not object in writing within 10 days. The written confirmation must:\n• provide a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed (eg, memo confirming the order)\n• contain the quantity of the goods or a means to determine it (eg, 12 pairs of vortex- lined ski gloves) and\n• contain a signature or other identification sufficient to bind the sender (eg, sender's fax number) Here, the manufacturer faxed the ski-shop operator a memo confirming the order for 12 pairs of vortex-lined ski gloves, which the ski-shop operator did not object to until three weeks later (ie, after the 10-day deadline). Since the fax number will identify the manufacturer as the sender, the memo will be sufficient to bind both parties—even without the ski-shop-operator's signature (Choice B). Therefore, the manufacturer has a cause of action for breach of contract. (Choice A) There are three exceptions to the UCC statute of frauds, one of which applies when some amount of payment or goods was received and accepted (not seen here). But since the parties here are merchants, the manufacturer's memo created an enforceable agreement.\n\n(Choice C) The gloves did not need to be tendered to the ski-shop operator to create an enforceable contract. The written confirmation merely needed to evidence the merchants' agreement and identify the quantity of the goods.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the manufacturer's faxed memo to the ski-shop operator was sufficient to make the agreement enforceable."},"questionZh":"一家滑雪店经营者在与一家手套制造商进行电话交谈时，以每打 600 美元的标价订购了 12 双涡流衬里滑雪手套，“30 天内交货”。手套制造商口头接受了报价，并立即将这份签署的备忘录传真给滑雪店经营者：“今天确认我们同意您以 600 美元购买十几副涡流衬里滑雪手套，货物将在 30 天内交付。”尽管滑雪商店经营者在发货后几分钟内就收到并阅读了制造商的消息，但三周后她改变了购买决定，并在货物及时到达时拒绝了合格的货物。得知拒绝后，制造商是否有理由对滑雪店经营者提起违约诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为滑雪商店经营者既没有支付也没有接受任何投标的货物。","B":"不，因为滑雪商店经营者从未签署过证明与制造商签订合同的书面文件。","C":"是的，因为手套已在合同中确定并交付给滑雪店经营者。","D":"是的，因为制造商传真给滑雪店经营者的备忘录足以使协议得以执行。"},"explanationZh":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\n根据反欺诈法，价值 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同只有在由被强制执行方以书面形式签署的情况下才可执行。 UCC 放宽了商家之间交易的这些要求，以便如果一方发送书面确认且接收方在 10 天内未提出书面反对，则双方均符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。书面确认必须：\n• 提供合理的依据以相信合同已成立（例如，确认订单的备忘录）\n• 包含货物数量或确定数量的方法（例如，12 双涡流衬里滑雪手套）以及\n• 包含足以约束发件人的签名或其他身份证明（例如，发件人的传真号码） 此处，制造商向滑雪店经营者传真了一份备忘录，确认 12 双涡流衬里滑雪手套的订单，滑雪店经营者直到三周后（即 10 天期限后）才提出反对。由于传真号码会将制造商识别为发件人，因此即使没有滑雪商店经营者的签名（选项 B），备忘录也足以约束双方。因此，制造商有违约理由。 （选项 A）UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）存在三种例外情况，其中之一适用于收到并接受一定金额的付款或货物的情况（此处未显示）。但由于双方都是商人，制造商的备忘录创建了一个可执行的协议。\n\n（选择 C）手套不需要提供给滑雪店经营者来创建可执行的合同。书面确认只需证明商家同意并确定货物数量即可。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，如果 (1) 一方发送符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认，并且 (2) 另一方在 10 天内未提出书面反对，则商家之间的交易就满足双方的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）正式要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，如果 (1) 一方发送符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认，并且 (2) 另一方在 10 天内未提出书面反对，则商家之间的交易对双方而言均满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。","iracZh":{"issue":"得知拒绝后，制造商是否有理由对滑雪店经营者提起违约诉讼？","rule":"根据 UCC，如果 (1) 一方发送符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认，并且 (2) 另一方在 10 天内未提出书面反对，则商家之间的交易对双方而言均满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。","analysis":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\n根据反欺诈法，价值 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同只有在由被强制执行方以书面形式签署的情况下才可执行。 UCC 放宽了商家之间交易的这些要求，以便如果一方发送书面确认且接收方在 10 天内未提出书面反对，则双方均符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。书面确认必须：\n• 提供合理的依据以相信合同已成立（例如，确认订单的备忘录）\n• 包含货物数量或确定数量的方法（例如，12 双涡流衬里滑雪手套）以及\n• 包含足以约束发件人的签名或其他身份证明（例如，发件人的传真号码） 此处，制造商向滑雪店经营者传真了一份备忘录，确认 12 双涡流衬里滑雪手套的订单，滑雪店经营者直到三周后（即 10 天期限后）才提出反对。由于传真号码会将制造商识别为发件人，因此即使没有滑雪商店经营者的签名（选项 B），备忘录也足以约束双方。因此，制造商有违约理由。 （选项 A）UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）存在三种例外情况，其中之一适用于收到并接受一定金额的付款或货物的情况（此处未显示）。但由于双方都是商人，制造商的备忘录创建了一个可执行的协议。\n\n（选择 C）手套不需要提供给滑雪店经营者来创建可执行的合同。书面确认只需证明商家同意并确定货物数量即可。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，如果 (1) 一方发送符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认，并且 (2) 另一方在 10 天内未提出书面反对，则商家之间的交易就满足双方的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）正式要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为制造商传真给滑雪店经营者的备忘录足以使协议得以执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"shipment","ipa":"/ʃˈɪpmənt/","syllables":"ship·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 装船, 出货；[经] 运送, 运送(寄出)货物, 装载","contextualMeaning":"装运：将货物交付承运人运输","wordForm":"词；词形 s:shipments","sourceSentence":"\" The glove manufacturer orally accepted the offer and immediately faxed to the ski- shop operator this signed memo: \"Confirming our agreement today for your purchase of a dozen pairs of vortex-lined ski gloves for $600, the shipment will be delivered in 30 days.","synonyms":["dispatch","despatch","cargo","lading"]},{"term":"conforming","ipa":"/kʌnfˈɔɹmɪŋ/","syllables":"con·form·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[计] 一致性的","contextualMeaning":"符合合同：货物或交付满足合同全部要求","wordForm":"词；原形 conform；词形 0:conform/1:i/i:conforming","sourceSentence":"\" Although the ski-shop operator received and read the manufacturer's message within minutes after its dispatch, she changed her mind three weeks later about the purchase and rejected the conforming shipment when it timely arrived.","synonyms":["adjust","adapt"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":15,"sourceQuestionPage":43,"sourceQuestionPages":[43],"sourceExplanationPages":[44,45],"sourceAnswerPages":[44,45],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-016","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A landowner, having suffered severe losses on bad investments, was money-poor but land- rich by inheritance, owning several thousand acres of unencumbered timberland. He had a large family, and his fixed personal expenses were high. Pressed for cash, he advertised a proposed sale of standing timber on a choice 2,000-acre tract. The only response was an offer by a logger who owned a large, integrated construction enterprise, after inspection of the advertised tract. The logger offered to buy, sever, and remove the standing timber from the advertised tract at a cash price 70% lower than the regionally prevailing price for comparable timber rights. The landholder, by then in desperate financial straits and knowing little about timber values, signed and delivered to the logger a letter accepting the offer. If, before the logger commences performance, the landholder's investment fortunes suddenly improve and he wishes to get out of the timber deal with the logger, which of the following legal concepts affords his best prospect of effective cancellation?","choices":{"A":"Bad faith.","B":"Duress.","C":"Equitable estoppel.","D":"Unconscionability."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The defense of unconscionability is available when a contract's bargaining process and/or the contract terms were so unfair to one party that no reasonable person would have agreed to it. When this occurs, the court can modify or cancel (ie, refuse to enforce) the contract. Factors used to determine whether a contract is unconscionable include:\n• sophistication and knowledge of each party (eg, the parties' unequal knowledge of the timber industry)\n• disparity between the parties' bargaining power (eg, the landowner is money-poor and the logger is the only potential buyer)\n• disparity between the value of consideration exchanged by the parties (eg, the sale of timber rights on choice land for a price that is significantly lower than the prevailing rate) and\n• one-sidedness of contract terms (eg, the contract terms overwhelmingly favor the logger). Therefore, unconscionability provides the landowner his best prospect of effective cancellation of the contract. (Choice A) Good faith and fair dealing demand only actual honesty; here, there is no indication that the logger failed to meet this standard. Therefore, bad faith is not the landowner's best argument. (Choice B) Duress serves as a defense to contract enforceability only if a contracting party's assent was induced by an improper threat—which did not occur here. (Choice C) Equitable estoppel is a doctrine that prevents one party from changing its position after the other party has detrimentally relied on a misrepresentation of fact made by the first party. Here, however, the logger did not make a misrepresentation of fact to the landowner, so this doctrine does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: A party may assert the defense of unconscionability if the bargaining process and/or terms of the contract were so unfair to that party when the contract was made that no reasonable person would have agreed to it.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 208 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unconscionable contract or contract term).","educationalObjective":"A party may assert the defense of unconscionability if the bargaining process and/or terms of the contract were so unfair to that party when the contract was made that no reasonable person would have agreed to it.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 208 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unconscionable contract or contract term).","rule":"A party may assert the defense of unconscionability if the bargaining process and/or terms of the contract were so unfair to that party when the contract was made that no reasonable person would have agreed to it.","irac":{"issue":"If, before the logger commences performance, the landholder's investment fortunes suddenly improve and he wishes to get out of the timber deal with the logger, which of the following legal concepts affords his best prospect of effective cancellation?","rule":"A party may assert the defense of unconscionability if the bargaining process and/or terms of the contract were so unfair to that party when the contract was made that no reasonable person would have agreed to it.","analysis":"The defense of unconscionability is available when a contract's bargaining process and/or the contract terms were so unfair to one party that no reasonable person would have agreed to it. When this occurs, the court can modify or cancel (ie, refuse to enforce) the contract. Factors used to determine whether a contract is unconscionable include:\n• sophistication and knowledge of each party (eg, the parties' unequal knowledge of the timber industry)\n• disparity between the parties' bargaining power (eg, the landowner is money-poor and the logger is the only potential buyer)\n• disparity between the value of consideration exchanged by the parties (eg, the sale of timber rights on choice land for a price that is significantly lower than the prevailing rate) and\n• one-sidedness of contract terms (eg, the contract terms overwhelmingly favor the logger). Therefore, unconscionability provides the landowner his best prospect of effective cancellation of the contract. (Choice A) Good faith and fair dealing demand only actual honesty; here, there is no indication that the logger failed to meet this standard. Therefore, bad faith is not the landowner's best argument. (Choice B) Duress serves as a defense to contract enforceability only if a contracting party's assent was induced by an improper threat—which did not occur here. (Choice C) Equitable estoppel is a doctrine that prevents one party from changing its position after the other party has detrimentally relied on a misrepresentation of fact made by the first party. Here, however, the logger did not make a misrepresentation of fact to the landowner, so this doctrine does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer D: Unconscionability."},"questionZh":"一位因投资不当而蒙受严重损失的土地所有者，虽然资金匮乏，但继承的土地却很丰富，拥有数千英亩无产权负担的林地。他有一个大家庭，个人固定开支很高。由于资金紧张，他在一块 2,000 英亩的精选土地上发布了出售立木的广告。唯一的回应是一位拥有一家大型综合建筑企业的伐木者在检查了广告中的土地后提出的报价。伐木者提出以比该地区同类木材权的现行价格低 70% 的现金价格购买、砍伐和清除广告区域中的立木。当时，土地所有者正陷入绝望的财务困境，而且对木材价值知之甚少，他签署并向伐木者递交了一封接受报价的信函。如果在伐木者开始履约之前，土地所有者的投资财富突然好转，并且他希望退出与伐木者的木材交易，那么以下哪种法律概念为他提供了有效取消的最佳前景？","choicesZh":{"A":"不守信用。","B":"胁迫。","C":"公平禁止反言。","D":"不合情理。"},"explanationZh":"当合同的谈判过程和/或合同条款对一方非常不公平以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意时，就可以对不合理性进行抗辩。发生这种情况时，法院可以修改或取消（即拒绝执行）合同。用于确定合同是否不合理的因素包括：\n• 各方的成熟度和知识（例如，各方对木材行业的知识不平等）\n• 双方讨价还价能力的差异（例如，土地所有者资金匮乏，而伐木者是唯一的潜在买家）\n• 双方交换对价的价值之间的差异（例如，以远低于现行价格的价格出售选定土地上的木材权）和\n• 合同条款的片面性（例如，合同条款压倒性地有利于记录者）。因此，不合理行为为土地所有者提供了有效取消合同的最佳机会。 （选项A）诚信和公平交易只需要真正的诚实；在这里，没有迹象表明记录器未能满足此标准。因此，恶意并不是地主最好的论据。 （选择 B）只有当缔约方的同意是由不当威胁引起的（此处没有发生）时，胁迫才可以作为合同可执行性的抗辩。 （选项 C） 衡平法禁止反言是一种原则，可防止一方在另一方不利地依赖第一方对事实的歪曲事实后改变其立场。然而，在这里，伐木者没有向土地所有者歪曲事实，因此该原则不适用。\n\n教育目标：如果在签订合同时谈判过程和/或合同条款对该方非常不公平，以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意，则一方可以主张不合理性抗辩。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 208（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不合情理的合同或合同条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果合同订立时的谈判过程和/或合同条款对该方来说非常不公平，以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意，那么该方可以主张不合理抗辩。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果在伐木者开始履约之前，土地所有者的投资财富突然好转，并且他希望退出与伐木者的木材交易，那么以下哪种法律概念为他提供了有效取消的最佳前景？","rule":"如果合同订立时的谈判过程和/或合同条款对该方来说非常不公平，以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意，那么该方可以主张不合理抗辩。","analysis":"当合同的谈判过程和/或合同条款对一方非常不公平以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意时，就可以对不合理性进行抗辩。发生这种情况时，法院可以修改或取消（即拒绝执行）合同。用于确定合同是否不合理的因素包括：\n• 各方的成熟度和知识（例如，各方对木材行业的知识不平等）\n• 双方讨价还价能力的差异（例如，土地所有者资金匮乏，而伐木者是唯一的潜在买家）\n• 双方交换对价的价值之间的差异（例如，以远低于现行价格的价格出售选定土地上的木材权）和\n• 合同条款的片面性（例如，合同条款压倒性地有利于记录者）。因此，不合理行为为土地所有者提供了有效取消合同的最佳机会。 （选项A）诚信和公平交易只需要真正的诚实；在这里，没有迹象表明记录器未能满足此标准。因此，恶意并不是地主最好的论据。 （选择 B）只有当缔约方的同意是由不当威胁引起的（此处没有发生）时，胁迫才可以作为合同可执行性的抗辩。 （选项 C） 衡平法禁止反言是一种原则，可防止一方在另一方不利地依赖第一方对事实的歪曲事实后改变其立场。然而，在这里，伐木者没有向土地所有者歪曲事实，因此该原则不适用。\n\n教育目标：如果在签订合同时谈判过程和/或合同条款对该方非常不公平，以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意，则一方可以主张不合理性抗辩。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 208（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不合情理的合同或合同条款）。","conclusion":"答案 D：不合情理。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"good faith","ipa":"/ɡˈʊd fˈeɪθ/","syllables":"good faith","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"善意：诚实并遵守合理商业公平标准","contextualMeaning":"善意：诚实并遵守合理商业公平标准","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Good faith and fair dealing demand only actual honesty; here, there is no indication that the logger failed to meet this standard.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"unconscionable","ipa":"/ʌnkˈɑnʃənəbəl/","syllables":"un·con·scionable","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 不受良心支配的, 不合理的, 过度的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“unconscionable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Factors used to determine whether a contract is unconscionable include: • sophistication and knowledge of each party (eg, the parties' unequal knowledge of the timber industry) • disparity between the parties' bargaining power (eg, the landowner is money-poor and the logger is the only potential buyer) • disparity between the value of consideration exchanged by the parties (eg, the sale of timber rights on choice lan","synonyms":["exorbitant","extortionate","outrageous","steep"]},{"term":"bargaining","ipa":"/bˈɑɹɡɪnɪŋ/","syllables":"bar·gain·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 讨价还价, 谈判, 交涉；[经] 议价, 讨价还价, 交易","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“bargaining”","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:bargain/1:i/i:bargaining","sourceSentence":"A party may assert the defense of unconscionability if the bargaining process and/or terms of the contract were so unfair to that party when the contract was made that no reasonable person would have agreed to it.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-464b35178a499466"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":16,"sourceQuestionPage":46,"sourceQuestionPages":[46],"sourceExplanationPages":[47,48],"sourceAnswerPages":[47,48],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-017","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A contractor agreed to remodel a homeowner's garage for $5,000. Just before the parties signed the one-page written contract, the homeowner called to the contractor's attention the fact that the contract did not specify a time of completion. The parties orally agreed but did not specify in the contract that the contractor would complete the work in 60 days, and then they both signed the contract. The contract did not contain a merger clause. The contractor failed to finish the work in 60 days. The homeowner has sued the contractor for breach of contract. Is the court likely to admit evidence concerning the parties' oral agreement that the work would be completed in 60 days?","choices":{"A":"No, because the court must ascertain the meaning of the agreement from the terms of the written contract.","B":"No, because the oral agreement was merely part of the parties' negotiations.","C":"Yes, because the contract is ambiguous.","D":"Yes, because the time limit is an additional term that does not contradict the partially integrated written contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Integration\n\nType Definition Effect\n\nComplete Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms\n• Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some terms\n• Parol evidence admissible to supplement (not contradict) contract The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to modify or contradict the unambiguous terms of an integrated writing. But if the writing is partially integrated—ie, sets forth the parties' final agreement about some, but not all, terms—the parties can introduce supplemental evidence (oral or written) that is consistent with the writing and does not contradict its terms. Here, just before the parties signed the written contract, they orally agreed that the remodeling of the garage was to be completed within 60 days. The contract was partially integrated because it did not specify a time to complete the remodeling and did not contain a merger clause. As a result, evidence of the parties' oral agreement is likely admissible because the time limit is an additional term that does not contradict the written contract (Choice B). (Choices A & C) Evidence used to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term is excepted from the parol evidence rule. But here, the terms of the written contract are not ambiguous. Instead, the parties' oral agreement is admissible because it is being used to supplement (and does not contradict) the terms of the written contract.\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to supplement (ie, add to) the terms of a partially integrated writing, provided that the evidence is consistent with and does not contradict the written terms.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 216 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (parol evidence is admissible to explain or supplement a term when agreement is partially integrated).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to supplement (ie, add to) the terms of a partially integrated writing, provided that the evidence is consistent with and does not contradict the written terms.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 216 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (parol evidence is admissible to explain or supplement a term when agreement is partially integrated).","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to supplement (ie, add to) the terms of a partially integrated writing, provided that the evidence is consistent with and does not contradict the written terms.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to admit evidence concerning the parties' oral agreement that the work would be completed in 60 days?","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to supplement (ie, add to) the terms of a partially integrated writing, provided that the evidence is consistent with and does not contradict the written terms.","analysis":"Integration\n\nType Definition Effect\n\nComplete Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms\n• Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some terms\n• Parol evidence admissible to supplement (not contradict) contract The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to modify or contradict the unambiguous terms of an integrated writing. But if the writing is partially integrated—ie, sets forth the parties' final agreement about some, but not all, terms—the parties can introduce supplemental evidence (oral or written) that is consistent with the writing and does not contradict its terms. Here, just before the parties signed the written contract, they orally agreed that the remodeling of the garage was to be completed within 60 days. The contract was partially integrated because it did not specify a time to complete the remodeling and did not contain a merger clause. As a result, evidence of the parties' oral agreement is likely admissible because the time limit is an additional term that does not contradict the written contract (Choice B). (Choices A & C) Evidence used to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term is excepted from the parol evidence rule. But here, the terms of the written contract are not ambiguous. Instead, the parties' oral agreement is admissible because it is being used to supplement (and does not contradict) the terms of the written contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the time limit is an additional term that does not contradict the partially integrated written contract."},"questionZh":"承包商同意以 5,000 美元改造房主的车库。就在双方签署一页纸的书面合同之前，房主提请承包商注意，合同中没有具体规定竣工时间。双方口头约定但未在合同中明确承包商将在60天内完成工作，随后双方签订了合同。该合同不包含合并条款。承包商未能在 60 天内完成工程。房主已起诉承包商违反合同。法院是否可能采纳有关双方口头约定工程将在 60 天内完成的证据？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为法院必须从书面合同的条款中确定协议的含义。","B":"不，因为口头协议只是双方谈判的一部分。","C":"是的，因为合同不明确。","D":"是的，因为期限是附加条款，与部分整合的书面合同并不矛盾。"},"explanationZh":"整合\n\n类型 定义 效果\n\n完整的写作阐述\n• 排除整合各方关于所有条款的最终协议范围内的口头证据\n• 口头证据不可用于补充（即添加）或反驳合同中规定的部分书面内容\n• 排除口头证据整合双方的最终协议与某些条款的协议不一致的情况\n• 可采用口头证据来补充（而不是反驳）合同 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，以修改或反驳综合书面的明确条款。但如果书面文件是部分整合的——即，阐述了双方对部分而非全部条款的最终协议——双方可以提出与书面文件一致且不与其条款相矛盾的补充证据（口头或书面）。在这里，就在双方签订书面合同之前，双方口头约定车库改造工作将在60天内完成。该合同是部分整合的，因为它没有规定完成改造的时间，也没有包含合并条款。因此，当事人口头协议的证据可能会被采纳，因为期限是一个与书面合同不矛盾的附加条款（选择 B）。 （选项 A 和 C）用于澄清不明确合同条款含义的证据不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。但在这里，书面合同的条款并不含糊。相反，双方的口头协议是可以接受的，因为它被用来补充（并且不矛盾）书面合同的条款。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据来补充（即添加）部分整合的书面条款，前提是该证据与书面条款一致且不矛盾。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 216（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当协议部分整合时，可以采用口头证据来解释或补充条款）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据来补充（即添加）部分整合的书面条款，前提是该证据与书面条款一致且不矛盾。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否可能采纳有关双方口头约定工程将在 60 天内完成的证据？","rule":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据来补充（即添加）部分整合的书面条款，前提是该证据与书面条款一致且不矛盾。","analysis":"整合\n\n类型 定义 效果\n\n完整的写作阐述\n• 排除整合各方关于所有条款的最终协议范围内的口头证据\n• 口头证据不可用于补充（即添加）或反驳合同中规定的部分书面内容\n• 排除口头证据整合双方的最终协议与某些条款的协议不一致的情况\n• 可采用口头证据来补充（而不是反驳）合同 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，以修改或反驳综合书面的明确条款。但如果书面文件是部分整合的——即，阐述了双方对部分而非全部条款的最终协议——双方可以提出与书面文件一致且不与其条款相矛盾的补充证据（口头或书面）。在这里，就在双方签订书面合同之前，双方口头约定车库改造工作将在60天内完成。该合同是部分整合的，因为它没有规定完成改造的时间，也没有包含合并条款。因此，当事人口头协议的证据可能会被采纳，因为期限是一个与书面合同不矛盾的附加条款（选择 B）。 （选项 A 和 C）用于澄清不明确合同条款含义的证据不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。但在这里，书面合同的条款并不含糊。相反，双方的口头协议是可以接受的，因为它被用来补充（并且不矛盾）书面合同的条款。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据来补充（即添加）部分整合的书面条款，前提是该证据与书面条款一致且不矛盾。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 216（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当协议部分整合时，可以采用口头证据来解释或补充条款）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为期限是附加条款，与部分整合的书面合同并不矛盾。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":17,"sourceQuestionPage":49,"sourceQuestionPages":[49],"sourceExplanationPages":[50,51],"sourceAnswerPages":[50,51],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-018","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A doctor was employed at a hospital when she applied for a personal loan from a bank. She accurately disclosed her income in the loan application. Before the bank had reached a decision regarding the loan application, the doctor was fired from the hospital. The doctor decided not to inform the bank of this fact because she feared that the bank would refuse to give her the loan. A few days later, a bank representative called the doctor and informed her that her loan application was approved. The doctor accepted the terms of the loan and signed the loan agreement. Before disbursing the funds, the bank learned of the doctor's firing and refused to honor the loan agreement. If the doctor sues the bank for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the doctor's ability to repay the loan was a condition precedent to the disbursement of the funds.","B":"No, because the doctor's nondisclosure of her firing was a fraudulent misrepresentation.","C":"Yes, because the bank's reliance on the doctor's disclosure of her income in the loan application was unjustifiable.","D":"Yes, because the loan agreement was memorialized in a signed writing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When disclosure is required to avoid misrepresentation\n\nPrevious assertion\n• Disclosure would prevent previous assertion from being a misrepresentation or fraudulent or material Mistake about basic\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about basic assumption assumption of contract AND\n\n• Failure to disclose would constitute lack of good faith & fair dealing Fiduciary\n• Other party is entitled to know fact because of relationship confidential/fiduciary relationship\n\nMistake about\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about writing contents or effect of writing that embodies agreement\n\nA misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when all of the following elements are met:\n• The misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with the intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent).*\n\n• The misrepresentation induced assent to the contract.\n\n• The adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nNondisclosure of a known fact is treated as an untrue assertion if, among other things, the nondisclosing party knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent. Here, the doctor accurately disclosed her income in the loan application but was then fired. She knew that she needed to disclose this fact to the bank to prevent her previous disclosure from being fraudulent but failed to do so. Since the bank was unaware of her firing, it justifiably agreed to give her a loan. Therefore, the contract is voidable by the bank, and the doctor is unlikely to prevail in her breach-of-contract suit. *A misrepresentation is also material if the maker knows that it will likely induce this particular party's assent—even if a reasonable person would not be induced. (Choice A) A condition precedent is an explicit contract term requiring a future event to occur before a party becomes obligated to perform. But here, there is no indication that the\n\nbank's obligation to disburse the funds was explicitly conditioned on the doctor's ability to repay the loan. (Choice C) Reliance is almost always justified unless the misrepresentation was obviously false or known by the adversely affected party to be false. Here, the doctor's disclosure was accurate (not obviously false) when it was made and the bank was unaware of her firing when it approved the loan, so the bank's reliance was justified. (Choice D) The fact that the loan agreement was memorialized in a signed writing does not prevent it from being voidable on the basis of the doctor's fraudulent misrepresentation.\n\nEducational objective: Nondisclosure of a known fact is treated as an untrue assertion of fact (ie, misrepresentation) if the nondisclosing party knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when nondisclosure is a misrepresentation).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Nondisclosure of a known fact is treated as an untrue assertion of fact (ie, misrepresentation) if the nondisclosing party knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when nondisclosure is a misrepresentation).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Nondisclosure of a known fact is treated as an untrue assertion of fact (ie, misrepresentation) if the nondisclosing party knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.","irac":{"issue":"If the doctor sues the bank for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","rule":"Nondisclosure of a known fact is treated as an untrue assertion of fact (ie, misrepresentation) if the nondisclosing party knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.","analysis":"When disclosure is required to avoid misrepresentation\n\nPrevious assertion\n• Disclosure would prevent previous assertion from being a misrepresentation or fraudulent or material Mistake about basic\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about basic assumption assumption of contract AND\n\n• Failure to disclose would constitute lack of good faith & fair dealing Fiduciary\n• Other party is entitled to know fact because of relationship confidential/fiduciary relationship\n\nMistake about\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about writing contents or effect of writing that embodies agreement\n\nA misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when all of the following elements are met:\n• The misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with the intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent).*\n\n• The misrepresentation induced assent to the contract.\n\n• The adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nNondisclosure of a known fact is treated as an untrue assertion if, among other things, the nondisclosing party knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent. Here, the doctor accurately disclosed her income in the loan application but was then fired. She knew that she needed to disclose this fact to the bank to prevent her previous disclosure from being fraudulent but failed to do so. Since the bank was unaware of her firing, it justifiably agreed to give her a loan. Therefore, the contract is voidable by the bank, and the doctor is unlikely to prevail in her breach-of-contract suit. *A misrepresentation is also material if the maker knows that it will likely induce this particular party's assent—even if a reasonable person would not be induced. (Choice A) A condition precedent is an explicit contract term requiring a future event to occur before a party becomes obligated to perform. But here, there is no indication that the\n\nbank's obligation to disburse the funds was explicitly conditioned on the doctor's ability to repay the loan. (Choice C) Reliance is almost always justified unless the misrepresentation was obviously false or known by the adversely affected party to be false. Here, the doctor's disclosure was accurate (not obviously false) when it was made and the bank was unaware of her firing when it approved the loan, so the bank's reliance was justified. (Choice D) The fact that the loan agreement was memorialized in a signed writing does not prevent it from being voidable on the basis of the doctor's fraudulent misrepresentation.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the doctor's nondisclosure of her firing was a fraudulent misrepresentation."},"questionZh":"一名医生在一家医院工作，向银行申请个人贷款。她在贷款申请中准确地披露了自己的收入。在银行就贷款申请做出决定之前，该医生被医院解雇了。医生决定不将这一事实告知银行，因为她担心银行会拒绝给她贷款。几天后，银行代表打电话给医生，告诉她她的贷款申请已获批准。医生接受了贷款条款并签署了贷款协议。在发放资金之前，银行得知医生被解雇，并拒绝履行贷款协议。如果医生起诉银行违约，她有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为医生偿还贷款的能力是支付资金的先决条件。","B":"不，因为医生未透露她被解雇的事实是一种欺诈性的失实陈述。","C":"是的，因为银行依赖医生在贷款申请中披露她的收入是不合理的。","D":"是的，因为贷款协议是以签字的书面形式记录下来的。"},"explanationZh":"当需要披露以避免虚假陈述时\n\n先前的断言\n• 披露将防止先前的断言成为失实陈述、欺诈或重大错误。\n• 披露将纠正其他方关于合同基本假设的错误以及\n\n• 不披露将构成缺乏诚信和公平交易 受托人\n• 由于保密/信托关系，另一方有权了解事实\n\n关于错误\n• 披露将纠正对方对体现协议的书写内容或书写效果的错误\n\n虚假陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，当满足以下所有要素时，会使受到不利影响的一方无效合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或鲁莽地做出，旨在诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）。*\n\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同。\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n除其他事项外，如果未披露方知道披露对于防止先前的断言是欺诈性的，则不披露已知事实将被视为不真实的断言。在这里，医生在贷款申请中准确地披露了她的收入，但随后被解雇。她知道她需要向银行披露这一事实，以防止她之前的披露被欺诈，但她没有这样做。由于银行不知道她被解雇，所以理所当然地同意给她贷款。因此，合同可以被银行撤销，医生的违约诉讼不太可能胜诉。 *如果制造者知道它可能会引起该特定方的同意（即使一个理性的人不会被诱导），那么失实陈述也是重大的。 （选择 A）先决条件是明确的合同条款，要求在一方有义务履行之前发生未来事件。但在这里，没有任何迹象表明\n\n银行支付资金的义务明确以医生偿还贷款的能力为条件。 （选择 C） 信赖几乎总是合理的，除非失实陈述明显是虚假的或受到不利影响的一方知道是虚假的。在这里，医生的披露在做出时是准确的（并非明显错误），并且银行在批准贷款时并不知道她被解雇，因此银行的依赖是合理的。 （选项 D） 贷款协议是以签字的书面形式记录的事实并不妨碍该协议因医生的欺诈性失实陈述而被撤销。\n\n教育目标：如果未披露方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性，有必要进行披露，则不披露已知事实将被视为对事实的不真实断言（即虚假陈述）。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 161（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当不披露属于失实陈述时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果未披露方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性，有必要进行披露，则不披露已知事实将被视为对事实的不真实断言（即虚假陈述）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果医生起诉银行违约，她有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"如果未披露方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性，有必要进行披露，则不披露已知事实将被视为对事实的不真实断言（即虚假陈述）。","analysis":"当需要披露以避免虚假陈述时\n\n先前的断言\n• 披露将防止先前的断言成为失实陈述、欺诈或重大错误。\n• 披露将纠正其他方关于合同基本假设的错误以及\n\n• 不披露将构成缺乏诚信和公平交易 受托人\n• 由于保密/信托关系，另一方有权了解事实\n\n关于错误\n• 披露将纠正对方对体现协议的书写内容或书写效果的错误\n\n虚假陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，当满足以下所有要素时，会使受到不利影响的一方无效合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或鲁莽地做出，旨在诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）。*\n\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同。\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n除其他事项外，如果未披露方知道披露对于防止先前的断言是欺诈性的，则不披露已知事实将被视为不真实的断言。在这里，医生在贷款申请中准确地披露了她的收入，但随后被解雇。她知道她需要向银行披露这一事实，以防止她之前的披露被欺诈，但她没有这样做。由于银行不知道她被解雇，所以理所当然地同意给她贷款。因此，合同可以被银行撤销，医生的违约诉讼不太可能胜诉。 *如果制造者知道它可能会引起该特定方的同意（即使一个理性的人不会被诱导），那么失实陈述也是重大的。 （选择 A）先决条件是明确的合同条款，要求在一方有义务履行之前发生未来事件。但在这里，没有任何迹象表明\n\n银行支付资金的义务明确以医生偿还贷款的能力为条件。 （选择 C） 信赖几乎总是合理的，除非失实陈述明显是虚假的或受到不利影响的一方知道是虚假的。在这里，医生的披露在做出时是准确的（并非明显错误），并且银行在批准贷款时并不知道她被解雇，因此银行的依赖是合理的。 （选项 D） 贷款协议是以签字的书面形式记录的事实并不妨碍该协议因医生的欺诈性失实陈述而被撤销。\n\n教育目标：如果未披露方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性，有必要进行披露，则不披露已知事实将被视为对事实的不真实断言（即虚假陈述）。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 161（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当不披露属于失实陈述时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为医生未透露她被解雇的事实是一种欺诈性的失实陈述。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"condition precedent","ipa":"/kʌndˈɪʃən pɹˈɛsɪdənt/","syllables":"con·di·tion prece·dent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"先决条件：发生后相应履行义务才到期的事件","contextualMeaning":"先决条件：发生后相应履行义务才到期的事件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the doctor's ability to repay the loan was a condition precedent to the disbursement of the funds.","synonyms":["condition before performance"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":18,"sourceQuestionPage":52,"sourceQuestionPages":[52],"sourceExplanationPages":[53,54],"sourceAnswerPages":[53,54],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-019","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A computer company contracted in writing with a bank to sell and deliver to the bank a mainframe computer using a new type of technology, then under development but not perfected by the company, at a price substantially lower than that of a similar computer using current technology. The contract's delivery term was \"F.O.B. bank, on or before July 31.\" The company tendered the computer to the bank on August 15. The bank rejected it because of the delay. If the company sues the bank for breach of contract, which of the following facts, if proved, will best support a recovery by the company?","choices":{"A":"A usage in the relevant trade allows computer sellers a 30-day leeway in a specified time of delivery, unless the usage is expressly negated by the contract.","B":"On the assumption that the bank was getting a \"super deal\" for its money, the company believed the bank would not reject the computer because of the late tender of delivery.","C":"The company's delay in tender was caused by a truckers' strike.","D":"The delay did not materially harm the bank."},"answer":"A","explanation":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, computers) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of trade usage can be used to explain or supplement the express terms of a final, written contract so long as the evidence is reasonably consistent with (ie, not negated by) those terms. Trade usage refers to a practice or method of dealing that is observed with such regularity in the industry that the parties to a contract would be expected to observe it. Here, the computer company and bank's written contract was silent as to leeway for delivery. As a result, evidence of trade usage can be used to supplement the contract. There is a practice in the relevant trade that allows computer sellers a 30-day leeway in a specified time of delivery. Since this fact would show that the bank breached the contract by rejecting the computer because of a 15-day delay, it would best support a recovery by the company. (Choice B) The computer company's belief that the bank would not reject the computer for late delivery because it was getting a \"super deal\" for its money is irrelevant. (Choice C) A seller's delay in delivery (or nondelivery) is not a breach if performance has become commercially impracticable because of unforeseen circumstances not within the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made. But a truckers' strike is not an unforeseen circumstance that would trigger impracticability.\n\n(Choice D) Under the UCC's perfect-tender rule, the goods and their delivery must conform to the contract in every respect. Therefore, the fact that the computer company's delay did not materially harm the bank would not support a recovery by the company.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC parol evidence rule allows the use of trade-usage evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final, written contract so long as the evidence is reasonably consistent with, not negated by, those terms.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (trade usage is admissible to explain or supplement contract's terms).\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (definition of trade usage).","educationalObjective":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows the use of trade-usage evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final, written contract so long as the evidence is reasonably consistent with, not negated by, those terms.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (trade usage is admissible to explain or supplement contract's terms).\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (definition of trade usage).","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows the use of trade-usage evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final, written contract so long as the evidence is reasonably consistent with, not negated by, those terms.","irac":{"issue":"If the company sues the bank for breach of contract, which of the following facts, if proved, will best support a recovery by the company?","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows the use of trade-usage evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final, written contract so long as the evidence is reasonably consistent with, not negated by, those terms.","analysis":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, computers) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of trade usage can be used to explain or supplement the express terms of a final, written contract so long as the evidence is reasonably consistent with (ie, not negated by) those terms. Trade usage refers to a practice or method of dealing that is observed with such regularity in the industry that the parties to a contract would be expected to observe it. Here, the computer company and bank's written contract was silent as to leeway for delivery. As a result, evidence of trade usage can be used to supplement the contract. There is a practice in the relevant trade that allows computer sellers a 30-day leeway in a specified time of delivery. Since this fact would show that the bank breached the contract by rejecting the computer because of a 15-day delay, it would best support a recovery by the company. (Choice B) The computer company's belief that the bank would not reject the computer for late delivery because it was getting a \"super deal\" for its money is irrelevant. (Choice C) A seller's delay in delivery (or nondelivery) is not a breach if performance has become commercially impracticable because of unforeseen circumstances not within the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made. But a truckers' strike is not an unforeseen circumstance that would trigger impracticability.\n\n(Choice D) Under the UCC's perfect-tender rule, the goods and their delivery must conform to the contract in every respect. Therefore, the fact that the computer company's delay did not materially harm the bank would not support a recovery by the company.","conclusion":"Answer A: A usage in the relevant trade allows computer sellers a 30-day leeway in a specified time of delivery, unless the usage is expressly negated by the contract."},"questionZh":"一家计算机公司与一家银行签订书面合同，向银行出售并交付一台采用新型技术的大型计算机，当时该公司正在开发但尚未完善，其价格远低于采用当前技术的类似计算机。该合同的交割期限为“7 月 31 日或之前，银行离岸价”。该公司于8月15日向银行投标计算机，但银行因延误而拒绝了该计算机。如果公司起诉银行违约，下列哪项事实如果得到证实最能支持公司追偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"相关贸易中的使用允许计算机卖家在指定的交货时间内有30天的回旋余地，除非合同明确否定该使用。","B":"假设银行的资金得到了“超级优惠”，该公司相信银行不会因为延迟交付而拒绝计算机。","C":"该公司推迟招标是由于卡车司机罢工造成的。","D":"延迟并未对银行造成重大损害。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品（例如计算机）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），贸易惯例的证据可用于解释或补充最终书面合同的明示条款，只要该证据与这些条款合理一致（即不被这些条款否定）。贸易惯例是指在行业中经常遵守的交易惯例或方法，因此期望合同双方遵守。在这里，计算机公司和银行的书面合同没有提及交货的余地。因此，贸易用途的证据可以用来补充合同。相关行业有一个惯例，允许电脑卖家在指定的交货时间内有30天的回旋余地。由于这一事实表明银行因延迟 15 天而拒绝接收计算机，从而违反了合同，因此最好支持公司追偿。 （选择 B） 计算机公司相信银行不会因为延迟交付而拒绝计算机，因为它的钱得到了“超级交易”，这是无关紧要的。 （选择C）如果由于签订合同时双方未预见到的不可预见的情况而导致履行在商业上不可行，则卖方延迟交货（或不交货）不构成违约。但卡车司机的罢工并不是一种无法预见的情况，不会导致不切实际。\n\n（选项D） 根据UCC 的完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），货物及其交付必须在各个方面都符合合同。因此，计算机公司的延误并未对银行造成重大损害，这一事实并不支持该公司的赔偿。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易使用证据来解释或补充最终书面合同的条款，只要该证据与这些条款合理一致，而不是被这些条款否定。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（允许贸易用途来解释或补充合同条款）。\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（贸易用途的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易使用证据来解释或补充最终书面合同的条款，只要该证据与这些条款合理一致，而不是被这些条款否定。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果公司起诉银行违约，下列哪项事实如果得到证实最能支持公司追偿？","rule":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易使用证据来解释或补充最终书面合同的条款，只要该证据与这些条款合理一致，而不是被这些条款否定。","analysis":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品（例如计算机）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），贸易惯例的证据可用于解释或补充最终书面合同的明示条款，只要该证据与这些条款合理一致（即不被这些条款否定）。贸易惯例是指在行业中经常遵守的交易惯例或方法，因此期望合同双方遵守。在这里，计算机公司和银行的书面合同没有提及交货的余地。因此，贸易用途的证据可以用来补充合同。相关行业有一个惯例，允许电脑卖家在指定的交货时间内有30天的回旋余地。由于这一事实表明银行因延迟 15 天而拒绝接收计算机，从而违反了合同，因此最好支持公司追偿。 （选择 B） 计算机公司相信银行不会因为延迟交付而拒绝计算机，因为它的钱得到了“超级交易”，这是无关紧要的。 （选择C）如果由于签订合同时双方未预见到的不可预见的情况而导致履行在商业上不可行，则卖方延迟交货（或不交货）不构成违约。但卡车司机的罢工并不是一种无法预见的情况，不会导致不切实际。\n\n（选项D） 根据UCC 的完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），货物及其交付必须在各个方面都符合合同。因此，计算机公司的延误并未对银行造成重大损害，这一事实并不支持该公司的赔偿。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易使用证据来解释或补充最终书面合同的条款，只要该证据与这些条款合理一致，而不是被这些条款否定。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（允许贸易用途来解释或补充合同条款）。\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（贸易用途的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 A：相关贸易中的使用允许计算机卖家在指定的交货时间内有30天的回旋余地，除非合同明确否定该使用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"course of performance","ipa":"/kˈɔɹs ˈəv pɝfˈɔɹməns/","syllables":"course of per·for·mance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"履行过程：本合同反复履行形成的解释依据","contextualMeaning":"履行过程：本合同反复履行形成的解释依据","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"UCC parol evidence rule Description Priority of evidence Inadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control Admissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage Course of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contr","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"perfect tender rule","ipa":"/pɝfˈɛkt tˈɛndɝ ɹˈul/","syllables":"per·fect ten·der rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"完美履行规则：货物或交付在任何方面不合约时买方原则上可拒收","contextualMeaning":"完美履行规则：货物或交付在任何方面不合约时买方原则上可拒收","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A computer company contracted in writing with a bank to sell and deliver to the bank a mainframe computer using a new type of technology, then under development but not perfected by the company, at a price substantially lower than that of a similar computer using current technology.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"course of dealing","ipa":"/kˈɔɹs ˈəv dˈilɪŋ/","syllables":"course of deal·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"交易过程：双方以往交易形成的共同理解","contextualMeaning":"交易过程：双方以往交易形成的共同理解","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"UCC parol evidence rule Description Priority of evidence Inadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control Admissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage Course of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contr","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"impracticable","ipa":"/ˌɪmpɹˈæktɪkəbəl/","syllables":"im·prac·ti·ca·ble","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 不能实施的, 难办的, 不能通行的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“impracticable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) A seller's delay in delivery (or nondelivery) is not a breach if performance has become commercially impracticable because of unforeseen circumstances not within the contemplation of the parties when the contract was made.","synonyms":["infeasible","unfeasible","unworkable"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":19,"sourceQuestionPage":55,"sourceQuestionPages":[55],"sourceExplanationPages":[56,57],"sourceAnswerPages":[56,57],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-020","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A cattle rancher operates a large ranch with many employees. State law requires all employees within the state to pay a state income tax, and all employers are required to accurately report their employees' incomes. Enforcement of the state law is a civil matter. There are no criminal penalties for failure to pay or failure to accurately report. The rancher entered a written employment contract with a veterinarian, who agreed to manage the health of the rancher's cattle for six months. Both parties knew about the state income tax, and the veterinarian wanted to avoid paying it. The rancher therefore agreed to compensate the veterinarian by paying the veterinarian's monthly mortgage directly to the bank without reporting the payments to the state. The rancher paid the veterinarian's mortgage for the first three months of the contract and then stopped making payments even though the veterinarian continued to work. If the veterinarian sues the rancher, what will he likely recover?","choices":{"A":"Expectation damages, because the rancher breached the contract and it did not involve any criminal conduct.","B":"Restitution damages, because the rancher withdrew from the agreement by failing to make additional mortgage payments.","C":"Restitution damages, because the veterinarian was less culpable than the rancher.","D":"Nothing, because the contract had an illegal purpose."},"answer":"D","explanation":"An illegal contract arises when one or both parties' purpose, formation, or performance is against the law—such as the rancher's agreement not to report the veterinarian's income as required by state law. Such contracts generally are void and there is no remedy for breach. However, an illegal contract is enforceable for the full amount of the nonbreaching party's expectation damages if:\n• the nonbreaching party was justifiably ignorant of the facts that made the contract illegal*\n• the nonbreaching party substantially performed and lacked an illegal purpose\n• the contract can be easily separated into legal and illegal parts or\n• the contract is illegal due to a violation of a licensing requirement designed only to raise revenue. None of these exceptions apply here. The veterinarian knew about the state income tax and purposely contracted to evade it. The agreed method of compensation is entirely illegal and cannot be separated from any legal part. Additionally, the illegality does not stem from a licensing violation. As a result, the veterinarian cannot recover expectation damages (Choice A). However, restitution damages may be an alternative form of recovery. A party to an illegal contract can recover such damages if that party conferred a benefit on the other party and:\n• was justifiably ignorant of the facts that made the contract illegal*\n• was less culpable than the other party or\n• withdrew before the contract's illegal purpose was achieved and did not engage in serious misconduct. Here, both parties knew about the law, so they were equally culpable in agreeing to violate it (Choice C). And only after the contract's illegal purpose was achieved through the first few payments did the rancher (not the veterinarian) withdraw by failing to make additional payments (Choice B). Therefore, the veterinarian also cannot recover restitution damages. *However, recovery on this basis will be barred if the other party also was justifiably ignorant.\n\nEducational objective: An illegal contract generally is void and there is no remedy for a breach. However, in limited situations, a party to an illegal contract can recover either expectation damages (eg, if the party was justifiably ignorant of the illegality) OR restitution damages (eg, if the party was less culpable than the other party).\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 178 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining when promises are unenforceable on public-policy grounds).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 197–199 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (availability of restitution).","educationalObjective":"An illegal contract generally is void and there is no remedy for a breach. However, in limited situations, a party to an illegal contract can recover either expectation damages (eg, if the party was justifiably ignorant of the illegality) OR restitution damages (eg, if the party was less culpable than the other party).","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 178 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining when promises are unenforceable on public-policy grounds).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 197–199 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (availability of restitution).","rule":"An illegal contract generally is void and there is no remedy for a breach. However, in limited situations, a party to an illegal contract can recover either expectation damages (eg, if the party was justifiably ignorant of the illegality) OR restitution damages (eg, if the party was less culpable than the other party).","irac":{"issue":"If the veterinarian sues the rancher, what will he likely recover?","rule":"An illegal contract generally is void and there is no remedy for a breach. However, in limited situations, a party to an illegal contract can recover either expectation damages (eg, if the party was justifiably ignorant of the illegality) OR restitution damages (eg, if the party was less culpable than the other party).","analysis":"An illegal contract arises when one or both parties' purpose, formation, or performance is against the law—such as the rancher's agreement not to report the veterinarian's income as required by state law. Such contracts generally are void and there is no remedy for breach. However, an illegal contract is enforceable for the full amount of the nonbreaching party's expectation damages if:\n• the nonbreaching party was justifiably ignorant of the facts that made the contract illegal*\n• the nonbreaching party substantially performed and lacked an illegal purpose\n• the contract can be easily separated into legal and illegal parts or\n• the contract is illegal due to a violation of a licensing requirement designed only to raise revenue. None of these exceptions apply here. The veterinarian knew about the state income tax and purposely contracted to evade it. The agreed method of compensation is entirely illegal and cannot be separated from any legal part. Additionally, the illegality does not stem from a licensing violation. As a result, the veterinarian cannot recover expectation damages (Choice A). However, restitution damages may be an alternative form of recovery. A party to an illegal contract can recover such damages if that party conferred a benefit on the other party and:\n• was justifiably ignorant of the facts that made the contract illegal*\n• was less culpable than the other party or\n• withdrew before the contract's illegal purpose was achieved and did not engage in serious misconduct. Here, both parties knew about the law, so they were equally culpable in agreeing to violate it (Choice C). And only after the contract's illegal purpose was achieved through the first few payments did the rancher (not the veterinarian) withdraw by failing to make additional payments (Choice B). Therefore, the veterinarian also cannot recover restitution damages. *However, recovery on this basis will be barred if the other party also was justifiably ignorant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Nothing, because the contract had an illegal purpose."},"questionZh":"牧场主经营着一个拥有许多员工的大牧场。州法律要求州内所有雇员缴纳州所得税，所有雇主都必须准确报告雇员的收入。州法律的执行是民事事务。未付款或未准确报告不会受到刑事处罚。牧场主与兽医签订了书面雇佣合同，兽医同意在六个月内管理牧场主牛的健康。双方都知道州所得税，而兽医想避免缴纳。因此，牧场主同意通过直接向银行支付兽医每月抵押贷款的方式来补偿兽医，而不向州报告付款情况。牧场主支付了兽医合同前三个月的抵押贷款，然后停止付款，尽管兽医继续工作。如果兽医起诉牧场主，他可能会得到什么赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"预期损害赔偿，因为牧场主违反了合同，并且不涉及任何犯罪行为。","B":"赔偿损失，因为牧场主因未能支付额外的抵押贷款而退出协议。","C":"赔偿损失，因为兽医的罪责比牧场主轻。","D":"没什么，因为合同有非法目的。"},"explanationZh":"当一方或双方的目的、形成或履行违反法律时，例如牧场主同意不按照州法律的要求报告兽医的收入，就会产生非法合同。此类合同通常是无效的，并且对于违约行为没有补救措施。然而，在以下情况下，非法合同可以按照非违约方预期损失的全额执行：\n• 守约方有理由不了解导致合同非法的事实*\n• 守约方实质上履行了义务并且没有非法目的\n• 合同可以很容易地分为合法部分和非法部分，或者\n• 由于违反了旨在增加收入的许可要求，该合同是非法的。这些例外情况均不适用于此处。兽医知道州所得税，并故意签订合同来逃避税。约定的赔偿方式完全违法，不能脱离任何法律部分。此外，违法行为并非源于违反许可规定。结果，兽医无法收回预期损失（选择 A）。然而，恢复原状可能是另一种恢复形式。非法合同的一方如果向另一方授予利益并且满足以下条件，则可以追偿此类损害：\n• 有理由不了解导致合同非法的事实*\n• 与另一方相比，其罪责较小，或者\n• 在合同非法目的实现之前退出，并且没有从事严重不当行为。在这里，双方都了解法律，因此他们同意违反法律同样有罪（选项C）。只有在通过前几笔付款实现了合同的非法目的之后，牧场主（而不是兽医）才因未支付额外付款而退出（选择B）。因此，兽医也无法收回损害赔偿。 *但是，如果对方也有理由不知情，则在此基础上进行的恢复将被禁止。\n\n教育目标：非法合同通常是无效的，并且对于违约行为没有补救措施。然而，在有限的情况下，非法合同的一方可以收回预期损害赔偿（例如，如果该方有理由不知道违法行为）或返还利益赔偿（例如，如果该方的罪责比另一方轻）。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 178（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释何时承诺因公共政策原因而无法执行）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 197–199（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（恢复原状）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非法合同通常是无效的，并且对于违约行为没有补救措施。然而，在有限的情况下，非法合同的一方可以收回预期损害赔偿（例如，如果该方有理由不知道违法行为）或返还利益赔偿（例如，如果该方的罪责比另一方轻）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果兽医起诉牧场主，他可能会得到什么赔偿？","rule":"非法合同通常是无效的，并且对于违约行为没有补救措施。然而，在有限的情况下，非法合同的一方可以收回预期损害赔偿（例如，如果该方有理由不知道违法行为）或返还利益赔偿（例如，如果该方的罪责比另一方轻）。","analysis":"当一方或双方的目的、形成或履行违反法律时，例如牧场主同意不按照州法律的要求报告兽医的收入，就会产生非法合同。此类合同通常是无效的，并且对于违约行为没有补救措施。然而，在以下情况下，非法合同可以按照非违约方预期损失的全额执行：\n• 守约方有理由不了解导致合同非法的事实*\n• 守约方实质上履行了义务并且没有非法目的\n• 合同可以很容易地分为合法部分和非法部分，或者\n• 由于违反了旨在增加收入的许可要求，该合同是非法的。这些例外情况均不适用于此处。兽医知道州所得税，并故意签订合同来逃避税。约定的赔偿方式完全违法，不能脱离任何法律部分。此外，违法行为并非源于违反许可规定。结果，兽医无法收回预期损失（选择 A）。然而，恢复原状可能是另一种恢复形式。非法合同的一方如果向另一方授予利益并且满足以下条件，则可以追偿此类损害：\n• 有理由不了解导致合同非法的事实*\n• 与另一方相比，其罪责较小，或者\n• 在合同非法目的实现之前退出，并且没有从事严重不当行为。在这里，双方都了解法律，因此他们同意违反法律同样有罪（选项C）。只有在通过前几笔付款实现了合同的非法目的之后，牧场主（而不是兽医）才因未支付额外付款而退出（选择B）。因此，兽医也无法收回损害赔偿。 *但是，如果对方也有理由不知情，则在此基础上进行的恢复将被禁止。\n\n教育目标：非法合同通常是无效的，并且对于违约行为没有补救措施。然而，在有限的情况下，非法合同的一方可以收回预期损害赔偿（例如，如果该方有理由不知道违法行为）或返还利益赔偿（例如，如果该方的罪责比另一方轻）。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 178（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释何时承诺因公共政策原因而无法执行）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 197–199（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（恢复原状）。","conclusion":"答案 D：没什么，因为合同有非法目的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"expectation damages","ipa":"/ˌɛkspɛktˈeɪʃən dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"ex·pec·ta·tion dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"期待利益赔偿：使守约方处于合同已履行的位置","contextualMeaning":"期待利益赔偿：使守约方处于合同已履行的位置","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Expectation damages, because the rancher breached the contract and it did not involve any criminal conduct.","synonyms":["benefit-of-the-bargain damages"]},{"term":"restitution damages","ipa":"/ɹˌɛstɪtˈuʃən dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"resti·tu·tion dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"返还利益：返还违约方从守约方取得的不当利益","contextualMeaning":"返还利益：返还违约方从守约方取得的不当利益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Restitution damages, because the rancher withdrew from the agreement by failing to make additional mortgage payments.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6c0166348577dc88"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":20,"sourceQuestionPage":58,"sourceQuestionPages":[58],"sourceExplanationPages":[59,60],"sourceAnswerPages":[59,60],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-021","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A father went to a music store to inquire about purchasing a violin and private music lessons for his daughter. Based on the store owner's recommendation, the father agreed to purchase a violin for $1,500. At the same time, the store owner orally agreed to give the daughter four lessons per month for the next six months at a rate of $50 per lesson. The father and the store owner then signed a contract for the sale of the violin. A provision in the contract stated: \"This writing reflects the entire agreement of the parties, and there are no other agreements or understandings not set forth herein.\" The father paid $1,500 to the store owner and took possession of the violin. Prior to the daughter's first lesson, the store owner signed a contract to play with the city symphony orchestra, and she notified all her customers that she would no longer be providing private lessons. If the father sues the store owner for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parties' agreement regarding the music lessons is inconsistent with the express language of the written contract.","B":"No, because the parties' agreement regarding the music lessons violates the statute of frauds.","C":"Yes, because contemporaneous agreements are admissible to prove the existence of additional terms that are consistent with a fully integrated writing.","D":"Yes, because the parties' agreement regarding the music lessons was collateral to the contract for the sale of the violin."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A written contract that contains a merger clause is presumed to be completely integrated—ie, a final expression of the parties' agreement as to all terms. As a result, the parol evidence rule generally bars any evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements or negotiations, regardless of whether they are consistent with the terms of the contract as written. But this rule does not apply when such evidence is offered to prove the existence of a collateral agreement—ie, a separate contract supported by separate consideration. Here, the written contract for the sale of the violin contained a merger clause (complete integration presumed). However, the parties' contemporaneous oral agreement regarding violin lessons created a separate contract that was supported by separate consideration (ie, lessons in exchange for $50 per lesson). As a result, the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of this collateral agreement. And since the store owner repudiated the agreement by notifying the father that she would no longer be providing the lessons, he will likely prevail. (Choice A) The written contract was silent as to music lessons, so the parties' oral agreement regarding the music lessons was consistent with (ie, did not contradict) the express language of the written contract. (Choice B) Under the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more must be in writing to be enforceable. But this would not prevent the father from prevailing in his breach-of-contract claim for the store owner's failure to provide a service (violin lessons). (Choice C) Parol evidence is admissible to prove the existence of consistent additional terms only if the contract at issue is partially integrated—ie, a final expression as to some, but not all, of the terms (not seen here). But evidence of the parties' oral agreement is still admissible here because it represents a collateral agreement.\n\nEducational objective: Even where a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), parol evidence can be used to prove the existence of a separate collateral agreement supported by its own consideration.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 216 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a collateral contract is not barred by an integrated one).","educationalObjective":"Even where a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), parol evidence can be used to prove the existence of a separate collateral agreement supported by its own consideration.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 216 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a collateral contract is not barred by an integrated one).","rule":"Even where a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), parol evidence can be used to prove the existence of a separate collateral agreement supported by its own consideration.","irac":{"issue":"If the father sues the store owner for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","rule":"Even where a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), parol evidence can be used to prove the existence of a separate collateral agreement supported by its own consideration.","analysis":"A written contract that contains a merger clause is presumed to be completely integrated—ie, a final expression of the parties' agreement as to all terms. As a result, the parol evidence rule generally bars any evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements or negotiations, regardless of whether they are consistent with the terms of the contract as written. But this rule does not apply when such evidence is offered to prove the existence of a collateral agreement—ie, a separate contract supported by separate consideration. Here, the written contract for the sale of the violin contained a merger clause (complete integration presumed). However, the parties' contemporaneous oral agreement regarding violin lessons created a separate contract that was supported by separate consideration (ie, lessons in exchange for $50 per lesson). As a result, the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of this collateral agreement. And since the store owner repudiated the agreement by notifying the father that she would no longer be providing the lessons, he will likely prevail. (Choice A) The written contract was silent as to music lessons, so the parties' oral agreement regarding the music lessons was consistent with (ie, did not contradict) the express language of the written contract. (Choice B) Under the statute of frauds, a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more must be in writing to be enforceable. But this would not prevent the father from prevailing in his breach-of-contract claim for the store owner's failure to provide a service (violin lessons). (Choice C) Parol evidence is admissible to prove the existence of consistent additional terms only if the contract at issue is partially integrated—ie, a final expression as to some, but not all, of the terms (not seen here). But evidence of the parties' oral agreement is still admissible here because it represents a collateral agreement.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the parties' agreement regarding the music lessons was collateral to the contract for the sale of the violin."},"questionZh":"一位父亲去一家乐器店询问为女儿购买小提琴和私人音乐课程的事宜。在店主的推荐下，父亲同意以1500美元购买一把小提琴。与此同时，店主口头同意在接下来的六个月里每月给女儿上四节课，每节课50美元。随后，父亲和店主签订了小提琴销售合同。合同中的一条规定：“本书面文件反映了双方的全部协议，除此之外没有任何其他协议或谅解。”父亲向店主支付了 1,500 美元，并获得了这把小提琴。在女儿上第一堂课之前，店主与市交响乐团签订了一份演奏合同，并通知所有顾客，她将不再提供私人课程。如果父亲起诉店主违约，他有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为双方关于音乐课程的约定与书面合同的明确语言不一致。","B":"不可以，因为双方关于音乐课程的协议违反了欺诈法。","C":"是的，因为同期协议可以证明存在与完全整合的写作一致的附加条款。","D":"是的，因为双方关于音乐课程的协议是小提琴销售合同的抵押品。"},"explanationZh":"包含合并条款的书面合同被认为是完全完整的，即双方对所有条款的协议的最终表达。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止任何先前或同期协议或谈判的证据，无论它们是否与书面合同条款一致。但当提供此类证据来证明附带协议（即由单独对价支持的单独合同）的存在时，该规则不适用。在此，小提琴销售的书面合同包含合并条款（假定完全合并）。然而，双方同时就小提琴课程达成的口头协议形成了一份单独的合同，该合同由单独的对价支持（即以每节课 50 美元换取课程）。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供该附属协议的证据。由于店主拒绝了协议，通知父亲她将不再提供课程，因此他很可能会获胜。 （选项A） 书面合同没有提及音乐课程，因此双方关于音乐课程的口头协议与书面合同的明示语言一致（即不矛盾）。 （选择 B） 根据反欺诈法，价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。但这并不能阻止父亲因店主未能提供服务（小提琴课程）而提出的违约索赔获胜。 （选择C）仅当所涉合同部分整合时，口头证据才可以用来证明一致的附加条款的存在——即，对某些但不是全部条款的最终表达（此处未看到）。但当事人口头协议的证据在这里仍然可以接受，因为它代表了附属协议。\n\n教育目标：即使合同完全整合（例如，由合并条款证明），口头证据也可用于证明存在由其自身对价支持的单独抵押协议。\n\n参考文献\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 216 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，综合合同并不禁止抵押合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"即使合同是完全整合的（例如，由合并条款证明），口头证据也可以用来证明存在由其自身对价支持的单独的抵押协议。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果父亲起诉店主违约，他有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"即使合同是完全整合的（例如，由合并条款证明），口头证据也可以用来证明存在由其自身对价支持的单独的抵押协议。","analysis":"包含合并条款的书面合同被认为是完全完整的，即双方对所有条款的协议的最终表达。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止任何先前或同期协议或谈判的证据，无论它们是否与书面合同条款一致。但当提供此类证据来证明附带协议（即由单独对价支持的单独合同）的存在时，该规则不适用。在此，小提琴销售的书面合同包含合并条款（假定完全合并）。然而，双方同时就小提琴课程达成的口头协议形成了一份单独的合同，该合同由单独的对价支持（即以每节课 50 美元换取课程）。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供该附属协议的证据。由于店主拒绝了协议，通知父亲她将不再提供课程，因此他很可能会获胜。 （选项A） 书面合同没有提及音乐课程，因此双方关于音乐课程的口头协议与书面合同的明示语言一致（即不矛盾）。 （选择 B） 根据反欺诈法，价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。但这并不能阻止父亲因店主未能提供服务（小提琴课程）而提出的违约索赔获胜。 （选择C）仅当所涉合同部分整合时，口头证据才可以用来证明一致的附加条款的存在——即，对某些但不是全部条款的最终表达（此处未看到）。但当事人口头协议的证据在这里仍然可以接受，因为它代表了附属协议。\n\n教育目标：即使合同完全整合（例如，由合并条款证明），口头证据也可用于证明存在由其自身对价支持的单独抵押协议。\n\n参考文献\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 216 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，综合合同并不禁止抵押合同）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为双方关于音乐课程的协议是小提琴销售合同的抵押品。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"additional terms","ipa":"/ʌdˈɪʃənəl tˈɝmz/","syllables":"ad·di·tional terms","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"附加条款：承诺中要约未涉及的新条款","contextualMeaning":"附加条款：承诺中要约未涉及的新条款","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because contemporaneous agreements are admissible to prove the existence of additional terms that are consistent with a fully integrated writing.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"repudiated","ipa":"/ɹipjˈudiˌeɪtɪd/","syllables":"re·pu·di·ated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. （正式地）否认( repudiate的过去式和过去分词 )； 拒绝接受； 拒绝与…往来","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“repudiated”","wordForm":"词；原形 repudiate；词形 0:repudiate/1:pd","sourceSentence":"And since the store owner repudiated the agreement by notifying the father that she would no longer be providing the lessons, he will likely prevail.","synonyms":["disown","renounce"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-b3fe78e02cb2791f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":21,"sourceQuestionPage":61,"sourceQuestionPages":[61],"sourceExplanationPages":[62,63],"sourceAnswerPages":[62,63],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-022","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"A retailer of guns in State X, U.S.A., received on June 1 the following signed letter from a gun wholesaler in another state: \"We have just obtained 100 of the assault rifles you inquired about and can supply them for $250 each. We can guarantee shipment no later than August 1.\" On June 10, the wholesaler sold and delivered the same rifles to another merchant for $300 each. Unaware of that transaction, the retailer on the morning of June 11 mailed the wholesaler a letter rejecting the latter's offer but, changing his mind an hour later, retrieved from his local post office the letter of rejection and immediately dispatched to the wholesaler a letter of acceptance, which the wholesaler received on June 14. On June 9, a valid federal statute making the interstate sale of assault rifles punishable as a crime had become effective, but neither the retailer nor the wholesaler was aware until June 15 that the statute was already in effect. As between the retailer and the wholesaler, which of the following is an accurate statement?","choices":{"A":"If a contract was formed, it is unenforceable because of supervening impracticability.","B":"If a contract was formed, it is voidable because of mutual mistake.","C":"No contract was formed, because the retailer's June 11 rejection was effective on dispatch.","D":"No contract was formed, because the wholesaler's June 10 sale of the rifles to another merchant revoked the offer to the retailer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Mutual mistake occurs when both parties enter into a contract based on the same mistake. In such cases, the contract may be voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n• the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. Here, both the retailer and wholesaler entered the contract based on an erroneous belief that assault rifles could lawfully be sold across state lines—a basic assumption of the contract. That mutual mistake materially affected their agreed-upon exchange since performance would subject them to criminal liability under the federal statute. Additionally, neither party assumed the risk of mistake since they did not know that the statute was in effect when they entered the contract. As a result, either party can avoid the contract (if formed*) because of mutual mistake. *This assumes that the wholesaler's offer was not terminated by law when the federal statute made the interstate sale of assault rifles a criminal offense. (Choice A) A party's duty to perform can be discharged by impracticability if performance becomes illegal after the contract was entered. Impracticability does not apply here since the statute criminalizing the interstate sale of assault rifles was enacted before the contract was entered. (Choice C) In contract negotiations by mail, a rejection is effective on receipt—not on dispatch. (Choice D) The wholesaler's sale of the rifles to another merchant did not revoke the offer to the retailer since the retailer was unaware of this transaction and a revocation is not effective until communicated to the offeree.\n\nEducational objective: Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 151 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of mistake).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when mutual mistake makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 151 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of mistake).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when mutual mistake makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"As between the retailer and the wholesaler, which of the following is an accurate statement?","rule":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","analysis":"Mutual mistake occurs when both parties enter into a contract based on the same mistake. In such cases, the contract may be voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n• the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. Here, both the retailer and wholesaler entered the contract based on an erroneous belief that assault rifles could lawfully be sold across state lines—a basic assumption of the contract. That mutual mistake materially affected their agreed-upon exchange since performance would subject them to criminal liability under the federal statute. Additionally, neither party assumed the risk of mistake since they did not know that the statute was in effect when they entered the contract. As a result, either party can avoid the contract (if formed*) because of mutual mistake. *This assumes that the wholesaler's offer was not terminated by law when the federal statute made the interstate sale of assault rifles a criminal offense. (Choice A) A party's duty to perform can be discharged by impracticability if performance becomes illegal after the contract was entered. Impracticability does not apply here since the statute criminalizing the interstate sale of assault rifles was enacted before the contract was entered. (Choice C) In contract negotiations by mail, a rejection is effective on receipt—not on dispatch. (Choice D) The wholesaler's sale of the rifles to another merchant did not revoke the offer to the retailer since the retailer was unaware of this transaction and a revocation is not effective until communicated to the offeree.","conclusion":"Answer B: If a contract was formed, it is voidable because of mutual mistake."},"questionZh":"美国X州的一家枪支零售商于6月1日收到了另一州一家枪支批发商的签名信：“我们刚刚收到了您询问的100支突击步枪，可以每支250美元的价格供货。我们可以保证最晚在8月1日发货。” 6月10日，批发商以每支300美元的价格将同样的步枪出售并交付给另一家商家。零售商在不知情的情况下，于 6 月 11 日上午向批发商邮寄了一封拒绝后者报价的信函，但一小时后改变了主意，从当地邮局取回了拒绝信，并立即向批发商发送了一封接受函，批发商于 6 月 14 日收到了该函。 6 月 9 日，一项规定州际销售突击步枪应作为犯罪行为予以处罚的有效联邦法规已生效，但直到零售商和批发商都没有意识到这一点。 6月15日该法规已生效。对于零售商和批发商来说，以下哪项表述是准确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"如果合同成立，则由于随之而来的不切实际而无法执行。","B":"如果合同成立，则由于双方的错误而可以撤销。","C":"没有形成合同，因为零售商 6 月 11 日的拒绝在发货时生效。","D":"没有形成合同，因为批发商 6 月 10 日将步枪出售给另一家商人，从而撤销了向零售商提供的报价。"},"explanationZh":"当双方基于相同的错误签订合同时，就会出现相互错误。在这种情况下，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可能会撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。在这里，零售商和批发商都基于一种错误的信念签订了合同，即突击步枪可以合法地跨州销售——这是合同的基本假设。这种共同的错误严重影响了他们商定的交换，因为根据联邦法规，这种行为将使他们承担刑事责任。此外，双方均未承担错误风险，因为他们在签订合同时并不知道该法规已生效。因此，任何一方都可以因共同错误而宣告合同（如果已成立*）。 *这假设当联邦法规将州际销售突击步枪定为刑事犯罪时，批发商的报价并未被法律终止。 （选择A）如果合同签订后履行变得不合法，一方的履行义务可以因不可行而解除。不切实际的情况在这里并不适用，因为将州际销售突击步枪定为犯罪的法规是在合同签订之前颁布的。 （选择 C）在通过邮件进行的合同谈判中，拒绝在收到时生效，而不是在发送时生效。 （选项 D） 批发商将步枪出售给另一家商人并没有撤销对零售商的要约，因为零售商并不知道这笔交易，并且在通知受要约人之前撤销不会生效。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，相互错误会导致受到不利影响的一方撤销合同：(1) 错误与合同的基本假设相关，(2) 错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 151（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（错误的定义）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当相互错误导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，相互错误使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于零售商和批发商来说，以下哪项表述是准确的？","rule":"在以下情况下，相互错误使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。","analysis":"当双方基于相同的错误签订合同时，就会出现相互错误。在这种情况下，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可能会撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。在这里，零售商和批发商都基于一种错误的信念签订了合同，即突击步枪可以合法地跨州销售——这是合同的基本假设。这种共同的错误严重影响了他们商定的交换，因为根据联邦法规，这种行为将使他们承担刑事责任。此外，双方均未承担错误风险，因为他们在签订合同时并不知道该法规已生效。因此，任何一方都可以因共同错误而宣告合同（如果已成立*）。 *这假设当联邦法规将州际销售突击步枪定为刑事犯罪时，批发商的报价并未被法律终止。 （选择A）如果合同签订后履行变得不合法，一方的履行义务可以因不可行而解除。不切实际的情况在这里并不适用，因为将州际销售突击步枪定为犯罪的法规是在合同签订之前颁布的。 （选择 C）在通过邮件进行的合同谈判中，拒绝在收到时生效，而不是在发送时生效。 （选项 D） 批发商将步枪出售给另一家商人并没有撤销对零售商的要约，因为零售商并不知道这笔交易，并且在通知受要约人之前撤销不会生效。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，相互错误会导致受到不利影响的一方撤销合同：(1) 错误与合同的基本假设相关，(2) 错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 151（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（错误的定义）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当相互错误导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：如果合同成立，则由于双方的错误而可以撤销。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"revocation","ipa":"/ɹˌɛvʌkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"re·vo·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 废止, 取消, 撤回；[经] 撤销","contextualMeaning":"撤回：要约人终止仍可撤销的要约","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:revocations","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) The wholesaler's sale of the rifles to another merchant did not revoke the offer to the retailer since the retailer was unaware of this transaction and a revocation is not effective until communicated to the offeree.","synonyms":["annulment"]},{"term":"revoke","ipa":"/ɹɪvˈoʊk/","syllables":"re·voke","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 撤回, 废除；vi. 藏牌；n. 藏牌","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“revoke”","wordForm":"名词；词形 d:revoked/p:revoked/i:revoking/3:revokes","sourceSentence":"No contract was formed, because the wholesaler's June 10 sale of the rifles to another merchant revoked the offer to the retailer.","synonyms":["renege","annul","lift","countermand"]},{"term":"discharged","ipa":"/dɪstʃˈɑɹdʒd/","syllables":"dis·charged","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 放电的；泻出的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“discharged”","wordForm":"词；原形 discharge；词形 0:discharge/1:dp/d:discharged","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) A party's duty to perform can be discharged by impracticability if performance becomes illegal after the contract was entered.","synonyms":["firing","firing off","release","waiver"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-e0cf66bd9133bec8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":22,"sourceQuestionPage":64,"sourceQuestionPages":[64],"sourceExplanationPages":[65,66],"sourceAnswerPages":[65,66],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-023","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A music artist and a talent manager orally agreed that the manager would oversee the business aspects of the artist's career for five years in return for 15% of the artist's gross earnings. Both the artist and the manager had the right to terminate the agreement at any time. The manager received less than 15% of the artist's gross earnings during the first year and no commissions during the following six months. At that time, the artist called the manager to say that he was terminating their agreement. The manager sued the artist for breach of contract, and the artist raised the statute of frauds as a defense. Is the artist's statute of frauds defense likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because both the artist and the manager had the right to terminate the agreement at any time.","B":"No, because the parties have partially performed the agreement.","C":"Yes, because neither party fully performed the agreement within the first year.","D":"Yes, because the parties' oral contract cannot be fully performed within one year."},"answer":"D","explanation":"One-year provision of statute of frauds\n\nApplicability\n• Contracts whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year – Excludes contracts of uncertain duration (eg, lifetime contracts) Effect of performance\n• Part performance – does not remove contract from statute, but restitution available for reasonable value of performance rendered\n• Full performance – removes contract from statute, whether or not performance is completed within a year Effect of option to\n• Majority rule – does not remove contract from statute, terminate within a because termination is not performance year*\n• Minority rule – removes contract from statute, because duration of contract is uncertain *Jurisdictions agree that where only the plaintiff has the option to terminate, the statute of frauds applies.\n\nA contract falls within the one-year provision of the statute of frauds—and must therefore be in writing to be enforceable—if its terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year of its making.* However, jurisdictions are split as to whether both parties' option to terminate the contract within a year removes it from the statute:\n• Minority rule – the option to terminate removes the contract from the statute, because the duration of the agreement is uncertain\n\n• Majority rule – the option to terminate does not remove the contract from the statute, because the agreement may not be fully performed before its termination\n\nHere, the parties' oral agreement that the manager would oversee the business aspects of the artist's career for five years cannot be fully performed within one year. This is true under the majority rule even though both parties had the option to terminate the agreement at any time (Choice A). Accordingly, the artist's statute of frauds defense will likely succeed. *The writing must be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought and state with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal. (Choice B) Part performance does not remove a contract from the one-year provision of the statute of frauds. But it may entitle a party (eg, the manager) to restitution for the reasonable value of the performance rendered, which is usually the contract value for that performance.\n\n(Choice C) Failing to fully perform a contract within one year does not mean that the one- year provision of the statute of frauds applies. Whether the provision applies turns on whether it is possible to fully perform the contract within one year.\n\nEducational objective: The one-year provision of the statute of frauds applies to a contract whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year. However, the parties' option to terminate the contract within a year does not remove it from the statute under the majority rule.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 130 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (one-year provision of the statute of frauds).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds § 19 (2020) (explaining that the option to terminate a contract within a year does not remove the contract from the statute of frauds).","educationalObjective":"The one-year provision of the statute of frauds applies to a contract whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year. However, the parties' option to terminate the contract within a year does not remove it from the statute under the majority rule.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 130 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (one-year provision of the statute of frauds).\n\n• 72 Am. Jur. 2d Statute of Frauds § 19 (2020) (explaining that the option to terminate a contract within a year does not remove the contract from the statute of frauds).","rule":"The one-year provision of the statute of frauds applies to a contract whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year. However, the parties' option to terminate the contract within a year does not remove it from the statute under the majority rule.","irac":{"issue":"Is the artist's statute of frauds defense likely to succeed?","rule":"The one-year provision of the statute of frauds applies to a contract whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year. However, the parties' option to terminate the contract within a year does not remove it from the statute under the majority rule.","analysis":"One-year provision of statute of frauds\n\nApplicability\n• Contracts whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year – Excludes contracts of uncertain duration (eg, lifetime contracts) Effect of performance\n• Part performance – does not remove contract from statute, but restitution available for reasonable value of performance rendered\n• Full performance – removes contract from statute, whether or not performance is completed within a year Effect of option to\n• Majority rule – does not remove contract from statute, terminate within a because termination is not performance year*\n• Minority rule – removes contract from statute, because duration of contract is uncertain *Jurisdictions agree that where only the plaintiff has the option to terminate, the statute of frauds applies.\n\nA contract falls within the one-year provision of the statute of frauds—and must therefore be in writing to be enforceable—if its terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year of its making.* However, jurisdictions are split as to whether both parties' option to terminate the contract within a year removes it from the statute:\n• Minority rule – the option to terminate removes the contract from the statute, because the duration of the agreement is uncertain\n\n• Majority rule – the option to terminate does not remove the contract from the statute, because the agreement may not be fully performed before its termination\n\nHere, the parties' oral agreement that the manager would oversee the business aspects of the artist's career for five years cannot be fully performed within one year. This is true under the majority rule even though both parties had the option to terminate the agreement at any time (Choice A). Accordingly, the artist's statute of frauds defense will likely succeed. *The writing must be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought and state with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal. (Choice B) Part performance does not remove a contract from the one-year provision of the statute of frauds. But it may entitle a party (eg, the manager) to restitution for the reasonable value of the performance rendered, which is usually the contract value for that performance.\n\n(Choice C) Failing to fully perform a contract within one year does not mean that the one- year provision of the statute of frauds applies. Whether the provision applies turns on whether it is possible to fully perform the contract within one year.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the parties' oral contract cannot be fully performed within one year."},"questionZh":"一位音乐艺术家和一位人才经理口头同意，经理将在五年内监督该艺术家职业生涯的商业方面，以换取该艺术家总收入的 15%。艺人和经纪人均有权随时终止协议。经纪人在第一年收到的佣金不到艺术家总收入的 15%，并且在接下来的六个月内没有佣金。当时，艺人打电话给经纪人，表示要解除合约。经纪人以违约为由起诉艺人，艺人则以诈骗罪为由进行抗辩。艺术家的欺诈辩护有可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为艺人和经纪人都有权随时终止协议。","B":"否，因为双方已部分履行协议。","C":"是的，因为双方都没有在第一年内完全履行协议。","D":"是的，因为双方的口头合同在一年内无法完全履行。"},"explanationZh":"为期一年的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）规定\n\n适用性\n• 其条款使任何一方无法在一年内完全履行的合同 – 不包括期限不确定的合同（例如终身合同） 履行的影响\n• 部分履行——并不将合同从法规中删除，但可针对所提供的履行的合理价值进行恢复原状\n• 全面履约——将合同从法规中删除，无论履约是否在一年内完成。\n• 多数规则 – 不会将合同从法规中删除，而是在 年内终止，因为终止不是履行年度*\n• 少数派规则——从法规中删除合同，因为合同期限不确定 *司法管辖区一致认为，只有原告有权选择终止的情况下，适用防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。\n\n如果合同条款使任何一方无法在签订后一年内完全履行合同，则该合同属于反防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的一年期规定，因此必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。*但是，对于双方选择在一年内终止合同是否会将其从法规中删除的问题，司法管辖区存在分歧：\n• 少数服从多数规则——终止的选择将合同从法规中删除，因为协议的期限不确定\n\n• 多数决定 – 终止的选择不会将合同从法规中删除，因为协议在终止之前可能无法完全履行\n\n在此，双方关于经纪人将在五年内监督艺人职业生涯的商业方面的口头协议无法在一年内完全履行。尽管双方都可以选择随时终止协议（选择 A），但多数规则下也是如此。因此，艺术家的欺诈辩护很可能会成功。 *该书面文件必须由寻求执行的一方签署，并以合理的确定性陈述交易的基本条款。 （选择 B） 部分履约并不排除合同的一年期防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）规定。但它可能使一方（例如，经理）有权就所提供的绩效的合理价值（通常是该绩效的合同价值）获得赔偿。\n\n（选项C）一年内未完全履行合同并不意味着适用欺诈法的一年规定。该规定是否适用取决于合同能否在一年内完全履行。\n\n教育目标：欺诈法的一年条款适用于合同条款使任何一方无法在一年内完全履行的合同。然而，双方选择在一年内终止合同并不能将其从多数规则的法规中删除。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 130（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的一年规定）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 欺诈法第 19 条 (2020)（解释说，一年内终止合同的选择并不会将合同从欺诈法中删除）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"欺诈法的一年期规定适用于合同条款规定任何一方均无法在一年内完全履行的合同。然而，双方选择在一年内终止合同并不能将其从多数规则的法规中删除。","iracZh":{"issue":"艺术家的欺诈辩护有可能成功吗？","rule":"欺诈法的一年期规定适用于合同条款规定任何一方均无法在一年内完全履行的合同。然而，双方选择在一年内终止合同并不能将其从多数规则的法规中删除。","analysis":"为期一年的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）规定\n\n适用性\n• 其条款使任何一方无法在一年内完全履行的合同 – 不包括期限不确定的合同（例如终身合同） 履行的影响\n• 部分履行——并不将合同从法规中删除，但可针对所提供的履行的合理价值进行恢复原状\n• 全面履约——将合同从法规中删除，无论履约是否在一年内完成。\n• 多数规则 – 不会将合同从法规中删除，而是在 年内终止，因为终止不是履行年度*\n• 少数派规则——从法规中删除合同，因为合同期限不确定 *司法管辖区一致认为，只有原告有权选择终止的情况下，适用防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。\n\n如果合同条款使任何一方无法在签订后一年内完全履行合同，则该合同属于反防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的一年期规定，因此必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。*但是，对于双方选择在一年内终止合同是否会将其从法规中删除的问题，司法管辖区存在分歧：\n• 少数服从多数规则——终止的选择将合同从法规中删除，因为协议的期限不确定\n\n• 多数决定 – 终止的选择不会将合同从法规中删除，因为协议在终止之前可能无法完全履行\n\n在此，双方关于经纪人将在五年内监督艺人职业生涯的商业方面的口头协议无法在一年内完全履行。尽管双方都可以选择随时终止协议（选择 A），但多数规则下也是如此。因此，艺术家的欺诈辩护很可能会成功。 *该书面文件必须由寻求执行的一方签署，并以合理的确定性陈述交易的基本条款。 （选择 B） 部分履约并不排除合同的一年期防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）规定。但它可能使一方（例如，经理）有权就所提供的绩效的合理价值（通常是该绩效的合同价值）获得赔偿。\n\n（选项C）一年内未完全履行合同并不意味着适用欺诈法的一年规定。该规定是否适用取决于合同能否在一年内完全履行。\n\n教育目标：欺诈法的一年条款适用于合同条款使任何一方无法在一年内完全履行的合同。然而，双方选择在一年内终止合同并不能将其从多数规则的法规中删除。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 130（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的一年规定）。\n\n• 上午 72 点。尤尔。 2d 欺诈法第 19 条 (2020)（解释说，一年内终止合同的选择并不会将合同从欺诈法中删除）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为双方的口头合同在一年内无法完全履行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"part performance","ipa":"/pˈɑɹt pɝfˈɔɹməns/","syllables":"part per·for·mance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"部分履行：在一定情形下使口头合同脱离防欺诈法","contextualMeaning":"部分履行：在一定情形下使口头合同脱离防欺诈法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"One-year provision of statute of frauds Applicability • Contracts whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year – Excludes contracts of uncertain duration (eg, lifetime contracts) Effect of performance • Part performance – does not remove contract from statute, but restitution available for reasonable value of performance rendered • Full performance – removes contract from statute, whe","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"option","ipa":"/ˈɑpʃən/","syllables":"op·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 选择权, 挑选, 选项；[计] 选项","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“option”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:options","sourceSentence":"However, the parties' option to terminate the contract within a year does not remove it from the statute under the majority rule.","synonyms":["choice","selection","pick","alternative"]},{"term":"impossible","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˈɑsəbəl/","syllables":"im·pos·si·ble","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 不可能的, 难以置信的, 令人无法忍受的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“impossible”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"The one-year provision of the statute of frauds applies to a contract whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year.","synonyms":["inconceivable","out of the question","unimaginable","unacceptable"]},{"term":"minority","ipa":"/maɪnˈɔɹəti/","syllables":"mi·nor·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 少数, 未成年, 少数民族；a. 少数的, 属于少数派的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“minority”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:minorities","sourceSentence":"One-year provision of statute of frauds Applicability • Contracts whose terms make it impossible for any party to fully perform within one year – Excludes contracts of uncertain duration (eg, lifetime contracts) Effect of performance • Part performance – does not remove contract from statute, but restitution available for reasonable value of performance rendered • Full performance – removes contract from statute, whe","synonyms":["nonage"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":23,"sourceQuestionPage":67,"sourceQuestionPages":[67],"sourceExplanationPages":[68,69],"sourceAnswerPages":[68,69],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-024","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A film star moved to the West Coast to further his acting career. He agreed to work for a motion picture studio for the next five years, leased a high-rise condominium for nine months, entered an agreement to purchase an exotic sports car for $250,000, and agreed to transfer ownership of his beach house to his girlfriend if she married him. Which of these transactions is most likely to be enforceable without a signed writing?","choices":{"A":"The employment agreement with the motion picture studio.","B":"The lease agreement with the luxury condominium corporation.","C":"The purchase agreement for the sports car.","D":"The agreement to transfer the beach house to the girlfriend."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The statute of frauds requires the following contracts to be evidenced by a writing that contains all essential terms and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (MY LEGS):\n• Marriage – a contract made in consideration of marriage\n• Year – a contract that cannot be completed within one year\n• Land – a contract for the sale or transfer of an interest in real property\n• Executor – a contract to pay an estate's debt out of the executor's personal funds\n• Goods – a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more\n• Suretyship – a contract to answer for the debt or duty of another Therefore, in this scenario, the following transactions must be evidenced by a signed writing to be enforceable:\n• The five-year employment agreement with the motion picture studio because it cannot be completed in less than one year (Choice A)\n\n• The purchase agreement for the sports car (a good) because the $250,000 sales price exceeds $500 (Choice C)\n\n• The agreement to transfer ownership of the beach house to the girlfriend because it (1) involves an interest in real property and (2) was made in consideration of her marriage to the film star (Choice D)\n\nHowever, the nine-month lease agreement with the luxury condominium corporation does not fall under the statute of frauds—even though it involves an interest in real property. The reason is that leases and easements for less than one year are usually excluded from the statute.* Therefore, this transaction is most likely to be enforceable without a signed writing. *Licenses and assignments of mortgages are also excluded from the statute of frauds.\n\nEducational objective: Contracts that fall under the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) must be contained in a signed writing to be enforceable. Although this generally includes contracts involving an interest in real property, leases and easements for less than one year do not fall under the statute.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 127 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (interests in land subject to the statute of frauds).","educationalObjective":"Contracts that fall under the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) must be contained in a signed writing to be enforceable. Although this generally includes contracts involving an interest in real property, leases and easements for less than one year do not fall under the statute.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 127 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (interests in land subject to the statute of frauds).","rule":"Contracts that fall under the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) must be contained in a signed writing to be enforceable. Although this generally includes contracts involving an interest in real property, leases and easements for less than one year do not fall under the statute.","irac":{"issue":"Which of these transactions is most likely to be enforceable without a signed writing?","rule":"Contracts that fall under the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) must be contained in a signed writing to be enforceable. Although this generally includes contracts involving an interest in real property, leases and easements for less than one year do not fall under the statute.","analysis":"The statute of frauds requires the following contracts to be evidenced by a writing that contains all essential terms and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (MY LEGS):\n• Marriage – a contract made in consideration of marriage\n• Year – a contract that cannot be completed within one year\n• Land – a contract for the sale or transfer of an interest in real property\n• Executor – a contract to pay an estate's debt out of the executor's personal funds\n• Goods – a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more\n• Suretyship – a contract to answer for the debt or duty of another Therefore, in this scenario, the following transactions must be evidenced by a signed writing to be enforceable:\n• The five-year employment agreement with the motion picture studio because it cannot be completed in less than one year (Choice A)\n\n• The purchase agreement for the sports car (a good) because the $250,000 sales price exceeds $500 (Choice C)\n\n• The agreement to transfer ownership of the beach house to the girlfriend because it (1) involves an interest in real property and (2) was made in consideration of her marriage to the film star (Choice D)\n\nHowever, the nine-month lease agreement with the luxury condominium corporation does not fall under the statute of frauds—even though it involves an interest in real property. The reason is that leases and easements for less than one year are usually excluded from the statute.* Therefore, this transaction is most likely to be enforceable without a signed writing. *Licenses and assignments of mortgages are also excluded from the statute of frauds.","conclusion":"Answer B: The lease agreement with the luxury condominium corporation."},"questionZh":"一位电影明星搬到西海岸以进一步发展他的演艺事业。他同意在未来五年内为一家电影制片厂工作，租用一套高层公寓九个月，签订协议以 25 万美元购买一辆异国情调的跑车，并同意如果女友嫁给他，将他海滨别墅的所有权转让给她。这些交易中哪一项最有可能在没有书面签名的情况下强制执行？","choicesZh":{"A":"与电影制片厂的雇佣协议。","B":"与豪华公寓公司的租赁协议。","C":"跑车的购买协议。","D":"将海滨别墅转让给女友的协议。"},"explanationZh":"欺诈法要求以下合同以包含所有基本条款的书面形式作为证据，并由寻求执行的一方（我的腿）签署：\n• 婚姻——以结婚为约因订立的合同\n• 年份——一年内无法完成的合同\n• 土地——出售或转让不动产权益的合同\n• 遗嘱执行人——用遗嘱执行人的个人资金偿还遗产债务的合同\n• 货物 – 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同\n• 担保 – 承担他人债务或责任的合同 因此，在这种情况下，以下交易必须由签署的书面文件证明才能强制执行：\n• 与电影制片厂的五年雇佣协议，因为它不能在一年内完成（选择A）\n\n• 跑车的购买协议（好），因为 250,000 美元的售价超过 500 美元（选择 C）\n\n• 同意将海滨别墅的所有权转让给女友，因为它 (1) 涉及不动产权益，并且 (2) 是考虑到她与电影明星的婚姻（选择 D）\n\n然而，与豪华公寓公司签订的为期九个月的租赁协议并不属于欺诈法的范围，尽管它涉及不动产权益。原因是一年以下的租赁和地役权通常被排除在法规之外。*因此，这项交易很可能无需签署书面文件即可强制执行。 *抵押贷款的许可和转让也不包括在欺诈法范围内。\n\n教育目标：属于防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds） (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同必须包含在签署的书面文件中才能强制执行。虽然这通常包括涉及不动产权益的合同，但一年以下的租赁和地役权不属于该法规的范围。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 127（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（土地权益受欺诈法约束）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"属于欺诈法 (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同必须包含在签署的书面文件中才能强制执行。虽然这通常包括涉及不动产权益的合同，但一年以下的租赁和地役权不属于该法规的范围。","iracZh":{"issue":"这些交易中哪一项最有可能在没有书面签名的情况下强制执行？","rule":"属于欺诈法 (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同必须包含在签署的书面文件中才能强制执行。虽然这通常包括涉及不动产权益的合同，但一年以下的租赁和地役权不属于该法规的范围。","analysis":"欺诈法要求以下合同以包含所有基本条款的书面形式作为证据，并由寻求执行的一方（我的腿）签署：\n• 婚姻——以结婚为约因订立的合同\n• 年份——一年内无法完成的合同\n• 土地——出售或转让不动产权益的合同\n• 遗嘱执行人——用遗嘱执行人的个人资金偿还遗产债务的合同\n• 货物 – 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同\n• 担保 – 承担他人债务或责任的合同 因此，在这种情况下，以下交易必须由签署的书面文件证明才能强制执行：\n• 与电影制片厂的五年雇佣协议，因为它不能在一年内完成（选择A）\n\n• 跑车的购买协议（好），因为 250,000 美元的售价超过 500 美元（选择 C）\n\n• 同意将海滨别墅的所有权转让给女友，因为它 (1) 涉及不动产权益，并且 (2) 是考虑到她与电影明星的婚姻（选择 D）\n\n然而，与豪华公寓公司签订的为期九个月的租赁协议并不属于欺诈法的范围，尽管它涉及不动产权益。原因是一年以下的租赁和地役权通常被排除在法规之外。*因此，这项交易很可能无需签署书面文件即可强制执行。 *抵押贷款的许可和转让也不包括在欺诈法范围内。\n\n教育目标：属于防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds） (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同必须包含在签署的书面文件中才能强制执行。虽然这通常包括涉及不动产权益的合同，但一年以下的租赁和地役权不属于该法规的范围。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 127（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（土地权益受欺诈法约束）。","conclusion":"答案 B：与豪华公寓公司的租赁协议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"suretyship","ipa":"/'ʃjərətiʃip, 'ʃjəti-/","syllables":"sure·ty·ship","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 保人的地位, 保人的责任, 保人的资格；[经] 保证人的地位","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“suretyship”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:suretyships","sourceSentence":"The statute of frauds requires the following contracts to be evidenced by a writing that contains all essential terms and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (MY LEGS): • Marriage – a contract made in consideration of marriage • Year – a contract that cannot be completed within one year • Land – a contract for the sale or transfer of an interest in real property • Executor – a contract to pay an","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-eb806e3154dc9a48"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":24,"sourceQuestionPage":70,"sourceQuestionPages":[70],"sourceExplanationPages":[71,72],"sourceAnswerPages":[71,72],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-025","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"In exchange for valid and sufficient consideration, a man orally promised his son, who had no car and wanted a minivan, \"to pay to anyone from whom you buy a minivan within the next six months the full purchase price thereof.\" Two months later, the son bought a used minivan on credit from a dealership for $8,000. At the time, the dealership was unaware of the man's earlier promise to the son, but learned of it shortly after the sale. Can the dealership enforce the man's promise to the son?","choices":{"A":"No, because the dealership was neither identified when the man's promise was made nor aware of it when the minivan-sale was made.","B":"No, because the man's promise to the son is unenforceable under the suretyship clause of the statute of frauds.","C":"Yes, because the dealership is an intended beneficiary of the contract between the man and the son.","D":"Yes, under the doctrine of promissory estoppel."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives an advantage or benefit from the contract. The third party can enforce the contract if the third party is an intended beneficiary (ie, one whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly). Although unusual, a third party does not need to be named in the contract to be an intended beneficiary or have enforcement rights. Here, the man promised to pay the full purchase price to \"anyone\" from whom the son bought a minivan within the next six months. The son bought a minivan from the dealership within that time frame, making the dealership an intended beneficiary of the contract between the man and the son. This is true even though the dealership was neither identified when the man's promise was made nor aware of the promise when the minivan was sold (Choice A). Therefore, the dealership can enforce the man's promise and recover the $8,000 purchase price. (Choice B) Under the statute of frauds, certain contracts must be in writing. This includes suretyships—ie, contracts in which one party (surety) promises a second party (obligee) that the surety will answer for the debt of a third party (principal). Here, the father's promise was to the son—not the dealership—so the suretyship clause of the statute of frauds does not apply. (Choice D) An intended beneficiary does not need to rely on the doctrine of promissory estoppel—which allows a party to enforce a promise on which it relied when no valid contract was formed—to enforce its rights.\n\nEducational objective: A third party who receives some advantage or benefit from a contract can enforce the contract if the third party is an intended beneficiary—even if the third party is not specifically named in the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 308 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (identification of beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"A third party who receives some advantage or benefit from a contract can enforce the contract if the third party is an intended beneficiary—even if the third party is not specifically named in the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 308 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (identification of beneficiaries).","rule":"A third party who receives some advantage or benefit from a contract can enforce the contract if the third party is an intended beneficiary—even if the third party is not specifically named in the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Can the dealership enforce the man's promise to the son?","rule":"A third party who receives some advantage or benefit from a contract can enforce the contract if the third party is an intended beneficiary—even if the third party is not specifically named in the contract.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives an advantage or benefit from the contract. The third party can enforce the contract if the third party is an intended beneficiary (ie, one whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly). Although unusual, a third party does not need to be named in the contract to be an intended beneficiary or have enforcement rights. Here, the man promised to pay the full purchase price to \"anyone\" from whom the son bought a minivan within the next six months. The son bought a minivan from the dealership within that time frame, making the dealership an intended beneficiary of the contract between the man and the son. This is true even though the dealership was neither identified when the man's promise was made nor aware of the promise when the minivan was sold (Choice A). Therefore, the dealership can enforce the man's promise and recover the $8,000 purchase price. (Choice B) Under the statute of frauds, certain contracts must be in writing. This includes suretyships—ie, contracts in which one party (surety) promises a second party (obligee) that the surety will answer for the debt of a third party (principal). Here, the father's promise was to the son—not the dealership—so the suretyship clause of the statute of frauds does not apply. (Choice D) An intended beneficiary does not need to rely on the doctrine of promissory estoppel—which allows a party to enforce a promise on which it relied when no valid contract was formed—to enforce its rights.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the dealership is an intended beneficiary of the contract between the man and the son."},"questionZh":"以有效且充分的约因为交换，一名男子口头承诺他的儿子没有汽车，想要一辆小型货车，“在未来六个月内向任何购买小型货车的人支付全额购买价格。”两个月后，儿子以 8,000 美元的价格从经销商那里赊购了一辆二手小型货车。当时，经销商并不知道该男子早些时候对儿子的承诺，但在出售后不久才得知。经销商能否履行该男子对儿子的承诺？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为当该男子作出承诺时，经销商既没有被识别出来，也没有在小型货车销售时意识到这一点。","B":"不可以，因为根据欺诈法的担保条款，该男子对儿子的承诺是无法执行的。","C":"是的，因为经销商是该男子和儿子之间合同的预期受益人。","D":"是的，根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从合同中获得优势或利益。如果第三方是预期受益人（即缔约方打算直接受益的人），则第三方可以执行合同。虽然不常见，但第三方不需要在合同中指定为预期受益人或拥有执行权。在此，该男子承诺在未来六个月内向儿子购买小型货车的“任何人”支付全额购买价格。儿子在这段时间内从经销商处购买了一辆小型货车，使经销商成为该男子与儿子之间合同的预期受益人。即使该男子做出承诺时经销商既没有被识别，也没有意识到小型货车出售时的承诺（选项 A），情况也是如此。因此，经销商可以执行该男子的承诺，收回 8,000 美元的购买价。 （选择 B）根据欺诈法，某些合同必须采用书面形式。这包括担保权，即一方（担保人）向第二方（债权人）承诺担保人将对第三方（委托人）的债务负责的合同。在这里，父亲的承诺是对儿子的，而不是对经销商的承诺，因此欺诈法的保证条款不适用。 （选项 D）预期受益人不需要依靠承诺禁止反悔原则（该原则允许一方在未形成有效合同时执行其所依赖的承诺）来执行其权利。\n\n教育目标：从合同中获得某些利益或利益的第三方如果是预期受益人，则可以执行合同 - 即使合同中没有具体指定第三方。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 308（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（受益人的识别）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"从合同中获得某些利益或利益的第三方如果是预期受益人，则可以执行合同——即使合同中没有具体指定第三方。","iracZh":{"issue":"经销商能否履行该男子对儿子的承诺？","rule":"从合同中获得某些利益或利益的第三方如果是预期受益人，则可以执行合同——即使合同中没有具体指定第三方。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从合同中获得优势或利益。如果第三方是预期受益人（即缔约方打算直接受益的人），则第三方可以执行合同。虽然不常见，但第三方不需要在合同中指定为预期受益人或拥有执行权。在此，该男子承诺在未来六个月内向儿子购买小型货车的“任何人”支付全额购买价格。儿子在这段时间内从经销商处购买了一辆小型货车，使经销商成为该男子与儿子之间合同的预期受益人。即使该男子做出承诺时经销商既没有被识别，也没有意识到小型货车出售时的承诺（选项 A），情况也是如此。因此，经销商可以执行该男子的承诺，收回 8,000 美元的购买价。 （选择 B）根据欺诈法，某些合同必须采用书面形式。这包括担保权，即一方（担保人）向第二方（债权人）承诺担保人将对第三方（委托人）的债务负责的合同。在这里，父亲的承诺是对儿子的，而不是对经销商的承诺，因此欺诈法的保证条款不适用。 （选项 D）预期受益人不需要依靠承诺禁止反悔原则（该原则允许一方在未形成有效合同时执行其所依赖的承诺）来执行其权利。\n\n教育目标：从合同中获得某些利益或利益的第三方如果是预期受益人，则可以执行合同 - 即使合同中没有具体指定第三方。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 308（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（受益人的识别）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为经销商是该男子和儿子之间合同的预期受益人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"obligee","ipa":"/.ɒbli'dʒi:/","syllables":"obligee","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 权利人, 债权人, 受惠人；[经] 债权人, 权利, 债主","contextualMeaning":"债权人：有权请求合同履行的人","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"This includes suretyships—ie, contracts in which one party (surety) promises a second party (obligee) that the surety will answer for the debt of a third party (principal).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"surety","ipa":"/ʃˈʊɹəti/","syllables":"surety","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 保证人, 保证, 担保；[法] 保证人, 担保人, 保证","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“surety”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:sureties","sourceSentence":"No, because the man's promise to the son is unenforceable under the suretyship clause of the statute of frauds.","synonyms":["security","guarantor","warrantor","warranter"]},{"term":"incidental","ipa":"/ˌɪnsɪdˈɛntəl/","syllables":"in·ci·den·tal","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 附带的, 偶然的, 容易发生的；n. 附带事件","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“incidental”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:incidentals","sourceSentence":"Third-party beneficiaries (ie, nonparties who benefit from contract) Type Definition Right to enforce Intended • Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them • Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental • Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them • Eg, A promises to buy B a car","synonyms":["incidental expense","minor expense","attendant","consequent"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":25,"sourceQuestionPage":73,"sourceQuestionPages":[73],"sourceExplanationPages":[74,75],"sourceAnswerPages":[74,75],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-026","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"A motorcycle collector and his friend had known each other for years. The collector sent a letter to the friend that said, \"I am willing to sell you my oldest motorcycle for $4,000.\" The friend sent the collector an email saying, \"I accept your offer.\" The collector owned three motorcycles: a 1985 model, a 1987 model, and a 1992 model. The friend did not know about the 1985 model, which was the one the collector intended to sell. The collector was unaware that the friend intended to buy the 1987 model. When the collector tendered the 1985 model, the friend refused to accept it. Is the friend likely to prevail in a breach of contract suit against the collector?","choices":{"A":"No, because by not inquiring further about the meaning of the phrase \"oldest motorcycle,\" the friend assumed the risk of being mistaken.","B":"No, because neither the friend nor the collector had reason to know of the other's interpretation of the words \"oldest motorcycle.\"","C":"Yes, because the friend had no reason to know of the collector's meaning and the collector could have avoided the confusion by more careful drafting.","D":"Yes, so long as a reasonable person in the friend's position would have considered the letter as referring to the 1987 model."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A misunderstanding occurs when the parties to a contract attach different meanings to the same term. When a misunderstanding involves a material term, whether a valid contract is formed depends on who knows or has reason to know about the misunderstanding:\n• neither party OR both parties – in which case no contract is formed because there is no meeting of the minds\n\n• one party – in which case a valid contract is formed, and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls Here, the friend accepted the collector's offer to sell the \"oldest motorcycle\" the collector owned. The friend did not know about the 1985 model (the one the collector intended to sell), and the collector did not know that the friend intended to buy the 1987 model. Since neither party knew nor had reason to know of the other's interpretation of \"oldest motorcycle,\" no contract was formed and the friend is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) A party's failure to know or discover facts before entering into a contract—eg, the friend's failure to inquire about the meaning of \"oldest motorcycle\"—does not qualify as an assumption of the risk that would prevent a party from avoiding a contract due to mistake. (Choices C & D) The friend's interpretation of \"oldest motorcycle\" would control, and the friend would prevail, only if the collector knew or had reason to know of the friend's interpretation. Neither a reasonable person's interpretation nor the collector's ability to avoid the confusion by more careful drafting is relevant.\n\nEducational objective: If neither (or both) of the parties to a contract know or have reason to know about a misunderstanding involving a material term, no contract is formed. But if one party knows or has reason to know, a valid contract is formed and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","educationalObjective":"If neither (or both) of the parties to a contract know or have reason to know about a misunderstanding involving a material term, no contract is formed. But if one party knows or has reason to know, a valid contract is formed and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","rule":"If neither (or both) of the parties to a contract know or have reason to know about a misunderstanding involving a material term, no contract is formed. But if one party knows or has reason to know, a valid contract is formed and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls.","irac":{"issue":"Is the friend likely to prevail in a breach of contract suit against the collector?","rule":"If neither (or both) of the parties to a contract know or have reason to know about a misunderstanding involving a material term, no contract is formed. But if one party knows or has reason to know, a valid contract is formed and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls.","analysis":"A misunderstanding occurs when the parties to a contract attach different meanings to the same term. When a misunderstanding involves a material term, whether a valid contract is formed depends on who knows or has reason to know about the misunderstanding:\n• neither party OR both parties – in which case no contract is formed because there is no meeting of the minds\n\n• one party – in which case a valid contract is formed, and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls Here, the friend accepted the collector's offer to sell the \"oldest motorcycle\" the collector owned. The friend did not know about the 1985 model (the one the collector intended to sell), and the collector did not know that the friend intended to buy the 1987 model. Since neither party knew nor had reason to know of the other's interpretation of \"oldest motorcycle,\" no contract was formed and the friend is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) A party's failure to know or discover facts before entering into a contract—eg, the friend's failure to inquire about the meaning of \"oldest motorcycle\"—does not qualify as an assumption of the risk that would prevent a party from avoiding a contract due to mistake. (Choices C & D) The friend's interpretation of \"oldest motorcycle\" would control, and the friend would prevail, only if the collector knew or had reason to know of the friend's interpretation. Neither a reasonable person's interpretation nor the collector's ability to avoid the confusion by more careful drafting is relevant.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because neither the friend nor the collector had reason to know of the other's interpretation of the words \"oldest motorcycle.\""},"questionZh":"一位摩托车收藏家和他的朋友相识多年。收藏家给朋友写了一封信，信中说：“我愿意以 4000 美元的价格卖给你我最旧的摩托车。”这位朋友给收藏家发了一封电子邮件说：“我接受你的报价。”该收藏家拥有三辆摩托车：一辆 1985 年款、一辆 1987 年款和一辆 1992 年款。这位朋友不知道 1985 年的型号，而这正是收藏家打算出售的型号。收藏家并不知道这位朋友打算购买 1987 年款。当收藏家投标 1985 年模型时，朋友拒绝接受。在针对收藏家的违约诉讼中，朋友有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为没有进一步询问“最古老的摩托车”这个短语的含义，这位朋友就承担了犯错的风险。","B":"不，因为朋友和收藏家都没有理由知道对方对“最古老的摩托车”一词的解释。","C":"是的，因为这位朋友没有理由知道收藏家的意思，而收藏家可以通过更仔细的起草来避免混乱。","D":"是的，只要一个理智的人站在朋友的立场上就会认为这封信指的是 1987 年的型号。"},"explanationZh":"当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。当误解涉及重大条款时，合同是否成立取决于谁知道或有理由知道该误解：\n• 任何一方或双方都没有——在这种情况下，由于没有达成一致意见，因此不会形成合同\n\n• 一方——在这种情况下，一份有效的合同成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。在这里，朋友接受了收藏家的提议，出售收藏家拥有的“最古老的摩托车”。这位朋友不知道1985年款（收藏家打算出售的那款），而收藏家也不知道这位朋友打算购买1987年款。由于双方都不知道也没有理由知道对方对“最古老的摩托车”的解释，因此没有签订合同，朋友也不太可能胜诉。 （选项A）一方当事人在签订合同之前未能了解或发现事实——例如，朋友未能询问“最旧的摩托车”的含义——并不构成承担防止一方因错误而解除合同的风险。 （选项 C 和 D） 仅当收藏者知道或有理由知道朋友的解释时，朋友对“最古老的摩托车”的解释将占主导地位，并且朋友将占上风。无论是理性人的解释还是收藏家通过更仔细的起草来避免混乱的能力都是无关的。\n\n教育目标：如果合同双方都不知道或没有理由知道涉及实质性条款的误解，则合同不成立。但如果一方知道或有理由知道，则一份有效的合同就成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果合同双方均不知道或没有理由知道涉及重大条款的误解，则合同不成立。但如果一方知道或有理由知道，则一份有效的合同就成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。","iracZh":{"issue":"在针对收藏家的违约诉讼中，朋友有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"如果合同双方均不知道或没有理由知道涉及重大条款的误解，则合同不成立。但如果一方知道或有理由知道，则一份有效的合同就成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。","analysis":"当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。当误解涉及重大条款时，合同是否成立取决于谁知道或有理由知道该误解：\n• 任何一方或双方都没有——在这种情况下，由于没有达成一致意见，因此不会形成合同\n\n• 一方——在这种情况下，一份有效的合同成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。在这里，朋友接受了收藏家的提议，出售收藏家拥有的“最古老的摩托车”。这位朋友不知道1985年款（收藏家打算出售的那款），而收藏家也不知道这位朋友打算购买1987年款。由于双方都不知道也没有理由知道对方对“最古老的摩托车”的解释，因此没有签订合同，朋友也不太可能胜诉。 （选项A）一方当事人在签订合同之前未能了解或发现事实——例如，朋友未能询问“最旧的摩托车”的含义——并不构成承担防止一方因错误而解除合同的风险。 （选项 C 和 D） 仅当收藏者知道或有理由知道朋友的解释时，朋友对“最古老的摩托车”的解释将占主导地位，并且朋友将占上风。无论是理性人的解释还是收藏家通过更仔细的起草来避免混乱的能力都是无关的。\n\n教育目标：如果合同双方都不知道或没有理由知道涉及实质性条款的误解，则合同不成立。但如果一方知道或有理由知道，则一份有效的合同就成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为朋友和收藏家都没有理由知道对方对“最古老的摩托车”一词的解释。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-322d1f1239d2d4a2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":26,"sourceQuestionPage":76,"sourceQuestionPages":[76],"sourceExplanationPages":[77,78],"sourceAnswerPages":[77,78],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-027","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"Responding to a county's written advertisement for bids, a tire company was the successful bidder for the sale of tires to a county for its vehicles. The company and the county entered into a signed, written agreement that specified, \"It is agreed that the company will deliver all tires required by this agreement to the county, in accordance with the attached bid form and specifications, for a one-year period beginning September 1, 2019.\" Attached to the agreement was a copy of the bid form and specifications. In the written advertisement to which the company had responded, but not in the bid form, the county had stated, \"Multiple awards may be issued if they are in the best interests of the county.\" No definite quantity of tires to be bought by the county from the company was specified in any of these documents. In January 2020, the company learned that the county was buying some of its tires from one of the company's competitors. Contending that the tire company–county agreement was a requirements contract, the company sued the county for the damages caused by the county's purchase of some of its tires from the competitor. If the county's defense is to offer proof of the advertisement concerning the possibility of multiple awards, should the court admit the evidence?","choices":{"A":"No, because it would make the contract illusory.","B":"No, because of the parol evidence rule.","C":"Yes, because the advertisement was in writing.","D":"Yes, because the provision in the written agreement, \"all tires required by this agreement,\" is ambiguous."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness Under the parol evidence rule, evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements or negotiations is not admissible to contradict the terms of an integrated contract. A contract is integrated when (1) it is in writing and (2) it embodies the final expression of the parties' intent regarding the contract's subject matter (as seen here). But even when a contract is integrated, such evidence is admissible to interpret an ambiguous contract term. Here, the provision in the written agreement, \"all tires required by this agreement,\" is ambiguous: it could mean as many tires as the county chooses to order during the one-year contract term OR as many tires as the county actually requires during that period (making this a requirements contract). Therefore, evidence of the written advertisement, which stated that the county could issue multiple tire awards, is admissible to determine whether the contract was, in fact, a requirements contract (Choice B). (Choice A) Where a promising party has discretion whether to perform at all, its promise is illusory. Because the county's evidence indicates that \"all tires required by this agreement\" means \"as many tires as the county chooses to order,\" it could make the contract illusory if admitted. But this is not a reason to exclude the evidence. (Choice C) The fact that the advertisement was in writing is irrelevant. Any evidence (oral or written) can be used to interpret the meaning of an ambiguous contract term.\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement, and such evidence is admissible when it is used to establish the meaning of an ambiguous contract term.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when parol evidence is admissible to explain meaning of ambiguous term).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement, and such evidence is admissible when it is used to establish the meaning of an ambiguous contract term.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when parol evidence is admissible to explain meaning of ambiguous term).","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement, and such evidence is admissible when it is used to establish the meaning of an ambiguous contract term.","irac":{"issue":"If the county's defense is to offer proof of the advertisement concerning the possibility of multiple awards, should the court admit the evidence?","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement, and such evidence is admissible when it is used to establish the meaning of an ambiguous contract term.","analysis":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness Under the parol evidence rule, evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements or negotiations is not admissible to contradict the terms of an integrated contract. A contract is integrated when (1) it is in writing and (2) it embodies the final expression of the parties' intent regarding the contract's subject matter (as seen here). But even when a contract is integrated, such evidence is admissible to interpret an ambiguous contract term. Here, the provision in the written agreement, \"all tires required by this agreement,\" is ambiguous: it could mean as many tires as the county chooses to order during the one-year contract term OR as many tires as the county actually requires during that period (making this a requirements contract). Therefore, evidence of the written advertisement, which stated that the county could issue multiple tire awards, is admissible to determine whether the contract was, in fact, a requirements contract (Choice B). (Choice A) Where a promising party has discretion whether to perform at all, its promise is illusory. Because the county's evidence indicates that \"all tires required by this agreement\" means \"as many tires as the county chooses to order,\" it could make the contract illusory if admitted. But this is not a reason to exclude the evidence. (Choice C) The fact that the advertisement was in writing is irrelevant. Any evidence (oral or written) can be used to interpret the meaning of an ambiguous contract term.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the provision in the written agreement, \"all tires required by this agreement,\" is ambiguous."},"questionZh":"一家轮胎公司根据某县的书面招标广告中标，向某县出售其车辆用轮胎。该公司与该县签署了一份书面协议，其中规定：“双方同意，公司将按照随附的投标表格和规格，向该县交付本协议所需的所有轮胎，期限从 2019 年 9 月 1 日开始，为期一年。”协议附有投标表格和规格的副本。在该公司回应的书面广告中，但没有在投标表格中，该县表示，“如果符合该县的最大利益，可以颁发多个奖项。”这些文件都没有具体说明该县从该公司购买轮胎的具体数量。 2020 年 1 月，该公司获悉该县正在从该公司的一家竞争对手那里购买部分轮胎。该公司认为轮胎公司与县之间的协议是一份需求合同，因此起诉该县，要求赔偿该县从竞争对手那里购买部分轮胎所造成的损失。如果县辩方要提供有关可能获得多项奖励的广告证据，法院是否应该采信该证据？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为这会使合同变得虚幻。","B":"不可以，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。","C":"是的，因为广告是书面的。","D":"是的，因为书面协议中“本协议要求的所有轮胎”的规定含糊不清。"},"explanationZh":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前或同期协议或谈判的证据不得与综合合同的条款相抵触。当 (1) 为书面形式且 (2) 体现了双方关于合同标的物的意图的最终表达（如此处所示）时，合同即为一体化合同。但即使合同是完整的，此类证据也可以用来解释不明确的合同条款。这里，书面协议中的条款“本协议所需的所有轮胎”含糊不清：它可能意味着该县在一年的合同期限内选择订购的轮胎数量，或者该县在此期间实际需要的轮胎数量（使其成为需求合同）。因此，书面广告的证据表明该县可以颁发多个轮胎奖励，可以用来确定该合同是否实际上是一份要求合同（选择 B）。 （选项A）如果承诺方可以自行决定是否履行，则其承诺是虚幻的。由于该县的证据表明，“本协议要求的所有轮胎”是指“该县选择订购的轮胎数量”，因此如果承认，该合同可能会变得虚幻。但这并不是排除证据的理由。 （选项C） 广告是书面形式的这一事实是无关紧要的。任何证据（口头或书面）都可以用来解释不明确的合同条款的含义。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，并且当此类证据用于确定不明确的合同条款的含义时，该证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当可以接受口头证据来解释不明确术语的含义时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，并且当此类证据用于确定不明确的合同条款的含义时，该证据是可接受的。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果县辩方要提供有关可能获得多项奖励的广告证据，法院是否应该采信该证据？","rule":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，并且当此类证据用于确定不明确的合同条款的含义时，该证据是可接受的。","analysis":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前或同期协议或谈判的证据不得与综合合同的条款相抵触。当 (1) 为书面形式且 (2) 体现了双方关于合同标的物的意图的最终表达（如此处所示）时，合同即为一体化合同。但即使合同是完整的，此类证据也可以用来解释不明确的合同条款。这里，书面协议中的条款“本协议所需的所有轮胎”含糊不清：它可能意味着该县在一年的合同期限内选择订购的轮胎数量，或者该县在此期间实际需要的轮胎数量（使其成为需求合同）。因此，书面广告的证据表明该县可以颁发多个轮胎奖励，可以用来确定该合同是否实际上是一份要求合同（选择 B）。 （选项A）如果承诺方可以自行决定是否履行，则其承诺是虚幻的。由于该县的证据表明，“本协议要求的所有轮胎”是指“该县选择订购的轮胎数量”，因此如果承认，该合同可能会变得虚幻。但这并不是排除证据的理由。 （选项C） 广告是书面形式的这一事实是无关紧要的。任何证据（口头或书面）都可以用来解释不明确的合同条款的含义。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，并且当此类证据用于确定不明确的合同条款的含义时，该证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当可以接受口头证据来解释不明确术语的含义时）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为书面协议中“本协议要求的所有轮胎”的规定含糊不清。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"requirements contract","ipa":"/ɹɪkwˈaɪɹmənts kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"re·quire·ments con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"需求合同：买方承诺向卖方购买其善意需求量","contextualMeaning":"需求合同：买方承诺向卖方购买其善意需求量","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Contending that the tire company–county agreement was a requirements contract, the company sued the county for the damages caused by the county's purchase of some of its tires from the competitor.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"rescission","ipa":"/ɹɪsˈɪʒən/","syllables":"rescis·sion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 废止, 取消, 使无效, 撤消；[经] 解除, 取消","contextualMeaning":"解除：终止合同双方尚未履行的义务","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule Evidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish: • whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially • meaning of ambiguous term • defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake) • ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation) • subsequent contract modifications • condition precedent to e","synonyms":["recission"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":27,"sourceQuestionPage":79,"sourceQuestionPages":[79],"sourceExplanationPages":[80,81],"sourceAnswerPages":[80,81],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-028","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A power company operated a 100-turbine wind farm. The turbines began experiencing mechanical problems and required 10 new gearboxes in order to produce electricity at full capacity. Because the manufacturer of the gearboxes was unable to guarantee speedy delivery, the power company contacted a separate shipping company. The two companies signed a fully integrated writing in which the shipping company promised to deliver the gearboxes by June 1. Before the agreement was executed, the power company told the shipping company, \"If you do not deliver the gearboxes by June 1, we will be unable to produce enough electricity to meet our existing contractual obligation to a nearby town.\" The shipping company was inexcusably late in delivering the gearboxes, and, as a result, the power company breached its contract with the nearby town and was found liable for damages. The power company then sued the shipping company, which was found to have breached its contract with the power company. At a hearing on damages, the shipping company now seeks to exclude any evidence of the conversation regarding timely delivery. How will the court likely rule?","choices":{"A":"Admit the evidence as proof of a collateral agreement.","B":"Admit the evidence as proof of the foreseeability of consequential damages.","C":"Exclude the evidence as a prior oral agreement even if it is consistent with the terms of the parties' written agreement.","D":"Exclude the evidence on the basis that the parties' written agreement was a fully integrated contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Integration\n\nType Definition Effect\n\nComplete Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms\n• Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some terms\n• Parol evidence admissible to supplement (not contradict) contract The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties that supplement or contradict a written contract that is completely or fully integrated. However, this rule does not apply, and such evidence is always admissible, when the evidence is used to determine remedies for breach. For example, parol evidence is admissible to prove that consequential damages were foreseeable and are therefore recoverable. Here, the two companies signed a fully integrated contract for the delivery of 10 gearboxes. Before doing so, the power company had told the shipping company that it would be unable to meet its contractual obligation to the town if the gearboxes were not delivered on time. Evidence of this conversation shows it was foreseeable that late delivery would cause the power company to suffer consequential damages arising from its contract with the town. Therefore, the court will likely admit the evidence as poof of the foreseeability of consequential damages. (Choice A) The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of a collateral agreement—ie, a separate deal with its own consideration. But here, the conversation about the delivery deadline pertained directly to the companies' written contract—not a separate deal. (Choices C & D) Prior oral (or written) agreements are inadmissible to supplement or contradict the terms of a fully integrated contract, even if they are consistent with the terms of the contract as written. But here, the conversation regarding delivery is admissible for a different purpose—to prove the foreseeability of consequential damages.\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a fully integrated, written contract. But such\n\nevidence is admissible to determine remedies for breach (eg, to prove the foreseeability of consequential damages).\n\nReferences\n• Barnett v. Hughey, 15 S.W. 464, 465 (Ark. 1891) (holding that parol evidence is admissible to determine damages in a contract dispute).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a fully integrated, written contract. But such\n\nevidence is admissible to determine remedies for breach (eg, to prove the foreseeability of consequential damages).","references":"• Barnett v. Hughey, 15 S.W. 464, 465 (Ark. 1891) (holding that parol evidence is admissible to determine damages in a contract dispute).","rule":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a fully integrated, written contract. But such\n\nevidence is admissible to determine remedies for breach (eg, to prove the foreseeability of consequential damages).","irac":{"issue":"How will the court likely rule?","rule":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a fully integrated, written contract. But such\n\nevidence is admissible to determine remedies for breach (eg, to prove the foreseeability of consequential damages).","analysis":"Integration\n\nType Definition Effect\n\nComplete Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence within integration parties' final agreement scope of agreement about all terms\n• Parol evidence inadmissible to supplement (ie, add to) or contradict contract Partial Writing that sets forth\n• Excludes parol evidence integration parties' final agreement inconsistent with agreement about some terms\n• Parol evidence admissible to supplement (not contradict) contract The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications between contracting parties that supplement or contradict a written contract that is completely or fully integrated. However, this rule does not apply, and such evidence is always admissible, when the evidence is used to determine remedies for breach. For example, parol evidence is admissible to prove that consequential damages were foreseeable and are therefore recoverable. Here, the two companies signed a fully integrated contract for the delivery of 10 gearboxes. Before doing so, the power company had told the shipping company that it would be unable to meet its contractual obligation to the town if the gearboxes were not delivered on time. Evidence of this conversation shows it was foreseeable that late delivery would cause the power company to suffer consequential damages arising from its contract with the town. Therefore, the court will likely admit the evidence as poof of the foreseeability of consequential damages. (Choice A) The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of a collateral agreement—ie, a separate deal with its own consideration. But here, the conversation about the delivery deadline pertained directly to the companies' written contract—not a separate deal. (Choices C & D) Prior oral (or written) agreements are inadmissible to supplement or contradict the terms of a fully integrated contract, even if they are consistent with the terms of the contract as written. But here, the conversation regarding delivery is admissible for a different purpose—to prove the foreseeability of consequential damages.","conclusion":"Answer B: Admit the evidence as proof of the foreseeability of consequential damages."},"questionZh":"一家电力公司运营着一个拥有 100 台涡轮机的风电场。涡轮机开始出现机械问题，需要 10 个新齿轮箱才能满负荷发电。由于变速箱制造商无法保证快速交货，电力公司联系了另一家运输公司。两家公司签署了一份完全一体化的书面文件，其中航运公司承诺在 6 月 1 日之前交付齿轮箱。在协议执行之前，电力公司告诉航运公司，“如果你们不在 6 月 1 日之前交付齿轮箱，我们将无法生产足够的电力来履行我们对附近城镇的现有合同义务。”航运公司迟迟未能交付变速箱，这是不可原谅的，结果，电力公司违反了与附近城镇的合同，并被判承担损害赔偿责任。电力公司随后起诉航运公司，发现航运公司违反了与电力公司的合同。在关于损害赔偿的听证会上，航运公司现在试图排除任何有关及时交货的谈话证据。法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"承认证据作为抵押协议的证据。","B":"承认证据作为间接损害可预见性的证据。","C":"即使该证据与当事人书面协议的条款一致，也将证据排除在事先口头协议之外。","D":"以当事人的书面协议是完全完整的合同为由排除证据。"},"explanationZh":"整合\n\n类型 定义 效果\n\n完整的写作阐述\n• 排除整合各方关于所有条款的最终协议范围内的口头证据\n• 口头证据不可用于补充（即添加）或反驳合同中规定的部分书面内容\n• 排除口头证据整合双方的最终协议与某些条款的协议不一致的情况\n• 口头证据可用于补充（而非反驳）合同 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供缔约方之间先前或同时进行的通信证据，以补充或反驳完整或完整的书面合同。然而，这条规则并不适用，并且当证据用于确定违约补救措施时，此类证据始终是可接受的。例如，可以采用口头证据来证明间接损失是可预见的，因此是可以追回的。在这里，两家公司签署了一份交付 10 台变速箱的完全一体化合同。在此之前，电力公司曾告诉航运公司，如果变速箱不能按时交付，它将无法履行对该镇的合同义务。这次谈话的证据表明，可以预见的是，延迟交付将导致电力公司因与该镇签订的合同而遭受间接损失。因此，法院可能会承认该证据证明间接损害的可预见性。 （选择A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止抵押协议的证据——即具有其自身对价的单独交易。但在这里，有关交货期限的讨论直接涉及公司的书面合同，而不是单独的协议。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 先前的口头（或书面）协议不得补充或抵触完全整合的合同条款，即使它们与书面合同条款一致。但在这里，有关交付的对话可以出于不同的目的而被接受——证明间接损害的可预见性。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止使用先前或同时进行的通信证据来补充或抵触完全整合的书面合同的条款。但这样的\n\n可以采用证据来确定违约补救措施（例如，证明间接损害的可预见性）。\n\n参考文献\n• Barnett 诉 Hughey，15 S.W. 464, 465 (Ark. 1891)（认为口头证据可以用来确定合同纠纷中的损害赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止使用先前或同时期的通信证据来补充或抵触完全整合的书面合同的条款。但这样的\n\n可以采用证据来确定违约补救措施（例如，证明间接损害的可预见性）。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止使用先前或同时期的通信证据来补充或抵触完全整合的书面合同的条款。但这样的\n\n可以采用证据来确定违约补救措施（例如，证明间接损害的可预见性）。","analysis":"整合\n\n类型 定义 效果\n\n完整的写作阐述\n• 排除整合各方关于所有条款的最终协议范围内的口头证据\n• 口头证据不可用于补充（即添加）或反驳合同中规定的部分书面内容\n• 排除口头证据整合双方的最终协议与某些条款的协议不一致的情况\n• 口头证据可用于补充（而非反驳）合同 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供缔约方之间先前或同时进行的通信证据，以补充或反驳完整或完整的书面合同。然而，这条规则并不适用，并且当证据用于确定违约补救措施时，此类证据始终是可接受的。例如，可以采用口头证据来证明间接损失是可预见的，因此是可以追回的。在这里，两家公司签署了一份交付 10 台变速箱的完全一体化合同。在此之前，电力公司曾告诉航运公司，如果变速箱不能按时交付，它将无法履行对该镇的合同义务。这次谈话的证据表明，可以预见的是，延迟交付将导致电力公司因与该镇签订的合同而遭受间接损失。因此，法院可能会承认该证据证明间接损害的可预见性。 （选择A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不禁止抵押协议的证据——即具有其自身对价的单独交易。但在这里，有关交货期限的讨论直接涉及公司的书面合同，而不是单独的协议。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 先前的口头（或书面）协议不得补充或抵触完全整合的合同条款，即使它们与书面合同条款一致。但在这里，有关交付的对话可以出于不同的目的而被接受——证明间接损害的可预见性。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止使用先前或同时进行的通信证据来补充或抵触完全整合的书面合同的条款。但这样的\n\n可以采用证据来确定违约补救措施（例如，证明间接损害的可预见性）。\n\n参考文献\n• Barnett 诉 Hughey，15 S.W. 464, 465 (Ark. 1891)（认为口头证据可以用来确定合同纠纷中的损害赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 B：承认证据作为间接损害可预见性的证据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"consequential damages","ipa":"/kˌɑnsʌkwˈɛnʃəl dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"con·se·quen·tial dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"后果性损害赔偿：因守约方特殊情形产生且可预见的损失","contextualMeaning":"后果性损害赔偿：因守约方特殊情形产生且可预见的损失","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Admit the evidence as proof of the foreseeability of consequential damages.","synonyms":["special damages"]},{"term":"foreseeable","ipa":"/fɔɹsˈiəbəl/","syllables":"fore·see·able","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 可预知的, 能预测的；[法] 可预见的, 可预料的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“foreseeable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"For example, parol evidence is admissible to prove that consequential damages were foreseeable and are therefore recoverable.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":28,"sourceQuestionPage":82,"sourceQuestionPages":[82],"sourceExplanationPages":[83,84],"sourceAnswerPages":[83,84],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-029","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"An insurance company issued an insurance policy to a homeowner. The policy failed to contain certain coverage terms required by a state insurance statute. When the homeowner suffered a loss due to a theft that was within the policy's terms, the insurance company refused to pay, claiming that the policy was unenforceable because it violated the statute. Will the homeowner likely succeed in an action against the insurance company to recover for the loss?","choices":{"A":"No, because the insurance policy is not a divisible contract.","B":"No, because the insurance policy violated the statute.","C":"Yes, because the homeowner belongs to the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute.","D":"Yes, because the insurance policy will be strictly construed against the insurance company as the drafter."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contracts unenforceable on public-policy grounds\n\nCommon examples:\n\n• Contracts unreasonably restraining trade\n• Contracts discouraging or restraining marriage\n• Contracts for sexual intercourse\n• Contracts for committing a tort\n• Contracts encouraging breach of a fiduciary duty\n• Contracts excusing liability for intentional, knowing, or reckless misconduct A contract that violates the law is often unenforceable on public-policy grounds. But there is an exception to this rule when the contract violates a law designed to protect the party seeking to enforce the contract. In that case, the contract is enforceable so as to avoid frustrating the public policy behind the statute. Here, the homeowner's insurance policy violated the state insurance statute by failing to contain certain coverage terms. That statute was intended to protect insureds like the homeowner, and the public policy behind the statute will be frustrated if the insurance company is allowed to escape liability for the homeowner's loss. Therefore, the insurance policy is enforceable, and the homeowner will likely succeed (Choice B). (Choice A) Some contracts are divisible, meaning that they can easily be separated into legal and illegal parts so that recovery is available on the legal part(s) only. But a contract entered into in violation of a statute need not be divisible for a court to enforce it. (Choice D) Although contracts are generally construed against the drafter, this rule matters only when there is a dispute involving the interpretation of ambiguous contract language (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: When a contract violates a law that was designed to protect the party seeking to enforce the contract, the contract can be enforced to avoid frustrating the public policy behind the statute.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 178 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a contract is unenforceable on public policy grounds).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 179 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when refusal to enforce frustrates public policy).","educationalObjective":"When a contract violates a law that was designed to protect the party seeking to enforce the contract, the contract can be enforced to avoid frustrating the public policy behind the statute.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 178 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a contract is unenforceable on public policy grounds).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 179 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when refusal to enforce frustrates public policy).","rule":"When a contract violates a law that was designed to protect the party seeking to enforce the contract, the contract can be enforced to avoid frustrating the public policy behind the statute.","irac":{"issue":"Will the homeowner likely succeed in an action against the insurance company to recover for the loss?","rule":"When a contract violates a law that was designed to protect the party seeking to enforce the contract, the contract can be enforced to avoid frustrating the public policy behind the statute.","analysis":"Contracts unenforceable on public-policy grounds\n\nCommon examples:\n\n• Contracts unreasonably restraining trade\n• Contracts discouraging or restraining marriage\n• Contracts for sexual intercourse\n• Contracts for committing a tort\n• Contracts encouraging breach of a fiduciary duty\n• Contracts excusing liability for intentional, knowing, or reckless misconduct A contract that violates the law is often unenforceable on public-policy grounds. But there is an exception to this rule when the contract violates a law designed to protect the party seeking to enforce the contract. In that case, the contract is enforceable so as to avoid frustrating the public policy behind the statute. Here, the homeowner's insurance policy violated the state insurance statute by failing to contain certain coverage terms. That statute was intended to protect insureds like the homeowner, and the public policy behind the statute will be frustrated if the insurance company is allowed to escape liability for the homeowner's loss. Therefore, the insurance policy is enforceable, and the homeowner will likely succeed (Choice B). (Choice A) Some contracts are divisible, meaning that they can easily be separated into legal and illegal parts so that recovery is available on the legal part(s) only. But a contract entered into in violation of a statute need not be divisible for a court to enforce it. (Choice D) Although contracts are generally construed against the drafter, this rule matters only when there is a dispute involving the interpretation of ambiguous contract language (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the homeowner belongs to the class of persons intended to be protected by the statute."},"questionZh":"一家保险公司向房主签发了一份保险单。该保单未能包含州保险法规要求的某些承保条款。当房主因盗窃而遭受保单条款范围内的损失时，保险公司拒绝赔付，声称保单因违反法规而无法执行。房主可能会向保险公司提起诉讼并获得损失赔偿吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为保险单不是可分割的合同。","B":"不可以，因为保险单违反了法规。","C":"是的，因为房主属于受法律保护的人群。","D":"是的，因为保单将严格以保险公司作为起草人的角度进行解释。"},"explanationZh":"合同因公共政策而无法执行\n\n常见示例：\n\n• 合同不合理地限制贸易\n• 阻碍或限制婚姻的合同\n• 性交合同\n• 侵权合同\n• 鼓励违反信托义务的合同\n• 免除故意、明知或鲁莽不当行为责任的合同 违反法律的合同通常因公共政策而无法执行。但当合同违反旨在保护寻求执行合同的一方的法律时，该规则有一个例外。在这种情况下，合同是可执行的，以避免破坏法规背后的公共政策。在此，房主的保险单未包含某些承保条款，违反了国家保险法规。该法规的目的是保护像房主这样的被保险人，如果允许保险公司逃避对房主损失的责任，该法规背后的公共政策将会受挫。因此，保险单是可执行的，房主很可能会成功（选择B）。 （选项 A）有些合同是可分割的，这意味着它们可以很容易地分为合法部分和非法部分，以便仅对合法部分进行追偿。但违反法规而签订的合同无需被法院分割即可执行。 （选项D）虽然合同通常被解释为不利于起草人，但该规则仅在存在涉及含糊合同语言解释的争议时才有意义（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：当合同违反旨在保护寻求执行合同的一方的法律时，可以执行合同以避免破坏法规背后的公共政策。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 178（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当合同因公共政策原因而无法执行时）。\n\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 179 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当拒绝执行阻碍公共政策时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当合同违反旨在保护寻求执行合同的一方的法律时，可以执行合同以避免破坏法规背后的公共政策。","iracZh":{"issue":"房主可能会向保险公司提起诉讼并获得损失赔偿吗？","rule":"当合同违反旨在保护寻求执行合同的一方的法律时，可以执行合同以避免破坏法规背后的公共政策。","analysis":"合同因公共政策而无法执行\n\n常见示例：\n\n• 合同不合理地限制贸易\n• 阻碍或限制婚姻的合同\n• 性交合同\n• 侵权合同\n• 鼓励违反信托义务的合同\n• 免除故意、明知或鲁莽不当行为责任的合同 违反法律的合同通常因公共政策而无法执行。但当合同违反旨在保护寻求执行合同的一方的法律时，该规则有一个例外。在这种情况下，合同是可执行的，以避免破坏法规背后的公共政策。在此，房主的保险单未包含某些承保条款，违反了国家保险法规。该法规的目的是保护像房主这样的被保险人，如果允许保险公司逃避对房主损失的责任，该法规背后的公共政策将会受挫。因此，保险单是可执行的，房主很可能会成功（选择B）。 （选项 A）有些合同是可分割的，这意味着它们可以很容易地分为合法部分和非法部分，以便仅对合法部分进行追偿。但违反法规而签订的合同无需被法院分割即可执行。 （选项D）虽然合同通常被解释为不利于起草人，但该规则仅在存在涉及含糊合同语言解释的争议时才有意义（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：当合同违反旨在保护寻求执行合同的一方的法律时，可以执行合同以避免破坏法规背后的公共政策。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 178（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当合同因公共政策原因而无法执行时）。\n\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 179 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当拒绝执行阻碍公共政策时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为房主属于受法律保护的人群。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"frustrated","ipa":"/fɹˈʌstɹˌeɪtəd/","syllables":"frus·trated","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 失意的, 挫败的；泄气的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“frustrated”","wordForm":"词；原形 frustrate；词形 0:frustrate/1:dp/d:frustrated/p:frustrated","sourceSentence":"That statute was intended to protect insureds like the homeowner, and the public policy behind the statute will be frustrated if the insurance company is allowed to escape liability for the homeowner's loss.","synonyms":["torment","rag","bedevil","crucify"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":29,"sourceQuestionPage":85,"sourceQuestionPages":[85],"sourceExplanationPages":[86,87],"sourceAnswerPages":[86,87],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-030","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A seller contracted to manufacture 1,000 toasters for a buyer for a specified price. The contract contained a provision that clearly stated: \"This contract may not be assigned, and any violation of this prohibition voids the contract.\" After the contract was signed, the seller informed the buyer that the toasters would be manufactured by a competitor of the seller. Citing the nonassignment provision, the buyer claimed that it was no longer bound by the contract. Toasters manufactured by the competitor were of equal quality to toasters manufactured by the seller. Is the buyer bound by the contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the seller assigned a right despite the contractual prohibition.","B":"No, because \"this contract may not be assigned\" means that duties may not be delegated, and the seller delegated a duty.","C":"Yes, because even though the seller breached the contract, there are no damages since the competitor's toasters are of equal quality to the seller's toasters.","D":"Yes, because the nonassignment provision is not enforceable since public policy favors free assignment and delegation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contract provisions prohibiting assignments/delegations\n\nType of Explicit prohibitions Assignment of Delegation of provision rights duties\n\nNone N/A Allowed\n\nSpecific Assignment of rights & Barred delegation of duties\n\nAssignment of rights only Barred Allowed\n\nDelegation of duties only Allowed Barred\n\nGeneral Assignment of \"the contract\"\n\nPublic policy favors the free assignment of contractual rights and the free delegation of contractual duties. Despite this public policy, contract provisions that prohibit assignment and/or delegation (ie, nonassignment provisions) are enforceable (Choice D). However, a general provision that prohibits assignment of \"the contract\"—without specifying whether it applies to rights or duties—will be construed to (1) allow the assignment of rights and (2) bar the delegation of duties. Here, the seller had a contractual duty to manufacture 1,000 toasters and sell them to the buyer. Since the nonassignment provision (\"[t]his contract may not be assigned\") did not specify whether it applied to rights or duties, it will be construed to mean that duties may not be delegated. But the seller informed the buyer that the seller had delegated its duty to the competitor. And since the contract stated that this violation of the nonassignment provision voided the contract, the buyer is no longer bound. (Choice A) The seller delegated its duty to manufacture the toasters but did not assign its right to receive payment from the buyer. And since the contract provision did not specify whether it applied to rights or duties, it would have allowed the seller to assign its right (had the seller done so), and the buyer would still have been bound. (Choice C) The buyer is no longer bound because the seller's violation of the nonassignment provision voided the contract. This is true regardless of whether the buyer suffered any damages.\n\nEducational objective: Nonassignment provisions are enforceable. But a general provision that prohibits assignment of \"the contract\" without specifying rights or duties will be construed to (1) allow the assignment of rights and (2) bar the delegation of duties.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 322 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a contract term prohibiting assignment of \"the contract\" bars only the delegation of a duty).","educationalObjective":"Nonassignment provisions are enforceable. But a general provision that prohibits assignment of \"the contract\" without specifying rights or duties will be construed to (1) allow the assignment of rights and (2) bar the delegation of duties.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 322 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a contract term prohibiting assignment of \"the contract\" bars only the delegation of a duty).","rule":"Nonassignment provisions are enforceable. But a general provision that prohibits assignment of \"the contract\" without specifying rights or duties will be construed to (1) allow the assignment of rights and (2) bar the delegation of duties.","irac":{"issue":"Is the buyer bound by the contract?","rule":"Nonassignment provisions are enforceable. But a general provision that prohibits assignment of \"the contract\" without specifying rights or duties will be construed to (1) allow the assignment of rights and (2) bar the delegation of duties.","analysis":"Contract provisions prohibiting assignments/delegations\n\nType of Explicit prohibitions Assignment of Delegation of provision rights duties\n\nNone N/A Allowed\n\nSpecific Assignment of rights & Barred delegation of duties\n\nAssignment of rights only Barred Allowed\n\nDelegation of duties only Allowed Barred\n\nGeneral Assignment of \"the contract\"\n\nPublic policy favors the free assignment of contractual rights and the free delegation of contractual duties. Despite this public policy, contract provisions that prohibit assignment and/or delegation (ie, nonassignment provisions) are enforceable (Choice D). However, a general provision that prohibits assignment of \"the contract\"—without specifying whether it applies to rights or duties—will be construed to (1) allow the assignment of rights and (2) bar the delegation of duties. Here, the seller had a contractual duty to manufacture 1,000 toasters and sell them to the buyer. Since the nonassignment provision (\"[t]his contract may not be assigned\") did not specify whether it applied to rights or duties, it will be construed to mean that duties may not be delegated. But the seller informed the buyer that the seller had delegated its duty to the competitor. And since the contract stated that this violation of the nonassignment provision voided the contract, the buyer is no longer bound. (Choice A) The seller delegated its duty to manufacture the toasters but did not assign its right to receive payment from the buyer. And since the contract provision did not specify whether it applied to rights or duties, it would have allowed the seller to assign its right (had the seller done so), and the buyer would still have been bound. (Choice C) The buyer is no longer bound because the seller's violation of the nonassignment provision voided the contract. This is true regardless of whether the buyer suffered any damages.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because \"this contract may not be assigned\" means that duties may not be delegated, and the seller delegated a duty."},"questionZh":"卖方签订合同，以指定价格为买方生产 1,000 台烤面包机。合同中有一条明确规定：“本合同不得转让，任何违反该禁令的行为均导致合同无效。”合同签订后，卖方通知买方，烤面包机将由卖方的竞争对手制造。买方援引不转让条款，声称其不再受合同约束。竞争对手生产的烤面包机与卖方生产的烤面包机质量相同。买方受合同约束吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为尽管合同禁止，卖方还是转让了一项权利。","B":"不可以，因为“本合同不得转让”意味着义务不得被委托，而卖方委托了义务。","C":"是的，因为即使卖方违反了合同，也不会造成任何损失，因为竞争对手的烤面包机与卖方的烤面包机质量相同。","D":"是的，因为不转让条款不可强制执行，因为公共政策有利于自由转让和授权。"},"explanationZh":"禁止转让/委托的合同条款\n\n明确禁止的类型 规定权利 义务的分配\n\n无 N/A 允许\n\n具体的权利分配和禁止的职责委托\n\n仅转让权利 禁止 允许\n\n只允许职责委派 禁止\n\n“合同”的一般转让\n\n公共政策有利于合同权利的自由转让和合同义务的自由委托。尽管有这项公共政策，禁止转让和/或授权的合同条款（即非转让条款）仍然是可执行的（选择 D）。然而，禁止转让“合同”的一般条款（未具体说明其是否适用于权利或义务）将被解释为（1）允许权利转让和（2）禁止义务委托。在此，卖方有合同义务制造 1,000 台烤面包机并将其出售给买方。由于不转让条款（“他的合同不得转让”）没有具体说明其是否适用于权利或义务，因此将被解释为义务不得转让。但卖方通知买方，卖方已将其责任委托给竞争对手。由于合同规定，违反不转让条款使合同无效，因此买方不再受约束。 （选项A） 卖方委托了制造烤面包机的责任，但没有转让其从买方收取付款的权利。而且由于合同条款没有具体说明它是否适用于权利或义务，因此它允许卖方转让其权利（如果卖方这样做），而买方仍然受到约束。 （选项C） 买方不再受约束，因为卖方违反了不转让条款，导致合同无效。无论买方是否遭受任何损失，情况都是如此。\n\n教育目标：不可分配的规定是可执行的。但是，禁止转让“合同”而不具体规定权利或义务的一般条款将被解释为（1）允许权利转让和（2）禁止职责委托。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 322（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了禁止转让“合同”的合同条款仅禁止义务的委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"不转让条款是可执行的。但是，禁止转让“合同”而不具体规定权利或义务的一般条款将被解释为（1）允许权利转让和（2）禁止职责委托。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方受合同约束吗？","rule":"不转让条款是可执行的。但是，禁止转让“合同”而不具体规定权利或义务的一般条款将被解释为（1）允许权利转让和（2）禁止职责委托。","analysis":"禁止转让/委托的合同条款\n\n明确禁止的类型 规定权利 义务的分配\n\n无 N/A 允许\n\n具体的权利分配和禁止的职责委托\n\n仅转让权利 禁止 允许\n\n只允许职责委派 禁止\n\n“合同”的一般转让\n\n公共政策有利于合同权利的自由转让和合同义务的自由委托。尽管有这项公共政策，禁止转让和/或授权的合同条款（即非转让条款）仍然是可执行的（选择 D）。然而，禁止转让“合同”的一般条款（未具体说明其是否适用于权利或义务）将被解释为（1）允许权利转让和（2）禁止义务委托。在此，卖方有合同义务制造 1,000 台烤面包机并将其出售给买方。由于不转让条款（“他的合同不得转让”）没有具体说明其是否适用于权利或义务，因此将被解释为义务不得转让。但卖方通知买方，卖方已将其责任委托给竞争对手。由于合同规定，违反不转让条款使合同无效，因此买方不再受约束。 （选项A） 卖方委托了制造烤面包机的责任，但没有转让其从买方收取付款的权利。而且由于合同条款没有具体说明它是否适用于权利或义务，因此它允许卖方转让其权利（如果卖方这样做），而买方仍然受到约束。 （选项C） 买方不再受约束，因为卖方违反了不转让条款，导致合同无效。无论买方是否遭受任何损失，情况都是如此。\n\n教育目标：不可分配的规定是可执行的。但是，禁止转让“合同”而不具体规定权利或义务的一般条款将被解释为（1）允许权利转让和（2）禁止职责委托。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 322（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了禁止转让“合同”的合同条款仅禁止义务的委托）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为“本合同不得转让”意味着义务不得被委托，而卖方委托了义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":30,"sourceQuestionPage":88,"sourceQuestionPages":[88],"sourceExplanationPages":[89,90],"sourceAnswerPages":[89,90],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-031","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A famous chef entered into a written agreement with a well-known interior decorator, who was respected for his unique designs, to design the interior of the chef's new restaurant and, upon the chef's approval of the design plan, to decorate and furnish the restaurant accordingly. The agreement was silent as to assignment or delegation by either party. Before beginning the work, the interior decorator sold his decorating business to a new decorator under an agreement in which the interior decorator assigned to the new decorator, and the new decorator agreed to complete, the chef's contract. The new decorator, also an experienced decorator of excellent repute, advised the chef of the assignment, and supplied him with information confirming both the new decorator's financial responsibility and past commercial success. Is the chef obligated to permit the new decorator to perform the agreement?","choices":{"A":"No, because the interior decorator's duties were of a personal nature, involving his reputation, taste, and skill.","B":"No, because the interior decorator's purported delegation to the new decorator of the interior decorator's obligations to the chef effected a novation.","C":"Yes, because the agreement contained no prohibition against assignment or delegation.","D":"Yes, because the chef received adequate assurances of the new decorator's ability to complete the job."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Nondelegable contractual duties\n\nDelegation not permitted when:\n\n• other contracting party has substantial interest in having delegating party perform (eg, in personal-services contract involving taste or special skill) or\n• delegation is prohibited by contract Although public policy favors free assignment of contractual rights and free delegation of contractual duties, delegation is not permitted when:\n• the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform (eg, in a personal-services contract involving taste or a special skill) or\n• the delegation is prohibited by the contract (eg, the contract says \"this contract may not be assigned\"). Here, the well-known interior decorator, who was respected for his unique designs, agreed in writing to design the chef's new restaurant. Although the agreement was silent as to assignment or delegation, the chef had a substantial interest in having the interior decorator perform. That is because the interior decorator's duties were of a personal nature, involving his reputation, taste, and skill. Therefore, the interior decorator could not delegate his duties, and the chef is not required to permit the new decorator to perform the agreement (Choice C). (Choice B) A novation is a substituted contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party. Here, the interior decorator's purported delegation to the new decorator was not a novation since the chef never agreed to replace the interior decorator with the new decorator. (Choice D) The fact that the chef received adequate assurances of the new decorator's ability to complete the job (eg, information regarding the new decorator's past commercial success) is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Delegation of contractual duties is not permitted when (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the contract prohibits delegation.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of performance of duty).","educationalObjective":"Delegation of contractual duties is not permitted when (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the contract prohibits delegation.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of performance of duty).","rule":"Delegation of contractual duties is not permitted when (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the contract prohibits delegation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the chef obligated to permit the new decorator to perform the agreement?","rule":"Delegation of contractual duties is not permitted when (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the contract prohibits delegation.","analysis":"Nondelegable contractual duties\n\nDelegation not permitted when:\n\n• other contracting party has substantial interest in having delegating party perform (eg, in personal-services contract involving taste or special skill) or\n• delegation is prohibited by contract Although public policy favors free assignment of contractual rights and free delegation of contractual duties, delegation is not permitted when:\n• the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform (eg, in a personal-services contract involving taste or a special skill) or\n• the delegation is prohibited by the contract (eg, the contract says \"this contract may not be assigned\"). Here, the well-known interior decorator, who was respected for his unique designs, agreed in writing to design the chef's new restaurant. Although the agreement was silent as to assignment or delegation, the chef had a substantial interest in having the interior decorator perform. That is because the interior decorator's duties were of a personal nature, involving his reputation, taste, and skill. Therefore, the interior decorator could not delegate his duties, and the chef is not required to permit the new decorator to perform the agreement (Choice C). (Choice B) A novation is a substituted contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party. Here, the interior decorator's purported delegation to the new decorator was not a novation since the chef never agreed to replace the interior decorator with the new decorator. (Choice D) The fact that the chef received adequate assurances of the new decorator's ability to complete the job (eg, information regarding the new decorator's past commercial success) is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the interior decorator's duties were of a personal nature, involving his reputation, taste, and skill."},"questionZh":"一位著名的厨师与一位因其独特的设计而受人尊敬的著名室内装饰师签订了一份书面协议，负责设计他的新餐厅的室内装饰，并在设计方案得到厨师批准后，对餐厅进行相应的装饰和布置。该协议没有提及任何一方的指派或授权。在开始工作之前，室内装饰师将其装饰业务出售给新的装饰师，根据协议，室内装饰师将其分配给新的装饰师，而新的装饰师同意完成厨师合同。新装修师也是一位经验丰富、声誉卓著的装修师，他向厨师提出了这项任务的建议，并向他提供了确认新装修师的财务责任和过去商业成功的信息。厨师有义务允许新装潢师履行协议吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为室内装饰师的职责具有个人性质，涉及他的声誉、品味和技能。","B":"不可以，因为室内装饰师所谓的将室内装饰师对厨师的义务委托给新装饰师，从而实现了更替。","C":"是的，因为该协议没有禁止转让或授权。","D":"是的，因为厨师得到了新装潢师完成工作能力的充分保证。"},"explanationZh":"不可委托的合同义务\n\n在以下情况下不允许委托：\n\n• 其他缔约方对委托方履行具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止授权 尽管公共政策支持合同权利的自由转让和合同义务的自由授权，但在以下情况下不允许授权：\n• 合同的另一方对委托方履行合同具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止委托（例如，合同规定“本合同不得转让”）。在这里，这位因其独特的设计而受到尊重的著名室内装饰师书面同意为厨师设计新餐厅。尽管协议没有提及分配或授权，但厨师对由该室内装饰师亲自履行非常感兴趣。这是因为室内装饰师的职责具有个人性质，涉及他的声誉、品味和技能。因此，室内装修师不能委托其职责，厨师也无需允许新装修师履行协议（选项C）。 （选择 B） 更替是一种替代合同，其中当事人同意用新当事人取代原合同当事人。在这里，所谓的室内装饰师委托给新装饰师并不是一种创新，因为厨师从未同意用新装饰师替换室内装饰师。 （选项 D） 厨师是否获得了新装潢师完成工作的能力的充分保证（例如，有关新装潢师过去商业成功的信息）这一事实是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，不允许委托合同职责：(1) 合同另一方对委托方履行职责具有重大利益，或 (2) 合同禁止委托。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行职责的委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，不允许委托合同职责：(1) 合同另一方对委托方履行职责拥有重大利益；或 (2) 合同禁止委托。","iracZh":{"issue":"厨师有义务允许新装潢师履行协议吗？","rule":"在以下情况下，不允许委托合同职责：(1) 合同另一方对委托方履行职责拥有重大利益；或 (2) 合同禁止委托。","analysis":"不可委托的合同义务\n\n在以下情况下不允许委托：\n\n• 其他缔约方对委托方履行具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止授权 尽管公共政策支持合同权利的自由转让和合同义务的自由授权，但在以下情况下不允许授权：\n• 合同的另一方对委托方履行合同具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止委托（例如，合同规定“本合同不得转让”）。在这里，这位因其独特的设计而受到尊重的著名室内装饰师书面同意为厨师设计新餐厅。尽管协议没有提及分配或授权，但厨师对由该室内装饰师亲自履行非常感兴趣。这是因为室内装饰师的职责具有个人性质，涉及他的声誉、品味和技能。因此，室内装修师不能委托其职责，厨师也无需允许新装修师履行协议（选项C）。 （选择 B） 更替是一种替代合同，其中当事人同意用新当事人取代原合同当事人。在这里，所谓的室内装饰师委托给新装饰师并不是一种创新，因为厨师从未同意用新装饰师替换室内装饰师。 （选项 D） 厨师是否获得了新装潢师完成工作的能力的充分保证（例如，有关新装潢师过去商业成功的信息）这一事实是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，不允许委托合同职责：(1) 合同另一方对委托方履行职责具有重大利益，或 (2) 合同禁止委托。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行职责的委托）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为室内装饰师的职责具有个人性质，涉及他的声誉、品味和技能。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"adequate assurances","ipa":"/ˈædəkwət ʌʃˈʊɹənsɪz/","syllables":"ad·e·quate as·sur·ances","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"充分保证：出现合理不安时要求对方确认将适当履行","contextualMeaning":"充分保证：出现合理不安时要求对方确认将适当履行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the chef received adequate assurances of the new decorator's ability to complete the job.","synonyms":["assurance of due performance"]},{"term":"novation","ipa":"/nəu'veiʃən/","syllables":"no·va·tion","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 更新；[经] 债券更新","contextualMeaning":"债务更新：经各方同意以新当事人取代原义务人并解除后者","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"No, because the interior decorator's purported delegation to the new decorator of the interior decorator's obligations to the chef effected a novation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":31,"sourceQuestionPage":91,"sourceQuestionPages":[91],"sourceExplanationPages":[92,93],"sourceAnswerPages":[92,93],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-032","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A written construction contract began with the following recital: \"This Agreement, between a real estate company (hereafter called the 'owner'), and a construction company and the construction company's president (hereafter called the 'contractor'), witnesseth:\" The signatures to the contract appeared in the following format: Real estate company By /s/ President; Construction company By /s/ Vice President and /s/ President, as representatives of Construction company. The construction company became insolvent and defaulted. The real estate company sued the president of the construction company individually for the breach, and at the trial the president offered evidence from the pre-contract negotiations that only the construction company was to be legally responsible for performing the contract. If the court finds the contract to be completely integrated, is the president's offered evidence admissible?","choices":{"A":"No, because of the application of the \"four corners\" rule, under which the meaning of a completely integrated contract must be ascertained solely from its own terms.","B":"No, because the legal effect of the president's signature cannot be altered by evidence of prior understandings.","C":"Yes, because the evidence would contradict neither the recital nor the form of the president's signature.","D":"Yes, because the writing is ambiguous as to whether or not the president was intended individually to be a contracting party."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreements—or other evidence outside the \"four corners\" of the contract—to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But the parol evidence rule does not bar such evidence, and such evidence is always admissible, when it is used to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. This is true regardless of whether the contract is completely or partially integrated. Here, the recital indicates that the construction company and its president are parties to the contract (together, \"the contractor\"), while the signature lines show the president signing merely as a representative of the company. This shows that the writing is ambiguous as to whether the president was intended individually to be a contracting party. Therefore, evidence from the parties' pre-contract negotiations is admissible to clarify the terms of the written contract. (Choice A) A court does not have to rely solely on the \"four corners\" of a written contract to determine its meaning. Extrinsic evidence such as the parties' pre-contract negotiations can be used to interpret ambiguous terms. (Choice B) The legal effect of the president's signature can be altered by evidence of prior understandings because the signature creates ambiguity regarding the contract's terms (eg, whether the president—as an individual—is a contracting party). (Choice C) Evidence that only the construction company was to be legally responsible would contradict the recital since the recital identifies both the company and the president as parties. But since the signature lines suggest that the president is not a party, evidence of the parties' pre-contract negotiations is admissible to clarify the ambiguity.\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements when it is being offered to establish the meaning of an ambiguous term.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (prior negotiations are admissible to explain meaning of ambiguous term).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements when it is being offered to establish the meaning of an ambiguous term.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (prior negotiations are admissible to explain meaning of ambiguous term).","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements when it is being offered to establish the meaning of an ambiguous term.","irac":{"issue":"If the court finds the contract to be completely integrated, is the president's offered evidence admissible?","rule":"The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements when it is being offered to establish the meaning of an ambiguous term.","analysis":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreements—or other evidence outside the \"four corners\" of the contract—to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But the parol evidence rule does not bar such evidence, and such evidence is always admissible, when it is used to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. This is true regardless of whether the contract is completely or partially integrated. Here, the recital indicates that the construction company and its president are parties to the contract (together, \"the contractor\"), while the signature lines show the president signing merely as a representative of the company. This shows that the writing is ambiguous as to whether the president was intended individually to be a contracting party. Therefore, evidence from the parties' pre-contract negotiations is admissible to clarify the terms of the written contract. (Choice A) A court does not have to rely solely on the \"four corners\" of a written contract to determine its meaning. Extrinsic evidence such as the parties' pre-contract negotiations can be used to interpret ambiguous terms. (Choice B) The legal effect of the president's signature can be altered by evidence of prior understandings because the signature creates ambiguity regarding the contract's terms (eg, whether the president—as an individual—is a contracting party). (Choice C) Evidence that only the construction company was to be legally responsible would contradict the recital since the recital identifies both the company and the president as parties. But since the signature lines suggest that the president is not a party, evidence of the parties' pre-contract negotiations is admissible to clarify the ambiguity.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the writing is ambiguous as to whether or not the president was intended individually to be a contracting party."},"questionZh":"书面建筑合同以以下内容开头：“本协议，由一家房地产公司（以下称为“业主”）与一家建筑公司和该建筑公司的总裁（以下称为“承包商”）签订，证人：”合同的签名采用以下格式：房地产公司/s/总裁；建筑公司 由/s/副总裁和/s/总裁作为建筑公司的代表。该建筑公司资不抵债并违约。房地产公司以违约为由单独起诉建筑公司总裁，庭审中，总裁提供了合同前谈判的证据，证明只有建筑公司才负有履行合同的法律责任。如果法院认为合同完全完整，总统提供的证据是否可以采信？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为应用了“四个角”规则，根据该规则，完全完整的合同的含义必须仅根据其本身的条款来确定。","B":"不可以，因为总统签名的法律效力不能因事先谅解的证据而改变。","C":"是的，因为证据既不会与总统的陈述或签名的形式相矛盾。","D":"是的，因为关于总统是否打算单独成为缔约方，文字内容含糊不清。"},"explanationZh":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时的口头或书面协议的证据（或合同“四个角”之外的其他证据）来修改或反驳综合书面条款。但口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止此类证据，并且当此类证据用于澄清不明确的合同条款的含义时，此类证据始终是可接受的。无论合同是完全集成还是部分集成，都是如此。此处，说明文字表明建筑公司及其总裁是合同当事人（统称为“承包商”），而签名行则显示总裁仅作为公司代表签署。这表明，对于总统是否打算单独成为缔约方，该文字的表述含糊不清。因此，双方合同前谈判的证据可以用来澄清书面合同的条款。 （选项A）法院不必仅依靠书面合同的“四个角”来确定其含义。诸如双方签订合同前的谈判等外在证据可用于解释不明确的条款。 （选择B） 总统签名的法律效力可以通过事先谅解的证据来改变，因为签名会导致合同条款含糊不清（例如，总统作为个人是否是缔约方）。 （选项C）只有建筑公司负有法律责任的证据与陈述相矛盾，因为陈述将公司和总统都认定为当事人。但由于签名行表明总统不是政党，因此可以采用双方合同前谈判的证据来澄清歧义。\n\n教育目标：当提供先前或同期协议的证据来确定模糊术语的含义时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允许事先协商解释不明确术语的含义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当提供先前或同期协议的证据来确定模糊术语的含义时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院认为合同完全完整，总统提供的证据是否可以采信？","rule":"当提供先前或同期协议的证据来确定模糊术语的含义时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据。","analysis":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时的口头或书面协议的证据（或合同“四个角”之外的其他证据）来修改或反驳综合书面条款。但口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止此类证据，并且当此类证据用于澄清不明确的合同条款的含义时，此类证据始终是可接受的。无论合同是完全集成还是部分集成，都是如此。此处，说明文字表明建筑公司及其总裁是合同当事人（统称为“承包商”），而签名行则显示总裁仅作为公司代表签署。这表明，对于总统是否打算单独成为缔约方，该文字的表述含糊不清。因此，双方合同前谈判的证据可以用来澄清书面合同的条款。 （选项A）法院不必仅依靠书面合同的“四个角”来确定其含义。诸如双方签订合同前的谈判等外在证据可用于解释不明确的条款。 （选择B） 总统签名的法律效力可以通过事先谅解的证据来改变，因为签名会导致合同条款含糊不清（例如，总统作为个人是否是缔约方）。 （选项C）只有建筑公司负有法律责任的证据与陈述相矛盾，因为陈述将公司和总统都认定为当事人。但由于签名行表明总统不是政党，因此可以采用双方合同前谈判的证据来澄清歧义。\n\n教育目标：当提供先前或同期协议的证据来确定模糊术语的含义时，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止使用先前或同期协议的证据。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允许事先协商解释不明确术语的含义）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为关于总统是否打算单独成为缔约方，文字内容含糊不清。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":32,"sourceQuestionPage":94,"sourceQuestionPages":[94],"sourceExplanationPages":[95,96],"sourceAnswerPages":[95,96],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-033","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A homeowner and a planner signed a detailed writing in which the planner, a landscape architect, agreed to landscape and replant the owner's residential property in accordance with a design prepared by the planner and incorporated in the writing. The owner agreed to pay $10,000 for the work upon its completion. The owner's spouse was not a party to the agreement and had no ownership interest in the premises. Shortly before the agreement was signed, the owner and the planner orally agreed that the writing would not become binding on either party unless the owner's spouse should approve the landscaping design. If the owner's spouse disapproves the design and the owner refuses to allow the planner to proceed with the work, is evidence of the oral agreement admissible in the planner's action against the owner for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parol evidence rule bars evidence of a prior oral agreement even if the latter is consistent with the terms of a partial integration.","B":"No, because the prior oral agreement contradicted the writing by making the parties' duties conditional.","C":"Yes, because the evidence shows that the writing was intended to take effect only if the approval occurred.","D":"Yes, because the oral agreement required approval by a third party."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But this rule does not apply when a party is attacking the validity of the agreement—eg, by asserting there was a condition precedent to effectiveness that failed to occur. As a result, evidence of such a condition precedent is admissible. Here, before the writing was signed, the parties orally agreed that it would become binding (ie, take effect) only if the owner's spouse approved the landscape design—a condition precedent to effectiveness. Therefore, the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of the oral agreement, and that evidence is likely admissible (Choice B). (Choice A) The parol evidence rule allows evidence of a prior agreement if that agreement is consistent with the terms of a partial integration (ie, a writing that sets forth the parties' final agreement about some, but not all, terms). (Choice D) The oral agreement is admissible because it evidences a condition precedent to effectiveness; the fact that the oral agreement required approval by a third party is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (integrated agreement subject to oral requirement).","educationalObjective":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (integrated agreement subject to oral requirement).","rule":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","irac":{"issue":"If the owner's spouse disapproves the design and the owner refuses to allow the planner to proceed with the work, is evidence of the oral agreement admissible in the planner's action against the owner for breach of contract?","rule":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","analysis":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But this rule does not apply when a party is attacking the validity of the agreement—eg, by asserting there was a condition precedent to effectiveness that failed to occur. As a result, evidence of such a condition precedent is admissible. Here, before the writing was signed, the parties orally agreed that it would become binding (ie, take effect) only if the owner's spouse approved the landscape design—a condition precedent to effectiveness. Therefore, the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of the oral agreement, and that evidence is likely admissible (Choice B). (Choice A) The parol evidence rule allows evidence of a prior agreement if that agreement is consistent with the terms of a partial integration (ie, a writing that sets forth the parties' final agreement about some, but not all, terms). (Choice D) The oral agreement is admissible because it evidences a condition precedent to effectiveness; the fact that the oral agreement required approval by a third party is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the evidence shows that the writing was intended to take effect only if the approval occurred."},"questionZh":"房主和规划师签署了一份详细的书面文件，其中规划师（景观设计师）同意按照规划师准备并纳入书面文件的设计对业主的住宅进行景观美化和重新种植。业主同意在工程完成后支付 10,000 美元。业主的配偶不是协议的一方，也不拥有该房产的所有权权益。在协议签署前不久，业主和规划师口头同意，除非业主的配偶批准景观设计，否则书面协议对任何一方均不具有约束力。如果业主的配偶不同意设计并且业主拒绝让规划师继续进行工作，规划师对业主提起违约诉讼时，口头协议的证据是否可以采信？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供先前口头协议的证据，即使后者与部分整合的条款一致。","B":"不可以，因为先前的口头协议与书面协议相矛盾，使双方的义务带有条件。","C":"是的，因为有证据表明，只有获得批准，该书面文件才会生效。","D":"是的，因为口头协议需要第三方的批准。"},"explanationZh":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是的话，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时同意修改或反驳综合写作条款的证据。但当一方攻击协议的有效性时，该规则不适用——例如，声称存在生效的先决条件但未能发生。因此，此类先决条件的证据是可接受的。在这里，在签署书面文件之前，双方口头同意，只有业主的配偶批准景观设计（这是生效的先决条件），该书面文件才具有约束力（即生效）。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止口头协议的证据，并且该证据很可能是可接受的（选择B）。 （选择A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许提供先前协议的证据，前提是该协议与部分整合的条款一致（即，阐述双方关于部分（但不是全部）条款的最终协议的书面文件）。 （选项D）口头协议是可以接受的，因为它证明了生效的先决条件；口头协议需要第三方批准这一事实无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于建立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 217（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（综合协议须遵守口头要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于确立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果业主的配偶不同意设计并且业主拒绝让规划师继续进行工作，规划师对业主提起违约诉讼时，口头协议的证据是否可以采信？","rule":"根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于确立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。","analysis":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是的话，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时同意修改或反驳综合写作条款的证据。但当一方攻击协议的有效性时，该规则不适用——例如，声称存在生效的先决条件但未能发生。因此，此类先决条件的证据是可接受的。在这里，在签署书面文件之前，双方口头同意，只有业主的配偶批准景观设计（这是生效的先决条件），该书面文件才具有约束力（即生效）。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止口头协议的证据，并且该证据很可能是可接受的（选择B）。 （选择A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许提供先前协议的证据，前提是该协议与部分整合的条款一致（即，阐述双方关于部分（但不是全部）条款的最终协议的书面文件）。 （选项D）口头协议是可以接受的，因为它证明了生效的先决条件；口头协议需要第三方批准这一事实无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于建立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 217（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（综合协议须遵守口头要求）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为有证据表明，只有获得批准，该书面文件才会生效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":33,"sourceQuestionPage":97,"sourceQuestionPages":[97],"sourceExplanationPages":[98,99],"sourceAnswerPages":[98,99],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-034","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A sugar distributor contracted to deliver to a bakery its sugar requirements for a one-year period. Before delivery of the first scheduled installment, the sugar distributor sold its business and assigned all of its sale contracts to a reputable and longtime sugar retailer. The sugar distributor informed the bakery of this transaction. The bakery accepted the sugar retailer's delivery of the first installment under the distributor-bakery contract, but the bakery paid the contract price for that installment to the distributor and refused to pay anything to the retailer. In an action by the retailer against the bakery for the contractual amount of the first installment, which of the following, if any, will be an effective defense for the bakery?","choices":{"A":"The bakery had not expressly agreed to accept the sugar retailer as its sugar supplier.","B":"The bakery had not expressly agreed to accept the sugar retailer as its sugar supplier, and the sugar distributor remained obligated to the bakery even though it had assigned the contract to the sugar retailer.","C":"The bakery's payment of the contractual installment to the sugar distributor discharged its obligation.","D":"No defense will be effective for the bakery."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract provisions prohibiting assignments/delegations\n\nType of Explicit prohibitions Assignment of Delegation of provision rights duties\n\nNone N/A Allowed\n\nSpecific Assignment of rights & Barred delegation of duties\n\nAssignment of rights only Barred Allowed\n\nDelegation of duties only Allowed Barred\n\nGeneral Assignment of \"the contract\"\n\nEither party to a contract can assign its rights or delegate its duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). A general assignment of the entire contract will be construed as both an assignment of rights AND a delegation of duties. For example, a seller's general assignment of an entire contract gives the delegatee the right to receive payment directly from the buyer provided that the delegatee delivers the goods. Here, the sugar distributor assigned the bakery's requirements contract to the sugar retailer, which operated as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties. The retailer then had a right to receive payment directly from the bakery since the retailer delivered the first installment of sugar to it. Therefore, the bakery's payment to the distributor did not discharge its obligation to pay the retailer (Choice C). (Choices A & B) Unless the contract states otherwise, contractual duties can be delegated without the promisee's express consent. Therefore, the bakery must accept the sugar retailer as its sugar supplier even though she did not expressly agree to do so. And though the sugar distributor remains liable to the bakery if the retailer fails to perform, the retailer did perform by delivering the first installment.\n\nEducational objective: A general assignment of an entire contract will be construed as both an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). For example, a seller's assignment of an entire contract gives the delegatee the duty to deliver the goods and the right to receive payment directly from the buyer.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a general assignment is an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties).","educationalObjective":"A general assignment of an entire contract will be construed as both an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). For example, a seller's assignment of an entire contract gives the delegatee the duty to deliver the goods and the right to receive payment directly from the buyer.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a general assignment is an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties).","rule":"A general assignment of an entire contract will be construed as both an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). For example, a seller's assignment of an entire contract gives the delegatee the duty to deliver the goods and the right to receive payment directly from the buyer.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the retailer against the bakery for the contractual amount of the first installment, which of the following, if any, will be an effective defense for the bakery?","rule":"A general assignment of an entire contract will be construed as both an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). For example, a seller's assignment of an entire contract gives the delegatee the duty to deliver the goods and the right to receive payment directly from the buyer.","analysis":"Contract provisions prohibiting assignments/delegations\n\nType of Explicit prohibitions Assignment of Delegation of provision rights duties\n\nNone N/A Allowed\n\nSpecific Assignment of rights & Barred delegation of duties\n\nAssignment of rights only Barred Allowed\n\nDelegation of duties only Allowed Barred\n\nGeneral Assignment of \"the contract\"\n\nEither party to a contract can assign its rights or delegate its duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). A general assignment of the entire contract will be construed as both an assignment of rights AND a delegation of duties. For example, a seller's general assignment of an entire contract gives the delegatee the right to receive payment directly from the buyer provided that the delegatee delivers the goods. Here, the sugar distributor assigned the bakery's requirements contract to the sugar retailer, which operated as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties. The retailer then had a right to receive payment directly from the bakery since the retailer delivered the first installment of sugar to it. Therefore, the bakery's payment to the distributor did not discharge its obligation to pay the retailer (Choice C). (Choices A & B) Unless the contract states otherwise, contractual duties can be delegated without the promisee's express consent. Therefore, the bakery must accept the sugar retailer as its sugar supplier even though she did not expressly agree to do so. And though the sugar distributor remains liable to the bakery if the retailer fails to perform, the retailer did perform by delivering the first installment.","conclusion":"Answer D: No defense will be effective for the bakery."},"questionZh":"一家食糖分销商签订合同，向一家面包店提供为期一年的食糖需求。在交付第一笔预定分期付款之前，糖经销商出售了其业务，并将其所有销售合同转让给了一家信誉良好的长期糖零售商。食糖经销商将这笔交易通知了面包店。面包店接受了食糖零售商根据分销商与面包店合同交付的第一批货款，但面包店向分销商支付了该批货的合同价，并拒绝向零售商支付任何费用。在零售商针对面包店要求支付第一期合同金额的诉讼中，以下哪一项（如果有）可以为面包店提供有效的抗辩？","choicesZh":{"A":"该面包店并未明确同意接受食糖零售商作为其食糖供应商。","B":"面包店并未明确同意接受食糖零售商作为其食糖供应商，而食糖分销商尽管已将合同转让给食糖零售商，但仍对面包店负有义务。","C":"面包店向糖经销商支付了合同分期付款，从而履行了其义务。","D":"任何防御措施对面包店都无效。"},"explanationZh":"禁止转让/委托的合同条款\n\n明确禁止的类型 条款权利委托的分配 职责的分配\n\n无 N/A 允许\n\n具体的权利分配和禁止的职责委托\n\n仅转让权利 禁止 允许\n\n只允许职责委派 禁止\n\n“合同”的一般转让\n\n合同的任何一方都可以将其权利或义务委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。整个合同的一般转让将被解释为权利转让和责任委托。例如，卖方对整个合同的一般转让赋予受托人直接从买方收取付款的权利，前提是受托人交付货物。在此，食糖分销商将面包店的需求合同分配给食糖零售商，该零售商以权利转让和职责委托的方式运作。由于零售商向面包店交付了第一批糖，因此零售商有权直接从面包店收到付款。因此，面包店向经销商付款并没有解除其向零售商付款的义务（选项C）。 （选项A和B）除非合同另有规定，合同义务可以在未经受诺人明确同意的情况下进行委托。因此，即使她没有明确同意，面包店也必须接受糖零售商作为其糖供应商。尽管如果零售商未能履约，食糖分销商仍需对面包店承担责任，但零售商确实通过交付第一期付款来履约。\n\n教育目标：整个合同的一般转让将被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。例如，卖方转让整个合同赋予受托人交付货物的义务和直接从买方收取付款的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释一般转让是权利转让和义务委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"整个合同的一般转让将被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。例如，卖方转让整个合同赋予受托人交付货物的义务和直接从买方收取付款的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"在零售商针对面包店要求支付第一期合同金额的诉讼中，以下哪一项（如果有）可以为面包店提供有效的抗辩？","rule":"整个合同的一般转让将被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。例如，卖方转让整个合同赋予受托人交付货物的义务和直接从买方收取付款的权利。","analysis":"禁止转让/委托的合同条款\n\n明确禁止的类型 条款权利委托的分配 职责的分配\n\n无 N/A 允许\n\n具体的权利分配和禁止的职责委托\n\n仅转让权利 禁止 允许\n\n只允许职责委派 禁止\n\n“合同”的一般转让\n\n合同的任何一方都可以将其权利或义务委托给非当事人（即受委托人）。整个合同的一般转让将被解释为权利转让和责任委托。例如，卖方对整个合同的一般转让赋予受托人直接从买方收取付款的权利，前提是受托人交付货物。在此，食糖分销商将面包店的需求合同分配给食糖零售商，该零售商以权利转让和职责委托的方式运作。由于零售商向面包店交付了第一批糖，因此零售商有权直接从面包店收到付款。因此，面包店向经销商付款并没有解除其向零售商付款的义务（选项C）。 （选项A和B）除非合同另有规定，合同义务可以在未经受诺人明确同意的情况下进行委托。因此，即使她没有明确同意，面包店也必须接受糖零售商作为其糖供应商。尽管如果零售商未能履约，食糖分销商仍需对面包店承担责任，但零售商确实通过交付第一期付款来履约。\n\n教育目标：整个合同的一般转让将被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。例如，卖方转让整个合同赋予受托人交付货物的义务和直接从买方收取付款的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释一般转让是权利转让和义务委托）。","conclusion":"答案 D：任何防御措施对面包店都无效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"contract price","ipa":"/kˈɑntɹˌækt pɹˈaɪs/","syllables":"con·tract price","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合同价格：双方协议约定的交易价款","contextualMeaning":"合同价格：双方协议约定的交易价款","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The bakery accepted the sugar retailer's delivery of the first installment under the distributor-bakery contract, but the bakery paid the contract price for that installment to the distributor and refused to pay anything to the retailer.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":34,"sourceQuestionPage":100,"sourceQuestionPages":[100],"sourceExplanationPages":[101,102],"sourceAnswerPages":[101,102],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-035","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"The owner of a gun store had been doing business with a hunter for many years. The hunter entered the gun store and spoke to the owner about purchasing a new hunting rifle on credit. The hunter disclosed that he intended to use the rifle on a turkey-hunting trip that weekend even though it was two weeks before turkey-hunting season began. The hunter left with the rifle after signing a written agreement to pay for the rifle in 12 monthly installments. The rifle functioned well on the hunter's trip, during which a friend advised the hunter that he could avoid paying for the rifle because the contract was illegal. The hunter took the friend's advice and never paid any installments. If the store owner sues for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the store owner failed to disaffirm the contract's illegal purpose before giving the hunter possession of the rifle.","B":"No, because the store owner knew that the hunter intended to use the rifle for an illegal purpose.","C":"Yes, because a contract to sell a rifle is not, in and of itself, illegal.","D":"Yes, because the store owner substantially performed and did not sell the rifle in order to further the hunter's illegal purpose."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Recovery for breach of illegal contract\n\nExpectation damages\n• Party justifiably ignorant of illegality*\n• Party substantially performed & lacked illegal (full value of lost purpose performance)\n• Contract separable into legal / illegal parts or\n• Contract illegal due to violation of licensing requirement designed only to raise revenue Restitution damages\n• Party justifiably ignorant of illegality*\n• Party less culpable than other party or (reasonable value of benefit conferred)\n• Party withdrew before illegal purpose achieved & did not engage in serious misconduct *Recovery on this basis is barred if other party is also justifiably ignorant.\n\nAn illegal contract arises when one or both parties' purpose, formation, or performance is against the law. These contracts are usually void, and there is no recovery for breach. However, an exception arises if one party lacked an illegal purpose and substantially performed under the contract. That party may recover expectation damages for breach—even if he/she knew of the other party's illegal purpose.* This is true unless:\n• the performing party took action to further the other party's illegal purpose or\n\n• the illegal purpose involves grave social harm (eg, threat to human life).\n\nHere, the contract was illegal because the hunter's purpose was to use the rifle to hunt outside the mandated turkey-hunting season. However, the store owner substantially performed by giving the hunter the rifle and had no illegal purpose of his own. And though the store owner knew of the hunter's illegal purpose, the store owner took no action to further it (Choice B). Additionally, hunting two weeks out of season does not involve grave social harm. Therefore, the store owner will likely prevail in a breach-of-contract suit. *If the performing party was unaware of the other party's illegal purpose, then no further analysis is necessary. (Choice A) The store owner did not need to disaffirm the contract's illegal purpose—eg, by telling the hunter not to use the rifle on the upcoming hunting trip. It is enough that the store owner did not act to further the hunter's illegal purpose and there was no threat of grave social harm.\n\n(Choice C) A contract to sell a rifle is not, in and of itself, illegal (absent statutory restrictions—not seen here). But the contract is still illegal because the hunter intended to use the rifle for an illegal purpose.\n\nEducational objective: Illegal contracts are usually void and there is no recovery for breach. However, a party who substantially performed and lacked an illegal purpose may recover—even if he/she knew of the other party's illegal purpose—unless (1) the performing party took action to further that illegal purpose or (2) the purpose involves grave social harm.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 182 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining the effect of performance under a contract when the other party's purpose is illegal).","educationalObjective":"Illegal contracts are usually void and there is no recovery for breach. However, a party who substantially performed and lacked an illegal purpose may recover—even if he/she knew of the other party's illegal purpose—unless (1) the performing party took action to further that illegal purpose or (2) the purpose involves grave social harm.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 182 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining the effect of performance under a contract when the other party's purpose is illegal).","rule":"Illegal contracts are usually void and there is no recovery for breach. However, a party who substantially performed and lacked an illegal purpose may recover—even if he/she knew of the other party's illegal purpose—unless (1) the performing party took action to further that illegal purpose or (2) the purpose involves grave social harm.","irac":{"issue":"If the store owner sues for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","rule":"Illegal contracts are usually void and there is no recovery for breach. However, a party who substantially performed and lacked an illegal purpose may recover—even if he/she knew of the other party's illegal purpose—unless (1) the performing party took action to further that illegal purpose or (2) the purpose involves grave social harm.","analysis":"Recovery for breach of illegal contract\n\nExpectation damages\n• Party justifiably ignorant of illegality*\n• Party substantially performed & lacked illegal (full value of lost purpose performance)\n• Contract separable into legal / illegal parts or\n• Contract illegal due to violation of licensing requirement designed only to raise revenue Restitution damages\n• Party justifiably ignorant of illegality*\n• Party less culpable than other party or (reasonable value of benefit conferred)\n• Party withdrew before illegal purpose achieved & did not engage in serious misconduct *Recovery on this basis is barred if other party is also justifiably ignorant.\n\nAn illegal contract arises when one or both parties' purpose, formation, or performance is against the law. These contracts are usually void, and there is no recovery for breach. However, an exception arises if one party lacked an illegal purpose and substantially performed under the contract. That party may recover expectation damages for breach—even if he/she knew of the other party's illegal purpose.* This is true unless:\n• the performing party took action to further the other party's illegal purpose or\n\n• the illegal purpose involves grave social harm (eg, threat to human life).\n\nHere, the contract was illegal because the hunter's purpose was to use the rifle to hunt outside the mandated turkey-hunting season. However, the store owner substantially performed by giving the hunter the rifle and had no illegal purpose of his own. And though the store owner knew of the hunter's illegal purpose, the store owner took no action to further it (Choice B). Additionally, hunting two weeks out of season does not involve grave social harm. Therefore, the store owner will likely prevail in a breach-of-contract suit. *If the performing party was unaware of the other party's illegal purpose, then no further analysis is necessary. (Choice A) The store owner did not need to disaffirm the contract's illegal purpose—eg, by telling the hunter not to use the rifle on the upcoming hunting trip. It is enough that the store owner did not act to further the hunter's illegal purpose and there was no threat of grave social harm.\n\n(Choice C) A contract to sell a rifle is not, in and of itself, illegal (absent statutory restrictions—not seen here). But the contract is still illegal because the hunter intended to use the rifle for an illegal purpose.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the store owner substantially performed and did not sell the rifle in order to further the hunter's illegal purpose."},"questionZh":"一家枪店的老板多年来一直与一名猎人做生意。猎人走进枪店，与店主商量赊购一把新猎枪的事宜。猎人透露，尽管距离火鸡狩猎季节开始还有两周，但他打算在那个周末的火鸡狩猎之旅中使用这支步枪。猎人在签署了一份书面协议，分 12 个月分期付款后，带着步枪离开了。这支步枪在猎人的旅途中表现良好，期间一位朋友建议猎人可以避免支付这支步枪的费用，因为合同是非法的。猎人听取了朋友的建议，从未支付任何分期付款。如果店主起诉违约，有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为店主在将步枪交给猎人之前未能否认合同的非法目的。","B":"不，因为店主知道猎人打算将步枪用于非法目的。","C":"是的，因为销售步枪的合同本身并不违法。","D":"是的，因为店主实质上履行了义务，并没有出售步枪，以进一步实现猎人的非法目的。"},"explanationZh":"违法合同违约追偿\n\n预期损失\n• 当事人有理由不知道违法行为*\n• 当事人实质上履行了职责并且没有违法（失去目的履行的全部价值）\n• 合同可分为合法/非法部分或\n• 由于违反旨在增加收入的许可要求而导致合同非法 赔偿损失\n• 当事人有理由不知道违法行为*\n• 一方的罪责比另一方轻，或者（所授予利益的合理价值）\n• 一方在达到非法目的之前退出并且没有从事严重不当行为 *如果另一方也有理由不知情，则禁止在此基础上进行恢复。\n\n当一方或双方的目的、形成或履行违反法律时，就会产生非法合同。这些合同通常无效，并且违约后无法获得赔偿。然而，如果一方不存在非法目的并且实质上按照合同履行，则出现例外情况。该方可能会因违约而获得预期损害赔偿——即使他/她知道另一方的非法目的。*这是正确的，除非：\n• 履约方采取行动促进另一方的非法目的或\n\n• 非法目的涉及严重的社会危害（例如，对人类生命的威胁）。\n\n在这里，合同是非法的，因为猎人的目的是在规定的火鸡狩猎季节之外使用步枪进行狩猎。不过，店主实质上是把步枪交给猎人，并无任何非法目的。尽管店主知道猎人的非法目的，但店主没有采取任何行动来进一步实现这一目的（选项 B）。此外，淡季两周的狩猎不会造成严重的社会危害。因此，店主很可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 *如果履行方不知道对方的非法目的，则无需进一步分析。 （选项A） 店主不需要否认合同的非法目的——例如，告诉猎人在即将到来的狩猎旅行中不要使用步枪。店主没有采取任何行动来助长猎人的非法目的，也没有造成严重社会危害的威胁，这就足够了。\n\n（选项 C）销售步枪的合同本身并不违法（没有法定限制——此处未见）。但该合同仍然是非法的，因为猎人打算将步枪用于非法目的。\n\n教育目标：非法合同通常是无效的，并且违约后无法获得赔偿。然而，实质性履行且不存在非法目的的一方即使知道另一方的非法目的，也可以获得赔偿，除非 (1) 履行方采取行动以推进该非法目的，或 (2) 该目的涉及严重的社会危害。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 182（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释当另一方的目的非法时根据合同履行的效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非法合同通常无效，并且违约后无法获得赔偿。然而，实质性履行且不存在非法目的的一方即使知道另一方的非法目的，也可以获得赔偿，除非 (1) 履行方采取行动以推进该非法目的，或 (2) 该目的涉及严重的社会危害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果店主起诉违约，有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"非法合同通常无效，并且违约后无法获得赔偿。然而，实质性履行且不存在非法目的的一方即使知道另一方的非法目的，也可以获得赔偿，除非 (1) 履行方采取行动以推进该非法目的，或 (2) 该目的涉及严重的社会危害。","analysis":"违法合同违约追偿\n\n预期损失\n• 当事人有理由不知道违法行为*\n• 当事人实质上履行了职责并且没有违法（失去目的履行的全部价值）\n• 合同可分为合法/非法部分或\n• 由于违反旨在增加收入的许可要求而导致合同非法 赔偿损失\n• 当事人有理由不知道违法行为*\n• 一方的罪责比另一方轻，或者（所授予利益的合理价值）\n• 一方在达到非法目的之前退出并且没有从事严重不当行为 *如果另一方也有理由不知情，则禁止在此基础上进行恢复。\n\n当一方或双方的目的、形成或履行违反法律时，就会产生非法合同。这些合同通常无效，并且违约后无法获得赔偿。然而，如果一方不存在非法目的并且实质上按照合同履行，则出现例外情况。该方可能会因违约而获得预期损害赔偿——即使他/她知道另一方的非法目的。*这是正确的，除非：\n• 履约方采取行动促进另一方的非法目的或\n\n• 非法目的涉及严重的社会危害（例如，对人类生命的威胁）。\n\n在这里，合同是非法的，因为猎人的目的是在规定的火鸡狩猎季节之外使用步枪进行狩猎。不过，店主实质上是把步枪交给猎人，并无任何非法目的。尽管店主知道猎人的非法目的，但店主没有采取任何行动来进一步实现这一目的（选项 B）。此外，淡季两周的狩猎不会造成严重的社会危害。因此，店主很可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 *如果履行方不知道对方的非法目的，则无需进一步分析。 （选项A） 店主不需要否认合同的非法目的——例如，告诉猎人在即将到来的狩猎旅行中不要使用步枪。店主没有采取任何行动来助长猎人的非法目的，也没有造成严重社会危害的威胁，这就足够了。\n\n（选项 C）销售步枪的合同本身并不违法（没有法定限制——此处未见）。但该合同仍然是非法的，因为猎人打算将步枪用于非法目的。\n\n教育目标：非法合同通常是无效的，并且违约后无法获得赔偿。然而，实质性履行且不存在非法目的的一方即使知道另一方的非法目的，也可以获得赔偿，除非 (1) 履行方采取行动以推进该非法目的，或 (2) 该目的涉及严重的社会危害。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 182（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释当另一方的目的非法时根据合同履行的效果）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为店主实质上履行了义务，并没有出售步枪，以进一步实现猎人的非法目的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"benefit conferred","ipa":"/bˈɛnəfɪt kʌnfˈɝd/","syllables":"ben·e·fit con·ferred","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"已授利益：一方实际提供并由另一方取得的价值","contextualMeaning":"已授利益：一方实际提供并由另一方取得的价值","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Recovery for breach of illegal contract Expectation damages • Party justifiably ignorant of illegality* • Party substantially performed & lacked illegal (full value of lost purpose performance) • Contract separable into legal / illegal parts or • Contract illegal due to violation of licensing requirement designed only to raise revenue Restitution damages • Party justifiably ignorant of illegality* • Party less culpab","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":35,"sourceQuestionPage":103,"sourceQuestionPages":[103],"sourceExplanationPages":[104,105],"sourceAnswerPages":[104,105],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-036","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A seller and a buyer have dealt with each other in hundreds of separate grain contracts over the last five years. In performing each contract, the seller delivered the grain to the buyer and, upon delivery, the buyer signed an invoice that showed an agreed-upon price for that delivery. Each invoice was silent in regard to any discount from the price for prompt payment. The custom of the grain trade is to allow a 2% discount from the invoice price for payment within 10 days of delivery. In all of their prior transactions and without objection from the seller, the buyer took 15 days to pay and deducted 5% from the invoice price. The seller and the buyer recently entered into a contract for a single delivery of wheat at a price of $300,000. The same delivery procedure and invoice were used for this contract as had been used previously. The seller delivered the wheat and the buyer then signed the invoice. On the third day after delivery, the buyer received the following note from the seller: \"Payment in full in accordance with signed invoice is due immediately. No discounts permitted. s/Seller.\" Which of the following statements concerning these facts is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The custom of the trade controls, and the buyer is entitled to take a 2% discount if he pays within 10 days.","B":"The parties' course of dealing controls, and the buyer is entitled to take a 5% discount if he pays within 15 days.","C":"The seller's retraction of his prior waiver controls, and the buyer is entitled to no discount.","D":"The written contract controls, and the buyer is entitled to no discount because of the parol evidence rule."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Course of performance, course of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding agreement performance between parties if:\n\n• agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by one party AND\n\n• other party accepts performance without objection Course of Sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between parties dealing that establishes common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n\nTrade usage Practice or method of dealing in parties' business or industry that is observed with enough regularity to justify expectation that it will be observed in instant case\n\nUnder the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of a prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But evidence of the parties' course of performance, the parties' course of dealing, or trade usage can always be used to explain or supplement those terms. If the evidence is inconsistent, priority is given as follows:\n• Express terms prevail over all others\n• Course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage\n• Course of dealing prevails over trade usage Here, the contract at issue was silent regarding discounts for prompt payment. However, in all of their hundreds of prior transactions and without objection from the seller, the buyer had taken 15 days to pay and deducted 5% from the invoice price. This established a course of dealing that can be used to supplement the terms of the contract (Choice D). And although the custom of the trade is to allow a 2% discount for payment within 10 days, the parties' course of dealing controls (Choice A). Therefore, the buyer is entitled to a 5% discount if he pays within 15 days. (Choice C) Waiver (or retraction thereof) is not an issue in this question because the contract was silent regarding discounts for prompt payment. In other words, there was no relevant term capable of being waived.\n\nEducational objective: Express contractual terms prevail over all others, the parties' course of performance prevails over their course of dealing and trade usage, and their course of dealing prevails over trade usage. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"Express contractual terms prevail over all others, the parties' course of performance prevails over their course of dealing and trade usage, and their course of dealing prevails over trade usage. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","rule":"Express contractual terms prevail over all others, the parties' course of performance prevails over their course of dealing and trade usage, and their course of dealing prevails over trade usage. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"A seller and a buyer have dealt with each other in hundreds of separate grain contracts over the last five years. In performing each contract, the seller delivered the grain to the buyer and, upon delivery, the buyer signed an invoice that showed an agreed-upon price for that delivery. Each invoice was silent in regard to any discount from the price for prompt payment. The custom of the grain trade is to allow a 2% discount from the invoice price for payment within 10 days of delivery. In all of their prior transactions and without objection from the seller, the buyer took 15 days to pay and deducted 5% from the invoice price. The seller and the buyer recently entered into a contract for a single delivery of wheat at a price of $300,000. The same delivery procedure and invoice were used for this contract as had been used previously. The seller delivered the wheat and the buyer then signed the invoice. On the third day after delivery, the buyer received the following note from the seller: \"Payment in full in accordance with signed invoice is due immediately. No discounts permitted. s/Seller.\" Which of the following statements concerning these facts is most accurate?","rule":"Express contractual terms prevail over all others, the parties' course of performance prevails over their course of dealing and trade usage, and their course of dealing prevails over trade usage. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Course of performance, course of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding agreement performance between parties if:\n\n• agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by one party AND\n\n• other party accepts performance without objection Course of Sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between parties dealing that establishes common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n\nTrade usage Practice or method of dealing in parties' business or industry that is observed with enough regularity to justify expectation that it will be observed in instant case\n\nUnder the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of a prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But evidence of the parties' course of performance, the parties' course of dealing, or trade usage can always be used to explain or supplement those terms. If the evidence is inconsistent, priority is given as follows:\n• Express terms prevail over all others\n• Course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage\n• Course of dealing prevails over trade usage Here, the contract at issue was silent regarding discounts for prompt payment. However, in all of their hundreds of prior transactions and without objection from the seller, the buyer had taken 15 days to pay and deducted 5% from the invoice price. This established a course of dealing that can be used to supplement the terms of the contract (Choice D). And although the custom of the trade is to allow a 2% discount for payment within 10 days, the parties' course of dealing controls (Choice A). Therefore, the buyer is entitled to a 5% discount if he pays within 15 days. (Choice C) Waiver (or retraction thereof) is not an issue in this question because the contract was silent regarding discounts for prompt payment. In other words, there was no relevant term capable of being waived.","conclusion":"Answer B: The parties' course of dealing controls, and the buyer is entitled to take a 5% discount if he pays within 15 days."},"questionZh":"过去五年里，卖方和买方在数百份单独的谷物合同中进行了交易。在履行每份合同时，卖方将谷物交付给买方，交货后，买方签署了一份发票，其中显示了该次交货的商定价格。每张发票都没有提及即时付款价格的任何折扣。粮食贸易的惯例是，交货后10天内付款可在发票价格的基础上享受2%的折扣。在之前的所有交易中，在没有卖方反对的情况下，买方花了15天的时间付款，并从发票价格中扣除了5%。卖方和买方最近签订了一份小麦单次交割合同，价格为 30 万美元。本合同使用与之前相同的交付程序和发票。卖方交付了小麦，买方随后在发票上签字。交货后第三天，买方收到卖方的以下通知：“应立即按照已签署的发票全额付款。不允许折扣。卖方。”关于这些事实，以下哪项陈述最准确？","choicesZh":{"A":"贸易惯例管制，买方如果在10天内付款，有权享受2%的折扣。","B":"双方交易过程受控制，买方如果在 15 天内付款，有权享受 5% 的折扣。","C":"卖方撤回其先前的放弃控制，买方无权享受任何折扣。","D":"以书面合同为准，并且由于口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），买方无权享受折扣。"},"explanationZh":"表现过程、交易过程和贸易用途\n\n与了解双方协议履行情况相关的行为顺序过程，如果：\n\n• 协议涉及一方重复履行且\n\n• 另一方无异议地接受履行 交易各方之间先前交易的行为顺序过程，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n\n贸易用途 在当事人的业务或行业中进行交易的惯例或方法，具有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在即时案件中得到遵守的预期\n\n根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前协议（或同期口头协议）的证据不能用来与最终书面协议的条款相矛盾。但当事人履约过程、交易过程或贸易惯例的证据始终可以用来解释或补充这些条款。如果证据不一致，优先考虑如下：\n• 明示条款优先于所有其他条款\n• 履行过程优先于交易和贸易使用过程\n• 交易过程优先于贸易用途 此处，争议合同未提及及时付款的折扣。然而，在之前的数百次交易中，在卖家没有提出异议的情况下，买家却花了15天才付款，并从发票价格中扣除了5%。这建立了一个可用于补充合同条款的交易过程（选择 D）。尽管贸易惯例允许 10 天内付款有 2% 的折扣，但双方的交易过程受到控制（选择 A）。因此，买家如果在15天内付款，则有权享受5%的折扣。 （选择 C） 放弃（或撤销）不是本问题的问题，因为合同没有提及及时付款的折扣。换言之，不存在可以豁免的相关条款。\n\n教育目标：明确的合同条款优先于所有其他条款，双方的履约过程优先于其交易过程和贸易惯例，其交易过程优先于贸易惯例。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"明确的合同条款优先于所有其他条款，双方的履行过程优先于其交易过程和贸易惯例，其交易过程优先于贸易惯例。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"过去五年里，卖方和买方在数百份单独的谷物合同中进行了交易。在履行每份合同时，卖方将谷物交付给买方，交货后，买方签署了一份发票，其中显示了该次交货的商定价格。每张发票都没有提及即时付款价格的任何折扣。粮食贸易的惯例是，交货后10天内付款可在发票价格的基础上享受2%的折扣。在之前的所有交易中，在没有卖方反对的情况下，买方花了15天的时间付款，并从发票价格中扣除了5%。卖方和买方最近签订了一份小麦单次交割合同，价格为 30 万美元。本合同使用与之前相同的交付程序和发票。卖方交付了小麦，买方随后在发票上签字。交货后第三天，买方收到卖方的以下通知：“应立即按照已签署的发票全额付款。不允许折扣。卖方。”关于这些事实，以下哪项陈述最准确？","rule":"明确的合同条款优先于所有其他条款，双方的履行过程优先于其交易过程和贸易惯例，其交易过程优先于贸易惯例。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"表现过程、交易过程和贸易用途\n\n与了解双方协议履行情况相关的行为顺序过程，如果：\n\n• 协议涉及一方重复履行且\n\n• 另一方无异议地接受履行 交易各方之间先前交易的行为顺序过程，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n\n贸易用途 在当事人的业务或行业中进行交易的惯例或方法，具有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在即时案件中得到遵守的预期\n\n根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前协议（或同期口头协议）的证据不能用来与最终书面协议的条款相矛盾。但当事人履约过程、交易过程或贸易惯例的证据始终可以用来解释或补充这些条款。如果证据不一致，优先考虑如下：\n• 明示条款优先于所有其他条款\n• 履行过程优先于交易和贸易使用过程\n• 交易过程优先于贸易用途 此处，争议合同未提及及时付款的折扣。然而，在之前的数百次交易中，在卖家没有提出异议的情况下，买家却花了15天才付款，并从发票价格中扣除了5%。这建立了一个可用于补充合同条款的交易过程（选择 D）。尽管贸易惯例允许 10 天内付款有 2% 的折扣，但双方的交易过程受到控制（选择 A）。因此，买家如果在15天内付款，则有权享受5%的折扣。 （选择 C） 放弃（或撤销）不是本问题的问题，因为合同没有提及及时付款的折扣。换言之，不存在可以豁免的相关条款。\n\n教育目标：明确的合同条款优先于所有其他条款，双方的履约过程优先于其交易过程和贸易惯例，其交易过程优先于贸易惯例。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","conclusion":"答案 B：双方交易过程受控制，买方如果在 15 天内付款，有权享受 5% 的折扣。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":36,"sourceQuestionPage":106,"sourceQuestionPages":[106],"sourceExplanationPages":[107,108],"sourceAnswerPages":[107,108],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-037","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A toy company contracted with a manufacturer to produce gel packets used to simulate water in its faux aquarium toy. On May 1, in a writing signed by both parties, the manufacturer agreed to deliver 1 million gel packets for a total price of $1 million to be paid on June 1, the date of delivery. The writing specified that any modification of its terms required the toy company's signed, written consent. On May 15, the manufacturer called the toy company to explain that it was running low on raw material and would need more time to produce the desired quantity of gel packets. The parties agreed in the telephone conversation to postpone the delivery date to July 1. On July 1, the manufacturer delivered the gel packets to the toy company, which refused to accept or pay for them. Is the manufacturer likely to prevail in a breach-of-contract suit against the toy company?","choices":{"A":"No, because any modification to the parties' agreement required the toy company's signed, written consent.","B":"No, because there was no consideration to support the May 15 agreement.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer reasonably relied on the toy company's having waived the signed-writing requirement.","D":"Yes, because the May 15 agreement was enforceable under the statute of frauds."},"answer":"C","explanation":"\"No oral modification\" (NOM) clause\n\nCommon law\n• NOM clause enforceable only if modification must be in writing under statute of frauds (contracts for services or real estate)\n\nUCC\n• All parties are merchants – NOM clause always enforceable\n• > 1 party is nonmerchant – NOM clause in merchant's form (contracts for sale of must be separately signed by nonmerchant(s) to be goods) enforceable UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nThe UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more (eg, 1 million gel packets for $1 million) and renders them unenforceable unless they are in a writing that:\n• provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed\n• states the quantity of goods to be sold and\n• is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. Modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies this statute generally need not be in writing because the enforceability of the initial contract passes to any modifications. However, the UCC will enforce a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause that requires modifications to be in writing. A NOM clause can be waived by the parties through words or conduct, and the waiver cannot be retracted if a party has materially changed its position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. Here, the parties' May 1 contract fell within and satisfied the statute of frauds. As a result, their May 15 oral agreement did not violate this statute (Choice D). Although the contract contained a NOM clause that prohibited modifications without the toy company's written consent, the toy company waived the clause on May 15 by orally agreeing to postpone the delivery date to July 1 (Choice A). And since the manufacturer materially and reasonably relied on the waiver by delivering the gel packets on that date, it will likely prevail. (Choice B) Consideration is not required to modify a contract for the sale of goods, but any modification must be sought in good faith and comply with reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC will enforce a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause even if the modification would not otherwise need to be in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds. NOM clauses can be waived through the parties' words or conduct, and the waiver cannot be retracted if a party has materially changed position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (formal requirements for the statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (modification, rescission, and waiver).","educationalObjective":"The UCC will enforce a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause even if the modification would not otherwise need to be in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds. NOM clauses can be waived through the parties' words or conduct, and the waiver cannot be retracted if a party has materially changed position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (formal requirements for the statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (modification, rescission, and waiver).","rule":"The UCC will enforce a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause even if the modification would not otherwise need to be in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds. NOM clauses can be waived through the parties' words or conduct, and the waiver cannot be retracted if a party has materially changed position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"Is the manufacturer likely to prevail in a breach-of-contract suit against the toy company?","rule":"The UCC will enforce a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause even if the modification would not otherwise need to be in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds. NOM clauses can be waived through the parties' words or conduct, and the waiver cannot be retracted if a party has materially changed position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"\"No oral modification\" (NOM) clause\n\nCommon law\n• NOM clause enforceable only if modification must be in writing under statute of frauds (contracts for services or real estate)\n\nUCC\n• All parties are merchants – NOM clause always enforceable\n• > 1 party is nonmerchant – NOM clause in merchant's form (contracts for sale of must be separately signed by nonmerchant(s) to be goods) enforceable UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nThe UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more (eg, 1 million gel packets for $1 million) and renders them unenforceable unless they are in a writing that:\n• provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed\n• states the quantity of goods to be sold and\n• is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. Modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies this statute generally need not be in writing because the enforceability of the initial contract passes to any modifications. However, the UCC will enforce a \"no oral modification\" (NOM) clause that requires modifications to be in writing. A NOM clause can be waived by the parties through words or conduct, and the waiver cannot be retracted if a party has materially changed its position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. Here, the parties' May 1 contract fell within and satisfied the statute of frauds. As a result, their May 15 oral agreement did not violate this statute (Choice D). Although the contract contained a NOM clause that prohibited modifications without the toy company's written consent, the toy company waived the clause on May 15 by orally agreeing to postpone the delivery date to July 1 (Choice A). And since the manufacturer materially and reasonably relied on the waiver by delivering the gel packets on that date, it will likely prevail. (Choice B) Consideration is not required to modify a contract for the sale of goods, but any modification must be sought in good faith and comply with reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the manufacturer reasonably relied on the toy company's having waived the signed-writing requirement."},"questionZh":"一家玩具公司与一家制造商签订合同，生产用于在其人造水族箱玩具中模拟水的凝胶包。 5 月 1 日，在双方签署的一份书面文件中，制造商同意交付 100 万包凝胶，总价为 100 万美元，并于 6 月 1 日（即交付日期）支付。该书面文件指出，对其条款的任何修改都需要玩具公司签署的书面同意。 5 月 15 日，制造商致电玩具公司，解释其原材料不足，需要更多时间来生产所需数量的凝胶包。双方在电话交谈中同意将交货日期推迟到7月1日。7月1日，制造商将凝胶包交付给玩具公司，但玩具公司拒绝接受或付款。制造商在针对玩具公司的违约诉讼中是否有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为对双方协议的任何修改都需要玩具公司签署的书面同意。","B":"不，因为没有约因支持5月15日的协议。","C":"是的，因为制造商合理地相信玩具公司已放弃签署书面要求。","D":"是的，因为 5 月 15 日的协议可以根据欺诈法强制执行。"},"explanationZh":"“禁止口头修改”(NOM) 条款\n\n普通法\n• 仅当修改必须根据防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（服务或房地产合同）以书面形式进行时，NOM 条款才可执行\n\nUCC\n• 各方都是商家 – NOM 条款始终可执行\n• > 1 方是非商户 – 商户形式的 NOM 条款（销售合同必须由非商户单独签署才能成为货物）可执行 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\nUCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（例如，价值 100 万美元的 100 万个凝胶包），并且使这些合同无法执行，除非合同采用以下书面形式：\n• 提供合理的基础以相信合同已成立\n• 说明要出售的商品数量以及\n• 由被寻求执行的一方签署。对属于并满足本法规的合同的修改通常不需要以书面形式进行，因为初始合同的可执行性会转移到任何修改。然而，UCC 将强制执行“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，要求修改必须以书面形式进行。双方可以通过言语或行为放弃 NOM 条款，并且如果一方合理依赖该放弃而实质性改变其立场，则该放弃不能撤销。在此，双方 5 月 1 日签订的合同符合并满足欺诈法的规定。因此，他们 5 月 15 日的口头协议并没有违反该法规（选项 D）。尽管合同中包含禁止未经玩具公司书面同意进行修改的NOM条款，但玩具公司于5月15日口头同意将交货日期推迟至7月1日，从而放弃了该条款（选项A）。由于制造商在该日期交付凝胶包，实质上且合理地依赖于豁免，因此它很可能会占上风。 （选择B）修改货物销售合同不需要约因对价，但任何修改都必须善意地寻求并符合公平交易的合理商业标准。\n\n教育目标：UCC 将执行“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，即使修改不需要以书面形式进行以满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。 NOM 条款可以通过双方的言语或行为予以放弃，并且如果一方在合理依赖放弃的情况下实质性改变了立场，则该放弃不能撤销。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的正式要求）。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（修改、撤销和弃权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 将强制执行“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，即使修改不需要以书面形式进行以满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。 NOM 条款可以通过双方的言语或行为予以放弃，并且如果一方在合理依赖放弃的情况下实质性改变了立场，则该放弃不能撤销。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"制造商在针对玩具公司的违约诉讼中是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"UCC 将强制执行“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，即使修改不需要以书面形式进行以满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。 NOM 条款可以通过双方的言语或行为予以放弃，并且如果一方在合理依赖放弃的情况下实质性改变了立场，则该放弃不能撤销。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"“禁止口头修改”(NOM) 条款\n\n普通法\n• 仅当修改必须根据防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（服务或房地产合同）以书面形式进行时，NOM 条款才可执行\n\nUCC\n• 各方都是商家 – NOM 条款始终可执行\n• > 1 方是非商户 – 商户形式的 NOM 条款（销售合同必须由非商户单独签署才能成为货物）可执行 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\nUCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（例如，价值 100 万美元的 100 万个凝胶包），并且使这些合同无法执行，除非合同采用以下书面形式：\n• 提供合理的基础以相信合同已成立\n• 说明要出售的商品数量以及\n• 由被寻求执行的一方签署。对属于并满足本法规的合同的修改通常不需要以书面形式进行，因为初始合同的可执行性会转移到任何修改。然而，UCC 将强制执行“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，要求修改必须以书面形式进行。双方可以通过言语或行为放弃 NOM 条款，并且如果一方合理依赖该放弃而实质性改变其立场，则该放弃不能撤销。在此，双方 5 月 1 日签订的合同符合并满足欺诈法的规定。因此，他们 5 月 15 日的口头协议并没有违反该法规（选项 D）。尽管合同中包含禁止未经玩具公司书面同意进行修改的NOM条款，但玩具公司于5月15日口头同意将交货日期推迟至7月1日，从而放弃了该条款（选项A）。由于制造商在该日期交付凝胶包，实质上且合理地依赖于豁免，因此它很可能会占上风。 （选择B）修改货物销售合同不需要约因对价，但任何修改都必须善意地寻求并符合公平交易的合理商业标准。\n\n教育目标：UCC 将执行“不得口头修改”(NOM) 条款，即使修改不需要以书面形式进行以满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。 NOM 条款可以通过双方的言语或行为予以放弃，并且如果一方在合理依赖放弃的情况下实质性改变了立场，则该放弃不能撤销。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的正式要求）。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（修改、撤销和弃权）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为制造商合理地相信玩具公司已放弃签署书面要求。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":37,"sourceQuestionPage":109,"sourceQuestionPages":[109],"sourceExplanationPages":[110,111],"sourceAnswerPages":[110,111],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-038","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A woman owned a large ranch, which she orally offered to sell to her stable manager for $250,000. The stable manager eagerly accepted her offer, and the woman wrote and gave him an unsigned note that read, \"I agree to sell you my ranch for $250,000.\" The stable manager gave the woman a small down payment to seal the deal and then took possession of the ranch with the woman's permission. Shortly thereafter, the stable manager received a letter from the woman stating that another buyer had offered to buy the ranch for $425,000 and that she would not sell it to the stable manager unless he buys it for that price. If the stable manager sues the woman for specific performance, which of the following concepts would best support his claim?","choices":{"A":"Anticipatory breach.","B":"Good faith and fair dealing.","C":"Part performance.","D":"Quantum meruit."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of land (eg, the ranch). It requires that the contract (1) be in writing, (2) be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and (3) contain all essential terms. Therefore, the woman and stable manager's oral agreement and the woman's unsigned note each violated this statute. However, a contract for the sale of land that violates this statute may still be enforceable if the part-performance exception applies. This requires proof that the buyer has done at least two of the following:\n• Taken possession of the property (as the stable manager did here)\n• Made substantial improvements to the property (not seen here)\n• Paid some or all of the purchase price (here, the stable manager made a small down payment) The buyer can then bring an action in equity for specific performance. However, part performance will not support an ordinary action at law for money damages. (Choice A) Anticipatory breach* occurs when one party communicates to the other party that it will not perform under the contract. The other party can treat this as a breach and sue immediately or demand performance. Here, although the woman told the stable manager that she would not sell him the ranch for the agreed-upon price, this would not overcome the statute of frauds. *This is also called anticipatory repudiation. (Choice B) The duty of good faith and fair dealing implied in every contract requires that neither party interfere with the other party's performance of the contract or right to receive the benefits thereof. But the stable manager cannot assert a breach of this duty without first proving that an enforceable contract exists. (Choice D) Quantum meruit entitles a party who has conferred a benefit on another in a quasi-contractual relationship to damages for the reasonable value of the benefit, but not specific performance.\n\nEducational objective: A contract involving land that violates the statute of frauds is enforceable on part- performance grounds if the buyer has done at least two of the following: taken possession of the property, substantially improved it, and/or paid any portion of the purchase price.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 129 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (action in reliance; specific performance).","educationalObjective":"A contract involving land that violates the statute of frauds is enforceable on part- performance grounds if the buyer has done at least two of the following: taken possession of the property, substantially improved it, and/or paid any portion of the purchase price.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 129 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (action in reliance; specific performance).","rule":"A contract involving land that violates the statute of frauds is enforceable on part- performance grounds if the buyer has done at least two of the following: taken possession of the property, substantially improved it, and/or paid any portion of the purchase price.","irac":{"issue":"If the stable manager sues the woman for specific performance, which of the following concepts would best support his claim?","rule":"A contract involving land that violates the statute of frauds is enforceable on part- performance grounds if the buyer has done at least two of the following: taken possession of the property, substantially improved it, and/or paid any portion of the purchase price.","analysis":"The statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of land (eg, the ranch). It requires that the contract (1) be in writing, (2) be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought, and (3) contain all essential terms. Therefore, the woman and stable manager's oral agreement and the woman's unsigned note each violated this statute. However, a contract for the sale of land that violates this statute may still be enforceable if the part-performance exception applies. This requires proof that the buyer has done at least two of the following:\n• Taken possession of the property (as the stable manager did here)\n• Made substantial improvements to the property (not seen here)\n• Paid some or all of the purchase price (here, the stable manager made a small down payment) The buyer can then bring an action in equity for specific performance. However, part performance will not support an ordinary action at law for money damages. (Choice A) Anticipatory breach* occurs when one party communicates to the other party that it will not perform under the contract. The other party can treat this as a breach and sue immediately or demand performance. Here, although the woman told the stable manager that she would not sell him the ranch for the agreed-upon price, this would not overcome the statute of frauds. *This is also called anticipatory repudiation. (Choice B) The duty of good faith and fair dealing implied in every contract requires that neither party interfere with the other party's performance of the contract or right to receive the benefits thereof. But the stable manager cannot assert a breach of this duty without first proving that an enforceable contract exists. (Choice D) Quantum meruit entitles a party who has conferred a benefit on another in a quasi-contractual relationship to damages for the reasonable value of the benefit, but not specific performance.","conclusion":"Answer C: Part performance."},"questionZh":"一位女士拥有一座大牧场，她口头提出以 25 万美元的价格将其出售给她的马厩经理。马厩经理热切地接受了她的提议，这位女士写了一张未签名的便条给了他，上面写着：“我同意以 25 万美元的价格卖给你我的牧场。”马厩经理给了这位女士一小笔首付以达成交易，然后在这位女士的许可下接管了牧场。此后不久，马厩经理收到了该女子的一封信，信中称另一位买家提出以 425,000 美元的价格购买该牧场，并且她不会将其出售给马厩经理，除非他以这个价格购买。如果马厩经理因特定表现而起诉该妇女，以下哪个概念最能支持他的主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"预期违约。","B":"诚信、公平交易。","C":"部分履行。","D":"量子功德。"},"explanationZh":"欺诈法适用于土地（例如牧场）销售合同。它要求合同 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 由寻求执行的一方签署，并且 (3) 包含所有基本条款。因此，该女子与马厩经理的口头协议以及该女子未签名的便条均违反了该法规。然而，如果部分履约例外适用，违反本法规的土地出售合同仍然可以强制执行。这需要证明买方已完成以下至少两项：\n• 占有财产（正如马厩经理在这里所做的那样）\n• 对财产进行了重大改进（此处未看到）\n• 支付了部分或全部购买价格（此处，马厩管理人支付了少量首付款），然后买方可以针对特定绩效提起股权诉讼。然而，部分履行不支持针对金钱赔偿的普通法律诉讼。 （选择 A）当一方告知另一方其不会履行合同时，就会发生预期违约*。另一方可以将此视为违约并立即提起诉讼或要求履行。在这里，尽管该妇女告诉马厩经理，她不会以约定的价格将牧场卖给他，但这并不能克服欺诈法。 *这也称为预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。 （选项B） 每份合同都隐含着诚实信用和公平交易的义务，要求任何一方不得干涉另一方履行合同或获得合同利益的权利。但是，在没有首先证明存在可执行合同的情况下，马厩管理者不能声称违反了这项义务。 （选项D）量子功绩使在准合同关系中向另一方授予利益的一方有权就该利益的合理价值而非具体履行获得损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：如果买方已完成以下至少两项行为，则涉及违反防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的土地的合同可以部分履行为由强制执行：占有该财产，对其进行实质性改进，和/或支付了购买价格的任何部分。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 129（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（信赖行为；具体履行）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果买方已完成以下至少两项行为，则涉及违反欺诈法的土地的合同可以部分履行为由强制执行：占有该财产、对其进行实质性改进和/或支付了购买价格的任何部分。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果马厩经理因特定表现而起诉该妇女，以下哪个概念最能支持他的主张？","rule":"如果买方已完成以下至少两项行为，则涉及违反欺诈法的土地的合同可以部分履行为由强制执行：占有该财产、对其进行实质性改进和/或支付了购买价格的任何部分。","analysis":"欺诈法适用于土地（例如牧场）销售合同。它要求合同 (1) 采用书面形式，(2) 由寻求执行的一方签署，并且 (3) 包含所有基本条款。因此，该女子与马厩经理的口头协议以及该女子未签名的便条均违反了该法规。然而，如果部分履约例外适用，违反本法规的土地出售合同仍然可以强制执行。这需要证明买方已完成以下至少两项：\n• 占有财产（正如马厩经理在这里所做的那样）\n• 对财产进行了重大改进（此处未看到）\n• 支付了部分或全部购买价格（此处，马厩管理人支付了少量首付款），然后买方可以针对特定绩效提起股权诉讼。然而，部分履行不支持针对金钱赔偿的普通法律诉讼。 （选择 A）当一方告知另一方其不会履行合同时，就会发生预期违约*。另一方可以将此视为违约并立即提起诉讼或要求履行。在这里，尽管该妇女告诉马厩经理，她不会以约定的价格将牧场卖给他，但这并不能克服欺诈法。 *这也称为预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。 （选项B） 每份合同都隐含着诚实信用和公平交易的义务，要求任何一方不得干涉另一方履行合同或获得合同利益的权利。但是，在没有首先证明存在可执行合同的情况下，马厩管理者不能声称违反了这项义务。 （选项D）量子功绩使在准合同关系中向另一方授予利益的一方有权就该利益的合理价值而非具体履行获得损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：如果买方已完成以下至少两项行为，则涉及违反防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的土地的合同可以部分履行为由强制执行：占有该财产，对其进行实质性改进，和/或支付了购买价格的任何部分。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 129（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（信赖行为；具体履行）。","conclusion":"答案 C：部分履行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"anticipatory repudiation","ipa":"/æntˈɪsʌpʌtˌɔɹi ɹɪpjˌudiˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"an·tic·i·pa·tory re·pu·di·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"预期违约：履行期前明确且无保留地表示不履行","contextualMeaning":"预期违约：履行期前明确且无保留地表示不履行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"*This is also called anticipatory repudiation.","synonyms":["anticipatory breach","prospective repudiation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-e5ddaafac7df99a4"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":38,"sourceQuestionPage":112,"sourceQuestionPages":[112],"sourceExplanationPages":[113,114],"sourceAnswerPages":[113,114],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-039","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A rancher and a fancier of horses signed the following writing: \"For $5,000, the rancher will sell to the fancier a gray horse that the fancier may choose from among the grays on the rancher's ranch.\" The fancier refused to accept delivery of a gray horse timely tendered by the rancher or to choose among those remaining, on the grounds that during their negotiations the rancher had orally agreed to include a saddle, worth $100, and also to give the fancier the option to choose a gray or a brown horse. The fancier insisted on one of the rancher's brown horses, but the rancher refused to part with any of his browns or with the saddle as demanded by the fancier. If the fancier sues the rancher for damages and seeks to introduce evidence of the alleged oral agreement, what evidence will the court likely admit?","choices":{"A":"Both the saddle and the option to choose a brown horse.","B":"The saddle, but not the option to choose a brown horse.","C":"The option to choose a brown horse, but not the saddle.","D":"Neither the saddle nor the option to choose a brown horse."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Admissibility of consistent additional terms\n\n(not barred by parol evidence rule)\n\nCommon Term would naturally be omitted from contract under the circumstances law\n\nUCC Term not so important that, if agreed upon, it certainly would have been included in contract\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nThe UCC parol evidence rule* generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the final written contract. But evidence of a consistent additional term is admissible unless a court determines the term is so important that it would have certainly been included in the contract. Because that standard is difficult to establish, such evidence is usually admissible. Here, the written contract signed by the parties allowed the fancier to purchase and select a gray horse. As a result, evidence of the oral agreement to allow the fancier to purchase and select a brown horse contradicted the contract and is not admissible (Choices A & C). But because the term to include a $100 saddle would not have certainly been included in the contract, evidence of that part of the oral agreement is admissible (Choice D). *Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, horses) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC).\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of consistent additional terms is admissible unless the court determines the term is so important that, if agreed upon, it would have certainly been included in the contract.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (when parol evidence of consistent additional term is admissible).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of consistent additional terms is admissible unless the court determines the term is so important that, if agreed upon, it would have certainly been included in the contract.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (when parol evidence of consistent additional term is admissible).","rule":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of consistent additional terms is admissible unless the court determines the term is so important that, if agreed upon, it would have certainly been included in the contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the fancier sues the rancher for damages and seeks to introduce evidence of the alleged oral agreement, what evidence will the court likely admit?","rule":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of consistent additional terms is admissible unless the court determines the term is so important that, if agreed upon, it would have certainly been included in the contract.","analysis":"Admissibility of consistent additional terms\n\n(not barred by parol evidence rule)\n\nCommon Term would naturally be omitted from contract under the circumstances law\n\nUCC Term not so important that, if agreed upon, it certainly would have been included in contract\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nThe UCC parol evidence rule* generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the final written contract. But evidence of a consistent additional term is admissible unless a court determines the term is so important that it would have certainly been included in the contract. Because that standard is difficult to establish, such evidence is usually admissible. Here, the written contract signed by the parties allowed the fancier to purchase and select a gray horse. As a result, evidence of the oral agreement to allow the fancier to purchase and select a brown horse contradicted the contract and is not admissible (Choices A & C). But because the term to include a $100 saddle would not have certainly been included in the contract, evidence of that part of the oral agreement is admissible (Choice D). *Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, horses) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC).","conclusion":"Answer B: The saddle, but not the option to choose a brown horse."},"questionZh":"一位牧场主和一位马匹爱好者签署了这样的文字：“牧场主将以 5,000 美元的价格向马迷出售一匹灰马，爱好者可以从牧场主牧场的灰马中选择。”鸽友拒绝接受牧场主及时交付的一匹灰马，也拒绝在剩下的马中进行选择，理由是在谈判过程中牧场主口头同意提供价值 100 美元的马鞍，并让鸽友可以选择灰马或棕马。鸽友坚持要牧场主的一匹棕色马，但牧场主拒绝按照鸽友的要求放弃他的任何一匹棕色马或马鞍。如果鸽友起诉牧场主要求赔偿并寻求提供所谓口头协议的证据，法院可能会接受哪些证据？","choicesZh":{"A":"马鞍和选择棕色马的选项。","B":"马鞍，但不是选择棕色马的选项。","C":"可以选择棕色的马，但不选择马鞍。","D":"既没有马鞍也没有选择棕色马的选项。"},"explanationZh":"一致附加条款的可接受性\n\n（不受口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的禁止）\n\n根据情况法，通用条款自然会从合同中省略\n\nUCC 条款并不重要，如果达成一致，它肯定会包含在合同中\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\nUCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）* 通常禁止提供与最终书面合同相矛盾的先前或同期协议的证据。但一致的附加条款的证据是可以接受的，除非法院确定该条款非常重要以至于肯定会包含在合同中。由于该标准很难确定，因此此类证据通常是可以接受的。在这里，双方签署的书面合同允许鸽友购买和选择一匹灰马。因此，允许鸽友购买和选择棕色马的口头协议的证据与合同相矛盾，因此不予受理（选择 A 和 C）。但由于包含 100 美元马鞍的条款肯定不会包含在合同中，因此口头协议中该部分的证据是可接受的（选项 D）。 *货物（例如马）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），一致的附加条款的证据是可接受的，除非法院确定该条款非常重要，以至于如果达成一致，它肯定会包含在合同中。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（当一致附加条款的口头证据可接受时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），一致的附加条款的证据是可接受的，除非法院确定该条款非常重要，如果达成一致，它肯定会包含在合同中。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果鸽友起诉牧场主要求赔偿并寻求提供所谓口头协议的证据，法院可能会接受哪些证据？","rule":"根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），一致的附加条款的证据是可接受的，除非法院确定该条款非常重要，如果达成一致，它肯定会包含在合同中。","analysis":"一致附加条款的可接受性\n\n（不受口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的禁止）\n\n根据情况法，通用条款自然会从合同中省略\n\nUCC 条款并不重要，如果达成一致，它肯定会包含在合同中\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\nUCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）* 通常禁止提供与最终书面合同相矛盾的先前或同期协议的证据。但一致的附加条款的证据是可以接受的，除非法院确定该条款非常重要以至于肯定会包含在合同中。由于该标准很难确定，因此此类证据通常是可以接受的。在这里，双方签署的书面合同允许鸽友购买和选择一匹灰马。因此，允许鸽友购买和选择棕色马的口头协议的证据与合同相矛盾，因此不予受理（选择 A 和 C）。但由于包含 100 美元马鞍的条款肯定不会包含在合同中，因此口头协议中该部分的证据是可接受的（选项 D）。 *货物（例如马）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），一致的附加条款的证据是可接受的，除非法院确定该条款非常重要，以至于如果达成一致，它肯定会包含在合同中。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（当一致附加条款的口头证据可接受时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：马鞍，但不是选择棕色马的选项。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":39,"sourceQuestionPage":115,"sourceQuestionPages":[115],"sourceExplanationPages":[116],"sourceAnswerPages":[116],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-040","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A developer contracted in writing with a buyer to sell a house on a one-acre lot for $100,000. The developer told the buyer that the lot abutted a national park and that the water for the house came from a natural artesian spring. The developer knew that both these representations were important to the buyer and that both were false. The buyer moved into the house and eight months later learned that a private golf course was being constructed on the adjacent land and that the water for the house came from the city reservoir. The buyer immediately sued the developer to avoid the contract. The construction of the golf course will probably increase the market value of the buyer's property, and the water from the city reservoir exceeds all established standards for drinking water. Is the buyer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because eight months exceeds a reasonable time for contract avoidance.","B":"No, because the developer's misstatements caused no economic harm to the buyer.","C":"Yes, because the buyer retained the power to avoid the contract due to fraud.","D":"Yes, because the contract was void ab initio."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Factors used to determine reasonable time to avoid contract\n\nExtent to which:\n\n• delay enabled (or might have enabled) party with power of avoidance to speculate at other party's risk\n• delay resulted (or might have resulted) in justifiable reliance by other party or third party\n• ground for avoidance was result of either party's fault\n• other party's conduct contributed to delay Misrepresentation is a defense to enforceability that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party. This defense is available when:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nIf the misrepresentation was fraudulent, then the adversely affected party must seek to avoid the contract within a reasonable time after learning of the misrepresentation.* Otherwise, the power to avoid the contract is lost. Here, the developer fraudulently misrepresented both the location of the lot and the water source, which induced the buyer to purchase the house. Because the developer's statements were not obviously false, the buyer justifiably relied on them. And since the buyer immediately sued the developer to avoid the contract after discovering the misrepresentation eight months later, the buyer retained the power to avoid the contract and will likely prevail (Choice A). *If the misrepresentation was nonfraudulent, then the reasonable time period begins to run when the party knows or has reason to know about the misrepresentation. (Choice B) A party may avoid a contract for misrepresentation even if the resulting contract causes that party no economic harm. (Choice D) The contract was not void ab initio (ie, invalid from the outset). Rather, it was voidable, meaning that it operated as a valid contract until the buyer took steps to avoid it.\n\nEducational objective: To avoid a contract for fraudulent misrepresentation, the adversely affected party must seek this remedy within a reasonable time after learning of the misrepresentation.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 381 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when power to avoid contract is lost due to delay).","educationalObjective":"To avoid a contract for fraudulent misrepresentation, the adversely affected party must seek this remedy within a reasonable time after learning of the misrepresentation.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 381 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when power to avoid contract is lost due to delay).","rule":"To avoid a contract for fraudulent misrepresentation, the adversely affected party must seek this remedy within a reasonable time after learning of the misrepresentation.","irac":{"issue":"Is the buyer likely to prevail?","rule":"To avoid a contract for fraudulent misrepresentation, the adversely affected party must seek this remedy within a reasonable time after learning of the misrepresentation.","analysis":"Factors used to determine reasonable time to avoid contract\n\nExtent to which:\n\n• delay enabled (or might have enabled) party with power of avoidance to speculate at other party's risk\n• delay resulted (or might have resulted) in justifiable reliance by other party or third party\n• ground for avoidance was result of either party's fault\n• other party's conduct contributed to delay Misrepresentation is a defense to enforceability that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party. This defense is available when:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nIf the misrepresentation was fraudulent, then the adversely affected party must seek to avoid the contract within a reasonable time after learning of the misrepresentation.* Otherwise, the power to avoid the contract is lost. Here, the developer fraudulently misrepresented both the location of the lot and the water source, which induced the buyer to purchase the house. Because the developer's statements were not obviously false, the buyer justifiably relied on them. And since the buyer immediately sued the developer to avoid the contract after discovering the misrepresentation eight months later, the buyer retained the power to avoid the contract and will likely prevail (Choice A). *If the misrepresentation was nonfraudulent, then the reasonable time period begins to run when the party knows or has reason to know about the misrepresentation. (Choice B) A party may avoid a contract for misrepresentation even if the resulting contract causes that party no economic harm. (Choice D) The contract was not void ab initio (ie, invalid from the outset). Rather, it was voidable, meaning that it operated as a valid contract until the buyer took steps to avoid it.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the buyer retained the power to avoid the contract due to fraud."},"questionZh":"一位开发商与一位买家签订了书面合同，以 10 万美元的价格出售一英亩土地上的房屋。开发商告诉买家，该地块毗邻国家公园，房屋的水来自天然自流泉。开发商知道这两种陈述对买方都很重要，而且都是错误的。买家搬进了这栋房子，八个月后才得知附近的土地上正在建设一个私人高尔夫球场，而房子的水来自城市水库。买方立即起诉开发商，要求撤销合同。高尔夫球场的建设可能会增加买方房产的市场价值，而城市水库的水超过了所有既定的饮用水标准。买方有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为八个月超出了合同无效的合理时间。","B":"不会，因为开发商的错误陈述没有给买方造成经济损失。","C":"是的，因为买方保留因欺诈而宣告合同无效的权力。","D":"是的，因为合同从一开始就无效。"},"explanationZh":"用于确定合理宣告合同无效时间的因素\n\n程度：\n\n• 延迟使（或可能使）具有回避权的一方推测另一方的风险\n• 延迟导致（或可能导致）其他方或第三方的合理依赖\n• 回避理由是任何一方的过错造成的\n• 另一方的行为导致了延误 虚假陈述是对可执行性的一种辩护，使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同。这种防御在以下情况下可用：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n如果虚假陈述具有欺诈性，那么受到不利影响的一方必须在得知虚假陈述后的合理时间内寻求撤销合同。*否则，将丧失撤销合同的权力。在此案中，开发商欺骗性地谎报了地块位置和水源，诱使买家购买房屋。由于开发商的陈述并无明显虚假之处，买方有理由信赖这些陈述。由于买方在八个月后发现虚假陈述后立即起诉开发商要求撤销合同，因此买方保留了撤销合同的权力，很可能胜诉（选项A）。 *如果失实陈述不是欺诈性的，则合理期限从当事人知道或有理由知道失实陈述时开始计算。 （选择 B）一方当事人可以因虚假陈述而宣告合同无效，即使由此产生的合同没有给该方造成经济损失。 （选项 D） 合同并非从一开始就无效（即从一开始就无效）。相反，它是可撤销的，这意味着它作为有效的合同运作，直到买方采取措施避免它为止。\n\n教育目标：为了避免因欺诈性失实陈述而签订合同，受到不利影响的一方必须在得知失实陈述后的合理时间内寻求补救措施。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 381 cmt。 b (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当因延误而丧失撤销合同的权力时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"为了避免因欺诈性虚假陈述而签订合同，受到不利影响的一方必须在得知虚假陈述后的合理时间内寻求补救措施。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方有可能获胜吗？","rule":"为了避免因欺诈性虚假陈述而签订合同，受到不利影响的一方必须在得知虚假陈述后的合理时间内寻求补救措施。","analysis":"用于确定合理宣告合同无效时间的因素\n\n程度：\n\n• 延迟使（或可能使）具有回避权的一方推测另一方的风险\n• 延迟导致（或可能导致）其他方或第三方的合理依赖\n• 回避理由是任何一方的过错造成的\n• 另一方的行为导致了延误 虚假陈述是对可执行性的一种辩护，使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同。这种防御在以下情况下可用：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n如果虚假陈述具有欺诈性，那么受到不利影响的一方必须在得知虚假陈述后的合理时间内寻求撤销合同。*否则，将丧失撤销合同的权力。在此案中，开发商欺骗性地谎报了地块位置和水源，诱使买家购买房屋。由于开发商的陈述并无明显虚假之处，买方有理由信赖这些陈述。由于买方在八个月后发现虚假陈述后立即起诉开发商要求撤销合同，因此买方保留了撤销合同的权力，很可能胜诉（选项A）。 *如果失实陈述不是欺诈性的，则合理期限从当事人知道或有理由知道失实陈述时开始计算。 （选择 B）一方当事人可以因虚假陈述而宣告合同无效，即使由此产生的合同没有给该方造成经济损失。 （选项 D） 合同并非从一开始就无效（即从一开始就无效）。相反，它是可撤销的，这意味着它作为有效的合同运作，直到买方采取措施避免它为止。\n\n教育目标：为了避免因欺诈性失实陈述而签订合同，受到不利影响的一方必须在得知失实陈述后的合理时间内寻求补救措施。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 381 cmt。 b (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当因延误而丧失撤销合同的权力时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为买方保留因欺诈而宣告合同无效的权力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"misrepresented","ipa":"/mˌɪsɹɛpɹɪzˈɛntɪd/","syllables":"mis·rep·re·sented","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. 故意对…作错误的报道( misrepresent的过去式和过去分词 )","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“misrepresented”","wordForm":"词；原形 misrepresent；词形 0:misrepresent/1:dp/p:misrepresented","sourceSentence":"Here, the developer fraudulently misrepresented both the location of the lot and the water source, which induced the buyer to purchase the house.","synonyms":["belie","fudge","manipulate","fake"]},{"term":"fraudulently","ipa":"/fɹˈɔduləntli/","syllables":"fraud·u·lently","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 欺骗地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“fraudulently”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Here, the developer fraudulently misrepresented both the location of the lot and the water source, which induced the buyer to purchase the house.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":40,"sourceQuestionPage":117,"sourceQuestionPages":[117],"sourceExplanationPages":[118,119],"sourceAnswerPages":[118,119],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-041","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A buyer purchased a new car from a dealer under a written contract that provided that the price of the car was $20,000 and that the buyer would receive a \"trade-in allowance of $7,000 for the buyer's old car.\" The old car had recently been damaged in an accident. The contract contained a merger clause stating: \"This writing constitutes the entire agreement of the parties, and there are no other understandings or agreements not set forth herein.\" When the buyer took possession of the new car, she delivered the old car to the dealer. At that time, the dealer claimed that the trade-in allowance included an assignment of the buyer's claim against her insurance company for damage to the old car. The buyer refused to provide the assignment. The dealer sued the buyer to recover the insurance payment. The dealer has offered evidence that the parties agreed during their negotiations for the new car that the dealer was entitled to the insurance payment. Should the court admit this evidence?","choices":{"A":"No, because the dealer's acceptance of the old car bars any additional claim by the dealer.","B":"No, because the merger clause bars any evidence of the parties' prior discussions concerning the trade-in allowance.","C":"Yes, because a merger clause does not bar evidence of fraud.","D":"Yes, because the merger clause does not bar evidence to explain what the parties meant by \"trade-in allowance.\""},"answer":"D","explanation":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The UCC parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior written agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) to contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But the parol evidence rule does not bar such evidence, and such evidence is always admissible, when it is used to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. This is true regardless of whether the contract is completely or partially integrated. Here, the written contract failed to state whether an assignment of the buyer's insurance claim was included in the term \"trade-in allowance.\" The contract contained a merger clause, which indicates that the contract was completely integrated.* But the merger clause does not bar evidence to explain what the parties meant by \"trade-in allowance.\" Therefore, the court should admit the evidence that the parties agreed during their negotiations for the new car that the dealer was entitled to the insurance payment (Choice B). *A merger clause is strong, but not conclusive, evidence that a contract is completely integrated. (Choice A) A buyer's mere acceptance of goods does not waive its potential claims against a seller. (Choice C) Evidence used to establish a defense to formation or enforceability of a contract (eg, fraud) is excepted from the parol evidence rule. But there is no indication of fraud in this case, so this exception is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Even when a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"Even when a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","rule":"Even when a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Should the court admit this evidence?","rule":"Even when a contract is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause), the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The UCC parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior written agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) to contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But the parol evidence rule does not bar such evidence, and such evidence is always admissible, when it is used to clarify the meaning of an ambiguous contract term. This is true regardless of whether the contract is completely or partially integrated. Here, the written contract failed to state whether an assignment of the buyer's insurance claim was included in the term \"trade-in allowance.\" The contract contained a merger clause, which indicates that the contract was completely integrated.* But the merger clause does not bar evidence to explain what the parties meant by \"trade-in allowance.\" Therefore, the court should admit the evidence that the parties agreed during their negotiations for the new car that the dealer was entitled to the insurance payment (Choice B). *A merger clause is strong, but not conclusive, evidence that a contract is completely integrated. (Choice A) A buyer's mere acceptance of goods does not waive its potential claims against a seller. (Choice C) Evidence used to establish a defense to formation or enforceability of a contract (eg, fraud) is excepted from the parol evidence rule. But there is no indication of fraud in this case, so this exception is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the merger clause does not bar evidence to explain what the parties meant by \"trade-in allowance.\""},"questionZh":"一名买家根据一份书面合同从经销商处购买了一辆新车，合同规定该车的价格为 20,000 美元，并且买家将获得“买家旧车的以旧换新补贴 7,000 美元”。这辆旧车最近在一次事故中受损。该合同包含一项合并条款，其中规定：“本书面文件构成双方的完整协议，除此之外，没有任何其他谅解或协议。”当买家收到新车后，她将旧车交付给经销商。当时，经销商声称，以旧换新津贴包括买方向其保险公司提出的旧车损坏索赔的转让。买方拒绝提供转让。经销商起诉买方，要求收回保险金。经销商提供的证据表明，双方在新车谈判期间同意经销商有权获得保险金。法院应该承认这个证据吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为经销商接受旧车后就禁止经销商提出任何额外索赔。","B":"不可以，因为合并条款禁止提供双方之前就以旧换新补贴进行讨论的任何证据。","C":"是的，因为合并条款并不禁止欺诈证据。","D":"是的，因为合并条款并不禁止提供证据来解释双方所说的“以旧换新补贴”的含义。"},"explanationZh":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供与综合书面条款相矛盾的先前书面协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据。但口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止此类证据，并且当此类证据用于澄清不明确的合同条款的含义时，此类证据始终是可接受的。无论合同是完全集成还是部分集成，都是如此。本案中，书面合同未明确“以旧换新津贴”一词是否包含买方保险索赔的转让。合同中包含合并条款，表明合同已完全整合。*但合并条款并不妨碍提供证据来解释双方“以旧换新”的含义。因此，法院应当采信双方在新车谈判过程中约定经销商有权获得保险金的证据（选项B）。 *合并条款是合同完全整合的有力证据，但不是决定性的。 （选择 A） 买方仅仅接受货物并不放弃其对卖方的潜在索赔。 （选项C）用于对合同的成立或可执行性进行抗辩的证据（例如欺诈）不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。但本案中没有任何欺诈迹象，因此该例外情况无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：即使合同完全整合（例如，通过合并条款证明），口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）也不妨碍提供证据来澄清不明确的合同条款的含义。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"即使合同完全整合（例如，通过合并条款证明），口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）也不妨碍提供证据来澄清不明确的合同条款的含义。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该承认这个证据吗？","rule":"即使合同完全整合（例如，通过合并条款证明），口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）也不妨碍提供证据来澄清不明确的合同条款的含义。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供与综合书面条款相矛盾的先前书面协议（或同时期口头协议）的证据。但口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止此类证据，并且当此类证据用于澄清不明确的合同条款的含义时，此类证据始终是可接受的。无论合同是完全集成还是部分集成，都是如此。本案中，书面合同未明确“以旧换新津贴”一词是否包含买方保险索赔的转让。合同中包含合并条款，表明合同已完全整合。*但合并条款并不妨碍提供证据来解释双方“以旧换新”的含义。因此，法院应当采信双方在新车谈判过程中约定经销商有权获得保险金的证据（选项B）。 *合并条款是合同完全整合的有力证据，但不是决定性的。 （选择 A） 买方仅仅接受货物并不放弃其对卖方的潜在索赔。 （选项C）用于对合同的成立或可执行性进行抗辩的证据（例如欺诈）不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）。但本案中没有任何欺诈迹象，因此该例外情况无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：即使合同完全整合（例如，通过合并条款证明），口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）也不妨碍提供证据来澄清不明确的合同条款的含义。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为合并条款并不禁止提供证据来解释双方所说的“以旧换新补贴”的含义。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"acceptance of goods","ipa":"/æksˈɛptəns ˈəv ɡˈʊdz/","syllables":"ac·cep·tance of goods","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"接受货物：买方以言行确认接收货物或未及时有效拒收","contextualMeaning":"接受货物：买方以言行确认接收货物或未及时有效拒收","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) A buyer's mere acceptance of goods does not waive its potential claims against a seller.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":41,"sourceQuestionPage":120,"sourceQuestionPages":[120],"sourceExplanationPages":[121,122],"sourceAnswerPages":[121,122],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-042","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"On March 1, a homeowner contacted a builder about constructing an addition to the homeowner's house. The builder orally offered to perform the work for $200,000 if his pending bid on another project was rejected. The homeowner accepted the builder's terms, and the builder then prepared a written contract that both parties signed. The contract did not refer to the builder's pending bid. One week later, upon learning that his pending bid on the other project had been accepted, the builder refused to perform any work for the homeowner. Can the homeowner recover for the builder's nonperformance?","choices":{"A":"No, because efficiency principles justify the builder's services being directed to a higher-value use.","B":"No, because the builder's duty to perform was subject to a condition.","C":"Yes, because the builder's attempt to condition his duty to perform rendered the contract illusory.","D":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule would bar the builder from presenting evidence of oral understandings not included in the final writing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But this rule does not apply when a party is attacking the validity of the agreement—eg, by asserting there was a condition precedent to effectiveness that failed to occur. As a result, evidence of such a condition precedent is admissible. Here, before the contract was signed, the parties orally agreed that it would become binding (ie, take effect) only if the builder's other pending bid was rejected—a condition precedent to effectiveness. Therefore, the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of the oral agreement to prove that the contract failed to take effect because the builder's other pending bid was accepted (Choice D). Accordingly, the homeowner cannot recover for the builder's nonperformance because the builder's performance never became due. (Choice A) Efficiency principles justify (but do not excuse) a breach if it is more economically advantageous for a party to breach and pay damages than to perform. But the builder did not breach the contract, and even if he had, the facts do not indicate that it was economically advantageous for him to do so. (Choice C) An illusory promise is one so vague that the promisor has discretion whether to perform. Here, the builder's promise was not illusory because he was obligated to perform if his pending bid was rejected.\n\nEducational objective: Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (integrated agreement subject to oral requirement).","educationalObjective":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (integrated agreement subject to oral requirement).","rule":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","irac":{"issue":"Can the homeowner recover for the builder's nonperformance?","rule":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","analysis":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But this rule does not apply when a party is attacking the validity of the agreement—eg, by asserting there was a condition precedent to effectiveness that failed to occur. As a result, evidence of such a condition precedent is admissible. Here, before the contract was signed, the parties orally agreed that it would become binding (ie, take effect) only if the builder's other pending bid was rejected—a condition precedent to effectiveness. Therefore, the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of the oral agreement to prove that the contract failed to take effect because the builder's other pending bid was accepted (Choice D). Accordingly, the homeowner cannot recover for the builder's nonperformance because the builder's performance never became due. (Choice A) Efficiency principles justify (but do not excuse) a breach if it is more economically advantageous for a party to breach and pay damages than to perform. But the builder did not breach the contract, and even if he had, the facts do not indicate that it was economically advantageous for him to do so. (Choice C) An illusory promise is one so vague that the promisor has discretion whether to perform. Here, the builder's promise was not illusory because he was obligated to perform if his pending bid was rejected.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the builder's duty to perform was subject to a condition."},"questionZh":"3 月 1 日，一位房主联系了一位建筑商，希望为其房屋进行扩建。建筑商口头提出，如果他对另一个项目的投标被拒绝，他将以 20 万美元的价格完成这项工作。房主接受了建筑商的条款，建筑商随后准备了双方签署的书面合同。合同没有提及建筑商的待定投标。一周后，在得知他对另一个项目的待定投标已被接受后，建筑商拒绝为房主进行任何工作。房主能否因建筑商的不履约而获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为效率原则证明建筑商的服务应该用于更高价值的用途。","B":"不可以，因为建筑商的履行义务是有条件的。","C":"是的，因为建筑商试图设定其履行义务的条件，导致合同变得虚幻。","D":"是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）将禁止建筑商提供最终书面材料中未包含的口头理解证据。"},"explanationZh":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时同意修改或反驳综合写作条款的证据。但当一方攻击协议的有效性时，该规则不适用——例如，声称存在生效的先决条件但未能发生。因此，此类先决条件的证据是可接受的。这里，在合同签订之前，双方口头同意，只有当建筑商的其他待决投标被拒绝时，合同才具有约束力（即生效）——这是生效的先决条件。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不妨碍口头协议的证据来证明合同因建筑商的其他待决投标被接受而未能生效（选项D）。因此，房主无法因建筑商的不履约而获得赔偿，因为建筑商的履约从未到期。 （选择 A）如果一方违约并支付损害赔偿金比履约在经济上更有利，则效率原则证明违约是合理的（但不是借口）。但建筑商并没有违约，即使他违约了，事实也并不表明他这样做在经济上有利。 （选项C）虚幻的承诺是指承诺者可以自行决定是否履行的模糊承诺。在这里，建筑商的承诺并不是虚幻的，因为如果他的投标被拒绝，他就有义务履行。\n\n教育目标：根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于建立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 217（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（综合协议须遵守口头要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于确立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。","iracZh":{"issue":"房主能否因建筑商的不履约而获得赔偿？","rule":"根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于确立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。","analysis":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时同意修改或反驳综合写作条款的证据。但当一方攻击协议的有效性时，该规则不适用——例如，声称存在生效的先决条件但未能发生。因此，此类先决条件的证据是可接受的。这里，在合同签订之前，双方口头同意，只有当建筑商的其他待决投标被拒绝时，合同才具有约束力（即生效）——这是生效的先决条件。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不妨碍口头协议的证据来证明合同因建筑商的其他待决投标被接受而未能生效（选项D）。因此，房主无法因建筑商的不履约而获得赔偿，因为建筑商的履约从未到期。 （选择 A）如果一方违约并支付损害赔偿金比履约在经济上更有利，则效率原则证明违约是合理的（但不是借口）。但建筑商并没有违约，即使他违约了，事实也并不表明他这样做在经济上有利。 （选项C）虚幻的承诺是指承诺者可以自行决定是否履行的模糊承诺。在这里，建筑商的承诺并不是虚幻的，因为如果他的投标被拒绝，他就有义务履行。\n\n教育目标：根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于建立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 217（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（综合协议须遵守口头要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为建筑商的履行义务是有条件的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"illusory promise","ipa":"/ˌɪlˈusɝi pɹˈɑməs/","syllables":"il·lu·sory promise","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"虚幻允诺：允诺人实质未受约束，不能构成约因","contextualMeaning":"虚幻允诺：允诺人实质未受约束，不能构成约因","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) An illusory promise is one so vague that the promisor has discretion whether to perform.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":42,"sourceQuestionPage":123,"sourceQuestionPages":[123],"sourceExplanationPages":[124,125],"sourceAnswerPages":[124,125],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-043","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A janitorial service contracted in writing with a hospital for a one-year term. Under the terms of the contract, the janitorial service agreed to clean the hospital daily in accordance with the hygiene standards of the city's health code. Because the janitorial service did not clean a patient's room in accordance with the required hygiene standards, the patient contracted an infection that required continued hospitalization. In addition to suing the hospital, the patient sued the janitorial service for breach of contract. Which of the following statements is most accurate with respect to the breach of contract claim against the janitorial service?","choices":{"A":"The janitorial service is liable to the patient as a matter of public policy, because it violated the city's health code.","B":"The patient cannot sue on the contract, because she was not named in the contract.","C":"The patient has no claim for breach of contract against the janitorial service, because she is an incidental beneficiary.","D":"The patient is an intended third-party beneficiary under the contract, because the janitorial service's promise was intended to benefit all hospital patients."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. There are two types of third-party beneficiaries:\n• Intended – who receive a direct benefit from the contract because the contracting parties so intended (eg, the contract provides that payment will go directly to a third party)\n• Incidental – who receive some indirect benefit from the contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them (ie, all third-party beneficiaries who are not intended beneficiaries) Only intended beneficiaries—not incidental beneficiaries—have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them. Here, the patient is an incidental beneficiary to the janitorial service–hospital contract because there is no indication that the parties intended for the contract to directly benefit the patient (Choice D). Instead, the contract was designed for all patients to indirectly benefit from hygienic rooms. Therefore, the patient has no claim for breach of contract against the janitorial service.* *The patient may, however, have a tort claim against the janitorial service. (Choice A) A contractual party's violation of public policy may render that contract unenforceable, but it does not provide a nonparty with the right to sue under that contract. (Choice B) The patient could have sued on the contract had she been a named or unnamed intended beneficiary.\n\nEducational objective: Intended beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended, while incidental beneficiaries receive an indirect benefit from a contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 315 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (no right to enforce for incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"Intended beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended, while incidental beneficiaries receive an indirect benefit from a contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 315 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (no right to enforce for incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"Intended beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended, while incidental beneficiaries receive an indirect benefit from a contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is most accurate with respect to the breach of contract claim against the janitorial service?","rule":"Intended beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended, while incidental beneficiaries receive an indirect benefit from a contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.","analysis":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. There are two types of third-party beneficiaries:\n• Intended – who receive a direct benefit from the contract because the contracting parties so intended (eg, the contract provides that payment will go directly to a third party)\n• Incidental – who receive some indirect benefit from the contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them (ie, all third-party beneficiaries who are not intended beneficiaries) Only intended beneficiaries—not incidental beneficiaries—have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them. Here, the patient is an incidental beneficiary to the janitorial service–hospital contract because there is no indication that the parties intended for the contract to directly benefit the patient (Choice D). Instead, the contract was designed for all patients to indirectly benefit from hygienic rooms. Therefore, the patient has no claim for breach of contract against the janitorial service.* *The patient may, however, have a tort claim against the janitorial service. (Choice A) A contractual party's violation of public policy may render that contract unenforceable, but it does not provide a nonparty with the right to sue under that contract. (Choice B) The patient could have sued on the contract had she been a named or unnamed intended beneficiary.","conclusion":"Answer C: The patient has no claim for breach of contract against the janitorial service, because she is an incidental beneficiary."},"questionZh":"与医院签订书面合同的清洁服务，为期一年。根据合同条款，保洁人员同意按照该市健康码的卫生标准每天清洁医院。由于清洁人员没有按照规定的卫生标准清洁患者的房间，导致患者感染，需要继续住院治疗。除了起诉医院外，患者还起诉清洁服务机构违反合同。关于针对清洁服务的违约索赔，以下哪项陈述最准确？","choicesZh":{"A":"作为一项公共政策，清洁服务机构应对患者承担责任，因为它违反了该市的健康法规。","B":"患者不能就合同提起诉讼，因为合同中没有提及她的名字。","C":"患者无权就清洁服务违反合同提出索赔，因为她是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）。","D":"患者是合同规定的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary），因为清洁服务的承诺是为了让所有住院患者受益。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）有两种类型：\n• 预期 – 谁从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方如此预期（例如，合同规定付款将直接支付给第三方）\n• 附带 - 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，但从合同中获得一些间接利益的人（即非预期受益人的所有第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）） 只有预期受益人（而不是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。在这里，患者是清洁服务-医院合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），因为没有迹象表明合同双方打算让患者直接受益（选项 D）。相反，该合同是为所有患者间接受益于卫生房间而设计的。因此，患者无权就清洁服务违反合同提出索赔。* *但是，患者可以针对清洁服务提出侵权索赔。 （选项 A）合同方违反公共政策可能会导致该合同无法执行，但并不为非当事人提供根据该合同提起诉讼的权利。 （选择 B）如果患者是指定或未指定的预期受益人，她可以就合同提起诉讼。\n\n教育目标：预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有这样的意图，而偶然受益人则从合同中获得间接利益，即使没有合同意图使他们受益。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 315（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（无权对偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）强制执行）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有这样的意图，而偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）则从合同中获得间接利益，即使没有合同意图使他们受益。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于针对清洁服务的违约索赔，以下哪项陈述最准确？","rule":"预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有这样的意图，而偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）则从合同中获得间接利益，即使没有合同意图使他们受益。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）有两种类型：\n• 预期 – 谁从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方如此预期（例如，合同规定付款将直接支付给第三方）\n• 附带 - 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，但从合同中获得一些间接利益的人（即非预期受益人的所有第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）） 只有预期受益人（而不是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。在这里，患者是清洁服务-医院合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），因为没有迹象表明合同双方打算让患者直接受益（选项 D）。相反，该合同是为所有患者间接受益于卫生房间而设计的。因此，患者无权就清洁服务违反合同提出索赔。* *但是，患者可以针对清洁服务提出侵权索赔。 （选项 A）合同方违反公共政策可能会导致该合同无法执行，但并不为非当事人提供根据该合同提起诉讼的权利。 （选择 B）如果患者是指定或未指定的预期受益人，她可以就合同提起诉讼。\n\n教育目标：预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有这样的意图，而偶然受益人则从合同中获得间接利益，即使没有合同意图使他们受益。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 315（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（无权对偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）强制执行）。","conclusion":"答案 C：患者无权就清洁服务违反合同提出索赔，因为她是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"incidental beneficiary","ipa":"/ˌɪnsɪdˈɛntəl bˌɛnʌfˈɪʃiˌɛɹi/","syllables":"in·ci·den·tal ben·e·fi·ciary","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"偶然受益人：仅附带获益、通常无合同执行权的第三人","contextualMeaning":"偶然受益人：仅附带获益、通常无合同执行权的第三人","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The patient has no claim for breach of contract against the janitorial service, because she is an incidental beneficiary.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-11777eced73cc39a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":43,"sourceQuestionPage":126,"sourceQuestionPages":[126],"sourceExplanationPages":[127,128],"sourceAnswerPages":[127,128],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-044","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"An accountant and a bookkeeper, as part of a contract dissolving their accounting business, agreed that each would contribute $100,000 to fund an annuity for a clerk who was a longtime employee of the business. The clerk's position would be terminated at the dissolution, and he did not have a retirement plan. The accountant and the bookkeeper informed the clerk of their plan to fund an annuity for him. The clerk, confident about his financial future because of the promised annuity, purchased a retirement home. The accountant later contributed his $100,000 to fund the annuity, but the bookkeeper stated that he could afford to contribute only $50,000. The accountant agreed in writing that the bookkeeper should contribute only $50,000. Does the clerk have a valid basis for an action against the bookkeeper for the unpaid $50,000?","choices":{"A":"No, because the clerk was bound by the modification of the contract made by the accountant and the bookkeeper.","B":"No, because the clerk was only a donee beneficiary of the contract between the accountant and the bookkeeper and had no vested rights.","C":"Yes, because the clerk's reliance on the promised annuity prevented the parties from changing the terms of the contract.","D":"Yes, because the promise to establish the annuity was made binding by consideration from the clerk's many years of employment."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. If the contracting parties intended for the contract to benefit the third party, the third party is an intended beneficiary. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries have enforceable rights under the contract. Those rights vest when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Once this occurs, the original contracting parties are bound to perform the contract. Any efforts to rescind or modify the contract after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification. Here, the clerk was an intended beneficiary of the accountant-bookkeeper contract because the parties' promise to fund an annuity for the clerk was clearly intended to benefit him. The clerk detrimentally relied on the promised annuity by purchasing a retirement home (vesting). And since he did so before the accountant and the bookkeeper agreed that the bookkeeper would contribute $50,000 less, their attempt to modify the contract was void. As a result, the clerk has a valid basis for an action against the bookkeeper for the unpaid $50,000 (Choice A). (Choice B) A donee beneficiary is an intended beneficiary of a gift promise, while a creditor beneficiary is an intended beneficiary to whom the promisee owed money. The rule for vesting is the same for both. (Choice D) Under the bargained-for exchange test for consideration, past performance (eg, the clerk's many years of employment) does not constitute the consideration required to\n\nenforce a contract. Instead, the clerk can enforce the accountant-bookkeeper contract because of his third-party beneficiary status.\n\nEducational objective: An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any efforts to rescind or modify the contract after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (variation of a duty to a beneficiary).","educationalObjective":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any efforts to rescind or modify the contract after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (variation of a duty to a beneficiary).","rule":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any efforts to rescind or modify the contract after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.","irac":{"issue":"Does the clerk have a valid basis for an action against the bookkeeper for the unpaid $50,000?","rule":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any efforts to rescind or modify the contract after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. If the contracting parties intended for the contract to benefit the third party, the third party is an intended beneficiary. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries have enforceable rights under the contract. Those rights vest when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Once this occurs, the original contracting parties are bound to perform the contract. Any efforts to rescind or modify the contract after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification. Here, the clerk was an intended beneficiary of the accountant-bookkeeper contract because the parties' promise to fund an annuity for the clerk was clearly intended to benefit him. The clerk detrimentally relied on the promised annuity by purchasing a retirement home (vesting). And since he did so before the accountant and the bookkeeper agreed that the bookkeeper would contribute $50,000 less, their attempt to modify the contract was void. As a result, the clerk has a valid basis for an action against the bookkeeper for the unpaid $50,000 (Choice A). (Choice B) A donee beneficiary is an intended beneficiary of a gift promise, while a creditor beneficiary is an intended beneficiary to whom the promisee owed money. The rule for vesting is the same for both. (Choice D) Under the bargained-for exchange test for consideration, past performance (eg, the clerk's many years of employment) does not constitute the consideration required to\n\nenforce a contract. Instead, the clerk can enforce the accountant-bookkeeper contract because of his third-party beneficiary status.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the clerk's reliance on the promised annuity prevented the parties from changing the terms of the contract."},"questionZh":"作为解散会计业务合同的一部分，一名会计师和一名簿记员同意每人捐献 10 万美元，为一名长期在该公司工作的职员提供年金。该职员的职位将在解散时终止，并且他没有退休计划。会计师和簿记员告诉职员他们计划为他提供年金。由于承诺的年金，这名职员对自己的财务前景充满信心，因此购买了一套养老院。会计师后来捐出了10万美元来支付年金，但簿记员表示他只能捐出5万美元。会计师书面同意簿记员只需出资 50,000 美元。书记员是否有有效的依据针对未付的 50,000 美元向簿记员提起诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为书记员受到会计师和簿记员所做的合同修改的约束。","B":"不能，因为办事员只是会计师和簿记员之间合同的受赠受益人，没有既得权利。","C":"是的，因为职员对承诺的年金的依赖阻止了双方更改合同条款。","D":"是的，因为建立年金的承诺是经过多年工作经验的考量才具有约束力的。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。如果缔约方意图使合同使第三方受益，则第三方为预期受益人。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才拥有合同项下的可执行权利。当受益人符合以下条件时，这些权利即归属：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。一旦发生这种情况，原缔约方就有义务履行合同。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。在这里，办事员是会计师-簿记员合同的预期受益人，因为双方承诺为办事员提供年金，显然是为了让他受益。该职员通过购买养老院（归属）来有害地依赖所承诺的年金。由于他在会计师和簿记员同意簿记员减少出资 50,000 美元之前就这样做了，所以他们修改合同的尝试是无效的。因此，书记员有有效的依据针对未付的 50,000 美元向簿记员提起诉讼（选择 A）。 （选项B）受赠人受益人是赠与承诺的预期受益人，而债权人受益人是受诺人欠款的预期受益人。两者的归属规则相同。 （选项D）根据讨价还价交换测试的考虑，过去的表现（例如，该职员的多年工作经历）并不构成要求考虑的考虑因素。\n\n执行合同。相反，由于其第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）身份，职员可以执行会计-簿记员合同。\n\n教育目标：当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所创建的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 311（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受益人的义务的变更）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。","iracZh":{"issue":"书记员是否有有效的依据针对未付的 50,000 美元向簿记员提起诉讼？","rule":"当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。如果缔约方意图使合同使第三方受益，则第三方为预期受益人。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才拥有合同项下的可执行权利。当受益人符合以下条件时，这些权利即归属：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。一旦发生这种情况，原缔约方就有义务履行合同。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。在这里，办事员是会计师-簿记员合同的预期受益人，因为双方承诺为办事员提供年金，显然是为了让他受益。该职员通过购买养老院（归属）来有害地依赖所承诺的年金。由于他在会计师和簿记员同意簿记员减少出资 50,000 美元之前就这样做了，所以他们修改合同的尝试是无效的。因此，书记员有有效的依据针对未付的 50,000 美元向簿记员提起诉讼（选择 A）。 （选项B）受赠人受益人是赠与承诺的预期受益人，而债权人受益人是受诺人欠款的预期受益人。两者的归属规则相同。 （选项D）根据讨价还价交换测试的考虑，过去的表现（例如，该职员的多年工作经历）并不构成要求考虑的考虑因素。\n\n执行合同。相反，由于其第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）身份，职员可以执行会计-簿记员合同。\n\n教育目标：当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所创建的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 311（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受益人的义务的变更）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为职员对承诺的年金的依赖阻止了双方更改合同条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":44,"sourceQuestionPage":129,"sourceQuestionPages":[129],"sourceExplanationPages":[130,131],"sourceAnswerPages":[130,131],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-045","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"On June 1, an appliance manufacturer telephoned a supplier to determine whether the supplier could provide 300 washing machine motors of a particular model by October 1. The supplier offered to do so at a price of $300 per motor (a total price of $90,000). The manufacturer's representative said, \"Deal.\" The next day the manufacturer's representative sent the supplier an unsigned note on company letterhead that stated, \"I am happy that you are going to supply us with the motors. I will call you soon to talk about another order.\" The manufacturer then sent catalogs to its regular customers advertising washing machines that included the specified motors. The manufacturer did not hear from the supplier until July 1, when the supplier called to say that it would be unable to supply the motors because it was no longer carrying that model. At that time, the manufacturer had received no orders for the machines with the specified motors. The manufacturer sued the supplier for breach of contract, and the supplier raised the statute of frauds as a defense. Is the supplier's statute of frauds defense likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the manufacturer distributed the catalogs in reliance on the contract, making the contract enforceable under the statute of frauds.","B":"No, because the supplier failed to object to the contents of the note sent by the manufacturer.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer's note failed to contain a signature.","D":"Yes, because there is no writing that contains the quantity term of the contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more (as seen here) must be in a writing that states the quantity of goods to be sold and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. If the contract is between merchants, then a written confirmation will bind both parties if:\n• the confirmation is sent within a reasonable time\n• the confirmation satisfies the statute of frauds as to the sender and\n• the recipient does not object in writing within 10 days. Here, the manufacturer's representative sent the supplier an unsigned note on company letterhead to confirm their agreement for the supply of motors. But this confirmation does not satisfy the statute of frauds because it failed to state the quantity of the motors to be sold. Therefore, the supplier's statute-of-frauds defense will succeed. Had the confirmation listed the quantity of motors being sold, it would have been binding as to both parties and defeated the supplier's statute-of-frauds defense. That is because (1) the manufacturer's letterhead was an identifying mark that satisfied the signature requirement under the statute of frauds and (2) the supplier never objected to the written confirmation (Choices B & C). (Choice A) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party's reliance may serve as an alternative basis for recovery when no valid contract was formed—but it does not make a contract enforceable under the statute of frauds. Additionally, this doctrine only applies if reliance was reasonable and caused substantial detriment (eg, had the manufacturer received orders).\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a written confirmation between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if it (1) is sent within a reasonable time, (2) satisfies the statute of frauds—ie, signed writing with quantity stated—as to the sender, and (3) is not objected to in writing within 10 days.\n\nReferences\n• UCC § 2-201 (statute of frauds requirements).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a written confirmation between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if it (1) is sent within a reasonable time, (2) satisfies the statute of frauds—ie, signed writing with quantity stated—as to the sender, and (3) is not objected to in writing within 10 days.","references":"• UCC § 2-201 (statute of frauds requirements).","rule":"Under the UCC, a written confirmation between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if it (1) is sent within a reasonable time, (2) satisfies the statute of frauds—ie, signed writing with quantity stated—as to the sender, and (3) is not objected to in writing within 10 days.","irac":{"issue":"Is the supplier's statute of frauds defense likely to succeed?","rule":"Under the UCC, a written confirmation between merchants satisfies the statute of frauds as to both parties if it (1) is sent within a reasonable time, (2) satisfies the statute of frauds—ie, signed writing with quantity stated—as to the sender, and (3) is not objected to in writing within 10 days.","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more (as seen here) must be in a writing that states the quantity of goods to be sold and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. If the contract is between merchants, then a written confirmation will bind both parties if:\n• the confirmation is sent within a reasonable time\n• the confirmation satisfies the statute of frauds as to the sender and\n• the recipient does not object in writing within 10 days. Here, the manufacturer's representative sent the supplier an unsigned note on company letterhead to confirm their agreement for the supply of motors. But this confirmation does not satisfy the statute of frauds because it failed to state the quantity of the motors to be sold. Therefore, the supplier's statute-of-frauds defense will succeed. Had the confirmation listed the quantity of motors being sold, it would have been binding as to both parties and defeated the supplier's statute-of-frauds defense. That is because (1) the manufacturer's letterhead was an identifying mark that satisfied the signature requirement under the statute of frauds and (2) the supplier never objected to the written confirmation (Choices B & C). (Choice A) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party's reliance may serve as an alternative basis for recovery when no valid contract was formed—but it does not make a contract enforceable under the statute of frauds. Additionally, this doctrine only applies if reliance was reasonable and caused substantial detriment (eg, had the manufacturer received orders).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because there is no writing that contains the quantity term of the contract."},"questionZh":"6月1日，某家电制造商致电某供应商，询问该供应商是否可以在10月1日之前提供300台特定型号的洗衣机电机。供应商提出，每台电机的价格为300美元（总价为9万美元）。制造商代表说：“成交。”第二天，制造商代表向供应商发送了一份带有公司抬头的未签名便条，上面写着：“我很高兴您将为我们提供电机。我很快就会给您打电话，讨论另一份订单。”然后，制造商向其老客户发送包含指定电机的洗衣机广告目录。直到 7 月 1 日，制造商才收到供应商的消息，当时供应商打电话说无法供应电机，因为不再提供该型号的电机。当时，制造商尚未收到配备指定电机的机器的订单。制造商起诉供应商违反合同，供应商提出欺诈法作为抗辩。供应商的欺诈辩护有可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为制造商根据合同分发目录，从而使合同可以根据欺诈法执行。","B":"不可以，因为供应商未能对制造商发送的通知内容提出异议。","C":"是的，因为制造商的注释中没有包含签名。","D":"是的，因为没有包含合同数量条款的文字。"},"explanationZh":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\n根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（如此处所示）必须采用书面形式，说明待售商品的数量并由寻求执行的一方签署。如果合同是商家之间签订的，则在以下情况下，书面确认对双方均具有约束力：\n• 在合理的时间内发送确认信息\n• 该确认符合发件人的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），并且\n• 接收者在10 天内未提出书面反对。在这里，制造商代表向供应商发送了一份带有公司抬头的未签名便条，以确认他们同意供应电机。但这一确认不符合欺诈法，因为它没有说明待售电机的数量。因此，供应商的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）辩护将会成功。如果确认书中列出了所售电机的数量，那么它将对双方都具有约束力，并推翻了供应商的欺诈法抗辩。这是因为 (1) 制造商的信头是一个识别标记，满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）定的签名要求，并且 (2) 供应商从未反对过书面确认（选项 B 和 C）。 （选项A）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，当未形成有效合同时，一方当事人的信赖可以作为恢复的替代依据，但它并不能使合同根据欺诈法得以执行。此外，该原则仅适用于信赖合理且造成重大损害的情况（例如，制造商收到订单）。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，商家之间的书面确认满足双方的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果该确认 (1) 在合理的时间内发送，(2) 满足发送者的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（即注明数量的签名书面文件），并且 (3) 在 10 天内没有人以书面形式提出反对。\n\n参考文献\n• UCC § 2-201（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，商家之间的书面确认满足双方的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果该确认 (1) 在合理的时间内发送，(2) 满足发送者的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（即注明数量的书面签名），并且 (3) 在 10 天内没有收到书面反对。","iracZh":{"issue":"供应商的欺诈辩护有可能成功吗？","rule":"根据 UCC，商家之间的书面确认满足双方的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果该确认 (1) 在合理的时间内发送，(2) 满足发送者的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（即注明数量的书面签名），并且 (3) 在 10 天内没有收到书面反对。","analysis":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\n根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（如此处所示）必须采用书面形式，说明待售商品的数量并由寻求执行的一方签署。如果合同是商家之间签订的，则在以下情况下，书面确认对双方均具有约束力：\n• 在合理的时间内发送确认信息\n• 该确认符合发件人的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），并且\n• 接收者在10 天内未提出书面反对。在这里，制造商代表向供应商发送了一份带有公司抬头的未签名便条，以确认他们同意供应电机。但这一确认不符合欺诈法，因为它没有说明待售电机的数量。因此，供应商的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）辩护将会成功。如果确认书中列出了所售电机的数量，那么它将对双方都具有约束力，并推翻了供应商的欺诈法抗辩。这是因为 (1) 制造商的信头是一个识别标记，满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）定的签名要求，并且 (2) 供应商从未反对过书面确认（选项 B 和 C）。 （选项A）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，当未形成有效合同时，一方当事人的信赖可以作为恢复的替代依据，但它并不能使合同根据欺诈法得以执行。此外，该原则仅适用于信赖合理且造成重大损害的情况（例如，制造商收到订单）。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，商家之间的书面确认满足双方的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果该确认 (1) 在合理的时间内发送，(2) 满足发送者的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）（即注明数量的签名书面文件），并且 (3) 在 10 天内没有人以书面形式提出反对。\n\n参考文献\n• UCC § 2-201（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为没有包含合同数量条款的文字。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":45,"sourceQuestionPage":132,"sourceQuestionPages":[132],"sourceExplanationPages":[133,134],"sourceAnswerPages":[133,134],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-046","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"A collector owned a large number of rare baseball cards. Each card was sealed and placed in a protective binder. Each binder was labeled by league, team, and decade. The collector met with a man who was interested in purchasing some of the cards. After reviewing all of the binders, the man offered to purchase the cards of \"every player who had ever played for [his favorite team].\" The collector accepted the offer. The man wired the purchase price to the collector and subsequently received all of the collector's binders that had been labeled with the name of the man's favorite team. However, the man had intended for his purchase to include cards from any other teams for which his favorite team's players had ever played. In an action by the man against the collector for specific performance, which of the following, if proved, would best support the man's claim?","choices":{"A":"Both parties knew that there was a misunderstanding.","B":"Neither party knew that there was a misunderstanding.","C":"The collector knew that there was a misunderstanding, but the man did not.","D":"The man knew that there was a misunderstanding, but the collector did not."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A misunderstanding arises when the parties to a contract assign different meanings to the same term. When the misunderstanding involves a material term, whether a valid contract was formed depends on who knew or had reason to know that there was a misunderstanding:*\n• neither party OR both parties – in which case no contract was formed because there was no meeting of the minds\n\n• one party – in which case a valid contract was formed, and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls\n\nHere, the man offered to purchase the cards of \"every player who had ever played for [his favorite team].\" He understood this to include cards from other teams while the collector did not, so the parties assigned different meanings to the same material term. If the collector knew (or had reason to know) that there was a misunderstanding but the man did not, then a valid contract formed according to the man's understanding of the term. Therefore, this fact would best support the man's claim for specific performance. *In determining what a party knew or had reason to know, the objective test typically used in contract law does not apply. Instead, it is each party's actual knowledge or reason to know of the misunderstanding that governs—not what a reasonable person would have known. (Choices A & B) No contract was formed if both parties OR neither party knew that there was a misunderstanding, so these circumstances would not support the man's claim for specific performance. (Choice D) Fairness governs the effect of misunderstandings on contract formation. Here, if the man knew about the misunderstanding but the collector did not, it would be unfair to force the collector to give the man the cards from the other teams.\n\nEducational objective: A misunderstanding involving a material term prevents contract formation when neither party OR both parties knew or had reason to know about the misunderstanding. However, if only one party knew or had reason to know, then a valid contract is formed using the unknowing party's meaning of the term.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","educationalObjective":"A misunderstanding involving a material term prevents contract formation when neither party OR both parties knew or had reason to know about the misunderstanding. However, if only one party knew or had reason to know, then a valid contract is formed using the unknowing party's meaning of the term.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","rule":"A misunderstanding involving a material term prevents contract formation when neither party OR both parties knew or had reason to know about the misunderstanding. However, if only one party knew or had reason to know, then a valid contract is formed using the unknowing party's meaning of the term.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the man against the collector for specific performance, which of the following, if proved, would best support the man's claim?","rule":"A misunderstanding involving a material term prevents contract formation when neither party OR both parties knew or had reason to know about the misunderstanding. However, if only one party knew or had reason to know, then a valid contract is formed using the unknowing party's meaning of the term.","analysis":"A misunderstanding arises when the parties to a contract assign different meanings to the same term. When the misunderstanding involves a material term, whether a valid contract was formed depends on who knew or had reason to know that there was a misunderstanding:*\n• neither party OR both parties – in which case no contract was formed because there was no meeting of the minds\n\n• one party – in which case a valid contract was formed, and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls\n\nHere, the man offered to purchase the cards of \"every player who had ever played for [his favorite team].\" He understood this to include cards from other teams while the collector did not, so the parties assigned different meanings to the same material term. If the collector knew (or had reason to know) that there was a misunderstanding but the man did not, then a valid contract formed according to the man's understanding of the term. Therefore, this fact would best support the man's claim for specific performance. *In determining what a party knew or had reason to know, the objective test typically used in contract law does not apply. Instead, it is each party's actual knowledge or reason to know of the misunderstanding that governs—not what a reasonable person would have known. (Choices A & B) No contract was formed if both parties OR neither party knew that there was a misunderstanding, so these circumstances would not support the man's claim for specific performance. (Choice D) Fairness governs the effect of misunderstandings on contract formation. Here, if the man knew about the misunderstanding but the collector did not, it would be unfair to force the collector to give the man the cards from the other teams.","conclusion":"Answer C: The collector knew that there was a misunderstanding, but the man did not."},"questionZh":"一位收藏家拥有大量稀有的棒球卡。每张卡片都被密封并放入保护活页夹中。每个活页夹都贴有联盟、球队和十年的标签。收藏家会见了一位有兴趣购买其中一些卡片的男子。在检查了所有活页夹后，该男子提出购买“每一位曾经为[他最喜欢的球队]效力过的球员”的卡片。收藏家接受了这个报价。该男子将购买价格电汇给收藏家，随后收到了收藏家的所有活页夹，上面贴有该男子最喜欢的球队的名字。然而，该男子原本打算购买包括他最喜欢的球队球员曾效力过的任何其他球队的卡。在该男子针对收藏家的特定履行（specific performance）提起诉讼时，以下哪项如果得到证实，最能支持该男子的主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"双方都知道其中存在误会。","B":"双方都不知道其中存在误会。","C":"收藏家知道其中有误会，但该男子却不知道。","D":"男子知道其中有误会，但收藏家却不知道。"},"explanationZh":"当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同含义时，就会产生误解。当误解涉及重大条款时，合同是否成立取决于谁知道或有理由知道存在误解：*\n• 任何一方或双方都没有——在这种情况下，由于没有达成一致意见，因此没有形成合同\n\n• 一方——在这种情况下，一份有效的合同成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制\n\n在这里，该男子提出购买“每一位曾经为[他最喜欢的球队]效力的球员”的卡片。他认为这包括来自其他团队的卡片，而收集者则不包括，因此各方为同一个材料术语赋予了不同的含义。如果收款人知道（或有理由知道）存在误解，而该人不知道，则根据该人对该术语的理解，有效的合同成立。因此，这一事实最能支持该男子的特定履行（specific performance）主张。 *在确定当事人知道或有理由知道什么时，合同法中通常使用的客观测试不适用。相反，起主导作用的是各方的实际知识或理由来了解误解，而不是一个有理智的人会知道的情况。 （选项A、B）如果双方当事人或双方均不知道存在误会，则合同不成立，因此这些情况不能支持男方具体履行的主张。 （选项 D）公平性决定了误解对合同订立的影响。在这里，如果该男子知道误会，但收集者不知道，那么强迫收集者将其他球队的牌交给该男子是不公平的。\n\n教育目标：当任何一方或双方都不知道或没有理由知道该误解时，涉及重大条款的误解会阻碍合同的成立。但是，如果只有一方知道或有理由知道，则使用不知情方对该术语的含义即可形成有效的合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当任何一方或双方都不知道或没有理由知道该误解时，涉及重大条款的误解会阻碍合同的成立。但是，如果只有一方知道或有理由知道，则使用不知情方对该术语的含义即可形成有效的合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"在该男子针对收藏家的特定履行（specific performance）提起诉讼时，以下哪项如果得到证实，最能支持该男子的主张？","rule":"当任何一方或双方都不知道或没有理由知道该误解时，涉及重大条款的误解会阻碍合同的成立。但是，如果只有一方知道或有理由知道，则使用不知情方对该术语的含义即可形成有效的合同。","analysis":"当合同双方对同一术语赋予不同含义时，就会产生误解。当误解涉及重大条款时，合同是否成立取决于谁知道或有理由知道存在误解：*\n• 任何一方或双方都没有——在这种情况下，由于没有达成一致意见，因此没有形成合同\n\n• 一方——在这种情况下，一份有效的合同成立，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制\n\n在这里，该男子提出购买“每一位曾经为[他最喜欢的球队]效力的球员”的卡片。他认为这包括来自其他团队的卡片，而收集者则不包括，因此各方为同一个材料术语赋予了不同的含义。如果收款人知道（或有理由知道）存在误解，而该人不知道，则根据该人对该术语的理解，有效的合同成立。因此，这一事实最能支持该男子的特定履行（specific performance）主张。 *在确定当事人知道或有理由知道什么时，合同法中通常使用的客观测试不适用。相反，起主导作用的是各方的实际知识或理由来了解误解，而不是一个有理智的人会知道的情况。 （选项A、B）如果双方当事人或双方均不知道存在误会，则合同不成立，因此这些情况不能支持男方具体履行的主张。 （选项 D）公平性决定了误解对合同订立的影响。在这里，如果该男子知道误会，但收集者不知道，那么强迫收集者将其他球队的牌交给该男子是不公平的。\n\n教育目标：当任何一方或双方都不知道或没有理由知道该误解时，涉及重大条款的误解会阻碍合同的成立。但是，如果只有一方知道或有理由知道，则使用不知情方对该术语的含义即可形成有效的合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 C：收藏家知道其中有误会，但该男子却不知道。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-c0466e058cb6656c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":46,"sourceQuestionPage":135,"sourceQuestionPages":[135],"sourceExplanationPages":[136,137],"sourceAnswerPages":[136,137],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-047","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"Collection of a debtor's $2,000 debt to a creditor was barred by the applicable statute of limitations. The debtor sold and delivered his car to a buyer under a written agreement, signed by the buyer, in which the buyer promised to pay the $2,000 purchase price to the creditor \"in satisfaction of [the debtor's] debt to [the creditor].\" Can the creditor recover the $2,000 from the buyer?","choices":{"A":"No, because payment of the $2,000 to the creditor would undermine the statutory public policy against enforcement of stale claims.","B":"No, because the creditor's rights as an intended beneficiary are subject to any defenses available to the contracting parties between themselves.","C":"Yes, because the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor is enforceable by the creditor regardless of whether the debtor was legally obligated to pay the creditor anything.","D":"Yes, because the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor revived the uncollectible debt."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. There are two types of third-party beneficiaries:\n• Intended – who receive a direct benefit from the contract because the contracting parties so intended (eg, the contract provides that payment will go directly to a third party)\n\n• Incidental – who receive some indirect benefit from the contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them (ie, all third-party beneficiaries who are not intended beneficiaries)\n\nOnly intended beneficiaries—not incidental beneficiaries—have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them. Here, the debtor and the buyer agreed that the buyer would pay the $2,000 purchase price directly to the creditor in satisfaction of the debtor's debt to the creditor. This made the creditor an intended third-party beneficiary of the debtor-buyer agreement. Therefore, the creditor can enforce the buyer's promise to pay the $2,000. This is true regardless of whether the debtor was legally obligated to pay the creditor anything (given that the applicable statute of limitations had run) (Choice D). (Choice A) There is no statutory public policy against enforcement of stale claims. And here, the buyer's promise to pay the creditor the $2,000 is enforceable even if the creditor was time-barred from collecting the debt from the debtor. (Choice B) The promisor (eg, the buyer) can raise any defense against the third-party beneficiary (eg, the creditor) that the promisor had against the original promisee (eg, the\n\ndebtor). But there is no indication that the buyer has a defense against the debtor that could be raised against the creditor in this case. (Choice D) The buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor did not revive the uncollectible debt—ie, a suit by the creditor to collect the $2,000 from the debtor would still be barred by the applicable statute of limitations.\n\nEducational objective: Third-party beneficiaries are nonparties who receive some advantage or benefit from a contract. But only intended beneficiaries have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them.\n\nReferences\n• 17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 426 (2020) (intended versus incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"Third-party beneficiaries are nonparties who receive some advantage or benefit from a contract. But only intended beneficiaries have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them.","references":"• 17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 426 (2020) (intended versus incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"Third-party beneficiaries are nonparties who receive some advantage or benefit from a contract. But only intended beneficiaries have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them.","irac":{"issue":"Collection of a debtor's $2,000 debt to a creditor was barred by the applicable statute of limitations. The debtor sold and delivered his car to a buyer under a written agreement, signed by the buyer, in which the buyer promised to pay the $2,000 purchase price to the creditor \"in satisfaction of [the debtor's] debt to [the creditor].\" Can the creditor recover the $2,000 from the buyer?","rule":"Third-party beneficiaries are nonparties who receive some advantage or benefit from a contract. But only intended beneficiaries have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. There are two types of third-party beneficiaries:\n• Intended – who receive a direct benefit from the contract because the contracting parties so intended (eg, the contract provides that payment will go directly to a third party)\n\n• Incidental – who receive some indirect benefit from the contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them (ie, all third-party beneficiaries who are not intended beneficiaries)\n\nOnly intended beneficiaries—not incidental beneficiaries—have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them. Here, the debtor and the buyer agreed that the buyer would pay the $2,000 purchase price directly to the creditor in satisfaction of the debtor's debt to the creditor. This made the creditor an intended third-party beneficiary of the debtor-buyer agreement. Therefore, the creditor can enforce the buyer's promise to pay the $2,000. This is true regardless of whether the debtor was legally obligated to pay the creditor anything (given that the applicable statute of limitations had run) (Choice D). (Choice A) There is no statutory public policy against enforcement of stale claims. And here, the buyer's promise to pay the creditor the $2,000 is enforceable even if the creditor was time-barred from collecting the debt from the debtor. (Choice B) The promisor (eg, the buyer) can raise any defense against the third-party beneficiary (eg, the creditor) that the promisor had against the original promisee (eg, the\n\ndebtor). But there is no indication that the buyer has a defense against the debtor that could be raised against the creditor in this case. (Choice D) The buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor did not revive the uncollectible debt—ie, a suit by the creditor to collect the $2,000 from the debtor would still be barred by the applicable statute of limitations.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor is enforceable by the creditor regardless of whether the debtor was legally obligated to pay the creditor anything."},"questionZh":"适用的诉讼时效禁止追收债务人欠债权人的 2,000 美元债务。债务人根据买方签署的书面协议将其汽车出售并交付给买方，其中买方承诺向债权人支付 2,000 美元的购买价，“以清偿[债务人]对[债权人]的债务。”债权人能否向买家追回 2,000 美元？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为向债权人支付 2,000 美元会破坏反对执行过时债权的法定公共政策。","B":"不可以，因为作为预期受益人的债权人的权利受到缔约方之间可用的任何抗辩的约束。","C":"是的，因为无论债务人是否有法律义务向债权人支付任何款项，买方向债权人支付 2,000 美元的承诺都可以由债权人强制执行。","D":"是的，因为买方承诺向债权人支付 2,000 美元，从而使无法收回的债务重新恢复。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）有两种类型：\n• 预期 – 谁从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方如此预期（例如，合同规定付款将直接支付给第三方）\n\n• 附带 – 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，但从合同中获得一些间接利益的人（即非预期受益人的所有第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary））\n\n只有预期受益人（而不是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。在此，债务人和买方同意买方将 2,000 美元的购买价直接支付给债权人，以清偿债务人对债权人的债务。这使得债权人成为债务人-买方协议的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。因此，债权人可以强制执行买方支付 2,000 美元的承诺。无论债务人是否有法律义务向债权人支付任何款项（假设适用的诉讼时效已经生效），情况都是如此（选项 D）。 （选项 A） 没有针对陈旧索赔执行的法定公共政策。在这里，即使债权人被禁止向债务人追债，买方向债权人支付 2,000 美元的承诺仍然可以强制执行。 （选择 B） 承诺人（例如，买方）可以针对第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）（例如，债权人）提出任何抗辩，就像承诺人对原受诺人（例如，\n\n债务人）。但没有迹象表明买方可以针对债务人对本案中的债权人提出抗辩。 （选项D） 买方承诺向债权人支付2,000 美元，但无法恢复无法收回的债务——即，债权人向债务人收取2,000 美元的诉讼仍会受到适用时效的限制。\n\n教育目标：第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些优势或利益的非当事人。但只有预期受益人才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。\n\n参考文献\n• 17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 426 (2020)（预期受益人和偶然受益人）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的非当事人。但只有预期受益人才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"适用的诉讼时效禁止追收债务人欠债权人的 2,000 美元债务。债务人根据买方签署的书面协议将其汽车出售并交付给买方，其中买方承诺向债权人支付 2,000 美元的购买价，“以清偿[债务人]对[债权人]的债务。”债权人能否向买家追回 2,000 美元？","rule":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的非当事人。但只有预期受益人才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）有两种类型：\n• 预期 – 谁从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方如此预期（例如，合同规定付款将直接支付给第三方）\n\n• 附带 – 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，但从合同中获得一些间接利益的人（即非预期受益人的所有第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary））\n\n只有预期受益人（而不是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。在此，债务人和买方同意买方将 2,000 美元的购买价直接支付给债权人，以清偿债务人对债权人的债务。这使得债权人成为债务人-买方协议的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。因此，债权人可以强制执行买方支付 2,000 美元的承诺。无论债务人是否有法律义务向债权人支付任何款项（假设适用的诉讼时效已经生效），情况都是如此（选项 D）。 （选项 A） 没有针对陈旧索赔执行的法定公共政策。在这里，即使债权人被禁止向债务人追债，买方向债权人支付 2,000 美元的承诺仍然可以强制执行。 （选择 B） 承诺人（例如，买方）可以针对第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）（例如，债权人）提出任何抗辩，就像承诺人对原受诺人（例如，\n\n债务人）。但没有迹象表明买方可以针对债务人对本案中的债权人提出抗辩。 （选项D） 买方承诺向债权人支付2,000 美元，但无法恢复无法收回的债务——即，债权人向债务人收取2,000 美元的诉讼仍会受到适用时效的限制。\n\n教育目标：第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些优势或利益的非当事人。但只有预期受益人才拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利。\n\n参考文献\n• 17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 426 (2020)（预期受益人和偶然受益人）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为无论债务人是否有法律义务向债权人支付任何款项，买方向债权人支付 2,000 美元的承诺都可以由债权人强制执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":47,"sourceQuestionPage":138,"sourceQuestionPages":[138],"sourceExplanationPages":[139,140],"sourceAnswerPages":[139,140],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-048","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A builder borrowed $10,000 from a lender to finance a small construction job under a contract with a homeowner. The builder gave the lender a writing that stated, \"Any money I receive from the homeowner will be paid immediately to the lender, regardless of any demands from other creditors.\" The builder died after completing the job but before the homeowner paid. The lender demanded that the homeowner pay the $10,000 due to the builder directly to the lender. The homeowner refused, saying that he would pay directly to the builder's estate everything that he owed the builder. Is the lender likely to succeed in an action against the homeowner for $10,000?","choices":{"A":"No, because the builder's death terminated the lender's right to receive payment directly from the homeowner.","B":"No, because the writing the builder gave to the lender did not transfer to the lender the right to receive payment from the homeowner.","C":"Yes, because the builder had manifested an intent that the homeowner pay the $10,000 directly to the lender.","D":"Yes, because the lender is an intended beneficiary of the builder-homeowner contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract An assignment is the transfer of rights under a contract to another (ie, assignee). No formalities are needed for an assignment, but there must be a present intent to transfer the rights immediately. A party's promise to pay money received pursuant to a contract to a third party is not an assignment of the party's contractual rights, but a promise of future payment. Therefore, the third party in that situation is not an assignee. Here, the builder promised to pay the lender the $10,000 the builder received from the homeowner pursuant to the builder-homeowner contract. This promise of future payment was not an assignment and therefore did not transfer to the lender the right to receive payment directly from the homeowner. As a result, the lender is unlikely to succeed in an action against the homeowner for $10,000. (Choice A) A gratuitous assignment (ie, one that is not supported by consideration) is automatically revoked upon the death, incapacity, or bankruptcy of the assignor. But no valid assignment occurred here, so the builder's death is irrelevant. (Choice C) Regardless of the builder's subjective intent, the lender was not an assignee and therefore has no right to receive the $10,000 payment directly from the homeowner. (Choice D) The lender was not an intended beneficiary of the builder-homeowner contract because the contract did not provide that the homeowner would pay the $10,000 directly to the lender.\n\nEducational objective: A party's promise to pay money received pursuant to a contract to a third party is not an assignment of the party's contractual rights, but a promise of future payment.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 321 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (assignment of future rights).","educationalObjective":"A party's promise to pay money received pursuant to a contract to a third party is not an assignment of the party's contractual rights, but a promise of future payment.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 321 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (assignment of future rights).","rule":"A party's promise to pay money received pursuant to a contract to a third party is not an assignment of the party's contractual rights, but a promise of future payment.","irac":{"issue":"Is the lender likely to succeed in an action against the homeowner for $10,000?","rule":"A party's promise to pay money received pursuant to a contract to a third party is not an assignment of the party's contractual rights, but a promise of future payment.","analysis":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract An assignment is the transfer of rights under a contract to another (ie, assignee). No formalities are needed for an assignment, but there must be a present intent to transfer the rights immediately. A party's promise to pay money received pursuant to a contract to a third party is not an assignment of the party's contractual rights, but a promise of future payment. Therefore, the third party in that situation is not an assignee. Here, the builder promised to pay the lender the $10,000 the builder received from the homeowner pursuant to the builder-homeowner contract. This promise of future payment was not an assignment and therefore did not transfer to the lender the right to receive payment directly from the homeowner. As a result, the lender is unlikely to succeed in an action against the homeowner for $10,000. (Choice A) A gratuitous assignment (ie, one that is not supported by consideration) is automatically revoked upon the death, incapacity, or bankruptcy of the assignor. But no valid assignment occurred here, so the builder's death is irrelevant. (Choice C) Regardless of the builder's subjective intent, the lender was not an assignee and therefore has no right to receive the $10,000 payment directly from the homeowner. (Choice D) The lender was not an intended beneficiary of the builder-homeowner contract because the contract did not provide that the homeowner would pay the $10,000 directly to the lender.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the writing the builder gave to the lender did not transfer to the lender the right to receive payment from the homeowner."},"questionZh":"一名建筑商根据与房主签订的合同，从贷方借了 10,000 美元，用于资助一项小型建筑工程。建筑商给贷方一份书面文件，其中写道：“我从房主那里收到的任何钱都将立即支付给贷方，无论其他债权人有什么要求。”建筑商在完成工作后但在房主付款之前就去世了。贷款人要求房主直接向贷款人支付欠建筑商的10,000美元。房主拒绝了，并表示他将直接向建筑商的财产支付他欠建筑商的一切。贷款人向房主提起 10,000 美元诉讼是否有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为建筑商的死亡终止了贷款人直接从房主那里收取付款的权利。","B":"不可以，因为建筑商向贷方提供的书面文件并未将向房主收取付款的权利转让给贷方。","C":"是的，因为建筑商已表达了房主直接向贷方支付 10,000 美元的意图。","D":"是的，因为贷款人是建筑商-房主合同的预期受益人。"},"explanationZh":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 转让是将合同项下的权利转让给另一人（即受让人）。转让不需要办理任何手续，但必须有立即转让权利的意图。一方当事人承诺将根据合同收到的款项支付给第三方，并不是该方合同权利的转让，而是对未来付款的承诺。因此，这种情况下的第三方不属于受让人。在此，建筑商承诺根据建筑商-房主合同向贷款人支付建筑商从房主处收到的 10,000 美元。这种未来付款的承诺不是转让，因此并未将直接从房主处收取付款的权利转移给贷方。因此，贷款人向房主索赔 10,000 美元的诉讼不太可能成功。 （选择A）无偿转让（即没有对价支持的转让）在转让人死亡、丧失行为能力或破产时自动撤销。但这里没有发生有效的分配，因此建造者的死亡无关紧要。 （选项C） 无论建筑商的主观意图如何，贷款人都不是受让人，因此无权直接从房主那里收取10,000 美元的付款。 （选项 D）贷款人不是建筑商-房主合同的预期受益人，因为合同没有规定房主将直接向贷款人支付 10,000 美元。\n\n教育目标：一方承诺将根据合同收到的款项支付给第三方，并不是对该方合同权利的转让，而是对未来付款的承诺。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 321（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（未来权利的转让）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一方当事人承诺将根据合同收到的款项支付给第三方，并不是该方合同权利的转让，而是对未来付款的承诺。","iracZh":{"issue":"贷款人向房主提起 10,000 美元诉讼是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"一方当事人承诺将根据合同收到的款项支付给第三方，并不是该方合同权利的转让，而是对未来付款的承诺。","analysis":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 转让是将合同项下的权利转让给另一人（即受让人）。转让不需要办理任何手续，但必须有立即转让权利的意图。一方当事人承诺将根据合同收到的款项支付给第三方，并不是该方合同权利的转让，而是对未来付款的承诺。因此，这种情况下的第三方不属于受让人。在此，建筑商承诺根据建筑商-房主合同向贷款人支付建筑商从房主处收到的 10,000 美元。这种未来付款的承诺不是转让，因此并未将直接从房主处收取付款的权利转移给贷方。因此，贷款人向房主索赔 10,000 美元的诉讼不太可能成功。 （选择A）无偿转让（即没有对价支持的转让）在转让人死亡、丧失行为能力或破产时自动撤销。但这里没有发生有效的分配，因此建造者的死亡无关紧要。 （选项C） 无论建筑商的主观意图如何，贷款人都不是受让人，因此无权直接从房主那里收取10,000 美元的付款。 （选项 D）贷款人不是建筑商-房主合同的预期受益人，因为合同没有规定房主将直接向贷款人支付 10,000 美元。\n\n教育目标：一方承诺将根据合同收到的款项支付给第三方，并不是对该方合同权利的转让，而是对未来付款的承诺。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 321（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（未来权利的转让）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为建筑商向贷方提供的书面文件并未将向房主收取付款的权利转让给贷方。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":48,"sourceQuestionPage":141,"sourceQuestionPages":[141],"sourceExplanationPages":[142,143],"sourceAnswerPages":[142,143],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-049","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"For several weeks, a buyer's agent and an aircraft salesperson negotiated over the purchase price of a plane. After agreeing to a purchase price of $250,000, the agent signed a written contract to purchase the plane on behalf of the buyer. The contract contained a merger clause and called for a $10,000 deposit that the aircraft company could retain as liquidated damages if the buyer failed to accept delivery of the plane. The agent gave the salesperson a check for $10,000. The buyer subsequently refused to accept delivery of the plane. The aircraft company tried to deposit the check pursuant to the liquidated damages provision, but the agent had issued a stop-payment order. The aircraft company sued the buyer to recover the $10,000. The buyer has offered the agent's testimony that the salesperson orally agreed during their negotiations that the deal would not be finalized until the buyer's wife approved it, which she did not. Should the court admit this testimony?","choices":{"A":"No, because the merger clause bars any evidence of the parties' prior discussions concerning the sale of the plane.","B":"No, because the testimony contradicts the writing by making the parties' duties conditional.","C":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule does not bar the introduction of evidence for the purpose of showing that no contract was formed.","D":"Yes, but only for the purpose of proving that the written contract was only partially integrated."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A written contract that contains a merger clause is presumed to be a complete integration—ie, a complete and final expression of the parties' agreement as to all of the terms. As a result, the parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the writing. However, the parol evidence rule does not apply to evidence offered to prove that no contract was formed— eg, because there was a condition precedent to the existence of the contract that failed to occur. Here, the agent signed a written contract to buy the plane on the buyer's behalf. The contract contained a merger clause, which ordinarily would bar any evidence of the parties' prior discussions concerning the sale. However, the agent's testimony shows that the parties agreed that the deal would not be finalized until the buyer's wife approved it—a condition precedent to the existence of the contract. Therefore, the court should admit the testimony to show that no contract was formed because the buyer's wife did not approve the contract (Choice A). (Choice B) Even if the testimony contradicts the writing by making the parties' duties conditional, the testimony is not barred by the parol evidence rule since it is offered to prove that no contract was formed. (Choice D) Although the merger clause creates a presumption that the agreement was completely integrated, this presumption can be rebutted with evidence that the agreement was only partially integrated—ie, a final expression as to some (but not all) of the terms. As a result, the agent's testimony is admissible for that purpose. But it is also admissible to prove that the written contract never came into being because a condition precedent failed to occur.\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a contract that is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause). But this rule does not apply to evidence offered to prove that no contract was formed.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a contract that is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause). But this rule does not apply to evidence offered to prove that no contract was formed.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 214 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (exceptions to parol evidence rule).","rule":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a contract that is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause). But this rule does not apply to evidence offered to prove that no contract was formed.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court admit this testimony?","rule":"The parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the terms of a contract that is completely integrated (eg, as evidenced by a merger clause). But this rule does not apply to evidence offered to prove that no contract was formed.","analysis":"A written contract that contains a merger clause is presumed to be a complete integration—ie, a complete and final expression of the parties' agreement as to all of the terms. As a result, the parol evidence rule bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous communications that supplement or contradict the writing. However, the parol evidence rule does not apply to evidence offered to prove that no contract was formed— eg, because there was a condition precedent to the existence of the contract that failed to occur. Here, the agent signed a written contract to buy the plane on the buyer's behalf. The contract contained a merger clause, which ordinarily would bar any evidence of the parties' prior discussions concerning the sale. However, the agent's testimony shows that the parties agreed that the deal would not be finalized until the buyer's wife approved it—a condition precedent to the existence of the contract. Therefore, the court should admit the testimony to show that no contract was formed because the buyer's wife did not approve the contract (Choice A). (Choice B) Even if the testimony contradicts the writing by making the parties' duties conditional, the testimony is not barred by the parol evidence rule since it is offered to prove that no contract was formed. (Choice D) Although the merger clause creates a presumption that the agreement was completely integrated, this presumption can be rebutted with evidence that the agreement was only partially integrated—ie, a final expression as to some (but not all) of the terms. As a result, the agent's testimony is admissible for that purpose. But it is also admissible to prove that the written contract never came into being because a condition precedent failed to occur.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the parol evidence rule does not bar the introduction of evidence for the purpose of showing that no contract was formed."},"questionZh":"几周来，买方代理人和飞机销售人员就飞机的购买价格进行了谈判。在同意 25 万美元的购买价格后，代理人代表买方签署了购买飞机的书面合同。该合同包含一项合并条款，并要求支付 10,000 美元的押金，如果买方未能接受飞机的交付，飞机公司可以保留这笔押金作为违约金。代理人给了销售人员一张 10,000 美元的支票。买方随后拒绝接受飞机的交付。飞机公司试图根据违约金条款存入支票，但代理人已发出止付令。飞机公司起诉买家，要求追回 10,000 美元。买方向代理人提供了证词，证明销售人员在谈判期间口头同意，只有买方妻子批准后，交易才会最终敲定，但她没有批准。法庭应该承认这个证词吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合并条款禁止提供双方之前就飞机销售进行讨论的任何证据。","B":"不可以，因为证词与书面内容相矛盾，使双方的义务成为有条件的。","C":"是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止引入证据来证明合同并未成立。","D":"是的，但只是为了证明书面合同仅部分完整。"},"explanationZh":"包含合并条款的书面合同被认为是完整的整合，即双方对所有条款的协议的完整和最终表达。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止补充或反驳书面内容的先前或同期通信的证据。然而，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不适用于用来证明合同并未成立的证据——例如，因为合同存在的先决条件未能发生。在这里，代理人代表买方签署了购买飞机的书面合同。该合同包含一项合并条款，该条款通常会禁止提供双方先前就出售事宜进行讨论的任何证据。然而，代理人的证词显示，双方同意，在买方妻子批准之前，交易不会最终完成——这是合同存在的先决条件。因此，法院应采信证明因买方妻子不同意合同而导致合同不成立的证词（选项A）。 （选择B）即使证词与书面内容相矛盾，使双方的义务成为有条件的，该证词也不受口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的禁止，因为它是用来证明没有形成合同的。 （选项D）虽然合并条款创建了协议已完全整合的推定，但可以用协议仅部分整合的证据（即，对某些（但不是全部）条款的最终表述）来反驳该推定。因此，代理人的证词为此目的是可接受的。但也可以证明书面合同因先决条件未发生而从未成立。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止补充或矛盾完全整合的合同条款（例如，由合并条款证明）的先前或同期通信的证据。但该规则不适用于证明合同未成立的证据。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止先前或同时期的通信证据，这些通信补充或与完全整合的合同条款相矛盾（例如，由合并条款证明）。但该规则不适用于证明合同未成立的证据。","iracZh":{"issue":"法庭应该承认这个证词吗？","rule":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止先前或同时期的通信证据，这些通信补充或与完全整合的合同条款相矛盾（例如，由合并条款证明）。但该规则不适用于证明合同未成立的证据。","analysis":"包含合并条款的书面合同被认为是完整的整合，即双方对所有条款的协议的完整和最终表达。因此，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止补充或反驳书面内容的先前或同期通信的证据。然而，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）不适用于用来证明合同并未成立的证据——例如，因为合同存在的先决条件未能发生。在这里，代理人代表买方签署了购买飞机的书面合同。该合同包含一项合并条款，该条款通常会禁止提供双方先前就出售事宜进行讨论的任何证据。然而，代理人的证词显示，双方同意，在买方妻子批准之前，交易不会最终完成——这是合同存在的先决条件。因此，法院应采信证明因买方妻子不同意合同而导致合同不成立的证词（选项A）。 （选择B）即使证词与书面内容相矛盾，使双方的义务成为有条件的，该证词也不受口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的禁止，因为它是用来证明没有形成合同的。 （选项D）虽然合并条款创建了协议已完全整合的推定，但可以用协议仅部分整合的证据（即，对某些（但不是全部）条款的最终表述）来反驳该推定。因此，代理人的证词为此目的是可接受的。但也可以证明书面合同因先决条件未发生而从未成立。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止补充或矛盾完全整合的合同条款（例如，由合并条款证明）的先前或同期通信的证据。但该规则不适用于证明合同未成立的证据。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 214（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的例外）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止引入证据来证明合同并未成立。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"liquidated damages","ipa":"/lˈɪkwɪdˌeɪtɪd dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"liq·ui·dated dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"约定损害赔偿：缔约时合理预估并约定的违约赔偿额","contextualMeaning":"约定损害赔偿：缔约时合理预估并约定的违约赔偿额","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The contract contained a merger clause and called for a $10,000 deposit that the aircraft company could retain as liquidated damages if the buyer failed to accept delivery of the plane.","synonyms":["agreed damages","stipulated damages"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":49,"sourceQuestionPage":144,"sourceQuestionPages":[144],"sourceExplanationPages":[145],"sourceAnswerPages":[145],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-050","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"An actor straight out of drama school and an agent entered into a one-year written contract that described the services the agent would provide. Because he was eager for work, the actor agreed, in the contract, to pay the agent 15% of his yearly earnings. At the end of the year, the actor was so pleased with his many roles that he gave the agent 20% of his earnings. After the first contract had expired, the actor and the agent decided to continue working together. They photocopied their old contract, changed the date, and signed it. At the end of the year, a dispute arose as to what percentage of earnings the actor owed. It is a trade practice in the acting profession for actors to pay their agents 10% of their yearly earnings, payable at the end of the year. What percentage of the actor's earnings is a court most likely to award the agent?","choices":{"A":"20%, because course of dealing is given greater weight than trade usage.","B":"15%, because it was an express term of the contract.","C":"10%, because trade usage is the applicable default rule.","D":"Nothing, because the contract is too indefinite."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Course of performance, course of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding agreement performance between parties if:\n\n• agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by one party AND\n\n• other party accepts performance without objection Course of Sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between parties dealing that establishes common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n\nTrade usage Practice or method of dealing in parties' business or industry that is observed with enough regularity to justify expectation that it will be observed in instant case\n\nCourts adhere to rules of construction when interpreting contracts. The purpose of these rules is to assist the court in determining the meaning of ambiguous terms. Evidence relevant to this determination includes the contract's express terms, the parties' course of performance, the parties' course of dealing, and the industry's trade usage. When these are inconsistent with one another, priority is given as follows: 1. Express terms 2. Course of performance 3. Course of dealing 4. Trade usage Here, the second contract executed by the actor and the agent expressly stated that the agent would receive 15% of the actor's yearly earnings. Although the actor gave 20% of his yearly earnings to the agent when the first contract ended, this was not a course of dealing between the parties because it occurred only once (Choice A). And even though the acting profession's practice is to pay agents 10% of yearly earnings, an express term is given greater weight than trade usage when they are inconsistent. Therefore, a court will likely award the agent 15% (Choice C). (Choice D) A contract may be void if certain essential terms (eg, subject matter, consideration, duration) are indefinite. But here, the essential terms of the contract were not indefinite—ie, the subject matter was the agent's services, the consideration was 15% of the actor's yearly earnings, and the duration was one year.\n\nEducational objective: In interpreting contracts, (1) express terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 203 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (rules of construction to interpret a contract).","educationalObjective":"In interpreting contracts, (1) express terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 203 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (rules of construction to interpret a contract).","rule":"In interpreting contracts, (1) express terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage.","irac":{"issue":"What percentage of the actor's earnings is a court most likely to award the agent?","rule":"In interpreting contracts, (1) express terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage.","analysis":"Course of performance, course of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding agreement performance between parties if:\n\n• agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by one party AND\n\n• other party accepts performance without objection Course of Sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between parties dealing that establishes common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n\nTrade usage Practice or method of dealing in parties' business or industry that is observed with enough regularity to justify expectation that it will be observed in instant case\n\nCourts adhere to rules of construction when interpreting contracts. The purpose of these rules is to assist the court in determining the meaning of ambiguous terms. Evidence relevant to this determination includes the contract's express terms, the parties' course of performance, the parties' course of dealing, and the industry's trade usage. When these are inconsistent with one another, priority is given as follows: 1. Express terms 2. Course of performance 3. Course of dealing 4. Trade usage Here, the second contract executed by the actor and the agent expressly stated that the agent would receive 15% of the actor's yearly earnings. Although the actor gave 20% of his yearly earnings to the agent when the first contract ended, this was not a course of dealing between the parties because it occurred only once (Choice A). And even though the acting profession's practice is to pay agents 10% of yearly earnings, an express term is given greater weight than trade usage when they are inconsistent. Therefore, a court will likely award the agent 15% (Choice C). (Choice D) A contract may be void if certain essential terms (eg, subject matter, consideration, duration) are indefinite. But here, the essential terms of the contract were not indefinite—ie, the subject matter was the agent's services, the consideration was 15% of the actor's yearly earnings, and the duration was one year.","conclusion":"Answer B: 15%, because it was an express term of the contract."},"questionZh":"一位刚从戏剧学校毕业的演员和一位经纪人签订了一份为期一年的书面合同，其中描述了经纪人将提供的服务。由于对工作的渴望，这位演员在合同中同意向经纪人支付他年收入的15%。年底，这位演员对自己的多个角色非常满意，他将收入的 20% 交给了经纪人。第一份合同到期后，演员和经纪人决定继续合作。他们复印了旧合同，更改了日期，然后签署了。年底，关于演员所欠收入的比例出现了争议。演员向经纪人支付年收入的 10%，并在年底支付，这是演艺界的一种贸易惯例。法院最有可能判给经纪人的演员收入的百分比是多少？","choicesZh":{"A":"20%，因为交易过程比交易用途更重要。","B":"15%，因为这是合同的明确条款。","C":"10%，因为交易使用是适用的默认规则。","D":"没什么，因为合同太不确定了。"},"explanationZh":"表现过程、交易过程和贸易用途\n\n与了解双方协议履行情况相关的行为顺序过程，如果：\n\n• 协议涉及一方重复履行且\n\n• 另一方无异议地接受履行 交易各方之间先前交易的行为顺序过程，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n\n贸易用途 在当事人的业务或行业中进行交易的惯例或方法，具有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在即时案件中得到遵守的预期\n\n法院在解释合同时遵守解释规则。这些规则的目的是协助法院确定不明确术语的含义。与此确定相关的证据包括合同的明示条款、当事人的履约过程、当事人的交易过程以及行业的贸易惯例。当两者不一致时，优先考虑如下： 1. 明示条款 2. 履行过程 3. 交易过程 4. 交易用途 这里，演员与经纪人签订的第二份合同明确规定经纪人将获得演员年收入的 15%。尽管演员在第一份合同结束时将其年收入的 20% 交给了经纪人，但这并不是双方之间的交易过程，因为这种情况只发生过一次（选项 A）。尽管演艺界的惯例是向经纪人支付年收入的 10%，但当明示条款不一致时，明示条款比交易使用更重要。因此，法院可能会判给代理人 15%（选项 C）。 （选择D）如果某些基本条款（例如标的物、对价、期限）不明确，则合同可能无效。但在这里，合同的基本条款并不是无限期的——即标的物是经纪人的服务，对价是演员年收入的15%，期限是一年。\n\n教育目标：在解释合同时，(1) 明示条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易惯例，(3) 交易过程优先于贸易惯例。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 203（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（合同解释规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在解释合同时，(1) 明示条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易惯例，以及 (3) 交易过程优先于贸易惯例。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院最有可能判给经纪人的演员收入的百分比是多少？","rule":"在解释合同时，(1) 明示条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易惯例，以及 (3) 交易过程优先于贸易惯例。","analysis":"表现过程、交易过程和贸易用途\n\n与了解双方协议履行情况相关的行为顺序过程，如果：\n\n• 协议涉及一方重复履行且\n\n• 另一方无异议地接受履行 交易各方之间先前交易的行为顺序过程，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n\n贸易用途 在当事人的业务或行业中进行交易的惯例或方法，具有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在即时案件中得到遵守的预期\n\n法院在解释合同时遵守解释规则。这些规则的目的是协助法院确定不明确术语的含义。与此确定相关的证据包括合同的明示条款、当事人的履约过程、当事人的交易过程以及行业的贸易惯例。当两者不一致时，优先考虑如下： 1. 明示条款 2. 履行过程 3. 交易过程 4. 交易用途 这里，演员与经纪人签订的第二份合同明确规定经纪人将获得演员年收入的 15%。尽管演员在第一份合同结束时将其年收入的 20% 交给了经纪人，但这并不是双方之间的交易过程，因为这种情况只发生过一次（选项 A）。尽管演艺界的惯例是向经纪人支付年收入的 10%，但当明示条款不一致时，明示条款比交易使用更重要。因此，法院可能会判给代理人 15%（选项 C）。 （选择D）如果某些基本条款（例如标的物、对价、期限）不明确，则合同可能无效。但在这里，合同的基本条款并不是无限期的——即标的物是经纪人的服务，对价是演员年收入的15%，期限是一年。\n\n教育目标：在解释合同时，(1) 明示条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易惯例，(3) 交易过程优先于贸易惯例。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 203（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（合同解释规则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：15%，因为这是合同的明确条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":50,"sourceQuestionPage":146,"sourceQuestionPages":[146],"sourceExplanationPages":[147,148],"sourceAnswerPages":[147,148],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-051","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"A real estate developer solicited bids from general contractors to build residences on 50 lots owned by the developer. A general contractor, in turn, solicited bids from subcontractors for the various aspects of the construction process. A company submitted a bid of $1 million for performing the electrical work. This bid was based on a careless omission of the cost of an electrical component called for in the specifications prepared by the developer. The other bids received by the general contractor for the electrical work ranged from $1.25 million to $1.3 million, which were in line with the general contractor's own estimate for the electrical work. The general contractor used the company's bid of $1 million for performing the electrical work in submitting its bid to the developer. After the general contractor's bid was accepted by the developer and the general contractor, in turn, accepted the company's bid, but before any work on the residences began, the company discovered its error. Although the company will make no profit from the job instead of the $100,000 profit it would have made had it correctly read the specifications, enforcement of its contract with the general contractor would not be unconscionable. Is it likely that the company can avoid the contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company's bid was based on its own carelessness in computing its bid.","B":"No, because the enforcement of the contract would not be unconscionable.","C":"Yes, because the general contractor had reason to know of the mistake.","D":"Yes, because there was a misunderstanding that prevented the formation of the contract."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration, such as when the general contractor accepted the company's bid of $1 million for performing the electrical work. Although the company omitted the cost of an item when computing its bid, its unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid the contract unless:\n• the mistake would make enforcement of the contract against the nonmistaken party unconscionable (eg, due to a substantial difference between contract price and actual value) or\n\n• the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.\n\nHere, the facts stipulate that enforcement of the contract would not be unconscionable (Choice B). But the other bids received by the general contractor ranged from $1.25 million to $1.3 million—a 25% to 30% discrepancy from the company's bid. Since the other bids were in line with the general contractor's own estimate for the electrical work, the general contractor had reason to know that the company's $1 million bid was based on a mistake. As a result, the company can likely avoid the contract. (Choice A) The mistaken party's negligence with regard to its mistake (eg, the company's careless computing of its bid) does not prevent that party from avoiding the contract. (Choice D) A misunderstanding occurs when the parties attach different meanings to the same material term (eg, price). Here, the erroneous $1 million bid did not create a misunderstanding that would prevent contract formation since \"$1 million\" has just one meaning.\n\nEducational objective: Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when unilateral mistake makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when unilateral mistake makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"Is it likely that the company can avoid the contract?","rule":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","analysis":"A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration, such as when the general contractor accepted the company's bid of $1 million for performing the electrical work. Although the company omitted the cost of an item when computing its bid, its unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid the contract unless:\n• the mistake would make enforcement of the contract against the nonmistaken party unconscionable (eg, due to a substantial difference between contract price and actual value) or\n\n• the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.\n\nHere, the facts stipulate that enforcement of the contract would not be unconscionable (Choice B). But the other bids received by the general contractor ranged from $1.25 million to $1.3 million—a 25% to 30% discrepancy from the company's bid. Since the other bids were in line with the general contractor's own estimate for the electrical work, the general contractor had reason to know that the company's $1 million bid was based on a mistake. As a result, the company can likely avoid the contract. (Choice A) The mistaken party's negligence with regard to its mistake (eg, the company's careless computing of its bid) does not prevent that party from avoiding the contract. (Choice D) A misunderstanding occurs when the parties attach different meanings to the same material term (eg, price). Here, the erroneous $1 million bid did not create a misunderstanding that would prevent contract formation since \"$1 million\" has just one meaning.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the general contractor had reason to know of the mistake."},"questionZh":"一家房地产开发商向总承包商招标，在其拥有的 50 块土地上建造住宅。总承包商则向分包商就施工过程的各个方面进行招标。一家公司提交了 100 万美元的投标，用于执行电力工作。该投标是基于开发商制定的规格中所要求的电气元件成本的粗心遗漏。总承建商接获的电力工程其他投标金额介乎125万元至130万元，与总承建商本身对电力工程的预算相符。总承包商在向开发商提交投标时使用了该公司 100 万美元的投标价格来执行电力工程。在总承包商的投标被开发商接受后，总承包商又接受了公司的投标，但在住宅工程开始之前，公司发现了自己的错误。尽管该公司不会从这项工作中获得任何利润，而如果它正确阅读了规范，它本可以赚取 100,000 美元的利润，但执行与总承包商的合同并非不合理。公司有可能撤销合同吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该公司的出价是基于其计算出价时的粗心大意。","B":"不，因为合同的执行不会不合理。","C":"是的，因为总承包商有理由知道这个错误。","D":"是的，因为存在误解，导致合同无法成立。"},"explanationZh":"当双方达成一致且有充分约因的情况下，例如总承包商接受了公司 100 万美元的电力工程投标时，通常就会形成有效的合同。尽管该公司在计算投标时遗漏了某件商品的成本，但其单方面错误并不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非：\n• 该错误会导致对无错误方的合同执行不合理（例如，由于合同价格与实际价值之间存在巨大差异）或\n\n• 无错方造成、或知道或有理由知道错误。\n\n此处，事实证明合同的执行不会不合理（选项B）。但总承包商收到的其他出价从 125 万美元到 130 万美元不等，与公司的出价相差 25% 到 30%。由于其他投标与总承包商自己对电力工程的估算一致，因此总承包商有理由知道该公司 100 万美元的投标是基于错误。因此，该公司很可能会撤销该合同。 （选择A） 错误方对其错误的疏忽（例如，公司粗心地计算其出价）并不妨碍该方撤销合同。 （选项D） 当双方对同一重要术语（例如价格）赋予不同的含义时，就会发生误解。在这里，错误的 100 万美元出价并没有造成妨碍合同成立的误解，因为“100 万美元”只有一个含义。\n\n教育目标：单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当单方面错误导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 非错误方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。","iracZh":{"issue":"公司有可能撤销合同吗？","rule":"单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 非错误方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。","analysis":"当双方达成一致且有充分约因的情况下，例如总承包商接受了公司 100 万美元的电力工程投标时，通常就会形成有效的合同。尽管该公司在计算投标时遗漏了某件商品的成本，但其单方面错误并不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非：\n• 该错误会导致对无错误方的合同执行不合理（例如，由于合同价格与实际价值之间存在巨大差异）或\n\n• 无错方造成、或知道或有理由知道错误。\n\n此处，事实证明合同的执行不会不合理（选项B）。但总承包商收到的其他出价从 125 万美元到 130 万美元不等，与公司的出价相差 25% 到 30%。由于其他投标与总承包商自己对电力工程的估算一致，因此总承包商有理由知道该公司 100 万美元的投标是基于错误。因此，该公司很可能会撤销该合同。 （选择A） 错误方对其错误的疏忽（例如，公司粗心地计算其出价）并不妨碍该方撤销合同。 （选项D） 当双方对同一重要术语（例如价格）赋予不同的含义时，就会发生误解。在这里，错误的 100 万美元出价并没有造成妨碍合同成立的误解，因为“100 万美元”只有一个含义。\n\n教育目标：单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当单方面错误导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为总承包商有理由知道这个错误。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"unilateral mistake","ipa":"/jˌunʌlˈætɝəl mɪstˈeɪk/","syllables":"uni·lat·eral mis·take","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"单方错误：仅一方对基本事实产生重大错误","contextualMeaning":"单方错误：仅一方对基本事实产生重大错误","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-4cdc797ecb06c57c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":51,"sourceQuestionPage":149,"sourceQuestionPages":[149],"sourceExplanationPages":[150,151],"sourceAnswerPages":[150,151],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-052","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"A landowner advertised a proposed sale of standing timber on a choice 2,000-acre tract. A construction enterprise offered a fair price for the timber rights in question. The landowner accepted the offer and the parties entered into a written contract. Later, without legal excuse and over the landowner's strong objection, the construction enterprise repudiated the contract before commencing performance. The landowner could not afford to hire a lawyer and take legal action, and he made no attempt to assign any cause of action he might have had against the construction enterprise. If the construction enterprise is sued for breach of the contract by the landowner's next- door neighbor, whose view of a nearby lake is obscured by the standing timber, will the neighbor prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the neighbor is a maintainer of nuisance litigation.","B":"No, because the neighbor is only an incidental beneficiary, if any, of the contract.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor is a surrogate for the landowner in view of his inability to enforce the contract.","D":"Yes, because the neighbor is a third-party intended beneficiary of the contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. Unlike intended beneficiaries, incidental beneficiaries—ie, those whom the contracting parties did not intend to benefit directly—have no right to sue under the contract. Here, the neighbor is at most an incidental beneficiary of the contract between the landowner and the construction enterprise since there was no contractual intent to benefit the neighbor (eg, improve the neighbor's view of the lake). As an incidental beneficiary, the neighbor has no right to sue under the contract. Therefore, the neighbor will not prevail in a breach-of-contract suit against the construction enterprise (Choice D). (Choice A) The neighbor would not be accused of maintaining nuisance litigation (ie, bringing a bad-faith claim to harass someone) since the neighbor presumably believed (incorrectly) that he/she had the right to enforce the contract. (Choice C) A surrogate is one who acts on behalf of another party. But here, the neighbor has no right under the contract or otherwise to enforce the contract on the landowner's behalf.\n\nEducational objective: An incidental beneficiary—ie, a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract even though the contracting parties did not intend to benefit him/her—has no right to sue under the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended & incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"An incidental beneficiary—ie, a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract even though the contracting parties did not intend to benefit him/her—has no right to sue under the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended & incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"An incidental beneficiary—ie, a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract even though the contracting parties did not intend to benefit him/her—has no right to sue under the contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the construction enterprise is sued for breach of the contract by the landowner's next- door neighbor, whose view of a nearby lake is obscured by the standing timber, will the neighbor prevail?","rule":"An incidental beneficiary—ie, a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract even though the contracting parties did not intend to benefit him/her—has no right to sue under the contract.","analysis":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. Unlike intended beneficiaries, incidental beneficiaries—ie, those whom the contracting parties did not intend to benefit directly—have no right to sue under the contract. Here, the neighbor is at most an incidental beneficiary of the contract between the landowner and the construction enterprise since there was no contractual intent to benefit the neighbor (eg, improve the neighbor's view of the lake). As an incidental beneficiary, the neighbor has no right to sue under the contract. Therefore, the neighbor will not prevail in a breach-of-contract suit against the construction enterprise (Choice D). (Choice A) The neighbor would not be accused of maintaining nuisance litigation (ie, bringing a bad-faith claim to harass someone) since the neighbor presumably believed (incorrectly) that he/she had the right to enforce the contract. (Choice C) A surrogate is one who acts on behalf of another party. But here, the neighbor has no right under the contract or otherwise to enforce the contract on the landowner's behalf.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the neighbor is only an incidental beneficiary, if any, of the contract."},"questionZh":"一位土地所有者在广告中提议出售一块 2,000 英亩的精选土地上的立木。某建筑企业就涉案林权提出了合理价格。土地所有者接受了要约，双方签订了书面合同。后来，施工企业在没有合法理由的情况下，不顾土地所有者的强烈反对，在合同开始履行前就毁约了。土地所有者无力聘请律师并采取法律行动，他也没有试图指定他可能对建筑企业提出的任何诉讼理由。如果地主的邻居因立木挡住了附近湖泊的视线而起诉建筑企业违约，邻居会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为邻居是滋扰诉讼的维护者。","B":"不，因为邻居只是合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（如果有的话）。","C":"是的，因为鉴于土地所有者无力执行合同，邻居是土地所有者的代理人。","D":"是的，因为邻居是合同的第三方预期受益人。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人。与预期受益人不同，偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（即缔约方不打算直接受益的人）无权根据合同提起诉讼。这里，邻居最多只是土地所有者与建筑企业之间合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），因为合同中并没有让邻居受益的意图（例如，改善邻居的湖景）。作为偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），邻居无权根据合同提起诉讼。因此，邻居对建筑企业提起违约诉讼时不会胜诉（选项D）。 （选择A）邻居不会被指控维持滋扰诉讼（即提出恶意索赔以骚扰某人），因为邻居可能（错误地）相信他/她有权执行合同。 （选项 C）代理人是代表另一方行事的人。但在这里，邻居无权根据合同或其他方式代表土地所有者执行合同。\n\n教育目标：偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（即，即使缔约方无意使他/她受益，但从合同中获得某些好处或利益的合同非当事人）无权根据合同提起诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（即，即使缔约方无意使他/她受益，但从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人）无权根据合同提起诉讼。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果地主的邻居因立木挡住了附近湖泊的视线而起诉建筑企业违约，邻居会胜诉吗？","rule":"偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（即，即使缔约方无意使他/她受益，但从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人）无权根据合同提起诉讼。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人。与预期受益人不同，偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（即缔约方不打算直接受益的人）无权根据合同提起诉讼。这里，邻居最多只是土地所有者与建筑企业之间合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），因为合同中并没有让邻居受益的意图（例如，改善邻居的湖景）。作为偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），邻居无权根据合同提起诉讼。因此，邻居对建筑企业提起违约诉讼时不会胜诉（选项D）。 （选择A）邻居不会被指控维持滋扰诉讼（即提出恶意索赔以骚扰某人），因为邻居可能（错误地）相信他/她有权执行合同。 （选项 C）代理人是代表另一方行事的人。但在这里，邻居无权根据合同或其他方式代表土地所有者执行合同。\n\n教育目标：偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（即，即使缔约方无意使他/她受益，但从合同中获得某些好处或利益的合同非当事人）无权根据合同提起诉讼。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为邻居只是合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（如果有的话）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-11777eced73cc39a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":52,"sourceQuestionPage":152,"sourceQuestionPages":[152],"sourceExplanationPages":[153,154],"sourceAnswerPages":[153,154],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-053","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"Before putting her home up for sale, a homeowner painted the living room ceiling to conceal major water damage caused by a leaking roof that had not yet been repaired. On the first day the home was offered for sale, the homeowner gave a buyer a personal tour. The homeowner made no statements at all regarding the water damage or the roof. Without discovering the water damage or the leaking roof and without consulting a lawyer, the buyer immediately agreed in writing to buy the home for $200,000. Before the closing date, the buyer discovered the water damage and the leaking roof. The cost of repair was estimated at $22,000. The buyer has refused to go through with the purchase. If the homeowner sues the buyer for breach of contract, is the homeowner likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because no contract was formed since the buyer did not have a real opportunity to understand the essential terms of the contract.","B":"No, because the homeowner concealed evidence of the water damage and of the leaking roof.","C":"Yes, because the buyer acted unreasonably by failing to employ an inspector to conduct an independent inspection of the home.","D":"Yes, because the homeowner made no affirmative statements of fact about the water damage or the leaking roof."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When reliance on misrepresentation is justified\n\nFact If untrue assertion is presented as fact, party's reliance justified unless: (almost always justified)\n• assertion is patently false or\n• party knows assertion is false Opinion* If untrue assertion is presented as opinion, party's reliance justified only if: (rarely justified)\n• facts as speaker understands them are consistent with or justify opinion\n• relationship of trust & confidence exists\n• party reasonably believes speaker has special expertise or\n• party is particularly susceptible to type of misrepresentation involved *A statement of opinion is also a statement of fact—and can therefore be a misrepresentation—because it states that a person has a particular state of mind.\n\nMisrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact made by words or conduct (eg, concealment—an act intended to prevent discovery of facts). When this occurs, a contract is voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Here, the homeowner painted the living room ceiling to conceal water damage and a leaking roof. As a result of this fraudulent misrepresentation, the buyer decided to purchase the home. And since the homeowner never mentioned the water damage or roof during the personal tour, the buyer justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Therefore, the buyer can avoid the contract and the homeowner is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) No contract is formed (ie, the apparent contract is void) if a fraudulent misrepresentation prevents a party from knowing the character or essential terms of the transaction and the party did not have a reasonable opportunity to understand those terms. But there is no evidence that the buyer did not understand the essential terms of the contract here.\n\n(Choice C) A party's reliance on a misrepresentation is justified unless the misrepresentation is patently false (eg, if the water damage was visible) or the party knows it to be false. Therefore, the buyer's failure to inspect the home does not bar relief. (Choice D) An affirmative statement is not required for a misrepresentation to occur. Affirmative conduct intended to prevent another from discovering a fact (eg, concealment) also qualifies as a misrepresentation.\n\nEducational objective: A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","rule":"A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.","irac":{"issue":"If the homeowner sues the buyer for breach of contract, is the homeowner likely to prevail?","rule":"A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.","analysis":"When reliance on misrepresentation is justified\n\nFact If untrue assertion is presented as fact, party's reliance justified unless: (almost always justified)\n• assertion is patently false or\n• party knows assertion is false Opinion* If untrue assertion is presented as opinion, party's reliance justified only if: (rarely justified)\n• facts as speaker understands them are consistent with or justify opinion\n• relationship of trust & confidence exists\n• party reasonably believes speaker has special expertise or\n• party is particularly susceptible to type of misrepresentation involved *A statement of opinion is also a statement of fact—and can therefore be a misrepresentation—because it states that a person has a particular state of mind.\n\nMisrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact made by words or conduct (eg, concealment—an act intended to prevent discovery of facts). When this occurs, a contract is voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Here, the homeowner painted the living room ceiling to conceal water damage and a leaking roof. As a result of this fraudulent misrepresentation, the buyer decided to purchase the home. And since the homeowner never mentioned the water damage or roof during the personal tour, the buyer justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Therefore, the buyer can avoid the contract and the homeowner is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) No contract is formed (ie, the apparent contract is void) if a fraudulent misrepresentation prevents a party from knowing the character or essential terms of the transaction and the party did not have a reasonable opportunity to understand those terms. But there is no evidence that the buyer did not understand the essential terms of the contract here.\n\n(Choice C) A party's reliance on a misrepresentation is justified unless the misrepresentation is patently false (eg, if the water damage was visible) or the party knows it to be false. Therefore, the buyer's failure to inspect the home does not bar relief. (Choice D) An affirmative statement is not required for a misrepresentation to occur. Affirmative conduct intended to prevent another from discovering a fact (eg, concealment) also qualifies as a misrepresentation.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the homeowner concealed evidence of the water damage and of the leaking roof."},"questionZh":"在出售房屋之前，一位房主对客厅的天花板进行了粉刷，以掩盖尚未修复的屋顶漏水造成的严重水灾。房屋出售的第一天，房主亲自带买家参观了房屋。房主没有就水灾或屋顶问题发表任何声明。在没有发现水灾或屋顶漏水的情况下，也没有咨询律师，买家立即书面同意以 20 万美元的价格购买该房屋。在交割日期之前，买方发现了水灾和屋顶漏水。维修费用估计为 22,000 美元。买方拒绝完成购买。如果房主起诉买家违约，房主胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为买方没有真正的机会了解合同的基本条款，因此合同并未成立。","B":"不，因为房主隐瞒了水灾和屋顶漏水的证据。","C":"是的，因为买方行为不合理，没有聘请检查员对房屋进行独立检查。","D":"是的，因为房主没有对水灾或屋顶漏水的事实做出肯定的陈述。"},"explanationZh":"当有理由相信虚假陈述时\n\n事实 如果不真实的断言被呈现为事实，则当事人的信赖是合理的，除非：（几乎总是合理的）\n• 断言明显错误或\n• 当事人知道断言是错误的 意见* 如果以意见的形式提出不真实的断言，则当事人的信赖仅在以下情况下才合理：（很少有正当理由）\n• 说话者理解的事实与观点一致或证明观点合理\n• 存在信任和信心的关系\n• 当事人合理地认为发言者具有特殊的专业知识或\n• 当事人特别容易受到所涉及的虚假陈述类型的影响 *意见陈述也是事实陈述，因此可能是虚假陈述，因为它表明一个人具有特定的心态。\n\n失实陈述是通过言语或行为对事实进行不真实的陈述（例如，隐瞒——旨在阻止发现事实的行为）。发生这种情况时，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。房主在这里对客厅天花板进行了粉刷，以掩盖水渍和屋顶漏水的情况。由于这种欺诈性的失实陈述，买家决定购买该房屋。由于房主在亲自参观期间从未提及水灾或屋顶问题，因此买家有理由相信这种虚假陈述。因此，买方可以撤销合同，而房主也不太可能胜诉。 （选择A）如果欺诈性失实陈述使一方无法了解交易的特征或基本条款，并且该方没有合理的机会理解这些条款，则合同不成立（即表面上的合同无效）。但没有证据表明买方不理解此处合同的基本条款。\n\n（选择C）一方当事人对虚假陈述的信赖是合理的，除非该虚假陈述明显是虚假的（例如，如果水灾是可见的）或者该方知道它是虚假的。因此，买方未能检查房屋并不妨碍救济。 （选项 D） 错误陈述的发生不需要肯定性陈述。旨在阻止他人发现事实的肯定行为（例如隐瞒）也属于失实陈述。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同因虚假陈述而无效：(1) 虚假陈述具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 诱导同意合同；(3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同因虚假陈述而无效，如果 (1) 虚假陈述是欺诈性的或重大的，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果房主起诉买家违约，房主胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"合同因虚假陈述而无效，如果 (1) 虚假陈述是欺诈性的或重大的，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。","analysis":"当有理由相信虚假陈述时\n\n事实 如果不真实的断言被呈现为事实，则当事人的信赖是合理的，除非：（几乎总是合理的）\n• 断言明显错误或\n• 当事人知道断言是错误的 意见* 如果以意见的形式提出不真实的断言，则当事人的信赖仅在以下情况下才合理：（很少有正当理由）\n• 说话者理解的事实与观点一致或证明观点合理\n• 存在信任和信心的关系\n• 当事人合理地认为发言者具有特殊的专业知识或\n• 当事人特别容易受到所涉及的虚假陈述类型的影响 *意见陈述也是事实陈述，因此可能是虚假陈述，因为它表明一个人具有特定的心态。\n\n失实陈述是通过言语或行为对事实进行不真实的陈述（例如，隐瞒——旨在阻止发现事实的行为）。发生这种情况时，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。房主在这里对客厅天花板进行了粉刷，以掩盖水渍和屋顶漏水的情况。由于这种欺诈性的失实陈述，买家决定购买该房屋。由于房主在亲自参观期间从未提及水灾或屋顶问题，因此买家有理由相信这种虚假陈述。因此，买方可以撤销合同，而房主也不太可能胜诉。 （选择A）如果欺诈性失实陈述使一方无法了解交易的特征或基本条款，并且该方没有合理的机会理解这些条款，则合同不成立（即表面上的合同无效）。但没有证据表明买方不理解此处合同的基本条款。\n\n（选择C）一方当事人对虚假陈述的信赖是合理的，除非该虚假陈述明显是虚假的（例如，如果水灾是可见的）或者该方知道它是虚假的。因此，买方未能检查房屋并不妨碍救济。 （选项 D） 错误陈述的发生不需要肯定性陈述。旨在阻止他人发现事实的肯定行为（例如隐瞒）也属于失实陈述。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同因虚假陈述而无效：(1) 虚假陈述具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 诱导同意合同；(3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为房主隐瞒了水灾和屋顶漏水的证据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":53,"sourceQuestionPage":155,"sourceQuestionPages":[155],"sourceExplanationPages":[156,157],"sourceAnswerPages":[156,157],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-054","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"The owner of a fleet of taxis contracted with a dealer in petroleum products for the purchase and sale of the fleet owner's total requirements of gasoline and oil for one year. As part of that agreement, the petroleum dealer also agreed with the fleet owner that for one year the petroleum dealer would place all his advertising with the fleet owner's wife, who owned her own small advertising agency. When the wife was informed of the contract, she declined to accept an advertising account from another company because she could not handle both accounts during the same year. The fleet owner performed his contract with the petroleum dealer for six months, and during that time the petroleum dealer placed his advertising with the wife. At the end of the six months, the fleet owner and the wife were divorced, and the fleet owner then told the petroleum dealer that he had no further obligation to place his advertising with the fleet owner's now ex-wife. The petroleum dealer thereupon notified the ex-wife that he would no longer place his advertising with her. In an action by the wife against the petroleum dealer for breach of contract, will the wife likely succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because absent a provision in the contract to the contrary, the promisor and promisee of a third-party beneficiary contract retain by law the right to modify or terminate the contract.","B":"No, because the agency relationship, if any, between the fleet owner and the wife terminated upon their divorce.","C":"Yes, because the fleet owner acted in bad faith in releasing the petroleum dealer from his duty with respect to the fleet owner's wife.","D":"Yes, because the petroleum dealer and the fleet owner could not, without the wife's consent, modify their contract so as to discharge the petroleum dealer's duties to the wife."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. If the contracting parties intended for the contract to benefit the third party, the third party is an intended beneficiary. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries have enforceable rights under the contract. Those rights vest when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Once this occurs, the original contracting parties are both bound to perform the contract. Any effort to rescind or modify the contract after vesting is void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification. Here, the wife was an intended beneficiary of the contract between the fleet owner and the petroleum dealer because the contract provided that the petroleum dealer would use the wife's advertising agency for a year. The wife detrimentally relied on the contract when she declined another account because she could not handle two accounts at the same time— vesting her rights. As a result, she will likely succeed because the fleet owner cannot rescind (ie, cancel) or modify the contract without the now ex-wife's consent (Choice A). (Choice B) The fleet owner was not acting as an agent for the wife because she did not direct him to enter into the contract on her behalf. Instead, the wife was an intended beneficiary of the contract, and her consent was required to modify the contract— regardless of the divorce proceeding. (Choice C) Because the wife's contractual rights had vested, the fleet owner could not in good or bad faith release the petroleum dealer from his duty with respect to the wife.\n\nEducational objective: An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any effort to rescind or modify the contract after vesting is void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (variation of a duty to a beneficiary).","educationalObjective":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any effort to rescind or modify the contract after vesting is void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (variation of a duty to a beneficiary).","rule":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any effort to rescind or modify the contract after vesting is void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the wife against the petroleum dealer for breach of contract, will the wife likely succeed?","rule":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) files a lawsuit to enforce it. Any effort to rescind or modify the contract after vesting is void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. If the contracting parties intended for the contract to benefit the third party, the third party is an intended beneficiary. Only intended (not incidental) beneficiaries have enforceable rights under the contract. Those rights vest when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Once this occurs, the original contracting parties are both bound to perform the contract. Any effort to rescind or modify the contract after vesting is void unless the beneficiary agrees to the rescission or modification. Here, the wife was an intended beneficiary of the contract between the fleet owner and the petroleum dealer because the contract provided that the petroleum dealer would use the wife's advertising agency for a year. The wife detrimentally relied on the contract when she declined another account because she could not handle two accounts at the same time— vesting her rights. As a result, she will likely succeed because the fleet owner cannot rescind (ie, cancel) or modify the contract without the now ex-wife's consent (Choice A). (Choice B) The fleet owner was not acting as an agent for the wife because she did not direct him to enter into the contract on her behalf. Instead, the wife was an intended beneficiary of the contract, and her consent was required to modify the contract— regardless of the divorce proceeding. (Choice C) Because the wife's contractual rights had vested, the fleet owner could not in good or bad faith release the petroleum dealer from his duty with respect to the wife.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the petroleum dealer and the fleet owner could not, without the wife's consent, modify their contract so as to discharge the petroleum dealer's duties to the wife."},"questionZh":"出租车车队的所有者与石油产品经销商签订了合同，购买和销售车队所有者一年内的汽油和石油总需求。作为该协议的一部分，石油经销商还与车队所有者达成一致，在一年内，石油经销商将把所有广告投放给车队所有者的妻子，她拥有自己的小型广告公司。当妻子得知合同后，她拒绝接受另一家公司的广告帐户，因为她无法在同一年处理这两个帐户。车队所有者与石油经销商履行了六个月的合同，在此期间石油经销商将他的广告投放给了妻子。六个月后，车队所有者和妻子离婚，车队所有者随后告诉石油经销商，他没有进一步的义务将广告投放到车队所有者现在的前妻身上。石油商随即通知前妻，不再向她投放广告。妻子向石油商提起违约诉讼，妻子会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合同中没有相反规定，第三方受益合同的承诺人和受诺人依法保留修改或终止合同的权利。","B":"不可以，因为车队所有者和妻子之间的代理关系（如果有的话）在离婚时终止。","C":"是的，因为船队所有者恶意地解除了石油经销商对船队所有者妻子的责任。","D":"是的，因为未经妻子同意，石油经销商和车队所有者不能修改合同以解除石油经销商对妻子的义务。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。如果缔约方意图使合同使第三方受益，则第三方为预期受益人。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才拥有合同项下的可执行权利。当受益人符合以下条件时，这些权利即归属：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。一旦发生这种情况，原合同双方都有义务履行合同。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。在这里，妻子是车队所有者和石油经销商之间合同的预期受益人，因为合同规定石油经销商将使用妻子的广告公司一年。当妻子拒绝另一个账户时，她对合同的依赖是不利的，因为她无法同时处理两个账户——赋予她的权利。因此，她很可能会成功，因为未经前妻同意，车队所有者不能撤销（即取消）或修改合同（选择 A）。 （选项B） 船东不是妻子的代理人，因为她没有指示他代表她签订合同。相反，妻子是合同的预期受益人，无论离婚程序如何，修改合同都需要得到她的同意。 （选项C） 由于妻子的合同权利已经既定，车队所有者无论善意还是恶意都不能免除石油经销商对妻子的义务。\n\n教育目标：当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所创建的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 311（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受益人的义务的变更）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。","iracZh":{"issue":"妻子向石油商提起违约诉讼，妻子会胜诉吗？","rule":"当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。如果缔约方意图使合同使第三方受益，则第三方为预期受益人。只有预期（而非偶然）受益人才拥有合同项下的可执行权利。当受益人符合以下条件时，这些权利即归属：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。一旦发生这种情况，原合同双方都有义务履行合同。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。在这里，妻子是车队所有者和石油经销商之间合同的预期受益人，因为合同规定石油经销商将使用妻子的广告公司一年。当妻子拒绝另一个账户时，她对合同的依赖是不利的，因为她无法同时处理两个账户——赋予她的权利。因此，她很可能会成功，因为未经前妻同意，车队所有者不能撤销（即取消）或修改合同（选择 A）。 （选项B） 船东不是妻子的代理人，因为她没有指示他代表她签订合同。相反，妻子是合同的预期受益人，无论离婚程序如何，修改合同都需要得到她的同意。 （选项C） 由于妻子的合同权利已经既定，车队所有者无论善意还是恶意都不能免除石油经销商对妻子的义务。\n\n教育目标：当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所创建的权利，(2) 明确同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。行权后任何解除或修改合同的行为均属无效，除非受益人同意解除或修改。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 311（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受益人的义务的变更）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为未经妻子同意，石油经销商和车队所有者不能修改合同以解除石油经销商对妻子的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":54,"sourceQuestionPage":158,"sourceQuestionPages":[158],"sourceExplanationPages":[159,160],"sourceAnswerPages":[159,160],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-055","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A buyer and a seller entered into a written contract for the sale of a copy machine, using the same form contract that they had used a number of times in the past. The contract stated that payment was due 30 days after delivery and provided that the writing contained the complete and exclusive statement of the parties' agreement. On several past occasions, the buyer had taken a 5% discount from the contract price when paying within 10 days of delivery, and the seller had not objected. On this occasion, when the buyer took a 5% discount for paying within 10 days, the seller objected because his profit margin on this particular machine was smaller than on his other machines. If the seller sues the buyer for breach of contract, may the buyer introduce evidence that the 5% discount was a term of the agreement?","choices":{"A":"No, because the seller timely objected to the buyer's proposal for different terms.","B":"No, because the writing contained the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties.","C":"Yes, because a modification made in good faith does not require consideration.","D":"Yes, because evidence of course of dealing is admissible even if the writing contains the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of a prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But the following evidence can be used to explain or supplement those terms, regardless of whether the agreement is completely or partially integrated:*\n• Course of performance – sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding an agreement between the parties if (1) the agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by a party and (2) the other party accepts performance without objection\n• Course of dealing – sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between the parties that establishes a common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n• Trade usage – any practice or method of dealing in the parties' business or industry that is practiced with enough regularity to justify an expectation that it will be practiced in the instant case Here, on several past occasions, the buyer had taken a 5% discount from the contract price when paying within 10 days of delivery, and the seller had not objected. This established a course of dealing between the parties that can be used to supplement the contract at issue, which was silent regarding discounts for early payment. Therefore, evidence of the 5% discount is admissible even if the writing contains the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties (ie, even if the writing is completely integrated) (Choice B). *Priority is given as follows: (1) express contractual terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage. (Choice A) There was an established course of dealing between the parties, so the seller's objection to the 5% discount—after the buyer had acted in accordance with the course of dealing—was ineffective. (Choice C) Under the UCC, a good-faith modification is enforceable without consideration. But the enforceability of a modification is not at issue here.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC parol evidence rule always allows course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement— even one that is completely integrated.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"The UCC parol evidence rule always allows course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement— even one that is completely integrated.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule always allows course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement— even one that is completely integrated.","irac":{"issue":"If the seller sues the buyer for breach of contract, may the buyer introduce evidence that the 5% discount was a term of the agreement?","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule always allows course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement— even one that is completely integrated.","analysis":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of a prior agreement (or contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But the following evidence can be used to explain or supplement those terms, regardless of whether the agreement is completely or partially integrated:*\n• Course of performance – sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding an agreement between the parties if (1) the agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by a party and (2) the other party accepts performance without objection\n• Course of dealing – sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between the parties that establishes a common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n• Trade usage – any practice or method of dealing in the parties' business or industry that is practiced with enough regularity to justify an expectation that it will be practiced in the instant case Here, on several past occasions, the buyer had taken a 5% discount from the contract price when paying within 10 days of delivery, and the seller had not objected. This established a course of dealing between the parties that can be used to supplement the contract at issue, which was silent regarding discounts for early payment. Therefore, evidence of the 5% discount is admissible even if the writing contains the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties (ie, even if the writing is completely integrated) (Choice B). *Priority is given as follows: (1) express contractual terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage. (Choice A) There was an established course of dealing between the parties, so the seller's objection to the 5% discount—after the buyer had acted in accordance with the course of dealing—was ineffective. (Choice C) Under the UCC, a good-faith modification is enforceable without consideration. But the enforceability of a modification is not at issue here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because evidence of course of dealing is admissible even if the writing contains the complete and exclusive agreement of the parties."},"questionZh":"买方和卖方签订了一份复印机销售书面合同，所用的合同格式与他们过去多次使用的合同格式相同。合同规定付款期限为交货后 30 天，并规定书面文件包含双方协议的完整且唯一的声明。过去几次，买方在交货后10天内付款时，都曾在合同价格的基础上享受5%的折扣，卖方也没有反对。这次，当买方在 10 天内付款时享受 5% 的折扣时，卖方提出了反对，因为他在这台特定机器上的利润率低于他在其他机器上的利润率。如果卖方起诉买方违约，买方是否可以提供证据证明 5% 折扣是协议条款？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为卖方及时反对了买方提出的不同条款的建议。","B":"不可以，因为书面文件包含双方的完整且排他的协议。","C":"是的，因为善意的修改不需要约因。","D":"是的，因为即使书面文件包含双方的完整且排他性协议，交易过程的证据也是可接受的。"},"explanationZh":"根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前协议（或同期口头协议）的证据不能用来与最终书面协议的条款相矛盾。但无论协议是否完全或部分整合，以下证据均可用于解释或补充这些条款：*\n• 履行过程 – 与理解双方之间的协议相关的行为顺序，如果 (1) 协议涉及一方重复履行且 (2) 另一方无异议地接受履行\n• 交易过程——双方之间先前交易的行为顺序，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n• 贸易使用——在双方业务或行业中进行交易的任何惯例或方法，其惯例或方法具有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在当前案件中惯例。在此，过去多次，买方在交货后 10 天内付款时获得了合同价格 5% 的折扣，并且卖方没有反对。这在双方之间建立了一个交易过程，可用于补充有争议的合同，该合同没有提及提前付款的折扣。因此，即使书面材料包含双方完整且排他性的协议（即，即使书面材料完全完整），5%折扣的证据也是可接受的（选择B）。 *优先顺序如下：(1) 明示合同条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易使用，以及 (3) 交易过程优先于贸易使用。 （选项A） 双方之间已有既定的交易程序，因此在买方按照交易程序行事后，卖方对5%折扣的反对无效。 （选项 C）根据 UCC，善意修改无需约因即可强制执行。但修改的可执行性并不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款，即使是完全整合的协议。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款——即使是完全整合的协议。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果卖方起诉买方违约，买方是否可以提供证据证明 5% 折扣是协议条款？","rule":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款——即使是完全整合的协议。","analysis":"根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前协议（或同期口头协议）的证据不能用来与最终书面协议的条款相矛盾。但无论协议是否完全或部分整合，以下证据均可用于解释或补充这些条款：*\n• 履行过程 – 与理解双方之间的协议相关的行为顺序，如果 (1) 协议涉及一方重复履行且 (2) 另一方无异议地接受履行\n• 交易过程——双方之间先前交易的行为顺序，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n• 贸易使用——在双方业务或行业中进行交易的任何惯例或方法，其惯例或方法具有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在当前案件中惯例。在此，过去多次，买方在交货后 10 天内付款时获得了合同价格 5% 的折扣，并且卖方没有反对。这在双方之间建立了一个交易过程，可用于补充有争议的合同，该合同没有提及提前付款的折扣。因此，即使书面材料包含双方完整且排他性的协议（即，即使书面材料完全完整），5%折扣的证据也是可接受的（选择B）。 *优先顺序如下：(1) 明示合同条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易使用，以及 (3) 交易过程优先于贸易使用。 （选项A） 双方之间已有既定的交易程序，因此在买方按照交易程序行事后，卖方对5%折扣的反对无效。 （选项 C）根据 UCC，善意修改无需约因即可强制执行。但修改的可执行性并不是这里的问题。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款，即使是完全整合的协议。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为即使书面文件包含双方的完整且排他性协议，交易过程的证据也是可接受的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"different terms","ipa":"/dˈɪfɝənt tˈɝmz/","syllables":"dif·fer·ent terms","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不同条款：承诺中与要约直接冲突的条款","contextualMeaning":"不同条款：承诺中与要约直接冲突的条款","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the seller timely objected to the buyer's proposal for different terms.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-a30e64c310b03a73"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":55,"sourceQuestionPage":161,"sourceQuestionPages":[161],"sourceExplanationPages":[162,163],"sourceAnswerPages":[162,163],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-056","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Capacity & Ratification","question":"A buyer, who was a minor both in fact and appearance, bought on credit and took delivery of a telescope from a 30-year-old seller for an agreed price of $100. Soon after reaching the age of majority, the buyer encountered the seller and said, \"I am sorry for not having paid you that $100 for the telescope when the money was due, but I found out it was only worth $75. So I now promise to pay you $75.\" The buyer subsequently repudiated her promise and refused to pay the seller anything. In an action for breach of contract by the seller against the buyer, what amount of damages will the seller likely recover?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the buyer was a minor at the time of the original transaction.","B":"Nothing, because there was no consideration for the promise made by the buyer after reaching majority.","C":"$75.","D":"$100."},"answer":"C","explanation":"An unmarried, unemancipated minor (ie, person under the age of 18) lacks capacity to enter a contract. This means that any contract the minor enters is voidable, so the minor can either:\n• void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming it through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under it or\n\n• ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) agreeing to be bound by it.\n\nThe contract becomes enforceable if the minor ratifies it. However, if the ratification is made by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform. Here, the buyer was a minor when she originally agreed to purchase a telescope from the seller for $100. Although the buyer had not paid the $100 when she reached the age of majority, she ratified the contract by retaining the telescope and promising to perform part of the contract by paying only $75. This made the contract enforceable—but only to the extent of the promise for partial performance. Therefore, the seller likely will only recover $75 (Choices A & D). (Choice B) Certain promises are enforceable even without consideration. This includes a party's promise to perform a voidable duty (ie, ratification), so long as the new promise is not also voidable. Therefore, the buyer's promise to pay $75 made after reaching majority is enforceable without consideration.\n\nEducational objective: A minor lacks capacity to contract, so the minor can choose to (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify it after turning 18. If the contract is ratified by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity based on infancy).","educationalObjective":"A minor lacks capacity to contract, so the minor can choose to (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify it after turning 18. If the contract is ratified by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity based on infancy).","rule":"A minor lacks capacity to contract, so the minor can choose to (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify it after turning 18. If the contract is ratified by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform.","irac":{"issue":"In an action for breach of contract by the seller against the buyer, what amount of damages will the seller likely recover?","rule":"A minor lacks capacity to contract, so the minor can choose to (1) void the contract before or soon after turning 18 or (2) ratify it after turning 18. If the contract is ratified by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform.","analysis":"An unmarried, unemancipated minor (ie, person under the age of 18) lacks capacity to enter a contract. This means that any contract the minor enters is voidable, so the minor can either:\n• void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming it through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under it or\n\n• ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) agreeing to be bound by it.\n\nThe contract becomes enforceable if the minor ratifies it. However, if the ratification is made by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform. Here, the buyer was a minor when she originally agreed to purchase a telescope from the seller for $100. Although the buyer had not paid the $100 when she reached the age of majority, she ratified the contract by retaining the telescope and promising to perform part of the contract by paying only $75. This made the contract enforceable—but only to the extent of the promise for partial performance. Therefore, the seller likely will only recover $75 (Choices A & D). (Choice B) Certain promises are enforceable even without consideration. This includes a party's promise to perform a voidable duty (ie, ratification), so long as the new promise is not also voidable. Therefore, the buyer's promise to pay $75 made after reaching majority is enforceable without consideration.","conclusion":"Answer C: $75."},"questionZh":"一名实际上和外表都未成年的买家以赊账方式从一名 30 岁的卖家那里购买了一台望远镜，约定价格为 100 美元。到了成年不久，买家遇到卖家说：“我很抱歉在付款时没有付给你那台望远镜 100 美元，但我发现它只值 75 美元。所以我现在答应付给你 75 美元。”买方随后否认了她的承诺，并拒绝向卖方支付任何费用。在卖方针对买方提起违约诉讼时，卖方可能会获得多少赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"没什么，因为买家在最初交易时是未成年人。","B":"没什么，因为没有考虑买方在达到多数后做出的承诺。","C":"75 美元。","D":"100 美元。"},"explanationZh":"未婚、无行为能力的未成年人（即 18 岁以下的人）缺乏签订合同的能力。这意味着未成年人签订的任何合同都是可撤销的，因此未成年人可以：\n• 在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久，通过以下方式使合同无效：(1) 通过言语或行动否定合同，以及 (2) 退还根据合同收到的任何仍然存在的福利，或者\n\n• 年满 18 岁后，通过 (1) 未能及时使合同无效或 (2) 同意受合同约束来追认合同。\n\n如果未成年人批准，合同即可强制执行。然而，如果批准是通过承诺仅履行部分合同而作出的，则执行仅限于部分履行的承诺。在这里，当买家最初同意以 100 美元从卖家那里购买望远镜时，她还是未成年人。尽管买方在成年时尚未支付 100 美元，但她追认了合同，保留了望远镜，并承诺仅支付 75 美元即可履行部分合同。这使得合同具有可执行性——但仅限于部分履行的承诺。因此，卖方可能只能收回 75 美元（选择 A 和 D）。 （选择 B）某些承诺即使不考虑也可以强制执行。这包括一方承诺履行可撤销的义务（即批准），只要新的承诺也不是可撤销的。因此，买方在达成多数意见后支付 75 美元的承诺无需对价即可强制执行。\n\n教育目标：未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此未成年人可以选择（1）在 18 岁之前或之后不久废除合同，或（2）在 18 岁之后追认合同。如果合同是通过仅承诺部分履行而追认的，则执行仅限于部分履行的承诺。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于婴儿期的无行为能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此未成年人可以选择（1）在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久撤销合同，或（2）在年满 18 岁之后追认合同。如果合同是通过仅承诺部分履行而追认的，则执行仅限于部分履行的承诺。","iracZh":{"issue":"在卖方针对买方提起违约诉讼时，卖方可能会获得多少赔偿？","rule":"未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此未成年人可以选择（1）在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久撤销合同，或（2）在年满 18 岁之后追认合同。如果合同是通过仅承诺部分履行而追认的，则执行仅限于部分履行的承诺。","analysis":"未婚、无行为能力的未成年人（即 18 岁以下的人）缺乏签订合同的能力。这意味着未成年人签订的任何合同都是可撤销的，因此未成年人可以：\n• 在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久，通过以下方式使合同无效：(1) 通过言语或行动否定合同，以及 (2) 退还根据合同收到的任何仍然存在的福利，或者\n\n• 年满 18 岁后，通过 (1) 未能及时使合同无效或 (2) 同意受合同约束来追认合同。\n\n如果未成年人批准，合同即可强制执行。然而，如果批准是通过承诺仅履行部分合同而作出的，则执行仅限于部分履行的承诺。在这里，当买家最初同意以 100 美元从卖家那里购买望远镜时，她还是未成年人。尽管买方在成年时尚未支付 100 美元，但她追认了合同，保留了望远镜，并承诺仅支付 75 美元即可履行部分合同。这使得合同具有可执行性——但仅限于部分履行的承诺。因此，卖方可能只能收回 75 美元（选择 A 和 D）。 （选择 B）某些承诺即使不考虑也可以强制执行。这包括一方承诺履行可撤销的义务（即批准），只要新的承诺也不是可撤销的。因此，买方在达成多数意见后支付 75 美元的承诺无需对价即可强制执行。\n\n教育目标：未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此未成年人可以选择（1）在 18 岁之前或之后不久废除合同，或（2）在 18 岁之后追认合同。如果合同是通过仅承诺部分履行而追认的，则执行仅限于部分履行的承诺。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于婴儿期的无行为能力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：75 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"ratification","ipa":"/ɹˌætʌfʌkˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"rat·i·fi·ca·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 批准, 承认, 认可；[经] 追认, 批准","contextualMeaning":"追认：在有能力后通过言行确认原可撤销合同","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:ratifications","sourceSentence":"However, if the ratification is made by a promise to perform only part of the contract, enforcement is limited to that promise to partially perform.","synonyms":["confirmation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-53e7aed7cf41638a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":56,"sourceQuestionPage":164,"sourceQuestionPages":[164],"sourceExplanationPages":[165,166],"sourceAnswerPages":[165,166],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-057","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A father and his adult daughter encountered an old family friend on the street. The daughter said to the family friend, \"How about lending me $1,000 to buy a used car? I'll pay you back with interest one year from today.\" The father added, \"And if she doesn't pay it back as promised, I will.\" The family friend thereupon wrote out and handed to the daughter his personal check, payable to her, for $1,000, and she subsequently used the funds to buy a used car. When the debt came due, both the daughter and the father refused to repay any part of it. In an action by the family friend against the father to recover $1,000 plus interest, which of the following statements would summarize the father's best defense?","choices":{"A":"He received no consideration for his conditional promise to the family friend.","B":"His conditional promise to the family friend was not made for the primary purpose of benefiting himself (the father).","C":"His conditional promise to the family friend was not to be performed in less than a year from the time it was made.","D":"The loan by the family friend was made without any agreement concerning the applicable interest rate."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contracts subject to the statute of frauds are enforceable only if they are (1) in a writing that provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed and (2) signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. Here, the check from the family friend may have satisfied the writing requirement. But since it was not signed by the father, the contract is unenforceable if it is subject to the statute of frauds. A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another (eg, the father's promise to pay the loan if the daughter does not). Under the main-purpose rule, suretyships are subject to the statute of frauds unless they are made for the promisor's own economic advantage. Therefore, the father's best defense is that his suretyship was primarily made to benefit his daughter, so it is subject to and unenforceable under the statute of frauds. (Choice A) The suretyship is supported by consideration because each promise was induced by the promises of the other parties. The family friend loaned the daughter $1,000 based on both promises to pay him back (and vice versa). (Choice C) A one-year contract is not subject to the statute of frauds if it is possible to perform within one year (even if the parties agree on a later completion date). Here, it was possible for the daughter or father to pay the loan within a year, so this would not trigger the statute of frauds. (Choice D) An agreement concerning an applicable interest rate was not needed to form an enforceable contract.\n\nEducational objective: A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another. Unless a suretyship is made for the promisor's own economic advantage, it is subject to the statute of frauds—ie, it must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 112 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirement of suretyship).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 116 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (main-purpose rule).","educationalObjective":"A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another. Unless a suretyship is made for the promisor's own economic advantage, it is subject to the statute of frauds—ie, it must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 112 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirement of suretyship).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 116 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (main-purpose rule).","rule":"A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another. Unless a suretyship is made for the promisor's own economic advantage, it is subject to the statute of frauds—ie, it must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the family friend against the father to recover $1,000 plus interest, which of the following statements would summarize the father's best defense?","rule":"A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another. Unless a suretyship is made for the promisor's own economic advantage, it is subject to the statute of frauds—ie, it must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought.","analysis":"Contracts subject to the statute of frauds are enforceable only if they are (1) in a writing that provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed and (2) signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. Here, the check from the family friend may have satisfied the writing requirement. But since it was not signed by the father, the contract is unenforceable if it is subject to the statute of frauds. A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another (eg, the father's promise to pay the loan if the daughter does not). Under the main-purpose rule, suretyships are subject to the statute of frauds unless they are made for the promisor's own economic advantage. Therefore, the father's best defense is that his suretyship was primarily made to benefit his daughter, so it is subject to and unenforceable under the statute of frauds. (Choice A) The suretyship is supported by consideration because each promise was induced by the promises of the other parties. The family friend loaned the daughter $1,000 based on both promises to pay him back (and vice versa). (Choice C) A one-year contract is not subject to the statute of frauds if it is possible to perform within one year (even if the parties agree on a later completion date). Here, it was possible for the daughter or father to pay the loan within a year, so this would not trigger the statute of frauds. (Choice D) An agreement concerning an applicable interest rate was not needed to form an enforceable contract.","conclusion":"Answer B: His conditional promise to the family friend was not made for the primary purpose of benefiting himself (the father)."},"questionZh":"一位父亲和他成年的女儿在街上遇到了一位老朋友。女儿对朋友说：“借我1000美元买一辆二手车怎么样？一年后我会连本带息还给你。”父亲补充道：“如果她不按承诺还钱，我会的。”随后，这位家庭朋友写了一张1000美元的个人支票交给女儿，并交给她，她随后用这笔钱购买了一辆二手车。当债务到期时，女儿和父亲都拒绝偿还任何一部分。在家庭朋友向父亲提起诉讼，要求追回 1,000 美元及利息时，以下哪项陈述可以概括父亲的最佳辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"他对家庭朋友的有条件承诺没有得到任何考虑。","B":"他对亲朋好友做出的有条件的承诺并不是为了让自己（父亲）受益。","C":"他对家族朋友的有条件承诺，自作出之日起不到一年内不得履行。","D":"家庭朋友的贷款是在没有就适用利率达成任何协议的情况下提供的。"},"explanationZh":"受欺诈法约束的合同只有在以下情况下才可执行：(1) 以书面形式提供合理的依据以相信合同已成立，并且 (2) 由寻求执行的一方签署。在这里，家人朋友的支票可能已经满足了书写要求。但由于合同不是由父亲签署的，如果受到欺诈法的约束，合同就无法执行。担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺（例如，如果女儿不偿还贷款，父亲承诺偿还贷款）。根据主要目的规则，担保须遵守欺诈法，除非担保是为了承诺人自身的经济利益而提供的。因此，父亲最好的辩护是，他的担保主要是为了让他的女儿受益，因此它受到欺诈法的约束并且无法执行。 （选项A） 保证是有对价支持的，因为每项承诺都是由其他各方的承诺引发的。家庭朋友借给女儿 1,000 美元，双方均承诺偿还（反之亦然）。 （选择 C）如果一年期合同可以在一年内履行（即使双方同意较晚的完成日期），则不受欺诈法的约束。在这里，女儿或父亲有可能在一年内偿还贷款，因此这不会触发欺诈法。 （选择 D） 无需就适用利率达成一致即可形成可执行合同。\n\n教育目标：担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺。除非保证是为了承诺人自身的经济利益而提供的，否则它须遵守欺诈法，即必须采用书面形式并由寻求执行的一方签署。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 112（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（担保要求）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 116（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（主要目的规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺。除非保证是为了承诺人自身的经济利益而提供的，否则它须遵守欺诈法，即必须采用书面形式并由寻求执行的一方签署。","iracZh":{"issue":"在家庭朋友向父亲提起诉讼，要求追回 1,000 美元及利息时，以下哪项陈述可以概括父亲的最佳辩护？","rule":"担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺。除非保证是为了承诺人自身的经济利益而提供的，否则它须遵守欺诈法，即必须采用书面形式并由寻求执行的一方签署。","analysis":"受欺诈法约束的合同只有在以下情况下才可执行：(1) 以书面形式提供合理的依据以相信合同已成立，并且 (2) 由寻求执行的一方签署。在这里，家人朋友的支票可能已经满足了书写要求。但由于合同不是由父亲签署的，如果受到欺诈法的约束，合同就无法执行。担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺（例如，如果女儿不偿还贷款，父亲承诺偿还贷款）。根据主要目的规则，担保须遵守欺诈法，除非担保是为了承诺人自身的经济利益而提供的。因此，父亲最好的辩护是，他的担保主要是为了让他的女儿受益，因此它受到欺诈法的约束并且无法执行。 （选项A） 保证是有对价支持的，因为每项承诺都是由其他各方的承诺引发的。家庭朋友借给女儿 1,000 美元，双方均承诺偿还（反之亦然）。 （选择 C）如果一年期合同可以在一年内履行（即使双方同意较晚的完成日期），则不受欺诈法的约束。在这里，女儿或父亲有可能在一年内偿还贷款，因此这不会触发欺诈法。 （选择 D） 无需就适用利率达成一致即可形成可执行合同。\n\n教育目标：担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺。除非保证是为了承诺人自身的经济利益而提供的，否则它须遵守欺诈法，即必须采用书面形式并由寻求执行的一方签署。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 112（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（担保要求）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 116（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（主要目的规则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：他对亲朋好友做出的有条件的承诺并不是为了让自己（父亲）受益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"conditional promise","ipa":"/kʌndˈɪʃənəl pɹˈɑməs/","syllables":"con·di·tional promise","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"附条件允诺：履行义务取决于约定事件的允诺","contextualMeaning":"附条件允诺：履行义务取决于约定事件的允诺","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"He received no consideration for his conditional promise to the family friend.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":57,"sourceQuestionPage":167,"sourceQuestionPages":[167],"sourceExplanationPages":[168],"sourceAnswerPages":[168],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-058","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A farmer contracted to sell 100,000 bushels of wheat to a buyer. When the wheat arrived at the destination, the buyer discovered that the farmer had delivered only 96,000 bushels. The buyer sued the farmer for breach of contract. At the trial of the case, the court found that the written contract was intended as a complete and exclusive statement of the terms of the agreement. The farmer offered to prove that in the wheat business, a promise to deliver a specified quantity is considered to be satisfied if the delivered quantity is within 5% of the specified quantity. The buyer objected to the offered evidence. Is the court likely to admit the evidence offered by the farmer?","choices":{"A":"No, because the offered evidence is inconsistent with the express language of the agreement.","B":"No, because the written contract was totally integrated.","C":"Yes, because the offered evidence demonstrates that the farmer substantially performed the contract.","D":"Yes, because the offered evidence explains or supplements the agreement by usage of trade."},"answer":"D","explanation":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC's parol evidence rule, the express terms of a completely integrated, written contract cannot be contradicted by evidence of inconsistent prior or contemporaneous agreements. However, evidence of trade usage can be admitted to explain or supplement contract terms so long as that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.* Trade usage refers to a practice or method of dealing that is observed with such regularity in the industry that the parties to a contract would be expected to observe it. Here, the farmer delivered 96,000 bushels of wheat instead of the 100,000 bushels expressly required by the completely integrated contract. However, evidence shows that in the wheat industry, a delivery is satisfied if the delivered quantity is within 5% of the quantity specified in the contract. And since this evidence of trade usage is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language, the court is likely to admit the farmer's evidence of trade usage to supplement the contract (Choices A & B). *Under the majority rule, trade usage is consistent with express language in the contract unless the usage completely negates specific express language. (Choice C) Under the UCC's perfect-tender rule, a seller breaches a contract for the sale of goods when those goods or their delivery do not perfectly conform to the contract's terms (as seen here). Therefore, evidence showing that the farmer substantially performed is unlikely to be admitted because it does not excuse the farmer's breach.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC parol evidence rule allows the admission of evidence of trade usage to explain or supplement a contract's terms if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the express language of the contract.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (trade usage is admissible to explain or supplement contract's terms).\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (definition of trade usage).","educationalObjective":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows the admission of evidence of trade usage to explain or supplement a contract's terms if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the express language of the contract.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (trade usage is admissible to explain or supplement contract's terms).\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (definition of trade usage).","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows the admission of evidence of trade usage to explain or supplement a contract's terms if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the express language of the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Is the court likely to admit the evidence offered by the farmer?","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows the admission of evidence of trade usage to explain or supplement a contract's terms if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the express language of the contract.","analysis":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC's parol evidence rule, the express terms of a completely integrated, written contract cannot be contradicted by evidence of inconsistent prior or contemporaneous agreements. However, evidence of trade usage can be admitted to explain or supplement contract terms so long as that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.* Trade usage refers to a practice or method of dealing that is observed with such regularity in the industry that the parties to a contract would be expected to observe it. Here, the farmer delivered 96,000 bushels of wheat instead of the 100,000 bushels expressly required by the completely integrated contract. However, evidence shows that in the wheat industry, a delivery is satisfied if the delivered quantity is within 5% of the quantity specified in the contract. And since this evidence of trade usage is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language, the court is likely to admit the farmer's evidence of trade usage to supplement the contract (Choices A & B). *Under the majority rule, trade usage is consistent with express language in the contract unless the usage completely negates specific express language. (Choice C) Under the UCC's perfect-tender rule, a seller breaches a contract for the sale of goods when those goods or their delivery do not perfectly conform to the contract's terms (as seen here). Therefore, evidence showing that the farmer substantially performed is unlikely to be admitted because it does not excuse the farmer's breach.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the offered evidence explains or supplements the agreement by usage of trade."},"questionZh":"一位农民签订了向买家出售 100,000 蒲式耳小麦的合同。当小麦到达目的地时，买家发现农民只交付了 96,000 蒲式耳。买方起诉农民违反合同。在审理此案时，法院认为书面合同旨在作为协议条款的完整且排他性的陈述。农户提出证明，在小麦业务中，如果交付数量在规定数量的5%以内，则视为履行了交付规定数量的承诺。买方对所提供的证据提出异议。法院是否会采信农民提供的证据？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为提供的证据与协议的明确语言不一致。","B":"不，因为书面合同是完全完整的。","C":"是的，因为提供的证据表明农民实质上履行了合同。","D":"是的，因为所提供的证据通过贸易用途解释或补充了协议。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC 的口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），完整的书面合同的明示条款不能与先前或同期协议不一致的证据相矛盾。然而，只要该证据与合同的明示语言合理一致，就可以接受贸易惯例的证据来解释或补充合同条款。*贸易惯例是指在行业中经常观察到的交易惯例或方法，以致合同双方都期望遵守它。在这里，农民交付了 96,000 蒲式耳小麦，而不是完全一体化合同明确要求的 100,000 蒲式耳。然而，有证据表明，在小麦行业，如果交货数量在合同规定数量的 5% 以内，则交货满意。而且，由于该贸易用途证据与合同的明确语言相当一致，法院很可能接受农民的贸易用途证据来补充合同（选项A和B）。 *根据多数规则，贸易惯例与合同中的明示语言一致，除非该惯例完全否定特定的明示语言。 （选项 C）根据 UCC 的完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），当货物或其交付不完全符合合同条款（如此处所示）时，卖方违反了货物销售合同。因此，表明农民实质性履行的证据不太可能被采纳，因为它不能成为农民违规行为的借口。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许采用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充合同条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（允许贸易用途来解释或补充合同条款）。\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（贸易用途的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许采用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充合同条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否会采信农民提供的证据？","rule":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许采用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充合同条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。","analysis":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC 的口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），完整的书面合同的明示条款不能与先前或同期协议不一致的证据相矛盾。然而，只要该证据与合同的明示语言合理一致，就可以接受贸易惯例的证据来解释或补充合同条款。*贸易惯例是指在行业中经常观察到的交易惯例或方法，以致合同双方都期望遵守它。在这里，农民交付了 96,000 蒲式耳小麦，而不是完全一体化合同明确要求的 100,000 蒲式耳。然而，有证据表明，在小麦行业，如果交货数量在合同规定数量的 5% 以内，则交货满意。而且，由于该贸易用途证据与合同的明确语言相当一致，法院很可能接受农民的贸易用途证据来补充合同（选项A和B）。 *根据多数规则，贸易惯例与合同中的明示语言一致，除非该惯例完全否定特定的明示语言。 （选项 C）根据 UCC 的完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），当货物或其交付不完全符合合同条款（如此处所示）时，卖方违反了货物销售合同。因此，表明农民实质性履行的证据不太可能被采纳，因为它不能成为农民违规行为的借口。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许采用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充合同条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（允许贸易用途来解释或补充合同条款）。\n\n• U.C.C. § 1-303 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（贸易用途的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为所提供的证据通过贸易用途解释或补充了协议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"usage of trade","ipa":"/jˈusədʒ ˈəv tɹˈeɪd/","syllables":"us·age of trade","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"行业惯例：特定行业经常遵循的交易做法","contextualMeaning":"行业惯例：特定行业经常遵循的交易做法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the offered evidence explains or supplements the agreement by usage of trade.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"destination","ipa":"/dˌɛstʌnˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"des·ti·na·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 目的地, 目标, 目的；[计] 目的文件, 目的单元","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“destination”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:destinations","sourceSentence":"When the wheat arrived at the destination, the buyer discovered that the farmer had delivered only 96,000 bushels.","synonyms":["terminus","address","name and address","finish"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":58,"sourceQuestionPage":169,"sourceQuestionPages":[169],"sourceExplanationPages":[170,171],"sourceAnswerPages":[170,171],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-059","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A seller borrowed $5,000 from a bank. Soon thereafter the seller filed for bankruptcy, having paid nothing on his debt to the bank. Five years after the debt had been discharged in bankruptcy, the seller contracted to sell certain goods to a buyer for $5,000. The contract provided that the buyer would pay the $5,000 to the bank. The only debt that the seller ever owed the bank is the $5,000 debt that was discharged in bankruptcy. The seller delivered the goods to the buyer, who accepted them. If the bank becomes aware of the contract between the seller and the buyer, and the buyer refuses to pay anything to the bank, is the bank likely to succeed in an action against the buyer for $5,000?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer's promise to pay the bank was not supported by consideration.","B":"No, because the seller's debt was discharged in bankruptcy.","C":"Yes, because no consideration is required to support a promise to pay a debt that has been discharged in bankruptcy.","D":"Yes, because the bank was an intended beneficiary of the contract between the buyer and the seller."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. If the contracting parties intended for the contract to benefit the third party, the third party is an intended beneficiary with enforceable rights under the contract. That right vests when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Once this occurs, the original contracting parties are bound to perform the contract. Here, the bank was an intended third-party beneficiary of the buyer-seller contract because the seller agreed to sell the buyer goods in exchange for the buyer paying the bank $5,000. The bank's right to enforce the buyer-seller contract will vest once the bank files a lawsuit to enforce it. Therefore, the bank will likely succeed in an action against the buyer for $5,000. (Choice A) The buyer's promise to pay the bank was supported by consideration since the buyer agreed to pay the bank $5,000 in exchange for the seller's goods. (Choice B) The fact that the seller's debt to the bank was discharged in bankruptcy is irrelevant because an intended beneficiary can be a creditor or a donee beneficiary. A creditor beneficiary can recover to satisfy a debt. A donee beneficiary can recover to receive a gift. Therefore, the bank is likely an intended donee beneficiary. (Choice C) Although no consideration is required to support a promise to pay a debt discharged in bankruptcy, this is not a reason for the bank to succeed. Instead, the bank will succeed because it is an intended beneficiary.\n\nEducational objective: Intended third-party beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties intended to benefit them. They have the right to file a lawsuit to enforce the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 304 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (duties owed to intended beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"Intended third-party beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties intended to benefit them. They have the right to file a lawsuit to enforce the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 304 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (duties owed to intended beneficiaries).","rule":"Intended third-party beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties intended to benefit them. They have the right to file a lawsuit to enforce the contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the bank becomes aware of the contract between the seller and the buyer, and the buyer refuses to pay anything to the bank, is the bank likely to succeed in an action against the buyer for $5,000?","rule":"Intended third-party beneficiaries receive a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties intended to benefit them. They have the right to file a lawsuit to enforce the contract.","analysis":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. If the contracting parties intended for the contract to benefit the third party, the third party is an intended beneficiary with enforceable rights under the contract. That right vests when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Once this occurs, the original contracting parties are bound to perform the contract. Here, the bank was an intended third-party beneficiary of the buyer-seller contract because the seller agreed to sell the buyer goods in exchange for the buyer paying the bank $5,000. The bank's right to enforce the buyer-seller contract will vest once the bank files a lawsuit to enforce it. Therefore, the bank will likely succeed in an action against the buyer for $5,000. (Choice A) The buyer's promise to pay the bank was supported by consideration since the buyer agreed to pay the bank $5,000 in exchange for the seller's goods. (Choice B) The fact that the seller's debt to the bank was discharged in bankruptcy is irrelevant because an intended beneficiary can be a creditor or a donee beneficiary. A creditor beneficiary can recover to satisfy a debt. A donee beneficiary can recover to receive a gift. Therefore, the bank is likely an intended donee beneficiary. (Choice C) Although no consideration is required to support a promise to pay a debt discharged in bankruptcy, this is not a reason for the bank to succeed. Instead, the bank will succeed because it is an intended beneficiary.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the bank was an intended beneficiary of the contract between the buyer and the seller."},"questionZh":"卖家从银行借了 5,000 美元。此后不久，卖方申请破产，未偿还银行债务。在破产清偿债务五年后，卖方签订合同，以 5,000 美元的价格向买方出售某些商品。合同规定买方将向银行支付 5,000 美元。卖方欠银行的唯一债务是破产时免除的 5,000 美元债务。卖方将货物交付给买方，买方接受了货物。如果银行知道卖方和买方之间的合同，而买方拒绝向银行支付任何费用，银行是否有可能在针对买方的诉讼中胜诉并赔偿 5,000 美元？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为买方向银行付款的承诺没有得到对价的支持。","B":"不可以，因为卖方的债务在破产时已被解除。","C":"是的，因为不需要对价来支持支付已在破产中解除的债务的承诺。","D":"是的，因为银行是买方和卖方之间合同的预期受益人。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人。如果缔约方意图通过合同使第三方受益，则第三方是具有合同项下可执行权利的预期受益人。当受益人符合以下条件时，该权利就归属：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。一旦发生这种情况，原缔约方就有义务履行合同。在此，银行是买卖合同的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary），因为卖方同意向买方出售货物，以换取买方向银行支付 5,000 美元。一旦银行提起诉讼强制执行，银行就将享有强制执行买卖合同的权利。因此，银行可能会成功起诉买方并赔偿 5,000 美元。 （选项A） 买方向银行付款的承诺得到了对价的支持，因为买方同意向银行支付5,000 美元以换取卖方的货物。 （选择B）卖方对银行的债务在破产中被解除这一事实是无关紧要的，因为预期受益人可以是债权人或受赠人受益人。债权人受益人可以追偿债务。受赠人受益人可以恢复接收礼物。因此，银行很可能是预期的受赠人受益人。 （选项C）虽然不需要对价来支持支付破产中解除的债务的承诺，但这并不是银行成功的理由。相反，银行将会成功，因为它是预期的受益者。\n\n教育目标：预期的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有意使他们受益。他们有权提起诉讼以强制执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 304（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对预期受益人的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有意使他们受益。他们有权提起诉讼以强制执行合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果银行知道卖方和买方之间的合同，而买方拒绝向银行支付任何费用，银行是否有可能在针对买方的诉讼中胜诉并赔偿 5,000 美元？","rule":"预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有意使他们受益。他们有权提起诉讼以强制执行合同。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得某些利益或利益的合同非当事人。如果缔约方意图通过合同使第三方受益，则第三方是具有合同项下可执行权利的预期受益人。当受益人符合以下条件时，该权利就归属：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。一旦发生这种情况，原缔约方就有义务履行合同。在此，银行是买卖合同的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary），因为卖方同意向买方出售货物，以换取买方向银行支付 5,000 美元。一旦银行提起诉讼强制执行，银行就将享有强制执行买卖合同的权利。因此，银行可能会成功起诉买方并赔偿 5,000 美元。 （选项A） 买方向银行付款的承诺得到了对价的支持，因为买方同意向银行支付5,000 美元以换取卖方的货物。 （选择B）卖方对银行的债务在破产中被解除这一事实是无关紧要的，因为预期受益人可以是债权人或受赠人受益人。债权人受益人可以追偿债务。受赠人受益人可以恢复接收礼物。因此，银行很可能是预期的受赠人受益人。 （选项C）虽然不需要对价来支持支付破产中解除的债务的承诺，但这并不是银行成功的理由。相反，银行将会成功，因为它是预期的受益者。\n\n教育目标：预期的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有意使他们受益。他们有权提起诉讼以强制执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 304（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对预期受益人的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为银行是买方和卖方之间合同的预期受益人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-11777eced73cc39a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":59,"sourceQuestionPage":172,"sourceQuestionPages":[172],"sourceExplanationPages":[173,174],"sourceAnswerPages":[173,174],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-060","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A radio manufacturer and a retailer, after extensive negotiations, entered into a final, written agreement in which the manufacturer agreed to sell and the retailer agreed to buy all of its requirements of radios, estimated at 20 units per month, during the period from January 1, 2017 to December 31, 2019, at a price of $50 per unit. A dispute arose in late December 2019, when the retailer returned 25 undefective radios to the manufacturer for full credit after the manufacturer had refused to extend the contract for a second three-year period. In an action by the radio manufacturer against the retailer for damages due to return of the 25 radios, the manufacturer introduces the written agreement, which expressly permitted the retailer to return defective radios for credit but was silent as to return of undefective radios for credit. The retailer seeks to introduce evidence that during the three years of the agreement it had returned, for various reasons, 125 undefective radios, for which the manufacturer had granted full credit. The manufacturer objects to the admissibility of this evidence. How will the trial court probably rule as to the admissibility of the evidence proffered by the retailer?","choices":{"A":"Admissible, because course-of-performance evidence, when available, is considered the best indication of what the parties intended the writing to mean.","B":"Admissible, because the evidence supports an agreement that is not within the relevant statute of frauds.","C":"Inadmissible, because the evidence is barred by the parol evidence rule.","D":"Inadmissible, because the express terms of the agreement control when those terms are inconsistent with the course of performance."},"answer":"A","explanation":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, radios) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC's parol evidence rule, the express terms of a final, written contract cannot be contradicted by evidence of inconsistent prior or contemporaneous agreements. But when a contract is silent on an issue, its terms can be supplemented with evidence of a course of performance—ie, a sequence of conduct under a contract that involves repeated occasions for performance. Here, the radio manufacturer and the retailer entered a final, written requirements contract for the sale of radios. Near the end of the contract, the retailer returned 25 undefective radios for credit, as it had previously done with 125 undefective radios. The contract expressly permitted the return of defective radios, but it was silent as to the return of undefective radios (Choice D). Therefore, evidence of this course of performance is admissible since it is the best indication of what the parties intended the writing to mean (Choice C). (Choice B) Under the statute of frauds, certain types of contracts—eg, contracts for the sale of goods greater than $500—are only enforceable if they are written and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. But here, the contract is within the statute of frauds since it called for the sale of an estimated 20 radios per month, at $50 per radio, for 36 months (ie, greater than $500).\n\nEducational objective: The parol evidence rule prohibits evidence of inconsistent prior agreements that contradict a final, written contract. But when a contract is silent on an issue, its terms can be explained or supplemented with evidence of a course of performance—ie, a sequence of conduct under a contract involving repeated occasions for performance.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"The parol evidence rule prohibits evidence of inconsistent prior agreements that contradict a final, written contract. But when a contract is silent on an issue, its terms can be explained or supplemented with evidence of a course of performance—ie, a sequence of conduct under a contract involving repeated occasions for performance.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","rule":"The parol evidence rule prohibits evidence of inconsistent prior agreements that contradict a final, written contract. But when a contract is silent on an issue, its terms can be explained or supplemented with evidence of a course of performance—ie, a sequence of conduct under a contract involving repeated occasions for performance.","irac":{"issue":"How will the trial court probably rule as to the admissibility of the evidence proffered by the retailer?","rule":"The parol evidence rule prohibits evidence of inconsistent prior agreements that contradict a final, written contract. But when a contract is silent on an issue, its terms can be explained or supplemented with evidence of a course of performance—ie, a sequence of conduct under a contract involving repeated occasions for performance.","analysis":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, radios) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC's parol evidence rule, the express terms of a final, written contract cannot be contradicted by evidence of inconsistent prior or contemporaneous agreements. But when a contract is silent on an issue, its terms can be supplemented with evidence of a course of performance—ie, a sequence of conduct under a contract that involves repeated occasions for performance. Here, the radio manufacturer and the retailer entered a final, written requirements contract for the sale of radios. Near the end of the contract, the retailer returned 25 undefective radios for credit, as it had previously done with 125 undefective radios. The contract expressly permitted the return of defective radios, but it was silent as to the return of undefective radios (Choice D). Therefore, evidence of this course of performance is admissible since it is the best indication of what the parties intended the writing to mean (Choice C). (Choice B) Under the statute of frauds, certain types of contracts—eg, contracts for the sale of goods greater than $500—are only enforceable if they are written and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. But here, the contract is within the statute of frauds since it called for the sale of an estimated 20 radios per month, at $50 per radio, for 36 months (ie, greater than $500).","conclusion":"Answer A: Admissible, because course-of-performance evidence, when available, is considered the best indication of what the parties intended the writing to mean."},"questionZh":"一家收音机制造商和一家零售商经过广泛谈判，达成了最终书面协议，制造商同意出售，零售商同意购买其所有所需的收音机，预计在 2017 年 1 月 1 日至 2019 年 12 月 31 日期间每月购买 20 台，每台价格为 50 美元。 2019 年 12 月下旬，在制造商拒绝将合同延长第二个三年后，零售商将 25 台无缺陷的收音机退还给制造商并获得全额退款，由此引发了一场纠纷。在收音机制造商针对零售商因退回 25 台收音机而造成的损失提起的诉讼中，制造商提出了一份书面协议，明确允许零售商退回有缺陷的收音机以获取信用，但没有提及是否可以退回无缺陷的收音机以获取信用。该零售商试图提供证据，证明在协议签订的三年内，由于各种原因，它已经退回了 125 台无缺陷的收音机，而制造商对此给予了全额退款。制造商反对接受这一证据。对于零售商提供的证据的可采性，初审法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"可以接受，因为履约证据（如果有）被认为是当事人书面意思的最佳指示。","B":"可以接受，因为证据支持的协议不属于相关防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的范围。","C":"不可接受，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止该证据。","D":"不可接受，因为当这些条款与履行过程不一致时，以协议的明示条款为准。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品销售合同（例如无线电）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC 的口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），最终书面合同的明确条款不能与先前或同期协议不一致的证据相矛盾。但是，当合同对某个问题没有提及时，其条款可以用履行过程的证据来补充，即合同下涉及重复履行的一系列行为。在这里，无线电制造商和零售商签订了无线电销售的最终书面要求合同。合同临近结束时，零售商退回了 25 台无缺陷的收音机以获取信用，就像之前对 125 台无缺陷的收音机所做的那样。合同明确允许退回有缺陷的无线电设备，但没有提及退回无缺陷的无线电设备（选项 D）。因此，这种履行过程的证据是可以接受的，因为它是当事人书面意思的最佳指示（选择C）。 （选择 B） 根据反欺诈法，某些类型的合同（例如，超过 500 美元的货物销售合同）只有由被寻求执行的一方书面并签署后才可执行。但在这里，该合同属于欺诈法，因为它要求每月销售大约 20 台收音机，每台收音机 50 美元，为期 36 个月（即超过 500 美元）。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供与最终书面合同相矛盾的不一致的先前协议的证据。但是，当合同对某个问题没有提及时，其条款可以用履行过程的证据来解释或补充，即合同下涉及重复履行的一系列行为。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供与最终书面合同相矛盾的不一致的先前协议的证据。但是，当合同对某个问题没有提及时，其条款可以用履行过程的证据来解释或补充，即合同下涉及重复履行的一系列行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"对于零售商提供的证据的可采性，初审法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供与最终书面合同相矛盾的不一致的先前协议的证据。但是，当合同对某个问题没有提及时，其条款可以用履行过程的证据来解释或补充，即合同下涉及重复履行的一系列行为。","analysis":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品销售合同（例如无线电）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC 的口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），最终书面合同的明确条款不能与先前或同期协议不一致的证据相矛盾。但是，当合同对某个问题没有提及时，其条款可以用履行过程的证据来补充，即合同下涉及重复履行的一系列行为。在这里，无线电制造商和零售商签订了无线电销售的最终书面要求合同。合同临近结束时，零售商退回了 25 台无缺陷的收音机以获取信用，就像之前对 125 台无缺陷的收音机所做的那样。合同明确允许退回有缺陷的无线电设备，但没有提及退回无缺陷的无线电设备（选项 D）。因此，这种履行过程的证据是可以接受的，因为它是当事人书面意思的最佳指示（选择C）。 （选择 B） 根据反欺诈法，某些类型的合同（例如，超过 500 美元的货物销售合同）只有由被寻求执行的一方书面并签署后才可执行。但在这里，该合同属于欺诈法，因为它要求每月销售大约 20 台收音机，每台收音机 50 美元，为期 36 个月（即超过 500 美元）。\n\n教育目标：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止提供与最终书面合同相矛盾的不一致的先前协议的证据。但是，当合同对某个问题没有提及时，其条款可以用履行过程的证据来解释或补充，即合同下涉及重复履行的一系列行为。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","conclusion":"答案 A：可以接受，因为履约证据（如果有）被认为是当事人书面意思的最佳指示。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":60,"sourceQuestionPage":175,"sourceQuestionPages":[175],"sourceExplanationPages":[176,177],"sourceAnswerPages":[176,177],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-061","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Capacity & Ratification","question":"A mechanic agreed in writing to make repairs to a landscaper's truck for $12,000. The mechanic properly made the repairs, but when the landscaper tendered payment, the mechanic refused to deliver the truck unless the landscaper promised to pay an additional $2,000. The customary charge for such work was $14,000. Because the landscaper needed the truck immediately to fulfill existing contractual obligations, and because no rental trucks of the same type were available, the landscaper promised in writing to pay the mechanic an additional $2,000. The mechanic then delivered the truck. Will the mechanic be able to enforce the landscaper's promise to pay the additional $2,000?","choices":{"A":"No, because the landscaper had no reasonable alternative but to yield to the mechanic's wrongful threat.","B":"No, because the mechanic exerted undue influence over the landscaper with respect to the modification.","C":"Yes, because the landscaper could have obtained possession of the truck through legal action rather than by agreeing to the increased payment.","D":"Yes, because the modified contract price did not exceed a reasonable price."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Duress may serve as a defense to contract enforceability if a contracting party's assent was induced by an improper threat from another contracting party—eg, a threat that breached the contractual duty of good faith and fair dealing. When this occurs, the contract becomes voidable and can be rescinded if the threatened party:\n• had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat and\n• promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) disaffirmed the contract. Here, the mechanic breached his duty of good faith and fair dealing when he refused to deliver the truck unless the landscaper paid an additional $2,000 (improper threat). Since the landscaper needed the truck immediately to fulfill existing contractual obligations and no similar rental trucks were available, he had no choice but to succumb to the threat (no reasonable alternative). As a result, his promise cannot be enforced if he promptly disaffirmed it. (Choice B) Undue influence makes a contract voidable when a party assented due to unfair or excessive persuasion from someone who dominated (eg, prison guard/prisoner) or held a special relationship of trust and confidence with (eg, attorney/client) the assenting party—neither of which is seen here. (Choice C) Since the landscaper needed the truck immediately to fulfill existing contractual obligations, his ability to later obtain possession through legal action was not a reasonable alternative. (Choice D) Although the modified contract price was customary and reasonable, this modification was still induced by an improper threat because it violated the mechanic's duty of good faith and fair dealing.\n\nEducational objective: A contract that is assented to under duress induced by an improper threat from another contracting party is voidable if the victim (1) had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat and (2) promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) disaffirmed the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 174–176 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (duress caused by physical compulsion and improper threat).","educationalObjective":"A contract that is assented to under duress induced by an improper threat from another contracting party is voidable if the victim (1) had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat and (2) promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) disaffirmed the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 174–176 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (duress caused by physical compulsion and improper threat).","rule":"A contract that is assented to under duress induced by an improper threat from another contracting party is voidable if the victim (1) had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat and (2) promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) disaffirmed the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Will the mechanic be able to enforce the landscaper's promise to pay the additional $2,000?","rule":"A contract that is assented to under duress induced by an improper threat from another contracting party is voidable if the victim (1) had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat and (2) promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) disaffirmed the contract.","analysis":"Duress may serve as a defense to contract enforceability if a contracting party's assent was induced by an improper threat from another contracting party—eg, a threat that breached the contractual duty of good faith and fair dealing. When this occurs, the contract becomes voidable and can be rescinded if the threatened party:\n• had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat and\n• promptly (ie, within a reasonable time) disaffirmed the contract. Here, the mechanic breached his duty of good faith and fair dealing when he refused to deliver the truck unless the landscaper paid an additional $2,000 (improper threat). Since the landscaper needed the truck immediately to fulfill existing contractual obligations and no similar rental trucks were available, he had no choice but to succumb to the threat (no reasonable alternative). As a result, his promise cannot be enforced if he promptly disaffirmed it. (Choice B) Undue influence makes a contract voidable when a party assented due to unfair or excessive persuasion from someone who dominated (eg, prison guard/prisoner) or held a special relationship of trust and confidence with (eg, attorney/client) the assenting party—neither of which is seen here. (Choice C) Since the landscaper needed the truck immediately to fulfill existing contractual obligations, his ability to later obtain possession through legal action was not a reasonable alternative. (Choice D) Although the modified contract price was customary and reasonable, this modification was still induced by an improper threat because it violated the mechanic's duty of good faith and fair dealing.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the landscaper had no reasonable alternative but to yield to the mechanic's wrongful threat."},"questionZh":"一名机械师书面同意修理一名园艺师的卡车，费用为 12,000 美元。机械师妥善进行了修理，但当园林设计师付款时，机械师拒绝交付卡车，除非园林设计师承诺额外支付 2,000 美元。此类工作的通常收费为 14,000 美元。由于园林设计师立即需要这辆卡车来履行现有的合同义务，并且由于没有同类型的租赁卡车，园林设计师书面承诺向机械师额外支付 2,000 美元。机械师随后交付了卡车。机械师能否履行景观设计师额外支付 2,000 美元的承诺？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为园林设计师没有合理的选择，只能屈服于机械师的错误威胁。","B":"不可以，因为机械师在修改方面对园林设计师施加了不当影响。","C":"是的，因为园艺师可以通过法律诉讼而不是同意增加付款来获得卡车的所有权。","D":"是的，因为修改后的合同价格没有超过合理价格。"},"explanationZh":"如果缔约方的同意是由另一缔约方的不当威胁（例如，违反诚实信用和公平交易的合同义务的威胁）引起的，则胁迫可以作为合同可执行性的抗辩。发生这种情况时，如果受威胁方出现以下情况，合同将无效并可以撤销：\n• 没有合理的选择，只能屈服于不当威胁，并且\n• 立即（即在合理的时间内）取消合同。在这里，机械师拒绝交付卡车，除非园林设计师额外支付 2,000 美元（不当威胁），这违反了他的诚信和公平交易义务。由于园林设计师需要立即使用卡车来履行现有的合同义务，并且没有类似的租赁卡车可用，因此他别无选择，只能屈服于威胁（没有合理的选择）。因此，如果他立即否认，他的承诺就无法执行。 （选择B）当一方因受到支配者（例如狱警/囚犯）或与同意方（例如律师/委托人）有特殊信任和信心关系的人的不公平或过度说服而同意时，不当影响使合同无效——这两种情况在这里都没有看到。 （选择 C）由于园艺师需要立即使用卡车来履行现有的合同义务，因此他后来通过法律诉讼获得占有权的能力并不是一个合理的选择。 （选项D）尽管修改后的合同价格符合惯例且合理，但这种修改仍然是由不当威胁引发的，因为它违反了机械师的诚实信用和公平交易义务。\n\n教育目标：如果受害人 (1) 没有合理的选择，只能屈从于不当威胁，并且 (2) 立即（即在合理的时间内）否认合同，则在另一缔约方的不当威胁诱导下签订的合同是无效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 174–176（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（由身体强迫和不当威胁造成的胁迫）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果受害人 (1) 没有合理的选择，只能屈服于不当威胁，并且 (2) 立即（即在合理的时间内）撤销合同，则在另一缔约方的不当威胁诱导下签订的合同可宣告无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"机械师能否履行景观设计师额外支付 2,000 美元的承诺？","rule":"如果受害人 (1) 没有合理的选择，只能屈服于不当威胁，并且 (2) 立即（即在合理的时间内）撤销合同，则在另一缔约方的不当威胁诱导下签订的合同可宣告无效。","analysis":"如果缔约方的同意是由另一缔约方的不当威胁（例如，违反诚实信用和公平交易的合同义务的威胁）引起的，则胁迫可以作为合同可执行性的抗辩。发生这种情况时，如果受威胁方出现以下情况，合同将无效并可以撤销：\n• 没有合理的选择，只能屈服于不当威胁，并且\n• 立即（即在合理的时间内）取消合同。在这里，机械师拒绝交付卡车，除非园林设计师额外支付 2,000 美元（不当威胁），这违反了他的诚信和公平交易义务。由于园林设计师需要立即使用卡车来履行现有的合同义务，并且没有类似的租赁卡车可用，因此他别无选择，只能屈服于威胁（没有合理的选择）。因此，如果他立即否认，他的承诺就无法执行。 （选择B）当一方因受到支配者（例如狱警/囚犯）或与同意方（例如律师/委托人）有特殊信任和信心关系的人的不公平或过度说服而同意时，不当影响使合同无效——这两种情况在这里都没有看到。 （选择 C）由于园艺师需要立即使用卡车来履行现有的合同义务，因此他后来通过法律诉讼获得占有权的能力并不是一个合理的选择。 （选项D）尽管修改后的合同价格符合惯例且合理，但这种修改仍然是由不当威胁引发的，因为它违反了机械师的诚实信用和公平交易义务。\n\n教育目标：如果受害人 (1) 没有合理的选择，只能屈从于不当威胁，并且 (2) 立即（即在合理的时间内）否认合同，则在另一缔约方的不当威胁诱导下签订的合同是无效的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 174–176（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（由身体强迫和不当威胁造成的胁迫）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为园林设计师没有合理的选择，只能屈服于机械师的错误威胁。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-59783d1d75553d09"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":61,"sourceQuestionPage":178,"sourceQuestionPages":[178],"sourceExplanationPages":[179,180],"sourceAnswerPages":[179,180],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-062","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Capacity & Ratification","question":"A 17-year-old boy started a business mowing lawns for his neighbors. As the boy's business grew, he received requests for lawn services from homeowners in neighborhoods located several miles away. The boy lived with his parents and had use of the family's old pickup truck after 4:00 p.m. each day to carry his lawn maintenance equipment to the other neighborhoods. However, the boy believed that it was necessary to own a truck that could be used for his business at any hour. He therefore entered a contract with a used-car dealership to purchase a truck. Which of the following statements best describes the contract between the boy and the used-car dealership?","choices":{"A":"The contract for necessaries is enforceable.","B":"The contract is voidable only by the boy.","C":"The contract is voidable by either party.","D":"The contract is void and unenforceable."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An unmarried, unemancipated minor (ie, person under the age of 18) lacks capacity to contract, so any contract entered by the minor is voidable by the minor. However, the contract is not voidable by the other contracting party. This means that the minor has the sole power to either:\n• void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming the contract through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under the contract or\n\n• ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) otherwise agreeing to be bound by it.\n\nHowever, if the minor seeks to void a contract for necessaries, then the minor can still incur liability for the reasonable value of the necessaries under a quasi-contract theory. \"Necessaries\" is a flexible term that generally includes food, clothing, shelter, and anything else that (1) is actually required for the minor's support or well-being and (2) has not been provided by the minor's parent or guardian. Here, the 17-year-old boy contracted with the dealership to purchase a truck because he believed that it was necessary to own a truck that could be used for his business. But the boy did not actually require a new truck because he had use of the family's pickup truck to carry his lawn maintenance equipment to other neighborhoods. Therefore, the necessaries exception does not apply here, and the contract is voidable only by the boy (Choices A, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: Minors lack capacity to contract, so a contract entered by a minor is voidable by the minor. However, a minor who chooses to disaffirm a contract for necessaries (eg, food, clothing, shelter) must pay for their reasonable value.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity based on infancy).\n\n• 42 Am. Jur. 2d Infants §§ 56–63 (2020) (infant liability for necessaries).\n\n• 42 Am. Jur. 2d Infants §§ 64–69 (2020) (examples of necessaries).","educationalObjective":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a contract entered by a minor is voidable by the minor. However, a minor who chooses to disaffirm a contract for necessaries (eg, food, clothing, shelter) must pay for their reasonable value.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 14 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (incapacity based on infancy).\n\n• 42 Am. Jur. 2d Infants §§ 56–63 (2020) (infant liability for necessaries).\n\n• 42 Am. Jur. 2d Infants §§ 64–69 (2020) (examples of necessaries).","rule":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a contract entered by a minor is voidable by the minor. However, a minor who chooses to disaffirm a contract for necessaries (eg, food, clothing, shelter) must pay for their reasonable value.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements best describes the contract between the boy and the used-car dealership?","rule":"Minors lack capacity to contract, so a contract entered by a minor is voidable by the minor. However, a minor who chooses to disaffirm a contract for necessaries (eg, food, clothing, shelter) must pay for their reasonable value.","analysis":"An unmarried, unemancipated minor (ie, person under the age of 18) lacks capacity to contract, so any contract entered by the minor is voidable by the minor. However, the contract is not voidable by the other contracting party. This means that the minor has the sole power to either:\n• void the contract before or soon after turning 18 by (1) disaffirming the contract through words or actions and (2) returning any still-existing benefit received under the contract or\n\n• ratify the contract after turning 18 by (1) failing to timely void the contract or (2) otherwise agreeing to be bound by it.\n\nHowever, if the minor seeks to void a contract for necessaries, then the minor can still incur liability for the reasonable value of the necessaries under a quasi-contract theory. \"Necessaries\" is a flexible term that generally includes food, clothing, shelter, and anything else that (1) is actually required for the minor's support or well-being and (2) has not been provided by the minor's parent or guardian. Here, the 17-year-old boy contracted with the dealership to purchase a truck because he believed that it was necessary to own a truck that could be used for his business. But the boy did not actually require a new truck because he had use of the family's pickup truck to carry his lawn maintenance equipment to other neighborhoods. Therefore, the necessaries exception does not apply here, and the contract is voidable only by the boy (Choices A, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer B: The contract is voidable only by the boy."},"questionZh":"一名 17 岁的男孩开始为邻居修剪草坪。随着男孩生意的增长，他收到了几英里外社区的房主提出的草坪服务请求。男孩与父母住在一起，下午 4:00 后使用了家里的旧皮卡车。每天把他的草坪维护设备带到其他社区。然而，男孩认为有必要拥有一辆可以随时用于他的生意的卡车。因此，他与一家二手车经销商签订了购买卡车的合同。以下哪项陈述最能描述男孩与二手车经销店之间的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"必需品合同是有效的。","B":"只有男孩可以撤销该合同。","C":"任何一方均可撤销该合同。","D":"该合同无效且不可执行。"},"explanationZh":"未婚、无行为能力的未成年人（即18岁以下的人）没有合同行为能力，因此未成年人签订的任何合同均可以被未成年人撤销。但是，另一方不得撤销合同。这意味着未成年人拥有以下唯一权力：\n• 在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久，通过以下方式使合同无效：(1) 通过言语或行动否定合同，以及 (2) 退还根据合同收到的任何仍然存在的福利，或\n\n• 年满 18 岁后，如果 (1) 未能及时使合同无效或 (2) 以其他方式同意受合同约束，则批准合同。\n\n然而，如果未成年人寻求使生活必需品合同无效，那么根据准合同理论，未成年人仍然可以对生活必需品的合理价值承担责任。 “必需品”是一个灵活的术语，通常包括食物、衣服、住所以及 (1) 未成年人的支持或福祉实际需要的以及 (2) 未成年人的父母或监护人未提供的任何其他物品。在这里，这位 17 岁的男孩与经销商签订了购买一辆卡车的合同，因为他认为有必要拥有一辆可以用于他的生意的卡车。但男孩实际上并不需要一辆新卡车，因为他使用家里的皮卡车将草坪维护设备运送到其他社区。因此，必要的例外情况在此不适用，并且只有男孩才可以撤销合同（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此未成年人签订的合同可由未成年人撤销。然而，选择取消必需品（例如食物、衣服、住所）合同的未成年人必须支付其合理价值。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于婴儿期的无行为能力）。\n\n• 上午 42 点。尤尔。 2d 婴儿 §§ 56–63 (2020)（婴儿必需品责任）。\n\n• 上午 42 点。尤尔。 2d 婴儿 §§ 64–69 (2020)（必需品示例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"未成年人没有订立合同的能力，因此未成年人签订的合同可以被未成年人撤销。然而，选择取消必需品（例如食物、衣服、住所）合同的未成年人必须支付其合理价值。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述最能描述男孩与二手车经销店之间的合同？","rule":"未成年人没有订立合同的能力，因此未成年人签订的合同可以被未成年人撤销。然而，选择取消必需品（例如食物、衣服、住所）合同的未成年人必须支付其合理价值。","analysis":"未婚、无行为能力的未成年人（即18岁以下的人）没有合同行为能力，因此未成年人签订的任何合同均可以被未成年人撤销。但是，另一方不得撤销合同。这意味着未成年人拥有以下唯一权力：\n• 在年满 18 岁之前或之后不久，通过以下方式使合同无效：(1) 通过言语或行动否定合同，以及 (2) 退还根据合同收到的任何仍然存在的福利，或\n\n• 年满 18 岁后，如果 (1) 未能及时使合同无效或 (2) 以其他方式同意受合同约束，则批准合同。\n\n然而，如果未成年人寻求使生活必需品合同无效，那么根据准合同理论，未成年人仍然可以对生活必需品的合理价值承担责任。 “必需品”是一个灵活的术语，通常包括食物、衣服、住所以及 (1) 未成年人的支持或福祉实际需要的以及 (2) 未成年人的父母或监护人未提供的任何其他物品。在这里，这位 17 岁的男孩与经销商签订了购买一辆卡车的合同，因为他认为有必要拥有一辆可以用于他的生意的卡车。但男孩实际上并不需要一辆新卡车，因为他使用家里的皮卡车将草坪维护设备运送到其他社区。因此，必要的例外情况在此不适用，并且只有男孩才可以撤销合同（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：未成年人缺乏订立合同的能力，因此未成年人签订的合同可由未成年人撤销。然而，选择取消必需品（例如食物、衣服、住所）合同的未成年人必须支付其合理价值。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 14（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于婴儿期的无行为能力）。\n\n• 上午 42 点。尤尔。 2d 婴儿 §§ 56–63 (2020)（婴儿必需品责任）。\n\n• 上午 42 点。尤尔。 2d 婴儿 §§ 64–69 (2020)（必需品示例）。","conclusion":"答案 B：只有男孩可以撤销该合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5991d25e65a10fc9"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":62,"sourceQuestionPage":181,"sourceQuestionPages":[181],"sourceExplanationPages":[182,183],"sourceAnswerPages":[182,183],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-063","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"On March 15, in a writing signed by both parties, a manufacturer agreed to sell 40,000 pens at $1 each to a retailer, delivery to be made in two equal installments on April 1 and May 1. The contract was silent as to the time of payment, but on March 25 the two parties orally agreed that the entire purchase price was to be paid on delivery of the second installment. On April 1, the manufacturer delivered 20,000 pens, and the retailer accepted them. The manufacturer then demanded payment of $20,000. When the retailer refused to make the payment, the manufacturer sued the retailer for breach of contract. In its defense, the retailer proffered evidence of the March 25 oral agreement. Is the manufacturer likely to succeed in its action?","choices":{"A":"No, because even though the March 25 oral agreement is not effective, payment is due at the time of the second installment.","B":"No, because the March 25 oral agreement was an effective modification of the written contract.","C":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule bars the introduction of evidence of an oral agreement modifying a written contract.","D":"Yes, because there was no consideration to support the modification."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Unwritten modifications of contracts\n\nContract silent on modification\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies (common law & UCC)\n\nContract clause Common\n• Clause unenforceable requires written law\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute modification of frauds applies UCC\n• Clause enforceable (ie, unwritten modification invalid) if: – contract written & signed – clause separately signed by nonmerchant if sued by merchant\n• Clause waived (ie, unwritten modification valid) if: – parties intended unwritten modification – one party materially changed position in reliance UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more (eg, 40,000 pens at $1 each) must be in a writing that states the quantity of goods to be sold and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. However, modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies this statute need not be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold. Here, the March 15 contract for the sale of pens needed to be, and was, in a writing that satisfied the statute of frauds. But the March 25 agreement that the entire purchase price would be paid on delivery of the second installment did not need to be in writing since it affected neither subject matter nor quantity. Therefore, the March 25 oral agreement was an effective modification of the written contract. And since the retailer's refusal to pay for the first installment on delivery was not a breach, the manufacturer is not likely to succeed. (Choice A) Where a contract for the sale of goods is silent as to the time of payment, the UCC \"fills the gap\" with a provision that payment is due when the buyer receives the goods. Therefore, had the March 25 oral agreement been ineffective, payment for each installment of pens would have been due upon receipt.\n\n(Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars extrinsic evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement (eg, March 15 contract). But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, March 25 oral agreement). (Choice D) Under the UCC, contract modifications are binding without consideration so long as they are made in good faith. This requires honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing in the trade.\n\nEducational objective: Modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies the UCC statute of frauds do NOT need to be in writing unless they affect subject matter or quantity.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (contract modification requirements).","educationalObjective":"Modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies the UCC statute of frauds do NOT need to be in writing unless they affect subject matter or quantity.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (contract modification requirements).","rule":"Modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies the UCC statute of frauds do NOT need to be in writing unless they affect subject matter or quantity.","irac":{"issue":"Is the manufacturer likely to succeed in its action?","rule":"Modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies the UCC statute of frauds do NOT need to be in writing unless they affect subject matter or quantity.","analysis":"Unwritten modifications of contracts\n\nContract silent on modification\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies (common law & UCC)\n\nContract clause Common\n• Clause unenforceable requires written law\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute modification of frauds applies UCC\n• Clause enforceable (ie, unwritten modification invalid) if: – contract written & signed – clause separately signed by nonmerchant if sued by merchant\n• Clause waived (ie, unwritten modification valid) if: – parties intended unwritten modification – one party materially changed position in reliance UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more (eg, 40,000 pens at $1 each) must be in a writing that states the quantity of goods to be sold and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. However, modifications to a contract that falls within and satisfies this statute need not be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold. Here, the March 15 contract for the sale of pens needed to be, and was, in a writing that satisfied the statute of frauds. But the March 25 agreement that the entire purchase price would be paid on delivery of the second installment did not need to be in writing since it affected neither subject matter nor quantity. Therefore, the March 25 oral agreement was an effective modification of the written contract. And since the retailer's refusal to pay for the first installment on delivery was not a breach, the manufacturer is not likely to succeed. (Choice A) Where a contract for the sale of goods is silent as to the time of payment, the UCC \"fills the gap\" with a provision that payment is due when the buyer receives the goods. Therefore, had the March 25 oral agreement been ineffective, payment for each installment of pens would have been due upon receipt.\n\n(Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars extrinsic evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement (eg, March 15 contract). But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, March 25 oral agreement). (Choice D) Under the UCC, contract modifications are binding without consideration so long as they are made in good faith. This requires honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing in the trade.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the March 25 oral agreement was an effective modification of the written contract."},"questionZh":"3月15日，在双方签署的一份书面文件中，一家制造商同意以每支1美元的价格向零售商出售40,000支钢笔，并于4月1日和5月1日分两次等额交货。合同中没有明确付款时间，但3月25日，双方口头同意在第二批交货时支付全部货款。 4月1日，制造商交付了20,000支钢笔，零售商接受了。制造商随后要求支付20,000美元。当零售商拒绝付款时，制造商起诉零售商违反合同。在辩护中，该零售商提供了 3 月 25 日口头协议的证据。制造商的行动有可能成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为尽管 3 月 25 日的口头协议无效，但第二期付款时仍需付款。","B":"不会，因为 3 月 25 日的口头协议是对书面合同的有效修改。","C":"是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止引入修改书面合同的口头协议的证据。","D":"是的，因为没有约因支持修改。"},"explanationZh":"合同的不成文修改\n\n合同对修改保持沉默\n• 除非欺诈法适用，否则不成文的修改有效（普通法和 UCC）\n\n合同条款 通用\n• 不可执行的条款需要成文法\n• 除非防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）修改适用 UCC，否则不成文修改有效\n• 条款可执行（即，非书面修改无效），如果： – 合同已书面签署 – 如果被商家起诉，则由非商家单独签署条款\n• 条款被放弃（即，不成文修改有效），如果： – 各方有意进行不成文修改 – 一方实质上改变了依赖UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）的立场。\n\n根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），销售价值 500 美元或以上的商品（例如，40,000 笔，每支 1 美元）的合同必须采用书面形式，说明待售商品的数量，并由寻求执行的一方签署。然而，符合本法规的合同修改不需要以书面形式进行，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售货物的数量。在这里，3 月 15 日的钢笔销售合同需要而且确实是符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面形式。但 3 月 25 日达成的关于在交付第二批货款时支付全部货款的协议不需要采用书面形式，因为它既不影响标的物也不影响数量。因此，3月25日的口头协议是对书面合同的有效修改。由于零售商拒绝支付交货时的第一期付款并不构成违约，因此制造商不太可能成功。 （选择A）如果货物销售合同没有明确规定付款时间，则UCC“填补了空白”，规定买方收到货物时即应付款。因此，如果 3 月 25 日的口头协议无效，则每笔分期付款应在收到时支付。\n\n（选择C） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）一般禁止使用事先口头或书面协议的外在证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款（例如，3 月15 日的合同）。但该规则并不禁止提供双方随后达成的协议的证据（例如，3 月 25 日的口头协议）。 （选项D）根据UCC，只要善意地进行合同修改，无需对价即可具有约束力。这需要诚实并遵守贸易中公平交易的合理商业标准。\n\n教育目标：对符合 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同的修改不需要采用书面形式，除非它们影响标的物或数量。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（合同修改要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对符合 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同的修改不需要采用书面形式，除非它们影响标的物或数量。","iracZh":{"issue":"制造商的行动有可能成功吗？","rule":"对符合 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同的修改不需要采用书面形式，除非它们影响标的物或数量。","analysis":"合同的不成文修改\n\n合同对修改保持沉默\n• 除非欺诈法适用，否则不成文的修改有效（普通法和 UCC）\n\n合同条款 通用\n• 不可执行的条款需要成文法\n• 除非防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）修改适用 UCC，否则不成文修改有效\n• 条款可执行（即，非书面修改无效），如果： – 合同已书面签署 – 如果被商家起诉，则由非商家单独签署条款\n• 条款被放弃（即，不成文修改有效），如果： – 各方有意进行不成文修改 – 一方实质上改变了依赖UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）的立场。\n\n根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），销售价值 500 美元或以上的商品（例如，40,000 笔，每支 1 美元）的合同必须采用书面形式，说明待售商品的数量，并由寻求执行的一方签署。然而，符合本法规的合同修改不需要以书面形式进行，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售货物的数量。在这里，3 月 15 日的钢笔销售合同需要而且确实是符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面形式。但 3 月 25 日达成的关于在交付第二批货款时支付全部货款的协议不需要采用书面形式，因为它既不影响标的物也不影响数量。因此，3月25日的口头协议是对书面合同的有效修改。由于零售商拒绝支付交货时的第一期付款并不构成违约，因此制造商不太可能成功。 （选择A）如果货物销售合同没有明确规定付款时间，则UCC“填补了空白”，规定买方收到货物时即应付款。因此，如果 3 月 25 日的口头协议无效，则每笔分期付款应在收到时支付。\n\n（选择C） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）一般禁止使用事先口头或书面协议的外在证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款（例如，3 月15 日的合同）。但该规则并不禁止提供双方随后达成的协议的证据（例如，3 月 25 日的口头协议）。 （选项D）根据UCC，只要善意地进行合同修改，无需对价即可具有约束力。这需要诚实并遵守贸易中公平交易的合理商业标准。\n\n教育目标：对符合 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同的修改不需要采用书面形式，除非它们影响标的物或数量。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（合同修改要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为 3 月 25 日的口头协议是对书面合同的有效修改。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":63,"sourceQuestionPage":184,"sourceQuestionPages":[184],"sourceExplanationPages":[185,186],"sourceAnswerPages":[185,186],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-064","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A debtor's $1,000 contractual obligation to the aunt was due on July 1. On the preceding June 15, the aunt called her niece and said, \"As my birthday gift to you, you may collect on July 1 the $1,000 the debtor owes me.\" The aunt also called the debtor and told him to pay the $1,000 to her niece on July 1. On July 1, the debtor, saying that he did not like the niece and wouldn't pay anything to her, paid the $1,000 to the aunt, who accepted it without objection. Will the niece succeed in an action for $1,000 against the debtor?","choices":{"A":"No, because the aunt's acceptance of the $1,000, without objection, was in effect the revocation of a gratuitous assignment.","B":"No, because the debtor cannot be compelled to render performance to an assignee whom he finds personally objectionable.","C":"Yes, because the aunt had effectively assigned the $1,000 debt to her.","D":"Yes, because the aunt's calls to the niece and the debtor effected a novation."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract An assignment is the transfer of rights under a contract to a third party. A gratuitous assignment—ie, one that is not supported by consideration such as money—can generally be revoked by words or conduct. Here, the aunt transferred her right to collect the $1,000 debt to her niece. The assignment was gratuitous because it was a birthday gift to the niece and the aunt asked for nothing in return. However, the debtor paid the $1,000 to the aunt—not the niece. By accepting the $1,000 without objection, the aunt effectively revoked the gratuitous assignment to the niece (Choice C). Therefore, the niece will not succeed in an action against the debtor. (Choice B) There is no rule that excuses a party from rendering performance to an assignee whom the party finds personally objectionable. (Choice D) A novation is a substitute contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party. But here, the debtor never agreed to replace the aunt with the niece (no novation).\n\nEducational objective: An assignment (ie, transfer of contractual rights) that is not supported by consideration can be revoked by words or conduct.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 321 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (assignment of future rights).","educationalObjective":"An assignment (ie, transfer of contractual rights) that is not supported by consideration can be revoked by words or conduct.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 321 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (assignment of future rights).","rule":"An assignment (ie, transfer of contractual rights) that is not supported by consideration can be revoked by words or conduct.","irac":{"issue":"Will the niece succeed in an action for $1,000 against the debtor?","rule":"An assignment (ie, transfer of contractual rights) that is not supported by consideration can be revoked by words or conduct.","analysis":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nLimitations\n• Assignment permitted unless it: – materially increases obligor's contractual duties or risks – materially reduces obligor's chance of obtaining performance or – is void (not merely prohibited) under contract provision Requirements\n• Present intent to immediately assign right\n• Consideration generally required to make assignment irrevocable – Exceptions: promissory estoppel, delivery of document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate), delivery of written & signed assignment Assignee's\n• Assumes all of assignor's rights as contract stands at time of rights assignment, subject to claims/defenses that: – accrued before obligor received notice of assignment or – stem from assignor's present or future failure to perform\n• Assignor loses rights & therefore cannot enforce contract An assignment is the transfer of rights under a contract to a third party. A gratuitous assignment—ie, one that is not supported by consideration such as money—can generally be revoked by words or conduct. Here, the aunt transferred her right to collect the $1,000 debt to her niece. The assignment was gratuitous because it was a birthday gift to the niece and the aunt asked for nothing in return. However, the debtor paid the $1,000 to the aunt—not the niece. By accepting the $1,000 without objection, the aunt effectively revoked the gratuitous assignment to the niece (Choice C). Therefore, the niece will not succeed in an action against the debtor. (Choice B) There is no rule that excuses a party from rendering performance to an assignee whom the party finds personally objectionable. (Choice D) A novation is a substitute contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party. But here, the debtor never agreed to replace the aunt with the niece (no novation).","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the aunt's acceptance of the $1,000, without objection, was in effect the revocation of a gratuitous assignment."},"questionZh":"债务人对阿姨的 1,000 美元合同债务将于 7 月 1 日到期。此前的 6 月 15 日，阿姨打电话给她的侄女说：“作为我送给你的生日礼物，你可以在 7 月 1 日领取债务人欠我的 1,000 美元。”阿姨还打电话给债务人，让他在7月1日把这1000元还给她的侄女。7月1日，债务人以不喜欢侄女，不会给她任何东西为由，把这1000元付给了阿姨，阿姨毫无异议地接受了。侄女向债务人索要 1,000 美元的诉讼会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为姨妈毫无异议地接受了这 1,000 美元，实际上就等于撤销无偿转让。","B":"不可以，因为债务人不能被迫向他个人反对的受让人履行义务。","C":"是的，因为阿姨实际上已经将 1,000 美元的债务分配给了她。","D":"是的，因为姨妈打电话给侄女和债务人实现了更替。"},"explanationZh":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 转让是将合同项下的权利转让给第三方。无偿转让——即没有金钱等对价支持的转让——通常可以通过言语或行为撤销。在这里，阿姨将追收1000美元债务的权利转让给了她的侄女。这项作业是无偿的，因为这是送给侄女的生日礼物，阿姨不求任何回报。然而，债务人将这 1,000 美元付给了阿姨，而不是侄女。通过毫无异议地接受 1,000 美元，阿姨实际上撤销了对侄女的无偿转让（选项 C）。因此，侄女对债务人的诉讼不会成功。 （选项B） 没有任何规则可以免除一方当事人向其个人反感的受让人履行义务的责任。 （选项D） 更替是一种替代合同，其中当事人同意用新当事人取代原来的当事人。但在这里，债务人从未同意用侄女取代阿姨（无更新）。\n\n教育目标：没有对价支持的转让（即合同权利的转让）可以通过言语或行为撤销。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 321（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（未来权利的转让）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"没有对价支持的转让（即合同权利的转让）可以通过言语或行为撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"侄女向债务人索要 1,000 美元的诉讼会成功吗？","rule":"没有对价支持的转让（即合同权利的转让）可以通过言语或行为撤销。","analysis":"合同权利的转让\n\n局限性\n• 允许转让，除非： – 实质上增加了债务人的合同义务或风险 – 实质上减少了债务人获得履约的机会，或者 – 根据合同条款无效（不仅仅是禁止） 要求\n• 提出立即分配权利的意图\n• 使转让不可撤销通常需要考虑 – 例外：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）、交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）、交付受让人书面和签名的转让\n• 假定转让人的所有权利在权利转让时均已生效，但须遵守以下索赔/抗辩： – 在债务人收到转让通知之前产生的权利，或者 – 源自转让人当前或将来未能履行的权利\n• 转让人失去权利，因此无法执行合同 转让是将合同项下的权利转让给第三方。无偿转让——即没有金钱等对价支持的转让——通常可以通过言语或行为撤销。在这里，阿姨将追收1000美元债务的权利转让给了她的侄女。这项作业是无偿的，因为这是送给侄女的生日礼物，阿姨不求任何回报。然而，债务人将这 1,000 美元付给了阿姨，而不是侄女。通过毫无异议地接受 1,000 美元，阿姨实际上撤销了对侄女的无偿转让（选项 C）。因此，侄女对债务人的诉讼不会成功。 （选项B） 没有任何规则可以免除一方当事人向其个人反感的受让人履行义务的责任。 （选项D） 更替是一种替代合同，其中当事人同意用新当事人取代原来的当事人。但在这里，债务人从未同意用侄女取代阿姨（无更新）。\n\n教育目标：没有对价支持的转让（即合同权利的转让）可以通过言语或行为撤销。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 321（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（未来权利的转让）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为姨妈毫无异议地接受了这 1,000 美元，实际上就等于撤销无偿转让。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":64,"sourceQuestionPage":187,"sourceQuestionPages":[187],"sourceExplanationPages":[188,189],"sourceAnswerPages":[188,189],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-065","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"An aircraft broker sought to purchase on behalf of its client a private jet from a fleet owned by a corporation. In a telephone conversation between the broker and the corporation's finance manager, the broker offered $9 million for the jet. The manager agreed on the condition that the broker give the corporation a $250,000 deposit toward the jet's purchase price and a copy of the broker's agreement with the client. The broker complied with the manager's request. Subsequently, the broker and the corporation exchanged written drafts of a contract memorializing their agreement. None of the drafts were signed by the parties. Two months later, the corporation notified the broker that the jet was not for sale and disputed the existence of an enforceable contract. Is there an enforceable contract between the broker and the corporation?","choices":{"A":"No, because partial payment of the jet's purchase price did not take the contract out of the statute of frauds.","B":"No, because the broker never signed a writing evidencing a contract with the corporation.","C":"Yes, because the agreement was memorialized in the written drafts of the contract exchanged by the parties.","D":"Yes, because the broker paid $250,000 for the jet and the corporation accepted that payment."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more (eg, private jet sold for $9 million). These contracts generally must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought to be enforceable. However, one exception applies when some amount of payment was made and accepted.* The effect of a partial payment turns on whether the contract is for:\n• separable goods – in which case, the contract is enforceable up to the quantity of goods paid for in full or\n\n• an indivisible good – in which case, the entire contract is enforceable.\n\nHere, the broker–corporation contract violated the statute of frauds because none of the written drafts exchanged by the parties were signed (Choice C). However, the broker paid $250,000 (partial payment) toward the jet (indivisible good) and the corporation presumably accepted that payment. Therefore, the contract is excepted from the statute of frauds and is enforceable in its entirety (Choice A). *The exception also applies when some amount of goods have been received and accepted. (Choice B) The fact that the broker never signed a writing evidencing a contract with the corporation is irrelevant. That is because the corporation is the party against whom enforcement is sought and the one whose signature was therefore needed to satisfy the statute of frauds.\n\nEducational objective: A contract is excepted from the UCC statute of frauds when some amount of payment was made and accepted. A partial payment for separable goods renders the contract enforceable only up to the quantity paid for in full, while a partial payment for an indivisible good renders the entire contract enforceable. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds formal requirements).","educationalObjective":"A contract is excepted from the UCC statute of frauds when some amount of payment was made and accepted. A partial payment for separable goods renders the contract enforceable only up to the quantity paid for in full, while a partial payment for an indivisible good renders the entire contract enforceable. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds formal requirements).","rule":"A contract is excepted from the UCC statute of frauds when some amount of payment was made and accepted. A partial payment for separable goods renders the contract enforceable only up to the quantity paid for in full, while a partial payment for an indivisible good renders the entire contract enforceable. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"Is there an enforceable contract between the broker and the corporation?","rule":"A contract is excepted from the UCC statute of frauds when some amount of payment was made and accepted. A partial payment for separable goods renders the contract enforceable only up to the quantity paid for in full, while a partial payment for an indivisible good renders the entire contract enforceable. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"The UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more (eg, private jet sold for $9 million). These contracts generally must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought to be enforceable. However, one exception applies when some amount of payment was made and accepted.* The effect of a partial payment turns on whether the contract is for:\n• separable goods – in which case, the contract is enforceable up to the quantity of goods paid for in full or\n\n• an indivisible good – in which case, the entire contract is enforceable.\n\nHere, the broker–corporation contract violated the statute of frauds because none of the written drafts exchanged by the parties were signed (Choice C). However, the broker paid $250,000 (partial payment) toward the jet (indivisible good) and the corporation presumably accepted that payment. Therefore, the contract is excepted from the statute of frauds and is enforceable in its entirety (Choice A). *The exception also applies when some amount of goods have been received and accepted. (Choice B) The fact that the broker never signed a writing evidencing a contract with the corporation is irrelevant. That is because the corporation is the party against whom enforcement is sought and the one whose signature was therefore needed to satisfy the statute of frauds.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the broker paid $250,000 for the jet and the corporation accepted that payment."},"questionZh":"一位飞机经纪人试图代表其客户从一家公司拥有的机队购买一架私人飞机。在经纪人和公司财务经理的电话交谈中，经纪人出价 900 万美元购买这架飞机。经理同意了，但条件是经纪人向公司支付 250,000 美元的飞机购买价格押金，并提供一份经纪人与客户的协议副本。经纪人答应了经理的要求。随后，经纪人和公司交换了一份书面合同草稿，以纪念他们的协议。各方均未签署任何草案。两个月后，该公司通知经纪人该飞机不予出售，并对可执行合同的存在提出异议。经纪人和公司之间是否有可执行的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为部分支付飞机购买价并不能使合同脱离欺诈法。","B":"不，因为经纪人从未签署过证明与公司签订合同的书面文件。","C":"是的，因为该协议已记录在双方交换的合同的书面草稿中。","D":"是的，因为经纪人为这架飞机支付了 250,000 美元，而公司接受了这笔付款。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（例如，以 900 万美元出售的私人飞机）。这些合同通常必须采用书面形式，并由寻求强制执行的一方签署。但是，当支付并接受一定金额的付款时，存在一个例外情况。*部分付款的效果取决于合同是否用于：\n• 可分离货物——在这种情况下，合同在全额支付的货物数量或\n\n• 不可分割的商品——在这种情况下，整个合同都是可执行的。\n\n在此，经纪公司合同违反了欺诈法，因为双方交换的书面草稿均未签署（选项C）。然而，经纪人为飞机（不可分割的商品）支付了 250,000 美元（部分付款），公司大概接受了这笔付款。因此，该合同不受欺诈法的约束，并且完全可执行（选择 A）。 *当已收到并接受一定数量的货物时，例外情况也适用。 （选择 B）经纪人从未签署证明与公司签订合同的书面文件这一事实是无关紧要的。这是因为公司是寻求执行的一方，因此需要其签名才能满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。\n\n教育目标：当支付并接受一定金额的付款时，合同不受 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的约束。对可分离货物的部分付款使合同仅在全额付款的数量范围内可执行，而对不可分割货物的部分付款使整个合同可执行。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）正式要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当支付并接受一定金额的付款时，合同不受 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的约束。对可分离货物的部分付款使合同仅在全额付款的数量范围内可执行，而对不可分割货物的部分付款使整个合同可执行。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"经纪人和公司之间是否有可执行的合同？","rule":"当支付并接受一定金额的付款时，合同不受 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的约束。对可分离货物的部分付款使合同仅在全额付款的数量范围内可执行，而对不可分割货物的部分付款使整个合同可执行。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（例如，以 900 万美元出售的私人飞机）。这些合同通常必须采用书面形式，并由寻求强制执行的一方签署。但是，当支付并接受一定金额的付款时，存在一个例外情况。*部分付款的效果取决于合同是否用于：\n• 可分离货物——在这种情况下，合同在全额支付的货物数量或\n\n• 不可分割的商品——在这种情况下，整个合同都是可执行的。\n\n在此，经纪公司合同违反了欺诈法，因为双方交换的书面草稿均未签署（选项C）。然而，经纪人为飞机（不可分割的商品）支付了 250,000 美元（部分付款），公司大概接受了这笔付款。因此，该合同不受欺诈法的约束，并且完全可执行（选择 A）。 *当已收到并接受一定数量的货物时，例外情况也适用。 （选择 B）经纪人从未签署证明与公司签订合同的书面文件这一事实是无关紧要的。这是因为公司是寻求执行的一方，因此需要其签名才能满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。\n\n教育目标：当支付并接受一定金额的付款时，合同不受 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的约束。对可分离货物的部分付款使合同仅在全额付款的数量范围内可执行，而对不可分割货物的部分付款使整个合同可执行。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）正式要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为经纪人为这架飞机支付了 250,000 美元，而公司接受了这笔付款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-ff717ef45c3ce87b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":65,"sourceQuestionPage":190,"sourceQuestionPages":[190],"sourceExplanationPages":[191,192],"sourceAnswerPages":[191,192],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-066","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"For an agreed price of $20 million, a construction company contracted with a real estate developer to design and build on the developer's commercial plot a 15-story office building. In excavating for the foundation and underground utilities, the construction company encountered a massive layer of granite at a depth of 15 feet. By reasonable safety criteria, the building's foundation required a minimum excavation of 25 feet. When the contract was made, neither the real estate developer nor the construction company was aware of the subsurface granite and neither party had hired a qualified expert to test for subsurface granite. Claiming accurately that removal of enough granite to permit the construction as planned would cost the construction company an additional $3 million and a probable net loss on the contract of $2 million, the construction company refused to proceed with the work unless the real estate developer would promise to pay an additional $2.5 million for the completed building. If the real estate developer refuses and sues the construction company for breach of contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?","choices":{"A":"The construction company is excused, because the contract is voidable on account of the parties' mutual mistake concerning an essential underlying fact.","B":"The construction company is excused under the modern doctrine of supervening impossibility, which includes severe impracticability.","C":"The real estate developer prevails, because the construction company assumed the risk of encountering subsurface granite that was unknown to the real estate developer.","D":"The real estate developer prevails, unless subsurface granite was previously unknown anywhere in the vicinity of the real estate developer's construction site."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When party assumes risk of mistake\n\nExpress Contract allocates risk to party\n\nConscious Party enters contract despite conscious ignorance of facts related to ignorance mistake\n\nAdjudication Court allocates risk to party because reasonable under circumstances\n\nWhen both parties enter a contract based on the same mistake (ie, mutual mistake), the contract is voidable by an adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n\n• the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nHere, when the contract was made, neither party knew that there was a massive layer of granite 15 feet below the surface. That mutual mistake related to a basic assumption of the contract since the building's foundation required a minimum excavation of 25 feet. And it materially affected the agreed-upon exchange since removing the granite would cost the construction company $3 million and likely cause a net loss of $2 million. However, the court will probably decide that the construction company assumed the risk of encountering the subsurface granite because:\n• there is a foreseeable risk of encountering subsurface granite in excavation projects like this one and\n\n• as a professional builder, the construction company should have been aware of this risk and so acted unreasonably by failing to hire a qualified expert to test for subsurface granite (Choice D).\n\nTherefore, the real estate developer will likely prevail in a breach-of-contract suit against the construction company (Choice A). (Choice B) As with mutual mistake, the doctrine of impossibility does not apply where the adversely affected party assumed the risk of the event that rendered performance impossible or severely impracticable (here, encountering subsurface granite).\n\nEducational objective: Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (one party's mistake makes contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when party bears risk of mistake).","educationalObjective":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (one party's mistake makes contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when party bears risk of mistake).","rule":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"If the real estate developer refuses and sues the construction company for breach of contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?","rule":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","analysis":"When party assumes risk of mistake\n\nExpress Contract allocates risk to party\n\nConscious Party enters contract despite conscious ignorance of facts related to ignorance mistake\n\nAdjudication Court allocates risk to party because reasonable under circumstances\n\nWhen both parties enter a contract based on the same mistake (ie, mutual mistake), the contract is voidable by an adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n\n• the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nHere, when the contract was made, neither party knew that there was a massive layer of granite 15 feet below the surface. That mutual mistake related to a basic assumption of the contract since the building's foundation required a minimum excavation of 25 feet. And it materially affected the agreed-upon exchange since removing the granite would cost the construction company $3 million and likely cause a net loss of $2 million. However, the court will probably decide that the construction company assumed the risk of encountering the subsurface granite because:\n• there is a foreseeable risk of encountering subsurface granite in excavation projects like this one and\n\n• as a professional builder, the construction company should have been aware of this risk and so acted unreasonably by failing to hire a qualified expert to test for subsurface granite (Choice D).\n\nTherefore, the real estate developer will likely prevail in a breach-of-contract suit against the construction company (Choice A). (Choice B) As with mutual mistake, the doctrine of impossibility does not apply where the adversely affected party assumed the risk of the event that rendered performance impossible or severely impracticable (here, encountering subsurface granite).","conclusion":"Answer C: The real estate developer prevails, because the construction company assumed the risk of encountering subsurface granite that was unknown to the real estate developer."},"questionZh":"一家建筑公司以 2000 万美元的商定价格与一家房地产开发商签订合同，在开发商的商业地块上设计并建造一座 15 层办公楼。在挖掘地基和地下公用设施时，建筑公司遇到了 15 英尺深的大块花岗岩层。根据合理的安全标准，建筑物的地基至少需要开挖 25 英尺。合同签订时，房地产开发商和建筑公司都不知道地下花岗岩，双方都没有聘请合格的专家对地下花岗岩进行检测。建筑公司准确地声称，拆除足够的花岗岩以允许按计划进行施工将导致建筑公司额外花费 300 万美元，并且可能导致合同净损失 200 万美元，因此建筑公司拒绝继续施工，除非房地产开发商承诺为完工的建筑额外支付 250 万美元。如果房地产开发商拒绝并起诉建筑公司违约，法院可能会判决以下哪项？","choicesZh":{"A":"建筑公司被免除责任，因为由于双方对基本事实的共同错误，合同可撤销。","B":"根据现代“不可能发生”的原则，建筑公司可以得到原谅，其中包括严重的不切实际。","C":"房地产开发商占了上风，因为建筑公司承担了遇到房地产开发商不知道的地下花岗岩的风险。","D":"除非房地产开发商的建筑工地附近的任何地方以前都不知道地下花岗岩，否则房地产开发商占上风。"},"explanationZh":"当一方承担错误风险时\n\n快速合同将风险分配给当事人\n\n尽管自觉不了解与无知错误相关的事实，但自觉方仍签订合同\n\n裁决法院将风险分配给当事人，因为在情况下是合理的\n\n当双方基于相同错误（即共同错误）签订合同时，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n在这里，签订合同时，双方都不知道地表以下 15 英尺处有一层巨大的花岗岩。这个共同的错误与合同的基本假设有关，因为建筑物的地基要求至少开挖 25 英尺。这对商定的交换产生了重大影响，因为拆除花岗岩将花费建筑公司 300 万美元，并可能导致 200 万美元的净损失。然而，法院可能会裁定建筑公司承担了遇到地下花岗岩的风险，因为：\n• 在像这样的挖掘项目中，存在遇到地下花岗岩的可预见的风险，\n\n• 作为专业建筑商，建筑公司应该意识到这一风险，因此采取了不合理的行为，没有聘请合格的专家来测试地下花岗岩（选择D）。\n\n因此，房地产开发商在针对建筑公司的违约诉讼中很可能胜诉（选项A）。 （选择 B）与共同错误一样，如果受到不利影响的一方承担了导致履行不可能或严重不切实际的事件的风险（此处为遇到地下花岗岩），则不可能原则不适用。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，相互错误会导致受到不利影响的一方撤销合同：(1) 错误与合同的基本假设相关，(2) 错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一方的错误导致合同无效）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方承担错误风险时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，相互错误使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果房地产开发商拒绝并起诉建筑公司违约，法院可能会判决以下哪项？","rule":"在以下情况下，相互错误使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。","analysis":"当一方承担错误风险时\n\n快速合同将风险分配给当事人\n\n尽管自觉不了解与无知错误相关的事实，但自觉方仍签订合同\n\n裁决法院将风险分配给当事人，因为在情况下是合理的\n\n当双方基于相同错误（即共同错误）签订合同时，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n在这里，签订合同时，双方都不知道地表以下 15 英尺处有一层巨大的花岗岩。这个共同的错误与合同的基本假设有关，因为建筑物的地基要求至少开挖 25 英尺。这对商定的交换产生了重大影响，因为拆除花岗岩将花费建筑公司 300 万美元，并可能导致 200 万美元的净损失。然而，法院可能会裁定建筑公司承担了遇到地下花岗岩的风险，因为：\n• 在像这样的挖掘项目中，存在遇到地下花岗岩的可预见的风险，\n\n• 作为专业建筑商，建筑公司应该意识到这一风险，因此采取了不合理的行为，没有聘请合格的专家来测试地下花岗岩（选择D）。\n\n因此，房地产开发商在针对建筑公司的违约诉讼中很可能胜诉（选项A）。 （选择 B）与共同错误一样，如果受到不利影响的一方承担了导致履行不可能或严重不切实际的事件的风险（此处为遇到地下花岗岩），则不可能原则不适用。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，相互错误会导致受到不利影响的一方撤销合同：(1) 错误与合同的基本假设相关，(2) 错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一方的错误导致合同无效）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方承担错误风险时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：房地产开发商占了上风，因为建筑公司承担了遇到房地产开发商不知道的地下花岗岩的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"impossibility","ipa":"/ˌɪmpˌɔsɪbˈɪlɪti/","syllables":"im·pos·si·bil·ity","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 不可能之事, 不可能；[法] 不可能性, 不可能的事","contextualMeaning":"履行不能：不可归责的意外事件使履行客观不可能","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:impossibilities","sourceSentence":"The construction company is excused under the modern doctrine of supervening impossibility, which includes severe impracticability.","synonyms":["impossible action","impossibleness"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":66,"sourceQuestionPage":193,"sourceQuestionPages":[193],"sourceExplanationPages":[194,195],"sourceAnswerPages":[194,195],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-067","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"During negotiations to purchase a used car, a buyer asked a dealer whether the car had ever been in an accident. The dealer replied: \"It is a fine car and has been thoroughly inspected and comes with a certificate of assured quality. Feel free to have the car inspected by your own mechanic.\" In actuality, the car had been in a major accident, and the dealer had repaired and repainted the car, successfully concealing evidence of the accident. The buyer declined to have the car inspected by his own mechanic, explaining that he would rely on the dealer's certificate of assured quality. At no time did the dealer disclose that the car had previously been in an accident. The parties then signed a contract of sale. After the car was delivered and paid for, the buyer learned that the car had been in a major accident. If the buyer sues the dealer to rescind the transaction, is the buyer likely to succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer had the opportunity to have the car inspected by his own mechanic and declined to do so.","B":"No, because the dealer did not affirmatively assert that the car had not been in an accident.","C":"Yes, because the contract was unconscionable.","D":"Yes, because the dealer's statement was intentionally misleading and the dealer concealed evidence of the accident."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact often made in an affirmative statement. It can also be asserted through conduct—ie, an act of concealment intended to prevent discovery of facts or nondisclosure (Choice B). When either form of misrepresentation occurs, a contract is voidable and can be rescinded by the adversely affected party if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied* on the misrepresentation. Here, the dealer repaired and repainted the car to conceal accident damage. This fraudulent misrepresentation, along with the dealer's misleading statements about the car's history, induced the buyer to purchase the car. And since the dealer's misrepresentations were not obviously false (eg, had the accident damage been visible) or known by the buyer to be false, he justifiably relied on them. Therefore, his action to rescind the contract is likely to succeed. *This element is met unless the misrepresentation was patently and obviously false or the adversely affected party knew that it was false. (Choice A) The buyer's failure to have the car inspected by his own mechanic does not make his reliance on the dealer's misrepresentations unjustified or prevent him from rescinding the contract. (Choice C) A contract is unconscionable when (1) the contract terms are one-sided or create unfair surprise or (2) the bargaining process provides no meaningful choice to a party (neither of which is seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 168–72 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when reliance on assertions of fact or opinion is justified).","educationalObjective":"A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 168–72 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when reliance on assertions of fact or opinion is justified).","rule":"A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues the dealer to rescind the transaction, is the buyer likely to succeed?","rule":"A contract is voidable for misrepresentation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.","analysis":"Misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact often made in an affirmative statement. It can also be asserted through conduct—ie, an act of concealment intended to prevent discovery of facts or nondisclosure (Choice B). When either form of misrepresentation occurs, a contract is voidable and can be rescinded by the adversely affected party if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied* on the misrepresentation. Here, the dealer repaired and repainted the car to conceal accident damage. This fraudulent misrepresentation, along with the dealer's misleading statements about the car's history, induced the buyer to purchase the car. And since the dealer's misrepresentations were not obviously false (eg, had the accident damage been visible) or known by the buyer to be false, he justifiably relied on them. Therefore, his action to rescind the contract is likely to succeed. *This element is met unless the misrepresentation was patently and obviously false or the adversely affected party knew that it was false. (Choice A) The buyer's failure to have the car inspected by his own mechanic does not make his reliance on the dealer's misrepresentations unjustified or prevent him from rescinding the contract. (Choice C) A contract is unconscionable when (1) the contract terms are one-sided or create unfair surprise or (2) the bargaining process provides no meaningful choice to a party (neither of which is seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the dealer's statement was intentionally misleading and the dealer concealed evidence of the accident."},"questionZh":"在购买二手车的谈判中，一位买家询问经销商这辆车是否曾经发生过事故。经销商回答：“这是一辆好车，已经过彻底检查，并附有质量保证证书。请放心让您自己的机械师检查这辆车。”事实上，这辆车曾发生过重大事故，经销商对车辆进行了修理和重新喷漆，成功​​掩盖了事故证据。买家拒绝让自己的机械师检查汽车，并解释说他将依赖经销商的质量保证证书。经销商从未透露该车此前曾发生过事故。随后双方签订了销售合同。车子交付并付款后，买家得知车子发生了重大事故。如果买家起诉经销商撤销交易，买家有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为买家本来有机会让自己的机械师检查汽车，但他拒绝这样做。","B":"不，因为经销商并没有肯定地断言该车没有发生事故。","C":"是的，因为合同不合理。","D":"是的，因为经销商的声明是故意误导的，而且经销商隐瞒了事故的证据。"},"explanationZh":"失实陈述是对事实的不真实断言，通常在肯定性陈述中作出。它也可以通过行为来断言——即旨在防止发现事实或不披露事实的隐瞒行为（选择B）。当发生任何一种形式的失实陈述时，如果出现以下情况，合同将无效，并且受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖*虚假陈述。在这里，经销商对汽车进行了修理和重新喷漆，以掩盖事故损坏。这种欺诈性的失实陈述，加上经销商对汽车历史的误导性陈述，诱使买家购买了这辆车。而且，由于经销商的虚假陈述并非明显虚假（例如，如果事故损坏是可见的），也不是买方知道是虚假的，那么他就有理由信赖这些虚假陈述。因此，他解除合同的行动很有可能成功。 *除非虚假陈述明显且明显虚假，或者受到不利影响的一方知道该陈述是虚假的，否则满足此要素。 （选择A）买方未能让自己的机械师检查汽车并不意味着他对经销商的虚假陈述的依赖是不合理的，也不妨碍他解除合同。 （选项 C）当（1）合同条款是单方面的或造成不公平的意外或（2）谈判过程没有为一方提供有意义的选择（此处均未看到）时，合同是不合理的。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同因虚假陈述而无效：(1) 虚假陈述具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 诱导同意合同；(3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该陈述。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 168–72 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当事实或意见的主张有合理依据时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同因虚假陈述而无效，如果 (1) 虚假陈述是欺诈性的或重大的，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖它。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买家起诉经销商撤销交易，买家有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"合同因虚假陈述而无效，如果 (1) 虚假陈述是欺诈性的或重大的，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖它。","analysis":"失实陈述是对事实的不真实断言，通常在肯定性陈述中作出。它也可以通过行为来断言——即旨在防止发现事实或不披露事实的隐瞒行为（选择B）。当发生任何一种形式的失实陈述时，如果出现以下情况，合同将无效，并且受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖*虚假陈述。在这里，经销商对汽车进行了修理和重新喷漆，以掩盖事故损坏。这种欺诈性的失实陈述，加上经销商对汽车历史的误导性陈述，诱使买家购买了这辆车。而且，由于经销商的虚假陈述并非明显虚假（例如，如果事故损坏是可见的），也不是买方知道是虚假的，那么他就有理由信赖这些虚假陈述。因此，他解除合同的行动很有可能成功。 *除非虚假陈述明显且明显虚假，或者受到不利影响的一方知道该陈述是虚假的，否则满足此要素。 （选择A）买方未能让自己的机械师检查汽车并不意味着他对经销商的虚假陈述的依赖是不合理的，也不妨碍他解除合同。 （选项 C）当（1）合同条款是单方面的或造成不公平的意外或（2）谈判过程没有为一方提供有意义的选择（此处均未看到）时，合同是不合理的。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同因虚假陈述而无效：(1) 虚假陈述具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 诱导同意合同；(3) 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该陈述。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 168–72 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当事实或意见的主张有合理依据时）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为经销商的声明是故意误导的，而且经销商隐瞒了事故的证据。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-856e76a381eeb1bb"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":67,"sourceQuestionPage":196,"sourceQuestionPages":[196],"sourceExplanationPages":[197,198],"sourceAnswerPages":[197,198],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-068","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"In order to raise revenue, a city required home-repair contractors who performed work within the city limits to pay a licensing fee to a city agency. A contractor who was unaware of the fee requirement agreed to perform home repairs for a city resident. After the contractor completed the work, the resident discovered that the contractor had not paid the licensing fee, and she refused to pay for the repairs, which were otherwise satisfactory. If the contractor sues the resident for breach of contract, how is the court likely to rule?","choices":{"A":"Although the contract violates the law and is void, the court will require the homeowner to pay the contractor the reasonable value of the work accepted.","B":"Although the contract violates the law, the court will find that public policy does not bar enforcement of the contract, because the purpose of the fee is merely to raise revenue.","C":"Because the contract violates the law and is void, the court will not enforce it.","D":"Because the purpose of the fee is merely to raise revenue, the court will find that the contract does not violate the law but will allow the contractor to recover his costs only."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Weighing public policy v. Interest in enforcing illegal contract\n\nPublic policy Interest in enforcing\n\n• Strength of policy as shown by\n• Parties' justified expectations legislature or courts\n• Forfeiture that would result from\n• Likelihood nonenforcement will nonenforcement further policy\n• Special public interest in\n• Seriousness and/or deliberateness of enforcement misconduct\n• Connection between misconduct & contract Illegal contracts are generally void as against public policy. But if a contract is illegal solely because a party does not have a required license, then the enforceability of the contract depends on whether the purpose behind the licensing requirement is:\n• economic (eg, raise revenue) – in which case the contract is enforceable or\n\n• regulatory (eg, protect public health or safety) – in which case the contract is unenforceable if the public policy behind the requirement clearly outweighs the interest in enforcing the contract.\n\nHere, the contract between the contractor and resident was illegal because the contractor had not paid the required licensing fee to the city agency. But since the purpose of this fee is merely to raise revenue for the city, the court will likely find that public policy does not bar enforcement of the contract (Choice C). (Choice A) Had the contract been void on public-policy grounds, then the court may have required the homeowner to pay restitution damages (ie, the reasonable value of the work accepted) to avoid unjust enrichment. (Choice D) The contract does violate the law. But public policy does not bar its enforcement, and the contractor is entitled to recover the full contract price—not his costs only.\n\nEducational objective: When a party does not have a required license, the enforceability of the contract depends on whether the purpose behind the requirement is (1) economic, in which case the contract is enforceable, or (2) regulatory, in which case it is unenforceable if the public policy behind the requirement clearly outweighs the interest in enforcement.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 181 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when failure to comply with licensing requirement makes contract unenforceable).","educationalObjective":"When a party does not have a required license, the enforceability of the contract depends on whether the purpose behind the requirement is (1) economic, in which case the contract is enforceable, or (2) regulatory, in which case it is unenforceable if the public policy behind the requirement clearly outweighs the interest in enforcement.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 181 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when failure to comply with licensing requirement makes contract unenforceable).","rule":"When a party does not have a required license, the enforceability of the contract depends on whether the purpose behind the requirement is (1) economic, in which case the contract is enforceable, or (2) regulatory, in which case it is unenforceable if the public policy behind the requirement clearly outweighs the interest in enforcement.","irac":{"issue":"If the contractor sues the resident for breach of contract, how is the court likely to rule?","rule":"When a party does not have a required license, the enforceability of the contract depends on whether the purpose behind the requirement is (1) economic, in which case the contract is enforceable, or (2) regulatory, in which case it is unenforceable if the public policy behind the requirement clearly outweighs the interest in enforcement.","analysis":"Weighing public policy v. Interest in enforcing illegal contract\n\nPublic policy Interest in enforcing\n\n• Strength of policy as shown by\n• Parties' justified expectations legislature or courts\n• Forfeiture that would result from\n• Likelihood nonenforcement will nonenforcement further policy\n• Special public interest in\n• Seriousness and/or deliberateness of enforcement misconduct\n• Connection between misconduct & contract Illegal contracts are generally void as against public policy. But if a contract is illegal solely because a party does not have a required license, then the enforceability of the contract depends on whether the purpose behind the licensing requirement is:\n• economic (eg, raise revenue) – in which case the contract is enforceable or\n\n• regulatory (eg, protect public health or safety) – in which case the contract is unenforceable if the public policy behind the requirement clearly outweighs the interest in enforcing the contract.\n\nHere, the contract between the contractor and resident was illegal because the contractor had not paid the required licensing fee to the city agency. But since the purpose of this fee is merely to raise revenue for the city, the court will likely find that public policy does not bar enforcement of the contract (Choice C). (Choice A) Had the contract been void on public-policy grounds, then the court may have required the homeowner to pay restitution damages (ie, the reasonable value of the work accepted) to avoid unjust enrichment. (Choice D) The contract does violate the law. But public policy does not bar its enforcement, and the contractor is entitled to recover the full contract price—not his costs only.","conclusion":"Answer B: Although the contract violates the law, the court will find that public policy does not bar enforcement of the contract, because the purpose of the fee is merely to raise revenue."},"questionZh":"为了增加收入，城市要求在城市范围内进行工作的房屋维修承包商向城市机构支付许可费。一名不知道收费要求的承包商同意为一名城市居民进行房屋维修。承包商完成工作后，居民发现承包商没有支付许可费，她拒绝支付原本令人满意的维修费用。如果承包商起诉住户违约，法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"尽管该合同违反了法律并且无效，但法院将要求房主向承包商支付所接受工程的合理价值。","B":"尽管合同违反了法律，但法院会发现公共政策并不妨碍合同的执行，因为收费的目的仅仅是为了增加收入。","C":"由于该合同违法且无效，法院不予执行。","D":"由于收取费用的目的仅仅是为了增加收入，法院会认为该合同并不违法，而只会允许承包商收回其成本。"},"explanationZh":"权衡公共政策与执行非法合同的利益\n\n公共政策执行的兴趣\n\n• 政策力度如下：\n• 各方的合理期望 立法机关或法院\n• 因以下原因而导致的没收\n• 不执行进一步政策的可能性\n• 特殊公众利益\n• 执法不当行为的严重性和/或故意\n• 不当行为与合同之间的联系 违反公共政策的非法合同通常无效。但是，如果合同仅仅因为一方没有所需许可而成为非法合同，那么合同的可执行性取决于许可要求背后的目的是否是：\n• 经济（例如，增加收入）——在这种情况下，合同是可执行的或\n\n• 监管（例如，保护公众健康或安全）——在这种情况下，如果要求背后的公共政策明显超过了执行合同的利益，则合同无法执行。\n\n在这里，承包商和居民之间的合同是非法的，因为承包商没有向城市机构支付所需的许可费。但由于这笔费用的目的仅仅是为了增加城市收入，法院可能会发现公共政策并不妨碍合同的执行（选项 C）。 （选择A）如果合同因公共政策而无效，那么法院可能会要求房主支付赔偿金（即所接受工作的合理价值）以避免不当得利。 （选项D） 该合同确实违反了法律。但公共政策并不妨碍其执行，承包商有权收回全部合同价格——而不仅仅是他的成本。\n\n教育目标：当一方没有所需的许可时，合同的可执行性取决于该要求背后的目的是否是（1）经济，在这种情况下合同是可执行的，或（2）监管，在这种情况下，如果要求背后的公共政策明显超过了执行的利益，则合同不可执行。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 181（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当不遵守许可要求导致合同无法执行时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一方没有所需的许可时，合同的可执行性取决于该要求背后的目的是否是（1）经济，在这种情况下合同是可执行的，或（2）监管，在这种情况下，如果要求背后的公共政策明显超过了执行的利益，则合同不可执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果承包商起诉住户违约，法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"当一方没有所需的许可时，合同的可执行性取决于该要求背后的目的是否是（1）经济，在这种情况下合同是可执行的，或（2）监管，在这种情况下，如果要求背后的公共政策明显超过了执行的利益，则合同不可执行。","analysis":"权衡公共政策与执行非法合同的利益\n\n公共政策执行的兴趣\n\n• 政策力度如下：\n• 各方的合理期望 立法机关或法院\n• 因以下原因而导致的没收\n• 不执行进一步政策的可能性\n• 特殊公众利益\n• 执法不当行为的严重性和/或故意\n• 不当行为与合同之间的联系 违反公共政策的非法合同通常无效。但是，如果合同仅仅因为一方没有所需许可而成为非法合同，那么合同的可执行性取决于许可要求背后的目的是否是：\n• 经济（例如，增加收入）——在这种情况下，合同是可执行的或\n\n• 监管（例如，保护公众健康或安全）——在这种情况下，如果要求背后的公共政策明显超过了执行合同的利益，则合同无法执行。\n\n在这里，承包商和居民之间的合同是非法的，因为承包商没有向城市机构支付所需的许可费。但由于这笔费用的目的仅仅是为了增加城市收入，法院可能会发现公共政策并不妨碍合同的执行（选项 C）。 （选择A）如果合同因公共政策而无效，那么法院可能会要求房主支付赔偿金（即所接受工作的合理价值）以避免不当得利。 （选项D） 该合同确实违反了法律。但公共政策并不妨碍其执行，承包商有权收回全部合同价格——而不仅仅是他的成本。\n\n教育目标：当一方没有所需的许可时，合同的可执行性取决于该要求背后的目的是否是（1）经济，在这种情况下合同是可执行的，或（2）监管，在这种情况下，如果要求背后的公共政策明显超过了执行的利益，则合同不可执行。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 181（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当不遵守许可要求导致合同无法执行时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：尽管合同违反了法律，但法院会发现公共政策并不妨碍合同的执行，因为收费的目的仅仅是为了增加收入。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"unjust enrichment","ipa":"/ʌndʒˈəst ɛnɹˈɪtʃmənt/","syllables":"un·just en·rich·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不当得利：无正当理由保有他人提供的利益","contextualMeaning":"不当得利：无正当理由保有他人提供的利益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice A) Had the contract been void on public-policy grounds, then the court may have required the homeowner to pay restitution damages (ie, the reasonable value of the work accepted) to avoid unjust enrichment.","synonyms":["inequitable retention of benefit"]},{"term":"forfeiture","ipa":"/fˈɔɹfətʃɝ/","syllables":"for·fei·ture","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 没收物, 罚金, 丧失；[经] 没收, 没收物, 罚金","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“forfeiture”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:forfeitures","sourceSentence":"Interest in enforcing illegal contract Public policy Interest in enforcing • Strength of policy as shown by • Parties' justified expectations legislature or courts • Forfeiture that would result from • Likelihood nonenforcement will nonenforcement further policy • Special public interest in • Seriousness and/or deliberateness of enforcement misconduct • Connection between misconduct & contract Illegal contracts are g","synonyms":["forfeit","sacrifice"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":68,"sourceQuestionPage":199,"sourceQuestionPages":[199],"sourceExplanationPages":[200,201],"sourceAnswerPages":[200,201],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-069","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"In a telephone conversation, a jewelry maker offered to buy 100 ounces of gold from a precious metals company if delivery could be made within 10 days. The jewelry maker did not specify a price, but the market price for 100 ounces of gold at the time of the conversation was approximately $65,000. Without otherwise responding, the company delivered the gold six days later. In the meantime, the project for which the jewelry maker planned to use the gold was canceled. The jewelry maker therefore refused to accept delivery of the gold or to pay the $65,000 demanded by the company. Is there an enforceable contract between the jewelry maker and the company?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parties did not agree on a price term.","B":"No, because the parties did not put their agreement in writing.","C":"Yes, because the absence of a price term does not defeat the formation of a valid contract for the sale of goods where the parties otherwise intended to form a contract.","D":"Yes, because the company relied on an implied promise to pay when it delivered the gold."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more (eg, gold valued at $65,000—as seen here) and renders such contracts unenforceable unless they are in a writing that:\n• provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed\n• states the quantity of goods to be sold* and\n• is signed with an identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought. However, this statute does not apply when (1) the goods are specially manufactured, (2) the party against whom enforcement is sought admits to the contract, or (3) some amount of payment or goods was received and accepted. Here, there is no indication that the gold was specially manufactured for the jewelry maker or that the jewelry maker admitted to a contract with the company. And though the company delivered the gold to the jewelry maker, the jewelry maker refused to accept delivery. Therefore, the contract is not excepted from the statute of frauds. And since the parties did not put their agreement into writing, it is unenforceable. *The contract is only enforceable up to the written quantity. (Choices A & C) A contract for the sale of goods is enforceable even if the price term is missing or incorrectly stated. But even though the absence of a price term does not affect the enforceability of the contract between the jewelry maker and the company, the contract is still unenforceable because the parties failed to put it in writing. (Choice D) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, the company's reliance on an implied promise to pay would provide an alternative basis for recovery only if such reliance was reasonable and caused the company substantial detriment (eg, turning down substantial business to fulfill the jewelry maker's request—not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more are not subject to the statute of frauds if (1) the goods are specially manufactured, (2) the party against whom enforcement is sought admits to the contract, or (3) some amount of goods or payment was received and accepted.\n\nReferences\n• UCC § 2-201 (statute of frauds formal requirements).","educationalObjective":"Contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more are not subject to the statute of frauds if (1) the goods are specially manufactured, (2) the party against whom enforcement is sought admits to the contract, or (3) some amount of goods or payment was received and accepted.","references":"• UCC § 2-201 (statute of frauds formal requirements).","rule":"Contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more are not subject to the statute of frauds if (1) the goods are specially manufactured, (2) the party against whom enforcement is sought admits to the contract, or (3) some amount of goods or payment was received and accepted.","irac":{"issue":"Is there an enforceable contract between the jewelry maker and the company?","rule":"Contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more are not subject to the statute of frauds if (1) the goods are specially manufactured, (2) the party against whom enforcement is sought admits to the contract, or (3) some amount of goods or payment was received and accepted.","analysis":"The UCC statute of frauds applies to contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more (eg, gold valued at $65,000—as seen here) and renders such contracts unenforceable unless they are in a writing that:\n• provides a reasonable basis to believe a contract was formed\n• states the quantity of goods to be sold* and\n• is signed with an identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought. However, this statute does not apply when (1) the goods are specially manufactured, (2) the party against whom enforcement is sought admits to the contract, or (3) some amount of payment or goods was received and accepted. Here, there is no indication that the gold was specially manufactured for the jewelry maker or that the jewelry maker admitted to a contract with the company. And though the company delivered the gold to the jewelry maker, the jewelry maker refused to accept delivery. Therefore, the contract is not excepted from the statute of frauds. And since the parties did not put their agreement into writing, it is unenforceable. *The contract is only enforceable up to the written quantity. (Choices A & C) A contract for the sale of goods is enforceable even if the price term is missing or incorrectly stated. But even though the absence of a price term does not affect the enforceability of the contract between the jewelry maker and the company, the contract is still unenforceable because the parties failed to put it in writing. (Choice D) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, the company's reliance on an implied promise to pay would provide an alternative basis for recovery only if such reliance was reasonable and caused the company substantial detriment (eg, turning down substantial business to fulfill the jewelry maker's request—not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the parties did not put their agreement in writing."},"questionZh":"在一次电话交谈中，一家珠宝制造商提出从一家贵金属公司购买 100 盎司黄金，前提是能在 10 天内交货。该珠宝制造商没有具体说明价格，但谈话时 100 盎司黄金的市场价格约为 65,000 美元。六天后，该公司在没有做出其他回应的情况下交付了黄金。与此同时，珠宝制造商计划使用黄金的项目被取消。因此，珠宝制造商拒绝接受黄金交付，也拒绝支付公司要求的 65,000 美元。珠宝制造商和公司之间是否有可执行的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为双方未就价格条款达成一致。","B":"不，因为双方没有书面同意。","C":"是的，因为如果当事人本来打算订立合同，则缺乏价格条款并不妨碍有效的货物销售合同的成立。","D":"是的，因为该公司依赖于交付黄金时付款的默示承诺。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（例如，价值 65,000 美元的黄金 - 如此处所示），并且使此类合同无法执行，除非其书面形式符合以下条件：\n• 提供合理的基础以相信合同已成立\n• 说明要出售的商品数量* 以及\n• 由被寻求执行的一方签署带有识别标记的签名。然而，本法规不适用于以下情况：(1) 货物是专门制造的，(2) 被寻求执行的一方承认合同，或 (3) 收到并接受了一定数额的付款或货物。在这里，没有迹象表明黄金是专门为珠宝制造商制造的，也没有迹象表明珠宝制造商承认与该公司签订了合同。尽管该公司将黄金交付给珠宝制造商，但珠宝制造商拒绝接受交付。因此，该合同不属于欺诈法的例外。由于双方未将协议书面化，因此无法执行。 *合同仅在书面数量范围内有效。 （选项 A 和 C）即使价格条款缺失或表述不正确，货物销售合同仍具有可执行性。但即使价格条款的缺失并不影响珠宝制造商与公司之间合同的可执行性，但由于双方未能以书面形式签订合同，该合同仍然无法执行。 （选项 D）根据禁止反言原则，公司对默示付款承诺的依赖只有在这种依赖合理且给公司造成重大损害的情况下才能提供替代的赔偿依据（例如，拒绝大量业务来满足珠宝制造商的要求——此处未见）。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 货物是专门制造的，(2) 寻求执行的一方承认该合同，或 (3) 收到并接受了一定数量的货物或付款，则价值 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同不受欺诈法的约束。\n\n参考文献\n• UCC § 2-201（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）正式要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"价值 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同不受欺诈法的约束，如果 (1) 货物是专门制造的，(2) 被寻求执行的一方承认该合同，或 (3) 已收到并接受一定数额的货物或付款。","iracZh":{"issue":"珠宝制造商和公司之间是否有可执行的合同？","rule":"价值 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同不受欺诈法的约束，如果 (1) 货物是专门制造的，(2) 被寻求执行的一方承认该合同，或 (3) 已收到并接受一定数额的货物或付款。","analysis":"UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）适用于价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同（例如，价值 65,000 美元的黄金 - 如此处所示），并且使此类合同无法执行，除非其书面形式符合以下条件：\n• 提供合理的基础以相信合同已成立\n• 说明要出售的商品数量* 以及\n• 由被寻求执行的一方签署带有识别标记的签名。然而，本法规不适用于以下情况：(1) 货物是专门制造的，(2) 被寻求执行的一方承认合同，或 (3) 收到并接受了一定数额的付款或货物。在这里，没有迹象表明黄金是专门为珠宝制造商制造的，也没有迹象表明珠宝制造商承认与该公司签订了合同。尽管该公司将黄金交付给珠宝制造商，但珠宝制造商拒绝接受交付。因此，该合同不属于欺诈法的例外。由于双方未将协议书面化，因此无法执行。 *合同仅在书面数量范围内有效。 （选项 A 和 C）即使价格条款缺失或表述不正确，货物销售合同仍具有可执行性。但即使价格条款的缺失并不影响珠宝制造商与公司之间合同的可执行性，但由于双方未能以书面形式签订合同，该合同仍然无法执行。 （选项 D）根据禁止反言原则，公司对默示付款承诺的依赖只有在这种依赖合理且给公司造成重大损害的情况下才能提供替代的赔偿依据（例如，拒绝大量业务来满足珠宝制造商的要求——此处未见）。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 货物是专门制造的，(2) 寻求执行的一方承认该合同，或 (3) 收到并接受了一定数量的货物或付款，则价值 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同不受欺诈法的约束。\n\n参考文献\n• UCC § 2-201（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）正式要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为双方没有书面同意。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5c856a35c76ade31"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":69,"sourceQuestionPage":202,"sourceQuestionPages":[202],"sourceExplanationPages":[203,204],"sourceAnswerPages":[203,204],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-070","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"The managing director of an investment firm orally offered to hire a foreign-exchange trader for a nine-month period at a salary identified only as \"more than you're making now.\" The trader accepted the director's offer, began work immediately, and thereafter received a check for $8,000 each month. The monthly check exceeded his prior salary. Two months after the trader started work at the firm, the director sent him this email: \"I hereby offer to extend your position for an additional six months beyond the original nine- month term at the amount stated in your monthly paycheck. Please confirm. /s/ Director.\" The trader's reply email read: \"I agree to work for six additional months.\" After the trader had completed nine months with the firm, the director notified the trader that his services were no longer needed. Is the trader likely to prevail in a breach of contract suit against the investment firm?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parties did not specify salary with reasonable certainty.","B":"No, because the trader did not rely on the director's promise to his detriment.","C":"Yes, because the parties' emails satisfy the statute-of-frauds requirement.","D":"Yes, because the trader's employment was extended for less than one year."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nThe following types of contracts (as entered or as modified) fall within the statute of frauds and must be in writing to be enforceable (MY LEGS):\n• Marriage – a contract made in consideration of marriage\n• Year – a contract that cannot be completed within one year\n• Land – a contract for the sale or transfer of an interest in real property\n• Executor – a contract to pay an estate's debt out of the executor's personal funds\n• Goods – a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more\n• Suretyship – a contract to answer for the debt or duty of another The writing must be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought and state with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal—eg, length of employment and salary in an employment contract. The writing need not be formal, and the essential terms may be in multiple writings so long as one of the writings references the other(s).* Here, the parties' nine-month oral employment agreement was enforceable without a writing. However, the parties subsequently agreed to extend the trader's employment for six additional months. Because the agreement as modified could not be completed within one year, it had to be in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (Choice D). The parties' emails satisfy this requirement. They contain the director's electronic signature and state the essential terms of the deal with reasonable certainty—eg, they specify employment length and salary by reference to the trader's monthly paychecks (Choice A). Therefore, the trader will likely prevail.\n\n*Even if the writing is lost or destroyed, it still satisfies the statute of frauds, and its prior existence can be proved by oral evidence. (Choice B) The fact that the trader did not detrimentally rely on the director's promise (eg, by declining a job offer) would preclude him from enforcing the promise on promissory estoppel grounds. But the trader need not rely on promissory estoppel since the parties have a valid contract.\n\nEducational objective: Contracts within the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) are unenforceable without a signed writing that states with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal. The essential terms may be in multiple writings so long as one of the writings references the other(s).\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 131–32 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (writing requirement for statute of frauds).","educationalObjective":"Contracts within the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) are unenforceable without a signed writing that states with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal. The essential terms may be in multiple writings so long as one of the writings references the other(s).","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 131–32 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (writing requirement for statute of frauds).","rule":"Contracts within the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) are unenforceable without a signed writing that states with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal. The essential terms may be in multiple writings so long as one of the writings references the other(s).","irac":{"issue":"Is the trader likely to prevail in a breach of contract suit against the investment firm?","rule":"Contracts within the statute of frauds (MY LEGS) are unenforceable without a signed writing that states with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal. The essential terms may be in multiple writings so long as one of the writings references the other(s).","analysis":"Statute of frauds requirements\n\nWriting Physical or electronic document(s) containing reasonable basis to believe contract was formed (eg, receipt, check, emails) AND\n\n• at common law – identifies parties, subject matter, consideration, time & nature of performance\n• under UCC – lists quantity of goods (contract enforceable only up to written quantity) Signature General rule – handwritten signature from, or other mark identifying, party to be charged (eg, letterhead, electronic signature, initials)\n\n• Merchant exception – between merchants, written confirmation that satisfies statute of frauds as to sender will bind both parties if no written objection within 10 days UCC = Uniform Commercial Code,\n\nThe following types of contracts (as entered or as modified) fall within the statute of frauds and must be in writing to be enforceable (MY LEGS):\n• Marriage – a contract made in consideration of marriage\n• Year – a contract that cannot be completed within one year\n• Land – a contract for the sale or transfer of an interest in real property\n• Executor – a contract to pay an estate's debt out of the executor's personal funds\n• Goods – a contract for the sale of goods for $500 or more\n• Suretyship – a contract to answer for the debt or duty of another The writing must be signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought and state with reasonable certainty the essential terms of the deal—eg, length of employment and salary in an employment contract. The writing need not be formal, and the essential terms may be in multiple writings so long as one of the writings references the other(s).* Here, the parties' nine-month oral employment agreement was enforceable without a writing. However, the parties subsequently agreed to extend the trader's employment for six additional months. Because the agreement as modified could not be completed within one year, it had to be in a writing that satisfies the statute of frauds (Choice D). The parties' emails satisfy this requirement. They contain the director's electronic signature and state the essential terms of the deal with reasonable certainty—eg, they specify employment length and salary by reference to the trader's monthly paychecks (Choice A). Therefore, the trader will likely prevail.\n\n*Even if the writing is lost or destroyed, it still satisfies the statute of frauds, and its prior existence can be proved by oral evidence. (Choice B) The fact that the trader did not detrimentally rely on the director's promise (eg, by declining a job offer) would preclude him from enforcing the promise on promissory estoppel grounds. But the trader need not rely on promissory estoppel since the parties have a valid contract.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the parties' emails satisfy the statute-of-frauds requirement."},"questionZh":"一家投资公司的董事总经理口头提出雇用一名外汇交易员，为期九个月，薪水仅“比你现在的工资高”。交易员接受了主管的提议，立即开始工作，此后每月收到一张 8,000 美元的支票。每月的支票超过了他之前的工资。该交易员在公司开始工作两个月后，主管向他发送了这封电子邮件：“我特此提议将您的职位在原来的九个月期限基础上再延长六个月，金额如您每月工资中所述。请确认。/s/董事。”该交易员的回复电子邮件中写道：“我同意再工作六个月。”交易员在公司工作了九个月后，主管通知交易员不再需要他的服务。交易者在针对投资公司的违约诉讼中是否有可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为双方没有合理确定薪资。","B":"不，因为交易员并没有依赖董事的承诺而对他造成损害。","C":"是的，因为双方的电子邮件满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。","D":"是的，因为该交易员的雇佣期限延长了不到一年。"},"explanationZh":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\n以下类型的合同（已签订或已修改）属于防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的范围，并且必须采用书面形式才能强制执行（我的法律）：\n• 婚姻——以结婚为约因订立的合同\n• 年份——一年内无法完成的合同\n• 土地——出售或转让不动产权益的合同\n• 遗嘱执行人——用遗嘱执行人的个人资金偿还遗产债务的合同\n• 货物 – 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同\n• 保证——为他人的债务或责任承担责任的合同 该书面文件必须由寻求执行的一方签署，并以合理的确定性说明交易的基本条款，例如雇佣合同中的雇佣期限和工资。书面形式不必是正式的，并且基本条款可以采用多份书面形式，只要其中一份书面文件引用了其他书面文件即可。*在此，双方的九个月口头雇佣协议无需书面形式即可强制执行。然而，双方随后同意将该交易员的雇佣期限再延长六个月。由于修改后的协议无法在一年内完成，因此必须采用符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）定的书面形式（选项 D）。当事人的电子邮件满足这一要求。它们包含董事的电子签名，并以合理的确定性陈述交易的基本条款，例如，它们参考交易者的月薪支票指定雇佣期限和工资（选择 A）。因此，交易者可能会占上风。\n\n*即使文字遗失或毁坏，仍符合欺诈罪的规定，并且可以通过口头证据证明其先前存在。 （选择B）交易者没有不利地依赖董事的承诺（例如，拒绝工作机会）这一事实将阻止他以禁止反言为由执行承诺。但交易者无需依赖允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel），因为双方都有有效的合同。\n\n教育目标：如果没有以合理的确定性说明交易基本条款的签署书面文件，则欺诈法 (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同将无法执行。基本术语可以出现在多篇文章中，只要其中一篇文章引用了其他文章即可。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 131–32（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果没有以合理的确定性说明交易基本条款的签署书面文件，则欺诈法 (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同将无法执行。基本术语可以出现在多篇文章中，只要其中一篇文章引用了其他文章即可。","iracZh":{"issue":"交易者在针对投资公司的违约诉讼中是否有可能胜诉？","rule":"如果没有以合理的确定性说明交易基本条款的签署书面文件，则欺诈法 (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同将无法执行。基本术语可以出现在多篇文章中，只要其中一篇文章引用了其他文章即可。","analysis":"防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）要求\n\n书写包含合理依据以相信合同已成立的物理或电子文件（例如收据、支票、电子邮件）并且\n\n• 在普通法中——确定当事人、标的物、考虑因素、履行时间和性质\n• UCC 下 – 列出货物数量（合同仅在书面数量范围内可执行） 签名 一般规则 – 收费方的手写签名或其他识别标记（例如信笺抬头、电子签名、姓名缩写）\n\n• 商户例外 – 商户之间，如果在 10 天内没有提出书面异议，则发送方满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面确认将对双方具有约束力 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC），\n\n以下类型的合同（已签订或已修改）属于防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的范围，并且必须采用书面形式才能强制执行（我的法律）：\n• 婚姻——以结婚为约因订立的合同\n• 年份——一年内无法完成的合同\n• 土地——出售或转让不动产权益的合同\n• 遗嘱执行人——用遗嘱执行人的个人资金偿还遗产债务的合同\n• 货物 – 500 美元或以上的货物销售合同\n• 保证——为他人的债务或责任承担责任的合同 该书面文件必须由寻求执行的一方签署，并以合理的确定性说明交易的基本条款，例如雇佣合同中的雇佣期限和工资。书面形式不必是正式的，并且基本条款可以采用多份书面形式，只要其中一份书面文件引用了其他书面文件即可。*在此，双方的九个月口头雇佣协议无需书面形式即可强制执行。然而，双方随后同意将该交易员的雇佣期限再延长六个月。由于修改后的协议无法在一年内完成，因此必须采用符合防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）定的书面形式（选项 D）。当事人的电子邮件满足这一要求。它们包含董事的电子签名，并以合理的确定性陈述交易的基本条款，例如，它们参考交易者的月薪支票指定雇佣期限和工资（选择 A）。因此，交易者可能会占上风。\n\n*即使文字遗失或毁坏，仍符合欺诈罪的规定，并且可以通过口头证据证明其先前存在。 （选择B）交易者没有不利地依赖董事的承诺（例如，拒绝工作机会）这一事实将阻止他以禁止反言为由执行承诺。但交易者无需依赖允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel），因为双方都有有效的合同。\n\n教育目标：如果没有以合理的确定性说明交易基本条款的签署书面文件，则欺诈法 (MY LEGS) 范围内的合同将无法执行。基本术语可以出现在多篇文章中，只要其中一篇文章引用了其他文章即可。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 131–32（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面要求）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为双方的电子邮件满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的要求。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":70,"sourceQuestionPage":205,"sourceQuestionPages":[205],"sourceExplanationPages":[206,207],"sourceAnswerPages":[206,207],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-071","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"The mother of a son and a daughter was dying. The daughter visited her mother in a hospice facility and said, \"You know that I have always been the good child, and my brother has always been the bad child. Even so, you have left your property in the will to us 50- 50. But it would be really nice if you would sell me the family home for $100,000.\" \"I don't know,\" said the mother. \"It is worth a lot more than that—at least $250,000.\" \"That is true,\" said the daughter. \"But I have always been good and visited you, and my brother has never visited you, so that ought to be worth something. And besides, if you won't sell me the house for that price, maybe I won't visit you anymore, either.\" \"Oh, I wouldn't want that,\" said the mother, and she signed a contract selling the house to her daughter for $100,000. Shortly thereafter, the mother died. When her son found out that the house had been sold and was not part of his mother's estate, he sued to have the contract avoided on behalf of the mother. On what ground would the contract most likely be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Duress.","B":"Inadequate consideration.","C":"Mistake.","D":"Undue influence."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A contract induced by undue influence is voidable by the assenting party. Undue influence arises when a party assents to a contract due to unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominates or holds a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party. Persuasion is unfair or excessive if it seriously impairs the assenting party's ability to exercise free and competent judgment. The following factors are often considered in making this determination:\n• unfairness of the resulting bargain (eg, sale of home worth $250,000 for $100,000)\n• unavailability of independent advice (eg, attorney unavailable to discuss sale)\n• susceptibility of the assenting party (eg, mental illness, under hospice care) Here, the daughter held a special relationship of trust with the mother because the daughter was the only child who visited the mother. The daughter's claimed status as the \"good child\" and her threat to stop visiting likely impaired the mother's judgment and persuaded her to sell her home to the daughter. This is further evidenced by the unfair resulting bargain and the mother's susceptibility. As a result, this contract would most likely be avoided on the ground of undue influence.* *Had undue influence come from a third party, the assenting party could avoid the contract unless the other contracting party (1) did not know of the undue influence and (2) had given value or relied on the contract in good faith. (Choice A) Duress arises when a party's assent is induced by physical coercion or an improper threat that leaves no reasonable alternative but to assent or face irreparable harm (not seen here). Therefore, the contract cannot be avoided on this ground. (Choice B) Adequate consideration exists when, as here, there is a bargained-for exchange of promises or performances. This is true even if that exchange is unequal—eg, $100,000 in exchange for a $250,000 home. (Choice C) A mistake is a belief not in accord with the facts (eg, had the mother not owned the home) that makes a contract voidable if it concerns a basic assumption on which the contract was made (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A contract is voidable for undue influence when (1) assent stems from unfair or excessive persuasion (2) by someone who dominates or holds a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 177 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (undue influence).","educationalObjective":"A contract is voidable for undue influence when (1) assent stems from unfair or excessive persuasion (2) by someone who dominates or holds a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 177 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (undue influence).","rule":"A contract is voidable for undue influence when (1) assent stems from unfair or excessive persuasion (2) by someone who dominates or holds a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","irac":{"issue":"On what ground would the contract most likely be avoided?","rule":"A contract is voidable for undue influence when (1) assent stems from unfair or excessive persuasion (2) by someone who dominates or holds a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party.","analysis":"A contract induced by undue influence is voidable by the assenting party. Undue influence arises when a party assents to a contract due to unfair or excessive persuasion by someone who dominates or holds a special relationship of trust and confidence with the assenting party. Persuasion is unfair or excessive if it seriously impairs the assenting party's ability to exercise free and competent judgment. The following factors are often considered in making this determination:\n• unfairness of the resulting bargain (eg, sale of home worth $250,000 for $100,000)\n• unavailability of independent advice (eg, attorney unavailable to discuss sale)\n• susceptibility of the assenting party (eg, mental illness, under hospice care) Here, the daughter held a special relationship of trust with the mother because the daughter was the only child who visited the mother. The daughter's claimed status as the \"good child\" and her threat to stop visiting likely impaired the mother's judgment and persuaded her to sell her home to the daughter. This is further evidenced by the unfair resulting bargain and the mother's susceptibility. As a result, this contract would most likely be avoided on the ground of undue influence.* *Had undue influence come from a third party, the assenting party could avoid the contract unless the other contracting party (1) did not know of the undue influence and (2) had given value or relied on the contract in good faith. (Choice A) Duress arises when a party's assent is induced by physical coercion or an improper threat that leaves no reasonable alternative but to assent or face irreparable harm (not seen here). Therefore, the contract cannot be avoided on this ground. (Choice B) Adequate consideration exists when, as here, there is a bargained-for exchange of promises or performances. This is true even if that exchange is unequal—eg, $100,000 in exchange for a $250,000 home. (Choice C) A mistake is a belief not in accord with the facts (eg, had the mother not owned the home) that makes a contract voidable if it concerns a basic assumption on which the contract was made (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Undue influence."},"questionZh":"一个儿子和一个女儿的母亲快要死了。女儿到临终关怀机构看望她的母亲，她说：“你知道我一直是好孩子，而我哥哥一直是坏孩子。即便如此，你还是在遗嘱中把你的财产留给了我们50-50。但如果你能以10万美元的价格把家里的房子卖给我，那就太好了。” “我不知道，”母亲说。 “它的价值远不止于此——至少 25 万美元。” “确实如此，”女儿说。 “不过我一直很好，经常来看你，而我哥哥从来没有来看过你，所以这应该是值得的。而且，如果你不以这个价格把房子卖给我，也许我也不会再去看你了。” “哦，我不想这样，”这位母亲说，然后她签署了一份合同，以 10 万美元的价格将房子卖给女儿。此后不久，母亲去世了。当她的儿子发现房子已被出售且不属于他母亲的遗产时，他代表母亲提起诉讼，要求撤销合同。基于什么理由该合同最有可能被撤销？","choicesZh":{"A":"胁迫。","B":"约因不充分。","C":"错误。","D":"不当影响。"},"explanationZh":"因不当影响而产生的合同可由同意方撤销。当一方因受到支配或与同意方有特殊信任和信任关系的人的不公平或过度说服而同意合同时，就会产生不当影响。如果说服严重损害同意方行使自由和合格判断的能力，则说服是不公平或过度的。做出此决定时通常会考虑以下因素：\n• 由此产生的讨价还价的不公平性（例如，以 100,000 美元的价格出售价值 250,000 美元的房屋）\n• 无法获得独立建议（例如律师无法讨论出售事宜）\n• 同意方的易感性（例如，精神疾病、接受临终关怀） 在这里，女儿与母亲保持着特殊的信任关系，因为女儿是唯一探望母亲的孩子。女儿声称自己是“好孩子”，并威胁不再来看望，这可能会损害母亲的判断力，并说服她把房子卖给女儿。由此产生的不公平交易和母亲的易感性进一步证明了这一点。因此，本合同很可能因不当影响而被宣告无效。* *如果不当影响来自第三方，同意方可以宣告合同无效，除非另一缔约方 (1) 不知道不当影响，并且 (2) 已给予价值或善意地依赖合同。 （选项 A）当一方通过人身胁迫或不当威胁而同意，除了同意或面临无法挽回的伤害（此处未看到）之外别无合理选择时，就会产生胁迫。因此，合同不能以此为由宣告无效。 （选择 B）当像这里一样，存在讨价还价的承诺或履行的交换时，就存在充分的考虑。即使交换是不平等的（例如，100,000 美元换取 250,000 美元的房屋）也是如此。 （选项C）错误是一种与事实不符的信念（例如，如果母亲不拥有房屋），如果涉及订立合同的基本假设（此处未看到），则该信念会使合同无效。\n\n教育目标：在下列情况下，合同因不当影响而无效：(1) 同意源于不公平或过度说服 (2) 同意方占主导地位或与同意方保持特殊信任和信心关系的人。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 177（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不当影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，合同因不当影响而无效：(1) 同意源于不公平或过度说服 (2) 由同意方占主导地位或与同意方保持特殊信任和信心关系的人同意。","iracZh":{"issue":"基于什么理由该合同最有可能被撤销？","rule":"在以下情况下，合同因不当影响而无效：(1) 同意源于不公平或过度说服 (2) 由同意方占主导地位或与同意方保持特殊信任和信心关系的人同意。","analysis":"因不当影响而产生的合同可由同意方撤销。当一方因受到支配或与同意方有特殊信任和信任关系的人的不公平或过度说服而同意合同时，就会产生不当影响。如果说服严重损害同意方行使自由和合格判断的能力，则说服是不公平或过度的。做出此决定时通常会考虑以下因素：\n• 由此产生的讨价还价的不公平性（例如，以 100,000 美元的价格出售价值 250,000 美元的房屋）\n• 无法获得独立建议（例如律师无法讨论出售事宜）\n• 同意方的易感性（例如，精神疾病、接受临终关怀） 在这里，女儿与母亲保持着特殊的信任关系，因为女儿是唯一探望母亲的孩子。女儿声称自己是“好孩子”，并威胁不再来看望，这可能会损害母亲的判断力，并说服她把房子卖给女儿。由此产生的不公平交易和母亲的易感性进一步证明了这一点。因此，本合同很可能因不当影响而被宣告无效。* *如果不当影响来自第三方，同意方可以宣告合同无效，除非另一缔约方 (1) 不知道不当影响，并且 (2) 已给予价值或善意地依赖合同。 （选项 A）当一方通过人身胁迫或不当威胁而同意，除了同意或面临无法挽回的伤害（此处未看到）之外别无合理选择时，就会产生胁迫。因此，合同不能以此为由宣告无效。 （选择 B）当像这里一样，存在讨价还价的承诺或履行的交换时，就存在充分的考虑。即使交换是不平等的（例如，100,000 美元换取 250,000 美元的房屋）也是如此。 （选项C）错误是一种与事实不符的信念（例如，如果母亲不拥有房屋），如果涉及订立合同的基本假设（此处未看到），则该信念会使合同无效。\n\n教育目标：在下列情况下，合同因不当影响而无效：(1) 同意源于不公平或过度说服 (2) 同意方占主导地位或与同意方保持特殊信任和信心关系的人。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 177（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不当影响）。","conclusion":"答案 D：不当影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-7c141650886b654a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":71,"sourceQuestionPage":208,"sourceQuestionPages":[208],"sourceExplanationPages":[209,210],"sourceAnswerPages":[209,210],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-072","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"An expert in installing equipment on top of tall buildings contracted in a signed writing to lift and emplace certain air-conditioning equipment on top of a building. An exculpatory clause in the contract provided that the expert would not be liable for any physical damage to the building occurring during installation of the air-conditioning equipment. There was also a clause providing for per diem damages if the expert did not complete performance by a specified date and a clause providing that \"time is of the essence.\" Another clause provided that any subsequent agreement for extra work under the contract must be in writing and signed by both parties. With ample time remaining under the contract for commencement and completion of his performance, the expert notified the owner of the building that he was selling his business to a contractor, who was equally an expert in installing equipment on top of tall buildings, and that the contractor had agreed to \"take over the contract.\" The owner orally agreed with the expert to accept the contractor's services. The contractor performed on time but negligently installed the wrong air-conditioning equipment. Will the owner succeed in an action against the expert for damages for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because by accepting the substitution of the contractor for the expert, the owner effected a novation, and the expert was thereby discharged of his duties under the contract.","B":"No, because the liquidated-damage clause in the contract provided only for damages caused by delay in performance.","C":"Yes, because the owner did not agree to release the expert from liability under the contract.","D":"Yes, because the owner received no consideration for the substitution of the contractor for the expert."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Nondelegable contractual duties\n\nDelegation not permitted when:\n\n• other contracting party has substantial interest in having delegating party perform (eg, in personal-services contract involving taste or special skill) or\n• delegation is prohibited by contract Obligations under a contract can generally be delegated unless:\n• the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform (eg, in a personal-services contract involving taste or a special skill) or\n\n• the delegation is prohibited by the contract (eg, states that \"this contract may not be assigned\").\n\nHowever, a party may consent to a delegation that would otherwise be prohibited. After a successful delegation, the delegator remains liable under the contract unless the other party agreed to release the delegator and substitute a new one (ie, agreed to a novation). Here, the building owner likely had a substantial interest in having the expert perform the contract—especially since the installation of the air-conditioning equipment required special skills. As a result, the expert could not delegate his duties to the contractor without the owner's consent. And although the owner consented, the owner did not agree to release the expert from liability under the contract. Therefore, the owner can sue the expert for damages caused by the contractor's negligent installation of the air-conditioning equipment. (Choice A) A novation is a substituted contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party. But merely consenting to a delegation (as seen here) does not create a novation. (Choice B) The liquidated-damage clause provided only for damages caused by delay in performance. It does not preclude recovery for damages caused by other breaches (eg, negligent installation). (Choice D) The building owner's consent to the delegation did not need to be supported by consideration to be valid, so this is not a basis for the owner to succeed. Instead, the owner will succeed because the owner did not release the expert from liability under the contract.\n\nEducational objective: After a delegation (ie, transfer of contractual duties), the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agreed to release the delegating party and substitute a new one (ie, agreed to a novation).\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of duties).","educationalObjective":"After a delegation (ie, transfer of contractual duties), the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agreed to release the delegating party and substitute a new one (ie, agreed to a novation).","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of duties).","rule":"After a delegation (ie, transfer of contractual duties), the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agreed to release the delegating party and substitute a new one (ie, agreed to a novation).","irac":{"issue":"Will the owner succeed in an action against the expert for damages for breach of contract?","rule":"After a delegation (ie, transfer of contractual duties), the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agreed to release the delegating party and substitute a new one (ie, agreed to a novation).","analysis":"Nondelegable contractual duties\n\nDelegation not permitted when:\n\n• other contracting party has substantial interest in having delegating party perform (eg, in personal-services contract involving taste or special skill) or\n• delegation is prohibited by contract Obligations under a contract can generally be delegated unless:\n• the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform (eg, in a personal-services contract involving taste or a special skill) or\n\n• the delegation is prohibited by the contract (eg, states that \"this contract may not be assigned\").\n\nHowever, a party may consent to a delegation that would otherwise be prohibited. After a successful delegation, the delegator remains liable under the contract unless the other party agreed to release the delegator and substitute a new one (ie, agreed to a novation). Here, the building owner likely had a substantial interest in having the expert perform the contract—especially since the installation of the air-conditioning equipment required special skills. As a result, the expert could not delegate his duties to the contractor without the owner's consent. And although the owner consented, the owner did not agree to release the expert from liability under the contract. Therefore, the owner can sue the expert for damages caused by the contractor's negligent installation of the air-conditioning equipment. (Choice A) A novation is a substituted contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party. But merely consenting to a delegation (as seen here) does not create a novation. (Choice B) The liquidated-damage clause provided only for damages caused by delay in performance. It does not preclude recovery for damages caused by other breaches (eg, negligent installation). (Choice D) The building owner's consent to the delegation did not need to be supported by consideration to be valid, so this is not a basis for the owner to succeed. Instead, the owner will succeed because the owner did not release the expert from liability under the contract.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the owner did not agree to release the expert from liability under the contract."},"questionZh":"在高层建筑顶部安装设备的专家以书面形式签订了在建筑物顶部吊装和安装某些空调设备的合同。合同中的免责条款规定，专家对空调设备安装过程中对建筑物造成的任何物理损坏不承担责任。还有一项条款规定，如果专家没有在指定日期之前完成履约，则按日赔偿损失，并有一项条款规定“时间至关重要”。另一项条款规定，合同项下任何后续额外工作的协议必须以书面形式并由双方签署。由于合同规定的开始和完成工作还有足够的时间，专家通知建筑物的业主，他正在将其业务出售给承包商，该承包商同样是在高层建筑顶部安装设备的专家，并且承包商已同意“接管合同”。业主口头同意专家接受承包商的服务。承包商按时履约，但疏忽安装了错误的空调设备。业主向专家提起违约赔偿诉讼会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为业主接受承包商替换专家后，就实现了更替，专家因此解除了合同规定的职责。","B":"不可以，因为合同中的违约金条款仅规定了因迟延履行而造成的损害赔偿。","C":"是的，因为业主不同意免除专家在合同项下的责任。","D":"是的，因为业主未提供约因以同意由承包商取代专家。"},"explanationZh":"不可委托的合同义务\n\n在以下情况下不允许委托：\n\n• 其他缔约方对委托方履行具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止委托 合同下的义务通常可以委托，除非：\n• 合同的另一方对委托方履行合同具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n\n• 合同禁止委托（例如，规定“本合同不得转让”）。\n\n然而，一方可以同意否则会被禁止的授权。成功委托后，除非另一方同意释放该委托人并更换新的委托人（即同意更新），否则委托人仍应承担合同项下的责任。在这里，业主可能对让专家履行合同非常感兴趣——特别是因为空调设备的安装需要特殊技能。因此，未经业主同意，专家不能将其职责委托给承包商。尽管业主同意了，但业主并不同意免除专家在合同项下的责任。因此，业主可以起诉专家，要求赔偿因承包商安装空调设备的疏忽而造成的损失。 （选择 A） 更替是一种替代合同，其中当事人同意用新当事人取代原来的当事人。但仅仅同意授权（如此处所示）并不会产生更新。 （选项B） 违约金条款仅规定了因迟延履行而造成的损失。它并不排除对其他违规行为（例如疏忽安装）造成的损害进行恢复。 （选项D） 建筑物业主对委托的同意无需约因考虑即可有效，因此这不是业主成功的基础。相反，业主会成功，因为业主没有免除专家在合同下的责任。\n\n教育目标：授权（即合同义务的转移）后，授权方仍对合同承担责任，除非另一方同意释放授权方并更换新的授权方（即同意更新）。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（职责委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"委托（即合同义务转移）后，委托方仍应承担合同规定的责任，除非另一方同意解除委托方并更换新的委托方（即同意更新）。","iracZh":{"issue":"业主向专家提起违约赔偿诉讼会成功吗？","rule":"委托（即合同义务转移）后，委托方仍应承担合同规定的责任，除非另一方同意解除委托方并更换新的委托方（即同意更新）。","analysis":"不可委托的合同义务\n\n在以下情况下不允许委托：\n\n• 其他缔约方对委托方履行具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止委托 合同下的义务通常可以委托，除非：\n• 合同的另一方对委托方履行合同具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n\n• 合同禁止委托（例如，规定“本合同不得转让”）。\n\n然而，一方可以同意否则会被禁止的授权。成功委托后，除非另一方同意释放该委托人并更换新的委托人（即同意更新），否则委托人仍应承担合同项下的责任。在这里，业主可能对让专家履行合同非常感兴趣——特别是因为空调设备的安装需要特殊技能。因此，未经业主同意，专家不能将其职责委托给承包商。尽管业主同意了，但业主并不同意免除专家在合同项下的责任。因此，业主可以起诉专家，要求赔偿因承包商安装空调设备的疏忽而造成的损失。 （选择 A） 更替是一种替代合同，其中当事人同意用新当事人取代原来的当事人。但仅仅同意授权（如此处所示）并不会产生更新。 （选项B） 违约金条款仅规定了因迟延履行而造成的损失。它并不排除对其他违规行为（例如疏忽安装）造成的损害进行恢复。 （选项D） 建筑物业主对委托的同意无需约因考虑即可有效，因此这不是业主成功的基础。相反，业主会成功，因为业主没有免除专家在合同下的责任。\n\n教育目标：授权（即合同义务的转移）后，授权方仍对合同承担责任，除非另一方同意释放授权方并更换新的授权方（即同意更新）。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（职责委托）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为业主不同意免除专家在合同项下的责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":72,"sourceQuestionPage":211,"sourceQuestionPages":[211],"sourceExplanationPages":[212,213],"sourceAnswerPages":[212,213],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-073","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"On May 4, a widow and a car dealership both signed a single document evidencing a contract for the sale by the dealership to the widow, \"as a wedding gift for the widow's son, a new luxury sedan, with all available equipment, for $180,000 cash on delivery not later than June 1.\" On May 5, the widow handed her son a carbon copy of this document. In reliance on the prospective gift, the son on May 20 sold his nearly new sports car to a dealer at a \"bargain\" price of $50,000 and immediately informed the widow and the dealership that he had done so. On May 25, however, the widow and the car dealership by mutual agreement rescinded in a signed writing \"any and all agreements heretofore made between the undersigned parties for the sale-and-purchase of a new luxury sedan.\" Later that day, the dealership sold for $190,000 cash to another buyer the only new luxury sedan that it had in stock or could readily obtain elsewhere. On June 1, the son tendered $180,000 in cash to the car dealership and demanded delivery to him \"within a reasonable time\" of a new luxury sedan with all available equipment. The dealership rejected the tender and denied any obligation. If the son sues the dealership for breach of contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?","choices":{"A":"The dealership wins, because it reasonably and prejudicially relied on its contract of mutual rescission with the widow by selling the only readily available new luxury sedan to another buyer.","B":"The dealership wins, because the son, if an intended beneficiary at all of the widow- dealership contract, was only a donee beneficiary.","C":"The son wins, because his rights as an assignee for value of the May 4 widow- dealership contract cannot be cut off by agreement between the original parties.","D":"The son wins, because his rights as a third-party intended beneficiary became vested by his prejudicial reliance in selling his sports car on May 20."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Intended third-party beneficiaries' recovery for breach\n\nType of Versus party obligated to pay for Versus party obligated to confer beneficiary benefit benefit\n\nDonee Cannot recover unless detrimental Can recover if rights vested reliance satisfied through: (recipient of gift)\n• detrimental reliance Creditor Can recover\n• manifestation of assent OR (satisfaction of\n• lawsuit debt)\n\nA contract formed for the primary purpose of benefitting a third party can be modified or rescinded until the rights of the third party (ie, intended beneficiary) have vested. Vesting occurs when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Vested rights cannot be modified without the beneficiary's consent, and vesting allows the beneficiary to recover for breach from the party obligated to confer the benefit. Here, the widow contracted with the dealership to buy a luxury sedan for her son—the intended beneficiary of the contract. On May 20, the son prejudicially (ie, detrimentally) relied on this prospective gift by selling his sports car at a bargain price. The son's rights vested at that time, so the widow and the dealership could not rescind the contract on May 25 without his consent. And because the dealership sold the sedan to another buyer, the son will probably win his breach-of-contract suit. (Choice A) It was unreasonable for the dealership to rely on its contract of mutual rescission since the son had already informed the dealership about the sale of his sports car in reliance on the prospective gift. (Choice B) The son was a donee beneficiary because the luxury sedan was a gift. But his vested rights could not be modified without his consent, regardless of whether he was a donee or creditor beneficiary. (Choice C) Although contract rights can be assigned (ie, transferred) for value, the son was an intended third-party beneficiary (not an assignee) since his gift was the primary purpose of the contract.\n\nEducational objective: An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) sues to enforce it. Once vesting occurs, the contracting parties cannot modify the beneficiary's rights without the beneficiary's consent.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (variation of a duty to a beneficiary).","educationalObjective":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) sues to enforce it. Once vesting occurs, the contracting parties cannot modify the beneficiary's rights without the beneficiary's consent.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 311 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (variation of a duty to a beneficiary).","rule":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) sues to enforce it. Once vesting occurs, the contracting parties cannot modify the beneficiary's rights without the beneficiary's consent.","irac":{"issue":"If the son sues the dealership for breach of contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?","rule":"An intended beneficiary's contractual rights vest when the beneficiary (1) detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) manifests assent to the contract, or (3) sues to enforce it. Once vesting occurs, the contracting parties cannot modify the beneficiary's rights without the beneficiary's consent.","analysis":"Intended third-party beneficiaries' recovery for breach\n\nType of Versus party obligated to pay for Versus party obligated to confer beneficiary benefit benefit\n\nDonee Cannot recover unless detrimental Can recover if rights vested reliance satisfied through: (recipient of gift)\n• detrimental reliance Creditor Can recover\n• manifestation of assent OR (satisfaction of\n• lawsuit debt)\n\nA contract formed for the primary purpose of benefitting a third party can be modified or rescinded until the rights of the third party (ie, intended beneficiary) have vested. Vesting occurs when the beneficiary:\n• detrimentally relies on the rights created\n• manifests assent to the contract at one party's request or\n• files a lawsuit to enforce the contract. Vested rights cannot be modified without the beneficiary's consent, and vesting allows the beneficiary to recover for breach from the party obligated to confer the benefit. Here, the widow contracted with the dealership to buy a luxury sedan for her son—the intended beneficiary of the contract. On May 20, the son prejudicially (ie, detrimentally) relied on this prospective gift by selling his sports car at a bargain price. The son's rights vested at that time, so the widow and the dealership could not rescind the contract on May 25 without his consent. And because the dealership sold the sedan to another buyer, the son will probably win his breach-of-contract suit. (Choice A) It was unreasonable for the dealership to rely on its contract of mutual rescission since the son had already informed the dealership about the sale of his sports car in reliance on the prospective gift. (Choice B) The son was a donee beneficiary because the luxury sedan was a gift. But his vested rights could not be modified without his consent, regardless of whether he was a donee or creditor beneficiary. (Choice C) Although contract rights can be assigned (ie, transferred) for value, the son was an intended third-party beneficiary (not an assignee) since his gift was the primary purpose of the contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: The son wins, because his rights as a third-party intended beneficiary became vested by his prejudicial reliance in selling his sports car on May 20."},"questionZh":"5 月 4 日，一位寡妇和一家汽车经销商签署了一份文件，证明经销商向寡妇出售了一份合同，“作为寡妇儿子的结婚礼物，一辆新的豪华轿车，配备所有可用的设备，价格为 180,000 美元，不晚于 6 月 1 日交货。” 5月5日，寡妇将这份文件的副本交给了她的儿子。依靠这份预期的礼物，儿子于 5 月 20 日以 5 万美元的“讨价还价”价格将他几乎全新的跑车卖给了经销商，并立即通知寡妇和经销商他已经这样做了。然而，5 月 25 日，遗孀和汽车经销店通过双方协议，在一份签署的书面文件中撤销了“迄今为止双方签署的有关新豪华轿车买卖的所有协议”。当天晚些时候，经销商以 190,000 美元现金的价格将其库存中或可以在其他地方轻松获得的唯一新款豪华轿车卖给了另一位买家。 6 月 1 日，儿子向汽车经销商支付了 18 万美元现金，并要求“在合理的时间内”向他交付一辆配备所有可用设备的新豪华轿车。经销商拒绝了投标并否认承担任何义务。如果儿子起诉经销商违约，法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"经销商获胜，因为它合理且有偏见地依赖与寡妇的相互解除合同，将唯一现成的新豪华轿车出售给另一位买家。","B":"经销商获胜，因为儿子即使是寡妇经销商合同的预期受益人，也只是受赠人受益人。","C":"儿子获胜，因为他作为 5 月 4 日寡妇经销合同价值受让人的权利不能通过原始当事人之间的协议而被切断。","D":"儿子获胜，因为他作为第三方预期受益人的权利是由于他在 5 月 20 日出售他的跑车时的偏见依赖而获得的。"},"explanationZh":"预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）的违约赔偿\n\n类型 承担支付义务的一方 承担受益人利益的一方\n\n受赠人无法恢复，除非有害 如果通过以下方式满足既得权利的信赖，则可以恢复：（礼物接受者）\n• 债权人可以恢复的有害信赖\n• 表示同意或（满足\n• 诉讼债务）\n\n以让第三方受益为主要目的而订立的合同可以修改或撤销，直到第三方（即预期受益人）的权利归属为止。当受益人出现以下情况时，归属发生：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。未经受益人同意，既得权利不得修改，并且归属允许受益人向有义务授予该利益的一方追偿违约行为。在这里，寡妇与经销商签订了合同，为她的儿子（合同的预期受益人）购买一辆豪华轿车。 5 月 20 日，儿子有偏见地（即不利地）依靠这份预期的礼物以便宜的价格出售了他的跑车。当时儿子的权利已归属，所以未经儿子同意，寡妇和经销商不能在5月25日解除合同。由于经销商将轿车卖给了另一位买家，儿子可能会赢得违约诉讼。 （选项A） 经销店以双方解除合同为依据是不合理的，因为儿子已经通知经销店他的跑车是依靠预期的礼物而出售的。 （选项B） 儿子是受赠人受益人，因为豪华轿车是一份礼物。但无论他是受赠人还是债权人受益人，未经他的同意，他的既定权利都不能修改。 （选项C）虽然合同权利可以有价转让（即转让），但儿子是预期的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）（而非受让人），因为他的礼物是合同的主要目的。\n\n教育目标：当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确表示同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。一旦归属发生，未经受益人同意，缔约方不得修改受益人的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 311（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受益人的义务的变更）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确表示同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。一旦归属发生，未经受益人同意，缔约方不得修改受益人的权利。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果儿子起诉经销商违约，法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","rule":"当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确表示同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。一旦归属发生，未经受益人同意，缔约方不得修改受益人的权利。","analysis":"预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）的违约赔偿\n\n类型 承担支付义务的一方 承担受益人利益的一方\n\n受赠人无法恢复，除非有害 如果通过以下方式满足既得权利的信赖，则可以恢复：（礼物接受者）\n• 债权人可以恢复的有害信赖\n• 表示同意或（满足\n• 诉讼债务）\n\n以让第三方受益为主要目的而订立的合同可以修改或撤销，直到第三方（即预期受益人）的权利归属为止。当受益人出现以下情况时，归属发生：\n• 有害地依赖所创建的权利\n• 应一方要求表明同意合同或\n• 提起诉讼以强制执行合同。未经受益人同意，既得权利不得修改，并且归属允许受益人向有义务授予该利益的一方追偿违约行为。在这里，寡妇与经销商签订了合同，为她的儿子（合同的预期受益人）购买一辆豪华轿车。 5 月 20 日，儿子有偏见地（即不利地）依靠这份预期的礼物以便宜的价格出售了他的跑车。当时儿子的权利已归属，所以未经儿子同意，寡妇和经销商不能在5月25日解除合同。由于经销商将轿车卖给了另一位买家，儿子可能会赢得违约诉讼。 （选项A） 经销店以双方解除合同为依据是不合理的，因为儿子已经通知经销店他的跑车是依靠预期的礼物而出售的。 （选项B） 儿子是受赠人受益人，因为豪华轿车是一份礼物。但无论他是受赠人还是债权人受益人，未经他的同意，他的既定权利都不能修改。 （选项C）虽然合同权利可以有价转让（即转让），但儿子是预期的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）（而非受让人），因为他的礼物是合同的主要目的。\n\n教育目标：当受益人 (1) 不利地依赖所设定的权利，(2) 明确表示同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的合同权利即归属。一旦归属发生，未经受益人同意，缔约方不得修改受益人的权利。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 311（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对受益人的义务的变更）。","conclusion":"答案 D：儿子获胜，因为他作为第三方预期受益人的权利是由于他在 5 月 20 日出售他的跑车时的偏见依赖而获得的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"detrimental reliance","ipa":"/dˌɛtɹʌmˈɛntəl ɹɪlˈaɪəns/","syllables":"detri·men·tal re·liance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不利信赖：因相信允诺而合理改变立场并遭受不利益","contextualMeaning":"不利信赖：因相信允诺而合理改变立场并遭受不利益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Intended third-party beneficiaries' recovery for breach Type of Versus party obligated to pay for Versus party obligated to confer beneficiary benefit benefit Donee Cannot recover unless detrimental Can recover if rights vested reliance satisfied through: (recipient of gift) • detrimental reliance Creditor Can recover • manifestation of assent OR (satisfaction of • lawsuit debt) A contract formed for the primary purp","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":73,"sourceQuestionPage":214,"sourceQuestionPages":[214],"sourceExplanationPages":[215,216],"sourceAnswerPages":[215,216],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-074","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"On July 15, in a writing signed by both parties, the seller agreed to deliver to the buyer five storage cabinets from inventory for a total price of $5,000 to be paid on August 15, the date of delivery. On August 1, the two parties orally agreed to postpone the delivery date to August 20. On August 20, the seller tendered the cabinets to the buyer, who refused to accept or pay for them on the ground that they were not tendered on August 15, even though they otherwise met the contract specifications. Assuming that all appropriate defenses have been raised, will the seller succeed in an action against the buyer for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the parol evidence rule will prevent proof of the August 1 agreement.","B":"No, because there was no consideration to support the August 1 agreement.","C":"Yes, because neither the July 15 agreement nor the August 1 agreement was required to be in writing.","D":"Yes, because the August 1 agreement was an effective modification of the July 15 agreement."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Unwritten modifications of contracts\n\nContract silent on modification\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies (common law & UCC)\n\nContract clause Common\n• Clause unenforceable requires written law\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute modification of frauds applies UCC\n• Clause enforceable (ie, unwritten modification invalid) if: – contract written & signed – clause separately signed by nonmerchant if sued by merchant\n• Clause waived (ie, unwritten modification valid) if: – parties intended unwritten modification – one party materially changed position in reliance UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more must be in a writing that states the quantity of goods to be sold (eg, five cabinets) and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (eg, the buyer). However, modifications of a contract that falls within and satisfies this statute generally need not be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold.* Here, the July 15 agreement to sell cabinets for $5,000 needed to be—and was—in a writing that satisfied the statute of frauds. However, the August 1 agreement to postpone the delivery date to August 20 did not need to be in writing since it affected neither subject matter nor quantity (Choice C). Therefore, the August 1 agreement effectively modified the July 15 agreement. And since the buyer's refusal to accept and pay for the cabinets on August 20 constituted a breach, the seller's action will succeed. *An attempted modification that violates the UCC statute of frauds operates as a waiver of the original term that cannot be retracted once a party has materially changed its position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. (Choice A) The parol evidence rule generally prevents a party to a written contract from presenting evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the terms of\n\nthe contract as written. But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, August 1 oral agreement to postpone delivery). (Choice B) Under the UCC, contract modifications are enforceable so long as they are made in good faith; new consideration is not required.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, modifications of a contract that falls within and satisfies the statute of frauds do not need to be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (contract modification requirements).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, modifications of a contract that falls within and satisfies the statute of frauds do not need to be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds).\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (contract modification requirements).","rule":"Under the UCC, modifications of a contract that falls within and satisfies the statute of frauds do not need to be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that all appropriate defenses have been raised, will the seller succeed in an action against the buyer for breach of contract?","rule":"Under the UCC, modifications of a contract that falls within and satisfies the statute of frauds do not need to be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"Unwritten modifications of contracts\n\nContract silent on modification\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies (common law & UCC)\n\nContract clause Common\n• Clause unenforceable requires written law\n• Unwritten modification valid unless statute modification of frauds applies UCC\n• Clause enforceable (ie, unwritten modification invalid) if: – contract written & signed – clause separately signed by nonmerchant if sued by merchant\n• Clause waived (ie, unwritten modification valid) if: – parties intended unwritten modification – one party materially changed position in reliance UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods for $500 or more must be in a writing that states the quantity of goods to be sold (eg, five cabinets) and is signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought (eg, the buyer). However, modifications of a contract that falls within and satisfies this statute generally need not be in writing unless they affect the subject matter of the contract or the quantity of goods to be sold.* Here, the July 15 agreement to sell cabinets for $5,000 needed to be—and was—in a writing that satisfied the statute of frauds. However, the August 1 agreement to postpone the delivery date to August 20 did not need to be in writing since it affected neither subject matter nor quantity (Choice C). Therefore, the August 1 agreement effectively modified the July 15 agreement. And since the buyer's refusal to accept and pay for the cabinets on August 20 constituted a breach, the seller's action will succeed. *An attempted modification that violates the UCC statute of frauds operates as a waiver of the original term that cannot be retracted once a party has materially changed its position in reasonable reliance on the waiver. (Choice A) The parol evidence rule generally prevents a party to a written contract from presenting evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the terms of\n\nthe contract as written. But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, August 1 oral agreement to postpone delivery). (Choice B) Under the UCC, contract modifications are enforceable so long as they are made in good faith; new consideration is not required.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the August 1 agreement was an effective modification of the July 15 agreement."},"questionZh":"7月15日，在双方签署的一份书面文件中，卖方同意从库存中向买方交付5个储物柜，总价为5,000美元，并于8月15日（即交货之日）支付。 8月1日，双方口头同意将交货期推迟至8月20日。8月20日，卖方将柜子交付给买方，买方以8月15日未交付为由拒绝收货或付款，尽管柜子在其他方面均符合合同规格。假设所有适当的抗辩均已提出，卖方对买方违约的诉讼是否会成功？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）将阻止对 8 月 1 日协议进行证明。","B":"不，因为没有约因支持8月1日的协议。","C":"是的，因为 7 月 15 日协议和 8 月 1 日协议都不需要采用书面形式。","D":"是的，因为8月1日的协议是7月15日协议的有效修改。"},"explanationZh":"合同的不成文修改\n\n合同对修改保持沉默\n• 除非欺诈法适用，否则不成文的修改有效（普通法和 UCC）\n\n合同条款 通用\n• 不可执行的条款需要成文法\n• 除非防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）修改适用 UCC，否则不成文修改有效\n• 条款可执行（即，非书面修改无效），如果： – 合同已书面签署 – 如果被商家起诉，则由非商家单独签署条款\n• 条款被放弃（即，不成文修改有效），如果： – 各方有意进行不成文修改 – 一方实质上改变了依赖UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）的立场。\n\n根据 UCC 欺诈法，价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同必须采用书面形式，说明待售商品的数量（例如，五个柜子），并由寻求执行的一方（例如，买方）签署。然而，符合并满足该法规的合同修改通常不需要采用书面形式，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售货物的数量。*在此，7 月 15 日以 5,000 美元出售橱柜的协议需要并且已经是满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面形式。但8月1日约定将交货期推迟至8月20日，无需书面形式，因为既不影响标的物，也不影响数量（选项C）。因此，8月1日的协议实际上修改了7月15日的协议。由于买方在8月20日拒绝收货和付款的行为构成违约，卖方的诉讼将成功。 *违反 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的尝试修改视为对原始条款的弃权，一旦一方合理依赖弃权而实质性改变其立场，则无法撤回。 （选择 A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常防止书面合同的一方提供与书面合同条款相矛盾的先前或同期协议的证据。\n\n书面合同。但该规则并不禁止提供双方后续协议的证据（例如，8月1日口头协议推迟交货）。 （选择B）根据UCC，只要善意地进行合同修改即可强制执行；不需要新的考虑。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法典》（UCC），属于并满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同修改不需要以书面形式进行，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售商品的数量。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（合同修改要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），属于并满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同修改不需要以书面形式进行，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售货物的数量。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设所有适当的抗辩均已提出，卖方对买方违约的诉讼是否会成功？","rule":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），属于并满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同修改不需要以书面形式进行，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售货物的数量。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"合同的不成文修改\n\n合同对修改保持沉默\n• 除非欺诈法适用，否则不成文的修改有效（普通法和 UCC）\n\n合同条款 通用\n• 不可执行的条款需要成文法\n• 除非防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）修改适用 UCC，否则不成文修改有效\n• 条款可执行（即，非书面修改无效），如果： – 合同已书面签署 – 如果被商家起诉，则由非商家单独签署条款\n• 条款被放弃（即，不成文修改有效），如果： – 各方有意进行不成文修改 – 一方实质上改变了依赖UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）的立场。\n\n根据 UCC 欺诈法，价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同必须采用书面形式，说明待售商品的数量（例如，五个柜子），并由寻求执行的一方（例如，买方）签署。然而，符合并满足该法规的合同修改通常不需要采用书面形式，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售货物的数量。*在此，7 月 15 日以 5,000 美元出售橱柜的协议需要并且已经是满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的书面形式。但8月1日约定将交货期推迟至8月20日，无需书面形式，因为既不影响标的物，也不影响数量（选项C）。因此，8月1日的协议实际上修改了7月15日的协议。由于买方在8月20日拒绝收货和付款的行为构成违约，卖方的诉讼将成功。 *违反 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的尝试修改视为对原始条款的弃权，一旦一方合理依赖弃权而实质性改变其立场，则无法撤回。 （选择 A）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常防止书面合同的一方提供与书面合同条款相矛盾的先前或同期协议的证据。\n\n书面合同。但该规则并不禁止提供双方后续协议的证据（例如，8月1日口头协议推迟交货）。 （选择B）根据UCC，只要善意地进行合同修改即可强制执行；不需要新的考虑。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法典》（UCC），属于并满足防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的合同修改不需要以书面形式进行，除非它们影响合同标的物或待售商品的数量。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n• U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（合同修改要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为8月1日的协议是7月15日协议的有效修改。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":74,"sourceQuestionPage":217,"sourceQuestionPages":[217],"sourceExplanationPages":[218,219],"sourceAnswerPages":[218,219],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-075","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A husband lost his job and decided to borrow money against his marital home. Each time he applied for a mortgage, his wife contacted the lender and withdrew the mortgage application. One Saturday evening, the husband forced the wife to sit at the kitchen table and placed a mortgage application in front of her. As he waved a large pair of scissors in front of the wife's face, he demanded that she promise to sign the application by the end of the week. The wife, frightened by her husband, agreed to do so. On Monday morning, the wife went to a notary public's office alone and signed the application. The notary asked the wife if she signed the application of her own free will, and the wife replied, \"It is what it is.\" Several months later, the mortgage went into default. Does the lender have a breach-of-contract claim against the wife?","choices":{"A":"No, because the husband physically compelled the wife to sign the mortgage application.","B":"No, because the husband threatened the wife with serious bodily harm.","C":"Yes, because the wife never voided the contract.","D":"Yes, because the wife was no longer under duress when she signed the mortgage application."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Duress is a defense to contract enforceability when a party was induced to assent by any of the following:\n• Physical compulsion – rendering the contract void if the party's assent was compelled by physical force OR imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm\n\n• Improper threat by contracting party – rendering the contract voidable by the threatened party if he/she had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat\n\n• Improper threat by third party – rendering the contract voidable by the threatened party if he/she had no choice but to succumb to the third party's improper threat (not seen here), unless the other contracting party did not know of the duress AND relied on the contract in good faith\n\nHere, the wife manifested her assent to the contract by signing the mortgage application. The husband did not physically compel her to do so—eg, by grabbing her hand and using it to sign (Choice A). And though waving scissors in front of the wife's face on Saturday may have threatened serious bodily harm, that threat was no longer imminent when she signed the application on Monday (Choice B). Therefore, duress is no defense, and the lender has a breach-of-contract claim against the wife. (Choice C) A contract may be voidable in certain situations, none of which are seen here. Therefore, the wife could not have voided the contract after willingly signing it.\n\nEducational objective: Duress occurs when a party's assent to a contract is compelled by physical force or imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm. Duress also occurs when a party receives an improper threat and has no reasonable alternative but to succumb.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 174–176 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discussing duress caused by physical compulsion and improper threat).","educationalObjective":"Duress occurs when a party's assent to a contract is compelled by physical force or imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm. Duress also occurs when a party receives an improper threat and has no reasonable alternative but to succumb.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 174–176 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discussing duress caused by physical compulsion and improper threat).","rule":"Duress occurs when a party's assent to a contract is compelled by physical force or imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm. Duress also occurs when a party receives an improper threat and has no reasonable alternative but to succumb.","irac":{"issue":"Does the lender have a breach-of-contract claim against the wife?","rule":"Duress occurs when a party's assent to a contract is compelled by physical force or imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm. Duress also occurs when a party receives an improper threat and has no reasonable alternative but to succumb.","analysis":"Duress is a defense to contract enforceability when a party was induced to assent by any of the following:\n• Physical compulsion – rendering the contract void if the party's assent was compelled by physical force OR imminent threat of death or serious bodily harm\n\n• Improper threat by contracting party – rendering the contract voidable by the threatened party if he/she had no reasonable alternative but to succumb to the improper threat\n\n• Improper threat by third party – rendering the contract voidable by the threatened party if he/she had no choice but to succumb to the third party's improper threat (not seen here), unless the other contracting party did not know of the duress AND relied on the contract in good faith\n\nHere, the wife manifested her assent to the contract by signing the mortgage application. The husband did not physically compel her to do so—eg, by grabbing her hand and using it to sign (Choice A). And though waving scissors in front of the wife's face on Saturday may have threatened serious bodily harm, that threat was no longer imminent when she signed the application on Monday (Choice B). Therefore, duress is no defense, and the lender has a breach-of-contract claim against the wife. (Choice C) A contract may be voidable in certain situations, none of which are seen here. Therefore, the wife could not have voided the contract after willingly signing it.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the wife was no longer under duress when she signed the mortgage application."},"questionZh":"一位丈夫失业了，决定以婚姻房屋为抵押借钱。每次他申请抵押贷款时，他的妻子都会联系贷款人并撤回抵押贷款申请。一个周六的晚上，丈夫强迫妻子坐在厨房的桌子旁，并将一份抵押贷款申请放在她面前。当他在妻子面前挥舞一把大剪刀时，他要求她承诺在本周末之前签署申请表。妻子被丈夫吓坏了，就同意了。周一早上，妻子独自前往公证处，在申请书上签字。公证员询问妻子是否自愿在申请书上签字，妻子回答说：“就是这样。”几个月后，抵押贷款违约。贷款人是否对妻子提出违约索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为丈夫用身体强迫妻子在抵押贷款申请上签字。","B":"不，因为丈夫威胁要严重伤害妻子的身体。","C":"是的，因为妻子从未废除合同。","D":"是的，因为妻子在抵押贷款申请上签字时不再受到胁迫。"},"explanationZh":"当一方因以下任何原因而同意时，胁迫是对合同可执行性的抗辩：\n• 身体强迫——如果当事人通过身体力量或迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁而同意，则合同无效\n\n• 缔约方的不当威胁——如果受威胁方没有合理的选择只能屈从于不当威胁，则合同无效\n\n• 第三方的不当威胁 – 如果受威胁方别无选择，只能屈从于第三方的不当威胁（此处未看到），则合同将被视为无效，除非另一缔约方不知道胁迫并善意地依赖合同\n\n在此，妻子通过签署抵押申请表表明她同意该合同。丈夫并没有用身体强迫她这样做——例如，抓住她的手并用它来签名（选项A）。尽管周六在妻子面前挥舞剪刀可能会威胁造成严重的身体伤害，但当她周一签署申请时，这种威胁就不再迫在眉睫（选项 B）。因此，胁迫不能作为抗辩理由，贷款人对妻子提出违约索赔。 （选项C）合同在某些情况下可能是无效的，但这里没有看到这些情况。因此，妻子在自愿签订合同后，不能解除合同。\n\n教育目标：当一方受到武力或迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁而同意合同时，就会发生胁迫。当一方受到不当威胁并且没有合理的选择只能屈服时，也会发生胁迫。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 174–176（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨论由身体强迫和不当威胁造成的胁迫）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一方受到武力或迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁而被迫同意合同时，就会发生胁迫。当一方受到不当威胁并且没有合理的选择只能屈服时，也会发生胁迫。","iracZh":{"issue":"贷款人是否对妻子提出违约索赔？","rule":"当一方受到武力或迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁而被迫同意合同时，就会发生胁迫。当一方受到不当威胁并且没有合理的选择只能屈服时，也会发生胁迫。","analysis":"当一方因以下任何原因而同意时，胁迫是对合同可执行性的抗辩：\n• 身体强迫——如果当事人通过身体力量或迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁而同意，则合同无效\n\n• 缔约方的不当威胁——如果受威胁方没有合理的选择只能屈从于不当威胁，则合同无效\n\n• 第三方的不当威胁 – 如果受威胁方别无选择，只能屈从于第三方的不当威胁（此处未看到），则合同将被视为无效，除非另一缔约方不知道胁迫并善意地依赖合同\n\n在此，妻子通过签署抵押申请表表明她同意该合同。丈夫并没有用身体强迫她这样做——例如，抓住她的手并用它来签名（选项A）。尽管周六在妻子面前挥舞剪刀可能会威胁造成严重的身体伤害，但当她周一签署申请时，这种威胁就不再迫在眉睫（选项 B）。因此，胁迫不能作为抗辩理由，贷款人对妻子提出违约索赔。 （选项C）合同在某些情况下可能是无效的，但这里没有看到这些情况。因此，妻子在自愿签订合同后，不能解除合同。\n\n教育目标：当一方受到武力或迫在眉睫的死亡或严重身体伤害威胁而同意合同时，就会发生胁迫。当一方受到不当威胁并且没有合理的选择只能屈服时，也会发生胁迫。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 174–176（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨论由身体强迫和不当威胁造成的胁迫）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为妻子在抵押贷款申请上签字时不再受到胁迫。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-308ead32e5178d30"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":75,"sourceQuestionPage":220,"sourceQuestionPages":[220],"sourceExplanationPages":[221,222],"sourceAnswerPages":[221,222],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-076","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A business made an agreement in writing with a shareholder to purchase all of her stock in a corporation at a specified price per share. At the time the agreement was executed, the business said to the shareholder, \"Our commitment to buy is conditioned on first obtaining approval of the purchase agreement from our parent company.\" The shareholder replied, \"That will not be a problem.\" The shareholder is willing and ready to consummate the sale of her stock to the business, but the business refuses to perform on the ground (which is true) that the parent company has firmly refused to approve the contract. If the shareholder sues the business for breach of contract and seeks to exclude any evidence of the oral condition requiring the parent company's approval, how will the court likely rule?","choices":{"A":"Admit the evidence as proof of a collateral agreement.","B":"Admit the evidence as proof of a condition to the existence of an enforceable obligation, and therefore not within the scope of the parol evidence rule.","C":"Exclude the evidence as contradicting the terms of the parties' written agreement, whether or not the writing was a complete integration of the contract.","D":"Exclude the evidence on the basis of a finding that the parties' written agreement was a complete integration of their contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But this rule does not apply when a party is attacking the validity of the agreement—eg, by asserting there was a condition precedent to effectiveness that failed to occur. As a result, evidence of such a condition precedent is admissible. Here, the parties orally agreed that the business would be committed to buy the stock (ie, the purchase agreement would take effect) only if the parent company approved the agreement—a condition precedent to effectiveness. Therefore, the parol evidence rule does not apply, and evidence of the oral condition is admissible to prove that the purchase agreement failed to take effect because the parent company refused to approve it. (Choice A) Evidence of the oral condition is not evidence of a collateral agreement—ie, one that is related to the subject matter of the contract but not part of its primary promise. That is because the oral condition is part of the primary purpose of the agreement—ie, the purchase of the stock. (Choices C & D) The contract was a partial integration because it set forth the parties' final agreement about some, but not all, terms. But regardless of whether the contract was a complete or partial integration, the oral agreement is admissible because it evidences a condition precedent to effectiveness.\n\nEducational objective: Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (integrated agreement subject to oral requirement).","educationalObjective":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 217 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (integrated agreement subject to oral requirement).","rule":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","irac":{"issue":"If the shareholder sues the business for breach of contract and seeks to exclude any evidence of the oral condition requiring the parent company's approval, how will the court likely rule?","rule":"Evidence used to establish a condition precedent that must occur before a contract becomes effective is admissible under the parol evidence rule.","analysis":"Exceptions to parol evidence rule\n\nEvidence of prior or contemporaneous oral or written agreement is admissible to establish:\n\n• whether writing is integrated and, if so, completely or partially\n• meaning of ambiguous term\n• defense to formation or enforcement (eg, fraud, duress, mistake)\n• ground for granting or denying remedy (eg, rescission, reformation)\n• subsequent contract modifications\n• condition precedent to effectiveness The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But this rule does not apply when a party is attacking the validity of the agreement—eg, by asserting there was a condition precedent to effectiveness that failed to occur. As a result, evidence of such a condition precedent is admissible. Here, the parties orally agreed that the business would be committed to buy the stock (ie, the purchase agreement would take effect) only if the parent company approved the agreement—a condition precedent to effectiveness. Therefore, the parol evidence rule does not apply, and evidence of the oral condition is admissible to prove that the purchase agreement failed to take effect because the parent company refused to approve it. (Choice A) Evidence of the oral condition is not evidence of a collateral agreement—ie, one that is related to the subject matter of the contract but not part of its primary promise. That is because the oral condition is part of the primary purpose of the agreement—ie, the purchase of the stock. (Choices C & D) The contract was a partial integration because it set forth the parties' final agreement about some, but not all, terms. But regardless of whether the contract was a complete or partial integration, the oral agreement is admissible because it evidences a condition precedent to effectiveness.","conclusion":"Answer B: Admit the evidence as proof of a condition to the existence of an enforceable obligation, and therefore not within the scope of the parol evidence rule."},"questionZh":"一家企业与一位股东签订了书面协议，以每股指定价格购买其在一家公司的所有股票。在协议签署时，该公司向股东表示：“我们承诺购买的条件是首先获得母公司批准购买协议。”股东回答说：“那不会有问题。”股东愿意并准备完成将其股票出售给企业，但企业拒绝履行，理由是（这是事实）母公司坚决拒绝批准合同。如果股东起诉企业违反合同，并寻求排除任何需要母公司批准的口头条件的证据，法院可能会如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"承认证据作为抵押协议的证据。","B":"承认证据作为存在可执行义务的条件的证据，因此不属于口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的范围。","C":"排除与双方书面协议条款相矛盾的证据，无论书面协议是否完整。","D":"基于双方的书面协议是其合同的完整整合的结论而排除证据。"},"explanationZh":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时同意修改或反驳综合写作条款的证据。但当一方攻击协议的有效性时，该规则不适用——例如，声称存在生效的先决条件但未能发生。因此，此类先决条件的证据是可接受的。在此，双方口头同意，只有在母公司批准协议的情况下，企业才会承诺购买股票（即购买协议生效）——这是生效的先决条件。因此，不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），可以采用口头条件证据来证明收购协议因母公司拒绝批准而未能生效。 （选择A）口头条件的证据不是附属协议的证据——即与合同标的物相关但不属于其主要承诺的一部分。这是因为口头条件是协议主要目的的一部分，即购买股票。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 该合同是部分整合，因为它规定了双方就某些（但不是全部）条款达成的最终协议。但无论合同是完整的还是部分的，口头协议都是可以接受的，因为它证明了生效的先决条件。\n\n教育目标：根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于建立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 217（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（综合协议须遵守口头要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于确立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果股东起诉企业违反合同，并寻求排除任何需要母公司批准的口头条件的证据，法院可能会如何裁决？","rule":"根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于确立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。","analysis":"口供证据规则的例外情况\n\n先前或同时口头或书面协议的证据可以用来证明：\n\n• 书写是否是完整的，如果是，是完整的还是部分的\n• 不明确术语的含义\n• 对形成或执行的抗辩（例如欺诈、胁迫、错误）\n• 给予或拒绝补救措施的理由（例如撤销、改革）\n• 随后的合同修改\n• 生效的先决条件 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同时同意修改或反驳综合写作条款的证据。但当一方攻击协议的有效性时，该规则不适用——例如，声称存在生效的先决条件但未能发生。因此，此类先决条件的证据是可接受的。在此，双方口头同意，只有在母公司批准协议的情况下，企业才会承诺购买股票（即购买协议生效）——这是生效的先决条件。因此，不适用口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），可以采用口头条件证据来证明收购协议因母公司拒绝批准而未能生效。 （选择A）口头条件的证据不是附属协议的证据——即与合同标的物相关但不属于其主要承诺的一部分。这是因为口头条件是协议主要目的的一部分，即购买股票。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 该合同是部分整合，因为它规定了双方就某些（但不是全部）条款达成的最终协议。但无论合同是完整的还是部分的，口头协议都是可以接受的，因为它证明了生效的先决条件。\n\n教育目标：根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），用于建立合同生效之前必须发生的先决条件的证据是可接受的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 217（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（综合协议须遵守口头要求）。","conclusion":"答案 B：承认证据作为存在可执行义务的条件的证据，因此不属于口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）的范围。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":76,"sourceQuestionPage":223,"sourceQuestionPages":[223],"sourceExplanationPages":[224,225],"sourceAnswerPages":[224,225],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-077","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A manufacturer agreed in writing with a beverage company to provide all the flavor syrup the beverage company's soft drink requires for the next three years. The beverage company's soft drink contains a small amount of caffeine. The manufacturer promised in the contract to \"promote in good faith the sale of the soft drink,\" but the contract said nothing about restrictions on the products that the manufacturer could produce. The contract also stated that it was the \"complete and final agreement\" between the manufacturer and the beverage company. Six months later, the manufacturer agreed with a competitor of the beverage company to produce flavor syrup for its caffeine-free beverages. If the beverage company sues the manufacturer for breach of their contract, which of the following facts, if established, would most strengthen the beverage company's case?","choices":{"A":"For many years in the soft-drink industry, it has been uniform practice for manufacturers to handle only one beverage brand at a time.","B":"Prior to signing the contract with the beverage company, a representative of the manufacturer said that the deal with the company would be \"an exclusive.\"","C":"Since the manufacturer began to produce the competitor's beverages, the beverage company's sales have dropped 3%.","D":"The competitor's national advertising campaign disparages the beverage company's product by saying, \"You don't need caffeine and neither does your soft drink.\""},"answer":"A","explanation":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the parol evidence rule, prior or contemporaneous agreements cannot be used to contradict the express terms of a fully integrated, written contract. However, the UCC allows evidence of trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final contract so long as that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.* Trade usage refers to a practice or method of dealing that is observed with such regularity in the industry that the parties to a contract would be expected to observe it. Here, the manufacturer and the beverage company had a fully integrated agreement because it was their \"complete and final agreement.\" But the contract is silent as to whether the manufacturer can work for a competing beverage company. Therefore, evidence of trade usage—e.g., manufacturers to handle only one beverage brand at a time—is admissible to supplement the contract. And since this evidence shows that the manufacturer breached the contract when it agreed to produce flavor syrup for a competitor, it would most strengthen the company's case. *Under the majority rule, trade usage is consistent with express language in the contract unless the usage completely negates specific express language. (Choice B) Under the UCC, the parol evidence rule bars the admission of a prior agreement if the term certainly would have been included in the final contract but was not. Here, a\n\nterm of exclusivity would certainly have been included had that been the parties' intent. Therefore, this evidence would be inadmissible. (Choice C) The fact that the beverage company's sales have dropped since the manufacturer's agreement with the competitor suggests that the agreement has negatively impacted the beverage company. But this does not provide direct evidence that the manufacturer breached the contract by failing to promote the sale of the soft drink in good faith. Therefore, this is not the strongest evidence for the beverage company's case. (Choice D) The competitor's advertising campaign has no bearing on whether the manufacturer promoted the beverage company's soft drink \"in good faith.\"\n\nEducational objective: The UCC parol evidence rule allows evidence of trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final contract if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.\n\nReferences\n• UCC § 2-202 (explaining that trade usage is admissible to explain or supplement contract's terms).\n\n• UCC § 1-303 (defining trade usage).","educationalObjective":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows evidence of trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final contract if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.","references":"• UCC § 2-202 (explaining that trade usage is admissible to explain or supplement contract's terms).\n\n• UCC § 1-303 (defining trade usage).","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows evidence of trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final contract if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.","irac":{"issue":"If the beverage company sues the manufacturer for breach of their contract, which of the following facts, if established, would most strengthen the beverage company's case?","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule allows evidence of trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final contract if that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.","analysis":"UCC parol evidence rule\n\nDescription Priority of evidence\n\nInadmissible Prior/contemporaneous agreements that Express terms always contradict written contract control\n\nAdmissible Course of Sequence of conduct under Course of performance contract involving repeated performance > course occasions for performance of dealing & trade usage\n\nCourse of Sequence of conduct pertaining Course of dealing > dealing to previous contracts trade usage\n\nTrade usage Regular practice or method of N/A dealing in that particular business/industry\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the parol evidence rule, prior or contemporaneous agreements cannot be used to contradict the express terms of a fully integrated, written contract. However, the UCC allows evidence of trade usage to be used to explain or supplement the terms of a final contract so long as that evidence is reasonably consistent with the contract's express language.* Trade usage refers to a practice or method of dealing that is observed with such regularity in the industry that the parties to a contract would be expected to observe it. Here, the manufacturer and the beverage company had a fully integrated agreement because it was their \"complete and final agreement.\" But the contract is silent as to whether the manufacturer can work for a competing beverage company. Therefore, evidence of trade usage—e.g., manufacturers to handle only one beverage brand at a time—is admissible to supplement the contract. And since this evidence shows that the manufacturer breached the contract when it agreed to produce flavor syrup for a competitor, it would most strengthen the company's case. *Under the majority rule, trade usage is consistent with express language in the contract unless the usage completely negates specific express language. (Choice B) Under the UCC, the parol evidence rule bars the admission of a prior agreement if the term certainly would have been included in the final contract but was not. Here, a\n\nterm of exclusivity would certainly have been included had that been the parties' intent. Therefore, this evidence would be inadmissible. (Choice C) The fact that the beverage company's sales have dropped since the manufacturer's agreement with the competitor suggests that the agreement has negatively impacted the beverage company. But this does not provide direct evidence that the manufacturer breached the contract by failing to promote the sale of the soft drink in good faith. Therefore, this is not the strongest evidence for the beverage company's case. (Choice D) The competitor's advertising campaign has no bearing on whether the manufacturer promoted the beverage company's soft drink \"in good faith.\"","conclusion":"Answer A: For many years in the soft-drink industry, it has been uniform practice for manufacturers to handle only one beverage brand at a time."},"questionZh":"一家制造商与一家饮料公司书面同意，在未来三年内提供该饮料公司软饮料所需的所有风味糖浆。该饮料公司的软饮料含有少量咖啡因。制造商在合同中承诺“真诚地促进软饮料的销售”，但合同没有提及对制造商可以生产的产品的限制。合同还指出，这是制造商和饮料公司之间的“完整且最终的协议”。六个月后，制造商与饮料公司的竞争对手达成协议，为其不含咖啡因的饮料生产风味糖浆。如果饮料公司起诉制造商违反合同，以下哪项事实如果成立，最能加强饮料公司的诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"多年来，在软饮料行业，制造商一次只生产一种饮料品牌的做法一直是一致的。","B":"在与饮料公司签署合同之前，制造商的一位代表表示，与该公司的交易将是“排他性的”。","C":"自从制造商开始生产竞争对手的饮料以来，饮料公司的销售额下降了3%。","D":"竞争对手的全国广告活动贬低了饮料公司的产品，称：“你不需要咖啡因，你的软饮料也不需要。”"},"explanationZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前或同期的协议不能用来与完全整合的书面合同的明确条款相矛盾。然而，UCC 允许使用贸易惯例的证据来解释或补充最终合同的条款，只要该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致即可。*贸易惯例是指在行业中经常观察到的交易惯例或方法，期望合同各方遵守。在这里，制造商和饮料公司达成了完全整合的协议，因为这是他们的“完整且最终的协议”。但合同并未提及制造商是否可以为竞争对手的饮料公司工作。因此，贸易用途的证据（例如制造商一次仅经营一种饮料品牌）可以作为合同的补充。由于这一证据表明制造商在同意为竞争对手生产风味糖浆时违反了合同，因此这将最有力地支持该公司的诉讼。 *根据多数规则，贸易惯例与合同中的明示语言一致，除非该惯例完全否定特定的明示语言。 （选择 B）根据 UCC，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止承认事先协议，如果该条款本来应该包含在最终合同中，但事实并非如此。在这里，一个\n\n如果双方有意的话，排他性条款肯定会被包括在内。因此，该证据不予采信。 （选项C）自制造商与竞争对手达成协议以来，饮料公司的销售额下降，这一事实表明该协议对饮料公司产生了负面影响。但这并不能直接证明制造商因未善意推销软饮料而违反合同。因此，这并不是饮料公司案件最有力的证据。 （选项D） 竞争对手的广告活动与制造商是否“真诚地”推销饮料公司的软饮料无关。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充最终合同的条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。\n\n参考文献\n• UCC § 2-202（解释允许使用贸易用途来解释或补充合同条款）。\n\n• UCC § 1-303（定义贸易用途）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充最终合同的条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果饮料公司起诉制造商违反合同，以下哪项事实如果成立，最能加强饮料公司的诉讼？","rule":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充最终合同的条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。","analysis":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）\n\n说明 证据的优先级\n\n不可接受的先前/同期协议，明示条款始终与书面合同控制相矛盾\n\n涉及重复履行的履约合同过程中可接受的行为顺序过程 > 交易和贸易用途履行过程的过程场合\n\n与交易过程相关的行为顺序过程 > 处理先前合同的交易用途\n\n贸易用途 特定业务/行业中的常规做法或不适用的交易方法\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），先前或同期的协议不能用来与完全整合的书面合同的明确条款相矛盾。然而，UCC 允许使用贸易惯例的证据来解释或补充最终合同的条款，只要该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致即可。*贸易惯例是指在行业中经常观察到的交易惯例或方法，期望合同各方遵守。在这里，制造商和饮料公司达成了完全整合的协议，因为这是他们的“完整且最终的协议”。但合同并未提及制造商是否可以为竞争对手的饮料公司工作。因此，贸易用途的证据（例如制造商一次仅经营一种饮料品牌）可以作为合同的补充。由于这一证据表明制造商在同意为竞争对手生产风味糖浆时违反了合同，因此这将最有力地支持该公司的诉讼。 *根据多数规则，贸易惯例与合同中的明示语言一致，除非该惯例完全否定特定的明示语言。 （选择 B）根据 UCC，口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止承认事先协议，如果该条款本来应该包含在最终合同中，但事实并非如此。在这里，一个\n\n如果双方有意的话，排他性条款肯定会被包括在内。因此，该证据不予采信。 （选项C）自制造商与竞争对手达成协议以来，饮料公司的销售额下降，这一事实表明该协议对饮料公司产生了负面影响。但这并不能直接证明制造商因未善意推销软饮料而违反合同。因此，这并不是饮料公司案件最有力的证据。 （选项D） 竞争对手的广告活动与制造商是否“真诚地”推销饮料公司的软饮料无关。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）允许使用贸易惯例证据来解释或补充最终合同的条款，前提是该证据与合同的明确语言合理一致。\n\n参考文献\n• UCC § 2-202（解释允许使用贸易用途来解释或补充合同条款）。\n\n• UCC § 1-303（定义贸易用途）。","conclusion":"答案 A：多年来，在软饮料行业，制造商一次只生产一种饮料品牌的做法一直是一致的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"integrated agreement","ipa":"/ˈɪntʌɡɹˌeɪtəd ʌɡɹˈimənt/","syllables":"in·te·grated agree·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"整合协议：当事人拟作为最终表达的书面合同","contextualMeaning":"整合协议：当事人拟作为最终表达的书面合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Here, the manufacturer and the beverage company had a fully integrated agreement because it was their \"complete and final agreement.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":77,"sourceQuestionPage":226,"sourceQuestionPages":[226],"sourceExplanationPages":[227,228],"sourceAnswerPages":[227,228],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-078","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"In financial straits and needing $4,000 immediately, a nephew asked his uncle for a $4,000 loan. The uncle said that he would lend the money to the nephew only if the nephew's mother would \"guarantee\" the loan. At the nephew's suggestion, the uncle then telephoned the nephew's mother, told her about the loan, and asked if she would guarantee it. She replied, \"Yes. Lend my son the $4,000, and I'll repay it if he doesn't.\" The uncle then lent $4,000 to the nephew, and the nephew orally agreed to repay that amount in six weeks. The next day, the nephew's mother wrote to him and concluded her letter with the words, \"Son, I was happy to do you a favor by promising your uncle I would repay your six-week $4,000 loan if you don't. /s/ Mother.\" Neither the nephew nor his mother repaid the loan when it came due, and the uncle sued the mother for breach of contract. In that action, the mother raised the statute of frauds as her only defense. Will the mother's statute-of-frauds defense likely be successful?","choices":{"A":"No, because the amount of the loan was less than $5,000.","B":"No, because the mother's letter satisfies the statute-of-frauds requirement.","C":"Yes, because the mother's promise to the uncle was oral.","D":"Yes, because the nephew's promise to the uncle was oral."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another (eg, the mother's promise to repay the loan if her son does not). Suretyships are generally subject to the statute of frauds and therefore are only enforceable if they are:\n• in a writing—ie, physical or electronic document(s) such as a receipt, check, or emails—containing a reasonable basis to believe that a contract was formed and\n• signed through a handwritten signature or other identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought.* Here, the mother's letter satisfied the writing requirement because it evidenced her promise to repay the loan to the uncle. It also satisfied the signature requirement because her \"/s/ Mother\" notation on the letter identified her as the sender. As a result, the mother's statute-of-frauds defense will likely fail, and the uncle can use the letter to enforce the mother's promise to repay him. *There is no requirement that the writing be addressed to a particular person. (Choice A) The amount of the loan is not relevant in determining whether a suretyship is subject to the statute of frauds. The dollar amount is only relevant in determining whether a contract for the sale of goods ($500 or more) is subject to the UCC statute of frauds. (Choice C) Although the mother's promise was oral, it was evidenced by a signed writing (ie, her letter to her son) that satisfied the statute of frauds. (Choice D) The son's oral promise to repay the loan is enforceable without a writing because this type of contract is not subject to the statute of frauds. In contrast, the mother's suretyship agreement is subject to the statute.\n\nEducational objective: Under the statute of frauds, a suretyship is enforceable if it is (1) in a writing—ie, physical or electronic document(s)—containing a reasonable basis to believe that a contract was formed and (2) signed via a handwritten signature or other identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 112 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirement of suretyship).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 116 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (main-purpose rule).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 136 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (time of memorandum).","educationalObjective":"Under the statute of frauds, a suretyship is enforceable if it is (1) in a writing—ie, physical or electronic document(s)—containing a reasonable basis to believe that a contract was formed and (2) signed via a handwritten signature or other identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 112 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirement of suretyship).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 116 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (main-purpose rule).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 136 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (time of memorandum).","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, a suretyship is enforceable if it is (1) in a writing—ie, physical or electronic document(s)—containing a reasonable basis to believe that a contract was formed and (2) signed via a handwritten signature or other identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Will the mother's statute-of-frauds defense likely be successful?","rule":"Under the statute of frauds, a suretyship is enforceable if it is (1) in a writing—ie, physical or electronic document(s)—containing a reasonable basis to believe that a contract was formed and (2) signed via a handwritten signature or other identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"A suretyship is a promise to assume responsibility for a duty incurred by another (eg, the mother's promise to repay the loan if her son does not). Suretyships are generally subject to the statute of frauds and therefore are only enforceable if they are:\n• in a writing—ie, physical or electronic document(s) such as a receipt, check, or emails—containing a reasonable basis to believe that a contract was formed and\n• signed through a handwritten signature or other identifying mark by the party against whom enforcement is sought.* Here, the mother's letter satisfied the writing requirement because it evidenced her promise to repay the loan to the uncle. It also satisfied the signature requirement because her \"/s/ Mother\" notation on the letter identified her as the sender. As a result, the mother's statute-of-frauds defense will likely fail, and the uncle can use the letter to enforce the mother's promise to repay him. *There is no requirement that the writing be addressed to a particular person. (Choice A) The amount of the loan is not relevant in determining whether a suretyship is subject to the statute of frauds. The dollar amount is only relevant in determining whether a contract for the sale of goods ($500 or more) is subject to the UCC statute of frauds. (Choice C) Although the mother's promise was oral, it was evidenced by a signed writing (ie, her letter to her son) that satisfied the statute of frauds. (Choice D) The son's oral promise to repay the loan is enforceable without a writing because this type of contract is not subject to the statute of frauds. In contrast, the mother's suretyship agreement is subject to the statute.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the mother's letter satisfies the statute-of-frauds requirement."},"questionZh":"一位侄子陷入财务困境，急需 4,000 美元，于是向叔叔借了 4,000 美元。叔叔表示，只有侄子的母亲“担保”贷款，他才会把钱借给侄子。在侄子的建议下，叔叔随后给侄子的母亲打电话，告诉她贷款的事情，并询问她是否愿意担保。她回答说：“是的。借给我儿子4000美元，如果他不借我就还。”然后叔叔借给侄子 4,000 美元，侄子口头同意在六周内偿还这笔钱。第二天，侄子的母亲写信给他，并在信的结尾写道：“儿子，我很高兴帮你一个忙，向你叔叔保证，如果你不偿还的话，我将偿还你为期六周的 4,000 美元贷款。/s/ 母亲。”侄子和他的母亲都没有偿还到期的贷款，叔叔起诉了母亲违约。在那次诉讼中，母亲提出了欺诈法作为她唯一的辩护。母亲的欺诈法辩护可能会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为贷款金额不到 5,000 美元。","B":"不，因为母亲的信符合欺诈法的要求。","C":"是的，因为母亲对叔叔的承诺是口头的。","D":"是的，因为侄子对叔叔的承诺是口头的。"},"explanationZh":"担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺（例如，如果儿子不偿还贷款，母亲承诺偿还贷款）。担保通常受欺诈法的约束，因此只有在以下情况下才可执行：\n• 以书面形式——即物理或电子文件，例如收据、支票或电子邮件——包含相信合同已成立的合理依据，并且\n• 由被请求执行方以手写签名或其他识别标记签署。*在这里，母亲的信满足了书面要求，因为它证明了她向叔叔偿还贷款的承诺。它还满足了签名要求，因为信上的“/s/ Mother”符号表明她是发件人。结果，母亲的欺诈法辩护很可能会失败，而叔叔可以利用这封信来执行母亲偿还他的承诺。 *不要求文章必须写给特定的人。 （选择 A）贷款金额与确定担保是否受欺诈法的约束无关。美元金额仅在确定商品销售合同（500 美元或以上）是否受 UCC 欺诈法管辖时相关。 （选项C） 虽然母亲的承诺是口头的，但它是由符合欺诈法的签名书面文件（即她给儿子的信）证明的。 （选项D）儿子口头承诺偿还贷款无需书面即可强制执行，因为此类合同不受欺诈法的约束。相比之下，母亲的担保协议则受法律管辖。\n\n教育目标：根据反欺诈法，如果保证是 (1) 以书面形式（即物理或电子文件）形式提供的，且包含相信合同已成立的合理依据，并且 (2) 由寻求执行的一方通过手写签名或其他识别标记签署，则该保证是可执行的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 112（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（担保要求）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 116（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（主要目的规则）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 136（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（备忘录时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据反欺诈法，如果保证是（1）以书面形式（即物理或电子文件）形式提供的，且包含相信合同已成立的合理依据，并且（2）由寻求强制执行的一方通过手写签名或其他识别标记签署，则该保证是可执行的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"母亲的欺诈法辩护可能会成功吗？","rule":"根据反欺诈法，如果保证是（1）以书面形式（即物理或电子文件）形式提供的，且包含相信合同已成立的合理依据，并且（2）由寻求强制执行的一方通过手写签名或其他识别标记签署，则该保证是可执行的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"担保是对他人承担的义务承担责任的承诺（例如，如果儿子不偿还贷款，母亲承诺偿还贷款）。担保通常受欺诈法的约束，因此只有在以下情况下才可执行：\n• 以书面形式——即物理或电子文件，例如收据、支票或电子邮件——包含相信合同已成立的合理依据，并且\n• 由被请求执行方以手写签名或其他识别标记签署。*在这里，母亲的信满足了书面要求，因为它证明了她向叔叔偿还贷款的承诺。它还满足了签名要求，因为信上的“/s/ Mother”符号表明她是发件人。结果，母亲的欺诈法辩护很可能会失败，而叔叔可以利用这封信来执行母亲偿还他的承诺。 *不要求文章必须写给特定的人。 （选择 A）贷款金额与确定担保是否受欺诈法的约束无关。美元金额仅在确定商品销售合同（500 美元或以上）是否受 UCC 欺诈法管辖时相关。 （选项C） 虽然母亲的承诺是口头的，但它是由符合欺诈法的签名书面文件（即她给儿子的信）证明的。 （选项D）儿子口头承诺偿还贷款无需书面即可强制执行，因为此类合同不受欺诈法的约束。相比之下，母亲的担保协议则受法律管辖。\n\n教育目标：根据反欺诈法，如果保证是 (1) 以书面形式（即物理或电子文件）形式提供的，且包含相信合同已成立的合理依据，并且 (2) 由寻求执行的一方通过手写签名或其他识别标记签署，则该保证是可执行的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 112（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（担保要求）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 116（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（主要目的规则）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 136（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（备忘录时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为母亲的信符合欺诈法的要求。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":78,"sourceQuestionPage":229,"sourceQuestionPages":[229],"sourceExplanationPages":[230,231],"sourceAnswerPages":[230,231],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-079","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A contractor was supervising a crew of subcontractors during a house renovation. The contractor saw one of the subcontractors, a carpenter, steal a watch from the house. The contractor said nothing about the theft, and the crew finished the work and left. Several weeks later, the contractor was hired to renovate another house. The contractor called the carpenter and said that he would need the carpenter to do this job for 25% less than his previous rate. The reduced rate reflected the market value of the work, but the carpenter refused. The contractor then told the carpenter that he had seen the carpenter steal the watch and that he would notify the police if the carpenter did not agree to the contractor's terms. The carpenter agreed to do the work for the lower rate. The carpenter never showed up to perform any work at the second house. If the contractor sues the carpenter for breach of contract, will the contractor be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contractor did not notify the police immediately after he saw the carpenter steal the watch.","B":"No, because the contractor threatened to notify the police that the carpenter had stolen the watch.","C":"Yes, because the contractor's proposed rate, which reflected the fair market value of the carpenter's work, was not unconscionable.","D":"Yes, because the contractor was legally authorized to notify the police that the carpenter had stolen the watch."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Improper threats\n\nAlways improper\n• Threat to commit crime or tort\n• Threat of criminal prosecution\n• Threat of civil litigation made in bad faith\n• Threat that breaches contractual duty of good faith & fair dealing Improper if resulting\n• Threatened act would harm recipient & not terms are unfair significantly benefit threatening party\n• Threat's effectiveness significantly increased by threatening party's prior unfair dealing\n• Threatened act would otherwise constitute use of power for illegitimate ends Some threats made during contract negotiations are proper—eg, threats to walk away if an offer is rejected. But the following threats are always improper:\n• Threats to commit a crime or a tort\n• Threats of criminal prosecution, even if the person threatened committed the crime\n• Threats of civil litigation made in bad faith\n• Threats that breach the duty of good faith and fair dealing under an existing contract with the recipient A contract is voidable on duress grounds if either party's assent was induced by an improper threat that left the recipient with no reasonable alternative but to assent. Here, the contractor made an improper threat of criminal prosecution when he said that he would notify the police that the carpenter had stolen a watch. This induced the carpenter to accept the contractor's terms, as evidenced by the carpenter's initial rejection of the lower rate. And since the carpenter actually stole the watch, he had no reasonable alternative but to assent to avoid prosecution. Therefore, the contract is voidable due to duress, and the contractor is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) The contractor had no duty to notify the police that the carpenter stole the watch. Therefore, had the contractor refrained from making an improper threat, his failure to notify the police immediately would not prevent him from prevailing in a breach-of- contract suit. (Choice C) A contract may be unenforceable based on unconscionability if its terms were so unfair to one party that no reasonable person would have agreed to it (not seen\n\nhere). But a contract whose terms are not unconscionable may still be unenforceable on duress grounds. (Choice D) The contractor was legally authorized to notify the police about the theft. But this did not give the contractor the right to use the criminal justice system as leverage in his contract negotiation with the carpenter.\n\nEducational objective: A contract is voidable due to duress if either party's assent was induced by an improper threat (eg, threat of criminal prosecution) that left the recipient with no reasonable alternative but to assent.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 175 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when duress by threat makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 176 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (improper threats).","educationalObjective":"A contract is voidable due to duress if either party's assent was induced by an improper threat (eg, threat of criminal prosecution) that left the recipient with no reasonable alternative but to assent.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 175 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when duress by threat makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 176 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (improper threats).","rule":"A contract is voidable due to duress if either party's assent was induced by an improper threat (eg, threat of criminal prosecution) that left the recipient with no reasonable alternative but to assent.","irac":{"issue":"If the contractor sues the carpenter for breach of contract, will the contractor be likely to prevail?","rule":"A contract is voidable due to duress if either party's assent was induced by an improper threat (eg, threat of criminal prosecution) that left the recipient with no reasonable alternative but to assent.","analysis":"Improper threats\n\nAlways improper\n• Threat to commit crime or tort\n• Threat of criminal prosecution\n• Threat of civil litigation made in bad faith\n• Threat that breaches contractual duty of good faith & fair dealing Improper if resulting\n• Threatened act would harm recipient & not terms are unfair significantly benefit threatening party\n• Threat's effectiveness significantly increased by threatening party's prior unfair dealing\n• Threatened act would otherwise constitute use of power for illegitimate ends Some threats made during contract negotiations are proper—eg, threats to walk away if an offer is rejected. But the following threats are always improper:\n• Threats to commit a crime or a tort\n• Threats of criminal prosecution, even if the person threatened committed the crime\n• Threats of civil litigation made in bad faith\n• Threats that breach the duty of good faith and fair dealing under an existing contract with the recipient A contract is voidable on duress grounds if either party's assent was induced by an improper threat that left the recipient with no reasonable alternative but to assent. Here, the contractor made an improper threat of criminal prosecution when he said that he would notify the police that the carpenter had stolen a watch. This induced the carpenter to accept the contractor's terms, as evidenced by the carpenter's initial rejection of the lower rate. And since the carpenter actually stole the watch, he had no reasonable alternative but to assent to avoid prosecution. Therefore, the contract is voidable due to duress, and the contractor is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) The contractor had no duty to notify the police that the carpenter stole the watch. Therefore, had the contractor refrained from making an improper threat, his failure to notify the police immediately would not prevent him from prevailing in a breach-of- contract suit. (Choice C) A contract may be unenforceable based on unconscionability if its terms were so unfair to one party that no reasonable person would have agreed to it (not seen\n\nhere). But a contract whose terms are not unconscionable may still be unenforceable on duress grounds. (Choice D) The contractor was legally authorized to notify the police about the theft. But this did not give the contractor the right to use the criminal justice system as leverage in his contract negotiation with the carpenter.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the contractor threatened to notify the police that the carpenter had stolen the watch."},"questionZh":"一名承包商在房屋装修期间监督一群分包商。承包商看到其中一个分包商，一名木匠，从房子里偷了一块手表。承包商对盗窃事件只字不提，工作人员完成工作后离开。几周后，承包商受聘翻新另一所房子。承包商打电话给木匠，说他需要木匠来做这项工作，价格比以前低 25%。降低的税率反映了作品的市场价值，但木匠拒绝了。承包商随后告诉木匠，他看到木匠偷了手表，如果木匠不同意承包商的条件，他会通知警方。木匠同意以较低的价格完成这项工作。木匠从未到第二所房子做任何工作。如果承包商起诉木匠违约，承包商胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为承包商看到木匠偷了手表后并没有立即通知警方。","B":"不，因为承包商威胁要通知警察木匠偷了手表。","C":"是的，因为承包商提出的费率反映了木匠作品的公平市场价值，这并非不合理。","D":"是的，因为承包商有权通知警方木匠偷了手表。"},"explanationZh":"不当威胁\n\n总是不合适\n• 威胁犯罪或侵权\n• 刑事起诉的威胁\n• 恶意提起民事诉讼的威胁\n• 威胁违反诚信和公平交易的合同义务 如果导致不当行为\n• 受威胁的行为会损害接收者的利益，而不是条款不公平，从而使威胁方受益匪浅\n• 通过威胁方先前的不公平交易，威胁的有效性显着提高\n• 威胁行为否则将构成利用权力达到非法目的 在合同谈判期间提出的一些威胁是适当的，例如，威胁如果报价被拒绝就走开。但以下威胁总是不恰当的：\n• 威胁实施犯罪或侵权行为\n• 威胁提起刑事诉讼，即使受威胁的人犯了罪\n• 恶意提起民事诉讼的威胁\n• 违反与接收方现有合同项下的诚信和公平交易义务的威胁 如果任何一方的同意是由于不当威胁而导致接收方除了同意而别无其他合理选择，则合同可因胁迫而无效。在此案中，承包商表示将通知警方木匠偷了一块手表，从而做出了刑事起诉的不当威胁。这促使木匠接受了承包商的条款，木匠最初拒绝较低的费率就证明了这一点。由于木匠实际上偷了手表，他别无选择，只能同意以避免起诉。因此，合同因胁迫而无效，承包商胜诉的可能性不大。 （选项A） 承包商没有义务通知警方木匠偷了手表。因此，如果承包商没有做出不当威胁，他没有立即通知警方，并不妨碍他在违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 C）如果合同的条款对一方如此不公平以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意，则合同可能会因不合理而无法执行（未见\n\n在这里）。但条款并非不合理的合同仍可能因胁迫而无法执行。 （选项 D） 承包商有合法授权向警方通报盗窃事件。但这并没有赋予承包商在与木匠的合同谈判中使用刑事司法系统作为杠杆的权利。\n\n教育目标：如果任何一方的同意是由不当威胁（例如，刑事起诉的威胁）诱导而导致接收者除了同意之外别无合理选择，则合同因胁迫而无效。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 175（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当威胁胁迫导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 176（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不当威胁）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果任何一方的同意是由于不当威胁（例如刑事起诉的威胁）而导致接收者除了同意而别无其他合理选择，则合同因胁迫而无效。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果承包商起诉木匠违约，承包商胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果任何一方的同意是由于不当威胁（例如刑事起诉的威胁）而导致接收者除了同意而别无其他合理选择，则合同因胁迫而无效。","analysis":"不当威胁\n\n总是不合适\n• 威胁犯罪或侵权\n• 刑事起诉的威胁\n• 恶意提起民事诉讼的威胁\n• 威胁违反诚信和公平交易的合同义务 如果导致不当行为\n• 受威胁的行为会损害接收者的利益，而不是条款不公平，从而使威胁方受益匪浅\n• 通过威胁方先前的不公平交易，威胁的有效性显着提高\n• 威胁行为否则将构成利用权力达到非法目的 在合同谈判期间提出的一些威胁是适当的，例如，威胁如果报价被拒绝就走开。但以下威胁总是不恰当的：\n• 威胁实施犯罪或侵权行为\n• 威胁提起刑事诉讼，即使受威胁的人犯了罪\n• 恶意提起民事诉讼的威胁\n• 违反与接收方现有合同项下的诚信和公平交易义务的威胁 如果任何一方的同意是由于不当威胁而导致接收方除了同意而别无其他合理选择，则合同可因胁迫而无效。在此案中，承包商表示将通知警方木匠偷了一块手表，从而做出了刑事起诉的不当威胁。这促使木匠接受了承包商的条款，木匠最初拒绝较低的费率就证明了这一点。由于木匠实际上偷了手表，他别无选择，只能同意以避免起诉。因此，合同因胁迫而无效，承包商胜诉的可能性不大。 （选项A） 承包商没有义务通知警方木匠偷了手表。因此，如果承包商没有做出不当威胁，他没有立即通知警方，并不妨碍他在违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 C）如果合同的条款对一方如此不公平以至于任何有理智的人都不会同意，则合同可能会因不合理而无法执行（未见\n\n在这里）。但条款并非不合理的合同仍可能因胁迫而无法执行。 （选项 D） 承包商有合法授权向警方通报盗窃事件。但这并没有赋予承包商在与木匠的合同谈判中使用刑事司法系统作为杠杆的权利。\n\n教育目标：如果任何一方的同意是由不当威胁（例如，刑事起诉的威胁）诱导而导致接收者除了同意之外别无合理选择，则合同因胁迫而无效。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 175（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当威胁胁迫导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 176（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不当威胁）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为承包商威胁要通知警察木匠偷了手表。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"fair market value","ipa":"/fˈɛɹ mˈɑɹkət vˈælju/","syllables":"fair mar·ket value","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"公平市场价值：自愿买卖双方在正常市场中的合理交易价值","contextualMeaning":"公平市场价值：自愿买卖双方在正常市场中的合理交易价值","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Yes, because the contractor's proposed rate, which reflected the fair market value of the carpenter's work, was not unconscionable.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":79,"sourceQuestionPage":232,"sourceQuestionPages":[232],"sourceExplanationPages":[233,234],"sourceAnswerPages":[233,234],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-080","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A lawyer wanted to retire from practicing law and enter the bakery business. The lawyer contacted the owner of a successful bakery that she wished to purchase. After several days of negotiations, the lawyer made a written offer to purchase the bakery for $500,000. The bakery owner reviewed the offer but was confused about who would be responsible for the outstanding mortgage on the bakery. The lawyer told her that one of the contract's provisions required the lawyer to assume the mortgage, but the lawyer knew that the provision did not actually require her do to so. The lawyer and the owner signed the offer, which purported to be a fully integrated writing. Thereafter, the lawyer refused to assume the mortgage. If the bakery owner sues the lawyer seeking reformation of the contract, will she be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a contract is void due to a fraudulent misrepresentation that induced a manifestation of assent.","B":"No, because prior oral assertions are inadmissible under the parol evidence rule when there is a fully integrated writing.","C":"Yes, because a contract can be reformed due to a fraudulent misrepresentation about the effect of the writing.","D":"Yes, because prior oral assertions are admissible to interpret an ambiguous term in a fully integrated writing."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Effect of misrepresentation\n\nNo contract\n• Misrepresentation as to character/essential terms of proposed formed contract\n• Induced appearance of assent to contract\n• Adversely affected party neither knew nor had reasonable opportunity to know of actual character/essential terms Contract\n• Fraudulent or material misrepresentation voidable\n• Induced assent to contract\n• Adversely affected party justifiably relied on misrepresentation Reformation\n• Fraudulent misrepresentation as to contents/effect of written available* contract\n• Induced assent to contract\n• Adversely affected party justifiably relied on misrepresentation *Adversely affected party can choose to avoid contract instead.\n\nWhere a party misrepresents the contents or effect of a writing that embodies all or part of a contract, a court can reform (ie, rewrite) the writing to comport with what had been represented if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent—ie, made knowingly or recklessly, with intent to induce the other party's assent\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the writing and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. However, a court will not do so to the extent that the rights of third parties (eg, good-faith purchasers for value) would be unfairly affected (not seen here). Here, the lawyer fraudulently misrepresented the legal effect of a contract provision when she said that it required her to assume the outstanding mortgage on the bakery even though she knew that it did not. This misrepresentation likely induced the bakery owner's assent to the writing, and since it was not obviously false or known by her to be false, she justifiably relied on it. Therefore, the bakery owner will likely prevail in her suit. (Choice A) A contract may be void if a misrepresentation induced conduct that only appeared to be a manifestation of assent—eg, if an actor unknowingly signed a contract after a fan asked for an autograph. But here, the bakery owner's manifestation of assent was genuine, even though she did not know the true effect of the mortgage provision. (Choices B & D) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements that modify or contradict the terms of an integrated\n\nwriting. However, there is an exception for evidence used to prove a fraudulent misrepresentation (or other ground) to justify reformation. There also is an exception for evidence used to interpret an ambiguous term, but none is at issue here.\n\nEducational objective: A misrepresentation about the contents or effect of a writing is grounds for reformation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party's reliance was justifiable.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 166 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining when a misrepresentation as to a writing justifies reformation).","educationalObjective":"A misrepresentation about the contents or effect of a writing is grounds for reformation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party's reliance was justifiable.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 166 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining when a misrepresentation as to a writing justifies reformation).","rule":"A misrepresentation about the contents or effect of a writing is grounds for reformation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party's reliance was justifiable.","irac":{"issue":"If the bakery owner sues the lawyer seeking reformation of the contract, will she be likely to prevail?","rule":"A misrepresentation about the contents or effect of a writing is grounds for reformation if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party's reliance was justifiable.","analysis":"Effect of misrepresentation\n\nNo contract\n• Misrepresentation as to character/essential terms of proposed formed contract\n• Induced appearance of assent to contract\n• Adversely affected party neither knew nor had reasonable opportunity to know of actual character/essential terms Contract\n• Fraudulent or material misrepresentation voidable\n• Induced assent to contract\n• Adversely affected party justifiably relied on misrepresentation Reformation\n• Fraudulent misrepresentation as to contents/effect of written available* contract\n• Induced assent to contract\n• Adversely affected party justifiably relied on misrepresentation *Adversely affected party can choose to avoid contract instead.\n\nWhere a party misrepresents the contents or effect of a writing that embodies all or part of a contract, a court can reform (ie, rewrite) the writing to comport with what had been represented if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent—ie, made knowingly or recklessly, with intent to induce the other party's assent\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the writing and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. However, a court will not do so to the extent that the rights of third parties (eg, good-faith purchasers for value) would be unfairly affected (not seen here). Here, the lawyer fraudulently misrepresented the legal effect of a contract provision when she said that it required her to assume the outstanding mortgage on the bakery even though she knew that it did not. This misrepresentation likely induced the bakery owner's assent to the writing, and since it was not obviously false or known by her to be false, she justifiably relied on it. Therefore, the bakery owner will likely prevail in her suit. (Choice A) A contract may be void if a misrepresentation induced conduct that only appeared to be a manifestation of assent—eg, if an actor unknowingly signed a contract after a fan asked for an autograph. But here, the bakery owner's manifestation of assent was genuine, even though she did not know the true effect of the mortgage provision. (Choices B & D) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements that modify or contradict the terms of an integrated\n\nwriting. However, there is an exception for evidence used to prove a fraudulent misrepresentation (or other ground) to justify reformation. There also is an exception for evidence used to interpret an ambiguous term, but none is at issue here.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because a contract can be reformed due to a fraudulent misrepresentation about the effect of the writing."},"questionZh":"一位律师想从律师行业退休并进入面包店行业。律师联系了一家成功的面包店的老板，她想购买这家面包店。经过几天的谈判，律师提出了以 50 万美元购买这家面包店的书面报价。面包店老板审查了报价，但对于谁将负责面包店的未偿抵押贷款感到困惑。律师告诉她，合同中的一项条款要求律师承担抵押贷款，但律师知道该条款实际上并没有要求她这样做。律师和业主签署了这份报价书，据称这是一份完全整合的书面文件。此后，律师拒绝承担抵押贷款。如果面包店老板起诉律师寻求修改合同，她有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为由于欺诈性失实陈述导致同意的表现，合同无效。","B":"不可以，因为根据口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），当存在完全完整的书面材料时，先前的口头主张是不可接受的。","C":"是的，因为合同可以因对书面效果的欺诈性失实陈述而被修改。","D":"是的，因为之前的口头主张可以用来解释完全整合的写作中模棱两可的术语。"},"explanationZh":"虚假陈述的影响\n\n无合同\n• 对拟定合同的特征/基本条款的虚假陈述\n• 诱导同意合同\n• 受到不利影响的一方既不知道也没有合理的机会了解合同的实际特征/基本条款\n• 欺诈或重大失实陈述可撤销\n• 诱导同意合同\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述改革\n• 对书面可用*合同的内容/效果进行欺诈性失实陈述\n• 诱导同意合同\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述 *受到不利影响的一方可以选择避免签订合同。\n\n如果一方歪曲了体现合同全部或部分内容的书面内容或效果，法院可以修改（即重写）该书面内容，使其与所表述的内容相符，如果：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性——即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意\n• 歪曲事实导致同意所写内容以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。然而，如果第三方（例如善意购买者的价值）的权利受到不公平的影响（此处未见），法院不会这样做。在这里，律师欺骗性地歪曲了合同条款的法律效力，她说合同条款要求她承担面包店的未偿还抵押贷款，尽管她知道合同条款没有这样做。这种失实陈述很可能促使面包店老板同意该文字，并且由于该文字并非明显错误或她不知道是错误的，因此她有理由相信它。因此，面包店老板的诉讼很可能胜诉。 （选择A）如果虚假陈述导致的行为只是表面上的同意，例如，如果演员在粉丝要求签名后不知不觉地签署了合同。但在这里，面包店老板的同意表现是真诚的，尽管她不知道抵押贷款条款的真实效果。 （选择 B 和 D）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，这些协议修改或与综合协议的条款相矛盾。\n\n写作。然而，用于证明欺诈性虚假陈述（或其他理由）以证明改革合理性的证据有一个例外。用于解释不明确术语的证据也有例外，但这里没有任何争议。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 虚假陈述具有欺诈性，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，并且 (3) 受到不利影响的一方的信赖是合理的，则对书面内容或效果的虚假陈述可以作为修改的理由。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 166（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了何时对书面陈述进行了修改）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对书面内容或效果的失实陈述可以作为修改的理由，如果 (1) 失实陈述是欺诈性的，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方的信赖是合理的。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果面包店老板起诉律师寻求修改合同，她有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"对书面内容或效果的失实陈述可以作为修改的理由，如果 (1) 失实陈述是欺诈性的，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方的信赖是合理的。","analysis":"虚假陈述的影响\n\n无合同\n• 对拟定合同的特征/基本条款的虚假陈述\n• 诱导同意合同\n• 受到不利影响的一方既不知道也没有合理的机会了解合同的实际特征/基本条款\n• 欺诈或重大失实陈述可撤销\n• 诱导同意合同\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述改革\n• 对书面可用*合同的内容/效果进行欺诈性失实陈述\n• 诱导同意合同\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述 *受到不利影响的一方可以选择避免签订合同。\n\n如果一方歪曲了体现合同全部或部分内容的书面内容或效果，法院可以修改（即重写）该书面内容，使其与所表述的内容相符，如果：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性——即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意\n• 歪曲事实导致同意所写内容以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。然而，如果第三方（例如善意购买者的价值）的权利受到不公平的影响（此处未见），法院不会这样做。在这里，律师欺骗性地歪曲了合同条款的法律效力，她说合同条款要求她承担面包店的未偿还抵押贷款，尽管她知道合同条款没有这样做。这种失实陈述很可能促使面包店老板同意该文字，并且由于该文字并非明显错误或她不知道是错误的，因此她有理由相信它。因此，面包店老板的诉讼很可能胜诉。 （选择A）如果虚假陈述导致的行为只是表面上的同意，例如，如果演员在粉丝要求签名后不知不觉地签署了合同。但在这里，面包店老板的同意表现是真诚的，尽管她不知道抵押贷款条款的真实效果。 （选择 B 和 D）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同期协议的证据，这些协议修改或与综合协议的条款相矛盾。\n\n写作。然而，用于证明欺诈性虚假陈述（或其他理由）以证明改革合理性的证据有一个例外。用于解释不明确术语的证据也有例外，但这里没有任何争议。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 虚假陈述具有欺诈性，(2) 它诱导了对合同的同意，并且 (3) 受到不利影响的一方的信赖是合理的，则对书面内容或效果的虚假陈述可以作为修改的理由。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 166（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了何时对书面陈述进行了修改）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为合同可以因对书面效果的欺诈性失实陈述而被修改。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":80,"sourceQuestionPage":235,"sourceQuestionPages":[235],"sourceExplanationPages":[236,237],"sourceAnswerPages":[236,237],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-081","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A woman prepared an accurate statement of her financial condition and submitted it with a loan application to a local bank with which she had done business for many years. Shortly thereafter, the woman's financial condition worsened significantly, but she failed to disclose this fact to the bank. Unaware of the woman's changed financial condition, the bank then agreed to lend money to the woman. The bank later learned of the woman's true financial condition. The bank refused to honor its promise to lend money to the woman, contending that the parties' contract was voidable at the bank's option because of the woman's misrepresentation. If the woman sues the bank for breach of contract, will the bank's misrepresentation defense likely succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman did not make any subsequent affirmative misrepresentations about her financial condition.","B":"No, because the woman's representations were accurate at the time she submitted the loan application to the bank.","C":"Yes, because the woman had an obligation to correct her previous representations about her financial condition in order to prevent them from being fraudulent.","D":"Yes, because the woman had an obligation to tell the bank about her changed financial condition due to the longstanding relationship of trust and confidence between them."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When disclosure is required to avoid misrepresentation\n\nPrevious assertion\n• Disclosure would prevent previous assertion from being a misrepresentation or fraudulent or material Mistake about basic\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about basic assumption assumption of contract AND\n\n• Failure to disclose would constitute lack of good faith & fair dealing Fiduciary\n• Other party is entitled to know fact because of relationship confidential/fiduciary relationship\n\nMistake about\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about writing contents or effect of writing that embodies agreement\n\nA misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when:\n• the misrepresentation is fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if, among other things, the party not disclosing the fact knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent. Here, the woman's representations were accurate when she submitted the loan application to the bank. However, she had an obligation to correct her previous representations to prevent them from being fraudulent (Choice B). The woman failed to do so, and because the bank was unaware of her financial condition, it justifiably agreed to lend money to the woman. Therefore, the bank's misrepresentation defense will likely succeed. (Choice A) The fact that the woman made no subsequent affirmative statements about her financial condition is irrelevant. Disclosure of her actual financial condition was still necessary to prevent her previous representations from being fraudulent. (Choice D) The relationship between the woman and the bank is not a confidential or fiduciary relationship (eg, attorney-client relationship) that would obligate her to disclose this information to the bank.\n\nEducational objective: Nondisclosure is equivalent to an assertion that a fact does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when nondisclosure is a misrepresentation).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Nondisclosure is equivalent to an assertion that a fact does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 161 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when nondisclosure is a misrepresentation).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when misrepresentation makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Nondisclosure is equivalent to an assertion that a fact does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.","irac":{"issue":"If the woman sues the bank for breach of contract, will the bank's misrepresentation defense likely succeed?","rule":"Nondisclosure is equivalent to an assertion that a fact does not exist if the party not disclosing the fact knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent.","analysis":"When disclosure is required to avoid misrepresentation\n\nPrevious assertion\n• Disclosure would prevent previous assertion from being a misrepresentation or fraudulent or material Mistake about basic\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about basic assumption assumption of contract AND\n\n• Failure to disclose would constitute lack of good faith & fair dealing Fiduciary\n• Other party is entitled to know fact because of relationship confidential/fiduciary relationship\n\nMistake about\n• Disclosure would correct other party's mistake about writing contents or effect of writing that embodies agreement\n\nA misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when:\n• the misrepresentation is fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. Nondisclosure of a known fact is tantamount to an assertion that it does not exist if, among other things, the party not disclosing the fact knows that disclosure is necessary to prevent a previous assertion from being fraudulent. Here, the woman's representations were accurate when she submitted the loan application to the bank. However, she had an obligation to correct her previous representations to prevent them from being fraudulent (Choice B). The woman failed to do so, and because the bank was unaware of her financial condition, it justifiably agreed to lend money to the woman. Therefore, the bank's misrepresentation defense will likely succeed. (Choice A) The fact that the woman made no subsequent affirmative statements about her financial condition is irrelevant. Disclosure of her actual financial condition was still necessary to prevent her previous representations from being fraudulent. (Choice D) The relationship between the woman and the bank is not a confidential or fiduciary relationship (eg, attorney-client relationship) that would obligate her to disclose this information to the bank.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the woman had an obligation to correct her previous representations about her financial condition in order to prevent them from being fraudulent."},"questionZh":"一名妇女准备了一份准确的财务状况报表，并将其与贷款申请一起提交给一家与她有多年业务往来的当地银行。此后不久，该女子的财务状况明显恶化，但她未能向银行透露这一事实。银行不知道该女子的财务状况发生了变化，于是同意借钱给该女子。银行后来了解到该女子的真实财务状况。该银行拒绝履行向该妇女借钱的承诺，称由于该妇女的虚假陈述，银行可以选择撤销双方的合同。如果这位女士起诉银行违约，银行的虚假陈述辩护可能会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该妇女随后没有对她的财务状况作出任何肯定性的虚假陈述。","B":"不会，因为该女士在向银行提交贷款申请时的陈述是准确的。","C":"是的，因为该妇女有义务纠正她之前关于其财务状况的陈述，以防止他们被欺诈。","D":"是的，因为由于她们之间长期的信任和信心关系，这位女士有义务告诉银行她的财务状况发生了变化。"},"explanationZh":"当需要披露以避免虚假陈述时\n\n先前的断言\n• 披露将防止先前的断言成为失实陈述、欺诈或重大错误。\n• 披露将纠正其他方关于合同基本假设的错误以及\n\n• 不披露将构成缺乏诚信和公平交易 受托人\n• 由于保密/信托关系，另一方有权了解事实\n\n关于错误\n• 披露将纠正对方对体现协议的书写内容或书写效果的错误\n\n失实陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，在以下情况下会使受到不利影响的一方无效合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。如果不披露该事实的一方知道有必要披露该事实以防止先前的断言具有欺诈性，那么不披露已知事实就相当于断言该事实不存在。在此，该女子向银行提交贷款申请时的陈述是准确的。然而，她有义务纠正她之前的陈述，以防止其被欺骗（选择B）。女子没有这么做，银行又因为不了解她的经济状况，也就理所应当地同意借钱给女子。因此，该银行的虚假陈述辩护很可能会成功。 （选项 A）该妇女随后没有对她的经济状况做出肯定的陈述这一事实是无关紧要的。为了防止她之前的陈述被欺诈，仍然有必要披露她的实际财务状况。 （选项 D） 该妇女与银行之间的关系不是保密或信托关系（例如律师与客户关系），因此她有义务向银行披露此信息。\n\n教育目标：如果不披露事实的一方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性而有必要进行披露，那么不披露就相当于断言该事实不存在。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 161（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当不披露属于失实陈述时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果未披露事实的一方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性而有必要进行披露，那么不披露就相当于断言该事实不存在。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果这位女士起诉银行违约，银行的虚假陈述辩护可能会成功吗？","rule":"如果未披露事实的一方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性而有必要进行披露，那么不披露就相当于断言该事实不存在。","analysis":"当需要披露以避免虚假陈述时\n\n先前的断言\n• 披露将防止先前的断言成为失实陈述、欺诈或重大错误。\n• 披露将纠正其他方关于合同基本假设的错误以及\n\n• 不披露将构成缺乏诚信和公平交易 受托人\n• 由于保密/信托关系，另一方有权了解事实\n\n关于错误\n• 披露将纠正对方对体现协议的书写内容或书写效果的错误\n\n失实陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，在以下情况下会使受到不利影响的一方无效合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，意图诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。如果不披露该事实的一方知道有必要披露该事实以防止先前的断言具有欺诈性，那么不披露已知事实就相当于断言该事实不存在。在此，该女子向银行提交贷款申请时的陈述是准确的。然而，她有义务纠正她之前的陈述，以防止其被欺骗（选择B）。女子没有这么做，银行又因为不了解她的经济状况，也就理所应当地同意借钱给女子。因此，该银行的虚假陈述辩护很可能会成功。 （选项 A）该妇女随后没有对她的经济状况做出肯定的陈述这一事实是无关紧要的。为了防止她之前的陈述被欺诈，仍然有必要披露她的实际财务状况。 （选项 D） 该妇女与银行之间的关系不是保密或信托关系（例如律师与客户关系），因此她有义务向银行披露此信息。\n\n教育目标：如果不披露事实的一方知道为了防止先前的断言具有欺诈性而有必要进行披露，那么不披露就相当于断言该事实不存在。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 161（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当不披露属于失实陈述时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当虚假陈述导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为该妇女有义务纠正她之前关于其财务状况的陈述，以防止他们被欺诈。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":81,"sourceQuestionPage":238,"sourceQuestionPages":[238],"sourceExplanationPages":[239,240],"sourceAnswerPages":[239,240],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-082","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A professional race car driver decided to retire and sell his drag racing car. An amateur driver approached him about buying the car. The amateur driver explained that she needed a car that could complete a quarter-mile race in less than nine seconds, and the professional driver boasted that his car could do so in eight to nine seconds. Unbeknownst to the professional driver, there was a calibration error in his timing equipment, and the car had never completed a quarter-mile race in less than 10 seconds. The two drivers settled on a price and signed a fully integrated, written contract. The amateur driver paid the sales price, took delivery of the car, and entered the next drag race. The car was unable to complete a quarter-mile race in less than 10 seconds, so the amateur driver contacted the professional driver and demanded her money back. Is the professional driver obligated to return the amateur driver's money?","choices":{"A":"No, because the professional driver's assertion was an unintentional misrepresentation.","B":"No, because the sales contract was memorialized in a fully integrated writing.","C":"Yes, because the professional driver's assertion was a fraudulent misrepresentation.","D":"Yes, because the professional driver's assertion was a material misrepresentation."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact made during contract negotiations. This renders the contract voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract (presumed for material misrepresentations) and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. The justifiable-reliance element is essentially always met unless the misrepresentation was obviously false or known by the adversely affected party to be false. Here, the professional driver falsely stated that his car could complete a quarter-mile race in eight to nine seconds. This misrepresentation was material and presumably induced the amateur driver to enter the contract since she had told the professional driver that she was looking for a car with that capability. The amateur driver's reliance on the misrepresentation was justifiable since it was not obviously false or known by her to be false. Therefore, the contract is voidable, and the professional driver is obligated to return the amateur driver's money. (Choices A & C) A misrepresentation is fraudulent when it is made knowingly or recklessly, with intent to induce assent to the contract. Here, the professional driver's misrepresentation was nonfraudulent since he had timed his car and knew nothing about the calibration error in his timing equipment. But even an unintentional, nonfraudulent misrepresentation may be grounds to avoid a contract when, as here, the misrepresentation was material. (Choice B) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But such evidence is excepted from this rule and is admissible when it is offered to prove defenses to enforcement (eg, misrepresentation—as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A misrepresentation renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 159, 162, 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defining material misrepresentation and explaining when it makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"A misrepresentation renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 159, 162, 164 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defining material misrepresentation and explaining when it makes a contract voidable).","rule":"A misrepresentation renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.","irac":{"issue":"Is the professional driver obligated to return the amateur driver's money?","rule":"A misrepresentation renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party if (1) the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material, (2) it induced assent to the contract, and (3) the adversely affected party justifiably relied on it.","analysis":"A misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact made during contract negotiations. This renders the contract voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent or material\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract (presumed for material misrepresentations) and\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation. The justifiable-reliance element is essentially always met unless the misrepresentation was obviously false or known by the adversely affected party to be false. Here, the professional driver falsely stated that his car could complete a quarter-mile race in eight to nine seconds. This misrepresentation was material and presumably induced the amateur driver to enter the contract since she had told the professional driver that she was looking for a car with that capability. The amateur driver's reliance on the misrepresentation was justifiable since it was not obviously false or known by her to be false. Therefore, the contract is voidable, and the professional driver is obligated to return the amateur driver's money. (Choices A & C) A misrepresentation is fraudulent when it is made knowingly or recklessly, with intent to induce assent to the contract. Here, the professional driver's misrepresentation was nonfraudulent since he had timed his car and knew nothing about the calibration error in his timing equipment. But even an unintentional, nonfraudulent misrepresentation may be grounds to avoid a contract when, as here, the misrepresentation was material. (Choice B) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to modify or contradict the terms of an integrated writing. But such evidence is excepted from this rule and is admissible when it is offered to prove defenses to enforcement (eg, misrepresentation—as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the professional driver's assertion was a material misrepresentation."},"questionZh":"一位职业赛车手决定退休并卖掉他的飙车。一位业余司机向他提出购买汽车的事宜。业余车手解释说，她需要一辆能够在九秒内完成四分之一英里比赛的汽车，而专业车手则夸口说他的车可以在八到九秒内完成。这位职业车手并不知道，他的计时设备存在校准错误，赛车从未在 10 秒内完成四分之一英里的比赛。两名司机敲定了价格并签署了一份完整的书面合同。业余车手支付了销售价格，提了车，并进入了下一场直线加速赛。由于该车无法在 10 秒内完成四分之一英里的比赛，因此业余车手联系了专业车手并要求退款。职业司机有义务退还业余司机的钱吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为职业司机的说法是无意的误传。","B":"不，因为销售合同是以完全完整的书面形式记录的。","C":"是的，因为职业司机的说法是欺诈性的失实陈述。","D":"是的，因为职业司机的说法是重大失实陈述。"},"explanationZh":"失实陈述是指在合同谈判期间对事实作出的不真实陈述。如果出现以下情况，则受不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性或重大\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同（推定为重大虚假陈述）以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。基本上总是满足合理信赖要素，除非失实陈述明显错误或被不利影响方知道是错误的。在这里，职业车手错误地声称他的汽车可以在八到九秒内完成四分之一英里的比赛。这种失实陈述很重要，可能诱使业余司机签订合同，因为她告诉专业司机她正在寻找一辆具有这种能力的汽车。业余司机对虚假陈述的依赖是合理的，因为该虚假陈述并非明显虚假或她不知道是虚假的。因此，合同是可以撤销的，职业车手有义务退还业余车手的钱。 （选项 A 和 C）如果故意或罔顾后果地做出虚假陈述，意图诱导同意合同，则该虚假陈述属于欺诈。在这里，专业司机的失实陈述并非欺诈，因为他对自己的汽车进行了计时，并且对计时设备中的校准误差一无所知。但即使是无意的、非欺诈性的虚假陈述也可能成为宣告合同无效的理由，因为此处的虚假陈述是重大的。 （选择B）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同期修改或反驳综合写作条款的协议的证据。但此类证据不属于本规则的范围，并且当其用于证明对执行的抗辩时（例如，虚假陈述——如此处所示），则可以接受。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 失实陈述具有欺诈性或重大，(2) 诱导同意合同，并且 (3) 受不利影响方有正当理由依赖该失实陈述，则失实陈述会导致受不利影响方无效的合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 159、162、164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（定义重大失实陈述并解释何时使合同无效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果出现下列情况，失实陈述将使受到不利影响的一方宣告合同无效：(1) 失实陈述具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 其诱导同意合同；(3) 受到不利影响的一方有正当理由依赖该陈述。","iracZh":{"issue":"职业司机有义务退还业余司机的钱吗？","rule":"如果出现下列情况，失实陈述将使受到不利影响的一方宣告合同无效：(1) 失实陈述具有欺诈性或重大；(2) 其诱导同意合同；(3) 受到不利影响的一方有正当理由依赖该陈述。","analysis":"失实陈述是指在合同谈判期间对事实作出的不真实陈述。如果出现以下情况，则受不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性或重大\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同（推定为重大虚假陈述）以及\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。基本上总是满足合理信赖要素，除非失实陈述明显错误或被不利影响方知道是错误的。在这里，职业车手错误地声称他的汽车可以在八到九秒内完成四分之一英里的比赛。这种失实陈述很重要，可能诱使业余司机签订合同，因为她告诉专业司机她正在寻找一辆具有这种能力的汽车。业余司机对虚假陈述的依赖是合理的，因为该虚假陈述并非明显虚假或她不知道是虚假的。因此，合同是可以撤销的，职业车手有义务退还业余车手的钱。 （选项 A 和 C）如果故意或罔顾后果地做出虚假陈述，意图诱导同意合同，则该虚假陈述属于欺诈。在这里，专业司机的失实陈述并非欺诈，因为他对自己的汽车进行了计时，并且对计时设备中的校准误差一无所知。但即使是无意的、非欺诈性的虚假陈述也可能成为宣告合同无效的理由，因为此处的虚假陈述是重大的。 （选择B）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前或同期修改或反驳综合写作条款的协议的证据。但此类证据不属于本规则的范围，并且当其用于证明对执行的抗辩时（例如，虚假陈述——如此处所示），则可以接受。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 失实陈述具有欺诈性或重大，(2) 诱导同意合同，并且 (3) 受不利影响方有正当理由依赖该失实陈述，则失实陈述会导致受不利影响方无效的合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 159、162、164（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（定义重大失实陈述并解释何时使合同无效）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为职业司机的说法是重大失实陈述。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-dc67ad79e4c738f7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":82,"sourceQuestionPage":241,"sourceQuestionPages":[241],"sourceExplanationPages":[242,243],"sourceAnswerPages":[242,243],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-083","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"A manufacturer of poultry-feed supplements contracted to purchase methylmercaptopropanol (MMP), a key component of its top-selling supplement, from a chemical company. The chemical company agreed to manufacture the MMP and ship it by rail to the manufacturer. The chemical company subsequently contracted with a rail carrier to ship hundreds of products to and from the chemical company's manufacturing facilities. Among the products to be shipped was the MMP. The rail contract required the rail carrier to \"ensure quality service to [the chemical company]\" by being \"responsive to the special needs of [the chemical company] whenever possible.\" The rail carrier inexcusably failed to transport sufficient amounts of MMP to the manufacturer. The reduced supply of MMP forced the manufacturer to suffer a significant reduction in the production and inventory of its top-selling supplement. The manufacturer had to transport smaller amounts of the supplement by faster and more expensive methods to satisfy customer demand. The manufacturer sued the rail carrier to recover the increased freight costs that resulted from the carrier's breach of the rail contract. Will the manufacturer be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the manufacturer and the rail carrier were not in privity of contract.","B":"No, because the manufacturer received only an incidental benefit under the rail contract.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer had a vested interest in performance of the rail contract.","D":"Yes, because the parties intended for the rail contract to directly benefit the manufacturer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some benefit from the contract. There are two types of beneficiaries: intended and incidental. A beneficiary is intended rather than incidental, as the name suggests, if the contracting parties clearly intended for him/her to benefit from the contract. Courts glean intent from what the parties said in their contract, not what they allegedly meant. And only intended beneficiaries (not incidental ones) can sue to enforce the contract. Here, the manufacturer benefited from the rail contract since the MMP was among the products to be shipped pursuant to that contract. However, nothing in the contract shows that the parties clearly intended to benefit the manufacturer—eg, had they included a provision that the contract was made for the manufacturer's benefit or that the manufacturer had the right to enforce it. This means that the manufacturer was, at most, an incidental beneficiary of the contract (Choice D). Therefore, it likely will not prevail in its suit against the rail carrier. (Choice A) Traditionally, the doctrine of privity of contract prevented individuals from enforcing contracts to which they were not a party. But this doctrine has been eroded to allow intended beneficiaries to enforce contracts made for their benefit. Therefore, had the manufacturer been an intended beneficiary of the rail contract, a lack of privity would not have defeated its claim. (Choice C) An intended beneficiary's rights vest when the beneficiary (1) reasonably and detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) assents to the contract at a party's request, or (3) sues to enforce the contract.* But here, the manufacturer was an incidental beneficiary, if any, of the rail contract with no rights to vest. Therefore, neither the manufacturer's suit to enforce the contract nor its reliance on the rail company's promises is relevant. *Once the beneficiary's rights have vested, the contracting parties are bound to perform the contract. Any efforts to terminate or modify the beneficiary's rights after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees.\n\nEducational objective: A third-party beneficiary is intended (as opposed to incidental) if the contracting parties clearly intended for him/her to benefit from the contract. Only intended beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (describing intended and incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"A third-party beneficiary is intended (as opposed to incidental) if the contracting parties clearly intended for him/her to benefit from the contract. Only intended beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (describing intended and incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"A third-party beneficiary is intended (as opposed to incidental) if the contracting parties clearly intended for him/her to benefit from the contract. Only intended beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Will the manufacturer be likely to prevail?","rule":"A third-party beneficiary is intended (as opposed to incidental) if the contracting parties clearly intended for him/her to benefit from the contract. Only intended beneficiaries can sue to enforce the contract.","analysis":"A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some benefit from the contract. There are two types of beneficiaries: intended and incidental. A beneficiary is intended rather than incidental, as the name suggests, if the contracting parties clearly intended for him/her to benefit from the contract. Courts glean intent from what the parties said in their contract, not what they allegedly meant. And only intended beneficiaries (not incidental ones) can sue to enforce the contract. Here, the manufacturer benefited from the rail contract since the MMP was among the products to be shipped pursuant to that contract. However, nothing in the contract shows that the parties clearly intended to benefit the manufacturer—eg, had they included a provision that the contract was made for the manufacturer's benefit or that the manufacturer had the right to enforce it. This means that the manufacturer was, at most, an incidental beneficiary of the contract (Choice D). Therefore, it likely will not prevail in its suit against the rail carrier. (Choice A) Traditionally, the doctrine of privity of contract prevented individuals from enforcing contracts to which they were not a party. But this doctrine has been eroded to allow intended beneficiaries to enforce contracts made for their benefit. Therefore, had the manufacturer been an intended beneficiary of the rail contract, a lack of privity would not have defeated its claim. (Choice C) An intended beneficiary's rights vest when the beneficiary (1) reasonably and detrimentally relies on the rights created, (2) assents to the contract at a party's request, or (3) sues to enforce the contract.* But here, the manufacturer was an incidental beneficiary, if any, of the rail contract with no rights to vest. Therefore, neither the manufacturer's suit to enforce the contract nor its reliance on the rail company's promises is relevant. *Once the beneficiary's rights have vested, the contracting parties are bound to perform the contract. Any efforts to terminate or modify the beneficiary's rights after vesting are void unless the beneficiary agrees.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the manufacturer received only an incidental benefit under the rail contract."},"questionZh":"一家家禽饲料补充剂制造商与一家化学公司签订合同，购买甲硫基丙醇 (MMP)，这是其最畅销补充剂的关键成分。该化学公司同意生产 MMP 并通过铁路运送给制造商。该化学公司随后与一家铁路承运商签订了合同，将数百种产品运送到该化学公司的制造工厂。即将发货的产品包括MMP。铁路合同要求铁路承运人“尽可能响应[化学公司]的特殊需求”，“确保为[化学公司]提供优质服务”。铁路运输公司无法向制造商运输足够数量的 MMP，这是不可原谅的。 MMP 供应的减少迫使制造商大幅减少其最畅销补充剂的产量和库存。制造商必须通过更快、更昂贵的方法运输少量的补充剂，以满足客户的需求。制造商起诉铁路承运人，要求赔偿因承运人违反铁路合同而增加的运费。制造商有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为制造商和铁路承运人不存在合同关系。","B":"不会，因为制造商仅根据铁路合同获得附带利益。","C":"是的，因为制造商在铁路合同的履行中拥有既得利益。","D":"是的，因为铁路合同双方的目的是让制造商直接受益。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得一些利益的合同非当事人。受益人有两种类型：预期受益人和偶然受益人。顾名思义，如果缔约方明确希望受益人从合同中受益，则受益人是有意的，而不是偶然的。法院从双方在合同中所说的内容中收集意图，而不是他们声称的意思。并且只有预期受益人（而非偶然受益人）才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。在这里，制造商从铁路合同中受益，因为 MMP 是根据该合同运输的产品之一。然而，合同中没有任何内容表明双方明确打算使制造商受益，例如，他们是否包含一项条款，表明合同是为了制造商的利益而制定的，或者制造商有权执行该合同。这意味着制造商至多是合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（选项D）。因此，它对铁路运输公司的诉讼很可能不会胜诉。 （选项 A） 传统上，合同相对性原则阻止个人执行他们不是当事人的合同。但这一原则已被削弱，允许预期受益人执行为其利益而制定的合同。因此，如果制造商是铁路合同的预期受益人，那么缺乏相互关系也不会导致其索赔失败。 （选项 C）当受益人 (1) 合理且有害地依赖所产生的权利，(2) 应一方请求同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的权利归属。*但在这里，制造商是铁路合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（如果有的话），无权归属。因此，无论是制造商要求执行合同的诉讼还是其对铁路公司承诺的依赖都无关紧要。 *一旦受益人的权利被授予，缔约方就有义务履行合同。除非受益人同意，任何在归属后终止或修改受益人权利的努力均无效。\n\n教育目标：如果缔约方明确希望第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中受益，则第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是有意的（而不是附带的）。只有预期受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（描述预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果缔约方明确希望第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中受益，则第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是有意的（而不是附带的）。只有预期受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"制造商有可能获胜吗？","rule":"如果缔约方明确希望第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中受益，则第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是有意的（而不是附带的）。只有预期受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是从合同中获得一些利益的合同非当事人。受益人有两种类型：预期受益人和偶然受益人。顾名思义，如果缔约方明确希望受益人从合同中受益，则受益人是有意的，而不是偶然的。法院从双方在合同中所说的内容中收集意图，而不是他们声称的意思。并且只有预期受益人（而非偶然受益人）才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。在这里，制造商从铁路合同中受益，因为 MMP 是根据该合同运输的产品之一。然而，合同中没有任何内容表明双方明确打算使制造商受益，例如，他们是否包含一项条款，表明合同是为了制造商的利益而制定的，或者制造商有权执行该合同。这意味着制造商至多是合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（选项D）。因此，它对铁路运输公司的诉讼很可能不会胜诉。 （选项 A） 传统上，合同相对性原则阻止个人执行他们不是当事人的合同。但这一原则已被削弱，允许预期受益人执行为其利益而制定的合同。因此，如果制造商是铁路合同的预期受益人，那么缺乏相互关系也不会导致其索赔失败。 （选项 C）当受益人 (1) 合理且有害地依赖所产生的权利，(2) 应一方请求同意合同，或 (3) 提起诉讼以强制执行合同时，预期受益人的权利归属。*但在这里，制造商是铁路合同的偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）（如果有的话），无权归属。因此，无论是制造商要求执行合同的诉讼还是其对铁路公司承诺的依赖都无关紧要。 *一旦受益人的权利被授予，缔约方就有义务履行合同。除非受益人同意，任何在归属后终止或修改受益人权利的努力均无效。\n\n教育目标：如果缔约方明确希望第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）从合同中受益，则第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是有意的（而不是附带的）。只有预期受益人才能提起诉讼以强制执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（描述预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为制造商仅根据铁路合同获得附带利益。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"carrier","ipa":"/kˈæɹiɝ/","syllables":"car·rier","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 运送者, 邮递员, 带菌者；[计] 载波","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“carrier”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:carriers","sourceSentence":"The chemical company subsequently contracted with a rail carrier to ship hundreds of products to and from the chemical company's manufacturing facilities.","synonyms":["aircraft carrier","flattop","attack aircraft carrier","common carrier"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-11777eced73cc39a"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":83,"sourceQuestionPage":244,"sourceQuestionPages":[244],"sourceExplanationPages":[245,246],"sourceAnswerPages":[245,246],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-084","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"On December 1, a broker contracted with a coin collector to sell her one of a certain type of rare coin for $12,000, delivery and payment to occur on the next March 1. To fulfill that contract, and without the collector's knowledge, the broker contracted on January 1 to purchase for $10,000 a specimen of that type coin from a man, delivery and payment to occur on February 1. The market price of such coins had unexpectedly fallen to $8,000 by February 1, when the man tendered the coin and the broker repudiated. On February 25, the market in such coins suddenly reversed and had stabilized at $12,000 on March 1. The broker, however, had failed to obtain a specimen of the coin and repudiated his agreement with the collector when she tendered the $12,000 agreed price on March 1. Later that day, after learning by chance of the broker's dealing with the collector, the man telephoned the collector and said: \"Listen, the broker probably owes me at least $2,000 in damages for refusing to buy my coin for $10,000 on February 1 when the market was down to $8,000. But I'm in good shape in view of the market's recovery since then, and I think you should sue the broker.\" If the collector immediately sues the broker for his breach of the broker-man contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?","choices":{"A":"The broker wins, because as of March 1 neither the man nor the collector had sustained any damage from the broker's repudiation of both contracts.","B":"The broker wins, because the collector, if a beneficiary at all of the broker-man contract, was only an incidental beneficiary.","C":"The collector wins, because she took an effective assignment of the man's claim for damages against the broker when the man suggested that the collector \"sue the broker.\"","D":"The collector wins, because she was an intended beneficiary of the broker-man contract, under which damages for the broker's repudiation became fixed on February 1."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. There are two types of third-party beneficiaries:\n• Intended – who receive a direct benefit from the contract because the contracting parties so intended and, therefore, have a right to enforce the contract\n• Incidental – who receive some indirect benefit from the contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them and, therefore, have no right to enforce the contract. Here, the collector may be a third-party beneficiary of the broker-man contract since the broker will sell the man's rare coin to the collector. However, the collector was only an incidental beneficiary since the broker-man contract does not show an intent to directly benefit her (Choice D). As a result, the broker will likely win since the collector has no right to sue for breach of the broker-man contract. (Choice A) The man likely sustained damages on February 1 since he would have made a $2,000 profit on his contract with the broker—ie, the contract price ($10,000) minus the market price ($8,000). Although the collector likely has not sustained damages, she can sue under her contract with the broker if she sustains damages in the future—eg, buys an equivalent coin for more than her $12,000 contract price. (Choice C) A party assigns his/her rights under a contract when he/she manifests a present intent to immediately transfer those rights. Here, the man did not assign his right to damages because he merely suggested that the collector sue the broker.\n\nEducational objective: An intended beneficiary receives a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended. An incidental beneficiary receives some indirect benefit from the\n\ncontract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. And only an intended (not incidental) beneficiary has a right to enforce the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 315 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (no right to enforce for incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"An intended beneficiary receives a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended. An incidental beneficiary receives some indirect benefit from the\n\ncontract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. And only an intended (not incidental) beneficiary has a right to enforce the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 315 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (no right to enforce for incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"An intended beneficiary receives a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended. An incidental beneficiary receives some indirect benefit from the\n\ncontract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. And only an intended (not incidental) beneficiary has a right to enforce the contract.","irac":{"issue":"On December 1, a broker contracted with a coin collector to sell her one of a certain type of rare coin for $12,000, delivery and payment to occur on the next March 1. To fulfill that contract, and without the collector's knowledge, the broker contracted on January 1 to purchase for $10,000 a specimen of that type coin from a man, delivery and payment to occur on February 1. The market price of such coins had unexpectedly fallen to $8,000 by February 1, when the man tendered the coin and the broker repudiated. On February 25, the market in such coins suddenly reversed and had stabilized at $12,000 on March 1. The broker, however, had failed to obtain a specimen of the coin and repudiated his agreement with the collector when she tendered the $12,000 agreed price on March 1. Later that day, after learning by chance of the broker's dealing with the collector, the man telephoned the collector and said: \"Listen, the broker probably owes me at least $2,000 in damages for refusing to buy my coin for $10,000 on February 1 when the market was down to $8,000. But I'm in good shape in view of the market's recovery since then, and I think you should sue the broker.\" If the collector immediately sues the broker for his breach of the broker-man contract, which of the following will the court probably decide?","rule":"An intended beneficiary receives a direct benefit from a contract because the contracting parties so intended. An incidental beneficiary receives some indirect benefit from the\n\ncontract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them. And only an intended (not incidental) beneficiary has a right to enforce the contract.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some advantage or benefit from that contract. There are two types of third-party beneficiaries:\n• Intended – who receive a direct benefit from the contract because the contracting parties so intended and, therefore, have a right to enforce the contract\n• Incidental – who receive some indirect benefit from the contract even though there was no contractual intent to benefit them and, therefore, have no right to enforce the contract. Here, the collector may be a third-party beneficiary of the broker-man contract since the broker will sell the man's rare coin to the collector. However, the collector was only an incidental beneficiary since the broker-man contract does not show an intent to directly benefit her (Choice D). As a result, the broker will likely win since the collector has no right to sue for breach of the broker-man contract. (Choice A) The man likely sustained damages on February 1 since he would have made a $2,000 profit on his contract with the broker—ie, the contract price ($10,000) minus the market price ($8,000). Although the collector likely has not sustained damages, she can sue under her contract with the broker if she sustains damages in the future—eg, buys an equivalent coin for more than her $12,000 contract price. (Choice C) A party assigns his/her rights under a contract when he/she manifests a present intent to immediately transfer those rights. Here, the man did not assign his right to damages because he merely suggested that the collector sue the broker.","conclusion":"Answer B: The broker wins, because the collector, if a beneficiary at all of the broker-man contract, was only an incidental beneficiary."},"questionZh":"12 月 1 日，一名经纪人与一名钱币收藏家签订合同，以 12,000 美元的价格向她出售一枚某种类型的稀有硬币，并于次年 3 月 1 日交付和付款。为了履行该合同，在收藏家不知情的情况下，经纪人于 1 月 1 日签订合同，以 10,000 美元的价格从一名男子手中购买一枚该类型硬币的样本，并于 2 月 1 日交付和付款。到 2 月，此类硬币的市场价格意外跌至 8,000 美元1、当男子提交硬币而经纪人拒绝时。 2月25日，此类钱币的行情突然反转，并于3月1日稳定在12,000美元。然而，经纪人未能获得该钱币的样本，并在3月1日以12,000美元的约定价格提出时，否认了与收藏家的协议。当天晚些时候，在偶然得知经纪人与收藏家的交易后，该男子打电话给收藏家说：“听着，经纪人可能至少欠我钱2 月 1 日，当市场跌至 8,000 美元时，我拒绝以 10,000 美元购买我的代币，因此获得了 2,000 美元的赔偿，但鉴于此后市场的复苏，我认为你应该起诉经纪人。”如果收藏家立即起诉经纪人违反经纪人合同，法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"经纪人获胜，因为截至 3 月 1 日，这名男子和收藏家都没有因经纪人拒绝履行两份合同而遭受任何损失。","B":"经纪人获胜，因为收藏家即使是经纪人合同的全部受益人，也只是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）。","C":"收藏家获胜，因为当该男子建议收藏家“起诉经纪人”时，她有效地接受了该男子向经纪人提出的损害赔偿索赔。","D":"收藏家获胜，因为她是经纪人合同的预期受益人，根据该合同，经纪人毁约的赔偿金于 2 月 1 日确定。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）有两种类型：\n• 预期 – 谁从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有这样的预期，因此有权执行合同\n• 附带的——即使合同中并无使他们受益的意图，但他们仍从合同中获得一些间接利益，因此无权执行合同。在这里，收藏家可能是经纪人-人合同的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary），因为经纪人将把该人的稀有硬币出售给收藏家。然而，收藏家只是偶然受益人，因为经纪人合同并没有显示出让她直接受益的意图（选项D）。因此，经纪人很可能会获胜，因为收藏家无权因违反经纪人合同而提起诉讼。 （选项 A） 该男子可能在 2 月 1 日遭受损失，因为他本可以从与经纪人的合同中赚取 2,000 美元的利润，即合同价格（10,000 美元）减去市场价格（8,000 美元）。尽管收藏家可能没有遭受损失，但如果她将来遭受损失，她可以根据与经纪人的合同提起诉讼——例如，以超过 12,000 美元的合同价格购买同等硬币。 （选择 C）当一方表现出立即转让合同项下的权利的意图时，他/她就转让这些权利。在此，该男子并未转让其损害赔偿权，因为他只是建议收藏家起诉经纪人。\n\n教育目标：预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方如此打算。偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）从该项目中获得一些间接利益\n\n即使没有合同意图使他们受益。并且只有预期（非偶然）受益人才有权执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 315（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（无权对偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）强制执行）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"由于缔约方的意图，预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益。偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）从该项目中获得一些间接利益\n\n即使没有合同意图使他们受益。并且只有预期（非偶然）受益人才有权执行合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"12 月 1 日，一名经纪人与一名钱币收藏家签订合同，以 12,000 美元的价格向她出售一枚某种类型的稀有硬币，并于次年 3 月 1 日交付和付款。为了履行该合同，在收藏家不知情的情况下，经纪人于 1 月 1 日签订合同，以 10,000 美元的价格从一名男子手中购买一枚该类型硬币的样本，并于 2 月 1 日交付和付款。到 2 月，此类硬币的市场价格意外跌至 8,000 美元1、当男子提交硬币而经纪人拒绝时。 2月25日，此类钱币的行情突然反转，并于3月1日稳定在12,000美元。然而，经纪人未能获得该钱币的样本，并在3月1日以12,000美元的约定价格提出时，否认了与收藏家的协议。当天晚些时候，在偶然得知经纪人与收藏家的交易后，该男子打电话给收藏家说：“听着，经纪人可能至少欠我钱2 月 1 日，当市场跌至 8,000 美元时，我拒绝以 10,000 美元购买我的代币，因此获得了 2,000 美元的赔偿，但鉴于此后市场的复苏，我认为你应该起诉经纪人。”如果收藏家立即起诉经纪人违反经纪人合同，法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","rule":"由于缔约方的意图，预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益。偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）从该项目中获得一些间接利益\n\n即使没有合同意图使他们受益。并且只有预期（非偶然）受益人才有权执行合同。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些优势或利益。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）有两种类型：\n• 预期 – 谁从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方有这样的预期，因此有权执行合同\n• 附带的——即使合同中并无使他们受益的意图，但他们仍从合同中获得一些间接利益，因此无权执行合同。在这里，收藏家可能是经纪人-人合同的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary），因为经纪人将把该人的稀有硬币出售给收藏家。然而，收藏家只是偶然受益人，因为经纪人合同并没有显示出让她直接受益的意图（选项D）。因此，经纪人很可能会获胜，因为收藏家无权因违反经纪人合同而提起诉讼。 （选项 A） 该男子可能在 2 月 1 日遭受损失，因为他本可以从与经纪人的合同中赚取 2,000 美元的利润，即合同价格（10,000 美元）减去市场价格（8,000 美元）。尽管收藏家可能没有遭受损失，但如果她将来遭受损失，她可以根据与经纪人的合同提起诉讼——例如，以超过 12,000 美元的合同价格购买同等硬币。 （选择 C）当一方表现出立即转让合同项下的权利的意图时，他/她就转让这些权利。在此，该男子并未转让其损害赔偿权，因为他只是建议收藏家起诉经纪人。\n\n教育目标：预期受益人从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约方如此打算。偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）从该项目中获得一些间接利益\n\n即使没有合同意图使他们受益。并且只有预期（非偶然）受益人才有权执行合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 315（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（无权对偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）强制执行）。","conclusion":"答案 B：经纪人获胜，因为收藏家即使是经纪人合同的全部受益人，也只是偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":84,"sourceQuestionPage":247,"sourceQuestionPages":[247],"sourceExplanationPages":[248,249],"sourceAnswerPages":[248,249],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-085","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A dry goods retailer telephoned a towel manufacturer and offered to buy a minimum of 500 and a maximum of 1,000 large bath towels for $5 each, to be delivered in 30 days. The manufacturer orally accepted this offer and promptly sent the following letter to the retailer, which the retailer received two days later: \"This confirms our agreement today by telephone to sell you 500 large bath towels for 30-day delivery. /s/ Manufacturer.\" Twenty-eight days later, the manufacturer tendered to the retailer 1,000 (not 500) conforming bath towels, all of which the retailer rejected because it had found a better price term from another supplier. Because of a glut in the towel market, the manufacturer cannot resell the towels except at a loss. In a suit by the manufacturer against the retailer, which of the following will most likely be the decision?","choices":{"A":"The manufacturer can enforce a contract for 1,000 towels, because the retailer ordered and the manufacturer tendered that quantity.","B":"The manufacturer can enforce a contract for 500 towels, because the manufacturer's letter of confirmation stated that quantity term.","C":"There is no enforceable agreement, because the manufacturer's letter of confirmation did not state a price term.","D":"There is no enforceable agreement, because the retailer never signed a writing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Exceptions to UCC statute of frauds\n\nApplicability Effect\n\nMerchant's\n• Both parties are\n• Enforceable against confirmation merchants both parties for\n• One party sends written quantity confirmed & signed confirmation\n• Recipient's signature\n• Recipient has reason to not required know contents\n• No written objection within 10 days Specially\n• Indication that goods are\n• Enforceable against manufactured for buyer both parties goods\n• Goods are specially\n• No writing required manufactured & not sellable in ordinary course of business\n• Seller substantially began manufacturing or committed to procurement Admission\n• One party admits to\n• Enforceable against that contract in pleadings or party for quantity court admitted\n• No writing required Payment/delivery\n• Payment was made &\n• Enforceable against accepted OR goods were both parties for delivered & accepted quantity paid for/delivered\n• No writing required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more must generally be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. However, if both parties are merchants and one party sends a written and signed confirmation, the contract will be enforceable against both parties—up to the quantity term stated in the confirmation—if the recipient:\n\n• receives the confirmation and has reason to know of its contents and\n• fails to object to the contents in writing within 10 days of receiving the confirmation. Here, the retailer offered to buy 500 to 1,000 large bath towels for $5 each. The manufacturer accepted that offer orally, and since the resulting contract is for $500 or more, it falls within the statute of frauds. The manufacturer then sent a signed letter of confirmation to the retailer for 500 large bath towels, which the retailer received. And although the retailer never signed a writing, both parties are merchants (Choice D). Therefore, the manufacturer's confirmation made the contract enforceable against both parties for 500 towels (the stated quantity term). (Choice A) The contract would be enforceable against the retailer for 1,000 towels if the retailer had accepted the manufacturer's tender of that quantity. But since the retailer rejected all the towels, the manufacturer's delivery did not make the contract enforceable. (Choice C) Under the UCC, an enforceable contract can be created without stating a price term. In that case, the UCC \"fills the gap\" with a reasonable price at the time of delivery.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC statute of frauds, a contract between two merchants is enforceable against both parties if (1) one party sends a written and signed confirmation of the contract, (2) the recipient has reason to know the contents of that confirmation, and (3) the recipient fails to object in writing within 10 days.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC statute of frauds, a contract between two merchants is enforceable against both parties if (1) one party sends a written and signed confirmation of the contract, (2) the recipient has reason to know the contents of that confirmation, and (3) the recipient fails to object in writing within 10 days.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of frauds).","rule":"Under the UCC statute of frauds, a contract between two merchants is enforceable against both parties if (1) one party sends a written and signed confirmation of the contract, (2) the recipient has reason to know the contents of that confirmation, and (3) the recipient fails to object in writing within 10 days.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the manufacturer against the retailer, which of the following will most likely be the decision?","rule":"Under the UCC statute of frauds, a contract between two merchants is enforceable against both parties if (1) one party sends a written and signed confirmation of the contract, (2) the recipient has reason to know the contents of that confirmation, and (3) the recipient fails to object in writing within 10 days.","analysis":"Exceptions to UCC statute of frauds\n\nApplicability Effect\n\nMerchant's\n• Both parties are\n• Enforceable against confirmation merchants both parties for\n• One party sends written quantity confirmed & signed confirmation\n• Recipient's signature\n• Recipient has reason to not required know contents\n• No written objection within 10 days Specially\n• Indication that goods are\n• Enforceable against manufactured for buyer both parties goods\n• Goods are specially\n• No writing required manufactured & not sellable in ordinary course of business\n• Seller substantially began manufacturing or committed to procurement Admission\n• One party admits to\n• Enforceable against that contract in pleadings or party for quantity court admitted\n• No writing required Payment/delivery\n• Payment was made &\n• Enforceable against accepted OR goods were both parties for delivered & accepted quantity paid for/delivered\n• No writing required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC statute of frauds, contracts for the sale of goods of $500 or more must generally be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. However, if both parties are merchants and one party sends a written and signed confirmation, the contract will be enforceable against both parties—up to the quantity term stated in the confirmation—if the recipient:\n\n• receives the confirmation and has reason to know of its contents and\n• fails to object to the contents in writing within 10 days of receiving the confirmation. Here, the retailer offered to buy 500 to 1,000 large bath towels for $5 each. The manufacturer accepted that offer orally, and since the resulting contract is for $500 or more, it falls within the statute of frauds. The manufacturer then sent a signed letter of confirmation to the retailer for 500 large bath towels, which the retailer received. And although the retailer never signed a writing, both parties are merchants (Choice D). Therefore, the manufacturer's confirmation made the contract enforceable against both parties for 500 towels (the stated quantity term). (Choice A) The contract would be enforceable against the retailer for 1,000 towels if the retailer had accepted the manufacturer's tender of that quantity. But since the retailer rejected all the towels, the manufacturer's delivery did not make the contract enforceable. (Choice C) Under the UCC, an enforceable contract can be created without stating a price term. In that case, the UCC \"fills the gap\" with a reasonable price at the time of delivery.","conclusion":"Answer B: The manufacturer can enforce a contract for 500 towels, because the manufacturer's letter of confirmation stated that quantity term."},"questionZh":"一家干货零售商打电话给一家毛巾制造商，提出以每条 5 美元的价格购买最少 500 条、最多 1,000 条大浴巾，并在 30 天内交货。制造商口头接受了这一报价，并立即向零售商发送了以下信件，零售商在两天后收到了这封信：“这确认了我们今天通过电话向您出售 500 条大浴巾的协议，并在 30 天内交货。/s/ 制造商。” 28 天后，制造商向零售商提供了 1,000 条（而不是 500 条）合格浴巾，但零售商拒绝了所有这些毛巾，因为它从另一家供应商那里找到了更好的价格条件。由于毛巾市场供过于求，制造商只能亏本转售毛巾。在制造商对零售商提起诉讼时，最有可能做出以下哪项判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"制造商可以执行 1,000 条毛巾的合同，因为零售商订购了该数量，而制造商则提供了该数量。","B":"制造商可以执行 500 条毛巾的合同，因为制造商的确认信中注明了数量条款。","C":"没有可执行的协议，因为制造商的确认函没有说明价格条款。","D":"没有可执行的协议，因为零售商从未签署过书面协议。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的例外情况\n\n适用效果\n\n商家的\n• 双方均\n• 可对双方确认商家执行\n• 一方发送书面数量确认并签字确认\n• 收件人签名\n• 接收者有理由不要求知道内容\n• 10 天内无书面异议 特别说明\n• 表明货物是\n• 可针对为买方双方制造的商品强制执行\n• 货物是特殊的\n• 无需书写，在正常业务过程中制造且不可销售\n• 卖方实质上开始制造或承诺采购 准入\n• 一方承认\n• 可在法庭承认的诉状或数量当事人中针对该合同强制执行\n• 无需书面付款/送货\n• 已付款&\n• 可对已接受的货物强制执行，或者双方均已交付且已接受的数量已付款/已交付的货物\n• 无需书写 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同通常必须采用书面形式，并由寻求执行的一方签署。但是，如果双方都是商家，并且其中一方发送了书面并签名的确认书，则合同将对双方均有效（不超过确认书中规定的数量条款），前提是收件人：\n\n• 收到确认并有理由知道其内容并且\n• 未在收到确认后10 天内以书面形式反对内容。在此，零售商提出以每条 5 美元的价格购买 500 至 1,000 条大浴巾。制造商口头接受了该报价，由于最终签订的合同金额为 500 美元或以上，因此属于欺诈法。随后，制造商向零售商发送了一份签署的 500 条大浴巾确认函，零售商收到了该确认函。尽管零售商从未签署书面文件，但双方都是商人（选项 D）。因此，制造商的确认使双方签订的 500 条毛巾（规定数量条款）的合同具有可执行性。 （选择 A） 如果零售商接受了制造商对 1,000 条毛巾的投标，则该合同将对零售商强制执行。但由于零售商拒绝了所有毛巾，制造商的交货并不能使合同生效。 （选择 C）根据 UCC，可以在不说明价格条款的情况下创建可执行合同。在这种情况下，UCC 在交货时以合理的价格“填补空白”。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果 (1) 一方发送书面并签名的合同确认书，(2) 接收者有理由知道该确认书的内容，并且 (3) 接收者未能在 10 天内提出书面反对，则两个商家之间的合同对双方均具有强制执行力。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果 (1) 一方发送书面并签名的合同确认书，(2) 接收者有理由知道该确认书的内容，并且 (3) 接收者未能在 10 天内提出书面反对，则两个商家之间的合同对双方均具有强制执行力。","iracZh":{"issue":"在制造商对零售商提起诉讼时，最有可能做出以下哪项判决？","rule":"根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果 (1) 一方发送书面并签名的合同确认书，(2) 接收者有理由知道该确认书的内容，并且 (3) 接收者未能在 10 天内提出书面反对，则两个商家之间的合同对双方均具有强制执行力。","analysis":"UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）的例外情况\n\n适用效果\n\n商家的\n• 双方均\n• 可对双方确认商家执行\n• 一方发送书面数量确认并签字确认\n• 收件人签名\n• 接收者有理由不要求知道内容\n• 10 天内无书面异议 特别说明\n• 表明货物是\n• 可针对为买方双方制造的商品强制执行\n• 货物是特殊的\n• 无需书写，在正常业务过程中制造且不可销售\n• 卖方实质上开始制造或承诺采购 准入\n• 一方承认\n• 可在法庭承认的诉状或数量当事人中针对该合同强制执行\n• 无需书面付款/送货\n• 已付款&\n• 可对已接受的货物强制执行，或者双方均已交付且已接受的数量已付款/已交付的货物\n• 无需书写 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），价值 500 美元或以上的商品销售合同通常必须采用书面形式，并由寻求执行的一方签署。但是，如果双方都是商家，并且其中一方发送了书面并签名的确认书，则合同将对双方均有效（不超过确认书中规定的数量条款），前提是收件人：\n\n• 收到确认并有理由知道其内容并且\n• 未在收到确认后10 天内以书面形式反对内容。在此，零售商提出以每条 5 美元的价格购买 500 至 1,000 条大浴巾。制造商口头接受了该报价，由于最终签订的合同金额为 500 美元或以上，因此属于欺诈法。随后，制造商向零售商发送了一份签署的 500 条大浴巾确认函，零售商收到了该确认函。尽管零售商从未签署书面文件，但双方都是商人（选项 D）。因此，制造商的确认使双方签订的 500 条毛巾（规定数量条款）的合同具有可执行性。 （选择 A） 如果零售商接受了制造商对 1,000 条毛巾的投标，则该合同将对零售商强制执行。但由于零售商拒绝了所有毛巾，制造商的交货并不能使合同生效。 （选择 C）根据 UCC，可以在不说明价格条款的情况下创建可执行合同。在这种情况下，UCC 在交货时以合理的价格“填补空白”。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds），如果 (1) 一方发送书面并签名的合同确认书，(2) 接收者有理由知道该确认书的内容，并且 (3) 接收者未能在 10 天内提出书面反对，则两个商家之间的合同对双方均具有强制执行力。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。","conclusion":"答案 B：制造商可以执行 500 条毛巾的合同，因为制造商的确认信中注明了数量条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":85,"sourceQuestionPage":250,"sourceQuestionPages":[250],"sourceExplanationPages":[251,252],"sourceAnswerPages":[251,252],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-086","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Third-Party Beneficiaries","question":"An elderly woman, who had two adult children, wished to employ a live-in companion so that she might continue to live in her own home. The woman, however, had only enough income to pay one-half of the companion's $2,000 monthly salary. Learning of their mother's plight, her son and daughter agreed with each other in a signed writing that on the last day of January and each succeeding month during their mother's lifetime, each would give the mother $500. The mother then hired the companion. The son and daughter made the agreed payments in January, February, and March. In April, however, the son refused to make any payment and notified his sister and mother that he would make no further payments. Will the mother succeed in an action for $500 brought against the son after April 30?","choices":{"A":"No, because a parent cannot sue her child for breach of a promise for support.","B":"No, because the son and daughter intended their payments to their mother to be gifts.","C":"Yes, because by making his first three payments, the son confirmed his intent to contract.","D":"Yes, because the mother is an intended beneficiary of a contract between the son and daughter."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some benefit from that contract. The third party can enforce the contract if he/she is an intended beneficiary— ie, one whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly. Here, the son and daughter entered into the contract to give the mother $500 each month. Although the mother was not a party to that contract, the son and the daughter intended to confer a gift upon the mother—$500 monthly from each child. Therefore, the mother was an intended beneficiary of the contract and will succeed in an enforcement action against the son (Choice B). (Choice A) There is no rule that a parent cannot sue a child for breach of a promise for support, but such a promise may be unenforceable for another reason—eg, if the promise lacks consideration. Here, however, the son and daughter's exchanged promises were supported by consideration. (Choice C) The son confirmed his intent to contract when he agreed with the daughter that each would give the mother $500 monthly. Therefore, the first three payments are irrelevant to the son's intent to contract.\n\nEducational objective: A person who is not a party to a contract may still enforce that contract if he/she is an intended beneficiary—ie, a third party whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"A person who is not a party to a contract may still enforce that contract if he/she is an intended beneficiary—ie, a third party whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"A person who is not a party to a contract may still enforce that contract if he/she is an intended beneficiary—ie, a third party whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly.","irac":{"issue":"Will the mother succeed in an action for $500 brought against the son after April 30?","rule":"A person who is not a party to a contract may still enforce that contract if he/she is an intended beneficiary—ie, a third party whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiaries\n\n(ie, nonparties who benefit from contract)\n\nType Definition Right to enforce\n\nIntended\n• Receive direct benefit from contract because contracting Yes parties intended to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to paint B's house if B promises to pay $500 to C Incidental\n• Receive indirect benefit from contract even though there No was no contractual intent to benefit them\n• Eg, A promises to buy B a car manufactured by C A third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to a contract who receives some benefit from that contract. The third party can enforce the contract if he/she is an intended beneficiary— ie, one whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly. Here, the son and daughter entered into the contract to give the mother $500 each month. Although the mother was not a party to that contract, the son and the daughter intended to confer a gift upon the mother—$500 monthly from each child. Therefore, the mother was an intended beneficiary of the contract and will succeed in an enforcement action against the son (Choice B). (Choice A) There is no rule that a parent cannot sue a child for breach of a promise for support, but such a promise may be unenforceable for another reason—eg, if the promise lacks consideration. Here, however, the son and daughter's exchanged promises were supported by consideration. (Choice C) The son confirmed his intent to contract when he agreed with the daughter that each would give the mother $500 monthly. Therefore, the first three payments are irrelevant to the son's intent to contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the mother is an intended beneficiary of a contract between the son and daughter."},"questionZh":"一位有两个成年子女的老年妇女希望雇用一名同居伴侣，以便她可以继续住在自己的家里。然而，这位女士的收入只够支付同伴 2,000 美元月薪的一半。得知母亲的困境后，她的儿子和女儿在一份签名信中约定，在母亲生前的一月最后一天以及随后的每个月，每人将给母亲 500 美元。母亲随后雇用了这名同伴。儿子和女儿在一月、二月和三月按约定付款。然而，四月份，儿子拒绝支付任何费用，并通知他的姐姐和母亲，他不会再支付任何费用。 4 月 30 日之后，母亲对儿子提起的 500 美元诉讼会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为父母不能因孩子违反赡养承诺而起诉孩子。","B":"不，因为儿子和女儿打算把给母亲的钱作为礼物。","C":"是的，因为通过支付前三笔款项，儿子确认了他签订合同的意图。","D":"是的，因为母亲是儿子和女儿之间合同的预期受益人。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些利益。如果第三方是预期受益人（即缔约方打算直接受益的人），则他/她可以执行合同。在这里，儿子和女儿签订了每月给母亲500美元的合同。尽管母亲不是该合同的当事人，但儿子和女儿打算向母亲赠送一份礼物——每个孩子每月 500 美元。因此，母亲是合同的预期受益人，并将成功对儿子采取强制执行行动（选项 B）。 （选项 A） 没有规定父母不能因子女违反赡养承诺而起诉子女，但此类承诺可能因其他原因而无法执行，例如，如果承诺缺乏约因。然而，在这里，儿子和女儿的交换承诺得到了考虑的支持。 （选项C） 当儿子与女儿同意每人每月给母亲500 美元时，确认了他的合同意向。因此，前三笔付款与儿子的签约意图无关。\n\n教育目标：非合同当事人如果是预期受益人（即合同双方打算直接受益的第三方），仍然可以执行该合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非合同当事人如果是预期受益人（即合同双方打算直接受益的第三方），仍然可以执行该合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"4 月 30 日之后，母亲对儿子提起的 500 美元诉讼会成功吗？","rule":"非合同当事人如果是预期受益人（即合同双方打算直接受益的第三方），仍然可以执行该合同。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）\n\n（即从合同中受益的非当事人）\n\n类型 定义 执行权\n\n预期\n• 从合同中获得直接利益，因为缔约各方旨在使他们受益\n• 例如，如果 B 承诺向 C 支付 500 美元，则 A 承诺粉刷 B 的房子\n• 即使没有合同意图使他们受益，也从合同中获得间接利益\n• 例如，A 承诺向B 购买由C 制造的汽车。第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是合同的非当事人，但从该合同中获得一些利益。如果第三方是预期受益人（即缔约方打算直接受益的人），则他/她可以执行合同。在这里，儿子和女儿签订了每月给母亲500美元的合同。尽管母亲不是该合同的当事人，但儿子和女儿打算向母亲赠送一份礼物——每个孩子每月 500 美元。因此，母亲是合同的预期受益人，并将成功对儿子采取强制执行行动（选项 B）。 （选项 A） 没有规定父母不能因子女违反赡养承诺而起诉子女，但此类承诺可能因其他原因而无法执行，例如，如果承诺缺乏约因。然而，在这里，儿子和女儿的交换承诺得到了考虑的支持。 （选项C） 当儿子与女儿同意每人每月给母亲500 美元时，确认了他的合同意向。因此，前三笔付款与儿子的签约意图无关。\n\n教育目标：非合同当事人如果是预期受益人（即合同双方打算直接受益的第三方），仍然可以执行该合同。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为母亲是儿子和女儿之间合同的预期受益人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":86,"sourceQuestionPage":253,"sourceQuestionPages":[253],"sourceExplanationPages":[254,255],"sourceAnswerPages":[254,255],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-087","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A flour manufacturer contracted to deliver to a baker her flour requirements for a one-year period. Before delivery of the first scheduled installment, the flour manufacturer sold its business and assigned all of its sale contracts to a reputable and longtime flour wholesaler. The flour manufacturer informed the baker of this transaction. When the flour wholesaler tendered the first installment to the baker in compliance with the manufacturer-baker contract, the baker refused to accept the goods. Which of the following arguments, if any, legally support(s) the baker's rejection of the goods?","choices":{"A":"Duties under an executory bilateral contract are assumable only by an express promise to perform on the part of the delegatee, and language of \"assignment\" in the transfer for value of a bilateral sale-of-goods contract effects only a transfer of rights, not a delegation of duties.","B":"Executory requirements contracts are nonassignable.","C":"Executory requirements contracts are nonassignable, duties under an executory bilateral contract are assumable only by an express promise to perform on the part of the delegatee, and language of \"assignment\" in the transfer for value of a bilateral sale- of-goods contract effects only a transfer of rights, not a delegation of duties.","D":"No argument legally supports the baker's rejection of the goods."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract provisions prohibiting assignments/delegations\n\nType of Explicit prohibitions Assignment of Delegation of provision rights duties\n\nNone N/A Allowed\n\nSpecific Assignment of rights & Barred delegation of duties\n\nAssignment of rights only Barred Allowed\n\nDelegation of duties only Allowed Barred\n\nGeneral Assignment of \"the contract\"\n\nUnless stated otherwise, bilateral contracts for the sale of goods can be assigned in their entirety, and this will be construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). Such assignments are generally valid, even for an executory requirements contract—ie, an uncompleted contract for the sale of as many goods as the buyer needs during a specified period (Choice B). The delegatee's acceptance of the assignment constitutes a promise to perform the duties, and the promisee must accept that performance. Here, the flour manufacturer assigned the baker's requirements contract to the flour wholesaler (delegatee), and the baker refused to accept the wholesaler's first installment of flour. However, this general language of \"assignment\" operates as a transfer of rights and a delegation of duties. The wholesaler's acceptance of that assignment is a promise to perform—ie, to sell the baker as much flour as she needs for one year (Choices A & C). Therefore, no argument legally supports the baker's rejection of the goods.\n\nEducational objective: The assignment of an entire contract is construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). The delegatee's acceptance of the assignment constitutes a promise to perform the delegator's duties, and the promisee must accept that performance.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a general assignment is an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties).","educationalObjective":"The assignment of an entire contract is construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). The delegatee's acceptance of the assignment constitutes a promise to perform the delegator's duties, and the promisee must accept that performance.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a general assignment is an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties).","rule":"The assignment of an entire contract is construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). The delegatee's acceptance of the assignment constitutes a promise to perform the delegator's duties, and the promisee must accept that performance.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments, if any, legally support(s) the baker's rejection of the goods?","rule":"The assignment of an entire contract is construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). The delegatee's acceptance of the assignment constitutes a promise to perform the delegator's duties, and the promisee must accept that performance.","analysis":"Contract provisions prohibiting assignments/delegations\n\nType of Explicit prohibitions Assignment of Delegation of provision rights duties\n\nNone N/A Allowed\n\nSpecific Assignment of rights & Barred delegation of duties\n\nAssignment of rights only Barred Allowed\n\nDelegation of duties only Allowed Barred\n\nGeneral Assignment of \"the contract\"\n\nUnless stated otherwise, bilateral contracts for the sale of goods can be assigned in their entirety, and this will be construed as an assignment of rights and a delegation of duties to a nonparty (ie, delegatee). Such assignments are generally valid, even for an executory requirements contract—ie, an uncompleted contract for the sale of as many goods as the buyer needs during a specified period (Choice B). The delegatee's acceptance of the assignment constitutes a promise to perform the duties, and the promisee must accept that performance. Here, the flour manufacturer assigned the baker's requirements contract to the flour wholesaler (delegatee), and the baker refused to accept the wholesaler's first installment of flour. However, this general language of \"assignment\" operates as a transfer of rights and a delegation of duties. The wholesaler's acceptance of that assignment is a promise to perform—ie, to sell the baker as much flour as she needs for one year (Choices A & C). Therefore, no argument legally supports the baker's rejection of the goods.","conclusion":"Answer D: No argument legally supports the baker's rejection of the goods."},"questionZh":"一家面粉制造商签订合同，在一年内向面包师提供其所需的面粉。在交付第一批预定款项之前，面粉制造商出售了其业务，并将其所有销售合同转让给了一家信誉良好的长期面粉批发商。面粉制造商将这笔交易通知了面包师。当面粉批发商按照制造商与面包师的合同向面包师交付第一笔货款时，面包师拒绝接受货物。以下哪一个论点（如果有）可以合法地支持面包师拒绝接收货物？","choicesZh":{"A":"可执行的双边合同项下的义务只能由受托人明确承诺履行，而双边货物销售合同价值转让中的“转让”语言仅影响权利的转让，而不是义务的委托。","B":"执行需求合同是不可转让的。","C":"执行要求合同是不可转让的，执行双边合同下的义务只能由受托人明确承诺履行，而双边货物销售合同价值转让中的“转让”语言仅影响权利的转让，而不是义务的委托。","D":"没有任何论据在法律上支持面包师拒收货物。"},"explanationZh":"禁止转让/委托的合同条款\n\n明确禁止的类型 条款权利委托的分配 职责的分配\n\n无 N/A 允许\n\n具体的权利分配和禁止的职责委托\n\n仅转让权利 禁止 允许\n\n只允许职责委派 禁止\n\n“合同”的一般转让\n\n除非另有说明，双边货物销售合同可以全部转让，这将被解释为向非当事人（即受托人）转让权利和义务。此类转让通常是有效的，即使对于可执行的需求合同也是如此，即在指定期间内销售买方需要的尽可能多的货物的未完成合同（选择 B）。受托人接受委托即构成履行其职责的承诺，受承诺人必须接受该履行。在此，面粉制造商将面包师的需求合同转让给面粉批发商（受托人），而面包师拒绝接受批发商的第一批面粉。然而，这种“转让”的通用语言表现为权利的转让和义务的委托。批发商接受这项任务就是履行承诺，即向面包师出售她一年所需的面粉（选项 A 和 C）。因此，没有任何论据在法律上支持面包师拒收货物。\n\n教育目标：整个合同的转让被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。受委托人接受委托即构成其履行委托人职责的承诺，受承诺人必须接受该履行。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释一般转让是权利转让和义务委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"整个合同的转让被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。受委托人接受委托即构成其履行委托人职责的承诺，受承诺人必须接受该履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一个论点（如果有）可以合法地支持面包师拒绝接收货物？","rule":"整个合同的转让被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。受委托人接受委托即构成其履行委托人职责的承诺，受承诺人必须接受该履行。","analysis":"禁止转让/委托的合同条款\n\n明确禁止的类型 条款权利委托的分配 职责的分配\n\n无 N/A 允许\n\n具体的权利分配和禁止的职责委托\n\n仅转让权利 禁止 允许\n\n只允许职责委派 禁止\n\n“合同”的一般转让\n\n除非另有说明，双边货物销售合同可以全部转让，这将被解释为向非当事人（即受托人）转让权利和义务。此类转让通常是有效的，即使对于可执行的需求合同也是如此，即在指定期间内销售买方需要的尽可能多的货物的未完成合同（选择 B）。受托人接受委托即构成履行其职责的承诺，受承诺人必须接受该履行。在此，面粉制造商将面包师的需求合同转让给面粉批发商（受托人），而面包师拒绝接受批发商的第一批面粉。然而，这种“转让”的通用语言表现为权利的转让和义务的委托。批发商接受这项任务就是履行承诺，即向面包师出售她一年所需的面粉（选项 A 和 C）。因此，没有任何论据在法律上支持面包师拒收货物。\n\n教育目标：整个合同的转让被解释为对非当事人（即受委托人）的权利转让和义务委托。受委托人接受委托即构成其履行委托人职责的承诺，受承诺人必须接受该履行。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-210 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释一般转让是权利转让和义务委托）。","conclusion":"答案 D：没有任何论据在法律上支持面包师拒收货物。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"bilateral contract","ipa":"/baɪlˈætɝəl kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"bi·lat·eral con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"双务合同：双方以相互允诺交换的合同","contextualMeaning":"双务合同：双方以相互允诺交换的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Duties under an executory bilateral contract are assumable only by an express promise to perform on the part of the delegatee, and language of \"assignment\" in the transfer for value of a bilateral sale-of-goods contract effects only a transfer of rights, not a delegation of duties.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":87,"sourceQuestionPage":256,"sourceQuestionPages":[256],"sourceExplanationPages":[257,258],"sourceAnswerPages":[257,258],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-088","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"A breeder bought a two-month-old registered boar at auction from a seller for $800. No express warranty was made. Fifteen months later, tests by experts proved conclusively that the boar had been born incurably sterile. If this had been known at the time of the sale, the boar would have been worth no more than $100. In an action by the breeder against the seller to avoid the contract and recover the price paid, the parties stipulate that, as both were and had been aware, the minimum age at which the fertility of a boar can be determined is about 12 months. Which of the following will the court probably decide?","choices":{"A":"The breeder wins, because the parties were mutually mistaken as to the boar's fertility when they made the agreement.","B":"The breeder wins, because the seller impliedly warranted that the boar was fit for breeding.","C":"The seller wins, because any mistake involved was unilateral, not mutual.","D":"The seller wins, because the breeder assumed the risk of the boar's sterility."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When party assumes risk of mistake\n\nExpress Contract allocates risk to party\n\nConscious Party enters contract despite conscious ignorance of facts related to ignorance mistake\n\nAdjudication Court allocates risk to party because reasonable under circumstances\n\nMutual mistake occurs when both parties enter into a contract based on the same mistake. In such cases, the contract may be voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n\n• the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nOne way a party assumes the risk of a mistake is by proceeding with conscious ignorance (ie, awareness of his/her limited knowledge) of the facts related to the mistake. Here, both the breeder and the seller likely entered the contract based on an erroneous belief that the boar would be fertile—a basic assumption of the contract—as evidenced by the $800 sales price (Choice C). That mutual mistake materially affected the agreed-upon exchange since the boar's actual worth was no more than $100. However, the breeder assumed the risk that the boar would turn out to be sterile because the breeder was aware that the boar's fertility could not be known at the time of the sale (Choice A). As a result, the seller probably wins. (Choice B) Under the UCC, a seller impliedly warrants that goods are fit for a particular purpose if the seller knows or should know that the buyer requires the goods for a particular purpose (eg, to breed) AND is relying upon the seller's expertise to provide suitable goods (eg, had the breeder lacked knowledge of fertility).\n\nEducational objective: Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when mutual mistake makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a party bears the risk of a mistake).","educationalObjective":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when mutual mistake makes a contract voidable).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a party bears the risk of a mistake).","rule":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following will the court probably decide?","rule":"Mutual mistake renders a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"When party assumes risk of mistake\n\nExpress Contract allocates risk to party\n\nConscious Party enters contract despite conscious ignorance of facts related to ignorance mistake\n\nAdjudication Court allocates risk to party because reasonable under circumstances\n\nMutual mistake occurs when both parties enter into a contract based on the same mistake. In such cases, the contract may be voidable by the adversely affected party if:\n• the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract\n\n• the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and\n\n• the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nOne way a party assumes the risk of a mistake is by proceeding with conscious ignorance (ie, awareness of his/her limited knowledge) of the facts related to the mistake. Here, both the breeder and the seller likely entered the contract based on an erroneous belief that the boar would be fertile—a basic assumption of the contract—as evidenced by the $800 sales price (Choice C). That mutual mistake materially affected the agreed-upon exchange since the boar's actual worth was no more than $100. However, the breeder assumed the risk that the boar would turn out to be sterile because the breeder was aware that the boar's fertility could not be known at the time of the sale (Choice A). As a result, the seller probably wins. (Choice B) Under the UCC, a seller impliedly warrants that goods are fit for a particular purpose if the seller knows or should know that the buyer requires the goods for a particular purpose (eg, to breed) AND is relying upon the seller's expertise to provide suitable goods (eg, had the breeder lacked knowledge of fertility).","conclusion":"Answer D: The seller wins, because the breeder assumed the risk of the boar's sterility."},"questionZh":"一名饲养员在拍卖会上以 800 美元的价格从卖家手中购买了一头两个月大的注册公猪。没有做出明确的保证。十五个月后，专家的测试最终证明，这头野猪生来就无法生育。如果在出售时就知道这一点，这头公猪的价值不会超过 100 美元。在饲养员向卖方提起诉讼，要求撤销合同并收回已支付的价款时，双方规定，正如双方都知道的那样，可以确定公猪生育能力的最低年龄约为 12 个月。法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"饲养员获胜，因为双方在达成协议时对公猪的生育力都存在误解。","B":"饲养员获胜，因为卖方暗示保证公猪适合繁殖。","C":"卖方获胜，因为所涉及的任何错误都是单方面的，而不是相互的。","D":"卖方获胜，因为饲养员承担了公猪不育的风险。"},"explanationZh":"当一方承担错误风险时\n\n快速合同将风险分配给当事人\n\n尽管自觉不了解与无知错误相关的事实，但自觉方仍签订合同\n\n裁决法院将风险分配给当事人，因为在情况下是合理的\n\n当双方基于相同的错误签订合同时，就会出现相互错误。在这种情况下，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可能会撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n一方承担错误风险的一种方式是在有意识地无知（即意识到他/她的知识有限）的情况下继续与错误相关的事实。在此，饲养员和卖方签订合同时可能都错误地认为公猪具有生育能力（这是合同的基本假设），800 美元的销售价格就证明了这一点（选项 C）。由于这头野猪的实际价值不超过 100 美元，这一共同错误对商定的交易产生了重大影响。然而，饲养员承担了公猪不育的风险，因为饲养员知道公猪的生育力在出售时无法得知（选择 A）。结果，卖家很可能获胜。 （选择B）根据UCC，如果卖方知道或应该知道买方需要货物用于特定目的（例如，繁殖）并且依赖卖方的专业知识来提供合适的货物（例如，如果繁殖者缺乏生育知识），则卖方默示保证货物适合特定目的。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，相互错误会导致受到不利影响的一方撤销合同：(1) 错误与合同的基本假设相关，(2) 错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当相互错误导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方承担错误风险时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，相互错误使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","rule":"在以下情况下，相互错误使得受到不利影响的一方可以撤销合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"当一方承担错误风险时\n\n快速合同将风险分配给当事人\n\n尽管自觉不了解与无知错误相关的事实，但自觉方仍签订合同\n\n裁决法院将风险分配给当事人，因为在情况下是合理的\n\n当双方基于相同的错误签订合同时，就会出现相互错误。在这种情况下，如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可能会撤销合同：\n• 该错误与合同的基本假设有关\n\n• 该错误严重影响了商定的履约交换（即，不能公平地要求受到不利影响的一方执行该交换）以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。\n\n一方承担错误风险的一种方式是在有意识地无知（即意识到他/她的知识有限）的情况下继续与错误相关的事实。在此，饲养员和卖方签订合同时可能都错误地认为公猪具有生育能力（这是合同的基本假设），800 美元的销售价格就证明了这一点（选项 C）。由于这头野猪的实际价值不超过 100 美元，这一共同错误对商定的交易产生了重大影响。然而，饲养员承担了公猪不育的风险，因为饲养员知道公猪的生育力在出售时无法得知（选择 A）。结果，卖家很可能获胜。 （选择B）根据UCC，如果卖方知道或应该知道买方需要货物用于特定目的（例如，繁殖）并且依赖卖方的专业知识来提供合适的货物（例如，如果繁殖者缺乏生育知识），则卖方默示保证货物适合特定目的。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，相互错误会导致受到不利影响的一方撤销合同：(1) 错误与合同的基本假设相关，(2) 错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误的风险。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 152 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当相互错误导致合同无效时）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 154 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方承担错误风险时）。","conclusion":"答案 D：卖方获胜，因为饲养员承担了公猪不育的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"express warranty","ipa":"/ɪkspɹˈɛs wˈɔɹənti/","syllables":"ex·press war·ranty","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"明示担保：关于货物事实、承诺、描述或样品成为交易基础","contextualMeaning":"明示担保：关于货物事实、承诺、描述或样品成为交易基础","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No express warranty was made.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":88,"sourceQuestionPage":259,"sourceQuestionPages":[259],"sourceExplanationPages":[260,261],"sourceAnswerPages":[260,261],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-089","subject":"Contracts","topic":"UCC Formation & Battle of Forms","question":"The owner of a new office building contracted with a well-known landscaper to design and install landscaping around the building for $30,000. The agreement was memorialized in writing, was signed by both parties, and called for a budget of $5,000 for trees, shrubs, sod, and materials. The contract required the landscaper to complete the work within six months. Due to an unexpected increase in the price of trees and shrubs, the landscaper abandoned the project and never completed any of the work. Three years after the landscaper's deadline, the building owner sued the landscaper for breach of contract. In the jurisdiction, the statute of limitations for breach of a services contract is two years after the breach, and the statute of limitations for breach of a sale-of- goods contract is four years. Can the owner recover damages from the landscaper?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contract is divisible with respect to the services and goods, and the landscaper's breach is therefore subject to the two-year statute of limitations.","B":"No, because the contract primarily calls for services, and the landscaper's breach is therefore subject to the two-year statute of limitations.","C":"Yes, because the landscaper's breach was a result of an increase in the price of goods, and his breach is therefore subject to the four-year statute of limitations.","D":"Yes, because the landscaper's breach was willful, and he is therefore estopped from denying that his breach is subject to the four-year statute of limitations."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), while contracts for services are governed by common law. However, some contracts involve the sale of goods and the rendering of services. To determine which law applies to a \"mixed\" or \"hybrid\" contract, courts ask whether its predominant purpose was the sale of goods or the rendering of services. The following factors are relevant to this determination:\n• The contract's language\n• The nature of the supplier's business (ie, whether it typically provides goods or services)\n• The relative value of the goods and services\n• The nature of the final product (ie, whether it can be described as a good or service) Here, the building owner contracted to buy goods (eg, trees, shrubs, sod) and services (ie, designing and installing the landscaping). The owner likely hired the well-known landscaper due to his skill in performing landscaping services, and the $5,000 budget for goods was just one-sixth of the $30,000 contract price. Therefore, the contract primarily calls for services and is subject to the jurisdiction's two-year statute of limitations. And since the owner sued three years after the breach, the owner cannot recover damages from the landscaper. (Choice A) The predominant-purpose test is unnecessary when a contract is divisible—ie, when the payment for goods can easily be separated from the payment for services. But here, the contract is likely indivisible since it combined the sale of the trees, shrubs, and sod with their installation. (Choices C & D) The predominant-purpose test focuses on the parties' reason for entering the contract—not for breaching it. Therefore, it is irrelevant that the landscaper's breach was (1) a result of an increase in the price of goods or (2) willful.\n\nEducational objective: Sale-of-goods contracts are governed by the UCC, while services contracts are governed by common law. When a contract calls for the sale of goods AND the rendering of services, the contract's primary purpose determines whether the UCC or common law applies.\n\nReferences\n• Bonebrake v. Cox, 499 F.2d 951, 960 (8th Cir. 1974) (applying the predominant- purpose test to determine which statute of limitations applies to a mixed contract for goods and services).\n\n• Princess Cruises, Inc. v. Gen. Elec. Co., 143 F.3d 828, 833 (4th Cir. 1998) (listing factors that courts consider when applying the predominant-purpose test).","educationalObjective":"Sale-of-goods contracts are governed by the UCC, while services contracts are governed by common law. When a contract calls for the sale of goods AND the rendering of services, the contract's primary purpose determines whether the UCC or common law applies.","references":"• Bonebrake v. Cox, 499 F.2d 951, 960 (8th Cir. 1974) (applying the predominant- purpose test to determine which statute of limitations applies to a mixed contract for goods and services).\n\n• Princess Cruises, Inc. v. Gen. Elec. Co., 143 F.3d 828, 833 (4th Cir. 1998) (listing factors that courts consider when applying the predominant-purpose test).","rule":"Sale-of-goods contracts are governed by the UCC, while services contracts are governed by common law. When a contract calls for the sale of goods AND the rendering of services, the contract's primary purpose determines whether the UCC or common law applies.","irac":{"issue":"Can the owner recover damages from the landscaper?","rule":"Sale-of-goods contracts are governed by the UCC, while services contracts are governed by common law. When a contract calls for the sale of goods AND the rendering of services, the contract's primary purpose determines whether the UCC or common law applies.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), while contracts for services are governed by common law. However, some contracts involve the sale of goods and the rendering of services. To determine which law applies to a \"mixed\" or \"hybrid\" contract, courts ask whether its predominant purpose was the sale of goods or the rendering of services. The following factors are relevant to this determination:\n• The contract's language\n• The nature of the supplier's business (ie, whether it typically provides goods or services)\n• The relative value of the goods and services\n• The nature of the final product (ie, whether it can be described as a good or service) Here, the building owner contracted to buy goods (eg, trees, shrubs, sod) and services (ie, designing and installing the landscaping). The owner likely hired the well-known landscaper due to his skill in performing landscaping services, and the $5,000 budget for goods was just one-sixth of the $30,000 contract price. Therefore, the contract primarily calls for services and is subject to the jurisdiction's two-year statute of limitations. And since the owner sued three years after the breach, the owner cannot recover damages from the landscaper. (Choice A) The predominant-purpose test is unnecessary when a contract is divisible—ie, when the payment for goods can easily be separated from the payment for services. But here, the contract is likely indivisible since it combined the sale of the trees, shrubs, and sod with their installation. (Choices C & D) The predominant-purpose test focuses on the parties' reason for entering the contract—not for breaching it. Therefore, it is irrelevant that the landscaper's breach was (1) a result of an increase in the price of goods or (2) willful.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the contract primarily calls for services, and the landscaper's breach is therefore subject to the two-year statute of limitations."},"questionZh":"一栋新办公楼的业主与一位知名园林设计师签订了合同，以 30,000 美元的价格设计和安装大楼周围的景观。该协议以书面形式签署，并由双方签署，并要求为树木、灌木、草皮和材料提供 5,000 美元的预算。合同要求园林设计师在六个月内完成工作。由于树木和灌木的价格意外上涨，园林设计师放弃了该项目，并且从未完成任何工作。园林设计师的最后期限三年后，业主起诉园林设计师违反合同。在该司法管辖区，违反服务合同的时效为违约后两年，违反销售合同的时效为四年。业主可以向园艺师追偿损失吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合同在服务和货物方面是可分割的，因此园林设计师的违约行为将受到两年时效的约束。","B":"不可以，因为合同主要要求提供服务，因此园林设计师的违约行为将受到两年时效的约束。","C":"是的，因为园林设计师的违约行为是商品价格上涨的结果，因此他的违约行为受到四年诉讼时效的约束。","D":"是的，因为园林设计师的违约行为是故意的，因此他不能否认他的违约行为受到四年诉讼时效的约束。"},"explanationZh":"货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖，而服务合同受普通法管辖。然而，有些合同涉及货物销售和服务提供。为了确定“混合”或“混合”合同适用哪种法律，法院会询问其主要目的是销售商品还是提供服务。以下因素与此决定相关：\n• 合同语言\n• 供应商业务的性质（即通常是否提供商品或服务）\n• 商品和服务的相对价值\n• 最终产品的性质（即是否可以被描述为商品或服务） 在此，建筑物业主签订合同购买商品（例如树木、灌木、草皮）和服务（即设计和安装景观美化）。业主很可能因为这位知名园林设计师在园林绿化服务方面的技术而聘请了他，而 5,000 美元的商品预算只是 30,000 美元合同价格的六分之一。因此，该合同主要要求提供服务，并受管辖区两年的时效限制。由于业主在违规行为三年后提起诉讼，业主无法向园艺师追偿损失。 （选择A）当合同可分割时，即当货物付款可以很容易地与服务付款分开时，主要目的测试是不必要的。但在这里，合同可能是不可分割的，因为它将树木、灌木和草皮的销售与其安装结合在一起。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 主要目的测试侧重于双方签订合同的原因，而不是违反合同的原因。因此，园林设计师的违约行为是（1）商品价格上涨的结果还是（2）故意的，这都是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：货物销售合同受 UCC 管辖，而服务合同受普通法管辖。当合同要求销售货物和提供服务时，合同的主要目的决定了适用 UCC 还是普通法。\n\n参考文献\n• Bonebrake v. Cox, 499 F.2d 951, 960 (8th Cir. 1974)（应用主要目的测试来确定哪种时效法规适用于商品和服务混合合同）。\n\n• Princess Cruises, Inc. 诉 Gen. Elec。 Co., 143 F.3d 828, 833 (4th Cir. 1998)（列出法院在应用主要目的测试时考虑的因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"货物销售合同受 UCC 管辖，而服务合同则受普通法管辖。当合同要求销售货物和提供服务时，合同的主要目的决定了适用 UCC 还是普通法。","iracZh":{"issue":"业主可以向园艺师追偿损失吗？","rule":"货物销售合同受 UCC 管辖，而服务合同则受普通法管辖。当合同要求销售货物和提供服务时，合同的主要目的决定了适用 UCC 还是普通法。","analysis":"货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖，而服务合同受普通法管辖。然而，有些合同涉及货物销售和服务提供。为了确定“混合”或“混合”合同适用哪种法律，法院会询问其主要目的是销售商品还是提供服务。以下因素与此决定相关：\n• 合同语言\n• 供应商业务的性质（即通常是否提供商品或服务）\n• 商品和服务的相对价值\n• 最终产品的性质（即是否可以被描述为商品或服务） 在此，建筑物业主签订合同购买商品（例如树木、灌木、草皮）和服务（即设计和安装景观美化）。业主很可能因为这位知名园林设计师在园林绿化服务方面的技术而聘请了他，而 5,000 美元的商品预算只是 30,000 美元合同价格的六分之一。因此，该合同主要要求提供服务，并受管辖区两年的时效限制。由于业主在违规行为三年后提起诉讼，业主无法向园艺师追偿损失。 （选择A）当合同可分割时，即当货物付款可以很容易地与服务付款分开时，主要目的测试是不必要的。但在这里，合同可能是不可分割的，因为它将树木、灌木和草皮的销售与其安装结合在一起。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 主要目的测试侧重于双方签订合同的原因，而不是违反合同的原因。因此，园林设计师的违约行为是（1）商品价格上涨的结果还是（2）故意的，这都是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：货物销售合同受 UCC 管辖，而服务合同受普通法管辖。当合同要求销售货物和提供服务时，合同的主要目的决定了适用 UCC 还是普通法。\n\n参考文献\n• Bonebrake v. Cox, 499 F.2d 951, 960 (8th Cir. 1974)（应用主要目的测试来确定哪种时效法规适用于商品和服务混合合同）。\n\n• Princess Cruises, Inc. 诉 Gen. Elec。 Co., 143 F.3d 828, 833 (4th Cir. 1998)（列出法院在应用主要目的测试时考虑的因素）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为合同主要要求提供服务，因此园林设计师的违约行为将受到两年时效的约束。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"predominant purpose test","ipa":"/pɹɪdˈɑmənənt pˈɝpəs tˈɛst/","syllables":"pre·dom·i·nant pur·pose test","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"主要目的测试：判断混合合同整体适用 UCC 或普通法","contextualMeaning":"主要目的测试：判断混合合同整体适用 UCC 或普通法","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The owner of a new office building contracted with a well-known landscaper to design and install landscaping around the building for $30,000.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"cover damages","ipa":"/kˈəvɝ dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"cover dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"替代购买赔偿：买方合理购买替代品后的价差与相关损失","contextualMeaning":"替代购买赔偿：买方合理购买替代品后的价差与相关损失","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Can the owner recover damages from the landscaper?","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-624152337975fcd6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":89,"sourceQuestionPage":262,"sourceQuestionPages":[262],"sourceExplanationPages":[263,264],"sourceAnswerPages":[263,264],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-090","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"An elderly aunt owned two cars, a new one and an old one. She offered to sell the old car to her niece for $500. Knowing that her aunt owned two cars, the niece tried to trick the aunt into selling the new car by drawing up a contract that stated: \"I agree to sell my nicest car to my niece for $500.\" The aunt noticed the niece's trick but signed the contract anyway, believing that it could not possibly refer to her new car, which was worth more than $30,000. The niece attempted to pay the aunt $500 several days later. However, the aunt refused to take the money or part with either car, stating that she had changed her mind in light of the niece's dirty trick. If the niece sues the aunt for breach of contract, what will the court likely find?","choices":{"A":"The aunt is contractually obligated to sell her new car for $500.","B":"The aunt is contractually obligated to sell her old car for $500.","C":"The aunt can elect to sell either car for $500 or void the contract.","D":"There was no contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract formation generally occurs when both parties objectively manifest their assent to an exchange—eg, when the aunt and niece signed a written contract for the sale of the aunt's \"nicest car.\" But sometimes there is a misunderstanding because the parties have attached different meanings to the same material term. This prevents contract formation under two circumstances:\n• Neither party knows or has reason to know of the misunderstanding.\n• Both parties know or have reason to know of the misunderstanding. The result is different when only one party knows or has reason to know of the misunderstanding. In that case, a valid contract is formed, and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls. Here, the aunt believed that \"nicest car\" could not possibly refer to her new car and so understood the term to mean her old car. The niece knew this because the aunt had offered to sell her old car—not her new car. However, the niece intended \"nicest car\" to mean the aunt's new car. The aunt knew this because she noticed the niece's trick. Therefore, both parties knew of the misunderstanding, and the court will likely find that there was no contract. (Choices A & B) Had the niece been unaware of the misunderstanding, her meaning (\"new car\") would control and the aunt would have been obligated to sell her new car. Alternatively, had the aunt been unaware of the misunderstanding, her meaning (\"old car\") would control and she would have been obligated to sell her old car. But since both parties knew of the misunderstanding, there was no contract. (Choice C) A misunderstanding may prevent contract formation (as seen here), but it is never grounds to void (ie, cancel) a contract.\n\nEducational objective: A misunderstanding occurs when the contracting parties attach different meanings to the same material term. This prevents contract formation when neither OR both parties know or have reason to know of the misunderstanding.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","educationalObjective":"A misunderstanding occurs when the contracting parties attach different meanings to the same material term. This prevents contract formation when neither OR both parties know or have reason to know of the misunderstanding.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of misunderstanding).","rule":"A misunderstanding occurs when the contracting parties attach different meanings to the same material term. This prevents contract formation when neither OR both parties know or have reason to know of the misunderstanding.","irac":{"issue":"If the niece sues the aunt for breach of contract, what will the court likely find?","rule":"A misunderstanding occurs when the contracting parties attach different meanings to the same material term. This prevents contract formation when neither OR both parties know or have reason to know of the misunderstanding.","analysis":"Contract formation generally occurs when both parties objectively manifest their assent to an exchange—eg, when the aunt and niece signed a written contract for the sale of the aunt's \"nicest car.\" But sometimes there is a misunderstanding because the parties have attached different meanings to the same material term. This prevents contract formation under two circumstances:\n• Neither party knows or has reason to know of the misunderstanding.\n• Both parties know or have reason to know of the misunderstanding. The result is different when only one party knows or has reason to know of the misunderstanding. In that case, a valid contract is formed, and the unknowing party's meaning of the term controls. Here, the aunt believed that \"nicest car\" could not possibly refer to her new car and so understood the term to mean her old car. The niece knew this because the aunt had offered to sell her old car—not her new car. However, the niece intended \"nicest car\" to mean the aunt's new car. The aunt knew this because she noticed the niece's trick. Therefore, both parties knew of the misunderstanding, and the court will likely find that there was no contract. (Choices A & B) Had the niece been unaware of the misunderstanding, her meaning (\"new car\") would control and the aunt would have been obligated to sell her new car. Alternatively, had the aunt been unaware of the misunderstanding, her meaning (\"old car\") would control and she would have been obligated to sell her old car. But since both parties knew of the misunderstanding, there was no contract. (Choice C) A misunderstanding may prevent contract formation (as seen here), but it is never grounds to void (ie, cancel) a contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: There was no contract."},"questionZh":"一位年迈的阿姨拥有两辆车，一辆新的和一辆旧的。她提出以 500 美元的价格将旧车卖给她的侄女。侄女知道姑姑拥有两辆车，于是试图诱骗姑姑卖掉新车，她起草了一份合同，上面写着：“我同意以 500 美元的价格把我最好的车卖给我侄女。”阿姨看出了侄女的诡计，但还是签了合同，因为她认为这不可能指的是她价值三万多美元的新车。几天后，侄女试图向阿姨支付 500 美元。然而，这位阿姨拒绝接受这笔钱，也拒绝分享任何一辆车，并表示，鉴于侄女的肮脏伎俩，她已经改变了主意。如果侄女起诉阿姨违反合同，法院可能会做出什么裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"根据合同，阿姨有义务以 500 美元的价格出售她的新车。","B":"根据合同，阿姨有义务以 500 美元的价格出售她的旧车。","C":"阿姨可以选择以 500 美元的价格出售其中一辆汽车，也可以选择作废合同。","D":"没有合同。"},"explanationZh":"当双方客观地表示同意交换时，合同通常就成立了——例如，当阿姨和侄女签署了一份出售阿姨的“最好的汽车”的书面合同时。但有时会出现误解，因为当事人对同一重要术语赋予了不同的含义。这可以防止在两种情况下签订合同：\n• 双方均不知道或没有理由知道存在误解。\n• 双方都知道或有理由知道存在误解。当只有一方知道或有理由知道误解时，结果就不同了。在这种情况下，有效的合同就成立了，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。在这里，阿姨认为“最好的车”不可能指她的新车，因此将这个词理解为她的旧车。侄女知道这一点，因为阿姨提出卖掉她的旧车，而不是她的新车。然而，侄女的本意是“最好的车”，意思是阿姨的新车。阿姨之所以知道这一点，是因为她注意到了侄女的诡计。因此，双方都知道存在误会，法院很可能会认定不存在合同。 （选项 A 和 B）如果侄女没有意识到这种误解，她的意思（“新车”）将占主导地位，阿姨将有义务卖掉她的新车。或者，如果阿姨没有意识到这种误解，她的意思（“旧车”）将占主导地位，她将有义务卖掉她的旧车。但由于双方都知道有误会，所以没有签订合同。 （选项 C）误解可能会阻止合同的成立（如此处所示），但绝不是使合同无效（即取消）的理由。\n\n教育目标：当缔约方对同一重要术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。当双方都不知道或有理由知道误解时，这可以防止合同的成立。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当缔约方对同一重要术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。当双方都不知道或有理由知道误解时，这可以防止合同的成立。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果侄女起诉阿姨违反合同，法院可能会做出什么裁决？","rule":"当缔约方对同一重要术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。当双方都不知道或有理由知道误解时，这可以防止合同的成立。","analysis":"当双方客观地表示同意交换时，合同通常就成立了——例如，当阿姨和侄女签署了一份出售阿姨的“最好的汽车”的书面合同时。但有时会出现误解，因为当事人对同一重要术语赋予了不同的含义。这可以防止在两种情况下签订合同：\n• 双方均不知道或没有理由知道存在误解。\n• 双方都知道或有理由知道存在误解。当只有一方知道或有理由知道误解时，结果就不同了。在这种情况下，有效的合同就成立了，并且由不知情的一方对该术语的含义进行控制。在这里，阿姨认为“最好的车”不可能指她的新车，因此将这个词理解为她的旧车。侄女知道这一点，因为阿姨提出卖掉她的旧车，而不是她的新车。然而，侄女的本意是“最好的车”，意思是阿姨的新车。阿姨之所以知道这一点，是因为她注意到了侄女的诡计。因此，双方都知道存在误会，法院很可能会认定不存在合同。 （选项 A 和 B）如果侄女没有意识到这种误解，她的意思（“新车”）将占主导地位，阿姨将有义务卖掉她的新车。或者，如果阿姨没有意识到这种误解，她的意思（“旧车”）将占主导地位，她将有义务卖掉她的旧车。但由于双方都知道有误会，所以没有签订合同。 （选项 C）误解可能会阻止合同的成立（如此处所示），但绝不是使合同无效（即取消）的理由。\n\n教育目标：当缔约方对同一重要术语赋予不同含义时，就会发生误解。当双方都不知道或有理由知道误解时，这可以防止合同的成立。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（误解的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 D：没有合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-322d1f1239d2d4a2"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":90,"sourceQuestionPage":265,"sourceQuestionPages":[265],"sourceExplanationPages":[266,267],"sourceAnswerPages":[266,267],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-091","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Defenses","question":"A chemical corporation owned and operated a chemical processing plant. An accidental spill occurred, which made the plant unsafe for processing any chemicals. The chemical corporation subsequently entered a written and signed contract to sell the plant to a paint manufacturer for $2 million. The paint manufacturer planned to move its operations to the plant after closing the sale, and the chemical corporation assured the paint manufacturer that the plant was suitable and safe for that purpose. The chemical corporation hired a hazardous waste company to clean up the chemical spill, and the plant was made safe for operations one week before closing the sale. Two days before closing, the paint manufacturer learned about the chemical spill from the local newspaper and refused to go through with the closing. If the chemical corporation sues the paint manufacturer for breach of contract, which party is likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"The chemical corporation, because it cured the misrepresentation regarding the suitability and safety of the plant before closing.","B":"The chemical corporation, because it had no duty to notify the paint manufacturer about the chemical spill.","C":"The paint manufacturer, because it can be presumed that the paint manufacturer was harmed by the chemical corporation's misrepresentation.","D":"The paint manufacturer, because the chemical spill rendered title to the plant unmarketable."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with the intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nHowever, a misrepresentation can be cured, and the contract is no longer voidable, if the circumstances change in such a way that the misrepresentation becomes true before the adversely affected party avoids the contract. Here, the chemical corporation fraudulently misrepresented that its plant was suitable and safe for operations, thereby inducing the paint manufacturer to enter the contract. But since the corporation cured the misrepresentation by making the plant safe for operations before closing, the manufacturer could not subsequently avoid the contract. Therefore, the manufacturer's refusal to close was a breach, and the corporation is likely to prevail. (Choice B) The chemical corporation did have a duty to notify the paint manufacturer about the spill since it knew that this made the plant unsafe for the manufacturer's intended operations. But the corporation will still prevail because it cured its misrepresentation. (Choice C) A misrepresentation cannot be cured to the extent that the adversely affected party can prove that it was actually harmed by relying on the misrepresentation. But there is no presumption to that effect, nor is there evidence that the manufacturer was harmed by the misrepresentation. (Choice D) In land-sale contracts, the seller impliedly warrants that it will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing. Here, the chemical spill affected the manufacturer's intended use of the plant but did not render title unmarketable. And regardless, this defect was cured before closing.\n\nEducational objective: A fraudulent or material misrepresentation can be cured—and the contract is no longer voidable by the adversely affected party—if the circumstances change in such a way that the misrepresentation becomes true before the adversely affected party avoids the contract.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 165 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a misrepresentation is cured if the facts come into accord with the assertion).","educationalObjective":"A fraudulent or material misrepresentation can be cured—and the contract is no longer voidable by the adversely affected party—if the circumstances change in such a way that the misrepresentation becomes true before the adversely affected party avoids the contract.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 165 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a misrepresentation is cured if the facts come into accord with the assertion).","rule":"A fraudulent or material misrepresentation can be cured—and the contract is no longer voidable by the adversely affected party—if the circumstances change in such a way that the misrepresentation becomes true before the adversely affected party avoids the contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the chemical corporation sues the paint manufacturer for breach of contract, which party is likely to prevail?","rule":"A fraudulent or material misrepresentation can be cured—and the contract is no longer voidable by the adversely affected party—if the circumstances change in such a way that the misrepresentation becomes true before the adversely affected party avoids the contract.","analysis":"A misrepresentation is an untrue assertion of fact that makes a contract voidable by the adversely affected party when:\n• the misrepresentation was fraudulent (ie, made knowingly or recklessly with the intent to induce the other party's assent to the contract) OR material (ie, likely to induce a reasonable person's assent)\n\n• the misrepresentation induced assent to the contract and\n\n• the adversely affected party justifiably relied on the misrepresentation.\n\nHowever, a misrepresentation can be cured, and the contract is no longer voidable, if the circumstances change in such a way that the misrepresentation becomes true before the adversely affected party avoids the contract. Here, the chemical corporation fraudulently misrepresented that its plant was suitable and safe for operations, thereby inducing the paint manufacturer to enter the contract. But since the corporation cured the misrepresentation by making the plant safe for operations before closing, the manufacturer could not subsequently avoid the contract. Therefore, the manufacturer's refusal to close was a breach, and the corporation is likely to prevail. (Choice B) The chemical corporation did have a duty to notify the paint manufacturer about the spill since it knew that this made the plant unsafe for the manufacturer's intended operations. But the corporation will still prevail because it cured its misrepresentation. (Choice C) A misrepresentation cannot be cured to the extent that the adversely affected party can prove that it was actually harmed by relying on the misrepresentation. But there is no presumption to that effect, nor is there evidence that the manufacturer was harmed by the misrepresentation. (Choice D) In land-sale contracts, the seller impliedly warrants that it will convey marketable title to the buyer upon closing. Here, the chemical spill affected the manufacturer's intended use of the plant but did not render title unmarketable. And regardless, this defect was cured before closing.","conclusion":"Answer A: The chemical corporation, because it cured the misrepresentation regarding the suitability and safety of the plant before closing."},"questionZh":"一家化学公司拥有并经营一家化学加工厂。发生了意外泄漏，导致该工厂无法安全地加工任何化学品。该化学公司随后签订了一份书面合同，以 200 万美元的价格将该工厂出售给一家涂料制造商。涂料制造商计划在完成销售后将其业务转移到该工厂，化学公司向涂料制造商保证该工厂适合且安全。该化学公司聘请了一家危险废物公司来清理化学品泄漏，并在交易结束前一周确保工厂安全运营。关闭前两天，油漆制造商从当地报纸了解到化学品泄漏事件，并拒绝关闭。如果化工公司起诉涂料生产商违约，哪一方可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"化学公司，因为它在关闭之前纠正了有关工厂适用性和安全性的虚假陈述。","B":"化学公司，因为它没有义务将化学品泄漏情况通知油漆制造商。","C":"涂料生产商，因为可以推定涂料生产商因化工公司的失实陈述而受到损害。","D":"油漆制造商，因为化学品泄漏导致该工厂的所有权无法出售。"},"explanationZh":"失实陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，在以下情况下会使受到不利影响的一方无效合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，旨在诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n然而，如果情况发生变化，在受到不利影响的一方撤销合同之前，虚假陈述变为真实，则虚假陈述可以被纠正，并且合同不再可撤销。在此案中，该化工公司欺骗性地谎称其工厂适合且安全运营，从而诱使涂料制造商签订合同。但由于该公司在关闭前确保工厂安全运营，从而纠正了虚假陈述，因此制造商随后无法撤销合同。因此，制造商拒绝关闭是一种违约行为，公司很可能胜诉。 （选择 B） 化学公司确实有责任将泄漏事件通知涂料制造商，因为它知道这会使工厂对于制造商的预期运营来说不安全。但该公司仍将获胜，因为它纠正了其虚假陈述。 （选项C）如果受到不利影响的一方能够证明自己确实因依赖虚假陈述而受到损害，则虚假陈述就无法得到纠正。但没有这种效果的推定，也没有证据表明制造商因虚假陈述而受到损害。 （选项 D） 在土地销售合同中，卖方默示保证其将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。在这里，化学品泄漏影响了制造商对工厂的预期用途，但并未导致所有权无法销售。不管怎样，这个缺陷在关闭之前就得到了解决。\n\n教育目标：如果情况发生变化，在受到不利影响的一方撤销合同之前，虚假陈述变为真实，则欺诈性或重大失实陈述可以得到纠正，并且合同不再可以被受到不利影响的一方撤销。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 165（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果事实与断言一致，则虚假陈述即可得到纠正）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果情况发生变化，导致在受到不利影响的一方撤销合同之前，虚假陈述变为真实，则欺诈性或重大失实陈述可以得到纠正，并且合同不再可以被受到不利影响的一方撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果化工公司起诉涂料生产商违约，哪一方可能胜诉？","rule":"如果情况发生变化，导致在受到不利影响的一方撤销合同之前，虚假陈述变为真实，则欺诈性或重大失实陈述可以得到纠正，并且合同不再可以被受到不利影响的一方撤销。","analysis":"失实陈述是对事实的不真实陈述，在以下情况下会使受到不利影响的一方无效合同：\n• 虚假陈述具有欺诈性（即故意或罔顾后果地做出，旨在诱导另一方同意合同）或材料（即可能诱导合理人士的同意）\n\n• 虚假陈述导致同意合同以及\n\n• 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖虚假陈述。\n\n然而，如果情况发生变化，在受到不利影响的一方撤销合同之前，虚假陈述变为真实，则虚假陈述可以被纠正，并且合同不再可撤销。在此案中，该化工公司欺骗性地谎称其工厂适合且安全运营，从而诱使涂料制造商签订合同。但由于该公司在关闭前确保工厂安全运营，从而纠正了虚假陈述，因此制造商随后无法撤销合同。因此，制造商拒绝关闭是一种违约行为，公司很可能胜诉。 （选择 B） 化学公司确实有责任将泄漏事件通知涂料制造商，因为它知道这会使工厂对于制造商的预期运营来说不安全。但该公司仍将获胜，因为它纠正了其虚假陈述。 （选项C）如果受到不利影响的一方能够证明自己确实因依赖虚假陈述而受到损害，则虚假陈述就无法得到纠正。但没有这种效果的推定，也没有证据表明制造商因虚假陈述而受到损害。 （选项 D） 在土地销售合同中，卖方默示保证其将在成交时将可出售的所有权转让给买方。在这里，化学品泄漏影响了制造商对工厂的预期用途，但并未导致所有权无法销售。不管怎样，这个缺陷在关闭之前就得到了解决。\n\n教育目标：如果情况发生变化，在受到不利影响的一方撤销合同之前，虚假陈述变为真实，则欺诈性或重大失实陈述可以得到纠正，并且合同不再可以被受到不利影响的一方撤销。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 165（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果事实与断言一致，则虚假陈述即可得到纠正）。","conclusion":"答案 A：化学公司，因为它在关闭之前纠正了有关工厂适用性和安全性的虚假陈述。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"cured","ipa":"/kjˈʊɹd/","syllables":"cured","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 治愈的；熟化的；熏制的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“cured”","wordForm":"词；原形 cure；词形 0:cure/1:dp/p:cured/d:cured","sourceSentence":"The chemical corporation, because it cured the misrepresentation regarding the suitability and safety of the plant before closing.","synonyms":["remedy","curative","therapeutic","bring around"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5e7874c2cf2dd42b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":91,"sourceQuestionPage":268,"sourceQuestionPages":[268],"sourceExplanationPages":[269,270],"sourceAnswerPages":[269,270],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-092","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"A farmer who wanted to sell her land received a letter from a developer that stated, \"I will pay you $1,100 an acre for your land.\" The farmer's letter of reply stated, \"I accept your offer.\" Unbeknownst to the farmer, the developer had intended to offer only $1,000 per acre but had mistakenly typed \"$1,100.\" As both parties knew, comparable land in the vicinity had been selling at prices between $1,000 and $1,200 per acre. Which of the following states the probable legal consequences of the correspondence between the parties?","choices":{"A":"There is a contract formed at a price of $1,000 per acre.","B":"There is a contract formed at a price of $1,100 per acre.","C":"There is no contract, because the parties attached materially different meanings to the price term.","D":"There is no enforceable contract, because the developer is entitled to rescission due to a mutual mistake as to a basic assumption of the contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration, such as when the developer offered to pay $1,100 an acre for the farmer's land and the farmer accepted. Although the developer intended to offer only $1,000, his unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid the contract unless:\n• the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable (eg, due to a substantial difference between contract price and actual value) or\n• the nonmistaken party (ie, the farmer) caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake. Here, the developer's mistake would not make enforcement of the contract unconscionable since the developer stands to lose just $100 per acre. The farmer did not cause the mistake and had no reason to know of it since comparable land in the vicinity had been selling at prices between $1,000 and $1,200 per acre. As a result, a valid contract was formed at a price of $1,100 per acre (Choice A). (Choice C) A misunderstanding occurs when the parties attach different meanings to the same material term (eg, price). Here, the mistakenly typed \"$1,100\" did not create a misunderstanding since \"$1,100\" has just one meaning. But if it had, the misunderstanding would have prevented the formation of a contract. (Choice D) There was no mutual mistake here since only the developer (not the farmer) was mistaken as to the contract price.\n\nEducational objective: Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when unilateral mistake makes a contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when unilateral mistake makes a contract voidable).","rule":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following states the probable legal consequences of the correspondence between the parties?","rule":"Unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid a contract unless (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","analysis":"A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration, such as when the developer offered to pay $1,100 an acre for the farmer's land and the farmer accepted. Although the developer intended to offer only $1,000, his unilateral mistake is not grounds to avoid the contract unless:\n• the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable (eg, due to a substantial difference between contract price and actual value) or\n• the nonmistaken party (ie, the farmer) caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake. Here, the developer's mistake would not make enforcement of the contract unconscionable since the developer stands to lose just $100 per acre. The farmer did not cause the mistake and had no reason to know of it since comparable land in the vicinity had been selling at prices between $1,000 and $1,200 per acre. As a result, a valid contract was formed at a price of $1,100 per acre (Choice A). (Choice C) A misunderstanding occurs when the parties attach different meanings to the same material term (eg, price). Here, the mistakenly typed \"$1,100\" did not create a misunderstanding since \"$1,100\" has just one meaning. But if it had, the misunderstanding would have prevented the formation of a contract. (Choice D) There was no mutual mistake here since only the developer (not the farmer) was mistaken as to the contract price.","conclusion":"Answer B: There is a contract formed at a price of $1,100 per acre."},"questionZh":"一位想要出售土地的农民收到了开发商的一封信，信中写道：“我将为您的土地支付每英亩 1,100 美元。”农民的回信说：“我接受你的提议。”农民不知道的是，开发商原本打算只提供每英亩 1,000 美元，但错误地输入了“1,100 美元”。双方都知道，附近类似土地的售价在每英亩 1,000 至 1,200 美元之间。以下哪项陈述了双方通信可能产生的法律后果？","choicesZh":{"A":"签订的合同价格为每英亩 1,000 美元。","B":"签订的合同价格为每英亩 1,100 美元。","C":"不存在合同，因为双方对价格条款的含义存在重大差异。","D":"不存在可执行的合同，因为开发商有权因对合同基本假设的共同错误而解除合同。"},"explanationZh":"当双方协议得到充分约因时，例如开发商提出以每英亩 1,100 美元的价格购买农民的土地并且农民接受时，通常会形成有效的合同。尽管开发商只打算出价 1,000 美元，但他的单方面错误并不能成为解除合同的理由，除非：\n• 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理（例如，由于合同价格与实际价值之间存在巨大差异）或\n• 无错误的一方（即农民）造成、或知道或有理由知道错误。在这里，开发商的错误不会导致合同的执行不合理，因为开发商每英亩仅损失 100 美元。这个错误不是农民造成的，也没有理由知道，因为附近的类似土地的售价在每英亩 1,000 美元到 1,200 美元之间。结果，有效合同成立，价格为每英亩 1,100 美元（选项 A）。 （选项C） 当双方对同一重要术语（例如价格）赋予不同的含义时，就会发生误解。在这里，错误输入的“$1,100”并没有造成误解，因为“$1,100”只有一个含义。但如果是的话，误解就会阻止合同的成立。 （选项D） 这里不存在相互错误，因为只有开发商（而不是农民）对合同价格有错误。\n\n教育目标：单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当单方面错误导致合同无效时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 非错误方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述了双方通信可能产生的法律后果？","rule":"单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 非错误方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。","analysis":"当双方协议得到充分约因时，例如开发商提出以每英亩 1,100 美元的价格购买农民的土地并且农民接受时，通常会形成有效的合同。尽管开发商只打算出价 1,000 美元，但他的单方面错误并不能成为解除合同的理由，除非：\n• 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理（例如，由于合同价格与实际价值之间存在巨大差异）或\n• 无错误的一方（即农民）造成、或知道或有理由知道错误。在这里，开发商的错误不会导致合同的执行不合理，因为开发商每英亩仅损失 100 美元。这个错误不是农民造成的，也没有理由知道，因为附近的类似土地的售价在每英亩 1,000 美元到 1,200 美元之间。结果，有效合同成立，价格为每英亩 1,100 美元（选项 A）。 （选项C） 当双方对同一重要术语（例如价格）赋予不同的含义时，就会发生误解。在这里，错误输入的“$1,100”并没有造成误解，因为“$1,100”只有一个含义。但如果是的话，误解就会阻止合同的成立。 （选项D） 这里不存在相互错误，因为只有开发商（而不是农民）对合同价格有错误。\n\n教育目标：单方面错误不能成为宣告合同无效的理由，除非 (1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当单方面错误导致合同无效时）。","conclusion":"答案 B：签订的合同价格为每英亩 1,100 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"mistakenly","ipa":"/mɪstˈeɪkənli/","syllables":"mis·tak·enly","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"adv. 错误地, 被误解地","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“mistakenly”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"\" Unbeknownst to the farmer, the developer had intended to offer only $1,000 per acre but had mistakenly typed \"$1,100.","synonyms":["erroneously"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-4cdc797ecb06c57c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":92,"sourceQuestionPage":271,"sourceQuestionPages":[271],"sourceExplanationPages":[272,273],"sourceAnswerPages":[272,273],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-093","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Parol Evidence & Integration","question":"A ceramics studio contracted with an artist to produce cups and saucers designed by the artist. The artist was an established designer of collectible ceramic dinnerware, and the studio did production work for many artists who created ceramic dinnerware. The price and quantity term of the contract read: \"2,000 sets of the cups and saucers at $5 each, payable on delivery.\" The contract contained a merger clause. The studio produced the cups and saucers and delivered them along with a bill for $20,000 (4,000 pieces at $5 per piece). The artist refused to pay more than $10,000 (2,000 sets at $5 per set). At the trial of the studio's action against the artist for breach of contract, the studio introduced evidence of an established practice in the studio industry to price cup-and- saucer sets on a per-piece, not a per-set, basis. Is the studio's evidence admissible?","choices":{"A":"No, because such evidence would vary an unambiguous term of the contract.","B":"No, because the agreement was completely integrated.","C":"Yes, because evidence of trade usage is always admissible.","D":"Yes, because the usage of trade is offered to give meaning to the contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of any prior agreement (or any contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But the following evidence can always be used to explain or supplement those terms, regardless of whether the terms appear ambiguous:*\n• Course of performance – sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding an agreement between the parties if (1) the agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by a party and (2) the other party accepts performance without objection\n• Course of dealing – sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between the parties that establishes a common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n• Trade usage – any practice or method of dealing in the parties' business or industry that is practiced with enough regularity to justify an expectation that it will be practiced in the instant case Therefore, the studio's evidence of an established practice in the studio industry to price cup-and-saucer sets on a per-piece (not a per-set) basis is admissible because it is offered to give meaning to the contract. *Priority is given as follows: (1) express contract terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage. (Choice A) Even if the terms of a written contract appear unambiguous, the UCC allows a party to explain or supplement the terms by evidence of trade usage, course of dealing, or course of performance. (Choice B) If the agreement was completely integrated (ie, intended as a final and complete statement of the parties' agreement), then the studio could not explain or supplement its terms with evidence of consistent additional terms. But integration has no effect on the studio's ability to do so with evidence of trade usage (or course of dealing or performance). (Choice C) Evidence of trade usage is admissible only to explain or supplement (not contradict) the terms of a final written agreement.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC parol evidence rule always allows use of the following evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement: course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage.\n\nReferences\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","educationalObjective":"The UCC parol evidence rule always allows use of the following evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement: course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage.","references":"• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (parol evidence rule).","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule always allows use of the following evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement: course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage.","irac":{"issue":"Is the studio's evidence admissible?","rule":"The UCC parol evidence rule always allows use of the following evidence to explain or supplement the terms of a final written agreement: course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage.","analysis":"Under the UCC parol evidence rule, evidence of any prior agreement (or any contemporaneous oral agreement) cannot be used to contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But the following evidence can always be used to explain or supplement those terms, regardless of whether the terms appear ambiguous:*\n• Course of performance – sequence of conduct that is relevant to understanding an agreement between the parties if (1) the agreement involves repeated occasions for performance by a party and (2) the other party accepts performance without objection\n• Course of dealing – sequence of conduct concerning previous transactions between the parties that establishes a common basis of understanding for interpreting their conduct\n• Trade usage – any practice or method of dealing in the parties' business or industry that is practiced with enough regularity to justify an expectation that it will be practiced in the instant case Therefore, the studio's evidence of an established practice in the studio industry to price cup-and-saucer sets on a per-piece (not a per-set) basis is admissible because it is offered to give meaning to the contract. *Priority is given as follows: (1) express contract terms prevail over all others, (2) course of performance prevails over course of dealing and trade usage, and (3) course of dealing prevails over trade usage. (Choice A) Even if the terms of a written contract appear unambiguous, the UCC allows a party to explain or supplement the terms by evidence of trade usage, course of dealing, or course of performance. (Choice B) If the agreement was completely integrated (ie, intended as a final and complete statement of the parties' agreement), then the studio could not explain or supplement its terms with evidence of consistent additional terms. But integration has no effect on the studio's ability to do so with evidence of trade usage (or course of dealing or performance). (Choice C) Evidence of trade usage is admissible only to explain or supplement (not contradict) the terms of a final written agreement.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the usage of trade is offered to give meaning to the contract."},"questionZh":"一家陶瓷工作室与一位艺术家签约，生产该艺术家设计的杯子和碟子。该艺术家是一位知名的收藏级陶瓷餐具设计师，该工作室为许多创作陶瓷餐具的艺术家提供制作工作。合同的价格和数量条款如下：“2000套杯子和碟子，每套5美元，交货时付款。”该合同包含合并条款。该工作室生产了杯子和碟子，并附有 20,000 美元的账单（4,000 件，每件 5 美元）。艺术家拒绝支付超过 10,000 美元（2,000 套，每套 5 美元）。在工作室针对艺术家违反合同的诉讼审判中，工作室提供了工作室行业既定做法的证据，即按件而不是按套定价杯碟套装。工作室的证据可以采纳吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为此类证据会改变合同的明确条款。","B":"不，因为协议是完全集成的。","C":"是的，因为贸易用途的证据始终是可接受的。","D":"是的，因为贸易的使用是为了赋予合同意义。"},"explanationZh":"根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），任何先前协议（或任何同期口头协议）的证据都不能用来与最终书面协议的条款相矛盾。但以下证据始终可用于解释或补充这些术语，无论这些术语是否显得含糊不清：*\n• 履行过程 – 与理解双方之间的协议相关的行为顺序，如果 (1) 协议涉及一方重复履行且 (2) 另一方无异议地接受履行\n• 交易过程——双方之间先前交易的行为顺序，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n• 贸易用途——在双方业务或行业中进行交易的任何做法或方法，其实施有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在本案中实施。因此，工作室提供的工作室行业中按件（而非每套）对杯碟套装进行定价的既定做法的证据是可以接受的，因为它是为了赋予合同意义而提供的。 *优先权如下：(1) 明示合同条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易使用，以及 (3) 交易过程优先于贸易使用。 （选择 A）即使书面合同的条款看起来很明确，UCC 也允许一方通过贸易惯例、交易过程或履约过程的证据来解释或补充条款。 （选择B）如果协议是完全整合的（即，旨在作为双方协议的最终且完整的声明），则工作室无法用一致的附加条款的证据来解释或补充其条款。但整合不会影响工作室通过交易使用证据（或交易过程或绩效）进行整合的能力。 （选项 C）贸易用途证据仅可用于解释或补充（不得反驳）最终书面协议的条款。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用以下证据来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款：履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用以下证据来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款：履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途。","iracZh":{"issue":"工作室的证据可以采纳吗？","rule":"UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用以下证据来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款：履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途。","analysis":"根据 UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule），任何先前协议（或任何同期口头协议）的证据都不能用来与最终书面协议的条款相矛盾。但以下证据始终可用于解释或补充这些术语，无论这些术语是否显得含糊不清：*\n• 履行过程 – 与理解双方之间的协议相关的行为顺序，如果 (1) 协议涉及一方重复履行且 (2) 另一方无异议地接受履行\n• 交易过程——双方之间先前交易的行为顺序，为解释其行为建立了共同的理解基础\n• 贸易用途——在双方业务或行业中进行交易的任何做法或方法，其实施有足够的规律性，足以证明其将在本案中实施。因此，工作室提供的工作室行业中按件（而非每套）对杯碟套装进行定价的既定做法的证据是可以接受的，因为它是为了赋予合同意义而提供的。 *优先权如下：(1) 明示合同条款优先于所有其他条款，(2) 履行过程优先于交易过程和贸易使用，以及 (3) 交易过程优先于贸易使用。 （选择 A）即使书面合同的条款看起来很明确，UCC 也允许一方通过贸易惯例、交易过程或履约过程的证据来解释或补充条款。 （选择B）如果协议是完全整合的（即，旨在作为双方协议的最终且完整的声明），则工作室无法用一致的附加条款的证据来解释或补充其条款。但整合不会影响工作室通过交易使用证据（或交易过程或绩效）进行整合的能力。 （选项 C）贸易用途证据仅可用于解释或补充（不得反驳）最终书面协议的条款。\n\n教育目标：UCC 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）始终允许使用以下证据来解释或补充最终书面协议的条款：履行过程、交易过程和贸易用途。\n\n参考文献\n• U.C.C. § 2-202 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为贸易的使用是为了赋予合同意义。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-a30e64c310b03a73"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":93,"sourceQuestionPage":274,"sourceQuestionPages":[274],"sourceExplanationPages":[275,276],"sourceAnswerPages":[275,276],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-094","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A patient owed a physician $25,000 for professional services. The physician orally assigned this claim to her adult daughter as a wedding gift. Shortly thereafter, on suffering sudden, severe losses in the stock market, the physician assigned by a signed writing the same claim to her stockbroker in partial satisfaction of advances legally made by the stockbroker in the physician's previous stock-market transactions. Subsequently, the patient, without knowledge of either assignment, paid the physician the $25,000 then due, which the physician promptly lost at a horse track. The physician remains solvent. Assume that Article 9 of the Uniform Commercial Code does NOT apply to either of the assignments in this situation. Which of the following is a correct statement of the parties' rights and liabilities?","choices":{"A":"As the assignee prior in time, the daughter can recover $25,000 from the patient, who acted at her peril in paying the physician.","B":"As the sole assignee for value, the stockbroker can recover $25,000 from the patient, who acted at her peril in paying the physician.","C":"Neither the daughter nor the stockbroker can recover from the patient, but the daughter, though not the stockbroker, can recover $25,000 from the physician.","D":"Neither the daughter nor the stockbroker can recover from the patient, but the stockbroker, though not the daughter, can recover $25,000 from the physician."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nType Revocability Warranties\n\nGratuitous Revocable unless: None\n\n• obligor already performed\n• document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate) delivered\n• written & signed assignment delivered\n• promissory estoppel applies For value Irrevocable Assignor warrants that he/she:\n\n• has right to assign\n• is not subject to limitations/defenses unknown to assignee\n• will not defeat/impair assigned rights An assignment is the transfer of a contractual right to another. Where there has been a subsequent assignment of the same right, the priority of those competing assignments depends on whether the first assignment was:\n• revocable – in which case, the first assignment is automatically revoked and the second assignment has priority or\n• irrevocable – in which case, the first assignment has priority unless the second assignee was a bona fide purchaser for value who lacked notice of the first assignment. Here, the physician orally assigned her claim for the $25,000 owed by the patient to her daughter as a wedding gift. That oral assignment was valid.* However, it was revocable because it was gratuitous and no exception applies (see above table). This means that the assignment to the daughter was automatically revoked when the physician subsequently assigned the same claim to her stockbroker. Accordingly, the daughter has no right to the $25,000 (Choices A & C). The physician's assignment to the stockbroker was for value (namely, in partial satisfaction of advances), so it was irrevocable. Every assignment for value carries an implied warranty that the assignor will not do anything to defeat or impair the\n\nassigned right. The physician breached that warranty by accepting the $25,000 owed by the patient and losing it at the horse track. As a result, the stockbroker can recover $25,000 from the physician. However, the stockbroker cannot recover that amount from the patient. The reason is that the assignee's rights against the obligor are subject to any defenses that the obligor had against the assignor. Payment by the obligor (patient) to the assignor (physician) can be raised as a defense, provided that the payment was made before the obligor had notice of the assignment (as seen here). Therefore, the patient did not act at her peril in paying the physician (Choice B). *Oral assignments are generally valid. However, a writing is required for (1) wage assignments, (2) assignments of interests in land, and (3) assignments intended as security instruments under Article 9 of the Uniform Commercial Code—none of which apply here.\n\nEducational objective: A subsequent assignment of the same right revokes any prior revocable assignment. If the first assignment was irrevocable, it has priority unless the second assignee was a bona fide purchaser for value who lacked notice.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 342 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (subsequent assignment of same right).","educationalObjective":"A subsequent assignment of the same right revokes any prior revocable assignment. If the first assignment was irrevocable, it has priority unless the second assignee was a bona fide purchaser for value who lacked notice.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 342 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (subsequent assignment of same right).","rule":"A subsequent assignment of the same right revokes any prior revocable assignment. If the first assignment was irrevocable, it has priority unless the second assignee was a bona fide purchaser for value who lacked notice.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is a correct statement of the parties' rights and liabilities?","rule":"A subsequent assignment of the same right revokes any prior revocable assignment. If the first assignment was irrevocable, it has priority unless the second assignee was a bona fide purchaser for value who lacked notice.","analysis":"Assignment of contractual rights\n\nType Revocability Warranties\n\nGratuitous Revocable unless: None\n\n• obligor already performed\n• document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate) delivered\n• written & signed assignment delivered\n• promissory estoppel applies For value Irrevocable Assignor warrants that he/she:\n\n• has right to assign\n• is not subject to limitations/defenses unknown to assignee\n• will not defeat/impair assigned rights An assignment is the transfer of a contractual right to another. Where there has been a subsequent assignment of the same right, the priority of those competing assignments depends on whether the first assignment was:\n• revocable – in which case, the first assignment is automatically revoked and the second assignment has priority or\n• irrevocable – in which case, the first assignment has priority unless the second assignee was a bona fide purchaser for value who lacked notice of the first assignment. Here, the physician orally assigned her claim for the $25,000 owed by the patient to her daughter as a wedding gift. That oral assignment was valid.* However, it was revocable because it was gratuitous and no exception applies (see above table). This means that the assignment to the daughter was automatically revoked when the physician subsequently assigned the same claim to her stockbroker. Accordingly, the daughter has no right to the $25,000 (Choices A & C). The physician's assignment to the stockbroker was for value (namely, in partial satisfaction of advances), so it was irrevocable. Every assignment for value carries an implied warranty that the assignor will not do anything to defeat or impair the\n\nassigned right. The physician breached that warranty by accepting the $25,000 owed by the patient and losing it at the horse track. As a result, the stockbroker can recover $25,000 from the physician. However, the stockbroker cannot recover that amount from the patient. The reason is that the assignee's rights against the obligor are subject to any defenses that the obligor had against the assignor. Payment by the obligor (patient) to the assignor (physician) can be raised as a defense, provided that the payment was made before the obligor had notice of the assignment (as seen here). Therefore, the patient did not act at her peril in paying the physician (Choice B). *Oral assignments are generally valid. However, a writing is required for (1) wage assignments, (2) assignments of interests in land, and (3) assignments intended as security instruments under Article 9 of the Uniform Commercial Code—none of which apply here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Neither the daughter nor the stockbroker can recover from the patient, but the stockbroker, though not the daughter, can recover $25,000 from the physician."},"questionZh":"一名患者欠医生 25,000 美元的专业服务费用。医生将这一主张口头指定给她成年的女儿，作为结婚礼物。此后不久，在股票市场遭受突然的严重损失后，经签署人指派的医生向她的股票经纪人提出了同样的索赔，部分满足了股票经纪人在该医生之前的股票市场交易中合法提供的预付款。随后，病人在不知道这两项任务的情况下，向医生支付了当时到期的 25,000 美元，但医生很快就在赛马场把这笔钱输掉了。医生仍有偿付能力。假设《统一商法典》（UCC）第 9 条不适用于这种情况下的任何转让。以下哪项对双方权利和责任的描述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"作为及时的受让人，女儿可以从患者那里追回 25,000 美元，而患者自行承担了向医生付款的风险。","B":"作为价值的唯一受让人，股票经纪人可以从患者那里收回 25,000 美元，而患者在向医生付款时承担了风险。","C":"女儿和股票经纪人都无法从病人那里得到赔偿，但女儿虽然不是股票经纪人，却可以从医生那里得到 25,000 美元的赔偿。","D":"女儿和股票经纪人都无法从病人那里得到赔偿，但股票经纪人虽然不是女儿，却可以从医生那里得到 25,000 美元的赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"合同权利的转让\n\n类型可撤销保证\n\n无偿可撤销，除非： 无\n\n• 债务人已经履行\n• 交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）\n• 交付书面并签名的作业\n• 禁止反言适用 对于价值 不可撤销的转让人保证他/她：\n\n• 有权转让\n• 不受受让人未知的限制/防御的约束\n• 不会破坏/损害转让的权利 转让是将合同权利转让给另一方。如果同一权利有后续转让，这些竞争转让的优先级取决于第一个转让是否：\n• 可撤销——在这种情况下，第一个分配将自动撤销，第二个分配具有优先权或\n• 不可撤销——在这种情况下，第一个转让具有优先权，除非第二个受让人是善意的价值购买者，但没有通知第一个转让。在这里，医生口头将患者欠她女儿的 25,000 美元作为结婚礼物的索赔转让给她。该口头转让是有效的。*但是，它是可撤销的，因为它是无偿的并且不适用任何例外情况（见上表）。这意味着，当医生随后将相同的索赔分配给她的股票经纪人时，分配给女儿的分配被自动撤销。因此，女儿无权获得 25,000 美元（选择 A 和 C）。医生对股票经纪人的指派是为了价值（即部分支付预付款），因此是不可撤销的。每项价值转让都带有默示保证，即转让人不会采取任何行动来破坏或损害价值\n\n分配的权利。这位医生接受了病人所欠的 25,000 美元，却在赛马场把它弄丢了，从而违反了保证。结果，股票经纪人可以从医生那里追回 25,000 美元。然而，股票经纪人无法从患者那里收回这笔金额。原因是受让人对债务人的权利受到债务人对转让人的任何抗辩的约束。债务人（患者）向转让人（医生）付款可以作为抗辩，前提是付款是在债务人收到转让通知之前进行的（如此处所示）。因此，患者支付医生费用时无需承担风险（选择 B）。 *口头作业通常有效。然而，(1) 工资转让，(2) 土地权益转让，以及 (3) 《统一商法典》（UCC）第 9 条规定的作为担保文书的转让均需要书面形式——这些均不适用于此处。\n\n教育目标：同一权利的后续转让将撤销任何先前的可撤销转让。如果第一个转让是不可撤销的，则它具有优先权，除非第二个受让人是善意的价值购买者，但没有通知。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 342（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（随后转让相同权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"同一权利的后续转让将撤销任何先前的可撤销转让。如果第一个转让是不可撤销的，则它具有优先权，除非第二个受让人是善意的价值购买者，但没有通知。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项对双方权利和责任的描述是正确的？","rule":"同一权利的后续转让将撤销任何先前的可撤销转让。如果第一个转让是不可撤销的，则它具有优先权，除非第二个受让人是善意的价值购买者，但没有通知。","analysis":"合同权利的转让\n\n类型可撤销保证\n\n无偿可撤销，除非： 无\n\n• 债务人已经履行\n• 交付象征转让权利的文件（例如股票证书）\n• 交付书面并签名的作业\n• 禁止反言适用 对于价值 不可撤销的转让人保证他/她：\n\n• 有权转让\n• 不受受让人未知的限制/防御的约束\n• 不会破坏/损害转让的权利 转让是将合同权利转让给另一方。如果同一权利有后续转让，这些竞争转让的优先级取决于第一个转让是否：\n• 可撤销——在这种情况下，第一个分配将自动撤销，第二个分配具有优先权或\n• 不可撤销——在这种情况下，第一个转让具有优先权，除非第二个受让人是善意的价值购买者，但没有通知第一个转让。在这里，医生口头将患者欠她女儿的 25,000 美元作为结婚礼物的索赔转让给她。该口头转让是有效的。*但是，它是可撤销的，因为它是无偿的并且不适用任何例外情况（见上表）。这意味着，当医生随后将相同的索赔分配给她的股票经纪人时，分配给女儿的分配被自动撤销。因此，女儿无权获得 25,000 美元（选择 A 和 C）。医生对股票经纪人的指派是为了价值（即部分支付预付款），因此是不可撤销的。每项价值转让都带有默示保证，即转让人不会采取任何行动来破坏或损害价值\n\n分配的权利。这位医生接受了病人所欠的 25,000 美元，却在赛马场把它弄丢了，从而违反了保证。结果，股票经纪人可以从医生那里追回 25,000 美元。然而，股票经纪人无法从患者那里收回这笔金额。原因是受让人对债务人的权利受到债务人对转让人的任何抗辩的约束。债务人（患者）向转让人（医生）付款可以作为抗辩，前提是付款是在债务人收到转让通知之前进行的（如此处所示）。因此，患者支付医生费用时无需承担风险（选择 B）。 *口头作业通常有效。然而，(1) 工资转让，(2) 土地权益转让，以及 (3) 《统一商法典》（UCC）第 9 条规定的作为担保文书的转让均需要书面形式——这些均不适用于此处。\n\n教育目标：同一权利的后续转让将撤销任何先前的可撤销转让。如果第一个转让是不可撤销的，则它具有优先权，除非第二个受让人是善意的价值购买者，但没有通知。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 342（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（随后转让相同权利）。","conclusion":"答案 D：女儿和股票经纪人都无法从病人那里得到赔偿，但股票经纪人虽然不是女儿，却可以从医生那里得到 25,000 美元的赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"revocability","ipa":"/revəkə'bɪlɪtɪ/","syllables":"re·vo·ca·bil·ity","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"n. 可撤回； 可撤销； 可解除","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“revocability”","wordForm":"名词","sourceSentence":"Assignment of contractual rights Type Revocability Warranties Gratuitous Revocable unless: None • obligor already performed • document symbolizing assigned right (eg, stock certificate) delivered • written & signed assignment delivered • promissory estoppel applies For value Irrevocable Assignor warrants that he/she: • has right to assign • is not subject to limitations/defenses unknown to assignee • will not defeat/","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"revocable","ipa":"/ɹˈɛvəkəbəl/","syllables":"re·vo·ca·ble","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 可废止的, 可取消的；[经] 可撤销的, 可废除的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“revocable”","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"A subsequent assignment of the same right revokes any prior revocable assignment.","synonyms":["revokable"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":94,"sourceQuestionPage":277,"sourceQuestionPages":[277],"sourceExplanationPages":[278,279],"sourceAnswerPages":[278,279],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-095","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A famous musician entered into a written agreement with an interior decorator, who was well known for his avant-garde designs, to design and decorate the musician's recording studio. The agreement was silent as to assignment or delegation by either party. Before beginning the work, the interior decorator sold his decorating business to a man under an agreement in which the interior decorator assigned to the man, and the man agreed to complete, the recording-studio contract. The man, a world-renowned decorator of excellent repute, advised the musician of the assignment, and supplied her with information confirming his financial responsibility and past commercial success all over the world. If the musician allows the man to perform and approves his design plan, but the man fails without legal excuse to complete the decorating as agreed, against whom does the musician have an enforceable claim for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"Either the first interior decorator, because his agreement with the man did not discharge his duty to the musician; or the man, because the musician was an intended beneficiary of the agreement between the first interior decorator and the man.","B":"The first interior decorator only, because his agreement with the man did not discharge his duty to the musician, and the man made no express promise to the musician.","C":"The man only, because the first interior decorator's duty to the musician was discharged when he obtained a decorator of excellent repute (the man) to perform the recording-studio contract.","D":"The man only, because the musician was an intended beneficiary of the agreement between the first interior decorator and the man, and the interior decorator's duty to the musician was discharged when she permitted the man to do the work and approved his design."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Nondelegable contractual duties\n\nDelegation not permitted when:\n\n• other contracting party has substantial interest in having delegating party perform (eg, in personal-services contract involving taste or special skill) or\n• delegation is prohibited by contract Obligations under a contract can generally be delegated unless:\n• the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform (eg, in a personal-services contract involving taste or a special skill) or\n\n• the delegation is prohibited by the contract (eg, the contract says \"this contract may not be assigned\").\n\nHowever, a party may consent to a delegation that would otherwise be prohibited. After a successful delegation, the delegator remains liable under the contract unless the other party agrees to a release. Here, the famous musician had a substantial interest in having the interior decorator perform the recording-studio contract—particularly since the interior decorator was well known for his avant-garde designs. As a result, the interior decorator cannot delegate his duties to the man without the musician's consent. And even if the musician does consent, the interior decorator will not be released from liability. Therefore, if the musician consents to the delegation and the man fails to complete the job, the musician can sue the interior decorator for breach. When, as here, a contracting party (the interior decorator) delegates his/her duties by forming a separate contract with the delegatee (the man), the other party to the original contract (the musician) becomes an intended beneficiary of that separate contract. The intended beneficiary can then sue the delegatee (in addition to the delegator) in the event of a breach. The musician, as the intended beneficiary of the agreement between the interior decorator and the man, can therefore sue the man for breach as well (Choices B, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: Delegation of contractual obligations is permitted unless (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the delegation is prohibited by the contract. After a delegation, the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agrees to a release.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of performance of duty).","educationalObjective":"Delegation of contractual obligations is permitted unless (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the delegation is prohibited by the contract. After a delegation, the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agrees to a release.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of performance of duty).","rule":"Delegation of contractual obligations is permitted unless (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the delegation is prohibited by the contract. After a delegation, the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agrees to a release.","irac":{"issue":"If the musician allows the man to perform and approves his design plan, but the man fails without legal excuse to complete the decorating as agreed, against whom does the musician have an enforceable claim for breach of contract?","rule":"Delegation of contractual obligations is permitted unless (1) the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform or (2) the delegation is prohibited by the contract. After a delegation, the delegating party remains liable under the contract unless the other party agrees to a release.","analysis":"Nondelegable contractual duties\n\nDelegation not permitted when:\n\n• other contracting party has substantial interest in having delegating party perform (eg, in personal-services contract involving taste or special skill) or\n• delegation is prohibited by contract Obligations under a contract can generally be delegated unless:\n• the other party to the contract has a substantial interest in having the delegating party perform (eg, in a personal-services contract involving taste or a special skill) or\n\n• the delegation is prohibited by the contract (eg, the contract says \"this contract may not be assigned\").\n\nHowever, a party may consent to a delegation that would otherwise be prohibited. After a successful delegation, the delegator remains liable under the contract unless the other party agrees to a release. Here, the famous musician had a substantial interest in having the interior decorator perform the recording-studio contract—particularly since the interior decorator was well known for his avant-garde designs. As a result, the interior decorator cannot delegate his duties to the man without the musician's consent. And even if the musician does consent, the interior decorator will not be released from liability. Therefore, if the musician consents to the delegation and the man fails to complete the job, the musician can sue the interior decorator for breach. When, as here, a contracting party (the interior decorator) delegates his/her duties by forming a separate contract with the delegatee (the man), the other party to the original contract (the musician) becomes an intended beneficiary of that separate contract. The intended beneficiary can then sue the delegatee (in addition to the delegator) in the event of a breach. The musician, as the intended beneficiary of the agreement between the interior decorator and the man, can therefore sue the man for breach as well (Choices B, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: Either the first interior decorator, because his agreement with the man did not discharge his duty to the musician; or the man, because the musician was an intended beneficiary of the agreement between the first interior decorator and the man."},"questionZh":"一位著名音乐家与一位以其前卫设计而闻名的室内设计师签订了一份书面协议，由他来设计和装饰这位音乐家的录音室。该协议没有提及任何一方的指派或授权。在开始工作之前，室内装饰师将其装饰业务出售给一名男子，根据协议，室内装饰师将其转让给该男子，而该男子同意完成录音室合同。该男子是一位享有盛誉的世界知名装饰师，他向音乐家提供了这项任务的建议，并向她提供了确认他的经济责任和过去在世界各地的商业成功的信息。如果音乐家允许该男子履行并批准其设计方案，但该男子无合法理由未能按照约定完成装修，音乐家可以向谁提出违约索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"要么是第一个室内装饰师，因为他与这个人的协议并没有履行他对音乐家的职责；或男人，因为音乐家是第一个室内装饰师和男人之间协议的预期受益人。","B":"只是第一个室内装饰师，因为他与该男子的协议并没有履行他对音乐家的义务，并且该男子没有向音乐家做出明确的承诺。","C":"仅限于该男子，因为当第一位室内装饰师获得一位声誉卓著的室内装饰师（该男子）来执行录音室合同时，他对音乐家的职责就被解除了。","D":"仅限于该男子，因为音乐家是第一个室内装饰师与该男子之间协议的预期受益人，当她允许该男子完成工作并批准他的设计时，室内装饰师对音乐家的义务就被解除了。"},"explanationZh":"不可委托的合同义务\n\n在以下情况下不允许委托：\n\n• 其他缔约方对委托方履行具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止委托 合同下的义务通常可以委托，除非：\n• 合同的另一方对委托方履行合同具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n\n• 合同禁止委托（例如，合同规定“本合同不得转让”）。\n\n然而，一方可以同意否则会被禁止的授权。成功委托后，除非另一方同意解除，否则委托人仍应承担合同规定的责任。在这里，这位著名的音乐家对让室内装饰师执行录音室合同非常感兴趣——特别是因为室内装饰师以其前卫的设计而闻名。因此，未经音乐家同意，室内装饰师不能将其职责委托给该男子。即使音乐家同意，室内装饰师也不会免除责任。因此，如果音乐家同意委托，而该男子未能完成工作，音乐家可以起诉室内装饰师违约。如此处所示，当缔约方（室内装饰师）通过与受托人（男子）签订单独的合同来委托其职责时，原始合同的另一方（音乐家）就成为该单独合同的预期受益人。如果发生违约行为，预期受益人可以起诉受委托人（除了委托人之外）。因此，音乐家作为室内装饰师与该男子之间协议的预期受益人，也可以起诉该男子违约（选项 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：允许委托合同义务，除非 (1) 合同的另一方对委托方履行具有重大利益，或 (2) 合同禁止委托。委托后，除非另一方同意免除责任，否则委托方仍应承担合同规定的责任。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行职责的委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"允许委托合同义务，除非 (1) 合同另一方对委托方履行具有重大利益，或 (2) 合同禁止委托。委托后，除非另一方同意免除责任，否则委托方仍应承担合同规定的责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果音乐家允许该男子履行并批准其设计方案，但该男子无合法理由未能按照约定完成装修，音乐家可以向谁提出违约索赔？","rule":"允许委托合同义务，除非 (1) 合同另一方对委托方履行具有重大利益，或 (2) 合同禁止委托。委托后，除非另一方同意免除责任，否则委托方仍应承担合同规定的责任。","analysis":"不可委托的合同义务\n\n在以下情况下不允许委托：\n\n• 其他缔约方对委托方履行具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n• 合同禁止委托 合同下的义务通常可以委托，除非：\n• 合同的另一方对委托方履行合同具有重大利益（例如，在涉及品味或特殊技能的个人服务合同中）或\n\n• 合同禁止委托（例如，合同规定“本合同不得转让”）。\n\n然而，一方可以同意否则会被禁止的授权。成功委托后，除非另一方同意解除，否则委托人仍应承担合同规定的责任。在这里，这位著名的音乐家对让室内装饰师执行录音室合同非常感兴趣——特别是因为室内装饰师以其前卫的设计而闻名。因此，未经音乐家同意，室内装饰师不能将其职责委托给该男子。即使音乐家同意，室内装饰师也不会免除责任。因此，如果音乐家同意委托，而该男子未能完成工作，音乐家可以起诉室内装饰师违约。如此处所示，当缔约方（室内装饰师）通过与受托人（男子）签订单独的合同来委托其职责时，原始合同的另一方（音乐家）就成为该单独合同的预期受益人。如果发生违约行为，预期受益人可以起诉受委托人（除了委托人之外）。因此，音乐家作为室内装饰师与该男子之间协议的预期受益人，也可以起诉该男子违约（选项 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：允许委托合同义务，除非 (1) 合同的另一方对委托方履行具有重大利益，或 (2) 合同禁止委托。委托后，除非另一方同意免除责任，否则委托方仍应承担合同规定的责任。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行职责的委托）。","conclusion":"答案 A：要么是第一个室内装饰师，因为他与这个人的协议并没有履行他对音乐家的职责；或男人，因为音乐家是第一个室内装饰师和男人之间协议的预期受益人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":95,"sourceQuestionPage":280,"sourceQuestionPages":[280],"sourceExplanationPages":[281,282],"sourceAnswerPages":[281,282],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-096","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Assignment & Delegation","question":"A tree-cutter who was an expert in removing branches that threaten tall buildings contracted, in a signed writing, to lift and trim branches near a building where a major conference was scheduled to occur. An exculpatory clause in the contract provided that the tree-cutter would not be liable for any physical damage to the building occurring during removal of the branches. There was also a clause providing for per diem damages if the tree-cutter did not complete performance by a specified date and a clause providing that \"time is of the essence.\" Another clause provided that any subsequent agreement for extra work under the contract must be in writing and signed by both parties. With ample time remaining under the contract for commencement and completion of his performance, the tree-cutter notified the building owner that he was selling his business to a landscaper, who was equally an expert in removing branches that threaten tall buildings, and that the landscaper had agreed to \"take over the contract.\" If the building owner refuses to accept the landscaper's services, which of the following clauses in the contract will best support the owner's contention that the tree-cutter's duties under the contract were not delegable without the owner's consent?","choices":{"A":"The exculpatory clause.","B":"The extra-work clause.","C":"The liquidated-damage clause.","D":"The \"time is of the essence\" clause."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Assignment v. Delegation\n\nAssignment Transfer of contractual rights to another (ie, assignee)\n\nDelegation Transfer of contractual duties to another (ie, delegatee)\n\nAn exculpatory clause releases a party (eg, the tree-cutter) from liability for damages caused during the execution of the contract. Such a clause would effectively release that party from liability for damages caused by a delegatee (eg, the landscaper) as well. This goes against the controlling principle that a delegating party is not released from liability for damages caused by a delegatee unless the other party to the contract (eg, the building owner) expressly agrees to a release. As a result, the exculpatory clause best supports the building owner's contention that the tree-cutter's duties were not delegable without the owner's consent. (Choice B) The extra-work clause limits the scope of work to be done, but it says nothing about who is permitted to perform that work. (Choice C) The liquidated-damage clause sets forth a fixed measure of damages for breach; it has no bearing on whether the tree-cutter can delegate his duties without the building owner's consent. (Choice D) The \"time is of the essence\" clause indicates that performance by the time specified in the contract is crucial for the building owner to receive the benefit of the bargain. But this clause does not determine whether the tree-cutter can delegate his duties without the building owner's consent.\n\nEducational objective: An exculpatory clause releases a party from liability for damages caused during the execution of the contract. This would include damages caused by a delegatee, so the party to be released cannot delegate his/her duties without the other party's consent.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of duties).","educationalObjective":"An exculpatory clause releases a party from liability for damages caused during the execution of the contract. This would include damages caused by a delegatee, so the party to be released cannot delegate his/her duties without the other party's consent.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (delegation of duties).","rule":"An exculpatory clause releases a party from liability for damages caused during the execution of the contract. This would include damages caused by a delegatee, so the party to be released cannot delegate his/her duties without the other party's consent.","irac":{"issue":"A tree-cutter who was an expert in removing branches that threaten tall buildings contracted, in a signed writing, to lift and trim branches near a building where a major conference was scheduled to occur. An exculpatory clause in the contract provided that the tree-cutter would not be liable for any physical damage to the building occurring during removal of the branches. There was also a clause providing for per diem damages if the tree-cutter did not complete performance by a specified date and a clause providing that \"time is of the essence.\" Another clause provided that any subsequent agreement for extra work under the contract must be in writing and signed by both parties. With ample time remaining under the contract for commencement and completion of his performance, the tree-cutter notified the building owner that he was selling his business to a landscaper, who was equally an expert in removing branches that threaten tall buildings, and that the landscaper had agreed to \"take over the contract.\" If the building owner refuses to accept the landscaper's services, which of the following clauses in the contract will best support the owner's contention that the tree-cutter's duties under the contract were not delegable without the owner's consent?","rule":"An exculpatory clause releases a party from liability for damages caused during the execution of the contract. This would include damages caused by a delegatee, so the party to be released cannot delegate his/her duties without the other party's consent.","analysis":"Assignment v. Delegation\n\nAssignment Transfer of contractual rights to another (ie, assignee)\n\nDelegation Transfer of contractual duties to another (ie, delegatee)\n\nAn exculpatory clause releases a party (eg, the tree-cutter) from liability for damages caused during the execution of the contract. Such a clause would effectively release that party from liability for damages caused by a delegatee (eg, the landscaper) as well. This goes against the controlling principle that a delegating party is not released from liability for damages caused by a delegatee unless the other party to the contract (eg, the building owner) expressly agrees to a release. As a result, the exculpatory clause best supports the building owner's contention that the tree-cutter's duties were not delegable without the owner's consent. (Choice B) The extra-work clause limits the scope of work to be done, but it says nothing about who is permitted to perform that work. (Choice C) The liquidated-damage clause sets forth a fixed measure of damages for breach; it has no bearing on whether the tree-cutter can delegate his duties without the building owner's consent. (Choice D) The \"time is of the essence\" clause indicates that performance by the time specified in the contract is crucial for the building owner to receive the benefit of the bargain. But this clause does not determine whether the tree-cutter can delegate his duties without the building owner's consent.","conclusion":"Answer A: The exculpatory clause."},"questionZh":"一位砍树者是清除威胁高层建筑的树枝的专家，他在一份签名的书面合同中签订了合同，负责在预定举行大型会议的建筑物附近抬起和修剪树枝。合同中的一项免责条款规定，砍伐树木的人对砍伐树枝过程中对建筑物造成的任何物理损坏不承担责任。还有一项条款规定，如果砍树者没有在指定日期之前完成工作，则按日赔偿损失，并有一项条款规定“时间至关重要”。另一项条款规定，合同项下任何后续额外工作的协议必须以书面形式并由双方签署。合同还剩下足够的时间来开始和完成他的工作，砍树者通知业主，他正在将自己的业务出售给一位园林设计师，这位园林设计师同样是清除威胁高层建筑的树枝的专家，并且园林设计师已同意“接管合同”。如果业主拒绝接受园林设计师的服务，合同中的以下哪一项条款最能支持业主的论点，即未经业主同意，不得将合同项下的砍伐树木的职责委托给他人？","choicesZh":{"A":"免责条款。","B":"加班条款。","C":"违约金条款。","D":"“时间至关重要”条款。"},"explanationZh":"转让与委托\n\n转让 将合同权利转让给另一方（即受让人）\n\n委托 将合同职责转移给另一个人（即受委托者）\n\n免责条款免除了一方（例如砍伐者）对合同执行期间造成的损害的责任。这样的条款也将有效地免除该方对受委托人（例如园林设计师）造成的损害的责任。这违反了控制原则，即除非合同另一方（例如，建筑物业主）明确同意免除责任，否则委托方不得免除受托人造成的损害的责任。因此，无罪条款最有力地支持了业主的论点，即未经业主同意，砍伐树木的职责不得委托他人。 （选项 B） 加班条款限制了要完成的工作范围，但没有说明谁可以执行该工作。 （选项C）违约金条款规定了违约赔偿的固定数额；这与砍伐树木者是否可以在未经业主同意的情况下委托其职责无关。 （选项D）“时间至关重要”条款表明，按合同规定的时间履行合同对于业主获得交易利益至关重要。但该条款并没有决定砍伐树木的人是否可以在未经业主同意的情况下委托其职责。\n\n教育目标：无罪条款免除一方对合同执行期间造成的损害的责任。这包括由受托人造成的损害，因此，未经另一方同意，被释放的一方不得委托其职责。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（职责委托）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"免责条款免除一方对合同执行期间造成的损害的责任。这包括由受托人造成的损害，因此，未经另一方同意，被释放的一方不得委托其职责。","iracZh":{"issue":"一位砍树者是清除威胁高层建筑的树枝的专家，他在一份签名的书面合同中签订了合同，负责在预定举行大型会议的建筑物附近抬起和修剪树枝。合同中的一项免责条款规定，砍伐树木的人对砍伐树枝过程中对建筑物造成的任何物理损坏不承担责任。还有一项条款规定，如果砍树者没有在指定日期之前完成工作，则按日赔偿损失，并有一项条款规定“时间至关重要”。另一项条款规定，合同项下任何后续额外工作的协议必须以书面形式并由双方签署。合同还剩下足够的时间来开始和完成他的工作，砍树者通知业主，他正在将自己的业务出售给一位园林设计师，这位园林设计师同样是清除威胁高层建筑的树枝的专家，并且园林设计师已同意“接管合同”。如果业主拒绝接受园林设计师的服务，合同中的以下哪一项条款最能支持业主的论点，即未经业主同意，不得将合同项下的砍伐树木的职责委托给他人？","rule":"免责条款免除一方对合同执行期间造成的损害的责任。这包括由受托人造成的损害，因此，未经另一方同意，被释放的一方不得委托其职责。","analysis":"转让与委托\n\n转让 将合同权利转让给另一方（即受让人）\n\n委托 将合同职责转移给另一个人（即受委托者）\n\n免责条款免除了一方（例如砍伐者）对合同执行期间造成的损害的责任。这样的条款也将有效地免除该方对受委托人（例如园林设计师）造成的损害的责任。这违反了控制原则，即除非合同另一方（例如，建筑物业主）明确同意免除责任，否则委托方不得免除受托人造成的损害的责任。因此，无罪条款最有力地支持了业主的论点，即未经业主同意，砍伐树木的职责不得委托他人。 （选项 B） 加班条款限制了要完成的工作范围，但没有说明谁可以执行该工作。 （选项C）违约金条款规定了违约赔偿的固定数额；这与砍伐树木者是否可以在未经业主同意的情况下委托其职责无关。 （选项D）“时间至关重要”条款表明，按合同规定的时间履行合同对于业主获得交易利益至关重要。但该条款并没有决定砍伐树木的人是否可以在未经业主同意的情况下委托其职责。\n\n教育目标：无罪条款免除一方对合同执行期间造成的损害的责任。这包括由受托人造成的损害，因此，未经另一方同意，被释放的一方不得委托其职责。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 318 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（职责委托）。","conclusion":"答案 A：免责条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"benefit of the bargain","ipa":"/bˈɛnəfɪt ˈəv ðə bˈɑɹɡən/","syllables":"ben·e·fit of the bar·gain","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"交易期待利益：合同适当履行时守约方应取得的利益","contextualMeaning":"交易期待利益：合同适当履行时守约方应取得的利益","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) The \"time is of the essence\" clause indicates that performance by the time specified in the contract is crucial for the building owner to receive the benefit of the bargain.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":96,"sourceQuestionPage":283,"sourceQuestionPages":[283],"sourceExplanationPages":[284],"sourceAnswerPages":[284],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-097","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"In a written contract, an architect agreed to draw up the plans for and to supervise construction of a client's new house. In return, the client agreed to pay the architect a fee of $10,000 to be paid upon the house's completion. After completion, the client claimed erroneously but in good faith that the architect's plans were defective. The client orally offered to pay the architect $7,500 in full settlement of the claim for the fee. The architect orally accepted that offer despite the fact that the reasonable value of his services was in fact $10,000. The client paid the architect $7,500 pursuant to their agreement. The architect subsequently sued the client for the remaining $2,500. In a preliminary finding, the trier of fact found that there were no defects in the architect's plans. Will the architect be likely to prevail in his action against the client for $2,500?","choices":{"A":"No, because the architect's acceptance of partial payment constituted a novation.","B":"No, because the architect's promise to accept $7,500 became binding when the client made the payment.","C":"Yes, because payment of $7,500 cannot furnish consideration for the architect's promise to surrender his claim.","D":"Yes, because the oral agreement to modify the written contract is not enforceable."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Exceptions to preexisting-duty rule\n\nMain rule Promise to perform (or performance of) preexisting duty is not consideration\n\nCommon-law New/altered New duty added or preexisting duty altered (eg, exceptions consideration acceleration of due date)\n\nRatification of New promise to perform voidable duty (eg, upon voidable duty reaching age of majority)\n\nHonest dispute Scope of duty honestly disputed\n\nUnexpected event Fair & equitable modification in view of unanticipated circumstances\n\nRenewed promise Renewed promise to pay preexisting debt despite technical defense (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) if written or partially performed\n\nThird party Third party offers additional promise that is contingent on performance of preexisting duty\n\nUCC exception Modification Modification sought in good faith—ie, honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nA settlement agreement must be supported by consideration. There is no consideration if one party merely promises to perform or performs a preexisting duty. But there is consideration if the scope of that duty is disputed in good faith. For example, a creditor can promise to settle an honestly disputed debt for a lesser amount than billed. If the debtor then pays that lesser amount, the creditor's promise becomes binding and the creditor cannot sue for the remainder of the billed amount even if it was actually owed. Here, the architect contracted to draw up the plans for and supervise the construction of the client's house for $10,000. After completion, the client claimed that the plans were defective and offered to settle for $7,500. Although there were no defects in the plans, the architect's promise to surrender his claim in exchange for $7,500 was supported by consideration since the client disputed the architect's performance in good faith (Choice C). And since this promise became binding when the client made the payment, the architect is unlikely to prevail.\n\n(Choice A) The architect's acceptance of partial payment was not a novation—ie, a new contract that substitutes a new party for one of the original parties. Instead, it was an accord and satisfaction—ie, the tender and acceptance of an alternate performance that discharges a contractual obligation. (Choice D) The architect and client were attempting to settle a dispute, not modify their contract. Oral settlement agreements are generally enforceable if their terms are sufficiently precise. And even when a writing is required under the statute of frauds, an oral settlement becomes enforceable when one party performs (eg, by making the settlement payment—as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A claim can be settled by entering a settlement agreement as long as it is supported by consideration. There is no consideration if one party merely promises to perform (or performs) a preexisting duty. But there is consideration if the scope of that duty is disputed in good faith.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","educationalObjective":"A claim can be settled by entering a settlement agreement as long as it is supported by consideration. There is no consideration if one party merely promises to perform (or performs) a preexisting duty. But there is consideration if the scope of that duty is disputed in good faith.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","rule":"A claim can be settled by entering a settlement agreement as long as it is supported by consideration. There is no consideration if one party merely promises to perform (or performs) a preexisting duty. But there is consideration if the scope of that duty is disputed in good faith.","irac":{"issue":"Will the architect be likely to prevail in his action against the client for $2,500?","rule":"A claim can be settled by entering a settlement agreement as long as it is supported by consideration. There is no consideration if one party merely promises to perform (or performs) a preexisting duty. But there is consideration if the scope of that duty is disputed in good faith.","analysis":"Exceptions to preexisting-duty rule\n\nMain rule Promise to perform (or performance of) preexisting duty is not consideration\n\nCommon-law New/altered New duty added or preexisting duty altered (eg, exceptions consideration acceleration of due date)\n\nRatification of New promise to perform voidable duty (eg, upon voidable duty reaching age of majority)\n\nHonest dispute Scope of duty honestly disputed\n\nUnexpected event Fair & equitable modification in view of unanticipated circumstances\n\nRenewed promise Renewed promise to pay preexisting debt despite technical defense (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) if written or partially performed\n\nThird party Third party offers additional promise that is contingent on performance of preexisting duty\n\nUCC exception Modification Modification sought in good faith—ie, honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nA settlement agreement must be supported by consideration. There is no consideration if one party merely promises to perform or performs a preexisting duty. But there is consideration if the scope of that duty is disputed in good faith. For example, a creditor can promise to settle an honestly disputed debt for a lesser amount than billed. If the debtor then pays that lesser amount, the creditor's promise becomes binding and the creditor cannot sue for the remainder of the billed amount even if it was actually owed. Here, the architect contracted to draw up the plans for and supervise the construction of the client's house for $10,000. After completion, the client claimed that the plans were defective and offered to settle for $7,500. Although there were no defects in the plans, the architect's promise to surrender his claim in exchange for $7,500 was supported by consideration since the client disputed the architect's performance in good faith (Choice C). And since this promise became binding when the client made the payment, the architect is unlikely to prevail.\n\n(Choice A) The architect's acceptance of partial payment was not a novation—ie, a new contract that substitutes a new party for one of the original parties. Instead, it was an accord and satisfaction—ie, the tender and acceptance of an alternate performance that discharges a contractual obligation. (Choice D) The architect and client were attempting to settle a dispute, not modify their contract. Oral settlement agreements are generally enforceable if their terms are sufficiently precise. And even when a writing is required under the statute of frauds, an oral settlement becomes enforceable when one party performs (eg, by making the settlement payment—as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the architect's promise to accept $7,500 became binding when the client made the payment."},"questionZh":"在一份书面合同中，建筑师同意为客户的新房制定计划并监督其施工。作为回报，客户同意向建筑师支付 10,000 美元的费用，并在房屋竣工后支付。竣工后，客户善意地错误地声称建筑师的计划有缺陷。客户口头提出向建筑师支付 7,500 美元，以全额解决索赔费用。尽管事实上他的服务的合理价值是 10,000 美元，但建筑师还是口头接受了这一报价。客户根据协议向建筑师支付了 7,500 美元。建筑师随后起诉客户，要求赔偿剩余的 2,500 美元。在初步调查中，事实审判者发现建筑师的计划没有缺陷。建筑师在针对客户的诉讼中是否有可能胜诉并支付 2,500 美元？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为建筑师接受部分付款构成了更新。","B":"不会，因为建筑师接受 7,500 美元的承诺在客户付款后就具有约束力。","C":"是的，因为支付 7,500 美元并不能作为建筑师放弃索赔的承诺的对价。","D":"是的，因为口头协议修改书面合同是不具有强制力的。"},"explanationZh":"既存义务规则的例外情况\n\n主要规则 承诺履行（或履行）先前存在的职责不构成对价\n\n普通法 新/更改 添加新义务或更改先前存在的义务（例如，考虑加速到期日的例外情况）\n\n批准履行可撤销义务的新承诺（例如，在可撤销义务达到成年年龄时）\n\n诚实争议 诚实争议的职责范围\n\n意外事件 鉴于意外情况进行公平公正的修改\n\n更新的承诺 如果书面或部分履行，则即使有技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），仍将支付先前存在的债务\n\n第三方 第三方提供额外承诺，该承诺取决于履行先前存在的义务\n\nUCC 例外修改 真诚地寻求修改，即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n和解协议必须得到对价的支持。如果一方仅承诺履行或履行一项先前存在的义务，则不予考虑。但如果对该义务的范围存在善意争议，则需要考虑。例如，债权人可以承诺以低于账单的金额解决诚实有争议的债务。如果债务人随后支付了较少的金额，则债权人的承诺具有约束力，即使实际欠款，债权人也不能就账单金额的剩余部分提起诉讼。在这里，建筑师以 10,000 美元的价格签订合同，为客户的房屋制定计划并监督施工。完成后，客户声称该计划有缺陷，并提出以 7,500 美元和解。尽管计划没有缺陷，但建筑师承诺放弃索赔以换取 7,500 美元，但由于客户对建筑师的善意表现提出异议（选项 C），因此得到了对价的支持。由于这一承诺在客户付款后就具有约束力，因此建筑师不太可能获胜。\n\n（选择A） 建筑师接受部分付款并不是更新，即用新的一方取代原一方的新合同。相反，它是一种协议和满足——即，投标并接受替代履行以履行合同义务。 （选项 D）建筑师和客户试图解决争端，而不是修改合同。如果口头和解协议的条款足够明确，则通常可以强制执行。即使根据欺诈法要求书面和解，当一方履行义务时，口头和解也变得可执行（例如，通过支付和解付款——如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：只要有对价支持，就可以通过签订和解协议来解决索赔。如果一方仅承诺履行（或履行）一项先前存在的义务，则不予考虑。但如果对该义务的范围存在善意争议，则需要考虑。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只要有对价支持，索赔就可以通过签订和解协议来解决。如果一方仅承诺履行（或履行）一项先前存在的义务，则不予考虑。但如果对该义务的范围存在善意争议，则需要考虑。","iracZh":{"issue":"建筑师在针对客户的诉讼中是否有可能胜诉并支付 2,500 美元？","rule":"只要有对价支持，索赔就可以通过签订和解协议来解决。如果一方仅承诺履行（或履行）一项先前存在的义务，则不予考虑。但如果对该义务的范围存在善意争议，则需要考虑。","analysis":"既存义务规则的例外情况\n\n主要规则 承诺履行（或履行）先前存在的职责不构成对价\n\n普通法 新/更改 添加新义务或更改先前存在的义务（例如，考虑加速到期日的例外情况）\n\n批准履行可撤销义务的新承诺（例如，在可撤销义务达到成年年龄时）\n\n诚实争议 诚实争议的职责范围\n\n意外事件 鉴于意外情况进行公平公正的修改\n\n更新的承诺 如果书面或部分履行，则即使有技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），仍将支付先前存在的债务\n\n第三方 第三方提供额外承诺，该承诺取决于履行先前存在的义务\n\nUCC 例外修改 真诚地寻求修改，即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n和解协议必须得到对价的支持。如果一方仅承诺履行或履行一项先前存在的义务，则不予考虑。但如果对该义务的范围存在善意争议，则需要考虑。例如，债权人可以承诺以低于账单的金额解决诚实有争议的债务。如果债务人随后支付了较少的金额，则债权人的承诺具有约束力，即使实际欠款，债权人也不能就账单金额的剩余部分提起诉讼。在这里，建筑师以 10,000 美元的价格签订合同，为客户的房屋制定计划并监督施工。完成后，客户声称该计划有缺陷，并提出以 7,500 美元和解。尽管计划没有缺陷，但建筑师承诺放弃索赔以换取 7,500 美元，但由于客户对建筑师的善意表现提出异议（选项 C），因此得到了对价的支持。由于这一承诺在客户付款后就具有约束力，因此建筑师不太可能获胜。\n\n（选择A） 建筑师接受部分付款并不是更新，即用新的一方取代原一方的新合同。相反，它是一种协议和满足——即，投标并接受替代履行以履行合同义务。 （选项 D）建筑师和客户试图解决争端，而不是修改合同。如果口头和解协议的条款足够明确，则通常可以强制执行。即使根据欺诈法要求书面和解，当一方履行义务时，口头和解也变得可执行（例如，通过支付和解付款——如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：只要有对价支持，就可以通过签订和解协议来解决索赔。如果一方仅承诺履行（或履行）一项先前存在的义务，则不予考虑。但如果对该义务的范围存在善意争议，则需要考虑。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为建筑师接受 7,500 美元的承诺在客户付款后就具有约束力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"accord and satisfaction","ipa":"/ʌkˈɔɹd ənd sˌætʌsfˈækʃən/","syllables":"ac·cord and sat·is·fac·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"和解与清偿：以新履行替代并清偿原争议债务","contextualMeaning":"和解与清偿：以新履行替代并清偿原争议债务","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Instead, it was an accord and satisfaction—ie, the tender and acceptance of an alternate performance that discharges a contractual obligation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"preexisting duty rule","ipa":"/pɹˈiɪɡzˈɪstɪŋ dˈuti ɹˈul/","syllables":"pre·ex·ist·ing duty rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"既存义务规则：仅履行已有义务通常不构成新约因","contextualMeaning":"既存义务规则：仅履行已有义务通常不构成新约因","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"In a written contract, an architect agreed to draw up the plans for and to supervise construction of a client's new house.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":97,"sourceQuestionPage":285,"sourceQuestionPages":[285],"sourceExplanationPages":[286,287],"sourceAnswerPages":[286,287],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-098","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A notorious spendthrift who was usually broke for that reason received the following letter from his uncle, a wealthy and prudent man: \"I understand you're in financial difficulties again. I promise to give you $5,000 on your birthday next month, but you'd better use it wisely or you'll never get another dime from me.\" The spendthrift thereupon signed a contract with a car dealer to purchase a $40,000 automobile and to make a $5,000 down payment on the day after his birthday. If the spendthrift sues the uncle for $5,000 after the uncle learned of the car-purchase contract and then repudiated his promise, which of the following is the uncle's best defense?","choices":{"A":"A promise to make a gift in the future is not enforceable.","B":"Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless injustice can be avoided only by such enforcement.","C":"Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless that reliance also results in an economic benefit to the promisor.","D":"Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless the value of the promised gift is substantially equivalent to the promisee's loss by reliance."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Promises binding without consideration\n\nPay debt barred by Promise to pay debt that would be enforceable absent technical technical defense defense to enforcement (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy)\n\nPerform voidable Promise to perform voidable duty, provided that new promise is duty not also voidable (eg, minor reaffirms promise to perform contract after reaching age of majority)\n\nPay for material Promise to pay for material benefits received in the past & not benefits received intended as a gift (eg, promise to pay for emergency services previously received)\n\nPromissory Any promise (eg, promise of future gift) if: estoppel\n• promisor should reasonably expect promisee to rely on promise\n• promisee relies on promise to his/her detriment and\n• injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of promise To be enforceable, a contract must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. A promise to make a gift—eg, the uncle's promise to give the spendthrift $5,000 on his birthday without asking for anything in return—does not involve a bargained-for exchange and is generally unenforceable. But under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a gift promise may be enforceable if:\n• the promisor should reasonably expect the promisee to rely on the promise\n• the promisee relies on the promise to his/her detriment and\n• injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of the promise (Choice A). Here, the uncle promised to gift the spendthrift $5,000 on his birthday. The uncle likely should have expected the spendthrift to rely on that promise since the uncle knew about the spendthrift's financial difficulties. And the spendthrift did rely on that promise when he entered into a car-purchase contract and agreed to make a $5,000 down payment the day after his birthday. As a result, the uncle's best defense is that the spendthrift's reliance on the promise does not make it enforceable unless injustice can be avoided only by such enforcement. (Choice C) Promissory estoppel (also known as detrimental reliance) does not require proof that the promisee's reliance resulted in an economic benefit to the promisor. (Choice D) The value of the promised gift need not be substantially equivalent to the promisee's loss by reliance for promissory estoppel to apply. However, the promisee's\n\nremedy is limited to actual loss suffered in reliance on the promise—not necessarily the full value of the promise.\n\nEducational objective: A party's promise to make a gift is enforceable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel if (1) the promisor should reasonably expect the promisee to rely on that promise, (2) the promisee detrimentally relies on that promise, and (3) injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of that promise.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promissory estoppel).","educationalObjective":"A party's promise to make a gift is enforceable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel if (1) the promisor should reasonably expect the promisee to rely on that promise, (2) the promisee detrimentally relies on that promise, and (3) injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of that promise.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promissory estoppel).","rule":"A party's promise to make a gift is enforceable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel if (1) the promisor should reasonably expect the promisee to rely on that promise, (2) the promisee detrimentally relies on that promise, and (3) injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of that promise.","irac":{"issue":"If the spendthrift sues the uncle for $5,000 after the uncle learned of the car-purchase contract and then repudiated his promise, which of the following is the uncle's best defense?","rule":"A party's promise to make a gift is enforceable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel if (1) the promisor should reasonably expect the promisee to rely on that promise, (2) the promisee detrimentally relies on that promise, and (3) injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of that promise.","analysis":"Promises binding without consideration\n\nPay debt barred by Promise to pay debt that would be enforceable absent technical technical defense defense to enforcement (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy)\n\nPerform voidable Promise to perform voidable duty, provided that new promise is duty not also voidable (eg, minor reaffirms promise to perform contract after reaching age of majority)\n\nPay for material Promise to pay for material benefits received in the past & not benefits received intended as a gift (eg, promise to pay for emergency services previously received)\n\nPromissory Any promise (eg, promise of future gift) if: estoppel\n• promisor should reasonably expect promisee to rely on promise\n• promisee relies on promise to his/her detriment and\n• injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of promise To be enforceable, a contract must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. A promise to make a gift—eg, the uncle's promise to give the spendthrift $5,000 on his birthday without asking for anything in return—does not involve a bargained-for exchange and is generally unenforceable. But under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a gift promise may be enforceable if:\n• the promisor should reasonably expect the promisee to rely on the promise\n• the promisee relies on the promise to his/her detriment and\n• injustice can be avoided only by enforcement of the promise (Choice A). Here, the uncle promised to gift the spendthrift $5,000 on his birthday. The uncle likely should have expected the spendthrift to rely on that promise since the uncle knew about the spendthrift's financial difficulties. And the spendthrift did rely on that promise when he entered into a car-purchase contract and agreed to make a $5,000 down payment the day after his birthday. As a result, the uncle's best defense is that the spendthrift's reliance on the promise does not make it enforceable unless injustice can be avoided only by such enforcement. (Choice C) Promissory estoppel (also known as detrimental reliance) does not require proof that the promisee's reliance resulted in an economic benefit to the promisor. (Choice D) The value of the promised gift need not be substantially equivalent to the promisee's loss by reliance for promissory estoppel to apply. However, the promisee's\n\nremedy is limited to actual loss suffered in reliance on the promise—not necessarily the full value of the promise.","conclusion":"Answer B: Reliance by the promisee on a promise to make a future gift does not make the promise enforceable unless injustice can be avoided only by such enforcement."},"questionZh":"一个臭名昭著的挥霍无度的人经常因此而破产，他收到了他的叔叔的一封信，他是一个富有而谨慎的人：“我知道你又陷入了经济困难。我保证下个月你生日时给你5000美元，但你最好明智地使用它，否则你再也不会从我这里得到一毛钱了。”于是，这位挥霍无度的人与一家汽车经销商签订了一份合同，购买一辆价值 40,000 美元的汽车，并在生日后的第二天支付 5,000 美元的首付。如果在叔叔得知购车合同并违背承诺后，挥霍者起诉叔叔要求 5,000 美元，以下哪项是叔叔最好的辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"未来赠送礼物的承诺是不可强制执行的。","B":"受诺人对未来赠送礼物的承诺的依赖并不会使该承诺具有可执行性，除非只有通过这种执行才能避免不公正。","C":"受诺人对未来赠送礼物的承诺的依赖并不会使该承诺具有可执行性，除非这种依赖也给承诺人带来了经济利益。","D":"受诺人对做出未来礼物的承诺的信赖并不使该承诺具有可执行性，除非所承诺的礼物的价值基本上相当于受诺人因信赖而造成的损失。"},"explanationZh":"承诺无需约因即可具有约束力\n\n支付被禁止的债务 承诺支付在没有技术防御的情况下可以强制执行的债务（例如，诉讼时效、破产）\n\n履行可撤销的承诺以履行可撤销的义务，前提是新的承诺是不可撤销的义务（例如，未成年人重申在达到成年年龄后履行合同的承诺）\n\n支付物质费用 承诺支付过去收到的物质利益，而不是收到的作为礼物的利益（例如，承诺支付以前收到的紧急服务费用）\n\n承诺 任何承诺（例如，未来礼物的承诺），如果： 禁止反言\n• 承诺者应合理地期望受诺者信赖承诺\n• 受诺人依赖承诺对他/她造成损害，并且\n• 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正 为了使合同具有可执行性，合同必须得到对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。赠送礼物的承诺——例如，叔叔承诺在挥霍者生日时送给他 5,000 美元而不要求任何回报——不涉及讨价还价的交换，并且通常无法执行。但根据禁止反言原则，在以下情况下，礼物承诺可能是可执行的：\n• 承诺人应合理预期受诺人会信赖该承诺\n• 受诺人依赖该承诺而对其造成损害，并且\n• 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正（选择A）。在这里，叔叔答应在这位挥霍无度的人生日时赠送他 5000 美元。叔叔可能应该期望挥霍者会依赖这一承诺，因为叔叔知道挥霍者的财务困难。当这位挥霍无度的人签订了一份汽车购买合同并同意在生日后的第二天支付 5,000 美元的首付款时，他确实依赖于这一承诺。因此，叔叔最好的辩护是，挥霍者对承诺的依赖并不能使其具有可执行性，除非只有通过这种执行才能避免不公正。 （选择 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）（也称为有害信赖）不需要证明受诺人的信赖给承诺人带来了经济利益。 （选项 D） 承诺礼物的价值不必实质上等于受诺人因信赖禁止反言而造成的损失。然而，受许诺人的\n\n补救措施仅限于因依赖承诺而遭受的实际损失，而不一定是承诺的全部价值。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 承诺人应合理地预期受诺人会依赖该承诺，(2) 受诺人不利地依赖该承诺，并且 (3) 只有通过执行该承诺才能避免不公正，则根据禁止反言原则，一方作出的赠送礼物的承诺是可执行的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，一方做出的赠与承诺是可以强制执行的，如果 (1) 承诺人应该合理地期望受诺人依赖该承诺，(2) 受诺人不利地依赖该承诺，以及 (3) 只有通过执行该承诺才能避免不公正。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果在叔叔得知购车合同并违背承诺后，挥霍者起诉叔叔要求 5,000 美元，以下哪项是叔叔最好的辩护？","rule":"根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，一方做出的赠与承诺是可以强制执行的，如果 (1) 承诺人应该合理地期望受诺人依赖该承诺，(2) 受诺人不利地依赖该承诺，以及 (3) 只有通过执行该承诺才能避免不公正。","analysis":"承诺无需约因即可具有约束力\n\n支付被禁止的债务 承诺支付在没有技术防御的情况下可以强制执行的债务（例如，诉讼时效、破产）\n\n履行可撤销的承诺以履行可撤销的义务，前提是新的承诺是不可撤销的义务（例如，未成年人重申在达到成年年龄后履行合同的承诺）\n\n支付物质费用 承诺支付过去收到的物质利益，而不是收到的作为礼物的利益（例如，承诺支付以前收到的紧急服务费用）\n\n承诺 任何承诺（例如，未来礼物的承诺），如果： 禁止反言\n• 承诺者应合理地期望受诺者信赖承诺\n• 受诺人依赖承诺对他/她造成损害，并且\n• 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正 为了使合同具有可执行性，合同必须得到对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。赠送礼物的承诺——例如，叔叔承诺在挥霍者生日时送给他 5,000 美元而不要求任何回报——不涉及讨价还价的交换，并且通常无法执行。但根据禁止反言原则，在以下情况下，礼物承诺可能是可执行的：\n• 承诺人应合理预期受诺人会信赖该承诺\n• 受诺人依赖该承诺而对其造成损害，并且\n• 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正（选择A）。在这里，叔叔答应在这位挥霍无度的人生日时赠送他 5000 美元。叔叔可能应该期望挥霍者会依赖这一承诺，因为叔叔知道挥霍者的财务困难。当这位挥霍无度的人签订了一份汽车购买合同并同意在生日后的第二天支付 5,000 美元的首付款时，他确实依赖于这一承诺。因此，叔叔最好的辩护是，挥霍者对承诺的依赖并不能使其具有可执行性，除非只有通过这种执行才能避免不公正。 （选择 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）（也称为有害信赖）不需要证明受诺人的信赖给承诺人带来了经济利益。 （选项 D） 承诺礼物的价值不必实质上等于受诺人因信赖禁止反言而造成的损失。然而，受许诺人的\n\n补救措施仅限于因依赖承诺而遭受的实际损失，而不一定是承诺的全部价值。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 承诺人应合理地预期受诺人会依赖该承诺，(2) 受诺人不利地依赖该承诺，并且 (3) 只有通过执行该承诺才能避免不公正，则根据禁止反言原则，一方作出的赠送礼物的承诺是可执行的。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））。","conclusion":"答案 B：受诺人对未来赠送礼物的承诺的依赖并不会使该承诺具有可执行性，除非只有通过这种执行才能避免不公正。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":98,"sourceQuestionPage":288,"sourceQuestionPages":[288],"sourceExplanationPages":[289,290],"sourceAnswerPages":[289,290],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-099","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A nonprofit organization that ran an after-school baseball program for at-risk elementary school students was fundraising for the purchase of a vacant lot to construct a baseball diamond. An executive, a well-known philanthropist, mailed a signed letter to the organization. In the letter, the executive pledged to give the organization $200,000 for the after-school baseball program. The organization accepted the letter. Shortly thereafter, the executive died. The organization filed a claim against the executive's estate for the $200,000 pledged in the letter. Is the organization likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a promise to make a gift at some time in the future is not legally enforceable.","B":"No, because an agreement to contribute money for a specific purpose is only binding when it is supported by consideration.","C":"Yes, because the estate is estopped from denying the executive's charitable contribution to the organization.","D":"Yes, because the organization's acceptance of the executive's promise established bargained-for consideration."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A promise to make a gift is generally unenforceable because it does not involve the bargained-for consideration required to form a valid contract. However, most courts will enforce a charitable subscription, which is a promise to contribute money or property to a charitable institution. They typically do so on promissory estoppel grounds, but this is somewhat of a legal fiction because detrimental reliance—the key to promissory estoppel—is not required. It is enough that the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise.* Here, the executive pledged to give the nonprofit organization $200,000 for its after-school baseball program, and he should have reasonably expected the organization to rely on his promise. Since the promise was a charitable subscription, the court can enforce it without proof that the organization detrimentally relied on it—eg, by purchasing the lot. Therefore, the organization is likely to prevail because the executive's estate is estopped from denying his charitable contribution to the organization. *Similarly, courts will enforce a gift promise made in anticipation of a marriage (ie, marriage settlement) without proof of detrimental reliance. The reason is that marriages— like charitable institutions—are deemed socially useful and desirable, so promises made to benefit or encourage them should be enforced. (Choice A) A promise to make a gift at some time in the future is legally enforceable if the elements of promissory estoppel are satisfied. (Choice B) Most courts reject the view that a charitable organization's promise to use a monetary gift for a specific purpose is sufficient consideration to form a binding contract. But here, the lack of consideration does not prevent the organization from enforcing the executive's promise because promissory estoppel applies. (Choice D) A promise to make a gift does not involve bargained-for consideration. Therefore, the organization's acceptance of the executive's promise did not establish a bargained-for exchange.\n\nEducational objective: Charitable subscriptions—ie, promises to contribute money or property to a charitable institution—are enforceable on promissory estoppel grounds without proof of detrimental reliance. All that is needed is proof that the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90(2) (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (charitable subscription binding without proof of detrimental reliance).","educationalObjective":"Charitable subscriptions—ie, promises to contribute money or property to a charitable institution—are enforceable on promissory estoppel grounds without proof of detrimental reliance. All that is needed is proof that the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90(2) (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (charitable subscription binding without proof of detrimental reliance).","rule":"Charitable subscriptions—ie, promises to contribute money or property to a charitable institution—are enforceable on promissory estoppel grounds without proof of detrimental reliance. All that is needed is proof that the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise.","irac":{"issue":"Is the organization likely to prevail?","rule":"Charitable subscriptions—ie, promises to contribute money or property to a charitable institution—are enforceable on promissory estoppel grounds without proof of detrimental reliance. All that is needed is proof that the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise.","analysis":"A promise to make a gift is generally unenforceable because it does not involve the bargained-for consideration required to form a valid contract. However, most courts will enforce a charitable subscription, which is a promise to contribute money or property to a charitable institution. They typically do so on promissory estoppel grounds, but this is somewhat of a legal fiction because detrimental reliance—the key to promissory estoppel—is not required. It is enough that the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise.* Here, the executive pledged to give the nonprofit organization $200,000 for its after-school baseball program, and he should have reasonably expected the organization to rely on his promise. Since the promise was a charitable subscription, the court can enforce it without proof that the organization detrimentally relied on it—eg, by purchasing the lot. Therefore, the organization is likely to prevail because the executive's estate is estopped from denying his charitable contribution to the organization. *Similarly, courts will enforce a gift promise made in anticipation of a marriage (ie, marriage settlement) without proof of detrimental reliance. The reason is that marriages— like charitable institutions—are deemed socially useful and desirable, so promises made to benefit or encourage them should be enforced. (Choice A) A promise to make a gift at some time in the future is legally enforceable if the elements of promissory estoppel are satisfied. (Choice B) Most courts reject the view that a charitable organization's promise to use a monetary gift for a specific purpose is sufficient consideration to form a binding contract. But here, the lack of consideration does not prevent the organization from enforcing the executive's promise because promissory estoppel applies. (Choice D) A promise to make a gift does not involve bargained-for consideration. Therefore, the organization's acceptance of the executive's promise did not establish a bargained-for exchange.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the estate is estopped from denying the executive's charitable contribution to the organization."},"questionZh":"一家为高危小学生开展课后棒球项目的非营利组织正在筹款购买一块空地来建造棒球场。一位知名慈善家的高管向该组织邮寄了一封签名信。在信中，该高管承诺为该组织提供 20 万美元用于课后棒球项目。该组织接受了这封信。此后不久，这位高管就去世了。该组织向该高管的遗产提出索赔，要求赔偿信中承诺的 20 万美元。该组织有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在未来某个时间赠送礼物的承诺在法律上不具有强制执行力。","B":"不可以，因为为特定目的捐款的协议只有在得到对价支持的情况下才具有约束力。","C":"是的，因为遗产委员会不得否认高管对组织的慈善捐款。","D":"是的，因为组织对高管承诺的接受是经过讨价还价的。"},"explanationZh":"赠送礼物的承诺通常无法执行，因为它不涉及形成有效合同所需的讨价还价对价。然而，大多数法院会强制执行慈善捐款，即向慈善机构捐赠金钱或财产的承诺。他们通常以禁止反言为由这样做，但这在某种程度上是一种法律虚构，因为不需要有害的依赖（禁止反言的关键）。承诺者合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖就足够了。*在这里，高管承诺向非营利组织提供 200,000 美元用于其课后棒球项目，他应该合理预期该组织会依赖他的承诺。由于该承诺是慈善捐款，法院可以强制执行，而无需证明该组织有害地依赖该承诺（例如，通过购买该批次）。因此，该组织很可能获胜，因为该高管的遗产不能否认他对该组织的慈善捐款。 *同样，法院将强制执行在预期结婚时做出的赠与承诺（即婚姻和解），而无需证明存在有害依赖。原因是婚姻——就像慈善机构一样——被认为对社会有用且令人向往，因此应该执行那些有益或鼓励婚姻的承诺。 （选项 A）如果满足禁止反言的要素，则在未来某个时间赠送礼物的承诺在法律上是可执行的。 （选择B） 大多数法院不同意这样的观点：慈善组织承诺将金钱礼物用于特定目的，足以构成具有约束力的合同。但在这里，缺乏约因并不妨碍组织执行高管的承诺，因为禁止反言适用。 （选项 D） 赠送礼物的承诺不涉及讨价还价。因此，组织接受高管的承诺并没有建立讨价还价的交换。\n\n教育目标：慈善捐款（即向慈善机构捐赠金钱或财产的承诺）可以基于禁止反言的理由强制执行，而无需证明存在有害依赖。所需要的只是证明承诺人合理地预期会引起对承诺的依赖。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90(2)（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（慈善认购具有约束力，无需证明有害信赖）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"慈善捐款——即向慈善机构捐赠金钱或财产的承诺——可以基于禁止反言的理由强制执行，而无需证明存在有害依赖。所需要的只是证明承诺人合理地预期会引起对承诺的依赖。","iracZh":{"issue":"该组织有可能获胜吗？","rule":"慈善捐款——即向慈善机构捐赠金钱或财产的承诺——可以基于禁止反言的理由强制执行，而无需证明存在有害依赖。所需要的只是证明承诺人合理地预期会引起对承诺的依赖。","analysis":"赠送礼物的承诺通常无法执行，因为它不涉及形成有效合同所需的讨价还价对价。然而，大多数法院会强制执行慈善捐款，即向慈善机构捐赠金钱或财产的承诺。他们通常以禁止反言为由这样做，但这在某种程度上是一种法律虚构，因为不需要有害的依赖（禁止反言的关键）。承诺者合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖就足够了。*在这里，高管承诺向非营利组织提供 200,000 美元用于其课后棒球项目，他应该合理预期该组织会依赖他的承诺。由于该承诺是慈善捐款，法院可以强制执行，而无需证明该组织有害地依赖该承诺（例如，通过购买该批次）。因此，该组织很可能获胜，因为该高管的遗产不能否认他对该组织的慈善捐款。 *同样，法院将强制执行在预期结婚时做出的赠与承诺（即婚姻和解），而无需证明存在有害依赖。原因是婚姻——就像慈善机构一样——被认为对社会有用且令人向往，因此应该执行那些有益或鼓励婚姻的承诺。 （选项 A）如果满足禁止反言的要素，则在未来某个时间赠送礼物的承诺在法律上是可执行的。 （选择B） 大多数法院不同意这样的观点：慈善组织承诺将金钱礼物用于特定目的，足以构成具有约束力的合同。但在这里，缺乏约因并不妨碍组织执行高管的承诺，因为禁止反言适用。 （选项 D） 赠送礼物的承诺不涉及讨价还价。因此，组织接受高管的承诺并没有建立讨价还价的交换。\n\n教育目标：慈善捐款（即向慈善机构捐赠金钱或财产的承诺）可以基于禁止反言的理由强制执行，而无需证明存在有害依赖。所需要的只是证明承诺人合理地预期会引起对承诺的依赖。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90(2)（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（慈善认购具有约束力，无需证明有害信赖）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为遗产委员会不得否认高管对组织的慈善捐款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-59c44b73b2761074"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":99,"sourceQuestionPage":291,"sourceQuestionPages":[291],"sourceExplanationPages":[292,293],"sourceAnswerPages":[292,293],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-100","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A man moved into his widowed grandfather's farmhouse. At the time, the man worked as a power plant operator earning a substantial salary plus benefits. Nevertheless, the man left that job to farm his grandfather's land after the grandfather orally promised the man that if he did so, he would inherit the farm. The man borrowed money to purchase farm equipment, cleared the land, installed irrigation, and planted crops. The grandfather told neighbors and family members that the farm belonged to the man. Fifteen years later, the grandfather died testate. The grandfather's will specifically devised the farm to his oldest son. The man immediately filed a complaint seeking legal title to the farm. Is the man likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because contracts for the sale or transfer of land fall under the statute of frauds and must be in writing in order to be enforceable.","B":"No, because the grandfather and man's conduct was insufficient to indicate the assent required to create an implied-in-fact contract.","C":"Yes, because the court will construct a quasi-contract between the man and the grandfather to remedy unjust enrichment of the grandfather's estate.","D":"Yes, because the man reasonably and detrimentally relied on the grandfather's promise."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contracts for the sale or transfer of land fall under the statute of frauds and must therefore be in writing to be enforceable. Since the grandfather's promise to the man was oral, there is no enforceable contract (Choice A). However, the man can still prevail under a theory of promissory estoppel. This equitable doctrine allows a party to enforce another's promise when no valid contract was formed. It applies when:\n• the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise\n• the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise\n• the enforcing party suffered substantial detriment as a result of such reliance and\n• injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise. Here, the grandfather should have reasonably expected the man to rely on his promise that the man would inherit the farm. In reasonable reliance on that promise, the man left his job to farm the land. This caused the man to suffer substantial detriment, as evidenced by the fact that he borrowed money and gave up a substantial salary plus benefits. And since it would be unjust not to enforce the grandfather's promise—especially after the man had farmed the land for 15 years—the man is likely to prevail. (Choice B) An implied-in-fact contract arises when the parties' conduct indicates the requisite assent to be bound. But here, whether the grandfather and man's conduct was sufficient to imply a contract is irrelevant because only an express, written contract to transfer the farm would have been enforceable. (Choice C) Courts can construct a quasi-contract* to compensate a party for a benefit conferred upon another and prevent the other's unjust enrichment. Here, quasi-contract might be a basis for the man to recover compensation for valuable improvements made to the land—but not title to the farm. *This is also called an implied-in-law contract.\n\nEducational objective: Promissory estoppel applies when (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the enforcing party reasonably relied on that promise, (3) the enforcing party suffered substantial detriment as a result, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (promise reasonably inducing action or forbearance).","educationalObjective":"Promissory estoppel applies when (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the enforcing party reasonably relied on that promise, (3) the enforcing party suffered substantial detriment as a result, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (promise reasonably inducing action or forbearance).","rule":"Promissory estoppel applies when (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the enforcing party reasonably relied on that promise, (3) the enforcing party suffered substantial detriment as a result, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.","irac":{"issue":"Is the man likely to prevail?","rule":"Promissory estoppel applies when (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the enforcing party reasonably relied on that promise, (3) the enforcing party suffered substantial detriment as a result, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale or transfer of land fall under the statute of frauds and must therefore be in writing to be enforceable. Since the grandfather's promise to the man was oral, there is no enforceable contract (Choice A). However, the man can still prevail under a theory of promissory estoppel. This equitable doctrine allows a party to enforce another's promise when no valid contract was formed. It applies when:\n• the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise\n• the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise\n• the enforcing party suffered substantial detriment as a result of such reliance and\n• injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise. Here, the grandfather should have reasonably expected the man to rely on his promise that the man would inherit the farm. In reasonable reliance on that promise, the man left his job to farm the land. This caused the man to suffer substantial detriment, as evidenced by the fact that he borrowed money and gave up a substantial salary plus benefits. And since it would be unjust not to enforce the grandfather's promise—especially after the man had farmed the land for 15 years—the man is likely to prevail. (Choice B) An implied-in-fact contract arises when the parties' conduct indicates the requisite assent to be bound. But here, whether the grandfather and man's conduct was sufficient to imply a contract is irrelevant because only an express, written contract to transfer the farm would have been enforceable. (Choice C) Courts can construct a quasi-contract* to compensate a party for a benefit conferred upon another and prevent the other's unjust enrichment. Here, quasi-contract might be a basis for the man to recover compensation for valuable improvements made to the land—but not title to the farm. *This is also called an implied-in-law contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the man reasonably and detrimentally relied on the grandfather's promise."},"questionZh":"一个男人搬进了他丧偶的祖父的农舍。当时，该男子是一名发电厂操作员，赚取丰厚的薪水和福利。然而，在祖父口头承诺如果他这样做，他将继承农场后，该男子辞去了工作，去耕种祖父的土地。该男子借钱购买农具、清理土地、安装灌溉系统并种植农作物。祖父告诉邻居和家人，农场属于他。十五年后，祖父去世并留有遗嘱。祖父的遗嘱明确将该农场遗赠给长子。该男子立即提出申诉，寻求农场的合法所有权。男人有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为土地出售或转让合同属于欺诈法范畴，必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。","B":"不可以，因为祖父和男子的行为不足以表明建立默示事实上的合同所需的同意。","C":"是的，因为法院将在该男子和祖父之间建立一份准合同，以纠正祖父遗产的不当得利。","D":"是的，因为这个男人合理且产生不利后果地信赖了祖父的承诺。"},"explanationZh":"土地出售或转让合同属于欺诈法的范畴，因此必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。由于祖父对男子的承诺是口头的，因此不存在可执行的合同（选择A）。然而，根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）理论，该男子仍然可以获胜。这一衡平法原则允许一方在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行另一方的承诺。它适用于以下情况：\n• 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖\n• 执行方合理地信赖承诺\n• 执行方因这种依赖而遭受重大损害\n• 只有履行承诺才能避免不公正。在这里，祖父应该合理地期望该男子相信他的承诺，即该男子将继承农场。出于对这一承诺的合理信赖，该男子辞去了工作去耕种土地。这导致该男子遭受了巨大的损失，他借了钱并放​​弃了可观的工资和福利。由于不履行祖父的承诺是不公正的——尤其是在该男子耕种土地 15 年之后——该男子很可能会获胜。 （选择 B）当双方的行为表明必须同意受约束时，默示事实合同即产生。但在这里，祖父和男人的行为是否足以暗示合同并不重要，因为只有明确的书面转让农场合同才可以执行。 （选择C）法院可以订立准合同*，以补偿一方当事人授予另一方的利益，并防止另一方不当得利。在这里，准合同可能是该人因对土地进行的有价值的改进而获得补偿的基础，但不是农场所有权的基础。 *这也称为默示法律合同。\n\n教育目标：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）适用于以下情况：(1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖，(2) 执行方合理地依赖该承诺，(3) 执行方因此遭受重大损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（合理诱导行动或克制的承诺）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）适用于以下情况：(1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖，(2) 执行方合理地依赖该承诺，(3) 执行方因此遭受重大损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。","iracZh":{"issue":"男人有可能获胜吗？","rule":"允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）适用于以下情况：(1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖，(2) 执行方合理地依赖该承诺，(3) 执行方因此遭受重大损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。","analysis":"土地出售或转让合同属于欺诈法的范畴，因此必须采用书面形式才能强制执行。由于祖父对男子的承诺是口头的，因此不存在可执行的合同（选择A）。然而，根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）理论，该男子仍然可以获胜。这一衡平法原则允许一方在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行另一方的承诺。它适用于以下情况：\n• 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖\n• 执行方合理地信赖承诺\n• 执行方因这种依赖而遭受重大损害\n• 只有履行承诺才能避免不公正。在这里，祖父应该合理地期望该男子相信他的承诺，即该男子将继承农场。出于对这一承诺的合理信赖，该男子辞去了工作去耕种土地。这导致该男子遭受了巨大的损失，他借了钱并放​​弃了可观的工资和福利。由于不履行祖父的承诺是不公正的——尤其是在该男子耕种土地 15 年之后——该男子很可能会获胜。 （选择 B）当双方的行为表明必须同意受约束时，默示事实合同即产生。但在这里，祖父和男人的行为是否足以暗示合同并不重要，因为只有明确的书面转让农场合同才可以执行。 （选择C）法院可以订立准合同*，以补偿一方当事人授予另一方的利益，并防止另一方不当得利。在这里，准合同可能是该人因对土地进行的有价值的改进而获得补偿的基础，但不是农场所有权的基础。 *这也称为默示法律合同。\n\n教育目标：允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）适用于以下情况：(1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖，(2) 执行方合理地依赖该承诺，(3) 执行方因此遭受重大损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（合理诱导行动或克制的承诺）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为这个男人合理且产生不利后果地信赖了祖父的承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-b097bf902da0f828"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":100,"sourceQuestionPage":294,"sourceQuestionPages":[294],"sourceExplanationPages":[295,296],"sourceAnswerPages":[295,296],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-101","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A debtor's liquidated and undisputed $1,000 debt to a creditor was due on March 1. When the debt was still unpaid on March 15, the creditor told the debtor that if the debtor promised to pay the $1,000 on or before December 1, the creditor would then not sue to collect the debt. The debtor orally agreed. On April 1, the creditor sued the debtor to collect the debt that had become due on March 1. The debtor moved to dismiss the creditor's complaint. Should the court grant the debtor's motion?","choices":{"A":"No, because there was no consideration to support the creditor's promise not to sue.","B":"No, because there was no consideration to support the debtor's promise to pay $1,000 on December 1.","C":"Yes, because a promise to allow a debtor to delay payment on a past debt is enforceable without consideration.","D":"Yes, because the debtor bargained for the creditor's forbearance."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation\n\nGift No bargained-for exchange\n\nToken consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value\n\nSham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed\n\nIllusory promise No legal detriment if apparent promise imposes no obligation\n\nFor any agreement to be enforceable, there must be consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty does not qualify as consideration. Under this preexisting duty rule, a debtor's promise to pay all or part of a liquidated debt (ie, one that is certain and undisputed) is not sufficient consideration to support a creditor's return promise. Here, the debtor owed the creditor a liquidated and undisputed $1,000 debt that was due on March 1. When the debtor failed to pay, the creditor promised not to sue if the debtor promised to pay $1,000 by December 1. But there was no consideration to support the creditor's promise because the debtor had a preexisting duty to pay the $1,000 and promised to do nothing more than he was already obligated to do. Therefore, the parties' agreement was invalid for lack of consideration, and the court should not grant the debtor's motion to dismiss. (Choices B & D) The creditor's promise not to sue (ie, forbearance) was sufficient consideration to support the debtor's promise to pay $1,000 on December 1. But there was no bargained-for consideration for the creditor's promise not to sue because the debtor promised to perform only a preexisting duty in return. (Choice C) Under the preexisting duty rule, a promise to allow a debtor to delay payment on an undisputed debt is not enforceable without consideration.\n\nEducational objective: Under the preexisting duty rule, a debtor's promise to pay a liquidated debt (ie, one that is certain and undisputed in amount) is not consideration for a creditor's return promise.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that payment of a liquidated and undisputed obligation is not consideration).","educationalObjective":"Under the preexisting duty rule, a debtor's promise to pay a liquidated debt (ie, one that is certain and undisputed in amount) is not consideration for a creditor's return promise.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that payment of a liquidated and undisputed obligation is not consideration).","rule":"Under the preexisting duty rule, a debtor's promise to pay a liquidated debt (ie, one that is certain and undisputed in amount) is not consideration for a creditor's return promise.","irac":{"issue":"Should the court grant the debtor's motion?","rule":"Under the preexisting duty rule, a debtor's promise to pay a liquidated debt (ie, one that is certain and undisputed in amount) is not consideration for a creditor's return promise.","analysis":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation\n\nGift No bargained-for exchange\n\nToken consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value\n\nSham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed\n\nIllusory promise No legal detriment if apparent promise imposes no obligation\n\nFor any agreement to be enforceable, there must be consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty does not qualify as consideration. Under this preexisting duty rule, a debtor's promise to pay all or part of a liquidated debt (ie, one that is certain and undisputed) is not sufficient consideration to support a creditor's return promise. Here, the debtor owed the creditor a liquidated and undisputed $1,000 debt that was due on March 1. When the debtor failed to pay, the creditor promised not to sue if the debtor promised to pay $1,000 by December 1. But there was no consideration to support the creditor's promise because the debtor had a preexisting duty to pay the $1,000 and promised to do nothing more than he was already obligated to do. Therefore, the parties' agreement was invalid for lack of consideration, and the court should not grant the debtor's motion to dismiss. (Choices B & D) The creditor's promise not to sue (ie, forbearance) was sufficient consideration to support the debtor's promise to pay $1,000 on December 1. But there was no bargained-for consideration for the creditor's promise not to sue because the debtor promised to perform only a preexisting duty in return. (Choice C) Under the preexisting duty rule, a promise to allow a debtor to delay payment on an undisputed debt is not enforceable without consideration.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because there was no consideration to support the creditor's promise not to sue."},"questionZh":"债务人对债权人的1,000美元已清算且无争议的债务将于3月1日到期。当债务在3月15日仍未偿还时，债权人告诉债务人，如果债务人承诺在12月1日或之前支付1,000美元，债权人就不会提起诉讼以收回债务。债务人口头同意。 4月1日，债权人起诉债务人，要求收回3月1日到期的债务。债务人动议驳回债权人的起诉。法院是否应批准债务人的动议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为没有约因支持债权人不起诉的承诺。","B":"不，因为没有约因支持债务人在 12 月 1 日支付 1,000 美元的承诺。","C":"是的，因为允许债务人延迟偿还过去债务的承诺无需约因即可强制执行。","D":"是的，因为债务人讨价还价，要求债权人宽恕。"},"explanationZh":"阻碍合同成立的对价问题\n\n礼品 不可讨价还价交换\n\n名义约因 如果履行完全没有价值，则没有讨价还价的诱因\n\n虚假对价 如果不打算完成所叙述的履约，则不会造成法律损害，也不会诱使讨价还价 先前存在的义务/过去 如果已经欠履约或完成对价，则不会造成法律损害\n\n虚幻的承诺 如果明显的承诺不施加任何义务，则不会造成法律损害\n\n任何协议若要可执行，就必须有对价，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不符合对价。根据这一既存的义务规则，债务人承诺支付全部或部分已清算债务（即确定且无争议的债务）并不足以支持债权人的回报承诺。在此案中，债务人欠债权人一笔已清偿且无争议的 1,000 美元债务，将于 3 月 1 日到期。当债务人未能付款时，债权人承诺，如果债务人承诺在 12 月 1 日之前支付 1,000 美元，则债权人不会提起诉讼。但没有约因支持债权人的承诺，因为债务人有支付 1,000 美元的预先存在的义务，并且承诺不会做任何超出其已有义务的事情。因此，当事人的协议因缺乏对价而无效，法院不应支持债务人的驳回动议。 （选择 B 和 D）债权人承诺不起诉（即宽容）足以支持债务人承诺在 12 月 1 日支付 1,000 美元。但债权人承诺不起诉没有讨价还价的对价，因为债务人承诺仅履行一项预先存在的义务作为回报。 （选项C）根据先前存在的义务规则，允许债务人延迟支付无争议债务的承诺在没有对价的情况下不可强制执行。\n\n教育目标：根据现有的义务规则，债务人支付已清算债务（即金额确定且无争议的债务）的承诺不构成债权人回报承诺的对价。\n\n参考文献\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 74 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，支付已清算且无争议的债务不是对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据现有的义务规则，债务人对偿还已清算债务（即金额确定且无争议的债务）的承诺不构成债权人回报承诺的对价。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院是否应批准债务人的动议？","rule":"根据现有的义务规则，债务人对偿还已清算债务（即金额确定且无争议的债务）的承诺不构成债权人回报承诺的对价。","analysis":"阻碍合同成立的对价问题\n\n礼品 不可讨价还价交换\n\n名义约因 如果履行完全没有价值，则没有讨价还价的诱因\n\n虚假对价 如果不打算完成所叙述的履约，则不会造成法律损害，也不会诱使讨价还价 先前存在的义务/过去 如果已经欠履约或完成对价，则不会造成法律损害\n\n虚幻的承诺 如果明显的承诺不施加任何义务，则不会造成法律损害\n\n任何协议若要可执行，就必须有对价，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不符合对价。根据这一既存的义务规则，债务人承诺支付全部或部分已清算债务（即确定且无争议的债务）并不足以支持债权人的回报承诺。在此案中，债务人欠债权人一笔已清偿且无争议的 1,000 美元债务，将于 3 月 1 日到期。当债务人未能付款时，债权人承诺，如果债务人承诺在 12 月 1 日之前支付 1,000 美元，则债权人不会提起诉讼。但没有约因支持债权人的承诺，因为债务人有支付 1,000 美元的预先存在的义务，并且承诺不会做任何超出其已有义务的事情。因此，当事人的协议因缺乏对价而无效，法院不应支持债务人的驳回动议。 （选择 B 和 D）债权人承诺不起诉（即宽容）足以支持债务人承诺在 12 月 1 日支付 1,000 美元。但债权人承诺不起诉没有讨价还价的对价，因为债务人承诺仅履行一项预先存在的义务作为回报。 （选项C）根据先前存在的义务规则，允许债务人延迟支付无争议债务的承诺在没有对价的情况下不可强制执行。\n\n教育目标：根据现有的义务规则，债务人支付已清算债务（即金额确定且无争议的债务）的承诺不构成债权人回报承诺的对价。\n\n参考文献\n• 合同重述（第二次）§ 74 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，支付已清算且无争议的债务不是对价）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为没有约因支持债权人不起诉的承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"legal detriment","ipa":"/lˈiɡəl dˈɛtɹəmənt/","syllables":"le·gal detri·ment","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"法律不利益：实施无义务实施的行为或放弃有权实施的行为","contextualMeaning":"法律不利益：实施无义务实施的行为或放弃有权实施的行为","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation Gift No bargained-for exchange Token consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value Sham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed Illusory promise No legal detrimen","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":101,"sourceQuestionPage":297,"sourceQuestionPages":[297],"sourceExplanationPages":[298,299],"sourceAnswerPages":[298,299],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-102","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"An automobile retailer had an adult daughter who needed a car in her employment but had only $3,000 with which to buy one. The retailer wrote to her, \"Give me your $3,000 and I'll give you the car on our lot that we have been using as a demonstrator.\" The daughter thanked her father and paid him the $3,000. As both the retailer and the daughter knew, the demonstrator was reasonably worth $10,000. After the daughter paid the $3,000, but before the car was delivered to her, one of the retailer's sales staff sold and delivered the same car to a customer for $10,000. Neither the salesperson nor the customer was aware of the transaction between the retailer and the daughter. Does the daughter, after rejecting a tendered return of the $3,000 by the retailer, have an action against him for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the payment of $3,000 was inadequate consideration to support the retailer's promise.","B":"No, because the salesperson's delivery of the car to the customer made it impossible for the retailer to perform.","C":"Yes, because the retailer's promise was supported by bargained-for consideration.","D":"Yes, because the retailer's promise was supported by the moral obligation a father owes his child as to the necessities of modern life."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\n• performing (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or\n• refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A valid contract is formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. If the exchange involves items with economic value (eg, a car in exchange for money), there is no additional requirement that their values be equivalent or even roughly equivalent. Here, the retailer promised to give the daughter the demonstrator car in exchange for $3,000. Although the car was worth $10,000, this difference in value did not prevent the daughter's payment from being consideration for the retailer's promise (Choice A). Therefore, a valid contract arose between the parties, and the daughter has a breach of contract action against the retailer. (Choice B) A party's contractual duties may be discharged if it has become objectively impossible to perform them. But impossibility does not apply when, as here, a seller or its agent sells contracted-for goods to someone else. (Choice D) A promise based on a moral obligation is not supported by consideration and will not give rise to a contract. In addition, parents have no obligation—moral or otherwise—to provide their adult child with a car.\n\nEducational objective: A difference in economic value between the items exchanged is not ground for finding that a contract did not exist due to inadequate consideration.\n\nReferences\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange requirement).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 79 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (adequacy of consideration).","educationalObjective":"A difference in economic value between the items exchanged is not ground for finding that a contract did not exist due to inadequate consideration.","references":"• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange requirement).\n\n• Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 79 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (adequacy of consideration).","rule":"A difference in economic value between the items exchanged is not ground for finding that a contract did not exist due to inadequate consideration.","irac":{"issue":"Does the daughter, after rejecting a tendered return of the $3,000 by the retailer, have an action against him for breach of contract?","rule":"A difference in economic value between the items exchanged is not ground for finding that a contract did not exist due to inadequate consideration.","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\n• performing (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or\n• refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A valid contract is formed when a mutual agreement is supported by adequate consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. If the exchange involves items with economic value (eg, a car in exchange for money), there is no additional requirement that their values be equivalent or even roughly equivalent. Here, the retailer promised to give the daughter the demonstrator car in exchange for $3,000. Although the car was worth $10,000, this difference in value did not prevent the daughter's payment from being consideration for the retailer's promise (Choice A). Therefore, a valid contract arose between the parties, and the daughter has a breach of contract action against the retailer. (Choice B) A party's contractual duties may be discharged if it has become objectively impossible to perform them. But impossibility does not apply when, as here, a seller or its agent sells contracted-for goods to someone else. (Choice D) A promise based on a moral obligation is not supported by consideration and will not give rise to a contract. In addition, parents have no obligation—moral or otherwise—to provide their adult child with a car.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the retailer's promise was supported by bargained-for consideration."},"questionZh":"一家汽车零售商有一个成年女儿，她在工作中需要一辆汽车，但只有 3,000 美元可以购买一辆。零售商写信给她，“给我 3,000 美元，我就把我们停车场上我们一直用作演示的那辆车给你。”女儿感谢父亲并付给了他3000美元。零售商和女儿都知道，这个演示器的价值相当可观，价值 10,000 美元。在女儿支付了 3,000 美元之后，但在汽车交付给她之前，零售商的一名销售人员以 10,000 美元的价格将同一辆车出售并交付给了一位顾客。销售人员和顾客均不知道零售商与女儿之间的交易。女儿拒绝零售商提出的 3,000 美元退货后，是否可以对他提起违约诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为 3,000 美元的付款不足以支持零售商的承诺。","B":"不可以，因为销售人员将汽车交付给客户导致零售商无法履约。","C":"是的，因为零售商的承诺得到了讨价还价的支持。","D":"是的，因为零售商的承诺得到了父亲对孩子承担的现代生活必需品的道德义务的支持。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n• 实施（或承诺实施）法律不要求的行为或\n• 克制（或承诺克制）履行法律允许的行为 当双方达成的协议得到充分对价（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行）的支持时，有效合同即告成立。如果交换涉及具有经济价值的物品（例如，用一辆汽车换取金钱），则无需额外要求其价值相等甚至大致相等。零售商承诺以 3,000 美元的价格向女儿赠送一辆演示车。尽管这辆车价值 10,000 美元，但这种价值差异并不妨碍女儿的付款是对零售商允诺的约因（选项 A）。因此，双方之间形成有效合同，女儿对零售商提起违约诉讼。 （选择B）如果客观上无法履行一方的合同义务，则可以解除合同义务。但当卖方或其代理人将合同货物出售给其他人时，不可能性并不适用，就像这里一样。 （选项 D）基于道德义务的承诺没有对价的支持，并且不会产生合同。此外，父母没有义务（道德上或其他方面）为成年子女提供汽车。\n\n教育目标：交换物品之间的经济价值差异并不能成为因对价不充分而认定合同不存在的理由。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换要求）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 79（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对价的充分性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"交换物品之间的经济价值差异不能作为因对价不充分而认定合同不存在的理由。","iracZh":{"issue":"女儿拒绝零售商提出的 3,000 美元退货后，是否可以对他提起违约诉讼？","rule":"交换物品之间的经济价值差异不能作为因对价不充分而认定合同不存在的理由。","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n• 实施（或承诺实施）法律不要求的行为或\n• 克制（或承诺克制）履行法律允许的行为 当双方达成的协议得到充分对价（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行）的支持时，有效合同即告成立。如果交换涉及具有经济价值的物品（例如，用一辆汽车换取金钱），则无需额外要求其价值相等甚至大致相等。零售商承诺以 3,000 美元的价格向女儿赠送一辆演示车。尽管这辆车价值 10,000 美元，但这种价值差异并不妨碍女儿的付款是对零售商允诺的约因（选项 A）。因此，双方之间形成有效合同，女儿对零售商提起违约诉讼。 （选择B）如果客观上无法履行一方的合同义务，则可以解除合同义务。但当卖方或其代理人将合同货物出售给其他人时，不可能性并不适用，就像这里一样。 （选项 D）基于道德义务的承诺没有对价的支持，并且不会产生合同。此外，父母没有义务（道德上或其他方面）为成年子女提供汽车。\n\n教育目标：交换物品之间的经济价值差异并不能成为因对价不充分而认定合同不存在的理由。\n\n参考文献\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换要求）。\n\n• 《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 79（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（对价的充分性）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为零售商的承诺得到了讨价还价的支持。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":102,"sourceQuestionPage":300,"sourceQuestionPages":[300],"sourceExplanationPages":[301,302],"sourceAnswerPages":[301,302],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-103","subject":"Contracts","topic":"UCC Formation & Battle of Forms","question":"A seller sent an email to a potential buyer, offering to sell his house to her for $150,000. The buyer immediately responded via email, asking whether the offer included the house's front porch swing. The seller emailed back: \"No, it doesn't.\" The buyer then ordered a front porch swing and emailed back to the seller: \"I accept your offer.\" The seller refused to sell the house to the buyer, claiming that the offer was no longer open. Is there a contract for the sale of the house?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer's initial email was a counteroffer.","B":"No, because the offer lapsed before the buyer accepted.","C":"Yes, because the buyer relied on the offer by ordering the swing.","D":"Yes, because the buyer's initial email merely asked for information."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Termination of offer before acceptance\n\nOfferor's\n• Offeror communicates revocation directly to offeree revocation\n• Offeree learns information from reliable source that reasonably indicates offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer) Offeree's\n• Offeree communicates rejection directly to offeror rejection\n• Offeree's counteroffer serves as rejection & new offer* Lapse\n• Time period specified in offer expires\n• After reasonable time if no time period specified in offer By law\n• Either party dies or is adjudicated insane\n• Subject matter of offer is destroyed or becomes illegal *Counteroffer does not terminate offer if offeree manifests intent to take offer under advisement.\n\nA binding contract is formed when an offeree accepts an offer before it terminates. An offer will terminate if the offeree rejects the offer or responds with a counteroffer—ie, a reply that presents different terms from those in the offer—prior to acceptance. However, merely asking for information (eg, whether the offer includes a porch swing) is not a counteroffer and will not terminate an offer (Choice A). An offer can also terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to respond within (1) the time period specified in the offer or (2) a reasonable time if no time period is specified. Here, no lapse occurred because the seller's email did not specify a time for acceptance and there is no evidence that the buyer's acceptance was emailed after an unreasonable delay (Choice B). Instead, a contract for the sale of the house was formed. (Choice C) An offeree's reliance on an offer can create an option contract—ie, a separate agreement not to revoke the offer—if the offeree incurred substantial expense and his/her reliance was foreseeable. But an option contract was unnecessary here since the seller did not try to revoke his offer before acceptance. And even if it was foreseeable that the buyer would order a porch swing, its cost is not substantial compared to a $150,000 house.\n\nEducational objective: An offer will terminate if the offeree makes a counteroffer—ie, a reply that presents different terms than the offer, not merely a request for information. An offer will also terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to respond within the time period specified in the offer or within a reasonable time (if no time period is specified).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 39 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirements for counteroffers).","educationalObjective":"An offer will terminate if the offeree makes a counteroffer—ie, a reply that presents different terms than the offer, not merely a request for information. An offer will also terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to respond within the time period specified in the offer or within a reasonable time (if no time period is specified).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 39 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirements for counteroffers).","rule":"An offer will terminate if the offeree makes a counteroffer—ie, a reply that presents different terms than the offer, not merely a request for information. An offer will also terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to respond within the time period specified in the offer or within a reasonable time (if no time period is specified).","irac":{"issue":"Is there a contract for the sale of the house?","rule":"An offer will terminate if the offeree makes a counteroffer—ie, a reply that presents different terms than the offer, not merely a request for information. An offer will also terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to respond within the time period specified in the offer or within a reasonable time (if no time period is specified).","analysis":"Termination of offer before acceptance\n\nOfferor's\n• Offeror communicates revocation directly to offeree revocation\n• Offeree learns information from reliable source that reasonably indicates offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer) Offeree's\n• Offeree communicates rejection directly to offeror rejection\n• Offeree's counteroffer serves as rejection & new offer* Lapse\n• Time period specified in offer expires\n• After reasonable time if no time period specified in offer By law\n• Either party dies or is adjudicated insane\n• Subject matter of offer is destroyed or becomes illegal *Counteroffer does not terminate offer if offeree manifests intent to take offer under advisement.\n\nA binding contract is formed when an offeree accepts an offer before it terminates. An offer will terminate if the offeree rejects the offer or responds with a counteroffer—ie, a reply that presents different terms from those in the offer—prior to acceptance. However, merely asking for information (eg, whether the offer includes a porch swing) is not a counteroffer and will not terminate an offer (Choice A). An offer can also terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to respond within (1) the time period specified in the offer or (2) a reasonable time if no time period is specified. Here, no lapse occurred because the seller's email did not specify a time for acceptance and there is no evidence that the buyer's acceptance was emailed after an unreasonable delay (Choice B). Instead, a contract for the sale of the house was formed. (Choice C) An offeree's reliance on an offer can create an option contract—ie, a separate agreement not to revoke the offer—if the offeree incurred substantial expense and his/her reliance was foreseeable. But an option contract was unnecessary here since the seller did not try to revoke his offer before acceptance. And even if it was foreseeable that the buyer would order a porch swing, its cost is not substantial compared to a $150,000 house.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the buyer's initial email merely asked for information."},"questionZh":"一位卖家向一位潜在买家发送了一封电子邮件，提出以 15 万美元的价格把他的房子卖给她。买家立即通过电子邮件回复，询问报价是否包括房子的前廊秋千。卖家回复邮件说：“不，没有。”然后，买家订购了一个前廊秋千，并通过电子邮件回复卖家：“我接受你的报价。”卖方拒绝将房子卖给买方，声称报价不再开放。房子买卖有合同吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为买家最初的电子邮件是还价。","B":"不可以，因为报价在买方接受之前就失效了。","C":"是的，因为买方通过订购摆动来依赖报价。","D":"是的，因为买家最初的电子邮件只是询问信息。"},"explanationZh":"接受之前终止要约\n\n报价人的\n• 要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销撤销的信息\n• 受要约人从可靠来源获悉信息，合理地表明要约已被撤销（例如，房屋出售给另一位买家）\n• 受要约人直接向要约人拒绝传达拒绝信息\n• 受要约人的还盘将被视为拒绝和新的要约* 失效\n• 优惠中指定的期限到期\n• 如果要约中没有规定时间段，则在合理的时间之后 法律规定\n• 任何一方死亡或被判定为精神错乱\n• 要约标的物被毁坏或变得非法 *如果受要约人在通知下表明接受要约的意图，还价不会终止要约。\n\n当受要约人在要约终止前接受要约时，具有约束力的合同即告成立。如果受要约人在接受之前拒绝要约或以还价（即提出与要约中的条款不同的条款的答复）作出回应，则要约将终止。然而，仅仅询问信息（例如，要约是否包括门廊秋千）不是还价，也不会终止要约（选择 A）。如果受要约人未能在（1）要约中指定的期限内或（2）合理时间内（如果未指定期限）做出回应，则要约也可能失效终止。这里，没有发生失误，因为卖方的电子邮件没有指定验收时间，并且没有证据表明买方的验收是在不合理的延迟后通过电子邮件发送的（选项B）。相反，签订了房屋出售合同。 （选择C）如果受要约人产生了大量费用并且他/她的依赖是可预见的，则受要约人对要约的依赖可以创建期权合同，即不撤销要约的单独协议。但这里没有必要签订期权合同，因为卖方在接受之前并没有试图撤销他的要约。即使可以预见买家会订购门廊秋千，与价值 15 万美元的房屋相比，其成本也并不高。\n\n教育目标：如果受要约人提出还盘，即提出与要约不同的条款的答复，而不仅仅是索要信息，则要约将终止。如果受要约人未能在要约中指定的期限内或在合理时间内（如果未指定期限）做出回应，要约也将失效终止。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 39 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（还盘要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果受要约人提出还盘，即提出与要约不同的条款的答复，而不仅仅是索要信息，则要约将终止。如果受要约人未能在要约中指定的期限内或在合理时间内（如果未指定期限）做出回应，要约也将失效终止。","iracZh":{"issue":"房子买卖有合同吗？","rule":"如果受要约人提出还盘，即提出与要约不同的条款的答复，而不仅仅是索要信息，则要约将终止。如果受要约人未能在要约中指定的期限内或在合理时间内（如果未指定期限）做出回应，要约也将失效终止。","analysis":"接受之前终止要约\n\n报价人的\n• 要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销撤销的信息\n• 受要约人从可靠来源获悉信息，合理地表明要约已被撤销（例如，房屋出售给另一位买家）\n• 受要约人直接向要约人拒绝传达拒绝信息\n• 受要约人的还盘将被视为拒绝和新的要约* 失效\n• 优惠中指定的期限到期\n• 如果要约中没有规定时间段，则在合理的时间之后 法律规定\n• 任何一方死亡或被判定为精神错乱\n• 要约标的物被毁坏或变得非法 *如果受要约人在通知下表明接受要约的意图，还价不会终止要约。\n\n当受要约人在要约终止前接受要约时，具有约束力的合同即告成立。如果受要约人在接受之前拒绝要约或以还价（即提出与要约中的条款不同的条款的答复）作出回应，则要约将终止。然而，仅仅询问信息（例如，要约是否包括门廊秋千）不是还价，也不会终止要约（选择 A）。如果受要约人未能在（1）要约中指定的期限内或（2）合理时间内（如果未指定期限）做出回应，则要约也可能失效终止。这里，没有发生失误，因为卖方的电子邮件没有指定验收时间，并且没有证据表明买方的验收是在不合理的延迟后通过电子邮件发送的（选项B）。相反，签订了房屋出售合同。 （选择C）如果受要约人产生了大量费用并且他/她的依赖是可预见的，则受要约人对要约的依赖可以创建期权合同，即不撤销要约的单独协议。但这里没有必要签订期权合同，因为卖方在接受之前并没有试图撤销他的要约。即使可以预见买家会订购门廊秋千，与价值 15 万美元的房屋相比，其成本也并不高。\n\n教育目标：如果受要约人提出还盘，即提出与要约不同的条款的答复，而不仅仅是索要信息，则要约将终止。如果受要约人未能在要约中指定的期限内或在合理时间内（如果未指定期限）做出回应，要约也将失效终止。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 39 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（还盘要求）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为买家最初的电子邮件只是询问信息。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"option contract","ipa":"/ˈɑpʃən kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"op·tion con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"选择权合同：以约因支持、使要约在期限内不可撤销的合同","contextualMeaning":"选择权合同：以约因支持、使要约在期限内不可撤销的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice C) An offeree's reliance on an offer can create an option contract—ie, a separate agreement not to revoke the offer—if the offeree incurred substantial expense and his/her reliance was foreseeable.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"counteroffer","ipa":"/kˈaʊntɝˌɔfɝ/","syllables":"coun·terof·fer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"[法] 还价, 反要约","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“counteroffer”","wordForm":"名词；词形 3:counteroffers/s:counteroffers","sourceSentence":"No, because the buyer's initial email was a counteroffer.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"offering","ipa":"/ˈɔfɝɪŋ/","syllables":"of·fer·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 提供, 奉献物, 牲礼, 上市的股票(或证券等)；[经] 出售物","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“offering”","wordForm":"名词；词形 0:offer/1:i/s:offerings/i:offering","sourceSentence":"A seller sent an email to a potential buyer, offering to sell his house to her for $150,000.","synonyms":["oblation","offer"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":103,"sourceQuestionPage":303,"sourceQuestionPages":[303],"sourceExplanationPages":[304,305],"sourceAnswerPages":[304,305],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-104","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On December 15, a lawyer received from a retailer of office supplies an offer consisting of its catalog and a signed letter stating, \"We will supply you with as many of the items in the enclosed catalog as you order during the next calendar year. We assure you that this offer and the prices in the catalog will remain firm throughout the coming year.\" No other correspondence passed between the retailer and the lawyer until the following April 15 (four months later), when the retailer received from the lawyer a faxed order for \"100 reams of your paper, catalog item #101.\" Did the lawyer's April 15 fax constitute an effective acceptance of the retailer's offer at the prices specified in the catalog?","choices":{"A":"No, because the lawyer did not accept the retailer's offer within a reasonable time.","B":"No, because under applicable law the irrevocability of the retailer's offer was limited to a period of three months.","C":"Yes, because a one-year option contract had been created by the retailer's offer.","D":"Yes, because the retailer had not revoked its offer before April 15."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of goods are governed by the UCC. Under the UCC firm offer rule, a merchant's signed,* written offer to buy or sell goods may contain assurances that it will remain open. If so, the offer is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time. But in either case, the period of irrevocability cannot exceed three months. Therefore, the retailer's firm offer to sell office supplies to the lawyer at the prices listed in the catalog became revocable after three months (March 15). However, the retailer never expressly revoked its offer (Choice B). And though a revocable offer with no deadline for acceptance will lapse after a reasonable amount of time given the circumstances surrounding the negotiation, the retailer's offer letter referenced orders placed \"during the next calendar year.\" This indicates that the reasonable time for acceptance was any time during that year (Choice A). Therefore, the lawyer's April 15 faxed order was an effective acceptance of the retailer's offer at the prices listed in the catalog. *A full handwritten signature is not always required, such as when (1) initialing the relevant clause is appropriate under the circumstances or (2) the merchant handwrites on his/her letterhead to confirm that a firm offer was already made. (Choice C) Although the UCC is silent as to option contracts, they can be created for the sale of goods (under the common law rule) if they are supported by consideration. But here, the retailer's promise to hold the offer open was not supported by consideration because the promise was not exchanged for an act or return promise.\n\nEducational objective: A merchant's firm offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time (not to exceed three months in either case). Once the offer becomes revocable, it may lapse (just like any other revocable offer) if it is not accepted within a reasonable amount of time. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-205 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (firm offers).","educationalObjective":"A merchant's firm offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time (not to exceed three months in either case). Once the offer becomes revocable, it may lapse (just like any other revocable offer) if it is not accepted within a reasonable amount of time. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-205 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (firm offers).","rule":"A merchant's firm offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time (not to exceed three months in either case). Once the offer becomes revocable, it may lapse (just like any other revocable offer) if it is not accepted within a reasonable amount of time. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"On December 15, a lawyer received from a retailer of office supplies an offer consisting of its catalog and a signed letter stating, \"We will supply you with as many of the items in the enclosed catalog as you order during the next calendar year. We assure you that this offer and the prices in the catalog will remain firm throughout the coming year.\" No other correspondence passed between the retailer and the lawyer until the following April 15 (four months later), when the retailer received from the lawyer a faxed order for \"100 reams of your paper, catalog item #101.\" Did the lawyer's April 15 fax constitute an effective acceptance of the retailer's offer at the prices specified in the catalog?","rule":"A merchant's firm offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time (not to exceed three months in either case). Once the offer becomes revocable, it may lapse (just like any other revocable offer) if it is not accepted within a reasonable amount of time. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of goods are governed by the UCC. Under the UCC firm offer rule, a merchant's signed,* written offer to buy or sell goods may contain assurances that it will remain open. If so, the offer is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time. But in either case, the period of irrevocability cannot exceed three months. Therefore, the retailer's firm offer to sell office supplies to the lawyer at the prices listed in the catalog became revocable after three months (March 15). However, the retailer never expressly revoked its offer (Choice B). And though a revocable offer with no deadline for acceptance will lapse after a reasonable amount of time given the circumstances surrounding the negotiation, the retailer's offer letter referenced orders placed \"during the next calendar year.\" This indicates that the reasonable time for acceptance was any time during that year (Choice A). Therefore, the lawyer's April 15 faxed order was an effective acceptance of the retailer's offer at the prices listed in the catalog. *A full handwritten signature is not always required, such as when (1) initialing the relevant clause is appropriate under the circumstances or (2) the merchant handwrites on his/her letterhead to confirm that a firm offer was already made. (Choice C) Although the UCC is silent as to option contracts, they can be created for the sale of goods (under the common law rule) if they are supported by consideration. But here, the retailer's promise to hold the offer open was not supported by consideration because the promise was not exchanged for an act or return promise.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the retailer had not revoked its offer before April 15."},"questionZh":"12 月 15 日，一名律师收到一家办公用品零售商的报价，其中包括其目录和一封签名信，信中写道：“我们将在下一个日历年向您提供所附目录中您订购的尽可能多的商品。我们向您保证，这一报价和目录中的价格将在未来一年保持不变。”直到接下来的 4 月 15 日（四个月后），零售商和律师之间没有再进行任何其他通信，当时零售商收到了律师发来的一份传真订单，要求“100 令纸张，目录项#101”。律师 4 月 15 日的传真是否构成对零售商按目录中指定价格提供的报价的有效接受？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为律师没有在合理时间内接受零售商的要约。","B":"不可以，因为根据适用法律，零售商报价的不可撤销期限仅限于三个月。","C":"是的，因为零售商的报价已经创建了一年期期权合同。","D":"是的，因为零售商在 4 月 15 日之前没有撤销其报价。"},"explanationZh":"商品销售合同受 UCC 管辖。根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家签署的*购买或出售商品的书面报价可能包含其将保持开放的保证。如果是这样，则要约在要约中规定的时间内不可撤销，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内不可撤销。但无论哪种情况，不可撤销的期限都不能超过三个月。因此，零售商以目录中列出的价格向律师出售办公用品的坚定要约在三个月后（3月15日）被撤销。然而，零售商从未明确撤销其报价（选项 B）。尽管鉴于谈判的情况，没有接受最后期限的可撤销要约将在合理的时间后失效，但零售商的要约函提到了“在下一个日历年”下的订单。这表明合理的接受时间是当年的任何时间（选择 A）。因此，律师 4 月 15 日的传真命令有效地接受了零售商按目录中列出的价格提供的报价。 *并不总是需要完整的手写签名，例如当 (1) 在具体情况下签署相关条款是适当的，或 (2) 商家在其信头上手写以确认已提出实盘。 （选项C）虽然UCC没有提及期权合约，但如果得到对价的支持，可以为货物销售（根据普通法规则）创建期权合约。但在这里，零售商保持报价的承诺没有得到对价的支持，因为该承诺并未交换为行为或退货承诺。\n\n教育目标：商家购买或出售商品的确定要约在要约中规定的时间内不可撤销，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（两种情况均不超过三个月）不可撤销。一旦要约可撤销，如果在合理时间内未接受，则该要约可能会失效（就像任何其他可撤销要约一样）。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-205（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（实盘）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商家购买或出售商品的确定要约在要约中规定的时间内不可撤销，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（两种情况均不超过三个月）不可撤销。一旦要约可撤销，如果在合理时间内未接受，则该要约可能会失效（就像任何其他可撤销要约一样）。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"12 月 15 日，一名律师收到一家办公用品零售商的报价，其中包括其目录和一封签名信，信中写道：“我们将在下一个日历年向您提供所附目录中您订购的尽可能多的商品。我们向您保证，这一报价和目录中的价格将在未来一年保持不变。”直到接下来的 4 月 15 日（四个月后），零售商和律师之间没有再进行任何其他通信，当时零售商收到了律师发来的一份传真订单，要求“100 令纸张，目录项#101”。律师 4 月 15 日的传真是否构成对零售商按目录中指定价格提供的报价的有效接受？","rule":"商家购买或出售商品的确定要约在要约中规定的时间内不可撤销，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（两种情况均不超过三个月）不可撤销。一旦要约可撤销，如果在合理时间内未接受，则该要约可能会失效（就像任何其他可撤销要约一样）。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"商品销售合同受 UCC 管辖。根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家签署的*购买或出售商品的书面报价可能包含其将保持开放的保证。如果是这样，则要约在要约中规定的时间内不可撤销，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内不可撤销。但无论哪种情况，不可撤销的期限都不能超过三个月。因此，零售商以目录中列出的价格向律师出售办公用品的坚定要约在三个月后（3月15日）被撤销。然而，零售商从未明确撤销其报价（选项 B）。尽管鉴于谈判的情况，没有接受最后期限的可撤销要约将在合理的时间后失效，但零售商的要约函提到了“在下一个日历年”下的订单。这表明合理的接受时间是当年的任何时间（选择 A）。因此，律师 4 月 15 日的传真命令有效地接受了零售商按目录中列出的价格提供的报价。 *并不总是需要完整的手写签名，例如当 (1) 在具体情况下签署相关条款是适当的，或 (2) 商家在其信头上手写以确认已提出实盘。 （选项C）虽然UCC没有提及期权合约，但如果得到对价的支持，可以为货物销售（根据普通法规则）创建期权合约。但在这里，零售商保持报价的承诺没有得到对价的支持，因为该承诺并未交换为行为或退货承诺。\n\n教育目标：商家购买或出售商品的确定要约在要约中规定的时间内不可撤销，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（两种情况均不超过三个月）不可撤销。一旦要约可撤销，如果在合理时间内未接受，则该要约可能会失效（就像任何其他可撤销要约一样）。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-205（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（实盘）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为零售商在 4 月 15 日之前没有撤销其报价。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"revocable offer","ipa":"/ɹˈɛvəkəbəl ˈɔfɝ/","syllables":"re·vo·ca·ble of·fer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"可撤销要约：在有效承诺前原则上可被撤回的要约","contextualMeaning":"可撤销要约：在有效承诺前原则上可被撤回的要约","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Once the offer becomes revocable, it may lapse (just like any other revocable offer) if it is not accepted within a reasonable amount of time.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"firm offer","ipa":"/fˈɝm ˈɔfɝ/","syllables":"firm of·fer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"商人确定要约：符合 UCC 要件时无需约因即可暂时不可撤销","contextualMeaning":"商人确定要约：符合 UCC 要件时无需约因即可暂时不可撤销","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A merchant's firm offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time (not to exceed three months in either case).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-08c896f2e576768e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":104,"sourceQuestionPage":306,"sourceQuestionPages":[306],"sourceExplanationPages":[307,308],"sourceAnswerPages":[307,308],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-105","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A developer owned 250 lots in a new residential development. A builder of single-family homes approached the developer with a signed, written offer \"to purchase a block of lots for a reasonable price to be determined by the market.\" Prior to that, three other residential construction companies had each purchased blocks of 50 lots from the developer for between $75,000 and $90,000 per lot, depending on lot size and location. The developer signed the builder's offer upon receiving it. A week later, the developer notified the builder that the developer had sold the remaining 100 lots to another builder. If the builder sues the developer for breach of contract, will the builder be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because all essential terms must be specified in the contract for the contract to be valid.","B":"No, because the contract is voidable by the developer since the actual market price was not stated in the agreement.","C":"Yes, because the court can use the other construction companies' purchases to \"fill the gap\" for the missing quantity and price terms.","D":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule does not bar extrinsic evidence of the reasonable market price."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Essential contract terms*\n\nParties Subject matter (eg, description of goods or services) Price or other consideration Quantity Duration of contract Time for each party's performance *Necessarily varies by contract.\n\nAt common law, all essential terms must be covered in a contract for it to be valid and enforceable. Those terms must be sufficiently certain and definite for the court to determine the existence of a breach and give an appropriate remedy. Otherwise, the contract fails for indefiniteness.* One essential term is the subject matter of the agreement—eg, a description of the property to be sold in a land-sales contract. A property description is sufficiently certain and definite if it enables the court to determine exactly what property is being sold. Here, the description (\"block of lots\") is too indefinite to make such a determination. As a result, the builder is unlikely to prevail. *Indefiniteness may be cured by surrounding circumstances or conduct by the parties that permits the court to supply the term. But here, there are no such circumstances or acts by the parties to identify exactly which lots were to be sold. (Choice B) Although the actual market price was not stated in the agreement, \"reasonable market price\" is a sufficiently certain and definite price term since market value can be determined objectively. Also, had this essential term been missing or otherwise insufficient, the contract would have been void—not voidable. (Choice C) The Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) encourages contract formation by \"filling the gap\" for many missing terms—including price but not quantity. Additionally, land-sales contracts are governed by common law—not the UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods. (Choice D) The parol evidence rule generally bars extrinsic evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. However, evidence of the reasonable market price is not barred by this rule.\n\nEducational objective: At common law, all essential terms must be covered in an agreement, and those terms must be sufficiently certain and definite. The property description in a land-sales contract meets this standard if it allows the court to determine the exact property being sold.\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 180 (2020) (requisites for definiteness and certainty of contract terms).","educationalObjective":"At common law, all essential terms must be covered in an agreement, and those terms must be sufficiently certain and definite. The property description in a land-sales contract meets this standard if it allows the court to determine the exact property being sold.","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 180 (2020) (requisites for definiteness and certainty of contract terms).","rule":"At common law, all essential terms must be covered in an agreement, and those terms must be sufficiently certain and definite. The property description in a land-sales contract meets this standard if it allows the court to determine the exact property being sold.","irac":{"issue":"If the builder sues the developer for breach of contract, will the builder be likely to prevail?","rule":"At common law, all essential terms must be covered in an agreement, and those terms must be sufficiently certain and definite. The property description in a land-sales contract meets this standard if it allows the court to determine the exact property being sold.","analysis":"Essential contract terms*\n\nParties Subject matter (eg, description of goods or services) Price or other consideration Quantity Duration of contract Time for each party's performance *Necessarily varies by contract.\n\nAt common law, all essential terms must be covered in a contract for it to be valid and enforceable. Those terms must be sufficiently certain and definite for the court to determine the existence of a breach and give an appropriate remedy. Otherwise, the contract fails for indefiniteness.* One essential term is the subject matter of the agreement—eg, a description of the property to be sold in a land-sales contract. A property description is sufficiently certain and definite if it enables the court to determine exactly what property is being sold. Here, the description (\"block of lots\") is too indefinite to make such a determination. As a result, the builder is unlikely to prevail. *Indefiniteness may be cured by surrounding circumstances or conduct by the parties that permits the court to supply the term. But here, there are no such circumstances or acts by the parties to identify exactly which lots were to be sold. (Choice B) Although the actual market price was not stated in the agreement, \"reasonable market price\" is a sufficiently certain and definite price term since market value can be determined objectively. Also, had this essential term been missing or otherwise insufficient, the contract would have been void—not voidable. (Choice C) The Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) encourages contract formation by \"filling the gap\" for many missing terms—including price but not quantity. Additionally, land-sales contracts are governed by common law—not the UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods. (Choice D) The parol evidence rule generally bars extrinsic evidence of prior or contemporaneous agreements to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. However, evidence of the reasonable market price is not barred by this rule.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because all essential terms must be specified in the contract for the contract to be valid."},"questionZh":"一位开发商在一个新的住宅开发项目中拥有 250 个地块。一家单户住宅建筑商向开发商提出了一份签署的书面报价，“以由市场决定的合理价格购买一块地块”。在此之前，另外三个住宅建筑公司分别从开发商处购买了 50 个地块，每块地块的价格在 75,000 美元到 90,000 美元之间，具体取决于地块面积和位置。开发商在收到建筑商的报价后签署了报价。一周后，开发商通知建筑商，开发商已将剩余的100块地块卖给了另一家建筑商。如果建筑商起诉开发商违约，建筑商胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为所有基本条款必须在合同中指定才能使合同有效。","B":"不可以，因为协议中没有注明实际的市场价格，所以开发商可以撤销合同。","C":"是的，因为法院可以使用其他建筑公司的采购来“填补”缺失的数量和价格条款。","D":"是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）并不禁止合理市场价格的外在证据。"},"explanationZh":"基本合同条款*\n\n各方 标的物（例如，商品或服务的描述） 价格或其他考虑因素 数量 合同期限 各方履行义务的时间 *必然因合同而异。\n\n根据普通法，合同中必须涵盖所有基本条款，合同才有效且可执行。这些条款必须足够确定和明确，以便法院确定是否存在违约行为并给予适当的补救措施。否则，合同将无限期失效。*一个基本条款是协议的标的物，例如，土地销售合同中对待售财产的描述。如果财产描述能够使法院准确确定正在出售的财产，那么该财产描述就足够确定和明确。这里，描述（“批次块”）过于不确定，无法做出这样的决定。因此，建筑商不太可能获胜。 *不确定性可以通过周围环境或当事人允许法院提供该期限的行为来解决。但在这里，当事人不存在这样的情况或行为来准确确定哪些拍卖品将被出售。 （选项B）虽然协议中没有明确实际市场价格，但“合理市场价格”是一个足够确定、明确的价格术语，因为市场价值是可以客观确定的。此外，如果这一基本条款缺失或不充分，合同就会无效——不可撤销。 （选项 C） 《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 鼓励通过“填补空白”许多缺失条款（包括价格但不包括数量）来订立合同。此外，土地销售合同受普通法管辖，而不是管辖货物销售合同的《统一商法典》（UCC）。 （选项 D） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止使用先前或同期协议的外在证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款。然而，该规则并不禁止提供合理市场价格的证据。\n\n教育目标：在普通法中，所有基本条款都必须包含在协议中，并且这些条款必须足够确定和明确。如果土地销售合同中的财产描述允许法院确定所出售的确切财产，则该合同中的财产描述符合此标准。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 180 (2020)（合同条款明确性和确定性的要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在普通法中，所有基本条款都必须包含在协议中，并且这些条款必须足够确定和明确。如果土地销售合同中的财产描述允许法院确定所出售的确切财产，则该合同中的财产描述符合此标准。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果建筑商起诉开发商违约，建筑商胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"在普通法中，所有基本条款都必须包含在协议中，并且这些条款必须足够确定和明确。如果土地销售合同中的财产描述允许法院确定所出售的确切财产，则该合同中的财产描述符合此标准。","analysis":"基本合同条款*\n\n各方 标的物（例如，商品或服务的描述） 价格或其他考虑因素 数量 合同期限 各方履行义务的时间 *必然因合同而异。\n\n根据普通法，合同中必须涵盖所有基本条款，合同才有效且可执行。这些条款必须足够确定和明确，以便法院确定是否存在违约行为并给予适当的补救措施。否则，合同将无限期失效。*一个基本条款是协议的标的物，例如，土地销售合同中对待售财产的描述。如果财产描述能够使法院准确确定正在出售的财产，那么该财产描述就足够确定和明确。这里，描述（“批次块”）过于不确定，无法做出这样的决定。因此，建筑商不太可能获胜。 *不确定性可以通过周围环境或当事人允许法院提供该期限的行为来解决。但在这里，当事人不存在这样的情况或行为来准确确定哪些拍卖品将被出售。 （选项B）虽然协议中没有明确实际市场价格，但“合理市场价格”是一个足够确定、明确的价格术语，因为市场价值是可以客观确定的。此外，如果这一基本条款缺失或不充分，合同就会无效——不可撤销。 （选项 C） 《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 鼓励通过“填补空白”许多缺失条款（包括价格但不包括数量）来订立合同。此外，土地销售合同受普通法管辖，而不是管辖货物销售合同的《统一商法典》（UCC）。 （选项 D） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止使用先前或同期协议的外在证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款。然而，该规则并不禁止提供合理市场价格的证据。\n\n教育目标：在普通法中，所有基本条款都必须包含在协议中，并且这些条款必须足够确定和明确。如果土地销售合同中的财产描述允许法院确定所出售的确切财产，则该合同中的财产描述符合此标准。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 180 (2020)（合同条款明确性和确定性的要求）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为所有基本条款必须在合同中指定才能使合同有效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":105,"sourceQuestionPage":309,"sourceQuestionPages":[309],"sourceExplanationPages":[310,311],"sourceAnswerPages":[310,311],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-106","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A home goods retailer, in a signed writing, contracted with an apartment complex for the sale to the apartment complex of 50 identical sets of specified bathroom faucets, 25 sets to be delivered on March 1, and the remaining 25 sets on April 1. The agreement did not specify the place of delivery, or the time or place of payment. Which of the following statements is correct?","choices":{"A":"The home goods retailer has no duty to deliver the 25 sets on March 1 at the retailer's place of business unless the apartment complex tenders the contract price for the 25 sets on that date.","B":"The home goods retailer must deliver 25 sets on March 1, and the apartment complex must pay the contract price for the 25 sets within a reasonable time after their delivery.","C":"The home goods retailer must deliver 25 sets on March 1, but the apartment complex's payment is due only upon the delivery of all 50 sets.","D":"The home goods retailer must tender 25 sets to the apartment complex at the complex's place of business on March 1, but does not have to turn them over to the complex until it pays the contract price for the 25 sets."},"answer":"A","explanation":"UCC gap-fillers\n\n(effect of missing terms)\n\nMissing terms Gap-fillers\n\nPlace of delivery Seller's place of business if applicable Seller's home Some other place if parties know goods are there Time for shipment/delivery Reasonable time\n\nPrice Reasonable price at time for delivery\n\nTime/place for payment Time & place buyer is to receive goods\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, faucets) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). The UCC \"fills the gap\" when certain terms are missing from a contract. For example, the gap filler for a missing place of delivery is generally the seller's place of business if the seller has one. And unless the contract specifies otherwise, payment is due at the time and place the buyer is to receive each shipment of goods. In that case, the seller is only required to deliver the goods if the buyer tenders the payment. Here, the home goods retailer contracted to sell bathroom faucets to the apartment complex. The time for delivery is specified (ie, 25 sets on March 1 and 25 sets on April 1). But since the place of delivery is missing, it must be made at the retailer's place of business—not the apartment complex's (Choice D). And since the time and place for payment are also missing, the contract price for the first 25 sets is due on March 1 (Choices B & C). Therefore, the retailer has no duty to deliver these sets unless the apartment complex tenders payment on that date.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC fills the gap when certain terms are missing from a sale-of-goods contract. For example, the gap filler for the place of delivery is generally the seller's place of business if the seller has one. And unless the contract specifies otherwise, payment is due at the time and place the buyer is to receive each shipment of goods.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-308 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (providing gap filler when place for delivery is missing).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-310 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (providing gap filler when time and place for payment are missing).","educationalObjective":"The UCC fills the gap when certain terms are missing from a sale-of-goods contract. For example, the gap filler for the place of delivery is generally the seller's place of business if the seller has one. And unless the contract specifies otherwise, payment is due at the time and place the buyer is to receive each shipment of goods.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-308 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (providing gap filler when place for delivery is missing).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-310 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (providing gap filler when time and place for payment are missing).","rule":"The UCC fills the gap when certain terms are missing from a sale-of-goods contract. For example, the gap filler for the place of delivery is generally the seller's place of business if the seller has one. And unless the contract specifies otherwise, payment is due at the time and place the buyer is to receive each shipment of goods.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements is correct?","rule":"The UCC fills the gap when certain terms are missing from a sale-of-goods contract. For example, the gap filler for the place of delivery is generally the seller's place of business if the seller has one. And unless the contract specifies otherwise, payment is due at the time and place the buyer is to receive each shipment of goods.","analysis":"UCC gap-fillers\n\n(effect of missing terms)\n\nMissing terms Gap-fillers\n\nPlace of delivery Seller's place of business if applicable Seller's home Some other place if parties know goods are there Time for shipment/delivery Reasonable time\n\nPrice Reasonable price at time for delivery\n\nTime/place for payment Time & place buyer is to receive goods\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, faucets) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). The UCC \"fills the gap\" when certain terms are missing from a contract. For example, the gap filler for a missing place of delivery is generally the seller's place of business if the seller has one. And unless the contract specifies otherwise, payment is due at the time and place the buyer is to receive each shipment of goods. In that case, the seller is only required to deliver the goods if the buyer tenders the payment. Here, the home goods retailer contracted to sell bathroom faucets to the apartment complex. The time for delivery is specified (ie, 25 sets on March 1 and 25 sets on April 1). But since the place of delivery is missing, it must be made at the retailer's place of business—not the apartment complex's (Choice D). And since the time and place for payment are also missing, the contract price for the first 25 sets is due on March 1 (Choices B & C). Therefore, the retailer has no duty to deliver these sets unless the apartment complex tenders payment on that date.","conclusion":"Answer A: The home goods retailer has no duty to deliver the 25 sets on March 1 at the retailer's place of business unless the apartment complex tenders the contract price for the 25 sets on that date."},"questionZh":"某家居用品零售商与某公寓楼签订书面合同，向该公寓楼销售50套相同的指定浴室​​水龙头，其中25套将于3月1日交付，其余25套于4月1日交付。协议没有具体说明交付地点、付款时间或地点。以下哪项陈述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"家居用品零售商没有义务于 3 月 1 日在零售商营业地点交付这 25 套，除非公寓大楼在该日投标了 25 套的合同价格。","B":"家居用品零售商必须在3月1日交付25套，公寓大楼必须在交付后的合理时间内支付这25套的合同价格。","C":"家居用品零售商必须在 3 月 1 日交付 25 套，但公寓大楼只有在交付全部 50 套后才需要付款。","D":"家居用品零售商必须在 3 月 1 日向公寓大楼的营业地点招标 25 套，但在支付这 25 套合同价格之前不必将其移交给公寓大楼。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 填补空白\n\n（缺失条款的影响）\n\n缺失术语 填补空白\n\n交货地点 卖方的营业地点（如果适用） 卖方的家 双方知道货物在那里的其他地点 装运/交货时间 合理时间\n\n价格 交货时价格合理\n\n付款时间/地点 买家收到货物的时间和地点\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品销售合同（例如水龙头）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。当合同中缺少某些条款时，UCC“填补了空白”。例如，如果卖方有营业地点，则缺少交货地点的填补通常是卖方的营业地点。除非合同另有规定，否则应在买方收到每批货物的时间和地点付款。在这种情况下，只有在买方付款的情况下，卖方才需要交付货物。在这里，家居用品零售商签订了向公寓大楼销售浴室水龙头的合同。指定发货时间（即3月1日发货25套，4月1日发货25套）。但由于缺少交货地点，因此必须在零售商的营业地点进行，而不是在公寓大楼的地点（选择 D）。由于付款时间和地点也缺失，前 25 套的合同价格将于 3 月 1 日到期（选择 B 和 C）。因此，除非公寓大楼在该日期付款，否则零售商没有义务交付这些套装。\n\n教育目标：当货物销售合同中缺少某些条款时，UCC 填补了空白。例如，如果卖方有营业地点，则交货地点的填补位置通常是卖方的营业地点。除非合同另有规定，否则应在买方收到每批货物的时间和地点付款。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-308 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（当交付地点缺失时提供空白填充）。\n\nUCC § 2-310 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（在付款时间和地点缺失时提供填补空白）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当货物销售合同中缺少某些条款时，UCC 填补了空白。例如，如果卖方有营业地点，则交货地点的填补位置通常是卖方的营业地点。除非合同另有规定，否则应在买方收到每批货物的时间和地点付款。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述是正确的？","rule":"当货物销售合同中缺少某些条款时，UCC 填补了空白。例如，如果卖方有营业地点，则交货地点的填补位置通常是卖方的营业地点。除非合同另有规定，否则应在买方收到每批货物的时间和地点付款。","analysis":"UCC 填补空白\n\n（缺失条款的影响）\n\n缺失术语 填补空白\n\n交货地点 卖方的营业地点（如果适用） 卖方的家 双方知道货物在那里的其他地点 装运/交货时间 合理时间\n\n价格 交货时价格合理\n\n付款时间/地点 买家收到货物的时间和地点\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品销售合同（例如水龙头）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。当合同中缺少某些条款时，UCC“填补了空白”。例如，如果卖方有营业地点，则缺少交货地点的填补通常是卖方的营业地点。除非合同另有规定，否则应在买方收到每批货物的时间和地点付款。在这种情况下，只有在买方付款的情况下，卖方才需要交付货物。在这里，家居用品零售商签订了向公寓大楼销售浴室水龙头的合同。指定发货时间（即3月1日发货25套，4月1日发货25套）。但由于缺少交货地点，因此必须在零售商的营业地点进行，而不是在公寓大楼的地点（选择 D）。由于付款时间和地点也缺失，前 25 套的合同价格将于 3 月 1 日到期（选择 B 和 C）。因此，除非公寓大楼在该日期付款，否则零售商没有义务交付这些套装。\n\n教育目标：当货物销售合同中缺少某些条款时，UCC 填补了空白。例如，如果卖方有营业地点，则交货地点的填补位置通常是卖方的营业地点。除非合同另有规定，否则应在买方收到每批货物的时间和地点付款。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-308 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（当交付地点缺失时提供空白填充）。\n\nUCC § 2-310 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（在付款时间和地点缺失时提供填补空白）。","conclusion":"答案 A：家居用品零售商没有义务于 3 月 1 日在零售商营业地点交付这 25 套，除非公寓大楼在该日投标了 25 套的合同价格。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":106,"sourceQuestionPage":312,"sourceQuestionPages":[312],"sourceExplanationPages":[313,314],"sourceAnswerPages":[313,314],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-107","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A bakery offered a chef a permanent full-time job as a pastry chef at a salary of $3,000 per month. The chef agreed to take the position and to begin work in two weeks. In her employment application, the chef had indicated that she was seeking a permanent job. One week after the chef was hired by the bakery, a hotel offered the chef a position as a restaurant manager at a salary of $3,500 a month. The chef accepted and promptly notified the bakery that she would not report for work at the bakery. Is the bakery likely to prevail in a lawsuit against the chef for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because a contract for permanent employment would be interpreted to mean that the chef could leave at any time.","B":"No, because the position the chef took with the hotel was not substantially comparable to the one she had agreed to take with the bakery.","C":"Yes, because the chef's acceptance of a permanent position meant that she agreed to leave the bakery only after a reasonable time.","D":"Yes, because the chef's failure to give the bakery a chance to match the salary offered by the hotel breached the implied right of first refusal."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Employment contracts\n\nDefinite duration Termination allowed only for cause or by mutual agreement\n\nIndefinite At-will employment can be terminated by either party at any time & duration for any reason\n\nPermanent Exception: termination violates public policy duration\n\nA contract to employ someone for an indefinite or permanent duration typically creates an at-will employment relationship. An at-will employment relationship allows either party to terminate the employment contract at any time and for any reason unless the termination violates public policy—eg, when an employee is discharged for filing a discrimination claim. Here, the contract for permanent employment formed an at-will employment relationship that allowed the chef to leave the bakery at any time (Choice C). The chef's termination of the employment contract did not violate public policy because society wants at-will employees to be free to take on new opportunities. Therefore, the chef did not breach the contract by accepting the job with the hotel, and the bakery is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) Whether the position the chef took with the hotel is substantially comparable to the position at the bakery would be relevant had the bakery breached the contract. In this scenario, the chef would have then had a duty to mitigate damages by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment. (Choice D) A right of first refusal is an express contractual right that gives the holder of the right the option to contract with a party before anyone else. This right cannot be created by implication.\n\nEducational objective: A contract for permanent employment creates an at-will employment relationship that entitles either party to terminate the contract at any time and for any reason, without the termination being considered a breach.\n\nReferences\n27 Am. Jur. 2d Employment Relationship § 30 (2020) (explaining that a permanent employment agreement is an at-will employment contract).","educationalObjective":"A contract for permanent employment creates an at-will employment relationship that entitles either party to terminate the contract at any time and for any reason, without the termination being considered a breach.","references":"27 Am. Jur. 2d Employment Relationship § 30 (2020) (explaining that a permanent employment agreement is an at-will employment contract).","rule":"A contract for permanent employment creates an at-will employment relationship that entitles either party to terminate the contract at any time and for any reason, without the termination being considered a breach.","irac":{"issue":"Is the bakery likely to prevail in a lawsuit against the chef for breach of contract?","rule":"A contract for permanent employment creates an at-will employment relationship that entitles either party to terminate the contract at any time and for any reason, without the termination being considered a breach.","analysis":"Employment contracts\n\nDefinite duration Termination allowed only for cause or by mutual agreement\n\nIndefinite At-will employment can be terminated by either party at any time & duration for any reason\n\nPermanent Exception: termination violates public policy duration\n\nA contract to employ someone for an indefinite or permanent duration typically creates an at-will employment relationship. An at-will employment relationship allows either party to terminate the employment contract at any time and for any reason unless the termination violates public policy—eg, when an employee is discharged for filing a discrimination claim. Here, the contract for permanent employment formed an at-will employment relationship that allowed the chef to leave the bakery at any time (Choice C). The chef's termination of the employment contract did not violate public policy because society wants at-will employees to be free to take on new opportunities. Therefore, the chef did not breach the contract by accepting the job with the hotel, and the bakery is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) Whether the position the chef took with the hotel is substantially comparable to the position at the bakery would be relevant had the bakery breached the contract. In this scenario, the chef would have then had a duty to mitigate damages by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment. (Choice D) A right of first refusal is an express contractual right that gives the holder of the right the option to contract with a party before anyone else. This right cannot be created by implication.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because a contract for permanent employment would be interpreted to mean that the chef could leave at any time."},"questionZh":"一家面包店向一名厨师提供了一份糕点师的永久全职工作，月薪为 3,000 美元。厨师同意接受该职位并在两周后开始工作。在她的就业申请中，厨师表示她正在寻找一份长期工作。厨师被面包店聘用一周后，一家酒店向厨师提供了餐厅经理的职位，月薪为 3,500 美元。厨师接受了，并立即通知面包店，她不会到面包店上班。面包店对厨师提起违约诉讼，有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为长期雇佣合同将被解释为厨师可以随时离开。","B":"不，因为厨师在酒店所采取的立场与她同意在面包店所采取的立场没有本质上的可比性。","C":"是的，因为厨师接受永久职位意味着她同意在合理的时间后离开面包店。","D":"是的，因为厨师没有给面包店提供与酒店提供的工资相匹配的机会，违反了隐含的优先购买权。"},"explanationZh":"雇佣合同\n\n确定期限 仅因某种原因或经双方同意才允许终止\n\n任何一方均可出于任何原因随时终止无限期随意雇佣关系\n\n永久例外：终止违反公共政策期限\n\n无限期或永久雇佣某人的合同通常会建立随意的雇佣关系。随意雇佣关系允许任何一方随时以任何理由终止雇佣合同，除非终止违反公共政策——例如，当雇员因提出歧视索赔而被解雇时。在这里，永久雇佣合同形成了一种随意雇佣关系，允许厨师随时离开面包店（选项C）。厨师终止雇佣合同并不违反公共政策，因为社会希望员工能够自由地接受新的机会。因此，厨师接受酒店工作并没有违反合同，面包店胜诉的可能性不大。 （选择 B） 如果面包店违反合同，厨师在酒店担任的职位是否与面包店的职位实质上可比，这将是相关的。在这种情况下，厨师有责任通过合理努力确保类似的就业来减轻损失。 （选项 D） 优先购买权是一项明确的合同权利，赋予权利持有人先于任何其他人与一方签订合同的选择权。这项权利不能通过暗示而产生。\n\n教育目标：永久雇佣合同建立了一种随意的雇佣关系，任何一方都有权随时以任何理由终止合同，且终止不被视为违约。\n\n参考文献\n27 上午。尤尔。 2d 雇佣关系 § 30 (2020)（解释永久雇佣协议是随意雇佣合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"永久雇佣合同建立了一种随意的雇佣关系，任何一方都有权随时以任何理由终止合同，且终止不被视为违约。","iracZh":{"issue":"面包店对厨师提起违约诉讼，有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"永久雇佣合同建立了一种随意的雇佣关系，任何一方都有权随时以任何理由终止合同，且终止不被视为违约。","analysis":"雇佣合同\n\n确定期限 仅因某种原因或经双方同意才允许终止\n\n任何一方均可出于任何原因随时终止无限期随意雇佣关系\n\n永久例外：终止违反公共政策期限\n\n无限期或永久雇佣某人的合同通常会建立随意的雇佣关系。随意雇佣关系允许任何一方随时以任何理由终止雇佣合同，除非终止违反公共政策——例如，当雇员因提出歧视索赔而被解雇时。在这里，永久雇佣合同形成了一种随意雇佣关系，允许厨师随时离开面包店（选项C）。厨师终止雇佣合同并不违反公共政策，因为社会希望员工能够自由地接受新的机会。因此，厨师接受酒店工作并没有违反合同，面包店胜诉的可能性不大。 （选择 B） 如果面包店违反合同，厨师在酒店担任的职位是否与面包店的职位实质上可比，这将是相关的。在这种情况下，厨师有责任通过合理努力确保类似的就业来减轻损失。 （选项 D） 优先购买权是一项明确的合同权利，赋予权利持有人先于任何其他人与一方签订合同的选择权。这项权利不能通过暗示而产生。\n\n教育目标：永久雇佣合同建立了一种随意的雇佣关系，任何一方都有权随时以任何理由终止合同，且终止不被视为违约。\n\n参考文献\n27 上午。尤尔。 2d 雇佣关系 § 30 (2020)（解释永久雇佣协议是随意雇佣合同）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为长期雇佣合同将被解释为厨师可以随时离开。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"duty to mitigate","ipa":"/dˈuti tˈu mˈɪtʌɡˌeɪt/","syllables":"duty to mit·i·gate","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"减损义务：守约方须采取合理措施避免可避免损失","contextualMeaning":"减损义务：守约方须采取合理措施避免可避免损失","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"In this scenario, the chef would have then had a duty to mitigate damages by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":107,"sourceQuestionPage":315,"sourceQuestionPages":[315],"sourceExplanationPages":[316,317],"sourceAnswerPages":[316,317],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-108","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A bottling company sent a purchase order to a wholesaler that stated, \"Ship 100,000 empty plastic bottles at the posted price.\" Two days after receipt of this purchase order, the wholesaler shipped the bottles and the bottling company accepted delivery of them. A week after the bottles were delivered, the bottling company received the wholesaler's acknowledgment form, which included a provision disclaiming consequential damages. After having used the wholesaler's bottles in its bottling operations for two months, the bottling company discovered a defect in the bottles that caused liquids to leak from them. The bottling company recalled 10,000 of the bottles that had been filled, incurring lost profits of $40,000. Assuming that all appropriate defenses are timely raised, will the bottling company likely succeed in recovering $40,000 in consequential damages from the wholesaler?","choices":{"A":"No, because buyers are generally not entitled to recover consequential damages.","B":"No, because the bottling company's acceptance of the goods also constituted an acceptance of the terms included in the wholesaler's acknowledgment.","C":"Yes, because the disclaimer of consequential damages is unconscionable.","D":"Yes, because the wholesaler's acknowledgment did not alter the terms of an existing contract between the parties."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Seller's acceptance of buyer's offer\n\n(UCC § 2-206)\n\nMethod Effective Contract terms\n\nPromise to ship When oral promise made All parties are merchants OR terms of offer + When written promise sent terms added by battle-of-forms rule ≥1 party is nonmerchant terms of offer new terms are merely proposed additions Shipment of When goods shipped Terms of offer goods\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC, a seller can accept an offer (eg, purchase order) by shipping or promising to ship the ordered goods. Acceptance by shipment is effective immediately and creates a contract with terms identical to those of the offer. Once created, the contract cannot be modified unless both parties agree to this. And if either party breaches, the other party can recover consequential damages (eg, lost profits). Here, the wholesaler received the bottling company's purchase order and shipped the bottles. That shipment—since it was sent before the acknowledgment form—created a contract on the purchase order's terms. And since the bottling company did not agree to a modification that disclaims consequential damages, the acknowledgment did not alter the terms of the existing contract. Therefore, the bottling company will likely recover $40,000 in consequential damages for its lost profits caused by the wholesaler's breach in sending defective bottles. (Choice A) Either party to a sale-of-goods contract (ie, buyer or seller) can recover consequential damages for breach as long as those damages were foreseeable when the contract was created. (Choice B) The bottling company's acceptance of the bottles could not have constituted an acceptance of the terms included in the wholesaler's acknowledgment since the bottles were delivered and accepted before the acknowledgment was received. (Choice C) A contract can limit or disclaim (ie, exclude) consequential damages unless doing so is unconscionable because it leaves no reasonable remedy for breach. But here,\n\nwhether the disclaimer of consequential damages is unconscionable is irrelevant since that term did not become part of the contract.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods by shipping or promising to ship the goods. Acceptance by shipment is effective immediately and creates a contract with terms identical to those of the offer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that an offer to buy goods can be accepted by shipping or promising to ship the goods).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods by shipping or promising to ship the goods. Acceptance by shipment is effective immediately and creates a contract with terms identical to those of the offer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that an offer to buy goods can be accepted by shipping or promising to ship the goods).","rule":"Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods by shipping or promising to ship the goods. Acceptance by shipment is effective immediately and creates a contract with terms identical to those of the offer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that all appropriate defenses are timely raised, will the bottling company likely succeed in recovering $40,000 in consequential damages from the wholesaler?","rule":"Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods by shipping or promising to ship the goods. Acceptance by shipment is effective immediately and creates a contract with terms identical to those of the offer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Seller's acceptance of buyer's offer\n\n(UCC § 2-206)\n\nMethod Effective Contract terms\n\nPromise to ship When oral promise made All parties are merchants OR terms of offer + When written promise sent terms added by battle-of-forms rule ≥1 party is nonmerchant terms of offer new terms are merely proposed additions Shipment of When goods shipped Terms of offer goods\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC, a seller can accept an offer (eg, purchase order) by shipping or promising to ship the ordered goods. Acceptance by shipment is effective immediately and creates a contract with terms identical to those of the offer. Once created, the contract cannot be modified unless both parties agree to this. And if either party breaches, the other party can recover consequential damages (eg, lost profits). Here, the wholesaler received the bottling company's purchase order and shipped the bottles. That shipment—since it was sent before the acknowledgment form—created a contract on the purchase order's terms. And since the bottling company did not agree to a modification that disclaims consequential damages, the acknowledgment did not alter the terms of the existing contract. Therefore, the bottling company will likely recover $40,000 in consequential damages for its lost profits caused by the wholesaler's breach in sending defective bottles. (Choice A) Either party to a sale-of-goods contract (ie, buyer or seller) can recover consequential damages for breach as long as those damages were foreseeable when the contract was created. (Choice B) The bottling company's acceptance of the bottles could not have constituted an acceptance of the terms included in the wholesaler's acknowledgment since the bottles were delivered and accepted before the acknowledgment was received. (Choice C) A contract can limit or disclaim (ie, exclude) consequential damages unless doing so is unconscionable because it leaves no reasonable remedy for breach. But here,\n\nwhether the disclaimer of consequential damages is unconscionable is irrelevant since that term did not become part of the contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the wholesaler's acknowledgment did not alter the terms of an existing contract between the parties."},"questionZh":"一家装瓶公司向批发商发送了一份采购订单，其中写道：“按标价运送 100,000 个空塑料瓶。”收到采购订单两天后，批发商发货了瓶子，装瓶公司接受了交货。瓶子交付一周后，装瓶公司收到了批发商的确认表，其中包括免责间接损失的条款。在装瓶作业中使用批发商的瓶子两个月后，装瓶公司发现瓶子存在缺陷，导致液体泄漏。装瓶公司召回了 10,000 个已灌装的瓶子，造成了 40,000 美元的利润损失。假设所有适当的抗辩都及时提出，装瓶公司是否可能成功地从批发商那里追回 40,000 美元的间接损失？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为买家通常无权要求赔偿间接损失。","B":"不可以，因为装瓶公司接受货物也构成对批发商确认书中包含的条款的接受。","C":"是的，因为免责间接损害是不合理的。","D":"是的，因为批发商的确认并没有改变双方之间现有合同的条款。"},"explanationZh":"卖方接受买方的报价\n\n（UCC § 2-206）\n\n方法 有效合同条款\n\n承诺发货 作出口头承诺时 各方都是商家或要约条款 + 当书面承诺发送时，通过形式之争规则添加的条款 ≥1 方是非商家要约条款 新条款仅是建议的补充 装运 货物何时装运 要约条款 货物\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC，卖方可以通过运送或承诺运送所订购的货物来接受报价（例如采购订单）。发货接受立即生效，并形成条款与要约相同的合同。合同一旦创建，除非双方同意，否则不得修改。如果任何一方违约，另一方可以获得间接损失（例如利润损失）。在这里，批发商收到了装瓶公司的采购订单并运送了瓶子。该货物（因为它是在确认表之前发送的）根据采购订单的条款创建了一份合同。由于装瓶公司不同意否认间接损害的修改，因此该确认并没有改变现有合同的条款。因此，装瓶公司可能会因批发商违反发送缺陷瓶子而造成的利润损失获得 40,000 美元的间接损失赔偿。 （选择A）货物销售合同的任何一方（即买方或卖方）都可以追偿因违约而造成的间接损失，只要这些损失在合同成立时是可以预见的。 （选择 B） 装瓶公司对瓶子的接受不能构成对批发商确认中包含的条款的接受，因为瓶子是在收到确认之前交付和接受的。 （选择C）合同可以限制或否认（即排除）间接损害，除非这样做是不合理的，因为它没有为违约行为留下合理的补救措施。但在这里，\n\n对间接损害的免责声明是否不合理并不重要，因为该条款并未成为合同的一部分。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，卖方可以通过运送或承诺运送货物来接受购买货物的要约。发货接受立即生效，并形成条款与要约相同的合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释购买商品的要约可以通过运送或承诺运送货物来接受）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，卖方可以通过运送或承诺运送货物来接受购买货物的要约。发货接受立即生效，并形成条款与要约相同的合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"假设所有适当的抗辩都及时提出，装瓶公司是否可能成功地从批发商那里追回 40,000 美元的间接损失？","rule":"根据 UCC，卖方可以通过运送或承诺运送货物来接受购买货物的要约。发货接受立即生效，并形成条款与要约相同的合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"卖方接受买方的报价\n\n（UCC § 2-206）\n\n方法 有效合同条款\n\n承诺发货 作出口头承诺时 各方都是商家或要约条款 + 当书面承诺发送时，通过形式之争规则添加的条款 ≥1 方是非商家要约条款 新条款仅是建议的补充 装运 货物何时装运 要约条款 货物\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC，卖方可以通过运送或承诺运送所订购的货物来接受报价（例如采购订单）。发货接受立即生效，并形成条款与要约相同的合同。合同一旦创建，除非双方同意，否则不得修改。如果任何一方违约，另一方可以获得间接损失（例如利润损失）。在这里，批发商收到了装瓶公司的采购订单并运送了瓶子。该货物（因为它是在确认表之前发送的）根据采购订单的条款创建了一份合同。由于装瓶公司不同意否认间接损害的修改，因此该确认并没有改变现有合同的条款。因此，装瓶公司可能会因批发商违反发送缺陷瓶子而造成的利润损失获得 40,000 美元的间接损失赔偿。 （选择A）货物销售合同的任何一方（即买方或卖方）都可以追偿因违约而造成的间接损失，只要这些损失在合同成立时是可以预见的。 （选择 B） 装瓶公司对瓶子的接受不能构成对批发商确认中包含的条款的接受，因为瓶子是在收到确认之前交付和接受的。 （选择C）合同可以限制或否认（即排除）间接损害，除非这样做是不合理的，因为它没有为违约行为留下合理的补救措施。但在这里，\n\n对间接损害的免责声明是否不合理并不重要，因为该条款并未成为合同的一部分。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，卖方可以通过运送或承诺运送货物来接受购买货物的要约。发货接受立即生效，并形成条款与要约相同的合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释购买商品的要约可以通过运送或承诺运送货物来接受）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为批发商的确认并没有改变双方之间现有合同的条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":108,"sourceQuestionPage":318,"sourceQuestionPages":[318],"sourceExplanationPages":[319,320],"sourceAnswerPages":[319,320],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-109","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A woman obtained a bid of $10,000 to tear down her old building and another bid of $90,000 to replace it with a new structure in which she planned to operate a sporting goods store. Having only limited cash available, the woman asked a local businessman for a $100,000 loan. After reviewing the plans for the project, the businessman in a signed writing promised to lend the woman $100,000 secured by a mortgage on the property and repayable over 10 years in equal monthly installments at 10% annual interest. The woman promptly accepted the demolition bid and the old building was removed, but the businessman thereafter refused to make the loan. Despite diligent efforts, the woman was unable to obtain a loan from any other source. Does the woman have a cause of action against the businessman?","choices":{"A":"No, because the woman's inability to obtain a loan from any other source demonstrated that the project lacked the financial soundness that was a constructive condition to the businessman's performance.","B":"No, because there was no bargained-for exchange of consideration for the businessman's promise to make the loan.","C":"Yes, because by having the building demolished, she accepted the businessman's offer to make the loan.","D":"Yes, because her reliance on the businessman's promise was substantial, reasonable, and foreseeable."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The equitable doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages stemming from another's failure to perform a promise when there is no valid contract (as seen here). Recovery under promissory estoppel is warranted if: the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise reliance on the promise caused the enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise. Here, the woman had not accepted the businessman's loan offer, so there was no valid contract between the two. However, the woman substantially, reasonably, and foreseeably relied on the businessman's promise of a loan by accepting the demolition bid. As a result, she can assert a cause of action for promissory estoppel against the businessman (and will likely prevail). (Choice A) A constructive condition is one that is not expressly stated in a contract but is implied by law. Financial soundness of a project is not a constructive condition to performance of a party's promise to make a loan. (Choice B) There was no bargained-for exchange of consideration for the businessman's promise, which would be required to recover under a breach-of-contract theory. But consideration is not needed to recover under a claim of promissory estoppel. (Choice C) There are no facts indicating that the businessman authorized the woman to accept his offer to make a loan by demolishing the building. Therefore, this is not a basis for a cause of action against the businessman.\n\nEducational objective: Promissory estoppel can be used to enforce a promise when no valid contract has been formed if (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the promise did induce such reliance, (3) that reliance caused the other party substantial detriment, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promissory estoppel).","educationalObjective":"Promissory estoppel can be used to enforce a promise when no valid contract has been formed if (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the promise did induce such reliance, (3) that reliance caused the other party substantial detriment, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promissory estoppel).","rule":"Promissory estoppel can be used to enforce a promise when no valid contract has been formed if (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the promise did induce such reliance, (3) that reliance caused the other party substantial detriment, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.","irac":{"issue":"Does the woman have a cause of action against the businessman?","rule":"Promissory estoppel can be used to enforce a promise when no valid contract has been formed if (1) the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise, (2) the promise did induce such reliance, (3) that reliance caused the other party substantial detriment, and (4) injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise.","analysis":"The equitable doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages stemming from another's failure to perform a promise when there is no valid contract (as seen here). Recovery under promissory estoppel is warranted if: the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise reliance on the promise caused the enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise. Here, the woman had not accepted the businessman's loan offer, so there was no valid contract between the two. However, the woman substantially, reasonably, and foreseeably relied on the businessman's promise of a loan by accepting the demolition bid. As a result, she can assert a cause of action for promissory estoppel against the businessman (and will likely prevail). (Choice A) A constructive condition is one that is not expressly stated in a contract but is implied by law. Financial soundness of a project is not a constructive condition to performance of a party's promise to make a loan. (Choice B) There was no bargained-for exchange of consideration for the businessman's promise, which would be required to recover under a breach-of-contract theory. But consideration is not needed to recover under a claim of promissory estoppel. (Choice C) There are no facts indicating that the businessman authorized the woman to accept his offer to make a loan by demolishing the building. Therefore, this is not a basis for a cause of action against the businessman.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because her reliance on the businessman's promise was substantial, reasonable, and foreseeable."},"questionZh":"一名妇女获得了 10,000 美元的投标，拆除了她的旧建筑，并获得了 90,000 美元的投标，以建造一座新建筑，她计划在其中经营一家体育用品店。由于现金有限，这位女士向当地商人借了 10 万美元。在审查了该项目的计划后，该商人在一份签署的书面文件中承诺以房产抵押贷款的形式向该女子借出10万美元，并在10年内按月等额分期偿还，年利率为10%。女子立即接受了拆迁标书，旧楼被拆除，但商人随后拒绝贷款。尽管尽了努力，该妇女仍无法从任何其他来源获得贷款。该女子是否有针对该商人的诉讼理由？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为该妇女无法从任何其他来源获得贷款，这表明该项目缺乏财务稳健性，而财务稳健性是商人业绩的建设性条件。","B":"不，因为商人承诺提供贷款并没有通过讨价还价交换对价。","C":"是的，因为通过拆除建筑物，她接受了商人的贷款提议。","D":"是的，因为她对商人承诺的依赖是实质性的、合理的、可预见的。"},"explanationZh":"允诺禁止反言的衡平法原则允许一方在没有有效合同的情况下追偿因另一方未能履行承诺而造成的损失（如此处所示）。在以下情况下，许诺禁止反言下的赔偿是有保证的： 许诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖 执行方合理地依赖该承诺 对承诺的依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，并且只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。在此，该女子并未接受商人的贷款提议，因此两人之间不存在有效的合同。然而，该妇女在很大程度上、合理且可预见地依赖了商人的贷款承诺，接受了拆迁投标。因此，她可以针对该商人主张禁止反言的诉讼理由（并且很可能会获胜）。 （选项A）推定条件是指合同中未明确规定但法律默示的条件。项目的财务稳健性并不是​​当事人履行贷款承诺的建设性条件。 （选项B） 商人的承诺没有讨价还价交换对价，根据违约理论，这需要收回。但根据承诺禁止反悔的主张，不需要约因恢复。 （选项C） 没有事实表明该商人授权该妇女接受他通过拆除建筑物进行贷款的提议。因此，这不是对该商人提起诉讼的依据。\n\n教育目标：在没有形成有效合同的情况下，可以使用禁止反言来强制执行承诺，如果 (1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖，(2) 承诺确实导致了这种依赖，(3) 依赖给另一方造成了实质性损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在未形成有效合同的情况下，禁止反言可用于强制执行承诺，如果 (1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖，(2) 承诺确实引起了这种信赖，(3) 信赖给另一方造成了重大损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。","iracZh":{"issue":"该女子是否有针对该商人的诉讼理由？","rule":"在未形成有效合同的情况下，禁止反言可用于强制执行承诺，如果 (1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖，(2) 承诺确实引起了这种信赖，(3) 信赖给另一方造成了重大损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。","analysis":"允诺禁止反言的衡平法原则允许一方在没有有效合同的情况下追偿因另一方未能履行承诺而造成的损失（如此处所示）。在以下情况下，许诺禁止反言下的赔偿是有保证的： 许诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的信赖 执行方合理地依赖该承诺 对承诺的依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，并且只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。在此，该女子并未接受商人的贷款提议，因此两人之间不存在有效的合同。然而，该妇女在很大程度上、合理且可预见地依赖了商人的贷款承诺，接受了拆迁投标。因此，她可以针对该商人主张禁止反言的诉讼理由（并且很可能会获胜）。 （选项A）推定条件是指合同中未明确规定但法律默示的条件。项目的财务稳健性并不是​​当事人履行贷款承诺的建设性条件。 （选项B） 商人的承诺没有讨价还价交换对价，根据违约理论，这需要收回。但根据承诺禁止反悔的主张，不需要约因恢复。 （选项C） 没有事实表明该商人授权该妇女接受他通过拆除建筑物进行贷款的提议。因此，这不是对该商人提起诉讼的依据。\n\n教育目标：在没有形成有效合同的情况下，可以使用禁止反言来强制执行承诺，如果 (1) 承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖，(2) 承诺确实导致了这种依赖，(3) 依赖给另一方造成了实质性损害，以及 (4) 只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为她对商人承诺的依赖是实质性的、合理的、可预见的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"constructive condition","ipa":"/kʌnstɹˈəktɪv kʌndˈɪʃən/","syllables":"con·struc·tive con·di·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"推定条件：为确定履行顺序与救济由法律推定的条件","contextualMeaning":"推定条件：为确定履行顺序与救济由法律推定的条件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because the woman's inability to obtain a loan from any other source demonstrated that the project lacked the financial soundness that was a constructive condition to the businessman's performance.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-75a888eff974cc58"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":109,"sourceQuestionPage":321,"sourceQuestionPages":[321],"sourceExplanationPages":[322,323],"sourceAnswerPages":[322,323],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-110","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"The owner of an unoccupied duplex needed to paint its interior in preparation for renting each unit. Before leaving the country on a three-month-long business trip, the owner offered a painter $3,000 to paint the interior of each unit of the duplex. The painter told the owner that he would try to fit the job into his busy schedule. The owner replied that she would be out of the country for three months but would leave the key to each unit under the front doormat. There was no further communication between them. Ten weeks later the painter began to paint one of the duplex units. Concerned that she had heard nothing from the painter about completing the task, the owner emailed a friend asking him to paint the duplex for $3,000. The friend sent an email accepting the owner's offer and began painting the other duplex unit the following day. Is the painter likely to prevail in a breach of contract action against the owner?","choices":{"A":"No, because an offeree must notify an offeror that performance has begun in order to create a contractual obligation between them.","B":"No, because notification is required to enforce a contractual duty when the offeror has no convenient way of ascertaining if performance has begun.","C":"Yes, because an offeree may always accept an offer by making a return promise or by beginning performance.","D":"Yes, because notification that an offeree has begun to perform is only required if the offeror explicitly requested notification."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An offer to form a bilateral contract—ie, an offer that invites acceptance by return promise—can be expressly accepted with a return promise or impliedly accepted by starting performance.* Notice is not required to accept an offer by starting performance unless the offer so requires (Choice A). However, if the offeree should know that the offeror has no convenient way of learning within a reasonable time that performance has begun, the offeree must notify the offeror. Failure to do so will discharge the offeror's contractual duty (Choice D). Here, the duplex owner offered the painter $3,000 to paint the interior of each unit. Although the painter made no return promise (he would \"try to fit the job into his schedule\"), he began to paint the duplex 10 weeks later (acceptance by starting performance). The painter knew that the owner was out of the country (no convenient way to learn about performance), so he had to notify her that performance had begun. Since he did not, the owner's contractual duty was discharged, and the painter is not likely to prevail in his breach-of-contract action. *Acceptance is also implied when the offeree accepts a valuable benefit (eg, money, property, services) by conduct or failure to act. This method of acceptance is not available here because the valuable benefit ($3,000 payment) could not be accepted until performance was completed.. (Choice C) The assertion that an offeree can always accept an offer by making a return promise or by beginning performance is overbroad. The offeror, as master of the offer, can dictate the manner or means of acceptance.\n\nEducational objective: Notice is not required to effectively accept an offer by starting performance unless the offer so requires. But if the offeree should know that the offeror has no convenient way of learning within a reasonable time that performance has begun, the offeree must notify the offeror. Otherwise, the offeror's contractual duty will be discharged.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of performance by offeree where offer invites either performance or promise).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 54 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (acceptance by performance and necessity of notification to offeror).","educationalObjective":"Notice is not required to effectively accept an offer by starting performance unless the offer so requires. But if the offeree should know that the offeror has no convenient way of learning within a reasonable time that performance has begun, the offeree must notify the offeror. Otherwise, the offeror's contractual duty will be discharged.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of performance by offeree where offer invites either performance or promise).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 54 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (acceptance by performance and necessity of notification to offeror).","rule":"Notice is not required to effectively accept an offer by starting performance unless the offer so requires. But if the offeree should know that the offeror has no convenient way of learning within a reasonable time that performance has begun, the offeree must notify the offeror. Otherwise, the offeror's contractual duty will be discharged.","irac":{"issue":"Is the painter likely to prevail in a breach of contract action against the owner?","rule":"Notice is not required to effectively accept an offer by starting performance unless the offer so requires. But if the offeree should know that the offeror has no convenient way of learning within a reasonable time that performance has begun, the offeree must notify the offeror. Otherwise, the offeror's contractual duty will be discharged.","analysis":"An offer to form a bilateral contract—ie, an offer that invites acceptance by return promise—can be expressly accepted with a return promise or impliedly accepted by starting performance.* Notice is not required to accept an offer by starting performance unless the offer so requires (Choice A). However, if the offeree should know that the offeror has no convenient way of learning within a reasonable time that performance has begun, the offeree must notify the offeror. Failure to do so will discharge the offeror's contractual duty (Choice D). Here, the duplex owner offered the painter $3,000 to paint the interior of each unit. Although the painter made no return promise (he would \"try to fit the job into his schedule\"), he began to paint the duplex 10 weeks later (acceptance by starting performance). The painter knew that the owner was out of the country (no convenient way to learn about performance), so he had to notify her that performance had begun. Since he did not, the owner's contractual duty was discharged, and the painter is not likely to prevail in his breach-of-contract action. *Acceptance is also implied when the offeree accepts a valuable benefit (eg, money, property, services) by conduct or failure to act. This method of acceptance is not available here because the valuable benefit ($3,000 payment) could not be accepted until performance was completed.. (Choice C) The assertion that an offeree can always accept an offer by making a return promise or by beginning performance is overbroad. The offeror, as master of the offer, can dictate the manner or means of acceptance.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because notification is required to enforce a contractual duty when the offeror has no convenient way of ascertaining if performance has begun."},"questionZh":"一套闲置复式公寓的业主需要粉刷其内部，为出租每个单元做准备。在离开该国进行为期三个月的商务旅行之前，业主向油漆工提供了 3,000 美元，要求他为复式公寓的每个单元进行内部油漆。油漆工告诉业主，他会尽量在繁忙的日程中安排这项工作。房主回答说，她将出国三个月，但会将每个单元的钥匙留在前门垫下。他们之间没有进一步的交流。十周后，画家开始粉刷其中一套复式单元。由于担心没有从油漆工那里听到任何有关完成任务的消息，业主给一位朋友发了电子邮件，请他以 3,000 美元的价格对复式公寓进行油漆。这位朋友发送了一封电子邮件接受业主的提议，并于第二天开始粉刷另一个复式单元。油漆工在针对业主的违约诉讼中是否可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为受要约人必须通知要约人履约已经开始，以便在他们之间建立合同义务。","B":"不可以，因为当要约人无法方便地确定履行是否已经开始时，需要发出通知来执行合同义务。","C":"是的，因为受要约人总是可以通过作出回报承诺或开始履行来接受要约。","D":"是的，因为仅当要约人明确请求通知时才需要通知受要约人已开始履行。"},"explanationZh":"形成双边合同的要约（即邀请通过退货承诺接受的要约）可以通过退货承诺明确接受，也可以通过开始履行来默示接受。*除非要约有此要求，否则不需要通过开始履行来接受要约（选择 A）。然而，如果受要约人知道要约人在合理时间内没有方便的方式获悉履行已经开始，则受要约人必须通知要约人。不这样做将解除要约人的合同义务（选择 D）。在这里，复式公寓的业主向油漆工提供了 3,000 美元来油漆每个单元的内部。尽管油漆工没有做出任何回报承诺（他会“尽力将这项工作纳入他的日程安排”），但他在 10 周后开始粉刷复式公寓（通过开始履行来接受）。画家知道主人出国了（没有方便的方式了解表演），所以他必须通知她表演开始了。由于他没有这样做，业主的合同义务就被解除了，油漆工的违约诉讼不太可能胜诉。 *当受要约人通过行为或不行为接受有价值的利益（例如金钱、财产、服务）时，也意味着接受。这种接受方法在这里不可用，因为在履行完成之前无法接受有价值的利益（3,000 美元付款）。（选项 C）受要约人始终可以通过做出回报承诺或开始履行来接受要约的主张过于宽泛。要约人作为要约的主人，可以决定接受的方式或方式。\n\n教育目标：除非要约有要求，否则不需要通知即可通过开始履行来有效接受要约。但如果受要约人知道要约人在履行开始的合理时间内没有方便的方式获悉，则受要约人必须通知要约人。否则，要约人的合同义务将被解除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 62（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约邀请履行或承诺的受要约人履行的效力）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 54（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（通过履行接受并通知要约人的必要性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非要约有此要求，否则不需要发出通知即可通过开始履约来有效接受要约。但如果受要约人知道要约人在履行开始的合理时间内没有方便的方式获悉，则受要约人必须通知要约人。否则，要约人的合同义务将被解除。","iracZh":{"issue":"油漆工在针对业主的违约诉讼中是否可能胜诉？","rule":"除非要约有此要求，否则不需要发出通知即可通过开始履约来有效接受要约。但如果受要约人知道要约人在履行开始的合理时间内没有方便的方式获悉，则受要约人必须通知要约人。否则，要约人的合同义务将被解除。","analysis":"形成双边合同的要约（即邀请通过退货承诺接受的要约）可以通过退货承诺明确接受，也可以通过开始履行来默示接受。*除非要约有此要求，否则不需要通过开始履行来接受要约（选择 A）。然而，如果受要约人知道要约人在合理时间内没有方便的方式获悉履行已经开始，则受要约人必须通知要约人。不这样做将解除要约人的合同义务（选择 D）。在这里，复式公寓的业主向油漆工提供了 3,000 美元来油漆每个单元的内部。尽管油漆工没有做出任何回报承诺（他会“尽力将这项工作纳入他的日程安排”），但他在 10 周后开始粉刷复式公寓（通过开始履行来接受）。画家知道主人出国了（没有方便的方式了解表演），所以他必须通知她表演开始了。由于他没有这样做，业主的合同义务就被解除了，油漆工的违约诉讼不太可能胜诉。 *当受要约人通过行为或不行为接受有价值的利益（例如金钱、财产、服务）时，也意味着接受。这种接受方法在这里不可用，因为在履行完成之前无法接受有价值的利益（3,000 美元付款）。（选项 C）受要约人始终可以通过做出回报承诺或开始履行来接受要约的主张过于宽泛。要约人作为要约的主人，可以决定接受的方式或方式。\n\n教育目标：除非要约有要求，否则不需要通知即可通过开始履行来有效接受要约。但如果受要约人知道要约人在履行开始的合理时间内没有方便的方式获悉，则受要约人必须通知要约人。否则，要约人的合同义务将被解除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 62（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约邀请履行或承诺的受要约人履行的效力）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 54（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（通过履行接受并通知要约人的必要性）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为当要约人无法方便地确定履行是否已经开始时，需要发出通知来执行合同义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-32737355faf54250"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":110,"sourceQuestionPage":324,"sourceQuestionPages":[324],"sourceExplanationPages":[325,326],"sourceAnswerPages":[325,326],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-111","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A buyer sent a signed letter to a seller that stated: \"Ship 100 boxes of nails at $3 per box, the price quoted in your circular.\" The seller mailed the buyer a signed form acknowledgment that agreed to the buyer's terms and stated on the reverse side: \"Disputes regarding quality shall be arbitrated.\" The buyer did not reply to the seller's acknowledgment, and the seller shipped the nails. When the buyer received the nails, he found their quality to be unsatisfactory and sued the seller for breach of warranty. The seller has asked an attorney whether the parties' contract requires arbitration of the buyer's claim. What is the best advice the attorney can provide?","choices":{"A":"A contract was formed pursuant to conduct when the buyer received the nails, and a court would exclude the arbitration provision from the contract.","B":"A contract was formed when the seller mailed its acknowledgment, and the arbitration term became part of the contract.","C":"A contract was formed when the seller mailed its acknowledgment, and the court must decide whether the arbitration term should be excluded as a material alteration of the contract.","D":"No contract exists, because the arbitration term in the seller's acknowledgment created a counteroffer that the buyer never accepted."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nThe UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods, uses the battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an acceptance of an offer is effective even if the acceptance contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless: the offer expressly requires the offeror's assent to new terms the offeror objects within a reasonable time or the new terms materially alter the contract. Here, the buyer ordered 100 boxes of nails at $3 per box (offer). The seller mailed an acknowledgment agreeing to the buyer's terms (acceptance), which was effective upon dispatch. And although that acknowledgment contained a new arbitration term, the buyer's offer did not expressly require assent to new terms, and the buyer did not object to the new term. Therefore, since both parties are likely merchants, the court must decide whether the arbitration term should be excluded as a material alteration of the contract. (Choice A) Had the seller shipped the nails prior to mailing the acknowledgment, his conduct would have created a contract when the nails were shipped (not received). Here, a contract was created pursuant to the seller's mailed acknowledgment—not his conduct in shipping the nails.\n\n(Choice B) Jurisdictions are split on whether the addition of an arbitration term materially alters a contract, so the court would need to decide this issue to determine whether the arbitration term became part of the contract. (Choice D) Under the common law mirror image rule, the seller's acknowledgment containing a new term would constitute a counteroffer. However, the UCC follows the battle-of-the-forms rule instead.\n\nEducational objective: Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).","educationalObjective":"Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).","rule":"Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"What is the best advice the attorney can provide?","rule":"Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nThe UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods, uses the battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an acceptance of an offer is effective even if the acceptance contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless: the offer expressly requires the offeror's assent to new terms the offeror objects within a reasonable time or the new terms materially alter the contract. Here, the buyer ordered 100 boxes of nails at $3 per box (offer). The seller mailed an acknowledgment agreeing to the buyer's terms (acceptance), which was effective upon dispatch. And although that acknowledgment contained a new arbitration term, the buyer's offer did not expressly require assent to new terms, and the buyer did not object to the new term. Therefore, since both parties are likely merchants, the court must decide whether the arbitration term should be excluded as a material alteration of the contract. (Choice A) Had the seller shipped the nails prior to mailing the acknowledgment, his conduct would have created a contract when the nails were shipped (not received). Here, a contract was created pursuant to the seller's mailed acknowledgment—not his conduct in shipping the nails.\n\n(Choice B) Jurisdictions are split on whether the addition of an arbitration term materially alters a contract, so the court would need to decide this issue to determine whether the arbitration term became part of the contract. (Choice D) Under the common law mirror image rule, the seller's acknowledgment containing a new term would constitute a counteroffer. However, the UCC follows the battle-of-the-forms rule instead.","conclusion":"Answer C: A contract was formed when the seller mailed its acknowledgment, and the court must decide whether the arbitration term should be excluded as a material alteration of the contract."},"questionZh":"一位买家向卖家发送了一封签名信，信中写道：“以每盒 3 美元的价格运送 100 盒钉子，价格在您的通知中。”卖方向买方邮寄了一份签字的确认书，同意买方的条款，并在背面注明：“有关质量的争议应进行仲裁。”买家没有回复卖家的确认，卖家就把钉子发货了。当买家收到钉子时，发现钉子质量不理想，并起诉卖家违反保修条款。卖方已询问律师，双方的合同是否要求对买方的索赔进行仲裁。律师可以提供的最佳建议是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"合同根据买方收到钉子时的行为成立，法院会从合同中排除仲裁条款。","B":"当卖方邮寄确认书时，合同即成立，仲裁条款成为合同的一部分。","C":"当卖方邮寄确认书时，合同即告成立，法院必须决定仲裁条款是否应作为合同的重大变更而被排除。","D":"不存在任何合同，因为卖方确认书中的仲裁条款产生了买方从未接受的还盘。"},"explanationZh":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\nUCC 负责管理货物销售合同，它使用形式斗争规则来订立合同。根据该规则，即使接受要约包含新条款，接受要约也是有效的。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非：要约明确要求要约人同意要约人在合理时间内反对的新条款，或者新条款对合同有重大改变。此处，买家以每盒 3 美元的价格订购了 100 盒钉子（报价）。卖方邮寄了一份同意买方条款的确认书（接受），该确认书在发货后生效。尽管该确认书包含新的仲裁条款，但买方的要约并未明确要求同意新条款，并且买方也没有反对新条款。因此，由于双方都可能是商人，法院必须决定仲裁条款是否应作为合同的重大变更而被排除。 （选择 A）如果卖方在邮寄确认书之前发货了钉子，那么他的行为就会在钉子发货（未收到）时形成合同。在这里，合同是根据卖方邮寄的确认书（而不是他在运输钉子时的行为）制定的。\n\n（选项B）对于增加仲裁条款是否对合同造成实质性改变，司法管辖区存在分歧，因此法院需要就该问题做出裁决，以确定仲裁条款是否成为合同的一部分。 （选项D） 根据普通法镜像规则，卖方确认包含新条款将构成还盘。然而，UCC 却遵循形式之战规则。\n\n教育目标：根据形式之战规则，即使包含新术语，接受也是有效的。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据形式之战规则，即使包含新条款，接受仍然有效。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"律师可以提供的最佳建议是什么？","rule":"根据形式之战规则，即使包含新条款，接受仍然有效。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\nUCC 负责管理货物销售合同，它使用形式斗争规则来订立合同。根据该规则，即使接受要约包含新条款，接受要约也是有效的。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非：要约明确要求要约人同意要约人在合理时间内反对的新条款，或者新条款对合同有重大改变。此处，买家以每盒 3 美元的价格订购了 100 盒钉子（报价）。卖方邮寄了一份同意买方条款的确认书（接受），该确认书在发货后生效。尽管该确认书包含新的仲裁条款，但买方的要约并未明确要求同意新条款，并且买方也没有反对新条款。因此，由于双方都可能是商人，法院必须决定仲裁条款是否应作为合同的重大变更而被排除。 （选择 A）如果卖方在邮寄确认书之前发货了钉子，那么他的行为就会在钉子发货（未收到）时形成合同。在这里，合同是根据卖方邮寄的确认书（而不是他在运输钉子时的行为）制定的。\n\n（选项B）对于增加仲裁条款是否对合同造成实质性改变，司法管辖区存在分歧，因此法院需要就该问题做出裁决，以确定仲裁条款是否成为合同的一部分。 （选项D） 根据普通法镜像规则，卖方确认包含新条款将构成还盘。然而，UCC 却遵循形式之战规则。\n\n教育目标：根据形式之战规则，即使包含新术语，接受也是有效的。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：当卖方邮寄确认书时，合同即告成立，法院必须决定仲裁条款是否应作为合同的重大变更而被排除。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"battle of the forms","ipa":"/bˈætəl ˈəv ðə fˈɔɹmz/","syllables":"bat·tle of the forms","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"格式之战：UCC § 2-207 下表格条款冲突的处理","contextualMeaning":"格式之战：UCC § 2-207 下表格条款冲突的处理","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A buyer sent a signed letter to a seller that stated: \"Ship 100 boxes of nails at $3 per box, the price quoted in your circular.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"material alteration","ipa":"/mʌtˈɪɹiəl ˌɔltɝˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"ma·te·r·ial al·ter·ation","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"重大变更：若纳入会造成意外或实质困难的附加条款","contextualMeaning":"重大变更：若纳入会造成意外或实质困难的附加条款","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A contract was formed when the seller mailed its acknowledgment, and the court must decide whether the arbitration term should be excluded as a material alteration of the contract.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"mirror image rule","ipa":"/mˈɪɹɝ ˈɪmədʒ ɹˈul/","syllables":"mir·ror im·age rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"镜像规则：普通法承诺必须与要约条款一致","contextualMeaning":"镜像规则：普通法承诺必须与要约条款一致","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) Under the common law mirror image rule, the seller's acknowledgment containing a new term would constitute a counteroffer.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"knockout rule","ipa":"/nˈɑkˌaʊt ɹˈul/","syllables":"knock·out rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"抵消规则：冲突条款彼此排除并由 UCC 补缺条款取代","contextualMeaning":"抵消规则：冲突条款彼此排除并由 UCC 补缺条款取代","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"UCC = Uniform Commercial Code Effect of new terms in reply to offer Common law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image) UCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised t","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":111,"sourceQuestionPage":327,"sourceQuestionPages":[327],"sourceExplanationPages":[328,329],"sourceAnswerPages":[328,329],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-112","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"For several weeks, a wealthy, unemployed widow and a car salesman negotiated unsuccessfully over the purchase price of a new luxury sedan, which, as the car salesman knew, the widow wanted her son to have as a wedding gift. On April 27, the salesman sent the widow a signed, dated memo saying, \"If we can arrive at the same price within the next week, do we have a deal?\" The widow wrote \"Yes\" and her signature at the bottom of this memo and delivered it back to the car salesman on April 29. On May 1, the widow wrote the car salesman a signed letter offering to buy \"one new luxury sedan, with all available equipment, for $180,000 cash on delivery not later than June 1.\" By coincidence, the salesman wrote the widow a signed letter on May 1 offering to sell her \"one new luxury sedan, with all available equipment, for $180,000 cash on delivery not later than June 1.\" These letters crossed in the mail and were respectively received and read by the widow and the salesman on May 2. If the widow subsequently asserts and the car company denies that the parties had a binding contract on May 3, which of the following most persuasively supports the widow's position?","choices":{"A":"A sale-of-goods contract does not require that an acceptance be a mirror image of the offer.","B":"A sale-of-goods contract may be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, even though the moment of its making is undetermined.","C":"Since the car company was a merchant in the transaction and the widow was not, the car company is estopped to deny that the parties' correspondence created a binding contract.","D":"With respect both to the making of an agreement and the requirement of consideration, identical cross-offers are functionally the same as an offer followed by a responsive acceptance."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Common law v. UCC\n\nCommon law UCC\n\nPromise to hold Must be supported by No consideration required when offer open consideration (ie, option merchant gives written & signed contract) assurance (ie, firm offer) Contract Must have offer & acceptance Any manner sufficient to show formation with knowledge of that offer agreement\n\nAcceptance Must agree to exact terms of Effective even with new/contradictory offer (ie, mirror-image rule) terms Required terms Description of Description of goods property/service Quantity Quantity Price Time for performance Bilateral Must be supported by new New consideration not required if modification consideration made in good faith\n\nUnilateral Death Impracticability discharge Destruction of subject matter Suing for breach Must be in privity (ie, party to No privity/beneficiary requirement contract) or intended beneficiary UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract formation requires (1) an offer with definite terms and (2) an acceptance with knowledge of that offer. But these formal requirements have been relaxed by the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), which governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, luxury sedans). Under the UCC, contract formation can occur through any manner sufficient to show the parties' agreement—even if the parties negotiate in an unusual way that makes the moment of acceptance uncertain. Here, the widow and the car salesman agreed in writing that they would have a deal if they settled on a price for the luxury sedan within one week. Less than a week later, both parties sent identical offers that crossed in the mail, each proposing a price of $180,000. Neither party knew of the other offer when they sent their own offer (no acceptance under common law). But these cross-offers are sufficient to show that the parties arrived at the same price\n\n(agreement), which supports the widow's assertion that there was a binding contract under the UCC. (Choice A) The UCC does not require that an acceptance be a mirror image of the offer. But this fact is irrelevant here since the two offers did mirror each other. (Choice C) There is no rule estopping (ie, preventing) a merchant from denying that its correspondence created a binding contract. (Choice D) An enforceable sale-of-goods contract requires consideration—ie, a bargained- for exchange of promises or performance. But the method of contract formation has nothing to do with the substance of the parties' exchange. And identical cross-offers can operate as an offer and acceptance only if the parties understood them to have the effect of forming a contract.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a sale-of-goods contract can be made in any manner sufficient to show the parties' agreement—even if they negotiate in an unusual way that makes the moment of acceptance uncertain.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-204 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a contract can be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, even though the moment of its making is undetermined).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a sale-of-goods contract can be made in any manner sufficient to show the parties' agreement—even if they negotiate in an unusual way that makes the moment of acceptance uncertain.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-204 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a contract can be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, even though the moment of its making is undetermined).","rule":"Under the UCC, a sale-of-goods contract can be made in any manner sufficient to show the parties' agreement—even if they negotiate in an unusual way that makes the moment of acceptance uncertain.","irac":{"issue":"If the widow subsequently asserts and the car company denies that the parties had a binding contract on May 3, which of the following most persuasively supports the widow's position?","rule":"Under the UCC, a sale-of-goods contract can be made in any manner sufficient to show the parties' agreement—even if they negotiate in an unusual way that makes the moment of acceptance uncertain.","analysis":"Common law v. UCC\n\nCommon law UCC\n\nPromise to hold Must be supported by No consideration required when offer open consideration (ie, option merchant gives written & signed contract) assurance (ie, firm offer) Contract Must have offer & acceptance Any manner sufficient to show formation with knowledge of that offer agreement\n\nAcceptance Must agree to exact terms of Effective even with new/contradictory offer (ie, mirror-image rule) terms Required terms Description of Description of goods property/service Quantity Quantity Price Time for performance Bilateral Must be supported by new New consideration not required if modification consideration made in good faith\n\nUnilateral Death Impracticability discharge Destruction of subject matter Suing for breach Must be in privity (ie, party to No privity/beneficiary requirement contract) or intended beneficiary UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract formation requires (1) an offer with definite terms and (2) an acceptance with knowledge of that offer. But these formal requirements have been relaxed by the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), which governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, luxury sedans). Under the UCC, contract formation can occur through any manner sufficient to show the parties' agreement—even if the parties negotiate in an unusual way that makes the moment of acceptance uncertain. Here, the widow and the car salesman agreed in writing that they would have a deal if they settled on a price for the luxury sedan within one week. Less than a week later, both parties sent identical offers that crossed in the mail, each proposing a price of $180,000. Neither party knew of the other offer when they sent their own offer (no acceptance under common law). But these cross-offers are sufficient to show that the parties arrived at the same price\n\n(agreement), which supports the widow's assertion that there was a binding contract under the UCC. (Choice A) The UCC does not require that an acceptance be a mirror image of the offer. But this fact is irrelevant here since the two offers did mirror each other. (Choice C) There is no rule estopping (ie, preventing) a merchant from denying that its correspondence created a binding contract. (Choice D) An enforceable sale-of-goods contract requires consideration—ie, a bargained- for exchange of promises or performance. But the method of contract formation has nothing to do with the substance of the parties' exchange. And identical cross-offers can operate as an offer and acceptance only if the parties understood them to have the effect of forming a contract.","conclusion":"Answer B: A sale-of-goods contract may be made in any manner sufficient to show agreement, even though the moment of its making is undetermined."},"questionZh":"几周来，一位富有的失业寡妇和一位汽车推销员就一辆新豪华轿车的购买价格进行了谈判，但没有成功，正如汽车推销员所知，寡妇希望她的儿子能将这辆轿车作为结婚礼物。 4 月 27 日，推销员向寡妇发送了一份签名并注明日期的备忘录，上面写着：“如果我们能在下周内以相同的价格到达，我们可以达成协议吗？”遗孀在这份备忘录底部写下了“是”并签名，并于 4 月 29 日将其交还给汽车销售员。5 月 1 日，遗孀给汽车销售员写了一封签名信，提出在 6 月 1 日之前购买“一辆配备所有可用设备的新豪华轿车，价格为 18 万美元，货到付款”。巧合的是，推销员于 5 月 1 日给寡妇写了一封签名信，提出在 6 月 1 日之前以 18 万美元货到付款的价格向她出售“一辆配备所有可用设备的新豪华轿车”。这些信件在邮件中交叉，分别于 5 月 2 日被遗孀和销售员收到并阅读。如果遗孀随后声称，而汽车公司否认双方在 5 月 3 日签订了具有​​约束力的合同，那么以下哪一项最有说服力地支持遗孀的立场？","choicesZh":{"A":"货物销售合同不要求接受是要约的镜像。","B":"货物销售合同可以以任何足以表明同意的方式订立，即使其订立时间尚未确定。","C":"由于汽车公司是交易中的商人，而寡妇不是，因此汽车公司不能否认双方的信件构成了具有约束力的合同。","D":"就达成协议和对价要求而言，相同的交叉要约在功能上与先要约并随后做出响应性接受相同。"},"explanationZh":"普通法诉 UCC\n\n普通法UCC\n\n承诺持有 必须得到以下方面的支持： 报价时无需对价 公开对价（即，期权商人提供书面并签署的合同） 保证（即，确定要约） 合同 必须有要约和接受 任何足以表明在了解该要约协议的情况下形成的方式\n\n接受 必须同意准确的条款 即使有新的/矛盾的要约（即镜像规则）条款 必需的条款 货物属性/服务描述的描述 数量 数量 价格 履约时间 双边 必须有新的支持 如果善意地作出修改考虑，则不需要新的考虑\n\n单方死亡 不切实际的解除 标的物被毁坏 起诉违约 必须是相互关系（即无相互关系/受益人要求合同的一方）或预期受益人 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同的成立需要（1）有明确条款的要约，以及（2）在了解该要约的情况下接受。但《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 放宽了这些正式要求，该法典管辖商品（例如豪华轿车）销售合同。根据 UCC，合同的订立可以通过任何足以表明双方同意的方式进行，即使双方以不寻常的方式进行谈判，导致接受时间不确定。在这里，寡妇和汽车推销员书面同意，如果他们在一周内确定豪华轿车的价格，他们就会达成协议。不到一周后，双方在邮件中发出了相同的报价，每份报价均为 180,000 美元。当双方发出自己的要约时，双方都不知道对方的要约（普通法下不接受）。但这些交叉报价足以表明双方以相同的价格达成\n\n（协议），支持寡妇的主张，即 UCC 下存在具有约束力的合同。 （选择 A）UCC 不要求接受是要约的镜像。但这一事实在这里无关紧要，因为这两个报价确实相互反映。 （选项C） 没有任何规则可以阻止（即阻止）商人否认其通信创建了具有约束力的合同。 （选项D）可执行的货物销售合同需要对价，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。但合同订立的方式与当事人交换的实质无关。相同的交叉要约只有在双方理解其具有形成合同的效果的情况下才能作为要约和承诺发挥作用。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，货物销售合同可以以任何足以表明双方同意的方式签订，即使他们以不寻常的方式进行谈判，导致接受时间不确定。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-204 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释合同可以以任何足以表明同意的方式订立，即使合同订立的时间尚未确定）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，货物销售合同可以以任何足以表明双方同意的方式签订，即使他们以不寻常的方式进行谈判，导致接受时间不确定。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果遗孀随后提出主张，而汽车公司否认双方在 5 月 3 日签订了具有约束力的合同，那么以下哪一项最有说服力地支持遗孀的立场？","rule":"根据 UCC，货物销售合同可以以任何足以表明双方同意的方式签订，即使他们以不寻常的方式进行谈判，导致接受时间不确定。","analysis":"普通法诉 UCC\n\n普通法UCC\n\n承诺持有 必须得到以下方面的支持： 报价时无需对价 公开对价（即，期权商人提供书面并签署的合同） 保证（即，确定要约） 合同 必须有要约和接受 任何足以表明在了解该要约协议的情况下形成的方式\n\n接受 必须同意准确的条款 即使有新的/矛盾的要约（即镜像规则）条款 必需的条款 货物属性/服务描述的描述 数量 数量 价格 履约时间 双边 必须有新的支持 如果善意地作出修改考虑，则不需要新的考虑\n\n单方死亡 不切实际的解除 标的物被毁坏 起诉违约 必须是相互关系（即无相互关系/受益人要求合同的一方）或预期受益人 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同的成立需要（1）有明确条款的要约，以及（2）在了解该要约的情况下接受。但《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 放宽了这些正式要求，该法典管辖商品（例如豪华轿车）销售合同。根据 UCC，合同的订立可以通过任何足以表明双方同意的方式进行，即使双方以不寻常的方式进行谈判，导致接受时间不确定。在这里，寡妇和汽车推销员书面同意，如果他们在一周内确定豪华轿车的价格，他们就会达成协议。不到一周后，双方在邮件中发出了相同的报价，每份报价均为 180,000 美元。当双方发出自己的要约时，双方都不知道对方的要约（普通法下不接受）。但这些交叉报价足以表明双方以相同的价格达成\n\n（协议），支持寡妇的主张，即 UCC 下存在具有约束力的合同。 （选择 A）UCC 不要求接受是要约的镜像。但这一事实在这里无关紧要，因为这两个报价确实相互反映。 （选项C） 没有任何规则可以阻止（即阻止）商人否认其通信创建了具有约束力的合同。 （选项D）可执行的货物销售合同需要对价，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。但合同订立的方式与当事人交换的实质无关。相同的交叉要约只有在双方理解其具有形成合同的效果的情况下才能作为要约和承诺发挥作用。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，货物销售合同可以以任何足以表明双方同意的方式签订，即使他们以不寻常的方式进行谈判，导致接受时间不确定。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-204 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释合同可以以任何足以表明同意的方式订立，即使合同订立的时间尚未确定）。","conclusion":"答案 B：货物销售合同可以以任何足以表明同意的方式订立，即使其订立时间尚未确定。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"offer and acceptance","ipa":"/ˈɔfɝ ənd æksˈɛptəns/","syllables":"of·fer and ac·cep·tance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"要约与承诺：形成合同合意的基本机制","contextualMeaning":"要约与承诺：形成合同合意的基本机制","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"And identical cross-offers can operate as an offer and acceptance only if the parties understood them to have the effect of forming a contract.","synonyms":["mutual assent","agreement formation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":112,"sourceQuestionPage":330,"sourceQuestionPages":[330],"sourceExplanationPages":[331,332],"sourceAnswerPages":[331,332],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-113","subject":"Contracts","topic":"UCC Formation & Battle of Forms","question":"An appliance store owner received a flyer advertising a cleaning service that dealt exclusively with commercial properties. The owner sent an email to the cleaning service that described the relevant features of the store and asked for a price quote. The owner received a reply email from the cleaning service offering to clean the store for $200 per week for a period of 52 weeks. The store owner responded, \"Fine. I'll pay once a month in advance.\" Has a contract been formed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the cleaning service is not a merchant.","B":"No, because the owner's response contained an additional term.","C":"Yes, because the owner did not expressly condition his acceptance on the cleaning service agreeing to the time-of-payment term.","D":"Yes, because the owner is a merchant."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for services (eg, cleaning services) are governed by the common law, which follows the mirror-image rule for contract formation. Under this aptly named rule, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms is treated as a rejection of the original offer and as a counteroffer.* Here, the cleaning service offered to clean the store for $200 per week for a period of 52 weeks. Because the store owner's response contained an additional term regarding when payment would be made (\"once a month in advance\"), his response was not an acceptance that created a contract. Instead, it constituted both a rejection of the cleaning service's offer and a counteroffer. *Mere suggestions and inquires, including requests for clarification and statements of intent, do not amount to rejections or counteroffers under the mirror-image rule. (Choices A, C & D) Under the UCC battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation, an acceptance that contains different or additional terms is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to those terms. Whether those terms are treated as part of the contract depends on whether the parties are merchants. But this contract is\n\ngoverned by the common law—not the UCC, which governs only contracts for the sale of goods.\n\nEducational objective: Under the common law mirror-image rule for contract formation, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as both a rejection and a counteroffer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 80 (2020) (mirror-image rule).","educationalObjective":"Under the common law mirror-image rule for contract formation, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as both a rejection and a counteroffer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 80 (2020) (mirror-image rule).","rule":"Under the common law mirror-image rule for contract formation, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as both a rejection and a counteroffer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"An appliance store owner received a flyer advertising a cleaning service that dealt exclusively with commercial properties. The owner sent an email to the cleaning service that described the relevant features of the store and asked for a price quote. The owner received a reply email from the cleaning service offering to clean the store for $200 per week for a period of 52 weeks. The store owner responded, \"Fine. I'll pay once a month in advance.\" Has a contract been formed?","rule":"Under the common law mirror-image rule for contract formation, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as both a rejection and a counteroffer. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for services (eg, cleaning services) are governed by the common law, which follows the mirror-image rule for contract formation. Under this aptly named rule, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms is treated as a rejection of the original offer and as a counteroffer.* Here, the cleaning service offered to clean the store for $200 per week for a period of 52 weeks. Because the store owner's response contained an additional term regarding when payment would be made (\"once a month in advance\"), his response was not an acceptance that created a contract. Instead, it constituted both a rejection of the cleaning service's offer and a counteroffer. *Mere suggestions and inquires, including requests for clarification and statements of intent, do not amount to rejections or counteroffers under the mirror-image rule. (Choices A, C & D) Under the UCC battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation, an acceptance that contains different or additional terms is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to those terms. Whether those terms are treated as part of the contract depends on whether the parties are merchants. But this contract is\n\ngoverned by the common law—not the UCC, which governs only contracts for the sale of goods.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the owner's response contained an additional term."},"questionZh":"一家电器店老板收到一张传单，宣传专门针对商业地产的清洁服务。店主给清洁服务人员发了一封电子邮件，描述了商店的相关特色，并询问了报价。店主收到了清洁服务公司的回复电子邮件，提出以每周 200 美元的价格清洁商店，为期 52 周。店主道：“好吧，我每月预付一次。”合同已经成立了吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为清洁服务不是商家。","B":"不，因为所有者的回复包含附加术语。","C":"是的，因为业主没有明确表示接受清洁服务的条件是同意付款时间条款。","D":"是的，因为主人是商人。"},"explanationZh":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n服务合同（例如清洁服务）受普通法管辖，遵循合同订立的镜像规则。根据这条恰当命名的规则，接受必须反映要约的条款。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受将被视为对原始报价的拒绝和还盘。*在此，清洁服务提供每周 200 美元的商店清洁服务，为期 52 周。由于店主的回复包含有关何时付款的附加条款（“提前一个月一次”），因此他的回复并不是创建合同的接受。相反，它既构成了对清洁服务报价的拒绝，也构成了还价。 *纯粹的建议和询问，包括要求澄清和意向声明，并不构成镜像规则下的拒绝或还盘。 （选项 A、C 和 D）根据 UCC 合同形式争夺规则，包含不同或附加条款的承诺是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意这些条款为条件。这些条款是否被视为合同的一部分取决于双方是否是商家。但这份合同是\n\n受普通法管辖，而非 UCC，后者仅管辖货物销售合同。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法合同订立的镜像规则，接受必须反映要约条款。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受既可视为拒绝，也可视为还盘。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 80 (2020)（镜像规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据普通法合同订立的镜像规则，承诺必须反映要约条款。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受既可视为拒绝，也可视为还盘。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"一家电器店老板收到一张传单，宣传专门针对商业地产的清洁服务。店主给清洁服务人员发了一封电子邮件，描述了商店的相关特色，并询问了报价。店主收到了清洁服务公司的回复电子邮件，提出以每周 200 美元的价格清洁商店，为期 52 周。店主道：“好吧，我每月预付一次。”合同已经成立了吗？","rule":"根据普通法合同订立的镜像规则，承诺必须反映要约条款。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受既可视为拒绝，也可视为还盘。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n服务合同（例如清洁服务）受普通法管辖，遵循合同订立的镜像规则。根据这条恰当命名的规则，接受必须反映要约的条款。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受将被视为对原始报价的拒绝和还盘。*在此，清洁服务提供每周 200 美元的商店清洁服务，为期 52 周。由于店主的回复包含有关何时付款的附加条款（“提前一个月一次”），因此他的回复并不是创建合同的接受。相反，它既构成了对清洁服务报价的拒绝，也构成了还价。 *纯粹的建议和询问，包括要求澄清和意向声明，并不构成镜像规则下的拒绝或还盘。 （选项 A、C 和 D）根据 UCC 合同形式争夺规则，包含不同或附加条款的承诺是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意这些条款为条件。这些条款是否被视为合同的一部分取决于双方是否是商家。但这份合同是\n\n受普通法管辖，而非 UCC，后者仅管辖货物销售合同。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法合同订立的镜像规则，接受必须反映要约条款。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受既可视为拒绝，也可视为还盘。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 80 (2020)（镜像规则）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为所有者的回复包含附加术语。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":113,"sourceQuestionPage":333,"sourceQuestionPages":[333],"sourceExplanationPages":[334,335],"sourceAnswerPages":[334,335],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-114","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A national distributor of windows selected a retailer to sell its windows in a specified geographic area. The parties negotiated a written distribution agreement, which stated that any order for windows placed by the retailer would be binding on the distributor \"only when expressly accepted by the distributor.\" For the next two years, the retailer forwarded orders to the distributor, and the distributor always filled the orders. In the third year, the distributor accused the retailer of overcharging customers to install the distributor's windows. The retailer responded that the distributor had no control over the retailer's installation prices. When the distributor received the retailer's next order for windows, it refused to fill the order. If the retailer sues the distributor for breach of contract, will it be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the retailer's forwarding of orders to the distributor did not give rise to an obligation on the distributor's part to fill the orders.","B":"No, because the retailer's practice of overcharging customers excused the distributor's refusal to fill the order.","C":"Yes, because the distributor's claim regarding overcharging customers is independent of its obligation to fill the retailer's order.","D":"Yes, because the parties' course of dealing binds the distributor to fill the retailer's order."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act At common law and under the UCC, contractual obligations must be supported by valuable consideration—i.e., a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. This requirement is not met if either party makes an illusory promise—i.e., one that essentially pledges nothing because it is vague or because the promising party can choose whether to honor it. In that case, there is no contract—and so there can be no breach. Here, the distribution agreement provided that orders placed by the retailer would be binding on the distributor only when expressly accepted by the distributor. Since the distributor can choose whether or not to accept the orders, its promise was illusory. Due to this lack of consideration, the distribution agreement did not create an enforceable contract. This means that when the distributor received the retailer's final order, the distributor was not obligated to fill it. Therefore, the retailer is unlikely to prevail in a breach of contract suit. (Choices B & C) If there had been an enforceable contract limiting the retailer's installation charges, the retailer's alleged practice of overcharging would be a breach that entitled the distributor to refuse additional orders. But since there was no such contract, the retailer's charging practices are irrelevant. (Choice D) Under the UCC, the parties' course of dealing (e.g., filling orders for two years) is relevant to interpreting their agreement. But the express terms of the agreement prevail if they are inconsistent with the parties' course of dealing. Therefore, the parties' course of dealing here does not bind the distributor to fill the retailer's order.\n\nEducational objective: An illusory promise—i.e., one that essentially pledges nothing because it is vague or because the promisor can choose whether to honor it—cannot supply the necessary consideration for contract formation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 2 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that there is no promise when performance is entirely optional).","educationalObjective":"An illusory promise—i.e., one that essentially pledges nothing because it is vague or because the promisor can choose whether to honor it—cannot supply the necessary consideration for contract formation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 2 cmt. e (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that there is no promise when performance is entirely optional).","rule":"An illusory promise—i.e., one that essentially pledges nothing because it is vague or because the promisor can choose whether to honor it—cannot supply the necessary consideration for contract formation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"If the retailer sues the distributor for breach of contract, will it be likely to prevail?","rule":"An illusory promise—i.e., one that essentially pledges nothing because it is vague or because the promisor can choose whether to honor it—cannot supply the necessary consideration for contract formation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act At common law and under the UCC, contractual obligations must be supported by valuable consideration—i.e., a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. This requirement is not met if either party makes an illusory promise—i.e., one that essentially pledges nothing because it is vague or because the promising party can choose whether to honor it. In that case, there is no contract—and so there can be no breach. Here, the distribution agreement provided that orders placed by the retailer would be binding on the distributor only when expressly accepted by the distributor. Since the distributor can choose whether or not to accept the orders, its promise was illusory. Due to this lack of consideration, the distribution agreement did not create an enforceable contract. This means that when the distributor received the retailer's final order, the distributor was not obligated to fill it. Therefore, the retailer is unlikely to prevail in a breach of contract suit. (Choices B & C) If there had been an enforceable contract limiting the retailer's installation charges, the retailer's alleged practice of overcharging would be a breach that entitled the distributor to refuse additional orders. But since there was no such contract, the retailer's charging practices are irrelevant. (Choice D) Under the UCC, the parties' course of dealing (e.g., filling orders for two years) is relevant to interpreting their agreement. But the express terms of the agreement prevail if they are inconsistent with the parties' course of dealing. Therefore, the parties' course of dealing here does not bind the distributor to fill the retailer's order.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the retailer's forwarding of orders to the distributor did not give rise to an obligation on the distributor's part to fill the orders."},"questionZh":"一家全国性的窗户分销商选择了一家零售商在指定的地理区域销售其窗户。双方协商达成了一份书面分销协议，其中规定，“只有在经销商明确接受的情况下”，零售商发出的任何窗户订单才对经销商具有约束力。在接下来的两年里，零售商将订单转发给分销商，而分销商总是履行订单。第三年，经销商指责零售商向顾客收取安装经销商窗户的过高费用。零售商回应称，经销商无法控制零售商的安装价格。当经销商收到零售商的下一份窗户订单时，它拒绝履行该订单。如果零售商起诉经销商违约，胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为零售商将订单转发给分销商并不会导致分销商承担履行订单的义务。","B":"不可以，因为零售商向顾客收取过高费用的做法为经销商拒绝履行订单提供了借口。","C":"是的，因为分销商关于向客户收取过高费用的索赔与其履行零售商订单的义务无关。","D":"是的，因为双方的交易过程约束分销商履行零售商的订单。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为在普通法和 UCC 下，合同义务必须得到有价值的约因的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约。如果任何一方做出虚幻的承诺，即由于含糊不清或承诺方可以选择是否兑现而实质上不做出任何承诺，则不满足这一要求。在这种情况下，不存在任何合同，因此不可能存在违约行为。在此，分销协议规定，零售商下达的订单只有在分销商明确接受的情况下才对分销商具有约束力。由于经销商可以选择是否接受订单，其承诺是虚幻的。由于缺乏对价，分销协议并未形成可执行的合同。这意味着，当分销商收到零售商的最终订单时，分销商没有义务履行订单。因此，零售商不太可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 B 和 C）如果存在限制零售商安装费用的可执行合同，则零售商所谓的多收费行为将构成违约，经销商有权拒绝额外订单。但由于没有这样的合同，零售商的收费做法无关紧要。 （选项 D）根据 UCC，双方的交易过程（例如，执行两年的订单）与解释其协议有关。但如果协议的明示条款与双方的交易过程不一致，则以协议的明示条款为准。因此，双方的交易过程并不约束分销商履行零售商的订单。\n\n教育目标：虚幻的承诺——即由于含糊不清或承诺者可以选择是否履行而本质上不做出任何承诺——无法为合同的订立提供必要的考虑。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 2 cmt。 e（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，当履行完全是可选的时，就没有承诺）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"虚幻的承诺——即由于含糊不清或承诺人可以选择是否履行而本质上不做出任何承诺——无法为合同的订立提供必要的对价。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"如果零售商起诉经销商违约，胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"虚幻的承诺——即由于含糊不清或承诺人可以选择是否履行而本质上不做出任何承诺——无法为合同的订立提供必要的对价。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为在普通法和 UCC 下，合同义务必须得到有价值的约因的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约。如果任何一方做出虚幻的承诺，即由于含糊不清或承诺方可以选择是否兑现而实质上不做出任何承诺，则不满足这一要求。在这种情况下，不存在任何合同，因此不可能存在违约行为。在此，分销协议规定，零售商下达的订单只有在分销商明确接受的情况下才对分销商具有约束力。由于经销商可以选择是否接受订单，其承诺是虚幻的。由于缺乏对价，分销协议并未形成可执行的合同。这意味着，当分销商收到零售商的最终订单时，分销商没有义务履行订单。因此，零售商不太可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 B 和 C）如果存在限制零售商安装费用的可执行合同，则零售商所谓的多收费行为将构成违约，经销商有权拒绝额外订单。但由于没有这样的合同，零售商的收费做法无关紧要。 （选项 D）根据 UCC，双方的交易过程（例如，执行两年的订单）与解释其协议有关。但如果协议的明示条款与双方的交易过程不一致，则以协议的明示条款为准。因此，双方的交易过程并不约束分销商履行零售商的订单。\n\n教育目标：虚幻的承诺——即由于含糊不清或承诺者可以选择是否履行而本质上不做出任何承诺——无法为合同的订立提供必要的考虑。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 2 cmt。 e（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，当履行完全是可选的时，就没有承诺）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为零售商将订单转发给分销商并不会导致分销商承担履行订单的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":114,"sourceQuestionPage":336,"sourceQuestionPages":[336],"sourceExplanationPages":[337,338],"sourceAnswerPages":[337,338],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-115","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A toy collector purchased 10 antique toys over several years and had had them restored by an expert in toy restoration. On June 1, the collector sent an 11th antique toy to the expert with a signed note that read: \"Here is another toy for you to restore. As with all prior jobs, I will pay $500 for the work, but no more.\" On June 4, after receipt of the collector's June 1 note and the toy, the expert began restoring the toy. On June 6, the collector unexpectedly died. On June 7, unaware of the collector's death, the expert sent the collector a note that stated that the restoration work had begun on June 4. The following day, the expert learned of the collector's death. Does a contract exist that binds the expert and the collector's estate?","choices":{"A":"No, because the collector died before the expert sent the June 7 note.","B":"No, because the offer lapsed when the collector died.","C":"Yes, because the expert sent the June 7 note before learning of the collector's death.","D":"Yes, because the offer was accepted before the collector's death."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Bilateral v. Unilateral contracts\n\nOffer Effect of beginning performance\n\nUnilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance Contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). An offer that invites acceptance by return promise—or an offer that does not specify a manner of acceptance— can be accepted by either (1) promising to perform the requested act or (2) performing the requested act. Acceptance by performance is effective as soon as the offeree begins to perform.* However, the offeror is not obligated to perform until receiving notice of the acceptance. And if the offeror dies prior to acceptance, the offer will terminate by law. Here, the toy collector offered to pay the restoration expert $500 in exchange for the restoration of an antique toy. The expert accepted that offer by beginning to restore the toy on June 4—before the collector's death on June 6. Therefore, a contract exists that binds the expert and the collector's estate. *Unlike in a bilateral contract, in a unilateral contract, only the offeree's complete performance constitutes acceptance. (Choices A, B & C) The offer would have terminated by law if the collector had died before acceptance. But the expert began to perform (acceptance) before the collector's death. Therefore, a binding contract existed regardless of whether the collector died before the expert sent the note or the expert sent the note before learning of the collector's death.\n\nEducational objective: Unless an offer specifies a manner of performance, it can be accepted by (1) promising to perform the requested act or (2) performing the requested act. Acceptance by performance is effective as soon as the offeree begins to perform, but the offeror is not obligated to perform until receiving notice of the acceptance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 32 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an offer generally invites acceptance by either performing or promising to perform).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the beginning of performance is an acceptance that obligates the offeree to complete the performance).","educationalObjective":"Unless an offer specifies a manner of performance, it can be accepted by (1) promising to perform the requested act or (2) performing the requested act. Acceptance by performance is effective as soon as the offeree begins to perform, but the offeror is not obligated to perform until receiving notice of the acceptance.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 32 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an offer generally invites acceptance by either performing or promising to perform).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the beginning of performance is an acceptance that obligates the offeree to complete the performance).","rule":"Unless an offer specifies a manner of performance, it can be accepted by (1) promising to perform the requested act or (2) performing the requested act. Acceptance by performance is effective as soon as the offeree begins to perform, but the offeror is not obligated to perform until receiving notice of the acceptance.","irac":{"issue":"Does a contract exist that binds the expert and the collector's estate?","rule":"Unless an offer specifies a manner of performance, it can be accepted by (1) promising to perform the requested act or (2) performing the requested act. Acceptance by performance is effective as soon as the offeree begins to perform, but the offeror is not obligated to perform until receiving notice of the acceptance.","analysis":"Bilateral v. Unilateral contracts\n\nOffer Effect of beginning performance\n\nUnilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance Contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). An offer that invites acceptance by return promise—or an offer that does not specify a manner of acceptance— can be accepted by either (1) promising to perform the requested act or (2) performing the requested act. Acceptance by performance is effective as soon as the offeree begins to perform.* However, the offeror is not obligated to perform until receiving notice of the acceptance. And if the offeror dies prior to acceptance, the offer will terminate by law. Here, the toy collector offered to pay the restoration expert $500 in exchange for the restoration of an antique toy. The expert accepted that offer by beginning to restore the toy on June 4—before the collector's death on June 6. Therefore, a contract exists that binds the expert and the collector's estate. *Unlike in a bilateral contract, in a unilateral contract, only the offeree's complete performance constitutes acceptance. (Choices A, B & C) The offer would have terminated by law if the collector had died before acceptance. But the expert began to perform (acceptance) before the collector's death. Therefore, a binding contract existed regardless of whether the collector died before the expert sent the note or the expert sent the note before learning of the collector's death.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the offer was accepted before the collector's death."},"questionZh":"一位玩具收藏家在几年内购买了 10 个古董玩具，并请玩具修复专家对它们进行了修复。 6 月 1 日，收藏家将第 11 个古董玩具寄给了专家，并附上一张签名纸条，上面写着：“这是另一个玩具供您修复。与之前的所有工作一样，我将为这件作品支付 500 美元，但不再支付更多。” 6月4日，收到收藏家的6月1日票据和玩具后，专家开始修复该玩具。 6月6日，收藏家意外去世。 6月7日，专家在不知道收藏家去世的情况下，给收藏家发了一张纸条，称修复工作已于6月4日开始。第二天，专家得知收藏家去世的消息。是否存在约束专家和收藏家财产的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为收藏家在专家寄出 6 月 7 日的便条之前就去世了。","B":"不可以，因为收藏家去世后报价就失效了。","C":"是的，因为专家在得知收藏家去世之前就发出了 6 月 7 日的便条。","D":"是的，因为该报价是在收藏家去世之前被接受的。"},"explanationZh":"双边合同与单边合同\n\n优惠期初表现效果\n\n单方邀请接受 创建期权合同，防止要约人履行撤销要约的行为 受要约人没有义务完成履行 双边邀请返回接受 作为对要约承诺的接受 受要约人有义务完成履约 合同的形成需要双方同意（即要约和接受）。通过返回承诺邀请接受的要约或未指定接受方式的要约可以通过 (1) 承诺执行所请求的行为或 (2) 执行所请求的行为来接受。一旦受要约人开始履行，通过履行的承诺即生效。*但是，要约人在收到承诺通知之前没有义务履行。如果要约人在接受之前死亡，则要约将依法终止。在这里，玩具收藏家提出向修复专家支付 500 美元，以换取古董玩具的修复。专家接受了这一提议，于 6 月 4 日（收藏家于 6 月 6 日去世之前）开始修复玩具。因此，存在一份约束专家和收藏家遗产的合同。 *与双边合同不同，在单方合同中，只有受要约人完全履行才构成承诺。 （选项 A、B 和 C）如果收藏家在接受之前去世，则该要约将依法终止。但专家在收藏家去世前就开始履行（验收）。因此，无论收藏家在专家发送照会之前死亡，还是专家在得知收藏家死亡之前发送照会，都存在具有约束力的合同。\n\n教育目标：除非要约指定了执行方式，否则可以通过 (1) 承诺执行所请求的行为或 (2) 执行所请求的行为来接受。履行承诺在受要约人开始履行时即生效，但要约人在收到承诺通知之前没有义务履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 32（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释要约通常要求通过履行或承诺履行来接受）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 62（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，履行的开始是受要约人有义务完成履行的承诺）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非要约指定了履行方式，否则可以通过 (1) 承诺执行所请求的行为或 (2) 执行所请求的行为来接受要约。履行承诺在受要约人开始履行时即生效，但要约人在收到承诺通知之前没有义务履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"是否存在约束专家和收藏家财产的合同？","rule":"除非要约指定了履行方式，否则可以通过 (1) 承诺执行所请求的行为或 (2) 执行所请求的行为来接受要约。履行承诺在受要约人开始履行时即生效，但要约人在收到承诺通知之前没有义务履行。","analysis":"双边合同与单边合同\n\n优惠期初表现效果\n\n单方邀请接受 创建期权合同，防止要约人履行撤销要约的行为 受要约人没有义务完成履行 双边邀请返回接受 作为对要约承诺的接受 受要约人有义务完成履约 合同的形成需要双方同意（即要约和接受）。通过返回承诺邀请接受的要约或未指定接受方式的要约可以通过 (1) 承诺执行所请求的行为或 (2) 执行所请求的行为来接受。一旦受要约人开始履行，通过履行的承诺即生效。*但是，要约人在收到承诺通知之前没有义务履行。如果要约人在接受之前死亡，则要约将依法终止。在这里，玩具收藏家提出向修复专家支付 500 美元，以换取古董玩具的修复。专家接受了这一提议，于 6 月 4 日（收藏家于 6 月 6 日去世之前）开始修复玩具。因此，存在一份约束专家和收藏家遗产的合同。 *与双边合同不同，在单方合同中，只有受要约人完全履行才构成承诺。 （选项 A、B 和 C）如果收藏家在接受之前去世，则该要约将依法终止。但专家在收藏家去世前就开始履行（验收）。因此，无论收藏家在专家发送照会之前死亡，还是专家在得知收藏家死亡之前发送照会，都存在具有约束力的合同。\n\n教育目标：除非要约指定了执行方式，否则可以通过 (1) 承诺执行所请求的行为或 (2) 执行所请求的行为来接受。履行承诺在受要约人开始履行时即生效，但要约人在收到承诺通知之前没有义务履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 32（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释要约通常要求通过履行或承诺履行来接受）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 62（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，履行的开始是受要约人有义务完成履行的承诺）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该报价是在收藏家去世之前被接受的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"unilateral contract","ipa":"/jˌunʌlˈætɝəl kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"uni·lat·eral con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"单务合同：以完成指定行为作为承诺方式的合同","contextualMeaning":"单务合同：以完成指定行为作为承诺方式的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Unilateral contracts Offer Effect of beginning performance Unilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance Contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"mutual assent","ipa":"/mjˈutʃuəl ʌsˈɛnt/","syllables":"mu·tual as·sent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合意：双方通过要约与承诺客观同意相同交易","contextualMeaning":"合意：双方通过要约与承诺客观同意相同交易","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Unilateral contracts Offer Effect of beginning performance Unilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance Contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance).","synonyms":["agreement","meeting of the minds"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":115,"sourceQuestionPage":339,"sourceQuestionPages":[339],"sourceExplanationPages":[340,341],"sourceAnswerPages":[340,341],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-116","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A developer, needing a water well on one of his projects, met several times about the matter with a well driller. Subsequently, the driller sent the developer an unsigned typewritten form captioned \"WELL DRILLING PROPOSAL\" and stating various terms the two had discussed but not agreed upon, including a \"proposed price of $5,000.\" The form concluded, \"This proposal will not become a contract until signed by you [the developer] and then returned to and signed by me [the driller].\" The developer signed the form and returned it to the driller, who neglected to sign it but promptly began drilling the well at the proposed site on the developer's project. After drilling for two days, the driller told the developer during one of the developer's daily visits that he would not finish unless the developer would agree to pay twice the price recited in the written proposal. The developer refused, the driller quit, and the developer hired a substitute driller to drill the well to completion for a price of $7,500. In an action by the developer against the driller for damages, which of the following is the probable decision?","choices":{"A":"The developer wins, because his signing of the driller's form constituted an acceptance of an offer by the driller.","B":"The developer wins, because the driller's commencement of performance constituted an acceptance by the driller of an offer by the developer and an implied promise by the driller to complete the well.","C":"The driller wins, because he never signed the proposal as required by its terms.","D":"The driller wins, because his commencement of performance merely prevented the developer from revoking his offer, made on a form supplied by the driller, and did not obligate the driller to complete the well."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Bilateral v. Unilateral contracts\n\nOffer Effect of beginning performance\n\nUnilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance An offer is a communication that gives the recipient the power to conclude a contract by accepting the offer in an appropriate manner, without the need for any further action by the other party. Here, the driller's initial proposal was not an offer because the developer could not complete the deal by signing the proposal and returning it to the driller, who also needed to sign (Choice A). But when the developer signed the proposal and returned it to the driller, he was making an offer on the terms described in the proposal. The driller could have accepted by signing the proposal as required by its terms, thereby making a return promise to complete the well. However, a bilateral offer that requires a return promise to accept can be accepted with a return promise or by starting performance (eg, beginning the well drilling) (Choice C). Starting performance operates as an implied promise to render complete performance (eg, complete the well). Therefore, the driller's failure to complete the well was a breach, and the developer will win in his action for damages. (Choice D) An offer to form a unilateral contract arises when one party promises to do something in exchange for an act of the other party—eg, pay a monetary reward in exchange for finding a lost dog (not seen here). Once performance has begun, the offer is irrevocable for a reasonable period of time to allow for complete performance. However, the recipient is not bound to complete performance.\n\nEducational objective: An offer is a communication that gives the recipient the power to conclude a contract by acceptance without further action by the other party. A bilateral offer that requires a return promise to accept can be accepted either with a return promise or by starting performance, which operates as an implied promise to render complete performance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (offer defined).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of beginning performance).","educationalObjective":"An offer is a communication that gives the recipient the power to conclude a contract by acceptance without further action by the other party. A bilateral offer that requires a return promise to accept can be accepted either with a return promise or by starting performance, which operates as an implied promise to render complete performance.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (offer defined).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 62 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of beginning performance).","rule":"An offer is a communication that gives the recipient the power to conclude a contract by acceptance without further action by the other party. A bilateral offer that requires a return promise to accept can be accepted either with a return promise or by starting performance, which operates as an implied promise to render complete performance.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the developer against the driller for damages, which of the following is the probable decision?","rule":"An offer is a communication that gives the recipient the power to conclude a contract by acceptance without further action by the other party. A bilateral offer that requires a return promise to accept can be accepted either with a return promise or by starting performance, which operates as an implied promise to render complete performance.","analysis":"Bilateral v. Unilateral contracts\n\nOffer Effect of beginning performance\n\nUnilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance An offer is a communication that gives the recipient the power to conclude a contract by accepting the offer in an appropriate manner, without the need for any further action by the other party. Here, the driller's initial proposal was not an offer because the developer could not complete the deal by signing the proposal and returning it to the driller, who also needed to sign (Choice A). But when the developer signed the proposal and returned it to the driller, he was making an offer on the terms described in the proposal. The driller could have accepted by signing the proposal as required by its terms, thereby making a return promise to complete the well. However, a bilateral offer that requires a return promise to accept can be accepted with a return promise or by starting performance (eg, beginning the well drilling) (Choice C). Starting performance operates as an implied promise to render complete performance (eg, complete the well). Therefore, the driller's failure to complete the well was a breach, and the developer will win in his action for damages. (Choice D) An offer to form a unilateral contract arises when one party promises to do something in exchange for an act of the other party—eg, pay a monetary reward in exchange for finding a lost dog (not seen here). Once performance has begun, the offer is irrevocable for a reasonable period of time to allow for complete performance. However, the recipient is not bound to complete performance.","conclusion":"Answer B: The developer wins, because the driller's commencement of performance constituted an acceptance by the driller of an offer by the developer and an implied promise by the driller to complete the well."},"questionZh":"一名开发商在其一个项目中需要一口水井，就此事与一名打井工人进行了多次会面。随后，钻工向开发商发送了一份未签名的打印表格，标题为“钻井提案”，并说明了两人讨论过但尚未达成一致的各种条款，包括“建议价格 5,000 美元”。该表格的结论是：“在您（开发商）签署并返回给我（司钻）并签字之前，该提案不会成为合同。”开发商签署了表格并将其返还给钻井人员，钻井人员忽略了签署，但立即开始在开发商项目的拟议地点钻井。钻探两天后，在开发商的一次日常拜访中，钻工告诉开发商，除非开发商同意支付书面提案中所列价格的两倍，否则他不会完成钻探工作。开发商拒绝了，钻工辞职，开发商以 7,500 美元的价格聘请了一名替代钻工来钻完井。在开发商向钻井者提起损害赔偿诉讼时，可能会做出以下哪项决定？","choicesZh":{"A":"开发商获胜，因为他在司钻表格上签字就构成了司钻接受报价。","B":"开发商获胜，因为司钻开始履约即表示司钻接受了开发商的要约以及司钻完成完井的默示承诺。","C":"司钻获胜，因为他从未按照提案条款的要求签署该提案。","D":"司钻获胜，因为他的履约开始仅仅阻止了开发商撤销其根据司钻提供的表格提出的报价，并且没有要求司钻完成该井。"},"explanationZh":"双边合同与单边合同\n\n优惠期初表现效果\n\n单方邀请接受 创建选项合同，防止要约人履行撤销要约的行为 受要约人没有义务完全履行 双边邀请返回接受 充当对要约承诺的接受 受要约人有义务完全履行 要约是一种通信，赋予接收者通过以适当方式接受要约来签订合同的权力，而无需另一方采取任何进一步行动。在这里，司钻的最初提案不是要约，因为开发商无法通过签署提案并将其返回给司钻来完成交易，而司钻也需要签署（选项 A）。但当开发商签署该提案并将其返还给司钻时，他是根据提案中描述的条款提出要约的。钻探者可以通过按照条款要求签署该提案来接受，从而做出完井的回报承诺。然而，需要回报承诺才能接受的双边要约可以通过回报承诺或开始履行（例如开始钻井）来接受（选择C）。开始绩效作为提供完整绩效（例如完成油井）的隐含承诺。因此，钻井人员未能完井属于违约行为，开发商将在其损害赔偿诉讼中获胜。 （选项D）当一方承诺做某事以换取另一方的行为时，即形成单方合同的要约——例如，支付金钱奖励以换取找到一只丢失的狗（此处未看到）。一旦履约开始，要约在一段合理的时间内不可撤销，以保证履约的完成。然而，接收者不必完全履行。\n\n教育目标：要约是一种沟通，赋予接收者通过接受而签订合同的权力，而无需另一方采取进一步行动。需要退货承诺才能接受的双边要约可以通过退货承诺或通过开始履行来接受，这作为提供完整履行的默示承诺。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 24（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约定义）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 62（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（开始履行的效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要约是一种通信，赋予接收者通过接受而签订合同的权力，而无需另一方采取进一步行动。需要退货承诺才能接受的双边要约可以通过退货承诺或通过开始履行来接受，这作为提供完整履行的默示承诺。","iracZh":{"issue":"在开发商向钻井者提起损害赔偿诉讼时，可能会做出以下哪项决定？","rule":"要约是一种通信，赋予接收者通过接受而签订合同的权力，而无需另一方采取进一步行动。需要退货承诺才能接受的双边要约可以通过退货承诺或通过开始履行来接受，这作为提供完整履行的默示承诺。","analysis":"双边合同与单边合同\n\n优惠期初表现效果\n\n单方邀请接受 创建选项合同，防止要约人履行撤销要约的行为 受要约人没有义务完全履行 双边邀请返回接受 充当对要约承诺的接受 受要约人有义务完全履行 要约是一种通信，赋予接收者通过以适当方式接受要约来签订合同的权力，而无需另一方采取任何进一步行动。在这里，司钻的最初提案不是要约，因为开发商无法通过签署提案并将其返回给司钻来完成交易，而司钻也需要签署（选项 A）。但当开发商签署该提案并将其返还给司钻时，他是根据提案中描述的条款提出要约的。钻探者可以通过按照条款要求签署该提案来接受，从而做出完井的回报承诺。然而，需要回报承诺才能接受的双边要约可以通过回报承诺或开始履行（例如开始钻井）来接受（选择C）。开始绩效作为提供完整绩效（例如完成油井）的隐含承诺。因此，钻井人员未能完井属于违约行为，开发商将在其损害赔偿诉讼中获胜。 （选项D）当一方承诺做某事以换取另一方的行为时，即形成单方合同的要约——例如，支付金钱奖励以换取找到一只丢失的狗（此处未看到）。一旦履约开始，要约在一段合理的时间内不可撤销，以保证履约的完成。然而，接收者不必完全履行。\n\n教育目标：要约是一种沟通，赋予接收者通过接受而签订合同的权力，而无需另一方采取进一步行动。需要退货承诺才能接受的双边要约可以通过退货承诺或通过开始履行来接受，这作为提供完整履行的默示承诺。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 24（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约定义）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 62（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（开始履行的效果）。","conclusion":"答案 B：开发商获胜，因为司钻开始履约即表示司钻接受了开发商的要约以及司钻完成完井的默示承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":116,"sourceQuestionPage":342,"sourceQuestionPages":[342],"sourceExplanationPages":[343,344],"sourceAnswerPages":[343,344],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-117","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A woman encountered her coworker and the coworker's friend at a local coffee shop. The woman, who knew that the coworker needed a new laptop for his personal use, had planned to offer her extra laptop to the coworker. The woman went to the counter to place her coffee order and, with her back to the table where the coworker and his friend sat, said, \"By the way, I know you are looking for a new laptop. I will sell you my laptop for $100.\" The friend immediately replied, \"I accept your offer!\" Has a contract been formed between the friend and the woman?","choices":{"A":"No, because the friend was mistaken as to the terms of the contract.","B":"No, because the friend was not the party with whom the woman intended to contract.","C":"Yes, because the friend reasonably believed that he had the power to accept the woman's offer.","D":"Yes, because the woman's objective intent was to contract with whoever overheard the offer."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The offeror is master of the offer. This means, among other things, that the power to accept an offer belongs only to the person (or class of persons) with whom the offeror intended to contract. In contract law, intent is measured by an objective standard, not by the subjective intent or belief of a party. Therefore, whether the offeror intended to contract with someone is judged by outward objective facts, as they would be interpreted by a reasonable person. Here, the woman knew that her coworker needed a new laptop. Her statement—\"I know you are looking for a new laptop\"—demonstrated her objective intent to contract with the coworker (Choice D). Although the friend overheard the woman's offer to sell her laptop, he could not reasonably believe that he had the power to accept it. This is especially true since there is no indication that the two had ever met (Choice C). Therefore, no contract was formed because the friend was not the party with whom the woman intended to contract. (Choice A) A mistake as to a basic assumption upon which a contract was made renders the contract voidable—not void. If a contract is voidable, then it is considered valid until set aside. But here, no contract was ever formed because the offer was not accepted by the intended offeree, so this defense is inapplicable.\n\nEducational objective: The power of acceptance belongs only to the person(s) with whom the offeror intended to contract. Such intent is judged by outward objective facts, as interpreted by a reasonable person.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 29 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (to whom an offer is addressed).","educationalObjective":"The power of acceptance belongs only to the person(s) with whom the offeror intended to contract. Such intent is judged by outward objective facts, as interpreted by a reasonable person.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 29 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (to whom an offer is addressed).","rule":"The power of acceptance belongs only to the person(s) with whom the offeror intended to contract. Such intent is judged by outward objective facts, as interpreted by a reasonable person.","irac":{"issue":"A woman encountered her coworker and the coworker's friend at a local coffee shop. The woman, who knew that the coworker needed a new laptop for his personal use, had planned to offer her extra laptop to the coworker. The woman went to the counter to place her coffee order and, with her back to the table where the coworker and his friend sat, said, \"By the way, I know you are looking for a new laptop. I will sell you my laptop for $100.\" The friend immediately replied, \"I accept your offer!\" Has a contract been formed between the friend and the woman?","rule":"The power of acceptance belongs only to the person(s) with whom the offeror intended to contract. Such intent is judged by outward objective facts, as interpreted by a reasonable person.","analysis":"The offeror is master of the offer. This means, among other things, that the power to accept an offer belongs only to the person (or class of persons) with whom the offeror intended to contract. In contract law, intent is measured by an objective standard, not by the subjective intent or belief of a party. Therefore, whether the offeror intended to contract with someone is judged by outward objective facts, as they would be interpreted by a reasonable person. Here, the woman knew that her coworker needed a new laptop. Her statement—\"I know you are looking for a new laptop\"—demonstrated her objective intent to contract with the coworker (Choice D). Although the friend overheard the woman's offer to sell her laptop, he could not reasonably believe that he had the power to accept it. This is especially true since there is no indication that the two had ever met (Choice C). Therefore, no contract was formed because the friend was not the party with whom the woman intended to contract. (Choice A) A mistake as to a basic assumption upon which a contract was made renders the contract voidable—not void. If a contract is voidable, then it is considered valid until set aside. But here, no contract was ever formed because the offer was not accepted by the intended offeree, so this defense is inapplicable.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the friend was not the party with whom the woman intended to contract."},"questionZh":"一位女士在当地一家咖啡店遇到了她的同事和同事的朋友。该女士知道该同事需要一台新笔记本电脑供个人使用，因此计划将多余的笔记本电脑提供给该同事。这位女士走到柜台点了咖啡，背对着同事和他的朋友坐的桌子说：“顺便说一句，我知道您正在寻找一台新笔记本电脑。我将以 100 美元的价格卖给您我的笔记本电脑。”朋友立即回复：“我接受你的提议！”朋友和该女子之间是否已签订合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为朋友对合同条款的理解有误。","B":"不，因为这位朋友并不是该女子打算与之签约的一方。","C":"是的，因为这位朋友有理由相信他有能力接受这位女士的提议。","D":"是的，因为这位女士的客观意图是与无意中听到该提议的人签订合同。"},"explanationZh":"要约人是要约的主人。除其他外，这意味着接受要约的权力仅属于要约人打算与其签订合同的人（或一类人）。在合同法中，意图是通过客观标准来衡量的，而不是通过当事人的主观意图或信念来衡量。因此，要约人是否打算与某人签订合同是根据外在的客观事实来判断的，就像一个理性的人会解释的那样。在这里，这位女士知道她的同事需要一台新笔记本电脑。她的陈述——“我知道您正在寻找一台新笔记本电脑”——表明了她与同事签订合同的客观意图（选项 D）。尽管这位朋友无意中听到该女子提出出售她的笔记本电脑，但他无法合理地相信自己有能力接受。尤其如此，因为没有迹象表明两人曾经见过面（选项 C）。因此，合同并未成立，因为该朋友并非该女子打算与之订立合同的当事人。 （选项 A）合同订立时所依据的基本假设的错误会导致合同无效——而不是无效。如果合同可撤销，则在撤销之前该合同被视为有效。但在这里，由于要约并未被预期受要约人接受，因此合同从未成立，因此这种抗辩不适用。\n\n教育目标： 接受权仅属于要约人拟与其签订合同的人。这种意图是根据外在的客观事实来判断的，并由一个有理智的人解释。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 29（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约的收件人）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"接受权仅属于要约人拟与其签订合同的人。这种意图是根据外在的客观事实来判断的，并由一个有理智的人解释。","iracZh":{"issue":"一位女士在当地一家咖啡店遇到了她的同事和同事的朋友。该女士知道该同事需要一台新笔记本电脑供个人使用，因此计划将多余的笔记本电脑提供给该同事。这位女士走到柜台点了咖啡，背对着同事和他的朋友坐的桌子说：“顺便说一句，我知道您正在寻找一台新笔记本电脑。我将以 100 美元的价格卖给您我的笔记本电脑。”朋友立即回复：“我接受你的提议！”朋友和该女子之间是否已签订合同？","rule":"接受权仅属于要约人拟与其签订合同的人。这种意图是根据外在的客观事实来判断的，并由一个有理智的人解释。","analysis":"要约人是要约的主人。除其他外，这意味着接受要约的权力仅属于要约人打算与其签订合同的人（或一类人）。在合同法中，意图是通过客观标准来衡量的，而不是通过当事人的主观意图或信念来衡量。因此，要约人是否打算与某人签订合同是根据外在的客观事实来判断的，就像一个理性的人会解释的那样。在这里，这位女士知道她的同事需要一台新笔记本电脑。她的陈述——“我知道您正在寻找一台新笔记本电脑”——表明了她与同事签订合同的客观意图（选项 D）。尽管这位朋友无意中听到该女子提出出售她的笔记本电脑，但他无法合理地相信自己有能力接受。尤其如此，因为没有迹象表明两人曾经见过面（选项 C）。因此，合同并未成立，因为该朋友并非该女子打算与之订立合同的当事人。 （选项 A）合同订立时所依据的基本假设的错误会导致合同无效——而不是无效。如果合同可撤销，则在撤销之前该合同被视为有效。但在这里，由于要约并未被预期受要约人接受，因此合同从未成立，因此这种抗辩不适用。\n\n教育目标： 接受权仅属于要约人拟与其签订合同的人。这种意图是根据外在的客观事实来判断的，并由一个有理智的人解释。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 29（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约的收件人）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为这位朋友并不是该女子打算与之签约的一方。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"power of acceptance","ipa":"/pˈaʊɝ ˈəv æksˈɛptəns/","syllables":"power of ac·cep·tance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"承诺权：受要约人通过有效承诺成立合同的权力","contextualMeaning":"承诺权：受要约人通过有效承诺成立合同的权力","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The power of acceptance belongs only to the person(s) with whom the offeror intended to contract.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-ce78e52ab15565e0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":117,"sourceQuestionPage":345,"sourceQuestionPages":[345],"sourceExplanationPages":[346,347],"sourceAnswerPages":[346,347],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-118","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A man, aged 60, who had no plans for early retirement, had worked for a manufacturing company for 20 years as a managerial employee-at-will when he had a conversation with the company's president about the man's post-retirement goal of extensive travel around the United States. A month later, the company president handed the man a written, signed resolution of the company's board of directors stating that when and if the man should decide to retire, at his option, the company, in recognition of his past service, would pay him a $2,000-per-month lifetime pension. (The company had no regularized retirement plan for at-will employees.) Shortly thereafter, the man retired and immediately bought a $30,000 recreational vehicle for his planned travels. After receiving the promised $2,000 monthly pension from the company for six months, the man, now unemployable elsewhere, received a letter from the company advising him that the pension would cease immediately because of recessionary budget constraints affecting in varying degrees all managerial salaries and retirement pensions. In a suit against the company for damages, will the man win or lose?","choices":{"A":"Lose, because he had been an employee-at-will throughout his active service with the company.","B":"Lose, because the board's promise to him of a lifetime pension was an unenforceable gift promise.","C":"Win, because he retired from the company as bargained-for consideration for the board's promise to him of a lifetime pension.","D":"Win, because he timed his decision to retire and to buy the recreational vehicle in reasonable reliance on the board's promise to him of a lifetime pension."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Promissory estoppel\n\nAllows enforcement of promise when no valid contract exists if:\n\npromisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on promise enforcing party reasonably relied on promise reliance caused enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcing promise Even when there is no valid contract (as seen here), a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief. For example, the doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages stemming from another's promise if: the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise reliance on the promise caused the enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise. Damages for promissory estoppel may be limited as justice requires. Often, this means reliance damages—ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses—are awarded. However, in some instances, courts have awarded expectation damages to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed. Here, there was no contract between the man and the company. But the man can recover under the equitable theory of promissory estoppel. That is because—in addition to the other requirements—the man reasonably relied upon the promised pension and suffered substantial detriment (unemployment). Therefore, the man is either entitled to reliance damages of $30,000 (for the recreational vehicle) or possibly expectation damages of $2,000 per month in lifetime pension. (Choice A) Employment is at will if (1) there is no employment contract or (2) the employment contract does not specify a term of employment. But the man's status as an at- will employee does not affect his recovery here. (Choice B) A gift is a gratuitous transfer of property or services without a return promise. A promise of a gift is unenforceable under contract law (no consideration). But a party may still recover under an equitable theory of relief (eg, promissory estoppel). (Choice C) The lifetime pension was not promised in exchange for the man's retirement but in recognition of his past service to the company. Therefore, an argument that the man's retirement was consideration for the pension is misplaced.\n\nEducational objective: Even when there is no valid contract, a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief such as promissory estoppel.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promissory estoppel).","educationalObjective":"Even when there is no valid contract, a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief such as promissory estoppel.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (promissory estoppel).","rule":"Even when there is no valid contract, a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief such as promissory estoppel.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit against the company for damages, will the man win or lose?","rule":"Even when there is no valid contract, a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief such as promissory estoppel.","analysis":"Promissory estoppel\n\nAllows enforcement of promise when no valid contract exists if:\n\npromisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on promise enforcing party reasonably relied on promise reliance caused enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcing promise Even when there is no valid contract (as seen here), a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief. For example, the doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages stemming from another's promise if: the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise reliance on the promise caused the enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can only be avoided by enforcing the promise. Damages for promissory estoppel may be limited as justice requires. Often, this means reliance damages—ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses—are awarded. However, in some instances, courts have awarded expectation damages to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed. Here, there was no contract between the man and the company. But the man can recover under the equitable theory of promissory estoppel. That is because—in addition to the other requirements—the man reasonably relied upon the promised pension and suffered substantial detriment (unemployment). Therefore, the man is either entitled to reliance damages of $30,000 (for the recreational vehicle) or possibly expectation damages of $2,000 per month in lifetime pension. (Choice A) Employment is at will if (1) there is no employment contract or (2) the employment contract does not specify a term of employment. But the man's status as an at- will employee does not affect his recovery here. (Choice B) A gift is a gratuitous transfer of property or services without a return promise. A promise of a gift is unenforceable under contract law (no consideration). But a party may still recover under an equitable theory of relief (eg, promissory estoppel). (Choice C) The lifetime pension was not promised in exchange for the man's retirement but in recognition of his past service to the company. Therefore, an argument that the man's retirement was consideration for the pension is misplaced.","conclusion":"Answer D: Win, because he timed his decision to retire and to buy the recreational vehicle in reasonable reliance on the board's promise to him of a lifetime pension."},"questionZh":"一名60岁的男子没有提前退休的计划，他在一家制造公司工作了20年，担任随意的管理人员，当时他与该公司的总裁谈论了该男子退休后的目标——广泛旅行美国。一个月后，公司总裁向该男子提交了一份书面的、经签署的公司董事会决议，其中规定，如果该男子决定何时退休，由他选择，公司将向他支付每月 2,000 美元的终身养老金，以表彰他过去的服务。 （该公司没有针对随意员工的定期退休计划。）此后不久，该男子退休了，并立即购买了一辆价值 30,000 美元的休闲车用于他计划的旅行。该男子在收到公司承诺的每月 2,000 美元的养老金六个月后，现在在其他地方失业，收到了公司的一封信，通知他养老金将立即停止，因为经济衰退的预算限制在不同程度上影响了所有管理人员的工资和退休金。如果向公司提起损害赔偿诉讼，该男子会赢还是输？","choicesZh":{"A":"输了，因为他在公司任职期间一直是随意的雇员。","B":"输了，因为董事会向他承诺的终身养老金是一项无法执行的赠与承诺。","C":"赢，因为他从公司退休是为了考虑董事会向他承诺的终身养老金。","D":"获胜，因为他合理地依赖董事会向他提供终身养老金的承诺，合理地决定退休并购买休闲车。"},"explanationZh":"禁止反言\n\n在不存在有效合同的情况下允许执行承诺，如果：\n\n承诺者合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖 执行方合理依赖承诺 依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正 即使没有有效的合同（如此处所示），一方也可以根据衡平法救济理论获得损害赔偿。例如，在以下情况下，允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许一方当事人就另一方的承诺获得损害赔偿： 承诺人合理预期会导致对承诺的依赖 执行方合理地依赖该承诺 对承诺的依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，并且只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。根据正义的要求，禁止反言的损害赔偿可能会受到限制。通常，这意味着信赖损害赔偿，即基于自付费用的损害赔偿。然而，在某些情况下，法院会判给预期损害赔偿，以使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。在这里，该男子与公司之间没有任何合同。但根据允诺禁止反悔的衡平法理论，该男子可以康复。这是因为，除了其他要求之外，该男子还合理地依赖承诺的养老金，并遭受了巨大的损失（失业）。因此，该男子要么有权获得 30,000 美元的信赖损害赔偿（休闲车），要么可能获得每月 2,000 美元的终身养老金预期损害赔偿。 （选择A）如果（1）没有雇佣合同或（2）雇佣合同没有规定雇佣期限，则雇佣是随意的。但该男子作为随意雇员的身份并不影响他在这里的康复。 （选择 B） 礼物是无偿转让财产或服务，且不附有回报承诺。根据合同法（无对价），礼物承诺不可执行。但一方仍可根据公平救济理论（例如，禁止反言）获得赔偿。 （选项C） 终身养老金并不是为了换取该人的退休，而是为了表彰他过去对公司的服务。因此，认为该男子退休是为了领取养老金的说法是错误的。\n\n教育目标：即使没有有效的合同，一方也可以根据公平的救济理论（例如禁止反言）获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"即使没有有效的合同，一方也可以根据衡平法救济理论（例如允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））获得损害赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果向公司提起损害赔偿诉讼，该男子会赢还是输？","rule":"即使没有有效的合同，一方也可以根据衡平法救济理论（例如允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））获得损害赔偿。","analysis":"禁止反言\n\n在不存在有效合同的情况下允许执行承诺，如果：\n\n承诺者合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖 执行方合理依赖承诺 依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正 即使没有有效的合同（如此处所示），一方也可以根据衡平法救济理论获得损害赔偿。例如，在以下情况下，允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许一方当事人就另一方的承诺获得损害赔偿： 承诺人合理预期会导致对承诺的依赖 执行方合理地依赖该承诺 对承诺的依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，并且只有通过执行承诺才能避免不公正。根据正义的要求，禁止反言的损害赔偿可能会受到限制。通常，这意味着信赖损害赔偿，即基于自付费用的损害赔偿。然而，在某些情况下，法院会判给预期损害赔偿，以使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。在这里，该男子与公司之间没有任何合同。但根据允诺禁止反悔的衡平法理论，该男子可以康复。这是因为，除了其他要求之外，该男子还合理地依赖承诺的养老金，并遭受了巨大的损失（失业）。因此，该男子要么有权获得 30,000 美元的信赖损害赔偿（休闲车），要么可能获得每月 2,000 美元的终身养老金预期损害赔偿。 （选择A）如果（1）没有雇佣合同或（2）雇佣合同没有规定雇佣期限，则雇佣是随意的。但该男子作为随意雇员的身份并不影响他在这里的康复。 （选择 B） 礼物是无偿转让财产或服务，且不附有回报承诺。根据合同法（无对价），礼物承诺不可执行。但一方仍可根据公平救济理论（例如，禁止反言）获得赔偿。 （选项C） 终身养老金并不是为了换取该人的退休，而是为了表彰他过去对公司的服务。因此，认为该男子退休是为了领取养老金的说法是错误的。\n\n教育目标：即使没有有效的合同，一方也可以根据公平的救济理论（例如禁止反言）获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel））。","conclusion":"答案 D：获胜，因为他合理地依赖董事会向他提供终身养老金的承诺，合理地决定退休并购买休闲车。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"reliance damages","ipa":"/ɹɪlˈaɪəns dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"re·liance dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"信赖利益赔偿：补偿因合理信赖合同而发生的损失","contextualMeaning":"信赖利益赔偿：补偿因合理信赖合同而发生的损失","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Often, this means reliance damages—ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses—are awarded.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":118,"sourceQuestionPage":348,"sourceQuestionPages":[348],"sourceExplanationPages":[349,350],"sourceAnswerPages":[349,350],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-119","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"An amateur computer whiz agreed in writing to build and deliver 50 new gaming laptops a year to a computer distributor over a five-year period. The writing provided, in a clause separately signed by the computer whiz, that \"[n]o modification shall be binding on the distributor unless made in writing and signed by the distributor's authorized representative.\" Because of family problems, the computer whiz delivered and the distributor accepted only 30 gaming laptops a year for the first three years; but the laptops were popular with gamers and the distributor made no objection. Accordingly, the computer whiz spent substantial sums on new laptop parts that would aid in speeding up future builds. In the first quarter of the fourth year, however, the distributor terminated the contract on the ground that the computer whiz had breached the annual-quantity term. In the computer whiz's suit against the distributor for damages, the jury found that the contract had been modified by conduct, and the trial court awarded the computer whiz substantial compensatory damages. Is this result likely to be reversed on appeal?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contract's no-oral-modification clause was unconscionable as against an amateur computer builder.","B":"No, because the distributor by its conduct waived the annual-quantity term and the computer whiz materially changed his position in reasonable reliance on that waiver.","C":"Yes, because the contract's no-oral-modification clause was a material part of the agreed exchange and could not be avoided without new consideration.","D":"Yes, because the contract's no-oral-modification clause was not expressly waived by the distributor."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Unwritten modifications of contracts\n\nContract silent on modification Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies (common law & UCC)\n\nContract clause requires Common Clause unenforceable written modification law Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies UCC Clause enforceable (ie, unwritten modification invalid) if: contract written & signed clause separately signed by nonmerchant if sued by merchant Clause waived (ie, unwritten modification valid) if: parties intended unwritten modification one party materially changed position in reliance UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC, contract clauses that require modifications to be in writing (ie, no-oral- modification clauses) are generally enforceable against non-merchants who separately sign the clause. But these clauses are waived if: the parties attempt to modify the contract orally or by their conduct (eg, tendering and accepting an alternate performance) and one party materially changes his/her position in reliance on that modification. In that case, the attempted modification is valid. And if either party breaches or terminates the modified contract, the other party is entitled to compensatory damages. Here, the computer whiz agreed to deliver 50 gaming laptops a year over a five-year period but delivered only 30 laptops a year for the first three years. By tendering and accepting this alternate performance, the parties attempted to waive the annual-quantity term. And since the computer whiz relied on that modification and materially changed his position by spending substantial sums on new laptop parts, the no-oral-modification clause was waived. Therefore, the computer whiz's compensatory damages will not be reversed on appeal.\n\n(Choice A) A court can refuse to enforce a contract clause that is unconscionable—eg, unfairly influenced by the parties' unequal bargaining power. But here, although the computer whiz was an amateur, there is no indication that the no-oral-modification clause was unfairly influenced by the distributor's superior bargaining power. (Choice C) A sale-of-goods contract can be modified without new consideration—even if the modification avoids a material part of the agreed exchange—so long as the modification is sought in good faith. (Choice D) The parties to a sale-of-goods contract can demonstrate their intent to waive a no-oral-modification clause expressly (eg, orally) OR by their conduct (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, no-oral-modification clauses are generally enforceable against non- merchants who separately sign the clause. But these clauses are waived if (1) the parties attempt to modify the contract orally OR by their conduct and (2) one party materially changes his/her position in reliance on that modification. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a contract clause requiring written modification is generally enforceable by a merchant against a non- merchant who separately signs the clause).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, no-oral-modification clauses are generally enforceable against non- merchants who separately sign the clause. But these clauses are waived if (1) the parties attempt to modify the contract orally OR by their conduct and (2) one party materially changes his/her position in reliance on that modification. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a contract clause requiring written modification is generally enforceable by a merchant against a non- merchant who separately signs the clause).","rule":"Under the UCC, no-oral-modification clauses are generally enforceable against non- merchants who separately sign the clause. But these clauses are waived if (1) the parties attempt to modify the contract orally OR by their conduct and (2) one party materially changes his/her position in reliance on that modification. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Is this result likely to be reversed on appeal?","rule":"Under the UCC, no-oral-modification clauses are generally enforceable against non- merchants who separately sign the clause. But these clauses are waived if (1) the parties attempt to modify the contract orally OR by their conduct and (2) one party materially changes his/her position in reliance on that modification. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Unwritten modifications of contracts\n\nContract silent on modification Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies (common law & UCC)\n\nContract clause requires Common Clause unenforceable written modification law Unwritten modification valid unless statute of frauds applies UCC Clause enforceable (ie, unwritten modification invalid) if: contract written & signed clause separately signed by nonmerchant if sued by merchant Clause waived (ie, unwritten modification valid) if: parties intended unwritten modification one party materially changed position in reliance UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC, contract clauses that require modifications to be in writing (ie, no-oral- modification clauses) are generally enforceable against non-merchants who separately sign the clause. But these clauses are waived if: the parties attempt to modify the contract orally or by their conduct (eg, tendering and accepting an alternate performance) and one party materially changes his/her position in reliance on that modification. In that case, the attempted modification is valid. And if either party breaches or terminates the modified contract, the other party is entitled to compensatory damages. Here, the computer whiz agreed to deliver 50 gaming laptops a year over a five-year period but delivered only 30 laptops a year for the first three years. By tendering and accepting this alternate performance, the parties attempted to waive the annual-quantity term. And since the computer whiz relied on that modification and materially changed his position by spending substantial sums on new laptop parts, the no-oral-modification clause was waived. Therefore, the computer whiz's compensatory damages will not be reversed on appeal.\n\n(Choice A) A court can refuse to enforce a contract clause that is unconscionable—eg, unfairly influenced by the parties' unequal bargaining power. But here, although the computer whiz was an amateur, there is no indication that the no-oral-modification clause was unfairly influenced by the distributor's superior bargaining power. (Choice C) A sale-of-goods contract can be modified without new consideration—even if the modification avoids a material part of the agreed exchange—so long as the modification is sought in good faith. (Choice D) The parties to a sale-of-goods contract can demonstrate their intent to waive a no-oral-modification clause expressly (eg, orally) OR by their conduct (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the distributor by its conduct waived the annual-quantity term and the computer whiz materially changed his position in reasonable reliance on that waiver."},"questionZh":"一位业余计算机高手书面同意在五年内每年向计算机经销商制造并交付 50 台新游戏笔记本电脑。该书面文件在由计算机高手单独签署的条款中规定，“除非以书面形式作出并由发行商的授权代表签署，否则任何修改均不对发行商具有约束力。”由于家庭问题，计算机高手在前三年每年只接受 30 台游戏笔记本电脑；但笔记本电脑很受游戏玩家欢迎，经销商也没有反对。因此，这位计算机高手花费了大量资金购买新的笔记本电脑部件，这将有助于加快未来的构建速度。然而，第四年第一季度，经销商以电脑高手违反了年度数量条款为由终止了合同。在电脑高手向经销商提起损害赔偿诉讼中，陪审团认定合同已被行为修改，初审法院判给电脑高手巨额赔偿金。这一结果是否可能在上诉后被推翻？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合同中的禁止口头修改条款对于业余计算机制造商来说是不合理的。","B":"不，因为分销商通过其行为放弃了年度数量条款，而计算机专家在合理依赖该放弃的情况下实质性地改变了他的立场。","C":"是的，因为合同中的禁止口头修改条款是约定交换的重要组成部分，如果不进行新的考虑就无法避免。","D":"是的，因为经销商没有明确放弃合同中的禁止口头修改条款。"},"explanationZh":"合同的不成文修改\n\n合同对修改保持沉默 不成文的修改有效，除非欺诈法适用（普通法和 UCC）\n\n合同条款要求通用条款不可执行的书面修改法 不成文修改有效，除非欺诈法适用 UCC 条款可执行（即不成文修改无效），如果： 合同书面和签署的条款由非商户单独签署，如果被商户起诉 条款被放弃（即，不成文修改有效）如果： 双方有意进行不成文修改，一方实质上改变了信赖的立场 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC，要求以书面形式修改的合同条款（即非口头修改条款）通常对单独签署该条款的非商家具有强制执行力。但如果出现以下情况，这些条款将被放弃： 双方试图口头或通过其行为（例如，提出并接受替代履约）修改合同，并且一方依据该修改实质性改变其立场。在这种情况下，尝试的修改是有效的。如果任何一方违反或终止修改后的合同，另一方有权要求赔偿损失。在这里，这位计算机高手同意在五年内每年交付 50 台游戏笔记本电脑，但在前三年每年只交付 30 台笔记本电脑。通过投标并接受这一替代履约，双方试图放弃年度数量条款。由于这位计算机高手依赖于这种修改，并通过花费大量资金购买新的笔记本电脑部件而实质性地改变了他的立场，因此免于口头修改条款被放弃。因此，计算机高手的赔偿损失不会因上诉而被撤销。\n\n（选择A）法院可以拒绝执行不合理的合同条款——例如，受到双方不平等的议价能力的不公平影响。但在这里，尽管计算机高手是业余爱好者，但没有迹象表明禁止口头修改条款受到了经销商优越议价能力的不公平影响。 （选择 C）只要善意地寻求修改，即使修改避免了商定交换的重要部分，货物销售合同也可以在没有新对价的情况下进行修改。 （选项 D）货物销售合同的当事人可以明确（例如口头）或通过其行为（如此处所示）表明其放弃非口头修改条款的意图。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，禁止口头修改条款通常对单独签署该条款的非商人具有强制执行力。但如果 (1) 双方试图口头或通过行为修改合同，并且 (2) 一方依据该修改而实质性改变其立场，则这些条款将被放弃。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释了需要书面修改的合同条款通常可由商人对单独签署该条款的非商人强制执行）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，禁止口头修改条款通常对单独签署该条款的非商家具有强制执行力。但如果 (1) 双方试图口头或通过行为修改合同，并且 (2) 一方依据该修改而实质性改变其立场，则这些条款将被放弃。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"这一结果是否可能在上诉后被推翻？","rule":"根据 UCC，禁止口头修改条款通常对单独签署该条款的非商家具有强制执行力。但如果 (1) 双方试图口头或通过行为修改合同，并且 (2) 一方依据该修改而实质性改变其立场，则这些条款将被放弃。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"合同的不成文修改\n\n合同对修改保持沉默 不成文的修改有效，除非欺诈法适用（普通法和 UCC）\n\n合同条款要求通用条款不可执行的书面修改法 不成文修改有效，除非欺诈法适用 UCC 条款可执行（即不成文修改无效），如果： 合同书面和签署的条款由非商户单独签署，如果被商户起诉 条款被放弃（即，不成文修改有效）如果： 双方有意进行不成文修改，一方实质上改变了信赖的立场 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC，要求以书面形式修改的合同条款（即非口头修改条款）通常对单独签署该条款的非商家具有强制执行力。但如果出现以下情况，这些条款将被放弃： 双方试图口头或通过其行为（例如，提出并接受替代履约）修改合同，并且一方依据该修改实质性改变其立场。在这种情况下，尝试的修改是有效的。如果任何一方违反或终止修改后的合同，另一方有权要求赔偿损失。在这里，这位计算机高手同意在五年内每年交付 50 台游戏笔记本电脑，但在前三年每年只交付 30 台笔记本电脑。通过投标并接受这一替代履约，双方试图放弃年度数量条款。由于这位计算机高手依赖于这种修改，并通过花费大量资金购买新的笔记本电脑部件而实质性地改变了他的立场，因此免于口头修改条款被放弃。因此，计算机高手的赔偿损失不会因上诉而被撤销。\n\n（选择A）法院可以拒绝执行不合理的合同条款——例如，受到双方不平等的议价能力的不公平影响。但在这里，尽管计算机高手是业余爱好者，但没有迹象表明禁止口头修改条款受到了经销商优越议价能力的不公平影响。 （选择 C）只要善意地寻求修改，即使修改避免了商定交换的重要部分，货物销售合同也可以在没有新对价的情况下进行修改。 （选项 D）货物销售合同的当事人可以明确（例如口头）或通过其行为（如此处所示）表明其放弃非口头修改条款的意图。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，禁止口头修改条款通常对单独签署该条款的非商人具有强制执行力。但如果 (1) 双方试图口头或通过行为修改合同，并且 (2) 一方依据该修改而实质性改变其立场，则这些条款将被放弃。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释了需要书面修改的合同条款通常可由商人对单独签署该条款的非商人强制执行）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为分销商通过其行为放弃了年度数量条款，而计算机专家在合理依赖该放弃的情况下实质性地改变了他的立场。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":119,"sourceQuestionPage":351,"sourceQuestionPages":[351],"sourceExplanationPages":[352,353],"sourceAnswerPages":[352,353],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-120","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"An attorney received a document at his office with an attached note from a client for whom he had just finished drafting a will. The note read as follows: \"Do you think this contract of sale for my boat complies with state law? I would have talked to you in person about this, but I'm on my way out of town. I will be back next week.\" The attorney reviewed the document and wrote a one-page letter to the client stating that the document complied with state law. The lawyer included a bill for $500, which was a reasonable fee. The client refused to pay the attorney anything, arguing that she had never agreed to retain the attorney and that she had received nothing of value from the attorney because the sales transaction was never concluded. Assume that there is no applicable statute or rule governing the formation of attorney-client relationships in the jurisdiction. If the attorney sues the client for the $500, will the attorney be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because even if the parties had an agreement, that agreement was discharged under the doctrine of frustration of purpose.","B":"No, because the attorney and the client never agreed on the essential terms of a contract.","C":"Yes, because the attorney took action on the client's note to his detriment.","D":"Yes, because the client's note and the attorney's performance created an implied-in- fact contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"To form a contract (ie, a legally enforceable agreement), there must be mutual assent (also called a meeting of the minds). This requires both: offer – a communication that creates an objectively reasonable expectation in the offeree that the offeror is willing to enter into a binding contract and\n\nacceptance – an objective manifestation of the offeree's assent to the terms of the offer that is made by the offeree in any reasonable manner, unless the offer requires a particular method of acceptance.\n\nMutual assent can be manifested by written or spoken words (creating an express contract) OR by conduct (creating an implied-in-fact contract). Here, the client's note asked the attorney whether the contract for the sale of her boat complied with state law. The parties already had an attorney-client relationship, so the attorney reasonably understood that the client was offering to hire him to review the contract and provide her with an opinion. The attorney's conduct in reviewing the contract and writing back to the client was a reasonable method of acceptance—especially since the client was headed out of town. Therefore, an implied-in-fact contract was created, and the attorney is likely to prevail. (Choice A) The doctrine of frustration of purpose only applies when the purpose of a contract becomes worthless due to an unexpected event. But the sales transaction's failure to conclude is not an unexpected event that would render the attorney's advice on the matter worthless. (Choice B) Even if an essential term is missing, a contract may be formed if it appears that the parties intended to form a contract and the court can supply the term with reasonable certainty. Here, the facts stipulate that $500 was a reasonable fee. (Choice C) The doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. A contract was formed here, so the attorney's reliance is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: The mutual assent required to form a binding contract can be manifested by written or spoken words (express) OR by conduct (implied-in-fact).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 4, 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conduct as manifestation of assent).","educationalObjective":"The mutual assent required to form a binding contract can be manifested by written or spoken words (express) OR by conduct (implied-in-fact).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 4, 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conduct as manifestation of assent).","rule":"The mutual assent required to form a binding contract can be manifested by written or spoken words (express) OR by conduct (implied-in-fact).","irac":{"issue":"If the attorney sues the client for the $500, will the attorney be likely to prevail?","rule":"The mutual assent required to form a binding contract can be manifested by written or spoken words (express) OR by conduct (implied-in-fact).","analysis":"To form a contract (ie, a legally enforceable agreement), there must be mutual assent (also called a meeting of the minds). This requires both: offer – a communication that creates an objectively reasonable expectation in the offeree that the offeror is willing to enter into a binding contract and\n\nacceptance – an objective manifestation of the offeree's assent to the terms of the offer that is made by the offeree in any reasonable manner, unless the offer requires a particular method of acceptance.\n\nMutual assent can be manifested by written or spoken words (creating an express contract) OR by conduct (creating an implied-in-fact contract). Here, the client's note asked the attorney whether the contract for the sale of her boat complied with state law. The parties already had an attorney-client relationship, so the attorney reasonably understood that the client was offering to hire him to review the contract and provide her with an opinion. The attorney's conduct in reviewing the contract and writing back to the client was a reasonable method of acceptance—especially since the client was headed out of town. Therefore, an implied-in-fact contract was created, and the attorney is likely to prevail. (Choice A) The doctrine of frustration of purpose only applies when the purpose of a contract becomes worthless due to an unexpected event. But the sales transaction's failure to conclude is not an unexpected event that would render the attorney's advice on the matter worthless. (Choice B) Even if an essential term is missing, a contract may be formed if it appears that the parties intended to form a contract and the court can supply the term with reasonable certainty. Here, the facts stipulate that $500 was a reasonable fee. (Choice C) The doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. A contract was formed here, so the attorney's reliance is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the client's note and the attorney's performance created an implied-in- fact contract."},"questionZh":"一位律师在他的办公室收到一份文件，附有一位客户的便条，他刚刚为该客户起草了一份遗嘱。纸条上写着：“你认为我的船的这份销售合同符合州法律吗？我本来会亲自和你谈谈这件事，但我正在出城的路上。我将于下周回来。”律师审阅了该文件，并向委托人写了一封一页的信函，声明该文件符合州法律。律师的账单中包括了 500 美元，这是一个合理的费用。客户拒绝向律师支付任何费用，辩称她从未同意保留律师，并且她没有从律师那里收到任何有价值的东西，因为销售交易从未完成。假设该司法管辖区没有适用的法规或规则来管理律师与委托人关系的形成。如果律师起诉委托人索要 500 美元，律师会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为即使双方达成协议，该协议也会根据目的受挫原则被解除。","B":"不，因为律师和委托人从未就合同的基本条款达成一致。","C":"是的，因为律师对委托人的说明采取了对他不利的行动。","D":"是的，因为客户的说明和律师的表现构成了默示的事实上的合同。"},"explanationZh":"要形成合同（即具有法律效力的协议），必须双方同意（也称为共识）。这需要：要约——一种沟通，使受要约人产生客观合理的期望，即要约人愿意签订具有约束力的合同，并且\n\n接受——受要约人以任何合理方式提出的要约条款的客观表现，除非要约要求特定的接受方式。\n\n相互同意可以通过书面或口头言语（创建明示合同）或通过行为（创建默示事实合同）来体现。在此，客户的说明询问律师其船只的销售合同是否符合州法律。双方已经建立了律师与客户的关系，因此律师合理地理解客户愿意聘请他来审查合同并向她提供意见。律师审查合同并向委托人回信的行为是一种合理的接受方式——特别是因为委托人要出城。因此，默示事实合同成立，律师很可能胜诉。 （选项A）目的落空原则仅适用于合同目的因意外事件而变得毫无价值的情况。但销售交易未能达成并非意外事件，不会导致律师就此事提出的建议变得毫无价值。 （选择B）即使缺少基本条款，如果双方当事人有意订立合同并且法院能够以合理的确定性提供该条款，则合同也可以成立。事实证明，500 美元是合理的费用。 （选项 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许一方在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。合同是在这里签订的，因此律师的信赖是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：形成具有约束力的合同所需的双方同意可以通过书面或口头言语（明示）或通过行为（事实上暗示）来体现。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 4, 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（作为同意的表现）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"形成具有约束力的合同所需的双方同意可以通过书面或口头言语（明示）或通过行为（事实上暗示）来体现。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果律师起诉委托人索要 500 美元，律师会胜诉吗？","rule":"形成具有约束力的合同所需的双方同意可以通过书面或口头言语（明示）或通过行为（事实上暗示）来体现。","analysis":"要形成合同（即具有法律效力的协议），必须双方同意（也称为共识）。这需要：要约——一种沟通，使受要约人产生客观合理的期望，即要约人愿意签订具有约束力的合同，并且\n\n接受——受要约人以任何合理方式提出的要约条款的客观表现，除非要约要求特定的接受方式。\n\n相互同意可以通过书面或口头言语（创建明示合同）或通过行为（创建默示事实合同）来体现。在此，客户的说明询问律师其船只的销售合同是否符合州法律。双方已经建立了律师与客户的关系，因此律师合理地理解客户愿意聘请他来审查合同并向她提供意见。律师审查合同并向委托人回信的行为是一种合理的接受方式——特别是因为委托人要出城。因此，默示事实合同成立，律师很可能胜诉。 （选项A）目的落空原则仅适用于合同目的因意外事件而变得毫无价值的情况。但销售交易未能达成并非意外事件，不会导致律师就此事提出的建议变得毫无价值。 （选择B）即使缺少基本条款，如果双方当事人有意订立合同并且法院能够以合理的确定性提供该条款，则合同也可以成立。事实证明，500 美元是合理的费用。 （选项 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许一方在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。合同是在这里签订的，因此律师的信赖是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：形成具有约束力的合同所需的双方同意可以通过书面或口头言语（明示）或通过行为（事实上暗示）来体现。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 4, 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（作为同意的表现）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为客户的说明和律师的表现构成了默示的事实上的合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"frustration of purpose","ipa":"/fɹʌstɹˈeɪʃən ˈəv pˈɝpəs/","syllables":"frus·tra·tion of pur·pose","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"合同目的落空：意外事件实质摧毁双方理解的主要目的","contextualMeaning":"合同目的落空：意外事件实质摧毁双方理解的主要目的","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"No, because even if the parties had an agreement, that agreement was discharged under the doctrine of frustration of purpose.","synonyms":["frustration doctrine"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-2aaf683d6eccf1a8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":120,"sourceQuestionPage":354,"sourceQuestionPages":[354],"sourceExplanationPages":[355,356],"sourceAnswerPages":[355,356],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-121","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A chef purchased the front portion of the land needed for a restaurant he desired to build and operate, but the back portion was the subject of a will dispute between a brother and sister. The sister's attorney advised her that her claim was doubtful. The chef, knowing only that the unresolved dispute existed, agreed in a signed writing to pay the sister $6,000, payable $1,000 annually, in exchange for a quitclaim deed (a deed containing no warranties). The sister promptly executed such a deed to the chef and received his first annual payment. Shortly thereafter, the probate court handed down a decision in the brother's favor, ruling that the sister had no interest in the land. This decision has become final. The chef subsequently defaulted when his second annual installment came due. In an action against the chef for breach of contract, will the sister probably win or lose?","choices":{"A":"Lose, because she suffered no legal detriment in executing the quitclaim deed.","B":"Lose, because she was aware at the time of the agreement with the chef that her claim to the property quitclaimed was doubtful.","C":"Win, because the chef bargained for and received in exchange a quitclaim deed from the sister.","D":"Win, because the chef, by paying the first $1,000 installment, is estopped to deny that his agreement with the sister is an enforceable contract."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. A promise to surrender a claim or defense constitutes consideration so long as: the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good faith dispute or the surrendering party honestly believes that the claim or defense may be valid. Issuing a quitclaim deed—ie, a deed that conveys whatever interest the grantor has in the property, if any, without any warranties—amounts to surrendering the grantor's claims against the property. A quitclaim deed can therefore serve as consideration if the foregoing requirements are met. Here, the chef promised to pay the sister $6,000, payable in $1,000 installments, in exchange for a quitclaim deed. Although the sister's claim to the property quitclaimed was doubtful, it was subject to an unresolved will dispute (good faith dispute) (Choice B). As a result, the sister's issuance of the quitclaim deed in exchange for the chef's promise of payment constituted bargained-for consideration. A valid contract was therefore formed, and the sister will probably win. (Choice A) Surrendering a claim or defense that may be valid (eg, the sister's issuing the quitclaim deed to the chef) does constitute a legal detriment. (Choice D) The doctrine of promissory estoppel lets a party enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. A valid contract arose between the parties here, so estoppel does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: A promise to surrender a claim or defense (eg, by issuing a quitclaim deed) can serve as consideration so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party honestly believes it may be valid.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims as consideration).","educationalObjective":"A promise to surrender a claim or defense (eg, by issuing a quitclaim deed) can serve as consideration so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party honestly believes it may be valid.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims as consideration).","rule":"A promise to surrender a claim or defense (eg, by issuing a quitclaim deed) can serve as consideration so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party honestly believes it may be valid.","irac":{"issue":"In an action against the chef for breach of contract, will the sister probably win or lose?","rule":"A promise to surrender a claim or defense (eg, by issuing a quitclaim deed) can serve as consideration so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party honestly believes it may be valid.","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A valid contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. A promise to surrender a claim or defense constitutes consideration so long as: the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good faith dispute or the surrendering party honestly believes that the claim or defense may be valid. Issuing a quitclaim deed—ie, a deed that conveys whatever interest the grantor has in the property, if any, without any warranties—amounts to surrendering the grantor's claims against the property. A quitclaim deed can therefore serve as consideration if the foregoing requirements are met. Here, the chef promised to pay the sister $6,000, payable in $1,000 installments, in exchange for a quitclaim deed. Although the sister's claim to the property quitclaimed was doubtful, it was subject to an unresolved will dispute (good faith dispute) (Choice B). As a result, the sister's issuance of the quitclaim deed in exchange for the chef's promise of payment constituted bargained-for consideration. A valid contract was therefore formed, and the sister will probably win. (Choice A) Surrendering a claim or defense that may be valid (eg, the sister's issuing the quitclaim deed to the chef) does constitute a legal detriment. (Choice D) The doctrine of promissory estoppel lets a party enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. A valid contract arose between the parties here, so estoppel does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer C: Win, because the chef bargained for and received in exchange a quitclaim deed from the sister."},"questionZh":"一位厨师购买了他想要建造和经营一家餐厅所需的前半部分土地，但后半部分是兄妹之间遗嘱纠纷的主题。姐姐的律师告诉她，她的主张值得怀疑。厨师只知道存在未解决的纠纷，同意在一份签署的书面文件中同意向姐姐支付 6,000 美元，每年支付 1,000 美元，以换取无偿索赔契约（不含任何保证的契约）。姐姐立即向厨师执行了这样的契约，并收到了他的第一笔年度付款。此后不久，遗嘱认证法院做出了对兄弟有利的裁决，裁定妹妹对这片土地没有兴趣。该决定已成为最终决定。当他的第二次年度分期付款到期时，这位厨师随后违约了。向厨师提起违约诉讼，姐姐到底会赢还是输呢？","choicesZh":{"A":"输了，因为她在执行放弃索赔契约时没有遭受任何法律损害。","B":"输了，因为她在与厨师达成协议时就知道她对所放弃财产的主张是值得怀疑的。","C":"获胜，因为厨师讨价还价并收到了姐姐的无偿债权契据作为交换。","D":"获胜，因为厨师在支付了第一笔 1,000 美元的分期付款后，就无法否认他与姐姐达成的协议是一份可执行的合同。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 当相互协议得到有价值的对价（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约）支持时，通常会形成有效的合同。放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺构成对价，只要：索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或者放弃方诚实地认为索赔或抗辩可能有效。签发无偿债权契据（即传达设保人在财产中拥有的任何利益（如果有的话）且没有任何保证的契据）相当于放弃设保人对财产的债权。因此，如果满足上述要求，无偿债权契约可以作为对价。在这里，厨师承诺向姐姐支付 6,000 美元，分期支付 1,000 美元，以换取放弃索赔契约。尽管姐姐对放弃主张的财产的主张存在疑问，但它受到未解决的遗嘱纠纷（善意纠纷）的影响（选项B）。因此，姐姐签发无偿债权契据以换取厨师承诺付款的行为构成了讨价还价。这样，一份有效的合同就成立了，姐姐很可能会获胜。 （选择A）放弃可能有效的索赔或抗辩（例如，姐妹向厨师签发无偿索赔契约）确实构成法律损害。 （选项 D） 禁止反言原则允许当事人在没有形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。双方之间已签订有效合同，因此禁止反言不适用。\n\n教育目标：只要 (1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃方真诚地相信其可能有效，则放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺（例如，通过签发放弃索赔契约）即可作为对价。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决作为对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺（例如，通过签发无偿索赔契约）可以作为对价，只要 (1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃方真诚地认为其可能有效。","iracZh":{"issue":"向厨师提起违约诉讼，姐姐到底会赢还是输呢？","rule":"放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺（例如，通过签发无偿索赔契约）可以作为对价，只要 (1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃方真诚地认为其可能有效。","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 当相互协议得到有价值的对价（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约）支持时，通常会形成有效的合同。放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺构成对价，只要：索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或者放弃方诚实地认为索赔或抗辩可能有效。签发无偿债权契据（即传达设保人在财产中拥有的任何利益（如果有的话）且没有任何保证的契据）相当于放弃设保人对财产的债权。因此，如果满足上述要求，无偿债权契约可以作为对价。在这里，厨师承诺向姐姐支付 6,000 美元，分期支付 1,000 美元，以换取放弃索赔契约。尽管姐姐对放弃主张的财产的主张存在疑问，但它受到未解决的遗嘱纠纷（善意纠纷）的影响（选项B）。因此，姐姐签发无偿债权契据以换取厨师承诺付款的行为构成了讨价还价。这样，一份有效的合同就成立了，姐姐很可能会获胜。 （选择A）放弃可能有效的索赔或抗辩（例如，姐妹向厨师签发无偿索赔契约）确实构成法律损害。 （选项 D） 禁止反言原则允许当事人在没有形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。双方之间已签订有效合同，因此禁止反言不适用。\n\n教育目标：只要 (1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃方真诚地相信其可能有效，则放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺（例如，通过签发放弃索赔契约）即可作为对价。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决作为对价）。","conclusion":"答案 C：获胜，因为厨师讨价还价并收到了姐姐的无偿债权契据作为交换。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":121,"sourceQuestionPage":357,"sourceQuestionPages":[357],"sourceExplanationPages":[358,359],"sourceAnswerPages":[358,359],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-122","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A U.S. West-Coast manufacturer gave a hardware retailer who was relocating to the East Coast the following signed \"letter of introduction\" to an East-Coast hardware wholesaler: \"This will introduce you to my good friend and former customer, who is a retailer and will be seeking to arrange the purchase of hardware inventory from you on credit. If you will let him have the goods, I will make good any loss up to $25,000 in the event of his default.\" The retailer presented the letter to a wholesaler, who then sold and delivered $20,000 worth of hardware to the retailer on credit. The wholesaler promptly notified the manufacturer of this sale. Which of the following is NOT an accurate statement concerning the arrangement between the manufacturer and the wholesaler?","choices":{"A":"Although the manufacturer received no consideration from the retailer, the manufacturer's promise is enforceable by the wholesaler.","B":"By extending the credit to the retailer, the wholesaler effectively accepted the manufacturer's offer for a unilateral contract.","C":"It was important to enforceability of the manufacturer's promise to the wholesaler that it be embodied in a signed writing.","D":"The manufacturer's promise is enforceable by the wholesaler whether or not the wholesaler gave the manufacturer seasonable notice of the extension of credit to the retailer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A unilateral contract arises when one party (offeror) promises to do something in return for performance of a specific act and the other party (offeree) accepts by completing the requested performance.* Notice is not required to make the acceptance effective unless the offer so requires. But if the offeree has reason to know that the offeror would not learn of the completed performance within a reasonable time, then the offeror's duty is discharged unless: the offeree exercises reasonable diligence to notify the offeror the offeror learns of the performance within a reasonable time or the offer indicates that notification of acceptance is not required. Here, the manufacturer promised to guarantee repayment of up to $25,000 if the wholesaler would let the retailer purchase hardware on credit (offer for unilateral contract). The wholesaler then sold and delivered $20,000 worth of hardware to the retailer on credit (acceptance) (Choice B). But the manufacturer would have had no way of learning about that transaction on its own. Therefore, the fact that the wholesaler notified the manufacturer of the wholesaler's extension of credit is the reason why the wholesaler can enforce the manufacturer's promise. *In contrast, an offer to enter into a bilateral contract can be accepted by making a return promise or by starting the requested performance. (Choice A) Although the manufacturer received no consideration from the retailer, its agreement with the wholesaler was supported by consideration. That is because the manufacturer's promise was induced by the wholesaler's extension of credit to the retailer (and vice versa). Therefore, the promise is not unenforceable for lack of consideration. (Choice C) The manufacturer's promise created a suretyship—ie, a contract where one party promises to assume responsibility for the duty of a third party to the contract. Therefore, it fell within the statute of frauds and needed to be in writing and signed by the party to be charged (the manufacturer).\n\nEducational objective: Notice of acceptance is not required to form a unilateral contract unless the offer requires notice OR the offeree has reason to know that the offeror would not learn of the completed performance within a reasonable time.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 54 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (acceptance by performance and necessity of notification to offeror).","educationalObjective":"Notice of acceptance is not required to form a unilateral contract unless the offer requires notice OR the offeree has reason to know that the offeror would not learn of the completed performance within a reasonable time.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 54 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (acceptance by performance and necessity of notification to offeror).","rule":"Notice of acceptance is not required to form a unilateral contract unless the offer requires notice OR the offeree has reason to know that the offeror would not learn of the completed performance within a reasonable time.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is NOT an accurate statement concerning the arrangement between the manufacturer and the wholesaler?","rule":"Notice of acceptance is not required to form a unilateral contract unless the offer requires notice OR the offeree has reason to know that the offeror would not learn of the completed performance within a reasonable time.","analysis":"A unilateral contract arises when one party (offeror) promises to do something in return for performance of a specific act and the other party (offeree) accepts by completing the requested performance.* Notice is not required to make the acceptance effective unless the offer so requires. But if the offeree has reason to know that the offeror would not learn of the completed performance within a reasonable time, then the offeror's duty is discharged unless: the offeree exercises reasonable diligence to notify the offeror the offeror learns of the performance within a reasonable time or the offer indicates that notification of acceptance is not required. Here, the manufacturer promised to guarantee repayment of up to $25,000 if the wholesaler would let the retailer purchase hardware on credit (offer for unilateral contract). The wholesaler then sold and delivered $20,000 worth of hardware to the retailer on credit (acceptance) (Choice B). But the manufacturer would have had no way of learning about that transaction on its own. Therefore, the fact that the wholesaler notified the manufacturer of the wholesaler's extension of credit is the reason why the wholesaler can enforce the manufacturer's promise. *In contrast, an offer to enter into a bilateral contract can be accepted by making a return promise or by starting the requested performance. (Choice A) Although the manufacturer received no consideration from the retailer, its agreement with the wholesaler was supported by consideration. That is because the manufacturer's promise was induced by the wholesaler's extension of credit to the retailer (and vice versa). Therefore, the promise is not unenforceable for lack of consideration. (Choice C) The manufacturer's promise created a suretyship—ie, a contract where one party promises to assume responsibility for the duty of a third party to the contract. Therefore, it fell within the statute of frauds and needed to be in writing and signed by the party to be charged (the manufacturer).","conclusion":"Answer D: The manufacturer's promise is enforceable by the wholesaler whether or not the wholesaler gave the manufacturer seasonable notice of the extension of credit to the retailer."},"questionZh":"一家美国西海岸制造商向一家搬迁到东海岸的硬件零售商提供了一份给东海岸硬件批发商的签名“介绍信”：“这将向您介绍我的好朋友和前客户，他是一位零售商，将寻求安排以赊购方式向您购买硬件库存。如果您让他收到货物，如果他违约，我将赔偿最高 25,000 美元的损失。”该零售商将这封信交给了一家批发商，然后批发商以赊销方式向零售商出售并交付了价值 20,000 美元的硬件。批发商立即将此次销售情况通知了制造商。关于制造商和批发商之间的安排，以下哪一项不正确？","choicesZh":{"A":"尽管制造商没有收到零售商的任何考虑，但制造商的承诺可由批发商强制执行。","B":"通过向零售商提供信贷，批发商实际上接受了制造商的单边合同要约。","C":"对于制造商向批发商做出的承诺的可执行性来说，将其体现在签署的书面文件中非常重要。","D":"制造商的承诺可由批发商强制执行，无论批发商是否及时向制造商发出向零售商提供信贷的通知。"},"explanationZh":"当一方（要约人）承诺做某事以换取履行特定行为，而另一方（受要约人）通过完成所要求的履行而接受时，单方合同就产生了。*除非要约要求，否则不需要发出通知即可使接受生效。但如果受要约人有理由知道要约人不会在合理时间内得知要约人已完成履约，则要约人的义务即被解除，除非：受要约人尽合理努力通知要约人要约人在合理时间内得知要约人已履约，或要约表明不需要通知接受。在此，制造商承诺，如果批发商允许零售商赊购硬件（单方合同报价），则制造商将保证最多 25,000 美元的还款。然后，批发商以赊销（承兑）方式向零售商出售并交付了价值 20,000 美元的硬件（选择 B）。但制造商无法自行了解该交易。因此，批发商向制造商通报批发商延长信用的情况，是批发商可以强制执行制造商承诺的原因。 *相比之下，签订双边合同的要约可以通过作出回报承诺或开始履行所要求的义务来接受。 （选项A）虽然制造商没有收到零售商的对价，但其与批发商的协议得到了对价的支持。这是因为制造商的承诺是由批发商向零售商提供信贷引起的（反之亦然）。因此，该承诺并非因缺乏约因而无法履行。 （选项C） 制造商的承诺创造了保证——即，一方承诺承担第三方对合同义务的责任的合同。因此，该行为属于欺诈行为，需要以书面形式并由被指控方（制造商）签字。\n\n教育目标：形成单方合同不需要发出接受通知，除非要约需要通知或受要约人有理由知道要约人不会在合理时间内获悉已完成的履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 54（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（通过履行接受并通知要约人的必要性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"形成单方合同不需要发出接受通知，除非要约需要通知或受要约人有理由知道要约人不会在合理时间内得知已完成的履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于制造商和批发商之间的安排，以下哪一项不正确？","rule":"形成单方合同不需要发出接受通知，除非要约需要通知或受要约人有理由知道要约人不会在合理时间内得知已完成的履行。","analysis":"当一方（要约人）承诺做某事以换取履行特定行为，而另一方（受要约人）通过完成所要求的履行而接受时，单方合同就产生了。*除非要约要求，否则不需要发出通知即可使接受生效。但如果受要约人有理由知道要约人不会在合理时间内得知要约人已完成履约，则要约人的义务即被解除，除非：受要约人尽合理努力通知要约人要约人在合理时间内得知要约人已履约，或要约表明不需要通知接受。在此，制造商承诺，如果批发商允许零售商赊购硬件（单方合同报价），则制造商将保证最多 25,000 美元的还款。然后，批发商以赊销（承兑）方式向零售商出售并交付了价值 20,000 美元的硬件（选择 B）。但制造商无法自行了解该交易。因此，批发商向制造商通报批发商延长信用的情况，是批发商可以强制执行制造商承诺的原因。 *相比之下，签订双边合同的要约可以通过作出回报承诺或开始履行所要求的义务来接受。 （选项A）虽然制造商没有收到零售商的对价，但其与批发商的协议得到了对价的支持。这是因为制造商的承诺是由批发商向零售商提供信贷引起的（反之亦然）。因此，该承诺并非因缺乏约因而无法履行。 （选项C） 制造商的承诺创造了保证——即，一方承诺承担第三方对合同义务的责任的合同。因此，该行为属于欺诈行为，需要以书面形式并由被指控方（制造商）签字。\n\n教育目标：形成单方合同不需要发出接受通知，除非要约需要通知或受要约人有理由知道要约人不会在合理时间内获悉已完成的履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 54（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（通过履行接受并通知要约人的必要性）。","conclusion":"答案 D：制造商的承诺可由批发商强制执行，无论批发商是否及时向制造商发出向零售商提供信贷的通知。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"seasonable notice","ipa":"/sˈizənəbəl nˈoʊtəs/","syllables":"sea·son·able no·tice","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"及时通知：在约定或合理时间内发出的通知","contextualMeaning":"及时通知：在约定或合理时间内发出的通知","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The manufacturer's promise is enforceable by the wholesaler whether or not the wholesaler gave the manufacturer seasonable notice of the extension of credit to the retailer.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-fa12b78262f1391b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":122,"sourceQuestionPage":360,"sourceQuestionPages":[360],"sourceExplanationPages":[361,362],"sourceAnswerPages":[361,362],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-123","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"The owner of a sports memorabilia store mailed a letter to a local collector offering to purchase a specific signed baseball from the collector for $500. The store owner placed no conditions on acceptance of the offer. The baseball was signed by the collector's favorite player, so she immediately mailed the store owner a letter rejecting his offer. An hour after sending the rejection letter, the collector realized she could use the money and sent the store owner an email accepting his offer. As between the store owner and the collector, which of the following is an accurate statement?","choices":{"A":"They do not have a contract, because the acceptance was sent by email and not by mail like the offer letter.","B":"They do not have a contract, because the collector's rejection letter was effective at the time it was sent.","C":"They have a contract, because the collector's subsequent acceptance effectively revoked her rejection of the offer.","D":"They have a contract, because the store owner received the emailed acceptance before he received the rejection letter."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract formation requires both an offer and an acceptance (ie, mutual assent). Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance sent by mail or similar means is generally effective upon dispatch, while a rejection is effective upon receipt (Choice B). However, the mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection and then sends an acceptance.* In that case, the first communication received by the offeror controls, regardless of whether the offeror actually reads that communication. Here, the collector mailed the store owner a letter rejecting his offer to purchase the signed baseball. An hour later, the collector sent the store owner an email accepting his offer. Because the rejection letter was sent first, the mailbox rule does not apply and whichever communication the store owner received first controls. The store owner necessarily received the emailed acceptance first—it was sent just one hour after the rejection letter was mailed—so the acceptance controls. Therefore, the store owner and the collector have a contract. *Conversely, if the offeree first sends an acceptance and then sends a rejection, the mailbox rule applies and a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance. However, the offeree may be estopped from enforcing the contract if the offeror received the rejection first and detrimentally relied on it (see image above). (Choice A) Any reasonable method of acceptance is allowed unless the offeror, as the master of the offer, specifically requires acceptance by a particular method (not seen here). Therefore, the store owner and the collector have a contract even though the collector sent her acceptance by email. (Choice C) The collector's rejection was ineffective because the store owner received the acceptance first. As a result, there was no rejection for the collector to revoke. Additionally, had the rejection been received first, the subsequent acceptance would have been treated as an offer to enter a contract on the terms of the acceptance—not a revocation of the earlier rejection.\n\nEducational objective: The mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection (generally effective upon receipt) and then an acceptance (generally effective upon dispatch). In that case, whichever communication is received first controls.\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (2020) (acceptance under the mailbox rule).","educationalObjective":"The mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection (generally effective upon receipt) and then an acceptance (generally effective upon dispatch). In that case, whichever communication is received first controls.","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (2020) (acceptance under the mailbox rule).","rule":"The mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection (generally effective upon receipt) and then an acceptance (generally effective upon dispatch). In that case, whichever communication is received first controls.","irac":{"issue":"As between the store owner and the collector, which of the following is an accurate statement?","rule":"The mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection (generally effective upon receipt) and then an acceptance (generally effective upon dispatch). In that case, whichever communication is received first controls.","analysis":"Contract formation requires both an offer and an acceptance (ie, mutual assent). Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance sent by mail or similar means is generally effective upon dispatch, while a rejection is effective upon receipt (Choice B). However, the mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection and then sends an acceptance.* In that case, the first communication received by the offeror controls, regardless of whether the offeror actually reads that communication. Here, the collector mailed the store owner a letter rejecting his offer to purchase the signed baseball. An hour later, the collector sent the store owner an email accepting his offer. Because the rejection letter was sent first, the mailbox rule does not apply and whichever communication the store owner received first controls. The store owner necessarily received the emailed acceptance first—it was sent just one hour after the rejection letter was mailed—so the acceptance controls. Therefore, the store owner and the collector have a contract. *Conversely, if the offeree first sends an acceptance and then sends a rejection, the mailbox rule applies and a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance. However, the offeree may be estopped from enforcing the contract if the offeror received the rejection first and detrimentally relied on it (see image above). (Choice A) Any reasonable method of acceptance is allowed unless the offeror, as the master of the offer, specifically requires acceptance by a particular method (not seen here). Therefore, the store owner and the collector have a contract even though the collector sent her acceptance by email. (Choice C) The collector's rejection was ineffective because the store owner received the acceptance first. As a result, there was no rejection for the collector to revoke. Additionally, had the rejection been received first, the subsequent acceptance would have been treated as an offer to enter a contract on the terms of the acceptance—not a revocation of the earlier rejection.","conclusion":"Answer D: They have a contract, because the store owner received the emailed acceptance before he received the rejection letter."},"questionZh":"一家体育纪念品商店的老板给当地一位收藏家寄了一封信，提出以 500 美元从收藏家那里购买一个特定的签名棒球。店主没有对接受报价提出任何条件。棒球是由收藏家最喜欢的球员签名的，所以她立即给店主寄了一封信，拒绝了他的报价。发送拒绝信一小时后，收藏家意识到她可以使用这笔钱，并向店主发送了一封电子邮件接受他的报价。店主和收藏家之间，以下哪项说法是准确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"他们没有合同，因为接受是通过电子邮件发送的，而不是像录取通知书那样通过邮件发送的。","B":"他们没有合同，因为收藏家的拒绝信在发送时有效。","C":"他们签订了合同，因为收藏家随后的接受实际上撤销了她对报价的拒绝。","D":"他们有合同，因为店主在收到拒绝信之前就收到了通过电子邮件发送的接受通知。"},"explanationZh":"合同的订立需要要约和接受（即双方同意）。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件或类似方式发送的接受通常在发出后生效，而拒绝在收到后生效（选择 B）。但是，如果受要约人先发送拒绝，然后发送接受，则邮箱规则不适用。*在这种情况下，以要约人收到的第一个通信为准，无论要约人是否实际阅读该通信。在这里，收藏家给店主寄了一封信，拒绝了他购买签名棒球的提议。一小时后，收藏家给店主发了一封电子邮件，接受了他的报价。由于拒绝信是先发送的，因此邮箱规则不适用，且由店主首先收到的通信控制。店主必须首先收到通过电子邮件发送的接受函——它是在拒绝信寄出后一小时内发送的——因此接受控制。因此，店主和收藏家之间是有合同的。 *相反，如果受要约人先发送承诺书，然后发送拒绝书，则适用邮箱规则，并在发送承诺书后即形成合同。然而，如果要约人首先收到拒绝并​​对其进行不利依赖，则受要约人可能会被阻止执行合同（见上图）。 （选项A）允许任何合理的接受方式，除非要约人作为要约的主人明确要求以特定方法接受（此处未见）。因此，尽管收藏家通过电子邮件发送了接受书，但店主和收藏家还是有合同的。 （选项C） 收藏家的拒绝没有效果，因为店主先收到了验收。结果，没有被收款人拒绝撤销。此外，如果首先收到拒绝，则随后的接受将被视为根据接受的条款签订合同的要约，而不是撤销先前的拒绝。\n\n教育目标：如果受要约者首先发送拒绝（通常在收到后生效）然后发送接受（通常在发送后生效），则邮箱规则不适用。在这种情况下，以首先接收到的通信为准。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (2020)（根据邮箱规则接受）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果受要约人首先发送拒绝（通常在收到后生效）然后发送接受（通常在发送后生效），则邮箱规则不适用。在这种情况下，以首先接收到的通信为准。","iracZh":{"issue":"店主和收藏家之间，以下哪项说法是准确的？","rule":"如果受要约人首先发送拒绝（通常在收到后生效）然后发送接受（通常在发送后生效），则邮箱规则不适用。在这种情况下，以首先接收到的通信为准。","analysis":"合同的订立需要要约和接受（即双方同意）。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件或类似方式发送的接受通常在发出后生效，而拒绝在收到后生效（选择 B）。但是，如果受要约人先发送拒绝，然后发送接受，则邮箱规则不适用。*在这种情况下，以要约人收到的第一个通信为准，无论要约人是否实际阅读该通信。在这里，收藏家给店主寄了一封信，拒绝了他购买签名棒球的提议。一小时后，收藏家给店主发了一封电子邮件，接受了他的报价。由于拒绝信是先发送的，因此邮箱规则不适用，且由店主首先收到的通信控制。店主必须首先收到通过电子邮件发送的接受函——它是在拒绝信寄出后一小时内发送的——因此接受控制。因此，店主和收藏家之间是有合同的。 *相反，如果受要约人先发送承诺书，然后发送拒绝书，则适用邮箱规则，并在发送承诺书后即形成合同。然而，如果要约人首先收到拒绝并​​对其进行不利依赖，则受要约人可能会被阻止执行合同（见上图）。 （选项A）允许任何合理的接受方式，除非要约人作为要约的主人明确要求以特定方法接受（此处未见）。因此，尽管收藏家通过电子邮件发送了接受书，但店主和收藏家还是有合同的。 （选项C） 收藏家的拒绝没有效果，因为店主先收到了验收。结果，没有被收款人拒绝撤销。此外，如果首先收到拒绝，则随后的接受将被视为根据接受的条款签订合同的要约，而不是撤销先前的拒绝。\n\n教育目标：如果受要约者首先发送拒绝（通常在收到后生效）然后发送接受（通常在发送后生效），则邮箱规则不适用。在这种情况下，以首先接收到的通信为准。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (2020)（根据邮箱规则接受）。","conclusion":"答案 D：他们有合同，因为店主在收到拒绝信之前就收到了通过电子邮件发送的接受通知。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"mailbox rule","ipa":"/mˈeɪlbˌɑks ɹˈul/","syllables":"mail·box rule","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"邮箱规则：除例外外，承诺在正确寄出时生效","contextualMeaning":"邮箱规则：除例外外，承诺在正确寄出时生效","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The mailbox rule does not apply if the offeree first sends a rejection (generally effective upon receipt) and then an acceptance (generally effective upon dispatch).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-c1c996d640008b3c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":123,"sourceQuestionPage":363,"sourceQuestionPages":[363],"sourceExplanationPages":[364,365],"sourceAnswerPages":[364,365],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-124","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"In January, a garden center contacted a farmer who owned a greenhouse about growing seedlings for sale in the spring. The garden center promised in writing to buy, at a fixed price, all of the seedlings that the farmer raised in his greenhouse. As a consequence, a few days later the farmer purchased containers and seeds and hired a worker to prepare the containers, plant the seeds, and tend to the seedlings. Later that week, he emailed the garden center to tell it he had taken these actions. Just prior to the delivery of any seedlings, the garden center notified the farmer that it would not purchase any of the seedlings. The farmer sold the seedlings at a price far below the price set by the garden center. The farmer filed a breach of contract action to recover damages. Will the farmer likely succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the garden center did not receive consideration for its promise to buy the farmer's output.","B":"No, because the garden center revoked its offer before it was accepted by the farmer.","C":"Yes, because the doctrine of promissory estoppel made the offer irrevocable.","D":"Yes, because the farmer accepted the garden center's offer by beginning performance."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract formation requires mutual assent, which occurs upon acceptance of an offer to enter a contract. Unless an offer specifies that it can only be accepted by performance (ie, unilateral contract), it can be accepted by promising or beginning performance. If acceptance is made by beginning performance, the offeree must notify the offeror within a reasonable time that performance has begun. Failure to do so will result in a lapse of the offer. Here, the garden center promised to buy all the seedlings raised by the farmer. Since the farmer began to raise the seedlings—eg, by purchasing containers and seeds and by hiring a worker to tend the seedlings—the farmer accepted the garden center's offer by beginning performance. And since the farmer notified the garden center of this acceptance within a reasonable time (\"later that week\"), the garden center could not subsequently revoke its offer and refuse to buy the seedlings (Choice B). Therefore, the farmer will likely succeed. (Choice A) The garden center did receive consideration because its promise induced the farmer's reciprocal commitment to produce and sell the seedlings. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an offer may become irrevocable if the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relies on it before acceptance. But this doctrine does not apply here since acceptance occurred, and a contract was formed, when the farmer began to raise the seedlings and notified the garden center.\n\nEducational objective: Unless an offer specifies that it can only be accepted by performance, it can be accepted by promising or beginning performance. An offeree who accepts by beginning performance must notify the offeror within a reasonable time that performance has begun.","educationalObjective":"Unless an offer specifies that it can only be accepted by performance, it can be accepted by promising or beginning performance. An offeree who accepts by beginning performance must notify the offeror within a reasonable time that performance has begun.","references":"","rule":"Unless an offer specifies that it can only be accepted by performance, it can be accepted by promising or beginning performance. An offeree who accepts by beginning performance must notify the offeror within a reasonable time that performance has begun.","irac":{"issue":"Will the farmer likely succeed?","rule":"Unless an offer specifies that it can only be accepted by performance, it can be accepted by promising or beginning performance. An offeree who accepts by beginning performance must notify the offeror within a reasonable time that performance has begun.","analysis":"Contract formation requires mutual assent, which occurs upon acceptance of an offer to enter a contract. Unless an offer specifies that it can only be accepted by performance (ie, unilateral contract), it can be accepted by promising or beginning performance. If acceptance is made by beginning performance, the offeree must notify the offeror within a reasonable time that performance has begun. Failure to do so will result in a lapse of the offer. Here, the garden center promised to buy all the seedlings raised by the farmer. Since the farmer began to raise the seedlings—eg, by purchasing containers and seeds and by hiring a worker to tend the seedlings—the farmer accepted the garden center's offer by beginning performance. And since the farmer notified the garden center of this acceptance within a reasonable time (\"later that week\"), the garden center could not subsequently revoke its offer and refuse to buy the seedlings (Choice B). Therefore, the farmer will likely succeed. (Choice A) The garden center did receive consideration because its promise induced the farmer's reciprocal commitment to produce and sell the seedlings. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an offer may become irrevocable if the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relies on it before acceptance. But this doctrine does not apply here since acceptance occurred, and a contract was formed, when the farmer began to raise the seedlings and notified the garden center.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the farmer accepted the garden center's offer by beginning performance."},"questionZh":"一月份，一家园艺中心联系了一位拥有温室的农民，希望种植苗木以便在春季出售。园艺中心书面承诺以固定价格购买农民在温室里种植的所有树苗。结果，几天后，农民购买了容器和种子，并聘请了一名工人来准备容器、种植种子和照料幼苗。那周晚些时候，他给花园中心发了电子邮件，告诉它他已经采取了这些行动。就在交付任何苗木之前，园艺中心通知农民不会购买任何苗木。农民以远低于园艺中心定价的价格出售树苗。农民提起违约诉讼，要求赔偿损失。农民可能会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为园艺中心承诺购买农民的农产品，但未获得约因。","B":"不，因为园艺中心在农民接受之前就撤回了其报价。","C":"是的，因为禁止反言原则使得该要约不可撤销。","D":"是的，因为农民接受了园艺中心的提议并开始履行。"},"explanationZh":"合同的成立需要双方同意，即在接受签订合同的要约后发生。除非要约明确规定只能通过履行（即单方合同）来接受，否则可以通过承诺或开始履行来接受。如果通过开始履行而作出承诺，受要约人必须在合理时间内通知要约人履行已开始。否则将导致要约失效。在这里，园艺中心承诺购买农民种植的所有苗木。自从农民开始培育幼苗（例如，通过购买容器和种子并雇用工人照料幼苗），农民就开始履行，接受了园艺中心的报价。由于农民在合理的时间内（“当周晚些时候”）通知了园艺中心接受该承诺，因此园艺中心随后无法撤销其报价并拒绝购买幼苗（选择 B）。因此，农民很可能会成功。 （选项A） 园艺中心确实受到了考虑，因为它的承诺引起了农民对生产和销售树苗的相互承诺。 （选项C）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果受要约人在接受之前合理且有害地依赖要约，则该要约可能变得不可撤销。但这一原则并不适用于此，因为当农民开始育苗并通知园艺中心时，就已经接受并签订了合同。\n\n教育目标：除非要约指定只能通过表现来接受，否则可以通过承诺或开始表现来接受。通过开始履行而接受的受要约人必须在合理时间内通知要约人履行已开始。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非要约规定只能通过履行来接受，否则可以通过承诺或开始履行来接受。通过开始履行而接受的受要约人必须在合理时间内通知要约人履行已开始。","iracZh":{"issue":"农民可能会成功吗？","rule":"除非要约规定只能通过履行来接受，否则可以通过承诺或开始履行来接受。通过开始履行而接受的受要约人必须在合理时间内通知要约人履行已开始。","analysis":"合同的成立需要双方同意，即在接受签订合同的要约后发生。除非要约明确规定只能通过履行（即单方合同）来接受，否则可以通过承诺或开始履行来接受。如果通过开始履行而作出承诺，受要约人必须在合理时间内通知要约人履行已开始。否则将导致要约失效。在这里，园艺中心承诺购买农民种植的所有苗木。自从农民开始培育幼苗（例如，通过购买容器和种子并雇用工人照料幼苗），农民就开始履行，接受了园艺中心的报价。由于农民在合理的时间内（“当周晚些时候”）通知了园艺中心接受该承诺，因此园艺中心随后无法撤销其报价并拒绝购买幼苗（选择 B）。因此，农民很可能会成功。 （选项A） 园艺中心确实受到了考虑，因为它的承诺引起了农民对生产和销售树苗的相互承诺。 （选项C）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果受要约人在接受之前合理且有害地依赖要约，则该要约可能变得不可撤销。但这一原则并不适用于此，因为当农民开始育苗并通知园艺中心时，就已经接受并签订了合同。\n\n教育目标：除非要约指定只能通过表现来接受，否则可以通过承诺或开始表现来接受。通过开始履行而接受的受要约人必须在合理时间内通知要约人履行已开始。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为农民接受了园艺中心的提议并开始履行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"output","ipa":"/ˈaʊtpˌʊt/","syllables":"out·put","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 输出, 产品, 产量；[计] 输出","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“output”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:outputs/d:output/0:output/1:d/3:outputs/i:outputting/f:outputs","sourceSentence":"No, because the garden center did not receive consideration for its promise to buy the farmer's output.","synonyms":["yield","end product","outturn","turnout"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-fa12b78262f1391b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":124,"sourceQuestionPage":366,"sourceQuestionPages":[366],"sourceExplanationPages":[367,368],"sourceAnswerPages":[367,368],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-125","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A real estate investor found a house for sale that he was interested in buying. The investor spoke with the owner of the house and sent her a written offer to purchase the house for $200,000. The offer stipulated that it would remain open for one month. One week later, the investor suffered a stroke and was hospitalized. The stroke caused severe brain damage and permanent mental disability. The owner, having no knowledge of the investor's condition, decided to accept his offer. She signed the written offer and mailed it to the investor's address one day before the offer was set to expire. The investor received the signed offer two days later. Is there an enforceable contract that binds the owner and the investor?","choices":{"A":"No, because the investor received the acceptance after the stipulated one-month period.","B":"No, because the investor's offer terminated when he became permanently mentally incapacitated.","C":"Yes, because the owner had no knowledge of the investor's condition when she accepted his offer.","D":"Yes, because the owner mailed the acceptance within the stipulated one-month period."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An offer to form a contract generally remains open for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time. However, an offer will terminate prematurely if the offeror dies OR becomes permanently mentally incapacitated* before the offer is accepted—with one exception. An offer that is an option will not terminate under such circumstances because consideration was paid to keep the offer open for a specified period of time. Here, the investor offered to buy the owner's house for $200,000. Although the offer stipulated that it would remain open for one month, it was not an option because the owner had not paid any consideration to keep the offer open. The investor then suffered a stroke that caused severe brain damage and permanent mental disability. The investor's permanent incapacity caused his offer to terminate prematurely, so the owner's subsequent attempt to accept it was ineffective. As a result, there is no contract that binds the parties. *Permanent mental incapacity is presumed if there has been a formal adjudication that a party lacks the capacity to enter into a contract with another. (Choices A & D) Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance that is mailed within the allotted response time is effective when it is sent—not when it is received. Because the owner's acceptance was mailed within the one-month period stipulated in the offer, it would have been effective had the investor's offer still been open.** But since the offer terminated when the investor became permanently mentally incapacitated, there is no contract. **Had the offer specified that acceptance must be received within one month, the mailbox rule would not apply and the owner's acceptance would have been ineffective. (Choice C) An offer terminates automatically upon death or permanent mental incapacity of the offeror (investor), regardless of the offeree's (owner's) knowledge thereof.\n\nEducational objective: An offer will terminate if the offeror dies or becomes permanently mentally incapacitated prior to acceptance. However, an exception exists for an offer that is an option because consideration was paid to keep the offer open.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 48 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (offer terminates when offeror is permanently deprived of capacity to contract).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 12 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (capacity to contract).","educationalObjective":"An offer will terminate if the offeror dies or becomes permanently mentally incapacitated prior to acceptance. However, an exception exists for an offer that is an option because consideration was paid to keep the offer open.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 48 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (offer terminates when offeror is permanently deprived of capacity to contract).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 12 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (capacity to contract).","rule":"An offer will terminate if the offeror dies or becomes permanently mentally incapacitated prior to acceptance. However, an exception exists for an offer that is an option because consideration was paid to keep the offer open.","irac":{"issue":"Is there an enforceable contract that binds the owner and the investor?","rule":"An offer will terminate if the offeror dies or becomes permanently mentally incapacitated prior to acceptance. However, an exception exists for an offer that is an option because consideration was paid to keep the offer open.","analysis":"An offer to form a contract generally remains open for the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, for a reasonable time. However, an offer will terminate prematurely if the offeror dies OR becomes permanently mentally incapacitated* before the offer is accepted—with one exception. An offer that is an option will not terminate under such circumstances because consideration was paid to keep the offer open for a specified period of time. Here, the investor offered to buy the owner's house for $200,000. Although the offer stipulated that it would remain open for one month, it was not an option because the owner had not paid any consideration to keep the offer open. The investor then suffered a stroke that caused severe brain damage and permanent mental disability. The investor's permanent incapacity caused his offer to terminate prematurely, so the owner's subsequent attempt to accept it was ineffective. As a result, there is no contract that binds the parties. *Permanent mental incapacity is presumed if there has been a formal adjudication that a party lacks the capacity to enter into a contract with another. (Choices A & D) Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance that is mailed within the allotted response time is effective when it is sent—not when it is received. Because the owner's acceptance was mailed within the one-month period stipulated in the offer, it would have been effective had the investor's offer still been open.** But since the offer terminated when the investor became permanently mentally incapacitated, there is no contract. **Had the offer specified that acceptance must be received within one month, the mailbox rule would not apply and the owner's acceptance would have been ineffective. (Choice C) An offer terminates automatically upon death or permanent mental incapacity of the offeror (investor), regardless of the offeree's (owner's) knowledge thereof.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the investor's offer terminated when he became permanently mentally incapacitated."},"questionZh":"一位房地产投资者找到了他有兴趣购买的待售房屋。投资者与房主进行了交谈，并向她发送了一份以 20 万美元购买该房屋的书面报价。该要约规定将保留一个月。一周后，投资者中风住院。中风造成严重的脑损伤和永久性精神残疾。业主不了解投资者的状况，决定接受他的报价。她签署了书面要约，并在要约到期前一天将其邮寄到投资者的地址。两天后，投资者收到了签署的要约。是否存在对所有者和投资者具有约束力的可执行合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为投资者在规定的一个月期限后收到了接受函。","B":"不可以，因为当投资者永久精神上丧失行为能力时，他的要约就终止了。","C":"是的，因为业主在接受投资者的报价时并不了解投资者的状况。","D":"是的，因为业主在规定的一个月内邮寄了验收单。"},"explanationZh":"形成合同的要约通常在要约中规定的时间内保持有效，或者，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内保持有效。然而，如果要约人在要约被接受之前死亡或永久精神上无行为能力*，要约将提前终止——但有一个例外。在这种情况下，作为期权的要约不会终止，因为已支付对价以使要约在指定时间内保持开放。在这里，投资者提出以 20 万美元购买业主的房子。尽管要约规定将保留一个月，但这并不是一种选择，因为业主没有支付任何对价来保持要约的开放。随后，这位投资者中风，导致严重脑损伤和永久性精神残疾。投资者的永久丧失行为能力导致其要约提前终止，因此业主随后试图接受该要约的尝试无效。因此，不存在对双方具有约束力的合同。 *如果有正式裁决，一方缺乏与另一方签订合同的能力，则可推定为永久精神上无行为能力。 （选项 A 和 D）根据邮箱规则，在指定的响应时间内邮寄的接受函在发送时生效，而不是在收到时生效。由于业主的接受书是在要约规定的一个月期限内邮寄的，因此如果投资者的要约仍然有效，则该承诺书将有效。**但由于要约在投资者永久精神上无行为能力时终止，因此不存在合同。 **如果要约规定必须在一个月内收到接受，则邮箱规则将不适用，业主的接受将无效。 （选项C）要约在要约人（投资者）死亡或永久精神丧失行为能力时自动终止，无论受要约人（所有者）是否知情。\n\n教育目标：如果要约人在接受之前死亡或永久精神上无行为能力，则要约将终止。然而，作为期权的要约存在例外情况，因为已支付对价以保持要约开放。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 48 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当要约人被永久剥夺缔约能力时，要约终止）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 12（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（合同能力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果要约人在接受之前死亡或永久精神上无行为能力，则要约将终止。然而，作为期权的要约存在例外情况，因为已支付对价以保持要约开放。","iracZh":{"issue":"是否存在对所有者和投资者具有约束力的可执行合同？","rule":"如果要约人在接受之前死亡或永久精神上无行为能力，则要约将终止。然而，作为期权的要约存在例外情况，因为已支付对价以保持要约开放。","analysis":"形成合同的要约通常在要约中规定的时间内保持有效，或者，如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内保持有效。然而，如果要约人在要约被接受之前死亡或永久精神上无行为能力*，要约将提前终止——但有一个例外。在这种情况下，作为期权的要约不会终止，因为已支付对价以使要约在指定时间内保持开放。在这里，投资者提出以 20 万美元购买业主的房子。尽管要约规定将保留一个月，但这并不是一种选择，因为业主没有支付任何对价来保持要约的开放。随后，这位投资者中风，导致严重脑损伤和永久性精神残疾。投资者的永久丧失行为能力导致其要约提前终止，因此业主随后试图接受该要约的尝试无效。因此，不存在对双方具有约束力的合同。 *如果有正式裁决，一方缺乏与另一方签订合同的能力，则可推定为永久精神上无行为能力。 （选项 A 和 D）根据邮箱规则，在指定的响应时间内邮寄的接受函在发送时生效，而不是在收到时生效。由于业主的接受书是在要约规定的一个月期限内邮寄的，因此如果投资者的要约仍然有效，则该承诺书将有效。**但由于要约在投资者永久精神上无行为能力时终止，因此不存在合同。 **如果要约规定必须在一个月内收到接受，则邮箱规则将不适用，业主的接受将无效。 （选项C）要约在要约人（投资者）死亡或永久精神丧失行为能力时自动终止，无论受要约人（所有者）是否知情。\n\n教育目标：如果要约人在接受之前死亡或永久精神上无行为能力，则要约将终止。然而，作为期权的要约存在例外情况，因为已支付对价以保持要约开放。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 48 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（当要约人被永久剥夺缔约能力时，要约终止）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 12（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（合同能力）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不可以，因为当投资者永久精神上丧失行为能力时，他的要约就终止了。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-000bc9ed3309e59c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":125,"sourceQuestionPage":369,"sourceQuestionPages":[369],"sourceExplanationPages":[370,371],"sourceAnswerPages":[370,371],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-126","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A door-to-door saleswoman for an extermination company introduced herself to a homeowner and stated, \"We are offering a one-time special. Our extermination truck is coming around this afternoon, and our technician will spray your entire yard for mosquitoes for just $100.\" The homeowner replied, \"I will give you $75 for that service, but no more.\" The saleswoman said, \"Unfortunately, I cannot accept $75, but the offer still stands at $100. I will be in the neighborhood for the next hour if you change your mind.\" The saleswoman left. After thinking it over, the homeowner decided that $100 was a good deal, so he found the saleswoman as she was returning to her car and said to her, \"I changed my mind. I accept your offer for $100.\" The saleswoman replied, \"I apologize, but I was just informed that the truck ran out of mosquito repellent for the day and the offer is no longer available.\" The extermination company never again sent its truck to the homeowner's neighborhood. If the homeowner sues the extermination company in small claims court for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the extermination company's duty to perform was discharged due to impracticability.","B":"No, because the homeowner's counteroffer of $75 was also a rejection of the saleswoman's offer.","C":"Yes, because the extermination company was a merchant and the firm-offer rule applies.","D":"Yes, because the saleswoman offered the service for $100 and the homeowner accepted the offer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"To form a contract, an offer must be accepted before it terminates. An offer will terminate if, for example, the offeree rejects it by clearly conveying that he/she does not intend to accept the offer OR by making a counteroffer. Once the offer has terminated, it cannot be accepted. But the offer can be revived if the offeror conveys that it is still open. This creates a renewed opportunity for acceptance by the offeree. If the offeree then accepts, the offer can no longer be rescinded and a binding contract is formed. Here, the saleswoman offered to spray the homeowner's yard for $100. This offer terminated when the homeowner rejected it by counteroffering for $75 (Choice B). However, the saleswoman then revived her original offer (\"The offer still stands at $100\"), and the homeowner accepted it shortly thereafter. The saleswoman was unable to rescind the offer once the man accepted it, and a binding contract was formed. The extermination company then breached the contract by failing to spray the homeowner's yard. Therefore, the homeowner is likely to prevail.* *However, the homeowner can only recover damages if the market value of the agreed- upon service (spraying his yard for mosquitoes) exceeds the contract price ($100). (Choice A) Contractual obligations can be discharged if they become impossible or impracticable to perform due to unforeseen circumstances. But here, even though the truck ran out of mosquito repellent that day, the truck could have sprayed the homeowner's yard on another day. (Choice C) Under the UCC's firm-offer rule, a merchant's offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for a stated or reasonable time. But since this rule does not apply to contracts for services (eg, spraying for mosquitoes), the extermination company's merchant status is irrelevant and the firm-offer rule does not apply.\n\nEducational objective: An offer cannot be accepted after it terminates (eg, is rejected by the offeree). But the offer can be revived if the offeror conveys that it is still open, which creates a renewed opportunity for the offeree to accept. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 84 (2020) (explaining that a rejected offer has new vitality if the offeror renews the offer).","educationalObjective":"An offer cannot be accepted after it terminates (eg, is rejected by the offeree). But the offer can be revived if the offeror conveys that it is still open, which creates a renewed opportunity for the offeree to accept. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 84 (2020) (explaining that a rejected offer has new vitality if the offeror renews the offer).","rule":"An offer cannot be accepted after it terminates (eg, is rejected by the offeree). But the offer can be revived if the offeror conveys that it is still open, which creates a renewed opportunity for the offeree to accept. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"If the homeowner sues the extermination company in small claims court for breach of contract, will he be likely to prevail?","rule":"An offer cannot be accepted after it terminates (eg, is rejected by the offeree). But the offer can be revived if the offeror conveys that it is still open, which creates a renewed opportunity for the offeree to accept. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"To form a contract, an offer must be accepted before it terminates. An offer will terminate if, for example, the offeree rejects it by clearly conveying that he/she does not intend to accept the offer OR by making a counteroffer. Once the offer has terminated, it cannot be accepted. But the offer can be revived if the offeror conveys that it is still open. This creates a renewed opportunity for acceptance by the offeree. If the offeree then accepts, the offer can no longer be rescinded and a binding contract is formed. Here, the saleswoman offered to spray the homeowner's yard for $100. This offer terminated when the homeowner rejected it by counteroffering for $75 (Choice B). However, the saleswoman then revived her original offer (\"The offer still stands at $100\"), and the homeowner accepted it shortly thereafter. The saleswoman was unable to rescind the offer once the man accepted it, and a binding contract was formed. The extermination company then breached the contract by failing to spray the homeowner's yard. Therefore, the homeowner is likely to prevail.* *However, the homeowner can only recover damages if the market value of the agreed- upon service (spraying his yard for mosquitoes) exceeds the contract price ($100). (Choice A) Contractual obligations can be discharged if they become impossible or impracticable to perform due to unforeseen circumstances. But here, even though the truck ran out of mosquito repellent that day, the truck could have sprayed the homeowner's yard on another day. (Choice C) Under the UCC's firm-offer rule, a merchant's offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for a stated or reasonable time. But since this rule does not apply to contracts for services (eg, spraying for mosquitoes), the extermination company's merchant status is irrelevant and the firm-offer rule does not apply.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the saleswoman offered the service for $100 and the homeowner accepted the offer."},"questionZh":"一家灭蚊公司的上门推销员向一位房主介绍自己，并表示：“我们正在提供一次性特价。我们的灭蚊卡车今天下午就到了，我们的技术人员将为您的整个院子喷洒蚊子，只需 100 美元。”房主回答说：“我会给你 75 美元的服务费，但不能再多了。”售货员说：“不幸的是，我不能接受 75 美元，但报价仍然是 100 美元。如果你改变主意，我将在接下来的一个小时里到附近。”售货员离开了。房主想了想，觉得100美元很划算，于是他找到了正在回到车上的售货员，对她说：“我改变主意了，我接受你100美元的报价。”售货员回答说：“抱歉，我刚刚得知卡车当天的驱蚊剂用完了，不再提供优惠。”消灭公司再也没有派卡车到房主的社区。如果房主以违约罪向小额索赔法庭起诉消灭公司，他有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为消灭公司的履行义务因不切实际而被解除。","B":"不，因为房主还价 75 美元，这也是对女售货员的拒绝。","C":"是的，因为消灭公司是商人，适用确定要约规则（firm-offer rule）。","D":"是的，因为女售货员以 100 美元的价格提供这项服务，而房主接受了这个报价。"},"explanationZh":"要形成合同，必须在要约终止之前接受要约。例如，如果受要约人明确表示他/她不打算接受要约或提出还盘而拒绝要约，则要约将终止。优惠一旦终止，将无法被接受。但如果要约人表示其仍处于开放状态，则可以恢复要约。这为受要约人接受创造了新的机会。如果受要约人接受，要约就不能再撤销，并形成具有约束力的合同。在这里，女售货员提出以 100 美元的价格为房主的院子喷洒农药。当房主还价 75 美元（选择 B）拒绝该报价时，该报价终止。然而，售货员随后又恢复了原来的报价（“报价仍然是100美元”），房主很快就接受了。一旦该男子接受了报价，女售货员就无法撤销该报价，并形成了具有约束力的合同。随后，灭虫公司未能对房主的院子进行喷洒，从而违反了合同。因此，房主很可能胜诉。* *但是，只有当约定的服务（在院子里喷洒灭蚊剂）的市场价值超过合同价格（100 美元）时，房主才能获得损害赔偿。 （选择A）如果由于不可预见的情况而无法或不切实际地履行合同义务，则可以解除合同义务。但在这里，即使那天卡车上的驱蚊剂用完了，改天卡车也可以给房主的院子喷洒。 （选项C） 根据UCC 的确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家购买或出售商品的报价在规定的​​或合理的时间内不可撤销。但由于该规则不适用于服务合同（例如喷洒蚊子），因此灭虫公司的商业地位无关紧要，确定要约规则（firm-offer rule）也不适用。\n\n教育目标：要约终止后就不能再接受（例如，被受要约人拒绝）。但如果要约人表示其仍处于开放状态，则要约可以恢复，这为受要约人提供了新的接受机会。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d Contracts § 84 (2020)（解释了如果要约人续订要约，被拒绝的要约将具有新的生命力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要约终止后就不能再被接受（例如，被受要约人拒绝）。但如果要约人表示其仍处于开放状态，则要约可以恢复，这为受要约人提供了新的接受机会。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果房主以违约罪向小额索赔法庭起诉消灭公司，他有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"要约终止后就不能再被接受（例如，被受要约人拒绝）。但如果要约人表示其仍处于开放状态，则要约可以恢复，这为受要约人提供了新的接受机会。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"要形成合同，必须在要约终止之前接受要约。例如，如果受要约人明确表示他/她不打算接受要约或提出还盘而拒绝要约，则要约将终止。优惠一旦终止，将无法被接受。但如果要约人表示其仍处于开放状态，则可以恢复要约。这为受要约人接受创造了新的机会。如果受要约人接受，要约就不能再撤销，并形成具有约束力的合同。在这里，女售货员提出以 100 美元的价格为房主的院子喷洒农药。当房主还价 75 美元（选择 B）拒绝该报价时，该报价终止。然而，售货员随后又恢复了原来的报价（“报价仍然是100美元”），房主很快就接受了。一旦该男子接受了报价，女售货员就无法撤销该报价，并形成了具有约束力的合同。随后，灭虫公司未能对房主的院子进行喷洒，从而违反了合同。因此，房主很可能胜诉。* *但是，只有当约定的服务（在院子里喷洒灭蚊剂）的市场价值超过合同价格（100 美元）时，房主才能获得损害赔偿。 （选择A）如果由于不可预见的情况而无法或不切实际地履行合同义务，则可以解除合同义务。但在这里，即使那天卡车上的驱蚊剂用完了，改天卡车也可以给房主的院子喷洒。 （选项C） 根据UCC 的确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家购买或出售商品的报价在规定的​​或合理的时间内不可撤销。但由于该规则不适用于服务合同（例如喷洒蚊子），因此灭虫公司的商业地位无关紧要，确定要约规则（firm-offer rule）也不适用。\n\n教育目标：要约终止后就不能再接受（例如，被受要约人拒绝）。但如果要约人表示其仍处于开放状态，则要约可以恢复，这为受要约人提供了新的接受机会。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d Contracts § 84 (2020)（解释了如果要约人续订要约，被拒绝的要约将具有新的生命力）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为女售货员以 100 美元的价格提供这项服务，而房主接受了这个报价。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-8b48e598a9398ca1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":126,"sourceQuestionPage":372,"sourceQuestionPages":[372],"sourceExplanationPages":[373,374],"sourceAnswerPages":[373,374],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-127","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"On July 18, a shovel manufacturer received an order for the purchase of 500 snow shovels from a wholesaler. The wholesaler had mailed the purchase order on July 15. The order required shipment of the shovels no earlier than September 15 and no later than October 15. Typed conspicuously across the front of the order form was the following: \"Wholesaler reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before September 1.\" The manufacturer's mailed response, \"We accept your order,\" was received by the wholesaler on July 21. As of July 22, which of the following is an accurate statement as to whether a contract was formed?","choices":{"A":"A contract was formed, but prior to September 1 it was an option contract terminable only at the will of the wholesaler.","B":"A contract was formed, but prior to September 1 it was terminable at the will of either party.","C":"No contract was formed, because of the wholesaler's reservation of the right to cancel.","D":"No contract was formed, because the wholesaler's order was only a revocable offer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation\n\nGift No bargained-for exchange\n\nToken consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value\n\nSham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed\n\nIllusory promise No legal detriment if apparent promise imposes no obligation\n\nUnder the UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods, contract formation requires: mutual assent – an offer and acceptance and valuable consideration – a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. Once a contract is formed, it cannot be terminated unilaterally (Choice B). But if either party's promise is illusory because it imposes no obligation on that party, then there is no consideration and no contract. Here, the wholesaler sent a purchase order to the shovel manufacturer and received a mailed acceptance on July 21. But the purchase order imposed no obligation on the wholesaler because it reserved the right to cancel at any time before September 1. As a result, the wholesaler's promise to purchase shovels was illusory. Due to this lack of consideration, no contract was formed as of July 22. (Choice A) An option is a type of contract in which one party promises to hold a separate offer open for a specified time in return for consideration. Here, however, there was no promise to hold the offer open for any amount of time. And if an option contract had formed, it would not be terminable at the will of either party. (Choice D) Offers are revocable prior to acceptance (with a few exceptions), so the revocability of an offer does not prevent contract formation. But since the wholesaler reserved the right to cancel (ie, revoke) at any time before September 1, its illusory promise to purchase shovels prevented contract formation.\n\nEducational objective: An illusory promise is one that imposes no obligation on the promising party and therefore fails to provide the consideration needed for contract formation.\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 77 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an apparent promise is not consideration if it makes performance entirely optional).","educationalObjective":"An illusory promise is one that imposes no obligation on the promising party and therefore fails to provide the consideration needed for contract formation.\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 77 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an apparent promise is not consideration if it makes performance entirely optional).","rule":"An illusory promise is one that imposes no obligation on the promising party and therefore fails to provide the consideration needed for contract formation.\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"As of July 22, which of the following is an accurate statement as to whether a contract was formed?","rule":"An illusory promise is one that imposes no obligation on the promising party and therefore fails to provide the consideration needed for contract formation.\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation\n\nGift No bargained-for exchange\n\nToken consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value\n\nSham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed\n\nIllusory promise No legal detriment if apparent promise imposes no obligation\n\nUnder the UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods, contract formation requires: mutual assent – an offer and acceptance and valuable consideration – a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. Once a contract is formed, it cannot be terminated unilaterally (Choice B). But if either party's promise is illusory because it imposes no obligation on that party, then there is no consideration and no contract. Here, the wholesaler sent a purchase order to the shovel manufacturer and received a mailed acceptance on July 21. But the purchase order imposed no obligation on the wholesaler because it reserved the right to cancel at any time before September 1. As a result, the wholesaler's promise to purchase shovels was illusory. Due to this lack of consideration, no contract was formed as of July 22. (Choice A) An option is a type of contract in which one party promises to hold a separate offer open for a specified time in return for consideration. Here, however, there was no promise to hold the offer open for any amount of time. And if an option contract had formed, it would not be terminable at the will of either party. (Choice D) Offers are revocable prior to acceptance (with a few exceptions), so the revocability of an offer does not prevent contract formation. But since the wholesaler reserved the right to cancel (ie, revoke) at any time before September 1, its illusory promise to purchase shovels prevented contract formation.","conclusion":"Answer C: No contract was formed, because of the wholesaler's reservation of the right to cancel."},"questionZh":"7月18日，一家铲子制造商接到批发商采购500台雪铲的订单。批发商于 7 月 15 日邮寄了采购订单。该订单要求在 9 月 15 日之前和 10 月 15 日之前发货铲子。订单正面显着的位置写着：“批发商保留在 9 月 1 日之前随时取消该订单的权利。”批发商于 7 月 21 日收到制造商邮寄的回复“我们接受您的订单”。截至 7 月 22 日，以下哪一项是关于合同是否成立的准确陈述？","choicesZh":{"A":"合同已形成，但在 9 月 1 日之前，它是一份期权合同，只能根据批发商的意愿终止。","B":"合同已签订，但在 9 月 1 日之前，合同可以根据任何一方的意愿终止。","C":"由于批发商保留取消权，因此合同并未成立。","D":"没有形成合同，因为批发商的订单只是一个可撤销的报价。"},"explanationZh":"阻碍合同成立的对价问题\n\n礼品 不可讨价还价交换\n\n名义约因 如果履行完全没有价值，则没有讨价还价的诱因\n\n虚假对价 如果不打算完成所叙述的履约，则不会造成法律损害，也不会诱使讨价还价 先前存在的义务/过去 如果已经欠履约或完成对价，则不会造成法律损害\n\n虚幻的承诺 如果明显的承诺不施加任何义务，则不会造成法律损害\n\n根据管理货物销售合同的《统一商法典》（UCC），合同的成立需要：双方同意——要约和接受以及有价值的对价——通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。合同一旦成立，就不能单方面终止（选择B）。但如果任何一方的承诺是虚幻的，因为它没有对该方施加任何义务，那么就没有对价，也没有合同。这里，批发商向铲子制造商发出了采购订单，并于7月21日收到了邮寄的验收单。但该采购订单并未对批发商施加任何义务，因为它保留在9月1日之前随时取消的权利。因此，批发商购买铲子的承诺是虚幻的。由于缺乏对价，截至 7 月 22 日尚未形成任何合同。（选择 A）期权是一种合同，其中一方承诺在指定时间内持有单独的要约以换取对价。然而，这里并没有承诺在任何时间内保留要约。而且，如果期权合约已经成立，则任何一方都不能随意终止该合约。 （选项 D） 要约在接受之前可以撤销（有少数例外），因此要约的可撤销并不妨碍合同的形成。但由于批发商保留在9月1日之前随时取消（即撤销）的权利，其购买铲子的虚假承诺阻碍了合同的成立。\n\n教育目标：虚幻的承诺是指不向承诺方施加任何义务，因此无法提供订立合同所需的对价。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 77（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果明显的承诺使履行完全是可选的，那么它就不是对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"虚假承诺是指不向承诺方施加任何义务，因此无法提供订立合同所需的对价。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"截至 7 月 22 日，下列哪一项关于合同是否成立的表述是准确的？","rule":"虚假承诺是指不向承诺方施加任何义务，因此无法提供订立合同所需的对价。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"阻碍合同成立的对价问题\n\n礼品 不可讨价还价交换\n\n名义约因 如果履行完全没有价值，则没有讨价还价的诱因\n\n虚假对价 如果不打算完成所叙述的履约，则不会造成法律损害，也不会诱使讨价还价 先前存在的义务/过去 如果已经欠履约或完成对价，则不会造成法律损害\n\n虚幻的承诺 如果明显的承诺不施加任何义务，则不会造成法律损害\n\n根据管理货物销售合同的《统一商法典》（UCC），合同的成立需要：双方同意——要约和接受以及有价值的对价——通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。合同一旦成立，就不能单方面终止（选择B）。但如果任何一方的承诺是虚幻的，因为它没有对该方施加任何义务，那么就没有对价，也没有合同。这里，批发商向铲子制造商发出了采购订单，并于7月21日收到了邮寄的验收单。但该采购订单并未对批发商施加任何义务，因为它保留在9月1日之前随时取消的权利。因此，批发商购买铲子的承诺是虚幻的。由于缺乏对价，截至 7 月 22 日尚未形成任何合同。（选择 A）期权是一种合同，其中一方承诺在指定时间内持有单独的要约以换取对价。然而，这里并没有承诺在任何时间内保留要约。而且，如果期权合约已经成立，则任何一方都不能随意终止该合约。 （选项 D） 要约在接受之前可以撤销（有少数例外），因此要约的可撤销并不妨碍合同的形成。但由于批发商保留在9月1日之前随时取消（即撤销）的权利，其购买铲子的虚假承诺阻碍了合同的成立。\n\n教育目标：虚幻的承诺是指不向承诺方施加任何义务，因此无法提供订立合同所需的对价。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 77（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果明显的承诺使履行完全是可选的，那么它就不是对价）。","conclusion":"答案 C：由于批发商保留取消权，因此合同并未成立。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":127,"sourceQuestionPage":375,"sourceQuestionPages":[375],"sourceExplanationPages":[376,377],"sourceAnswerPages":[376,377],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-128","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"On April 1, the owner of a house and a real estate investor signed a writing in which the owner, \"in consideration of $100 to be paid to the owner by the investor,\" offered the investor the right to purchase the house for $100,000 within 30 days. The writing further provided, \"This offer will become effective as an option only if and when the $100 consideration is in fact paid.\" On April 20, the owner, having received no payment or other communication from the investor, sold and conveyed the house to the investor's business rival for $120,000. On April 21, the owner received a letter from the investor enclosing a cashier's check for $100 payable to the owner and stating, \"I am hereby exercising my option to purchase the house and am prepared to close whenever you're ready.\" Which of the following, if proved, best supports the investor's suit against the owner for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"On April 15, the investor decided to purchase the house, and applied for and obtained a commitment from a bank for a $75,000 loan to help finance the purchase.","B":"The investor was unaware of the sale to his business rival when the owner received the letter and check from the investor on April 21.","C":"The owner and the investor are both professional dealers in real estate.","D":"When the April 1 writing was signed, the owner said to the investor, \"Don't worry about the $100; the recital of '$100 to be paid' makes this deal binding.\""},"answer":"B","explanation":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nAn option is a contract in which the offeree gives consideration (eg, money) in exchange for the offeror's promise to keep an outstanding offer open for a specified period of time. An offer to form an option is revocable prior to acceptance of the option. Revocation occurs when: the offeror directly communicates the revocation to the offeree or\n\nthe offeree learns information from a reliable source that reasonably indicates the offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer).\n\nHere, the owner offered the investor the option to purchase the house for $100,000 within 30 days. The investor had to pay $100 to accept this option, which the owner received on April 21.* Although the owner had already sold the house to someone else, he had not told the investor that the offer to form an option was revoked. So if the investor was unaware of\n\nthe other sale, there was no revocation prior to his acceptance and exercise of the option on April 21, and the owner breached the resulting sales contract. *Under the mailbox rule, acceptance of an offer is generally effective upon dispatch. However, one exception to the mailbox rule applies to option contracts, where acceptance is effective upon receipt. (Choice A) An offer may become irrevocable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel (also called detrimental reliance) if the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relied upon that offer. But here, it was unreasonable for the investor to obtain a loan before paying the $100 required to keep the offer open. (Choice C) Under Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code, a firm offer from a merchant is binding. But this only applies to contracts for the sale of goods, not real property. (Choice D) A binding option requires actual consideration—not sham consideration (eg, a recital of consideration that is not intended to be paid). Here, the investor's statement (\"Don't worry about the $100\") would show that the consideration was not intended to be paid.\n\nEducational objective: An offer to form an option contract is revocable before the option is accepted. Revocation occurs when (1) the offeror directly communicates the revocation to the offeree or (2) the offeree obtains reliable information that reasonably indicates the offer was revoked.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 42–43 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (direct and indirect communication of revocation).","educationalObjective":"An offer to form an option contract is revocable before the option is accepted. Revocation occurs when (1) the offeror directly communicates the revocation to the offeree or (2) the offeree obtains reliable information that reasonably indicates the offer was revoked.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 42–43 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (direct and indirect communication of revocation).","rule":"An offer to form an option contract is revocable before the option is accepted. Revocation occurs when (1) the offeror directly communicates the revocation to the offeree or (2) the offeree obtains reliable information that reasonably indicates the offer was revoked.","irac":{"issue":"On April 1, the owner of a house and a real estate investor signed a writing in which the owner, \"in consideration of $100 to be paid to the owner by the investor,\" offered the investor the right to purchase the house for $100,000 within 30 days. The writing further provided, \"This offer will become effective as an option only if and when the $100 consideration is in fact paid.\" On April 20, the owner, having received no payment or other communication from the investor, sold and conveyed the house to the investor's business rival for $120,000. On April 21, the owner received a letter from the investor enclosing a cashier's check for $100 payable to the owner and stating, \"I am hereby exercising my option to purchase the house and am prepared to close whenever you're ready.\" Which of the following, if proved, best supports the investor's suit against the owner for breach of contract?","rule":"An offer to form an option contract is revocable before the option is accepted. Revocation occurs when (1) the offeror directly communicates the revocation to the offeree or (2) the offeree obtains reliable information that reasonably indicates the offer was revoked.","analysis":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nAn option is a contract in which the offeree gives consideration (eg, money) in exchange for the offeror's promise to keep an outstanding offer open for a specified period of time. An offer to form an option is revocable prior to acceptance of the option. Revocation occurs when: the offeror directly communicates the revocation to the offeree or\n\nthe offeree learns information from a reliable source that reasonably indicates the offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer).\n\nHere, the owner offered the investor the option to purchase the house for $100,000 within 30 days. The investor had to pay $100 to accept this option, which the owner received on April 21.* Although the owner had already sold the house to someone else, he had not told the investor that the offer to form an option was revoked. So if the investor was unaware of\n\nthe other sale, there was no revocation prior to his acceptance and exercise of the option on April 21, and the owner breached the resulting sales contract. *Under the mailbox rule, acceptance of an offer is generally effective upon dispatch. However, one exception to the mailbox rule applies to option contracts, where acceptance is effective upon receipt. (Choice A) An offer may become irrevocable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel (also called detrimental reliance) if the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relied upon that offer. But here, it was unreasonable for the investor to obtain a loan before paying the $100 required to keep the offer open. (Choice C) Under Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code, a firm offer from a merchant is binding. But this only applies to contracts for the sale of goods, not real property. (Choice D) A binding option requires actual consideration—not sham consideration (eg, a recital of consideration that is not intended to be paid). Here, the investor's statement (\"Don't worry about the $100\") would show that the consideration was not intended to be paid.","conclusion":"Answer B: The investor was unaware of the sale to his business rival when the owner received the letter and check from the investor on April 21."},"questionZh":"4月1日，一栋房屋的业主和一名房地产投资者签署了一份书面文件，业主“以投资者向业主支付100美元为代价”，向投资者提供在30天内以10万美元购买该房屋的权利。该文进一步指出，“只有当 100 美元的对价确实支付时，此要约才会作为一种选择生效。” 4 月 20 日，业主在没有收到投资者的任何付款或其他信息的情况下，以 12 万美元的价格将房屋出售并转让给投资者的商业竞争对手。 4 月 21 日，业主收到投资者的一封信，其中附有一张应付给业主的 100 美元银行本票，上面写着：“我特此行使我的购买选择权，并准备在您准备好时关闭。”以下哪项如果得到证实，最能支持投资者对业主提起违约诉讼？","choicesZh":{"A":"4 月 15 日，投资者决定购买该房屋，并向银行申请并获得银行承诺提供 75,000 美元的贷款，以帮助购买该房屋。","B":"当业主于 4 月 21 日收到投资者的信件和支票时，投资者并不知道该交易已出售给其商业竞争对手。","C":"业主和投资者都是房地产专业经销商。","D":"当 4 月 1 日的书面文件签署时，业主对投资者说：“不用担心 100 美元；‘需支付 100 美元’的背诵使这笔交易具有约束力。”"},"explanationZh":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n期权是一种合同，其中受要约人给予对价（例如金钱），以换取要约人承诺在指定的时间内保持未完成的要约。形成期权的要约在接受期权之前可以撤销。撤销发生在： 要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销信息或\n\n受要约人从可靠来源获悉信息，合理地表明要约已被撤销（例如，房屋出售给另一位买家）。\n\n在这里，业主向投资者提供了在 30 天内以 10 万美元购买房屋的选择权。投资者必须支付 100 美元才能接受此选择权，业主于 4 月 21 日收到该选择权。*尽管业主已将房屋出售给其他人，但他没有告诉投资者形成选择权的要约已被撤销。所以如果投资者不知道\n\n另一次出售，在他于 4 月 21 日接受并行使选择权之前没有撤销，业主违反了由此产生的销售合同。 *根据邮箱规则，接受报价通常在发送后生效。然而，邮箱规则的一项例外情况适用于期权合约，接受即在收到后生效。 （选择 A）如果受要约人合理且有害地依赖该要约，则根据禁止反言原则（也称为有害信赖），该要约可能成为不可撤销的。但在这里，投资者在支付保持报价所需的 100 美元之前获得贷款是不合理的。 （选项C） 根据《统一商法典》（UCC）第2 条，商家的实盘具有约束力。但这仅适用于货物销售合同，不适用于不动产。 （选项D）具有约束力的选择需要实际考虑——而不是虚假考虑（例如，不打算支付的考虑的陈述）。在这里，投资者的声明（“不要担心 100 美元”）将表明不打算支付对价。\n\n教育目标：在期权被接受之前，形成期权合约的要约是可以撤销的。当（1）要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销信息或（2）受要约人获得合理表明要约被撤销的可靠信息时，就会发生撤销。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 42–43（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（直接和间接撤销通知）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在期权被接受之前，形成期权合约的要约可以撤销。当（1）要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销信息或（2）受要约人获得合理表明要约被撤销的可靠信息时，就会发生撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"4月1日，一栋房屋的业主和一名房地产投资者签署了一份书面文件，业主“以投资者向业主支付100美元为代价”，向投资者提供在30天内以10万美元购买该房屋的权利。该文进一步指出，“只有当 100 美元的对价确实支付时，此要约才会作为一种选择生效。” 4 月 20 日，业主在没有收到投资者的任何付款或其他信息的情况下，以 12 万美元的价格将房屋出售并转让给投资者的商业竞争对手。 4 月 21 日，业主收到投资者的一封信，其中附有一张应付给业主的 100 美元银行本票，上面写着：“我特此行使我的购买选择权，并准备在您准备好时关闭。”以下哪项如果得到证实，最能支持投资者对业主提起违约诉讼？","rule":"在期权被接受之前，形成期权合约的要约可以撤销。当（1）要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销信息或（2）受要约人获得合理表明要约被撤销的可靠信息时，就会发生撤销。","analysis":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n期权是一种合同，其中受要约人给予对价（例如金钱），以换取要约人承诺在指定的时间内保持未完成的要约。形成期权的要约在接受期权之前可以撤销。撤销发生在： 要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销信息或\n\n受要约人从可靠来源获悉信息，合理地表明要约已被撤销（例如，房屋出售给另一位买家）。\n\n在这里，业主向投资者提供了在 30 天内以 10 万美元购买房屋的选择权。投资者必须支付 100 美元才能接受此选择权，业主于 4 月 21 日收到该选择权。*尽管业主已将房屋出售给其他人，但他没有告诉投资者形成选择权的要约已被撤销。所以如果投资者不知道\n\n另一次出售，在他于 4 月 21 日接受并行使选择权之前没有撤销，业主违反了由此产生的销售合同。 *根据邮箱规则，接受报价通常在发送后生效。然而，邮箱规则的一项例外情况适用于期权合约，接受即在收到后生效。 （选择 A）如果受要约人合理且有害地依赖该要约，则根据禁止反言原则（也称为有害信赖），该要约可能成为不可撤销的。但在这里，投资者在支付保持报价所需的 100 美元之前获得贷款是不合理的。 （选项C） 根据《统一商法典》（UCC）第2 条，商家的实盘具有约束力。但这仅适用于货物销售合同，不适用于不动产。 （选项D）具有约束力的选择需要实际考虑——而不是虚假考虑（例如，不打算支付的考虑的陈述）。在这里，投资者的声明（“不要担心 100 美元”）将表明不打算支付对价。\n\n教育目标：在期权被接受之前，形成期权合约的要约是可以撤销的。当（1）要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销信息或（2）受要约人获得合理表明要约被撤销的可靠信息时，就会发生撤销。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 42–43（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（直接和间接撤销通知）。","conclusion":"答案 B：当业主于 4 月 21 日收到投资者的信件和支票时，投资者并不知道该交易已出售给其商业竞争对手。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"irrevocable offer","ipa":"/ˌɪɹˈɛvəkəbəl ˈɔfɝ/","syllables":"ir·rev·o·ca·ble of·fer","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不可撤销要约：在适用期间要约人无权撤回的要约","contextualMeaning":"不可撤销要约：在适用期间要约人无权撤回的要约","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Irrevocable offers Type Description Consideration Duration UCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time Common Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration Partial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contra","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"foresee","ipa":"/fɔɹsˈi/","syllables":"fore·see","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"vt. 预见, 预知","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“foresee”","wordForm":"词；词形 d:foreseen/p:foresaw/3:foresees/i:foreseeing","sourceSentence":"Irrevocable offers Type Description Consideration Duration UCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time Common Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration Partial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contra","synonyms":["anticipate","previse","foreknow","envision"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":128,"sourceQuestionPage":378,"sourceQuestionPages":[378],"sourceExplanationPages":[379,380],"sourceAnswerPages":[379,380],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-129","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A car dealership had an inventory of 200 cars near the end of the year. The inventory manager decided to reduce the price on these cars to make room for the next year's models. He purchased an advertisement in a local newspaper that stated: \"Sale! Our entire inventory of 200 cars will be liquidated immediately. Every car will be sold for no more than $20,000. First come, first served!\" The owner of the dealership decided that the sale was unnecessary, so he placed a poster in the front window that stated: \"Sale canceled. All cars priced as marked.\" Only a small minority of the inventory cars were marked with a price of $20,000 or less. A father read the newspaper advertisement and decided to purchase a car for his teenage son. The father drove to the dealership but did not see the poster cancelling the sale. When the father inquired about the advertisement, the dealership refused to honor the sale. The father bought a car in the dealership's inventory for $25,000. Several weeks later, the father regretted paying more than $20,000 for the car. If the father sues the dealership for breach of contract, how much is he likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the dealership revoked its general offer by placing the poster in the window.","B":"Nothing, because the dealership's advertisement was merely an invitation to negotiate.","C":"Nothing, because the father could have chosen one of the inventory cars marked with a price of $20,000 or less.","D":"$5,000, because the dealership's revocation was not publicized as heavily as its general offer."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate rather than offers to form contracts. However, an advertisement can be a general offer—ie, an offer made to a large number of people—if the advertisement: specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and places a reasonable limit on who may accept the offer. Here, the car dealership purchased a newspaper advertisement stating that 200 cars (quantity and subject matter) would be sold for no more than $20,000 each (price). Since the ad also placed a reasonable limit on who could accept (\"First come, first served\"), it was a general offer—not merely an invitation to negotiate (Choice B). A general offer can be revoked prior to acceptance if the revocation is publicized at least as heavily as the offer. If not, the attempted revocation is ineffective and the offer remains open except as to any offeree who actually learns of the revocation. Here, the general offer was never revoked since placing a poster in the dealership's window was less public than the newspaper ad (Choice A). And since the father did not see the poster cancelling the sale, he is likely to recover compensatory damages of $5,000 ($25,000 - $20,000). (Choice C) Although the father could have chosen one of the cars marked at $20,000 or less, the dealership offered to sell its entire inventory of 200 cars for no more than $20,000 each.\n\nEducational objective: An advertisement is a general offer, not a mere invitation to negotiate, if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who may accept the offer. A revocation of a general offer is only effective if it is publicized at least as heavily as the offer.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (revocation of general offer).","educationalObjective":"An advertisement is a general offer, not a mere invitation to negotiate, if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who may accept the offer. A revocation of a general offer is only effective if it is publicized at least as heavily as the offer.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 46 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (revocation of general offer).","rule":"An advertisement is a general offer, not a mere invitation to negotiate, if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who may accept the offer. A revocation of a general offer is only effective if it is publicized at least as heavily as the offer.","irac":{"issue":"If the father sues the dealership for breach of contract, how much is he likely to recover?","rule":"An advertisement is a general offer, not a mere invitation to negotiate, if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who may accept the offer. A revocation of a general offer is only effective if it is publicized at least as heavily as the offer.","analysis":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate rather than offers to form contracts. However, an advertisement can be a general offer—ie, an offer made to a large number of people—if the advertisement: specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and places a reasonable limit on who may accept the offer. Here, the car dealership purchased a newspaper advertisement stating that 200 cars (quantity and subject matter) would be sold for no more than $20,000 each (price). Since the ad also placed a reasonable limit on who could accept (\"First come, first served\"), it was a general offer—not merely an invitation to negotiate (Choice B). A general offer can be revoked prior to acceptance if the revocation is publicized at least as heavily as the offer. If not, the attempted revocation is ineffective and the offer remains open except as to any offeree who actually learns of the revocation. Here, the general offer was never revoked since placing a poster in the dealership's window was less public than the newspaper ad (Choice A). And since the father did not see the poster cancelling the sale, he is likely to recover compensatory damages of $5,000 ($25,000 - $20,000). (Choice C) Although the father could have chosen one of the cars marked at $20,000 or less, the dealership offered to sell its entire inventory of 200 cars for no more than $20,000 each.","conclusion":"Answer D: $5,000, because the dealership's revocation was not publicized as heavily as its general offer."},"questionZh":"临近年底，一家汽车经销商的库存为 200 辆汽车。库存经理决定降低这些汽车的价格，为明年的车型腾出空间。他在当地报纸上购买了一则广告，上面写着：“促销！我们所有库存的200辆车将立即清仓。每辆车的售价不超过20,000美元。先到先得！”经销店的老板认为没有必要进行销售，因此他在前窗贴了一张海报，上面写着：“销售取消。所有汽车均按标记定价。”只有一小部分库存汽车的标价为 20,000 美元或以下。一位父亲看到报纸广告后决定为他十几岁的儿子购买一辆车。父亲开车去了经销店，但没有看到取消销售的海报。当父亲询问广告时，经销商拒绝兑现销售。父亲以 25,000 美元在经销商库存中购买了一辆汽车。几周后，父亲后悔花了 2 万多美元买了这辆车。如果父亲起诉经销商违约，他可能获得多少赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"什么也没有，因为经销商将海报贴在橱窗上，从而撤销了一般报价。","B":"没什么，因为经销商的广告只是一个谈判邀请。","C":"没什么，因为父亲可以选择一辆标价为 20,000 美元或以下的库存汽车。","D":"5,000 美元，因为经销商的撤销并未像其全面要约那样广为人知。"},"explanationZh":"广告通常被视为谈判邀请，而不是签订合同的要约。然而，如果广告： 指定了主题、数量和价格，并对可以接受该要约的人设置了合理的限制，则该广告可以是一般要约，即向大量人群提出的要约。在此，汽车经销商购买了一份报纸广告，声明将出售 200 辆汽车（数量和主题），每辆汽车的售价不超过 20,000 美元（价格）。由于该广告还对谁可以接受提出了合理的限制（“先到先得”），因此这是一个普遍要约，而不仅仅是一个谈判邀请（选择 B）。如果撤销的公开程度至少与要约一样大，则一般要约可以在接受之前撤销。如果不是，则尝试撤销无效，并且要约保持开放状态，除非任何真正获悉撤销的受要约人。在这里，全面要约从未被撤销，因为在经销商的橱窗里张贴海报比报纸广告更不公开（选项 A）。由于父亲没有看到取消出售的海报，他可能会获得 5,000 美元（25,000 美元 - 20,000 美元）的赔偿金。 （选项 C） 虽然父亲可以选择其中一辆标价为 20,000 美元或以下的汽车，但经销商提出以每辆不超过 20,000 美元的价格出售 200 辆汽车的全部库存。\n\n教育目标：如果广告 (1) 明确了标的物、数量和价格，并且 (2) 对接受要约的人员设置了合理的限制，则该广告就是一般性要约，而不仅仅是谈判邀请。撤销全面要约只有在其公开程度至少与要约一样大的情况下才有效。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 46（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（撤销全面要约）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果广告 (1) 明确了标的物、数量和价格，并且 (2) 对接受要约的人员设置了合理的限制，那么该广告就是一般要约，而不仅仅是谈判邀请。撤销全面要约只有在其公开程度至少与要约一样大的情况下才有效。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果父亲起诉经销商违约，他可能获得多少赔偿？","rule":"如果广告 (1) 明确了标的物、数量和价格，并且 (2) 对接受要约的人员设置了合理的限制，那么该广告就是一般要约，而不仅仅是谈判邀请。撤销全面要约只有在其公开程度至少与要约一样大的情况下才有效。","analysis":"广告通常被视为谈判邀请，而不是签订合同的要约。然而，如果广告： 指定了主题、数量和价格，并对可以接受该要约的人设置了合理的限制，则该广告可以是一般要约，即向大量人群提出的要约。在此，汽车经销商购买了一份报纸广告，声明将出售 200 辆汽车（数量和主题），每辆汽车的售价不超过 20,000 美元（价格）。由于该广告还对谁可以接受提出了合理的限制（“先到先得”），因此这是一个普遍要约，而不仅仅是一个谈判邀请（选择 B）。如果撤销的公开程度至少与要约一样大，则一般要约可以在接受之前撤销。如果不是，则尝试撤销无效，并且要约保持开放状态，除非任何真正获悉撤销的受要约人。在这里，全面要约从未被撤销，因为在经销商的橱窗里张贴海报比报纸广告更不公开（选项 A）。由于父亲没有看到取消出售的海报，他可能会获得 5,000 美元（25,000 美元 - 20,000 美元）的赔偿金。 （选项 C） 虽然父亲可以选择其中一辆标价为 20,000 美元或以下的汽车，但经销商提出以每辆不超过 20,000 美元的价格出售 200 辆汽车的全部库存。\n\n教育目标：如果广告 (1) 明确了标的物、数量和价格，并且 (2) 对接受要约的人员设置了合理的限制，则该广告就是一般性要约，而不仅仅是谈判邀请。撤销全面要约只有在其公开程度至少与要约一样大的情况下才有效。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 46（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（撤销全面要约）。","conclusion":"答案 D：5,000 美元，因为经销商的撤销并未像其全面要约那样广为人知。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":129,"sourceQuestionPage":381,"sourceQuestionPages":[381],"sourceExplanationPages":[382],"sourceAnswerPages":[382],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-130","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Breach, Cure & Installments","question":"A shop owner faxed the following signed message to his long-time widget supplier: \"Urgently need blue widgets. Ship immediately three gross at your current list price of $600.\" Upon receipt of the fax, the supplier shipped three gross of red widgets to the shop owner and faxed to him the following message: \"Temporarily out of blue. In case red will help, am shipping three gross at the same price. Hope you can use them.\" Upon the shop owner's timely receipt of both the shipment and the supplier's fax, which of the following best describes the rights and duties of the shop owner and the supplier?","choices":{"A":"The shop owner may accept the shipment, in which case he must pay the supplier the list price, less any damages sustained because of the nonconforming shipment, or he may reject the shipment and recover from the supplier for total breach of contract, subject to the supplier's right to cure.","B":"The shop owner may accept the shipment, in which case he must pay the supplier the list price, less any damages sustained because of the nonconforming shipment, or he may reject the shipment provided that he promptly covers by obtaining conforming widgets from another supplier.","C":"The shop owner may accept the shipment, in which case he must pay the supplier the list price, or he may reject the shipment, in which case he has no further rights against the supplier.","D":"The shop owner may accept the shipment, in which case he must pay the supplier the list price, or he must reject the shipment and recover from the supplier for total breach of contract."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Seller's reply to buyer's offer\n\n(UCC § 2-206)\n\nReply Effect\n\nPromise to ship Acceptance\n\nShipment of conforming goods Acceptance & performance\n\nShipment of nonconforming With notice of Rejection & counteroffer goods accommodation\n\nWith no notice Acceptance & breach\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods. The UCC allows a seller to accept an offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship or actually shipping goods that conform to the offer. But a shipment of nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and a counteroffer if the seller: made no prior promise to ship conforming goods and\n\nnotifies the buyer that the nonconforming shipment is merely an accommodation—ie, an offer of similar goods for the buyer to consider.\n\nIn that case, the buyer may accept the shipment (creating a contract for the nonconforming goods) OR reject the shipment (creating no contract). Here, the shop owner offered to buy blue widgets for immediate shipment, but the supplier rejected that offer by shipping red widgets with a notice of accommodation (\"[i]n case red will help\"). Since the shop owner's offer was rejected—ie, no contract for blue widgets was formed—she did not sustain any damages (Choices A & B). However, the shop owner can still accept the shipment, thereby creating a contract for red widgets at the list price. Alternatively, the shop owner may reject the shipment. There would then be no contract for blue or red widgets, and the shop owner would have no further rights against the supplier. This also means that: the shop owner could not recover for breach of contract (Choice D)\n\nthe supplier would have no right to cure (ie, fix a breach by shipping conforming goods) (Choice A) and\n\nthe shop owner would not be required to cover (ie, purchase substitute goods to mitigate the damages after a breach) (Choice B).\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship or actually shipping conforming goods. However, shipping nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and counteroffer if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a shipment of nonconforming goods is not an acceptance if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship or actually shipping conforming goods. However, shipping nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and counteroffer if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that a shipment of nonconforming goods is not an acceptance if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation).","rule":"Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship or actually shipping conforming goods. However, shipping nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and counteroffer if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation.","irac":{"issue":"A shop owner faxed the following signed message to his long-time widget supplier: \"Urgently need blue widgets. Ship immediately three gross at your current list price of $600.\" Upon receipt of the fax, the supplier shipped three gross of red widgets to the shop owner and faxed to him the following message: \"Temporarily out of blue. In case red will help, am shipping three gross at the same price. Hope you can use them.\" Upon the shop owner's timely receipt of both the shipment and the supplier's fax, which of the following best describes the rights and duties of the shop owner and the supplier?","rule":"Under the UCC, a seller can accept an offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship or actually shipping conforming goods. However, shipping nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and counteroffer if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation.","analysis":"Seller's reply to buyer's offer\n\n(UCC § 2-206)\n\nReply Effect\n\nPromise to ship Acceptance\n\nShipment of conforming goods Acceptance & performance\n\nShipment of nonconforming With notice of Rejection & counteroffer goods accommodation\n\nWith no notice Acceptance & breach\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods. The UCC allows a seller to accept an offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship or actually shipping goods that conform to the offer. But a shipment of nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and a counteroffer if the seller: made no prior promise to ship conforming goods and\n\nnotifies the buyer that the nonconforming shipment is merely an accommodation—ie, an offer of similar goods for the buyer to consider.\n\nIn that case, the buyer may accept the shipment (creating a contract for the nonconforming goods) OR reject the shipment (creating no contract). Here, the shop owner offered to buy blue widgets for immediate shipment, but the supplier rejected that offer by shipping red widgets with a notice of accommodation (\"[i]n case red will help\"). Since the shop owner's offer was rejected—ie, no contract for blue widgets was formed—she did not sustain any damages (Choices A & B). However, the shop owner can still accept the shipment, thereby creating a contract for red widgets at the list price. Alternatively, the shop owner may reject the shipment. There would then be no contract for blue or red widgets, and the shop owner would have no further rights against the supplier. This also means that: the shop owner could not recover for breach of contract (Choice D)\n\nthe supplier would have no right to cure (ie, fix a breach by shipping conforming goods) (Choice A) and\n\nthe shop owner would not be required to cover (ie, purchase substitute goods to mitigate the damages after a breach) (Choice B).","conclusion":"Answer C: The shop owner may accept the shipment, in which case he must pay the supplier the list price, or he may reject the shipment, in which case he has no further rights against the supplier."},"questionZh":"一位店主将以下签名消息传真给他的长期小部件供应商：“迫切需要蓝色小部件。立即按照当前 600 美元的标价发货三毛。”供应商收到传真后，向店主运送了三毛红色小部件，并传真给他以下信息：“暂时没有蓝色。如果红色有帮助，我将以相同的价格运送三毛。希望您能使用它们。”在店主及时收到货物和供应商的传真后，以下哪项最能描述店主和供应商的权利和义务？","choicesZh":{"A":"店主可以接受货物，在这种情况下，他必须向供应商支付标价，减去因货物不合格而造成的任何损失，或者他可以拒绝货物，并以完全违约为由向供应商追偿，但供应商有补救权。","B":"店主可以接受货物，在这种情况下，他必须向供应商支付标价，减去因不合格货物而造成的任何损失，或者他可以拒绝货物，前提是他立即从其他供应商那里获得合格的小部件。","C":"店主可以接受货物，在这种情况下，他必须向供应商支付标价，或者他可以拒绝货物，在这种情况下，他对供应商没有进一步的权利。","D":"店主可以接受货物，在这种情况下，他必须向供应商支付标价，或者他必须拒绝货物并以完全违约为由向供应商追偿。"},"explanationZh":"卖方对买方报价的回复\n\n（UCC § 2-206）\n\n回复效果\n\n承诺发货验收\n\n合格货物的装运验收和履行\n\n不合格品的装运 附有拒绝通知和还价货物仅作为替代交付的通知\n\n恕不另行通知接受和违约\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖货物销售合同。 UCC 允许卖方通过承诺运送或实际运送符合要约的货物来接受立即或立即装运的货物购买要约。但是，如果卖方满足以下条件，则装运不合格货物既构成拒绝，也构成还盘： 没有事先承诺装运合格货物，并且\n\n通知买方，不合格货物仅仅是一种替代交付（accommodation），即提供类似货物供买方考虑。\n\n在这种情况下，买方可以接受发货（为不合格货物创建合同）或拒绝发货（不创建合同）。在这里，店主提出购买蓝色小部件并立即发货，但供应商拒绝了这一提议，并附上替代交付通知（accommodation notice）运输红色小部件（“如果红色会有所帮助”）。由于店主的报价被拒绝——即没有形成蓝色小部件的合同——她没有遭受任何损失（选择 A 和 B）。然而，店主仍然可以接受发货，从而以标价创建红色小部件的合同。或者，店主可以拒绝发货。这样就不会签订蓝色或红色小部件的合同，店主也不会再对供应商享有任何权利。这也意味着：店主无法因违约而获得赔偿（选项D）\n\n供应商无权补救（即通过运送合格货物来修复违规行为）（选择 A）并且\n\n店主无需承担赔偿（即购买替代商品以减轻违约后的损失）（选择 B）。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或实际运送符合要求的商品来接受购买商品并立即或立即发货的要约。然而，如果卖方通知买方该批货物只是一种替代交付（accommodation），则运送不合格货物既构成拒绝，也构成还盘。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释说，如果卖方通知买方该批货物只是一种替代交付（accommodation），则不合格货物的装运并不构成验收）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或实际运送符合要求的货物来接受购买货物并立即或立即装运的要约。然而，如果卖方通知买方该批货物只是一种替代交付（accommodation），则运送不合格货物既构成拒绝，也构成还盘。","iracZh":{"issue":"一位店主将以下签名消息传真给他的长期小部件供应商：“迫切需要蓝色小部件。立即按照当前 600 美元的标价发货三毛。”供应商收到传真后，向店主运送了三毛红色小部件，并传真给他以下信息：“暂时没有蓝色。如果红色有帮助，我将以相同的价格运送三毛。希望您能使用它们。”在店主及时收到货物和供应商的传真后，以下哪项最能描述店主和供应商的权利和义务？","rule":"根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或实际运送符合要求的货物来接受购买货物并立即或立即装运的要约。然而，如果卖方通知买方该批货物只是一种替代交付（accommodation），则运送不合格货物既构成拒绝，也构成还盘。","analysis":"卖方对买方报价的回复\n\n（UCC § 2-206）\n\n回复效果\n\n承诺发货验收\n\n合格货物的装运验收和履行\n\n不合格品的装运 附有拒绝通知和还价货物仅作为替代交付的通知\n\n恕不另行通知接受和违约\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖货物销售合同。 UCC 允许卖方通过承诺运送或实际运送符合要约的货物来接受立即或立即装运的货物购买要约。但是，如果卖方满足以下条件，则装运不合格货物既构成拒绝，也构成还盘： 没有事先承诺装运合格货物，并且\n\n通知买方，不合格货物仅仅是一种替代交付（accommodation），即提供类似货物供买方考虑。\n\n在这种情况下，买方可以接受发货（为不合格货物创建合同）或拒绝发货（不创建合同）。在这里，店主提出购买蓝色小部件并立即发货，但供应商拒绝了这一提议，并附上替代交付通知（accommodation notice）运输红色小部件（“如果红色会有所帮助”）。由于店主的报价被拒绝——即没有形成蓝色小部件的合同——她没有遭受任何损失（选择 A 和 B）。然而，店主仍然可以接受发货，从而以标价创建红色小部件的合同。或者，店主可以拒绝发货。这样就不会签订蓝色或红色小部件的合同，店主也不会再对供应商享有任何权利。这也意味着：店主无法因违约而获得赔偿（选项D）\n\n供应商无权补救（即通过运送合格货物来修复违规行为）（选择 A）并且\n\n店主无需承担赔偿（即购买替代商品以减轻违约后的损失）（选择 B）。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或实际运送符合要求的商品来接受购买商品并立即或立即发货的要约。然而，如果卖方通知买方该批货物只是一种替代交付（accommodation），则运送不合格货物既构成拒绝，也构成还盘。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释说，如果卖方通知买方该批货物只是一种替代交付（accommodation），则不合格货物的装运并不构成验收）。","conclusion":"答案 C：店主可以接受货物，在这种情况下，他必须向供应商支付标价，或者他可以拒绝货物，在这种情况下，他对供应商没有进一步的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"nonconforming goods","ipa":"/'nɔnkən'fɔ:miŋ ɡˈʊdz/","syllables":"non·con·form·ing goods","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"不合格货物：在任何方面不符合合同要求的货物","contextualMeaning":"不合格货物：在任何方面不符合合同要求的货物","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"However, shipping nonconforming goods serves as both a rejection and counteroffer if the seller notifies the buyer that the shipment was merely an accommodation.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"right to cure","ipa":"/ɹˈaɪt tˈu kjˈʊɹ/","syllables":"right to cure","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"补救权：卖方在法定条件下更换或修正不合格履行","contextualMeaning":"补救权：卖方在法定条件下更换或修正不合格履行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The shop owner may accept the shipment, in which case he must pay the supplier the list price, less any damages sustained because of the nonconforming shipment, or he may reject the shipment and recover from the supplier for total breach of contract, subject to the supplier's right to cure.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":130,"sourceQuestionPage":383,"sourceQuestionPages":[383],"sourceExplanationPages":[384,385],"sourceAnswerPages":[384,385],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-131","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A construction company contracted with a regional distributor to construct for $500,000 a warehouse and a driveway at highway level. Shortly after commencing work on the driveway, which required for the specified level some excavation and removal of surface material, the construction company unexpectedly encountered a large mass of solid rock. The construction company informed the distributor (accurately) that because of the rock, the driveway as specified would cost at least $20,000 more than figured and demanded for that reason a total contract price of $520,000. Since the distributor was expecting warehousing customers immediately after the agreed completion date, he signed a writing promising to pay the additional $20,000. Following timely completion of the warehouse and driveway, which conformed to the contract in all respects, the distributor refused to pay the construction company more than $500,000. What is the maximum amount to which the construction company is entitled?","choices":{"A":"$500,000, because the distributor's promise to pay the additional $20,000 was exacted under duress.","B":"$500,000, because there was no consideration for the distributor's promise to pay the additional $20,000.","C":"$520,000, because the modification was fair and was made in the light of circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the original contract was made.","D":"$520,000, provided that the reasonable value of the construction company's total performance was that much or more."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration to be enforceable. This requires both parties to alter their duties in some way. However, modifications are enforceable without new consideration if the modification: rests on circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the contract was made* and\n\nis fair and equitable in light of those circumstances.\n\nHere, the construction company and the distributor originally agreed on a contract price of $500,000. During excavations, the construction company unexpectedly encountered a large mass of solid rock. This unanticipated circumstance was going to increase construction costs by at least $20,000, so increasing the contract price by $20,000 was fair and equitable. Therefore, the modification was effective even though the construction company provided no new consideration, and the construction company is entitled to $520,000 (Choice B). *Circumstances prompting the modification must have been unanticipated by the parties, but they need not have been completely unforeseeable. (Choice A) A contract modification is voidable if it was induced by duress—ie, physical force or improper threat. But here, even though the distributor was expecting warehousing customers immediately after the agreed completion date, there is no indication that his promise to pay the $20,000 was exacted under duress.\n\n(Choice D) Contract law does not require the value of a party's performance to be equal to or greater than the contract price. Therefore, the reasonable value of the construction company's performance need not be $520,000 or more for the distributor to owe it.\n\nEducational objective: Under common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way. But new consideration is not required if the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that modification of a contract without new consideration is binding if it is fair and equitable in view of unanticipated circumstances).","educationalObjective":"Under common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way. But new consideration is not required if the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that modification of a contract without new consideration is binding if it is fair and equitable in view of unanticipated circumstances).","rule":"Under common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way. But new consideration is not required if the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances.","irac":{"issue":"What is the maximum amount to which the construction company is entitled?","rule":"Under common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way. But new consideration is not required if the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances.","analysis":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration to be enforceable. This requires both parties to alter their duties in some way. However, modifications are enforceable without new consideration if the modification: rests on circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the contract was made* and\n\nis fair and equitable in light of those circumstances.\n\nHere, the construction company and the distributor originally agreed on a contract price of $500,000. During excavations, the construction company unexpectedly encountered a large mass of solid rock. This unanticipated circumstance was going to increase construction costs by at least $20,000, so increasing the contract price by $20,000 was fair and equitable. Therefore, the modification was effective even though the construction company provided no new consideration, and the construction company is entitled to $520,000 (Choice B). *Circumstances prompting the modification must have been unanticipated by the parties, but they need not have been completely unforeseeable. (Choice A) A contract modification is voidable if it was induced by duress—ie, physical force or improper threat. But here, even though the distributor was expecting warehousing customers immediately after the agreed completion date, there is no indication that his promise to pay the $20,000 was exacted under duress.\n\n(Choice D) Contract law does not require the value of a party's performance to be equal to or greater than the contract price. Therefore, the reasonable value of the construction company's performance need not be $520,000 or more for the distributor to owe it.","conclusion":"Answer C: $520,000, because the modification was fair and was made in the light of circumstances not anticipated by the parties when the original contract was made."},"questionZh":"一家建筑公司与一家地区分销商签订合同，斥资 50 万美元建造一座仓库和一条高速公路车道。在车道上开始施工后不久，施工公司意外地遇到了大块坚硬的岩石，这需要对指定水平进行一些挖掘和清除表面材料。建筑公司（准确地）告知经销商，由于岩石的原因，规定的车道成本至少比预计高出 20,000 美元，因此要求合同总价为 520,000 美元。由于分销商预计在约定的完成日期后立即收到仓储客户，因此他签署了一份书面承诺支付额外的 20,000 美元。仓库和车道按时竣工，各方面均符合合同规定，经销商拒绝向建筑公司支付超过 50 万美元。建筑公司有权获得的最高金额是多少？","choicesZh":{"A":"50万美元，因为经销商在胁迫下承诺支付额外的2万美元。","B":"500,000 美元，因为没有考虑经销商承诺支付额外 20,000 美元的费用。","C":"520,000美元，因为修改是公平的，并且是根据双方在签订原始合同时未预见到的情况而进行的。","D":"520,000 美元，前提是建筑公司总业绩的合理价值是这么多或更多。"},"explanationZh":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同修改通常必须得到新约因的支持才能执行。这需要双方以某种方式改变他们的职责。但是，如果修改满足以下条件，则修改无需重新约因即可强制执行： 取决于双方签订合同时未预见到的情况*并且\n\n鉴于这些情况，这是公平和公正的。\n\n这里，建筑公司和经销商最初商定的合同价格为50万美元。在挖掘过程中，建筑公司意外地遇到了一大块坚硬的岩石。这种意外情况将使建筑成本增加至少20,000美元，因此合同价格增加20,000美元是公平合理的。因此，即使建筑公司没有提供新的对价，修改仍然有效，建筑公司有权获得 520,000 美元（选择 B）。 *促使修改的情况必须是当事人未预料到的，但不一定是完全不可预见的。 （选择 A）如果合同修改是由胁迫（即武力或不当威胁）引起的，则该合同修改是无效的。但在这里，尽管经销商预计在约定的完成日期后立即收到仓储客户，但没有迹象表明他支付 20,000 美元的承诺是在胁迫下兑现的。\n\n（选项D） 合同法并不要求一方当事人的履约价值等于或大于合同价格。因此，建筑公司业绩的合理价值不必是经销商所欠的 520,000 美元或更多。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法，合同修改通常必须有新约因的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其职责。但如果考虑到意外情况，修改是公平公正的，则无需重新约因。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 89（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果考虑到意外情况，合同的修改是公平公正的，则无需新约因的合同修改具有约束力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据普通法，合同修改通常必须有新约因的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其义务。但如果考虑到意外情况，修改是公平公正的，则无需重新约因。","iracZh":{"issue":"建筑公司有权获得的最高金额是多少？","rule":"根据普通法，合同修改通常必须有新约因的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其义务。但如果考虑到意外情况，修改是公平公正的，则无需重新约因。","analysis":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同修改通常必须得到新约因的支持才能执行。这需要双方以某种方式改变他们的职责。但是，如果修改满足以下条件，则修改无需重新约因即可强制执行： 取决于双方签订合同时未预见到的情况*并且\n\n鉴于这些情况，这是公平和公正的。\n\n这里，建筑公司和经销商最初商定的合同价格为50万美元。在挖掘过程中，建筑公司意外地遇到了一大块坚硬的岩石。这种意外情况将使建筑成本增加至少20,000美元，因此合同价格增加20,000美元是公平合理的。因此，即使建筑公司没有提供新的对价，修改仍然有效，建筑公司有权获得 520,000 美元（选择 B）。 *促使修改的情况必须是当事人未预料到的，但不一定是完全不可预见的。 （选择 A）如果合同修改是由胁迫（即武力或不当威胁）引起的，则该合同修改是无效的。但在这里，尽管经销商预计在约定的完成日期后立即收到仓储客户，但没有迹象表明他支付 20,000 美元的承诺是在胁迫下兑现的。\n\n（选项D） 合同法并不要求一方当事人的履约价值等于或大于合同价格。因此，建筑公司业绩的合理价值不必是经销商所欠的 520,000 美元或更多。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法，合同修改通常必须有新约因的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其职责。但如果考虑到意外情况，修改是公平公正的，则无需重新约因。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 89（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果考虑到意外情况，合同的修改是公平公正的，则无需新约因的合同修改具有约束力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：520,000美元，因为修改是公平的，并且是根据双方在签订原始合同时未预见到的情况而进行的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":131,"sourceQuestionPage":386,"sourceQuestionPages":[386],"sourceExplanationPages":[387,388],"sourceAnswerPages":[387,388],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-132","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A plumber went to a home to inspect a broken water heater. After the plumber had finished the inspection, he told the homeowner, \"I have my tools and the replacement part with me. I can repair your water heater now for $600.\" The homeowner then asked, \"Would you consider taking less?\" The plumber replied, \"No, $600 is the lowest price I can offer.\" The homeowner was undecided, so the plumber left. The next day, the homeowner called the plumber and said, \"I accept your offer at $600.\" The plumber replied, \"I used the part on another job yesterday afternoon. And I just found out today that my parts supplier increased its prices, so I cannot do the job for less than $700.\" The homeowner refused to pay $700 and demanded that the plumber repair the water heater for $600. Is there an enforceable contract between the homeowner and the plumber?","choices":{"A":"No, because the homeowner failed to accept the plumber's offer to repair the water heater for $600 before their conversation ended.","B":"No, because the homeowner rejected the plumber's offer by asking if he would take less than $600.","C":"Yes, because the homeowner accepted the plumber's offer to repair the water heater for $600 before it lapsed.","D":"Yes, because the plumber was a merchant with respect to water heaters, so his quote for $600 was a firm offer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Contract formation begins with an offer, which must be accepted before it terminates. An offer will terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to timely respond to the offer. The time frame to accept the offer is either: the time stated in the offer or if no time is stated, a reasonable time after the offer is received. In determining what is reasonable, the question is what a reasonable person in the offeree's position would think is acceptable to the offeror. For example, the offeror in face-to-face or telephone negotiations typically expects an immediate response, so the time for acceptance does not extend beyond the end of the conversation. Here, the plumber offered to \"repair [the homeowner's] water heater now for $600.\" No time for acceptance was specified, so the homeowner had a reasonable amount of time to accept. Since this was a face-to-face negotiation, the reasonable time for acceptance passed—and the plumber's offer for $600 lapsed—when the conversation ended (Choice C). Therefore, the homeowner's next-day attempt to accept was ineffective, and there is no contract. (Choice B) An offer will terminate if the offeree rejects it by clearly stating that he/she does not intend to accept or by making a counteroffer. However, a mere suggestion or inquiry (eg, \"Would you consider taking less?\") is not a rejection or counteroffer. (Choice D) Under the UCC's firm-offer rule, a merchant's written and signed offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for a stated or a reasonable time. But this rule does not apply to contracts for services such as repairing water heaters, so the plumber's merchant status is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: An offer will lapse if it is not accepted within the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, within a reasonable time—eg, before the conversation ends in a face-to-face negotiation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 41 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (reasonable time for acceptance in face-to-face or telephone negotiations).","educationalObjective":"An offer will lapse if it is not accepted within the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, within a reasonable time—eg, before the conversation ends in a face-to-face negotiation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 41 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (reasonable time for acceptance in face-to-face or telephone negotiations).","rule":"An offer will lapse if it is not accepted within the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, within a reasonable time—eg, before the conversation ends in a face-to-face negotiation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"Is there an enforceable contract between the homeowner and the plumber?","rule":"An offer will lapse if it is not accepted within the time stated in the offer or, if no time is stated, within a reasonable time—eg, before the conversation ends in a face-to-face negotiation. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"Contract formation begins with an offer, which must be accepted before it terminates. An offer will terminate by lapse if the offeree fails to timely respond to the offer. The time frame to accept the offer is either: the time stated in the offer or if no time is stated, a reasonable time after the offer is received. In determining what is reasonable, the question is what a reasonable person in the offeree's position would think is acceptable to the offeror. For example, the offeror in face-to-face or telephone negotiations typically expects an immediate response, so the time for acceptance does not extend beyond the end of the conversation. Here, the plumber offered to \"repair [the homeowner's] water heater now for $600.\" No time for acceptance was specified, so the homeowner had a reasonable amount of time to accept. Since this was a face-to-face negotiation, the reasonable time for acceptance passed—and the plumber's offer for $600 lapsed—when the conversation ended (Choice C). Therefore, the homeowner's next-day attempt to accept was ineffective, and there is no contract. (Choice B) An offer will terminate if the offeree rejects it by clearly stating that he/she does not intend to accept or by making a counteroffer. However, a mere suggestion or inquiry (eg, \"Would you consider taking less?\") is not a rejection or counteroffer. (Choice D) Under the UCC's firm-offer rule, a merchant's written and signed offer to buy or sell goods is irrevocable for a stated or a reasonable time. But this rule does not apply to contracts for services such as repairing water heaters, so the plumber's merchant status is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the homeowner failed to accept the plumber's offer to repair the water heater for $600 before their conversation ended."},"questionZh":"一名水管工前往一户人家检查损坏的热水器。水管工检查完毕后，对房主说：“我带着工具和替换零件。我现在可以修理你的热水器，价格为 600 美元。”房主又问：“你能考虑少拿一些吗？”水管工回答说：“不，600 美元是我能提供的最低价格。”房主拿不定主意，水管工就离开了。第二天，房主打电话给水管工说：“我接受你 600 美元的报价。”水管工回答说：“昨天下午我在另一项工作中使用了这个零件。今天我才发现我的零件供应商提高了价格，所以我不能以低于 700 美元的价格完成这项工作。”房主拒绝支付700美元，并要求水管工修理热水器600美元。房主和水管工之间是否有可执行的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为在谈话结束之前，房主没有接受水管工以 600 美元修理热水器的提议。","B":"不，因为房主拒绝了水管工的提议，询问他是否愿意接受低于 600 美元的费用。","C":"是的，因为在热水器失效之前，房主接受了水管工以 600 美元修理热水器的提议。","D":"是的，因为水管工是热水器商人，所以他的600美元报价是实盘。"},"explanationZh":"合同的成立始于要约，在合同终止之前必须接受要约。如果受要约人未能及时回应要约，要约将失效。接受要约的时间范围是：要约中规定的时间，或者如果没有规定时间，则为收到要约后的合理时间。在确定什么是合理时，问题是处于受要约人地位的合理人会认为什么是要约人可以接受的。例如，在面对面或电话谈判中，要约人通常期望立即得到答复，因此接受的时间不会超过对话结束。在这里，水管工提出“现在修理（房主的）热水器，价格为 600 美元”。没有指定验收时间，因此房主有合理的时间来验收。由于这是一次面对面的谈判，当谈话结束时，合理的接受时间已经过去，水管工提出的 600 美元的报价也失效了（选项 C）。因此，房主次日试图验收无效，合同不成立。 （选择B）如果受要约人明确表示不打算接受或提出还盘而拒绝要约，则要约终止。然而，单纯的建议或询问（例如，“您会考虑少拿一些吗？”）并不构成拒绝或还价。 （选项 D） 根据 UCC 的确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家书面并签署的购买或出售商品的要约在规定的或合理的时间内不可撤销。但这条规则不适用于维修热水器等服务合同，因此水管工的商人身份无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：如果在要约中规定的时间内未接受要约，或者如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（例如，在面对面谈判结束之前）未接受要约，则要约将失效。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 41 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（面对面或电话谈判中接受的合理时间）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果在要约中规定的时间内未接受要约，或者如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（例如，在面对面谈判结束之前）未接受要约，则要约将失效。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"房主和水管工之间是否有可执行的合同？","rule":"如果在要约中规定的时间内未接受要约，或者如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（例如，在面对面谈判结束之前）未接受要约，则要约将失效。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"合同的成立始于要约，在合同终止之前必须接受要约。如果受要约人未能及时回应要约，要约将失效。接受要约的时间范围是：要约中规定的时间，或者如果没有规定时间，则为收到要约后的合理时间。在确定什么是合理时，问题是处于受要约人地位的合理人会认为什么是要约人可以接受的。例如，在面对面或电话谈判中，要约人通常期望立即得到答复，因此接受的时间不会超过对话结束。在这里，水管工提出“现在修理（房主的）热水器，价格为 600 美元”。没有指定验收时间，因此房主有合理的时间来验收。由于这是一次面对面的谈判，当谈话结束时，合理的接受时间已经过去，水管工提出的 600 美元的报价也失效了（选项 C）。因此，房主次日试图验收无效，合同不成立。 （选择B）如果受要约人明确表示不打算接受或提出还盘而拒绝要约，则要约终止。然而，单纯的建议或询问（例如，“您会考虑少拿一些吗？”）并不构成拒绝或还价。 （选项 D） 根据 UCC 的确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家书面并签署的购买或出售商品的要约在规定的或合理的时间内不可撤销。但这条规则不适用于维修热水器等服务合同，因此水管工的商人身份无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：如果在要约中规定的时间内未接受要约，或者如果没有规定时间，则在合理的时间内（例如，在面对面谈判结束之前）未接受要约，则要约将失效。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 41 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（面对面或电话谈判中接受的合理时间）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为在谈话结束之前，房主没有接受水管工以 600 美元修理热水器的提议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"acceptable","ipa":"/æksˈɛptəbəl/","syllables":"ac·cept·able","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"a. 可接受的, 合意的, 可忍受的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“acceptable”","wordForm":"形容词","sourceSentence":"In determining what is reasonable, the question is what a reasonable person in the offeree's position would think is acceptable to the offeror.","synonyms":["satisfactory","decent"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-c7034f3dcd1d83d6"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":132,"sourceQuestionPage":389,"sourceQuestionPages":[389],"sourceExplanationPages":[390,391],"sourceAnswerPages":[390,391],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-133","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A landowner and a contractor entered into a written contract under which the contractor agreed to build a building and pave an adjacent sidewalk for the landowner for $200,000. Later, while construction was proceeding, the landowner and the contractor entered into an oral modification under which the contractor was not obligated to pave the sidewalk but still would be entitled to $200,000 upon completion of the building. The contractor completed the building. The landowner, after discussions with his landscaper, demanded that the contractor pave the adjacent sidewalk. The contractor refused. Has the contractor breached the contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the oral modification was in good faith and therefore enforceable.","B":"Yes, because a discharge of a contractual obligation must be in writing.","C":"Yes, because the parol evidence rule bars proof of the oral modification.","D":"Yes, because there was no consideration for the discharge of the contractor's duty to pave the sidewalk."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for services are governed by the common law. Under the common law, contract modifications must be supported by new consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance—to be enforceable. This requirement is only met if both parties alter their duties in some way. As a result, the promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is insufficient for new consideration and will not discharge the parties' duties under the initial (ie, unmodified) contract. Here, the contractor agreed to build a building and pave a sidewalk for the landowner for $200,000. The parties later agreed that the contractor did not have to pave the sidewalk. However, there was no consideration for this modification because the landowner's duty to pay the full $200,000 contract price remained the same. This means that the modification is not binding and did not discharge the contractor's duty to pave. As a result, the contractor breached the contract by refusing to pave the sidewalk. (Choice A) Under the UCC, contract modifications need only be made in good faith to be enforceable—new consideration is not required. But since the UCC only applies to contracts for the sale of goods, the common law applies here. (Choice B) There is no rule requiring the discharge of a contractual obligation to be in writing. (Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, the oral modification that occurred after the contract was entered).\n\nEducational objective: Under the common law, contract modifications are only binding if they are supported by new consideration. This requires both parties to alter their duties in some way.","educationalObjective":"Under the common law, contract modifications are only binding if they are supported by new consideration. This requires both parties to alter their duties in some way.","references":"","rule":"Under the common law, contract modifications are only binding if they are supported by new consideration. This requires both parties to alter their duties in some way.","irac":{"issue":"Has the contractor breached the contract?","rule":"Under the common law, contract modifications are only binding if they are supported by new consideration. This requires both parties to alter their duties in some way.","analysis":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for services are governed by the common law. Under the common law, contract modifications must be supported by new consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance—to be enforceable. This requirement is only met if both parties alter their duties in some way. As a result, the promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is insufficient for new consideration and will not discharge the parties' duties under the initial (ie, unmodified) contract. Here, the contractor agreed to build a building and pave a sidewalk for the landowner for $200,000. The parties later agreed that the contractor did not have to pave the sidewalk. However, there was no consideration for this modification because the landowner's duty to pay the full $200,000 contract price remained the same. This means that the modification is not binding and did not discharge the contractor's duty to pave. As a result, the contractor breached the contract by refusing to pave the sidewalk. (Choice A) Under the UCC, contract modifications need only be made in good faith to be enforceable—new consideration is not required. But since the UCC only applies to contracts for the sale of goods, the common law applies here. (Choice B) There is no rule requiring the discharge of a contractual obligation to be in writing. (Choice C) The parol evidence rule generally bars evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the terms of a final written agreement. But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, the oral modification that occurred after the contract was entered).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because there was no consideration for the discharge of the contractor's duty to pave the sidewalk."},"questionZh":"一名土地所有者和一名承包商签订了一份书面合同，承包商同意以 20 万美元的价格为土地所有者建造一座建筑物并铺设相邻的人行道。后来，在施工过程中，土地所有者和承包商进行了口头修改，根据该修改，承包商没有义务铺设人行道，但在建筑竣工后仍有权获得20万美元。承包商完成了大楼的建设。土地所有者与景观设计师讨论后，要求承包商铺设相邻的人行道。承包商拒绝了。承包商是否违反合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为口头修改是善意的，因此是可执行的。","B":"是的，因为合同义务的解除必须以书面形式进行。","C":"是的，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）禁止口头修改的证据。","D":"是的，因为没有考虑承包商铺设人行道的义务。"},"explanationZh":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n服务合同受普通法管辖。根据普通法，合同修改必须有新的对价的支持——即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行——才能强制执行。仅当双方以某种方式改变其职责时才能满足此要求。因此，履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不足以作为新约因，并且不会解除双方在初始（即未经修改的）合同下的义务。在这里，承包商同意以 20 万美元的价格为土地所有者建造一座建筑物并铺设一条人行道。双方后来同意承包商不必铺设人行道。然而，没有考虑这一修改，因为土地所有者支付全部 200,000 美元合同价格的义务保持不变。这意味着修改不具有约束力，并且并未解除承包商的铺路义务。结果，承包商拒绝铺设人行道，违反了合同。 （选择 A）根据 UCC，合同修改只需善意地进行即可强制执行，不需要新的对价。但由于 UCC 仅适用于货物销售合同，因此此处适用普通法。 （选择 B） 没有任何规则要求以书面形式履行合同义务。 （选择 C） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前口头或书面协议的证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款。但该规则并不排除当事人之间后续协议的证据（例如，合同签订后发生的口头修改）。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法，合同修改只有得到新考虑的支持才具有约束力。这需要双方以某种方式改变他们的职责。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据普通法，合同修改只有得到新对价的支持才具有约束力。这需要双方以某种方式改变他们的职责。","iracZh":{"issue":"承包商是否违反合同？","rule":"根据普通法，合同修改只有得到新对价的支持才具有约束力。这需要双方以某种方式改变他们的职责。","analysis":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n服务合同受普通法管辖。根据普通法，合同修改必须有新的对价的支持——即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行——才能强制执行。仅当双方以某种方式改变其职责时才能满足此要求。因此，履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不足以作为新约因，并且不会解除双方在初始（即未经修改的）合同下的义务。在这里，承包商同意以 20 万美元的价格为土地所有者建造一座建筑物并铺设一条人行道。双方后来同意承包商不必铺设人行道。然而，没有考虑这一修改，因为土地所有者支付全部 200,000 美元合同价格的义务保持不变。这意味着修改不具有约束力，并且并未解除承包商的铺路义务。结果，承包商拒绝铺设人行道，违反了合同。 （选择 A）根据 UCC，合同修改只需善意地进行即可强制执行，不需要新的对价。但由于 UCC 仅适用于货物销售合同，因此此处适用普通法。 （选择 B） 没有任何规则要求以书面形式履行合同义务。 （选择 C） 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常禁止提供先前口头或书面协议的证据来修改或反驳最终书面协议的条款。但该规则并不排除当事人之间后续协议的证据（例如，合同签订后发生的口头修改）。\n\n教育目标：根据普通法，合同修改只有得到新考虑的支持才具有约束力。这需要双方以某种方式改变他们的职责。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为没有考虑承包商铺设人行道的义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":133,"sourceQuestionPage":392,"sourceQuestionPages":[392],"sourceExplanationPages":[393,394],"sourceAnswerPages":[393,394],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-134","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On December 15, a doctor received from a retailer of office supplies an offer consisting of its catalog and a signed letter stating, \"We will supply you with as many of the items in the enclosed catalog as you order during the next calendar year. We assure you that this offer and the prices in the catalog will remain firm throughout the coming year.\" On January 15, having at that time received no reply from the doctor, the retailer notified the doctor that effective February 1, it was increasing the prices of certain specified items in its catalog. Is the price increase effective with respect to catalog orders the retailer receives from the doctor during the month of February?","choices":{"A":"No, because the retailer is a merchant with respect to office supplies; and its original offer, including the price term, was irrevocable throughout the month of February.","B":"No, because the retailer's original offer, including the price term, became irrevocable under the doctrine of promissory estoppel.","C":"Yes, because the period for which the retailer gave assurance that it would not raise prices was longer than three months.","D":"Yes, because the retailer received no consideration to support its assurance that it would not increase prices."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the UCC firm-offer rule, merchants are defined as: persons who regularly deal in goods of the kind involved in the contract\n\npersons who, by their occupation, hold themselves out as having knowledge or skills peculiar to the practices or goods involved or\n\nany businessperson when the transaction is of a commercial nature.\n\nA merchant's firm offer is irrevocable—and cannot be modified—for the time stated in the offer or a reasonable time if none is stated. But in no case will a firm offer remain irrevocable for more than three months. Here, on December 15, the doctor received a catalog and a signed letter from the retailer offering to supply office supplies at the catalog prices and assuring that the offer would remain open for one year. The retailer is a merchant who regularly deals in office supplies, so its offer was firm. And although the specified time period exceeds the three-month limit, the retailer's offer is still irrevocable for three months—until March 15 (Choice C). Therefore, the retailer's attempt to increase prices was not effective with respect to February orders. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an offer becomes irrevocable if (1) the offeror could reasonably foresee that the offeree would rely on the offer and (2) the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relied on that offer. But here, there is no indication that the doctor relied on the retailer's offer or suffered any detriment. (Choice D) A firm offer is irrevocable even if there is no consideration for the assurance that the offer will be held open. Therefore, the lack of consideration does not make the retailer's price increase effective.\n\nEducational objective: A merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will be held open (ie, firm offer) can never exceed three months—even if the offer states that it will be held open for a longer period. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-205 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (firm offers).","educationalObjective":"A merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will be held open (ie, firm offer) can never exceed three months—even if the offer states that it will be held open for a longer period. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-205 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (firm offers).","rule":"A merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will be held open (ie, firm offer) can never exceed three months—even if the offer states that it will be held open for a longer period. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Is the price increase effective with respect to catalog orders the retailer receives from the doctor during the month of February?","rule":"A merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will be held open (ie, firm offer) can never exceed three months—even if the offer states that it will be held open for a longer period. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Under the UCC firm-offer rule, merchants are defined as: persons who regularly deal in goods of the kind involved in the contract\n\npersons who, by their occupation, hold themselves out as having knowledge or skills peculiar to the practices or goods involved or\n\nany businessperson when the transaction is of a commercial nature.\n\nA merchant's firm offer is irrevocable—and cannot be modified—for the time stated in the offer or a reasonable time if none is stated. But in no case will a firm offer remain irrevocable for more than three months. Here, on December 15, the doctor received a catalog and a signed letter from the retailer offering to supply office supplies at the catalog prices and assuring that the offer would remain open for one year. The retailer is a merchant who regularly deals in office supplies, so its offer was firm. And although the specified time period exceeds the three-month limit, the retailer's offer is still irrevocable for three months—until March 15 (Choice C). Therefore, the retailer's attempt to increase prices was not effective with respect to February orders. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an offer becomes irrevocable if (1) the offeror could reasonably foresee that the offeree would rely on the offer and (2) the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relied on that offer. But here, there is no indication that the doctor relied on the retailer's offer or suffered any detriment. (Choice D) A firm offer is irrevocable even if there is no consideration for the assurance that the offer will be held open. Therefore, the lack of consideration does not make the retailer's price increase effective.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the retailer is a merchant with respect to office supplies; and its original offer, including the price term, was irrevocable throughout the month of February."},"questionZh":"12 月 15 日，一名医生收到一家办公用品零售商的报价，其中包括其目录和一封签名信，信中写道：“我们将在下一个日历年向您提供所附目录中您订购的所有物品。我们向您保证，这一报价和目录中的价格将在未来一年保持不变。” 1 月 15 日，由于当时没有收到医生的回复，零售商通知医生，自 2 月 1 日起，其目录中某些特定商品的价格将提高。对于零售商在 2 月份从医生处收到的目录订单，提价是否有效？","choicesZh":{"A":"不是，因为零售商是办公用品商人；其最初的报价，包括价格条款，在整个二月份都是不可撤销的。","B":"不可以，因为根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，零售商的原始报价（包括价格条款）变得不可撤销。","C":"是的，因为零售商保证不涨价的期限超过三个月。","D":"是的，因为零售商没有收到任何报酬来支持其不会提高价格的保证。"},"explanationZh":"根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商人被定义为： 经常交易合同中涉及的商品的人\n\n因其职业而自称拥有所涉及的做法或商品所特有的知识或技能的人，或\n\n当交易具有商业性质时，任何商人。\n\n商家的确定报价在报价中规定的时间内或合理的时间内（如果没有规定）是不可撤销的，并且不能修改。但在任何情况下，实盘报价都不会在超过三个月内保持不可撤销。 12 月 15 日，医生收到了一份目录和一封来自零售商的签名信，信中承诺以目录价格供应办公用品，并保证该报价将持续一年。该零售商是一家经常经营办公用品的商人，因此其报价很坚挺。尽管指定的时间段超过了三个月的限制，但零售商的报价在三个月内仍然不可撤销——直到3月15日（选项C）。因此，零售商的涨价尝试对于2月份的订单并没有起到作用。 （选择B）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果（1）要约人能够合理地预见受要约人将依赖该要约，并且（2）受要约人合理且有害地依赖该要约，则要约就不可撤销。但在这里，没有迹象表明医生依赖了零售商的报价或遭受了任何损害。 （选择D）即使没有考虑要约将被保留，确认要约也是不可撤销的。因此，缺乏约因并不能让零售商的涨价发挥作用。\n\n教育目标：商家书面并签署的保证报价将保持开放（即确定报价）的时间不得超过三个月，即使报价声明其将保持更长时间。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-205（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（实盘）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"商家书面并签署的保证报价将保持开放（即确定报价）的期限不得超过三个月，即使报价声明其将开放更长时间。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"对于零售商在 2 月份从医生处收到的目录订单，提价是否有效？","rule":"商家书面并签署的保证报价将保持开放（即确定报价）的期限不得超过三个月，即使报价声明其将开放更长时间。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商人被定义为： 经常交易合同中涉及的商品的人\n\n因其职业而自称拥有所涉及的做法或商品所特有的知识或技能的人，或\n\n当交易具有商业性质时，任何商人。\n\n商家的确定报价在报价中规定的时间内或合理的时间内（如果没有规定）是不可撤销的，并且不能修改。但在任何情况下，实盘报价都不会在超过三个月内保持不可撤销。 12 月 15 日，医生收到了一份目录和一封来自零售商的签名信，信中承诺以目录价格供应办公用品，并保证该报价将持续一年。该零售商是一家经常经营办公用品的商人，因此其报价很坚挺。尽管指定的时间段超过了三个月的限制，但零售商的报价在三个月内仍然不可撤销——直到3月15日（选项C）。因此，零售商的涨价尝试对于2月份的订单并没有起到作用。 （选择B）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果（1）要约人能够合理地预见受要约人将依赖该要约，并且（2）受要约人合理且有害地依赖该要约，则要约就不可撤销。但在这里，没有迹象表明医生依赖了零售商的报价或遭受了任何损害。 （选择D）即使没有考虑要约将被保留，确认要约也是不可撤销的。因此，缺乏约因并不能让零售商的涨价发挥作用。\n\n教育目标：商家书面并签署的保证报价将保持开放（即确定报价）的时间不得超过三个月，即使报价声明其将保持更长时间。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-205（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（实盘）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不是，因为零售商是办公用品商人；其最初的报价，包括价格条款，在整个二月份都是不可撤销的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-08c896f2e576768e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":134,"sourceQuestionPage":395,"sourceQuestionPages":[395],"sourceExplanationPages":[396,397],"sourceAnswerPages":[396,397],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-135","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A buyer sent a seller an offer to buy 50 tons of cotton of a specified quality. The offer contained no terms except those specifying the amount and quality of the cotton. The seller then sent an acknowledgment by fax. The acknowledgment repeated the terms of the buyer's offer and stated that shipment would occur within five days. Among 12 printed terms on the acknowledgment was a statement that any dispute about the cotton's quality would be submitted to arbitration. Neither the buyer nor the seller said anything further about arbitration. The seller shipped the cotton, and it was accepted by the buyer. A dispute arose between the buyer and the seller as to the quality of the cotton, and the seller asserted that the dispute had to be submitted to arbitration. The buyer instead sued the seller in court. In that suit, which of the following arguments best supports the seller's position that the buyer must submit the dispute to arbitration?","choices":{"A":"Arbitration is a more efficient method of resolving disputes than resolving them in court.","B":"The provision for arbitration did not contradict any term in the buyer's offer.","C":"The provision for arbitration did not materially alter the parties' contract.","D":"The seller's acknowledgment containing a provision for arbitration constituted a counteroffer that was accepted by the buyer when it accepted delivery of the cotton."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract formation is governed by the mirror-image rule. But the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC)—which applies to contracts for the sale of goods (eg, cotton)— follows the battle-of-the-forms rule. Under this rule, an acceptance of an offer is effective even if it contains new terms. And if both parties (ie, offeror and offeree) are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless: the offer expressly requires the offeror's assent to new terms the offeror objects within a reasonable time or the new terms materially alter the contract. Here, both parties are likely merchants since the buyer offered to buy 50 tons of cotton. This offer was silent as to arbitration, so that provision in the seller's acknowledgement (acceptance) was a new term. The buyer's offer did not expressly require her assent to new terms, and she accepted the shipment without objecting to the new term. Therefore, the arbitration provision became part of the contract if it did not materially alter the contract, which supports the seller's position that the dispute must be resolved through arbitration.\n\n(Choice A) To determine whether a contract dispute must be arbitrated, courts consider the intent of the parties as expressed in the contract—not whether arbitration is more efficient. (Choice B) The standard for determining whether a new term becomes part of the contract is whether that term materially alters the contract—not whether it contradicts the offer. (Choice D) Under the common law mirror-image rule, the seller's acknowledgment containing a new term would constitute a counteroffer. But since this transaction is governed by the UCC, the battle-of-the-forms rule applies.\n\nEducational objective: Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).","educationalObjective":"Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).","rule":"Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.","irac":{"issue":"In that suit, which of the following arguments best supports the seller's position that the buyer must submit the dispute to arbitration?","rule":"Under the battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even when it contains new terms. If both parties are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless (1) the offer expressly required the offeror's assent to new terms, (2) the offeror objected within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.","analysis":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract formation is governed by the mirror-image rule. But the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC)—which applies to contracts for the sale of goods (eg, cotton)— follows the battle-of-the-forms rule. Under this rule, an acceptance of an offer is effective even if it contains new terms. And if both parties (ie, offeror and offeree) are merchants, the new terms become part of the contract unless: the offer expressly requires the offeror's assent to new terms the offeror objects within a reasonable time or the new terms materially alter the contract. Here, both parties are likely merchants since the buyer offered to buy 50 tons of cotton. This offer was silent as to arbitration, so that provision in the seller's acknowledgement (acceptance) was a new term. The buyer's offer did not expressly require her assent to new terms, and she accepted the shipment without objecting to the new term. Therefore, the arbitration provision became part of the contract if it did not materially alter the contract, which supports the seller's position that the dispute must be resolved through arbitration.\n\n(Choice A) To determine whether a contract dispute must be arbitrated, courts consider the intent of the parties as expressed in the contract—not whether arbitration is more efficient. (Choice B) The standard for determining whether a new term becomes part of the contract is whether that term materially alters the contract—not whether it contradicts the offer. (Choice D) Under the common law mirror-image rule, the seller's acknowledgment containing a new term would constitute a counteroffer. But since this transaction is governed by the UCC, the battle-of-the-forms rule applies.","conclusion":"Answer C: The provision for arbitration did not materially alter the parties' contract."},"questionZh":"买方向卖方发出购买 50 吨指定质量棉花的报价。除了具体说明棉花数量和质量的条款外，报价中不包含任何条款。卖方随后通过传真发送了一份确认书。该确认书重申了买方的报价条款，并表示将在五天内发货。确认书上印刷的 12 条条款中有一项声明，即任何有关棉花质量的争议都将提交仲裁。买方和卖方都没有就仲裁发表任何进一步的言论。卖方发货了棉花，买方已接受。买卖双方就棉花质量发生争议，卖方主张应将争议提交仲裁。买方反而将卖方告上法庭。在那起诉讼中，以下哪项论点最能支持卖方关于买方必须将争议提交仲裁的立场？","choicesZh":{"A":"仲裁是比在法庭上解决争议更有效的解决争议的方法。","B":"仲裁条款与买方报价中的任何条款均不矛盾。","C":"仲裁条款并未对双方合同产生实质性改变。","D":"卖方的确认函包含仲裁条款，构成还盘，买方在接受棉花交货时接受了还盘。"},"explanationZh":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同的成立受镜像规则管辖。但适用于商品（例如棉花）销售合同的《统一商法典》（UCC）遵循形式之战规则。根据该规则，即使要约包含新条款，接受要约也是有效的。如果双方（即要约人和受要约人）都是商人，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非： 要约明确要求要约人在合理时间内同意要约人反对的新条款，或者新条款对合同有重大改变。在这里，双方都可能是商人，因为买家提出购买 50 吨棉花。该要约没有提及仲裁，因此卖方确认（接受）中的条款是一个新术语。买方的要约没有明确要求她同意新条款，她接受了这批货物，但没有反对新条款。因此，仲裁条款在没有对合同造成实质性改变的情况下就成为合同的一部分，这支持了卖方关于争议必须通过仲裁解决的立场。\n\n（选项A）在确定合同纠纷是否必须仲裁时，法院考虑的是合同中表达的当事人的意图，而不是仲裁是否更有效率。 （选项B） 确定新条款是否成为合同一部分的标准是该条款是否对合同产生实质性改变，而不是它是否与要约相矛盾。 （选项D） 根据普通法镜像规则，卖方的确认包含新条款将构成还盘。但由于该交易受 UCC 管辖，因此适用形式之战规则。\n\n教育目标：根据形式之战规则，即使包含新术语，接受也是有效的。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据形式之战规则，即使包含新条款，接受仍然有效。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。","iracZh":{"issue":"在那起诉讼中，以下哪项论点最能支持卖方关于买方必须将争议提交仲裁的立场？","rule":"根据形式之战规则，即使包含新条款，接受仍然有效。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。","analysis":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同的成立受镜像规则管辖。但适用于商品（例如棉花）销售合同的《统一商法典》（UCC）遵循形式之战规则。根据该规则，即使要约包含新条款，接受要约也是有效的。如果双方（即要约人和受要约人）都是商人，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非： 要约明确要求要约人在合理时间内同意要约人反对的新条款，或者新条款对合同有重大改变。在这里，双方都可能是商人，因为买家提出购买 50 吨棉花。该要约没有提及仲裁，因此卖方确认（接受）中的条款是一个新术语。买方的要约没有明确要求她同意新条款，她接受了这批货物，但没有反对新条款。因此，仲裁条款在没有对合同造成实质性改变的情况下就成为合同的一部分，这支持了卖方关于争议必须通过仲裁解决的立场。\n\n（选项A）在确定合同纠纷是否必须仲裁时，法院考虑的是合同中表达的当事人的意图，而不是仲裁是否更有效率。 （选项B） 确定新条款是否成为合同一部分的标准是该条款是否对合同产生实质性改变，而不是它是否与要约相矛盾。 （选项D） 根据普通法镜像规则，卖方的确认包含新条款将构成还盘。但由于该交易受 UCC 管辖，因此适用形式之战规则。\n\n教育目标：根据形式之战规则，即使包含新术语，接受也是有效的。如果双方都是商家，则新条款将成为合同的一部分，除非 (1) 要约明确要求要约人同意新条款，(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：仲裁条款并未对双方合同产生实质性改变。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":135,"sourceQuestionPage":398,"sourceQuestionPages":[398],"sourceExplanationPages":[399,400],"sourceAnswerPages":[399,400],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-136","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On July 18, a printer manufacturer received an order for the purchase of 500 printers from a wholesaler. The wholesaler had mailed the purchase order on July 15. The order required shipment of the printers no earlier than September 15 and no later than October 15. Typed conspicuously across the front of the order form was the following: \"Wholesaler reserves the right to cancel this order at any time before September 1.\" The manufacturer's mailed response, \"We accept your order,\" was received by the wholesaler on July 21. The wholesaler did not cancel the order, and the manufacturer shipped the printers to the wholesaler on September 15. When the printers, conforming to the order in all respects, arrived on October 10, the wholesaler refused to accept them. Which of the following is an accurate statement as of October 10 after the wholesaler rejected the printers?","choices":{"A":"In view of the wholesaler's right to cancel its order prior to September 1, the shipment of the printers on September 15 was only an offer by the manufacturer.","B":"The wholesaler's order for the printers, even if initially illusory, became a binding promise to accept and pay for them.","C":"The wholesaler's order was an offer that became an option after shipment by the manufacturer.","D":"The wholesaler's right to cancel was a condition subsequent, the failure of which resulted in an enforceable contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An offer is a communication that expresses a present intent to be legally bound to a contract and gives power to the recipient to conclude a contract by acceptance. However, there is no present intent to be legally bound if the offer is illusory because it imposes no obligation on or causes no legal detriment to the offeror. As a result, acceptance of an offer that is illusory does not form a contract. But such an offer will become legally binding if: circumstances change such that the offer imposes obligations on both parties and the offer is accepted after the change in circumstances. Once an offer is legally binding, it can be accepted to create an enforceable contract. Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods can be accepted by shipping or promising to ship conforming goods. Here, the wholesaler's purchase order was initially illusory because the wholesaler reserved the right to cancel the order at any time before September 1. But the order became non-illusory after that date and was effectively accepted when the manufacturer shipped conforming printers on September 15. As a result, the wholesaler must accept and pay for those printers. (Choice A) The manufacturer's shipment of the printers would have been an offer (specifically, a counteroffer) had it shipped nonconforming printers with a notice of accommodation. But since the manufacturer shipped conforming printers, that shipment was an acceptance (not an offer). (Choice C) An option is a type of contract in which one party promises to hold a separate offer open for a specified period of time in exchange for consideration (not seen here). (Choice D) The wholesaler's right to cancel the order was not a condition subsequent—ie, an uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses a party's existing duty to perform under a contract.\n\nEducational objective: An offer that is illusory will become legally binding if (1) circumstances change such that the offer imposes obligations on both parties and (2) the offer is accepted after the change in circumstances. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 77 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discussing illusory and alternative promises).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (explaining that an order to buy goods for shipment may be accepted by the shipment of conforming goods).","educationalObjective":"An offer that is illusory will become legally binding if (1) circumstances change such that the offer imposes obligations on both parties and (2) the offer is accepted after the change in circumstances. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 77 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discussing illusory and alternative promises).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (explaining that an order to buy goods for shipment may be accepted by the shipment of conforming goods).","rule":"An offer that is illusory will become legally binding if (1) circumstances change such that the offer imposes obligations on both parties and (2) the offer is accepted after the change in circumstances. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is an accurate statement as of October 10 after the wholesaler rejected the printers?","rule":"An offer that is illusory will become legally binding if (1) circumstances change such that the offer imposes obligations on both parties and (2) the offer is accepted after the change in circumstances. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"An offer is a communication that expresses a present intent to be legally bound to a contract and gives power to the recipient to conclude a contract by acceptance. However, there is no present intent to be legally bound if the offer is illusory because it imposes no obligation on or causes no legal detriment to the offeror. As a result, acceptance of an offer that is illusory does not form a contract. But such an offer will become legally binding if: circumstances change such that the offer imposes obligations on both parties and the offer is accepted after the change in circumstances. Once an offer is legally binding, it can be accepted to create an enforceable contract. Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods can be accepted by shipping or promising to ship conforming goods. Here, the wholesaler's purchase order was initially illusory because the wholesaler reserved the right to cancel the order at any time before September 1. But the order became non-illusory after that date and was effectively accepted when the manufacturer shipped conforming printers on September 15. As a result, the wholesaler must accept and pay for those printers. (Choice A) The manufacturer's shipment of the printers would have been an offer (specifically, a counteroffer) had it shipped nonconforming printers with a notice of accommodation. But since the manufacturer shipped conforming printers, that shipment was an acceptance (not an offer). (Choice C) An option is a type of contract in which one party promises to hold a separate offer open for a specified period of time in exchange for consideration (not seen here). (Choice D) The wholesaler's right to cancel the order was not a condition subsequent—ie, an uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses a party's existing duty to perform under a contract.","conclusion":"Answer B: The wholesaler's order for the printers, even if initially illusory, became a binding promise to accept and pay for them."},"questionZh":"7月18日，某打印机制造商接到批发商采购500台打印机的订单。批发商于 7 月 15 日邮寄了采购订单。订单要求在 9 月 15 日和 10 月 15 日之前发货打印机。订单正面显着的位置写着：“批发商保留在 9 月 1 日之前随时取消此订单的权利。”批发商于7月21日收到制造商邮寄的回复“我们接受您的订单”。批发商没有取消订单，制造商于9月15日将打印机运送给批发商。当打印机在各方面与订单相符时，10月10日到达时，批发商拒绝接受。截至 10 月 10 日，批发商拒绝打印机后，以下哪项说法是准确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"鉴于批发商有权取消9月1日之前的订单，因此9月15日发货只是制造商的报价。","B":"批发商的打印机订单，即使最初是虚幻的，也成为接受并付款的具有约束力的承诺。","C":"批发商的订单是一种报价，在制造商发货后成为一种选择。","D":"批发商的取消权是随后的一个条件，如果不满足该条件，就会产生可执行的合同。"},"explanationZh":"要约是一种沟通，表达了受合同法律约束的当前意图，并赋予接收者通过接受来签订合同的权力。然而，如果要约是虚幻的，则目前不存在受法律约束的意图，因为它不会对要约人施加任何义务或不会对要约人造成任何法律损害。因此，接受虚幻的要约并不构成合同。但在以下情况下，此类要约将具有法律约束力： 情况发生变化，致使要约对双方施加义务，并且要约在情况发生变化后被接受。一旦要约具有法律约束力，就可以接受它以创建可执行的合同。根据 UCC，购买商品的要约可以通过运送或承诺运送符合要求的商品来接受。在此，批发商的采购订单最初是虚幻的，因为批发商保留在 9 月 1 日之前随时取消订单的权利。但该日期之后，订单就变得非虚幻，并在制造商于 9 月 15 日发货合格打印机时被有效接受。因此，批发商必须接受并支付这些打印机的费用。 （选择 A）如果制造商在运送不合格打印机时附有替代交付通知（accommodation notice），则其打印机的运送将是一个报价（具体来说，是还价）。但由于制造商运送了合格的打印机，因此该运送是一种接受（而不是要约）。 （选项 C） 期权是一种合同，其中一方承诺在指定时间内持有单独的要约以换取对价（此处未看到）。 （选项D） 批发商取消订单的权利不是后续条件，即不确定的未来事件，如果该事件发生，则可以免除一方根据合同履行的现有义务。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 情况发生变化，致使要约对双方施加义务，并且 (2) 在情况发生变化后接受要约，则虚幻的要约将具有法律约束力。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 77（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨论虚幻和替代承诺）。\n\nUCC § 2-206（解释了购买待装运货物的订单可以被合格货物的装运所接受）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果出现以下情况，虚假要约将具有法律约束力：(1) 情况发生变化，致使要约对双方施加义务；(2) 在情况发生变化后要约被接受。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"截至 10 月 10 日，批发商拒绝打印机后，以下哪项说法是准确的？","rule":"如果出现以下情况，虚假要约将具有法律约束力：(1) 情况发生变化，致使要约对双方施加义务；(2) 在情况发生变化后要约被接受。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"要约是一种沟通，表达了受合同法律约束的当前意图，并赋予接收者通过接受来签订合同的权力。然而，如果要约是虚幻的，则目前不存在受法律约束的意图，因为它不会对要约人施加任何义务或不会对要约人造成任何法律损害。因此，接受虚幻的要约并不构成合同。但在以下情况下，此类要约将具有法律约束力： 情况发生变化，致使要约对双方施加义务，并且要约在情况发生变化后被接受。一旦要约具有法律约束力，就可以接受它以创建可执行的合同。根据 UCC，购买商品的要约可以通过运送或承诺运送符合要求的商品来接受。在此，批发商的采购订单最初是虚幻的，因为批发商保留在 9 月 1 日之前随时取消订单的权利。但该日期之后，订单就变得非虚幻，并在制造商于 9 月 15 日发货合格打印机时被有效接受。因此，批发商必须接受并支付这些打印机的费用。 （选择 A）如果制造商在运送不合格打印机时附有替代交付通知（accommodation notice），则其打印机的运送将是一个报价（具体来说，是还价）。但由于制造商运送了合格的打印机，因此该运送是一种接受（而不是要约）。 （选项 C） 期权是一种合同，其中一方承诺在指定时间内持有单独的要约以换取对价（此处未看到）。 （选项D） 批发商取消订单的权利不是后续条件，即不确定的未来事件，如果该事件发生，则可以免除一方根据合同履行的现有义务。\n\n教育目标：如果 (1) 情况发生变化，致使要约对双方施加义务，并且 (2) 在情况发生变化后接受要约，则虚幻的要约将具有法律约束力。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 77（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨论虚幻和替代承诺）。\n\nUCC § 2-206（解释了购买待装运货物的订单可以被合格货物的装运所接受）。","conclusion":"答案 B：批发商的打印机订单，即使最初是虚幻的，也成为接受并付款的具有约束力的承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"condition subsequent","ipa":"/kʌndˈɪʃən sˈəbsəkwənt/","syllables":"con·di·tion sub·se·quent","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"解除条件：发生后消灭既有履行义务的事件","contextualMeaning":"解除条件：发生后消灭既有履行义务的事件","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The wholesaler's right to cancel was a condition subsequent, the failure of which resulted in an enforceable contract.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-e2dd8e4651bdb264"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":136,"sourceQuestionPage":401,"sourceQuestionPages":[401],"sourceExplanationPages":[402,403],"sourceAnswerPages":[402,403],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-137","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"An employer encourages \"wellness\" on the part of his employees and supports various physical fitness programs to that end. Learning that one of his employees was a dedicated jogger, the employer promised to pay her a special award of $100 if she were to run one mile in less than six minutes on the following Saturday. The employee thanked him and did in fact run a mile in less than six minutes on the day specified. Shortly thereafter, however, the employer discovered that for more than a year the employee had been running at least one mile in less than six minutes every day as part of her personal fitness program. He then refused to pay the $100. In an action by the employee against the employer for breach of contract, which of the following best summarizes the probable decision of the court?","choices":{"A":"The employee wins, because running a mile in less than six minutes is a significantly demanding enterprise.","B":"The employee wins, because she ran the specified mile as requested, and her motives for doing so are irrelevant.","C":"The employer wins, because it is a compelling inference that his promise did not induce the employee to run the specified mile.","D":"The employer wins, because the employee's running of the specified mile was beneficial, not detrimental, to her in any event."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act Forming a valid contract requires consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. This requires each party to: perform or promise to perform some act that is not legally required or refrain or promise to refrain from performing some legally permissible act. Here, the employer promised to pay the employee $100 if she were to run a mile in less than six minutes on the following Saturday, and the employee ran the mile as requested. This exchange (employer's promise for employee's performance) provided the necessary consideration to form a binding contract—regardless of the employee's motives for running the mile. Therefore, the court should decide that the employee wins. (Choice A) The fact that running a mile in less than six minutes is a significantly demanding enterprise is irrelevant to determining whether bargained-for consideration exists. (Choice C) Although the employee runs at least one mile in less than six minutes every day, this does not give rise to an inference that the employer's promise did not induce her to run the specified mile. (Choice D) The employee suffered a legal detriment (as is required for consideration) by running the specified mile because she was not legally required to perform this act.\n\nEducational objective: Contracts must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance—which requires each party to (1) perform or promise to perform an act that is not legally required or (2) refrain or promise to refrain from performing some legally permissible act.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange as consideration).","educationalObjective":"Contracts must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance—which requires each party to (1) perform or promise to perform an act that is not legally required or (2) refrain or promise to refrain from performing some legally permissible act.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange as consideration).","rule":"Contracts must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance—which requires each party to (1) perform or promise to perform an act that is not legally required or (2) refrain or promise to refrain from performing some legally permissible act.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the employee against the employer for breach of contract, which of the following best summarizes the probable decision of the court?","rule":"Contracts must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance—which requires each party to (1) perform or promise to perform an act that is not legally required or (2) refrain or promise to refrain from performing some legally permissible act.","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act Forming a valid contract requires consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. This requires each party to: perform or promise to perform some act that is not legally required or refrain or promise to refrain from performing some legally permissible act. Here, the employer promised to pay the employee $100 if she were to run a mile in less than six minutes on the following Saturday, and the employee ran the mile as requested. This exchange (employer's promise for employee's performance) provided the necessary consideration to form a binding contract—regardless of the employee's motives for running the mile. Therefore, the court should decide that the employee wins. (Choice A) The fact that running a mile in less than six minutes is a significantly demanding enterprise is irrelevant to determining whether bargained-for consideration exists. (Choice C) Although the employee runs at least one mile in less than six minutes every day, this does not give rise to an inference that the employer's promise did not induce her to run the specified mile. (Choice D) The employee suffered a legal detriment (as is required for consideration) by running the specified mile because she was not legally required to perform this act.","conclusion":"Answer B: The employee wins, because she ran the specified mile as requested, and her motives for doing so are irrelevant."},"questionZh":"雇主鼓励员工“健康”，并为此支持各种健身计划。得知他的一名员工是一位热心的慢跑者后，雇主承诺，如果她在下周六能在六分钟内跑完一英里，就向她支付 100 美元的特别奖励。这名员工向他表示感谢，并在指定的日子里用不到六分钟的时间跑了一英里。然而，此后不久，雇主发现一年多以来，作为个人健身计划的一部分，该员工每天至少跑一英里不到六分钟。然后他拒绝支付这100美元。在雇员因违反合同而对雇主提起诉讼时，以下哪项最能概括法院可能做出的判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"员工获胜，因为在不到六分钟内跑完一英里是一项要求极高的任务。","B":"员工获胜，因为她按照要求跑了指定的英里数，而她这样做的动机无关紧要。","C":"雇主获胜，因为有一个令人信服的推论，即他的承诺没有诱使雇员跑完指定的英里数。","D":"雇主获胜，因为无论如何，雇员跑完指定的英里对她来说都是有益的，而不是有害的。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 形成有效的合同需要对价，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约。这要求各方：执行或承诺执行某些法律不要求的行为，或者不执行或承诺不执行某些法律允许的行为。在这里，雇主承诺如果雇员在下个星期六在六分钟内跑一英里，则支付她 100 美元，而雇员按照要求跑了一英里。这种交换（雇主对雇员表现的承诺）为形成具有约束力的合同提供了必要的考虑——无论雇员跑步的动机如何。因此，法院应判决该员工胜诉。 （选择 A） 在六分钟内跑完一英里是一项要求极高的任务，这一事实与确定讨价还价是否存在无关。 （选项C）虽然雇员每天至少在六分钟内跑完一英里，但这并不能推断雇主的承诺没有促使她跑完规定的英里数。 （选项 D）该雇员因跑了规定的英里数而遭受了法律损害（根据考虑），因为法律上没有要求她执行此行为。\n\n教育目标：合同必须得到对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约，这要求各方 (1) 履行或承诺履行法律不要求的行为，或 (2) 克制或承诺克制不执行某些法律允许的行为。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换作为对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同必须得到对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约，这要求各方 (1) 履行或承诺履行法律未要求的行为，或 (2) 不履行或承诺不履行某些法律允许的行为。","iracZh":{"issue":"在雇员因违反合同而对雇主提起诉讼时，以下哪项最能概括法院可能做出的判决？","rule":"合同必须得到对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约，这要求各方 (1) 履行或承诺履行法律未要求的行为，或 (2) 不履行或承诺不履行某些法律允许的行为。","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 形成有效的合同需要对价，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约。这要求各方：执行或承诺执行某些法律不要求的行为，或者不执行或承诺不执行某些法律允许的行为。在这里，雇主承诺如果雇员在下个星期六在六分钟内跑一英里，则支付她 100 美元，而雇员按照要求跑了一英里。这种交换（雇主对雇员表现的承诺）为形成具有约束力的合同提供了必要的考虑——无论雇员跑步的动机如何。因此，法院应判决该员工胜诉。 （选择 A） 在六分钟内跑完一英里是一项要求极高的任务，这一事实与确定讨价还价是否存在无关。 （选项C）虽然雇员每天至少在六分钟内跑完一英里，但这并不能推断雇主的承诺没有促使她跑完规定的英里数。 （选项 D）该雇员因跑了规定的英里数而遭受了法律损害（根据考虑），因为法律上没有要求她执行此行为。\n\n教育目标：合同必须得到对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约，这要求各方 (1) 履行或承诺履行法律不要求的行为，或 (2) 克制或承诺克制不执行某些法律允许的行为。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换作为对价）。","conclusion":"答案 B：员工获胜，因为她按照要求跑了指定的英里数，而她这样做的动机无关紧要。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":137,"sourceQuestionPage":404,"sourceQuestionPages":[404],"sourceExplanationPages":[405,406],"sourceAnswerPages":[405,406],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-138","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"Which of the following fact patterns most clearly suggests an implied-in-fact contract?","choices":{"A":"A contractor, thinking that he was paving a customer's driveway, for which the contractor had an express contract, mistakenly paved the neighbor's driveway while the neighbor looked on without saying anything or raising any objection.","B":"A county tax assessor mistakenly bills a person for taxes on the neighbor's property, which the person, in good faith, pays.","C":"A physician treated a horseback rider without the rider's knowledge or consent while he was unconscious as the result of a fall from his horse.","D":"At her mother's request, an accountant filled out and filed her mother's \"E-Z\" income- tax form (a simple, short form)."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Implied contracts\n\nImplied-in- Formed when party manifests assent by conduct rather than spoken or fact written words Eg, contractor mistakenly paves driveway & owner does not object Implied-in- Constructed by court to prevent unjust enrichment when party receives law* benefit from another who reasonably expects compensation Eg, physician treats unconscious person (\"quasi\")\n\n*Implied-in-law contracts are not true contracts since they lack mutual assent.\n\nAn implied-in-fact contract arises when a party's assent to enter a contract is inferred from the party's conduct or failure to act. This inference arises when a party (1) intentionally engages or fails to engage in conduct and (2) knows or has reason to know that the conduct may cause the other party to understand that the party assents. Here, the neighbor looked on without saying anything or raising any objection while the contractor mistakenly paved the neighbor's driveway. The neighbor's failure to object creates a reasonable inference that the neighbor assented to the work, so this fact pattern most clearly suggests an implied-in-fact contract. (Choice B) Property owners have a preexisting legal duty to pay property taxes. Therefore, a tax bill is a demand to perform that preexisting duty—not an offer to form a contract. (Choice C) In the absence of a contract between the parties, the court may construct an implied-in-law contract to prevent one party from being unjustly enriched by a benefit conferred upon it by another (eg, when an unconscious person receives treatment from a physician—as seen here). (Choice D) A communication is not an offer unless it creates an objectively reasonable expectation that the offeror is willing to enter into a binding contract. A reasonable person would not interpret the mother's request that her daughter fill out a simple income-tax form as willingness to enter into a contract, so none was formed here.\n\nEducational objective: An implied-in-fact contract can be created by a party's conduct or failure to act if the party (1) intentionally engages or fails to engage in conduct and (2) knows or has reason to know that assent can be inferred from that conduct.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts &#167; 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conduct as manifestation of assent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts &#167; 69 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (acceptance by silence).","educationalObjective":"An implied-in-fact contract can be created by a party's conduct or failure to act if the party (1) intentionally engages or fails to engage in conduct and (2) knows or has reason to know that assent can be inferred from that conduct.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts &#167; 19 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conduct as manifestation of assent).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts &#167; 69 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (acceptance by silence).","rule":"An implied-in-fact contract can be created by a party's conduct or failure to act if the party (1) intentionally engages or fails to engage in conduct and (2) knows or has reason to know that assent can be inferred from that conduct.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following fact patterns most clearly suggests an implied-in-fact contract?","rule":"An implied-in-fact contract can be created by a party's conduct or failure to act if the party (1) intentionally engages or fails to engage in conduct and (2) knows or has reason to know that assent can be inferred from that conduct.","analysis":"Implied contracts\n\nImplied-in- Formed when party manifests assent by conduct rather than spoken or fact written words Eg, contractor mistakenly paves driveway & owner does not object Implied-in- Constructed by court to prevent unjust enrichment when party receives law* benefit from another who reasonably expects compensation Eg, physician treats unconscious person (\"quasi\")\n\n*Implied-in-law contracts are not true contracts since they lack mutual assent.\n\nAn implied-in-fact contract arises when a party's assent to enter a contract is inferred from the party's conduct or failure to act. This inference arises when a party (1) intentionally engages or fails to engage in conduct and (2) knows or has reason to know that the conduct may cause the other party to understand that the party assents. Here, the neighbor looked on without saying anything or raising any objection while the contractor mistakenly paved the neighbor's driveway. The neighbor's failure to object creates a reasonable inference that the neighbor assented to the work, so this fact pattern most clearly suggests an implied-in-fact contract. (Choice B) Property owners have a preexisting legal duty to pay property taxes. Therefore, a tax bill is a demand to perform that preexisting duty—not an offer to form a contract. (Choice C) In the absence of a contract between the parties, the court may construct an implied-in-law contract to prevent one party from being unjustly enriched by a benefit conferred upon it by another (eg, when an unconscious person receives treatment from a physician—as seen here). (Choice D) A communication is not an offer unless it creates an objectively reasonable expectation that the offeror is willing to enter into a binding contract. A reasonable person would not interpret the mother's request that her daughter fill out a simple income-tax form as willingness to enter into a contract, so none was formed here.","conclusion":"Answer A: A contractor, thinking that he was paving a customer's driveway, for which the contractor had an express contract, mistakenly paved the neighbor's driveway while the neighbor looked on without saying anything or raising any objection."},"questionZh":"以下哪种事实模式最清楚地表明了默示事实合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"一名承包商以为自己正在铺设客户的车道，而承包商对此有明确的合同，因此错误地铺设了邻居的车道，而邻居则在一旁观看，没有说什么或提出任何反对。","B":"县税务评估员错误地向某人收取邻居财产税，而该人善意地缴纳了税款。","C":"一名医生在骑手不知情或未同意的情况下对他进行治疗，当时他因从马背上摔下来而失去知觉。","D":"应她母亲的要求，一名会计师填写并提交了她母亲的“E-Z”所得税表（一种简单的简短表格）。"},"explanationZh":"默示合同\n\n隐含- 当一方通过行为而不是口头或事实书面表达同意时 例如，承包商错误地铺设了车道且业主不反对 隐含- 当一方从合理期望赔偿的另一方获得法律*利益时，由法院构建，以防止不当得利 例如，医生治疗失去知觉的人（“准”）\n\n*默示法律合同不是真正的合同，因为它们缺乏双方同意。\n\n当从一方的行为或不行为推断一方同意签订合同时，默示事实合同即产生。当一方 (1) 故意从事或未从事某种行为，并且 (2) 知道或有理由知道该行为可能导致另一方理解该方同意时，就会出现这种推论。在这里，当承包商错误地铺设了邻居的车道时，邻居袖手旁观，没有说什么，也没有提出任何异议。邻居未能反对会产生邻居同意这项工作的合理推断，因此这种事实模式最清楚地表明了默示的事实合同。 （选择 B） 业主有缴纳财产税的既定法律义务。因此，税单是履行先前存在的义务的要求，而不是形成合同的要约。 （选择C）在双方之间没有合同的情况下，法院可以制定默示法律合同，以防止一方因另一方给予的利益而获得不公正的利益（例如，当失去知觉的人接受医生的治疗时，如此处所示）。 （选项 D）除非沟通产生了要约人愿意签订具有约束力的合同的客观合理期望，否则该沟通不是要约。一个理智的人不会将母亲要求女儿填写简单所得税表的要求解释为愿意签订合同，因此这里没有形成任何合同。\n\n教育目标：如果一方 (1) 故意从事或未从事某项行为，并且 (2) 知道或有理由知道可以从该行为中推断出同意，则可以通过该方的行为或不作为来创建默示事实合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 19（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（作为同意的表现）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 69（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（沉默接受）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果一方 (1) 故意从事或未从事某种行为，并且 (2) 知道或有理由知道可以从该行为中推断出同意，则默示事实合同可以通过一方的行为或不作为而成立。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪种事实模式最清楚地表明了默示事实合同？","rule":"如果一方 (1) 故意从事或未从事某种行为，并且 (2) 知道或有理由知道可以从该行为中推断出同意，则默示事实合同可以通过一方的行为或不作为而成立。","analysis":"默示合同\n\n隐含- 当一方通过行为而不是口头或事实书面表达同意时 例如，承包商错误地铺设了车道且业主不反对 隐含- 当一方从合理期望赔偿的另一方获得法律*利益时，由法院构建，以防止不当得利 例如，医生治疗失去知觉的人（“准”）\n\n*默示法律合同不是真正的合同，因为它们缺乏双方同意。\n\n当从一方的行为或不行为推断一方同意签订合同时，默示事实合同即产生。当一方 (1) 故意从事或未从事某种行为，并且 (2) 知道或有理由知道该行为可能导致另一方理解该方同意时，就会出现这种推论。在这里，当承包商错误地铺设了邻居的车道时，邻居袖手旁观，没有说什么，也没有提出任何异议。邻居未能反对会产生邻居同意这项工作的合理推断，因此这种事实模式最清楚地表明了默示的事实合同。 （选择 B） 业主有缴纳财产税的既定法律义务。因此，税单是履行先前存在的义务的要求，而不是形成合同的要约。 （选择C）在双方之间没有合同的情况下，法院可以制定默示法律合同，以防止一方因另一方给予的利益而获得不公正的利益（例如，当失去知觉的人接受医生的治疗时，如此处所示）。 （选项 D）除非沟通产生了要约人愿意签订具有约束力的合同的客观合理期望，否则该沟通不是要约。一个理智的人不会将母亲要求女儿填写简单所得税表的要求解释为愿意签订合同，因此这里没有形成任何合同。\n\n教育目标：如果一方 (1) 故意从事或未从事某项行为，并且 (2) 知道或有理由知道可以从该行为中推断出同意，则可以通过该方的行为或不作为来创建默示事实合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 19（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（作为同意的表现）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 69（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（沉默接受）。","conclusion":"答案 A：一名承包商以为自己正在铺设客户的车道，而承包商对此有明确的合同，因此错误地铺设了邻居的车道，而邻居则在一旁观看，没有说什么或提出任何反对。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":138,"sourceQuestionPage":407,"sourceQuestionPages":[407],"sourceExplanationPages":[408,409],"sourceAnswerPages":[408,409],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-139","subject":"Contracts","topic":"UCC Formation & Battle of Forms","question":"A restorer of classic cars offered to completely repair and refurbish his neighbor's car for $35,000. The neighbor was anxious to return her classic car to its former glory but had not expected the restoration to cost $35,000. She said to the restorer, \"The car is already in good condition. Will you consider taking $29,000 to restore it?\" The restorer was incensed and said, \"Absolutely not!\" The neighbor then agreed to pay the restorer $35,000 to restore her car. Is there a binding contract between the restorer and the neighbor for the restoration of the classic car?","choices":{"A":"No, because the neighbor's statement was both a rejection of the restorer's offer and a counteroffer that the restorer did not accept.","B":"No, because the restorer is a merchant with respect to car restoration and his offer to restore the car was not firm.","C":"Yes, because the neighbor's statement was a valid acceptance that contained only minor additional terms.","D":"Yes, because the neighbor's statement was not a rejection of the restorer's offer but rather an inquiry into different terms."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contracts for services (eg, restoration) are governed by the common law, which follows the mirror-image rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an acceptance must match the terms of the offer exactly. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as a rejection of the original offer and as a counteroffer. However, mere suggestions or inquiries that the offeror is free to accept or reject do not. The offeree can therefore accept the original offer in spite of such suggestions or inquiries. Here, the restorer offered to repair and refurbish the neighbor's classic car for $35,000. Although the neighbor asked if the restorer would consider taking $29,000 instead, this was a mere inquiry that the restorer could accept or reject—not a rejection of the restorer's offer and a counteroffer (Choice A). Therefore, the neighbor's subsequent acceptance of the restorer's $35,000 offer was effective and created a binding contract between them for the restoration of the classic car. (Choice B) Under the UCC, a merchant's offer to buy or sell goods is firm (ie, irrevocable) if it is written, is signed by the merchant-offeror, and provides assurances that it will remain open for a period of time. However, the UCC does not apply to contracts for services (as seen here). (Choice C) Under the UCC battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation, an acceptance that contains revised or new terms is valid unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to those terms. But this contract is governed by the common law mirror-image rule, under which an acceptance that contains different or additional terms (not seen here) is invalid.\n\nEducational objective: Under the mirror-image rule, a purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as a rejection and as a counteroffer; however, mere suggestions or inquiries that the offeror is free to accept or reject do not. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 80 (2020) (necessity that acceptance comply with offer, generally).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 86 (2020) (collateral or immaterial matters; inquiries, requests, suggestions, or directions).","educationalObjective":"Under the mirror-image rule, a purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as a rejection and as a counteroffer; however, mere suggestions or inquiries that the offeror is free to accept or reject do not. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 80 (2020) (necessity that acceptance comply with offer, generally).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 86 (2020) (collateral or immaterial matters; inquiries, requests, suggestions, or directions).","rule":"Under the mirror-image rule, a purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as a rejection and as a counteroffer; however, mere suggestions or inquiries that the offeror is free to accept or reject do not. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Is there a binding contract between the restorer and the neighbor for the restoration of the classic car?","rule":"Under the mirror-image rule, a purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as a rejection and as a counteroffer; however, mere suggestions or inquiries that the offeror is free to accept or reject do not. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Contracts for services (eg, restoration) are governed by the common law, which follows the mirror-image rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an acceptance must match the terms of the offer exactly. A purported acceptance that contains different or additional terms acts as a rejection of the original offer and as a counteroffer. However, mere suggestions or inquiries that the offeror is free to accept or reject do not. The offeree can therefore accept the original offer in spite of such suggestions or inquiries. Here, the restorer offered to repair and refurbish the neighbor's classic car for $35,000. Although the neighbor asked if the restorer would consider taking $29,000 instead, this was a mere inquiry that the restorer could accept or reject—not a rejection of the restorer's offer and a counteroffer (Choice A). Therefore, the neighbor's subsequent acceptance of the restorer's $35,000 offer was effective and created a binding contract between them for the restoration of the classic car. (Choice B) Under the UCC, a merchant's offer to buy or sell goods is firm (ie, irrevocable) if it is written, is signed by the merchant-offeror, and provides assurances that it will remain open for a period of time. However, the UCC does not apply to contracts for services (as seen here). (Choice C) Under the UCC battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation, an acceptance that contains revised or new terms is valid unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to those terms. But this contract is governed by the common law mirror-image rule, under which an acceptance that contains different or additional terms (not seen here) is invalid.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the neighbor's statement was not a rejection of the restorer's offer but rather an inquiry into different terms."},"questionZh":"一位老爷车修复师提出以 35,000 美元的价格彻底修复和翻新邻居的汽车。邻居急于让她的老爷车恢复昔日的辉煌，但没想到修复费用高达 35,000 美元。她对修复师说：“车况已经很好了，你会考虑花29,000美元修复它吗？”修复师勃然大怒，道：“绝对不行！”邻居随后同意向修复者支付 35,000 美元来修复她的汽车。修复者和邻居之间是否有关于修复老爷车的具有约束力的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为邻居的说法既是对修复者报价的拒绝，也是修复者不接受的还价。","B":"不，因为修复者是汽车修复商人，他修复汽车的报价并不坚定。","C":"是的，因为邻居的声明是有效的接受，仅包含少量附加条款。","D":"是的，因为邻居的声明并不是拒绝修复者的提议，而是对不同条款的询问。"},"explanationZh":"服务合同（例如修复）受普通法管辖，遵循合同订立的镜像规则。根据这一规则，接受必须与要约条款完全一致。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受相当于对原始要约的拒绝和还盘。然而，要约人可以自由接受或拒绝的单纯建议或询问则不然。因此，尽管有此类建议或询问，受要约人仍可以接受原始要约。修复师提出以 35,000 美元的价格修理和翻新邻居的老爷车。尽管邻居询问修复者是否会考虑接受 29,000 美元，但这只是修复者可以接受或拒绝的询问，而不是拒绝修复者的报价和还价（选项 A）。因此，邻居随后接受修复者 35,000 美元的报价是有效的，并在他们之间建立了一份具有约束力的修复老爷车的合同。 （选择B）根据UCC，如果商家购买或出售商品的要约是书面的、由商家要约人签署并提供在一段时间内保持开放的保证，则该要约是确定的（即不可撤销的）。但是，UCC 不适用于服务合同（如此处所示）。 （选项 C）根据 UCC 合同形式争夺规则，包含修订或新条款的承诺是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意这些条款为条件。但本合同受普通法镜像规则管辖，根据该规则，包含不同或附加条款（此处未见）的承诺无效。\n\n教育目标：根据镜像规则，包含不同或附加条款的声称接受将充当拒绝和还盘；然而，要约人可以自由接受或拒绝的单纯建议或询问则不然。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 80 (2020)（一般情况下，接受与要约一致的必要性）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 86 (2020)（附带或非实质性事项；查询、请求、建议或指示）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据镜像规则，包含不同或附加条款的声称接受将被视为拒绝和还盘；然而，要约人可以自由接受或拒绝的单纯建议或询问则不然。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"修复者和邻居之间是否有关于修复老爷车的具有约束力的合同？","rule":"根据镜像规则，包含不同或附加条款的声称接受将被视为拒绝和还盘；然而，要约人可以自由接受或拒绝的单纯建议或询问则不然。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"服务合同（例如修复）受普通法管辖，遵循合同订立的镜像规则。根据这一规则，接受必须与要约条款完全一致。包含不同或附加条款的声称接受相当于对原始要约的拒绝和还盘。然而，要约人可以自由接受或拒绝的单纯建议或询问则不然。因此，尽管有此类建议或询问，受要约人仍可以接受原始要约。修复师提出以 35,000 美元的价格修理和翻新邻居的老爷车。尽管邻居询问修复者是否会考虑接受 29,000 美元，但这只是修复者可以接受或拒绝的询问，而不是拒绝修复者的报价和还价（选项 A）。因此，邻居随后接受修复者 35,000 美元的报价是有效的，并在他们之间建立了一份具有约束力的修复老爷车的合同。 （选择B）根据UCC，如果商家购买或出售商品的要约是书面的、由商家要约人签署并提供在一段时间内保持开放的保证，则该要约是确定的（即不可撤销的）。但是，UCC 不适用于服务合同（如此处所示）。 （选项 C）根据 UCC 合同形式争夺规则，包含修订或新条款的承诺是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意这些条款为条件。但本合同受普通法镜像规则管辖，根据该规则，包含不同或附加条款（此处未见）的承诺无效。\n\n教育目标：根据镜像规则，包含不同或附加条款的声称接受将充当拒绝和还盘；然而，要约人可以自由接受或拒绝的单纯建议或询问则不然。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 80 (2020)（一般情况下，接受与要约一致的必要性）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 86 (2020)（附带或非实质性事项；查询、请求、建议或指示）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为邻居的声明并不是拒绝修复者的提议，而是对不同条款的询问。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6bf59854e4f06e15"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":139,"sourceQuestionPage":410,"sourceQuestionPages":[410],"sourceExplanationPages":[411,412],"sourceAnswerPages":[411,412],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-140","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"On June 1, a widget manufacturer entered into a written agreement with a tool maker, in which the tool maker agreed to produce and sell to the widget manufacturer 12 sets of newly designed dies to be delivered August 1 for the price of $50,000, payable ten days after delivery. Encountering unexpected expenses in the purchase of special alloy steel required for the dies, the tool maker advised the widget manufacturer that production costs would exceed the contract price. On July 1 the widget manufacturer and the tool maker signed a modification to the June 1 agreement increasing the contract price to $60,000. After timely receipt of 12 sets of dies conforming to the contract specifications, the widget manufacturer paid the tool maker $50,000 but refused to pay more. Which of the following concepts of the Uniform Commercial Code, dealing expressly with the sale of goods, best supports an action by the tool maker to recover $10,000 for breach of the widget manufacturer's July 1 promise?","choices":{"A":"Bargained-for exchange.","B":"Modification of contracts without consideration.","C":"Promissory estoppel.","D":"Unconscionability in the formation of contracts."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract modification generally requires a new bargained-for exchange that alters the duties of both parties in some way (ie, new consideration). But the UCC, which deals expressly with the sale of goods, allows modifications without consideration so long as they are sought in good faith. Good faith requires honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing. And if either party fails to perform his/her obligations under the modified contract, then the other party can recover for breach. Here, the widget manufacturer entered a contract to buy dies (ie, cutting tools) from the tool maker. Due to unexpected expenses in purchasing the steel required for the dies, the parties signed a modification agreement increasing the contract price by $10,000. This modification was not a bargained-for exchange since the tool maker's duties were unaltered (Choice A). But since dies are goods, the UCC rule applies and allows modification without consideration, which supports the tool maker's action to recover the unpaid $10,000. (Choice C) Had the tool maker's action been for promissory estoppel instead of breach, the tool maker might have been able to recover damages for its reliance on the widget manufacturer's promise to pay the increased price. However, promissory estoppel is a common law doctrine, not a UCC concept. (Choice D) A court can refuse to enforce a contract if there was unconscionability in its formation—ie, unfairness due to surprise or unequal bargaining power. But even if the formation of the contract was unconscionable, the court's likely response (ie, refusing to enforce the contract) would mean that neither party could recover for breach.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, contracts for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration provided that the modification is sought in good faith. Good faith entails honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that an agreement modifying a sale-of-goods contract needs no consideration to be binding).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, contracts for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration provided that the modification is sought in good faith. Good faith entails honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that an agreement modifying a sale-of-goods contract needs no consideration to be binding).","rule":"Under the UCC, contracts for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration provided that the modification is sought in good faith. Good faith entails honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following concepts of the Uniform Commercial Code, dealing expressly with the sale of goods, best supports an action by the tool maker to recover $10,000 for breach of the widget manufacturer's July 1 promise?","rule":"Under the UCC, contracts for the sale of goods can be modified without consideration provided that the modification is sought in good faith. Good faith entails honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing.","analysis":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract modification generally requires a new bargained-for exchange that alters the duties of both parties in some way (ie, new consideration). But the UCC, which deals expressly with the sale of goods, allows modifications without consideration so long as they are sought in good faith. Good faith requires honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing. And if either party fails to perform his/her obligations under the modified contract, then the other party can recover for breach. Here, the widget manufacturer entered a contract to buy dies (ie, cutting tools) from the tool maker. Due to unexpected expenses in purchasing the steel required for the dies, the parties signed a modification agreement increasing the contract price by $10,000. This modification was not a bargained-for exchange since the tool maker's duties were unaltered (Choice A). But since dies are goods, the UCC rule applies and allows modification without consideration, which supports the tool maker's action to recover the unpaid $10,000. (Choice C) Had the tool maker's action been for promissory estoppel instead of breach, the tool maker might have been able to recover damages for its reliance on the widget manufacturer's promise to pay the increased price. However, promissory estoppel is a common law doctrine, not a UCC concept. (Choice D) A court can refuse to enforce a contract if there was unconscionability in its formation—ie, unfairness due to surprise or unequal bargaining power. But even if the formation of the contract was unconscionable, the court's likely response (ie, refusing to enforce the contract) would mean that neither party could recover for breach.","conclusion":"Answer B: Modification of contracts without consideration."},"questionZh":"6月1日，一家小部件制造商与一家工具制造商签订了一份书面协议，其中工具制造商同意向该小部件制造商生产和销售12套新设计的模具，并于8月1日交付，价格为50,000美元，交货后10天付款。由于在购买模具所需的特殊合金钢时遇到意外费用，模具制造商向部件制造商告知生产成本将超过合同价格。 7 月 1 日，小部件制造商和工具制造商签署了对 6 月 1 日协议的修改，将合同价格提高到 60,000 美元。在及时收到符合合同规格的 12 套模具后，部件制造商向工具制造商支付了 5 万美元，但拒绝支付更多费用。 《统一商法典》（UCC）中明确涉及商品销售的以下哪项概念最能支持工具制造商因违反小部件制造商 7 月 1 日承诺而采取的追偿 10,000 美元的行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"讨价还价交换。","B":"无偿修改合同。","C":"允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）。","D":"订立合同时的不合理性。"},"explanationZh":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同修改通常需要新的协商交换，以某种方式改变双方的义务（即新的对价）。但 UCC 明确涉及商品销售，只要善意寻求，就允许不经考虑进行修改。诚信要求诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。如果任何一方未能履行修改后的合同项下的义务，则另一方可以因违约而获得赔偿。在这里，小部件制造商签订了从工具制造商那里购买模具（即切削工具）的合同。由于购买模具所需钢材的意外费用，双方签署了修改协议，将合同价格增加了 10,000 美元。这种修改不是一种讨价还价的交换，因为工具制造商的职责没有改变（选择 A）。但由于模具是商品，因此适用 UCC 规则并允许无需约因即可进行修改，这支持了工具制造商收回未付的 10,000 美元的行动。 （选项C）如果工具制造商的行为是禁止反言而不是违约，那么工具制造商可能能够因其依赖小部件制造商支付增加价格的承诺而获得损害赔偿。然而，禁止反言是普通法原则，而不是 UCC 概念。 （选项D）如果合同的形成存在不合理性，即由于意外或不平等的议价能力而导致不公平，法院可以拒绝执行合同。但即使合同的订立是不合理的，法院可能的反应（即拒绝执行合同）也意味着任何一方都无法因违约而获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，只要善意地寻求修改，就可以不经考虑地修改货物销售合同。诚信意味着诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释修改货物销售合同的协议无需约因即可具有约束力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），只要善意地寻求修改，货物销售合同就可以在没有约因的情况下进行修改。诚信意味着诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。","iracZh":{"issue":"《统一商法典》（UCC）中明确涉及商品销售的以下哪项概念最能支持工具制造商因违反小部件制造商 7 月 1 日承诺而采取的追偿 10,000 美元的行动？","rule":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），只要善意地寻求修改，货物销售合同就可以在没有约因的情况下进行修改。诚信意味着诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。","analysis":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同修改通常需要新的协商交换，以某种方式改变双方的义务（即新的对价）。但 UCC 明确涉及商品销售，只要善意寻求，就允许不经考虑进行修改。诚信要求诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。如果任何一方未能履行修改后的合同项下的义务，则另一方可以因违约而获得赔偿。在这里，小部件制造商签订了从工具制造商那里购买模具（即切削工具）的合同。由于购买模具所需钢材的意外费用，双方签署了修改协议，将合同价格增加了 10,000 美元。这种修改不是一种讨价还价的交换，因为工具制造商的职责没有改变（选择 A）。但由于模具是商品，因此适用 UCC 规则并允许无需约因即可进行修改，这支持了工具制造商收回未付的 10,000 美元的行动。 （选项C）如果工具制造商的行为是禁止反言而不是违约，那么工具制造商可能能够因其依赖小部件制造商支付增加价格的承诺而获得损害赔偿。然而，禁止反言是普通法原则，而不是 UCC 概念。 （选项D）如果合同的形成存在不合理性，即由于意外或不平等的议价能力而导致不公平，法院可以拒绝执行合同。但即使合同的订立是不合理的，法院可能的反应（即拒绝执行合同）也意味着任何一方都无法因违约而获得赔偿。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，只要善意地寻求修改，就可以不经考虑地修改货物销售合同。诚信意味着诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（解释修改货物销售合同的协议无需约因即可具有约束力）。","conclusion":"答案 B：无偿修改合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":140,"sourceQuestionPage":413,"sourceQuestionPages":[413],"sourceExplanationPages":[414,415],"sourceAnswerPages":[414,415],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-141","subject":"Contracts","topic":"UCC Formation & Battle of Forms","question":"A lawn service company agreed in writing to purchase from a supplier all of its requirements for lawn care products during the next calendar year. In the writing, the supplier agreed to fulfill those requirements and to give the company a 10% discount off its published prices, but it reserved the right to increase the published prices during the year. After the parties had performed under the agreement for three months, the supplier notified the company that it would no longer give the company the 10% discount off the published prices. Does the company have a viable claim against the supplier for breach of contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the absence of a minimum quantity term rendered the company's promise illusory.","B":"No, because the supplier could, and did, revoke its offer with respect to future deliveries.","C":"Yes, because part performance of the agreement by both parties made it enforceable for the full year.","D":"Yes, because the company's agreement to buy all of its lawn care products from the supplier made the agreement enforceable."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract terms for sale of goods\n\n(Article 2 UCC)\n\nTerms Requirements Effect if missing\n\nGoods to be Must be reasonably identified No contract sold\n\nQuantity Must be certain or able to be made certain by reference\n\nto objective facts\n\nPrice N/A Gap-filler: reasonable price\n\nat time of delivery\n\nTime of Gap-filler: reasonable delivery time\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods. Under the UCC, an offer becomes irrevocable once it is accepted. The resulting contract must contain definite terms that: reasonably identify the goods to be sold and\n\nspecify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts.\n\nThe quantity term is sufficiently definite in a requirements contract—ie, a contract for the sale of as many goods as the buyer needs during a specified period—because the buyer's actual requirements can be determined objectively. Here, the lawn service company and the supplier entered into a requirements contract wherein the lawn service company agreed to buy all of its lawn care products from the supplier for one year at a 10% discount.* Although this contract did not contain a certain quantity, the terms were sufficiently definite because the company's actual requirements could be determined objectively. Therefore, the contract was enforceable for its entire one- year term, and the supplier breached the contract by refusing to give the discount three months later (Choice B).\n\n*The supplier reserved the right to increase its published prices—not to revoke the 10% discount on those prices. (Choice A) A promise is illusory, and therefore unenforceable, if the promisor has discretion whether to perform at all (eg, X promises to buy such goods as X wishes to order from Y). But here, the absence of a minimum quantity term does not render the company's promise illusory since it is still required to buy (and the supplier is still required to sell) all lawn care products it actually needs. (Choice C) A contract whose terms would otherwise be too vague to be enforced may become enforceable based on part performance if the parties' subsequent acts supply the needed clarification of the terms (not needed here).\n\nEducational objective: Contracts for the sale of goods must specify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts—eg, the buyer's actual requirements for a product can be used to determine quantity under a requirements contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-306 cmt. 2 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that the quantity term is sufficiently definite in output and requirements contracts).","educationalObjective":"Contracts for the sale of goods must specify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts—eg, the buyer's actual requirements for a product can be used to determine quantity under a requirements contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-306 cmt. 2 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (explaining that the quantity term is sufficiently definite in output and requirements contracts).","rule":"Contracts for the sale of goods must specify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts—eg, the buyer's actual requirements for a product can be used to determine quantity under a requirements contract.","irac":{"issue":"Does the company have a viable claim against the supplier for breach of contract?","rule":"Contracts for the sale of goods must specify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts—eg, the buyer's actual requirements for a product can be used to determine quantity under a requirements contract.","analysis":"Contract terms for sale of goods\n\n(Article 2 UCC)\n\nTerms Requirements Effect if missing\n\nGoods to be Must be reasonably identified No contract sold\n\nQuantity Must be certain or able to be made certain by reference\n\nto objective facts\n\nPrice N/A Gap-filler: reasonable price\n\nat time of delivery\n\nTime of Gap-filler: reasonable delivery time\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods. Under the UCC, an offer becomes irrevocable once it is accepted. The resulting contract must contain definite terms that: reasonably identify the goods to be sold and\n\nspecify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts.\n\nThe quantity term is sufficiently definite in a requirements contract—ie, a contract for the sale of as many goods as the buyer needs during a specified period—because the buyer's actual requirements can be determined objectively. Here, the lawn service company and the supplier entered into a requirements contract wherein the lawn service company agreed to buy all of its lawn care products from the supplier for one year at a 10% discount.* Although this contract did not contain a certain quantity, the terms were sufficiently definite because the company's actual requirements could be determined objectively. Therefore, the contract was enforceable for its entire one- year term, and the supplier breached the contract by refusing to give the discount three months later (Choice B).\n\n*The supplier reserved the right to increase its published prices—not to revoke the 10% discount on those prices. (Choice A) A promise is illusory, and therefore unenforceable, if the promisor has discretion whether to perform at all (eg, X promises to buy such goods as X wishes to order from Y). But here, the absence of a minimum quantity term does not render the company's promise illusory since it is still required to buy (and the supplier is still required to sell) all lawn care products it actually needs. (Choice C) A contract whose terms would otherwise be too vague to be enforced may become enforceable based on part performance if the parties' subsequent acts supply the needed clarification of the terms (not needed here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the company's agreement to buy all of its lawn care products from the supplier made the agreement enforceable."},"questionZh":"一家草坪服务公司书面同意在下一个日历年从供应商购买其所需的所有草坪护理产品。在书面形式中，供应商同意满足这些要求，并给予公司公布价格 10% 的折扣，但保留在年内提高公布价格的权利。双方履行协议三个月后，供应商通知该公司不再给予该公司公布价格10%的折扣。公司是否对供应商违约提出可行的索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为缺乏最低数量条款使得公司的承诺变得商业上不可行。","B":"不，因为供应商可以并且确实撤销了有关未来交货的报价。","C":"是的，因为双方部分履行该协议使其在全年内可执行。","D":"是的，因为该公司同意从供应商那里购买所有草坪护理产品，使得该协议具有可执行性。"},"explanationZh":"货物销售合同条款\n\n（《UCC》第 2 条）\n\n条款 要求 缺失的影响\n\n待售货物 必须合理标识 未售出合同\n\n数量 必须确定或能够通过参考确定\n\n以客观事实为依据\n\n价格 N/A 填补空白：价格合理\n\n交货时\n\n填隙时间：合理的交货时间\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖货物销售合同。根据 UCC，要约一旦被接受就不可撤销。由此产生的合同必须包含明确的条款：合理地确定要出售的货物，以及\n\n指定待售货物的数量，该数量是确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的。\n\n需求合同中的数量条款是足够明确的，即在特定时期内销售买方需要的尽可能多的货物的合同，因为买方的实际需求可以客观地确定。在此，草坪服务公司与供应商签订了一份需求合同，其中草坪服务公司同意以10%的折扣从供应商处购买其所有草坪护理产品一年。*虽然该合同没有明确数量，但条款足够明确，因为公司的实际需求可以客观确定。因此，合同在整个一年期限内有效，而供应商在三个月后拒绝给予折扣，从而违反了合同（选项B）。\n\n*供应商保留提高其公布价格的权利，而不是撤销这些价格的 10% 折扣。 （选择A）如果承诺者可以自行决定是否履行，则承诺是虚幻的，因此无法执行（例如，X承诺购买X希望从Y订购的商品）。但在这里，缺乏最低数量条款并不会使公司的承诺变得虚幻，因为它仍然需要购买（并且供应商仍然需要出售）其实际需要的所有草坪护理产品。 （选择 C）如果双方随后的行为提供了所需的条款澄清（此处不需要），则其条款过于模糊而无法执行的合同可以根据部分履行而变得可执行。\n\n教育目标：货物销售合同必须规定待售货物的数量，该数量是确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的，例如，买方对产品的实际需求可用于确定需求合同下的数量。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-306 cmt。 2（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（解释产量和需求合同中的数量条款足够明确）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"货物销售合同必须规定待售货物的数量，该数量是确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的，例如，买方对产品的实际需求可以用来确定需求合同中的数量。","iracZh":{"issue":"公司是否对供应商违约提出可行的索赔？","rule":"货物销售合同必须规定待售货物的数量，该数量是确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的，例如，买方对产品的实际需求可以用来确定需求合同中的数量。","analysis":"货物销售合同条款\n\n（《UCC》第 2 条）\n\n条款 要求 缺失的影响\n\n待售货物 必须合理标识 未售出合同\n\n数量 必须确定或能够通过参考确定\n\n以客观事实为依据\n\n价格 N/A 填补空白：价格合理\n\n交货时\n\n填隙时间：合理的交货时间\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖货物销售合同。根据 UCC，要约一旦被接受就不可撤销。由此产生的合同必须包含明确的条款：合理地确定要出售的货物，以及\n\n指定待售货物的数量，该数量是确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的。\n\n需求合同中的数量条款是足够明确的，即在特定时期内销售买方需要的尽可能多的货物的合同，因为买方的实际需求可以客观地确定。在此，草坪服务公司与供应商签订了一份需求合同，其中草坪服务公司同意以10%的折扣从供应商处购买其所有草坪护理产品一年。*虽然该合同没有明确数量，但条款足够明确，因为公司的实际需求可以客观确定。因此，合同在整个一年期限内有效，而供应商在三个月后拒绝给予折扣，从而违反了合同（选项B）。\n\n*供应商保留提高其公布价格的权利，而不是撤销这些价格的 10% 折扣。 （选择A）如果承诺者可以自行决定是否履行，则承诺是虚幻的，因此无法执行（例如，X承诺购买X希望从Y订购的商品）。但在这里，缺乏最低数量条款并不会使公司的承诺变得虚幻，因为它仍然需要购买（并且供应商仍然需要出售）其实际需要的所有草坪护理产品。 （选择 C）如果双方随后的行为提供了所需的条款澄清（此处不需要），则其条款过于模糊而无法执行的合同可以根据部分履行而变得可执行。\n\n教育目标：货物销售合同必须规定待售货物的数量，该数量是确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的，例如，买方对产品的实际需求可用于确定需求合同下的数量。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-306 cmt。 2（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（解释产量和需求合同中的数量条款足够明确）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该公司同意从供应商那里购买所有草坪护理产品，使得该协议具有可执行性。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":141,"sourceQuestionPage":416,"sourceQuestionPages":[416],"sourceExplanationPages":[417,418],"sourceAnswerPages":[417,418],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-142","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A man sent an email to a friend that stated: \"Because you have been a great friend to me, I am going to give you a rare book that I own.\" The friend replied by an email that said: \"Thanks for the rare book. I am going to give you my butterfly collection.\" The rare book was worth $10,000; the butterfly collection was worth $100. The friend delivered the butterfly collection to the man, but the man refused to deliver the book. If the friend sues the man to recover the value of the book, how should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"For the friend, because she conferred a benefit on the man by delivering the butterfly collection.","B":"For the friend, because she gave the butterfly collection to the man in reliance on receiving the book.","C":"For the man, because the consideration given for his promise was inadequate.","D":"For the man, because there was no bargained-for exchange to support his promise."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act Formation of a contract generally requires a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance (ie, valuable consideration). The bargained-for exchange requirement is not met unless each party's willingness to enter into the contract was induced by the other party's act or promise. So if either of the parties intended to make a gift, this requirement cannot be met. Here, the man promised to give a rare book to his friend. The man clearly intended to make a gift since he did not ask for anything in exchange. This means that the man's promise was not induced by the friend's promise to give him her butterfly collection, so there was no bargained-for exchange (ie, consideration) to support his promise. Therefore, no contract arose between the parties, and the court should rule in favor of the man. (Choice A) The mere fact that the friend conferred a benefit on the man by delivering the butterfly collection does not mean that the man has an enforceable obligation to deliver the book to the friend. (Choice B) In the absence of consideration, the doctrine of promissory estoppel provides a basis to enforce a promise upon which a party reasonably and detrimentally relied. However, this doctrine can only be invoked if it is the only way to avoid substantial injustice (not seen here). (Choice C) Instead of giving inadequate consideration, the friend gave no consideration at all since the man did not bargain for her return promise to deliver the butterfly collection. And parties generally cannot challenge a contract on the ground that the consideration given for their promises was inadequate (eg, due to a difference in economic value).\n\nEducational objective: A promise to make a gift is generally unenforceable because it does not involve the bargained-for exchange that is required to constitute valuable consideration.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange).","educationalObjective":"A promise to make a gift is generally unenforceable because it does not involve the bargained-for exchange that is required to constitute valuable consideration.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange).","rule":"A promise to make a gift is generally unenforceable because it does not involve the bargained-for exchange that is required to constitute valuable consideration.","irac":{"issue":"If the friend sues the man to recover the value of the book, how should the court rule?","rule":"A promise to make a gift is generally unenforceable because it does not involve the bargained-for exchange that is required to constitute valuable consideration.","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act Formation of a contract generally requires a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance (ie, valuable consideration). The bargained-for exchange requirement is not met unless each party's willingness to enter into the contract was induced by the other party's act or promise. So if either of the parties intended to make a gift, this requirement cannot be met. Here, the man promised to give a rare book to his friend. The man clearly intended to make a gift since he did not ask for anything in exchange. This means that the man's promise was not induced by the friend's promise to give him her butterfly collection, so there was no bargained-for exchange (ie, consideration) to support his promise. Therefore, no contract arose between the parties, and the court should rule in favor of the man. (Choice A) The mere fact that the friend conferred a benefit on the man by delivering the butterfly collection does not mean that the man has an enforceable obligation to deliver the book to the friend. (Choice B) In the absence of consideration, the doctrine of promissory estoppel provides a basis to enforce a promise upon which a party reasonably and detrimentally relied. However, this doctrine can only be invoked if it is the only way to avoid substantial injustice (not seen here). (Choice C) Instead of giving inadequate consideration, the friend gave no consideration at all since the man did not bargain for her return promise to deliver the butterfly collection. And parties generally cannot challenge a contract on the ground that the consideration given for their promises was inadequate (eg, due to a difference in economic value).","conclusion":"Answer D: For the man, because there was no bargained-for exchange to support his promise."},"questionZh":"一名男子向一位朋友发送了一封电子邮件，其中写道：“因为你一直是我的好朋友，所以我将送给你一本我拥有的稀有书籍。”这位朋友回复了一封电子邮件，其中写道：“感谢您提供这本珍本。我将把我的蝴蝶收藏送给您。”这本善本价值 10,000 美元；蝴蝶收藏价值100美元。朋友将蝴蝶收藏品交给了该男子，但该男子拒绝交付书。如果朋友起诉男子要求追回书的价值，法院该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"对于朋友来说，因为她通过交付蝴蝶收藏品为该男子带来了好处。","B":"对于朋友来说，因为她凭着收到书的理由，把蝴蝶收藏送给了那个男人。","C":"对于男人来说，因为对他的允诺的约因不够。","D":"对于这个人来说，因为没有任何讨价还价的交换来支持他的承诺。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 合同的形成通常需要通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约（即有价值的约因）。除非各方的行为或承诺促使各方愿意签订合同，否则讨价还价的交换要求就不会得到满足。因此，如果任何一方打算赠送礼物，则不能满足此要求。在这里，该男子答应赠送一本善本给他的朋友。这名男子显然是想送礼，因为他并没有要求任何交换。这意味着该男子的承诺并不是由朋友承诺将她的蝴蝶收藏品送给他而诱发的，因此没有讨价还价的交换（即对价）来支持他的承诺。因此，双方之间不存在任何合同，法院应作出有利于该男子的裁决。 （选项 A） 朋友通过交付蝴蝶收藏品给该男子带来好处这一事实并不意味着该男子有将这本书交付给朋友的可执行义务。 （选择B）在没有约因的情况下，允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则为执行一方合理且有害地依赖的承诺提供了基础。然而，只有当它是避免实质性不公正的唯一方法时（此处未见），才能援引该原则。 （选项C） 这位朋友并没有给予足够的考虑，而是根本没有给予任何考虑，因为该男子没有讨价还价，承诺交付蝴蝶收藏品。当事人通常不能以对其承诺的对价不充分（例如，由于经济价值的差异）为由对合同提出质疑。\n\n教育目标：赠送礼物的承诺通常无法执行，因为它不涉及构成有价值对价所需的讨价还价交换。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"赠送礼物的承诺通常无法执行，因为它不涉及构成有价值对价所需的讨价还价交换。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果朋友起诉男子要求追回书的价值，法院该如何判决？","rule":"赠送礼物的承诺通常无法执行，因为它不涉及构成有价值对价所需的讨价还价交换。","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 合同的形成通常需要通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约（即有价值的约因）。除非各方的行为或承诺促使各方愿意签订合同，否则讨价还价的交换要求就不会得到满足。因此，如果任何一方打算赠送礼物，则不能满足此要求。在这里，该男子答应赠送一本善本给他的朋友。这名男子显然是想送礼，因为他并没有要求任何交换。这意味着该男子的承诺并不是由朋友承诺将她的蝴蝶收藏品送给他而诱发的，因此没有讨价还价的交换（即对价）来支持他的承诺。因此，双方之间不存在任何合同，法院应作出有利于该男子的裁决。 （选项 A） 朋友通过交付蝴蝶收藏品给该男子带来好处这一事实并不意味着该男子有将这本书交付给朋友的可执行义务。 （选择B）在没有约因的情况下，允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则为执行一方合理且有害地依赖的承诺提供了基础。然而，只有当它是避免实质性不公正的唯一方法时（此处未见），才能援引该原则。 （选项C） 这位朋友并没有给予足够的考虑，而是根本没有给予任何考虑，因为该男子没有讨价还价，承诺交付蝴蝶收藏品。当事人通常不能以对其承诺的对价不充分（例如，由于经济价值的差异）为由对合同提出质疑。\n\n教育目标：赠送礼物的承诺通常无法执行，因为它不涉及构成有价值对价所需的讨价还价交换。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换）。","conclusion":"答案 D：对于这个人来说，因为没有任何讨价还价的交换来支持他的承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":142,"sourceQuestionPage":419,"sourceQuestionPages":[419],"sourceExplanationPages":[420,421],"sourceAnswerPages":[420,421],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-143","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A restaurant supplier sent a letter to a regular customer offering to sell the customer an industrial freezer for $10,000. Two days later, the customer responded with a letter that stated: \"I accept your offer on the condition that you provide me with a warranty that the freezer is merchantable.\" In response to the customer's letter, the supplier called the customer and stated that the offer was no longer open. The supplier promptly sold the freezer to another buyer for $11,000. If the customer sues the supplier for breach of contract, is the customer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the customer's letter added a term, making it a counteroffer.","B":"No, because the subsequent sale to a bona fide purchaser for value cut off the claims of the customer.","C":"Yes, because the customer's letter was an acceptance of the supplier's offer, since the warranty of merchantability was already implied in the sale.","D":"Yes, because the supplier's letter was a firm offer that could not be revoked for a reasonable time."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the common law, an acceptance is only effective if it is the mirror-image of the terms in the offer. This rule has been relaxed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), which governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, industrial freezers). The UCC uses the battle-of-the-forms rule, where any reasonable manifestation of acceptance—even one including new terms—is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to the new terms. Here, the customer's letter conditioned his acceptance on the freezer's warranty of merchantability. But under the UCC, this warranty is implied in all contracts for the sale of goods by a merchant—ie, one who regularly deals in goods of the kind involved in the contract (like the supplier). Therefore, the letter did not add a term (Choice A). Instead, it was an acceptance of the supplier's offer that created an enforceable contract. And since the supplier breached that contract by selling the freezer to another buyer, the customer is likely to prevail. (Choice B) The sale of the freezer to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who purchased for value and in good faith—may prevent the customer from seeking specific performance from the supplier. However, this would not prevent the buyer from obtaining a legal remedy (ie, damages) for the supplier's breach.\n\n(Choice D) A firm offer is a merchant's signed, written offer to buy or sell goods that promises to hold the offer open for a period of time—not seen here.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, any reasonable manifestation of acceptance (even one including new terms) is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to the new terms.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (implied warranty of merchantability).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, any reasonable manifestation of acceptance (even one including new terms) is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to the new terms.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (implied warranty of merchantability).","rule":"Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, any reasonable manifestation of acceptance (even one including new terms) is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to the new terms.","irac":{"issue":"If the customer sues the supplier for breach of contract, is the customer likely to prevail?","rule":"Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, any reasonable manifestation of acceptance (even one including new terms) is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to the new terms.","analysis":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the common law, an acceptance is only effective if it is the mirror-image of the terms in the offer. This rule has been relaxed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), which governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, industrial freezers). The UCC uses the battle-of-the-forms rule, where any reasonable manifestation of acceptance—even one including new terms—is effective unless it is expressly conditioned on the offeror's assent to the new terms. Here, the customer's letter conditioned his acceptance on the freezer's warranty of merchantability. But under the UCC, this warranty is implied in all contracts for the sale of goods by a merchant—ie, one who regularly deals in goods of the kind involved in the contract (like the supplier). Therefore, the letter did not add a term (Choice A). Instead, it was an acceptance of the supplier's offer that created an enforceable contract. And since the supplier breached that contract by selling the freezer to another buyer, the customer is likely to prevail. (Choice B) The sale of the freezer to a bona fide purchaser—ie, one who purchased for value and in good faith—may prevent the customer from seeking specific performance from the supplier. However, this would not prevent the buyer from obtaining a legal remedy (ie, damages) for the supplier's breach.\n\n(Choice D) A firm offer is a merchant's signed, written offer to buy or sell goods that promises to hold the offer open for a period of time—not seen here.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the customer's letter was an acceptance of the supplier's offer, since the warranty of merchantability was already implied in the sale."},"questionZh":"一家餐厅供应商致函一位老客户，提出以 10,000 美元的价格向该客户出售一台工业冰柜。两天后，客户回信说：“我接受你们的报价，条件是你们向我提供冰箱适销的保证。”针对客户的来信，供应商致电客户并表示不再提供报价。供应商立即以 11,000 美元的价格将冰箱卖给了另一位买家。如果客户起诉供应商违约，客户胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为客户的信中添加了一个术语，使其成为还盘。","B":"不可以，因为随后以价值出售给善意购买者的行为切断了客户的索赔权。","C":"是的，因为客户的信函是对供应商要约的接受，因为销售中已经暗示了适销性的保证。","D":"是的，因为供应商的信函是实盘，在合理时间内无法撤销。"},"explanationZh":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据普通法，只有当承诺与要约条款一致时，承诺才有效。《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 第 2 条放宽了该规则，该法典管辖商品（例如工业冷冻柜）销售合同。 UCC 使用形式之战规则，任何合理的接受表现（即使包括新条款）都是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意新条款为条件。在此，客户的信函以冰箱的适销性默示担保作为其接受的条件。但根据 UCC，这一保证隐含在商人（即经常交易合同中涉及的那种货物的商人（如供应商））的所有货物销售合同中。因此，信中没有添加任何术语（选项A）。相反，接受供应商的报价才创建了可执行的合同。由于供应商将冰箱出售给另一位买家，从而违反了合同，因此客户很可能会获胜。 （选择 B） 将冰箱出售给善意购买者（即出于价值和善意购买的购买者）可能会阻止客户向供应商寻求特定的绩效。然而，这并不妨碍买方就供应商的违约行为获得法律补救（即损害赔偿）。\n\n（选项 D） 实盘是商家签署的购买或出售商品的书面要约，承诺在一段时间内保留该要约 - 此处未见。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 的形式之争规则，任何合理的接受表现（即使包括新条款）都是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意新条款为条件。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。\n\nUCC § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019)（默示适销性默示担保）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC 的形式之争规则，任何合理的接受表现（即使包括新条款）都是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意新条款为条件。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果客户起诉供应商违约，客户胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"根据 UCC 的形式之争规则，任何合理的接受表现（即使包括新条款）都是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意新条款为条件。","analysis":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据普通法，只有当承诺与要约条款一致时，承诺才有效。《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 第 2 条放宽了该规则，该法典管辖商品（例如工业冷冻柜）销售合同。 UCC 使用形式之战规则，任何合理的接受表现（即使包括新条款）都是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意新条款为条件。在此，客户的信函以冰箱的适销性默示担保作为其接受的条件。但根据 UCC，这一保证隐含在商人（即经常交易合同中涉及的那种货物的商人（如供应商））的所有货物销售合同中。因此，信中没有添加任何术语（选项A）。相反，接受供应商的报价才创建了可执行的合同。由于供应商将冰箱出售给另一位买家，从而违反了合同，因此客户很可能会获胜。 （选择 B） 将冰箱出售给善意购买者（即出于价值和善意购买的购买者）可能会阻止客户向供应商寻求特定的绩效。然而，这并不妨碍买方就供应商的违约行为获得法律补救（即损害赔偿）。\n\n（选项 D） 实盘是商家签署的购买或出售商品的书面要约，承诺在一段时间内保留该要约 - 此处未见。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 的形式之争规则，任何合理的接受表现（即使包括新条款）都是有效的，除非明确以要约人同意新条款为条件。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。\n\nUCC § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019)（默示适销性默示担保）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为客户的信函是对供应商要约的接受，因为销售中已经暗示了适销性的保证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"merchantable","ipa":"/'mə:tʃəntəbl/","syllables":"mer·chantable","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 可买卖的, 有销路的；[经] 有销路的, 可销售的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“merchantable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"Two days later, the customer responded with a letter that stated: \"I accept your offer on the condition that you provide me with a warranty that the freezer is merchantable.","synonyms":["marketable","sellable","vendable","vendible"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":143,"sourceQuestionPage":422,"sourceQuestionPages":[422],"sourceExplanationPages":[423,424],"sourceAnswerPages":[423,424],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-144","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A restaurant owner sent a signed order form to a produce supplier that read: \"Please ship us 100 pounds of potatoes at your current price.\" The supplier received the order form on November 9. Later the same day, the supplier mailed the owner a letter with the proper address and postage. The letter stated that the order had been accepted at the supplier's current price for potatoes. On November 10, before receiving the supplier's reply, the owner telephoned the supplier to inform the supplier that the owner had found a closer supplier and was canceling the order. The supplier asked the owner to reconsider, but the owner refused. On November 11, the owner received the supplier's letter. Relying on the owner's telephone call, the supplier never shipped any potatoes to the restaurant. As of November 12, is there an enforceable contract between the supplier and the owner?","choices":{"A":"No, because the owner effectively revoked its offer before it was properly accepted.","B":"No, because the owner's offer could be accepted only by shipment of the goods, and the supplier never shipped the potatoes.","C":"Yes, because the owner's offer was irrevocable for a reasonable time.","D":"Yes, because the supplier effectively accepted the owner's offer before the owner revoked it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, potatoes) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, a seller can accept a buyer's offer to purchase goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship, or by shipping, the goods. Once accepted, the offer cannot be revoked because a contract has been formed. Here, the restaurant owner sent the produce supplier a signed order form that read: \"Please ship us 100 pounds of potatoes at your current price.\" This was an offer that the supplier could accept by promising to ship, or by shipping, the potatoes (Choice B). On November 9, the supplier mailed the owner an acceptance letter that was effective when sent—not upon receipt.* This means that the owner's attempt to revoke its offer on November 10 was ineffective, and there is an enforceable contract between the parties (Choice A). *An acceptance by mail is generally effective when sent (not upon receipt). But this mailbox rule does not apply to (1) option contracts, which require that the acceptance be received before the offer expires, or (2) offers that specify that acceptance must be received by a certain date. (Choice C) Certain offers are irrevocable. This includes firm offers—ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that the offer will be held open for the time stated in the offer or a reasonable time (not to exceed 90 days). No firm offer was made here, however, so the owner could revoke its offer at any time prior to acceptance.\n\nEducational objective: An offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment can be accepted by promising to ship (or by shipping) the goods. Once the offer has been accepted, it cannot be revoked because a contract has already been formed.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (offer and acceptance).","educationalObjective":"An offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment can be accepted by promising to ship (or by shipping) the goods. Once the offer has been accepted, it cannot be revoked because a contract has already been formed.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (offer and acceptance).","rule":"An offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment can be accepted by promising to ship (or by shipping) the goods. Once the offer has been accepted, it cannot be revoked because a contract has already been formed.","irac":{"issue":"As of November 12, is there an enforceable contract between the supplier and the owner?","rule":"An offer to buy goods for prompt or immediate shipment can be accepted by promising to ship (or by shipping) the goods. Once the offer has been accepted, it cannot be revoked because a contract has already been formed.","analysis":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, potatoes) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, a seller can accept a buyer's offer to purchase goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship, or by shipping, the goods. Once accepted, the offer cannot be revoked because a contract has been formed. Here, the restaurant owner sent the produce supplier a signed order form that read: \"Please ship us 100 pounds of potatoes at your current price.\" This was an offer that the supplier could accept by promising to ship, or by shipping, the potatoes (Choice B). On November 9, the supplier mailed the owner an acceptance letter that was effective when sent—not upon receipt.* This means that the owner's attempt to revoke its offer on November 10 was ineffective, and there is an enforceable contract between the parties (Choice A). *An acceptance by mail is generally effective when sent (not upon receipt). But this mailbox rule does not apply to (1) option contracts, which require that the acceptance be received before the offer expires, or (2) offers that specify that acceptance must be received by a certain date. (Choice C) Certain offers are irrevocable. This includes firm offers—ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that the offer will be held open for the time stated in the offer or a reasonable time (not to exceed 90 days). No firm offer was made here, however, so the owner could revoke its offer at any time prior to acceptance.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the supplier effectively accepted the owner's offer before the owner revoked it."},"questionZh":"一家餐馆老板向农产品供应商发送了一份签名的订单，上面写着：“请按当前价格向我们运送 100 磅土豆。”供应商于 11 月 9 日收到订单。当天晚些时候，供应商向业主邮寄了一封信，其中注明了正确的地址和邮资。信中表示，订单已按供应商当前的土豆价格接受。 11月10日，在收到供应商的回复之前，货主打电话给供应商，告知供应商，货主已经找到了更接近的供应商，正在取消订单。供应商要求业主重新约因，但业主拒绝。 11月11日，业主收到供应商的来信。根据店主的电话，供应商从未向餐厅运送任何土豆。截至 11 月 12 日，供应商与业主之间是否存在可执行的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为业主在其报价被正确接受之前实际上撤销了其报价。","B":"不可以，因为业主的报价只能通过发货来接受，而供应商从未发货过土豆。","C":"是的，因为业主的报价在合理时间内是不可撤销的。","D":"是的，因为在业主撤销之前，供应商实际上已经接受了业主的报价。"},"explanationZh":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n商品（例如土豆）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或通过运送货物来接受买方购买货物并立即或立即装运的要约。一旦接受，要约就不能撤销，因为合同已经形成。在这里，餐厅老板向农产品供应商发送了一份签名的订单，上面写着：“请按当前价格向我们运送 100 磅土豆。”供应商可以通过承诺运送或运送土豆来接受这一报价（选择 B）。 11 月 9 日，供应商向业主邮寄了一封接受函，该函在发出后即生效，而非收到后生效。*这意味着业主在 11 月 10 日试图撤销其报价的尝试无效，并且双方之间存在可执行的合同（选择 A）。 *邮寄接受通常在发送时生效（而不是在收到时生效）。但此邮箱规则不适用于 (1) 期权合约，要求在要约到期之前收到接受，或 (2) 指定必须在特定日期之前收到接受的要约。 （选择 C）某些优惠是不可撤销的。这包括确定报价，即商家书面并签署的保证，保证报价将在报价中规定的时间内或合理的时间内（不超过 90 天）保持开放。然而，这里没有提出确定的报价，因此业主可以在接受之前随时撤销其报价。\n\n教育目标：通过承诺运送（或通过运送）货物，可以接受购买并立即或立即运送货物的要约。一旦要约被接受，就不能撤销，因为合同已经形成。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（要约和接受）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"购买商品并立即发货的要约可以通过承诺发货（或通过发货）来接受。一旦要约被接受，就不能撤销，因为合同已经形成。","iracZh":{"issue":"截至 11 月 12 日，供应商与业主之间是否存在可执行的合同？","rule":"购买商品并立即发货的要约可以通过承诺发货（或通过发货）来接受。一旦要约被接受，就不能撤销，因为合同已经形成。","analysis":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n商品（例如土豆）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或通过运送货物来接受买方购买货物并立即或立即装运的要约。一旦接受，要约就不能撤销，因为合同已经形成。在这里，餐厅老板向农产品供应商发送了一份签名的订单，上面写着：“请按当前价格向我们运送 100 磅土豆。”供应商可以通过承诺运送或运送土豆来接受这一报价（选择 B）。 11 月 9 日，供应商向业主邮寄了一封接受函，该函在发出后即生效，而非收到后生效。*这意味着业主在 11 月 10 日试图撤销其报价的尝试无效，并且双方之间存在可执行的合同（选择 A）。 *邮寄接受通常在发送时生效（而不是在收到时生效）。但此邮箱规则不适用于 (1) 期权合约，要求在要约到期之前收到接受，或 (2) 指定必须在特定日期之前收到接受的要约。 （选择 C）某些优惠是不可撤销的。这包括确定报价，即商家书面并签署的保证，保证报价将在报价中规定的时间内或合理的时间内（不超过 90 天）保持开放。然而，这里没有提出确定的报价，因此业主可以在接受之前随时撤销其报价。\n\n教育目标：通过承诺运送（或通过运送）货物，可以接受购买并立即或立即运送货物的要约。一旦要约被接受，就不能撤销，因为合同已经形成。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（要约和接受）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为在业主撤销之前，供应商实际上已经接受了业主的报价。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":144,"sourceQuestionPage":425,"sourceQuestionPages":[425],"sourceExplanationPages":[426,427],"sourceAnswerPages":[426,427],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-145","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"On November 1, an accountant and a lawyer contracted for the sale of the law books the accountant had inherited from his father. The lawyer agreed to pay the purchase price of $10,000 when the accountant delivered the books on December 1. On November 10, the lawyer received a signed letter from the accountant that stated: \"I have decided to dispose of the bookshelves containing the law books you have already purchased. If you want the shelves, I will deliver them to you along with the books on December 1 at no additional cost to you. Let me know before November 15 whether you want them. I will not sell them to anyone else before then.\" On November 14, the lawyer faxed and the accountant received the following message: \"I accept your offer of the shelves.\" The accountant was not a merchant with respect to either law books or bookshelves. Why is the accountant contractually obligated to deliver the shelves?","choices":{"A":"Because the accountant's letter (received by the lawyer on November 10) and the lawyer's fax message of November 14 constituted an effective modification of the original sale-of-books contract.","B":"Because the accountant's letter (received by the lawyer on November 10) waived the bargained-for consideration that would otherwise be required.","C":"Because the lawyer provided a new bargained-for exchange by agreeing to take the shelves.","D":"Because the lawyer's fax message of November 14 operated to rescind unilaterally the original sale-of-books contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, law books) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, an existing contract cannot be modified or rescinded unilaterally (Choice D). Instead, all parties must agree to the modification. Additionally, the modification must be sought in good faith, which requires honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing. But new consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance that was not part of the original contract—is not required. Here, the lawyer contracted to buy law books from the accountant for $10,000. Both parties agreed to modify that contract when the lawyer (1) received the accountant's letter offering to deliver the bookshelves with the books and (2) accepted that offer by fax. There is no indication that either party acted in bad faith. And since new consideration was not required to modify the contract, the accountant is contractually obligated to deliver the books and the shelves (Choice B). (Choice C) The lawyer's agreement to take the shelves did not provide a new bargained-for exchange because the accountant was going to dispose of the shelves anyway. In other words, the accountant was not induced by the lawyer's agreement. But this is immaterial since consideration is not required to modify a sale-of-goods contract.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a contract for the sale of goods can be modified by agreement of all the parties if that modification is sought in good faith. New consideration is not required.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (modification of existing contracts).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a contract for the sale of goods can be modified by agreement of all the parties if that modification is sought in good faith. New consideration is not required.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (modification of existing contracts).","rule":"Under the UCC, a contract for the sale of goods can be modified by agreement of all the parties if that modification is sought in good faith. New consideration is not required.","irac":{"issue":"Why is the accountant contractually obligated to deliver the shelves?","rule":"Under the UCC, a contract for the sale of goods can be modified by agreement of all the parties if that modification is sought in good faith. New consideration is not required.","analysis":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, law books) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, an existing contract cannot be modified or rescinded unilaterally (Choice D). Instead, all parties must agree to the modification. Additionally, the modification must be sought in good faith, which requires honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing. But new consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance that was not part of the original contract—is not required. Here, the lawyer contracted to buy law books from the accountant for $10,000. Both parties agreed to modify that contract when the lawyer (1) received the accountant's letter offering to deliver the bookshelves with the books and (2) accepted that offer by fax. There is no indication that either party acted in bad faith. And since new consideration was not required to modify the contract, the accountant is contractually obligated to deliver the books and the shelves (Choice B). (Choice C) The lawyer's agreement to take the shelves did not provide a new bargained-for exchange because the accountant was going to dispose of the shelves anyway. In other words, the accountant was not induced by the lawyer's agreement. But this is immaterial since consideration is not required to modify a sale-of-goods contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: Because the accountant's letter (received by the lawyer on November 10) and the lawyer's fax message of November 14 constituted an effective modification of the original sale-of-books contract."},"questionZh":"11 月 1 日，一名会计师和一名律师签订了出售该会计师从其父亲那里继承的法律书籍的合同。当会计师于12月1日交付书籍时，律师同意支付10,000美元的购买价。11月10日，律师收到会计师的一封签名信，信中写道：“我已决定处理掉你已经购买的法律书籍的书架。如果你想要书架，我会在12月1日将它们连同书籍一起交付给你，无需额外费用。11月15日之前告诉我你是否想要它们。在此之前我不会将它们卖给任何其他人。”那么。” 11月14日，律师发传真，会计师收到如下信息：“我接受你们的货架报价。”无论是法律书籍还是书架，会计师都不是商人。为什么会计师按照合同有义务交付货架？","choicesZh":{"A":"因为会计师的信函（律师于11月10日收到）和律师11月14日的传真信息构成了对原图书销售合同的有效修改。","B":"因为会计师的信函（律师于 11 月 10 日收到）放弃了本来需要的讨价还价。","C":"因为律师通过同意上架提供了新的讨价还价的交换。","D":"因为11月14日律师的传真信息操作单方面解除了原来的图书销售合同。"},"explanationZh":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品销售合同（例如法律书籍）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，现有合同不能单方面修改或撤销（选择 D）。相反，所有各方都必须同意修改。此外，修改必须出于善意，这需要诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。但不需要新的对价，即不属于原始合同一部分的承诺或履行的讨价还价交换。在这里，律师与会计师签订了合同，以 10,000 美元的价格购买法律书籍。当律师 (1) 收到会计师提出将书架连同书籍一起交付的信函并且 (2) 通过传真接受该提议时，双方同意修改该合同。没有迹象表明任何一方有恶意行为。由于修改合同不需要新的对价，会计师有合同义务交付书籍和书架（选择 B）。 （选项C） 律师同意拿走货架并没有提供新的讨价还价交换，因为会计师无论如何都会处理掉货架。也就是说，会计师并没有受到律师同意的诱导。但这并不重要，因为修改货物销售合同不需要对价。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法典》（UCC），如果真诚地寻求修改，则经各方同意可以修改货物销售合同。不需要新的考虑。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（现有合同的修改）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，如果善意地寻求修改，则经各方同意可以修改货物销售合同。不需要新的考虑。","iracZh":{"issue":"为什么会计师按照合同有义务交付货架？","rule":"根据 UCC，如果善意地寻求修改，则经各方同意可以修改货物销售合同。不需要新的考虑。","analysis":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n商品销售合同（例如法律书籍）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，现有合同不能单方面修改或撤销（选择 D）。相反，所有各方都必须同意修改。此外，修改必须出于善意，这需要诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准。但不需要新的对价，即不属于原始合同一部分的承诺或履行的讨价还价交换。在这里，律师与会计师签订了合同，以 10,000 美元的价格购买法律书籍。当律师 (1) 收到会计师提出将书架连同书籍一起交付的信函并且 (2) 通过传真接受该提议时，双方同意修改该合同。没有迹象表明任何一方有恶意行为。由于修改合同不需要新的对价，会计师有合同义务交付书籍和书架（选择 B）。 （选项C） 律师同意拿走货架并没有提供新的讨价还价交换，因为会计师无论如何都会处理掉货架。也就是说，会计师并没有受到律师同意的诱导。但这并不重要，因为修改货物销售合同不需要对价。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法典》（UCC），如果真诚地寻求修改，则经各方同意可以修改货物销售合同。不需要新的考虑。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-209 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（现有合同的修改）。","conclusion":"答案 A：因为会计师的信函（律师于11月10日收到）和律师11月14日的传真信息构成了对原图书销售合同的有效修改。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":145,"sourceQuestionPage":428,"sourceQuestionPages":[428],"sourceExplanationPages":[429,430],"sourceAnswerPages":[429,430],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-146","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A private high school was in the market for new desks and chairs for its classrooms. It had inquired into the cost of acquiring 1,000 new desks and chairs from a particular vendor. On June 15, the vendor sent a signed letter to the private high school offering to sell 1,000 desks and chairs for $30,000. The letter stated that the private high school's acceptance would be effective only if the vendor actually received it by June 25. On June 23, the private high school mailed its signed, written acceptance of the vendor's offer. On June 24, the vendor, after concluding that the price it originally requested was too low, directly notified the private high school that it was revoking its offer. The vendor received the private high school's acceptance on June 26. Was an enforceable contract between the private high school and the vendor formed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the vendor did not actually receive the acceptance until June 26.","B":"No, because the vendor revoked the offer on June 24.","C":"Yes, because the private high school mailed its acceptance on June 23.","D":"Yes, because under the UCC firm-offer rule, the vendor's offer was irrevocable."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nOnce an offer has been made, a binding contract will be formed if the offeree accepts the offer before it terminates (eg, before the allotted response time expires). Under the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is: permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance—eg, because the offer was communicated by mail—and\n\neffective upon dispatch—ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage.\n\nHowever, the mailbox rule does not apply if it is inconsistent with the offer's terms, such as when the offer states that acceptance is effective only upon receipt.* Here, the vendor's offer stated that an acceptance would be effective only if it was actually received by June 25. Therefore, the mailbox rule does not apply. And since the vendor did not actually receive the private high school's acceptance until June 26—after the allotted time for acceptance had expired—it was ineffective. As a result, no contract was formed. *The reason is that the offeror, as master of the offer, can dictate the manner and means by which the offer may be accepted. (Choices B & D) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, desks, chairs) are governed by the UCC. Under the UCC firm-offer rule, a merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will remain open is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer. The vendor's offer was\n\ntherefore irrevocable through June 25, and the vendor could not revoke it on June 24. Nevertheless, there is no contract because the high school's acceptance was not received in time. (Choice C) Although the private high school mailed its acceptance on June 23, the vendor's offer stated that acceptance would be effective when received—not upon dispatch.\n\nEducational objective: The mailbox rule does not apply if it is inconsistent with the offer's terms, such as when the offer states that acceptance is effective only upon receipt. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (2019) (mailbox rule).","educationalObjective":"The mailbox rule does not apply if it is inconsistent with the offer's terms, such as when the offer states that acceptance is effective only upon receipt. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (2019) (mailbox rule).","rule":"The mailbox rule does not apply if it is inconsistent with the offer's terms, such as when the offer states that acceptance is effective only upon receipt. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"Was an enforceable contract between the private high school and the vendor formed?","rule":"The mailbox rule does not apply if it is inconsistent with the offer's terms, such as when the offer states that acceptance is effective only upon receipt. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nOnce an offer has been made, a binding contract will be formed if the offeree accepts the offer before it terminates (eg, before the allotted response time expires). Under the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is: permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance—eg, because the offer was communicated by mail—and\n\neffective upon dispatch—ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage.\n\nHowever, the mailbox rule does not apply if it is inconsistent with the offer's terms, such as when the offer states that acceptance is effective only upon receipt.* Here, the vendor's offer stated that an acceptance would be effective only if it was actually received by June 25. Therefore, the mailbox rule does not apply. And since the vendor did not actually receive the private high school's acceptance until June 26—after the allotted time for acceptance had expired—it was ineffective. As a result, no contract was formed. *The reason is that the offeror, as master of the offer, can dictate the manner and means by which the offer may be accepted. (Choices B & D) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, desks, chairs) are governed by the UCC. Under the UCC firm-offer rule, a merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will remain open is irrevocable for the time stated in the offer. The vendor's offer was\n\ntherefore irrevocable through June 25, and the vendor could not revoke it on June 24. Nevertheless, there is no contract because the high school's acceptance was not received in time. (Choice C) Although the private high school mailed its acceptance on June 23, the vendor's offer stated that acceptance would be effective when received—not upon dispatch.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the vendor did not actually receive the acceptance until June 26."},"questionZh":"一所私立高中正在市场上为其教室购买新课桌椅。它调查了从特定供应商处购买 1,000 张新课桌椅的成本。 6月15日，卖家向这所私立高中发出了一封签名信，提出以3万美元的价格出售1000张课桌椅。信中指出，私立高中的接受只有在卖方在 6 月 25 日之前实际收到的情况下才生效。6 月 23 日，私立高中邮寄了其签署的卖方报价书面接受书。 6月24日，卖家认为自己最初要求的价格太低后，直接通知私立高中撤销报价。卖方于6月26日收到私立高中的录取通知书。私立高中与卖方之间是否已形成可执行的合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有，因为供应商直到 6 月 26 日才真正收到验收单。","B":"不，因为供应商已于 6 月 24 日撤销了报价。","C":"是的，因为私立高中于 6 月 23 日邮寄了录取通知书。","D":"是的，因为根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），卖方的报价是不可撤销的。"},"explanationZh":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n一旦发出要约，如果受要约人在要约终止之前（例如，在指定的响应时间到期之前）接受要约，则将形成具有约束力的合同。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受是： 如果邮件是传达接受的合理方法（例如，因为要约是通过邮件传达的），则允许\n\n寄出后生效，即在收据上正确注明地址并以已付邮资的方式投递至邮政部门后生效。\n\n但是，如果与要约条款不一致，例如当要约声明接受仅在收到后才生效时，则邮箱规则不适用。*此处，供应商的要约声明只有在 6 月 25 日之前实际收到才有效。因此，邮箱规则不适用。而且，由于卖方直到 6 月 26 日才真正收到私立高中的录取通知书——在规定的录取时间已过之后——所以它是无效的。结果，合同没有成立。 *原因是要约人作为要约的主人，可以决定接受要约的方式和方式。 （选择 B 和 D）商品（例如桌子、椅子）销售合同受 UCC 管辖。根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家书面并签署的保证报价将保持开放状态的保证在报价中规定的时间内不可撤销。供应商的报价是\n\n因此，在 6 月 25 日之前不可撤销，而卖方在 6 月 24 日也无法撤销。尽管如此，由于没有及时收到高中的录取通知书，因此没有签订合同。 （选项 C） 尽管私立高中于 6 月 23 日邮寄了录取通知书，但供应商的要约表明，录取通知书在收到时生效，而不是在发送时生效。\n\n教育目的：如果邮箱规则与要约条款不一致，例如当要约声明接受仅在收到后才生效时，则邮箱规则不适用。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (2019)（邮箱规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果邮箱规则与要约条款不一致，例如当要约声明接受仅在收到后才生效时，则邮箱规则不适用。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"私立高中和供应商之间是否签订了可执行的合同？","rule":"如果邮箱规则与要约条款不一致，例如当要约声明接受仅在收到后才生效时，则邮箱规则不适用。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n一旦发出要约，如果受要约人在要约终止之前（例如，在指定的响应时间到期之前）接受要约，则将形成具有约束力的合同。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受是： 如果邮件是传达接受的合理方法（例如，因为要约是通过邮件传达的），则允许\n\n寄出后生效，即在收据上正确注明地址并以已付邮资的方式投递至邮政部门后生效。\n\n但是，如果与要约条款不一致，例如当要约声明接受仅在收到后才生效时，则邮箱规则不适用。*此处，供应商的要约声明只有在 6 月 25 日之前实际收到才有效。因此，邮箱规则不适用。而且，由于卖方直到 6 月 26 日才真正收到私立高中的录取通知书——在规定的录取时间已过之后——所以它是无效的。结果，合同没有成立。 *原因是要约人作为要约的主人，可以决定接受要约的方式和方式。 （选择 B 和 D）商品（例如桌子、椅子）销售合同受 UCC 管辖。根据 UCC 确定要约规则（firm-offer rule），商家书面并签署的保证报价将保持开放状态的保证在报价中规定的时间内不可撤销。供应商的报价是\n\n因此，在 6 月 25 日之前不可撤销，而卖方在 6 月 24 日也无法撤销。尽管如此，由于没有及时收到高中的录取通知书，因此没有签订合同。 （选项 C） 尽管私立高中于 6 月 23 日邮寄了录取通知书，但供应商的要约表明，录取通知书在收到时生效，而不是在发送时生效。\n\n教育目的：如果邮箱规则与要约条款不一致，例如当要约声明接受仅在收到后才生效时，则邮箱规则不适用。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (2019)（邮箱规则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：没有，因为供应商直到 6 月 26 日才真正收到验收单。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":146,"sourceQuestionPage":431,"sourceQuestionPages":[431],"sourceExplanationPages":[432,433],"sourceAnswerPages":[432,433],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-147","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A niece had worked in her aunt's bookstore for many years. The bookstore business, which was housed in a building that the aunt leased, was independently appraised at $200,000. The aunt decided to retire. She wrote to the niece, expressing her affection for the niece and offering to sell her the bookstore business for $125,000 if the landlord would agree to a transfer of the lease. The letter also specified when the aunt would transfer the business. The niece wrote back accepting her aunt's offer. In a phone call to the niece, the aunt stated that the landlord had approved the transfer of the lease and that she would now ask her attorney to draft a written contract so that there would be a record of the terms. Before the attorney had finished drafting the document, the aunt changed her mind about selling the business and informed the niece of her decision. In an action for breach of contract brought by the niece against her aunt, is the niece likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the motivation for the transfer of the business was the aunt's affection for her niece, not the price.","B":"No, because the promised consideration was inadequate in light of the market value of the business.","C":"Yes, because the condition concerning the landlord's assent to the transfer of the lease was beyond the control of either party.","D":"Yes, because the document being drafted by the attorney was merely a record of an agreement already made, not a condition to it."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract conditions\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, creates party's obligation to precedent perform (e.g., A agrees to hire B if B graduates college)\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses party's existing subsequent obligation to perform (e.g., B agrees to work for C until B graduates college)\n\nA binding contract is formed when one party (ie, the offeror) makes an offer and the other party (ie, the offeree) accepts it. The resulting contract can contain certain conditions affecting the parties' duty to perform: condition precedent – an uncertain future event that, if it occurs, creates a party's duty to perform\n\ncondition subsequent – an uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses a party's existing duty to perform\n\nHere, the niece accepted the aunt's offer to sell the bookstore for $125,000 if the landlord agreed to transfer the lease. This created a condition precedent that triggered the aunt's duty to perform once the landlord agreed to the transfer. The aunt then told the niece that she would ask her attorney to draft a written contract. But this did not create a condition because conditions cannot be added unilaterally after contract formation. Additionally, the parties' agreement to memorialize the contract in a final writing after a binding contract had been formed does not prevent enforcement of their original informal agreement. This is true even if the contemplated writing is never prepared. Therefore, the niece is likely to prevail in her breach of contract action. (Choice A & B) Contracts must be supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained- for exchange of promises or performance. Here, the aunt's promise to sell her business and the niece's promise to pay $125,000 constituted the necessary bargained-for exchange— even if the aunt was partially motivated by affection. And since equivalence in value is not required, it does not matter that the business is worth $200,000. (Choice C) Contracts are often conditioned on events that are beyond the control of either party (eg, the landlord's assent to the transfer of the lease), and this fact has no effect on the outcome of this dispute.\n\nEducational objective: Once a binding contract is formed, the fact that the parties further agree that the contract will be memorialized in a final writing does not prevent enforcement of the original agreement—even if the writing is never prepared.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (existence of contract where written memorial is contemplated).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defining and explaining contract conditions).","educationalObjective":"Once a binding contract is formed, the fact that the parties further agree that the contract will be memorialized in a final writing does not prevent enforcement of the original agreement—even if the writing is never prepared.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 27 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (existence of contract where written memorial is contemplated).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (defining and explaining contract conditions).","rule":"Once a binding contract is formed, the fact that the parties further agree that the contract will be memorialized in a final writing does not prevent enforcement of the original agreement—even if the writing is never prepared.","irac":{"issue":"In an action for breach of contract brought by the niece against her aunt, is the niece likely to prevail?","rule":"Once a binding contract is formed, the fact that the parties further agree that the contract will be memorialized in a final writing does not prevent enforcement of the original agreement—even if the writing is never prepared.","analysis":"Contract conditions\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, creates party's obligation to precedent perform (e.g., A agrees to hire B if B graduates college)\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses party's existing subsequent obligation to perform (e.g., B agrees to work for C until B graduates college)\n\nA binding contract is formed when one party (ie, the offeror) makes an offer and the other party (ie, the offeree) accepts it. The resulting contract can contain certain conditions affecting the parties' duty to perform: condition precedent – an uncertain future event that, if it occurs, creates a party's duty to perform\n\ncondition subsequent – an uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses a party's existing duty to perform\n\nHere, the niece accepted the aunt's offer to sell the bookstore for $125,000 if the landlord agreed to transfer the lease. This created a condition precedent that triggered the aunt's duty to perform once the landlord agreed to the transfer. The aunt then told the niece that she would ask her attorney to draft a written contract. But this did not create a condition because conditions cannot be added unilaterally after contract formation. Additionally, the parties' agreement to memorialize the contract in a final writing after a binding contract had been formed does not prevent enforcement of their original informal agreement. This is true even if the contemplated writing is never prepared. Therefore, the niece is likely to prevail in her breach of contract action. (Choice A & B) Contracts must be supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained- for exchange of promises or performance. Here, the aunt's promise to sell her business and the niece's promise to pay $125,000 constituted the necessary bargained-for exchange— even if the aunt was partially motivated by affection. And since equivalence in value is not required, it does not matter that the business is worth $200,000. (Choice C) Contracts are often conditioned on events that are beyond the control of either party (eg, the landlord's assent to the transfer of the lease), and this fact has no effect on the outcome of this dispute.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the document being drafted by the attorney was merely a record of an agreement already made, not a condition to it."},"questionZh":"一个侄女在姨妈的书店工作了很多年。书店业务位于阿姨租用的一栋大楼内，独立评估为 20 万美元。阿姨决定退休。她写信给侄女，表达了她对侄女的感情，并提出如果房东同意转让租约，她愿意以 125,000 美元的价格将书店业务卖给她。信中还明确了阿姨何时将业务转让。侄女回信接受姨妈的提议。在给侄女的电话中，阿姨表示房东已经批准了租约的转让，她现在将要求她的律师起草一份书面合同，以便记录条款。在律师起草完文件之前，阿姨改变了出售公司的决定，并将她的决定告知了侄女。侄女对姑姑提起违约诉讼，侄女胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不是，因为生意转让的动机是阿姨对侄女的感情，而不是价格。","B":"不可以，因为考虑到企业的市场价值，承诺的对价是不够的。","C":"是的，因为房东同意转让租约的条件超出了任何一方的控制范围。","D":"是的，因为律师起草的文件只是已经达成的协议的记录，而不是协议的条件。"},"explanationZh":"合同条件\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则一方有义务履行先例（例如，如果 B 大学毕业，A 同意雇用 B）\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则可以免除一方现有的后续履行义务（例如，B 同意为 C 工作直至 B 大学毕业）\n\n当一方（即要约人）提出要约且另一方（即受要约人）接受要约时，具有约束力的合同即成立。由此产生的合同可能包含影响双方履行义务的某些条件： 先决条件 – 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则产生一方履行义务\n\n后续条件 – 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则可以免除一方现有的履行义务\n\n在这里，侄女接受了阿姨的提议，如果房东同意转让租约，她将以 125,000 美元的价格出售书店。这就创造了一个先决条件，一旦房东同意转让，就触发阿姨履行义务。阿姨随后告诉侄女，她会请她的律师起草一份书面合同。但这并没有创造条件，因为合同成立后不能单方面添加条件。此外，双方同意在具有约束力的合同签订后以最终书面形式记录合同，并不妨碍执行其最初的非正式协议。即使从未准备好所考虑的写作也是如此。因此，侄女在违约诉讼中很可能胜诉。 （选择 A 和 B） 合同必须得到有价值的对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约。在这里，阿姨出售她的生意的承诺和侄女承诺支付 125,000 美元构成了必要的讨价还价交换——即使阿姨的部分动机是出于感情。由于不要求价值相等，因此业务价值 200,000 美元并不重要。 （选择C） 合同通常以任何一方无法控制的事件为条件（例如，房东同意转让租约），而这一事实对争议的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：一旦形成具有约束力的合同，双方进一步同意以最终书面形式记录该合同这一事实并不妨碍原始协议的执行——即使书面文件从未准备好。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 27（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（考虑书面备忘录的合同的存在）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（定义和解释合同条件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一旦形成具有约束力的合同，双方进一步同意以最终书面形式记录该合同这一事实并不妨碍原始协议的执行——即使该书面文件从未准备好。","iracZh":{"issue":"侄女对姑姑提起违约诉讼，侄女胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"一旦形成具有约束力的合同，双方进一步同意以最终书面形式记录该合同这一事实并不妨碍原始协议的执行——即使该书面文件从未准备好。","analysis":"合同条件\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则一方有义务履行先例（例如，如果 B 大学毕业，A 同意雇用 B）\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则可以免除一方现有的后续履行义务（例如，B 同意为 C 工作直至 B 大学毕业）\n\n当一方（即要约人）提出要约且另一方（即受要约人）接受要约时，具有约束力的合同即成立。由此产生的合同可能包含影响双方履行义务的某些条件： 先决条件 – 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则产生一方履行义务\n\n后续条件 – 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则可以免除一方现有的履行义务\n\n在这里，侄女接受了阿姨的提议，如果房东同意转让租约，她将以 125,000 美元的价格出售书店。这就创造了一个先决条件，一旦房东同意转让，就触发阿姨履行义务。阿姨随后告诉侄女，她会请她的律师起草一份书面合同。但这并没有创造条件，因为合同成立后不能单方面添加条件。此外，双方同意在具有约束力的合同签订后以最终书面形式记录合同，并不妨碍执行其最初的非正式协议。即使从未准备好所考虑的写作也是如此。因此，侄女在违约诉讼中很可能胜诉。 （选择 A 和 B） 合同必须得到有价值的对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约。在这里，阿姨出售她的生意的承诺和侄女承诺支付 125,000 美元构成了必要的讨价还价交换——即使阿姨的部分动机是出于感情。由于不要求价值相等，因此业务价值 200,000 美元并不重要。 （选择C） 合同通常以任何一方无法控制的事件为条件（例如，房东同意转让租约），而这一事实对争议的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：一旦形成具有约束力的合同，双方进一步同意以最终书面形式记录该合同这一事实并不妨碍原始协议的执行——即使书面文件从未准备好。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 27（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（考虑书面备忘录的合同的存在）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（定义和解释合同条件）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为律师起草的文件只是已经达成的协议的记录，而不是协议的条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":147,"sourceQuestionPage":434,"sourceQuestionPages":[434],"sourceExplanationPages":[435,436],"sourceAnswerPages":[435,436],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-148","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A homeowner and a contractor entered into a contract under which the homeowner agreed to pay the contractor $50,000 for remodeling the homeowner's basement according to a set of plans. After the work was completed, the homeowner honestly believed that there were defects in the contractor's work as well as departures from the plans. In fact, the contractor had fully performed. The homeowner offered to pay the contractor $35,000 in full settlement in exchange for the contractor's promise to surrender his entire claim. The contractor accepted the homeowner's offer, and the homeowner paid the contractor $35,000. The reasonable value of the work was $35,000. Is the contractor likely to succeed in an action challenging the validity of the settlement agreement?","choices":{"A":"No, because the homeowner honestly disputed the amount he owed the contractor.","B":"No, because the reasonable value of the work was only $35,000.","C":"Yes, because the contractor reasonably relied on the homeowner's contractual promise to pay the full $50,000, and that promise should be enforced to avoid injustice.","D":"Yes, because the homeowner's payment of $35,000 cannot furnish consideration for the contractor's relinquishment of a claim for $50,000."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nOne method of discharging contractual obligations is by an accord (new contract) and satisfaction (performance of the accord). An accord is created when the parties to a contract agree to give and accept something different in satisfaction of their existing contractual obligations. Since an accord is a new contract, it must be supported by consideration. Where the new consideration is worth less than what was agreed to in the original contract, it will only be sufficient if: there is a good-faith dispute as to the amount owed or the new consideration is of a different type than what was owed under the original contract (eg, goods in lieu of cash). Here, the homeowner offered to pay the contractor $35,000 in full satisfaction of his contractual obligation to pay the contractor $50,000. The contractor agreed to that lesser amount (accord), and the homeowner paid it (satisfaction). Since the homeowner honestly believed that there were defects in the contractor's work and departures from the plans (good-faith dispute), the homeowner's payment of $35,000 furnished sufficient consideration to form a valid accord (Choice D). Therefore, the contractor will likely not succeed. (Choice B) The fact that the reasonable value of the work was only $35,000 does not excuse the homeowner from his obligation to pay the $50,000 contract price. Instead, the homeowner's obligation was discharged by an accord (new contract for $35,000) and satisfaction (payment of the $35,000). (Choice C) The doctrine of promissory estoppel enables a party who reasonably and detrimentally relied on a promise to enforce that promise even though no valid contract was formed. But here, the contractor's reliance on the homeowner's contractual promise is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: An accord requires consideration to be valid. That consideration can only be worth less than what was agreed to in the original contract if (1) there is a good-faith dispute as to the amount owed or (2) the new consideration is of a different type than what was owed under the original contract.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (accord and satisfaction).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that payment of a disputed amount is consideration for a return promise).","educationalObjective":"An accord requires consideration to be valid. That consideration can only be worth less than what was agreed to in the original contract if (1) there is a good-faith dispute as to the amount owed or (2) the new consideration is of a different type than what was owed under the original contract.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (accord and satisfaction).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that payment of a disputed amount is consideration for a return promise).","rule":"An accord requires consideration to be valid. That consideration can only be worth less than what was agreed to in the original contract if (1) there is a good-faith dispute as to the amount owed or (2) the new consideration is of a different type than what was owed under the original contract.","irac":{"issue":"Is the contractor likely to succeed in an action challenging the validity of the settlement agreement?","rule":"An accord requires consideration to be valid. That consideration can only be worth less than what was agreed to in the original contract if (1) there is a good-faith dispute as to the amount owed or (2) the new consideration is of a different type than what was owed under the original contract.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nOne method of discharging contractual obligations is by an accord (new contract) and satisfaction (performance of the accord). An accord is created when the parties to a contract agree to give and accept something different in satisfaction of their existing contractual obligations. Since an accord is a new contract, it must be supported by consideration. Where the new consideration is worth less than what was agreed to in the original contract, it will only be sufficient if: there is a good-faith dispute as to the amount owed or the new consideration is of a different type than what was owed under the original contract (eg, goods in lieu of cash). Here, the homeowner offered to pay the contractor $35,000 in full satisfaction of his contractual obligation to pay the contractor $50,000. The contractor agreed to that lesser amount (accord), and the homeowner paid it (satisfaction). Since the homeowner honestly believed that there were defects in the contractor's work and departures from the plans (good-faith dispute), the homeowner's payment of $35,000 furnished sufficient consideration to form a valid accord (Choice D). Therefore, the contractor will likely not succeed. (Choice B) The fact that the reasonable value of the work was only $35,000 does not excuse the homeowner from his obligation to pay the $50,000 contract price. Instead, the homeowner's obligation was discharged by an accord (new contract for $35,000) and satisfaction (payment of the $35,000). (Choice C) The doctrine of promissory estoppel enables a party who reasonably and detrimentally relied on a promise to enforce that promise even though no valid contract was formed. But here, the contractor's reliance on the homeowner's contractual promise is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the homeowner honestly disputed the amount he owed the contractor."},"questionZh":"一名房主与一名承包商签订了一份合同，房主同意向承包商支付 50,000 美元，用于根据一套计划改造房主的地下室。工程完成后，房主诚实地认为承包商的工程存在缺陷，且与计划有偏差。事实上，承包商已经完全履行了职责。房主提出向承包商支付 35,000 美元作为全额和解金，以换取承包商承诺放弃全部索赔。承包商接受了房主的报价，房主向承包商支付了 35,000 美元。该作品的合理价值为 35,000 美元。承包商是否有可能在质疑和解协议有效性的诉讼中胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为房主诚实地对他欠承包商的金额提出异议。","B":"不，因为该作品的合理价值仅为 35,000 美元。","C":"是的，因为承包商合理地依赖房主的合同承诺支付全额 50,000 美元，并且应该执行该承诺以避免不公正。","D":"是的，因为房主支付的 35,000 美元不能作为承包商放弃 50,000 美元索赔的对价。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n履行合同义务的一种方法是通过协议（新合同）和履行（履行协议）。当合同双方同意给予和接受不同的东西来满足其现有的合同义务时，协议就形成了。由于协议是新合同，因此必须有对价的支持。如果新对价的价值低于原始合同中约定的价值，则仅在以下情况下才足够： 对于所欠金额存在善意争议，或者新对价的类型与原始合同中所欠金额的类型不同（例如，以货物代替现金）。在此，房主提出向承包商支付 35,000 美元，以完全履行其向承包商支付 50,000 美元的合同义务。承包商同意较少的金额（同意），房主支付了费用（满意）。由于房主真诚地认为承包商的工作存在缺陷并且偏离了计划（善意争议），因此房主支付的 35,000 美元提供了形成有效协议的充分约因（选项 D）。因此，承包商很可能不会成功。 （选择 B） 该工程的合理价值仅为 35,000 美元这一事实并不能免除房主支付 50,000 美元合同价的义务。相反，房主的义务是通过协议（35,000 美元的新合同）和清偿（支付 35,000 美元）来解除的。 （选项 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则使得合理且有害地依赖承诺的一方即使没有形成有效的合同，也能够执行该承诺。但在这里，承包商对房主合同承诺的依赖是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：协议需要考虑才有效。只有在以下情况下，该对价的价值才会低于原始合同中约定的价格：(1) 对于所欠金额存在善意争议，或者 (2) 新对价的类型与原始合同中所欠金额的类型不同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 281（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一致和满意）。\n\n合同重述（第二次）§ 74 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释有争议金额的支付是退货承诺的对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"协议需要考虑才有效。只有在以下情况下，该对价的价值才会低于原始合同中约定的价格：(1) 对于所欠金额存在善意争议，或者 (2) 新对价的类型与原始合同中所欠金额的类型不同。","iracZh":{"issue":"承包商是否有可能在质疑和解协议有效性的诉讼中胜诉？","rule":"协议需要考虑才有效。只有在以下情况下，该对价的价值才会低于原始合同中约定的价格：(1) 对于所欠金额存在善意争议，或者 (2) 新对价的类型与原始合同中所欠金额的类型不同。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n履行合同义务的一种方法是通过协议（新合同）和履行（履行协议）。当合同双方同意给予和接受不同的东西来满足其现有的合同义务时，协议就形成了。由于协议是新合同，因此必须有对价的支持。如果新对价的价值低于原始合同中约定的价值，则仅在以下情况下才足够： 对于所欠金额存在善意争议，或者新对价的类型与原始合同中所欠金额的类型不同（例如，以货物代替现金）。在此，房主提出向承包商支付 35,000 美元，以完全履行其向承包商支付 50,000 美元的合同义务。承包商同意较少的金额（同意），房主支付了费用（满意）。由于房主真诚地认为承包商的工作存在缺陷并且偏离了计划（善意争议），因此房主支付的 35,000 美元提供了形成有效协议的充分约因（选项 D）。因此，承包商很可能不会成功。 （选择 B） 该工程的合理价值仅为 35,000 美元这一事实并不能免除房主支付 50,000 美元合同价的义务。相反，房主的义务是通过协议（35,000 美元的新合同）和清偿（支付 35,000 美元）来解除的。 （选项 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则使得合理且有害地依赖承诺的一方即使没有形成有效的合同，也能够执行该承诺。但在这里，承包商对房主合同承诺的依赖是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：协议需要考虑才有效。只有在以下情况下，该对价的价值才会低于原始合同中约定的价格：(1) 对于所欠金额存在善意争议，或者 (2) 新对价的类型与原始合同中所欠金额的类型不同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 281（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一致和满意）。\n\n合同重述（第二次）§ 74 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释有争议金额的支付是退货承诺的对价）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为房主诚实地对他欠承包商的金额提出异议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":148,"sourceQuestionPage":437,"sourceQuestionPages":[437],"sourceExplanationPages":[438,439],"sourceAnswerPages":[438,439],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-149","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A business contracted in writing with a shareholder who owned all of a corporation's outstanding stock to purchase the stock at a specified price per share. At the time this contract was executed, the business's contracting officer said to the shareholder, \"Of course, our commitment to buy is conditioned on our obtaining approval of the contract from our parent company.\" The shareholder replied, \"Fine. No problem.\" The parent company orally approved the contract, but the shareholder changed her mind and refused to consummate the sale on two grounds: (1) when the agreement was made there was no consideration for her promise to sell; and (2) the parent company's approval of the contract was invalid. If the business sues the shareholder for breach of contract, is the business likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because mutuality of obligation between the parties was lacking when the agreement was made.","B":"No, because the condition of the parent company's approval of the contract was an essential part of the agreed exchange and was not in a signed writing.","C":"Yes, because any possible lack of consideration for the shareholder's promise to sell was expressly waived by the shareholder when the agreement was made.","D":"Yes, because the business's promise to buy, bargained for and made in exchange for the shareholder's promise to sell, was good consideration even though it was expressly conditioned on an event that was not certain to occur."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A contract is a legally enforceable agreement that is created when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs Condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs Here, the business promised to purchase, and the shareholder promised to sell, all of the shareholder's stock in a corporation. This exchange of promises provided valuable consideration to form a valid contract. But the parties' performance of that contract was contingent upon the business obtaining approval of the agreement from its parent company—a condition precedent. Since that condition precedent was met when the parent company approved the purchase, the business would likely prevail in a breach-of-contract action based on the shareholder's refusal to sell. *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). (Choices A & C) Mutuality of obligation between the parties was not lacking, and their agreement was supported by consideration, because the business's promise to purchase stock was induced by the shareholder's promise to sell it (and vice versa). (Choice B) There is no rule requiring that a condition be included in a signed writing.\n\nEducational objective: A contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or excuse (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","educationalObjective":"A contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or excuse (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","references":"","rule":"A contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or excuse (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","irac":{"issue":"If the business sues the shareholder for breach of contract, is the business likely to prevail?","rule":"A contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or excuse (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A contract is a legally enforceable agreement that is created when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs Condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs Here, the business promised to purchase, and the shareholder promised to sell, all of the shareholder's stock in a corporation. This exchange of promises provided valuable consideration to form a valid contract. But the parties' performance of that contract was contingent upon the business obtaining approval of the agreement from its parent company—a condition precedent. Since that condition precedent was met when the parent company approved the purchase, the business would likely prevail in a breach-of-contract action based on the shareholder's refusal to sell. *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). (Choices A & C) Mutuality of obligation between the parties was not lacking, and their agreement was supported by consideration, because the business's promise to purchase stock was induced by the shareholder's promise to sell it (and vice versa). (Choice B) There is no rule requiring that a condition be included in a signed writing.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the business's promise to buy, bargained for and made in exchange for the shareholder's promise to sell, was good consideration even though it was expressly conditioned on an event that was not certain to occur."},"questionZh":"与拥有公司所有已发行股票的股东签订书面合同的企业，以指定的每股价格购买股票。在签署这份合同时，该企业的签约官员对股东说：“当然，我们承诺购买的条件是我们获得母公司批准该合同。”股东回答：“好，没问题。”母公司口头同意了该合同，但股东却改变了主意，拒绝完成出售，理由有二：（一）协议签订时没有考虑其出售的承诺； （二）母公司批准的合同无效。如果企业起诉股东违约，企业胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不能，因为协议签订时双方之间缺乏相互义务。","B":"不可以，因为母公司批准合同的条件是约定交换的重要组成部分，并且没有以书面形式签署。","C":"是的，因为任何可能缺乏对股东承诺出售的考虑的情况都已在协议签订时被股东明确放弃。","D":"是的，因为企业的购买承诺、讨价还价并换取股东的出售承诺，是很好的考虑因素，尽管它明确以不确定发生的事件为条件。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 合同是一种具有法律效力的协议，当相互协议得到有价值的约因（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约）的支持时，该协议就成立。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生 后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生则免除履行 此处，企业承诺购买公司股东的所有股票，而股东承诺出售公司的所有股东股票。这种承诺的交换为形成有效的合同提供了宝贵的对价。但双方对该合同的履行取决于该企业获得其母公司对该协议的批准——这是一个先决条件。由于母公司批准收购时已满足该先决条件，因此该业务很可能会因股东拒绝出售而提起违约诉讼。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。 （选项A和C）双方之间并不缺乏相互义务，而且他们的协议得到了对价的支持，因为企业购买股票的承诺是由股东出售股票的承诺诱导的（反之亦然）。 （选择 B） 没有规则要求签署的书面材料中必须包含条件。\n\n教育目标：合同通常在双方协议得到有价值对价的支持时成立，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或借口（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同通常在双方协议得到有价值对价的支持时成立，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或借口（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果企业起诉股东违约，企业胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"合同通常在双方协议得到有价值对价的支持时成立，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或借口（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n执行（或承诺执行）法律不要求的行为，或克制（或承诺克制）执行法律允许的行为 合同是一种具有法律效力的协议，当相互协议得到有价值的约因（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约）的支持时，该协议就成立。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生 后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生则免除履行 此处，企业承诺购买公司股东的所有股票，而股东承诺出售公司的所有股东股票。这种承诺的交换为形成有效的合同提供了宝贵的对价。但双方对该合同的履行取决于该企业获得其母公司对该协议的批准——这是一个先决条件。由于母公司批准收购时已满足该先决条件，因此该业务很可能会因股东拒绝出售而提起违约诉讼。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。 （选项A和C）双方之间并不缺乏相互义务，而且他们的协议得到了对价的支持，因为企业购买股票的承诺是由股东出售股票的承诺诱导的（反之亦然）。 （选择 B） 没有规则要求签署的书面材料中必须包含条件。\n\n教育目标：合同通常在双方协议得到有价值对价的支持时成立，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或借口（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为企业的购买承诺、讨价还价并换取股东的出售承诺，是很好的考虑因素，尽管它明确以不确定发生的事件为条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":149,"sourceQuestionPage":440,"sourceQuestionPages":[440],"sourceExplanationPages":[441,442],"sourceAnswerPages":[441,442],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-150","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A general contractor about to bid on a construction job for an office building invited a carpenter and several others to bid on the carpentry work. The carpenter agreed to bid if the general contractor would agree to give the carpenter the job provided that his bid was lowest and the general contractor was awarded the main contract. The general contractor so agreed. The carpenter, incurring time and expense in preparing his bid, submitted the lowest carpentry bid. The general contractor used the carpenter's bid in calculating its own bid, which was successful. Which of the following best supports the carpenter's position that the general contractor is obligated to award the carpentry subcontract to the carpenter?","choices":{"A":"The carpenter detrimentally relied on the general contractor's conditional promise in preparing his bid.","B":"The carpenter gave consideration for the general contractor's conditional promise to award the carpentry subcontract to the carpenter.","C":"The general contractor has an implied duty to deal fairly and in good faith with all bidders whose bids the general contractor used in calculating its main bid to the building owner.","D":"The general contractor has an obligation to the owner of the building to subcontract with the carpenter because the carpenter's bid was used in calculating the general contractor's bid, and the carpenter is an intended beneficiary of that obligation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A contract is generally formed when an exchange of promises is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. However, performance under that agreement can be made contingent upon a condition. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent – where a party's duty to perform arises upon the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an uncertain future event (ie, the event creates the duty)\n\nCondition subsequent – where a party's duty to perform is released upon the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an uncertain future event (ie, the event extinguishes the duty)\n\nHere, the carpenter offered to bid on the carpentry work in exchange for the contractor's promise to award the carpenter the subcontract. This exchange of promises provided valuable consideration to form a binding agreement. The contractor's performance of that agreement was contingent upon the carpenter submitting the lowest bid and the contractor receiving the main contract. Since these conditions precedent occurred, the contractor was obligated to award the subcontract to the carpenter. (Choice A) The doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. A contract was formed here, so the carpenter's reliance is irrelevant. (Choice C) The duty of good faith and fair dealing is implied in every contract—but not bids and other mere offers. Therefore, this duty would extend to the carpenter, but not other bidders with whom no contract was formed. (Choice D) An intended beneficiary is a person who is intentionally given an advantage under a contract to which the person is not a party and may therefore enforce those contractual obligations. Here, the carpenter's bid was used in the contractor and building owner's agreement. But since there is no evidence that this agreement was intended to benefit the carpenter, he is merely an incidental beneficiary who has no right to enforce the agreement.\n\nEducational objective: A contract is generally formed when an exchange of promises is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or extinguish (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange as consideration).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 447 (conditions precedent).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 453 (conditions subsequent).","educationalObjective":"A contract is generally formed when an exchange of promises is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or extinguish (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 71 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (bargained-for exchange as consideration).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 447 (conditions precedent).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 453 (conditions subsequent).","rule":"A contract is generally formed when an exchange of promises is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or extinguish (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best supports the carpenter's position that the general contractor is obligated to award the carpentry subcontract to the carpenter?","rule":"A contract is generally formed when an exchange of promises is supported by valuable consideration and may include conditions that delay (condition precedent) or extinguish (condition subsequent) a party's promised performance.","analysis":"Elements of valuable consideration\n\nBargained-for Each party's willingness to enter agreement must be induced by exchange other party's act/promise\n\nLegal detriment Each party must relinquish legal right by either:\n\nperforming (or promising to perform) act that is not legally required or refraining (or promising to refrain) from performing legally permissible act A contract is generally formed when an exchange of promises is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. However, performance under that agreement can be made contingent upon a condition. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent – where a party's duty to perform arises upon the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an uncertain future event (ie, the event creates the duty)\n\nCondition subsequent – where a party's duty to perform is released upon the occurrence or nonoccurrence of an uncertain future event (ie, the event extinguishes the duty)\n\nHere, the carpenter offered to bid on the carpentry work in exchange for the contractor's promise to award the carpenter the subcontract. This exchange of promises provided valuable consideration to form a binding agreement. The contractor's performance of that agreement was contingent upon the carpenter submitting the lowest bid and the contractor receiving the main contract. Since these conditions precedent occurred, the contractor was obligated to award the subcontract to the carpenter. (Choice A) The doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. A contract was formed here, so the carpenter's reliance is irrelevant. (Choice C) The duty of good faith and fair dealing is implied in every contract—but not bids and other mere offers. Therefore, this duty would extend to the carpenter, but not other bidders with whom no contract was formed. (Choice D) An intended beneficiary is a person who is intentionally given an advantage under a contract to which the person is not a party and may therefore enforce those contractual obligations. Here, the carpenter's bid was used in the contractor and building owner's agreement. But since there is no evidence that this agreement was intended to benefit the carpenter, he is merely an incidental beneficiary who has no right to enforce the agreement.","conclusion":"Answer B: The carpenter gave consideration for the general contractor's conditional promise to award the carpentry subcontract to the carpenter."},"questionZh":"一位总承包商准备投标一栋办公楼的建筑工程，邀请一名木匠和其他几人投标木工工程。如果总承包商同意将这份工作交给木匠，则木匠同意投标，前提是他的出价最低，并且总承包商获得了主合同。总承包商同意了。木匠在准备投标时花费了时间和费用，提交了最低的木工投标。总承包商用木匠的报价来计算自己的报价，结果成功了。以下哪一项最能支持木匠的立场，即总承包商有义务将木工分包合同授予木匠？","choicesZh":{"A":"木匠在准备投标时不利地依赖了总承包商的有条件承诺。","B":"木匠提供了约因，以支持总承包商将木工分包合同授予木匠的有条件承诺。","C":"总承包商有隐含的义务，以公平和诚信的方式与所有投标人进行交易，总承包商在计算其向业主提出的主要投标时使用了这些投标人的投标。","D":"总承包商对建筑物业主有义务与木匠分包，因为木匠的投标用于计算总承包商的投标，而木匠是该义务的预期受益人。"},"explanationZh":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n履行（或承诺履行）法律不要求的行为或不履行（或承诺不履行）法律允许的行为 当承诺交换得到有价值的约因的支持时（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行），合同通常成立。然而，该协议下的履行可以视条件而定。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件 – 当不确定的未来事件发生或不发生时，一方的履行义务产生（即，该事件产生了义务）\n\n后续条件——当不确定的未来事件发生或不发生时，一方履行的义务被解除（即该事件消除了义务）\n\n在这里，木匠提出对木工工作进行投标，以换取承包商承诺将分包合同授予木匠。这种交换承诺为形成具有约束力的协议提供了有价值的约因。承包商履行该协议取决于木匠提交最低出价和承包商收到主合同。由于这些先决条件已经发生，承包商有义务将分包合同授予木匠。 （选项 A） 禁止反言原则允许当事人在未形成有效合同的情况下执行其合理且有害地依赖的承诺。合同在这里成立，所以木匠的依赖是无关紧要的。 （选项 C）每一份合同都隐含着诚实信用和公平交易的义务，但出价和其他纯粹的报价则不然。因此，这项义务将延伸至木匠，但不适用于未与其签订合同的其他投标人。 （选项 D）预期受益人是指在非合同当事人的合同下故意获得利益并因此可以执行这些合同义务的人。在这里，木匠的出价被用于承包商和业主的协议中。但由于没有证据表明该协议是为了使木匠受益，因此木匠只是一个偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），无权执行该协议。\n\n教育目标：合同通常在有价值的约因支持的承诺交换中形成，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或消除（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换作为对价）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 447（先决条件）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 453（随后的条件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同通常在有价值的约因支持的承诺交换时成立，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或消除（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最能支持木匠的立场，即总承包商有义务将木工分包合同授予木匠？","rule":"合同通常在有价值的约因支持的承诺交换时成立，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或消除（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。","analysis":"有价值的约因因素\n\n讨价还价 各方达成协议的意愿必须通过交换另一方的行为/承诺来诱导\n\n法律损害 各方必须通过以下任一方式放弃合法权利：\n\n履行（或承诺履行）法律不要求的行为或不履行（或承诺不履行）法律允许的行为 当承诺交换得到有价值的约因的支持时（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行），合同通常成立。然而，该协议下的履行可以视条件而定。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件 – 当不确定的未来事件发生或不发生时，一方的履行义务产生（即，该事件产生了义务）\n\n后续条件——当不确定的未来事件发生或不发生时，一方履行的义务被解除（即该事件消除了义务）\n\n在这里，木匠提出对木工工作进行投标，以换取承包商承诺将分包合同授予木匠。这种交换承诺为形成具有约束力的协议提供了有价值的约因。承包商履行该协议取决于木匠提交最低出价和承包商收到主合同。由于这些先决条件已经发生，承包商有义务将分包合同授予木匠。 （选项 A） 禁止反言原则允许当事人在未形成有效合同的情况下执行其合理且有害地依赖的承诺。合同在这里成立，所以木匠的依赖是无关紧要的。 （选项 C）每一份合同都隐含着诚实信用和公平交易的义务，但出价和其他纯粹的报价则不然。因此，这项义务将延伸至木匠，但不适用于未与其签订合同的其他投标人。 （选项 D）预期受益人是指在非合同当事人的合同下故意获得利益并因此可以执行这些合同义务的人。在这里，木匠的出价被用于承包商和业主的协议中。但由于没有证据表明该协议是为了使木匠受益，因此木匠只是一个偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary），无权执行该协议。\n\n教育目标：合同通常在有价值的约因支持的承诺交换中形成，并且可能包括延迟（先决条件）或消除（后续条件）一方承诺履行的条件。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 71（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（讨价还价交换作为对价）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 447（先决条件）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 453（随后的条件）。","conclusion":"答案 B：木匠提供了约因，以支持总承包商将木工分包合同授予木匠的有条件承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":150,"sourceQuestionPage":443,"sourceQuestionPages":[443],"sourceExplanationPages":[444,445],"sourceAnswerPages":[444,445],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-151","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"On June 1, a wholesaler received a purchase-order form from a retailer that was a new customer, in which the retailer ordered 1,000 anti-recoil widgets for delivery no later than August 30 at a delivered total price of $10,000, as quoted in the wholesaler's current catalog. Both parties are merchants with respect to widgets of all types. On June 2, the wholesaler mailed to the retailer its own form, across the top of which the wholesaler's president had written, \"We are pleased to accept your order.\" This form contained the same terms as the retailer's form except for an additional printed clause in the wholesaler's form that provided for a maximum liability of $100 for any breach of contract by the wholesaler. As of June 5, when the retailer received the wholesaler's acceptance form, which of the following is an accurate statement concerning the legal relationship between the retailer and wholesaler?","choices":{"A":"There is an enforceable contract whose terms do not include the liability-limitation clause in the wholesaler's form.","B":"There is an enforceable contract whose terms include the liability-limitation clause in the wholesaler's form, because liquidation of damages is expressly authorized by the Uniform Commercial Code.","C":"There is no contract, because the liability-limitation clause in the wholesaler's form is a material alteration of the retailer's offer.","D":"There is no contract, because the retailer did not consent to the liability-limitation clause in the wholesaler's form."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), which follows the battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an enforceable contract is created once there has been an offer and a reasonable manifestation of acceptance (even one that includes new terms). But in a contract between merchants, the new terms will fall away—ie, will not become part of the contract—if: the offer expressly required assent to new terms (and the offeror does not assent) the offeror objects within a reasonable time or the new terms materially alter the contract (eg, change a party's risks or remedies). Here, the retailer sent a purchase order for 1,000 widgets. The wholesaler then mailed an acceptance form, which created an enforceable contract despite containing an additional clause limiting the wholesaler's liability for breach to $100 (Choice C). The retailer's order (ie, offer) did not expressly require consent to new terms, and the retailer did not object to the new term. But since the new term materially alters the contract by limiting the retailer's remedy for breach, the contract does not include the liability-limitation clause. (Choice B) The UCC expressly authorizes liquidation of damages—ie, contractually stipulating to a reasonable estimate of the actual damages to be recovered if one party\n\nbreaches. But $100 is not a reasonable estimate for a $10,000 sales contract. And even if it were, the new liability-limitation clause still materially alters the contract. (Choice D) Had the retailer's offer expressly required assent to new terms, the retailer's failure to consent would prevent the new term from becoming part of the contract. But it would not prevent contract formation.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even if it contains new terms. But in a contract between merchants, the new terms fall away if (1) the offer expressly required assent to new terms and no assent was given, (2) the offeror objects within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even if it contains new terms. But in a contract between merchants, the new terms fall away if (1) the offer expressly required assent to new terms and no assent was given, (2) the offeror objects within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019) (battle-of-the-forms rule).","rule":"Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even if it contains new terms. But in a contract between merchants, the new terms fall away if (1) the offer expressly required assent to new terms and no assent was given, (2) the offeror objects within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.","irac":{"issue":"As of June 5, when the retailer received the wholesaler's acceptance form, which of the following is an accurate statement concerning the legal relationship between the retailer and wholesaler?","rule":"Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule, an acceptance is effective even if it contains new terms. But in a contract between merchants, the new terms fall away if (1) the offer expressly required assent to new terms and no assent was given, (2) the offeror objects within a reasonable time, or (3) the new terms materially alter the contract.","analysis":"Effect of new terms in reply to offer\n\nCommon law Offer rejected Reply treated as counteroffer (mirror-image)\n\nUCC ≥1 party is Offer accepted unless reply expressly required nonmerchant assent to new/revised terms (battle of the New/revised terms treated as proposed additions forms) to contract All parties are Offer accepted unless reply expressly required merchants assent to new/revised terms New terms become part of contract unless: offer expressly required assent to new terms new terms materially alter contract or offeror objects within reasonable time Revised terms follow split authority & are either: treated as new terms or cancelled out under knockout rule UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC), which follows the battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an enforceable contract is created once there has been an offer and a reasonable manifestation of acceptance (even one that includes new terms). But in a contract between merchants, the new terms will fall away—ie, will not become part of the contract—if: the offer expressly required assent to new terms (and the offeror does not assent) the offeror objects within a reasonable time or the new terms materially alter the contract (eg, change a party's risks or remedies). Here, the retailer sent a purchase order for 1,000 widgets. The wholesaler then mailed an acceptance form, which created an enforceable contract despite containing an additional clause limiting the wholesaler's liability for breach to $100 (Choice C). The retailer's order (ie, offer) did not expressly require consent to new terms, and the retailer did not object to the new term. But since the new term materially alters the contract by limiting the retailer's remedy for breach, the contract does not include the liability-limitation clause. (Choice B) The UCC expressly authorizes liquidation of damages—ie, contractually stipulating to a reasonable estimate of the actual damages to be recovered if one party\n\nbreaches. But $100 is not a reasonable estimate for a $10,000 sales contract. And even if it were, the new liability-limitation clause still materially alters the contract. (Choice D) Had the retailer's offer expressly required assent to new terms, the retailer's failure to consent would prevent the new term from becoming part of the contract. But it would not prevent contract formation.","conclusion":"Answer A: There is an enforceable contract whose terms do not include the liability-limitation clause in the wholesaler's form."},"questionZh":"6月1日，一家批发商收到一家新客户零售商的采购订单，其中零售商订购了1,000个防后坐力小部件，并在8月30日之前交货，交货总价为10,000美元，如批发商当前目录中的报价。双方都是所有类型小部件的商人。 6 月 2 日，批发商向零售商邮寄了自己的表格，批发商总裁在表格顶部写道：“我们很高兴接受您的订单。”该表格包含与零售商表格相同的条款，但批发商表格中的附加打印条款规定批发商违反合同的最高责任为 100 美元。截至6月5日，零售商收到批发商受理单时，关于零售商与批发商之间法律关系，下列哪一项是准确的表述？","choicesZh":{"A":"有一份可执行的合同，其条款不包括批发商表格中的责任限制条款。","B":"有一个可执行的合同，其条款包括批发商形式的责任限制条款，因为损害赔偿的清算是由《统一商法典》（UCC）明确授权的。","C":"没有合同，因为批发商表格中的责任限制条款是对零售商报价的重大变更。","D":"没有合同，因为零售商不同意批发商表格中的责任限制条款。"},"explanationZh":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖，该条遵循合同订立的形式斗争规则。根据这一规则，一旦提出要约并合理地表示接受（即使是包含新条款的合同），一份可执行的合同即告成立。但在商家之间的合同中，如果出现以下情况，新条款将失效，即不会成为合同的一部分： 要约明确要求同意新条款（而要约人不同意） 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或者新条款实质性地改变了合同（例如，改变了一方的风险或补救措施）。此处，零售商发送了 1,000 个小部件的采购订单。然后，批发商邮寄了一份接受表格，该表格创建了一份可执行的合同，尽管其中包含将批发商的违约责任限制为 100 美元的附加条款（选项 C）。零售商的订单（即报价）没有明确要求同意新条款，零售商也没有反对新条款。但由于新条款限制了零售商对违约行为的补救措施，从而对合同进行了重大修改，因此合同中不包括责任限制条款。 （选择 B）UCC 明确授权损害赔偿的清算，即在合同中规定，如果一方\n\n违规行为。但对于价值 10,000 美元的销售合同来说，100 美元并不是一个合理的估计。即使是这样，新的责任限制条款仍然对合同产生了重大改变。 （选项 D）如果零售商的报价明确要求同意新条款，则零售商不同意将导致新条款无法成为合同的一部分。但这不会阻止合同的形成。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 的形式之战规则，即使包含新术语，接受也是有效的。但在商家之间的合同中，如果出现以下情况，新条款将失效：(1) 要约明确要求同意新条款但未给予同意；(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对；或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC 的形式之战规则，即使包含新条款，接受也是有效的。但在商家之间的合同中，如果出现以下情况，新条款将失效：(1) 要约明确要求同意新条款但未给予同意；(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对；或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。","iracZh":{"issue":"截至6月5日，零售商收到批发商受理单时，关于零售商与批发商之间法律关系，下列哪一项是准确的表述？","rule":"根据 UCC 的形式之战规则，即使包含新条款，接受也是有效的。但在商家之间的合同中，如果出现以下情况，新条款将失效：(1) 要约明确要求同意新条款但未给予同意；(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对；或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。","analysis":"新条款对要约回复的影响\n\n普通法要约被拒绝 回复被视为还盘（镜像）\n\nUCC ≥1 方被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 非商户同意新的/修订的条款（新的/修订的条款之争被视为拟议的补充形式）合同 所有各方都被接受要约，除非明确要求答复 商户同意新的/修订的条款 新条款成为合同的一部分，除非： 明确要求要约同意新条款 新条款在合理时间内实质性地改变合同或要约人对象 修订后的条款遵循分割授权并且要么：被视为新条款或被取消根据淘汰规则 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖，该条遵循合同订立的形式斗争规则。根据这一规则，一旦提出要约并合理地表示接受（即使是包含新条款的合同），一份可执行的合同即告成立。但在商家之间的合同中，如果出现以下情况，新条款将失效，即不会成为合同的一部分： 要约明确要求同意新条款（而要约人不同意） 要约人在合理时间内提出反对，或者新条款实质性地改变了合同（例如，改变了一方的风险或补救措施）。此处，零售商发送了 1,000 个小部件的采购订单。然后，批发商邮寄了一份接受表格，该表格创建了一份可执行的合同，尽管其中包含将批发商的违约责任限制为 100 美元的附加条款（选项 C）。零售商的订单（即报价）没有明确要求同意新条款，零售商也没有反对新条款。但由于新条款限制了零售商对违约行为的补救措施，从而对合同进行了重大修改，因此合同中不包括责任限制条款。 （选择 B）UCC 明确授权损害赔偿的清算，即在合同中规定，如果一方\n\n违规行为。但对于价值 10,000 美元的销售合同来说，100 美元并不是一个合理的估计。即使是这样，新的责任限制条款仍然对合同产生了重大改变。 （选项 D）如果零售商的报价明确要求同意新条款，则零售商不同意将导致新条款无法成为合同的一部分。但这不会阻止合同的形成。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC 的形式之战规则，即使包含新术语，接受也是有效的。但在商家之间的合同中，如果出现以下情况，新条款将失效：(1) 要约明确要求同意新条款但未给予同意；(2) 要约人在合理时间内提出反对；或 (3) 新条款对合同有重大改变。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2019）（形式之战规则）。","conclusion":"答案 A：有一份可执行的合同，其条款不包括批发商表格中的责任限制条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"liquidation","ipa":"/lˌɪkwɪdˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"liq·ui·da·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 偿还, 偿付, 清算；[化] 液化； 溶离法","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“liquidation”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:liquidations","sourceSentence":"There is an enforceable contract whose terms include the liability-limitation clause in the wholesaler's form, because liquidation of damages is expressly authorized by the Uniform Commercial Code.","synonyms":["elimination","settlement","extermination"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":151,"sourceQuestionPage":446,"sourceQuestionPages":[446],"sourceExplanationPages":[447,448],"sourceAnswerPages":[447,448],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-152","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A producer of small engines contracted to supply 5,000 engines per month of a specified model to a lawn mower manufacturer for the next three years at an agreed-upon price. The producer sent the manufacturer a form quotation that included a standard \"boilerplate\" indemnification clause requiring the manufacturer to indemnify the producer for losses arising from the transaction. The producer's form also included a statute-of-limitations provision requiring that claims arising from defective engines be brought within one year of delivery. The manufacturer subsequently ordered engines using a form requiring the producer to indemnify the manufacturer for any loss arising from engine defects and specifying a three- year statute of limitations for claims arising from the engines. Two years later, the manufacturer discovered that 20% of the engines delivered in the first year were defective and filed suit against the producer seeking indemnification. The jurisdiction has adopted the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) gap-filling provisions, which include a default four-year statute of limitations. Is the manufacturer likely to prevail in its suit against the producer for losses arising from the defective engines?","choices":{"A":"No, because a contract is only enforceable under the mirror-image rule if there is a meeting of the minds on all terms.","B":"No, because the producer is master of the offer and therefore the contractual terms in its form quotation control.","C":"Yes, because the producer failed to object to the conflicting terms when it accepted the manufacturer's purchase order.","D":"Yes, because the UCC operates to replace the conflicting statutes of limitations with its four-year statute of limitations."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, engines) are governed by the UCC. Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation, an acceptance is effective even if it contains revised (ie, conflicting) terms that are different from the offer. In contracts between merchants, the revised terms are canceled out by the knockout rule (adopted in most jurisdictions). When gaps are created after applying the knockout rule, the court uses the UCC's gap-filling provisions to patch the holes. Here, the manufacturer brought suit after two years. The producer's form quotation (offer) had specified a one-year statute of limitations for defective-engine claims, while the manufacturer's order form (acceptance) specified a three-year statute of limitations. Both parties are merchants, so the conflicting statute-of-limitations terms are canceled out by the knockout rule. Since the UCC operates to replace those terms with its four-year statute of limitations, the manufacturer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) Under the common law mirror-image rule, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that includes revised or new terms is treated as both a rejection of the original offer and a counteroffer. But since this rule only applies to services and real estate contracts, it does not apply here. (Choice B) The offeror, as master of the offer, can dictate the manner and means of acceptance. Here, however, the producer's (offeror's) form quotation did not require the manufacturer to accept the one-year statute of limitations provision for an acceptance to be effective. (Choice C) A few jurisdictions do not follow the knockout rule and instead treat a revised term in an acceptance as a \"new\" term. New terms are automatically included in a contract between merchants unless, inter alia,* the offeror objects within a reasonable time. But this minority rule does not apply here. Other exceptions to the automatic inclusion of new terms arise when (1) the term materially alters the original contract or (2) the offer expressly limited acceptance to its terms.\n\nEducational objective: In contracts between merchants, revised (ie, conflicting) terms in the offer and acceptance are canceled out under the knockout rule and replaced with UCC gap-filling provisions.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (new terms in acceptance or confirmation).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-725 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of limitations in contracts for sale).","educationalObjective":"In contracts between merchants, revised (ie, conflicting) terms in the offer and acceptance are canceled out under the knockout rule and replaced with UCC gap-filling provisions.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (new terms in acceptance or confirmation).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-725 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (statute of limitations in contracts for sale).","rule":"In contracts between merchants, revised (ie, conflicting) terms in the offer and acceptance are canceled out under the knockout rule and replaced with UCC gap-filling provisions.","irac":{"issue":"Is the manufacturer likely to prevail in its suit against the producer for losses arising from the defective engines?","rule":"In contracts between merchants, revised (ie, conflicting) terms in the offer and acceptance are canceled out under the knockout rule and replaced with UCC gap-filling provisions.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, engines) are governed by the UCC. Under the UCC's battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation, an acceptance is effective even if it contains revised (ie, conflicting) terms that are different from the offer. In contracts between merchants, the revised terms are canceled out by the knockout rule (adopted in most jurisdictions). When gaps are created after applying the knockout rule, the court uses the UCC's gap-filling provisions to patch the holes. Here, the manufacturer brought suit after two years. The producer's form quotation (offer) had specified a one-year statute of limitations for defective-engine claims, while the manufacturer's order form (acceptance) specified a three-year statute of limitations. Both parties are merchants, so the conflicting statute-of-limitations terms are canceled out by the knockout rule. Since the UCC operates to replace those terms with its four-year statute of limitations, the manufacturer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) Under the common law mirror-image rule, an acceptance must mirror the terms of the offer. A purported acceptance that includes revised or new terms is treated as both a rejection of the original offer and a counteroffer. But since this rule only applies to services and real estate contracts, it does not apply here. (Choice B) The offeror, as master of the offer, can dictate the manner and means of acceptance. Here, however, the producer's (offeror's) form quotation did not require the manufacturer to accept the one-year statute of limitations provision for an acceptance to be effective. (Choice C) A few jurisdictions do not follow the knockout rule and instead treat a revised term in an acceptance as a \"new\" term. New terms are automatically included in a contract between merchants unless, inter alia,* the offeror objects within a reasonable time. But this minority rule does not apply here. Other exceptions to the automatic inclusion of new terms arise when (1) the term materially alters the original contract or (2) the offer expressly limited acceptance to its terms.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the UCC operates to replace the conflicting statutes of limitations with its four-year statute of limitations."},"questionZh":"一家小型发动机生产商与一家割草机制造商签订了合同，每月按约定价格向一家割草机制造商供应 5,000 台特定型号的发动机。生产商向制造商发送了一份报价单，其中包括标准的“样板”赔偿条款，要求制造商赔偿生产商因交易而造成的损失。生产商的表格还包括一项时效条款，要求因发动机缺陷引起的索赔应在交付后一年内提出。制造商随后订购了发动机，并使用了一份表格，要求生产商赔偿制造商因发动机缺陷而造成的任何损失，并规定了因发动机引起的索赔的三年时效期限。两年后，制造商发现第一年交付的发动机中有 20% 有缺陷，并向生产商提起诉讼，寻求赔偿。该司法管辖区采用了《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 的填补空白条款，其中包括默认的四年诉讼时效。制造商就发动机缺陷造成的损失向生产商提起诉讼是否可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为只有在所有条款都达成共识的情况下，合同才可以根据镜像规则执行。","B":"不，因为生产者是报价的主人，因此合同条款的报价形式是控制的。","C":"是的，因为生产商在接受制造商的采购订单时未能反对冲突条款。","D":"是的，因为 UCC 的作用是用四年的诉讼时效取代相互冲突的诉讼时效。"},"explanationZh":"货物（例如发动机）销售合同受 UCC 管辖。根据 UCC 合同形成形式之争规则，即使接受包含与要约不同的修订（即冲突）条款，接受也是有效的。在商家之间的合同中，修订后的条款被淘汰规则（大多数司法管辖区采用）取消。当应用淘汰规则后出现空白时，法院将使用 UCC 的空白填补条款来修补漏洞。两年后，制造商提起诉讼。生产商的报价单（要约）规定了有缺陷的发动机索赔的一年时效期限，而制造商的订单（接受）则规定了三年的时效期限。双方都是商人，因此相互冲突的时效条款被淘汰规则所抵消。由于 UCC 会用四年的诉讼时效来取代这些条款，因此制造商很可能会胜诉。 （选项 A） 根据普通法镜像规则，承诺必须反映要约条款。包含修订或新条款的声称接受将被视为拒绝原始要约和还盘。但由于该规则仅适用于服务和房地产合同，因此不适用于此处。 （选择B）要约人作为要约的主人，可以决定要约的方式和方式。然而，此处生产商（要约人）的报价并不要求制造商接受一年的诉讼时效条款，以使接受生效。 （选项C）一些司法管辖区不遵循淘汰规则，而是将接受中的修订条款视为“新”条款。新条款将自动包含在商家之间的合同中，除非要约人在合理时间内提出反对。但这个少数派规则在这里并不适用。自动包含新条款的其他例外情况出现在以下情况：(1) 该条款对原始合同有重大改变，或 (2) 要约明确限制对其条款的接受。\n\n教育目标：在商家之间的合同中，要约和接受中的修订（即冲突）条款在淘汰规则下被取消，并被 UCC 填补条款所取代。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（接受或确认中的新条款）。\n\nUCC § 2-725 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（销售合同的时效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在商家之间的合同中，要约和接受中修订的（即冲突的）条款根据淘汰规则被取消，并由 UCC 填补条款所取代。","iracZh":{"issue":"制造商就发动机缺陷造成的损失向生产商提起诉讼是否可能胜诉？","rule":"在商家之间的合同中，要约和接受中修订的（即冲突的）条款根据淘汰规则被取消，并由 UCC 填补条款所取代。","analysis":"货物（例如发动机）销售合同受 UCC 管辖。根据 UCC 合同形成形式之争规则，即使接受包含与要约不同的修订（即冲突）条款，接受也是有效的。在商家之间的合同中，修订后的条款被淘汰规则（大多数司法管辖区采用）取消。当应用淘汰规则后出现空白时，法院将使用 UCC 的空白填补条款来修补漏洞。两年后，制造商提起诉讼。生产商的报价单（要约）规定了有缺陷的发动机索赔的一年时效期限，而制造商的订单（接受）则规定了三年的时效期限。双方都是商人，因此相互冲突的时效条款被淘汰规则所抵消。由于 UCC 会用四年的诉讼时效来取代这些条款，因此制造商很可能会胜诉。 （选项 A） 根据普通法镜像规则，承诺必须反映要约条款。包含修订或新条款的声称接受将被视为拒绝原始要约和还盘。但由于该规则仅适用于服务和房地产合同，因此不适用于此处。 （选择B）要约人作为要约的主人，可以决定要约的方式和方式。然而，此处生产商（要约人）的报价并不要求制造商接受一年的诉讼时效条款，以使接受生效。 （选项C）一些司法管辖区不遵循淘汰规则，而是将接受中的修订条款视为“新”条款。新条款将自动包含在商家之间的合同中，除非要约人在合理时间内提出反对。但这个少数派规则在这里并不适用。自动包含新条款的其他例外情况出现在以下情况：(1) 该条款对原始合同有重大改变，或 (2) 要约明确限制对其条款的接受。\n\n教育目标：在商家之间的合同中，要约和接受中的修订（即冲突）条款在淘汰规则下被取消，并被 UCC 填补条款所取代。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-207 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（接受或确认中的新条款）。\n\nUCC § 2-725 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（销售合同的时效）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为 UCC 的作用是用四年的诉讼时效取代相互冲突的诉讼时效。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-3123522f62b158ce"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":152,"sourceQuestionPage":449,"sourceQuestionPages":[449],"sourceExplanationPages":[450,451],"sourceAnswerPages":[450,451],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-153","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A store owner mailed a signed order to a warehouse that read: \"Please ship us 10,000 widgets at your current price.\" The warehouse manager received the order on January 7 and that same day mailed to the store owner a properly stamped, addressed, and signed letter stating that the order was accepted at the warehouse's current price of $10 per widget. On January 8, before receipt of the manager's letter, the store owner telephoned the manager and said, \"I hereby revoke my order.\" The manager protested to no avail. The store owner received the manager's letter on January 9. Because of the store owner's January 8 telephone message, the warehouse never shipped the goods. Under the relevant and prevailing rules, is there a contract between the store owner and the warehouse as of January 10?","choices":{"A":"No, because the order was an offer that could be accepted only by shipping the goods, and the offer was effectively revoked before shipment.","B":"No, because the store owner never effectively agreed to the $10 price term.","C":"Yes, because the order was an offer that the warehouse effectively accepted before the store owner attempted to revoke it.","D":"Yes, because the order was, for a reasonable time, an irrevocable offer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). Once an offer has been made, a contract will be formed if the offeree accepts the offer before it is revoked. Under the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is: permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance—eg, when the offer was communicated by mail and\n\neffective upon dispatch—ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage.\n\nHere, the warehouse manager received the store owner's order for 10,000 widgets on January 7. Since that offer was communicated by mail, the warehouse manager could reasonably accept by mail or by shipping the goods (Choice A). Therefore, the warehouse manager effectively accepted the offer when he mailed a properly stamped and addressed acceptance letter on January 7—before the store owner tried to revoke the offer on January 8. (Choice B) A sale-of-goods contract can be formed even if the price is to be set by just one of the parties. Therefore, the store owner did effectively agree to the $10 price when he ordered widgets at the manufacturer's \"current price.\"\n\n(Choice D) Certain types of offers are irrevocable, including firm offers—ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will be held open for a specified period of time (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Acceptance by mail is (1) permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance (eg, if the offer was communicated by mail) and (2) effective upon dispatch (ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage).\n\nReferences\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019) (mailbox rule).","educationalObjective":"Acceptance by mail is (1) permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance (eg, if the offer was communicated by mail) and (2) effective upon dispatch (ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage).","references":"17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019) (mailbox rule).","rule":"Acceptance by mail is (1) permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance (eg, if the offer was communicated by mail) and (2) effective upon dispatch (ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage).","irac":{"issue":"Under the relevant and prevailing rules, is there a contract between the store owner and the warehouse as of January 10?","rule":"Acceptance by mail is (1) permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance (eg, if the offer was communicated by mail) and (2) effective upon dispatch (ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage).","analysis":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). Once an offer has been made, a contract will be formed if the offeree accepts the offer before it is revoked. Under the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is: permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance—eg, when the offer was communicated by mail and\n\neffective upon dispatch—ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage.\n\nHere, the warehouse manager received the store owner's order for 10,000 widgets on January 7. Since that offer was communicated by mail, the warehouse manager could reasonably accept by mail or by shipping the goods (Choice A). Therefore, the warehouse manager effectively accepted the offer when he mailed a properly stamped and addressed acceptance letter on January 7—before the store owner tried to revoke the offer on January 8. (Choice B) A sale-of-goods contract can be formed even if the price is to be set by just one of the parties. Therefore, the store owner did effectively agree to the $10 price when he ordered widgets at the manufacturer's \"current price.\"\n\n(Choice D) Certain types of offers are irrevocable, including firm offers—ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that an offer will be held open for a specified period of time (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the order was an offer that the warehouse effectively accepted before the store owner attempted to revoke it."},"questionZh":"一位店主向仓库邮寄了一份签名订单，上面写着：“请按照当前价格向我们运送 10,000 个小部件。”仓库经理于 1 月 7 日收到订单，并于当天向店主邮寄了一封盖有适当印章、地址和签名的信件，表明仓库已按每个小部件 10 美元的当前价格接受订单。 1月8日，在收到经理的信之前，店主打电话给经理说：“我特此撤销订单。”经理抗议无果。店主1月9日收到经理的来信，因店主1月8日电话留言，仓库一直没有发货。根据相关现行规则，截至1月10日，店主与仓库之间是否有合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该订单是只有发货后才能接受的要约，而该要约在发货前已被有效撤销。","B":"不，因为店主从未真正同意 10 美元的价格条款。","C":"是的，因为在店主试图撤销该订单之前，仓库实际上已经接受了该订单。","D":"是的，因为该订单在合理时间内是不可撤销的要约。"},"explanationZh":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，合同的成立需要双方同意（即要约和接受）。一旦发出要约，如果受要约人在撤销要约之前接受要约，则合同成立。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受是： 如果邮件是传达接受的合理方法，则允许 - 例如，当要约通过邮件传达并且\n\n寄出后生效，即在收据上正确注明地址并以已付邮资的方式投递至邮政部门后生效。\n\n在此，仓库经理于 1 月 7 日收到了店主的 10,000 个小部件的订单。由于该报价是通过邮件传达的，因此仓库经理可以合理地通过邮寄或运送货物来接受（选择 A）。因此，仓库经理在 1 月 7 日邮寄了一封盖有正确邮票并注明地址的接受函时，实际上接受了要约——然后店主在 1 月 8 日试图撤销要约。（选择 B）即使价格仅由其中一方确定，也可以签订货物销售合同。因此，当店主以制造商的“当前价格”订购小部件时，他实际上同意了 10 美元的价格。\n\n（选项 D）某些类型的优惠是不可撤销的，包括确定优惠，即商家书面并签署的保证，优惠将在指定的时间内保留（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标： (1) 如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法（例如，如果要约是通过邮件传达的），则允许通过邮寄接受；(2) 在发送后生效（即，当接受正确写明地址并以已付邮资的方式交付给邮政服务时）。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019)（邮箱规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"通过邮寄接受是 (1) 如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法（例如，如果要约是通过邮件传达的），并且 (2) 在发送后生效（即，当接受正确写明地址并以已付邮资的方式交付给邮政服务时）。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据相关现行规则，截至1月10日，店主与仓库之间是否有合同？","rule":"通过邮寄接受是 (1) 如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法（例如，如果要约是通过邮件传达的），并且 (2) 在发送后生效（即，当接受正确写明地址并以已付邮资的方式交付给邮政服务时）。","analysis":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，合同的成立需要双方同意（即要约和接受）。一旦发出要约，如果受要约人在撤销要约之前接受要约，则合同成立。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受是： 如果邮件是传达接受的合理方法，则允许 - 例如，当要约通过邮件传达并且\n\n寄出后生效，即在收据上正确注明地址并以已付邮资的方式投递至邮政部门后生效。\n\n在此，仓库经理于 1 月 7 日收到了店主的 10,000 个小部件的订单。由于该报价是通过邮件传达的，因此仓库经理可以合理地通过邮寄或运送货物来接受（选择 A）。因此，仓库经理在 1 月 7 日邮寄了一封盖有正确邮票并注明地址的接受函时，实际上接受了要约——然后店主在 1 月 8 日试图撤销要约。（选择 B）即使价格仅由其中一方确定，也可以签订货物销售合同。因此，当店主以制造商的“当前价格”订购小部件时，他实际上同意了 10 美元的价格。\n\n（选项 D）某些类型的优惠是不可撤销的，包括确定优惠，即商家书面并签署的保证，优惠将在指定的时间内保留（此处未显示）。\n\n教育目标： (1) 如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法（例如，如果要约是通过邮件传达的），则允许通过邮寄接受；(2) 在发送后生效（即，当接受正确写明地址并以已付邮资的方式交付给邮政服务时）。\n\n参考文献\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019)（邮箱规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为在店主试图撤销该订单之前，仓库实际上已经接受了该订单。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":153,"sourceQuestionPage":452,"sourceQuestionPages":[452],"sourceExplanationPages":[453,454],"sourceAnswerPages":[453,454],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-154","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A tile floor installer sent a general contractor this fax: \"Will install all marble tile flooring in new office building, per owner's specs, for $50,000 if you accept within reasonable time after main contract awarded.\" Three competitors sent the general contractor similar bids to install marble tile floor in the office building in the respective amounts of $55,000, $58,000, and $60,000. The general contractor used the tile floor installer's $50,000 figure in preparing and submitting her own sealed bid on the office building. Before the bids were opened, the tile floor installer truthfully advised the general contractor that the former's sub-bid had been based on a $5,000 computational error and was therefore revoked. Shortly thereafter, the general contractor was awarded the office building construction contract and subsequently contracted with a competitor to install the marble floors for a price of $55,000. The general contractor then sued the tile floor installer to recover $5,000. Which of the following, if proved, would best support the tile floor installer's defense?","choices":{"A":"Before submitting her own bid, the general contractor had reason to suspect that the tile floor installer had made a computational mistake in figuring its sub-bid.","B":"Even after paying $55,000 for the marble floor installation, the general contractor would make a net profit of $150,000 on the office building contract.","C":"The general contractor gave the tile floor installer no consideration for an irrevocable sub-bid.","D":"The tile floor installer's sub-bid expressly requested the general contractor's acceptance after awarding of the main contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nUnder the doctrine of promissory estoppel (ie, detrimental reliance), an offer is treated as an option contract and is therefore irrevocable for a reasonable period of time if: the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance the offeree reasonably relied on the offer and that reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment. This is true even if no consideration (eg, money) was given to support the offer's irrevocability (Choice C). Here, the tile floor installer knew that the general contractor would factor the installer's sub-bid into her own bid on the main contract. However, if the general contractor had reason to suspect that the installer had made a mistake in computing its sub-bid, her\n\nreliance on that sub-bid would have been unreasonable. Therefore, this fact, if proved, would best support the tile floor installer's defense. (Choice B) The fact that the general contractor would make a $150,000 profit on the main contract even after the tile floor installer revoked its sub-bid (ie, offer) would not support the installer's defense. That is because the general contractor would have made a $155,000 profit had the installer not revoked. (Choice D) Although the tile floor installer's sub-bid expressly requested the general contractor's acceptance after awarding of the main contract, the general contractor could still recover if she reasonably relied on that bid before acceptance (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 87 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (option contract by detrimental reliance).","educationalObjective":"An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 87 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (option contract by detrimental reliance).","rule":"An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following, if proved, would best support the tile floor installer's defense?","rule":"An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.","analysis":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nUnder the doctrine of promissory estoppel (ie, detrimental reliance), an offer is treated as an option contract and is therefore irrevocable for a reasonable period of time if: the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance the offeree reasonably relied on the offer and that reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment. This is true even if no consideration (eg, money) was given to support the offer's irrevocability (Choice C). Here, the tile floor installer knew that the general contractor would factor the installer's sub-bid into her own bid on the main contract. However, if the general contractor had reason to suspect that the installer had made a mistake in computing its sub-bid, her\n\nreliance on that sub-bid would have been unreasonable. Therefore, this fact, if proved, would best support the tile floor installer's defense. (Choice B) The fact that the general contractor would make a $150,000 profit on the main contract even after the tile floor installer revoked its sub-bid (ie, offer) would not support the installer's defense. That is because the general contractor would have made a $155,000 profit had the installer not revoked. (Choice D) Although the tile floor installer's sub-bid expressly requested the general contractor's acceptance after awarding of the main contract, the general contractor could still recover if she reasonably relied on that bid before acceptance (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer A: Before submitting her own bid, the general contractor had reason to suspect that the tile floor installer had made a computational mistake in figuring its sub-bid."},"questionZh":"瓷砖地板安装商向总承包商发送了这样一份传真：“如果您在授予主合同后的合理时间内接受，将按照业主的规格在新办公楼安装所有大理石瓷砖地板，价格为 50,000 美元。”三个竞争对手向总承包商发出了类似的投标，要求在办公楼安装大理石瓷砖地板，金额分别为 55,000 美元、58,000 美元和 60,000 美元。总承包商使用瓷砖地板安装商的 50,000 美元来准备和提交她自己的办公楼密封投标。在开标前，瓷砖地板安装商如实告知总承包商，前者的分标是基于5,000美元的计算错误，因此被撤销。此后不久，总承包商获得了办公楼建设合同，随后与竞争对手签订了安装大理石地板的合同，价格为 55,000 美元。总承包商随后起诉瓷砖地板安装商，要求赔偿 5,000 美元。如果得到证实，以下哪一项最能支持瓷砖地板安装人员的辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"在提交自己的投标之前，总承包商有理由怀疑瓷砖地板安装商在计算分标时犯了计算错误。","B":"即使支付了 55,000 美元的大理石地板安装费用，总承包商也能在办公楼合同中获得 150,000 美元的净利润。","C":"总承包商没有考虑瓷砖地板安装商不可撤销的分标。","D":"瓷砖地板安装商的分标明确要求总承包商在授予主合同后接受。"},"explanationZh":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则（即有害信赖），要约被视为期权合同，因此在合理时间内不可撤销，如果：要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖，受要约人合理地依赖要约，并且该信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损害。即使没有考虑（例如金钱）来支持要约的不可撤销性（选项 C），情况也是如此。在这里，瓷砖地板安装人员知道总承包商会将安装人员的分标纳入她自己的主合同投标中。然而，如果总承包商有理由怀疑安装商在计算其分价时犯了错误，她的\n\n依赖该次级投标是不合理的。因此，这一事实如果得到证实，将最好地支持瓷砖地板安装商的辩护。 （选择B）即使在瓷砖地板安装商撤销其分标（即报价）之后，总承包商仍将从主合同中获得150,000 美元的利润，这一事实不能支持安装商的抗辩。这是因为，如果安装商没有被撤销，总承包商将获得 155,000 美元的利润。 （选项D）虽然瓷砖地板安装商的分标在主合同授予后明确要求总承包商接受，但如果总承包商在接受之前合理地依赖该投标，仍然可以获得赔偿（此处未见）。\n\n教育目标：要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此不可撤销，如果 (1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的依赖，(2) 受要约人合理依赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 87（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有害依赖的期权合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此在以下情况下不可撤销：(1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖，(2) 受要约人合理地信赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果得到证实，以下哪一项最能支持瓷砖地板安装人员的辩护？","rule":"要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此在以下情况下不可撤销：(1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖，(2) 受要约人合理地信赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。","analysis":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则（即有害信赖），要约被视为期权合同，因此在合理时间内不可撤销，如果：要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖，受要约人合理地依赖要约，并且该信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损害。即使没有考虑（例如金钱）来支持要约的不可撤销性（选项 C），情况也是如此。在这里，瓷砖地板安装人员知道总承包商会将安装人员的分标纳入她自己的主合同投标中。然而，如果总承包商有理由怀疑安装商在计算其分价时犯了错误，她的\n\n依赖该次级投标是不合理的。因此，这一事实如果得到证实，将最好地支持瓷砖地板安装商的辩护。 （选择B）即使在瓷砖地板安装商撤销其分标（即报价）之后，总承包商仍将从主合同中获得150,000 美元的利润，这一事实不能支持安装商的抗辩。这是因为，如果安装商没有被撤销，总承包商将获得 155,000 美元的利润。 （选项D）虽然瓷砖地板安装商的分标在主合同授予后明确要求总承包商接受，但如果总承包商在接受之前合理地依赖该投标，仍然可以获得赔偿（此处未见）。\n\n教育目标：要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此不可撤销，如果 (1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的依赖，(2) 受要约人合理依赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 87（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有害依赖的期权合同）。","conclusion":"答案 A：在提交自己的投标之前，总承包商有理由怀疑瓷砖地板安装商在计算分标时犯了计算错误。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":154,"sourceQuestionPage":455,"sourceQuestionPages":[455],"sourceExplanationPages":[456,457],"sourceAnswerPages":[456,457],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-155","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Breach, Cure & Installments","question":"A fruit vendor ordered from an orchard 500 bushels of No. 1 Royal Fuzz peaches, at a specified price, \"for prompt shipment.\" The orchard promptly shipped 500 bushels but by mistake shipped No. 2 Royal Fuzz peaches instead of No. 1. The error in shipment was caused by the negligence of the orchard's shipping clerk. Which of the following best states the fruit vendor's rights and duties upon delivery of the peaches?","choices":{"A":"Although a contract was formed when the orchard shipped the peaches, the fruit vendor does not have to accept them.","B":"The fruit vendor must accept the peaches because a contract was formed when the orchard shipped them.","C":"The orchard's shipment of the peaches was a counteroffer and the fruit vendor can refuse to accept them.","D":"The orchard's shipment of the peaches was a counteroffer but, since peaches are perishable, the fruit vendor, if it does not want to accept them, must reship the peaches to the orchard in order to mitigate its losses."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of goods (e.g., peaches) are governed by Article 2 of the UCC. Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods for prompt shipment can be accepted by: promising to ship the goods shipping goods that conform to the order or shipping nonconforming goods without notice that they are being offered only as an accommodation.* Any of these methods of acceptance create a contract. However, the shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation also constitutes an immediate breach of the contract. In that case, the buyer may (1) accept or reject the goods and (2) sue the seller for breach. Here, the fruit vendor ordered 500 bushels of No. 1 Royal Fuzz peaches for prompt shipment, but the orchard shipped nonconforming No. 2 Royal Fuzz peaches with no notice of accommodation. Although this shipment created a contract, it also constituted a breach of that contract (Choice B). Therefore, the vendor does not have to accept the peaches. *Conversely, the shipment of nonconforming goods with notice that they are being offered only as an accommodation (i.e., notice of accommodation) constitutes a rejection of the offer and a counteroffer that the buyer can accept or reject. (Choices C & D) The orchard's shipment of nonconforming peaches was not a counteroffer because there was no notice of accommodation. In addition, a merchant who rightfully rejects perishable goods (e.g., peaches) must mitigate losses by making reasonable efforts to sell the goods on the market—not reship them to the original seller.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods for prompt shipment can be accepted by (1) promising to ship the goods, (2) shipping conforming goods, or (3) shipping nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation. However, the shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation is also a breach of the resulting contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (explaining that a shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation constitutes an acceptance and breach).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods for prompt shipment can be accepted by (1) promising to ship the goods, (2) shipping conforming goods, or (3) shipping nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation. However, the shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation is also a breach of the resulting contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-206 (explaining that a shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation constitutes an acceptance and breach).","rule":"Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods for prompt shipment can be accepted by (1) promising to ship the goods, (2) shipping conforming goods, or (3) shipping nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation. However, the shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation is also a breach of the resulting contract.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the fruit vendor's rights and duties upon delivery of the peaches?","rule":"Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods for prompt shipment can be accepted by (1) promising to ship the goods, (2) shipping conforming goods, or (3) shipping nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation. However, the shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation is also a breach of the resulting contract.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of goods (e.g., peaches) are governed by Article 2 of the UCC. Under the UCC, an offer to buy goods for prompt shipment can be accepted by: promising to ship the goods shipping goods that conform to the order or shipping nonconforming goods without notice that they are being offered only as an accommodation.* Any of these methods of acceptance create a contract. However, the shipment of nonconforming goods without a notice of accommodation also constitutes an immediate breach of the contract. In that case, the buyer may (1) accept or reject the goods and (2) sue the seller for breach. Here, the fruit vendor ordered 500 bushels of No. 1 Royal Fuzz peaches for prompt shipment, but the orchard shipped nonconforming No. 2 Royal Fuzz peaches with no notice of accommodation. Although this shipment created a contract, it also constituted a breach of that contract (Choice B). Therefore, the vendor does not have to accept the peaches. *Conversely, the shipment of nonconforming goods with notice that they are being offered only as an accommodation (i.e., notice of accommodation) constitutes a rejection of the offer and a counteroffer that the buyer can accept or reject. (Choices C & D) The orchard's shipment of nonconforming peaches was not a counteroffer because there was no notice of accommodation. In addition, a merchant who rightfully rejects perishable goods (e.g., peaches) must mitigate losses by making reasonable efforts to sell the goods on the market—not reship them to the original seller.","conclusion":"Answer A: Although a contract was formed when the orchard shipped the peaches, the fruit vendor does not have to accept them."},"questionZh":"一位水果摊贩以指定价格从果园订购了 500 蒲式耳 1 号皇家绒毛桃子，“以便及时发货”。果园及时发货了500蒲式耳，但错误地发货了2号皇家绒毛桃，而不是1号。这次发货错误是由于果园发货员的疏忽造成的。以下哪一项最好地说明了水果供应商在交付桃子时的权利和义务？","choicesZh":{"A":"虽然果园运桃子时已经签订了合同，但水果商并不一定要接受。","B":"水果商必须接受桃子，因为果园运送桃子时就已经签订了合同。","C":"果园运送桃子属于还价，水果商可以拒绝接受。","D":"果园运桃子是还价，但由于桃子容易腐烂，果商如果不接受，就必须将桃子重新运回果园，以减少损失。"},"explanationZh":"货物销售合同（例如桃子）受 UCC 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，购买货物并立即发货的要约可以通过以下方式接受：承诺发货、发货符合订单的货物或发货不合格的货物，而不通知它们仅作为住宿提供。*任何这些接受方法都会形成合同。然而，在未附替代交付通知的情况下运送不合格货物也构成立即违约。在这种情况下，买方可以 (1) 接受或拒绝货物以及 (2) 起诉卖方违约。这里，水果商订购了 500 蒲式耳 1 号皇家绒毛桃，以便立即发货，但果园在没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice）的情况下运送了不合格的 2 号皇家绒毛桃。尽管这批货物构成了合同，但也构成了对合同的违约（选项B）。因此，商贩不必接受桃子。 *相反，如果装运不合格货物并通知其仅作为住宿（即替代交付通知（accommodation notice）），则构成对要约的拒绝以及买方可以接受或拒绝的还盘。 （选择C&D）果园装运不合格桃子，因没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice），不属于还盘。此外，正确拒绝易腐烂商品（例如桃子）的商人必须通过合理努力在市场上出售商品来减轻损失，而不是将其重新运送给原始卖家。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，购买货物并立即装运的要约可以通过以下方式被接受：(1) 承诺装运货物，(2) 装运符合要求的货物，或 (3) 在没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice）的情况下装运不符合要求的货物。然而，在未附替代交付通知的情况下运送不合格货物也构成对由此产生的合同的违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206（解释了在没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice）的情况下运送不合格货物构成接受和违约）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，购买货物并立即装运的要约可以通过以下方式被接受：(1) 承诺装运货物，(2) 装运合格货物，或 (3) 装运不合格货物而不提供舱位通知。然而，在未附替代交付通知的情况下运送不合格货物也构成对由此产生的合同的违约。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最好地说明了水果供应商在交付桃子时的权利和义务？","rule":"根据 UCC，购买货物并立即装运的要约可以通过以下方式被接受：(1) 承诺装运货物，(2) 装运合格货物，或 (3) 装运不合格货物而不提供舱位通知。然而，在未附替代交付通知的情况下运送不合格货物也构成对由此产生的合同的违约。","analysis":"货物销售合同（例如桃子）受 UCC 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，购买货物并立即发货的要约可以通过以下方式接受：承诺发货、发货符合订单的货物或发货不合格的货物，而不通知它们仅作为住宿提供。*任何这些接受方法都会形成合同。然而，在未附替代交付通知的情况下运送不合格货物也构成立即违约。在这种情况下，买方可以 (1) 接受或拒绝货物以及 (2) 起诉卖方违约。这里，水果商订购了 500 蒲式耳 1 号皇家绒毛桃，以便立即发货，但果园在没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice）的情况下运送了不合格的 2 号皇家绒毛桃。尽管这批货物构成了合同，但也构成了对合同的违约（选项B）。因此，商贩不必接受桃子。 *相反，如果装运不合格货物并通知其仅作为住宿（即替代交付通知（accommodation notice）），则构成对要约的拒绝以及买方可以接受或拒绝的还盘。 （选择C&D）果园装运不合格桃子，因没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice），不属于还盘。此外，正确拒绝易腐烂商品（例如桃子）的商人必须通过合理努力在市场上出售商品来减轻损失，而不是将其重新运送给原始卖家。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，购买货物并立即装运的要约可以通过以下方式被接受：(1) 承诺装运货物，(2) 装运符合要求的货物，或 (3) 在没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice）的情况下装运不符合要求的货物。然而，在未附替代交付通知的情况下运送不合格货物也构成对由此产生的合同的违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206（解释了在没有替代交付通知（accommodation notice）的情况下运送不合格货物构成接受和违约）。","conclusion":"答案 A：虽然果园运桃子时已经签订了合同，但水果商并不一定要接受。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-bf15eb43d59360d5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":155,"sourceQuestionPage":458,"sourceQuestionPages":[458],"sourceExplanationPages":[459,460],"sourceAnswerPages":[459,460],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-156","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A fine clothing store placed an advertisement in a local newspaper that read: \"Sale! This weekend only, fur coats for as low as $100!\" A local shopper read the advertisement and drove to the clothing store that weekend. All the fur coats were priced between $500 and $1,500. The shopper inquired about the sale. The salesperson told her that the advertisement was incorrect because the sale was not scheduled to occur until the following weekend. The shopper replied, \"Why don't you just sell me one of these coats for $100 today?\" The salesperson refused, and the shopper angrily left the store. If the shopper sues the store for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the shopper's question was too equivocal to be an acceptance of the store's offer.","B":"No, because the store's advertisement was merely an invitation to negotiate.","C":"Yes, because the shopper detrimentally relied on the store's advertisement by driving to the store.","D":"Yes, because the shopper had accepted the store's general offer to sell a fur coat for $100."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate or solicitations of offers, which create no power of acceptance in the recipient. However, an advertisement can constitute a general offer—the acceptance of which creates a binding contract—if the advertisement: specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and places a reasonable limit on who can accept the offer. Here, the clothing store placed an advertisement in the newspaper stating that fur coats (subject matter) would be sold for as low as $100 (price). However, the ad failed to specify the quantity of fur coats to be sold at this price and placed no limit on who could accept. As a result, the ad was merely an invitation to negotiate. And since there was no offer that could be accepted, the shopper is unlikely to prevail in her breach-of-contract suit. (Choices A & D) An acceptance must be unequivocal to bind either party. Here, the shopper's question—\"Why don't you just sell me one of these coats for $100 today?\"—was unequivocal since it clearly indicated her intent to purchase a coat. But her attempted acceptance was ineffective since there was no offer. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an unaccepted offer may become irrevocable if the offeree reasonably relies on it and suffers a substantial detriment as a result. But here, the store's advertisement was not an offer. And even if it were, the shopper did not suffer a substantial detriment by driving to the store.\n\nEducational objective: Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate instead of offers to form contracts. However, an advertisement can constitute a general offer if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who can accept the offer.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 26 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an advertisement is not an offer unless it contains language of commitment).","educationalObjective":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate instead of offers to form contracts. However, an advertisement can constitute a general offer if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who can accept the offer.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 26 cmt. b (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an advertisement is not an offer unless it contains language of commitment).","rule":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate instead of offers to form contracts. However, an advertisement can constitute a general offer if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who can accept the offer.","irac":{"issue":"If the shopper sues the store for breach of contract, will she be likely to prevail?","rule":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate instead of offers to form contracts. However, an advertisement can constitute a general offer if it (1) specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and (2) places a reasonable limit on who can accept the offer.","analysis":"Advertisements are generally considered invitations to negotiate or solicitations of offers, which create no power of acceptance in the recipient. However, an advertisement can constitute a general offer—the acceptance of which creates a binding contract—if the advertisement: specifies the subject matter, quantity, and price and places a reasonable limit on who can accept the offer. Here, the clothing store placed an advertisement in the newspaper stating that fur coats (subject matter) would be sold for as low as $100 (price). However, the ad failed to specify the quantity of fur coats to be sold at this price and placed no limit on who could accept. As a result, the ad was merely an invitation to negotiate. And since there was no offer that could be accepted, the shopper is unlikely to prevail in her breach-of-contract suit. (Choices A & D) An acceptance must be unequivocal to bind either party. Here, the shopper's question—\"Why don't you just sell me one of these coats for $100 today?\"—was unequivocal since it clearly indicated her intent to purchase a coat. But her attempted acceptance was ineffective since there was no offer. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an unaccepted offer may become irrevocable if the offeree reasonably relies on it and suffers a substantial detriment as a result. But here, the store's advertisement was not an offer. And even if it were, the shopper did not suffer a substantial detriment by driving to the store.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the store's advertisement was merely an invitation to negotiate."},"questionZh":"一家高档服装店在当地报纸上刊登了一则广告，上面写着：“促销！仅限本周末，皮草大衣低至 100 美元！”一位当地购物者读了广告后，在周末开车去了这家服装店。所有皮草大衣的售价都在 500 美元到 1,500 美元之间。购物者询问销售情况。销售人员告诉她，广告不正确，因为促销活动要到下周末才会进行。购物者回答道：“今天你为什么不以 100 美元的价格卖给我一件这样的外套呢？”销售人员拒绝了，购物者愤怒地离开了商店。如果购物者起诉商店违反合同，她有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为购物者的问题太模棱两可，不足以构成接受商店的报价。","B":"不，因为商店的广告只是一个谈判邀请。","C":"是的，因为购物者开车前往商店，对商店的广告产生了不利的依赖。","D":"是的，因为购物者接受了商店的一般报价，以 100 美元的价格出售一件毛皮大衣。"},"explanationZh":"广告通常被视为谈判邀请或要约招揽，不构成接受者接受的权力。然而，如果广告：明确了标的物、数量和价格，并对接受要约的人设定了合理的限制，则该广告可以构成一般要约，接受该要约即构成具有约束力的合同。在这里，服装店在报纸上刊登广告，声称毛皮大衣（主题）的售价将低至 100 美元（价格）。不过，该广告并未具体说明按此价格出售的皮草大衣的数量，也没有限制谁可以接受。结果，这则广告只是一个谈判邀请。由于没有可以接受的报价，购物者不太可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 A 和 D）接受必须明确，以约束任何一方。在这里，购物者的问题——“你今天为什么不以 100 美元的价格卖给我一件外套？”——是明确的，因为这清楚地表明了她购买外套的意图。但她的接受尝试没有成功，因为没有收到任何offer。 （选项C）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果受要约人合理依赖该要约并因此遭受重大损害，则未接受的要约可能会变得不可撤销。但在这里，商店的广告并不是报价。即使是这样，购物者开车去商店也不会遭受重大损失。\n\n教育目标：广告通常被视为谈判邀请，而不是签订合同的要约。然而，如果广告满足以下条件，则可以构成全面要约：(1) 明确标的物、数量和价格；(2) 对接受要约的人员设置合理限制。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 26 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，除非包含承诺语言，否则广告不是要约）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"广告通常被视为谈判邀请，而不是形成合同的要约。然而，如果广告满足以下条件，则可以构成全面要约：(1) 明确标的物、数量和价格；(2) 对接受要约的人员设置合理限制。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果购物者起诉商店违反合同，她有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"广告通常被视为谈判邀请，而不是形成合同的要约。然而，如果广告满足以下条件，则可以构成全面要约：(1) 明确标的物、数量和价格；(2) 对接受要约的人员设置合理限制。","analysis":"广告通常被视为谈判邀请或要约招揽，不构成接受者接受的权力。然而，如果广告：明确了标的物、数量和价格，并对接受要约的人设定了合理的限制，则该广告可以构成一般要约，接受该要约即构成具有约束力的合同。在这里，服装店在报纸上刊登广告，声称毛皮大衣（主题）的售价将低至 100 美元（价格）。不过，该广告并未具体说明按此价格出售的皮草大衣的数量，也没有限制谁可以接受。结果，这则广告只是一个谈判邀请。由于没有可以接受的报价，购物者不太可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 （选择 A 和 D）接受必须明确，以约束任何一方。在这里，购物者的问题——“你今天为什么不以 100 美元的价格卖给我一件外套？”——是明确的，因为这清楚地表明了她购买外套的意图。但她的接受尝试没有成功，因为没有收到任何offer。 （选项C）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果受要约人合理依赖该要约并因此遭受重大损害，则未接受的要约可能会变得不可撤销。但在这里，商店的广告并不是报价。即使是这样，购物者开车去商店也不会遭受重大损失。\n\n教育目标：广告通常被视为谈判邀请，而不是签订合同的要约。然而，如果广告满足以下条件，则可以构成全面要约：(1) 明确标的物、数量和价格；(2) 对接受要约的人员设置合理限制。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 26 cmt。 b（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，除非包含承诺语言，否则广告不是要约）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为商店的广告只是一个谈判邀请。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-b32782c8ffb8a9d0"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":156,"sourceQuestionPage":461,"sourceQuestionPages":[461],"sourceExplanationPages":[462,463],"sourceAnswerPages":[462,463],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-157","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"An entrepreneur owed his neighbor $1,000, plus interest at 8% until paid, on a long- overdue promissory note, collection of which would become barred by the statute of limitations on June 30. On the preceding April 1, the entrepreneur and the neighbor both signed a writing in which the entrepreneur promised to pay the note in full on the following December 31, plus interest at 8% until that date, and the neighbor promised not to sue on the note in the meantime. The neighbor, having received some advice from his nonlawyer brother-in-law, became concerned about the legal effect of the April 1 agreement. On May 1, acting pro se as permitted by the rules of the local small claims court, the neighbor filed suit to collect the note. Assuming that there is no controlling statute, is the April 1 agreement an effective defense for the entrepreneur?","choices":{"A":"No, because the entrepreneur's April 1 promise is enforceable with or without consideration.","B":"No, because there was no consideration for the neighbor's promise not to sue, in that the entrepreneur was already obligated to pay $1,000 plus interest at 8% until the payment date.","C":"Yes, because the entrepreneur's promise to pay interest until December 31 was consideration for the neighbor's promise not to sue.","D":"Yes, because the law creates a presumption that the entrepreneur relied on the neighbor's promise not to sue."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Exceptions to preexisting-duty rule\n\nMain rule Promise to perform (or performance of) preexisting duty is not consideration\n\nCommon-law New/altered New duty added or preexisting duty altered (eg, exceptions consideration acceleration of due date)\n\nRatification of New promise to perform voidable duty (eg, upon voidable duty reaching age of majority)\n\nHonest dispute Scope of duty honestly disputed\n\nUnexpected event Fair & equitable modification in view of unanticipated circumstances\n\nRenewed promise Renewed promise to pay preexisting debt despite technical defense (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) if written or partially performed\n\nThird party Third party offers additional promise that is contingent on performance of preexisting duty\n\nUCC exception Modification Modification sought in good faith—ie, honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nA valid claim can be permanently or temporarily settled by entering a written settlement agreement in which each party's promise or performance is consideration (ie, exchanged) for the other party's promise or performance. A promise to perform (or the performance of) a preexisting contractual duty is generally not consideration. But if the duty is altered in any way (eg, acceleration of a due date), there is consideration, and the agreement will provide an effective defense in a lawsuit that asserts the settled claim. Here, the entrepreneur owed the neighbor $1,000 plus 8% interest until paid. On April 1, they signed a settlement agreement in which the entrepreneur promised to repay the debt by December 31. Although the entrepreneur was already obligated to pay 8% interest, the new due date altered his duties (Choice B). As a result, the entrepreneur's promise was consideration for the neighbor's promise not to sue before December 31. Since the neighbor sued before then (on May 1), the April 1 agreement is an effective defense for the entrepreneur.\n\n(Choice A) The entrepreneur's April 1 promise to repay the debt by December 31 would be unenforceable without consideration. But that promise is supported by consideration—ie, the neighbor's return promise not to sue before December 31. (Choice D) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a promise can become enforceable if the promisee (eg, the entrepreneur) proves that he/she reasonably and detrimentally relied on that promise. But the promisee's reliance will not be presumed.\n\nEducational objective: A promise to perform (or the performance of) a preexisting contractual duty is not consideration unless that duty is altered in some way (eg, acceleration of a due date).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","educationalObjective":"A promise to perform (or the performance of) a preexisting contractual duty is not consideration unless that duty is altered in some way (eg, acceleration of a due date).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","rule":"A promise to perform (or the performance of) a preexisting contractual duty is not consideration unless that duty is altered in some way (eg, acceleration of a due date).","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that there is no controlling statute, is the April 1 agreement an effective defense for the entrepreneur?","rule":"A promise to perform (or the performance of) a preexisting contractual duty is not consideration unless that duty is altered in some way (eg, acceleration of a due date).","analysis":"Exceptions to preexisting-duty rule\n\nMain rule Promise to perform (or performance of) preexisting duty is not consideration\n\nCommon-law New/altered New duty added or preexisting duty altered (eg, exceptions consideration acceleration of due date)\n\nRatification of New promise to perform voidable duty (eg, upon voidable duty reaching age of majority)\n\nHonest dispute Scope of duty honestly disputed\n\nUnexpected event Fair & equitable modification in view of unanticipated circumstances\n\nRenewed promise Renewed promise to pay preexisting debt despite technical defense (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) if written or partially performed\n\nThird party Third party offers additional promise that is contingent on performance of preexisting duty\n\nUCC exception Modification Modification sought in good faith—ie, honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nA valid claim can be permanently or temporarily settled by entering a written settlement agreement in which each party's promise or performance is consideration (ie, exchanged) for the other party's promise or performance. A promise to perform (or the performance of) a preexisting contractual duty is generally not consideration. But if the duty is altered in any way (eg, acceleration of a due date), there is consideration, and the agreement will provide an effective defense in a lawsuit that asserts the settled claim. Here, the entrepreneur owed the neighbor $1,000 plus 8% interest until paid. On April 1, they signed a settlement agreement in which the entrepreneur promised to repay the debt by December 31. Although the entrepreneur was already obligated to pay 8% interest, the new due date altered his duties (Choice B). As a result, the entrepreneur's promise was consideration for the neighbor's promise not to sue before December 31. Since the neighbor sued before then (on May 1), the April 1 agreement is an effective defense for the entrepreneur.\n\n(Choice A) The entrepreneur's April 1 promise to repay the debt by December 31 would be unenforceable without consideration. But that promise is supported by consideration—ie, the neighbor's return promise not to sue before December 31. (Choice D) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a promise can become enforceable if the promisee (eg, the entrepreneur) proves that he/she reasonably and detrimentally relied on that promise. But the promisee's reliance will not be presumed.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the entrepreneur's promise to pay interest until December 31 was consideration for the neighbor's promise not to sue."},"questionZh":"一位企业家欠邻居 1,000 美元，外加一张逾期未付的 8% 利息，该票据逾期已久，6 月 30 日的诉讼时效将禁止其领取。去年 4 月 1 日，企业家和邻居双方签署了一份书面文件，其中企业家承诺在次年 12 月 31 日全额支付票据，外加该日期前 8% 的利息，邻居承诺在此期间不会就该票据提起诉讼。这位邻居在听取了非律师姐夫的一些建议后，开始担心 4 月 1 日协议的法律效力。 5月1日，邻居根据当地小额索赔法院规则提起诉讼，要求收回票据。假设没有控制法规，4 月 1 日的协议对企业家来说是有效的辩护吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为企业家的 4 月 1 日承诺无论是否考虑都可以强制执行。","B":"不可以，因为没有考虑邻居不起诉的承诺，因为企业家已经有义务在付款日期之前支付 1,000 美元加上 8% 的利息。","C":"是的，因为企业家承诺在12月31日之前支付利息是对邻居承诺不起诉的考虑。","D":"是的，因为法律假定企业家依赖邻居的不起诉承诺。"},"explanationZh":"既存义务规则的例外情况\n\n主要规则 承诺履行（或履行）先前存在的职责不构成对价\n\n普通法 新/更改 添加新义务或更改先前存在的义务（例如，考虑加速到期日的例外情况）\n\n批准履行可撤销义务的新承诺（例如，在可撤销义务达到成年年龄时）\n\n诚实争议 诚实争议的职责范围\n\n意外事件 鉴于意外情况进行公平公正的修改\n\n更新的承诺 如果书面或部分履行，则即使有技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），仍将支付先前存在的债务\n\n第三方 第三方提供额外承诺，该承诺取决于履行先前存在的义务\n\nUCC 例外修改 真诚地寻求修改，即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n有效的索赔可以通过签订书面和解协议来永久或暂时解决，其中各方的承诺或履行是另一方承诺或履行的对价（即交换）。履行（或履行）先前存在的合同义务的承诺通常不是对价。但如果义务以任何方式改变（例如，提前到期日），则需要考虑，并且该协议将在主张已解决索赔的诉讼中提供有效的辩护。在这里，这位企业家欠邻居 1,000 美元，外加 8% 的利息，直至付清为止。 4月1日，他们签署了一份和解协议，其中企业家承诺在12月31日之前偿还债务。尽管企业家已经有义务支付8%的利息，但新的到期日改变了他的义务（选择B）。因此，企业家的承诺是对邻居承诺在12月31日之前不起诉的考虑。由于邻居在此之前（5月1日）提起了诉讼，因此4月1日的协议对企业家来说是有效的抗辩。\n\n（选择 A） 企业家于 4 月 1 日做出的在 12 月 31 日之前偿还债务的承诺将在未经考虑的情况下无法执行。但这一承诺得到了对价的支持，即邻居的回报承诺在 12 月 31 日之前不会提起诉讼。（选择 D）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果受诺人（例如企业家）证明他/她合理且有害地依赖该承诺，则该承诺可以强制执行。但不能推定受诺人的信赖。\n\n教育目标：履行（或履行）先前存在的合同义务的承诺不构成对价，除非该义务以某种方式改变（例如，提前到期日）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"履行（或履行）先前存在的合同义务的承诺不构成对价，除非该义务以某种方式改变（例如，提前到期日）。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设没有控制法规，4 月 1 日的协议对企业家来说是有效的辩护吗？","rule":"履行（或履行）先前存在的合同义务的承诺不构成对价，除非该义务以某种方式改变（例如，提前到期日）。","analysis":"既存义务规则的例外情况\n\n主要规则 承诺履行（或履行）先前存在的职责不构成对价\n\n普通法 新/更改 添加新义务或更改先前存在的义务（例如，考虑加速到期日的例外情况）\n\n批准履行可撤销义务的新承诺（例如，在可撤销义务达到成年年龄时）\n\n诚实争议 诚实争议的职责范围\n\n意外事件 鉴于意外情况进行公平公正的修改\n\n更新的承诺 如果书面或部分履行，则即使有技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），仍将支付先前存在的债务\n\n第三方 第三方提供额外承诺，该承诺取决于履行先前存在的义务\n\nUCC 例外修改 真诚地寻求修改，即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n有效的索赔可以通过签订书面和解协议来永久或暂时解决，其中各方的承诺或履行是另一方承诺或履行的对价（即交换）。履行（或履行）先前存在的合同义务的承诺通常不是对价。但如果义务以任何方式改变（例如，提前到期日），则需要考虑，并且该协议将在主张已解决索赔的诉讼中提供有效的辩护。在这里，这位企业家欠邻居 1,000 美元，外加 8% 的利息，直至付清为止。 4月1日，他们签署了一份和解协议，其中企业家承诺在12月31日之前偿还债务。尽管企业家已经有义务支付8%的利息，但新的到期日改变了他的义务（选择B）。因此，企业家的承诺是对邻居承诺在12月31日之前不起诉的考虑。由于邻居在此之前（5月1日）提起了诉讼，因此4月1日的协议对企业家来说是有效的抗辩。\n\n（选择 A） 企业家于 4 月 1 日做出的在 12 月 31 日之前偿还债务的承诺将在未经考虑的情况下无法执行。但这一承诺得到了对价的支持，即邻居的回报承诺在 12 月 31 日之前不会提起诉讼。（选择 D）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果受诺人（例如企业家）证明他/她合理且有害地依赖该承诺，则该承诺可以强制执行。但不能推定受诺人的信赖。\n\n教育目标：履行（或履行）先前存在的合同义务的承诺不构成对价，除非该义务以某种方式改变（例如，提前到期日）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为企业家承诺在12月31日之前支付利息是对邻居承诺不起诉的考虑。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":157,"sourceQuestionPage":464,"sourceQuestionPages":[464],"sourceExplanationPages":[465,466],"sourceAnswerPages":[465,466],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-158","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A college student asked her friend to lend her $2,000. The friend replied that he would do so only if the student's father, a wealthy businessman, would guarantee the loan. At the student's request, the businessman mailed a signed letter to the friend: \"If you lend $2,000 to my daughter, I will repay it if she doesn't.\" On September 15, the friend, having read the businessman's letter, lent $2,000 to the student, which she agreed to repay in installments of $200 plus accrued interest on the last day of each month beginning October 31. The businessman died on September 16. Later that same day, unaware of the businessman's death, the friend mailed a letter to the businessman advising that he had made the $2,000 loan to the student on September 15. The student failed to repay any of the debt, and the entire $2,000 is due. In an action by the friend against the businessman's estate for $2,000 plus accrued interest, which of the following, if any, will serve as (an) effective defense(s) for the businessman's estate?","choices":{"A":"The businessman died before the friend accepted his offer.","B":"There was no consideration to support the businessman's promise, because he did not receive any benefit.","C":"There was no consideration to support the businessman's promise, because he did not receive any benefit, and the businessman died before the friend notified him that his offer had been accepted.","D":"None of the above."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A suretyship arises when one party (guarantor) promises a second party (creditor) that the guarantor will pay the debt of a third party (principal) if he/she defaults. To be enforceable, a suretyship must be in writing and supported by consideration. The consideration requirement is met if the guarantor is compensated for the promise. But even if the guarantor is uncompensated, the consideration requirement is still satisfied if: the creditor conditioned the loan to the principal on the guarantor's promise to pay or\n\nthe guarantor made the promise to pay before or at the same time as the creditor made the loan to the principal.\n\nIn either case, the loan itself serves as valid consideration for the guarantor's promise— even if the guarantor is not notified when the loan is made. That is because the loan is a legal detriment to the creditor that was induced by the guarantor's promise. Here, the friend would agree to loan the student $2,000 only on the condition that the student's father, the businessman, guarantee it. The businessman was not compensated, but he promised to guarantee the loan in writing before the friend loaned $2,000 to the student. Therefore, although the businessman died the following day before receiving notice of the loan, his promise was supported by consideration (ie, the loan to his daughter) (Choices B & C). Additionally, a guarantor's offer to form a suretyship can be accepted by promising to give the loan or giving the loan. Here, the businessman's offer to form a suretyship was accepted when the friend loaned the student $2,000 on September 15—before the businessman died on September 16 (Choice A). As a result, none of these will serve as effective defenses for the businessman's estate.\n\nEducational objective: A promise to guarantee a debt is supported by consideration if (1) the guarantor is compensated, (2) the loan is conditioned on obtaining a guarantee, or (3) the promise is made before or at the same time as the loan.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 88 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a promise to guarantee a loan is binding if the promisor should reasonably expect that promise to induce the loan).","educationalObjective":"A promise to guarantee a debt is supported by consideration if (1) the guarantor is compensated, (2) the loan is conditioned on obtaining a guarantee, or (3) the promise is made before or at the same time as the loan.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 88 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a promise to guarantee a loan is binding if the promisor should reasonably expect that promise to induce the loan).","rule":"A promise to guarantee a debt is supported by consideration if (1) the guarantor is compensated, (2) the loan is conditioned on obtaining a guarantee, or (3) the promise is made before or at the same time as the loan.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the friend against the businessman's estate for $2,000 plus accrued interest, which of the following, if any, will serve as (an) effective defense(s) for the businessman's estate?","rule":"A promise to guarantee a debt is supported by consideration if (1) the guarantor is compensated, (2) the loan is conditioned on obtaining a guarantee, or (3) the promise is made before or at the same time as the loan.","analysis":"A suretyship arises when one party (guarantor) promises a second party (creditor) that the guarantor will pay the debt of a third party (principal) if he/she defaults. To be enforceable, a suretyship must be in writing and supported by consideration. The consideration requirement is met if the guarantor is compensated for the promise. But even if the guarantor is uncompensated, the consideration requirement is still satisfied if: the creditor conditioned the loan to the principal on the guarantor's promise to pay or\n\nthe guarantor made the promise to pay before or at the same time as the creditor made the loan to the principal.\n\nIn either case, the loan itself serves as valid consideration for the guarantor's promise— even if the guarantor is not notified when the loan is made. That is because the loan is a legal detriment to the creditor that was induced by the guarantor's promise. Here, the friend would agree to loan the student $2,000 only on the condition that the student's father, the businessman, guarantee it. The businessman was not compensated, but he promised to guarantee the loan in writing before the friend loaned $2,000 to the student. Therefore, although the businessman died the following day before receiving notice of the loan, his promise was supported by consideration (ie, the loan to his daughter) (Choices B & C). Additionally, a guarantor's offer to form a suretyship can be accepted by promising to give the loan or giving the loan. Here, the businessman's offer to form a suretyship was accepted when the friend loaned the student $2,000 on September 15—before the businessman died on September 16 (Choice A). As a result, none of these will serve as effective defenses for the businessman's estate.","conclusion":"Answer D: None of the above."},"questionZh":"一名大学生向她的朋友借了2000美元。朋友回答说，只有学生的父亲是一位富商，他才能为这笔贷款提供担保。应学生的要求，商人给朋友寄了一封署名信：“如果你借给我女儿2000美元，如果她不借，我就还。” 9 月 15 日，这位朋友读完该商人的信后，借给该学生 2,000 美元，她同意分期偿还 200 美元，并在 10 月 31 日开始的每月最后一天支付应计利息。该商人于 9 月 16 日去世。当天晚些时候，该朋友不知道该商人的去世，向该学生寄了一封信，告知他已于9 月 15 日。学生未能偿还任何债务，2,000 美元全部到期。在朋友以 2,000 美元加应计利息针对商人遗产提起的诉讼中，以下哪项（如果有）将作为商人遗产的有效辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"在朋友接受他的提议之前，商人就去世了。","B":"没有约因支持商人的承诺，因为他没有得到任何好处。","C":"没有约因支持商人的承诺，因为他没有得到任何好处，而且在朋友通知他的报价已被接受之前，商人就去世了。","D":"以上都不是。"},"explanationZh":"当一方（担保人）向另一方（债权人）承诺，如果第三方（委托人）违约，担保人将偿还第三方（委托人）的债务，则担保权就产生了。为了具有可执行性，担保必须采用书面形式并获得约因支持。如果担保人因承诺而得到补偿，则满足对价要求。但即使担保人没有得到补偿，如果满足以下条件，对价要求仍然得到满足： 债权人以担保人承诺付款为条件向本金发放贷款，或者\n\n担保人在债权人向委托人发放贷款之前或同时做出付款承诺。\n\n无论哪种情况，贷款本身都可以作为担保人承诺的有效对价——即使贷款发放时没有通知担保人。这是因为贷款是由担保人的承诺引起的对债权人的法律损害。在这里，朋友同意借给学生 2,000 美元，但条件是学生的父亲（商人）提供担保。商人没有得到补偿，但在朋友借给学生2000美元之前，他承诺以书面形式担保贷款。因此，尽管商人在收到贷款通知的第二天就去世了，但他的承诺得到了对价（即给他女儿的贷款）的支持（选择B和C）。此外，担保人提出的提供担保的提议可以通过承诺提供贷款或提供贷款来接受。在这里，当商人的朋友在 9 月 15 日借给学生 2,000 美元时，商人提出的提供担保的提议被接受，之后商人于 9 月 16 日去世（选项 A）。因此，这些都无法有效保护商人的财产。\n\n教育目标：担保债务的承诺是有对价支持的，如果 (1) 担保人得到补偿，(2) 贷款以获得担保为条件，或 (3) 承诺是在贷款之前或同时做出的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 88（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果承诺人合理地预期该承诺会产生贷款，则担保贷款的承诺具有约束力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果 (1) 担保人得到补偿，(2) 贷款以获得担保为条件，或 (3) 在贷款之前或同时做出承诺，则担保债务的承诺受到对价的支持。","iracZh":{"issue":"在朋友以 2,000 美元加应计利息针对商人遗产提起的诉讼中，以下哪项（如果有）将作为商人遗产的有效辩护？","rule":"如果 (1) 担保人得到补偿，(2) 贷款以获得担保为条件，或 (3) 在贷款之前或同时做出承诺，则担保债务的承诺受到对价的支持。","analysis":"当一方（担保人）向另一方（债权人）承诺，如果第三方（委托人）违约，担保人将偿还第三方（委托人）的债务，则担保权就产生了。为了具有可执行性，担保必须采用书面形式并获得约因支持。如果担保人因承诺而得到补偿，则满足对价要求。但即使担保人没有得到补偿，如果满足以下条件，对价要求仍然得到满足： 债权人以担保人承诺付款为条件向本金发放贷款，或者\n\n担保人在债权人向委托人发放贷款之前或同时做出付款承诺。\n\n无论哪种情况，贷款本身都可以作为担保人承诺的有效对价——即使贷款发放时没有通知担保人。这是因为贷款是由担保人的承诺引起的对债权人的法律损害。在这里，朋友同意借给学生 2,000 美元，但条件是学生的父亲（商人）提供担保。商人没有得到补偿，但在朋友借给学生2000美元之前，他承诺以书面形式担保贷款。因此，尽管商人在收到贷款通知的第二天就去世了，但他的承诺得到了对价（即给他女儿的贷款）的支持（选择B和C）。此外，担保人提出的提供担保的提议可以通过承诺提供贷款或提供贷款来接受。在这里，当商人的朋友在 9 月 15 日借给学生 2,000 美元时，商人提出的提供担保的提议被接受，之后商人于 9 月 16 日去世（选项 A）。因此，这些都无法有效保护商人的财产。\n\n教育目标：担保债务的承诺是有对价支持的，如果 (1) 担保人得到补偿，(2) 贷款以获得担保为条件，或 (3) 承诺是在贷款之前或同时做出的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 88（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果承诺人合理地预期该承诺会产生贷款，则担保贷款的承诺具有约束力）。","conclusion":"答案 D：以上都不是。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"guarantor","ipa":"/ɡˌɛɹʌntˈɔɹ/","syllables":"guar·an·tor","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 保证人；[经] 保证人, 担保人","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“guarantor”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:guarantors","sourceSentence":"A promise to guarantee a debt is supported by consideration if (1) the guarantor is compensated, (2) the loan is conditioned on obtaining a guarantee, or (3) the promise is made before or at the same time as the loan.","synonyms":["surety","warrantor","warranter"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6c0bd9d15d11e0b8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":158,"sourceQuestionPage":467,"sourceQuestionPages":[467],"sourceExplanationPages":[468,469],"sourceAnswerPages":[468,469],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-159","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A wallpaper hanger sent a general contractor this fax: \"Will do all paperhanging on the doctor's new building, per owner's specs, for $14,000 if you accept within reasonable time after main contract awarded.\" Three competitors sent the general contractor similar bids to hang wallpaper in the doctor's building in the respective amounts of $18,000, $19,000, and $20,000. The general contractor used the wallpaper hanger's $14,000 figure in preparing and submitting her own sealed bid on the doctor's building. Before the bids were opened, the wallpaper hanger truthfully advised the general contractor that the former's sub-bid had been based on a $4,000 computational error and was therefore revoked. Shortly thereafter, the general contractor was awarded the doctor's building construction contract and subsequently contracted with a competitor to wallpaper the building for a price of $18,000. The general contractor then sued the wallpaper hanger to recover $4,000. Which of the following, if proved, would most strengthen the general contractor's prospect of recovery?","choices":{"A":"After the wallpaper hanger's notice of revocation, the general contractor made a reasonable effort to subcontract with another paperhanger at the lowest possible price.","B":"The general contractor dealt with all of her subcontractors in good faith and without seeking to renegotiate (lower) the prices they had bid.","C":"The general contractor had been required by the owner to submit a bid bond in the amount of $50,000 and could not have withdrawn or amended her bid on the main contract without forfeiting that bond.","D":"The wallpaper hanger was negligent in erroneously calculating the amount of his sub- bid."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nUnder the doctrine of promissory estoppel (ie, detrimental reliance), an offer is binding as an option contract and is therefore irrevocable for a reasonable period of time if: the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance the offeree reasonably relied on the offer and reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment. When such an offer is revoked before a reasonable period of time has passed, the offeree's remedies range from enforcement of the offer to reimbursement of expenses incurred in reliance on the offer. Here, the wallpaper hanger knew that the general contractor would factor the wallpaper hanger's sub-bid into her bid for the main contract (reasonable expectation of reliance). The general contractor then used the wallpaper hanger's sub-bid in preparing\n\nand submitting her bid (reasonable reliance). Therefore, proof that the general contractor could not have amended or withdrawn her bid without forfeiting a bid bond (substantial detriment) would most strengthen her prospect for recovery. (Choice A) The general contractor's reasonable efforts to subcontract with another paperhanger at the lowest price (ie, her efforts to mitigate damages) go to the amount of her recovery, not to whether she can recover. (Choice B) The general contractor's good-faith dealing with all subcontractors is irrelevant to whether she can recover damages stemming from the revocation of the wallpaper hanger's offer. (Choice D) The wallpaper hanger's negligence in erroneously calculating the amount of his sub-bid has no bearing on the general contractor's chances of recovery.\n\nEducational objective: An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 87 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (option contract by detrimental reliance).","educationalObjective":"An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 87 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (option contract by detrimental reliance).","rule":"An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following, if proved, would most strengthen the general contractor's prospect of recovery?","rule":"An offer is binding as an option contract and therefore irrevocable if (1) the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance, (2) the offeree reasonably relied on the offer, and (3) reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment.","analysis":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nUnder the doctrine of promissory estoppel (ie, detrimental reliance), an offer is binding as an option contract and is therefore irrevocable for a reasonable period of time if: the offeror reasonably expected to induce reliance on the offer before acceptance the offeree reasonably relied on the offer and reliance caused the offeree to suffer substantial detriment. When such an offer is revoked before a reasonable period of time has passed, the offeree's remedies range from enforcement of the offer to reimbursement of expenses incurred in reliance on the offer. Here, the wallpaper hanger knew that the general contractor would factor the wallpaper hanger's sub-bid into her bid for the main contract (reasonable expectation of reliance). The general contractor then used the wallpaper hanger's sub-bid in preparing\n\nand submitting her bid (reasonable reliance). Therefore, proof that the general contractor could not have amended or withdrawn her bid without forfeiting a bid bond (substantial detriment) would most strengthen her prospect for recovery. (Choice A) The general contractor's reasonable efforts to subcontract with another paperhanger at the lowest price (ie, her efforts to mitigate damages) go to the amount of her recovery, not to whether she can recover. (Choice B) The general contractor's good-faith dealing with all subcontractors is irrelevant to whether she can recover damages stemming from the revocation of the wallpaper hanger's offer. (Choice D) The wallpaper hanger's negligence in erroneously calculating the amount of his sub-bid has no bearing on the general contractor's chances of recovery.","conclusion":"Answer C: The general contractor had been required by the owner to submit a bid bond in the amount of $50,000 and could not have withdrawn or amended her bid on the main contract without forfeiting that bond."},"questionZh":"一家壁纸架给总承包商发了一份传真：“如果您在主合同授予后的合理时间内接受，我们将根据业主的规格，为医生的新大楼做所有的裱糊工作，价格为 14,000 美元。”三个竞争对手向总承包商发出了类似的投标，要求在医生大楼内悬挂壁纸，金额分别为 18,000 美元、19,000 美元和 20,000 美元。总承包商用壁纸架上的 14,000 美元准备并提交了她自己对医生大楼的密封投标。在开标前，壁纸架如实告知总承包商，前者的分标是基于4000美元的计算错误，因此被撤销。此后不久，总承包商获得了医生大楼的施工合同，随后与竞争对手签订了为大楼贴壁纸的合同，价格为 18,000 美元。总承包商随后起诉壁纸架，要求赔偿 4,000 美元。以下哪一项如果得到证实，最能增强总承包商的复苏前景？","choicesZh":{"A":"在该壁纸架发出撤销通知后，总承包商做出了合理的努力，以尽可能低的价格与另一家壁纸架分包。","B":"总承包商真诚地与所有分包商打交道，并且没有寻求重新谈判（降低）他们的投标价格。","C":"业主要求总承包商提交 50,000 美元的投标保证金，并且如果不放弃该保证金，就不能撤回或修改其对主合同的投标。","D":"壁纸安装者疏忽大意，错误地计算了他的投标金额。"},"explanationZh":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n根据允诺禁止反言原则（即有害信赖），要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此在合理时间内不可撤销，如果： 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖；受要约人合理地依赖要约，并且信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损害。当此类要约在一段合理的时间过去之前被撤销时，受要约人的补救措施包括执行要约和偿还因依赖要约而产生的费用。在这里，壁纸架知道总承包商会将壁纸架的分标纳入其主合同的投标中（合理的信赖期望）。总承包商随后使用壁纸架的分标来准备\n\n并提交她的出价（合理的信赖）。因此，证明总承包商无法在不丧失投标保证金（实质性损害）的情况下修改或撤回其投标，将最有可能增强其收回成本的前景。 （选项A） 总承包商以最低价格分包给另一家裱糊工的合理努力（即她为减轻损害所做的努力）取决于她的恢复金额，而不是她能否恢复。 （选项B） 总承包商与所有分包商打交道的诚意与她是否能获得因撤销壁纸架报价而造成的损失无关。 （选项D） 壁纸安装商因疏忽而错误地计算了其分标金额，这对总承包商的追偿机会没有影响。\n\n教育目标：要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此不可撤销，如果 (1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的依赖，(2) 受要约人合理依赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 87（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有害依赖的期权合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此在以下情况下不可撤销：(1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖，(2) 受要约人合理地信赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项如果得到证实，最能增强总承包商的复苏前景？","rule":"要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此在以下情况下不可撤销：(1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖，(2) 受要约人合理地信赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。","analysis":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n根据允诺禁止反言原则（即有害信赖），要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此在合理时间内不可撤销，如果： 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的信赖；受要约人合理地依赖要约，并且信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损害。当此类要约在一段合理的时间过去之前被撤销时，受要约人的补救措施包括执行要约和偿还因依赖要约而产生的费用。在这里，壁纸架知道总承包商会将壁纸架的分标纳入其主合同的投标中（合理的信赖期望）。总承包商随后使用壁纸架的分标来准备\n\n并提交她的出价（合理的信赖）。因此，证明总承包商无法在不丧失投标保证金（实质性损害）的情况下修改或撤回其投标，将最有可能增强其收回成本的前景。 （选项A） 总承包商以最低价格分包给另一家裱糊工的合理努力（即她为减轻损害所做的努力）取决于她的恢复金额，而不是她能否恢复。 （选项B） 总承包商与所有分包商打交道的诚意与她是否能获得因撤销壁纸架报价而造成的损失无关。 （选项D） 壁纸安装商因疏忽而错误地计算了其分标金额，这对总承包商的追偿机会没有影响。\n\n教育目标：要约作为期权合同具有约束力，因此不可撤销，如果 (1) 要约人在接受之前合理预期会引起对要约的依赖，(2) 受要约人合理依赖要约，以及 (3) 信赖导致受要约人遭受重大损失。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 87（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有害依赖的期权合同）。","conclusion":"答案 C：业主要求总承包商提交 50,000 美元的投标保证金，并且如果不放弃该保证金，就不能撤回或修改其对主合同的投标。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"forfeiting","ipa":"/fˈɔɹfətɪŋ/","syllables":"for·feit·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"v. （因违反协议、犯规、受罚等）丧失, 失去( forfeit的现在分词 )","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“forfeiting”","wordForm":"词；原形 forfeit；词形 0:forfeit/1:i","sourceSentence":"The general contractor had been required by the owner to submit a bid bond in the amount of $50,000 and could not have withdrawn or amended her bid on the main contract without forfeiting that bond.","synonyms":["forfeiture","sacrifice","give up","throw overboard"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":159,"sourceQuestionPage":470,"sourceQuestionPages":[470],"sourceExplanationPages":[471,472],"sourceAnswerPages":[471,472],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-160","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"Upon entering a bank to open an account, a customer read a poster on the bank's wall that said, \"New Customers! $25 FOR 5 MINUTES. If you stand in line for more than five minutes, we will pay you $25! We like happy customers!\" The customer stood in line and started timing his wait. Just as five minutes was about to pass, the bank manager tore the poster down and announced, \"The $25 stand-in-line promotion is over.\" The customer waited in line for 10 more minutes before being served. Does the customer have a claim against the bank for $25?","choices":{"A":"No, because the bank withdrew its offer before the customer completed the requested performance.","B":"No, because the bank's statement was a nonbinding gift promise.","C":"Yes, because the bank could not revoke its offer once the customer had commenced performance.","D":"Yes, because the customer's presence in line served as notice to the bank that he had accepted."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Bilateral v. Unilateral contracts\n\nOffer Effect of beginning performance\n\nUnilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance An offer to form a unilateral contract arises when one party (ie, offeror) invites another party (ie, offeree) to accept an offer only by performing a specified act. The offer becomes irrevocable once the offeree begins performance, thereby creating an option contract—ie, a separate agreement under which the offer will remain open and irrevocable for a period of time. The offer is accepted once the offeree completes the requested performance, and a binding unilateral contract is formed.* Here, the bank's poster offered to pay $25 to any new customer who stood in line for more than five minutes. The new customer read the poster and began performance by standing in line, so the manager could not subsequently withdraw the offer by tearing the poster down and announcing that the promotion was over (Choice A). A binding contract was then formed when the customer completed performance by waiting in line for the specified time. As a result, the customer has a claim against the bank for breaching that contract by failing to pay him $25. *If the offeree begins but fails to complete the requested performance (ie, partial performance), there is no contract because only complete performance constitutes an acceptance of the offer. As a result, the offeror cannot assert a breach-of-contract claim against the offeree in that scenario. (Choice B) The bank's statement was not a gift promise because the $25 was offered in exchange for a legal detriment—ie, standing in line for more than five minutes when the customer had no legal obligation to do so. (Choice D) The customer's presence in line merely created an option contract since a unilateral contract can only be accepted by completing the requested performance. But had the bank offered to form a bilateral contract, the customer's presence in line (ie, beginning of performance) would have served as notice of his acceptance.\n\nEducational objective: To form a unilateral contract, an offeror must invite the offeree to accept an offer by performing a specified act. An irrevocable option contract is created once the offeree begins\n\nperformance. A binding unilateral contract will then be formed if the offeree accepts the offer by fully performing the specified act.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 45 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an option contract is formed when an offeree begins performance after an offer to form a unilateral contract).","educationalObjective":"To form a unilateral contract, an offeror must invite the offeree to accept an offer by performing a specified act. An irrevocable option contract is created once the offeree begins\n\nperformance. A binding unilateral contract will then be formed if the offeree accepts the offer by fully performing the specified act.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 45 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an option contract is formed when an offeree begins performance after an offer to form a unilateral contract).","rule":"To form a unilateral contract, an offeror must invite the offeree to accept an offer by performing a specified act. An irrevocable option contract is created once the offeree begins\n\nperformance. A binding unilateral contract will then be formed if the offeree accepts the offer by fully performing the specified act.","irac":{"issue":"Does the customer have a claim against the bank for $25?","rule":"To form a unilateral contract, an offeror must invite the offeree to accept an offer by performing a specified act. An irrevocable option contract is created once the offeree begins\n\nperformance. A binding unilateral contract will then be formed if the offeree accepts the offer by fully performing the specified act.","analysis":"Bilateral v. Unilateral contracts\n\nOffer Effect of beginning performance\n\nUnilateral Invites acceptance by Creates option contract that prevents offeror performance of act from revoking offer Offeree is not bound to complete performance Bilateral Invites acceptance by return Serves as acceptance of offer promise Offeree is bound to complete performance An offer to form a unilateral contract arises when one party (ie, offeror) invites another party (ie, offeree) to accept an offer only by performing a specified act. The offer becomes irrevocable once the offeree begins performance, thereby creating an option contract—ie, a separate agreement under which the offer will remain open and irrevocable for a period of time. The offer is accepted once the offeree completes the requested performance, and a binding unilateral contract is formed.* Here, the bank's poster offered to pay $25 to any new customer who stood in line for more than five minutes. The new customer read the poster and began performance by standing in line, so the manager could not subsequently withdraw the offer by tearing the poster down and announcing that the promotion was over (Choice A). A binding contract was then formed when the customer completed performance by waiting in line for the specified time. As a result, the customer has a claim against the bank for breaching that contract by failing to pay him $25. *If the offeree begins but fails to complete the requested performance (ie, partial performance), there is no contract because only complete performance constitutes an acceptance of the offer. As a result, the offeror cannot assert a breach-of-contract claim against the offeree in that scenario. (Choice B) The bank's statement was not a gift promise because the $25 was offered in exchange for a legal detriment—ie, standing in line for more than five minutes when the customer had no legal obligation to do so. (Choice D) The customer's presence in line merely created an option contract since a unilateral contract can only be accepted by completing the requested performance. But had the bank offered to form a bilateral contract, the customer's presence in line (ie, beginning of performance) would have served as notice of his acceptance.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the bank could not revoke its offer once the customer had commenced performance."},"questionZh":"一位顾客进入银行开户时，看到银行墙上贴着一张海报，上面写着：“新顾客！5 分钟 25 美元。如果您排队超过 5 分钟，我们将付给您 25 美元！我们喜欢快乐的顾客！”顾客排队并开始计时。就在五分钟过去的时候，银行经理撕下了海报，宣布：“25美元排队促销活动结束了。”顾客又排队等了10分钟才得到服务。客户是否向银行索赔 25 美元？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为银行在客户完成要求的履行之前撤回了报价。","B":"不，因为银行的声明是不具约束力的礼物承诺。","C":"是的，因为一旦客户开始履约，银行就无法撤销其报价。","D":"是的，因为客户排队就等于向银行发出了他已接受的通知。"},"explanationZh":"双边合同与单边合同\n\n优惠期初表现效果\n\n单方面邀请接受 创建防止要约人履行行为以撤销要约的期权合同 受要约人没有义务完全履行 双边邀请返回接受 作为对要约承诺的接受 受要约人有义务完全履行 当一方（即要约人）邀请另一方（即受要约人）仅通过执行特定行为来接受要约时，形成单方合同的要约就产生了。一旦受要约人开始履行，要约就不可撤销，从而创建了一份期权合约，即一份单独的协议，根据该协议，要约将在一段时间内保持开放且不可撤销。一旦受要约人完成所要求的履行，并形成具有约束力的单方合同，该要约即被接受。*此处，银行的海报提出向任何排队超过五分钟的新客户支付 25 美元。新顾客阅读了海报并开始排队表演，因此经理随后无法通过撕下海报并宣布促销结束来撤回优惠（选项 A）。当客户在规定的时间内排队等候完成履行后，具有约束力的合同就成立了。因此，客户向银行提出索赔，指控银行未能向他支付 25 美元，从而违反了合同。 *如果受要约人开始但未能完成所要求的履行（即部分履行），则不存在合同，因为只有完全履行才构成对要约的接受。因此，在这种情况下，要约人不能向受要约人提出违约索赔。 （选项 B） 银行的声明不是礼物承诺，因为提供 25 美元是为了换取法律损害，即在客户没有法律义务的情况下排队超过五分钟。 （选项D） 客户的出现仅仅创建了一份期权合约，因为单方合约只有在完成所要求的履行后才能被接受。但如果银行提出签订双边合同，客户的出现（即履约开始）将被视为其接受的通知。\n\n教育目标：要形成单方合同，要约人必须通过执行特定行为来邀请受要约人接受要约。一旦受要约人开始，不可撤销的期权合约就创建了\n\n性能。如果受要约人通过完全履行指定行为接受要约，则具有约束力的单方合同将成立。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 45（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，当受要约人在要约形成单方合同后开始履约时，期权合同即成立）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要形成单方合同，要约人必须通过执行特定行为邀请受要约人接受要约。一旦受要约人开始，不可撤销的期权合约就创建了\n\n性能。如果受要约人通过完全履行指定行为接受要约，则具有约束力的单方合同将成立。","iracZh":{"issue":"客户是否向银行索赔 25 美元？","rule":"要形成单方合同，要约人必须通过执行特定行为邀请受要约人接受要约。一旦受要约人开始，不可撤销的期权合约就创建了\n\n性能。如果受要约人通过完全履行指定行为接受要约，则具有约束力的单方合同将成立。","analysis":"双边合同与单边合同\n\n优惠期初表现效果\n\n单方面邀请接受 创建防止要约人履行行为以撤销要约的期权合同 受要约人没有义务完全履行 双边邀请返回接受 作为对要约承诺的接受 受要约人有义务完全履行 当一方（即要约人）邀请另一方（即受要约人）仅通过执行特定行为来接受要约时，形成单方合同的要约就产生了。一旦受要约人开始履行，要约就不可撤销，从而创建了一份期权合约，即一份单独的协议，根据该协议，要约将在一段时间内保持开放且不可撤销。一旦受要约人完成所要求的履行，并形成具有约束力的单方合同，该要约即被接受。*此处，银行的海报提出向任何排队超过五分钟的新客户支付 25 美元。新顾客阅读了海报并开始排队表演，因此经理随后无法通过撕下海报并宣布促销结束来撤回优惠（选项 A）。当客户在规定的时间内排队等候完成履行后，具有约束力的合同就成立了。因此，客户向银行提出索赔，指控银行未能向他支付 25 美元，从而违反了合同。 *如果受要约人开始但未能完成所要求的履行（即部分履行），则不存在合同，因为只有完全履行才构成对要约的接受。因此，在这种情况下，要约人不能向受要约人提出违约索赔。 （选项 B） 银行的声明不是礼物承诺，因为提供 25 美元是为了换取法律损害，即在客户没有法律义务的情况下排队超过五分钟。 （选项D） 客户的出现仅仅创建了一份期权合约，因为单方合约只有在完成所要求的履行后才能被接受。但如果银行提出签订双边合同，客户的出现（即履约开始）将被视为其接受的通知。\n\n教育目标：要形成单方合同，要约人必须通过执行特定行为来邀请受要约人接受要约。一旦受要约人开始，不可撤销的期权合约就创建了\n\n性能。如果受要约人通过完全履行指定行为接受要约，则具有约束力的单方合同将成立。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 45（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，当受要约人在要约形成单方合同后开始履约时，期权合同即成立）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为一旦客户开始履约，银行就无法撤销其报价。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":160,"sourceQuestionPage":473,"sourceQuestionPages":[473],"sourceExplanationPages":[474,475],"sourceAnswerPages":[474,475],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-161","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A distributor of industrial products was unhappy with the quality of its previous supplier's heavy-duty electric wall sockets. On April 7, the distributor reached out to a new supplier in an email that stated in its entirety: \"Offer to purchase as many industrial straight-blade sockets as you can supply for delivery by May 6.\" That same day, the supplier responded to the distributor, \"Order received. Delivery by May 7.\" The supplier sent the distributor 200 of the only industrial straight-blade sockets it carried, at $1 over market price per unit, payment due on delivery. The distributor took delivery of the sockets on May 7 but rejected them without stating any basis for doing so. If the new supplier sues the distributor for breach of contract, will the supplier be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the quantity of sockets was not specified.","B":"No, because the supplier's response contained a different delivery date than the offer.","C":"Yes, because the court will apply UCC gap-fillers when the quantity of goods is not specified.","D":"Yes, because the offer included a means for determining the quantity of sockets to be sold."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract terms must be sufficiently certain and definite for the court to determine the existence of a breach and give an appropriate remedy. While the common law requires that all essential terms be covered in the agreement,* the UCC—which governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, sockets)—is more liberal. It encourages contract formation by providing \"gap-fillers\" for many missing terms, but not the: identity of the contracting parties subject matter of the contract or quantity of goods to be sold (Choice C). A contract must therefore specify a quantity that is certain or determinable by reference to objective facts, such as the buyer's actual requirements or the seller's actual output in a requirements or output contract. If it does not, the contract fails for indefiniteness. Here, the parties did not specify the exact quantity of sockets to be sold (Choice A). However, the distributor offered to purchase as many straight-blade sockets as the supplier could supply by May 6. This provides a quantity term that is determinable by reference to objective facts, such as the supplier's inventory between the date the offer was received and May 6. Therefore, the supplier is likely to prevail in a breach-of-contract suit. *The omission of an essential term is not necessarily fatal if it appears that the parties intended to form a binding agreement. When this occurs, the court can look to the surrounding circumstances and the parties' conduct to fill in the term. (Choice B) Under the UCC, parties can form a contract even if the offer and the acceptance include different terms. When both parties are merchants (as seen here), most jurisdictions will \"knock out\" the differing terms and replace them with UCC gap-fillers as needed. Therefore, the differing delivery dates will be knocked out and delivery will be required within a reasonable time.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC \"fills the gap\" for missing contract terms other than the parties, subject matter, and quantity. The quantity term must specify an amount that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-201 cmt. 1 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (required terms for contracts for sale of goods).\n\nU.C.C. §§ 2-305–2-310 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (UCC gap-fillers).","educationalObjective":"The UCC \"fills the gap\" for missing contract terms other than the parties, subject matter, and quantity. The quantity term must specify an amount that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-201 cmt. 1 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (required terms for contracts for sale of goods).\n\nU.C.C. §§ 2-305–2-310 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (UCC gap-fillers).","rule":"The UCC \"fills the gap\" for missing contract terms other than the parties, subject matter, and quantity. The quantity term must specify an amount that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts.","irac":{"issue":"If the new supplier sues the distributor for breach of contract, will the supplier be likely to prevail?","rule":"The UCC \"fills the gap\" for missing contract terms other than the parties, subject matter, and quantity. The quantity term must specify an amount that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts.","analysis":"Contract terms must be sufficiently certain and definite for the court to determine the existence of a breach and give an appropriate remedy. While the common law requires that all essential terms be covered in the agreement,* the UCC—which governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, sockets)—is more liberal. It encourages contract formation by providing \"gap-fillers\" for many missing terms, but not the: identity of the contracting parties subject matter of the contract or quantity of goods to be sold (Choice C). A contract must therefore specify a quantity that is certain or determinable by reference to objective facts, such as the buyer's actual requirements or the seller's actual output in a requirements or output contract. If it does not, the contract fails for indefiniteness. Here, the parties did not specify the exact quantity of sockets to be sold (Choice A). However, the distributor offered to purchase as many straight-blade sockets as the supplier could supply by May 6. This provides a quantity term that is determinable by reference to objective facts, such as the supplier's inventory between the date the offer was received and May 6. Therefore, the supplier is likely to prevail in a breach-of-contract suit. *The omission of an essential term is not necessarily fatal if it appears that the parties intended to form a binding agreement. When this occurs, the court can look to the surrounding circumstances and the parties' conduct to fill in the term. (Choice B) Under the UCC, parties can form a contract even if the offer and the acceptance include different terms. When both parties are merchants (as seen here), most jurisdictions will \"knock out\" the differing terms and replace them with UCC gap-fillers as needed. Therefore, the differing delivery dates will be knocked out and delivery will be required within a reasonable time.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the offer included a means for determining the quantity of sockets to be sold."},"questionZh":"一家工业产品分销商对其先前供应商的重型墙壁插座的质量不满意。 4 月 7 日，该经销商在一封电子邮件中联系了一家新供应商，邮件全文如下：“愿意购买尽可能多的工业直插式插座，并在 5 月 6 日之前交货。”当天，供应商回复经销商：“订单已收到，5月7日前发货。”供应商向分销商发送了 200 个其仅有的工业直插片插座，每件价格比市场价格高出 1 美元，交货时付款。经销商于 5 月 7 日收到了插座，但拒绝了它们，并且没有说明这样做的任何依据。如果新供应商起诉经销商违约，供应商胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"否，因为未指定插座数量。","B":"否，因为供应商的回复包含与报价不同的交货日期。","C":"是的，因为当货物数量未指定时，法院将适用 UCC 补缺。","D":"是的，因为该报价包括确定要销售的插座数量的方法。"},"explanationZh":"合同条款必须足够确定和明确，以便法院确定是否存在违约行为并给予适当的补救措施。虽然普通法要求协​​议中涵盖所有基本条款，* UCC（管辖商品销售合同（例如插座））则更为自由。它通过为许多缺失的条款提供“空白填充”来鼓励合同的形成，但不包括：缔约方的身份、合同的标的物或待售货物的数量（选项 C）。因此，合同必须指定一个确定的或可根据客观事实确定的数量，例如需求或产量合同中买方的实际需求或卖方的实际产量。如果不这样做，合同就会无限期失效。在此，双方没有具体说明要出售的插座的确切数量（选项A）。然而，经销商提出在5月6日之前购买供应商能够提供的尽可能多的直插片插座。这提供了一个可以参考客观事实确定的数量条款，例如供应商在收到报价之日到5月6日之间的库存。因此，供应商很可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 *如果双方似乎打算达成具有约束力的协议，则省略基本条款并不一定是致命的。发生这种情况时，法院可以根据周围情况和当事人的行为来填写该条款。 （选择B）根据UCC，即使要约和承诺包含不同的条款，双方也可以签订合同。当双方都是商家时（如此处所示），大多数司法管辖区将“淘汰”不同的条款，并根据需要用 UCC 填补空白的条款进行替换。因此，不同的交货日期将被淘汰，并要求在合理的时间内交货。\n\n教育目标：UCC“填补了”除了当事人、标的物和数量之外缺失的合同条款的空白。数量术语必须指定一个确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的数量。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-201 cmt。 1 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（货物销售合同的必需条款）。\n\nUCC §§ 2-305–2-310（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（UCC 填补空白）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC“填补了”除了当事人、标的物和数量之外缺失的合同条款的空白。数量术语必须指定一个确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的数量。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果新供应商起诉经销商违约，供应商胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"UCC“填补了”除了当事人、标的物和数量之外缺失的合同条款的空白。数量术语必须指定一个确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的数量。","analysis":"合同条款必须足够确定和明确，以便法院确定是否存在违约行为并给予适当的补救措施。虽然普通法要求协​​议中涵盖所有基本条款，* UCC（管辖商品销售合同（例如插座））则更为自由。它通过为许多缺失的条款提供“空白填充”来鼓励合同的形成，但不包括：缔约方的身份、合同的标的物或待售货物的数量（选项 C）。因此，合同必须指定一个确定的或可根据客观事实确定的数量，例如需求或产量合同中买方的实际需求或卖方的实际产量。如果不这样做，合同就会无限期失效。在此，双方没有具体说明要出售的插座的确切数量（选项A）。然而，经销商提出在5月6日之前购买供应商能够提供的尽可能多的直插片插座。这提供了一个可以参考客观事实确定的数量条款，例如供应商在收到报价之日到5月6日之间的库存。因此，供应商很可能在违约诉讼中胜诉。 *如果双方似乎打算达成具有约束力的协议，则省略基本条款并不一定是致命的。发生这种情况时，法院可以根据周围情况和当事人的行为来填写该条款。 （选择B）根据UCC，即使要约和承诺包含不同的条款，双方也可以签订合同。当双方都是商家时（如此处所示），大多数司法管辖区将“淘汰”不同的条款，并根据需要用 UCC 填补空白的条款进行替换。因此，不同的交货日期将被淘汰，并要求在合理的时间内交货。\n\n教育目标：UCC“填补了”除了当事人、标的物和数量之外缺失的合同条款的空白。数量术语必须指定一个确定的或能够通过参考客观事实确定的数量。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-201 cmt。 1 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（货物销售合同的必需条款）。\n\nUCC §§ 2-305–2-310（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（UCC 填补空白）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为该报价包括确定要销售的插座数量的方法。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"output contract","ipa":"/ˈaʊtpˌʊt kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"out·put con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"产量合同：卖方承诺向买方出售其善意产量","contextualMeaning":"产量合同：卖方承诺向买方出售其善意产量","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A contract must therefore specify a quantity that is certain or determinable by reference to objective facts, such as the buyer's actual requirements or the seller's actual output in a requirements or output contract.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5d4f7a7a427ced30"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":161,"sourceQuestionPage":476,"sourceQuestionPages":[476],"sourceExplanationPages":[477,478],"sourceAnswerPages":[477,478],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-162","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"On June 1, a seller received a mail order from a buyer requesting prompt shipment of a specified computer model at the seller's current catalog price. On June 2, the seller mailed to the buyer a letter accepting the order and assuring the buyer that the computer would be shipped on June 3. On June 3, the seller realized that he was out of that computer model, shipped a different computer model to the buyer, and mailed a separate notice of accommodation. On June 5, the buyer received the seller's June 2 letter and the different computer model, but not the notice of accommodation. On June 5, which of the following is a correct statement of the parties' legal rights and duties?","choices":{"A":"The buyer can either accept or reject the different computer model and, in either event, recover damages, if any, for breach of contract.","B":"The buyer can either accept or reject the different computer model, but if he rejects it, he will thereby waive any remedy for breach of contract.","C":"The seller's notice of accommodation was timely mailed, and his shipment of the different computer model constituted a counteroffer.","D":"The seller's prompt shipment of nonconforming goods constituted an acceptance of the buyer's offer, thereby creating a contract for the sale of the different computer model."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, computers). Under the UCC, a seller can accept a buyer's offer to purchase goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship, or shipping, the goods. Once the offer is accepted, the shipped goods must conform perfectly to the contract. Nonconformance is a breach that allows the buyer to accept or reject the goods in whole or in part. The buyer can also recover any damages suffered as a result of the breach. Here, the buyer mailed an order to the seller requesting prompt shipment of a specified computer model. This was an offer that the seller could accept by promising to ship, or shipping, the computer. The seller promised to ship the computer. However, the seller breached the resulting contract by shipping a different, nonconforming computer model. As a result, the buyer can either accept or reject the different computer model and, in either event, recover any damages for breach of contract (Choice B). (Choices C & D) The seller's shipment of the nonconforming computer was not an acceptance. That is because the seller had already accepted the buyer's offer by promising to ship the specified computer model. As a result, the notice of accommodation is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC allows a buyer to accept or reject nonconforming goods in whole or in part and, in either event, recover damages for breach.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (offer and acceptance).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's rights on improper delivery).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's remedies for breach).","educationalObjective":"The UCC allows a buyer to accept or reject nonconforming goods in whole or in part and, in either event, recover damages for breach.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (offer and acceptance).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's rights on improper delivery).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's remedies for breach).","rule":"The UCC allows a buyer to accept or reject nonconforming goods in whole or in part and, in either event, recover damages for breach.","irac":{"issue":"On June 5, which of the following is a correct statement of the parties' legal rights and duties?","rule":"The UCC allows a buyer to accept or reject nonconforming goods in whole or in part and, in either event, recover damages for breach.","analysis":"Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, computers). Under the UCC, a seller can accept a buyer's offer to purchase goods for prompt or immediate shipment by promising to ship, or shipping, the goods. Once the offer is accepted, the shipped goods must conform perfectly to the contract. Nonconformance is a breach that allows the buyer to accept or reject the goods in whole or in part. The buyer can also recover any damages suffered as a result of the breach. Here, the buyer mailed an order to the seller requesting prompt shipment of a specified computer model. This was an offer that the seller could accept by promising to ship, or shipping, the computer. The seller promised to ship the computer. However, the seller breached the resulting contract by shipping a different, nonconforming computer model. As a result, the buyer can either accept or reject the different computer model and, in either event, recover any damages for breach of contract (Choice B). (Choices C & D) The seller's shipment of the nonconforming computer was not an acceptance. That is because the seller had already accepted the buyer's offer by promising to ship the specified computer model. As a result, the notice of accommodation is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: The buyer can either accept or reject the different computer model and, in either event, recover damages, if any, for breach of contract."},"questionZh":"6 月 1 日，卖方收到买方的邮购订单，要求按卖方当前的目录价格立即发货指定的计算机型号。 6 月 2 日，卖方向买方邮寄了一封接受订单的信函，并向买方保证计算机将于 6 月 3 日发货。6 月 3 日，卖方意识到自己没有该型号的计算机，于是向买方发送了另一台计算机型号，并单独邮寄了一份替代交付通知（accommodation notice）。 6月5日，买方收到了卖方6月2日的信函和不同的电脑型号，但没有收到替代交付通知（accommodation notice）。 6月5日，下列关于当事人合法权利和义务的表述正确的是？","choicesZh":{"A":"买方可以接受或拒绝不同的计算机型号，并且在任何一种情况下，都可以因违反合同而获得损害赔偿（如果有）。","B":"买方可以接受或拒绝不同的计算机型号，但如果他拒绝，他将放弃任何违约补救措施。","C":"卖方的替代交付通知（accommodation notice）已及时寄出，他所装运的不同型号的计算机构成了还盘。","D":"卖方及时发运不合格货物即构成对买方要约的接受，从而订立了不同计算机型号的销售合同。"},"explanationZh":"《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 第 2 条管辖商品（例如计算机）销售合同。根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或装运货物来接受买方购买货物并立即或立即装运的要约。一旦报价被接受，装运的货物必须完全符合合同。不合格是指允许买方接受或拒绝全部或部分货物的违约行为。买方还可以追偿因违约行为而遭受的任何损失。在此，买方向卖方邮寄订单，要求立即发货指定的计算机型号。卖方可以通过承诺运送或运送计算机来接受这一要约。卖家承诺运送电脑。然而，卖方因运输不同的、不合格的计算机型号而违反了由此产生的合同。因此，买方可以接受或拒绝不同的计算机型号，并且无论哪种情况，都可以获得因违约而造成的任何损害赔偿（选择 B）。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 卖方对不合格​​计算机的发货并不构成验收。这是因为卖方已经接受了买方的报价并承诺运送指定的电脑型号。因此，替代交付通知（accommodation notice）是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：UCC 允许买方接受或拒绝全部或部分不合格货物，并且在任何一种情况下均可获得违约损失赔偿。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（要约和接受）。\n\nUCC § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方对不当交付的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的违约补救措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 允许买方接受或拒绝全部或部分不合格货物，并且在任何一种情况下均可获得违约损失赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"6月5日，下列关于当事人合法权利和义务的表述正确的是？","rule":"UCC 允许买方接受或拒绝全部或部分不合格货物，并且在任何一种情况下均可获得违约损失赔偿。","analysis":"《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 第 2 条管辖商品（例如计算机）销售合同。根据 UCC，卖方可以通过承诺运送或装运货物来接受买方购买货物并立即或立即装运的要约。一旦报价被接受，装运的货物必须完全符合合同。不合格是指允许买方接受或拒绝全部或部分货物的违约行为。买方还可以追偿因违约行为而遭受的任何损失。在此，买方向卖方邮寄订单，要求立即发货指定的计算机型号。卖方可以通过承诺运送或运送计算机来接受这一要约。卖家承诺运送电脑。然而，卖方因运输不同的、不合格的计算机型号而违反了由此产生的合同。因此，买方可以接受或拒绝不同的计算机型号，并且无论哪种情况，都可以获得因违约而造成的任何损害赔偿（选择 B）。 （选择 C ​​和 D） 卖方对不合格​​计算机的发货并不构成验收。这是因为卖方已经接受了买方的报价并承诺运送指定的电脑型号。因此，替代交付通知（accommodation notice）是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：UCC 允许买方接受或拒绝全部或部分不合格货物，并且在任何一种情况下均可获得违约损失赔偿。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-206 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（要约和接受）。\n\nUCC § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方对不当交付的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的违约补救措施）。","conclusion":"答案 A：买方可以接受或拒绝不同的计算机型号，并且在任何一种情况下，都可以因违反合同而获得损害赔偿（如果有）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-bf15eb43d59360d5"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":162,"sourceQuestionPage":479,"sourceQuestionPages":[479],"sourceExplanationPages":[480,481],"sourceAnswerPages":[480,481],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-163","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"A retiree was known to walk her beloved dog around the neighborhood daily. One evening, she forgot to shut her gate, and her dog escaped. The retiree placed posters around the neighborhood stating, \"Lost dog! $500 reward. No questions asked.\" The dog had wandered to a nearby store, and the store owner kept the dog and fed it for a few days. A customer saw the dog and told the store owner that it looked like the retiree's dog. The store owner took the dog to the retiree's home, and the dog was happily reunited with its owner. The store owner had not seen the posters and did not ask for the reward. On the way back to the store, the store owner saw one of the posters and called the retiree to inquire about the reward. The retiree refused to pay the reward. If the store owner sues the retiree for breach of contract, will the store owner be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the reward poster did not manifest a willingness to enter into an agreement.","B":"No, because the store owner was unaware of the reward poster when he returned the dog.","C":"Yes, because the parties formed a contract when the store owner returned the dog.","D":"Yes, because the retiree's offer could not be revoked after the store owner completed performance."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An offer to form a unilateral contract invites acceptance only by performance of a specified act, while an offer to form a bilateral contract invites acceptance by a promise to perform the act. But regardless of whether the offer is for a unilateral or bilateral contract, the offer must be reasonably communicated to the offeree and the offeree must be aware of the offer to accept it. Otherwise, there is no mutual assent to form a contract. Here, the retiree's posters constituted a unilateral offer to pay $500 for the return of her lost dog. The store owner completed the requested performance by returning the dog to the retiree's home. But since the store owner had not yet seen the posters, he was unaware of the unilateral offer when he returned the dog. This means that there was no mutual assent, no contract, and no breach (Choice C). Therefore, the store owner is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) A communication is an offer if it manifests the offeror's willingness to enter an agreement. Here, the reward poster did just that—it manifested the retiree's willingness to exchange $500 for the return of her dog. (Choice D) An offer becomes irrevocable after it has been accepted. But here, the store owner did not accept the offer by returning the dog because he was unaware of the retiree's posters at that time. Therefore, the retiree could revoke the offer and refuse to pay the reward.\n\nEducational objective: Offers to form unilateral or bilateral contracts must be reasonably communicated to the offeree, and the offeree must be aware of the offer to accept it. Otherwise, there can be no mutual assent and therefore no contract.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 23 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an acceptance must be made with reference to the offer).","educationalObjective":"Offers to form unilateral or bilateral contracts must be reasonably communicated to the offeree, and the offeree must be aware of the offer to accept it. Otherwise, there can be no mutual assent and therefore no contract.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 23 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an acceptance must be made with reference to the offer).","rule":"Offers to form unilateral or bilateral contracts must be reasonably communicated to the offeree, and the offeree must be aware of the offer to accept it. Otherwise, there can be no mutual assent and therefore no contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the store owner sues the retiree for breach of contract, will the store owner be likely to prevail?","rule":"Offers to form unilateral or bilateral contracts must be reasonably communicated to the offeree, and the offeree must be aware of the offer to accept it. Otherwise, there can be no mutual assent and therefore no contract.","analysis":"An offer to form a unilateral contract invites acceptance only by performance of a specified act, while an offer to form a bilateral contract invites acceptance by a promise to perform the act. But regardless of whether the offer is for a unilateral or bilateral contract, the offer must be reasonably communicated to the offeree and the offeree must be aware of the offer to accept it. Otherwise, there is no mutual assent to form a contract. Here, the retiree's posters constituted a unilateral offer to pay $500 for the return of her lost dog. The store owner completed the requested performance by returning the dog to the retiree's home. But since the store owner had not yet seen the posters, he was unaware of the unilateral offer when he returned the dog. This means that there was no mutual assent, no contract, and no breach (Choice C). Therefore, the store owner is unlikely to prevail. (Choice A) A communication is an offer if it manifests the offeror's willingness to enter an agreement. Here, the reward poster did just that—it manifested the retiree's willingness to exchange $500 for the return of her dog. (Choice D) An offer becomes irrevocable after it has been accepted. But here, the store owner did not accept the offer by returning the dog because he was unaware of the retiree's posters at that time. Therefore, the retiree could revoke the offer and refuse to pay the reward.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the store owner was unaware of the reward poster when he returned the dog."},"questionZh":"据了解，一位退休人员每天都会在附近遛她心爱的狗。一天晚上，她忘记关门，她的狗逃走了。这位退休人员在附近张贴海报，上面写着：“狗丢了！悬赏 500 美元。不问任何问题。”这只狗流浪到了附近的一家商店，店主把狗养了起来，并喂了它几天。一位顾客看到了这只狗，告诉店主它看起来像退休人员的狗。店主把狗带到了退休人员家里，狗很高兴与主人团聚。店主没有看到海报，也没有要求打赏。回店的路上，店主看到了其中一张海报，便打电话给退休人员询问奖励事宜。退休人员拒绝支付奖金。如果店主起诉退休人员违约，店主胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为悬赏海报并没有表现出签订协议的意愿。","B":"没有，因为店主归还狗时并不知道有奖励海报。","C":"是的，因为店主归还狗时双方就签订了合同。","D":"是的，因为退休人员的报价在店主完成业绩后无法撤销。"},"explanationZh":"形成单方合同的要约仅通过履行特定行为来邀请接受，而形成双边合同的要约则通过承诺履行该行为来邀请接受。但无论要约是针对单边合同还是双边合同，要约都必须合理地传达给受要约人，并且受要约人必须知道要约才能接受要约。否则，双方均不同意订立合同。在这里，这位退休人员的海报构成了单方面的提议，愿意支付 500 美元来归还她丢失的狗。店主将狗送回退休人员家中，完成了要求的履行。但由于店主还没有看到海报，所以在归还狗时他并不知道单方面的报价。这意味着没有双方同意、没有合同、也没有违约（选项 C）。因此，店主获胜的可能性不大。 （选项 A）如果沟通表明要约人愿意达成协议，则该沟通是要约。在这里，奖励海报就做到了这一点——它表明退休人员愿意用 500 美元换回她的狗。 （选择D）要约一经接受就不可撤销。但在这里，店主并没有接受归还狗的提议，因为他当时不知道退休人员的海报。因此，退休人员可以撤销该提议并拒绝支付奖励。\n\n教育目标：形成单边或双边合同的要约必须合理地传达给受要约人，并且受要约人必须知道要约并接受它。否则，就无法达成双方同意，也就无法签订合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 23 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（解释必须参照要约作出接受）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"形成单边或双边合同的要约必须合理地传达给受要约人，并且受要约人必须知道要约才能接受。否则，就无法达成双方同意，也就无法签订合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果店主起诉退休人员违约，店主胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"形成单边或双边合同的要约必须合理地传达给受要约人，并且受要约人必须知道要约才能接受。否则，就无法达成双方同意，也就无法签订合同。","analysis":"形成单方合同的要约仅通过履行特定行为来邀请接受，而形成双边合同的要约则通过承诺履行该行为来邀请接受。但无论要约是针对单边合同还是双边合同，要约都必须合理地传达给受要约人，并且受要约人必须知道要约才能接受要约。否则，双方均不同意订立合同。在这里，这位退休人员的海报构成了单方面的提议，愿意支付 500 美元来归还她丢失的狗。店主将狗送回退休人员家中，完成了要求的履行。但由于店主还没有看到海报，所以在归还狗时他并不知道单方面的报价。这意味着没有双方同意、没有合同、也没有违约（选项 C）。因此，店主获胜的可能性不大。 （选项 A）如果沟通表明要约人愿意达成协议，则该沟通是要约。在这里，奖励海报就做到了这一点——它表明退休人员愿意用 500 美元换回她的狗。 （选择D）要约一经接受就不可撤销。但在这里，店主并没有接受归还狗的提议，因为他当时不知道退休人员的海报。因此，退休人员可以撤销该提议并拒绝支付奖励。\n\n教育目标：形成单边或双边合同的要约必须合理地传达给受要约人，并且受要约人必须知道要约并接受它。否则，就无法达成双方同意，也就无法签订合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 23 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（解释必须参照要约作出接受）。","conclusion":"答案 B：没有，因为店主归还狗时并不知道有奖励海报。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-adc3233c22077cf7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":163,"sourceQuestionPage":482,"sourceQuestionPages":[482],"sourceExplanationPages":[483,484],"sourceAnswerPages":[483,484],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-164","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mutual Assent & Misunderstanding","question":"A wealthy rancher was drinking at a local bar. The rancher's neighbor walked into the bar, unaware of the rancher's intoxication. On several previous occasions, the neighbor had tried to persuade the rancher to sell his vintage muscle car, but the rancher had refused. Upon seeing the rancher, the neighbor said, \"Hey, did you change your mind about selling me that vintage muscle car? I'll increase my offer to $15,000.\" The rancher was annoyed and decided to play a joke on the neighbor. The rancher had no intention of selling his car but said, \"Fine, I will sell you the car.\" The rancher then grabbed a napkin and wrote: \"You finally bugged me enough! I will sell you my vintage muscle car for $15,000.\" The rancher signed the napkin and handed it to the neighbor. The neighbor happily walked away, believing that they had formed a binding agreement. The following day, the neighbor approached the rancher to consummate the sale. The rancher refused to part with the car, claiming that he was intoxicated and merely joking when he wrote the note on the napkin. If the neighbor sues the rancher for breach of contract, will the neighbor be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the rancher was joking when he wrote the note, so there was no meeting of the minds.","B":"No, because the rancher wrote the note while he was intoxicated, which renders the contract voidable.","C":"Yes, because the rancher's oral statement and written note were objective manifestations of his intent to enter a contract.","D":"Yes, because the written note estops the rancher from asserting any defense based on incapacity to contract."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). During the negotiation process, the parties are entitled to rely on a reasonable interpretation of each other's words and actions since they cannot know each other's subjective intent. This means that a contract is formed when both parties objectively express their assent to enter a binding agreement. Once formed, the contract cannot be rescinded because of one party's secret intent not to be bound. Here, the neighbor offered to buy the rancher's muscle car for $15,000. This was an objective expression of his intent to enter a contract, particularly since he had tried to buy the car on several previous occasions. The rancher orally accepted this offer and wrote the agreement on a napkin (objective assent), without giving any indication that he was joking (secret intent). Therefore, a contract was formed, and the neighbor is likely to prevail in his breach-of-contract suit. (Choice A) At common law, contract formation required a subjective \"meeting of the minds.\" The modern rule, however, looks to the parties' objective expressions of assent. Therefore, it is irrelevant that the rancher was subjectively joking and that there was no actual meeting of the minds. (Choice B) A contract entered while intoxicated is voidable by the intoxicated party if (1) that party was too intoxicated to reasonably understand the nature or consequences of the contract and (2) the other party had reason to know about the intoxication. Here, there is no indication that the rancher was intoxicated to this extent. And since the neighbor had just walked into the bar, he had no reason to know that the rancher was intoxicated. (Choice D) Both oral and written contracts are voidable by a party who lacked capacity at the time of formation. Therefore, the written note does not estop (ie, prevent) the rancher from asserting an incapacity defense.\n\nEducational objective: Contract formation requires mutual assent, which occurs when both parties objectively convey their assent to enter a binding agreement. The resulting contract cannot be rescinded due to one party's secret intent not to be bound.\n\nReferences\nLucy v. Zehmer, 196 Va. 493, 503 (1954) (finding a binding contract for the sale of a farm even though the seller was drinking and possibly joking when he signed the agreement).","educationalObjective":"Contract formation requires mutual assent, which occurs when both parties objectively convey their assent to enter a binding agreement. The resulting contract cannot be rescinded due to one party's secret intent not to be bound.","references":"Lucy v. Zehmer, 196 Va. 493, 503 (1954) (finding a binding contract for the sale of a farm even though the seller was drinking and possibly joking when he signed the agreement).","rule":"Contract formation requires mutual assent, which occurs when both parties objectively convey their assent to enter a binding agreement. The resulting contract cannot be rescinded due to one party's secret intent not to be bound.","irac":{"issue":"If the neighbor sues the rancher for breach of contract, will the neighbor be likely to prevail?","rule":"Contract formation requires mutual assent, which occurs when both parties objectively convey their assent to enter a binding agreement. The resulting contract cannot be rescinded due to one party's secret intent not to be bound.","analysis":"Contract formation requires mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). During the negotiation process, the parties are entitled to rely on a reasonable interpretation of each other's words and actions since they cannot know each other's subjective intent. This means that a contract is formed when both parties objectively express their assent to enter a binding agreement. Once formed, the contract cannot be rescinded because of one party's secret intent not to be bound. Here, the neighbor offered to buy the rancher's muscle car for $15,000. This was an objective expression of his intent to enter a contract, particularly since he had tried to buy the car on several previous occasions. The rancher orally accepted this offer and wrote the agreement on a napkin (objective assent), without giving any indication that he was joking (secret intent). Therefore, a contract was formed, and the neighbor is likely to prevail in his breach-of-contract suit. (Choice A) At common law, contract formation required a subjective \"meeting of the minds.\" The modern rule, however, looks to the parties' objective expressions of assent. Therefore, it is irrelevant that the rancher was subjectively joking and that there was no actual meeting of the minds. (Choice B) A contract entered while intoxicated is voidable by the intoxicated party if (1) that party was too intoxicated to reasonably understand the nature or consequences of the contract and (2) the other party had reason to know about the intoxication. Here, there is no indication that the rancher was intoxicated to this extent. And since the neighbor had just walked into the bar, he had no reason to know that the rancher was intoxicated. (Choice D) Both oral and written contracts are voidable by a party who lacked capacity at the time of formation. Therefore, the written note does not estop (ie, prevent) the rancher from asserting an incapacity defense.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the rancher's oral statement and written note were objective manifestations of his intent to enter a contract."},"questionZh":"一位富有的牧场主正在当地一家酒吧喝酒。牧场主的邻居走进酒吧，没有意识到牧场主喝醉了。此前，邻居曾多次试图说服牧场主卖掉他的老式肌肉车，但牧场主都拒绝了。邻居看到牧场主后说道：“嘿，你改变主意要卖给我那辆老式肌肉车了吗？我会把报价提高到 15,000 美元。”牧场主很生气，决定跟邻居开个玩笑。牧场主并没有卖车的意思，只是说：“好吧，我把车卖给你吧。”然后牧场主抓起一张餐巾纸写道：“你终于够烦我了！我将以 15,000 美元的价格卖给你我的老式肌肉车。”牧场主在餐巾上签了字，然后递给了邻居。邻居高兴地走开了，相信他们已经达成了具有约束力的协议。第二天，邻居联系牧场主以完成出售。牧场主拒绝放弃这辆车，声称他在餐巾纸上写下这张纸条时只是喝醉了，只是在开玩笑。如果邻居起诉牧场主违约，邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为牧场主写纸条时是在开玩笑，所以没有达成共识。","B":"不可以，因为牧场主是在喝醉酒时写下这张纸条的，这使得合同无效。","C":"是的，因为牧场主的口头陈述和书面说明是其签订合同意图的客观体现。","D":"是的，因为书面说明阻止牧场主以无能力签订合同为由提出任何抗辩。"},"explanationZh":"合同的订立需要双方同意（即要约和接受）。在谈判过程中，当事人无法了解对方的主观意图，因此有权对对方的言行做出合理的解释。这意味着当双方客观地表示同意签订具有约束力的协议时，合同即成立。合同一经成立，不得因一方有意不接受约束而撤销。在这里，邻居提出以 15,000 美元购买牧场主的肌肉车。这是他签订合同意图的客观表达，特别是因为他之前曾多次试图购买这辆车。牧场主口头接受了这一提议，并将协议写在餐巾纸上（客观同意），没有表明他在开玩笑（秘密意图）。因此，合同成立，邻居的违约诉讼很可能胜诉。 （选项 A） 在普通法中，合同的成立需要主观的“意见一致”。然而，现代规则注重当事人客观的同意表达。因此，牧场主主观上是在开玩笑，并且没有真正达成共识，这无关紧要。 （选择 B）如果（1）该方醉酒过度而无法合理理解合同的性质或后果，并且（2）另一方有理由知道该醉酒情况，则醉酒期间签订的合同可以被醉酒方撤销。在这里，没有迹象表明牧场主醉酒到这种程度。而且邻居刚刚走进酒吧，他没有理由知道牧场主喝醉了。 （选项 D） 口头和书面合同均可被在成立时缺乏行为能力的一方撤销。因此，书面说明不会阻止（即阻止）牧场主提出无行为能力抗辩。\n\n教育目标：合同的成立需要双方同意，当双方客观地表示同意签订具有约束力的协议时，就会发生这种情况。由此产生的合同不能因一方秘密意图不接受约束而撤销。\n\n参考文献\nLucy v. Zehmer, 196 Va. 493, 503 (1954)（找到一份具有约束力的农场出售合同，即使卖方在签署协议时正在喝酒并且可能在开玩笑）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"合同的成立需要双方同意，当双方客观地表示同意签订具有约束力的协议时，就会发生这种情况。由此产生的合同不能因一方秘密意图不接受约束而撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果邻居起诉牧场主违约，邻居胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"合同的成立需要双方同意，当双方客观地表示同意签订具有约束力的协议时，就会发生这种情况。由此产生的合同不能因一方秘密意图不接受约束而撤销。","analysis":"合同的订立需要双方同意（即要约和接受）。在谈判过程中，当事人无法了解对方的主观意图，因此有权对对方的言行做出合理的解释。这意味着当双方客观地表示同意签订具有约束力的协议时，合同即成立。合同一经成立，不得因一方有意不接受约束而撤销。在这里，邻居提出以 15,000 美元购买牧场主的肌肉车。这是他签订合同意图的客观表达，特别是因为他之前曾多次试图购买这辆车。牧场主口头接受了这一提议，并将协议写在餐巾纸上（客观同意），没有表明他在开玩笑（秘密意图）。因此，合同成立，邻居的违约诉讼很可能胜诉。 （选项 A） 在普通法中，合同的成立需要主观的“意见一致”。然而，现代规则注重当事人客观的同意表达。因此，牧场主主观上是在开玩笑，并且没有真正达成共识，这无关紧要。 （选择 B）如果（1）该方醉酒过度而无法合理理解合同的性质或后果，并且（2）另一方有理由知道该醉酒情况，则醉酒期间签订的合同可以被醉酒方撤销。在这里，没有迹象表明牧场主醉酒到这种程度。而且邻居刚刚走进酒吧，他没有理由知道牧场主喝醉了。 （选项 D） 口头和书面合同均可被在成立时缺乏行为能力的一方撤销。因此，书面说明不会阻止（即阻止）牧场主提出无行为能力抗辩。\n\n教育目标：合同的成立需要双方同意，当双方客观地表示同意签订具有约束力的协议时，就会发生这种情况。由此产生的合同不能因一方秘密意图不接受约束而撤销。\n\n参考文献\nLucy v. Zehmer, 196 Va. 493, 503 (1954)（找到一份具有约束力的农场出售合同，即使卖方在签署协议时正在喝酒并且可能在开玩笑）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为牧场主的口头陈述和书面说明是其签订合同意图的客观体现。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-8cf5a77d1a8fb53e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":164,"sourceQuestionPage":485,"sourceQuestionPages":[485],"sourceExplanationPages":[486,487],"sourceAnswerPages":[486,487],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-165","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A borrower owed a lender $50,000 due on March 1. On January 10, the lender telephoned the borrower and said that he would discharge the debt if the borrower would promise to pay the lender $45,000 by January 15. The borrower responded, \"I will attempt to get the money together.\" On January 11, the lender again telephoned the borrower and said that he had changed his mind and would expect the borrower to make full payment on March 1. On January 15, the borrower tendered $45,000 as full payment, which the lender refused to accept. On March 1, the borrower refused the lender's demand for $50,000, and the lender sued for that amount. Which of the following statements best supports the lender's position?","choices":{"A":"The borrower's January 10 statement was not a return promise, and therefore the lender effectively revoked his offer on January 11.","B":"The January 10 telephone conversation between the lender and the borrower created an executory accord and therefore did not operate as a discharge of the $50,000 debt.","C":"The lender's offer to discharge the debt was a gift promise and therefore was not binding on the lender.","D":"The lender's promise to discharge the $50,000 debt was not enforceable because it was not in writing."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Termination of offer before acceptance\n\nOfferor's Offeror communicates revocation directly to offeree revocation Offeree learns information from reliable source that reasonably indicates offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer) Offeree's Offeree communicates rejection directly to offeror rejection Offeree's counteroffer serves as rejection & new offer* Lapse Time period specified in offer expires After reasonable time if no time period specified in offer By law Either party dies or is adjudicated insane Subject matter of offer is destroyed or becomes illegal *Counteroffer does not terminate offer if offeree manifests intent to take offer under advisement.\n\nTo form a new contract or modify an existing one, there must be an offer that is accepted and supported by valuable consideration. Acceptance occurs when the offeree makes an objective manifestation to be bound by the terms of the offer. The offeror is entitled to know in clear terms whether the offeree accepts the offer, so the offeree's acceptance must be unequivocal (ie, certain) or it will not bind either party. And until the offeree accepts the offer, the offeror can revoke it. Here, the lender's offer to discharge the borrower's $50,000 debt due on March 1 asked that the borrower accept by making a return promise to pay $45,000 by January 15. But the borrower's January 10 statement that he would \"attempt to get the money together\" was not a clear and unequivocal promise to make the earlier payment (no acceptance). Therefore, the lender effectively revoked his offer on January 11 by telling the borrower that he had changed his mind—which supports his position that the borrower still owes the original $50,000 debt. (Choice B) An executory accord is a contract that is created when parties to an existing contract agree to give and accept something different in satisfaction of their existing contractual obligations. Once the accord is performed, the parties' obligations under the old contract are discharged. Here, an accord could not be created since there was no mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). (Choice C) A gift promise is a promise made without consideration. But since the lender offered to discharge the borrower's debt in exchange for an earlier payment, that offer was not a gift promise.\n\n(Choice D) A contract need only be in a writing signed by the party to be charged if the statute of frauds applies—not seen here. Therefore, the lack of a writing would not affect the enforceability of the lender's promise.\n\nEducational objective: Acceptance requires the offeree to make an objective manifestation of assent that is clear and unequivocal. Until the offeree does so, the offer can be revoked.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an offeror is not bound by an equivocal acceptance).","educationalObjective":"Acceptance requires the offeree to make an objective manifestation of assent that is clear and unequivocal. Until the offeree does so, the offer can be revoked.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 57 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an offeror is not bound by an equivocal acceptance).","rule":"Acceptance requires the offeree to make an objective manifestation of assent that is clear and unequivocal. Until the offeree does so, the offer can be revoked.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements best supports the lender's position?","rule":"Acceptance requires the offeree to make an objective manifestation of assent that is clear and unequivocal. Until the offeree does so, the offer can be revoked.","analysis":"Termination of offer before acceptance\n\nOfferor's Offeror communicates revocation directly to offeree revocation Offeree learns information from reliable source that reasonably indicates offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer) Offeree's Offeree communicates rejection directly to offeror rejection Offeree's counteroffer serves as rejection & new offer* Lapse Time period specified in offer expires After reasonable time if no time period specified in offer By law Either party dies or is adjudicated insane Subject matter of offer is destroyed or becomes illegal *Counteroffer does not terminate offer if offeree manifests intent to take offer under advisement.\n\nTo form a new contract or modify an existing one, there must be an offer that is accepted and supported by valuable consideration. Acceptance occurs when the offeree makes an objective manifestation to be bound by the terms of the offer. The offeror is entitled to know in clear terms whether the offeree accepts the offer, so the offeree's acceptance must be unequivocal (ie, certain) or it will not bind either party. And until the offeree accepts the offer, the offeror can revoke it. Here, the lender's offer to discharge the borrower's $50,000 debt due on March 1 asked that the borrower accept by making a return promise to pay $45,000 by January 15. But the borrower's January 10 statement that he would \"attempt to get the money together\" was not a clear and unequivocal promise to make the earlier payment (no acceptance). Therefore, the lender effectively revoked his offer on January 11 by telling the borrower that he had changed his mind—which supports his position that the borrower still owes the original $50,000 debt. (Choice B) An executory accord is a contract that is created when parties to an existing contract agree to give and accept something different in satisfaction of their existing contractual obligations. Once the accord is performed, the parties' obligations under the old contract are discharged. Here, an accord could not be created since there was no mutual assent (ie, offer and acceptance). (Choice C) A gift promise is a promise made without consideration. But since the lender offered to discharge the borrower's debt in exchange for an earlier payment, that offer was not a gift promise.\n\n(Choice D) A contract need only be in a writing signed by the party to be charged if the statute of frauds applies—not seen here. Therefore, the lack of a writing would not affect the enforceability of the lender's promise.","conclusion":"Answer A: The borrower's January 10 statement was not a return promise, and therefore the lender effectively revoked his offer on January 11."},"questionZh":"借款人欠贷款人 3 月 1 日到期的 50,000 美元。1 月 10 日，贷款人打电话给借款人，并表示如果借款人承诺在 1 月 15 日之前向贷款人支付 45,000 美元，他将免除债务。借款人回答说：“我会尽力凑齐这笔钱。” 1月11日，贷款人再次致电借款人，表示已改变主意，希望借款人在3月1日全额付款。1月15日，借款人提出全额付款45,000美元，但贷款人拒绝接受。 3 月 1 日，借款人拒绝了贷方 5 万美元的要求，贷方因此提起诉讼，索要这笔金额。以下哪项陈述最能支持贷方的立场？","choicesZh":{"A":"借款人 1 月 10 日的声明并非回报承诺，因此贷方实际上于 1 月 11 日撤销了其要约。","B":"1 月 10 日，贷款人和借款人之间的电话交谈达成了一项可执行协议，因此并不能免除 50,000 美元的债务。","C":"贷款人解除债务的提议是赠与承诺，因此对贷款人不具有约束力。","D":"贷款人解除 50,000 美元债务的承诺无法执行，因为它不是书面的。"},"explanationZh":"接受之前终止要约\n\n要约人的要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销要约 受要约人从可靠来源获悉合理表明要约被撤销的信息（例如，将房屋出售给另一位买家） 受要约人的受要约人直接向要约人拒绝要约 受要约人的还盘充当拒绝和新要约* 失效 要约中指定的期限 如果要约中没有指定时间段，则在合理时间后到期 法律规定 任何一方死亡或被判定精神失常 要约标的物被毁坏或变得非法 *还要约如果受要约人根据通知表明有意接受要约，则不会终止要约。\n\n要签订新合同或修改现有合同，必须有一个被接受并得到有价值对价支持的要约。当受要约人客观地表明受要约条款的约束时，即表示接受。要约人有权明确知道受要约人是否接受要约，因此受要约人的接受必须是明确的（即确定的），否则不会对任何一方产生约束力。在受要约人接受要约之前，要约人可以撤销要约。在此，贷款人提出要清偿借款人 3 月 1 日到期的 50,000 美元债务，要求借款人通过做出在 1 月 15 日之前支付 45,000 美元的回报承诺来接受。但借款人 1 月 10 日的声明称他将“尝试筹集资金”，这并不是一个明确且毫不含糊的提前付款承诺（不接受）。因此，贷方在 1 月 11 日告诉借款人他改变了主意，实际上撤销了他的要约，这支持了他的立场，即借款人仍欠原来的 50,000 美元债务。 （选择 B） 可执行协议是当现有合同的各方同意给予和接受不同的东西来满足其现有合同义务时创建的合同。一旦协议履行，双方在旧合同下的义务即解除。在这里，由于没有双方同意（即要约和接受），因此无法达成一致。 （选项C） 礼物承诺是不考虑任何考虑而作出的承诺。但由于贷款人提出免除借款人的债务以换取提前付款，因此该提议并不是礼物承诺。\n\n（选择 D）如果适用欺诈法，则只需由被指控方签署书面合同即可（此处未见）。因此，缺乏书面文件不会影响贷款人承诺的可执行性。\n\n教育目标：接受要求受要约人客观、明确地表示同意。在受要约人这样做之前，要约可以被撤销。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 57（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释要约人不受模棱两可的承诺的约束）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"接受要求受要约人客观、明确、明确地表示同意。在受要约人这样做之前，要约可以被撤销。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述最能支持贷方的立场？","rule":"接受要求受要约人客观、明确、明确地表示同意。在受要约人这样做之前，要约可以被撤销。","analysis":"接受之前终止要约\n\n要约人的要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销要约 受要约人从可靠来源获悉合理表明要约被撤销的信息（例如，将房屋出售给另一位买家） 受要约人的受要约人直接向要约人拒绝要约 受要约人的还盘充当拒绝和新要约* 失效 要约中指定的期限 如果要约中没有指定时间段，则在合理时间后到期 法律规定 任何一方死亡或被判定精神失常 要约标的物被毁坏或变得非法 *还要约如果受要约人根据通知表明有意接受要约，则不会终止要约。\n\n要签订新合同或修改现有合同，必须有一个被接受并得到有价值对价支持的要约。当受要约人客观地表明受要约条款的约束时，即表示接受。要约人有权明确知道受要约人是否接受要约，因此受要约人的接受必须是明确的（即确定的），否则不会对任何一方产生约束力。在受要约人接受要约之前，要约人可以撤销要约。在此，贷款人提出要清偿借款人 3 月 1 日到期的 50,000 美元债务，要求借款人通过做出在 1 月 15 日之前支付 45,000 美元的回报承诺来接受。但借款人 1 月 10 日的声明称他将“尝试筹集资金”，这并不是一个明确且毫不含糊的提前付款承诺（不接受）。因此，贷方在 1 月 11 日告诉借款人他改变了主意，实际上撤销了他的要约，这支持了他的立场，即借款人仍欠原来的 50,000 美元债务。 （选择 B） 可执行协议是当现有合同的各方同意给予和接受不同的东西来满足其现有合同义务时创建的合同。一旦协议履行，双方在旧合同下的义务即解除。在这里，由于没有双方同意（即要约和接受），因此无法达成一致。 （选项C） 礼物承诺是不考虑任何考虑而作出的承诺。但由于贷款人提出免除借款人的债务以换取提前付款，因此该提议并不是礼物承诺。\n\n（选择 D）如果适用欺诈法，则只需由被指控方签署书面合同即可（此处未见）。因此，缺乏书面文件不会影响贷款人承诺的可执行性。\n\n教育目标：接受要求受要约人客观、明确地表示同意。在受要约人这样做之前，要约可以被撤销。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 57（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释要约人不受模棱两可的承诺的约束）。","conclusion":"答案 A：借款人 1 月 10 日的声明并非回报承诺，因此贷方实际上于 1 月 11 日撤销了其要约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":165,"sourceQuestionPage":488,"sourceQuestionPages":[488],"sourceExplanationPages":[489,490],"sourceAnswerPages":[489,490],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-166","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"On March 1, an apartment complex received from an appliance company a signed letter offering to sell 1,200 window air conditioners suitable for the apartments in the complex. The appliance company's offer stated that it would remain open until March 20, but that the apartment complex's acceptance must be received on or before that date. On March 16, the apartment complex posted a letter of acceptance. On March 17, the appliance company emailed the complex to advise that it was revoking the offer. The email was received by the apartment complex on March 17, but the complex's letter did not arrive at the appliance company's address until March 21. As of March 22, which of the following is a correct statement?","choices":{"A":"The email revoking the offer was effective upon receipt.","B":"The mail was the only authorized means of revocation.","C":"The offer was revocable at any time for lack of consideration.","D":"Under the terms of the appliance company's offer, the apartment complex's attempted acceptance was ineffective."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Termination of offer before acceptance\n\nOfferor's Offeror communicates revocation directly to offeree revocation Offeree learns information from reliable source that reasonably indicates offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer) Offeree's Offeree communicates rejection directly to offeror rejection Offeree's counteroffer serves as rejection & new offer* Lapse Time period specified in offer expires After reasonable time if no time period specified in offer By law Either party dies or is adjudicated insane Subject matter of offer is destroyed or becomes illegal *Counteroffer does not terminate offer if offeree manifests intent to take offer under advisement.\n\nContract formation (ie, the creation of a legally enforceable agreement) requires both an offer and an acceptance (ie, mutual assent). To effectively accept an offer, the offeree must: manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer and\n\nfollow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance.\n\nUnder the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is generally effective upon dispatch. But the offeror is master of the offer and can therefore override this rule—eg, by stating that acceptance must be received by a certain date. Here, the appliance company's offer to sell air conditioners specified that acceptance must be received, not merely dispatched, by March 20. Since the apartment complex's reply letter did not arrive at the appliance company's address until March 21, the apartment complex's attempted acceptance was ineffective. (Choices A, B & C) A merchant's offer is firm (ie, irrevocable) if the merchant provides a written and signed assurance that the offer will be held open for a period of time—no consideration is required. Here, the appliance company (merchant) gave a written and signed assurance that its offer would remain open until March 20. As a result, the offer could not be revoked by email (or any other means) before March 20.\n\nEducational objective: To accept an offer, the offeree must manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer AND follow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance (which override the mailbox rule).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 60 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an offer's prescriptions regarding the time, place, or manner of acceptance must be complied with to create a contract).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (2019) (stating that the parties can modify the default mailbox rule).","educationalObjective":"To accept an offer, the offeree must manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer AND follow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance (which override the mailbox rule).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 60 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an offer's prescriptions regarding the time, place, or manner of acceptance must be complied with to create a contract).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (2019) (stating that the parties can modify the default mailbox rule).","rule":"To accept an offer, the offeree must manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer AND follow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance (which override the mailbox rule).","irac":{"issue":"As of March 22, which of the following is a correct statement?","rule":"To accept an offer, the offeree must manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer AND follow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance (which override the mailbox rule).","analysis":"Termination of offer before acceptance\n\nOfferor's Offeror communicates revocation directly to offeree revocation Offeree learns information from reliable source that reasonably indicates offer was revoked (eg, house sold to another buyer) Offeree's Offeree communicates rejection directly to offeror rejection Offeree's counteroffer serves as rejection & new offer* Lapse Time period specified in offer expires After reasonable time if no time period specified in offer By law Either party dies or is adjudicated insane Subject matter of offer is destroyed or becomes illegal *Counteroffer does not terminate offer if offeree manifests intent to take offer under advisement.\n\nContract formation (ie, the creation of a legally enforceable agreement) requires both an offer and an acceptance (ie, mutual assent). To effectively accept an offer, the offeree must: manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer and\n\nfollow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance.\n\nUnder the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is generally effective upon dispatch. But the offeror is master of the offer and can therefore override this rule—eg, by stating that acceptance must be received by a certain date. Here, the appliance company's offer to sell air conditioners specified that acceptance must be received, not merely dispatched, by March 20. Since the apartment complex's reply letter did not arrive at the appliance company's address until March 21, the apartment complex's attempted acceptance was ineffective. (Choices A, B & C) A merchant's offer is firm (ie, irrevocable) if the merchant provides a written and signed assurance that the offer will be held open for a period of time—no consideration is required. Here, the appliance company (merchant) gave a written and signed assurance that its offer would remain open until March 20. As a result, the offer could not be revoked by email (or any other means) before March 20.","conclusion":"Answer D: Under the terms of the appliance company's offer, the apartment complex's attempted acceptance was ineffective."},"questionZh":"3月1日，某公寓楼收到一家家电公司的署名函，表示愿意出售适合该公寓楼的1200台窗式空调。该家电公司的报价称，报价将持续到 3 月 20 日，但必须在该日期或之前收到公寓大楼的接受。 3月16日，公寓楼贴出了录取通知书。 3 月 17 日，该家电公司向该综合体发送电子邮件，告知其将撤销该报价。公寓大楼于3月17日收到了该电子邮件，但公寓大楼的信件直到3月21日才到达家电公司的地址。截至3月22日，以下哪项表述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"撤销要约的电子邮件自收到后生效。","B":"邮件是唯一授权的撤销方式。","C":"由于缺乏约因，该要约可以随时撤销。","D":"根据该电器公司的报价条款，公寓大楼的接受尝试并未成功。"},"explanationZh":"接受之前终止要约\n\n要约人的要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销要约 受要约人从可靠来源获悉合理表明要约被撤销的信息（例如，将房屋出售给另一位买家） 受要约人的受要约人直接向要约人拒绝要约 受要约人的还盘充当拒绝和新要约* 失效 要约中指定的期限 如果要约中没有指定时间段，则在合理时间后到期 法律规定 任何一方死亡或被判定精神失常 要约标的物被毁坏或变得非法 *还要约如果受要约人根据通知表明有意接受要约，则不会终止要约。\n\n合同的形成（即，制定具有法律效力的协议）需要要约和接受（即双方同意）。为了有效地接受要约，受要约人必须： 表现出受要约条款约束的客观意图，并且\n\n遵守要约中有关接受时间、地点或方式的任何规定。\n\n根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受通常在发送后生效。但要约人是要约的主人，因此可以推翻这一规则，例如，声明必须在某个日期之前收到接受。其中，家电公司销售空调的要约明确规定，必须在3月20日之前收到，而不仅仅是发出验收。由于公寓楼的回信直到3月21日才到达家电公司的地址，因此公寓楼的验收无效。 （选项 A、B 和 C）如果商家提供书面并签名的保证，表明报价将在一段时间内保持开放，则商家的报价是确定的（即不可撤销的），无需约因任何因素。在此，家电公司（商家）书面签署并保证其报价将持续到 3 月 20 日。因此，在 3 月 20 日之前无法通过电子邮件（或任何其他方式）撤销报价。\n\n教育目标：要接受要约，受要约者必须表现出受要约条款约束的客观意图，并遵循要约中有关接受时间、地点或方式的任何规范（优先于邮箱规则）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 60（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释要约关于时间、地点或接受方式的规定必须遵守才能签订合同）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d Contracts § 98 (2019)（规定双方可以修改默认邮箱规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要接受要约，受要约人必须表现出受要约条款约束的客观意图，并遵循要约中有关接受要约的时间、地点或方式（优先于邮箱规则）的任何规范。","iracZh":{"issue":"截至 3 月 22 日，下列哪项表述是正确的？","rule":"要接受要约，受要约人必须表现出受要约条款约束的客观意图，并遵循要约中有关接受要约的时间、地点或方式（优先于邮箱规则）的任何规范。","analysis":"接受之前终止要约\n\n要约人的要约人直接向受要约人传达撤销要约 受要约人从可靠来源获悉合理表明要约被撤销的信息（例如，将房屋出售给另一位买家） 受要约人的受要约人直接向要约人拒绝要约 受要约人的还盘充当拒绝和新要约* 失效 要约中指定的期限 如果要约中没有指定时间段，则在合理时间后到期 法律规定 任何一方死亡或被判定精神失常 要约标的物被毁坏或变得非法 *还要约如果受要约人根据通知表明有意接受要约，则不会终止要约。\n\n合同的形成（即，制定具有法律效力的协议）需要要约和接受（即双方同意）。为了有效地接受要约，受要约人必须： 表现出受要约条款约束的客观意图，并且\n\n遵守要约中有关接受时间、地点或方式的任何规定。\n\n根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受通常在发送后生效。但要约人是要约的主人，因此可以推翻这一规则，例如，声明必须在某个日期之前收到接受。其中，家电公司销售空调的要约明确规定，必须在3月20日之前收到，而不仅仅是发出验收。由于公寓楼的回信直到3月21日才到达家电公司的地址，因此公寓楼的验收无效。 （选项 A、B 和 C）如果商家提供书面并签名的保证，表明报价将在一段时间内保持开放，则商家的报价是确定的（即不可撤销的），无需约因任何因素。在此，家电公司（商家）书面签署并保证其报价将持续到 3 月 20 日。因此，在 3 月 20 日之前无法通过电子邮件（或任何其他方式）撤销报价。\n\n教育目标：要接受要约，受要约者必须表现出受要约条款约束的客观意图，并遵循要约中有关接受时间、地点或方式的任何规范（优先于邮箱规则）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 60（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释要约关于时间、地点或接受方式的规定必须遵守才能签订合同）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d Contracts § 98 (2019)（规定双方可以修改默认邮箱规则）。","conclusion":"答案 D：根据该电器公司的报价条款，公寓大楼的接受尝试并未成功。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"intent to be bound","ipa":"/ˌɪntˈɛnt tˈu bˈi bˈaʊnd/","syllables":"in·tent to be bound","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"受约束意图：客观上愿意使合意产生法律效力的表示","contextualMeaning":"受约束意图：客观上愿意使合意产生法律效力的表示","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"To accept an offer, the offeree must manifest an objective intent to be bound by the terms of the offer AND follow any specifications in the offer as to the time, place, or manner of acceptance (which override the mailbox rule).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"conditioners","ipa":"/kʌndˈɪʃənɝz/","syllables":"con·di·tion·ers","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 调理的物品或物质, 护发素( conditioner的复数形式 )","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“conditioners”","wordForm":"名词；原形 conditioner；词形 0:conditioner/1:s","sourceSentence":"On March 1, an apartment complex received from an appliance company a signed letter offering to sell 1,200 window air conditioners suitable for the apartments in the complex.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":166,"sourceQuestionPage":491,"sourceQuestionPages":[491],"sourceExplanationPages":[492,493],"sourceAnswerPages":[492,493],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-167","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On November 1, an accountant and a lawyer contracted for the sale of the law books the accountant had inherited from his father. The lawyer agreed to pay the purchase price of $10,000 when the accountant delivered the books on December 1. On November 10, the lawyer received a signed letter from the accountant that stated: \"I have decided to dispose of the bookshelves containing the law books you have already purchased. If you want the shelves, I will deliver them to you along with the books on December 1 at no additional cost to you. Let me know before November 15 whether you want them. I will not sell them to anyone else before then.\" On November 14, the lawyer faxed and the accountant received the following message: \"I accept your offer of the shelves.\" The accountant was not a merchant with respect to either law books or bookshelves. On November 12, the accountant told the lawyer that he had decided not to part with the shelves. Will this communication operate as a legally effective revocation of his offer to deliver the shelves?","choices":{"A":"No, because by delaying his acceptance until November 14, the lawyer detrimentally relied on the accountant's promise not to sell the shelves to anyone else in the meantime.","B":"No, because the accountant had given a signed assurance that the offer would be held open until November 15.","C":"Yes, because the accountant was not a merchant with respect to bookshelves.","D":"Yes, because the lawyer had a preexisting obligation to pay $10,000 for the law books."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nUnder the UCC, an existing contract can be modified by agreement (ie, offer and acceptance) of the parties. An offer to modify a contract is generally revocable prior to acceptance. But such an offer is irrevocable if it is a firm offer—ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that his/her offer will be kept open for a period of time. Here, the accountant's letter offering to modify the sales contract was signed and assured the lawyer that the offer would be held open until November 15. Nevertheless, the accountant's offer was revocable since he is not a merchant with respect to bookshelves (Choice B). Therefore, the accountant effectively revoked the offer before it was accepted by telling the lawyer on November 12 that he had decided not to part with the shelves. (Choice A) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an otherwise revocable offer becomes irrevocable if (1) the offeror could reasonably foresee that the offeree would rely on the offer and (2) the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relied on that offer. But even\n\nif the lawyer reasonably relied on the accountant's offer, he suffered no detriment—eg, expenses to prepare for the shelves' delivery. (Choice D) The fact that the lawyer had a preexisting obligation to pay $10,000 for the law books shows that there would have been no new consideration for the proposed modification. But consideration is not required to modify a sale-of-goods contract.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, an offer to modify a contract is generally revocable unless it is a firm offer— ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that his/her offer will remain open for a period of time.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-205 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (firm offers).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, an offer to modify a contract is generally revocable unless it is a firm offer— ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that his/her offer will remain open for a period of time.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-205 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (firm offers).","rule":"Under the UCC, an offer to modify a contract is generally revocable unless it is a firm offer— ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that his/her offer will remain open for a period of time.","irac":{"issue":"Will this communication operate as a legally effective revocation of his offer to deliver the shelves?","rule":"Under the UCC, an offer to modify a contract is generally revocable unless it is a firm offer— ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that his/her offer will remain open for a period of time.","analysis":"Irrevocable offers\n\nType Description Consideration Duration\n\nUCC Firm offer Merchant gives written Not required Specified or & signed assurance that reasonable offer will remain open time\n\nCommon Option contract Offeror promises to Required law keep offer open in exchange for consideration\n\nPartial Offeror invites Reasonable performance acceptance only by time for full (unilateral performance & offeree performance contracts) begins to perform\n\nPromissory Offeror could Not required Reasonable estoppel* reasonably foresee time reliance on offer & offeree reasonably relies to his/her detriment\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*Also referred to as \"detrimental reliance.\"\n\nUnder the UCC, an existing contract can be modified by agreement (ie, offer and acceptance) of the parties. An offer to modify a contract is generally revocable prior to acceptance. But such an offer is irrevocable if it is a firm offer—ie, a merchant's written and signed assurance that his/her offer will be kept open for a period of time. Here, the accountant's letter offering to modify the sales contract was signed and assured the lawyer that the offer would be held open until November 15. Nevertheless, the accountant's offer was revocable since he is not a merchant with respect to bookshelves (Choice B). Therefore, the accountant effectively revoked the offer before it was accepted by telling the lawyer on November 12 that he had decided not to part with the shelves. (Choice A) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, an otherwise revocable offer becomes irrevocable if (1) the offeror could reasonably foresee that the offeree would rely on the offer and (2) the offeree reasonably and detrimentally relied on that offer. But even\n\nif the lawyer reasonably relied on the accountant's offer, he suffered no detriment—eg, expenses to prepare for the shelves' delivery. (Choice D) The fact that the lawyer had a preexisting obligation to pay $10,000 for the law books shows that there would have been no new consideration for the proposed modification. But consideration is not required to modify a sale-of-goods contract.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the accountant was not a merchant with respect to bookshelves."},"questionZh":"11 月 1 日，一名会计师和一名律师签订了出售该会计师从其父亲那里继承的法律书籍的合同。当会计师于12月1日交付书籍时，律师同意支付10,000美元的购买价。11月10日，律师收到会计师的一封签名信，信中写道：“我已决定处理掉你已经购买的法律书籍的书架。如果你想要书架，我会在12月1日将它们连同书籍一起交付给你，无需额外费用。11月15日之前告诉我你是否想要它们。在此之前我不会将它们卖给任何其他人。”那么。” 11月14日，律师发传真，会计师收到如下信息：“我接受你们的货架报价。”无论是法律书籍还是书架，会计师都不是商人。 11月12日，会计师告诉律师，他决定不放弃货架。此通信是否会在法律上有效地撤销他交付货架的提议？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为律师将他的接受推迟到 11 月 14 日，这对会计师的承诺不利，即在此期间不会将货架出售给其他任何人。","B":"不会，因为会计师已签署保证，要约将持续到 11 月 15 日。","C":"是的，因为会计师不是书架商人。","D":"是的，因为律师有义务支付 10,000 美元购买法律书籍。"},"explanationZh":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n根据 UCC，现有合同可以经双方同意（即要约和接受）进行修改。修改合同的要约通常在接受之前可以撤销。但如果是确定报价，即商家书面并签署的保证其报价将在一段时间内保持有效，则该报价是不可撤销的。在此，会计师提出修改销售合同的信函已签署，并向律师保证该提议将持续到 11 月 15 日。然而，会计师的提议是可撤销的，因为他不是书架商人（选项 B）。因此，会计师在 11 月 12 日告诉律师他已决定不放弃货架，从而在报价被接受之前有效地撤销了报价。 （选择A）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果（1）要约人能够合理地预见受要约人将依赖该要约，并且（2）受要约人合理且有害地依赖该要约，则原本可撤销的要约将变为不可撤销。但即使\n\n如果律师合理地相信会计师的提议，他就不会遭受任何损失，例如准备货架交付的费用。 （选项 D）律师先前有义务支付 10,000 美元购买法律书籍的事实表明，拟议的修改不会有新的考虑。但修改货物销售合同不需要对价。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，修改合同的要约通常是可撤销的，除非它是确定要约，即商家书面并签署的保证其要约将在一段时间内保持有效。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-205（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（实盘）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，修改合同的要约通常是可撤销的，除非它是确定要约，即商家书面并签署的保证其要约将在一段时间内保持有效。","iracZh":{"issue":"此通信是否会在法律上有效地撤销他交付货架的提议？","rule":"根据 UCC，修改合同的要约通常是可撤销的，除非它是确定要约，即商家书面并签署的保证其要约将在一段时间内保持有效。","analysis":"不可撤销的优惠\n\n类型 说明 约因 / 期限\n\nUCC 实盘 商家提供书面 不需要 指定或签署保证合理报价将保持开放时间\n\n共同期权合同要约人承诺按照法律规定保持要约开放以换取对价\n\n部分要约人仅在全面（单方履约和受要约人履约合同）开始履约时邀请合理履约接受\n\n承诺要约人可能 不需要 合理禁止反言* 合理预见对要约的时间依赖以及受要约人合理依赖他/她的损害\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n*也称为“有害依赖”。\n\n根据 UCC，现有合同可以经双方同意（即要约和接受）进行修改。修改合同的要约通常在接受之前可以撤销。但如果是确定报价，即商家书面并签署的保证其报价将在一段时间内保持有效，则该报价是不可撤销的。在此，会计师提出修改销售合同的信函已签署，并向律师保证该提议将持续到 11 月 15 日。然而，会计师的提议是可撤销的，因为他不是书架商人（选项 B）。因此，会计师在 11 月 12 日告诉律师他已决定不放弃货架，从而在报价被接受之前有效地撤销了报价。 （选择A）根据允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则，如果（1）要约人能够合理地预见受要约人将依赖该要约，并且（2）受要约人合理且有害地依赖该要约，则原本可撤销的要约将变为不可撤销。但即使\n\n如果律师合理地相信会计师的提议，他就不会遭受任何损失，例如准备货架交付的费用。 （选项 D）律师先前有义务支付 10,000 美元购买法律书籍的事实表明，拟议的修改不会有新的考虑。但修改货物销售合同不需要对价。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，修改合同的要约通常是可撤销的，除非它是确定要约，即商家书面并签署的保证其要约将在一段时间内保持有效。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-205（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（实盘）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为会计师不是书架商人。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":167,"sourceQuestionPage":494,"sourceQuestionPages":[494],"sourceExplanationPages":[495,496],"sourceAnswerPages":[495,496],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-168","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A car dealer owed a bank $10,000, due on June 1. The dealer subsequently sold a car to a buyer at a price of $10,000, payable at $1,000 per month beginning on June 1. The dealer then telephoned the bank to ask whether the bank would accept payments of $1,000 per month for 10 months beginning June 1, without interest, in payment of the dealer's debt to the bank. The bank agreed to that arrangement, and the dealer then asked the buyer to make his car payments directly to the bank. When the buyer tendered the first payment to the bank, the bank refused the payment, asserting that it would accept payment only from the dealer. On June 2, the bank demanded that the dealer pay the debt in full immediately. The dealer refused to pay, and the bank sued the dealer to recover the $10,000. In this suit, which of the following arguments best supports the bank's claim for immediate payment?","choices":{"A":"The agreement to extend the time for payment was not in writing.","B":"The dealer could not delegate its duty to pay to the buyer.","C":"The dealer gave no consideration for the agreement to extend the time of payment.","D":"The dealer's conduct was an attempted novation that the bank could reject."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation\n\nGift No bargained-for exchange\n\nToken consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value\n\nSham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed\n\nIllusory promise No legal detriment if apparent promise imposes no obligation\n\nContract modifications must generally be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way—unless the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances. The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is insufficient for new consideration and will not discharge the parties' duties under the initial (ie, unmodified) contract. Here, the car dealer owed the bank $10,000, due on June 1. When the dealer asked whether the bank would accept installment payments beginning on June 1, the bank agreed. But the reason for the dealer's request (ie, the car sale) is not a circumstance that renders consideration unnecessary since it was completely unrelated to the debt owed to the bank. And since the dealer had a preexisting duty to pay that debt, he gave no consideration for the agreement to extend the time of payment. Therefore, the bank has a claim for immediate payment. (Choice A) A writing is not required to modify a contract unless the statute of frauds applies to the modified contract (not seen here). (Choice B) Absent a contrary term, either party to a contract can delegate its duty of performance to a third party. However, this would not extinguish the party's liability for breach should the third party fail to perform. (Choice D) A novation is a new contract that (1) substitutes a new party for one of the original parties, (2) discharges the original party's obligations, and (3) requires agreement by all the parties involved. But here, the bank never agreed to substitute the buyer or discharge the dealer's debt.\n\nEducational objective: Modification of an existing contract requires new consideration unless the modification is fair and equitable due to unanticipated circumstances. A promise to perform a preexisting duty does not constitute new consideration.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that performance of a pre-existing legal duty is not consideration).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that modification of a contract without new consideration is binding if it is fair and equitable in view of unanticipated circumstances).","educationalObjective":"Modification of an existing contract requires new consideration unless the modification is fair and equitable due to unanticipated circumstances. A promise to perform a preexisting duty does not constitute new consideration.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that performance of a pre-existing legal duty is not consideration).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that modification of a contract without new consideration is binding if it is fair and equitable in view of unanticipated circumstances).","rule":"Modification of an existing contract requires new consideration unless the modification is fair and equitable due to unanticipated circumstances. A promise to perform a preexisting duty does not constitute new consideration.","irac":{"issue":"In this suit, which of the following arguments best supports the bank's claim for immediate payment?","rule":"Modification of an existing contract requires new consideration unless the modification is fair and equitable due to unanticipated circumstances. A promise to perform a preexisting duty does not constitute new consideration.","analysis":"Consideration problems that prevent contract formation\n\nGift No bargained-for exchange\n\nToken consideration No inducement to bargain if performance entirely devoid of value\n\nSham consideration No legal detriment & no inducement to bargain if recited performance not intended to be completed Preexisting duty / past No legal detriment if performance already owed or consideration completed\n\nIllusory promise No legal detriment if apparent promise imposes no obligation\n\nContract modifications must generally be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way—unless the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances. The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is insufficient for new consideration and will not discharge the parties' duties under the initial (ie, unmodified) contract. Here, the car dealer owed the bank $10,000, due on June 1. When the dealer asked whether the bank would accept installment payments beginning on June 1, the bank agreed. But the reason for the dealer's request (ie, the car sale) is not a circumstance that renders consideration unnecessary since it was completely unrelated to the debt owed to the bank. And since the dealer had a preexisting duty to pay that debt, he gave no consideration for the agreement to extend the time of payment. Therefore, the bank has a claim for immediate payment. (Choice A) A writing is not required to modify a contract unless the statute of frauds applies to the modified contract (not seen here). (Choice B) Absent a contrary term, either party to a contract can delegate its duty of performance to a third party. However, this would not extinguish the party's liability for breach should the third party fail to perform. (Choice D) A novation is a new contract that (1) substitutes a new party for one of the original parties, (2) discharges the original party's obligations, and (3) requires agreement by all the parties involved. But here, the bank never agreed to substitute the buyer or discharge the dealer's debt.","conclusion":"Answer C: The dealer gave no consideration for the agreement to extend the time of payment."},"questionZh":"一家汽车经销商欠银行 10,000 美元，到期日为 6 月 1 日。经销商随后以 10,000 美元的价格向买家出售一辆汽车，从 6 月 1 日起每月支付 1,000 美元。经销商随后打电话给银行，询问银行是否愿意接受从 6 月 1 日开始的 10 个月内每月 1,000 美元的无息付款，以偿还经销商欠银行的债务。银行同意了这一安排，经销商随后要求买家直接向银行支付汽车付款。当买方向银行支付第一笔付款时，银行拒绝付款，声称只接受经销商的付款。 6月2日，银行要求经销商立即全额偿还债务。经销商拒绝付款，银行起诉经销商追回 10,000 美元。在本案中，以下哪项论据最能支持银行要求立即付款的主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"延长付款时间的协议并未以书面形式达成。","B":"经销商不能将其付款义务委托给买方。","C":"经销商没有考虑同意延长付款时间。","D":"交易商的行为是一次尝试性的更替，银行可以拒绝。"},"explanationZh":"阻碍合同成立的对价问题\n\n礼品 不可讨价还价交换\n\n名义约因 如果履行完全没有价值，则没有讨价还价的诱因\n\n虚假对价 如果不打算完成所叙述的履约，则不会造成法律损害，也不会诱使讨价还价 先前存在的义务/过去 如果已经欠履约或完成对价，则不会造成法律损害\n\n虚幻的承诺 如果明显的承诺不施加任何义务，则不会造成法律损害\n\n合同修改通常必须有新约因的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其职责，除非修改在不可预见的情况下是公平和公正的。履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不足以引起新的考虑，并且不会解除双方在初始（即未经修改的）合同下的义务。在此，汽车经销商欠银行 10,000 美元，到期日为 6 月 1 日。当经销商询问银行是否接受从 6 月 1 日开始的分期付款时，银行同意了。但经销商提出要求（即出售汽车）的原因并不是无需约因的情况，因为它与欠银行的债务完全无关。由于经销商已有偿还该债务的义务，因此他没有考虑延长付款时间的协议。因此，银行主张立即付款。 （选择 A）修改合同不需要书面形式，除非欺诈法适用于修改后的合同（此处未显示）。 （选择B）在没有相反条款的情况下，合同任何一方都可以将其履行义务委托给第三方。但是，如果第三方不履行义务，这并不能免除当事人的违约责任。 （选项 D） 更替是一种新合同，其 (1) 用新当事人替代原当事人之一，(2) 解除原当事人的义务，以及 (3) 需要所有相关当事人的同意。但在这里，银行从未同意更换买家或免除经销商的债务。\n\n教育目标：修改现有合同需要重新约因，除非由于意外情况而修改是公平公正的。履行先前存在的义务的承诺并不构成新的考虑。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 73（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说履行预先存在的法律义务不是对价）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 89（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果考虑到意外情况，合同的修改是公平公正的，则无需新约因的合同修改具有约束力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"修改现有合同需要重新约因，除非由于意外情况而修改是公平公正的。履行先前存在的义务的承诺并不构成新的考虑。","iracZh":{"issue":"在本案中，以下哪项论据最能支持银行要求立即付款的主张？","rule":"修改现有合同需要重新约因，除非由于意外情况而修改是公平公正的。履行先前存在的义务的承诺并不构成新的考虑。","analysis":"阻碍合同成立的对价问题\n\n礼品 不可讨价还价交换\n\n名义约因 如果履行完全没有价值，则没有讨价还价的诱因\n\n虚假对价 如果不打算完成所叙述的履约，则不会造成法律损害，也不会诱使讨价还价 先前存在的义务/过去 如果已经欠履约或完成对价，则不会造成法律损害\n\n虚幻的承诺 如果明显的承诺不施加任何义务，则不会造成法律损害\n\n合同修改通常必须有新约因的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其职责，除非修改在不可预见的情况下是公平和公正的。履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不足以引起新的考虑，并且不会解除双方在初始（即未经修改的）合同下的义务。在此，汽车经销商欠银行 10,000 美元，到期日为 6 月 1 日。当经销商询问银行是否接受从 6 月 1 日开始的分期付款时，银行同意了。但经销商提出要求（即出售汽车）的原因并不是无需约因的情况，因为它与欠银行的债务完全无关。由于经销商已有偿还该债务的义务，因此他没有考虑延长付款时间的协议。因此，银行主张立即付款。 （选择 A）修改合同不需要书面形式，除非欺诈法适用于修改后的合同（此处未显示）。 （选择B）在没有相反条款的情况下，合同任何一方都可以将其履行义务委托给第三方。但是，如果第三方不履行义务，这并不能免除当事人的违约责任。 （选项 D） 更替是一种新合同，其 (1) 用新当事人替代原当事人之一，(2) 解除原当事人的义务，以及 (3) 需要所有相关当事人的同意。但在这里，银行从未同意更换买家或免除经销商的债务。\n\n教育目标：修改现有合同需要重新约因，除非由于意外情况而修改是公平公正的。履行先前存在的义务的承诺并不构成新的考虑。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 73（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说履行预先存在的法律义务不是对价）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 89（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果考虑到意外情况，合同的修改是公平公正的，则无需新约因的合同修改具有约束力）。","conclusion":"答案 C：经销商没有考虑同意延长付款时间。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":168,"sourceQuestionPage":497,"sourceQuestionPages":[497],"sourceExplanationPages":[498,499],"sourceAnswerPages":[498,499],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-169","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"On May 1, an uncle mailed a letter to his adult nephew that stated: \"I am thinking of selling my pickup truck, which you have seen and ridden in. I would consider taking $7,000 for it.\" On May 3, the nephew mailed the following response: \"I will buy your pickup for $7,000 cash.\" The uncle received this letter on May 5 and on May 6 mailed a note that stated: \"It's a deal.\" On May 7, before the nephew had received the letter of May 6, he phoned his uncle to report that he no longer wanted to buy the pickup truck because his driver's license had been suspended. Which of the following statements concerning this exchange is accurate?","choices":{"A":"There was a contract as of May 3.","B":"There was a contract as of May 5.","C":"There was a contract as of May 6.","D":"There is no contract."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nContract formation begins with an offer that expresses more than a mere willingness to negotiate. The offer must (1) communicate definite terms to the offeree and (2) make an objectively reasonable expression of the offeror's intent to enter a binding contract. Here, the uncle's May 1 letter was not an offer since he was merely \"thinking\" of selling his pickup truck and would \"consider\" taking $7,000. In contrast, the nephew's May 3 response was an offer because it expressed definite terms (\"$7,000 cash\") and an intent to be bound (\"I will buy\"). Once an offer has been made, a contract will be formed if the offeree accepts the offer before it is revoked. Under the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is: permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance—eg, when the offer was communicated by mail and\n\neffective upon dispatch—ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage.\n\nHere, the uncle received the nephew's offer on May 5. The uncle reasonably accepted this offer by mailing a note on May 6 since the parties had been negotiating by mail. As a result, there was a binding contract as of May 6 that prevented the nephew from revoking his offer on May 7 (Choices A & B).\n\n(Choice D) Had the subject matter of the offer become illegal prior to acceptance, the offer would have been terminated by law and no contract would have been formed. Here, the nephew's suspended license only made it illegal for him to drive the truck—not to purchase it. Therefore, this did not cause the offer to terminate.\n\nEducational objective: An offer to form a contract must (1) communicate definite terms to the offeree and (2) make an objectively reasonable expression of the offeror's intent to enter a binding contract. Such an offer can then be accepted by mail, and will be effective upon dispatch, if a mailing is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirements for an offer).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirements for acceptance).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019) (mailbox rule).","educationalObjective":"An offer to form a contract must (1) communicate definite terms to the offeree and (2) make an objectively reasonable expression of the offeror's intent to enter a binding contract. Such an offer can then be accepted by mail, and will be effective upon dispatch, if a mailing is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 24 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirements for an offer).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (requirements for acceptance).\n\n17A Am. Jur. 2d Contracts § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019) (mailbox rule).","rule":"An offer to form a contract must (1) communicate definite terms to the offeree and (2) make an objectively reasonable expression of the offeror's intent to enter a binding contract. Such an offer can then be accepted by mail, and will be effective upon dispatch, if a mailing is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements concerning this exchange is accurate?","rule":"An offer to form a contract must (1) communicate definite terms to the offeree and (2) make an objectively reasonable expression of the offeror's intent to enter a binding contract. Such an offer can then be accepted by mail, and will be effective upon dispatch, if a mailing is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance.","analysis":"Contract negotiations by mail*\n\nOfferor's Offer Effective upon receipt actions Revocation of offer\n\nOfferee's Acceptance Effective upon dispatch actions Exceptions—effective upon receipt if: (mailbox rule) improperly stamped/addressed acceptance by mail unreasonable rejection mailed before acceptance (first received is effective) acceptance of option contract offer states acceptance effective only upon receipt Rejection/counteroffer Effective upon receipt\n\n*Applicable under common law & UCC.\n\nContract formation begins with an offer that expresses more than a mere willingness to negotiate. The offer must (1) communicate definite terms to the offeree and (2) make an objectively reasonable expression of the offeror's intent to enter a binding contract. Here, the uncle's May 1 letter was not an offer since he was merely \"thinking\" of selling his pickup truck and would \"consider\" taking $7,000. In contrast, the nephew's May 3 response was an offer because it expressed definite terms (\"$7,000 cash\") and an intent to be bound (\"I will buy\"). Once an offer has been made, a contract will be formed if the offeree accepts the offer before it is revoked. Under the mailbox rule, acceptance by mail is: permitted if mail is a reasonable method of communicating acceptance—eg, when the offer was communicated by mail and\n\neffective upon dispatch—ie, when the acceptance is properly addressed and placed with the postal service with paid postage.\n\nHere, the uncle received the nephew's offer on May 5. The uncle reasonably accepted this offer by mailing a note on May 6 since the parties had been negotiating by mail. As a result, there was a binding contract as of May 6 that prevented the nephew from revoking his offer on May 7 (Choices A & B).\n\n(Choice D) Had the subject matter of the offer become illegal prior to acceptance, the offer would have been terminated by law and no contract would have been formed. Here, the nephew's suspended license only made it illegal for him to drive the truck—not to purchase it. Therefore, this did not cause the offer to terminate.","conclusion":"Answer C: There was a contract as of May 6."},"questionZh":"5 月 1 日，一位叔叔给他成年的侄子寄了一封信，信中写道：“我正在考虑卖掉我的皮卡车，你已经看过并乘坐过它。我会考虑花 7,000 美元买下它。” 5 月 3 日，侄子邮寄了以下回复：“我将以 7,000 美元现金购买你的皮卡。”叔叔于 5 月 5 日收到这封信，并于 5 月 6 日寄出一张纸条，上面写着：“成交了。” 5月7日，在侄子收到5月6日的信之前，他打电话给叔叔说，他不再想购买皮卡车了，因为他的驾驶执照已被吊销。以下关于本次交换的哪些表述是准确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"截至5月3日，合同已签订。","B":"截至5月5日，合同已签订。","C":"截至5月6日，合同已签订。","D":"没有合同。"},"explanationZh":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n合同的订立始于一份要约，该要约不仅仅表达了谈判的意愿。要约必须 (1) 向受要约人传达明确的条款，并且 (2) 客观合理地表达要约人签订具有约束力的合同的意图。在这里，叔叔 5 月 1 日的信并不是要约，因为他只是“考虑”出售他的皮卡车，并会“考虑”接受 7,000 美元。相比之下，侄子 5 月 3 日的回应是一个要约，因为它表达了明确的条款（“7,000 美元现金”）和受约束的意图（“我会购买”）。一旦发出要约，如果受要约人在撤销要约之前接受要约，则合同成立。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受是： 如果邮件是传达接受的合理方法，则允许 - 例如，当要约通过邮件传达并且\n\n寄出后生效，即在收据上正确注明地址并以已付邮资的方式投递至邮政部门后生效。\n\n在此，叔叔于 5 月 5 日收到了侄子的报价。由于双方一直通过邮件进行谈判，叔叔于 5 月 6 日通过邮寄便条合理地接受了该报价。因此，截至 5 月 6 日，有一份具有约束力的合同，使侄子无法在 5 月 7 日撤销他的要约（选择 A 和 B）。\n\n（选项D）如果要约的标的物在接受之前变得非法，则要约将被法律终止，并且合同不会成立。在这里，侄子的驾照被吊销只是让他驾驶卡车变得非法，而不是购买卡车。因此，这并没有导致要约终止。\n\n教育目标：形成合同的要约必须 (1) 向受要约人传达明确的条款，并且 (2) 客观合理地表达要约人签订具有约束力的合同的意图。如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法，则可以通过邮件接受此类要约，并将在发送后生效。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 24（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约要求）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（接受要求）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019)（邮箱规则）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"形成合同的要约必须 (1) 向受要约人传达明确的条款，并且 (2) 客观合理地表达要约人签订具有约束力的合同的意图。如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法，则可以通过邮件接受此类要约，并将在发送后生效。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下关于本次交换的哪些表述是准确的？","rule":"形成合同的要约必须 (1) 向受要约人传达明确的条款，并且 (2) 客观合理地表达要约人签订具有约束力的合同的意图。如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法，则可以通过邮件接受此类要约，并将在发送后生效。","analysis":"通过邮件进行合同谈判*\n\n要约人的要约 在收到行动后生效 撤销要约\n\n受要约人的接受 在发送行动后生效 例外情况——在收到后生效，如果： （邮箱规则） 盖章/地址不正确 通过邮件接受接受 在接受之前邮寄接受的不合理拒绝（首先收到即有效） 接受期权合约要约表明接受仅在收到后生效 拒绝/还盘 在收到后生效\n\n*适用于普通法和 UCC。\n\n合同的订立始于一份要约，该要约不仅仅表达了谈判的意愿。要约必须 (1) 向受要约人传达明确的条款，并且 (2) 客观合理地表达要约人签订具有约束力的合同的意图。在这里，叔叔 5 月 1 日的信并不是要约，因为他只是“考虑”出售他的皮卡车，并会“考虑”接受 7,000 美元。相比之下，侄子 5 月 3 日的回应是一个要约，因为它表达了明确的条款（“7,000 美元现金”）和受约束的意图（“我会购买”）。一旦发出要约，如果受要约人在撤销要约之前接受要约，则合同成立。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受是： 如果邮件是传达接受的合理方法，则允许 - 例如，当要约通过邮件传达并且\n\n寄出后生效，即在收据上正确注明地址并以已付邮资的方式投递至邮政部门后生效。\n\n在此，叔叔于 5 月 5 日收到了侄子的报价。由于双方一直通过邮件进行谈判，叔叔于 5 月 6 日通过邮寄便条合理地接受了该报价。因此，截至 5 月 6 日，有一份具有约束力的合同，使侄子无法在 5 月 7 日撤销他的要约（选择 A 和 B）。\n\n（选项D）如果要约的标的物在接受之前变得非法，则要约将被法律终止，并且合同不会成立。在这里，侄子的驾照被吊销只是让他驾驶卡车变得非法，而不是购买卡车。因此，这并没有导致要约终止。\n\n教育目标：形成合同的要约必须 (1) 向受要约人传达明确的条款，并且 (2) 客观合理地表达要约人签订具有约束力的合同的意图。如果邮寄是传达接受的合理方法，则可以通过邮件接受此类要约，并将在发送后生效。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 24（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（要约要求）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（接受要求）。\n\n17A 上午。尤尔。 2d 合同 § 98 (Am. Law Inst. 2019)（邮箱规则）。","conclusion":"答案 C：截至5月6日，合同已签订。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":169,"sourceQuestionPage":500,"sourceQuestionPages":[500],"sourceExplanationPages":[501,502],"sourceAnswerPages":[501,502],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-170","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"An Impressionist painting valued at $400,000 was stolen from an art collector's home. The collector, who had insured the painting for $300,000 with an insurance company, promised to pay $25,000 to a full-time investigator for the insurance company if he effected the return of the painting to her in good condition. The insurance company's rules permit its investigators to accept and retain rewards from policyholders for the recovery of insured property. The investigator, by long and skillful detective work, recovered the picture and returned it undamaged to the collector. If the collector refuses to pay the investigator anything, and he sues her for $25,000, what is the probable result under the prevailing modern rule?","choices":{"A":"The collector wins, because the insurance company, the investigator's employer, had a preexisting duty to return the recovered painting to the collector.","B":"The collector wins, because the investigator owed the insurance company a preexisting duty to recover the picture if possible.","C":"The investigator wins, because the collector will benefit more from return of the $400,000 painting than from receiving the $300,000 policy proceeds.","D":"The investigator wins, because the preexisting duty rule does not apply if the promisee's (the investigator's) duty was owed to a third person."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Preexisting duty rule\n\nTraditional common Promise to perform, or performance of, preexisting duty does law rule not constitute consideration\n\nModern exception Consideration exists if preexisting duty owed to third person (ie, nonparty to contract)\n\nAn enforceable contract must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. Under the preexisting duty rule, the promise to perform, or the performance of, a legal duty that is already owed generally does not qualify as consideration. But under the modern exception to this rule, such a promise or performance is consideration if the preexisting duty is owed to a third person—ie, someone who is not a party to the contract in question. Here, the art collector promised to pay the investigator $25,000 to find and return her stolen painting. Although the investigator owed his employer (the insurance company) a preexisting duty to recover that painting if possible, the insurance company was a third person with respect to the investigator's agreement with the collector (Choice B). This means that the traditional preexisting duty rule does not apply, and the investigator's recovery of the painting is sufficient consideration for the collector's promise. Therefore, the investigator will probably win. (Choice A) Had the collector promised to pay the reward to the insurance company, then its preexisting duty under the insurance policy would make the collector's promise unenforceable. But since the collector promised to pay the investigator, the insurance company's preexisting duty is irrelevant. (Choice C) The fact that the collector will benefit more from the return of the painting than from receiving the policy proceeds has no effect on the outcome of this dispute.\n\nEducational objective: The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is not consideration unless the preexisting duty is owed to a third person (ie, someone who is not a party to the contract in question).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the performance of a preexisting contractual duty can be consideration if the duty is not owed to the promisor).","educationalObjective":"The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is not consideration unless the preexisting duty is owed to a third person (ie, someone who is not a party to the contract in question).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the performance of a preexisting contractual duty can be consideration if the duty is not owed to the promisor).","rule":"The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is not consideration unless the preexisting duty is owed to a third person (ie, someone who is not a party to the contract in question).","irac":{"issue":"If the collector refuses to pay the investigator anything, and he sues her for $25,000, what is the probable result under the prevailing modern rule?","rule":"The promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is not consideration unless the preexisting duty is owed to a third person (ie, someone who is not a party to the contract in question).","analysis":"Preexisting duty rule\n\nTraditional common Promise to perform, or performance of, preexisting duty does law rule not constitute consideration\n\nModern exception Consideration exists if preexisting duty owed to third person (ie, nonparty to contract)\n\nAn enforceable contract must be supported by consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. Under the preexisting duty rule, the promise to perform, or the performance of, a legal duty that is already owed generally does not qualify as consideration. But under the modern exception to this rule, such a promise or performance is consideration if the preexisting duty is owed to a third person—ie, someone who is not a party to the contract in question. Here, the art collector promised to pay the investigator $25,000 to find and return her stolen painting. Although the investigator owed his employer (the insurance company) a preexisting duty to recover that painting if possible, the insurance company was a third person with respect to the investigator's agreement with the collector (Choice B). This means that the traditional preexisting duty rule does not apply, and the investigator's recovery of the painting is sufficient consideration for the collector's promise. Therefore, the investigator will probably win. (Choice A) Had the collector promised to pay the reward to the insurance company, then its preexisting duty under the insurance policy would make the collector's promise unenforceable. But since the collector promised to pay the investigator, the insurance company's preexisting duty is irrelevant. (Choice C) The fact that the collector will benefit more from the return of the painting than from receiving the policy proceeds has no effect on the outcome of this dispute.","conclusion":"Answer D: The investigator wins, because the preexisting duty rule does not apply if the promisee's (the investigator's) duty was owed to a third person."},"questionZh":"一幅价值 40 万美元的印象派画作从一位艺术收藏家的家中被盗。这位收藏家向一家保险公司为这幅画投保了 30 万美元，并承诺如果他能将这幅画完好无损地归还给她，他将向保险公司的全职调查员支付 25,000 美元。保险公司的规则允许其调查人员接受并保留保单持有人追回受保财产的报酬。调查员通过长期而熟练的侦探工作，找到了这幅画并将其完好无损地归还给收藏家。如果收藏家拒绝向调查员支付任何费用，而调查员起诉她要求 25,000 美元，那么根据现行的现代规则，可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"收藏家获胜，因为调查员的雇主保险公司有义务将追回的画作归还给收藏家。","B":"收藏家获胜，因为调查员欠保险公司一项预先存在的责任，即在可能的情况下收回照片。","C":"调查员获胜，因为收藏家从归还价值 400,000 美元的画作中获得的利益比从收到 300,000 美元的保单收益中获得的利益更多。","D":"调查员获胜，因为如果受诺人（调查员）的义务是对第三人承担的，则先前存在的义务规则不适用。"},"explanationZh":"既存义务规则\n\n传统的共同承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务法律规则不构成对价\n\n现代例外 如果先前存在的对第三方（即非合同方）的义务，则存在对价\n\n可执行的合同必须有对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。根据先前存在的义务规则，承诺履行或履行已承担的法律义务通常不符合对价。但根据这一规则的现代例外，如果先前存在的义务是对第三人（即非相关合同当事人的人）负有的，则这种承诺或履行就是对价。在此，这位艺术收藏家承诺向调查员支付 25,000 美元，以找到并归还她被盗的画作。尽管调查员对其雇主（保险公司）负有在可能的情况下收回该画作的既存义务，但就调查员与收藏家的协议而言，保险公司是第三人（选项B）。这意味着传统的既存义务规则不适用，调查员收回画作已是对收藏家承诺的充分约因。因此，调查员很可能会获胜。 （选项A）如果收款人承诺将报酬支付给保险公司，那么保险公司在保单项下原有的义务将使收款人的承诺无法执行。但由于收款人承诺向调查员付款，保险公司先前存在的义务就无关紧要了。 （选项C）收藏家从画作归还中获得的利益多于从保单收益中获得的利益这一事实对本次纠纷的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：除非先前存在的义务是对第三方（即，不是相关合同一方的人）负有的，否则承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务不构成对价。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 73 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果承诺人不承担先前存在的合同义务，则该义务的履行可以被视为对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非先前存在的义务是对第三方（即非相关合同当事人）承担的，否则承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务不构成对价。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果收藏家拒绝向调查员支付任何费用，而调查员起诉她要求 25,000 美元，那么根据现行的现代规则，可能的结果是什么？","rule":"除非先前存在的义务是对第三方（即非相关合同当事人）承担的，否则承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务不构成对价。","analysis":"既存义务规则\n\n传统的共同承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务法律规则不构成对价\n\n现代例外 如果先前存在的对第三方（即非合同方）的义务，则存在对价\n\n可执行的合同必须有对价的支持，即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履行。根据先前存在的义务规则，承诺履行或履行已承担的法律义务通常不符合对价。但根据这一规则的现代例外，如果先前存在的义务是对第三人（即非相关合同当事人的人）负有的，则这种承诺或履行就是对价。在此，这位艺术收藏家承诺向调查员支付 25,000 美元，以找到并归还她被盗的画作。尽管调查员对其雇主（保险公司）负有在可能的情况下收回该画作的既存义务，但就调查员与收藏家的协议而言，保险公司是第三人（选项B）。这意味着传统的既存义务规则不适用，调查员收回画作已是对收藏家承诺的充分约因。因此，调查员很可能会获胜。 （选项A）如果收款人承诺将报酬支付给保险公司，那么保险公司在保单项下原有的义务将使收款人的承诺无法执行。但由于收款人承诺向调查员付款，保险公司先前存在的义务就无关紧要了。 （选项C）收藏家从画作归还中获得的利益多于从保单收益中获得的利益这一事实对本次纠纷的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：除非先前存在的义务是对第三方（即，不是相关合同一方的人）负有的，否则承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务不构成对价。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 73 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果承诺人不承担先前存在的合同义务，则该义务的履行可以被视为对价）。","conclusion":"答案 D：调查员获胜，因为如果受诺人（调查员）的义务是对第三人承担的，则先前存在的义务规则不适用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":170,"sourceQuestionPage":503,"sourceQuestionPages":[503],"sourceExplanationPages":[504,505],"sourceAnswerPages":[504,505],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-171","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A testator, whose nephew was his only heir, died leaving a will that gave his entire estate to charity. His nephew, knowing full well that the testator was of sound mind all of his life, and having no evidence to the contrary, nevertheless filed a suit contesting the testator's will on the ground that the testator was incompetent when the will was signed. The testator's executor offered the nephew $5,000 to settle the suit, and the nephew agreed. If the executor then repudiates the agreement and the foregoing facts are proved or admitted in the nephew's suit against the executor for breach of contract, is the nephew entitled to recover under the prevailing view?","choices":{"A":"No, because an agreement to oust the court of its jurisdiction to decide a will contest is contrary to public policy.","B":"No, because the nephew did not bring the will contest in good faith.","C":"Yes, because the agreement was a bargained-for exchange.","D":"Yes, because the law encourages the settlement of disputed claims."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. A promise to surrender a claim or defense can serve as consideration for a settlement agreement so long as: the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good-faith dispute or the surrendering party honestly believes that the claim or defense may be valid. If the surrendering party knows that the claim or defense is invalid, an agreement to surrender it lacks consideration and is therefore unenforceable. Here, the nephew agreed to settle his suit contesting the testator's will in exchange for $5,000. But the nephew did not honestly believe that his claim was valid (ie, he did not bring the will contest in good faith) since he knew that the testator was of sound mind his whole life and had no contrary evidence. Therefore, the nephew's settlement agreement with the executor is unenforceable for lack of bargained-for consideration, and the nephew is not entitled to recover (Choice C). (Choices A & D) The law does encourage the settlement of legal claims—but not those brought in bad faith. Had the nephew brought the will contest in good faith, there would have been no issue with settling out of court.\n\nEducational objective: A promise to surrender a claim or defense constitutes consideration for a settlement agreement so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good-faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party believes that the claim or defense may be valid.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","educationalObjective":"A promise to surrender a claim or defense constitutes consideration for a settlement agreement so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good-faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party believes that the claim or defense may be valid.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","rule":"A promise to surrender a claim or defense constitutes consideration for a settlement agreement so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good-faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party believes that the claim or defense may be valid.","irac":{"issue":"If the executor then repudiates the agreement and the foregoing facts are proved or admitted in the nephew's suit against the executor for breach of contract, is the nephew entitled to recover under the prevailing view?","rule":"A promise to surrender a claim or defense constitutes consideration for a settlement agreement so long as (1) the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good-faith dispute or (2) the surrendering party believes that the claim or defense may be valid.","analysis":"A contract is generally formed when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration—ie, a bargained-for exchange of promises or performance. A promise to surrender a claim or defense can serve as consideration for a settlement agreement so long as: the claim or defense is valid or subject to a good-faith dispute or the surrendering party honestly believes that the claim or defense may be valid. If the surrendering party knows that the claim or defense is invalid, an agreement to surrender it lacks consideration and is therefore unenforceable. Here, the nephew agreed to settle his suit contesting the testator's will in exchange for $5,000. But the nephew did not honestly believe that his claim was valid (ie, he did not bring the will contest in good faith) since he knew that the testator was of sound mind his whole life and had no contrary evidence. Therefore, the nephew's settlement agreement with the executor is unenforceable for lack of bargained-for consideration, and the nephew is not entitled to recover (Choice C). (Choices A & D) The law does encourage the settlement of legal claims—but not those brought in bad faith. Had the nephew brought the will contest in good faith, there would have been no issue with settling out of court.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the nephew did not bring the will contest in good faith."},"questionZh":"一位遗嘱人的侄子是他唯一的继承人，他去世时留下了一份遗嘱，将他的全部财产捐给了慈善机构。他的侄子明知立遗嘱人一生心智健全，又无相反证据，却以立遗嘱时立遗嘱人无行为能力为由，提起诉讼，对立遗嘱人的遗嘱提出异议。遗嘱执行人向侄子提供 5,000 美元以解决诉讼，侄子同意了。如果执行人随后否认该协议，并且上述事实在侄子对执行人提起的违约诉讼中得到证明或承认，那么根据普遍观点，侄子是否有权获得赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为同意取消法院对遗嘱争议的管辖权是违反公共政策的。","B":"不，因为侄子没有善意地进行遗嘱竞赛。","C":"是的，因为该协议是一项讨价还价的交换。","D":"是的，因为法律鼓励解决有争议的索赔。"},"explanationZh":"当相互协议得到有价值的约因（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约）来支持时，合同通常就成立了。放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺可以作为和解协议的对价，只要：索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或者放弃方诚实地认为索赔或抗辩可能有效。如果放弃方知道该主张或抗辩无效，则放弃协议缺乏对价，因此无法执行。在这里，侄子同意以 5,000 美元作为对立遗嘱人遗嘱争议的诉讼和解。但侄子并不真诚地相信他的主张是有效的（即他没有善意地进行遗嘱争夺），因为他知道立遗嘱人一生心智健全，没有相反的证据。因此，侄子与执行人的和解协议因缺乏讨价还价而无法执行，并且侄子无权追偿（选项C）。 （选择 A 和 D）法律确实鼓励解决合法索赔，但不鼓励恶意提出的索赔。如果侄子善意地提起遗嘱争议，那么庭外和解就不存在任何问题。\n\n教育目标：只要满足以下条件，放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺就构成和解协议的对价：(1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃索赔或抗辩的一方认为索赔或抗辩可能有效。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只要 (1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃索赔或抗辩可能有效，承诺放弃索赔或抗辩就构成和解协议的对价。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果执行人随后否认该协议，并且上述事实在侄子对执行人提起的违约诉讼中得到证明或承认，那么根据普遍观点，侄子是否有权获得赔偿？","rule":"只要 (1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃索赔或抗辩可能有效，承诺放弃索赔或抗辩就构成和解协议的对价。","analysis":"当相互协议得到有价值的约因（即通过讨价还价交换承诺或履约）来支持时，合同通常就成立了。放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺可以作为和解协议的对价，只要：索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或者放弃方诚实地认为索赔或抗辩可能有效。如果放弃方知道该主张或抗辩无效，则放弃协议缺乏对价，因此无法执行。在这里，侄子同意以 5,000 美元作为对立遗嘱人遗嘱争议的诉讼和解。但侄子并不真诚地相信他的主张是有效的（即他没有善意地进行遗嘱争夺），因为他知道立遗嘱人一生心智健全，没有相反的证据。因此，侄子与执行人的和解协议因缺乏讨价还价而无法执行，并且侄子无权追偿（选项C）。 （选择 A 和 D）法律确实鼓励解决合法索赔，但不鼓励恶意提出的索赔。如果侄子善意地提起遗嘱争议，那么庭外和解就不存在任何问题。\n\n教育目标：只要满足以下条件，放弃索赔或抗辩的承诺就构成和解协议的对价：(1) 索赔或抗辩有效或存在善意争议，或 (2) 放弃索赔或抗辩的一方认为索赔或抗辩可能有效。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为侄子没有善意地进行遗嘱竞赛。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-ca8220406264bfab"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":171,"sourceQuestionPage":506,"sourceQuestionPages":[506],"sourceExplanationPages":[507,508],"sourceAnswerPages":[507,508],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-172","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A woman was driving home from a crowded shopping mall when a passenger van ran through a stoplight at great speed and hit the woman's car. A physician who witnessed the incident pulled over to the woman's car and saw that her shoulder was seriously injured. The physician told the woman he was a licensed surgeon before helping her out of the car. The woman was delirious and too shaken to speak as the physician removed several glass fragments from her shoulder and applied temporary sutures to close the wound. When the physician finished, he took the woman's contact information and promised to check on her condition later. If the physician subsequently sends the woman a bill for his services, will she be obligated to pay it?","choices":{"A":"No, because it would be unconscionable to require that the woman pay for the physician's emergency medical services.","B":"No, because the physician had a duty to render aid to the woman once he saw that she was injured.","C":"Yes, because an implied-in-fact contract was created when the woman allowed the physician to treat her.","D":"Yes, because the law will imply a promise to pay for emergency services the physician rendered to the woman."},"answer":"D","explanation":"In the absence of any contractual relationship between the parties, courts have the power to construct an implied-in-law (\"quasi\") contract to prevent unjust enrichment when: the plaintiff has conferred a measurable benefit (eg, medical services) on the defendant the plaintiff acted without gratuitous intent and it would be unfair to let the defendant retain the benefit without compensating the plaintiff. Unfair retention of a benefit arises when (1) the defendant had an opportunity to decline the benefit but knowingly accepted it or (2) the plaintiff had a reasonable excuse for not giving the defendant such opportunity—often because of an emergency. This allows the plaintiff in a quasi-contract action to recover restitutionary damages equal to the reasonable value of the benefit conferred. Here, the physician conferred a measurable benefit on the woman when he removed glass from her shoulder and closed the wound. Medical services are typically provided with the expectation of compensation. And since the woman was delirious and too shaken to speak, it was reasonable for the physician to act without obtaining her express consent. Therefore, she must pay the physician's bill because the law will imply a promise to pay for his service. (Choice A) Allowing the woman to retain a benefit without compensating the physician would be unjust—not unconscionable. Unconscionability instead arises when a contract is so unfair to one party that no reasonable person in that position would have agreed to it. (Choice B) The physician may have had a moral duty to aid the woman once he saw that she was injured, but he had no legal duty to do so. (Choice C) An implied-in-fact contract arises when the parties' conduct (or failure to act) demonstrates their mutual intent to contract. But here, a court would not find that the woman intended to contract with the physician since she was delirious and unable to speak.\n\nEducational objective: Courts will construct an implied-in-law (\"quasi\") contract where the plaintiff has conferred a measurable benefit on the defendant without gratuitous intent and it would be unfair to let the defendant retain the benefit without compensating the plaintiff.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Restitution & Unjust Enrichment § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (protection of another's life or health).","educationalObjective":"Courts will construct an implied-in-law (\"quasi\") contract where the plaintiff has conferred a measurable benefit on the defendant without gratuitous intent and it would be unfair to let the defendant retain the benefit without compensating the plaintiff.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Restitution & Unjust Enrichment § 20 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (protection of another's life or health).","rule":"Courts will construct an implied-in-law (\"quasi\") contract where the plaintiff has conferred a measurable benefit on the defendant without gratuitous intent and it would be unfair to let the defendant retain the benefit without compensating the plaintiff.","irac":{"issue":"If the physician subsequently sends the woman a bill for his services, will she be obligated to pay it?","rule":"Courts will construct an implied-in-law (\"quasi\") contract where the plaintiff has conferred a measurable benefit on the defendant without gratuitous intent and it would be unfair to let the defendant retain the benefit without compensating the plaintiff.","analysis":"In the absence of any contractual relationship between the parties, courts have the power to construct an implied-in-law (\"quasi\") contract to prevent unjust enrichment when: the plaintiff has conferred a measurable benefit (eg, medical services) on the defendant the plaintiff acted without gratuitous intent and it would be unfair to let the defendant retain the benefit without compensating the plaintiff. Unfair retention of a benefit arises when (1) the defendant had an opportunity to decline the benefit but knowingly accepted it or (2) the plaintiff had a reasonable excuse for not giving the defendant such opportunity—often because of an emergency. This allows the plaintiff in a quasi-contract action to recover restitutionary damages equal to the reasonable value of the benefit conferred. Here, the physician conferred a measurable benefit on the woman when he removed glass from her shoulder and closed the wound. Medical services are typically provided with the expectation of compensation. And since the woman was delirious and too shaken to speak, it was reasonable for the physician to act without obtaining her express consent. Therefore, she must pay the physician's bill because the law will imply a promise to pay for his service. (Choice A) Allowing the woman to retain a benefit without compensating the physician would be unjust—not unconscionable. Unconscionability instead arises when a contract is so unfair to one party that no reasonable person in that position would have agreed to it. (Choice B) The physician may have had a moral duty to aid the woman once he saw that she was injured, but he had no legal duty to do so. (Choice C) An implied-in-fact contract arises when the parties' conduct (or failure to act) demonstrates their mutual intent to contract. But here, a court would not find that the woman intended to contract with the physician since she was delirious and unable to speak.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the law will imply a promise to pay for emergency services the physician rendered to the woman."},"questionZh":"一名女子从拥挤的购物中心开车回家时，一辆客货车以极快的速度冲过红绿灯，撞上了该女子的车。一名目睹这一事件的医生将车停在该女子的车旁，发现她的肩膀严重受伤。医生在帮助这名女子下车之前告诉她，他是一名有执照的外科医生。当医生从她肩膀上取下几块玻璃碎片并临时缝合伤口时，这名女子神志不清，颤抖得说不出话来。医生说完后，拿走了女子的联系方式，并承诺稍后会检查她的病情。如果医生随后向该妇女发送了一份服务账单，她是否有义务支付？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为要求妇女支付医生的紧急医疗服务费用是不合理的。","B":"不可以，因为医生有义务在看到妇女受伤后对其进行救助。","C":"是的，因为当妇女允许医生为她治疗时，就形成了默示的事实合同。","D":"是的，因为法律将意味着承诺支付医生为该妇女提供的紧急服务费用。"},"explanationZh":"在双方之间不存在任何合同关系的情况下，法院有权在以下情况下构建默示法律（“准”）合同，以防止不当得利： 原告已向被告授予了可衡量的利益（例如医疗服务） 原告的行为没有无偿意图 并且让被告保留利益而不补偿原告是不公平的。当 (1) 被告有机会拒绝该福利但故意接受时，或 (2) 原告有合理的理由不给被告这样的机会（通常是因为紧急情况）时，就会出现不公平地保留福利。这允许原告在准合同诉讼中获得相当于所赋予利益的合理价值的返还利益赔偿。在这里，当医生从这位女士的肩膀上取下玻璃并缝合伤口时，他给这位女士带来了明显的好处。提供医疗服务时通常期望得到补偿。而且由于这名女子神志不清，颤抖得说不出话来，医生在没有征得她明确同意的情况下采取行动也是合理的。因此，她必须支付医生的账单，因为法律意味着她承诺支付他的服务费用。 （选择 A）允许妇女保留福利而不补偿医生是不公正的——并非不合理。相反，当合同对一方非常不公平，以至于处于该位置的任何理性人都不会同意该合同时，就会出现不合情合理的情况。 （选择 B） 医生在看到该妇女受伤后可能有道义上的义务去帮助她，但他没有法律上的义务这样做。 （选择C）当双方的行为（或不作为）表明他们共同的缔约意图时，默示事实上的合同就产生了。但在这里，法院不会认定该女子有意与医生签订合同，因为她神志不清且无法说话。\n\n教育目标：法院将构建默示法律（“准”）合同，其中原告无偿地向被告授予了可衡量的利益，并且让被告保留该利益而不补偿原告是不公平的。\n\n参考文献\n赔偿和不当得利重述（第三）§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（保护他人生命或健康）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果原告无偿地向被告授予了可衡量的利益，并且让被告保留该利益而不补偿原告，则法院将构建默示法律（“准”）合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果医生随后向该妇女发送了一份服务账单，她是否有义务支付？","rule":"如果原告无偿地向被告授予了可衡量的利益，并且让被告保留该利益而不补偿原告，则法院将构建默示法律（“准”）合同。","analysis":"在双方之间不存在任何合同关系的情况下，法院有权在以下情况下构建默示法律（“准”）合同，以防止不当得利： 原告已向被告授予了可衡量的利益（例如医疗服务） 原告的行为没有无偿意图 并且让被告保留利益而不补偿原告是不公平的。当 (1) 被告有机会拒绝该福利但故意接受时，或 (2) 原告有合理的理由不给被告这样的机会（通常是因为紧急情况）时，就会出现不公平地保留福利。这允许原告在准合同诉讼中获得相当于所赋予利益的合理价值的返还利益赔偿。在这里，当医生从这位女士的肩膀上取下玻璃并缝合伤口时，他给这位女士带来了明显的好处。提供医疗服务时通常期望得到补偿。而且由于这名女子神志不清，颤抖得说不出话来，医生在没有征得她明确同意的情况下采取行动也是合理的。因此，她必须支付医生的账单，因为法律意味着她承诺支付他的服务费用。 （选择 A）允许妇女保留福利而不补偿医生是不公正的——并非不合理。相反，当合同对一方非常不公平，以至于处于该位置的任何理性人都不会同意该合同时，就会出现不合情合理的情况。 （选择 B） 医生在看到该妇女受伤后可能有道义上的义务去帮助她，但他没有法律上的义务这样做。 （选择C）当双方的行为（或不作为）表明他们共同的缔约意图时，默示事实上的合同就产生了。但在这里，法院不会认定该女子有意与医生签订合同，因为她神志不清且无法说话。\n\n教育目标：法院将构建默示法律（“准”）合同，其中原告无偿地向被告授予了可衡量的利益，并且让被告保留该利益而不补偿原告是不公平的。\n\n参考文献\n赔偿和不当得利重述（第三）§ 20（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（保护他人生命或健康）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为法律将意味着承诺支付医生为该妇女提供的紧急服务费用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-269917db7f6d3c59"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":172,"sourceQuestionPage":509,"sourceQuestionPages":[509],"sourceExplanationPages":[510,511],"sourceAnswerPages":[510,511],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-173","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A businesswoman entered into a contract with a painter by the terms of which he was to paint the businesswoman's office for $1,000 and was required to do all the work over the following weekend so as to avoid disruption of her business. If the painter had started to paint on the following Saturday morning, he could have finished before Sunday evening. However, he stayed home that Saturday morning and did not start to paint until Saturday afternoon. By late Saturday afternoon, the painter realized that he had underestimated the time it would take to finish the job if he continued to work alone. The painter phoned the businesswoman at her home and accurately informed her that it was impossible to finish the work over the weekend unless he hired a helper. He also stated that to do so would require an additional charge of $200 for the work. The businesswoman told the painter that she apparently had no choice but to pay \"whatever it takes\" to get the work done as scheduled. The painter hired an assistant to help finish the painting and paid him $200. The businesswoman has offered to pay the painter $1,000. The painter is demanding $1,200. How much is the painter likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"$1,000 only, because the businesswoman received no consideration for her promise to pay the additional sum.","B":"$1,000 only, because the businesswoman's promise to pay \"whatever it takes\" is too uncertain to be enforceable.","C":"$1,200, because the impossibility of the painter's completing the work alone discharged the original contract and a new contract was formed","D":"$1,200, in order to prevent the businesswoman's unjust enrichment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract modifications must be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way—unless the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances. But the promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is insufficient for new consideration and cannot support a modification. Here, the painter attempted to modify the $1,000 contract by telling the businesswoman that he needed to hire a helper, which would require an additional charge of $200. Although the businesswoman agreed to pay \"whatever it takes,\" the painter had a preexisting duty to finish painting over the weekend. Therefore, the businesswoman received no consideration for her promise to pay the additional sum, and the painter is likely to recover $1,000 only. (Choice B) There is no consideration if either party makes an illusory promise—ie, one that essentially pledges nothing because it is too uncertain (or because the promising party can choose whether to honor it). But the businesswoman's promise to pay \"whatever it takes\" was not too uncertain since it was a response to the painter's statement that he needed an additional $200 to finish the work. (Choice C) Contractual duties can be discharged due to impossibility—eg, when an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impossible to perform. But here, the painter's inability to finish the work was due to his own delay—not an unanticipated or extraordinary event. (Choice D) A party may recover restitution damages to prevent unjust enrichment if the party conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on another without a contractual obligation to do so. Here, the painter conferred a non-gratuitous benefit by paying the helper $200 to finish\n\nthe work. However, since the painter was already obligated to paint the office for $1,000, the businesswoman was not unjustly enriched by his expenditure.\n\nEducational objective: At common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration, which requires both parties to alter their duties in some way. Performing or promising to perform a preexisting duty is insufficient for consideration and will not support a modification.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that performance of a preexisting legal duty is not consideration).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that modification of a contract without new consideration is binding if it is fair and equitable in view of unanticipated circumstances).","educationalObjective":"At common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration, which requires both parties to alter their duties in some way. Performing or promising to perform a preexisting duty is insufficient for consideration and will not support a modification.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that performance of a preexisting legal duty is not consideration).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 89 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that modification of a contract without new consideration is binding if it is fair and equitable in view of unanticipated circumstances).","rule":"At common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration, which requires both parties to alter their duties in some way. Performing or promising to perform a preexisting duty is insufficient for consideration and will not support a modification.","irac":{"issue":"How much is the painter likely to recover?","rule":"At common law, contract modifications generally must be supported by new consideration, which requires both parties to alter their duties in some way. Performing or promising to perform a preexisting duty is insufficient for consideration and will not support a modification.","analysis":"Modifying existing contracts\n\nGoverning law Requirements\n\nCommon Traditional Parties' agreement law view New consideration Modern view Parties' agreement New consideration unless modification is fair & equitable due to unanticipated circumstances UCC Parties' agreement Good faith (ie, honesty & observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing) New consideration not required UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nAt common law, contract modifications must be supported by new consideration—ie, each party must alter its duties in some way—unless the modification is fair and equitable in light of unanticipated circumstances. But the promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty is insufficient for new consideration and cannot support a modification. Here, the painter attempted to modify the $1,000 contract by telling the businesswoman that he needed to hire a helper, which would require an additional charge of $200. Although the businesswoman agreed to pay \"whatever it takes,\" the painter had a preexisting duty to finish painting over the weekend. Therefore, the businesswoman received no consideration for her promise to pay the additional sum, and the painter is likely to recover $1,000 only. (Choice B) There is no consideration if either party makes an illusory promise—ie, one that essentially pledges nothing because it is too uncertain (or because the promising party can choose whether to honor it). But the businesswoman's promise to pay \"whatever it takes\" was not too uncertain since it was a response to the painter's statement that he needed an additional $200 to finish the work. (Choice C) Contractual duties can be discharged due to impossibility—eg, when an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impossible to perform. But here, the painter's inability to finish the work was due to his own delay—not an unanticipated or extraordinary event. (Choice D) A party may recover restitution damages to prevent unjust enrichment if the party conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on another without a contractual obligation to do so. Here, the painter conferred a non-gratuitous benefit by paying the helper $200 to finish\n\nthe work. However, since the painter was already obligated to paint the office for $1,000, the businesswoman was not unjustly enriched by his expenditure.","conclusion":"Answer A: $1,000 only, because the businesswoman received no consideration for her promise to pay the additional sum."},"questionZh":"一名女商人与一名油漆工签订了一份合同，合同条款规定油漆工以 1,000 美元的价格粉刷该女商人的办公室，并要求在接下来的周末完成所有工作，以免影响她的生意。如果画家在下一个星期六早上开始作画，他本可以在星期日晚上之前完成。然而，周六早上他呆在家里，直到周六下午才开始画画。到了周六下午晚些时候，画家意识到，如果他继续独自工作，他低估了完成这项工作所需的时间。油漆工给女商人家里打电话，准确地告诉她，除非雇个帮手，周末不可能完成工作。他还表示，这样做需要额外支付 200 美元的费用。这位女商人告诉画家，她显然别无选择，只能支付“不惜一切代价”才能按期完成工作。画家雇了一名助手帮助完成这幅画，并付给他 200 美元。这位女商人愿意支付画家 1,000 美元。油漆工要价 1,200 美元。画家有可能恢复多少钱？","choicesZh":{"A":"只有 1,000 美元，因为这位女商人承诺支付额外费用，但没有得到任何报酬。","B":"只要 1,000 美元，因为这位女商人“不惜一切代价”支付的承诺太不确定，无法执行。","C":"1,200美元，因油漆工无法单独完成工作，解除了原合同，并签订了新合同","D":"1,200美元，以防止女商人不当得利。"},"explanationZh":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同修改必须得到新考虑的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其义务，除非修改在不可预见的情况下是公平公正的。但履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不足以引起新的考虑，并且不能支持修改。在此，油漆工试图修改 1,000 美元的合同，告诉女商人他需要雇用一名帮手，这将需要额外支付 200 美元的费用。尽管女商人同意“不惜一切代价”支付费用，但画家有义务在周末完成绘画。因此，女商人承诺支付额外款项并没有得到任何报酬，油漆工很可能只收回1000美元。 （选择B）如果任何一方做出虚幻的承诺，即由于太不确定（或者因为承诺方可以选择是否兑现）而基本上不做出任何承诺，则无需约因。但这位女商人“不惜一切代价”的承诺并不太不确定，因为这是对画家声称他需要额外 200 美元才能完成作品的回应。 （选择 C）合同义务可以因不可能而被解除——例如，当意外或异常事件导致合同义务无法履行时。但在这里，画家无法完成作品是由于他自己的延误，而不是意外或非同寻常的事件。 （选择 D）如果一方当事人在没有合同义务的情况下向另一方授予非无偿利益，则该方可以追偿损害赔偿，以防止不当得利。在这里，油漆工给予了一个无偿的好处，付给帮手 200 美元来完成\n\n工作。然而，由于油漆工已经有义务以 1,000 美元粉刷办公室，这位女商人并没有因为他的支出而暴富。\n\n教育目标：在普通法中，合同修改通常必须得到新考虑的支持，这要求双方以某种方式改变其职责。履行或承诺履行先前存在的职责不足以考虑，并且不支持修改。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 73（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说履行先前存在的法律义务不是对价）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 89（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果考虑到意外情况，合同的修改是公平公正的，则无需新约因的合同修改具有约束力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在普通法中，合同修改通常必须有新的对价的支持，这要求双方以某种方式改变其义务。履行或承诺履行先前存在的职责不足以考虑，并且不支持修改。","iracZh":{"issue":"画家有可能恢复多少钱？","rule":"在普通法中，合同修改通常必须有新的对价的支持，这要求双方以某种方式改变其义务。履行或承诺履行先前存在的职责不足以考虑，并且不支持修改。","analysis":"修改现有合同\n\n适用法律要求\n\n共同 传统 当事人协议法观点 新约因 现代观点 当事人协议 新约因，除非由于意外情况而进行公平和公正的修改 UCC 当事人协议 善意（即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准） 不需要新约因 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n在普通法中，合同修改必须得到新考虑的支持，即各方必须以某种方式改变其义务，除非修改在不可预见的情况下是公平公正的。但履行或履行先前存在的义务的承诺不足以引起新的考虑，并且不能支持修改。在此，油漆工试图修改 1,000 美元的合同，告诉女商人他需要雇用一名帮手，这将需要额外支付 200 美元的费用。尽管女商人同意“不惜一切代价”支付费用，但画家有义务在周末完成绘画。因此，女商人承诺支付额外款项并没有得到任何报酬，油漆工很可能只收回1000美元。 （选择B）如果任何一方做出虚幻的承诺，即由于太不确定（或者因为承诺方可以选择是否兑现）而基本上不做出任何承诺，则无需约因。但这位女商人“不惜一切代价”的承诺并不太不确定，因为这是对画家声称他需要额外 200 美元才能完成作品的回应。 （选择 C）合同义务可以因不可能而被解除——例如，当意外或异常事件导致合同义务无法履行时。但在这里，画家无法完成作品是由于他自己的延误，而不是意外或非同寻常的事件。 （选择 D）如果一方当事人在没有合同义务的情况下向另一方授予非无偿利益，则该方可以追偿损害赔偿，以防止不当得利。在这里，油漆工给予了一个无偿的好处，付给帮手 200 美元来完成\n\n工作。然而，由于油漆工已经有义务以 1,000 美元粉刷办公室，这位女商人并没有因为他的支出而暴富。\n\n教育目标：在普通法中，合同修改通常必须得到新考虑的支持，这要求双方以某种方式改变其职责。履行或承诺履行先前存在的职责不足以考虑，并且不支持修改。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 73（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说履行先前存在的法律义务不是对价）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 89（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果考虑到意外情况，合同的修改是公平公正的，则无需新约因的合同修改具有约束力）。","conclusion":"答案 A：只有 1,000 美元，因为这位女商人承诺支付额外费用，但没有得到任何报酬。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":173,"sourceQuestionPage":512,"sourceQuestionPages":[512],"sourceExplanationPages":[513,514],"sourceAnswerPages":[513,514],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-174","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Offer, Acceptance & Revocation","question":"An ice hockey goalie received word that his favorite team's only backup goaltender had been injured in an accident and would likely be unable to play during the upcoming season. The goalie sent a letter to the team's general manager on September 18 offering to play for the team for an annual salary of $450,000. The general manager received the offer letter on September 21 and, in desperation, immediately mailed the goalie a letter accepting the offer on behalf of the team. On September 22, the team's injured goaltender made a miraculous recovery and the general manager sent the goalie a second letter rejecting the goalie's offer. The goalie received the general manager's rejection on September 25 and received the general manager's acceptance the following day. Was a contract formed between the goalie and the ice hockey team?","choices":{"A":"No, because the goalie received the general manager's rejection before the acceptance.","B":"No, because there was no meeting of the minds between the goalie and the general manager.","C":"Yes, because the team assumed the risk that the backup goaltender would recover from his injury.","D":"Yes, because the team effectively accepted the goalie's offer prior to the attempted rejection."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract formation requires both an offer and an acceptance (ie, mutual assent). Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance by mail is generally effective upon dispatch, while a rejection is effective only upon receipt. If the offeree has a change of heart and sends: An acceptance followed by a rejection – a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance, regardless of which communication is received first\n\nA rejection followed by an acceptance – a contract is formed if the acceptance is received first; in contrast, no contract is formed if the rejection is received first*\n\nHere, the general manager sent a letter accepting the goalie's offer followed by a second letter rejecting the offer. Because the acceptance was sent first, a contract was formed upon dispatch of the acceptance. This is true even though the goalie received the general manager's rejection before the acceptance (Choice A). Therefore, an enforceable contract exists between the goalie and the ice hockey team because the team effectively accepted the goalie's offer prior to the attempted rejection. *However, the offeree may be estopped from enforcing the contract if the offeror received the rejection first and detrimentally relied upon it. (Choice B) \"Meeting of the minds\" refers to the parties' mutual assent to form a contract based on agreed-upon terms. Here, there was a meeting of the minds because the goalie offered to play for the team for $450,000 and the general manager accepted the terms of that offer. (Choice C) The fact that the team assumed the risk of the injured goaltender's recovery would prevent the team from asserting certain defenses to enforcement of a contract (eg, impracticability). But here, the question is whether the parties formed a contract.\n\nEducational objective: Where an offeree mails an acceptance and later mails a rejection, a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance—regardless of which communication is received first.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (time when acceptance takes effect).","educationalObjective":"Where an offeree mails an acceptance and later mails a rejection, a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance—regardless of which communication is received first.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (time when acceptance takes effect).","rule":"Where an offeree mails an acceptance and later mails a rejection, a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance—regardless of which communication is received first.","irac":{"issue":"Was a contract formed between the goalie and the ice hockey team?","rule":"Where an offeree mails an acceptance and later mails a rejection, a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance—regardless of which communication is received first.","analysis":"Contract formation requires both an offer and an acceptance (ie, mutual assent). Under the mailbox rule, an acceptance by mail is generally effective upon dispatch, while a rejection is effective only upon receipt. If the offeree has a change of heart and sends: An acceptance followed by a rejection – a contract is formed upon dispatch of the acceptance, regardless of which communication is received first\n\nA rejection followed by an acceptance – a contract is formed if the acceptance is received first; in contrast, no contract is formed if the rejection is received first*\n\nHere, the general manager sent a letter accepting the goalie's offer followed by a second letter rejecting the offer. Because the acceptance was sent first, a contract was formed upon dispatch of the acceptance. This is true even though the goalie received the general manager's rejection before the acceptance (Choice A). Therefore, an enforceable contract exists between the goalie and the ice hockey team because the team effectively accepted the goalie's offer prior to the attempted rejection. *However, the offeree may be estopped from enforcing the contract if the offeror received the rejection first and detrimentally relied upon it. (Choice B) \"Meeting of the minds\" refers to the parties' mutual assent to form a contract based on agreed-upon terms. Here, there was a meeting of the minds because the goalie offered to play for the team for $450,000 and the general manager accepted the terms of that offer. (Choice C) The fact that the team assumed the risk of the injured goaltender's recovery would prevent the team from asserting certain defenses to enforcement of a contract (eg, impracticability). But here, the question is whether the parties formed a contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the team effectively accepted the goalie's offer prior to the attempted rejection."},"questionZh":"一名冰球守门员收到消息，他最喜欢的球队唯一的替补守门员在一次事故中受伤，很可能无法在即将到来的赛季中参加比赛。这位守门员于9月18日致信球队总经理，提出为球队效力，年薪为45万美元。总经理在9月21日收到了报价信，无奈之下，立即给守门员邮寄了一封代表球队接受报价的信。 9月22日，球队受伤的守门员奇迹般康复，总经理向守门员发出第二封信，拒绝了守门员的报价。这名守门员在9月25日收到了总经理的拒绝，第二天就收到了总经理的接受。守门员和冰球队之间是否签订了合同？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为守门员在接受之前就收到了总经理的拒绝。","B":"不，因为守门员和总经理之间没有达成共识。","C":"是的，因为球队承担了替补守门员伤愈的风险。","D":"是的，因为球队在试图拒绝之前实际上接受了守门员的提议。"},"explanationZh":"合同的订立需要要约和接受（即双方同意）。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受通常在发送后生效，而拒绝仅在收到后生效。如果受要约人改变主意并发送： 接受后拒绝 – 合同在发送接受后成立，无论先收到哪条通信\n\n先拒绝后接受——如果先收到接受，则合同成立；相反，如果先收到拒绝，则不会形成合同*\n\n在这里，总经理发送了一封信接受守门员的报价，随后又发送了第二封信拒绝该报价。由于验收是先发出的，因此合同在发出验收后即成立。即使守门员在接受之前收到了总经理的拒绝（选择 A），情况也是如此。因此，守门员和冰球队之间存在可执行的合同，因为球队在试图拒绝之前实际上接受了守门员的报价。 *但是，如果要约人首先收到拒绝并​​有害地依赖该拒绝，则受要约人可能会被阻止执行合同。 （选项B）“协商一致”是指当事人双方一致同意，按照约定的条款订立合同。在这里，双方达成了共识，因为守门员提出以 45 万美元为球队效力，而总经理接受了该报价的条款。 （选择C） 球队承担受伤守门员康复的风险这一事实将阻止球队对合同的执行提出某些抗辩（例如，不切实际）。但这里的问题是当事人是否订立了合同。\n\n教育目标：如果受要约人邮寄了接受书，然后又邮寄了拒绝书，则合同在发送接受书后即告成立，无论首先收到哪一条通信。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（接受生效的时间）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果受要约人邮寄了承诺书，随后又邮寄了拒绝书，则合同在承诺书发出后即告成立，无论先收到哪一条通信。","iracZh":{"issue":"守门员和冰球队之间是否签订了合同？","rule":"如果受要约人邮寄了承诺书，随后又邮寄了拒绝书，则合同在承诺书发出后即告成立，无论先收到哪一条通信。","analysis":"合同的订立需要要约和接受（即双方同意）。根据邮箱规则，通过邮件接受通常在发送后生效，而拒绝仅在收到后生效。如果受要约人改变主意并发送： 接受后拒绝 – 合同在发送接受后成立，无论先收到哪条通信\n\n先拒绝后接受——如果先收到接受，则合同成立；相反，如果先收到拒绝，则不会形成合同*\n\n在这里，总经理发送了一封信接受守门员的报价，随后又发送了第二封信拒绝该报价。由于验收是先发出的，因此合同在发出验收后即成立。即使守门员在接受之前收到了总经理的拒绝（选择 A），情况也是如此。因此，守门员和冰球队之间存在可执行的合同，因为球队在试图拒绝之前实际上接受了守门员的报价。 *但是，如果要约人首先收到拒绝并​​有害地依赖该拒绝，则受要约人可能会被阻止执行合同。 （选项B）“协商一致”是指当事人双方一致同意，按照约定的条款订立合同。在这里，双方达成了共识，因为守门员提出以 45 万美元为球队效力，而总经理接受了该报价的条款。 （选择C） 球队承担受伤守门员康复的风险这一事实将阻止球队对合同的执行提出某些抗辩（例如，不切实际）。但这里的问题是当事人是否订立了合同。\n\n教育目标：如果受要约人邮寄了接受书，然后又邮寄了拒绝书，则合同在发送接受书后即告成立，无论首先收到哪一条通信。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 63 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（接受生效的时间）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为球队在试图拒绝之前实际上接受了守门员的提议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-c1c996d640008b3c"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":174,"sourceQuestionPage":515,"sourceQuestionPages":[515],"sourceExplanationPages":[516,517],"sourceAnswerPages":[516,517],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-175","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A debtor owed a lender $1,500. The statute of limitations barred recovery on the claim. The debtor wrote to the lender, stating, \"I promise to pay you $500 if you will extinguish the debt.\" The lender agreed. Is the debtor's promise to pay the lender $500 enforceable?","choices":{"A":"No, because the debtor made no promise not to plead the statute of limitations as a defense.","B":"No, because there was no consideration for the debtor's promise.","C":"Yes, because the debtor's promise provided a benefit to the lender.","D":"Yes, because the debtor's promise to pay part of the barred antecedent debt needs no consideration to be enforceable."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Exceptions to preexisting-duty rule\n\nMain rule Promise to perform (or performance of) preexisting duty is not consideration\n\nCommon-law New/altered New duty added or preexisting duty altered (eg, exceptions consideration acceleration of due date)\n\nRatification of New promise to perform voidable duty (eg, upon voidable duty reaching age of majority)\n\nHonest dispute Scope of duty honestly disputed\n\nUnexpected event Fair & equitable modification in view of unanticipated circumstances\n\nRenewed promise Renewed promise to pay preexisting debt despite technical defense (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) if written or partially performed\n\nThird party Third party offers additional promise that is contingent on performance of preexisting duty\n\nUCC exception Modification Modification sought in good faith—ie, honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nA promise to perform a preexisting duty is generally unenforceable for lack of consideration. But under a long-established exception to this rule, a debtor's renewed promise to pay all or part of a preexisting debt is enforceable if: the debtor renewed the promise despite the existence of a technical defense to recovery of the original debt (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) and\n\nthe renewed promise was made in writing or partially performed.\n\nHere, the statute of limitations barred recovery on the lender's claim against the debtor for $1,500. Despite this defense, the debtor made a renewed promise to pay $500 of the preexisting debt. And though there was no consideration for this promise, it was made in a written letter (Choice B). Therefore, the debtor's promise to pay $500 is enforceable without consideration.\n\n(Choice A) Although the debtor made no promise to waive his statute-of-limitations defense, his renewed promise to pay part of the debt is still enforceable since it was made in writing despite being time-barred. And that promise will defeat the statute-of-limitations defense with respect to $500 of the debt. (Choice C) Early case law described the consideration requirement in terms of benefit to the promisee (here, the lender), whereas modern courts focus instead on legal detriment to the promisor (here, the debtor). But whether the debtor's promise was a benefit or a detriment is irrelevant because consideration is not required.\n\nEducational objective: A promise to perform a preexisting duty is generally unenforceable. But a debtor's renewed promise to pay all or part of a preexisting debt is enforceable if the debtor renewed his/her promise despite a technical defense (eg, statute of limitations) AND the renewed promise was made in writing or partially performed.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 82 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a renewed promise to pay a preexisting debt is binding if that debt would be binding except for the statute of limitations).","educationalObjective":"A promise to perform a preexisting duty is generally unenforceable. But a debtor's renewed promise to pay all or part of a preexisting debt is enforceable if the debtor renewed his/her promise despite a technical defense (eg, statute of limitations) AND the renewed promise was made in writing or partially performed.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 82 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a renewed promise to pay a preexisting debt is binding if that debt would be binding except for the statute of limitations).","rule":"A promise to perform a preexisting duty is generally unenforceable. But a debtor's renewed promise to pay all or part of a preexisting debt is enforceable if the debtor renewed his/her promise despite a technical defense (eg, statute of limitations) AND the renewed promise was made in writing or partially performed.","irac":{"issue":"Is the debtor's promise to pay the lender $500 enforceable?","rule":"A promise to perform a preexisting duty is generally unenforceable. But a debtor's renewed promise to pay all or part of a preexisting debt is enforceable if the debtor renewed his/her promise despite a technical defense (eg, statute of limitations) AND the renewed promise was made in writing or partially performed.","analysis":"Exceptions to preexisting-duty rule\n\nMain rule Promise to perform (or performance of) preexisting duty is not consideration\n\nCommon-law New/altered New duty added or preexisting duty altered (eg, exceptions consideration acceleration of due date)\n\nRatification of New promise to perform voidable duty (eg, upon voidable duty reaching age of majority)\n\nHonest dispute Scope of duty honestly disputed\n\nUnexpected event Fair & equitable modification in view of unanticipated circumstances\n\nRenewed promise Renewed promise to pay preexisting debt despite technical defense (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) if written or partially performed\n\nThird party Third party offers additional promise that is contingent on performance of preexisting duty\n\nUCC exception Modification Modification sought in good faith—ie, honesty and observance of reasonable commercial standards of fair dealing\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nA promise to perform a preexisting duty is generally unenforceable for lack of consideration. But under a long-established exception to this rule, a debtor's renewed promise to pay all or part of a preexisting debt is enforceable if: the debtor renewed the promise despite the existence of a technical defense to recovery of the original debt (eg, statute of limitations, bankruptcy) and\n\nthe renewed promise was made in writing or partially performed.\n\nHere, the statute of limitations barred recovery on the lender's claim against the debtor for $1,500. Despite this defense, the debtor made a renewed promise to pay $500 of the preexisting debt. And though there was no consideration for this promise, it was made in a written letter (Choice B). Therefore, the debtor's promise to pay $500 is enforceable without consideration.\n\n(Choice A) Although the debtor made no promise to waive his statute-of-limitations defense, his renewed promise to pay part of the debt is still enforceable since it was made in writing despite being time-barred. And that promise will defeat the statute-of-limitations defense with respect to $500 of the debt. (Choice C) Early case law described the consideration requirement in terms of benefit to the promisee (here, the lender), whereas modern courts focus instead on legal detriment to the promisor (here, the debtor). But whether the debtor's promise was a benefit or a detriment is irrelevant because consideration is not required.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the debtor's promise to pay part of the barred antecedent debt needs no consideration to be enforceable."},"questionZh":"债务人欠贷方 1,500 美元。时效限制了索赔的追偿。债务人写信给贷方说：“如果你愿意清偿债务，我保证支付你 500 美元。”贷款人同意了。债务人向贷方支付 500 美元的承诺是否可执行？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为债务人没有承诺不以时效为由进行抗辩。","B":"不，因为没有考虑债务人的承诺。","C":"是的，因为债务人的承诺为贷方提供了利益。","D":"是的，因为债务人支付部分禁止的先前债务的承诺不需要约因即可强制执行。"},"explanationZh":"既存义务规则的例外情况\n\n主要规则 承诺履行（或履行）先前存在的职责不构成对价\n\n普通法 新/更改 添加新义务或更改先前存在的义务（例如，考虑加速到期日的例外情况）\n\n批准履行可撤销义务的新承诺（例如，在可撤销义务达到成年年龄时）\n\n诚实争议 诚实争议的职责范围\n\n意外事件 鉴于意外情况进行公平公正的修改\n\n更新的承诺 如果书面或部分履行，则即使有技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），仍将支付先前存在的债务\n\n第三方 第三方提供额外承诺，该承诺取决于履行先前存在的义务\n\nUCC 例外修改 真诚地寻求修改，即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n由于缺乏约因，履行先前存在的义务的承诺通常无法执行。但根据该规则长期确立的例外情况，债务人重新承诺偿还全部或部分先前存在的债务是可执行的，如果： 尽管存在收回原始债务的技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），债务人仍重新承诺，并且\n\n新的承诺以书面形式作出或部分履行。\n\n在此，诉讼时效禁止贷款人向债务人索取 1,500 美元的索赔。尽管有这样的抗辩，债务人仍再次承诺支付 500 美元的先前债务。尽管没有考虑到这一承诺，但它是通过书面信函做出的（选项 B）。因此，债务人支付 500 美元的承诺无需对价即可强制执行。\n\n（选项A）虽然债务人没有承诺放弃其诉讼时效抗辩，但他重新承诺支付部分债务仍然具有可执行性，因为该承诺是在时效期间以书面形式做出的。这一承诺将推翻有关 500 美元债务的诉讼时效抗辩。 （选项C）早期判例法以受诺人（此处为贷款人）的利益来描述对价要求，而现代法院则侧重于对诺言人（此处为债务人）的法律损害。但债务人的承诺是有益还是有害并不重要，因为不需要约因对价。\n\n教育目标：履行先前存在的义务的承诺通常是无法执行的。但是，如果债务人在技术抗辩（例如诉讼时效）的情况下重新承诺，并且新的承诺以书面形式作出或部分履行，则债务人重新承诺偿还全部或部分先前存在的债务是可执行的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 82（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果债务在时效之外具有约束力，则支付先前存在债务的新承诺也具有约束力）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"履行先前存在的义务的承诺通常是无法执行的。但是，如果债务人在技术抗辩（例如诉讼时效）的情况下重新承诺，并且新的承诺以书面形式作出或部分履行，则债务人重新承诺偿还全部或部分先前存在的债务是可执行的。","iracZh":{"issue":"债务人向贷方支付 500 美元的承诺是否可执行？","rule":"履行先前存在的义务的承诺通常是无法执行的。但是，如果债务人在技术抗辩（例如诉讼时效）的情况下重新承诺，并且新的承诺以书面形式作出或部分履行，则债务人重新承诺偿还全部或部分先前存在的债务是可执行的。","analysis":"既存义务规则的例外情况\n\n主要规则 承诺履行（或履行）先前存在的职责不构成对价\n\n普通法 新/更改 添加新义务或更改先前存在的义务（例如，考虑加速到期日的例外情况）\n\n批准履行可撤销义务的新承诺（例如，在可撤销义务达到成年年龄时）\n\n诚实争议 诚实争议的职责范围\n\n意外事件 鉴于意外情况进行公平公正的修改\n\n更新的承诺 如果书面或部分履行，则即使有技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），仍将支付先前存在的债务\n\n第三方 第三方提供额外承诺，该承诺取决于履行先前存在的义务\n\nUCC 例外修改 真诚地寻求修改，即诚实并遵守公平交易的合理商业标准\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n由于缺乏约因，履行先前存在的义务的承诺通常无法执行。但根据该规则长期确立的例外情况，债务人重新承诺偿还全部或部分先前存在的债务是可执行的，如果： 尽管存在收回原始债务的技术抗辩（例如时效、破产），债务人仍重新承诺，并且\n\n新的承诺以书面形式作出或部分履行。\n\n在此，诉讼时效禁止贷款人向债务人索取 1,500 美元的索赔。尽管有这样的抗辩，债务人仍再次承诺支付 500 美元的先前债务。尽管没有考虑到这一承诺，但它是通过书面信函做出的（选项 B）。因此，债务人支付 500 美元的承诺无需对价即可强制执行。\n\n（选项A）虽然债务人没有承诺放弃其诉讼时效抗辩，但他重新承诺支付部分债务仍然具有可执行性，因为该承诺是在时效期间以书面形式做出的。这一承诺将推翻有关 500 美元债务的诉讼时效抗辩。 （选项C）早期判例法以受诺人（此处为贷款人）的利益来描述对价要求，而现代法院则侧重于对诺言人（此处为债务人）的法律损害。但债务人的承诺是有益还是有害并不重要，因为不需要约因对价。\n\n教育目标：履行先前存在的义务的承诺通常是无法执行的。但是，如果债务人在技术抗辩（例如诉讼时效）的情况下重新承诺，并且新的承诺以书面形式作出或部分履行，则债务人重新承诺偿还全部或部分先前存在的债务是可执行的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 82（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果债务在时效之外具有约束力，则支付先前存在债务的新承诺也具有约束力）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为债务人支付部分禁止的先前债务的承诺不需要约因即可强制执行。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":175,"sourceQuestionPage":518,"sourceQuestionPages":[518],"sourceExplanationPages":[519,520],"sourceAnswerPages":[519,520],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-176","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A written construction contract, under which a contractor agreed to build a new house for a landowner at a fixed price of $200,000, contained the following provision: \"Prior to construction or during the course thereof, this contract may be modified by mutual agreement of the parties as to 'extras' or other departures from the plans and specifications provided by the landowner and attached hereto. Such modifications, however, may be authorized only in writing, signed by both parties.\" During construction, the contractor incorporated into the structure overhanging gargoyles and other \"extras\" orally requested by the landowner for orally agreed prices in addition to the contract price. The landowner subsequently refused to pay anything for such extras, aggregating $30,000 at the agreed prices, solely on the ground that no written, signed authorization for them was ever effected. If the contractor sues the owner on account of the \"extras,\" which, if any, of the following will effectively support the landowner's defense?","choices":{"A":"The parol evidence rule and the failure of an express condition.","B":"The parol evidence rule and the statute of frauds.","C":"The preexisting duty rule and the failure of an express condition.","D":"Neither the parol evidence rule, the preexisting duty rule, the failure of an express condition, nor the statute of frauds."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The parol evidence rule generally prevents a party to a written contract from presenting evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the terms of the contract as written. But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, the parties' oral modifications regarding \"extras\" that occurred after the contract was entered) (Choices A & B). An express condition is an explicit clause stating that an uncertain future event will (1) create a duty to perform (ie, condition precedent) or (2) extinguish a duty to perform (ie, condition subsequent). In addition, a party's duty to perform is excused if a condition precedent fails to occur. Here, the landowner's duty to pay for extras required a written modification signed by both parties—not the occurrence of an uncertain future event. Therefore, the failure to make such a writing was not a failure of an express condition (Choices A & C). Under the statute of frauds, certain types of contract modifications must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. But a signed writing is not required to modify a construction contract if the modified contract can be completed within one year. And here, there is no indication that construction would necessarily take more than one year (Choice B). Contract modifications require new consideration—ie, each party must alter his/her duties in some way. But a promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty does not constitute consideration. Here, the parties agreed that any \"extras\" would require a written modification—ie, there was no preexisting duty to provide extras. Therefore, the parties' oral modifications were supported by consideration (Choice C).\n\nEducational objective: In a contract dispute, (1) the parol evidence rule can exclude evidence that contradicts a written contract, (2) the failure of an express condition can excuse a duty to perform, (3) the statute of frauds can invalidate an unwritten or unsigned contract, and (4) the preexisting duty rule can make a contract unenforceable for lack of consideration.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 213 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an integrated writing supersedes inconsistent terms of prior agreements).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the non- occurrence of a condition precedent discharges the duty to perform when the condition can no longer occur).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 125 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (statute of frauds vis-à-vis transfers of land).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that performance of a preexisting legal duty is not consideration).","educationalObjective":"In a contract dispute, (1) the parol evidence rule can exclude evidence that contradicts a written contract, (2) the failure of an express condition can excuse a duty to perform, (3) the statute of frauds can invalidate an unwritten or unsigned contract, and (4) the preexisting duty rule can make a contract unenforceable for lack of consideration.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 213 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an integrated writing supersedes inconsistent terms of prior agreements).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the non- occurrence of a condition precedent discharges the duty to perform when the condition can no longer occur).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 125 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (statute of frauds vis-à-vis transfers of land).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 73 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that performance of a preexisting legal duty is not consideration).","rule":"In a contract dispute, (1) the parol evidence rule can exclude evidence that contradicts a written contract, (2) the failure of an express condition can excuse a duty to perform, (3) the statute of frauds can invalidate an unwritten or unsigned contract, and (4) the preexisting duty rule can make a contract unenforceable for lack of consideration.","irac":{"issue":"If the contractor sues the owner on account of the \"extras,\" which, if any, of the following will effectively support the landowner's defense?","rule":"In a contract dispute, (1) the parol evidence rule can exclude evidence that contradicts a written contract, (2) the failure of an express condition can excuse a duty to perform, (3) the statute of frauds can invalidate an unwritten or unsigned contract, and (4) the preexisting duty rule can make a contract unenforceable for lack of consideration.","analysis":"The parol evidence rule generally prevents a party to a written contract from presenting evidence of a prior or contemporaneous agreement that contradicts the terms of the contract as written. But this rule does not bar evidence of subsequent agreements between the parties (eg, the parties' oral modifications regarding \"extras\" that occurred after the contract was entered) (Choices A & B). An express condition is an explicit clause stating that an uncertain future event will (1) create a duty to perform (ie, condition precedent) or (2) extinguish a duty to perform (ie, condition subsequent). In addition, a party's duty to perform is excused if a condition precedent fails to occur. Here, the landowner's duty to pay for extras required a written modification signed by both parties—not the occurrence of an uncertain future event. Therefore, the failure to make such a writing was not a failure of an express condition (Choices A & C). Under the statute of frauds, certain types of contract modifications must be in writing and signed by the party against whom enforcement is sought. But a signed writing is not required to modify a construction contract if the modified contract can be completed within one year. And here, there is no indication that construction would necessarily take more than one year (Choice B). Contract modifications require new consideration—ie, each party must alter his/her duties in some way. But a promise to perform, or the performance of, a preexisting duty does not constitute consideration. Here, the parties agreed that any \"extras\" would require a written modification—ie, there was no preexisting duty to provide extras. Therefore, the parties' oral modifications were supported by consideration (Choice C).","conclusion":"Answer D: Neither the parol evidence rule, the preexisting duty rule, the failure of an express condition, nor the statute of frauds."},"questionZh":"一份书面建筑合同中，承包商同意以 20 万美元的固定价格为土地所有者建造一座新房屋，其中包含以下条款：“在施工之前或施工过程中，经双方同意，本合同可根据土地所有者提供的并附在本合同中的计划和规格进行‘额外’或其他偏离的修改。但是，此类修改只能以书面形式授权，并由双方签署。”在施工过程中，承包商将悬垂的石像鬼和土地所有者口头要求的其他“额外费用”纳入到结构中，并以除合同价格之外的口头商定价格。土地所有者随后拒绝支付任何此类额外费用，仅以未获得任何书面、签署的授权为由，按约定价格总计支付 30,000 美元。如果承包商以“额外费用”为由起诉业主，以下哪一项（如果有的话）将有效支持土地所有者的辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）和明示条件的失败。","B":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）和防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）。","C":"先前存在的义务规则和明示条件的失败。","D":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）、既存义务规则、明示条件失败或防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）均不适用。"},"explanationZh":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常防止书面合同的一方提供与书面合同条款相矛盾的先前或同期协议的证据。但该规则并不排除当事人之间后续协议的证据（例如，当事人在合同订立后发生的有关“额外费用”的口头修改）（选项A和B）。明示条件是明确的条款，规定不确定的未来事件将（1）产生履行义务（即先决条件）或（2）消除履行义务（即后续条件）。此外，如果先决条件未能发生，则一方的履行义务可以免除。在这里，土地所有者支付额外费用的义务需要双方签署的书面修改，而不是发生不确定的未来事件。因此，未能做出这样的写作并不是明确条件的失败（选项A和C）。根据欺诈法，某些类型的合同修改必须采用书面形式，并由寻求执行的一方签署。但是，如果修改后的合同能够在一年内完成，则修改合同不需要签署书面文件。在这里，没有迹象表明施工必然需要一年以上的时间（选项 B）。合同修改需要新的考虑——即各方必须以某种方式改变他/她的职责。但承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务并不构成对价。在这里，双方同意任何“额外”都需要书面修改——即，没有预先存在的提供额外服务的义务。因此，当事人的口头修改得到了考虑的支持（选择C）。\n\n教育目标：在合同纠纷中，(1) 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）可以排除与书面合同相矛盾的证据，(2) 不存在明确条件可以免除履行义务，(3) 欺诈法可以使不成文或未签署的合同无效，(4) 既存义务规则可以使合同因缺乏对价而无法执行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 213（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说综合的书面内容取代了先前协议中不一致的条款）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果先决条件不发生，则当该条件不再发生时，履行义务就解除）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 125（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（与土地转让有关的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 73（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说履行先前存在的法律义务不是对价）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在合同纠纷中，（1）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）可以排除与书面合同相矛盾的证据，（2）未明确条件可以免除履行义务，（3）防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）可以使不成文或未签署的合同无效，以及（4）预先存在的义务规则可以使合同因缺乏对价而无法执行。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果承包商以“额外费用”为由起诉业主，以下哪一项（如果有的话）将有效支持土地所有者的辩护？","rule":"在合同纠纷中，（1）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）可以排除与书面合同相矛盾的证据，（2）未明确条件可以免除履行义务，（3）防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）可以使不成文或未签署的合同无效，以及（4）预先存在的义务规则可以使合同因缺乏对价而无法执行。","analysis":"口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常防止书面合同的一方提供与书面合同条款相矛盾的先前或同期协议的证据。但该规则并不排除当事人之间后续协议的证据（例如，当事人在合同订立后发生的有关“额外费用”的口头修改）（选项A和B）。明示条件是明确的条款，规定不确定的未来事件将（1）产生履行义务（即先决条件）或（2）消除履行义务（即后续条件）。此外，如果先决条件未能发生，则一方的履行义务可以免除。在这里，土地所有者支付额外费用的义务需要双方签署的书面修改，而不是发生不确定的未来事件。因此，未能做出这样的写作并不是明确条件的失败（选项A和C）。根据欺诈法，某些类型的合同修改必须采用书面形式，并由寻求执行的一方签署。但是，如果修改后的合同能够在一年内完成，则修改合同不需要签署书面文件。在这里，没有迹象表明施工必然需要一年以上的时间（选项 B）。合同修改需要新的考虑——即各方必须以某种方式改变他/她的职责。但承诺履行或履行先前存在的义务并不构成对价。在这里，双方同意任何“额外”都需要书面修改——即，没有预先存在的提供额外服务的义务。因此，当事人的口头修改得到了考虑的支持（选择C）。\n\n教育目标：在合同纠纷中，(1) 口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）可以排除与书面合同相矛盾的证据，(2) 不存在明确条件可以免除履行义务，(3) 欺诈法可以使不成文或未签署的合同无效，(4) 既存义务规则可以使合同因缺乏对价而无法执行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 213（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说综合的书面内容取代了先前协议中不一致的条款）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，如果先决条件不发生，则当该条件不再发生时，履行义务就解除）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 125（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（与土地转让有关的防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds））。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 73（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说履行先前存在的法律义务不是对价）。","conclusion":"答案 D：口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）、既存义务规则、明示条件失败或防欺诈法（Statute of Frauds）均不适用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"express condition","ipa":"/ɪkspɹˈɛs kʌndˈɪʃən/","syllables":"ex·press con·di·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"明示条件：以明确条件语言约定、通常要求严格满足","contextualMeaning":"明示条件：以明确条件语言约定、通常要求严格满足","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The parol evidence rule and the failure of an express condition.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":176,"sourceQuestionPage":521,"sourceQuestionPages":[521],"sourceExplanationPages":[522,523],"sourceAnswerPages":[522,523],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-177","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A seller and a farmer formed a written, signed contract for the sale of a 500-acre tract of farmland. The contract provided for exchange of the deed for $500,000 in cash on January 15. On January 15, the seller notified the farmer that because the tenant currently residing on the farmland wrongfully refused to quit the premises, the seller could not deliver possession until January 30. On January 30, the farmer accepted the conveyance, paid the $500,000, and took possession of the farmland while reserving his rights to any damages caused by the seller's breach. The farmer had purchased 500 head of cattle to be delivered to the farmland on January 15. Because he did not have possession of the farmland on that date, he had to rent another pasture for $2,000 to graze the cattle for 15 days. The seller had no reason to know the farmer purchased cattle or planned to graze them on the farmland. Throughout January, the farmland's market value was $510,000 and its fair monthly rental value was $5,000. In an action by the farmer against the seller for damages, what is the farmer is entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"$2,500 only (the fair rental value of the farmland for 15 days).","B":"$2,500 (the fair rental value of the farmland for 15 days), plus $2,000 (the cost of grazing the cattle elsewhere for 15 days).","C":"Nominal damages only, because the market value of the land exceeded the contract price.","D":"Nothing, because by paying the purchase price on January 30, he waived whatever cause of action he may have had."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Alternative bases for recovery when loss in value uncertain\n\nDelayed use of property Fair rental value of property during period of delay OR\n\nInterest on value of property made unproductive by breach Defective or unfinished Diminution in market price of property caused by construction breach OR\n\nReasonable cost of completing performance or remedying defects A nonbreaching party's recovery is typically based on the loss in value to that party caused by the breach. But if the loss in value cannot be proved with reasonable certainty and there is a reasonable alternative to loss in value, the nonbreaching party may claim damages based on that alternative. For example, if a breach delays the use of property, the nonbreaching party may recover damages based on: the fair rental value of the property during the period of the delay or interest on the value of the property that has been made unproductive by the breach. Here, the seller breached the land-sale contract by failing to deliver possession of the farmland on January 15 as agreed. On January 30, the farmer accepted the conveyance, paid the purchase price, and took possession of the farmland while reserving his right to sue for any damages caused by the seller's breach (Choice D). As a result, the farmer is entitled to recover $2,500—the fair rental value of the farmland for the 15-day period during which he was denied possession. However, the farmer is not entitled to recover $2,000 for the cost of grazing the cattle elsewhere for 15 days. The reason is that consequential damages arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances are only recoverable if the breaching party (1) knew about the special circumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of those circumstances, that would result from the breach. Here, the seller had no reason to know that the farmer had purchased cattle or planned to graze them on the farmland (Choice B). (Choice C) The fact that the market value of the farmland ($510,000) exceeded the contract price ($500,000) does not limit the farmer's recovery to nominal damages only.\n\nEducational objective: If a breach delays the use of property, the nonbreaching party may recover damages based on (1) the fair rental value of the property during the delay period or (2) interest on the value of the property made unproductive by the breach.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 348 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (alternatives to loss in value of performance).","educationalObjective":"If a breach delays the use of property, the nonbreaching party may recover damages based on (1) the fair rental value of the property during the delay period or (2) interest on the value of the property made unproductive by the breach.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 348 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (alternatives to loss in value of performance).","rule":"If a breach delays the use of property, the nonbreaching party may recover damages based on (1) the fair rental value of the property during the delay period or (2) interest on the value of the property made unproductive by the breach.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the farmer against the seller for damages, what is the farmer is entitled to recover?","rule":"If a breach delays the use of property, the nonbreaching party may recover damages based on (1) the fair rental value of the property during the delay period or (2) interest on the value of the property made unproductive by the breach.","analysis":"Alternative bases for recovery when loss in value uncertain\n\nDelayed use of property Fair rental value of property during period of delay OR\n\nInterest on value of property made unproductive by breach Defective or unfinished Diminution in market price of property caused by construction breach OR\n\nReasonable cost of completing performance or remedying defects A nonbreaching party's recovery is typically based on the loss in value to that party caused by the breach. But if the loss in value cannot be proved with reasonable certainty and there is a reasonable alternative to loss in value, the nonbreaching party may claim damages based on that alternative. For example, if a breach delays the use of property, the nonbreaching party may recover damages based on: the fair rental value of the property during the period of the delay or interest on the value of the property that has been made unproductive by the breach. Here, the seller breached the land-sale contract by failing to deliver possession of the farmland on January 15 as agreed. On January 30, the farmer accepted the conveyance, paid the purchase price, and took possession of the farmland while reserving his right to sue for any damages caused by the seller's breach (Choice D). As a result, the farmer is entitled to recover $2,500—the fair rental value of the farmland for the 15-day period during which he was denied possession. However, the farmer is not entitled to recover $2,000 for the cost of grazing the cattle elsewhere for 15 days. The reason is that consequential damages arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances are only recoverable if the breaching party (1) knew about the special circumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of those circumstances, that would result from the breach. Here, the seller had no reason to know that the farmer had purchased cattle or planned to graze them on the farmland (Choice B). (Choice C) The fact that the market value of the farmland ($510,000) exceeded the contract price ($500,000) does not limit the farmer's recovery to nominal damages only.","conclusion":"Answer A: $2,500 only (the fair rental value of the farmland for 15 days)."},"questionZh":"一名卖家和一名农民签订了一份书面的、签署的合同，出售一块 500 英亩的农田。合同规定于 1 月 15 日将契据换成 50 万美元现金。1 月 15 日，卖方通知农民，由于目前居住在该农田上的租户错误地拒绝搬出该房屋，卖方要到 1 月 30 日才能交付所有权。1 月 30 日，农民接受了转让书，支付了 50 万美元，并占有了该农田，同时保留了对因该农田所造成的任何损害的权利。卖方的违约行为。该农民于1月15日购买了500头牛运往农田，由于当天他还没有土地所有权，因此他不得不以2000美元租用另一个牧场来放牧15天。卖方没有理由知道农民购买了牛或计划在农田上放牧。整个 1 月份，农田的市场价值为 510,000 美元，每月公平租金为 5,000 美元。在农民向卖方提起损害赔偿诉讼时，农民有权获得什么赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"仅 2,500 美元（农田 15 天的公平租金价值）。","B":"2,500 美元（农田 15 天的公平租金价值），加上 2,000 美元（在其他地方放牧 15 天的费用）。","C":"仅名义损失，因为土地的市场价值超过了合同价格。","D":"没什么，因为通过在 1 月 30 日支付购买价格，他放弃了他可能拥有的任何诉讼理由。"},"explanationZh":"当价值损失不确定时恢复的替代依据\n\n延迟使用财产 延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或\n\n因违约而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息 有缺陷或未完工 因施工违约导致财产市场价格下降或\n\n完成履约或补救缺陷的合理成本 非违约方的赔偿通常基于该方因违约而造成的价值损失。但如果无法以合理的确定性证明价值损失，并且存在替代价值损失的合理替代方案，守约方可以根据该替代方案要求损害赔偿。例如，如果违约行为延迟了财产的使用，守约方可以根据以下内容获得损害赔偿：延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或因违约而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息。本案中，出卖人未按约定于1月15日交付耕地所有权，违反了土地出让合同。 1月30日，农民接受了转让书，支付了购买价款，并取得了农田的所有权，同时保留对卖方违约造成的任何损失提起诉讼的权利（选项D）。因此，该农民有权收回 2,500 美元，即他被拒绝拥有农田的 15 天内农田的公平租金价值。然而，该农民无权就在其他地方放牧 15 天的费用获得 2,000 美元的补偿。原因是，只有在违约方（1）了解特殊情况或（2）能够根据这些情况合理预见违约将造成的损害的情况下，因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的间接损害才能获得赔偿。在这里，卖方没有理由知道农民购买了牛或计划在农田上放牧（选项B）。 （选项C） 农田的市场价值（510,000 美元）超过合同价格（500,000 美元）的事实并不将农民的赔偿仅限于名义损失。\n\n教育目的：如果违约行为延迟了财产的使用，非违约方可以根据 (1) 延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或 (2) 因违约行为而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息来获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 348（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履约价值损失的替代方案）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果违约行为延迟了财产的使用，守约方可以根据 (1) 延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或 (2) 因违约行为而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息来获得损害赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在农民向卖方提起损害赔偿诉讼时，农民有权获得什么赔偿？","rule":"如果违约行为延迟了财产的使用，守约方可以根据 (1) 延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或 (2) 因违约行为而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息来获得损害赔偿。","analysis":"当价值损失不确定时恢复的替代依据\n\n延迟使用财产 延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或\n\n因违约而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息 有缺陷或未完工 因施工违约导致财产市场价格下降或\n\n完成履约或补救缺陷的合理成本 非违约方的赔偿通常基于该方因违约而造成的价值损失。但如果无法以合理的确定性证明价值损失，并且存在替代价值损失的合理替代方案，守约方可以根据该替代方案要求损害赔偿。例如，如果违约行为延迟了财产的使用，守约方可以根据以下内容获得损害赔偿：延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或因违约而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息。本案中，出卖人未按约定于1月15日交付耕地所有权，违反了土地出让合同。 1月30日，农民接受了转让书，支付了购买价款，并取得了农田的所有权，同时保留对卖方违约造成的任何损失提起诉讼的权利（选项D）。因此，该农民有权收回 2,500 美元，即他被拒绝拥有农田的 15 天内农田的公平租金价值。然而，该农民无权就在其他地方放牧 15 天的费用获得 2,000 美元的补偿。原因是，只有在违约方（1）了解特殊情况或（2）能够根据这些情况合理预见违约将造成的损害的情况下，因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的间接损害才能获得赔偿。在这里，卖方没有理由知道农民购买了牛或计划在农田上放牧（选项B）。 （选项C） 农田的市场价值（510,000 美元）超过合同价格（500,000 美元）的事实并不将农民的赔偿仅限于名义损失。\n\n教育目的：如果违约行为延迟了财产的使用，非违约方可以根据 (1) 延迟期间财产的公平租金价值或 (2) 因违约行为而丧失生产性的财产价值的利息来获得损害赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 348（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履约价值损失的替代方案）。","conclusion":"答案 A：仅 2,500 美元（农田 15 天的公平租金价值）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":177,"sourceQuestionPage":524,"sourceQuestionPages":[524],"sourceExplanationPages":[525,526],"sourceAnswerPages":[525,526],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-178","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A fugitive was wanted for murder. The authorities offered the following reward: \"$20,000 to anyone who provides information leading to the arrest and conviction of this fugitive.\" A private detective knew of the reward, located the fugitive, and brought him to the authorities, who arrested him. The authorities then determined that although the fugitive had, in fact, committed the crime, he had been directed to do so by his boss. The authorities and the fugitive then agreed that in exchange for the fugitive's testimony against his boss, all charges against the fugitive would be dropped. The fugitive testified and was released. The authorities refused to pay the reward to the private detective on the ground that the fugitive was never convicted. Would the private detective be likely to prevail in a breach of contract action against the authorities?","choices":{"A":"No, because the express conditions set out in the reward offer were not met.","B":"No, because the private detective failed to notify the authorities that he had accepted the reward offer.","C":"Yes, because the authorities' agreement with the fugitive was against public policy.","D":"Yes, because the authorities themselves prevented the conviction of the fugitive."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA party may generally avoid performance until a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both party's performance becomes due—has occurred. The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused, however, if the party protected by the condition wrongfully prevents or interferes with its occurrence (eg, through a breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing). Here, the authorities' duty to pay the reward was subject to two conditions precedent—the arrest and conviction of the fugitive. The implied duty of good faith and fair dealing imposed a duty on the authorities to refrain from hindering either condition's occurrence. However, they breached that duty by agreeing to drop all charges against the fugitive despite their determination that he had committed the crime, thus excusing the express condition of conviction that was not met (Choice A). Therefore, the detective's suit will likely prevail. (Choice B) In a unilateral contract, an offeree who accepts by completing the requested performance must give notice if he/she has reason to know that the completed performance will not come to the offeror's attention within a reasonable time. Here, there was no need for the detective to notify the authorities that he had accepted their reward offer because they learned of his performance when he brought the fugitive to them. (Choice C) Whether the authorities' agreement with the fugitive was against public policy goes to the enforceability of the agreement between the fugitive and the authorities; it has no bearing on the outcome of this dispute.\n\nEducational objective: The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party protected by the condition wrongfully prevents or interferes with its occurrence (eg, through a breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of non-occurrence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 245 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a party's wrongful interference excuses a condition).","educationalObjective":"The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party protected by the condition wrongfully prevents or interferes with its occurrence (eg, through a breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of non-occurrence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 245 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (when a party's wrongful interference excuses a condition).","rule":"The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party protected by the condition wrongfully prevents or interferes with its occurrence (eg, through a breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing).","irac":{"issue":"Would the private detective be likely to prevail in a breach of contract action against the authorities?","rule":"The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party protected by the condition wrongfully prevents or interferes with its occurrence (eg, through a breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing).","analysis":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA party may generally avoid performance until a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both party's performance becomes due—has occurred. The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused, however, if the party protected by the condition wrongfully prevents or interferes with its occurrence (eg, through a breach of the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing). Here, the authorities' duty to pay the reward was subject to two conditions precedent—the arrest and conviction of the fugitive. The implied duty of good faith and fair dealing imposed a duty on the authorities to refrain from hindering either condition's occurrence. However, they breached that duty by agreeing to drop all charges against the fugitive despite their determination that he had committed the crime, thus excusing the express condition of conviction that was not met (Choice A). Therefore, the detective's suit will likely prevail. (Choice B) In a unilateral contract, an offeree who accepts by completing the requested performance must give notice if he/she has reason to know that the completed performance will not come to the offeror's attention within a reasonable time. Here, there was no need for the detective to notify the authorities that he had accepted their reward offer because they learned of his performance when he brought the fugitive to them. (Choice C) Whether the authorities' agreement with the fugitive was against public policy goes to the enforceability of the agreement between the fugitive and the authorities; it has no bearing on the outcome of this dispute.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the authorities themselves prevented the conviction of the fugitive."},"questionZh":"一名逃犯因谋杀罪被通缉。当局悬赏如下：“任何提供有助于逮捕并定罪该逃犯信息的人，将获得 20,000 美元的奖励。”一名私家侦探知道悬赏后，找到了逃犯，并将他带到当局，当局逮捕了他。当局随后认定，虽然逃犯确实犯了罪，但他是受其老板指使这样做的。当局和逃犯随后同意，作为逃犯对老板的证词的交换条件，对逃犯的所有指控都将被撤销。逃犯作证并被释放。当局以逃犯从未被定罪为由，拒绝向私家侦探支付悬赏。私家侦探在针对当局的违约诉讼中是否可能胜诉？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为奖励优惠中规定的明确条件未得到满足。","B":"不，因为私家侦探没有通知当局他已接受悬赏。","C":"是的，因为当局与逃犯的协议违反了公共政策。","D":"是的，因为当局自己阻止了对逃犯的定罪。"},"explanationZh":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n一方通常可以避免履行，直到先决条件（即，在任何一方或双方的履行到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）发生。然而，如果受条件保护的一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则条件的不发生可以被原谅。在这里，当局支付悬赏金的义务取决于两个先决条件——逮捕逃犯并对其定罪。善意和公平交易的隐含义务要求当局有责任避免阻碍任何一种情况的发生。然而，他们违反了这一义务，尽管他们认定逃犯犯有犯罪行为，但仍同意撤销对逃犯的所有指控，从而为未满足的明确定罪条件开脱（选择 A）。因此，侦探的诉讼很可能会胜诉。 （选择B）在单方合同中，通过完成所要求的履行而接受的受要约人，如果他/她有理由知道已完成的履行不会在合理时间内引起要约人的注意，则必须发出通知。在这里，侦探没有必要通知当局他已经接受了悬赏，因为当他将逃犯交给他们时，他们就知道了他的表现。 （选项C）当局与逃犯的协议是否违反公共政策取决于逃犯与当局之间协议的可执行性；这对这场争端的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：如果受条件保护的一方错误地阻止或干扰某条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 245 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方的不当干预成为某种条件的借口时）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果受条件保护的一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则可以免除条件的不发生。","iracZh":{"issue":"私家侦探在针对当局的违约诉讼中是否可能胜诉？","rule":"如果受条件保护的一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则可以免除条件的不发生。","analysis":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n一方通常可以避免履行，直到先决条件（即，在任何一方或双方的履行到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）发生。然而，如果受条件保护的一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则条件的不发生可以被原谅。在这里，当局支付悬赏金的义务取决于两个先决条件——逮捕逃犯并对其定罪。善意和公平交易的隐含义务要求当局有责任避免阻碍任何一种情况的发生。然而，他们违反了这一义务，尽管他们认定逃犯犯有犯罪行为，但仍同意撤销对逃犯的所有指控，从而为未满足的明确定罪条件开脱（选择 A）。因此，侦探的诉讼很可能会胜诉。 （选择B）在单方合同中，通过完成所要求的履行而接受的受要约人，如果他/她有理由知道已完成的履行不会在合理时间内引起要约人的注意，则必须发出通知。在这里，侦探没有必要通知当局他已经接受了悬赏，因为当他将逃犯交给他们时，他们就知道了他的表现。 （选项C）当局与逃犯的协议是否违反公共政策取决于逃犯与当局之间协议的可执行性；这对这场争端的结果没有影响。\n\n教育目标：如果受条件保护的一方错误地阻止或干扰某条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 245 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（当一方的不当干预成为某种条件的借口时）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为当局自己阻止了对逃犯的定罪。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":178,"sourceQuestionPage":527,"sourceQuestionPages":[527],"sourceExplanationPages":[528,529],"sourceAnswerPages":[528,529],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-179","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On May 1, a seller and a buyer entered into a written contract, signed by both parties, for the sale of a tract of land for $100,000. Delivery of the deed and payment of the purchase price were scheduled for July 1. On June 1, the buyer received a letter from the seller repudiating the contract. On June 5, the buyer bought a second tract of land at a higher price as a substitute for the first tract. On June 10, the seller communicated a retraction of the repudiation to the buyer. The buyer did not tender the purchase price for the first tract on July 1 but subsequently sued the seller for breach of contract. Will the buyer likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer's tender of the purchase price on July 1 was a constructive condition to the seller's duty to tender a conveyance.","B":"No, because the seller retracted the repudiation prior to the agreed time for performance.","C":"Yes, because the buyer bought the second tract as a substitute for the first tract prior to the seller's retraction.","D":"Yes, because the seller's repudiation was not retractable after it was communicated to the buyer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A party's failure to perform his/her contractual duties at the time performance is due constitutes a breach. A breach can also occur before the time for performance is due under the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation. This doctrine applies when a party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise. A repudiation can be retracted if the nonrepudiating party receives notice of the retraction before: canceling the contract materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation or indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final (Choice D). But until the repudiation is retracted, the nonrepudiating party may (1) treat the repudiation as a breach or (2) ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. The nonrepudiating party may also suspend its own performance if commercially reasonable. Here, the seller anticipatorily repudiated the contract on June 1. The seller then attempted to retract the repudiation on June 10. However, the buyer had already materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation by purchasing a substitute tract of land on June 5 (Choice B). As a result, the seller's retraction was ineffective. And since the seller's unretracted repudiation is a breach of contract, the buyer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) In a contract for real property, the tender of the purchase price and the conveyance of the deed generally must occur simultaneously. However, the seller's repudiation allowed the buyer to suspend payment.\n\nEducational objective: A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 256 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (retraction of repudiation).","educationalObjective":"A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 256 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (retraction of repudiation).","rule":"A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final.","irac":{"issue":"Will the buyer likely prevail?","rule":"A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final.","analysis":"A party's failure to perform his/her contractual duties at the time performance is due constitutes a breach. A breach can also occur before the time for performance is due under the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation. This doctrine applies when a party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise. A repudiation can be retracted if the nonrepudiating party receives notice of the retraction before: canceling the contract materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation or indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final (Choice D). But until the repudiation is retracted, the nonrepudiating party may (1) treat the repudiation as a breach or (2) ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. The nonrepudiating party may also suspend its own performance if commercially reasonable. Here, the seller anticipatorily repudiated the contract on June 1. The seller then attempted to retract the repudiation on June 10. However, the buyer had already materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation by purchasing a substitute tract of land on June 5 (Choice B). As a result, the seller's retraction was ineffective. And since the seller's unretracted repudiation is a breach of contract, the buyer is likely to prevail. (Choice A) In a contract for real property, the tender of the purchase price and the conveyance of the deed generally must occur simultaneously. However, the seller's repudiation allowed the buyer to suspend payment.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the buyer bought the second tract as a substitute for the first tract prior to the seller's retraction."},"questionZh":"5月1日，卖方和买方签订了一份双方签署的书面合同，以10万美元的价格出售一块土地。交付契据和支付货款的日期定于 7 月 1 日。6 月 1 日，买方收到卖方拒绝履行合同的信函。 6月5日，买家以更高的价格购买了第二块土地，作为第一块土地的替代品。 6月10日，卖方向买方发出撤回拒付通知。 7 月 1 日，买方没有支付第一块土地的购买价格，但随后起诉卖方违约。买家可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为买方于 7 月 1 日投标购买价格是卖方投标转让义务的推定条件。","B":"不可以，因为卖方在约定的履约时间之前撤回了拒付。","C":"是的，因为在卖方撤回之前，买方购买了第二张小册子作为第一张小册子的替代品。","D":"是的，因为卖方的拒付在传达给买方后就不可撤销。"},"explanationZh":"一方未能在到期时履行合同义务即构成违约。根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，违约也可能发生在履行期限到期之前。当一方明确且毫不含糊地拒绝（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，该原则适用。如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤回通知，则可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：取消合同，实质性改变依赖否认的立场或表明他/她认为否认是最终的（选择D）。但在否认撤销之前，非毁约方可以 (1) 将否认视为违约，或 (2) 忽略它并要求根据合同履行。如果商业上合理，非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行。在此，卖方预期于 6 月 1 日毁约。随后，卖方于 6 月 10 日试图撤销毁约。然而，买方已经在 6 月 5 日购买了一块替代土地，从而实质上改变了依赖毁约的立场（选择 B）。结果，卖家的撤回并没有起到作用。由于卖方未撤回的拒付属于违约行为，因此买方很可能胜诉。 （选择 A） 在不动产合同中，购买价格的投标和契约的转让通常必须同时进行。然而，卖方的拒付允许买方暂停付款。\n\n教育目标：预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，如果非毁约方在 (1) 取消合同、(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场，或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的之前收到撤回通知。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 256（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（撤销否认）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤销通知，则预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：(1) 取消合同；(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场；或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的。","iracZh":{"issue":"买家可能会获胜吗？","rule":"如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤销通知，则预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：(1) 取消合同；(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场；或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的。","analysis":"一方未能在到期时履行合同义务即构成违约。根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，违约也可能发生在履行期限到期之前。当一方明确且毫不含糊地拒绝（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，该原则适用。如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤回通知，则可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：取消合同，实质性改变依赖否认的立场或表明他/她认为否认是最终的（选择D）。但在否认撤销之前，非毁约方可以 (1) 将否认视为违约，或 (2) 忽略它并要求根据合同履行。如果商业上合理，非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行。在此，卖方预期于 6 月 1 日毁约。随后，卖方于 6 月 10 日试图撤销毁约。然而，买方已经在 6 月 5 日购买了一块替代土地，从而实质上改变了依赖毁约的立场（选择 B）。结果，卖家的撤回并没有起到作用。由于卖方未撤回的拒付属于违约行为，因此买方很可能胜诉。 （选择 A） 在不动产合同中，购买价格的投标和契约的转让通常必须同时进行。然而，卖方的拒付允许买方暂停付款。\n\n教育目标：预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，如果非毁约方在 (1) 取消合同、(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场，或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的之前收到撤回通知。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 256（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（撤销否认）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为在卖方撤回之前，买方购买了第二张小册子作为第一张小册子的替代品。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6092dc1e72c5f076"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":179,"sourceQuestionPage":530,"sourceQuestionPages":[530],"sourceExplanationPages":[531,532],"sourceAnswerPages":[531,532],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-180","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A painter contracted with an apartment manager to paint three identical apartment units for $1,000 each. The contract provided for the manager's payment of $3,000 upon the painter's completion of the work on all three units. The painter did not ask for any payment when the first unit was completely painted, but she demanded $2,000 after painting the second unit. Assume that the manager rightfully refused the painter's demand for payment. If the painter immediately terminates the contract without painting the third unit, what is the painter entitled to recover from the manager?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because payment was expressly conditioned on completion of all three units.","B":"The amount equal to the increase in the combined value of the two painted units resulting from the painter's work.","C":"The painter's expenditures plus anticipated \"profit\" in painting the first two units, up to a maximum recovery of $2,000.","D":"The reasonable value of the painter's services in painting the two units, less the manager's damages, if any, for the painter's failure to paint the third unit."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Restitutionary damages\n\nDefinition Damages awarded to restore to claimant the value of whatever benefit was conferred upon recipient Purpose Prevent unjust enrichment\n\nMeasure Reasonable value of work performed or services rendered (ie, cost of recipient obtaining comparable performance) If applicable, extent to which recipient's property has increased in value or recipient's other interests have been advanced In the event of breach, the breaching party can recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of the work performed before the breach, less any damages suffered by the nonbreaching party. Restitutionary damages ensure that the nonbreaching party is not unjustly enriched by the work performed by the breaching party prior to the breach. Here, the painter breached the contract by immediately terminating the contract after the apartment manager rightfully refused the painter's early payment demand. As the breaching party, the painter is entitled to the reasonable value of her services in painting the two units, less the manager's damages, if any, for her failure to paint the third unit (eg, the increased cost of another painter) (Choices A & B). (Choice C) A nonbreaching party who has partially performed can generally recover for the costs of the work performed plus expectation damages (ie, anticipated profits). But here the painter is the breaching party and so is not entitled to recover this amount.\n\nEducational objective: The breaching party can recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of the work performed before the breach, less any damages suffered by the nonbreaching party.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 371 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of restitution interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 374 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (restitution in favor of breaching party).","educationalObjective":"The breaching party can recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of the work performed before the breach, less any damages suffered by the nonbreaching party.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 371 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of restitution interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 374 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (restitution in favor of breaching party).","rule":"The breaching party can recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of the work performed before the breach, less any damages suffered by the nonbreaching party.","irac":{"issue":"If the painter immediately terminates the contract without painting the third unit, what is the painter entitled to recover from the manager?","rule":"The breaching party can recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of the work performed before the breach, less any damages suffered by the nonbreaching party.","analysis":"Restitutionary damages\n\nDefinition Damages awarded to restore to claimant the value of whatever benefit was conferred upon recipient Purpose Prevent unjust enrichment\n\nMeasure Reasonable value of work performed or services rendered (ie, cost of recipient obtaining comparable performance) If applicable, extent to which recipient's property has increased in value or recipient's other interests have been advanced In the event of breach, the breaching party can recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of the work performed before the breach, less any damages suffered by the nonbreaching party. Restitutionary damages ensure that the nonbreaching party is not unjustly enriched by the work performed by the breaching party prior to the breach. Here, the painter breached the contract by immediately terminating the contract after the apartment manager rightfully refused the painter's early payment demand. As the breaching party, the painter is entitled to the reasonable value of her services in painting the two units, less the manager's damages, if any, for her failure to paint the third unit (eg, the increased cost of another painter) (Choices A & B). (Choice C) A nonbreaching party who has partially performed can generally recover for the costs of the work performed plus expectation damages (ie, anticipated profits). But here the painter is the breaching party and so is not entitled to recover this amount.","conclusion":"Answer D: The reasonable value of the painter's services in painting the two units, less the manager's damages, if any, for the painter's failure to paint the third unit."},"questionZh":"一名油漆工与一名公寓经理签订合同，以每套 1,000 美元的价格粉刷三套相同的公寓。合同规定，油漆工完成所有三个单元的工作后，经理将支付 3,000 美元。当第一个单元全部粉刷完毕时，油漆工没有要求任何付款，但在粉刷第二个单元后，她要求支付2000美元。假设经理正确地拒绝了油漆工的付款要求。如果油漆工立即终止合同而没有油漆第三个单位，油漆工有权向经理追偿什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"没什么，因为付款明确以完成所有三个单元为条件。","B":"该金额等于画家工作所产生的两个绘画单位的总价值的增加。","C":"油漆工的支出加上油漆前两个单元的预期“利润”，最多可收回 2,000 美元。","D":"油漆工在对两个单元进行油漆时的服务的合理价值，减去经理因油漆工未能对第三个单元进行油漆而造成的损失（如果有的话）。"},"explanationZh":"赔偿损失\n\n定义 为恢复索赔人所获得的任何利益的价值而授予的损害赔偿 目的 防止不当得利\n\n衡量所完成的工作或所提供的服务的合理价值（即接收者获得可比履行的成本） 如果适用，接收者的财产增值或接收者的其他利益已增加的程度 如果发生违约，违约方可以根据违约前所执行工作的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿，减去非违约方遭受的任何损害。返还利益赔偿确保非违约方不会因违约方在违约前所做的工作而获得不公正的利益。本案中，在公寓经理合理拒绝了油漆工提前付款的要求后，油漆工立即终止了合同，从而违反了合同。作为违约方，油漆工有权获得其在对两个单元进行油漆时提供的服务的合理价值，减去经理因未能对第三个单元进行油漆而造成的损失（如果有的话）（例如，另一名油漆工增加的成本）（选择 A 和 B）。 （选择C） 部分履行的非违约方通常可以收回已完成工作的成本加上预期损害（即预期利润）。但这里画家是违约方，因此无权追回这笔款项。\n\n教育目标：违约方可以根据违约前所完成工作的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿，减去非违约方遭受的任何损害。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 371（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（归还利息的衡量标准）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 374（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有利于违约方的赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"违约方可以根据违约前所完成工作的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿，减去非违约方遭受的任何损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果油漆工立即终止合同而没有油漆第三个单位，油漆工有权向经理追偿什么？","rule":"违约方可以根据违约前所完成工作的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿，减去非违约方遭受的任何损害。","analysis":"赔偿损失\n\n定义 为恢复索赔人所获得的任何利益的价值而授予的损害赔偿 目的 防止不当得利\n\n衡量所完成的工作或所提供的服务的合理价值（即接收者获得可比履行的成本） 如果适用，接收者的财产增值或接收者的其他利益已增加的程度 如果发生违约，违约方可以根据违约前所执行工作的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿，减去非违约方遭受的任何损害。返还利益赔偿确保非违约方不会因违约方在违约前所做的工作而获得不公正的利益。本案中，在公寓经理合理拒绝了油漆工提前付款的要求后，油漆工立即终止了合同，从而违反了合同。作为违约方，油漆工有权获得其在对两个单元进行油漆时提供的服务的合理价值，减去经理因未能对第三个单元进行油漆而造成的损失（如果有的话）（例如，另一名油漆工增加的成本）（选择 A 和 B）。 （选择C） 部分履行的非违约方通常可以收回已完成工作的成本加上预期损害（即预期利润）。但这里画家是违约方，因此无权追回这笔款项。\n\n教育目标：违约方可以根据违约前所完成工作的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿，减去非违约方遭受的任何损害。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 371（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（归还利息的衡量标准）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 374（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有利于违约方的赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 D：油漆工在对两个单元进行油漆时的服务的合理价值，减去经理因油漆工未能对第三个单元进行油漆而造成的损失（如果有的话）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":180,"sourceQuestionPage":533,"sourceQuestionPages":[533],"sourceExplanationPages":[534],"sourceAnswerPages":[534],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-181","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Impossibility, Impracticability & Frustration","question":"A producer contracted to pay an inexperienced performer a specified salary to act in a small role in a play the producer was taking on a six-week road tour. The contract was for the duration of the tour. On the third day of the tour, the performer was hospitalized with a stomach disorder. The producer replaced her in the cast with an experienced actor. One week later, the performer recovered, but the producer refused to allow her to resume her original role for the remainder of the tour. In an action by the performer against the producer for breach of contract, which of the following, if proved, would be the producer's best defense?","choices":{"A":"Both the producer and the performer knew that a year earlier the performer had been incapacitated for a short period of time by the same kind of stomach disorder.","B":"The actor, by general acclaim, was much better in the role than the performer had been.","C":"The actor was the only replacement the producer could find, and the actor would accept nothing less than a contract for the remainder of the six-week tour.","D":"The producer offered to employ the performer as the actor's understudy for the remainder of the six-week tour at the performer's original salary, but the performer declined."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA party's duty to perform under a contract can be dismissed by impracticability. The defense of impracticability is available when: an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for the party to perform the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and the party seeking discharge was not at fault. However, temporary impracticability merely suspends the party's duty to perform until the circumstances giving rise to the impracticability cease to exist—with one exception. If the delay makes performance materially more burdensome, the party's duty to perform is dismissed. Here, the performer's illness suspended the producer's duty to employ the performer during the period that her illness prevented her from acting. The producer then replaced her with an experienced actor. If the actor was the only replacement the producer could find and would not take a contract for less than the remainder of the six-week tour, the producer's obligation to the performer would be dismissed. The reason is that it would be materially more burdensome for the producer to employ them both. Therefore, if proved, that is the producer's best defense. (Choice A) If both the producer and the performer knew that a year earlier the performer had been incapacitated by the same kind of stomach disorder, the performer's illness would have been foreseeable. As a result, this fact would undermine the producer's impracticability defense. (Choice B) The relative quality of the actor's performance is not a circumstance that would suspend or discharge the producer's contractual duty to the performer. (Choice D) The producer's action in offering the performer a job as an understudy is irrelevant to the critical issue of whether the producer had the right to cancel the contract.\n\nEducational objective: A period of temporary impracticability suspends a party's duty to perform until the circumstances giving rise to the impracticability pass. But if the delay caused by the impracticability makes performance materially more burdensome, then the party's duty to perform is discharged.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discharge by supervening impracticability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 269 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (temporary impracticability).","educationalObjective":"A period of temporary impracticability suspends a party's duty to perform until the circumstances giving rise to the impracticability pass. But if the delay caused by the impracticability makes performance materially more burdensome, then the party's duty to perform is discharged.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discharge by supervening impracticability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 269 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (temporary impracticability).","rule":"A period of temporary impracticability suspends a party's duty to perform until the circumstances giving rise to the impracticability pass. But if the delay caused by the impracticability makes performance materially more burdensome, then the party's duty to perform is discharged.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the performer against the producer for breach of contract, which of the following, if proved, would be the producer's best defense?","rule":"A period of temporary impracticability suspends a party's duty to perform until the circumstances giving rise to the impracticability pass. But if the delay caused by the impracticability makes performance materially more burdensome, then the party's duty to perform is discharged.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA party's duty to perform under a contract can be dismissed by impracticability. The defense of impracticability is available when: an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for the party to perform the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and the party seeking discharge was not at fault. However, temporary impracticability merely suspends the party's duty to perform until the circumstances giving rise to the impracticability cease to exist—with one exception. If the delay makes performance materially more burdensome, the party's duty to perform is dismissed. Here, the performer's illness suspended the producer's duty to employ the performer during the period that her illness prevented her from acting. The producer then replaced her with an experienced actor. If the actor was the only replacement the producer could find and would not take a contract for less than the remainder of the six-week tour, the producer's obligation to the performer would be dismissed. The reason is that it would be materially more burdensome for the producer to employ them both. Therefore, if proved, that is the producer's best defense. (Choice A) If both the producer and the performer knew that a year earlier the performer had been incapacitated by the same kind of stomach disorder, the performer's illness would have been foreseeable. As a result, this fact would undermine the producer's impracticability defense. (Choice B) The relative quality of the actor's performance is not a circumstance that would suspend or discharge the producer's contractual duty to the performer. (Choice D) The producer's action in offering the performer a job as an understudy is irrelevant to the critical issue of whether the producer had the right to cancel the contract.","conclusion":"Answer C: The actor was the only replacement the producer could find, and the actor would accept nothing less than a contract for the remainder of the six-week tour."},"questionZh":"一位制片人与一位缺乏经验的演员签订了一份合同，支付一定的工资，让他在该剧中扮演一个小角色，并进行为期六周的巡回演出。合同有效期为巡演期间。巡演第三天，表演者因胃病住院。制片人用一位经验丰富的演员取代了她的演员阵容。一周后，表演者康复了，但制片人拒绝让她在巡演剩余时间里恢复原来的角色。在表演者因违反合同而对制片人提起诉讼时，如果得到证实，以下哪项将是制片人的最佳抗辩？","choicesZh":{"A":"制作人和表演者都知道，一年前，表演者曾因同一种胃病而在短时间内丧失行为能力。","B":"受到普遍好评，这位演员在这个角色中比表演者表现得更好。","C":"该演员是制片人能找到的唯一替代者，而该演员只会接受为期六周巡演剩余时间的合同。","D":"制片人提出聘请该表演者作为该演员的替补，完成为期六周的巡回演出，并支付该表演者原来的工资，但该表演者拒绝了。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n一方当事人根据合同履行的义务可以因不切实际而被解除。不可行抗辩适用于以下情况： 意外或异常事件导致当事人无法履行合同，并且是在该事件不会发生且寻求解除合同的一方没有过错的基本假设下成立的。然而，暂时的不可行只是中止当事人的履行义务，直到导致不可行的情况不再存在为止——但有一个例外。如果延迟履行造成实质上的负担，则当事人的履行义务被解除。在这里，表演者的疾病使制片人在表演者因病无法表演期间暂停了雇用该表演者的义务。制片人随后用一位经验丰富的演员取代了她。如果该演员是制片人能找到的唯一替代者，并且不会接受少于六周巡演剩余时间的合同，那么制片人对表演者的义务将被解除。原因是同时使用这两者对生产者来说会造成更大的负担。因此，如果得到证实，那就是制片人最好的辩护。 （选项A） 如果制作人和表演者都知道一年前表演者因同一种胃病而丧失行为能力，那么表演者的病就可以预见。因此，这一事实将削弱生产者的不切实际抗辩。 （选择B） 演员表演的相对质量并不构成暂停或解除制片人对表演者的合同义务的情况。 （选项D）制片人为表演者提供替补工作的行为与制片人是否有权解除合同的关键问题无关。\n\n教育目标：一段暂时不可行的时期中止一方履行义务，直至导致不可行的情况过去。但是，如果由于不可行而造成的延误使履行变得更加繁重，则当事人的履行义务就被解除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（因随之而来的不切实际而解除）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 269 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（暂时不可行）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"暂时不可行的时期会中止一方履行义务，直至导致不可行的情况过去。但是，如果由于不可行而造成的延误使履行变得更加繁重，则当事人的履行义务就被解除。","iracZh":{"issue":"在表演者因违反合同而对制片人提起诉讼时，如果得到证实，以下哪项将是制片人的最佳抗辩？","rule":"暂时不可行的时期会中止一方履行义务，直至导致不可行的情况过去。但是，如果由于不可行而造成的延误使履行变得更加繁重，则当事人的履行义务就被解除。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n一方当事人根据合同履行的义务可以因不切实际而被解除。不可行抗辩适用于以下情况： 意外或异常事件导致当事人无法履行合同，并且是在该事件不会发生且寻求解除合同的一方没有过错的基本假设下成立的。然而，暂时的不可行只是中止当事人的履行义务，直到导致不可行的情况不再存在为止——但有一个例外。如果延迟履行造成实质上的负担，则当事人的履行义务被解除。在这里，表演者的疾病使制片人在表演者因病无法表演期间暂停了雇用该表演者的义务。制片人随后用一位经验丰富的演员取代了她。如果该演员是制片人能找到的唯一替代者，并且不会接受少于六周巡演剩余时间的合同，那么制片人对表演者的义务将被解除。原因是同时使用这两者对生产者来说会造成更大的负担。因此，如果得到证实，那就是制片人最好的辩护。 （选项A） 如果制作人和表演者都知道一年前表演者因同一种胃病而丧失行为能力，那么表演者的病就可以预见。因此，这一事实将削弱生产者的不切实际抗辩。 （选择B） 演员表演的相对质量并不构成暂停或解除制片人对表演者的合同义务的情况。 （选项D）制片人为表演者提供替补工作的行为与制片人是否有权解除合同的关键问题无关。\n\n教育目标：一段暂时不可行的时期中止一方履行义务，直至导致不可行的情况过去。但是，如果由于不可行而造成的延误使履行变得更加繁重，则当事人的履行义务就被解除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（因随之而来的不切实际而解除）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 269 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（暂时不可行）。","conclusion":"答案 C：该演员是制片人能找到的唯一替代者，而该演员只会接受为期六周巡演剩余时间的合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"performer","ipa":"/pɝfˈɔɹmɝ/","syllables":"per·former","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 表演者, 执行者, 完成者；[法] 执行者, 履行者, 实行者","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“performer”","wordForm":"名词；词形 s:performers","sourceSentence":"A producer contracted to pay an inexperienced performer a specified salary to act in a small role in a play the producer was taking on a six-week road tour.","synonyms":["performing artist"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":181,"sourceQuestionPage":535,"sourceQuestionPages":[535],"sourceExplanationPages":[536,537],"sourceAnswerPages":[536,537],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-182","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A homeowner and a planner signed a detailed writing in which the planner, a landscape architect, agreed to landscape and replant the owner's residential property in accordance with a design prepared by the planner and incorporated in the writing. The owner agreed to pay $10,000 for the work upon its completion. At the owner's insistence, the written agreement contained a provision that neither party would be bound unless the owner's law partner, an avid student of landscaping, should approve the planner's design. Before the planner commenced the work, the owner's law partner, in the presence of both the owner and the planner, expressly disapproved the landscaping design. Nevertheless, the owner ordered the planner to proceed with the work, and the planner reluctantly did so. When the planner's performance was 40% complete, the owner repudiated his duty, if any, to pay the contract price or any part thereof. If the planner now sues the owner for damages for breach of contract, which of the following concepts best supports the planner's claim?","choices":{"A":"Irrevocable waiver of condition.","B":"Promissory estoppel.","C":"Substantial performance.","D":"Unjust enrichment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA contracting party may generally avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both parties' performance becomes due—has not occurred. The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused, however, if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. And the waiving party cannot retract the waiver if the other party has detrimentally relied on it. Here, the agreement provided that it would be binding if the owner's law partner approved the planner's landscape design (condition precedent). The law partner disapproved the design (nonoccurrence), but the owner ordered the planner to proceed with the work anyway (waiver). And because the planner detrimentally relied on that waiver by completing 40% of the work, the owner could not retract it (ie, the waiver was irrevocable). Therefore, the concept of irrevocable waiver of condition best supports the planner's breach-of-contract claim. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. But because a contract was formed here, this doctrine does not apply. (Choice C) The planner did not substantially perform under the contract since his performance was only 40% complete. (Choice D) The doctrine of unjust enrichment applies in the absence of a valid contract to prevent one party from receiving a benefit from another that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep.\n\nEducational objective: Nonoccurrence of a condition precedent may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. The waiving party cannot retract the waiver if the other party has detrimentally relied on it.","educationalObjective":"Nonoccurrence of a condition precedent may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. The waiving party cannot retract the waiver if the other party has detrimentally relied on it.","references":"","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition precedent may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. The waiving party cannot retract the waiver if the other party has detrimentally relied on it.","irac":{"issue":"If the planner now sues the owner for damages for breach of contract, which of the following concepts best supports the planner's claim?","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition precedent may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. The waiving party cannot retract the waiver if the other party has detrimentally relied on it.","analysis":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA contracting party may generally avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both parties' performance becomes due—has not occurred. The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused, however, if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. And the waiving party cannot retract the waiver if the other party has detrimentally relied on it. Here, the agreement provided that it would be binding if the owner's law partner approved the planner's landscape design (condition precedent). The law partner disapproved the design (nonoccurrence), but the owner ordered the planner to proceed with the work anyway (waiver). And because the planner detrimentally relied on that waiver by completing 40% of the work, the owner could not retract it (ie, the waiver was irrevocable). Therefore, the concept of irrevocable waiver of condition best supports the planner's breach-of-contract claim. (Choice B) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. But because a contract was formed here, this doctrine does not apply. (Choice C) The planner did not substantially perform under the contract since his performance was only 40% complete. (Choice D) The doctrine of unjust enrichment applies in the absence of a valid contract to prevent one party from receiving a benefit from another that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep.","conclusion":"Answer A: Irrevocable waiver of condition."},"questionZh":"房主和规划师签署了一份详细的书面文件，其中规划师（景观设计师）同意按照规划师准备并纳入书面文件的设计对业主的住宅进行景观美化和重新种植。业主同意在工程完成后支付 10,000 美元。在业主的坚持下，书面协议中包含一项条款，即除非业主的法律合伙人（一位热衷于景观美化的学生）批准规划师的设计，否则双方均不受约束。在规划师开始工作之前，业主的律师合伙人当着业主和规划师的面明确表示不同意景观设计。尽管如此，业主还是命令规划师继续进行这项工作，规划师无奈地照做了。当规划者的履约完成 40% 时，业主拒绝支付合同价款或其任何部分的义务（如果有）。如果规划师现在起诉业主，要求其违约赔偿，那么以下哪个概念最能支持规划师的主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可撤销地放弃条件。","B":"允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）。","C":"实质性的表现。","D":"不当得利。"},"explanationZh":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n如果先决条件（即，在一方或双方履约到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，缔约方通常可以避免履约。然而，如果从该条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。如果另一方不利地依赖了弃权，则弃权方不能撤回弃权。此处，协议规定，如果业主的法律合伙人批准规划师的景观设计（先决条件），则协议具有约束力。法律合伙人不同意该设计（未发生），但业主命令规划师无论如何都要继续工作（弃权）。而且由于规划者通过完成 40% 的工作而有害地依赖该弃权，因此业主无法撤回该弃权（即该弃权是不可撤销的）。因此，不可撤销的条件放弃的概念最能支持规划者的违约索赔。 （选择B）根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。但由于合同是在这里成立的，因此该学说不适用。 （选项C） 策划者没有实质履行合同义务，因为他的履行只完成了40%。 （选项D）不当得利原则适用于没有有效合同的情况，以防止一方当事人从另一方获得非正当赚取的且不公平保留的利益。\n\n教育目标：如果从先决条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该先决条件，则该先决条件的不发生可以被原谅。如果另一方不利地依赖了弃权，则弃权方不能撤回弃权。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果从先决条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃先决条件，则该先决条件的不发生可以被免除。如果另一方不利地依赖了弃权，则弃权方不能撤回弃权。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果规划师现在起诉业主，要求其违约赔偿，那么以下哪个概念最能支持规划师的主张？","rule":"如果从先决条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃先决条件，则该先决条件的不发生可以被免除。如果另一方不利地依赖了弃权，则弃权方不能撤回弃权。","analysis":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n如果先决条件（即，在一方或双方履约到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，缔约方通常可以避免履约。然而，如果从该条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。如果另一方不利地依赖了弃权，则弃权方不能撤回弃权。此处，协议规定，如果业主的法律合伙人批准规划师的景观设计（先决条件），则协议具有约束力。法律合伙人不同意该设计（未发生），但业主命令规划师无论如何都要继续工作（弃权）。而且由于规划者通过完成 40% 的工作而有害地依赖该弃权，因此业主无法撤回该弃权（即该弃权是不可撤销的）。因此，不可撤销的条件放弃的概念最能支持规划者的违约索赔。 （选择B）根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。但由于合同是在这里成立的，因此该学说不适用。 （选项C） 策划者没有实质履行合同义务，因为他的履行只完成了40%。 （选项D）不当得利原则适用于没有有效合同的情况，以防止一方当事人从另一方获得非正当赚取的且不公平保留的利益。\n\n教育目标：如果从先决条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该先决条件，则该先决条件的不发生可以被原谅。如果另一方不利地依赖了弃权，则弃权方不能撤回弃权。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可撤销地放弃条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"substantial performance","ipa":"/sʌbstˈænʃəl pɝfˈɔɹməns/","syllables":"sub·stan·tial per·for·mance","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"实质履行：虽有轻微偏差但已基本完成普通法义务","contextualMeaning":"实质履行：虽有轻微偏差但已基本完成普通法义务","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Substantial performance.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":182,"sourceQuestionPage":538,"sourceQuestionPages":[538],"sourceExplanationPages":[539,540],"sourceAnswerPages":[539,540],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-183","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Risk of Loss & Delivery","question":"A buyer and a seller entered into a contract for the sale of 10,000 novelty bracelets. The seller had the bracelets in stock. The contract specified that the seller would ship the bracelets by a third-party carrier. However, the contract did not specify either who was to pay the costs of carriage or the place of tender for the bracelets. On the above facts, when would the risk of loss of the bracelets pass to the buyer?","choices":{"A":"When the bracelets were delivered to a carrier and a proper contract for their carriage was made.","B":"When the bracelets were identified to the contract by the seller, assuming that the goods conformed to the contract.","C":"When the bracelets were unloaded on the buyer's premises by the carrier.","D":"When the contract was made."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Risk of loss in the absence of breach\n\n(UCC § 2-509)\n\nGeneral rule If seller is merchant, risk of loss passes to buyer when buyer takes physical possession of goods If not, risk of loss passes to buyer when goods are delivered Goods to be Shipment contract (presumed) – risk of loss passes to buyer when shipped by goods are given to carrier & proper contract for shipment is made carrier Destination contract – risk of loss passes to buyer when goods are delivered at place specified in contract Goods to be held Risk of loss passes to buyer when: by bailee buyer receives negotiable document of title covering goods (eg, warehouse receipt) or bailee (eg, warehouseman) acknowledges buyer's right to possess goods UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, novelty bracelets—as seen here) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, the risk of loss generally passes to the buyer when the buyer receives the goods. But if the contract requires the seller to deliver the goods through a third-party carrier, the risk of loss passes to the buyer at different times depending on whether the contract is a: shipment contract* – a contract that does not require delivery at a particular location, in which case the risk of loss passes to the buyer when goods are delivered to the carrier and a proper contract for their shipment is made or destination contract – a contract that requires delivery at a particular location, in which case the risk of loss passes to the buyer when goods are delivered at the place specified in the contract Here, the contract between the buyer and the seller specified that the seller would ship the goods by a third-party carrier but did not specify the place of tender (ie, delivery) for the bracelets. As a result, it is a shipment contract, and the risk of loss would pass to the buyer when the bracelets were delivered to a third-party carrier and a proper contract for their carriage (ie, shipment) was made (Choices B, C & D). *The UCC presumes that a contract is a shipment contract unless it contains a \"free on board\" term or other term explicitly allocating the risk of loss. Merely indicating a shipping address does not make a contract a destination contract.\n\nEducational objective: A contract that requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier but does not specify a delivery location is a shipment contract. Under such a contract, the risk of loss shifts to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier and makes a proper contract for their shipment.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (risk of loss in the absence of breach).","educationalObjective":"A contract that requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier but does not specify a delivery location is a shipment contract. Under such a contract, the risk of loss shifts to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier and makes a proper contract for their shipment.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (risk of loss in the absence of breach).","rule":"A contract that requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier but does not specify a delivery location is a shipment contract. Under such a contract, the risk of loss shifts to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier and makes a proper contract for their shipment.","irac":{"issue":"On the above facts, when would the risk of loss of the bracelets pass to the buyer?","rule":"A contract that requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier but does not specify a delivery location is a shipment contract. Under such a contract, the risk of loss shifts to the buyer when the seller delivers the goods to the carrier and makes a proper contract for their shipment.","analysis":"Risk of loss in the absence of breach\n\n(UCC § 2-509)\n\nGeneral rule If seller is merchant, risk of loss passes to buyer when buyer takes physical possession of goods If not, risk of loss passes to buyer when goods are delivered Goods to be Shipment contract (presumed) – risk of loss passes to buyer when shipped by goods are given to carrier & proper contract for shipment is made carrier Destination contract – risk of loss passes to buyer when goods are delivered at place specified in contract Goods to be held Risk of loss passes to buyer when: by bailee buyer receives negotiable document of title covering goods (eg, warehouse receipt) or bailee (eg, warehouseman) acknowledges buyer's right to possess goods UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, novelty bracelets—as seen here) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, the risk of loss generally passes to the buyer when the buyer receives the goods. But if the contract requires the seller to deliver the goods through a third-party carrier, the risk of loss passes to the buyer at different times depending on whether the contract is a: shipment contract* – a contract that does not require delivery at a particular location, in which case the risk of loss passes to the buyer when goods are delivered to the carrier and a proper contract for their shipment is made or destination contract – a contract that requires delivery at a particular location, in which case the risk of loss passes to the buyer when goods are delivered at the place specified in the contract Here, the contract between the buyer and the seller specified that the seller would ship the goods by a third-party carrier but did not specify the place of tender (ie, delivery) for the bracelets. As a result, it is a shipment contract, and the risk of loss would pass to the buyer when the bracelets were delivered to a third-party carrier and a proper contract for their carriage (ie, shipment) was made (Choices B, C & D). *The UCC presumes that a contract is a shipment contract unless it contains a \"free on board\" term or other term explicitly allocating the risk of loss. Merely indicating a shipping address does not make a contract a destination contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: When the bracelets were delivered to a carrier and a proper contract for their carriage was made."},"questionZh":"买方和卖方签订了一份销售 10,000 个新颖手镯的合同。卖家有手镯库存。合同规定卖方将由第三方承运人运送手镯。然而，合同没有具体说明谁来支付运输费用或手镯的投标地点。根据上述事实，手链丢失的风险什么时候会转移给买家？","choicesZh":{"A":"当手镯被交付给承运人并签订适当的运输合同时。","B":"当卖方鉴定手镯符合合同规定时，即认为货物符合合同规定。","C":"当承运人将手镯卸载到买方场所时。","D":"合同签订的时候。"},"explanationZh":"不存在违约情况下的损失风险\n\n（UCC § 2-509）\n\n一般规则 如果卖方是商人，则当买方实际占有货物时，损失风险转移给买方 如果不是，当货物交付时，损失风险转移给买方 待发货货物 装运合同（假定） – 当货物交付给承运人并签订适当的运输合同时，损失风险转移给买方 目的地合同 – 当货物在合同规定的地点交付时，损失风险转移给买方 待持有的货物 在下列情况下，损失风险转移给买方： 由受托人收到涵盖货物的可转让所有权凭证（例如，仓单）或受托人（例如，仓库管理员）承认买方拥有货物的权利 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n商品销售合同（例如，新奇手镯——如此处所示）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据UCC，损失风险通常在买方收到货物时转移给买方。但如果合同要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物，则损失风险会在不同时间转移给买方，具体取决于合同是否为： 装运合同* – 不要求在特定地点交货的合同，在这种情况下，当货物交付给承运人并就其装运签订适当的合同时，损失风险转移给买方；或 目的地合同 – 要求在特定地点交货的合同，在这种情况下，当货物在合同中指定的地点交付时，损失风险转移给买方在此，买方和卖方之间的合同规定卖方将由第三方承运人运送货物，但没有规定手镯的投标（即交货）地点。因此，这是一份运输合同，当手镯被交付给第三方承运商并签订适当的运输（即运输）合同时，损失风险将转移给买方（选择 B、C 和 D）。 *UCC 假定合同为装运合同，除非其中包含“船上交货”条款或明确分配损失风险的其他条款。仅指明送货地址并不能使合同成为目的地合同。\n\n教育目的：要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物但未指定交付地点的合同为装运合同。根据此类合同，当卖方将货物交付给承运人并就其运输签订适当的合同时，损失风险就转移给买方。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（在不违反的情况下存在损失风险）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物但未指定交货地点的合同为装运合同。根据此类合同，当卖方将货物交付给承运人并就其运输签订适当的合同时，损失风险就转移给买方。","iracZh":{"issue":"根据上述事实，手链丢失的风险什么时候会转移给买家？","rule":"要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物但未指定交货地点的合同为装运合同。根据此类合同，当卖方将货物交付给承运人并就其运输签订适当的合同时，损失风险就转移给买方。","analysis":"不存在违约情况下的损失风险\n\n（UCC § 2-509）\n\n一般规则 如果卖方是商人，则当买方实际占有货物时，损失风险转移给买方 如果不是，当货物交付时，损失风险转移给买方 待发货货物 装运合同（假定） – 当货物交付给承运人并签订适当的运输合同时，损失风险转移给买方 目的地合同 – 当货物在合同规定的地点交付时，损失风险转移给买方 待持有的货物 在下列情况下，损失风险转移给买方： 由受托人收到涵盖货物的可转让所有权凭证（例如，仓单）或受托人（例如，仓库管理员）承认买方拥有货物的权利 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n商品销售合同（例如，新奇手镯——如此处所示）受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据UCC，损失风险通常在买方收到货物时转移给买方。但如果合同要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物，则损失风险会在不同时间转移给买方，具体取决于合同是否为： 装运合同* – 不要求在特定地点交货的合同，在这种情况下，当货物交付给承运人并就其装运签订适当的合同时，损失风险转移给买方；或 目的地合同 – 要求在特定地点交货的合同，在这种情况下，当货物在合同中指定的地点交付时，损失风险转移给买方在此，买方和卖方之间的合同规定卖方将由第三方承运人运送货物，但没有规定手镯的投标（即交货）地点。因此，这是一份运输合同，当手镯被交付给第三方承运商并签订适当的运输（即运输）合同时，损失风险将转移给买方（选择 B、C 和 D）。 *UCC 假定合同为装运合同，除非其中包含“船上交货”条款或明确分配损失风险的其他条款。仅指明送货地址并不能使合同成为目的地合同。\n\n教育目的：要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物但未指定交付地点的合同为装运合同。根据此类合同，当卖方将货物交付给承运人并就其运输签订适当的合同时，损失风险就转移给买方。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（在不违反的情况下存在损失风险）。","conclusion":"答案 A：当手镯被交付给承运人并签订适当的运输合同时。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"destination contract","ipa":"/dˌɛstʌnˈeɪʃən kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"des·ti·na·tion con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"目的地合同：卖方须将货物交至指定目的地","contextualMeaning":"目的地合同：卖方须将货物交至指定目的地","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Risk of loss in the absence of breach (UCC § 2-509) General rule If seller is merchant, risk of loss passes to buyer when buyer takes physical possession of goods If not, risk of loss passes to buyer when goods are delivered Goods to be Shipment contract (presumed) – risk of loss passes to buyer when shipped by goods are given to carrier & proper contract for shipment is made carrier Destination contract – risk of lo","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"shipment contract","ipa":"/ʃˈɪpmənt kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"ship·ment con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"装运合同：卖方将货物交给承运人并作适当安排即完成交付义务","contextualMeaning":"装运合同：卖方将货物交给承运人并作适当安排即完成交付义务","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A contract that requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier but does not specify a delivery location is a shipment contract.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]},{"term":"risk of loss","ipa":"/ɹˈɪsk ˈəv lˈɔs/","syllables":"risk of loss","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"损失风险：货物意外灭失由哪一方承担的规则","contextualMeaning":"损失风险：货物意外灭失由哪一方承担的规则","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"On the above facts, when would the risk of loss of the bracelets pass to the buyer?","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":183,"sourceQuestionPage":541,"sourceQuestionPages":[541],"sourceExplanationPages":[542,543],"sourceAnswerPages":[542,543],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-184","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Breach, Cure & Installments","question":"A mill and a bakery entered into a written contract that obligated the mill to deliver to the bakery 1,000 pounds of flour every Monday for 26 weeks at a specified price per pound. The mill delivered the proper quantity of flour in a timely manner for the first 15 weeks. However, the 16th delivery was tendered on a Tuesday, and amounted to only 800 pounds. The mill told the bakery that the 200-pound shortage would be made up on the delivery due the following Monday. The late delivery and the 200-pound shortage will not significantly disrupt the bakery's operations. How may the bakery legally respond to the nonconforming tender?","choices":{"A":"Accept the 800 pounds tendered, but notify the mill that the bakery will cancel the contract if the exact amount is not delivered on the following Monday.","B":"Accept the 800 pounds tendered, but notify the mill that the bakery will deduct from the price any damages for losses due to the nonconforming tender.","C":"Reject the 800 pounds tendered, and notify the mill that the bakery is canceling the contract.","D":"Reject the 800 pounds tendered, but notify the mill that the bakery will accept delivery the following Monday if it is conforming."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Installment contracts\n\nDefinition Goods delivered in multiple shipments that buyer separately accepts\n\nNonconforming Buyer may reject shipment only if nonconformity: shipment substantially impairs value of that shipment to buyer and cannot be cured Canceling contract Buyer may cancel entire contract if nonconformity substantially impairs value of entire contract to buyer\n\nContracts for the sale of goods generally follow the UCC's perfect-tender rule. This rule provides that a seller must tender goods conforming to the warranty obligations in the contract. Substantial performance will not suffice. However, this rule does not apply to installment contracts, which provide for the delivery of goods in multiple shipments, each to be separately accepted by the buyer (as seen here). Instead, installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule. This rule allows the buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment to the buyer (eg, by significantly disrupting bakery operations) and cannotbe cured (Choice D). The buyer may also cancel the entire contract if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the entire contract to the buyer. But when, as here, the seller makes adequate assurances that he/she can cure the nonconformity, the buyer must accept the shipment. However, the buyer can deduct any damages for losses due to the nonconformity from the contract price. (Choices A & C) The bakery cannot cancel or threaten to cancel the contract since the nonconformity did not substantially impair the value of the entire contract.\n\nEducational objective: Under the substantial-impairment rule, a buyer may reject tender of nonconforming goods in an installment contract when the nonconformity (1) substantially impairs the value of that shipment and (2) cannot be cured. A buyer may also cancel the entire contract if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-612 (discussing breach of an installment contract).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-717 (explaining the deduction of damages from price for breach).","educationalObjective":"Under the substantial-impairment rule, a buyer may reject tender of nonconforming goods in an installment contract when the nonconformity (1) substantially impairs the value of that shipment and (2) cannot be cured. A buyer may also cancel the entire contract if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-612 (discussing breach of an installment contract).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-717 (explaining the deduction of damages from price for breach).","rule":"Under the substantial-impairment rule, a buyer may reject tender of nonconforming goods in an installment contract when the nonconformity (1) substantially impairs the value of that shipment and (2) cannot be cured. A buyer may also cancel the entire contract if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"How may the bakery legally respond to the nonconforming tender?","rule":"Under the substantial-impairment rule, a buyer may reject tender of nonconforming goods in an installment contract when the nonconformity (1) substantially impairs the value of that shipment and (2) cannot be cured. A buyer may also cancel the entire contract if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the contract. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"Installment contracts\n\nDefinition Goods delivered in multiple shipments that buyer separately accepts\n\nNonconforming Buyer may reject shipment only if nonconformity: shipment substantially impairs value of that shipment to buyer and cannot be cured Canceling contract Buyer may cancel entire contract if nonconformity substantially impairs value of entire contract to buyer\n\nContracts for the sale of goods generally follow the UCC's perfect-tender rule. This rule provides that a seller must tender goods conforming to the warranty obligations in the contract. Substantial performance will not suffice. However, this rule does not apply to installment contracts, which provide for the delivery of goods in multiple shipments, each to be separately accepted by the buyer (as seen here). Instead, installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule. This rule allows the buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment to the buyer (eg, by significantly disrupting bakery operations) and cannotbe cured (Choice D). The buyer may also cancel the entire contract if the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of the entire contract to the buyer. But when, as here, the seller makes adequate assurances that he/she can cure the nonconformity, the buyer must accept the shipment. However, the buyer can deduct any damages for losses due to the nonconformity from the contract price. (Choices A & C) The bakery cannot cancel or threaten to cancel the contract since the nonconformity did not substantially impair the value of the entire contract.","conclusion":"Answer B: Accept the 800 pounds tendered, but notify the mill that the bakery will deduct from the price any damages for losses due to the nonconforming tender."},"questionZh":"一家工厂和一家面包店签订了一份书面合同，要求工厂每周一向面包店交付 1,000 磅面粉，为期 26 周，每磅指定价格。工厂在前 15 周内及时交付了适量的面粉。然而，第 16 批货物是在周二交付的，数量仅为 800 英镑。工厂告诉面包店，200 磅的短缺将在下周一交货时弥补。延迟交货和 200 磅的短缺不会对面包店的运营造成重大干扰。面包店如何合法应对不合格投标？","choicesZh":{"A":"接受 800 磅的投标，但通知工厂，如果下周一未能交付确切数量，面包店将取消合同。","B":"接受 800 英镑的投标，但通知工厂，面包店将从价格中扣除因投标不合格而造成的任何损失。","C":"拒绝 800 磅的投标，并通知工厂面包店将取消合同。","D":"拒绝 800 磅的投标，但通知工厂，如果符合要求，面包店将在下周一接受交货。"},"explanationZh":"分期付款合同\n\n定义 买方分别接受的分批交付的货物\n\n不合格买方仅可在下列情况下拒绝装运： 装运严重损害了该批货物对买方的价值且无法修复 取消合同 如果不合格严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方可以取消整个合同\n\n货物销售合同通常遵循 UCC 的完全投标规则。该规则规定卖方必须提供符合合同约定保修义务的货物。仅仅有实质性的表现是不够的。然而，这条规则不适用于分期付款合同，分期合同规定分批交付货物，每批货物均由买方单独接受（如此处所示）。相反，分期付款合同遵循重大减值规则。当不合格品严重损害了买方的货物价值（例如，通过严重扰乱面包店运营）并且无法解决时，该规则允许买方拒绝不合格品的投标（选择D）。如果不合格项严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方也可以取消整个合同。但是，当卖方充分保证他/她可以解决不合格问题时，如此处所示，买方必须接受货物。但是，买方可以从合同价格中扣除因不符合要求而造成的损失。 （选项 A 和 C） 面包店不能取消或威胁取消合同，因为不符合项并未严重损害整个合同的价值。\n\n教育目标：根据实质性损害规则，当不合格品 (1) 严重损害该批货物的价值且 (2) 无法修复时，买方可以拒绝分期付款合同中不合格品的投标。如果不合格项严重损害了合同的价值，买方也可以取消整个合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-612（讨论违反分期付款合同）。\n\nUCC § 2-717（解释从违约价格中扣除损害赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据实质性损害规则，当不合格品 (1) 严重损害该批货物的价值且 (2) 无法修复时，买方可以拒绝分期付款合同中不合格品的投标。如果不合格项严重损害了合同的价值，买方也可以取消整个合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"面包店如何合法应对不合格投标？","rule":"根据实质性损害规则，当不合格品 (1) 严重损害该批货物的价值且 (2) 无法修复时，买方可以拒绝分期付款合同中不合格品的投标。如果不合格项严重损害了合同的价值，买方也可以取消整个合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"分期付款合同\n\n定义 买方分别接受的分批交付的货物\n\n不合格买方仅可在下列情况下拒绝装运： 装运严重损害了该批货物对买方的价值且无法修复 取消合同 如果不合格严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方可以取消整个合同\n\n货物销售合同通常遵循 UCC 的完全投标规则。该规则规定卖方必须提供符合合同约定保修义务的货物。仅仅有实质性的表现是不够的。然而，这条规则不适用于分期付款合同，分期合同规定分批交付货物，每批货物均由买方单独接受（如此处所示）。相反，分期付款合同遵循重大减值规则。当不合格品严重损害了买方的货物价值（例如，通过严重扰乱面包店运营）并且无法解决时，该规则允许买方拒绝不合格品的投标（选择D）。如果不合格项严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方也可以取消整个合同。但是，当卖方充分保证他/她可以解决不合格问题时，如此处所示，买方必须接受货物。但是，买方可以从合同价格中扣除因不符合要求而造成的损失。 （选项 A 和 C） 面包店不能取消或威胁取消合同，因为不符合项并未严重损害整个合同的价值。\n\n教育目标：根据实质性损害规则，当不合格品 (1) 严重损害该批货物的价值且 (2) 无法修复时，买方可以拒绝分期付款合同中不合格品的投标。如果不合格项严重损害了合同的价值，买方也可以取消整个合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-612（讨论违反分期付款合同）。\n\nUCC § 2-717（解释从违约价格中扣除损害赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 B：接受 800 英镑的投标，但通知工厂，面包店将从价格中扣除因投标不合格而造成的任何损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"installment contract","ipa":"/ˌɪnstˈɔlmənt kˈɑntɹˌækt/","syllables":"in·stall·ment con·tract","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"分批交付合同：货物分批交付并分别接受的合同","contextualMeaning":"分批交付合同：货物分批交付并分别接受的合同","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Under the substantial-impairment rule, a buyer may reject tender of nonconforming goods in an installment contract when the nonconformity (1) substantially impairs the value of that shipment and (2) cannot be cured.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":184,"sourceQuestionPage":544,"sourceQuestionPages":[544],"sourceExplanationPages":[545,546],"sourceAnswerPages":[545,546],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-185","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A landscaper agreed to maintain the yard of a homeowner for six months for a fee of $300 per month, payable at the end of the six-month period. This amount was the fair market value of the work to be performed. The landscaper performed the work for four months but was then offered another job for $500 per month and, after notifying the homeowner, stopped doing the promised work and took the other job. The homeowner refused to pay the landscaper anything. The homeowner could have hired a comparable landscaper to complete the work for $300 per month. In a lawsuit between the landscaper and the homeowner, what is the likely result?","choices":{"A":"Neither the landscaper nor the homeowner will recover, because neither has suffered any loss.","B":"The homeowner will recover $400, which constitutes the unjust enrichment of the landscaper.","C":"The homeowner will recover $600, which is the cost of completing the work at prevailing market rates.","D":"The landscaper will recover $1,200 for the work done before the breach."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Restitutionary damages\n\nDefinition Damages awarded to restore to claimant the value of whatever benefit was conferred upon recipient Purpose Prevent unjust enrichment\n\nMeasure Reasonable value of work performed or services rendered (ie, cost of recipient obtaining comparable performance) If applicable, extent to which recipient's property has increased in value or recipient's other interests have been advanced A breaching party who incurs loss may not sue for compensatory damages for the party's actual losses. However, if the breaching party has significantly performed, he/she may recover restitutionary damages from the nonbreaching party for the reasonable value of the work performed.* This ensures that the nonbreaching party is not unjustly enriched. The reasonable value of the work performed is typically measured by the cost of obtaining comparable performance. Here, the landscaper breached the parties' agreement after maintaining the homeowner's yard for four out of the agreed-upon six months. The landscaper had significantly performed two-thirds of the contract, so the landscaper is entitled to restitution for the reasonable value of the work done before the breach. And since a comparable landscaper could have done the work for $300 per month, the landscaper will recover $1,200 ($300 per month x 4 months) (Choice A). *The breaching party's restitutionary recovery is limited to the contract price, reduced by any damages that the nonbreaching party incurred as a result of the breach and could not reasonably avoid. (Choice B) The homeowner (not the landscaper) was unjustly enriched because the homeowner received lawn-maintenance services for four months but paid nothing. The fact that the landscaper will receive an additional $400 (($500-300) per month x 2 months) from a different job has no bearing on the outcome of this dispute. (Choice C) As the nonbreaching party, the homeowner could recover expectation damages, measured by the difference between the contract price and the cost of completing the work at prevailing market rates. But the contract and market prices are the same here ($300 per month), so the homeowner will recover nothing.\n\nEducational objective: A breaching party who has significantly performed may recover restitutionary damages from the nonbreaching party for the reasonable value of the work performed, as measured by the cost of obtaining comparable performance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 371 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of restitution interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 374 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (restitution in favor of breaching party).","educationalObjective":"A breaching party who has significantly performed may recover restitutionary damages from the nonbreaching party for the reasonable value of the work performed, as measured by the cost of obtaining comparable performance.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 371 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of restitution interest).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 374 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (restitution in favor of breaching party).","rule":"A breaching party who has significantly performed may recover restitutionary damages from the nonbreaching party for the reasonable value of the work performed, as measured by the cost of obtaining comparable performance.","irac":{"issue":"In a lawsuit between the landscaper and the homeowner, what is the likely result?","rule":"A breaching party who has significantly performed may recover restitutionary damages from the nonbreaching party for the reasonable value of the work performed, as measured by the cost of obtaining comparable performance.","analysis":"Restitutionary damages\n\nDefinition Damages awarded to restore to claimant the value of whatever benefit was conferred upon recipient Purpose Prevent unjust enrichment\n\nMeasure Reasonable value of work performed or services rendered (ie, cost of recipient obtaining comparable performance) If applicable, extent to which recipient's property has increased in value or recipient's other interests have been advanced A breaching party who incurs loss may not sue for compensatory damages for the party's actual losses. However, if the breaching party has significantly performed, he/she may recover restitutionary damages from the nonbreaching party for the reasonable value of the work performed.* This ensures that the nonbreaching party is not unjustly enriched. The reasonable value of the work performed is typically measured by the cost of obtaining comparable performance. Here, the landscaper breached the parties' agreement after maintaining the homeowner's yard for four out of the agreed-upon six months. The landscaper had significantly performed two-thirds of the contract, so the landscaper is entitled to restitution for the reasonable value of the work done before the breach. And since a comparable landscaper could have done the work for $300 per month, the landscaper will recover $1,200 ($300 per month x 4 months) (Choice A). *The breaching party's restitutionary recovery is limited to the contract price, reduced by any damages that the nonbreaching party incurred as a result of the breach and could not reasonably avoid. (Choice B) The homeowner (not the landscaper) was unjustly enriched because the homeowner received lawn-maintenance services for four months but paid nothing. The fact that the landscaper will receive an additional $400 (($500-300) per month x 2 months) from a different job has no bearing on the outcome of this dispute. (Choice C) As the nonbreaching party, the homeowner could recover expectation damages, measured by the difference between the contract price and the cost of completing the work at prevailing market rates. But the contract and market prices are the same here ($300 per month), so the homeowner will recover nothing.","conclusion":"Answer D: The landscaper will recover $1,200 for the work done before the breach."},"questionZh":"一位园林设计师同意维护房主的院子六个月，每月收费 300 美元，在六个月期限结束时支付。该金额是待完成工作的公平市场价值。园艺师做了四个月的工作，但随后又以每月 500 美元的价格获得了另一份工作，在通知房主后，他停止了承诺的工作并接受了另一份工作。房主拒绝向园艺师支付任何费用。房主可以每月花费 300 美元聘请一位类似的园林设计师来完成这项工作。在园林设计师和房主之间的诉讼中，可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"园艺师和房主都不会康复，因为他们都没有遭受任何损失。","B":"房主将追回400美元，这构成园林设计师的不当得利。","C":"房主将收回 600 美元，这是按现行市场价格完成这项工作的费用。","D":"园林设计师将为违规前完成的工作收回 1,200 美元。"},"explanationZh":"赔偿损失\n\n定义 为恢复索赔人所获得的任何利益的价值而授予的损害赔偿 目的 防止不当得利\n\n衡量所完成的工作或提供的服务的合理价值（即接收者获得可比绩效的成本） 如果适用，接收者的财产增值或接收者的其他利益已增加的程度 遭受损失的违约方不得就该方的实际损失提出赔偿损失赔偿请求。然而，如果违约方显着履行了义务，他/她可以根据其所完成工作的合理价值向守约方追偿损害赔偿。*这确保了守约方不会获得不当得利。所完成工作的合理价值通常是通过获得可比绩效的成本来衡量的。在这里，园林设计师在约定的六个月中维护了房主的院子四个月，违反了双方的协议。园林设计师已显着履行了合同的三分之二，因此园林设计师有权就违约前完成的工作的合理价值获得赔偿。由于类似的园林设计师可以每月 300 美元完成这项工作，因此园林设计师将收回 1,200 美元（每月 300 美元 x 4 个月）（选择 A）。 *违约方的赔偿金额仅限于合同价格，减去非违约方因违约而遭受且无法合理避免的任何损失。 （选项 B） 房主（不是园艺师）获得了不公正的利益，因为房主接受了四个月的草坪维护服务，但没有支付任何费用。园林设计师将从另一份工作中额外获得 400 美元（每月（500-300 美元 x 2 个月））这一事实与本次纠纷的结果无关。 （选项C）作为非违约方，房主可以收回预期损害赔偿，赔偿金额以合同价格与按现行市场价格完成工程的成本之间的差额来衡量。但这里的合同价格和市场价格是一样的（每月 300 美元），所以房主将一无所获。\n\n教育目标：表现出色的违约方可以根据其所完成工作的合理价值（通过获得可比绩效的成本来衡量）向非违约方索取返还利益赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 371（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（归还利息的衡量标准）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 374（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有利于违约方的赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"显着履行的违约方可以根据所执行工作的合理价值（以获得可比绩效的成本来衡量）向非违约方追偿损害赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"在园林设计师和房主之间的诉讼中，可能的结果是什么？","rule":"显着履行的违约方可以根据所执行工作的合理价值（以获得可比绩效的成本来衡量）向非违约方追偿损害赔偿。","analysis":"赔偿损失\n\n定义 为恢复索赔人所获得的任何利益的价值而授予的损害赔偿 目的 防止不当得利\n\n衡量所完成的工作或提供的服务的合理价值（即接收者获得可比绩效的成本） 如果适用，接收者的财产增值或接收者的其他利益已增加的程度 遭受损失的违约方不得就该方的实际损失提出赔偿损失赔偿请求。然而，如果违约方显着履行了义务，他/她可以根据其所完成工作的合理价值向守约方追偿损害赔偿。*这确保了守约方不会获得不当得利。所完成工作的合理价值通常是通过获得可比绩效的成本来衡量的。在这里，园林设计师在约定的六个月中维护了房主的院子四个月，违反了双方的协议。园林设计师已显着履行了合同的三分之二，因此园林设计师有权就违约前完成的工作的合理价值获得赔偿。由于类似的园林设计师可以每月 300 美元完成这项工作，因此园林设计师将收回 1,200 美元（每月 300 美元 x 4 个月）（选择 A）。 *违约方的赔偿金额仅限于合同价格，减去非违约方因违约而遭受且无法合理避免的任何损失。 （选项 B） 房主（不是园艺师）获得了不公正的利益，因为房主接受了四个月的草坪维护服务，但没有支付任何费用。园林设计师将从另一份工作中额外获得 400 美元（每月（500-300 美元 x 2 个月））这一事实与本次纠纷的结果无关。 （选项C）作为非违约方，房主可以收回预期损害赔偿，赔偿金额以合同价格与按现行市场价格完成工程的成本之间的差额来衡量。但这里的合同价格和市场价格是一样的（每月 300 美元），所以房主将一无所获。\n\n教育目标：表现出色的违约方可以根据其所完成工作的合理价值（通过获得可比绩效的成本来衡量）向非违约方索取返还利益赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 371（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（归还利息的衡量标准）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 374（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（有利于违约方的赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 D：园林设计师将为违规前完成的工作收回 1,200 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":185,"sourceQuestionPage":547,"sourceQuestionPages":[547],"sourceExplanationPages":[548,549],"sourceAnswerPages":[548,549],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-186","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A young lawyer asked her neighbor to lend her $1,000. On September 15, the neighbor lent $1,000 to the lawyer, which she agreed to repay in installments of $100 plus accrued interest on the last day of each month beginning October 31. The lawyer did not pay the installments due on October 31, November 30, or December 31 and has informed the neighbor that she will be unable to make repayments in the foreseeable future. On January 15, the neighbor is entitled to a judgment against the lawyer for which of the following amounts?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because if the neighbor sues before the entire amount is due, he will be splitting his cause of action.","B":"$300 plus the accrued interest, because the lawyer's breach is only a partial breach.","C":"$1,000 plus the accrued interest, because the failure to pay her debts as they come due indicates that the lawyer is insolvent and the neighbor is thereby entitled to accelerate payment of the debt.","D":"$1,000 plus the accrued interest, because the lawyer's unexcused failure to pay three installments is a material breach."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Installment contracts\n\nDeliveries Price for each installment generally due at time & place of each delivery Seller's damages for breach amount to missed payments Buyer's damages for breach amount to fair market value minus contract price for missed deliveries Payments Creditor's damages for breach amount to missed payments\n\nBreach by nonperformance coupled with repudiation of the contract typically gives rise to a claim for damages for total breach. This is true unless, at the time of the breach, the only contractual obligation that remains is for the breaching party to make installment payments of money. In that circumstance, the breaching party's failure to pay—whether or not coupled with a repudiation—gives rise to a claim for damages for partial breach only. This means that the nonbreaching party can recover the missed payments. Here, the lawyer failed to pay the first three installments of $100 plus accrued interest (breach by nonperformance) and informed the neighbor that she would be unable to make repayments in the foreseeable future (repudiation). However, the only contractual obligation remaining on either side was for the lawyer to make the rest of the installment payments. Therefore, the neighbor has a claim for damages for partial breach only, and he is entitled to a judgment against the lawyer for $300 plus the accrued interest (missed payments). (Choice A) After breach of an installment contract, the nonbreaching party can (1) wait until the end of the contract and sue for the total damages or (2) split the cause of action— ie, sue for partial breach immediately and reserve the right to sue again for later breaches. (Choice C) Under the UCC, each party to a sale-of-goods contract gains certain rights when the other party becomes insolvent. However, this does not apply to loan contracts, which are governed by common law. (Choice D) A material breach occurs when the nonbreaching party fails to receive the substantial benefit of the contract. But failing to make a few payments of an installment loan is only a partial breach.\n\nEducational objective: Breach by nonperformance coupled with repudiation typically gives rise to a claim for total breach. But where the only duty that remains at the time of breach is for the breaching party to pay installments, the failure to do so—whether or not coupled with a repudiation— gives rise to a claim for partial breach only. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 243 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of breach by nonperformance).","educationalObjective":"Breach by nonperformance coupled with repudiation typically gives rise to a claim for total breach. But where the only duty that remains at the time of breach is for the breaching party to pay installments, the failure to do so—whether or not coupled with a repudiation— gives rise to a claim for partial breach only. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 243 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of breach by nonperformance).","rule":"Breach by nonperformance coupled with repudiation typically gives rise to a claim for total breach. But where the only duty that remains at the time of breach is for the breaching party to pay installments, the failure to do so—whether or not coupled with a repudiation— gives rise to a claim for partial breach only. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"On January 15, the neighbor is entitled to a judgment against the lawyer for which of the following amounts?","rule":"Breach by nonperformance coupled with repudiation typically gives rise to a claim for total breach. But where the only duty that remains at the time of breach is for the breaching party to pay installments, the failure to do so—whether or not coupled with a repudiation— gives rise to a claim for partial breach only. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"Installment contracts\n\nDeliveries Price for each installment generally due at time & place of each delivery Seller's damages for breach amount to missed payments Buyer's damages for breach amount to fair market value minus contract price for missed deliveries Payments Creditor's damages for breach amount to missed payments\n\nBreach by nonperformance coupled with repudiation of the contract typically gives rise to a claim for damages for total breach. This is true unless, at the time of the breach, the only contractual obligation that remains is for the breaching party to make installment payments of money. In that circumstance, the breaching party's failure to pay—whether or not coupled with a repudiation—gives rise to a claim for damages for partial breach only. This means that the nonbreaching party can recover the missed payments. Here, the lawyer failed to pay the first three installments of $100 plus accrued interest (breach by nonperformance) and informed the neighbor that she would be unable to make repayments in the foreseeable future (repudiation). However, the only contractual obligation remaining on either side was for the lawyer to make the rest of the installment payments. Therefore, the neighbor has a claim for damages for partial breach only, and he is entitled to a judgment against the lawyer for $300 plus the accrued interest (missed payments). (Choice A) After breach of an installment contract, the nonbreaching party can (1) wait until the end of the contract and sue for the total damages or (2) split the cause of action— ie, sue for partial breach immediately and reserve the right to sue again for later breaches. (Choice C) Under the UCC, each party to a sale-of-goods contract gains certain rights when the other party becomes insolvent. However, this does not apply to loan contracts, which are governed by common law. (Choice D) A material breach occurs when the nonbreaching party fails to receive the substantial benefit of the contract. But failing to make a few payments of an installment loan is only a partial breach.","conclusion":"Answer B: $300 plus the accrued interest, because the lawyer's breach is only a partial breach."},"questionZh":"一位年轻的律师请求她的邻居借给她 1000 美元。 9月15日，邻居借给律师1000美元，她同意分期偿还100美元，并在10月31日开始的每月最后一天支付应计利息。律师没有支付10月31日、11月30日或12月31日到期的分期付款，并已通知邻居她在可预见的未来将无法还款。 1 月 15 日，邻居有权要求律师赔偿下列哪一项金额？","choicesZh":{"A":"没什么，因为如果邻居在全额到期之前提起诉讼，他将分割诉讼理由。","B":"300 美元加上应计利息，因为律师的违约行为只是部分违约行为。","C":"1,000美元加上应计利息，因为未能偿还到期债务表明律师无力偿债，邻居因此有权加速偿还债务。","D":"1,000 美元加上应计利息，因为律师无故未能支付三期分期付款，属于重大违约。"},"explanationZh":"分期付款合同\n\n交货 每期付款的价格通常在每次交货的时间和地点到期 卖方违约损失赔偿金额与未付款项金额 买方违约损失金额金额为公平市场价值减去未交货合同价格 付款 债权人因违约损失金额与未付款项金额\n\n不履行合同并拒绝履行合同通常会导致因完全违约而提出损害赔偿索赔。情况确实如此，除非在违约时，唯一剩下的合同义务是违约方分期付款。在这种情况下，违约方未能付款（无论是否伴有拒付）只会引起部分违约的损害赔偿索赔。这意味着非违约方可以追回错过的付款。在此，律师未能支付前三期 100 美元加应计利息（不履约违约），并通知邻居她在可预见的未来将无法还款（赖账）。然而，双方唯一剩下的合同义务是律师支付剩余的分期付款。因此，邻居仅就部分违约提出损害赔偿要求，并且有权要求律师赔偿 300 美元加上应计利息（未付款）。 （选择A）分期付款合同违约后，守约方可以（1）等到合同结束后提起全部损害赔偿诉讼，或者（2）分案起诉，即立即起诉部分违约行为，并保留对以后的违约行为再次提起诉讼的权利。 （选项C）根据《统一商法典》（UCC），货物销售合同的每一方在另一方无力偿债时都获得某些权利。然而，这不适用于受普通法管辖的贷款合同。 （选项D）当守约方未能获得合同的实质利益时，即构成重大违约。但未能支付部分分期贷款只是部分违约。\n\n教育目标：不履行违约行为加上否认通常会导致完全违约索赔。但是，如果违约时剩下的唯一义务是违约方分期付款，则未能这样做（无论是否伴有否认）只会引起部分违约的索赔。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 243（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不履行违约的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"不履行违约行为加上拒绝履行通常会导致完全违约索赔。但是，如果违约时剩下的唯一义务是违约方分期付款，则未能这样做（无论是否伴有否认）只会引起部分违约的索赔。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"1 月 15 日，邻居有权要求律师赔偿下列哪一项金额？","rule":"不履行违约行为加上拒绝履行通常会导致完全违约索赔。但是，如果违约时剩下的唯一义务是违约方分期付款，则未能这样做（无论是否伴有否认）只会引起部分违约的索赔。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"分期付款合同\n\n交货 每期付款的价格通常在每次交货的时间和地点到期 卖方违约损失赔偿金额与未付款项金额 买方违约损失金额金额为公平市场价值减去未交货合同价格 付款 债权人因违约损失金额与未付款项金额\n\n不履行合同并拒绝履行合同通常会导致因完全违约而提出损害赔偿索赔。情况确实如此，除非在违约时，唯一剩下的合同义务是违约方分期付款。在这种情况下，违约方未能付款（无论是否伴有拒付）只会引起部分违约的损害赔偿索赔。这意味着非违约方可以追回错过的付款。在此，律师未能支付前三期 100 美元加应计利息（不履约违约），并通知邻居她在可预见的未来将无法还款（赖账）。然而，双方唯一剩下的合同义务是律师支付剩余的分期付款。因此，邻居仅就部分违约提出损害赔偿要求，并且有权要求律师赔偿 300 美元加上应计利息（未付款）。 （选择A）分期付款合同违约后，守约方可以（1）等到合同结束后提起全部损害赔偿诉讼，或者（2）分案起诉，即立即起诉部分违约行为，并保留对以后的违约行为再次提起诉讼的权利。 （选项C）根据《统一商法典》（UCC），货物销售合同的每一方在另一方无力偿债时都获得某些权利。然而，这不适用于受普通法管辖的贷款合同。 （选项D）当守约方未能获得合同的实质利益时，即构成重大违约。但未能支付部分分期贷款只是部分违约。\n\n教育目标：不履行违约行为加上否认通常会导致完全违约索赔。但是，如果违约时剩下的唯一义务是违约方分期付款，则未能这样做（无论是否伴有否认）只会引起部分违约的索赔。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 243（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不履行违约的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 B：300 美元加上应计利息，因为律师的违约行为只是部分违约行为。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"material breach","ipa":"/mʌtˈɪɹiəl bɹˈitʃ/","syllables":"ma·te·r·ial breach","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"重大违约：严重剥夺对方预期利益并允许暂停履行","contextualMeaning":"重大违约：严重剥夺对方预期利益并允许暂停履行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"$1,000 plus the accrued interest, because the lawyer's unexcused failure to pay three installments is a material breach.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":186,"sourceQuestionPage":550,"sourceQuestionPages":[550],"sourceExplanationPages":[551,552],"sourceAnswerPages":[551,552],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-187","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A tenant rented a commercial building from a landlord and operated a business in it. The building's large front window was smashed by vandals six months before expiration of the tenant-landlord lease. The tenant, who was obligated thereunder to effect and pay for repairs in such cases, promptly contracted with a window store to replace the window for $2,000, due 30 days after satisfactory completion of the work. The landlord was then unaware of the tenant-store contract. The store was aware that the building was under lease but dealt entirely with the tenant. Sixty days after the store's satisfactory completion of the window replacement, and prior to the expiration of the tenant's lease, the tenant ceased doing business and vacated the building. In so doing, the tenant forfeited his right to the return of a $2,000 security deposit with the landlord under the lease provisions. The deposit had been required, however, for the express purpose (as stated in the lease) of covering any damage to the leased property except ordinary wear and tear. The only such damage occurring during the tenant's occupancy was the smashed window. The window store's $2,000 bill for the window replacement is wholly unpaid. Assuming that the window store has no remedy quasi in rem under the relevant state mechanic's lien statute, which of the following would provide the store's best chance of an effective remedy in personam against the landlord?","choices":{"A":"An action as third-party intended beneficiary of the tenant-landlord lease.","B":"An action based on an implied-in-fact contract.","C":"An action based on promissory estoppel.","D":"An action in quasi-contract for the reasonable value of a benefit non-gratuitously conferred on the landlord."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Implied contracts\n\nImplied-in- Formed when party manifests assent by conduct rather than spoken or fact written words Eg, contractor mistakenly paves driveway & owner does not object Implied-in- Constructed by court to prevent unjust enrichment when party receives law* benefit from another who reasonably expects compensation Eg, physician treats unconscious person (\"quasi\")\n\n*Implied-in-law contracts are not true contracts since they lack mutual assent.\n\nAn \"in personam\" remedy is effective against a particular defendant, as opposed to the world at large. In personam remedies are available in most causes of action, including quasi-contract. A plaintiff can recover in quasi-contract—despite having no contractual relationship with the defendant—if the plaintiff conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on the defendant that resulted in unjust enrichment. Here, the tenant hired the window store to replace a broken window for $2,000 but failed to pay the bill. Although the landlord had no contractual relationship with the window store, he benefited from the window replacement in his building. This unjustly enriched the landlord since the tenant had forfeited his $2,000 security deposit and there was no other damage during the tenant's lease for which the landlord had to use the deposit. Therefore, quasi-contract provides the store's best chance for an effective in personam remedy against the landlord. (Choice A) An intended third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to the contract whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly. Intended beneficiaries have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them. But here, the landlord and tenant had no intent to benefit the window store when they entered the lease agreement. (Choice B) Implied-in-fact contracts are formed when the parties manifest assent by conduct rather than spoken or written words. But the landlord and window store could not have formed one here since the store dealt entirely with the tenant. (Choice C) Promissory estoppel provides a basis for a party to enforce a promise on which it relied when no valid contract was formed. But promissory estoppel would be ineffective here since the landlord never promised to pay the window store's bill.\n\nEducational objective: A plaintiff can recover under a quasi-contract theory—despite having no contractual relationship with the defendant—if the plaintiff conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on the defendant that resulted in unjust enrichment.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Third) of Restitution and Unjust Enrichment § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (explaining that a party is subject to liability for restitution when he/she is unjustly enriched at the expense of another).","educationalObjective":"A plaintiff can recover under a quasi-contract theory—despite having no contractual relationship with the defendant—if the plaintiff conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on the defendant that resulted in unjust enrichment.","references":"Restatement (Third) of Restitution and Unjust Enrichment § 1 (Am. Law Inst. 2011) (explaining that a party is subject to liability for restitution when he/she is unjustly enriched at the expense of another).","rule":"A plaintiff can recover under a quasi-contract theory—despite having no contractual relationship with the defendant—if the plaintiff conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on the defendant that resulted in unjust enrichment.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that the window store has no remedy quasi in rem under the relevant state mechanic's lien statute, which of the following would provide the store's best chance of an effective remedy in personam against the landlord?","rule":"A plaintiff can recover under a quasi-contract theory—despite having no contractual relationship with the defendant—if the plaintiff conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on the defendant that resulted in unjust enrichment.","analysis":"Implied contracts\n\nImplied-in- Formed when party manifests assent by conduct rather than spoken or fact written words Eg, contractor mistakenly paves driveway & owner does not object Implied-in- Constructed by court to prevent unjust enrichment when party receives law* benefit from another who reasonably expects compensation Eg, physician treats unconscious person (\"quasi\")\n\n*Implied-in-law contracts are not true contracts since they lack mutual assent.\n\nAn \"in personam\" remedy is effective against a particular defendant, as opposed to the world at large. In personam remedies are available in most causes of action, including quasi-contract. A plaintiff can recover in quasi-contract—despite having no contractual relationship with the defendant—if the plaintiff conferred a non-gratuitous benefit on the defendant that resulted in unjust enrichment. Here, the tenant hired the window store to replace a broken window for $2,000 but failed to pay the bill. Although the landlord had no contractual relationship with the window store, he benefited from the window replacement in his building. This unjustly enriched the landlord since the tenant had forfeited his $2,000 security deposit and there was no other damage during the tenant's lease for which the landlord had to use the deposit. Therefore, quasi-contract provides the store's best chance for an effective in personam remedy against the landlord. (Choice A) An intended third-party beneficiary is a nonparty to the contract whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly. Intended beneficiaries have contractual rights and may sue to enforce them. But here, the landlord and tenant had no intent to benefit the window store when they entered the lease agreement. (Choice B) Implied-in-fact contracts are formed when the parties manifest assent by conduct rather than spoken or written words. But the landlord and window store could not have formed one here since the store dealt entirely with the tenant. (Choice C) Promissory estoppel provides a basis for a party to enforce a promise on which it relied when no valid contract was formed. But promissory estoppel would be ineffective here since the landlord never promised to pay the window store's bill.","conclusion":"Answer D: An action in quasi-contract for the reasonable value of a benefit non-gratuitously conferred on the landlord."},"questionZh":"一名租户向房东租用了一栋商业大楼并在其中经营业务。在租客与房东的租约到期前六个月，该建筑的大前窗被破坏者砸碎。在这种情况下，租户有义务进行维修并支付维修费用，因此立即与一家橱窗商店签订了更换窗户的合同，费用为 2,000 美元，并在工程圆满完成后 30 天内支付。房东当时并不知道租户-商店合同。该商店知道该建筑正在出租，但完全与租户打交道。在商店圆满完成窗户更换工作后六十天，在租户租约到期之前，租户停止营业并搬出大楼。这样一来，租户就丧失了根据租赁条款向房东退还 2,000 美元保证金的权利。然而，押金的明确目的（如租约中所述）是为了弥补租赁财产除普通磨损之外的任何损坏。租户入住期间唯一发生的此类损坏是窗户被打碎。橱窗店更换窗户的 2,000 美元账单完全没有支付。假设根据相关州机械师的留置权法规，橱窗商店没有准对物救济，以下哪一项将为商店提供针对房东的有效人身救济的最佳机会？","choicesZh":{"A":"作为租户-房东租约的第三方预期受益人而采取的行动。","B":"基于默示事实合同的行动。","C":"基于承诺禁止反悔的诉讼。","D":"准合同中为非无偿地给予房东合理价值的利益而采取的行动。"},"explanationZh":"默示合同\n\n隐含- 当一方通过行为而不是口头或事实书面表达同意时 例如，承包商错误地铺设了车道且业主不反对 隐含- 当一方从合理期望赔偿的另一方获得法律*利益时，由法院构建，以防止不当得利 例如，医生治疗失去知觉的人（“准”）\n\n*默示法律合同不是真正的合同，因为它们缺乏双方同意。\n\n“对人”补救措施对特定被告有效，而不是对整个世界有效。大多数诉讼事由（包括准合同）均提供对人补救措施。如果原告向被告授予非无偿利益，导致不当得利，尽管原告与被告没有合同关系，但原告仍可以通过准合同获得赔偿。在此，租户花费 2,000 美元聘请窗户商店更换破损的窗户，但未能付款。尽管房东与橱窗店没有合同关系，但他从自己大楼的窗户更换中受益。这不公正地使房东获利，因为租客没收了他的 2,000 美元保证金，并且在租客租约期间没有发生房东必须使用押金来赔偿的其他损坏。因此，准合同为商店提供了针对房东进行有效人身救济的最佳机会。 （选择 A） 预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是指缔约​​方打算直接受益的合同非当事人。预期受益人拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼以强制执行这些权利。但在这里，房东和租客在签订租赁协议时并没有让橱窗店受益的意图。 （选择B）当双方通过行为而不是口头或书面文字表示同意时，默示事实合同即成立。但房东和橱窗商店不可能在这里合二为一，因为商店完全与租户打交道。 （选项 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）为一方在未形成有效合同时执行其所依赖的承诺提供了基础。但在此情况下，禁止反言是无效的，因为房东从未承诺支付橱窗商店的账单。\n\n教育目标：根据准合同理论，尽管原告与被告没有合同关系，但如果原告向被告授予非无偿利益，导致不当得利，原告就可以获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《归还和不当得利重述（第三）》第 1 条（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（解释当一方以牺牲另一方为代价而获得不正当得利时，他/她须承担归还责任）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据准合同理论，尽管原告与被告没有合同关系，但如果原告向被告授予非无偿利益，导致不当得利，原告就可以获得赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设根据相关州机械师的留置权法规，橱窗商店没有准对物救济，以下哪一项将为商店提供针对房东的有效人身救济的最佳机会？","rule":"根据准合同理论，尽管原告与被告没有合同关系，但如果原告向被告授予非无偿利益，导致不当得利，原告就可以获得赔偿。","analysis":"默示合同\n\n隐含- 当一方通过行为而不是口头或事实书面表达同意时 例如，承包商错误地铺设了车道且业主不反对 隐含- 当一方从合理期望赔偿的另一方获得法律*利益时，由法院构建，以防止不当得利 例如，医生治疗失去知觉的人（“准”）\n\n*默示法律合同不是真正的合同，因为它们缺乏双方同意。\n\n“对人”补救措施对特定被告有效，而不是对整个世界有效。大多数诉讼事由（包括准合同）均提供对人补救措施。如果原告向被告授予非无偿利益，导致不当得利，尽管原告与被告没有合同关系，但原告仍可以通过准合同获得赔偿。在此，租户花费 2,000 美元聘请窗户商店更换破损的窗户，但未能付款。尽管房东与橱窗店没有合同关系，但他从自己大楼的窗户更换中受益。这不公正地使房东获利，因为租客没收了他的 2,000 美元保证金，并且在租客租约期间没有发生房东必须使用押金来赔偿的其他损坏。因此，准合同为商店提供了针对房东进行有效人身救济的最佳机会。 （选择 A） 预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）是指缔约​​方打算直接受益的合同非当事人。预期受益人拥有合同权利，并可以提起诉讼以强制执行这些权利。但在这里，房东和租客在签订租赁协议时并没有让橱窗店受益的意图。 （选择B）当双方通过行为而不是口头或书面文字表示同意时，默示事实合同即成立。但房东和橱窗商店不可能在这里合二为一，因为商店完全与租户打交道。 （选项 C） 允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）为一方在未形成有效合同时执行其所依赖的承诺提供了基础。但在此情况下，禁止反言是无效的，因为房东从未承诺支付橱窗商店的账单。\n\n教育目标：根据准合同理论，尽管原告与被告没有合同关系，但如果原告向被告授予非无偿利益，导致不当得利，原告就可以获得赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《归还和不当得利重述（第三）》第 1 条（Am. Law Inst. 2011）（解释当一方以牺牲另一方为代价而获得不正当得利时，他/她须承担归还责任）。","conclusion":"答案 D：准合同中为非无偿地给予房东合理价值的利益而采取的行动。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":187,"sourceQuestionPage":553,"sourceQuestionPages":[553],"sourceExplanationPages":[554,555],"sourceAnswerPages":[554,555],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-188","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A man who knew nothing about horses inherited a thoroughbred colt whose disagreeable behavior made him a pest around the barn. The man sold the horse for $1,500 to a buyer who was an experienced racehorse-trainer and knew of the man's ignorance about horses. At the time of sale, the man said to the buyer, \"I hate to say it, but this horse is bad- tempered and nothing special.\" Which one of the following scenarios would best support an action by the buyer to rescind the sale?","choices":{"A":"A week after the sale, the horse went berserk and inflicted injuries upon the buyer that required his hospitalization for six months and a full year for his recovery.","B":"After the horse had won three races for the buyer, it was discovered that by clerical error, unknown to either party, the horse's official birth registration listed an undistinguished racehorse as the sire rather than the famous racehorse that in fact was the sire.","C":"In his first race after the sale, the horse galloped to a huge lead but dropped dead 100 yards from the finish line because of a rare congenital heart defect that was undiscoverable except by autopsy.","D":"The horse won $5 million for the buyer over a three-year racing career but upon being retired was found to be incurably sterile and useless as a breeder."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Mistake as grounds for rescission\n\nMutual mistake Adversely affected party can rescind contract if:\n\nmistake relates to basic assumption of contract mistake materially affects agreed-upon exchange of performances AND\n\nadversely affected party did not assume risk of mistake Unilateral In addition to above elements for mutual mistake, either: mistake mistake makes enforcement of contract unconscionable OR\n\nnonmistaken party caused, or knew or should have known of, mistake When both parties enter into a contract based on the same mistake (ie, mutual mistake), the adversely affected party can rescind (ie, cancel) the contract if: the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. Here, the parties most likely entered the contract based on an erroneous belief that the horse was healthy and fit to race—a basic assumption of the contract. That mutual mistake materially affected the agreed-upon exchange since the horse that actually had been born with a rare congenital heart defect. And since that defect was undiscoverable except by autopsy, the buyer did not assume the risk that the horse would turn out to be unhealthy and unfit to race. As a result, the buyer can likely rescind the sale. (Choice A) The grounds for rescission must have existed when the contract was made. Because the horse went berserk a week after the sale, the buyer cannot seek rescission on this basis. (Choice B) Although both parties were mistaken as to the colt's true sire (a famous racehorse), the buyer would not be able to rescind the contract on this basis since the mistake made the colt more valuable to the buyer. (Choice D) Because the horse (which was sold for a mere $1,500) won $5 million for the buyer over three years, the buyer cannot successfully claim that it would be unfair to enforce the sale (ie, no material effect).\n\nEducational objective: Rescission is available due to a mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (material effect of mutual mistake making contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"Rescission is available due to a mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 152 cmt. c (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (material effect of mutual mistake making contract voidable).","rule":"Rescission is available due to a mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"A man who knew nothing about horses inherited a thoroughbred colt whose disagreeable behavior made him a pest around the barn. The man sold the horse for $1,500 to a buyer who was an experienced racehorse-trainer and knew of the man's ignorance about horses. At the time of sale, the man said to the buyer, \"I hate to say it, but this horse is bad- tempered and nothing special.\" Which one of the following scenarios would best support an action by the buyer to rescind the sale?","rule":"Rescission is available due to a mutual mistake if (1) the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract, (2) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances, and (3) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake.","analysis":"Mistake as grounds for rescission\n\nMutual mistake Adversely affected party can rescind contract if:\n\nmistake relates to basic assumption of contract mistake materially affects agreed-upon exchange of performances AND\n\nadversely affected party did not assume risk of mistake Unilateral In addition to above elements for mutual mistake, either: mistake mistake makes enforcement of contract unconscionable OR\n\nnonmistaken party caused, or knew or should have known of, mistake When both parties enter into a contract based on the same mistake (ie, mutual mistake), the adversely affected party can rescind (ie, cancel) the contract if: the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (ie, the adversely affected party cannot fairly be required to carry it out) and the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake. Here, the parties most likely entered the contract based on an erroneous belief that the horse was healthy and fit to race—a basic assumption of the contract. That mutual mistake materially affected the agreed-upon exchange since the horse that actually had been born with a rare congenital heart defect. And since that defect was undiscoverable except by autopsy, the buyer did not assume the risk that the horse would turn out to be unhealthy and unfit to race. As a result, the buyer can likely rescind the sale. (Choice A) The grounds for rescission must have existed when the contract was made. Because the horse went berserk a week after the sale, the buyer cannot seek rescission on this basis. (Choice B) Although both parties were mistaken as to the colt's true sire (a famous racehorse), the buyer would not be able to rescind the contract on this basis since the mistake made the colt more valuable to the buyer. (Choice D) Because the horse (which was sold for a mere $1,500) won $5 million for the buyer over three years, the buyer cannot successfully claim that it would be unfair to enforce the sale (ie, no material effect).","conclusion":"Answer C: In his first race after the sale, the horse galloped to a huge lead but dropped dead 100 yards from the finish line because of a rare congenital heart defect that was undiscoverable except by autopsy."},"questionZh":"一个对马一无所知的人继承了一匹纯种小马，它的令人讨厌的行为使他成为谷仓周围的害虫。该男子以 1,500 美元的价格将这匹马卖给了一位买家，买家是一位经验丰富的赛马训练师，并且知道该男子对马匹的无知。出售时，该男子对买家说：“我不想这么说，但这匹马脾气不好，没什么特别的。”以下哪一种情况最能支持买方撤销销售的行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"出售一周后，这匹马发狂并导致买家受伤，需要住院六个月和一整年才能康复。","B":"当这匹马为买家赢得三场比赛后，人们发现，由于双方均不知情的文书错误，这匹马的官方出生登记将一匹普通赛马列为父亲，而不是实际上是著名赛马的父亲。","C":"在出售后的第一场比赛中，这匹马以巨大的领先优势驰骋，但却在距终点线 100 码处倒地身亡，原因是罕见的先天性心脏缺陷，这种缺陷只能通过尸检才能发现。","D":"这匹马在三年的赛马生涯中为买家赢得了 500 万美元，但退役后发现它无法治愈，并且作为饲养员毫无用处。"},"explanationZh":"错误作为撤销理由\n\n相互错误 如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以解除合同：\n\n与合同基本假设相关的错误 错误对商定的履约交换产生重大影响并且\n\n受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误风险 单方面 除了上述共同错误的要素之外，还可以是： 错误 错误导致合同的执行不合理或\n\n非错误方造成、或知道或应当知道错误 当双方基于同一错误（即共同错误）签订合同时，如果出现以下情况，受不利影响的一方可以解除（即取消）合同： 该错误涉及合同的基本假设 该错误对商定的履约交换产生重大影响（即，无法公平地要求受不利影响的一方履行该义务）并且受不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。在这里，双方很可能基于错误地认为马匹健康且适合比赛而签订合同——这是合同的基本假设。这个共同的错误严重影响了商定的交换，因为这匹马实际上出生时患有罕见的先天性心脏缺陷。由于除非通过尸检，否则无法发现该缺陷，因此买家不会承担马匹不健康且不适合比赛的风险。因此，买方可能会撤销销售。 （选项A） 解除合同的理由必须在合同订立时已经存在。由于马匹在出售后一周就发疯了，买家不能以此为由寻求撤销。 （选项B）虽然双方都对小马驹的真正父亲（著名赛马）有误解，但买方不能据此解除合同，因为这一错误使小马驹对买方来说更有价值。 （选项 D） 由于这匹马（仅以 1,500 美元的价格出售）在三年内为买方赢得了 500 万美元，因此买方不能成功地声称强制出售是不公平的（即没有实质性影响）。\n\n教育目标：如果出现以下情况，则可以因共同错误而解除合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 152 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（共同错误的重大影响导致合同无效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果存在以下情况，则可以因共同错误而解除合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。","iracZh":{"issue":"一个对马一无所知的人继承了一匹纯种小马，它的令人讨厌的行为使他成为谷仓周围的害虫。该男子以 1,500 美元的价格将这匹马卖给了一位买家，买家是一位经验丰富的赛马训练师，并且知道该男子对马匹的无知。出售时，该男子对买家说：“我不想这么说，但这匹马脾气不好，没什么特别的。”以下哪一种情况最能支持买方撤销销售的行动？","rule":"如果存在以下情况，则可以因共同错误而解除合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。","analysis":"错误作为撤销理由\n\n相互错误 如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以解除合同：\n\n与合同基本假设相关的错误 错误对商定的履约交换产生重大影响并且\n\n受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误风险 单方面 除了上述共同错误的要素之外，还可以是： 错误 错误导致合同的执行不合理或\n\n非错误方造成、或知道或应当知道错误 当双方基于同一错误（即共同错误）签订合同时，如果出现以下情况，受不利影响的一方可以解除（即取消）合同： 该错误涉及合同的基本假设 该错误对商定的履约交换产生重大影响（即，无法公平地要求受不利影响的一方履行该义务）并且受不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。在这里，双方很可能基于错误地认为马匹健康且适合比赛而签订合同——这是合同的基本假设。这个共同的错误严重影响了商定的交换，因为这匹马实际上出生时患有罕见的先天性心脏缺陷。由于除非通过尸检，否则无法发现该缺陷，因此买家不会承担马匹不健康且不适合比赛的风险。因此，买方可能会撤销销售。 （选项A） 解除合同的理由必须在合同订立时已经存在。由于马匹在出售后一周就发疯了，买家不能以此为由寻求撤销。 （选项B）虽然双方都对小马驹的真正父亲（著名赛马）有误解，但买方不能据此解除合同，因为这一错误使小马驹对买方来说更有价值。 （选项 D） 由于这匹马（仅以 1,500 美元的价格出售）在三年内为买方赢得了 500 万美元，因此买方不能成功地声称强制出售是不公平的（即没有实质性影响）。\n\n教育目标：如果出现以下情况，则可以因共同错误而解除合同：(1) 该错误与合同的基本假设有关，(2) 该错误对约定的履约交换产生重大影响，以及 (3) 受到不利影响的一方没有承担该错误的风险。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 152 cmt。 c（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（共同错误的重大影响导致合同无效）。","conclusion":"答案 C：在出售后的第一场比赛中，这匹马以巨大的领先优势驰骋，但却在距终点线 100 码处倒地身亡，原因是罕见的先天性心脏缺陷，这种缺陷只能通过尸检才能发现。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":188,"sourceQuestionPage":556,"sourceQuestionPages":[556],"sourceExplanationPages":[557,558],"sourceAnswerPages":[557,558],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-189","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"Responding to a county's written advertisement for bids, a tire company was the successful bidder for the sale of tires to the county for its vehicles. The tire company and the county entered into a signed, written agreement that specified, \"It is agreed that the tire company will deliver all tires required by this agreement to the county, in accordance with the attached bid form and specifications, for a one-year period beginning September 1, 2019.\" Attached to the agreement was a copy of the bid form and specifications. No definite quantity of tires to be bought by the county from the tire company was specified in any of these documents. In January 2020, the tire company learned that the county was buying some of its tires from one of the company's competitors. A court has determined the tire company–county agreement is a requirements contract. If the tire company sues the county for damages caused by the county's purchase of some of its tires from the competitor, is the tire company likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the agreement is indefinite as to quantity.","B":"No, because the agreement lacks mutuality of obligation.","C":"Yes, under the contracts clause of the United States Constitution.","D":"Yes, under the provisions of the Uniform Commercial Code."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Buyer's duty of good faith & fair dealing under requirements contract\n\nBuyer must purchase goods in question from seller only Requirements must approximate reasonably foreseeable figure Reasonable elasticity in requirements permitted (so long as any variations are in good faith) Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods. Under the UCC, a requirements contract is a contract for the sale of as many goods as the buyer requires during a specified period. This creates an exclusive agreement between the buyer and seller. As a result, the duty of good faith and fair dealing implied in every contract requires the buyer to purchase goods from the seller only. A failure to do so violates that duty and is a breach of contract. Here, a court determined that the tire company–county agreement was a requirements contract. As a result, the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing required the county to purchase all its tires from the tire company. The county violated that duty, and breached the contract, when it purchased tires from the tire company's competitor. Therefore, the tire company will likely prevail in its suit against the county for damages. (Choice A) The quantity term is sufficiently definite in a requirements contract because the quantity can be made certain by reference to objective facts (ie, the buyer's actual requirements). (Choice B) An agreement lacks mutuality of obligation when one of the parties to the agreement is not obligated to perform. Requirements contracts have mutuality of obligation because the seller must provide, and the buyer must purchase, as many goods as the buyer requires during the contract term. (Choice C) The contracts clause generally prohibits state legislatures from enacting laws that substantially impair existing contract rights (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A requirements contract is an exclusive agreement between the buyer and seller for the sale of as many goods as the buyer requires during a specified period. The buyer's failure to purchase the goods from the seller violates the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing and constitutes a breach of contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (requirements contract).","educationalObjective":"A requirements contract is an exclusive agreement between the buyer and seller for the sale of as many goods as the buyer requires during a specified period. The buyer's failure to purchase the goods from the seller violates the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing and constitutes a breach of contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (requirements contract).","rule":"A requirements contract is an exclusive agreement between the buyer and seller for the sale of as many goods as the buyer requires during a specified period. The buyer's failure to purchase the goods from the seller violates the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing and constitutes a breach of contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the tire company sues the county for damages caused by the county's purchase of some of its tires from the competitor, is the tire company likely to prevail?","rule":"A requirements contract is an exclusive agreement between the buyer and seller for the sale of as many goods as the buyer requires during a specified period. The buyer's failure to purchase the goods from the seller violates the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing and constitutes a breach of contract.","analysis":"Buyer's duty of good faith & fair dealing under requirements contract\n\nBuyer must purchase goods in question from seller only Requirements must approximate reasonably foreseeable figure Reasonable elasticity in requirements permitted (so long as any variations are in good faith) Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods. Under the UCC, a requirements contract is a contract for the sale of as many goods as the buyer requires during a specified period. This creates an exclusive agreement between the buyer and seller. As a result, the duty of good faith and fair dealing implied in every contract requires the buyer to purchase goods from the seller only. A failure to do so violates that duty and is a breach of contract. Here, a court determined that the tire company–county agreement was a requirements contract. As a result, the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing required the county to purchase all its tires from the tire company. The county violated that duty, and breached the contract, when it purchased tires from the tire company's competitor. Therefore, the tire company will likely prevail in its suit against the county for damages. (Choice A) The quantity term is sufficiently definite in a requirements contract because the quantity can be made certain by reference to objective facts (ie, the buyer's actual requirements). (Choice B) An agreement lacks mutuality of obligation when one of the parties to the agreement is not obligated to perform. Requirements contracts have mutuality of obligation because the seller must provide, and the buyer must purchase, as many goods as the buyer requires during the contract term. (Choice C) The contracts clause generally prohibits state legislatures from enacting laws that substantially impair existing contract rights (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, under the provisions of the Uniform Commercial Code."},"questionZh":"一家轮胎公司根据某县的书面招标广告中标，向该县出售其车辆用轮胎。轮胎公司和该县签署了一份书面协议，其中规定：“双方同意，轮胎公司将按照所附投标表格和规格，向该县交付本协议所需的所有轮胎，为期一年，从 2019 年 9 月 1 日开始。”协议附有投标表格和规格的副本。这些文件都没有具体说明该县从轮胎公司购买轮胎的具体数量。 2020 年 1 月，该轮胎公司获悉该县正在从该公司的一家竞争对手那里购买部分轮胎。法院已确定轮胎公司与县之间的协议属于要求合同。如果轮胎公司起诉县政府，要求该县从竞争对手那里购买部分轮胎而造成损失，轮胎公司有可能胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为协议的数量是不确定的。","B":"不，因为该协议缺乏相互的义务。","C":"是的，根据美国宪法的合同条款。","D":"是的，根据《统一商法典》（UCC）的规定。"},"explanationZh":"买方在要求合同下的诚信义务和公平交易\n\n买方必须仅从卖方购买有问题的商品 要求必须接近合理可预见的数字 允许的要求的合理弹性（只要任何变化是善意的） 《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 第 2 条管辖商品销售合同。根据 UCC，需求合同是指在指定期间内销售买方所需数量的货物的合同。这在买方和卖方之间建立了排他性协议。因此，每份合同中隐含的诚实信用和公平交易的义务要求买方仅从卖方购买货物。如果不这样做，就违反了该义务，并且构成违约。在这里，法院判定轮胎公司与县之间的协议是一份需求合同。因此，隐含的诚信和公平交易义务要求该县从轮胎公司购买所有轮胎。当该县从轮胎公司的竞争对手那里购买轮胎时，违反了这项义务并违反了合同。因此，该轮胎公司在向该县提起的损害赔偿诉讼中很可能会胜诉。 （选项A）需求合同中的数量条款足够明确，因为数量可以通过参考客观事实（即买方的实际需求）来确定。 （选择B）当协议一方没有义务履行时，协议缺乏相互义务。需求合同具有相互义务，因为在合同期限内，卖方必须提供买方需要的货物，买方必须购买尽可能多的货物。 （选项 C）合同条款通常禁止州立法机构颁布严重损害现有合同权利的法律（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：需求合同是买方和卖方之间的排他性协议，用于在指定期间内销售买方所需数量的商品。买方未向卖方购买货物违反了诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务，构成违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（要求合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"需求合同是买方和卖方之间的排他性协议，用于在指定期限内销售买方需要的尽可能多的货物。买方未向卖方购买货物违反了诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务，构成违约。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果轮胎公司起诉县政府，要求该县从竞争对手那里购买部分轮胎而造成损失，轮胎公司有可能胜诉吗？","rule":"需求合同是买方和卖方之间的排他性协议，用于在指定期限内销售买方需要的尽可能多的货物。买方未向卖方购买货物违反了诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务，构成违约。","analysis":"买方在要求合同下的诚信义务和公平交易\n\n买方必须仅从卖方购买有问题的商品 要求必须接近合理可预见的数字 允许的要求的合理弹性（只要任何变化是善意的） 《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 第 2 条管辖商品销售合同。根据 UCC，需求合同是指在指定期间内销售买方所需数量的货物的合同。这在买方和卖方之间建立了排他性协议。因此，每份合同中隐含的诚实信用和公平交易的义务要求买方仅从卖方购买货物。如果不这样做，就违反了该义务，并且构成违约。在这里，法院判定轮胎公司与县之间的协议是一份需求合同。因此，隐含的诚信和公平交易义务要求该县从轮胎公司购买所有轮胎。当该县从轮胎公司的竞争对手那里购买轮胎时，违反了这项义务并违反了合同。因此，该轮胎公司在向该县提起的损害赔偿诉讼中很可能会胜诉。 （选项A）需求合同中的数量条款足够明确，因为数量可以通过参考客观事实（即买方的实际需求）来确定。 （选择B）当协议一方没有义务履行时，协议缺乏相互义务。需求合同具有相互义务，因为在合同期限内，卖方必须提供买方需要的货物，买方必须购买尽可能多的货物。 （选项 C）合同条款通常禁止州立法机构颁布严重损害现有合同权利的法律（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：需求合同是买方和卖方之间的排他性协议，用于在指定期间内销售买方所需数量的商品。买方未向卖方购买货物违反了诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务，构成违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（要求合同）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，根据《统一商法典》（UCC）的规定。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":189,"sourceQuestionPage":559,"sourceQuestionPages":[559],"sourceExplanationPages":[560,561],"sourceAnswerPages":[560,561],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-190","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"On November 15, a carpenter in a signed writing contracted with a homeowner for an agreed price to personally remodel the homeowner's kitchen according to specifications provided by the homeowner, and to start work on December 1. The carpenter agreed to provide all materials for the job in addition to all of the labor required. On November 26, the carpenter without legal excuse repudiated the contract. After a reasonable and prolonged effort, the homeowner could not find anyone to remodel his kitchen for a price approximating the price agreed to by the carpenter. One year later, the homeowner brings an action for specific performance against the carpenter. Which of the following will provide the carpenter with the best defense?","choices":{"A":"An action for equitable relief not brought within a reasonable time is barred by laches.","B":"Specific performance is generally not available as a remedy in the case of an anticipatory repudiation.","C":"Specific performance is not available as a remedy to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services.","D":"Specific performance is not available as a remedy where even nominal damages could have been recovered as a remedy at law."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Personal-services contracts\n\nDefinition Contract involving party's personal taste, skill, or talent—eg, entertainer, designer, lawyer, architect Enforcement Nonbreaching party entitled to compensatory damages stemming from breach Specific performance not available Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby a court orders a breaching party to perform a contractual duty when monetary damages would be inadequate—eg, breach of contract for the sale of land. However, this remedy is not available to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services because courts are unwilling to compel personal associations after a dispute. Here, the contract provided that the carpenter would personally remodel the homeowner's kitchen. When the carpenter repudiated the contract, the homeowner brought an action for specific performance. Therefore, the carpenter's best defense is that this remedy is not available for breach of personal-services contracts. (Choice A) The equitable defense of laches is commonly asserted in specific-performance actions. To prevail on this defense, the breaching party must show that the enforcing party's unreasonable delay in bringing the claim has resulted in prejudice—ie, substantially increased the cost or difficulty of performance. Here, the homeowner reasonably delayed suit while looking for a similarly priced carpenter and no prejudice to the carpenter is shown. (Choice B) Specific performance is available as a remedy in cases of anticipatory repudiation—ie, where one contracting party unequivocally indicates prior to the time for performance that he/she will not perform. (Choice D) Specific performance, like other equitable remedies, will not be granted where monetary damages are adequate. But nominal damages (eg, $1) alone are typically not an adequate remedy.\n\nEducational objective: Specific performance—ie, court-ordered performance of a contractual duty—is not available as a remedy to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 367 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that specific performance is not available to enforce personal contracts).","educationalObjective":"Specific performance—ie, court-ordered performance of a contractual duty—is not available as a remedy to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 367 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that specific performance is not available to enforce personal contracts).","rule":"Specific performance—ie, court-ordered performance of a contractual duty—is not available as a remedy to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following will provide the carpenter with the best defense?","rule":"Specific performance—ie, court-ordered performance of a contractual duty—is not available as a remedy to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services.","analysis":"Personal-services contracts\n\nDefinition Contract involving party's personal taste, skill, or talent—eg, entertainer, designer, lawyer, architect Enforcement Nonbreaching party entitled to compensatory damages stemming from breach Specific performance not available Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby a court orders a breaching party to perform a contractual duty when monetary damages would be inadequate—eg, breach of contract for the sale of land. However, this remedy is not available to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services because courts are unwilling to compel personal associations after a dispute. Here, the contract provided that the carpenter would personally remodel the homeowner's kitchen. When the carpenter repudiated the contract, the homeowner brought an action for specific performance. Therefore, the carpenter's best defense is that this remedy is not available for breach of personal-services contracts. (Choice A) The equitable defense of laches is commonly asserted in specific-performance actions. To prevail on this defense, the breaching party must show that the enforcing party's unreasonable delay in bringing the claim has resulted in prejudice—ie, substantially increased the cost or difficulty of performance. Here, the homeowner reasonably delayed suit while looking for a similarly priced carpenter and no prejudice to the carpenter is shown. (Choice B) Specific performance is available as a remedy in cases of anticipatory repudiation—ie, where one contracting party unequivocally indicates prior to the time for performance that he/she will not perform. (Choice D) Specific performance, like other equitable remedies, will not be granted where monetary damages are adequate. But nominal damages (eg, $1) alone are typically not an adequate remedy.","conclusion":"Answer C: Specific performance is not available as a remedy to enforce a contractual duty to perform personal services."},"questionZh":"11月15日，一名木匠与房主签订书面合同，按约定价格按照房主提供的规格亲自改造房主的厨房，并于12月1日开始工作。木匠同意提供该工作所需的所有材料以及所需的所有劳动力。 11月26日，木匠无正当理由拒签合同。经过合理和长期的努力，房主找不到任何人以接近木匠同意的价格来改造他的厨房。一年后，房主针对木匠的特定履行（specific performance）提起诉讼。以下哪一项将为木匠提供最好的防御？","choicesZh":{"A":"未在合理时间内提出的公平救济诉讼将因疏忽而被禁止。","B":"在发生预期拒付的情况下，通常无法使用特定履行作为补救措施。","C":"具体履行不能作为执行个人服务合同义务的补救措施。","D":"即使是名义上的损害也可以作为法律补救措施予以追偿，但具体履行不能作为补救措施。"},"explanationZh":"个人服务合同\n\n定义 涉及当事人个人品味、技能或才干的合同——例如演艺人员、设计师、律师、建筑师 执行 不违约方有权获得因违约而产生的补偿性损害赔偿 无法获得具体履行 具体履行是一种公平的补救措施，当金钱损害赔偿不足时，法院可命令违约方履行合同义务——例如，违反土地出售合同。然而，这种补救措施不适用于执行提供个人服务的合同义务，因为法院不愿意在发生争议后强制个人协会。这里，合同规定木匠要亲自改造房主的厨房。当木匠拒绝履行合同时，房主提起了具体履行诉讼。因此，木匠最好的辩护是，这种补救措施不适用于违反个人服务合同的情况。 （选项 A） 对懈怠的公平辩护通常在具体绩效诉讼中主张。为了赢得这一抗辩，违约方必须证明执行方无理拖延提出索赔已导致损害，即大幅增加了履约成本或难度。在这里，房主在寻找价格相近的木匠时合理地推迟了诉讼，并且没有表现出对木匠的偏见。 （选择 B）特定履行可作为预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的情况下的一种补救措施，即一方当事人在履行之前明确表示他/她不会履行。 （选择 D）在金钱赔偿足够的情况下，不会给予特定履约，如其他衡平法补救措施。但仅名义损害赔偿（例如 1 美元）通常不足以作为补救措施。\n\n教育目标：特定履行（即法院命令履行合同义务）不能作为执行个人服务合同义务的补救措施。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 367（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了具体履行不可用于执行个人合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"具体履行（即法院命令履行合同义务）不能作为执行个人服务合同义务的补救措施。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项将为木匠提供最好的防御？","rule":"具体履行（即法院命令履行合同义务）不能作为执行个人服务合同义务的补救措施。","analysis":"个人服务合同\n\n定义 涉及当事人个人品味、技能或才干的合同——例如演艺人员、设计师、律师、建筑师 执行 不违约方有权获得因违约而产生的补偿性损害赔偿 无法获得具体履行 具体履行是一种公平的补救措施，当金钱损害赔偿不足时，法院可命令违约方履行合同义务——例如，违反土地出售合同。然而，这种补救措施不适用于执行提供个人服务的合同义务，因为法院不愿意在发生争议后强制个人协会。这里，合同规定木匠要亲自改造房主的厨房。当木匠拒绝履行合同时，房主提起了具体履行诉讼。因此，木匠最好的辩护是，这种补救措施不适用于违反个人服务合同的情况。 （选项 A） 对懈怠的公平辩护通常在具体绩效诉讼中主张。为了赢得这一抗辩，违约方必须证明执行方无理拖延提出索赔已导致损害，即大幅增加了履约成本或难度。在这里，房主在寻找价格相近的木匠时合理地推迟了诉讼，并且没有表现出对木匠的偏见。 （选择 B）特定履行可作为预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的情况下的一种补救措施，即一方当事人在履行之前明确表示他/她不会履行。 （选择 D）在金钱赔偿足够的情况下，不会给予特定履约，如其他衡平法补救措施。但仅名义损害赔偿（例如 1 美元）通常不足以作为补救措施。\n\n教育目标：特定履行（即法院命令履行合同义务）不能作为执行个人服务合同义务的补救措施。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 367（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了具体履行不可用于执行个人合同）。","conclusion":"答案 C：具体履行不能作为执行个人服务合同义务的补救措施。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":190,"sourceQuestionPage":562,"sourceQuestionPages":[562],"sourceExplanationPages":[563,564],"sourceAnswerPages":[563,564],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-191","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A life coach entered into a contract with an introverted woman to provide a weeklong course that served as an introduction to the life coach's yearlong program. Under the terms of the contract, the life coach would work with the woman full-time for a week to start teaching her to interact with people with more confidence. The woman would then participate in a speed-dating event as her \"graduation\" from the course. The contract also provided that unless the woman received an offer for a second date at the speed-dating event, she had no obligation to pay for the introductory course. If she received an offer for a second date at the speed-dating event, she would be required to pay the life coach $1,000 within three days of the event. After working with the life coach pursuant to the terms of the contract, the woman attended the speed-dating event. Four days after the event, when the woman still had not paid the life coach, the life coach brought a breach-of-contract action against the woman to recover $1,000 under the contract. Which of the following best states the burden of proof that will apply in this case?","choices":{"A":"Because the contract involved a condition precedent, the life coach must prove that the woman received an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event to recover.","B":"Because the contract involved a condition precedent, the woman must prove that she did not receive an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event to avoid liability.","C":"Because the contract involved a condition subsequent, the life coach must prove that the woman received an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event to recover.","D":"Because the contract involved a condition subsequent, the woman must prove that she did not receive an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event to avoid liability."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Performance is generally due once a contract is formed—eg, when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration. However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail\n\nCondition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability\n\nHere, the contract provided that the woman would be required to pay the life coach for the introductory course only if she received an offer for a second date at the speed-dating event. This created a condition precedent that delayed the woman's obligation to make that payment (Choices C & D). Therefore, the life coach must prove that the woman received an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event (ie, that the specified event occurred) to recover in his breach-of-contract action (Choice B). *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously).\n\nEducational objective: A condition precedent delays performance until a specified event occurs and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail. Conversely, a condition subsequent excuses performance once a specified event occurs and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–30 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions related to performance).","educationalObjective":"A condition precedent delays performance until a specified event occurs and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail. Conversely, a condition subsequent excuses performance once a specified event occurs and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–30 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions related to performance).","rule":"A condition precedent delays performance until a specified event occurs and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail. Conversely, a condition subsequent excuses performance once a specified event occurs and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following best states the burden of proof that will apply in this case?","rule":"A condition precedent delays performance until a specified event occurs and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail. Conversely, a condition subsequent excuses performance once a specified event occurs and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability.","analysis":"Performance is generally due once a contract is formed—eg, when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration. However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail\n\nCondition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability\n\nHere, the contract provided that the woman would be required to pay the life coach for the introductory course only if she received an offer for a second date at the speed-dating event. This created a condition precedent that delayed the woman's obligation to make that payment (Choices C & D). Therefore, the life coach must prove that the woman received an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event (ie, that the specified event occurred) to recover in his breach-of-contract action (Choice B). *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously).","conclusion":"Answer A: Because the contract involved a condition precedent, the life coach must prove that the woman received an offer of a second date at the speed-dating event to recover."},"questionZh":"一位生活教练与一位性格内向的女性签订了一份合同，提供为期一周的课程，作为生活教练为期一年的计划的介绍。根据合同条款，生活教练将与该女子全职工作一周，开始教她如何更自信地与人互动。然后，这位女士将参加快速约会活动，作为她从课程中“毕业”的机会。合同还规定，除非该女子在快速约会活动中收到第二次约会的邀请，否则她没有义务支付入门课程的费用。如果她在快速约会活动中收到第二次约会的邀请，她将需要在活动后三天内向生活教练支付 1,000 美元。根据合同条款与生活教练合作后，该女子参加了快速约会活动。事件发生四天后，当该女子仍未向生活教练付款时，生活教练对该女子提起违约诉讼，要求根据合同追回 1,000 美元。以下哪一项最好地说明了适用于本案的举证责任？","choicesZh":{"A":"由于合同涉及先决条件，生活教练必须证明该女子在快速约会活动中收到了第二次约会的邀请，以恢复健康。","B":"由于合同涉及先决条件，该女子必须证明她没有在快速约会活动中收到第二次约会的邀请，以避免承担责任。","C":"由于合同涉及后续条件，生活教练必须证明该女子在快速约会活动中收到了第二次约会的邀请才能康复。","D":"由于合同涉及后续条件，该女子必须证明她没有在快速约会活动中收到第二次约会的邀请，以避免承担责任。"},"explanationZh":"合同一旦形成，通常就应履行——例如，当双方达成的协议得到有价值的对价的支持时。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至指定事件发生，并要求原告证明该事件的发生优先\n\n后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生，就可以免除履行义务，并要求被告证明该事件已发生以避免承担责任\n\n此处，合同规定，只有当该女子在快速约会活动中收到第二次约会的邀请时，才需要向生活教练支付入门课程的费用。这创造了一个先决条件，延迟了妇女支付该款项的义务（选择 C ​​和 D）。因此，生活教练必须证明该女子在快速约会活动中收到了第二次约会的邀请（即发生了指定的事件），以便在他的违约行为中获得赔偿（选择B）。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。\n\n教育目标：先决条件延迟履行，直到特定事件发生，并要求原告证明该事件发生才有效。相反，一旦特定事件发生，随后的条件可以免除履行义务，并要求被告证明该事件发生以避免承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–30 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（与履行相关的条件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"先决条件会延迟履行，直到特定事件发生，并要求原告证明该事件发生才有效。相反，一旦特定事件发生，随后的条件可以免除履行义务，并要求被告证明该事件发生以避免承担责任。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一项最好地说明了适用于本案的举证责任？","rule":"先决条件会延迟履行，直到特定事件发生，并要求原告证明该事件发生才有效。相反，一旦特定事件发生，随后的条件可以免除履行义务，并要求被告证明该事件发生以避免承担责任。","analysis":"合同一旦形成，通常就应履行——例如，当双方达成的协议得到有价值的对价的支持时。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至指定事件发生，并要求原告证明该事件的发生优先\n\n后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生，就可以免除履行义务，并要求被告证明该事件已发生以避免承担责任\n\n此处，合同规定，只有当该女子在快速约会活动中收到第二次约会的邀请时，才需要向生活教练支付入门课程的费用。这创造了一个先决条件，延迟了妇女支付该款项的义务（选择 C ​​和 D）。因此，生活教练必须证明该女子在快速约会活动中收到了第二次约会的邀请（即发生了指定的事件），以便在他的违约行为中获得赔偿（选择B）。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。\n\n教育目标：先决条件延迟履行，直到特定事件发生，并要求原告证明该事件发生才有效。相反，一旦特定事件发生，随后的条件可以免除履行义务，并要求被告证明该事件发生以避免承担责任。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–30 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（与履行相关的条件）。","conclusion":"答案 A：由于合同涉及先决条件，生活教练必须证明该女子在快速约会活动中收到了第二次约会的邀请，以恢复健康。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-0a7a3927ad7f8605"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":191,"sourceQuestionPage":565,"sourceQuestionPages":[565],"sourceExplanationPages":[566,567],"sourceAnswerPages":[566,567],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-192","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A landowner entered into a single contract with a builder to have three different structures built on separate pieces of property owned by the landowner. Each structure was distinct from the other two, and the parties agreed on a specific price for each. After completing the first structure in accordance with the terms of the contract, the builder demanded payment of the specified price for that structure. At the same time, the builder told the landowner that the builder was \"tired of the construction business\" and would not even begin the other two structures. The landowner refused to pay anything to the builder. Is the builder likely to prevail in a suit for the agreed-upon price of the first structure?","choices":{"A":"No, because substantial performance is a constructive condition to the landowner's duty to pay at the contract rate.","B":"No, because the builder's cessation of performance without legal excuse was a willful breach of the contract.","C":"Yes, because the contract is divisible, and the landowner will be required to bring a separate claim for the builder's failure to complete the other two structures.","D":"Yes, because the contract is divisible, but the landowner will be able to deduct any recoverable damages caused by the builder's failure to complete the contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Divisible contracts\n\nDefinition Contract composed of multiple bargained-for exchanges that can be separately enforced Recovery after Breaching party entitled to return performance for any completed or breach substantially performed promise Nonbreaching party entitled to damages stemming from breach Divisible contracts contain two or more bargained-for exchanges, each of which can be enforced separately. Under such a contract, a party is entitled to return performance (eg, payment) for any completed or substantially performed promise. This is true even if that party breaches the other, separately enforceable agreement. However, the nonbreaching party may deduct from any payment made to the breaching party damages stemming from the breach. Here, the builder-landowner contract was divisible because it consisted of three bargained- for exchanges—ie, three distinct structures, each at a specified price—that could be enforced separately. Since the builder completed the first structure, it is entitled to the agreed-upon price for that structure despite its failure to complete the others. However, the landowner will be able to deduct any recoverable damages caused by the builder's failure to complete the contract. (Choice A) A party is entitled to return performance for completing or substantially performing a divisible part of the contract—even if that party failed to substantially perform the entire contract. (Choice B) The builder's cessation of performance without legal excuse was a willful breach of the contract. And willfulness is a factor in determining whether a breach is material—ie, significant enough to deprive the other party of the substantial benefit of the bargain. But willfulness does not affect recovery in this case. (Choice C) The nonbreaching party to a divisible contract need not bring separate actions for damages stemming from each breach. That is because it is still a single contract.\n\nEducational objective: Divisible contracts contain two or more bargained-for exchanges that can be separately enforced. Under a divisible contract, a party is entitled to return performance for a completed or substantially performed promise—even if that party breaches another part of the contract.","educationalObjective":"Divisible contracts contain two or more bargained-for exchanges that can be separately enforced. Under a divisible contract, a party is entitled to return performance for a completed or substantially performed promise—even if that party breaches another part of the contract.","references":"","rule":"Divisible contracts contain two or more bargained-for exchanges that can be separately enforced. Under a divisible contract, a party is entitled to return performance for a completed or substantially performed promise—even if that party breaches another part of the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Is the builder likely to prevail in a suit for the agreed-upon price of the first structure?","rule":"Divisible contracts contain two or more bargained-for exchanges that can be separately enforced. Under a divisible contract, a party is entitled to return performance for a completed or substantially performed promise—even if that party breaches another part of the contract.","analysis":"Divisible contracts\n\nDefinition Contract composed of multiple bargained-for exchanges that can be separately enforced Recovery after Breaching party entitled to return performance for any completed or breach substantially performed promise Nonbreaching party entitled to damages stemming from breach Divisible contracts contain two or more bargained-for exchanges, each of which can be enforced separately. Under such a contract, a party is entitled to return performance (eg, payment) for any completed or substantially performed promise. This is true even if that party breaches the other, separately enforceable agreement. However, the nonbreaching party may deduct from any payment made to the breaching party damages stemming from the breach. Here, the builder-landowner contract was divisible because it consisted of three bargained- for exchanges—ie, three distinct structures, each at a specified price—that could be enforced separately. Since the builder completed the first structure, it is entitled to the agreed-upon price for that structure despite its failure to complete the others. However, the landowner will be able to deduct any recoverable damages caused by the builder's failure to complete the contract. (Choice A) A party is entitled to return performance for completing or substantially performing a divisible part of the contract—even if that party failed to substantially perform the entire contract. (Choice B) The builder's cessation of performance without legal excuse was a willful breach of the contract. And willfulness is a factor in determining whether a breach is material—ie, significant enough to deprive the other party of the substantial benefit of the bargain. But willfulness does not affect recovery in this case. (Choice C) The nonbreaching party to a divisible contract need not bring separate actions for damages stemming from each breach. That is because it is still a single contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the contract is divisible, but the landowner will be able to deduct any recoverable damages caused by the builder's failure to complete the contract."},"questionZh":"土地所有者与建筑商签订了一份合同，在土地所有者拥有的不同财产上建造三座不同的建筑物。每种结构都与其他两种不同，双方商定了每种结构的具体价格。根据合同条款完成第一个结构后，建筑商要求支付该结构的指定价格。与此同时，建筑商告诉土地所有者，建筑​​商“厌倦了建筑业务”，甚至不会开始另外两座建筑。地主拒绝向建筑商支付任何费用。建筑商是否有可能在针对第一座建筑的商定价格的诉讼中获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为实质性履约是土地所有者按合同费率付款的义务的推定条件。","B":"不可以，因为建筑商在没有合法理由的情况下停止履行合同是故意违约。","C":"是的，因为合同是可分割的，土地所有者将被要求就建筑商未能完成其他两座建筑提出单独的索赔。","D":"是的，因为合同是可分割的，但土地所有者将能够扣除因建筑商未能完成合同而造成的任何可恢复的损失。"},"explanationZh":"可分割合同\n\n定义 由多个可单独执行的议价交换组成的合同 违约方有权就任何已完成或实质上履行的承诺返还履约 违约方有权因违约而获得损害赔偿 可分割的合同包含两个或多个议价交换，每一个都可以单独执行。根据此类合同，一方有权返还任何已完成或实质履行的承诺的履行（例如付款）。即使该方违反了另一方单独可执行的协议，也是如此。然而，非违约方可以从向违约方支付的任何款项中扣除因违约而造成的损失。在这里，建筑商-土地所有者合同是可分割的，因为它由三个讨价还价的交换组成——即三个不同的结构，每个结构都有一个特定的价格——可以单独执行。由于建筑商完成了第一个结构，因此尽管未能完成其他结构，但它有权获得该结构的商定价格。然而，土地所有者将能够扣除因建筑商未能完成合同而造成的任何可恢复的损失。 （选择 A）一方有权返还因完成或实质履行合同的可分割部分而导致的履约行为——即使该方未能实质履行整个合同。 （选项B） 建筑商无正当理由停止履行合同，构成故意违约。故意是确定违约行为是否重大的一个因素，即是否严重到足以剥夺另一方从交易中获得的实质性利益。但本例中的任性并不影响康复。 （选择 C）可分割合同的非违约方无需针对每次违约造成的损失单独提起诉讼。这是因为它仍然是单一合同。\n\n教育目标：可分割合同包含两个或多个可以单独执行的讨价还价交换。根据可分割合同，一方有权返还已完成或实质履行的承诺，即使该方违反了合同的另一部分。","educationalObjectiveZh":"可分割的合同包含两个或多个可以单独执行的议价交换。根据可分割合同，一方有权返还已完成或实质履行的承诺，即使该方违反了合同的另一部分。","iracZh":{"issue":"建筑商是否有可能在针对第一座建筑的商定价格的诉讼中获胜？","rule":"可分割的合同包含两个或多个可以单独执行的议价交换。根据可分割合同，一方有权返还已完成或实质履行的承诺，即使该方违反了合同的另一部分。","analysis":"可分割合同\n\n定义 由多个可单独执行的议价交换组成的合同 违约方有权就任何已完成或实质上履行的承诺返还履约 违约方有权因违约而获得损害赔偿 可分割的合同包含两个或多个议价交换，每一个都可以单独执行。根据此类合同，一方有权返还任何已完成或实质履行的承诺的履行（例如付款）。即使该方违反了另一方单独可执行的协议，也是如此。然而，非违约方可以从向违约方支付的任何款项中扣除因违约而造成的损失。在这里，建筑商-土地所有者合同是可分割的，因为它由三个讨价还价的交换组成——即三个不同的结构，每个结构都有一个特定的价格——可以单独执行。由于建筑商完成了第一个结构，因此尽管未能完成其他结构，但它有权获得该结构的商定价格。然而，土地所有者将能够扣除因建筑商未能完成合同而造成的任何可恢复的损失。 （选择 A）一方有权返还因完成或实质履行合同的可分割部分而导致的履约行为——即使该方未能实质履行整个合同。 （选项B） 建筑商无正当理由停止履行合同，构成故意违约。故意是确定违约行为是否重大的一个因素，即是否严重到足以剥夺另一方从交易中获得的实质性利益。但本例中的任性并不影响康复。 （选择 C）可分割合同的非违约方无需针对每次违约造成的损失单独提起诉讼。这是因为它仍然是单一合同。\n\n教育目标：可分割合同包含两个或多个可以单独执行的讨价还价交换。根据可分割合同，一方有权返还已完成或实质履行的承诺，即使该方违反了合同的另一部分。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为合同是可分割的，但土地所有者将能够扣除因建筑商未能完成合同而造成的任何可恢复的损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":192,"sourceQuestionPage":568,"sourceQuestionPages":[568],"sourceExplanationPages":[569,570],"sourceAnswerPages":[569,570],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-193","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"On April 1, a carpenter contracted with a homeowner to remodel the homeowner's home for $10,000, the contract price to be paid on completion of the work. On May 29, relying on his expectation that he would finish the work and have the homeowner's payment on June 1, the carpenter contracted to buy a car under the following terms: \"$10,000 in cash, if payment is made on June 1; if payment is made thereafter, the price is $12,000.\" The carpenter completed the work according to specifications on June 1 and demanded payment from the homeowner on that date. The homeowner, without any excuse, refused to pay. As a result, the carpenter became very excited, suffered a minor heart attack, and incurred related medical expenses of $4,000. The reasonable value of the carpenter's services in remodeling the homeowner's home was $13,000. In an action by the carpenter against the homeowner, which of the following should be the carpenter's measure of recovery?","choices":{"A":"$10,000, the contract price.","B":"$12,000, the contract price plus $2,000, the bargain that was lost because the carpenter could not pay cash for the car on June 1.","C":"$13,000, the amount the homeowner was enriched by the carpenter's services.","D":"$14,000, the contract price plus $4,000 for the medical expenses incurred because the homeowner refused to pay."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach The primary goal of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position it would have been in had the contract been performed. This includes expectation damages for the value of the breaching party's performance that was lost (eg, the contract price). But it only includes consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances if the breaching party: knew about the nonbreaching party's special circumstances or could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the nonbreaching party's special circumstances, that would result from a breach. Here, the homeowner refused to pay after the carpenter completed all the contractual work. As a result, the carpenter can recover the $10,000 contract price for the work done (expectation damages). But the carpenter cannot recover $2,000 for the loss of the bargain on the car or $4,000 for the medical expenses incurred (consequential damages). That is because those losses were an unforeseeable result of the breach (Choices B & D). Therefore, the carpenter is entitled to $10,000. (Choice C) When a breaching party is unjustly enriched as a result of the breach, the nonbreaching party can seek restitution (ie, the value of the benefit conferred on the breaching party) as an alternative to expectation damages. However, this is not permitted when, as here, the work giving rise to the unjust-enrichment claim has been fully performed and the breaching party's only remaining obligation is payment of the contract price.\n\nEducational objective: The nonbreaching party is entitled to consequential damages for losses arising from its special circumstances only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special circumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","educationalObjective":"The nonbreaching party is entitled to consequential damages for losses arising from its special circumstances only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special circumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","references":"","rule":"The nonbreaching party is entitled to consequential damages for losses arising from its special circumstances only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special circumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the carpenter against the homeowner, which of the following should be the carpenter's measure of recovery?","rule":"The nonbreaching party is entitled to consequential damages for losses arising from its special circumstances only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special circumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","analysis":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach The primary goal of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position it would have been in had the contract been performed. This includes expectation damages for the value of the breaching party's performance that was lost (eg, the contract price). But it only includes consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances if the breaching party: knew about the nonbreaching party's special circumstances or could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the nonbreaching party's special circumstances, that would result from a breach. Here, the homeowner refused to pay after the carpenter completed all the contractual work. As a result, the carpenter can recover the $10,000 contract price for the work done (expectation damages). But the carpenter cannot recover $2,000 for the loss of the bargain on the car or $4,000 for the medical expenses incurred (consequential damages). That is because those losses were an unforeseeable result of the breach (Choices B & D). Therefore, the carpenter is entitled to $10,000. (Choice C) When a breaching party is unjustly enriched as a result of the breach, the nonbreaching party can seek restitution (ie, the value of the benefit conferred on the breaching party) as an alternative to expectation damages. However, this is not permitted when, as here, the work giving rise to the unjust-enrichment claim has been fully performed and the breaching party's only remaining obligation is payment of the contract price.","conclusion":"Answer A: $10,000, the contract price."},"questionZh":"4 月 1 日，一名木匠与一位房主签订合同，以 10,000 美元的价格改造房主的房屋，合同价格在工程完成后支付。 5月29日，木匠期望能在6月1日完成工作并收到房主付款，于是签订了购买汽车的合同，条款如下：“如果在6月1日付款，则现金10,000美元；如果此后付款，价格为12,000美元。”木匠于6月1日按照规格完成了工作，并要求房主当天付款。房主无故拒绝付款。结果，木匠变得非常激动，出现了轻微的心脏病，并支付了4000美元的相关医疗费用。木匠改造房主房屋的服务合理价值为 13,000 美元。在木匠对房主提起的诉讼中，木匠应采取以下哪项赔偿措施？","choicesZh":{"A":"合同价10,000美元。","B":"12,000 美元，合同价加上 2,000 美元，因为木匠无法在 6 月 1 日支付现金购买汽车，所以损失了这笔交易。","C":"13,000 美元，房主因木匠的服务而致富。","D":"14,000美元，合同价加上4,000美元因房主拒绝支付而产生的医疗费用。"},"explanationZh":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 合同损害赔偿的主要目的是使非违约方处于与履行合同时相同的境地。这包括对违约方履约损失价值（例如合同价格）的预期损害赔偿。但它仅包括因守约方的特殊情况而造成的损失的间接损害，如果违约方：知道守约方的特殊情况，或者能够根据守约方的特殊情况合理预见违约将造成的损害。在这里，木匠完成所有合同工作后，房主拒绝付款。结果，木匠可以收回已完成工作的 10,000 美元合同价（预期损失）。但木匠无法就汽车讨价还价的损失获得 2,000 美元的赔偿，也无法就所产生的医疗费用（间接损失）获得 4,000 美元的赔偿。这是因为这些损失是违规造成的不可预见的结果（Choices B & D）。因此，木匠有权获得 10,000 美元。 （选择C）当违约方因违约而获得不公正的利益时，守约方可以寻求赔偿（即授予违约方的利益的价值）作为预期损害的替代方案。然而，当引起不当得利索赔的工作已经完全完成并且违约方唯一剩余的义务是支付合同价款时，这是不允许的。\n\n教育目的：只有在违约方（1）了解特殊情况或（2）能够根据特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害的情况下，守约方才有权就其特殊情况造成的损失获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有在违约方（1）知道特殊情况或（2）能够根据特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害的情况下，守约方才有权就其特殊情况造成的损失获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。","iracZh":{"issue":"在木匠对房主提起的诉讼中，木匠应采取以下哪项赔偿措施？","rule":"只有在违约方（1）知道特殊情况或（2）能够根据特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害的情况下，守约方才有权就其特殊情况造成的损失获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。","analysis":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 合同损害赔偿的主要目的是使非违约方处于与履行合同时相同的境地。这包括对违约方履约损失价值（例如合同价格）的预期损害赔偿。但它仅包括因守约方的特殊情况而造成的损失的间接损害，如果违约方：知道守约方的特殊情况，或者能够根据守约方的特殊情况合理预见违约将造成的损害。在这里，木匠完成所有合同工作后，房主拒绝付款。结果，木匠可以收回已完成工作的 10,000 美元合同价（预期损失）。但木匠无法就汽车讨价还价的损失获得 2,000 美元的赔偿，也无法就所产生的医疗费用（间接损失）获得 4,000 美元的赔偿。这是因为这些损失是违规造成的不可预见的结果（Choices B & D）。因此，木匠有权获得 10,000 美元。 （选择C）当违约方因违约而获得不公正的利益时，守约方可以寻求赔偿（即授予违约方的利益的价值）作为预期损害的替代方案。然而，当引起不当得利索赔的工作已经完全完成并且违约方唯一剩余的义务是支付合同价款时，这是不允许的。\n\n教育目的：只有在违约方（1）了解特殊情况或（2）能够根据特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害的情况下，守约方才有权就其特殊情况造成的损失获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。","conclusion":"答案 A：合同价10,000美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":193,"sourceQuestionPage":571,"sourceQuestionPages":[571],"sourceExplanationPages":[572,573],"sourceAnswerPages":[572,573],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-194","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A brick mason was hired by a builder under a written one-year contract, at an annual salary of $45,000, with employment to begin on March 1. Because the builder was unable to secure enough building contracts to keep all its employees busy during the season beginning March 1, it notified the brick mason on February 15 that it could not afford to employ him as a mason. At the same time, however, the builder offered to employ the mason, for the same contract period, as a night guard at an annual salary of $25,000. The mason declined the offer and remained unemployed during the year. No employment for brick masons was available in the community during the year, but the mason could have obtained other employment as a day laborer that would have paid up to $25,000 a year. At the end of the year, in an action against the builder for breach of contract, how much, if anything, is the mason entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"$20,000 (the $45,000 contract price less the $25,000 the mason could have earned in other employment).","B":"$45,000 (the contract price).","C":"Nothing, because the builder did not act in bad faith when it discharged the mason.","D":"Nothing, because the mason did not mitigate his damages."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution A nonbreaching party's remedies for breach include compensatory damages—ie, a sum of money that would put that party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. Therefore, when an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee is entitled to recover the unpaid salary under the contract. The employee's total recovery will be reduced, however, by compensation the employee earned or could have earned had he/she made reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment (ie, mitigate damages). Here, the builder breached the employment contract before the brick mason's employment began. As a result, the mason can seek compensatory damages for his unpaid salary under the contract. And since there was no masonry work available in the community for the one- year contract period, the mason could not reasonably mitigate his damages by securing comparable employment. Therefore, the mason is entitled to recover the full $45,000 contract price (Choices A & D). (Choice C) A lack of bad faith (ie, dishonesty or improper motive) does not excuse a party from paying damages after breaching a contract.\n\nEducational objective: When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the employee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","educationalObjective":"When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the employee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","references":"","rule":"When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the employee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","irac":{"issue":"At the end of the year, in an action against the builder for breach of contract, how much, if anything, is the mason entitled to recover?","rule":"When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the employee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","analysis":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution A nonbreaching party's remedies for breach include compensatory damages—ie, a sum of money that would put that party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. Therefore, when an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee is entitled to recover the unpaid salary under the contract. The employee's total recovery will be reduced, however, by compensation the employee earned or could have earned had he/she made reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment (ie, mitigate damages). Here, the builder breached the employment contract before the brick mason's employment began. As a result, the mason can seek compensatory damages for his unpaid salary under the contract. And since there was no masonry work available in the community for the one- year contract period, the mason could not reasonably mitigate his damages by securing comparable employment. Therefore, the mason is entitled to recover the full $45,000 contract price (Choices A & D). (Choice C) A lack of bad faith (ie, dishonesty or improper motive) does not excuse a party from paying damages after breaching a contract.","conclusion":"Answer B: $45,000 (the contract price)."},"questionZh":"一名砖瓦匠被一家建筑商聘用，签订了一份为期一年的书面合同，年薪为 45,000 美元，从 3 月 1 日开始受雇。由于建筑商无法获得足够的建筑合同，无法让所有员工在 3 月 1 日开始的季节里保持忙碌，因此它于 2 月 15 日通知砖瓦匠，无法雇用他作为泥瓦匠。然而，与此同时，建筑商提出在相同的合同期限内雇用这名石匠担任夜间警卫，年薪为 25,000 美元。石匠拒绝了这个提议，并在这一年中一直处于失业状态。这一年，社区没有砖瓦匠的就业机会，但砖瓦匠可以找到其他工作，作为日工，年薪高达 25,000 美元。年底时，在针对建筑商违约的诉讼中，泥瓦匠有权获得多少赔偿（如果有的话）？","choicesZh":{"A":"20,000 美元（45,000 美元的合同价格减去泥瓦匠在其他工作中可以赚取的 25,000 美元）。","B":"45,000 美元（合同价）。","C":"没什么，因为建筑商在解雇石匠时并没有恶意。","D":"没什么，因为石匠没有减轻他的损失。"},"explanationZh":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将非违约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（预期 预期措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 后退 当预期措施过于投机时，将非违约方置于与未签订合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约赔偿 恢复原状 守约方对违约行为的补救措施包括补偿性损害赔偿，即一笔金钱这将使该方处于与合同已履行相同的境地。因此，当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者有权追偿合同项下未支付的工资。然而，雇员的总康复将减少，因为如果雇员做出合理努力以获得类似的就业机会（即减轻损害），他/她可以获得或本可以获得的补偿。在此，建筑商在砖瓦匠开始受雇之前就违反了雇佣合同。因此，石匠可以根据合同要求赔偿其未支付的工资。而且，由于在一年的合同期内，社区中没有石工工作，石匠无法通过获得类似的就业机会来合理地减轻自己的损失。因此，石匠有权收回 45,000 美元的全部合同价（选择 A 和 D）。 （选项C） 缺乏恶意（即不诚实或不正当动机）并不能成为一方在违约后支付损害赔偿金的借口。\n\n教育目的：当雇主违反雇佣协议时，雇员可以根据合同追讨未支付的工资。这种恢复会因雇员通过合理努力获得类似就业而获得或本可以获得的补偿而减少。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者可以根据合同追偿未支付的工资。这种恢复会因雇员通过合理努力获得类似就业而获得或本可以获得的补偿而减少。","iracZh":{"issue":"年底时，在针对建筑商违约的诉讼中，泥瓦匠有权获得多少赔偿（如果有的话）？","rule":"当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者可以根据合同追偿未支付的工资。这种恢复会因雇员通过合理努力获得类似就业而获得或本可以获得的补偿而减少。","analysis":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将非违约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（预期 预期措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 后退 当预期措施过于投机时，将非违约方置于与未签订合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约赔偿 恢复原状 守约方对违约行为的补救措施包括补偿性损害赔偿，即一笔金钱这将使该方处于与合同已履行相同的境地。因此，当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者有权追偿合同项下未支付的工资。然而，雇员的总康复将减少，因为如果雇员做出合理努力以获得类似的就业机会（即减轻损害），他/她可以获得或本可以获得的补偿。在此，建筑商在砖瓦匠开始受雇之前就违反了雇佣合同。因此，石匠可以根据合同要求赔偿其未支付的工资。而且，由于在一年的合同期内，社区中没有石工工作，石匠无法通过获得类似的就业机会来合理地减轻自己的损失。因此，石匠有权收回 45,000 美元的全部合同价（选择 A 和 D）。 （选项C） 缺乏恶意（即不诚实或不正当动机）并不能成为一方在违约后支付损害赔偿金的借口。\n\n教育目的：当雇主违反雇佣协议时，雇员可以根据合同追讨未支付的工资。这种恢复会因雇员通过合理努力获得类似就业而获得或本可以获得的补偿而减少。","conclusion":"答案 B：45,000 美元（合同价）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"incidental damages","ipa":"/ˌɪnsɪdˈɛntəl dˈæmədʒəz/","syllables":"in·ci·den·tal dam·ages","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"附随损害赔偿：处理违约所合理发生的附带费用","contextualMeaning":"附随损害赔偿：处理违约所合理发生的附带费用","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Purpose of compensatory damages Primary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages L","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":194,"sourceQuestionPage":574,"sourceQuestionPages":[574],"sourceExplanationPages":[575,576],"sourceAnswerPages":[575,576],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-195","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"On June 1, a seller and a buyer contracted in writing for the sale and purchase of the seller's cattle ranch (a large single tract), and to close the transaction on December 1. On October 1, the buyer told the seller, \"I'm increasingly unhappy about our June 1 contract because of the current cattle market, and do not intend to buy your ranch unless I'm legally obligated to do so.\" On October 15, the seller sued the buyer for breach of contract. Is the seller likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer's October 1 statement to the seller was neither a repudiation nor a present breach of the June 1 contract.","B":"No, because the parties contracted for the sale and conveyance of a single tract, and the seller cannot bring suit for breach of such a contract prior to the agreed closing date.","C":"Yes, because the buyer committed a total breach by anticipatory repudiation on October 1.","D":"Yes, because the buyer's October 1 statement created reasonable grounds for the seller's insecurity with respect to the buyer's performance."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Anticipatory repudiation\n\nStandard Party's clear & unequivocal words or actions indicate unwillingness to perform\n\nRetraction Party may retract repudiation unless nonrepudiating party:\n\ncancels contract materially changes position in reliance thereon or indicates repudiation is final Effect Upon repudiation, nonrepudiating party may:*\n\ntreat repudiation as breach or ignore repudiation & demand performance *Nonrepudiating party may also suspend own performance\n\nA party's failure to perform his/her contractual duties at the time performance is due constitutes a breach. A breach can also occur before the time for performance arises or elapses under the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation. This doctrine applies when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, indicates an unwillingness to perform his/her contractual duties. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the buyer's October 1 statement was not a present breach of the contract since performance is not due until December 1. And though the buyer stated that she did \"not intend to buy\" the seller's ranch, that statement was not an anticipatory repudiation. That is because the statement included the equivocal phrase \"unless I'm legally obligated to do so\" (Choice C). Therefore, the seller is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation allows a party to sue for breach of any contract before the time for performance is due—including contracts for the sale of a single tract of property before the closing date. (Choice D) The buyer's statement created reasonable grounds for the seller's insecurity with respect to the buyer's performance. However, the seller's recourse in this situation is to demand adequate assurances. If the buyer fails to provide such assurances, the buyer will be deemed to have anticipatorily repudiated the contract. The seller can then treat the repudiation as a breach.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally indicates an unwillingness to perform. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 250 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally indicates an unwillingness to perform. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 250 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally indicates an unwillingness to perform. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Is the seller likely to prevail?","rule":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally indicates an unwillingness to perform. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.","analysis":"Anticipatory repudiation\n\nStandard Party's clear & unequivocal words or actions indicate unwillingness to perform\n\nRetraction Party may retract repudiation unless nonrepudiating party:\n\ncancels contract materially changes position in reliance thereon or indicates repudiation is final Effect Upon repudiation, nonrepudiating party may:*\n\ntreat repudiation as breach or ignore repudiation & demand performance *Nonrepudiating party may also suspend own performance\n\nA party's failure to perform his/her contractual duties at the time performance is due constitutes a breach. A breach can also occur before the time for performance arises or elapses under the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation. This doctrine applies when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, indicates an unwillingness to perform his/her contractual duties. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the buyer's October 1 statement was not a present breach of the contract since performance is not due until December 1. And though the buyer stated that she did \"not intend to buy\" the seller's ranch, that statement was not an anticipatory repudiation. That is because the statement included the equivocal phrase \"unless I'm legally obligated to do so\" (Choice C). Therefore, the seller is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation allows a party to sue for breach of any contract before the time for performance is due—including contracts for the sale of a single tract of property before the closing date. (Choice D) The buyer's statement created reasonable grounds for the seller's insecurity with respect to the buyer's performance. However, the seller's recourse in this situation is to demand adequate assurances. If the buyer fails to provide such assurances, the buyer will be deemed to have anticipatorily repudiated the contract. The seller can then treat the repudiation as a breach.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the buyer's October 1 statement to the seller was neither a repudiation nor a present breach of the June 1 contract."},"questionZh":"6 月 1 日，卖方和买方就卖方的养牛牧场（一大片单一土地）的买卖签订了书面合同，并于 12 月 1 日完成交易。10 月 1 日，买方告诉卖方，“由于当前的养牛市场，我对 6 月 1 日的合同越来越不满意，并且不打算购买您的牧场，除非我有法律义务这样做。” 10月15日，卖方起诉买方违约。卖家有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为买方 10 月 1 日向卖方发出的声明既不是否认，也不是当前违反 6 月 1 日的合同。","B":"不可以，因为双方签订的是单张土地的销售和转让合同，卖方不能在约定的交割日期之前就违反此类合同提起诉讼。","C":"是的，因为买方在 10 月 1 日因预期拒付而完全违约。","D":"是的，因为买方 10 月 1 日的声明为卖方对买方表现的不安全感创造了合理的理由。"},"explanationZh":"预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）\n\n标准方明确无误的言行表明不愿意履行\n\n毁约方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，除非非毁约方：\n\n取消合同实质上改变了对合同的信赖状况或表明否认是最终的效果一旦否认，非毁约方可以：*\n\n将否认视为违约或忽略否认并要求履行*非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行\n\n一方未能在到期时履行合同义务即构成违约。根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，违约也可能发生在履行时间出现或过去之前。当一方通过言语或行为明确无误地表明不愿意履行其合同义务时，适用该原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。在这里，买方 10 月 1 日的声明并不构成当前违约，因为履约期要到 12 月 1 日。尽管买方表示她“不打算购买”卖方的牧场，但该声明并不是预期的违约。这是因为该声明包含了模棱两可的短语“除非我有法律义务这样做”（选项 C）。因此，卖方获胜的可能性不大。 （选择B）预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则允许一方当事人在履行期限到期之前起诉违反任何合同的行为，包括在交割日期之前出售单块财产的合同。 （选项 D） 买方的陈述为卖方对买方履约行为的不安全感提供了合理的理由。然而，在这种情况下，卖方的诉求是要求充分的保证。如果买方未能提供此类保证，则买方将被视为预先拒绝履行合同。然后，卖方可以将拒绝视为违约。\n\n教育目标：当缔约方明确且毫不含糊地表明不愿意履约时，预期毁约原则适用。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 250（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（否认）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当缔约方明确且毫不含糊地表明不愿意履约时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"卖家有可能获胜吗？","rule":"当缔约方明确且毫不含糊地表明不愿意履约时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。","analysis":"预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）\n\n标准方明确无误的言行表明不愿意履行\n\n毁约方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，除非非毁约方：\n\n取消合同实质上改变了对合同的信赖状况或表明否认是最终的效果一旦否认，非毁约方可以：*\n\n将否认视为违约或忽略否认并要求履行*非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行\n\n一方未能在到期时履行合同义务即构成违约。根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，违约也可能发生在履行时间出现或过去之前。当一方通过言语或行为明确无误地表明不愿意履行其合同义务时，适用该原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。在这里，买方 10 月 1 日的声明并不构成当前违约，因为履约期要到 12 月 1 日。尽管买方表示她“不打算购买”卖方的牧场，但该声明并不是预期的违约。这是因为该声明包含了模棱两可的短语“除非我有法律义务这样做”（选项 C）。因此，卖方获胜的可能性不大。 （选择B）预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则允许一方当事人在履行期限到期之前起诉违反任何合同的行为，包括在交割日期之前出售单块财产的合同。 （选项 D） 买方的陈述为卖方对买方履约行为的不安全感提供了合理的理由。然而，在这种情况下，卖方的诉求是要求充分的保证。如果买方未能提供此类保证，则买方将被视为预先拒绝履行合同。然后，卖方可以将拒绝视为违约。\n\n教育目标：当缔约方明确且毫不含糊地表明不愿意履约时，预期毁约原则适用。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 250（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（否认）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为买方 10 月 1 日向卖方发出的声明既不是否认，也不是当前违反 6 月 1 日的合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":195,"sourceQuestionPage":577,"sourceQuestionPages":[577],"sourceExplanationPages":[578,579],"sourceAnswerPages":[578,579],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-196","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A man and a woman were the parents of an adult son. The son aspired to become a successful music producer but was having difficulty balancing his full-time job with his musical aspirations. As a result, the man and woman agreed in a signed writing that on the last day of January and each succeeding month, each would give the son $3,000 to cover his living expenses and allow him the freedom to pursue his dream. According to the signed writing, the payments to the son were to continue until the son was named \"Music Producer of the Year\" at a national music awards show. The man and the woman made the agreed payments in January, February, and March. In April, however, fed up with the son's lack of progress, the man refused to make any payment and notified the woman and the son that he would make no further payments. Assume that there is a valid contract between the man and woman and that the son has declined to sue the man. Will the woman succeed in an action against the man in which she asks the court to order the man to continue to make his payments to the son under the terms of the contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because a court will not grant specific performance of a promise to pay money.","B":"No, because the man's breach of contract has caused no economic harm to the woman.","C":"Yes, because the woman's burden of supporting her son will be increased if the man does not contribute his share.","D":"Yes, because the woman's remedy at law is inadequate."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Third-party beneficiary contract enforcement\n\n(promisee v. promisor)\n\nType of Cause of action Remedies beneficiary\n\nDonee Promisee (ie, party wishing to make gift of Promisee entitled to: performance) has cause of action for (recipient of promisor's nonperformance actual damages (rare) gift) nominal damages OR specific performance if other remedies inadequate Creditor Promisee (ie, party wishing to satisfy debt Promisee entitled to: to beneficiary) has cause of action for (satisfaction promisor's nonperformance reimbursement in amount of of debt) debt (if promisee paid debt) OR specific performance (if promisee has not paid debt) A party to a contract with an intended beneficiary can sue the other party for breach of that contract. But in some cases, the party's remedy at law (ie, monetary damages) is inadequate. For example, when the breaching party's performance is a gift to the intended beneficiary, the nonbreaching party typically cannot show actual damages and can therefore recover only nominal damages (eg, $1). In that situation, the court may order specific performance—ie, an equitable remedy whereby the court orders the breaching party to perform. Here, the man and the woman agreed that each would give $3,000 per month to their adult son—the intended beneficiary of the contract. The man breached that contract when he prematurely stopped making monthly payments. Because the man's performance was a gift to the son, the woman is unlikely to prove actual damages. Additionally, nominal damages would be inadequate to protect her interests under the contract (ie, giving the son financial freedom). Therefore, she will succeed in her action for specific performance (Choice A). (Choice B) The woman need not prove that she suffered economic harm for the court to grant her specific performance. She need only show that money damages are inadequate to protect her interests. (Choice C) The woman's burden of supporting her son may increase if the man does not contribute his share (eg, if she pays his share). But this is no basis for granting her specific performance. Instead, a finding that her legal remedy is inadequate is required.\n\nEducational objective: Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby the court orders the breaching party to perform a contractual duty. This remedy is appropriate when the nonbreaching party's remedy at law (ie, money damages) is inadequate—eg, in a suit between parties to a contract that gives a gift to an intended beneficiary.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 357 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (specific performance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby the court orders the breaching party to perform a contractual duty. This remedy is appropriate when the nonbreaching party's remedy at law (ie, money damages) is inadequate—eg, in a suit between parties to a contract that gives a gift to an intended beneficiary.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 357 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (specific performance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby the court orders the breaching party to perform a contractual duty. This remedy is appropriate when the nonbreaching party's remedy at law (ie, money damages) is inadequate—eg, in a suit between parties to a contract that gives a gift to an intended beneficiary.","irac":{"issue":"Will the woman succeed in an action against the man in which she asks the court to order the man to continue to make his payments to the son under the terms of the contract?","rule":"Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby the court orders the breaching party to perform a contractual duty. This remedy is appropriate when the nonbreaching party's remedy at law (ie, money damages) is inadequate—eg, in a suit between parties to a contract that gives a gift to an intended beneficiary.","analysis":"Third-party beneficiary contract enforcement\n\n(promisee v. promisor)\n\nType of Cause of action Remedies beneficiary\n\nDonee Promisee (ie, party wishing to make gift of Promisee entitled to: performance) has cause of action for (recipient of promisor's nonperformance actual damages (rare) gift) nominal damages OR specific performance if other remedies inadequate Creditor Promisee (ie, party wishing to satisfy debt Promisee entitled to: to beneficiary) has cause of action for (satisfaction promisor's nonperformance reimbursement in amount of of debt) debt (if promisee paid debt) OR specific performance (if promisee has not paid debt) A party to a contract with an intended beneficiary can sue the other party for breach of that contract. But in some cases, the party's remedy at law (ie, monetary damages) is inadequate. For example, when the breaching party's performance is a gift to the intended beneficiary, the nonbreaching party typically cannot show actual damages and can therefore recover only nominal damages (eg, $1). In that situation, the court may order specific performance—ie, an equitable remedy whereby the court orders the breaching party to perform. Here, the man and the woman agreed that each would give $3,000 per month to their adult son—the intended beneficiary of the contract. The man breached that contract when he prematurely stopped making monthly payments. Because the man's performance was a gift to the son, the woman is unlikely to prove actual damages. Additionally, nominal damages would be inadequate to protect her interests under the contract (ie, giving the son financial freedom). Therefore, she will succeed in her action for specific performance (Choice A). (Choice B) The woman need not prove that she suffered economic harm for the court to grant her specific performance. She need only show that money damages are inadequate to protect her interests. (Choice C) The woman's burden of supporting her son may increase if the man does not contribute his share (eg, if she pays his share). But this is no basis for granting her specific performance. Instead, a finding that her legal remedy is inadequate is required.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the woman's remedy at law is inadequate."},"questionZh":"一男一女是一个成年儿子的父母。儿子渴望成为一名成功的音乐制作人，但很难平衡他的全职工作和他的音乐抱负。结果，这对男女在一份签名书面上同意，在一月的最后一天以及随后的每个月，每人将给儿子 3000 美元，用于支付他的生活费用，让他可以自由地追求自己的梦想。根据签署的文字，向儿子支付的款项将持续到儿子在全国音乐颁奖典礼上被评为“年度音乐制作人”为止。男女双方在一月、二月和三月支付了约定的款项。然而，到了四月，该男子厌倦了儿子毫无进展，拒绝支付任何款项，并通知女子和儿子，他将不再支付任何款项。假设男女之间存在有效的合同，并且儿子拒绝起诉该男子。女子向男子提起诉讼，要求法院命令该男子继续根据合同条款向儿子付款，她会成功吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为法院不会批准具体履行付款承诺。","B":"不会，因为男方违约并未给女方造成经济损失。","C":"是的，因为如果男人不贡献自己的那一份，女人抚养儿子的负担就会增加。","D":"是的，因为妇女的法律补救措施不充分。"},"explanationZh":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）合同执行\n\n（承诺人诉承诺人）\n\n案由类型 补救措施 受益人\n\n受赠人受诺人（即希望向受诺人赠送礼物，有权：履行）有诉讼理由（承诺人不履行实际损害赔偿（罕见）礼物的接受者）名义损害赔偿或具体履约，如果其他补救措施不充分。受诺人已偿付债务）或具体履行（如果受诺人未偿付债务） 与预期受益人签订合同的一方可以起诉另一方违反该合同。但在某些情况下，当事人的法律救济（即金钱赔偿）是不够的。例如，当违约方的履约行为是给预期受益人的礼物时，非违约方通常无法证明实际损害，因此只能收回名义损害（例如，1 美元）。在这种情况下，法院可以命令具体履行，即法院命令违约方履行的公平补救措施。在这里，男人和女人同意每人每月给他们成年的儿子（合同的预期受益人）3,000 美元。该男子提前停止每月付款，从而违反了合同。由于该男子的表现是给儿子的一份礼物，因此该女子不太可能证明实际损失。此外，名义损害赔偿不足以保护她在合同项下的利益（即给予儿子财务自由）。因此，她将在特定履行（specific performance）的行动中取得成功（选择A）。 （选择B） 该妇女无需证明她遭受了经济损失，法院即可准予她的具体履行。她只需要证明金钱赔偿不足以保护她的利益。 （选择C） 如果男人不贡献他的份额（例如，如果她支付他的份额），则妇女抚养儿子的负担可能会增加。但这并不是授予她特定履行（specific performance）的依据。相反，需要裁定她的法律补救措施不充分。\n\n教育目标：具体履行是一种公平的补救措施，法院命令违约方履行合同义务。当非违约方的法律补救措施（即金钱赔偿）不充分时，这种补救措施是适当的——例如，在向预期受益人赠送礼物的合同双方之间的诉讼中。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 357（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（具体履行）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"具体履行是一种公平救济，法院命令违约方履行合同义务。当非违约方的法律补救措施（即金钱赔偿）不充分时，这种补救措施是适当的——例如，在向预期受益人赠送礼物的合同双方之间的诉讼中。","iracZh":{"issue":"女子向男子提起诉讼，要求法院命令该男子继续根据合同条款向儿子付款，她会成功吗？","rule":"具体履行是一种公平救济，法院命令违约方履行合同义务。当非违约方的法律补救措施（即金钱赔偿）不充分时，这种补救措施是适当的——例如，在向预期受益人赠送礼物的合同双方之间的诉讼中。","analysis":"第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）合同执行\n\n（承诺人诉承诺人）\n\n案由类型 补救措施 受益人\n\n受赠人受诺人（即希望向受诺人赠送礼物，有权：履行）有诉讼理由（承诺人不履行实际损害赔偿（罕见）礼物的接受者）名义损害赔偿或具体履约，如果其他补救措施不充分。受诺人已偿付债务）或具体履行（如果受诺人未偿付债务） 与预期受益人签订合同的一方可以起诉另一方违反该合同。但在某些情况下，当事人的法律救济（即金钱赔偿）是不够的。例如，当违约方的履约行为是给预期受益人的礼物时，非违约方通常无法证明实际损害，因此只能收回名义损害（例如，1 美元）。在这种情况下，法院可以命令具体履行，即法院命令违约方履行的公平补救措施。在这里，男人和女人同意每人每月给他们成年的儿子（合同的预期受益人）3,000 美元。该男子提前停止每月付款，从而违反了合同。由于该男子的表现是给儿子的一份礼物，因此该女子不太可能证明实际损失。此外，名义损害赔偿不足以保护她在合同项下的利益（即给予儿子财务自由）。因此，她将在特定履行（specific performance）的行动中取得成功（选择A）。 （选择B） 该妇女无需证明她遭受了经济损失，法院即可准予她的具体履行。她只需要证明金钱赔偿不足以保护她的利益。 （选择C） 如果男人不贡献他的份额（例如，如果她支付他的份额），则妇女抚养儿子的负担可能会增加。但这并不是授予她特定履行（specific performance）的依据。相反，需要裁定她的法律补救措施不充分。\n\n教育目标：具体履行是一种公平的补救措施，法院命令违约方履行合同义务。当非违约方的法律补救措施（即金钱赔偿）不充分时，这种补救措施是适当的——例如，在向预期受益人赠送礼物的合同双方之间的诉讼中。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 357（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（具体履行）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为妇女的法律补救措施不充分。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":196,"sourceQuestionPage":580,"sourceQuestionPages":[580],"sourceExplanationPages":[581,582],"sourceAnswerPages":[581,582],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-197","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A general contractor agreed to build a refrigerated storage facility for a company that transports perishable food. A subcontractor agreed with the general contractor to install the facility's refrigeration system. Their agreement included the express condition that all pipes for the refrigeration system were to be made of solid steel. However, the subcontractor completed the job using galvanized steel pipes that he had on hand. Unlike solid steel, the galvanized steel degrades quickly upon coming into contact with the type of refrigerant used in the refrigeration system. The general contractor discovered the substitution after the job was completed. The subcontractor, who was fully engaged out-of-state on another company's job site, declined to replace the pipes. The general contractor subsequently refused to pay the subcontractor pursuant to the contract and hired another subcontractor to replace the pipes so that the facility could be made operational. If the subcontractor sues the general contractor for breach of contract, how is the court likely to rule?","choices":{"A":"The subcontractor can recover in quantum meruit despite his failure to comply with an express condition in the contract.","B":"The subcontractor cannot recover damages under the contract because he committed a material breach.","C":"The subcontractor is entitled to damages for services performed because the use of a different type of pipe was a minor breach.","D":"The subcontractor is entitled to the full contract price because he substantially performed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Factors considered to determine material breach\n\nExtent to which nonbreaching party will suffer loss Extent to which nonbreaching party can be adequately compensated for loss Extent to which breaching party will suffer forfeiture Likelihood of cure by breaching party Absence of good faith or fair dealing by breaching party A material breach is the absence of substantial performance. Stated differently, it is a breach so serious that it defeats the contract's purpose or deprives the nonbreaching party of the substantial benefit of its bargain. Unlike a party who has substantially performed, a party in material breach cannot recover damages under the contract. Additionally, the material breach discharges the other party's duty to perform and entitles that party to terminate the contract if the breaching party is unwilling or unable to cure its breach in a reasonable time.* Here, the subcontractor ignored the express condition that the pipes were to be made of solid steel and instead installed galvanized steel pipes. This was a material breach because it defeated the purpose of the contract by making the facility's refrigeration system entirely nonoperational (Choice C). And the subcontractor declined to cure this breach by replacing the pipes. Therefore, the court will likely find that the general contractor had the right to terminate the contract due to the subcontractor's material breach so the subcontractor cannot recover damages. *If the breach is not material, then the nonbreaching party is limited to a claim for damages and cannot terminate the contract. (Choice A) Quantum meruit entitles a party who has conferred a benefit on another in a quasi-contractual relationship to damages for the reasonable value of that benefit. But this doctrine does not apply when, as here, there is an express contractual relationship. (Choice D) An express condition must be fully complied with unless excused (not seen here), so substantial performance will not suffice. But had the subcontractor substantially performed, he could have recovered the full contract price minus any amount that it will cost the general contractor to obtain the promised full performance.\n\nEducational objective: A party in material breach cannot sue to recover contract damages. Additionally, a material breach discharges the other party's duty to perform and entitles that party to terminate the contract if the breaching party is unwilling or unable to cure the breach.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 241 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (factors used to determine whether a breach of contract is material).","educationalObjective":"A party in material breach cannot sue to recover contract damages. Additionally, a material breach discharges the other party's duty to perform and entitles that party to terminate the contract if the breaching party is unwilling or unable to cure the breach.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 241 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (factors used to determine whether a breach of contract is material).","rule":"A party in material breach cannot sue to recover contract damages. Additionally, a material breach discharges the other party's duty to perform and entitles that party to terminate the contract if the breaching party is unwilling or unable to cure the breach.","irac":{"issue":"If the subcontractor sues the general contractor for breach of contract, how is the court likely to rule?","rule":"A party in material breach cannot sue to recover contract damages. Additionally, a material breach discharges the other party's duty to perform and entitles that party to terminate the contract if the breaching party is unwilling or unable to cure the breach.","analysis":"Factors considered to determine material breach\n\nExtent to which nonbreaching party will suffer loss Extent to which nonbreaching party can be adequately compensated for loss Extent to which breaching party will suffer forfeiture Likelihood of cure by breaching party Absence of good faith or fair dealing by breaching party A material breach is the absence of substantial performance. Stated differently, it is a breach so serious that it defeats the contract's purpose or deprives the nonbreaching party of the substantial benefit of its bargain. Unlike a party who has substantially performed, a party in material breach cannot recover damages under the contract. Additionally, the material breach discharges the other party's duty to perform and entitles that party to terminate the contract if the breaching party is unwilling or unable to cure its breach in a reasonable time.* Here, the subcontractor ignored the express condition that the pipes were to be made of solid steel and instead installed galvanized steel pipes. This was a material breach because it defeated the purpose of the contract by making the facility's refrigeration system entirely nonoperational (Choice C). And the subcontractor declined to cure this breach by replacing the pipes. Therefore, the court will likely find that the general contractor had the right to terminate the contract due to the subcontractor's material breach so the subcontractor cannot recover damages. *If the breach is not material, then the nonbreaching party is limited to a claim for damages and cannot terminate the contract. (Choice A) Quantum meruit entitles a party who has conferred a benefit on another in a quasi-contractual relationship to damages for the reasonable value of that benefit. But this doctrine does not apply when, as here, there is an express contractual relationship. (Choice D) An express condition must be fully complied with unless excused (not seen here), so substantial performance will not suffice. But had the subcontractor substantially performed, he could have recovered the full contract price minus any amount that it will cost the general contractor to obtain the promised full performance.","conclusion":"Answer B: The subcontractor cannot recover damages under the contract because he committed a material breach."},"questionZh":"一家总承包商同意为一家运输易腐食品的公司建造冷藏设施。分包商与总承包商同意安装该设施的制冷系统。他们的协议包括明确的条件，即制冷系统的所有管道均由实心钢制成。然而，分包商使用他手头的镀锌钢管完成了这项工作。与实心钢不同，镀锌钢在接触制冷系统中使用的制冷剂类型后会迅速降解。总承包商在工作完成后发现了替代情况。该分包商在州外另一家公司的工作现场全力投入，拒绝更换管道。总承包商随后拒绝按照合同向分包商付款，并聘请另一家分包商更换管道，以便设施投入运营。如果分包商起诉总承包商违约，法院可能如何裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"尽管分包商未能遵守合同中的明确条件，但他仍可以收回金额。","B":"分包商无法根据合同要求赔偿损失，因为他犯了重大违约。","C":"分包商有权就所提供的服务获得损害赔偿，因为使用不同类型的管道属于轻微违规行为。","D":"分包商因实质履约而有权获得全额合同价款。"},"explanationZh":"确定重大违规时考虑的因素\n\n守约方遭受损失的程度 守约方能够获得充分赔偿的程度 违约方遭受没收的程度 违约方补救的可能性 违约方缺乏诚信或公平交易 重大违约是指没有实质性履行。换句话说，这是一种严重的违约行为，以至于违背了合同的目的或剥夺了非违约方从其交易中获得的实质性利益。与实质性履约方不同，实质性违约方无法根据合同要求赔偿损失。此外，重大违约解除了另一方的履行义务，如果违约方不愿意或无法在合理时间内纠正违约行为，则该方有权终止合同。*在此，分包商忽略了管道应由实心钢制成的明确条件，而是安装了镀锌钢管。这是重大违约，因为它使设施的制冷系统完全无法运行，从而违背了合同的目的（选项 C）。分包商拒绝通过更换管道来解决这一问题。因此，法院很可能会认定，由于分包商的重大违约，总承包商有权终止合同，因此分包商无法获得损失赔偿。 *如果违约行为并不严重，则守约方仅限于提出损害赔偿要求，并且不能终止合同。 （选择A）量子功绩使在准合同关系中向另一方授予利益的一方有权获得该利益的合理价值的损害赔偿。但当存在明确的合同关系时（如此处所示），该原则并不适用。 （选项D）除非有借口，否则必须完全遵守明确的条件（此处未看到），因此实质性履行是不够的。但如果分包商实质性履约，他本可以收回全部合同价格减去总承包商为获得承诺的全部履约而花费的任何费用。\n\n教育目标：重大违约方不能提起诉讼要求赔偿合同损失。此外，重大违约解除了另一方履行合同的义务，并且如果违约方不愿意或无法纠正违约行为，则该方有权终止合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 241（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（用于确定是否严重违反合同的因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"重大违约方不能提起诉讼要求赔偿合同损失。此外，重大违约解除了另一方履行合同的义务，并且如果违约方不愿意或无法纠正违约行为，则该方有权终止合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果分包商起诉总承包商违约，法院可能如何裁决？","rule":"重大违约方不能提起诉讼要求赔偿合同损失。此外，重大违约解除了另一方履行合同的义务，并且如果违约方不愿意或无法纠正违约行为，则该方有权终止合同。","analysis":"确定重大违规时考虑的因素\n\n守约方遭受损失的程度 守约方能够获得充分赔偿的程度 违约方遭受没收的程度 违约方补救的可能性 违约方缺乏诚信或公平交易 重大违约是指没有实质性履行。换句话说，这是一种严重的违约行为，以至于违背了合同的目的或剥夺了非违约方从其交易中获得的实质性利益。与实质性履约方不同，实质性违约方无法根据合同要求赔偿损失。此外，重大违约解除了另一方的履行义务，如果违约方不愿意或无法在合理时间内纠正违约行为，则该方有权终止合同。*在此，分包商忽略了管道应由实心钢制成的明确条件，而是安装了镀锌钢管。这是重大违约，因为它使设施的制冷系统完全无法运行，从而违背了合同的目的（选项 C）。分包商拒绝通过更换管道来解决这一问题。因此，法院很可能会认定，由于分包商的重大违约，总承包商有权终止合同，因此分包商无法获得损失赔偿。 *如果违约行为并不严重，则守约方仅限于提出损害赔偿要求，并且不能终止合同。 （选择A）量子功绩使在准合同关系中向另一方授予利益的一方有权获得该利益的合理价值的损害赔偿。但当存在明确的合同关系时（如此处所示），该原则并不适用。 （选项D）除非有借口，否则必须完全遵守明确的条件（此处未看到），因此实质性履行是不够的。但如果分包商实质性履约，他本可以收回全部合同价格减去总承包商为获得承诺的全部履约而花费的任何费用。\n\n教育目标：重大违约方不能提起诉讼要求赔偿合同损失。此外，重大违约解除了另一方履行合同的义务，并且如果违约方不愿意或无法纠正违约行为，则该方有权终止合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 241（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（用于确定是否严重违反合同的因素）。","conclusion":"答案 B：分包商无法根据合同要求赔偿损失，因为他犯了重大违约。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":197,"sourceQuestionPage":583,"sourceQuestionPages":[583],"sourceExplanationPages":[584,585],"sourceAnswerPages":[584,585],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-198","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"On March 1, an excavator entered into a contract with a contractor to perform excavation work on a large project. The contract expressly required that the excavator begin work on June 1 to enable other subcontractors to install utilities. On May 15, the excavator requested a 30-day delay in the start date for the excavation work because he was seriously behind schedule on another project. When the contractor refused to grant the delay, the excavator stated that he would try to begin the work for the contractor on June 1. Does the contractor have valid legal grounds to cancel the contract with the excavator and hire a replacement?","choices":{"A":"No, because the excavator did not state unequivocally that he would delay the beginning of his work.","B":"No, because the excavator would be entitled to specific performance of the contract if he could begin by June 1.","C":"Yes, because the excavator breached the implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing.","D":"Yes, because the excavator committed an anticipatory repudiation of the contract by causing the contractor to feel insecure about the performance."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Anticipatory repudiation\n\nStandard Party's clear & unequivocal words or actions indicate unwillingness to perform\n\nRetraction Party may retract repudiation unless nonrepudiating party:\n\ncancels contract materially changes position in reliance thereon or indicates repudiation is final Effect Upon repudiation, nonrepudiating party may:*\n\ntreat repudiation as breach or ignore repudiation & demand performance *Nonrepudiating party may also suspend own performance\n\nUnder the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation, a contracting party may treat another party's repudiation as a breach and cancel the contract. A repudiation occurs when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, expresses an unwillingness to perform. But a party's mere expression of doubt as to its ability to perform or request that the contract be modified does not constitute a repudiation. Here, the excavator requested a 30-day delay in the start date of his work. After the contractor denied this request, the excavator stated that he would try to begin work on the start date. Neither of these statements is an unequivocal assertion that the excavator would delay the beginning of his work. Therefore, the excavator did not anticipatorily repudiate the contract, and the contractor has no valid legal ground to cancel it. (Choice B) Specific performance is an available remedy when monetary damages would be inadequate. Here, monetary damages would be adequate to compensate the excavator if the contractor unjustifiably canceled the contract. (Choice C) All contracts contain an implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing, which prohibits the parties from engaging in improper behavior (eg, deceit). But the excavator did not breach this covenant since his request for a delay was reasonable considering his circumstances. (Choice D) A party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance may demand adequate assurances that the other party will perform. If the other party fails to provide those assurances within a reasonable time, the contract is anticipatorily repudiated. Although the excavator's statements were reasonable grounds for insecurity, the contractor did not demand assurances.\n\nEducational objective: An anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, indicates an intention not to perform pursuant to the contract. Mere expressions of doubt regarding one's ability to perform or a request to modify the contract does not suffice.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 250 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"An anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, indicates an intention not to perform pursuant to the contract. Mere expressions of doubt regarding one's ability to perform or a request to modify the contract does not suffice.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 250 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"An anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, indicates an intention not to perform pursuant to the contract. Mere expressions of doubt regarding one's ability to perform or a request to modify the contract does not suffice.","irac":{"issue":"Does the contractor have valid legal grounds to cancel the contract with the excavator and hire a replacement?","rule":"An anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, indicates an intention not to perform pursuant to the contract. Mere expressions of doubt regarding one's ability to perform or a request to modify the contract does not suffice.","analysis":"Anticipatory repudiation\n\nStandard Party's clear & unequivocal words or actions indicate unwillingness to perform\n\nRetraction Party may retract repudiation unless nonrepudiating party:\n\ncancels contract materially changes position in reliance thereon or indicates repudiation is final Effect Upon repudiation, nonrepudiating party may:*\n\ntreat repudiation as breach or ignore repudiation & demand performance *Nonrepudiating party may also suspend own performance\n\nUnder the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation, a contracting party may treat another party's repudiation as a breach and cancel the contract. A repudiation occurs when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or acts, expresses an unwillingness to perform. But a party's mere expression of doubt as to its ability to perform or request that the contract be modified does not constitute a repudiation. Here, the excavator requested a 30-day delay in the start date of his work. After the contractor denied this request, the excavator stated that he would try to begin work on the start date. Neither of these statements is an unequivocal assertion that the excavator would delay the beginning of his work. Therefore, the excavator did not anticipatorily repudiate the contract, and the contractor has no valid legal ground to cancel it. (Choice B) Specific performance is an available remedy when monetary damages would be inadequate. Here, monetary damages would be adequate to compensate the excavator if the contractor unjustifiably canceled the contract. (Choice C) All contracts contain an implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing, which prohibits the parties from engaging in improper behavior (eg, deceit). But the excavator did not breach this covenant since his request for a delay was reasonable considering his circumstances. (Choice D) A party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance may demand adequate assurances that the other party will perform. If the other party fails to provide those assurances within a reasonable time, the contract is anticipatorily repudiated. Although the excavator's statements were reasonable grounds for insecurity, the contractor did not demand assurances.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the excavator did not state unequivocally that he would delay the beginning of his work."},"questionZh":"3月1日，一台挖掘机与承包商签订了一项大型项目挖掘工作合同。合同明确要求挖掘机于6月1日开始工作，以便其他分包商安装公用设施。 5月15日，挖掘机要求将挖掘工作的开工日期推迟30天，因为他的另一个项目严重落后于计划。当承包商拒绝延期时，挖掘机表示将尝试在6月1日为承包商开始工作。承包商是否有有效的法律依据取消与挖掘机的合同并雇用替代者？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为挖掘机并没有明确表示要推迟开工。","B":"不可以，因为如果挖掘机能够在 6 月 1 日之前开始工作，他将有权具体履行合同。","C":"是的，因为挖掘机违反了诚信和公平交易的默示契约。","D":"是的，因为挖掘机导致承包商对履约感到不安全，因此预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行合同。"},"explanationZh":"预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）\n\n标准方明确无误的言行表明不愿意履行\n\n毁约方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，除非非毁约方：\n\n取消合同实质上改变了对合同的信赖状况或表明否认是最终的效果一旦否认，非毁约方可以：*\n\n将否认视为违约或忽略否认并要求履行*非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行\n\n根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，缔约方可以将另一方的否认视为违约并取消合同。当一方通过言语或行为明确无误地表示不愿意履行义务时，就会发生否认。但一方当事人仅对其履行能力表示怀疑或要求修改合同并不构成毁约。在此，挖掘机要求推迟 30 天开工。在承包商拒绝这一要求后，挖掘机表示将尝试在开工日期开始工作。这些陈述都不是明确断言挖掘机会推迟他的工作开始。因此，挖掘机并未预见性地否认合同，承包商也没有有效的法律依据解除合同。 （选择 B）当金钱赔偿不足以弥补损失时，具体履行是一种可用的补救措施。在这里，如果承包商无理取消合同，金钱损失足以补偿挖掘机。 （选项C） 所有合同都包含善意和公平交易的默示契约，禁止双方从事不当行为（例如欺骗）。但挖掘机并没有违反这一约定，因为考虑到他的情况，他提出的延期请求是合理的。 （选择 D） 如果一方有合理理由对另一方的履约感到不安全，则可以要求另一方提供充分的履约保证。如果另一方未能在合理时间内提供这些保证，则合同将被预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。尽管挖掘机的声明是不安全的合理理由，但承包商并没有要求保证。\n\n教育目标：当一方通过言语或行为清楚明确地表明不履行合同的意图时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。仅仅对某人的履约能力表示怀疑或要求修改合同是不够的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 250（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（否认）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一方通过言语或行为清楚明确地表明不履行合同的意图时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。仅仅对某人的履约能力表示怀疑或要求修改合同是不够的。","iracZh":{"issue":"承包商是否有有效的法律依据取消与挖掘机的合同并雇用替代者？","rule":"当一方通过言语或行为清楚明确地表明不履行合同的意图时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。仅仅对某人的履约能力表示怀疑或要求修改合同是不够的。","analysis":"预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）\n\n标准方明确无误的言行表明不愿意履行\n\n毁约方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，除非非毁约方：\n\n取消合同实质上改变了对合同的信赖状况或表明否认是最终的效果一旦否认，非毁约方可以：*\n\n将否认视为违约或忽略否认并要求履行*非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行\n\n根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，缔约方可以将另一方的否认视为违约并取消合同。当一方通过言语或行为明确无误地表示不愿意履行义务时，就会发生否认。但一方当事人仅对其履行能力表示怀疑或要求修改合同并不构成毁约。在此，挖掘机要求推迟 30 天开工。在承包商拒绝这一要求后，挖掘机表示将尝试在开工日期开始工作。这些陈述都不是明确断言挖掘机会推迟他的工作开始。因此，挖掘机并未预见性地否认合同，承包商也没有有效的法律依据解除合同。 （选择 B）当金钱赔偿不足以弥补损失时，具体履行是一种可用的补救措施。在这里，如果承包商无理取消合同，金钱损失足以补偿挖掘机。 （选项C） 所有合同都包含善意和公平交易的默示契约，禁止双方从事不当行为（例如欺骗）。但挖掘机并没有违反这一约定，因为考虑到他的情况，他提出的延期请求是合理的。 （选择 D） 如果一方有合理理由对另一方的履约感到不安全，则可以要求另一方提供充分的履约保证。如果另一方未能在合理时间内提供这些保证，则合同将被预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。尽管挖掘机的声明是不安全的合理理由，但承包商并没有要求保证。\n\n教育目标：当一方通过言语或行为清楚明确地表明不履行合同的意图时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。仅仅对某人的履约能力表示怀疑或要求修改合同是不够的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 250（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（否认）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为挖掘机并没有明确表示要推迟开工。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":198,"sourceQuestionPage":586,"sourceQuestionPages":[586],"sourceExplanationPages":[587,588],"sourceAnswerPages":[587,588],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-199","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"Under the terms of a written contract, a builder agreed to construct a garage for a homeowner for $10,000. Nothing was stated in the parties' negotiations or in the contract about progress payments during the course of the work. After completing 25 percent of the garage according to the homeowner's specifications, the builder demanded $2,000 as a reasonable progress payment. The homeowner refused, and the builder abandoned the job. If each party sues the other for breach of contract, which of the following will the court decide?","choices":{"A":"Both parties are in breach, and each is entitled to damages, if any, from the other.","B":"Only the builder is in breach and liable for the homeowner's damages, if any.","C":"Only the homeowner is in breach and liable for the builder's damages, if any.","D":"Both parties took reasonable positions, and neither is in breach."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Order of performances*\n\nSimultaneous Condition concurrent implied (ie, performance due performance possible simultaneously)\n\nOne performance requires Condition precedent implied (ie, durational performance due time before respective duty triggered)\n\n*These rules apply unless express contractual language or circumstances indicate otherwise.\n\nA party breaches a contract by failing to perform a contractual duty at the time performance is due. Where only one party's performance requires a period of time to complete, that party's performance is due first unless the contract or the circumstances indicate otherwise. If that party fails to complete performance or repudiates the contract, the nonbreaching party is entitled to withhold performance and recover damages. Here, the builder needed a period of time to construct the garage, and the parties' contract did not provide for progress payments—ie, payments made at certain stages in the process. As a result, the builder was required to complete construction before the homeowner was required to pay anything. The builder then repudiated the contract by abandoning the job, so the homeowner was entitled to withhold payment. Therefore, only the builder is in breach and liable for the homeowner's damages, if any (Choices A, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: Where one party's performance requires a period of time to complete (eg, construction), that party must complete performance before the other party is required to perform (eg, payment). Failure to perform results in a breach of the contract, entitling the nonbreaching party to withhold performance and recover damages.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 234 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (order of performance).","educationalObjective":"Where one party's performance requires a period of time to complete (eg, construction), that party must complete performance before the other party is required to perform (eg, payment). Failure to perform results in a breach of the contract, entitling the nonbreaching party to withhold performance and recover damages.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 234 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (order of performance).","rule":"Where one party's performance requires a period of time to complete (eg, construction), that party must complete performance before the other party is required to perform (eg, payment). Failure to perform results in a breach of the contract, entitling the nonbreaching party to withhold performance and recover damages.","irac":{"issue":"If each party sues the other for breach of contract, which of the following will the court decide?","rule":"Where one party's performance requires a period of time to complete (eg, construction), that party must complete performance before the other party is required to perform (eg, payment). Failure to perform results in a breach of the contract, entitling the nonbreaching party to withhold performance and recover damages.","analysis":"Order of performances*\n\nSimultaneous Condition concurrent implied (ie, performance due performance possible simultaneously)\n\nOne performance requires Condition precedent implied (ie, durational performance due time before respective duty triggered)\n\n*These rules apply unless express contractual language or circumstances indicate otherwise.\n\nA party breaches a contract by failing to perform a contractual duty at the time performance is due. Where only one party's performance requires a period of time to complete, that party's performance is due first unless the contract or the circumstances indicate otherwise. If that party fails to complete performance or repudiates the contract, the nonbreaching party is entitled to withhold performance and recover damages. Here, the builder needed a period of time to construct the garage, and the parties' contract did not provide for progress payments—ie, payments made at certain stages in the process. As a result, the builder was required to complete construction before the homeowner was required to pay anything. The builder then repudiated the contract by abandoning the job, so the homeowner was entitled to withhold payment. Therefore, only the builder is in breach and liable for the homeowner's damages, if any (Choices A, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer B: Only the builder is in breach and liable for the homeowner's damages, if any."},"questionZh":"根据书面合同条款，建筑商同意以 10,000 美元的价格为房主建造一个车库。双方的谈判或合同中均未提及工程过程中的进度付款。根据房主的规格完成车库 25% 的建设后，建筑商要求支付 2,000 美元作为合理的进度付款。房主拒绝了，建筑商放弃了工作。如果双方起诉对方违约，法院将做出以下哪项裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"双方均违约，双方均有权向对方索取损害赔偿（如有）。","B":"只有建筑商违约并对房主的损失（如果有）承担责任。","C":"只有房主违约并对建筑商造成的损失（如果有）承担责任。","D":"双方立场合理，均无违约行为。"},"explanationZh":"演出顺序*\n\n同时条件：双方履行可同时发生时的默示条件\n\n一项履行需要隐含的先决条件（即触发相应职责之前的持续履行到期时间）\n\n*除非明确的合同语言或情况另有说明，否则这些规则适用。\n\n一方当事人未能在到期时履行合同义务，即构成违约。如果只有一方的履行需要一段时间才能完成，则该方应首先履行，除非合同或情况另有规定。如果一方未能履行合同或者拒绝履行合同，守约方有权拒绝履行合同并要求赔偿损失。在此，建筑商需要一段时间来建造车库，而双方的合同并没有规定进度款，即在过程中的某些阶段支付的款项。结果，建筑商被要求在房主支付任何费用之前完成施工。建筑商随后放弃了工作，从而拒绝履行合同，因此房主有权扣留付款。因此，只有建筑商违约并对房主的损害（如果有）承担责任（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：如果一方的履行需要一段时间才能完成（例如，施工），则该方必须在要求另一方履行（例如，付款）之前完成履行。不履行合同即构成违约，守约方有权拒绝履行合同并要求赔偿损失。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 234（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行顺序）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果一方的履约需要一段时间才能完成（例如，施工），则该方必须在要求另一方履约（例如，付款）之前完成履约。不履行合同即构成违约，守约方有权拒绝履行合同并要求赔偿损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果双方起诉对方违约，法院将做出以下哪项裁决？","rule":"如果一方的履约需要一段时间才能完成（例如，施工），则该方必须在要求另一方履约（例如，付款）之前完成履约。不履行合同即构成违约，守约方有权拒绝履行合同并要求赔偿损失。","analysis":"演出顺序*\n\n同时条件：双方履行可同时发生时的默示条件\n\n一项履行需要隐含的先决条件（即触发相应职责之前的持续履行到期时间）\n\n*除非明确的合同语言或情况另有说明，否则这些规则适用。\n\n一方当事人未能在到期时履行合同义务，即构成违约。如果只有一方的履行需要一段时间才能完成，则该方应首先履行，除非合同或情况另有规定。如果一方未能履行合同或者拒绝履行合同，守约方有权拒绝履行合同并要求赔偿损失。在此，建筑商需要一段时间来建造车库，而双方的合同并没有规定进度款，即在过程中的某些阶段支付的款项。结果，建筑商被要求在房主支付任何费用之前完成施工。建筑商随后放弃了工作，从而拒绝履行合同，因此房主有权扣留付款。因此，只有建筑商违约并对房主的损害（如果有）承担责任（选项 A、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目标：如果一方的履行需要一段时间才能完成（例如，施工），则该方必须在要求另一方履行（例如，付款）之前完成履行。不履行合同即构成违约，守约方有权拒绝履行合同并要求赔偿损失。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 234（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行顺序）。","conclusion":"答案 B：只有建筑商违约并对房主的损失（如果有）承担责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":199,"sourceQuestionPage":589,"sourceQuestionPages":[589],"sourceExplanationPages":[590],"sourceAnswerPages":[590],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-200","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On June 1, a woman telegraphed a vendor, \"At what price will you sell 100 of your basic garbage-disposal units for delivery around June 10?\" On June 2, the vendor telegraphed the woman, \"You're in luck. We have only 100 basic garbage-disposal units, all on clearance at 50% off usual wholesale of $120 per unit, for delivery at our shipping platform on June 12.\" On June 5, the vendor received a letter from the woman that stated, \"I accept. Would prefer to pay in full 30 days after invoice.\" On June 6, the woman received a telegram from the vendor that stated, \"You must pick up at our platform and pay upon delivery.\" On June 9, the vendor received a letter from the woman that stated, \"I don't deal with people who can't accommodate my simple requests.\" On June 10, the vendor received a telegram from the woman that stated, \"Okay. I'm over a barrel and will pick up the goods on your terms on June 12.\" The vendor had sold all 100 of the units to another buyer earlier that day. The woman sued the vendor for breach of contract. Which of the following arguments will best serve the vendor's defense?","choices":{"A":"The vendor's telegraph on June 2 was merely a price quotation, not an offer.","B":"The woman's letter on June 5 was not an acceptance because it varied from the terms of the vendor's initial telegraph.","C":"The woman's letter on June 9 was an unequivocal refusal to perform that excused the vendor even if the parties had previously formed a contract.","D":"The woman's use of the mail in response to the vendor's initial telegraph was an ineffective method of acceptance."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Anticipatory repudiation\n\nStandard Party's clear & unequivocal words or actions indicate unwillingness to perform\n\nRetraction Party may retract repudiation unless nonrepudiating party:\n\ncancels contract materially changes position in reliance thereon or indicates repudiation is final Effect Upon repudiation, nonrepudiating party may:*\n\ntreat repudiation as breach or ignore repudiation & demand performance *Nonrepudiating party may also suspend own performance\n\nThe doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a party clearly and unequivocally indicates an unwillingness to perform a promise before the time for performance is due. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Additionally, any performance owed by the nonrepudiating party is excused. Here, the parties likely formed a contract for the sale of the garbage-disposal units, which the vendor then sold to another buyer on June 10. However, the vendor's performance may be excused by the woman's anticipatory repudiation on June 9. That is because her June 9 letter could be interpreted as an unequivocal refusal to perform since it stated, \"I don't deal with people who can't accommodate my simple requests.\" Therefore, this argument will best serve the vendor's defense.* *The woman will not be able to argue that she retracted her repudiation because her attempted retraction occurred after the vendor had materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation by selling the units to another buyer. (Choice A) An offer must express definite terms and an intent to be bound, thereby justifying acceptance by the offeree. Here, the vendor's June 2 telegraph would probably be interpreted as an offer (not a price quotation) because its terms were definite (100 units at 50% off) and it gave the woman the power to accept. (Choice B) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, garbage-disposal units) are governed by the UCC, which follows the battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an enforceable contract is created once there has been an offer and acceptance—even if the acceptance varies the terms of the offer (as seen here).\n\n(Choice D) The woman's use of the mail was an effective method of acceptance since the vendor did not specify how acceptance was to be rendered.\n\nEducational objective: When a party anticipatorily repudiates a contract, the nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance. Additionally, any performance owed by the nonrepudiating party is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C.§ 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"When a party anticipatorily repudiates a contract, the nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance. Additionally, any performance owed by the nonrepudiating party is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C.§ 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"When a party anticipatorily repudiates a contract, the nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance. Additionally, any performance owed by the nonrepudiating party is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments will best serve the vendor's defense?","rule":"When a party anticipatorily repudiates a contract, the nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance. Additionally, any performance owed by the nonrepudiating party is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Anticipatory repudiation\n\nStandard Party's clear & unequivocal words or actions indicate unwillingness to perform\n\nRetraction Party may retract repudiation unless nonrepudiating party:\n\ncancels contract materially changes position in reliance thereon or indicates repudiation is final Effect Upon repudiation, nonrepudiating party may:*\n\ntreat repudiation as breach or ignore repudiation & demand performance *Nonrepudiating party may also suspend own performance\n\nThe doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a party clearly and unequivocally indicates an unwillingness to perform a promise before the time for performance is due. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Additionally, any performance owed by the nonrepudiating party is excused. Here, the parties likely formed a contract for the sale of the garbage-disposal units, which the vendor then sold to another buyer on June 10. However, the vendor's performance may be excused by the woman's anticipatory repudiation on June 9. That is because her June 9 letter could be interpreted as an unequivocal refusal to perform since it stated, \"I don't deal with people who can't accommodate my simple requests.\" Therefore, this argument will best serve the vendor's defense.* *The woman will not be able to argue that she retracted her repudiation because her attempted retraction occurred after the vendor had materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation by selling the units to another buyer. (Choice A) An offer must express definite terms and an intent to be bound, thereby justifying acceptance by the offeree. Here, the vendor's June 2 telegraph would probably be interpreted as an offer (not a price quotation) because its terms were definite (100 units at 50% off) and it gave the woman the power to accept. (Choice B) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, garbage-disposal units) are governed by the UCC, which follows the battle-of-the-forms rule for contract formation. Under this rule, an enforceable contract is created once there has been an offer and acceptance—even if the acceptance varies the terms of the offer (as seen here).\n\n(Choice D) The woman's use of the mail was an effective method of acceptance since the vendor did not specify how acceptance was to be rendered.","conclusion":"Answer C: The woman's letter on June 9 was an unequivocal refusal to perform that excused the vendor even if the parties had previously formed a contract."},"questionZh":"6月1日，一位女士给一位商贩打电话：“你们的100台基本垃圾处理机，6月10日左右交货，你们打算以什么价格出售？” 6月2日，供应商给这位女士发电报说：“你很幸运。我们只有100个基本垃圾处理器，全部清仓，每台120美元，平时批发价有50%的折扣，将于6月12日在我们的运输平台发货。” 6月5日，卖家收到了该女士的一封信，信中写道：“我接受。希望在发票后30天内全额付款。” 6月6日，女子收到卖家发来的电报，称“您必须到我们平台提货，货到付款”。 6月9日，卖家收到了女子的来信，信中写道：“我不会和那些不能满足我简单要求的人打交道。” 6月10日，商贩收到女子发来的电报，电报中写道：“好的，我已经满桶了，6月12日按照你的条件提货。”当天早些时候，卖家已将全部 100 套公寓卖给了另一位买家。该女子起诉供应商违反合同。以下哪项论点最有利于供应商的辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"6月2日卖方的电报只是报价，而不是报价。","B":"该女士 6 月 5 日的信函并未被接受，因为它与卖方最初电报的条款不同。","C":"该女士在 6 月 9 日的信中明确拒绝履行，即使双方之前签订了合同，也为卖方开脱了责任。","D":"该妇女使用邮件来回应供应商最初的电报是一种无效的接受方法。"},"explanationZh":"预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）\n\n标准方明确无误的言行表明不愿意履行\n\n毁约方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，除非非毁约方：\n\n取消合同实质上改变了对合同的信赖状况或表明否认是最终的效果一旦否认，非毁约方可以：*\n\n将否认视为违约或忽略否认并要求履行*非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行\n\n当一方在履行期限到期之前明确表示不愿意履行承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。此外，非毁约方所欠的任何履约均被免除。在此，双方可能签订了垃圾处理器的销售合同，卖方随后于 6 月 10 日将其出售给另一位买方。然而，卖方的履约可能会因该女子在 6 月 9 日的预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）而得到原谅。这是因为她 6 月 9 日的信可以被解释为明确拒绝履约，因为她说：“我不与不能满足我简单要求的人打交道。”因此，这一论点最有利于卖方的辩护。* *该妇女将无法辩称她撤回了她的拒绝，因为她试图撤回是在卖方通过将房屋出售给另一位买家而依靠拒绝而实质性改变立场之后。 （选择 A） 要约必须表达明确的条款和受要约约束的意图，从而证明受要约人接受是合理的。在这里，供应商 6 月 2 日的电报可能会被解释为要约（而不是报价），因为其条款是明确的（100 件可享受 50% 的折扣），并且赋予该妇女接受的权力。 （选择B） 货物销售合同（例如垃圾处理装置）受UCC 管辖，该合同遵循合同订立的形式斗争规则。根据此规则，一旦提出要约并接受，即使接受改变了要约条款（如此处所示），也将创建可执行的合同。\n\n（选项D） 妇女使用邮寄方式是一种有效的收货方式，因为供应商没有具体说明如何进行收货。\n\n教育目标：当一方预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同时，非毁约方可能会将否认视为违约或忽略它并要求履行。此外，非毁约方所欠的任何履约均被免除。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nU.C.C.§ 2-610（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一方预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同时，非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，或者忽略它并要求履行。此外，非毁约方所欠的任何履约均被免除。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项论点最有利于供应商的辩护？","rule":"当一方预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同时，非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，或者忽略它并要求履行。此外，非毁约方所欠的任何履约均被免除。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）\n\n标准方明确无误的言行表明不愿意履行\n\n毁约方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，除非非毁约方：\n\n取消合同实质上改变了对合同的信赖状况或表明否认是最终的效果一旦否认，非毁约方可以：*\n\n将否认视为违约或忽略否认并要求履行*非毁约方也可以暂停自己的履行\n\n当一方在履行期限到期之前明确表示不愿意履行承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。此外，非毁约方所欠的任何履约均被免除。在此，双方可能签订了垃圾处理器的销售合同，卖方随后于 6 月 10 日将其出售给另一位买方。然而，卖方的履约可能会因该女子在 6 月 9 日的预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）而得到原谅。这是因为她 6 月 9 日的信可以被解释为明确拒绝履约，因为她说：“我不与不能满足我简单要求的人打交道。”因此，这一论点最有利于卖方的辩护。* *该妇女将无法辩称她撤回了她的拒绝，因为她试图撤回是在卖方通过将房屋出售给另一位买家而依靠拒绝而实质性改变立场之后。 （选择 A） 要约必须表达明确的条款和受要约约束的意图，从而证明受要约人接受是合理的。在这里，供应商 6 月 2 日的电报可能会被解释为要约（而不是报价），因为其条款是明确的（100 件可享受 50% 的折扣），并且赋予该妇女接受的权力。 （选择B） 货物销售合同（例如垃圾处理装置）受UCC 管辖，该合同遵循合同订立的形式斗争规则。根据此规则，一旦提出要约并接受，即使接受改变了要约条款（如此处所示），也将创建可执行的合同。\n\n（选项D） 妇女使用邮寄方式是一种有效的收货方式，因为供应商没有具体说明如何进行收货。\n\n教育目标：当一方预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同时，非毁约方可能会将否认视为违约或忽略它并要求履行。此外，非毁约方所欠的任何履约均被免除。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nU.C.C.§ 2-610（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","conclusion":"答案 C：该女士在 6 月 9 日的信中明确拒绝履行，即使双方之前签订了合同，也为卖方开脱了责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":200,"sourceQuestionPage":591,"sourceQuestionPages":[591],"sourceExplanationPages":[592,593],"sourceAnswerPages":[592,593],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-201","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A buyer agreed to purchase and took delivery of a telescope from a seller for a price of $100. She promised to pay him $100 \"as soon as I am able.\" What effect does the quoted language have on the enforceability of the buyer's promise?","choices":{"A":"None.","B":"It makes the promise illusory.","C":"It requires the buyer to prove her inability to pay.","D":"It requires the seller to prove the buyer's ability to pay."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Performance is generally due once a contract is formed (eg, when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration). However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs (eg, buyer's promise to pay as soon as she is able) and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail Condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs (eg, buyer's promise to pay conditioned on delivery of telescope) and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability (Choice C) *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). Here, a contract was formed when the buyer agreed to purchase the telescope from the seller for $100. The buyer's promise to pay \"as soon as I am able\" created a condition precedent that delayed her obligation to make the promised payment. This language therefore requires the seller to prove the buyer's ability to pay (ie, that the specified event occurred) before he can prevail in his action for breach of contract (Choice A). (Choice B) A promise is illusory, and therefore unenforceable, if it is so indefinite that only one party is required to perform—eg, had the buyer promised to pay $100 \"if I want to.\" In contrast, the buyer's promise to pay $100 \"as soon as I am able\" defines when she must perform and can be enforced once the condition precedent occurs.\n\nEducational objective: Performance is generally due when a contract is formed. However, the contract can contain a condition precedent that delays performance until—or a condition subsequent that will excuse performance when—a specified event occurs.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–230 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions related to performance).","educationalObjective":"Performance is generally due when a contract is formed. However, the contract can contain a condition precedent that delays performance until—or a condition subsequent that will excuse performance when—a specified event occurs.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–230 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions related to performance).","rule":"Performance is generally due when a contract is formed. However, the contract can contain a condition precedent that delays performance until—or a condition subsequent that will excuse performance when—a specified event occurs.","irac":{"issue":"A buyer agreed to purchase and took delivery of a telescope from a seller for a price of $100. She promised to pay him $100 \"as soon as I am able.\" What effect does the quoted language have on the enforceability of the buyer's promise?","rule":"Performance is generally due when a contract is formed. However, the contract can contain a condition precedent that delays performance until—or a condition subsequent that will excuse performance when—a specified event occurs.","analysis":"Performance is generally due once a contract is formed (eg, when a mutual agreement is supported by valuable consideration). However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: Condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs (eg, buyer's promise to pay as soon as she is able) and requires the plaintiff to prove that the event occurred to prevail Condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs (eg, buyer's promise to pay conditioned on delivery of telescope) and requires the defendant to prove that the event occurred to avoid liability (Choice C) *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). Here, a contract was formed when the buyer agreed to purchase the telescope from the seller for $100. The buyer's promise to pay \"as soon as I am able\" created a condition precedent that delayed her obligation to make the promised payment. This language therefore requires the seller to prove the buyer's ability to pay (ie, that the specified event occurred) before he can prevail in his action for breach of contract (Choice A). (Choice B) A promise is illusory, and therefore unenforceable, if it is so indefinite that only one party is required to perform—eg, had the buyer promised to pay $100 \"if I want to.\" In contrast, the buyer's promise to pay $100 \"as soon as I am able\" defines when she must perform and can be enforced once the condition precedent occurs.","conclusion":"Answer D: It requires the seller to prove the buyer's ability to pay."},"questionZh":"买家同意以 100 美元的价格从卖家处购买并接收了一台望远镜。她承诺“一旦有能力”就付给他 100 美元。引用的语言对买方承诺的可执行性有什么影响？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有任何。","B":"它让承诺变得虚幻。","C":"它要求买方证明她无力付款。","D":"它要求卖方证明买方的付款能力。"},"explanationZh":"合同一旦形成（例如，当双方协议得到有价值的对价的支持时），通常就应履行义务。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生（例如，买方承诺尽快付款），并要求原告证明该事件已发生；随后条件 – 一旦特定事件发生，就免除履行义务（例如，买方承诺以交付望远镜为条件付款），并要求被告证明该事件已发生以避免承担责任（选择 C） *当双方的履行义务均为先决条件时，即出现并发条件。另一方的履行义务（即，当同时履行时）。在这里，当买方同意以 100 美元从卖方购买望远镜时，合同就成立了。买方承诺“尽快”付款，这创造了一个先决条件，推迟了她履行承诺付款的义务。因此，这种语言要求卖方证明买方有能力付款（即发生了指定的事件），然后才能在其违约诉讼中获胜（选择 A）。 （选择 B）如果承诺无限期以至于只需要一方履行，那么该承诺就是虚幻的，因此无法执行——例如，买方承诺“如果我愿意”支付 100 美元。相比之下，买方承诺“一旦有能力”就支付 100 美元，这定义了她必须履行的时间，并且一旦先决条件发生就可以强制执行。\n\n教育目标： 绩效通常在合同签订时到期。但是，合同可以包含延迟履行的先决条件，或者在特定事件发生时免除履行的后续条件。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–230 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（与履行相关的条件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"履行通常是在合同签订时到期。但是，合同可以包含延迟履行的先决条件，或者在特定事件发生时免除履行的后续条件。","iracZh":{"issue":"买家同意以 100 美元的价格从卖家处购买并接收了一台望远镜。她承诺“一旦有能力”就付给他 100 美元。引用的语言对买方承诺的可执行性有什么影响？","rule":"履行通常是在合同签订时到期。但是，合同可以包含延迟履行的先决条件，或者在特定事件发生时免除履行的后续条件。","analysis":"合同一旦形成（例如，当双方协议得到有价值的对价的支持时），通常就应履行义务。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生（例如，买方承诺尽快付款），并要求原告证明该事件已发生；随后条件 – 一旦特定事件发生，就免除履行义务（例如，买方承诺以交付望远镜为条件付款），并要求被告证明该事件已发生以避免承担责任（选择 C） *当双方的履行义务均为先决条件时，即出现并发条件。另一方的履行义务（即，当同时履行时）。在这里，当买方同意以 100 美元从卖方购买望远镜时，合同就成立了。买方承诺“尽快”付款，这创造了一个先决条件，推迟了她履行承诺付款的义务。因此，这种语言要求卖方证明买方有能力付款（即发生了指定的事件），然后才能在其违约诉讼中获胜（选择 A）。 （选择 B）如果承诺无限期以至于只需要一方履行，那么该承诺就是虚幻的，因此无法执行——例如，买方承诺“如果我愿意”支付 100 美元。相比之下，买方承诺“一旦有能力”就支付 100 美元，这定义了她必须履行的时间，并且一旦先决条件发生就可以强制执行。\n\n教育目标： 绩效通常在合同签订时到期。但是，合同可以包含延迟履行的先决条件，或者在特定事件发生时免除履行的后续条件。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–230 (Am. Law Inst. 1981)（与履行相关的条件）。","conclusion":"答案 D：它要求卖方证明买方的付款能力。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-0a7a3927ad7f8605"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":201,"sourceQuestionPage":594,"sourceQuestionPages":[594],"sourceExplanationPages":[595,596],"sourceAnswerPages":[595,596],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-202","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"In January, a teacher contracted with a summer camp to serve as its head counselor at a salary of $10,000 for 10 weeks, from the first of June to the middle of August. In March, the camp notified the teacher that it had hired someone else as head counselor and that the teacher's services would not be needed. In April, the teacher spent $200 traveling to interview at the only other nearby summer camp for a position as its head counselor. She was not chosen for that job. The teacher then took a position teaching in a local summer school at a salary of $6,000 for the same 10-week period. How much is the teacher entitled to recover as damages in a breach-of-contract action against the camp?","choices":{"A":"$4,000.","B":"$4,200.","C":"$10,000.","D":"$10,200."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Expectation (ie, benefit-of-the-bargain) damages\n\nDefinition Losses arising naturally & obviously from breach\n\nPurpose Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed\n\nMeasure General rule – difference between contract price & market price (or cost of purchasing substitute performance) Construction contracts – difference between contract price & cost of construction by another builder The primary purpose of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed; therefore, the nonbreaching party is entitled to recover expectation damages. Calculating these damages begins with the contract price, but any losses that the nonbreaching party avoided or could have avoided (ie, mitigated) after the breach are subtracted from the total damage award. Reasonable expenses incurred in an effort to mitigate damages, whether or not successful, are recoverable. Here, the teacher contracted to serve as the summer camp's head counselor at a salary of $10,000 for 10 weeks. After the summer camp breached the contract by hiring someone else, the teacher found substitute employment at a salary of $6,000 for the same 10-week period. As a result, the teacher can recover expectation damages of $4,000 ($10,000 − $6,000). In addition, the teacher can recover the $200 in travel expenses incurred in attempting to find substitute employment (Choice A). Therefore, the teacher is entitled to recover $4,200 in total. (Choices C & D) The summer camp and summer school jobs were for the same 10-week period—ie, the teacher could not have worked both jobs simultaneously. Consequently, $10,000 (or $10,200) would overcompensate the teacher for her loss rather than place her in the same position had the contract been performed.\n\nEducational objective: Any losses that the nonbreaching party avoided or could have avoided (ie, mitigated) post- breach are subtracted from the total damage award. However, reasonable expenses incurred in an effort to mitigate damages are recoverable.","educationalObjective":"Any losses that the nonbreaching party avoided or could have avoided (ie, mitigated) post- breach are subtracted from the total damage award. However, reasonable expenses incurred in an effort to mitigate damages are recoverable.","references":"","rule":"Any losses that the nonbreaching party avoided or could have avoided (ie, mitigated) post- breach are subtracted from the total damage award. However, reasonable expenses incurred in an effort to mitigate damages are recoverable.","irac":{"issue":"How much is the teacher entitled to recover as damages in a breach-of-contract action against the camp?","rule":"Any losses that the nonbreaching party avoided or could have avoided (ie, mitigated) post- breach are subtracted from the total damage award. However, reasonable expenses incurred in an effort to mitigate damages are recoverable.","analysis":"Expectation (ie, benefit-of-the-bargain) damages\n\nDefinition Losses arising naturally & obviously from breach\n\nPurpose Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed\n\nMeasure General rule – difference between contract price & market price (or cost of purchasing substitute performance) Construction contracts – difference between contract price & cost of construction by another builder The primary purpose of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed; therefore, the nonbreaching party is entitled to recover expectation damages. Calculating these damages begins with the contract price, but any losses that the nonbreaching party avoided or could have avoided (ie, mitigated) after the breach are subtracted from the total damage award. Reasonable expenses incurred in an effort to mitigate damages, whether or not successful, are recoverable. Here, the teacher contracted to serve as the summer camp's head counselor at a salary of $10,000 for 10 weeks. After the summer camp breached the contract by hiring someone else, the teacher found substitute employment at a salary of $6,000 for the same 10-week period. As a result, the teacher can recover expectation damages of $4,000 ($10,000 − $6,000). In addition, the teacher can recover the $200 in travel expenses incurred in attempting to find substitute employment (Choice A). Therefore, the teacher is entitled to recover $4,200 in total. (Choices C & D) The summer camp and summer school jobs were for the same 10-week period—ie, the teacher could not have worked both jobs simultaneously. Consequently, $10,000 (or $10,200) would overcompensate the teacher for her loss rather than place her in the same position had the contract been performed.","conclusion":"Answer B: $4,200."},"questionZh":"1月份，一名教师与一个夏令营签订了合同，担任该夏令营的首席辅导员，从6月1日到8月中旬，为期10周，薪水为1万美元。今年三月，营地通知该老师，已聘请其他人担任首席辅导员，不再需要该老师的服务。 4月份，这位老师花了200美元前往附近唯一的另一个夏令营面试首席辅导员的职位。她没有被选中担任那项工作。随后，这位老师在当地一所暑期学校任教，为期 10 周的薪水为 6,000 美元。在针对营地的违约诉讼中，教师有权获得多少赔偿金？","choicesZh":{"A":"4,000 美元。","B":"4,200 美元。","C":"10,000 美元。","D":"10,200 美元。"},"explanationZh":"预期（即讨价还价）损害\n\n定义 自然且明显因违约而产生的损失\n\n目的 将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的位置\n\n措施 一般规则 – 合同价格与市场价格（或购买替代履约成本）之间的差额 施工合同 – 合同价格与其他建筑商的施工成本之间的差额 合同损害赔偿的主要目的是使非违约方处于与已履行合同相同的地位；因此，守约方有权要求赔偿预期损失。这些损害赔偿从合同价格开始计算，但违约方在违约后避免或本可以避免（即减轻）的任何损失将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。为减轻损害而产生的合理费用，无论是否成功，都是可以收回的。在这里，老师签约担任夏令营的首席辅导员，薪水为10,000美元，为期10周。在夏令营违反合同聘用他人后，这位老师找到了替代工作，薪水为 6,000 美元，为期 10 周。因此，教师可以获得 4,000 美元（10,000 美元 – 6,000 美元）的预期赔偿。此外，教师还可以收回因试图寻找替代工作而产生的 200 美元旅费（选择 A）。因此，该老师有权总共追讨$4,200。 （选项 C 和 D） 夏令营和暑期学校的工作时间相同，为期 10 周，即教师不能同时从事这两项工作。因此，10,000 美元（或 10,200 美元）会过度补偿老师的损失，而不是让她处于合同履行时的相同位置。\n\n教育目标：从总损害赔偿金中扣除非违约方在违约后避免或本可以避免（即减轻）的任何损失。然而，为减轻损失而产生的合理费用是可以收回的。","educationalObjectiveZh":"非违约方在违约后避免或本可以避免（即减轻）的任何损失将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。然而，为减轻损失而产生的合理费用是可以收回的。","iracZh":{"issue":"在针对营地的违约诉讼中，教师有权获得多少赔偿金？","rule":"非违约方在违约后避免或本可以避免（即减轻）的任何损失将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。然而，为减轻损失而产生的合理费用是可以收回的。","analysis":"预期（即讨价还价）损害\n\n定义 自然且明显因违约而产生的损失\n\n目的 将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的位置\n\n措施 一般规则 – 合同价格与市场价格（或购买替代履约成本）之间的差额 施工合同 – 合同价格与其他建筑商的施工成本之间的差额 合同损害赔偿的主要目的是使非违约方处于与已履行合同相同的地位；因此，守约方有权要求赔偿预期损失。这些损害赔偿从合同价格开始计算，但违约方在违约后避免或本可以避免（即减轻）的任何损失将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。为减轻损害而产生的合理费用，无论是否成功，都是可以收回的。在这里，老师签约担任夏令营的首席辅导员，薪水为10,000美元，为期10周。在夏令营违反合同聘用他人后，这位老师找到了替代工作，薪水为 6,000 美元，为期 10 周。因此，教师可以获得 4,000 美元（10,000 美元 – 6,000 美元）的预期赔偿。此外，教师还可以收回因试图寻找替代工作而产生的 200 美元旅费（选择 A）。因此，该老师有权总共追讨$4,200。 （选项 C 和 D） 夏令营和暑期学校的工作时间相同，为期 10 周，即教师不能同时从事这两项工作。因此，10,000 美元（或 10,200 美元）会过度补偿老师的损失，而不是让她处于合同履行时的相同位置。\n\n教育目标：从总损害赔偿金中扣除非违约方在违约后避免或本可以避免（即减轻）的任何损失。然而，为减轻损失而产生的合理费用是可以收回的。","conclusion":"答案 B：4,200 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":202,"sourceQuestionPage":597,"sourceQuestionPages":[597],"sourceExplanationPages":[598],"sourceAnswerPages":[598],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-203","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"The owner of a fleet of taxis contracted with a dealer in petroleum products for the purchase and sale of the fleet owner's total requirements of gasoline and oil for one year. As part of that agreement, the petroleum dealer also agreed with the fleet owner that for one year the petroleum dealer would place all his advertising with the fleet owner's wife, who owned her own small advertising agency. When the wife was informed of the contract, she declined to accept an advertising account from another company because she could not handle both accounts during the same year. During the first month of the contract, the fleet owner purchased substantial amounts of his gasoline from a supplier other than the petroleum dealer, and the petroleum dealer thereupon notified the wife that he would no longer place his advertising with her agency. In an action by the wife against the petroleum dealer for breach of contract, will the wife succeed?","choices":{"A":"No, because the failure of a constructive condition precedent excused the petroleum dealer's duty to place his advertising with the wife.","B":"No, because the wife did not provide any consideration to support the petroleum dealer's promise to place his advertising with her.","C":"Yes, because she is a third-party beneficiary of the contract.","D":"Yes, because the fleet owner was acting as the wife's agent when he contracted with the petroleum dealer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Common examples of constructive (ie, implied) conditions\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's performance performance\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's cooperation cooperation with that performance\n\nOrder of Simultaneous performance possible – each party's performance is performance condition concurrent to other party's duty to perform (ie, performance due simultaneously) One party's performance takes time – party's completion of longer performance is condition precedent to other party's duty to perform The wife is an intended beneficiary—ie, a nonparty to a contract whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly—of the contract between the fleet owner and the petroleum dealer. As a result, she has contractual rights and may sue to enforce them (Choice C). However, when the original contracting parties each make a promise to the other, each party's performance of its own promise is a constructive condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. If either party fails to substantially perform, the other party's duty is excused.* Here, the fleet owner promised to purchase its total gasoline and oil requirements from the petroleum dealer, and the petroleum dealer promised to use the wife's advertising agency in return. It is implied that each party's performance is a constructive condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. And since the fleet owner failed to substantially perform his end of the bargain by purchasing substantial amounts of gasoline from another supplier, the petroleum dealer's duty to use the wife's agency was excused. Therefore, she will not succeed. *In contrast, express conditions (ie, ones that appear in the contract) require strict compliance. (Choice B) Although the petroleum dealer received no consideration from the wife, his agreement with the fleet owner was supported by consideration. That is because the petroleum dealer's promise was induced by the fleet owner's promise to purchase gasoline and oil (and vice versa). And the wife, as an intended beneficiary, could sue to enforce her rights. But she will not succeed, because the petroleum dealer's duty was excused. (Choice D) The fleet owner was not acting as an agent for the wife because she did not direct him to enter into the contract on her behalf. Instead, she was an intended third-party beneficiary of the contract.\n\nEducational objective: Each party's performance of its own promise is a constructive (ie, implied) condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. If either party fails to substantially perform, the other party's duty is excused.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 234 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (order of performances).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","educationalObjective":"Each party's performance of its own promise is a constructive (ie, implied) condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. If either party fails to substantially perform, the other party's duty is excused.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 234 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (order of performances).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 302 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (intended and incidental beneficiaries).","rule":"Each party's performance of its own promise is a constructive (ie, implied) condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. If either party fails to substantially perform, the other party's duty is excused.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the wife against the petroleum dealer for breach of contract, will the wife succeed?","rule":"Each party's performance of its own promise is a constructive (ie, implied) condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. If either party fails to substantially perform, the other party's duty is excused.","analysis":"Common examples of constructive (ie, implied) conditions\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's performance performance\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's cooperation cooperation with that performance\n\nOrder of Simultaneous performance possible – each party's performance is performance condition concurrent to other party's duty to perform (ie, performance due simultaneously) One party's performance takes time – party's completion of longer performance is condition precedent to other party's duty to perform The wife is an intended beneficiary—ie, a nonparty to a contract whom the contracting parties intended to benefit directly—of the contract between the fleet owner and the petroleum dealer. As a result, she has contractual rights and may sue to enforce them (Choice C). However, when the original contracting parties each make a promise to the other, each party's performance of its own promise is a constructive condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. If either party fails to substantially perform, the other party's duty is excused.* Here, the fleet owner promised to purchase its total gasoline and oil requirements from the petroleum dealer, and the petroleum dealer promised to use the wife's advertising agency in return. It is implied that each party's performance is a constructive condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. And since the fleet owner failed to substantially perform his end of the bargain by purchasing substantial amounts of gasoline from another supplier, the petroleum dealer's duty to use the wife's agency was excused. Therefore, she will not succeed. *In contrast, express conditions (ie, ones that appear in the contract) require strict compliance. (Choice B) Although the petroleum dealer received no consideration from the wife, his agreement with the fleet owner was supported by consideration. That is because the petroleum dealer's promise was induced by the fleet owner's promise to purchase gasoline and oil (and vice versa). And the wife, as an intended beneficiary, could sue to enforce her rights. But she will not succeed, because the petroleum dealer's duty was excused. (Choice D) The fleet owner was not acting as an agent for the wife because she did not direct him to enter into the contract on her behalf. Instead, she was an intended third-party beneficiary of the contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the failure of a constructive condition precedent excused the petroleum dealer's duty to place his advertising with the wife."},"questionZh":"出租车车队的所有者与石油产品经销商签订了合同，购买和销售车队所有者一年内的汽油和石油总需求。作为该协议的一部分，石油经销商还与车队所有者达成一致，在一年内，石油经销商将把所有广告投放给车队所有者的妻子，她拥有自己的小型广告公司。当妻子得知合同后，她拒绝接受另一家公司的广告帐户，因为她无法在同一年处理这两个帐户。在合同的第一个月，车队所有者从石油经销商以外的供应商处购买了大量汽油，石油经销商随即通知妻子，他将不再在她的代理机构上投放广告。妻子起诉石油商违约，妻子会胜诉吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为推定条件先例的失败免除了石油经销商向妻子投放广告的责任。","B":"不，因为妻子没有提供任何报酬来支持石油经销商向她投放广告的承诺。","C":"是的，因为她是合同的第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。","D":"是的，因为车队老板与石油经销商签订合同时是作为妻子的代理人。"},"explanationZh":"推定（即隐含）条件的常见示例\n\n各方的履行情况以另一方的履行情况为条件\n\n各方履行的条件以另一方对该履行的配合为条件\n\n可能同时履行的顺序——每一方的履行是与另一方履行义务同时履行的履行条件（即同时到期的履行） 一方的履行需要时间——一方完成较长时间的履行是另一方履行义务的先决条件 妻子是船队所有者和石油经销商之间的合同的预期受益人，即缔约方打算直接受益的合同的非当事人。因此，她拥有合同权利并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利（选择 C）。然而，当原缔约方各自向对方做出承诺时，每一方履行自己的承诺是另一方履行义务的推定条件。如果任何一方未能实质性履行，则另一方的义务被免除。*在这里，车队所有者承诺从石油经销商处购买其全部汽油和石油需求，而石油经销商承诺使用妻子的广告公司作为回报。这意味着各方的履行是另一方履行义务的先决条件。由于车队所有者未能从另一家供应商处购买大量汽油，从而实质上履行了他的协议，石油经销商利用妻子代理机构的责任就被免除了。所以，她不会成功。 *相反，明示条件（即合同中出现的条件）需要严格遵守。 （选项B） 虽然石油商没有得到妻子的对价，但他与船队老板的协议却得到了对价的支持。这是因为石油经销商的承诺是由车队所有者购买汽油和石油的承诺诱导的（反之亦然）。妻子作为预期受益人可以提起诉讼以维护自己的权利。但她不会成功，因为石油经销商的职责被免除了。 （选项D） 车队所有者不是妻子的代理人，因为她没有指示他代表她签订合同。相反，她是合同的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。\n\n教育目标：各方履行自己的承诺是另一方履行义务的推定（即默示）先决条件。如果任何一方未能实质性履行，则另一方的义务被免除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 234（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行顺序）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"各方履行自己的承诺是另一方履行义务的推定（即默示）先决条件。如果任何一方未能实质性履行，则另一方的义务被免除。","iracZh":{"issue":"妻子起诉石油商违约，妻子会胜诉吗？","rule":"各方履行自己的承诺是另一方履行义务的推定（即默示）先决条件。如果任何一方未能实质性履行，则另一方的义务被免除。","analysis":"推定（即隐含）条件的常见示例\n\n各方的履行情况以另一方的履行情况为条件\n\n各方履行的条件以另一方对该履行的配合为条件\n\n可能同时履行的顺序——每一方的履行是与另一方履行义务同时履行的履行条件（即同时到期的履行） 一方的履行需要时间——一方完成较长时间的履行是另一方履行义务的先决条件 妻子是船队所有者和石油经销商之间的合同的预期受益人，即缔约方打算直接受益的合同的非当事人。因此，她拥有合同权利并可以提起诉讼来强制执行这些权利（选择 C）。然而，当原缔约方各自向对方做出承诺时，每一方履行自己的承诺是另一方履行义务的推定条件。如果任何一方未能实质性履行，则另一方的义务被免除。*在这里，车队所有者承诺从石油经销商处购买其全部汽油和石油需求，而石油经销商承诺使用妻子的广告公司作为回报。这意味着各方的履行是另一方履行义务的先决条件。由于车队所有者未能从另一家供应商处购买大量汽油，从而实质上履行了他的协议，石油经销商利用妻子代理机构的责任就被免除了。所以，她不会成功。 *相反，明示条件（即合同中出现的条件）需要严格遵守。 （选项B） 虽然石油商没有得到妻子的对价，但他与船队老板的协议却得到了对价的支持。这是因为石油经销商的承诺是由车队所有者购买汽油和石油的承诺诱导的（反之亦然）。妻子作为预期受益人可以提起诉讼以维护自己的权利。但她不会成功，因为石油经销商的职责被免除了。 （选项D） 车队所有者不是妻子的代理人，因为她没有指示他代表她签订合同。相反，她是合同的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。\n\n教育目标：各方履行自己的承诺是另一方履行义务的推定（即默示）先决条件。如果任何一方未能实质性履行，则另一方的义务被免除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 234（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行顺序）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 302（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期受益人和偶然受益人（incidental beneficiary））。","conclusion":"答案 A：不，因为推定条件先例的失败免除了石油经销商向妻子投放广告的责任。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":203,"sourceQuestionPage":599,"sourceQuestionPages":[599],"sourceExplanationPages":[600,601],"sourceAnswerPages":[600,601],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-204","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"By the terms of a written contract signed by both parties on January 15, an electronics store agreed to sell a specific personal computer to a college student for $3,000, and the college student agreed to pick up and pay for the computer at the store on February 1. The college student unjustifiably repudiated on February 1. Without notifying the college student, the electronics store subsequently sold at private sale the same specific computer to another customer, who paid the same price ($3,000) in cash. The personal computer at issue is a popular product. The electronics store can buy from the manufacturer more units than it can sell at retail. If the electronics store sues the college student for breach of contract, what will the electronics store probably recover?","choices":{"A":"$3,000 (the contract price), because the college student intentionally breached the contract by repudiation.","B":"Nothing, because the electronics store failed to give the college student proper notice of its intention to resell.","C":"Nothing, because the electronics store received a price on resale equal to the contract price that the college student had agreed to pay.","D":"The electronics store's anticipated profit on the sale to the college student plus incidental damages, if any, because the electronics store lost that sale."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When a buyer repudiates (ie, refuses to perform) a contract for the sale of goods, the seller's remedies include compensatory damages for the seller's actual losses. If the seller chooses to resell the goods, then the standard measure of damages is the difference between the contract price and the resale price plus incidental damages. But this measure of damages is inadequate when the seller is a lost-volume seller—ie: has a practically inexhaustible inventory of the type of goods in question (eg, electronics store can buy more personal computers of the kind at issue than it can sell at retail) and but for the breach, would have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one (eg, personal computer at issue is a popular product). In such cases, the seller is entitled to the lost profit from the original sale plus incidental damages arising from the breach (eg, storage or shipping costs). Therefore, the electronics store can recover the anticipated profit on the sale to the college student plus incidental damages, if any, because it lost that sale (Choice C). (Choice A) The fact that the college student intentionally breached the contract by repudiation does not entitle the electronics store to the full contract price. But had the store been unable to resell the computer after making reasonable efforts to do so at a reasonable price, then it may have been able to recover the $3,000 contract price. (Choice B) A seller must reasonably notify the buyer of its intention to resell goods at a private sale only if the seller seeks to recover the standard measure of damages. But here, the store would seek its lost profit from the breach since the standard measure would yield no damages ($3,000 contract price - $3,000 resale price = $0).\n\nEducational objective: When a seller has a practically inexhaustible inventory of the goods and but for a buyer's breach would have made two sales and two profits rather than one (ie, lost-volume seller), the seller is entitled to lost profit from the original sale plus incidental damages.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's damages for nonacceptance or repudiation).","educationalObjective":"When a seller has a practically inexhaustible inventory of the goods and but for a buyer's breach would have made two sales and two profits rather than one (ie, lost-volume seller), the seller is entitled to lost profit from the original sale plus incidental damages.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's damages for nonacceptance or repudiation).","rule":"When a seller has a practically inexhaustible inventory of the goods and but for a buyer's breach would have made two sales and two profits rather than one (ie, lost-volume seller), the seller is entitled to lost profit from the original sale plus incidental damages.","irac":{"issue":"If the electronics store sues the college student for breach of contract, what will the electronics store probably recover?","rule":"When a seller has a practically inexhaustible inventory of the goods and but for a buyer's breach would have made two sales and two profits rather than one (ie, lost-volume seller), the seller is entitled to lost profit from the original sale plus incidental damages.","analysis":"When a buyer repudiates (ie, refuses to perform) a contract for the sale of goods, the seller's remedies include compensatory damages for the seller's actual losses. If the seller chooses to resell the goods, then the standard measure of damages is the difference between the contract price and the resale price plus incidental damages. But this measure of damages is inadequate when the seller is a lost-volume seller—ie: has a practically inexhaustible inventory of the type of goods in question (eg, electronics store can buy more personal computers of the kind at issue than it can sell at retail) and but for the breach, would have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one (eg, personal computer at issue is a popular product). In such cases, the seller is entitled to the lost profit from the original sale plus incidental damages arising from the breach (eg, storage or shipping costs). Therefore, the electronics store can recover the anticipated profit on the sale to the college student plus incidental damages, if any, because it lost that sale (Choice C). (Choice A) The fact that the college student intentionally breached the contract by repudiation does not entitle the electronics store to the full contract price. But had the store been unable to resell the computer after making reasonable efforts to do so at a reasonable price, then it may have been able to recover the $3,000 contract price. (Choice B) A seller must reasonably notify the buyer of its intention to resell goods at a private sale only if the seller seeks to recover the standard measure of damages. But here, the store would seek its lost profit from the breach since the standard measure would yield no damages ($3,000 contract price - $3,000 resale price = $0).","conclusion":"Answer D: The electronics store's anticipated profit on the sale to the college student plus incidental damages, if any, because the electronics store lost that sale."},"questionZh":"根据双方于1月15日签署的书面合同条款，一家电子商店同意以3,000美元的价格向一名大学生出售一台特定的个人电脑，该大学生同意于2月1日到该商店提货并付款。该大学生于2月1日无理否认。该电子商店随后在未通知该大学生的情况下，将同一台特定的电脑私下出售给另一位顾客，该顾客以相同的价格（3,000美元）现金支付。涉案个人电脑是一种流行产品。电子产品商店可以从制造商处购买的数量多于其零售数量。如果电器店起诉大学生违约，电器店大概能追回什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"3000美元（合同价），因为该大学生故意毁约违约。","B":"没什么，因为电子商店没有向大学生发出转售意图的适当通知。","C":"没什么，因为电子商店收到的转售价格等于大学生同意支付的合同价格。","D":"电子产品商店向大学生销售产品的预期利润加上因电子产品商店失去销售而造成的附带损失（如果有）。"},"explanationZh":"当买方否认（即拒绝履行）货物销售合同时，卖方的补救措施包括赔偿卖方的实际损失。如果卖方选择转售货物，则标准损害赔偿金额为合同价格与转售价格之间的差额加上附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）。但是，当卖方是销量损失的卖方时，这种损害赔偿措施是不够的，即：拥有几乎取之不尽的相关商品类型的库存（例如，电子商店可以购买比零售销售更多的此类个人电脑），并且如果没有违约行为，则可能会进行两次销售（和两笔利润），而不是一次（例如，涉案个人电脑是一种受欢迎的产品）。在这种情况下，卖方有权获得原始销售的利润损失以及因违约而造成的附带损害（例如仓储或运输费用）。因此，电子商店可以收回向大学生销售的预期利润，加上附带损失（如果有的话），因为它失去了那次销售（选择C）。 （选项A）大学生故意毁约的事实并不能使电子商店获得全额合同价款。但如果商店在尽合理努力以合理价格转售电脑后仍无法转售，那么它可能能够收回 3,000 美元的合同价格。 （选择 B）仅当卖方寻求收回标准损害赔偿时，卖方才必须合理地通知买方其在私下销售中转售货物的意图。但在这里，商店将寻求因违约而损失的利润，因为标准措施不会产生任何损害（3,000 美元的合同价格 - 3,000 美元的转售价格 = 0 美元）。\n\n教育目标：当卖方拥有几乎用之不竭的货物库存，但如果买方违约，卖方将获得两次销售和两次利润，而不是一次（即销量损失的卖方），则卖方有权从原始销售中损失利润加上附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方因不接受或拒绝而造成的损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当卖方拥有几乎取之不尽用之不竭的货物库存时，如果买方没有违约，卖方将获得两次销售和两次利润，而不是一次（即损失数量的卖方），则卖方有权从原始销售中损失利润加上附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果电器店起诉大学生违约，电器店大概能追回什么？","rule":"当卖方拥有几乎取之不尽用之不竭的货物库存时，如果买方没有违约，卖方将获得两次销售和两次利润，而不是一次（即损失数量的卖方），则卖方有权从原始销售中损失利润加上附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）。","analysis":"当买方否认（即拒绝履行）货物销售合同时，卖方的补救措施包括赔偿卖方的实际损失。如果卖方选择转售货物，则标准损害赔偿金额为合同价格与转售价格之间的差额加上附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）。但是，当卖方是销量损失的卖方时，这种损害赔偿措施是不够的，即：拥有几乎取之不尽的相关商品类型的库存（例如，电子商店可以购买比零售销售更多的此类个人电脑），并且如果没有违约行为，则可能会进行两次销售（和两笔利润），而不是一次（例如，涉案个人电脑是一种受欢迎的产品）。在这种情况下，卖方有权获得原始销售的利润损失以及因违约而造成的附带损害（例如仓储或运输费用）。因此，电子商店可以收回向大学生销售的预期利润，加上附带损失（如果有的话），因为它失去了那次销售（选择C）。 （选项A）大学生故意毁约的事实并不能使电子商店获得全额合同价款。但如果商店在尽合理努力以合理价格转售电脑后仍无法转售，那么它可能能够收回 3,000 美元的合同价格。 （选择 B）仅当卖方寻求收回标准损害赔偿时，卖方才必须合理地通知买方其在私下销售中转售货物的意图。但在这里，商店将寻求因违约而损失的利润，因为标准措施不会产生任何损害（3,000 美元的合同价格 - 3,000 美元的转售价格 = 0 美元）。\n\n教育目标：当卖方拥有几乎用之不竭的货物库存，但如果买方违约，卖方将获得两次销售和两次利润，而不是一次（即销量损失的卖方），则卖方有权从原始销售中损失利润加上附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方因不接受或拒绝而造成的损失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：电子产品商店向大学生销售产品的预期利润加上因电子产品商店失去销售而造成的附带损失（如果有）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-dd7fb8c587ea9e40"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":204,"sourceQuestionPage":602,"sourceQuestionPages":[602],"sourceExplanationPages":[603,604],"sourceAnswerPages":[603,604],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-205","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A construction company contracted with a regional distributor to construct a warehouse and an access driveway at highway level for $500,000. Shortly after commencing work on the driveway, the construction company unexpectedly encountered a large mass of solid rock. Instead of incurring additional costs to remove the rock formation, the construction company built the access driveway over the rock with a steep grade down to the highway. The distributor, who was out of town for several days, was unaware of this nonconformity until the driveway had been finished. As built, it is too steep to be used safely by trucks or cars, particularly in the wet or icy weather frequently occurring in the area. It would cost $30,000 to tear out and rebuild the driveway at highway level. As built, the warehouse, including the driveway, has a fair market value of $550,000. The distributor has paid $470,000 to the construction company, but refuses to pay more because of the nonconforming driveway, which the construction company has refused to tear out and rebuild. If the construction company sues the distributor for monetary relief, what is the maximum amount the construction company is entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"$30,000, because the construction company substantially performed and the cost of correcting the driveway would involve economic waste.","B":"$30,000, because the fair market value of the warehouse and driveway \"as is\" exceeds the contract price by $50,000 (more than the cost of correcting the driveway).","C":"$30,000, minus whatever amount the construction company saved by not building the driveway at the specified level.","D":"Nothing, because the distributor is entitled to damages for the cost of correcting the driveway."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nWhen a contract is breached, the nonbreaching party is entitled to recover compensatory damages. Compensatory damages can arise, for example, when a builder breaches a contract during construction (ie, nonconforming construction). In that case, the owner's damages are the cost of completing or correcting the nonconforming construction, provided that this would not involve any undue economic waste. One way to recover these damages is to deduct them from the contract price owed by the owner. Here, the $500,000 contract called for the construction of a driveway at highway level. The construction company breached that contract by building the driveway over a rock mass with a steep grade that makes the driveway unsafe. The $30,000 cost to correct the driveway is not unduly wasteful since this expense is necessary to safely access the warehouse (Choice A). And since the distributor was entitled to deduct that amount from the contract price and pay no more than $470,000, the construction company can recover nothing. (Choice B) Compensatory damages attempt to put the nonbreaching party (eg, the distributor) in the same position as if the contract had been performed as promised. Therefore, the distributor was entitled to deduct his damages for the nonconforming driveway even though the value of the warehouse exceeds the contract price by a greater amount than the cost of correcting the driveway. (Choice C) If the owner breaches a construction contract prior to completion, the builder is entitled to recover the contract price minus whatever amount the builder saved by not\n\nhaving to complete the construction (ie, the cost of completion). But here, since the builder (ie, the construction company) breached, it is not entitled to recover any amount, and it is irrelevant how much was saved by not building the driveway at the specified level.\n\nEducational objective: If a builder breaches a contract during construction, the owner can recover the cost of completing or correcting the construction if it involves no undue economic waste.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 348 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the measure of damages for defective construction is the cost of completion as long as it is not clearly disproportionate to the loss in value).","educationalObjective":"If a builder breaches a contract during construction, the owner can recover the cost of completing or correcting the construction if it involves no undue economic waste.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 348 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the measure of damages for defective construction is the cost of completion as long as it is not clearly disproportionate to the loss in value).","rule":"If a builder breaches a contract during construction, the owner can recover the cost of completing or correcting the construction if it involves no undue economic waste.","irac":{"issue":"If the construction company sues the distributor for monetary relief, what is the maximum amount the construction company is entitled to recover?","rule":"If a builder breaches a contract during construction, the owner can recover the cost of completing or correcting the construction if it involves no undue economic waste.","analysis":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nWhen a contract is breached, the nonbreaching party is entitled to recover compensatory damages. Compensatory damages can arise, for example, when a builder breaches a contract during construction (ie, nonconforming construction). In that case, the owner's damages are the cost of completing or correcting the nonconforming construction, provided that this would not involve any undue economic waste. One way to recover these damages is to deduct them from the contract price owed by the owner. Here, the $500,000 contract called for the construction of a driveway at highway level. The construction company breached that contract by building the driveway over a rock mass with a steep grade that makes the driveway unsafe. The $30,000 cost to correct the driveway is not unduly wasteful since this expense is necessary to safely access the warehouse (Choice A). And since the distributor was entitled to deduct that amount from the contract price and pay no more than $470,000, the construction company can recover nothing. (Choice B) Compensatory damages attempt to put the nonbreaching party (eg, the distributor) in the same position as if the contract had been performed as promised. Therefore, the distributor was entitled to deduct his damages for the nonconforming driveway even though the value of the warehouse exceeds the contract price by a greater amount than the cost of correcting the driveway. (Choice C) If the owner breaches a construction contract prior to completion, the builder is entitled to recover the contract price minus whatever amount the builder saved by not\n\nhaving to complete the construction (ie, the cost of completion). But here, since the builder (ie, the construction company) breached, it is not entitled to recover any amount, and it is irrelevant how much was saved by not building the driveway at the specified level.","conclusion":"Answer D: Nothing, because the distributor is entitled to damages for the cost of correcting the driveway."},"questionZh":"一家建筑公司与一家地区经销商签订合同，以 50 万美元的价格建造一座仓库和一条高速公路通道。在车道上开始施工后不久，建筑公司意外地遇到了一大块坚硬的岩石。建筑公司没有花费额外的费用来清除岩层，而是在岩石上修建了通往高速公路的陡坡车道。经销商已经出城好几天了，直到车道完工后才意识到这一不符合规定的情况。建成后，它太陡了，卡车或汽车无法安全使用，特别是在该地区经常出现潮湿或结冰的天气时。拆除并重建高速公路上的车道将花费 30,000 美元。建成后，包括车道在内的仓库的公平市场价值为 550,000 美元。该分销商已向建筑公司支付了 47 万美元，但由于车道不合格而拒绝支付更多费用，而建筑公司拒绝将其拆除并重建。如果建筑公司起诉经销商要求金钱救济，建筑公司有权追回的最高金额是多少？","choicesZh":{"A":"30,000 美元，因为建筑公司表现良好，而纠正车道的成本会造成经济浪费。","B":"30,000 美元，因为仓库和车道“按原样”的公平市场价值超出合同价格 50,000 美元（超过纠正车道的成本）。","C":"30,000 美元，减去建筑公司因未按照指定高度建造车道而节省的金额。","D":"没什么，因为经销商有权就纠正车道的费用获得损害赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n违反合同时，守约方有权要求赔偿损失。例如，当建筑商在施工期间违反合同（即不合格施工）时，可能会产生补偿性损害。在这种情况下，业主的损失是完成或纠正不合格建筑的费用，前提是这不会涉及任何不当的经济浪费。弥补这些损失的一种方法是从业主所欠的合同价格中扣除。这份价值 50 万美元的合同要求在高速公路上修建一条车道。建筑公司违反了合同，将车道建造在陡峭的岩体上，导致车道不安全。纠正车道的 30,000 美元成本并不算过度浪费，因为这笔费用是安全进入仓库所必需的（选择 A）。由于经销商有权从合同价格中扣除该金额，且支付的金额不超过 47 万美元，因此建筑公司无法收回任何款项。 （选择B）补偿性损害赔偿试图使非违约方（例如经销商）处于与合同已按承诺履行相同的境地。因此，即使仓库价值超出合同价格的金额大于纠正车道的费用，经销商仍有权扣除不合格车道造成的损失。 （选择C）如果业主在竣工前违反了建筑合同，建筑商有权收回合同价款，减去建筑商因未履行合同而节省的金额。\n\n必须完成建设（即完成成本）。但在这里，由于建筑商（即建筑公司）违约，它无权追回任何金额，并且不按照规定的高度建造车道节省了多少钱也无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：如果建筑商在施工过程中违反合同，业主可以收回完成或纠正施工的费用，前提是不涉及不当的经济浪费。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 348（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，只要不明显与价值损失不成比例，缺陷建筑的损害赔偿措施就是竣工成本）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果建筑商在施工期间违反合同，在不造成不当经济浪费的情况下，业主可以收回完成或纠正施工的费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果建筑公司起诉经销商要求金钱救济，建筑公司有权追回的最高金额是多少？","rule":"如果建筑商在施工期间违反合同，在不造成不当经济浪费的情况下，业主可以收回完成或纠正施工的费用。","analysis":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n违反合同时，守约方有权要求赔偿损失。例如，当建筑商在施工期间违反合同（即不合格施工）时，可能会产生补偿性损害。在这种情况下，业主的损失是完成或纠正不合格建筑的费用，前提是这不会涉及任何不当的经济浪费。弥补这些损失的一种方法是从业主所欠的合同价格中扣除。这份价值 50 万美元的合同要求在高速公路上修建一条车道。建筑公司违反了合同，将车道建造在陡峭的岩体上，导致车道不安全。纠正车道的 30,000 美元成本并不算过度浪费，因为这笔费用是安全进入仓库所必需的（选择 A）。由于经销商有权从合同价格中扣除该金额，且支付的金额不超过 47 万美元，因此建筑公司无法收回任何款项。 （选择B）补偿性损害赔偿试图使非违约方（例如经销商）处于与合同已按承诺履行相同的境地。因此，即使仓库价值超出合同价格的金额大于纠正车道的费用，经销商仍有权扣除不合格车道造成的损失。 （选择C）如果业主在竣工前违反了建筑合同，建筑商有权收回合同价款，减去建筑商因未履行合同而节省的金额。\n\n必须完成建设（即完成成本）。但在这里，由于建筑商（即建筑公司）违约，它无权追回任何金额，并且不按照规定的高度建造车道节省了多少钱也无关紧要。\n\n教育目标：如果建筑商在施工过程中违反合同，业主可以收回完成或纠正施工的费用，前提是不涉及不当的经济浪费。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 348（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，只要不明显与价值损失不成比例，缺陷建筑的损害赔偿措施就是竣工成本）。","conclusion":"答案 D：没什么，因为经销商有权就纠正车道的费用获得损害赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":205,"sourceQuestionPage":605,"sourceQuestionPages":[605],"sourceExplanationPages":[606,607],"sourceAnswerPages":[606,607],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-206","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A baseball player contracted with the local orchestra to perform for $5,000 at a children's concert. Shortly before the concert, the baseball player became embroiled in a highly publicized controversy over whether he had cursed and assaulted a baseball fan. The orchestra canceled the contract out of concern that attendance might be adversely affected by the baseball player's appearance. The baseball player sued the orchestra for breach of contract. His business agent testified without contradiction that the cancellation had resulted in the baseball player not getting other contracts for performances and endorsements. The trial court instructed the jury, in part, as follows: \"If you find for the plaintiff, you may award damages for losses which at the time of contracting could reasonably have been foreseen by the defendant as a probable result of its breach. However, the law does not permit recovery for the loss of prospective profits of a new business caused by breach of contract.\" On the baseball player's appeal from a jury verdict for the baseball player awarding damages only for the $5,000 fee promised by the orchestra, what will be the likely result?","choices":{"A":"Affirmed, because the issue of damages for breach of contract was solely a jury question.","B":"Affirmed, because the trial court stated the law correctly.","C":"Reversed, because the test for limiting damages is what the breaching party could reasonably have foreseen at the time of the breach.","D":"Reversed, because under the prevailing modern view, lost profits of a new business are recoverable if they are established with reasonable certainty."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach After a breach of contract, the nonbreaching party can recover compensatory damages to compensate for actual economic losses. Compensatory damages include: expectation damages – the lost value of the breaching party's performance (eg, the $5,000 fee promised by the orchestra) and\n\nconsequential damages – losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances (eg, the loss of prospective profits from other contracts) that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was entered (Choice C).\n\nConsequential damages cannot be speculative. Instead, the nonbreaching party must prove the dollar amount with reasonable certainty. Here, the trial court instructed the jury that \"the law does not permit recovery for the loss of prospective profits of a new business caused by breach of contract.\" However, the law does permit recovery of such consequential damages, provided that the dollar amount is established with reasonable certainty. For this reason, the jury verdict for the baseball player awarding only expectation damages for the $5,000 fee promised by the orchestra will likely be reversed (Choice B). But even if the jury charge had been correct, it is unlikely that the baseball player would have recovered these consequential damages. That is because lost profits, especially for a new venture, are difficult to prove and there is no indication that the baseball player proved their dollar amount with reasonable certainty here. (Choice A) Although the amount of damages for breach is a question of fact for the jury, the court erred by improperly stating the law to be applied for lost profits.\n\nEducational objective: Consequential damages (eg, lost profits) are recoverable only if the nonbreaching party proves the dollar amount with reasonable certainty.","educationalObjective":"Consequential damages (eg, lost profits) are recoverable only if the nonbreaching party proves the dollar amount with reasonable certainty.","references":"","rule":"Consequential damages (eg, lost profits) are recoverable only if the nonbreaching party proves the dollar amount with reasonable certainty.","irac":{"issue":"A baseball player contracted with the local orchestra to perform for $5,000 at a children's concert. Shortly before the concert, the baseball player became embroiled in a highly publicized controversy over whether he had cursed and assaulted a baseball fan. The orchestra canceled the contract out of concern that attendance might be adversely affected by the baseball player's appearance. The baseball player sued the orchestra for breach of contract. His business agent testified without contradiction that the cancellation had resulted in the baseball player not getting other contracts for performances and endorsements. The trial court instructed the jury, in part, as follows: \"If you find for the plaintiff, you may award damages for losses which at the time of contracting could reasonably have been foreseen by the defendant as a probable result of its breach. However, the law does not permit recovery for the loss of prospective profits of a new business caused by breach of contract.\" On the baseball player's appeal from a jury verdict for the baseball player awarding damages only for the $5,000 fee promised by the orchestra, what will be the likely result?","rule":"Consequential damages (eg, lost profits) are recoverable only if the nonbreaching party proves the dollar amount with reasonable certainty.","analysis":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach After a breach of contract, the nonbreaching party can recover compensatory damages to compensate for actual economic losses. Compensatory damages include: expectation damages – the lost value of the breaching party's performance (eg, the $5,000 fee promised by the orchestra) and\n\nconsequential damages – losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances (eg, the loss of prospective profits from other contracts) that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was entered (Choice C).\n\nConsequential damages cannot be speculative. Instead, the nonbreaching party must prove the dollar amount with reasonable certainty. Here, the trial court instructed the jury that \"the law does not permit recovery for the loss of prospective profits of a new business caused by breach of contract.\" However, the law does permit recovery of such consequential damages, provided that the dollar amount is established with reasonable certainty. For this reason, the jury verdict for the baseball player awarding only expectation damages for the $5,000 fee promised by the orchestra will likely be reversed (Choice B). But even if the jury charge had been correct, it is unlikely that the baseball player would have recovered these consequential damages. That is because lost profits, especially for a new venture, are difficult to prove and there is no indication that the baseball player proved their dollar amount with reasonable certainty here. (Choice A) Although the amount of damages for breach is a question of fact for the jury, the court erred by improperly stating the law to be applied for lost profits.","conclusion":"Answer D: Reversed, because under the prevailing modern view, lost profits of a new business are recoverable if they are established with reasonable certainty."},"questionZh":"一名棒球运动员与当地管弦乐队签约，以 5,000 美元的价格在儿童音乐会上表演。音乐会前不久，这位棒球运动员卷入了一场广为人知的争议，即他是否咒骂并袭击了一名棒球迷。由于担心棒球运动员的外表可能会对观众产生不利影响，乐团取消了合同。棒球运动员起诉乐团违反合同。他的商业经纪人毫不矛盾地作证说，取消导致这位棒球运动员没有获得其他表演和代言合同。初审法院对陪审团的部分指示如下：“如果原告作出裁决，则可以就被告在签订合同时合理预见到的可能因违约而造成的损失判给损害赔偿金。但是，法律不允许赔偿因违约而造成的新业务的预期利润损失。”棒球运动员对陪审团的判决提出上诉，陪审团只判给棒球运动员赔偿乐团承诺的 5,000 美元费用，可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"肯定，因为违约损害赔偿问题只是陪审团的问题。","B":"确认，因为一审法院的法律表述正确。","C":"相反，因为限制损害赔偿的标准是违约方在违约时可以合理预见的。","D":"相反，因为根据流行的现代观点，如果新业务的建立具有合理的确定性，则其利润损失是可以弥补的。"},"explanationZh":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 违约后，守约方可以要求赔偿损失，以补偿实际经济损失。补偿性损害赔偿包括： 预期损害赔偿——违约方履行价值的损失（例如，乐团承诺的 5,000 美元费用）以及\n\n间接损害——因非违约方特殊情况（例如其他合同的预期利润损失）而造成的损失，而违约方在签订合同时可以合理预见到（选择C）。\n\n间接损失不能是推测性的。相反，非违约方必须以合理的确定性证明美元金额。在此，初审法院指示陪审团“法律不允许赔偿因违反合同而造成的新业务的预期利润损失”。然而，法律确实允许追偿此类间接损失，前提是金额具有合理的确定性。因此，陪审团对棒球运动员仅判给乐团承诺的 5,000 美元费用的预期损害赔偿的判决可能会被推翻（选项 B）。但即使陪审团的指控是正确的，棒球运动员也不太可能挽回这些间接损失。这是因为利润损失，特别是对于新企业来说，很难证明，并且没有迹象表明棒球运动员在这里以合理的确定性证明了他们的美元金额。 （选项A） 尽管违约损害赔偿金额对于陪审团来说是一个事实问题，但法院错误地阐述了利润损失所适用的法律，从而犯了错误。\n\n教育目标：只有当非违约方以合理的确定性证明美元金额时，间接损失（例如利润损失）才能得到赔偿。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有当守约方以合理的确定性证明美元金额时，间接损失（例如，利润损失）才能得到赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"一名棒球运动员与当地管弦乐队签约，以 5,000 美元的价格在儿童音乐会上表演。音乐会前不久，这位棒球运动员卷入了一场广为人知的争议，即他是否咒骂并袭击了一名棒球迷。由于担心棒球运动员的外表可能会对观众产生不利影响，乐团取消了合同。棒球运动员起诉乐团违反合同。他的商业经纪人毫不矛盾地作证说，取消导致这位棒球运动员没有获得其他表演和代言合同。初审法院对陪审团的部分指示如下：“如果原告作出裁决，则可以就被告在签订合同时合理预见到的可能因违约而造成的损失判给损害赔偿金。但是，法律不允许赔偿因违约而造成的新业务的预期利润损失。”棒球运动员对陪审团的判决提出上诉，陪审团只判给棒球运动员赔偿乐团承诺的 5,000 美元费用，可能的结果是什么？","rule":"只有当守约方以合理的确定性证明美元金额时，间接损失（例如，利润损失）才能得到赔偿。","analysis":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 违约后，守约方可以要求赔偿损失，以补偿实际经济损失。补偿性损害赔偿包括： 预期损害赔偿——违约方履行价值的损失（例如，乐团承诺的 5,000 美元费用）以及\n\n间接损害——因非违约方特殊情况（例如其他合同的预期利润损失）而造成的损失，而违约方在签订合同时可以合理预见到（选择C）。\n\n间接损失不能是推测性的。相反，非违约方必须以合理的确定性证明美元金额。在此，初审法院指示陪审团“法律不允许赔偿因违反合同而造成的新业务的预期利润损失”。然而，法律确实允许追偿此类间接损失，前提是金额具有合理的确定性。因此，陪审团对棒球运动员仅判给乐团承诺的 5,000 美元费用的预期损害赔偿的判决可能会被推翻（选项 B）。但即使陪审团的指控是正确的，棒球运动员也不太可能挽回这些间接损失。这是因为利润损失，特别是对于新企业来说，很难证明，并且没有迹象表明棒球运动员在这里以合理的确定性证明了他们的美元金额。 （选项A） 尽管违约损害赔偿金额对于陪审团来说是一个事实问题，但法院错误地阐述了利润损失所适用的法律，从而犯了错误。\n\n教育目标：只有当非违约方以合理的确定性证明美元金额时，间接损失（例如利润损失）才能得到赔偿。","conclusion":"答案 D：相反，因为根据流行的现代观点，如果新业务的建立具有合理的确定性，则其利润损失是可以弥补的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":206,"sourceQuestionPage":608,"sourceQuestionPages":[608],"sourceExplanationPages":[609,610],"sourceAnswerPages":[609,610],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-207","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A homeowner and a contractor entered into a contract for the construction of a home for $300,000. The contractor was to earn a profit of $10,000 for the job. After the contractor had spent $45,000 on labor and materials, including $5,000 on oak flooring not yet installed, the homeowner informed the contractor that the homeowner had lost her job and could not pay the contractor for his services. The homeowner told the contractor to stop working immediately. The reasonable market value of the labor and materials provided by the contractor at that point, including the oak flooring, was $40,000. The contractor used the oak flooring on another job. In an action by the contractor against the homeowner for damages, which of the following would be the largest amount of damages recoverable by the contractor?","choices":{"A":"$40,000, the contractor's construction costs.","B":"$40,000, the reasonable value of the services the contractor had provided.","C":"$50,000, the contractor's construction costs of $45,000 plus the $10,000 profit minus the $5,000 saved by reusing the oak flooring on another job.","D":"$55,000, the contractor's construction costs of $45,000 plus the $10,000 profit."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nCompensatory damages are intended to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed (ie, give that party the benefit of the bargain). Where an owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder is entitled to recover the profits it would have earned had the contract been completed plus costs incurred up to that point. However, any costs saved or otherwise avoided (ie, mitigated) by the builder will be subtracted to arrive at the final damages award. Here, the contractor had spent $45,000 on labor and materials when the homeowner stopped the work. And the contractor had expected to make a $10,000 profit for this job. As a result, he would be entitled to recover $55,000 except that he saved $5,000 by using the oak flooring on another job. Therefore, the largest amount of damages recoverable by the contractor is $50,000 ($10,000 expected profit + $45,000 costs incurred - $5,000 costs saved) (Choice D). (Choice A) The contractor's $40,000 construction costs ($45,000 costs incurred - $5,000 costs saved) represent his reliance damages—ie, damages intended to place the injured party in the same position as if the contract had never been entered. But this measure of recovery fails to account for the contractor's $10,000 anticipated profit. (Choice B) Restitution damages are measured by the benefit conferred on another party. But here, the $40,000 amount (reasonable value of the services the contractor had provided) is an incorrect measure of restitution damages since it fails to deduct the $5,000\n\nworth of oak flooring the contractor used on another job. $35,000 would be the correct measure, and this represents the smallest amount of recoverable damages.\n\nEducational objective: When an owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder's damages are equal to the builder's lost profits plus costs incurred, minus any costs saved or otherwise avoided.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 344 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (purpose of remedies).","educationalObjective":"When an owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder's damages are equal to the builder's lost profits plus costs incurred, minus any costs saved or otherwise avoided.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 344 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (purpose of remedies).","rule":"When an owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder's damages are equal to the builder's lost profits plus costs incurred, minus any costs saved or otherwise avoided.","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the contractor against the homeowner for damages, which of the following would be the largest amount of damages recoverable by the contractor?","rule":"When an owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder's damages are equal to the builder's lost profits plus costs incurred, minus any costs saved or otherwise avoided.","analysis":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nCompensatory damages are intended to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed (ie, give that party the benefit of the bargain). Where an owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder is entitled to recover the profits it would have earned had the contract been completed plus costs incurred up to that point. However, any costs saved or otherwise avoided (ie, mitigated) by the builder will be subtracted to arrive at the final damages award. Here, the contractor had spent $45,000 on labor and materials when the homeowner stopped the work. And the contractor had expected to make a $10,000 profit for this job. As a result, he would be entitled to recover $55,000 except that he saved $5,000 by using the oak flooring on another job. Therefore, the largest amount of damages recoverable by the contractor is $50,000 ($10,000 expected profit + $45,000 costs incurred - $5,000 costs saved) (Choice D). (Choice A) The contractor's $40,000 construction costs ($45,000 costs incurred - $5,000 costs saved) represent his reliance damages—ie, damages intended to place the injured party in the same position as if the contract had never been entered. But this measure of recovery fails to account for the contractor's $10,000 anticipated profit. (Choice B) Restitution damages are measured by the benefit conferred on another party. But here, the $40,000 amount (reasonable value of the services the contractor had provided) is an incorrect measure of restitution damages since it fails to deduct the $5,000\n\nworth of oak flooring the contractor used on another job. $35,000 would be the correct measure, and this represents the smallest amount of recoverable damages.","conclusion":"Answer C: $50,000, the contractor's construction costs of $45,000 plus the $10,000 profit minus the $5,000 saved by reusing the oak flooring on another job."},"questionZh":"房主和承包商签订了一份价值 30 万美元的房屋建造合​​同。承包商将为这项工作赚取 10,000 美元的利润。在承包商花费了 45,000 美元的人工和材料费用（其中包括 5,000 美元用于尚未安装的橡木地板）后，房主通知承包商，房主失业了，无法向承包商支付服务费用。房主告诉承包商立即停止工作。当时承包商提供的劳动力和材料（包括橡木地板）的合理市场价值为 40,000 美元。承包商在另一项工作中使用了橡木地板。在承包商向房主提起损害赔偿诉讼时，以下哪项是承包商可追回的最大损害赔偿金额？","choicesZh":{"A":"40,000 美元，承包商的建筑成本。","B":"40,000 美元，承包商提供的服务的合理价值。","C":"50,000 美元，承包商的建筑成本为 45,000 美元，加上 10,000 美元利润，减去在另一项工作中重复使用橡木地板节省的 5,000 美元。","D":"55,000 美元，承包商的建筑成本为 45,000 美元加上 10,000 美元的利润。"},"explanationZh":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n补偿性损害赔偿旨在使非违约方处于与合同已履行相同的地位（即，让该方从交易中获益）。如果业主在施工前或施工过程中违反施工合同，建筑商有权收回合同完成时本应赚取的利润以及截至该点所发生的成本。然而，建筑商节省或以其他方式避免（即减轻）的任何成本将被减去，以得出最终的损害赔偿金。当房主停止施工时，承包商已在人工和材料上花费了 45,000 美元。承包商预计这项工作将获得 10,000 美元的利润。因此，他有权收回 55,000 美元，除非他在另一项工作中使用橡木地板节省了 5,000 美元。因此，承包商可赔偿的最大损失金额为 50,000 美元（10,000 美元预期利润 + 45,000 美元发生成本 - 5,000 美元节省成本）（选项 D）。 （选择A） 承包商的40,000 美元建筑成本（产生的成本为45,000 美元 - 节省的成本为5,000 美元）代表了他的信赖损害赔偿，即旨在使受害方处于与从未签订合同相同的境地的损害赔偿。但这种回收措施未能考虑到承包商 10,000 美元的预期利润。 （选择B）返还利益以赋予另一方的利益衡量。但在这里，40,000 美元的金额（承包商提供的服务的合理价值）是赔偿损失的不正确衡量标准，因为它未能扣除 5,000 美元\n\n承包商在另一项工作中使用的橡木地板的价值。 35,000 美元是正确的衡量标准，这代表了可恢复损失的最小金额。\n\n教育目标：当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商的损失等于建筑商的利润损失加上发生的成本，减去节省或以其他方式避免的任何成本。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 344（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（补救措施的目的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商的损失等于建筑商的利润损失加上发生的成本，减去节省或以其他方式避免的成本。","iracZh":{"issue":"在承包商向房主提起损害赔偿诉讼时，以下哪项是承包商可追回的最大损害赔偿金额？","rule":"当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商的损失等于建筑商的利润损失加上发生的成本，减去节省或以其他方式避免的成本。","analysis":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n补偿性损害赔偿旨在使非违约方处于与合同已履行相同的地位（即，让该方从交易中获益）。如果业主在施工前或施工过程中违反施工合同，建筑商有权收回合同完成时本应赚取的利润以及截至该点所发生的成本。然而，建筑商节省或以其他方式避免（即减轻）的任何成本将被减去，以得出最终的损害赔偿金。当房主停止施工时，承包商已在人工和材料上花费了 45,000 美元。承包商预计这项工作将获得 10,000 美元的利润。因此，他有权收回 55,000 美元，除非他在另一项工作中使用橡木地板节省了 5,000 美元。因此，承包商可赔偿的最大损失金额为 50,000 美元（10,000 美元预期利润 + 45,000 美元发生成本 - 5,000 美元节省成本）（选项 D）。 （选择A） 承包商的40,000 美元建筑成本（产生的成本为45,000 美元 - 节省的成本为5,000 美元）代表了他的信赖损害赔偿，即旨在使受害方处于与从未签订合同相同的境地的损害赔偿。但这种回收措施未能考虑到承包商 10,000 美元的预期利润。 （选择B）返还利益以赋予另一方的利益衡量。但在这里，40,000 美元的金额（承包商提供的服务的合理价值）是赔偿损失的不正确衡量标准，因为它未能扣除 5,000 美元\n\n承包商在另一项工作中使用的橡木地板的价值。 35,000 美元是正确的衡量标准，这代表了可恢复损失的最小金额。\n\n教育目标：当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商的损失等于建筑商的利润损失加上发生的成本，减去节省或以其他方式避免的任何成本。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 344（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（补救措施的目的）。","conclusion":"答案 C：50,000 美元，承包商的建筑成本为 45,000 美元，加上 10,000 美元利润，减去在另一项工作中重复使用橡木地板节省的 5,000 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":207,"sourceQuestionPage":611,"sourceQuestionPages":[611],"sourceExplanationPages":[612,613],"sourceAnswerPages":[612,613],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-208","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A landowner contracted in a signed writing to sell a 500-acre tract of farmland to a farmer. The contract provided for exchange of the deed and purchase price of $500,000 in cash on January 15. Possession was to be given to the farmer on the same date. On January 15, the landowner notified the farmer that because the tenant on the farmland wrongfully refused to quit the premises until January 30, the landowner would be unable to deliver possession of the farmland until then, but he assured the farmer that he would tender the deed and possession on that date. When the landowner tendered the deed and possession on January 30, the farmer refused to accept either, and refused to pay the $500,000. Throughout the month of January, the market value of the farmland was $510,000, and its fair monthly rental value was $5,000. Will the landowner likely succeed in an action against the farmer for specific performance?","choices":{"A":"No, because the landowner breached by failing to tender the deed and possession on January 15.","B":"No, because the landowner's remedy at law for monetary relief is adequate.","C":"Yes, because the court will excuse the delay in tender on the ground that there was a temporary impossibility caused by the tenant's holding over.","D":"Yes, because time is ordinarily not of the essence in a land-sale contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Seller's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement & recover possession of property restitution Rental value & physical damages are recoverable Buyer not liable for purchase price Specific Seek an injunction ordering buyer to pay agreed purchase price performance May be unavailable if money damages provide adequate compensation & property is not unique eg, condominium identical to other units in complex Damages Loss of bargain = contract price – market value at time of breach Liquidated damages = amount designated in the sales contract as compensation for breach Retain buyer's deposit (up to amount of seller's actual damages) Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable In a land-sale contract, the seller is required to deliver marketable title to the buyer at the closing date. Failure to do so constitutes a breach of the contract. But if the seller conveys the land within a reasonable time after the closing date, the buyer cannot withhold payment. That is because time is ordinarily not of the essence in a land-sale contract unless the circumstances indicate that performance by that date is important. Here, the landowner breached the contract by failing to tender the deed and possession of the farm to the farmer on January 15 (closing date). But the landowner did so on January 30 (reasonable time after closing). The circumstances do not indicate that time was of the essence, so the farmer breached the contract by refusing to pay the purchase price (Choice A). A seller's potential remedies for a buyer's breach include specific performance, which allows the court to issue an injunction that orders the buyer to pay the agreed purchase price. Specific performance is appropriate when monetary damages are inadequate (eg, unique land). Farms are unique since each has different characteristics. Therefore, the landowner can obtain specific performance as a remedy against the farmer (Choice B). (Choice C) Performance is discharged (ie, excused) when an unexpected and extraordinary event beyond the parties' control makes performance impossible. Here, the landowner should have known that the tenant on the farmland could hold over. As a result, the landowner's delay is not excused due to impossibility.\n\nEducational objective: Time is ordinarily not of the essence in a contract for the sale of land, so the buyer cannot withhold payment if the seller conveys the land within a reasonable time after closing. If the buyer breaches by refusing to pay, the seller can obtain specific performance when monetary damages would be inadequate (eg, unique land).\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 242 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of delayed performance).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 445 (2020) (seller's remedies).","educationalObjective":"Time is ordinarily not of the essence in a contract for the sale of land, so the buyer cannot withhold payment if the seller conveys the land within a reasonable time after closing. If the buyer breaches by refusing to pay, the seller can obtain specific performance when monetary damages would be inadequate (eg, unique land).","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 242 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of delayed performance).\n\n77 Am. Jur. 2d Vendor and Purchaser § 445 (2020) (seller's remedies).","rule":"Time is ordinarily not of the essence in a contract for the sale of land, so the buyer cannot withhold payment if the seller conveys the land within a reasonable time after closing. If the buyer breaches by refusing to pay, the seller can obtain specific performance when monetary damages would be inadequate (eg, unique land).","irac":{"issue":"Will the landowner likely succeed in an action against the farmer for specific performance?","rule":"Time is ordinarily not of the essence in a contract for the sale of land, so the buyer cannot withhold payment if the seller conveys the land within a reasonable time after closing. If the buyer breaches by refusing to pay, the seller can obtain specific performance when monetary damages would be inadequate (eg, unique land).","analysis":"Seller's remedies\n\nRescission & Rescind (ie, cancel) agreement & recover possession of property restitution Rental value & physical damages are recoverable Buyer not liable for purchase price Specific Seek an injunction ordering buyer to pay agreed purchase price performance May be unavailable if money damages provide adequate compensation & property is not unique eg, condominium identical to other units in complex Damages Loss of bargain = contract price – market value at time of breach Liquidated damages = amount designated in the sales contract as compensation for breach Retain buyer's deposit (up to amount of seller's actual damages) Incidental & consequential damages that were contemplated during sales contract or reasonably foreseeable In a land-sale contract, the seller is required to deliver marketable title to the buyer at the closing date. Failure to do so constitutes a breach of the contract. But if the seller conveys the land within a reasonable time after the closing date, the buyer cannot withhold payment. That is because time is ordinarily not of the essence in a land-sale contract unless the circumstances indicate that performance by that date is important. Here, the landowner breached the contract by failing to tender the deed and possession of the farm to the farmer on January 15 (closing date). But the landowner did so on January 30 (reasonable time after closing). The circumstances do not indicate that time was of the essence, so the farmer breached the contract by refusing to pay the purchase price (Choice A). A seller's potential remedies for a buyer's breach include specific performance, which allows the court to issue an injunction that orders the buyer to pay the agreed purchase price. Specific performance is appropriate when monetary damages are inadequate (eg, unique land). Farms are unique since each has different characteristics. Therefore, the landowner can obtain specific performance as a remedy against the farmer (Choice B). (Choice C) Performance is discharged (ie, excused) when an unexpected and extraordinary event beyond the parties' control makes performance impossible. Here, the landowner should have known that the tenant on the farmland could hold over. As a result, the landowner's delay is not excused due to impossibility.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because time is ordinarily not of the essence in a land-sale contract."},"questionZh":"一位土地所有者签署了一份书面合同，将一块 500 英亩的农田出售给一位农民。合同规定于 1 月 15 日交换契约和 500,000 美元现金的购买价格。所有权将在同一天移交给农民。 1月15日，土地所有者通知农民，由于农田的租户错误地拒绝在1月30日之前搬出土地，因此土地所有者在此之前无法交付农田的所有权，但他向农民保证，他会在那一天提交契约和所有权。当土地所有者于 1 月 30 日提交契约和占有时，农民拒绝接受，并拒绝支付 50 万美元。整个 1 月份，农田的市场价值为 510,000 美元，每月公平租金为 5,000 美元。土地所有者针对农民的特定履行（specific performance）提起诉讼是否可能成功？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为土地所有者未能在 1 月 15 日提交契据和占有权，因此违反了规定。","B":"不会，因为土地所有者通过法律获得的金钱救济是足够的。","C":"是的，因为法院会以租客拖延导致暂时无法投标为由，原谅延迟投标。","D":"是的，因为时间通常在土地出售合同中并不重要。"},"explanationZh":"卖家的补救措施\n\n解除和解除（即取消）协议并恢复财产所有权归还 租金价值和有形损害可收回 买方不对购买价格承担具体责任 寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格履约 如果金钱损害提供足够的赔偿且财产不是唯一的，例如，公寓与综合体中的其他单元相同，则可能不可用 损害赔偿 议价损失 = 合同价格 – 违约时的市场价值 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 保留买方押金（最高可达卖方实际损失的金额） 销售合同期间预期或合理预见的附带和间接损失 在土地销售合同中，卖方必须在交割日向买方交付可出售的所有权。如果不这样做，就构成违反合同。但如果卖方在交割日后的合理时间内转让土地，买方就不能扣留付款。这是因为时间通常在土地销售合同中并不重要，除非情况表明在该日期之前履行很重要。此处，土地所有者未能在 1 月 15 日（截止日期）将农场的契约和所有权交给农民，从而违反了合同。但土地所有者在 1 月 30 日（关闭后的合理时间）这样做了。情况并不表明时间至关重要，因此农民拒绝支付收购价款，从而违反了合同（选项A）。卖方对买方违约行为的潜在补救措施包括特定履约，即允许法院发布禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格。当金钱损害赔偿不足时（例如，独特的土地），特定履行（specific performance）是适当的。农场是独一无二的，因为每个农场都有不同的特点。因此，土地所有者可以获得特定履约作为对农民的补救措施（选择B）。 （选择C） 当双方无法控制的意外和特殊事件导致无法履行时，履行被解除（即免除）。在这里，土地所有者应该知道农田上的佃户可以继续持有。因此，土地所有者的拖延并不能因为不可能而得到原谅。\n\n教育目的：在土地出售合同中，时间通常不是最重要的，因此，如果卖方在成交后的合理时间内转让土地，则买方不能扣留付款。如果买方因拒绝付款而违约，则当金钱赔偿不足以（例如，独特的土地）时，卖方可以获得特定履约。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 242（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（延迟履行的影响）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 445 (2020)（卖方的补救措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在土地出售合同中，时间通常不是最重要的，因此如果卖方在成交后的合理时间内转让土地，买方不能扣留付款。如果买方因拒绝付款而违约，则当金钱赔偿不足以（例如，独特的土地）时，卖方可以获得特定履约。","iracZh":{"issue":"土地所有者针对农民的特定履行（specific performance）提起诉讼是否可能成功？","rule":"在土地出售合同中，时间通常不是最重要的，因此如果卖方在成交后的合理时间内转让土地，买方不能扣留付款。如果买方因拒绝付款而违约，则当金钱赔偿不足以（例如，独特的土地）时，卖方可以获得特定履约。","analysis":"卖家的补救措施\n\n解除和解除（即取消）协议并恢复财产所有权归还 租金价值和有形损害可收回 买方不对购买价格承担具体责任 寻求禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格履约 如果金钱损害提供足够的赔偿且财产不是唯一的，例如，公寓与综合体中的其他单元相同，则可能不可用 损害赔偿 议价损失 = 合同价格 – 违约时的市场价值 违约赔偿金 = 销售合同中指定的违约赔偿金额 保留买方押金（最高可达卖方实际损失的金额） 销售合同期间预期或合理预见的附带和间接损失 在土地销售合同中，卖方必须在交割日向买方交付可出售的所有权。如果不这样做，就构成违反合同。但如果卖方在交割日后的合理时间内转让土地，买方就不能扣留付款。这是因为时间通常在土地销售合同中并不重要，除非情况表明在该日期之前履行很重要。此处，土地所有者未能在 1 月 15 日（截止日期）将农场的契约和所有权交给农民，从而违反了合同。但土地所有者在 1 月 30 日（关闭后的合理时间）这样做了。情况并不表明时间至关重要，因此农民拒绝支付收购价款，从而违反了合同（选项A）。卖方对买方违约行为的潜在补救措施包括特定履约，即允许法院发布禁令，命令买方支付约定的购买价格。当金钱损害赔偿不足时（例如，独特的土地），特定履行（specific performance）是适当的。农场是独一无二的，因为每个农场都有不同的特点。因此，土地所有者可以获得特定履约作为对农民的补救措施（选择B）。 （选择C） 当双方无法控制的意外和特殊事件导致无法履行时，履行被解除（即免除）。在这里，土地所有者应该知道农田上的佃户可以继续持有。因此，土地所有者的拖延并不能因为不可能而得到原谅。\n\n教育目的：在土地出售合同中，时间通常不是最重要的，因此，如果卖方在成交后的合理时间内转让土地，则买方不能扣留付款。如果买方因拒绝付款而违约，则当金钱赔偿不足以（例如，独特的土地）时，卖方可以获得特定履约。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 242（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（延迟履行的影响）。\n\n77 上午。尤尔。 2d 卖方和买方 § 445 (2020)（卖方的补救措施）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为时间通常在土地出售合同中并不重要。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":208,"sourceQuestionPage":614,"sourceQuestionPages":[614],"sourceExplanationPages":[615,616],"sourceAnswerPages":[615,616],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-209","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Breach, Cure & Installments","question":"A wholesaler contracted in a signed writing to sell to a bakery 10,000 pounds of flour each week for 10 weeks, the flour to be delivered to the bakery on Mondays and payment to be made on Wednesdays of each week. The bakery did all of its weekly bread baking on Tuesdays. On Monday morning of the first week, the wholesaler tendered delivery of 8,000 pounds of flour to the bakery, and the bakery accepted it on the wholesaler's assurance that the remaining 2,000 pounds would be delivered later that evening, which it was. The bakery paid for both deliveries on Wednesday. On Monday of the second week, the wholesaler tendered delivery of 5,000 pounds of flour to the bakery and said that the remaining 5,000 pounds could not be delivered on Monday but would be delivered by Wednesday. The bakery rejected the tender. Was the bakery legally justified in rejecting the tender of the 5,000 pounds of flour?","choices":{"A":"No, because by accepting the first 8,000 pounds on Monday of the first week, the bakery had waived the condition of perfect tender and had not reinstated it.","B":"No, because the tender was not a substantial impairment of the entire contract, and the wholesaler had given assurance of a cure.","C":"Yes, because the bakery was legally entitled to reject any tender that did not conform perfectly to the contract.","D":"Yes, because the tender was a substantial impairment of that installment and could not be cured."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Installment contracts\n\nDefinition Goods delivered in multiple shipments that buyer separately accepts\n\nNonconforming Buyer may reject shipment only if nonconformity: shipment substantially impairs value of that shipment to buyer and cannot be cured Canceling contract Buyer may cancel entire contract if nonconformity substantially impairs value of entire contract to buyer\n\nContracts for the sale of goods generally follow the UCC's perfect-tender rule, under which a seller must tender goods conforming to the warranty obligations in the contract. Substantial performance will not suffice. But this rule does not apply to installment contracts, which provide for the delivery of goods in multiple shipments, each to be separately accepted by the buyer (as seen here). Instead, installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule. This rule allows the buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment to the buyer and cannot be cured. Here, during the second week, the wholesaler delivered only 5,000 pounds of flour instead of the 10,000 pounds promised in their installment contract. This nonconformity substantially impaired the value of that shipment and could not be cured since the remaining 5,000 pounds could not be delivered until Wednesday—one day after the bakery does its weekly bread baking. As a result, the bakery was justified in rejecting the nonconforming tender. (Choices A & C) The perfect-tender rule does not apply to installment contracts (as seen here). Additionally, the bakery's acceptance of the nonconforming tender the previous week does not waive its right to reject the nonconforming tender during the second week. That is because each shipment in an installment contract must be separately accepted by the buyer. (Choice B) The buyer may cancel an entire installment contract if a nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the entire contract to the buyer. The nonconformance here likely did not do so, but it did substantially impair that shipment. And a buyer need only accept a nonconforming shipment if the seller makes adequate assurances that he/she can timely cure the nonconformity, which the wholesaler did not do here.\n\nEducational objective: Installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule, which allows a buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment and cannot be cured. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (breach of installment contract).","educationalObjective":"Installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule, which allows a buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment and cannot be cured. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (breach of installment contract).","rule":"Installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule, which allows a buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment and cannot be cured. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Was the bakery legally justified in rejecting the tender of the 5,000 pounds of flour?","rule":"Installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule, which allows a buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment and cannot be cured. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Installment contracts\n\nDefinition Goods delivered in multiple shipments that buyer separately accepts\n\nNonconforming Buyer may reject shipment only if nonconformity: shipment substantially impairs value of that shipment to buyer and cannot be cured Canceling contract Buyer may cancel entire contract if nonconformity substantially impairs value of entire contract to buyer\n\nContracts for the sale of goods generally follow the UCC's perfect-tender rule, under which a seller must tender goods conforming to the warranty obligations in the contract. Substantial performance will not suffice. But this rule does not apply to installment contracts, which provide for the delivery of goods in multiple shipments, each to be separately accepted by the buyer (as seen here). Instead, installment contracts follow the substantial-impairment rule. This rule allows the buyer to reject tender of nonconforming goods when the nonconformity substantially impairs the value of that shipment to the buyer and cannot be cured. Here, during the second week, the wholesaler delivered only 5,000 pounds of flour instead of the 10,000 pounds promised in their installment contract. This nonconformity substantially impaired the value of that shipment and could not be cured since the remaining 5,000 pounds could not be delivered until Wednesday—one day after the bakery does its weekly bread baking. As a result, the bakery was justified in rejecting the nonconforming tender. (Choices A & C) The perfect-tender rule does not apply to installment contracts (as seen here). Additionally, the bakery's acceptance of the nonconforming tender the previous week does not waive its right to reject the nonconforming tender during the second week. That is because each shipment in an installment contract must be separately accepted by the buyer. (Choice B) The buyer may cancel an entire installment contract if a nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the entire contract to the buyer. The nonconformance here likely did not do so, but it did substantially impair that shipment. And a buyer need only accept a nonconforming shipment if the seller makes adequate assurances that he/she can timely cure the nonconformity, which the wholesaler did not do here.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the tender was a substantial impairment of that installment and could not be cured."},"questionZh":"一家批发商签署了一份书面合同，在 10 周内每周向一家面包店出售 10,000 磅面粉，每周一将面粉运送到面包店，并在每周三付款。这家面包店每周的面包烘焙工作都在周二完成。第一周的星期一早上，批发商向面包店交付了 8,000 磅面粉，面包店接受了，因为批发商保证剩余的 2,000 磅面粉将在当天晚上晚些时候交付，事实也确实如此。周三，面包店支付了两次送货费用。第二周的周一，批发商向面包店交付了 5000 磅面粉，并表示剩余的 5000 磅无法在周一交付，但会在周三交付。面包店拒绝了投标。面包店拒绝 5,000 磅面粉的投标是否合法？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为通过在第一周的星期一接受前 8,000 英镑，面包店就放弃了完美招标的条件，并且没有恢复它。","B":"不会，因为投标并没有对整个合同造成重大损害，而且批发商已经保证可以治愈。","C":"是的，因为面包店在法律上有权拒绝任何不完全符合合同的投标。","D":"是的，因为招标对该分期付款造成了重大损害，并且无法弥补。"},"explanationZh":"分期付款合同\n\n定义 买方分别接受的分批交付的货物\n\n不合格买方仅可在下列情况下拒绝装运： 装运严重损害了该批货物对买方的价值且无法修复 取消合同 如果不合格严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方可以取消整个合同\n\n货物销售合同通常遵循 UCC 的完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），根据该规则，卖方必须投标符合合同中保修义务的货物。仅仅有实质性的表现是不够的。但该规则不适用于分期付款合同，该合同规定分批交付货物，每批货物均由买方单独接受（如此处所示）。相反，分期付款合同遵循重大减值规则。当不合格品严重损害买方货物的价值且无法修复时，该规则允许买方拒绝不合格品的投标。第二周，批发商只交付了 5,000 磅面粉，而不是分期付款合同中承诺的 10,000 磅面粉。这种不合格情况严重损害了该批货物的价值，并且无法解决，因为剩余的 5,000 磅要到周三（面包店每周烘烤面包的一天后）才能交付。因此，面包店拒绝不合格投标是合理的。 （选项 A 和 C）完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule）不适用于分期付款合同（如此处所示）。此外，面包店在前一周接受不合格投标并不放弃其在第二周拒绝不合格投标的权利。这是因为分期付款合同中的每批货物都必须由买方单独接受。 （选择 B）如果不合格的装运严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方可以取消整个分期付款合同。这里的不合格品可能不会造成这种情况，但它确实严重损害了该批货物。如果卖方充分保证他/她能够及时解决不合格问题，则买方只需接受不合格货物，而批发商在此没有这样做。\n\n教育目标：分期付款合同遵循实质性损害规则，当不合格品严重损害了货物的价值且无法修复时，买方可以拒绝不合格品的投标。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（违反分期付款合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"分期付款合同遵循实质性损害规则，当不合格品严重损害了货物的价值且无法修复时，买方可以拒绝不合格品的投标。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"面包店拒绝 5,000 磅面粉的投标是否合法？","rule":"分期付款合同遵循实质性损害规则，当不合格品严重损害了货物的价值且无法修复时，买方可以拒绝不合格品的投标。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"分期付款合同\n\n定义 买方分别接受的分批交付的货物\n\n不合格买方仅可在下列情况下拒绝装运： 装运严重损害了该批货物对买方的价值且无法修复 取消合同 如果不合格严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方可以取消整个合同\n\n货物销售合同通常遵循 UCC 的完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），根据该规则，卖方必须投标符合合同中保修义务的货物。仅仅有实质性的表现是不够的。但该规则不适用于分期付款合同，该合同规定分批交付货物，每批货物均由买方单独接受（如此处所示）。相反，分期付款合同遵循重大减值规则。当不合格品严重损害买方货物的价值且无法修复时，该规则允许买方拒绝不合格品的投标。第二周，批发商只交付了 5,000 磅面粉，而不是分期付款合同中承诺的 10,000 磅面粉。这种不合格情况严重损害了该批货物的价值，并且无法解决，因为剩余的 5,000 磅要到周三（面包店每周烘烤面包的一天后）才能交付。因此，面包店拒绝不合格投标是合理的。 （选项 A 和 C）完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule）不适用于分期付款合同（如此处所示）。此外，面包店在前一周接受不合格投标并不放弃其在第二周拒绝不合格投标的权利。这是因为分期付款合同中的每批货物都必须由买方单独接受。 （选择 B）如果不合格的装运严重损害了整个合同对买方的价值，买方可以取消整个分期付款合同。这里的不合格品可能不会造成这种情况，但它确实严重损害了该批货物。如果卖方充分保证他/她能够及时解决不合格问题，则买方只需接受不合格货物，而批发商在此没有这样做。\n\n教育目标：分期付款合同遵循实质性损害规则，当不合格品严重损害了货物的价值且无法修复时，买方可以拒绝不合格品的投标。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（违反分期付款合同）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为招标对该分期付款造成了重大损害，并且无法弥补。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":209,"sourceQuestionPage":617,"sourceQuestionPages":[617],"sourceExplanationPages":[618,619],"sourceAnswerPages":[618,619],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-210","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A woman owed her friend $5,000, plus interest at 6% until paid. Collection of the debt would become barred by the statute of limitations on July 31. On the preceding May 1, the woman and the friend both signed a writing in which the woman promised to pay the debt in full on the following December 31, plus interest at 6% until that date, and the friend promised not to sue to collect the debt in the meantime. The friend became concerned about the legal effect of the May 1 agreement, and on June 1 filed suit to collect the debt. On January 2 of the following year, the friend's suit has not come to trial, and the woman has not paid the debt. The friend amends the complaint to add a second count to enforce the promise the woman made in the May 1 agreement. Does the new count state a claim upon which relief can be granted?","choices":{"A":"No, because such relief would undermine the policy of the statute of limitations against enforcement of stale claims.","B":"No, because the woman's May 1 promise was lawfully conditioned upon the friend's promise not to sue prior to December 31.","C":"Yes, because the woman's failure to pay the debt, plus interest, on December 31 makes the friend's breach of promise not to sue before that date no longer material.","D":"Yes, because the woman's May 1 promise is enforceable by reason of her moral obligation to pay the debt."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Common examples of constructive (ie, implied) conditions\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's performance performance\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's cooperation cooperation with that performance\n\nOrder of Simultaneous performance possible – each party's performance is performance condition concurrent to other party's duty to perform (ie, performance due simultaneously) One party's performance takes time – party's completion of longer performance is condition precedent to other party's duty to perform A settlement agreement is a written and signed contract in which one party forgoes his/her right to sue on a claim in exchange for consideration. Unless stated otherwise in the agreement, each party's duty to perform is impliedly conditioned upon the other party's performance. This means that a breach by one party excuses the nonbreaching party from performing, and the breaching party cannot enforce the agreement. Here, on May 1, the woman and the friend signed a settlement agreement in which the woman agreed to repay her overdue debt by December 31, and the friend promised not to sue in the meantime. But the friend breached this agreement by filing suit on June 1. And since the woman's May 1 promise was lawfully conditioned upon the friend's promise not to sue before December 31, the woman was not required to repay the debt on December 31 (Choice C). Therefore, the friend cannot enforce the May 1 agreement. (Choice A) The underlying policy behind statutes of limitation is one of procedural fairness to the defendant—not one against the enforcement of stale claims. (Choice D) A promise made out of a sense of moral obligation to compensate someone for a benefit previously received is generally unenforceable due to lack of consideration (ie, moral or past consideration). But here, the woman's promise to repay the debt by December 31 was made in exchange for the friend's promise not to sue—not because of the woman's moral obligation to pay the debt.\n\nEducational objective: Unless stated otherwise, each party's duty to perform under a settlement agreement is conditioned upon the other party's performance. This means that a breach of the agreement excuses the nonbreaching party from performing, and the breaching party cannot enforce the agreement.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","educationalObjective":"Unless stated otherwise, each party's duty to perform under a settlement agreement is conditioned upon the other party's performance. This means that a breach of the agreement excuses the nonbreaching party from performing, and the breaching party cannot enforce the agreement.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 74 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (settlement of claims).","rule":"Unless stated otherwise, each party's duty to perform under a settlement agreement is conditioned upon the other party's performance. This means that a breach of the agreement excuses the nonbreaching party from performing, and the breaching party cannot enforce the agreement.","irac":{"issue":"Does the new count state a claim upon which relief can be granted?","rule":"Unless stated otherwise, each party's duty to perform under a settlement agreement is conditioned upon the other party's performance. This means that a breach of the agreement excuses the nonbreaching party from performing, and the breaching party cannot enforce the agreement.","analysis":"Common examples of constructive (ie, implied) conditions\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's performance performance\n\nCondition of Each party's performance is conditioned on other party's cooperation cooperation with that performance\n\nOrder of Simultaneous performance possible – each party's performance is performance condition concurrent to other party's duty to perform (ie, performance due simultaneously) One party's performance takes time – party's completion of longer performance is condition precedent to other party's duty to perform A settlement agreement is a written and signed contract in which one party forgoes his/her right to sue on a claim in exchange for consideration. Unless stated otherwise in the agreement, each party's duty to perform is impliedly conditioned upon the other party's performance. This means that a breach by one party excuses the nonbreaching party from performing, and the breaching party cannot enforce the agreement. Here, on May 1, the woman and the friend signed a settlement agreement in which the woman agreed to repay her overdue debt by December 31, and the friend promised not to sue in the meantime. But the friend breached this agreement by filing suit on June 1. And since the woman's May 1 promise was lawfully conditioned upon the friend's promise not to sue before December 31, the woman was not required to repay the debt on December 31 (Choice C). Therefore, the friend cannot enforce the May 1 agreement. (Choice A) The underlying policy behind statutes of limitation is one of procedural fairness to the defendant—not one against the enforcement of stale claims. (Choice D) A promise made out of a sense of moral obligation to compensate someone for a benefit previously received is generally unenforceable due to lack of consideration (ie, moral or past consideration). But here, the woman's promise to repay the debt by December 31 was made in exchange for the friend's promise not to sue—not because of the woman's moral obligation to pay the debt.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the woman's May 1 promise was lawfully conditioned upon the friend's promise not to sue prior to December 31."},"questionZh":"一位女士欠她朋友 5,000 美元，外加 6% 的利息，直至还清。 7月31日，追债将受到诉讼时效的限制。此前的5月1日，该女子和该朋友双方签署了一份书面文件，其中该女子承诺在次年12月31日全额偿还债务，并在此之前加收6%的利息，而该朋友承诺在此期间不会起诉追债。这位朋友开始担心5月1日协议的法律效力，并于6月1日提起诉讼追讨债务。次年1月2日，朋友的诉讼尚未开庭，女子也未还清债务。这位朋友修改了投诉，添加了第二项罪名，以执行该女子在 5 月 1 日协议中做出的承诺。新的计数是否说明了可以给予救济的索赔？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为这种救济会破坏针对执行过时索赔的诉讼时效政策。","B":"不会，因为该女士 5 月 1 日的承诺在法律上是以朋友承诺在 12 月 31 日之前不起诉为条件的。","C":"是的，因为这位女士未能在 12 月 31 日偿还债务和利息，使得这位朋友违反在该日期之前不起诉的承诺不再重要。","D":"是的，因为这位女士在 5 月 1 日的承诺是可以强制执行的，因为她有偿还债务的道德义务。"},"explanationZh":"推定（即隐含）条件的常见示例\n\n各方的履行情况以另一方的履行情况为条件\n\n各方履行的条件以另一方对该履行的配合为条件\n\n可能同时履行的顺序 – 各方的履行是与另一方履行义务同时履行的履行条件（即同时到期履行） 一方的履行需要时间 – 一方完成较长时间的履行是另一方履行义务的先决条件 和解协议是一份书面并签署的合同，其中一方放弃其对索赔提起诉讼的权利以换取对价。除非协议中另有规定，否则各方的履行义务均以另一方的履行为默示条件。这意味着一方违约可以免除守约方履行协议的责任，而违约方则无法执行协议。 5月1日，该女子与朋友签署和解协议，女子同意在12月31日前偿还逾期债务，朋友承诺在此期间不起诉。但该朋友于6月1日提起诉讼，违反了该协议。而且由于该女子5月1日的承诺以朋友承诺在12月31日之前不起诉为合法条件，因此该女子无需在12月31日偿还债务（选项C）。因此，这位朋友无法执行5月1日的协议。 （选项 A）诉讼时效背后的基本政策是对被告的程序公平性，而不是反对执行过时的索赔。 （选项D）出于道德义务感而做出的承诺，要补偿某人先前收到的利益，通常由于缺乏约因（即道德或过去的考虑）而无法执行。但在这里，这位女士承诺在 12 月 31 日之前偿还债务，是为了换取朋友承诺不起诉，而不是因为该女士有偿还债务的道义义务。\n\n教育目标：除非另有说明，和解协议中各方履行的义务取决于另一方的履行情况。这意味着，违约方可以免除守约方履行协议的责任，而违约方则无法执行协议。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"除非另有说明，和解协议中各方履行的义务以另一方的履行为条件。这意味着，违约方可以免除守约方履行协议的责任，而违约方则无法执行协议。","iracZh":{"issue":"新的计数是否说明了可以给予救济的索赔？","rule":"除非另有说明，和解协议中各方履行的义务以另一方的履行为条件。这意味着，违约方可以免除守约方履行协议的责任，而违约方则无法执行协议。","analysis":"推定（即隐含）条件的常见示例\n\n各方的履行情况以另一方的履行情况为条件\n\n各方履行的条件以另一方对该履行的配合为条件\n\n可能同时履行的顺序 – 各方的履行是与另一方履行义务同时履行的履行条件（即同时到期履行） 一方的履行需要时间 – 一方完成较长时间的履行是另一方履行义务的先决条件 和解协议是一份书面并签署的合同，其中一方放弃其对索赔提起诉讼的权利以换取对价。除非协议中另有规定，否则各方的履行义务均以另一方的履行为默示条件。这意味着一方违约可以免除守约方履行协议的责任，而违约方则无法执行协议。 5月1日，该女子与朋友签署和解协议，女子同意在12月31日前偿还逾期债务，朋友承诺在此期间不起诉。但该朋友于6月1日提起诉讼，违反了该协议。而且由于该女子5月1日的承诺以朋友承诺在12月31日之前不起诉为合法条件，因此该女子无需在12月31日偿还债务（选项C）。因此，这位朋友无法执行5月1日的协议。 （选项 A）诉讼时效背后的基本政策是对被告的程序公平性，而不是反对执行过时的索赔。 （选项D）出于道德义务感而做出的承诺，要补偿某人先前收到的利益，通常由于缺乏约因（即道德或过去的考虑）而无法执行。但在这里，这位女士承诺在 12 月 31 日之前偿还债务，是为了换取朋友承诺不起诉，而不是因为该女士有偿还债务的道义义务。\n\n教育目标：除非另有说明，和解协议中各方履行的义务取决于另一方的履行情况。这意味着，违约方可以免除守约方履行协议的责任，而违约方则无法执行协议。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 74（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（索赔的解决）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为该女士 5 月 1 日的承诺在法律上是以朋友承诺在 12 月 31 日之前不起诉为条件的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"past consideration","ipa":"/pˈæst kʌnsˌɪdɝˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"past con·sid·er·a·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"既往约因：允诺作出前已完成的行为，通常不能支持新允诺","contextualMeaning":"既往约因：允诺作出前已完成的行为，通常不能支持新允诺","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"(Choice D) A promise made out of a sense of moral obligation to compensate someone for a benefit previously received is generally unenforceable due to lack of consideration (ie, moral or past consideration).","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":210,"sourceQuestionPage":620,"sourceQuestionPages":[620],"sourceExplanationPages":[621,622],"sourceAnswerPages":[621,622],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-211","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"In a writing signed by both parties on December 1, a buyer agreed to buy from a seller a gasoline engine for $1,000, delivery to be made on the following February 1. Through a secretarial error, the writing called for delivery on March 1, but neither party noticed the error until February 1. Before signing the agreement, the parties orally agreed that the contract of sale would be effective only if the buyer should notify the seller in writing not later than January 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the engine to a third person. Otherwise, they agreed orally, \"There is no deal.\" On December 15, the buyer entered into a contract with a third party to resell the engine to the third party at a profit. The buyer did not give the seller notice of the resale agreement until January 25, and the seller received it by mail on January 26. In the meantime, the value of the engine had unexpectedly increased about 75% since December 1, and the seller renounced the agreement. If the buyer sues the seller on February 2 for breach of contract, which of the following is the seller's best defense?","choices":{"A":"In view of the unexpected 75% increase in value of the engine after December 1, the seller's performance is excused by the doctrine of commercial frustration.","B":"The agreement, if any, is unenforceable because a material term was not included in the writing.","C":"The buyer not giving written notice by January 2 of his resale agreement was a failure of a condition precedent to the existence of a contract.","D":"The secretarial error in the written delivery-term was a mutual mistake concerning a basic fact, and the agreement is voidable by either party."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A party's performance under a contract can be made contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent. In that case, the party's performance will not become due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. Here, the parties orally agreed that the contract would be effective only if the buyer gave the seller written notice by January 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the gasoline engine (condition precedent). Because the buyer failed to notify the seller of his resale agreement until January 25 (nonoccurrence) and there is no indication that the condition's nonoccurrence was excused (eg, by waiver), the contract did not become effective. Therefore, the seller's best defense is that there was no breach due to failure of a condition precedent. (Choice A) The doctrine of frustration of purpose applies only when the contract's purpose becomes worthless due to an unexpected event. But this doctrine is not available because a 75% increase in the gasoline engine's value is not an unexpected event that would render the sales contract worthless. (Choice B) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, gasoline engine) are governed by the UCC. The UCC specifically rejects the idea that a contract is unenforceable because it does not contain all material terms. Rather, the UCC limits enforcement of the contract to the quantity of goods in the writing (here, one gasoline engine). (Choice D) When both parties are mistaken as to a contract's contents or legal effect (eg, due to a clerical error), reformation is available and neither party can avoid the contract.\n\nEducational objective: When performance under a contract is contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent, performance is not due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).\n\nUCC § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (formal requirements for the sale of goods).","educationalObjective":"When performance under a contract is contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent, performance is not due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).\n\nUCC § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (formal requirements for the sale of goods).","rule":"When performance under a contract is contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent, performance is not due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues the seller on February 2 for breach of contract, which of the following is the seller's best defense?","rule":"When performance under a contract is contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent, performance is not due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"A party's performance under a contract can be made contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent. In that case, the party's performance will not become due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. Here, the parties orally agreed that the contract would be effective only if the buyer gave the seller written notice by January 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the gasoline engine (condition precedent). Because the buyer failed to notify the seller of his resale agreement until January 25 (nonoccurrence) and there is no indication that the condition's nonoccurrence was excused (eg, by waiver), the contract did not become effective. Therefore, the seller's best defense is that there was no breach due to failure of a condition precedent. (Choice A) The doctrine of frustration of purpose applies only when the contract's purpose becomes worthless due to an unexpected event. But this doctrine is not available because a 75% increase in the gasoline engine's value is not an unexpected event that would render the sales contract worthless. (Choice B) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, gasoline engine) are governed by the UCC. The UCC specifically rejects the idea that a contract is unenforceable because it does not contain all material terms. Rather, the UCC limits enforcement of the contract to the quantity of goods in the writing (here, one gasoline engine). (Choice D) When both parties are mistaken as to a contract's contents or legal effect (eg, due to a clerical error), reformation is available and neither party can avoid the contract.","conclusion":"Answer C: The buyer not giving written notice by January 2 of his resale agreement was a failure of a condition precedent to the existence of a contract."},"questionZh":"在双方于 12 月 1 日签署的一份书面文件中，买方同意以 1,000 美元的价格向卖方购买一台汽油发动机，并于次年 2 月 1 日交货。由于秘书错误，该书面文件要求在 3 月 1 日交货，但双方直到 2 月 1 日才注意到该错误。在签署协议之前，双方口头同意，只有买方在 1 月 2 日之前以书面形式通知卖方买方已安排将发动机转售给第三方，销售合同才有效。人。否则，他们口头同意“没有达成协议”。 12月15日，买方与第三方签订合同，将发动机转售给第三方以获取利润。买方直到1月25日才向卖方发出转售协议通知，卖方于1月26日通过邮件收到。与此同时，发动机的价值自12月1日以来意外增加了约75%，卖方放弃了该协议。如果买方于2月2日起诉卖方违约，以下哪项是卖方的最佳抗辩？","choicesZh":{"A":"鉴于12月1日后发动机价值意外增加75%，卖方的履约行为以商业受挫主义为借口。","B":"该协议（如果有）不可执行，因为书面文件中未包含重要条款。","C":"买方在 1 月 2 日之前未就其转售协议发出书面通知，构成合同存在的先决条件的失败。","D":"书面交付条款中的秘书错误是关于基本事实的共同错误，任何一方均可撤销该协议。"},"explanationZh":"合同一方的履行可以视先决条件的发生而定。在这种情况下，除非该情况发生或该情况不发生得到原谅，否则该方的履约不会到期。在此，双方口头同意，只有买方在 1 月 2 日之前书面通知卖方买方已安排转售汽油发动机（先决条件），合同才生效。由于买方直到 1 月 25 日才将其转售协议通知卖方（未发生），并且没有迹象表明该条件的未发生已得到原谅（例如，通过弃权），因此合同并未生效。因此，卖方最好的抗辩是，不存在因先决条件不成立而导致的违约行为。 （选项A）目的落空原则仅适用于合同目的因意外事件而变得毫无价值的情况。但这一原则并不适用，因为汽油发动机价值增加 75% 并不是一个意外事件，不会导致销售合同变得毫无价值。 （选择B） 货物销售合同（例如汽油发动机）受UCC 管辖。 UCC 特别拒绝认为合同因未包含所有重要条款而无法执行的观点。相反，UCC 将合同的执行限制为书面货物数量（此处为一台汽油发动机）。 （选项D）当双方对合同的内容或法律效力有误解（例如，由于笔误）时，可以进行改正，并且任何一方都不能撤销合同。\n\n教育目标：当合同下的履行取决于先决条件的发生时，只有在该条件发生或该条件不发生得到原谅后，才应履行合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。\n\nUCC § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（商品销售的正式要求）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当合同的履行取决于先决条件的发生时，只有在该条件发生或该条件不发生得到原谅后，才应履行合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买方于2月2日起诉卖方违约，以下哪项是卖方的最佳抗辩？","rule":"当合同的履行取决于先决条件的发生时，只有在该条件发生或该条件不发生得到原谅后，才应履行合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"合同一方的履行可以视先决条件的发生而定。在这种情况下，除非该情况发生或该情况不发生得到原谅，否则该方的履约不会到期。在此，双方口头同意，只有买方在 1 月 2 日之前书面通知卖方买方已安排转售汽油发动机（先决条件），合同才生效。由于买方直到 1 月 25 日才将其转售协议通知卖方（未发生），并且没有迹象表明该条件的未发生已得到原谅（例如，通过弃权），因此合同并未生效。因此，卖方最好的抗辩是，不存在因先决条件不成立而导致的违约行为。 （选项A）目的落空原则仅适用于合同目的因意外事件而变得毫无价值的情况。但这一原则并不适用，因为汽油发动机价值增加 75% 并不是一个意外事件，不会导致销售合同变得毫无价值。 （选择B） 货物销售合同（例如汽油发动机）受UCC 管辖。 UCC 特别拒绝认为合同因未包含所有重要条款而无法执行的观点。相反，UCC 将合同的执行限制为书面货物数量（此处为一台汽油发动机）。 （选项D）当双方对合同的内容或法律效力有误解（例如，由于笔误）时，可以进行改正，并且任何一方都不能撤销合同。\n\n教育目标：当合同下的履行取决于先决条件的发生时，只有在该条件发生或该条件不发生得到原谅后，才应履行合同。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。\n\nUCC § 2-201 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（商品销售的正式要求）。","conclusion":"答案 C：买方在 1 月 2 日之前未就其转售协议发出书面通知，构成合同存在的先决条件的失败。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-0a7a3927ad7f8605"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":211,"sourceQuestionPage":623,"sourceQuestionPages":[623],"sourceExplanationPages":[624,625],"sourceAnswerPages":[624,625],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-212","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A seller and a buyer entered into a written agreement providing that the seller was to deliver 1,000 cases of candy bars to the buyer during the months of May and June. Under the agreement, the buyer was obligated to make a selection by March 1 of the quantities of the various candy bars to be delivered under the contract. The buyer did not make the selection by March 1, and on March 2 the seller notified the buyer that because of the buyer's failure to select, the seller would not deliver the candy bars. The seller had all the necessary candy bars on hand on March 1 and made no additional sales or purchases on March 1 or March 2. On March 2, after receiving the seller's notice that it would not perform, the buyer notified the seller of its selection and insisted that the seller perform. The seller refused. If the buyer sues the seller for breach of contract, is the buyer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a contract did not exist until the buyer selected the specific candy bars, and the seller withdrew its offer before selection.","B":"No, because the buyer's selection of the candy bars by March 1 was an express condition to the seller's duty to perform.","C":"Yes, because the delay of one day in making the selection did not have a material effect on the seller.","D":"Yes, because upon the buyer's failure to make a selection by March 1, the seller had a duty to make a reasonable selection."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, candy bars) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). When a sale-of-goods contract fails to specify the assortment of goods to be delivered (eg, the quantities of various candy bars), the UCC imposes a duty on the buyer to select the assortment of goods.* If the buyer fails to timely do so AND the delay materially impacts the seller's performance, then the seller can: proceed in any reasonable manner (eg, choose a reasonable assortment of goods) or treat the buyer's non-selection as a breach (Choice D). Here, the parties agreed that the buyer would select an assortment of candy bars by March 1. Although the buyer made the selection on March 2, the delay did not materially impact the seller's performance. That is because the seller had all the necessary candy bars on hand and had entered into no new contracts at that time. As a result, the seller unjustifiably refused to accept the buyer's selection, and the buyer will likely prevail. *In contrast, arrangements relating to shipping are the seller's duty to specify. (Choice A) The UCC rejects the idea that a contract must specify the assortment of goods to be valid. Here, the contract stated the buyer could select the assortment later. (Choice B) Absent language requiring strict compliance, the buyer's selection of the candy bars was a constructive or implied condition to the seller's duty to perform, which could be satisfied by substantially complying (eg, selecting a day late).\n\nEducational objective: If the buyer fails to timely select the assortment of goods for delivery and the delay materially impacts the seller's performance, then the seller can (1) proceed in any reasonable manner or (2) treat the failure as a breach.\n\nReferences\nUCC § 2-311 (Am. Law Inst. &amp; Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (options and cooperation respecting performance).","educationalObjective":"If the buyer fails to timely select the assortment of goods for delivery and the delay materially impacts the seller's performance, then the seller can (1) proceed in any reasonable manner or (2) treat the failure as a breach.","references":"UCC § 2-311 (Am. Law Inst. &amp; Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (options and cooperation respecting performance).","rule":"If the buyer fails to timely select the assortment of goods for delivery and the delay materially impacts the seller's performance, then the seller can (1) proceed in any reasonable manner or (2) treat the failure as a breach.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues the seller for breach of contract, is the buyer likely to prevail?","rule":"If the buyer fails to timely select the assortment of goods for delivery and the delay materially impacts the seller's performance, then the seller can (1) proceed in any reasonable manner or (2) treat the failure as a breach.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, candy bars) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). When a sale-of-goods contract fails to specify the assortment of goods to be delivered (eg, the quantities of various candy bars), the UCC imposes a duty on the buyer to select the assortment of goods.* If the buyer fails to timely do so AND the delay materially impacts the seller's performance, then the seller can: proceed in any reasonable manner (eg, choose a reasonable assortment of goods) or treat the buyer's non-selection as a breach (Choice D). Here, the parties agreed that the buyer would select an assortment of candy bars by March 1. Although the buyer made the selection on March 2, the delay did not materially impact the seller's performance. That is because the seller had all the necessary candy bars on hand and had entered into no new contracts at that time. As a result, the seller unjustifiably refused to accept the buyer's selection, and the buyer will likely prevail. *In contrast, arrangements relating to shipping are the seller's duty to specify. (Choice A) The UCC rejects the idea that a contract must specify the assortment of goods to be valid. Here, the contract stated the buyer could select the assortment later. (Choice B) Absent language requiring strict compliance, the buyer's selection of the candy bars was a constructive or implied condition to the seller's duty to perform, which could be satisfied by substantially complying (eg, selecting a day late).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the delay of one day in making the selection did not have a material effect on the seller."},"questionZh":"卖方和买方签订了一份书面协议，规定卖方将在 5 月和 6 月期间向买方交付 1,000 箱棒棒糖。根据协议，买方有义务在 3 月 1 日之前选择合同项下交付的各种棒棒糖的数量。 3月1日，买家仍未选货，3月2日，卖家通知买家，因买家未选货，卖家不再发货。 3月1日，卖方手头备有所有必需的棒棒糖，3月1日或3月2日没有进行额外销售或购买。3月2日，买方收到卖方不履行合同的通知后，通知卖方其选择，并坚持要求卖方履行。卖家拒绝了。如果买方起诉卖方违约，买方胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为在买方选择特定的棒棒糖之前，合同并不存在，而卖方在选择之前撤回了报价。","B":"不可以，因为买方在 3 月 1 日之前选择棒棒糖是卖方履行义务的明确条件。","C":"是的，因为延迟一天的选择并没有对卖家产生实质性影响。","D":"是的，因为买方未能在3月1日之前做出选择，卖方就有义务做出合理的选择。"},"explanationZh":"商品（例如棒棒糖）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。当货物销售合同未能指定要交付的货物种类（例如，各种棒棒糖的数量）时，UCC规定买方有责任选择货物种类。*如果买方未能及时这样做并且延迟对卖方的履约产生重大影响，那么卖方可以：以任何合理的方式继续（例如，选择合理的货物种类）或将买方的不选择视为违约（选择D）。在此，双方同意买方将在 3 月 1 日之前选择各种糖果。尽管买方在 3 月 2 日做出了选择，但延迟并未对卖方的履约产生重大影响。这是因为卖方手头有所有必要的棒棒糖，并且当时没有签订新合同。结果，卖方无理地拒绝接受买方的选择，而买方很可能获胜。 *相反，与运输相关的安排是卖方有责任指定的。 （选项 A） UCC 拒绝合同必须指定货物种类才有效的观点。在这里，合同规定买方可以稍后选择品种。 （选择B） 由于缺乏要求严格遵守的语言，买方对棒棒糖的选择是卖方履行义务的推定或暗示条件，可以通过实质遵守（例如，选择晚一天）来满足。\n\n教育目标：如果买方未能及时选择要交付的货物种类，并且这种延误对卖方的履约造成重大影响，则卖方可以 (1) 以任何合理的方式继续进行或 (2) 将这种失败视为违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-311 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（尊重绩效的选择和合作）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果买方未能及时选择要交付的货物种类，并且这种延误对卖方的履约造成重大影响，则卖方可以（1）以任何合理的方式继续进行或（2）将这种失败视为违约。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买方起诉卖方违约，买方胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果买方未能及时选择要交付的货物种类，并且这种延误对卖方的履约造成重大影响，则卖方可以（1）以任何合理的方式继续进行或（2）将这种失败视为违约。","analysis":"商品（例如棒棒糖）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。当货物销售合同未能指定要交付的货物种类（例如，各种棒棒糖的数量）时，UCC规定买方有责任选择货物种类。*如果买方未能及时这样做并且延迟对卖方的履约产生重大影响，那么卖方可以：以任何合理的方式继续（例如，选择合理的货物种类）或将买方的不选择视为违约（选择D）。在此，双方同意买方将在 3 月 1 日之前选择各种糖果。尽管买方在 3 月 2 日做出了选择，但延迟并未对卖方的履约产生重大影响。这是因为卖方手头有所有必要的棒棒糖，并且当时没有签订新合同。结果，卖方无理地拒绝接受买方的选择，而买方很可能获胜。 *相反，与运输相关的安排是卖方有责任指定的。 （选项 A） UCC 拒绝合同必须指定货物种类才有效的观点。在这里，合同规定买方可以稍后选择品种。 （选择B） 由于缺乏要求严格遵守的语言，买方对棒棒糖的选择是卖方履行义务的推定或暗示条件，可以通过实质遵守（例如，选择晚一天）来满足。\n\n教育目标：如果买方未能及时选择要交付的货物种类，并且这种延误对卖方的履约造成重大影响，则卖方可以 (1) 以任何合理的方式继续进行或 (2) 将这种失败视为违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-311 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（尊重绩效的选择和合作）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为延迟一天的选择并没有对卖家产生实质性影响。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-90ec399bb0609f8b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":212,"sourceQuestionPage":626,"sourceQuestionPages":[626],"sourceExplanationPages":[627,628],"sourceAnswerPages":[627,628],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-213","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A collector bought from a gallery a painting correctly described in the parties' signed contract as a \"one-of-a-kind self-portrait\" by a famous artist who had recently died. The contract price was $500,000 in cash, payable one month after delivery of the painting by a truck carrier to the collector. The painting was damaged in transit. The collector timely rejected it after inspection, immediately notified the gallery of the rejection, and told the gallery that the painting would be available for pickup. The gallery then sold the painting to a third party. It informed the collector that it would pick up the painting within a couple of weeks. Two weeks later, before the gallery picked up the painting, the collector sold and delivered the painting to an art admirer for $550,000 cash, after notifying the admirer about the damage. The collector sent no money to the gallery. If the collector's sale of the painting was NOT an acceptance of the goods, what is the maximum amount that the gallery is entitled to recover from the collector?","choices":{"A":"$50,000 (the excess of the market price over the contract price).","B":"$500,000 (the collector-gallery contract price).","C":"$550,000 (damages for conversion).","D":"Only the allowance of lost profit to the gallery as a volume dealer."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Buyer duties regarding rightfully rejected goods\n\n(UCC § 2-602)\n\nBuyer who rightfully rejects nonconforming goods must:\n\ntake reasonable care of goods in its possession until seller has had sufficient time to retrieve them and not exercise ownership over rejected goods (eg, by selling them) UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nUnder the UCC, a seller must deliver goods that conform perfectly to the contract (\"perfect- tender rule\"). A buyer can reject nonconforming goods within a reasonable time after delivery by promptly notifying the seller of the rejection. After rejection, the buyer must take reasonable care of any goods in its possession until the seller has had sufficient time to retrieve them. Any exercise of ownership over the goods (eg, selling the goods) is wrongful conversion and entitles the seller to recover the fair market value of the goods at the time of conversion. Here, the collector rightfully rejected the damaged painting. But before the gallery could pick up the painting, the collector sold and delivered the painting to the art admirer for $550,000 cash. That sale constituted wrongful conversion, and the price paid by the art admirer is evidence of the painting's fair market value at the time of the conversion. Therefore, the gallery is entitled to recover $550,000 in damages for conversion. (Choices A & B) Neither $50,000 (excess of the market price over the contract price) nor $500,000 (collector-gallery contract price) would properly compensate the gallery for its loss. Both amounts are less than $550,000 (damages for conversion). (Choice D) A lost volume seller is one with more goods to sell than customers to buy them. Lost volume sellers can recover lost profits from a breaching buyer because, but for the buyer's breach, the seller would have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one. But the gallery is not a lost volume seller because the painting was a one-of-a-kind good.\n\nEducational objective: If a buyer wrongfully converts rejected goods (eg, by selling them), the seller is entitled to recover the fair market value of the goods at the time of conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-602 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (rightful rejection).","educationalObjective":"If a buyer wrongfully converts rejected goods (eg, by selling them), the seller is entitled to recover the fair market value of the goods at the time of conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-602 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (rightful rejection).","rule":"If a buyer wrongfully converts rejected goods (eg, by selling them), the seller is entitled to recover the fair market value of the goods at the time of conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"If the collector's sale of the painting was NOT an acceptance of the goods, what is the maximum amount that the gallery is entitled to recover from the collector?","rule":"If a buyer wrongfully converts rejected goods (eg, by selling them), the seller is entitled to recover the fair market value of the goods at the time of conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Buyer duties regarding rightfully rejected goods\n\n(UCC § 2-602)\n\nBuyer who rightfully rejects nonconforming goods must:\n\ntake reasonable care of goods in its possession until seller has had sufficient time to retrieve them and not exercise ownership over rejected goods (eg, by selling them) UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nUnder the UCC, a seller must deliver goods that conform perfectly to the contract (\"perfect- tender rule\"). A buyer can reject nonconforming goods within a reasonable time after delivery by promptly notifying the seller of the rejection. After rejection, the buyer must take reasonable care of any goods in its possession until the seller has had sufficient time to retrieve them. Any exercise of ownership over the goods (eg, selling the goods) is wrongful conversion and entitles the seller to recover the fair market value of the goods at the time of conversion. Here, the collector rightfully rejected the damaged painting. But before the gallery could pick up the painting, the collector sold and delivered the painting to the art admirer for $550,000 cash. That sale constituted wrongful conversion, and the price paid by the art admirer is evidence of the painting's fair market value at the time of the conversion. Therefore, the gallery is entitled to recover $550,000 in damages for conversion. (Choices A & B) Neither $50,000 (excess of the market price over the contract price) nor $500,000 (collector-gallery contract price) would properly compensate the gallery for its loss. Both amounts are less than $550,000 (damages for conversion). (Choice D) A lost volume seller is one with more goods to sell than customers to buy them. Lost volume sellers can recover lost profits from a breaching buyer because, but for the buyer's breach, the seller would have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one. But the gallery is not a lost volume seller because the painting was a one-of-a-kind good.","conclusion":"Answer C: $550,000 (damages for conversion)."},"questionZh":"一位收藏家从一家画廊购买了一幅画，双方签署的合同中正确地将其描述为一位最近去世的著名艺术家的“独一无二的自画像”。合同价格为 50 万美元现金，需在卡车承运商将这幅画交付给收藏家后一个月支付。这幅画在运输途中受损。藏家检查后及时拒收，并立即通知画廊，并告诉画廊该画可以提货。画廊随后将这幅画卖给了第三方。它通知收藏家将在几周内领取这幅画。两周后，在画廊收到这幅画之前，收藏家在通知一位艺术崇拜者损坏情况后，以 55 万美元现金的价格将这幅画出售并交付给了一位艺术崇拜者。收藏家没有向画廊寄钱。如果收藏家出售画作并不构成对货物的接受，那么画廊有权向收藏家追回的最高金额是多少？","choicesZh":{"A":"50,000 美元（市场价格超出合同价格的部分）。","B":"500,000 美元（收藏家-画廊合同价）。","C":"550,000 美元（侵占损害）。","D":"仅允许画廊作为批量经销商损失利润。"},"explanationZh":"买方对正当拒收货物的责任\n\n（UCC § 2-602）\n\n正确拒绝不合格商品的买方必须：\n\n合理保管其占有的货物，直到卖方有足够的时间取回货物，并且不对被拒绝的货物行使所有权（例如，通过出售它们） UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n根据 UCC，卖方必须交付完全符合合同的货物（“完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule）”）。买方可以在交货后的合理时间内拒绝不合格的货物，并及时通知卖方拒绝。拒收后，买方必须合理保管其所拥有的任何货物，直到卖方有足够的时间取回它们。任何对货物所有权的行使（例如出售货物）都是侵占行为，卖方有权收回侵占发生时货物的公平市场价值。在这里，收藏家理所当然地拒绝了这幅受损的画作。但在画廊收到这幅画之前，收藏家以 55 万美元的现金将这幅画出售并交付给了这位艺术崇拜者。该出售构成了错误的转换，而艺术欣赏者支付的价格是该画作在侵占发生时的公平市场价值的证据。因此，画廊有权获得 55 万美元的侵占损害赔偿。 （选项 A 和 B）无论是 50,000 美元（市场价格超过合同价格的部分）还是 500,000 美元（收藏家-画廊合同价格）都不足以补偿画廊的损失。两项金额均低于 550,000 美元（侵占损害）。 （选项 D） 销量损失卖家是指待售商品数量多于顾客购买商品数量的卖家。销量损失的卖家可以从违规买家那里追回损失的利润，因为如果买家没有违规，卖家可能会进行两笔销售（和两笔利润），而不是一笔。但画廊并不是卖失货的，因为这幅画是独一无二的。\n\n教育目标：如果买方错误侵占被拒收货物（例如，通过出售它们），卖方有权收回货物在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-602（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（合法拒绝）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果买方错误侵占被拒收货物（例如，通过出售它们），卖方有权收回货物在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"如果收藏家出售画作并不构成对货物的接受，那么画廊有权向收藏家追回的最高金额是多少？","rule":"如果买方错误侵占被拒收货物（例如，通过出售它们），卖方有权收回货物在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"买方对正当拒收货物的责任\n\n（UCC § 2-602）\n\n正确拒绝不合格商品的买方必须：\n\n合理保管其占有的货物，直到卖方有足够的时间取回货物，并且不对被拒绝的货物行使所有权（例如，通过出售它们） UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n根据 UCC，卖方必须交付完全符合合同的货物（“完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule）”）。买方可以在交货后的合理时间内拒绝不合格的货物，并及时通知卖方拒绝。拒收后，买方必须合理保管其所拥有的任何货物，直到卖方有足够的时间取回它们。任何对货物所有权的行使（例如出售货物）都是侵占行为，卖方有权收回侵占发生时货物的公平市场价值。在这里，收藏家理所当然地拒绝了这幅受损的画作。但在画廊收到这幅画之前，收藏家以 55 万美元的现金将这幅画出售并交付给了这位艺术崇拜者。该出售构成了错误的转换，而艺术欣赏者支付的价格是该画作在侵占发生时的公平市场价值的证据。因此，画廊有权获得 55 万美元的侵占损害赔偿。 （选项 A 和 B）无论是 50,000 美元（市场价格超过合同价格的部分）还是 500,000 美元（收藏家-画廊合同价格）都不足以补偿画廊的损失。两项金额均低于 550,000 美元（侵占损害）。 （选项 D） 销量损失卖家是指待售商品数量多于顾客购买商品数量的卖家。销量损失的卖家可以从违规买家那里追回损失的利润，因为如果买家没有违规，卖家可能会进行两笔销售（和两笔利润），而不是一笔。但画廊并不是卖失货的，因为这幅画是独一无二的。\n\n教育目标：如果买方错误侵占被拒收货物（例如，通过出售它们），卖方有权收回货物在侵占发生时的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-602（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（合法拒绝）。","conclusion":"答案 C：550,000 美元（侵占损害）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":213,"sourceQuestionPage":629,"sourceQuestionPages":[629],"sourceExplanationPages":[630,631],"sourceAnswerPages":[630,631],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-214","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A client consulted with his lawyer about handling the sale of the client's building, and asked the lawyer what her legal fee would be. The lawyer replied that her usual charge was $100 per hour, and estimated that the legal work on behalf of the client would cost about $5,000 at that rate. The client said, \"Okay; let's proceed with it,\" and the lawyer timely and successfully completed the work. Because of unexpected title problems, the lawyer reasonably spent 75 hours on the matter and shortly thereafter mailed the client a bill for $7,500, with a letter itemizing the work performed and time spent. The client responded by a letter expressing his good-faith belief that the lawyer had agreed to a total fee of no more than $5,000. The client enclosed a check in the amount of $5,000 payable to the lawyer and conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full for legal services in connection with the sale of my building.\" Despite reading the \"Payment in full...\" language, the lawyer, without any notation of protest or reservation of rights, endorsed and deposited the check to her bank account. The check was duly paid by the client's bank. A few days later, the lawyer unsuccessfully demanded payment from the client of the $2,500 difference between the amount of her bill and the check, and now sues the client for that difference. What, if anything, can the lawyer recover from the client?","choices":{"A":"$2,500, because the client agreed to an hourly rate for as many hours as the work reasonably required, and the sum of $5,000 was merely an estimate.","B":"Nothing, because the amount of the lawyer's fee was disputed in good faith by the client, and the lawyer impliedly agreed to an accord and satisfaction.","C":"Nothing, because the risk of unexpected title problems in a real-property transaction is properly allocable to the seller's attorney and thus to the lawyer in this case.","D":"The reasonable value of the lawyer's services in excess of $5,000, if any, because there was no specific agreement on the total amount of the lawyer's fee."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can offer to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous notation stating that it constitutes payment in full. The creditor can then: cash the check – in which case the creditor impliedly agrees to an accord and satisfaction, and the debt is discharged or reject the check – in which case the debt is not discharged. Here, the lawyer agreed to handle the sale of the client's building for $100 per hour at an estimated cost of $5,000. Due to unexpected title problems, the lawyer reasonably spent 75 hours on the matter and billed the client $7,500. However, the client replied with a letter disputing the fee in good faith and enclosing a $5,000 check conspicuously marked \"Payment in full.\" Since the lawyer cashed the check, she impliedly agreed to an accord and satisfaction. Therefore, the client's debt was discharged, and the lawyer can recover nothing from the client. (Choice A) A court could find that the client agreed to an hourly rate for as many hours as the work reasonably required and that the $5,000 sum was merely an estimate. But since the client disputed the $7,500 fee in good faith, he was entitled to offer a lesser amount to discharge the debt. (Choice C) The parties never indicated who would bear the risk of unexpected title problems, and there is no rule automatically placing that risk on the seller's attorney (here, the lawyer). And regardless of where the risk is placed, the lawyer cannot recover since she cashed the check. (Choice D) Had the lawyer rejected the check, she might be able to recover the reasonable value of her services in excess of $5,000 under a quasi-contract (ie, unjust enrichment) theory.\n\nEducational objective: When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can offer to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. The debt is then discharged if the creditor chooses to cash the check.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a creditor who cashes a check marked \"payment in full\" may have formed an enforceable accord).","educationalObjective":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can offer to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. The debt is then discharged if the creditor chooses to cash the check.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a creditor who cashes a check marked \"payment in full\" may have formed an enforceable accord).","rule":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can offer to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. The debt is then discharged if the creditor chooses to cash the check.","irac":{"issue":"What, if anything, can the lawyer recover from the client?","rule":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can offer to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. The debt is then discharged if the creditor chooses to cash the check.","analysis":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can offer to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous notation stating that it constitutes payment in full. The creditor can then: cash the check – in which case the creditor impliedly agrees to an accord and satisfaction, and the debt is discharged or reject the check – in which case the debt is not discharged. Here, the lawyer agreed to handle the sale of the client's building for $100 per hour at an estimated cost of $5,000. Due to unexpected title problems, the lawyer reasonably spent 75 hours on the matter and billed the client $7,500. However, the client replied with a letter disputing the fee in good faith and enclosing a $5,000 check conspicuously marked \"Payment in full.\" Since the lawyer cashed the check, she impliedly agreed to an accord and satisfaction. Therefore, the client's debt was discharged, and the lawyer can recover nothing from the client. (Choice A) A court could find that the client agreed to an hourly rate for as many hours as the work reasonably required and that the $5,000 sum was merely an estimate. But since the client disputed the $7,500 fee in good faith, he was entitled to offer a lesser amount to discharge the debt. (Choice C) The parties never indicated who would bear the risk of unexpected title problems, and there is no rule automatically placing that risk on the seller's attorney (here, the lawyer). And regardless of where the risk is placed, the lawyer cannot recover since she cashed the check. (Choice D) Had the lawyer rejected the check, she might be able to recover the reasonable value of her services in excess of $5,000 under a quasi-contract (ie, unjust enrichment) theory.","conclusion":"Answer B: Nothing, because the amount of the lawyer's fee was disputed in good faith by the client, and the lawyer impliedly agreed to an accord and satisfaction."},"questionZh":"一位客户向他的律师咨询了处理客户大楼出售事宜，并询问律师她的律师费是多少。律师回答说，她通常的收费是每小时 100 美元，并估计按照这个费率代表客户的法律工作将花费约 5,000 美元。委托人说：“好吧，我们继续吧”，律师及时、顺利地完成了工作。由于意外的产权问题，律师合理地花了 75 个小时处理此事，不久之后向客户邮寄了一份 7,500 美元的账单，并附有一封详细列出所做工作和所花费时间的信函。客户回复一封信，表示他真诚地相信律师已同意总费用不超过 5,000 美元。客户附上一张支票，金额为 5,000 美元，应付给律师，并明显注明：“全额支付与出售我的建筑物有关的法律服务费用。”尽管读到了“全额付款……”的字样，律师在没有任何抗议或保留权利的情况下，签署了支票并将其存入她的银行账户。支票已由客户银行正式支付。几天后，律师要求客户支付帐单金额与支票金额之间 2,500 美元的差额，但没有成功，现在律师就该差额起诉客户。律师可以从委托人那里追回什么（如果有的话）？","choicesZh":{"A":"2,500 美元，因为客户同意按照合理的工作时间按小时收费，而 5,000 美元只是一个估计值。","B":"没什么，因为委托人善意地对律师费的数额提出了争议，而律师默示同意和解。","C":"没什么，因为不动产交易中出现意外所有权问题的风险可以适当地分配给卖方的律师，因此也可以分配给本案中的律师。","D":"律师服务的合理价值超过 5,000 美元（如有），因为对于律师费的总额没有具体约定。"},"explanationZh":"当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张支票来解决债务，并在支票上注明已全额付款。然后债权人可以：兑现支票——在这种情况下，债权人默示同意协议和清偿，债务被解除或拒绝支票——在这种情况下，债务未被解除。在此，律师同意以每小时 100 美元的价格处理客户大楼的出售，预计成本为 5,000 美元。由于意外的产权问题，律师合理地花了 75 个小时处理此事，并向客户收取了 7,500 美元的费用。然而，客户回复了一封信，真诚地对费用提出异议，并附上一张 5,000 美元的支票，上面明显标有“全额付款”。既然律师兑现了支票，她就默示同意和满意。因此，委托人的债务已被解除，律师无法向委托人追回任何损失。 （选择 A）法院可能会发现，客户同意按照工作合理需要的小时数支付小时费率，并且 5,000 美元的金额只是一个估计值。但由于客户善意地对 7,500 美元的费用提出异议，他有权提供较少的金额来偿还债务。 （选择C）双方从未表明谁将承担意外所有权问题的风险，并且没有规则自动将该风险置于卖方律师（此处为律师）身上。而且无论风险在哪里，律师兑现支票后都无法挽回。 （选项D）如果律师拒绝了支票，根据准合同（即不当得利）理论，她也许能够收回超过5,000 美元的合理服务价值。\n\n教育目标：当善意地对债务产生争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有明显“全额付款”符号的支票来解决债务。如果债权人选择兑现支票，则债务将被解除。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 281 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，兑现标有“全额付款”支票的债权人可能已形成可执行协议）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有明显“全额付款”字样的支票来解决债务。如果债权人选择兑现支票，则债务将被解除。","iracZh":{"issue":"律师可以从委托人那里追回什么（如果有的话）？","rule":"当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有明显“全额付款”字样的支票来解决债务。如果债权人选择兑现支票，则债务将被解除。","analysis":"当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张支票来解决债务，并在支票上注明已全额付款。然后债权人可以：兑现支票——在这种情况下，债权人默示同意协议和清偿，债务被解除或拒绝支票——在这种情况下，债务未被解除。在此，律师同意以每小时 100 美元的价格处理客户大楼的出售，预计成本为 5,000 美元。由于意外的产权问题，律师合理地花了 75 个小时处理此事，并向客户收取了 7,500 美元的费用。然而，客户回复了一封信，真诚地对费用提出异议，并附上一张 5,000 美元的支票，上面明显标有“全额付款”。既然律师兑现了支票，她就默示同意和满意。因此，委托人的债务已被解除，律师无法向委托人追回任何损失。 （选择 A）法院可能会发现，客户同意按照工作合理需要的小时数支付小时费率，并且 5,000 美元的金额只是一个估计值。但由于客户善意地对 7,500 美元的费用提出异议，他有权提供较少的金额来偿还债务。 （选择C）双方从未表明谁将承担意外所有权问题的风险，并且没有规则自动将该风险置于卖方律师（此处为律师）身上。而且无论风险在哪里，律师兑现支票后都无法挽回。 （选项D）如果律师拒绝了支票，根据准合同（即不当得利）理论，她也许能够收回超过5,000 美元的合理服务价值。\n\n教育目标：当善意地对债务产生争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有明显“全额付款”符号的支票来解决债务。如果债权人选择兑现支票，则债务将被解除。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 281 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，兑现标有“全额付款”支票的债权人可能已形成可执行协议）。","conclusion":"答案 B：没什么，因为委托人善意地对律师费的数额提出了争议，而律师默示同意和解。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-37beec99f1abd332"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":214,"sourceQuestionPage":632,"sourceQuestionPages":[632],"sourceExplanationPages":[633,634],"sourceAnswerPages":[633,634],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-215","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Risk of Loss & Delivery","question":"A buyer ordered a new machine from a manufacturer. The machine arrived on time and conformed in all respects to the contract. The buyer, however, rejected the machine because he no longer needed it in his business and returned the machine to the manufacturer. The manufacturer sold many such machines each year, and its factory was not operating at full capacity. In an action by the manufacturer against the buyer for breach of contract, which of the following is NOT a proper measure of the manufacturer's damages?","choices":{"A":"The contract price of the machine.","B":"The difference between the contract price and the market price of the machine.","C":"The difference between the contract price and the price obtained from a proper resale of the machine.","D":"The profit the manufacturer would have made on the sale of the machine to the buyer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Seller's action for contract price of goods\n\n(UCC § 2-709)\n\nWhen buyer fails to pay for goods, seller can recover contract price of goods* if:\n\nbuyer accepted goods goods were lost/damaged within commercially reasonable time after risk of loss passed to buyer or buyer returned or rejected goods & seller was unable after reasonable efforts to resell them UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\n*Seller can also recover incidental damages\n\nUnder the UCC, when a buyer fails to pay for goods, the seller can recover the contract price of the goods under three circumstances: Where the buyer accepted the goods Where the goods were lost or damaged within a commercially reasonable time after the risk of loss had passed to the buyer Where the buyer returned or rejected the goods and the seller was unable after reasonable efforts to resell them The seller is also entitled to any incidental damages arising from the transport, resale, or other action taken incident to the buyer's breach. Here, the buyer rejected the machine (no acceptance) and returned it intact to the manufacturer (no loss/damage). And since the manufacturer sold many of these machines each year and its factory was not operating at full capacity, it should be able to resell the buyer's machine. Therefore, the machine's contract price is not a proper measure of the manufacturer's damages. (Choices B & C) A nonbreaching seller can recover the difference between the contract price and the market price of the goods at the time of tender. Alternatively, the seller can resell the goods for a reasonable price and recover the difference between the contract price and the resale price. As a result, either could be a proper measure of the seller's damages. (Choice D) The manufacturer is a lost-volume seller, as evidenced by the fact that it sold many of these machines each year and was not operating at full capacity. Lost-volume sellers can recover lost profits because, but for the buyer's breach, they could have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one.\n\nEducational objective: A seller can only recover the contract price of the goods after the buyer failed to pay if (1) the buyer accepted the goods, (2) the goods were lost or damaged within a commercially reasonable time after the risk of loss passed to the buyer, or (3) the buyer returned or rejected the goods and the seller could not resell them. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's damages – non- acceptance/repudiation).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-709 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (action for price).","educationalObjective":"A seller can only recover the contract price of the goods after the buyer failed to pay if (1) the buyer accepted the goods, (2) the goods were lost or damaged within a commercially reasonable time after the risk of loss passed to the buyer, or (3) the buyer returned or rejected the goods and the seller could not resell them. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's damages – non- acceptance/repudiation).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-709 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (action for price).","rule":"A seller can only recover the contract price of the goods after the buyer failed to pay if (1) the buyer accepted the goods, (2) the goods were lost or damaged within a commercially reasonable time after the risk of loss passed to the buyer, or (3) the buyer returned or rejected the goods and the seller could not resell them. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the manufacturer against the buyer for breach of contract, which of the following is NOT a proper measure of the manufacturer's damages?","rule":"A seller can only recover the contract price of the goods after the buyer failed to pay if (1) the buyer accepted the goods, (2) the goods were lost or damaged within a commercially reasonable time after the risk of loss passed to the buyer, or (3) the buyer returned or rejected the goods and the seller could not resell them. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Seller's action for contract price of goods\n\n(UCC § 2-709)\n\nWhen buyer fails to pay for goods, seller can recover contract price of goods* if:\n\nbuyer accepted goods goods were lost/damaged within commercially reasonable time after risk of loss passed to buyer or buyer returned or rejected goods & seller was unable after reasonable efforts to resell them UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\n*Seller can also recover incidental damages\n\nUnder the UCC, when a buyer fails to pay for goods, the seller can recover the contract price of the goods under three circumstances: Where the buyer accepted the goods Where the goods were lost or damaged within a commercially reasonable time after the risk of loss had passed to the buyer Where the buyer returned or rejected the goods and the seller was unable after reasonable efforts to resell them The seller is also entitled to any incidental damages arising from the transport, resale, or other action taken incident to the buyer's breach. Here, the buyer rejected the machine (no acceptance) and returned it intact to the manufacturer (no loss/damage). And since the manufacturer sold many of these machines each year and its factory was not operating at full capacity, it should be able to resell the buyer's machine. Therefore, the machine's contract price is not a proper measure of the manufacturer's damages. (Choices B & C) A nonbreaching seller can recover the difference between the contract price and the market price of the goods at the time of tender. Alternatively, the seller can resell the goods for a reasonable price and recover the difference between the contract price and the resale price. As a result, either could be a proper measure of the seller's damages. (Choice D) The manufacturer is a lost-volume seller, as evidenced by the fact that it sold many of these machines each year and was not operating at full capacity. Lost-volume sellers can recover lost profits because, but for the buyer's breach, they could have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one.","conclusion":"Answer A: The contract price of the machine."},"questionZh":"一位买家从制造商那里订购了一台新机器。机器准时到达并在所有方面都符合合同。然而，买家拒绝了这台机器，因为他的生意不再需要它，并将机器退还给制造商。该制造商每年销售许多此类机器，而其工厂并未满负荷运转。在制造商因买方违约而向买方提起诉讼时，以下哪项不是衡量制造商损失的适当措施？","choicesZh":{"A":"机器的合同价格。","B":"机器的合同价与市场价之间的差额。","C":"合同价格与正确转售机器获得的价格之间的差额。","D":"制造商通过将机器出售给买方而获得的利润。"},"explanationZh":"卖方针对货物合同价格采取的行动\n\n（UCC § 2-709）\n\n当买方未能支付货款时，卖方可以收回货物的合同价款*，如果：\n\n买方接受货物 货物在损失风险转移给买方后在商业上合理的时间内丢失/损坏，或者买方退回或拒绝货物并且卖方在合理努力后无法转售它们 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n*卖方还可以追偿附带损坏\n\n根据《统一商法典》（UCC），当买方未能支付货款时，卖方可以在以下三种情况下收回货物的合同价款： 买方接受了货物 损失风险转移给买方后，在商业上合理的时间内货物丢失或损坏 买方退回或拒收货物，且卖方经过合理努力后仍无法转售货物 卖方还有权要求因运输、转售或因买方违约而采取的其他行动而产生的任何附带损害。在这里，买方拒绝了机器（不接受）并将其完好无损地退还给制造商（没有丢失/损坏）。由于制造商每年出售许多此类机器，并且其工厂并未满负荷运转，因此它应该能够转售买方的机器。因此，机器的合同价格并不是衡量制造商损失的适当标准。 （选项B和C）非违约卖方可以收回合同价格与投标时货物市场价格之间的差额。或者，卖方可以以合理价格转售货物，并收回合同价格与转售价格之间的差额。因此，这两种方法都可以作为衡量卖方损失的适当标准。 （选项 D） 该制造商是一个销量损失的销售商，其每年售出许多此类机器且并未满负荷运营的事实就证明了这一点。销量损失的卖家可以收回利润损失，因为如果没有买家违约，他们可能会进行两次销售（和两次利润），而不是一次。\n\n教育目的：只有在以下情况下，卖方才能在买方未付款后收回货物的合同价款：(1) 买方接受了货物，(2) 在损失风险转移给买方后，货物在商业上合理的时间内丢失或损坏，或 (3) 买方退回或拒绝货物且卖方无法转售货物。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方的损害赔偿 – 不接受/否认）。\n\nUCC § 2-709 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（价格诉讼）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有在以下情况下，卖方才能在买方未付款后收回货物的合同价款：（1）买方接受了货物，（2）货物在损失风险转移给买方后的商业合理时间内丢失或损坏，或（3）买方退回或拒绝货物且卖方无法转售货物。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"在制造商因买方违约而向买方提起诉讼时，以下哪项不是衡量制造商损失的适当措施？","rule":"只有在以下情况下，卖方才能在买方未付款后收回货物的合同价款：（1）买方接受了货物，（2）货物在损失风险转移给买方后的商业合理时间内丢失或损坏，或（3）买方退回或拒绝货物且卖方无法转售货物。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"卖方针对货物合同价格采取的行动\n\n（UCC § 2-709）\n\n当买方未能支付货款时，卖方可以收回货物的合同价款*，如果：\n\n买方接受货物 货物在损失风险转移给买方后在商业上合理的时间内丢失/损坏，或者买方退回或拒绝货物并且卖方在合理努力后无法转售它们 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n*卖方还可以追偿附带损坏\n\n根据《统一商法典》（UCC），当买方未能支付货款时，卖方可以在以下三种情况下收回货物的合同价款： 买方接受了货物 损失风险转移给买方后，在商业上合理的时间内货物丢失或损坏 买方退回或拒收货物，且卖方经过合理努力后仍无法转售货物 卖方还有权要求因运输、转售或因买方违约而采取的其他行动而产生的任何附带损害。在这里，买方拒绝了机器（不接受）并将其完好无损地退还给制造商（没有丢失/损坏）。由于制造商每年出售许多此类机器，并且其工厂并未满负荷运转，因此它应该能够转售买方的机器。因此，机器的合同价格并不是衡量制造商损失的适当标准。 （选项B和C）非违约卖方可以收回合同价格与投标时货物市场价格之间的差额。或者，卖方可以以合理价格转售货物，并收回合同价格与转售价格之间的差额。因此，这两种方法都可以作为衡量卖方损失的适当标准。 （选项 D） 该制造商是一个销量损失的销售商，其每年售出许多此类机器且并未满负荷运营的事实就证明了这一点。销量损失的卖家可以收回利润损失，因为如果没有买家违约，他们可能会进行两次销售（和两次利润），而不是一次。\n\n教育目的：只有在以下情况下，卖方才能在买方未付款后收回货物的合同价款：(1) 买方接受了货物，(2) 在损失风险转移给买方后，货物在商业上合理的时间内丢失或损坏，或 (3) 买方退回或拒绝货物且卖方无法转售货物。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方的损害赔偿 – 不接受/否认）。\n\nUCC § 2-709 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（价格诉讼）。","conclusion":"答案 A：机器的合同价格。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":215,"sourceQuestionPage":635,"sourceQuestionPages":[635],"sourceExplanationPages":[636,637],"sourceAnswerPages":[636,637],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-216","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A homeowner contracted in writing with a kitchen contractor to renovate her kitchen for $25,000, \"subject to the homeowner's complete personal satisfaction.\" The contractor replaced the cabinets, flooring, and countertops and then sought payment from the homeowner. The homeowner paid the contractor only $20,000, truthfully saying that she did not like the finish on the cabinets and was therefore not satisfied. If the contractor sues the homeowner for the balance of the contract price, will the contractor be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because a condition to the homeowner's obligation to pay was not satisfied.","B":"No, because the contractor breached his duty of good faith and fair dealing by supplying unsatisfactory materials.","C":"Yes, because the homeowner breached the covenant of good faith and fair dealing by rejecting the cabinets without justification.","D":"Yes, because the homeowner was the first party to breach the contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Meeting condition of satisfaction\n\nObjective Whether reasonable person would be satisfied with performance standard\n\n(default)\n\nSubjective Whether promisor is personally satisfied with performance (provided standard that dissatisfaction is honest & in good faith)\n\n(aesthetics)\n\nMany contracts include a condition precedent that a party's duty to pay will not become due unless the party is satisfied with the other party's performance (ie, condition of satisfaction). Satisfaction is typically measured objectively, meaning that the condition is met if a reasonable person would be satisfied. But if aesthetic taste is involved, satisfaction is measured subjectively. In that case, the condition is met only if the promisor is personally satisfied, provided that any dissatisfaction is honest and in good faith. Here, the homeowner's duty to pay for the kitchen renovation was subject to her \"complete personal satisfaction.\" This condition of satisfaction was not met because the homeowner was honestly dissatisfied with the cabinets' finish. As a result, the homeowner was not obligated to make the promised payment, and the contractor likely will not prevail in a suit to recover the balance of the contract price (Choices C & D). (Choice B) There is no indication that the materials supplied by the contractor were unsatisfactory. And even if they were, using unsatisfactory materials is not a breach of the covenant of good faith and fair dealing.\n\nEducational objective: When a contract conditions a duty to pay on satisfaction with another party's performance, satisfaction is measured objectively. But when the condition involves a party's aesthetic taste, the condition is only met if that party is personally satisfied with the other party's performance, provided that any dissatisfaction is honest and in good faith.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 228 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (party's satisfaction as a condition).","educationalObjective":"When a contract conditions a duty to pay on satisfaction with another party's performance, satisfaction is measured objectively. But when the condition involves a party's aesthetic taste, the condition is only met if that party is personally satisfied with the other party's performance, provided that any dissatisfaction is honest and in good faith.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 228 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (party's satisfaction as a condition).","rule":"When a contract conditions a duty to pay on satisfaction with another party's performance, satisfaction is measured objectively. But when the condition involves a party's aesthetic taste, the condition is only met if that party is personally satisfied with the other party's performance, provided that any dissatisfaction is honest and in good faith.","irac":{"issue":"If the contractor sues the homeowner for the balance of the contract price, will the contractor be likely to prevail?","rule":"When a contract conditions a duty to pay on satisfaction with another party's performance, satisfaction is measured objectively. But when the condition involves a party's aesthetic taste, the condition is only met if that party is personally satisfied with the other party's performance, provided that any dissatisfaction is honest and in good faith.","analysis":"Meeting condition of satisfaction\n\nObjective Whether reasonable person would be satisfied with performance standard\n\n(default)\n\nSubjective Whether promisor is personally satisfied with performance (provided standard that dissatisfaction is honest & in good faith)\n\n(aesthetics)\n\nMany contracts include a condition precedent that a party's duty to pay will not become due unless the party is satisfied with the other party's performance (ie, condition of satisfaction). Satisfaction is typically measured objectively, meaning that the condition is met if a reasonable person would be satisfied. But if aesthetic taste is involved, satisfaction is measured subjectively. In that case, the condition is met only if the promisor is personally satisfied, provided that any dissatisfaction is honest and in good faith. Here, the homeowner's duty to pay for the kitchen renovation was subject to her \"complete personal satisfaction.\" This condition of satisfaction was not met because the homeowner was honestly dissatisfied with the cabinets' finish. As a result, the homeowner was not obligated to make the promised payment, and the contractor likely will not prevail in a suit to recover the balance of the contract price (Choices C & D). (Choice B) There is no indication that the materials supplied by the contractor were unsatisfactory. And even if they were, using unsatisfactory materials is not a breach of the covenant of good faith and fair dealing.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because a condition to the homeowner's obligation to pay was not satisfied."},"questionZh":"一位房主与厨房承包商签订了书面合同，以 25,000 美元的价格翻新她的厨房，“以房主个人完全满意为准”。承包商更换了橱柜、地板和台面，然后向房主索要付款。房主只付给承包商2万元，并如实表示不喜欢橱柜的饰面，因此不满意。如果承包商起诉房主索取合同价款的余额，承包商胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为房主付款义务的条件未得到满足。","B":"不可以，因为承包商提供了不合格的材料，违反了诚信和公平交易的义务。","C":"是的，因为房主无理拒绝橱柜，违反了诚信和公平交易的约定。","D":"是的，因为房主是第一个违约方。"},"explanationZh":"满足满足条件\n\n目标 理性的人是否会对绩效标准感到满意\n\n（默认）\n\n主观 承诺者个人对绩效是否满意（提供的标准是不满意是诚实的、善意的）\n\n（美学）\n\n许多合同都包含一个先决条件，即除非一方对另一方的履行感到满意（即满足条件），否则一方的付款义务不会到期。满意度通常是客观衡量的，这意味着如果一个理性的人感到满意，则满足条件。但如果涉及审美趣味，满意度是主观衡量的。在这种情况下，只有当许诺人个人感到满意时，只要任何不满意都是诚实且善意的，该条件就满足。在这里，房主支付厨房装修费用的义务取决于她“完全的个人满意度”。这个满意的条件没有得到满足，因为房主对橱柜的饰面确实不满意。因此，房主没有义务支付承诺的付款，承包商很可能不会在收回合同价格余额的诉讼中获胜（选择 C ​​和 D）。 （选择B）没有迹象表明承包商提供的材料不令人满意。即使是，使用不令人满意的材料也不违反诚信和公平交易的盟约。\n\n教育目标：当合同规定了对另一方表现的满意度而支付的义务时，满意度是客观衡量的。但当该条件涉及一方的审美趣味时，只有该方个人对另一方的表现感到满意，并且任何不满意都是诚实和善意的，才满足该条件。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 228（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一方满意作为条件）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当合同以对另一方履行的满意度为条件规定付款义务时，满意度是客观衡量的。但当该条件涉及一方的审美趣味时，只有该方个人对另一方的表现感到满意，并且任何不满意都是诚实和善意的，才满足该条件。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果承包商起诉房主索取合同价款的余额，承包商胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"当合同以对另一方履行的满意度为条件规定付款义务时，满意度是客观衡量的。但当该条件涉及一方的审美趣味时，只有该方个人对另一方的表现感到满意，并且任何不满意都是诚实和善意的，才满足该条件。","analysis":"满足满足条件\n\n目标 理性的人是否会对绩效标准感到满意\n\n（默认）\n\n主观 承诺者个人对绩效是否满意（提供的标准是不满意是诚实的、善意的）\n\n（美学）\n\n许多合同都包含一个先决条件，即除非一方对另一方的履行感到满意（即满足条件），否则一方的付款义务不会到期。满意度通常是客观衡量的，这意味着如果一个理性的人感到满意，则满足条件。但如果涉及审美趣味，满意度是主观衡量的。在这种情况下，只有当许诺人个人感到满意时，只要任何不满意都是诚实且善意的，该条件就满足。在这里，房主支付厨房装修费用的义务取决于她“完全的个人满意度”。这个满意的条件没有得到满足，因为房主对橱柜的饰面确实不满意。因此，房主没有义务支付承诺的付款，承包商很可能不会在收回合同价格余额的诉讼中获胜（选择 C ​​和 D）。 （选择B）没有迹象表明承包商提供的材料不令人满意。即使是，使用不令人满意的材料也不违反诚信和公平交易的盟约。\n\n教育目标：当合同规定了对另一方表现的满意度而支付的义务时，满意度是客观衡量的。但当该条件涉及一方的审美趣味时，只有该方个人对另一方的表现感到满意，并且任何不满意都是诚实和善意的，才满足该条件。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 228（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一方满意作为条件）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为房主付款义务的条件未得到满足。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":216,"sourceQuestionPage":638,"sourceQuestionPages":[638],"sourceExplanationPages":[639,640],"sourceAnswerPages":[639,640],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-217","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A produce distributor contracted to provide a grocer with eight crates of lettuce at the distributor's listed price. The distributor's shipping clerk mistakenly shipped only seven crates to the grocer. The grocer accepted delivery of the seven crates but immediately notified the distributor that the delivery did not conform to the contract. The distributor's listed price for seven crates of lettuce was 7/8 of its listed price for eight crates. The distributor shipped no more lettuce to the grocer, and the grocer has not yet paid for any of the lettuce. How much, if anything, is the distributor entitled to collect from the grocer?","choices":{"A":"Nothing, because the tender of all eight crates was a condition precedent to the grocer's duty to pay.","B":"The listed price for the seven crates of lettuce.","C":"The listed price for the seven crates of lettuce, minus the grocer's damages, if any, for the distributor's failure to deliver the full order.","D":"The reasonable value of the seven crates of lettuce, minus the grocer's damages, if any, for the distributor's failure to deliver the full order."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, lettuce). Under the UCC, goods must conform perfectly to the contract. Failure to provide such goods is a breach that, unless cured in the time remaining to perform, allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. When the buyer accepts the goods, the buyer must pay the seller the contract price of the accepted goods minus any damages incurred as a result of the breach—eg, damages incurred to cover (ie, purchase) substitute goods. Here, the distributor contracted to provide the grocer with eight crates of lettuce. The distributor then mistakenly shipped only seven crates of lettuce and did not cure this defect (breach). The grocer accepted the seven crates, so the distributor is entitled to collect the listed contract price (not the reasonable value) for those crates (Choice D). However, that price must be reduced by the grocer's damages, if any, resulting from the distributor's breach (Choice B). (Choice A) The tender of all eight crates was not a condition precedent to the grocer's duty to pay. That is because the grocer had the option to accept or reject the nonconforming goods. But since the grocer accepted seven crates of lettuce, it must pay for those seven crates minus any damages incurred.\n\nEducational objective: If goods do not conform perfectly to the contract, it is a breach under the UCC. The UCC allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. When the buyer accepts the goods, the buyer must pay the seller the contract price of the accepted goods minus any damages incurred as a result of the breach.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's rights on improper delivery).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-717 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer may deduct damages from price due).","educationalObjective":"If goods do not conform perfectly to the contract, it is a breach under the UCC. The UCC allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. When the buyer accepts the goods, the buyer must pay the seller the contract price of the accepted goods minus any damages incurred as a result of the breach.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's rights on improper delivery).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-717 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer may deduct damages from price due).","rule":"If goods do not conform perfectly to the contract, it is a breach under the UCC. The UCC allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. When the buyer accepts the goods, the buyer must pay the seller the contract price of the accepted goods minus any damages incurred as a result of the breach.","irac":{"issue":"How much, if anything, is the distributor entitled to collect from the grocer?","rule":"If goods do not conform perfectly to the contract, it is a breach under the UCC. The UCC allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. When the buyer accepts the goods, the buyer must pay the seller the contract price of the accepted goods minus any damages incurred as a result of the breach.","analysis":"The Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, lettuce). Under the UCC, goods must conform perfectly to the contract. Failure to provide such goods is a breach that, unless cured in the time remaining to perform, allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. When the buyer accepts the goods, the buyer must pay the seller the contract price of the accepted goods minus any damages incurred as a result of the breach—eg, damages incurred to cover (ie, purchase) substitute goods. Here, the distributor contracted to provide the grocer with eight crates of lettuce. The distributor then mistakenly shipped only seven crates of lettuce and did not cure this defect (breach). The grocer accepted the seven crates, so the distributor is entitled to collect the listed contract price (not the reasonable value) for those crates (Choice D). However, that price must be reduced by the grocer's damages, if any, resulting from the distributor's breach (Choice B). (Choice A) The tender of all eight crates was not a condition precedent to the grocer's duty to pay. That is because the grocer had the option to accept or reject the nonconforming goods. But since the grocer accepted seven crates of lettuce, it must pay for those seven crates minus any damages incurred.","conclusion":"Answer C: The listed price for the seven crates of lettuce, minus the grocer's damages, if any, for the distributor's failure to deliver the full order."},"questionZh":"一家农产品经销商签订合同，按照经销商的标价向一家杂货商提供八箱生菜。分销商的货运职员错误地只向杂货店运送了七个板条箱。杂货商接受了七个板条箱的交付，但立即通知经销商，交付的货物与合同不符。分销商七箱生菜的标价是八箱生菜标价的 7/8。分销商不再向杂货商运送生菜，杂货商也尚未支付任何生菜的费用。经销商有权向杂货商收取多少费用（如果有的话）？","choicesZh":{"A":"没什么，因为所有八个板条箱的投标是杂货商付款义务的先决条件。","B":"七箱生菜的标价。","C":"七箱生菜的标价减去杂货商因经销商未能交付全部订单而造成的损失（如果有）。","D":"七箱生菜的合理价值，减去杂货商因分销商未能交付全部订单而造成的损失（如果有）。"},"explanationZh":"《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 管辖商品（例如生菜）销售合同。根据 UCC，货物必须完全符合合同。未能提供此类货物属于违约行为，除非在剩余履行时间内予以纠正，否则买方可以拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。当买方接受货物时，买方必须向卖方支付所接受货物的合同价，减去因违约而产生的任何损失，例如因购买（即购买）替代货物而产生的损失。在这里，经销商签订合同向杂货商提供八箱生菜。随后，分销商错误地只运送了七箱生菜，并且没有解决这一缺陷（违规）。杂货商接受了这七个板条箱，因此分销商有权收取这些板条箱所列出的合同价格（而不是合理价值）（选项 D）。然而，该价格必须减去杂货商因分销商违约而造成的损失（如果有）（选择 B）。 （选择 A） 所有八个板条箱的投标并不是杂货商付款义务的先决条件。这是因为杂货商可以选择接受或拒绝不合格商品。但由于杂货商接受了七箱生菜，因此必须赔偿这七箱生菜，减去所造成的任何损失。\n\n教育目标：如果货物与合同不完全相符，则属于 UCC 规定的违约行为。 UCC 允许买方拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。当买方接受货物时，买方必须向卖方支付所接受货物的合同价款，减去因违约而造成的任何损失。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方对不当交付的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-717 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方可以从应付价格中扣除损害赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果货物与合同不完全相符，则属于 UCC 规定的违约行为。 UCC 允许买方拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。当买方接受货物时，买方必须向卖方支付所接受货物的合同价款，减去因违约而造成的任何损失。","iracZh":{"issue":"经销商有权向杂货商收取多少费用（如果有的话）？","rule":"如果货物与合同不完全相符，则属于 UCC 规定的违约行为。 UCC 允许买方拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。当买方接受货物时，买方必须向卖方支付所接受货物的合同价款，减去因违约而造成的任何损失。","analysis":"《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 管辖商品（例如生菜）销售合同。根据 UCC，货物必须完全符合合同。未能提供此类货物属于违约行为，除非在剩余履行时间内予以纠正，否则买方可以拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。当买方接受货物时，买方必须向卖方支付所接受货物的合同价，减去因违约而产生的任何损失，例如因购买（即购买）替代货物而产生的损失。在这里，经销商签订合同向杂货商提供八箱生菜。随后，分销商错误地只运送了七箱生菜，并且没有解决这一缺陷（违规）。杂货商接受了这七个板条箱，因此分销商有权收取这些板条箱所列出的合同价格（而不是合理价值）（选项 D）。然而，该价格必须减去杂货商因分销商违约而造成的损失（如果有）（选择 B）。 （选择 A） 所有八个板条箱的投标并不是杂货商付款义务的先决条件。这是因为杂货商可以选择接受或拒绝不合格商品。但由于杂货商接受了七箱生菜，因此必须赔偿这七箱生菜，减去所造成的任何损失。\n\n教育目标：如果货物与合同不完全相符，则属于 UCC 规定的违约行为。 UCC 允许买方拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。当买方接受货物时，买方必须向卖方支付所接受货物的合同价款，减去因违约而造成的任何损失。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方对不当交付的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-717 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方可以从应付价格中扣除损害赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 C：七箱生菜的标价减去杂货商因经销商未能交付全部订单而造成的损失（如果有）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-69007d6a1dee741f"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":217,"sourceQuestionPage":641,"sourceQuestionPages":[641],"sourceExplanationPages":[642,643],"sourceAnswerPages":[642,643],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-218","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"An art collector paid a gallery $1,000 to purchase a framed drawing from the gallery's collection. The price included shipping by the gallery to the collector's home. The gallery's owner used inadequate materials to wrap the drawing. The frame broke during shipment and scratched the drawing, reducing the drawing's value to $300. The collector complained to the gallery owner, who told the collector to take the drawing to a specific art restorer to have the drawing repaired. The collector paid the restorer $400 to repair the drawing, but not all of the scratches could be fixed. The drawing, after being repaired, was worth $700. The gallery owner subsequently refused to pay either for the repairs or for the damage to the drawing. In an action by the collector against the gallery owner for damages, which of the following awards is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Nothing.","B":"$300.","C":"$400.","D":"$700."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Under the UCC, contracts for the sale of goods come with an implied warranty of merchantability if the seller is a merchant with respect to the type of goods involved. Under this warranty, damage caused by the seller's failure to adequately package an item constitutes a breach. If the buyer nevertheless chooses to accept the goods, the buyer's damages are the difference between: the value of the goods as accepted and the value of the goods as warranted. Repair costs are often used to determine this difference in value. But if the repairs fail to restore the goods to their value as warranted, the buyer can recover the repair costs plus the difference between the value of the goods as warranted and the value of the goods after the repairs.* Here, the gallery's use of inadequate materials to wrap the drawing constituted a breach of warranty. The resulting damage during shipment reduced the drawing's value to $300. And although the art collector paid the restorer $400 to repair the drawing, it was worth just $700 after the repairs. As a result, the collector can recover the repair costs ($400) plus the difference between the drawing's value after the repairs and its value as warranted ($1,000 - $700 = $300). Therefore, an award of $700 is most likely (Choices A, B & C). *This assumes that the buyer paid for the repairs.\n\nEducational objective: When repairs fail to restore goods to their value as warranted, the buyer's damages are equal to the cost of repairs plus the difference between the value of the goods after the repairs and the value of the goods as warranted. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (implied warranty of merchantability).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-714 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's damages for breach in regard to accepted goods).","educationalObjective":"When repairs fail to restore goods to their value as warranted, the buyer's damages are equal to the cost of repairs plus the difference between the value of the goods after the repairs and the value of the goods as warranted. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (implied warranty of merchantability).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-714 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's damages for breach in regard to accepted goods).","rule":"When repairs fail to restore goods to their value as warranted, the buyer's damages are equal to the cost of repairs plus the difference between the value of the goods after the repairs and the value of the goods as warranted. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"In an action by the collector against the gallery owner for damages, which of the following awards is most likely?","rule":"When repairs fail to restore goods to their value as warranted, the buyer's damages are equal to the cost of repairs plus the difference between the value of the goods after the repairs and the value of the goods as warranted. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Under the UCC, contracts for the sale of goods come with an implied warranty of merchantability if the seller is a merchant with respect to the type of goods involved. Under this warranty, damage caused by the seller's failure to adequately package an item constitutes a breach. If the buyer nevertheless chooses to accept the goods, the buyer's damages are the difference between: the value of the goods as accepted and the value of the goods as warranted. Repair costs are often used to determine this difference in value. But if the repairs fail to restore the goods to their value as warranted, the buyer can recover the repair costs plus the difference between the value of the goods as warranted and the value of the goods after the repairs.* Here, the gallery's use of inadequate materials to wrap the drawing constituted a breach of warranty. The resulting damage during shipment reduced the drawing's value to $300. And although the art collector paid the restorer $400 to repair the drawing, it was worth just $700 after the repairs. As a result, the collector can recover the repair costs ($400) plus the difference between the drawing's value after the repairs and its value as warranted ($1,000 - $700 = $300). Therefore, an award of $700 is most likely (Choices A, B & C). *This assumes that the buyer paid for the repairs.","conclusion":"Answer D: $700."},"questionZh":"一位艺术收藏家向一家画廊支付了 1,000 美元，从该画廊的收藏中购买了一幅镶框画作。价格包括由画廊运送到收藏家家中的费用。画廊的主人使用了不足的材料来包裹这幅画。运输过程中框架破裂并划伤了画作，使画作的价值降至 300 美元。收藏家向画廊老板投诉，画廊老板要求收藏家将画作带到特定的艺术修复师处进行修复。收藏家付给修复者 400 美元来修复这幅画，但并不是所有的划痕都可以修复。这幅画修复后价值 700 美元。画廊老板随后拒绝支付维修费用或画作损坏费用。在收藏家向画廊所有者提起损害赔偿诉讼时，最有可能获得以下哪项赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"没有什么。","B":"300 美元。","C":"400 美元。","D":"700 美元。"},"explanationZh":"根据 UCC，如果卖方是所涉及商品类型的商人，则商品销售合同附带适销性默示保证。根据本保证，由于卖方未能充分包装物品而造成的损坏构成违约。如果买方仍然选择接受货物，则买方的损失为以下两者之间的差额：已接受的货物的价值与保证的货物的价值。维修成本通常用于确定这种价值差异。但如果修复未能使货物恢复到其保证的价值，则买方可以收回修复费用加上保证的货物价值与修复后的货物价值之间的差额。*在此，画廊使用不适当的材料来包裹图画，构成了违反保证的行为。由于运输过程中造成的损坏，这幅画的价值降至 300 美元。尽管艺术品收藏家支付了修复者 400 美元来修复这幅画，但修复后它的价值仅为 700 美元。因此，收藏家可以收回修复费用（400 美元）加上修复后的图纸价值与其保证价值之间的差额（1,000 美元 - 700 美元 = 300 美元）。因此，最有可能获得 700 美元的奖励（选项 A、B 和 C）。 *这假设买家支付了维修费用。\n\n教育目的：当修理未能使货物恢复到其保证价值时，买方的损失等于修理费用加上修理后货物的价值与保证货物的价值之间的差额。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（默示适销性默示担保）。\n\nUCC § 2-714 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方因接受货物的违约而造成的损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当修理未能使货物恢复到保证的价值时，买方的损失等于修理费用加上修理后的货物价值与保证的货物价值之间的差额。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"在收藏家向画廊所有者提起损害赔偿诉讼时，最有可能获得以下哪项赔偿？","rule":"当修理未能使货物恢复到保证的价值时，买方的损失等于修理费用加上修理后的货物价值与保证的货物价值之间的差额。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"根据 UCC，如果卖方是所涉及商品类型的商人，则商品销售合同附带适销性默示保证。根据本保证，由于卖方未能充分包装物品而造成的损坏构成违约。如果买方仍然选择接受货物，则买方的损失为以下两者之间的差额：已接受的货物的价值与保证的货物的价值。维修成本通常用于确定这种价值差异。但如果修复未能使货物恢复到其保证的价值，则买方可以收回修复费用加上保证的货物价值与修复后的货物价值之间的差额。*在此，画廊使用不适当的材料来包裹图画，构成了违反保证的行为。由于运输过程中造成的损坏，这幅画的价值降至 300 美元。尽管艺术品收藏家支付了修复者 400 美元来修复这幅画，但修复后它的价值仅为 700 美元。因此，收藏家可以收回修复费用（400 美元）加上修复后的图纸价值与其保证价值之间的差额（1,000 美元 - 700 美元 = 300 美元）。因此，最有可能获得 700 美元的奖励（选项 A、B 和 C）。 *这假设买家支付了维修费用。\n\n教育目的：当修理未能使货物恢复到其保证价值时，买方的损失等于修理费用加上修理后货物的价值与保证货物的价值之间的差额。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-314 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（默示适销性默示担保）。\n\nUCC § 2-714 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方因接受货物的违约而造成的损失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：700 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-aedf7bb5faa40b63"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":218,"sourceQuestionPage":644,"sourceQuestionPages":[644],"sourceExplanationPages":[645,646],"sourceAnswerPages":[645,646],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-219","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A high-volume pleasure-boat retailer with a nearly inexhaustible supply of boats entered into a written contract with a boater, signed by both parties, to sell him a power boat for $12,000. The manufacturer's price of the boat delivered to the retailer was $9,500. As the contract provided, the boater paid the retailer $4,000 in advance and promised to pay the full balance upon the boat's delivery. The contract contained no liquidated damages provision. Prior to the agreed delivery date, the boater notified the retailer that he would be financially unable to conclude the purchase; and the retailer thereupon resold the same boat that the boater had ordered to a third person for $12,000 cash. If the boater sues the retailer for restitution of the $4,000 advance payment, how should the court decide?","choices":{"A":"The boater's claim should be denied, because, as the party in default, he is deemed to have lost any right to restitution of a benefit conferred on the retailer.","B":"The boater's claim should be denied, because, but for his repudiation, the retailer would have made a profit on two boat-sales instead of one.","C":"The boater's claim should be upheld in the amount of $3,500 ($4,000 minus $500 as statutory damages under the UCC).","D":"The boater's claim should be upheld in the amount of $4,000 minus the amount of the retailer's lost profit under its contract with the boater."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Breaching buyer's recovery of advance payment\n\n(UCC § 2-718)\n\nBuyer who made advance payment before breach can recover amount paid minus seller's:\n\nliquidated damages – amount set forth in contract statutory damages – 20% of contract price or $500, whichever is less (if no valid liquidated damages clause) OR\n\nactual damages – expectation damages plus any incidental damages (if amount exceeds statutory damages) UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC, a breaching buyer is entitled to restitution damages for the value of any advance payments it made to the seller. This ensures that the seller is not unjustly enriched at the buyer's expense (Choice A). However, the buyer's damages award will be offset by the seller's damages. If, as here, there is no liquidated damages provision, then the seller's offset is the greater of the following: Statutory damages – $500 or 20% of the total contract price, whichever is smaller Actual damages – expectation damages (eg, lost profits if the seller is a lost-volume seller), plus any incidental damages (eg, shipping or transportation costs) Therefore, an injured seller is not limited to a $500 statutory offset when the seller's actual damages exceed that amount (Choice C). Here, the retailer is a lost-volume seller, as evidenced by its nearly inexhaustible supply of boats. But for the boater's breach, the retailer would have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one. As a result, the retailer's actual damages are its lost profit from the sale to the boater plus incidental damages (none are indicated). The boater's claim should therefore be upheld in the amount of $4,000 for the advance payment minus the amount of the retailer's lost profit. (Choice B) The retailer would have made a profit on two boat sales instead of one had the boater not repudiated the contract. However, since the boater is entitled to restitution of the $4,000 advance payment, the retailer's lost profit (from the sale to the boater) will be subtracted from—but not negate—the boater's claim.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a breaching buyer is entitled to restitution damages for the value of any advance payments it made to the seller, minus the amount of the seller's damages.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-718 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (liquidation or limitation of damages and deposits).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a breaching buyer is entitled to restitution damages for the value of any advance payments it made to the seller, minus the amount of the seller's damages.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-718 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (liquidation or limitation of damages and deposits).","rule":"Under the UCC, a breaching buyer is entitled to restitution damages for the value of any advance payments it made to the seller, minus the amount of the seller's damages.","irac":{"issue":"If the boater sues the retailer for restitution of the $4,000 advance payment, how should the court decide?","rule":"Under the UCC, a breaching buyer is entitled to restitution damages for the value of any advance payments it made to the seller, minus the amount of the seller's damages.","analysis":"Breaching buyer's recovery of advance payment\n\n(UCC § 2-718)\n\nBuyer who made advance payment before breach can recover amount paid minus seller's:\n\nliquidated damages – amount set forth in contract statutory damages – 20% of contract price or $500, whichever is less (if no valid liquidated damages clause) OR\n\nactual damages – expectation damages plus any incidental damages (if amount exceeds statutory damages) UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nUnder the UCC, a breaching buyer is entitled to restitution damages for the value of any advance payments it made to the seller. This ensures that the seller is not unjustly enriched at the buyer's expense (Choice A). However, the buyer's damages award will be offset by the seller's damages. If, as here, there is no liquidated damages provision, then the seller's offset is the greater of the following: Statutory damages – $500 or 20% of the total contract price, whichever is smaller Actual damages – expectation damages (eg, lost profits if the seller is a lost-volume seller), plus any incidental damages (eg, shipping or transportation costs) Therefore, an injured seller is not limited to a $500 statutory offset when the seller's actual damages exceed that amount (Choice C). Here, the retailer is a lost-volume seller, as evidenced by its nearly inexhaustible supply of boats. But for the boater's breach, the retailer would have made two sales (and two profits) rather than one. As a result, the retailer's actual damages are its lost profit from the sale to the boater plus incidental damages (none are indicated). The boater's claim should therefore be upheld in the amount of $4,000 for the advance payment minus the amount of the retailer's lost profit. (Choice B) The retailer would have made a profit on two boat sales instead of one had the boater not repudiated the contract. However, since the boater is entitled to restitution of the $4,000 advance payment, the retailer's lost profit (from the sale to the boater) will be subtracted from—but not negate—the boater's claim.","conclusion":"Answer D: The boater's claim should be upheld in the amount of $4,000 minus the amount of the retailer's lost profit under its contract with the boater."},"questionZh":"一家拥有几乎取之不尽的船只供应的大批量游船零售商与一位划船者签订了一份双方签署的书面合同，以 12,000 美元的价格向他出售一艘动力船。交付给零售商的船的制造商价格为 9,500 美元。按照合同规定，船主向零售商预付了 4,000 美元，并承诺在船交付时支付全部余额。合同中没有约定违约金条款。在约定的交货日期之前，船主通知零售商他在经济上无法完成购买；随后，零售商以 12,000 美元现金将船夫订购的同一艘船转售给第三人。如果船主起诉零售商要求返还4000美元预付款，法院该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"船主的主张应当被驳回，因为作为违约方，他被视为丧失了要求归还零售商所享有利益的任何权利。","B":"船主的主张应该被驳回，因为如果没有他的否认，零售商就会从两艘船的销售中获利，而不是一艘。","C":"船主的索赔金额为 3,500 美元（4,000 美元减去 UCC 规定的法定损害赔偿金 500 美元），应予以支持。","D":"船主的索赔金额应为 4,000 美元，减去零售商根据与船主的合同所损失的利润。"},"explanationZh":"违反买方收回预付款的规定\n\n（UCC§2-718）\n\n在违约前支付预付款的买方可以收回已支付的金额减去卖方的金额：\n\n违约金 – 合同中规定的金额法定赔偿金 – 合同价格的 20% 或 500 美元，以较低者为准（如果没有有效的违约金条款）或\n\n实际损害 – 预期损害加上任何附带损害（如果金额超过法定损害） UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC，违约买方有权就其向卖方支付的任何预付款的价值获得赔偿。这确保了卖方不会因买方的损失而获得不公正的利益（选择 A）。然而，买方的损害赔偿金将被卖方的损害赔偿金所抵消。如果像这里一样，没有违约金条款，那么卖方的抵消额是以下两者中的较大者： 法定损害赔偿金 – 500 美元或合同总价的 20%，以较小者为准 实际损害赔偿 – 预期损害赔偿（例如，如果卖方是销量损失的卖方，则利润损失），加上任何附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）（例如，运输或运输费用） 因此，当卖方的实际损害赔偿超过 500 美元时，受损害的卖方不仅限于 500 美元的法定抵消赔偿金额（选项 C）。在这里，零售商是一个销量损失的卖家，其几乎取之不尽的船只供应就证明了这一点。如果没有划船者的违约，零售商将进行两次销售（和两次利润），而不是一次。因此，零售商的实际损失是其向船主销售的利润损失加上附带损失（未注明）。因此，船主的索赔金额为 4,000 美元，预付款减去零售商的利润损失，应予支持。 （选择 B）如果船主没有拒绝合同，零售商本来可以通过出售两艘船而不是一艘船来获利。然而，由于船主有权要求归还 4,000 美元的预付款，因此零售商的利润损失（来自向船主的销售）将从船主的索赔中扣除（但不会否定）。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法典》（UCC），违约买方有权要求赔偿损失，其金额相当于其向卖方支付的任何预付款的价值减去卖方损失的金额。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-718 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（损害赔偿金和押金的清算或限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），违约买方有权要求赔偿其向卖方支付的任何预付款的价值，减去卖方的损害赔偿金额。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果船主起诉零售商要求返还4000美元预付款，法院该如何判决？","rule":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），违约买方有权要求赔偿其向卖方支付的任何预付款的价值，减去卖方的损害赔偿金额。","analysis":"违反买方收回预付款的规定\n\n（UCC§2-718）\n\n在违约前支付预付款的买方可以收回已支付的金额减去卖方的金额：\n\n违约金 – 合同中规定的金额法定赔偿金 – 合同价格的 20% 或 500 美元，以较低者为准（如果没有有效的违约金条款）或\n\n实际损害 – 预期损害加上任何附带损害（如果金额超过法定损害） UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n根据 UCC，违约买方有权就其向卖方支付的任何预付款的价值获得赔偿。这确保了卖方不会因买方的损失而获得不公正的利益（选择 A）。然而，买方的损害赔偿金将被卖方的损害赔偿金所抵消。如果像这里一样，没有违约金条款，那么卖方的抵消额是以下两者中的较大者： 法定损害赔偿金 – 500 美元或合同总价的 20%，以较小者为准 实际损害赔偿 – 预期损害赔偿（例如，如果卖方是销量损失的卖方，则利润损失），加上任何附随损害赔偿（incidental damages）（例如，运输或运输费用） 因此，当卖方的实际损害赔偿超过 500 美元时，受损害的卖方不仅限于 500 美元的法定抵消赔偿金额（选项 C）。在这里，零售商是一个销量损失的卖家，其几乎取之不尽的船只供应就证明了这一点。如果没有划船者的违约，零售商将进行两次销售（和两次利润），而不是一次。因此，零售商的实际损失是其向船主销售的利润损失加上附带损失（未注明）。因此，船主的索赔金额为 4,000 美元，预付款减去零售商的利润损失，应予支持。 （选择 B）如果船主没有拒绝合同，零售商本来可以通过出售两艘船而不是一艘船来获利。然而，由于船主有权要求归还 4,000 美元的预付款，因此零售商的利润损失（来自向船主的销售）将从船主的索赔中扣除（但不会否定）。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法典》（UCC），违约买方有权要求赔偿损失，其金额相当于其向卖方支付的任何预付款的价值减去卖方损失的金额。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-718 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（损害赔偿金和押金的清算或限制）。","conclusion":"答案 D：船主的索赔金额应为 4,000 美元，减去零售商根据与船主的合同所损失的利润。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":219,"sourceQuestionPage":647,"sourceQuestionPages":[647],"sourceExplanationPages":[648,649],"sourceAnswerPages":[648,649],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-220","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Impossibility, Impracticability & Frustration","question":"Under a written agreement, a pastry supplier promised to sell its entire output of baked buns at a specified unit price to a retailer, for one year. The retailer promised not to sell any other supplier's baked buns. The parties' contract included a provision for termination by either party at any time upon reasonable notice. After six months of performance on both sides, the supplier, claiming that its old bun-baker had become uneconomical and that it could not afford a new one, dismantled the bun-baker and began using the space for making dog biscuits. The supplier's output of baked buns having ceased, the retailer sued for breach of contract. The retailer moves for summary judgment on liability, and the supplier moves for summary judgment dismissing the retailer's claim. How should the court rule?","choices":{"A":"Deny both motions, because the supplier may legally cease production of baked buns, but under the circumstances it must share with the retailer its profits from the manufacture of dog biscuits until the end of the first year.","B":"Deny both motions, because there are triable issues of fact as to whether the supplier gave reasonable notice of termination or whether its losses from continued production of baked buns were sufficiently substantial to justify cessation of production.","C":"Grant summary judgment for the retailer, because as a matter of law the supplier could not discontinue production of baked buns merely because it was losing money on that product.","D":"Grant summary judgment for the supplier, because its cessation of baked-bun production and the retailer's awareness thereof amounted as a matter of law to valid notice of termination as permitted by the contract."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA motion for summary judgment is a request that the court enter judgment without a full trial because there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. A fact issue is material if it is of consequence to the outcome of the case. In this breach of contract case, one material fact issue is whether the supplier's cessation of baked-bun production and the retailer's awareness thereof amounted to reasonable notice of termination as required by the parties' contract. Reasonableness is a question of fact that must be answered by the trier of fact, so it cannot be decided on summary judgment (Choice D). Another material fact issue is whether the supplier's monetary losses from the continued production of baked buns were substantial enough to give rise to an impracticability defense or otherwise discharge the supplier's contractual obligation. The defense of impracticability is available when: an unanticipated event makes a party's performance impracticable nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Although non-extraordinary increases in the cost of performance do not make performance impracticable, there is no bright line rule for determining as a matter of law whether an increase in cost rises to the level of impracticability. Instead, this is a question of fact to be answered by the trier of fact (Choice C). Accordingly, both parties' motions for summary judgment should be denied. (Choice A) Even if the supplier can legally cease production of baked buns, there is no indication that the contract entitles the retailer to a share of the supplier's profits from the manufacture of dog biscuits.\n\nEducational objective: The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated event makes performance impracticable, (2) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract, and (3) the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Non-extraordinary increases in the cost of performance do not make performance impracticable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (impracticability).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).","educationalObjective":"The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated event makes performance impracticable, (2) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract, and (3) the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Non-extraordinary increases in the cost of performance do not make performance impracticable.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (impracticability).\n\nFed. R. Civ. P. 56 (summary judgment).","rule":"The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated event makes performance impracticable, (2) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract, and (3) the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Non-extraordinary increases in the cost of performance do not make performance impracticable.","irac":{"issue":"How should the court rule?","rule":"The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated event makes performance impracticable, (2) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract, and (3) the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Non-extraordinary increases in the cost of performance do not make performance impracticable.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA motion for summary judgment is a request that the court enter judgment without a full trial because there is no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment as a matter of law. A fact issue is material if it is of consequence to the outcome of the case. In this breach of contract case, one material fact issue is whether the supplier's cessation of baked-bun production and the retailer's awareness thereof amounted to reasonable notice of termination as required by the parties' contract. Reasonableness is a question of fact that must be answered by the trier of fact, so it cannot be decided on summary judgment (Choice D). Another material fact issue is whether the supplier's monetary losses from the continued production of baked buns were substantial enough to give rise to an impracticability defense or otherwise discharge the supplier's contractual obligation. The defense of impracticability is available when: an unanticipated event makes a party's performance impracticable nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Although non-extraordinary increases in the cost of performance do not make performance impracticable, there is no bright line rule for determining as a matter of law whether an increase in cost rises to the level of impracticability. Instead, this is a question of fact to be answered by the trier of fact (Choice C). Accordingly, both parties' motions for summary judgment should be denied. (Choice A) Even if the supplier can legally cease production of baked buns, there is no indication that the contract entitles the retailer to a share of the supplier's profits from the manufacture of dog biscuits.","conclusion":"Answer B: Deny both motions, because there are triable issues of fact as to whether the supplier gave reasonable notice of termination or whether its losses from continued production of baked buns were sufficiently substantial to justify cessation of production."},"questionZh":"根据一份书面协议，一家糕点供应商承诺将其全部产量的烤面包以指定单价出售给零售商，为期一年。该零售商承诺不会出售任何其他供应商的烤面包。双方的合同包括任何一方在合理通知后随时终止的条款。双方协商六个月后，供应商声称其旧面包机已经不经济，无力购买新面包机，于是将面包机拆除，并开始利用其空间制作狗饼干。供应商停止生产面包，零售商因违反合同而提起诉讼。零售商提出了关于责任的简易判决，而供应商则提出了驳回零售商索赔的简易判决。法院应该如何判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"驳回这两项动议，因为供应商可以合法地停止生产烤面包，但在这种情况下，它必须与零售商分享生产狗饼干的利润，直到第一年年底。","B":"驳回这两项动议，因为存在一些事实问题，即供应商是否发出合理的终止通知，或者其继续生产烤面包造成的损失是否足以证明停止生产是合理的。","C":"对零售商作出简易判决，因为根据法律规定，供应商不能仅仅因为该产品亏损而停止生产烤面包。","D":"对供应商作出简易判决，因为其停止烤面包生产以及零售商对此的认识在法律上相当于合同允许的有效终止通知。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n简易判决动议是请求法院在不进行全面审理的情况下做出判决的请求，因为不存在重大事实的真正问题，动议者有权根据法律要求作出判决。如果事实问题对案件的结果有重要影响，则该事实问题是重大的。在这起违约案件中，一个重要的事实问题是供应商停止生产烤面包以及零售商是否意识到这一点是否构成双方合同要求的合理终止通知。合理性是一个事实问题，必须由事实审判者来回答，因此不能通过简易判断来决定（选项D）。另一个重大事实问题是，供应商因继续生产烘焙面包而遭受的金钱损失是否足够大，足以引起履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩或以其他方式解除供应商的合同义务。在以下情况下，可以提出履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩： 意外事件使一方的履约变得商业上不可行 事件的不发生是合同的基本假设，并且寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。尽管履约成本的非异常增加并不会使履约变得商业上不可行，但从法律上来说，并没有明确的规则来确定成本的增加是否达到不切实际的水平。相反，这是一个由事实审判者回答的事实问题（选项C）。据此，双方请求简易判决的请求均应驳回。 （选项A）即使供应商可以合法地停止生产烤面包，也没有迹象表明合同赋予零售商从供应商生产狗饼干的利润中分享的权利。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，可以对不可行进行抗辩：(1) 意外事件导致履约不可行；(2) 事件未发生是合同的基本假设；(3) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。履行成本的非异常增加并不会使性能变得商业上不可行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不切实际）。\n\n美联储。 R.公民。第 56 页（简易判断）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，可以提出履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩：(1) 意外事件导致履约变得商业上不可行；(2) 事件未发生是合同的基本假设；(3) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。履行成本的非异常增加并不会使性能变得商业上不可行。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应该如何判决？","rule":"在以下情况下，可以提出履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩：(1) 意外事件导致履约变得商业上不可行；(2) 事件未发生是合同的基本假设；(3) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。履行成本的非异常增加并不会使性能变得商业上不可行。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n简易判决动议是请求法院在不进行全面审理的情况下做出判决的请求，因为不存在重大事实的真正问题，动议者有权根据法律要求作出判决。如果事实问题对案件的结果有重要影响，则该事实问题是重大的。在这起违约案件中，一个重要的事实问题是供应商停止生产烤面包以及零售商是否意识到这一点是否构成双方合同要求的合理终止通知。合理性是一个事实问题，必须由事实审判者来回答，因此不能通过简易判断来决定（选项D）。另一个重大事实问题是，供应商因继续生产烘焙面包而遭受的金钱损失是否足够大，足以引起履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩或以其他方式解除供应商的合同义务。在以下情况下，可以提出履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩： 意外事件使一方的履约变得商业上不可行 事件的不发生是合同的基本假设，并且寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。尽管履约成本的非异常增加并不会使履约变得商业上不可行，但从法律上来说，并没有明确的规则来确定成本的增加是否达到不切实际的水平。相反，这是一个由事实审判者回答的事实问题（选项C）。据此，双方请求简易判决的请求均应驳回。 （选项A）即使供应商可以合法地停止生产烤面包，也没有迹象表明合同赋予零售商从供应商生产狗饼干的利润中分享的权利。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，可以对不可行进行抗辩：(1) 意外事件导致履约不可行；(2) 事件未发生是合同的基本假设；(3) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。履行成本的非异常增加并不会使性能变得商业上不可行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不切实际）。\n\n美联储。 R.公民。第 56 页（简易判断）。","conclusion":"答案 B：驳回这两项动议，因为存在一些事实问题，即供应商是否发出合理的终止通知，或者其继续生产烤面包造成的损失是否足以证明停止生产是合理的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":220,"sourceQuestionPage":650,"sourceQuestionPages":[650],"sourceExplanationPages":[651,652],"sourceAnswerPages":[651,652],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-221","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A seller entered into a contract with a buyer to sell a house for a price of $150,000. The contract contained the following clause: \"This contract is conditional on the buyer securing bank financing at an interest rate of 7% or below.\" The buyer did not make an application for bank financing and therefore did not secure it and refused to proceed with the purchase. The seller sued the buyer for breach of contract. Is the seller likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer did not secure bank financing.","B":"No, because the contract did not expressly impose on the buyer any obligation to apply for bank financing.","C":"Yes, because a court will excuse the condition to avoid a disproportionate forfeiture.","D":"Yes, because a court will imply a term imposing on the buyer a duty to use reasonable efforts to secure bank financing."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Contract conditions\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, creates party's obligation to precedent perform (e.g., A agrees to hire B if B graduates college)\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses party's existing subsequent obligation to perform (e.g., B agrees to work for C until B graduates college)\n\nPerformance is generally due once a contract is formed. However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs A condition precedent will be excused if a party whose performance is subject to that condition wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing. As a result, the condition no longer must occur for that party's performance to become due. Here, the buyer's performance was delayed by the condition that he secured financing at an interest rate of 7% or below. The implied duty of good faith and fair dealing required the buyer to make reasonable efforts to secure financing. He breached that duty by making no effort to secure financing, so the condition precedent was excused and the buyer's performance became due. Therefore, the seller is likely to prevail (Choices A & B). *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). (Choice C) The seller would not be subject to any significant forfeiture (ie, loss of benefit or right) if the buyer's condition to obtain financing is not excused. That is because the seller can sell the house to someone else.\n\nEducational objective: A condition precedent will be excused if a party whose performance is subject to that condition wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 229 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (excuse of condition to avoid forfeiture).","educationalObjective":"A condition precedent will be excused if a party whose performance is subject to that condition wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 229 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (excuse of condition to avoid forfeiture).","rule":"A condition precedent will be excused if a party whose performance is subject to that condition wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing.","irac":{"issue":"Is the seller likely to prevail?","rule":"A condition precedent will be excused if a party whose performance is subject to that condition wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing.","analysis":"Contract conditions\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, creates party's obligation to precedent perform (e.g., A agrees to hire B if B graduates college)\n\nCondition Uncertain future event that, if it occurs, excuses party's existing subsequent obligation to perform (e.g., B agrees to work for C until B graduates college)\n\nPerformance is generally due once a contract is formed. However, performance can be delayed or discharged by a condition—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due or is discharged. There are two types of conditions: condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs A condition precedent will be excused if a party whose performance is subject to that condition wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing. As a result, the condition no longer must occur for that party's performance to become due. Here, the buyer's performance was delayed by the condition that he secured financing at an interest rate of 7% or below. The implied duty of good faith and fair dealing required the buyer to make reasonable efforts to secure financing. He breached that duty by making no effort to secure financing, so the condition precedent was excused and the buyer's performance became due. Therefore, the seller is likely to prevail (Choices A & B). *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). (Choice C) The seller would not be subject to any significant forfeiture (ie, loss of benefit or right) if the buyer's condition to obtain financing is not excused. That is because the seller can sell the house to someone else.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because a court will imply a term imposing on the buyer a duty to use reasonable efforts to secure bank financing."},"questionZh":"卖方与买方签订合同，以 150,000 美元的价格出售房屋。该合同包含以下条款：“本合同的条件是买方以 7% 或以下的利率获得银行融资。”买方没有申请银行融资，因此无法获得贷款并拒绝继续购买。卖方起诉买方违反合同。卖家有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为买方没有获得银行融资。","B":"不会，因为合同没有明确规定买方有任何申请银行融资的义务。","C":"是的，因为法院会原谅该条件以避免不成比例的没收。","D":"是的，因为法院将暗示一项条款，要求买方有义务采取合理的努力来获得银行融资。"},"explanationZh":"合同条件\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则一方有义务履行先例（例如，如果 B 大学毕业，A 同意雇用 B）\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则可以免除一方现有的后续履行义务（例如，B 同意为 C 工作直至 B 大学毕业）\n\n合同一旦签订，通常就应履行。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生 后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生就免除履行 如果履行该条件的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，违反了善意和公平交易的默示义务），则先决条件将被免除。因此，该方的履约到期时不再必须出现该条件。在此，买方的履约被推迟，因为他必须以 7% 或以下的利率获得融资。诚实信用和公平交易的隐含义务要求买方做出合理的努力以获得融资。他没有努力获得融资，从而违反了该义务，因此先决条件被免除，买方的履约到期。因此，卖方很可能获胜（选择 A 和 B）。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。 （选择C） 如果买方获得融资的条件不能得到免除，则卖方不会遭受任何重大没收（即利益或权利的损失）。这是因为卖方可以将房子卖给别人。\n\n教育目标：如果履行先决条件的一方错误地阻止了先决条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则该先决条件将被免除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 229（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（避免没收的条件借口）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果履行先决条件的一方错误地阻止了先决条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则该先决条件将被免除。","iracZh":{"issue":"卖家有可能获胜吗？","rule":"如果履行先决条件的一方错误地阻止了先决条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则该先决条件将被免除。","analysis":"合同条件\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则一方有义务履行先例（例如，如果 B 大学毕业，A 同意雇用 B）\n\n条件 不确定的未来事件，如果发生，则可以免除一方现有的后续履行义务（例如，B 同意为 C 工作直至 B 大学毕业）\n\n合同一旦签订，通常就应履行。然而，履行可能会因某种条件而延迟或解除，即在履行到期或解除之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生 后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生就免除履行 如果履行该条件的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，违反了善意和公平交易的默示义务），则先决条件将被免除。因此，该方的履约到期时不再必须出现该条件。在此，买方的履约被推迟，因为他必须以 7% 或以下的利率获得融资。诚实信用和公平交易的隐含义务要求买方做出合理的努力以获得融资。他没有努力获得融资，从而违反了该义务，因此先决条件被免除，买方的履约到期。因此，卖方很可能获胜（选择 A 和 B）。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。 （选择C） 如果买方获得融资的条件不能得到免除，则卖方不会遭受任何重大没收（即利益或权利的损失）。这是因为卖方可以将房子卖给别人。\n\n教育目标：如果履行先决条件的一方错误地阻止了先决条件的发生（例如，通过违反诚实信用和公平交易的默示义务），则该先决条件将被免除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 229（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（避免没收的条件借口）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为法院将暗示一项条款，要求买方有义务采取合理的努力来获得银行融资。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":221,"sourceQuestionPage":653,"sourceQuestionPages":[653],"sourceExplanationPages":[654,655],"sourceAnswerPages":[654,655],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-222","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A dealer needed a certain rare baseball card to complete a set that he had contracted to assemble and sell to a collector. On March 1, the dealer obtained such a card from a woman in exchange for $1,000 and a written promise to redeliver to the woman \"no later than December 31 of this year\" a comparable specimen of the same kind of card. On March 2, the dealer consummated the sale of the complete set to the collector. On September 1, the market price of rare baseball cards suddenly began a rapid, sustained rise. On September 10, the woman wrote the dealer for assurance that the dealer would timely meet his card-replacement commitment to her. On September 15, the dealer telephoned the woman and said, \"I absolutely will not replace your card until the market drops far below its present level.\" The woman sued the dealer on October 15 for the market value of a comparable replacement-card. If the dealer moves to dismiss the woman's complaint, which of the following is the woman's best argument in opposing the motion?","choices":{"A":"The dealer breached an implied warranty by failing to supply the baseball card.","B":"The woman complied with her duty to mitigate her losses by promptly suing when the market rose.","C":"The woman may resort to any appropriate remedy for breach of contract because the dealer repudiated his performance.","D":"The woman may treat the dealer's anticipatory repudiation as an immediately actionable tort."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A contract for the sale of goods (eg, a baseball card) is governed by the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. A failure to provide those assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation. The nonrepudiating party may then treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the woman had reasonable grounds for insecurity about the dealer's promise to replace the rare baseball card since the market price of rare cards had begun a rapid, sustained rise. The dealer then failed to provide adequate assurance and explicitly repudiated his performance by stating, \"I absolutely will not replace your card until the market drops far below its present level.\" As a result, the woman's best argument in opposing the dealer's motion to dismiss is that the woman may resort to any appropriate remedy for breach of contract. (Choice A) The dealer did not breach any implied warranty by failing to supply the card. However, the dealer anticipatorily repudiated the contract by failing to provide adequate assurance that he would perform. (Choice B) Although the woman had the right to bring an immediate suit once the dealer repudiated, she was not required to mitigate her losses by promptly suing when the market rose. (Choice D) The woman may treat the dealer's anticipatory repudiation as an immediately actionable breach of contract, not a tort—ie, a wrongful or injurious act, other than breach of contract, subjecting the actor to civil liability.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance may make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Failure to provide assurance within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation, which the nonrepudiating party may treat as a breach or ignore.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance may make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Failure to provide assurance within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation, which the nonrepudiating party may treat as a breach or ignore.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance may make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Failure to provide assurance within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation, which the nonrepudiating party may treat as a breach or ignore.","irac":{"issue":"If the dealer moves to dismiss the woman's complaint, which of the following is the woman's best argument in opposing the motion?","rule":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance may make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Failure to provide assurance within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation, which the nonrepudiating party may treat as a breach or ignore.","analysis":"A contract for the sale of goods (eg, a baseball card) is governed by the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. A failure to provide those assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation. The nonrepudiating party may then treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the woman had reasonable grounds for insecurity about the dealer's promise to replace the rare baseball card since the market price of rare cards had begun a rapid, sustained rise. The dealer then failed to provide adequate assurance and explicitly repudiated his performance by stating, \"I absolutely will not replace your card until the market drops far below its present level.\" As a result, the woman's best argument in opposing the dealer's motion to dismiss is that the woman may resort to any appropriate remedy for breach of contract. (Choice A) The dealer did not breach any implied warranty by failing to supply the card. However, the dealer anticipatorily repudiated the contract by failing to provide adequate assurance that he would perform. (Choice B) Although the woman had the right to bring an immediate suit once the dealer repudiated, she was not required to mitigate her losses by promptly suing when the market rose. (Choice D) The woman may treat the dealer's anticipatory repudiation as an immediately actionable breach of contract, not a tort—ie, a wrongful or injurious act, other than breach of contract, subjecting the actor to civil liability.","conclusion":"Answer C: The woman may resort to any appropriate remedy for breach of contract because the dealer repudiated his performance."},"questionZh":"经销商需要一张稀有的棒球卡来完成一套他已签约组装并出售给收藏家的套装。 3 月 1 日，经销商以 1,000 美元的价格从一名妇女处获得了此类卡片，并书面承诺“不迟于今年 12 月 31 日”向该妇女重新交付同类卡片的类似样本。 3月2日，经销商向收藏家完成了整套出售。 9月1日，稀有棒球卡的市场价格突然开始快速持续上涨。 9月10日，女子给庄家写信，要求庄家及时履行对她的换卡承诺。 9月15日，庄家打电话给这位女士说：“除非市场价格远低于目前水平，否则我绝对不会换卡。”该女子于 10 月 15 日起诉经销商，要求赔偿类似替换卡的市场价值。如果经销商提出驳回该女士的投诉，以下哪项是该女士反对该动议的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"经销商未能提供棒球卡，从而违反了默示保证。","B":"这位女士履行了自己的职责，在市场上涨时立即提起诉讼，以减轻损失。","C":"由于经销商拒绝履行合同，该妇女可以采取任何适当的违约补救措施。","D":"妇女可以将经销商的预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）视为可立即提起诉讼的侵权行为。"},"explanationZh":"商品销售合同（例如棒球卡）受《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 管辖。根据《统一商法典》（UCC），一方有合理理由对另一方的履行感到不安全时，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供这些保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。然后，非毁约方可以将否认视为违约或忽略它并要求根据合同履行。在这里，这位女士有合理的理由对经销商更换稀有棒球卡的承诺感到不安全，因为稀有卡的市场价格已经开始快速、持续上涨。随后，该经销商未能提供足够的保证，并明确否认了自己的表现，并表示：“除非市场价格远低于目前水平，否则我绝对不会更换您的卡。”因此，该妇女反对经销商驳回动议的最佳论据是，该妇女可以就违约行为采取任何适当的补救措施。 （选择 A） 经销商并未因未能提供该卡而违反任何默示保证。然而，经销商未能提供充分的履约保证，因此预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行合同。 （选项B）虽然庄家拒付后，女士有权立即提起诉讼，但她并不需要在市场上涨时立即提起诉讼，以减轻损失。 （选项D） 妇女可以将经销商的预期毁约视为可立即起诉的违约行为，而不是侵权行为，即除违约行为之外的不法或伤害性行为，使行为者承担民事责任。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，有合理理由对另一方的表现不安全的一方可以提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行职责。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation），非毁约方可以将其视为违约或忽视。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据 UCC，一方有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全，可以书面要求另一方保证履约。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation），非毁约方可以将其视为违约或忽视。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果经销商提出驳回该女士的投诉，以下哪项是该女士反对该动议的最佳论据？","rule":"根据 UCC，一方有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全，可以书面要求另一方保证履约。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation），非毁约方可以将其视为违约或忽视。","analysis":"商品销售合同（例如棒球卡）受《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 管辖。根据《统一商法典》（UCC），一方有合理理由对另一方的履行感到不安全时，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供这些保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。然后，非毁约方可以将否认视为违约或忽略它并要求根据合同履行。在这里，这位女士有合理的理由对经销商更换稀有棒球卡的承诺感到不安全，因为稀有卡的市场价格已经开始快速、持续上涨。随后，该经销商未能提供足够的保证，并明确否认了自己的表现，并表示：“除非市场价格远低于目前水平，否则我绝对不会更换您的卡。”因此，该妇女反对经销商驳回动议的最佳论据是，该妇女可以就违约行为采取任何适当的补救措施。 （选择 A） 经销商并未因未能提供该卡而违反任何默示保证。然而，经销商未能提供充分的履约保证，因此预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行合同。 （选项B）虽然庄家拒付后，女士有权立即提起诉讼，但她并不需要在市场上涨时立即提起诉讼，以减轻损失。 （选项D） 妇女可以将经销商的预期毁约视为可立即起诉的违约行为，而不是侵权行为，即除违约行为之外的不法或伤害性行为，使行为者承担民事责任。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，有合理理由对另一方的表现不安全的一方可以提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行职责。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation），非毁约方可以将其视为违约或忽视。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","conclusion":"答案 C：由于经销商拒绝履行合同，该妇女可以采取任何适当的违约补救措施。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-2f69e55d0030a614"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":222,"sourceQuestionPage":656,"sourceQuestionPages":[656],"sourceExplanationPages":[657,658],"sourceAnswerPages":[657,658],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-223","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Statute of Frauds","question":"A buyer entered into a written contract to purchase from a seller 1,000 sets of specially manufactured ball bearings of a nonstandard dimension for a price of $10 per set. The seller correctly calculated that it would cost $8 to manufacture each set. Delivery was scheduled for 60 days later. Fifty-five days later, after the seller had completed production of the 1,000 sets, the buyer abandoned the project that required the specially manufactured ball bearings and repudiated the contract with the seller. After notifying the buyer of his intention to resell, the seller sold the 1,000 sets of ball bearings to a salvage company for $2 per set. The seller then sued the buyer for damages. What damages should the court award to the seller?","choices":{"A":"$2 per set, representing the difference between the cost of manufacture and the price the buyer agreed to pay.","B":"$6 per set, representing the difference between the cost of manufacture and the salvage price.","C":"$8 per set, representing the lost profits plus the unrecovered cost of manufacture.","D":"Nominal damages, because the seller failed to resell the goods by public auction."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the UCC, the seller can recover damages when the buyer breaches by repudiating the contract or wrongfully refusing to accept the goods. The court will use a measure of damages that puts the seller in the same position as if the contract had been performed, most often based on the market-price differential. But when this measure of damages is inadequate—eg, the contract is for specially manufactured goods of a nonstandard dimension (like the ball bearings here)—the seller's damages are equal to: the profit the seller would have made (eg, $10 per set contract price − $8 per set cost of manufacture = $2 per set) plus an allowance for costs reasonably incurred (eg, $8 per set cost of manufacture) minus payments received for the resale of the goods (eg, $2 per set salvage price). Therefore, the court should award the seller $8 per set ($2 + $8 − $2), representing the seller's lost profits plus his unrecovered cost of manufacture. (Choice A) $2 would be a correct measure of damages only if the seller had not yet begun manufacturing the ball bearings. In that case, there would have been no manufacturing costs or need to resell the goods. (Choice B) The difference between the seller's cost to manufacture the ball bearings and the salvage price (ie, $6) fails to compensate him for lost profits. Therefore, this would not be a proper damages award. (Choice D) There is no provision in the UCC requiring these goods to be resold by public auction.\n\nEducational objective: When a buyer breaches by repudiating the contract or wrongfully refusing to accept goods, the seller is entitled to damages that place it in the same position as if the contract had been performed. For specially manufactured goods, this includes expected profits plus costs reasonably incurred minus any proceeds from reselling the goods.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's damages for non- acceptance/repudiation).","educationalObjective":"When a buyer breaches by repudiating the contract or wrongfully refusing to accept goods, the seller is entitled to damages that place it in the same position as if the contract had been performed. For specially manufactured goods, this includes expected profits plus costs reasonably incurred minus any proceeds from reselling the goods.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's damages for non- acceptance/repudiation).","rule":"When a buyer breaches by repudiating the contract or wrongfully refusing to accept goods, the seller is entitled to damages that place it in the same position as if the contract had been performed. For specially manufactured goods, this includes expected profits plus costs reasonably incurred minus any proceeds from reselling the goods.","irac":{"issue":"What damages should the court award to the seller?","rule":"When a buyer breaches by repudiating the contract or wrongfully refusing to accept goods, the seller is entitled to damages that place it in the same position as if the contract had been performed. For specially manufactured goods, this includes expected profits plus costs reasonably incurred minus any proceeds from reselling the goods.","analysis":"Under the UCC, the seller can recover damages when the buyer breaches by repudiating the contract or wrongfully refusing to accept the goods. The court will use a measure of damages that puts the seller in the same position as if the contract had been performed, most often based on the market-price differential. But when this measure of damages is inadequate—eg, the contract is for specially manufactured goods of a nonstandard dimension (like the ball bearings here)—the seller's damages are equal to: the profit the seller would have made (eg, $10 per set contract price − $8 per set cost of manufacture = $2 per set) plus an allowance for costs reasonably incurred (eg, $8 per set cost of manufacture) minus payments received for the resale of the goods (eg, $2 per set salvage price). Therefore, the court should award the seller $8 per set ($2 + $8 − $2), representing the seller's lost profits plus his unrecovered cost of manufacture. (Choice A) $2 would be a correct measure of damages only if the seller had not yet begun manufacturing the ball bearings. In that case, there would have been no manufacturing costs or need to resell the goods. (Choice B) The difference between the seller's cost to manufacture the ball bearings and the salvage price (ie, $6) fails to compensate him for lost profits. Therefore, this would not be a proper damages award. (Choice D) There is no provision in the UCC requiring these goods to be resold by public auction.","conclusion":"Answer C: $8 per set, representing the lost profits plus the unrecovered cost of manufacture."},"questionZh":"买方签订了一份书面合同，以每套 10 美元的价格从卖方购买 1,000 套特制的非标准尺寸滚珠轴承。卖家正确计算出每套的制造成本为 8 美元。交货时间定于 60 天后。五十五天后，卖方生产完1000套后，买方放弃了需要特制滚珠轴承的项目，并与卖方解除了合同。在通知买家转售意向后，卖家将这 1,000 套滚珠轴承以每套 2 美元的价格卖给了一家废品公司。随后，卖方起诉买方，要求赔偿损失。法院应判给卖方哪些损害赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"每套 2 美元，代表制造成本与买方同意支付的价格之间的差额。","B":"每套 6 美元，代表制造成本和废品价格之间的差额。","C":"每套 8 美元，即利润损失加上未收回的制造成本。","D":"名义损失，因为卖方未能通过公开拍卖转售货物。"},"explanationZh":"根据UCC，当买方因拒绝履行合同或错误地拒绝接受货物而违约时，卖方可以要求赔偿损失。法院将使用一种损害赔偿措施，使卖方处于与合同已履行相同的地位，通常基于市场价格差异。但是，当这种损害赔偿措施不充分时——例如，合同是针对非标准尺寸的特制货物（如此处的滚珠轴承）——卖方的损害赔偿等于：卖方本应赚取的利润（例如，每套合同价格 10 美元 - 每套制造成本 8 美元 = 每套 2 美元）加上合理发生的成本补贴（例如，每套制造成本 8 美元）减去转售货物收到的付款（例如，每套残值价格 2 美元）。因此，法院应判给卖方每套 8 美元（2 美元 + 8 美元 - 2 美元），即卖方的利润损失加上其未收回的制造成本。 （选择 A）仅当卖方尚未开始制造滚珠轴承时，2 美元才是正确的损失衡量标准。在这种情况下，就没有制造成本或需要转售货物。 （选择B） 卖方制造滚珠轴承的成本与废品价格（即6 美元）之间的差额未能补偿他的利润损失。因此，这不是适当的损害赔偿金。 （选项D） UCC 中没有规定要求这些货物通过公开拍卖转售。\n\n教育目的：当买方因拒绝履行合同或错误地拒绝接受货物而违约时，卖方有权获得损害赔偿，使其处于与已履行合同相同的地位。对于特殊制造的商品，这包括预期利润加上合理发生的成本减去转售商品的任何收益。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方因不接受/否认而造成的损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当买方因拒绝履行合同或错误地拒绝接受货物而违约时，卖方有权获得损害赔偿，使其处于与已履行合同相同的地位。对于特殊制造的商品，这包括预期利润加上合理发生的成本减去转售商品的任何收益。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院应判给卖方哪些损害赔偿？","rule":"当买方因拒绝履行合同或错误地拒绝接受货物而违约时，卖方有权获得损害赔偿，使其处于与已履行合同相同的地位。对于特殊制造的商品，这包括预期利润加上合理发生的成本减去转售商品的任何收益。","analysis":"根据UCC，当买方因拒绝履行合同或错误地拒绝接受货物而违约时，卖方可以要求赔偿损失。法院将使用一种损害赔偿措施，使卖方处于与合同已履行相同的地位，通常基于市场价格差异。但是，当这种损害赔偿措施不充分时——例如，合同是针对非标准尺寸的特制货物（如此处的滚珠轴承）——卖方的损害赔偿等于：卖方本应赚取的利润（例如，每套合同价格 10 美元 - 每套制造成本 8 美元 = 每套 2 美元）加上合理发生的成本补贴（例如，每套制造成本 8 美元）减去转售货物收到的付款（例如，每套残值价格 2 美元）。因此，法院应判给卖方每套 8 美元（2 美元 + 8 美元 - 2 美元），即卖方的利润损失加上其未收回的制造成本。 （选择 A）仅当卖方尚未开始制造滚珠轴承时，2 美元才是正确的损失衡量标准。在这种情况下，就没有制造成本或需要转售货物。 （选择B） 卖方制造滚珠轴承的成本与废品价格（即6 美元）之间的差额未能补偿他的利润损失。因此，这不是适当的损害赔偿金。 （选项D） UCC 中没有规定要求这些货物通过公开拍卖转售。\n\n教育目的：当买方因拒绝履行合同或错误地拒绝接受货物而违约时，卖方有权获得损害赔偿，使其处于与已履行合同相同的地位。对于特殊制造的商品，这包括预期利润加上合理发生的成本减去转售商品的任何收益。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-708 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方因不接受/否认而造成的损失）。","conclusion":"答案 C：每套 8 美元，即利润损失加上未收回的制造成本。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-eb9d9fa946f3f56e"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":223,"sourceQuestionPage":659,"sourceQuestionPages":[659],"sourceExplanationPages":[660,661],"sourceAnswerPages":[660,661],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-224","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A mother whose adult son was a law school graduate contracted with a tutor to give the son a bar exam preparation course. \"If my son passes the bar exam,\" the mother explained to the tutor, \"he has been promised a job with a law firm that will pay $55,000 a year.\" The tutor agreed to do the work for $5,000, although the going rate was $6,000. Before the instruction was to begin and before any payment was made, the tutor repudiated the contract. Although the mother or the son reasonably could have employed, for $6,000, an equally qualified instructor to replace the tutor, neither did so. The son failed the bar exam, and the law firm refused to employ him. It can be shown that had the son received the tutor's instruction, he would have passed the bar exam. If the mother and the son join as plaintiffs and sue the tutor for breach of contract, how much, if anything, are they entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"$1,000, because all other damages could have been avoided by employing another equally qualified instructor.","B":"$55,000, because damages of that amount were within the contemplation of the parties at the time they contracted.","C":"Nominal damages only, because the mother was not injured by the breach and the tutor made no promise to the son.","D":"Nothing, because neither the mother nor the son took steps to avoid the consequences of the tutor's breach."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Expectation (ie, benefit-of-the-bargain) damages\n\nDefinition Losses arising naturally & obviously from breach\n\nPurpose Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed\n\nMeasure General rule – difference between contract price & market price (or cost of purchasing substitute performance) Construction contracts – difference between contract price & cost of construction by another builder The primary goal of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been fully performed. This typically means that the nonbreaching party can recover: consequential damages – losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances that the breaching party could reasonably foresee when the contract was made and/or expectation damages – losses arising naturally and obviously from the breach, which can be measured by the difference between the contract price and the market price. However, damages that could have been mitigated (ie, avoided or minimized) through reasonable efforts are not recoverable.* Here, the tutor repudiated the contract prior to performing. Although it was foreseeable that the son would lose his job offer if he failed the bar exam, that loss could have been avoided by employing an equally qualified instructor for $6,000. As a result, the mother and son cannot claim $55,000 in consequential damages for the son's lost salary (Choice B). However, this does not prevent them from recovering the difference between the $5,000 contract price and the $6,000 market price—a total of $1,000 in expectation damages (Choice D). *The nonbreaching party will not be precluded from recovery to the extent that he/she has made reasonable but unsuccessful efforts to avoid loss. (Choice C) Although the tutor made no promise to the son, the mother-tutor contract was made to benefit the son (the one who would be receiving tutoring). This means that the son was an intended third-party beneficiary of the contract. As a result, both the mother and the son were entitled to sue for damages. In addition, they can recover $1,000 in expectation damages—not merely nominal damages.\n\nEducational objective: The nonbreaching party cannot recover damages for losses that could have been mitigated (ie, avoided or minimized) through reasonable efforts.","educationalObjective":"The nonbreaching party cannot recover damages for losses that could have been mitigated (ie, avoided or minimized) through reasonable efforts.","references":"","rule":"The nonbreaching party cannot recover damages for losses that could have been mitigated (ie, avoided or minimized) through reasonable efforts.","irac":{"issue":"If the mother and the son join as plaintiffs and sue the tutor for breach of contract, how much, if anything, are they entitled to recover?","rule":"The nonbreaching party cannot recover damages for losses that could have been mitigated (ie, avoided or minimized) through reasonable efforts.","analysis":"Expectation (ie, benefit-of-the-bargain) damages\n\nDefinition Losses arising naturally & obviously from breach\n\nPurpose Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed\n\nMeasure General rule – difference between contract price & market price (or cost of purchasing substitute performance) Construction contracts – difference between contract price & cost of construction by another builder The primary goal of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been fully performed. This typically means that the nonbreaching party can recover: consequential damages – losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances that the breaching party could reasonably foresee when the contract was made and/or expectation damages – losses arising naturally and obviously from the breach, which can be measured by the difference between the contract price and the market price. However, damages that could have been mitigated (ie, avoided or minimized) through reasonable efforts are not recoverable.* Here, the tutor repudiated the contract prior to performing. Although it was foreseeable that the son would lose his job offer if he failed the bar exam, that loss could have been avoided by employing an equally qualified instructor for $6,000. As a result, the mother and son cannot claim $55,000 in consequential damages for the son's lost salary (Choice B). However, this does not prevent them from recovering the difference between the $5,000 contract price and the $6,000 market price—a total of $1,000 in expectation damages (Choice D). *The nonbreaching party will not be precluded from recovery to the extent that he/she has made reasonable but unsuccessful efforts to avoid loss. (Choice C) Although the tutor made no promise to the son, the mother-tutor contract was made to benefit the son (the one who would be receiving tutoring). This means that the son was an intended third-party beneficiary of the contract. As a result, both the mother and the son were entitled to sue for damages. In addition, they can recover $1,000 in expectation damages—not merely nominal damages.","conclusion":"Answer A: $1,000, because all other damages could have been avoided by employing another equally qualified instructor."},"questionZh":"一位母亲的成年儿子是法学院毕业生，她与一名导师签约，为儿子提供律师资格考试准备课程。 “如果我的儿子通过了律师资格考试，”这位母亲向导师解释道，“他就被许诺在一家律师事务所找到一份工作，年薪为 55,000 美元。”导师同意以 5,000 美元的价格完成这项工作，尽管现行价格为 6,000 美元。在教学开始之前和付款之前，导师否认了合同。尽管母亲或儿子本可以花 6,000 美元聘请一位同等资格的教师来代替家庭教师，但他们都没有这样做。儿子未能通过律师考试，律师事务所拒绝雇用他。可见，如果儿子接受了导师的指导，他一定可以通过律师资格考试。如果母子俩作为原告起诉家庭教师违反合同，他们有权获得多少赔偿（如果有的话）？","choicesZh":{"A":"1,000 美元，因为通过雇用另一位同等资格的教练可以避免所有其他损害。","B":"55,000 美元，因为该金额的损害赔偿在双方签订合同时的预期范围之内。","C":"仅名义上的损害赔偿，因为母亲没有因违约行为而受伤，而且家庭教师也没有向儿子做出任何承诺。","D":"没有什么，因为母亲和儿子都没有采取措施避免导师违约的后果。"},"explanationZh":"预期（即讨价还价）损害\n\n定义 自然且明显因违约而产生的损失\n\n目的 将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的位置\n\n衡量标准 一般规则 – 合同价格与市场价格（或购买替代履约成本）之间的差额 施工合同 – 合同价格与其他建筑商的施工成本之间的差额 合同损害赔偿的主要目标是使非违约方处于与合同已完全履行相同的地位。这通常意味着守约方可以获得： 间接损失 – 守约方在订立合同时可以合理预见的特殊情况而造成的损失和/或预期损失 – 因违约而自然而明显地产生的损失，可以通过合同价格与市场价格之间的差额来衡量。然而，通过合理努力可以减轻（即避免或最小化）的损害是不可挽回的。*在此，导师在履行合同之前否认了合同。尽管可以预见，如果儿子未能通过律师资格考试，他将失去工作机会，但如果花 6,000 美元聘请一位同等资格的讲师，就可以避免这种损失。因此，母子俩不能就儿子的工资损失索赔 55,000 美元的间接损失赔偿（选择 B）。然而，这并不妨碍他们收回 5,000 美元的合同价格和 6,000 美元的市场价格之间的差额——总共 1,000 美元的预期损失（选项 D）。 *只要守约方为避免损失做出了合理但不成功的努力，则不排除其获得赔偿。 （选项C）虽然家教没有向儿子做出任何承诺，但母家教合同是为了儿子（接受家教的人）的利益而签订的。这意味着儿子是合同的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。因此，母子双方均有权提起诉讼，要求赔偿损失。此外，他们还可以获得 1,000 美元的预期损害赔偿，而不仅仅是名义损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：非违约方无法通过合理努力减轻（即避免或最小化）损失的损失。","educationalObjectiveZh":"对于通过合理努力可以减轻（即避免或最小化）的损失，非违约方无法获得损害赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果母子俩作为原告起诉家庭教师违反合同，他们有权获得多少赔偿（如果有的话）？","rule":"对于通过合理努力可以减轻（即避免或最小化）的损失，非违约方无法获得损害赔偿。","analysis":"预期（即讨价还价）损害\n\n定义 自然且明显因违约而产生的损失\n\n目的 将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的位置\n\n衡量标准 一般规则 – 合同价格与市场价格（或购买替代履约成本）之间的差额 施工合同 – 合同价格与其他建筑商的施工成本之间的差额 合同损害赔偿的主要目标是使非违约方处于与合同已完全履行相同的地位。这通常意味着守约方可以获得： 间接损失 – 守约方在订立合同时可以合理预见的特殊情况而造成的损失和/或预期损失 – 因违约而自然而明显地产生的损失，可以通过合同价格与市场价格之间的差额来衡量。然而，通过合理努力可以减轻（即避免或最小化）的损害是不可挽回的。*在此，导师在履行合同之前否认了合同。尽管可以预见，如果儿子未能通过律师资格考试，他将失去工作机会，但如果花 6,000 美元聘请一位同等资格的讲师，就可以避免这种损失。因此，母子俩不能就儿子的工资损失索赔 55,000 美元的间接损失赔偿（选择 B）。然而，这并不妨碍他们收回 5,000 美元的合同价格和 6,000 美元的市场价格之间的差额——总共 1,000 美元的预期损失（选项 D）。 *只要守约方为避免损失做出了合理但不成功的努力，则不排除其获得赔偿。 （选项C）虽然家教没有向儿子做出任何承诺，但母家教合同是为了儿子（接受家教的人）的利益而签订的。这意味着儿子是合同的预期第三方受益人（third-party beneficiary）（third-party beneficiary）。因此，母子双方均有权提起诉讼，要求赔偿损失。此外，他们还可以获得 1,000 美元的预期损害赔偿，而不仅仅是名义损害赔偿。\n\n教育目标：非违约方无法通过合理努力减轻（即避免或最小化）损失的损失。","conclusion":"答案 A：1,000 美元，因为通过雇用另一位同等资格的教练可以避免所有其他损害。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":224,"sourceQuestionPage":662,"sourceQuestionPages":[662],"sourceExplanationPages":[663,664],"sourceAnswerPages":[663,664],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-225","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On July 1, a man and a developer contracted in writing for the sale and purchase of the man's farm, and to close the transaction on December 1. On September 1, the developer unequivocally repudiated the contract. On September 15, the man urged the developer to change her mind and proceed with the scheduled closing on December 1. On October 1, having heard nothing further from the developer, the man sold and conveyed his farm to a farmer without notifying the developer. On December 1, the developer attempted to close under the July 1 contract by tendering the full purchase price to the man. The man rejected the tender. The developer sued the man for breach of contract. Will the developer likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because acceptance of the purchase price by the man was a concurrent condition to the man's obligation to convey the farm to the developer on December 1.","B":"No, because the developer did not retract her repudiation before the man materially changed his position in reliance thereon by selling the farm to the farmer.","C":"Yes, because the man failed seasonably to notify the developer of any pending sale to the farmer.","D":"Yes, because the man waived the developer's September 1 repudiation by urging her to retract it on September 15."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. A repudiation can be retracted if the nonrepudiating party receives notice of the retraction before: canceling the contract materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation or indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final. If the repudiation is not retracted, the nonrepudiating party may (1) treat the repudiation as a breach or (2) ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the developer unequivocally repudiated the contract for the sale of the farm on September 1. One month later, the man sold and conveyed the farm to the farmer because the man had heard nothing from the developer. Since the developer did not retract her repudiation before the man materially changed his position in reliance thereon, the developer has no recourse for breach of contract. Therefore, the developer likely will not prevail. (Choice A) The fact that the man did not accept the purchase price when the developer tendered it on December 1 is not the reason the developer will lose. Instead, the developer will lose because she repudiated the contract on September 1 and the man materially changed his position in reliance thereon by selling the farm to someone else. (Choice C) The nonrepudiating party need not notify the repudiating party that the nonrepudiating party materially changed his/her position in reliance on the repudiation. (Choice D) The fact that the man urged the developer to retract her repudiation did not waive the repudiation because only the developer can retract it.\n\nEducational objective: A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation final.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 256 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (retraction of repudiation).","educationalObjective":"A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation final.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 253 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of an anticipatory repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 256 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (retraction of repudiation).","rule":"A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation final.","irac":{"issue":"Will the developer likely prevail?","rule":"A party who anticipatorily repudiates a contract may retract the repudiation if the nonrepudiating party is notified of the retraction before (1) canceling the contract, (2) materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicating that he/she considers the repudiation final.","analysis":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. A repudiation can be retracted if the nonrepudiating party receives notice of the retraction before: canceling the contract materially changing position in reliance on the repudiation or indicating that he/she considers the repudiation to be final. If the repudiation is not retracted, the nonrepudiating party may (1) treat the repudiation as a breach or (2) ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the developer unequivocally repudiated the contract for the sale of the farm on September 1. One month later, the man sold and conveyed the farm to the farmer because the man had heard nothing from the developer. Since the developer did not retract her repudiation before the man materially changed his position in reliance thereon, the developer has no recourse for breach of contract. Therefore, the developer likely will not prevail. (Choice A) The fact that the man did not accept the purchase price when the developer tendered it on December 1 is not the reason the developer will lose. Instead, the developer will lose because she repudiated the contract on September 1 and the man materially changed his position in reliance thereon by selling the farm to someone else. (Choice C) The nonrepudiating party need not notify the repudiating party that the nonrepudiating party materially changed his/her position in reliance on the repudiation. (Choice D) The fact that the man urged the developer to retract her repudiation did not waive the repudiation because only the developer can retract it.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the developer did not retract her repudiation before the man materially changed his position in reliance thereon by selling the farm to the farmer."},"questionZh":"7月1日，一名男子与一名开发商就该男子农场的买卖签订了书面合同，并于12月1日完成交易。9月1日，开发商明确否认了该合同。 9月15日，该男子敦促开发商改变主意，继续按计划于12月1日交房。10月1日，在没有收到开发商的进一步消息后，该男子在未通知开发商的情况下，将自己的农场出售并转让给了一名农民。 12 月 1 日，开发商试图根据 7 月 1 日的合同完成交割，向该男子提供全额购买价格。该男子拒绝了投标。开发商起诉该男子违反合同。开发商可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该男子接受购买价格是该男子有义务在 12 月 1 日将农场转让给开发商的同时条件。","B":"不，因为在该男子通过将农场出售给农民而实质上改变了对开发商的依赖之前，开发商并没有撤回她的拒绝。","C":"是的，因为该男子未能及时通知开发商任何待售给农民的事宜。","D":"是的，因为该男子在 9 月 15 日敦促开发商撤回，从而放弃了开发商 9 月 1 日的拒绝。"},"explanationZh":"当缔约方在履行时间出现或过去之前明确且毫不含糊地拒绝履行（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤回通知，则可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：取消合同，实质性改变依赖否认的立场或表明他/她认为否认是最终的。如果否认未撤销，则非毁约方可以 (1) 将否认视为违约，或 (2) 忽略它并要求根据合同履行。在此，开发商于9月1日明确否认了农场出售合同。一个月后，该男子因没有收到开发商的任何消息，将农场出售并转让给了农户。由于开发商在该男子实质性改变其依赖立场之前并未撤回其拒绝履行的义务，因此开发商无权追究违约责任。因此，开发商很可能不会获胜。 （选项A） 12月1日开发商招标时，该男子不接受购买价的事实并不是开发商失败的原因。相反，开发商将会失败，因为她在 9 月 1 日拒绝了合同，而该男子通过将农场出售给其他人，实质性地改变了他对合同的依赖立场。 （选择C） 非毁约方无需通知否认方，非毁约方依赖于否认而实质性改变了他/她的立场。 （选项D） 男子敦促开发商撤回毁约的事实并不等于放弃毁约，因为只有开发商才能撤回毁约。\n\n教育目标：预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，如果非毁约方在 (1) 取消合同、(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场，或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的之前收到撤回通知。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 256（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（撤销否认）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤销通知，则预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：(1) 取消合同；(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场；或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的。","iracZh":{"issue":"开发商可能会获胜吗？","rule":"如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤销通知，则预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：(1) 取消合同；(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场；或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的。","analysis":"当缔约方在履行时间出现或过去之前明确且毫不含糊地拒绝履行（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。如果非毁约方在以下情况之前收到撤回通知，则可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行：取消合同，实质性改变依赖否认的立场或表明他/她认为否认是最终的。如果否认未撤销，则非毁约方可以 (1) 将否认视为违约，或 (2) 忽略它并要求根据合同履行。在此，开发商于9月1日明确否认了农场出售合同。一个月后，该男子因没有收到开发商的任何消息，将农场出售并转让给了农户。由于开发商在该男子实质性改变其依赖立场之前并未撤回其拒绝履行的义务，因此开发商无权追究违约责任。因此，开发商很可能不会获胜。 （选项A） 12月1日开发商招标时，该男子不接受购买价的事实并不是开发商失败的原因。相反，开发商将会失败，因为她在 9 月 1 日拒绝了合同，而该男子通过将农场出售给其他人，实质性地改变了他对合同的依赖立场。 （选择C） 非毁约方无需通知否认方，非毁约方依赖于否认而实质性改变了他/她的立场。 （选项D） 男子敦促开发商撤回毁约的事实并不等于放弃毁约，因为只有开发商才能撤回毁约。\n\n教育目标：预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）合同的一方可以撤回预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，如果非毁约方在 (1) 取消合同、(2) 实质性改变依赖否认的立场，或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的之前收到撤回通知。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 253（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）的效果）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 256（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（撤销否认）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为在该男子通过将农场出售给农民而实质上改变了对开发商的依赖之前，开发商并没有撤回她的拒绝。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6092dc1e72c5f076"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":225,"sourceQuestionPage":665,"sourceQuestionPages":[665],"sourceExplanationPages":[666,667],"sourceAnswerPages":[666,667],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-226","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contract Interpretation","question":"In a single writing, a painter contracted with a farmer to paint three identical barns on her rural estate for $2,000 each. The contract provided for the farmer's payment of $6,000 upon the painter's completion of the work on all three barns. The painter did not ask for any payment when the first barn was completely painted, but she demanded $4,000 after painting the second barn. Is the farmer obligated to make the $4,000 payment?","choices":{"A":"No, because the farmer has no duty under the contract to pay anything to the painter until all three barns have been painted.","B":"No, because the painter waived her right, if any, to payment on a per-barn basis by failing to demand $2,000 upon completion of the first barn.","C":"Yes, because the contract is divisible.","D":"Yes, because the painter has substantially performed the entire contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Order of performances*\n\nSimultaneous Condition concurrent implied (ie, performance due performance possible simultaneously)\n\nOne performance requires Condition precedent implied (ie, durational performance due time before respective duty triggered)\n\n*These rules apply unless express contractual language or circumstances indicate otherwise.\n\nWhen a party performs one part of a divisible contract, the party is entitled to the agreed equivalent for that part—even if the party fails to perform the other parts of the contract. In other words, that party's performance of the entire contract is not a condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. A contract is divisible if: the parties' duties can be broken down into at least two corresponding pairs of performances and those pairs of performances can fairly be regarded as agreed (ie, bargained-for) equivalents. Although courts prefer to interpret contracts as divisible for reasons having to do with fairness, they will not do so if the contract expressly states that it is indivisible. Here, the parties' contract provided that the farmer had no duty to pay the painter anything until she had painted all three barns. As a result, the contract was expressly indivisible, and the painter's completion of the work on all three barns was a condition precedent to the farmer's duty to pay. Therefore, the farmer is not obligated to pay the painter $4,000 (ie, $2,000 per barn for two barns) (Choice C). (Choice B) The painter did not have a right to expect payment for each barn, so she could not waive such a right. (Choice D) The painter did not substantially perform the contract since she failed to paint one of the three barns—a significant part of the performance for which the farmer contracted.\n\nEducational objective: Although courts prefer to interpret contracts as divisible for fairness reasons, they will not do so in contradiction of the express terms of the contract.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 234 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (order of performances).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 240 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (part performances as agreed equivalents).","educationalObjective":"Although courts prefer to interpret contracts as divisible for fairness reasons, they will not do so in contradiction of the express terms of the contract.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 234 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (order of performances).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 240 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (part performances as agreed equivalents).","rule":"Although courts prefer to interpret contracts as divisible for fairness reasons, they will not do so in contradiction of the express terms of the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Is the farmer obligated to make the $4,000 payment?","rule":"Although courts prefer to interpret contracts as divisible for fairness reasons, they will not do so in contradiction of the express terms of the contract.","analysis":"Order of performances*\n\nSimultaneous Condition concurrent implied (ie, performance due performance possible simultaneously)\n\nOne performance requires Condition precedent implied (ie, durational performance due time before respective duty triggered)\n\n*These rules apply unless express contractual language or circumstances indicate otherwise.\n\nWhen a party performs one part of a divisible contract, the party is entitled to the agreed equivalent for that part—even if the party fails to perform the other parts of the contract. In other words, that party's performance of the entire contract is not a condition precedent to the other party's duty to perform. A contract is divisible if: the parties' duties can be broken down into at least two corresponding pairs of performances and those pairs of performances can fairly be regarded as agreed (ie, bargained-for) equivalents. Although courts prefer to interpret contracts as divisible for reasons having to do with fairness, they will not do so if the contract expressly states that it is indivisible. Here, the parties' contract provided that the farmer had no duty to pay the painter anything until she had painted all three barns. As a result, the contract was expressly indivisible, and the painter's completion of the work on all three barns was a condition precedent to the farmer's duty to pay. Therefore, the farmer is not obligated to pay the painter $4,000 (ie, $2,000 per barn for two barns) (Choice C). (Choice B) The painter did not have a right to expect payment for each barn, so she could not waive such a right. (Choice D) The painter did not substantially perform the contract since she failed to paint one of the three barns—a significant part of the performance for which the farmer contracted.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the farmer has no duty under the contract to pay anything to the painter until all three barns have been painted."},"questionZh":"在一篇文章中，一位画家与一位农民签订了合同，在她的乡村庄园上绘制三个相同的谷仓，每个谷仓 2,000 美元。合同规定，油漆工完成所有三个谷仓的工作后，农民将支付 6,000 美元。当第一个谷仓全部粉刷完毕时，油漆工没有要求任何付款，但在粉刷完第二个谷仓后，她索要4000美元。农民有义务支付 4,000 美元吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为根据合同，在三个谷仓全部粉刷完毕之前，农民没有义务向油漆工支付任何费用。","B":"不，因为画家在第一个谷仓竣工后未能索要 2,000 美元，因此放弃了按每个谷仓付款的权利（如果有的话）。","C":"是的，因为合同是可分割的。","D":"是的，因为油漆工基本上履行了整个合同。"},"explanationZh":"演出顺序*\n\n同时条件：双方履行可同时发生时的默示条件\n\n一项履行需要隐含的先决条件（即触发相应职责之前的持续履行到期时间）\n\n*除非明确的合同语言或情况另有说明，否则这些规则适用。\n\n当一方履行可分合同的一部分时，该方有权获得该部分约定的等价物——即使该方未能履行合同的其他部分。换句话说，该方履行整个合同并不是另一方履行义务的先决条件。如果满足以下条件，则合同是可分割的： 双方的义务可以分解为至少两个相应的履行，并且这些履行可以公平地视为约定（即讨价还价）的等价物。尽管出于与公平有关的原因，法院更愿意将合同解释为可分割的，但如果合同明确规定其不可分割，则法院不会这样做。在这里，双方的合同规定，在油漆工完成所有三个谷仓之前，农民没有义务向油漆工支付任何费用。因此，合同显然是不可分割的，油漆工完成所有三个谷仓的工作是农民付款义务的先决条件。因此，农民没有义务向油漆工支付 4,000 美元（即两个谷仓每个谷仓 2,000 美元）（选项 C）。 （选项B） 油漆工没有权利要求每个谷仓付款，因此她不能放弃这样的权利。 （选项 D） 油漆工并未实质履行合同，因为她未能粉刷三个谷仓之一——这是农夫合同履行的一个重要部分。\n\n教育目标：尽管法院出于公平原因更倾向于将合同解释为可分割的，但他们不会这样做与合同的明确条款相矛盾。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 234（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行顺序）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 240（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（部分履行为商定的同等内容）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"尽管出于公平原因，法院更愿意将合同解释为可分割的，但他们不会这样做与合同的明确条款相矛盾。","iracZh":{"issue":"农民有义务支付 4,000 美元吗？","rule":"尽管出于公平原因，法院更愿意将合同解释为可分割的，但他们不会这样做与合同的明确条款相矛盾。","analysis":"演出顺序*\n\n同时条件：双方履行可同时发生时的默示条件\n\n一项履行需要隐含的先决条件（即触发相应职责之前的持续履行到期时间）\n\n*除非明确的合同语言或情况另有说明，否则这些规则适用。\n\n当一方履行可分合同的一部分时，该方有权获得该部分约定的等价物——即使该方未能履行合同的其他部分。换句话说，该方履行整个合同并不是另一方履行义务的先决条件。如果满足以下条件，则合同是可分割的： 双方的义务可以分解为至少两个相应的履行，并且这些履行可以公平地视为约定（即讨价还价）的等价物。尽管出于与公平有关的原因，法院更愿意将合同解释为可分割的，但如果合同明确规定其不可分割，则法院不会这样做。在这里，双方的合同规定，在油漆工完成所有三个谷仓之前，农民没有义务向油漆工支付任何费用。因此，合同显然是不可分割的，油漆工完成所有三个谷仓的工作是农民付款义务的先决条件。因此，农民没有义务向油漆工支付 4,000 美元（即两个谷仓每个谷仓 2,000 美元）（选项 C）。 （选项B） 油漆工没有权利要求每个谷仓付款，因此她不能放弃这样的权利。 （选项 D） 油漆工并未实质履行合同，因为她未能粉刷三个谷仓之一——这是农夫合同履行的一个重要部分。\n\n教育目标：尽管法院出于公平原因更倾向于将合同解释为可分割的，但他们不会这样做与合同的明确条款相矛盾。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 234（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（履行顺序）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 240（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（部分履行为商定的同等内容）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不可以，因为根据合同，在三个谷仓全部粉刷完毕之前，农民没有义务向油漆工支付任何费用。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":226,"sourceQuestionPage":668,"sourceQuestionPages":[668],"sourceExplanationPages":[669,670],"sourceAnswerPages":[669,670],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-227","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A well-established paper mill and a logging company signed a written contract in which the mill agreed to buy from the company all the logs the mill would need for one year. The company was unable to keep up with the mill's needs, and its log deliveries fell short by 10% to 15% in each of the four quarters of the year. The mill paid the company on time for all delivered logs. The mill paid an attorney $2,000 for advice concerning its options in enforcing the contract. It paid a broker a reasonable fee of $5,000 to find additional logs to make up for the company's shortfall. The mill also incurred reasonable costs of $25,000 to transport the additional logs to its facility. Despite the mill's efforts to mitigate damages, it sustained an additional $200,000 in losses because of the company's failure to timely deliver enough logs. The mill has sued the company for breach of contract. If the court finds for the mill, how much should it award in damages?","choices":{"A":"$205,000.","B":"$225,000.","C":"$230,000.","D":"$232,000."},"answer":"C","explanation":"UCC damages for seller's breach when buyer covers\n\nCost of cover Difference between contract price & cost of purchasing substitute goods* Incidental damages Reasonable expenses incurred in inspection, receipt, transportation & care of goods rightfully rejected Commercially reasonable charges, expenses, or commissions incurred in connection with effecting cover Other reasonable expenses incident to delay/breach Consequential Losses resulting from buyer's particular circumstances that: damages seller had reason to know at time of contracting and could not reasonably be prevented Injury to person/property proximately resulting from breach of warranty UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*The substitute goods must have been purchased promptly & in good faith.\n\nUnder the UCC, the buyer can recover damages if the seller breaches the contract by failing to deliver some or all of the goods. If the buyer chooses to cover (ie, purchase substitute goods), then the buyer can recover the following: Cost of cover – the difference between the contract price and the cost of purchasing substitute goods Incidental damages – includes commercially reasonable charges, expenses, or commissions incurred in effecting cover (eg, transportation costs, broker commission) Consequential damages – includes losses resulting from the buyer's particular circumstances that (1) the seller had reason to know of at the time of contracting and (2) could not reasonably be prevented by cover or otherwise (eg, business losses) No UCC provision provides for recovery of attorney's fees, however, so they are not recoverable in the absence of a contract provision allowing them. In addition, although a party has no duty to mitigate its losses, its recovery will be reduced by the amount that could have been reasonably avoided or minimized. Here, the paper mill will necessarily recover the $200,000 in losses it sustained because of the logging company's failure to deliver enough logs. These are presumably consequential damages. The mill can also recover incidental damages of $25,000 for transporting the additional logs and $5,000 for the broker's commission—$30,000 in total. However, the\n\n$2,000 in attorney's fees are not recoverable (Choice D). Therefore, the court should award $230,000 in damages if it finds for the mill (Choices A & B).\n\nEducational objective: When a seller breaches a contract by failing to deliver some or all of the goods, the buyer's recoverable damages include the cost of cover, incidental damages, and consequential damages—but not attorney's fees. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's remedies in general).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-712 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's procurement of substitute goods).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-715 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's incidental & consequential damages).","educationalObjective":"When a seller breaches a contract by failing to deliver some or all of the goods, the buyer's recoverable damages include the cost of cover, incidental damages, and consequential damages—but not attorney's fees. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's remedies in general).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-712 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's procurement of substitute goods).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-715 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's incidental & consequential damages).","rule":"When a seller breaches a contract by failing to deliver some or all of the goods, the buyer's recoverable damages include the cost of cover, incidental damages, and consequential damages—but not attorney's fees. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"If the court finds for the mill, how much should it award in damages?","rule":"When a seller breaches a contract by failing to deliver some or all of the goods, the buyer's recoverable damages include the cost of cover, incidental damages, and consequential damages—but not attorney's fees. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"UCC damages for seller's breach when buyer covers\n\nCost of cover Difference between contract price & cost of purchasing substitute goods* Incidental damages Reasonable expenses incurred in inspection, receipt, transportation & care of goods rightfully rejected Commercially reasonable charges, expenses, or commissions incurred in connection with effecting cover Other reasonable expenses incident to delay/breach Consequential Losses resulting from buyer's particular circumstances that: damages seller had reason to know at time of contracting and could not reasonably be prevented Injury to person/property proximately resulting from breach of warranty UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\n*The substitute goods must have been purchased promptly & in good faith.\n\nUnder the UCC, the buyer can recover damages if the seller breaches the contract by failing to deliver some or all of the goods. If the buyer chooses to cover (ie, purchase substitute goods), then the buyer can recover the following: Cost of cover – the difference between the contract price and the cost of purchasing substitute goods Incidental damages – includes commercially reasonable charges, expenses, or commissions incurred in effecting cover (eg, transportation costs, broker commission) Consequential damages – includes losses resulting from the buyer's particular circumstances that (1) the seller had reason to know of at the time of contracting and (2) could not reasonably be prevented by cover or otherwise (eg, business losses) No UCC provision provides for recovery of attorney's fees, however, so they are not recoverable in the absence of a contract provision allowing them. In addition, although a party has no duty to mitigate its losses, its recovery will be reduced by the amount that could have been reasonably avoided or minimized. Here, the paper mill will necessarily recover the $200,000 in losses it sustained because of the logging company's failure to deliver enough logs. These are presumably consequential damages. The mill can also recover incidental damages of $25,000 for transporting the additional logs and $5,000 for the broker's commission—$30,000 in total. However, the\n\n$2,000 in attorney's fees are not recoverable (Choice D). Therefore, the court should award $230,000 in damages if it finds for the mill (Choices A & B).","conclusion":"Answer C: $230,000."},"questionZh":"一家老牌造纸厂和一家伐木公司签署了一份书面合同，该厂同意从该公司购买该厂一年所需的所有原木。该公司无法满足工厂的需求，其原木交付量在今年四个季度中每个季度都下降了 10% 至 15%。工厂按时向公司支付了所有交付原木的费用。该工厂向律师支付了 2,000 美元，请其就执行合同的选择提供建议。它向经纪人支付了 5,000 美元的合理费用，以寻找更多的日志来弥补公司的短缺。该工厂还支付了 25,000 美元的合理费用来将额外的原木运输到其工厂。尽管该工厂努力减轻损失，但由于公司未能及时交付足够的原木，该工厂又蒙受了 20 万美元的损失。该工厂已起诉该公司违反合同。如果法院裁定工厂胜诉，应判给工厂多少赔偿金？","choicesZh":{"A":"205,000 美元。","B":"225,000 美元。","C":"230,000 美元。","D":"232,000 美元。"},"explanationZh":"UCC 因买方承保时卖方违约而遭受损害\n\n保险费用 合同价格与购买替代商品的费用之间的差额* 附带损坏 在检查、接收、运输和照管被理所拒绝的货物时发生的合理费用 与实施保险相关的商业上合理的费用、费用或佣金 与延误/违约有关的其他合理费用 因买方特殊情况而造成的间接损失： 卖方在签订合同时有理由知道且无法合理避免的损失 因违反保证而直接造成的人身/财产伤害 UCC = 统一商业代码。\n\n*替代商品必须是及时且真诚地购买的。\n\n根据 UCC，如果卖方因未能交付部分或全部货物而违反合同，买方可以要求赔偿损失。如果买方选择承保（即购买替代货物），那么买方可以获得以下赔偿： 承保成本 – 合同价格与购买替代货物的成本之间的差额 附带损害 – 包括商业上合理的费用、支出或在履行承保过程中产生的佣金（例如运输费用、经纪人佣金） 间接损害 – 包括因买方的特殊情况而造成的损失，这些情况 (1) 卖方在订立合同时有理由知道，并且 (2) 无法通过承保或其他方式合理阻止（例如，商业损失） 然而，UCC 条款中没有规定律师费的追回，因此，如果没有合同条款允许，律师费就无法追回。此外，尽管一方没有义务减轻其损失，但其追偿金额将减少原本可以合理避免或最小化的金额。在这里，造纸厂必然会挽回因伐木公司未能交付足够原木而遭受的 20 万美元损失。这些大概是间接损害。该工厂还可获得 25,000 美元的额外原木运输附带损失赔偿以及 5,000 美元的经纪人佣金，总计 30,000 美元。然而，\n\n2,000 美元的律师费不可收回（选择 D）。因此，如果法院认定工厂有 230,000 美元的损害赔偿（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n教育目的：当卖方因未能交付部分或全部货物而违反合同时，买方的可赔偿损失包括保险费用、附带损失和间接损失，但不包括律师费。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的一般补救措施）。\n\nUCC § 2-712 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方采购替代商品）。\n\nUCC § 2-715 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的偶然和间接损害）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当卖方因未能交付部分或全部货物而违反合同时，买方的可赔偿损失包括保险费用、附带损失和间接损失，但不包括律师费。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"如果法院裁定工厂胜诉，应判给工厂多少赔偿金？","rule":"当卖方因未能交付部分或全部货物而违反合同时，买方的可赔偿损失包括保险费用、附带损失和间接损失，但不包括律师费。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"UCC 因买方承保时卖方违约而遭受损害\n\n保险费用 合同价格与购买替代商品的费用之间的差额* 附带损坏 在检查、接收、运输和照管被理所拒绝的货物时发生的合理费用 与实施保险相关的商业上合理的费用、费用或佣金 与延误/违约有关的其他合理费用 因买方特殊情况而造成的间接损失： 卖方在签订合同时有理由知道且无法合理避免的损失 因违反保证而直接造成的人身/财产伤害 UCC = 统一商业代码。\n\n*替代商品必须是及时且真诚地购买的。\n\n根据 UCC，如果卖方因未能交付部分或全部货物而违反合同，买方可以要求赔偿损失。如果买方选择承保（即购买替代货物），那么买方可以获得以下赔偿： 承保成本 – 合同价格与购买替代货物的成本之间的差额 附带损害 – 包括商业上合理的费用、支出或在履行承保过程中产生的佣金（例如运输费用、经纪人佣金） 间接损害 – 包括因买方的特殊情况而造成的损失，这些情况 (1) 卖方在订立合同时有理由知道，并且 (2) 无法通过承保或其他方式合理阻止（例如，商业损失） 然而，UCC 条款中没有规定律师费的追回，因此，如果没有合同条款允许，律师费就无法追回。此外，尽管一方没有义务减轻其损失，但其追偿金额将减少原本可以合理避免或最小化的金额。在这里，造纸厂必然会挽回因伐木公司未能交付足够原木而遭受的 20 万美元损失。这些大概是间接损害。该工厂还可获得 25,000 美元的额外原木运输附带损失赔偿以及 5,000 美元的经纪人佣金，总计 30,000 美元。然而，\n\n2,000 美元的律师费不可收回（选择 D）。因此，如果法院认定工厂有 230,000 美元的损害赔偿（选项 A 和 B）。\n\n教育目的：当卖方因未能交付部分或全部货物而违反合同时，买方的可赔偿损失包括保险费用、附带损失和间接损失，但不包括律师费。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的一般补救措施）。\n\nUCC § 2-712 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方采购替代商品）。\n\nUCC § 2-715 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的偶然和间接损害）。","conclusion":"答案 C：230,000 美元。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":227,"sourceQuestionPage":671,"sourceQuestionPages":[671],"sourceExplanationPages":[672,673],"sourceAnswerPages":[672,673],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-228","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A seller and a buyer entered into a contract obligating the seller to convey title to a parcel of land to the buyer in exchange for $100,000. The agreement provided that the buyer's obligation to purchase the parcel was expressly conditioned upon the buyer's obtaining a loan at an interest rate no higher than 10%. The buyer was unable to do so but did obtain a loan at an interest rate of 10.5% and timely tendered the purchase price. Because the value of the land had increased since the time of contracting, the seller refused to perform. The buyer sued the seller. Is the buyer likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because an express condition will be excused only to avoid forfeiture.","B":"No, because the contract called for a loan at an interest rate not to exceed 10% and it could not be modified without the consent of the seller.","C":"Yes, because the buyer detrimentally changed position in reliance on the seller's promise to convey.","D":"Yes, because the buyer's obtaining a loan at an interest rate no higher than 10% was not a condition to the seller's duty to perform."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nPerformance is generally due once a contract is formed. However, a party may avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both parties' performance becomes due—has not occurred, and the nonoccurrence is not excused. Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused by waiver if the party who benefits from the condition chooses to perform despite the nonoccurrence. Here, the buyer's duty to perform under the contract by purchasing the land was expressly conditioned on obtaining a loan at an interest rate no higher than 10% (condition precedent). The buyer was unable to do so (nonoccurrence) but tendered the purchase price anyway (waiver). Since the buyer waived the condition, the seller was also required to perform by conveying title to the buyer. Therefore, the buyer will likely prevail. (Choice A) It is untrue that an express condition (ie, one that appears in the contract) will be excused only to avoid disproportionate forfeiture. There are other excuses, such as waiver. (Choice B) The buyer's waiver of the condition was not a contract modification requiring the seller's consent since it did not alter the material terms of the contract (eg, purchase price). (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. But here, a valid contract was formed, so the buyer's reliance is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused by waiver if the party that benefits from the condition chooses to perform despite the nonoccurrence.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 224 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of condition).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of non-occurrence of condition).","educationalObjective":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused by waiver if the party that benefits from the condition chooses to perform despite the nonoccurrence.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 224 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of condition).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of non-occurrence of condition).","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused by waiver if the party that benefits from the condition chooses to perform despite the nonoccurrence.","irac":{"issue":"Is the buyer likely to prevail?","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused by waiver if the party that benefits from the condition chooses to perform despite the nonoccurrence.","analysis":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nPerformance is generally due once a contract is formed. However, a party may avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both parties' performance becomes due—has not occurred, and the nonoccurrence is not excused. Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused by waiver if the party who benefits from the condition chooses to perform despite the nonoccurrence. Here, the buyer's duty to perform under the contract by purchasing the land was expressly conditioned on obtaining a loan at an interest rate no higher than 10% (condition precedent). The buyer was unable to do so (nonoccurrence) but tendered the purchase price anyway (waiver). Since the buyer waived the condition, the seller was also required to perform by conveying title to the buyer. Therefore, the buyer will likely prevail. (Choice A) It is untrue that an express condition (ie, one that appears in the contract) will be excused only to avoid disproportionate forfeiture. There are other excuses, such as waiver. (Choice B) The buyer's waiver of the condition was not a contract modification requiring the seller's consent since it did not alter the material terms of the contract (eg, purchase price). (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. But here, a valid contract was formed, so the buyer's reliance is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the buyer's obtaining a loan at an interest rate no higher than 10% was not a condition to the seller's duty to perform."},"questionZh":"卖方和买方签订了一份合同，要求卖方将一块土地的所有权转让给买方，以换取 100,000 美元。该协议规定，买方购买该地块的义务明确以买方获得利率不高于10%的贷款为条件。买方无法这样做，但最终获得了利率为 10.5% 的贷款，并及时支付了购买价格。由于自签订合同以来土地价值已经增加，卖方拒绝履行。买方起诉卖方。买方有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为明确的条件只是为了避免没收而被原谅。","B":"不能，因为合同规定贷款利率不超过10%，而且未经卖方同意不得修改。","C":"是的，因为买方依赖卖方的承诺而不利地改变了立场。","D":"是的，因为买方以不高于10%的利率获得贷款并不是卖方履行义务的条件。"},"explanationZh":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n合同一旦签订，通常就应履行。然而，如果先决条件（即，在一方或双方履约到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，并且不发生的情况不能作为借口，一方可以避免履约。如果从条件中受益的一方尽管不发生但仍选择履行某项条件，则可以通过放弃来免除该条件的不发生。在此，买方通过购买土地履行合同义务的明确条件是获得利率不高于 10% 的贷款（先决条件）。买方无法这样做（未发生），但仍提交了购买价格（放弃）。由于买方放弃了该条件，卖方还必须履行将所有权转让给买方的义务。因此，买方很可能会占上风。 （选项A） 认为明示条件（即合同中出现的条件）只是为了避免不成比例的没收而被免除的说法是不真实的。还有其他借口，例如放弃。 （选项B） 买方放弃条件并不属于需要卖方同意的合同修改，因为它没有改变合同的实质性条款（例如购买价格）。 （选项C） 根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。但在这里，有效的合同已经形成，因此买方的信赖是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：如果受益于某种状况的一方尽管没有发生但仍选择履行，则可以通过放弃来免除该状况的未发生。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 224（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件的定义）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（未发生条件的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果从条件中受益的一方尽管不发生但仍选择履行某项条件，则可以通过放弃来免除该条件的不发生。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方有可能获胜吗？","rule":"如果从条件中受益的一方尽管不发生但仍选择履行某项条件，则可以通过放弃来免除该条件的不发生。","analysis":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n合同一旦签订，通常就应履行。然而，如果先决条件（即，在一方或双方履约到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，并且不发生的情况不能作为借口，一方可以避免履约。如果从条件中受益的一方尽管不发生但仍选择履行某项条件，则可以通过放弃来免除该条件的不发生。在此，买方通过购买土地履行合同义务的明确条件是获得利率不高于 10% 的贷款（先决条件）。买方无法这样做（未发生），但仍提交了购买价格（放弃）。由于买方放弃了该条件，卖方还必须履行将所有权转让给买方的义务。因此，买方很可能会占上风。 （选项A） 认为明示条件（即合同中出现的条件）只是为了避免不成比例的没收而被免除的说法是不真实的。还有其他借口，例如放弃。 （选项B） 买方放弃条件并不属于需要卖方同意的合同修改，因为它没有改变合同的实质性条款（例如购买价格）。 （选项C） 根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。但在这里，有效的合同已经形成，因此买方的信赖是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：如果受益于某种状况的一方尽管没有发生但仍选择履行，则可以通过放弃来免除该状况的未发生。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 224（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件的定义）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（未发生条件的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为买方以不高于10%的利率获得贷款并不是卖方履行义务的条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":228,"sourceQuestionPage":674,"sourceQuestionPages":[674],"sourceExplanationPages":[675,676],"sourceAnswerPages":[675,676],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-229","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Breach, Cure & Installments","question":"In a signed writing, a man contracted to purchase a 25-foot travel trailer from a dealership for $15,000, cash on delivery, no later than June 1. The man arrived at the dealership's sales lot on Sunday, May 31, but he refused to pay for and take delivery of the trailer as contracted when he discovered that the spare tire was missing. The dealership offered to install a spare tire on Monday when its service department would open, but the man replied that he did not want the trailer and would purchase another one elsewhere. Which of the following is accurate?","choices":{"A":"The man had a right to reject the trailer and terminate the contract under the perfect- tender rule.","B":"The man had a right to reject the trailer, but the dealership was entitled to a reasonable opportunity to cure the defect.","C":"The man was required to accept the trailer, because the defect could be readily cured.","D":"The man was required to accept the trailer, because the defect did not substantially impair its value."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Seller's right to cure\n\n(UCC § 2-508)\n\nWithin time for Seller has right to cure by: performance giving reasonable notice to buyer of intention to cure AND\n\nmaking conforming delivery within time for performance After time for If seller had reasonable grounds to believe goods were acceptable performance at delivery, seller has right to cure by:\n\ngiving reasonable notice to buyer of intention to cure AND\n\nmaking conforming delivery within reasonable time UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC perfect-tender rule, a buyer may reject goods in a single-delivery contract—ie, a one-time bargained-for exchange—that fail to conform to the contract in any way. But if the time for performance has not yet expired, a seller may cure (ie, remedy) a defect by: giving reasonable notice to the buyer of its intention to cure and making a conforming delivery within the time for performance under the contract. Here, the man contracted to purchase a 25-foot travel trailer from a dealership (single- delivery contract). The trailer was missing a spare tire (nonconforming good) when the man attempted to take delivery on May 31, so he had a right to reject the trailer. But since delivery was to occur no later than June 1, the dealership had a right to cure the defect by notifying the man of its intention to cure and then delivering a conforming trailer by June 1 (Choices A & C). (Choice D) To reject nonconforming goods under an installment contract, the nonconformity must (1) substantially impair the value of the goods and (2) be incurable. But under a single-delivery contract, a buyer may reject goods that fail to conform to the contract in any way.\n\nEducational objective: In a single-delivery contract, the buyer may reject nonconforming goods. But if there is still time to perform under the contract, the seller has the right to cure the defect by (1) giving\n\nthe buyer reasonable notice of its intention to cure and (2) making a conforming delivery within the time for performance under the contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-508 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's right to cure).","educationalObjective":"In a single-delivery contract, the buyer may reject nonconforming goods. But if there is still time to perform under the contract, the seller has the right to cure the defect by (1) giving\n\nthe buyer reasonable notice of its intention to cure and (2) making a conforming delivery within the time for performance under the contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-508 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (seller's right to cure).","rule":"In a single-delivery contract, the buyer may reject nonconforming goods. But if there is still time to perform under the contract, the seller has the right to cure the defect by (1) giving\n\nthe buyer reasonable notice of its intention to cure and (2) making a conforming delivery within the time for performance under the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is accurate?","rule":"In a single-delivery contract, the buyer may reject nonconforming goods. But if there is still time to perform under the contract, the seller has the right to cure the defect by (1) giving\n\nthe buyer reasonable notice of its intention to cure and (2) making a conforming delivery within the time for performance under the contract.","analysis":"Seller's right to cure\n\n(UCC § 2-508)\n\nWithin time for Seller has right to cure by: performance giving reasonable notice to buyer of intention to cure AND\n\nmaking conforming delivery within time for performance After time for If seller had reasonable grounds to believe goods were acceptable performance at delivery, seller has right to cure by:\n\ngiving reasonable notice to buyer of intention to cure AND\n\nmaking conforming delivery within reasonable time UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC perfect-tender rule, a buyer may reject goods in a single-delivery contract—ie, a one-time bargained-for exchange—that fail to conform to the contract in any way. But if the time for performance has not yet expired, a seller may cure (ie, remedy) a defect by: giving reasonable notice to the buyer of its intention to cure and making a conforming delivery within the time for performance under the contract. Here, the man contracted to purchase a 25-foot travel trailer from a dealership (single- delivery contract). The trailer was missing a spare tire (nonconforming good) when the man attempted to take delivery on May 31, so he had a right to reject the trailer. But since delivery was to occur no later than June 1, the dealership had a right to cure the defect by notifying the man of its intention to cure and then delivering a conforming trailer by June 1 (Choices A & C). (Choice D) To reject nonconforming goods under an installment contract, the nonconformity must (1) substantially impair the value of the goods and (2) be incurable. But under a single-delivery contract, a buyer may reject goods that fail to conform to the contract in any way.","conclusion":"Answer B: The man had a right to reject the trailer, but the dealership was entitled to a reasonable opportunity to cure the defect."},"questionZh":"在一份签署的书面文件中，一名男子签订合同，在 6 月 1 日之前以 15,000 美元的价格从经销商处购买一辆 25 英尺旅行拖车，货到付款。该男子于 5 月 31 日星期日到达经销商的销售场地，但当他发现备胎丢失时，他拒绝按照合同付款和接收拖车。经销商提出在周一其服务部门开门时安装备用轮胎，但该男子回答说他不想要这辆拖车，将在其他地方再购买一个。以下哪项是准确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"根据完全投标规则，该男子有权拒绝拖车并终止合同。","B":"该男子有权拒绝拖车，但经销商有权获得合理的机会来修复缺陷。","C":"该男子被要求接受拖车，因为缺陷很容易修复。","D":"该男子被要求接受拖车，因为缺陷并未严重损害其价值。"},"explanationZh":"卖方的补救权\n\n（UCC § 2-508）\n\n在规定的时间内，卖方有权通过以下方式进行修复： 向买方发出合理的通知，告知其修复意图；以及\n\n在履约时间内进行符合要求的交付 如果卖方有合理的理由相信货物在交付时的性能是可接受的，则卖方有权通过以下方式进行补救：\n\n向买方发出合理的通知以表示其想要治愈并且\n\n在合理时间内进行符合要求的交付 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC 完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），买方可以拒绝单一交货合同（即一次性讨价还价交换）中任何不符合合同规定的货物。但如果履行期限尚未届满，卖方可以通过以下方式纠正（即补救）缺陷：向买方发出合理通知，表明其纠正的意图，并在合同规定的履行期限内进行符合要求的交货。在此，该男子签订了从经销商处购买一辆 25 英尺旅行拖车的合同（单一交付合同）。 5月31日，该男子试图提货时，拖车缺少备胎（不合格品），因此他有权拒绝拖车。但由于交货时间不迟于 6 月 1 日，经销商有权通过通知该男子其修复意图来修复缺陷，然后在 6 月 1 日之前交付合格的拖车（选择 A 和 C）。 （选择 D）要根据分期付款合同拒绝不合格货物，不合格货物必须 (1) 严重损害货物的价值，并且 (2) 无法治愈。但在单一交货合同下，买方可以以任何方式拒绝不符合合同规定的货物。\n\n教育目标：在单一交货合同中，买方可以拒绝不合格货物。但如果仍有时间履行合同，卖方有权通过以下方式纠正缺陷：(1)\n\n买方合理通知其修复意图，并且 (2) 在合同规定的履行时间内进行合格的交付。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-508 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方的补救权）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在单一交货合同中，买方可以拒绝不合格的货物。但如果仍有时间履行合同，卖方有权通过以下方式纠正缺陷：(1)\n\n买方合理通知其修复意图，并且 (2) 在合同规定的履行时间内进行合格的交付。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项是准确的？","rule":"在单一交货合同中，买方可以拒绝不合格的货物。但如果仍有时间履行合同，卖方有权通过以下方式纠正缺陷：(1)\n\n买方合理通知其修复意图，并且 (2) 在合同规定的履行时间内进行合格的交付。","analysis":"卖方的补救权\n\n（UCC § 2-508）\n\n在规定的时间内，卖方有权通过以下方式进行修复： 向买方发出合理的通知，告知其修复意图；以及\n\n在履约时间内进行符合要求的交付 如果卖方有合理的理由相信货物在交付时的性能是可接受的，则卖方有权通过以下方式进行补救：\n\n向买方发出合理的通知以表示其想要治愈并且\n\n在合理时间内进行符合要求的交付 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC 完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），买方可以拒绝单一交货合同（即一次性讨价还价交换）中任何不符合合同规定的货物。但如果履行期限尚未届满，卖方可以通过以下方式纠正（即补救）缺陷：向买方发出合理通知，表明其纠正的意图，并在合同规定的履行期限内进行符合要求的交货。在此，该男子签订了从经销商处购买一辆 25 英尺旅行拖车的合同（单一交付合同）。 5月31日，该男子试图提货时，拖车缺少备胎（不合格品），因此他有权拒绝拖车。但由于交货时间不迟于 6 月 1 日，经销商有权通过通知该男子其修复意图来修复缺陷，然后在 6 月 1 日之前交付合格的拖车（选择 A 和 C）。 （选择 D）要根据分期付款合同拒绝不合格货物，不合格货物必须 (1) 严重损害货物的价值，并且 (2) 无法治愈。但在单一交货合同下，买方可以以任何方式拒绝不符合合同规定的货物。\n\n教育目标：在单一交货合同中，买方可以拒绝不合格货物。但如果仍有时间履行合同，卖方有权通过以下方式纠正缺陷：(1)\n\n买方合理通知其修复意图，并且 (2) 在合同规定的履行时间内进行合格的交付。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-508 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（卖方的补救权）。","conclusion":"答案 B：该男子有权拒绝拖车，但经销商有权获得合理的机会来修复缺陷。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":229,"sourceQuestionPage":677,"sourceQuestionPages":[677],"sourceExplanationPages":[678,679],"sourceAnswerPages":[678,679],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-230","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A man obtained a bid of $10,000 to tear down an old structure on his newly purchased farm and another bid of $90,000 to replace it with a structure in which the man planned to house a new herd of dairy cows. Having only limited cash available, the man asked his uncle for a $100,000 loan. The next day, the man's uncle in a signed writing promised to lend the man $100,000 repayable over 10 years in equal monthly installments at 10% annual interest. The man promptly accepted the demolition bid and the old structure was removed, but the uncle thereafter refused to make the loan. Despite diligent efforts, the man was unable to obtain a loan from any other source. In a successful claim for monetary relief against the uncle based on promissory estoppel, what is the probable measure of the man's recovery?","choices":{"A":"Expectancy damages, measured by the difference between the value of the new structure and the old structure, less the amount of the proposed loan ($100,000).","B":"Expectancy damages, measured by the estimated profits from operating the dairy farm for 10 years, less the cost of repaying a $100,000 loan at 10% interest over 10 years.","C":"Nominal damages only, because both expectancy and reliance damages are speculative.","D":"Reliance damages, measured by the $10,000 expense of removing the old structure, adjusted by the decrease or increase in the market value of the man's land immediately thereafter."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Promissory-estoppel damages\n\nReliance Intended to put enforcing party in same position as if promise had not been made Measured by out-of-pocket expenses incurred by enforcing party in reliance on promise Expectation Intended to put enforcing party in same position as if promise had been performed (rare) Measured by nonspeculative expectation of enforcing party Promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages stemming from another's failure to perform a promise when no valid contract exists. However, such damages may be limited as justice requires. This typically leads to an award of reliance damages based on the out-of- pocket expenses incurred by the enforcing party. This is designed to place the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had never been made and to avoid unjust enrichment of the enforcing party. However, in some promissory-estoppel cases, courts have awarded expectation damages. Expectation damages are designed to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed. These damages usually exceed reliance damages but cannot be speculative—eg, the value of a nonexistent building, estimated profits from a nonexistent farm (Choices A & B). Therefore, the probable measure of the man's recovery is reliance damages, measured by the expense of removing the old structure in reliance on the uncle's promise ($10,000). This amount should then be adjusted by the decrease or increase in the market value of the land after demolition to further compensate the man for losses or avoid his unjust enrichment, respectively. (Choice C) Nominal damages (eg, $1) may be awarded when no reliance or expectation damages can be shown. But here, the man's reliance damages of $10,000 can be shown, so an award of nominal damages is inappropriate.\n\nEducational objective: Damages for promissory estoppel may be limited as justice requires. This typically results in an award of reliance damages based on the claimant's out-of-pocket expenses, but some courts have awarded expectation damages to put the claimant in the same position as if the promise had been performed.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (damages for promissory- estoppel claim).","educationalObjective":"Damages for promissory estoppel may be limited as justice requires. This typically results in an award of reliance damages based on the claimant's out-of-pocket expenses, but some courts have awarded expectation damages to put the claimant in the same position as if the promise had been performed.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 90 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (damages for promissory- estoppel claim).","rule":"Damages for promissory estoppel may be limited as justice requires. This typically results in an award of reliance damages based on the claimant's out-of-pocket expenses, but some courts have awarded expectation damages to put the claimant in the same position as if the promise had been performed.","irac":{"issue":"In a successful claim for monetary relief against the uncle based on promissory estoppel, what is the probable measure of the man's recovery?","rule":"Damages for promissory estoppel may be limited as justice requires. This typically results in an award of reliance damages based on the claimant's out-of-pocket expenses, but some courts have awarded expectation damages to put the claimant in the same position as if the promise had been performed.","analysis":"Promissory-estoppel damages\n\nReliance Intended to put enforcing party in same position as if promise had not been made Measured by out-of-pocket expenses incurred by enforcing party in reliance on promise Expectation Intended to put enforcing party in same position as if promise had been performed (rare) Measured by nonspeculative expectation of enforcing party Promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages stemming from another's failure to perform a promise when no valid contract exists. However, such damages may be limited as justice requires. This typically leads to an award of reliance damages based on the out-of- pocket expenses incurred by the enforcing party. This is designed to place the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had never been made and to avoid unjust enrichment of the enforcing party. However, in some promissory-estoppel cases, courts have awarded expectation damages. Expectation damages are designed to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed. These damages usually exceed reliance damages but cannot be speculative—eg, the value of a nonexistent building, estimated profits from a nonexistent farm (Choices A & B). Therefore, the probable measure of the man's recovery is reliance damages, measured by the expense of removing the old structure in reliance on the uncle's promise ($10,000). This amount should then be adjusted by the decrease or increase in the market value of the land after demolition to further compensate the man for losses or avoid his unjust enrichment, respectively. (Choice C) Nominal damages (eg, $1) may be awarded when no reliance or expectation damages can be shown. But here, the man's reliance damages of $10,000 can be shown, so an award of nominal damages is inappropriate.","conclusion":"Answer D: Reliance damages, measured by the $10,000 expense of removing the old structure, adjusted by the decrease or increase in the market value of the man's land immediately thereafter."},"questionZh":"一名男子获得了 10,000 美元的出价，拆除了他新购买的农场中的一座旧建筑，并获得了另一笔 90,000 美元的出价，以建造一座该男子计划饲养新群奶牛的建筑。由于现金有限，该男子向叔叔借了 10 万美元。第二天，该男子的叔叔在一份签字的书面文件中承诺借给该男子10万美元，分10年偿还，按月等额分期付款，年利率10%。该男子立即接受了拆迁报价，旧建筑被拆除，但叔叔随后拒绝贷款。尽管竭尽全力，该男子仍无法从任何其他来源获得贷款。在基于允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）而成功地向叔叔提出金钱救济的索赔中，该男子康复的可能衡量标准是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"预期损害，以新结构和旧结构的价值之间的差额减去拟议贷款金额（100,000 美元）来衡量。","B":"预期损失是指经营奶牛场 10 年的预计利润减去在 10 年内以 10% 的利率偿还 100,000 美元贷款的成本。","C":"仅名义损害赔偿，因为预期损害赔偿和信赖损害都是推测性的。","D":"依赖损害，以拆除旧建筑的 10,000 美元费用衡量，并根据此后该人土地市场价值的减少或增加进行调整。"},"explanationZh":"允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）损害赔偿\n\n信赖 旨在使执行方处于与未做出承诺相同的位置 通过执行方因依赖承诺而产生的自付费用来衡量 期望 旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的位置（罕见） 通过执行方的非投机期望来衡量 禁止反言允许一方在不存在有效合同的情况下追偿因另一方未能履行承诺而造成的损失。然而，根据正义的要求，这种损害可能是有限的。这通常会导致根据执行方产生的自付费用判定信赖损害赔偿。这样做的目的是让执行方处于如同从未做出过承诺一样的境地，并避免执行方获得不当得利。然而，在一些禁止反言案件中，法院判给了预期损害赔偿。预期损害赔偿旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。这些损害通常超过信赖损害，但不能是推测性的，例如，不存在的建筑物的价值、不存在的农场的估计利润（选择 A 和 B）。因此，该男子康复的可能衡量标准是依赖损害，以依赖叔叔的承诺拆除旧结构的费用（10,000 美元）来衡量。该数额应根据拆迁后土地市场价值的减少或增加进行调整，以分别进一步补偿该人的损失或避免其不当得利。 （选择C）当无法证明信赖或预期损害时，可以判给名义损害赔偿（例如，1 美元）。但在这里，可以显示该男子 10,000 美元的信赖损害赔偿，因此象征性损害赔偿金是不合适的。\n\n教育目标：根据正义的要求，禁止反言的损害赔偿可能受到限制。这通常会导致根据索赔人的自付费用判决信赖损害赔偿，但一些法院判给预期损害赔偿，以使索赔人处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（本票禁止反言索赔的损害赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据正义的要求，禁止反言的损害赔偿可能会受到限制。这通常会导致根据索赔人的自付费用判决信赖损害赔偿，但一些法院判给预期损害赔偿，以使索赔人处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。","iracZh":{"issue":"在基于允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）而成功地向叔叔提出金钱救济的索赔中，该男子康复的可能衡量标准是什么？","rule":"根据正义的要求，禁止反言的损害赔偿可能会受到限制。这通常会导致根据索赔人的自付费用判决信赖损害赔偿，但一些法院判给预期损害赔偿，以使索赔人处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。","analysis":"允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）损害赔偿\n\n信赖 旨在使执行方处于与未做出承诺相同的位置 通过执行方因依赖承诺而产生的自付费用来衡量 期望 旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的位置（罕见） 通过执行方的非投机期望来衡量 禁止反言允许一方在不存在有效合同的情况下追偿因另一方未能履行承诺而造成的损失。然而，根据正义的要求，这种损害可能是有限的。这通常会导致根据执行方产生的自付费用判定信赖损害赔偿。这样做的目的是让执行方处于如同从未做出过承诺一样的境地，并避免执行方获得不当得利。然而，在一些禁止反言案件中，法院判给了预期损害赔偿。预期损害赔偿旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。这些损害通常超过信赖损害，但不能是推测性的，例如，不存在的建筑物的价值、不存在的农场的估计利润（选择 A 和 B）。因此，该男子康复的可能衡量标准是依赖损害，以依赖叔叔的承诺拆除旧结构的费用（10,000 美元）来衡量。该数额应根据拆迁后土地市场价值的减少或增加进行调整，以分别进一步补偿该人的损失或避免其不当得利。 （选择C）当无法证明信赖或预期损害时，可以判给名义损害赔偿（例如，1 美元）。但在这里，可以显示该男子 10,000 美元的信赖损害赔偿，因此象征性损害赔偿金是不合适的。\n\n教育目标：根据正义的要求，禁止反言的损害赔偿可能受到限制。这通常会导致根据索赔人的自付费用判决信赖损害赔偿，但一些法院判给预期损害赔偿，以使索赔人处于与承诺已履行相同的境地。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 90（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（本票禁止反言索赔的损害赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 D：依赖损害，以拆除旧建筑的 10,000 美元费用衡量，并根据此后该人土地市场价值的减少或增加进行调整。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"expectancy","ipa":"/ɪkspˈɛktənsi/","syllables":"ex·pectancy","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 期待, 期望的事物, 嘱望, 希望, 期望, 预期；[医] 预期","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“expectancy”","wordForm":"词；词形 s:expectancies","sourceSentence":"Expectancy damages, measured by the difference between the value of the new structure and the old structure, less the amount of the proposed loan ($100,000).","synonyms":["anticipation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":230,"sourceQuestionPage":680,"sourceQuestionPages":[680],"sourceExplanationPages":[681,682],"sourceAnswerPages":[681,682],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-231","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A manufacturer of flat screen televisions and a big box retailer had a written contract in which the manufacturer agreed to sell and the retailer agreed to buy all of its requirements of a certain type of television for three years. A dispute arose near the end of the third year, when the retailer returned 25 undefective televisions to the manufacturer for full credit along with a check payable to the manufacturer for the balance admittedly due on all other merchandise sold and delivered to the retailer. The check was conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full for all goods sold to the retailer to date.\" The manufacturer's credit manager, reading this check notation and knowing that the retailer had also returned the 25 televisions for full credit, deposited the check without protest in the manufacturer's local bank account. The canceled check was returned to the retailer a week later. Which of the following defenses would best serve the retailer in an action by the television manufacturer against the retailer for damages due to return of the 25 televisions?","choices":{"A":"By depositing the check without protest and with knowledge of its wording, the television manufacturer discharged any remaining duty to pay on the part of the retailer.","B":"By depositing the check without protest and with knowledge of its wording, the television manufacturer entered into a novation discharging any remaining duty to pay on the part of the retailer.","C":"The parties' good-faith dispute over return of the televisions suspended the duty of the retailer, if any, to pay any balance due.","D":"The television manufacturer's deposit of the check and its return to the retailer after payment estopped the manufacturer thereafter to assert that the retailer owed any additional amount."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full.\" The creditor can then: deposit the check – in which case the debtor's duty to pay is discharged or reject the check – in which case the debtor's duty to pay is not discharged. Here, the retailer disputed the amount owed to the television manufacturer since the retailer returned 25 undefective televisions for credit. The retailer included a check— conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full\"—in the amount of the balance due on all merchandise sold and delivered to the retailer other than the 25 undefective televisions. Therefore, the retailer's best defense is that, by depositing that check instead of rejecting it, the manufacturer discharged any remaining duty to pay on the part of the retailer. (Choice B) A novation is a subsequent contract that substitutes a new party for one of the original parties. But here, the retailer attempted to settle a debt by tendering a check—not by substituting a new party. (Choice C) When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle the debt for less than the claimed amount. But the debtor is still required to pay the undisputed portion of the debt, if any. (Choice D) The television manufacturer is estopped (ie, prohibited) from asserting a claim for any additional amount because the manufacturer deposited the check—not because the check was returned to the retailer after payment.\n\nEducational objective: When the amount of a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle by giving the creditor a check conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full.\" The debtor's duty to pay will be discharged only if the creditor deposits the check.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a creditor who cashes a check marked \"payment in full\" may have formed an enforceable accord).","educationalObjective":"When the amount of a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle by giving the creditor a check conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full.\" The debtor's duty to pay will be discharged only if the creditor deposits the check.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a creditor who cashes a check marked \"payment in full\" may have formed an enforceable accord).","rule":"When the amount of a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle by giving the creditor a check conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full.\" The debtor's duty to pay will be discharged only if the creditor deposits the check.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following defenses would best serve the retailer in an action by the television manufacturer against the retailer for damages due to return of the 25 televisions?","rule":"When the amount of a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle by giving the creditor a check conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full.\" The debtor's duty to pay will be discharged only if the creditor deposits the check.","analysis":"When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle the debt by giving the creditor a check conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full.\" The creditor can then: deposit the check – in which case the debtor's duty to pay is discharged or reject the check – in which case the debtor's duty to pay is not discharged. Here, the retailer disputed the amount owed to the television manufacturer since the retailer returned 25 undefective televisions for credit. The retailer included a check— conspicuously marked, \"Payment in full\"—in the amount of the balance due on all merchandise sold and delivered to the retailer other than the 25 undefective televisions. Therefore, the retailer's best defense is that, by depositing that check instead of rejecting it, the manufacturer discharged any remaining duty to pay on the part of the retailer. (Choice B) A novation is a subsequent contract that substitutes a new party for one of the original parties. But here, the retailer attempted to settle a debt by tendering a check—not by substituting a new party. (Choice C) When a debt is disputed in good faith, the debtor can attempt to settle the debt for less than the claimed amount. But the debtor is still required to pay the undisputed portion of the debt, if any. (Choice D) The television manufacturer is estopped (ie, prohibited) from asserting a claim for any additional amount because the manufacturer deposited the check—not because the check was returned to the retailer after payment.","conclusion":"Answer A: By depositing the check without protest and with knowledge of its wording, the television manufacturer discharged any remaining duty to pay on the part of the retailer."},"questionZh":"一家平板电视制造商和一家大型零售商签订了一份书面合同，其中制造商同意销售，零售商同意购买其对某种类型电视的所有需求，为期三年。第三年年底附近发生了一场纠纷，当时零售商将 25 台无缺陷电视机退还给制造商，以获得全额信用，并附上一张应付给制造商的支票，以支付所有其他出售并交付给零售商的商品的余额。支票上明显标有“迄今为止向零售商出售的所有商品的全额付款”。制造商的信贷经理阅读了此支票符号并知道零售商也已退回 25 台电视机以获得全额信用，因此毫无异议地将支票存入制造商的当地银行帐户。一周后，取消的支票被退还给零售商。在电视制造商针对零售商提起因退回 25 台电视而造成的损害赔偿诉讼中，以下哪一项抗辩最有利于零售商？","choicesZh":{"A":"电视制造商在没有抗议并了解其措辞的情况下存入支票，从而免除了零售商的任何剩余付款义务。","B":"电视制造商在不提出异议并了解其措辞的情况下存入支票，从而进行了一项变革，免除了零售商方面的任何剩余付款义务。","C":"双方就退回电视问题发生的善意争议暂停了零售商支付任何应付余额的义务（如果有）。","D":"电视制造商存入支票并在付款后将其返还给零售商，这使得制造商此后无法断言零售商欠任何额外金额。"},"explanationZh":"当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张明显标有“全额付款”的支票来尝试解决债务。然后，债权人可以： 存入支票——在这种情况下，债务人的付款义务被解除；或拒绝支票——在这种情况下，债务人的付款义务未被解除。在此，零售商对欠电视制造商的金额提出异议，因为零售商退回了 25 台无缺陷的电视以获取信用。零售商附上一张支票，上面明显标有“全额付款”字样，金额为除 25 台无缺陷电视机之外的所有销售并交付给零售商的商品的应付余额。因此，零售商最好的辩护是，通过存入支票而不是拒绝支票，制造商免除了零售商的任何剩余付款义务。 （选择 B） 更替是一种后续合同，用新的当事人替代原当事人之一。但在这里，零售商试图通过提供支票来解决债务，而不是通过更换新的一方。 （选项C）当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以尝试以低于索赔金额的金额清偿债务。但债务人仍须偿还债务中无争议的部分（如有）。 （选项D）电视制造商被禁止（即禁止）提出任何额外金额的索赔，因为制造商存入了支票，而不是因为支票在付款后退还给零售商。\n\n教育目标：当善意地对债务金额产生争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张明显标有“全额付款”的支票来尝试解决。只有当债权人存入支票时，债务人的付款义务才会被解除。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 281 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，兑现标有“全额付款”支票的债权人可能已形成可执行协议）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当善意地对债务金额产生争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张明显标有“全额付款”的支票来尝试解决。只有当债权人存入支票时，债务人的付款义务才会被解除。","iracZh":{"issue":"在电视制造商针对零售商提起因退回 25 台电视而造成的损害赔偿诉讼中，以下哪一项抗辩最有利于零售商？","rule":"当善意地对债务金额产生争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张明显标有“全额付款”的支票来尝试解决。只有当债权人存入支票时，债务人的付款义务才会被解除。","analysis":"当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张明显标有“全额付款”的支票来尝试解决债务。然后，债权人可以： 存入支票——在这种情况下，债务人的付款义务被解除；或拒绝支票——在这种情况下，债务人的付款义务未被解除。在此，零售商对欠电视制造商的金额提出异议，因为零售商退回了 25 台无缺陷的电视以获取信用。零售商附上一张支票，上面明显标有“全额付款”字样，金额为除 25 台无缺陷电视机之外的所有销售并交付给零售商的商品的应付余额。因此，零售商最好的辩护是，通过存入支票而不是拒绝支票，制造商免除了零售商的任何剩余付款义务。 （选择 B） 更替是一种后续合同，用新的当事人替代原当事人之一。但在这里，零售商试图通过提供支票来解决债务，而不是通过更换新的一方。 （选项C）当债务存在善意争议时，债务人可以尝试以低于索赔金额的金额清偿债务。但债务人仍须偿还债务中无争议的部分（如有）。 （选项D）电视制造商被禁止（即禁止）提出任何额外金额的索赔，因为制造商存入了支票，而不是因为支票在付款后退还给零售商。\n\n教育目标：当善意地对债务金额产生争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张明显标有“全额付款”的支票来尝试解决。只有当债权人存入支票时，债务人的付款义务才会被解除。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 281 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，兑现标有“全额付款”支票的债权人可能已形成可执行协议）。","conclusion":"答案 A：电视制造商在没有抗议并了解其措辞的情况下存入支票，从而免除了零售商的任何剩余付款义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-37beec99f1abd332"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":231,"sourceQuestionPage":683,"sourceQuestionPages":[683],"sourceExplanationPages":[684,685],"sourceAnswerPages":[684,685],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-232","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A lumber supplier agreed to sell to a furniture manufacturer all the lumber that the manufacturer required over a two-year period. The sales contract provided that payment was due 60 days after delivery, but that a 3% discount would be allowed if the manufacturer paid within 10 days of delivery. During the first year of the contract, the manufacturer regularly paid within the 10-day period and received the 3% discount. Fifteen days after the supplier had made its most recent lumber delivery to the manufacturer, the supplier had received no payment from the manufacturer. At this time, the supplier became aware of rumors from a credible source that the manufacturer's financial condition was precarious. The supplier wrote the manufacturer, demanding assurances regarding the manufacturer's financial status. The manufacturer immediately mailed to the supplier its latest audited financial statements as well as a satisfactory credit report prepared by the manufacturer's banker. The rumors proved to be false. Nevertheless, the supplier refused to resume deliveries. The manufacturer has sued the supplier for breach of contract. Will the manufacturer likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contract was unenforceable, since the manufacturer had not committed to purchase a definite quantity of lumber.","B":"No, because the supplier had reasonable grounds for insecurity and was therefore entitled to cancel the contract and refuse to make any future deliveries.","C":"Yes, because the credit report and audited financial statements provided adequate assurance of due performance under the contract.","D":"Yes, because the supplier was not entitled to condition resumption of deliveries on the receipt of financial status information."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. The insecure party may also suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Between merchants, the reasonableness of insecurity and the adequacy of assurance are determined by commercial standards (eg, financial status information). Failure to provide adequate assurance within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation. Here, the supplier had reasonable grounds for insecurity about the manufacturer's ability to pay after being told by a credible source that the manufacturer's financial condition was precarious. As a result, the supplier was entitled to condition resumption of deliveries on the receipt of adequate assurance (Choice D). The manufacturer provided adequate assurance with its audited financial statements and credit report. Therefore, the supplier was not entitled to cancel the contract, and the manufacturer will likely prevail in its breach-of-contract suit (Choice B). (Choice A) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, lumber) must contain a quantity term. Here, the contract did not specify a definite quantity of lumber. But the quantity could be made certain under this requirements contract because the supplier agreed to deliver all the lumber the manufacturer required over a two-year period.\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Between merchants, the reasonableness of insecurity and the adequacy of assurance are determined by commercial standards. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Between merchants, the reasonableness of insecurity and the adequacy of assurance are determined by commercial standards. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"U.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).","rule":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Between merchants, the reasonableness of insecurity and the adequacy of assurance are determined by commercial standards. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Will the manufacturer likely prevail?","rule":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. Between merchants, the reasonableness of insecurity and the adequacy of assurance are determined by commercial standards. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform. The insecure party may also suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Between merchants, the reasonableness of insecurity and the adequacy of assurance are determined by commercial standards (eg, financial status information). Failure to provide adequate assurance within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation. Here, the supplier had reasonable grounds for insecurity about the manufacturer's ability to pay after being told by a credible source that the manufacturer's financial condition was precarious. As a result, the supplier was entitled to condition resumption of deliveries on the receipt of adequate assurance (Choice D). The manufacturer provided adequate assurance with its audited financial statements and credit report. Therefore, the supplier was not entitled to cancel the contract, and the manufacturer will likely prevail in its breach-of-contract suit (Choice B). (Choice A) Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, lumber) must contain a quantity term. Here, the contract did not specify a definite quantity of lumber. But the quantity could be made certain under this requirements contract because the supplier agreed to deliver all the lumber the manufacturer required over a two-year period.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the credit report and audited financial statements provided adequate assurance of due performance under the contract."},"questionZh":"一家木材供应商同意向一家家具制造商出售该制造商两年内所需的所有木材。销售合同规定，交货后60天内付款，但如果制造商在交货后10天内付款，可享受3%的折扣。在合同第一年，制造商定期在10天内付款并获得3%的折扣。在供应商向制造商交付最近一次木材之后十五天，供应商没有收到制造商的任何付款。这时，供应商从可靠消息来源得知制造商财务状况岌岌可危的传言。供应商写信给制造商，要求制造商保证其财务状况。制造商立即向供应商邮寄了最新的经审计的财务报表以及制造商银行家编制的令人满意的信用报告。谣言被证明是错误的。尽管如此，供应商拒绝恢复交货。制造商已起诉供应商违反合同。制造商可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合同无法执行，因为制造商没有承诺购买一定数量的木材。","B":"不可以，因为供应商有合理的理由感到不安全，因此有权取消合同并拒绝将来交货。","C":"是的，因为信用报告和经审计的财务报表为合同的应有履行提供了充分的保证。","D":"是的，因为供应商无权以收到财务状况信息为恢复交货的条件。"},"explanationZh":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履约。不安全方也可以暂停自己的履行，直到提供保证为止。在商家之间，不安全感的合理性和保证的充分性是由商业标准（例如财务状况信息）决定的。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供充分保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。在这里，供应商在从可靠消息来源得知制造商的财务状况不稳定后，有合理的理由对制造商的付款能力感到不安。因此，供应商有权在收到充分保证后恢复交货（选择 D）。制造商通过其经审计的财务报表和信用报告提供了充分的保证。因此，供应商无权取消合同，制造商的违约诉讼很可能胜诉（选项B）。 （选择 A）货物（例如木材）销售合同必须包含数量条款。在这里，合同没有规定木材的具体数量。但根据该需求合同，数量可以确定，因为供应商同意在两年内交付制造商所需的所有木材。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法》，有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履约。在商人之间，不安全感的合理性和保证的充分性是由商业标准决定的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履约。在商人之间，不安全感的合理性和保证的充分性是由商业标准决定的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"制造商可能会获胜吗？","rule":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履约。在商人之间，不安全感的合理性和保证的充分性是由商业标准决定的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履约。不安全方也可以暂停自己的履行，直到提供保证为止。在商家之间，不安全感的合理性和保证的充分性是由商业标准（例如财务状况信息）决定的。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供充分保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。在这里，供应商在从可靠消息来源得知制造商的财务状况不稳定后，有合理的理由对制造商的付款能力感到不安。因此，供应商有权在收到充分保证后恢复交货（选择 D）。制造商通过其经审计的财务报表和信用报告提供了充分的保证。因此，供应商无权取消合同，制造商的违约诉讼很可能胜诉（选项B）。 （选择 A）货物（例如木材）销售合同必须包含数量条款。在这里，合同没有规定木材的具体数量。但根据该需求合同，数量可以确定，因为供应商同意在两年内交付制造商所需的所有木材。\n\n教育目标：根据《统一商法》，有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履约。在商人之间，不安全感的合理性和保证的充分性是由商业标准决定的。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为信用报告和经审计的财务报表为合同的应有履行提供了充分的保证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5d663b8546088378"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":232,"sourceQuestionPage":686,"sourceQuestionPages":[686],"sourceExplanationPages":[687,688],"sourceAnswerPages":[687,688],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-233","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Risk of Loss & Delivery","question":"On June 1, a seller agreed to sell an antique car to a buyer for $20,000, in a writing signed by both the seller and the buyer. At the time, the car was on display in a museum in a different city and was to be delivered to the buyer on August 1. On July 15, before the risk of loss had passed to the buyer, the car was destroyed by fire without fault of either party. Subsequent to the contract but before the fire, the car had increased in value to $30,000. The seller sued the buyer for the contract price of $20,000, and the buyer counterclaimed for $30,000. What is the likely outcome of this suit?","choices":{"A":"Both claims will fail.","B":"Both claims will succeed, and the buyer will recover $10,000.","C":"Only the buyer's claim will prevail.","D":"Only the seller's claim will succeed."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Effect of destruction or damage to identified goods\n\n(UCC § 2-613)\n\nComplete Contract avoided (ie, neither party is required to perform) destruction\n\nPartial Buyer may demand inspection & (1) treat contract as avoided or (2) destruction accept goods at reduced price but with no further right against seller\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, the risk of loss generally remains with the seller until the buyer receives the goods. However, if the contract deals with specifically identified goods (eg, the antique car on display), then complete destruction of the goods excuses each party's duty to perform so long as the destruction occurred: without fault of either party and before the risk of loss passed to the buyer. Here, the buyer and seller formed a contract for the sale of an antique car. That car was destroyed by fire without fault of either party and before the risk of loss passed to the buyer. Therefore, the contract was avoided and neither the buyer nor the seller was required to perform. This means that both their claims will fail (Choices B, C & D).\n\nEducational objective: When a contract deals with specifically identified goods, complete destruction of the goods excuses each party's duty to perform if the destruction occurred (1) without fault of either party and (2) before the risk of loss passed to the buyer.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (risk of loss in the absence of breach).\n\nUCC § 2-613 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (casualty to identified goods).","educationalObjective":"When a contract deals with specifically identified goods, complete destruction of the goods excuses each party's duty to perform if the destruction occurred (1) without fault of either party and (2) before the risk of loss passed to the buyer.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (risk of loss in the absence of breach).\n\nUCC § 2-613 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (casualty to identified goods).","rule":"When a contract deals with specifically identified goods, complete destruction of the goods excuses each party's duty to perform if the destruction occurred (1) without fault of either party and (2) before the risk of loss passed to the buyer.","irac":{"issue":"What is the likely outcome of this suit?","rule":"When a contract deals with specifically identified goods, complete destruction of the goods excuses each party's duty to perform if the destruction occurred (1) without fault of either party and (2) before the risk of loss passed to the buyer.","analysis":"Effect of destruction or damage to identified goods\n\n(UCC § 2-613)\n\nComplete Contract avoided (ie, neither party is required to perform) destruction\n\nPartial Buyer may demand inspection & (1) treat contract as avoided or (2) destruction accept goods at reduced price but with no further right against seller\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nContracts for the sale of goods are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, the risk of loss generally remains with the seller until the buyer receives the goods. However, if the contract deals with specifically identified goods (eg, the antique car on display), then complete destruction of the goods excuses each party's duty to perform so long as the destruction occurred: without fault of either party and before the risk of loss passed to the buyer. Here, the buyer and seller formed a contract for the sale of an antique car. That car was destroyed by fire without fault of either party and before the risk of loss passed to the buyer. Therefore, the contract was avoided and neither the buyer nor the seller was required to perform. This means that both their claims will fail (Choices B, C & D).","conclusion":"Answer A: Both claims will fail."},"questionZh":"6 月 1 日，卖方同意以 20,000 美元的价格向买方出售一辆古董车，并签署了一份由卖方和买方双方签署的书面文件。当时，这辆车正在另一个城市的博物馆展出，原定于 8 月 1 日交付给买家。7 月 15 日，在损失风险转移给买家之前，这辆车被火灾烧毁，双方均无过错。合同签订后、火灾发生前，这辆车的价值已增至 30,000 美元。卖方起诉买方，要求赔偿合同价款20,000美元，买方反诉，索赔30,000美元。这起诉讼可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"这两种主张都将失败。","B":"两项索赔都会成功，买家将获得 10,000 美元的赔偿。","C":"只有买方的索赔才有效。","D":"只有卖家的索赔才会成功。"},"explanationZh":"对已识别货物的毁坏或损坏的影响\n\n（UCC § 2-613）\n\n完整的合同避免了（即双方都不需要执行）销毁\n\n部分买方可以要求检验并 (1) 将合同视为无效或 (2) 销毁并以降价接受货物，但没有针对卖方的进一步权利\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，在买方收到货物之前，损失风险通常由卖方承担。然而，如果合同涉及具体确定的货物（例如，展出的古董车），那么只要发生销毁，货物的完全销毁就可以免除各方履行的义务：没有任何一方的过错，并且在损失风险转移给买方之前。在这里，买方和卖方签订了古董车销售合同。该车在损失风险转移给买方之前就被火灾烧毁，双方均无过错。因此，合同无效，买方和卖方均无需履行。这意味着他们的主张都将失败（选项 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目的：当合同涉及明确指定的货物时，如果销毁发生（1）没有任何一方的过错且（2）在损失风险转移给买方之前，则货物的完全销毁可以免除各方履行的义务。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（在不违反的情况下存在损失风险）。\n\nUCC § 2-613 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（已识别货物的伤亡）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当合同涉及具体确定的货物时，如果销毁发生（1）没有任何一方的过失且（2）在损失风险转移给买方之前，则货物的完全销毁可以免除各方履行的义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"这起诉讼可能的结果是什么？","rule":"当合同涉及具体确定的货物时，如果销毁发生（1）没有任何一方的过失且（2）在损失风险转移给买方之前，则货物的完全销毁可以免除各方履行的义务。","analysis":"对已识别货物的毁坏或损坏的影响\n\n（UCC § 2-613）\n\n完整的合同避免了（即双方都不需要执行）销毁\n\n部分买方可以要求检验并 (1) 将合同视为无效或 (2) 销毁并以降价接受货物，但没有针对卖方的进一步权利\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n货物销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，在买方收到货物之前，损失风险通常由卖方承担。然而，如果合同涉及具体确定的货物（例如，展出的古董车），那么只要发生销毁，货物的完全销毁就可以免除各方履行的义务：没有任何一方的过错，并且在损失风险转移给买方之前。在这里，买方和卖方签订了古董车销售合同。该车在损失风险转移给买方之前就被火灾烧毁，双方均无过错。因此，合同无效，买方和卖方均无需履行。这意味着他们的主张都将失败（选项 B、C 和 D）。\n\n教育目的：当合同涉及明确指定的货物时，如果销毁发生（1）没有任何一方的过错且（2）在损失风险转移给买方之前，则货物的完全销毁可以免除各方履行的义务。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-509 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（在不违反的情况下存在损失风险）。\n\nUCC § 2-613 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（已识别货物的伤亡）。","conclusion":"答案 A：这两种主张都将失败。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":233,"sourceQuestionPage":689,"sourceQuestionPages":[689],"sourceExplanationPages":[690],"sourceAnswerPages":[690],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-234","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"On June 1, a general contractor and a subcontractor entered into a contract under which the subcontractor agreed to deliver all of the steel joists that the general contractor required in the construction of a hospital building. The contract provided that delivery of the steel joists would begin on September 1. Although the general contractor had no reason to doubt the subcontractor's ability to perform, the general contractor wanted to be sure that the subcontractor was on track for delivery in September. He therefore wrote a letter on July 1 to the subcontractor demanding that the subcontractor provide assurance of its ability to meet the September 1 deadline. The subcontractor refused to provide such assurance. The general contractor then immediately obtained the steel joists from another supplier. If the subcontractor sues the general contractor for breach of contract, is the subcontractor likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the contract failed to specify a definite quantity.","B":"No, because the subcontractor anticipatorily repudiated the contract when it failed to provide adequate assurance.","C":"Yes, because a demand for assurance constitutes a breach of contract when the contract does not expressly authorize a party to demand assurance.","D":"Yes, because the subcontractor's failure to provide assurance was not a repudiation since there were no reasonable grounds for the general contractor's insecurity."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, steel joists). Under UCC § 2-609, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Failure to provide such assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation of the contract. The party that made the demand may then treat the repudiation as a breach of contract or ignore it and demand performance. Here, the general contractor had no reason to doubt the subcontractor's ability to perform pursuant to the contract. As a result, the general contractor had no right to demand assurances, and the subcontractor's failure to provide assurance was not an anticipatory repudiation (Choice B). The general contractor then breached the contract by obtaining the steel joists from another supplier. Therefore, the subcontractor will likely prevail. (Choice A) Contracts for the sale of goods must contain a quantity term. Here, the contract did not specify a definite quantity of steel joists. But the quantity could be made certain under this requirements contract because the subcontractor agreed to deliver all the steel joists that the general contractor required. (Choice C) A demand for assurances does not constitute a breach of contract when the contract does not expressly authorize a party to demand assurances. That is because the UCC impliedly authorizes a party to demand assurances when reasonable grounds for insecurity exist (not seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Failure to provide such assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Failure to provide such assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Failure to provide such assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation.","irac":{"issue":"If the subcontractor sues the general contractor for breach of contract, is the subcontractor likely to prevail?","rule":"Under the UCC, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Failure to provide such assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation.","analysis":"The Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, steel joists). Under UCC § 2-609, a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other party's performance has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Failure to provide such assurances within 30 days after a valid demand constitutes an anticipatory repudiation of the contract. The party that made the demand may then treat the repudiation as a breach of contract or ignore it and demand performance. Here, the general contractor had no reason to doubt the subcontractor's ability to perform pursuant to the contract. As a result, the general contractor had no right to demand assurances, and the subcontractor's failure to provide assurance was not an anticipatory repudiation (Choice B). The general contractor then breached the contract by obtaining the steel joists from another supplier. Therefore, the subcontractor will likely prevail. (Choice A) Contracts for the sale of goods must contain a quantity term. Here, the contract did not specify a definite quantity of steel joists. But the quantity could be made certain under this requirements contract because the subcontractor agreed to deliver all the steel joists that the general contractor required. (Choice C) A demand for assurances does not constitute a breach of contract when the contract does not expressly authorize a party to demand assurances. That is because the UCC impliedly authorizes a party to demand assurances when reasonable grounds for insecurity exist (not seen here).","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the subcontractor's failure to provide assurance was not a repudiation since there were no reasonable grounds for the general contractor's insecurity."},"questionZh":"6月1日，总承包商与分包商签订合同，分包商同意交付总承包商建造医院大楼所需的全部钢托梁。合同规定，钢托梁将于9月1日开始交付。尽管总承包商没有理由怀疑分包商的履约能力，但总承包商希望确保分包商能在9月份按计划交付。因此，他于7月1日写信给分包商，要求分包商保证其有能力在9月1日的最后期限前完成任务。分包商拒绝提供此类保证。总承包商随后立即从另一家供应商处获得了钢托梁。如果分包商起诉总承包商违约，分包商胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为合同没有规定具体数量。","B":"不，因为分包商在未能提供充分保证时预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行合同。","C":"是的，因为当合同没有明确授权一方要求保证时，要求保证就构成违约。","D":"是的，因为分包商未能提供保证并不构成违约，因为总承包商没有合理的理由感到不安全。"},"explanationZh":"《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 管辖货物销售合同（例如钢托梁）。根据 UCC § 2-609，一方有合理理由对另一方的履行不安全，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供此类保证将构成对合同的预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。提出要求的一方可以将拒绝视为违约，或者忽略它并要求履行。在此，总承包商没有理由怀疑分包商履行合同的能力。因此，总承包商无权要求保证，而分包商未提供保证不构成预期赖账（选项B）。总承包商随后违反合同，从另一家供应商处获得了钢托梁。因此，分包商可能会占上风。 （选择 A） 货物销售合同必须包含数量条款。这里，合同没有规定钢托梁的具体数量。但根据这份需求合同，数量是可以确定的，因为分包商同意交付总承包商所需的所有钢托梁。 （选项C）当合同未明确授权一方要求保证时，要求保证不构成违约。这是因为 UCC 默示授权一方在存在合理的不安全理由时要求保证（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，一方有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供此类保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），一方有合理理由对另一方的履行感到不安全时，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供此类保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果分包商起诉总承包商违约，分包商胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"根据《统一商法典》（UCC），一方有合理理由对另一方的履行感到不安全时，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供此类保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。","analysis":"《统一商法典》（UCC） (UCC) 管辖货物销售合同（例如钢托梁）。根据 UCC § 2-609，一方有合理理由对另一方的履行不安全，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供此类保证将构成对合同的预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。提出要求的一方可以将拒绝视为违约，或者忽略它并要求履行。在此，总承包商没有理由怀疑分包商履行合同的能力。因此，总承包商无权要求保证，而分包商未提供保证不构成预期赖账（选项B）。总承包商随后违反合同，从另一家供应商处获得了钢托梁。因此，分包商可能会占上风。 （选择 A） 货物销售合同必须包含数量条款。这里，合同没有规定钢托梁的具体数量。但根据这份需求合同，数量是可以确定的，因为分包商同意交付总承包商所需的所有钢托梁。 （选项C）当合同未明确授权一方要求保证时，要求保证不构成违约。这是因为 UCC 默示授权一方在存在合理的不安全理由时要求保证（此处未看到）。\n\n教育目标：根据 UCC，一方有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全，有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。未能在有效要求后 30 天内提供此类保证构成预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。\n\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为分包商未能提供保证并不构成违约，因为总承包商没有合理的理由感到不安全。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5d663b8546088378"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":234,"sourceQuestionPage":691,"sourceQuestionPages":[691],"sourceExplanationPages":[692,693],"sourceAnswerPages":[692,693],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-235","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Breach, Cure & Installments","question":"A home goods distributor signed a written contract with a nursing home to sell the nursing home 100 identical bathroom sinks, 50 sinks to be delivered on March 1, and the remaining 50 sinks on April 1. The agreement did not specify the place of delivery, or the time or place of payment. On March 1, the distributor tendered 49 sinks to the nursing home and explained, \"One of the 50 sinks was damaged in transit from the manufacturer to us, but we will deliver a replacement within 5 days.\" Which of the following statements is correct?","choices":{"A":"The nursing home is entitled to accept any number of the 49 sinks and to reject the rest, but is not entitled to cancel the contract as to any rejected sinks or the lot due on April 1.","B":"The nursing home is entitled to accept any number of the 49 sinks, reject the rest, and cancel the contract, both as to any rejected sinks and the lot due on April 1.","C":"The nursing home must accept the 49 sinks and is not entitled to cancel the rest of the contract.","D":"The nursing home must accept the 49 sinks but is entitled to cancel the rest of the contract."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Buyer's rights under installment contract\n\n(UCC § 2-612)\n\nEach Buyer may reject nonconforming shipment that: installment substantially impairs value of installment cannot be cured Buyer must accept: conforming shipment nonconforming shipment if seller promises to cure Whole Buyer can cancel future installments if one or more nonconforming contract shipments substantially impair value of whole contract Buyer cannot cancel future installments if he/she: accepts nonconforming shipment without notice of cancellation brings action only for past installments OR demands performance of future installments UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, sinks). Such contracts often call for the delivery of goods in separate lots (ie, installment contracts). In that case, the buyer must accept each conforming shipment. But even if a shipment is nonconforming, the buyer must accept it if the seller gives adequate assurance that he/she will cure the nonconformity. And the buyer cannot cancel the rest of the contract unless the nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the whole contract. Here, the home goods distributor entered an installment contract for the delivery of 100 sinks in two separate lots. The first delivery was nonconforming since 1 of the 49 sinks was damaged. But the distributor offered to cure by delivering a replacement within five days. Therefore, the nursing home must accept the 49 sinks (Choices A & B). And since a short delay in the delivery of 1 sink does not substantially impair the value of the whole contract for 100 sinks, the nursing home is not entitled to cancel the rest of the contract (Choices B & D).\n\nEducational objective: In an installment contract for the sale of goods, the buyer must accept a nonconforming shipment if the seller gives adequate assurance that he/she will cure the defect. And the buyer cannot cancel the rest of the contract unless the nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the whole contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (installment contracts).","educationalObjective":"In an installment contract for the sale of goods, the buyer must accept a nonconforming shipment if the seller gives adequate assurance that he/she will cure the defect. And the buyer cannot cancel the rest of the contract unless the nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the whole contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (installment contracts).","rule":"In an installment contract for the sale of goods, the buyer must accept a nonconforming shipment if the seller gives adequate assurance that he/she will cure the defect. And the buyer cannot cancel the rest of the contract unless the nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the whole contract.","irac":{"issue":"A home goods distributor signed a written contract with a nursing home to sell the nursing home 100 identical bathroom sinks, 50 sinks to be delivered on March 1, and the remaining 50 sinks on April 1. The agreement did not specify the place of delivery, or the time or place of payment. On March 1, the distributor tendered 49 sinks to the nursing home and explained, \"One of the 50 sinks was damaged in transit from the manufacturer to us, but we will deliver a replacement within 5 days.\" Which of the following statements is correct?","rule":"In an installment contract for the sale of goods, the buyer must accept a nonconforming shipment if the seller gives adequate assurance that he/she will cure the defect. And the buyer cannot cancel the rest of the contract unless the nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the whole contract.","analysis":"Buyer's rights under installment contract\n\n(UCC § 2-612)\n\nEach Buyer may reject nonconforming shipment that: installment substantially impairs value of installment cannot be cured Buyer must accept: conforming shipment nonconforming shipment if seller promises to cure Whole Buyer can cancel future installments if one or more nonconforming contract shipments substantially impair value of whole contract Buyer cannot cancel future installments if he/she: accepts nonconforming shipment without notice of cancellation brings action only for past installments OR demands performance of future installments UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, sinks). Such contracts often call for the delivery of goods in separate lots (ie, installment contracts). In that case, the buyer must accept each conforming shipment. But even if a shipment is nonconforming, the buyer must accept it if the seller gives adequate assurance that he/she will cure the nonconformity. And the buyer cannot cancel the rest of the contract unless the nonconforming shipment substantially impairs the value of the whole contract. Here, the home goods distributor entered an installment contract for the delivery of 100 sinks in two separate lots. The first delivery was nonconforming since 1 of the 49 sinks was damaged. But the distributor offered to cure by delivering a replacement within five days. Therefore, the nursing home must accept the 49 sinks (Choices A & B). And since a short delay in the delivery of 1 sink does not substantially impair the value of the whole contract for 100 sinks, the nursing home is not entitled to cancel the rest of the contract (Choices B & D).","conclusion":"Answer C: The nursing home must accept the 49 sinks and is not entitled to cancel the rest of the contract."},"questionZh":"一家家居用品经销商与一家疗养院签订了书面合同，向疗养院出售100个一模一样的浴室水槽，其中50个水槽将于3月1日交付，其余50个水槽于4月1日交付。协议中没有具体说明交付地点，也没有指定付款时间或地点。 3月1日，经销商向疗养院招标了49个水槽，并解释说：“这50个水槽中有一个在从制造商到我们的运输途中损坏，但我们将在5天内交付替换品。”以下哪项陈述是正确的？","choicesZh":{"A":"疗养院有权接受 49 个水槽中的任意数量并拒绝其余水槽，但无权取消任何被拒绝的水槽或 4 月 1 日到期的合同。","B":"对于任何被拒绝的水槽和 4 月 1 日到期的批次，疗养院有权接受 49 个水槽中的任意数量，拒绝其余水槽，并取消合同。","C":"疗养院必须接受 49 个水槽，并且无权取消合同的其余部分。","D":"疗养院必须接受 49 个水槽，但有权取消合同的其余部分。"},"explanationZh":"分期付款合同下买方的权利\n\n（UCC § 2-612）\n\n每位买方都可以拒绝不符合条件的货物，如果： 分期付款严重损害分期付款的价值且无法修复 买方必须接受： 合格的货物 不合格的货物，如果卖方承诺修复 整个买方可以取消未来的分期付款 如果一个或多个不合格的合同货物严重损害整个合同的价值 买方不能取消未来的分期付款，如果他/她： 接受不合格的货物而没有取消通知 仅对过去的分期付款采取行动或要求履行未来的分期付款 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）第 2 条管辖商品（例如水槽）销售合同。此类合同通常要求分批交付货物（即分期付款合同）。在这种情况下，买方必须接受每批合格的货物。但即使货物不合格，如果卖方充分保证他/她将解决不合格问题，买方也必须接受该货物。买方不能取消合同的其余部分，除非不合格的货物严重损害了整个合同的价值。在此，家居用品经销商签订了一份分期付款合同，分两批交付 100 个水槽。第一次交付的产品不合格，因为 49 个水槽中有 1 个损坏。但经销商提出在五天内提供替换品来解决问题。因此，疗养院必须接受 49 个水槽（选项 A 和 B）。由于 1 个水槽的交付短暂延迟不会大幅影响 100 个水槽的整个合同的价值，因此疗养院无权取消合同的其余部分（Choices B & D）。\n\n教育目标：在货物销售分期付款合同中，如果卖方充分保证他/她将解决缺陷，则买方必须接受不合格货物。买方不能取消合同的其余部分，除非不合格的货物严重损害了整个合同的价值。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（分期付款合同）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在货物销售分期付款合同中，如果卖方充分保证他/她将解决缺陷，则买方必须接受不合格货物。买方不能取消合同的其余部分，除非不合格的货物严重损害了整个合同的价值。","iracZh":{"issue":"一家家居用品经销商与一家疗养院签订了书面合同，向疗养院出售100个一模一样的浴室水槽，其中50个水槽将于3月1日交付，其余50个水槽于4月1日交付。协议中没有具体说明交付地点，也没有指定付款时间或地点。 3月1日，经销商向疗养院招标了49个水槽，并解释说：“这50个水槽中有一个在从制造商到我们的运输途中损坏，但我们将在5天内交付替换品。”以下哪项陈述是正确的？","rule":"在货物销售分期付款合同中，如果卖方充分保证他/她将解决缺陷，则买方必须接受不合格货物。买方不能取消合同的其余部分，除非不合格的货物严重损害了整个合同的价值。","analysis":"分期付款合同下买方的权利\n\n（UCC § 2-612）\n\n每位买方都可以拒绝不符合条件的货物，如果： 分期付款严重损害分期付款的价值且无法修复 买方必须接受： 合格的货物 不合格的货物，如果卖方承诺修复 整个买方可以取消未来的分期付款 如果一个或多个不合格的合同货物严重损害整个合同的价值 买方不能取消未来的分期付款，如果他/她： 接受不合格的货物而没有取消通知 仅对过去的分期付款采取行动或要求履行未来的分期付款 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）第 2 条管辖商品（例如水槽）销售合同。此类合同通常要求分批交付货物（即分期付款合同）。在这种情况下，买方必须接受每批合格的货物。但即使货物不合格，如果卖方充分保证他/她将解决不合格问题，买方也必须接受该货物。买方不能取消合同的其余部分，除非不合格的货物严重损害了整个合同的价值。在此，家居用品经销商签订了一份分期付款合同，分两批交付 100 个水槽。第一次交付的产品不合格，因为 49 个水槽中有 1 个损坏。但经销商提出在五天内提供替换品来解决问题。因此，疗养院必须接受 49 个水槽（选项 A 和 B）。由于 1 个水槽的交付短暂延迟不会大幅影响 100 个水槽的整个合同的价值，因此疗养院无权取消合同的其余部分（Choices B & D）。\n\n教育目标：在货物销售分期付款合同中，如果卖方充分保证他/她将解决缺陷，则买方必须接受不合格货物。买方不能取消合同的其余部分，除非不合格的货物严重损害了整个合同的价值。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-612 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（分期付款合同）。","conclusion":"答案 C：疗养院必须接受 49 个水槽，并且无权取消合同的其余部分。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":235,"sourceQuestionPage":694,"sourceQuestionPages":[694],"sourceExplanationPages":[695,696],"sourceAnswerPages":[695,696],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-236","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"The owner of an apartment complex contracted in writing with a landscaper to install a sprinkler system for $20,000. The contract required the landscaper to install a specific brand of brass sprinkler heads. It also required the landscaper to install a $500 anti- backflow device, which was required by the city plumbing code but would not affect the sprinkler system's performance. In order to speed up installation, the landscaper installed the sprinkler system using brass sprinkler heads left over from a previous job that, while not the brand specifically required by the contract, were equal in quality and performance. However, the landscaper did not install the anti-backflow device, even though the city plumbing code required it and the owner could be subject to a fine if the device were not installed. The landscaper completed the installation work and then sought payment from the owner. The owner refused to pay the landscaper anything on the ground that the work did not comply with the contract. If the landscaper sues the owner for breach of contract, what is the likely result?","choices":{"A":"The landscaper will not recover, because he willfully and materially breached the contract.","B":"The landscaper will recover restitution for the cost spent on labor and materials.","C":"The landscaper will recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance.","D":"The landscaper will recover the full contract price."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Substantial performance is less than full performance that, while imperfect, does not defeat the contract's main purpose. Under the common law—which governs contracts for services (eg, installing a sprinkler system)—a party that has substantially performed can recover damages based on the contract if the other party fails to perform.* Those damages are generally equal to the contract price minus the cost to remedy any defects in performance. Here, the landscaper imperfectly performed by using the wrong brand of sprinkler heads and not installing the anti-backflow device. However, this did not defeat the contract's main purpose of providing the owner with a working sprinkler system. That is because the sprinkler heads were equal in quality and performance to the ones called for in the contract and the anti-backflow device did not affect performance. Therefore, the landscaper substantially performed and will likely recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance (Choice D). *In contrast, the Uniform Commercial Code requires \"perfect tender,\" and substantial performance of a sale-of-goods contract does not allow a party to recover damages based on the contract except for installment contracts or when the parties agree otherwise. (Choice A) The extent to which a party has acted in bad faith (eg, by committing a willful breach) is one of several factors that courts consider in deciding whether a breach is material. Here, although the landscaper's breach was willful, it was not material since he fulfilled the contract's main purpose. (Choice B) A party's failure to substantially perform the contract is a material breach. A party in material breach cannot recover on the contract but may be entitled to restitution damages for the reasonable value of benefits conferred on the other party—eg, the cost spent on labor and materials. But here, the landscaper did substantially perform so there was no material breach.\n\nEducational objective: At common law, a party that has substantially performed—ie, rendered imperfect performance without defeating the contract's main purpose—can recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 237 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of substantial performance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 241 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (factors used to determine whether a breach of contract is material).","educationalObjective":"At common law, a party that has substantially performed—ie, rendered imperfect performance without defeating the contract's main purpose—can recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 237 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of substantial performance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 241 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (factors used to determine whether a breach of contract is material).","rule":"At common law, a party that has substantially performed—ie, rendered imperfect performance without defeating the contract's main purpose—can recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance.","irac":{"issue":"If the landscaper sues the owner for breach of contract, what is the likely result?","rule":"At common law, a party that has substantially performed—ie, rendered imperfect performance without defeating the contract's main purpose—can recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance.","analysis":"Substantial performance is less than full performance that, while imperfect, does not defeat the contract's main purpose. Under the common law—which governs contracts for services (eg, installing a sprinkler system)—a party that has substantially performed can recover damages based on the contract if the other party fails to perform.* Those damages are generally equal to the contract price minus the cost to remedy any defects in performance. Here, the landscaper imperfectly performed by using the wrong brand of sprinkler heads and not installing the anti-backflow device. However, this did not defeat the contract's main purpose of providing the owner with a working sprinkler system. That is because the sprinkler heads were equal in quality and performance to the ones called for in the contract and the anti-backflow device did not affect performance. Therefore, the landscaper substantially performed and will likely recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance (Choice D). *In contrast, the Uniform Commercial Code requires \"perfect tender,\" and substantial performance of a sale-of-goods contract does not allow a party to recover damages based on the contract except for installment contracts or when the parties agree otherwise. (Choice A) The extent to which a party has acted in bad faith (eg, by committing a willful breach) is one of several factors that courts consider in deciding whether a breach is material. Here, although the landscaper's breach was willful, it was not material since he fulfilled the contract's main purpose. (Choice B) A party's failure to substantially perform the contract is a material breach. A party in material breach cannot recover on the contract but may be entitled to restitution damages for the reasonable value of benefits conferred on the other party—eg, the cost spent on labor and materials. But here, the landscaper did substantially perform so there was no material breach.","conclusion":"Answer C: The landscaper will recover the contract price less the cost to remedy any defects in performance."},"questionZh":"公寓大楼的业主与景观设计师签订了书面合同，以 20,000 美元的价格安装喷水灭火系统。合同要求园林设计师安装特定品牌的黄铜喷头。它还要求园林设计师安装价值 500 美元的防回流装置，这是城市管道规范所要求的，但不会影响喷水灭火系统的性能。为了加快安装速度，园林设计师使用之前工作中遗留下来的黄铜喷头安装了喷水灭火系统，虽然不是合同特别要求的品牌，但质量和性能相同。然而，园林设计师没有安装防回流装置，尽管城市管道法规要求安装防回流装置，如果不安装该装置，业主可能会被罚款。园林设计师完成了安装工作，然后向业主索要付款。业主以工程不符合合同为由，拒绝向园林设计师支付任何费用。如果园艺师起诉业主违反合同，可能的结果是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"园林设计师将无法康复，因为他故意严重违反了合同。","B":"园林设计师将收回劳动力和材料成本的赔偿。","C":"园林设计师将收回合同价格减去纠正任何履行瑕疵的成本。","D":"园林设计师将收回全部合同价。"},"explanationZh":"实质性履行低于全面履行，虽然不完美，但不会违背合同的主要目的。根据管辖服务合同（例如，安装喷水灭火系统）的普通法，如果另一方未能履行合同，实质履行合同的一方可以根据合同获得损害赔偿。*这些损害赔偿通常等于合同价格减去纠正任何履约缺陷的成本。在这里，园林设计师使用了错误品牌的喷头并且没有安装防倒流装置，表现不完美。然而，这并没有违背合同的主要目的，即为业主提供可用的自动喷水灭火系统。这是因为喷头的质量和性能与合同要求的喷头相同，并且防倒流装置不影响性能。因此，园林设计师实质上履行了义务，并且很可能收回合同价格减去补救任何履行缺陷的成本（选择 D）。 *相比之下，《统一商法典》（UCC）要求“完美投标”，并且除分期付款合同或双方另有约定外，货物销售合同的实质性履行不允许一方根据合同获得损害赔偿。 （选择 A） 一方恶意行为的程度（例如，故意违约）是法院在决定违约是否严重时考虑的几个因素之一。在这里，尽管园林设计师的违约行为是故意的，但并不重要，因为他履行了合同的主要目的。 （选择B） 一方未能实质履行合同即构成重大违约。严重违约的一方无法从合同中获得赔偿，但有权根据给予另一方的合理利益价值（例如，劳动力和材料成本）获得损害赔偿。但在这里，园林设计师确实表现出色，因此没有出现重大违规情况。\n\n教育目标：在普通法中，实质履行（即在不违背合同主要目的的情况下履行了不完美的义务）的一方可以收回合同价格，减去纠正任何履行缺陷的成本。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 237 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（实质性履行的影响）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 241（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（用于确定是否严重违反合同的因素）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在普通法中，实质履行的一方（即在没有违背合同主要目的的情况下履行了不完美的义务）可以收回合同价格，减去补救任何履行缺陷的成本。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果园艺师起诉业主违反合同，可能的结果是什么？","rule":"在普通法中，实质履行的一方（即在没有违背合同主要目的的情况下履行了不完美的义务）可以收回合同价格，减去补救任何履行缺陷的成本。","analysis":"实质性履行低于全面履行，虽然不完美，但不会违背合同的主要目的。根据管辖服务合同（例如，安装喷水灭火系统）的普通法，如果另一方未能履行合同，实质履行合同的一方可以根据合同获得损害赔偿。*这些损害赔偿通常等于合同价格减去纠正任何履约缺陷的成本。在这里，园林设计师使用了错误品牌的喷头并且没有安装防倒流装置，表现不完美。然而，这并没有违背合同的主要目的，即为业主提供可用的自动喷水灭火系统。这是因为喷头的质量和性能与合同要求的喷头相同，并且防倒流装置不影响性能。因此，园林设计师实质上履行了义务，并且很可能收回合同价格减去补救任何履行缺陷的成本（选择 D）。 *相比之下，《统一商法典》（UCC）要求“完美投标”，并且除分期付款合同或双方另有约定外，货物销售合同的实质性履行不允许一方根据合同获得损害赔偿。 （选择 A） 一方恶意行为的程度（例如，故意违约）是法院在决定违约是否严重时考虑的几个因素之一。在这里，尽管园林设计师的违约行为是故意的，但并不重要，因为他履行了合同的主要目的。 （选择B） 一方未能实质履行合同即构成重大违约。严重违约的一方无法从合同中获得赔偿，但有权根据给予另一方的合理利益价值（例如，劳动力和材料成本）获得损害赔偿。但在这里，园林设计师确实表现出色，因此没有出现重大违规情况。\n\n教育目标：在普通法中，实质履行（即在不违背合同主要目的的情况下履行了不完美的义务）的一方可以收回合同价格，减去纠正任何履行缺陷的成本。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 237 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（实质性履行的影响）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 241（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（用于确定是否严重违反合同的因素）。","conclusion":"答案 C：园林设计师将收回合同价格减去纠正任何履行瑕疵的成本。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-545e8252b08878e8"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":236,"sourceQuestionPage":697,"sourceQuestionPages":[697],"sourceExplanationPages":[698,699],"sourceAnswerPages":[698,699],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-237","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A bank agreed to lend a merchant $10,000 for one year at 8% interest. The loan proceeds were to be disbursed within two weeks. The merchant intended to use the loan proceeds to purchase a specific shipment of carpets for resale at an expected profit of $5,000 but said nothing about these plans to the bank. The bank failed to disburse the proceeds and refused to assure the merchant that it would do so. The merchant was able to secure a loan from another lender at 10% interest for one year. However, by the time the merchant started the application process for a substitute loan, it was too late to pursue the opportunity to buy the shipment of carpets. In an action against the bank for breach of contract, which of the following amounts is the merchant likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"$5,000, the merchant's foreseeable loss.","B":"Nothing, because damages for lost opportunities are not recoverable.","C":"Nothing, because the parties failed to tacitly agree that the merchant would be entitled to damages in the event of a breach by the bank.","D":"The difference in cost over time between a loan at 10% and a loan at 8%."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Expectation (ie, benefit-of-the-bargain) damages\n\nDefinition Losses arising naturally & obviously from breach\n\nPurpose Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed\n\nMeasure General rule – difference between contract price & market price (or cost of purchasing substitute performance) Construction contracts – difference between contract price & cost of construction by another builder The primary goal of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been fully performed. This typically means that the nonbreaching party can recover: expectation damages – losses arising naturally and obviously from the breach, which can be measured by the difference between the contract price and the market price and/or consequential damages – losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances (eg, lost opportunities) that the breaching party could reasonably foresee when the contract was made. The \"contract price\" of a loan is the interest costs incurred over the life of the loan. This means that if a lender breaches a loan agreement, the borrower can recover the difference between the interest cost of the original loan (contract price) and the interest cost of a substitute loan (market price). The merchant here can therefore recover expectation damages equal to the difference in cost over time between a loan at 10% and one at 8%. (Choices A & B) A nonbreaching party can recover consequential damages for losses the breaching party could reasonably foresee at the time of contracting. But here, the bank knew nothing of the merchant's intent to purchase the carpets when the contract was entered. As a result, damages for the merchant's lost opportunity to purchase the carpets and lost profits ($5,000) are not recoverable. But the merchant may still recover expectation damages. (Choices C) The tacit-agreement test considers (1) whether the parties contemplated a loss when the contract was made and (2) how they likely would have allocated that risk. Consequential damages would be awarded (or not) accordingly. However, the courts and the UCC have rejected this test.\n\nEducational objective: If a lender breaches a loan agreement, the borrower can recover expectation damages equal to the difference between the interest cost of the original loan (contract price) and the interest cost of a substitute loan (market price).\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of damages).","educationalObjective":"If a lender breaches a loan agreement, the borrower can recover expectation damages equal to the difference between the interest cost of the original loan (contract price) and the interest cost of a substitute loan (market price).\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of damages).","rule":"If a lender breaches a loan agreement, the borrower can recover expectation damages equal to the difference between the interest cost of the original loan (contract price) and the interest cost of a substitute loan (market price).\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"In an action against the bank for breach of contract, which of the following amounts is the merchant likely to recover?","rule":"If a lender breaches a loan agreement, the borrower can recover expectation damages equal to the difference between the interest cost of the original loan (contract price) and the interest cost of a substitute loan (market price).\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Expectation (ie, benefit-of-the-bargain) damages\n\nDefinition Losses arising naturally & obviously from breach\n\nPurpose Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed\n\nMeasure General rule – difference between contract price & market price (or cost of purchasing substitute performance) Construction contracts – difference between contract price & cost of construction by another builder The primary goal of contract damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been fully performed. This typically means that the nonbreaching party can recover: expectation damages – losses arising naturally and obviously from the breach, which can be measured by the difference between the contract price and the market price and/or consequential damages – losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances (eg, lost opportunities) that the breaching party could reasonably foresee when the contract was made. The \"contract price\" of a loan is the interest costs incurred over the life of the loan. This means that if a lender breaches a loan agreement, the borrower can recover the difference between the interest cost of the original loan (contract price) and the interest cost of a substitute loan (market price). The merchant here can therefore recover expectation damages equal to the difference in cost over time between a loan at 10% and one at 8%. (Choices A & B) A nonbreaching party can recover consequential damages for losses the breaching party could reasonably foresee at the time of contracting. But here, the bank knew nothing of the merchant's intent to purchase the carpets when the contract was entered. As a result, damages for the merchant's lost opportunity to purchase the carpets and lost profits ($5,000) are not recoverable. But the merchant may still recover expectation damages. (Choices C) The tacit-agreement test considers (1) whether the parties contemplated a loss when the contract was made and (2) how they likely would have allocated that risk. Consequential damages would be awarded (or not) accordingly. However, the courts and the UCC have rejected this test.","conclusion":"Answer D: The difference in cost over time between a loan at 10% and a loan at 8%."},"questionZh":"一家银行同意以 8% 的利率向一位商人提供一年期 10,000 美元的贷款。贷款收益将在两周内支付。该商人打算用贷款收益购买一批特定的地毯进行转售，预计利润为 5,000 美元，但没有向银行透露这些计划。银行未能支付收益，并拒绝向商家保证会这样做。该商人能够以 10% 的利率从另一家贷方获得一年期贷款。然而，当商家开始申请替代贷款时，已经来不及再寻求购买这批地毯的机会了。在因违反合同而对银行提起诉讼时，商家可能会收回以下哪一项金额？","choicesZh":{"A":"5,000美元，商家的可预见损失。","B":"没什么，因为失去机会造成的损失是无法挽回的。","C":"没什么，因为双方未能默认商户有权在银行违约的情况下获得损害赔偿。","D":"10% 贷款和 8% 贷款之间随时间推移的成本差异。"},"explanationZh":"预期（即讨价还价）损害\n\n定义 自然且明显因违约而产生的损失\n\n目的 将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的位置\n\n衡量标准 一般规则 – 合同价格与市场价格（或购买替代履约成本）之间的差额 施工合同 – 合同价格与其他建筑商的施工成本之间的差额 合同损害赔偿的主要目标是使非违约方处于与合同已完全履行相同的地位。这通常意味着守约方可以获得： 预期损害 – 因违约而自然而明显地产生的损失，可以通过合同价格与市场价格之间的差额来衡量和/或间接损害 – 因守约方在签订合同时可以合理预见的特殊情况（例如，失去机会）而产生的损失。贷款的“合同价格”是贷款期限内产生的利息成本。这意味着，如果贷款人违反贷款协议，借款人可以收回原始贷款的利息成本（合同价格）与替代贷款的利息成本（市场价格）之间的差额。因此，这里的商家可以获得的预期损失等于 10% 贷款和 8% 贷款之间随时间推移的成本差异。 （选择 A 和 B）非违约方可以就违约方在签订合同时合理预见的损失获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。但在这里，银行在签订合同时对商人购买地毯的意图一无所知。因此，商家失去购买地毯的机会和利润损失（5,000 美元）的损失无法挽回。但商家仍有可能获得预期损失。 （选项C）默契测试考虑（1）双方在订立合同时是否考虑到损失以及（2）他们可能如何分配该风险。将相应地判给（或不判给）后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。然而，法院和 UCC 拒绝了这一测试。\n\n教育目标：如果贷款人违反贷款协议，借款人可以获得相当于原始贷款利息成本（合同价格）与替代贷款利息成本（市场价格）之间差额的预期损失。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（损害赔偿措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果贷款人违反贷款协议，借款人可以获得相当于原始贷款利息成本（合同价格）与替代贷款利息成本（市场价格）之间差额的预期损失。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"在因违反合同而对银行提起诉讼时，商家可能会收回以下哪一项金额？","rule":"如果贷款人违反贷款协议，借款人可以获得相当于原始贷款利息成本（合同价格）与替代贷款利息成本（市场价格）之间差额的预期损失。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"预期（即讨价还价）损害\n\n定义 自然且明显因违约而产生的损失\n\n目的 将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的位置\n\n衡量标准 一般规则 – 合同价格与市场价格（或购买替代履约成本）之间的差额 施工合同 – 合同价格与其他建筑商的施工成本之间的差额 合同损害赔偿的主要目标是使非违约方处于与合同已完全履行相同的地位。这通常意味着守约方可以获得： 预期损害 – 因违约而自然而明显地产生的损失，可以通过合同价格与市场价格之间的差额来衡量和/或间接损害 – 因守约方在签订合同时可以合理预见的特殊情况（例如，失去机会）而产生的损失。贷款的“合同价格”是贷款期限内产生的利息成本。这意味着，如果贷款人违反贷款协议，借款人可以收回原始贷款的利息成本（合同价格）与替代贷款的利息成本（市场价格）之间的差额。因此，这里的商家可以获得的预期损失等于 10% 贷款和 8% 贷款之间随时间推移的成本差异。 （选择 A 和 B）非违约方可以就违约方在签订合同时合理预见的损失获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。但在这里，银行在签订合同时对商人购买地毯的意图一无所知。因此，商家失去购买地毯的机会和利润损失（5,000 美元）的损失无法挽回。但商家仍有可能获得预期损失。 （选项C）默契测试考虑（1）双方在订立合同时是否考虑到损失以及（2）他们可能如何分配该风险。将相应地判给（或不判给）后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。然而，法院和 UCC 拒绝了这一测试。\n\n教育目标：如果贷款人违反贷款协议，借款人可以获得相当于原始贷款利息成本（合同价格）与替代贷款利息成本（市场价格）之间差额的预期损失。\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（损害赔偿措施）。","conclusion":"答案 D：10% 贷款和 8% 贷款之间随时间推移的成本差异。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":237,"sourceQuestionPage":700,"sourceQuestionPages":[700],"sourceExplanationPages":[701,702],"sourceAnswerPages":[701,702],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-238","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A builder contracted in writing to construct a small greenhouse on a homeowner's property for $20,000, payable upon completion. After the builder spent $9,000 framing the greenhouse and an additional $1,000 for materials not yet incorporated into the greenhouse, the homeowner wrongfully ordered the builder to stop work. The builder then resold the unused materials that he had already purchased for the greenhouse to another contractor for $1,000. At the time the homeowner stopped the work, it would have cost the builder an additional $5,000 to complete the project. The partially built greenhouse increased the value of the homeowner's property by $3,000. In a suit by the builder against the homeowner, how much is the builder likely to recover?","choices":{"A":"$3,000, the increase in the value of the homeowner's property.","B":"$10,000, the total cost expended by the builder at the time of the breach.","C":"$14,000, the total cost expended by the builder ($10,000) plus the builder's expected profit ($5,000), minus the loss avoided by the resale of the unused materials ($1,000).","D":"$15,000, the contract price ($20,000) minus the costs saved by the breach ($5,000)."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nContract damages are meant to compensate the nonbreaching party by putting the party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. For example, if an owner breaches a construction contract during construction, the builder can recover any costs incurred up to that point plus the profits it would have earned. However, any costs the builder avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award. Here, the builder had incurred $10,000 in costs ($9,000 completed frame + $1,000 in additional materials) at the time of breach. The builder lost an expected profit of $5,000 ($20,000 contract price − $10,000 costs − $5,000 cost to complete the greenhouse). But he was able to mitigate some of those costs by selling the additional materials for $1,000. Therefore, the builder is likely to recover $14,000, the total cost he expended ($10,000) plus his expected profit ($5,000), minus the loss avoided by the resale of the unused materials ($1,000) (Choices B & D). (Choice A) The builder is entitled to seek restitution for the value of the benefit conferred on the homeowner (ie, the $3,000 increase in property value) in lieu of the standard measure of damages. But the builder is unlikely to seek restitution since he can recover $14,000 under the contract instead.\n\nEducational objective: When an owner breaches a construction contract during construction, the builder is entitled to lost profits plus costs incurred. But any costs avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 344 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (purposes of remedies).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of damages in general).","educationalObjective":"When an owner breaches a construction contract during construction, the builder is entitled to lost profits plus costs incurred. But any costs avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 344 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (purposes of remedies).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of damages in general).","rule":"When an owner breaches a construction contract during construction, the builder is entitled to lost profits plus costs incurred. But any costs avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award.","irac":{"issue":"In a suit by the builder against the homeowner, how much is the builder likely to recover?","rule":"When an owner breaches a construction contract during construction, the builder is entitled to lost profits plus costs incurred. But any costs avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award.","analysis":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nContract damages are meant to compensate the nonbreaching party by putting the party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. For example, if an owner breaches a construction contract during construction, the builder can recover any costs incurred up to that point plus the profits it would have earned. However, any costs the builder avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award. Here, the builder had incurred $10,000 in costs ($9,000 completed frame + $1,000 in additional materials) at the time of breach. The builder lost an expected profit of $5,000 ($20,000 contract price − $10,000 costs − $5,000 cost to complete the greenhouse). But he was able to mitigate some of those costs by selling the additional materials for $1,000. Therefore, the builder is likely to recover $14,000, the total cost he expended ($10,000) plus his expected profit ($5,000), minus the loss avoided by the resale of the unused materials ($1,000) (Choices B & D). (Choice A) The builder is entitled to seek restitution for the value of the benefit conferred on the homeowner (ie, the $3,000 increase in property value) in lieu of the standard measure of damages. But the builder is unlikely to seek restitution since he can recover $14,000 under the contract instead.","conclusion":"Answer C: $14,000, the total cost expended by the builder ($10,000) plus the builder's expected profit ($5,000), minus the loss avoided by the resale of the unused materials ($1,000)."},"questionZh":"一家建筑商以书面合同签订合同，在房主的房产上建造一座小型温室，费用为 20,000 美元，完工后付款。建筑商花费了 9,000 美元建造温室，并额外花费了 1,000 美元购买尚未纳入温室的材料，房主错误地命令建筑商停止工作。然后，建筑商将他已经为温室购买的未使用的材料以 1,000 美元的价格转售给另一家承包商。当房主停止施工时，建筑商需要额外花费 5,000 美元才能完成该项目。部分建成的温室使房主的财产价值增加了​​ 3,000 美元。在建筑商对房主提起诉讼时，建筑商可能会获得多少赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"3,000美元，房主财产的价值增加。","B":"10,000 美元，即违规发生时建筑商花费的总成本。","C":"14,000 美元，建筑商支出的总成本（10,000 美元）加上建筑商的预期利润（5,000 美元），减去转售未使用材料所避免的损失（1,000 美元）。","D":"15,000 美元，合同价格（20,000 美元）减去因违约而节省的成本（5,000 美元）。"},"explanationZh":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n合同损害赔偿是指通过将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的地位来补偿非违约方。例如，如果业主在施工过程中违反施工合同，建筑商可以收回截至该时间点发生的任何成本以及其本应赚取的利润。然而，建筑商避免的任何成本（例如，通过缓解）将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。此处，建筑商在违规时已承担 10,000 美元的成本（9,000 美元的已完成框架 + 1,000 美元的额外材料）。建筑商损失了 5,000 美元的预期利润（20,000 美元合同价格 - 10,000 美元成本 - 完成温室的 5,000 美元成本）。但他以 1,000 美元的价格出售额外材料，从而降低了部分成本。因此，建筑商可能收回 14,000 美元，即他花费的总成本（10,000 美元）加上他的预期利润（5,000 美元），减去转售未使用的材料所避免的损失（1,000 美元）（Choices B & D）。 （选择 A） 建筑商有权要求赔偿房主所获得的利益价值（即财产价值增加 3,000 美元），以代替标准损害赔偿措施。但建筑商不太可能寻求赔偿，因为他可以根据合同收回 14,000 美元。\n\n教育目标：当业主在施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商有权获得利润损失和产生的成本。但任何避免的成本（例如，通过缓解）将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 344（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（补救措施的目的）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一般损害赔偿措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当业主在施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商有权获得利润损失和产生的成本。但任何避免的成本（例如，通过缓解）将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。","iracZh":{"issue":"在建筑商对房主提起诉讼时，建筑商可能会获得多少赔偿？","rule":"当业主在施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商有权获得利润损失和产生的成本。但任何避免的成本（例如，通过缓解）将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。","analysis":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n合同损害赔偿是指通过将非违约方置于与合同已履行相同的地位来补偿非违约方。例如，如果业主在施工过程中违反施工合同，建筑商可以收回截至该时间点发生的任何成本以及其本应赚取的利润。然而，建筑商避免的任何成本（例如，通过缓解）将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。此处，建筑商在违规时已承担 10,000 美元的成本（9,000 美元的已完成框架 + 1,000 美元的额外材料）。建筑商损失了 5,000 美元的预期利润（20,000 美元合同价格 - 10,000 美元成本 - 完成温室的 5,000 美元成本）。但他以 1,000 美元的价格出售额外材料，从而降低了部分成本。因此，建筑商可能收回 14,000 美元，即他花费的总成本（10,000 美元）加上他的预期利润（5,000 美元），减去转售未使用的材料所避免的损失（1,000 美元）（Choices B & D）。 （选择 A） 建筑商有权要求赔偿房主所获得的利益价值（即财产价值增加 3,000 美元），以代替标准损害赔偿措施。但建筑商不太可能寻求赔偿，因为他可以根据合同收回 14,000 美元。\n\n教育目标：当业主在施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商有权获得利润损失和产生的成本。但任何避免的成本（例如，通过缓解）将从总损害赔偿金中扣除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 344（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（补救措施的目的）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（一般损害赔偿措施）。","conclusion":"答案 C：14,000 美元，建筑商支出的总成本（10,000 美元）加上建筑商的预期利润（5,000 美元），减去转售未使用材料所避免的损失（1,000 美元）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"mitigation","ipa":"/mˌɪtɪɡˈeɪʃən/","syllables":"mit·i·ga·tion","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"n. 缓和, 减轻；[法] 减, 减轻, 缓和","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“mitigation”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"But any costs avoided (eg, by mitigation) will be subtracted from the total damages award.","synonyms":["moderation","extenuation","palliation"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":238,"sourceQuestionPage":703,"sourceQuestionPages":[703],"sourceExplanationPages":[704,705],"sourceAnswerPages":[704,705],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-239","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"The owner of a house and a real estate investor signed a writing in which the owner, \"in consideration of $100 to be paid to the owner by the investor,\" offered the investor the right to purchase the house for $100,000 within 30 days. The writing further provided, \"This offer will become effective as an option only if and when the $100 consideration is in fact paid.\" Two weeks later, the owner, having received no payment or other communication from the investor, sold and conveyed the house to the investor's business rival for $120,000. The following day, the owner received a letter from the investor enclosing a cashier's check for $100 payable to the owner and stating, \"I am hereby exercising my option to purchase the house and am prepared to close whenever you're ready.\" Assume that, for whatever reason, the investor prevails in a suit against the owner for breach of contract. Which of the following is the investor entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"$20,000, plus the amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the house on the date of the owner's breach exceeded $120,000.","B":"Nominal damages only, because the remedy of specific performance was not available to the investor.","C":"The amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the house on the date of the owner's breach exceeded $100,000.","D":"The fair market value, if any, of an assignable option to purchase the house for $100,000."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A buyer who enters a contract for the sale of land expects to receive the property (worth its fair market value) in exchange for the contract price. If the seller breaches by refusing to sell, the buyer can recover for loss of bargain (ie, lost expectation), which amounts to the property's fair market value on the date of the breach minus the contract price. Here, the real estate investor paid $100 for the option to buy the owner's house for $100,000. The investor exercised this option, and the owner breached the resulting land- sales contract since he had already sold the house to the business rival. Therefore, the investor is entitled to recover for his lost expectation, which is the amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the house on the date of the breach exceeded $100,000. (Choice A) Although the owner sold the house to the business rival for $20,000 more than the investor had agreed to pay, the investor cannot automatically recover that $20,000. This is because the investor's expectation is based on his own contract price—not the business rival's. (Choice B) Nominal damages (eg, $1) can be awarded for breach when there was no substantial loss—not because specific performance is unavailable. Here, the investor likely did suffer a substantial loss since, based on the business rival's $120,000 payment, the fair market value of the house likely exceeded $100,000. (Choice D) Options are generally assignable (ie, transferable). But the damages for breaching an option are equal to the lost expectation of the nonbreaching party—which is distinct from (and likely greater than) the fair market value of the assignable option itself.\n\nEducational objective: When a seller breaches a land-sales contract by refusing to sell the property to the buyer, the buyer can recover for loss of bargain—ie, the fair market value of the property on the date of the breach minus the contract price.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a nonbreaching party's lost expectation is equal to the loss in value to him/her caused by the other party's failure to perform).","educationalObjective":"When a seller breaches a land-sales contract by refusing to sell the property to the buyer, the buyer can recover for loss of bargain—ie, the fair market value of the property on the date of the breach minus the contract price.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a nonbreaching party's lost expectation is equal to the loss in value to him/her caused by the other party's failure to perform).","rule":"When a seller breaches a land-sales contract by refusing to sell the property to the buyer, the buyer can recover for loss of bargain—ie, the fair market value of the property on the date of the breach minus the contract price.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is the investor entitled to recover?","rule":"When a seller breaches a land-sales contract by refusing to sell the property to the buyer, the buyer can recover for loss of bargain—ie, the fair market value of the property on the date of the breach minus the contract price.","analysis":"A buyer who enters a contract for the sale of land expects to receive the property (worth its fair market value) in exchange for the contract price. If the seller breaches by refusing to sell, the buyer can recover for loss of bargain (ie, lost expectation), which amounts to the property's fair market value on the date of the breach minus the contract price. Here, the real estate investor paid $100 for the option to buy the owner's house for $100,000. The investor exercised this option, and the owner breached the resulting land- sales contract since he had already sold the house to the business rival. Therefore, the investor is entitled to recover for his lost expectation, which is the amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the house on the date of the breach exceeded $100,000. (Choice A) Although the owner sold the house to the business rival for $20,000 more than the investor had agreed to pay, the investor cannot automatically recover that $20,000. This is because the investor's expectation is based on his own contract price—not the business rival's. (Choice B) Nominal damages (eg, $1) can be awarded for breach when there was no substantial loss—not because specific performance is unavailable. Here, the investor likely did suffer a substantial loss since, based on the business rival's $120,000 payment, the fair market value of the house likely exceeded $100,000. (Choice D) Options are generally assignable (ie, transferable). But the damages for breaching an option are equal to the lost expectation of the nonbreaching party—which is distinct from (and likely greater than) the fair market value of the assignable option itself.","conclusion":"Answer C: The amount, if any, by which the fair market value of the house on the date of the owner's breach exceeded $100,000."},"questionZh":"一栋房屋的业主和一位房地产投资者签署了一份书面文件，其中业主“以投资者向业主支付的 100 美元为代价”，向投资者提供在 30 天内以 10 万美元购买该房屋的权利。该文进一步指出，“只有当 100 美元的对价确实支付时，此要约才会作为一种选择生效。”两周后，业主在没有收到投资者的任何付款或其他信息的情况下，以 12 万美元的价格将房子出售并转让给投资者的商业竞争对手。第二天，业主收到了投资者的一封信，其中附有一张应付给业主的 100 美元银行本票，并写道：“我特此行使我的选择权来购买这所房子，并准备在您准备好时关闭。”假设无论出于何种原因，投资者在针对业主违反合同的诉讼中胜诉。投资者有权追回以下哪一项？","choicesZh":{"A":"20,000 美元，加上业主违约之日房屋公平市场价值超过 120,000 美元的金额（如有）。","B":"仅名义损害赔偿，因为投资者无法获得具体履行的补救措施。","C":"在业主违约之日房屋的公平市场价值超过 100,000 美元的金额（如果有）。","D":"以 100,000 美元购买房屋的可转让选择权的公平市场价值（如果有）。"},"explanationZh":"签订土地销售合同的买方期望以合同价格换取房产（价值其公平市场价值）。如果卖方因拒绝出售而违约，买方可以获得讨价还价损失（即预期损失）的赔偿，金额相当于违约之日房产的公平市场价值减去合同价格。在这里，房地产投资者支付了 100 美元，购买了以 100,000 美元购买业主房屋的期权。投资者行使了这一选择权，而业主则违反了由此产生的土地出售合同，因为他已经将房子卖给了商业竞争对手。因此，投资者有权赔偿其损失的预期，即违约之日房屋公平市场价值超过 100,000 美元的金额（如果有）。 （选项A）虽然业主以比投资者同意支付的价格高出20,000美元的价格将房屋卖给商业竞争对手，但投资者不能自动收回这20,000美元。这是因为投资者的预期是基于他自己的合同价格，而不是商业竞争对手的合同价格。 （选择B）如果没有造成重大损失，而不是因为无法获得具体履行，则可以对违规行为判以名义损害赔偿（例如，1 美元）。在这里，投资者可能确实遭受了重大损失，因为根据商业竞争对手支付的 120,000 美元，房子的公平市场价值可能超过 100,000 美元。 （选项D） 期权通常是可转让的（即可转让的）。但违反期权的损失等于非违约方损失的期望，这与（并且可能大于）可转让期权本身的公平市场价值不同。\n\n教育目标：当卖方因拒绝将房产出售给买方而违反土地销售合同时，买方可以获得讨价还价损失的赔偿，即违约之日房产的公平市场价值减去合同价格。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，非违约方的期望损失等于另一方未能履约对他/她造成的价值损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当卖方因拒绝将房产出售给买方而违反土地销售合同时，买方可以获得讨价还价损失的赔偿，即违约之日房产的公平市场价值减去合同价格。","iracZh":{"issue":"投资者有权追回以下哪一项？","rule":"当卖方因拒绝将房产出售给买方而违反土地销售合同时，买方可以获得讨价还价损失的赔偿，即违约之日房产的公平市场价值减去合同价格。","analysis":"签订土地销售合同的买方期望以合同价格换取房产（价值其公平市场价值）。如果卖方因拒绝出售而违约，买方可以获得讨价还价损失（即预期损失）的赔偿，金额相当于违约之日房产的公平市场价值减去合同价格。在这里，房地产投资者支付了 100 美元，购买了以 100,000 美元购买业主房屋的期权。投资者行使了这一选择权，而业主则违反了由此产生的土地出售合同，因为他已经将房子卖给了商业竞争对手。因此，投资者有权赔偿其损失的预期，即违约之日房屋公平市场价值超过 100,000 美元的金额（如果有）。 （选项A）虽然业主以比投资者同意支付的价格高出20,000美元的价格将房屋卖给商业竞争对手，但投资者不能自动收回这20,000美元。这是因为投资者的预期是基于他自己的合同价格，而不是商业竞争对手的合同价格。 （选择B）如果没有造成重大损失，而不是因为无法获得具体履行，则可以对违规行为判以名义损害赔偿（例如，1 美元）。在这里，投资者可能确实遭受了重大损失，因为根据商业竞争对手支付的 120,000 美元，房子的公平市场价值可能超过 100,000 美元。 （选项D） 期权通常是可转让的（即可转让的）。但违反期权的损失等于非违约方损失的期望，这与（并且可能大于）可转让期权本身的公平市场价值不同。\n\n教育目标：当卖方因拒绝将房产出售给买方而违反土地销售合同时，买方可以获得讨价还价损失的赔偿，即违约之日房产的公平市场价值减去合同价格。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，非违约方的期望损失等于另一方未能履约对他/她造成的价值损失）。","conclusion":"答案 C：在业主违约之日房屋的公平市场价值超过 100,000 美元的金额（如果有）。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"assignable","ipa":"/ə'sainəbl/","syllables":"as·sign·a·ble","stress":"单音节或词典未标重音","commonMeaning":"a. 可分配的, 可指定的, 可归属的；[经] 可以过户, 可分配的","contextualMeaning":"本题中指：本题合同法语境中的“assignable”","wordForm":"词","sourceSentence":"The fair market value, if any, of an assignable option to purchase the house for $100,000.","synonyms":["conveyable","negotiable","transferable","transferrable"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-22efbd312615b9be"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":239,"sourceQuestionPage":706,"sourceQuestionPages":[706],"sourceExplanationPages":[707,708],"sourceAnswerPages":[707,708],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-240","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A buyer agreed in writing to purchase a car from a seller for $15,000, with the price to be paid on a specified date at the seller's home. The contract provided, and both parties intended, that time was of the essence. Before the specified date, however, the seller sold the car to a third party for $18,000. On the specified date, the buyer arrived at the seller's home prepared to tender payment. The seller was not there, so the buyer called the seller to ask where he was. The seller then told the buyer that he had sold the car to the third party. If the buyer sues the seller for breach of contract, will the buyer be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer did not tender her performance on the specified date.","B":"No, because the contractual obligations were discharged on the ground of impossibility.","C":"Yes, because the seller anticipatorily repudiated the contract when he sold the car to the third party.","D":"Yes, because the seller did not inform the buyer of his repudiation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A contract is breached when a party fails to perform contractual duties at the time performance is due. A breach can also occur before the time for performance arises or elapses under the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation. This doctrine applies when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or actions, indicates an unwillingness to perform.* Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party can suspend his/her own performance and either: treat the repudiation as a breach and sue immediately or ignore the repudiation and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the seller clearly and unequivocally repudiated the contract with the buyer by selling the car to a third party. Since the buyer has the right to treat this anticipatory repudiation as a breach of contract, she will likely prevail in a suit against the seller. *If a party's words or actions provide reasonable grounds for insecurity about the party's performance, the other party may demand adequate assurances. (Choice A) The buyer arrived at the seller's home on the specified date to tender payment. However, since the seller had anticipatorily repudiated the contract, the buyer's duty to tender performance was discharged. (Choice B) Performance is discharged due to impossibility when an unexpected and extraordinary event beyond the parties' control makes performance impossible. But since the seller's own actions made it impossible to sell the car, his contractual obligations were not discharged. (Choice D) The seller did not need to inform the buyer of his repudiation because an anticipatory repudiation can be express or implied. It is express if the repudiating party informs the other of his/her intention not to perform and is implied if the repudiating party's actions prevent him/her from being able to perform (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: Anticipatory repudiation occurs when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally, by words or actions, indicates that he/she will not perform. Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party can suspend his/her own performance and either (1) treat the repudiation as a breach and sue or (2) ignore it and demand performance.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"Anticipatory repudiation occurs when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally, by words or actions, indicates that he/she will not perform. Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party can suspend his/her own performance and either (1) treat the repudiation as a breach and sue or (2) ignore it and demand performance.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"Anticipatory repudiation occurs when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally, by words or actions, indicates that he/she will not perform. Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party can suspend his/her own performance and either (1) treat the repudiation as a breach and sue or (2) ignore it and demand performance.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues the seller for breach of contract, will the buyer be likely to prevail?","rule":"Anticipatory repudiation occurs when a contracting party clearly and unequivocally, by words or actions, indicates that he/she will not perform. Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party can suspend his/her own performance and either (1) treat the repudiation as a breach and sue or (2) ignore it and demand performance.","analysis":"A contract is breached when a party fails to perform contractual duties at the time performance is due. A breach can also occur before the time for performance arises or elapses under the doctrine of anticipatory repudiation. This doctrine applies when a party clearly and unequivocally, by words or actions, indicates an unwillingness to perform.* Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party can suspend his/her own performance and either: treat the repudiation as a breach and sue immediately or ignore the repudiation and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the seller clearly and unequivocally repudiated the contract with the buyer by selling the car to a third party. Since the buyer has the right to treat this anticipatory repudiation as a breach of contract, she will likely prevail in a suit against the seller. *If a party's words or actions provide reasonable grounds for insecurity about the party's performance, the other party may demand adequate assurances. (Choice A) The buyer arrived at the seller's home on the specified date to tender payment. However, since the seller had anticipatorily repudiated the contract, the buyer's duty to tender performance was discharged. (Choice B) Performance is discharged due to impossibility when an unexpected and extraordinary event beyond the parties' control makes performance impossible. But since the seller's own actions made it impossible to sell the car, his contractual obligations were not discharged. (Choice D) The seller did not need to inform the buyer of his repudiation because an anticipatory repudiation can be express or implied. It is express if the repudiating party informs the other of his/her intention not to perform and is implied if the repudiating party's actions prevent him/her from being able to perform (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the seller anticipatorily repudiated the contract when he sold the car to the third party."},"questionZh":"买方以书面形式同意以 15,000 美元的价格从卖方购买一辆汽车，并在指定日期在卖方家中支付货款。合同规定，而且双方都有意这样做，时间至关重要。然而，在指定日期之前，卖家以 18,000 美元的价格将汽车卖给了第三方。在指定日期，买方到达卖方家中准备付款。卖家不在，所以买家打电话给卖家询问他在哪里。卖方随后告诉买方，他已将汽车卖给第三方。如果买方起诉卖方违约，买方胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为买方没有在指定日期履行履约义务。","B":"不可以，因为合同义务因不可能而被解除。","C":"是的，因为卖方在将汽车卖给第三方时就预见到了拒绝履行合同的情况。","D":"是的，因为卖家没有通知买家他的拒付。"},"explanationZh":"当一方未能在到期时履行合同义务时，即构成合同违约。根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，违约也可能发生在履行时间出现或过去之前。当一方通过言语或行动明确无误地表明不愿意履行时，该原则适用。*一旦拒绝履行，非毁约方可以暂停自己的履行，并且可以：将拒绝视为违约并立即提起诉讼，或者忽略拒绝并要求根据合同履行。在此，卖方通过将汽车出售给第三方，明确无误地拒绝与买方签订的合同。由于买方有权将这种预期拒付视为违约行为，因此她很可能会在针对卖方的诉讼中胜诉。 *如果一方的言论或行为为该方的表现提供了不安全感的合理理由，另一方可以要求充分的保证。 （选项A）买方在指定日期到达卖方家中付款。然而，由于卖方预先拒绝履行合同，买方的履约义务被解除。 （选择B）当超出双方控制范围的意外和非常事件导致无法履行时，因无法履行而解除履行。但由于卖方自己的行为导致汽车无法出售，因此其合同义务并未解除。 （选项D） 卖方无需将其拒绝履行通知买方，因为预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行可以是明示的，也可以是暗示的。如果拒绝方告知对方他/她不履行合同的意图，则这是明示的；如果拒绝方的行为阻止他/她无法履行合同，则这是暗示的（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当缔约方通过言语或行动明确无误地表明他/她不会履行义务时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。一旦预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，非毁约方可以暂停自己的履行，并且 (1) 将拒绝视为违约并提起诉讼，或 (2) 忽略它并要求履行。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当缔约方通过言语或行动明确无误地表明他/她不会履约时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。一旦预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，非毁约方可以暂停自己的履行，并且 (1) 将拒绝视为违约并提起诉讼，或 (2) 忽略它并要求履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买方起诉卖方违约，买方胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"当缔约方通过言语或行动明确无误地表明他/她不会履约时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。一旦预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，非毁约方可以暂停自己的履行，并且 (1) 将拒绝视为违约并提起诉讼，或 (2) 忽略它并要求履行。","analysis":"当一方未能在到期时履行合同义务时，即构成合同违约。根据预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则，违约也可能发生在履行时间出现或过去之前。当一方通过言语或行动明确无误地表明不愿意履行时，该原则适用。*一旦拒绝履行，非毁约方可以暂停自己的履行，并且可以：将拒绝视为违约并立即提起诉讼，或者忽略拒绝并要求根据合同履行。在此，卖方通过将汽车出售给第三方，明确无误地拒绝与买方签订的合同。由于买方有权将这种预期拒付视为违约行为，因此她很可能会在针对卖方的诉讼中胜诉。 *如果一方的言论或行为为该方的表现提供了不安全感的合理理由，另一方可以要求充分的保证。 （选项A）买方在指定日期到达卖方家中付款。然而，由于卖方预先拒绝履行合同，买方的履约义务被解除。 （选择B）当超出双方控制范围的意外和非常事件导致无法履行时，因无法履行而解除履行。但由于卖方自己的行为导致汽车无法出售，因此其合同义务并未解除。 （选项D） 卖方无需将其拒绝履行通知买方，因为预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行可以是明示的，也可以是暗示的。如果拒绝方告知对方他/她不履行合同的意图，则这是明示的；如果拒绝方的行为阻止他/她无法履行合同，则这是暗示的（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当缔约方通过言语或行动明确无误地表明他/她不会履行义务时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。一旦预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行，非毁约方可以暂停自己的履行，并且 (1) 将拒绝视为违约并提起诉讼，或 (2) 忽略它并要求履行。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为卖方在将汽车卖给第三方时就预见到了拒绝履行合同的情况。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6092dc1e72c5f076"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":240,"sourceQuestionPage":709,"sourceQuestionPages":[709],"sourceExplanationPages":[710,711],"sourceAnswerPages":[710,711],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-241","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"The manager of a state fair contracted with a renowned hog breeder to exhibit the breeder's only world-champion hog for the three weeks of the annual fair. At the conclusion of the fair the breeder would receive $300. Two days before the opening of the fair, the hog became sick with a communicable disease among swine and, under the applicable state quarantine law, could not be exhibited for at least a month. Upon learning this, which of the following courses of action can the manager legally pursue with respect to his contract with the breeder?","choices":{"A":"Suspend his own performance and recover damages from the breeder for breach of contract unless the breeder at once supplies a healthy hog of exhibition quality as a substitute for the world-champion hog.","B":"Suspend his own performance, demand assurances from the breeder, and treat a failure by the breeder to produce the hog as an actionable repudiation.","C":"Terminate his own performance and treat the hog's illness as discharging all remaining duties under the contract.","D":"Terminate the contract, but only if the manager promptly seeks to obtain for the exhibit a suitable substitute for the world-champion hog from another hog owner."},"answer":"C","explanation":"When an unanticipated event makes a party's contractual duties impracticable to perform, those duties will be discharged if: nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Here, the manager contracted to exhibit the breeder's only world-champion hog at the annual state fair. Two days before the fair opened, the hog became sick. That unanticipated event rendered performance impracticable since the hog could not be exhibited for at least a month under state law. And since the hog's ability to be exhibited was a basic assumption of the contract and the manager was not at fault, the manager can terminate his own performance and treat the hog's illness as discharging all remaining duties under the contract. (Choice A) If possible, a party must render a reasonable substitute performance in place of the impracticable part. But a healthy hog of exhibition quality is not a reasonable substitute for the breeder's only world-champion hog. (Choice B) If reasonable grounds arise to believe that a party will not perform because of impracticability, the other party cannot demand assurances of performance and treat a failure to give them as a repudiation. That is because the prospective nonperformance would not be a breach. (Choice D) The manager's right to terminate his performance under the contract does not depend on whether he first seeks to obtain a suitable substitute for the world-champion hog from another hog owner.\n\nEducational objective: When an unanticipated event makes a party's contractual duties impracticable to perform, those duties will be discharged if (1) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the party seeking discharge is not at fault.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (impracticability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 278 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (substituted performance).","educationalObjective":"When an unanticipated event makes a party's contractual duties impracticable to perform, those duties will be discharged if (1) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the party seeking discharge is not at fault.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (impracticability).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 278 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (substituted performance).","rule":"When an unanticipated event makes a party's contractual duties impracticable to perform, those duties will be discharged if (1) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the party seeking discharge is not at fault.","irac":{"issue":"Upon learning this, which of the following courses of action can the manager legally pursue with respect to his contract with the breeder?","rule":"When an unanticipated event makes a party's contractual duties impracticable to perform, those duties will be discharged if (1) nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and (2) the party seeking discharge is not at fault.","analysis":"When an unanticipated event makes a party's contractual duties impracticable to perform, those duties will be discharged if: nonoccurrence of the event was a basic assumption of the contract and the party seeking discharge is not at fault. Here, the manager contracted to exhibit the breeder's only world-champion hog at the annual state fair. Two days before the fair opened, the hog became sick. That unanticipated event rendered performance impracticable since the hog could not be exhibited for at least a month under state law. And since the hog's ability to be exhibited was a basic assumption of the contract and the manager was not at fault, the manager can terminate his own performance and treat the hog's illness as discharging all remaining duties under the contract. (Choice A) If possible, a party must render a reasonable substitute performance in place of the impracticable part. But a healthy hog of exhibition quality is not a reasonable substitute for the breeder's only world-champion hog. (Choice B) If reasonable grounds arise to believe that a party will not perform because of impracticability, the other party cannot demand assurances of performance and treat a failure to give them as a repudiation. That is because the prospective nonperformance would not be a breach. (Choice D) The manager's right to terminate his performance under the contract does not depend on whether he first seeks to obtain a suitable substitute for the world-champion hog from another hog owner.","conclusion":"Answer C: Terminate his own performance and treat the hog's illness as discharging all remaining duties under the contract."},"questionZh":"州博览会的经理与一位著名的生猪饲养员签订了合同，在年度博览会的三周内展示该饲养员唯一的世界冠军生猪。展会结束时，饲养员将获得 300 美元。展会开幕前两天，这头生猪感染了猪传染性疾病，根据国家检疫法，至少一个月内不能展出。了解这一情况后，经理可以就其与饲养员的合同合法地采取以下哪些行动？","choicesZh":{"A":"除非饲养员立即提供一头达到展览质量的健康生猪作为世界冠军生猪的替代品，否则暂停其表演并向饲养员追偿违约损失。","B":"暂停他自己的表现，要求饲养者做出保证，并将饲养者未能生产生猪视为可起诉的拒绝。","C":"终止自己的履约，并将生猪的疾病视为履行了合同项下的所有剩余义务。","D":"终止合同，但前提是经理立即寻求从另一位生猪所有者那里获得世界冠军生猪的合适替代品。"},"explanationZh":"当意外事件导致一方的合同义务无法履行时，在以下情况下，这些义务将被解除：该事件的不发生是合同的基本假设，并且寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。在这里，经理签约在年度州博览会上展示饲养员唯一的世界冠军生猪。集市开幕前两天，那头猪生病了。这一意外事件使得表演变得商业上不可行，因为根据州法律，这头生猪至少一个月不能展示。而且，由于生猪的表现能力是合同的基本假设，且管理者并无过错，因此管理者可以终止自己的表现，并将生猪的患病视为履行了合同项下的所有剩余义务。 （选择A）如果可能，一方必须提供合理的替代履行来代替不可行的部分。但一头达到展览品质的健康生猪并不能合理替代饲养者唯一的世界冠军生猪。 （选择B）如果有合理的理由相信一方因不可行而不会履行，另一方不能要求履行保证并将不提供履行的保证视为拒绝履行。这是因为预期的不履行行为不会构成违约。 （选项D） 经理根据合同终止其履约的权利并不取决于他是否首先寻求从另一个猪主那里获得世界冠军生猪的合适替代品。\n\n教育目标：当意外事件导致一方的合同义务无法履行时，如果 (1) 事件未发生是合同的基本假设，并且 (2) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错，则这些义务将被解除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不切实际）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 278（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（替代履行）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当意外事件导致一方的合同义务无法履行时，如果 (1) 该事件的不发生是合同的基本假设，并且 (2) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错，则这些义务将被解除。","iracZh":{"issue":"了解这一情况后，经理可以就其与饲养员的合同合法地采取以下哪些行动？","rule":"当意外事件导致一方的合同义务无法履行时，如果 (1) 该事件的不发生是合同的基本假设，并且 (2) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错，则这些义务将被解除。","analysis":"当意外事件导致一方的合同义务无法履行时，在以下情况下，这些义务将被解除：该事件的不发生是合同的基本假设，并且寻求解除合同的一方没有过错。在这里，经理签约在年度州博览会上展示饲养员唯一的世界冠军生猪。集市开幕前两天，那头猪生病了。这一意外事件使得表演变得商业上不可行，因为根据州法律，这头生猪至少一个月不能展示。而且，由于生猪的表现能力是合同的基本假设，且管理者并无过错，因此管理者可以终止自己的表现，并将生猪的患病视为履行了合同项下的所有剩余义务。 （选择A）如果可能，一方必须提供合理的替代履行来代替不可行的部分。但一头达到展览品质的健康生猪并不能合理替代饲养者唯一的世界冠军生猪。 （选择B）如果有合理的理由相信一方因不可行而不会履行，另一方不能要求履行保证并将不提供履行的保证视为拒绝履行。这是因为预期的不履行行为不会构成违约。 （选项D） 经理根据合同终止其履约的权利并不取决于他是否首先寻求从另一个猪主那里获得世界冠军生猪的合适替代品。\n\n教育目标：当意外事件导致一方的合同义务无法履行时，如果 (1) 事件未发生是合同的基本假设，并且 (2) 寻求解除合同的一方没有过错，则这些义务将被解除。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不切实际）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 278（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（替代履行）。","conclusion":"答案 C：终止自己的履约，并将生猪的疾病视为履行了合同项下的所有剩余义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-b49877b73fd004a7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":241,"sourceQuestionPage":712,"sourceQuestionPages":[712],"sourceExplanationPages":[713,714],"sourceAnswerPages":[713,714],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-242","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Impossibility, Impracticability & Frustration","question":"A businesswoman entered into a contract with a painter by the terms of which he was to paint the businesswoman's office for $1,000 and was required to do all the work over the following weekend so as to avoid disruption of her business. The painter commenced work on Saturday morning and had finished half the painting by the time he quit work for the day. That night, without the fault of either party, the office building was destroyed by fire. Which of the following is an accurate statement?","choices":{"A":"Both parties' contractual duties are discharged, and the painter can recover nothing from the businesswoman.","B":"Both parties' contractual duties are discharged, but the painter can recover in quasi- contract from the businesswoman.","C":"Only the painter's contractual duty is discharged, and the painter can recover his reliance damages from the businesswoman.","D":"Only the painter's contractual duty is discharged, because the businesswoman's performance (payment of the agreed price) is not impossible."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nThe occurrence of an unexpected or extraordinary event results in the rescission (ie, cancellation) of a contract if: that event (1) makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform their duties or (2) frustrates the purpose of the contract the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. Rescission discharges both parties' duties, so they cannot sue on the contract (eg, for breach). However, a party can still recover in quasi-contract for the value of any work done prior to rescission. Here, the painter contracted to paint the businesswoman's office. After one day of painting, the office building was destroyed by fire, which made it impossible for the painter to finish and frustrated the purpose of the contract. A basic assumption of the contract was that the building would not be destroyed. And since neither party was at fault, both parties' contractual duties are discharged. However, the painter can still recover in quasi-contract from the businesswoman for finishing half the painting before the fire (Choice A). (Choices C & D) Although the businesswoman could still pay the agreed price, the impossibility of the painter's duty discharges the duties of both parties. And although the painter could recover reliance damages (ie, costs) under a theory of promissory estoppel, he may be able to recover a larger amount under a theory of quasi-contract, which compensates for costs and expected profits.\n\nEducational objective: The duties of both parties to a contract are discharged if (1) an unexpected event makes it impossible for one or both parties to perform their duties or frustrates the purpose of the\n\ncontract, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 237, 267 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an unexpected event can discharge a party's duty due to impossibility or impracticability, which then discharges the other party's duty).","educationalObjective":"The duties of both parties to a contract are discharged if (1) an unexpected event makes it impossible for one or both parties to perform their duties or frustrates the purpose of the\n\ncontract, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 237, 267 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an unexpected event can discharge a party's duty due to impossibility or impracticability, which then discharges the other party's duty).","rule":"The duties of both parties to a contract are discharged if (1) an unexpected event makes it impossible for one or both parties to perform their duties or frustrates the purpose of the\n\ncontract, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is an accurate statement?","rule":"The duties of both parties to a contract are discharged if (1) an unexpected event makes it impossible for one or both parties to perform their duties or frustrates the purpose of the\n\ncontract, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nThe occurrence of an unexpected or extraordinary event results in the rescission (ie, cancellation) of a contract if: that event (1) makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform their duties or (2) frustrates the purpose of the contract the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. Rescission discharges both parties' duties, so they cannot sue on the contract (eg, for breach). However, a party can still recover in quasi-contract for the value of any work done prior to rescission. Here, the painter contracted to paint the businesswoman's office. After one day of painting, the office building was destroyed by fire, which made it impossible for the painter to finish and frustrated the purpose of the contract. A basic assumption of the contract was that the building would not be destroyed. And since neither party was at fault, both parties' contractual duties are discharged. However, the painter can still recover in quasi-contract from the businesswoman for finishing half the painting before the fire (Choice A). (Choices C & D) Although the businesswoman could still pay the agreed price, the impossibility of the painter's duty discharges the duties of both parties. And although the painter could recover reliance damages (ie, costs) under a theory of promissory estoppel, he may be able to recover a larger amount under a theory of quasi-contract, which compensates for costs and expected profits.","conclusion":"Answer B: Both parties' contractual duties are discharged, but the painter can recover in quasi- contract from the businesswoman."},"questionZh":"一名女商人与一名油漆工签订了一份合同，合同条款规定油漆工以 1,000 美元的价格粉刷该女商人的办公室，并要求在接下来的周末完成所有工作，以免影响她的生意。画家于周六早上开始工作，到下班时已经完成了一半的画作。当晚，在没有任何一方过错的情况下，办公楼被大火烧毁。以下哪项是准确的说法？","choicesZh":{"A":"双方的合同义务均已解除，油漆工无法向女商人收回任何款项。","B":"双方的合同义务均已解除，但油漆工可以以准合同形式向女商人追偿。","C":"只有油漆工的合同义务得到履行，油漆工才能向女商人追回信赖损失。","D":"只有油漆工的合同义务被解除，因为女商人的履行（支付约定的价格）并非不可能。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n如果发生意外或非常事件，则导致合同解除（即取消）：该事件 (1) 使一方或双方履行其职责不可能或不可行，或 (2) 妨碍合同目的 合同是在该事件不会发生且双方均无过错导致该事件发生的基本假设下成立的。解除合同解除了双方的义务，因此他们不能就合同提起诉讼（例如违约）。然而，一方仍然可以在准合同中收回解除前已完成的任何工作的价值。在这里，油漆工承包了为女商人的办公室粉刷油漆。粉刷一天后，办公楼被大火烧毁，导致油漆工无法完成，合同目的落空。合同的一个基本假设是建筑物不会被摧毁。由于双方均无过错，双方的合同义务均已解除。然而，画家仍然可以通过准合同从女商人那里获得在火灾发生前完成一半画作的补偿（选项A）。 （选项C和D）虽然女商人仍然可以支付约定的价格，但油漆工的义务的不可能解除了双方的义务。尽管画家可以根据承诺禁止反悔理论追回信赖损害（即成本），但根据准合同理论，他可能能够追回更大的金额，从而补偿成本和预期利润。\n\n教育目标：如果出现以下情况，则合同双方的义务均被解除：(1) 意外事件导致一方或双方无法履行其职责或妨碍合同目的的实现\n\n合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的，并且 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 237, 267（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，意外事件可以因不可能或不切实际而解除一方的义务，从而解除另一方的义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"下列情形之一的，合同双方的义务均被解除：（1）不可预见的事件导致一方或者双方无法履行其义务或者妨碍合同目的的实现的。\n\n合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的，并且 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项是准确的说法？","rule":"下列情形之一的，合同双方的义务均被解除：（1）不可预见的事件导致一方或者双方无法履行其义务或者妨碍合同目的的实现的。\n\n合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的，并且 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n如果发生意外或非常事件，则导致合同解除（即取消）：该事件 (1) 使一方或双方履行其职责不可能或不可行，或 (2) 妨碍合同目的 合同是在该事件不会发生且双方均无过错导致该事件发生的基本假设下成立的。解除合同解除了双方的义务，因此他们不能就合同提起诉讼（例如违约）。然而，一方仍然可以在准合同中收回解除前已完成的任何工作的价值。在这里，油漆工承包了为女商人的办公室粉刷油漆。粉刷一天后，办公楼被大火烧毁，导致油漆工无法完成，合同目的落空。合同的一个基本假设是建筑物不会被摧毁。由于双方均无过错，双方的合同义务均已解除。然而，画家仍然可以通过准合同从女商人那里获得在火灾发生前完成一半画作的补偿（选项A）。 （选项C和D）虽然女商人仍然可以支付约定的价格，但油漆工的义务的不可能解除了双方的义务。尽管画家可以根据承诺禁止反悔理论追回信赖损害（即成本），但根据准合同理论，他可能能够追回更大的金额，从而补偿成本和预期利润。\n\n教育目标：如果出现以下情况，则合同双方的义务均被解除：(1) 意外事件导致一方或双方无法履行其职责或妨碍合同目的的实现\n\n合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的，并且 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 237, 267（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，意外事件可以因不可能或不切实际而解除一方的义务，从而解除另一方的义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：双方的合同义务均已解除，但油漆工可以以准合同形式向女商人追偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":242,"sourceQuestionPage":715,"sourceQuestionPages":[715],"sourceExplanationPages":[716,717],"sourceAnswerPages":[716,717],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-243","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"On April 1, a buyer agreed to purchase an electric motor from a seller for $1,000, delivery to be made on the following June 1. Before signing the agreement, the parties orally agreed that the contract of sale would be effective only if the buyer should notify the seller in writing not later than May 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the motor to a third party. On April 15, the buyer entered into a contract with a third party to resell the motor to the third party at a profit. On April 16, the buyer notified the seller by phone of the buyer's resale agreement with the third party and explained that a written notice was unfeasible, because the buyer's secretary was ill. The seller replied, \"That's okay. I'll get the motor to you on June 1, as we agreed.\" Having learned, however, that the motor had increased in value about 75% since April 1, the seller renounced the agreement on June 1. If the buyer sues the seller on June 2 for breach of contract, which of the following concepts best supports the buyer's claim?","choices":{"A":"Nonoccurrence of a condition subsequent.","B":"Novation of buyers.","C":"Substantial performance.","D":"Waiver of condition."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA party to a contract may avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due—has not occurred, and the nonoccurrence is not excused. Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.* Here, the parties orally agreed that their written contract would be effective only if the buyer gave the seller written notice by May 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the electric motor (condition precedent). Although the buyer notified the seller about the resale agreement by phone on April 16 (nonoccurrence), the seller said that oral notice was \"okay\" and promised to deliver the motor on June 1 as agreed (waiver). Since the seller waived the condition, both parties were obligated to perform. As a result, waiver best supports the buyer's breach-of-contract claim. *The waiving party may retract the waiver unless the other party has detrimentally relied on it. (Choice A) The notice requirement was a condition precedent—not a condition subsequent, which discharges a party's duty to perform once the specified event occurs. (Choice B) A novation is a substitute contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party (not seen here). (Choice C) Where an agreement makes performance contingent upon a condition precedent (eg, written notice of the resale), substantial performance is not sufficient to overcome the condition's nonoccurrence.\n\nEducational objective: Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the contracting party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).","educationalObjective":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the contracting party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the contracting party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.","irac":{"issue":"If the buyer sues the seller on June 2 for breach of contract, which of the following concepts best supports the buyer's claim?","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the contracting party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.","analysis":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA party to a contract may avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before performance becomes due—has not occurred, and the nonoccurrence is not excused. Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.* Here, the parties orally agreed that their written contract would be effective only if the buyer gave the seller written notice by May 2 that the buyer had arranged to resell the electric motor (condition precedent). Although the buyer notified the seller about the resale agreement by phone on April 16 (nonoccurrence), the seller said that oral notice was \"okay\" and promised to deliver the motor on June 1 as agreed (waiver). Since the seller waived the condition, both parties were obligated to perform. As a result, waiver best supports the buyer's breach-of-contract claim. *The waiving party may retract the waiver unless the other party has detrimentally relied on it. (Choice A) The notice requirement was a condition precedent—not a condition subsequent, which discharges a party's duty to perform once the specified event occurs. (Choice B) A novation is a substitute contract in which the parties agree to replace an original contracting party with a new party (not seen here). (Choice C) Where an agreement makes performance contingent upon a condition precedent (eg, written notice of the resale), substantial performance is not sufficient to overcome the condition's nonoccurrence.","conclusion":"Answer D: Waiver of condition."},"questionZh":"4月1日，买方同意以1,000美元的价格向卖方购买一台电动机，并于次年6月1日交货。在签署协议之前，双方口头约定，只有买方在5月2日之前以书面形式通知卖方买方已安排将电动机转售给第三方，销售合同才有效。 4月15日，买方与第三方签订合同，将电机转售给第三方以获取利润。 4月16日，买方通过电话通知卖方买方与第三方签订的转售协议，并解释说书面通知不可行，因为买方的秘书生病了。卖家回复：“没关系，按照我们约定的，我会在6月1日把电机送给你。”然而，在得知该电机自 4 月 1 日以来价值已上涨约 75% 后，卖方于 6 月 1 日放弃了协议。如果买方于 6 月 2 日起诉卖方违约，以下哪个概念最能支持买方的主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"随后条件不再发生。","B":"买家更新。","C":"实质性的表现。","D":"条件豁免。"},"explanationZh":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n如果先决条件（即，在履行到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，并且不作为不发生的情况，合同一方可以避免履行合同。如果从该条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被免除。*在此，双方口头同意，只有买方在 5 月 2 日之前向卖方发出书面通知，表明买方已安排转售电动机（先决条件），他们的书面合同才有效。尽管买方于4月16日通过电话通知卖方转售协议（未发生），但卖方表示口头通知“可以”，并承诺按照约定于6月1日交付电机（弃权）。由于卖方放弃了该条件，双方都有义务履行。因此，弃权最能支持买方的违约索赔。 *弃权方可以撤回弃权，除非另一方不利地依赖弃权。 （选项 A）通知要求是一个先决条件，而不是后续条件，一旦指定事件发生，该条件就解除了一方履行义务的义务。 （选择 B） 更替是一种替代合同，其中双方同意用新的一方（此处未显示）取代原合同方。 （选择C）如果协议规定履约取决于先决条件（例如转售的书面通知），则实质性履约不足以克服该条件不发生的情况。\n\n教育目标：如果从某种条件中受益的缔约方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果受益于某一条件的缔约方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被免除。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果买方于 6 月 2 日起诉卖方违约，以下哪个概念最能支持买方的主张？","rule":"如果受益于某一条件的缔约方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被免除。","analysis":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n如果先决条件（即，在履行到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，并且不作为不发生的情况，合同一方可以避免履行合同。如果从该条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被免除。*在此，双方口头同意，只有买方在 5 月 2 日之前向卖方发出书面通知，表明买方已安排转售电动机（先决条件），他们的书面合同才有效。尽管买方于4月16日通过电话通知卖方转售协议（未发生），但卖方表示口头通知“可以”，并承诺按照约定于6月1日交付电机（弃权）。由于卖方放弃了该条件，双方都有义务履行。因此，弃权最能支持买方的违约索赔。 *弃权方可以撤回弃权，除非另一方不利地依赖弃权。 （选项 A）通知要求是一个先决条件，而不是后续条件，一旦指定事件发生，该条件就解除了一方履行义务的义务。 （选择 B） 更替是一种替代合同，其中双方同意用新的一方（此处未显示）取代原合同方。 （选择C）如果协议规定履约取决于先决条件（例如转售的书面通知），则实质性履约不足以克服该条件不发生的情况。\n\n教育目标：如果从某种条件中受益的缔约方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。","conclusion":"答案 D：条件豁免。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":243,"sourceQuestionPage":718,"sourceQuestionPages":[718],"sourceExplanationPages":[719,720],"sourceAnswerPages":[719,720],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-244","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A seller entered into an agreement to sell a machine to a buyer for $5,000. At the time of the order, the buyer gave the seller a down payment of $1,000. The buyer then built a foundation for the machine at a cost of $250. The seller failed to deliver the machine. The buyer made reasonable efforts to find a similar machine and bought one for $5,500 that did not fit on the foundation. The buyer sued the seller for breach of contract. Which of these amounts claimed by the buyer, if any, could best be described as restitution?","choices":{"A":"The $250 cost of the foundation.","B":"The $500 difference in price.","C":"The $1,000 down payment.","D":"None of the claimed amounts."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Restitutionary damages\n\nDefinition Damages awarded to restore to claimant the value of whatever benefit was conferred upon recipient Purpose Prevent unjust enrichment\n\nMeasure Reasonable value of work performed or services rendered (ie, cost of recipient obtaining comparable performance) If applicable, extent to which recipient's property has increased in value or recipient's other interests have been advanced Restitutionary damages are awarded to restore to a contracting party the value of any benefit conferred on the other party. Under the UCC, where the seller fails to deliver the promised goods or the buyer rightfully rejects the goods, the buyer is entitled to the return of any payment made on the goods as restitution. Here, the buyer paid the seller a $1,000 down payment for a machine that the seller failed to deliver. As a result, the $1,000 down payment is the amount that could best be described as restitution (Choice D). (Choices A & B) Neither the $250 cost of the foundation nor the $500 difference between the contract price of the seller's machine and the price of the substitute machine (ie, cost of cover) conferred a benefit on the seller (not restitution).\n\nEducational objective: Where a seller fails to deliver the promised goods or the buyer rightfully rejects them, the buyer is entitled to the return of any payment made on the goods as restitution. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 344 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (purpose of remedies).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's remedies in general).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-713 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's damages for nondelivery/repudiation).","educationalObjective":"Where a seller fails to deliver the promised goods or the buyer rightfully rejects them, the buyer is entitled to the return of any payment made on the goods as restitution. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 344 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (purpose of remedies).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's remedies in general).\n\nU.C.C. § 2-713 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (buyer's damages for nondelivery/repudiation).","rule":"Where a seller fails to deliver the promised goods or the buyer rightfully rejects them, the buyer is entitled to the return of any payment made on the goods as restitution. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","irac":{"issue":"Which of these amounts claimed by the buyer, if any, could best be described as restitution?","rule":"Where a seller fails to deliver the promised goods or the buyer rightfully rejects them, the buyer is entitled to the return of any payment made on the goods as restitution. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code","analysis":"Restitutionary damages\n\nDefinition Damages awarded to restore to claimant the value of whatever benefit was conferred upon recipient Purpose Prevent unjust enrichment\n\nMeasure Reasonable value of work performed or services rendered (ie, cost of recipient obtaining comparable performance) If applicable, extent to which recipient's property has increased in value or recipient's other interests have been advanced Restitutionary damages are awarded to restore to a contracting party the value of any benefit conferred on the other party. Under the UCC, where the seller fails to deliver the promised goods or the buyer rightfully rejects the goods, the buyer is entitled to the return of any payment made on the goods as restitution. Here, the buyer paid the seller a $1,000 down payment for a machine that the seller failed to deliver. As a result, the $1,000 down payment is the amount that could best be described as restitution (Choice D). (Choices A & B) Neither the $250 cost of the foundation nor the $500 difference between the contract price of the seller's machine and the price of the substitute machine (ie, cost of cover) conferred a benefit on the seller (not restitution).","conclusion":"Answer C: The $1,000 down payment."},"questionZh":"卖方签订了一份协议，以 5,000 美元的价格向买方出售一台机器。下订单时，买方向卖方支付了 1,000 美元的定金。然后买家花费 250 美元为机器打了一个地基。卖家未能交付机器。买家花了 5,500 美元购买了一台与地基不匹配的类似机器。买方起诉卖方违反合同。买方索赔的这些金额中，哪一项（如果有的话）最能被描述为赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"基金会的费用为 250 美元。","B":"价格相差500美元。","C":"1,000美元的首付。","D":"没有任何索赔金额。"},"explanationZh":"赔偿损失\n\n定义 为恢复索赔人所获得的任何利益的价值而授予的损害赔偿 目的 防止不当得利\n\n衡量所完成的工作或提供的服务的合理价值（即，接受者获得可比绩效的成本） 如果适用，接受者的财产增值或接受者的其他利益已增加的程度 判给返还利益赔偿，以恢复缔约方所获得的任何利益的价值。根据UCC，如果卖方未能交付承诺的货物或买方合理拒绝货物，买方有权要求退还货物支付的任何款项作为赔偿。在此，买方向卖方支付了 1,000 美元的定金，购买了一台卖方未能交付的机器。因此，1,000 美元的首付金额最能被描述为恢复原状（选项 D）。 （选项 A 和 B） 250 美元的基础成本和卖方机器的合同价格与替代机器价格之间的 500 美元差额（即保险成本）都没有给卖方带来好处（而不是恢复原状）。\n\n教育目标：如果卖方未能交付承诺的货物或买方合理拒绝货物，则买方有权要求退还货物的任何付款作为赔偿。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 344（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（补救措施的目的）。\n\nUCC § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的一般补救措施）。\n\nUCC § 2-713 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方因未交货/拒绝而造成的损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果卖方未能交付承诺的货物或买方合理拒绝货物，则买方有权要求退还货物支付的任何款项作为赔偿。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","iracZh":{"issue":"买方索赔的这些金额中，哪一项（如果有的话）最能被描述为赔偿？","rule":"如果卖方未能交付承诺的货物或买方合理拒绝货物，则买方有权要求退还货物支付的任何款项作为赔偿。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）","analysis":"赔偿损失\n\n定义 为恢复索赔人所获得的任何利益的价值而授予的损害赔偿 目的 防止不当得利\n\n衡量所完成的工作或提供的服务的合理价值（即，接受者获得可比绩效的成本） 如果适用，接受者的财产增值或接受者的其他利益已增加的程度 判给返还利益赔偿，以恢复缔约方所获得的任何利益的价值。根据UCC，如果卖方未能交付承诺的货物或买方合理拒绝货物，买方有权要求退还货物支付的任何款项作为赔偿。在此，买方向卖方支付了 1,000 美元的定金，购买了一台卖方未能交付的机器。因此，1,000 美元的首付金额最能被描述为恢复原状（选项 D）。 （选项 A 和 B） 250 美元的基础成本和卖方机器的合同价格与替代机器价格之间的 500 美元差额（即保险成本）都没有给卖方带来好处（而不是恢复原状）。\n\n教育目标：如果卖方未能交付承诺的货物或买方合理拒绝货物，则买方有权要求退还货物的任何付款作为赔偿。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 344（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（补救措施的目的）。\n\nUCC § 2-711 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方的一般补救措施）。\n\nUCC § 2-713 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（买方因未交货/拒绝而造成的损失）。","conclusion":"答案 C：1,000美元的首付。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":244,"sourceQuestionPage":721,"sourceQuestionPages":[721],"sourceExplanationPages":[722],"sourceAnswerPages":[722],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-245","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Mistake","question":"An employer offered to pay a terminated employee $50,000 to release all claims the employee might have against the employer. The employee orally accepted the offer. The employer then prepared an unsigned release agreement and sent it to the employee for him to sign. The employee carefully prepared, signed, and sent to the employer a substitute release agreement that was identical to the original except that it excluded from the release any age discrimination claims. The employer signed the substitute release without reading it. Shortly thereafter, the employee notified the employer that he intended to sue the employer for age discrimination. Is the employer likely to prevail in an action seeking reformation of the release to conform to the parties' oral agreement?","choices":{"A":"No, because the employer acted unreasonably by failing to read the substitute release prior to signing it.","B":"No, because the parol evidence rule will preclude evidence of the oral agreement.","C":"Yes, because the employee's fraudulent behavior induced the employer's unilateral mistake.","D":"Yes, because the parties were mutually mistaken regarding the contents of the signed release."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Availability of reformation as a remedy\n\nMisrepresentation One party makes untrue assertion of fact that is fraudulent or material Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation Mutual mistake Both parties are mistaken as to contract's contents or legal effect Unilateral mistake One party is mistaken because other party intentionally misrepresented contract's content or legal effect (from fraudulent Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation) misrepresentation Reformation is an equitable remedy courts can use to rewrite a contract to reflect the parties' original intent. This remedy is always available when misrepresentation or mutual mistake occurs. But a unilateral mistake only serves as a basis for reformation if that mistake stemmed from fraudulent misrepresentation, which occurs when: one party intentionally misrepresents the content or legal effect of a contract to induce the other party to enter into the contract and the other party justifiably relies* on that misrepresentation. Here, the employer reduced the parties' oral agreement to writing and sent the unsigned release to the employee. The employee then carefully prepared, signed, and sent to the employer a substitute release that omitted age discrimination claims (intentional misrepresentation). Because the substitute release was otherwise identical to the original, the employer did not notice the omission and signed the release (justifiable reliance). And since the employee's fraudulent behavior caused the employer's unilateral mistake, the court will likely reform the release to reflect the parties' oral agreement (Choice D). *This element is always met unless the misrepresentation was patently and obviously false. (Choice A) The employer may have acted unreasonably by failing to read the substitute release prior to signing it. But a party's negligence in failing to read a writing does not preclude reformation if the writing does not accurately reflect the parties' prior agreement. (Choice B) The parol evidence rule generally precludes extrinsic evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the unambiguous terms of a final written agreement. But such evidence is excepted from this rule and is always admissible when it is used to establish a ground for granting or denying a contractual remedy like reformation (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A court can reform a contract to reflect the parties' original intent when one party's fraudulent misrepresentation caused the other party's unilateral mistake.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 166 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (reformation based on fraudulent misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 172 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of justifiable reliance).","educationalObjective":"A court can reform a contract to reflect the parties' original intent when one party's fraudulent misrepresentation caused the other party's unilateral mistake.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 166 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (reformation based on fraudulent misrepresentation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 172 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of justifiable reliance).","rule":"A court can reform a contract to reflect the parties' original intent when one party's fraudulent misrepresentation caused the other party's unilateral mistake.","irac":{"issue":"Is the employer likely to prevail in an action seeking reformation of the release to conform to the parties' oral agreement?","rule":"A court can reform a contract to reflect the parties' original intent when one party's fraudulent misrepresentation caused the other party's unilateral mistake.","analysis":"Availability of reformation as a remedy\n\nMisrepresentation One party makes untrue assertion of fact that is fraudulent or material Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation Mutual mistake Both parties are mistaken as to contract's contents or legal effect Unilateral mistake One party is mistaken because other party intentionally misrepresented contract's content or legal effect (from fraudulent Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation) misrepresentation Reformation is an equitable remedy courts can use to rewrite a contract to reflect the parties' original intent. This remedy is always available when misrepresentation or mutual mistake occurs. But a unilateral mistake only serves as a basis for reformation if that mistake stemmed from fraudulent misrepresentation, which occurs when: one party intentionally misrepresents the content or legal effect of a contract to induce the other party to enter into the contract and the other party justifiably relies* on that misrepresentation. Here, the employer reduced the parties' oral agreement to writing and sent the unsigned release to the employee. The employee then carefully prepared, signed, and sent to the employer a substitute release that omitted age discrimination claims (intentional misrepresentation). Because the substitute release was otherwise identical to the original, the employer did not notice the omission and signed the release (justifiable reliance). And since the employee's fraudulent behavior caused the employer's unilateral mistake, the court will likely reform the release to reflect the parties' oral agreement (Choice D). *This element is always met unless the misrepresentation was patently and obviously false. (Choice A) The employer may have acted unreasonably by failing to read the substitute release prior to signing it. But a party's negligence in failing to read a writing does not preclude reformation if the writing does not accurately reflect the parties' prior agreement. (Choice B) The parol evidence rule generally precludes extrinsic evidence of a prior oral or written agreement to modify or contradict the unambiguous terms of a final written agreement. But such evidence is excepted from this rule and is always admissible when it is used to establish a ground for granting or denying a contractual remedy like reformation (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because the employee's fraudulent behavior induced the employer's unilateral mistake."},"questionZh":"雇主提出向被解雇的雇员支付 50,000 美元，以解除该雇员可能对雇主提出的所有索赔。该员工口头接受了这一提议。雇主随后准备了一份未签署的解除协议，并将其发送给雇员以供其签署。该员工精心准备、签署并向雇主发送了一份替代解除协议，该协议与原始协议相同，但其中排除了任何年龄歧视索赔。雇主在未阅读替代声明的情况下签署了该声明。此后不久，该雇员通知雇主，他打算起诉雇主年龄歧视。雇主是否有可能在寻求改革释放以符合双方口头协议的诉讼中获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为雇主在签署替代授权书之前没有阅读替代授权书，其行为不合理。","B":"不可以，因为口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）将排除口头协议的证据。","C":"是的，因为雇员的欺诈行为导致了雇主的单方面错误。","D":"是的，因为双方对签署的新闻稿的内容都存在误解。"},"explanationZh":"改革作为补救措施的可用性\n\n失实陈述 一方对欺诈性或重大事实做出不真实的断言 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该失实陈述 相互错误 双方对合同内容或法律效力均存在误解 单方面错误 一方因另一方故意歪曲合同内容或法律效力而产生错误（来自欺诈性 受到不利影响的一方有正当理由依赖该失实陈述） 失实陈述 改革是法院可以用来改写的一种衡平法补救措施反映双方原始意图的合同。当发生虚假陈述或相互错误时，这种补救措施总是可用的。但是，只有当该错误源于欺诈性失实陈述时，单方面错误才可作为改正的依据，这种情况发生在：一方故意歪曲合同的内容或法律效力以诱使另一方签订合同，并且另一方有正当理由依赖*该失实陈述。在这里，雇主将双方的口头协议简化为书面协议，并将未签署的协议发送给雇员。然后，该雇员仔细准备、签署并向雇主发送了一份替代声明，其中省略了年龄歧视索赔（故意失实陈述）。由于替代授权书在其他方面与原始授权书相同，因此雇主没有注意到遗漏并签署了授权书（合理信赖）。并且由于雇员的欺诈行为导致了雇主的单方面错误，法院可能会修改释放以反映双方的口头协议（选择D）。 *除非虚假陈述明显且明显错误，否则始终满足此要素。 （选项 A） 雇主可能因在签署替代授权书之前未阅读替代授权书而采取了不合理的行为。但是，如果书面文件没有准确反映双方事先达成的协议，则一方当事人未阅读书面文件的疏忽并不妨碍改革。 （选择 B）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常排除事先口头或书面协议的外在证据，以修改或反驳最终书面协议的明确条款。但此类证据被排除在本规则之外，并且当它被用来建立批准或拒绝诸如改革之类的合同补救措施的理由时，总是可以接受的（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当一方的欺诈性失实陈述导致另一方的单方面错误时，法院可以修改合同以反映双方的初衷。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 166（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于欺诈性虚假陈述的改革）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 172（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（合理信赖的定义）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一方的欺诈性失实陈述导致另一方的单方面错误时，法院可以修改合同以反映双方的原始意图。","iracZh":{"issue":"雇主是否有可能在寻求改革释放以符合双方口头协议的诉讼中获胜？","rule":"当一方的欺诈性失实陈述导致另一方的单方面错误时，法院可以修改合同以反映双方的原始意图。","analysis":"改革作为补救措施的可用性\n\n失实陈述 一方对欺诈性或重大事实做出不真实的断言 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该失实陈述 相互错误 双方对合同内容或法律效力均存在误解 单方面错误 一方因另一方故意歪曲合同内容或法律效力而产生错误（来自欺诈性 受到不利影响的一方有正当理由依赖该失实陈述） 失实陈述 改革是法院可以用来改写的一种衡平法补救措施反映双方原始意图的合同。当发生虚假陈述或相互错误时，这种补救措施总是可用的。但是，只有当该错误源于欺诈性失实陈述时，单方面错误才可作为改正的依据，这种情况发生在：一方故意歪曲合同的内容或法律效力以诱使另一方签订合同，并且另一方有正当理由依赖*该失实陈述。在这里，雇主将双方的口头协议简化为书面协议，并将未签署的协议发送给雇员。然后，该雇员仔细准备、签署并向雇主发送了一份替代声明，其中省略了年龄歧视索赔（故意失实陈述）。由于替代授权书在其他方面与原始授权书相同，因此雇主没有注意到遗漏并签署了授权书（合理信赖）。并且由于雇员的欺诈行为导致了雇主的单方面错误，法院可能会修改释放以反映双方的口头协议（选择D）。 *除非虚假陈述明显且明显错误，否则始终满足此要素。 （选项 A） 雇主可能因在签署替代授权书之前未阅读替代授权书而采取了不合理的行为。但是，如果书面文件没有准确反映双方事先达成的协议，则一方当事人未阅读书面文件的疏忽并不妨碍改革。 （选择 B）口头证据规则（parol evidence rule）（parol evidence rule）通常排除事先口头或书面协议的外在证据，以修改或反驳最终书面协议的明确条款。但此类证据被排除在本规则之外，并且当它被用来建立批准或拒绝诸如改革之类的合同补救措施的理由时，总是可以接受的（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：当一方的欺诈性失实陈述导致另一方的单方面错误时，法院可以修改合同以反映双方的初衷。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 166（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（基于欺诈性虚假陈述的改革）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 172（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（合理信赖的定义）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为雇员的欺诈行为导致了雇主的单方面错误。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":245,"sourceQuestionPage":723,"sourceQuestionPages":[723],"sourceExplanationPages":[724,725],"sourceAnswerPages":[724,725],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-246","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Modification, Rescission & Accord","question":"A person who knew nothing about horses inherited a thoroughbred colt whose disagreeable behavior made him a pest around the barn. The person sold the colt for $1,500 to a buyer who was an experienced racehorse-trainer and knew of the person's ignorance about horses. At the time of sale, the person said to the buyer, \"I hate to say it, but this horse is bad-tempered and nothing special.\" Soon after the sale, the colt won three races and earned $400,000 for the buyer. Which of the following additional facts, if established by the person, would best support his chance of obtaining rescission of the sale to the buyer?","choices":{"A":"At a pre-sale exercise session of which the buyer knew that the person was not aware, the buyer clocked the colt in record-setting time, far surpassing any previous performance.","B":"At the time of the sale, the person was angry and upset over an incident in which the colt had reared and thrown a rider.","C":"The colt was the only thoroughbred that the person owned, and the person did not know how to evaluate young and untested racehorses.","D":"The person did not know until after the sale that the buyer was an experienced racehorse-trainer."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Mistake as grounds for rescission\n\nMutual mistake Adversely affected party can rescind contract if:\n\nmistake relates to basic assumption of contract mistake materially affects agreed-upon exchange of performances AND\n\nadversely affected party did not assume risk of mistake Unilateral In addition to above elements for mutual mistake, either: mistake mistake makes enforcement of contract unconscionable OR\n\nnonmistaken party caused, or knew or should have known of, mistake Rescission is a remedy that allows the court to cancel a contract and discharge the parties' contractual obligations. This remedy is available to a party who entered into a contract based on a unilateral mistake if: the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract (eg, the person thought the colt was \"nothing special\" and so agreed to sell it for $1,500) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (eg, the colt was actually worth much more, as evidenced by its winning three races and earning $400,000) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake (eg, the person knew nothing about horses) AND either (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake. Therefore, the fact that the buyer had clocked the colt in record-setting time at a pre-sale exercise session, of which the buyer knew that the person was not aware, would best support the person's chance of obtaining rescission. (Choices B, C & D) None of these additional facts show that (1) enforcement of the sale would be unconscionable or (2) the buyer (nonmistaken party) caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the person's mistake. As a result, these facts do not support the person's chance of obtaining rescission of the sale to the buyer.\n\nEducational objective: A party's unilateral mistake is grounds for rescission only if (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unilateral mistake making contract voidable).","educationalObjective":"A party's unilateral mistake is grounds for rescission only if (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 153 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unilateral mistake making contract voidable).","rule":"A party's unilateral mistake is grounds for rescission only if (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following additional facts, if established by the person, would best support his chance of obtaining rescission of the sale to the buyer?","rule":"A party's unilateral mistake is grounds for rescission only if (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake.","analysis":"Mistake as grounds for rescission\n\nMutual mistake Adversely affected party can rescind contract if:\n\nmistake relates to basic assumption of contract mistake materially affects agreed-upon exchange of performances AND\n\nadversely affected party did not assume risk of mistake Unilateral In addition to above elements for mutual mistake, either: mistake mistake makes enforcement of contract unconscionable OR\n\nnonmistaken party caused, or knew or should have known of, mistake Rescission is a remedy that allows the court to cancel a contract and discharge the parties' contractual obligations. This remedy is available to a party who entered into a contract based on a unilateral mistake if: the mistake relates to a basic assumption of the contract (eg, the person thought the colt was \"nothing special\" and so agreed to sell it for $1,500) the mistake materially affects the agreed-upon exchange of performances (eg, the colt was actually worth much more, as evidenced by its winning three races and earning $400,000) the adversely affected party did not assume the risk of the mistake (eg, the person knew nothing about horses) AND either (1) the mistake would make enforcement of the contract unconscionable or (2) the nonmistaken party caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the mistake. Therefore, the fact that the buyer had clocked the colt in record-setting time at a pre-sale exercise session, of which the buyer knew that the person was not aware, would best support the person's chance of obtaining rescission. (Choices B, C & D) None of these additional facts show that (1) enforcement of the sale would be unconscionable or (2) the buyer (nonmistaken party) caused, or knew or had reason to know of, the person's mistake. As a result, these facts do not support the person's chance of obtaining rescission of the sale to the buyer.","conclusion":"Answer A: At a pre-sale exercise session of which the buyer knew that the person was not aware, the buyer clocked the colt in record-setting time, far surpassing any previous performance."},"questionZh":"一个对马一无所知的人继承了一匹纯种小马，它的不良行为使他成为谷仓里的害虫。该人以 1,500 美元的价格将这匹小马卖给了一位买家，这位买家是一位经验丰富的赛马训练师，并且知道该人对马匹的无知。出售时，该人对买家说：“我不想这么说，但这匹马脾气不好，没什么特别的。”出售后不久，这匹小马赢得了三场比赛，为买家赚了 40 万美元。如果该人提出以下哪项附加事实，最能支持他获得撤销对买方的销售的机会？","choicesZh":{"A":"在一次预售练习中，买家知道对方并不知道，买家以创纪录的时间给这匹小马打了钟，远远超过了之前的任何表现。","B":"在出售时，该人对小马驹抬起并摔倒骑手的事件感到愤怒和不安。","C":"这匹小马是该人拥有的唯一纯种马，而该人不知道如何评价年轻且未经测试的赛马。","D":"直到出售后，该人才知道买家是一位经验丰富的赛马训练师。"},"explanationZh":"错误作为撤销理由\n\n相互错误 如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以解除合同：\n\n与合同基本假设相关的错误 错误对商定的履约交换产生重大影响并且\n\n受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误风险 单方面 除了上述共同错误的要素之外，还可以是： 错误 错误导致合同的执行不合理或\n\n非错误方造成、或知道或应该知道错误 撤销是一种补救措施，允许法院取消合同并解除双方的合同义务。如果出现以下情况，基于单方错误签订合同的一方可以使用这种补救措施： 该错误涉及合同的基本假设（例如，该人认为这匹小马“没什么特别”，因此同意以 1,500 美元的价格出售它） 该错误对商定的履行交换产生重大影响（例如，这匹小马实际上价值更高，正如它赢得三场比赛并赚取 400,000 美元所证明的那样） 受到不利影响的一方没有承担以下风险：该错误（例如，该人对马一无所知）并且 (1) 该错误将使合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。因此，买方在预售活动中以创纪录的时间为小马打钟，而买方知道该人并不知道这一事实，这一事实将最好地支持该人获得撤销的机会。 （选项 B、C 和 D）这些附加事实均未表明 (1) 执行销售是不合理的，或 (2) 买方（非错误方）造成或知道或有理由知道该人的错误。因此，这些事实并不支持该人获得撤销对买方的销售的机会。\n\n教育目标：只有在以下情况下，一方的单方面错误才成为解除合同的理由：(1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（单方面错误导致合同无效）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有在以下情况下，一方的单方面错误才可以作为解除合同的理由：(1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理；或者 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果该人提出以下哪项附加事实，最能支持他获得撤销对买方的销售的机会？","rule":"只有在以下情况下，一方的单方面错误才可以作为解除合同的理由：(1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理；或者 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。","analysis":"错误作为撤销理由\n\n相互错误 如果出现以下情况，受到不利影响的一方可以解除合同：\n\n与合同基本假设相关的错误 错误对商定的履约交换产生重大影响并且\n\n受到不利影响的一方没有承担错误风险 单方面 除了上述共同错误的要素之外，还可以是： 错误 错误导致合同的执行不合理或\n\n非错误方造成、或知道或应该知道错误 撤销是一种补救措施，允许法院取消合同并解除双方的合同义务。如果出现以下情况，基于单方错误签订合同的一方可以使用这种补救措施： 该错误涉及合同的基本假设（例如，该人认为这匹小马“没什么特别”，因此同意以 1,500 美元的价格出售它） 该错误对商定的履行交换产生重大影响（例如，这匹小马实际上价值更高，正如它赢得三场比赛并赚取 400,000 美元所证明的那样） 受到不利影响的一方没有承担以下风险：该错误（例如，该人对马一无所知）并且 (1) 该错误将使合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。因此，买方在预售活动中以创纪录的时间为小马打钟，而买方知道该人并不知道这一事实，这一事实将最好地支持该人获得撤销的机会。 （选项 B、C 和 D）这些附加事实均未表明 (1) 执行销售是不合理的，或 (2) 买方（非错误方）造成或知道或有理由知道该人的错误。因此，这些事实并不支持该人获得撤销对买方的销售的机会。\n\n教育目标：只有在以下情况下，一方的单方面错误才成为解除合同的理由：(1) 该错误会导致合同的执行不合理，或者 (2) 无错误的一方造成、或知道或有理由知道该错误。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 153（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（单方面错误导致合同无效）。","conclusion":"答案 A：在一次预售练习中，买家知道对方并不知道，买家以创纪录的时间给这匹小马打了钟，远远超过了之前的任何表现。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":246,"sourceQuestionPage":726,"sourceQuestionPages":[726],"sourceExplanationPages":[727,728],"sourceAnswerPages":[727,728],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-247","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A rental company contracted to lease a collapsible sports dome to a private tennis club. The dome would be used to cover the club's outdoor tennis courts so that the courts could be used for the club's Annual Winter Tennis Tournament beginning on November 16. The contract required the rental company to finish installing the dome no later than November 15. On November 10, the installation crew arrived to lay out the anchoring system required for the dome. However, the crew did not have enough anchors to secure the dome. The club's owner overheard a crew member say that the rental company had forgotten to order the rest of the anchors and that he did not expect them to arrive for at least a month. That same day, the club owner canceled the contract and leased an indoor space near the club. The rental company has sued the tennis club for breach of contract. Will the rental company likely prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the crew member's November 10 statement was an anticipatory repudiation that gave the club owner the right to cancel the contract.","B":"No, because the rental company's failure to order the remaining anchors made it impossible to finish installing the dome by November 15.","C":"Yes, because the club owner failed to seasonably notify the rental company of his intention to cancel the contract before leasing the indoor space.","D":"Yes, because the club owner never demanded and did not obtain the rental company's assurance of due performance under the contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Anticipatory repudiation occurs when one party to a contract unequivocally communicates to the other party that it will not perform under the contract. This gives the other party the right to cancel the contract and sue for breach.* In contrast, prospective inability to perform involves words or conduct that create reasonable grounds for insecurity that a party will perform. This gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance. Failure to provide adequate assurance within a reasonable time is an anticipatory repudiation. Here, the club owner overheard the crew member say that he did not expect the remaining anchors to arrive for at least a month. This was not an unequivocal statement by the rental company that it would not perform (no anticipatory repudiation) (Choice A). However, the statement gave the owner reasonable grounds for insecurity about the company's ability to install the dome by November 15 (prospective inability to perform). But since the owner never demanded and did not obtain the company's assurance, the owner's cancellation of the contract on November 10 was premature. Therefore, the company will likely prevail. *Alternatively, the other party may ignore the repudiation and demand performance pursuant to the contract. (Choice B) A repudiation can be communicated through words or through conduct that renders due performance impossible. Here, although the lack of anchors made it impossible to install the dome on November 10, the rental company still had five days to perform. (Choice C) Had the rental company repudiated the contract, it could have retracted the repudiation by notifying the club owner of the retraction before he (1) cancelled the contract, (2) materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicated that he considered the repudiation to be final. But the club owner would not have been required to notify the rental company before doing any of those things.\n\nEducational objective: A contracting party's prospective inability to perform entitles the other party to demand assurance of performance. Failure to provide such assurance within a reasonable time is an anticipatory repudiation that justifies canceling the contract.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 250 (Am. Law Inst. 2020) (repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 251 (Am. Law Inst. 2020) (failure to provide assurance as repudiation).","educationalObjective":"A contracting party's prospective inability to perform entitles the other party to demand assurance of performance. Failure to provide such assurance within a reasonable time is an anticipatory repudiation that justifies canceling the contract.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 250 (Am. Law Inst. 2020) (repudiation).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 251 (Am. Law Inst. 2020) (failure to provide assurance as repudiation).","rule":"A contracting party's prospective inability to perform entitles the other party to demand assurance of performance. Failure to provide such assurance within a reasonable time is an anticipatory repudiation that justifies canceling the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Will the rental company likely prevail?","rule":"A contracting party's prospective inability to perform entitles the other party to demand assurance of performance. Failure to provide such assurance within a reasonable time is an anticipatory repudiation that justifies canceling the contract.","analysis":"Anticipatory repudiation occurs when one party to a contract unequivocally communicates to the other party that it will not perform under the contract. This gives the other party the right to cancel the contract and sue for breach.* In contrast, prospective inability to perform involves words or conduct that create reasonable grounds for insecurity that a party will perform. This gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance. Failure to provide adequate assurance within a reasonable time is an anticipatory repudiation. Here, the club owner overheard the crew member say that he did not expect the remaining anchors to arrive for at least a month. This was not an unequivocal statement by the rental company that it would not perform (no anticipatory repudiation) (Choice A). However, the statement gave the owner reasonable grounds for insecurity about the company's ability to install the dome by November 15 (prospective inability to perform). But since the owner never demanded and did not obtain the company's assurance, the owner's cancellation of the contract on November 10 was premature. Therefore, the company will likely prevail. *Alternatively, the other party may ignore the repudiation and demand performance pursuant to the contract. (Choice B) A repudiation can be communicated through words or through conduct that renders due performance impossible. Here, although the lack of anchors made it impossible to install the dome on November 10, the rental company still had five days to perform. (Choice C) Had the rental company repudiated the contract, it could have retracted the repudiation by notifying the club owner of the retraction before he (1) cancelled the contract, (2) materially changed position in reliance on the repudiation, or (3) indicated that he considered the repudiation to be final. But the club owner would not have been required to notify the rental company before doing any of those things.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the club owner never demanded and did not obtain the rental company's assurance of due performance under the contract."},"questionZh":"一家租赁公司与一家私人网球俱乐部签订了租赁可折叠运动圆顶的合同。该穹顶将用于覆盖俱乐部的室外网球场，以便该球场可用于俱乐部于 11 月 16 日开始的年度冬季网球锦标赛。合同要求租赁公司不迟于 11 月 15 日完成穹顶的安装。11 月 10 日，安装人员抵达布置穹顶所需的锚固系统。然而，船员们没有足够的锚来固定圆顶。俱乐部老板无意中听到一名工作人员说，租赁公司忘记订购其余的主播，他预计他们至少要一个月才能到达。当天，俱乐部老板取消了合同，并租用了俱乐部附近的一个室内空间。租赁公司已起诉网球俱乐部违反合同。租赁公司可能会占上风吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为该船员 11 月 10 日的声明属于预期违约（anticipatory repudiation），俱乐部老板有权取消合同。","B":"不可以，因为租赁公司未能订购剩余的锚杆，导致无法在 11 月 15 日之前完成圆顶的安装。","C":"是的，因为俱乐部老板在租赁室内空间之前没有及时通知租赁公司他打算取消合同。","D":"是的，因为俱乐部老板从未要求也没有获得租赁公司对合同项下应有履行的保证。"},"explanationZh":"当合同一方明确告知另一方其不会履行合同时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。这使另一方有权取消合同并起诉违约行为。*相反，预期无法履行涉及为一方履行的不安全感造成合理理由的言语或行为。这赋予另一方要求履行保证的权利。未能在合理时间内提供充分保证属于预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。这边，俱乐部老板无意中听到工作人员说，没想到剩下的主播至少还要一个月才能到达。这并不是租赁公司不会履行的明确声明（无预期拒付）（选项 A）。然而，该声明为业主提供了合理的理由，让他们对公司在 11 月 15 日之前安装圆顶的能力感到不安全（预期无法履行）。但由于业主从未提出要求，也没有获得公司的保证，所以业主在11月10日取消合同还为时过早。因此，该公司很可能会获胜。 *另外，另一方也可以忽略毁约并要求按照合同履行。 （选择 B） 拒绝可以通过言语或通过导致无法履行应有义务的行为来传达。在这里，虽然由于缺乏锚栓导致无法在11月10日安装穹顶，但租赁公司仍有五天的时间来执行。 （选择 C）如果租赁公司拒绝履行合同，则可以在俱乐部所有者 (1) 取消合同、(2) 因拒绝而实质性改变立场或 (3) 表明他认为拒绝是最终决定之前，通过通知俱乐部所有者撤销合同来撤回拒绝。但俱乐部老板在做这些事情之前不需要通知租赁公司。\n\n教育目标：缔约方预期无法履约，另一方有权要求履约保证。未能在合理时间内提供此类保证属于预期拒付，因此有理由取消合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 250 (Am. Law Inst. 2020)（否认）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 251 (Am. Law Inst. 2020)（未能提供保证视为否认）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"缔约方可能无法履约，另一方有权要求履约保证。未能在合理时间内提供此类保证属于预期拒付，因此有理由取消合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"租赁公司可能会占上风吗？","rule":"缔约方可能无法履约，另一方有权要求履约保证。未能在合理时间内提供此类保证属于预期拒付，因此有理由取消合同。","analysis":"当合同一方明确告知另一方其不会履行合同时，就会发生预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。这使另一方有权取消合同并起诉违约行为。*相反，预期无法履行涉及为一方履行的不安全感造成合理理由的言语或行为。这赋予另一方要求履行保证的权利。未能在合理时间内提供充分保证属于预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。这边，俱乐部老板无意中听到工作人员说，没想到剩下的主播至少还要一个月才能到达。这并不是租赁公司不会履行的明确声明（无预期拒付）（选项 A）。然而，该声明为业主提供了合理的理由，让他们对公司在 11 月 15 日之前安装圆顶的能力感到不安全（预期无法履行）。但由于业主从未提出要求，也没有获得公司的保证，所以业主在11月10日取消合同还为时过早。因此，该公司很可能会获胜。 *另外，另一方也可以忽略毁约并要求按照合同履行。 （选择 B） 拒绝可以通过言语或通过导致无法履行应有义务的行为来传达。在这里，虽然由于缺乏锚栓导致无法在11月10日安装穹顶，但租赁公司仍有五天的时间来执行。 （选择 C）如果租赁公司拒绝履行合同，则可以在俱乐部所有者 (1) 取消合同、(2) 因拒绝而实质性改变立场或 (3) 表明他认为拒绝是最终决定之前，通过通知俱乐部所有者撤销合同来撤回拒绝。但俱乐部老板在做这些事情之前不需要通知租赁公司。\n\n教育目标：缔约方预期无法履约，另一方有权要求履约保证。未能在合理时间内提供此类保证属于预期拒付，因此有理由取消合同。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 250 (Am. Law Inst. 2020)（否认）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 251 (Am. Law Inst. 2020)（未能提供保证视为否认）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为俱乐部老板从未要求也没有获得租赁公司对合同项下应有履行的保证。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"prospective inability to perform","ipa":"/pɹʌspˈɛktɪv ˌɪnʌbˈɪlɪti tˈu pɝfˈɔɹm/","syllables":"prospec·tive in·abil·ity to per·form","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"预期履行不能：尚非明确拒绝，但客观上显露不能履行","contextualMeaning":"预期履行不能：尚非明确拒绝，但客观上显露不能履行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"A contracting party's prospective inability to perform entitles the other party to demand assurance of performance.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-16e26cd601960b3b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":247,"sourceQuestionPage":729,"sourceQuestionPages":[729],"sourceExplanationPages":[730,731],"sourceAnswerPages":[730,731],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-248","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A popular singer expressed interest in performing in a theater producer's musical production. The producer and the singer agreed that there would be no enforceable contractual obligations between them until they had entered into a final, signed contract. Then they began negotiating the terms of the contract. While the parties were negotiating, the producer began to spend money on the production. When the parties were unable to agree on the terms of a final contract, the producer made reasonable but unsuccessful efforts to hire another singer before abandoning the production. If the producer sues the singer, what will he likely recover?","choices":{"A":"Expectation damages in the form of the profits that the production would have made if it had been performed.","B":"Nothing, because it was not reasonable for the producer to expect to be reimbursed for expenses incurred when he knew that the singer was not obligated until there was a final, signed agreement.","C":"Reliance damages in the form of the actual expenses the producer incurred while negotiating with the singer.","D":"Restitution for any harm done to the producer's reputation when the production was abandoned."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Promissory estoppel\n\nAllows enforcement of promise when no valid contract exists if:\n\npromisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on promise enforcing party reasonably relied on promise reliance caused enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcing promise Nonperformance of a contractual duty constitutes a breach. This allows the nonbreaching party to seek remedies—including compensatory damages (eg, expectation or reliance damages). But even when there is no valid contract (as seen here), a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief. For example, the doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages for expenses stemming from another's promise if: the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise reliance on the promise caused the enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcing the promise. A successful promissory-estoppel claim often results in an award of reliance damages—ie, damages based on the out-of-pocket expenses incurred by the enforcing party. However, in some instances, courts have awarded expectation damages—ie, damages intended to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed. Here, the producer and singer did not enter into a valid contract, so the producer cannot recover compensatory damages for breach. Although the doctrine of promissory estoppel allows for recovery of damages when there is no contract, that doctrine requires reasonable reliance on a promise. And since the parties agreed that they would not be obligated until a final agreement was signed, it was unreasonable for the producer to incur expenses during negotiations. Therefore, the producer is likely to recover nothing (Choices A & C). (Choice D) An award of restitution is appropriate when one party is unjustly enriched by the other (not seen here). Therefore, the producer's reputational harm is not a basis upon which to claim restitution.\n\nEducational objective: A successful promissory-estoppel claim (1) most often results in an award of reliance damages (ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses) and (2) rarely results in an award of expectation damages (ie, damages intended to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed).","educationalObjective":"A successful promissory-estoppel claim (1) most often results in an award of reliance damages (ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses) and (2) rarely results in an award of expectation damages (ie, damages intended to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed).","references":"","rule":"A successful promissory-estoppel claim (1) most often results in an award of reliance damages (ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses) and (2) rarely results in an award of expectation damages (ie, damages intended to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed).","irac":{"issue":"If the producer sues the singer, what will he likely recover?","rule":"A successful promissory-estoppel claim (1) most often results in an award of reliance damages (ie, damages based on out-of-pocket expenses) and (2) rarely results in an award of expectation damages (ie, damages intended to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed).","analysis":"Promissory estoppel\n\nAllows enforcement of promise when no valid contract exists if:\n\npromisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on promise enforcing party reasonably relied on promise reliance caused enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcing promise Nonperformance of a contractual duty constitutes a breach. This allows the nonbreaching party to seek remedies—including compensatory damages (eg, expectation or reliance damages). But even when there is no valid contract (as seen here), a party may recover damages under an equitable theory of relief. For example, the doctrine of promissory estoppel allows a party to recover damages for expenses stemming from another's promise if: the promisor reasonably expected to induce reliance on the promise the enforcing party reasonably relied on the promise reliance on the promise caused the enforcing party to suffer substantial detriment and injustice can be avoided only by enforcing the promise. A successful promissory-estoppel claim often results in an award of reliance damages—ie, damages based on the out-of-pocket expenses incurred by the enforcing party. However, in some instances, courts have awarded expectation damages—ie, damages intended to put the enforcing party in the same position as if the promise had been performed. Here, the producer and singer did not enter into a valid contract, so the producer cannot recover compensatory damages for breach. Although the doctrine of promissory estoppel allows for recovery of damages when there is no contract, that doctrine requires reasonable reliance on a promise. And since the parties agreed that they would not be obligated until a final agreement was signed, it was unreasonable for the producer to incur expenses during negotiations. Therefore, the producer is likely to recover nothing (Choices A & C). (Choice D) An award of restitution is appropriate when one party is unjustly enriched by the other (not seen here). Therefore, the producer's reputational harm is not a basis upon which to claim restitution.","conclusion":"Answer B: Nothing, because it was not reasonable for the producer to expect to be reimbursed for expenses incurred when he knew that the singer was not obligated until there was a final, signed agreement."},"questionZh":"一位受欢迎的歌手表示有兴趣在剧院制作人的音乐作品中表演。制作人和歌手同意，在签订最终签署的合同之前，他们之间不存在可执行的合同义务。然后他们开始谈判合同条款。就在双方谈判的同时，制片方开始花钱制作。当双方无法就最终合同条款达成一致时，制片人在放弃制作之前做出了合理但不成功的努力，聘请了另一位歌手。如果制作人起诉歌手，他可能会得到什么赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"预期损害的形式是生产如果进行的话将会获得的利润。","B":"没什么，因为制作人知道歌手在最终签署协议之前没有义务，因此期望得到报销所产生的费用是不合理的。","C":"信赖损失以制作人在与歌手谈判时产生的实际费用的形式出现。","D":"因制作被放弃而对生产者声誉造成的任何损害进行赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"禁止反言\n\n在不存在有效合同的情况下允许执行承诺，如果：\n\n承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖 执行方合理依赖承诺 依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害和不公正，只有通过执行承诺才能避免 不履行合同义务构成违约。这使得非违约方能够寻求补救措施，包括补偿性损害赔偿（例如，预期损害赔偿或信赖损害赔偿）。但即使没有有效的合同（如此处所示），一方也可以根据衡平法救济理论获得损害赔偿。例如，在以下情况下，允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许一方当事人就因另一方承诺而产生的费用获得损害赔偿： 承诺人合理预期会导致对承诺的信赖 执行方合理地依赖该承诺 对承诺的依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，并且只有通过执行该承诺才能避免不公正。成功的禁止反言索赔通常会导致信赖损害赔偿金，即基于执行方自付费用的损害赔偿金。然而，在某些情况下，法院会判给预期损害赔偿，即旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的境地的损害赔偿。本案中，制作人与歌手并未签订有效合同，因此制作人无法追偿违约金。尽管允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许在没有合同的情况下追回损害赔偿，但该原则要求合理信赖承诺。而且由于双方同意在签署最终协议之前不会承担任何义务，因此生产商在谈判过程中承担费用是不合理的。因此，生产者很可能一无所获（选择 A 和 C）。 （选项 D）当一方因另一方不当得利（此处未见）时，裁定赔偿是适当的。因此，生产者的名誉损害不能作为要求赔偿的依据。\n\n教育目标：成功的允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）索赔（1）通常会导致信赖损害赔偿（即基于自付费用的损害赔偿），并且（2）很少会导致预期损害赔偿（即旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的地位的损害赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"成功的许诺禁止反悔索赔 (1) 通常会导致信赖损害赔偿（即基于自付费用的损害赔偿），并且 (2) 很少会导致预期损害赔偿（即旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的地位的损害赔偿）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果制作人起诉歌手，他可能会得到什么赔偿？","rule":"成功的许诺禁止反悔索赔 (1) 通常会导致信赖损害赔偿（即基于自付费用的损害赔偿），并且 (2) 很少会导致预期损害赔偿（即旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的地位的损害赔偿）。","analysis":"禁止反言\n\n在不存在有效合同的情况下允许执行承诺，如果：\n\n承诺人合理预期会引起对承诺的依赖 执行方合理依赖承诺 依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害和不公正，只有通过执行承诺才能避免 不履行合同义务构成违约。这使得非违约方能够寻求补救措施，包括补偿性损害赔偿（例如，预期损害赔偿或信赖损害赔偿）。但即使没有有效的合同（如此处所示），一方也可以根据衡平法救济理论获得损害赔偿。例如，在以下情况下，允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许一方当事人就因另一方承诺而产生的费用获得损害赔偿： 承诺人合理预期会导致对承诺的信赖 执行方合理地依赖该承诺 对承诺的依赖导致执行方遭受重大损害，并且只有通过执行该承诺才能避免不公正。成功的禁止反言索赔通常会导致信赖损害赔偿金，即基于执行方自付费用的损害赔偿金。然而，在某些情况下，法院会判给预期损害赔偿，即旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的境地的损害赔偿。本案中，制作人与歌手并未签订有效合同，因此制作人无法追偿违约金。尽管允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）原则允许在没有合同的情况下追回损害赔偿，但该原则要求合理信赖承诺。而且由于双方同意在签署最终协议之前不会承担任何义务，因此生产商在谈判过程中承担费用是不合理的。因此，生产者很可能一无所获（选择 A 和 C）。 （选项 D）当一方因另一方不当得利（此处未见）时，裁定赔偿是适当的。因此，生产者的名誉损害不能作为要求赔偿的依据。\n\n教育目标：成功的允诺禁反言（promissory estoppel）索赔（1）通常会导致信赖损害赔偿（即基于自付费用的损害赔偿），并且（2）很少会导致预期损害赔偿（即旨在使执行方处于与承诺已履行相同的地位的损害赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 B：没什么，因为制作人知道歌手在最终签署协议之前没有义务，因此期望得到报销所产生的费用是不合理的。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":248,"sourceQuestionPage":732,"sourceQuestionPages":[732],"sourceExplanationPages":[733,734],"sourceAnswerPages":[733,734],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-249","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A sailor owns a boat that she charters for sport fishing at a $500 daily rate. The fee includes the use of the boat with the sailor as the captain, and one other crew member, as well as fishing tackle and bait. On May 1, a customer agreed with the sailor that the customer would have the full-day use of the boat on May 15 for himself and his family for $500. The customer paid an advance deposit of $200 and signed an agreement that the deposit could be retained by the sailor as liquidated damages in the event the customer canceled or failed to appear. At the time of contracting, the sailor told the customer to be at the dock at 5:00 a.m. on May 15. The customer and his family, however, did not show up on May 15 until noon. In the meantime, the sailor agreed at 10:00 a.m. to take a third party and her family out fishing for the rest of the day. The third party had happened to come by and inquire about the possibility of such an outing. In view of the late hour, the sailor charged the third party $400 and stayed out two hours beyond the customary return time. The customer's failure to appear until noon was due to the fact that he had been trying to charter another boat across the bay at a lower rate and had gotten lost after he was unsuccessful in getting such a charter. Which of the following is an accurate statement concerning the legal obligations of the parties?","choices":{"A":"The sailor can retain the $200 paid by the customer, because it would be difficult for the sailor to establish her actual damages and the sum appears to have been a reasonable forecast in light of anticipated loss of profit from the charter.","B":"The sailor is entitled to retain only $50 (10% of the contract price) and must return $150 to the customer.","C":"The sailor must return $100 to the customer, because the liquidated-damage clause under the circumstances would operate as a penalty.","D":"The sailor must return $100 to the customer in order to avoid her own unjust enrichment at the customer's expense."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution To recover damages, a party must prove the dollar amount of the damages with reasonable certainty. Where it would be difficult to establish actual damages, the parties may agree to liquidated damages—ie, a fixed measure of damages for breach set forth in the contract. However, a liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large in relation to actual or anticipated damages that it punishes the breaching party (as opposed to merely compensating the nonbreaching party). Here, the customer contracted to use the sailor's boat for a full day for $500. The customer paid a $200 deposit, which the sailor could retain as liquidated damages if the customer canceled or failed to appear. Although the sailor ultimately collected $600 for the day ($200 retained deposit + $400 from the third party), she also stayed out two hours later than the customary return time as a result of the customer's breach. As a result, the $200 appears to have been a reasonable measure of damages, and the sailor can retain the full amount (Choice C). (Choice B) There is no rule that, if applied, would result in the sailor retaining $50 (10% of the contract price). (Choice D) Restitutionary damages ensure that the nonbreaching party (here, the sailor) is not unjustly enriched—ie, does not receive a benefit that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. But here, the parties agreed to the deposit as a reasonable amount of damages in the event of the customer's breach.\n\nEducational objective: A liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large in relation to actual or anticipated damages that it punishes the nonbreaching party.","educationalObjective":"A liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large in relation to actual or anticipated damages that it punishes the nonbreaching party.","references":"","rule":"A liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large in relation to actual or anticipated damages that it punishes the nonbreaching party.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is an accurate statement concerning the legal obligations of the parties?","rule":"A liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large in relation to actual or anticipated damages that it punishes the nonbreaching party.","analysis":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution To recover damages, a party must prove the dollar amount of the damages with reasonable certainty. Where it would be difficult to establish actual damages, the parties may agree to liquidated damages—ie, a fixed measure of damages for breach set forth in the contract. However, a liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large in relation to actual or anticipated damages that it punishes the breaching party (as opposed to merely compensating the nonbreaching party). Here, the customer contracted to use the sailor's boat for a full day for $500. The customer paid a $200 deposit, which the sailor could retain as liquidated damages if the customer canceled or failed to appear. Although the sailor ultimately collected $600 for the day ($200 retained deposit + $400 from the third party), she also stayed out two hours later than the customary return time as a result of the customer's breach. As a result, the $200 appears to have been a reasonable measure of damages, and the sailor can retain the full amount (Choice C). (Choice B) There is no rule that, if applied, would result in the sailor retaining $50 (10% of the contract price). (Choice D) Restitutionary damages ensure that the nonbreaching party (here, the sailor) is not unjustly enriched—ie, does not receive a benefit that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. But here, the parties agreed to the deposit as a reasonable amount of damages in the event of the customer's breach.","conclusion":"Answer A: The sailor can retain the $200 paid by the customer, because it would be difficult for the sailor to establish her actual damages and the sum appears to have been a reasonable forecast in light of anticipated loss of profit from the charter."},"questionZh":"一名水手拥有一艘船，她以每天 500 美元的价格租用这艘船进行游钓。费用包括使用由水手担任船长和另一名船员的船只，以及渔具和鱼饵。 5月1日，一位顾客与水手达成一致，顾客可以在5月15日以500美元的价格为自己和家人全天使用这艘船。顾客预付了200美元的押金，并签署了一份协议，如果顾客取消预订或未能出现，水手可以保留这笔押金作为违约金。签约时，水手告诉客户5月15日凌晨5点到码头。然而，客户及其家人直到5月15日中午才出现。与此同时，水手同意在上午 10:00 带着第三方及其家人在当天剩下的时间里出去钓鱼。第三者碰巧过来询问是否有这样的郊游的可能性。鉴于时间已晚，该水手向第三方收取了400美元的费用，并在正常返回时间之外停留了两个小时。该客户直到中午才出现，是因为他一直试图以较低的价格租另一艘船穿过海湾，但在租船失败后迷路了。关于当事人的法律义务，下列哪一项是正确的表述？","choicesZh":{"A":"水手可以保留客户支付的 200 美元，因为水手很难确定她的实际损失，而且考虑到租船的预期利润损失，这笔金额似乎是合理的预测。","B":"水手有权仅保留 50 美元（合同价格的 10%），并且必须向客户退还 150 美元。","C":"水手必须向顾客退还 100 美元，因为在这种情况下，违约金条款将作为罚款。","D":"水手必须向顾客退还 100 美元，以避免自己因损害顾客的利益而获得不当利益。"},"explanationZh":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将守约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（预期 期望措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 回退 当期望措施过于投机时，将守约方置于与未签订合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约赔偿 赔偿 为了追回损害赔偿，一方必须以合理的确定性证明损害赔偿的美元金额。如果难以确定实际损害赔偿，双方可以约定违约赔偿金，即合同中规定的违约损害赔偿的固定数额。然而，如果违约金条款相对于实际或预期损害赔偿数额过大以至于惩罚违约方（而不是仅仅赔偿非违约方），则违约金条款作为处罚是不可执行的。在此，客户签订了使用水手船一整天的合同，费用为 500 美元。顾客支付了 200 美元的押金，如果顾客取消预订或未能出现，水手可以保留这笔押金作为违约金。尽管该水手最终收取了当天的 600 美元（200 美元保留押金 + 第三方支付的 400 美元），但她也因为顾客的违约而比正常返回时间晚了两个小时。因此，200 美元似乎是合理的损害赔偿标准，水手可以保留全额金额（选项 C）。 （选择 B） 没有任何规则表明，如果适用，将导致水手扣留 50 美元（合同价格的 10%）。 （选项D）返还利益赔偿确保非违约方（此处为水手）不会不公正地致富，即不会获得不正当获得且保留不公平的利益。但在这里，双方同意将押金作为客户违约时合理的损害赔偿金额。\n\n教育目标：如果违约赔偿金条款相对于实际或预期损害赔偿数额过大以致于对非违约方进行惩罚，则该条款作为处罚是不可执行的。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果违约金条款相对于实际或预期损害赔偿数额过大以致于对非违约方进行惩罚，则该违约金条款作为处罚是不可执行的。","iracZh":{"issue":"关于当事人的法律义务，下列哪一项是正确的表述？","rule":"如果违约金条款相对于实际或预期损害赔偿数额过大以致于对非违约方进行惩罚，则该违约金条款作为处罚是不可执行的。","analysis":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将守约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（预期 期望措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 回退 当期望措施过于投机时，将守约方置于与未签订合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约赔偿 赔偿 为了追回损害赔偿，一方必须以合理的确定性证明损害赔偿的美元金额。如果难以确定实际损害赔偿，双方可以约定违约赔偿金，即合同中规定的违约损害赔偿的固定数额。然而，如果违约金条款相对于实际或预期损害赔偿数额过大以至于惩罚违约方（而不是仅仅赔偿非违约方），则违约金条款作为处罚是不可执行的。在此，客户签订了使用水手船一整天的合同，费用为 500 美元。顾客支付了 200 美元的押金，如果顾客取消预订或未能出现，水手可以保留这笔押金作为违约金。尽管该水手最终收取了当天的 600 美元（200 美元保留押金 + 第三方支付的 400 美元），但她也因为顾客的违约而比正常返回时间晚了两个小时。因此，200 美元似乎是合理的损害赔偿标准，水手可以保留全额金额（选项 C）。 （选择 B） 没有任何规则表明，如果适用，将导致水手扣留 50 美元（合同价格的 10%）。 （选项D）返还利益赔偿确保非违约方（此处为水手）不会不公正地致富，即不会获得不正当获得且保留不公平的利益。但在这里，双方同意将押金作为客户违约时合理的损害赔偿金额。\n\n教育目标：如果违约赔偿金条款相对于实际或预期损害赔偿数额过大以致于对非违约方进行惩罚，则该条款作为处罚是不可执行的。","conclusion":"答案 A：水手可以保留客户支付的 200 美元，因为水手很难确定她的实际损失，而且考虑到租船的预期利润损失，这笔金额似乎是合理的预测。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":249,"sourceQuestionPage":735,"sourceQuestionPages":[735],"sourceExplanationPages":[736,737],"sourceAnswerPages":[736,737],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-250","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"On June 15, a teacher accepted a contract for a one-year position teaching math at a public high school at a salary of $50,000, starting in September. On June 22, the school district informed the teacher that, due to a change in its planned math curriculum, the high school no longer needed a full-time math teacher. The school district offered instead to employ the teacher as a part-time academic counselor at a salary of $20,000, starting in September. The teacher refused the school district's offer. On June 29, the teacher was offered a one-year position to teach math at a nearby private academy for $47,000, starting in September. The teacher, however, decided to spend the year completing work on a graduate degree in mathematics and declined the academy's offer. If the teacher sues the school district for breach of contract, what is her most likely recovery?","choices":{"A":"$3,000, the full contract amount less the amount the teacher could have earned in the teaching position at the academy.","B":"$30,000, the full contract amount less the amount the teacher could have earned in the counselor position offered by the school district.","C":"$50,000, the full contract amount.","D":"Nothing, because the school district notified the teacher of its decision before the teacher had acted in substantial reliance on the contract."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution The primary purpose of compensatory damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed (ie, give that party the \"benefit of the bargain\"). Therefore, when an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. But to avoid a reduction in the total damages award, the employee must make reasonable efforts to mitigate losses by securing comparable employment.* Here, the school district breached the contract by firing the teacher before her employment began. As a result, the teacher suffered damages of $50,000—the amount of her unpaid salary. But since the teacher failed to mitigate her losses by accepting a comparable teaching position at a nearby private academy, her total damages award will be reduced by the $47,000 salary she could have earned at that job. Therefore, the teacher is entitled to recover $3,000 ($50,000 - $47,000 = $3,000) from the school district (Choice C). *Recovery will not be reduced if the employee made reasonable but unsuccessful efforts to secure comparable employment. (Choice B) It is unlikely that a court would consider the part-time counseling position to be comparable employment, so the teacher's damages of $50,000 should not be reduced by the $20,000 she could have earned in that position. As a result, $30,000 is not the appropriate measure of recovery. (Choice D) Arguably, the teacher had acted in substantial reliance on the contract by discontinuing her search for employment. But reliance is irrelevant to the teacher's right to recover damages under the teaching contract.\n\nEducational objective: Following an employer's breach of an employment contract, the employee is expected to make reasonable efforts to mitigate losses by securing comparable employment. Failure to do so results in a reduced recovery from the employer (ie, unpaid salary minus loss that could have been avoided by accepting comparable employment).","educationalObjective":"Following an employer's breach of an employment contract, the employee is expected to make reasonable efforts to mitigate losses by securing comparable employment. Failure to do so results in a reduced recovery from the employer (ie, unpaid salary minus loss that could have been avoided by accepting comparable employment).","references":"","rule":"Following an employer's breach of an employment contract, the employee is expected to make reasonable efforts to mitigate losses by securing comparable employment. Failure to do so results in a reduced recovery from the employer (ie, unpaid salary minus loss that could have been avoided by accepting comparable employment).","irac":{"issue":"If the teacher sues the school district for breach of contract, what is her most likely recovery?","rule":"Following an employer's breach of an employment contract, the employee is expected to make reasonable efforts to mitigate losses by securing comparable employment. Failure to do so results in a reduced recovery from the employer (ie, unpaid salary minus loss that could have been avoided by accepting comparable employment).","analysis":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution The primary purpose of compensatory damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed (ie, give that party the \"benefit of the bargain\"). Therefore, when an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. But to avoid a reduction in the total damages award, the employee must make reasonable efforts to mitigate losses by securing comparable employment.* Here, the school district breached the contract by firing the teacher before her employment began. As a result, the teacher suffered damages of $50,000—the amount of her unpaid salary. But since the teacher failed to mitigate her losses by accepting a comparable teaching position at a nearby private academy, her total damages award will be reduced by the $47,000 salary she could have earned at that job. Therefore, the teacher is entitled to recover $3,000 ($50,000 - $47,000 = $3,000) from the school district (Choice C). *Recovery will not be reduced if the employee made reasonable but unsuccessful efforts to secure comparable employment. (Choice B) It is unlikely that a court would consider the part-time counseling position to be comparable employment, so the teacher's damages of $50,000 should not be reduced by the $20,000 she could have earned in that position. As a result, $30,000 is not the appropriate measure of recovery. (Choice D) Arguably, the teacher had acted in substantial reliance on the contract by discontinuing her search for employment. But reliance is irrelevant to the teacher's right to recover damages under the teaching contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: $3,000, the full contract amount less the amount the teacher could have earned in the teaching position at the academy."},"questionZh":"6 月 15 日，一名教师接受了一份合同，在一所公立高中教数学，为期一年，薪水为 5 万美元，从 9 月份开始。 6月22日，学区通知老师，由于计划的数学课程发生变化，高中不再需要全职数学老师。学区提出从 9 月份开始聘请该老师担任兼职学术顾问，薪水为 20,000 美元。老师拒绝了学区的提议。 6 月 29 日，该老师获得了一份为期一年的职位，在附近的一所私立学院教授数学，薪资为 47,000 美元，从 9 月份开始。然而，这位老师决定用一年的时间完成数学研究生学位的工作，并拒绝了学院的邀请。如果老师起诉学区违约，她最有可能获得什么赔偿？","choicesZh":{"A":"3,000 美元，合同总额减去教师在学院担任教学职位可以赚取的金额。","B":"30,000 美元，合同总额减去教师在学区提供的辅导员职位上本可以赚取的金额。","C":"50,000 美元，全额合同金额。","D":"没什么，因为学区在教师充分依赖合同行事之前就将其决定通知了教师。"},"explanationZh":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将守约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（期望 预期措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 回退 当期望措施过于投机时，将守约方置于与未形成合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约金 恢复原状 补偿性损害赔偿的主要目的是使守约方处于与合同相同的位置已经履行（即给予该方“交易的好处”）。因此，当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者可以根据合同追偿未支付的工资。但为了避免损害赔偿总额减少，雇员必须做出合理的努力，通过获得类似的就业机会来减轻损失。*在此，学区在教师开始就业之前解雇了她，从而违反了合同。结果，这位老师遭受了 5 万美元的损失——相当于她未付工资的金额。但由于该老师未能通过接受附近一所私立学院的类似教学职位来减轻她的损失，她的总损害赔偿金将减少她在该工作中本可以赚取的 47,000 美元的工资。因此，教师有权向学区追回 $3,000 ($50,000 - $47,000 = $3,000)（选项 C）。 *如果员工为获得类似就业做出了合理但不成功的努力，则恢复不会减少。 （选择 B） 法院不太可能将兼职咨询职位视为可比就业，因此教师 50,000 美元的损害赔偿不应减去她在该职位本可赚取的 20,000 美元。因此，30,000 美元并不是适当的恢复衡量标准。 （选项 D） 可以说，该老师停止寻找工作的行为严重依赖了合同。但信赖与教师根据教学合同获得损害赔偿的权利无关。\n\n教育目标：在雇主违反雇佣合同后，雇员应做出合理努力，通过获得类似的就业机会来减轻损失。如果不这样做，就会导致雇主收回的金额减少（即，未付工资减去接受类似就业可以避免的损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"雇主违反雇佣合同后，雇员应做出合理努力，通过获得类似的就业机会来减轻损失。如果不这样做，就会导致雇主收回的金额减少（即，未付工资减去接受类似就业可以避免的损失）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果老师起诉学区违约，她最有可能获得什么赔偿？","rule":"雇主违反雇佣合同后，雇员应做出合理努力，通过获得类似的就业机会来减轻损失。如果不这样做，就会导致雇主收回的金额减少（即，未付工资减去接受类似就业可以避免的损失）。","analysis":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将守约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（期望 预期措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 回退 当期望措施过于投机时，将守约方置于与未形成合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约金 恢复原状 补偿性损害赔偿的主要目的是使守约方处于与合同相同的位置已经履行（即给予该方“交易的好处”）。因此，当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者可以根据合同追偿未支付的工资。但为了避免损害赔偿总额减少，雇员必须做出合理的努力，通过获得类似的就业机会来减轻损失。*在此，学区在教师开始就业之前解雇了她，从而违反了合同。结果，这位老师遭受了 5 万美元的损失——相当于她未付工资的金额。但由于该老师未能通过接受附近一所私立学院的类似教学职位来减轻她的损失，她的总损害赔偿金将减少她在该工作中本可以赚取的 47,000 美元的工资。因此，教师有权向学区追回 $3,000 ($50,000 - $47,000 = $3,000)（选项 C）。 *如果员工为获得类似就业做出了合理但不成功的努力，则恢复不会减少。 （选择 B） 法院不太可能将兼职咨询职位视为可比就业，因此教师 50,000 美元的损害赔偿不应减去她在该职位本可赚取的 20,000 美元。因此，30,000 美元并不是适当的恢复衡量标准。 （选项 D） 可以说，该老师停止寻找工作的行为严重依赖了合同。但信赖与教师根据教学合同获得损害赔偿的权利无关。\n\n教育目标：在雇主违反雇佣合同后，雇员应做出合理努力，通过获得类似的就业机会来减轻损失。如果不这样做，就会导致雇主收回的金额减少（即，未付工资减去接受类似就业可以避免的损失）。","conclusion":"答案 A：3,000 美元，合同总额减去教师在学院担任教学职位可以赚取的金额。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":250,"sourceQuestionPage":738,"sourceQuestionPages":[738],"sourceExplanationPages":[739,740],"sourceAnswerPages":[739,740],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-251","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Impossibility, Impracticability & Frustration","question":"A tech company contracted with a private university to sell and deliver to the university a mainframe computer using a new type of technology then under development but not perfected by the company, at a price substantially lower than that of a similar computer using current technology. After making the contract with the university, the tech company discovered that the new technology it intended to use was unreliable and that no computer manufacturer could yet build a reliable computer using that technology. The tech company thereupon notified the university that it was impossible for the tech company or anyone else to build the contracted-for computer \"in the present state of the art.\" If the university sues the tech company for failure to perform its computer contract, which party will the court most likely find in favor of?","choices":{"A":"The tech company, because a contract to build a machine using technology under development imposes only a duty on the builder to use its best efforts to achieve the result contracted for.","B":"The tech company, because its performance of the contract was objectively impossible.","C":"The university, because the law of impossibility does not apply to merchants under the applicable law.","D":"The university, because the tech company assumed the risk, in the given circumstances, that the projected new technology would not work reliably."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Commercial impracticability\n\n(UCC § 2-615)\n\nSeller's delay in delivery or nondelivery (in whole or in part) excused if:\n\nunanticipated or extraordinary event made it impossible or commercially impracticable for seller to perform as agreed nonoccurrence of event was basic assumption of contract seller did not assume risk of event's occurrence (eg, by contractual language) UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, computers) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, the doctrine of impracticability will excuse a seller's* nonperformance (eg, late or nondelivery of goods) if: an unanticipated or extraordinary event made it impossible or commercially impracticable for the seller to perform as agreed (Choice C) and the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur. But this doctrine does not apply if the seller assumed the risk of the event—eg, the circumstances indicate that the seller foresaw the risk yet failed to make contractual provision for it. Here, after entering the contract with the university, the tech company learned that the new technology it intended to use was unreliable and that it was impossible to build the contracted-for computer. However, there is always a foreseeable risk that new technology will not work reliably. Since the tech company failed to make contractual provision for this risk, it assumed the risk that the new technology would fail. Therefore, the tech company's failure to perform will not be excused for impracticability, and the court will likely find for the university (Choice B). *Under the UCC, impracticability almost always affects the seller (not the buyer) because the buyer's only obligation is to pay for the goods. (Choice A) There is no rule that a contract to build a machine using technology under development imposes only a duty on the builder to use its \"best efforts\" to achieve the contracted-for result.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC's doctrine of impracticability does not apply, and a party's performance will not be excused, if the party assumed the risk of the event giving rise to the impracticability.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-615 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (excuse by failure of presupposed conditions).","educationalObjective":"The UCC's doctrine of impracticability does not apply, and a party's performance will not be excused, if the party assumed the risk of the event giving rise to the impracticability.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-615 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (excuse by failure of presupposed conditions).","rule":"The UCC's doctrine of impracticability does not apply, and a party's performance will not be excused, if the party assumed the risk of the event giving rise to the impracticability.","irac":{"issue":"A tech company contracted with a private university to sell and deliver to the university a mainframe computer using a new type of technology then under development but not perfected by the company, at a price substantially lower than that of a similar computer using current technology. After making the contract with the university, the tech company discovered that the new technology it intended to use was unreliable and that no computer manufacturer could yet build a reliable computer using that technology. The tech company thereupon notified the university that it was impossible for the tech company or anyone else to build the contracted-for computer \"in the present state of the art.\" If the university sues the tech company for failure to perform its computer contract, which party will the court most likely find in favor of?","rule":"The UCC's doctrine of impracticability does not apply, and a party's performance will not be excused, if the party assumed the risk of the event giving rise to the impracticability.","analysis":"Commercial impracticability\n\n(UCC § 2-615)\n\nSeller's delay in delivery or nondelivery (in whole or in part) excused if:\n\nunanticipated or extraordinary event made it impossible or commercially impracticable for seller to perform as agreed nonoccurrence of event was basic assumption of contract seller did not assume risk of event's occurrence (eg, by contractual language) UCC = Uniform Commercial Code\n\nContracts for the sale of goods (eg, computers) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, the doctrine of impracticability will excuse a seller's* nonperformance (eg, late or nondelivery of goods) if: an unanticipated or extraordinary event made it impossible or commercially impracticable for the seller to perform as agreed (Choice C) and the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur. But this doctrine does not apply if the seller assumed the risk of the event—eg, the circumstances indicate that the seller foresaw the risk yet failed to make contractual provision for it. Here, after entering the contract with the university, the tech company learned that the new technology it intended to use was unreliable and that it was impossible to build the contracted-for computer. However, there is always a foreseeable risk that new technology will not work reliably. Since the tech company failed to make contractual provision for this risk, it assumed the risk that the new technology would fail. Therefore, the tech company's failure to perform will not be excused for impracticability, and the court will likely find for the university (Choice B). *Under the UCC, impracticability almost always affects the seller (not the buyer) because the buyer's only obligation is to pay for the goods. (Choice A) There is no rule that a contract to build a machine using technology under development imposes only a duty on the builder to use its \"best efforts\" to achieve the contracted-for result.","conclusion":"Answer D: The university, because the tech company assumed the risk, in the given circumstances, that the projected new technology would not work reliably."},"questionZh":"一家科技公司与一所私立大学签订合同，向该大学出售并交付一台采用当时正在开发但尚未完善的新型技术的大型计算机，其价格远低于采用当前技术的类似计算机。在与大学签订合同后，这家科技公司发现它打算使用的新技术并不可靠，而且还没有计算机制造商能够使用该技术制造出可靠的计算机。该科技公司随后通知大学，该科技公司或任何其他人都不可能“以目前的技术水平”建造合同计算机。如果大学起诉科技公司未能履行其计算机合同，法院最有可能支持哪一方？","choicesZh":{"A":"科技公司，因为使用正在开发的技术建造机器的合同只规定建造者有责任尽最大努力实现合同规定的结果。","B":"科技公司，因为其履行合同客观上是不可能的。","C":"大学，因为不可能法不适用于适用法律下的商人。","D":"大学，因为科技公司承担了在特定情况下预计的新技术无法可靠运行的风险。"},"explanationZh":"商业上不可行\n\n（UCC § 2-615）\n\n在以下情况下，卖方延迟交货或不交货（全部或部分）可以原谅：\n\n意外或异常事件使卖方不可能或在商业上不可行 事件不发生是合同的基本假设 卖方不承担事件发生的风险（例如，通过合同语言） UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n商品（例如计算机）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，在以下情况下，不可行原则将为卖方*不履约（例如，延迟或不交付货物）提供借口： 意外或异常事件导致卖方不可能或在商业上不可行（选择 C），并且合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的。但如果卖方承担了该事件的风险，例如，情况表明卖方预见到了该风险，但未能就此作出合同规定，则该原则不适用。在这里，在与大学签订合同后，科技公司得知其打算使用的新技术不可靠，并且不可能建造合同中的计算机。然而，总是存在可预见的风险，即新技术无法可靠地工作。由于科技公司未能就该风险制定合同规定，因此承担了新技术失败的风险。因此，科技公司的不履约不能成为不切实际的借口，法院很可能会判决大学胜诉（选项B）。 *根据 UCC，不切实际几乎总是影响卖方（而不是买方），因为买方的唯一义务是支付货款。 （选择A） 没有规定使用正在开发的技术建造机器的合同仅规定建造者有义务尽其“最大努力”来实现合同规定的结果。\n\n教育目标：UCC 的履行艰难（impracticability）原则不适用，如果一方承担了导致不切实际事件的风险，则该方的履约行为不会被免除。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-615 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（以不满足预设条件为由）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 的不可行原则不适用，如果一方承担了导致不可行的事件的风险，则该方的履行不会被免除。","iracZh":{"issue":"一家科技公司与一所私立大学签订合同，向该大学出售并交付一台采用当时正在开发但尚未完善的新型技术的大型计算机，其价格远低于采用当前技术的类似计算机。在与大学签订合同后，这家科技公司发现它打算使用的新技术并不可靠，而且还没有计算机制造商能够使用该技术制造出可靠的计算机。该科技公司随后通知大学，该科技公司或任何其他人都不可能“以目前的技术水平”建造合同计算机。如果大学起诉科技公司未能履行其计算机合同，法院最有可能支持哪一方？","rule":"UCC 的不可行原则不适用，如果一方承担了导致不可行的事件的风险，则该方的履行不会被免除。","analysis":"商业上不可行\n\n（UCC § 2-615）\n\n在以下情况下，卖方延迟交货或不交货（全部或部分）可以原谅：\n\n意外或异常事件使卖方不可能或在商业上不可行 事件不发生是合同的基本假设 卖方不承担事件发生的风险（例如，通过合同语言） UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）\n\n商品（例如计算机）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据 UCC，在以下情况下，不可行原则将为卖方*不履约（例如，延迟或不交付货物）提供借口： 意外或异常事件导致卖方不可能或在商业上不可行（选择 C），并且合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的。但如果卖方承担了该事件的风险，例如，情况表明卖方预见到了该风险，但未能就此作出合同规定，则该原则不适用。在这里，在与大学签订合同后，科技公司得知其打算使用的新技术不可靠，并且不可能建造合同中的计算机。然而，总是存在可预见的风险，即新技术无法可靠地工作。由于科技公司未能就该风险制定合同规定，因此承担了新技术失败的风险。因此，科技公司的不履约不能成为不切实际的借口，法院很可能会判决大学胜诉（选项B）。 *根据 UCC，不切实际几乎总是影响卖方（而不是买方），因为买方的唯一义务是支付货款。 （选择A） 没有规定使用正在开发的技术建造机器的合同仅规定建造者有义务尽其“最大努力”来实现合同规定的结果。\n\n教育目标：UCC 的履行艰难（impracticability）原则不适用，如果一方承担了导致不切实际事件的风险，则该方的履约行为不会被免除。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-615 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（以不满足预设条件为由）。","conclusion":"答案 D：大学，因为科技公司承担了在特定情况下预计的新技术无法可靠运行的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"best efforts","ipa":"/bˈɛst ˈɛfɝts/","syllables":"best ef·forts","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"最大努力：为实现约定目的采取合理积极措施","contextualMeaning":"最大努力：为实现约定目的采取合理积极措施","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The tech company, because a contract to build a machine using technology under development imposes only a duty on the builder to use its best efforts to achieve the result contracted for.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":251,"sourceQuestionPage":741,"sourceQuestionPages":[741],"sourceExplanationPages":[742,743],"sourceAnswerPages":[742,743],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-252","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"An engineer signed a two-year contract to serve as the chief safety engineer for a coal mine at a salary of $7,000 per month. The position required the engineer to work underground each workday. After one week on the job, the engineer became very ill. He requested testing of the mine air system, which revealed the presence of a chemical agent to which the engineer had a rare allergic reaction. The engineer promptly quit. The coal mine then hired a qualified replacement for the remainder of the engineer's contract at a salary of $7,500 per month. Assume that no statute or regulation applies. If the coal mine sues the engineer for breach of contract, is the coal mine likely to recover damages?","choices":{"A":"No, because an at-will employee has the right to terminate an employment contract.","B":"No, because the risk to the engineer's health excused his nonperformance of the contract.","C":"Yes, because the coal mine acted in good faith in mitigating the effect of the engineer's failure to finish the contract term.","D":"Yes, because the mine is reasonably safe for most people."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA contract is rescinded (ie, canceled) due to the occurrence of an unexpected or extraordinary event if: that event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform their duties the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. Rescission excuses both parties' nonperformance of the contract, which means that there can be no recovery of damages for breach. Here, the engineer contracted to work in a mine each workday for two years. After one week, the engineer unexpectedly became very ill due to a rare allergy to a chemical in the mine—making it impracticable, if not impossible, for him to perform his duties. The parties must have assumed that the engineer did not have this rare allergy, and nothing indicates that either party caused the chemical's presence in the mine. Therefore, the engineer's health excused his nonperformance, and the coal mine is unlikely to recover damages. (Choice A) The engineer was not an at-will employee since he agreed to work for a term of two years. But had he been one, he would have had the right to terminate his employment for any reason. (Choice C) After a breach of contract, the nonbreaching party must act in good faith in mitigating (ie, minimizing) his/her damages. But here, there was no breach since the engineer's nonperformance was excused. (Choice D) Although the mine is reasonably safe for most people, the doctrines of impossibility and impracticability apply even when one of the parties is particularly vulnerable to the unexpected event (as seen here).\n\nEducational objective: A contract is rescinded, and both parties' nonperformance is excused, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an unexpected event can discharge a party's duty to perform due to impossibility or impracticability).","educationalObjective":"A contract is rescinded, and both parties' nonperformance is excused, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an unexpected event can discharge a party's duty to perform due to impossibility or impracticability).","rule":"A contract is rescinded, and both parties' nonperformance is excused, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","irac":{"issue":"If the coal mine sues the engineer for breach of contract, is the coal mine likely to recover damages?","rule":"A contract is rescinded, and both parties' nonperformance is excused, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA contract is rescinded (ie, canceled) due to the occurrence of an unexpected or extraordinary event if: that event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform their duties the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. Rescission excuses both parties' nonperformance of the contract, which means that there can be no recovery of damages for breach. Here, the engineer contracted to work in a mine each workday for two years. After one week, the engineer unexpectedly became very ill due to a rare allergy to a chemical in the mine—making it impracticable, if not impossible, for him to perform his duties. The parties must have assumed that the engineer did not have this rare allergy, and nothing indicates that either party caused the chemical's presence in the mine. Therefore, the engineer's health excused his nonperformance, and the coal mine is unlikely to recover damages. (Choice A) The engineer was not an at-will employee since he agreed to work for a term of two years. But had he been one, he would have had the right to terminate his employment for any reason. (Choice C) After a breach of contract, the nonbreaching party must act in good faith in mitigating (ie, minimizing) his/her damages. But here, there was no breach since the engineer's nonperformance was excused. (Choice D) Although the mine is reasonably safe for most people, the doctrines of impossibility and impracticability apply even when one of the parties is particularly vulnerable to the unexpected event (as seen here).","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the risk to the engineer's health excused his nonperformance of the contract."},"questionZh":"一名工程师签订了一份为期两年的合同，担任一家煤矿的首席安全工程师，月薪为 7,000 美元。该职位要求工程师每个工作日都在地下工作。工作一周后，工程师病得很重。他要求对矿井空气系统进行测试，结果发现存在一种化学制剂，工程师对此产生了罕见的过敏反应。工程师立即辞职。随后，煤矿聘请了一名合格的替代者来履行该工程师合同的剩余部分，月薪为 7,500 美元。假设没有适用的法律或法规。如果煤矿起诉工程师违约，煤矿有可能追回损失吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为随意劳动者有权解除劳动合同。","B":"不，因为工程师的健康风险可以作为他不履行合同的理由。","C":"是的，因为煤矿真诚地减轻了工程师未能完成合同期限的影响。","D":"是的，因为矿井对大多数人来说相当安全。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n在以下情况下，合同因意外或异常事件的发生而被撤销（即取消）： 该事件使一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行其职责 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，并且任何一方都没有导致该事件发生的过错。解除合同是双方不履行合同的借口，这意味着违约损失无法获得赔偿。在这里，工程师签订了为期两年的每个工作日在矿井工作的合同。一周后，这位工程师意外地因对矿井中的一种化学物质过敏而病重，这使得他无法履行职责，甚至不可能履行职责。双方肯定都假设工程师没有这种罕见的过敏症，并且没有任何迹象表明任何一方导致了矿井中存在这种化学品。因此，工程师的健康状况可以原谅他的不履行职责，而煤矿也不太可能挽回损失。 （选项A） 该工程师不是随意雇员，因为他同意工作期限为两年。但如果他是其中之一，他就有权以任何理由终止雇佣关系。 （选择C） 违约后，守约方必须善意地采取行动，减轻（即最小化）其损失。但在这里，由于工程师的不履行职责得到了原谅，因此并没有发生违规行为。 （选择 D）虽然矿井对大多数人来说相当安全，但即使其中一方特别容易受到意外事件的影响，不可能和不切实际的原则也适用（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同被撤销，双方不履行合同均得到原谅：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行合同；(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的；(3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 261 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说意外事件可以因不可能或不切实际而解除当事人的履行义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当出现以下情况时，合同将被撤销，并且双方不履行合同均可以得到原谅：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行合同；(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的；(3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果煤矿起诉工程师违约，煤矿有可能追回损失吗？","rule":"当出现以下情况时，合同将被撤销，并且双方不履行合同均可以得到原谅：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行合同；(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的；(3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n在以下情况下，合同因意外或异常事件的发生而被撤销（即取消）： 该事件使一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行其职责 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，并且任何一方都没有导致该事件发生的过错。解除合同是双方不履行合同的借口，这意味着违约损失无法获得赔偿。在这里，工程师签订了为期两年的每个工作日在矿井工作的合同。一周后，这位工程师意外地因对矿井中的一种化学物质过敏而病重，这使得他无法履行职责，甚至不可能履行职责。双方肯定都假设工程师没有这种罕见的过敏症，并且没有任何迹象表明任何一方导致了矿井中存在这种化学品。因此，工程师的健康状况可以原谅他的不履行职责，而煤矿也不太可能挽回损失。 （选项A） 该工程师不是随意雇员，因为他同意工作期限为两年。但如果他是其中之一，他就有权以任何理由终止雇佣关系。 （选择C） 违约后，守约方必须善意地采取行动，减轻（即最小化）其损失。但在这里，由于工程师的不履行职责得到了原谅，因此并没有发生违规行为。 （选择 D）虽然矿井对大多数人来说相当安全，但即使其中一方特别容易受到意外事件的影响，不可能和不切实际的原则也适用（如此处所示）。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同被撤销，双方不履行合同均得到原谅：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行合同；(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的；(3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 261 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说意外事件可以因不可能或不切实际而解除当事人的履行义务）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为工程师的健康风险可以作为他不履行合同的理由。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":252,"sourceQuestionPage":744,"sourceQuestionPages":[744],"sourceExplanationPages":[745,746],"sourceAnswerPages":[745,746],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-253","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Impossibility, Impracticability & Frustration","question":"On August 1, a nursing home for the elderly admitted an 84-year-old man for a trial period of two months. On September 25, the man and the nursing home entered into a written lifetime-care contract with an effective commencement date of October 1. The full contract price was $20,000, which, as required by the terms of the contract, the man prepaid to the nursing home on September 25. The nursing home rejected a woman's application for lifetime care on September 26 due to its commitment to the man. On October 2, the man died of a heart attack. In a restitutionary action, will the administrator of the man's estate likely recover on behalf of the estate either all or part of the $20,000 paid to the nursing home on September 25?","choices":{"A":"No, because the man's life span and the duration of the nursing home's commitment to him were risks assumed by both parties.","B":"No, because the nursing home rejected the woman's application due to its commitment to the man.","C":"Yes, because the doctrine of frustration of purpose applies.","D":"Yes, because the nursing home would otherwise be unjustly enriched at the man's expense."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA contract can be rescinded after an unexpected event if (1) the event frustrates the purpose of the contract, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. The frustrating event must be so severe that it cannot fairly be regarded as within the risks that the parties assumed under the contract. Additionally, a party who conferred a benefit before the contract was rescinded can recover restitutionary damages to prevent unjust enrichment. Here, the 84-year-old man and the nursing home entered into a written contract in which he prepaid $20,000 for lifetime care that began on October 1. Although the man unexpectedly died one day later, his life span and the duration of the nursing home's commitment to him were risks assumed by both parties. That is because: the man assumed the risk that he would die before receiving the full value of his payment and the nursing home assumed the risk that the man would live so long that his payment would be insufficient. Therefore, the doctrine of frustration of purpose does not apply, and the administrator of the man's estate is unlikely to recover restitutionary damages for all or part of the $20,000 paid to the nursing home (Choice C). (Choice B) The fact that the nursing home rejected the woman's application has no effect on the outcome of this dispute. (Choice D) Unjust enrichment occurs when a party receives a benefit from another that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. But here, it would not be unfair for the nursing home to retain the $20,000, and the nursing home rightfully earned it, because the nursing home assumed the risk that the man would live so long that his payment would be inadequate.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of frustration of purpose applies only where the frustrating event is so severe that it cannot fairly be regarded as within the risks that the parties assumed under the contract.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 265 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (frustration-of-purpose doctrine).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 377 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (restitution for frustration of purpose).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of frustration of purpose applies only where the frustrating event is so severe that it cannot fairly be regarded as within the risks that the parties assumed under the contract.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 265 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (frustration-of-purpose doctrine).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 377 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (restitution for frustration of purpose).","rule":"The doctrine of frustration of purpose applies only where the frustrating event is so severe that it cannot fairly be regarded as within the risks that the parties assumed under the contract.","irac":{"issue":"In a restitutionary action, will the administrator of the man's estate likely recover on behalf of the estate either all or part of the $20,000 paid to the nursing home on September 25?","rule":"The doctrine of frustration of purpose applies only where the frustrating event is so severe that it cannot fairly be regarded as within the risks that the parties assumed under the contract.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA contract can be rescinded after an unexpected event if (1) the event frustrates the purpose of the contract, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. The frustrating event must be so severe that it cannot fairly be regarded as within the risks that the parties assumed under the contract. Additionally, a party who conferred a benefit before the contract was rescinded can recover restitutionary damages to prevent unjust enrichment. Here, the 84-year-old man and the nursing home entered into a written contract in which he prepaid $20,000 for lifetime care that began on October 1. Although the man unexpectedly died one day later, his life span and the duration of the nursing home's commitment to him were risks assumed by both parties. That is because: the man assumed the risk that he would die before receiving the full value of his payment and the nursing home assumed the risk that the man would live so long that his payment would be insufficient. Therefore, the doctrine of frustration of purpose does not apply, and the administrator of the man's estate is unlikely to recover restitutionary damages for all or part of the $20,000 paid to the nursing home (Choice C). (Choice B) The fact that the nursing home rejected the woman's application has no effect on the outcome of this dispute. (Choice D) Unjust enrichment occurs when a party receives a benefit from another that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. But here, it would not be unfair for the nursing home to retain the $20,000, and the nursing home rightfully earned it, because the nursing home assumed the risk that the man would live so long that his payment would be inadequate.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the man's life span and the duration of the nursing home's commitment to him were risks assumed by both parties."},"questionZh":"8月1日，一家敬老院收治了一名84岁的男子，试用期两个月。 9月25日，该男子与疗养院签订了书面终身护理合同，生效日期为10月1日。合同全额价格为2万美元，按照合同条款要求，该男子于9月25日向疗养院预付。疗养院以对男子的承诺为由，于9月26日拒绝了女子的终身护理申请。 10月2日，该男子因心脏病去世。在赔偿行动中，该男子遗产管理人是否可能代表遗产收回 9 月 25 日支付给疗养院的 20,000 美元的全部或部分？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为男人的寿命和养老院对他的承诺期限都是双方承担的风险。","B":"不，因为疗养院因对男子的承诺而拒绝了女子的申请。","C":"是的，因为目的受挫原则适用。","D":"是的，因为否则疗养院就会以牺牲该男子的利益为代价而获得不公正的财富。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n如果发生意外事件，合同可以被撤销，如果（1）该事件妨碍了合同的目的，（2）合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，并且（3）任何一方都没有导致该事件发生的过错。令人沮丧的事件必须严重到不能公平地被视为在双方根据合同承担的风险范围之内。此外，在合同解除前给予利益的一方可以追回损害赔偿，以防止不当得利。在这里，这位84岁的老人和疗养院签订了一份书面合同，其中他预付了2万美元的终生护理费用，从10月1日开始。尽管该男子在一天后意外死亡，但他的寿命和疗养院对他的承诺期限都是双方承担的风险。这是因为：该男子承担了他在收到全额付款之前死亡的风险，而疗养院承担了该男子寿命太长而其付款不足的风险。因此，目的受挫原则不适用，该男子的遗产管理人不太可能针对支付给疗养院的全部或部分 20,000 美元追回损害赔偿（选项 C）。 （选项B） 疗养院拒绝妇女的申请这一事实对本次纠纷的结果没有影响。 （选项D）不当得利是指一方从另一方获得并非正当赚取且不公平保留的利益。但在这里，疗养院保留这2万美元并没有什么不公平的，而且疗养院理所当然地赚到了这笔钱，因为疗养院承担了该男子寿命太长而支付的费用不足的风险。\n\n教育目标：目的受挫原则仅适用于受挫事件严重到无法公平地视为双方根据合同承担的风险的情况。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 265（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（目的落空原则）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 377（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（因目的受挫而恢复原状）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"目的受挫原则仅适用于受挫事件严重到无法公平地视为双方根据合同承担的风险的情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"在赔偿行动中，该男子遗产管理人是否可能代表遗产收回 9 月 25 日支付给疗养院的 20,000 美元的全部或部分？","rule":"目的受挫原则仅适用于受挫事件严重到无法公平地视为双方根据合同承担的风险的情况。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n如果发生意外事件，合同可以被撤销，如果（1）该事件妨碍了合同的目的，（2）合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，并且（3）任何一方都没有导致该事件发生的过错。令人沮丧的事件必须严重到不能公平地被视为在双方根据合同承担的风险范围之内。此外，在合同解除前给予利益的一方可以追回损害赔偿，以防止不当得利。在这里，这位84岁的老人和疗养院签订了一份书面合同，其中他预付了2万美元的终生护理费用，从10月1日开始。尽管该男子在一天后意外死亡，但他的寿命和疗养院对他的承诺期限都是双方承担的风险。这是因为：该男子承担了他在收到全额付款之前死亡的风险，而疗养院承担了该男子寿命太长而其付款不足的风险。因此，目的受挫原则不适用，该男子的遗产管理人不太可能针对支付给疗养院的全部或部分 20,000 美元追回损害赔偿（选项 C）。 （选项B） 疗养院拒绝妇女的申请这一事实对本次纠纷的结果没有影响。 （选项D）不当得利是指一方从另一方获得并非正当赚取且不公平保留的利益。但在这里，疗养院保留这2万美元并没有什么不公平的，而且疗养院理所当然地赚到了这笔钱，因为疗养院承担了该男子寿命太长而支付的费用不足的风险。\n\n教育目标：目的受挫原则仅适用于受挫事件严重到无法公平地视为双方根据合同承担的风险的情况。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 265（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（目的落空原则）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 377（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（因目的受挫而恢复原状）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为男人的寿命和养老院对他的承诺期限都是双方承担的风险。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":253,"sourceQuestionPage":747,"sourceQuestionPages":[747],"sourceExplanationPages":[748,749],"sourceAnswerPages":[748,749],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-254","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A sailor owns an exceptionally seaworthy boat that she charters for sport fishing at a $500 daily rate. The fee includes the use of the boat with the sailor as the captain, and one other crew member, as well as fishing tackle and bait. On May 1, a customer agreed with the sailor that the customer would have the full-day use of the boat on May 15 for himself and his family for $500. The customer paid an advance deposit of $200 and signed an agreement that the deposit could be retained by the sailor as liquidated damages in the event the customer canceled or failed to appear. On May 15 at 1:00 a.m., the Coast Guard issued offshore \"heavy weather\" warnings and prohibited all small vessels the size of the sailor's boat from leaving the harbor. This prohibition remained in effect throughout the day. The customer did not appear at all on May 15, because he had heard the weather warnings on his radio. Which of the following is an accurate statement of the parties' legal relationship?","choices":{"A":"The contract is discharged because of impossibility, and the customer is entitled to return of his deposit.","B":"The contract is discharged because of mutual mistake concerning an essential fact, and the customer is entitled to return of his deposit.","C":"The contract is not discharged, and the customer is not entitled to return of his deposit, because the liquidated-damage clause in effect allocated the risk of bad weather to the customer.","D":"The contract is not discharged, because its performance was possible in view of the exceptional seaworthiness of the sailor's boat, and the customer is not entitled to return of his deposit."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA contract is canceled or rescinded due to the occurrence of an unexpected or extraordinary event if: that event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform their duties* the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. Rescission excuses both parties' nonperformance of the contract (ie, discharges their obligations). This means that damages for breach cannot be recovered and any deposit or other benefit conferred on the other party must be returned. Here, the customer contracted to use the sailor's boat and paid a $200 deposit. But the Coast Guard issued a heavy-weather warning and prohibited all small vessels from leaving the harbor—making it objectively impossible to use the boat despite its exceptional seaworthiness (Choice D). The parties must have assumed that the Coast Guard would not prohibit the use of the boat, and neither party could control its decision to do so. Therefore, the contract is discharged because of impossibility, and the customer is entitled to return of his deposit. *Impossibility will generally be found only in these specific situations: (1) the death or incapacity of someone necessary for performance, (2) the destruction of the contract's subject matter, and (3) the prevention of performance by governmental regulation or order (as seen here). (Choice B) In contract law, a mistake is an erroneous belief regarding the facts and/or law as they exist at the time of contracting. This could not be a case of mutual mistake since the Coast Guard prohibition was not in effect when the contract was entered.\n\n(Choice C) A liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large that it punishes the breaching party (as opposed to merely compensating the nonbreaching party). But this would not be a reason to discharge a contract.\n\nEducational objective: A contract is rescinded, and both parties' contractual obligations are discharged, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes performance impossible or impracticable, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an unexpected event can discharge a party's duty to perform due to impossibility or impracticability).","educationalObjective":"A contract is rescinded, and both parties' contractual obligations are discharged, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes performance impossible or impracticable, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that an unexpected event can discharge a party's duty to perform due to impossibility or impracticability).","rule":"A contract is rescinded, and both parties' contractual obligations are discharged, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes performance impossible or impracticable, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following is an accurate statement of the parties' legal relationship?","rule":"A contract is rescinded, and both parties' contractual obligations are discharged, when (1) an unexpected or extraordinary event makes performance impossible or impracticable, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur.","analysis":"Ways to discharge contractual obligations\n\nFull performance of contractual obligations Impossibility, impracticability, or frustration of purpose Release (in writing only) Mutual rescission Substituted contract Contract or covenant not to sue Accord & satisfaction Novation Mnemonic: FIRM SCAN\n\nA contract is canceled or rescinded due to the occurrence of an unexpected or extraordinary event if: that event makes it impossible or impracticable for one or both parties to perform their duties* the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and neither party was at fault in causing the event to occur. Rescission excuses both parties' nonperformance of the contract (ie, discharges their obligations). This means that damages for breach cannot be recovered and any deposit or other benefit conferred on the other party must be returned. Here, the customer contracted to use the sailor's boat and paid a $200 deposit. But the Coast Guard issued a heavy-weather warning and prohibited all small vessels from leaving the harbor—making it objectively impossible to use the boat despite its exceptional seaworthiness (Choice D). The parties must have assumed that the Coast Guard would not prohibit the use of the boat, and neither party could control its decision to do so. Therefore, the contract is discharged because of impossibility, and the customer is entitled to return of his deposit. *Impossibility will generally be found only in these specific situations: (1) the death or incapacity of someone necessary for performance, (2) the destruction of the contract's subject matter, and (3) the prevention of performance by governmental regulation or order (as seen here). (Choice B) In contract law, a mistake is an erroneous belief regarding the facts and/or law as they exist at the time of contracting. This could not be a case of mutual mistake since the Coast Guard prohibition was not in effect when the contract was entered.\n\n(Choice C) A liquidated-damages clause is unenforceable as a penalty if the amount is so unreasonably large that it punishes the breaching party (as opposed to merely compensating the nonbreaching party). But this would not be a reason to discharge a contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: The contract is discharged because of impossibility, and the customer is entitled to return of his deposit."},"questionZh":"一位水手拥有一艘非常适合航海的船，她以每天 500 美元的价格租用这艘船进行游钓。费用包括使用由水手担任船长和另一名船员的船只，以及渔具和鱼饵。 5月1日，一位顾客与水手达成一致，顾客可以在5月15日以500美元的价格为自己和家人全天使用这艘船。顾客预付了200美元的押金，并签署了一份协议，如果顾客取消预订或未能出现，水手可以保留这笔押金作为违约金。 5月15日凌晨1点，海岸警卫队发布近海“恶劣天气”警告，并禁止所有水手船大小的小型船只离开港口。该禁令全天有效。 5 月 15 日，这位顾客根本没有出现，因为他听到了收音机里的天气警报。下列哪一项是对双方法律关系的准确表述？","choicesZh":{"A":"合同因不可能而解除，客户有权退还押金。","B":"由于双方对基本事实的错误而解除合同，客户有权退还押金。","C":"合同并未解除，客户无权退还押金，因为违约金条款实际上将恶劣天气的风险分配给了客户。","D":"合同未被解除，因为考虑到水手的船具有出色的适航性，合同的履行是可能的，并且客户无权退还其押金。"},"explanationZh":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n如果出现以下情况，则合同因意外或异常事件的发生而被取消或撤销： 该事件导致一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行其职责* 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，并且双方均无过错导致该事件发生。解除合同可以为双方不履行合同提供借口（即解除其义务）。这意味着违约造成的损失无法追回，并且必须退还对方的任何押金或其他利益。在这里，客户签订了使用水手船的合同并支付了 200 美元的押金。但海岸警卫队发布了恶劣天气警告，并禁止所有小型船只离开港口，这使得尽管该船具有出色的适航性，但客观上无法使用该船（选项 D）。双方肯定都假设海岸警卫队不会禁止使用该船，而且双方都无法控制其这样做的决定。因此，合同因不可能而解除，客户有权退还押金。 *通常仅在以下特定情况下才会发现不可能性：(1) 履行所需人员死亡或丧失能力，(2) 合同标的物遭到破坏，以及 (3) 政府法规或命令阻止履行（如此处所示）。 （选项 B） 在合同法中，错误是指对订立合同时存在的事实和/或法律的错误信念。这不可能是共同错误的情况，因为签订合同时海岸警卫队的禁令尚未生效。\n\n（选项C）如果违约金条款的金额过大以致于惩罚违约方（而不是仅补偿非违约方），则该违约金条款作为处罚是不可执行的。但这不能成为解除合同的理由。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同被撤销，双方的合同义务均被解除：(1) 意外或异常事件导致履行不可能或不切实际，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下订立的，以及 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 261 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说意外事件可以因不可能或不切实际而解除当事人的履行义务）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当出现以下情况时，合同被撤销，双方的合同义务均被解除：(1) 意外或异常事件导致履行不可能或不切实际，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，以及 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。","iracZh":{"issue":"下列哪一项是对双方法律关系的准确表述？","rule":"当出现以下情况时，合同被撤销，双方的合同义务均被解除：(1) 意外或异常事件导致履行不可能或不切实际，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，以及 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。","analysis":"履行合同义务的方式\n\n完全履行合同义务 不可能、不切实际或目的受挫 解除（仅限书面形式） 相互解除 替代合同 不起诉的合同或契约 一致和满意 更替助记符：FIRM SCAN\n\n如果出现以下情况，则合同因意外或异常事件的发生而被取消或撤销： 该事件导致一方或双方不可能或不切实际地履行其职责* 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，并且双方均无过错导致该事件发生。解除合同可以为双方不履行合同提供借口（即解除其义务）。这意味着违约造成的损失无法追回，并且必须退还对方的任何押金或其他利益。在这里，客户签订了使用水手船的合同并支付了 200 美元的押金。但海岸警卫队发布了恶劣天气警告，并禁止所有小型船只离开港口，这使得尽管该船具有出色的适航性，但客观上无法使用该船（选项 D）。双方肯定都假设海岸警卫队不会禁止使用该船，而且双方都无法控制其这样做的决定。因此，合同因不可能而解除，客户有权退还押金。 *通常仅在以下特定情况下才会发现不可能性：(1) 履行所需人员死亡或丧失能力，(2) 合同标的物遭到破坏，以及 (3) 政府法规或命令阻止履行（如此处所示）。 （选项 B） 在合同法中，错误是指对订立合同时存在的事实和/或法律的错误信念。这不可能是共同错误的情况，因为签订合同时海岸警卫队的禁令尚未生效。\n\n（选项C）如果违约金条款的金额过大以致于惩罚违约方（而不是仅补偿非违约方），则该违约金条款作为处罚是不可执行的。但这不能成为解除合同的理由。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，合同被撤销，双方的合同义务均被解除：(1) 意外或异常事件导致履行不可能或不切实际，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下订立的，以及 (3) 任何一方均无过错导致该事件发生。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 261 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说意外事件可以因不可能或不切实际而解除当事人的履行义务）。","conclusion":"答案 A：合同因不可能而解除，客户有权退还押金。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":254,"sourceQuestionPage":750,"sourceQuestionPages":[750],"sourceExplanationPages":[751,752],"sourceAnswerPages":[751,752],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-255","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A buyer contracted with a seller to purchase 10,000 bushels of soybeans at market price. The soybeans were to be delivered in 90 days. Two days after the soybean contract was made, the buyer and the seller entered into another contract under which the buyer agreed to purchase 10,000 bushels of wheat from the seller at market price. Before the time for delivery of the soybeans, the seller notified the buyer that it would not deliver the wheat because the seller's wheat supplier had refused to extend additional credit to the seller and therefore the seller had no wheat available for the buyer. Which of the following statements best describes the effect of the seller's repudiation of the wheat contract on the buyer's rights under the soybean contract?","choices":{"A":"It gives the buyer the right to demand assurances that the seller will perform the soybean contract.","B":"It gives the buyer the right to terminate the soybean contract, because of the doctrine of dependent covenants.","C":"It gives the buyer the right to terminate the soybean contract, because the buyer does not have to deal with a party that breaches a contract.","D":"It has no effect on the buyer's rights, because the two contracts are entirely separate."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, soybeans) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, if one party has reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance, the insecure party has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Here, the seller notified the buyer that it was repudiating the wheat contract because it was unable to get additional credit from its wheat supplier and therefore had no wheat available for the buyer. This gave the buyer reasonable grounds for insecurity about the seller's ability to obtain and deliver another promised commodity—the soybeans. Accordingly, the buyer has the right to demand assurances that the seller will perform the separate soybean contract (Choice D). (Choice B) The relevant covenants in this case are not dependent since there is no indication that the buyer's duty to perform under the soybean contract is dependent on the seller's performance of the wheat contract. (Choice C) There is no rule that gives a party the right to terminate a contract because the other party breached an entirely different contract.\n\nEducational objective: The UCC gives a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).","educationalObjective":"The UCC gives a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).","rule":"The UCC gives a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements best describes the effect of the seller's repudiation of the wheat contract on the buyer's rights under the soybean contract?","rule":"The UCC gives a party with reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract.","analysis":"Contracts for the sale of goods (eg, soybeans) are governed by Article 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC). Under the UCC, if one party has reasonable grounds for insecurity about the other's performance, the insecure party has the right to make a written demand for assurances that the other party will perform the contract. Here, the seller notified the buyer that it was repudiating the wheat contract because it was unable to get additional credit from its wheat supplier and therefore had no wheat available for the buyer. This gave the buyer reasonable grounds for insecurity about the seller's ability to obtain and deliver another promised commodity—the soybeans. Accordingly, the buyer has the right to demand assurances that the seller will perform the separate soybean contract (Choice D). (Choice B) The relevant covenants in this case are not dependent since there is no indication that the buyer's duty to perform under the soybean contract is dependent on the seller's performance of the wheat contract. (Choice C) There is no rule that gives a party the right to terminate a contract because the other party breached an entirely different contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: It gives the buyer the right to demand assurances that the seller will perform the soybean contract."},"questionZh":"买方与卖方签订合同，以市场价格购买 10,000 蒲式耳大豆。大豆将于 90 天内交货。大豆合约签订两天后，买方和卖方又签订了另一份合同，买方同意以市场价格向卖方购买 10,000 蒲式耳小麦。在大豆交割时间之前，卖方通知买方不会交付小麦，因为卖方的小麦供应商拒绝向卖方提供额外信贷，因此卖方没有可供买方使用的小麦。以下哪项陈述最准确地描述了卖方拒绝小麦合同对大豆合同下买方权利的影响？","choicesZh":{"A":"它赋予买方要求卖方保证履行大豆合同的权利。","B":"由于附属契约原则，它赋予买方终止大豆合同的权利。","C":"它赋予买方终止大豆合同的权利，因为买方不必与违约方打交道。","D":"它对买方的权利没有影响，因为两份合同是完全分开的。"},"explanationZh":"商品（例如大豆）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据《统一商法典》（UCC），如果一方有合理理由对另一方的履行存在不安全感，则缺乏安全感的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。在此，卖方通知买方，它拒绝履行小麦合同，因为它无法从小麦供应商那里获得额外的信贷，因此没有小麦可供买方使用。这给买方提供了合理的理由，让他们对卖方获得和交付另一种承诺的商品——大豆的能力感到不安全。因此，买方有权要求卖方保证履行单独的大豆合同（选择D）。 （选项B） 本案中的相关契约不具有依赖性，因为没有迹象表明买方在大豆合同下的履行义务取决于卖方对小麦合同的履行。 （选项C） 没有任何规则规定一方有权因另一方违反了完全不同的合同而终止合同。\n\n教育目标：UCC 赋予有合理理由对另一方履行合同不放心的一方提出书面要求以保证另一方将履行合同的权利。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"UCC 赋予一方有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的权利，以书面要求保证另一方将履行合同。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述最准确地描述了卖方拒绝小麦合同对大豆合同下买方权利的影响？","rule":"UCC 赋予一方有合理理由对另一方的履约不安全的权利，以书面要求保证另一方将履行合同。","analysis":"商品（例如大豆）销售合同受《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖。根据《统一商法典》（UCC），如果一方有合理理由对另一方的履行存在不安全感，则缺乏安全感的一方有权提出书面要求，要求保证另一方将履行合同。在此，卖方通知买方，它拒绝履行小麦合同，因为它无法从小麦供应商那里获得额外的信贷，因此没有小麦可供买方使用。这给买方提供了合理的理由，让他们对卖方获得和交付另一种承诺的商品——大豆的能力感到不安全。因此，买方有权要求卖方保证履行单独的大豆合同（选择D）。 （选项B） 本案中的相关契约不具有依赖性，因为没有迹象表明买方在大豆合同下的履行义务取决于卖方对小麦合同的履行。 （选项C） 没有任何规则规定一方有权因另一方违反了完全不同的合同而终止合同。\n\n教育目标：UCC 赋予有合理理由对另一方履行合同不放心的一方提出书面要求以保证另一方将履行合同的权利。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。","conclusion":"答案 A：它赋予买方要求卖方保证履行大豆合同的权利。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-5d663b8546088378"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":255,"sourceQuestionPage":753,"sourceQuestionPages":[753],"sourceExplanationPages":[754],"sourceAnswerPages":[754],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-256","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"On April 1, a mechanic contracted to repair a toy company's molding machine and to complete the job by April 6. On April 2, the toy company contracted to manufacture and deliver specified toys to a customer on April 15. The toy company knew that it would have to use the machine then under repair to perform this contract. Because the customer's order was for a weeklong toy conference, the customer and the toy company included in their contract a liquidated damages clause, providing that the toy company would pay $5,000 for each day's delay in delivery after April 15. The mechanic was inexcusably five days late in repairing the machine, and, as a result, the toy company was five days late in delivering the toys to the customer. The toy company paid $25,000 to the customer as liquidated damages and now sues the mechanic for $25,000. Both the mechanic and the toy company knew when making their contract on April 1 that, under ordinary circumstances, the toy company would sustain little or no damages of any kind as a result of a five-day delay in the machine repair. Assuming that the $5,000 liquidated damages clause in the customer's contract is valid, which of the following arguments will serve as the mechanic's best defense to the toy company's action?","choices":{"A":"By entering into the contract with the customer while knowing that its molding machine was being repaired, the toy company assumed the risk of any delay loss to the customer.","B":"In all probability, the liquidated damages paid by the toy company to the customer are not the same amount as the actual damages sustained by the customer in consequence of the late delivery of the toys.","C":"The mechanic had no reason to foresee on April 1 that the toy company would suffer consequential damages in the amount of $25,000.","D":"Time was not of the essence in the contract between the mechanic and the toy company."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach After establishing a breach-of-contract claim, the nonbreaching party is entitled to recover compensatory damages to compensate for actual economic losses. Compensatory damages include: expectation damages – the lost value of the breaching party's performance and consequential damages – losses that arise from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances (eg, an unrelated contract) that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when his/her contract was formed. Here, the mechanic was inexcusably five days late in repairing the toy company's molding machine. As a result, the company lost $25,000 under its unrelated contract with the customer (special circumstance) formed on April 2. But when the mechanic contracted with the company on April 1, he knew that the company ordinarily would sustain little or no damages from a five-day delay. Therefore, the mechanic's best defense to the company's action is that on April 1 he had no reason to foresee the company's consequential damages. (Choice A) Consequential damages are unavailable if the nonbreaching party assumed the risk of that loss when the contract was formed. This is true even when the loss was foreseeable to the breaching party. But here, the toy company's allocation of risk with the customer has no bearing on its allocation of risk with the mechanic. (Choice B) A liquidated damages clause is invalid if the liquidated amount was unreasonable in relation to the damages that might be sustained. But since liquidated damages are designed to protect the parties when the actual damages are uncertain, liquidated damages can differ from the actual damages sustained. (Choice D) To determine whether a party's late performance is a material breach, courts consider all the circumstances, including whether the contract stated that time was of the essence. But even if this language is missing (as seen here), a party's late performance can still be a material breach.\n\nEducational objective: Compensatory damages for breach include (1) expectation damages for the lost value of the breaching party's performance and (2) consequential damages for losses that arise from the\n\nnonbreaching party's special circumstances that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was formed.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 351 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that consequential damages must be foreseeable).","educationalObjective":"Compensatory damages for breach include (1) expectation damages for the lost value of the breaching party's performance and (2) consequential damages for losses that arise from the\n\nnonbreaching party's special circumstances that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was formed.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 351 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that consequential damages must be foreseeable).","rule":"Compensatory damages for breach include (1) expectation damages for the lost value of the breaching party's performance and (2) consequential damages for losses that arise from the\n\nnonbreaching party's special circumstances that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was formed.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that the $5,000 liquidated damages clause in the customer's contract is valid, which of the following arguments will serve as the mechanic's best defense to the toy company's action?","rule":"Compensatory damages for breach include (1) expectation damages for the lost value of the breaching party's performance and (2) consequential damages for losses that arise from the\n\nnonbreaching party's special circumstances that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was formed.","analysis":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach After establishing a breach-of-contract claim, the nonbreaching party is entitled to recover compensatory damages to compensate for actual economic losses. Compensatory damages include: expectation damages – the lost value of the breaching party's performance and consequential damages – losses that arise from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances (eg, an unrelated contract) that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when his/her contract was formed. Here, the mechanic was inexcusably five days late in repairing the toy company's molding machine. As a result, the company lost $25,000 under its unrelated contract with the customer (special circumstance) formed on April 2. But when the mechanic contracted with the company on April 1, he knew that the company ordinarily would sustain little or no damages from a five-day delay. Therefore, the mechanic's best defense to the company's action is that on April 1 he had no reason to foresee the company's consequential damages. (Choice A) Consequential damages are unavailable if the nonbreaching party assumed the risk of that loss when the contract was formed. This is true even when the loss was foreseeable to the breaching party. But here, the toy company's allocation of risk with the customer has no bearing on its allocation of risk with the mechanic. (Choice B) A liquidated damages clause is invalid if the liquidated amount was unreasonable in relation to the damages that might be sustained. But since liquidated damages are designed to protect the parties when the actual damages are uncertain, liquidated damages can differ from the actual damages sustained. (Choice D) To determine whether a party's late performance is a material breach, courts consider all the circumstances, including whether the contract stated that time was of the essence. But even if this language is missing (as seen here), a party's late performance can still be a material breach.","conclusion":"Answer C: The mechanic had no reason to foresee on April 1 that the toy company would suffer consequential damages in the amount of $25,000."},"questionZh":"4 月 1 日，一名机械师签约修理一家玩具公司的成型机，并在 4 月 6 日之前完成工作。4 月 2 日，该玩具公司签约生产指定玩具，并于 4 月 15 日向客户交付。该玩具公司知道必须使用当时正在维修的机器来履行该合同。由于客户的订单是为期一周的玩具会议，客户和玩具公司在合同中包含了违约金条款，规定玩具公司将在 4 月 15 日之后为每延迟一天的交货支付 5,000 美元。机械师修理机器的时间迟到了 5 天，结果是玩具公司迟到了 5 天才向客户交付玩具。该玩具公司向客户支付了 25,000 美元作为违约金，现在起诉机械师，要求赔偿 25,000 美元。机械师和玩具公司在 4 月 1 日签订合同时都知道，在正常情况下，玩具公司不会因为机器维修延迟五天而遭受很少或不会造成任何损害。假设客户合同中 5,000 美元的违约金条款有效，以下哪项论据将作为机械师对玩具公司诉讼的最佳辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"玩具公司在知道客户的成型机正在维修的情况下与客户签订合同，承担了客户延迟损失的风险。","B":"玩具公司向顾客支付的违约金很可能与顾客因玩具迟交而遭受的实际损失并不相同。","C":"机械师没有理由在 4 月 1 日预见到玩具公司将遭受 25,000 美元的间接损失。","D":"在机械师和玩具公司之间的合同中，时间并不是最重要的。"},"explanationZh":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 在提出违约索赔后，守约方有权要求赔偿损失，以补偿实际经济损失。补偿性损害赔偿包括：预期损害赔偿——违约方履约的价值损失和后果性损害——因守约方在合同成立时可以合理预见的特殊情况（例如，不相关的合同）而产生的损失。在这里，机械师修理玩具公司的成型机迟到了五天，这是不可原谅的。结果，该公司根据 4 月 2 日与客户签订的无关合同（特殊情况）损失了 25,000 美元。但是，当机械师于 4 月 1 日与该公司签订合同时，他知道公司通常不会因为五天的延误而遭受很少的损失或不会遭受损失。因此，机械师对公司行为的最佳辩护是，在4月1日，他没有理由预见到公司的后果性损失。 （选择A）如果非违约方在合同成立时承担了损失的风险，则无法获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。即使违约方可以预见损失，情况也是如此。但在这里，玩具公司与客户的风险分配与其与机械师的风险分配无关。 （选项B）如果违约金相对于可能遭受的损害不合理，则违约金条款无效。但由于违约金旨在在实际损失不确定时保护当事人，因此违约金可能与实际遭受的损失不同。 （选项D）为了确定一方的逾期履约是否构成重大违约，法院会考虑所有情况，包括合同中是否规定时间至关重要。但即使缺少这种语言（如此处所示），一方迟到的表现仍然可能构成重大违约。\n\n教育目标：违约的补偿性损害赔偿包括 (1) 违约方履约损失价值的预期损害赔偿，以及 (2) 因违约行为而产生的损失的后果性损害赔偿\n\n守约方在合同成立时可以合理预见的特殊情况。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 351（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释间接损害必须是可预见的）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"违约赔偿包括（1）违约方履约损失价值的预期损害赔偿和（2）因违约方的行为而产生的损失的后果性损害赔偿。\n\n守约方在合同成立时可以合理预见的特殊情况。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设客户合同中 5,000 美元的违约金条款有效，以下哪项论据将作为机械师对玩具公司诉讼的最佳辩护？","rule":"违约赔偿包括（1）违约方履约损失价值的预期损害赔偿和（2）因违约方的行为而产生的损失的后果性损害赔偿。\n\n守约方在合同成立时可以合理预见的特殊情况。","analysis":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 在提出违约索赔后，守约方有权要求赔偿损失，以补偿实际经济损失。补偿性损害赔偿包括：预期损害赔偿——违约方履约的价值损失和后果性损害——因守约方在合同成立时可以合理预见的特殊情况（例如，不相关的合同）而产生的损失。在这里，机械师修理玩具公司的成型机迟到了五天，这是不可原谅的。结果，该公司根据 4 月 2 日与客户签订的无关合同（特殊情况）损失了 25,000 美元。但是，当机械师于 4 月 1 日与该公司签订合同时，他知道公司通常不会因为五天的延误而遭受很少的损失或不会遭受损失。因此，机械师对公司行为的最佳辩护是，在4月1日，他没有理由预见到公司的后果性损失。 （选择A）如果非违约方在合同成立时承担了损失的风险，则无法获得后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。即使违约方可以预见损失，情况也是如此。但在这里，玩具公司与客户的风险分配与其与机械师的风险分配无关。 （选项B）如果违约金相对于可能遭受的损害不合理，则违约金条款无效。但由于违约金旨在在实际损失不确定时保护当事人，因此违约金可能与实际遭受的损失不同。 （选项D）为了确定一方的逾期履约是否构成重大违约，法院会考虑所有情况，包括合同中是否规定时间至关重要。但即使缺少这种语言（如此处所示），一方迟到的表现仍然可能构成重大违约。\n\n教育目标：违约的补偿性损害赔偿包括 (1) 违约方履约损失价值的预期损害赔偿，以及 (2) 因违约行为而产生的损失的后果性损害赔偿\n\n守约方在合同成立时可以合理预见的特殊情况。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 351（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释间接损害必须是可预见的）。","conclusion":"答案 C：机械师没有理由在 4 月 1 日预见到玩具公司将遭受 25,000 美元的间接损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":256,"sourceQuestionPage":755,"sourceQuestionPages":[755],"sourceExplanationPages":[756,757],"sourceAnswerPages":[756,757],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-257","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"Under a written agreement, a pastry supplier promised to sell its entire output of baked buns at a specified unit price to a retailer, for one year. The retailer promised not to sell any other supplier's baked buns. Shortly after making the contract, and before the supplier had tendered any buns, the retailer decided that the contract had become undesirable because of a sudden, sharp decline in its customers' demand for baked buns. It renounced the agreement, and the supplier sues for breach of contract. Which of the following will the court probably decide?","choices":{"A":"The retailer wins, because mutuality of obligation was lacking in that the retailer made no express promise to buy any of the supplier's baked buns.","B":"The retailer wins, because the agreement was void for indefiniteness of quantity and total price for the year involved.","C":"The supplier wins, because the retailer's promise to sell the supplier's baked buns exclusively, if it sold any such buns at all, implied a promise to use its best efforts to sell the supplier's one-year output of baked buns.","D":"The supplier wins, because under the applicable law both parties to a sale-of-goods contract impliedly assume the risk of price and demand fluctuations."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Output v. Requirements contracts\n\n(UCC § 2-306)\n\nOutput Contract for sale of as many goods as seller produces during specified period\n\nRequirements Contract for sale of as many goods as buyer requires during specified period\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, baked buns). Under the UCC, a contract for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned (eg, output or requirements contract) carries with it an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale. Here, the pastry supplier promised to sell its entire output of baked buns to the retailer for one year. The retailer made no express promise to buy any of the supplier's baked buns. But since the retailer promised to sell the supplier's baked buns exclusively, if it sold any such buns at all, the retailer impliedly promised that it would use its best efforts to sell the supplier's one-year output of baked buns (Choice A). Therefore, the supplier probably wins. (Choice B) Contracts for the sale of goods must contain definite terms that (1) reasonably identify the goods to be sold and (2) specify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts. The quantity term is sufficiently definite in output contracts (as seen here) since the seller's actual output can be determined objectively. (Choice D) Although both parties to a sale-of-goods contract impliedly assume the risk of price and demand fluctuations, this is not a basis for the supplier's success. Instead, the supplier will win because the retailer is required to use its best efforts to sell the supplier's baked buns.\n\nEducational objective: Contracts for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned carry with them an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale.\n\nReferences\nUCC § 2-306 (output, requirements, and exclusive dealings).","educationalObjective":"Contracts for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned carry with them an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale.","references":"UCC § 2-306 (output, requirements, and exclusive dealings).","rule":"Contracts for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned carry with them an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following will the court probably decide?","rule":"Contracts for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned carry with them an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale.","analysis":"Output v. Requirements contracts\n\n(UCC § 2-306)\n\nOutput Contract for sale of as many goods as seller produces during specified period\n\nRequirements Contract for sale of as many goods as buyer requires during specified period\n\nUCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nArticle 2 of the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, baked buns). Under the UCC, a contract for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned (eg, output or requirements contract) carries with it an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale. Here, the pastry supplier promised to sell its entire output of baked buns to the retailer for one year. The retailer made no express promise to buy any of the supplier's baked buns. But since the retailer promised to sell the supplier's baked buns exclusively, if it sold any such buns at all, the retailer impliedly promised that it would use its best efforts to sell the supplier's one-year output of baked buns (Choice A). Therefore, the supplier probably wins. (Choice B) Contracts for the sale of goods must contain definite terms that (1) reasonably identify the goods to be sold and (2) specify a quantity of goods to be sold that is certain or capable of being made certain by reference to objective facts. The quantity term is sufficiently definite in output contracts (as seen here) since the seller's actual output can be determined objectively. (Choice D) Although both parties to a sale-of-goods contract impliedly assume the risk of price and demand fluctuations, this is not a basis for the supplier's success. Instead, the supplier will win because the retailer is required to use its best efforts to sell the supplier's baked buns.","conclusion":"Answer C: The supplier wins, because the retailer's promise to sell the supplier's baked buns exclusively, if it sold any such buns at all, implied a promise to use its best efforts to sell the supplier's one-year output of baked buns."},"questionZh":"根据一份书面协议，一家糕点供应商承诺将其全部产量的烤面包以指定单价出售给零售商，为期一年。该零售商承诺不会出售任何其他供应商的烤面包。签订合同后不久，在供应商提交任何面包之前，零售商认为该合同已不再受欢迎，因为客户对烘焙面包的需求突然急剧下降。它放弃了协议，供应商因违反合同而提起诉讼。法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","choicesZh":{"A":"零售商获胜，因为缺乏相互义务，零售商没有明确承诺购买供应商的任何烤面包。","B":"零售商获胜，因为该协议因涉及当年的数量和总价不确定而无效。","C":"供应商获胜，因为零售商承诺独家销售供应商的烤面包，如果它出售任何此类面包，则意味着承诺尽最大努力销售供应商一年产量的烤面包。","D":"供应商获胜，因为根据适用的法律，货物销售合同的双方都默示承担价格和需求波动的风险。"},"explanationZh":"产出与需求合同\n\n（UCC § 2-306）\n\n输出合同，用于销售卖方在指定期限内生产的尽可能多的货物\n\n要求 在指定期限内根据买方要求销售尽可能多的货物的合同\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖商品（例如烤面包）销售合同。根据《统一商法典》（UCC），有关货物类型的独家经营合同（例如，产出或需求合同）包含卖方尽最大努力供应货物和买方促进其销售的隐含义务。在此，糕点供应商承诺将其全部产量的烤面包出售给零售商一年。该零售商没有明确承诺购买任何供应商的烤面包。但由于零售商承诺独家销售供应商的烤面包，因此如果零售商真的出售此类面包，则零售商隐含承诺将尽最大努力出售供应商一年产量的烤面包（选项A）。因此，供应商很可能获胜。 （选择B）货物销售合同必须包含明确的条款，以（1）合理地确定待售货物；（2）规定根据客观事实确定或能够确定的待售货物数量。由于卖方的实际产量可以客观地确定，因此产量合同中的数量条款是足够明确的（如此处所示）。 （选项D） 尽管货物销售合同双方默示承担价格和需求波动的风险，但这并不是供应商成功的基础。相反，供应商将会获胜，因为零售商需要尽最大努力来销售供应商的烤面包。\n\n教育目标：有关货物类型的独家经营合同附带了卖方尽最大努力供应货物和买方促进货物销售的隐含义务。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-306（输出、要求和独家交易）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"有关货物类型的独家经营合同包含卖方尽最大努力供应货物和买方促进货物销售的隐含义务。","iracZh":{"issue":"法院可能会做出以下哪项裁决？","rule":"有关货物类型的独家经营合同包含卖方尽最大努力供应货物和买方促进货物销售的隐含义务。","analysis":"产出与需求合同\n\n（UCC § 2-306）\n\n输出合同，用于销售卖方在指定期限内生产的尽可能多的货物\n\n要求 在指定期限内根据买方要求销售尽可能多的货物的合同\n\nUCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n《统一商法典》（UCC）(UCC) 第 2 条管辖商品（例如烤面包）销售合同。根据《统一商法典》（UCC），有关货物类型的独家经营合同（例如，产出或需求合同）包含卖方尽最大努力供应货物和买方促进其销售的隐含义务。在此，糕点供应商承诺将其全部产量的烤面包出售给零售商一年。该零售商没有明确承诺购买任何供应商的烤面包。但由于零售商承诺独家销售供应商的烤面包，因此如果零售商真的出售此类面包，则零售商隐含承诺将尽最大努力出售供应商一年产量的烤面包（选项A）。因此，供应商很可能获胜。 （选择B）货物销售合同必须包含明确的条款，以（1）合理地确定待售货物；（2）规定根据客观事实确定或能够确定的待售货物数量。由于卖方的实际产量可以客观地确定，因此产量合同中的数量条款是足够明确的（如此处所示）。 （选项D） 尽管货物销售合同双方默示承担价格和需求波动的风险，但这并不是供应商成功的基础。相反，供应商将会获胜，因为零售商需要尽最大努力来销售供应商的烤面包。\n\n教育目标：有关货物类型的独家经营合同附带了卖方尽最大努力供应货物和买方促进货物销售的隐含义务。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-306（输出、要求和独家交易）。","conclusion":"答案 C：供应商获胜，因为零售商承诺独家销售供应商的烤面包，如果它出售任何此类面包，则意味着承诺尽最大努力销售供应商一年产量的烤面包。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"exclusive dealing","ipa":"/ɪksklˈusɪv dˈilɪŋ/","syllables":"ex·clu·sive deal·ing","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"独家交易：一方在约定范围内仅与另一方交易","contextualMeaning":"独家交易：一方在约定范围内仅与另一方交易","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"Contracts for exclusive dealing in the type of goods concerned carry with them an implied obligation by the seller to use its best efforts to supply the goods and by the buyer to promote their sale.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":257,"sourceQuestionPage":758,"sourceQuestionPages":[758],"sourceExplanationPages":[759,760],"sourceAnswerPages":[759,760],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-258","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A business contracted with a shareholder to purchase all of the shareholder's stock in a corporation at a specified price per share. At the time this contract was executed, the business's financial officer said to the shareholder, \"Our commitment to buy is conditioned on our obtaining approval of the contract from our parent company.\" The shareholder replied, \"Fine. No problem.\" The shareholder subsequently refused to consummate the sale on the ground that the business had neglected to request the parent company's approval of the contract, which was true. The parent company's chief executive officer, however, is prepared to testify that the parent company would have routinely approved the contract if requested to do so. The business can also prove that it has made a substantial sale of other assets to finance the stock purchase, although it admittedly had not anticipated any such necessity when it entered into the stock-purchase agreement. If the business sues the shareholder for breach of contract, is the business likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because obtaining the parent company's approval of the contract was an event within the business's control and the business's failure to obtain it was itself a material breach of contract.","B":"No, because the express condition of the parent company's approval had not occurred prior to the lawsuit.","C":"Yes, because the business detrimentally relied on the shareholder's commitment by selling off other assets to finance the stock purchase.","D":"Yes, because the condition of the parent company's approval of the contract, being designed to protect only the business and the parent company, can be and has been waived by those entities."},"answer":"D","explanation":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA party may generally avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both parties' performance becomes due—has not occurred. The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused, however, if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. The waiving party may retract the waiver unless the other party has detrimentally relied on it. Here, the business's obligation to buy the shareholder's stock was conditioned on obtaining the parent company's approval (condition precedent). Although the business neglected to obtain that approval (nonoccurrence), the parent company's CEO is prepared to testify that the company would have routinely approved the contract if requested to do so (waiver). Because the condition protected the business and parent company and they waived it, the shareholder was required to perform by selling the stock (Choice B). Therefore, the business will likely prevail. (Choice A) Obtaining the parent company's approval was not an event within the business's control because the parent company could have refused to approve the contract. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. But this doctrine does not apply here because a contract was formed, and performance became due when the condition precedent was waived.\n\nEducational objective: Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).","educationalObjective":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts §§ 224–25 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (conditions and effect of nonoccurrence).","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.","irac":{"issue":"If the business sues the shareholder for breach of contract, is the business likely to prevail?","rule":"Nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct.","analysis":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nA party may generally avoid performance if a condition precedent—ie, an uncertain future event that must occur before either or both parties' performance becomes due—has not occurred. The nonoccurrence of a condition may be excused, however, if the party who benefits from the condition waives it by words or conduct. The waiving party may retract the waiver unless the other party has detrimentally relied on it. Here, the business's obligation to buy the shareholder's stock was conditioned on obtaining the parent company's approval (condition precedent). Although the business neglected to obtain that approval (nonoccurrence), the parent company's CEO is prepared to testify that the company would have routinely approved the contract if requested to do so (waiver). Because the condition protected the business and parent company and they waived it, the shareholder was required to perform by selling the stock (Choice B). Therefore, the business will likely prevail. (Choice A) Obtaining the parent company's approval was not an event within the business's control because the parent company could have refused to approve the contract. (Choice C) Under the doctrine of promissory estoppel, a party can enforce a promise on which it reasonably and detrimentally relied when no valid contract was formed. But this doctrine does not apply here because a contract was formed, and performance became due when the condition precedent was waived.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because the condition of the parent company's approval of the contract, being designed to protect only the business and the parent company, can be and has been waived by those entities."},"questionZh":"与股东签订合同，以每股指定价格购买公司所有股东股票的企业。在执行该合同时，该公司的财务官对股东表示：“我们承诺购买的条件是我们获得母公司批准该合同。”股东回答：“好，没问题。”该股东随后拒绝完成出售，理由是该企业忽视了请求母公司批准该合同，而这确实是事实。然而，母公司首席执行官准备作证说，如果有要求，母公司通常会批准该合同。该企业还可以证明它已经大量出售其他资产来为股票购买提供资金，尽管它承认在签订股票购买协议时并没有预见到任何这种必要性。如果企业起诉股东违约，企业胜诉的可能性大吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为获得母公司对合同的批准是企业可以控制的事件，而企业未能获得母公司的批准本身就是重大违约。","B":"不可以，因为母公司批准的明确条件在诉讼之前并未发生。","C":"是的，因为该公司通过出售其他资产来为股票购买融资，从而有害地依赖股东的承诺。","D":"是的，因为母公司批准合同的条件旨在仅保护业务和母公司，这些实体可以而且已经放弃了该条件。"},"explanationZh":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n如果先决条件（即，在一方或双方履约到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，一方通常可以避免履约。然而，如果从该条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。弃权方可以撤回弃权，除非另一方不利地依赖弃权。在这里，企业购买股东股票的义务是以获得母公司的批准为条件的（先决条件）。尽管该企业未能获得该批准（未发生），但母公司首席执行官准备作证，如果要求的话，该公司通常会批准该合同（弃权）。由于该条件保护了企业和母公司，而他们放弃了该条件，因此股东必须通过出售股票来履行（选择B）。因此，生意很可能会占上风。 （选择 A）获得母公司的批准不是企业可以控制的事件，因为母公司可能拒绝批准合同。 （选项C） 根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。但这一原则在这里并不适用，因为合同已经成立，并且当先决条件被放弃时，履约就到期了。\n\n教育目标：如果从某种条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果从条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果企业起诉股东违约，企业胜诉的可能性大吗？","rule":"如果从条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。","analysis":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n如果先决条件（即，在一方或双方履约到期之前必须发生的不确定的未来事件）尚未发生，一方通常可以避免履约。然而，如果从该条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。弃权方可以撤回弃权，除非另一方不利地依赖弃权。在这里，企业购买股东股票的义务是以获得母公司的批准为条件的（先决条件）。尽管该企业未能获得该批准（未发生），但母公司首席执行官准备作证，如果要求的话，该公司通常会批准该合同（弃权）。由于该条件保护了企业和母公司，而他们放弃了该条件，因此股东必须通过出售股票来履行（选择B）。因此，生意很可能会占上风。 （选择 A）获得母公司的批准不是企业可以控制的事件，因为母公司可能拒绝批准合同。 （选项C） 根据禁止反言原则，一方当事人可以在未形成有效合同的情况下强制执行其合理且不利地依赖的承诺。但这一原则在这里并不适用，因为合同已经成立，并且当先决条件被放弃时，履约就到期了。\n\n教育目标：如果从某种条件中受益的一方通过言语或行为放弃该条件，则该条件的不发生可以被原谅。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§§ 224–25（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不发生的条件和影响）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为母公司批准合同的条件旨在仅保护业务和母公司，这些实体可以而且已经放弃了该条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":258,"sourceQuestionPage":761,"sourceQuestionPages":[761],"sourceExplanationPages":[762,763],"sourceAnswerPages":[762,763],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-259","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A seller and a buyer, standing on a 10-acre tract of land, orally agreed to its sale and purchase for $5,000, and orally marked its bounds as \"that line of trees down there, the ditch that intersects them, the fence on the other side, and that street on the fourth side.\" In which of the following is the remedy of reformation most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"As later reduced to writing, the agreement by clerical mistake included two acres that are actually beyond the fence.","B":"The buyer reasonably thought that a dilapidated shed backed up against the fence was to be torn down and removed as part of the agreement, but the seller did not. As later reduced to writing, the agreement said nothing about the shed.","C":"The buyer reasonably thought that the price orally agreed upon was $4,500, but the seller did not. As later reduced to writing, the agreement said $5,000.","D":"The buyer reasonably thought that two acres beyond the fence were included in the oral agreement but the seller did not. As later reduced to writing, the agreement included the two acres."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Availability of reformation as a remedy\n\nMisrepresentation One party makes untrue assertion of fact that is fraudulent or material Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation Mutual mistake Both parties are mistaken as to contract's contents or legal effect Unilateral mistake One party is mistaken because other party intentionally misrepresented contract's content or legal effect (from fraudulent Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation) misrepresentation Reformation is an equitable remedy that courts can use to rewrite a contract to reflect the parties' original intent. This remedy is available when a writing that embodies an agreement fails to express the agreement because of a mistake by both parties as to its content or legal effect. When reformation is available to cure a mutual mistake, neither party can avoid the contract. Here, the parties agreed to the sale and purchase of a 10-acre tract of land and orally marked its bounds, including \"the fence on the other side.\" Due to a clerical mistake, the written agreement included two acres beyond the fence (ie, two acres neither party intended to include as part of the transaction). Therefore, at the request of either party, the court may reform the writing to cure their mutual mistake. (Choices B, C & D) The buyer and seller did not agree to the same terms, so it would be impossible to reform the writing to reflect their actual agreement. Instead, no contract exists because there was no meeting of the minds.\n\nEducational objective: A court may reform a contract that fails to accurately express the parties' agreement because of a mistake by both parties as to its content or legal effect.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 155 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (mistake of both parties justifying reformation).","educationalObjective":"A court may reform a contract that fails to accurately express the parties' agreement because of a mistake by both parties as to its content or legal effect.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 155 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (mistake of both parties justifying reformation).","rule":"A court may reform a contract that fails to accurately express the parties' agreement because of a mistake by both parties as to its content or legal effect.","irac":{"issue":"A seller and a buyer, standing on a 10-acre tract of land, orally agreed to its sale and purchase for $5,000, and orally marked its bounds as \"that line of trees down there, the ditch that intersects them, the fence on the other side, and that street on the fourth side.\" In which of the following is the remedy of reformation most appropriate?","rule":"A court may reform a contract that fails to accurately express the parties' agreement because of a mistake by both parties as to its content or legal effect.","analysis":"Availability of reformation as a remedy\n\nMisrepresentation One party makes untrue assertion of fact that is fraudulent or material Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation Mutual mistake Both parties are mistaken as to contract's contents or legal effect Unilateral mistake One party is mistaken because other party intentionally misrepresented contract's content or legal effect (from fraudulent Adversely affected party justifiably relies on that misrepresentation) misrepresentation Reformation is an equitable remedy that courts can use to rewrite a contract to reflect the parties' original intent. This remedy is available when a writing that embodies an agreement fails to express the agreement because of a mistake by both parties as to its content or legal effect. When reformation is available to cure a mutual mistake, neither party can avoid the contract. Here, the parties agreed to the sale and purchase of a 10-acre tract of land and orally marked its bounds, including \"the fence on the other side.\" Due to a clerical mistake, the written agreement included two acres beyond the fence (ie, two acres neither party intended to include as part of the transaction). Therefore, at the request of either party, the court may reform the writing to cure their mutual mistake. (Choices B, C & D) The buyer and seller did not agree to the same terms, so it would be impossible to reform the writing to reflect their actual agreement. Instead, no contract exists because there was no meeting of the minds.","conclusion":"Answer A: As later reduced to writing, the agreement by clerical mistake included two acres that are actually beyond the fence."},"questionZh":"卖方和买方站在一块 10 英亩的土地上，口头同意以 5,000 美元的价格买卖这块土地，并口头将其边界标记为“下面的那排树、与它们相交的沟渠、另一边的栅栏和第四边的那条街道”。以下哪项改革补救措施最合适？","choicesZh":{"A":"后来写成文字时，由于文书错误，协议中包括了实际上超出围栏的两英亩土地。","B":"买方合理地认为，作为协议的一部分，靠着栅栏的一座破旧棚屋将被拆除，但卖方却没有这么做。后来写成书面协议时，该协议只字未提有关棚屋的事。","C":"买方合理地认为口头商定的价格是 4,500 美元，但卖方却不这么认为。协议后来简化为书面形式，金额为 5,000 美元。","D":"买方有理由认为口头协议中包含了围栏以外的两英亩土地，但卖方却没有。后来写成书面协议时，协议中包括了两英亩土地。"},"explanationZh":"改革作为补救措施的可用性\n\n失实陈述 一方对欺诈性或重大事实作出不真实的断言 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该失实陈述 相互错误 双方对合同内容或法律效力均存在错误 单方面错误 一方因另一方故意歪曲合同内容或法律效力而产生错误（来自欺诈性 受到不利影响的一方有正当理由依赖该失实陈述） 失实陈述 改革是法院可以用来重写合同以反映双方的初衷。当包含协议的书面文件由于双方对其内容或法律效力的错误而未能表达该协议时，可以使用这种补救措施。当可以通过改革来纠正共同的错误时，任何一方都不能撤销合同。在这里，双方同意买卖一块10英亩的土地，并口头划定了边界，包括“另一边的栅栏”。由于文书错误，书面协议中包括了围栏外的两英亩（即双方都不打算将两英亩纳入交易的一部分）。因此，应任何一方的请求，法院可以修改书面文字以纠正双方的错误。 （选项 B、C 和 D） 买方和卖方不同意相同的条款，因此不可能修改书面内容以反映他们的实际协议。相反，由于没有达成共识，所以不存在任何合同。\n\n教育目的：法院可以对因双方对其内容或法律效力的错误而未能准确表达双方同意的合同进行修改。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 155（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（双方都错误地证明了改革的合理性）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"法院可以对因双方当事人对其内容或法律效力的错误而未能准确表达双方同意的合同进行修改。","iracZh":{"issue":"卖方和买方站在一块 10 英亩的土地上，口头同意以 5,000 美元的价格买卖这块土地，并口头将其边界标记为“下面的那排树、与它们相交的沟渠、另一边的栅栏和第四边的那条街道”。以下哪项改革补救措施最合适？","rule":"法院可以对因双方当事人对其内容或法律效力的错误而未能准确表达双方同意的合同进行修改。","analysis":"改革作为补救措施的可用性\n\n失实陈述 一方对欺诈性或重大事实作出不真实的断言 受到不利影响的一方有理由依赖该失实陈述 相互错误 双方对合同内容或法律效力均存在错误 单方面错误 一方因另一方故意歪曲合同内容或法律效力而产生错误（来自欺诈性 受到不利影响的一方有正当理由依赖该失实陈述） 失实陈述 改革是法院可以用来重写合同以反映双方的初衷。当包含协议的书面文件由于双方对其内容或法律效力的错误而未能表达该协议时，可以使用这种补救措施。当可以通过改革来纠正共同的错误时，任何一方都不能撤销合同。在这里，双方同意买卖一块10英亩的土地，并口头划定了边界，包括“另一边的栅栏”。由于文书错误，书面协议中包括了围栏外的两英亩（即双方都不打算将两英亩纳入交易的一部分）。因此，应任何一方的请求，法院可以修改书面文字以纠正双方的错误。 （选项 B、C 和 D） 买方和卖方不同意相同的条款，因此不可能修改书面内容以反映他们的实际协议。相反，由于没有达成共识，所以不存在任何合同。\n\n教育目的：法院可以对因双方对其内容或法律效力的错误而未能准确表达双方同意的合同进行修改。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 155（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（双方都错误地证明了改革的合理性）。","conclusion":"答案 A：后来写成文字时，由于文书错误，协议中包括了实际上超出围栏的两英亩土地。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":259,"sourceQuestionPage":764,"sourceQuestionPages":[764],"sourceExplanationPages":[765,766],"sourceAnswerPages":[765,766],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-260","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Consideration & Promissory Estoppel","question":"A manufacturer contracted in a signed writing to provide a company with industrial ceiling tiles for its new production facility. The contract specified that the manufacturer would deliver the tiles by third-party carrier, \"F.O.B. the company's production facility.\" While in transit to the production facility, one-third of the tiles fell off the carrier's truck and were destroyed. The manufacturer subsequently emailed the company that it did not intend to replace the tiles. The company sent a letter to the manufacturer that read in relevant part: \"You are not responsible for replacing the damaged tiles. We will sue the carrier.\" The company signed the letter. Shortly thereafter, the carrier filed for bankruptcy and the company decided to sue the manufacturer for breach of contract. Will the company be likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the company's signed letter operated as a release and discharged the manufacturer from its remaining contractual duties.","B":"No, because the tiles were specifically identified goods that were destroyed through no fault of either party.","C":"Yes, because the manufacturer bore the risk of loss when the tiles were destroyed and breached the contract by refusing to replace the tiles.","D":"Yes, because there was no new consideration to support the release of the manufacturer's remaining contractual duties."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Under the UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods, the risk of loss generally passes to the buyer when the buyer receives the goods. But if the contract requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier, then the risk of loss passes to the buyer at different times depending on whether the contract is a: shipment contract – does not require delivery at a particular location, so the risk of loss passes to the buyer when the goods are properly delivered to the carrier or destination contract – requires delivery at a particular location, so the risk of loss passes to the buyer when goods are delivered at the named location. Destination contracts are often identified by the letters \"F.O.B.\" (free on board) followed by a location (as seen here). And since the ceiling tiles were in transit to the company's production facility when they were destroyed, the risk of loss was with the manufacturer at that time. Therefore, the manufacturer was in breach when it informed the company that it did not intend to replace the tiles (Choice C). However, the company then sent the manufacturer a letter stating that the manufacturer was not responsible for replacing the damaged tiles. This operated as a release—ie, a writing that manifests an intent to discharge another party from an existing duty. The UCC requires that a release be signed by the releasing party (as the company did here), but no consideration is needed (Choice D).* Therefore, the company is unlikely to prevail in its suit against the manufacturer. *For common-law contracts, the release must be supported by consideration or a consideration substitute (eg, detrimental reliance) to be effective. (Choice B) A seller's performance is excused if specifically identified items of inventory are destroyed through no fault of either party before the risk of loss passes to the buyer. Since the tiles here were general inventory items, the manufacturer's performance was not excused on this basis.\n\nEducational objective: A release is a writing that manifests an intent to discharge another party from an existing duty. The UCC requires the release to be signed by the releasing party, but new consideration is not required for the release to be effective.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 1-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (waiver or renunciation of claim or right after breach).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 284 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (release).","educationalObjective":"A release is a writing that manifests an intent to discharge another party from an existing duty. The UCC requires the release to be signed by the releasing party, but new consideration is not required for the release to be effective.","references":"U.C.C. § 1-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (waiver or renunciation of claim or right after breach).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 284 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (release).","rule":"A release is a writing that manifests an intent to discharge another party from an existing duty. The UCC requires the release to be signed by the releasing party, but new consideration is not required for the release to be effective.","irac":{"issue":"Will the company be likely to prevail?","rule":"A release is a writing that manifests an intent to discharge another party from an existing duty. The UCC requires the release to be signed by the releasing party, but new consideration is not required for the release to be effective.","analysis":"Under the UCC, which governs contracts for the sale of goods, the risk of loss generally passes to the buyer when the buyer receives the goods. But if the contract requires the seller to deliver the goods by third-party carrier, then the risk of loss passes to the buyer at different times depending on whether the contract is a: shipment contract – does not require delivery at a particular location, so the risk of loss passes to the buyer when the goods are properly delivered to the carrier or destination contract – requires delivery at a particular location, so the risk of loss passes to the buyer when goods are delivered at the named location. Destination contracts are often identified by the letters \"F.O.B.\" (free on board) followed by a location (as seen here). And since the ceiling tiles were in transit to the company's production facility when they were destroyed, the risk of loss was with the manufacturer at that time. Therefore, the manufacturer was in breach when it informed the company that it did not intend to replace the tiles (Choice C). However, the company then sent the manufacturer a letter stating that the manufacturer was not responsible for replacing the damaged tiles. This operated as a release—ie, a writing that manifests an intent to discharge another party from an existing duty. The UCC requires that a release be signed by the releasing party (as the company did here), but no consideration is needed (Choice D).* Therefore, the company is unlikely to prevail in its suit against the manufacturer. *For common-law contracts, the release must be supported by consideration or a consideration substitute (eg, detrimental reliance) to be effective. (Choice B) A seller's performance is excused if specifically identified items of inventory are destroyed through no fault of either party before the risk of loss passes to the buyer. Since the tiles here were general inventory items, the manufacturer's performance was not excused on this basis.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the company's signed letter operated as a release and discharged the manufacturer from its remaining contractual duties."},"questionZh":"一家制造商以书面形式签订合同，为一家公司的新生产设施提供工业天花板。合同规定制造商将通过第三方承运人“离岸价该公司的生产设施”交付瓷砖。在运往生产设施的途中，三分之一的瓷砖从承运商的卡车上掉落并被毁坏。制造商随后向该公司发送电子邮件表示不打算更换瓷砖。该公司向制造商发出了一封信，相关部分写道：“你们不负责更换损坏的瓷砖。我们将起诉承运人。”该公司签署了该信函。此后不久，承运人申请破产，公司决定起诉制造商违约。该公司有可能获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为公司签署的信函起到了释放作用，并免除了制造商剩余的合同义务。","B":"不会，因为这些瓷砖是经过专门鉴定的货物，其销毁并非由于任何一方的过错。","C":"是的，因为厂家承担了瓷砖被毁坏造成损失的风险，并且违反合同拒绝更换瓷砖。","D":"是的，因为没有新约因来支持释放制造商剩余的合同义务。"},"explanationZh":"根据管辖货物销售合同的 UCC，损失风险通常在买方收到货物时转移给买方。但如果合同要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物，则损失风险会在不同时间转移给买方，具体取决于合同是否为： 装运合同 - 不要求在特定地点交货，因此当货物正确交付给承运人时，损失风险转移给买方；或目的地合同 - 要求在特定地点交货，因此当货物在指定地点交付时，损失风险转移给买方。目的地合同通常以字母“F.O.B.”标识。 （船上免费），然后是位置（如此处所示）。由于天花板在运往公司生产设施的途中被毁坏，因此损失风险由当时的制造商承担。因此，当制造商告知公司不打算更换瓷砖时，制造商就违约了（选项C）。不过，该公司随后向制造商发出了一封信函，表示制造商不负责更换损坏的瓷砖。这作为一种释放，即表明意图解除另一方现有义务的书面形式。 UCC 要求发布方必须签署发布（正如该公司在此所做的那样），但不需要约因（选择 D）。*因此，该公司不太可能在针对制造商的诉讼中胜诉。 *对于普通法合同，解除必须有对价或对价替代物（例如，有害信赖）的支持才能生效。 （选择B）如果在损失风险转移给买方之前，非任何一方的过失而导致明确指定的库存物品被销毁，则卖方的履约行为可以被免除。由于这里的瓷砖属于一般库存商品，因此不能以此为理由原谅制造商的业绩。\n\n教育目标：释放是一种书面形式，表明意图解除另一方现有的职责。 UCC 要求发布必须由发布方签署，但发布无需新约因即可生效。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 1-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（放弃或放弃索赔或在违约后立即放弃）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 284（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（发布）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"免责声明是一种书面形式，表明意图解除另一方现有义务。 UCC 要求发布必须由发布方签署，但发布无需新约因即可生效。","iracZh":{"issue":"该公司有可能获胜吗？","rule":"免责声明是一种书面形式，表明意图解除另一方现有义务。 UCC 要求发布必须由发布方签署，但发布无需新约因即可生效。","analysis":"根据管辖货物销售合同的 UCC，损失风险通常在买方收到货物时转移给买方。但如果合同要求卖方通过第三方承运人交付货物，则损失风险会在不同时间转移给买方，具体取决于合同是否为： 装运合同 - 不要求在特定地点交货，因此当货物正确交付给承运人时，损失风险转移给买方；或目的地合同 - 要求在特定地点交货，因此当货物在指定地点交付时，损失风险转移给买方。目的地合同通常以字母“F.O.B.”标识。 （船上免费），然后是位置（如此处所示）。由于天花板在运往公司生产设施的途中被毁坏，因此损失风险由当时的制造商承担。因此，当制造商告知公司不打算更换瓷砖时，制造商就违约了（选项C）。不过，该公司随后向制造商发出了一封信函，表示制造商不负责更换损坏的瓷砖。这作为一种释放，即表明意图解除另一方现有义务的书面形式。 UCC 要求发布方必须签署发布（正如该公司在此所做的那样），但不需要约因（选择 D）。*因此，该公司不太可能在针对制造商的诉讼中胜诉。 *对于普通法合同，解除必须有对价或对价替代物（例如，有害信赖）的支持才能生效。 （选择B）如果在损失风险转移给买方之前，非任何一方的过失而导致明确指定的库存物品被销毁，则卖方的履约行为可以被免除。由于这里的瓷砖属于一般库存商品，因此不能以此为理由原谅制造商的业绩。\n\n教育目标：释放是一种书面形式，表明意图解除另一方现有的职责。 UCC 要求发布必须由发布方签署，但发布无需新约因即可生效。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 1-306 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（放弃或放弃索赔或在违约后立即放弃）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 284（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（发布）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为公司签署的信函起到了释放作用，并免除了制造商剩余的合同义务。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-60f7d4bca30ee9f1"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":260,"sourceQuestionPage":767,"sourceQuestionPages":[767],"sourceExplanationPages":[768,769],"sourceAnswerPages":[768,769],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-261","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Performance & Conditions","question":"A buyer agreed to purchase a seller's house for $250,000 \"on condition that the buyer obtain mortgage financing within 30 days.\" Thirty days later, the buyer told the seller that he would not purchase the house because he had not obtained mortgage financing. The seller asked the buyer where he had tried to obtain mortgage financing, and the buyer responded, \"I was busy and didn't have time to seek mortgage financing.\" If the seller sues the buyer for breach of contract, is the court likely to find the buyer in breach?","choices":{"A":"No, because the buyer's performance was subject to a condition that did not occur.","B":"No, because the promise was illusory since the buyer was not obligated to do anything.","C":"Yes, because a promise was implied that the buyer had to make reasonable efforts to obtain mortgage financing.","D":"Yes, because a reasonable interpretation of the agreement is that the buyer had an obligation to purchase the house for $250,000 in 30 days."},"answer":"C","explanation":"A binding contract is formed when one party makes an offer and the other party accepts. Performance is generally due once the contract is formed but performance can be contingent upon the occurrence of a condition. There are two types of conditions: condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs If a party whose duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing—then the condition will be excused. In other words, the condition no longer needs to occur for that party's performance to become due. Here, the agreement to purchase the house was conditioned on the buyer's ability to obtain financing within 30 days. The implied duty of good faith and fair dealing required the buyer to make reasonable efforts to secure financing. But since the buyer breached that duty by making no effort to obtain financing, the condition precedent was excused and his performance became due. Therefore, the court is likely to find the buyer is in breach (Choice A). *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). (Choice B) A promise is illusory, and therefore unenforceable, if the promisor can choose whether to honor it—eg, had the buyer promised to purchase the house \"if I want to.\" Here, however, the buyer's promise was not illusory because it implicitly obligated him to make efforts to obtain financing within 30 days. (Choice D) The duty of good faith and fair dealing did not require the buyer to purchase the house but rather to make a reasonable effort to obtain financing within 30 days.\n\nEducational objective: If a party whose duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent wrongfully prevents that condition from occurring—eg, by violating the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing—the condition will be excused and that party must perform.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of a nonoccurrence of a condition).","educationalObjective":"If a party whose duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent wrongfully prevents that condition from occurring—eg, by violating the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing—the condition will be excused and that party must perform.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of a nonoccurrence of a condition).","rule":"If a party whose duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent wrongfully prevents that condition from occurring—eg, by violating the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing—the condition will be excused and that party must perform.","irac":{"issue":"A buyer agreed to purchase a seller's house for $250,000 \"on condition that the buyer obtain mortgage financing within 30 days.\" Thirty days later, the buyer told the seller that he would not purchase the house because he had not obtained mortgage financing. The seller asked the buyer where he had tried to obtain mortgage financing, and the buyer responded, \"I was busy and didn't have time to seek mortgage financing.\" If the seller sues the buyer for breach of contract, is the court likely to find the buyer in breach?","rule":"If a party whose duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent wrongfully prevents that condition from occurring—eg, by violating the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing—the condition will be excused and that party must perform.","analysis":"A binding contract is formed when one party makes an offer and the other party accepts. Performance is generally due once the contract is formed but performance can be contingent upon the occurrence of a condition. There are two types of conditions: condition precedent* – delays performance until a specified event occurs condition subsequent – excuses performance once a specified event occurs If a party whose duty to perform is subject to a condition precedent wrongfully prevents the condition from occurring—eg, by breaching the implied duty of good faith and fair dealing—then the condition will be excused. In other words, the condition no longer needs to occur for that party's performance to become due. Here, the agreement to purchase the house was conditioned on the buyer's ability to obtain financing within 30 days. The implied duty of good faith and fair dealing required the buyer to make reasonable efforts to secure financing. But since the buyer breached that duty by making no effort to obtain financing, the condition precedent was excused and his performance became due. Therefore, the court is likely to find the buyer is in breach (Choice A). *A condition concurrent arises when each party's obligation to perform is a condition precedent to the other party's obligation to perform (ie, when performance is due simultaneously). (Choice B) A promise is illusory, and therefore unenforceable, if the promisor can choose whether to honor it—eg, had the buyer promised to purchase the house \"if I want to.\" Here, however, the buyer's promise was not illusory because it implicitly obligated him to make efforts to obtain financing within 30 days. (Choice D) The duty of good faith and fair dealing did not require the buyer to purchase the house but rather to make a reasonable effort to obtain financing within 30 days.","conclusion":"Answer C: Yes, because a promise was implied that the buyer had to make reasonable efforts to obtain mortgage financing."},"questionZh":"买方同意以 25 万美元购买卖方的房屋，“条件是买方在 30 天内获得抵押贷款融资”。三十天后，买方告诉卖方，他不会购买该房子，因为他还没有获得抵押贷款融资。卖方询问买方曾在哪里寻求抵押融资，买方回答说：“我很忙，没有时间寻求抵押融资。”如果卖方起诉买方违约，法院是否有可能判定买方违约？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为买方的履约受到未发生的条件的影响。","B":"不，因为该承诺是虚幻的，因为买方没有义务做任何事情。","C":"是的，因为暗示了买方必须做出合理努力才能获得抵押融资的承诺。","D":"是的，因为对该协议的合理解释是，买方有义务在30天内以25万美元的价格购买房子。"},"explanationZh":"当一方提出要约且另一方接受时，具有约束力的合同即告成立。合同一旦成立，通常就应履行义务，但履行义务也可以视条件的发生而定。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生 后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生，则免除履行 如果履行义务受先决条件约束的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，通过违反善意和公平交易的默示义务），则该条件将被免除。换句话说，该方的履约不再需要满足该条件即可到期。在这里，购买房屋的协议的条件是买方有能力在 30 天内获得融资。诚实信用和公平交易的隐含义务要求买方做出合理的努力以获得融资。但由于买方没有努力获得融资而违反了该义务，因此先决条件被免除，其履约到期。因此，法院很可能认定买方违约（选项A）。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。 （选择B）如果承诺者可以选择是否履行承诺，那么承诺就是虚幻的，因此无法执行——例如，如果买方承诺“如果我愿意”就会购买房子。然而，在这里，买方的承诺并不是虚幻的，因为它隐含着他有义务在30天内努力获得融资。 （选项D） 诚实信用和公平交易的义务并不要求买方购买房屋，而是要做出合理的努力以在30天内获得融资。\n\n教育目标：如果履行义务受先决条件约束的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，通过违反善意和公平交易的隐含义务），则该条件将被免除，并且该方必须履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件未发生的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果履行义务受先决条件约束的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，通过违反善意和公平交易的默示义务），则该条件将被免除，并且该方必须履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"买方同意以 25 万美元购买卖方的房屋，“条件是买方在 30 天内获得抵押贷款融资”。三十天后，买方告诉卖方，他不会购买该房子，因为他还没有获得抵押贷款融资。卖方询问买方曾在哪里寻求抵押融资，买方回答说：“我很忙，没有时间寻求抵押融资。”如果卖方起诉买方违约，法院是否有可能判定买方违约？","rule":"如果履行义务受先决条件约束的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，通过违反善意和公平交易的默示义务），则该条件将被免除，并且该方必须履行。","analysis":"当一方提出要约且另一方接受时，具有约束力的合同即告成立。合同一旦成立，通常就应履行义务，但履行义务也可以视条件的发生而定。有两种类型的条件： 先决条件* – 延迟履行直至特定事件发生 后续条件 – 一旦特定事件发生，则免除履行 如果履行义务受先决条件约束的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，通过违反善意和公平交易的默示义务），则该条件将被免除。换句话说，该方的履约不再需要满足该条件即可到期。在这里，购买房屋的协议的条件是买方有能力在 30 天内获得融资。诚实信用和公平交易的隐含义务要求买方做出合理的努力以获得融资。但由于买方没有努力获得融资而违反了该义务，因此先决条件被免除，其履约到期。因此，法院很可能认定买方违约（选项A）。 *当每一方的履行义务是另一方履行义务的先决条件时（即，当同时履行时），就会出现并发条件。 （选择B）如果承诺者可以选择是否履行承诺，那么承诺就是虚幻的，因此无法执行——例如，如果买方承诺“如果我愿意”就会购买房子。然而，在这里，买方的承诺并不是虚幻的，因为它隐含着他有义务在30天内努力获得融资。 （选项D） 诚实信用和公平交易的义务并不要求买方购买房屋，而是要做出合理的努力以在30天内获得融资。\n\n教育目标：如果履行义务受先决条件约束的一方错误地阻止了该条件的发生（例如，通过违反善意和公平交易的隐含义务），则该条件将被免除，并且该方必须履行。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件未发生的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 C：是的，因为暗示了买方必须做出合理努力才能获得抵押融资的承诺。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-0a7a3927ad7f8605"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":261,"sourceQuestionPage":770,"sourceQuestionPages":[770],"sourceExplanationPages":[771,772],"sourceAnswerPages":[771,772],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-262","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A businesswoman sold her business to a company for $25 million in cash pursuant to a written contract signed by both parties. Under the contract, the company agreed to employ the businesswoman for two years as a vice president at a salary of $150,000 per year. After six months, the company, without cause, fired the businesswoman. Which of the following statements best describes the businesswoman's rights after being fired?","choices":{"A":"She can get specific performance of her right to serve as a vice president of the company for two years.","B":"She can recover the promised salary for the remainder of the two years if no comparable job is reasonably available and she does not take another job.","C":"She can recover the promised salary for the remainder of the two years if she remains ready to work.","D":"She can rescind the contract of sale and get back her business upon tender to the company of $25 million."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution The primary objective of compensatory damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been fully performed. Therefore, when an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee is entitled to recover the unpaid salary under the contract. The employee's total recovery is reduced, however, by any compensation the employee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment (ie, mitigate damages). Here, the company breached the contract by firing the businesswoman without cause after six months. To avoid a reduction in her damages, the businesswoman must make reasonable efforts to mitigate damages (ie, do more than remain ready to work) (Choice C). However, if no comparable job is reasonably available and she does not take another job, then she has the right to recover her promised salary for the remainder of the two years. (Choice A) Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby the court orders a party to perform its contract because monetary damages are inadequate to compensate the loss. But courts usually refuse to order breaching employers (or employees) to perform an employment agreement because money damages are sufficient. (Choice D) Absent a material breach of contract, rescission (ie, cancellation of the contract) is an inappropriate remedy. Here, the company's unjustified termination of the businesswoman likely was not a material breach because it represented only a small part of a large contract and can easily be remedied by an award of damages.\n\nEducational objective: When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the\n\nemployee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of damages).","educationalObjective":"When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the\n\nemployee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (measure of damages).","rule":"When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the\n\nemployee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements best describes the businesswoman's rights after being fired?","rule":"When an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee can recover the unpaid salary under the contract. That recovery is reduced by the compensation the\n\nemployee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment.","analysis":"Purpose of compensatory damages\n\nPrimary Place nonbreaching party in same position as if contract had been performed (expectation Expectation measure includes: measure) Expectation damages Incidental damages Consequential damages Fallback When expectation measure too speculative, place nonbreaching party in same position as if no contract had been formed (reliance Reliance measure includes: measure) Reliance damages Liquidated damages Restitution The primary objective of compensatory damages is to place the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been fully performed. Therefore, when an employer breaches an employment agreement, the employee is entitled to recover the unpaid salary under the contract. The employee's total recovery is reduced, however, by any compensation the employee earned or could have earned by making reasonable efforts to secure comparable employment (ie, mitigate damages). Here, the company breached the contract by firing the businesswoman without cause after six months. To avoid a reduction in her damages, the businesswoman must make reasonable efforts to mitigate damages (ie, do more than remain ready to work) (Choice C). However, if no comparable job is reasonably available and she does not take another job, then she has the right to recover her promised salary for the remainder of the two years. (Choice A) Specific performance is an equitable remedy whereby the court orders a party to perform its contract because monetary damages are inadequate to compensate the loss. But courts usually refuse to order breaching employers (or employees) to perform an employment agreement because money damages are sufficient. (Choice D) Absent a material breach of contract, rescission (ie, cancellation of the contract) is an inappropriate remedy. Here, the company's unjustified termination of the businesswoman likely was not a material breach because it represented only a small part of a large contract and can easily be remedied by an award of damages.","conclusion":"Answer B: She can recover the promised salary for the remainder of the two years if no comparable job is reasonably available and she does not take another job."},"questionZh":"一名女商人根据双方签署的书面合同，以 2500 万美元现金将她的企业出售给一家公司。根据合同，公司同意聘用这名女商人担任副总裁两年，年薪为 15 万美元。六个月后，公司无故解雇了这名女商人。以下哪项陈述最能描述女商人被解雇后的权利？","choicesZh":{"A":"她可以获得担任公司副总裁两年的权利的具体履行。","B":"如果没有合理的类似工作并且她不接受其他工作，她可以在剩余的两年内收回承诺的工资。","C":"如果她仍然做好工作准备，她可以在剩下的两年内收回承诺的工资。","D":"她可以解除销售合同，并以 2500 万美元的价格向公司收回其业务。"},"explanationZh":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将守约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（期望 预期措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 回退 当期望措施过于投机时，将守约方置于与未形成合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约金 恢复原状 补偿性损害赔偿的主要目标是使守约方处于与合同相同的位置已得到充分履行。因此，当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者有权追偿合同项下未支付的工资。然而，雇员的总康复会因雇员通过合理努力获得类似就业（即减轻损害）而获得或可能获得的任何补偿而减少。在这里，该公司违反了合同，在六个月后无故解雇了这名女商人。为了避免损失减少，女商人必须做出合理的努力来减轻损失（即，做的不仅仅是做好工作准备）（选择 C）。然而，如果没有合理的类似工作，并且她没有接受其他工作，那么她有权收回其承诺的剩余两年的工资。 （选项A） 具体履行是一种衡平法救济，法院命令一方履行合同，因为金钱赔偿不足以补偿损失。但法院通常拒绝命令违约雇主（或雇员）履行雇佣协议，因为金钱赔偿就足够了。 （选择D）如果没有严重违反合同，解除合同（即取消合同）是一种不适当的补救措施。在此，该公司无理解雇该女商人的行为可能并不构成重大违约，因为它只代表了一份大型合同的一小部分，并且很容易通过赔偿损失来补救。\n\n教育目的：当雇主违反雇佣协议时，雇员可以根据合同追讨未支付的工资。该恢复量因补偿而减少\n\n员工通过合理努力获得类似的就业机会而获得或本可以获得的收入。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（损害赔偿措施）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者可以根据合同追偿未支付的工资。该恢复量因补偿而减少\n\n员工通过合理努力获得类似的就业机会而获得或本可以获得的收入。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述最能描述女商人被解雇后的权利？","rule":"当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者可以根据合同追偿未支付的工资。该恢复量因补偿而减少\n\n员工通过合理努力获得类似的就业机会而获得或本可以获得的收入。","analysis":"损害赔偿的目的\n\n主要 将守约方置于与已履行合同相同的位置（期望 预期措施包括：措施） 预期损害 附带损害 后果性损害 回退 当期望措施过于投机时，将守约方置于与未形成合同相同的位置（信赖 信赖措施包括：措施） 信赖损害 违约金 恢复原状 补偿性损害赔偿的主要目标是使守约方处于与合同相同的位置已得到充分履行。因此，当用人单位违反劳动合同时，劳动者有权追偿合同项下未支付的工资。然而，雇员的总康复会因雇员通过合理努力获得类似就业（即减轻损害）而获得或可能获得的任何补偿而减少。在这里，该公司违反了合同，在六个月后无故解雇了这名女商人。为了避免损失减少，女商人必须做出合理的努力来减轻损失（即，做的不仅仅是做好工作准备）（选择 C）。然而，如果没有合理的类似工作，并且她没有接受其他工作，那么她有权收回其承诺的剩余两年的工资。 （选项A） 具体履行是一种衡平法救济，法院命令一方履行合同，因为金钱赔偿不足以补偿损失。但法院通常拒绝命令违约雇主（或雇员）履行雇佣协议，因为金钱赔偿就足够了。 （选择D）如果没有严重违反合同，解除合同（即取消合同）是一种不适当的补救措施。在此，该公司无理解雇该女商人的行为可能并不构成重大违约，因为它只代表了一份大型合同的一小部分，并且很容易通过赔偿损失来补救。\n\n教育目的：当雇主违反雇佣协议时，雇员可以根据合同追讨未支付的工资。该恢复量因补偿而减少\n\n员工通过合理努力获得类似的就业机会而获得或本可以获得的收入。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（损害赔偿措施）。","conclusion":"答案 B：如果没有合理的类似工作并且她不接受其他工作，她可以在剩余的两年内收回承诺的工资。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":262,"sourceQuestionPage":773,"sourceQuestionPages":[773],"sourceExplanationPages":[774,775],"sourceAnswerPages":[774,775],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-263","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A construction firm in a signed writing contracted with a doctor for an agreed price to build a medical office according to specifications provided by the doctor, and to start work on December 1. The construction firm agreed to provide all materials for the job in addition to all of the labor required. The doctor, without legal excuse, repudiated the contract one week before the construction firm was to start work. Notwithstanding the doctor's repudiation, however, the construction firm subsequently purchased for $50,000 materials that could only be used in building the doctor's medical office, and promptly notified the doctor that it intended to hold him to the contract. If allowed to perform, the construction firm would have made a profit of $30,000 on the job. If the doctor refuses to retract his repudiation, and the construction firm sues him for damages, what is the maximum that the construction firm is entitled to recover?","choices":{"A":"$30,000, the construction firm's expectancy damages.","B":"$50,000, on a restitutionary theory.","C":"$50,000, the construction firm's reliance damages, plus $30,000, its expectancy damages.","D":"Nothing, because the construction firm failed to mitigate its damages."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nBreach of a construction contract (eg, by repudiation) entitles the nonbreaching party to compensatory damages that will place that party in the same position as performance under the contract. When the breaching party is the owner and the breach occurs before or during construction, the builder can recover its lost profits (ie, expectation/expectancy damages) plus any reasonably incurred costs. Costs incurred after a breach are not reasonable and therefore cannot be recovered. Here, the doctor repudiated the construction contract before construction began. As a result, the construction firm is entitled to recover its lost profits of $30,000 as expectancy damages. But the construction firm cannot recover the $50,000 it spent on materials after the breach since those costs were unreasonable. (Choice B) A party may recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of a benefit conferred on another if that benefit was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. But here, the construction firm did not confer a benefit on the doctor by incurring $50,000 in expenses post-breach. (Choice C) When a party's expectation damages cannot be measured reliably, reliance damages can be awarded to place that party in the same position as if no contract had been entered. Reliance damages are awarded in lieu of expectation damages, so an award of reliance damages plus expectation damages is always inappropriate. (Choice D) The construction firm's failure to mitigate its damages by incurring costs after the breach will not stop it from recovering lost profits on the construction contract.\n\nEducational objective: When the owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder can recover its lost profits (ie, expectation damages) plus any reasonably incurred costs. Post-breach costs are not recoverable.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining expectation damages).","educationalObjective":"When the owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder can recover its lost profits (ie, expectation damages) plus any reasonably incurred costs. Post-breach costs are not recoverable.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 347 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining expectation damages).","rule":"When the owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder can recover its lost profits (ie, expectation damages) plus any reasonably incurred costs. Post-breach costs are not recoverable.","irac":{"issue":"If the doctor refuses to retract his repudiation, and the construction firm sues him for damages, what is the maximum that the construction firm is entitled to recover?","rule":"When the owner breaches a construction contract before or during construction, the builder can recover its lost profits (ie, expectation damages) plus any reasonably incurred costs. Post-breach costs are not recoverable.","analysis":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nBreach of a construction contract (eg, by repudiation) entitles the nonbreaching party to compensatory damages that will place that party in the same position as performance under the contract. When the breaching party is the owner and the breach occurs before or during construction, the builder can recover its lost profits (ie, expectation/expectancy damages) plus any reasonably incurred costs. Costs incurred after a breach are not reasonable and therefore cannot be recovered. Here, the doctor repudiated the construction contract before construction began. As a result, the construction firm is entitled to recover its lost profits of $30,000 as expectancy damages. But the construction firm cannot recover the $50,000 it spent on materials after the breach since those costs were unreasonable. (Choice B) A party may recover restitutionary damages for the reasonable value of a benefit conferred on another if that benefit was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. But here, the construction firm did not confer a benefit on the doctor by incurring $50,000 in expenses post-breach. (Choice C) When a party's expectation damages cannot be measured reliably, reliance damages can be awarded to place that party in the same position as if no contract had been entered. Reliance damages are awarded in lieu of expectation damages, so an award of reliance damages plus expectation damages is always inappropriate. (Choice D) The construction firm's failure to mitigate its damages by incurring costs after the breach will not stop it from recovering lost profits on the construction contract.","conclusion":"Answer A: $30,000, the construction firm's expectancy damages."},"questionZh":"一家建筑公司与一位医生签订了一份书面合同，以商定的价格按照医生提供的规格建造一间医疗办公室，并于 12 月 1 日开始工作。建筑公司同意提供除所需的所有劳动力外的所有材料。医生在建筑公司开工前一周无故解除了合同。然而，尽管医生拒绝了，建筑公司随后还是花费了 5 万美元购买了只能用于建造医生医疗办公室的材料，并立即通知医生打算让他遵守合同。如果允许执行，建筑公司将在这项工作中赚取 30,000 美元的利润。如果医生拒绝收回毁约，而建筑公司起诉他要求赔偿，建筑公司最多有权追偿多少？","choicesZh":{"A":"30,000 美元，建筑公司的预期损失。","B":"根据赔偿理论，50,000 美元。","C":"建筑公司的信赖损失为 50,000 美元，预期损失为 30,000 美元。","D":"没什么，因为建筑公司未能减轻损失。"},"explanationZh":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n违反建筑合同（例如通过毁约）使非违约方有权获得损害赔偿，这将使该方处于与合同下的履约相同的地位。当违约方是业主且违约发生在施工之前或期间时，建筑商可以收回其利润损失（即预期/预期损害）加上任何合理产生的成本。违规后产生的费用是不合理的，因此无法收回。在这里，医生在施工开始前就拒绝了施工合同。因此，建筑公司有权以预期损失的形式追回 30,000 美元的利润损失。但该建筑公司无法收回在违规事件后花费在材料上的 50,000 美元，因为这些费用不合理。 （选择 B）如果该利益不是合法获得的，并且保留该利益是不公平的，则一方可以根据授予另一方的利益的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿。但在这里，建筑公司并没有通过在违规后承担 50,000 美元的费用来给医生带来好处。 （选择C）当一方的预期损害无法可靠地计量时，可以判给信赖损害赔偿，使该方处于与未签订合同相同的境地。信赖损害赔偿金代替预期损害赔偿金，因此信赖损害赔偿金加上预期损害赔偿金总是不合适的。 （选项 D） 建筑公司未能在违约后通过承担费用来减轻损失，但这并不妨碍其收回建筑合同中的利润损失。\n\n教育目标：当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商可以收回其利润损失（即预期损害）加上任何合理产生的成本。违规后的成本无法收回。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释预期损害赔偿）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商可以收回其利润损失（即预期损害）加上任何合理产生的成本。违规后的成本无法收回。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果医生拒绝收回毁约，而建筑公司起诉他要求赔偿，建筑公司最多有权追偿多少？","rule":"当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商可以收回其利润损失（即预期损害）加上任何合理产生的成本。违规后的成本无法收回。","analysis":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n违反建筑合同（例如通过毁约）使非违约方有权获得损害赔偿，这将使该方处于与合同下的履约相同的地位。当违约方是业主且违约发生在施工之前或期间时，建筑商可以收回其利润损失（即预期/预期损害）加上任何合理产生的成本。违规后产生的费用是不合理的，因此无法收回。在这里，医生在施工开始前就拒绝了施工合同。因此，建筑公司有权以预期损失的形式追回 30,000 美元的利润损失。但该建筑公司无法收回在违规事件后花费在材料上的 50,000 美元，因为这些费用不合理。 （选择 B）如果该利益不是合法获得的，并且保留该利益是不公平的，则一方可以根据授予另一方的利益的合理价值获得返还利益赔偿。但在这里，建筑公司并没有通过在违规后承担 50,000 美元的费用来给医生带来好处。 （选择C）当一方的预期损害无法可靠地计量时，可以判给信赖损害赔偿，使该方处于与未签订合同相同的境地。信赖损害赔偿金代替预期损害赔偿金，因此信赖损害赔偿金加上预期损害赔偿金总是不合适的。 （选项 D） 建筑公司未能在违约后通过承担费用来减轻损失，但这并不妨碍其收回建筑合同中的利润损失。\n\n教育目标：当业主在施工前或施工期间违反施工合同时，建筑商可以收回其利润损失（即预期损害）加上任何合理产生的成本。违规后的成本无法收回。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 347（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释预期损害赔偿）。","conclusion":"答案 A：30,000 美元，建筑公司的预期损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":263,"sourceQuestionPage":776,"sourceQuestionPages":[776],"sourceExplanationPages":[777,778],"sourceAnswerPages":[777,778],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-264","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A manufacturer of computer chipsets for smartphones contracted to sell to a tech company 1 million chipsets each month for three years, payment for each installment to be made upon delivery. Before delivering the first installment, the manufacturer told the tech company, \"Our manufacturing plant is undergoing renovations. This will likely have a temporary impact on the quantity, but not the quality, of chipsets in future installments.\" The manufacturer delivered the first three installments to the tech company in compliance with the contract. However, the fourth installment contained only 500,000 chipsets. The tech company accepted the installment on the manufacturer's assurance that the remaining 500,000 chipsets would be delivered in two weeks, which they were. Then the manufacturer communicated that the fifth installment likely would contain only 500,000 chipsets. Which of the following statements best describes the tech company's rights after receiving the manufacturer's most recent communication regarding the fifth installment?","choices":{"A":"It can demand adequate assurance that the fifth installment will conform to the contract and treat a failure to give immediate assurance as a repudiation.","B":"It can treat the manufacturer's communication as a repudiation and sue for breach of contract.","C":"It cannot demand adequate assurance that the fifth installment will comply with the contract because it accepted a nonconforming installment.","D":"It cannot sue for breach of contract if it demands, and the manufacturer gives, adequate assurance that the fifth installment will comply with the contract."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A party's statements or conduct may cast doubt on its ability or willingness to perform under the contract. This prospective inability to perform is not a breach of contract because—unlike anticipatory repudiation—the party has not clearly and unequivocally refused to perform. However, it gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance and suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Failure to provide such assurance within a reasonable time (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) constitutes a breach. Here, the manufacturer communicated that the fifth installment likely would contain only 500,000 chipsets. Although this cast doubt on the manufacturer's ability to deliver 1 million chipsets as promised (prospective inability to perform), it was not a clear and unequivocal refusal to do so (no anticipatory repudiation). Therefore, the tech company cannot sue for breach of contract if it demands, and the manufacturer gives, adequate assurance that the fifth installment will comply with the contract (Choice B). (Choice A) A party that receives a proper demand for assurance has a reasonable time to provide assurance (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) before the other party can treat the failure to do so as a repudiation and sue for breach. As a result, the tech company cannot treat a failure to give immediate assurance as a repudiation. (Choice C) In an installment contract, a party that accepts a nonconforming payment or delivery (eg, the fourth installment of 500,000 chipsets) does not forfeit the right to demand adequate assurance that future installments will comply with the contract.\n\nEducational objective: One party's prospective inability to perform under the contract gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance and suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Failure to provide assurance within a reasonable time (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) constitutes a breach.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 251 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining when a party's failure to give adequate assurance may be treated as repudiation).","educationalObjective":"One party's prospective inability to perform under the contract gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance and suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Failure to provide assurance within a reasonable time (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) constitutes a breach.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (right to adequate assurance of performance).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 251 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining when a party's failure to give adequate assurance may be treated as repudiation).","rule":"One party's prospective inability to perform under the contract gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance and suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Failure to provide assurance within a reasonable time (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) constitutes a breach.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following statements best describes the tech company's rights after receiving the manufacturer's most recent communication regarding the fifth installment?","rule":"One party's prospective inability to perform under the contract gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance and suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Failure to provide assurance within a reasonable time (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) constitutes a breach.","analysis":"A party's statements or conduct may cast doubt on its ability or willingness to perform under the contract. This prospective inability to perform is not a breach of contract because—unlike anticipatory repudiation—the party has not clearly and unequivocally refused to perform. However, it gives the other party the right to demand assurance of performance and suspend its own performance until assurance is provided. Failure to provide such assurance within a reasonable time (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) constitutes a breach. Here, the manufacturer communicated that the fifth installment likely would contain only 500,000 chipsets. Although this cast doubt on the manufacturer's ability to deliver 1 million chipsets as promised (prospective inability to perform), it was not a clear and unequivocal refusal to do so (no anticipatory repudiation). Therefore, the tech company cannot sue for breach of contract if it demands, and the manufacturer gives, adequate assurance that the fifth installment will comply with the contract (Choice B). (Choice A) A party that receives a proper demand for assurance has a reasonable time to provide assurance (not to exceed 30 days under the UCC) before the other party can treat the failure to do so as a repudiation and sue for breach. As a result, the tech company cannot treat a failure to give immediate assurance as a repudiation. (Choice C) In an installment contract, a party that accepts a nonconforming payment or delivery (eg, the fourth installment of 500,000 chipsets) does not forfeit the right to demand adequate assurance that future installments will comply with the contract.","conclusion":"Answer D: It cannot sue for breach of contract if it demands, and the manufacturer gives, adequate assurance that the fifth installment will comply with the contract."},"questionZh":"一家智能手机计算机芯片组制造商与一家科技公司签订合同，在三年内每月向一家科技公司出售 100 万个芯片组，每期付款均在交货时支付。在交付第一批芯片之前，制造商告诉科技公司：“我们的制造工厂正在进行翻修。这可能会对未来分批芯片组的数量产生暂时影响，但不会影响质量。”制造商按照合同向科技公司交付了前三期。然而，第四批仅包含 500,000 个芯片组。该科技公司接受了分期付款，因为制造商保证剩余的 500,000 个芯片组将在两周内交付，事实也确实如此。然后制造商表示第五批可能只包含 500,000 个芯片组。以下哪项陈述最准确地描述了科技公司在收到制造商有关第五批的最新通信后的权利？","choicesZh":{"A":"它可以要求充分保证第五批付款将符合合同，并将未能立即提供保证的行为视为违约。","B":"它可以将制造商的沟通视为否认并起诉违约行为。","C":"它不能要求充分保证第五批货物将遵守合同，因为它接受了不合格的货物。","D":"如果制造商要求并充分保证第五批货物将遵守合同，那么它就不能以违反合同为由提起诉讼。"},"explanationZh":"一方的声明或行为可能会让人对其履行合同的能力或意愿产生怀疑。这种预期无法履行的情况并不构成违约，因为与预期拒付不同的是，该方并未明确且毫不含糊地拒绝履行。然而，它赋予另一方要求履行保证的权利，并在提供保证之前暂停自己的履行。未能在合理时间内（根据 UCC 规定不超过 30 天）提供此类保证即构成违约。在此，制造商表示第五批可能仅包含 500,000 个芯片组。尽管这让人对制造商是否有能力按照承诺交付 100 万个芯片组（预期无法履行）产生怀疑，但这并不是明确且毫不含糊地拒绝这样做（没有预期的否认）。因此，如果技术公司要求且制造商提供充分保证第五批货将遵守合同，则科技公司不能以违约为由提起诉讼（选择B）。 （选择 A）收到适当保证要求的一方有合理的时间提供保证（根据 UCC，不超过 30 天），然后另一方可以将未能提供保证的情况视为否认并起诉违约行为。因此，科技公司不能将未能立即提供保证视为拒绝。 （选择C） 在分期付款合同中，接受不合格付款或交付（例如，第四期500,000 个芯片组）的一方并不放弃要求充分保证未来分期付款将遵守合同的权利。\n\n教育目标：一方可能无法履行合同，另一方有权要求履行保证，并在提供保证之前暂停自己的履行。未能在合理时间内（根据 UCC 规定不超过 30 天）提供保证将构成违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 251（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了当一方未能提供充分保证时可能被视为否认）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"一方可能无法履行合同，另一方有权要求履行保证，并在提供保证之前暂停履行合同。未能在合理时间内（根据 UCC 规定不超过 30 天）提供保证将构成违约。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项陈述最准确地描述了科技公司在收到制造商有关第五批的最新通信后的权利？","rule":"一方可能无法履行合同，另一方有权要求履行保证，并在提供保证之前暂停履行合同。未能在合理时间内（根据 UCC 规定不超过 30 天）提供保证将构成违约。","analysis":"一方的声明或行为可能会让人对其履行合同的能力或意愿产生怀疑。这种预期无法履行的情况并不构成违约，因为与预期拒付不同的是，该方并未明确且毫不含糊地拒绝履行。然而，它赋予另一方要求履行保证的权利，并在提供保证之前暂停自己的履行。未能在合理时间内（根据 UCC 规定不超过 30 天）提供此类保证即构成违约。在此，制造商表示第五批可能仅包含 500,000 个芯片组。尽管这让人对制造商是否有能力按照承诺交付 100 万个芯片组（预期无法履行）产生怀疑，但这并不是明确且毫不含糊地拒绝这样做（没有预期的否认）。因此，如果技术公司要求且制造商提供充分保证第五批货将遵守合同，则科技公司不能以违约为由提起诉讼（选择B）。 （选择 A）收到适当保证要求的一方有合理的时间提供保证（根据 UCC，不超过 30 天），然后另一方可以将未能提供保证的情况视为否认并起诉违约行为。因此，科技公司不能将未能立即提供保证视为拒绝。 （选择C） 在分期付款合同中，接受不合格付款或交付（例如，第四期500,000 个芯片组）的一方并不放弃要求充分保证未来分期付款将遵守合同的权利。\n\n教育目标：一方可能无法履行合同，另一方有权要求履行保证，并在提供保证之前暂停自己的履行。未能在合理时间内（根据 UCC 规定不超过 30 天）提供保证将构成违约。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-609 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（获得充分履行保证的权利）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 251（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释了当一方未能提供充分保证时可能被视为否认）。","conclusion":"答案 D：如果制造商要求并充分保证第五批货物将遵守合同，那么它就不能以违反合同为由提起诉讼。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-16e26cd601960b3b"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":264,"sourceQuestionPage":779,"sourceQuestionPages":[779],"sourceExplanationPages":[780,781],"sourceAnswerPages":[780,781],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-265","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"An experienced rancher contracted to harvest his neighbor's wheat crop for $1,000 \"when the crop [was] ripe.\" In early September, the neighbor told the rancher that the crop was ripe. The rancher delayed because he had other customers to attend to. The neighbor was concerned that the delay might cause the crop to be lost, for hailstorms were common in that part of the country in the fall. In fact, in early October, before the crop was harvested, it was destroyed by a hailstorm. Is the rancher liable for the loss?","choices":{"A":"No, because no time for performance was established in the contract.","B":"No, because the neighbor failed to tell the rancher that the crop might be destroyed by a hailstorm.","C":"Yes, because a party who undertakes a contractual obligation is liable for all the consequences that flow from his breach.","D":"Yes, because at the time the contract was made, the rancher had reason to foresee the loss as a probable result of his breach."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach The purpose of compensatory damages is to put the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed as agreed. Compensatory damages include: expectation damages – the lost value of the breaching party's performance and consequential damages – losses that arise from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was made. Here, the rancher delayed harvesting the neighbor's wheat crop because he had other customers to serve. As a result of the delay, the crop was destroyed by a hailstorm before it could be harvested (special circumstances). This loss was foreseeable when the rancher– neighbor contract was made, given the rancher's experience and the frequency of hailstorms in the fall. Therefore, the rancher is liable for the loss of the crop. (Choice A) The language in the contract stating that the rancher was to harvest the crop \"when the crop was ripe\" was sufficient to establish the time of the rancher's performance. (Choice B) Although the neighbor failed to tell the rancher that the crop might be destroyed by a hailstorm, the rancher's experience and the frequency of hailstorms combined to make the loss foreseeable. (Choice C) A party who undertakes a contractual obligation is liable only for the foreseeable consequences that flow from his/her breach.\n\nEducational objective: Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are recoverable only if they were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was made.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 351 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unforeseeability and related limitations on damages).","educationalObjective":"Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are recoverable only if they were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was made.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 351 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unforeseeability and related limitations on damages).","rule":"Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are recoverable only if they were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was made.","irac":{"issue":"Is the rancher liable for the loss?","rule":"Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are recoverable only if they were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was made.","analysis":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach The purpose of compensatory damages is to put the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed as agreed. Compensatory damages include: expectation damages – the lost value of the breaching party's performance and consequential damages – losses that arise from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances that were reasonably foreseeable to the breaching party when the contract was made. Here, the rancher delayed harvesting the neighbor's wheat crop because he had other customers to serve. As a result of the delay, the crop was destroyed by a hailstorm before it could be harvested (special circumstances). This loss was foreseeable when the rancher– neighbor contract was made, given the rancher's experience and the frequency of hailstorms in the fall. Therefore, the rancher is liable for the loss of the crop. (Choice A) The language in the contract stating that the rancher was to harvest the crop \"when the crop was ripe\" was sufficient to establish the time of the rancher's performance. (Choice B) Although the neighbor failed to tell the rancher that the crop might be destroyed by a hailstorm, the rancher's experience and the frequency of hailstorms combined to make the loss foreseeable. (Choice C) A party who undertakes a contractual obligation is liable only for the foreseeable consequences that flow from his/her breach.","conclusion":"Answer D: Yes, because at the time the contract was made, the rancher had reason to foresee the loss as a probable result of his breach."},"questionZh":"一位经验丰富的牧场主以 1,000 美元的价格签订合同，“在小麦成熟时”收割邻居的小麦作物。九月初，邻居告诉牧场主庄稼成熟了。牧场主耽搁了，因为他还有其他顾客要照顾。邻居担心延误可能会导致庄稼损失，因为该国那个地区秋季经常遭遇冰雹。事实上，十月初，庄稼还没有收获，就被冰雹摧毁了。牧场主是否需要承担损失？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为合同中没有规定履行时间。","B":"不，因为邻居没有告诉牧场主庄稼可能会被冰雹毁掉。","C":"是的，因为承担合同义务的一方应对其违约所产生的一切后果负责。","D":"是的，因为在签订合同时，牧场主有理由预见到他的违约可能会导致损失。"},"explanationZh":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 补偿性损害赔偿的目的是使守约方处于与按照约定履行合同相同的境地。补偿性损害赔偿包括：预期损害赔偿——违约方履约造成的价值损失和间接损害——因守约方在订立合同时可以合理预见的特殊情况而产生的损失。在这里，牧场主推迟了邻居小麦作物的收割，因为他还有其他顾客需要服务。由于延误，农作物在收获前就被冰雹毁坏了（特殊情况）。考虑到牧场主的经验和秋季冰雹的频率，这种损失在牧场主与邻居签订合同时是可以预见的。因此，牧场主应对农作物的损失承担责任。 （选项 A） 合同中规定牧场主将在“农作物成熟时”收获农作物的措辞足以确定牧场主履行职责的时间。 （选项B） 虽然邻居没有告诉牧场主庄稼可能会被冰雹毁坏，但牧场主的经验和冰雹发生的频率结合在一起，损失是可以预见的。 （选择C）承担合同义务的一方仅对其违约所产生的可预见后果承担责任。\n\n教育目标： 间接损失（即因非违约方的特殊情况而产生的损失）只有在违约方在订立合同时可以合理预见到的情况下才能得到赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 351（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不可预见性和相关的损害限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"间接损失（即因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失）只有在违约方在订立合同时可以合理预见到的情况下才能得到赔偿。","iracZh":{"issue":"牧场主是否需要承担损失？","rule":"间接损失（即因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失）只有在违约方在订立合同时可以合理预见到的情况下才能得到赔偿。","analysis":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 补偿性损害赔偿的目的是使守约方处于与按照约定履行合同相同的境地。补偿性损害赔偿包括：预期损害赔偿——违约方履约造成的价值损失和间接损害——因守约方在订立合同时可以合理预见的特殊情况而产生的损失。在这里，牧场主推迟了邻居小麦作物的收割，因为他还有其他顾客需要服务。由于延误，农作物在收获前就被冰雹毁坏了（特殊情况）。考虑到牧场主的经验和秋季冰雹的频率，这种损失在牧场主与邻居签订合同时是可以预见的。因此，牧场主应对农作物的损失承担责任。 （选项 A） 合同中规定牧场主将在“农作物成熟时”收获农作物的措辞足以确定牧场主履行职责的时间。 （选项B） 虽然邻居没有告诉牧场主庄稼可能会被冰雹毁坏，但牧场主的经验和冰雹发生的频率结合在一起，损失是可以预见的。 （选择C）承担合同义务的一方仅对其违约所产生的可预见后果承担责任。\n\n教育目标： 间接损失（即因非违约方的特殊情况而产生的损失）只有在违约方在订立合同时可以合理预见到的情况下才能得到赔偿。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 351（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不可预见性和相关的损害限制）。","conclusion":"答案 D：是的，因为在签订合同时，牧场主有理由预见到他的违约可能会导致损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":265,"sourceQuestionPage":782,"sourceQuestionPages":[782],"sourceExplanationPages":[783,784],"sourceAnswerPages":[783,784],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-266","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A firm contracted with a municipality to repair a drawbridge and began work on February 1. The contract provided that the firm would be paid an additional $1,000 for each day the repair was completed before the April 1 completion deadline. On March 14, an unusual gear on the bridge broke. On that same day, the firm contracted with a supplier for a March 15 delivery to the bridge site of the only available replacement gear. The supplier did not know the firm's contract contained an incentive-pay provision regarding early completion. The supplier misdirected the delivery of the gear, and the firm did not receive it until March 20. The work on the bridge was completed on March 21. Had it not been for the late delivery of the gear, however, the firm would have completed the bridge repair on March 16. In an action against the supplier for breach of contract, will the firm be permitted to recover, as part of its damages, the additional $5,000 early-completion incentive pay it would have received from the municipality but for the supplier's breach?","choices":{"A":"No, because the supplier did not expressly agree to be responsible for such damages.","B":"No, because the supplier did not know and had no reason to know about the incentive- pay provision regarding early completion.","C":"Yes, because, by the nature of its contract with the firm, the supplier should have known that time was of the essence.","D":"Yes, because the supplier is liable for all damages flowing directly from its breach."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach After a contract is breached, the nonbreaching party may seek compensatory damages. Generally, these damages aim to put the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. As a result, the calculation begins by determining expectation damages—ie, the natural and obvious losses from the breach. And in some cases, consequential damages—ie, losses arising from a party's special circumstances—are also warranted. Consequential damages are appropriate only if the breaching party: knew about the nonbreaching party's special circumstances or could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the nonbreaching party's special circumstances, that would result from a breach. Here, the firm stood to gain $1,000 for each day the drawbridge repair was completed before the April 1 completion deadline—$5,000 in total. Had the supplier known about those special circumstances or otherwise been able to foresee the harm that its breach would cause, consequential damages of $5,000 would be warranted. But since there is no indication the supplier knew or had reason to know of the pay provision regarding early completion, the firm will not be permitted to recover the $5,000. (Choice A) There is no rule requiring that a party expressly agree to be responsible for consequential damages stemming from a breach to hold that party responsible. (Choice C) When a contract calls for a specific completion date, time is considered \"of the essence,\" and a failure to perform by that date is a breach. However, this alone is not sufficient for recovering consequential damages. (Choice D) A party is not liable for all damages flowing directly from its breach—eg, damages related to special circumstances of which the breaching party did not know of and could not reasonably foresee.\n\nEducational objective: Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from a nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are appropriate if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","educationalObjective":"Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from a nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are appropriate if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","references":"","rule":"Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from a nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are appropriate if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","irac":{"issue":"In an action against the supplier for breach of contract, will the firm be permitted to recover, as part of its damages, the additional $5,000 early-completion incentive pay it would have received from the municipality but for the supplier's breach?","rule":"Consequential damages (ie, losses arising from a nonbreaching party's special circumstances) are appropriate if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","analysis":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach After a contract is breached, the nonbreaching party may seek compensatory damages. Generally, these damages aim to put the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. As a result, the calculation begins by determining expectation damages—ie, the natural and obvious losses from the breach. And in some cases, consequential damages—ie, losses arising from a party's special circumstances—are also warranted. Consequential damages are appropriate only if the breaching party: knew about the nonbreaching party's special circumstances or could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the nonbreaching party's special circumstances, that would result from a breach. Here, the firm stood to gain $1,000 for each day the drawbridge repair was completed before the April 1 completion deadline—$5,000 in total. Had the supplier known about those special circumstances or otherwise been able to foresee the harm that its breach would cause, consequential damages of $5,000 would be warranted. But since there is no indication the supplier knew or had reason to know of the pay provision regarding early completion, the firm will not be permitted to recover the $5,000. (Choice A) There is no rule requiring that a party expressly agree to be responsible for consequential damages stemming from a breach to hold that party responsible. (Choice C) When a contract calls for a specific completion date, time is considered \"of the essence,\" and a failure to perform by that date is a breach. However, this alone is not sufficient for recovering consequential damages. (Choice D) A party is not liable for all damages flowing directly from its breach—eg, damages related to special circumstances of which the breaching party did not know of and could not reasonably foresee.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the supplier did not know and had no reason to know about the incentive- pay provision regarding early completion."},"questionZh":"一家公司与市政当局签订了修复一座吊桥的合同，并于 2 月 1 日开始工作。合同规定，在 4 月 1 日完工期限之前，每完成一天的修复工作，该公司将额外获得 1,000 美元的报酬。 3月14日，桥上一个不寻常的齿轮坏了。同一天，该公司与一家供应商签订了合同，将于 3 月 15 日向桥梁现场交付唯一可用的替换装备。供应商并不知道该公司的合同包含有关提前完工的奖励条款。供应商错误地交付了设备，公司直到 3 月 20 日才收到。桥梁工程于 3 月 21 日完成。然而，如果没有延迟交付设备，该公司本可以在 3 月 16 日完成桥梁修复。在针对供应商违约的诉讼中，是否允许该公司收回其本应从市政府获得的额外 5,000 美元提前竣工奖励金，作为其损害赔偿的一部分。供应商违约？","choicesZh":{"A":"不可以，因为供应商没有明确同意对此类损害负责。","B":"不，因为供应商不知道也没有理由知道有关提前完工的激励工资条款。","C":"是的，因为根据与公司签订的合同的性质，供应商应该知道时间至关重要。","D":"是的，因为供应商应对其违约直接造成的所有损失负责。"},"explanationZh":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 合同违约后，守约方可以寻求损害赔偿。一般来说，这些损害赔偿的目的是使非违约方处于与合同已履行相同的境地。因此，计算首先确定预期损失，即违约造成的自然和明显损失。在某些情况下，间接损害（即因一方的特殊情况而造成的损失）也是合理的。仅当违约方满足以下条件时，后果性损害赔偿才是适当的： 了解守约方的特殊情况，或者能够根据守约方的特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害。在这里，在 4 月 1 日完工期限之前完成吊桥修复工作，该公司每天将获得 1,000 美元的收益——总共 5,000 美元。如果供应商了解这些特殊情况或能够预见其违规行为将造成的损害，则可以保证 5,000 美元的间接损失。但由于没有迹象表明供应商知道或有理由知道有关提前完工的付款条款，因此该公司将不被允许收回 5,000 美元。 （选择A）没有规则要求一方明确同意对因违约而造成的间接损害负责，以追究该方的责任。 （选择 C）当合同要求具体的完成日期时，时间被视为“至关重要”，未能在该日期之前履行即构成违约。然而，仅此不足以弥补间接损失。 （选择 D） 一方不对直接因其违约行为而造成的所有损害负责，例如，与违约方不知道且无法合理预见的特殊情况相关的损害。\n\n教育目标：如果违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况，则后果性损害（即因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失）是适当的\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况，则后果性损害赔偿（即因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失）是适当的\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"在针对供应商违约的诉讼中，作为损害赔偿的一部分，公司是否可以获准追回如果供应商违约，该公司本应从市政府获得的额外 5,000 美元提前完工奖励金？","rule":"如果违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况，则后果性损害赔偿（即因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失）是适当的\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。","analysis":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 合同违约后，守约方可以寻求损害赔偿。一般来说，这些损害赔偿的目的是使非违约方处于与合同已履行相同的境地。因此，计算首先确定预期损失，即违约造成的自然和明显损失。在某些情况下，间接损害（即因一方的特殊情况而造成的损失）也是合理的。仅当违约方满足以下条件时，后果性损害赔偿才是适当的： 了解守约方的特殊情况，或者能够根据守约方的特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害。在这里，在 4 月 1 日完工期限之前完成吊桥修复工作，该公司每天将获得 1,000 美元的收益——总共 5,000 美元。如果供应商了解这些特殊情况或能够预见其违规行为将造成的损害，则可以保证 5,000 美元的间接损失。但由于没有迹象表明供应商知道或有理由知道有关提前完工的付款条款，因此该公司将不被允许收回 5,000 美元。 （选择A）没有规则要求一方明确同意对因违约而造成的间接损害负责，以追究该方的责任。 （选择 C）当合同要求具体的完成日期时，时间被视为“至关重要”，未能在该日期之前履行即构成违约。然而，仅此不足以弥补间接损失。 （选择 D） 一方不对直接因其违约行为而造成的所有损害负责，例如，与违约方不知道且无法合理预见的特殊情况相关的损害。\n\n教育目标：如果违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况，则后果性损害（即因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失）是适当的\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。","conclusion":"答案 B：不，因为供应商不知道也没有理由知道有关提前完工的激励工资条款。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":266,"sourceQuestionPage":785,"sourceQuestionPages":[785],"sourceExplanationPages":[786,787],"sourceAnswerPages":[786,787],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-267","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A contractor agreed to build a power plant for a public utility. A concrete company agreed with the contractor to lay the foundation for $200,000. The concrete company supplied goods and services worth $150,000, for which the contractor made progress payments aggregating $100,000 as required by the subcontract. The concrete company then breached by unjustifiably refusing to perform further. The contractor reasonably spent $120,000 to have the work completed by another foundation layer. The concrete company sues the contractor for the reasonable value of benefits conferred, and the contractor counterclaims for breach of contract. Which of the following should be the court's decision?","choices":{"A":"The concrete company recovers $50,000, the benefit conferred on the contractor for which the concrete company has not been paid.","B":"The concrete company recovers $30,000, the benefit the concrete company conferred on the contractor minus the $20,000 in damages incurred by the contractor.","C":"The contractor recovers $20,000, the excess over the contract price that was paid by the contractor for the performance it had bargained to receive from the concrete company.","D":"Neither party recovers anything, because the concrete company committed a material, unexcused breach and the contractor received a $50,000 benefit from the concrete company for which the concrete company has not been paid."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nBreach of a construction contract allows the nonbreaching party to seek compensatory damages that will place that party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. When the breaching party is the builder (as seen here) and the breach occurs during construction, the nonbreaching party can generally recover the cost of completion over the contract price.* But the builder can only recover the value of unpaid-for work performed before the breach if the nonbreaching party was unjustly enriched by that work. Here, the concrete company breached the construction contract by refusing to complete the foundation as agreed. At that time, the contractor had already paid the company $100,000. The contractor then spent $120,000 to have the work completed by another foundation layer—bringing the contractor's total payment to $220,000. Since the contract price was $200,000, the contractor should recover the $20,000 cost of completion over the contract price (Choice D). *When the cost of completion over the contract price is clearly excessive compared to the loss in value caused by the breach (ie, involves undue economic waste), recovery is instead based on the value of the promised construction minus the value of the nonconforming construction. (Choices A & B) Although the concrete company provided the contractor $50,000 worth of goods and services for which it has not been paid, the contractor was not unjustly enriched—ie, did not receive a benefit that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. The reason is that the contractor still had to pay $20,000 in excess of the contract\n\nprice, so any payment to the concrete company would just make the contractor's damages worse.\n\nEducational objective: When a builder breaches a construction contract during construction, the nonbreaching party can recover the cost of completion over the contract price.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 348 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the measure of damages for defective construction is the cost of completion unless it is clearly disproportionate to the loss in value).","educationalObjective":"When a builder breaches a construction contract during construction, the nonbreaching party can recover the cost of completion over the contract price.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 348 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that the measure of damages for defective construction is the cost of completion unless it is clearly disproportionate to the loss in value).","rule":"When a builder breaches a construction contract during construction, the nonbreaching party can recover the cost of completion over the contract price.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following should be the court's decision?","rule":"When a builder breaches a construction contract during construction, the nonbreaching party can recover the cost of completion over the contract price.","analysis":"Compensatory damages for breach of construction contracts\n\nBefore During construction After construction construction starts completed\n\nBreach by Owner entitled to Owner entitled to: N/A builder cost of completion cost of completion/correction if no (owner undue economic waste OR paid) value of promised construction minus nonconforming construction Breach by Builder entitled to: Builder entitled to owner contract price lost profits plus costs incurred OR (did not contract price minus cost of completion pay)\n\nBreach of a construction contract allows the nonbreaching party to seek compensatory damages that will place that party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. When the breaching party is the builder (as seen here) and the breach occurs during construction, the nonbreaching party can generally recover the cost of completion over the contract price.* But the builder can only recover the value of unpaid-for work performed before the breach if the nonbreaching party was unjustly enriched by that work. Here, the concrete company breached the construction contract by refusing to complete the foundation as agreed. At that time, the contractor had already paid the company $100,000. The contractor then spent $120,000 to have the work completed by another foundation layer—bringing the contractor's total payment to $220,000. Since the contract price was $200,000, the contractor should recover the $20,000 cost of completion over the contract price (Choice D). *When the cost of completion over the contract price is clearly excessive compared to the loss in value caused by the breach (ie, involves undue economic waste), recovery is instead based on the value of the promised construction minus the value of the nonconforming construction. (Choices A & B) Although the concrete company provided the contractor $50,000 worth of goods and services for which it has not been paid, the contractor was not unjustly enriched—ie, did not receive a benefit that was not rightfully earned and would be unfair to keep. The reason is that the contractor still had to pay $20,000 in excess of the contract\n\nprice, so any payment to the concrete company would just make the contractor's damages worse.","conclusion":"Answer C: The contractor recovers $20,000, the excess over the contract price that was paid by the contractor for the performance it had bargained to receive from the concrete company."},"questionZh":"承包商同意为公用事业建造一座发电厂。一家混凝土公司与承包商同意以 20 万美元打地基。该混凝土公司提供了价值 150,000 美元的货物和服务，承包商按照分包合同的要求支付了总计 100,000 美元的进度款。随后，混凝土公司无理拒绝进一步履约，从而违约。承包商合理地花费了 120,000 美元，让另一层基础层完成了这项工作。混凝土公司就所给予的合理利益价值起诉承包商，承包商则以违约为由反诉。以下哪项应该是法院的判决？","choicesZh":{"A":"混凝土公司收回了 50,000 美元，这是承包商获得的、但混凝土公司尚未获得报酬的利益。","B":"混凝土公司获得了 30,000 美元的赔偿，即混凝土公司给予承包商的利益减去承包商遭受的 20,000 美元的损失。","C":"承包商获得了 20,000 美元的赔偿，这是承包商为履行与混凝土公司讨价还价而支付的合同价格之外的部分。","D":"双方均未获得任何赔偿，因为混凝土公司犯下了重大、无故的违约行为，承包商从混凝土公司获得了 50,000 美元的利益，而混凝土公司尚未收到报酬。"},"explanationZh":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n违反建筑合同允许非违约方寻求损害赔偿，这将使该方处于与合同已履行相同的地位。当违约方是建筑商时（如图所示），并且违约发生在施工过程中，守约方通常可以收回合同价格之外的完工成本。*但只有当守约方因该工程不当致富时，建筑商才能收回违约前已完成的未付工程的价值。本案中，混凝土公司拒绝按照约定完成地基施工，违反了施工合同。当时，承包商已经向该公司支付了10万美元。随后，承包商花费了 120,000 美元通过另一层基础层完成了这项工作，从而使承包商的总付款达到了 220,000 美元。由于合同价格为 200,000 美元，承包商应在合同价格之上收回 20,000 美元的完工成本（选项 D）。 *当与违约造成的价值损失相比，超出合同价格的竣工成本明显过高（即涉及过度的经济浪费）时，赔偿金额则基于承诺施工的价值减去不合格施工的价值。 （选项 A 和 B）虽然混凝土公司向承包商提供了价值 50,000 美元的商品和服务，但尚未付款，但承包商并没有获得不当得利，即没有获得不正当赚取的利益，并且保留这​​种利益是不公平的。原因是承包商仍需多付合同金额20,000美元\n\n价格，因此向混凝土公司付款只会使承包商的损失更加严重。\n\n教育目标：当建筑商在施工过程中违反施工合同时，守约方可以在合同价格的基础上收回完工成本。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 348（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，有缺陷的建筑的损害赔偿标准是竣工成本，除非它与价值损失明显不成比例）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"建设单位在施工过程中违反施工合同的，守约方可以在合同价款的基础上追偿竣工费用。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪项应该是法院的判决？","rule":"建设单位在施工过程中违反施工合同的，守约方可以在合同价款的基础上追偿竣工费用。","analysis":"违反建设工程合同的损害赔偿\n\n施工前 施工中 施工后 施工完成\n\n业主的违约行为 业主有权： 不适用 建筑商 竣工成本 竣工成本/纠正，如果没有（业主不当经济浪费或已支付） 承诺的建筑价值减去不合格建筑 建筑商的违约行为，有权获得： 建筑商有权获得 业主合同价格损失利润加上发生的成本或（未支付合同价格减去竣工成本）\n\n违反建筑合同允许非违约方寻求损害赔偿，这将使该方处于与合同已履行相同的地位。当违约方是建筑商时（如图所示），并且违约发生在施工过程中，守约方通常可以收回合同价格之外的完工成本。*但只有当守约方因该工程不当致富时，建筑商才能收回违约前已完成的未付工程的价值。本案中，混凝土公司拒绝按照约定完成地基施工，违反了施工合同。当时，承包商已经向该公司支付了10万美元。随后，承包商花费了 120,000 美元通过另一层基础层完成了这项工作，从而使承包商的总付款达到了 220,000 美元。由于合同价格为 200,000 美元，承包商应在合同价格之上收回 20,000 美元的完工成本（选项 D）。 *当与违约造成的价值损失相比，超出合同价格的竣工成本明显过高（即涉及过度的经济浪费）时，赔偿金额则基于承诺施工的价值减去不合格施工的价值。 （选项 A 和 B）虽然混凝土公司向承包商提供了价值 50,000 美元的商品和服务，但尚未付款，但承包商并没有获得不当得利，即没有获得不正当赚取的利益，并且保留这​​种利益是不公平的。原因是承包商仍需多付合同金额20,000美元\n\n价格，因此向混凝土公司付款只会使承包商的损失更加严重。\n\n教育目标：当建筑商在施工过程中违反施工合同时，守约方可以在合同价格的基础上收回完工成本。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 348（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，有缺陷的建筑的损害赔偿标准是竣工成本，除非它与价值损失明显不成比例）。","conclusion":"答案 C：承包商获得了 20,000 美元的赔偿，这是承包商为履行与混凝土公司讨价还价而支付的合同价格之外的部分。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":267,"sourceQuestionPage":788,"sourceQuestionPages":[788],"sourceExplanationPages":[789,790],"sourceAnswerPages":[789,790],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-268","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Impossibility, Impracticability & Frustration","question":"An engineer entered into a written contract with an owner to serve in the essential position of on-site supervisor for construction of an office building. The day after signing the contract, the engineer was injured while bicycling and was rendered physically incapable of performing as the on-site supervisor. The engineer offered to serve as an off-site consultant for the same pay as originally agreed to by the parties. Is the owner likely to prevail in an action against the engineer for damages resulting from his failure to perform under the contract?","choices":{"A":"No, because the engineer offered a reasonable substitute by offering to serve as an off- site consultant.","B":"No, because the engineer's physical ability to perform as on-site supervisor was a basic assumption of the contract.","C":"Yes, because the engineer breached the contract by disappointing the owner's expectations.","D":"Yes, because the engineer's duty to perform was personal and absolute."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A contracting party's duty to perform can be discharged by impracticability. The defense of impracticability is available when: an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for the party to perform the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and the party seeking discharge was not at fault in causing the event to occur. Here, the engineer entered into a contract to serve in the essential position of on-site supervisor for construction of an office building. The next day, the engineer suffered a bicycle injury that rendered him physically incapable of performing. Because the engineer's physical ability to perform as on-site supervisor was a basic assumption of the contract and there is no indication that the injury was his fault, his duty to perform was discharged. Therefore, the engineer did not breach the contract by failing to perform, and the owner is unlikely to prevail (Choice C). (Choice A) An offer by a party to perform less than what was contracted for (eg, the engineer's offer to serve as an off-site consultant) will not excuse a breach. This is true even if the offered performance is a reasonable substitute. (Choice D) Although the engineer's duty to perform was personal and absolute, he is not in breach—and the owner will not prevail—because the doctrine of impracticability excused the engineer's nonperformance under the contract.\n\nEducational objective: The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for a party to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) the party seeking discharge was not at fault in causing the event to occur.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discharge by supervening impracticability).","educationalObjective":"The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for a party to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) the party seeking discharge was not at fault in causing the event to occur.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 261 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (discharge by supervening impracticability).","rule":"The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for a party to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) the party seeking discharge was not at fault in causing the event to occur.","irac":{"issue":"Is the owner likely to prevail in an action against the engineer for damages resulting from his failure to perform under the contract?","rule":"The defense of impracticability is available when (1) an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for a party to perform, (2) the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur, and (3) the party seeking discharge was not at fault in causing the event to occur.","analysis":"A contracting party's duty to perform can be discharged by impracticability. The defense of impracticability is available when: an unanticipated or extraordinary event makes it impracticable for the party to perform the contract was formed under a basic assumption that the event would not occur and the party seeking discharge was not at fault in causing the event to occur. Here, the engineer entered into a contract to serve in the essential position of on-site supervisor for construction of an office building. The next day, the engineer suffered a bicycle injury that rendered him physically incapable of performing. Because the engineer's physical ability to perform as on-site supervisor was a basic assumption of the contract and there is no indication that the injury was his fault, his duty to perform was discharged. Therefore, the engineer did not breach the contract by failing to perform, and the owner is unlikely to prevail (Choice C). (Choice A) An offer by a party to perform less than what was contracted for (eg, the engineer's offer to serve as an off-site consultant) will not excuse a breach. This is true even if the offered performance is a reasonable substitute. (Choice D) Although the engineer's duty to perform was personal and absolute, he is not in breach—and the owner will not prevail—because the doctrine of impracticability excused the engineer's nonperformance under the contract.","conclusion":"Answer B: No, because the engineer's physical ability to perform as on-site supervisor was a basic assumption of the contract."},"questionZh":"一名工程师与业主签订了一份书面合同，担任办公楼施工现场监理的重要职位。合同签订的第二天，工程师在骑自行车时受伤，导致身体无法担任现场主管。该工程师提出担任场外顾问，报酬与双方最初商定的相同。业主是否有可能在针对工程师因未能履行合同而造成损失的诉讼中获胜？","choicesZh":{"A":"不，因为工程师通过担任场外顾问提供了合理的替代方案。","B":"不会，因为工程师担任现场主管的身体能力是合同的基本假设。","C":"是的，因为工程师违反了合同，辜负了业主的期望。","D":"是的，因为工程师的履行职责是个人的、绝对的。"},"explanationZh":"缔约方的履行义务可以因不可行而解除。不可行抗辩适用于以下情况： 不可预见的或异常的事件使得当事人无法履行合同，并且是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的，并且寻求解除合同的一方在导致该事件发生时没有过错。在这里，工程师签订了一份合同，担任办公楼施工现场监理的重要职位。第二天，工程师在骑自行车时受伤，导致他无法继续工作。由于工程师履行现场监督职责的身体能力是合同的基本假设，并且没有迹象表明受伤是他的过错，因此他的履行义务被解除。因此，工程师不履行合同并不构成违约，业主胜诉的可能性不大（选项C）。 （选择 A） 一方提出执行少于合同规定的任务（例如，工程师提出担任场外顾问）并不能成为违约行为的借口。即使提供的履行是合理的替代品，情况也是如此。 （选项D）虽然工程师的履约义务是个人的和绝对的，但他并没有违约，而且业主也不会胜诉，因为履行艰难（impracticability）原则为工程师不履约提供了借口。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，可以对不切实际进行抗辩：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方无法履行合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，以及 (3) 寻求解除合同的一方在导致该事件发生时没有过错。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（因随之而来的不切实际而解除）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"在以下情况下，可以提出履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方无法履行合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，以及 (3) 寻求解除合同的一方在导致该事件发生时没有过错。","iracZh":{"issue":"业主是否有可能在针对工程师因未能履行合同而造成损失的诉讼中获胜？","rule":"在以下情况下，可以提出履行艰难（impracticability）抗辩：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方无法履行合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，以及 (3) 寻求解除合同的一方在导致该事件发生时没有过错。","analysis":"缔约方的履行义务可以因不可行而解除。不可行抗辩适用于以下情况： 不可预见的或异常的事件使得当事人无法履行合同，并且是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下成立的，并且寻求解除合同的一方在导致该事件发生时没有过错。在这里，工程师签订了一份合同，担任办公楼施工现场监理的重要职位。第二天，工程师在骑自行车时受伤，导致他无法继续工作。由于工程师履行现场监督职责的身体能力是合同的基本假设，并且没有迹象表明受伤是他的过错，因此他的履行义务被解除。因此，工程师不履行合同并不构成违约，业主胜诉的可能性不大（选项C）。 （选择 A） 一方提出执行少于合同规定的任务（例如，工程师提出担任场外顾问）并不能成为违约行为的借口。即使提供的履行是合理的替代品，情况也是如此。 （选项D）虽然工程师的履约义务是个人的和绝对的，但他并没有违约，而且业主也不会胜诉，因为履行艰难（impracticability）原则为工程师不履约提供了借口。\n\n教育目标：在以下情况下，可以对不切实际进行抗辩：(1) 意外或异常事件导致一方无法履行合同，(2) 合同是在该事件不会发生的基本假设下签订的，以及 (3) 寻求解除合同的一方在导致该事件发生时没有过错。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 261（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（因随之而来的不切实际而解除）。","conclusion":"答案 B：不会，因为工程师担任现场主管的身体能力是合同的基本假设。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-b49877b73fd004a7"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":268,"sourceQuestionPage":791,"sourceQuestionPages":[791],"sourceExplanationPages":[792,793],"sourceAnswerPages":[792,793],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-269","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"A tenant rented a commercial building from a landlord and operated a business in it. The building's large front window was smashed by vandals. The tenant, who was obligated thereunder to effect and pay for repairs in such cases, promptly contracted with a window store to replace the window for $2,000. After the store's satisfactory completion of the window replacement, the tenant ceased doing business and vacated the building. Upon vacating the building, the tenant mailed a $1,000 check to the window store bearing on its face the following conspicuous notation: \"This check is in full and final satisfaction of your $2,000 window replacement bill.\" Without noticing this notation, the window store cashed the check and then sued the tenant for the $1,000 difference. If the tenant's only defense is accord and satisfaction, is the tenant likely to prevail?","choices":{"A":"No, because the amount owed by the tenant to the window store was liquidated and undisputed.","B":"No, because the window store failed to notice the tenant's notation on the check.","C":"Yes, because by cashing the check the window store impliedly agreed to accept the $1,000 as full payment of its claim.","D":"Yes, because the window store failed to write a reservation-of-rights notation on the check before cashing it."},"answer":"A","explanation":"When a good-faith dispute exists as to the amount of a debt, the debtor can offer to settle that debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous notation stating that it constitutes payment in full. The creditor can then: cash the check – in which case the debt is satisfied or reject the check – in which case the debt is not satisfied. But if the debt is liquidated (ie, certain and undisputed in amount), it cannot be satisfied by a check for a lesser amount. This is true even if the creditor cashes that check. Here, the tenant offered to satisfy his debt to the window store by mailing a $1,000 check with a conspicuous notation stating that \"[t]his check is in full and final satisfaction of the $2,000 bill.\" But since this notation acknowledged that the tenant owed the store $2,000 (liquidated amount), his check for the lesser amount of $1,000 could not satisfy that debt. Therefore, the tenant is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) A check with a conspicuous notation is sufficient to satisfy a disputed debt— even if the creditor fails to notice that notation. (Choice C) Had there been a good faith dispute as to the amount of the debt, then the window store, by cashing the check, would have impliedly agreed to accept the $1,000 as full payment of its claim. But there is no implied agreement to settle when the debt is liquidated. (Choice D) A creditor cannot reserve its right to collect the full amount of a debt by writing a reservation-of-rights notation (eg, \"under protest\") on a check before cashing it. Therefore, the window store's failure to do so is irrelevant.\n\nEducational objective: If a debt is disputed in good faith, then the debtor can offer to satisfy the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. But if the debt is liquidated (ie, certain and undisputed in amount), then it cannot be satisfied by a check for a lesser amount—even if the creditor cashes the check.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a creditor who cashes a check marked \"payment in full\" may have formed an enforceable accord).","educationalObjective":"If a debt is disputed in good faith, then the debtor can offer to satisfy the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. But if the debt is liquidated (ie, certain and undisputed in amount), then it cannot be satisfied by a check for a lesser amount—even if the creditor cashes the check.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 281 cmt. d (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (explaining that a creditor who cashes a check marked \"payment in full\" may have formed an enforceable accord).","rule":"If a debt is disputed in good faith, then the debtor can offer to satisfy the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. But if the debt is liquidated (ie, certain and undisputed in amount), then it cannot be satisfied by a check for a lesser amount—even if the creditor cashes the check.","irac":{"issue":"If the tenant's only defense is accord and satisfaction, is the tenant likely to prevail?","rule":"If a debt is disputed in good faith, then the debtor can offer to satisfy the debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous \"payment-in-full\" notation. But if the debt is liquidated (ie, certain and undisputed in amount), then it cannot be satisfied by a check for a lesser amount—even if the creditor cashes the check.","analysis":"When a good-faith dispute exists as to the amount of a debt, the debtor can offer to settle that debt by giving the creditor a check with a conspicuous notation stating that it constitutes payment in full. The creditor can then: cash the check – in which case the debt is satisfied or reject the check – in which case the debt is not satisfied. But if the debt is liquidated (ie, certain and undisputed in amount), it cannot be satisfied by a check for a lesser amount. This is true even if the creditor cashes that check. Here, the tenant offered to satisfy his debt to the window store by mailing a $1,000 check with a conspicuous notation stating that \"[t]his check is in full and final satisfaction of the $2,000 bill.\" But since this notation acknowledged that the tenant owed the store $2,000 (liquidated amount), his check for the lesser amount of $1,000 could not satisfy that debt. Therefore, the tenant is unlikely to prevail. (Choice B) A check with a conspicuous notation is sufficient to satisfy a disputed debt— even if the creditor fails to notice that notation. (Choice C) Had there been a good faith dispute as to the amount of the debt, then the window store, by cashing the check, would have impliedly agreed to accept the $1,000 as full payment of its claim. But there is no implied agreement to settle when the debt is liquidated. (Choice D) A creditor cannot reserve its right to collect the full amount of a debt by writing a reservation-of-rights notation (eg, \"under protest\") on a check before cashing it. Therefore, the window store's failure to do so is irrelevant.","conclusion":"Answer A: No, because the amount owed by the tenant to the window store was liquidated and undisputed."},"questionZh":"一名租户向房东租用了一栋商业大楼并在其中经营业务。该建筑的大前窗被破坏者砸碎。在这种情况下，租户有义务实施并支付维修费用，因此立即与一家窗户商店签订了合同，以 2,000 美元的价格更换窗户。商店圆满完成窗户更换工作后，租户停止营业并腾出大楼。租客搬出大楼后，向窗户商店邮寄了一张 1,000 美元的支票，上面写着以下明显的字样：“这张支票完全且最终满足了您 2,000 美元的窗户更换账单。”橱窗商店没有注意到这个符号，就兑现了支票，然后起诉租户，要求赔偿 1,000 美元的差额。如果租客唯一的抗辩是同意和满意，那么租客可能会获胜吗？","choicesZh":{"A":"不会，因为租户欠橱窗店的金额已经清算且没有争议。","B":"不，因为橱窗商店没有注意到支票上租户的批注。","C":"是的，因为通过兑现支票，橱窗商店暗示同意接受 1,000 美元作为其索赔的全额付款。","D":"是的，因为橱窗商店在兑现支票之前未能在支票上写上保留权利的注释。"},"explanationZh":"当对债务金额存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张支票来解决该债务，并在支票上注明其已全额付款。然后债权人可以：兑现支票——在这种情况下，债务得到清偿；或者拒绝支票——在这种情况下，债务没有得到清偿。但如果债务被清算（即金额确定且无争议），则不能通过较小金额的支票来偿还。即使债权人兑现该支票也是如此。在这里，租户提出通过邮寄一张 1,000 美元的支票来偿还他对橱窗商店的债务，并附上明显的注释，注明“他的支票完全且最终满足了 2,000 美元的账单”。但由于该符号承认租户欠商店 2,000 美元（清算金额），因此他的 1,000 美元较少金额的支票无法偿还该债务。因此，租客获胜的可能性不大。 （选择 B）带有明显标记的支票足以清偿有争议的债务——即使债权人没有注意到该标记。 （选项 C）如果对于债务金额存在善意争议，那么橱窗商店通过兑现支票，就默示同意接受 1,000 美元作为其索赔的全额付款。但在债务清算时并没有默示的和解协议。 （选项D） 债权人不能通过在兑现支票之前在支票上写下权利保留注释（例如“抗议”）来保留收取全额债务的权利。因此，橱窗店不这样做是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：如果善意地对债务有争议，则债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有明显“全额付款”符号的支票来偿还债务。但如果债务被清算（即金额确定且无可争议），则即使债权人兑现支票，也无法通过金额较少的支票来偿还债务。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 281 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，兑现标有“全额付款”支票的债权人可能已形成可执行协议）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果善意地对债务有争议，则债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有显眼的“全额付款”符号的支票来提出清偿债务。但如果债务被清算（即金额确定且无可争议），则即使债权人兑现支票，也无法通过金额较少的支票来偿还债务。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果租客唯一的抗辩是同意和满意，那么租客可能会获胜吗？","rule":"如果善意地对债务有争议，则债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有显眼的“全额付款”符号的支票来提出清偿债务。但如果债务被清算（即金额确定且无可争议），则即使债权人兑现支票，也无法通过金额较少的支票来偿还债务。","analysis":"当对债务金额存在善意争议时，债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张支票来解决该债务，并在支票上注明其已全额付款。然后债权人可以：兑现支票——在这种情况下，债务得到清偿；或者拒绝支票——在这种情况下，债务没有得到清偿。但如果债务被清算（即金额确定且无争议），则不能通过较小金额的支票来偿还。即使债权人兑现该支票也是如此。在这里，租户提出通过邮寄一张 1,000 美元的支票来偿还他对橱窗商店的债务，并附上明显的注释，注明“他的支票完全且最终满足了 2,000 美元的账单”。但由于该符号承认租户欠商店 2,000 美元（清算金额），因此他的 1,000 美元较少金额的支票无法偿还该债务。因此，租客获胜的可能性不大。 （选择 B）带有明显标记的支票足以清偿有争议的债务——即使债权人没有注意到该标记。 （选项 C）如果对于债务金额存在善意争议，那么橱窗商店通过兑现支票，就默示同意接受 1,000 美元作为其索赔的全额付款。但在债务清算时并没有默示的和解协议。 （选项D） 债权人不能通过在兑现支票之前在支票上写下权利保留注释（例如“抗议”）来保留收取全额债务的权利。因此，橱窗店不这样做是无关紧要的。\n\n教育目标：如果善意地对债务有争议，则债务人可以通过向债权人提供一张带有明显“全额付款”符号的支票来偿还债务。但如果债务被清算（即金额确定且无可争议），则即使债权人兑现支票，也无法通过金额较少的支票来偿还债务。\n\n参考文献\n合同重述（第二次）§ 281 cmt。 d（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（解释说，兑现标有“全额付款”支票的债权人可能已形成可执行协议）。","conclusion":"答案 A：不会，因为租户欠橱窗店的金额已经清算且没有争议。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-37beec99f1abd332"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":269,"sourceQuestionPage":794,"sourceQuestionPages":[794],"sourceExplanationPages":[795,796],"sourceAnswerPages":[795,796],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-270","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Anticipatory Repudiation & Assurances","question":"A broker needed a certain rare coin to complete a set that he had contracted to assemble and sell to a collector. On February 1, the broker obtained such a coin from a hoarder in exchange for $1,000 and a written promise to redeliver to the hoarder \"no later than December 31 of this year\" a comparable specimen of the same kind of coin. On February 2, the broker consummated the sale of the complete set to the collector. On October 1, the market price of rare coins suddenly began a rapid, sustained rise. On October 15, the broker wrote the hoarder, \"In view of the surprising market, it seems unfair that I should have to replace your coin within the next few weeks.\" On October 17, the hoarder responded in writing that she was treating the broker's repudiation as final and canceled the contract. On November 15, the hoarder sued the broker for the market value of a comparable replacement-coin. If the broker moves to dismiss the hoarder's complaint, which of the following is the broker's best argument in support of the motion?","choices":{"A":"Even if the broker repudiated on October 15, the hoarder has no remedy because she failed to demand in writing that the broker retract his repudiation.","B":"Even if the broker repudiated on October 15, the hoarder's only action would be for specific performance because the coin is a unique chattel.","C":"The broker did not repudiate the contract on October 15 and may still perform no later than the contract deadline of December 31.","D":"Under the doctrine of impossibility, the broker is temporarily excused by the market conditions from timely performance of his coin-replacement obligation."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation is applicable when a contracting party indicates an unwillingness to perform a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. The repudiation must be clear and unequivocal, and it may be by words or actions. Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party may either treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the broker wrote the hoarder that \"it seems unfair that I should have to replace your coin within the next few weeks.\" This statement did not clearly and unequivocally demonstrate the broker's intention not to perform pursuant to their contract. As a result, the broker's best argument supporting dismissal of the hoarder's breach-of-contract claim is that he did not repudiate the contract and may still perform no later than the contract deadline.* *The broker's statement presents reasonable grounds for insecurity about his willingness to perform, so the hoarder has the right to make a written demand for assurances. The broker's failure to provide such assurances within 30 days would result in an anticipatory repudiation. (Choice A) A repudiation may be retracted unless the nonrepudiating party has (1) canceled the contract, (2) materially changed position, or (3) indicated that he/she considers the repudiation final. But there is no requirement that the nonrepudiating party demand in writing that the repudiating party issue a retraction. (Choice B) Specific performance is an available remedy when monetary damages would be inadequate because the property or chattel at issue is unique. But monetary damages would be adequate here since the parties contracted for a comparable specimen of the same kind of coin—not the exact same coin. (Choice D) The doctrine of impossibility discharges a party's contractual duties when an unexpected and extraordinary event beyond the parties' control makes performance impossible. However, a change in market conditions is neither unexpected nor extraordinary. And the broker is able to perform since he can still purchase the coin, albeit at a higher price.\n\nEducational objective: The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.\n\nReferences\nU.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","educationalObjective":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.","references":"U.C.C. § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (anticipatory repudiation).","rule":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.","irac":{"issue":"If the broker moves to dismiss the hoarder's complaint, which of the following is the broker's best argument in support of the motion?","rule":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation applies when a party clearly and unequivocally repudiates (ie, indicates an unwillingness to perform) a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. The nonrepudiating party may treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract.","analysis":"The doctrine of anticipatory repudiation is applicable when a contracting party indicates an unwillingness to perform a promise before the time for performance arises or elapses. The repudiation must be clear and unequivocal, and it may be by words or actions. Upon repudiation, the nonrepudiating party may either treat the repudiation as a breach or ignore it and demand performance pursuant to the contract. Here, the broker wrote the hoarder that \"it seems unfair that I should have to replace your coin within the next few weeks.\" This statement did not clearly and unequivocally demonstrate the broker's intention not to perform pursuant to their contract. As a result, the broker's best argument supporting dismissal of the hoarder's breach-of-contract claim is that he did not repudiate the contract and may still perform no later than the contract deadline.* *The broker's statement presents reasonable grounds for insecurity about his willingness to perform, so the hoarder has the right to make a written demand for assurances. The broker's failure to provide such assurances within 30 days would result in an anticipatory repudiation. (Choice A) A repudiation may be retracted unless the nonrepudiating party has (1) canceled the contract, (2) materially changed position, or (3) indicated that he/she considers the repudiation final. But there is no requirement that the nonrepudiating party demand in writing that the repudiating party issue a retraction. (Choice B) Specific performance is an available remedy when monetary damages would be inadequate because the property or chattel at issue is unique. But monetary damages would be adequate here since the parties contracted for a comparable specimen of the same kind of coin—not the exact same coin. (Choice D) The doctrine of impossibility discharges a party's contractual duties when an unexpected and extraordinary event beyond the parties' control makes performance impossible. However, a change in market conditions is neither unexpected nor extraordinary. And the broker is able to perform since he can still purchase the coin, albeit at a higher price.","conclusion":"Answer C: The broker did not repudiate the contract on October 15 and may still perform no later than the contract deadline of December 31."},"questionZh":"一位经纪人需要某种稀有的硬币来完成一套他已签约组装并出售给收藏家的硬币。 2 月 1 日，经纪人以 1,000 美元的价格从一名囤积者处获得了一枚这样的硬币，并书面承诺“不迟于今年 12 月 31 日”向囤积者重新交付一枚同类硬币的类似样本。 2月2日，经纪人完成向收藏家出售整套作品。 10月1日，稀有钱币的市场价格突然开始快速持续上涨。 10 月 15 日，经纪人写信给囤积者，“鉴于令人惊讶的市场，我必须在未来几周内更换你的硬币，这似乎不公平。” 10月17日，囤积者书面回应称，她将经纪人的拒绝视为最终决定，并取消了合同。 11 月 15 日，囤积者就类似替代硬币的市场价值起诉经纪人。如果经纪人提出驳回囤积者投诉的动​​议，以下哪项是经纪人支持该动议的最佳论据？","choicesZh":{"A":"即使经纪人在10月15日拒付，囤积者也没有补救措施，因为她没有书面要求经纪人撤回拒付。","B":"即使经纪人在 10 月 15 日拒绝，囤积者唯一的行动也将是为了特定的表现，因为硬币是一种独特的动产。","C":"经纪人在10月15日并未否认合同，并且仍可在合同截止日期12月31日之前履行合同。","D":"根据不可能原则，经纪人因市场状况而暂时无法及时履行其代币更换义务。"},"explanationZh":"当缔约方在履行时间出现或过去之前表明不愿意履行承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。否认必须明确、毫不含糊，可以通过言语或行动进行。预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行后，非毁约方可以将拒绝视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求根据合同履行。经纪人写信给囤积者说：“我必须在接下来的几周内更换你的硬币，这似乎不公平。”该声明并未清楚明确地表明经纪人不履行合同的意图。因此，经纪人支持驳回囤积者违约索赔的最佳论据是，他没有否认合同，并且仍可以在合同截止日期之前履行合同。* *经纪人的声明对他的履约意愿提供了合理的理由，因此囤积者有权提出书面保证要求。经纪人未能在 30 天内提供此类保证将导致预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。 （选择 A）除非非毁约方 (1) 取消合同，(2) 实质性改变立场，或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的，否则否认可以撤销。但并不要求非毁约方以书面形式要求否认方撤回。 （选择 B）当由于所涉财产或动产是独特的而金钱赔偿不足以弥补时，特定履约是一种可用的补救措施。但这里金钱赔偿就足够了，因为双方签订了同类硬币的可比较样本的合同，而不是完全相同的硬币。 （选项D） 当超出双方控制范围的意外和非常事件导致合同履行不可能时，不可能原则就解除了合同方的合同义务。然而，市场状况的变化既不是意外的，也不是非同寻常的。经纪人能够履行职责，因为他仍然可以购买代币，尽管价格更高。\n\n教育目标：当一方在履行时间出现或过去之前明确且毫不含糊地拒绝（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","educationalObjectiveZh":"当一方在履行时间出现或过去之前明确且明确地拒绝（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果经纪人提出驳回囤积者投诉的动议，以下哪项是经纪人支持该动议的最佳论据？","rule":"当一方在履行时间出现或过去之前明确且明确地拒绝（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。","analysis":"当缔约方在履行时间出现或过去之前表明不愿意履行承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。否认必须明确、毫不含糊，可以通过言语或行动进行。预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）履行后，非毁约方可以将拒绝视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求根据合同履行。经纪人写信给囤积者说：“我必须在接下来的几周内更换你的硬币，这似乎不公平。”该声明并未清楚明确地表明经纪人不履行合同的意图。因此，经纪人支持驳回囤积者违约索赔的最佳论据是，他没有否认合同，并且仍可以在合同截止日期之前履行合同。* *经纪人的声明对他的履约意愿提供了合理的理由，因此囤积者有权提出书面保证要求。经纪人未能在 30 天内提供此类保证将导致预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）。 （选择 A）除非非毁约方 (1) 取消合同，(2) 实质性改变立场，或 (3) 表明他/她认为否认是最终的，否则否认可以撤销。但并不要求非毁约方以书面形式要求否认方撤回。 （选择 B）当由于所涉财产或动产是独特的而金钱赔偿不足以弥补时，特定履约是一种可用的补救措施。但这里金钱赔偿就足够了，因为双方签订了同类硬币的可比较样本的合同，而不是完全相同的硬币。 （选项D） 当超出双方控制范围的意外和非常事件导致合同履行不可能时，不可能原则就解除了合同方的合同义务。然而，市场状况的变化既不是意外的，也不是非同寻常的。经纪人能够履行职责，因为他仍然可以购买代币，尽管价格更高。\n\n教育目标：当一方在履行时间出现或过去之前明确且毫不含糊地拒绝（即表明不愿意履行）承诺时，适用预期违约（anticipatory repudiation）原则。非毁约方可以将否认视为违约，也可以忽略它并要求按照合同履行。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-610 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（预期违约（anticipatory repudiation））。","conclusion":"答案 C：经纪人在10月15日并未否认合同，并且仍可在合同截止日期12月31日之前履行合同。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":["contracts-chart-6092dc1e72c5f076"],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":270,"sourceQuestionPage":797,"sourceQuestionPages":[797],"sourceExplanationPages":[798,799],"sourceAnswerPages":[798,799],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-271","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"On March 1, a mechanic contracted to repair a textile company's knitting machine by March 6. On March 2, the textile company contracted to manufacture and deliver specified cloth to a customer on March 15. The textile company knew that it would have to use the machine then under repair to perform this contract. Because the customer's order was for a rush job, the customer and the textile company included in their contract a liquidated damages clause, providing that the textile company would pay the customer $5,000 for each day's delay in delivery after March 15. The mechanic was inexcusably five days late in repairing the machine, and, as a result, the textile company was five days late in delivering the cloth to the customer. The textile company paid $25,000 to the customer as liquidated damages and then sued the mechanic for $25,000. Both the mechanic and the textile company knew when making their contract on March 1 that, under ordinary circumstances, the textile company would sustain few or no damages of any kind as a result of a five-day delay in the machine repair. Assuming that the $5,000-per-day liquidated damages clause in the contract between the textile company and the customer is valid, which of the following arguments will serve as the mechanic's best defense to the textile company's action?","choices":{"A":"By entering into the contract with the customer while knowing that its knitting machine was being repaired, the textile company assumed the risk of any delay loss to the customer.","B":"In all probability, the liquidated damages paid by the textile company to the customer are not the same amount as the actual damages sustained by the customer in consequence of the late delivery of the cloth.","C":"The mechanic had no reason to foresee on March 1 that the textile company would suffer consequential damages in the amount of $25,000.","D":"Time was not of the essence in the contract between the mechanic and the textile company."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach The primary goal of contract damages is to put the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. This includes expectation damages for the value of the breaching party's performance that was lost. However, it includes consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances only if the breaching party: knew about the nonbreaching party's special circumstances or could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the nonbreaching party's special circumstances, that would result from a breach. Here, the mechanic was inexcusably five days late in repairing the knitting machine. Under normal circumstances, the textile company would have sustained few or no damages as a result of the breach. However, the textile company did not inform the mechanic of its contract with the customer. Therefore, the fact that the mechanic had no reason to foresee that the textile company would suffer $25,000 in consequential damages ($5,000 for each day's delay) is the mechanic's best defense. (Choice A) The facts do not indicate that the textile company assumed the risk of any delay loss to the customer. The textile company had contracted with the mechanic for repairs to be completed within a time frame that would have allowed it to timely fulfill its contractual obligations to the customer. (Choice B) The facts state that the liquidated damages clause is valid; they do not provide information concerning the actual damages sustained by the customer. (Choice D) Although the contract did not state that time was of the essence, the mechanic agreed to complete the repairs by a specified time. His failure to do so constituted a breach entitling the textile company to damages. Nevertheless, the textile company cannot recover the $25,000 because those damages were unforeseeable.\n\nEducational objective: An award of consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances is appropriate only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 351 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unforeseeability and related limitations on damages).","educationalObjective":"An award of consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances is appropriate only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 351 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (unforeseeability and related limitations on damages).","rule":"An award of consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances is appropriate only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","irac":{"issue":"Assuming that the $5,000-per-day liquidated damages clause in the contract between the textile company and the customer is valid, which of the following arguments will serve as the mechanic's best defense to the textile company's action?","rule":"An award of consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances is appropriate only if the breaching party (1) knew about the special\n\ncircumstances or (2) could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the special circumstances, that would result from a breach.","analysis":"Consequential damages\n\nDamages for losses stemming from nonbreaching party's special circumstances if breaching party:\n\nknew of those special circumstances OR\n\ncould have reasonably foreseen harm caused by breach The primary goal of contract damages is to put the nonbreaching party in the same position as if the contract had been performed. This includes expectation damages for the value of the breaching party's performance that was lost. However, it includes consequential damages for losses arising from the nonbreaching party's special circumstances only if the breaching party: knew about the nonbreaching party's special circumstances or could have reasonably foreseen the harm, in light of the nonbreaching party's special circumstances, that would result from a breach. Here, the mechanic was inexcusably five days late in repairing the knitting machine. Under normal circumstances, the textile company would have sustained few or no damages as a result of the breach. However, the textile company did not inform the mechanic of its contract with the customer. Therefore, the fact that the mechanic had no reason to foresee that the textile company would suffer $25,000 in consequential damages ($5,000 for each day's delay) is the mechanic's best defense. (Choice A) The facts do not indicate that the textile company assumed the risk of any delay loss to the customer. The textile company had contracted with the mechanic for repairs to be completed within a time frame that would have allowed it to timely fulfill its contractual obligations to the customer. (Choice B) The facts state that the liquidated damages clause is valid; they do not provide information concerning the actual damages sustained by the customer. (Choice D) Although the contract did not state that time was of the essence, the mechanic agreed to complete the repairs by a specified time. His failure to do so constituted a breach entitling the textile company to damages. Nevertheless, the textile company cannot recover the $25,000 because those damages were unforeseeable.","conclusion":"Answer C: The mechanic had no reason to foresee on March 1 that the textile company would suffer consequential damages in the amount of $25,000."},"questionZh":"3月1日，一名机械师签订合同，在3月6日之前修复一家纺织公司的针织机。3月2日，该纺织公司签订合同，于3月15日生产并交付指定的布料给客户。纺织公司知道，必须使用当时正在维修的机器来履行该合同。由于客户的订单是加急订单，客户和纺织公司在合同中约定了违约金条款，规定纺织公司在3月15日之后，每延迟一天交货，就要向客户支付5000美元。机修工不可原谅地迟到了五天才修好机器，结果纺织公司迟到了五天才将布料交付给客户。纺织公司向客户支付了 25,000 美元作为违约金，然后起诉机械师，要求赔偿 25,000 美元。机械师和纺织公司在3月1日签订合同时都知道，在正常情况下，纺织公司不会因为机器维修延误五天而遭受很少或没有任何损害。假设纺织公司与客户之间的合同中每天 5,000 美元的违约金条款有效，那么以下哪项论据将作为机械师对纺织公司诉讼的最佳辩护？","choicesZh":{"A":"纺织公司在明知客户的针织机正在维修的情况下与客户签订合同，承担了客户延迟损失的风险。","B":"纺织公司向客户支付的违约金很可能与客户因布料延迟交货而遭受的实际损失并不相同。","C":"3 月 1 日，机械师没有理由预见到纺织公司将遭受 25,000 美元的间接损失。","D":"在机械师和纺织公司之间的合同中，时间并不是最重要的。"},"explanationZh":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 合同损害赔偿的主要目的是使非违约方处于与合同已履行相同的境地。这包括对违约方的履约损失价值的预期损害赔偿。然而，只有在违约方知道守约方的特殊情况或能够根据守约方的特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害的情况下，它才包括因守约方的特殊情况而造成的损失的后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。在这里，机械师修理针织机的时间迟到了五天，这是不可原谅的。在正常情况下，纺织公司因违规行为而遭受的损失很少或根本没有。然而，纺织公司没有将其与客户的合同告知机械师。因此，机械师没有理由预见纺织公司将遭受 25,000 美元的间接损失（每天延误 5,000 美元），这一事实是机械师最好的辩护。 （选项A） 事实并不表明纺织公司承担了客户延迟损失的风险。该纺织公司已与机械师签订合同，要求在规定的时间内完成维修，以便其能够及时履行对客户的合同义务。 （选项B）事实证明违约金条款有效；他们不提供有关客户遭受的实际损失的信息。 （选项D） 尽管合同中没有规定时间至关重要，但机械师同意在规定的时间内完成修理。他没有这样做就构成了违约行为，纺织公司有权获得损失赔偿。然而，纺织公司无法收回 25,000 美元，因为这些损失是不可预见的。\n\n教育目标：只有在违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况的情况下，对非违约方的特殊情况造成的损失判以后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）才是适当的。\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 351（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不可预见性和相关的损害限制）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"只有在违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况的情况下，才可以对因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失判以后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）金。\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。","iracZh":{"issue":"假设纺织公司与客户之间的合同中每天 5,000 美元的违约金条款有效，那么以下哪项论据将作为机械师对纺织公司诉讼的最佳辩护？","rule":"只有在违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况的情况下，才可以对因非违约方的特殊情况而造成的损失判以后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）金。\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。","analysis":"间接损失\n\n因守约方特殊情况造成损失的损害赔偿，如果违约方：\n\n知道这些特殊情况或\n\n能够合理预见违约造成的损害 合同损害赔偿的主要目的是使非违约方处于与合同已履行相同的境地。这包括对违约方的履约损失价值的预期损害赔偿。然而，只有在违约方知道守约方的特殊情况或能够根据守约方的特殊情况合理预见违约可能造成的损害的情况下，它才包括因守约方的特殊情况而造成的损失的后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）。在这里，机械师修理针织机的时间迟到了五天，这是不可原谅的。在正常情况下，纺织公司因违规行为而遭受的损失很少或根本没有。然而，纺织公司没有将其与客户的合同告知机械师。因此，机械师没有理由预见纺织公司将遭受 25,000 美元的间接损失（每天延误 5,000 美元），这一事实是机械师最好的辩护。 （选项A） 事实并不表明纺织公司承担了客户延迟损失的风险。该纺织公司已与机械师签订合同，要求在规定的时间内完成维修，以便其能够及时履行对客户的合同义务。 （选项B）事实证明违约金条款有效；他们不提供有关客户遭受的实际损失的信息。 （选项D） 尽管合同中没有规定时间至关重要，但机械师同意在规定的时间内完成修理。他没有这样做就构成了违约行为，纺织公司有权获得损失赔偿。然而，纺织公司无法收回 25,000 美元，因为这些损失是不可预见的。\n\n教育目标：只有在违约方 (1) 了解特殊情况的情况下，对非违约方的特殊情况造成的损失判以后果性损害赔偿（consequential damages）才是适当的。\n\n(2) 根据特殊情况可以合理预见违约行为可能造成的损害。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 351（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（不可预见性和相关的损害限制）。","conclusion":"答案 C：3 月 1 日，机械师没有理由预见到纺织公司将遭受 25,000 美元的间接损失。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":271,"sourceQuestionPage":800,"sourceQuestionPages":[800],"sourceExplanationPages":[801,802],"sourceAnswerPages":[801,802],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-272","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Contracts","question":"An architect agreed with a developer to design a large residential development. Because the architect had a history of substance abuse problems, the parties agreed that the developer's duty to accept and pay for the plans would be conditioned on the architect's abstaining from alcohol during the six months it would take to do the work. After two months, the architect began having several alcoholic drinks each day. The developer became aware of the architect's drinking before the architect showed the developer the preliminary plans. When the developer saw the preliminary plans, the developer told the architect that the concept was impressive and that he looked forward to seeing the final plans. The architect continued to have several alcoholic drinks each day and completed the plans within the specified contract period. However, the developer declined to review, accept, or pay for the final plans and stated that it was because of the architect's continued use of alcohol. The architect has sued the developer for breach of contract. Which of the following arguments best supports the architect's claim?","choices":{"A":"The alcohol use was not a material breach of contract by the architect, since it did not affect the quality of the work.","B":"The developer waived the condition of no alcohol use.","C":"The no-alcohol term was functionally a penalty clause and therefore was unenforceable.","D":"The no-alcohol term would be interpreted as a promise and not a condition."},"answer":"B","explanation":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nPerformance is generally due once a contract is formed but can be made contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent. In that case, performance will not become due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. One way for nonoccurrence to be excused is for the party who benefits from the condition to waive the condition—ie, indicate by words or conduct that the condition need no longer occur. Here, the developer's duty to accept and pay for the plans was conditioned on the architect abstaining from alcohol while the plans were designed (condition precedent). Although the architect did not abstain from alcohol (nonoccurrence) and the developer was aware of this, the developer approved the preliminary plans (waiver). As a result, the condition's nonoccurrence was excused and the developer's performance became due. Therefore, waiver is the argument that best supports the architect's claim of breach of contract. (Choice A) Express conditions such as the no-alcohol-use condition require strict compliance, so the fact that the architect's alcohol use did not affect the quality of the work is irrelevant. (Choice C) A penalty is a liquidated damages clause so unreasonably large that it punishes a party for breach (as opposed to merely compensating the other party). But the no-alcohol term in this case is not a damages clause and is not meant to compensate the developer for breach. (Choice D) Ambiguity about whether an event creates a promise or a condition is generally resolved in favor of a promise unless the event was within the obligee's control or he/she assumed its risk. There was no ambiguity here, however, because the developer's duty to pay was expressly conditioned on the architect's abstaining from alcohol.\n\nEducational objective: If the party who benefits from the condition waives the nonoccurrence of the condition—ie, indicates by words or conduct that the condition need no longer occur—then that party's performance will become due.\n\nReferences\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 224 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of condition).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of nonoccurrence of condition).","educationalObjective":"If the party who benefits from the condition waives the nonoccurrence of the condition—ie, indicates by words or conduct that the condition need no longer occur—then that party's performance will become due.","references":"Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 224 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (definition of condition).\n\nRestatement (Second) of Contracts § 225 (Am. Law Inst. 1981) (effect of nonoccurrence of condition).","rule":"If the party who benefits from the condition waives the nonoccurrence of the condition—ie, indicates by words or conduct that the condition need no longer occur—then that party's performance will become due.","irac":{"issue":"Which of the following arguments best supports the architect's claim?","rule":"If the party who benefits from the condition waives the nonoccurrence of the condition—ie, indicates by words or conduct that the condition need no longer occur—then that party's performance will become due.","analysis":"When nonoccurrence of condition is excused\n\nWaiver Party waives condition by words or conduct\n\nWrongful interference Party wrongfully prevents or interferes with condition's occurrence Estoppel Party indicates condition will not be enforced AND\n\nOther party reasonably & detrimentally relies on belief that condition has been waived Disproportionate Party substantially performed & will be significantly harmed if forfeiture condition is enforced\n\nPerformance is generally due once a contract is formed but can be made contingent upon the occurrence of a condition precedent. In that case, performance will not become due until the condition occurs or its nonoccurrence is excused. One way for nonoccurrence to be excused is for the party who benefits from the condition to waive the condition—ie, indicate by words or conduct that the condition need no longer occur. Here, the developer's duty to accept and pay for the plans was conditioned on the architect abstaining from alcohol while the plans were designed (condition precedent). Although the architect did not abstain from alcohol (nonoccurrence) and the developer was aware of this, the developer approved the preliminary plans (waiver). As a result, the condition's nonoccurrence was excused and the developer's performance became due. Therefore, waiver is the argument that best supports the architect's claim of breach of contract. (Choice A) Express conditions such as the no-alcohol-use condition require strict compliance, so the fact that the architect's alcohol use did not affect the quality of the work is irrelevant. (Choice C) A penalty is a liquidated damages clause so unreasonably large that it punishes a party for breach (as opposed to merely compensating the other party). But the no-alcohol term in this case is not a damages clause and is not meant to compensate the developer for breach. (Choice D) Ambiguity about whether an event creates a promise or a condition is generally resolved in favor of a promise unless the event was within the obligee's control or he/she assumed its risk. There was no ambiguity here, however, because the developer's duty to pay was expressly conditioned on the architect's abstaining from alcohol.","conclusion":"Answer B: The developer waived the condition of no alcohol use."},"questionZh":"一位建筑师与开发商同意设计一个大型住宅开发项目。由于建筑师有药物滥用问题的历史，双方同意开发商有义务接受并支付计划费用，条件是建筑师在完成这项工作的六个月内戒酒。两个月后，建筑师开始每天喝几杯酒精饮料。在建筑师向开发商展示初步计划之前，开发商就意识到建筑师喝酒了。当开发商看到初步计划时，开发商告诉建筑师这个概念令人印象深刻，他期待看到最终的计划。建筑师继续每天喝几杯酒，并在规定的合同期限内完成了计划。然而，开发商拒绝审查、接受或支付最终计划，并表示这是因为建筑师继续酗酒。建筑师已起诉开发商违反合同。以下哪一个论点最能支持建筑师的主张？","choicesZh":{"A":"使用酒精并不构成建筑师的重大违约，因为它不会影响工作质量。","B":"发展商豁免不饮酒的条件。","C":"禁酒条款在功能上是一项惩罚条款，因此无法执行。","D":"无酒精一词将被解释为承诺而不是条件。"},"explanationZh":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n合同通常在合同成立后就应履行，但也可以根据先决条件的发生而履行。在这种情况下，只有在该情况发生或有理由不发生该情况时，履约才会到期。免除不发生的一种方式是从该条件中受益的一方放弃该条件，即通过言语或行为表明该条件不再需要发生。在这里，开发商接受和支付计划的义务是以建筑师在计划设计期间戒酒为条件的（先决条件）。尽管建筑师没有戒酒（非发生）并且开发商也意识到这一点，但开发商还是批准了初步计划（弃权）。结果，该情况的未发生被原谅，开发商的履约也到期了。因此，弃权是最能支持建筑师违约主张的论据。 （选项A）明示的条件，例如禁止饮酒的条件，需要严格遵守，因此建筑师饮酒没有影响工作质量这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项 C） 罚金是一种违约赔偿金条款，其金额过大，以至于对违约一方进行惩罚（而不是仅仅补偿另一方）。但本案中的禁酒条款并不是损害赔偿条款，也不意味着对开发商的违约行为进行赔偿。 （选项D）关于事件是否产生承诺或条件的模糊性通常会以有利于承诺的方式解决，除非该事件在债权人（obligee）的控制范围内或他/她承担了风险。然而，这里并没有含糊之处，因为开发商的付款义务明确以建筑师戒酒为条件。\n\n教育目标：如果从该状况中受益的一方放弃该状况的不发生，即通过言语或行为表明该状况不再需要发生，那么该方的表现将成为应有的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 224（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件的定义）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件未发生的影响）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果从该条件中受益的一方放弃该条件的不发生，即通过言语或行为表明该条件不再需要发生，那么该方的履行将到期。","iracZh":{"issue":"以下哪一个论点最能支持建筑师的主张？","rule":"如果从该条件中受益的一方放弃该条件的不发生，即通过言语或行为表明该条件不再需要发生，那么该方的履行将到期。","analysis":"当没有发生任何情况时\n\n弃权方通过言语或行为放弃条件\n\n不当干涉 一方错误地阻止或干扰条件的发生 禁止反言 一方表示条件不会被执行并且\n\n另一方合理且有害地相信条件已被放弃 不成比例的一方实质上履行了义务，如果执行没收条件，将会受到重大损害\n\n合同通常在合同成立后就应履行，但也可以根据先决条件的发生而履行。在这种情况下，只有在该情况发生或有理由不发生该情况时，履约才会到期。免除不发生的一种方式是从该条件中受益的一方放弃该条件，即通过言语或行为表明该条件不再需要发生。在这里，开发商接受和支付计划的义务是以建筑师在计划设计期间戒酒为条件的（先决条件）。尽管建筑师没有戒酒（非发生）并且开发商也意识到这一点，但开发商还是批准了初步计划（弃权）。结果，该情况的未发生被原谅，开发商的履约也到期了。因此，弃权是最能支持建筑师违约主张的论据。 （选项A）明示的条件，例如禁止饮酒的条件，需要严格遵守，因此建筑师饮酒没有影响工作质量这一事实是无关紧要的。 （选项 C） 罚金是一种违约赔偿金条款，其金额过大，以至于对违约一方进行惩罚（而不是仅仅补偿另一方）。但本案中的禁酒条款并不是损害赔偿条款，也不意味着对开发商的违约行为进行赔偿。 （选项D）关于事件是否产生承诺或条件的模糊性通常会以有利于承诺的方式解决，除非该事件在债权人（obligee）的控制范围内或他/她承担了风险。然而，这里并没有含糊之处，因为开发商的付款义务明确以建筑师戒酒为条件。\n\n教育目标：如果从该状况中受益的一方放弃该状况的不发生，即通过言语或行为表明该状况不再需要发生，那么该方的表现将成为应有的。\n\n参考文献\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 224（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件的定义）。\n\n《合同法重述（第二版）》§ 225（Am. Law Inst. 1981）（条件未发生的影响）。","conclusion":"答案 B：发展商豁免不饮酒的条件。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"verified-current","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已依据 NCBE Contracts 考试范围及现行 UCC Articles 1、2 作规则层面复核；未发现需要改变本题答案的后续规则。2003 年 Article 2 修订已撤回，并非 MBE 所假定的现行官方文本。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[{"term":"penalty clause","ipa":"/pˈɛnəlti klˈɔz/","syllables":"penalty clause","stress":"主重音见 ˈ；次重音见 ˌ","commonMeaning":"惩罚性条款：数额不合理、旨在惩罚而非补偿，通常不可执行","contextualMeaning":"惩罚性条款：数额不合理、旨在惩罚而非补偿，通常不可执行","wordForm":"法律词组 / phrase","sourceSentence":"The no-alcohol term was functionally a penalty clause and therefore was unenforceable.","synonyms":["—（无严格同义词）"]}],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":272,"sourceQuestionPage":803,"sourceQuestionPages":[803],"sourceExplanationPages":[804,805],"sourceAnswerPages":[804,805],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"pdf-contracts-273","subject":"Contracts","topic":"Remedies & Damages","question":"A collector agreed to sell his collection of authentic extras' costumes from a cult-classic 80's show to a costume store for $10,000, payable one month after the collection was delivered to the store via a third-party carrier. Due to the time and expense that went into accumulating and repairing the costumes, the collector expected a $2,000 profit. The costumes suffered minor water damage in transit, and the store immediately notified the collector that it was rejecting the collection and would hold the collection until the collector picked it up. The collector told the store that he would look for a new buyer and would pick up the collection in a few weeks. The collector quickly found another buyer willing to pay the original contract price. However, before the collector could retrieve the collection, the store sold and delivered the costumes to a theater company that knowingly accepted the costumes despite the water damage. The theater company paid the store $15,000 for the collection, and the store retained the payment. If the store's sale of the costumes was NOT an acceptance, what is the highest-value remedy available to the collector?","choices":{"A":"$2,000, the collector's lost profit.","B":"$5,000, the difference between the market price and the contract price.","C":"$10,000, the collector-store contract price.","D":"$15,000, damages for conversion."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The UCC governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, costumes). Under the UCC perfect- tender rule, goods must conform perfectly to the contract. Failure to provide such goods is a breach that, unless cured in the time remaining to perform, allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. Upon rejection, the buyer must retain possession of the goods for a reasonable time so that the seller can reclaim them. If the buyer wrongfully disposes of the rejected goods, the seller may sue the buyer for conversion to recover the fair market value of the converted property at the time of the conversion. Here, the store rightfully rejected the collection of minorly damaged costumes because they did not perfectly conform to the agreement. However, the store wrongfully sold and delivered the collection to a theater company before the collector could pick it up. As a result, the collector can sue the store for conversion and recover the collection's fair market value of $15,000. (Choice A) The collector could have recovered his lost profit ($2,000) had he been a lost- volume seller—ie, a seller who has an unlimited supply of goods and makes a profit per item. But here, the collector is merely selling his limited supply of authentic extras' costumes. (Choice B) When a buyer wrongfully rejects tendered goods, the seller may ordinarily recover the contract price minus the market price at the time and place of tender (here, $5,000). But here, the store rightfully rejected the goods because they were nonconforming. (Choice C) When a buyer has accepted the goods and fails to pay for them, the seller may sue for the contract price (here, $10,000). But since the store rejected and then converted the costumes, the collector is instead entitled to the fair market price ($15,000).\n\nEducational objective: If a buyer wrongfully disposes of rejected goods, the seller may sue the buyer for conversion to recover the fair market value of the converted property at the time of the conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.\n\nReferences\nUCC § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (perfect-tender rule).\n\nUCC § 2-708(2) (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (lost profits for volume sellers).","educationalObjective":"If a buyer wrongfully disposes of rejected goods, the seller may sue the buyer for conversion to recover the fair market value of the converted property at the time of the conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","references":"UCC § 2-601 (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (perfect-tender rule).\n\nUCC § 2-708(2) (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020) (lost profits for volume sellers).","rule":"If a buyer wrongfully disposes of rejected goods, the seller may sue the buyer for conversion to recover the fair market value of the converted property at the time of the conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","irac":{"issue":"If the store's sale of the costumes was NOT an acceptance, what is the highest-value remedy available to the collector?","rule":"If a buyer wrongfully disposes of rejected goods, the seller may sue the buyer for conversion to recover the fair market value of the converted property at the time of the conversion. UCC = Uniform Commercial Code.","analysis":"The UCC governs contracts for the sale of goods (eg, costumes). Under the UCC perfect- tender rule, goods must conform perfectly to the contract. Failure to provide such goods is a breach that, unless cured in the time remaining to perform, allows the buyer to reject or accept the goods in whole or in part. Upon rejection, the buyer must retain possession of the goods for a reasonable time so that the seller can reclaim them. If the buyer wrongfully disposes of the rejected goods, the seller may sue the buyer for conversion to recover the fair market value of the converted property at the time of the conversion. Here, the store rightfully rejected the collection of minorly damaged costumes because they did not perfectly conform to the agreement. However, the store wrongfully sold and delivered the collection to a theater company before the collector could pick it up. As a result, the collector can sue the store for conversion and recover the collection's fair market value of $15,000. (Choice A) The collector could have recovered his lost profit ($2,000) had he been a lost- volume seller—ie, a seller who has an unlimited supply of goods and makes a profit per item. But here, the collector is merely selling his limited supply of authentic extras' costumes. (Choice B) When a buyer wrongfully rejects tendered goods, the seller may ordinarily recover the contract price minus the market price at the time and place of tender (here, $5,000). But here, the store rightfully rejected the goods because they were nonconforming. (Choice C) When a buyer has accepted the goods and fails to pay for them, the seller may sue for the contract price (here, $10,000). But since the store rejected and then converted the costumes, the collector is instead entitled to the fair market price ($15,000).","conclusion":"Answer D: $15,000, damages for conversion."},"questionZh":"一位收藏家同意以 10,000 美元的价格将他收集的 80 年代经典演出中的正品群众演员服装出售给一家服装店，并在该系列通过第三方承运人运送到商店后一个月付款。由于收集和修理服装需要时间和费用，收藏家预计利润为 2,000 美元。服装在运输途中遭受了轻微的水渍，商店立即通知收藏家拒收藏品，并将保留藏品直至收藏家取走。收藏家告诉商店，他将寻找新买家，并将在几周内提走藏品。收藏家很快找到了另一位愿意支付原合同价的买家。然而，在收藏家取回藏品之前，商店将服装出售并交付给一家剧院公司，该剧院公司在知情的情况下接受了这些服装，尽管有水损坏。剧院公司向商店支付了 15,000 美元的收藏费用，商店保留了这笔款项。如果商店出售的服装不被接受，那么对收藏家来说最有价值的补救措施是什么？","choicesZh":{"A":"2,000美元，收藏家的利润损失。","B":"5000美元，市场价与合同价之间的差额。","C":"10,000 美元，收藏家商店合同价。","D":"15,000 美元，侵占（conversion）损害赔偿。"},"explanationZh":"【现行法校正——答案不变】原资料把“完美履行规则”标注为 UCC § 2-503；该规则的直接条文是 UCC § 2-601。题干、答案与实体解析不受影响。\n\nUCC 管辖商品（例如服装）销售合同。根据 UCC 完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），货物必须完全符合合同。未能提供此类货物属于违约行为，除非在剩余履行时间内予以纠正，否则买方可以拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。拒绝后，买方必须在合理的时间内保留对货物的占有，以便卖方可以收回货物。如果买方错误地处置被拒绝的货物，卖方可以起诉买方主张侵占（conversion），以追回侵占发生时财产的公平市场价值。在这里，商店理所当然地拒绝了轻微损坏的服装的收藏，因为它们不完全符合协议。然而，该商店在收藏家提货之前错误地将藏品出售并交付给一家剧院公司。因此，收藏家可以起诉商店主张侵占（conversion），并收回藏品的公平市场价值 15,000 美元。 （选项 A）如果收藏家是一个损失销量卖方，即拥有无限量的商品供应并从每件商品中赚取利润的卖家，他本可以收回损失的利润（2,000 美元）。但在这里，收藏家只是出售他有限的正品群众演员服装。 （选择B）当买方错误地拒绝交付货物时，卖方通常可以根据合同价格减去投标时间和地点的市场价格（此处为5,000美元）来获得赔偿。但在这里，商店理所当然地拒绝了这些货物，因为它们不合格。 （选项C）当买方接受货物但未付款时，卖方可以就合同价格（此处为10,000美元）提起诉讼。但由于商店拒绝并改装了这些服装，收藏家反而有权获得公平的市场价格（15,000 美元）。\n\n教育目的：如果买方错误地处置被拒绝的货物，卖方可以起诉买方主张侵占（conversion），以追回侵占发生时财产的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-503（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule））。\n\nUCC § 2-708(2) (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（批量销售商的利润损失）。","educationalObjectiveZh":"如果买方错误地处置被拒绝的货物，卖方可以起诉买方主张侵占（conversion），以追回侵占发生时财产的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","iracZh":{"issue":"如果商店出售的服装不被接受，那么对收藏家来说最有价值的补救措施是什么？","rule":"如果买方错误地处置被拒绝的货物，卖方可以起诉买方主张侵占（conversion），以追回侵占发生时财产的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。","analysis":"UCC 管辖商品（例如服装）销售合同。根据 UCC 完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule），货物必须完全符合合同。未能提供此类货物属于违约行为，除非在剩余履行时间内予以纠正，否则买方可以拒绝或接受全部或部分货物。拒绝后，买方必须在合理的时间内保留对货物的占有，以便卖方可以收回货物。如果买方错误地处置被拒绝的货物，卖方可以起诉买方主张侵占（conversion），以追回侵占发生时财产的公平市场价值。在这里，商店理所当然地拒绝了轻微损坏的服装的收藏，因为它们不完全符合协议。然而，该商店在收藏家提货之前错误地将藏品出售并交付给一家剧院公司。因此，收藏家可以起诉商店主张侵占（conversion），并收回藏品的公平市场价值 15,000 美元。 （选项 A）如果收藏家是一个损失销量卖方，即拥有无限量的商品供应并从每件商品中赚取利润的卖家，他本可以收回损失的利润（2,000 美元）。但在这里，收藏家只是出售他有限的正品群众演员服装。 （选择B）当买方错误地拒绝交付货物时，卖方通常可以根据合同价格减去投标时间和地点的市场价格（此处为5,000美元）来获得赔偿。但在这里，商店理所当然地拒绝了这些货物，因为它们不合格。 （选项C）当买方接受货物但未付款时，卖方可以就合同价格（此处为10,000美元）提起诉讼。但由于商店拒绝并改装了这些服装，收藏家反而有权获得公平的市场价格（15,000 美元）。\n\n教育目的：如果买方错误地处置被拒绝的货物，卖方可以起诉买方主张侵占（conversion），以追回侵占发生时财产的公平市场价值。 UCC = 《统一商法典》（UCC）。\n\n参考文献\nUCC § 2-503（Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020）（完美履行规则（perfect-tender rule））。\n\nUCC § 2-708(2) (Am. Law Inst. & Unif. Law Comm'n 2020)（批量销售商的利润损失）。","conclusion":"答案 D：15,000 美元，侵占（conversion）损害赔偿。"},"translationMeta":{"questionAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","questionMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual","explanationAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z","explanationMethod":"AI-assisted-offline-bilingual"},"legalReview":{"status":"corrected-citation-answer-unchanged","reviewedAsOf":"2026-08-02","noteZh":"已将完美履行规则的条文号由 UCC § 2-503 校正为 § 2-601；答案不变。","authorities":[{"title":"NCBE — MBE Subject Matter Outline (Contracts)","url":"https://www.ncbex.org/sites/default/files/2023-01/MBE_Subject_Matter_Outline.pdf"},{"title":"Uniform Law Commission — Uniform Commercial Code, Article 2 (Sales)","url":"https://www.uniformlaws.org/acts/ucc"}]},"coreVocabulary":[],"explanationAssetIds":[],"attempts":[],"favorite":false,"isWrong":false,"masteredWrong":false,"correctStreak":0,"wrongReasons":[],"notes":"","source":"2025MBE QBank Contracts.pdf","sourceQuestionNumber":273,"sourceQuestionPage":806,"sourceQuestionPages":[806],"sourceExplanationPages":[807],"sourceAnswerPages":[807],"createdAt":"2026-08-02T00:00:00.000Z"}];